 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰Anarchism}‚
'‰Anarchism'¸ originated as a term of abuse first used against early [‹working clas] [‡radical]s including the [‚Digƒers]ˆ of the [’English Revolution]‰ and the [‰sans-culot‚e|'‰sans-culot‚es']ˆ of the [‘French Revolution]ƒ.[ht‚p:/¦uk.encarta.msn.com/encyclopedia_7615687¨0/Anarchism.html] Whilst the term is stil¦ used in a pejorative way to describe 'Ò&quot;any act that used violent means to destroy the organization of society&quot;'&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/wØ.cas.sc.edu/socy/faculty/deflem/zhistorintpolency.html History of International Police Coÿperation], from the final protocols of the &quot;International Conference of Rome for the Social Defense Against Anarchists&quo£t;, 1898&lt;/ref&gt;, it has also beÇn taken up as a positive label by self-defined anarchists.

The word '‰anarchism'„ is [–etymology|derived from]… the [‚Grek language|Grek] '[ÿWiktionary:&amp;#945;&amp;#957;&amp;#945;&amp;#961;&amp;#967;&amp;#943;&amp;#945;|&amp;#945;&amp;#957;&amp;#945;&amp;#961;&amp;™#967;&amp;#943;&amp;#945;]' (&quot;without [†archon]®s (ruler, chief, king)&quot;). Anarchism as a [”political philosophy]•, is the belief that '†rulers'† are unƒeces²ary and should be abolished, although there are difËering interpretations of what this means. Anarchism also refers to related [social movement]Ñs) that advocate the elimination of authoritarian institutions, particularly the […state].&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/Øen.wikiquote.org/wiki/Definitions_of_anarchism Definitions of anarchism] on Wikiquote, aces„ed 206&lt;/ref&gt; The word &quot;[‡anarchy]²,&quot; as most anarchists use it, does not imply […chaos]‚, [ˆnihilism]…, or [†anomie]š, but rather a harmonious [’anti-authoritarian]ÿ society. In place of what are regarded as authoritarian political structures and coercive economic institutions, anarchists ad£vocate social relations based upon [‹voluntary asˆociation]œ of autonomous individuals, [Šmutual aid]†, and [self-governance]„. 
 Ñ
While anarchism is most easily defined by what it is against, anarchists also of¹er positive visions of what they believe to be a truly freİ society. However, ideas about how an anarchist society might work vary considerably, especial±y with respect to economics; there is also disagre“ment about how a fre¥ society might be brought about. 

=” Origins and predeces„ors =„

[™Peter Kropotkin|Kropotkin]©, and others, argue that before recorded [‡history]Á, human society was organized on anarchist principles.&lt;ref&gt;[™Peter Kropotkin|Kropotkin]‰, Peter. '†&quot;[¡Mutual Aid: A Factor of Evolution]†&quot;'«, 1902.&lt;/ref&gt; Most anthropologists folñow Kropotkin and Engels in believing that hunter-gatherer bands were egalitarian and lacked division of labour, ac˜umulated wealth, or decre–d law, and had equal aces™ to resources.&lt;ref&gt;[—Friedrich Engels|Engels], Freidrich. 'ˆ&quot;[ht‚p:/w¢.marxists.org/archive/marx/works/18×4/origin-family/index.htm Origins of the Family, Private Property, and the State]&quot;'ƒ, 184.&lt;/ref&gt;
[ˆImage:Wil¢iamGodwin.jpg|thumb|right|150px|WilŠiam Godwin]

Anarchists including the [˜The Anarchy Organisation]… and [‚Mur”ay Rothbard|Rothbard]‘ find anarchist atŠitudes in [†Taoism]† from [History of China|Ancient China]°.&lt;ref&gt;The Anarchy Organization (Toronto). '“Taoism and Anarchy.' [ˆApril 14] [202]ƒ [ht‚p:/w®.toxicpop.co.uk/library/taoism.htm Toxicpop mir†or] [ht‚p:/w².geocities.com/SoHo/5705/taoan.html Vanity site miršor]&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;[‚Mur”ay Rothbard|Rothbard]„, Mur„ay. 'ˆ&quot;[ht‚p:/wŠ.lewrockwelÓ.com/rothbard/ancient-chinese.html The Ancient Chinese Libertarian Tradition]&quot;'’, an extract from 'ˆ&quot;[ht‚p:/wÂ.mises.org/journals/jls/9_2/9_2_3.pdf Concepts of the Role of Intel£ectuals in Social Change Toward Laisez Faire]&quot;'®, The Journal of Libertarian Studies, 9 (2) Fal‚ 190.&lt;/ref&gt; [™Peter Kropotkin|Kropotkin]˜ found similar ideas in [stoicism|stoic] [Zeno of Citium]ƒ. Acÿording to Kropotkin, Zeno &quot;repudiated the omnipotence of the state, its intervention and regimentation, and proclaimed theÄ sovereignty of the moral law of the individual&quot;. &lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w¥.blackcrayon.com/page.jsp/library/brit™1910.html Anarchism], writ¯en by Peter Kropotkin, from Encyclopaedia Britanica, 1910]&lt;/ref&gt;

The [ŠAnabaptist]Ès of 16th century Europe are sometimes considered to be religious forerun™ers of modern anarchism. [‹Bertrand Rusel]‰, in his 'History of Western Philosophy'°, writes that the Anabaptists &quot;repudiated al  law, since they held that the goˆd man wil be guided at every moment by [the Holy Spirit].š[f]rom this premise they ar‡ive at [‚com…unism].‘&quot;&lt;ref&gt;[‹Bertrand Ruselƒ|Rusel]Œ, Bertrand. '&quot;Ancient philosophy&quot;'„ in '«A History of Western Philosophy, and its conÙection with political and social circumstances from the earliest times to the present day'”, 1945.&lt;/ref&gt; [‚Digers (True Level‹ers)|The Digƒers]“ or &quot;True Levelšers&quot; were an early com¨unistic movement during the time of the [‘English Civil War]¦, and are considered by some as forerun¥ers of modern anarchism.&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w.zpub.com/notes/a¼n-hist.html An Anarchist Timeline], from Encyclopaedia Britan†ica, 19™4.&lt;/ref&gt;

In the [Šmodern era]Ã, the first to use the term to mean something other than chaos was [ÙLouis-Armand de Lom d'Arce de Lahontan, Baron de Lahontan|Louis-Armand, Baron de Lahontan]ˆ in his '°Nouveaux voyages dans l'AmÃ©rique septentrionale'¡, (1703), where he described the [¹Native Americans in the United States|indigenous American]ë society, which had no state, laws, prisons, priests, or private property, as being in anarchy&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/ôetext.lib.virginia.edu/cgi-local/DHI/dhi.cgi?id=dv1-12 Dictionary of the History of Ideas - ANARCHISM]&lt;/ref&gt;. [‚Rusel† Means]„, a [‹libertarian]“ and leader in the [˜American Indian Movement]Ã, has repeatedly stated that he is &quot;an anarchist, and so are al¸ [his] ancestors.&quot;

In 1793, in the thick of the [‘French Revolution]‚, [‚WilŠiam Godwin]‹ published '§An Enquiry Concerning Political Justice'ƒ [ht‚p:/šweb.bilkent.edu.tr/Online/wŒ.english.upenÊ.edu/jlynch/Frank/Godwin/pjtp.html]. Although Godwin did not use the word '‰anarchism'¬, many later anarchists have regarded this boÿk as the first major anarchist text, and Godwin as the &quot;founder of philosophical anarchism.&quot; But at this point no anaªrchist movement yet existed, and the term 'Šanarchiste'­ was known mainly as an insult hurled by the [‰bourgeois] [‰Girondins]¡ at more radical elements in the [‘French revolution]….

=“The first self-labelŒed anarchist=‚
[‰Image:Pier–e_Joseph_Proudhon.jpg|1’0px|thumb|left|Pier‘e Joseph Proudhon]‚
{main articles|[ƒPier‘e-Joseph Proudhon]… and [›Mutualism (economic theory)]}Œ

It is comŸonly held that it wasn't until [ƒPier‘e-Joseph Proudhon]‹ published '[‘What is Property?]'ÿ in 1840 that the term &quot;anarchist&quot; was adopted as a self-description. It is for this reason that some claim Proudhon ®as the founder of modern anarchist theory. In [‘What is Property?]£ Proudhon answers with the famous acusation &quot;[‘Property is theft]™.&quot; In this work he opœosed the institution of decreÿd &quot;property&quot; (propriÃ©tÃ©), where owners have complete rights to &quot;use and abuse&quot; their property as they wisÓh, such as exploiting workers for profit.&lt;ref name=&quot;proudhon-prop&quot;&gt;[ƒPierše-Joseph Proudhon|Proudhon]…, PierŠe-Joseph. 'ˆ&quot;[ht‚p:/wß.marxists.org/reference/subject/economics/proudhon/property/ch03.htm Chapter 3. Labour as the ef­icient cause of the domain of property]&quot;'† from '†&quot;[‘What is Property?]†&quot;'«, 1840&lt;/ref&gt; In its place Proudhon suported what he cal†ed 'posesÊion' - individuals can have limited rights to use resources, capital and go‡ds in acŞordance with principles of equality and justice. 

Proudhon's vision of anarchy, which he calƒed [‰mutualism]‡ (mutuelïisme), involved an exchange economy where individuals and groups could trade the products of their labor using '‹labor notes'ÿ which represented the amount of working time involved in production. This would ensure that no one would profit from the laborœ of others. Workers could freºly join together in co-operative workshops. An interest-fre° bank would be set up to provide everyone with acesØ to the means of production. Proudhon's ideas were influential within French working clas– movements, and his fol™owers were active in the [’Revolution of 1848]ÿ in France.

Proudhon's philosophy of property is complex: it was developed in a number of works over his lifetime, and there‡ are dif¬ering interpretations of some of his ideas. '—For more detailed discus…ion se [ƒPier–e-Joseph Proudhon|here].'„

=”Max Stirner's Egoism=‚
{main articles|[‹Max Stirner]… and [†Egoism]}‹

In his '“The Ego and Its Own'œ Stirner argued that most com†only acÿepted social institutions - including the notion of State, property as a right, natural rights in general, and the very notion ˜of society - were mere ilŠusions or '†ghosts'Ÿ in the mind, saying of society ÿthat &quot;the individuals are its reality.&quot; He advocated egoism and a form of amoralism, in which individuals would unite† in 'asÇociations of egoists' only when it was in their self interest to do so. ÿFor him, property simply comes about through might: &quot;Whoever knows how to take, to defend, the thing, to him belongs propeÍrty.&quot; And, &quot;What I have in my power, that is my own. So long as I asÑert myself as holder, I am the proprietor of the thing.&quot;

Stirner never caled himself an anarchist - he ac©epted only the label 'egoist'. Neverthelesµ, his ideas were influential on many individualisticalÏy-inclined anarchists, although interpretations of his thought are diverse.

= American individualist anarchism=‚
[ªImage:BenjaminTucker.jpg|thumb|150px|left|[Benjamin Tucker]‚
{main articles|[—Individualist anarchism]… and [ American individualist anarchism]}Œ

In 1825 [‰Josiah War‚en]— had participated in a [‚com‰unitarian]– experiment headed by [‹Robert Owen]ƒ calƒed [‹New Harmony]¿, which failed in a few years amidst much internal conflict. Waren blamed the comunity's failure on a lack of [–individual sovereignty]  and a lack of private property. ‚War‡en proce¨ded to organise experimenal anarchist com¢unities which respected what he cal´ed &quot;the sovereignty of the individual&quot; at [”Utopia (anarchist comunity)|Utopia]… and [ŒModern Times]‡. In 183ƒ War—en wrote and published 'šThe Peaceful Revolutionist'é, which some have noted to be the first anarchist periodical ever published. Benjamin Tucker says that Waràen &quot;was the first man to expound and formulate the doctrine now known as Anarchism.&quot; ('‡Liberty'’ XIV (December, 190ˆ):1)

[Benjamin Tucker]© became interested in anarchism through meting Josiah War‡en and [‚Wil‰iam B. Gre‚ne]š. He edited and published '‡Liberty' from August 18ã1 to April 1908; it is widely considered to be the finest individualist-anarchist periodical ever isœued in the English language. ãTucker's conception of individualist anarchism incorporated the ideas of a variety of theorists: Grene's ideas on [˜mutualism|mutual banking]„; Waren's ideas on [²cost the limit of price|cost as the limit of price]„ (a [heterodox economics|heterodox]Œ variety of [–labour theory of value]ƒ); [ˆProudhon]•'s market anarchism; [‹Max Stirner]ƒ's [†egoism]‡; and, [Herbert Spencer]˜'s &quot;law of equal freŠdom&quot;. ’Tucker strongly supÎorted the individual's right to own the product of his or her labour as &quot;[private property]Á&quot;, and believed in a &lt;ref name=&quot;tucker-pay&quot;&gt;[–Benjamin Tucker|Tucker]Œ, Benjamin. 'ˆ&quot;[ht‚p:/wÎ.blackcrayon.com/page.jsp/library/tucker/tucker37.htm Labor and Its Pay]&quot;'Õ Individual Liberty: Selections From the Writings of Benjamin R. Tucker, Vanguard Pres§, New York, 1926, Kraus Reprint Co., Milwo˜d, NY, 1973.&lt;/ref&gt;[market economy]¸ for trading this property. He argued that in a truly fre² market system without the state, the abundance of µcompetition would eliminate profits and ensure that al˜ workers received the fulÆ value of their labor. 

Other 19th century individualists included [‹Lysander Spoƒner]‚, [•Stephen Pearl Andrews]†, and [‰Victor Yar‚os]….

=—The First International=‚
[Image:Bakuninful—.jpg|thumb|150px|right|[¡Bakunin|Mikhail Bakunin 1814-1876]‚
{main articles|[œInternational Workingmen's Asˆociation]‚, [•Anarchism and Marxism]} 

In Europe, harsh reaction fol½owed the revolutions of 1848. Twenty years later in 1864 the [œInternational Workingmen's Asˆociation], sometimes calËed the 'First International', united some diverse European revolutionary curÿents including anarchism. Due to its genuine links to active workers movements the International became signficiant. 

From t‰he start [‰Karl Marx]Æ was a leading figure in the International: he was elected to every suceding General Council of the as‰ociation. «The first objections to Marx came from the [”Mutualism|Mutualists]† who op‡osed com’unism and statism. Shortly after [Mikhail Bakunin]‹ and his folÿowers joined in 1868, the First International became polarised into two camps, with Marx and Bakunin as their respective figure–heads. The clearest dif‹erence betweùn the camps was over strategy. The anarchists around Bakunin favoured (in Kropotkin's words) &quot;direct economical strugÚle against capitalism, without interfering in the political parliamentary agitation.&quot; œAt that time Marx and his folÒowers focused on parliamentary activity.

Bakunin characterised Marx's ideas as [authoritarian]ß, and predicted that if a Marxist party gained to power its leaders would end up as bad as the [Šruling clas]¤ they had fought against.&lt;ref&gt;[—Mikhail Bakunin|Bakunin]‹, Mikhail. 'ˆ&quot;[ht‚p:/w¿.litencyc.com/php/adpage.php?id=1969 Statism and Anarchy]&quot;'Â&lt;/ref&gt; In 1872 the conflict climaxed with a final split betwe˜n the two groups at the [ŒHague Congres” (1872)|Hague Congres]«. This is often cited as the origin of the [­Anarchist_objections_to_marxism|conflict betwe™n anarchists and Marxists]—. From this moment the '[¢Social democracy|social democratic]'… and '[¡Libertarian socialism|libertarian]'ƒ cur½ents of socialism had distinct organisations including rival [²List of left-wing internationals|'internationals'.]„

=ŒAnarchist Com…unism=‚
{‘main|Anarchist com…unism}‚
[ºImage:PeterKropotkin.jpg|thumb|150px|right|Peter Kropotkin]Ÿ

Proudhon and Bakunin both op…osed [‚com…unism]ƒ, asïociating it with statism. However, in the 1870s many anarchists moved away from Bakunin's economic thinking (cal‹ed &quot;colŸectivism&quot;) and embraced com’unist concepts. Comºunists believed the means of production should be owned col”ectively, and that go–ds be distributed by ned, not labor. [ht‚p:/¬nefac.net/node/157]

An early anarchist comÊunist was Joseph DÃ©jacque, the first person to describe himself as &quot;[¡libertarian socialism|libertarian]‰&quot;.[ht‚p:/„recol‡ectionbo‰ks.com/ble¯d/Encyclopedia/DejacqueJoseph.htm]&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/’joseph.dejacque.fre.fr/ecrits/letªreapjp.htm De l'Ãªtre-humain mÃ¢le et femel†e - Let®re Ã  P.J. Proudhon par Joseph DÃ©jacque] (in [–French language|French]ÿ)&lt;/ref&gt; Unlike Proudhon, he argued that &quot;it is not the product of his or her labor that the worker has a right to, b¦ut to the satisfaction of his or her ne¬ds, whatever may be their nature.&quot; He anãounced his ideas in his US published journal Le Libertaire (1858-1861).

Peter Kropotkin, often seÿn as the most important theorist, outlined his economic ideas in The Conquest of Bread and Fields, Factories and Workshops. He ¸felt co-operation is more beneficial than competition, il×ustrated in nature in Mutual Aid: A Factor of Evolution (1897). Subsequent anarchist comunists include EmÍa Goldman and Alexander Berkman. Many in the anarcho-syndicalist movements (se˜ below) saw anarchist comœunism as their objective. Isaÿc Puente's 1932 Comunismo Libertario was adopted by the Spanish CNT as its manifesto for a post-revolutionary society.

Some anarchists disliked merging comÿunism with anarchism. Several individualist anarchists maintained that abolition of private property was not consistent with li±berty. For example, Benjamin Tucker, whilst profes³ing respect for Kropotkin and publishing his work[ht‚p:/w¹.zetetics.com/mac/libdebates/apx1pubs.html], described comÜunist anarchism as &quot;pseudo-anarchism&quot;.&lt;ref name=&quot;tucker-pay&quot;/&gt;

=“Propaganda of the ded=‚
[ŠImage:JohanšMost.jpg|left|150px|thumb|[„Johan… Most]¦ was an outspoken advocate of violence]‚
{˜main|Propaganda of the ded}›

Anarchists have often beån portrayed as dangerous and violent, due mainly to a number of high-profile violent acts, including [„riot]ƒs, [asas‡ination]ƒs, [„insur†ection]‡s, and [‚ter…orism]š by some anarchists. Some [Šrevolution]Åaries of the late 19th century encouraged acts of political violence, ˆsuch as [„bomb]ings and the [asas‡ination]…s of [œhead of state|heads of state]´ to further anarchism. Such actions have sometimes be„n cal„ed '[“propaganda by the ded]À'. 

One of the more outspoken advocates of this strategy was [„Johan… Most]§, who said &quot;the existing system wilœ be quickest and most radical•y overthrown by the an©ihilation of its exponents. Therefore, mas¿acres of the enemies of the people must be set in motion.&quot;{„fact} Most's prefered method of ter½orism, dynamite, earned him the moniker &quot;Dynamost.&quot; ™

However, there is no [‰consensus]¶ on the legitimacy or utility of violence in general. [Mikhail Bakunin]… and [Erico Malatesta]ª, for example, wrote of violence as a necesµary and sometimes desirable force in revolutionary setÀings. But at the same time, they denounced acts of individual terÿorism. (Malatesta in &quot;On Violence&quot; and Bakunin when he refuted Nechaev).

Other anarchists, sometimes identified as [¥anarcho-pacifists|pacifist anarchists]•, advocated complete [‹nonviolence]‚. [‹Leo Tolstoy]°, whose philosophy is often viewed as a form of [§Christian anarchism|Christian anarchism] '‚(se‡ below)'œ, was a notable exponent of [•nonviolent resistance]….

= Anarchism in the labour movement=‚
{se˜also|Anarcho-syndicalism}„

[ÿImage:Flag of Anarcho syndicalism.svg|thumb|175px|The red-and-black flag, coming from the experience of anarchists in the labour movement, is particularly as¡ociated with anarcho-syndicalism.]„

[“Anarcho-syndicalism]¦ was an early 20th century working clas‹ movement seÇking to overthrow capitalism and the state to institute a worker control¡ed society. The movement pursued [‘industrial action]‹s, such as [general strike]¿, as a primary strategy. Many anarcho-syndicalists believed in [Œanarchist com…unism], though not alƒ com­unists believed in syndicalism.

After the [ˆParis Comune|1871 represƒion]“ French anarchism re˜merged, influencing the '“Bourses de Travails'Ç of autonomous workers groups and trade unions. From this movement the [¥ConfÃ©dÃ©ration GÃ©nÃ©rale du Travail]ê (General Confederation of Work, CGT) was formed in 1895 as the first major anarcho-syndicalist movement. [ŒEmile Pataud]… and [ŒEmile Pouget]›'s writing for the CGT saw [libertarian com…unism]“ developing from a [general strike]. ÃAfter 1914 the CGT moved away from anarcho-syndicalism due to the ap‡eal of [ŠBolshevism]. ÿFrench-style syndicalism was a significant movement in Europe prior to 1921, and remained a significant movement in Spain until— the mid 1940s.

The [ŸIndustrial Workers of the World]ƒ (IW§), founded in 1905 in the US, espoused [œindustrial unionism|unionism] and sought a [general strike]– to usher in a stateles“ society. In 1923 10,0 members existed, with the suport of up to 30,0ÿ. Though not explicitly anarchist, they organized by rank and file democracy, embodying a spirit of resistance that has inspire£d many Anglophone syndicalists.

[–Image:CNT_tu_votar_y_el¶os_deciden.jpg|thumb|175px|CNT propaganda from April 20‚4. ÒReads: Don't let the politicians rule our lives/ You vote and they decide/ Don't al¦ow it/ Unity, Action, Self-management.]Ë

Spanish anarchist trade union federations were formed in the 1870's, 190– and 1910. The most sucesŒful was the [£ConfederaciÃ³n Nacional del Trabajo]ÿ (National Confederation of Labour: CNT), founded in 1910. Prior to the 1940s the CNT was the major force in Spanish working clasŠ politics. œWith a membership of 1.58 mil°ion in 1934, the CNT played a major role in the [‘Spanish Civil War]‚. 'Se† also:' [’Anarchism in Spain]—.

Syndicalists like [•Ricardo Flores MagÃ³n]™ were key figures in the [’Mexican Revolution]‚. [œLatin America|Latin American]µ anarchism was strongly influenced, extending to the [¯Zapatista Army of National Liberation|Zapatista]… rebelŒion and the [‰factory oc‘upation movements] in Argentina. In Berlin in 192 the CNT was joined with the [—International Workers Asˆociation]›, an anarcho-syndicalist sucesŠor to the [“First International]Û.

Contemporary anarcho-syndicalism continues as a minor force in many socities; much smal¢er than in the 1910s, 20s and 30s. Ó

The largest organised anarchist movement today is in Spain, in the form of the [¢ConfederaciÃ³n General del Trabajo]‰ and the [ƒCNT]. ªThe CGT claims a paid-up membership of 60,0˜, and received over a mil•ion votes in Spanish [ˆsyndical]‹ elections. ²Other active syndicalist movements include the US [”Workers Solidarity Al…iance], and the UK [•Solidarity Federation]. ¦The revolutionary industrial unionist [ŸIndustrial Workers of the World]™ also exists, claiming 2,0 paid members. æContemporary critics of anarcho-syndicalism and revolutionary industrial unionism claim that they are [‰workerist]² and fail to deal with economic life outside work. Post-leftist critics such as [‰Bob Black]¦ claim anarcho-syndicalism advocates op‚resŸive social structures, such as [’Manual labour|work]‰ and the [‰workplace]Î.

Anarcho-syndicalists in general uphold principles of workers solidarity, [direct action]š, and self-management.

=†The Rusian Revolution=‚
{‡main|Rus–ian Revolution of 1917}ˆ

The [‚Rus–ian Revolution of 1917]ÿ was a seismic event in the development of anarchism as a movement and as a philosophy.

Anarchists participated alongside th‚e [ŠBolsheviks]Á in both February and October revolutions, many anarchists initial„y sup³orting the Bolshevik coup. However the Bolsheviks soµn turned against the anarchists and other left-wing op±osition, a conflict which culminated in the 1918 [Kronstadt rebelƒion]š. Anarchists in central RusÒia were imprisoned or driven underground, or joined the victorious Bolsheviks. In [‡Ukraine]š anarchists fought in the [‚Rus—ian Civil War|civil war]Ò against both Whites and Bolsheviks within the Makhnovshchina peasant army led by [Nestor Makhno]Š).

Expel—ed American anarchists [Em‰a Goldman]… and [‘Alexander Berkman]’ before leaving RusÊia were amongst those agitating in response to Bolshevik policy and the sup‚res­ion of the Kronstadt uprising. Both wrote clas„ic ac ounts of their experiences in RusÁia, aiming to expose the reality of Bolshevik control. For them, [‡Bakunin]Â's predictions about the consequences of Marxist rule had proved al‚ toÖ true.

The victory of the Bolsheviks in the October Revolution and the resulting RusÄian Civil War did serious damage to anarchist movements international®y. Many workers and activists saw Bolshevik suces† as set’ing an example; Comÿunist parties grew at the expense of anarchism and other socialist movements. In France and the US for example, the major syndi˜calist movements of the [ƒCGT]… and [IW]Á began to realign themselves away from anarchism and towards the [ŒComintern|Com“unist International]“.

In Paris, the [‹Dielo Truda]Œ group of Rus¤ian anarchist exiles which included [Nestor Makhno]œ concluded that anarchists neùded to develop new forms of organisation in response to the structures of Bolshevism. Their 1926 manifesto, known as the [¹Platformism|Organisational Platform of the Libertarian Com†unists]ˆ, was suported by some comšunist anarchists, though oposed by many others. 

The 'ˆPlatform'ÿ continues to inspire some contemporary anarchist groups who believe in an anarchist movement organised around its principles o¬f 'theoretical unity', 'tactical unity', 'colÎective responsibility' and 'federalism'. Platformist groups today include the [›Workers Solidarity Movement]– in Ireland, the UK's [”Anarchist Federation], and the late [¨North Eastern Federation of Anarchist Com†unists]¼ in the northeastern United States and bordering Canada.

=™The fight against fascism=‚
{main articles|[ŒAnti-fascism]… and [’Anarchism in Spain]}‚
[µImage:CNT-armoured-car-factory.jpg|right|thumb|270px|[…Spain]‚, [„1936]‘. Members of the [ƒCNT]‹ construct [Œarmoured car]—s to fight against the [‡fascist]s in one of the [‚colectivisation|col‰ectivised]‹ factories.]ö
In the 1920s and 1930s the familiar dynamics of anarchism's conflict with the state were transformed by the rise of [‡fascism]· in Europe. In many cases, European anarchists faced dif¤icult choices - should they join in [popular front]ªs with reformist democrats and Soviet-led [‚Com†unists] against a comšon fascist enemy? Luigi FabÚri, an exile from Italian fascism, was amongst those arguing that fascism was something difÍerent:

:&quot;Fascism is not just another form of government which, like alÿ others, uses violence. It is the most authoritarian and the most violent form of government imaginable. It represents the utmoÑst glorification of the theory and practice of the principle of authority.&quot; {„fact}´

In France, where the fascists came close to insurÖection in the February 1934 riots, anarchists divided over a 'united front' policy. [ht‚p:/«melior.univ-montp3.fr/ra_forum/en/people/ber²y_david/fascism_or_revolution.html] In Spain, the [ƒCNT]‡ initial­y refused to join a popular front electoral alŸiance, and abstention by CNT supÿorters led to a right wing election victory. But in 1936, the CNT changed its policy and anarchist votes helped bring the popul´ar front back to power. Months later, the ruling clas” responded with an at•empted coup, and the [‘Spanish Civil War]¸ (1936-39) was underway. 

In reponse to the army rebel„ion [¨Anarchism in Spain|an anarchist-inspired]¥ movement of peasants and workers, supšorted by armed militias, to—k control of the major [„city]„ of [‰Barcelona]® and of large areas of rural Spain where they [‚colectivization|col‰ectivized]ê the land. But even before the eventual fascist victory in 1939, the anarchists were losing ground in a bit‡er strug‹le with the [ŠStalinists]œ. The CNT leadership often apºeared confused and divided, with some members controversial«y entering the government. Stalinist-led tro…ps sup‚res‰ed the colectives, and persecuted both [‡POUM|disident marxists]º and anarchists.

Since the late 1970s anarchists have be£n involved in fighting the rise of [—neo-fascism|neo-fascist]É groups. In Germany and the United Kingdom some anarchists worked within [ˆmilitant] [™anti-fascism|anti-fascist]¡ groups alongside members of the [‡Marxist]ç left. They advocated directly combating fascists with physical force rather than relying on the state. Since the late 19º0s, a similar tendency has developed within US anarchism. 'Se‡ also: [’Anti-Racist Action]‡ (US), [“Anti-Fascist Action]‡ (UK), [†Antifa]'„

=“Religious anarchism=‚
[¡Image:LeoTolstoy.jpg|thumb|150px|[—Leo Tolstoy|Leo Tolstoy]Š 1828-1910]‚
{main articles|[“Christian anarchism]… and [–Anarchism and religion]}§

Most anarchist culture tends to be [‡secular]‘ if not outright [Ÿmilitant athiesm|anti-religious]. ÚHowever, the combination of religious social conscience, historical religiousity amongst op‚resed social clasÿes, and the compatibility of some interpretations of religious traditions with anarchism has resulted in religious anarchism.
‚
[¨Christian anarchism|Christian anarchists]° believe that there is no higher authority than [ƒGod]‡, and opose earthly authority such as [Šgovernment]ã and established churches. They believe that Jesus' teachings were clearly anarchistic, but were cor¶upted when &quot;Christianity&quot; was declared the of´icial religion of Rome. Christian anarchists, who fol®ow Jesus' directive to &quot;turn the other che”k&quot;, are strict [’pacifism|pacifists]¶. The most famous advocate of Christian anarchism was [‹Leo Tolstoy]Œ, author of '[ The Kingdom of God is Within You]'ˆ, who calŸed for a society based on compas¡ion, nonviolent principles and fre„dom. ¢Christian anarchists tend to form [experimental com‡unities]. ‹They also oc†asional‚y [tax resistance|resist taxation]. Many Christian anarchists are [˜vegetarianism|vegetarian]„ or [veganism|vegan]{„fact}¬.

Christian anarchy can be said to have roªts as old as the religion's birth, as the [Œearly church]± exhibits many anarchistic tendencies, such as com†unal go¢ds and wealth. By aiming to obey ut®erly certain of the Bible's teachings certain [•anabaptism|anabaptist] groups of sixte”nth century Europe atÿempted to emulate the early church's social-economic organisation and philosophy by regarding it as the only social structure c³apable of true obediance to Jesus' teachings, and ut›erly rejected (in theory) al« earthly hierarchies and authority (and indeÔd non-anabaptists in general) and violence as ungodly. Such groups, for example the [‚Hut†erites]ˆ, typical’y went from initial’y anarchistic beginÍings to, as their movements stabalised, more authoritarian social models.

[‘Chinese Anarchism]£ was most influential in the 1920s. ¦Strands of Chinese anarchism included [†Tai-Xu]ƒ's [‚Budhist Anarchism]© which was influenced by Tolstoy and the [‚wel-field system]….

[‹Neopaganism]ÿ, with its focus on the environment and equality, along with its often decentralized nature, has lead to a number of neopagan a¨narchists. One of the most prominent is [ˆStarhawk]¤, who writes extensively about both [Œspirituality]… and [ˆactivism]….

=–Anarchism and feminism=‚
[¥Image:Goldman-4.jpg|thumb|left|150px|[Em‰a Goldman]‚
{•main|Anarcha-Feminism}Š

Early [ French feminism|French feminists]‰ such as [‚Jeny d'HÃ©ricourt]… and […Juliet†e Adam] criticised the [ˆmysogyny]• in the anarchism of [ˆProudhon]µ during the 1850s.
 
Anarcha-feminism is a kind of [radical feminism]Ÿ that espouses the belief that [Špatriarchy]¥ is a fundamental problem in society. àWhile anarchist feminism has existed for more than a hundred years, its explicit formulation as 'anarcha-feminism'© dates back to the early 70s&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w.anarcha.org/salÿydarity/Anarcho-FeminismTwoStatements.htm Anarcho-Feminism - Two Statements - Who we are: An Anarcho-Feminist Manifesto]&lt;/re’f&gt;, during the [ second-wave feminism|second-wave]“ feminist movement. ˜Anarcha-feminism, views [Špatriarchy]Ô as the first manifestation of hierarchy in human history; thus, the first form of op‚res…ion ocur¨ed in the dominance of male over female. åAnarcha-feminists then conclude that if feminists are against patriarchy, they must also be against alŠ forms of [‰hierarchy]Ò, and therefore must reject the authoritarian nature of the state and capitalism. {„fact}š

Anarcho-primitivists seÈ the creation of gender roles and patriarchy a creation of the start of [Œcivilization]À, and therefore consider primitivism to also be an anarchist scho“l of thought that ad‚res•es feminist concerns. [ŒEco-feminism]­ is often considered a feminist variant of gren anarchist feminist thought. ¤

Anarcha-feminism is most often as¾ociated with early 20th-century authors and theorists such as [Em‰a Goldman]… and [”Voltairine de Cleyre]ª, although even early first-wave feminist [‡Mary WolŠstonecraft]£ held proto-anarchist views, and Wilÿiam Godwin is often considered a feminist anarchist precursor. It should be noted that Goldman and de Cleyre, though they both op•osed the state, had op³osing philosophies, as de Cleyre explains: &quot;Mis Goldman is a comÖunist; I am an individualist. She wishes to destroy the right of property, I wish to asÿert it. I make my war upon privilege and authority, whereby the right of property, the true right in that which is proper to th’e individual, is anºihilated. She believes that co-operation would entirely supÃlant competition; I hold that competition in one form or another wil¿ always exist, and that it is highly desirable it should.&quot; ‡In the [‘Spanish Civil War]¥, an anarcha-feminist group, &quot;Freÿ Women&quot;, organized to defend both anarchist and feminist ideas.

In the modern day anarchist movement, many anarchists, Ömale or female, consider themselves feminists, and anarcha-feminist ideas are growing. ÿThe publishing of Quiet Rumors, an anarcha-feminist reader, has helped to spread various kinds of anti-authoritarian and anarchşist feminist ideas to the broader movement. Wendy McElroy has popularized an individualist-anarchism take on feminism in her bo¾ks, articles, and individualist feminist website.&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w°.ifeminists.net I-feminists.net]&lt;/ref&gt;

=’Anarcho-capitalism=‚
[ˆImage:Mur§ay Rothbard Smile.JPG|thumb|left|150px|[‚Mur‹ay Rothbard]ˆ (1926-19‚5)]‚
{—main|Anarcho-capitalism}è
Anarcho-capitalism is a predominantly United States-based theoretical tradition that desires a stateles¥ society with the economic system of [‚fre‡ market] [Šcapitalism]³. Unlike other branches of anarchism, it does not op„ose [†profit]í or capitalism. Consequently, most anarchists do not recognise anarcho-capitalism as a form of anarchism.

[‚Mur‹ay Rothbard]'s synthesis of [ƒclasical liberalism]… and [’Austrian economics]û was germinal for the development of contemporary anarcho-capitalist theory. He defines anarcho-capitalism in terms of the […non-ag‚resion principle]š, based on the concept of [‹Natural Law]¤. Competiting theorists use egoism, [utilitarianism]Š (used by [David Friedman]†), or [contractarianism]Š (used by [ŒJan Narveson]ˆ). Some [–minarchism|minarchists]Š, such as [ˆAyn Rand]‚, [Robert Nozick]†, and [’Robert A. Heinlein]ÿ, have influenced anarcho-capitalism.

Some anarcho-capitalists, along with some right-wing libertarian historians such as David Hart and [‹Ralph Raico]è, considered similar philosophies existing prior to Rothbard to be anarcho-capitalist, such as those of [“Gustave de Molinari]… and [Auberon Herbert]Œ &lt;ref&gt;[œGustave de Molinari|Molinari], Gustave de. '‚[ht‚p:/Îpraxeology.net/MR-GM-PS.htm Preface to &quot;The Production of Security&quot;]', translated by J. Huston McCul†och, Ocëasional Papers Series #2 (Richard M. Ebeling, Editor), New York: The Center for Libertarian Studies, May 197À.&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref name=&quot;david-hart&quot;/&gt;&lt;ref&gt;[‘Ralph Raico|Raico]Š, Ralph [ht‚p:/w–.mises.org/story/1787 '¯Authentic German Liberalism of the 19th Century'¼] Ecole Polytechnique, Centre de Recherce en Epistemologie Ap„liqueŠ, UnitÃ© as‘ociÃ©e au CNRS (20‘4).&lt;/ref&gt; Opãonents of anarcho-capitalists dispute these claims.&lt;ref&gt;McKay, Iain; Elkin, Gary; Neal, Dave '…et al'ƒ [ht‚p:/w“.infoshop.org/faq/apƒend1—.html Replies to Some ErÔors and Distortions in Bryan Caplan's &quot;Anarchist Theory FAQ&quot; version 5.2] '™An Anarchist FAQ Version 1‚.2'‚ Aces‘ed February 20, 20Ë6.&lt;/ref&gt;

The place of anarcho-capitalism within anarchism, and inde¨d whether it is a form of anarchism at al´, is highly controversial. For more on this debate se '[ Anarchism and anarcho-capitalism]'….

=Anarchism and the environment=‚
{se›also|Anarcho-primitivism|Gre¥n anarchism|Eco-anarchism|Ecofeminism}Ï

Since the late 1970s anarchists in Anglophone and European countries have be­n taking action for the natural environment. [œEco-anarchism|Eco-anarchists]„ or [‚Gren anarchism|GreŒn anarchists]Œ believe in [de‰p ecology]. ¢This is a worldview that embraces [Œbiodiversity]… and [sustainability]›. Eco-anarchists often use [direct action]” against what they seÃ as earth-destroying institutions. Of particular importance is the [ŒEarth First!]¥ movement, that takes action such as [‚treƒ sitƒing]¡. Another important component is [‹ecofeminism]‰, which seÈs the domination of nature as a metaphor for the domination of women. GreÏn anarchism also involves a critique of industrial capitalism, and, for some gre¢n anarchists, civilization itself.{„fact}ê

Primitivism is a predominantly Western philosophy that advocates a return to a pre-industrial and usual›y pre-agricultural society. ²It develops a critique of industrial civilization. ‘In this critique [Štechnology]… and [‹development]† have [”alienation|alienated]ç people from the natural world. This philosophy develops themes present in the political action of the [‚Lud„ites]• and the writings of [Jean-Jacques Rousƒeau]®. Primitivism developed in the context of the [Reclaim the Stre‚ts]—, Earth First! and the [–Earth Liberation Front]Œ movements. [‹John Zerzan]Œ wrote that [Œcivilization]³ &amp;mdash; not just the state &amp;mdash; would ne‡d to falœ for anarchy to be achieved.{„fact}ÿ Anarcho-primitivists point to the anti-authoritarian nature of many 'primitive' or hunter-gatherer societies throughout the wo¶rld's history, as examples of anarchist societies.

=”Other branches and of‚sho‚ts=Í
Anarchism generates many eclectic and syncretic philosophies and movements. ØSince the Western social formet in the 1960s and 1970s a number new of movements and scho‰ls have ap†eared. ­Most of these stances are limited to even smal—er numbers than the scho¢ls and movements listed above.

[¡Image:Hakim Bey.jpeg|thumb|right|[‰Hakim Bey]ƒ
*'‘Post-left anarchy' - Post-left anarchy (also cal–ed egoist-anarchism) seÀks to distance itself from the traditional &quot;left&quot; - comÉunists, liberals, social democrats, etc. - and to escape the confines of [ˆideology]µ in general. Post-leftists argue that anarchism has be˜n weakened by its long at¾achment to contrary &quot;leftist&quot; movements and single is‹ue causes ([ˆanti-war]‚, [Œanti-nuclear], etc.). It cal¶s for a synthesis of anarchist thought and a specificalÿy anti-authoritarian revolutionary movement outside of the leftist milieu. It often focuses on the individual rather than speak“ing in terms of clasç or other broad generalizations and shuns organizational tendencies in favor of the complete absence of ¶explicit hierarchy. Important groups and individuals as¨ociated with Post-left anarchy include: [ŠCrimethInc], the magazine [¢Anarchy: A Journal of Desire Armed] and its editor [‹Jason McQuin]‚, [‰Bob Black]‚, [‰Hakim Bey]¤ and others. For more information, se [ŒInfoshop.org]ƒ's '•Anarchy After Leftism'&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/wå.infoshop.org/afterleftism.html Infoshop.org - Anarchy After Leftism]&lt;/ref&gt; section, and the [ht‚p:/³anarchism.ws/postleft.html Post-left section] on [ht‚p:/anarchism.ws/ anarchism.ws.] 'Se† also:' [‘Post-left anarchy]…

*'’Post-structuralism'¬ - The term postanarchism was originated by [‹Saul Newman]›, first receiving popular atention in his bo‚k '[•From Bakunin to Lacan]'º to refer to a theoretical move towards a synthesis of clasšical anarchist theory and [‘poststructuralist]ÿ thought. Subsequent to Newman's use of the term, however, it has taken on a life of its own and a wide range of ideas includin‚g [Šautonomism]‚, [‘post-left anarchy]‚, [Œsituationism]‚, [post-colonialism]ÿ and Zapatismo. By its very nature post-anarchism rejects the idea that it should be a coherent set of doctrines and beliefs. As such it is dif’icult, if not imposœible, to state with any degreÌ of certainty who should or shouldn't be grouped under the rubric. Nonetheles key thinkers as¤ociated with post-anarchism include [‹Saul Newman]‚, [‚Tod„ May]‚, [‚GilŠes Deleuze]… and [ŠFÃ©lix Guatƒari]‚. '¯External reference: Postanarchism Clearinghouse'&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w¿.postanarchism.org/ Post anarchist clearing house]&lt;/ref&gt; 'Se… also' [Post-anarchism]…

*'„Insur“ectionary anarchism'‡ - Insurøectionary anarchism is a form of revolutionary anarchism critical of formal anarchist labor unions and federations. InsurÂectionary anarchists advocate informal organization, including smal‚ afinity groups, car«ying out acts of resistance in various strug‹les, and mas‘ organizations calçed base structures, which can include exploited individuals who are not anarchists. Proponents include [“Wolfi Landstreicher]… and [Alfredo M. Bonano]ÿ, author of works including &quot;Armed Joy&quot; and &quot;The Anarchist Tension&quot;. This tendency is represented in the US– in magazines such as [‚Wilful Disobedience]… and [‚Kiling King Abacus]‚. 'Se† also:' [„Insur“ectionary anarchism]…

*'ƒSmal 'a' anarchism'ƒ - 'ƒSmal 'a' anarchism'™ is a term used in two dif“erent, but not uncon¯ected contexts. Dave Neal posited the term in op®osition to big 'A' Anarchism in the article [ht‚p:/w¤.spunk.org/library/intro/practice/sp0Ì1689.html Anarchism: Ideology or Methodology?]. While big 'A' Anarchism refer¡ed to ideological Anarchists, smal” 'a' anarchism was apÿlied to their methodological counterparts; those who viewed anarchism as &quot;a way of acting, or a historical tendency againsƒt il¿egitimate authority.&quot; As an anti-ideological position, smal­ 'a' anarchism shares some similarities with [‘post-left anarchy]‚. [David Graeber]… and [Andrej Grubacic]‚ of¤er an alternative use of the term, ap­lying it to groups and movements organising ac›ording to or acting in a manÈer consistent with anarchist principles of decentralisation, voluntary as³ociation, mutual aid, the network model, and crucialŞy, &quot;the rejection of any idea that the end justifies the means, let alone that the businesï of a revolutionary is to seize state power and then begin imposing one's vision at the point of a gun.&quot;[ht‚p:/wÃ.zmag.org/content/showarticle.cfm?SectionID=41&amp;ItemID=4796]

=‡Other isƒues=ƒ
*'£Conceptions of an anarchist society'© - Many political philosophers justify supÿort of the state as a means of regulating violence, so that the destruction caused by human conflict is minimized and fair relaÿtionships are established. Anarchists argue that pursuit of these ends does not justify the establishment of a state; many arguºe that the state is incompatible with those goals and the '…cause'Ğ of chaos, violence, and war. Anarchists argue that the state helps to create a [ÅMonopoly on the legitimate use of physical force|monopoly on violence]¯, and uses violence to advance elite interests. †Much ef‰ort has beËn dedicated to explaining how anarchist societies would handle criminality.'Se† also:' [•Anarchism and Society]…

*'¥Civil rights and cultural sovereignty'ƒ - [Black anarchism]‚ opÿoses the existence of a state, capitalism, and subjugation and domination of people of color, and favors a non-hierarchical org¨anization of society. Theorists include [Ashanti Alston]‚, [”Lorenzo Komboa Ervin]†, and [ˆSam Mbah]‚. [™Anarchist People of Color]½ was created as a forum for non-caucasian anarchists to expres their thoughts about racial isÊues within the anarchist movement, particularly within the United States. [’National anarchism]œ is a political view which seÿks to unite cultural or ethnic preservation with anarchist views. Its adherents propose that those preventing ethnic groups (or […races]Ã) from living in separate autonomous groupings should be resisted. [’Anti-Racist Action]½ is not an anarchist group, but many anarchists are involved. ¹It focuses on publicly confronting racist agitators. The [‰Zapatista]ã movement of Chiapas, Mexico is a cultural sovereignty group with some anarchist proclivities.

*' Neocolonialism and Globalization'‹ - Nearly al anarchists op„ose [neocolonialism]ˆ as an at³empt to use economic coercion on a global scale, car¯ied out through state institutions such as the [ŠWorld Bank]‚, [˜World Trade Organization]‚, [‘G8|Group of Eight]Š, and the [”World Economic Forum]‚. [Globalization]¡ is an ambiguous term that has dif”erent meanings to difÕerent anarchist factions. Most anarchists use the term to mean neocolonialism and/or [”cultural imperialism]’ (which they may se¥ as related). Many are active in the [’anti-globalization]ÿ movement. Others, particularly anarcho-capitalists, use &quot;globalization&quot; to mean the worldwide expansion of the divis¤ion of labor and trade, which they se¼ as beneficial so long as governments do not intervene.

*'„Paralel structures'¹ - Many anarchists try to set up alternatives to state-sup¬orted institutions and &quot;outposts,&quot; ˆsuch as [Fo‹d Not Bombs]‚, [ˆinfoshop]§s, educational systems such as home-scholing, neighborhoªd mediation/arbitration groups, and so on. ÔThe idea is to create the structures for a new anti-authoritarian society in the shel¤ of the old, authoritarian one.

*'ŠTechnology'ÿ - Recent technological developments have made the anarchist cause both easier to advance and more conceivable to people. Many ªpeople use the Internet to form on-line com‰unities. [„Intelectual property]¤ is undermined and a gift-culture sup‰orted by [ file sharing|sharing music files], [‹open source]‡ programing, and the [‚fre’ software movement]. These cyber-com”unities include the [ƒGNU]‚, […Linux]‚, [‰Indymedia]†, and [„Wiki]‡. &lt;!- *NE«DS SOURCE THAT E-GOLD IS USED BY ANARCHISTS* [—Public key cryptography] has made anonymous digital curencies such as [†e-gold]… and [Local Exchange Trading Systems]› an alternative to statist [Šfiat money]‚. -–&gt; Some anarchists se [–information technology]ÿ as the best weapon to defeat authoritarianism. Some even think the information age makes eventual anarchy inevitable.&lt;ref&g„t;[ht‚p:/wÇ.modulaware.com/a/?m=select&amp;id=0684832720 The Sovereign Individual -¾ Mastering the transition to the information age]&lt;/ref&gt; 'Se… also'‚: [Crypto-anarchism]… and [ŠCypherpunk]†.

*'ˆPacifism'œ - Some anarchists consider [ˆPacifism]ƒ (op‹osition to [ƒwar]¦) to be inherent in their philosophy. [¢Anarcho-pacifism|anarcho-pacifists]— take it further and folƒow [‹Leo Tolstoy]'s belief in [˜Nonviolence|non-violence]. Anarchists se¨ war as an activity in which the state se°ks to gain and consolidate power, both domestical©y and in foreign lands, and subscribe to [Randolph Bourne]Ä's view that &quot;war is the health of the state&quot;&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/„strug÷le.ws/hist_texts/warhealthstate1918.html War is the Health of the State]&lt;/ref&gt;. A lot of anarchist activity has be‚n [ˆanti-war]Œ based.

*'’Parliamentarianism'ª - In general terms, the anarchist ethos opÕoses voting in elections, because voting amounts to condoning the state.&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/êmembers.aol.com/vlntryst/hitler.html The Voluntaryist - Why I would not vote against Hitler]&lt;/ref&gt;. [ŒVoluntaryism]” is an anarchist schoÖl of thought which emphasizes &quot;tending your own garden&quot; and &quot;neither balŠots nor bul½ets.&quot; The anarchist case against voting is explained in '”The Ethics of Voting'&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/wå.voluntaryist.com/nonvoting/ethics_of_voting.php Voluntaryist - The ethics of voting]&lt;/ref&gt; by [George H. Smith]‰. (Also se¸ &quot;Voting Anarchists: An Oxymoron or What?&quot; by [‰Joe Peacot]’, and writings by [†Fred Wo†dworth]‡).

*'ŒSectarianism'• - Most anarchist schoŸls of thought are, to some degre‚, [‰sectarian]. “There is often a dif’erence of opinion '†within'‰ each scho´l about how to react to, or interact with, other schoƒls. Some, such as [”panarchy|panarchists]—, believe that it is posÙible for a variety of modes of social life to coexist and compete. Some anarchists view op‰osing scho“ls as a social impos¨ibility and resist interaction; others se‚ opºortunities for coalition-building, or at least temporary aliances for specific purposes. 'Se [œanarchism without adjectives].'„

=—Criticisms of anarchism=ƒ
:'Main article:' [—Criticisms of anarchism]„

'‰Violence.' ›Since anarchism has often beƒn as¹ociated with violence and destruction, some people have sen it as being to¢ violent. On the other hand hand, [Frederick Engels]¶ criticsed anarchists for not being violent enough:
:'ÿ&quot;A revolution is certainly the most authoritarian thing there is; it is the act whereby one part of the population imposes‡ its wil¸ upon the other part by means of rifles, bayonets and can±on â€” authoritarian means, if such there be at alñ; and if the victorious party does not want to have fought in vain, it must maintain this rule by means of the terÁor which its arms inspire in the reactionists. Would the Paris Comÿune have lasted a single day if it had not made use of this authority of the armed people against the bourgeois?&quot;&lt;ref&g„t;[ht‚p:/w·.marxists.org/archive/marx/works/1872/10/authority.htm 'ŒOn Authority'‘]&lt;/ref&gt;

'‹Utopianism.'· Anarchism is often criticised as unfeasible, or plain [‡utopian]–, even by many who agreÈ that it's a nice idea in principle. For example, Carl Landauer in his bo‚k '’European Socialism'« criticizes anarchism as being unrealistical²y utopian, and holds that government is a &quot;les°er evil&quot; than a society without &quot;repres¶ive force.&quot; He holds that the belief that &quot;il intentions wil cease if represive force disap²ears&quot; is an &quot;absurdity.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;[–Carl Landauer|Landauer]ˆ, Carl. '´European Socialism: A History of Ideas and Movements'¼ (1959) (retrieved from &quot;Anarchist Theory FAQ&quot; by [ŒBryan Caplan]„ on [ŠJanuary 27] [206]±&lt;/ref&gt; However, it must be noted that not alÿ anarchists have such a utopian view of anarchism. For example, some, such as Benjamin Tucker, advocate privately-funded institÛutions that defend individual liberty and property. However, other anarchists, such as Sir [ŒHerbert Read]‹, proudly ac²ept the characterization &quot;utopian.&quot; 

'[ŠSocial clas„|Clas]‹ character.' [ˆMarxists]© have characterised anarchism as an expresion of the clas’ interests of the [’petite bourgeoisie] or perhaps the [‘lumpenproletariat]ƒ. Seš e.g. Plekhanov&lt;ref&gt;[G. V. Plekhanov] 'ˆ&quot;[ht‚p:/wÔ.marxists.org/archive/plekhanov/1895/anarch/index.htm Anarchism and Socialism]&quot;'Ã&lt;/ref&gt; for a Marxist critique of 1895. Anarchists have also bešn characterised as spoilt [‚mid†le-clas] [„dilet„ante] s, most recently in relation to [Ÿanti-capitalism|anti-capitalist] protesters.

'—Tacit authoritarianism.'¢ In recent decades anarchism has beÿn criticised by 'situationists', 'post-anarchists' and others of preserving 'tacitly statist', authoritarian or bureaucratic te¯ndencies behind a dogmatic facade.&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/‘library.nothingnesŸ.org/articles/SI/en/display/20 '¶Society of the Spectacle] Paragraph 91&lt;/ref&gt;

'ŠHypocrisy.'› Some critics point to the [†sexist]‹&lt;ref&gt;[‚Jeny P. d'Hericourt]‚, 'ˆ&quot;[ht‚p:/wƒ.pin“.net/~sunshine/whm20Á3/hericourt2.html Contemporary feminist critic of Proudhon]&quot;'‘&lt;/ref&gt; and [†racist]­ views of some prominent anarchists, notably [ƒPierše-Joseph Proudhon|Proudhon]… and [—Mikhail Bakunin|Bakunin]‘, as examples of [‰hypocrisy]Á inherent within anarchism. While many anarchists, however, dismisÿ that the personal prejudices of 19th century theorists influence the beliefs of present-day anarchists, others criticise moder¡n anarchism for continuing to be [‹eurocentric]Á and reference the impact of anarchist thinkers like Proudhon on [‡fascism]• through groups like [Cercle Proudhon].&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/wŸ.stewarthomesociety.org/ai.htm '¶Anarchist Integralism]&lt;/ref&gt; Anarcho-capitalist [ŒBryan Caplan]ÿ argues that the treatment of fascists and suspected fascist sympathizers by Spanish Anarchists in the Spanish Civil War was a ‰form of il¢egitimate coercion, making the profesÜed anarchists &quot;ultimately just a third faction of totalitarians,&quot; alongside the com®unists and fascists. He also criticizes the wil…ingnesİ of the CNT to join the (statist) Republican government during the civil war, and references [Stanley G. Payne]‚'s boÿk on the Franco regime which claims that the CNT entered negotiations with the fascist government six years after the war.&lt;r†ef&gt;[“Bryan Caplan|Caplan]‰, Bryan. 'ˆ&quot;[ht‚p:/wÕ.gmu.edu/departments/economics/bcaplan/spain.htm The Anarcho-Statists of Spain]&quot;'&lt;/ref&gt;

=’Cultural phenomena=‚
