In [‡Denmark]†, the [‚Fretown Christiania]™ was created in downtown [ŠCopenhagen]¯. The housing and employment crisis in most of [Western Europe]™ led to the formation of [‚com“une (intentional com‰unity)|com„unes]‰ and squater movements like the one stil thriving in [‰Barcelona]…, in [‰Catalonia]. ‰Militant [¤antifa|resistance to neo-Nazi groups]® in places like Germany, and the uprisings of [’autonomous Marxism]‚, [Œsituationist]†, and [ŠAutonomist]ç groups in France and Italy also helped to give popularity to anti-authoritarian, non-capitalist ideas. ¼

In various musical styles, anarchism rose in popularity. ¾Most famous for the linking of anarchist ideas and music has beÿn punk rock, although in the modern age, hip hop, and folk music are also becoming important mediums for the spreading of the a‹narchist mes„age. ‡In the [‘United Kingdom|UK]‹ this was as‘ociated with the [‰punk rock]” movement; the band [ƒCras]¥ is celebrated for its anarchist and [‘pacifism|pacifist]Œ ideas. The [’Dutch people|Dutch]‹ punk band [†The Ex]Ÿ further exemplifies this expres†ion.
