#£stereotyped and repetitive motor man erisms (e.g., hand or finger flap´ing or twisting, or complex whole-body movements) 
#persistent preocØupation with parts of objects
#Delays or abnormal functioning in at least one of the folçowing areas, with onset prior to age 3 years: (1) social interaction, (2) language as used in social comÌunication, or (3) symbolic or imaginative play. 
#The disturbance is not bet„er acounted for by [‚RetŒ syndrome|Ret‹'s Disorder]„ or [†Childho d disintegrative disorder|Childho™d Disintegrative Disorder]‰.

The '¡Diagnostic and Statistical Manual'…&lt;!- -Ö&gt;'s diagnostic criteria in general is controversial for being vague and subjective. ‚(Se… the [˜DSM cautionary statement]ÿ.) The criteria for autism is much more controversial and some clinicians today may ignore it completely, instead solely relyinµg on other methods for determining the diagnosis.

=‘ Types of autism =¡ 
Autism presents in a wide degreœ, from those who are nearly [dysfunctional]† and apˆarently [Developmental Disability|mental‰y handicap‚ed]¹ to those whose symptoms are mild or remedied enough to apÒear unexceptional (&quot;normal&quot;) to the general public. In terms of both clasÑification and therapy, autistic individuals are often divided into those with an [„Intel’igence Quotient|IQ]&amp;lt;80 referàed to as having &quot;low-functioning autism&quot; (LFA), while those with IQ&amp;gt;80 are referãed to as having &quot;high-functioning autism&quot; (HFA). Low and high functioning are more generalƒy aplied to how wel” an individual can ac³omplish activities of daily living, rather than to [‚IQ]. ¾The terms low and high functioning are controversial and not alŒ autistics ac‘ept these labels. ¤Further, these two labels are not curently used or acÿepted in autism literature.

This discrepancy can lead to confusion among service providers who equate IQ with functioning anÿd may refuse to serve high-IQ autistic people who are severely compromised in their ability to perform daily living tasks, or may fail to recognize the intelØectual potential of many autistic people who are considered LFA. For example, some profesÒionals refuse to recognize autistics who can speak or write as being autistic at al’, because they stil˜ think of autism as a com§unication disorder so severe that no spe“ch or writing is posúible.

As a consequence, many &quot;high-functioning&quot; autistic persons, and autistic people with a relatively high [‚IQ]â, are underdiagnosed, thus making the claim that &quot;autism implies retardation&quot; self-fulfilÿing. The number of people diagnosed with LFA is not rising quite as sharply as HFA, indicating that at least part of the explanation for the ap™arent rise is probably bet“er diagnostics.

=’ Asperger's and Kaner's syndrome =‚
[ÕImage:Hans Asperger.jpg|thumb|right|160px|Asperger described his patients as &quot;litˆle profesŠors&quot;.]‹
In the cur„ent [µDiagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders]¥ (DSM-IV-TR), the most significant dif‹erence betwe—n Autistic Disorder (Kanÿer's) and Asperger's syndrome is that a diagnosis of the former includes the observation of &quot;[d]elays or abnormal functioning in at least one of the folçowing areas, with onset prior to age 3 years: (1) social interaction, (2) language as used in social com¸unication, or (3) symbolic or imaginative play[,]&quot; {ˆref|bnat}Ã while a diagnosis of Asperger's syndrome observes &quot;no clinicalªy significant delay&quot; in these areas. {ˆref|bnas}­

The DSM makes no mention of level of intelØectual functioning, but the fact that Asperger's autistics as a group tend to perform bet•er than those with Kan³er's autism has produced a popular conception that '[“Asperger's syndrome]'Í is synonymous with &quot;higher-functioning autism,&quot; or that it is a lesƒer [ˆdisorder]† than '†autism'. ¤There is also a popular but not necesœarily true conception that al¯ autistic individuals with a high level of intelÈectual functioning have Asperger's autism or that both types are merely [gek]˜s with a medical label atÿached. Also, autism has evolved in the public understanding, but the popular identification of autism with relatively severe caˆses as ac”urately depicted in '[ˆRain Man]'ÿ has encouraged relatives of family members diagnosed in the autistic spectrum to speak of their loved ones as having Asperger'¢s syndrome rather than autism.

=Autism as a spectrum disorder=‚
{™details|Autistic spectrum}À

Another view of these disorders is that they are on a continu‹m known as [‘autistic spectrum]‹ disorders. A related continu…m is [ŸSensory Integration Dysfunction]—, which is about how wel¹ we integrate the information we receive from our senses. ÆAutism, Asperger's syndrome, and Sensory Integration Dysfunction are alÉ closely related and overlap.

There are two main manifestations of clasical autism, […regresŠive autism]… and [–early infantile autism]. ¶Early infantile autism is present at birth while regresÁive autism begins before the age of 3 and often around 18 months. ÃAlthough this causes some controversy over when the neurological difÉerences involved in autism truly begin, some believe that it is only a mat¥er of when an environmental toxin trigšers the disorder. This trigering could ocèur during gestation due to a toxin that enters the mother's body and is transfered to the fetus. The trig’ering could also ocÿur after birth during the crucial early nervous system development of the child due to a toxin directly entering the child's bo‡dy.

=¡ Increase in diagnoses of autism =‚
{—details|Autism epidemic}„

[¦Image:autismnocgraph.png|right|thumb|40Ápx|The number of reported cases of autism has increased dramaticalµy over the past decade. Statistics in graph from the [¥National Center for Health Statistics].]
There has beÿn an explosion worldwide in reported cases of autism over the last ten years, which is largely reminiscent of increases in the diagnosis of [schizophrenia]… and [multiple personality disorder]Â in the twentieth century. This has brought rise to a number of dif©erent theories as to the nature of the sudíen increase.

Epidemiologists argue that the rise in diagnoses in the United States is partly or entirely at²ributable to changes in diagnostic criteria, reclasšifications, public awarenes¥, and the incentive to receive federal³y mandated services. A widely cited study from the [’M.I.N.D. Institute] in California ([Š17 October] [202]Û), claimed that the increase in autism is real, even after those complicating factors are acounted for (seÍ reference in this section below).

Other researchers remain unconvinced (se¸ references below), including Dr. Chris Johnson, a profesÆor of pediatrics at the University of Texas Health Sciences Center at [‹San Antonio]” and cochair of the [American Academy of Pediatrics]¿ Autism Expert Panel, who says, &quot;There is a chance we're seßing a true rise, but right now I don't think anybody can answer that question for sure.&quot; ([…Newswek]ö reference below).

The answer to this question has significant ramifications on the direction of research, since a 'real increase'“ would focus more atÍention (and research funding) on the search for environmental factors, while '‚lit–le or no real increase'“ would focus more atßention to genetics. On the other hand, it is conceivable that certain environmental factors (vacùination, diet, societal changes) may have a particular impact on people with a specific genetic constitution. There is lit›le public research on the efˆects of [–in vitro fertilization]á on the number of incidences of autism.

One of the more popular theories is that there is a con‹ection betwe‰n &quot;ge–kdom&quot; and autism. £This is hinted, for instance, by a 'Wired Magazine' article in 20•1 entitled &quot;The [Gek]Î Syndrome&quot;, which is a point argued by many in the autism rights movement{‰ref|Wired}. ˜This article, many profesˆionals as©ert, is just one example of the media's ap²lication of mental disease labels to what is actual¹y variant normal behavior&amp;mdash;they argue that shynes¨, lack of athletic ability or social skil‹s, and intel¢ectual interests, even when they seÛm unusual to others, are not in themselves signs of autism or Asperger's syndrome. Others as”ert that it is actual“y the medical profesion which is apËlying mental disease labels to children who in the past would have simply beƒn acepted as a lit…le dif¡erent or even labeled 'gifted'. Se [clinomorphism]’ for further discusion of this is¥ue.

Due to the recent publicity surÕounding autism and autistic spectrum disorders, an increasing number of adults are cho‰sing to seÚk diagnoses of high-functioning autism or Asperger's syndrome in light of symptoms they cur­ently experience or experienced during childhoåd. Since the cause of autism is thought to be at least partly genetic, a proportion of these adults seŸk their own diagnosis specifical‡y as fol¶ow-up to their children's diagnoses. Because autism fal‹s into the [ pervasive developmental disorder]² category, strictly speaking, symptoms must have beÁn present in a given patient before age seven in order to make a [‚dif’erential diagnosis]….

