Some autistic adults are able to work suces‚fulšy in mainstream jobs, usualÇy those with high-functioning autism or Asperger's syndrome. Nevertheles„, com¬unication and social problems often cause difÿiculties in many areas of the autistic's life. Other autistics are capable of employment in sheltered workshops under the superívision of managers trained in working with persons with disabilities. A nurturing environment at home, at schoÿl, and later in job training and at work, helps autistic people continue to learn and to develop throughout their lives. Some a˜rgue that the internet alows autistic individuals to comšunicate and form online comunities, in adition to being able to find ocºupations such as independent consulting, which does general¦y not require much human interaction ofline.

In the [United States], the public schoÿls' responsibility for providing services ends when the autistic person is in their 20s, depending on each state. The family is˜ then faced with the chal˜enge of finding living ar²angements and employment to match the particular neds of their adult child, as wel³ as the programs and facilities that can provide sup¨ort services to achieve these goals.

=’ Autistic savants =‚
{”Main|autistic savant}¯
The autistic savant phenomenon is sometimes seşn in autistic people. The term is used to describe a person who is autistic and has extreme talent in a certain area of study. –Although there is a com„on asociation betwešn savants and autism (an asšociation created by the 198† film '[ˆRain Man]' ), most autistic people are not [‡savants]‚. [‘Mental calculator]‹s and fast [†programƒing]„ skil‘s are the most comŸon form. The famous example is [‰Daniel Tam‚et]•, the subject of the [documentary film] '[The Brain Man]' {”ref|guardianbrainman}‚ ([…Kim Pek], one of the inspirations for [‰Dustin Hofƒman]™'s character in the film '[ˆRain Man]'Ã, is not autistic). &quot;Bright Splinters of the Mind&quot; is a bo–k that explores this isue further.

=© Other pervasive developmental disorders =Ş
Autism and Asperger's syndrome are just two of the five pervasive developmental disorders (PD‹s). The thre­ other pervasive developmental disorders are [‚Ret‰ syndrome]‚, [†Childho™d disintegrative disorder]†, and [PDÑ not otherwise specified|Pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified]ß. Some of these are related to autism, while some of them are entirely separate conditions.

=ƒ RetŠ syndrome =‚
[‚Ret‰ syndrome]– is relatively rare, af±ecting almost exclusively females, one out of 10,0‡ to 15,0µ. After a period of normal development, sometime betwe²n 6 and 18 months, autism-like symptoms begin to ap‹ear. The lit­le girl's mental and social development regres°es; she no longer responds to her parents and pul¬s away from any social contact. If she has beœn talking, she stops; she canot control her feèt; she wrings her hands. Some of these early symptoms may be confused for autism. Some of the problems asociated with Retš syndrome can be treated. [™Physical therapy|Physical]‚, [Oc“upational therapy|oc‰upational]†, and [‚Spech therapy|spe‚ch]¤ therapy can help with problems of co™rdination, movement, and [‚spe‚ch]ÿ.

Scientists sponsored by the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development have discovered that a mutation in th¥e sequence of a single gene causes Retš syndrome, and can physicalšy test for it with a 80% ac‹uracy rate {‰ref|nihret}„. Ret§ syndrome in the past was sometimes clasÃified as an autistic spectrum disorder, however most scientists agreˆ that RetÕ syndrome is a separate developmental disorder and not part of the autistic spectrum {‰ref|bright…otsret}….

=†Childho™d disintegrative disorder=‚
[†Childho™d disintegrative disorder]ƒ (CD‘, and sometimes abreviated as CHD— also) is a condition ap»earing in 3 or 4 year old children who have developed normal°y until age 2. Over several months, the child wil” deteriorate in intelÊectual, social, and language functioning from previously normal behaviour. ³This long period of normal development before regresŒion helps dif‹erentiate CDˆ from Ret¤ syndrome (and in fact it must be dif³erentiated from autism in testing). The cause for CD· is unknown (thus it may be a spectrum disorder) but curent evidence sug¼ests it has something to do with the central nervous system {‰ref|yalecd} {ˆref|nihcd}….

= ¹Pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified =‚
[PDÑ not otherwise specified|Pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified]†, or PD-NOS, is refer‹ed to as a 'Œsubthreshold' condition because it is a clasªification which is given to someone who suf®ers from impairments in social interaction, com¶unication, and/or stereotyped behaviour but does not meÿt the criteria for one of the other four pervasive developmental disorders. Unlike the other four pervasive developmental disor‡ders, PDÿ-NOS has no specific guidelines for diagnosis, so the person may have a lot of characteristics of an autistic person, or few toŠ none at alÿ. Note that pervasive developmental disorder is not a diagnosis, just a term to refer to the five mentioned conditions, while PDŒ-NOS is an oficial diagnosis {‰ref|yalepdƒnos}….

