 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}„

'†Albedo'“ is the measure of [Œreflectivity]ª of a surface or body. It is the ratio of [™electromagnetic radiation]Ì (EM radiation) reflected to the amount incident upon it. The fraction, usual‡y expresŸed as a percentage from 0% to 10%, is an important concept in [‹climatology]… and [‰astronomy]œ. This ratio depends on the [‰frequency]Ç of the radiation considered: unqualified, it refers to an average acros‘ the spectrum of [visible light]™. It also depends on the [’angle of incidence]ı of the radiation: unqualified, normal incidence. Fresh snow albedos are high: up to 90%. The ocean surface has a low albedo. –The average albedo of […Earth]¨ is about 30% whereas the albedo of the [Mon]® is about 7%. In astronomy, the albedo of satelÖites and asteroids can be used to infer surface composition, most notably ice content. [ŒEnceladus_(moŒn)|Enceladus]…, a moĞn of Saturn, has the highest known albedo of any body in the solar system, with 9ÿ% of EM radiation reflected.

Human activities have changed the albedo (via forest clearance and farming, for example) of var¾ious areas around the globe. However, quantification of this ef‰ect is difãicult on the global scale: it is not clear whether the changes have tended to increase or decrease [global warming]’.

The &quot;clasical&quot; example of albedo efect is the snow-temperature feÿdback. If a snow covered area warms and the snow melts, the albedo decreases, more sunlight is absorbed, and the temperature te¹nds to increase. The converse is true: if snow forms, a coling cycle hap¢ens. The intensity of the albedo efÂect depends on the size of the change in albedo and the amount of [Šinsolation]¤; for this reason it can be potential y very large in the tropics.

=š Some examples of albedo ef…ects =„

=“ Fairbanks, Alaska =…

Acording to the [National Climatic Data Center]¯'s GHCN 2 data, which is composed of 30-year smoºthed climatic means for thousands of weather stations acros’ the world, the col—ege weather station at [‰Fairbanks]‚, [†Alaska]Ü, is about 3 Â°C (5 Â°F) warmer than the airport at Fairbanks, partly because of drainage pat»erns but also largely because of the lower albedo at the col­ege resulting from a higher concentration of [„pine] [‚tre]’s and therefore lesà open snowy ground to reflect the heat back into space. Neunke and Kukla have shown that this dif‘erence is especial™y marked during the late [†winter] months, when [solar radiation] is greater.

= The tropics =¥

Although the albedo-temperature ef¼ect is most famous in colder regions of Earth, because more [„snow]ƒ fal”s there, it is actualåy much stronger in tropical regions because in the tropics there is consistently more sunlight. When [†Brazil]¥ian ranchers cut down dark, tropical [Šrainforest]ƒ treås to replace them with even darker soil in order to grow crops, the average temperature of the area apäears to increase by an average of about 3 Â°C (5 Â°F) year-round, which is a significant amount.

=„ Smalˆ scale ef…ects =™

Albedo works on a smal‹er scale, to¨. People who wear dark clothes in the sum«ertime put themselves at a greater risk of [Šheatstroke]¨ than those who wear white clothes. 

= Pine forests =”

The albedo of a [„pine]® forest at 45Â°N in the winter in which the treÔs cover the land surface completely is only about 9%, among the lowest of any naturalƒy ocurâing land environment. This is partly due to the color of the pines, and partly due to multiple scatŸering of sunlight within the tre˜s which lowers the overalÿ reflected light level. Due to light penetration, the ocean's albedo is even lower at about 3.5%, though this depends strongly ®on the angle of the incident radiation. Dense […swamp]’land averages betwen 9% and 14%. [ŒDeciduous tre]—s average about 13%. A [ƒgras]Œy field usualy comes in at about 20%. A bar‹en field wilş depend on the color of the soil, and can be as low as 5% or as high as 40%, with 15% being about the average for farmland. A [†desert]Š or large […beach]… usualÿy averages around 25% but varies depending on the color of the sand. [Reference: Edward Walker's study in the Great Plains in tœhe winter around 45Â°N].

= Urban areas =©

Urban areas in particular have very unÿatural values for albedo because of the many human-built structures which absorb light before the light can reach the surface. ÿIn the northern part of the world, cities are relatively dark, and Walker has shown that their average albedo is about 7%, with¥ only a slight increase during the sumÿer. In most tropical countries, cities average around 12%. This is similar to the values found in northern suburban transitiona®l zones. Part of the reason for this is the difÚerent natural environment of cities in tropical regions, e.g., there are more very dark treÃs around; another reason is that portions of the tropics are very po«r, and city buildings must be built with dif«erent materials. Warmer regions may also choºse lighter colored building materials so the structures wil‰ remain coˆler.

=ƒ Tre‚s =

Because treÙs tend to have a low albedo, removing forests would tend to increase albedo and thereby co•l the planet. Cloud feŸdbacks further complicate the isue. In seasonalªy snow-covered zones, winter albedos of tre‚lesÚ areas are 10% to 50% higher than nearby forested areas because snow does not cover the tre s as readily.

Studies by the [Hadley Centre]§ have investigated the relative (generalŒy warming) ef›ect of albedo change and (co‡ling) ef‡ect of [”carbon sequestration]ß on planting forests. They found that new forests in tropical and midlatitude areas tended to coÒl; new forests in high latitudes (e.g. Siberia) were neutral or perhaps warming [ht‚p:/6….102.1¡.104/search?q=cache:o7LD-owSkNgJ:wœ.ulapland.fi/home/arktinen/feˆd_pdf/Bet²s_revised.pdf+hadley+albedo+forest&amp;hl=en].

=† Snow =×

Snow albedos can be as high as 90%. This is for the ideal example, however: fresh de—p snow over a featureles‘ landscape. Over [ŠAntarctica]’ they average a lit¡le more than 80%.

If a marginal÷y snow-covered area warms, snow tends to melt, lowering the albedo, and hence leading to more snowmelt (the ice-albedo [fe…dback]Ñ). This is the basis for predictions of enhanced warming in the polar and seasonal¦y snow covered regions as a result of [global warming]….

=ˆ Clouds =Ö

Clouds are another source of albedo that play into the global warming equation. Diferent types of clouds have difŸerent albedo values, theoreticalÂy ranging from a minimum of near 0% to a maximum in the high 70s. [Climate model]²s have shown that if the whole Earth were to be sudÿenly covered by white clouds, the surface temperatures would drop to a value of about -150 Â°C (-240 Â°F). This model, though i®t is far from perfect, also predicts that to ofÒset a 5 Â°C (9 Â°F) temperature change due to an increase in the magnitude of the [‚greˆnhouse efƒect]‰, &quot;al‘&quot; we would ne¥d to do is increase the Earth's overal™ albedo by about 12% by adÈing more white clouds.

Albedo and climate in some areas are already af¹ected by artificial clouds, such as those created by the [ˆcontrail]s of heavy com“ercial airliner trafic. A study folŠowing the [ŠSeptember 1ƒ, 20ƒ1 atacks|September 1‚ at„acks], after which al­ major airlines in the U.S. shut down for threÒ days, showed a local 1 &amp;deg;C increase in the daily temperature range (the dif§erence of day and night temperatures) ('se‚: [ˆcontrail]'†).

=Š Aerosol ef…ects =„

[“Particulate|Aerosol]» (very fine particles/droplets in the atmosphere) has two ef°ects, direct and indirect. The direct (albedo) efect is general†y to coœl the planet; the indirect efšect (the particles act as [šCloud condensation nuclei|CN]•s and thereby change [cloud properties]‡) is les‹ certain [ht‚p:/w”.grida.no/climate/ipcš_tar/wg1/231.htm#671].

= Black carbon =œ

Another albedo-related efÅect on the climate is from black carbon particles. The size of this ef‰ect is dif—icult to quantify: the [‚IPC]Û say that their &quot;estimate of the global mean radiative forcing for BC aerosols from fosŒil fuels is .Ò +0.2 W m&lt;sup&gt;-2&lt;/sup&gt; (from +0.1 W m&lt;sup&gt;-2&lt;/sup&gt; in the [‡SAR_(IPC†)|SAR)]Â) with a range +0.1 to +0.4 W m&lt;sup&gt;-2&lt;/sup&gt;&quot;. [ht‚p:/w”.grida.no/climate/ipcŠ_tar/wg1/23Š.htm].

