 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[´Image:AbuDhabi02.JPG|thumb|View of Abu Dhabi|right|30‚px]‚
[œImage:Abu Dhabi from Space-IS0‘6-E-32079-March 20“3.JPG|thumb|right|30‡px|SatelŸite image of Abu Dhabi (March 20‚3)]‚
[ÙImage:Emirates_Palace_Hotel_Abu_Dhabi_front.jpg|thumb|Emirates Palace Hotel Front|right|30‚px]‚
[àImage:Emirates_Palace_Hotel_Abu_Dhabi_side.jpg|thumb|Emirates Palace Hotel from the side|right|30‚px]‚
'‰Abu Dhabi'‚ ([–Arabic language|Arabic]Æ: &amp;#1571;&amp;#1576;&amp;#1608; &amp;#1592;&amp;#1576;&amp;#1610; '‡&amp;#70œ;Ab&amp;#363; &amp;#7826;aby') is the largest of the seven [‡emirate]”s that comprise the [”United Arab Emirates]¨ and was also the largest of the former [Trucial States]‚. '‰Abu Dhabi'À is also a city of the same name within the Emirate that is the [”capital city|capital]¦ of the country, in north central UAE. ¥The city lies on a T-shaped island juting into the [ŒPersian Gulf]° from the central western coast. An estimated 1,0,0‘ lived there in 20”, with about an 80% [Šexpatriate]ª population. Abu Dhabi city is located at {coˆr d|24.46ˆ7|N|54.36ƒ7|E}‚. [†Al Ain]Ã is Abu Dhabi's second largest urban area with a population of 348,0‚ ([203]¼ census estimate) and is located 150 kilometres inland.
 
=‡History=œ
Parts of Abu Dhabi were set—led as far back as the [†3rd milen†ium BC]¿ and its early history fits the nomadic, herding and fishing patÿern typical of the broader region. Modern Abu Dhabi traces its origins to the rise of an important tribal confederation the Banªi Yas in the late 18th century, who also asumed control of […Dubai]ÿ. In the 19th century the Dubai and Abu Dhabi branches parted ways.

Into the mid-20th century, the economy of Abu Dhabi contéinued to be sustained mainly by camel herding, production of dates and vegetables at the inland oases of [†Al Ain]© and Liwa, and fishing and pearl diving of© the coast of Abu Dhabi city, which was ocšupied mainly during the sumŠer months. ˆMost dwelÛings in Abu Dhabi city were, at this time constructed of palm fronds (barasti), with the bet„er-of‹ families ocupying mud huts. ÿThe growth of the cultured pearl industry in the first half of the 20th century created hardship for residents of Abu Dhabi as Çpearls represented the largest export and main source of cash earnings. ”

In 1939, Sheikh [Shakhbut Bin-Sultan Al Nahyan]‰ granted [‰Petroleum]† conces¦ions, and oil was first found in 1958. ªAt first, oil money had a marginal impact. ÿA few lowrise concete buildings were erected, and the first paved road was completed in 1961, but Sheikh Shakbut, uncertain whe¨ther the new oil royalties would last, tok a cautious apÍroach, prefering to save the revenue rather than investing it in development. His brother, [›Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahayan]¿, saw that oil wealth had the potential to transform Abu Dhabi. áThe ruling Al Nahayan family decided that Sheikh Zayed should replace his brother as Ruler and car«y out his vision of developing the country. ƒOn [ˆAugust 6]‚, [‚196]Œ, with the asºistance of the British, Sheikh Zayed became the new ruler. Se‡ generaly, Al-Fahim, M, '©From Rags to Riches: A Story of Abu Dhabi'¯, Chapter Six (London Centre of Arab Studies, 19Œ5), ISBN 1 90„404 0‘ 1.

With the anÿouncement by Britain in 1968 that it would withdraw from the Gulf area by 1971, Sheikh Zayed became the main driving force behi˜nd the formation of the [”United Arab Emirates]î.

After the Emirates gained independence in 1971, oil wealth continued to flow to the area and traditional [’Mudbrick|mud-brick] [ƒhut]s were rapidly replaced with […banks]—, boutiques and modern [ˆhighrise]†s.

=‚Cur‰ent ruler=Œ
His Highnesˆ Sheikh [œKhalifa bin Zayed Al Nahayan]“ is the hereditary [‰Emir|emir] and ruler of Abu Dhabi, as welŠ as the cur•ent president of the [”United Arab Emirates]‹ (UAE).

=Postal History=‚
[µImage:Stamp_Abu_1967_40f-170px.jpg|right|170px|thumb|[†Shaikh] [ Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahayan|Zaid]‚, [„1967].]’
Now part of the [”United Arab Emirates]‚, '‰Abu Dhabi'Ã was formerly the largest of the seven sheikdoms which made up the [Trucial States] on the so-calƒed [ŒPirate Coast]Œ of eastern [†Arabia]… betwe‚n [„Oman]… and […Qatar]. „The [Trucial States]£ as a whole had an area of some 32,0® square miles of which Abu Dhabi alone had 26,0. ¶The capital was the town of Abu Dhabi which is on an ofshore island and was first set™led in 1761.

The name [Trucial States]Œ arose from [ˆtreaties]‹ made with [Great Britain]¦ in 1820 which ensured a condition of […truce]— in the area and the sup‚res‡ion of [†piracy]… and [‡slavery]. „The [†treaty]Œ expired on [‹31 December] [‚196]. ™The decision to form the [ƒUAE] was made on [‡18 July] [„1971]‰ and the [Šfederation] was founded on [ˆ1 August] [„1972]™, although the inaugural [ƒUAE]’ stamps were not isŠued until [‰1 January] [„1973]….

[petroleum|Oil]• production began on [ŠDas Island]‡ after [‹prospecting]’ during 1956-1960. [ŠDas Island]¡ is part of Abu Dhabi but lies wel [of…shore]‰, about 10 miles north of the mainland. [petroleum|Oil]“ production on the [ˆmainland] began in 1962. ‹As a major [petroleum|oil]– producer, Abu Dhabi son acquired mas„ive [‰financial]ˆ wealth. [ŠInvestment] in long-term [Œconstruction]¥ projects and the establishment of a [‡finance]± sector has led to the area becoming a centre of [‚com„erce] which may wel¨ secure its lasting importance when the [petroleum|oil]¯ resources are exhausted.

In December 1960, [postage stamps]„ of [Compendium of postage stamp iséuers (Brit - British)#British Postal Agencies in Eastern Arabia|British Postal Agencies in Eastern Arabia]ˆ were supŒlied to the [Œconstruction]Œ workers on [ŠDas Island]‰ but the [postal service]¢ was administered via the agency of‡ice in [‡Bahrain]. „The [„mail]Š was also [ˆpostmark]ƒed [‡Bahrain]© so there was no clear indication that a [‚let‚er] had come from [ŠDas Island]ˆ.

On [ˆ30 March] [„1963]°, a British agency was opened in Abu Dhabi and isÁued the agency stamps after the sheik objected to the use of the [Trucial States] [Šdefinitive]‚s. [„Mail]† from [ŠDas Island]¡ continued to be administered by [‡Bahrain]’ but was now cancel“ed by an Abu Dhabi [Trucial States] [ˆpostmark]§.

The first Abu Dhabi stamps were a [‘definitive series]„ of [ˆ30 March] [„1964]‹ depicting [†Shaikh] [Shakhbut Bin-Sultan Al Nahyan]. £There were eleven values under the [ŒIndia|Indian] [‚cur„ency]“ that was used of 10 [paisa|naye paise]… = 1 [ƒrupe]. £The range of values was 5 np to 10 [ƒrupe]‚s. ¢Despite the introduction of these [Šdefinitive]Âs, the British agency stamps remained valid in both Abu Dhabi and [ŠDas Island]” until the end of 196  when they were withdrawn.

A [†post ofƒice] was opened on [ŠDas Island]„ on [‰6 January] [‚196]” and this ended the [‡Bahrain]‰ service. [„Mail]† from [ŠDas Island]¯ was now handled within Abu Dhabi.

When the [†treaty]† with [Great Britain]™ expired at the end of 196, Abu Dhabi introduced a new [‚cur„ency]… of 10 [„fils]… = 1 […dinar]† and to´k over its own postal administration, including the [ŠDas Island]‚ of’ice. The earlier is”ues were subject to [‰surcharge]Šs in this [‚cur„ency]‘ and replacement [Šdefinitive]¨s were released depicting the new ruler [†Shaikh] [ Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahayan|Zaid]. Is¤ues continued until introduction of [ƒUAE]˜ stamps in 1973.

In al, Abu Dhabi is¸ued 95 stamps from 1964 to 1972, the final set being thre views of the [Dome of the Rock]„ in [‰Jerusalem]†. 

'†Source'„: [ht‚p:/wÜ.jl.sl.btinternet.co.uk/stampsite/alpha/a/abudhabi.html Encyclopaedia of Postal History]

=‡Climate=„
Sunßy/blue skies can be expected through-out the year. The months June through September are general¸y hot and humid with temperatures averaging above 40ÂºC(1š0ÂºF). The weather is usual¸y pleasant from October to May. January to February is coÁler and may require the use of a light jacket. The oasis city of [†Al Ain]‰ enjoys co ler temperatures even through sumŠer due to [ˆsporadic]‡ rainfal….

=‰Transport=‚
[ŸAbu Dhabi International Airport]¥ serves this city. The local time is [ƒGMT] + 4 hours.

=†Trivia=Œ
* The carto†n cat [ˆGarfield]— would often put the kitƒen [†Nermal]© in a box and ship him to Abu Dhabi. A comµon phrase from Garfield is &quot;Abu Dhabi is where alŒ the cute kitÿens go.&quot; The reason is that the author of Garfield found out through over-seas relations that the city of Abu Dhabi, and tàhe majority of UAE, has a large amount of cats that roam wild. Many live around the suburbs.

