=…Usage=…
In [˜English language|English]ˆ, the let“er A by itself usualy denotes the [lax open front unrounded vowel]‚ ({‡IPA2|Ã¦}ˆ) as in 'ƒpad'†, the [™open back unrounded vowel]‚ ({‡IPA2|É‘}ˆ) as in '†father'«, or, in concert with a later orthographic [e], the diphthong {ˆIPA2|eÊ²}º (though the pronunciation varies with the dialect) as in 'ƒace'Š, due to efŒects of the [‘Great vowel shift]À.

In most other languages that use the Latin alphabet, the let—er A denotes either an [™open back unrounded vowel]‚ ({‡IPA2|É‘}‰), or an [œopen central unrounded vowel]‚ ({†IPA2|a}). 

In the [ŸInternational Phonetic Alphabet]”, variants of the let”er A denote various […vowel]†s. In [‡X-SAMPA]˜, capital A denotes the [™open back unrounded vowel] and lowercase a denotes the [šopen front unrounded vowel]›.

A also is the English [’indefinite article], extended to [‚an]” before a vowel.

