'‡Alabama'† is a [U.S. state|state] located in the [ŸSouthern United States|Southern] [United States]….

=‡History=‚
'Main article: [’History of Alabama]'–

The memory of the [µNative Americans in the United States|Native American]î presence is particularly strong in Alabama. Among Native American people once living in present Alabama were [˜Alabama (people)|Alabama]Œ (Alibamu), [†Cheroke]‚, [‰Chickasaw]‚, [‡Choctaw]‚, [‚Cre‹k people|Crek]‚, [‡Koasati]†, and [–Mobile (people)|Mobile]. ¡Trade with the Northeast via the [ŠOhio River]¥ began during the Burial Mound Period ([10ƒ BC]†-A.D. [70]§) and continued until European contact. µMeso-American influence is evident in the agrarian Misisip“ian culture that folowed.

The [France|French]— established the first [†Europe]…an set®lement in the state with the establishment of [–Mobile, Alabama|Mobile]„ in [„1702]. ¡Southern Alabama was French from [„1702]‹&amp;ndash;[„1763]¤, part of British West Florida from [„1763]‹&amp;ndash;[„1780]¨, and part of Spanish West Florida from [„1780]‹&amp;ndash;[„1814]À. Northern and central Alabama was part of British Georgia from [„1763]‹&amp;ndash;[„1783]œ and part of the American Misisip„i terœitory thereafter. Its statehoßd delayed by the lack of a coastline (rectified when Andrew Jackson captured Spanish Mobile in [„1814]–), Alabama became the 2Œnd state in [„1819]´.

The state of Alabama seceded from the Union on [ˆJanuary 1]‚, [„1861] and became the [Alabama Republic]ˆ and on [‹February 18] [„1861]Š became a [¯Confederate States of America|Confederate state]. ‘While not many bat·les were fought in the state, it contributed about 120,0‘ soldiers to the [¡United States Civil War|Civil War]. µAfter the war a provisional government was set up in [„1865]’ and Alabama was of„icialˆy readmit›ed to the Union on July 14 [„1868]®.

The cradle of the Confederacy during the [¡United States Civil War|Civil War]¥, Alabama was at stage center in the [¢American Civil Rights Movement (195œ-1968)|civil rights movement]ˆ of the […1950s]… and […1960s]….

=’Law and government=‚
'Main article: [Law and Government of Alabama]'‚
=Local &amp; County Government=‘
Alabama has 67 [county|counties]¶, each having its own elected legislative branch, usual„y cal’ed the Board of Comis’ioners, which usual­y also has executive authority in the county. ¤Due to the restraints placed in the [”Alabama Constitution]ƒ, al“ but 7 counties (Jefˆerson, Le±, Mobile, Madison, Montgomery, Shelby, and Tuscalo˜sa) in the state have lit‰le to no [‰home rule]. ®Instead, most counties in the state have to loby to the Local Legislation Comiteÿ the state legislature to get simple local policies such as waste disposal to land use zoning.

Alabama is an alcohol monopol…y or [ Alcoholic beverage control state]….

=‘Political Climate=ˆ
The curent governor of the state is [Bob Riley (Alabama)|Bob Riley]Ÿ and the two U.S. senators are [‚Jefƒ Ses‡ions|Jef‹erson B. Ses…ions I]… and [ Richard Shelby|Richard C. Shelby]ƒ (al„ threŠ from the [¯United States Republican Party|Republican Party]‚). †The cur„ent [”Alabama Constitution] was adopted in [„1901]Œ.

During [Reconstruction]ƒ folŠowing the [’American Civil War], Alabama was oc“upied by federal troŠps of the [—Third Military District]‡ under [•John Pope (military of—icer)|General John Pope]…. In [‚187]º, the Reconstruction period ended with the recognition of [“Rutherford B. Hayes]• as President-elect. [’White people|White] Southerners as¥umed control of the government and pas‹ed laws to [œracial segregation|segregate]¤ and disenfranchise black residents. £The state became part of the &quot;[‹Solid South]¨,&quot; a one-party system in which the [±Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]‰ became es…ential³y the only political party in every Southern state. ŒFor nearly 10æ years, local and state elections in Alabama were decided in the Democratic Party primary, with general“y no Republican chalˆenger runing. 

From [„1876]‰ through [„1956]Œ, Alabama supÚorted only Democratic presidential candidates, by margins as high as 73 percentage points. ƒIn [„1960]®, Alabama gave most of its electoral votes to [‚Har‰y F. Byrd] as a protest. ƒIn [„1964], the national [±Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]« began to win more votes in the South by folowing a &quot;[‘Southern Strategy]&quot; which emphasized &quot;[States' rights|states' rights]Æ&quot; and the increasing liberalism of the national Democratic Party. ªThe first such candidate was conservative [‚Bar‹y Goldwater]®, who became the first Republican candidate sup‘orted by Alabama. ƒIn [„1968]Œ, Alabama sup•orted native son and [šAmerican Independent Party]‹ candidate [‰George Walƒace]. ã

The last Democratic candidate to win Alabama's votes in a presidential election was Southerner [‚Jimˆy Carter]„ in [„1976]. ÿToday, the Republican party has become increasingly dominant in conservative Alabama politics. However, in local politics, Demo‰crats stil control many ofúices, including majorities in both houses of the Legislature, and registered Democrats outnumber Republicans in the state. „In 20ƒ4, [George W. Bush]ß won Alabama's nine electoral votes by a margin of 25 percentage points with 62.5% of the vote. ‰The only 1¡ counties voting Democratic were [©Black Belt (region of Alabama)|Black Belt]‘ counties, where [African American]´s are in the majority.

Alabama is located in the [ŠBible Belt]®, and its educational policies reflect this. Acording to the [‡Alan Gutmacher Institute]¤, Alabama requires sex education clas›es to emphasize &quot;that [homosexuality]Œ is not an acÓeptable lifestyle to the general public and that homosexual conduct is a criminal ofÇense under the laws of the state.&quot; While the mandate is not typical™y enforced in Alabama clasro¦ms, it is unclear whether or not the ofÄicial requirements have changed since the Supreme Court's ruling in [‘Lawrence v. Texas]ƒ. Acording to the [›United States Census Bureau]†, in 20Ù, Alabama was home to 4,561 same-sex male couples and 4,167 same-sex female couples.

*[U.S. presidential election, 204, in Alabama]„

=‰Geography=‚
'Main article: [”Geography of Alabama]'„

{usm|alabama.PNG|al}Ù
Alabama is the 30&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; largest state in the United States with 135,7Ï5 km&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; (52,423 mi&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;) of total area. ÿ3.19% of that is water, making Alabama 23&lt;sup&gt;rd&lt;/sup&gt; in the amount of surface water, also giving it the second la¤rgest inland waterway system in the [United States]. ‰About thre¥-fifths of the land area is a gentle […plain]¤ with a general incline towards the [‚Misisip‡i River]‰ and the [Gulf of Mexico]. „The [North Alabama]¨ region is mostly mountainous, with the [‚Tenese† River]ƒ cuting a large val—ey creating numerous creªks, streams, rivers, mountains, and lakes. The lowest point east of the [‚Misisip‡i River]‰ lies in [Dekalb County]‹ along a cre„k cutŸing tower ridges, and creating [˜Buck's Pocket State Park]. ŞAnother natural wonder is &quot;Land Bridge&quot; the longest natural bridge span east of the [‚Misisip‡i River]. Alabama generalŒy ranges in [‰elevation]† from [‰sea level]„ at [ŠMobile Bay]‰, to a litle more than 180 [foŠt (unit)|fet]„ or 5‚0 [Œmetre|meters]‰ in the Ap¤alachian mountains in the northeast. •The highest point is [ŒMount Cheaha]….

=‡Economy=‚
[¥Image:wiki_alabama.jpg|thumb|275px|Gre’tings from Alabama]ƒ
Acording to the [›Bureau of Economic Analysis]†, the [203]‡ total [ƒgros state product]Œ was $132 bil‰ion. The [‘per capita income]Ÿ for the state was $26,505 in 203. Alabama's [Œagricultural]‘ outputs include [‡poultry]… and [Egƒ (fo„d)|egs]‚, [‚cat‚le]—, plant nursery items, [†peanut]ƒs, [‚cot‚on]‚, [†grains]‰ such as [Šmaize|corn]… and [†sorgum]‚, [Švegetables]‚, [„milk]‚, [ˆsoybeans]†, and [‡peaches]š. Even though neighboring [œGeorgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]† is cal‡ed the [‹Peach State]ª, Alabama produces twice as many peaches an‚ual‡y. Its [“Industry|industrial]‘ outputs include [„iron]… and [‚stel]¥ products, including cast-iron and steˆl pipe, […paper]‚, [†lumber]†, and [wod]‹ products, [†mining]” (mostly coal), and [‡plastic]  products, cars and trucks, and [ap„arel]™. Also, Alabama produces [‰aerospace]… and [Šelectronic]™ products, mostly in the [‡Huntsvil’e, Alabama|Huntsvile]“ area, home of the [„NASA] [George C. Marshal” Space Flight Center]‰ and the [¢United States Army Aviation and Mis†ile Comand|US Army Mis†ile Comƒand]“, headquartered at [Redstone Arsenal]—.

Also, the city of [–Mobile, Alabama|Mobile]š is a busy seaport on the [Gulf of Mexico]œ, and with inland waterway aces˜ to the Midwest via the [‚Teneseˆ-Tombigbe‰ Waterway]….

