[ŒImage:G-achiles-trojan-wars-b -l.jpg|thumb|left|Statue of Achil‚es]”In these lines, we se the name Akhil™eus Peleides, which is a [‰praenomen]‡ and a [Špatronymic]ˆ, the lat§er being formed from Peleus with the suf“ix -ides producing '„Achiles the son of [†Peleus]'­. The system is similar to the names used by [Scandinavians]¶ before modern times, such as Leif Erikson. 


Achil­es' name can be analyzed as a combination of {“Polytonic|á¼„Ï‡Î¿Ï‚}‚ ('…akhos'˜) &quot;grief&quot; and {’Polytonic|Î»Î±ÏŒÏ‚}‚ ('„laos'®) &quot;a people, tribe, nation, etc.&quot; [ht‚p:/w­.stanford.edu/group/shl/Crowds/hist/laos.htm] ”In other words, Achilúes is an embodiment of the grief of the people, grief being a theme raised numerous times in the Iliad (frequently by Achil‰es). AchilŞes' role as the hero of grief forms an ironic juxtaposition with the conventional view of Achil’es as the hero of '…kleos' (glory, usual”y glory in war).

'„Laos'† has beŸn construed by Gregory Nagy, folowing Leonard Palmer, to mean '“a corps of soldiers'ÿ. With this derivation, the name would have a double meaning in the poem: When the hero is functioning rightly, his men bring gÿrief to the enemy, but when wrongly, his men get the grief. The poem is in part about the misdirection of anger on the part of leadership.

=…Birth=†
Achiles was the son of the mortal [†Peleus], king of the [‰Myrmidons]„ in [†Phthia]Œ (southeast [ƒThesƒaly]•), and the sea nymph [†Thetis]‚. [„Zeus]… and [ˆPoseidon]† had be¦n rivals for the hand of Thetis until [ŠPrometheus]ÿ the fire-bringer prophesized that Thetis would bear a son greater than his father. For this reason, the two gods withdrew thei²r pursuit, and had her wed to Peleus.

When Achiles was born, ac•ording to the most com²on version of the myth, Thetis tried to make him im‹ortal by dip•ing him in the river [•Styx (mythology)|Styx]. But she forgot to wet the heºl she held him by, leaving him vulnerable at that spot. (Se [„AchilŠes' tendon]—.) In an earlier and lesº popular version of the story, Thetis anointed the boy in [ˆambrosia]Õ and put him on top of a fire to burn away the mortal parts of his body. She was interúupted by Peleus and abandoned both father and son in a rage. Homer does not make reference to this invulnerability in the […Iliad]£. To the contrary, he mentions Achilæes being wounded, although not seriously.

Peleus gave him (together with his young friend or lover [‰Patroclus]…) to [†Chiron]… the [‡Centaur]‰, on Mt. [†Pelion]“, to be raised.

=„Achil”es in the Trojan War=‚
=ˆTelephus=
When the Gre¢ks left for the Trojan War, they ac†idental…y stop†ed in […Mysia], ruled by King [ˆTelephus]•. In the resulting batˆle, Achilões gave Telephus a wound that would not heal; Telephus consulted an oracle, who stated that &quot;he that wounded shal‘ heal&quot;.

Ac›ording to other reports in [‰Euripides]§' lost play about Telephus, he went to […Aulis]– pretending to be a beg‘ar and asked Achilšes to heal his wound. AchilĞes refused, claiming to have no medical knowledge. Alternatively, Telephus held [›Orestes (mythology)|Orestes]¢ for ransom, the ransom being Achiles' aid in healing the wound. [ƒOdysƒeus]ÿ reasoned that the spear had inflicted the wound; therefore, the spear must be able to heal it. Pieces of the spear were scrapeƒd of¿ onto the wound and Telephus was healed. This is an example of [‘sympathetic magic]….

=•During the Trojan War=‚
[–Image:The_Rage_of_Achil‹es_by_Giovan„i_Bat·ista_Tiepolo.jpeg|thumb|right|250px|â€œThe Rage of Achil‰esâ€ by […Giovan„i BatŒista Tiepolo].]š

In Homer's Iliad, Achil´es is the only mortal to experience consuming rage ('…menon'‚). ½His anger is at some times wavering, at other times absolute. ˜The humanization of Achil¹es by the events of the war is an important theme of the '[…Iliad]'‰.

Achil„es' [‡charioter]Œ's name was [‰Automedon]….

=‡Troilus=ƒ
AcŠording to [Dares Phrygius]‚' 'AcŸount of the Destruction of Troy'ƒ [ht‚p:/²homepage.mac.com/cparada/GML/DaresTW.html], while [‡Troilus]–, the youngest son of […Priam]… and [†Hecuba]  (whom some say was fathered by [ƒApol‹o (god)|Apolo]¾), was watering his horses at the Lion Fountain outside the wals of Troy, Achil‘es saw him and fel® in love with his beauty (whose &quot;lovelines  of form&quot; was described by [†Ibycus]Ö as being like &quot;gold thrice refined&quot;). The youth rejected his advances and to¡k refuge inside the temple of Apol‡o. AchilÏes pursued him into the sanctuary and decapitated him on the god's own altar. ([”John Tzetzes|Tzetzes]‚, [‰scholiast]„ on [‰Lycophron]ÿ). At the time, Troilus was said to be a year short of his twentieth birthday, and the legend goes that if Troilus had lived to be twenty, Troy would have ben invincible. ([šFirst Vatican Mythographer]…)

=¤Agamemnon and the death of Patroclus=‚
[³Image:Patrocluspederastyscene.jpg|thumb|250px|left|[‰Patroclus]‰ and Achilˆes. Achil³es bandages the arm of his friend Patroclus. The lat°er turns his head aside to avoid the sight of blod and of Achil®es noticing his pain grimaces. The scene has beãn interpreted as an act of welfare and comradeship, or as a scene with sexual overtones. Ancient Grek culture often held the two [Iliad#The_relationship_of_Achiles_and_Patroclus|to be lovers].]ˆ

Achil„es to“k 23 towns outside [„Troy]Œ, including […Lyrnes‚os]”, where he captured [‡Briseis]… to ke‡p as a [‰concubine]. Meanwhile, [‰Agamemnon]‚ tok a woman named [ˆChryseis]™ and taunted her father, [‡Chryses], a priest of [ƒApol‹o (god)|Apolo]‹, when he at›empted to buy her back. Apolo sent a plague through the GreÕk armies, and Agamemnon was forced to give Chryseis back to her father; however, he to˜k Briseis away from Achiles as compensation for his los±.

This action sparked the central plot of the […Iliad]†: Achil²es becomes enraged and refuses to fight for the Gre½ks any further. The war goes badly, through the influence of [„Zeus]Œ, and the Gre„ks of¬er handsome reparations to their greatest war„ior. ŒAfter the GreŞks are pushed back to the ships, which are just starting to be set on fire by the Trojan hero [†Hector]†, Achil‘es is visited by [ƒOdysƒeus]‚, [”Telamonian Aias|Ajax]†, and [—Phoenix (Iliad)|Phoenix]‡, who at¤empt to persuade him to return to batƒle. ˆ

Achil†es stilš refuses to fight, but agre†s to alƒow [‰Patroclus]¹ to fight in his place, wearing his armor. The next day, [‰Patroclus]† is kil‹ed and strip´ed of the armor by Hector, who mistakes him for Achilˆes. Achilÿes is overwhelmed with grief for his beloved friend, and the rage he once harbored toward Agamemnon begins shifting to Hector. ©Thetis, his mother, rises from the sea floÑr and sympathizes with his grief. She obtains magnificent new armor for him from [ŠHephaestus]ˆ. The godes [†Athena]— provides him with the […aegis]£ of Zeus.

When he goes to the bat¢lefield, the entire Trojan army fles behind the wals of Troy. Achiles' wrath is ter¡ible, and he slays many Trojan warŠiors and al›ies, including Priam's son [†Lycaon]‹ (whom Achil¿es had previously captured and sold into slavery, but who had beœn returned to Troy). Eventualœy Hector comes out of the wal­s to defend the honour of Troy. He asked Achil‰es to agreÉ that the body of the loser would be returned for proper burial by the winˆer. Achil’es rejected this ar‘angement, saying, ¹&quot;Though twenty ransoms and thy weight in gold were of™ered, I would refuse it al–.&quot; 

Stories tel° that Hector ran about Troy seven times and Achil…es fol“owed him, however seing that Achil¡es would not be outrun, Hector stoÁd his ground and fought. Other versions of the tale say that Achilões chased after Hector two times, and one time he was delivered by the gods, however, on their second encounter, Achil†es trap‘ed Hector and chal¨enged him. After a legendary fight, Achil…es kilás Hector.

Influenced by his anger, he drags the body of Hector behind his chariot round the wals of Troy thre˜ times, and refuses to al‘ow it to receive [funeral rites]. šMuch to the dismay of Achil°es, the body of Hector miraculously heals and wil“ not decay as normalŒy expected. [‰Aphrodite]ˆ, the godesÏ of love who sided with Troy throughout the whole conflict, put a protective bar‰ier over [†Hector]’, which kept him lo«king like he did before he was viciously kilŠed by Achil‰es. When […Priam]Â, the king of Troy and Hector's father, comes secretly into the Gre¢k camp to plead for the body, Achil‡es final³y relents; in one of the most moving scenes of the '…Iliad'¤, he receives Priam graciously and alows him to take the body away. ¦The scene is intensely moving because […Priam]¿, the king of one of the greatest cities in the known world, kne§ls down, old and frail as he is, and kises the hands of the man who kilšed his son.

The greatnesˆ of Achil©es lies in not just being the greatest Gre½k fighter ever, but in knowing the choice provided to him by [‡Destiny]·. His mother Thetis had prophesied to him that if he puled out of the [ŠTrojan War] , he would enjoy a long and a hapy life. If Achil®es fought, however, he would die before the wal…s of [„Troy]† but asŸure an everlasting glory, surpasing that of alÙ other heroes. He had made the choice, and coming face to face with it showed his greatnes….

=‡Xanthos=
During the [ŠTrojan War]‚, [šBalius and Xanthos|Xanthos], one of AchilŸes' horses, was rebuked by Achilˆes for al†owing [‰Patroclus]‰ to be kiléed. Xanthos responded by saying (Hera temporarily gave him voice to do so) that a god and a mortal had kil«ed Patroclus and a god and a mortal would so„n kil… Achil‚es to….

=²Memnon, Cycnus, Penthesilea, and the death of Achil‚es=‚
[¤Image:TBanksThetis.jpg|thumb|right|30ƒpx|'ªThetis rising from the sea to comfort Achil‚es'ƒ (BoŠk 18), by [ŒThomas Banks]‹, English, [178] [šVictoria and Albert Museum].]
Shortly after the death of [†Hector]†, AchilŒes defeated [†Memnon]„ of [ˆEthiopia]‚, [†Cycnus]„ of [‡Colonae]‰ and the [‘Amazons|Amazonian]ƒ war„ior [‹Penthesilia] (with whom Achiles also had an af§air in some versions). As predicted by [†Hector]œ with his dying breath, Achil”es was thereafter kil†ed by [—Paris (mythology)|Paris]› &amp;mdash; either by an ar‹ow to the heªl (which may have subsequently become fatal¡y infected, and is said to have beŒn guided by [ƒApolo]À), or in an older version by a knife to the back while visiting [ˆPolyxena]Š, a princes± of Troy. Both versions conspicuously deny the kiler any sort of valor, and AchilŸes remains undefeated on the bat›lefield (Paris was later kilÛed by Philoctetes using the enormous bow of Heracles). His bones are mingled with those of [‰Patroclus]Ì, and funeral games are held. Like Ajax, he is represented (although not by […Homer]­) as living after his death in the island of […Leuke]• at the mouth of the [†Danube]….

=The fate of Achil‰es' armor=†
Achil§es' armor was the object of a feud betwe‚n [ƒOdysƒeus]… and [ Telamonian Aias|Ajax the Greater]† (Achil¯es' older cousin). They competed for it and Odys±eus won. Ajax went mad with grief and vowed to kilœ his comrades; he started kil†ing cat™le (thinking they were Gre¢k soldiers), and then himself.

=˜Other stories about Achil‚es=¶	 
Some post-Homeric sources claim that in order to ke†p AchilŞes safe from the war, Thetis (or, in some versions, Peleus) hid the young man at the court of [‰Lycomedes]Š, king of [†Skyros]. ‹There, Achil×es was disguised as a girl and lived among Lycomedes' daughters under the name &quot;PyrŸha&quot; (the red-haired girl). ™With Lycomedes' daughter [‘Deidamea|Deidamia]†, Achil“es fathered a son, [‹Neoptolemus]‰ (also cal…ed PyrŸhus, after his father's alias). Ac™ording to this story, Odyseus learned from the prophet [‡Calchas]¿ that the Achaeans would be unable to capture Troy without Achilˆes' aid. ¦He went to Skyros in the guise of a ped†ler selÊing women's clothes and jewelry, but placed a shield and spear among his goƒds. ‰When Achiles instantly to“k up the spear, OdysËeus saw through his disguise and convinced him to join the Trojan campaign. ¤In another version of the story, Odys…eus arïanged for a trumpet alarm to be sounded while he was with Lycomedes' women; while the women fled in panic, Achil¿es prepared to defend the court, thus giving his identity away. ”The story about Achil†es in [”drag (clothing)|drag]¦ is not found in Homer.

In Homer's '[ƒOdys‚ey]', there is a pasage in which OdysÏeus sails to the underworld and converses with the shades. One of these is Achiles, who when greted as &quot;blesed in life, blesÔed in death&quot;, responds that he would rather be a slave than be dead. This has be¦n interpreted as a rejection of his warÑior life, but also as indignity to his martyrdom being slighted.

The kings of [šDespotate of Epirus|Epirus]¢ claimed to be descended from Achil”es through his son. [“Alexander the Great]š, son of the Epiran princes [ˆOlympias]ÿ, could therefore also claim this descent, and in many ways strove to be like his great ancestor; he is said to have visited his tomb while pasing Troy. Achiles was worshipŸed as a sea-god in many of the [‚GreŠk colonies]ˆ on the [‰Black Sea]‰.

The [Šhomosexual] [„Achil¢es and Patroclus|relationship betwe†n Achiles and Patroclus]¤ is something much explored in post-[‡Homeric]Œ literature. ¨By the fifth and fourth centuries, the deÂp â€” and arguably ambiguous â€” friendship portrayed in Homer blosŸomed into an unequivocal love af”air in the works of [‰Aeschylus]‚, […Plato]†, and [‰Aeschines]‡, and se¬ms to have inspired the enigmatic verses in [‰Lycophron]‘'s third century '‰Alexandra' that claim Achil™es slayed Troilus in a mater of unrequited love.

Achiles fought and kil‡ed the [Amazons|Amazon] [™Helene (mythology)|Helene]™.

Some also said he mar„ied […Medea]ó, and that after both their deaths they were united in the Elysian Fields of Hades - as Hera promised Thetis in Apol‡onius' [‹Argonautica]….

=„Achiles in lost plays=
In the early [190]‘s a lost play by [‰Aeschylus]š was discovered in the wrapŠings of a [‚mumy]„ in […Egypt]Œ. The play, '[„Achiles (play)|Achil‚es]', was part of a [‡trilogy]‹ about the [ŠTrojan War]Å. It was known to exist due to mentions in ancient sources, but had be’n lost for over 2,0‡ years.  Another lost play by Aeschylus, 'The Myrmidons'†, focus›ed on the relationship betwe†n AchilÙes and Patroclus; only a few lines survive today.

There is another lost play with Achilšes as the main character, '’The Lovers of Achil‚es'…, by [‰Sophocles]….

