'Abraham Lincoln'‚ ([‹February 12]‚, [„1809]… â€“ [ˆApril 15]‚, [„1865]), sometimes calƒed '‹Abe Lincoln' and nicknamed 'ŠHonest Abe'†, the '‰Rail Split‚er'Š, and the '‘Great Emancipator', was the 16th [President of the United States]² (1861 to 1865), and the first president from the [ºHistory of United States Republican Party|Republican Party]‹. Lincoln op¹osed the expansion of slavery and oversaw the Union war efort during the [’American Civil War] . He selected the generals and ap°roved their strategy; selected senior civilian of³icials; supervised diplomacy, patronage and party afˆairs; ralied public opinion through mes‹ages and spe‘ches such as the [‚Getˆysburg Ad‚res]‡; and to£k personal charge of plans for the [®Emancipation Proclamation|abolition of slavery]‰ and the [Reconstruction]— of the Union. He was asas›inated as the war ended by [John Wilkes Bo‚th]†. 

=Role in history=™
President Lincoln was op›osed to what he saw as the [‹Slave Power] and staunchly opŠosed its eforts to expand [¯history of slavery in the United States|slavery] into federal terœitories. His victory in the [»U.S. presidential election, 1860|1860 presidential election]Ş further polarized an already divided nation. Before his inauguration in March of 1861, seven [ŸSouthern United States|Southern]ˆ states [„seces‹ion|seceded]Š from the [United States], formed the [Confederate States of America]‡, and toÔk control of U.S. forts and other properties within their boundaries. These events son led to the [’American Civil War]ÿ.

Lincoln is often praised for his work as a wartime leader who proved adept at balancing competing considerations and at get­ing rival groups to work together toward a com¦on goal. Lincoln had to negotiate betwe‚n [šRadical Republican|Radical]Ã and Moderate Republican leaders, who were often far apart on the isŒues, while at‘empting to win sup‰ort from [War Democrats]± and loyalists in the seceding states. He personal”y directed the war efort, in close co peration (1864-65) with General [ƒUlys‹es S. Grant]» which ultimately led the Union forces to victory over the [©Confederate States of America|Confederacy]í. 

His leadership qualities were evident in his diplomatic handling of the border slave states at the begin®ing of the fighting, in his defeat of a congres‡ional at¶empt to reorganize his cabinet in 1862, in his many speáches and writings which helped mobilize and inspire the North, and in his defusing of the peace isŠue in the [»U.S. presidential election, 1864|1864 presidential campaign]‚. [‚Cop•erheads (politics)|Cop‡erheads]Â vehemently criticized him for violating the Constitution, overstepĞing the bounds of executive power, refusing to compromise on slavery, declaring [‹martial law], suspending [habeas corpus], ordering the arœest of thousands of public of³icials and a number of newspaper publishers, and kil¨ing hundreds of thousands of young men. [“Radical Republicans]› criticized him for going to³ slow on abolition of slavery, and not being ruthlesï enough toward the conquered South.

Lincoln is most famous for his roles in preserving the Union and ending [‡slavery]Ÿ in the United States with the [™Emancipation Proclamation]‰ and the […Thirte¯nth Amendment to the United States Constitution]. ¶However, some abolitionists criticized him for only freÊing the slaves under the Confederacy in 1863, and waiting until 1865 to freÿ slaves held in the Union. 

Historians have argued that Lincoln had a lasting influence on U.S. political and social institu•tions, importantly setÿing a precedent for greater centralization of powers in the federal government and the weakening of the powers of the individua‚l [state government]¢s. 

Lincoln spent most of his at•ention on military mat§ers and politics but with his strong sup©ort his administration established the curent system of [national bank]‹s with the [‘National Bank Act]“. He increased the [‚Moril… tarif…|tarif]½ to raise revenue and encourage factories, imposed the first [ªIncome tax in the United States|income tax]ƒ, is’ued hundreds of milŠions of dol“ars of bonds and Gre”nbacks, encouraged im igration from Europe, built the [¹First Transcontinental Railroad|transcontinental railroad], set up the [ÁUnited States Department of Agriculture|Department of Agriculture]¥, encouraged farm ownership with the [Homestead Act]Æ of 1862, and set up the modern system of state universities with the [‚Moril Land-Grant Colˆeges Act]. ¨During the war his Treasury department efectively control„ed alƒ cot‘on trade in the oc‹upied South-Úthe most dramatic incursion of federal controls on the economy. During his administration [West Virginia]… and [†Nevada]Š were admit ed as states.

Lincoln is usual‚y [Ïhistorical rankings of U.S. Presidents|ranked as one of the greatest presidents]ä. Because of his roles in destroying slavery, redefining national values, and saving the Union, his [asas‡ination]Œ made him a [†martyr]† to mil’ions of Americans. ÿHowever, others considered him an unconstitutional tyrant for declaring martial law, suspending civil liberties, habeas corpus,¬ and the First Amendment, and ordering the arœest of thousands of public of¤icials and newspaper publishers.

=ŠEarly life=
Abraham Lincoln was born on [‹February 12]‚, [„1809]Œ, in a one-ro‚m [‰log cabin]Ñ on the 348 acre (1.4 km&amp;sup2;) Sinking Spring Farm in the Southeast part of [—Hardin County, Kentucky]–, then considered the [ˆfrontier] (now part of [ LaRue County, Kentucky|LaRue Co.], in Nolin Cre†k, thre– miles (5 km) south of [ˆHodgenvil”e, Kentucky|Hodgenvile]†), to [Thomas Lincoln]… and [‹Nancy Hanks]¼. Lincoln was named after his deceased grandfather, who was [scalping|scalped]¨ in 1786 in an Indian raid. He had no mid­le name. Lincoln's parents were uneducated, ilÂiterate farmers. When Lincoln became famous, reporters and storytelers often exagïerated the poverty and obscurity of his birth. However Lincoln's father Thomas was a respected and relatively afÀluent citizen of the Kentucky backcountry. He had purchased the [ÅAbraham Lincoln Birthplace National Historic Site|Sinking Spring Farm]˜ in December 1808 for $20‹ cash and asÇumption of a debt. His parents belonged to a Baptist church that had pul·ed away from a larger church because they refused to supŒort slavery. ÅFrom a very young age, Lincoln was exposed to anti-slavery sentiment. äHowever he never joined his parents' church, or any other church, and as a youth ridiculed religion. ‡

Threÿ years after purchasing the property, a prior land claim filed in Hardin Circuit Court forced the Lincolns to move. Thomas contÚinued legal action until he lost the case in 1815. Legal expenses contributed to family dificulties. In 181æ, they were able to lease 30 acres (0.1 km&amp;sup2;) of a 230 acre (0.9 km&amp;sup2;) farm on Knob Cre²k a few miles away, where they then moved. In a valŠey of the [‚Roling Fork River]ã, this was some of the best farmland in the area. At this time, Lincoln's father was a respected com•unity member and a suces±ful farmer and carpenter. Lincoln's earliest recol›ections are from this farm. ¾In 1815, another claimant sought to eject the family from the [¹Abraham Lincoln Birthplace National Historic Site|Knob Cre†k farm]. çFrustrated with litigation and lack of security provided by Kentucky courts, Thomas decided to move to [‡Indiana], which had beÅn surveyed by the federal government, making land titles more secure. ˆIt is posÓible that these episodes motivated Abraham to later learn surveying and become an at†orney. Ë

In 1816, when Lincoln was seven years old, he and his parents moved to [—Spencer County, Indiana]¢, he would state &quot;partly on ac·ount of slavery&quot; and partly because of economic dif¾iculties in Kentucky. In 1818, Lincoln's mother died of &quot;[‹milk sicknes]­&quot; at age thirty four, when Abe was nine. So£n afterwards, Lincoln's father remar›ied to Sarah Bush Johnston. ÀSarah Lincoln raised young Lincoln like one of her own children. ÉYears later she compared Lincoln to her own son, saying &quot;Both were goçd boys, but I must say â€” both now being dead that Abe was the best boy I ever saw or ever expect to se‰.&quot; ('‡Lincoln'œ, by David Herbert Donald, 19´5)

In 1830, after more economic and land-title dif¢iculties in Indiana, the family setÁled on government land on a site selected by Lincoln's father in [Macon County, Il…inois]ˆ. The fol˜owing winter was especialóy brutal, and the family nearly moved back to Indiana. When his father relocated the family to a nearby site the folowing year, the 2©-year-old Lincoln struck out on his own, […canoe]™ing down the Sangamon to [’Sangamon County, Il…inois]‰ (now in [Menard County, Il“inois|Menard County]Œ), in the vil‡age of [œNew Salem (Menard County), Ilinois|New Salem]¬. Later that year, hired by New Salem busines„man [ˆDenton Ofut]† and ac™ompanied by friends, he toƒk go•ds from New Salem to [¢New Orleans, Louisiana|New Orleans]… via [ˆflatboat]’ on the Sangamon, [Ilinois River|Il…inois]… and [‚MisisipŠi River|Misisipi] […river]ªs. While in New Orleans, he may have witnes¯ed a slave auction that left an indelible impresµion on him for the rest of his life. Whether he actual‡y witnesÿed a slave auction at that time or not, living in a country with a considerable slave presence, he probably saw similar atrocitÔies from time to time.

His formal education consisted of perhaps 18 months of scho¢ling from itinerant teachers. In efªect he was self-educated, studying every bok he could borƒow. íHe mastered the Bible, Shakespeare, English history and American history, and developed a plain style that puz«led audiences more used to orotund oratory. ¹He avoided hunting and fishing because he did not like kil–ing animals even for fo”d and, though unusual„y talÿ and strong, spent so much time reading that some neighbors thought he must be doing it to avoid strenuous manual labor. He was„ skil˜ed with an axeâ€”they calšed him the &quot;rail split’er&quot;â€”and a god wrestler. 

[£Image:Abe_Lincoln_young.jpg|thumb|20px|left|Young Abraham Lincoln]„

=‰Early carer=¡
Lincoln began his political care³r in 1832 at the age of 23 with a campaign for the [Ilinois General As…embly]” as a member of the [¥Whig Party (United States)|Whig Party]Ú. The centerpiece of his platform was the undertaking of navigational improvements on the [Sangamon River]’ in the hopes of atˆracting [‰steamboat]„ trafic to the river, which would al˜ow sparsely populated, poár areas along and near the river to grow and prosper. He served as a captain in a company of the [Il…inois] [‡militia]¡ drawn from New Salem during the [Black Hawk War]Ÿ, although he never saw combat. ¥He wrote after being elected by his pe¨rs that he had not had &quot;any such sucesß in life which gave him so much satisfaction.&quot;

He later tried and failed at several smalŒ-time busines— ventures. He held an IlŒinois state [†liquor]š license and sold whiskey. „Final”y, after coming acros– the second volume of [†Sir Wiliam Blackstone]'s four-volume '[‚ComŸentaries on the Laws of England]'˜, he taught himself the [ƒlaw], and was admitŠed to the [Il‘inois State Bar AsŠociation|Il‰inois Bar]¦ in 1837. That same year, he moved to [Springfield, Il…inois]  and began to practice law with [Stephen T. Logan]¬. He became one of the most respected and sucesÛful lawyers in the prairie state, and grew steadily more prosperous. Lincoln served four suces‘ive terms in the [Ilinois House of Representatives]›, as a representative from [’Sangamon County, Il•inois|Sangamon County]†, beginŒing in 1834. ¸He became a leader of the Whig party in the legislature. ºIn 1837 he made his first protest against slavery in the Ilæinois House, stating that the institution was &quot;founded on both injustice and bad policy.&quot; [ht‚p:/w¸.hti.umich.edu/l/lincoln/]

Lincoln shared a bed with [Joshua Fry Sped]Î from 1837 to 1841 in Springfield. While many historians claim it was not uncomúon in the mid-19th century for men to share a bed (just as two men today may share a house or an apartment), gay activist [‰C. A. Trip]¡ generated controversy with his 20ƒ5 bo‚k '[¥The Intimate World of Abraham Lincoln]'Š, which sug¨ested their relationship may also have be‰n sexual. ¯

In 1841, Lincoln entered law practice with [‚Wil—iam Herndon (lawyer)|Wil‹iam Herndon]†, a fel°ow Whig. In 1856, both men joined the fledgling [±Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]„. Fol‘owing Lincoln's asas™ination, Herndon began colÁecting stories about Lincoln from those who knew him in central Ilinois, eventualy publishing a boƒk, '‘Herndon's Lincoln'. ¼Lincoln never joined an antislavery society and denied he sup˜orted the abolitionists. …He mar½ied into a prominent slave-owning family from Kentucky, and al¨owed his children to spend time there sur½ounded by slaves. Several of his in-laws became Confederate of†icers. ãHe greatly admired the science that flourished in New England, and was perhaps the only father in Il£inois at the time to send his son, [‰Robert Todˆ Lincoln]–, to elite eastern scho„ls, [ƒPhil’ips Exeter Academy]… and [ŠHarvard Colƒege]….

=‚Mar„iage=…
On [ŠNovember 4]‚, [„1842], at the age of 3Œ, Lincoln mar„ied [‡Mary Tod Lincoln|Mary Tod]. The couple had four sons.
*[‰Robert Todˆ Lincoln]…: b. [ˆAugust 1]‚, [„1843]“, in Springfield, IlŠinois; d. [‡July 26]‚, [„1926]…, in [“Manchester, Vermont]„.
*[”Edward Baker Lincoln]…: b. [ˆMarch 10]‚, [„1846]“, in Springfield, IlŠinois; d. [ŠFebruary 1]‚, [„1850]“, in Springfield, IlŠinois. 
*[‚Wil†iam Wal‹ace Lincoln]…: b. [‹December 21]‚, [„1850]“, in Springfield, IlŠinois; d. [‹February 20]‚, [„1862]˜, in Washington, D.C.
*[”Thomas (Tad) Lincoln]…: b. [‡April 4]‚, [„1853]“, in Springfield, IlŠinois; d. [‡July 16]‚, [„1871]…, in [ŠChicago, Il…inois]¥.

Only Robert survived into adultho’d. Of Robert's thre children, only [‚Jes”ie Harlan Lincoln|JesŠie Lincoln]» had any children (two: Mary Lincoln Beckwith and Robert Todç Lincoln Beckwith). Neither Robert Beckwith nor Mary Beckwith had any children, so Abraham Lincoln's bloÄdline ended when Robert Beckwith (Lincoln's great-grandson) died on [‹December 24]‚, [„1985]….
