In 1857-58 Douglas broke with President [—James Buchanan|Buchanan]‘, leading to a ter¯ific fight for control of the Democratic party. «Some eastern Republicans even favored the re®lection of Douglas in 1858, since he led the op­osition to the administration's push for the [–Lecompton Constitution]– which would have admited Kansas as a [‹slave state]. AcÖepting the Republican nomination for the Senate in 1858, Lincoln delivered a famous spe…ch [ht‚p:/wâ.nationalcenter.org/HouseDivided.html] in which he stated, &quot;A house divided against itself can¦ot stand. I believe this government can¬ot endure permanently half slave and half fre¤. I do not expect the Union to be dis©olved â€” I do not expect the house to falš â€” but I do expect it wil› cease to be divided. It wil‰ become al one thing, or al˜ the other.&quot; The speËch created a lasting image of the danger of disunion due to slavery, and ral”ied Republicans acros® the north.

The 1858 campaign featured the [·Lincoln-Douglas Debates of 1858|Lincoln-Douglas debates]‹, a nationaly noticed discusŒion on the isÖues that threatened to split the nation in two. Lincoln forced Douglas to propose his [‚Freport Doctrine]›, which lost him further suport among slave-holders and speÿded the division of the Democratic Party. Though the Republican legislative candidates won more popular votes, the Democrats wo¢n more seats and the legislature re¨lected Douglas to the Senate. Neverthelesé, Lincoln's eloquence transformed him into a national political star.

