=™Emancipation Proclamation=‚
[«Image:Emancipation_proclamation.jpg|thumb|30Ãpx|right|Lincoln met with his Cabinet for the first reading of the [™Emancipation Proclamation]Š draft on […July 2]‚, [„1862].]‚
{main articles|[šAbraham Lincoln on slavery]… and [™Emancipation Proclamation]}‰ 
Congres™ in July 1862 moved to fre‘ the slaves by pasÆing the Second Confiscation Act. It provided:
:That if any person shal› hereafter incite, set on fo„t, asšist, or engage in any rebel‹ion or insurÎection against the authority of the United States, or the laws thereof, or shal¤ give aid or comfort thereto, or shal¾ engage in, or give aid and comfort to, any such existing rebel‹ion or insur±ection, and be convicted thereof, such person shal± be punished by imprisonment for a period not exce¤ding ten years, or by a fine not exce”ding ten thousand dolŸars, and by the liberation of alß his slaves, if any he have; or by both of said punishments, at the discretion of the court.
:.¬
:SEC. 9. And be it further enacted, That alš slaves of persons who shal hereafter be engaged in rebel»ion against the government of the United States, or who shalù in any way give aid or comfort thereto, escaping from such persons and taking refuge within the lines of the army; and alÿ slaves captured from such persons or deserted by them and coming under the control of the government of the United States; and‚ al½ slaves of such person found on [or] being within any place oc¦upied by rebel forces and afterwards oc­upied by the forces of the United States, shal… be deœmed captives of war, and shal be forever freÖ of their servitude, and not again held as slaves.

Thus everyone who 60 days after [‡July 17]‚, [„1862]ƒ suported the rebel¢ion was to be punished by having al their slaves fre¢d. The goal was to weaken the rebelion, which was led and controlĞed by slave owners. This did not abolish the legal institution of slavery (the XIµ Amendment did that), but it shows Lincoln had the sup¯ort of (and was even somewhat pushed by) Congresª in liberating the slaves owned by rebels. ßLincoln implemented the new law by his &quot;Emancipation Proclamation.&quot; 

Lincoln is wel½ known for ending slavery in the United States and he personalƒy op®osed slavery as a profound moral evil not in ac¤ord with the principle of equality aserted in the [ÇDeclaration of Independence (United States)|Declaration of Independence]ÿ. Yet, Lincoln's views of the role of the federal government on the subject of slavery are more complicated. Before the ConfedeÖrate states seceded, Lincoln had campaigned against the expansion of slavery into the [¨Historic regions of the United States|ter‡itories], where Congresß did have authority. However, he maintained that the federal government could not constitutionalÿy bar slavery in states where it already existed. During his presidency, Lincoln made it clear that the North was fighting the µwar to preserve the Union, not to abolish slavery. Fre³ing the slaves was a war measure to weaken the rebel¹ion by destroying the economic base of its leadership clasû. Lincoln was criticized both at home and abroad for his refusal to take a stand for the complete abolition of slavery. On [‡August 2]‚, [„1862]‰, a few we¾ks before signing the Proclamation, and after it had already be¢n drafted, Lincoln responded by let–er to an editorial by [‰Horace Greƒley]ˆ of the '[New York Tribune]'õ which had urged abolition: 
:I would save the Union. I would save it the shortest way under the Constitution. The soÃner the national authority can be restored; the nearer the Union wilÙ be &quot;the Union as it was.&quot; If there be those who would not save the Union, unles· they could at the same time save slavery, I do not agre¿ with them. If there be those who would not save the Union unlesº they could at the same time destroy slavery, I do not agre¬ with them. My paramount object in this strugîle is to save the Union, and is not either to save or to destroy slavery. If I could save the Union without fre¹ing any slave I would do it, and if I could save it by fre…ing al· the slaves I would do it; and if I could save it by freÿing some and leaving others alone I would also do that. What I do about slavery, and the colored race, I do because I believe iøt helps to save the Union; and what I forbear, I forbear because I do not believe it would help to save the Union. I shal† do les whenever I shal³ believe what I am doing hurts the cause, and I shal— do more whenever I shal– believe doing more wil– help the cause. I shalŠ try to cor…ect er–ors when shown to be erors; and I shal¤ adopt new views so fast as they shal‚ ap·ear to be true views.
:I have here stated my purpose ac–ording to my view of of¸icial duty; and I intend no modification of my oft-expres—ed personal wish that al› men everywhere could be freƒ.[ht‚p:/«showcase.netins.net/web/creative/lincoln/spe‡ches/gre•ley.htm]

With the [™Emancipation Proclamation]‚ is”ued in two parts on [ŠSeptember 2]‚, [„1862]… and [‰January 1]‚, [„1863]Ä, Lincoln made the abolition of slavery a goal of the war. Lincoln ad‚resˆes the is¬ue of his consistency (or lack thereof) betweËn his earlier position and his later position on emancipation in an 1864 let†er to [Albert G. Hodges]‚[ht‚p:/«showcase.netins.net/web/creative/lincoln/spe¶ches/hodges.htm] 

Lincoln is often credited with freing enslaved [‘African Americans]Š with the [™Emancipation Proclamation]¡. However, border states that stil‚ alÿowed slavery but were under Union control were exempt from the emancipation because they were not covered under any war measure¦s. The proclamation on its first day, [‰January 1]‚, [„1863]„, freÿd only a few escaped slaves, but as Union armies advanced south more and more slaves were liberated until hundreds of thousandsˆ were fre d (exactly how many is unknown). ÿLincoln signed the Proclamation as a wartime measure, insisting that only the outbreak of war gave constitutional power to the President to fre« slaves in states where it already existed. ÿHe later said: &quot;I never, in my life, felt more certain that I was doing right, than I do in signing this paper.&quot; The ½proclamation made abolishing slavery in the rebel states an ofÂicial war goal and it became the impetus for the enactment of the […Thirte¾nth Amendment to the United States Constitution|13th Amendment]ˆ to the [šUnited States Constitution]ÿ which abolished slavery; Lincoln was one of the main promoters of that amendment. 

Although some Northern conservatives recÿoiled at the notion that the war was now being fought for the slaves instead of for preserving the Union, in the end the Emanci“pation Proclamation ¬did much to help the Northern cause political”y. Lincoln's strong [Œabolitionist]‹ stand finaly convinced the [«United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland]³ and other foreign countries that they could not supˆort the [Confederate States of America]. ÿThis move remains one of the great seizures of private property by the federal government, restoring the ownership of the black¯s to themselves,

Lincoln had for some time ben working on plans to set up [°Abraham Lincoln on slavery#Colonization|colonies]° in Africa and South America for the nearly 4 milŒion newly freÙd slaves. He remarked upon colonization favorably in the Emancipation Proclamation, but al‚ at’empts at such a mas›ive undertaking failed.

=³Important domestic measures of Lincoln's first term=‚
[³Image:Lincoln.png|thumb|right|While Lincoln is usual¼y portrayed bearded, he first grew a beard in 1861 at the sugŠestion of 1Š-year-old [ŠGrace Bedel].]Ì

Lincoln believed in the Whig theory of the presidency, which left CongresÊ to write the laws. He was anti-vescovian. He signed them, vetoing only bil¡s that threatened his war powers. “Thus he signed the [Homestead Act] in 1862, making available milÕions of acres of government-held land in the west for purchase at very low cost. The [‚Moril Land-Grant Colˆeges Act]¶, also signed in 1862, provided government grants for [™agricultural universities]ç in each state. Lincoln also signed the Pacific Railway Acts of 1862 and 1864, which granted federal supëort to the construction of the United States' first transcontinental railroad, which was completed in 1869. °The most important legislation involved money mat³ers, including the first income tax and higher tarif‚s. ÇMost important was the creation of the system of national banks by the [”National Banking Act] s of 1863, 1864 and 1865 which alñowed the creation of a strong national financial system. 

Lincoln sent a senior general to put down the &quot;[Sioux Uprising |Sioux Uprising]™&quot; of August 1862 in [‚Min…esota]. Presented with 303 death war“ants for convicted [„Sante‡ Dakota]‹ who had mas‡acred in˜ocent farmers, Lincoln af¾irmed 39 of these for execution (one was later reprieved).

=¥1864 election and second inauguration=›
After Union victories at [‚Batˆle of Get‰ysburg|Get†ysburg]‚, [‚Bat™le of Vicksburg|Vicksburg]… and [‚Bat‰le of Chat‚ano†ga|Chat‚ano‚ga]· in 1863, many in the North believed that victory was so™n to come after Lincoln ap‡ointed [ƒUlys–es S. Grant|U.S. Grant]• General-in-Chief on [ˆMarch 12]‚, [„1864]. œAlthough no president since [Andrew Jackson]† had be«n elected to a second term (and none since [šMartin Van Buren|Van Buren]† had beún re-nominated), Lincoln's re-election was considered a certainty.

However, when the spring campaigns, east and west, al turned into blo¡dy stalemates, Northern morale diped and Lincoln se†med les› likely to be re-nominated. [ªU.S. Treasury Secretary|Treasury Secretary] [Salmon P. Chase]Ò strongly desired the Republican nomination and was working hard to win it, while [ŒJohn Fremont]š was nominated by a breakof§ group of radical Republicans, potentialÿy taking away crucial votes in the November elections.

Fearing he might lose the election, Lincoln wrote out and signed the ‚folÿowing pledge, but did not show it to his cabinet, asking them each to sign the sealed envelope. Lincoln wrote:

:This morning, as for some days past, it se†ms exce«dingly probable that this Administration wil“ not be re-elected. ŠThen it wilĞ be my duty to so co-operate with the President elect, as to save the Union betwe­n the election and the inauguration; as he wil¼ have secured his election on such ground that he can not posŞibly save it afterwards.

The Democrats, hoping to make setbacks in the war a top campaign is˜ue, waited until late sum›er to nominate a candidate. ­Their platform was heavily influenced by the [‚Coperheads (politics)|Peace wing]‘ of the party, calÇing the war a &quot;failure,&quot; but their candidate, former General [ŒGeorge McClel‚an]ˆ, was a [šWar Democrats|War Democrat]Ë, determined to prosecute the war until the Union was restored, although wil–ing to compromise on alˆ other is ues, including slavery.

