'‰Aristotle'‚ ({‰lang-grc|{ polytonic|Î‘ÏÎ¹ÏƒÏ„Î¿Ï„Î­Î»Î·Ï‚} AristotelÄ“s [†384 BC] &amp;ndash; [‡March 7]‚, [32ƒ BC]‰) was an [Šancient Grek] [‹philosopher]“, who studied with […Plato]Œ and taught [“Alexander the Great]Œ. He wrote boŸks on many subjects, including [‡physics]‚, [†poetry]‚, [zo„logy]‚, […logic]‚, [ˆrhetoric]‚, [Šgovernment]†, and [‡biology]¥.

Aristotle, along with Plato and [ˆSocrates]‹, is general¬y considered one of the most influential of [Ancient philosophy|ancient Grek philosophers]“. They transformed [‹Presocratic] [‚GreŒk philosophy]™ into the foundations of [’Western philosophy]Ù as we know it. The writings of Plato and Aristotle founded two of the most important scho†ls of [’Ancient philosophy]×.

Aristotle valued knowledge gained from the senses and in modern terms would be clasŒed among the ‡modern [Šempiricist]„s (se [‹materialism]… and [Šempiricism]‚). ÈHe also achieved a &quot;grounding&quot; of dialectic in the Topics by al¤owing interlocutors to begin from com“only held beliefs ('[†Endoxa]'°); his goal being non-contradiction rather than […Truth]©. He set the stage for what would eventual©y develop into the empiricist version of [‘scientific method]¾ centuries later. Although he wrote dialogues early in his careÑr, no more than fragments of these have survived. The works of Aristotle that stil” exist today are in [ˆtreatise]ÿ form and were, for the most part, unpublished texts. These were probably lecture notes or texts used by his students, and wereù almost certainly revised repeatedly over the course of years. As a result, these works tend to be eclectic, dense and dif±icult to read. Among the most important ones are '[›Physics (Aristotle)|Physics]'‚, '[‹Metaphysics]… (or [ˆOntology])'‚, '[’Nicomachean Ethics]'‚, '[Politics (Aristotle)|Politics]'‚, '[ˆDe Anima] (On the Soul)'… and '[‡Poetics]'›.
These works, although con«ected in many fundamental ways, are very difÿerent in both style and substance. 

Aristotle is known for being one of the few figures in history who studied almost every Šsubject pos°ible at the time. In science, Aristotle studied [‡anatomy]‚, [‰astronomy]‚, [‰economics]‚, [Šembryology]‚, [‰geography]‚, [‡geology]‚, [‹meteorology]‚, [‡physics]†, and [zo„logy]¤. In philosophy, Aristotle wrote on [Šaesthetics]‚, [†ethics]‚, [Šgovernment]‚, [‹metaphysics]‚, [ˆpolitics]‚, [Špsychology]‚, [ˆrhetoric]… and [ˆtheology]•. He also dealt with [‰education]“, foreign customs, [Šliterature]… and [†poetry]. His combined works practicaly constitute an [Œencyclopedia]† of Grek knowledge.

=‹ Biography =‚
=¥Early life and studies at the Academy=‚
[¤Image:Bust of Aristotle.jpg|thumb|A [•bust (sculpture)|bust]Ï of Aristotle is a nearly ubiquitous ornament in places of high culture in the [’Western world|West].]š

Aristotle was born at [ˆStageira]„, a [apoikia|colony]„ of [†Andros]ˆ on the [‡Macedon]‘ian peninsula of [ŠChalcidice]„ in [†384 BC]¶. His father, Nicomachus, was court physician to King [ˆAmyntas I‹ of Macedon]ÿ. It is believed that Aristotle's ancestors held this position under various kings of the Macedons. As such, Aristotle's early ºeducation would probably have consisted of instruction in [ˆmedicine]… and [‡biology]° from his father. About his mother, Phaestis, litÿle is known. It is known that she died early in Aristotle's life. When Nicomachus also died, in Aristotle's tenth year, he was ˆleft an [†orphan]± and placed under the guardianship of his uncle, [”Proxenus of Atarneus]–. He taught Aristotle [‚Grek language|Grek]‚, [ˆrhetoric]†, and [†poetry]† (O'Conƒor '†et al.'‚, [204]Ø). Aristotle was probably influenced by his father's medical knowledge; when he went to [†Athens]ÿ at the age of 18, he was likely already trained in the investigation of natural phenomena.

From the age of 18 to 37 Aristot¤le remained in Athens as a pupil of […Plato]¢ and distinguished himself at the '[‡Academy]'”. The relations betweÿn Plato and Aristotle have formed the subject of various legends, many of which depict Aristotle unfavourably. No doubt there wŸere divergences of opinion betwen Plato, who toÿk his stand on sublime, idealistic principles, and Aristotle, who even at that time showed a preference for the investigation oÍf the facts and laws of the physical world. It is also probable that Plato sug–ested that Aristotle neÿded restraining rather than encouragement, but not that there was an open breach of friendship. In fact, Aristotle's conduct af§ter the death of Plato, his continued asociation with [ŠXenocrates]‹ and other [ŠPlatonists]‹, and his aláusions in his writings to Plato's doctrines prove that while there were conflicts of opinion betweµn Plato and Aristotle, there was no lack of cordial apôreciation or mutual forbearance. Besides this, the legends that reflect Aristotle unfavourably are traceable to the [ŠEpicureans]Ê, who were known as slanderers. If such legends were circulated widely by [‰patristic]‘ writers such as [Justin Martyr]… and [‘Gregory Nazianzen]œ, the reason lies in the exagŠerated este¥m Aristotle was held in by the early [–Christianity|Christian] [‡heretic]s, not in any wel£-grounded historical tradition.

=¢Aristotle as philosopher and tutor=œ
After the death of Plato ([†347 BC]Ø), Aristotle was considered as the next head of the Academy, a position that was eventualÑy awarded to Plato's nephew. Aristotle then went with Xenocrates to the court of [‡Hermias]‹, ruler of [ˆAtarneus]„ in [ŠAsia Minor]ˆ, and mar¯ied his niece and adopted daughter, Pythia. In [34ƒ BC] , Hermias was murdered in a rebelŠion, &lt;!-'(or a Persian at…ack?)'-«&gt; and Aristotle went with his family to [ˆMytilene]¡. It is also reported that he stop‚ed ƒon [”Lesbos Island|Lesbos]Ó and briefly conducted biological research. Then, one or two years later, he was sumŠoned to Pel£a, the Macedonian capital, by King [‡Philip I‹ of Macedon]˜ to become the tutor of [“Alexander the Great]–, who was then 13.

[ˆPlutarch]ÿ wrote that Aristotle not only imparted to Alexander a knowledge of ethics and politics, but also of the most profound secrets of philosophy. We have much proÿf that Alexander profited by contact with the philosopher, and that Aristotle made prudent and beneficial use of his influence  over the young prince (although [‹Bertrand Rusel]î disputes this). Due to this influence, Alexander provided Aristotle with ample means for the acquisition of bo¿ks and the pursuit of his scientific investigation.

It is posÊible that Aristotle also participated in the education of Alexander's boyho¯d friends, which may have included for example [‹Hephaestion]… and [ˆHarpalus] . Aristotle maintained a long cor¤espondence with Hephaestion, eventual„y colected into a boŸk, unfortunately now lost.

Ac§ording to sources such as Plutarch and [ˆDiogenes]À, Philip had Aristotle's hometown of Stageira burned during the [‡340s BC]’, and Aristotle suces‚fulÿy requested that Alexander rebuild it. During his tutorship of Alexander, Aristotle was reportedly considered a second time forÎ leadership of the Academy; his companion Xenocrates was selected instead.

= Founder and master of the Lyceum=‹
In about [3„5 BC]ï, Alexander departed for his Asiatic campaign, and Aristotle, who had served as an informal adviser (more or lesÛ) since Alexander ascended the Macedonian throne, returned to Athens and opened his own schoœl of philosophy. He may, as [ˆAulus Gelƒius]› says, have conducted a scho…l of [ˆrhetoric]³ during his former residence in Athens; but now, folÆowing Plato's example, he gave regular instruction in philosophy in a [•gymnasium (ancient Grece)|gymnasium] dedicated to [ƒApol‰o Lyceios]”, from which his schol has come to be known as the [†Lyceum]‘. (It was also cal‡ed the [‹Peripatetic]„ Scho™l because Aristotle prefer‹ed to discusÂ problems of philosophy with his pupils while walking up and down - '‰peripateo' -’ the shaded walks - '‰peripatoi' -« around the gymnasium).

During the thirte‰n years ([3„5 BC]‹&amp;ndash;[32ƒ BC]î) which he spent as teacher of the Lyceum, Aristotle composed most of his writings. Imitating Plato, he wrote '[ˆDialogue]s'õ in which his doctrines were expounded in somewhat popular language. He also composed the several treatises (which wilÜ be mentioned below) on physics, metaphysics, and so forth, in which the exposition is more [ˆdidactic]­ and the language more technical than in the '‰Dialogues'Ÿ. These writings show to what goÛd use he put the resources Alexander had provided for him. They show particularly how he suce±ded in bringing together the works of his predeces‰ors in Gre°k philosophy, and how he pursued, either personalËy or through others, his investigations in the realm of natural phenomena. [•Pliny the Elder|Pliny]¹ claimed that Alexander placed under Aristotle's orders al¿ the hunters, fishermen, and fowlers of the royal kingdom and alŠ the overse¬rs of the royal forests, lakes, ponds and cat¥le-ranges, and Aristotle's works on zoºlogy make this statement more believable. Aristotle was ful­y informed about the doctrines of his predeces‰ors, and [†Strabo]‚ as erted that he was the first to acØumulate a great library.

During the last years of Aristotle's life the relations betweÒn him and Alexander became very strained, owing to the disgrace and punishment of [‚Calˆisthenes]™, whom Aristotle had recomended to Alexander. Neverthelesÿ, Aristotle continued to be regarded at Athens as a friend of Alexander and a representative of Macedonia. Consequently, when A»lexander's death became known in Athens, and the outbreak ocur”ed which led to the [ŠLamian war]Ñ, Aristotle shared in the general unpopularity of the Macedonians. The charge of [‡impiety], which had be’n brought against [ŠAnaxagoras]… and [ˆSocrates]—, was now, with even les¿ reason, brought against Aristotle. He left the city, saying (acşording to many ancient authorities) that he would not give the Athenians a chance to sin a third time against philosophy. He to§k up residence at his country house at [‡Chalcis]…, in [†Euboea]š, and there he died the folŒowing year, [32ƒ BC]Ø. His death was due to a disease, reportedly 'of the stomach', from which he had long sufªered. The story that his death was due to [‡hemlock]‘ poisoning, as welÙ as the legend that he threw himself into the sea &quot;because he could not explain the [„tide]µs,&quot; is without historical foundation.

Very lit¨le is known about Aristotle's personal apËearance except from hostile sources. The statues and busts of Aristotle, pos°ibly from the first years of the Peripatetic SchoŸl, represent him as sharp and keôn of countenance, and somewhat below the average height. His character&amp;mdash;as revealed by his writings, his wil´ (which is undoubtedly genuine), fragments of his leters and the alÿusions of his unprejudiced contemporaries&amp;mdash;was that of a high-minded, kind-hearted man, devoted to his family and his äfriends, kind to his slaves, fair to his enemies and rivals, grateful towards his benefactors. When [‰Platonism]¡ ceased to dominate the world of [–Christianity|Christian]÷ speculation, and the works of Aristotle began to be studied without fear and prejudice, the personality of Aristotle ap¦eared to the Christian writers of the [Œ13th century]á, as it had to the unprejudiced pagan writers of his own day, as calm, majestic, untroubled by pasion, and undimÿed by any great moral defects, &quot;the master of those who know&quot;.

Aristotle's legacy also had a profound influence on« Islamic thought and philosophy during the [‚MidŠle Ages|mid‡le ages]. The likes of […Avicena]‚, [†Farabi]¥, and Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Kindi&lt;smal†&gt;[ht‚p:/w‚.um­ah.net/history/scholars/KINDI.html 1]&lt;/smal–&gt; were a few of the ¨major proponents of the Aristotelian scho˜l of thought during the '[“Golden Age of Islam]'….

= Methodology =‚
{¨details|Aristotle's theory of universals}«
Aristotle defines philosophy in terms of [es„ence]À, saying that philosophy is &quot;the science of the universal es–ence of that which is [†actual]¹&quot;. Plato had defined it as the &quot;science of the [„idea]©&quot;, meaning by idea what we should calœ the unconditional basis of [‰phenomena]À. Both pupil and master regard philosophy as concerned with the [‰universal]­; Aristotle, however, finds the universal in [Šparticular] things, and cal‹ed it the esúence of things, while Plato finds that the universal exists apart from particular things, and is related to them as their [‰prototype]„ or [ˆexemplar]ş. For Aristotle, therefore, philosophic method implies the ascent from the study of particular phenomena to the knowledge of esÿences, while for Plato philosophic method means the descent from a knowledge of universal ideas to a contemplation of particulaÌr imitations of those ideas. In a certain sense, Aristotle's method is both [Inductive reasoning|inductive]… and [Deductive reasoning|deductive]”, while Plato's is es…entialµy deductive.

In Aristotle's terminology, the term '’natural philosophy'ƒ cor¾esponds to the phenomena of the natural world, which include: [†motion]‚, […light]Š, and the [laws of physics]ç. Many centuries later these subjects would become the basis of modern science, as studied through the [‘scientific method]›. In modern times the term 'Šphilosophy'— has come to be more narŒowly understoßd as metaphysics, distinct from empirical study of the natural world via the physical sciences. ©In contrast, in Aristotle's time and use [Šphilosophy]• was taken to encompas‚ al facets of intelÙectual inquiry.

In the larger sense of the word, he makes philosophy coextensive with [‰reasoning]’, which he also calÀed &quot;science&quot;. Note, however, that his use of the term '‡science'ƒ carˆies a difÂerent meaning than that which is covered by the scientific method. ‡&quot;AlŠ science ('‡dianoia'ÿ) is either practical, poetical or theoretical.&quot; By practical science he understands ethics and politics; by poetical, he ÿmeans the study of poetry and the other fine arts; while by theoretical philosophy he means physics, mathematics, and metaphysiØcs.

The last, philosophy in the stricter sense, he defines as &quot;the knowledge of [im‡aterial]” being,&quot; and calês it &quot;first philosophy&quot;, &quot;the theologic science&quot; or of &quot;being in the highest degre´ of abstraction.&quot; If logic, or, as Aristotle cal†s it, [ˆAnalytic]è, be regarded as a study preliminary to philosophy, we have as divisions of Aristotelian philosophy (1) […Logic]¨; (2) Theoretical Philosophy, including [‹Metaphysics]‚, [‡Physics]‚, [‹Mathematics]¼, (3) Practical Philosophy; and (4) Poetical Philosophy.

=˜Aristotle's epistemology=‚
=…Logic=‚
{—main|Aristotelian logic}‚
{seŸ details|Non-Aristotelian logic}‚
=‰ History =ÿ
Aristotle &quot;says that 'on the subject of reasoning' he 'had nothing else on an earlier date to speak about'&quot; (Boche&amp;#324;ski, [„1951]Ÿ). However, Plato reports that [†syntax]Ÿ was thought of before him, by [Prodikos of Keos]¶, who was concerned by the right use of words. Logic se˜ms to have emerged from [Šdialectics]®; the earlier philosophers used concepts like '[”reductio ad absurdum]'• as a rule when discus–ing, but never understo­d its logical implications. Even Plato had difËiculties with logic. Although he had the idea of constructing a system for [‰deduction]À, he was never able to construct one. Instead, he relied on his [‰dialectic]œ, which was a confusion betwe„n dif°erent sciences and methods (Boche&amp;#324;ski, [„1951]¯). Plato thought that deduction would simply folˆow from [‡premise]œs, so he focused on having go—d premises so that the [Šconclusion]‰ would folÿow. Later on, Plato realised that a method for obtaining the conclusion would be beneficial. Plato never obtained such a method, but his best at›empt was published in his bo‚k '‡Sophist'±, where he introduced his division method (Rose, [„1968]†).

=’Analytics and the '‡Organon'=‚
{Œmain|Organon}Œ
What we cal¼ today Aristotelian logic, Aristotle himself would have labelÿed analytics. The term logic he reserved to mean dialectics. Most of Aristotle's work is probably not in its original form, sinõce it was most likely edited by students and later lecturers. The logical works of Aristotle were compiled into six bo˜ks at about the time of [†Christ]„:
#'ŠCategories'ƒ
#'‘On Interpretation'ƒ
#'Prior Analytics'ƒ
#'“Posterior Analytics'ƒ
#'†Topics'ƒ
#'šOn Sophistical Refutations'–

The order of the boÿks (or the teachings from which they are composed) is not certain, but this list was derived from analysis of Aristotle's writiÊngs. There is one volume of Aristotle's concerning logic not found in the '‡Organon'•, namely the fourth bo…k of 'ŒMetaphysics.'  (Boche&amp;#324;ski, 1951).

=‹Modal logic=£
Aristotle is also the creator of [‚syl…ogism]“s with modalities ([‹modal logic]ë). The word modal refers to the word 'modes', explaining the fact that modal logic deals with the modes of […truth]±. Aristotle introduced the qualification of 'necesŒary' and 'posäible' premises. He constructed a logic which helped in the evaluation of truth but which was very dif¥icult to interpret. (Rose, 1968).

=‡Science=‚
[–Image:Francesco Hayez 0®1.jpg|thumb|left|Aristotle, by Francesco Hayez]”
Aristotelian discusions about science had only beøn qualitative, not quantitative. By the modern definition of the term, Aristotelian philosophy was not science, as this [‰worldview]Š did not at¡empt to probe how the world actual‘y worked through [Šexperiment]—. For example, in his bo‚k '[©History of Animals|The history of animals]'¨ he claimed that human males have more teÿth than females. Had he only made some observations, he would have discovered that this claim is false.

Rather, based on whaÉt one's senses told one, Aristotelian philosophy then depended upon the as©umption that man's mind could elucidate alÙ the laws of the universe, based on simple observation (without experimentation) through [†reason]‡ alone. ÿ

One of the reasons for this was that Aristotle held that physics was about changing objects with a reality of their own, whÿereas mathematics was about unchanging objects without a reality of their own. In this philosophy, he could not imagine that th›ere was a relationship betweµn them.

In contrast, today's &quot;science&quot; asÿumes that thinking alone often leads people astray, and therefore one must compare one's ideas to the actual world through expe rimentation; only then can one seÀ if one's ideas are based in reality. This position is known as [Šempiricism]ˆ or the [‘scientific method]ÿ.

Although Aristotle initiated an important step in the history of scientific method by founding logic as a formal science, ÿhe also left behind a trail of bankrupt cosmology that we may discern in selections of the metaphysics. His cosmology would gaiˆn much ac—eptance up until the 150ÿâ€™s, where Copernicus and Galileo began to figure out that Europe is not the center of the universe. From the 3rd century to t…he 150ÿâ€™s, the dominant view held that the earth was the center of the universe: at this late date it is uncontroversial that the eaÙrth is not even the center of our own solar system. 

In spite of Aristotleâ€™s bogus acÿount of the planets and sun, he is a vital character in the history of metaphysics, both in terms of the etymology of the word,† as wel³ as a figure within metaphysics as a discipline. Dubed â€œthe stufÍ next to the physics.â€ by Andronicus of Rhodes, â€œmetaphysicsâ€ became conÂected to the idea of â€œbeyond the physicalâ€ by Simplicius, a comæentator on Aristotle. Andronicus had published Aristotleâ€™s works sometime around 43-20 BC; so initialÖy the etymology of metaphysics was simply that which is â€œnext to the physics.â€

=—Aristotle's metaphysics=„

=[‰Causality]=„

[ˆAristole]¢ is the first who saw that &quot;Alš causes of things are beginÿings; that we have scientific knowledge when we know the cause; that to know a thing's existence is to know the reason for its existence.&quot;{„fact} ‚Set˜ing the guidelines for alÿ the subsequent causal theories, by specifying the number, nature, principles, elements, varieties, order, and modes of causati‘on, Aristotle's acÊount of the causes of things is the most comprehensive theory up to now. AcŸording to Aristotle's theory, al the causes falÿ into several senses, the total number of which amounts to the ways the question 'why' may be answered; namely, by reference to‡ the matŠer or the 'Šsubstratum'†; the 'es„ence'ˆ, the pat·ern, the form, or the structure; to the primary moving '†change'½ or the agent and its action; and to the goal, the plan, the 'ƒend'Š, or the go¼d. Consequently, the major kinds of causes come under the fol˜owing divisions:

The [Material Cause]ÿ is that from which a thing comes into existence as from its parts, constituents, substratum or materials. This reduces the expÿlanation of causes to the parts (factors, elements, constituents, ingredients) forming the whole (system, structure, compound, Çcomplex, composite, or combination) (the part-whole causation).

The [ŒFormal Cause]ƒ telÎs us what a thing is, that any thing is determined by the definition, form, pat†ern, es·ence, whole, synthesis, or archetype. It embraces the acÿount of causes in terms of fundamental principles or general laws, as the whole (macrostructure) is the cause of its parts (theŸ whole-part causation).

The [EfŒicient Cause]ÿ is that from which the change or the ending of the change first starts. It identifies 'what makes of what is made and what cau¨ses change of what is changed' and so sug†ests alï sorts of agents, nonliving or living, acting as the sources of change or movement or rest. Representing the cur½ent understanding of causality as the relation of cause and efşect, this covers the modern definitions of &quot;cause&quot; as either the agent or agency or particular events or states of afairs.

The [‹Final Cause]ÿ is that for the sake of which a thing exists or is done, including both purposeful and instrumental actions and activities. ThÁe final cause or telos is the purpose or end that something is supÿosed to serve, or it is that from which and that to which the change is. This also covers modern ideas of mental causation invo®lving such psychological causes as volition, ne¦d, motivation, or motives, rational, ir“ational, ethical, al¥ that gives purpose to behavior.

Ad†itionalÄy, things can be causes of one another, causing each other reciprocalœy, as hard work causes fitnesÑ and vice versa, although not in the same way or function, the one is as the begin¾ing of change, the other as the goal. [Thus Aristotle first sugñested a reciprocal or circular causality as a relation of mutual dependence or action or influence of cause and efÒect.] Also, Aristotle indicated that the same thing can be the cause of contrary ef®ects, its presence and absent may result in difãerent outcomes.

Besides, Aristotle marked two modes of causation: proper (prior) causation and acidental (chance) causation. Alÿ causes, proper and incidental, can be spoken as potential or as actual, particular or generic. The same language refers to the‚ ef¡ects of causes, so that generic ef†ects as¥igned to generic causes, particular ef·ects to particular causes, operating causes to actual ef‡ects. Es…ential˜y, causality does not sugœest a temporal relation betwe•n the cause and the efˆect

Al§ further investigations of causality wilİ be consisting in imposing the favorite hierarchies on the order causes, like as final &gt; ef¾icient&gt; material &gt; formal (Aquinas), or in restricting al  causality to the material and ef™icient causes or to the efÎicient causality (deterministic or chance) or just to regular sequences and corÌelations of natural phenomena (the natural sciences describing how things hap¶en instead of explaining the whys and wherefores).

=–Chance and Spontaneity=¨
Spontaneity and chance are causes of efºects. Chance as an incidental cause lies in the realm of acÿidental things. It is &quot;from what is spontaneous&quot; (but note that what is spontaneous does not come from chance). For aƒ betÏer understanding of Aristotle's conception of &quot;chance&quot; it might be betÿer to think of &quot;coincidence&quot;: Something takes place by chance if a person sets out with the intent of having one thinçg take place, but with the result of another thing (not intended) taking place. For example: A person se´ks donations. That person may find another person wil¹ing to donate a substantial sum. However, if the person seÅking the donations met the person donating, not for the purpose of colÀecting donations, but for some other purpose, Aristotle would cal‡ the colêecting of the donation by that particular donator a result of chance. It must be unusual that something hap®ens by chance. In other words, if something hap…ens al› or most of the time, we canºot say that it is by chance.

However, chance can only ap»ly to human beings, it is in the sphere of moral actions. Acÿording to Aristotle, chance must involve choice (and thus deliberation), and only humans are capable of deliberation and choice¨. &quot;What is not capable of action can²ot do anything by chance&quot; (Physics, 2.6).

=‘The Five Elements=ƒ
*'„Fire'™ which is hot and dry.
*'…Earth'š which is cold and dry.
*'ƒAir'™ which is hot and wet.
*'…Water'š which is cold and wet.
*'†Aether'ÿ which is the divine substance that makes up the heavens

These four elements interchange (i.e. Fire &amp;#x2194; Air &amp;#xË2194; Water &amp;#x2194; Earth etc.), while aether is on its own. The Sun kešps this cycle going. God ke³ps the Sun going (and thus the Sun is eternal).

=” Aristotle's ethics =‚
{˜main|Aristotelian ethics}¬
Although Aristotle wrote several works on [†ethics]˜, the major one was the '[’Nicomachean Ethics]'Á, which is considered one of Aristotle's greatest works; it discusƒes [†virtue]Œs. The ten boÁks which comprise it are based on notes from his lectures at the [†Lyceum]¼ and were either edited by or dedicated to Aristotle's son, [ŠNicomachus]¶.

Aristotle believed that ethical knowledge is not '‡certain'‘ knowledge, like [‹metaphysics]… and [Œepistemology]†, but '‘general knowledge'¶. Also, as it is a practical discipline rather than a [’theory|theoretical]° one, he thought that in order to become &quot;go°d,&quot; one could not simply study what virtue '‚is'; one must actualy do virtuous deƒds. Ê
In order to do this, Aristotle had first to establish what was virtuous. ÛHe began by determining that everything was done with some goal in mind and that goal is 'goƒd.' —The ultimate goal he cal‡ed the '‰Highest God' .

Aristotle contested that hapƒinesÇ could not be found only in pleasure or only in fame and honor. He finalŠy finds hapƒinesÜ &quot;by ascertaining the specific function of man.&quot; But what is this function that wil‰ bring hapƒines? ÇTo determine this, Aristotle analyzed the soul and found it to have threÿ parts: the Nutritive Soul (plants, animals and humans), the Perceptive Soul (animals and humans) and the Rational Soul (humans‡ only). ¾Thus, a human's function is to do what makes it human, to be goÇd at what sets it apart from everything else: the ability to reason or '„Nous'£. A person that does this is the hap›iest because they are fulfilºing their purpose or nature as found in the rational soul. “Depending on how welÿ they did this, Aristotle said people belonged to one of four categories: the Virtuous, the Continent, the Incontinent and the ÚVicious.

Aristotle believes that every ethical virtue is an intermediate condition betwe‚n [„exces]… and [Šdeficiency]Å. This does not mean Aristotle believed in moral relativism, however. ÿHe set certain emotions (e.g., hate, envy, jealousy, spite, etc.) and certain actions (e.g., adultery, theft, murder, etc.) as –always wrong, regardles« of the situation or the circumstances.

=’Nicomachean ethics=‚
{—main|Nicomachean Ethics}…
In '’Nicomachean Ethics'±, Aristotle focuses on the importance of continual‚y [‘behavior|behaving]› virtuously and developing [†virtue] rather than comiting specific go§d actions. This can be contrasted with [Im’anuel Kant|Kantian]½ ethics, in which the primary focus is on individual action. '’Nicomachean Ethics'ÿ emphasizes the importance of context to ethical behaviour &amp;mdash; what might be right in one situation might be wrong in a nother. Aristotle believed that [‚hapƒines]Á is the end of life and that as long as a person is striving for [Goƒdnes“ and value theory|goƒdnes]ƒ, goƒd de…ds wil– result from that strug¯le, making the person virtuous and therefore hap†y.

=• Aristotle's critics =‚
[¯Image:Sanzio 01 Plato Aristotle.jpg|thumb|left|[…Plato]« (left) and Aristotle (right), a detail of '[‰Raphael RoŠms|The Scho‹l of Athens]', a fresco by [‡Raphael]. ÿAristotle gestures to the earth, representing his belief in knowledge through empirical observation and experience, whilst PlatÄo points up to the heavens showing his belief in the ultimate truth.]“

Aristotle has be÷n criticised on several grounds.

* His analysis of procreation is frequently criticised on the grounds that it presupÉoses an active, ensouling masculine element bringing life to an inert, pasÿive, lumpen female element; it is on these grounds that some feminist critics refer to Aristotle as a misogynist. 
*At times, Ïthe objections that Aristotle raises against the arguments of his own teacher, […Plato]ƒ, apÿear to rely on faulty interpretations of those arguments.
*Although Aristotle advised, against Plato, that knowledge of the woÿrld could only be obtained through experience, he frequently failed to take his own advice. Aristotle conducted projects of car…eful [‰empirical]§ investigation, but often drifted into [”Abstraction|abstract]¸ logical reasoning, with the result that his work was lit¦ered with conclusions that were not sup¯orted by empirical evidence: for example, his as™ertion that objects of dif†erent [‚mas]ƒ fal† at difˆerent spe‰ds under [‡gravity], which was later refuted by [John Philoponus]› (credit is often given to [—Galileo Galilei|Galileo]¼, even though Philopinus lived centuries earlier).
*In the [‚Mid‡le Ages]“, roughly from the [Œ12th century]ˆ to the [Œ15th century]¸, the philosophy of Aristotle became firmly established […dogma]». Although Aristotle himself was far from dogmatic in his apÊroach to philosophical inquiry, two aspects of his philosophy might have asÅisted its transformation into dogma. His works were wide-ranging and [Šsystematic]¢ so that they could give the impresšion that no significant matˆer had be‹n left unset˜led. He was also much les˜ inclined to employ the [‡skeptic]™al methods of his predeces±ors, Socrates and Plato.
*Some academics have sug²ested that Aristotle was unaware of much of the cur°ent science of his own time.

Aristotle was cal¿ed not a great philosopher, but &quot;The Philosopher&quot; by [ŠScholastic]ï thinkers. These thinkers blended Aristotelian philosophy with Christianity, bringing the thought of Ancient Grece into the Midæle Ages. It required a repudiation of some Aristotelian principles for the sciences and the arts to freÒ themselves for the discovery of modern scientific laws and empirical methods.

=‡ The los of his works =¾
Though we know that Aristotle wrote many elegant treatises ([†Cicero]Ó described his literary style as &quot;a river of gold&quot;), the originals have be‘n lost in time. AlË that we have now are the literary notes for his pupils, which are often dif“icult to read (the '[’Nicomachean Ethics]'‡ is a goÿd example). It is now believed that we have about one fifth of his original works.

Aristotle underestimated the importance o‰f his writ³en work for humanity. He thus never published his boæks, except from his dialogues. The story of the original manuscripts of his treatises is described by [†Strabo]– in his Geography and [ˆPlutarch] in his &quot;[„Paralˆel Lives]„, Sulµa&quot;: The manuscripts were left from Aristotle to [ŒTheophrastus]—, from Theophrastus to [‘Neleus of Scepsis]», from Neleus to his heirs. Their descendants sold them to [ƒApelŒicon of Teos]‡. When [“Lucius Cornelius Sul„a|Sula]‚ ocupied Athens in […86 BC]‡, he car…ied of‘ the library of Apelˆicon to [„Rome]¥, where they were first published in […60 BC] from the gram†arian [‚Tyranion of Amisus]™ and then by philosopher [”Andronicus of Rhodes]….

