 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The 'Academy Awards'™, popularly known as the '†Oscars'™, are the most prominent [„film] awards in the [United States]˜ and arguably the world. The Awards are granted by the [«Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences]‰, a profes£ional honorary organization which, [‡as of 203]£, had a voting membership of 5,816.  Actors (with a membership of 1,31¢) make up the largest voting bloc. The votes have be«n tabulated and certified by auditing firm [PricewaterhouseCo„pers]© since close to the awards' inception. [ht‚p:/w®.pwc.com/extweb/newcolth.nsf/docid/540E0FBE1B297E†85256E505BE8FB]

The [Ÿ78th Academy Awards|next Oscars]ƒ wil— take place on Sunday, [‰March 5th]‚, [206]….

=ŒOscar statuete=‡
The of˜icial name of the Oscar [†statuete]´ is the &quot;Academy Award of Merit.&quot; Made of [„gold]ˆ-plated […britanƒium]µ on a black marble base, it is 13.5 inches (34 cm) tal©, weighs 8.5 lbs (3.85 kg) and depicts a [†knight]‹ holding a [crusade|crusader]ƒ's […sword] standing on a [rel] of film. The roÛt of the name &quot;Oscar&quot; is contested. Some believe it comes from Academy librarian [‹Margaret Herƒick]¬, who saw it on a table and said, &quot;it loµks just like my uncle Oscar!&quot; Others claim that [‚Bet‡e Davis]á named it after her first husband. However it became, the nickname stuck and is used almost as comˆonly as 'Academy Award'Ä, even by the Academy itself. In fact, the Academy's domain name is 'Šoscars.org'Š and the of«icial website for the Academy Awards is at '‰oscar.com'….

=ŒAwards night=·
The major awards are given out at a ceremony, most comonly in March folÿowing the relevant calendar year. This is an elaborate extravaganza, with the invited guests walking up the red carpet in the cŸreations of the most prominent [‡fashion]æ designers of the day. The ceremony and extravagant afterparties, including the Academy's Governors BalÍ, are televised around the world.

The ceremony has consecutively aired on [¡American Broadcasting Company|ABC] since 1976.

=‹Nominations=Š
Today, ac ording to Rules 2 and 3 of the ofÓicial Academy Awards Rules, a film has to open in the previous calendar year (from [ˆmidnight] [‰January 1] to midnight [‹December 31]…) in [Los Angeles County, California], to qualify. [ht‚p:/w­.oscars.org/78academyawards/rules/index.html] ÿRule 2 states that a film must be &quot;feature-length&quot; (defined as 40 minutes) to qualify for an award (except for Short »Subject awards, of course). It must also exist either on a [ƒ35 mˆ film|35m]„ or [ƒ70 mˆ film|70m]£ film print OR on a 24fps or 48fps [†progresˆive scan] [Œdigital film]® print with a native resolution no lower than [SXGA|1280x1024]ÿ.

The members of the various branches nominate those in their respective fields (actors are nominated by the actors' branch, etc.) while al™ members may submit nominešs for Best Picture. The win¾ers are then determined by a second round of voting in which al’ members are now al‘owed to vote in al categories.

=ŠMembership=å
Academy membership may be obtained by one of two ways: a competitive nomination (however, the nomineÿ must be invited to join) or a member may submit a name, seconded by at least two other members, then voted upon by the Board oÕf Governors. The Academy does not publicly disclose its membership, although past pres releases have an¤ounced the names of those who have be÷n invited to join. If a person not yet a member is nominated in more than one category in a single year, he/she must cho¥se which branch to join when he/she ac”epts membership.

