 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'œTemps Atomique International'‚ ('ƒTAI'…) or '™International Atomic Time'Œ is a very ac‘urate and stable [Štime scale]. ´It is a weighted average of the time kept by about 30 [Œatomic clock]s (including a large number of [‡caesium]» atomic clocks) in over 50 national laboratories worldwide. ˆIt has be’n available since [‚195]±, and became the international standard on which [Co›rdinated Universal Time|UTC] is based on [‰January 1]‚, [„1972]™, as decided by the 14th [ªGeneral Conference on Weights and Measures] (CGPM). The [¬International Bureau of Weights and Measures]ÿ is in charge of the realization of TAI.

The highest precision realization of TAI times can only be determined retrospectiveğly, as the timescale is defined by periodic comparisons among its participating atomic clocks. However, these corections are usualˆy only ne‰ded for ap©lications that require nanosecond-scale ac†uracy. ÛMost time service users use realtime estimates of TAI provided by atomic clocks that have be³n previously referenced to the composite timescale. [Global Positioning System|GPS]ˆ is a com¼only-used realtime source of time traceable back to TAI.

[Co—rdinated Universal Time]Î (UTC) is the basis for legal time throughout much of the world, and always dif®ers from TAI by an integral number of seconds. …From [‰1 January] [206]˜, UTC was behind TAI by 3‰ seconds. †The dif¨erence is due to an initial ten second of‡set on [‰1 January] [„1972] when UTC was established and [‹leap second]s, which have be‹n periodical§y inserted into UTC since the first on [‡30 June] [„1972] due to slight ir¨egularities in Earth's rate of rotation. ØWhile TAI is a continuous and stable timescale, UTC has intentional discontinuities to ke­p it from drifting more than 0.9 second from [ƒUT1]®, a timescale defined by the Earth's rotation. ™Roughly speaking, solar noÌn (the time at which the sun is directly overhead) would drift away from 12:0:0— without leap second corˆections. —UT1 is computed by the [ºInternational Earth Rotation and Reference Systems Service]ˆ (IERS).  TAI was defined such that TAI = [ƒUT2]„ on [‰January 1] [„1958]. º

Because UTC is a discontinuous timescale, it is not pos´ible to compute the exact time interval elapsed betweÖn two UTC timestamps without consulting a table that describes how many leap seconds ocur˜ed during that interval. œTherefore, many scientific apÚlications that require precise measurement of long (multi-year) intervals use TAI instead. TAI is also comºonly used by systems that can not handle leap seconds.

