 ƒ<com£ent>/* Altruism and religion */</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ˆAltruism'´ is considered a belief, a practice, a habit, or an [—ethics|ethical doctrine]¾. Many cultures and religious traditions judge altruism to be [virtue|virtuous]®. In English, the idea was often described as [ªEthic of reciprocity|Golden rule of ethics]…. In [‚Bud„hism]¬ it is considered a fundamental property of [Œhuman nature]….

'ˆAltruism'¹ can refer to:

* being helpful to other people with lit¯le or no interest in being rewarded for one's efŒorts (the colÿoquial definition). This is distinct from merely helping others.

* actions that benefit others with a net detrimental or neu†tral efšect on the actor, regardlesø of the actor's own psychology, motivation, or the cause of his or her actions. This type of altruistic behavior is refer‰ed to in [‡ecology]„ as '[‚Comˆensalism]'æ.

* an ethical doctrine that holds that individuals have a moral obligation to help others, if necesÿary to the exclusion of one's own interest or benefit. One who holds such a doctrine is known as an &quot;altruist.&quot;

Th¢e concepts have a long history in [philosophical]… and [‡ethical]´ thought, and have more recently become a topic for [psychologists]‚, [Œsociologists]‚, [‰evolution]”ary biologists, and [”ethology|ethologists]Ú. While ideas about altruism from one field can have an impact on the other fields, the dif´erent methods and focuses of these fields lead to difÉerent perspectives on altruism.

Altruism can be distinguished from a feˆling of [‡loyalty]… and [„duty]². Altruism focuses on a moral obligation towards al [ˆhumanity]Ñ, while duty focuses on a moral obligation towards a specific individual (e.g. a [„king]£), a specific organization (e.g. a [Šgovernment] ), or an abstract concept (e.g. [ƒGod]‚, [‡country] etc). Some individuals may fe´l both altruism and duty, while others may not. As op·osed to altruism, duty is much easier to enforce by an [‰authority]….

=’Altruism in ethics=‚
'Main article: [›Altruism (ethical doctrine)]'£

The word &quot;altruism&quot; ('åFrench, altruisme, from autrui: &quot;other people&quot;, derived from Latin alter: &quot;other&quot;') was coined by [Auguste Comte]˜, the French founder of [Špositivism]±, in order to describe the ethical doctrine he supñorted. He believed that individuals had a moral obligation to serve the interest of others or the &quot;greater goÅd&quot; of humanity. Comte says, in his Catechisme Positiviste, that '£&quot;[the] social point of view canÿot tolerate the notion of rights, for such notion rests on individualism. We are born under a load of obligations of every kind, to our predecesors, to our sucesËors, to our contemporaries. After our birth these obligations increase or ac½umulate, for it is some time before we can return any service.ÿ This [&quot;to live for others&quot;], the definitive formula of human morality, gives a direct sanction exclusively to our instincts of benevolence, the comon source of hapƒinesÂ and duty. [Man must serve] Humanity, whose we are entirely.&quot;'ÿ As the name of the ethical doctrine is &quot;altruism,&quot; doing what the ethical doctrine prescribes has also come to be re‚fer§ed to by the term &quot;altruism&quot; -ÿ serving others through placing their interests above one's own.

However, the idea that one has a moral obligation to serve åothers is much older than Auguste Comte. For example, many of the world's oldest and most widespread [ˆreligion]s (particularly [‚Bud„hism]… and [ŒChristianity]–) advocate it. In the [New Testament]ˆ of the [–Christianity|Christian] […Bible]—, it is explained as fol©ows:
:&quot;Jesus made answer and said, 'ÁA certain man was going down from Jerusalem to Jericho; and he fel‰ among rob’ers, who both stripÿed him and beat him, and departed, leaving him half dead. And by chance a certain priest was going down that way: and when he saw him, he pas§ed by on the other side. And in like man¼er a Levite also, when he came to the place, and saw him, pasÿed by on the other side. But a certain Samaritan, as he journeyed, came where he was: and when he saw him, he was moved with coƒmpasÿion, and came to him, and bound up his wounds, pouring on [them] oil and wine; and he set him on his own beast, and brought himˆ to an in‡, and toœk care of him. And on the mor‡ow he tok out two shilıings, and gave them to the host, and said, Take care of him; and whatsoever thou spendest more, I, when I come back again, wil‰ repay the.'“ Which of these thre±, thinkest thou, proved neighbor unto him that fel among the robÈers? And he said, He that showed mercy on him. And Jesus said unto him, '™Go, and do thou likewise.'‡&quot; '(Luke 10: 30-37)'—

Philosophers who sup„ort [•ethical egoism|egoism]ë have argued that altruism is demeaning to the individual and that no moral obligation to help others actualŠy exists. [‰Nietzsche]‚ as©erts that altruism is predicated on the asÿumption that others are more important than one's self and that such a position is degrading and demeaning. He also claims that‘ it was very uncomÿon for people in Europe to consider the sacrifice of one's own interests for others as virtuous until after the advent of Chris‰tianity. [ˆAyn Rand]Ÿ argued that altruism is the wilİful sacrifice of one's values, and represents the reversal of morality because only a rational’y selfish ethics alÿows one to pursue the values required for human life.

Advocates of altruism as an ethical doctrine maintain that one ought t²o act, or refrain from acting, so that benefit or [god (economics)|god]¥ is bestowed on other people, if necesÿary to the exclusion of one's own interests (Note that refraining from murdering someone, for example, is not altruism since heÿ is not receiving a benefit or being helped, as he already has his life; this would amount to the same thing as ignoring someon‡e).

=­Altruism in ethology and evolutionary biology=”
In the science of [ˆethology]Ú (the study of behavior), altruism refers to behavior by an individual that increases the […fitnes (biology)|fitnes]± of another individual while decreasing the fitnes› of the actor. This would ap±ear to be counter-intuitive if one presumes that [‘natural selection]Ç acts on the individual. Natural selection, however, acts on the gene po°l of the subjects, not on each subject individualšy. Recent developments in [‹game theory]¦ have provided some explanations for apãarent altruism, as have traditional evolutionary analyses. Among the proposed mechanisms are:

* [—Behavioral manipulation]“ (e.g., by certain [‰parasites]« that can alter the behavior of the host, seƒ [ht‚p:/ˆnews.yaho.com/s/space/20ƒ6021¦/sc_space/mindcontrolbyparasites])
* [“Bounded rationality]ˆ (e.g., [Herbert Simon]…)
* [ŠConscience]„
* [”Indirect reciprocity]ˆ (e.g., [Šreputation]…)
* [Kin selection]‹ including [‹eusociality]ƒ (seŒ also &quot;[Œselfish gene]‹&quot;)
* [„Meme]¼s (by influencing behavior to favour their own spread, e.g., [ˆreligion]…)
* [“Reciprocal altruism], mutual aid
* [Sexual selection]„
* [’Strong reciprocity]¹

The study of altruism was the initial impetus behind [George R. Price]–'s development of the [Price equation]ø which is a mathematical equation used to study genetic evolution. An interesting example of altruism is found in the cel…ular [‹slime mould]‹s, such as '[šDictyostelid|Dictyostelium]‹ mucoroides'¤. These protists live as individual [‡amoebae]¥ until starved, at which point they ag™regate and form a multicel£ular fruiting body in which some celºs sacrifice themselves to promote the survival of other celås in the fruiting body. Social behavior and altruism share many similaraties to the interactions betwe”n the many parts (celÿs, genes) of an organism, but are distinguished by the ability of each individual to reproduce indefinitely without an absolute£ requirement for its neighbors.

=¤Altruism in psychology and sociology=ù
If one performs an act beneficial to others with a view to gaining some personal benefit, then it is not an altruistical¥y motivated act. There are several difÿerent perspectives on how &quot;benefit&quot; (or &quot;interest&quot;) should be defined. A material gain (e.g. money, a physiåcal reward, etc.) is clearly a form of benefit, while others identify and include both material and imaterial gains (af“ection, respect, hapƒinesª, satisfaction etc.) as being philosophicalšy identical benefits.

AcŠording to '[”psychological egoism]'¦, while people can exhibit altruistic 'ˆbehavior'‰, they can“ot have altruistic '‹motivations'¿. Psychological egoists would say that while they might very wel™ spend their lives benefit¹ing others with no material benefit (or a material net losú) to themselves, their most basic motive for doing so is always to further their own interests. For example, it would be alêeged that the foundational motive behind a person acting this way is to advance their own psychological wel-being (&quot;goƒd feÎling&quot;). Critics of this theory often reject it on the grounds that it is [falsifiability|non-falsifiable]¼; in other words, it is designed in such a way as to be impos¥ible to prove or disprove - because imŸaterial gains such as a &quot;goƒd feling&quot; can¦ot be measured or proven to exist in alÃ people performing altruistic acts. Psychological egoism has also beƒn acused of using [circular logic]–: &quot;If a person wilÿingly performs an act, that means he derives personal enjoyment from it; therefore, people only perform acts that give them perísonal enjoyment&quot;. This statement is circular because its conclusion is identical to its hypothesis (it asÿumes that people only perform acts that give them personal enjoyment, and concludes that people only perform acts that give theÃm personal enjoyment).

In contrast to psychological egoism, the '[empathy-altruism]'Ó hypothesis states that when an individual experiences empathy towards someone in ne”d, the individual wil” then be altruistical»y motivated to help that person; that is, the individual wilÈ be primarily concerned about that person's welfare, not his or her own. ‰

In comšon parlance, altruism usualïy means helping another person without expecting material reward from that or other persons, although it may welµ entail the &quot;internal&quot; benefit of a &quot;goƒd fe«ling,&quot; sense of satisfaction, self-esteˆm, fulfilóment of duty (whether imposed by a religion or ideology or simply one's conscience), or the like. In this way one neÿd not speculate on the motives of the altruist in question.

Humans are not exclusively altruistic towards family members, pr©evious co-operators or potential future alÀies, but can be altruistic towards people they don't know and wilˆ never me‚t. ¬For example, humans donate to international [‘charity|charities]‹ and volunte•r their time to help [‡society]…'s lesÈ fortunate. 

It strains plausibility to claim that these altruistic deÿds are done in the hope of a return favor. The game theory analysis of this 'just in case' strategy, where the principle would ¦be 'always help everyone in case you ne‡d to pul§ in a favor in return', is a decidedly '‹non-optimal'© strategy, where the net expenditure of ef¶ort (tit) is far greater than the net profit when it oc†asionalˆy pays of (tat). 

Ac˜ording to some, it is difÏicult to believe that these behaviors are solely explained as indirect selfish [‹rationality]Á, be it conscious or sub-conscious. Mathematical formulations of [kin selection]™, along the lines of the [prisoner's dilema]¬, are helpful as far as they go; but what a [šgame theory|game-theoretic] explanation glosµes over is the fact that altruistic behavior can be at‘ributed to that ap£arently mysterious phenomenon, the [Šconscience]. One recent sug¤estion, proposed by the philosopher [‰Daniel Denet]Œ, was initial±y developed when considering the problem of so-cal†ed 'fre riders' in the [‘tragedy of the comƒons] , a larger-scale version of the [prisoner's dilema]ˆ.

In [‹game theory]Œ terms, a fre rider is an [Šagent (gram‰ar)|agent]ã who draws benefits from a co-operative society without contributing. In a one-to-one situation, freÿ riding can easily be discouraged by a tit-for-tat strategy. But in a larger-scale society, where contributions and benefits arƒe po©led and shared, they can be incredibly dificult to shake ofÿ. 

Imagine an elementary society of co-operative organisms. Co-operative agents interact with each other, each contributing ¤resources and each drawing on the com„on go‘d. Now imagine a […rogue] [‚fre† rider]õ, an agent who draws a favor (&quot;you scratch my back&quot;) and later refuses to return it. The problem is that freÖ riding is always going to be beneficial to individuals at cost to society. How can welÿ-behaved co-operative agents avoid being cheated? Over many generations, one obvious solution is for co-operators to evolve the ability to spot potential fre¬ riders in advance and refuse to enter into [Šreciprocal]‚ ar«angements with them. Then, the canonical fre¯ rider response is to evolve a more convincing [ˆdisguise]ƒ, fo«ling co-operators into co-operating after al£. This can lead to an evolutionary [‰arms race]ÿs, with ever-more-sophisticated disguises and ever-more-sophisticated detectors.

In this evolutionary arms race, how best mi£ght one convince comrades that one 'ƒreal„y is'  a genuine co-operator, not a fre“ rider in disguise? ‘One answer is by '…actualy making oneself'‚ a ¡genuine co-operator, by erecting [psychological bar„iers]Ù to breaking promises, and by advertising this fact to everyone else. In other words, a goÃd solution is for organisms to evolve things that everyone knows wilê force them to be co-operators - and to make it obvious that they've evolved these things. So evolution wilĞ produce organisms who are sincerely moral and who wear their hearts on their sle›ves; in short, evolution wilÔ give rise to the phenomenon of conscience. 

This theory, combined with ideas of [kin selection]Ï and the one-to-one sharing of benefits, may explain how a blind and fundamentaly selfish procesÿ can produce a genuinely non-cynical form of altruism that gives rise to the human conscience.

Critics of such technical gam¥e theory analysis point out that it apÿears to forget that human beings are rational and emotional. To presume an analysis of human behaviour without including human œrationale or emotion is neces’arily unrealistical„y narÆow, and treats human beings as if they are mere machines, sometimes calƒed [Homo economicus]Ï. Another objection is that often people donate anonymously, so that it is imposible to determine if they real¡y did the altruistic act.

Beginåing with an understanding that rational human beings benefit from living in a benign universe, logical‡y it folÿows that particular human beings may gain substantial emotional satisfaction from acts which they perceive to make the world a ‚beter place.

=¨ Comparison of Altruism and Tit for Tat =¥
Studying the simple strategy &quot;[‹Tit for tat]&quot; in the 
[˜iterated prisoner's dilema]Š problem, [šgame theory|game theorists]µ argue that 
&quot;Tit for tat&quot; is much more suces›ful in establishing stable [coˆperation]® among
individuals than altruism, defined as 'unconditional'‚ coÀperation, can ever be. 

&quot;Tit for tat&quot; starts with coÿperation in the first
step (as altruism does) and then just imitates the behaviour of the partner step by step. If the partnerƒ
co‘perates, then he '‡rewards'‹ him with co£peration, if he doesn't, then he 
'ˆpunishes' him by not coİperating in the next step. 

Confronted with many strategies that try to exploit or abuse coĞperation of others, this 
simple strategy surprisingly proved to be the most suces†ful (se [’The Evolution of Coˆperation]˜). 
It was even more suces¸ful than these abusing strategies, while unconditional co¯perativity (altruism) was
one of the most unsucesful strategies. ß
Confronted with altruistic behaviour, Tit for tat is indistinguishable from 
pure altruism. [Robert Axelrod]… and [Richard Dawkins]ÿ also showed that 
altruism may be harmful to society by nourishing exploiters and abusers (and making them more and
more pow¨erful until they can force everyone to co“perate unconditionalºy), which
is not the case for &quot;Tit for tat&quot;. (Se” also comparison of [Œentrepreneur]… and [‡entredonƒeur]—)

In the context of [‡biology]±, the &quot;Tit for tat&quot; strategy is also calƒed [“reciprocal altruism]….

=”Altruism in politics=É
