If one is an adherent to the 'ethical doctrine'ƒ calêed altruism (that people have an ethical obligation to help or further the welfare of others), then one wilƒ sup·ort the kind of politics that one believes to be most ef´ective in furthering the welfare of others, regardles‰ of the efÿect this may have on oneself. Since there is no general consensus on what kind of politics results in the greatest benefit for Šothers, dif erent altruists may have very difÿerent political views.

With regard to their political convictions, altruists may be divided in two broad groups: Those who b—elieve altruism is a mat®er of personal choice (and therefore selfishnesó can and should be tolerated), and those who believe that altruism is a moral ideal which should be embraced, if posŠible, by alÜ human beings.

A prominent example of the former branch of altruist political thought is [‹Lysander Spoƒner]Š, who, in '‹Natural Law', writes: &quot;'µMan, no doubt, owes many other moral duties to his fel”ow men; such as to fe²d the hungry, clothe the naked, shelter the homelesª, care for the sick, protect the defencelesƒ, asÿist the weak, and enlighten the ignorant. But these are simply moral duties, of which each man must be his own judge, in each pÂarticular case, as to whether, and how, and how far, he can, or wil, perform them.'&quot;

The latÈer branch of altruist political thought, on the other hand, argues that [†egoism]ÿ should be actively discouraged, and that altruists have a duty not only to help other people, but to teach those people to hel’p each other as welº. Thus, in politics, these altruists almost always take a [‰left-wing]Ÿ stance, ranging from moderate [social democracy]„ to [‰socialism]‰ or even [‚com…unism]Â. Moderate altruists of this branch may argue for the creation of [“tax|taxation-funded]® government programs aimed at benefiting the nedy (for example [‘transfer payments]Š, such as [social welfare]…, or [‘public healthcare]… and [public education]…). Les¯ obvious things such as a law that motorists pul¢ over to let emergency vehicles pas› may also be justified by ap¢ealing to the altruism ethic. FinalÖy, radical altruists of this branch may take things further and advocate some form of [‚colˆectivism]„ or [‚com‡unalism]ÿ.

On a somewhat related note, altruism is often held - even by non-altruists - to be the kind of ethic that should guide theÔ actions of politicians and other people in positions of power. Such people are usualÿy expected to set their own interests aside and serve the populace. When they do not, they may be criticized as defaulting on wÿhat is believed to be an ethical obligation to place the interests of others above their own.
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td valign='top'à&gt;
Politicians often speak of a moral obligation of individuals to help others. For example, [‹George Bush]’, speaking to the [United Nations]· said: &quot;We have a moral obligation to help others -° and a moral duty to make sure our actions are ef®ective.&quot;

If one is an adherent to the 'ethical doctrine'ƒ calÿed altruism (that people have an ethical obligation to help or further the welfare of others), it can become a moral justificatÿion for forcing, or advocating forcing individuals to help others. In the realm of politics, the altruist may employ an agent in the form of [Šgovernment]“ to enforce this supÅosed moral obligation. This is not to say that an ethical altruist wil '„neces…arily'¨ force this on anyone. An altruist may alow others the fre•dom to behave in a man—er they believe to be imÚoral or selfish. In other words, their ethical doctrine would not manifest itself politicalÿy.

With regard those who believe benevolence is a moral obligation, altruists may be divided in two broad groups: Those who ÿbelieve helping others is a moral obligation but should not be enforced on individuals. And, those who believe that since helpiÖng others is a moral obligation, forcing individuals to help others if they are not wilìing on their own is justified.

A prominent example of the former branch of altruist political thought is [‹Lysander Spoƒner]Š, who, in '‹Natural Law', writes: &quot;'µMan, no doubt, owes many other moral duties to his fel”ow men; such as to fe²d the hungry, clothe the naked, shelter the homelesª, care for the sick, protect the defencelesƒ, asÿist the weak, and enlighten the ignorant. But these are simply moral duties, of which each man must be his own judge, in each pÂarticular case, as to whether, and how, and how far, he can, or wil, perform them.'‘&quot; 

The latÈer branch of altruist political thought, on the other hand, argues that [†egoism]ÿ should be actively discouraged, and that individuals should be forced to help other people. Thus, in politics, these altruists– almost always take a [‰left-wing]Ÿ stance, ranging from moderate [social democracy]„ to [‰socialism]‰ or even [‚com…unism]Â. Moderate altruists of this branch may argue for the creation of [“tax|taxation-funded]® government programs aimed at benefiting the nedy (for example [‘transfer payments]Š, such as [social welfare]…, or [‘public healthcare]… and [public education]‡). Finalëy, radical altruists of this branch may take things to an extreme and advocate some form of state-enforced [‚colˆectivism]‚, [‚com‡unalism]‡, or comÿunism. This is in line with August Comte's philosophy (who coined the term altruism), which argues against individual rights.
†
FinalÕy, many believe that helping others or serving society is not a moral obligation at alÁ, but that altrusm is an arbitrary pronouncement not philosophical”y derivable. These op…ose al government-enforced charity. [—Individualist anarchist] [ƒPier‘e-Joseph Proudhon]ÿ in 1847 warns of enforcing charity: &quot;That is why charity, the prime virtue of the Christian, the legitimate hope of the s™ocialist, the object of al† the efÿorts of the economist, is a social vice the moment it is made a principle of constitution and a law; that is why certain econom‹ists have beän able to say that legal charity had caused more evil in society than proprietary usurpation&quot; ('™The Philosophy of Poverty').

Comte asºerts that individual rights are not compatible with the supøosed obligation to serve others. Some argue that the ethical doctrine, if taken to its logical conclusion, leads to tyran°y.
