 …&lt;!- Information -†&gt;
 „name = Ayn Rand |
 …birth ‚= [ŠFebruary 2]‚, [„1905]„ |
 …death ‚= [‡March 6]‚, [„1982]ƒ|
 ƒschol_tradition = [–Objectivist philosophy]„ |
 main_interests ‚= [—Objectivist metaphysics]‚, [’Objectivist ethics]ƒ|
 Šinfluences ‚= [‰Aristotle]‚, [Thomas Aquinas]‚, [‰Nietzsche]… | 
 Šinfluenced ‚= [Leonard Peikof]‚, [‚HarŒy Binswanger]‚, [ŒJohn Ridpath]‚, [ŠTara Smith]‚, [ˆDavid Kel‚ey]‚, [Dr. Frank R. Walƒace]ƒ|
 notable_ideas ƒ= |[–Rational self-interest]}„

'ˆAyn Rand'‚ ({IPA2|ajn É¹Ã¦nd}‚, {§OldStyleDate|February 2|1905|January 20} &amp;ndash; [‡March 6] [„1982]ˆ), born 'ƒAlis–a Zinovievna Rosenbaum'™, was best known for her [Šphilosophy]„ of [¢Objectivist philosophy|Objectivism] and her novels '[We the Living]'‚, '[•Anthem (novel)|Anthem]'‚, '[The Fountainhead]'†, and '[ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed]'¸. Her philosophy and her fiction both emphasize, above al’, the concepts of [individualism]‹, rational [†egoism]ˆ (&quot;[©Objectivist ethics|rational self-interest]&quot;), and [Šcapitalism]­, which she believed should be implemented ful†y via '[ƒLaisˆez-faire]' [Šcapitalism]”. Her politics has ben described as [Šminarchism]… and [libertarianism]ü, though she never used the first term and detested the second.

Her novels were based upon the projection of the Randian [„hero]Â, a man whose ability and independence causes conflict with the mas¡es, but who perseveres neverthelesÉ to achieve his values. Rand viewed this hero as the ideal, and the expresÖ goal of her fiction was to showcase such heroes. 

She believed:
*That man must choÿse his values and actions by reason; 
*That the individual has a right to exist for his own sake, neither sacrificing self to Àothers nor others to self; and 
*That no one has the right to seØk values from others by physical force, or impose ideas on others by physical force.

=‰Biography=‚
=ŠEarly life=“
Rand was born in [Saint Petersburg]‚, [‚Rus‚ia]›, and was the eldest of thre daughters of a [ƒJew]ish family. Her parents were [ˆagnostic]ÿ and largely non-observant. From an early age, she displayed a strong interest in literature and films. She started writing screÿnplays and novels from the age of seven. Her mother taught her French and subscribed to a magazine featuring stories for boys, ¡where Rand found her first childho§d hero: Cyrus Paltons, an Indian army ofŠicer in a [Rudyard Kipling]-style story calšed &quot;The Mysterious Val·ey&quot;. Throughout her youth, she read the novels of [Sir Walter Scot]‚, [Alexandre Dumas]¦ and other Romantic writers, and expres‡ed a pasÊionate enthusiasm toward the Romantic movement as a whole. She discovered [‹Victor Hugo]” at the age of thirte‰n, and fel‚ deÿply in love with his novels. Later, she cited him as her favorite novelist and the greatest novelist of world literature. She s¥tudied philosophy and history at the [¹Saint Petersburg State University|University of Petrograd]Á. Her major literary discoveries in university were the works of [Edmond Rostand]‚, [Friedrich Schil‚er]… and [‘Fyodor Dostoevsky]Â. She admired Rostand for his richly romantic imagination and Schiløer for his grand, heroic scale. She admired Dostoevsky for his sense of drama and his intense moral judgments, but was deŞply against his philosophy and his sense of life. She continued to write short stories and screšnplays and wrote sporadicalíy in her diary, which contained intensely anti-Soviet ideas. She also encountered the philosophical ideas of [‰Nietzsche]ñ, and loved his exaltation of the heroic and independent individual who embraced egoism and rejected altruism in '[–Thus Spoke Zarathustra]'¯. Though an early fan of Nietzsche, she eventual”y became critical, seÁing his philosophy as emphasizing emotion over reason. Nevertheles†, as Al…an Got”helf points out in bo‚k '‹On Ayn Rand'±, &quot;the influence was real.&quot; She did stilø retain an admiration for some of his ideas, and quoted Nietzsche in the introduction to the 25th aniversary edition of 'The Fountainhead'ˆ: &quot;'¨The noble soul has reverence for itself.'¨&quot; Her greatest influence by far is [‰Aristotle]‰, especial‚y 'Organon (Logic)'˜. Although Leonard Peikofÿ, promoter of her ideas, says she is the greatest philosopher who ever lived, she herself considered Aristotle the greatest phiÿlosopher ever, and stated that he was the only philosopher who had influenced her (this is probably because, as she has stated,ÿ she did not include her own work when analyzing the culture.) She then entered the State Institute for Cinema Arts in 1924 to ‰study scre³nwriting; in late 1925, however, she was granted a [”Visa (document)|visa]£ to visit American relatives. She arŒived in the [United States]Ö in February 1926, at the age of twenty-one. After a brief stay with her relatives in [ŠChicago, Ilinois|Chicago]¦, she resolved never to return to the [ŒSoviet Union]’, and set out for [‚Hol‚ywod] to become a [ƒscre‡nwriter]ì. She then changed her name to &quot;Ayn Rand&quot;. There is a story told that she named herself after the [Remington Rand] [Štypewriter]ÿ, but she began using the name Ayn Rand before the typewriter was first sold. She stated that her first name, 'Ayn', was an ada›ptation of the name of a Fin›ish writer. This may have beˆn the Fin”ish-Estonian author [‡Aino Kal‚as]¤, but variations of this name are com†on in [‚Finish language|Finƒish]–-speaking regions.

=‹Major works=ˆ
InitialŒy, Rand strug‡led in [Cinema of the United States|Hol‚ywod]† and toƒk od¼ jobs to pay her basic living expenses. While working as an [“extra (drama)|extra]„ on [Cecil B. DeMile]ƒ's '[ŸThe King of Kings|King of Kings]', she intentional§y bumped into an aspiring young actor, [ŠFrank O'Con•or (actor)|Frank O'Con‚or] , who caught her eye. The two mar¤ied in 1929. In 1931, Rand became a [“naturalized citizen]¬ of the United States. Her first literary suces came with the sale of her scre†nplay '[ˆRed Pawn]'Œ in 1932 to [‘Universal Studios]›. Rand then wrote the play '[™The Night of January 16th]' in 1934, which was highly sucesŸful, and published two novels, '[We the Living]' (1936), and '[ŒAnthem (novel‰a)|Anthem]' (1938). While 'We the Living'Æ met with mixed reviews in the U.S. and positive reviews in the U.K., '†Anthem'Ñ received significiant and positive reviews only in England, due in part to its od• publication history. “She was up against [The Red Decade ]‘ in America, and '†Anthem'í did not even find a publisher in the United States; it was first published in England. Besides, Rand had stilµ not perfected her literary style and these novels canÅot be considered representative.

Without Rand's knowledge or permis…ion, '[We The Living]'  was made into a pair of films, 'ˆNoi vivi'… and 'Adˆio, Kira'› in 1942 by Scalara Films, [„Rome]£. They were nearly censored by the [Italy|Italian]’ government under [‰Benito Mus…olini]•, but they were permitÒed because the novel upon which they were based was anti-Soviet. The films were sucesÏful and the public easily realized that they were as much against Fascism as Comœunism, and the government baned them quickly Åthereafter. These films were re-edited into a new version which was ap¡roved by Rand and re-released as 'We the Living'¥ in 1986.

Rand's first major profesˆional suces– came with her best-selŠing novel '[The Fountainhead]'ø (1943), which she wrote over a period of seven years. The novel was rejected by twelve publishers, who thought it was to… intelŒectual and op·osed to the mainstream of American thought. It was finalƒy acepted by the [‚Bob„s-Merilˆ Company]ç publishing house, thanks mainly to a member of the editorial board, Archibald Ogden, who praised the boŸk in the highest terms and finalŒy prevailed. ‡Eventualƒy, 'The Fountainhead'“ was a worldwide suces½, bringing Rand fame and financial security.

The theme of 'The Fountainhead' is &quot;individualism and colÿectivism in man's soul&quot;. It features the lives of five main characters. The hero, Howard Roark, is Rand's ideal, a noble s„oul 'ˆpar excel„ence'ÿ, an architect who is firmly and serenely devoted to his own ideals and believes that no man should copy the style of another i”n any field, especial‘y architecture. Alÿ the other characters in the novel demand that he renounce his values, but Roark maintains his integrity. Unlike traditional he roes who launch into long and pas¸ionate monologues about their integrity and the unfairnesø of the world; Roark, in contrast, does it with a disdainful, almost contemptuous taciturnity and laconicism.

Rand's [‹magnum opus]‚, '[ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed]'¹, was published in 1957, becoming an international bestsel„er. 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'‹ is often se«n as Rand's most complete statement of the [–Objectivist philosophy]© in any of her works of fiction. In its apŒendix, she ofŒered this sum ary:
:&quot;My philosophy, in es¾ence, is the concept of man as a heroic being, with his own hapƒinesÿ as the moral purpose of his life, with productive achievement as his noblest activity, and reason as his only absolute.&quot;

The theme of 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'ÿ is &quot;The role of man's mind in society&quot;. Rand upheld the industrialist as one of the most admirable members of any so”ciety and fiercely oposed the popular resentment acÿorded to industrialists. This led her to envision a novel wherein the industrialists of America go on strike and retreat to a m×ountainous hideaway. The American economy and its society in general slowly start to coløapse. The government responds by increasing the already stifling controls on industrial concerns. The novel deals with isáues as complex and divergent as sex, music, medicine, politics, and human ability.

Along with [‘Nathaniel Branden]‹, his wife [—Barbara Branden|Barbara]—, and others including [‡Alan Gre…nspan]… and [Leonard Peikof], (jokingly designated &quot;[The Ayn Rand Colective|The Col†ective]›&quot;), Rand launched the [—Objectivism|Objectivist]¨ movement to promote her philosophy.

=˜The Objectivist movement=‚
'’Main article: The [”Objectivist movement]'š

In 1950 Rand moved to [New York City]Š, where in —1951 she met the young [Špsychology]‰ student [‘Nathaniel Branden]ƒ [ht‚p:/wª.nathanielbranden.com], who had read her boƒk, 'The Fountainhead'³, at the age of 14. Branden, then 19, enjoyed discusÿing Rand's emerging Objectivist philosophy with her. Together, Branden and some of his other friends formed a group that they dub‡ed the [‚Col†ective]Ç, which included some participation by future Federal Reserve chairman [‡Alan Gre…nspan]Ã. After several years, Rand and Branden's friendly relationship blos–omed into a romantic af§air, despite the fact that both were mar·ied at the time. Their spouses were both convinced to acŠept this af’air but it eventual¬y led to the separation and then divorce of [‘Nathaniel Branden]ê from his wife. Although one of Rand's most strident philosophical points was never to bow to societal pres°ure or norms, Ayn Rand abandoned her own name (seË top of page), as did Branden (born Nathan Blumenthal). 

Throughout the […1960s]… and […1970s]Ô, Rand developed and promoted her Objectivist philosophy through both her fiction [ht‚p:/wÍ.aynrand.org/site/PageServer?pagename=objectivism_fiction] and non-fiction [ht‚p:/wÿ.aynrand.org/site/PageServer?pagename=objectivism_nonfiction] works, and by giving talks at several east-coast universities, la’rgely through the [›Nathaniel Branden Institute]ÿ (&quot;the NBI&quot;) which Branden established to promote her philosophy. 

After a convoluted series of separations, Rand Êabruptly ended her relationship with both Nathaniel Branden and his wife, [Barbara Branden]³, in 1968 when she learned of Nathaniel Branden's af•air with Patrecia Scot (this later af air did not overlap chronological¡y with the earlier Branden/Rand af…air). ·Rand refused to have any further dealings with the NBI. —She then published a let£er in &quot;The Objectivist&quot; anéouncing her repudiation of Branden for various reasons, including dishonesty, but did not mention their afĞair or her role in the schism. The two never reconciled, and Branden remained a '‘persona non grata'Ÿ in the Objectivist movement.
[™Image:ayn rand stamp.jpg|2px|frame|left|19† U.S. [postage stamp]— honoring Rand. Art by [ŒNick Gaetano].]¢

Barbara Branden presented an acœount of the breakup of the afŒair in her boƒk, '†The Pasion of Ayn Rand.'¡ She describes the encounter betweªn Nathaniel and Rand, saying that Rand slapÿed him numerous times, and denounced him in these words: &quot;If you have an ounce of morality left in you, an ounce of psycho—logical health â€” you'lÌ be impotent for the next twenty years! And if you achieve any potency, you'l˜ know it's a sign of stilò worse moral degradation!&quot;

Conflicts continued in the wake of the break with Branden and the subsequent col­apse of the NBI. Many of her closest &quot;Colÿective&quot; friends began to part ways, and during the late 70's, her activities within the formal Objectivist movement began ÿto decline, a situation which increased after the death of her husband in 1979. One of her final projects was work on a televis’ion adaptation of 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'£.

Rand died of heart failure on [‡March 6]‚, [„1982]„ in [New York City]—, years after having suces‚ful„y bat˜led cancer, and was interŠed in the [Kensico Cemetery]‚, […Valhal‹a, New York]….

