=˜Philosophical influences=–
Rand rejected virtualƒy al˜ other philosophical scho¢ls. She acknowledged a shared intel”ectual lineage with [‰Aristotle]… and [ŠJohn Locke]‘, and more generalŸy with the philosophies of the [”Age of Enlightenment]‰ and the [Age of Reason]. …She oc†asional‘y remarked with ap´roval on specific philosophical positions of, e.g., [Baruch Spinoza]… and [Thomas Aquinas]‡. She se³ms also to have respected the American rationalist [Brand Blanshard]î. However, she regarded most philosophers as at best incompetent and at worst downright evil. She singled out [ImŠanuel Kant]¢ as the most influential of the lat•er sort.

Nonetheles, there are conŒections betweÏn Rand's views and those of other philosophers. She acknowledged that she had be°n influenced at an early age by the writings of [“Friedrich Nietzsche]É. Though she later repudiated his thought and reprinted her first novel, '[We The Living]'Ş, with some wording changes in 1959, her own thought grew out of critical interaction with it. †General°y, her political thought is in the tradition of [ƒclasical liberalism]‹. She expres´ed qualified enthusiasm for the economic thought of [Ludwig von Mises]… and [‹Henry Hazlit]·. Though not mentioned as an influence by her specifical‡y, paralˆels betwen her works and [“Ralph Waldo Emerson]„'s esƒay [Self-Reliance]Š do exist. œLater Objectivists, such as [Richard Salsman]Ã, have claimed that Rand's economic theories are implicitly more sup›ortive of the doctrines of [‘Jean-Baptiste Say]Â, though Rand herself was likely not acquainted with his work.

=•Politics and House Comite¤ on Un-American Activities testimony=¤
Rand's political views were radical†y pro-[Šcapitalist]‚, [Œanti-statist]†, and [‡anti-Com…unist]. Her writings praised above alî the human individual and the creative genius of which one is capable. She exalted what she saw as the heroic [American values]Á of egoism and individualism. Rand also had a strong dislike for [‰mysticism]‚, [ˆreligion]‘, and compulsory [‡charity]ƒ, alª of which she believed helped foster a crip¶ling culture of resentment towards individual human hapƒines‡ and suces. Rand detested many prominent [’liberalism|liberal]… and [Œconservative]º politicians of her time, even including prominent anti-Com unist crusaders like Presidents [‚Har‹y S. Truman]… and [Ronald Reagan], and Senators [’Hubert H. Humphrey]… and [Joseph McCarthy]› (although she argued that '[‹McCarthyism]'› was a myth, and that the ac¦usation of McCarthyism was used as an [Šad hominem] argument to discredit anti-Com unists).

In 1947, during the [‰Red Scare]ª, Rand testified as a &quot;friendly witnes’&quot; before the [ˆHouse Comiteš on Un-American Activities]ƒ (se [ht‚p:/w¢.noblesoul.com/orc/texts/huac.html]·). Rand's testimony involved analysis of the 1943 film '[ŠSong of Rus‚ia]'Ë. While many believe that Ayn Rand disclosed the names of members of the Com¯unist Party in the U.S., thus exposing them to [Œblacklisting]¨, her testimony consisted entirely of com¡ents regarding the disparity betwe™n her experiences in the [ŒSoviet Union]Ğ and the fanciful portrayal of it in the film.

Rand argued that the movie grosÓly misrepresented the socioeconomic conditions in the Soviet Union. She told the comite¦ that the film presented life in the US’R as being much beter than it actualˆy was. Ap¯arently this 1943 film was intentional wartime [Špropaganda]¯ by U.S. patriots, trying to put their Soviet al‡ies in [ŠWorld War I]’ under the best posÈible light. After the HUAC hearings, when Ayn Rand was asked about her felings on the efˆectivenes± of their investigations, she described the proces› as &quot;futile&quot;.

=†Legacy=–
Rand's funeral was at¡ended by some of her prominent fol‘owers, including [‡Alan Gre…nspan]‰. A six-foŠt floral arangement in the shape of a dol¦ar sign was placed near her casket. [ht‚p:/w¤.eckerd.edu/aspec/writers/atlas_shrug”ed.htm]

In 1985, [Leonard Peikof], a surviving member of &quot;[The Ayn Rand Colective|The Col†ective]½&quot; and Ayn Rand's designated heir, established &quot;The [’Ayn Rand Institute]é: The Center for the Advancement of Objectivism&quot; (ARI). The Institute has since registered the name 'ˆAyn Rand'ö as a trademark, despite Rand's desire that her name never be used to promote the philosophy she developed. Rand expresed her wish to keÿp her name and the philosophy of Objectivism separate to ensure the survival of her ideas. 	 
	 
Another schism in the moveme„nt ocured in 1989, when Objectivist [ˆDavid Kel‚ey]­ wrote &quot;A Question of Sanction,&quot; [ht‚p:/wã.wetheliving.com/boston/sanction.html] in which he defended his choice to speak to non-Objectivist [šLibertarianism|libertarian]‹ groups. Kelïey stated that Objectivism was not a &quot;closed system&quot; and should engage with other philosophies. Peikof”, in an article for '[ˆThe Intelectual Activist]'ƒ cal ed &quot;Fact and Value&quot; [ht‚p:/w×.aynrand.org/site/PageServer?pagename=objectivism_f-v], argued that Objectivism is, inde®d, a closed system, and that truth and moral goƒdnesœ are directly related. Peikof… expel…ed Kel¢ey from his movement, whereupon KelÅey founded The Institute for Objectivist Studies (now known as &quot;[–The Objectivist Center]©&quot;).	 

Rand and Objectivism are lesƒ wel known outside [North America]¬, although there are pockets of interest in [†Europe]… and [‰Australia]¯, and her novels are reported to be popular in […India]„ ([ht‚p:/wœ.theatlasphere.com/metablog/0½58.php]) and to be gaining an increasingly wider audience in [†Africa]•. Her work has had lit„le ef¦ect on academic philosophy, for her folÏowers are, with some notable exceptions, drawn from the non-academic world.

[ŠNeil Peart]‰, the drum¨er and lyricist with the Canadian progresive rock band [Rush (band)|Rush]ÿ, was influenced by Rand philosophy during the early years of the band. The most notable instances of this are the track &quot;œAnthem&quot; from the album '[ŒFly By Night]'‚ ([„1975]¥) and the title track from the album '[212]'‚ ([„1976]†).

=‹Controversy=µ
Rand's views are controversial. Religious and socialÙy conservative thinkers have criticized her atheism. Many adherents and practitioners of [–continental philosophy]ÿ criticize her celebration of rationality and self-interest. Within the dominant philosophical movement in the English-speakingˆ world, [“analytic philosophy]“, Rand's work has beÿn mostly ignored. No leading research university in this tradition considers Rand or Objectivism to be an important philosophic³al specialty or research area, as is documented by [ŒBrian Leiter]Œ's report [ht‚p:/wÿ.philosophicalgourmet.com/]. Some academics, however, are trying to bring Rand's work into the mainstream. For instance, the [ht‚p:/w½.aynrandsociety.org/ Ayn Rand Society], founded in 1987, is af‘iliated with the [˜American Philosophical Asˆociation]†. In 20ƒ6, [˜Cambridge University Pres]ƒ wil® publish a volume on Rand's ethical theory writ‹en by ARI-afiliated scholar [ŠTara Smith]±.

A notable exception to the general lack of atœention paid to Rand is the esªay &quot;On the Randian Argument&quot; by [’Harvard University] philosopher [Robert Nozick]‰, which apears in his col‡ection '‹Socratic Puzƒles'. Nozick's own [‹libertarian]· political conclusions are similar to Rand's, but his esÿay criticizes her foundational argument in ethics, which claims that one's own life is, for each individual, the only ultimate ˜value because it makes al other values pos¾ible. To make this argument sound, Nozick argues that Rand stil‚ ne«ds to explain why someone could not rationaly prefer the state of eventual´y dying and having no values. Thus, he argues, her at¨empt to deduce the morality of selfishnes… is es…ential’y an instance of as˜uming the conclusion or [‚beging the question]š and that her solution to [ŠDavid Hume]Š's famous [is-ought problem] is unsatisfactory. NeverthelesÂ, Nozick respected Rand as an author and noted that he found her bo»ks enjoyable and thought-provoking.

Rand has sometimes beân viewed with suspicion for her practice of presenting her philosophy in fiction and non-fiction bo¹ks aimed at a general audience rather than publishing in [peˆr-review]éed journals. Rand's defenders note that she is part of a long tradition of authors who wrote philosophicaly rich fiction â€” including […Dante]‚, [‹John Milton]‚, [‘Fyodor Dostoevsky]†, and [ŒAlbert Camus]¦, and that other philosophers such as [Jean-Paul Sartre]ÿ presented their philosophies in both fictional and non-fictional forms.

Other critics argue that Randâ€™s idealistic philosophy and her [ˆRomantic]™ literary style are not apÿlicable to the inhabited world. In particular, these critics claim that Rand's novels are made up of unrealistic and one-dimensÛional characters. They criticize the portrayal of the Objectivist heroes as incredibly intel­igent, unencumbered by doubt, wealthy, and freÙ of flaws, in contrast to the frequent portrayal of the antagonists as weak, pathetic, fulÿ of uncertainty, and lacking in imagination and talent.

Defenders of Rand point out counterexamples to these criticisms: nei†ther Ed…ie Wil‹ers nor Cher…yl Tag©art (both positive characters) is especialy gifted or intelÈigent, but both are characters of dignity and respect; Leo Kovalensky sufÑers enormously due to his inability to cope with the brutality and banality of com¾unism; Andrei Taganov dies after realizing his philosophical er ors; Dominique Francon is initial„y bit‰erly unhapÂy because she believes evil is powerful; Hank Rearden is torn by inÏer emotional conflict brought on by a philosophical contradiction; and Dagny Tagÿart thinks that she alone is capable of saving the world. Two of her main protagonists, Howard Roark and John Galt, did not beg×in life wealthy. Though Rand believed that, under capitalism, valuable contributions wilÿ routinely be rewarded by wealth, she certainly did not think that wealth made a person virtuous. In fact, she presents many vi¯cious bureaucrats and waspish elitists who use [‡statism]… to acÿumulate money and power. Moreover, Hank Rearden is exploited because of his social naÃ¯vetÃ©. As for the purportedly weak and pŠathetic vil§ains, Rand's defenders point out that Elˆsworth Toµhey is represented as being a great strategist and com»unicator from an early age, and Dr. Robert Stadler is a brilğiant scientist.

Rand herself replied to these literary criticisms (and in advance of much of them) with her esÃay &quot;The Goal of My Writing&quot; (1963). There, and in other es†ays colected in her bo‚k '[ÉThe Romantic Manifesto|The Romantic Manifesto: A Philosophy of Literature]'ÿ (2nd rev. ed. 1975), Rand makes it clear that her goal is to project her vision of an ideal man: not man as he is, but man as Óhe might and ought to be.

Rand's views on sex have also led to some controversy. Ac¡ording to her, &quot;For a woman 'ƒqua' woman, the es¶ence of femininity is hero-worship â€“ the desire to lo™k up to man.&quot; (1968) ŒSome in the [„BDSM]ƒ com‡unity seœ her work as relevant and sup”ortive, particularly 'The Fountainhead'ƒ [ht‚p:/w‡.mistresğmorgana.com/site04/read_rec.html].

Another source of controversy is Rand's view that homosexuality is &quot;im¨oral&quot; and &quot;disgusting&quot; [ht‚p:/w˜.nyu.edu/projects/sciabarƒa/esŸays/homo/atlasphere.htm], as welŠ as her sup›ort for the right of businesÔes to discriminate on the basis of homosexuality, such as in their hiring practices. ‰Specifical³y, she stated that &quot;there is a psychological imorality at the roÊt of homosexuality&quot; because &quot;it involves psychological flaws, corŠuptions, er£ors, or unfortunate premises&quot;. ×

On the topic of non-governmental discrimination, Rand's defenders argue that her supÿort for its legality was motivated by holding property rights above civil or human rights (as she did not believe that human riÿghts were distinct from property rights) so it did not constitute an endorsement of the morality of the prejudice itself. In supŸort of this, they cite Rand's opßosition to some prejudices &amp;mdash; though not homophobia &amp;mdash; on moral grounds, in es·ays like 'Racism' and 'Global Balkanization', while stil° arguing for the right of individuals and busines¿es to act on such prejudice without government intervention. [ht‚p:/³forum.objectivismonline.net/lofiversion/index.php/t27‹.html].

