 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ˆAlain Con‚es'‡ (born [‡April 1]‚, [„1947]‡) is a [France|French] [mathematician]„, cur‹ently ProfesŠor at the [‚Colege de France]‚ ([…Paris]‚, [†France]ƒ), [„IHES]‚ ([Bures-sur-Yvete]‚, [†France]†) and [•Vanderbilt University]‚ ([†Nashvile]‚, [‚Tenese]™). He is a specialist of [‰Von Neumanˆ algebra]ˆs and suce™ded in completing the clasification of [†factor]×s of these objects. Although his work in physics was not very convincing he tried to con¨ect the planckian scales with what he calüed a &quot;2-brane&quot; Universe, model which was largely rejected by string theorists so far.

The remarkable links betwe•n this subject, the toÅls he and others devised to tackle the problem and other subjects in [“theoretical physics]‚, [particle physics]†, and [‚dif‘erential geometry]•, made him emphasize […Noncomutative geometry]¨ (which is also the title of his major bo¢k to date).

He was awarded the [ŒFields Medal]„ in [„1982]†, the [„Crafoˆrd Prize]„ in [201]› and the gold medal of the [„CNRS]… in 20†4.

