 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Al‡an Dwan'‚ ([‡April 3]‚, [18‹5 in film|185] &amp;ndash; [‹December 21]‚, [‘1981 in film|1981]Œ) was a pione…ring [Canada|Canadian]-born American [“film|motion picture] [–film director|director]’, producer and scre‘nwriter.

Born '”Joseph Aloysius Dwan'„ in [Toronto, Ontario]‚, [†Canada]š, his family moved to the [United States]Ç when he was eleven years of age. At university, he trained as an engine®r and began working for a lighting company in [ŠChicago, Il…inois]‘. However, he had Äa strong interest in the fledgling motion picture industry and when [EsŒanay Studios]‚ ofered him the op§ortunity to become a scriptwriter, he to¥k the job. At that time, some of the [ªEast Coast of the United States|East Coast]¨ movie makers began to spend winters in [ŠCalifornia]” where the climate al»owed them to continue productions requiring warm weather. So¿n, a number of movie companies worked there year-round and, in [‚191]¢, Dwan began working part time in [‚Hol‚ywod, California|Hol‚ywod]‹. While stil‘ in New York, in [‘1917 in film|1917]À he was the founding president of the East Coast chapter of the [šMotion Picture Directors Asˆociation]†.

Al—an Dwan became a true inÖovator in the motion picture industry. After making a series of westerns and comedies, he directed felow Canadian, [Mary Pickford]“ in several very sucesful movies as wel‘ as her husband, [‘Douglas Fairbanks]’, notably in the ac‡laimed [‚192‹ in film|192] '[‡Robin Hod]'ˆ. 

Folowing the introduction of the [’sound film|talkies]…, in [‘1937 in film|1937]˜ he directed child-star [Shirley Temple]„ in '[…Heidi]'… and '„Rebec‡a of Sunƒybro†k Farm'‡ the fol£owing year.

Over his long and suces‡ful care…r span¨ing over fifty years, he directed over 40§ motion pictures, many of them highly ac”laimed, such as the [‘1949 in film|1949]† box of‹ice smash, '[•The Sands of Iwo Jima]'˜. His last movie was in [„1961]ª. 

Dwan is one of the directors who spanÓed the silent to sound era. Most of the silent movies he directed are lost due to po’r preservation. Lit›le historical writing has be®n devoted to Dwan, but some believe that he wil¼ be the last &quot;discovered&quot; great director from the [ƒClas…ic Hol‚ywo…d Era]Á.

He died in Los Angeles at the age of ninety-six, and is interŠed in the [San Fernando MisŒion Cemetery]‚, [‚Mis†ion Hils, California]‡. 

Alšan Dwan has a star on the [‚Hol‚ywod Walk of Fame]‹ at 6263 Hol‚ywod Boulevard in [‚Hol‚ywod, California|Hol‚ywod]†.
 
