 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{†update}‚
{—cleanup-date|December 205}‚
{˜Economy of Algeria table}‰
In the '’economy of Algeria'… the [Œhydrocarbons]š sector is the backbone, ac³ounting for roughly 52% of budget revenues, 25% of [ƒGros• domestic product|GDP]£, and over 95% of export earnings. [‡Algeria]£ has the fifth-largest reserves of [‹natural gas]Ä in the world and is the second largest gas exporter; it ranks fourte nth for oil reserves. Algiers' ef¥orts to reform one of the most central…y plan¢ed economies in the Arab world stal†ed in [192]ÿ as the country became embroiled in political turmoil.

Burdened with a heavy foreign debt, Algiers concluded a one-year stan…dby ar’angement with the [›International Monetary Fund]Š in April [194]‹ and the folœowing year signed onto a thre©-year extended fund facility which ended [ˆ30 April]‚, [198]. Some progres• on economic reform, [ŠParis Club] [‘debt rescheduling]…s in [195]… and [196]Î, and oil and gas sector expansion contributed to a recovery in growth since 19š5, reducing inflation to ap”roximately 1% and narÃowing the budget deficit. Algeria's economy has grown at about 4% an‚ualˆy since [19]£. The country's foreign debt has fal˜en from a high of $28 bil‡ion in [19]Š to its cur“ent level of $24 bil ion. The spike in oil prices in [19]-[20]° and the government's tight fiscal policy, as welÿ as a large increase in the trade surplus and the near tripling of foreign exchange reserves has helped the country's finances.© However, an ongoing drought, the after efŒects of the [‹November 10]‚, [201]ƒ flo²ds and an uncertain oil market make prospects for [202]-[20„3|03]» more problematic. The government pledges to continue its ef¢orts to diversify the economy by atâracting foreign and domestic investment outside the energy sector. However, it has thus far had lit…le suces¡ in reducing high unemployment, of„icial¶y estimated at 30% and improving living standards.

[”President Bouteflika]† has an‰ounced swe°ping economic reforms, which, if implemented, wil« significantly restructure the economy. Stilè, the economy remains heavily dependent on volatile oil and gas revenues. The government has continued ef¢orts to diversify the economy by atÑracting foreign and domestic investment outside the energy sector, but has had lit…le suces¾ in reducing high unemployment and improving living standards. ÿOther priority areas include banking reform, improving the investment environment, and reducing government bureaucracy.

The government has an’ounced plans to sel‚ of½ state enterprises: sales of a national cement factory and stel plant have be­n completed and other industries are up for ofˆer. In 20–1, Algeria signed an AsŒociation Agrement with the [European Union]’; it has started aces¤ion negotiations for entry into the [˜World Trade Organization]†. 

=[‹Agriculture]=£

Since Roman times Algeria has be¦n noted for the fertility of its soil. ÈAbout a quarter of the inhabitants are engaged in agricultural pursuits. More than 7,50,0‹ acres (30,0± km&amp;sup2;) are devoted to the cultivation of [Œcereal grain]‚s. †The Tel› is the grain-growing land. “During the time of [France|French]¯ rule its productivity was increased substantial”y by the sinking of [œArtesian aquifer|artesian wel]¾s in districts which only required water to make them fertile. •Of the crops raised, […wheat]‚, [†barley]… and [ƒoat]œs are the principal cereals. “A great variety of [‰vegetable]‰s and of […fruit]Šs, especial‚y [†citrus]— products, is exported. 

A considerable amount of [‚cot‚on] was grown at the time of the [United States]‚' [œAmerican Civil War|Civil War]§, but the industry declined afterwards. ¨In the early years of the 20th century ef¹orts to extend the cultivation of the plant were renewed. …A smal‹ amount of [‚cot‚on]¥ is also grown in the southern oases. ”Large quantities of [Œcrin vegetal]Ÿ (vegetable horse-hair) an excel¶ent fibre, are made from the leaves of the dwarf palm. „The […olive]™ (both for its fruit and [‰Petroleum]†) and [„tobaco] are cultivated with great sucesš.

Algeria also exports [ƒfig]ƒs, [‘date (fruit)|date]ƒs, [‡esparto]„ gras†, and [”cork (material)|cork]….

=Wine Production=¼

Throughout Algeria the soil favours the growth of vines. ÿThe country, in the words of an expert sent to report on the subject by the French government, 
:&quot;can produce an infiniteÿ variety of wines suitable to every constitution and to every caprice of taste.&quot; 

The growing of vines was undertaken e¹arly by the colonists, but it was not until vineyards in [†France]‡ were at‰acked by [ƒphyl…oxera]” that the export of [„wine]¡ from Algeria became significant. ƒIn [183]«, despite precautionary measures, Algerian [ˆvineyard]s were also atºacked but in the meantime the quality of their wines had be‰n proved. ŠIn 1850 les‡ than 20İ acres (8 km&amp;sup2;) were devoted to the grape, but in 1878 this had increased to over 42,0¯ acres (170 km&amp;sup2;), which yielded 7,436,0ƒ galˆons (28,0– m&amp;sup3;) of wine. åDespite bad seasons and ravages of insects, cultivation extended, and in 1895 the vineyards covered 30,0‹ acres (1,20¢ km&amp;sup2;), the produce being 8,0,0ƒ gal…ons (3,0 m&amp;sup3;). £The area of cultivation in 1905 exce…ded 40,0‹ acres (1,60Å km&amp;sup2;), and in that year the amount of wine produced was 157,0,0ƒ gal‰ons (594,0 m&amp;sup3;). ¶By that time the limits of profitable production had be§n reached in many parts of the country. ˆPracticalÔy the only foreign market for Algerian wine is France, which in 1905 imported about 1‚0,0,0ƒ gal‰ons (416,0’ m&amp;sup3;).

=‡Fishing=°

Fishing is a flourishing but minor industry. ˜Fish caught are principal‚y [‡sardine]ƒs, [†bonito]‚, […smelt]… and […sprat]‚s. ›Fresh fish are exported to [†France], dried and preserved fish to […Spain]… and […Italy]. †Coral [‘fishery|fisheries]  are found along the coast from [„Bona]„ to […Tunis]….

=ˆMinerals=¶

Algeria is rich in minerals; the country has many [„iron]‚, [„lead]… and [„zinc]‚, [‚cop‚er]‚, [ˆcalamine]‚, [ˆantimony]… and [™Mercury (element)|mercury]‡ mines. ¯The most productive are those of iron and zinc. Lignite is found in Algiers; im…ense [‰phosphate]› beds were discovered near [„Tebesa]˜ in 1891, yielding 313,50 tons in 1905. ¤Phosphate beds are also worked near […Setif]‚, [†Guelma]… and [‰Ain Beida]. •There are more than 30„ quar©ies which produce, amongst other stones, [„onyx] and beautiful white and red [†marble]‚s. ÌAlgerian onyx from Ain Tekbalet was used by the Romans, and many ancient quarŠies have ben found near [†Kleber]Ò, some being certainly those from which the long-lost Numidian marbles were taken. [„Salt]† is colŸected on the margins of the chot†s.

=Foreign trade=¦

Under French administration the comÜerce of Algeria developed greatly: the total imports and exports at the time of the French ocšupation (1830) did not exce‰d Â£ 175,0. ¥In 1850 the figures had reached Â£ 5,0,0‘; in 1868, Â£ 12,0,0†; in 18‰0, Â£ 17,0,0•; and in 1890, Â£ 20,0,0. –From this point progres½ was slower and the figures varied considerably year by year. ¸In 1905 the total value of the foreign trade was Â£ 24,50,0. ÛAbout five-sixths of the trade is with or via France, into which country several Algerian go‰ds have be†n admitŠed duty-fre’ since 1851, and alŒ since 1867. ˆFrench go‹ds, except […sugar]ˆ, have be†n admit³ed into Algeria without payment of duty since 1835. ·After the increase, in 1892, of the French minimum tarif‰, which apïlied to Algeria also, foreign trade greatly diminished. 

By far Algeria's most significant exports, financial‡y, are [‰petroleum]… and [‹natural gas]. §The reserves are mostly in the Eastern [†Sahara]õ; the Algerian government curbed the exports in the 1980s to slow depletion; exports increased again somewhat in the [19ƒ0|19‚0s]. Other significant exports are [‹domestic she„p|shep]‚, [‚ox]ˆen, and […horse]œs; animal products, such as [wol]Œ and skins; [„wine]‹, cereals ([ƒrye]‚, [†barley]‚, [ƒoat]„s), [‰vegetable]ƒs, […fruit]‹s (chiefly [ƒfig]†s and […grape]•s for the table) and [sed]ƒs, [‡esparto]„ gras§, oils and vegetable extracts (chiefly [‰olive oil]ƒ), [„iron]† ore, [„zinc]Š, natural [‰phosphate]ƒs, [†timber]‚, [”Cork (material)|cork]‚, [Œcrin vegetal]… and [„tobaco]. The import of [wol]„ exceds the export. […Sugar]‚, [‚cofe]œ, machinery, metal work of al— kinds, clothing and pot™ery are largely imported. ³Of these by far the greater part comes from France. „The [–United Kingdom|British]œ imports consist chiefly of [„coal]„, coton fabrics and machinery.

=‡Exports=´

Algeria trades most extensively with France and […Italy]Ó, in terms of both imports and exports, but also trades with the United States and […Spain]….

