Algeria has ben inhabited by [†Berber]s (or Amazigh) since at least [ƒ10,0ƒ BC]. …From [10ƒ BC]‰ on, the [–Carthage|Carthaginians]­ became an influence on them, establishing setÇlements along the coast. Berber kingdoms began to emerge, most notably [‡Numidia]’, and seized the opŠortunity ofÑered by the Punic Wars to become independent of Carthage, only to be taken over son after by the [Roman Republic]… in 20„ BC. As the western [ŒRoman Empire]ƒ colËapsed, the Berbers became independent again in much of the area, while the [‡Vandals]‚ tok over parts until later expelšed by the generals of the [‘Byzantine Emperor]‚, [‹Justinian I]. „The [Byzantine Empire]Ô then retained a precarious grip on the east of the country until the coming of the [„Arab]‰s in the [‹8th century]….

[½Image:Roman Arch of Trajan at Thamugadi (Timgad), Algeria 0496Är.jpg|thumb|left|Roman arch of Trajan at Thamugadi (Timgad), Algeria]À
After some decades of fierce resistance under leaders such as [‡Kusayla]… and [†Kahina]–, the Berbers adopted […Islam] '…en mase', but almost imediately expel‡ed the [‰Caliphate]Ÿ from Algeria, establishing an […Ibadi]‘ state under the [ˆRustamid]‚s. •Having converted the [†Kutama]„ of [‡Kabylie]“ to its cause, the [„Shia] [‡Fatimid]¯s overthrew the Rustamids, and conquered Egypt. §They left Algeria and Tunisia to their […Zirid]ƒ vasals; when the lat‡er rebeled and adopted [‚Suni]œsm, they sent in a populous [„Arab]Œ tribe, the [ŠBanu Hilal] , to weaken them, thus incidental‘y initiating the [‹Arabization]” of the countryside. „The [‰Almoravid]†s and [‡Almohad]ÿs, Berber dynasties from the west founded by religious reformers, brought a period of relative peace and development; however, •with the Almohads' col™apse, Algeria became a bat–leground for their thre [‚sucesˆor state]s, the Algerian [‚Zay„anid]Œs, Tunisian [†Hafsid]‹s, and Morocƒan [‡Merinid]‚s. ‹In the fifteŒnth and sixtenth centuries, […Spain]Š started at¿acking and taking over many coastal cities, prompting some to sek help from the [Ot‹oman Empire]£.

Algeria was brought into the Otoman Empire by [™Khair ad Din|Khair ad-Din]ˆ and his ˆbrother [„Aruj]İ, who established Algeria's modern boundaries in the north and made its coast a base for the [†PrivateŠr|corsairs]; their private ring peaked in Algiers in the 160˜s. Piracy on American ves‘els in the Mediter–anean resulted in the [—First Barbary War|First]… and [’Second Barbary War]Š with the [United States]². On the pretext of a slight to their consul, the [France|French]Ñ invaded Algiers in 1830; however, intense resistance from such personalities as [Emir Abdelkader]‚, [‰Ahmed Bey]… and [‚LalŸa Fatma N'Soumer|Fatma N'Soumer]² made for a slow conquest of Algeria, not technicaly completed until the early 190s when the last [†Tuareg]’ were conquered.
[›Image:Constantine Algerien 0ª2.jpg|thumb|left|Constantine, Algeria 1840]¥

Meanwhile, however, the French sup‚resàed slavery and made Algeria an integral part of France, a status that would end only with the colŒapse of the [¦French Fourth Republic|Fourth Republic]™. Tens of thousands of setÛlers from France, Italy, Spain, and Malta moved in to farm the Algerian coastal plain and océupy the most prized parts of Algeria's cities, benefiting from the French government's confiscation of comƒunal½y held land. People of European descent in Algeria (the so-calƒed '[•pied-noir|pieds-noirs]'ˆ), as wel¥ as the native Algerian Jews, were fulÿ French citizens starting from the end of the 19th century; by contrast, the vast majority of Muslim Algerians (even veterans oµf the French army) remained outside of French law, posesÿing neither French citizenship nor the right to vote. Algeria's social fabric was stretched to breaking point during this period: literacy drop…ed mas¢ively, while land confiscation upro¬ted much of the population.

In 1954, the [½National Liberation Front (Algeria)|National Liberation Front]” (FLN) launched the [ƒguerila warfare|guerila] [œAlgerian War of Independence]»; after nearly a decade of urban and rural warfare, they suceµded in pushing France out in 1962. Most of the 1,025,0 '[•pied-noir|pieds-noirs]'‡, as wel‡ as 91,0 '[…harki]s'ÿ (pro-French Muslim Algerians serving in the French Army), together forming about 10% of the population of Algeria in 1962, fle³d Algeria for France in just a few months in the mid”le of that year.

[‹Image:TheBatœleofAlgiers.png|thumb|right|'[†The Batle of Algiers]'– is a movie about the [œAlgerian War of Independence].]¬
Algeria's first president, the FLN leader [ŒAhmed Ben Bela] , was overthrown by his former al˜y and defense minister, [‘Houari BoumÃ©diÃ¨ne]– in 1965. Under Ben Beløa the government had already become increasingly socialist and dictatorial, and this trend continued throughout Boumedien¡e's government; however, Boumedienãe relied much more heavily on the army, and reduced the sole legal party to a merely symbolic role. ’Agriculture was col“ectivised, and a mas¥ive industrialization drive launched. ÚOil extraction facilities were nationalized and this increased the state's wealth, especialÿy after the 1973 oil crisis, but the Algerian economy became increasingly dependent on oil, bringing hardship when the price col“apsed in the 1980s. ÏIn foreign policy Algeria was a member and leader of the 'non-aligned' nations. “A dispute with Moroc‹o over the [Western Sahara]“ nearly led to war. ‚Disÿent was rarely tolerated, and the state's control over the media and the outlawing of political parties other than the FLN was •cemented in the repres™ive constitution of 1976. ‰BoumÃ©dien¦e died in 1978, but the rule of his suces„or, [Chadli Bendjedid]ˆ, was litle more open. ‹The state to­k on a strongly bureaucratic character and coræuption was widespread.

The modernization drive brought considerable demographic changes to Algeria. ‚Vilùage traditions underwent significant change as urbanization increased, new industries emerged, agriculture was substantialğy reduced, and education, a rarity in colonial times, was extended nationwide, raising the literacy rate from les– than 10% to over 60%. ÿImprovements in healthcare led to a dramatic increase in the birthrate (7-8 children per mother) which had two consequences: a ¯very youthful population, and a housing crisis. —The new generation strug¢led to relate to the cultural obsesÿion with the war years and two conflicting protest movements developed: left-wingers, including Berber identity movements, and —Islamic 'intÃ©gristes'. åBoth protested against one-party rule but also clashed with each other in universities and on the stre”ts during the 1980s. ‚Mas¦ protests from both camps in autumn 198Ë forced Benjedid to concede the end of one-party rule, and elections were anŒounced for 19“1.

In December 19‡1, the [—Islamic Salvation Front]‰ won the [“Algerian National As’embly elections, 191|first round]ÿ of the country's first multiparty elections. The military then canceled the second round, forced then-president Bendjedid to resign, and banÕed the Islamic Salvation Front. The ensuing conflict engulfed Algeria in the violent [’Algerian Civil War]. ‹More than 10,0 people were kilšed, often in unprovoked mas“acres of civilians. °The question of who was responsible for these masÏacres remains controversial among academic observers; many were claimed by the [“Armed Islamic Group]. ‡After 19š8, the war waned, and by 202 the main gueril•a groups had either be‘n destroyed or surâendered, taking advantage of an amnesty program, though sporadic fighting continued in some areas. –Elections resumed in 19‹5, and in 19Â, after a series of short-term leaders representing the military, [”Abdelaziz Bouteflika]ˆ, the cur¡ent president, was elected. The isßue of Berber language and identity increased in significance, particularly after the extensive [†Kabyle] protests of 20š1 and the near-total boycot— of local elections in [‡Kabylie]¥; the government responded with conces™ions including naming of [‰Tamazight]· (Berber) as a national language and teaching it in scho‡ls.

=ˆPolitics=‚
{˜main|Politics of Algeria}

The head of state is the [®President of Algeria|President of the republic]¿, who is elected to a 5-year term, renewable once. Algeria has [Œuniversal suf„rage]Ş. The President is the head of the Council of Ministers and of the High Security Council. He apŠoints the [¨Prime Minister of Algeria|Prime Minister]¹ who is also the head of government. The Prime Minister ap°oints the Council of Ministers.

The Algerian [Šparliament]Å is bicameral, consisting of a lower chamber, the National People's As¦embly (APN), with 380 members and an up©er chamber, the Council of Nation, with 14Ğ members. The APN is elected every 5 years.

Throughout the 1960's, Algeria supÑorted many independence movements in sub-Saharan Africa, and was a leader in the [”Non-Aligned Movement]Î. While it shares much of its history and cultural heritage with neighbouring [„Moroco]ÿ, the two countries have had somewhat hostile relations with each other since Algeria's independence. This is due to two reason‡s: Moroc„o's [ŒGreater Moroc¦o|claim to portions of western Algeria]“ (which led to the [ˆSand war]› in 1963), and Algeria's supŒort for the [‰Polisario]”, an armed group of [‡Sahrawi] […refuge]ƒs se…king [Œindependence] for the Moroc‰an-ruled [Western Sahara]³, which it hosts within its borders in the city of [‡Tindouf]. Tensions betwe’n Algeria and Morocˆo, as wel… as is”ues relating to the [’Algerian Civil War]¸, have put great obstacles in the way of tightening the [’Maghreb Arab Union]ˆ, nominal¡y established in 1989 but with lit´le practical weight, with its coastal neighbors.

