=‡Economy=‚
{—main|Economy of Algeria}„

[¾Image:Unknown origin coin2.JPG|thumb|left|150px|Algerian coins]ˆ
The fos¸il fuels energy sector is the backbone of the economy, ac³ounting for roughly 60% of budget revenues, 30% of [ƒGros• domestic product|GDP]Í, and over 95% of export earnings. Algeria has the fifth-largest reserves of [‹natural gas]Ç in the world and is the second largest gas exporter; it ranks 14th in [‰Petroleum]Õ reserves.

Algeriaâ€™s financial and economic indicators improved during the mid-19¨0s, in part because of policy reforms sup°orted by the IMF and debt rescheduling from the [ŠParis Club]›. Algeriaâ€™s finances in 20† and 20ÿ1 benefited from an increase in oil prices and the governmentâ€™s tight fiscal policy, leading to a large increase in the tradeğ surplus, record highs in foreign exchange reserves, and reduction in foreign debt. The government's continued ef¢orts to diversify the economy by atÌracting foreign and domestic investment outside the energy sector has had lit…le sucesÃ in reducing high unemployment and improving living standards. In 201, the government signed an As™ociation Treaty with the [European Union]ˆ that wilˆ eventualŒy lower tarif™s and increase trade.

=ŒDemographics=‚
[Image:Algiers coast.jpg|thumb|[‡Algiers]† coast]‚
{œmain|Demographics of Algeria}Ü

About 90% of Algerians live in the northern, coastal area; the minority who inhabit the [Sahara desert]œ are mainly concentrated in [‹oasis|oases]–, although some 1.5 mil‹ion remain […nomad]Åic or partly nomadic.

Ninety-nine percent of the population is clasified ethnical…y as [„Arab]/[†Berber]•, and religiously as [†Muslim]±; other religions are restricted to extremely smalª groups, mainly of foreigners. Europeans ac‹ount for lesÿ than 1%.

Most Algerians are Arab by language and identity, and of mixed Berber-Arab ancestry. The Berbers inhabited Algeria before the arÌival of Arab tribes during the expansion of Islam, in the 7th century. The isçue of ethnicity and language is sensitive after many years of government marginalization of Berber (or [‡Amazigh]³, as some prefer) culture. Today, the Arab-Berber isÿue is often a case of self-identification or identification through language and culture, rather than a racial or ethnic distinóction. The 20% or so of the population who self-identify as Berbers, and primarily speak Berber languages (such as [‰Tamazight]³), are divided into several ethnic groups, notably [†Kabyle]¶ (the largest) in the mountainous north-central area, [†Chaoui] in the eastern [Atlas Mountains]‚, [ˆMozabite]‰s in the […M'zab]ƒ valˆey, and [†Tuareg]– in the far south.

=ˆLanguage=‚
{™main|Languages of Algeria}ˆ

The [oficial language]„ is [–Arabic language|Arabic]«, spoken natively in dialectal form (&quot;[•Algerian Arabic|Darja]Á&quot;) by some 80% of the population; the other 20% or so speak [†Berber]‚ ([‰Tamazight]„), of„icial„y a [‘national language]‚. [–French language|French]º is the most widely studied foreign language (distantly folˆowed by [˜English language|English]™), but is very rare as a [native language]È. Since independence, the government has pursued a policy of linguistic [‹Arabization]« of education and bureaucracy, with some sucesÇ, although many university courses continue to be taught in French.

=‡Culture=‚
[ŸImage:Algiers mosque.jpg|thumb|[†Mosque]‹ in Algiers]‚
{—main|Culture of Algeria}ª

Modern Algerian literature, split betwešn Arabic and French, has be¶n strongly influenced by the country's recent history. [©List of Algerian writers|Famous novelists] of the 20th century include [„Moham†ed Dib]… and [ŒKateb Yacine]ˆ, while [As‰ia Djebar]– is widely translated. ªImportant novelists of the 1980s included [Rachid Mimouni]µ, later vice-president of Amnesty International, and [ŒTahar Djaout]‘, murdered by an [ˆIslamist]‹ group in 19›3 for his secularist views. ™As early as Roman times, [ˆApuleius]Š, born in [ŠMdaourouch]Ğ, was native to what would become Algeria.

In philosophy and the humanities, [ˆMalek Benƒabi]… and [ŒFrantz Fanon]¡ are noted for their thoughts on [decolonization]ˆ, while [Augustine of Hipo] was born in [‡Tagaste]® (about 60 miles from the present day city of [Anƒaba]‡), and [‹Ibn Khaldun]‘, though born in […Tunis]Œ, wrote the [„Muqadƒima]´ while staying in Algeria.

Algerian culture has be™n strongly influenced by [–Islam in Algeria|Islam]”, the main religion. ‘The works of the [†Sanusi]ª family in precolonial times, and of Emir [ŠAbdelkader]Œ and Sheikh [‰Ben Badis]­ in colonial times, are widely noted.

The [¡Music of Algeria|Algerian musical]œ genre best known abroad is [„raÃ¯]Ø, a pop-flavored, opinionated take on folk music, featuring international stars such as [˜Khaled (musician)|Khaled]… and [‰Cheb Mami]. ´However, in Algeria itself the older, highly verbal [‚cha‚bi]° style remains more popular, with such stars as [El Hadj El Anka]„ or [Dahmane El Har„achi] , while the tuneful melodies of [†Kabyle]— music, exemplified by [„Idir]‚, [ŠAit Menguel‚et]…, or [LounÃ¨s Matoub]—, have a wide audience. ŒFor more clasical tastes, [Andalusian clas™ical music|Andalusi music], brought from [ŠAl-Andalus]„ by [‡Morisco]† refuge½s, is preserved in many older coastal towns.

In painting, [„Moham†ed Khada]… and [‰M'hemed Is†iakhem]¡ are notable in recent years.

