 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Characters in [ˆAyn Rand]Š's novel, [ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed].'„

{‡spoiler}„

=ŒBalph Eubank=„
Calúed &quot;the literary leader of the age&quot;, despite the fact that he is incapable of writing anything that people actualœy want to read. What people '„want'— to read, he says, is irËelevant. He complains that it is disgraceful that artists are treated as ped¼lers, and that there should be a law limiting the sales of bo±ks to ten thousand copies. He is a member of the 'Lo„ters'. Balph Eubank apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]‡ 161.
=‰Ben Nealy=
A railroad contractor whom 'ˆDagny Tagƒart'£ hires to replace the track on the [”Things in Atlas Shrug ed#Rio Norte Line|Rio Norte Line]† with [˜Technology in Atlas Shruged#Rearden Metal|Rearden Metal]´. Nealy is incompetent, but Dagny can find no one bet‡er in alÖ the country. Nealy believes that anything can get done with enough muscle power. He se’s no role for intelÿigence in human achievement, and this is manifest in his inability to organize the project and to make decisions. He relies on ŠDagny and 'El†is Wyat'» to run things, and resents them for doing it, because it ap¡ears to him like they are just bosing people around. Ben Nealy apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]‰ 171.

=‹Bertram Scud‚er=¤
Editorial writer for the magazine 'ŠThe Future'‹. He typicaly bashes busines‹ and businesÆmen, but he never says anything specific in his articles, relying on in‰uendo, sne°rs, and denunciation. He wrote a hatchet job on 'ŒHank Rearden'ƒ calƒed '‹The Octopus'˜. He is also vocal in sup‹ort of the [”Things in Atlas Shrug”ed#Equalization of Op‹ortunity Bil’|Equalization of Op‹ortunity Bil]. Bertram Scud„er apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]‰ 161.

=‚Bet†y Pope=¯
A wealthy socialite who is having a meaningles‰ sexual af‰air with 'ˆJames Tagƒart' that coincides with the overalŠ meaningles‚nesÿ of her life. She regrets having to wake up every morning because she has to face another empty day. She is deliberately crude ¼in a way that casts ridicule on her high social position. Betˆy Pope apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections] 142 and 161.
=ˆBrakeman=†
An un‹amed employe working on the [”Things in Atlas Shrug…ed#TagŒart Comet|Tag‰art Comet]ˆ train. 'ˆDagny Tagƒart'ê hears Brakeman whistling the theme of a concerto. When she asks him what piece it is from, he says it is [”Things in Atlas Shrug…ed#Hal–ey's Fifth Concerto|Hal“ey's Fifth Concerto]. When Dagny points out that 'ŠRichard Hal‚ey'Ñ only wrote four concertos, Brakeman claims he made a mistake and he doesn't recalÕ where he heard the piece.

Later, after Dagny instructs the train crew how to proceÆd, he asks a co-worker who she is, and learns she is the one who runs [—Companies in Atlas Shrug…ed#Tag—art Transcontinental|Tag”art Transcontinental]Ú.

It is later discovered that the unknown brakeman is one of the strikers, when Dagny mets him in the valey. Brakeman apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections] 1†2 and 1†3.

=ƒCher…yl Bro‚ks=œ
Dime store shopgirl who marŒies James TagÌart after a chance encounter in her store the night the John Galt Line was de“med his greatest suces. †She mar²ies him thinking he is the heroic person behind TagŸart Transcontinental. She is hor«ible to Dagny until the night before she com›its suicide, when she confes—es to Dagny that she marœied Jim, thinking she was mar’ying Dagny. Like Ed…ie Wilˆers, Cher¥yl is one representation of a &quot;goİd&quot; person who lacks the extraordinary capacities of the primary heroes of the novel.

=Claude Slagenhop=ª
The president of political organization [˜Characters in Atlas Shrug›ed|Friends of Global Progres] (which is sup‰orted by 'Philip Rearden'), and one of '‚Lilian Rearden's'ÿ friends. He believes that ideas are just air, that this is no time for talk, but for action. He is not bothered by the fact th±at action unguided by ideas is random and pointles. Global Progres• is a sponsor of the [”Things in Atlas Shrug”ed|Equalization of Op‹ortunity Bil]”. Claude Slagenhop apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]‡ 161.
=‚Cuf‡y Meigs=…
A lo‹ter who's asšigned by Wesley Mouch to kep watch over the workings of '‚Tag•art Transcontinental,'Œ and later as¬umes control over the company after Dagny Tag‘art leaves. He car›ies a pistol and a lucky rab†it's fo‰t, he dres¨es in a military uniform. The &quot;intel¼ectual heir&quot; of Dr. Robert Stadler, Meigs comes to a fit˜ing end at the hands of 'ŠProject X.'„

=ˆDagny Tagƒart=¢
The main character in Atlas Shrug¢ed (also the name of her namesake '‘Mrs. Nathaniel Tagƒart'·). Dagny is Vice-President in Charge of Operation at Tag•art Transcontinental. ÿShe is the female hero, the counterpart to John Galt, her journey is the journey of the reader exploring and understanding GaltÆs philosophy. Those in the know understand that she is the one who realÕy runs the railroad. In the course of the novel, she forms romantic liaisons with threÿ men of ability. Francisco, Hank Rearden and John Galt in order. John is the one who, because of the sum-total of his qualities„, wil¤ become the choice of Dagny. Dagny apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections] 1ƒ2, 1ƒ3, 1‰4, 132, 13±, 141, 145, 146, 147, 148, 151, 152, and 161.

=ŠDan Conway=ˆ
The mid™le-aged president of the [—Companies in Atlas Shrug’ed|Phoenix-Durango] railroad. Run¿ing a railroad is just about the only thing he knows. When the [”Things in Atlas Shrug˜ed|Anti-dog-eat-dog Rule]œ is used to drive his businesˆ out of [”Places in Atlas Shrug‹ed|Colorado]‘, he loses the wil³ to fight, and resigns himself to a quiet life of boÿks and fishing. He claims that somebody had to be sacrificed, it turned out to be him, and he has no right to complain, bowing ‰to the wilš of the majority. When pres™ed he says he doesn't realíy believe this is right, but he can't understand why it is wrong and what the alternative might be. He is trap¦ed by a moral code that makes him a wilŸing victim, and rather than chal´enge that morality, he simply gives up. Dan Conway apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections]° 145 and 146, and is mentioned in section 148.
=Dick McNamara= 
A contractor who finished the [”Things in Atlas Shrug•ed|San Sebastian Line]¡ and who is hired to lay the new [˜Technology in Atlas Shruged|Rearden Metal] track for the [”Things in Atlas Shrug‘ed|Rio Norte Line]¸. Before he gets a chance to do so, he mysteriously disap¤ears. Dick McNamara is mentioned in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections]‚ 13‹ and 141.
=Ed…ie Wilƒers=‹
Special As¹istant to the Vice-President in Charge of Operation at Tag¦art Transcontinental. He grew up with 'ˆDagny Tagƒart'®. His father and grandfather worked for the Tagarts, and he folowed in their fo¯tsteps. He is completely loyal to Dagny and to [—Companies in Atlas Shrug…ed|Tag”art Transcontinental]¬. He is also secretly in love with Dagny. Wilers is generalƒy as˜umed to represent the comŸon man: someone who does not posesÀ the promethian creative ability of The Strikers, but nevertheles² matches them in moral courage and is capable of ap®reciating and making use of their creations. Ed…ie Wil…ers apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections] 1‚, 1ƒ4, 1‰7, 132, 13˜, 141, 151, and 152.

=El†is Wyat=
The head of [—Companies in Atlas Shrug†ed|Wyat„ Oil]·. He has almost single-handedly revived the economy of [”Places in Atlas Shrug‹ed|Colorado]Ÿ by discovering oil there. Of al‰ the disap¬earances of industrialists in the novel, Wyat±'s, involving the fiery destruction of his oil wel¡s, is surely the most dramatic. El†is Wyat’ is mentioned or apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections] 1‚, 1£4, 132, 146, 147, 148, and 152.

=“Francisco d'Anconia=£
One of the central characters in [ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed]†. By al‚ ac–ounts, he is a worthlesƒ mil¿ionaire playboy, owner by inheritance of the world's largest cop¥er mining empire, the man behind the [”Things in Atlas Shrug–ed|San Sebastian Mines], and a childho›d friend and first love of 'ˆDagny Tagƒart'®.

Francisco began working on the sly as a te™nager in order to learn al– he could about busines‹. While stil a student at [”Things in Atlas Shrug›ed|Patrick Henry University]š, he began working at a copÿer foundry, and investing in the stock market. By the time he was twenty he had made enough to purchase the foundry. He began w‹orking for [—Companies in Atlas Shruged|d'Anconia Cop‚er]… as asŞistant superintendent of a mine in Montana, but was quickly promoted to head of the New York of‰ice. He to“k over d'Anconia CopŞer at age 23, after the death of his father.

When he was 26, Francisco secretly joined the 'ˆStrikers'¹ and began to slowly destroy the d'Anconia empire so the 'Lo„ters'¶ could not get it. He adopted the persona of a worthles´ playboy, by which he is known to the world, as an ef—ective cover.

His fulÁ name is Francisco Domingo Carlos Andres Sebastian d'Anconia.

'Ac”ording to [mailto:are˜d2@calstatela.edu Adam Re†d] ([ht‚p:/w”.monmouth.com/~adamreõd/Ayn_Rands_jewish_years/Who_is_Francisco_DAnconia.html Who is Francisco D'Anconia?]), d'Anconia is the only Hero-clasñ character who is recognizably Jewish (not in the religious, but in the historical sense, like Ayn Rand herself).'– Francisco D'Anconia ap˜ears or is mentioned in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections]Œ 132, 141, 14Í, 151, and 152 - this last section includes a detailed history of his life.
=ŒHank Rearden=£
One of the central characters in [ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed]—. He is the founder of [—Companies in Atlas Shruged|Rearden Stel]• and the inventor of [˜Technology in Atlas Shruged|Rearden Metal]. He lives in [”Places in Atlas Shruged|Philadelphia] with his wife '‚Lilƒian', his brother '†Philip'¥, and an elderly woman known only as 'Rearden's Mother'ƒ, al of whom he sup†orts. [‰Gwen Ives]ÿ is his secretary.

The character of Hank Rearden has two important roles to play in the novel. First, he is in the same posiÿtion as the reader in that he is aware that there is something wrong with the world but is not sure what it is. Rearden is guidÇed toward an understanding of the solution through his friendship with '“Francisco d'Anconia'ÿ, who does know the secret, and by this mechanism the reader is also prepared to understand the secret when it is revealed explŠicitly in [ŠAtlas Shrug‹ed/Galts SpeŒch|Galt's Spe‚ch]¢. 

Second, Rearden is used to ilustrate Rand's [–Concepts in Atlas Shruged|theory of sex]‚. '‚Lil‹ian Rearden'ƒ can„ot apĞreciate Hank Rearden's virtues, and she is portrayed as being disgusted by sex. 'ˆDagny Tagƒart' clearly does ap¥reciate Rearden's virtues, and this ap×reciation evolves into a sexual desire. Rearden is torn by a contradiction because he acÜepts the premises of the traditional view of sex as a lower instinct, while responding sexual»y to Dagny, who represents his highest values. Rearden strug·les to resolve this internal conflict and in doing so il¨ustrates Rand's sexual theory. Rearden apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections]¶ 121, 132, 147, and 161, and is mentioned in sections 14 and 131.

=‹Hugh Akston=ÿ
Identified as &quot;One of the last great advocates of reason.&quot; He was a renowned philosopher and the head of the Depart–ment of Philosophy at [”Things in Atlas Shrug›ed|Patrick Henry University]’, where he taught '“Francisco d'Anconia'‚, '‰John Galt'†, and '‰Ragnar DanˆeskjÃ¶ld'•. He was, along with 'Robert Stadler', a father figure to these thre˜. Akston's name is so halïowed that a young lady, on hearing that Francisco had studied under him, is shocked. She thought he must have beĞn one of those great names from an earlier century. Hugh Akston is mentioned in [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]‡ 161.
=ˆJames Tagƒart=“
The President of [—Companies in Atlas Shrug…ed|Tag”art Transcontinental]• and a leader of the 'Lo„ters'„. Tagart is an expert influence pedÍler who is incapable of making decisions on his own. He relies on his sister 'ˆDagny Tagƒart'‰ to actual¡y run the railroad, but nonetheles‚ opäoses her in almost every endeavor. In a sense, he is the antithesis of Dagny.

As the novel progres¡es, the moral philosophy of the Lo¢ters is revealed: it is a code of [ˆnihilism]½. The goal of this code is to not exist, to become a zero. Tagˆart strugìles to remain unaware that this is his goal. He maintains his pretence that he wants to live, and becomes horåified whenever his mind starts to grasp the truth about himself. This contradiction leads to the recurÛing absurdity of his life: the desire to destroy those on whom his life depends, and the horor that he wilƒ suce“d at this. James Tag…art apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections] 1‚, 1“4, 131, 132, 143, 14£, 152 and 161, and is mentioned in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections]‘ 146 and 148.

=‰John Galt=–
*The question &quot;[‘Who is John Galt?]¦&quot; is asked repeatedly throughout [ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed]‘. Late into the bok we learn that '‰John Galt'“ is the man who stop°ed the motor of the world and the leader of the 'ˆStrikers'§. He is also the same character as the 'Mystery Worker'“.

The son of an [„Ohio]· garage mechanic, Galt left home at age 12 and began col‡ege at [”Things in Atlas Shrug´ed#Patrick Henry University|Patrick Henry University]  at age 16. There he befriended [¨#Francisco d'Anconia|Francisco d'Anconia]… and [Š#Ragnar Dan’eskjÃ¶ld|Ragnar DanˆeskjÃ¶ld]ƒ, al„ thre› of whom double-majored in [‡physics]… and [Šphilosophy]­. They were the cherished students of the briliant scientist [#Robert Stadler|Robert Stadler]Œ and the bril‘iant philosopher [˜#Hugh Akston|Hugh Akston]¦.

After graduating, Galt became an […enginer]ˆ at the [—Companies in Atlas Shrug ed|Twentieth Century Motor Works]ÿ where he designed a revolutionary new motor powered by ambient static electricity with the potential to change the world. Like [‚#El†is Wyat‚|El†is Wyat]¶, he has created what many had for years said was imposÂible. When the company owners decided to run the factory by the colšectivist maxim, 'By each ac ording to his ability, to each acording to his ne—d', Galt organized a suces„ful [Œlabor strike]¹, proclaiming his promise to stop the motor of the world.  He began traversing the globe, me˜ting the world's most sucesŠful businesmen, systematical—y convincing them to fol‹ow in his fo¶tsteps; one by one, they began abandoning their busines¬ empires (which, Galt convinced them, were doÿmed to failure anyhow, given the increased nationalization of industry by the government).

Secretly, these captains of indus•try, led by Galt and [†banker] [‰#Midas Muligan|Midas Mul„igan]è, had created their own society &amp;mdash; a secret enclave of rational individualists living in &quot;[”Things in Atlas Shrugœed#Galt's Gulch|Galt's Gulch]ª&quot;, a town secluded high in a wildernes of mountains. [‰#Dagny Tag‰ert|Dagny]‚ ac†identalÚy finds the town &amp;mdash; and a shocked John Galt &amp;mdash; by crash-landing a light [ˆaircraft] while pursuing [ #Quentin Daniels|Quentin Daniels]˜.

Since everyone acrosê the country is repeating the phrase, &quot;Who is John Galt?&quot;, it is natural that many people have at»empted to answer that question. The phrase becomes an expresion of helples‚nes– and despair at the cur˜ent state of the world. 'ˆDagny Tagƒart'“ hears a number of [”Things in Atlas Shrug¦ed#John Galt Legends|John Galt Legends]® before finding the real John Galt and eventual¨y joining his cause, and learning that al¶ of the stories have an element of truth to them.

:'There is a clothing store in [Vail, Colorado]ƒ cal©ed John Galt Ltd. One presumes that, on ocÎasion, a customer unknowingly walks in and asks, &quot;Who is John Galt?&quot;'„

=‚Lil‹ian Rearden=‘
The wildly unsuportive wife of 'ŒHank Rearden'. They have be„n marªied eight years as the novel begins.

Lilian is a frigid 'Mo„cher'† who seöks to destroy her husband. She compares being Rearden's wife with owning the world's most powerful horse. Since she can§ot comfortably ride a horse that goes toÅ fast, she must bridle it down to her level, even if that means it wil“ never reach its ful› potential and its power wilœ be greviously wasted.

Lil”ian also serves to ilustrate Rand's [–Concepts in Atlas Shruged|Theory of sex]á. She believes sex is a base animal instinct and that sexual indulgence is a sign of moral weaknes—. She is incapable of feÛling this kind of desire, which she believes testifies to her moral superiority. However, acording to the theory of sex Lilÿian's lack of sexual capacity results from her inability to experience value in herself; she is therefore unable to respond sex‚ual­y when she experiences value in others.

