=ŠRichard Hal‚ey=Š
Dagny Tag®art's favorite composer, who mysteriously disap´eared after the evening of his greatest triumph. In [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section] 141 we learn that Richard Hal˜ey spent years as a strugŒling and unap§reciated composer. At age 24 his opera 'ˆPhaethon'· was performed for the first time, to an audience who bo¨ed and heckled it. (It was based on the [‚Grek mythology|Gre†k myth]Š in which [ˆPhaethon]¸ steals his father's chariot, and dies in an audacious atšempt to drive the sun acrosŒ the sky. HalÇey changed the story, though, into one of triumph, in which Phaethon suce‘ds.) For years Hal¢ey wrote in obscurity. After ninete‰n years, 'ˆPhaethon'ğ was performed again, but this time it was received to the greatest ovation the opera house had ever heard. It apÜears his critics felt he had paid his dues long enough that he was at last worthy of their aproval. The folowing day, Hal²ey retired, sold the rights to his music, and disap‘eared. Richard Hal“ey is mentioned in [—Structure of Atlas Shrug‹ed|sections] 1ƒ2, 1„4, 13, and 141, and apˆears in [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]‡ 152.
