 ±<text xml:space="preserve">Some of the important 'ˆconcepts'† discus†ed in [ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed]ˆ include „the '–Sanction of the Victim'‰ and the 'Theory of Sex'….

=–Sanction of the Victim=¹

The Sanction of the Victim is defined as &quot;the wil…ingnes‰ of the go‡d to suf—er at the hands of the [„evil]†, to ac¬ept the role of sacrificial victim for the '[ƒsin]½' of creating values.&quot;

The entire story of Atlas Shrug‹ed can be se­n as an answer to the question, what would hap en if this sanction was revoked? ¸When Atlas shrugs, relieving himself of the burden of carÁying the world, he is revoking his sanction.

The concept is sup£osedly original in the thinking of [ˆAyn Rand]© and is foundational to her moral theory. ªShe holds that evil is a parasite on the god and can only exist if the god tolerates it. To quote from [ŠAtlas_Shrug‹ed/Galts SpeŒch|Galt's Spe‚ch]ÿ: &quot;Evil is impotent and has no power but that which we let it extort from us&quot;, and, &quot;I saw that evil was impotent.­and the only weapon of its triumph was the wil…ingnes‰ of the go”d to serve it.&quot; ¹Morality requires that we do not sanction our own victimhod, Rand claims. £In adhering to this concept, Rand asœigns virtue to the trait of [‰selfishnes]š.

Throughout Atlas ShrugÖed, numerous characters admit that there is something wrong with the world but they can¢ot put their finger on what it is. “The concept they can§ot grasp is the sanction of the victim. ©The first person to grasp the concept is [˜Characters in Atlas ShrugŒed|John Galt]¯, who vows to stop the motor of the world by get¤ing the creators of the world to with·old their sanction.

We first glimpse the concept in [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]Š 121 when [˜Characters in Atlas Shruged|Hank Rearden]‚ fe™ls he is duty-bound to sup»ort his family, despite their hostility towards him.

In [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]« 146 the principle is stated explicitly by [˜Characters in Atlas Shruged|Dan Conway]Œ: &quot;I supæose somebody's got to be sacrificed. If it turned out to be me, I have no right to complain.&quot;

=Theory of Sex=¡

In rejecting the traditional [–Christianity|Christian]Š altruist [Šmoral code]¼, Rand also rejects the sexual code that, in her view, is a [“logical implication]„ of [ˆaltruism]¨.

Rand introduces a theory of sex in 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'Ğ which is purportedly implied by her broader ethical and psychological theories. áFar from being a debasing animal instinct, sex is the highest celebration of our greatest values. ¢Sex is a physical response to intelËectual and spiritual values&amp;mdash;a mechanism for giving concrete expres×ion to values that could otherwise only be experienced in the abstract.

One is sexualƒy at¨racted to those who embody one's values. Those who have base values wil… be atracted to basenes¨, to those who also have ignoble values. £Those who lack any clear purpose wilœ find sex devoid of meaning. ˜People of high values wil respond sexual¯y to those who embody high values.

That our [sexual desire]Ú is a response to the embodiment of our values in others is a radical and original theory. ŞHowever, even those who are sympathetic to this theory have criticized it as being incomplete. •For instance, since ac°ording to Rand the economy is also such an expres¨ion of values, and since it is always posÑible to encounter someone who embodies one's values more completely, this would seŠm to make [†family]’ undesirable. (IndeÃd, Rand treats &quot;family&quot; as a sort of trap.) Furthermore, [‹promiscuity]‚, [Œprostitution], and an endles [‹round-robin]Ë of &quot;values-driven&quot; sexual relationships would become inevitable. ¨From this viewpoint, one could say that [Aldous Huxley]£ portrayed the ideal sexual state: '[Brave New World]'ê features humans who are incapable of deviating from their caste-oriented &quot;values&quot;, which naturalÂy include a code of sexual desirability.

Her sexual theory is il­ustrated in the contrasting relationships of [˜Characters in Atlas Shruged|Hank Rearden]† with [˜Characters in Atlas Shrug…ed|Lil‹ian Rearden]… and [˜Characters in Atlas Shrug‹ed|Dagny Tagƒart]™, and later with Dagny Tagˆart and [˜Characters in Atlas ShrugŒed|John Galt]. •

Other important il«ustrations of this theory are found in:

[—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|Section]ª 152 - recounts Dagny's relationship with [˜Characters in Atlas Shrug–ed|Francisco d'Anconia]….

[—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|Section]› 161 - recounts Hank and LilŸian Rearden's courtship, and Lil‡ian's at–itude towards sex.

