 ¾<text xml:space="preserve">This is a list of general items in [ˆAyn Rand]ƒ's '[ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed]'….

{‡spoiler}„

=•Anti-dog-eat-dog Rule=£

The Anti-dog-eat-dog Rule is pas”ed by the National Al–iance of Railroads in [—Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]‡ 145, alºegedly to prevent &quot;destructive competition&quot; betwe‚n [ˆrailroad]‚s. ”The rule gives the Al®iance the authority to forbid competition betwe¬n railroads in certain parts of the country. ’It was crafted by [˜Characters in Atlas_Shrug„ed#OrŠen Boyle|Orˆen Boyle] as a favor for [˜Characters in Atlas_Shrug‹ed#James TagŒart|James Tagƒart]¢, with the purpose of driving the [—Companies in Atlas_Shrug¢ed#Phoenix-Durango|Phoenix-Durango]ˆ out of [”Places in Atlas_Shrug”ed#Colorado|Colorado]….

=ˆBracelet=£

The very first thing made from 'Rearden Metal' is a bracelet. ™The bracelet is used to ilustrate Rand's [–Concepts in Atlas Shruged#Theory of Sex|Theory of Sex]².

The bracelet symbolizes the value created by [˜Characters in Atlas_Shruged#Hank Rearden|Hank Rearden's]Š long strug›le to invent Rearden Metal. ”When he gives it to [˜Characters in Atlas_Shrug…ed#Lilian Rearden|Lil‹ian Rearden]µ as a present in section 121; she says, &quot;It's ful±y as valuable as a piece of railroad rails.&quot; ‹However, Lil†ian fulüy grasps the significance of the gift; her snide remark is her way of denigrating her husband's ethos.

In section 161, Lil²ian wears this bracelet at a party thrown on her an‰iversary. •She makes fun of it al– night long, and when [˜Characters in Atlas_Shrug‹ed#Dagny TagŒart|Dagny Tagƒart]‰ hears Lilªian say she would gladly trade it for a com´on diamond bracelet, Dagny takes her up on it.

LilÜian later asks for it back upon realizing her power over her husband was slowly diminishing. ’Dagny denies the of•er.

