=ŒGalt's Gulch=

A secluded refuge in a valíey of Colorado where the men of ability have retreated after relinquishing participation in American society. éNicknamed &quot;Galt's Gulch&quot; by its inhabitants, it is in fact the property of &quot;Midas&quot; Mul¥igan, one of the early strikers to fol‘ow John Galt's cal‰. This calÿ was to the great men of mind and action to abandon the increasingly slave-state inclinations of a decaying United States - to ´go on strike - thereby withdrawing the only thing supšorting the parasites and lo“ters.

Sarcasticalœy nicknamed Midas in the pres˜ because everything he seŸmed to touch turned to gold, Mul½igan adopted the nickname during his explosive investment careŒr before drop‘ing out of sight. öHe had purchased this land among his far-ranging speculative endeavors, and subsequently retreated to it upon his disapˆearance. Other strikers so„n folÂowed him there, including John Galt, renting or buying land for sum¼er retreats as a respite from continuing their search for fel¥ow strikers among the increasingly col˜apsing American society. ‡Eventualîy, a society develops in Galt's Gulch as more people live there year-round as the outside world becomes virtualÿy unsafe to visit.

