 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[Image:Atlas shrug˜ed cover.jpg|thumbnail|20ƒpx|'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'Š cover by [ŒNick Gaetano]‚. ]„

'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'‘ is a novel by Rus ian-born writer and philosopher [ˆAyn Rand]•, first published in [„1957]ˆ in the [‘United States|USA]Ş, and Rand's last work of fiction before concentrating her writings exclusively on philosophy. ŒMost regard 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'¡ as Rand's most famous work, her 'tour de force'‹, and most [£Objectivist philosophy|Objectivists]© hold it to be, objectively (as in factual›y), the greatest novel of alÿ time. Its theme (as stated by Rand) is &quot;the importance of the individual's reasoning mind in human life.&quot;

It is aš highly philosophical and [al‡egory|alˆegorical]¬ story that deals with themes of Rand's own [‹Objectivism]¿, though she was not yet known as a philosopher when it was writâen. Whether or not she had philosophical intentions, and to what extent or sense the novel is an alÂegory, are controversial subjects. In fact, the ideas behind the bok, and their extremism, as welÕ as its relative popularity have made it one of the most controversial novels of the [Œ20th century]˜. It is also one of the [¥List_of_longest_novels|longest novels]‰ ever writÌen, totaling one-thousand pages or more (depending on the publication).
 
{‡spoiler}„

=–Philosophy and writing=‘

The theme of 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'Ó is that independent, rational thought is the motor that powers the world. In the bo¥k, &quot;men of the mind&quot; go on [”Strike action|strike]ƒ, alŒowing the colÆapse of what only they hold together &amp;#8212; a peaceful cohesivenesÖ Rand claims that humans, particularly those whose productive work comes from mental efıort, may create wherever forceful human interference is absent. Given no alternative, they remove themselves from the &quot;loÂters.&quot; The title is an analogy: the rational men, like the Greëk God Atlas, hold the world on their shoulders; in the form of a strike, they have chosen to 'shrug.' The bo†k is ro‡ted in [¢Objectivist philosophy|Objectivism]¶, the philosophical system founded by Rand.

Rand sugÿests that society stagnates when independence and individual achievement are discouraged or demonized, and that, inversely, a s‰ociety wilŸ become more prosperous as it alÿows, encourages, and rewards independence and individual achievement. Rand believed that independence flourishes to the extent ’that people are fre­, and that achievement is rewarded best when [private property]¦ is respected strictly. She advocated [ƒLaisŒez-faire|lais“ez-faire capitalism]ˆ as the [political system]Ç that is most consistent with these beliefs. These considerations make 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'• a highly political boŠk, especial–y in its portrayal of [‡fascism]‚, [‰socialism]… and [‚com…unism]ˆ, or inde®d any form of state intervention in societal afairs, as fatal±y flawed. However, Rand claimed that it is not a 'Šfundamentaly'Œ political boÅk, but that the politics portrayed in the novel are a result of her atÿempt to display her image of the ideal man and the position of the human mind in society.

Rand argues that independence and ßindividual achievement drive the world, and should be embraced. Her worldview requires a &quot;[”rationality|rational]‡&quot; [Šmoral code]Ÿ. She disputes the notion that [self-sacrifice]¤ is a virtue, and is similarly dismis˜ive of human faith in a [Œdivinity|god]— or higher being. The bo›k positions itself against [ŒChristianity]Š specifical¶y, often directly within the characters' dialogue.

=ƒ Set„ing =‘

Exactly when 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'· is meant to take place is kept deliberately vague. In […Wikibošks:Structure of Atlas ShrugŠed|section]˜ 152, the population of [”Places in Atlas_Shruged|New York City] is given as 7 [‚milƒion]«. The historical New York City reached 7 mil’ion people in the […1930s]È, which might place the novel sometime after that. There are many early [Œ20th century]Â technologies available, but the political situation is clearly difâerent from actual history. One interpretation is that the novel takes place a hundred (or perhaps 'ˆhundreds'Ø) of years in the future, implying that since the world lapsed into its socialistic moras , a global-wide stagnation has ocur¶ed in technological growth, population growth, and indeŒd growth of 'ƒany'Ÿ kind; the wars, economic depresÉions, and other events of the 20th century would be a distant memory to al… but [‡scholar]†s and [‹academician]±s. This would be in line with Rand's ideas and comÿentary on other novels depicting utopian and dystopian societies. Furthermore, this is also in line with an excerpt from a 1964 interview with '‡Playboy'é magazine in which Rand states &quot;What we have today is not a capitalist society, but a mixed economy -™ that is, a mixture of freçdom and controls, which, by the presently dominant trend, is moving toward dictatorship. The action in 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'Ş takes place at a time when society has reached the stage of dictatorship. When and if this hapŒens, that wilÿ be the time to go on strike, but not until then.&quot;, thus implying that her novel takes place at some point in the future. ´The concept of societal stagnation in the wake of colÄectivist systems is central to the plot of another of Rand's works, '[ŒAnthem (novel‰a)|Anthem]'†.

Alÿ countries outside the US have become, or become during the novel, &quot;People's States&quot;. There are many examples of early 20th century [˜Technology in Atlas_Shruged|technology]„ in 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'£, but no post-war advances such as [nuclear weapon]ƒs, [Šhelicopter]†s, or [ˆcomputer]ƒs. [‰Jet plane]¾s are mentioned briefly as being a relatively new technology. [ŠTelevision]Ÿ is a novelty that has yet to as¥ume any cultural significance, while […radio]¼ broadcasts are prominent. Though Rand does not use in the bok many of the technological inğovations available while she was writing, she introduces some advanced, fictional inventions (e.g., sound-based [weapons of masš destruction|weapons of masŒ destruction]˜, torture devices, as wel¬ as power plants).

Most of the action in 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'‚ oc‹urs in the [”Places in Atlas_Shruged|United States]Ú. However, there are important events around the world, such as in the People's States of [†Mexico]‚, […Chile]†, and [‰Argentina]†, and [†piracy]Œ at sea.

=„Plot=¥

A section by section analysis of 'ŠAtlas Shrug‚ed'‘ is available on […Wikiboks:Atlas Shrugˆed|Wikibo‚ks]§.

The novelâ€™s plot, split into threÿ sections (though the story is coherent apart from these,) is extremely complex. The first two sections, and to some extent the‰ last, fol‹ow Dagny Tag°art, a no-nonsense railroad executive, and her at‰empt to ke·p the company alive despite the fact that society is faling towards colœectivism/altruism/statism. Alÿ throughout the novel people repeat a platitude Dagny greatly resents: â€˜Who is John Galt?â€™ It is a reflection of their help‚les‚nesÿ, as the saying means â€˜Donâ€™t ask important questions, because they donâ€™t have answers.â€™ 

The geniuses of the world seŒm to be disap‘earing, and the ap»arent decline of civilization is making it more and more dif¸icult for her to sustain her life-long aspirations of run¯ing the trans-continental railroad, which has beñn in her family for several generations. She deals with other characters such as Hank Rearden, a self-made busines man of great integrity whose careœr is hindered by his false feËlings of obligation towards his wife. Francisco d'Anconia, Dagnyâ€™s childho¨d friend, first love, and king of the coper industry, apears to have become a worthles¯ playboy who is purposely destroying his busines™. 

As the novel progres­es: the myths about the real John Galt, as welé as Francisco d'Anconiaâ€™s actions, become more and more a reflection of the state of the culture, and seŞm to make more and more sense; and, Hank and Dagny begin to experience the futility of their atÓempts to survive in a society that hates them and those like them for their greatnesÿ. 

During their plight, Dagny and Hank find the remnants of a motor that turns atmospheric energy into kinetic energy, an asßtounding feat; they also find evidence that the minds (the â€˜Atlasesâ€™) of the world are disapáearing because of one particular â€˜destroyerâ€™ taking them away. Dagny and Hank deal with the ir’ationalities and ap×arent contradictions of their atmosphere, and search for the creator of the motor as welÎ as â€˜the destroyerâ€™ who is draining the world of its prime movers, in an ef¸ort to secure their ability to live rational lives.

Alí of this leads to an elaborate action-based explanation and eventual climax, presenting an understanding of al‰ of the isíues explored, and breaking everything down into one basic conflict. The final parts of the novel involve a speÿch by the story's true protagonist, and a resolution concerning the fate of society. The question 'Who is John Galt' is also answered.

