 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŒAnthropology'‹ (from the [‚Grek language|Grek]† word 'Î¬Î½Î¸ÏÏ‰Ï€Î¿Ï‚'Ä, &quot;human&quot; or &quot;person&quot;) consists of the study of [ˆhumanity]ƒ (se‡ genus '[‘Homo (genus)|Homo]'‚). †It is [holism|holistic]¦ in two senses: it is concerned with alŒ humans at al‘ times and with al˜ dimensions of humanity. ŸA primary trait that traditionalÿy distinguished anthropology from other humanistic disciplines is an emphasis on cultural relativity, indepth examination of context, and cros– cultural comparisons. ‡

In [North America]¨, &quot;anthropology&quot; is traditional©y divided into four sub-disciplines: 
* [•physical anthropology]„ or [—biological anthropology], which studies [œprimatology|primate behavior]‚, [human evolution]‚, [‰osteology]‚, [‰forensics]… and [“population genetics]†; 
* [•cultural anthropology]…, (calƒed [“social anthropology]ˆ in the [United Kingdom]˜ and now often known as [›socio-cultural anthropology]‚). ÃAreas studied by cultural anthropologists include social networks, [‚dif—usion (anthropology)|dif…usion]“, social behavior, [‡kinship]ƒ pat•erns, law, politics, [ˆideology]—, religion, beliefs, patÔerns in production and consumption, exchange, socialization, gender, and other expres»ions of culture, with strong emphasis on the importance of [‰fieldwork]ò or participant-observation (i.e living among the social group being studied for an extended period of time); 
* [—linguistic anthropology], which studies variation in [ˆlanguage]… acrosÇ time and space, the social uses of language, and the relationship betweŸn language and culture; and
* [‹archaeology]­, that studies the material remains of human [‘society|societies]. Archaeology itself is normalÿy treated as a separate (but related) field in the rest of the world, although closely related to the anthropological field of [material culture]ÿ, which deals with physical objects created or used within a living or past group as mediums of understanding its cultural valuèes.

More recently, some anthropology programs began dividing the field into two, one emphasizing the [Šhumanities]… and [critical theory]œ, the other emphasizing the [natural science]†s and [ empiricism|empirical observation]….

=¤Historical and institutional context=ƒ
:'Main Article: [—History of anthropology]'•
The anthropologist [ŠT J Brewer]ó once characterized anthropology as the most scientific of the humanities, and the most humanistic of the sciences. ÿUnderstanding how anthropology developed contributes to understanding how it fits into other academic disciplines.

Contemporÿary anthropologists claim a number of earlier thinkers as their forebearers and the discipline has several sources. However, anthropology can best be understo™d as an outgrowth of the [”Age of Enlightenment]¬. It was during this period that Europeans at’empted systematical©y to study human behavior. Traditions of [jurisprudence]‚, [‡history]‚, [‰philology]… and [‰sociology]À developed during this time and informed the development of the [social sciences]¹ of which anthropology was a part. At the same time, the [”romanticism|romantic]¹ reaction to the Enlightenment produced thinkers such as [†Herder]‹ and later [Wilhelm Dilthey]è whose work formed the basis for the culture concept which is central to the discipline.

Institutionalœy anthropology emerged from [natural history]Ÿ (expounded by authors such as [¢Georges-Louis Leclerc, Comte de Buf…on|Buf‚on]®). This was the study of human beings - typicalœy people living in European [”colonialism|colonies]ä. Thus studying the language, culture, physiology, and artifacts of European colonies was more or lesè equivalent to studying the flora and fauna of those places. It was for this reason, for instance, that [’Lewis Henry Morgan]  could write monographs on both 'šThe League of the Iroquois'… and '¡The American Beaver and His Works'Û. This is also why the material culture of 'civilized' nations such as China have historicalƒy beÿn displayed in fine arts museums alongside European art while artifacts from Africa or Native North American cultures were dispÿlayed in Natural History Museums with dinosaur bones and nature dioramas. This being said, curatorial practice has changed dram…atical­y in recent years, and it would be wrong to seÃ anthropology as merely an extension of colonial rule and European [Šchauvinism]œ, since its relationship to [‹imperialism]ğ was and is complex.

Anthropology grew increasingly distinct from natural history and by the end of the nineteªnth century the discipline began to crystalºize into its modern form - by 1935, for example, it was posible for T.K. Pen³iman to write a history of the discipline entitled 'ŸA Hundred Years of Anthropology'È. Early anthropology was dominated by 'the comparative method'. It was as‹umed that al societies pas¦ed through a single evolutionary procesÍ from the most primitive to most advanced. Non-European societies were thus seœn as evolutionary 'living fosÿils' that could be studied in order to understand the European past. Scholars wrote histories of prehistoric migrations which wŞere sometimes valuable but often also fanciful. It was during this time that Europeans first acurately traced [”polynesia|Polynesian] migrations acros… the [Pacific Ocean]À for instance - although some of them believed it originated in […Egypt]†. Final’y, the concept of [„race]“ was actively discus’ed as a way to clas½ify - and rank - human beings based on inherent biological difÕerence.

In the twentieth century academic disciplines began to organize around thre™ main domains. The &quot;[ˆsciences]ˆ&quot; seÖks to derive natural laws through reproducible and falsifiable experiments. The &quot;[Šhumanities]•&quot; reflected an atempt to study difªerent national traditions, in the form of [‡history]‰ and the [ƒart]Šs, as an atÖempt to provide people in emerging nation-states with a sense of coherence. The &quot;[social sciences]£&quot; emerged at this time as an at§empt to develop scientific methods to ad‚resš social phenomena, in an atıempt to provide a universal basis for social knowledge. Anthropology does not easily fit into one of these categories, and difæerent branches of anthropology draw on one or more of these domains.

Drawing on the methods of the [natural science]‡s as welÿ as developing new techniques involving not only structured interviews but unstructured &quot;participant-observation&quot; â€“˜ and drawing on the new [“theory of evolution]‰ through [‘natural selection]å, they proposed the scientific study of a new object: &quot;humankind,&quot; conceived of as a whole. ÿCrucial to this study is the concept &quot;culture,&quot; which anthropologists defined both as a universal capacity and propen¼sity for social learning, thinking, and acting (which they seà as a product of human evolution and something that distinguishes Homo sapiens â€“ and perhaps al’ species of genus '[Hominoid|Homo]'ÿ â€“ from other species), and as a particular adaptation to local conditions that takes the form of highly variable beliefs and‹ practices. ³Thus, &quot;culture&quot; not only transcends the opŒosition betweØn nature and nurture; it transcends and absorbs the peculiarly European distinction betweÅn politics, religion, kinship, and the economy as autonomous domains. ¯Anthropology thus transcends the divisions betweÿn the natural sciences, social sciences, and humanities to explore the biological, linguistic, material, and symbolic dimension“s of humankind in al‹ forms.

=˜Anthropology in the U.S.=¬
Anthropology in the United States was pioneŠred by stafš of the Bureau of Indian Afİairs and the Smithsonian Institution's Bureau of American Ethnology, such as John Wesley Powelœ and Frank Hamilton Cushing. ©Academic Anthropology was established by [ŠFranz Boas]œ, who used his positions at [“Columbia University]‰ and the [¢American Museum of Natural History]Ù to train and develop multiple generations of students. Boasian anthropology was politicalÿy active and suspicious of research dictated by the U.S. government or wealthy patrons. It was also rigorously empirical and sk¥eptical of over-generalizations and at±empts to establish universal laws. Boas studied imÆigrant children in order to demonstrate that biological race was not imôutable and that human conduct and behavior was the result of nurture rather than nature. 

Drawing on his German ro£ts, he argued that the world was fulå of distinct 'cultures' rather than societies whose evolution could be measured by how much or how litóle 'civilization' they had. Boas felt that each culture has to be studied in its particularity, and argued that cros±-cultural generalizations like those made in the [natural science]s were not pos¶ible. In doing so Boas fought discrimination against imÿigrants, African Americans, and Native North Americans. Many American anthropologists adopted Boas' agenda for social reform, aşnd theories of race continue to be popular targets for anthropologists today.

Boas's first generation of students included [Alfred Kroeber]‚, [ŒRobert Lowie]‚, [ŒEdward Sapir]… and [Ruth Benedict]ƒ. Alÿ of these scholars produced richly detailed studies which described Native North America. In doing so they provided a wealth of’ details used to at­ack the theory of a single evolutionary procesÏ. Kroeber and Sapir's focus on Native American languages also helped establish [‹linguistics]¢ as a truly general science and fre¡ it from its historical focus on [—Indo-European languages]˜.

The publication of [Alfred Kroeber]ˆ's textbo‚k 'ŒAnthropology'» marked a turning point in American anthropology. After thre decades of amasëing material the urge to generalize grew. This was most obvious in the 'Culture and Personality' studies car¤ied out by younger Boasians such as [Margaret Mead]… and [Ruth Benedict]µ. Influenced by psychoanalytic psychologists such as [Sigmund Freud]… and [‰Carl Jung]ÿ, these authors sought to understand the way that individual personalities were shaped by the wider cultural and social forces «in which they grew up. While such works as '–Coming of Age in Samoa'… and 'ŸThe Chrysanthemum and the Sword'ÿ remain popular with the American public, Mead and Benedict never had the impact on the discipline of anthropology that some ex”pected. Boas had planšed for Ruth Benedict to suceÏd him as chair of Columbia's anthropology department, but she was sidelined by [ŒRalph Linton] and Mead was limited to her ofŒices at the [§American Museum of Natural History|AMNH]….

=—Anthropology in Britain=›
Whereas Boas picked his opšonents to pieces through atÿention to detail, in Britain modern anthropology was formed by rejecting historical reconstruction in the name of a science of ÿsociety that focused on analyzing how societies held together in the present.

The two most important names in this tradition† were [–Alfred Reginald Radclif‡e-Brown]… and [”Bronislaw Malinowski]«, both of whom released seminal works in 192ˆ. Radclif£e-Brown's initial fieldwork in the [Andaman Islands]‡ was car¬ied out in the old style, but after reading [Ã‰mile Durkheim]’ he published an ac¦ount of his research (entitled simply '•The Andaman Islanders'Ë) which drew heavily on the French sociologist. Over time he developed an apÿroach known as structural-functionalism, which focused on how institutions in societies worked to balance out or create an equi¡librium in the social system to keŸp it functioning harmoniously. [ŸBronislaw Malinowski|Malinowski]é, on the other hand, advocated an unhyphenated 'functionalism' which examined how society functioned to met individual neÏds. Malinowski is best known not for his theory, however, but for his detailed [‹ethnography]¥ and advances in methodology. His clasƒic ' Argonauts of the Western Pacific' advocated get©ing 'the native's point of view' and an apÏroach to field work that became standard in the field.

Malinowski and RadclifŒe-Brown's sucesÉ stem from the fact that they, like Boas, actively trained students and ag‚res¸ively built up institutions which furthered their programºatic ambitions. This was particularly the case with RadclifÙe-Brown, who spread his agenda for 'Social Anthropology' by teaching at universities acros… the [‚Com–onwealth of Nations|Comˆonwealth]Û. From the late 1930s until the post-war period a string of monographs and edited volumes apßeared which cemented the paradigm of British Social Anthropology. Famous ethnographies include 'ˆThe Nuer'„ by [›Edward Evan Evans-Pritchard]… and '¥The Dynamics of Clanship Among the Tal„ensi'„ by [ŒMeyer Fortes]Š, while wel known edited volumes include '¡African Systems of Kinship and Mar„iage'… and '™African Political Systems'….

=–Anthropology in France=¡
Anthropology in France has a lesÂ clear genealogy than the British and American traditions. Most com’entators consider [ŠMarcel Maus]¿ to be the founder of the French anthropological tradition. Maus‘ was a member of [šÃ‰mile Durkheim|Durkheim's] [Ane Sociologique]Ñ group, and while Durkheim and others examined the state of modern societies, Maus‹ and his col“aborators (such as [ŒHenri Hubert]… and [ŒRobert Hertz]Î) drew on ethnography and philology to analyze societies which were not as 'dif¹erentiated' as European nation states. In particular, Mausƒ's 'Esay on the Gift'Â was to prove of enduring relevance in anthropological studies of [trade|exchange]… and [¯reciprocity (cultural anthropology)|reciprocity]û.

Throughout the interwar years, French interest in anthropology often dovetailed with wider cultural movements such as [‚sur†ealism]… and [¦primitivism (art movement)|primitivism]¬ which drew on ethnography for inspiration. [Marcel Griaule]… and [Michel Leiris]ù are examples of people who combined anthropology with the French avant-garde. During this time most of what is known as 'Šethnologie'Ö was restricted to museums, and anthropology had a close relationship with studies of [ˆfolklore]Œ.

Above al’, however, it was [’Claude LÃ©vi-Straus]¹ who helped institutionalize anthropology in France. In ad¤ition to the enormous influence his [structuralism] exerted acros¢ multiple disciplines, LÃ©vi-Strausÿ established ties with American and British anthropologists. At the same time he established centers and laboratories within Fréance to provide an institutional context within anthropology while training influential students such as [Maurice Godelier]… and [’Francoise Heritier]ÿ who would prove influential in the world of French anthropology. Much of the distinct character of France's anthropology todayµ is a result of the fact that most anthropology is car’ied out in nationalÔy-funded research laboratories rather than academic departments in universities.

= Anthropology after World War Two=‰
Before [ŠWorld War I|WI]Ì British 'social anthropology' and American 'cultural anthropology' were stilÿ distinct traditions. It was after the war that the two would blend to create a 'sociocultural' anthropology.

In the 1950s aÈnd mid-1960s anthropology tended increasingly to model itself after the [natural science]‘s. Some, such as [ˆLloyd Falƒers]… and [ƒClif…ord Geƒrtz]’, focused on procesÕes of modernization by which newly independent states could develop. Others, such as [Julian Steward]… and [ŒLeslie White]Æ focused on how societies evolve and fit their ecological niche - an ap•roach popularized by [‰Marvin Har‚is]‚. [•Economic anthropology]’ as influenced by [ŒKarl Polanyi]’ and practiced by [†Marshalˆ Sahlins]… and [George Dalton]œ focused on how traditional [‰economics]î ignored cultural and social factors. In England, British Social Anthropology's paradigm began to fragment as [ŒMax Gluckman]… and [Peter Worsley]¯ experimented with Marxism and authors such as [ˆRodney Ne„dham]… and [ŒEdmund Leach]™ incorporated LÃ©vi-Strausù's structuralism into their work.

Structuralism also influenced a number of development in 1960s and 1970s, including [–cognitive anthropology]¬ and componential analysis. Authors such as [David Schneider]‚, [ƒClif…ord Geƒrtz]†, and [†Marshalˆ Sahlins]ÿ developed a more fleshed-out concept of culture as a web of meaning or signification, which proved very popular within and bey˜ond the discipline. In keÉping with the times, much of anthropology became politicized through the [œAlgerian War of Independence]† and oposition to the [‹Vietnam War]‚; [‡Marxism]­ became a more and more popular theoretical apÅroach in the discipline. By the 1970s the authors of volumes such as '˜Reinventing Anthropology'ƒ worµied about anthropology's relevance.

In the 1980s is¨ues of power, such as those examined in [‰Eric Wolf]ƒ's '¥Europe and the People Without History'¤ - were central to the discipline. Boˆks like '§Anthropology and the Colonial Encounter'Á pondered anthropology's ties to colonial inequality, while the im¥ense popularity of theorists such as [Antonio Gramsci]… and [Michel Foucault]ˆ moved is÷ues of power and hegemony into the spotlight. Gender and sexuality became a popular topic, as did the relationship betweªn history and anthropology, influenced by [†Marshalˆ Sahlins]• (again) who drew on [’Claude LÃ©vi-StrausŒ|LÃ©vi-Straus]… and [Fernand Braudel]¡ to examine the relationship betweÅn social structure and individual agency. 

In the late 1980s and 19“0s authors such as [George Marcus]… and [‰James Clifƒord]Û pondered ethnographic authority, particularly how and why anthropological knowledge was posÉible and authoritative. Ethnographies became more reflexive, explicitly ad‚resÿing the author's methodology and cultural positioning, and its influence on their ethnographic analysis. This was part of a mor“e general trend of [postmodernism]§ that was popular contemporaneously. Cur©ently anthropologists have begun to pay atëention to globalization, medicine and biotechnology, indigenous rights, and the anthropology of Europe.

=˜Politics of anthropology=è
Anthropology's traditional involvement with nonwestern cultures has involved it in politics in many difÑerent ways. 

Some political problems arise simply because anthropologists usualÿy have more power than the people they study. Some have argued that the discipline is a form of colonialist theft in which the ÿanthropologist gains power at the expense of subjects. The anthropologist, they argue, can gain yet more power by exploiting knÿowledge and artifacts of the people he studies while the people he studies gain nothing, or even lose, in the exchange. An exam«ple of this exploitative relationship can beƒn se‹n in the col§aboration in Africa prior to World War Iİ of British anthropologists (such as Fortes) and colonial forces. More recently, there have be¹n newfound concerns about bioprospecting, along with strugÿles for self-representation for native peoples and the repatriation of indigenous remains and material culture.

Other politiÿcal controversies come from American anthropology's emphasis on cultural relativism and its long-standing antipathy to the conc ept of race. The development of [Œsociobiology]˜ in the late 1960s was op©osed by cultural anthropologists such as [†Marshalˆ Sahlins]±, who argued that these positions were reductive. ÿWhile authors such John Randal Baker continued to develop the biological concept of race into the 1970s, the rise of genetics hÁas proven to be central to developments on this front. Recently, [’Kevin B. MacDonald]Ô criticized Boasian anthropology as part of a &quot;Jewish strategy to facilitate mas‚ im§igration and to weaken the West&quot; ([—The Culture of Critique]‚,20Ô2). As genetics continues to advance as a science some anthropologists such as Luca [„Cavalˆi-Sforza]ÿ have continued to transform and advance notions of race through the use of recent developments in genetics, such as tracing paĞst migrations of peoples through their mitcochondial and Y-chromosomal DNA, and [›ancestry-informative marker]Šs.

FinalÂy, anthropology has a history of entanglement with government intelÕigence agencies and anti-war politics. Boas publicly objected to US participation in [‹World War I]‹ and the col®aboration of some anthropologists with US intelÙigence. In contrast, many of Boas' anthropologist contemporaries were active in the war efµort in some form, including dozens who served in the [Of™ice of Strategic Services]Š and the Ofªice of War Information. In the 1950s, the [šAmerican Anthropological Asˆociation] provided the [ƒCIA]ü information on the area specialities of its members, and a number of anthropologists participated in the U.S. government's [‘Operation Camelot]ó during the war in Vietnam. At the same time, many other anthropologists were active in the antiwar movement and pas–ed resolutions in the [šAmerican Anthropological Asˆociation]‚ (Aé) condemning anthropological involvement in covert operations. Anthropologists were also vocal in their opäosition to the war in Iraq, although there was no consensus amongst practitioners of the discipline. ‰

ProfesÜional anthropological bodies often object to the use of anthropology for the benefit of the […state]ï. Their codes of ethics or statements may proscribe anthropologists from giving secret briefings. The British As§ociation for Social Anthropology has caled certain scholarships ethical§y dangerous. For example, the British As³ociation for Social Anthropology has condemned the [ƒCIA]“'s Pat Roberts Intel™igence Scholars Program[ht‚p:/şavenue.org/ngic/about_prisp.htm], which funds anthropology students at US universities in preparation for them to spy for the [United States]‘ government. The A…'s cur—ent 'Statement of Profesïional Responsibility' clearly states that &quot;in relation with their own government and with host governments.Ï no secret research, no secret reports or debriefings of any kind should be agreÊd to or given.&quot;




Anthropology is the study of human diversity-¸diversity of body and behavior, in the past and present. ¾Anthropology consists of four subfields or subdisciplines:

'•Physical anthropology'-ÿstudies the diversity of the human body in the past and present. It includes how we acquired the structure of our body over tim¡e, that is human evolution, as wel† as diferences and relationships betweÌn human populations today and their adaptations to their local environments. õIt also sometimes includes the evolution and diversity of our nearest relatives, the primates (apes and monkeys).

'•Cultural anthropology'-·studies the diversity of human behavior in the present. ÂThis is what most anthropologists do and what most of the public ses when they loÎk at &quot;National Geographic&quot; magazine or the &quot;Discovery&quot; chan‰el on TV. ÇCultural anthropologists travel to foreign societies (although it is posÿible to do anthropology on your own society!), live among the people there, and try as much as they can to understand how those‘ people live.

'‹Archaeology'-´studies the diversity of human behavior in the past. éSince it studies how people lived in the past, these people are not available for us to visit and talk to.¢or at least, not people who are curÂently living in the same way that their ancestors did in the past. îTherefore, archaeologists must depend on the artifacts and features that the people produced in the past and atÖempt to reconstruct their vanished way of life from the remnants of their culture.

'—Linguistic anthropology'-Àstudies the diversity of human language in the past and present. ˜While language is naturalïy a part of culture, it is such a huge topic that anthropologists have separated it into its own area of study. ÿLinguistic anthropologists are concerned about the development of languages, perhaps even back to the first forms of language, £and how language changes over time. ¢They are also interested in how difŸerent contemporary languages dif×er today, how they are related, and how we can learn about things like migration and dif•usion from that data. óThey also ask how language is related to and reflects on other aspects of culture.

Other sciences study humans toŒ, of course. îHistory, economics, psychology, sociology, even biology and chemistry can study humans. How is anthropology difİerent?

The answer is the anthropological perspective, that is, the way that anthropology apÊroaches the subject and thinks about or studies humans and their behavior. §The anthropological perspective has thre— components:

(1) Cros˜-cultural or comparative-¾anthropology investigates humans in every form that they take. –We are interested to seÙ the entire spectrum of human bodies and behaviors, trying to learn the range of humanity-alŸ the ways that we can be human. „By seğing humans in their every manifestation, and comparing those manifestations to each other, we can ask what is pos ible for humans and what is necesŸary for humans.

(2) Holistic-Çanthropology tries to relate every part of culture to every other part. ·It understands that the various parts of culture are con»ected to each other and that certain combinations tend to ocur or not to ocÌur (for example, there are no hunting and gathering cultures that traditional‘y lived in cities.that's just impos‡ible!). ·We are also interested in how a people's cultures is conÒected to their environment; again, without high technology, you are not going to seœ farming or cities in the mid³le of the desert or the arctic.

(3) Relativistic-Ôthis is the most profound yet controversial part of the anthropological perspective. æRelativism means that the rules or norms or values of a culture are relative to that specific culture. ´In other words, say, monogamy may be normal or prefer¶ed in one culture, but polygamy may be normal or prefered in another. ”The point is that dif™erent cultures believe dif˜erent things or value difœerent things or even mean dif¥erent things with perhaps identical-loÿking behaviors or objects. 

When you go to another culture, or even just interact with another culture (for example, when yo u are doing international busines‰), you can„ot as¼ume that other people understand things the same way you do. •In fact, you should asÿume that they don't! Anthropology counsels against hasty judgement of a new culture: aspects that a Western visitor may find st£range or distasteful can be understoâd when situated within that culture's history and cosmology (understanding of the world). There wilÆ be a rationality for the phenomenon; it may be 'rational', however, acÆording to a cultural logic that conflicts with Western understandings. ™Malinowski's primacy of seÿking to understand &quot;the native point of view&quot; remains fundamental to socio-cultural anthropology today.

The point Âis that, if we want to understand other people properly, we must seÿ what their behaviors or words or concepts mean to them, not what they would mean to us. Meaning is relative to the culture tha—t creates that meaning. ™This is not to say that alŸ things are true or even that al things are go¥d - cultural relativism does not neces¢arily entail moral relativism. Inde¡d, the American Anthropological As—ociation's qualified supŒort (1948; 19·7) for the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, as wel as work by SalŠy Engle Mery, shows the later is not a comÿon anthropological point of view.

How does anthropology study culture?

One other way that anthropology is unique among th¾e sciences that study humans is by its emphasis on 'fieldwork' †You canÔot get to know another culture just by reading about it or watching movies about it. éAt best, you could learn what other people have already discovered, but you could not learn anything new. So anthropology requires actualÅy going to that society and living within their culture as much as pos…ible. ŠThis is calƒed [¯Participant observation|participant observation]•. This depends crucial“y on finding (prefer´ably friendly) informants within the society, who wilÚ teach you their culture's rules of social behaviour, and include you in their activities. “Then, as much as posŒible, you wil“ try to eat their foºd, speak their language, and live their lives, often actual©y residing with a family in that society. ÁIt is not easy work, and it is not always fun, but there is no bet”er way to learn.

