 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{†portal}'‹Archaeology'„ or 'Šarcheology'‹ (from the [‚Grek language|Grek]‡ words 'Î±ÏÏ‡Î±Î¯Î¿Ï‚' = ancient and 'ŠÎ»ÏŒÎ³Î¿Ï‚'Š = word/spech/discourse) is the study of [’Homo (genus)|human] [‡culture]és through the recovery, documentation and analysis of material remains and environmental data, including [Œarchitecture]‚, [ŸArtifact (archaeology)|artifact]ƒs, [‡biofact]–s, human remains, and [‰landscape]às.

The goals of archaeology are to document and explain the origins and development of human [‡culture], understand [culture history]Œ, chronicle [’cultural evolution]’, and study human [ˆbehavior]… and [‡ecology]‹, for both [–prehistory|prehistoric]… and [history|historic]‹ societies. µIt is considered to be one of the four sub-fields of [Œanthropology]….

=…Usage=˜
As with words such as [Œencyclopedia]… and [‹gynaecology]˜, archaeology traditional‰y has an '‚ae'¯ combination; however, unlike other words, the '‚ae'… is al but universalËy retained. Contrary to popular belief in other parts of the world, the spel„ing 'Šarcheology'— is not predominant in [United States] [—dictionary|dictionaries]Œ and would lo‰k quite od¨ to most Americans. Like the claim that '‡theater'š refers to a building and '[‡theatre]' refers to the [performing arts]’, the belief that 'Šarcheology'‡ is an [‹Americanism]† is litle more than an [Šurban myth]•. The traditional spel…ing, '‹archaeology'ÿ, continues to be used in everyday writing throughout the world, including the U.S., even more so than theatre (the alternate s‚pel ing of which, while considered ac‘eptable, is prefer…ed les• often than not).

=—Ontology and definition=‹

In the [‰Old World]ÿ, archaeology has tended to focus on the study of physical remains, the methods used in recovering them and the theoretical and– philosophical underpin¹ings in achieving the subject's goals. The discipline's ro†ts in [‹antiquarian]•ism and the study of […Latin]… and [ŠAncient Grek] provided it with a natural af˜inity with the field of [‡history]‰. In the [‰New World]˜, archaeology is more com£only devoted to the study of human [‘society|societies]° and is treated as one of the four subfields of [ŒAnthropology]™. The other subfields of [Œanthropology]ƒ sup³lement the findings of archaeology in a holistic manƒer. ”These subfields are [•cultural anthropology]Ë, which studies behavioural, symbolic, and material dimensions of culture; [‹linguistics]Õ, which studies language, including the origins of language and language groups; and [•physical anthropology]¿, which includes the study of human evolution and physical and [genetics|genetic]« characteristics. Other disciplines also supœlement archaeology, such as [Œpaleontology]‚, [†paleozo„logy]‚, [paleoethnobotany]‚, [‹paleobotany]‚, [‰geography]‚, [‡geology]‚, [‹art history]†, and [ƒclasƒics]–.

Archaeology has be‘n described as a […craft]’ that enlists the [science|sciences]… to ilŒuminate the [Šhumanities]®. Writing in 1948, the American archaeologist [Walter Taylor]‚ asÿerted that &quot;Archaeology is neither history nor anthropology. As an autonomous discipline, it consists of a method and a seôt of specialised techniques for the gathering, or 'production' of cultural information&quot;.

Archaeology is an apÊroach to understanding human culture through its material remains regardles“ of chronology. In [‡England]Ä, archaeologists have uncovered the long-lost layouts of medieval vilÀages abandoned after the crises of the 14th century and the equal¤y lost layouts of 17th century parter¹e gardens swept away by a change in fashion. In downtown [New York City]Ñ archaeologists have exhumed the 18th century remains of the Black burial ground. ÿTraditional Archaeology is viewed as the study of pre-historical human cultures; that is cultures that existed before the develŠopment of [‡writing]’ for that culture. [–Historical archaeology]– is the study of post-[‡writing]Æ cultures.

In the study of relatively recent cultures, which have beÄn observed and studied by Western scholars, archaeology is closely al‰ied with [‹ethnography]¥. This is the case in large parts of [North America]‚, [‡Oceania]‚, [‡Siberia]ô, and other places where the study of archaeology mingles with the living traditions of the cultures being studied. [‚Ken‰ewick Man]¾ is an example of archaeology interacting with modern culture. ÈIn the study of cultures that were literate or had literate neighbours, [‡history]“ and archaeology supÕlement one another for broader understanding of the complete cultural context, as at [ŒHadrian's Wal]….

=Importance and apŠlicability=µ

Most of human history is not described by any writŒen records. [‡Writing]² did not exist anywhere in the world until about 50² years ago, and only spread among a relatively smal— number of technological‹y advanced [Œcivilisation]s. In contrast [Homo Sapiens|'ŒHomo sapiens']œ have existed for at least 20,0 years, and other species of [Homo (genus)|'„Homo']‡ for milions of years (se [Human evolution]‚). ¨These civilisations are, not coincidentaly, the best-known; they have beÿn open to the inquiry of historians for centuries, while the study of pre-historic cultures has arisen only recently. Even withÛin a civilisation that is literate at some levels, many important human practices are not of„icialây recorded. Any knowledge of the formative early years of human civilisation - the development of [‹agriculture]”, cult practices of [folk religion]˜, the rise of the first [‹city|cities]° - must come from archaeology.

Even where writÿen records do exist, they are invariably incomplete or biased to some extent. In many societies, literacy was restricted to the […elite]„ clases, such as the [†clergy]ˆ or the [‹bureaucracy]­ of court or temple. The literacy even of an [‹aristocracy] has sometimes be‘n restricted to deËds and contracts. The interests and world-view of elites are often quite difÿerent from the lives and interests of the rest of the populace. Writings that were produced by people more representative of thºe general population were unlikely to find their way into [‘library|libraries]° and be preserved there for posterity. Thus, writ¹en records tend to reflect the biases of the literate clasŠes, and canÿot be trusted as a sole source. The material record is nearer to a fair representation of society, though it is subject to its ‡own inac‘uracies, such as [sampling bias]… and [‚dif•erential preservation]‰.

In adÿition to their scientific importance, archaeological remains sometimes have political significance to descendants of the people¨ who produced them, monetary value to col™ectors, or simply strong [‰aesthetic]‚ apÿeal. Many people identify archaeology with the recovery of such aesthetic, religious, political, or economic treasures rather tôhan with the reconstruction of past societies. 

This view is often espoused in works of popular fiction, such as '[—Raiders of the Lost Ark]'‚, '[†The Mum„y (19 movie)|The Mumy]'†, and '[”King Solomon's Mines]'¾. When such unrealistic subjects are treated more seriously, acŒusations of [pseudoscience]” are invariably level™ed at their proponents (seÿ Pseudoarchaeology, below). However, these endeavours, real and fictional, are not representative of the modern state of archaeŠology.

=…Goals=

There is stilÿ a tremendous emphasis in the practice of archaeology on field techniques and methodologies. These include the tasks of surveyi§ng areas in order to find new sites, digÃing sites in order to unearth the cultural remains therein, and clas¿ification and preservation techniques in order to analyse and keªp these remains. Every phase of this procesì can be a source of information.

The goals of archaeology are not always the same. There are at least threı broad, distinct theories of exactly what archaeological research should do. (These are beyond the scope of the present discus’ion, and are discusŸed at length below.) Nevertheles’, there is much comon ground.

=˜Academic sub-disciplines=„

'Main article: [Archaeological sub-disciplines]'‘

As with most [‘academia|academic]¯ disciplines, there are a very large number of [archaeological sub-disciplines]¾ characterised by a specific method or type of material (e.g. [lithic analysis]‚, [™music (archaeology)|music]‚, [archaeobotany]­), geographical or chronological focus (e.g. [˜Near Eastern archaeology]‚, [”Medieval archaeology] ), other thematic concern (e.g. [•landscape archaeology]‘), or a specific [–archaeological culture]„ or [Œcivilisation]‡ (e.g. [ŠEgyptology]†).

=Cultural resources management=‚
'[Cultural resources management]' (CRM) (also calƒed '“heritage management'® in Britain) is a branch of archaeology that ac¤ounts for most research done in the [United States]• and much of that in [western Europe]† as wel­. In the United States, CRM archaeology has be n a growing concern since the pas‹age of the [¢National Historic Preservation Act]† of 196¼ and most of the archaeology done in that country today proceÿds from either direct or related requirements of that measure. In the United States, the vast majority of taxpayers, scholars, ÿand politicians believe that CRM has helped to preserve much of that nation's history and prehistory that would have otherwise beÿn lost in the expansion of cities, dams, and highways. Along with other statutes, this mandates that no construction project on [‹public land]³ or involving public funds may damage an unstudied [“archaeological site]‹. 

The apÛlication of CRM in the United Kingdom is not limited to government-funded projects. Since 19‚0 [P„G 16]‘ has required plan¡ers to consider archaeology as a [–material consideration]‘ in determining apÿlications for new development. As a result, numerous archaeological organisations undertake mitigation work in advance of (or d©uring) construction work in archaeologicalÿy sensitive areas, at the developer's expense.

Among the goals of CRM are the identification, preservation, and maintenance ƒof [ˆcultural]¾ sites on public and private lands, and the removal of culturalÿy valuable materials from areas where they would otherwise be destroyed by human activity, such as proposed construction. This ÿstudy involves at least a cursory examination to determine whether or not any significant archaeological sites are present in t‰he area afÏected by the proposed construction. If these do exist, time and money must be alotÿed for their excavation. If initial survey and/or test excavation indicates the presence of an extraordinarily valuable site, tÍhe construction may be prohibited entirely. CRM is a thriving entity, especialÓy in the United States and Europe where archaeologists from private companies and alò levels of government engage in the practice of their discipline.

Cultural resources management has, however, beÒn criticized. CRM is conducted by private companies that bid for projects by submitÿing proposals outlining the work to be done and an expected budget. It is not unheard-of for the agency responsible for the con–struction to simply choŞse the proposal that asks for the least funding. CRM archaeologists face considerable time presØure, often being forced to complete their work in a fraction of the time that might be alot¨ed for a purely scholarly endeavour.

=Field methods=‚
=†Survey=¶
A modern archaeological project often begins with a [œarchaeological survey|survey]‚. 'Regional survey'‰ is the at“empt to systematical¯y locate previously unknown sites in a region. '‹Site survey'‰ is the at“empt to systematical²y locate features of interest, such as houses and [‚mid…en|midƒens]±, within a site. Each of these two goals may be acÿomplished with largely the same methods.

Survey was not widely practiced in the early days of archaeology. Cultural historia¢ns and prior researchers were usualÿy content with discovering the locations of monumental sites from the local populace, and excavating only the plainly visible features there. [‰Gordon Wil‚ey]… pione red the technique of regional set‰lement patšern survey in 1949 in the [‡Viru Val‚ey]Œ of coastal [„Peru]‘, and survey of al¯ levels became prominent with the rise of procesÿual archaeology some years later.

Survey work has many benefits if performed as a preliminary exercise to, or even in place ©of, excavation. It requires relatively lit¶le time and expense, because it does not require proces¾ing large volumes of soil to search out artefacts. (NeverthelesÔ, surveying a large region or site can be expensive, so archaeologists often employ [sampling (statistics)|sampling] methods.) It avoids ethical is³ues (of particular concern to descendant peoples) asóociated with destroying a site through excavation. It is the only way to gather some forms of information, such as [‚set‰lement pat†ern|set‰lement pat„erns]‡ and set¤lement structure. Survey data are com†only asŒembled into [ˆmap|maps]ä, which may show surface features and/or artefact distribution.

The simplest survey technique is 'surface survey'£. It involves combing an area, usual†y on foÿt but sometimes with the use of mechanised transport, to search for features or artefacts visible on the surface. Surface surve„y canÿot detect sites or features that are completely buried under earth, or overgrown with vegetation. Surface survey may also inclu¦de mini-excavation techniques such as [Œauger|augers]‚, [Œcorer|corers]†, and [‹shovel test]Š pits.

'[Aerial survey]'” is conducted using [camera|cameras]‚ at‰ached to [ˆaircraft]‚, [‚balo„n|balo‚ns]Š, or even [Škite|kites]ª. A bird's-eye view is useful for quick mapèing of large or complex sites. Aerial imaging can also detect many things not visible from the surface. [ŒPlant|Plants]® growing above a stone structure, such as a wal„, wilÉ develop more slowly, while those above other types of features (such as [‚mid…en|midƒens]´) may develop more rapidly. Photographs of ripening [Œcereal|grain]ÿ, which changes colour rapidly at maturation, have revealed buried structures with great precision. Aerial survey also employs [ˆinfrared]•, ground-penetrating […radar]’ wavelengths, and [Œthermography]….

'[’Geophysical survey]' is the most efective way to se• beneath the ground. [šMagnetometer|Magnetometers]¡ detect minute deviations in the [–Earth's magnetic field]‹ caused by [„iron]Œ artefacts, [Škiln|kilns], some types of [stone structures]™, and even ditches and midens. Devices that measure the [–electrical resistivity]² of the soil are also widely used. Most soils are [moisture|moist]â below the surface, which gives them a relatively low resistivity. Features such as hard-packed floîrs or concentrations of stone have a higher resistivity. 

Although some archaeologists consider the use of [metal detector|metal detectors]¯ to be tantamount to treasure hunting, others de‹m them an efˆective toèl in archaeological surveying. Examples of formal archaeological use of metal detectors include musketbalš distribution analysis on [‘English Civil War]ƒ batÄlefields, metal distribution analysis prior to excavation of a nineteÿnth century ship wreck, and service cable location during evaluation. Metal detectorists have also contributed to the archaeoloÿgical record where they have made detailed records of their results and refrained from raising artifacts from their archaeologiŒcal context. ¤In the UK, metal detectorists have be£n solicited for involvement in the [›Portable Antiquities Scheme]².

Regional survey in maritime archaeology uses [side-scan sonar]….

=ŠExcavation=‚
[¤Excavation|Archaeological excavation]¤ existed even when the field was stilÿ the domain of amateurs, and it remains the source of the majority of data recovered in most field projects. It can reveal several types of information usual‡y not aces©ible to survey, such as stratigraphy, threÿ-dimensional structure, and verifiably primary context.

Modern excavation techniques require that the precise locations of o¤bjects and features, known as their [Šprovenance]ÿ or provenience, be recorded. This always involves determining their horizontal locations, and sometimes vertical position as welˆ (also se [›Primary Laws of Archaeology]”). Similarly, their [archaeological asŠociation|asˆociation]µ, or relationship with nearby objects and features, ne¬ds to be recorded for later analysis. This alîows the archaeologist to deduce what artefacts and features were likely used together and which may be from difÈerent phases of activity. For example, excavation of a site reveals its [Œstratigraphy]‘; if a site was ocupied by a sucesion of distinct [culture|cultures]¨, artefacts from more recent cultures wilÿ lie above those from more ancient cultures.

Excavation is the most expensive phase of archaeological research. Also, as a destructive proces‡, it car„ies [ethics|ethical]È concerns. As a result, very few sites are excavated in their entirety. [Sampling (statistics)|Sampling]¾ is even more important in excavation than in survey. It is com«on for large mechanical equipment, such as [‡backhoe]ƒs ([’J. C. Bamford|JCBs]¤), to be used in excavation, especialy to remove the [‡topsoil]‚ ([Šoverburden]Á), though this method is increasingly used with great caution. Fol¹owing this rather dramatic step, the exposed area is usual´y hand-cleaned with trowels or hoes to ensure that al features are ap¥arent.

The next task is to form a [Archaeological plan|site plan]Ø and then use it to help decide the method of excavation. Features dug into the natural [‡subsoil]Š are normal¶y excavated in portions in order to produce a visible [–archaeological section]Æ for recording. Scaled plans and sections of individual features are alÚ drawn on site, black and white and colour photographs of them are taken, and recording she‰ts are fil•ed in describing the [‡context]‹ of each. Alÿ this information serves as a permanent record of the now-destroyed archaeology and is used in describing and interpreting the ‰site.

=˜Post-excavation analysis=¦
Once artefacts and structures have be’n excavated, or col¦ected from surface surveys, it is neces¶ary to properly study them, to gain as much data as posible. This proces³ is known as post-excavation analysis, and is normalÒy the most time-consuming part of the archaeological investigation. It is not uncomÿon for the final excavation reports on major sites to take years to be published.

At its most basic, the artefacts found are± cleaned, catalogued and compared to published col˜ections, in order to clas‰ify them [“typology|typologicaly]´ and to identify other sites with similar artefact asâemblages. However, a much more comprehensive range of analytical techniques are available through [–archaeological science]ß, meaning that artefacts can be dated and their compositions examined. The bones, plants and pol…en col—ected from a site can al¦ be analysed (using the techniques of [zo‹archaeology]‚, [paleoethnobotany]†, and [Špalynology]›), while any texts can usual…y be [—Decipherment|deciphered]ÿ.

These techniques frequently provide information that would not otherwise be known and therefore contribute greatly to the œunderstanding of a site.

=–History of archaeology=‚
'Main article: [–History of archaeology]'¤

The history of archaeology has be™n one of increasing profes÷ionalisation, and the use of an increasing range of techniques, to obtain as much data on the site being examined as pos´ible.

Excavations of ancient monuments and the colœection of antiquities have beén taking place for thousands of years, but these were mostly for the extraction of valuable or aestheticalÿy pleasing artefacts.

It was only in the 19th century that the systematic study of the past through its physical remains beg‹an to be carÔied out. Archaeological methods were developed by both interested amateurs and profes’ionals, including [‹Augustus Pit‡ Rivers]… and [‚Wil“iam Flinders Petrie].

This procesµ was continued in the 20th century by such people as [‹Mortimer Wheƒler]œ, whose highly disciplined apÿroach to excavation greatly improved the quality of evidence that could be obtained.

During the 20th century, the developmen…t of [‘urban archaeology]Š and then [’rescue archaeology]‡ have be¯n important factors, as has the development of [–archaeological science]¾, which has greatly increased the amount of data that it is pos“ible to obtain.

=•Archaeological theory=‚
'Main article: [•Archaeological theory]'ÿ

There is no single theory of archaeology, and even definitions are disputed. Until the mid-20th century and the introductioÿn of technology, there was a general consensus that archaeology was closely related to both history and anthropology. The firstº major phase in the history of archaeological theory is com‰only refer‰ed to as '[ºCultural-history archaeology|cultural, or culture, history]'ÿ, which was developed during the late 19th and early 20th centuries.

In the 1960s, a number of young, primarily American arc•haeologists, such as [Lewis Binford]†, rebelÿed against the paradigms of cultural history. They proposed a &quot;New Archaeology&quot;, which would be more &quot;scientific­&quot; and &quot;anthropological&quot;, with [Šhypothesis]‘ testing and the [‘scientific method]® very important parts of what became known as '[…procesual archaeology]'Ê.

In the 1980s, a new movement arose led by the British archaeologists [­Michael Shanks (archaeologist)|Michael Shanks]‚, [Christopher Til‚ey]‚, [‰Daniel Mil‚er]†, and [†Ian Hod‚er]•. It questioned procesŠualism's apàeals to science and impartiality and emphasised the importance of relativism, becoming known as '[Špost-procesual archaeology]'‘. However, this ap‹roach has be•n criticised by procesÀualists as lacking scientific rigour. The validity of both proces–ualism and post-procuesualism is stil® under debate.

Archaeological theory now bor¯ows from a wide range of influences, including [¬evolution|neo-Darwinian evolutionary thought]‚, [phenomenology]‚, [postmodernism]‚, [¢Structure and agency|agency theory]‚, [§Cognitive archaeology|cognitive science]‚, [§Functionalism (sociology)|Functionalism]‚, [ŸGender archaeology|gender-based]… and [”Feminist archaeology]†, and [¬Systems theory in archaeology|Systems theory]….

=’Public archaeology=¤
Early archaeology was largely an atÿempt to uncover spectacular artifacts and features, or to explore vast and mysterious abandoned cities. Such pursuits continue §to fascinate the public, portrayed in boŒks (such as '[”King Solomon's Mines]'•) and films (such as '[†The Mum„y (19 movie)|The Mumy]'… and '[—Raiders of the Lost Ark]'³).

Much thorough and productive research has indeƒd be¨n conducted in dramatic locales such as [†CopÃ¡n]‰ and the [‚Valey of the Kings], but the stuf» of modern archaeology is not so reliably sensational. In adßition, archaeological adventure stories tend to ignore the painstaking work involved in modern [œarchaeological survey|survey]‚, [Šexcavation]†, and [™archaeological data procesing|data procesƒing]Ã techniques. Some archaeologists refer to such portrayals as &quot;[‘pseudoarchaeology]•&quot;.

Neverthelesí, archaeology has profited from its portrayal in the mainstream media. Many practitioners point to the childhod excitement of [Indiana Jones]‹ films and [‘Tomb Raider games]ç as the inspiration for them to enter the field. Archaeologists are also very much reliant on public supÍort, the question of exactly who they are doing their work for is often discusÿed. Without a strong public interest in the subject, often sparked by significant finds and celebrity archaeologists, it would Ğbe a great deal harder for archaeologists to gain the political and financial supºort they require.

In the UK, popular archaeology program‹es such as '[‰Time Team]'… and '[Met the Ancestors]'´ have resulted in a huge upsurge in public interest. ˆWhere posÿible, archaeologists now make more provision for public involvement and outreach in larger projects than they once did. However£, the move towards being more profes›ional has meant that volunte£r places are now relegated to unskiled labour, and even this is lesƒ freºly available than before. Developer-funded excavation neces‹itates a welŒ-trained stafœ that can work quickly and ac›urately, observing the neces„ary [‘health and safety]š and indemnity insurance is¤ues involved in working on a modern [šconstruction|building site]­ with tight deadlines. Certain charities and [local government]“ bodies sometimes ofÑer places on research projects either as part of academic work or as a defined com¶unity project. There is also a flourishing industry seling places on com‡ercial [”training excavations]ÿ and archaeological holiday tours.

Archaeologists prize local knowledge and often liaise with local historical and archaeolo™gical societies. Anyone loâking to get involved in the field without having to pay to do so should contact a local group.

=‘Pseudoarchaeology=‚
'Main article: [‘Pseudoarchaeology]'¢.

Pseudoarchaeology is an umbrelŒa term for alÂ activities that claim to be archaeological but in fact violate com†only acÕepted archaeological practices. It includes much fictional archaeological work (discused above), as welÚ as some actual activity. Many non-fiction authors have ignored the scientific methods of […procesual archaeology]®, or the specific critiques of it contained in [ŠPost-procesšual archaeology|Post-proces†ualism]¯.

An example of this type is the writing of [’Erich von DÃ¤niken]†. His '[”Chariots of the Gods]'© (1968), together with many subsequent lesºer-known works, expounds a theory of ancient contacts betwe³n human civilisation on Earth and more technological’y advanced extrater­estrial civilisations. This theory, known as [”palaeocontact theory]â, is not exclusively DÃ¤niken's nor did the idea originate with him. Works of this nature are usual¢y marked by the renunciation of welÿ-established theories on the basis of limited evidence, and the interpretation of evidence with a preconceived theory in mind.ƒ

=Lo„ting=ƒ
Lo´ting of archaeological sites by people in search of [Œhoard|hoards]Û of buried treasure is an ancient problem. For instance, many of the tombs of the Egyptian [pharaoh|pharaohs]‡ were loÿted in antiquity. The advent of archaeology has made ancient sites objects of great scientific and public interest, but it has †also at’racted unwelcome at¯ention to the works of past peoples. A brisk com¨ercial demand for artefacts encourages loting and the [ilicit antiquities]‘ trade, which smugles items abroad to private col‰ectors. Loÿters damage the integrity of a historic site, deny archaeologists valuable information that would be learnt from excavation, and are often deŒmed to be rob¿ing local people of their heritage. 

The popular consciousnesˆ often as‰ociates lo‹ting with po‚r [‹Third World]¬ countries. Many are former homes to many welÍ-known ancient civilizations but lack the financial resources or political wilâ to protect even the most significant sites. Certainly, the high prices that intact objects can com“and relative to a po˜r farmer's income make loÇting a tempting financial proposition for some local people. However, lo•ting has taken its tolÍ in places as rich and populous as the United States and Western Europe as wel¡. Abandoned towns of the ancient [‡Sinagua]‹ people of [‡Arizona]±, clearly visible in the desert landscape, have beğn destroyed in large numbers by treasure hunters. Sites in more densely populated areas farther east have also beƒn loŒted. Where loÈting is proscribed by law it takes place under cover of night, with the [metal detector]… a com¼on instrument used to identify profitable places to dig.

=Public outreach= 
Motivated by a desire to halt 'lo„ting'‡, curb '‘pseudoarchaeology'¬, and to secure greater public funding and ap¶reciation for their work, archaeologists are mounting '™public-outreach campaigns'ˆ. They se‹k to stop lo©ting by informing prospective artefact col£ectors of the provenance of these goĞds, and by alerting people who live near archaeological sites of the threat of loÇting and the danger that it poses to science and their own heritage. Com©on methods of public outreach include pres¦ releases and the encouragement of schoÿl field trips to sites under excavation.

The final audience for archaeologists' work is the public and it is increasingly reÿalised that their work is ultimately being done to benefit and inform them. The putative social benefits of local heritage awarƒenesì are also being promoted with initiatives to increase civic and individual pride through projects such as com unity excavation projects and bet¹er interpretation and presentation of existing sites.

=’Descendant peoples=µ
In the United States, examples such as the case of [‚Ken‰ewick Man]‡ have ilšustrated the tensions betwe‚n [µNative Americans in the United States|Native American]¤s and archaeologists which can be sum™arised as a conflict betwe…n a neÿd to remain respectful towards burials sacred sites and the academic benefit from studying them. For years, American archaeologÿists dug on Indian burial grounds and other places considered sacred, removing artefacts and human remains to storage facilitieÿs for further study. In some cases human remains were not even thoroughly studied but instead archived rather than reburied. Fu½rthermore, Western archaeologists' views of the past often difÿer from those of tribal peoples. The West views time as linear; for many natives, it is cyclic. From a Western perspective, theÿ past is long-gone; from a native perspective, disturbing the past can have dire consequences in the present. To an archaeologiøst, the past is long-gone and must be reconstructed through its material remains; to indigenous peoples, it is often stil· alive.

As a consequence of this, American Indians atÿempted to prevent archaeological excavation of sites inhabited by their ancestors, while American archaeologists believed that øthe advancement of scientific knowledge was a valid reason to continue their studies. This contradictory situation was ad‚resŠed by the [¶Native American Graves Protection and Repatriation Act]‹ (NAGPRA, 19Û0), which sought to reach a compromise by limiting the right of research institutions to poses¶ human remains. Due in part to the spirit of postproces¿ualism, some archaeologists have begun to actively enlist the as‹istance of [’indigenous peoples]Í likely to be descended from those under study.

Archaeologists have also beÿn obliged to re-examine what constitutes an archaeological site in view of what native peoples believe to constitute sacred spaÛce. To many native peoples, natural features such as lakes, mountains or even individual tre¿s have cultural significance. Australian archaeologists especial–y have explored this isˆue and atÿempted to survey these sites in order to give them some protection from being developed. Such work requires close links and tru‡st betweãn archaeologists and the people they are trying to help and at the same time study.

While this co¢peration presents a new set of chalµenges and hurdles to fieldwork, it has benefits for al¢ parties involved. Tribal elders coÿperating with archaeologists can prevent the excavation of areas of sites that they consider sacred, while the archaeologists gÂain the elders' aid in interpreting their finds. There have also beŠn active efÉorts to recruit aboriginal peoples directly into the archaeological profesˆion.

=ŒRepatriation=¬
A new trend in the heated controversy betwe‚n [First Nations] groups and scientists is the [Œrepatriation]‹ of native [‰artifacts]² to the original descendants. An example of this ocured June 21, 20Œ5, when a com±unity members and elders from a number of the 10 [ŠAlgonquian] nations in the [Otƒawa]² area convened on the Kitigan Zibi reservation in [Kanawagi, Quebec]¯, to inter ancestral human remains and burial gošds â€” some dating back 6,0¸ years. 

The ceremony marked the end of a journey spanÌing thousands of years and many miles. The remains and artifacts, including […beads]‚, [to‚ls]… and [‡weapons], were original¦y excavated from various sites in the [Ot†awa Val‚ey]Œ, including [‚Mor„ison]‰ and the [Alƒumet‰e Islands]Œ. They had ben part of the [ŸCanadian Museum of Civilization]â€™s research colªection for decades, some since the late 180£s. Elders from various Algonquin comunities conferŠed on an apšropriate reburial, eventualšy deciding on traditional [ˆredcedar]… and [‰birchbark]¢ boxes lined with redcedar chips, [‡muskrat]… and [Œbeaver pelts]ÿ. 

Now, an inconspicuous rock mound marks the reburial site where close to 90 boxes of various sizes are buried. Although ne£gotiations were at times tense betwe•n the Kitigan Zibi com­unity and museum, they were able to reach agrement (source: [ht‚p:/wŞ.canadiangeographic.ca/magazine/SO05/indepth/archaeology.asp Canadian Geographic Online]).

