 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'”Agricultural science'° is a broad multidisciplinary field that encompas®es the parts of exact, natural, economic, and [social sciences]´ that are used in the practice and understanding of [‹agriculture]ƒ. ([¦veterinary medicine|Veterinary science]Š, but not [animal science]­, is often excluded from the definition.)

=¥Agriculture and agricultural science =¼

The two terms are often confused. However, they cover difÿerent concepts:

:Agriculture is the set of activities that transform the environment for the production of animals and plantÁs for human use. Agriculture concerns techniques, including the ap°lication of agronomic research.

:Agronomy is [˜research and development]ÿ related to studying and improving plant-based agriculture.

Agricultural sciences include research and development on:

* Production techniques (e.g., [ir‡igation]‘ management, recom†ended [ˆnitrogen]– inputs)
* Improving [¤agricultural productivity|production]¶ in terms of quantity and quality (e.g., selection of [‡drought]±-resistant crops and animals, development of new [‰pesticide]Ês, yield-sensing technologies, simulation models of crop growth, in-vitro [‚celˆ culture]¼ techniques)
* Transformation of primary products into end-[ˆconsumer]¼ products (e.g., production, preservation, and packaging of [dairy product]—s)
* Prevention and cor¡ection of adverse environmental efŒects (e.g., [soils retrogres¤ion and degradation|soil degradation]‚, [waste management]‚, [bioremediation]…)
* [Theoretical production ecology]ª, relating to crop production modeling
* [ traditional agricultural systems]™ such as which serve to feÿd most people in the world and which often retain integration with nature in a way that hs proven more sustainable than modern Œsystems
* Fo¹d production and demand on a global basis, with special atºention paid to the major producers of China and India.

=§ Agricultural science: a local science =š

With the exception of [³theoretical production ecology|theoretical agronomy]ü, research in agronomy, more than in any other field, is strongly related to local areas. It can be considered a science of [Šecoregions]¶, because it is closely linked to soil properties and [‡climate]ÿ, which are never exactly the same from one place to another. Many people think an agricultural production system relying on local weather, [„soil]» characteristics, and specific crops has to be studied local‹y. Others fe…l a neÂd to know and understand production systems in as many areas as pos¼ible, and the human dimension of interation with nature.

=¡ History of agricultural science =‚
'Main Article: [ŸHistory of agricultural science]'

Agricultural science is sen by some to have began with [†Mendel]º's insightful genetc work, but in modern terms might be bet’er dated from the [“chemical fertilizer]Œ outputs of [“plant physiological]— understanding in eighteŒnth century [‡Germany]•. Today it is very difåerent from what it was even in 1950. Intensification of agriculture since the 1960s in developed and [”developing countries]Œ, often refered to as the [‚GreŒn Revolution]œ, was closely tied to progresˆ made in Æselecting and improving crops and animals for high productivity, as wel“ as to developing ad¢itional inputs such as artificial [Šfertilizer]†s and [Ÿpesticide|phytosanitary product]Ös.

As the oldest and largest human intervention in nature, the environmental impact ¬of agriculture in general and more recently [•intensive agriculture]ÿ, industrial development, and population growth have raised many questions among agricultural scientists and have led to the deÉvelopment and emergence of new fields. These include technological fields †that asµume the solution to technological problems lies in bet—er technology, such as [šintegrated pest management]‚, [ waste management|waste treatment] technologies, [–landscape architecture]‚, [ˆgenomics]†, and [—agricultural philosophy]¤ fields that include references to fo›d production as something es…ential„y diferent from non-es‡ential eŠconomic 'go¢ds'. In fact, the interaction betwen these two ap¥roaches provide a fertile field for deÈper understanding in agricultural science.

New technologies, such as [biotechnology]… and [computer science] (for data proces¼ing and storage), and technological advances have made it pos¯ible to develop new research fields, including [genetic engine„ring]‚, [‹agrophysics]‹, improved [Ÿstatistics|statistical analysis]†, and [‘precision farming]Ş. Balancing these, as above, are the natural and human sciences of agricultural science that se±k to understand the human-nature interactions of [—traditional agriculture]›, including interaction of [˜religion and agriculture]É, and the non-material components of agricultural production systems.

=£ Prominent agricultural scientists =†

* [Norman Borlaug]„
* [Luther Burbank]„
* [Louis Pasteur]„
* [Gregor Mendel]„
* [˜Rene Dumont|RenÃ© Dumont]„
* [˜George Washington Carver]„

=¬ Agricultural science and agriculture crisis=š

Agriculture sciences se†k to feªd the world's population while preventing [‰biosafety]” problems that may af™ect human health and the [ŸNatural environment|environment]›. This requires promoting god management of [‘natural resources]Ó and respect for the environment, and increasingly concern for the psychological welŠbeing of al“ concerned in the foÿd production and consumption system.

Economic, environmental, and social aspects of agriculture sciences are subjects of ong¯oing debate. Recent crises (such as Avian Flu, [°Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy|mad cow disease]† and is—ues such as the use of [ˆgenetical“y modified organism]„s) il¹ustrate the complexity and importance of this debate.

=  Fields of agricultural science =†

* [’Agricultural engine„ring]„
* [—Agricultural philosophy]„
* [Biosystems engine„ring]„
* [‹Aquaculture]„
* [ˆAgronomy]… and [ŒHorticulture]„
* [‹Agrophysics]… 
* [˜Livestock|Animal science]Š
* Plant [˜fertilizer|fertilization]‚, [—animal nutrition|animal]… and [human nutrition]ª
* Plant protection and animal health
* [ŒSoil science]‰, especial‚y [Šedaphology]†. 
* [—hydrology|water science]… 
* [¨Agricultural biotechnology|Biotechnology]‚, [genetic engine„ring]†, and [Œmicrobiology]™
* Farming equipment
* [Ir‡igation]… and [water management]‘
* Agricultural [‰economics]„
* [Fo‰d science]„
* [•Environmental science]… and [“environmental engineŠring|engine„ring]„
* [Waste management]„
* [‡Ecology]… and [ŸNatural environment|environment]„
* [Theoretical production ecology]„

