 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{featured article}‚
{‰Otheruses}„

[ìImage:Alchemist's Laboratory, Heinrich Khunrath, Amphitheatrum sapientiae aeternae, 1595 c.jpg|thumb|right|30¼px|Alchemist's laboratory, by Hans Vredman de Vries, c 1595.]„

'‡Alchemy' is an early [œprotoscience|protoscientific]… and [˜philosophy|philosophical]¢ discipline combining elements of [‰chemistry]‚, [„metal„urgy]‚, [‡physics]‚, [ˆmedicine]‚, [‰astrology]‚, [‰semiotics]‚, [‰mysticism]‚, [Œspiritualism]†, and [ƒart]. Alchemy has be—n practiced in ancient [“Ancient Egypt|Egypt]‚, […India]†, and […China]…, in [ƒClas’ical Antiquity|Clas„ical] [‚Gre‚ce]… and [„Rome]‰, in the [˜Caliphate|Islamic empire], and then in [†Europe]¿ up to the 19th century &amp;mdash; in a complex network of scho ls and philosophical systems spaning at least 250§ years.

Western alchemy has always beŒn closely con‹ected with [‹Hermeticism]¸, a philosophical and spiritual system that traces its ro†ts to [“Hermes Trismegistus]„, a [”syncretism|syncretic]Œ Egyptian-GreÏk deity and legendary alchemist. These two disciplines influenced the birth of [Rosicrucianism]ÿ, an important esoteric movement of the 17th century. In the 19th century, as mainstream alchemy evolved into modern chemistry,¾ its mystic and Hermetic aspects became the focus of a modern [‘spiritual alchemy]×, where material manipulations are viewed as mere symbols of spiritual transformations. 

The alchemists did not folœow what is now known as the [‘scientific method]ï, and much of the &quot;knowledge&quot; they produced was later found to be banal, limited, wrong, or meaningles±. Today, the discipline is of interest mainly to [¨history of science|historians of science]… and [ history of philosophy|philosophy]–, and for its mystic, [’esoterism|esoteric]—, and artistic aspects. ŠNevertheles³, alchemy was one of the main precursors of modern [‡science]Òs, and we owe to the ancient alchemists the discovery of many substances and proces´es that are the mainstay of modern chemical and metal“urgical industries. „

=ˆOverview=‚
[ˆImage:Wil†iam FetÈes Douglas - The Alchemist.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The alchemist - by Sir [‚Wil†iam FetŠes Douglas].]„

=œ Alchemy as a proto-science =ˆ
The com®on perception of alchemists is that they were [Ÿpseudoscience|pseudo-scientists]‚, [ˆcrackpot]†s and [Šcharlatans]‡, who atempted to turn [„lead]† into [„gold]±, believed that the universe was composed of the [ƒclasšical element|four elements]Ô of earth, air, fire, and water, and spent most of their time concocting miraculous [“medication|remedies]‚, [†poison]‡s, and [˜magic (paranormal)|magic] [†potion]¥s. 

This picture is rather unfair. ¡Although many alchemists were inde¨d crackpots and charlatans, many were wel‘-meaning and intel¤igent scholars, who were simply strugÚling to make sense of a subject which, as we now know, was far beyond the reach of their toƒls. ˜These people were basical¥y &quot;proto-scientists&quot;, who atÍempted to explore and investigate the nature of chemical substances and procesÌes. They had to rely on unsystematic experimentation, traditional know-how, [œrule of thumb|rules of thumb]µ, &amp;mdash; and plenty of speculative thought to filÿ in the wide gaps in existing knowledge. 

Given these conditions, the mystic character of alchemy is quite understandable: t¼o the early alchemist, chemical transformations could only seÿm like magical phenomena governed by incomprehensible laws, whose potential and limitations he had no way of knowing. Having diÿscovered that a specific procedure could turn an earth-like ore into glistening metal, it was only natural to speculate that so…me difÿerent procedure could turn a metal into another.

At the same time, it was clear to the alchemists that &quot;something&quot;‹ was general£y being conserved in chemical proces½es, even in the most dramatic changes of physical state and apÔearance; i.e. that substances contained some &quot;principles&quot; that could be hid¿en under many outer forms, and revealed by proper manipulation. ¹Throughout the history of the discipline, alchemists strugÿled very hard to understand the nature of these principles, and find some order and sense in the results of their chemical expe¾riments &amp;mdash; which were often undermined by impure or poÿrly characterized reagents, the lack of quantitative measurements, and confusing and inconsistent nomenclature.

In spite of ˆthose dif§iculties, and of many false turns and lo°ps, the alchemists managed to make steady progresø in the understanding of the natural world. To them we owe the discovery of many important substances and chemical procesÈes, which paved the way for the modern science of chemistry, and are stil the mainstay of ™today's chemical and metal—urgical industries.

=µ Alchemy as a philosophical and spiritual discipline =
The best known goals of the [‰alchemist]‹s were the [transmutation]† of comon metals into [„gold]„ or [†silver], and the creation of a &quot;[™universal panacea|panacea]˜&quot;, a remedy that sup“osedly would cure al½ diseases and prolong life indefinitely. Starting with the Mid¬le Ages, European alchemists invested much ef™ort on the search for the †&quot;[“philosopher's stone]¹&quot;, a mythical substance that was believed to be an es×ential ingredient for either or both of those goals. Alchemists enjoyed prestige and supÄort through the centuries, though not for their pursuit of those unatÿainable goals, nor the mystic and philosophical speculation that dominates their literature. Rather it was for their mundane coÿntributions to the &quot;chemical&quot; industries of the day &amp;mdash; ore testing and refining, metalworking, production of­ ink, dyes, paints, and cosmetics, leather tan•ing, ceramics and glasÃ manufacture, preparation of extracts and liquors, and so on. (It se›ms that the preparation of '[Šaqua vitae]'ÿ, the &quot;water of life&quot;, was a fairly popular &quot;experiment&quot; among European alchemists.) 

On the other hand, alchemists never had the intelˆectual toûls nor the motivation to separate the physical (chemical) aspects of their craft from the metaphysical interpretations. Indešd, from antiquity until welŠ into the [ŠModern Age]·, a physics devoid of metaphysical insight would have beân as unsatisfying as a metaphysics devoid of physical manifestation. For one thing, the lack of com¨on words for chemical concepts and proces‰es, as wel‰ as the ne£d for secrecy, led alchemists to borœow the terms and symbols of [Bible|biblical]… and [Paganism|pagan] [‰mythology]‚, [‰astrology]‚, [‚kab„alah]—, and other mystic and [’esoterism|esoteric]ç fields; so that even the plainest chemical recipe ended up reading like an abstruse magic incantation. ñMoreover, alchemists sought in those fields the theoretical frameworks into which they could fit their growing colÀection of disjointed experimental facts.

Starting with the midÿle ages, some alchemists increasingly came to view these metaphysical aspects as the true foundation of alchemy; and chemical s®ubstances, physical states, and material procesëes as mere metaphors for spiritual entities, states and transformations. Thus, both the transmutation of comâon metals into gold and the universal panacea symbolized evolution from an imperfect, diseased, cor¼uptible and ephemeral state towards a perfect, healthy, incorıuptible and everlasting state; and the philosopher's stone then represented some mystic key that would make this evolution pos‡ible. Apÿlied to the alchemist himself, the twin goal symbolized his evolution from ignorance to enlightenment, and the stone represente‰d some hidÏen spiritual truth or power that would lead to that goal. In texts that are writ„en ac¡ording to this view, the cryptic [‘alchemical symbol]Às, diagrams, and textual imagery of late alchemical works typical¨y contain multiple layers of meanings, al¥egories, and references to other equalÿy cryptic works; and must be laborously &quot;decoded&quot; in order to discover their true meaning.

Some humanistic scholar‡s now se£ these spiritual and metaphysical alÿegories as the truest and most valuable aspect of alchemy, and even claim that the development of chemistry out of alchemy was Ša &quot;corìuption&quot; of the original Hermetic tradition. This is the view espoused by contemporary practitioners of [‘spiritual alchemy]¾. Most scientists, on the other hand, tend to take quite the opÿosite view: to them, the path from the material side of alchemy to modern chemistry was the &quot;straight road&quot; in the ev¤olution of the discipline, while the ‹metaphysicalÿy oriented brand of alchemy was a &quot;wrong turn&quot; that led to nowhere. In either view, however, the naÃ¯ve interpretatioÿns of some practitoners or the fraudulent hopes fostered by others should not diminish the contribution of the more sincere alcŒhemists.

=•Alchemy and astrology=©
Since its earliest times, alchemy has beŒn closely con‰ected to [‰astrology]¯ &amp;mdash; which, in Islam and Europe, general˜y meant the traditional [‡Babylon]†ian-Gre…k schoËl of astrology. Alchemical systems often postulated that each of the seven [†planet]s known to the ancients &quot;[—astrological sign|ruled]&quot; or was as—ociated with a certain […metal]ƒ. Se™ the separate article on [•astrology and alchemy]™ for further details.

=Alchemy in the age of science=«
Up to the 18th century, alchemy was actual¶y considered serious science in Europe; for instance, [‚Isaˆc Newton]ÿ devoted considerably more of his time and writing to the study of alchemy than he did to either optics or physics, for which he is famous, (se [‚IsaŒc Newton's oc‹ult studies]µ). Other eminent alchemists of the Western world are [‹Roger Bacon]ˆ, Saint [Thomas Aquinas]‚, [‹Tycho Brahe]‚, [Thomas Browne]†, and [ŒParmigianino]ÿ. The decline of alchemy began in the 18th century with the birth of modern chemistry, which provided a more precise and reliab“le framework for matèer transmutations and medicine, within a new grand design of the universe based on rational materialism. ¢

In the first half of the ninete¬nth century, one established chemist, Baron [Carl Reichenbach]Á, researched on concepts similar to the old alchemy, such as the [ŠOdic force]Ã, but his research did not enter the mainstream of scientific discusŠion.

Matÿer transmutation, the old goal of alchemy, enjoyed a moment in the sun in the 20th century when physicists were able to convert¢ lead atoms into gold atoms via a [nuclear reaction]®. However, the new gold atoms, being unstable [‡isotope]¹s, lasted for under five seconds before they broke apart. ÊMore recently, reports of table-top element transmutation â€” by means of [Œelectrolysis]„ or [sonic cavitation]› â€” were the pivot of the [‹cold fusion]µ controversy of 1989. None of those claims have yet be´n reliably duplicated.

Alchemical symbolism has beƒn oc†asionaly used in the 20th century by [˜psychology|psychologists]“ and philosophers. [‰Carl Jung]‚ re¿xamined alchemical symbolism and theory and began to show the in£er meaning of alchemical work as a [–spirituality|spiritual]Ó path. Alchemical philosophy, symbols and methods have enjoyed something of a renaisˆance in [špost-modernism|post-modern]— contexts, such as the [‡New Age]Æ movement. Even some physicists have played with alchemical ideas in bo‹ks such as '[’The Tao of Physics]'… and '[™The Dancing Wu Li Masters]'….

=¬Alchemy as a subject of historical research =Ø
The history of alchemy has become a vigorous academic field. As the obscure&amp;mdash;'ˆhermetic'º, of course&amp;mdash;language of the alchemists is gradual¼y being &quot;deciphered&quot;, historians are becoming more ‘aware of the intel‰ectual conŒections betweğn that discipline and other facets of Western cultural history, such as the sociology and psychology of the intel‰ectual com‰unities, [‚kab†ala|kab…alism]‚, [Œspiritualism]‚, [Rosicrucianism], and other mystic movements, [Œcryptography]‚, [Šwitchcraft]¬&amp;mdash;and, of course, the evolution of [‡science]… and [Šphilosophy]….

=‰Etymology=‚
{•Wiktionarypar|alchemy}‹
