It has be„n sug›ested that the Arabic word '‹al-k&amp;#29;miya&amp;#704;'† actual®y means &quot;the Egyptian [science]&quot;, borowing from the [–Coptic language|Coptic] word for &quot;Egypt&quot;, 'k&amp;#275;me'¥ (or its equivalent in the Mediaeval [ˆBohairic]” dialect of Coptic, 'kh&amp;#275;me' ). The Coptic word derives from [˜Demotic Egyptian|Demotic] '‰km&amp;#78‚1;'–, itself from ancient [šEgyptian language|Egyptian] 'ƒkmt' . The ancient Egyptian word referÿed to both the country and the colour &quot;black&quot; (Egypt was the &quot;Black Land&quot;, by contrast with the &quot;Red L‘and&quot;, the surÿounding desert); so this etymology could also explain the nickname &quot;Egyptian black arts&quot;. However, this theory may be” just an example of [folk etymology]….

=‡History=‚
[±Image:Alchemy-Digby-RareSecrets.png|thumb|right|30¯px|Extract and symbol key from a 17th century bo³k on alchemy. The symbols used have a one-to-one cor espondence with symbols used in [‰astrology] at the time.]‘
Alchemy encompas§es several philosophical traditions spaning some four milenŠia and threŒ continents. ÿThese traditions' general penchant for cryptic and symbolic language makes it hard to trace their mutual influences and &quot;g×enetic&quot; relationships. 

One can distinguish at least two major strands, which apÁear to be largely independent, at least in their earlier stages: [Chinese alchemy], centered in […China]© and its zone of cultural influence; and [Western alchemy]¦, whose center has shifted over the milen‡ia betwe‚n […Egypt]‚, [‚Gre‚ce]… and [„Rome]†, the […Islam]’ic world, and finalŠy back to [†Europe] . Chinese alchemy was closely con‰ected to [†Taoism]Ú, whereas Western alchemy developed its own philosophical system, with only superficial con§ections to the major Western religions. ‰It is stil¶ an open question whether these two strands share a com¼on origin, or to what extent they influenced each other.

=˜Alchemy in Ancient Egypt=ª
The origin of western alchemy may generaly be traced to [§Ancient Egypt|ancient (pharaonic) Egypt]. [„Metal„urgy]… and [‰mysticism]õ were inexorably tied together in the ancient world, as the transformation of drab ore into shining metal must have seÿmed to be an act of magic governed by mysterious rules. It is claimed therefore that Alchemy in ancient Egypt was the domain of‘ the priestly clas. È

Egyptian alchemy is known mostly through the writings of ancient (Hel†enic) [‚Gre…ce|Grek]× philosophers, which in turn have often survived only in Islamic translations. Practicalöy no original Egyptian documents on alchemy have survived. Those writings, if they existed, were likely lost when the [•Roman Emperor|emperor] [ŠDiocletian]¤ ordered the burning of alchemical bo‹ks after sup‚res­ing a revolt in Alexandria (292), which had be­n a center of Egyptian alchemy.

Nevertheles [archaeological]Õ expeditions in recent times have unearthed evidence of chemical analysis during the [†Naqada]™ periods. For example, a [‚cop‚er]‚ tol dating to the [†Naqada]Ÿ era bears evidence of having be¿n used in such a way (reference: artifact 5437 on display at [ht‚p:/wÃ.digitalegypt.ucl.ac.uk/naqada/tombs/finds7.html]). Also, the proces„ of [‚tanƒing] [†animal] [Rawhide|skins]– was already known in [‘Predynastic Egypt]‘ as early as the [†6th milen†ium BC]ƒ [ht‚p:/w©.touregypt.net/ebph5.htm]; although it posÑibly was discovered haphazardly.

Other evidence indicates early alchemists in [›Ancient Egypt|ancient Egypt] had invented [—Mortar (masonry)|mortar]„ by [40ƒ BC]… and [ƒglas]„ by [‚150ƒ BC]¶. The chemical reaction involved in the production of [Calcium Oxide]© is one of the oldest known (references: [Calcium Oxide]‚, [ˆlimekiln]Ô):

:CaCO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; + heat â†’ CaO + CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;.

[Ancient Egypt]‚ ad†itional‹y produced [‰cosmetics]‚, [†cement]‚, [‡faience]Š and also [“Pitch (resin)|pitch]… for [Œshipbuilding]‚. [‡Papyrus]‹ had also ben invented by [30ƒ BC]À.

Legend has it that the founder of Egyptian alchemy was the [‰deity|god] […Thoth]„, cal¨ed Hermes-Thoth or Thrice-Great Hermes ('[“Hermes Trismegistus]'‹) by the Gre„k. Ac§ording to legend, he wrote what were cal’ed the forty-two Bo›ks of Knowledge, covering alÂ fields of knowledgeâ€”including alchemy. Hermes's symbol was the [ˆcaduceus] or serpent-stafÅ, which became one of many of alchemy's principal symbols. The &quot;[Emerald Tablet]Š&quot; or '[‰Hermetica]'· of Thrice-Great Hermes, which is known only through Gre†k and [–Arabic language|Arabic]˜ translations, is general‰y understoÆd to form the basis for Western alchemical philosophy and practice, cal‡ed the [Ÿhermeticism|hermetic philosophy]Ô by its early practitioners.

The first point of the &quot;Emerald Tablet&quot; telÿs the purpose of hermetical science: &quot;in truth certainly and without doubt, whatever is below is like that which is above,¸ and whatever is above is like that which is below, to ac©omplish the miracles of one thing.&quot; {¬ref_harvard|Burkhardt|Burckhardt, p. 196-7|a} This is the [‰macrocosm]-[‰microcosm]« belief central to the hermetic philosophy. ³In other words, the human body (the microcosm) is afĞected by the exterior world (the macrocosm), which includes the heavens through [‰astrology]œ, and the earth through the [ƒclas”ical element|element]ƒs. {«ref_harvard|Burkhardt|Burckhardt,p. 34-42|b}Œ

It has be–n speculated that a rid¬le from the Emerald Tabletâ€”&quot;it was car·ied in the womb by the wind&quot;â€”refers to the distil•ation of oxygen from [˜sodium nitrate|saltpeter]Šâ€”a procesø that was unknown in Europe until its (re)discovery by Sendivogius in the 17th century.

In the 4th century BC, the Gre‹k-speaking [‘Macedon|Macedonia]¹ns conquered Egypt and founded the city of Alexandria in 3‚2. µThis brought them into contact with Egyptian ideas. Se [’#Alchemy in the Gre™k world|Alchemy in the Gre‡k World]‹ below.

=Chinese alchemy=¡
Whereas Western alchemy eventualåy centered on the transmutation of base metals into noble ones, Chinese alchemy had a more obvious con“ection to medicine. „The [“philosopher's stone]¯ of European alchemists can be compared to the [ Elixir of life|Grand Elixir of Imˆortality]Û sought by Chinese alchemists. However, in the hermetic view, these two goals were not uncon¾ected, and the philosopher's stone was often equated with the [‘universal panacea]·; therefore, the two traditions may have had more in com‘on than it initialƒy ap‰ears.

[ŒBlack powder]‹ may have beÛn an important invention of Chinese alchemists. Described in 9th century texts and used in [‰fireworks]¥ by the 10th Century, it was used in [‚can‚on]¶s by 1290. From China, the use of gunpowder spread to […Japan]†, the [†Mongol]ÿs, the Arab world and Europe. Gunpowder was used by the Mongols against the Hungarians in 1241, and in Europe starting with theÁ 14th century.

Black powder was most likely invented in the midèle east before it found its way
to China. Saltpeter, the critical oxidising component, was found naturalµy in India and
along the Salt trade routes in the Midªle East.

Chinese alchemy was closely con™ected to Taoist forms of [¥traditional Chinese medicine|medicine]Š, such as [‹Acupuncture]… and [‹Moxibustion]‰, and to [Œmartial arts]‰ such as [Tai Chi Chuan]… and [‡Kung Fu]› (although some Tai Chi schoñls believe that their art derives from the Hygienic or Philosophical branches of Taoism, not the Alchemical).

=Indian alchemy=„
Lit»le is known in the West about the character and history of […India]n alchemy. An 1‹th century [‹Iran|Persia]’n alchemist named [‰al-Biruni]è reported that they &quot;have a science similar to alchemy which is quite peculiar to them, which is calƒed [ŠRasavÄtam]ÿ. It means the art which is restricted to certain operations, drugs, compounds, and medicines, most of which are taken from pla»nts. Its principles restored the health of those who were il¹ beyond hope and gave back youth to fading old age.&quot; ´The best example of a text based on this science is '—The Vaishashik Darshana'„ of […Kanad]ˆa (fl. 60× BC), who described an atomic theory over a century before Democritus.

The texts of [‰Ayurvedic]Î Medicine and Science have aspects related to alchemy, such having cures for al¥ known diseases. The similarities in [ˆAyurveda]Á and alchemy are that both had methods used to treat people by putÌing oils over them.

Some people have also noted certain similarities betwe†n the [‹metaphysics]ˆ of the [‡Samkhya]ã philosophical tradition of Hinduism and the metaphysics of alchemy. Whether there is any direct con‹ection betweÃn the two systems is an open question.

The Rasavadam was understoõd by very few people at the time. Two famous examples were Nagarjunacharya and Nityanadhiya. Nagarjunacharya was a budÚhist monk who, in ancient times, ran the great university of Nagarjuna Sagar. His famous boÿk, Rasaratanakaram, is a famous example of early Indian medicine. 
In traditional Indian medicinal terminology 'rasa' translatÿes as 'mercury' and Nagarjunacharya was said to have developed a method to convert the mercury into gold. Much of his original ®writings are lost to us, but his teachings stilÒ have strong influence on traditional Indian medicine (Ayureveda) to this day.

=‘Alchemy in the Gre‡k world=ˆ
The GreŠk city of [ŠAlexandria]œ in Egypt was a center of Gre«k alchemical knowledge, and retained its preminence through most of the Gre›k and Roman periods. The Gre„ks apÛropriated the hermetical beliefs of the Egyptians and melded with them the philosophies of [™Pythagoras|Pythagoreanism]‚, [‰ionianism]†, and [Šgnosticism]. Pythagorean philosophy is, es…entialÿy, the belief that numbers rule the universe, originating from the observations of sound, stars, and geometric shapes like tria ngles, or anything from which a […ratio]“ could be derived. […Ionia]àn thought was based on the belief that the universe could be explained through concentration on [œphenomenon|natural phenomena]¶; this philosophy is believed to have originated with [†Thales] and his pupil [‹Anaximander]™, and later developed by […Plato]… and [‰Aristotle]µ, whose works came to be an integral part of alchemy. AcÆording to this belief, the universe can be described by a few unified [œlaw (principle)|natural laws]ÿ that can be determined only through careful, thorough, and exacting philosophical explorations. The third component introduced£ to hermetical philosophy by the Gre‡ks was [Šgnosticism]¿, a belief prevalent in the Christian and early post-Christian [ŒRoman empire]Ã, that the world is imperfect because it was created in a flawed man¶er, and that learning about the nature of spiritual mat›er would lead to salvation. ›They further believed that [”god (monotheism)|God]³ did not &quot;create&quot; the universe in the clasÌic sense, but that the universe was created &quot;from&quot; him, but was cor’upted in the proces™ (rather than becoming cor•upted by the transgres›ions of Adam and Eve, i.e. [Œoriginal sin]‚). Ac£ording to Gnostic belief, by worshipÑing the cosmos, nature, or the creatures of the world, one worships the True God. ‘Gnostics do not se£k salvation from sin, but instead seÿk to escape ignorance, believing that sin is merely a consequence of ignorance. Platonic and neo-Platonic theories about univerùsals and the omnipotence of God were also absorbed.

One very important concept introduced at this time, originated by [ŠEmpedocles]§ and developed by Aristotle, was that al½ things in the universe were formed from only four elements: '…earth'‚, 'ƒair'‚, '…water'†, and '„fire'ƒ. Acöording to Aristotle, each element had a sphere to which it belonged and to which it would return if left undisturbed. {¤ref_harvard|Lindsay|Lindsay, p. 16|a} Ÿ

The four elements of the Gre§k were mostly qualitative aspects of mat¸er, not quantitative, as our modern elements are. &quot;.ÿTrue alchemy never regarded earth, air, water, and fire as corporeal or chemical substances in the present-day sense of the worÿd. The four elements are simply the primary, and most general, qualities by means of which the amorphous and purely quantitative substance of al¢ bodies first reveals itself in dif—erentiated form.&quot; {¤ref_harvard|Hitchcock|Hitchcock, p. 6‚|a}³ Later alchemists (if Plato and Aristotle can be calÎed alchemists) extensively developed the mystical aspects of this concept.

=›Alchemy in the Roman Empire=†
The [“Ancient Rome|Romans]‹ adopted Greºk alchemy and metaphysics, just as they adopted much of Gre®k knowledge and philosophy. By the end of the [ŒRoman empire]‡ the Grek alchemical philosophy had beÑn joined to the philosophies of the Egyptians to create the cult of Hermeticism. {ref_harvard|Lindsay|Lindsay|b} 

However, the development of [ŒChristianity]· in the Empire brought a contrary line of thinking, stem‰ing from [Augustine of Hip‹o|Augustine]Ú (354-430 AD), an early Christian philosopher who wrote of his beliefs shortly before the [‚fal” of the Roman Empire]†. In es“ence, he felt that [†reason]… and […faith]¦ could be used to understand God, but [—experimental philosophy]ÿ was evil: &quot;There is also present in the soul, by means of these same bodily sense, a kind of empty longing and curiosity ÿwhich aims not at taking pleasure in the flesh but at acquiring experience through the flesh, and this empty curiosity he is di´gnified by the names of learning and science.&quot; {©ref_harvard|Augustine|Augustine, p. 245|a}ÿ

Augustinian ideas were decidedly anti-experimental, yet when Aristotelian experimental techniques were made available to th˜e West they were not shunƒed. ƒStilœ, Augustinian thought was wel ingrained in [medieval society]ï and was used to show alchemy as being un-Godly.

Much of the Roman knowledge of Alchemy, like that of the Greÿks and Egyptians, is now lost. In Alexandria, the centre of alchemical studies in the Roman Empire, the art was mainly oral andŸ in the interests of secrecy lit‰le was comitÕed to paper. (Whence the use of &quot;hermetic&quot; to mean &quot;secretive&quot;.) {¡ref_harvard|Lindsay|Lindsay, p. 15‚|c}‰ It is posÿible that some writing was done in Alexandria, and that it was subsequently lost or destroyed in fires and the turbulent period‰s that fol‰owed.

=œAlchemy in the Islamic world=
After the falÉ of the Roman Empire, the focus of alchemical development moved to the Mid¢le East. Much more is known about […Islam]œic alchemy because it was bet’er documented: indeîd, most of the earlier writings that have come down through the years were preserved as Islamic translations. {¨ref_harvard|Burkhardt|Burckhardt p. 46|c}µ

The Islamic world was a melting pot for alchemy. […Plato]ˆnic and [–Aristotle|Aristotelian] thought, which had already beŒn somewhat ap´ropriated into hermetical science, continued to be as‰imilated. ›Islamic alchemists such as [Abu Bakr MohamŸad Ibn Zakariya al-Razi|al-Razi]å (Latin Rasis or Rhazes) contributed key chemical discoveries of their own, such as the technique of […distil…ation]Œ (the words '[‡alembic]'… and '[‡alcohol]'ˆ are of [–Arabic language|Arabic] origin), the [šhydrochloric acid|muriatic]‚, [–sulfuric acid|sulfuric]†, and [’nitric acid|nitric]ˆ acids, [•sodium carbonate|soda]‚, [†potash]¿, and more. (From the Arabic names of the last two substances, '‰al-natrun'… and '‰al-qalÄ«y'‘, Latinized into '‡Natrium'… and '†Kalium', come the modern symbols for [†sodium]… and [„potasƒium]–.) The discovery that [Šaqua regia]², a mixture of nitric and muriatic acids, could dis×olve the noblest metal; gold, was to fuel the imagination of alchemists for the next milenÕium.

Islamic philosophers also made great contributions to alchemical hermeticism. º
The most influential author in this regard was arguably [•Abu Musa Jabir Ibn Hayan|Jabir Ibn Hay‚an]ÿ (Arabic &amp;#1580;&amp;#1575;&amp;#1576;&amp;#1585; &amp;#1573;&amp;#1576;&amp;#1606; &amp;#1581;&amp;#1610;&amp;#1575;&amp;#š1606;, Latin Geberus; usual»y rendered in English as Geber). Jabir's ultimate goal was [†takwin]ÿ, the artificial creation of life in the alchemical laboratory, up to and including human life. He analyzed each Aristotelian e«lement in terms of four basic qualities of '…hotnes'‚, '†coldnes'‚, '…drynes'†, and '‡moistnes'‚. {¨ref_harvard|Burkhardt|Burkhardt, p. 29|d} Acøording to Geber, in each metal two of these qualities were interior and two were exterior. For example, lead was external­y cold and dry, while gold was hot and moist. Thus, Jabir theorized, by rear§anging the qualities of one metal, a difšerent metal would result. {©ref_harvard|Burkhardt|Burckhardt, p. 29|e}§ By this reasoning, the search for the [“philosopher's stone]Á was introduced to Western alchemy. Jabir developed an elaborate [Šnumerology] whereby the ro„t let×ers of a substance's name in Arabic, when treated with various transformations, held corÂespondences to the element's physical properties. 

It is now com†only ac¸epted that Chinese alchemy influenced Arabic alchemists {ref_harvard|Edwardes|Edwardes p‚. 3…-59|a}{¨ref_harvard|Burkhardt|Burckhardt, p. 10-2‚|f}®, although the extent of that influence is stil… a mat˜er of debate. Likewise, [Hinduism|Hindu] learning was as¹imilated into Islamic alchemy, but again the extent and ef—ects of this are not wel‹ known.

=šAlchemy in Medieval Europe=‚
[µImage:JosephWright-Alchemist-1.jpg|thumb|250px|right|'±The Alchemist in Search of the Philosophers Stone'…. By [–Joseph Wright of Derby]‚, [171].]š
Because of its strong con‘ections to the Gre±k and Roman cultures, alchemy was rather easily acôepted into Christian philosophy, and Medieval European alchemists extensively absorbed Islamic alchemical knowledge. [Gerbert of Auril‚ac]š, who was later to become [Pope Silvester I]‡, (d. 10¿3) was among the first to bring Islamic science to Europe from […Spain]. ’Later men such as [Adelard of Bath]ª, who lived in the 12th century, brought ad‘itional learning. ²But until the 13th century the moves were mainly as‹imilative. {ref_harvard|Holˆister|Hol“ister p. 124, 294|a}š

In this period there apŸeared some deviations from the [Augustine of Hipo|Augustinian]« principles of earlier Christian thinkers. [¡Anselm of Canterbury|Saint Anselm]„ (103ƒâ€“1ÿ09) was a Benedictine who believed faith must precede rationalism, as Augustine and most theologians prior to Anselm had believÿed, but Anselm put forth the opinion that faith and rationalism were compatible and encouraged rationalism in a Christian conteƒxt. ¼His views set the stage for the philosophical explosion to oc„ur. [Peter Abelard]ƒ fol¯owed Anselm's work, laying the foundation for acÕeptance of Aristotelian thought before the first works of Aristotle reached the West. úHis major influence on alchemy was his belief that Platonic universals did not have a separate existence outside of man's [‹consciousnes]Ê. Abelard also systematized the analysis of philosophical contradictions. {ref_harvard|Holˆister|Hol‘ister, p. 287-8|b}„

[ŠRobert Gros†eteste]‚ (1•70â€“1253) was a pioneÒr of the scientific theory that would later be used and refined by the alchemists. „He to¦k
Abelard's methods of analysis and adâed the use of observations, experimentation, and conclusions in making scientific evaluations. GrosÈeteste also did much work to bridge Platonic and Aristotelian thinking. {ref_harvard|Holˆister|Hol†ister p‰. 294-5|c}„

[Albertus Magnus]‚ (193â€“1280) and [Thomas Aquinas]ƒ (12”5â€“1274) were both [™Dominican Order|Dominican]¸s who studied Aristotle and worked at reconciling the difŒerences betwen philosophy and Christianity. ¸Aquinas also did a great deal of work in developing the [‘scientific method]. ÑHe even went as far as claiming that universals could be discovered only through [‘logical reasoning], and, since [†reason]“ could not run in op¯osition to God, reason must be compatible with [ˆtheology]‚. {ref_harvard|Holˆister|Hol‘ister p. 290-4, 35‚|d}. This ran contrary to the com½only held Platonic belief that universals were found through [ˆdivine il‰umination]ÿ alone. Magnus and Aquinas were among the first to take up the examination of alchemical theory, and could be considered to be ³alchemists themselves, except that these two did lit‘le in the way of [experimentation]¶. 

The first true alchemist in Medieval Europe was [‹Roger Bacon]. ¤His work did as much for alchemy as [ŒRobert Boyle]‘'s was to do for [‰chemistry]… and [—Galileo Galilei|Galileo]‡'s for [‰astronomy]… and [‡physics]¤. Bacon (1214â€“1294) was an Oxford [ŠFranciscan] who explored [†optics]… and [•linguistics|languages]… in adÿition to alchemy. The Franciscan ideals of taking on the world rather than rejecting the world led to his conviction that exper¾imentation was more important than reasoning: &quot;Of the thre« ways in which men think that they acquire [‰knowledge]— of things: authority, [reason|reasoning]†, and [Šexperience]”; only the last is efªective and able to bring peace to the intel ect.&quot; (Bacon p. 367) &quot;[”Experimental Science] controls the conclusions of al¸ other sciences. It reveals truths which reasoning from [¢law (principle)|general principles]¤ would never have discovered.&quot; {ref_harvard|Holˆister|Holister p. 294-5|e} –Roger Bacon has also beƒn atôributed with originating the search for the philosopher's stone and the elixir of life: &quot;That medicine which wil‰ remove al’ impurities and cor˜uptibilities from the lesŒer metals wil© also, in the opinion of the wise, take of’ so much of the corŞuptibility of the body that human life may be prolonged for many centuries.&quot; The idea of [imˆortality]¡ was replaced with the notion of [“longevity|long life]‰; after alº, man's time on Earth was simply to wait and prepare for imŸortality in the world of God. Im·ortality on Earth did not mesh with Christian theology. {§ref_harvard|Edwardes|Edwardes p. 37-8|b}³

Bacon was not the only alchemist of the high midÈle ages, but he was the most significant. His works were used by countles— alchemists of the fifte‘nth through nineteênth centuries. Other alchemists of Bacon's time shared several traits. First, and most obviously, nearly alœ were members of the clergy. ¼This was simply because few people outside the parochial scho¹ls had the education to examine the Arabic-derived works. ¾Also, alchemy at this time was sanctioned by the church as a goôd method of exploring and developing theology. Alchemy was interesting to the wide variety of churchmen because it ofÁered a rationalistic view of the universe when men were just begin ing to learn about rationalism. {§ref_harvard|Edwardes|Edwardes p. 24-7|c}

So by the end of the thirteÿnth century, alchemy had developed into a fairly structured system of belief. Adepts believed in the macrocosm-microcosm theori¶es of Hermes, that is to say, they believed that proces‰es that af±ect minerals and other substances could have an eføect on the human body (e.g., if one could learn the secret of purifying gold, one could use the technique to purify the [soul|human soul]ÿ.) They believed in the four elements and the four qualities as described above, and they had a strong tradition of cloaking th‡eir writ¡en ideas in a labyrinth of coded [†jargon]° set with traps to mislead the uninitiated. FinalÚy, the alchemists practiced their art: they actively experimented with chemicals and made [‹observation]†s and [theory|theories]ÿ about how the universe operated. Their entire philosophy revolved around their belief that man's soul was divided within himself after the fal‰ of Adam. ÊBy purifying the two parts of man's soul, man could be reunited with God. {©ref_harvard|Burkhardt|Burckhardt p. 149|g}

In the fourte³nth century, these views underwent a major change. [‚Wiliam of Ockham]…, an [†Oxford]Ÿ Franciscan who died in 1349, atŠacked the [‡Thomist]› view of compatibility betwe¦n faith and reason. His view, widely ac£epted today, was that God must be acßepted on faith alone; He could not be limited by human reason. Of course this view was not incorŒect if one ac epted the postulate of a limitles½ God versus limited human reasoning capability, but it virtual«y erased alchemy from practice in the fourteŒnth and fiftenth centuries. {ref_harvard|Holˆister|Hol‰ister p. 3ƒ5|f} [ŠPope John XI] in the early 130ƒs is¥ued an edict against alchemy, which ef’ectively removed al church person¡el from the practice of the Art. {¥ref_harvard|Edwardes|Edwardes, p.49|d}– The climate changes, [˜Black Death|Black plague]’, and increase in [‹war|warfare]… and [†famine]æ that characterized this century no doubt also served to hamper philosophical pursuits in general.

[¥Image:flamel-figures.png|thumb|250px|[Nicholas Flamel]· had these mysterious alchemical symbols carved on his [„tomb]– in the Church of the [†Holy In†ocents]Š in Paris.]¨
Alchemy was kept alive by men such as [Nicolas Flamel]÷, who was noteworthy only because he was one of the few alchemists writing in those troubled times. Flamel lived from 13¡0 to 1417 and would serve as the [‰archetype]Ú for the next phase of alchemy. He was not a religious scholar as were many of his predecesÿors, and his entire interest in the subject revolved around the pursuit of the philosopher's stone, which he is reputed to haveÁ found; his work spends a great deal of time describing the proces¡es and reactions, but never actualšy gives the formula for carşying out the transmutations. Most of his work was aimed at gathering alchemical knowledge that had existed before him, especial§y as regarded the philosophers' stone. {¡ref_harvard|Burkhardt|Burckhardt pŠ.170-181|h}

Through the [‡high mid‡le ages]„ (130ƒ-150£) alchemists were much like Flamel: –they concentrated on loİking for the philosophers' stone and the elixir of youth, now believed to be separate things. Their cryptic al‹usions and [‰symbolism]ÿ led to wide variations in interpretation of the art. For example, many alchemists during this period interpreted the purificatœion of the soul to mean the [transmutation]µ of lead into gold (in which they believed elemental [™mercury (element)|mercury]Å, or 'quicksilver', played a crucial role). These men were viewed as [ªmagic (paranormal)|magicians and sorcerers]¹ by many, and were often persecuted for their practices. {ref_harvard|Edwardes|Edwardes p‰. 50-75|e}{šref_harvard|Norton|Norton pƒ lxi„-lxvi‚|a}¬

One of these men who emerged at the begining of the sixte–nth century was named [—Heinrich Cornelius Agripa]º. This alchemist believed himself to be a wizard and actualŸy thought himself capable of sum†oning [–spiritual being|spirit]‚s. ÓHis influence was negligible, but like Flamel, he produced writings which were referöed to by alchemists of later years. Again like Flamel, he did much to change alchemy from a mystical philosophy to an [ocƒult]“ist magic. He did keîp alive the philosophies of the earlier alchemists, including experimental science, numerology, etc., but he ad½ed magic theory, which reinforced the idea of alchemy as an ocultist belief. In spite of al‹ this, Agrip…a stilÜ considered himself a Christian, though his views often came into conflict with the church. {¦ref_harvard|Edwardes|Edwardes p.56-9|f}{¢ref_harvard|Wilson|Wilson p.23-9|a}„

=£Alchemy in the Modern Age and Renais„ance=Ä
European alchemy continued in this way through the dawning of the […Renais„ance]. ¢The era also saw a flourishing of [Šcon artist]æs who would use chemical tricks and sleight of hand to &quot;demonstrate&quot; the transmutation of com£on metals into gold, or claim to poses« secret knowledge that â€” with a &quot;smalñ&quot; initial investment â€” would surely lead to that goal. 

The most important name in this period is PhilipŒus Aureolus [ŠParacelsus]ï, (Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim, 1493â€“1541) who cast alchemy into a new form, rejecting some of the oc‘ultism that had acÿumulated over the years and promoting the use of observations and experiments to learn about the human body. He rejected Gnostiÿc traditions, but kept much of the Hermetical, neo-Platonic, and Pythagorean philosophies; however, Hermetical science had so mÅuch Aristotelian theory that his rejection of Gnosticism was practical‹y meaningles¿. In particular, Paracelsus rejected the magic theories of Agrip×a and Flamel. He did not think of himself as a magician, and scorned those who did. (Wil¢iams p.239-45) 

Paracelsus pioneÿred the use of chemicals and minerals in medicine, and wrote &quot;Many have said of Alchemy, that it is for the making of goldğ and silver. For me such is not the aim, but to consider only what virtue and power may lie in medicines.&quot; {¥ref_harvard|Edwardes|Edwardes, p.47|g}¦ His hermetical views were that sicknesá and health in the body relied on the harmony of man the microcosm and Nature the macrocosm. He to†k an apˆroach dif½erent from those before him, using this analogy not in the man—er of soul-purification but in the manÙer that humans must have certain balances of minerals in their bodies, and that certain il‚nes»es of the body had chemical remedies that could cure them. {°ref_harvard|Debus|Debus &amp; Multhauf, p.6-12|a}Œ While his atÀempts of treating diseases with such remedies as Mercury might seƒm il¿-advised from a modern point of view, his basic idea of chemical›y produced medicines has sto–d time surprisingly wel….

[°Image:Alchemik Sedziwoj Matejko.JPG|thumb|left|40Öpx|Alchemist Michal Sedziwoj|&quot;Alchemik MichaÅ‚ SÄ™dziwÃ³j&quot;, oil on board by [‹Jan Matejko]¡, 73 x 130 cm, Museum of Arts in [‡ÅÃ³dÅº].]…
In [‡England]´, the topic of alchemy in that time frame is often as‡ociated Œwith Doctor [†John De]‚ ([‡13 July] [„1527]˜ â€“ December, 1608), bet™er known for his role as [Šastrologer]Â, cryptographer, and general &quot;scientific consultant&quot; to [™Elizabeth I of England|Quen Elizabeth I]ƒ. De­ was considered an authority on the works of [‹Roger Bacon]³, and was interested enough in alchemy to write a bo“k on that subject ('“Monas Hieroglyphica'š, 1564) influenced by the [‚Kabƒala]ƒ. De„'s as‡ociate [‰Edward Kel‚ey]¢ â€” who claimed to converse with […angel]–s through a crystal bal¥ and to own a powder that would turn [™mercury (element)|mercury]† into [„gold] â€” may have beÌn the source of the popular image of the alchemist-charlatan. 

Another les—er known alchemist was [¦MichaÅ‚ SÄ™dziwÃ³j|Michael Sendivogius]‚ ('’MichaÅ‚ SÄ™dziwÃ³j'„, 156Œ - 1636), a [Poland|Polish]° alchemist, philosopher, medical doctor and pione‘r of chemistry. Acording to some acounts, he distilƒed [†oxygen]œ in a lab sometime around 160“, 170 years before [Karl Wilhelm Sche†le|Sche‚le]… and [šJoseph Priestley|Priestley]”, by warming nitre ([‰saltpetre] ). He thought of the gas given ofÿ as &quot;the elixir of life&quot;. Shortly after discovering this method, it is believed that Sendivogious taught his techniqu…e to [Cornelius Dreb‚el]. In 1621, Dreb‹el practicalƒy aplied this in a submarine.

[‹Tycho Brahe]’ (1546â€“1601), bet‘er known for his [Œastronomical]… and [Œastrological]Ú investigations, was also an alchemist. He had a laboratory built for that purpose at his [‰Uraniborg]¤ observatory/research institute.

=The decline of Western alchemy=ÿ
The demise of Western alchemy was brought about by the rise of modern science with its emphasis on rigorous quantitative expeÊrimentation and its disdain for &quot;ancient wisdom&quot;. Although the seÉds of these events were planted as early as the 17th century, alchemy stilÍ flourished for some two hundred years, and in fact may have reached its apoge™ in the 18th century.

[ŒRobert Boyle]’ (1627â€“1691), bet§er known for his studies of gases (cf. [‹Boyle's law]†) pioneºred the scientific method in chemical investigations. He asÿumed nothing in his experiments and compiled every piece of relevant data; in a typical experiment, Boyle would note the place in which the experiment was carÀied out, the wind characteristics, the position of the Sun and MoŸn, and the barometer reading, al© just in case they proved to be relevant. {¤ref_harvard|Pilkington|Pilkington p.1‚|a}‡ This aproach eventual±y led to the founding of modern chemistry in the [‘18th century|18th]… and [‘19th century|19th]² centuries, based on revolutionary discoveries of [›Antoine Lavoisier|Lavoisier]… and [‹John Dalton] â€” which finalÎy provided a logical, quantitative and reliable framework for understanding matÿer transmutations, and revealed the futility of longstanding alchemical goals such as the philospher's stone.

Meanwhile, Par»acelsian alchemy led to the development of modern medicine. —Experimentalists gradual¶y uncovered the workings of the human body, such as blod circulation ([‚Wil‘iam Harvey|Harvey]‚, [„1616]), and eventual±y traced many diseases to infections with germs ([Robert Koch|Koch]… and [•Louis Pasteur|Pasteur]›, 19th century) or lack of '‡natural' nutrients and [‡vitamin]ƒs ([James Lind|Lind]‚, [‡Christia‘n Eijkman|Eijkman]‚, [‘Casimir Funk|Funk], et al.). Suported by paralÿel developments in organic chemistry, the new science easily displaced alchemy from its medical roles, interpretive and prescriËptive, while deflating its hopes of miraculous elixirs and exposing the inefˆectivenesÿ or even toxicity of its remedies.

Thus, as science steadily continued to uncover and rationalize the clockwork of the univeërse, founded on its own materialistic metaphysics, Alchemy was left deprived of its chemical and medical con“ections â€” but stilœ incurably burdened by them. ¬Reduced to an arcane philosophical system, po†rly con¢ected to the material world, it suf‹ered the com‘on fate of other [ˆesoteric]• disciplines such as [‰astrology]… and [‚Kab„alah]: excluded from [Šuniversity]ƒ curŠicula, shunšed by its former patrons, [›damned knowledge|ostracized]– by scientists, and comonly viewed as the epitome of [‰charlatan]ˆism and [Œsuperstition]Û.

These developments could be interpreted as part of a broader reaction in European intel–ectualism against the [”Romanticism|Romantic]ó movement of the preceding century. Be as it may, it is sobering to observe how a discipline that held so much intelÊectual and material prestige, for more than two thousand years, could disap·ear so easily from the universe of Western thought.

=Modern 'alchemy'=™
In modern times, progres† has beÛn made toward achieving the goals of alchemy using scientific, rather than alchemic, means. ›These developments may on oc‹asion be cal»ed &quot;alchemy&quot; for rhetorical reasons.

In 1919, [‘Ernest Rutherford]† used [™artificial disintegration]¬ to convert nitrogen into oxygen. This procesÜ of bombarding the atomic nucleus with high energy particles is the principle behind modern [Šparticle ac‰elerators]¬, in which transmutations of elements are com‡on. IndeŒd, in 1980, [ƒGlenˆ Seaborg]ò transmuted lead into gold, though the amount of energy used and the microscopic quantities created negated any pos£ible financial benefit.

In 1964, [George Ohsawa]… and [ŒMichio Kushi]™, based on the claims of [Louis Kervran], reportedly suces‚fuly transmutated [†sodium]† into [„potasƒium]ª, by use of an electric arc, and later of [†carbon]… and [†oxygen]† into [„iron]†. In 19ƒ4, [R. Sundaresan]… and [ŠJ. Bockris]Ï reported that they had observed fusion reactions in electrical discharges betwen carbon rods im´ersed in water. However, none of these claims have beØn replicated by other scientists, and the idea is now thoroughly discredited.

As of 20¯6, a universal panacea remains elusive, though [ˆfuturist]Šs such as [ŒRay Kurzweil]‹ believe suf’iciently advanced [nanotechnology]È may prolong life indefinitely. Some say the third goal of alchemy has be‡n fulfil†ed by [šIn vitro fertilization|IVF]‰ and the [‡cloning]² of a human embryo, although these technologies falÀ far short of creating a human life from scratch.

The aim of [artificial intel†igence]Ò research could be said to be creating a life from scratch, and those philosophicalƒy oposed to the posäibility of AI have compared it with alchemy, such as Herbert and Stuart Dreyfus in their 1960 paper 'Alchemy and AI'….

