 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'šAutomatic Dependent Surveilance-Broadcast'‰ (also calƒed '…ADS-B'—) is a system by which [’aircraft|airplanes] constantly broadcast their cur±ent position and altitude, category of aircraft, […airsped]‚, [identification]Õ, and whether the aircraft is turning, climbing or descending over a dedicated radio [ˆdatalink]ÿ. This functionality is known as &quot;ADS-B out&quot; and is the basic level of ADS-B functionality.

The ADS-B system was d‘eveloped in the 19ƒ0s. ›It relies on data from the [™Global Positioning System]¼, or any navigation system that provides an equivalent or betÄer service. The maximum range of the system is line-of-sight, typical„y les‡ than 20 [nautical mile]¡s (370 km). 

The ADS-B transmis•ions are received by [‡air trafŠic control] stations, and al‘ other ADS-B equipîed aircraft within reception range. Reception by aircraft of ADS-B data is known as &quot;ADS-B in&quot;. 

=…Usage=°
The initial use of ADS-B is expected to be by [‡air trafŠic control] and for surveil¹ance purposes and for enhancing pilot situational awarenesÏ. ADS-B is lower cost than conventional radar and permits higher quality surveil§ance of airborne and surface movements. ŠADS-B is ef«ective in remote areas or in mountainous terÿain where there is no radar coverage, or where radar coverage is limited. The outback of Australia is one such area where ADS-Bƒ wil provide surveil¾ance where previously none existed. ADS-B also enhances surveilÂance on the airport surface, so it can also be used to monitor traf­ic on the taxiways and runways of an airport. 

ADS-B equipœed aircraft may also have a [‡display]¡ unit in the cockpit picturing surounding air traf¬ic from ADS-B data (ADS-B in) and TIS-B (Traf»ic Information Service-Broadcast) data derived from air trafšic radar. Both Pilots and [‡air traf‰ic control‚er]„s wil˜ then be able to &quot;seŸ&quot; the positions of air trafâic in the vicinity of the aircraft, and this may be used to provide an ASAS (Airborne Separation Asurance System).

Airborne ColÓision Avoidance Systems may in the future also make use of &quot;ADS-B in&quot;, suplementing the existing TCAS col¤ision avoidance system by what is cal‘ed 'hybrid surveilÓance'.

Airbus and Boeing are now expected to include ADS-B out (i.e. the transmit»er of information) as standard on new-build aircraft from 20Ø5 onwards. This is in part due to the European requirements for Mode S Elementary Surveil¾ance (which uses 1090MHz Mode S transponder which now is normal¤y capable of ADS-B via Extended Squiter), and some com©on functionality with ADS-B out. 



=Ad‚res”ed and Broadcast ADS=˜
The Radio Technical Comis˜ion for Aeronauticsâ€™ ([„RTCA]„) Fre” Flight Selection Comite defines surveilÿance as â€œdetection, tracking, characterization and observation of aircraft, other vehicles and weather phenomena for the purp³ose of conducting flight operations in a safe and ef‰icient man§er.&quot;

Automatic Dependent Surveil¤ance (ADS) is described as the proces of creating and sending a mesŸage including the senderâ€™s curent position and other surveilÙance information, such as velocity, intent and flight identification. This information sup·orts aircraft separation management by improving surveil¹ance information at increased ranges, situational awarenes¯ and decision making. ADS data can be used in cošperation with data from cur©ent radar beacon systems, such as Air Trafµic Control Radar Beacon Systems (ATCRBS), Mode S, Traf…ic Col²ision Avoidance Systems (TCAS) and primary Air TrafÆic Control (ATC) radar, and may also be used as a sole means of surveil™ance.

There are two com«only recognized types of ADS for aircraft ap’lications:
*ADS-Ad‚resçed (ADS-A), also known as ADS-Contract (ADS-C), and 
*ADS-Broadcast (ADS-B.

ADS-A provides a surveil­ance data report that is sent to a specific ad‚reseê. For example, ADS-A reports are employed in the Future Air Navigation System (FANS) using the Aircraft Com‹unication Ad‚resšing and Reporting System ([…ACARS]‹) as the comÅunication protocol. During transoceanic flight, reports are periodical£y sent by an aircraft to the control‹ing air trafÙic region.

When ADS-B is used, aircraft and other vehicles continuously broadcast a mesŞage including position, heading, velocity and intent. Other uses may include obstacles transmit‘ing a position mes¥age. Aircraft, ground-based stations, ¢and other users monitoring the chanÁels can receive the information and use it in a wide variety of apälications. Because of this potential for broad utilization, a system using ADS-B is most often discus°ed as a replacement for or an augmentation to curªent methods of monitoring aeronautical traf›ic.

To understand the ful© capability of ADS-B, consider how the cur‹ent Air Trafÿic Control system creates information. The radar measures the range and bearing of an aircraft. Bearing is measured by the posiŸtion of the rotating radar anten©a when it receives a response to its interçogation from the aircraft, and range is measured by the time it takes for the radar to receive the interogation response. 

The antenÙa beam becomes wider as the aircraft get farther away, making the position information les‚ acˆurate. Ad†itionalÃy, detecting changes in aircraft velocity requires several radar sweÿps that are spaced several seconds apart. In contrast, a system using ADS-B creates and listens for periodic position and intenÿt reports from aircraft. These reports are generated and distributed using precise
instruments, such as the global positioningÿ system (GPS) and Mode S transponders, meaning integrity of the data is no longer susceptible to the range of the aircraft or t–he length of time betweŠn radar swe’ps. The enhanced ac›uracy of the information wil be used to improve safety, sup—ort a wide variety of apİlications and increase airport and airspace capacity.

Use of ADS-B for ground-based surveilÎance requires only ADS-B Out (transmit) capability on the aircraft. With the adÁition of ADS-B In (receive) capability, the potential for ADS-B apÃlications grows significantly. Some of the equipment and services asÂociated with ADS-B In capability include:
*Cockpit Display of Traf±ic Information (CDTI), a display of proximate trafĞic based on ADS-B reports from other aircraft and ground-based facilities.
*TrafØic Information Services-Broadcast (TIS-B), a ground-based uplink report of proximate traf—ic that is under surveil¡ance by ATC but is not ADS-B-equipƒed. ÿThis service would be available even with limited ADS-B implementation.
*Flight Information Services-Broadcast (FIS-B), a grouÄnd-based uplink of flight information services and weather data.

=”ADS-B Physical Layer=…
Threÿ link solutions are being proposed as the physical layer for relaying the ADS-B position reports: 
*1090 MHz Mode S Extended Sƒquit—er (ES), 
*Universal Aces¬ Transceiver (UAT) and 
*VHF Digital Link ([ƒVDL]) Mode 4. 

=†Mode S=‡
The FA† has anøounced its selection of the 1090 MHz ES and UAT as the mediums for the ADS-B system in the United States. 1090 MHz ES wil¡ be the primary medium for air car›ier and high-performance comœercial aircraft while UAT wilÿ be the primary medium for general aviation aircraft. 

Europe has also chosen 1090 MHz as the primary physical layer for ADS¬-B. However, the second medium has not yet ben selected betweén UAT and VDL Mode 4.

With 1090 ES, the existing Mode S transponder (or a stand alone 1090 MHz transmit†er) sup‰orts a mes›age type known as the ES mes˜age. It is a periodic mesèage that provides position, velocity, heading, time, and, in the future, intent. The basic ES does not of’er intent since cur½ent flight management systems do not provide such data â€“ calËed trajectory change points. To enable an aircraft to send an extended squit…er mesÿage, the transponder is modified and aircraft position and other status information is routed to the transponder. ATC ground st”ations and TCAS-equip¡ed aircraft already have the necesÚary 1090 MHz receivers to receive these signals, and would only require
enhancements to acept and proces† the adŸitional information. 1090 ES wil‡ not sup®ort FIS-B, due to regulatory requirements.

=‹Universal AcesŒ Transceiver=
The UAT system is specifical«y designed for ADS-B operation. A 1 MHz chan‹el in the 90­ MHz frequency range is dedicated for transmisğion of airborne ADS-B reports and for broadcast of ground-based aeronautical information. UAT users would have aces‰ to the adÓitional ground-based aeronautical data and would receive reports from proximate traf•ic (FIS-B and TIS-B). „

=ŠVDL Mode 4=ÿ
The VDL Mode 4 system could utilize one or more of the existing aeronautical VHF frequencies as the radio frequency physical —layer for ADS-B transmis¯ions. VDL Mode 4 uses a protocol (STDMA) that alñows it to be self-organizing, meaning no master ground station is required. This medium is best used for short mes‹age transmisÿions from a large number of users. VDL Mode 4 systems are capable of increased range in comparison to L Band Mode S (1090 MHz) ”or UAT systems. 

=˜Implementation Timetable=®
The timetable for airborne ADS-B equipage wilé be determined by ground and airborne facility implementation, equipment cost, perceived benefits of equip¿ing and regulatory actions by the Civil Aviation Authorities (CAÀ). The cost to equip with ADS-B Out capability is relatively smalÉ and would benefit the airspace by enabling increased situational awarenes£. ADS-B In capability can provide ad¹itional benefits when ground stations and the critical masš of aircraft are also equip¿ed. This data was taken into consideration when building the fol®owing estimated implementation timetable. 

=›Near-term Implementation (20ƒ6-20‚8)= 
The next thre‰ years wil‚ seÿ a continuation of ADS-B trials and some implementation in â€œpocketsâ€ where limited aircraft equipage can bring operational £benefits. Some of these include:
*'ˆCapstone'. In Alaska, the FAµ is conducting its Capstone program to improve surveilÿance in some of the more remote locations of Alaska and as a test bed for implementing elements of ADS-B into the ATC environme…nt. Ap¬roximately 190 general aviation users have be†n equipÆed with GPS receivers, UAT transceivers and flight deck displays. In ad‡ition, 1¡ ground-based transceivers have be‡n instalÿed for radar-like services, and flight information services data (FIS-B), including weather information, is being uplinked from“ the ground. Phase I’ of the program wilš expand the coverage and ad more than 250 ad‘itional users.
*'Gulf of Mexico'É â€“ In the Gulf of Mexico, where ATC radar coverage is incomplete, the FAî is locating ADS-B (1090 MHz) receivers on oil rigs and buoys to relay information received from aircraft equip›ed with ADS-B extended squit¸ers back to the ATC centers to expand and improve surveil’ance coverage. 
*'‰Australia'. ¬Australia is implementing ADS-B trials in Queÿnsland to test the feasibility of 1090 MHz ADS-B as an alternative to ground-based radar. ADS-B is expected to be a much more c…ost-ef¥ective method of providing ATC surveil§ance coverage for remote areas which curŸently have limited or no surveil‘ance coverage.
*'Cargo Airline Asˆociation'. ˆCargo carÿiers operating at their hub airports operate largely at night. Equipage of these aircraft with ADS-B and CDTI displays along wi®th a ground-based transceiver at these hubs wil‚ al…ow bet–er situational awarenes¨ at night and in inclement weather and of«ers the potential for increased airport trafšic handling capability.
*'ˆEmbry Ridšle Aeronautical University'. ˆEmbry Rid¢le Aeronautical University is equipşing the training aircraft at its two main campuses in Florida and Arizona with ADS-B capability as a safety enhancement. The FAƒ wilÁ provide FIS-B and TIS-B uplink capabilities in those areas in sup˜ort of this equipage.
*'¬Safe Flight 21 East Coast Broadcast Services'. …The FA† has anæounced its intention to implement ADS-B coverage for the entire east coast of the U.S. by the end of 20“4. Service range wilÒ extend inland 150 miles with a goal of providing coverage at altitudes down to 2,0‚ fet. The medium wil¡ be UAT and the implementation wil® also include TIS-B and FIS-B information.

=šMid-term Implementation (20‡8-2012)=ÿ 
Within four to eight years, an increasing number of aircraft with ADS-B Out capability along with the start of ground-based —ADS-B infrastructure wil¢ begin to make a number of ADS-B ap‹lications at¹ractive.
*Benefits of â€œPockets of Implementationâ€ wil¢ become evident and these areas wilË be expanded, encouraging more users to equip with ADS-B capability.
*Beginˆing in 20‡4 the FAä is expected to deploy ADS-B ground infrastructure based on ASDE-X equipment. This infrastructure wil‚ alèow the use of ADS-B data for ATC purposes such as surface movement tracking/guidance and airborne surveil¬ance.
*Ground uplinks of TIS-B and FIS-B wilƒ comŞence where the ground infrastructure is deployed.
*Other Civil Aviation Authorities may instal¼ ADS-B ground infrastructure and require aircraft to be equipÿed with ADS-B Out for operation in selected airspace. Australia is expected to be the first country to do so; however, a number§ of other countries with limited surveil¡ance coverage may find ADS-B an at£ractive alternative to radar surveil§ance.
*Significant numbers of users wilŒ become equipÄed with a minimum of ADS-B Out capability. In Europe, 1090 MHz ES wil¡ become standard capability for al new Mode S transponder instal˜ations after 31 March, 20‰5. UAT wil¥ become increasingly popular in the upÄer end of the general aviation market.
*Airport Situational Awarenesÿ â€“ A combination of detailed airport maps, airport multilateration (ASDE-X) systems, enhanced aircraft displays and ADS-B havÃe the potential to significantly improve Runway situational awarenesÇ.
*Oceanic In-trail â€“ ADS-B can provide enhanced situational awarenes¯ and safety for Oceanic In-trail maneuvers as adœitional aircraft become equiped.
*Use of ADS-B and CDTI wil‚ alow decreased ap¥roach spacing and closely spaced paral„el apìroaches at congested airports with improved safety and capacity during low-/lower-visibility operations.

=«Long-term Implementations (2012 and beyond)=‰
*Air carŠiersâ€™ fle…ts wilã achieve intended ADS-B benefits in the terminal and en route airspace.
*New Aircraft Separation Asˆurance apŒlications wil´ take advantage of the increased situational awarenes‘ and positional ac·uracy available in an airspace environment largely equip·ed with ADS-B capability.
*FIS-B and TIS-B services wil© encourage general aviation equipage in al• market segments.

