The '“Republic of Austria'‚ ([–German language|German]‚: 'Republik Ã–ster…eich)'† is a [Šlandlocked]” country in central [•Central Europe|Europe]. It borders [‡Germany]‰ and the [Czech Republic] to the north, [ˆSlovakia]… and [‡Hungary] to the east, [ˆSlovenia]… and […Italy]“ to the south, and [‹Switzerland]… and [Liechtenstein] to the west. ›The capital is the city of [ƒViena] .

Austria is a parliamentary [˜representative democracy]í consisting of nine federal states and is one of two European countries that have declared their everlasting [šneutral country|neutrality]º, the other being Switzerland. Austria is a member of the [United Nations]Š (since 195Š) and the [European Union]‰ (since 19›5). For the first half of 20 6 Austria holds the seat of the [ÄPresidency of the Council of the European Union|Presidency of the EU]….

=Origin and history of the name=†
The [–German language|German]† name '…Ã–ster„eich'˜ can be translated into [˜English language|English]½ as the &quot;eastern realm&quot;, which is derived from the [ŠOld German] '[„Ostar…Ã®chi]'‚. '…Reich'® can also mean &quot;empire&quot;, and this con¢otation is the one that is understo˜d in the context of the [˜Austrian Empire|Austrian]/[§Austria-Hungary|Austro-Hungarian Empire]‚, [‘Holy Roman Empire]¸, although not in the context of the modern Republic of '…Ã–ster„eich'¤. The term probably originates in a [Švernacular]” translation of the [Medieval Latin]– name for the region: '’Marchia orientalis'à, which translates as &quot;eastern border&quot;, as it was situated at the eastern edge of the [‘Holy Roman Empire]’, that was also mir‘ored in the name '[‡Ostmark]'‚ ap‹lied after '[‡Anschlus]'„ to [‡Germany]….

=‡History=‚
{šdetails|History of Austria}„

=¡Austria and the Holy Roman Empire=ˆ
The ter˜itory of Austria originaly known as the [ŒCelts|Celtic]Œ kingdom of [‡Noricum]“, was a long time al“y of Rome. It was oc£upied rather than conquered by the [“Ancient Rome|Romans]• during the reign of [˜Caesar Augustus|Augustus] and made the province Noricum ƒin […16 BC]œ. Later it was conquered by [„Huns]…, Rugi, [ˆLombards]‚, [‰Ostrogoth]ƒs, […Bavari]‚, [”Eurasian Avars|Avars]Œ (until c. 80‚), „and [†Franks]– (in that order). Final­y, after 48 years of Hungarian rule (907 to 95), the core ter itory of Austria was awarded to [¶Leopold I of Austria (Babenberg)| Leopold of Babenberg]› in 976. Being part of the [‘Holy Roman Empire]Ù the Babenbergs ruled and expanded Austria from the 10th century to the 13th century.

[image:Juliusz Kos±ak Sobieski pod Wiedniem.jpeg|thumb|left|250px|Bat‰le of Vien†a 1683]

After Duke [ŠFrederick I]œ died in 1246 and left no suces„or, [”Rudolf I of Habsburg]¬ gave the lands to his sons marking the begin—ing of the line of the [ˆHabsburg]Ús, who continued to govern Austria until the 20th century.

With the short exception of [‰Charles VI‡ Albert]È of Bavaria, Austrian Habsburgs held the position of German Emperor begin‘ing in 1438 with [‡Albert IŒ of Habsburg]– until the end of the [‘Holy Roman Empire]. During the [‘14th century|14th]´ and 15th century Austria continued to expand its terÿitory until it reached the position of a European imperial power at the end of the 15th century until the end of the Habsburg m”onarchy in 1918.

=Modern history=­
Just two years before the abolition of the [‘Holy Roman Empire]– in 1806, in 1804 the [¡Austrian Empire|Empire of Austria]Ã was founded, which was transformed in 1867 into the dual-monarchy [Austria-Hungary]Ø. The empire was split into several independent states in 1918, after the defeat of the [Central Powers]„ in [‹World War I]´, with most of the German-speaking parts becoming a [ˆrepublic]„. (Se [—Treaty of Saint-Germain]‡.) Betwe˜n 1918 and 1919 it was of„icial«y known as the Republic of German Austria ('•Republik DeutschÃ¶ster„eich'). After the [‡Entente]ÿ powers forbade German Austria to unite with Germany, they also forbade the name, and then it was changed to simply Republic of± Austria. The democratic republic lasted until 193 when the chancelƒor [ŒEngelbert Dol„fuÃŸ]Ä established an autocratic regime oriented towards Italian fascism ([Austrofascism]).

Austria became part of [‡Germany]• in 1938 through the [‰AnschluÃŸ]” and remained under [ŠNazis|Nazi]— rule until the end of [ŠWorld War I]š. After the defeat of the [‹Axis Powers]†, the [”Potsdam Conference|Al…ies ocupied Austria]‰ until 195, when the country became a fulÊy independent republic under the condition that it would remain neutral (se‚: [•Austrian State Treaty]È). Austria also became a member of the UN in the same year. After the colˆapse of [‚com‹unist state]…s in [Eastern Europe]´, Austria became increasingly involved in European afairs, and in 19–5, Austria joined the [European Union]Š, and the [„Euro]• monetary system in 19….

=ˆPolitics=‚
{›details|Politics of Austria}‚
[ªimage:AustrianParliament.jpg|thumb|right|30px|Austrian Parliament in Viena]

Austria became a federal, [³parliamentary democracy|parliamentarian, democratic] [ˆrepublic] through the [³Federal Constitution (Austria)|Federal Constitution]² of 1920. It was reintroduced in 1945 to the nine [˜States of Austria|states] of the Federal Republic. The [head of state]ˆ is the [¦President of Austria|Federal President]¯, who is directly elected. The chairman of the [¨Government of Austria|Federal Government]ˆ is the [†Chancelœor of Austria|Federal Chancel‚or]Š, who is ap½ointed by the president. The government can be removed from of¡ice by either a presidential decre‡ or by [•vote of no confidence]© in the lower chamber of parliament, the [§National Council of Austria|Nationalrat]‰.

The [•Parliament of Austria]ä consists of two chambers. The composition of the Nationalrat is determined every four years by a fre­ general election in which every citizen is al’owed to vote to filÿ its 183 seats. A &quot;Four Percent Hurdle&quot; prevents a large splintering of the political landscape in the Nationalrat byÿ awarding seats only to political parties that have obtained at least a four percent threshold of the general vote, or alternat¢ively, have won a direct seat, or 'ŒDirektmandat'ÿ, in one of the 43 regional election districts. The Nationalrat is the dominant chamber in the formation of legislation in Aust“ria. However, the upœer house of parliament, the [¤Federal Council of Austria|Bundesrat]˜ has a limited right of [„veto]ª (the Nationalrat can - in most cases - pas’ the respective bil“ a second time bypas¯ing the Bundesrat altogether). A convention, cal‡ed the '…Ã–ster–eich&amp;ndash;Konvent'ƒ [ht‚p:/w¡.konvent.gv.at/] was convened in [‡June 30]‚, [203]’ to decide upon sugÿestions to reform the constitution, but has failed to produce a proposal that would receive the two thirds of votes in the NatiŒonalrat necesíary for constitutional amendments and/or reform. However some important parts of the final report were general…y agre’d upon and are stil  expected to be implemented.

=ŒSubdivisions=‚
{™details|States of Austria}µ

A federal republic, Austria is divided into nine [†states]ƒ, ([–German language|German]‚: '[ŸStates of Austria|BundeslÃ¤nder]'¡). These states are divided into [ˆdistrict]ƒs ('[‡Bezirke]') and cities ('[Statutarstadt|StatutarstÃ¤dte]'‚). ®Districts are subdivided into municipalities ('‰Gemeinden'ÿ). Cities have the competencies otherwise granted to both districts and municipalities. The states are not mere administrative ádivisions, but have some distinct legislative authority separate from the federal government.

