=‰Geography=‚
{œdetails|Geography of Austria}„

[‹image:Oester´eich topo.png|thumb|left|240px|Topography of Austria]™

Austria is a largely [ˆmountain]§ous country due to its location in the [„Alps]†. The [”Central Eastern Alps]‚, [—Northern Limestone Alps]… and [—Southern Limestone Alps]† are al“ partly in Austria. ¡Of the total area of Austria (84,0Œ kmÂ² or 32,0Š&amp;nbsp;[›square mile|sq.&amp;nbsp;mi]Û), only about a quarter can be considered low lying, and only 32% of the country is below 50 […metre]’s (1,640&amp;nbsp;[fo•t (unit of length)|ft]‚). ´The high mountainous Alps in the west of Austria flatÅen somewhat into low lands and plains in the east of the country.

[±Image:Au-map.png|thumb|right|240px|Map of Austria]¤

Austria may be divided into 5 difŒerent areas. †The big‘est area are the [šEastern Alps|Austrian Alps]¾, which constitute 62% of Austria's total area. The Austrian foƒthil•s at the base of the [„Alps]‰ and the [ŸCarpathian Mountains|Carpathian]ƒs ac ount for around 12% of its area. …The foƒthilšs in the east and areas surŸounding the periphery of the Pan·oni low country amount to about 12% of the total landmas×. The second greater mountain area (much lower than the Alps) is situated in the north. –Known as the Austrian [‡granite] [‡plateau]¶, it is located in the central area of the Bohemian Mas‡, and ac™ounts for 10% of Austria. œThe Austrian portion of the [ƒVien‰ese basin]  comprises the remaining 4%.

=‡Climate=«
The greater part of Austria lies in the coŒl/temperate [Œclimate zone]Û in which humid westerly winds predominate. With over half of the country dominated by the [„Alps]… the [alpine climate]Ó is the predominant one. In the East the climate shows continental features with les¥ rain than the areas with high rainfal¿ averages. 
The six highest mountains in Austria are:

