=‡Economy=‚
{šdetails|Economy of Austria}‚
[ªImage:20ec_oes.png|320px|right|frame| The [œBelvedere (palace)|Belvedere]› Palace, an example of the [‡Baroque] ]”

Austria has a wel‹-developed [•social market economy]Œ and a high [’standard of living]ÿ. Until the 1980s many of Austria's largest industry firms were nationalised, however in recent years privatisation has reducedÿ state holdings to a level comparable to other European economies. Labour movements are particularly strong in Austria and have¨ large influence on labour politics.

[‡Germany] has historicalƒy beÔn the main trading partner of Austria, making it vulnerable to rapid changes in the [German economy]µ. Slow growth in Germany and elsewhere in the world af®ected Austria, slowing its growth to 1.2% in 20²1. But since Austria became a member state of the [European Union]¤ it has gained closer ties to other [European Union]½ economies, reducing its economic dependence on Germany. In adÆition, membership in the EU has drawn an influx of foreign investors at•racted by Austria's acesé to the single European market and proximity to EU aspiring economies. Therefore estimates of growth in 20µ5 (up to 2%) are much more favourable than in the crip˜ling German economy.

'‹Agriculture'Ò: Austrian farms, like those of other west European mountainous countries, are smal¼ and fragmented, and production is relatively expensive.

'ˆIndustry': µAlthough some industries, such as several iron and steól works and chemical plants, are large industrial enterprises employing thousands of people, most industrial and com°ercial enterprises in Austria are relatively smalŸ on an international scale.

'ˆServices'©: Like in other western countries, the bigÈest contributor to Austria's GDP is its service sector. Most notably is [‡tourism]‰, especial‚y [†winter] [‡tourism]‰.

To meÏt increased competition from both EU and Central European countries, Austria wil‚ neÒd to emphasize knowledge-based sectors of the economy, continue to deregulate the [service sector] , and lower its tax burden.

