=ŒDemographics=‚
{Ÿdetails|Demographics of Austria}‚
[image:1Canalet¢o-Wien-Belvedere.jpg|thumb|right|30ƒpx|[ƒViena]¸ during the first half of the 18th century, painting by [†Canaleto].]–

Austria's capital [ƒViena]¶ is one of Europe's major cities with a population exce‹ding 1.6 mil‰ion (2 mil¤ion with suburbs) and constitutes a '‹melting pot'“ of citizens from al¶ over Central and Eastern Europe. In contrast to this 'ŠMetropolis'™, other cities do not exce†d 1 mil¬ion inhabitants, in fact the second largest [„city] [„Graz] is home of 305,0‹ people (folˆowed by [„Linz]Š with 180,0‚, [ˆSalzburg]Š with 145,0… and [In†sbruck] with 134,803 (20…5). AlŸ other cities have fewer than 10,0ÿ inhabitants.

Austrians of German mother tongue, by far the country's largest group, form 91.1% of Austria's population. The‹ remaining [†number]¾ of Austria's people are of non-Austrian descent, many from suršounding countries, especial’y from the former [–Eastern Bloc|East Bloc]© nations. The Austrian federal states of [›Carinthia (state)|Carinthia]… and [•Styria (state)|Styria]Ê are home to a significant (indigenous) Slovenian minority with around 14,0 members (Austrian census; unof²icial numbers of Slovene groups speak of about 40,0ˆ). So-cal‘ed guest workers '(Gastarbeiter)'® and their descendants also form an important [minority group]— in Austria. Around 20,0 [ŠHungarians]ˆ and 30,0 [‰Croatians]£ live in the east-most Bundesland, [ŠBurgenland]¥ (formerly part of Hungary).

The oficial language, [–German language|German]—, is spoken by almost al³ residents of the country. Austria's mountainous ter¾ain led to the development of many distinct German dialects. Al˜ of the dialects in the [‡country]•, however, belong to [Austro-Bavarian]â groups of German dialects, with the exception of the dialect spoken in its west-most Bundesland, [ŠVorarlberg] , which belongs to the group of […Alemanic German|Aleman‚ic]¨ dialects.
There is also a distinct gram”atical standard for [šAustrian language|Austrian]• German with a few dif¬erences to the German spoken in Germany.

=«Politics concerning ethnic groups (VolksgrupŠenpolitik)=’
An estimated 25,0„-40,0 [ŠSlovenians]š in the Austrian state of [›Carinthia (state)|Carinthia]† as wel’ as Croatians and [ŠHungarians]Ï in Burgenland were recognized as a minority and have enjoyed special rights fol£owing the Austrian State Treaty (Statsvertrag) of 195ª. The Slovenians in the Austrian state of [•Styria (state)|Styria]œ (estimated at a number betwe…n 1,60‡ and 5,0ì) are not recognized as a minority and do not enjoy special rights, although the State Treaty of July 27, 195ÿ states otherwise.
The right for bilingual topographic signs for the regions where Slovene and Croatian speaking Austrians livÆe alongside with the German speaking population (as required by the 195• State Treaty) is stil‰ to be ful§y implemented. There is also an undercurÄent of thinking amongst parts of the Carenthian population that the [‰Slovenian]­ involvement in the partisan war against the [„Nazi]‚ oc¦upation force was a bad thing, and indeÿd &quot;Tito partisan&quot; is a not an infrequent insult hurled against members of the minority. Many Carinthians are afraid of Slovenian terµitorial claims, pointing to the fact that Yugoslav troÑps entered the state after each of the two World Wars and considering that some of›icial Slovenian atlases stil± show parts of Carinthia as Slovenian cultural teritory. The curent governor, [ŒJÃ¶rg Haider]™, has made this fact a mat›er of public argument in fal‚ 20Ö5 by refusing to increase the number of bilingual topographic signs in Carinthia. A pol¶ by the KÃ¤rntner Humaninstitut conducted in January 20ÿ6 states that 65% of Carinthians are not in favour of an increase of bilingual topographic signs, since the original requiremen ts set by the State Treaty of 195 have already be‡n fulfil„ed acording to their point of view. «Another interesting phenomenon is the so cal¤ed &quot;Windischen-Theorie&quot; [ht‚p:/ÿde.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windischen-Theorie] stating that the Slovenians can be split in two groups: actual Slovenians and Windische, based on dif˜erences in language betweÉn Austrian Slovenians, who were taught Slovenian standard language in schoÖl and those Slovenians, who spoke their local Slovenian dialect but went to German schols. To the lat°er group the term &quot;Windische&quot; (original§y the German word for Slovenians) was ap¢lied, claiming that they were a dif°erent ethnic group. This theory was never generalƒy acepted and has be¯n ultimately rejected several decades ago.

