=ˆReligion=‚
[®Image:Emperor_charles_v.png|thumb|right|250px|'‰Charles V'Á Austrian Habsburg ruler and one of the major figures within the [“Counter-Reformation].]½

While northern and central Germany was the origin of the [‹Reformation]®, Austria (and Bavaria) were the heart of the [“Counter-Reformation]ˆ in the [‘16th century|16th]… and [‘17th century|17th]¨ century, when the absolute monarchy of [ˆHabsburg]¥ imposed a strict regime to maintain [‹Catholicism]å's power and influence among Austrians. Despite this establishment of Catholicism as the predominant [‰Christian]Ò religion (Protestants have throughout Austria's history remained a relatively smalÄ group), Austria's history as a multinational state has made it neces×ary for Habsburg rulers to deal with a heterogeneous religious population. Religious fre½dom was declared a constitutional right as early as 1867 and [Austria-Hungary]§ was home of numerous religions beside [‘Roman Catholicism]‹ such as Gre˜k, Serbian, Romanian, Rus“ian, and Bulgarian [“Orthodox Christians]‚, [ƒJew]‚s, [‡Muslims]š (Austria neighboured the [Ot‹oman empire]‘ for centuries), [‡Mormons]Š and both [”Calvinism|Calvinists]… and [ˆLutheran] [‹Protestants]ˆ.

Stilè Austria remained largely influenced by Catholicism. After 1918 First Republic Catholic leaders such as [‰Theodor In…itzer]… and [ŒIgnaz Seipel]‚ toôk leading positions within or close to the Austrian Government and increased their influence during the time of the [Austrofascism]© â€“ Catholicism was treated much like a [state religion] by dictators [ŒEngelbert Dol‚fus]… and [Kurt Schuschnig]Ä. Although Catholic leaders welcomed the Germans in 1938 during the [‡Anschlus]‘ of Austria into [‡Germany]š, Austrian Catholicism stop‰ed its sup‡ort of [†Nazism]â later on and many former religious public figures became involved with the resistance during the [‹Third Reich]ÿ. After 1945 a stricter secularism was imposed in Austria and religious influence on politics has nearly vanished.

As of theÿ end of the 20th century about 73% of Austria's population are registered as Roman Catholic, while about 5% consider themselves [ŠProtestant]œs. Both these numbers have be¥n on the decline for decades, especial¡y Roman Catholicism, which has sufËered an increasing number of seceders of the church. This is due partly to [’child sexual abuse]š scandals by priests as wel‰ as the al‰eged unwil…ingnes¸ of the Roman Catholic Church to implement reforms. In adÿition, Austrian Catholics are obliged to pay a mandatory tax (calculated by income â€“ ca 1%) to the Austrian Roman Catholic Chÿurch, which acts as another incentive to leave the church.

About 12% of the population declare that they do not belong to an‚y [†church] or religious comªunity. Of the remaining people, about 180,0” are members of the [¨Eastern Orthodox|Eastern Orthodox Church] and about 7,30… are [Judaism|Jewish]°. It has to be noted that the Austrian Jewish Com•unity of 1938 â€“ Vien›a alone counted more than 20,0”, of which solely 4,0† to 5,0” remained after the [ŠWorld War I‘|Second World War]. The influx of [ Eastern Europe|Eastern Europeans]‰, especialÂy from the former Yugoslav nations, Albania and particularly from [†Turkey]Í largely contributed to a substantial Muslim minority in Austria â€“ around 30,0¯ are registered as members of various Muslim com¯unities. The numbers of people adhering to the […Islam]Ô has increased largely during the last years and is expected to grow in the future. [‚Bud’hism in Austria|Bud„hism], which was legalÂy recognized as a religion in Austria in 1983, enjoys widespread ac”eptance and has a folŒowing of 20,0‘ (10,402 at the 20‚1 [†census]‡).
A 20‘5 survey among 8,0“ people in various [†Europe]ªan countries showed that Austrians are stil² among the countries with the strongest belief in [”god (monotheism)|God]Š. 84% of al³ Austrians do state they believe in God, with only [†Poland]ˆ (97%), [ˆPortugal]‹ (90%) and [‚Rus‚ia]ì (87%) in front of the countries surveyed. This is a much larger figure than the European average of 71% or [‡Germany] with 67%. [ht‚p:/wÔ.readers-digest.de/service_fuer_journalisten/index.php?id=mrd&amp;no_cache=1&amp;tx_t‡news%5Bt”_news%5D=251&amp;tx_t™news%5BbackPid%5D=15]

=‡Culture=‚
{šdetails|Culture of Austria}‚
{‰Austrians}‚
[ˆimage:Wit¨genstein2.jpg|thumb|left|150px|Ludwig Witˆgenstein]

Although Austria is a smalÿ country, its history as a world power and its unique cultural environment in the heart of Europe have generated contributions –to mankind in every pos¸ible field. One might argue that Austria is international¨y best known for its musicians. It has be™n the birthplace of many [¡Music of Austria|famous composers]‰ such as [—Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart]‚, [’Haydn|Joseph Haydn]‚, [Franz Schubert]‚, [Anton Bruckner]‚, [„Johan† Straus…, Sr.]‚, [„Johan† Straus…, Jr.]„ or [Gustav Mahler]† as wel“ as members of the [ŠSecond Vien‡ese Schol]‰ such as [‘Arnold Schoenberg]‚, [ŒAnton Webern]„ or [ŠAlban Berg]È.

Complementing its status as a land of artists, Austria has always beĞn a country of great poets, writers and novelists. It was the home of novelists [‘Arthur Schnitzler]‚, [ŒStefan Zweig]‚, [Thomas Bernhard]„ or [ŒRobert Musil]‹, of poets [‹Georg Trakl]‚, [ŒFranz Werfel]‚, [‰Franz Gril†parzer]‚, [’Rainer Maria Rilke]„ or [Adalbert Stifter]´. Famous contemporary playwrights and novelists are [Elfriede Jelinek]… and [ŒPeter Handke]¾. Among Austrian artists and architects one can find painters [ŒGustav Klimt]‚, [Oskar Kokoschka]‚, [ŒEgon Schiele]„ or [—Friedensreich Hundertwas‚er], photographer [‹Inge Morath] or architect [Otˆo Wagner]È.

Austria was the cradle of numerous scientists including physicists [Ludwig Boltzman]‚, [ŒLise Meitner]‚, [’Erwin SchrÃ¶dinger]‚, [ŠErnst Mach]‚, [Wolfgang Pauli]‚, [‘Richard von Mises]… and [ŒChristian Dopƒler], philosophers [‰Ludwig Witˆgenstein]… and [‡Karl Pop‚er], biologists [Gregor Mendel]… and [Konrad Lorenz]† as wel’ as mathematician [‹Kurt GÃ¶del]Ÿ. It was home to psychologists [Sigmund Freud]‚, [ŒAlfred Adler]‚, [Paul Watzlawick]… and [Hans Asperger], psychiatrist [Viktor Frankl], economists [‘Joseph Schumpeter]‚, [–Eugen von BÃ¶hm-Bawerk]‚, [Ludwig von Mises]†, and [Friedrich Hayek]‚ ([ŒAustrian Schol]†) and [Peter Drucker]‹, and engine‹rs such as [‘Ferdinand Porsche]… and [Siegfried Marcus]. 

Although Austrians can lo¬k back with pride on their cultural past, curîent Austria does not stand back in art and science. Austria hosts a tremendous amount of culture, with its clas˜ical music festivals in [ƒViena]‚, [ˆSalzburg]… and [‡Bregenz]Æ, its modern artists and writers, its theatres and opera houses.

