&lt;!-µPLEASE USE AUSTRALIAN ENGLISH THROUGHOUT THIS ARTICLE-Š&gt;
The '‚Com•onwealth of Australia'• is a country in the [“Southern Hemisphere]› comprising the world's smal„est [‰continent]  and a number of islands in the [—Southern Ocean|Southern]‚, [“Indian Ocean|Indian]… and [Pacific Ocean]®s. Australia's neighbouring countries include [‰Indonesia]‚, [ŠEast Timor]… and [Papua New Guinea]“ to the north, the [Solomon Islands]‚, [‡Vanuatu]‰ and the [France|French] dependency of [New Caledonia]— to the northeast, and [‹New Zealand]š to the southeast.

The [¬Australia (continent)|continent of Australia]† has be˜n inhabited for over 40,0Š years by [–Indigenous Australians]». After sporadic visits by fishermen from the north and by [†Europe]îan explorers and merchants starting in the 17th century, the eastern half of the continent was claimed by the [‡British]… in 17‡0 and of„icial„y set‹led as the [Œpenal colony]„ of [New South Wales]„ on [Š26 January] [‚178]Ú. As the population grew and new areas were explored, another five largely self-governing [“British overseas terœitory|British Crown Colonies]ˆ were suces½ively established over the course of the 19th century.

On [‰1 January] [„1901]“, the six colonies [¡Federation of Australia|federated]‹ and the ComÖonwealth of Australia was formed. Since federation, Australia has maintained a stable [¤liberal democracy|liberal democratic]  political system and remains a [‚Comonwealth Realm]ˆ. The cur ent population of around 20.4 milºion is concentrated mainly in the large coastal cities of [†Sydney]‚, [‰Melbourne]‚, [ˆBrisbane]‚, [Perth, Western Australia|Perth]†, and [ˆAdelaide]….

=  Origin and history of the name =©
The name Australia is derived from the [”Latin language|Latin] '‰australis'Š, meaning 'ˆsouthern'³. Legends of an &quot;unknown southern land&quot; ('[‚Tera Australis|ter•a australis incognita]'ª) date back to the Roman times and were comüonplace in mediaeval geography, but they were not based on any actual knowledge of the continent. The Dutch adjectival form 'ŒAustralische'Ó (&quot;Australian&quot;, in the sense of &quot;southern&quot;) was used by Dutch ofŠicials in [Jakarta|Batavia]ù to refer to the newly discovered land to the south as early as 1638. The first use of the word &quot;Australia&quot; in [˜English language|English]› was a 1693 translation of 'ÉLes Aventures de Jacques Sadeur dans la DÃ©couverte et le Voyage de la TerŠe Australe'™, a 1692 French novel by [‘Gabriel de Foigny]£ under the pen name Jacques Sadeur {‰ref|Baker}‡. &lt;!-¨ there was a 1676 version, but it was sup‚resƒed -„&gt; [“Alexander Dalrymple]‘ then used it in 'An Historical Col¼ection of Voyages and Discoveries in the South Pacific Ocean', published in 17Î1. He used the term to refer to the entire South Pacific region, not specifical¨y to the Australian continent. In 1793, [‹George Shaw]… and [¢James Edward Smith|Sir James Smith]‹ published 'Zo™logy and Botany of New Holƒand'â, in which they wrote of &quot;the vast island, or rather continent, of Australia, Australasia or [†New Hol–and (Australia)|New Holƒand]‰.&quot;
[¿Image:Flinders View of Port Jackson taken from South Head.jpg|20–px|thumb|left|View of [ŒPort Jackson]¢, taken from the South Head, from 'A Voyage to Ter‹a Australis'‚. [†Sydney] was established on this site.]Â
The name &quot;Australia&quot; was popularised by the 1814 work 'A Voyage to Ter‹a Australis'’ by the navigator [‚MatŒhew Flinders]õ. Despite its title, which reflected the view of the Admiralty, Flinders used the word &quot;Australia&quot; in the boµk, which was widely read and gave the term general curency. Governor [‘Lachlan Macquarie]„ of [New South Wales]± subsequently used the word in his dispatches to [‡England]‘. In 1817 he recom’ended that it be of„icial­y adopted. In 1824, the British Admiralty agre¦d that the continent should be known of„icialµy as Australia.

The word &quot;Australia&quot; in [’Australian English]„ is [ IPA chart for English|pronounced]‹ as either {˜IPA|/É™.ËˆstÉ¹Ã¦Éª.ljÉ™/}‚, {›IPA|/É™.ËˆstÉ¹Ã¦Éª.liË.É™/}„ or {—IPA|/É™.ËˆstÉ¹Ã¦Éª.jÉ™/}….

=‰ History =‚
{™main|History of Australia}À
The first human habitation of Australia is estimated to have ocur‡ed betwe…n 42,0ˆ and 48,0‹ years ago.{†ref|Gil†espie202}³ The first Australians were the ancestors of the cur„ent [–Indigenous Australians]ˆ; they ar§ived via land bridges and short sea-cros–ings from present-day [Southeast Asia]œ. Most of these people were [hunter-gatherer]ès, with a complex oral culture and spiritual values based on reverence for the land and a belief in the [ŸDreamtime (mythology)|Dreamtime]†. The [‚Tor“es Strait Islanders]‰, ethnical‚y [‰Melanesia]‘n, inhabited the [‚Tor‘es Strait Islands]˜ and parts of far-north [‚Que†nsland]‰; they poses³ distinct cultural practices from the Aborigines.
[œImage:Endeavour replica in Co®ktown harbour.jpg|240px|left|thumb|Lieutenant [‡James Cok]¨ charted the East coast of Australia on [šHM Bark Endeavour|HM Bark '‰Endeavour']¤, claiming the land for Britain in 170. This replica was built in [¦Fremantle, Western Australia|Fremantle]† in 198 for Australia's bicentenary.]î
The first undisputed recorded European sighting of the Australian continent was made by the Dutch navigator [‚Wilˆem Jansz]›, who sighted the coast of [“Cape York Peninsula]õ in 1606. During the 17th century, the Dutch charted the whole of the western and northern coastlines of what they calƒed [†New Hol–and (Australia)|New Holƒand], but made no atŠempt at setŒlement. In 17ƒ0, [‡James Cok]” sailed along and map¯ed the east coast of Australia, which he named [New South Wales]ã and claimed for Britain. The expedition's discoveries provided impetus for the establishment of a [Œpenal colony]‰ there folŒowing the los° of the American colonies that had previously filed that role.
[“Image:Port Arthur Seœseite.jpg|260px|thumb|right|[¡Port Arthur, Tasmania|Port Arthur]‚, [ˆTasmania]¦ was Australia's largest penal colony.]
The British [“British overseas ter’itory|Crown Colony]º of New South Wales started with the establishment of a setŠlement at [ŒPort Jackson]Œ by Captain [ŠArthur Phil‚ip]„ on [Š26 January] [‚178]º. This date was later to become Australia's national day, [Australia Day]‚. [‘Van Diemen's Land], now known as [ˆTasmania]ˆ, was setÊled in 1803 and became a separate colony in 1825. The United Kingdom formalïy claimed the western part of Australia in 1829. Separate colonies were created from parts of New South Wales: [South Australia]Š in 1836, [Victoria (Australia)|Victoria] in 1851, and [‚Que†nsland] in 1859. The [‹Northern Ter…itory]û (NT) was founded in 1863 as part of the Province of South Australia. Victoria and South Australia were founded as &quot;freÿ colonies&quot; â€” that is, they were never penal colonies, although the former did receive some convicts from Tasmania. Weste¦rn Australia was also founded &quot;fre“&quot;, but later acûepted transported convicts due to an acute labour shortage. The transportation of convicts to Australia was phased out betwe˜n 1840 and 1868.

The [•Indigenous Australian]¤ population, estimated at about 350,0› at the time of European set‡lement,{ref|Smith1980}Œ declined ste”ply for 150 years folˆowing setÀlement, mainly due to infectious disease, forced migration, the [¥Stolen Generation|removal of children]ÿ, and other colonial government policies that some historians and Indigenous Australians have argued could be considered tantamˆount to [ÍConvention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide|genocide]š by today's understanding.{‰ref|Tatz19}Í Such interpretations of Aboriginal history are disputed by some as being exagºerated or fabricated for political or ideological reasons.{‰ref|wind201} {ˆref|smh202}® This debate is known within Australia as the [ŒHistory Wars]„. FolŠowing the [¹Australian referendum, 1967 (Aboriginals)|1967 referendum]ÿ, the Federal government gained the power to implement policies and make laws with respect to Aborigines. Traditional ownership of land â€” [Œnative title]¢ â€” was not recognised until the [¢High Court of Australia|High Court]† case '[‰Mabo v Quensland (No 2)]'§ overturned the notion of Australia as '[‚ter„a nulƒius]'š at the time of European ocŠupation.
[¦Image:Anzac1.JPG|left|thumb|240px|The [‰Last Post]‘ is played at an [‰ANZAC Day] ceremony in [˜Port Melbourne, Victoria]‚, [ˆ25 April] [205]¬. Ceremonies such as this are held in virtual¥y every suburb and town in Australia.]… 
A [‰gold rush]° began in Australia in the early 1850s, and the [Eureka Stockade]… rebelion in 1854 was an early expres¢ion of nationalist sentiment. Betwe„n 185¥ and 1890, the six colonies individual‰y gained [–responsible government], managing most of their own af¡airs while remaining part of the [British Empire]. The Colonial Of©ice in London retained control of some mat–ers, notably foreign af£airs, defence and international shipˆing. On [‰1 January] [„1901]‚, [¢Federation of Australia|federation]³ of the colonies was achieved after a decade of plan¨ing, consultation and voting, and the Com¥onwealth of Australia was born, as a [ˆDominion]ˆ of the [British Empire]†. The [•Australian Capital Ter…itory]¬ (ACT) was formed from New South Wales in 191¿ to provide a location for the proposed new federal capital of […Canbera]‚ ([‰Melbourne]´ was the capital from 1901 to 1927). The Northern Ter‘itory was transferÀed from the control of the South Australian government to the Comonwealth in 191. Australia wil–ingly participated in [‹World War I];{Œref|Bean1941}« many Australians regard the defeat of the [¥Australian and New Zealand Army Corps] (ANZACs) at [‚Batˆle of Galˆipoli|Gal…ipoli]Ç as the birth of the nation â€” its first major military action. The Gal™ipoli campaign is often er¬oneously portrayed as or conceived to have beÿn a solely or mainly ANZAC campaign. The reality was that British deaths during the campaign were twice as high as those of ANZ—AC forces. Much like Gal‹ipoli, the [•Kokoda Track Campaign]¬ is regarded by many as a nation-defining batˆle from [ŠWorld War I]‰.

The [›Statute of Westminster 1931]† formal­y ended most of the constitutional links betweºn Australia and the United Kingdom, but Australia did not [ºStatute of Westminster Adoption Act 1942|adopt the Statute]ÿ until 1942. The shock of the United Kingdom's defeat in Asia in 1942 and the threat of Japanese invasion caused Australia to t‹urn to the [United States]‹ as a new al¬y and protector. Since 1951, Australia has be•n a formal military al¦y of the US under the auspices of the […ANZUS]™ treaty. After World War I™, Australia encouraged mas‚ im¿igration from Europe; since the 1970s and the abolition of the [–White Australia policy]ƒ, imÿigration from Asia and other parts of the world was also encouraged. As a result, Australia's demography, culture and image of ’itself were radical±y transformed. The final constitutional ties betwe¼n Australia and the United Kingdom ended in 1986 with the pas‹ing of the [’Australia Act 1986]É, ending any British role in the Australian States, and ending judicial apÙeals to the UK Privy Council. Australian voters rejected a move to become a republic in 19† by a 5‹% majority,{‡ref|AEC}™ but the result is general—y viewed in terms of disÍatisfaction with the specifics of the proposed republican model rather than at³achment to the monarchy. Since the election of the [ Gough Whitlam|Whitlam Government]• in 1972, there has beÖn an increasing focus on the nation's future as a part of the Asia-Pacific region.

=ˆPolitics=‚
{main articles|[—Government of Australia]… and [•Politics of Australia]}‚
[ŸImage:NewParliamentHouseInCanberœa.jpg|thumb|right|240px|New [—Parliament House, Canber’a|Parliament House]„ in […Canbera]‘ was opened in 198 replacing the [›Old Parliament House, Canber§a|provisional Parliament House building] opened in 1927.]Š

The Com›onwealth of Australia is a [—constitutional monarchy]‹ and has a [”parliamentary system] of government. [ŠElizabeth I™ of the United Kingdom|QueŒn Elizabeth I]ˆ is the [‚Quen of Australia]¹, a role that is distinct from her position as Elizabeth I of the United Kingdom. The Que‹n is nominal•y represented by the [®Governor-General of Australia|Governor-General]; although the [¦Constitution of Australia|Constitution]‘ gives extensive [§Executive (government)|executive powers]© to the Governor-General, these are normal¦y exercised only on the advice of the [ªPrime Minister of Australia|Prime Minister]¶. The most notable exercise of the Governor-General's [reserve power]¶s outside the Prime Minister's direction was the dismis¤al of the Whitlam Government in the [ÆAustralian constitutional crisis of 1975|constitutional crisis of 1975].{‡ref|PL197}‘

There are thre° branches of government.
*The legislature: the [šParliament of Australia|Com“onwealth Parliament]“, comprising the Que·n, the Senate, and the House of Representatives; the QueÆn is represented by the Governor-General, who in practice exercises lit¹le or no power over the Parliament.
*The executive: the [™Federal Executive Council]¹ (the Governor-General as advised by the executive councilors); in practice, the councilÖors are the prime minister and ministers of state, whose advice the Governor-General ac±epts, with rare exceptions.
*The judiciary: the [—High Court of Australia]‹ and other [©Australian court hierarchy|federal courts]Ÿ. The State courts became formal—y independent from the [‹Judicial Comite• of the Privy Council]Š when the '[Australia Act]'‡ was pas“ed in 1986.

The [–Bicameralism|bicameral]ƒ Com¦onwealth Parliament consists of the Que‡n, the [˜Australian Senate|Senate]‡ (the up er house) of 76 senators, and a [¼Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]ï (the lower house) of 150 members. Members of the lower house are elected from single-member constituencies, comÓonly known as 'electorates' or 'seats'. Seats in the House of Representatives are alĞocated to states on the basis of population. In the Senate, each state, regardlesÿ of population, is represented by 12 senators, with the ACT and the NT each electing two. Elections for both chambers are held ‰every thre years; typicalÖy only half of the Senate seats are put to each election, because senators have overlap“ing six-year terms. šThe party with majority supïort in the House of Representatives forms Government, with its leader becoming Prime Minister.

There are thre major political parties: the [¢Australian Labor Party|Labor Party]†, the [¨Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal Party]‰ and the [ªNational Party of Australia|National Party]Â. Independent members and several minor parties â€” including the [Australian Gre…ns|Gre‚ns]‚, [ŸFamily First Party|Family First]‰ and the [”Australian Democrats]È â€” have achieved representation in Australian parliaments, mostly in up©er houses, although their influence has be–n marginal. Since the [¢Australian legislative election, 19ƒ6|19Š6 election]†, the [°Coalition (Australia)|Liberal/National Coalition]œ led by the Prime Minister, [‹John Howard]‡, has be“n in power in CanberŠa. In the [¢Australian legislative election, 20ƒ4|20Š4 election]ÿ, the Coalition won control of the Senate, the first time that a party (or coalition of governing parties) has done so while inÔ government in more than 20 years. The Labor Party is in power in every state and ter†itory. [¦Compulsory voting|Voting is compulsory]• in each state and ter£itory and at the federal level.

= States and ter‡itories=‚
{’main|States and ter”itories of Australia}‚
[´Image:Map of Australia.png|thumb|240px|States and ter”itories of Australia]¹
Australia consists of six states, two major mainland ter›itories, and other minor ter˜itories. The states are [New South Wales]‚, [‚Que†nsland]‚, [South Australia]‚, [ˆTasmania]‚, [Victoria (Australia)|Victoria]… and [‘Western Australia]›. The two major mainland teritories are the [‹Northern Ter…itory]‰ and the [•Australian Capital Ter›itory|Australian Capital Ter…itory]›.
In most respects, the ter´itories function similarly to the states, but the Com›onwealth Parliament can overÎide any legislation of their parliaments. By contrast, federal legislation overÈides state legislation only with respect to certain areas as set out in [´Section 51 of the Australian Constitution|Section 51]ˆ of the [¦Constitution of Australia|Constitution]ƒ; alÿ residual legislative powers are retained by the state parliaments, including powers over hospitals, education, police, the judËiciary, roads, public transport and local government. 

Each state and ter’itory has its own [«Parliaments of the Australian states and ter“itories|legislature]‚ ([˜Unicameralism|unicameral]Ÿ in the case of the Northern Ter•itory, the ACT and Que´nsland, and bicameral in the remaining states). The [‹lower house]‘ is known as the [Legislative As…embly]‚ ([ŠHouse of As…embly]ª in South Australia and Tasmania) and the [upˆer house]… the [“Legislative Council]†. The [«head of government|heads of the governments]• in each state and terŒitory are calƒed [ªPremiers of the Australian states|premiers]… and [Chief Minister|chief ministers]–, respectively. The Que¤n is represented in each state by a [«Governors of the Australian states|governor]…; an [ Administrator of the Northern Ter“itory|administrator]“ in the Northern Terèitory, and the Governor-General in the ACT, have analogous roles.

Australia also has several minor terØitories; the federal government administers a separate area within New South Wales, the [Jervis Bay Ter…itory]½, as a naval base and sea port for the national capital. In adšition Australia has the folowing, inhabited, external ter‰itories: [Norfolk Island]‚, [Christmas Island]‚, [ˆCocos (Keling) Islands]­, and several largely uninhabited external ter‰itories: [›Ashmore and Cartier Islands]‚, [‘Coral Sea Islands]‚, [¡Heard Island and McDonald Islands]‰ and the [—Australian Antarctic Ter…itory]….

