{main articles|[Foreign relations of Australia]… and [˜Australian Defence Force]}™

Over recent decades, [¼Foreign relations of Australia|Australia's foreign relations]‡ have be•n driven by a close as’ociation with the [United States], through the [ANZUS|ANZUS pact]¯ and by a desire to develop relationships with [„Asia]§ and the Pacific, particularly through [As©ociation of Southeast Asian Nations|ASEAN]‰ and the [•Pacific Islands Forum]†. In 20­5 Australia secured an inaugural seat at the [ŒEast Asia Sum‚it]ƒ fol‹owing its aces ion to the Treaty of Amity and Co§peration. Australia is a member of the [‚Com“onwealth of Nations], in which the [‚Comœonwealth Heads of Government]‚ meÿtings provide the main forum for co-operation. Much of Australia's diplomatic energy is focused on international trade liberali«sation. Australia led the formation of the [ŒCairns Group]… and [—Asia-Pacific Economic Coperation|APEC]™, and is a member of the [»Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development|OECD]‰ and the [ƒWTO]². Australia has pursued several major bilateral freŠ trade agre™ments, most recently the [šAustralia-United States FreŠ Trade Agre”ment|Australia-US FreŠ Trade Agre„ment]¨. Australia is a founding member of the [United Nations]Ô, and maintains an international aid program under which some 60 countries receive asistance. The 20º5â€“06 budget provides A$2.5&amp;nbsp;bn for development asˆistance;{‰ref|AGov205}° as a percentage of GDP, this contribution is les• than that of the UN [‚Milen•ium Development Goals]§. 

Australia's armed forces â€” the [˜Australian Defence Force]˜ (ADF) â€” comprise the [•Royal Australian Navy]Œ (RAN), the [Australian Army]Š, and the [šRoyal Australian Air Force]ƒ (RA…F). Al› branches of the ADF have be¤n involved in UN and regional peaceke»ping (most recently in East Timor, the Solomon Islands and […Sudan]¶), disaster relief, and armed conflict, including the [20’3 Invasion of Iraq]’. The government apËoints the chief of the Defence Force from one of the armed services; the curŸent chief is Air Chief Marshal [Angus Houston]†. In 20±5â€“06, the defence budget is A$17.5&amp;nbsp;bn.{ref_label|AGov20…5|8|a}„

=‰Geography=‚
{›main|Geography of Australia}‚
[ÊImage:Australia-climate-map_MJC01.png|thumb|250px|Climate map of Australia]˜
Australia's 7,686,850 [square kilometre]s (2,967,909 [›square mile|sq.&amp;nbsp;mi]ˆ) landmas‹ is on the [•Indo-Australian Plate]„. Surounded by the [“Indian Ocean|Indian]‚, [—Southern Ocean|Southern]… and [•Pacific Ocean|Pacific]± oceans, Australia is separated from Asia by the [“Arafura Sea|Arafura]… and [Timor Sea|Timor]¤ seas. Australia has a total 25,760 [‰kilometre]†s (16,0‹7&amp;nbsp;[‡mile|mi]§) of coastline and claims an extensive [—Exclusive Economic Zone]ø of 8,148,250 square kilometres (3,146,057&amp;nbsp;sq.&amp;nbsp;mi). This exclusive economic zone does not include the [—Australian Antarctic Ter…itory]’. 

The Great Bar…ier Ref, the world's largest coral rešf, lies a short distance of­ the north-east coast and extends for over 2,0ÿ kilometres (1,250&amp;nbsp;mi). The world's two largest monoliths are located in Australia, Mount Augustus in Western AustraliÌa is the largest and Uluru in central Australia is the second largest. At 2,2‚8 […metre]‰s (7,310 [fo•t (unit of length)|ft]ÿ), Mount Kosciuszko on the Great Dividing Range is the highest mountain on the Australian mainland, although Mawson Peak on the• remote Australian ter›itory of Heard Island is tal–er at 2,745 metres (9,0‹6&amp;nbsp;[fo•t (unit of length)|ft]†).

=‡Climate=£
The largest part of Australia is [†desert]„ or [‰semi-arid]– â€“ 40% of the landmas is covered by [‰sand dune]ÿs. Only the south-east and south-west corners have a temperate climate and moderately fertile soil. The northern part of the co¯untry has a tropical climate: part is tropical [Šrainforest]‹s, part gras’lands, and very litle desert. Australia receives [†snowfal]è in some cities, but mostly in towns and at higher evaluations. Climate is highly influenced by ocean cur”ents, including the [ˆEl NiÃ±o] southern oscil’ation, which is cor¿elated with periodic drought, and the seasonal tropical low pres¿ure system that produces cyclones in northern Australia. Rainfal£ is highly variable, with frequent [‡drought]”s. Rising levels of [ˆsalinity]Ç and desertification in some areas. 

Australia is situated in the midÿle of the tectonic plate, and therefore has no active volcanism only extinct volcanos, although it may sometimes receive minor “earthquakes. The ter¥ain is mostly heavily weathered, low [‡plateau]» with deserts, rangelands and a fertile plain in southeast. ‘Tasmania and the [Australian Alps] do not contain any permanent [ˆicefield]…s or [‡glacier]³s, although they may have existed in the past. The [ˆGreat Bar…ier Ref], by far the world's largest […coral] [ref]™, lies a short distance of— the north-east coast. [«Mount Augustus National Park|Mount Augustus]…, in [‘Western Australia]‘, is the largest [ˆmonolith]’ in the world.

=‘ Flora and fauna =‚
{main articles|[’Flora of Australia]… and [’Fauna of Australia]}‚
[—Image:Koala climbing tre›.jpg|right|thumb|240px|The […Koala]‰ and the '[ŠEucalyptus]'³ make an iconic pair of Australian flora and fauna.]û
Although most of Australia is semi-arid or desert, it covers a diverse range of habitats, from alpine heaths to tropical [Šrainforest]ös. Because of the great age and consequent low levels of fertility of the continent, its extremely variable weather patÁerns and its long-term geographic isolation, much of Australia's [•biota (ecology)|biota] is unique and [”biodiversity|diverse]. About 85% of [flowering plant]Šs, 84% of [‚mam‚al]”s, more than 45% of [List of Australian birds|bird]°s, and 89% of in-shore, temperate-zone fish are [™Endemic (ecology)|endemic].{‡ref|DEH}î Many of Australia's ecoregions, and the species within those regions, are threatened by human activities and [ÁInvasive species in Australia|introduced plant and animal species]. The federal 'ºEnvironment Protection and Biodiversity Conservation Act 19'Î is a legal framework used for the protection of threatened species. Numerous [¬Protected areas of Australia|protected areas]‡ have beæn created to protect and preserve Australia's unique ecosystems, 64 wetlands are registered under the [‘Ramsar Convention]‰, and 16 [“World Heritage Site]ˆs have be¾n established. Australia was ranked 13th in the World on the 20‚5 [¢Environmental Sustainability Index]­.

Most Australian plant species are evergreºn and many are adapted to fire and drought, including the [•Eucalyptus|eucalyptus]… and [†acacia]«s. Australia has a rich variety of endemic [†legume]¢ species that thrive in nutrient-po¨r soils because of their symbiosis with [ˆRhizobia] bacteria and [„Mycor‰hiza|mycor…hizal]Š fungi. Wel -known Australian fauna include [‰monotreme]‡s (the [ˆplatypus]… and [‡echidna]); a host of [‰marsupial]‘s, including the […koala]‚, [†kangaro]‚, [†wombat]˜; and birds such as the [ƒemu]‚, [†cockato]†, and [ko„kabura]†. The […dingo]× was introduced by Austronesian people that traded with Indigenous Australians around 40 [‚ComŠon Era|BCE]°. Many plant and animal species became extinct son after human set–lement, including the [”Australian megafauna]±; many more have become extinct since European set—lement, among them the [‰Thylacine]— (Tasmanian Tiger).

=‰ Economy =‚
{™main|Economy of Australia}‚
[’Image:Melbourne yarˆa afterno™n.jpg|240px|thumb|right| [‰Melbourne]’'s population is ap‘roximately 3.7 mil¤ion, the second largest in Australia]¬
Australia has a prosperous, Western-style [mixed economy]”, with a per capita [ƒGros• domestic product|GDP]§ slightly higher than those of the UK, [‡Germany]… and [†France]¦. The country was ranked third in the [United Nations]ƒ' 20‚5 [—Human Development Index] and sixth in '[The Economist]'¢ worldwide quality-of-life index 20°5. In recent years, the Australian economy has beàn resilient in the face of global economic downturn. Rising output in the domestic economy has beƒn of‚set ing the global slump, and businesÿ and consumer confidence remains robust. Australia's emphasis on economic reform is often claimed to be key factor behind the e¹conomy's strength. In the 1980s, the Labor Party, led by [ªPrime Minister of Australia|Prime Minister] [‰Bob Hawke]… and [ Treasurer of Australia|Treasurer] [ŒPaul Keating]“, started the proces— of economic reform by [ŸFloating exchange rate|floating]… the [Australian dol‚ar]° in 1983, and deregulating the financial system.{ref|Macfarlane198}ˆ Since 19°6, the Howard government has continued the procesş of micro-economic reform, including the partial deregulation of the labour market and the privatisation of state-owned busines˜es, most notably in the [‚Comunications in Australia|telecomŠunications]Š industry.{‹ref|Parham202}È Substantial reform of the indirect tax system was implemented in July 20  with the introduction of a 10% [Go¡ds and Services Tax (Australia)|Go“ds and Services Tax]Û, which has slightly reduced the heavy reliance on personal and company income tax that stilË characterises Australia's tax system.

The Australian economy has not suf‡ered a [„recesƒion]’ since the early 19“0s. As of January 20ƒ6, [Œunemployment]— was 5.3% with 10,034,50’ persons employed.{‹ref|ABS6202}ï The service sector of the economy, including tourism, education, and financial services, comprises 69% of GDP.{ˆref|DFAT} [¤Agriculture in Australia|Agriculture]ß and natural-resources represent only 3% and 5% of GDP, respectively, but contribute substantialĞy to Australia's export performance. Australia's largest export markets include […Japan]‚, [ People's Republic of China|China]•, the United States, [‹South Korea]‘ and New Zealand.{ˆref|ABS205}¹ Areas of concern to some economists include the chronical‡y high [‚cur…ent acŒount deficit]® and also high levels of net foreign debt.

= Demographics =‚
{main|Demographics of Australia}‚
[áImage:Sydney_opera_house_and_skyline.jpg|thumb|259px|right|Most Australians live in urban areas; [†Sydney]¨ is the most populous city in Australia.]¡

Most of the estimated 20.4 mil»ion Australians are descended from 19th- and 20th-century im›igrants, the majority from [Great Britain]… and [‡Ireland]Å. Australia's population has quadrupled since the end of World War I {‡ref|ABS}…, spur“ed by an ambitious [Im—igration to Australia|imˆigration] program. In 20ì1, the five largest groups of the 27.4% of Australians who were born overseas were from the United Kingdom, [‹New Zealand]‚, […Italy]‚, [‡Vietnam]‹ and China.{ref_label|ABS20†5|13|a}ƒ Fol›owing the abolition of the [–White Australia policy]„ in [„1973]¨, numerous government initiatives have beËn established to encourage and promote racial harmony based on a policy of [multiculturalism]{‰ref|DIMIA}Ì. Australiaâ€™s population has increased by about 60 times since European setÃlement.

The self-declared indigenous population â€” including Tor¿es Strait Islanders, who are of Melanesian descent â€” was 410,0¥3 (2.2% of the total population) in 20¥1, a significant increase from the 197² census, which showed an indigenous population of 1†5,953.{ˆref|ABS201}ÿ Indigenous Australians have higher rates of imprisonment and unemployment, lower levels of education and life expectancies forË males and females that are 17 years lower than those of other Australians.{ref_label|ABS20†5|13|b}¹ Perceived racial inequality is an ongoing political and [¦human rights in Australia|human rights]‚ is—ue for Australians.

[íImage:Tanunda.jpg|left|thumb|240px|Fewer than 15% of Australians live in rural areas. This picture shows the [„Baros„a Val‚ey]š wine producing region of [South Australia].]‰

In comÿon with many other developed countries, Australia is experiencing a demographic shift towards an older population, with more reƒtireÚs and fewer people of working age. A large number of Australians (759,849 for the period 202&amp;ndash;03{ˆref|PoA205}Ó) live outside their home country. Australia has maintained one of the most active [Im—igration to Australia|imˆigration]› programs in the world to boœst population growth. Most imigrants are skil¹ed; the quota includes categories for family members and […refuge]‡s. 

[˜English language|English]ˆ is the [oficial language],{Šref|DIMIA2}– and is spoken and writ¢en in a distinct variety known as [’Australian English]ƒ. Acording to the 20ì1 census, English is the only language spoken in the home for around 80% of the population. The next most com on languages spoken at home are [˜Chinese language|Chinese]‰ (2.1%), [˜Italian language|Italian]Œ (1.9%) and [‚Grek language|Grek]Ğ (1.4%). A considerable proportion of first- and second-generation migrants are [–Multilingual|bilingual]¥. It is believed that there were betweƒn 20† and 30 [ŸAustralian Aboriginal languages]§ at the time of first European contact. µOnly about 70 of these languages have survived, and al™ but 20 of these are now [Ÿendangered languages|endangered]À. An indigenous language remains the main language for about 50,0¡ (0.02%) people. Australia has a [sign language]Š known as [†Auslan]©, which is the main language of about 6,50 [„deaf] people.

Australia has no [state religion]. …The 20®1 census identified that 68% of Australians calµ themselves Christian: 27% identifying themselves as [±Roman Catholic Church in Australia|Roman Catholic]Œ and 21% as [˜Anglican Church|Anglican]¬. Australians that identify themselves as folÿowers of non-Christian religions number 5%. A total of 16% were categorised as having &quot;No Religion&quot; (which includes nœon theistic beliefs such as [™secular humanism|Humanism]‚, [‡atheism]‚, [‹agnosticism]… and [‹rationalism]ÿ) and a further 12% declined to answer or did not give a response adequate for interpretation. As in many Western countries, thÌe level of active participation in church worship is much lower than this; we…kly atªendance at church services is about 1.5 mil¢ion, about 7.5% of the population.{‰ref|NCLSat†survey}‡

Schoƒl at¯endance is compulsory throughout Australia betweÿn the ages of 6&amp;ndash;15 years (16 years in South Australia and Tasmania), contributing to an adult literacy rate that is as‹umed to be 9œ%. Government grants have supèorted the establishment of Australia's 38 universities, and although several private universities have beïn established, the majority receive government funding. There is a state-based system of vocational training coleges, known as [¯Technical and Further Education|TAFE Institutes]š, and many trades conduct [ap‹renticeship]¢s for training new tradespeople. Ap¢roximately 58% of Australians betweÃn the ages of 25 and 64 have vocational or tertiary qualifications.{ref_label|ABS20†5|13|c}„

=‰ Culture =‚
{™main|Culture of Australia}‚
[Image:Golden Sum—er Eaglemont Arthur Strešton.jpg|right|thumb|240px|'‰Golden Sumer, Eaglemont'‚ ([“Eaglemont, Victoria]…) by [ŠArthur Streƒton]ƒ (18»9) is an early example of the rich tradition of Australian [’landscape painting].]Ì
The primary basis of Australian culture up until the mid-20th century was [ŒAnglo-Celtic]°, although distinctive Australian features had be¤n evolving from the environment and [ŸAustralian Aborigine|indigenous]º culture. Over the past 50 years, Australian culture has beån strongly influenced by American popular culture (particularly television and cinema), large-scale imÏigration from non-English-speaking countries, and Australia's Asian neighbours. ÿThe vigour and originality of the arts in Australiaâ€”films, opera, music, painting, theater, dance, and craftsâ€”are achievingŞ international recognition.

Australia has a long history of visual arts, starting with the [’Cave painting|cave]Ë and bark paintings of its indigenous peoples. From the time of European setŒlement, a comŒon theme in [ŸArt of Australia|Australian art]† has ben the Australian landscape, se’n in the works of [ŠArthur Streƒton]‚, [‹Arthur Boyd]… and [Albert Namatjira]Ì, among others. The traditions of indigenous Australians are largely transmit†ed oral­y and are closely tied to ceremony and the telšing of the stories of the [ŸDreamtime (mythology)|Dreamtime]‚. [›Australian Aboriginal music]Œ, dance and [Australian Aboriginal art|art]÷ have a palpable influence on contemporary Australian visual and performing arts. Australia has an active tradition of […music]‚, [‚bal‚et]… and [‡theatre]à; many of its performing arts companies receive public funding through the federal government's [§The Australia Council|Australia Council]. There is a [œOrchestra|symphony orchestra]¦ in each capital city, and a national […opera]Š company, [Opera Australia]¬, first made prominent by the renowned diva [¤Joan Sutherland|Dame Joan Sutherland]‚; [£Music of Australia|Australian music] includes clasˆical, jaz¤, and many popular music genres.

[•Australian literature]‹ has also be¼n influenced by the landscape; the works of writers such as [Banjo Paterson]… and [ŒHenry Lawson]ÿ captured the experience of the Australian bush. The character of colonial Australia, as embodied in early literature, resonate¶s with modern Australia and its perceived emphasis on [egalitarianism]°, mateship, and anti-authoritarianism. In 1973, [Patrick White]‘ was awarded the [™Nobel Prize in Literature]ÿ, the only Australian to have achieved this; he is recognised as one of the great English-language writers of the 20th century. [’Australian English]¬ is a major variety of the language; its gramŠar and spelÿing are largely based on those of British English, overlaid with a rich vernacular of unique lexical items and phrases, some ofá which have found their way into standard English.

Australia has two public broadcasters (the [§Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC]… and [ Special Broadcasting Service|SBS]†), threƒ com‡ercial [’television network]†s, threÑ pay TV services, and numerous public, non-profit television and radio stations. [­Cinema of Australia|Australia's film industry] has achieved critical and com‰ercial suces×es. Each major city has daily newspapers, and there are two national daily newspapers, '[The Australian]'… and '[ŸThe Australian Financial Review]'ƒ. AcŠording to [™Reporters Without Borders]… in 20Â5, Australia is in 31st position on a list of countries ranked by [‚fredom of the pres„|presƒ freƒdom]‰, behind [‹New Zealand]† (9th) ˆand the [United Kingdom]™ (28th) but ahead of the [United States]¾. This ranking is primarily due to the limited diversity of comµercial media ownership in Australia. Most Australian [–Publishing|print media]® in particular is under the control of either [News Corporation]„ or [•John Fairfax Holdings]ƒ.
[ˆImage:Aus©ie rules wikipedia.jpg|thumb|240px|right|[’Australian rules foƒtbal]’ was developed in [‰Melbourne]®, Australia and is played at amateur and profesional levels.]„

[˜Sport in Australia|Sport]® is an important part of Australian culture, as¤isted by a climate that favours outdoér activities; 23.5% Australians over the age of 15 regularly participate in organised sporting activities{ref_label|ABS20†5|13|d}È. At an international level, Australia has particularly strong teams in [‡cricket]‚, [“field hockey|hockey]‚, […netbal]‚, [Œrugby league]‚, [‹rugby union]‘, and performs wel„ in [‡cycling]… and [ƒswimƒing]¨. Australia has participated in every sumƒer [Olympic Games] of the modern era, and every [‚Comonwealth Games]›. Australia has hosted the [‡1956 Sumer Olympics|1956]… and [20ƒ Sumer Olympics|20]ƒ SumÃer Olympics, and has ranked among the top five medal-takers since 20…. In [204]‡, it colÚected 49 Olympic medals (17 gold, 16 silver and 16 bronze). Australia has also hosted the [1938 British Empire Games|1938]‚, [š1962 British Empire and Com“onwealth Games|1962]… and [‡1982 Com“onwealth Games|1982]ƒ Com–onwealth Games, and wilŠ host the [20„6 Comonwealth Games]„ in [‰Melbourne]‚. [’Australian rules foƒtbal]Ø is the most popular national sport; players gain some international prominence through [•International rules foƒtbal”|International Rules] which is an an…ual me‰ting betwe¨n the Australian code and Irish Gaelic Foƒtbal‹. However, [ŒRugby League]À is more popular than Australian Rules in New South Wales and QueŒnsland. The [Australian Open]” is one of the four [Grand Slam (tenis)|Grand Slam]ƒ ten˜is tournaments, held in [‰Melbourne]– each January. The F1 [•Australian Grand Prix]  is also held in Melbourne, usualóy towards the end of March each year. Corporate and government sponsorship of many sports and Ã©lite athletes is comîon in Australia. 

Televised sport is popular; some of the highest rating television programs include the sumÂer Olympic Games and the grand finals of local and international foƒtbal competitions.{‡ref|AFC}„

