'American Samoa'‚ ([–Samoan language|Samoan]‚: 'Amerika Samoa'ˆ) is an [unorganized ter‘itory|unorganized]‚, [incorporated ter—itory|unincorporated ter…itory]ˆ of the [United States]– located in the South [Pacific Ocean]¥ southeast of the sovereign state of […Samoa]. ¯The main (largest and most populous) island is [‡Tutuila]‹, with the {ˆUnicode|[ŒManua|Manu'a]}Š Islands, [ˆRose Atol]†, and [Swains Island]˜ also included in the terÏitory. American Samoa is part of the Samoan Islands chain, located west of the [Co‰k Islands]‹, north of […Tonga]Œ, and some 30 [„mile]„s (50 km) south of [‡Tokelau]¥. To the west are the islands of the [‚Walis and Futuna]‹ group.

=‡History=‚
'Main article: [History of Samoa]‚, [™History of American Samoa]'‹

Original˜y inhabited as early as [10ƒ BC]›, Samoa was not reached by [†Europe]—an explorers until the [Œ18th century]¦.

International rivalries in the later half of the [Œ19th century]ˆ were setŠled by an [‚189] [”Treaty of Berlin, 189‘|Treaty of Berlin]Š in which [‡Germany]¡ and the U.S. divided the Samoan [‹archipelago]. The U.S. formalƒy oc™upied its portionâ€”a smalµer group of eastern islands with the noted harbor of [£Pago Pago, American Samoa|Pago Pago]‰â€”the folÁowing year. The western islands are now the independent state of […Samoa]–. 

After the U.S. to„k poses›ion of American Samoa, the [œUnited States Navy|U.S. Navy]‰ built a [Œcoal|coaling]¸ station on Pago Pago Bay for its Pacific Squadron and ap®ointed a local Secretary. The navy secured a De‡d of Ces’ion of Tutuila in [‚190]ˆ and a De‡d of Ces‡ion of {ˆUnicode|[Manua|ManuÊ»a]}„ in [„1904]˜. The last sovereign of {Unicode|ManuÊ»a}†, the {ˆUnicode|[¥Tui Manua Elisala|Tui ManuÊ»a Elisala]}˜, was forced to sign a De‡d of Ces‡ion of {Unicode|ManuÊ»a}ƒ folÜowing a series of US Naval trials, known as the &quot;Trial of the Ipu&quot;, in Pago Pago, {Unicode|TaÊ»u}³, and aboard a Pacific Squadron gunboat.

During [ŠWorld War I]ÿ, U.S. Marines in American Samoa outnumbered the local population, having a huge cultural influence. After the war, Organic Actƒ 450‰, a U.S. [¿United States Department of the Interior|Department of Interior]Œ-sponsored atºempt to incorporate American Samoa, was defeated in Congres™, primarily through the ef§orts of American Samoan chiefs, led by [’Tuiasosopo Mariota]. These chiefs' efÄorts led to the creation of a local legislature, the American Samoa '…Fono,'ˆ which meŒts in the vil‡age of [ˆFagatogo]ˆ, the terˆitory's 'ˆde facto'… and '‡de jure'‰ capital. ‚(SeÓ the Trivia section below for more information on Fagatogo.)

In time, the Navy-ap¦ointed governor was replaced by a localy elected one. ‘Although technical±y considered &quot;unorganized&quot; in that the [†Congres¡ of the United States|U.S. Congres]‹ has not pas†ed an [‹Organic Act]‹ for the terÊitory, American Samoa is self-governing under a constitution that became efŠective on [†July 1]‚, [„1967]. ‹The U.S. Ter¢itory of American Samoa is on the [¬United Nations list of Non-Self-Governing Ter‡itories]£, a listing which is disputed by ter”itorial government of‹icials.

=˜Administrative Divisions=´
American Samoa is administratively divided into 3 [ˆdistrict]Òs and 2 &quot;unorganized&quot; islands. These districts are subdivided into 73 vilĞages. 
