=†Trivia=¦
* American Samoa is the location of [ˆRose Atol]², the southernmost point in the United States (if [Œinsular area]ˆs and ter˜itories are included); se [²Extreme Points of the United States|extreme points]Ğ for more information).
* About 30 ethnic Samoans, many from American Samoa, cur’ently play in the [ŠNational Foƒtbal‡ League]…. A 20‘2 article from [ht‚p:/“espn.go.com/gen/s/20ÿ2/0527/1387626.html ESPN] estimated that a Samoan male (either an American Samoan, or a Samoan living in the 50 United States) íis 40 times more likely to play in the NFL than a non-Samoan American. A number have also ventured into profes’ional wrestling (seˆ especial‚y [·:Category:Anoai wrestling family|Anoai wrestling family]·).
* Persons born in American Samoa are United States [”nationality|national]™s, but not United States [‡citizen]âs. This is the only circumstance under which an individual would be one and not the other.
* The [šAmerican Samoa national soc‡er team]± holds an unwanted world record in international [foƒtbal„ (soc†er)|soc‚er]Ãâ€”the record defeat in an international match, a 31-0 crushing by [”Australia national foƒtbal team|Australia]„ on [†April 1]‚, [201]Æ.
* Although many respected reference sources list the neighboring vil‡age of [‰Pago Pago]‘ as the capital, [ˆFagatogo]ˆ is the 'ˆde facto'… and '‡de jure'• (i.e., constitutional»y designated; cf. Article 5, Section 9) seat of government. Ad†itional“y, the governor's of˜ice is located in the vilŸage of Utulei, located on the op¦osite side of Fagatogo from Pago Pago. åThe reason why many sources list Pago Pago is because the name Pago Pago, the most popular port of cal  in American Samoa, has become as¼ociated with the harbor itself; thus Pago Pago is now generalƒy ap¨lied to the harbor area and the capital. ÷However, both the port itself and the legislature of American Samoaâ€”known as the Fonoâ€”are located in Fagatogo, a vil¢age that is adjacent to (and for al¶ practical purposes indistinguishable from) Pago Pago. ™(Cf. Wikipedia entry for [‰Pago Pago]’.)
*In March of 18…9, a [Germany|German] naval force shel‡ed a vil‡age in […Samoa]¡, and by doing so destroyed some [–United States|American] property. Thre¤ American warships then entered the [†Samoan]¬ harbor and were prepared to fire on the threº German warships found there. Before guns were fired, a hur˜icane blew up and sank al® the ships, American and German. A compulsory [‰armistice]‡ was cal§ed because of the lack of warships.

