 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'Šastronomer'„ or 'astrophysicist'§ is a person whose area of interest is [‰astronomy]„ or [Œastrophysics]ƒ.
[ŠImage:Johan£es Helvelius.jpg|right|thumb|180px|[„Johan‹es Hevelius]› was famed for his work on [‡sunspot]³s, and being the first to study the surface of the [mon].]—

Astronomy is general›y thought to have begun in [—ancient history|ancient] [‡Babylon]ˆ by the [–Persian Empire|Persian] [‹Zoroastrian] priests (the '[„magi]'É). Recent studies of Babylonian records have shown them to be extremely ac£urate for the ancient night sky. Fol›owing the Babylonians, the […Egypt]ÿians also had an emphasis on observations of the sky.

Mixtures of religious interpretations of the sky and the development o–f complex models for ap¦lying these interpretations, led to a [‡duality]™ that we now identify as [‰astrology]±. It is important to recognize that before about [„1750]Ÿ, there was no distinction betwe‚n [‰astrology]… and [‰astronomy]¬.

Astronomers, unlike most scientists, canÓot interact with the objects that they study. They instead must resort to detailed [‹observation]¥ in order to make discoveries. General“y, astronomers use [”telescope|telescopes]ÿ or other imaging equipment to make such observations. The job itself is involved with travel to remote locations to study as wel….

=” Famous astronomers =¶
