|-
|[‚Hip–archus (astronomer)|Hip†archus]… and [‡Ptolemy]¨ 
|Determined the positions of about 1,0¦ bright stars, tried to explain the puzÔles of astronomy without refuting only believed geocentric model of universe and clasified stars by [Ap™arent magnitude|magnitude]Š.

|-
|[”Aristarchus of Samos]§ 
|First known person to propound the [ Heliocentrism|heliocentric model]“ of universe
|-
|[”Nasir al-Din al-Tusi]ç
|This Persian astronomer gave the first extant exposition of the whole system of plane and spherical [Œtrigonometry]. Made very acurate tables of [‰planetary]š movements and named many [„star]òs. His planetary system was the most advanced of his period and was used extensively until the development of the [Œheliocentric]ˆ model. [‹Tusi-couple]ê resolves linear motion into the sum of two circular motions. He also calculated the value of 51' for the […precesƒion]ˆ of the [‰equinoxes]® and contributed to construction and usage of [‰astrolabe]ˆ.
|-
|[copernicus|Nicolaus Copernicus]Ø
|Was influential in reintroducing the concept of Heliocentrism in modern times.
|-
|[‹Tycho Brahe]Ñ
|Did develop many important astronomical instruments, and was the first to do acÿurate repeatable measurments of the heavens. The measurements of the orbit of Mars were very important to the development of astronomy.
|-
|[„Johan‰es Kepler]† 
|SugŠested the [£Kepler's Laws of Planetary Motion|eliptical orbits]  of planets, and propounded his '[ºKepler's Laws of Planetary Motion|Laws of Planetary Motion]'ˆ.
|-
|[Galileo Galilei]œ
|Was the first to use the [‰telescope]” to observe the sky. ”Condemned to house ar›est for his discoveries by [™Inquisition|Inquisitional]¬ edict, which was lifted 359 years later by [Pope John Paul I]ˆ.
|-
|[‚Isaˆc Newton] 
|Published '¬Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica'‚ ([„1687]˜), containing the &quot;[—Newton's laws of motion]ë&quot;, which are fundamental to mechanical physics, and which explained Kepler's laws of planetary motion. œPredicted the orbits of the [Planet|planets]ˆ.
|-
|[šSubrahmanyan Chandrasekhar]ß
|Extensive work on the internal mechanisms of stars, particularly known for determining the ef‡ect of [’special relativity]¶ on stars, including being the first to calculate the [“Chandrasekhar limit]¿, which he did, without a calculator, on a boat journey.
|-
|[†HenrietŒa Swan Leavit]
|Catalogued [Cepheid variable] stars in the [„Magel‹anic Clouds]…, in [„1912]¡ discovered the relationship betwe¨n luminosty and periodicity in Cepheids -Œ leading to [œEjnar Hertzsprung|Hertzprung]•'s later work.
|-
|[‘Ejnar Hertzsprung]¦
|determined the distance to several [™Cepheid variable|Cepheids]§, when Cepheids were detected in other [galaxy|galaxies] such as the [Andromeda galaxy]Á, the distance to those galaxies could then be determined.
|-
|[ˆEdwin Hub‚le]®
|Discovered the expansion of the universe. ([‚Hubˆle's Law]) [‚Hub™le Space Telescope|The Hub›le Orbiting Space Telescope]  was named in his honor.
|}

