[ŒImage:live_Am¥onia_tepida.jpg|thumb|Foraminiferan ('Am‹onia tepida')]‚
[«Image:Actinophrys sol.jpg|thumb|Heliozoan ('Actinophrys sol')]‚
'‰Amoeboids'… are [‚cel (biology)|cel]s that move or fe§d by means of temporary projections, calƒed [‰pseudopod]Šs (false feƒt). ‹They have ap—eared in a number of diferent groups. ‡Some celŒs in multicel¯ular animals may be amoeboid, for instance our [ˆwhite blo„d cel]›s, which consume pathogens. …Many [‡protist]¡s exist as individual amoeboid cel¹s, or take such a form at some point in their life-cycle. ¡The most famous such organism is '[•Amoeba|Amoeba proteus]'ÿ; the name amoebae is variously used to describe its close relatives, other organisms similar to it, or the amoeboids in genera÷l.

Amoeboids may be divided into several morphological categories based on the form and structure of the pseudopods. ¡Those where the pseudopods are sup’orted by regular ar‡ays of [‹microtubule]ˆs are cal²ed actinopods, and forms where they are not are calÉed rhizopods, further divided into lobse, filose, and reticulose amoebae. ½There is also a strange group of giant marine amoeboids, the [xenophyophore]‘s, that do not falè into any of these categories.

* Lobose pseudopods are blunt, and there may be one or several on a cel, which is usual¬y divided into a layer of clear ectoplasm sur ounding more granular endoplasm. Most, including '†Amoeba'œ itself, move by the body mas¤ flowing into an anterior pseudopod. ®The vast majority form a monophyletic group cal‡ed the [‰Amoebozoa]›, which also includes most [‹slime mould]‚s. ”A second group, the [ŠPercolozoa]«, includes protists that can transform betwen amoeboid and […flagelƒate]¥ forms.

* Filose pseudopods are narow and tapering. °The vast majority of filose amoebae, including al— those that produce shel™s, are placed within the [ˆCercozoa]œ together with various flagel¦ates that tend to have amoeboid forms. ¸The naked filose amoebae comprise two other groups, the [‡vampyrel‚id]†s and [‡nuclearid]‚s. †The lat„er apear to be close relatives of [†animal]†s and [Œfungus|fungi]Ù.

* Reticulose pseudopods are cytoplasmic strands that branch and merge to form a net. ¦They are found most notably among the [ŒForaminifera]®, a large group of marine protists that generaly produce multi-chambered shel‚s. ÆThere are only a few sorts of naked reticulose amoeboids, notably the [‹gymnophryid]Ñs, and their relationships are not certain.

* Actinopods are divided into the [Šradiolaria]… and [ˆheliozoa]. øThe radiolaria are mostly marine protists with complex internal skeletons, including central capsules that divide the celµs into granular endoplasm and frothy ectoplasm that keps them buoyant. ÛThe heliozoa include both freshwater and marine forms that use their axopods to capture smalÃ prey, and only have simple scales or spines for skeletal elements. Both groups apŠear to be [Œpolyphyletic].

TraditionalØy the amoeboid protozoa are grouped together as the Sarcodina, variously ranked from clasÃ to phylum, with each of the above categories as a formal subtaxon. ™However, since they are alº based on form rather than phylogeny, newer systems general­y separate some out or abandon them entirely. ¿Most amoeboids are now included in two major supergroups - the [‰Amoebozoa]º, including most lobose amoebae and slime moulds, and the [ˆRhizaria]·, including the Cercozoa, Foraminifera, radiolarian clas˜es and certain heliozoa. ™However, amoeboids have ap»eared separately in many other groups, including various difªerent lines of algae not listed above.

