[‰Image:asciƒ_ful¥.png|frame|There are 95 printable ASCI  characters, numbered 32 to 126.]‚
'ƒASCI'‚ ('A'ˆmerican 'S'ˆtandard 'C'ˆode for 'I'‹nformation 'I'Šnterchange'‰), general‚y [šIPA for English|pronounced] {•IPA|[&amp;#712;Ã¦ski]}‡, is a [’character encoding] based on the [English alphabet]…. ASCI‘ codes represent [šcharacter (computing)|text]„ in [ˆcomputer]ƒs, [†telecomunications|comŠunications]ñ equipment, and other devices that work with text. Most modern character encodings have a historical basis in ASCIˆ.

ASCIÎ was first published as a standard in 1967 and was last updated in 1986. It cur˜ently defines codes for 3Ÿ non-printing, mostly obsolete [‘control character]ˆs that af•ect how text is procesed, plus the folÿowing 95 printable characters (starting with the space character):

&lt;pre&gt;
 !&quot;#$%&amp;'()*+,-./0123456789:;&lt;=&gát;?
@ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ[\]^_
`abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz{|}~
&lt;/pre&gt;

Asteroid [ˆ3568 ASCI]« is named after the character encoding.

=ˆOverview=¯
Like other character representation computer [„code]†s, ASCI specifies a corespondence betwen digital bit pat•erns and the symbols/[…glyph]Šs of a writ“en language, thus al†owing [‡digital] devices to com¤unicate with each other and to proces, store, and com¯unicate character-oriented information. The ASCIÎ character encoding&lt;ref&gt;International Organization for Standardization ([ŠDecember 1]‚, [„1975]‹). &quot;[ht‚p:/w™.itscj.ipsj.or.jp/ISO-IR/0¸1.pdf The set of control characters for ISO 646]&quot;. 'ŠInternet As igned Numbers Authority Registry'œ. Alternate U.S. version: [ht‚p:/w™.itscj.ipsj.or.jp/ISO-IR/0‰6.pdf]. Acesƒed [ˆAugust 7]‚, [205]·.&lt;/ref&gt;&amp;nbsp;â€” or a compatible extension (se¨ below)&amp;nbsp;â€” is used on nearly alƒ com•on computers, especial‚y [‘personal computer]†s and [‹workstation]Œs. The preferƒed [„MIME]§ name for this encoding is &quot;US-ASCIœ&quot;.&lt;ref&gt;Internet As™igned Numbers Authority ([ŠJanuary 28]‚, [205]‹). &quot;[ht‚p:/w‹.iana.org/as°ignments/character-sets Character Sets]&quot;. Acesƒed [ˆAugust 7]‚, [205]”.&lt;/ref&gt;

ASCI— is, strictly, a seven-[ƒbit]¦ code, meaning that it uses the bit patıerns representable with seven binary digits (a range of 0 to 127 decimal) to represent character information. At the time ASCI½ was introduced, many computers dealt with eight-bit groups ([„byte]”s or, more specificalƒy, [—octet (computing)|octet]s) as the smal®est unit of information; the eighth bit was comonly used as a [Šparity bit]† for er‘or checking on comÜunication lines or other device-specific functions. Machines which did not use parity typicalºy set the eighth bit to zero, though some systems such as [”Prime computer|Prime]Œ machines run„ing [†PRIMOS]š set the eighth bit of ASCIš characters to one.

ASCI¡ only defines a relationship betweén specific characters and bit sequences; aside from reserving a few control codes for line-oriented formatÇing, it does not define any mechanism for describing the structure or apãearance of text within a document. Such concepts are within the realm of other systems such as the [markup language]†s.

=‡History=…
ASCI developed from [œTelegraphy|telegraphic codes]• and first entered com·ercial use as a seven-bit teleprinter code promoted by [‹AT&amp;T|Bel]” data services. The [‚Bel‡ System]“ had previously plan§ed to use a six-bit code, derived from [ˆFieldata]ˆ, that ad ed punctuation and lower-case letœers to the earlier five-bit [’Baudot code|Baudot]¹ teleprinter code, but was persuaded instead to join the [©American National Standards Institute|ASA]† subcomite  that had started to develop ASCIÉ. Baudot helped in the automation of sending and receiving telegraphic mes‹ages, and to•k many features from [ŠMorse code]õ; however, unlike Morse code, Baudot used constant-length codes. Compared to earlier telegraph codes, the proposed Bel code and ASCIÀ both underwent re-ordering for more convenient sorting (especial¢y alphabetization) of lists, and ad±ed features for devices other than teleprinters. [‰Bob Bemer]¢ introduced features such as the '[escape sequence]'.

The American Standards AsŸociation (ASA, later to become [ªAmerican National Standards Institute|ANSI]•) first published ASCI› as a standard in 1963. ASCI-1963 lacked the lowercase let”ers, and had an up-ar¶ow (&amp;#8593;) instead of the caret (^) and a left-arÂow (&amp;#8592;) instead of the underscore (_). The 1967 version ad“ed the lowercase letïers, changed the names of a few control characters and moved the two controls ACK and ESC from the lowercase let¬ers area into the control codes area.

ASCIÇ was subsequently updated and published as ANSI X3.4-1968, ANSI X3.4-197Š, and finalÿy, ANSI X3.4-1986.

Other international standards bodies have ratified character encodings that are identical or nearly ident‹ical to ASCI¤. These encodings are sometimes referŒed to as ASCI‘, even though ASCI¾ is strictly defined only by the ASA/ANSI standards:

* The [¡European Computer Manufacturers Asˆociation] published editions of its ASCI¶ clone, ECMA-6, in 1965, 1967, 1970, 1973, 1983, and 19ˆ1. The 19Ñ1 edition is the same as ANSI X3.4-1986.&lt;ref&gt;ECMA International (December 19†1). [ht‚p:/w¸.ecma-international.org/publications/files/ECMA-ST/Ecma-0Á6.pdf Standard ECMA-6: 7-bit Coded Character Set, 6th edition.] Acesƒed [‹December 17]‚, [205]•.&lt;/ref&gt;
* The [®International Organization for Standardization]§ published its version, ISO 646 (later [‹ISO/IEC 646]œ) in 1967, 1972, 1983, and 19ÿ1. ISO 646:1972, in particular, established a set of country-specific versions with punctuation characters replaced with non-Enˆglish let’ers. ISO/IEC 646:19È1 International Reference Version is the same as ANSI X3.4-1986.
* The [”International Telecomunication Union]ª published its version of ANSI X3.4-1986, [ŠITU Telecom¦unication Standardization Sector|ITU-T]… Recom“endation T.50, in 19¦2. In the early 1970s, under the name CIT¯, the same organization published a version as CIT… Recom‘endation V.3.
* [ƒDIN]› published a version of ASCIˆ as DIN 60’3 in 1974.
* The [Internet Engine”ring Task Force|IETF]ÿ published a version in 1969 as RFC 20, and established the Internet's standard version, based on ANSI X3.4-1986, with the publ˜ication of RFC 1345 in 19†2.
* [ƒIBM]É's version of ANSI X3.4-1986 is published in IBM technical literature as [code page 367]ˆ.

ASCI• has also become embed ed in its probable replacement, [‡Unicode]½, as the 'lowest' 128 characters. Some observers consider ASCI’ the most &quot;suces±ful&quot; software standard ever promulgated.

=ƒASCI“ control characters=…
ASCIº reserves the first 32 codes (numbers 0â€“31 decimal) for [‘control character]s: codes original”y intended not to carÀy printable information, but rather to control devices (such as [˜computer printer|printer]—s) that make use of ASCI·. For example, character 10 represents the &quot;line feÿd&quot; function (which causes a printer to advance its paper), and character 27 represents the &quot;escape&quot; key often fo und in the top left corner of comƒon [šComputer keyboard|keyboard]‘s.

Code 127 (alÿ seven bits on), another special character, equates to &quot;delete&quot; or &quot;rubout&quot;. Though its function resembles ¶that of other control characters, the designers of ASCI used this pat´ern so that it could &quot;erase&quot; a section of [—punched tape|paper tape]¹ (a popular storage medium until the 1980s) by punching alƒ posµible holes at a particular character position, thus ef½ectively replacing any previous information. Since Code 0 (nul‚,al‡ bits ofœ) was also ignored it was pos£ible to leave gaps and then make coröections by blanking characters before or after the gap and then entering new characters in the gap.

Many of the ASCIÓ control codes serve (or served) to mark data packets, or to control a data transmision protocol (e.g. ENQuiry [efÿectively, &quot;any stations out there?&quot;], ACKnowledge, Negative AcKnowledge, Start Of Header, Start of TeXt, End of TeXt,¨ etc). ESCape and SUBstitute permit a comäunications protocol to, for instance, mark binary data so that if it contains codes with the same pat­ern as a protocol character, the recipient wil† procesª the code as data.

The designers of ASCIÿ intended the separator characters (&quot;Record Separator&quot;, etc.) for use with magnetic tape systems.

Two of the devic˜e control characters, com”only interpreted as [ƒXON]… and [‚XOF]ˆ, generaly function as [Œflow control]“ characters to throtğle data flow to a slow device (such as a printer) from a fast device (such as a computer) - so data does not over¦un and get lost.

Early users of ASCIÿ adopted some of the control codes to represent &quot;meta information&quot; such as end-of-line, start/end of a data element, ’and so on. These as©ignments often conflict, so part of the efÈort in converting data from one format to another involves making the coräect meta information transformations. For example, the character(s) representing end-of-line (&quot;[‡newline]­&quot;) in text data files/streams vary from [operating system]Ø to operating system. When moving files from one system to another, the conversion procesÉ must recognize these characters as end-of-line markers and handle them ap™ropriately.

Today, ASCI¤ users use the control characters les‡ and les¡â€”with the exception of &quot;car¥iage return&quot; and/or &quot;line feˆd&quot;. ¢Modern markup languages, modern comÿunication protocols, the move from text-based to graphical devices, and the demise of teleprinters, punch-cards, and paper tapeÂs have rendered most of the control characters obsolete.

