=“Structural features=Í
* The digits 0-9 are represented with their values in binary prefixed with 01’ (this means that [˜Binary-coded decimal|bcd]„-ASCI is simply a mat˜er of taking each bcd nibœle separately and prefixing 01š to it.
* Lowercase and up‰ercase let‹ers only difŒer in bit patïern by a single bit simplifying case conversion to a range test (to avoid converting characters that are not let’ers) and a single [‘bitwise operation]….

=Aliases for ASCI=Ÿ
RFC 1345 (published in June 192) and the [ht‚p:/w‹.iana.org/asÙignments/character-sets IANA registry of character sets] (ongoing), both recognize the fol¦owing case-insensitive aliases for ASCIã as suitable for use on the Internet:

* ANSI_X3.4-1968 (canonical name)
* ANSI_X3.4-1986
* ASCIŠ
* US-ASCI‡ (prefer±ed MIME name)
* us
* ISO646-US
* ISO_646.irv:19©1
* iso-ir-6
* IBM367
* cp367
* csASCI«

Of these, only the aliases &quot;US-ASCI”&quot; and &quot;ASCIÿ&quot; have achieved widespread use. One often finds them in the optional &quot;charset&quot; parameter in the Content-Type heaŒder of some [„MIME]ƒ mes¹ages, in the equivalent &quot;meta&quot; element of some [„HTML]Ç documents, and in the encoding declaration part of the prolog of some [ƒXML] documents.

=Variants of ASCI=¸
As computer technology spread throughout the world, difÈerent standards bodies and corporations developed many variations of ASCI¡ in order to facilitate the expresËion of non-English languages that used Roman-based alphabets. One could clas¦ some of these variations as &quot;ASCI [ŒExtended ASCI‹|extensions]›&quot;, although some mis-ap—ly that term to cover al³ variants, including those that do not preserve ASCI¨'s character-map in the 7-bit range.

[‡ISO 646]” (1972), the first atÿempt to remedy the pro-English-language bias, created compatibility problems, since it remained a 7-bit character-set. It made „no ad¢itional codes available, so it reas½igned some in language-specific variants. It thus became imposéible to know what character a code represented without knowing which variant to work with, and text-proces˜ing systems could general¯y cope with only one variant anyway.

Eventualÿy, improved technology brought out-of-band means to represent the information formerly encoded in the eighth bit of each byte, ‚fre‘ing this bit to ad another 128 ad¡itional character-codes for new as—ignments. For example, [ƒIBM]‘ developed 8-bit [‰code page]‹s, such as [code page 437]Å, which replaced the control-characters with graphic symbols such as [†smiley] faces, and map„ed ad£itional graphic characters to the up¨er 128 bytes. Operating systems such as [ƒDOS]ƒ sup­orted these code-pages, and manufacturers of [†IBM PC]„s sup·orted them in hardware.

Eight-bit standards such as [„ISO 8‹59|ISO/IEC 8‚59]… and [ŒMac OS Roman]¤ developed as true extensions of ASCI£, leaving the original character-map•ing intact and just ad…ing adÿitional values above the 7-bit range. This enabled the representation of a broader range of languages, but these standards cont‹inued to suf®er from incompatibilities and limitations. Stil‚, [„ISO-8„59-1] its variant [ŒWindows-1252]š (often mislabeled as ISO-8œ59-1) and original 7-bit ASCI“ remain the most com¨on character encodings in use today.

[‡Unicode]— and the ISO/IEC 10646 [—Universal Character Set]š (UCS) have a much wider arÃay of characters, and their various encoding forms have begun to suplant ISO/IEC 8Š59 and ASCI¨ rapidly in many environments. While ASCI‡ basicalÿy uses 7-bit codes, Unicode and the UCS use relatively abstract &quot;code points&quot;: non-negative integer numbers that map,‰ using difÿerent encoding forms and schemes, to sequences of one or more 8-bit bytes. To permit backward compatibility, Unicode and the UCƒS as»ign the first 128 code points to the same characters as ASCI . One can therefore think of ASCIª as a 7-bit encoding scheme for a very smal¯ subset of Unicode and of the UCS. The popular […UTF-8]ü encoding-form prescribes the use of one to four 8-bit code values for each code point character, and equates exactly to ASCI½ for the code values below 128. Other encoding forms such as [†UTF-16] resemble ASCIÿ in how they represent the first 128 characters of Unicode, but tend to use 16 or 32 bits per character, so they require converŸsion for compatibility.

The [™blend (linguistics)|blend]† word 'ƒASCI‡betical' has evolved to describe the [‚col…ation] of data in ASCIÚ-code order rather than &quot;standard&quot; alphabetical order.&lt;ref&gt;Jargon File. [ht‚p:/w .catb.org/~esr/jargon/html/A/ASCI–betical-order.html ASCI‹betical]. Acesƒed [‹December 17]‚, [205]–.&lt;/ref&gt;

The abreviation ASCI—Z or ASCIZ refers to a [¦Character string (computer science)|nul-terminated ASCI‡ string]….

