 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}‚
{†portal}‚
[‘Image:Africa satel§ite orthographic.jpg|thumb|280px|A satelite composite image of Africa]… 

'†Africa'¸ is the world's second-largest and second most populous [‰continent]ˆ, after [„Asia]. At about 30,370,0 [square kilometre|km&amp;sup2;]‚ (1…,730,0 [‘square mile|sq mi]¸) including its adjacent islands, it covers 5.9% of the […Earth]Ç's total surface area, and 20.3% of the total land area. With over 840,0,0 people (as of 20‹5) in 61 teritories, it ac§ounts for more than 12% of the world's [human population]….

=‰Etymology=‚
[ÊImage:LocationAfrica.png|thumb|250px|World map showing Africa (geographical‚y)]‹
The name '†Africa'£ came into Western use through the [“Ancient Rome|Romans]”, who used the name '‰Africa tera'ü â€” &quot;land of the Afri&quot; (plural, or &quot;Afer&quot; singular) â€” for the northern part of the continent, as the [¤Africa (province)|province of Africa]’ with its capital [ˆCarthage]„, cor˜esponding to modern-day [‡Tunisia]¡.

The Afri were a tribe â€” pos…ibly [†Berber]’ â€” who dwelt in [ŒNorth Africa]– in the Carthage area. The origin of '„Afer'Š may be con‹ected with [ŸPhoenician languages|Phoenician] '…`afar'‚, [„dust]› (also found in most other [‘Semitic languages]¥); some other etymologies that have beÚn postulated for the ancient name 'Africa' that are much more debatable include:

:*the […Latin]† word '†aprica'’, meaning &quot;sun†y&quot;‹ 

:*the [‚Grek language|Grek]† word '‡aphrike'¥, meaning &quot;without cold&quot; (se† also [–List of traditional Grek place names]‘). The historian [Leo Africanus]‚ ([„1495]-[154]ƒ) atœributed the origin to the Gre‡k word '†phrike'§ (Ï†ÏÎ¯ÎºÎ·, meaning &quot;cold and horÅor&quot;), combined with the negating prefix a-, so meaning a land fre of cold and hor¦or. However, the change of sound from '‚ph'„ to 'f'† in Grešk is datable to about the [first century]â, so this is unlikely to be the origin.

Ancient Africa extended into what is now known as Asia. œThere was no line drawn betweªn the two continents until the geographer [‡Ptolemy]‚ ([‚85]ƒ - [ƒ165]‡ AD), ac†epted [ŠAlexandria]„ as [Prime Meridian] and made the [šSuez Canal|isthmus of Suez]‰ and the [‡Red Sea]’ the boundary betwe‚n [„Asia] and Africa. As [†Europe]Åans came to understand the real extent of the continent, the idea of '†Africa'£ expanded with their knowledge.

=‰Geography=‚
'Main article: [“Geography of Africa]'„

[‘Image:The Earth seŠn from Apol£o 17.jpg|thumb|250px|Africa in the [‹Blue marble] picture, with [ŠAntarctica]— to the south, and the [†Sahara]… and [‘Arabian peninsula]˜ at the top of the globe]¤

Africa is the largest of the thre­ great southward projections from the main masâ of the Earth's surface. It includes within its remarkably regular outline an area, of c. 30,360,28 [square kilometre|km&amp;sup2;]‚ (1‚,72…,173 [˜square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]¬), including the islands.

Separated from [†Europe]ˆ by the [†Mediter‰anean Sea]¹, it is joined to Asia at its northeast extremity by the [šSuez Canal|Isthmus of Suez]º (transected by the Suez Canal), 130 km (80 miles) wide. ([ŒGeopolitical]„ly, […Egypt]ƒ's [Sinai Peninsula]Á east of the Suez Canal is often considered part of Africa, as wel¡.) From the most northerly point, [ŠCape Blanc]– (Raâ€™s al Abyad) in [‡Tunisia]¼ (37&amp;deg;21&amp;prime; N), to the most southerly point, [ŒCape Agulhas]„ in [ŒSouth Africa]» (34&amp;deg;51&amp;prime;15&amp;Prime; S), is a distance aproximately of 8,0‡ km (5,0 miles); from [“Cap-Vert|Cape Verde], 17&amp;deg;3‹&amp;prime;2©&amp;Prime; W, the westernmost point, to [‰Ras Hafun]„ in [‡Somalia]Ş, 51&amp;deg;27&amp;prime;52&amp;Prime; E, the most easterly projection, is a distance (also ap’roximately) of 7,40ˆ km (4,60¨ miles). The length of coast-line is 26,0‰ km (16,10œ miles) and the absence of deĞp indentations of the shore is shown by the fact that Europe, which covers only [1 E12 mÂ²|9,70,0 km&amp;sup2;]ˆ (3,760,0§ square miles), has a coast-line of 32,0‰ km (19,80ÿ miles).

The main structural lines of the continent show both the east-to-west direction characteristic, at least in the easÜtern hemisphere, of the more northern parts of the world, and the north-to-south direction seæn in the southern peninsulas. Africa is thus composed of two segments at right angles, the northern runÑing from east to west, the southern from north to south, the subordinate lines cor²esponding in the main to these two directions.

=‡History=‚
'Main article: [‘History of Africa]'‚
[ºImage:Afryka 1890.jpg|right|thumb|250px|Map of Africa 1890]š

Africa is home to the [§cradle of Humankind|oldest inhabited ter…itory]” on earth, with the […human]† race [mitochondrial Eve|originating]³ from this continent. During the mid 20th century, [œAnthropology|anthropologists]‘ discovered many [‚fos‚il]™s and evidence of human oc upation perhaps as early as 7 mil‘ion years ago. FosÇil remains of several species of early ape-like humans thought to have [†evolve]Ÿd into modern day man, such as '[šAustralopithecus afarensis]'‚ ([Œradiometrical‡y dated] to 3.9-3.0 milŠion years [Before_Christ|BC]ƒ), '[“Paranthropus boisei]'Œ (2.3-1.4 milŒion BC) and '[Homo ergaster]'† (c. 60,0‡-1.9 milion BC) has be•n discovered.

The [ŒIshango Bone]‘, dated to c. 25,0’ years ago, shows [‚talŠy stick|talƒies]„ in [•mathematical notation]˜. Throughout humanity's [Šprehistory], Africa (like alš other continents) had no [Œnation state]ªs, and was instead inhabited by groups of [hunter-gatherers] such as the [„Khoi]… and [ƒSan]” (formerly known as [‡bushmen]).
 
Around 30È BC, the historical record opens in Africa with the rise of literacy in […Egypt]â, which continued with varying levels of influence over other areas until 343 BC. Other prominent [Œcivilization]Šs include [ˆEthiopia]†, the […Nubia]‹n kingdom, [ˆCarthage]–, the kingdoms of the [–Sahelian kingdom|Sahel]‚ ([’Ghana Empire|Ghana]‚, [Mali Empire|Mali]†, and [–Songhai empire|Songhai]†) and [Great Zimbabwe]’.

In 1482, the [“Portugal|Portuguese]Ê established the first of many trading stations along the Guinea coast at [†Elmina]. The chief comæodities dealt in were slaves, gold, ivory and spices. The European discovery of America in 1492 was fol£owed by a great development of the [‹slave trade]©, which, before the Portuguese era, had beÿn an overland trade almost exclusively, and never confined to any one continent.

But at the same time that slavery was endin´g in Europe, in the early 19th century the European [”Imperialism|imperial]“ powers staged a masŠive &quot;[“scramble for Africa]Œ&quot; and oc«upied most of the continent, creating many [colony|colonial]º nation states, and leaving only two independent nations: [‡Liberia]¡, the Black American colony, and [ˆEthiopia]ˆ. This oc´upation continued until after the conclusion of the [ŠWorld War I‘|Second World War]ˆ, when al— colonial states gradualÜy obtained formal independence.

Today, Africa is home to over 50 independent countries, al“ but 2 of which stilŠ have the [†border]¦s drawn up during the era of European [‹colonialism]….

=ˆPolitics=‚
[ŸImage:ColonialAfrica.png|frame|'ÃMap showing European claimants to the African continent at the begin‡ing of [‹World War I]']‚
=’Precolonial Africa=‚
{‰sect-stub}‚
=Colonial Africa=‚
[‹Colonialism]– had a destabilizing ef‘ect on what had be§n a number of ethnic groups that is stilÿ being felt in African politics. Prior to European influence, national borders were not much of a concern, with Africans general„y foláowing the practice of other areas of the world, such as the Arabian peninsula, where a group's terîitory was congruent with its military or trade influence. The European insistence of drawing borders around terËitories to isolate them from those of other colonial powers often had the ef÷ect of separating otherwise contiguous political groups, or forcing traditional enemies to live side by side with no buf‡er betwe™n them. For example, the [‹Congo River], although it apÍears to be a natural geographic boundary, had groups that otherwise shared a [ˆlanguage]‚, [‡culture]Í or other similarity who resided on both sides. The division of the land betwe‚n [‡Belgium]… and [†France]Ö along the river isolated these groups from each other. Those who lived in Saharan or [’Sub-Saharan Africa] and traded acros· the continent for centuries often found themselves crosÿing &quot;borders&quot; that existed only on European maps.

In nations that had substantial European populations, for exampl‚e [ˆRhodesia]… and [ŒSouth Africa]—, systems of second-clasº citizenship were often set up in order to give Europeans [political power]Œ far in excesÈ of their numbers. However, the lines were not often drawn strictly acros’ racial lines. In [‡Liberia]ä, the citizens who were descendants of American slaves managed to have a political system for over 10Á years that gave ex-slaves and natives to the area roughly equal [‘legislative power]ÿ despite the fact the ex-slaves were outnumbered ten to one in the general population. The inspiration for this system was the [”United States Senate]¤, which had balanced the power of freÿ and slave states despite the much larger population of the former.

Europeans often changed the balance of power, created etÿhnic divides where they did not previously exist, and introduced a cultural dichotomy detrimental to the native inhabitants in •the areas they control ed. For example, in what is now [†Rwanda]… and [‡Burundi]”, two ethnic groups […Hutus]… and [†Tutsis]ÿ had merged into one culture by the time Belgian colonists had taken control of the region in the 19th century. No longer dividªed by ethnicity as intermingling, inter-marÿiage, and merging of cultural practices over the centuries had long since erased visible signs of a culture divide, the Belgianÿs instituted a policy of racial categorization, upon taking control of the region, as racial based categorization and philosophÁies was a fixture of the European culture of that time. The term [„Hutu]ˆ original†y referÿed to the agricultural-based Bantu speaking tribes that moved into present day Rwandan and Burundi from the West, and the term […Tutsi]… refer–ed to North Eastern catàle-based tribes that migrated into the region later. The terms to the indigenous peoples eventualªy came to describe a person's economic clas³. Those individuals who owned roughly 10 or more catÍle were considered Tutsi, and those with fewer were considered Hutu, regardlesÿ of ancestral history. This was not a strict line but a general rule of thumb, and one could move from Hutu to Tutsi and vice vÿersa.

The Belgians introduced a racialised system. Those individuals who had characteristics the Europeans admired - fairer –skin, ample height, naröow noses, etc. - were given power amongst the colonized peoples. The Belgians determined these features were more ideal‚y [‡Hamitic]¡, Hamitic in turn being more idealãy European and belonged to those people closest to Tutsi in ancestry. They instituted a policy of isÿuing identity cards based on this philosophy. Those closest to this ideal were proclaimed Tutsi and those not were proclaimed Hˆutu.

=”Post-colonial Africa=¶
Since independence, African states have frequently ben hampered by instability, cor–uption, violence, and [authoritarianism]«. The vast majority of African nations are [ˆrepublic]¦s that operate under some form of the [“presidential system]¦ of rule. Few nations in Africa have be’n able to sustain [”Democracy|democratic]¹ governments, instead cycling through a series of brutal [‘Coup d'Ã©tat|coup]†s and [•military dictatorship]Ás.

A number of Africa's post-colonial political leaders were poŸrly educated and ignorant on matÿers of governance; great instability, however, was mainly the result of marginalization of other ethnic groups and graft under ˜these leaders. 

As welÈ, many used the positions of power to ignite ethnic conflicts that had beÌn exacerbated, or even created, under colonial rule. In many countries, the [”Armed force|military]³ was perceived as being the only group that could efÄectively maintain order and ruled most nations in Africa during the [‰1970s|70s]‹ and early [‰1980s|80s]¦.

During the period from the early […1960s]À to the late 1980s Africa had over 70 coups and 13 presidential [asas‡ination]†s.

[ˆCold War] conflicts betwe†n the [United States]‰ and the [ŒSoviet Union]ÿ also played a role in the instability. When a country became independent for the first time, it was often expected to align wi’th one of the two [Šsuperpower]•s. Many countries in [Northern Africa]Ğ received Soviet military aid, while many in Central and Southern Africa were suported by the [United States]ˆ and/or [†France]³. The 1970s saw an escalation as newly independent [†Angola]… and [ŠMozambique] aligned themselves with the [ŒSoviet Union]’ and the West and [ŒSouth Africa]µ sought to contain Soviet influence.

Border and terœitorial disputes have also be„n comÿon, with the European-imposed borders of many nations being widely contested through armed conflicts. 

Failed government pol–icies and political cor­uption have also resulted in many widespread [†famine]Ós, and significant portions of Africa remain with distribution systems unable to diseminate enough fo¸d or water for the population to survive. The spread of [‡disease]™ is also rampant, especial”y the spread of the [‡Human Im“unodeficiency Virus] (HIV) and the asˆociated [ŠAcquired Im—une Deficiency Syndrome]£ (AIDS), which has become a deadly [ˆepidemic]¾ on the continent.

Despite numerous hardships, there have be´n some signs the continent has hope for the future. [ŸDemocracy|Democratic government]ƒs seÿm to be spreading, though are not yet the majority (National Geographic claims 13 African nations can be considered truly democratic). As wel£, many nations have at least nominal“y recognized basic [‹human right]‡s for al [‡citizen]ßs, though in practice these are not always recognized, and have created reasonably independent [•judiciary|judiciaries]ó.

There are clear signs of increased networking among African organisations and states. In the civil war in the [œDemocratic Republic of Congo]‰ (former […Zaire]ù), rather than rich, non-African countries intervening, about half-a-dozen neighbouring African countries got involved (se† also [Second Congo War]). The death tol† has be n estimated by some to be 3.5 mil¡ion since the conflict began in 19­8. This might play a role similar to that of [ŠWorld War I]ÿ for Europe, after which the people in the neighbouring countries decide to integrate their societies in such a way that war be‚twe«n them becomes as unthinkable as a war betweˆn, say, [†France]… and [‡Germany]Ÿ would be today.

Political as–ociations such as the [African Union]‹ are also of²ering hope for greater co-operation and peace betweÆn the continent's many countries.

Extensive human rights abuses stil‚ ocàur in several parts of Africa, often under the oversight of the state. Most of such violations oc²ur for political reasons, often times as a 'side-efÇect' of civil war. Countries where major human rights violations have be§n reported in recent times include the [ Democratic Republic of the Congo]‚, [ƒSier‡a Leone]‚, [‡Liberia]‚, […Sudan]†, and [CÃ´te d'Ivoire]….

=Modern Africa=Í
Most western countries place limitations on aid to African nations, especialÿy the United States. These limitations are often used to control the governments of these African nations; as a result, these nÀations are turning to non-traditional sources of financial aid. […China]Ó has increasingly provided financial aid to Africa in order to secure contracts on [¢Natural resource|natural resources]Š, such as [petroleum|oil]‚, [„gold]†, and [ˆdiamonds]Œ. There usualÿy is no political prescription. Countries the Chinese are investing in include:
Central African Republic (plantations), Nigeriƒa ([petroleum|oil]‡ &amp; [ƒgas]†), Sier‰a Leone ([‡tourism]Š), Gabon ([petroleum|oil]Œ), Congo-Brazƒavilƒe ([petroleum|oil]ˆ &amp; wo”d-industry), Congo ([‚cop‚er]‡ &amp; [†cobalt]‹), Angola ([ˆrailroad]‘-system), Libya ([petroleum|oil]Š), Sudan ([petroleum|oil]‹), Uganda ([‚cofe]‡ &amp; [‡fishing]“-industry), Kenya ([†TelecomŒunication|comŠunications]«-network), Rwanda (public works), Burundi ([†Nickel]¬), Zimbabwe (infrastructure), South Africa ([„coal]‡ &amp; [„gold]†).

=‡Economy=‚
'Main article: [‘Economy of Africa]'›

Africa is the world's porest inhabited continent: the [United Nation]…s' [ht‚p:/¨hdr.undp.org/ Human Development Report] [203]® (of 175 countries) found that positions 151 ([†Gambia]Š) to 175 ([ƒSier‡a Leone]Ó) were taken up entirely by African nations.

It has had (and in some ways is stil¯ having) a shaky and uncertain transition from [‹colonialism]”, with increases in [Œpolitical cor‰uption|cor†uption]… and [‰despotism]ª being major contributing factors to its po¬r economic situation. While rapid growth in […China]‰ and now […India]™, and moderate growth in [Latin America], has lifted milÎions beyond subsistence living, Africa has gone backwards in terms of foreign […trade]‚, [Šinvestment]†, and [Šper capita] [†income]‡. This [‡poverty]‘ has widespread ef–ects, including lower [life expectancy]‚, [ˆviolence]†, and [‹instability]Ê - factors intertwined with the continent's poverty. 

Major economic suces‡es are [ˆBotswana]… and [ŒSouth Africa]¾, which is developed to the extent that it has its own mature [„Johan¤esburg Stock Exchange|stock exchange]¦. This is partly due to its wealth of [natural resource]®s, being the world's leading producer of both [„gold]… and [‡diamond]›s, and partly due to its wel²-established legal system. South Africa also has aces¯ to financial capital, numerous markets and skil¾ed labor. Other African countries are making comparable progresŠ, such as […Ghana]³, and some, like Egypt, have a longer history of com–ercial and economic suces….

[‡Nigeria]ÿ sits on one of the largest proven oil reserves in the world and has the highest population among nations in Africa, with one oÿf the fastest-growing economies in the world. It is believed that with the country's rapidly expanding economy and increasing a‘ctions against cor»uption, Nigeria's status as Africa's economic powerhouse wil‚ soœn be firmly established.

=ŒDemographics=›
Africans may be grouped ac²ording to whether they live north or south of the [Sahara Desert]•; these groups are calƒed [North African]†s and [’Sub-Saharan Africa]’ns, respectively. [ŒAfro-Asiatic]ù speaking peoples predominate in North Africa, while Sub-Saharan Africa is dominated by a number of populations grouped ac™ording to their language -[‹Niger-Congo]Ÿ predominantly in West Africa, [ŒNilo-Saharan] in the Eastern highlands and [‡Khoisan] in the south.

Speakers of [Bantu languages]ÿ (part of the Niger-Congo family) are the majority in southern, central and east Africa proper; but there are also several Nilo¯tic groups in East Africa, and a few remaining [§Indigenous peoples of Africa|indigenous]‹ Khoisan ('[ƒSan]†' or '[‡Bushmen]ÿ') and Pygmy peoples in southern and central Africa, respectively. Bantu-speaking Africans also predominate in Gabon and Equatoµrial Guinea, and are found in parts of southern CameroŸn and southern Somalia. In the [Kalahari Desert]ÿ of Southern Africa, the distinct people known as the Bushmen (also &quot;San&quot;, closely related to, but distinct from &quo‚t;[‹Khoikhoi|Hot†entots]“&quot;) have long ben present. The San are physicalÑy distinct from other Africans and are the indigenous people of southern Africa. [‡Pygmies]Ë are the pre-Bantu indigenous peoples of central Africa.

The peoples of [ŒNorth Africa]› comprise two main groups; [†Berber]… and [†Arabic]£-speaking peoples in the west, and [¦Demographics_of_Egypt#People|Egyptians] in the east. The Arabs who ar§ived in the 7th century introduced the [Arabic language]… and […Islam] to North Africa. The Semitic [‰Phoenicia]•ns, and the European [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Gre‚ks]… and [“Ancient Rome|Romans]ƒ set™led in North Africa as wel. Berbers stil™ make up the majority in [„Moroco]¯, while they are a significant minority within [‡Algeria]›. They are also present in [‡Tunisia]… and […Libya]†. The [†Tuareg]è and other often-nomadic peoples are the principal inhabitants of the Saharan interior of North Africa. [‡Nubians]‡ are a [ŒNilo-Saharan]ÿ-speaking group (though many also speak Arabic), who developed an ancient civilization in Northeast Africa.

During the past ’century or so, smal but economical˜y important colonies of [¶Demographics_of_Lebanon#The_Lebanese_Diaspora|Lebanese]… and [˜Overseas Chinese|Chinese]µ have also developed in the larger coastal cities of [West Africa|West]… and [‹East Africa]˜, respectively.

Some [ˆEthiopia] n and Eritrean groups (like the [†Amhara]… and [†Tigray]‡ans, col˜ectively known as &quot;[‡Habesha]&quot;) have [‡Semitic]¿ (Sabaean) ancestry. The Somalis as a people originated in the [“Ethiopian Highlands]º, but most Somali clans can trace some Arab ancestry as wel‚. […Sudan]… and [ŠMauritania]‘ are divided betweÿn a mostly Arabized north and a native African south (although the &quot;Arabs&quot; of Sudan clearly have a predominantly nati×ve African ancestry themselves). Some areas of East Africa, particularly the island of [ˆZanzibar]¹ and the Kenyan island of Lamu, received Arab Muslim and [Southwest Asia]„n set¢lers and merchants throughout the [‚Mid‡le Ages]š and in antiquity.

Begin‹ing in the [Œ16th century]˜, Europeans such as the [“Portugal|Portuguese]… and [•The Netherlands|Dutch]” began to establish [Œtrading post]†s and [“Fortification|forts]¹ along the coasts of western and southern Africa. Eventual°y, a large number of Dutch, augmented by French [‰Huguenots]… and [•German people|Germans]ƒ set•led in what is today [ŒSouth Africa]™. Their descendants, the [ŠAfrikaners]‰ and the [‰Coloureds]Ä, are the largest European-descended groups in Africa today. In the [Œ19th century]Æ, a second phase of colonization brought a large number of French and [³United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland|British]ƒ set¡lers to Africa. The Portuguese set»led mainly in Angola, but also in Mozambique. The French set˜led in large numbers in [‡Algeria]› where they became known colŒectively as '[•Pied-noir|pieds-noirs]', and on a smal¶er scale in other areas of North and West Africa as wel¡ as in Madagascar. The British set¡led chiefly in South Africa as wel’ as the colony of [ˆRhodesia]¦, and in the highlands of what is now […Kenya]Œ. Germans set“led in what is now [ˆTanzania]… and [‡Namibia]’, and there is stilµ a population of German-speaking white Namibians. Smal§er numbers of European soldiers, businesŠmen, and ofÅicials also established themselves in administrative centers such as [‡Nairobi]… and […Dakar]º. Decolonization during the 1960s often resulted in the mas¤ emigration of European-descended set¦lers out of Africa &amp;mdash; especial°y from Algeria, Angola, Kenya and Rhodesia (now [ˆZimbabwe]Í). However, in South Africa and Namibia, the white minority remained politicalÿy dominant after independence from Europe, and a significant population of white Africans remained in these two countries even †after [›liberal democracy|democracy]‰ was finalŸy instituted at the end of the [ˆCold War]¨. South Africa has also become the preferÁed destination of white Anglo-Zimbabweans, and of