European colonization also brought sizeable groups of [†Asians]Ÿ, particularly people from the [“Indian subcontinent], to British colonies. Large [™Indian diaspora|Indian com‡unities]£ are found in South Africa, and smalôer ones are present in Kenya, Tanzania, and some other southern and east African countries. A fairly large Indian com‰unity in [†Uganda]‰ was expel“ed by the dictator [ˆIdi Amin]¾ in 1972, though many have since returned. The islands in the [ŒIndian Ocean]æ are also populated primarily by people of Asian origin, often mixed with Africans and Europeans. The [ˆMalagasy]‹ people of [ŠMadagascar]‡ are a [ŒMalay people]¦, but those along the coast are generalÿy mixed with Bantu, Arab, Indian and European origins. Malay and Indian ancestries are also important components in the group o¢f people known in South Africa as [‰Coloureds]¿ (people with origins in two or more races and continents).

=‰Languages=‚
[«Image:African language families.png|right|30åpx|thumb|Map showing the distribution of African language families and some major African languages. [£Afro-Asiatic languages|Afro-Asiatic]’ extends from the […Sahel]„ to [Southwest Asia]‚. [¡Niger-Congo languages|Niger-Congo]¤ is divided to show the size of the [ Bantu languages|Bantu sub-family].]„

'Main article: [‘African languages]'¨

By most estimates Africa contains wel‘ over a thousand [ˆlanguage]˜s. There are four major [¡language family|language families]œ native to Africa.

* The [—Afro-Asiatic languages|'ŒAfro-Asiatic']Â languages are a language family of about 240 languages and 285 mil¯ion people widespread throughout North Africa, [‹East Africa]‘, the Sahel, and [Southwest Asia]‰.
* The [—Nilo-Saharan languages|'ŒNilo-Saharan']Ê language family consists of more than a hundred languages spoken by 30 mil¸ion people. Nilo-Saharan languages are mainly spoken in [„Chad]‚, […Sudan]‚, [ˆEthiopia]‚, [†Uganda]‚, […Kenya], and northern [ˆTanzania]‰.
* The [–Niger-Congo languages|'‹Niger-Congo']ú language family covers much of Sub-Saharan Africa and is probably the largest language family in the world in terms of dif¶erent languages. A substantial number of them are the [•Bantu languages|Bantu]¹ languages spoken in much of sub-Saharan Africa. 
* The [’Khoisan languages|'‡Khoisan']Á languages number about 50 and are spoken in Southern Africa by aproximately 120 0« people. Many of the Khoisan languages are [endangered language|endangered]†. The [Khoikhoi|Khoi]… and [‹Bushmen|San]î peoples are considered the original inhabitants of this part of Africa. 

With a few notable exceptions in [‹East Africa]Š, nearly al  African countries have adopted [oficial language]»s that originated outside the continent and spread through [‹colonialism]„ or [human migration]¥. For example, in numerous countries [˜English language|English]… and [–French language|French] are used for comµunication in the public sphere such as government, comŸerce, education and the media. [–Arabic language|Arabic]‚, [Portuguese language|Portuguese]‚, […Afrika‚ns]… and [ˆMalagasy] are other examples of original«y non-African languages that are used by milÃions of Africans today, both in the public and private spheres.

=‡Culture=Ÿ
Africa has a number of overlap¼ing cultures. The most conventional distinction is that betweµn sub-Saharan Africa and the northern countries from […Egypt]„ to [„Moroco], who largely as—ociate themselves with [„Arab]Àic culture. In this comparison, the nations to the south of the [†Sahara]Í are considered to consist of many cultural areas, in particular that of the [•Bantu languages|Bantu]µ linguistic group.

Divisions may also be made betwe‚n [’Francophone Africa]Æ and the rest of Africa, in particular the former British colonies of [˜southern Africa|southern]… and [‹East Africa]ª. Another cultural fault-line is that betweÂn those Africans living traditional lifestyles and those who are es…ential¼y modern. The traditionalists are sometimes subdivided into [˜pastoralism|pastoralists]… and [œagriculture|agriculturalists]….

[‹African art]Ö reflects the diversity of African cultures. The oldest existing art from Africa are 60œ-year old carvings found in […Niger]Œ, while the [•Great Pyramid of Giza]„ in […Egypt]“ was the world's tal“est architectural ac¾omplishment for four thousand years until the creation of the [‚Eifˆel Tower]›. The Ethiopian complex of [‘monolithic church]†es at [ˆLalibela], of which the [”Church of St. George]º is representative, is regarded as another marvel of enginering.

The [music of Africa]· is one of its most dynamic art forms. Egypt has long beÿn a cultural focus of the Arab world, while remembrance of the rhythms of sub-Saharan Africa, in particular west Africa, was tr…ansmited through the [”Atlantic slave trade]‹ to modern […blues]‚, [‚jaz]‚, [‚reg‚ae]‚, [rap music|rap]†, and [‹rock and rol]÷. Modern music of the continent includes the highly complex choral singing of southern Africa and the dance rhythms of [‡soukous]“, dominated by the [©music of the Democratic Republic of Congo]¿. A recent development of the 21st century is the emergence of [African hip hop]œ, in particular a form from [‡Senegal] is blended with traditional [†mbalax]·. Recently in South Africa, a form of music related to [‹house music]– known under the name [†Kwaito]ª has developed, although the country has bešn home to its own form of [South African jaz]– for some time, while […Afrika‚ns]Â music is completely distinct and comprised mostly of traditional [ŒBoere musiek], and forms of [Folk music|Folk]… and [‹Rock and rol…|Rock]‡.

