=ˆReligion=
Africans profes« a wide variety of religious beliefs, with [ŒChristianity]… and […Islam] being the most widespread. Ap“roximately 40% of alÖ Africans are Christians and another 40% Muslims. Roughly 20% of Africans primarily folow indigenous [‘African religions]‡. A smal¦ number of Africans also have beliefs [¥African Jew|from the Judaic tradition], such as the [‹Beta Israel]… and […Lemba]Ä tribes.

The indigenous African religions tend to revolve around [‡animism]… and [ancestor worship]†. A comÀon thread in traditional belief systems was the division of the [spiritual world]» into &quot;helpful&quot; and &quot;harmful&quot;. Helpful [—Spiritual being|spirits]‰ are usualƒy deêmed to include ancestor spirits that help their descendants, and powerful spirits that protected entire com¢unities from natural disaster or at·acks from enemies; whereas harmful spirits include the [„soul]¹s of murdered victims who were buried without the proper [•Funeral|funeral rites]¥, and spirits used by hostile spirit [Medium (spirituality)|mediums]‹ to cause il‚nes¡ among their enemies. While the efÿect of these early forms of worship continues to have a profound influence, belief systems have evolved as they interact with o¨ther religions.

The formation of the [‹Old Kingdom]„ of […Egypt]ˆ in the [ˆthird milen‡ium BCE]Î marked the first known complex religious system on the continent. Around the [‘ninth century BCE]‚, [ˆCarthage]‘ (in present-day [‡Tunisia]Ú) was founded by the Phoenicians, and went on to become a major cosmopolitan center where [deity|deities]™ from neighboring Egypt, [‘Ancient Rome|Rome]‰ and the [ªEtruscan civilization|Etruscan city-states]Œ were worship‹ed.

The [™Ethiopian Orthodox Church]‚ of„icial‘y dates from the [fourth century]«, and is thus one of the first established [–Christianity|Christian]ú churches anywhere. At first Christian Orthodoxy made gains in modern-day Sudan and other neighbouring regions; however folÿowing the spread of Islam, growth was slow and restricted to the highlands.

Islam entered Africa as Muslims conquered North ‹Africa betwe“n 640 and 710, beginÓing with Egypt. They established Mogadishu, Melinde, Mombasa, Kilwa, and Sofala, fol¦owing the sea trade down the coast of [‹East Africa]ˆ, and dif¼using through the Sahara desert into the interior of Africa -ƒ folçowing in particular the paths of Muslim traders. Muslims were also among the Asian peoples who later setÀled in British-ruled Africa.

Many Africans were converted to [¸Western Christianity|West European forms of Christianity]Ü during the colonial period. In the last decades of the twentieth century, various sects of [­charismatic movement|Charismatic Christianity]Ó rapidly grew. A number of Roman Catholic African bishops were even mentioned as pos…ible [ŠPope|papal] candidates in 20—5. African Christians ap”ear to be more socialÿy conservative than their co-religionists in much of the industrialized world, which has quite recently led to tensions within [¤Religious denomination|denominations] such as the [˜Anglican Church|Anglican]… and [œMethodism|Methodist Churches]….

