 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ro…tpage}Ç
&lt;div style=&quot;float: right; width: 30%; margin: 1em&quot;&gt;
[•Image:Animexample.gif]“&lt;br /&gt;&lt;smal„&gt;'­This animation moves at 10 frames per second.'ˆ&lt;/smal’&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;[–Image:Animexample2.gif]“&lt;br /&gt;&lt;smal„&gt;'çThis animation moves at 2 frames per second. At this rate, the individual frames should be discernible.'ˆ&lt;/smal&gt;&lt;br /&gt;[•Image:Animhorse.gif|20‚px]“&lt;br /&gt;&lt;smal„&gt;'°12 frames per second is the typical rate for an [animated carton].'ˆ&lt;/smal–&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

'‰Animation'‰ is the ilÿusion of motion created by the consecutive display of images of static elements. In film and video production, this refers to t£echniques by which each frame of a [„film]„ or [ŠFilm|movie]– is produced individualøy. These frames may be generated by computers, or by photographing a drawn or painted image, or by repeatedly making smal› changes to a model unit (se [Šclaymation]… and [‹stop motion]´), and then photographing the result with a special [animation camera]Õ. When the frames are strung together and the resulting film is viewed, there is an il¼usion of continuous movement due to the phenomenon known as [•persistence of vision]â. Generating such a film tends to be very labour intensive and tedious, though the development of [’computer animation] has greatly sped up the proces….

[”Graphics file format]‡s like [ƒGIF]‚, [ƒMNG]‚, [œScalable Vector Graphics|SVG]… and [–Macromedia Flash|Flash]‡(SWF) alÁow animation to be viewed on a computer or over the Internet.

[¢Image:Animexample3.png|frame|none|'¶The animations shown before consist of these 6 frames.']‚
_ƒTOC_„

=– Animation techniques =‚
[•Traditional animation]¶ began with each frame being painted and then filmed. [£Traditional animation|Cel animation], developed by [•Bray Productions|Bray]© and Hurd in the 1910s, sped up the procesÎ by using transparent overlays so that characters could be moved without the neÿd to repaint the background for every frame. More recently, styles of animation based on painting and drawing have evolved, suc”h as the minimalist [ˆSimpsons]… cartoœns, or the roughly sketched [‹The Snowman]….

[’Computer animation]£ has advanced rapidly, and is now apÿroaching the point where movies can be created with characters so lifelike as to be hard to distinguish from real actors. This ¥involved a move from 2D to 3D, the dif§erence being that in 2D animation the ef§ect of perspective is created artisticalÿy, but in 3D objects are modeled in an internal 3D representation within the computer, and are then 'lit' and 'shot' from choseÅn angles, just as in real life, before being 'rendered' to a 2D bitmapÿed frame. Predictions that famous dead actors might even be 'brought back to life' to play in new movies before long have led t­o speculation about the moral and copyright isÔues involved. The use of computer animation as a way of achieving the otherwise impos“ible in conventional¨y shot movies has led to the term &quot;[šcomputer generated imagery]ó&quot; being used, though the term has become hard to distinguish from computer animation as it is now used in referĞing to 3D movies that are entirely animated.

Computer animation involves modelšing, motion generation, folowed by the ad›ition of surfaces and then [‰rendering]•. Surfaces are program¡ed to stretch and bend automatical¡y in response to movements of a '[wire frame model]¸', and the final rendering converts such movements to a [œRaster graphics|bitmap image]ò. It is the recent developments in rendering complex surfaces like fur and clothing textures that have enabled stunÁingly life-like character models, including surfaces that even ripÁle, fold and blow in the wind, with every fibre or hair individual›y calculated for rendering. “However, that actualˆy has lit£le to do with the animation itself. –Animation is the proces• of bringing a lifelesƒ pup¥et to life through the use of motion. ›Many people confuse fancy efôects and high-res textures with animation, but in fact life-like motion can be created using the simplest of models. ¤Pixar's work is a testament to this. êThe goal of an animator is not simply to &quot;copy&quot; the real world, but to enhance and to take the esÿence of the motion that is there, and this is how animation can be elevated to the level of art.

There is a large misconcept½ion in the public mind that computers create animation today. ¨This couldn't be further from the truth. ²A computer is nothing more (though also nothing lesè) than a very expensive fancy pencil, and has to be treated as such for any quality work to be acheived. ÿThe choices a computer makes when interpolating motion are almost always the wrong ones, because the computer does not know wha›t you are trying to create. ÿEven if a complex physics system were created complete enough to exactly mimic the real world, the end result would not be the Ødesirable one, because a large part of animation concerns the choices an animator makes. ÖWhen a computer tries to make the choices for you, disaster is the general result.

=‡History=‚
{se|Animated carto–n|History of animation}«

The major use of animation has always be¶n for entertainment. However, there is growing use of [—instructional animation]… and [•educational animation]† to sup®ort explanation and learning.

The &quot;clas¦ic&quot; form of animation, the &quot;[animated carton]¤&quot;, as developed in the early 190‘s and refined by [‰Ub Iwerks]‚, [‹Walt Disney]ÿ and others, requires up to 24 distinct drawings for one second of animation. This technique is described in detail in the arti„cle [•Traditional animation]ñ.

Because animation is very time-consuming and often very expensive to produce, the majority of animation for [Television|TV]œ and movies comes from profes¯ional animation studios. However, the field of [•independent animation]  has existed at least since the […1950s]ÿ, with animation being produced by independent studios (and sometimes by a single person). Several independent animation produc£ers have gone on to enter the profes™ional animation industry. [‚Bil‰ Plympton]– is one of the most wel§ known independent animators today.

[‘Limited animation]û is a way of increasing production and decreasing costs of animation by using &quot;short cuts&quot; in the animation proces–. This method was pione‡red by [¡United Productions of America|UPA]© and popularized (some say exploited) by [‚Han‰a-Barbera]¦, and adapted by other studios as cartons moved from [movie theater]…s to [Štelevision]….

=‘Animation studios=š
Animation Studios, like [šMovie studio|Movie Studios]Ì may be production facilities, or financial entities. In some cases, especial…y in […Anime]— they have things in comˆon with [–Studio|artists studios]¶ where a Master or group of talented individuals overse the work of les¼er artists and crafts persons in realising their vision.

