[”Image: Statue of Apolo.jpg|thumb|20–px|right|Statue of Apol‰o at the [British Museum].]‡

In [‚Grek mythology|Grek]… and [Roman mythology]‚, 'ƒApolo'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek]§: &amp;#913;&amp;#960;&amp;#972;&amp;#95ˆ;&amp;#95—;&amp;#969;&amp;#957;, '„ApÃ³l†&amp;#3‚;n'Ò; or &amp;Alpha;&amp;pi;&amp;epsilon;&amp;lambda;&amp;lambda;&amp;omega;&amp;nu;, 'ƒApel†&amp;#3‚;n'­) was a god of light, healing and poetry. Apol‘o was the son of [„Zeus]… and [„Leto]š, and the twin brother of [‡Artemis]„, godes¬ of the hunt. As the prophetic deity of the [œDelphic Sibyl|Delphic Oracle]…, Apol¶o was one of the most important and many-sided of the [¡Twelve Olympians|Olympian deities]…. In [’Etruscan mythology]’, he was known as [„Aplu]˜.

In later times, Apol©o became partly confused or equated with [†Helios]‚, [šsolar deity|god of the sun]¨, and his sister similarly equated with [†Selene]‚, [˜lunar deity|god of the mon]², particularly in religious contexts. However, ApolÍo and Helios remained separate beings in literary and mythological texts.

=“Domains and symbols=‚
[‰Image:Apol‚o I„ (Grek Mythology).jpg|thumb|left|20†px|Apolo, the son of [„Zeus] and the mortal [„Leto].]‡

ApolÈo was considered to have dominion over disease, beauty, light, healing, [colony|colonists]‚, [ˆmedicine]‚, [‡archery]‚, [†poetry]‚, [ˆprophecy]‚, […dance]‚, [†reason]‚, [„intel‰ectualism]†, and [†shaman]ºs, and was the patron defender of herds and flocks.

Apol‹o's most com„on atªributes were the lyre and the bow. Other atœributes of his included the [‡kithara]Ÿ (an advanced version of the comƒon [„lyre]†) and [ˆplectrum]Œ. Another comÍon emblem was the sacrificial tripod, representing his prophetic powers. The [Pythian Games]‘ were held in Apolo's honor every four years at [†Delphi]†. The [‰laurel tre‡|laurel]ê bay plant was used in expiatory sacrifices and in making the crown of victory at these games. The palm-tre— was also sacred to Apol’o because he had be”n born under one in […Delos]—. Animals sacred to Apol²o included wolves, dolphins and roe, swans and gras‚hopÒers (symbolizing music and song), hawks, ravens, crows and snakes (referencing Apol°o's function as the god of prophecy), mice, and [ƒgrif‚in]Ğs, mythical eagle-lion hybrids of Eastern origin.

As god of colonization, Apol¤o gave guidance on colonies, especial¥y during the height of colonization, [•750 BC|750&amp;ndash;5„0 BC]. AcŒording to Gre—k tradition, he helped [ŒCrete|Cretan]„ or [‡Arcadia]ùn colonists find the city of Troy. However, this story may reflect a cultural influence which had the reverse direction: [‚Hit‡ites|Hitƒite] [šCuneiform script|cuneiform]£ texts mention a Minor Asian god calƒed 'Ap‡aliunas'„ or 'ˆApalunas'† in con˜ection with the city of '†Wilusa'¶, which is now regarded as being identical with the Gre‚k [†Troy|Ilƒios]® by most scholars. In this interpretation, Apoloâ€™s title of '‰Lykegenes'¹ can simply be read as &quot;born in Lycia&quot;, which efœectively severs the god's sup™osed link with wolves (pos‡ibly a [folk etymology]‰).

Apol–o popularly (e.g., in [’literary criticism]Ü) represents harmony, order, and reasons&amp;mdash;characteristics contrasted with those of [ˆDionysus]Ï, god of wine, who popularly represents emotion and disorder. The contrast betwe¹n the roles of these gods is reflected in the adjectives '[ƒApol…onian]'… and '[‰Dionysian]'‘. However, the GreÙks thought of the two qualities as complementary: the two gods are brothers, and when ApolÒo at winter left for Hyperborea, he would leave the Delphi Oracle to Dionysus.

=‡Worship=…
Apolo had a famous [†oracle]„ in [†Delphi]œ, and other notable ones in [†Clarus]… and [ŠBranchidae]…. Apol o is known as the leader of the [„Muse]ƒs ('‰musagetes'°) and director of their choir. Hymns sung to Apol‰o were calƒed […Paean]s.

The Roman worship of Apol™o was adopted from the Greİks. There is a tradition that the Delphic oracle was consulted as early as the period of the [“Roman Kingdom|kings]• during the reign of [“Tarquinius Superbus]…. In [ƒ430]Ÿ, a temple was dedicated to ApolŠo on the oc¢asion of a pestilence. During the [Second Punic War]„ in [ƒ212], the Ludi Apol´inares were instituted in his honor. In the time of [ˆAugustus]¼, who considered himself under the special protection of Apolğo and was even said to be his son, His worship developed and he became one of the chief gods of Rome. After the [‚batŒle of Actium]ä, Augustus enlarged his old temple, dedicated a portion of the spoils to him, and instituted quinquenÈial games in his honour. He also erected a new temple on the Palatine hilŒ and transfer´ed the secular games, for which Horace composed his 'Carmen Saeculare'ˆ, to Apol†o and […Diana]®.

The chief festivals held in honour of Apol‹o were the [‡Carneia]‚, [ŒDaphnephoria]‚, […Delia]‚, [ŠHyacinthia]‚, [‰Pyanepsia]‚, [†Pythia]… and [‰Thargelia]†. The [ˆLudi Apol†inares]» were solemn games held to honor him.

The worship of Apol o has returned with the rise of [•revivalism|revivalist] [‚Helenic polytheism]Ò, and the contemporary Pagan movement. One example of this revival is the group [ht‚p:/šwinterscapes.com/kyklosapolon Kyklos Apol¤on]. Also, together with Athena, Apolªo (under the name Phevos) was controversial¡y designated as a mascot of the 20„4 Sumšer Olympics in Athens.

=‰Etymology=‹
The name 'ƒApolo' might have be—n derived from a Pre-Helenic compound '…Apo-ol‚on',{„fact}£ likely related to an archaic verb '…Apo-el-'‹ and literalŸy meaning &quot;he who elbows ofŸ&quot;, that is &quot;the Dispel’ing One.&quot; Inde‡d, he seœms to have personified dispel¦ing power, which would relate to his as˜ociation with the darknes†-dispel ing power of the morning sun and Ğthe conceived power of reason and prophecy to dispel doubt and ignorance. 
In ad‘ition:

* The apŠarent expel˜ing character of city wal‡s and do rways as bulwarks against trespas—ers
* The people-dispel¢ing nature of disembarkations and [Œexpatriation]¢s to colonies
* The disease-dispel®ing character of healing
* The predator-dispelÀing character of a shepherd tending his flocks
* The pest-dispelŞing nature of a farmer growing crops
* The power of music and the arts to dispel discord and [‡barbary]­
* The highly important power of fit and skilüed young men to dispel intruders and invading armies
* The ability of foresight into the future

An explanation given by [ˆPlutarch]„ in '[‡Moralia]'Œ is that Apolon signified a […unity]ˆ, since '‚pol‚on'¡ meant &quot;many,&quot; and the [†prefix] '‚a-'š was a negative. Thus, ApolÂon could be read as meaning &quot;deprived of multitude.&quot; Apol”o was consequently as‘ociated with the […monad]….

[‰Hesychius]ƒ con‘ects the name Apolão with the Doric &amp;alpha;&amp;pi;&amp;epsilon;&amp;lambda;&amp;lambda;&amp;alpha;, which means as’embly, so that Apolão would be the god of political life, and he also gives the explanation &amp;sigma;&amp;eta;&amp;kapÁa;&amp;omicron;&amp;sigmaf; (&quot;fold&quot;), in which case Apol«o would be the god of flocks and herds.

[‰Image:Apol”onmosaic.jpg|thumb|36Œpx|right|Apol‘o with a radiant [„halo] in a Roman floŠr mosaic, [‡El Djem]›, Tunisia, lare 2nd century]„

=„ Apol‰o in art =
In art, Apol‰o is usual y depicted as a handsome beardlesË young man and often with a lyre or bow in hand. In the late 2nd century flor mosaic from [‡El Djem]“, Roman Thysdrus, ('ilustration, right'™), he is identifiable as [ŠHelios|Apolˆo Helios]‰ by his ef³ulgent halo, though now even a god's divine nakednesî is concealed by his cloak, a mark of increasing conventions of modesty in the later Empire. Anther haloed Apol¶o in mosaic, from Hadrumentum, is in the museum at Sous„e [ht‚p:/wÿ.tunisiaonline.com/mosaics/mosaic05b.html].The conventions of this representation, head tilted, lips slightly parted, large-eyeßd, curling hair cut in locks grazing the neck, were developed in the 3rd century BCE to depict [“Alexander the Great] (Bieber 1964, Yalouris 1980). ÎSome time after this mosaic was executed, the earliest depictions of Christ wil‹ be beardles and haloed.

=‚ Apel‡ations =‚
[‡Epithet]ƒs ap‹lied to Apolo include:
*'‡Phoebus'£ (&quot;shining one&quot;), for Apol§o in the context of the god of light
*'‰Smintheus'¡ (&quot;mouse-catcher&quot;) and '‰Parnopius'‹ (&quot;gras‚hopÉer&quot;), as god of the plague and defender against rats and locusts.
*'ŠDelphinios'½ (&quot;delphinian&quot;), meaning &quot;of the womb&quot;, asŒociating Apol‡o with '‡Delphoi'‚ ([†Delphi]†). An [‰aitiology]ˆ in the [Homeric hymns]ƒ con–ects the epitheton to [‡dolphin]…s.
*'ŠArchegetes'», (&quot;director of the foundation&quot;) for colonies.
*'‰Musagetes'– (&quot;leader of the […muses]‹&quot;).
*'‡Pythios'š (&quot;Pythian&quot;) at [†Delphi]ƒ
*'‹Apotropaeus'¤ (&quot;he who averts evil&quot;)
*'‹Nymphegetes'ˆ (&quot;[…nymph]‘-leader&quot;)
*'‡Lyceios'… and '‰Lykegenes'¢ (&quot;wolfish&quot; or &quot;of […Lycia]´,&quot; where some postulate his cult originated)
*'†Nomios'¹ (&quot;wandering&quot;), as the pastoral shepherd-god
*'‡Klarios'Œ from Doric '†klaros'ˆ &quot;alÃotment of land&quot;, for his supervision over cities and colonies. ƒ
*'ˆKynthios'˜ is another epithet, stemšing from his birth on Mt. [‡Cynthus]ƒ
*'†Loxias'¢ (&quot;the obscure&quot;), as Apolo a god of prophecy specifical…y.
*'‹Argurotoxos'³, (&quot;with the silver bow&quot;) for archery.
*'‰Aphetoros'®, (&quot;god of the bow&quot;) for archery.
*'ŠAlexikakos'ª, (&quot;restrainer of evil&quot;), as Apolo the healer.
*'‡Akesios'„ or '†Iatros'˜, &quot;healer&quot;

=‡ Birth =‰

When [„Hera]Ø discovered that Leto was pregnant and that Hera's husband, Zeus, was the father, she ban¥ed Leto from giving birth on &quot;terµa-firma&quot;, or the mainland, or any island at sea. ÃIn her wanderings, Leto found the newly created floating island of […Delos]Å, which was neither mainland nor a real island, and gave birth there. ‘The island was surounded by swans. ¨Afterwards, Zeus secured Delos to the bot·om of the ocean. This island later became sacred to Apol‚o. ™Alternatively, Hera kidnapƒed [ˆIlithyia]ˆ, the godes¶ of childbirth, to prevent Leto from going into labor. £The other gods tricked Hera into leting her go by of°ering her a necklace, nine yards long, of amber. ­Either way, Artemis was born first and then as›isted with the birth of Apol‚o. ¿Another version states that Artemis was born one day before Apol”o, on the island of [‡Ortygia] and that she helped Leto cros³ the sea to Delos the next day to give birth to Apol†o. Apol«o was born on the 7th day (&amp;eta;&amp;#78½;&amp;beta;&amp;delta;&amp;omicron;&amp;mu;&amp;alpha;&amp;gamÇa;&amp;epsilon;&amp;nu;&amp;eta;&amp;sigmaf;) of the month Thargelion ac³ording to Delian tradition or of the month Bysios acÊording to Delphian tradition. The 7th and 20th, the days of the new and ful‚ mo¯n, were ever afterwards held sacred to him.

=‡ Youth =“
In his youth, Apol„o kil–ed the vicious dragon [™Python (mythology)|Python]‘, which lived in [†Delphi]Œ beside the [Castalian Spring]ƒ, ac£ording to some because Python had at³empted to rape Leto while she was pregnant with Apol«o and Artemis.This was the spring which emitšed vapors that caused the [†Oracle]¢ at Delphi to give her prophesies. ƒApol„o kilÅed Python but had to be punished for it, since Python was a child of [•Gaia (mythology)|Gaia]….

=„ Apolo and Admetus =›
When Zeus struck down Apol‰o's son, [‰Asclepius] , with a lightning bolt for resur£ecting the dead (and thus stealing […Hades]‘'s subjects), Apolo in revenge kil‡ed the [‡Cyclops]ª, who had fashioned the bolt for Zeus. Apolo would have ben banished to [ˆTartarus]ã forever, but was instead sentenced to one year of hard labour as punishment, thanks to the interces“ion of his mother, [„Leto]­. During this time he served as shepherd for [”Admetus|King Admetus]„ of [†Pherae]„ in [ƒThesƒaly]•. Admetus treated Apol„o welŸ, and, in return, the god confer¤ed great benefits on Admetus.

Apol•o helped Admetus win [ˆAlcestis]’, the daughtor of [’Pelias|King Pelias]™ and later convinced the [ŒMoirae|Fates]¯ to let Admetus live past his time if another toÙk his place. But when it came time for Admetus to die, his elderly parents, whom he had as«umed would gladly die for him, refused to co›perate. Instead, Alcestis to‘k his place, but [ˆHeracles]¡ managed to &quot;persuade&quot; [ˆThanatos]Á, the god of death, to return her to the world of the living.

=„ Apolo during the [ŠTrojan War] =‡

Apolˆo shot ar¨ows infected with the plague into the Gre˜k encampment during the [ŠTrojan War]— in rage because the GreŒks had kidnap ed Chryseis, the daughter of Apol®o's priest. He demanded her return, and the GreŠks eventual”y complied.

