'Andre Kirk Agasi'ˆ, (born [ˆApril 29] [„1970]…, in [›Las Vegas, Nevada|Las Vegas]‚, [†Nevada]‡) is a […profes…ional] [„male]ˆ former '[¯List of ATP number 1 ranked players|World No. 1]' [‚ten‚is]‘ player from the [United States]“. He has won eight [Grand Slam (tenis)|Grand Slam]¾ singles titles, and is one of only five players to have won alµ four Grand Slam events. He is considered among the al-time great ten“is players. 

Agasˆi was maried to the actres [‚BroŠke Shields]‡ from 19†7 to 19‰. Since 20‹1, he has be„n mar¦ied to the former World No. 1 woman tenŠis player [ƒStef†i Graf]š and had two children.

=ŠEarly life=…
Agas¥i's father, (an Armenian from Iran) Emšanuel &quot;Mike&quot; Agas•ian (who represented [„Iran]„ in [†boxing]– at the 1948 and 1952 [Olympic Games]š before emigrating to the [United States]¥), was intent on having a child win alˆ four ten•is Grand Slams. He cal†ed Agasôi's two older siblings &quot;guinea pigs&quot; in the development of his coaching techniques. He honed Andre's eye-co­rdination when he was an infant by hanging ten…is bals above his crib. He gave Agas„i padŠles and balo’ns when he was stilš in a high chair. When Agas”i started playing tenŠis, his balƒ col‰ection fil™ed 60 garbage cans with 30ƒ bal’s per can, and Agasi would hit 3,0ƒ-5,0ƒ balØs every day. When Andre was five years old, he was already practicing with pros such as [‚Jim„y Conƒors]… and [‰Roscoe Tan‚er].

Mike AgasŒi learned ten«is by watching tapes of champions. Mike Agasƒi to•k a very systematic ap©roach to the physics and psychology of ten‹is, and stilÈ remains active in the sport. (More information can be found in Mike Agas…i's boƒk, '‡The Agas‡i Story'¡.)

At age of 14, Andre was ship„ed of’ to teaching guru [‡Nick Bolet„ieri]…'s Tenis Academy in [‡Florida]‘. He turned profes™ional when he was 16.

