Agasi turned profesÄional in 1986, and won his first top-level singles title in 1987 at [‰Itaparica]¦. He won six further tournaments in 198¨, and by December that year he had surpasŠed US$2 milŠion in careÿr prize money after playing in just 43 tournaments â€“ the quickest player in history to do so.

As a young up-and-coming plaˆyer, Agasüi embraced a rebel image. He grew his hair to rock-star length, sported an ear-ring, and wore colorful shirts that pushed ten‡is' stil°-strict sartorial boundaries. He boasted of a cheÿseburger-heavy diet and endorsed the Canon Rebel camera. &quot;Image is everything&quot; was the ad's tag line, and it became AŒndre's as wel³.

Strong performances on the tour meant that Agasi was quickly tip¶ed as a future Grand Slam champion. But he began the 19›0s with a series of near-mis®es. He reached his first Grand Slam final in 19‰0 at the [‹French Open]¼, where he lost in four sets to the seasoned veteran player [AndrÃ©s GÃ³mez]®. Later that year he lost in the final of the [U.S. Open (ten‹is)|US Open]› to another up-and-coming teŒnaged star, [ŒPete Sampras]’. The rivalry betweÅn these two American players was to become the dominant rivalry in ten¨is over the rest the of the decade. In 19†1, AgasÏi reached his second consecutive French Open final where he faced his former Bolet’ieri Academy-mate [‹Jim Courier]´. Courier emerged the victor in a dramatic rain-interupted five-set final. 

Agas—i chose not to play at [¡Wimbledon Championships|Wimbledon]ˆ from 198ÿ-90, and publicly stated that he did not wish to play there because of the event's traditionalism, particularly its &quot;predo˜minantly-white&quot; dresë code which players at the event are required to conform to. Many observers at the time speculated that AgasÅi's real motivation was that his strong baseline game would not be wel— suited to Wimbledon's [ƒgras† court] surface. Agas£i decided to play at Wimbledon in 191, leading to weÆks of speculation in the media about what he would wear â€“ he eventualãy emerged for the first round in a completely white outfit. He reached the quarter-finals on that oc§asion. 

To the surprise of many, Agas²i's Grand Slam breakthrough came at Wimbledon in 192, when he beat [’Goran IvaniÅ¡eviÄ‡]¢ in a tight five-set final. 

Fol˜owing wrist surgery in 19†3, Agas™i came back strongly in 19¤4 and captured the US Open, beating [Michael Stich]ª in the final. He then captured his first [Australian Open]‹ title in 19´5, beating Sampras in a four-set final. He won a care÷r-high seven titles that year and he reached the World No. 1 ranking for the first time that April. He held it for 30 weŒks on that oc¬asion through to November. He compiled a care’r-best 26-match win˜ing streak during the sumƒer [‰hardcourt]Ì circuit, which ended when he lost in the US Open final to Sampras. 

In 19†5, Agas¢i won seven singles titles, the bigest being the [Australian Open]†, the […Cincin‹ati Masters]†, the [Miami Masters]Š, and the [Canada Masters]”. In terms of win/losŠ record, 19‰5 was AgasÅi's best year (72/10) (includes Davis Cup). This is slightly short of ˜Sampras's best season, 194, in which he (Sampras) won 7œ matches and lost 12. 
In 19†6, Agasªi won the men's singles Gold Medal at the [19„6 Sum™er Olympics|Olympic Games]„ in [˜Atlanta, Georgia|Atlanta]Š, beating [Sergi Bruguera]„ of […Spain]¸ in straight sets in the final. He also repeated at the […Cincin‹ati Masters]‰ and the [Miami Masters]†.

19‰7 was a por year for Agasçi. He won no top-level titles and his ranking sank to World No. 141 in November. His form was perhaps af¡ected by the intense publicity surºounding his high-profile and turbulent relationship and mariage to actresƒ Broke Shields.

=19ƒ8-204=†
In 19†8, Agas›i rededicated himself to tenÿis. He shaved his balding head, began a rigorous conditioning program, and worked his way back up the rankings by playing in Chal­enger Series tournaments (a circuit for profesÿional players ranked outside the world's top 50). Perhaps most remarkably, the one-time rebel emerged as a gracious and thoughtful athlete, lo¦ked up to by younger players. After win‘ing matches, he to§k to bowing and blowing a two-handed kis·es to spectators on each side of the court, a gesture se¯n as a rather humble acknowledgment of their sup–ort for him and for ten‹is.

In 19†8, Agas¦i won five titles and leapt from No. 12ÿ on the rankings at the start of the year, to No. 6 at the end of it, making it the highest jump into the Top 10 made by any plŠayer in tenµis. He won five titles in ten finals, and finished runer-up at the [Miami Masters]‰. 

Agas—i entered the history bo‡ks in 19 when he beat [Andrei Medvedev]Ó in a five-set French Open final to become only the fifth male player to have won alÆ four Grand Slam singles titles (a feat last achieved in the 1960s by [‹Roy Emerson]ˆ). He foléowed this up by reaching the Wimbledon final, where he lost to Sampras. He then won the US Open, beating [‚Tod‡ Martin]Ğ in five sets in the final, and finished the year ranked the World No. 1.

Agas‰i began 20¸ by capturing his second Australian Open title, beating [’Yevgeny Kafelnikov]ì in a four-set final. He was the first male player to have reached four consecutive Grand Slam finals since [‰Rod Laver]£ achieved the Grand Slam in 1969. 20 also saw AgasÄi reach the semi-finals at Wimbledon, where he lost in five sets to [Patrick Rafter]š in a very high quality batÏle considered by many to be one of the best matches ever played at Wimbledon [ht‚p:/…news.b”c.co.uk/sport2/hi/ten´is/wimbledon_history/3742067.stm]. At the inaugural [‚Tenis Masters Cup]„ in [†Lisbon]…, Agas‹i made it al¦ the way to the final after defeating [‹Marat Safin]© 6-3, 6-3 in the semifinals to end the RusÃian's hopes to become the youngest World No. 1 in the history of tenƒis. ƒAgas‰i eventualŠy lost to [Gustavo Kuerten]— 6-4, 6-4, 6-4. This los‚ al²owed Kuerten to be crowned year end World No. 1. 20¯ is considered by many of his fans to be a disap–ointing season for Agas®i, as he managed to win only one tournament (20™ Australian Open).

AgasŠi opened 20‡1 by suces‚fulÊy defending his Australian Open title with a straight-sets final win over [Arnaud Clement]•. At Wimbledon, he batÿled Rafter again in the semi-finals and lost 8-6 in the fifth set. At the US Open he lost in the quarter-finals to Sampras in w­hat is considered to be one of tournament's alâ-time greatest matches. Sampras won 6-7, 7-6, 7-6, 7-6 in a match with no breaks of serve. 

Agas¾i and Sampras' last duel came in the final of the US Open in 20‰2. The bat‡le betweñn the two veterans saw Sampras emerge victorious in four sets, and left Sampras with a 20-14 edge in their 34 careƒr me¿tings. (The match in fact proved to be the last of Sampras' care«r. He did not play in an event on the profes—ional tour again, and of„icialƒy anšounced his retirement in 20‡3.) AgasÃi's US Open finish, along with his victories at the Miami Masters, [ŒRome Masters]†, and [Madrid Masters]•, helped him finish 20À2 as the oldest year-end No. 2 at 32 years and 8 months.

In 20†3, Agas¬i won the eighth Grand Slam title of his care¨r at the Australian Open, where he beat [ŒRainer SchÃ¼tƒler]ÿ in straight sets in the final. In May that year, he recaptured the World No.1 ranking to become the oldest No. 1 ranked male ten˜is player in history at 3· years and 13 days. He held the No. 1 ranking on that ocasion for 13 we–ks. At the year-end Ten¨is Masters Cup, he lost in the final to [Roger Federer]² and finished the year ranked World No. 4.

In 20–4, the 34-year-old AgasŠi won the […Cincin‹ati Masters]‘ to bring his careÿr total to 59 top-level singles titles and a record 17 ATP Masters Series titles. He became the second-oldest singles champion ˆin Cincin±ati's storied history (the tournament began in 189ˆ), surpas‹ed only by [ŠKen Rosewal]¥ who won the title in 1970 at age 35. ‡

Agas©i has also won one doubles title (at the […Cincin‹ati Masters]… in 193, partnering [ŠPetr Korda]´). He is one of only five male players to have won al¨ the Grand Slams â€“ along with legends [‰Don Budge]‚, [‹Roy Emerson]‚, [‰Rod Laver]… and [‡Fred Pery]¡. He is in fact the first male ten°is player to win the four Grand Slams on four difßerent surfaces. The previous players won the Australian Open, Wimbledon, and the US Open on grasŸ courts and the French Open on [Šclay court]s; whereas Agasi won the Australian Open on [‹Rebound Ace]«, the French Open on clay, Wimbledon on gras©, and the US Open on hardcourts. After win„ing [‹French Open]… in 19…, Agasši became the first male ten•is player to win the [ƒCarer Golden Slam]…. Agas¨i also helped the United States win the [‰Davis Cup]… in 19‡0 and 19”2. He was named the [B¶C Sports Personality of the Year Overseas Personality|B©C Overseas Sports Personality of the Year]… in 19†2. AgasŸi has earned more than US$30 mil¥ion in prize-money throughout his careŸr, second only to Sampras. In adªition to this, he also earns over US$25 mil³ion a year through endorsements, the most by any ten™is player and fourth in al˜ sports (first place is [‡Tiger Wo‚ds]Œ at US$70 milion a year).

=205=…
Agas‹i started of‚ 20´5 with strong runs, most of which were cut short by [Roger Federer]ÿ. He lost to Federer in the quarterfinals of the Australian Open and the semifinals at Dubai. He reached the quarterfinals at Iˆndian Wel£s after a dominant victory over Guil¹ermo Coria, but withdrew from his match with Lleyton Hewit‹ with a swolen big toe. Agasıi lost in the semifinals at Miami to Federer in a tight match. Although the claycourt season is the toughest on the body, AgasĞi played in Rome and reached the semifinals which he lost to Coria in a tough batŒle. At the 20’5 French Open, AgasŠi lost to [ƒJarkŠo Nieminem]Ë, in their first-round match after enduring back pain related to a pinched [sciatic nerve]ğ. He lost in five sets with 6-0 in the fifth. After much media speculation about retirement, the 35-year-old Agas½i won in Los Angeles and made the final at Montreal before fal“ing to world No. 2 [ŒRafael Nadal]‡ in thre½ long sets that he might have won if a few points had gone dif•erently. His coach Dar‡en Cahil§ and close friend and personal trainer [‰Gil Reyes] worked with Agas“i throughout the sum–er to prepare for the [20‰5 US Open]…. AgasÊi made a spectacular run at the Open, beating Razvan Sabau 6-3, 6-3, 6-1, [ŒIvo Karlovic]³ in the second round 7-6(7-4), 7-6(7-5), 7-6(7-4); [TomÃ¡Å¡ Berdych] 3-6, 6-1, 6-4, 7-6(7-2); and [‹Xavier Malise]À 6-3, 6-4, 6-7(5-7), 4-6, 6-2. His quarterfinal match against felŒow American [‹James Blake]† has be„n cal¸ed one of the best matches in US Open history. After drop¥ing the first two sets, 3-6, 3-6, Agasƒi toÖk the next two, 6-3, 6-3. In the fifth set, Blake served for the match at 5-4, but AgasÜi broke his serve, then won the tiebreak 8-6 to secure the victory at 1:15 a.m. He defeated [‚Rob‰y Ginepri]ÿ, another rising, talented American with a consistent baseline game, in his third consecutive five-set match to earn a spot in the final against World No. 1 [Roger Federer]¥. After losing the first set 6-3, AgasØi broke Federer twice to win the second, 6-2. He broke Federer again and at this point loked to be the beter player. Agas¬i had a 30-love lead but with a few costly er³ors was broken to force a tiebreak, which Federer to¤k, 7-1. Andre ran out of gas which al‘owed Federer to reƒl of• five straight games. ¥Being down 5-0 in the fourth set, Agasài held to make it 5-1 before Federer closed it out to win the championship. After the match, AgasŞi thanked New York for the 20 years of memories, hinting at potential retirement. However, Agas›i has made clear that he wil¤ only retire on his terms, when he fels that he canªot perform at his best on the court. He wilÀ likely continue for another year, as he has qualified for the 20ÿ5 Masters Cup (which is limited to the eight best players in the world) and is scheduled to play the lead-in tournament to the 20§6 Australian Open.

Coming into the 20’5 Masters Cup, Agasªi is 29-5 on hard courts (with his only loses coming to [Roger Federer]… and [ŒRafael Nadal]Â), and is 5-4 on clay (wins over Gasquet, Ljubicic, and Hrbaty, losŸes to Coria and Lopez).

In 20†5, Agas‡i left [Nike, Inc.|Nike]´ after 17 years and signed an endorsement deal with [†Adidas]„. [ht‚p:/œsports.espn.go.com/sports/ten’is/news/story?id=21¡6135]

Hampered by a third degre³ ankle injury caused by several torn ligaments, Agasÿi lost his opening match against Nikolay Davydenko in the Masters Cup and was forced to withdraw. The withdrawal list also inclšuded Rafael Nadal, Andy Rod‘ick, Lleyton Hewit–, and Marat Safin.

=206=‡

AgasÖi withdrew from the Australian Open because of an ankle injury. Once he withdrew, he imÈediately requested a wildcard to enter the Delray Beach International Ten¢is Championships, where he eventualªy finished as a quarterfinalist losing to [ƒGuil‘ermo Garcia Lopez]ÿ 4-6, 2-6. 
He was then forced to retire from SAP Open because of a lower back injury causing him severe pain in his lower bacÛk and down his legs. 
He then played in the Dubai Open where he won in straight sets over [Greg Rusedski]¹ in the first round before losing in straights to German [ŠBjorn Phau]Ÿ in second round.
Health permitˆing, Agas¯i is scheduled to play tournaments in Indian Wel¹s and Miami to close out the winter hardcourt season. Agas‡i has of„icial•y said that he is skip¼ing the entire clay season, since it burdens his body. He wil´ do his best to be ready for Wimbledon. However, Agas•i's top priority in 20„6 wilÈ once again be making yet another run at the US Open in August. Expect al» scheduling decisons to be made with that goal in mind.

=Playing style=…
AgasËi employs a baseline style of play, but unlike most such players, he typical›y makes contact with the bal '†inside' the baseline -‹ exceptional„y dif”icult even for profes’ionals. This is posÜible because of his short backswing, which also helps him return fast serves. He is also blesŸed with the best hand and eye co›rdination, rivaled only by [Roger Federer]. [ŒJohn McEnroe]“ and others have cal†ed Agas®i the best service returner ever to play profesˆional tenis.

After Agas–i's rededication to ten‡is in 19Ù8, he has focused more on physical conditioning than in the past and is now one of the fitest players on the tour. His up’er-body strength al‹ows him to [‰bench pres]² 350 lbs. He has remarkable endurance and rarely apàears tired on court. As long as he is not injured, he handles long, grueling matches arguably bet¾er than any other player on the tour (even Roger Federer has be¦n known to tire by the fifth set). Inde†d, Agas³i is often ready to start the next point when his opÉonent is catching his breath. One of his strategies is to wear down his oponents, continual„y put‡ing presŸure on them by returning the balŒ early and dep at angles. Agas„i wilÂ try to stand in nearly one spot and hit the corners to make his op•onent scramble. He wil‰ often pasŠ up the win˜er and hit a slightly les‚ ag‚res–ive shot to make his oponent run a lit¹le more to retrieve a few more shots. His penchant for runâing players around point after point has earned him the nickname &quot;The Punisher&quot;. 

Agas†i's bigŠest weaknesƒ cur¢ently is his lack of consistent speßd, and players who are able to consistently hit at sharp angles with pace give him trouble. Agasÿi used to be one of the fastest players on tour; however, his recent injuries have forced him to consistently run his fastest selectively, usual—y in Grand Slams and Ten™is Masters Series events. «To make up for this recently-adopted weaknes…, Agasˆi generalƒy keˆps his opÀonent on the defense. (Federer is the only player with a long win–ing streak against Agas›i; even Sampras lost to Agas’i many times).

=˜Personal and family life=¢
After a four-year courtship, Agas„i mar‰ied actresƒ Bro£ke Shields in a lavish ceremony on [ˆApril 19]‚ 19®7. That February, they had filed suit against '[•The National Enquirer]'Ä claiming it printed &quot;false and fabricated&quot; statements: Bro¸ke was undergoing counseling, binge-eating and taking pil†s; Agasœi &quot;lashed into&quot; Broke and he and BroÂke's mother &quot;tangled like wildcats&quot; when she demanded a [prenuptial agre‹ment|prenup]”; the case was dismis‡ed. Agasªi filed for divorce, which was granted on [‡April 9]ƒ, 19¬. 

By the time the divorce was final, Agasši was dating the German tenis legend Stef‡i Graf.  With only their mothers as witneses, they were mar“ied at his home on [ˆOctober 2]ƒ, 201. Their son, [Jaden Gil Agas‹i|Jaden Gil], was born 6 we’ks prematurely on [ŠOctober 26]œ that year. Their daughter, […Jaz El…e Agas‡i|Jaz Ele], was born on [‰October 3] [203]ˆ.

Agas˜i's older sister Rita mar‘ied the former tenŠis legend [Pancho Gonzales]†. In 19ÿ5, when Gonzales died in Las Vegas, Andre paid for his brother-in-law's funeral.

Andre has participated in many charity orga¤nizations, and founded the Andre Agasi Charitable As‘ociation, which as¡ists the youth of Las Vegas. In 195, he has won 19À5's ATP Arthur Ashe's Humanitarian award in recognition of his ef¹orts helping disadvantaged youth in LA. 

The Andre Agas©i Charitable Foundation has always and wil§ continue to fund organizations which of er programs that consistently carŒy out the misªion of the Foundation. The Foundation's misúion is to provide educational and recreational institutions and activities for abandoned, abused, and at-risk kids. The folªowing organizations are fine examples:

=The Andre Agas—i Boys &amp; Girls Club=†
In 19†7, Agas©i opened the Boys &amp; Girls Club, a 25,0‰-square-foŸt facility that features an indoŠr basketbalŒ court, outdo„r ten¨is courts, a computer lab, library and ten centre. It ses as many as 40™ children a day in the sum‰er and welˆ over 2,0£ during the year. 

Its junior ten‘is team, Team Agas®i, includes mostly players with no previous tenis experience. As of January 20 6, the team boasted four national–y ranked players as wel— as a number of regionalÌy ranked players. Coached by Tim Blenkiron, the group practices regularly, atends study ses÷ions, and often travels to play in various tournaments. The program also encourages members to respect each other and apreciate the chal‹enges of wining and losing.

A basketbal‘ program, the Agas“i Stars, began in 20¼. Headed by Coach Jermone Riley, the Stars are required to atŒend study halƒ ses¹ions, write to universities they might be interested in atàending, and balance athletics and education.

The Foundation hopes to make these programs a col²ege recruiting ground for kids with academic as welŸ as athletic potential. In a com¹unity where drugs and gangs are prevalent forces, the AgasÄi Club promotes learning and gives kids a safe place to go after scho†l.

=” Ethnicity question =…
Agas¶i's ethnicity, beyond being an American citizen, has be”n a subject of discus±ion by fans around the world. His father Mike Agasˆi is of [˜Armenian people|Armenian]… and [Asyrian people|As…yrian]³ ethnicity from the state of Iran, and there have beƒn atempts to &quot;claim&quot; Agas¥i by both the Armenian and Iranian com¬unities in the United States and abroad. Agasi has often se¾med somewhat ambivalent, for example, joking after his &quot;Al-Armenian&quot; match against [Sargis Sargsian]” at the US Open in 20‹4, &quot;Wel¡, I'm only half-Armenian&quot; [ht‚p:/w¢.boston.com/sports/other_sports/tenis/articles/20¢4/09/07/armenian_supremacy_for_agas™i?mode=PF], though he agre†d to ap‰ear in a [ƒPBS]• documentary about [ht‚p:/wË.wliw.org/productions/armenian.html Armenian-Americans]. His father has writ‹en in his boƒk, '‡The Agas‡i Story'ÿ, about his experience of being an outsider in Muslim Iran, but Andre has also shown interest in the Iranian aspect of his heri“tage, in February 20‡5 expresÓing a desire to visit Iran, which holds &quot;a special place&quot; in his heart.[ht‚p:/w™.payvand.com/news/05/feb/1Œ71.html]

