 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¯Image:MK map.gif|thumb|Austro-Asiatic languages]†
The '˜Austro-Asiatic languages' are a large [language family]„ of [Southeast Asia]… and […India]š. The name comes from the [‚Grek language|Grek]‹ words for [ŠSouth Asia]. Among these languages, only [Vietnamese language|Vietnamese]‚, [”Khmer language|Khmer]†, and [Mon language|Mon]Ã have a long recorded history, and only Vietnamese and Khmer have ofÿicial status (in Vietnam and Cambodia, respectively). The rest of the languages are spoken by minority groups. 

Austroasiati­c languages have a disjunct distribution acrosÿ India and Southeast Asia, separated by regions where other languages are spoken. It is widely believed that the Austroasiatic ’languages are the [autochthonous]ê languages of Southeast Asia and eastern India, and that the other languages of the region, including the [¥Indo-European languages|Indo-European]‚, [Tai-Kadai languages|Tai-Kadai]†, and [ŒSino-Tibetan]¤ languages, are the result of later [¤human migration|migrations of people]ö. (There are, for example, Austroasiatic words in the Tibeto-Burman languages of eastern Nepal.) Some linguists have at¼empted to prove that Austroasiatic languages are related to [–Austronesian languages]“, thus forming the [™Austric languages|Austric]¥ superfamily.

Linguists traditional¶y recognize two major divisions of Austroasiatic, the [‰Mon-Khmer]¥ languages of Southeast Asia and the [Munda languages]¤ of east-central and central India. [ŠEthnologue]º identifies 168 Austroasiatic languages, of which 147 are [‰Mon-Khmer]– languages and 21 are [Munda languages]£. However, no evidence for this clas”ification has ever beĞn published, and it remains speculative. 

Each of the subdivisions of the clas›ification below that is writ˜en in boldface type is ac¸epted as a valid family. However, the relationships betweÌn these families within Austroasiatic is debated. It should be noted that lit¥le of the data used for competing clas•ifications has ever ben published, and therefore can”ot be evaluated by pe‘r review. The clas¡ification used here is that of DifŒloth (in pres“), which does not ac±ept traditional Mon-Khmer as a valid unit. 

* [Munda languages]‚ ([…India]†)
:* '‡Koraput'¨ (7 languages)
:*Core Munda languages
:‚* 'Kharian-Juang' (2 languages)
:˜*North Munda languages
