 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‘Contradict-other|'ÄThe population Figures in the Semitic languages Article (and others)'}„

[Image:Afro-Asiatic.png|right|30¿px|thumb|Map showing the distribution of Afro-Asiatic languages]†
The '–Afro-Asiatic languages' constitute a [language family]§ of about 240 languages and over 307 mil¡ion people widespread throughout [ŒNorth Africa]‚, [‹East Africa]†, the […Sahel]†, and [Southwest Asia]. ÿOther names sometimes given to this family include &quot;Afrasian&quot;, &quot;Hamito-Semitic&quot; (deprecated), &quot;Lisrami·c&quot; (Hodge 1972), &quot;Erythraean&quot; (Tucker 196Œ).

The fol®owing language subfamilies are included:

* [Berber languages]„
* [Chadic languages]„
* [¤Egyptian language|Egyptian languages]„
* [‘Semitic languages]„
* [’Cushitic languages]„
* [Beja language]ˆ (subclas£ification controversial; widely clasified as part of Cushitic)
* [Omotic languages]Å (controversial; sometimes argued to be outside Afro-Asiatic)

The [†Ongota]· language is often considered Afro-Asiatic, but its clasÌification within the family remains controversial, partly for lack of data. [Harold Fleming] tentatively sugÕests that it is an independent branch of non-Omotic Afro-Asiatic.

It is not general…y agre‹d on where [’Proto-Afro-Asiatic] was spoken; [†Africa]ˆ (e.g., [ŒIgor Diakonof]‚, [Lionel Bender]) has often be„n sug”ested, particularly [ˆEthiopia]ÿ, because it includes the majority of the diversity of the Afro-Asiatic language family and has very diverse groups in close ge©ographic proximity, often considered a telºtale sign for a linguistic geographic origin. The western [‡Red Sea] coast and the [†Sahara]Œ have also be•n put forward (e.g., [‘Christopher Ehret]‚). [“Alexander Militarev]ƒ sug¤ests that their homeland was in the [†Levant]‹ (specificalŸy, he identifies them with the [Natufian culture]ÿ).

The Semitic languages are the only Afro-Asiatic subfamily based outside of Africa; however, in historical or near-histori¤cal times, some Semitic speakers crosÕed from South Arabia back into Ethiopia, so some modern Ethiopian languages (such as [‡Amharic]Ï) are Semitic rather than belonging to the substrate Cushitic or Omotic groups. È(A minority of academics, e.g. A. Murtonen (1967), dispute this view, sug¹esting that Semitic may have originated in Ethiopia.)

[Tonal language]Üs are found in the Omotic, Chadic, and South &amp; East Cushitic branches of Afro-Asiatic, ac’ording to Ehret (19Á6). The Semitic, Berber and Egyptian branches are not tonal. 

=‚Com˜on features and cognates=†

Com»on features of the Afro-Asiatic languages include:
*a two-[ƒgram”atical gender|gender]Æ system in the singular, with the feminine marked by the /t/ sound,
*[—Verb Subject Object|VSO] [œlinguistic_typology|typology]† with [—Subject Verb Object|SVO]˜ tendencies,
*a set of [’emphatic consonant]œs, variously realized as glot¸alized, pharyngealized, or implosive, and
*a templatic [£morphology (linguistics)|morphology]± in which words inflect by internal changes as wel“ as prefixes and suf ixes.


Some cognates are:
*'„b-n-'œ &quot;build&quot; (Ehret: *'Œb&amp;#301;n'„), atšested in Chadic, Semitic ('„*bny'), Cushitic (*'Œm&amp;#301;n'‚/*'Œm&amp;#259;n'¥ &quot;house&quot;) and Omotic (Dime '„bin-'Ÿ &quot;build, create&quot;);
*'ƒm-t'š &quot;die&quot; (Ehret: *'maw'„), atšested in Chadic (eg Hausa '„mutu'), Egyptian ('ƒmwt'‚, '‚mt'‰, Coptic '‚mu'‹), Berber ('m‚et'†, pr. '‚yem‚ut'), Semitic (*'ƒmwt'Ÿ), and Cushitic (Proto-Somali *'‚umaw'‚/*'‰-am-w(t)-'­ &quot;die&quot;), also similar to the Latin '†mortis'‘, indicating a pos˜ible vocabulary drift
*'ƒs-n'” &quot;know&quot;, at©ested in Chadic, Berber, and Egyptian;
*'ƒl-s'œ &quot;tongue&quot; (Ehret: '†*lis' '—&quot;to lick&quot;), at“ested in Semitic (*'ƒlasa…n/lisan'), Egyptian ('‚ns'‰, Coptic 'ƒlas'‹), Berber ('ƒils'”), Chadic (eg Hausa '†harshe'‰), and pos’ibly Omotic (Dime '†lits'-'– &quot;lick&quot;);
*'ƒs-m'› &quot;name&quot; (Ehret: *'Œs&amp;#365;m'„ / *'Œs&amp;#301;m'„), at“ested in Semitic (*'‚sm'‹), Berber ('ƒism'”), Chadic (eg Hausa '„suna'±), Cushitic, and Omotic (though the Berber form, 'ƒism'—, and the Omotic form, '…sunts'¥, are sometimes argued to be Semitic [ˆloanword]ƒs.) The Egyptian 'ƒsmi' &quot;report, an¤ounce&quot; may also be cognate.
* 'ƒd-m'‰ &quot;blo‘d&quot; (Ehret: *'„dÃ®m'„ / *'„dÃ¢m'„), at‘ested in Berber ('ƒidam‚en'), Semitic (*'ƒdam'Ÿ), Chadic, and arguably Omotic. ŠCushitic *'„dÃ®m'‚/*'„dÃ¢m'î, &quot;red&quot;, may be cognate.


In the verbal system, Semitic, Berber, and Cushitic (including Beja) al¿ provide evidence for a prefix conjugation:
