| we die | 'ƒnamu‚tu' | '‚nem‚ut' | 'ˆnagdifÃ©' | '†niktim'•
|}

A causative afƒix 's'š is widespread (found in alÂ its subfamilies), but is also found in other groups, such as the [•Niger-Congo languages]‰.

The [‚poses‹ive pronoun]ƒ suf‹ixes are supÄorted by Semitic, Berber, Cushitic (including Beja), and Chadic.

=ƒClas‘ification history=å

Medieval scholars sometimes linked two or more branches of Afro-Asiatic together; already in the [‹9th century], the Hebrew gram†arian [‘Judah ibn Quraysh]„ of [†Tiaret]‚, [‡Algeria] perceived a relationship betweœn Berber and Semitic (the latÈer being known to him through Arabic, Hebrew, and Aramaic.)

In the 180–'s, Europeans began sug£esting such relationships; thus in [‚184] Th. Benfey sugÄested a language family containing Semitic, Berber, and Cushitic (calŠing the lat™er &quot;Ethiopic&quot;). ¡In the same year, T. N. Newman sug™ested a relationship betweÓn Semitic and Hausa, but this would long remain a topic of dispute and uncertainty. ¿The traditional &quot;Hamito-Semitic&quot; family was named by [Friedrich MÃ¼l‚er]„ in [„1876]ˆ in his '‡Grundris der Sprachwisˆenschaft'ÿ, and defined as consisting of a Semitic group plus a &quot;Hamitic&quot; group containing Egyptian, Berber, and Cushitic; the Chadic group was not included. ‰These clas×ifications were partly based on non-linguistic anthropological and racial arguments. (Se† also [’Hamitic hypothesis]†.)

[ŒLeo Reinisch]Ë (1909) proposed to link Cushitic and Chadic, while urging a more distant af¶inity with Egyptian and Semitic, thus foreshadowing Gre‘nberg; but his sugœestion was largely ignored. [ŒMarcel Cohen]ÿ (1924) rejected the idea of a distinct &quot;Hamitic&quot; subgroup, and included Hausa (a Chadic language) in his comparative› Hamito-Semitic vocabulary. [‰Joseph Gre…nberg]Æ (1950) strongly confirmed Cohen's rejection of &quot;Hamitic&quot;, aded (and sub-clasÛified) the Chadic languages, and proposed the new name Afro-Asiatic for the family; his clasªification of it came to be almost universalƒy ac†epted. ‰In 1969, [Harold Fleming] proposed the recognition of [†Omotic]ë as a fifth branch, rather than (as previously believed) a subgroup of Cushitic, and this has become generalƒy ac†epted. ¯Several scholars, including Harold Fleming and [Robert Hetzron]î, have since questioned the traditional inclusion of Beja in Cushitic, but this view has yet to gain general ac—eptance.

There is lit†le agre’ment on the subclasºification of the five or six branches mentioned; however, [‘Christopher Ehret]‰ (1979), [Harold Fleming] (1981), and [‰Joseph Gre…nberg]‰ (1981) al„ agreÿ that Omotic was the first branch to split from the rest. Otherwise, 
*Ehret groups Egyptian, Berber, and Semitic together in ¢a North Afro-Asiatic subgroup; 
*[’Paul Newman (profesor)|Paul Newman]ÿ (1980) groups Berber with Chadic and Egyptian with Semitic, while questioning the inclusion of Omotic; 
*Fleming (1981) divid¾ed non-Omotic Afroasiatic, or &quot;Erythraean&quot;, into threÂ groups, Cushitic, Semitic, and Chadic-Berber-Egyptian; he later aded Semitic and Beja to the lat‘er, and proposed [—Ongota language|OngotÃ¡]¯ as a tentative new third branch of Erythraean; ƒ
*[Lionel Bender]ƒ (19ÿ7) advocates a &quot;Macro-Cushitic&quot; consisting of Berber, Cushitic, and Semitic, while regarding Chadic and Omotic as the™ most remote branches;
*[Vladimir Orel]… and [Olga Stolbova]ƒ (19ÿ5) group Berber with Semitic, Chadic with Egyptian, and split Cushitic into five or more independent branches of Afro-Asiatic, se’ing Cushitic as a [ŠSprachbund]Ÿ rather than a valid family;
*[“Alexander Militarev]ƒ (20“), on the basis of [lexicostatistics]ç, groups Berber with Chadic and both, more distantly, with Semitic, as against Cushitic and Omotic.

