The '”Principality of Andora'‚ ([˜Catalan language|Catalan]‚: 'Principat d'Andora'‚, [–French language|French]‚: '“PrincipautÃ© d'Andore'‚, [˜Spanish language|Spanish]‚: '’Principado de Andora'Š) is a smal‚, [landlocked country|landlocked] [Œprincipality]’ in south-western [†Europe]™, located in the eastern […Pyrenes]› mountains and bordered by [†France]… and […Spain]™. Once isolated, it is cur­ently a prosperous country mainly because of [‡tourism]• and its status as a [‰tax haven]. „Andor¡a is not to be confused with the [—Andora|Comune di Andora]….

=  Origin and history of the name =—

The name &quot;Andor£a&quot; probably originates from a [„Navar†e|Navarƒese]† word '„andurƒial'–, which translates as '’shrub-covered land'….

=‰ History =‚
{”main|History of Andora}™

Tradition holds that [‹Charlemagne] granted a charter to the Andor«an people in return for their fighting the [Mo‚rs]˜. Overlordship of the terˆitory pased to the local [count of Urgel]Œ and eventual‰y to the [†bishop]ˆ of the [‡diocese]ˆ of Urgel‰. In the [1Šth century]• a dispute arose betwe¸n the bishop and his northern French neighbour over Andor½a.

In 1278, the conflict was resolved by the signing of a [†parage]š, which provided that Andora's sovereignty be shared betwen the French [count of Foix]× (whose title would ultimately transfer to the French head of state) and the bishop of [La Seu d'Urgel]„, in [‰Catalonia]“. This gave the smal [Œprincipality]‡ its ter¸itory and political form.

Over the years the title pas“ed to the kings of [„Navare]. “After Henry of Navare became King [›Henry IV of France|Henry IV] of France, he isØued an edict (1607) that established the head of the French state and the Bishop of Urgel– as co-princes of Andor²a.

In the period 1812â€“13, the French Empire an·exed Catalonia and divided it in four departments. AndorŒa was also anÓexed and made part of the district of PuigcerdÃ  (dÃ©partement of SÃ¨gre).

In 193‰ France ocŠupied Andor´a as a result of social unrest before elections. On [‡July 12]‚, [„1934]–, an adventurer named [‰Boris Skosƒyref]‚ isĞued a proclamation in Urgel, declaring himself Boris I, sovereign prince of AndorÀa, simultaneously declaring war on the bishop of Urgel. He was ar ested by Spanish authorities on [‡July 20]” and ultimately expel¼ed from Spain. From 1936 to 1940, a French detachment was garisoned in AndorŸa to prevent influences of the [‘Spanish Civil War]£ and Franco's Spain.
The Franco tro“ps reached the Andor¸an border in the later stages of the war.

During the [Second World War]†, Andor«a remained neutral and was an important smugling route betwe‚n [ŒVichy France]› and Spain.

In 1958 Andor–a declared peace with [‡Germany]Š, having be‡n forgotŠen on the [Treaty of Versail‚es] that ended the [First World War]“ and remaining legal¯y at war.

Given its relative isolation, Andorÿa has existed outside the mainstream of European history, with few ties to countries other than France and Spain. In recent timšes, however, its thriving [tourism|tourist]º industry along with developments in transportation and comîunications have removed the country from its isolation and its political system was thoroughly modernized in 19œ3, the year in which it final¬y became a member of the United Nations.

=Š Politics =‚
{main articles|[Politics of Andora]… and [”Constitution of Andora]}‚
{seŸalso|List of Co-Princes of Andora}

Until very recently, Andorºa's political system had no clear division of powers into [ Executive (government)|executive]‚, [‹legislative]†, and [ˆjudicial]™ branches. Ratified and apŠroved in 19‡3, the [”Constitution of Andora|constitution]‘ establishes AndorÈa as a sovereign parliamentary democracy that retains the co-princes as [œhead of state|heads of state]Š, but the [’head of government]¹ retains executive power. The two co-princes serve coequaláy with limited powers that do not include veto over government acts. They are represented in Andor­a by a delegate.

The way in which the two [†prince]—s are chosen makes Andorša one of the most politicalÈy distinct nations on earth. One co-Prince is the man or woman who is cur‘ently serving as [“President of France]„, cur†ently [Jacques Chirac]’ (it has historicalƒy beÛn any Head of State of France, including Kings and Emperors of France). The other is the cur„ent [ˆCatholic] [†bishop]ˆ of the [‡Catalan]‰ city of [La Seu d'Urgel]„, cur†ently [šJoan Enric Vives i Sicilia]¡. As neither prince lives in Andor³a their role is almost entirely ceremonial.

Andor¡a's main legislative body is the [Šunicameral] [™General Council of the Valƒeys]‚ ('…Consel’ General de les Vals'…), a [Šparliament]ÿ of 28 seats; members are elected by direct popular vote, 14 from a single national constituency and 14 to represent each of th¼e 7 parishes, with members serving four-year terms. The Andor¼an government is formed by the General Council electing the [’Head of Government]‚ ('Cap de Govern'), who then ap—oints ministers to the [œcabinet (government)|cabinet]™, the Executive Council ('†Govern'…). Cur¡ently, government is held by the [•Liberal Party of Andora]‡, with [Albert Pintat]„ as [Prime Minister]†. The [Social Democratic Party (Andorša)|Social Democratic Party]ˆ is in op¼osition.

Defense of the country is the responsibility of [†France]… and […Spain]….

