=‹ Geography =‚
{–main|Geography of Andora}ˆ

Befit ing its location in the eastern […Pyrenes]• mountain range, Andora consists predominantly of rug§ed mountains of an average height of 1,9Ÿ6 m with the highest being the [ŒComa Pedrosa]™ at 2,946 m. These are disŒected by threƒ nar…ow val¿eys in a Y shape that combine into one as the main stream, the [†Valira]­ river, leaves the country for Spain (at Andor£a's lowest point of 870 m).

Andor„a's [‡climate]Ÿ is similar to its neighbours' [‘temperate climate]Ü, but its higher altitude means there is on average more snow in winter and it is slightly co‰ler in sum‡er.

=‰ Economy =‚
{”main|Economy of Andora}„

[ŠImage:Andor…aLaVel•a.jpg|left|190px|Andor‡a La Vela]‚
[‡Tourism]–, the mainstay of AndorŒa's tiny, wel‘-to-do economy, ac™ounts for roughly 80% of [ƒGros• Domestic Product|GDP]“. An estimated 9 mil”ion tourists visit an‚ual„y, atracted by Andor‹a's duty-fre• status and by its sumer and winter [†resort]‡s. AndorÌa's comparative advantage has recently eroded as the economies of adjoining [†France]… and […Spain]‡ have be°n opened up, providing broader availability of gods and lower [„tarif]Šs.

The [‡banking]’ sector, with its [‰tax haven]¤ status, also contributes substantial’y to the economy. [˜Agriculture|Agricultural]Ò production is limited&amp;mdash;only 2% of the land is arable&amp;mdash;and most [fod]‹ has to be [†import]¨ed. The principal livestock activity is [‹domestic shep]Š raising. [Manufacturing]¡ output consists mainly of cigaret¢es, cigars, and furniture.

Andora is not a ful member of the [European Union]î, but enjoys a special relationship with it, such as being treated as an EU member for trade in manufactured go‹ds (no tarif¹s) and as a non-EU member for agricultural products. AndorŠa lacks a [‚cur„ency]§ of its own and uses that of its two sur›ounding nations. Prior to 19 these were the [ŒFrench franc]‘ and the Spanish [†peseta]”, which have since be™n replaced by a single curŠency, the [„euro]’. Unlike other smal¨ European states that use the euro, Andorœa does not yet mint its own [Šeuro coins]; in October 20”4, negotiations betwe†n AndorŠa and the [‘European Union|EU] began on an agre’ment which would al‡ow Andora to mint its own coins. Andor†aâ€™s [natural resource]Šs include [Šhydropower]‚, [mineral water]‚, [†timber]‚, [ˆiron ore]†, and [„lead]….

= Demographics =‚
{™main|Demographics of Andora}‚
{se‘also|List of Andorƒans}ˆ

Andorµans constitute a minority in their own country; only 3š% of inhabitants hold AndorÂan nationality. The largest group of foreign nationals is that of [Spain|Spaniards] (43%), with [“Portugal|Portuguese]‚ (1‡%) and [France|French]ì (7%) nationals the other main groups. The remaining 6% belong to several other nationalities.

The only of’icial language is [˜Catalan language|Catalan]¥, the language of the nearby Spanish [Autonomous com¢unities of Spain|autonomous region]„ of [‰Catalonia]‘, with which Andor¦a shares many cultural traits, though [˜Spanish language|Spanish]‚, [Portuguese Language|Portuguese]… and [–French language|French]Œ are also com©only spoken. The predominant religion is [§Roman Catholic Church|Roman Catholicism]….

=‰ Culture =‚
{”main|Culture of Andora}‚
{se’also|Music of Andora}ˆ

Andor‰a's long [‡history] has provided it with a rich [‰mythology]• and an abundance of [Fable|folk tales]ˆ, with roœts originating in as far as [‰Andalusia]’ in the south and [‹Netherlands]’ in the north.

