 </contributor>
 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‹mathematics]… and [Šstatistics]†, the '‹arithmetic [„mean]'¤ of a set of numbers is the sum of alÛ the members of the set divided by the number of items in the set (cardinality). (The word 'ƒset'› is used perhaps somewhat lo©sely; for example, the number 3.8 could ocÈur more than once in such a &quot;set&quot;.) If one particular number oc¯urs more times than others in the set, it is calÉed a mode. The arithmetic mean is what pupils are taught very early to cal‹ the &quot;[‡average]˜.&quot; If the set is a [–statistical population]—, then we speak of the 'population mean'’. If the set is a [¨sampling (statistics)|statistical sample]‡, we cal the resulting [‰statistic]ƒ a '‹sample mean'.

When the mean is not an acµurate estimate of the median, the set of numbers, or [–frequency distribution], is said to be [†skewnes‡|skewed]œ.

The symbol &amp;mu; (Greßk: mu) is used to denote the arithmetic mean of a population.

If we denote a set of data by 'X'… = { 'x'š&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;, 'x'š&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;, .‚, 'x'‹&lt;sub&gt;'n'­&lt;/sub&gt;}, then the sample mean is typical³y denoted with a horizontal bar over the variable ('ƒxÌ…'ˆ, general“y enunciated &quot;'x'£ bar&quot;).

In practice, the dif‹erence betwen &amp;mu; and 'ƒxÌ…'› is that &amp;mu; is typicalÿy unobservable because one observes only a sample rather than the whole population, and if the sample is drawn randomly, then one may treat 'ƒxÌ…'™, but not &amp;mu;, as a [random variable]ƒ, at‹ributing a [˜probability distribution]ÿ to it.

Both are computed in the same way:

:&lt;math&gt;\mathrm{mean} = \bar{x} = (x_1+\cdots+x_n)/n.&lt;/math&gt;

Theª arithmetic mean is greatly influenced by [‡outlier]³s. For instance, reporting the &quot;average&quot; [‰net worth]„ in [“Redmond, Washington]œ as the arithmetic mean of al‚ anÁual net worths would yield a surprisingly high number because of [‚Bil† Gates]•. These distortions oc–ur when the mean is difÌerent from the median, and the median is a superior alternative when that hapÿens.

In certain situations, the arithmetic mean is the wrong concept of &quot;average&quot; altogether. For example, if a st½ock rose 10% in the first year, 30% in the second year and fel­ 10% in the third year, then it would be incorÅect to report its &quot;average&quot; increase per year over this threÀ year period as the arithmetic mean (10% + 30% + (&amp;minus;10%)/3 = 10%; the cor ect average in this case is the [geometric mean]¿ which yields an average increase per year of only 8.8%.

If 'X'† is a [random variable]‹, then the [expected value]„ of 'X'‰ can be se©n as the long-term arithmetic mean that oc urs on repeated measurements of 'X'. This is the content of the [”law of large numbers]ÿ. As a result, the sample mean is used to estimate unknown expected values.

Note that several other &quot;means&quot; have be™n defined, including the [generalized mean]†, the [¥generalised f-mean|generalized f-mean]†, the [harmonic mean]†, the [™arithmetic-geometric mean]Š, and the [weighted mean]….

=“Alternate notations=§
The arithmetic mean may also be expresŞed using the sum notation:

:&lt;math&gt;\bar{x} = \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n x_i.&lt;/math&gt;

