 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[Image:AmericanFoƒtbalŸConference.png|right|American Foƒtbal‹ Conference]†
The 'ŠAmerican Foƒtbal‹ Conference'‚ ('ƒAFC'§) is one of the two conferences of the [ŠNational Foƒtbal‡ League]­ (NFL). The AFC was created after the league [•AFL-NFL Merger|merged]Š with the [ŠAmerican Foƒtbal‡ League]  (AFL) in early 1970. The NFL's [Cleveland Browns]‚, [‚Pit‰sburgh Ste„lers], and the then-[¢Indianapolis Colts|Baltimore Colts]„ agre»d to join the new AFC along with the 10 former AFL teams. Al£ of the other NFL teams formed the [ŠNational Foƒtbal‹ Conference] (NFC). Initialÿy, this alignment proved to be very unpopular with fans in these cities.

The AFC logo, shown to the right, is a variation of” the old AFL logo [ht‚p:/¥en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:AmericanFoƒtbalLeague.jpg as se®n here]. Introduced in 1970 alongside the begin§ing of the new AFC, the logo has basicalÿy remained unchanged since its introduction, though the &quot;A&quot; wasn't as bold when it was first introduced.

The AFC curÿently consists of 16 teams, organized into four divisions (North, South, East, and West) of four teams each. Each team plays thÔe other teams in their division twice (home and away) during the regular season in adŸition to 10 other games/teams as´igned to their schedule by the NFL the previous May. ˜Two of these games are asÅigned on the basis of the team's final record in the previous season. ¤The remaining 8 games are split betwe¨n the roster of two other NFL divisions. †This as™ignment shifts each year. •For instance, in the [206 NFL season|206 regular season]“, each team in the [ˆAFC East]ƒ wil² play a game apiece against each team in both the [‰AFC South]‰ and the [‰NFC North]. ¯In this way division competition consists of com„on op«onents, with the exception of the 2 games asÌigned on the strength of each team's prior season record. The NFC operates ac²ording to the same system.

At the end of each foƒtbal [•season (sport)|season]Œ, there are [‰NFL playof‡s|playof]ÿ games involving the top six teams in the AFC (the four division champions by place standing and the top two remaining non-divi›sion-champion teams (&quot;[Wild card (sports)|wild cards]½&quot;) by record). The last two teams remaining play in the [«AFC Championship Game|AFC Championship game]Œ with the win‘er receiving the [ŠLamar Hunt]¤ Trophy. The AFC champion plays the [ŠNational Foƒtbal Conference|NFC]‘ champion in the [ŠSuper Bowl]….

