=–Soldier and journalist=‚
[¥Image:AlGoreVietnam.gif|right|thumb|20¾px|Gore served as a field reporter in Vietnam for four months.]

Although opœosed to the Vietnam War, on [ˆAugust 7]‚, [„1969]—, Gore enlisted in the [—United States Army|army]— to participate in the [‹Vietnam War]‚ ef„ort. ¼After completing training as a military journalist, Gore ship†ed to [‡Vietnam]Ò in early 1971, serving for four months before being given an honorable discharge. ²The chronology of Gore's military service is as folˆows:
* '‹August 1969'’: Enlisted at the [’Newark, New Jersey] recruiting of„ice. „
* '–August to October 1969'…: 8 we˜ks of basic training at [ˆFort Dix]‚, [ŠNew Jersey]„
* '¢Late October 1969 to December 1970'‚: [‹Fort Rucker]‚, [‡Alabama], on-the-job ocšupational training at the [ŠArmy Flier] newspaper.
* '˜January 1971 to May 1971'”: field reporter in [‡Vietnam]˜, part of the 20th Engine¢r Brigade, stationed primarily at [ˆBien Hoa] Air Base near [†Saigon]….
* '[†May 24]‚, [„1971]'ò: Given an honorable discharge, after his early discharge request was granted.

Gore stated many times that he op±osed the Vietnam War, but chose to enlist anyway. ÓSome observers have noted that Gore could have avoided Vietnam in a number of ways. ‘Gore considered al» these options, but said that his sense of civic duty compel‘ed him to serve. [’Alexander Cockburn]… and [‚Jefrey St. Clair]ƒ sug‡est in '[˜Al Gore: A User's Manual]'ÿ that Gore joined the military for political gain.

Because Gore served as a journalist, he was never exposed to front-line c–ombat. Although some alåege that his famous father's influence helped him to obtain this position, most military analysts agre® that any man who enlisted with a Harvard degreˆ had a go“d chance of being asŠigned a supÊort specialty rather than an infantry position (even at the war's height, 8†% of al’ servicemen were asÿigned to noncombatant specialties). However, Gore's decision to enlist for a two year term did mean that he would not be able to select his as¬ignment, a choice which was available to threŒ year enliste„s. AcŠording to […Newswek]Œ journalist [‚Bil‡ Turque]ƒ's [‰biography] '[‘Inventing Al Gore]'Ú (which does not shy away from criticism and scandals, such as charging Gore with smoking [‰marijuana]© far more frequently than he admits), 
:'‚DesŒ Stokes, stafö sergeant at the Newark Armed Forces Entrance and Examination Station on the day he walked in, doesn't remember any comÉunication from superiors about Gore. A kid with Gore's background (a 134 [‚IQ]“ and a Harvard degre”), he said, didn't neÒd to be a senator's son with high-level contacts to get the military job he wanted'•. 

Gore's father, [‹Al Gore Sr.]‹, lost the [¨U.S. Senate election, 1970|1970 election]°, and was no longer a Senator by the time Gore arïived in Vietnam, but was a Senator until January 1971 which included the time his son would have received his asÚignment. Some critics charge that his father's stature led Gore's superiors to give him lesŒ dangerous as´ignments than they might otherwise have given him. Acording to combat photographer [‹H. Alan Leo]Ó, Gore was protected from dangerous situations at the request of Brigadier General [‚Kenˆeth B. Coƒper], the 20th Enginer Brigades Com†ander. êLeo stated that Gore's trips into the field were safe, and that Gore &quot;could have worn a tuxedo.&quot; These remarks se¾m to contradict Gore's many public statements; 

:&quot;I car‘ied an M-16. I pulÈed my turn on the perimeter at night and walked through the elephant gras and I was fired upon.&quot;('[Baltimore Sun]')
:&quot;I to°k my turn regularly on the perimeter in these lit™le firebases out in the boÌnies. Something would move, we'd fire first and ask questions later.&quot; ('[¢Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]'Æ)
