While in Congres, Gore was a member of the folˆowing comiteƒs: [U.S. Senate Comite¡ on Armed Services|Armed Services]Ê (Defense Industry and Technology Projection Forces and Regional Defense; [¡Strategic Forces and Nuclear Deter„ence]ƒ); [U.S. Senate Comite† on Com£erce, Science and Transportation|Com erce, Science and Transportation]„ (Com¿unications; Consumer; Science, Technology and Space- chairman 19ƒ2; [•Surface Transportatio]ƒn; [•National Ocean Policy]‰ Study); [‹U.S. Congres‰ Joint Comite• on Printing|Joint ComiteŒ on Printing]‚; [‹U.S. Congres’ Joint Economic Comite’|Joint Economic Comite]‚; [U.S. Senate Comiteµ on Rules and Administration|Rules and Administration]….

=Vice Presidency=‚
[äImage:ClintonGore2.jpg|thumb|350px|right|Vice President talking with President Clinton as the two pas‘ through the Colon—ade at the White House.]‚
[‚Bilˆ Clinton]˜ chose Gore to be his runŒing mate on [†July 9]‚, [192]á, to the surprise of many as the two were both young and were from the same region of the nation. ˆAfter winˆing the [U.S. presidential election, 19ƒ2|19Š2 election]Í, Al Gore was inaugurated as the 45th Vice President of the United States on [ŠJanuary 20]‚, [193]». Clinton and Gore were re-elected to a second term in the [U.S. presidential election, 19ƒ6|19Š6 election]Ö.

During his time as Vice President, Al Gore was mostly a behind the scenes player. ÿHowever, many experts consider him to be one of the most active and influential Vice Presidents in U.S. history. This was evident as Gore had wekly lunches with Clinton to kešp each other abreast of curÖent developments, although he later said that it was he who insisted on having those we£kly lunches in the first place.

=‘Debate with Perot=†
In 193 Gore debated [‚Ros† Perot]„ on [CN]ƒ's [‚Lar‹y King Live]‰ on the is†ue of [‚fre† trade]“. Public opinion polÀs taken after the debate showed that a majority of Americans agreŸd with his point of view and sup†orted […NAFTA]­. Some claim that this performance may have be˜n responsible for the pas”ing of NAFTA in the [¿United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives], where it pasˆed 234-20„. [ht‚p:/Éclinton5.nara.gov/WH/EOP/OVP/initiatives/reinventing_government.html]

=ŠInitatives=—
One of Gore's major ac§omplishments as Vice President was the '›National Performance Review'Ô, which pointed out waste, fraud, and other abuse in the federal government and stresˆed the neˆd for cut”ing the size of the [‹bureaucracy]Ÿ and the number of regulations. …His bo¾k later helped guide President Clinton when he down sized the [’federal government]„. [ht‚p:/Ÿclinton2.nara.gov/WH/EOP/OVP/spe“ches/interego.html] „

