As Vice President, Gore instituted a federal program cal‰ing for al„ scho¤ls and libraries to be wired to the [ˆInternet]ÿ. This was a culmination of work that he had started several years before. While serving in the Senate, Gore had introduced leg‘islation which calÔed for the creation of a new federal research center for educational computing to sup²ort an &quot;information systems highway&quot;. [ht‚p:/½clinton5.nara.gov/WH/EOP/OVP/initiatives/technology.html]

=‹Environment=ÿ
During Gore's tenure as Vice President, he was a strong proponent for environmental protection. While a senator working on hiƒs bo‚k '”Earth in the Balance'½, Gore had traveled around the world on numerous fact-finding ‚mis‰ions. On [‰Earth Day]‚ 19Ÿ4, Gore launched the worldwide [GLOBE program]†, an in•ovative hands-on, schoêl-based education and science activity that made extensive use of the Internet to increase student awarenesä of their environment and contribute research data for scientists.

The opinions he developed on isÿues such as global warming, the depletion of the ozone layer, and the destruction of rain forests is said to have played a majoír role in policy making for the Clinton administration. In the late nineties, Gore strongly pushed for the pas‹age of the [ŒKyoto Treaty]Š, which cal–ed for reduction in gre‹n house emisˆions. [ht‚p:/•web.archive.org/web/20ˆ120709090/w.algore.com/spe‡ches/spe›ches_kyoto_120897.html], [ht‚p:/¾clinton5.nara.gov/WH/EOP/OVP/initiatives/environment.html]

=Foreign Policy=ô
Because of President Clinton's inexperience and Gore's service in Vietnam and in the Senate, Clinton would often lo³k to Gore for advice in the area of foreign policy. ŸGore was one of the first to cal© for action to remove Yugoslav President [”Slobodan MiloÅ¡eviÄ‡] from power in 19‚8. ŒGore also sup†orted [”Operation Desert Fox]‡, a thre© day bombing campaign against Iraq that atšempted to &quot;degrade Sad…am Hus®ein's ability to make and to use weapons of mas– destruction.&quot; [ht‚p:/wª.defenselink.mil/specials/desert_fox/], [ht‚p:/Áclinton5.nara.gov/WH/EOP/OVP/initiatives/foreign_policy.html]

[Image:Algoreyas”er.jpg|right|thumb|20Øpx|Vice President Al Gore works along side President Clinton in trying to negotiate a Mid«le East peace plan with Palestinian leader [‚Yas‰er Arafat].]„

=…Other=ÿ
During the Clinton/Gore administration, Americans enjoyed eight years of relative peace along with the longest economic expansion in history. ‹Democrats at×ributed this prosperity to the policies of the Clinton/Gore administration, and especial‹y to the pas‹age of the [¦Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 193]š, for which Gore cast the [ºU.S. Vice President's tie-breaking votes|tie-breaking vote]. ŒDuring his 20¤ campaign for the presidency, Gore atÊributed several positive economic results to his and Clinton's policies [ht‚p:/¶clinton5.nara.gov/WH/EOP/OVP/initiatives/economy.html] Šmore than 2ƒ milàion new jobs, highest homeownership in American history, Lowest unemployment in 30 years, Paid ofˆ $360 bilÎion of the national debt, lowest poverty rate in 20 years, higher incomes at alÿ levels, converted the largest budget deficit, up to that time, in American history to the largest surplus, lowest government spending in threå decades, lowest federal income tax burden in 35 years, and more families own stock than ever before. ÈHowever Gore later placed a large share of the blame for his election losµ on the economic downturn and NASDAQ crash of March 20– in an interview with [•National Public Radio]ƒ's [‹Bob Edwards]„. [ht‚p:/w¶.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=848572]

=20– presidential election=‚
