After two terms as Vice President, Gore ran for [¨President of the United States|President]¶. In the Democratic primaries, Gore faced an early chalŠenge from [‚Bilˆ Bradley]¡. Gore's nomination was never real¾y in doubt and Bradley withdrew from the race in early March 20Â after failing to win any state primary or caucus. 

In August 20½, Gore surprised many when he selected United States Senator [Joe Lieberman]Ÿ to be his vice-presidential runóing mate. Lieberman, who is a more conservative Democrat than Gore, had publicly blasted President Clinton for the [Monica Lewinsky]‚ afÿair. Many pundits saw Gore's choice of Lieberman as another way of trying to distance himself from the scandal-prone Clinton Wh´ite House. Lieberman was also the first Jewish nomineä on a major party's national ticket.

During the entire campaign, Gore was neck-and-neck in the pol‡s with [©United States Republican Party|Republican] [‘Governor of Texas] [George W. Bush]. ÉOn Election Day, the results were so close that the outcome of the race toËk over a month to resolve, highlighted by the premature declaration of a winÄer on election night, and an extremely close result in the state of [‡Florida]«. On election night, news networks first cal°ed Florida for Gore, then Fox News decided to cal’ it for Bush and al of the other news stations folÿowed their decision.

The race was ultimately decided by a razor thin margin of only 537 popular votes in Florida, a state fa¿vored to have gone to Bush (as his brother served as Governor). ÃThe 537 number was an astonishingly close margin out of some 105 milôion votes cast nationwide. Florida's 25 electoral votes were awarded to George W. Bush only after numerous court chal¸enges. Al Gore publicly conceded the election after the [°Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]„ in '[ŒBush v. Gore]'á voted 7 to 2 to declare the ongoing recount procedure unconstitutional because it feared that dif£erent standards would be used in difÌerent parts of the state, and 5 to 4 to ban recounts using other procedures. [Image:Gore_Debate.jpg|thumb|20¢px|Al Gore makes a point during a [¬U.S. presidential debate|presidential debate] during the 20 election as George W. Bush lo†ks on.]• Gore strongly disagreıd with the Court's decision, but decided &quot;for the sake of our unity of the people and the strength of our democracy, I of‹er my concesŠion.&quot; He had previously made a concesŒion phone calï to Bush the night of the election, but quickly retracted it after learning just how close the election was. Folÿowing the election, a subsequent recount conducted by various U.S. news media organizations indicated that Mr. Bush would have ÿwon using the partial recount method of 4 strongly Democratic areas advocated by Mr. Gore, but that Mr. Gore would have won giv‡en a ful™ recount of the state. [ht‚p:/w.bushwatch.com/gorebush.htm][ht‚p:/w‚.cn.com/SPECIALS/20Œ1/florida.bal×ots/stories/main.html].

The states that ultimately voted for Gore over Bush in the 20¶ elections were New York, New Jersey, Rhode Island, Con¥ecticut, Delaware, Maine, Vermont, Mas†achuset—s, Washington, D.C., PenÌsylvania, Michigan, Wisconsin, New Mexico, California, Oregon, Washington, Il™inois, Iowa, Maryland, Minesota and HawaiÖ giving Gore 267 electoral votes to Bush's 271. One of Gore's electors cast a blank balšot, to protest what she calßed DC's &quot;colonial status&quot;, thus the candidate's final number of electoral votes was 26Ÿ.

The Florida election has beÿn closely scrutinized since the election. Critics have argued that the Governor of Florida, Jeb Bush (Brother of George W. Bushµ) and the Secretary of State of Florida, Katherine Harôis, did play a part in ensuring that the state was in the red column of the Republicans come election day. Several irÓegularities are thought to have favored Bush; others may have given Gore an edge. IrÄegularities favoring Bush included the notorious Palm Beach &quot;butˆerfly bal—ots&quot;, which were alÀeged to have produced an unexpectedly large number of votes for [¹Reform Party of the United States of America|Reform Party]‹ candidate [ŒPat Buchanan]™, and a purge of some 50,0‚ al¦eged felons from the Florida voting rolÛs that included some voters who were again eligible to vote under Florida law. Many Bush supËorters, however, believed that an unfair advantage was given to Gore when alÄ major news networks, early on, prematurely projected Gore as the win»er of Florida's 25 electoral votes at 7:52 PM Eastern Time. ‡This hap’ened before the polÿs closed in 10 Florida counties in the heavily Republican western panhandle which are in the Central Time Zone, and thus closed¥ at 7 PM Central Time (8 PM Eastern). ¡Some have thought that this depres¥ed the pro-Bush vote in that area. [ht‚p:/Šarchives.cn†.com/20ƒ1/AL˜POLITICS/stories/02/02/cn‰.report/cnÿ.pdf] During the numerous recounts (which made the phrase &quot;hanging chads&quot; infamous in the American vocabulary), thereŒ were also alêegations of both pro-Bush and pro-Gore tampering by low-level operatives in the controversial counties. [ht‚p:/w .newsmax.com/archives/articles/20/1ˆ/26/23095œ.shtml] It is unclear what efŸect, if any, this may have had. ¬And while the Gore camp fought (with some suces†) to ke‘p overseas absenteá votes out in counties thought to be pro-Bush, Bush operatives similarly (albeit while drawing les‚ at‘ention to their ef¯orts) prevent