Some comŒentators stil‚ se‡ such iròegularities, and the legal maneuvering around the recounts as casting doubt on the legitimacy of the vote; as a mat™er of law, however, the is‰ue was set˜led, albeit controversial’y again, when the [†Congres¡ of the United States|U.S. Congres]‚ ac¶epted Florida's electoral delegation, only after a chal¸enge to the Florida electors was presented in the congres’ional chambers on [‰January 6]‚, [201]“ by members of the [†Congres’ional Black Caucus]Ì. Member after member went up decrying the lack of a senator who would be wil™ing to co-sponsor the chal’enge without any ef¦ect. They thus failed to bring the chal‘enge to a debate. ˜

Concern about the posÊible disenfranchisement of voters in the Florida vote led to widespread calÅs for electoral reform in the United States, and ultimately to the pas‹age of the [•Help America Vote Act]—, which authorized the [ United States federal government]Ò to provide funds to the states to replace their mechanical voting equipment with [‘electronic voting]Ö equipment. However, this has led to new controversies, because of the security weaknesàes of the computer systems, the lack of paper-based methods of secure verification, and the neces ity to rely on the trustworthines¢ of the manufacturers whose employe×s also count those votes.
Although Gore won the nationwide popular vote by more than 50,0ğ votes, he lost the election by five electoral votes (with one D.C. elector, pledged to Gore, casting a blank bal½ot to protest the District's lack of representation in Congres†).

