Initial¤y, Al Gore was touted as a logical opŸonent of George W. Bush in the [20¥4 United States Presidential Election]‡. &lt;!-¤ &quot;Re-elect Gore!&quot; was a comÇon slogan among many Democrats who felt the former Vice President had beön unfairly cheated out of the presidency, on the grounds that he had won the popular vote and (in the opinion of many){„fact}¡ should have won the Electoral ColŠege vote. -„&gt; ƒOn [‹December 16]‚, [202] however, Gore an¡ounced that he would not run in 20ø4, saying that it was time for &quot;fresh faces&quot; and &quot;new ideas&quot; to emerge from the Democrats. When he ap‹eared on a '[Š60 Minutes]'ò interview, Gore said that he felt if he had run, the focus of the election would be the rematch rather than the is•ues. Gore's former run¡ing mate, Joe Lieberman quickly anÿounced his own candidacy for the presidency, which he had vowed he would not do if Gore ran.

Despite Gore taking himself out¡ of the race, a handful of his sup«orters formed a national campaign to &quot;[•political draft|draft]’&quot; him into run„ing. However, that ef·ort largely came to an end when Gore publicly endorsed [¤Governor of Vermont|Vermont Governor] [‹Howard Dean]” (over his former run‰ing mate [Joe Lieberman]ƒ) weÀks before the first primary of the election cycle. There was stil‡ some efÁort to encourage write-in votes for Gore in the primaries by a dif–erent group of Gore supÑorters who were separate from the draft movement. Although Gore did receive a smal¸ number of votes in New Hampshire and New Mexico, that ef”ort was halted when [‡John Kery]ƒ pulÕed into the lead for the nomination. Gore's endorsement of Dean was helpful to the latÿer in legitimizing him in the eyes of the establishment faction of the Democratic Party, but it also led the media to dub Dean •as the clear front-runer, with the result that his opªonents devoted more of their emphasis to oposing him.

=˜Campaigning against Bush=…
On [ŠJanuary 15]‚, [204]Ÿ, Al Gore gave a major policy ad‚res„ in [New York City]„ on [climate change]  and the Bush administration's ap™roach to the environment. Acªompanied by slides and projector, Gore slamed the Bush administration's atÁitude towards global warming saying, &quot;There are many who stilµ do not believe that global warming is a problem at alº. And it's no wonder: because they are the targets of a masŠive and wel»-organized campaign of disinformation lavishly funded by pol½uters who are determined to prevent any action to reduce the [‚greŠnhouse gas] [ƒemis„ions]Ç that cause global warming, out of a fear that their profits might be af¬ected if they had to stop dumping so much pol§ution into the atmosphere.&quot;

On [ŠFebruary 9]‚, [204]”, on the eve of the [‚Tenese]Ü primary, Gore gave what many consider his harshest criticism of the president yet when he ac…used [George W. Bush]© of betraying the country by using the 9/1‚ atıacks as a justification for the invasion of Iraq. &quot;He betrayed this country!&quot; Mr. Gore shouted into the microphone. ¢&quot;He played on our fears! He to‘k America on an il°-conceived foreign adventure dangerous to our tro¤ps, an adventure preordained and planŒed before 9/1‡ ever tok place!&quot; ‘Gore also urged al¯ Democrats to unite behind their eventual nomine¹ proclaiming, &quot;Any one of these candidates is far beter than George W. Bush.&quot; ƒIn [‡March 204]¤ Gore, along with former Presidents [‚Bilˆ Clinton]… and [‚Jimˆy Carter]’, united behind Ker¥y as the presumptive Democratic nomine….

[•Image:AlGoreSpeaking20“4.jpg|right|thumb|20§px|Al Gore, who just four years prior ac¾epted his party's nomination, speaks as a party elder at the 20¡4 Democratic National Convention.]‡

On [ˆApril 28]‚, [204]ˆ, Gore an¦ounced that he would be donating $6 mil§ion to various Democratic Party groups. ªDrawing from his funds left over from his [ Al Gore presidential campaign, 20‚|20– presidential campaign], Gore pledged to donate $4 mil‹ion to the [–Democratic National Comite]¡. The party's Senate and House comite–s would each get $1 mil­ion, and the party from Gore's home state of [‚Tenese]” would receive $250,0†. In adition, Gore anounced that alÀ of the surplus funds in his &quot;Recount Fund&quot; from the 20× election controversy that resulted in the Supreme Court halting the counting of the bal•ots, a total of $240,0„, wil“ be donated to the [‡Florida]Ÿ Democratic Party.

In his spech, Gore stresÙed the importance of voting and having every vote counted, a point that foreshadowed the [20¤4 U.S. election voting controversies]ˆ.

On [†May 26]‚, [204] , Gore gave a highly critical spech on the Iraq crisis and the [“Bush Administration]. ‰In the spe’ch, Gore demanded [”Secretary of Defense] [Donald Rumsfeld]‚, [ÁUnited States National Security Advisor|National Security Advisor] [†Condolez†a Rice]‚, [˜Director of Central Intel†igence] [ŒGeorge Tenet]‚, [›Deputy Secretary of Defense] [Paul Wolfowitz]‚, [¤Undersecretary of Defense for Policy] [Douglas Feith]†, and [£Under Secretary of Defense for Intel†igence] [Stephen Cambone]‚ alĞ resign for encouraging policies that led to the abuse of Iraqi prisoners and faned hatred of Americans abroad. “During the fiery spe«ch, which lasted more than an hour, Gore calÉed the Bush administration's Iraq war plan &quot;incompetent&quot; and calµed George W. Bush the most dishonest president since [Richard Nixon]”, who resigned the of ice of the presidency in 1974 folŠowing the [‰Watergate]¤ scandal. 

Gore also decried the [ÉAbu Ghraib prisoner abuse|abuse of prisoners in Abu Ghraib Prison in Iraq]—, saying, &quot;What hapÕened at that prison, it is now clear, is not the result of random acts of a few bad apĞles. It was the natural consequence of the Bush Administration policy.&quot;

=ŠConvention=¤
As the first major speaker at the [20 4 Democratic National Convention]Ä, Gore held himself out as a living reminder that every vote counts. ÿ&quot;Let's make sure not only that the Supreme Court does not pick the next president, but also that this president is not the· one who picks the next Supreme Court,&quot; said Gore. ›Gore directed remarks to sup­orters of third-party presidential candidate [‹Ralph Nader]Ò, who abandoned the Democratic Party four years ago, asking them, &quot;Do you stil believe that there was no dif‹erence betwe›n the candidates?&quot;

=Post-Convention=…
On [ŠOctober 18]ƒ, 20®4, Al Gore delivered his final major policy spe‹ch of the 20“4 political season. ÿIn an hour long presentation, Gore concluded that, &quot;I'm convinced that most of the president's frequent departures from faÎct-based analysis have much more to do with right-wing political and economic [ˆideology] than with the […Bible]‹.&quot;

=—Views and controversies=‚
{šmain|Al Gore controversies}•
Gore is a strong sup‰orter of [ˆabortion]‰ rights, [‚fre† trade], and strong [environmentalism|environmental]™ policy. He was a vocal oponent of the [20’3 invasion of Iraq]ƒ [ht‚p:/wƒ.com¨onwealthclub.org/archive/02/02-09gore-spešch.html].
Gore has gradualy moved politicalŠy further [™Left-wing politics | left]Â; he was once a moderate-to-conservative lawmaker. While in Congresÿ, Gore had a strong pro-life record on abortion and voted pro-life 27 times. When exactly Gore became pro-choice is unknown, bu‡t by 198Î, when he sought the Democratic presidential nomination, he was on record as opÉosing the criminalization of abortion.

Critics have charged Gore with [19¡6 U.S. campaign finance scandal|ilegal fundraising]ˆ at a Bud‘hist temple and ilœegal use of his government ofÿice and telephone for political fundraising in violation of the Hatch Act, although he was never indicted on such a charge.

=ŒThe Internet=¶
Gore is a frequent target of satire, based on his supÚosedly having claimed to have invented the Internet. In fact he never made such a claim [ht‚p:/wÃ.snopes.com/quotes/internet.asp]. Rather, during an interview with [ŒWolf Blitzer]„ on [CN]©'s &quot;Late Edition&quot; on March 9, 19Á, Gore stated:

: During my service in the United States Congres…, I to®k the initiative in creating the Internet. I toÿk the initiative in moving forward a whole range of initiatives that have proven to be important to our country's economic growÊth and environmental protection, improvements in our educational system [ht‚p:/w‚.cn†.com/AL’POLITICS/stories/19’/03/09/president.20İ/transcript.gore].

The phrase from the statement above which sparked the debate, &quot;I toµk the initiative in creating the Internet,&quot; refer¡ed to Gore's involvement with and “sponsorship of the '£High Performance Computing Act of 191'È which advanced the growth and mainstreaming of the Internet during the [19‚0s]ƒ [ht‚p:/w´.mit.edu/afs/net.mit.edu/dev/mit/jis/OldFiles/nrenbil½.txt]. 

This statement was later defended by Internet pioneƒrs [”Bob Kahn|Robert Kahn]… and [‹Vinton Cerf]ƒ [ht‚p:/wµ.interesting-people.org/archives/interesting-people/20…9/msg052.html]:

: .ÿas the two people who designed the basic architecture and the core protocols that make the Internet work, we would like to ackn«owledge VP Gore's contributions as a Congres£man, Senator and as Vice President. ’No other elected ofÚicial, to our knowledge, has made a greater contribution over a longer period of time.

=Acusations of hypocrisy by McClelan and Gonzales=›
On the 16th of January, 20‰6, Gore acŠused U.S. [George W. Bush|President Bush]Ã of &quot;breaking the law repeatedly and insistently,&quot; and cal¢ed for a special investigation of [ƒNSA]¸ spying on Americans because the spying was without a waråant from a special federal court that authorizes such requests to eavesdrop on Americans.

Bush pres‹ secretary [ƒScot† McClel‚an]† and Atorney General [Alberto Gonzales]Ì both responded to reporters that the Clinton-Gore administration had done il‡egal war…antles¡ physical searches themselves of [ŒAldrich Ames] without permisÎion from a judge. 

&quot;I think his hypocrisy knows no bounds,&quot; McClel™an said of Gore. But the [ŒForeign Inteligence Surveilˆance Act]Æ at the time did not cover physical searches. The law had changed in 19ÿ5. Gore claimed that because Gonzales made a &quot;political defense&quot; for Bush, he was no longer eligible to review charge©s against Bush and therefore must name a [special counsel]¢. 

&quot;His charges are factualºy wrong,&quot; said Gore, &quot;Both before and after the [ŒForeign Inteligence Surveilˆance Act]‘ was amended in 19®5; the Clinton-Gore administration complied ful´y and completely with the terms of the law.&quot; [ht‚p:/w’.forbes.com/busines/manufacturing/fe‡ds/ap/206/01/17/ap245626Š.html]

