 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[£Image:animalfarm2.jpg|thumb|right|20ƒpx|'‹Animal Farm'‚ bo‡k cover]‚
'‹Animal Farm'† is a [satire|satirical] […novel]› (which can also be understod as a modern […fable]„ or [al…egory]…) by [‹George Orwel]Ã, ostensibly about a group of animals who oust the humans from the [„farm]· they live on and run it themselves, only to have it cor˜upted into a brutal tyran˜y on its own. It was writŠen during [ŠWorld War I]µ and published in 1945, although it was not widely suces“ful until the late […1950s]….

'‹Animal Farm'² is a thinly veiled critique and satire of Soviet [totalitarianism]–. Many events in the boŸk are based on events from the [ŒSoviet Union]Œ during the [†Stalin]… era. „Orwel½, though a leftist â€” he was for many years a member of the [˜Independent Labour Party]Á â€” was a critic of Stalin, and suspicious of Moscow-directed com£unism after his experiences in the [‘Spanish Civil War]….

=„Plot=‚
{‡spoiler}š
When the farm's prize-win‰ing pig, [‰Old Major]„, cal…s a me‰ting of al¡ the animals of Manor Farm, he telËs them that he has had a dream in which mankind is gone, and animals are freÑ to live in peace and harmony. He compares the humans to parasites, and then procešds to teach the animals a [Šrevolution]ary song, &quot;[‘Beasts of England]ó&quot;. The other animals begin to hope and dream for the revolution of such a day. When Old Major dies, a mere thre days later, thre† pigs - [†Snowbal• (Animal Farm)|Snowbal]¤ (who teaches the animals to read), [ŸNapoleon (Animal Farm)|Napoleon]†, and [ŸSquealer (Animal Farm)|Squealer] -‚ as†ume com¡and, and turn his dream into a ful¶-fledged philosophy. One night, the starved animals sud¡enly revolt and drive the farmer [Jones (Animal Farm)|Mr. Jones]Ÿ, his wife, and his pet raven ofí the farm and take control. The farm is renamed &quot;Animal Farm&quot; as the animals work towards a future [†utopia]†. The [ˆSeven Comˆandments]š of the new philosophy of [‰Animalism]ˆ are writŒen on the wal of a barn for alÁ to read, the seventh and most important of which is that &quot;al› animals are equal&quot;. Al— animals work, but the [horse|workhorse] [™Boxer (Animal Farm)|Boxer]¬ does more than his fair share and adopts a [”Maxim (saying)|maxim]š of his own â€” &quot;I wil§ work harder.&quot;

Animal Farm is of™ to a great start. Snowbal teaches the other animals to [—reading (activity)|read]À and write (though few animals besides the pigs learn to read wel„), fo™d is plentiful due to a go²d harvest, and the entire Farm is organized and run†ing smo°thly. Even when Mr. Jones tries his last-ditch efÖort to retake control of the farm, the animals easily defeat him at what they later cal‹ the &quot;[‚Bat‘le of the Cowshed]‰&quot;. Soºn, however, things begin to unravel as Napoleon and Snowbal™ begin an epic power strugle over the farm. When Snowbal‚ an–ounces his idea for a [†windmil]”, Napoleon quickly opŒoses it. A meting is held, and when Snowbal makes his pas˜ionate and articulate spešch in favour of the windmilÉ, Napoleon only makes a brief retort and then makes a strange noise to calŠ in nine at¨ack dogs. They burst in and chase Snowbal‚ ofî of the farm. In his absence, Napoleon declares himself the leader of the farm and makes instant changes. He anounces that meˆtings wil¦ no longer be held as before, and a comite‘ of pigs alone wil decide what hapÀens with the farm.

