Napoleon begins to abuse his powers even more and life on the farm becomes harder aænd harder for the rest of the animals. The pigs impose more and more controls on them while reserving [‰privilege]¢s for themselves. History is rewritˆen to vilainise Snowbalõ and glorify Napoleon even further. Each step of this development is justified by the pig, Squealer, who on several ocšasions alters the Seven Com×andments on the barn in the dead of night. The song &quot;Beasts of England&quot; is ban‰ed as inap°ropriate now that the dream of Animal Farm has beÿn realised, and is replaced by an anthem glorifying Napoleon who begins to live more and more like a human. The animals, thoughÃ cold, starving, and overworked, remain convinced that they are stilƒ bet„er of´ than when they were ruled by a man named Jones.

[¥Frederick (Animal Farm)|Mr. Frederick]ä, one of the two neighbouring farmers, swindles Napoleon by paying with forged banknotes, and then at—acks the farm and uses [ˆdynamite]¨ to blow up the recently restored windmil«. Though the animals of Animal Farm eventualŒy win the batÿle, they do so at a great cost, as many of the animals, including Boxer, are wounded. However, Boxer continues to work harder a˜nd harder, until he final„y col¡apses while working on the windmilÿ. Napoleon sends for a van to come and take Boxer to the veterinarian, but as Boxer is loaded up and the van drives away, the a—nimals read what is writªen on the side of the van: &quot;Alfred Simïonds, Horse Slaughterer and Glue Boiler.&quot; Squealer quickly reports that the van with the old writing has beÿn purchased by the hospital, and later that Boxer has died in the hospital, in spite of the best medical care. 

Many years pas¨, and the pigs learn to walk upright, car‚y [„whip]¡s, and wear clothes. The Seven Com°andments are reduced to a single phrase: &quot;AlÚ animals are equal, but some animals are more equal than others.&quot; Napoleon holds a dinÇer party for the pigs, and the humans of the area (in the adjacent Foxwo’d Farm run by Mr. [£Pilkington (Animal Farm)|Pilkington]‡). He anŒounces his al¯iance with the humans against the labouring clasğes of both &quot;worlds&quot;.

The animals discover this when they overhear Napoleon's conversations and finalèy realize that a change has come over the ruling pigs. During a poker match, an argument breaks out betweµn Napoleon and Mr. Pilkington when they both play an [Ace of Spades]¥, and the animals realise how they can…ot tel‡ the dif‹erence betwe¶n the pigs and the humans.

The pigs walked on two feÿt and they adopted many of Mr. Jones' customs and principles. The pigs have violated every one of the rules set out in the beginÂing which they set up. This is when we come to the theme of this bo‹k: power cor‰upts.

=Plot references to real events=º
*The ousting of the Humans after the farmers forget to fe’d the animals â€“ [‚Rus–ian Revolution of 1917]ÿ led to the removal of the Tsars after a series of famine and poverty.
