*Napoleon's removal of †Snowbal… â€“ [†Stalin]â€™s removal of [ŒLeon Trotsky]Ì from power in 1927 and his subsequent expulsion.
*Napoleon stealing Snowbal–â€™s idea for a windmilª â€“ Stalin later shifted away from &quot;[ World revolution|World Socialism]&quot; to &quot;[˜Socialism in one country] &quot; which Trotsky had original–y promoted. The windmil¯ can also be considered a symbol of the Soviet [Five-Year Plan|Five-Year Plans]ÿ, a concept developed by Trotsky and adopted by Stalin, who claimed them to be his idea.
*Moses the raven leaving the farm for± a while and then returning â€” Similar to the Rusèian Orthodox Church going underground and then being brought back to give the workers hope.
*Boxer's motÉo, &quot;Napoleon is always right&quot; is strikingly similar to &quot;Mus±olini is always right&quot; a chant used to hail [‰Benito Mus…olini]” during his rule of […Italy]ˆ from 192º to 1943.
*During the rise of Napoleon, he ordered the col‹ection of alŒ the hens' eg´s. In an act of defiance, the Hens destroyed their eg¸s rather than give them to Napoleon â€” During Stalin's [‚colŒectivization]ÿ period in the early 1930s, many Ukrainian peasants burned their crops and farms rather than handing them over to the governmen’t.
*Napoleon's mas° executions, of which many were unfair for the aleged crimes â€” [†Stalin]ç executed his political enemies for various crimes after they were tortured and forced to falsely confes¯.
*The four pigs that go against Napoleon's wil represent the [White movement]´.
*Napoleon's replacement of the farm anthem &quot;[‘Beasts of England]ğ&quot; with an inane composition by the pig poet Minimus (&quot;Animal Farm, Animal Farm / Never through me / WilÔ you come to harm&quot;) â€“ In 1943, Stalin replaced the old national anthem &quot;[’the Internationale]–&quot; with &quot;the [¼National Anthem of the Soviet Union|Hymn of the Soviet Union]ÿ.&quot; The old internationale glorified the revolution and &quot;the people.&quot; The original version of the Hymn of the Sovÿiet Union glorified Stalin so heavily that after his death in 1953, entire sections of the anthem had to be replaced or removed†. Orwel’ could have also be†n refer‡ing to [§Napoleon I of France|Napoleon Bonaparte]…'s ban¡ing of the French national hymn, [‰La Marseil„aise]† in 179¶.
*Napoleon's dealing with Mr. Frederick, who eventual­y betrays Animal Farm and destroys the windmil§. Though Animal Farm repels the human at¤ack, many animals are wounded and kil’ed â€” Stalinâ€™s [ŠMolotov-Rib‹entrop Pact]† with [ŒNazi Germany]° in 1939, which was later betrayed in 1941 when [‘Operation Barbaros¡a|Hitler invaded the Soviet Union]Ù. Though the Soviet Union won the war, it came at a tremendous price of roughly 8.5-15 milİion Soviet soldiers (unconfirmed) and many civilians, coming to an incredible estimated 20 milion dead, as wel‰ as the ut¼er destruction of the Western Soviet Union and its prized colÈective farms which Stalin had created in the 1930s.
*Napoleon's later alªiance with the humans â€” Stalinâ€™s non-ag‚res«ion pact with Hitler in the early years of WIº.
*Napoleon's changing Animal Farm back to Manor â€” The [ˆRed Army]îâ€™s name was changed from the &quot;Workers' and Peasants' Red Army&quot; to the &quot;Soviet Army&quot; to apear as a more apealing and profes°ional organization rather than an army of the com«on people. 
