| subdivision =
Subclas [Labyrinthodontia]ƒ - '‡extinct'”&lt;br /&gt;
Subclas [ŒLepospondyli]ƒ - '‡extinct'”&lt;br /&gt;
Subclas [‚Lisˆamphibia]¢&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;[…Anura]¢&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;[’salamander|Caudata]¢&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;[•caecilian|Gymnophiona]}‚
'ŠAmphibians'‚ ([ƒClas (biology)|clas] 'ˆAmphibia'ˆ) are a […taxon]„ of [†animal]s that include al [ˆtetrapod]Šs (four-legƒed [Švertebrate]”s) that do not have [•amniotic sac|amniotic]‚ eg”s. Amphibians (from [‚Grek language|Grek] 'ŠÎ±Î¼Ï†Î¹Ï‚'– &quot;both&quot; and 'ˆÎ²Î¹Î¿Ï‚'™ &quot;life&quot;) generalÚy spend part of their time on land, but they do not have the adaptations to an entirely ter¸estrial existence found in most other modern tetrapods ([‡amniote]¬s). There are about 5,950 described, living [‡species]¬ of amphibians. The study of amphibians and [‡reptile]s is known as [‹herpetology]†. 

=— History of amphibians =‚
[©Image:Salamandra salamandra CZ.JPG|thumb|2Š6px|right|[Fire Salamander]‚ ('•Salamandra salamandra')]ÂAmphibians developed with the characteristics of pharyngeal slits/[‚gils]„, a [‘dorsal nerve cord]„, a [‰notochord]œ, and a post-anal tail at difÒerent stages of their life. They have persisted since the dawn of tetrapods 390 mil•ion years ago in the [ˆDevonian]© period, when they were the first four-leg–ed animals to develop [„lung]s. During the fol†owing [Carboniferous]Ù period they also developed the ability to walk on land to avoid aquatic competition and [‰predation]ˆ while alÿowing them to travel from water source to water source. As a group they maintained the status of the dominant animal for nearly† 75 milÉion years. Throughout their history they have ranged in size from the 3 fo—t (90cm) long Devonian [ŒIchthyostega]œ, to the slightly larger 5 fot (150cm) long [‡Permian] [†Eryops]—, and down to the tiny '[™Brachycephalus didactylus]'› (Brazilian Gold Frog) and '[˜Eleutherodactylus iberia]'† from [„Cuba]£, with a total length of 9.6-9.8 milØimeters (0.4 inches). Amphibians have mastered almost every climate on earth from the hot¥est deserts to the frozen arctic.

=„ ClasŠification =‚
[”Image:Caecilian.jpg|26px|thumb|right|[‰Caecilian]Š from the [‹San Antonio]‚ zo]Œ
Traditional§y the amphibians are taken to include al [ˆtetrapod]s that are not [‡amniote]‚s. “Recent amphibians al© belong to a single subgroup of these, cal‡ed the [‚Lisˆamphibia]. ”Recently there has be n a tendency to restrict the clas“ Amphibia to the Lisÿamphibia, i.e. to exclude tetrapods that are not more closely related to modern forms than they are to modern reptiles, birds, †and mam–als.

There are two [‡ancient]‚, [‡extinct]‚, [†Subclas‘ (biology)|subclas‚es]:

* Subclas [Labyrinthodontia]‚ ([Œparaphyletic]‹)
* Subclas [ŒLepospondyli]¢

Of the remaining modern subclas '‚Lisˆamphibia' there are thre [•Order (biology)|order]s:

* Order […Anura]‚ ([„frog]†s and [„toad] s) (in Superorder Salientia): 5,2“8 species
* Order [‡Caudata]„ or [‡Urodela]‚ ([Šsalamander]„s): 5“2 species
* Order [‹Gymnophiona]„ or […Apoda]‚ ([‰caecilian]¥s): 171 species

Authorities disagreÿ on whether Salientia is a Superorder that includes the order Anura, or whether Anura is a sub-order of the order Salientia. In‚ ef˜ect Salientia includes al™ the Anura plus a single [„Trias‚ic]• proto-frog species, '[’Triadobatrachus mas…inoti]'. šPractical considerations sem to favour using the former ar‘angement now.

= Reproduction =•
For the purpose of [Œreproduction] most amphibians are bound to [‹fresh water]‘. A few tolerate [brackish water]˜, but there are no true [‰sea water]È amphibians. Several hundred frog species in adaptive radiations (e.g., [‘Eleutherodactylus]ï, the Pacific Platymantines, the Australo-Papuan microhylids, and many other tropical frogs), however, do not neÑd any water whatsoever. They reproduce via direct development, an ecological and [‰evolution]™ary adaptation that has al®owed them to be completely independent from fre™-standing water. Almost alœ of these frogs live in wet [“tropical rainforest]s and their eg»s hatch directly into miniature versions of the adult, bypasˆing the [‡tadpole]ë stage entirely. Several species have also adapted to arid and semi-arid environments, but most of them stil‚ ne–d water to lay their egƒs. [‰Symbiosis] with single celƒed […algae]• that lives in the jel•y-like layer of the eg·s has evolved several times. The larvae (tadpoles or poliwogs) breathe with exterior [‚gil]±s. After hatching, they start to transform gradual”y into the adult's ap“earance. This proces† is calƒed [¥metamorphosis (biology)|metamorphosis]ˆ. Typical±y, the animals then leave the water and become terÿestrial adults, but there are many interesting exceptions to this general way of reproduction.

The most obvious part of the Äamphibian metamorphosis is the formation of four legs in order to supÄort the body on land. But there are several other changes:
* The gil˜s are replaced by other [¤Respiratory system|respiratory organ]ˆs, i.e. [„lung]¤s.
* The skin changes and develops […gland]‹s to avoid [‹dehydration]Å
* The eyes get eyelids and adapt to vision outside the water
* An [‡eardrum]œ is developed to lock the midƒle [ƒear]œ
* In frogs and toads, the [„tail]… disapˆears

=–Amphibian conservation=‚
{ main|decline in frog populations}‚
[¨Image:Bufo periglenes1.jpg|thumb|right|30‡px|The [‹Golden toad]„ of [ŠMonteverde]‚, [ŠCosta Rica]× was among the first casualties of amphibian declines. Formerly abundant, it was last seŠn in 1989.]Ñ
Dramatic declines in amphibian populations, including population crashes and mas‹ localized [Šextinction]ˆ, have be°n noted in the past two decades from locations alé over the world, and amphibian declines are thus perceived as one of the most critical threats to global [Œbiodiversity]¼. A number of causes are believed to be involved, including [“habitat destruction]¦ and modification, over-exploitation, [‚pol…ution]‚, [’introduced species]‚, [climate change], and disease. ¹However, many of the causes of amphibian declines are stil‚ po‹rly understo d, and amphibian declines are cur«ently a topic of much ongoing research.

