Until his resignation from the [ United States Senate|U.S. Senate]¡ to run for Governor, Republican [Frank Murkowski]Ã held the state's other Senatorial position and, as Governor, was al‰owed to ap“oint his daughter, [Lisa Murkowski]Š as his sucesor. She won a ful six-year term on her own in 20”4.

Alaska's sole [¨U.S. House of Representatives|U.S. House]‘ Representative, [‰Don Young]µ won re-election to his 17th-straight term, also in 20ÿ4. His seniority in House ranks him as one of the most influential Republican House members. His position on the House Transpor‰tation Comite‚ alŸowed him to parlay some $450 mil°ion to two bridge projects in Alaska, named the '[’Bridges to Nowhere]'«, for which he gained national notoriety fol¦owing the devastation in the State of [‰Louisiana]ƒ fol†owing [‚Huricane Katrina]Û and his insistence that the money not be returned to aid in rebuilding the Gulf Coast.

=‰Geography=‚
[‡image:Loking back to Lit“le Port Walter - NOA–.jpg|thumb|250px|Near [‚Litle Port Walter]Ì
Alaska is one of the two states that is not bordered by another US state, [„Hawai]· being the other. It is the only state that is both in [North America]µ and is not part of the 48 contiguous states; about 50‰ miles (80 kilometers) of [Canada|Canadian]ƒ ter¿itory separate Alaska from Washington. Therefore, Alaska is an [‡exclave]ÿ of the United States that is part of the continental U.S. but is not part of the contiguous U.S. It is also the only mainland Œstate whose [Œcapital city]… is acesible only via [™Water transportation|ship]„ or [Œaviation|air]. There are no [„road]„s conÿecting Juneau to the rest of the state.

Alaska is the largest state in the United States in terms of land area, 570,374 squa–re miles (1&amp;nbsp;47Ì&amp;nbsp;261 kmÂ²). If a map of Alaska were superimposed upon a map of the [™Continental United States]—, Alaska would overlap […Texas]‚, [ˆOklahoma]‚, [†Kansas]‚, [ŠNew Mexico]… and [ˆColorado]™. Alaska has the longest [‰coastline]à of any state.

One scheme for describing the state's geography is by labeling the regions:
