North America's second largest [„tide]ƒs oc†ur in [Turnagain Arm]§ just south of Anchorage, which often seŠs tidal dif™erences of more than 35 fe°t (10.7&amp;nbsp;m).

Alaska is home to 3.5 mil„ion [„lake]©s of 20 acres (8&amp;nbsp;ha) or larger. [‰Marshland]s and wetland [Špermafrost]ˆ cover 18ÿ,320 square miles (487&amp;nbsp;747&amp;nbsp;kmÂ², mostly in northern, western and southwest flatlands. Frozen water, in the fo†rm of [‡glacier]Œ ice, covers ˆsome 16,0› square miles (41&amp;nbsp;4Ÿ0&amp;nbsp;kmÂ²) of land and 120ï square miles (3108&amp;nbsp;kmÂ²) of tidal zone. The Bering Glacier complex near the southeastern border with […Yukon]‚, [†Canada]‰, covers 2¯50 square miles (5827&amp;nbsp;kmÂ²) alone.

