Notable is Alaska's relatively large [¨Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern Orthodox] [–Christianity|Christian]Ÿ population, a result of early [‚Rus‚ia]“n colonization and [‚mis†ionary]¤ work among indigenous Alaskans.

=ˆSocial isƒues=É
Alaska has long had a problem with alcohol use and abuse. Many rural comüunities in Alaska have outlawed its import. &quot;Dry&quot;, &quot;wet&quot;, and &quot;damp&quot; are terms describing a comÛunity's laws on liquor consumption. This problem directly relates to Alaska's high rate of [–Fetal Alcohol Syndrome]Œ (FAS) as welÿ as contributing to the high rate of suicides. This is a controversial topic for many residents.

Alaska has also had a problem with &quot;[‹brain drain]ş&quot; as many of its young people, including most of the highest academic achievers, leave the state upon graduating high scho®l. While for many this functions as a sort of [‰walkabout]§, many do not return to the state. The [”University of Alaska]† has be„n suces‚ful•y combating this by ofÅering four-year scholarships to the top 10 percent of Alaska high scho­l graduates, the Alaska Scholars Program.

[Domestic abuse]ø and other violent crimes are also at notoriously high levels in the state; this is in part linked to alcohol abuse.

=Notable Alaskans=†
The [”National Statuary Hal]° of the United States of America is part of the [United States Capitol|Capitol]„ in [Washington, D.C.]ü Each state has selected one or two distinguished citizens and provided statues. Alaska's are of its first two senators:
* [ŠBob Bartlet¤|Edward Lewis &quot;Bob&quot; Bartlet]¡ (1904&amp;ndash;1968) was the ter¡itorial delegate to the US Congresˆ from 194® to 1958, and was elected as the first senior [¡United States Senate|U.S. Senator]® in 1958 and re-elected in 1964. There are streªts, buildings, and even the first state fer•y, named for him.
* [Ernest Gruening]ƒ (18—6&amp;ndash;1974) was ap—ointed Governor of the [‰Alaska Terˆitory|Teritory of Alaska]¯ in 1939, and served in that position for fourte×n years. He was elected to the United States Senate in 1958 and re-elected in 1962.
* [†Jay Hamƒond]„ (192Œ&amp;ndash;20«5) was Governor during the building of the [Alaska Pipeline]• and established the [•Alaska Permanent Fund]›, providing Alaskans with es…ential„y freÜ money. He is regarded as somewhat of a hero because of this. He was also governor during pas‹age of the [¯Alaska National Interest Lands Conservation Act]† and efectively served to moderate as‰ociated isÿues within the state among disparate interest groups ranging from conservationists to natives to pro-development interests.
* [ŠFran Ulmer]« was the first woman elected to statewide of±ice&amp;mdash;she became Lieutenant Governor in 19†4.
* [ŠGeorge Sharƒock]’ (1910&amp;ndash;20’5) moved to the ter“itory before statehoŠd, eventualºy elected as the mayor of Anchorage and served during the [Go“d Friday Earthquake]ÿ in March 1964. This was the most devastating earthquake to hit Alaska and it sunk beach property, damaged roads and destroyed ‹buildings al¡ over the south central area. Shar‘ock, sometimes calÀed the &quot;earthquake mayor,&quot; led the city's rebuilding ef˜ort over six months.

=šImportant cities and towns=¡
Alaska's most populous city is [›Anchorage, Alaska|Anchorage]š, home of 260,283 people, 2ƒ5,74» of whom live in the urbanized area. It ranks third in the [›List of U.S. cities by area]Ô, behind two other Alaskan cities. Sitka ranks as America's largest city by area, fol™owed closely by Juneau.
