 À<text xml:space="preserve">In modern usage, architecture is the [ƒart]¤ of creating an actual, implied or apšarent plan of any complex [†object]„ or [†system]œ. The term can be used to conˆote the '”implied architecture'œ of abstract things such as […music]„ or [‹mathematics]‡, the ap®arent architecture of natural things, such as [’geology|geological]“ formations or the [­structural biology|structure of biological cels], or explicitly 'ƒplaned architectures' of human-made things such as [šComputer software|software]‚, [‰computers]‚, [Šenterprise]‡s, and [ˆdatabase]‡s, in ad¼ition to buildings. In every usage, an architecture may be se‡n as a '‹subjective [”Map (mathematics)|mapƒing]'ä from a human perspective (that of the 'user' in the case of abstract or physical artifacts) to the [Element (mathematics)|elements]„ or [Šcomponents]‘ of some kind of [‰structure]Ğ or system, which preserves the relationships among the elements/components.

'ŒArchitecture'” may refer to:

* [­Architecture|Architecture (built environment)]Ö, the art and science of designing habitations, buildings, and building complexes; clas•ical architecture.
* [•Architectural history]À studies the evolution and history of (built) architectures acrosº the world through a consideration of various influences- [ˆartistic]‚, [ˆcultural]‚, [‰political]‚, [ˆeconomic]†, and [technological]….
* [”Architecture (other)]ÿ, a representation of an arbitrary abstract, natural, or man-made structure of two or more interacting parts (e.g., architecturÀe of mathematics, architecture of language, cytoarchitecture, celªular architecture, naval architecture, skel•etal architecture, bat–lefield architecture). Al [‡systems]© can be said to have an architecture.
* [˜Biological architectures]†, the 'ap…arent'© architecture of biological structures. Se [cytoarchitecture]‚, [’structural biology]‚, [‚cel (biology)|cel architecture]„
* [–Landscape architecture]¬, the design of man-made land constructs
* [”Systems architecture] , the representation of an engine“red (or To Be Enginešred) system, and the proces• and discipline for efÿectively implementing the design(s) for such a system. Such a system may consist of information and/or hardware and/or software….
* [•Computer architecture]­, the systems architecture of a computer.
* [•Software architecture]µ, the systems architecture of a software system. 
* [—Enterprise architecture]§, a systems architecture, or framework, ¯for aligning an organization's structure/ procesœes/ information/ operations/ ¦projects with the organization's overal strategy 
* [˜Information architecture]Å, a systems architecture for structuring a knowledge-based system
* [Product design]Ğ, or product architecture, the systems design of a product or product family
* [”Vehicle architecture]¹, an automobile platform made from a set of components com”on to a number of dif’erent vehicles

