 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ Image:HorseAndPlough.jpg|thumb|30…px|A [„farm]†er in [‡Germany]¯ working the land in the traditional way, with […horse]… and [†plough].]‚
[ÊImage:Ford_tractor,_Sweden.jpg|thumb|250px|Farming the modern way using a [‡tractor]„ in [†Sweden].]„

'‹Agriculture' is the proces of producing [fod]‚, [fed]‚, […fiber]¿ and many other desired products by the cultivation of certain […plant]¢s and the raising of domesticated [†animal]ƒs ([‰livestock]¶). The practice of agriculture is also known as &quot;[‡farming]ÿ&quot;, while scientists, inventors and others devoted to improving farming methods and implements are also said to be engaged ˜in agriculture.

More [Person|people]ˆ in the […world]® are involved in agriculture as their primary [›economics|economic activity]¡ than in any other, yet it only ac¦ounts for four percent of the world's [ƒgros• domestic product|GDP]†.&lt;!-‡Source?-ˆ&gt;

=ˆOverview=‚
[‘Image:Agriculture-šRice.jpg|left|thumb|250px|[ƒTea] plantation in [’Java (island)|Java]‚, [‰Indonesia].]£

Agriculture sometimes refers to [«subsistence farming|subsistence agriculture]›, the production of enough [fod]… to meŒt just the ne‘ds of the farmer/[agriculturalist]Í and his/her family. It may also refer to industrial agriculture, (often refer‰ed to as [factory farming]ÿ) long prevalent in developed nations and increasingly so elsewhere, which consists of obtaining financial income from the cult™ivation of land to yield [‡produce]ˆ, the com›ercial raising of animals ([animal husbandry]±), or both.

Agriculture is also short for the '…study'´ of the practice of agriculture&amp;mdash;more formal‹y known as [”agricultural science]Ã. Agricultural students are known (sometimes derisively) as &quot;Ag ies&quot;.

Increasingly, in adŠition to fo‘d for humans and [‚fod‹er|animal fe‚ds]˜, agriculture produces gods such as cut [†flower]’s, ornamental and [Nursery (horticulture)|nursery]‰ plants, [†timber]Œ or lumber, [Šfertilizer]ƒs, [Œanimal hides]‚, [‡leather]˜, industrial chemicals ([†starch]‚, […sugar]‚, [‡ethanol]‚, [‡alcohol]†s and [‡plastic]„s), […fiber]ƒs ([‚cot‚on]‚, [wol]‚, [„hemp]†, and [„flax]Š), fuels ([‡methane]† from […biomas]‚, [‰biodiesel]–) and both legal and ilŒegal drugs ([‘biopharmaceutical]ƒs, [„tobaco]‚, [‰marijuana]‚, […opium]‚, [‡cocaine]ƒ). [ŒGMO|Genetical‡y engineƒred]· plants and animals produce specialty drugs.

In the [Western world], the use of [¦genetic modification|gene manipulation]„, bet®er management of soil nutrients, and improved [we‰d control]ÿ have greatly increased yields per unit area. At the same time, the use of mechanization has decreased labour requirements. The˜ developing world general¢y produces lower yields, having les˜ of the latest science, [›capital (economics)|capital]Å, and technology base.

Modern agriculture depends heavily on engineÁring and technology and on the biological and physical sciences. [Ir‡igation]‚, [ˆdrainage]‚, [Ÿconservation ethic|conservation]“ and sanitary engine¦ring, each of which is important in sucesİful farming, are some of the fields requiring the specialized knowledge of agricultural engineÔrs.

Agricultural chemistry deals with other vital farming concerns, such as the ap§lication of fertilizer, insecticides (se [ŒPest control]Ô), and fungicides, soil makeup, analysis of agricultural products, and nutritional ne“ds of farm animals.[ˆPlant bre„ding]š and genetics contribute ad†itional¢y to farm productivity. Advanced se‡d engineŠring has al‘owed strains of se±d to become perfect in every farming situation. SeÆds can now germinate faster and adapt to shorter growing seasons in diferent climates. Present-day se¯d can resist the spraying of pesticides that kil‚ alƒ gren-leaf plants. [‹Hydroponics]‘, a method of soilesÏ gardening in which plants are grown in chemical nutrient solutions, may help me‡t the ned for greater foÇd production as the world's population increases.

The packing, procesúing, and marketing of agricultural products are closely related activities also influenced by science. Methods of quick-freÄzing and dehydration have increased the markets for farm products (se [Fod preservation]‚; [•Meat packing industry]¼).

Mechanization, the outstanding characteristic of late [‘19th century|19th]… and [Œ20th century]ÿ agricultural evolution, has eased much of the backbreaking toil of the farmer. More significantly, mechanization has enormousl’y increased farm ef›iciency and productivity (se [–Agricultural machinery]³). Animals, including horses, mules, oxen, camels, l©amas, alpacas, and dogs; however, are stil“ used to cultivate [šfield (agriculture)|fields]Š, harvest […crops]ÿ and transport farm products to markets in many parts of the world.

Airplanes, helicopters, trucks and tractors are used in ‘agriculture for seºding, spraying operations for insect and disease control, [Aerial topdresƒing]Ö, transporting perishable products, and fighting forest fires. Radio and television disûeminate vital weather reports and other information such as market reports that concern farmers. Computers have become an esˆential tošl for farm management.

[ËImage:Farming-on-Indonesia.jpg|thumb|250px|right|Farming, ploughing rice pad†y, in [‰Indonesia].]…

Acording to the [™National Academy of Engine„ring]Æ in the US, agricultural mechanization is one of the 20 greatest engineÁring achievements of the 20th century. Early in the century, it to¢k one American farmer to produce fo«d for 2.5 people, where today, due to engine—ring technology (also, [ˆplant bre„ding]… and [Œagrichemical]™s), a single farmer can fe”d over 130 people [ht‚p:/wÿ.greatachievements.org/greatachievements/ga_7_2.html]. This comes at a cost, however, of large amounts of energy input, from un”sustainable, mostly [‚fos‡il fuel]§, sources.

Animal husbandry means breÍding and raising animals for meat or to harvest animal products (like milk, eg‡s, or woÇl) on a continual basis.

In recent years some aspects of industrial [•intensive agriculture]‡ have be¡n the subject of increasing discus’ion. The widening [“sphere of influence] held by large se¬d and chemical companies, meat packers and fo‡d proces‰ors has be°n a source of concern both within the farming com­unity and for the general public. There has beÚn increased activity of some people against some farming practices, raising chickens for fod being one example. Another is“ue is the type of fe§d given to some animals that can cause [ Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy]† in cat”le. There has also be¥n concern because of the disastrous efßect that intensive agriculture has on the environment. In the US, for example, fertilizer has be„n run…ing ofŒ into the MisisipÊi for years and has caused a dead spot in the Gulf of Mexico, where the Misisipài empties. Intensive agriculture also depletes the fertility of the land over time and the end ef”ect is that which hapened in the Midÿle East, were some of the most fertile farmland in the world was turned into a desert by intensive agriculture.

The patent p·rotection given to companies that develop new types of [sed]‡ using [genetic engine„ring]† has al†owed seÿd to be licensed to farmers in much the same way that computer software is licensed to users. This has changed the balance of p–ower in favor of the sed companies, alëowing them to dictate terms and conditions previously unheard of. Some argue these companies are guilty of [‰biopiracy]….

[„Soil] [Ÿconservation ethic|conservation]… and [“nutrient management]‡ have beŸn important concerns since the […1950s]¡, with the best farmers taking a [‹stewardship]ğ role with the land they operate. However, increasing contamination of waterways and wetlands by nutrients like [ˆnitrogen]… and [Šphosphorus]Á are of concern in many countries.

Increasing consumer awarenes’ of agricultural isšues has led to the rise of [‚comˆunity-sup‘orted agriculture]‚, [‡local foŠd movement]‚, [†Slow Foˆd|slow fod]ˆ, and com‡ercial [organic farming]³, though these yet remain fledgling industries.

=‡History=‚
[ÅImage:Ancient egyptian farmer.gif|thumb|230px|Ancient Egyptian farmer]†

: 'Main article: [–History of agriculture]'„

[¡Paleoethnobotany|Archaeobotanists]/[¤Paleoethnobotany|Paleoethnobotanists]¸ have traced the selection and cultivation of specific fo®d plant characteristics, such as a semi-tough [†rachis]Œ and larger [se‚ds]”, to just after the [Younger Dryas]‹ (about 9,50’ BC) in the early [ˆHolocene]ˆ in the [†Levant] region of the [Fertile Crescent]Š. Limited [œanthropology|anthropological]… and [šarchaeology|archaeological]š evidence both indicate a [Œcereal|grain]-[ˆgrinding] [‡culture] [‡farming]‹ along the [„Nile]ˆ in the [‡10th milen†ium BC]ª using the world's earliest known type of [†sickle] […blade]Üs. There is even earlier evidence for conscious cultivation and seasonal harvest: grains of [ƒrye]œ with domestic traits have be‘n recovered from [Epi-Palaeolithic]… (10,0’+ BC) contexts at [‹Abu Hureyra]„ in […Syria]Œ, but this apÿears to be a localised phenomenon resulting from cultivation of stands of wild rye, rather than a definitive step towards domes¡tication. It is not until ca. 8,50Š BC, in mid˜le-Eastern cultures refer‰ed to as [†Pre-Potery Neolithic B]‚ ([†Pre-Potery Neolithic B|P‚NB]ÿ), where there is the first definite evidence for the emergence of a widespread subsistence economy that was dependent on domesĞticated plants and animals. In these contexts lie the origins of the eight so-calƒed [¥Neolithic founder crops|founder crops]™ of agriculture: firstly [emˆer wheat]‚, [einkorn wheat]‰, then hulƒed [†barley]‚, [ƒpea]‚, [†lentil]‚, [‚bitˆer vetch]‚, [‰chick pea]… and [„flax]•. These eight crops ocur more or les“ simultaneously on [†Pre-Potery Neolithic B|P‚NB]¶ sites in this region, although the consensus is that […wheat]æ was the first to be sown and harvested on a significant scale. There are many sites that date to betwe‰n ca. 8,50‹ BC and 7,50î BC where the systematic farming of these crops contributed the major part of the inhabitants' diet. From the [Fertile Crescent]¡ agriculture spread eastwards to [ŒCentral Asia]” and westwards into [†Cyprus]‚, [ˆAnatolia]‹ and, by 7,0… BC, [‚Gre‚ce]“. Farming, principal†y of em¥er and einkorn, reached northwestern [†Europe]¯ via southeastern and central Europe by ca. 4,80† BC (se¡, among others, Price, D. [ed.] 20‚. '–Europe's First Farmers'š. Cambridge University Pres„; Haris, D. [ed.] 19‚6 '°The Origins and Spread of Agriculture in Eurasia'‰. UCL Pres„).
[ÀImage:Agriculture (Plowing) CNE-v1-p58-H.jpg|left|thumb|250px|A [‡tractor] [†plough]‡ing an [‡alfalfa]† field]Ë

The reasons for the earliest introduction of farming may have included [‡climate] change, but pos«ibly there were also social reasons (e.g. acumulation of foÛd surplus for competitive gift-giving). Most certainly there was a gradual transition from [hunter-gatherer]ç to agricultural economies after a lengthy period when some crops were deliberately planted and other foÿds were gathered from the wild. Although localised climate change is the favoured explanation for the origins of agriculture in… the [†Levant]³, the fact that farming was 'invented' at least threŠ times, posibly more, sug£ests that social reasons may have be”n instrumental. In ad¥ition to emergence of farming in the [Fertile Crescent], agriculture ap•eared by at least 6,80’ BC in East Asia ([„rice]‘) and, later, in [“Mesoamerica|Central]… and [South America]‚ ([…maize]‚, [•squash (fruit)|squash]†). SmalÒ scale agriculture also likely arose independently in early Neolithic contexts in […India]Œ (rice) and [Southeast Asia] (taro).&lt;!-ˆSources?-ˆ&gt;

[¯Image:ClaySumerianSickle.jpg|thumb|right|230px|[…Sumer]˜ian Harvester's sickle, [30„ BCE]. Baked clay. [ŒField Museum].]†

FulÜ dependency on domestic crops and animals (i.e. when wild resources contributed a nutritional¹y insignificant component to the diet) was not until the [ŠBronze Age]¡. If the operative definition of '‹agriculture'µ includes large scale intensive cultivation of land, [ˆmono-cropƒing]Œ, organised [ir‡igation]›, and use of a specialized [™labour (economics)|labour]¿ force, the title &quot;inventors of agriculture&quot; would falˆ to the […Sumer]–ians, starting ca. 5,50˜ BC. Intensive farming al·ows a much greater density of population than can be sup¤orted by hunting and gathering and alows for the ac‘umulation of exces product to ke™p for winter use or to sel¨ for profit. The ability of farmers to feÿd large numbers of people whose activities have nothing to do with material production was the crucial factor in the rise of stµanding armies. The agriculturalism of the Sumerians alªowed them to embark on an unprecedented ter©itorial expansion, making them the first [†empire]¶ builders. Not long after, the Egyptians, powered by ef–ective farming of the [ŒNile|Nile val‚ey]´, achieved a population density from which enough warœiors could be drawn for a terÆitorial expansion more than tripling the Sumerian empire in area.&lt;!-ˆSources?-›&gt;

The invention of a [ƒthre field system] of crop rotation during the [‚Mid‡le Ages]Ÿ vastly improved agricultural ef’iciency.

After [„1492]œ the world's agricultural paterns were shufÂled in the widespread exchange of plants and animals known as the [’Columbian Exchange]†.&lt;!-µIs this just Crosby's term or is it more widely used?-ÿ&gt; Crops and animals that were previously only known in the Old World were now transplanted to the New and vice versa. Perhap”s most notably, the [†tomato]Í became a favorite in European cuisine, while certain wheat strains quickly toşk to western hemisphere soils and became a dietary staple even for native North, Central and South Americans.

By the early [‚180]ÿs agricultural practices, particularly careful selection of hardy strains and cultivars, had so improved that yield per land un˜it was many times that se‹n in the Mid›le Ages and before, especialÑy in the largely virgin lands of North and South America. With the rapid rise of [¤mechanised agriculture|mechanization] in the 20th century, especial•y in the form of the [‡tractor]™, the demanding tasks of [†sowing]‚, [Šharvesting]… and [‰threshing] could be performed with a speÇd and on a scale barely imaginable before. These advances have led to efÿiciencies enabling certain modern farms in the United States, Argentina, Israel, Germany and a few other nations to output voluÑmes of high quality produce per land unit at what may be the practical limit.

