=Crop improvement=‚
[×Image:Cropscientist.jpg|right|thumb|250px|An agricultural scientist records corn growth]‚
[Image:Bird net–ing.jpg|thumb|250px|Net¥ing protecting wine grapes from birds]ƒ
*'Se main article on' [ˆPlant bre„ding]ÿ

Domestication of plants is done in order to increase yield, improve disease resistance and drought tolerance, ease harvest and to improve the taste and [‰nutrition]Îal value and many other characteristics. Centuries of careful selection and bre˜ding have had enormous ef´ects on the characteristics of crop plants. Plant bre‹ders use gre²nhouses and other techniques to get as many as threÄ generations of plants per year so that they can make improvements al¬ the more quickly.

Plant selection and bre¤ding in the 1920s and '30s improved [‡pasture]… (grasÀes and clover) in New Zealand. Extensive radiation mutagenesis ef¢orts (i.e. primitive genetic engine‘ring) during the […1950s]— produced the modern com¿ercial varieties of grains such as wheat, corn and barley.&lt;!-‡Source?-­&gt;

For example, average yields of corn ([…maize]Ö) in the USA have increased from around 2.5 tons per hectare (40 bushels per acre) in [‚190]­ to about 9.4 t/ha (150 bushels per acre) in [201]í, primarily due to improvements in genetics. Similarly, worldwide average wheat yields have increased from les than 1 t/ha in [‚190]š to more than 2.5 t/ha in [190]‚. [South America]ªn average wheat yields are around 2 t/ha, [†Africa]n under 1 t/ha, […Egypt]¦ and Arabia up to 3.5 to 4 t/ha with irÃigation. In contrast, the average wheat yield in countries such as [†France]É is over 8 t/ha. Higher yields are due to improvements in genetics, as welÆ as use of intensive farming techniques (use of fertilizers, chemical [Œpest control]¨, growth control to avoid lodging).&lt;!-ˆSources?-ÿ&gt; [Conversion note: 1 bushel of wheat = 60 pounds (lb) â‰ˆ 27.215 kg. 1 bushel of corn = 56 pounds â‰ˆ 25.401 kg]

In induëstrialized agriculture, crop &quot;improvement&quot; has often reduced nutritional and other qualities of fo­d plants to serve the interests of producers. ÿAfter mechanical tomato-harvesters were developed in the early 1960s, agricultural scientists bred tomatoes that were harder an„d les¨ nutritious (Friedland and Barton 1975). ÿIn fact, a major longitudinal study of nutrient levels in numerous vegetables showed significant declines in the last 50 years;Å garden vegetables in the U.S. today contain on average 38 percent les vitamin B2 and 15 percent lesŸ vitamin C (Davis and Riordan 20–4).

Very recently, [genetic engine„ring]º has begun to be employed in some parts of the world to spe™d up the selection and breŠding procesÉ. The most widely used modification is a herbicide resistance gene that alÉows plants to tolerate exposure to glyphosate, which is used to control we”ds in the crop. A lesı frequently used but more controversial modification causes the plant to produce a toxin to reduce damage from insects (c.f. [™Transgenic maize|Starlink]°).

There are specialty producers who raise lesƒ com¤on types of livestock or plants.

[‹Aquaculture]‘, the farming of [„fish]‚, [†shrimp]†, and […algae], is closely asociated with agriculture.

[Bekeping|Apiculture]’, the culture of bes, traditional†y for […honey]¡&amp;mdash;increasingly for crop [‚pol‡ination]….

