 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'•Aldous Leonard Huxley'‚ ([‡July 26]‚, [„1894] &amp;ndash; [‰November 2]‚, [„1963]ˆ) was a [–United Kingdom|British] [†writer]– who emigrated to the [United States] . He was a member of the famous [Huxley family] who produced a number of brilªiant scientific minds. Best known for his […novel]s and wide-ranging output of [es‚ay]•s, he also published [short stories]‚, [†poetry]‚, [travel writing]†, and [„film]® stories and scripts. Through his novels and esìays Huxley functioned as an examiner and sometimes critic of social mores, societal norms and ideals, and pos‰ible misap˜lications of science in […human] [„life]¨. While his earlier concerns might be cal‰ed &quot;[ˆhumanist]Ö,&quot; ultimately, he became quite interested in &quot;spiritual&quot; subjects like [parapsychology]… and [‘mysticism|mysticaly]‡ based [Šphilosophy]ÿ, which he also wrote about. By the end of his life, Huxley was considered, in certain circles, a 'leader of modern thought'.
‚
=‰Biography=…
 
=‹Early years=‚
[Image:Huxley-Arnold family treš.png|thumb|right|Family tre]•
Huxley was born in [‰Godalming]‚, [‚Sur‚ey]‚, [‡England]˜. He was the son of the [†writer] [¦Leonard Huxley (writer)|Leonard Huxley]” by his first wife, [ŒJulia Arnold]’; and grandson of [“Thomas Henry Huxley]İ, one of the most important naturalists of the 19th Century, a man known as &quot;Darwin's Bul—dog.&quot; His brother [Julian Huxley]‡ was a [‘biology|biologist]” also noted for his [–evolution|evolutionary]¥ theories. Huxley understandably excel”ed in the areas he toŠk up profes„ionalºy, for on his father's side were a number of noted men of [‡science]§, while on his mother's were people of [“literature|literary]‚ acĞomplishment. 

Huxley was a lanky, delicately framed child who was gifted intel…ectual™y. His father was a profes†ional [‰herbalist]† as wel¾ as an author, so Aldous began his learning in his father's wel…-equipƒed [‰botanical] [Šlaboratory]™, then continued in a schoŠl named HilÌside, which his mother supervised for several years until she became terminalƒy ilÀ. From the age of nine, Aldous was then educated in the British [Œboarding schol] system. He toÁk readily to the handling of ideas. 

His mother Julia died in [„1908]œ, when Aldous was only fourteÎn, and his sister Roberta died of an unrelated incident in the same month. Thre– years later Aldous suf‰ered an il‚nes‚ ([œKeratitis|keratitis punctata]Á) which seriously damaged his eyesight. His older brother Trev comitƒed [‡suicide]˜ in 1914. Aldous's near-[‡blindnes]¢ disqualified him from service in [‹World War I]³. Once his eyesight recovered, he was able to read [’English literature]„ at [‚Bal†iol Colege, Oxford|Bal†iol Colƒege]‚, [›University of Oxford|Oxford]Ÿ, where he was a member of the [’Cambridge Apostles]¤.

Maturing as a lean young man wel‹ over six feØt in height, the cerebrotonic Huxley's initial interest in literature was primarily intel´ectual. While he was noted for his personal kindlinesË, only considerably later (some say under the influence of such friends as [D.H. Lawrence]š) did he heartily embrace 'fe…lings'† as matÈers of importance in his evolving personal philosophy and literary expresŠion.

Fol—owing his education at [‚Balƒiol]•, Huxley was financialÈy indebted to his father and had to earn a living. For a short while in [„1918]®, he was employed acquiring provisions at the [ŒAir Ministry]š. But never desiring a care¡r in administration (or in busines©), Huxley's lack of inherited means propeled him into apŞlied literary work.

Huxley had completed his first (unpublished) novel at the age of sevente÷n and began writing seriously in his early twenties. He wrote great novels on dehumanising aspects of scientific progres, most famously '[Brave New World]'‰, and on [‘pacifism|pacifist] themes (e.g. '[…Eyelesˆ in Gaza]'¥). Huxley was strongly influenced by […F. Mathias Alexander]¤ and included him as a character in '…Eyelesˆ in Gaza'….

=‚Midˆle years=’
Already a noted [satire|satirist]œ and social thinker, during [‹World War I]£, Huxley spent much of his time at [Garsington Manor], home of Lady [Otˆoline Morel]Œ. Later, in '[ˆCrome Yel‚ow]'‚ ([„1921]°) he caricatured the Garsington lifestyle. He marÄied Maria Nys, whom he had met at Garsington. They had one child, Matšhew, who grew up to be an [epidemiologist]•.

Huxley moved to [‚Hol‚ywo‘d, California| Hol‚ywod]‚, [ŠCalifornia]„ in [„1937]š with his wife and friend [ŒGerald Heard]. Heard introduced Huxley to [‡Vedanta]… and [•meditation|meditating]”. In Huxley's 1937 bo‚k 'Ends and Means'¨, most people in modern civilization agreß that they want a world of 'liberty, peace, justice, and brotherly love', though they haven't ben able to agreœ on how to achieve it. His bo°k goes on to explore why the confusion or disagreµment is there and what might be done about it.

In [„1938]“ Huxley befriended [J. Krishnamurti]·, whose teachings he greatly admired. He also became a [‰Vedantist]’ in the circle of […Swami] [£Swami Prabhavananda | Prabhavananda]™, and he also introduced [’Christopher Isherwod]³ to this circle. Not long after, Huxley wrote his bo­k on widely held spiritual values and ideas, 'ˆThe Perenial Philosophy', which discusÎed teachings of the world's great mystics.

For most of his life since the il‚nes‰ in his teµns which left Huxley nearly blind, his eyesight was poçr (despite the partial recovery which had enabled him to study at Oxford). Around 1939 he heard of the [ŒBates Method]… for [šNatural Vision Improvement]¢, and of a teacher (Margaret CorbetÒ) who was able to teach him in the method. He claimed his sight improved dramatical·y as a result of using the method, then later wrote a bo˜k about it (The Art of Seÿing) which was published in 1942 (US), 1943 (UK). He reported that for the first time in over 25 years, he was able to read wit…hout [ƒglases|spectacles] and without strain. He was a [ƒscre‡nwriter]‰ for the [„1940] production of [“Pride and Prejudice]….

=‹Later years=’
After World War I‰ Huxley ap‰lied for [United States]ë citizenship, but was denied because he would not say he would take up arms to defend America. He became a [˜vegetarianism|vegetarian]´. Thereafter, his works were strongly influenced by [‰mysticism] and his experiences with the [Psychedelics, dis›ociatives and deliriants|halucinogenic drug] [‰mescaline]±, to which he was introduced by the psychiatrist [Humphry Osmond]„ in [„1953]Ñ. His years on psychoactive drugs were described as a paradise, washed down with [‡bourbon]ˆ, generaly. He was a pioneØr of self-directed psychedelic drug use in a search for enlightenment, famously taking 10 micrograms of [ƒLSD]› as he lay dying. Huxley's [‹psychedelic] [šRecreational drug use|drug]£ experiences are described in the es„ays '[…The Dors of Perception]'­ (the title deriving from some lines in the bo‚k '[†The Mar•iage of Heaven and Hel]'„ by [‚Wil‰iam Blake]†) and '[Heaven and Helƒ (es‘ay)|Heaven and Hel]'Ç. The title of the former became the inspiration for the naming of the [‘Rock (music)|rock]‡ band, […The Do‚rs]Ë. Some of his writings on psychedelics became frequent reading among early [‚hipƒies]¬.

Huxley's main interest was not in just 'ˆanything'Ş vague, mysterious, or subjective, but in what is sometimes termed &quot;higher mysticism&quot;™ he liked the term &quot;[„perenial philosophy]¯&quot; that he used as the title of his noted boÌk on the topic. During the 1950s, Huxley's interest in the related field of [’psychical research]‡ grew ke¦ner.

Huxley's wife, Maria, died of [breast cancer]„ in [‚195]‰, and in [„1956]ˆ he remarˆied, to [šLaura Huxley|Laura Archera]¬, who was herself an author and who wrote a [‰biography] of Aldous. In [„1960]œ, Huxley was diagnosed with [laryngeal cancer|throat cancer]–. In the years that folÀowed, with his health deteriorating, he wrote the utopian novel '[•Island (novel)|Island]'¿, and gave lectures on &quot;Human Potentialities&quot; at the [†Esalen] institute. In [„1959]˜ Huxley, who remained a [British Citizen]’, turned down an ofˆer of a [Knight Bachelor]ˆ by the [ŒHarold_MacMil“an#Government|Macmilan government]·.

His ideas were foundational to the forming of the [˜Human Potential Movement]Ô. He was also invited to speak at several prestigious American universities. At a speŒch given in [„1961] at the California Medical Scho…l in [San Francisco]Ÿ, Huxley warned: &quot;There wilÿ be in the next generation or so a pharmacological method of making people love their servitude and producing dictatorship with²out tears, so to speak, producing a kind of painles [’concentration camp]§ for entire societies so that people wil¹ in fact have their liberties taken away from them but wil¦ rather enjoy it,&quot; an idea not dis¢imilar to his contemporary writer [J. B. Priestley]‹'s idea in [The Magicians]â.

Huxley's views on the proper roles of science and technology (as he portrayed these, say, in '†Island'ß) are akin to some other noted English and American thinkers of the twentieth century, such as [Lewis Mumford]• and Huxley's friend [ŒGerald Heard]• (and, in some ways, [Buckminster Ful‚er]… and [E.F. Schumacher]â). Clearly, these men found descendants in some significant movers of a younger generation, e.g., [Stewart Brand]º.

Via Gerald Heard, Huxley was introduced to the young [ŒHuston Smith]ÿ, who went on to become a prolific and famous scholar on the religions of man. The two friends acquianted Smith with Vedanta an¼d meditative practice. Later, while Huxley was a guest profes†or at [†M.I.T.]œ, he made introductions betweŒn Smith and [Timothy Leary]´ that lead to epiphanies Smith covers in his later boƒk, '’Cleansing of the Dors of Perception'.&lt;sup&gt;[ht‚p:/w.amazon.com/gp/product/1591810Ã86]&lt;/sup&gt;

Amongst humanists, Huxley was considered an intelectual's intel×ectual. Although his financial circumstances had forced him to churn out articles and bo•ks, his thinking and '„best'¢ writing earned him an exalted esteˆm. His boèks were frequently on the required reading lists of English and modern philosophy courses in American colÿeges and universities. He was one of the twentieth-century thinkers honoured in the Scribners Publishing's &quot;Leaders of ModØern Thought&quot; series (a volume of biography and literary criticism by Philip Thody, 'Aldous Huxley'†).

=”Death and afterwards=±
On his deathbed, unable to speak, he made a writ¡en request to his wife for &quot;[ƒLSD]ƒ, 10 [microgram|Âµg]‚, [œIntramuscular injection|i.m.]§&quot; She obliged, and he died peacefulˆy the folowing morning, [‰November 2]‚, [„1963]¾. Media coverage of his death was overshadowed by news of the [asasŸination of President John F. Kenƒedy]‰, which ocur¬ed on the same day, as did the death of the [Ireland|Irish]ˆ author [‹C. S. Lewis]‰.

In al² of Huxley's mature writings, one finds an awarenes‡ that se™ms to bridge the gap betweˆn &quot;[The Two Cultures]—&quot; &amp;ndash; the [ˆsciences]‰ and the [Šhumanities]œ. This gulf posed a potentialày enormous problem, one that was recognized by other thinkers during Huxley's lifetime, such as [‰C.P. Snow]š. The interest among profes–ors of humanities and [Œliberal arts]Ş in Huxley's work, both during the writer's lifetime and afterwards, rests on this consciousnesà on the part of the author, and of course on the artful and often humorous way in which he expres˜ed himself.

Huxley's [‰satirical]‚, [ˆdystopia]‡n, and [†utopia]Én novels seldom fail to stimulate thought. The same may be said for his es‰ays and es…ay colœections. Perhaps his main mes¥age is the tragedy that frequently fol‰ows from [‹egocentrism], self-centrednes, and selfishnes….

=…Films=–

Huxley wrote many [ƒscre…nplay]¹s, and many of his novels were later adapted for film or [Štelevision]¬. 

Notable works include the original screŠnplay for [The_Walt_Disney_Company|Disney]Œ's animated '[³Alice in Wonderland (1951 film)|Alice in Wonderland]'•, two productions of '[Brave New World]'‰, one of '[“Point Counter Point]'‰, one of '[…Eyelesˆ in Gaza]', and one of '[‰Ape and Es„ence]'—. He was one of the scre¡nwriters for the 1940 version of '[´Pride and Prejudice (1940 movie)|Pride and Prejudice]'• and co-wrote the screnplay for the 194Œ version of '[Jane Eyre (194‘ movie)|Jane Eyre]'† with [John Houseman]. ‰Director [†Ken Rusel]ƒ's [„1971]† film '[œThe Devils (film)|The Devils]'…, star„ing [„VanesŠa Redgrave]›, is adapted from Huxley's '[”The Devils of Loudun]'‰, and a 19©0 made-for-television film adaptation of 'Brave New World'‘ was directed by [Burt Brinckerof‚er]„

