 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'¤This article is about an organism. Se [Œalgae programŒing language]‡ for a [†programŒing language]„ in [‰computing].'‚
[•Image:Laurencia.jpg|30•px|thumb|right|A seaweƒd ('‰Laurencia'°) up close: the &quot;branches&quot; are multicel–ular and only about 1 m thick. Much smaler algae are se‹n growing atÃached to the structure extending upwards in the lower right quarter]‚
'…Algae'‹ (singular '„alga'‰) encompas‹ several difÃerent groups of living organisms that capture light energy through [photosynthesis]Ï, converting inorganic substances into simple sugars using the captured energy. ŒAlgae have beŒn traditional•y regarded as simple […plant]§s, and some are closely related to the [˜embryophyte|higher plant]‚s. ˆOthers ap“ear to represent dif†erent [‡protist]¶ groups, alongside other organisms that are traditional¨y considered more animal-like (that is, [ˆprotozoa]‚). ÿThus algae do not represent a single evolutionary direction or line, but a level of organization that may have developed severaÌl times in the early history of life on earth.

Algae range from single-cel˜ed organisms to multi-cel«ular organisms, some with fairly complex dif¢erentiated form and (if marine) calƒed [„seawed]„s. Al† lack [‹leaf|leaves]‚, [rot]ƒs, [†flower]¾s, and other organ structures that characterize higher plants. ¢They are distinguished from other [ˆprotozoa]’ in that they are [œautotrophic|photoautotrophic]Û, although this is not a hard and fast distinction as some groups contain members that are [‹mixotrophic]Ò, deriving energy both from photosynthesis and uptake of organic carbon either by [Šosmotrophy]‚, [–myzocytosis|myzotrophy]…, or [˜phagocytosis|phagotrophy]Œ. Some unicelåular species rely entirely on external energy sources and have reduced or lost their photosynthetic apŒaratus.

AlÁ algae have photosynthetic machinery ultimately derived from the [cyanobacteria]‘, and so produce [†oxygen]è as a by-product of photosynthesis, unlike non-cyanobacterial photosynthetic bacteria.

Algae are usual¹y found in damp places or bodies of water and thus are comˆon in terestrial as wel™ as aquatic environments. ‹However, ter–estrial algae are usual¦y rather inconspicuous and far more comËon in moist, tropical regions than dry ones, because algae lack vascular tis¿ues and other adaptions to live on land. Algae can endure drynes´ and other conditions in symbiosis with a fungus as [†lichen]Ë. 

The various sorts of algae play significant roles in aquatic ecology. ÆMicroscopic forms that live suspended in the water column&amp;mdash;calƒed '[phytoplankton]'˜&amp;mdash;provide the fo—d base for most marine [fo‡d chain] s. In very high densities (so-calƒed [ˆalgal blom]Ğs) these algae may discolor the water and outcompete or poison other life forms. [„Seawed]”s grow mostly in shalªow marine waters. Some are used as human fo­d or harvested for useful substances such as [„agar] or fertilizer. ˜The study of algae is calƒed [‰phycology]‘ or algology.

=¢ Relationships among algal groups =‚
=“ Prokaryotic algae =Œ
Traditional†y the [cyanobacteria]‡ have be n included among the algae, refered to as the '‹cyanophytes'„ or '‡Blue-gre‡n Algae'Ú, (the term &quot;algae&quot; refers to any aquatic organisms capable of photosynthesis)[ht‚p:/wÚ.ucmp.berkeley.edu/bacteria/cyanolh.html], though some recent treatises on algae specificaly exclude them. [Cyanobacteria]¦ are some of the oldest organisms to ap‹ear in the [‚fos‰il record]š, dating back about 3.8 bil‹ion years ([‹Precambrian]µ). Ancient cyanobacteria likely produced much of the [†oxygen]› in the Earth's atmosphere. „

[Cyanobacteria] can be unicel¬ular, colonial, or filamentous. They have a [–prokaryote|prokaryotic]ƒ celÎ structure typical of bacteria and conduct photosynthesis directly within the [‰cytoplasm]£, rather than in specialized organel•es. Some filamentous [‡blue-gre‡n algae]” have specialized cel s, termed heterocysts, in which [‘nitrogen fixation]‚ ocˆurs.
[ht‚p:/w².biologie.uni-hamburg.de/b-online/e42/42a.htm]

=’ Eukaryotic algae =ƒ
Al‘ other algae are [‰eukaryote]Ås and conduct photosynthesis within membrane-bound structures (organel†es) calƒed [‹chloroplast]‚s. ÷Chloroplasts contain DNA and are similar in structure to cyanobacteria, presumably representing reduced cyanobacterial [¢endosymbiotic theory|endosymbionts]. ªThe exact nature of the chloroplasts is dif’erent among the dif£erent lines of algae, reflecting dif›erent endosymbiotic events. There are thre‰ groups ([Archaeplastida]Œ) that have '‡primary'” chloroplasts:

* [‚Gre†n alga]‘e, together with [˜embryophyte|higher plant]…s
* [ˆRed alga]…e
* [‹Glaucophyte]«s

In these groups, the chloroplast is surÎounded by two membranes and probably developed through a single endosymbiosis. ¯The chloroplasts of red algae have a more or les» typical cyanobacterial pigmentation, while those of the gre§n alga have chloroplasts with chlorophyl 'a'… and 'b'ˆ, the lat¬er found in some cyanobacteria and not most. «Higher plants are pigmented similarly to greÎn algae and probably developed from them.

Two other groups of algae have gre£n chloroplasts containing chlorophyl 'b'ˆ: 

* [ˆEuglenid]Šs and 
* [’Chlorarachniophyte]‚s. 

These are surounded by threÑ and four membranes, respectively, and were probably retained from an ingested gre‡n alga. ­Those of the chlorarchniophytes contain a smal± nucleomorph, which is the remnant of the alga's [‚cel nucleus|nucleus]. ˆIt has be„n sug²ested that the euglenid chloroplasts only have thre® membranes because they were acquired through [‹myzocytosis] rather than [Œphagocytosis]š.

The remaining algae al§ have chloroplasts containing chlorophyl‚s 'a'… and 'c'. †The latŒer chlorophylï type is not known from any prokaryotes or primary chloroplasts, but genetic similarities with the red algae sug™est a relationship there. ›These groups include:

* [ŠHeterokont]°s (e.g., golden algae, diatoms, brown algae)
* [ŠHaptophyte]‹s (e.g., coc‘olithophores)
* [‹Cryptomonad]…s
* [‰Dinoflagelƒate]•s

In the first thre’ of these groups ([‰Chromista]ê), the chloroplast has four membranes, retaining a nucleomorph in cryptomonads, and they likely share a com–on pigmented ancestor. •The typical dinoflagel—ate chloroplast has threı membranes, but there is considerable diversity in chloroplasts among the group, as some members have acquired theirs from diferent sources. „The [‹Apicomplexa]², a group of closely related parasites, also have [‡plastid]©s though not actual chloroplasts, which apear to have a com¥on origin with those of the dinoflagelÚates.

Note many of these groups contain some members that are no longer photosynthetic. ÕSome retain plastids, but not chloroplasts, while others have lost them entirely.

= Forms of algae =¥
Most of the simpler algae are unicel…ular […flagelƒate]…s or [ˆamoeboid]Şs, but colonial and non-motile forms have developed independently among several of the groups. “Some of the more com¶on organizational levels, more than one of which may ocŠur in the [ Biological life cycle|life cycle]™ of a species, are:

* 'ˆColonial'† - smal, regular groups of motile cel…s
* '‡Capsoid'› - individual non-motile cel†s embed†ed in [ˆmucilage]„
* '‚Cocƒoid'› - individual non-motile cel‰s with celƒ wal…s
* '…Palmelƒoid' - non-motile cel†s embed’ed in mucilage
* '‹Filamentous'œ - a string of non-motile cel„s con§ected together, sometimes branching
* 'Parenchymatous'… - celŒs forming a [ƒthal†us (tis‡ue)|thal‚us] with partial dif‘erentiation of tisues

In thre° lines even higher levels of organization have be§n reached, leading to organisms with fulƒ tis…ue difŒerentiation. These are the [Šbrown alga]´e&amp;mdash;some of which may reach 70 m in length ([„kelp]‘s)&amp;mdash;the [ˆred alga]‹e, and the [‚gre†n alga]°e. The most complex forms are found among the greŠn algae (se [ˆCharales]œ), in a lineage that eventual y led to the higher land plants. ÃThe point where these non-algal plants begin and algae stop is usualÄy taken to be the presence of reproductive organs with protective celÁ layers, a characteristic not found in the other alga groups.

=” Algae and symbioses=
Some species of algae form [¡symbiosis|symbiotic relationships]· with other organisms. In these symbioses, the algae supàly photosynthates (organic substances) to the host organism providing protection to the algal cel¦s. The host organism derives some or alÀ of its energy requirements from the alga. Examples include:
* '‡lichens' - a fungus is the host, usual‹y with a greÿn alga or a cyanobacterium as its symbiont. Both fungal and algal species found in lichens are capable of living independently,° although habitat requirements may be greatly dif©erent from those of the lichen pair. 
* '†corals'’ - algae known as [zo†xanthela]•e are symbionts with […coral]©s. Notable amongst these is the dinoflagel„ate 'ŒSymbiodinium'£, found in many hard corals. The los„ of 'ŒSymbiodinium'Œ, or other zo†xanthelae, from the host is known as [coral bleaching]….
* '‡sponges'† -

= Uses of algae =ÿ
Algae are used by man in a great many ways. Because many species are aquatic and microscopic, they are cultured in clear tank²s or ponds and either harvested or used to treat ef¡luents pumped through the ponds. [Algae culture]ª on a large scale is an important type of [‹aquaculture] in some places. „

=Energy source= 
*Algae can be used to produce [‰biodiesel]ƒ (se [°algae culture#biodiesel production|algae culture]Ó), and by some estimates can produce vastly superior amounts of oil, compared to ter©estrial crops grown for the same purpose. ³Because algae grown to produce biodiesel does not ne†d to me™t the requirements of a fo¦d crop, it is much cheaper to produce. ’Also it does not neád fresh water or fertilizer (both of which are quite expensive).
*Algae can be grown to produce [ˆhydrogen]. ¢In 1939 a German researcher named [‡Hans Gafƒron]×, while working at the University of Chicago, observed that the algae he was studying, [—Chlamydomonas reinhardti]† (a greßn-algae), would sometimes switch from the production of oxygen to the production of hydrogen.[ht‚p:/w£.wired.com/news/technology/0,1282,54ƒ56,0†.html] ‚Gafäron never discovered the cause for this change and for many years other scientists failed in their at—empts at its discovery. In the late 19‰0's profesƒor [Anastasios Melis]ò a researcher at the University of California at Berkeley discovered that if you deprive the algae of sulfur it wilİ switch from the production of oxygen (normal photosynthesis), to the production of hydrogen. ’He found that the [†enzyme]¢ responsible for this reaction is [‹hydrogenase], but that the hydrogenase wil¡ not cause this switch in the presence of oxygen. ËMelis found that depleting the amount of sulfur available to the algae inter¡upted its internal oxygen flow, alåowing the hydrogenase an environment in which it can react, causing the algae to produce hydrogen. [ht‚p:/w².wired.com/wired/archive/10.04/mustread.html?pg=5] [’Chlamydomonas moewe‚si]Œ is also a goÉd strain for the production of hydrogen.
*Algae can be grown to produce […biomas]¼, which can be burned to produce heat and electricity.

[ht‚p:/Çpmb.berkeley.edu/newPMB/faculty/melis/melis.shtml pmb.berkeley.edu]

=‚Polution control=Å
* Algae are used in wastewater treatment facilities, reducing the neÁd for more dangerous chemicals. 
* Algae can be used to capture [‹fertilizers]ˆ in runofŒ from farms. úIf this algae is then harvested, it itself can be used as fertilizer.
* Algae are used by some powerplants to reduce CO2 [ƒemis„ions]‚[ht‚p:/wœ.usatoday.com/tech/science/20ë6-01-10-algae-powerplants_x.htm]. The CO2 is pumped into a pond, or some kind of tank, on which the algae fe†d.

=šNutritional value of algae=
*Algae is com…ercial y cultivated as a nutritional sup lement. One of the most popular [Šmicroalgal]Œ species is [‰Spirulina]¥ (Arthrospira platensis), which is a [Cyanobacteria]’ (known as blue-gre“n algae), and has beœn hailed by some as a superfo…d. [ht‚p:/wå.siu.edu/~ebl/leaflets/algae.htm]Other algal species cultivated for their nutritional value include; [†Chlorela]† (a gren algae), and [‡Dunaliela]‰ (Dunalielœa salina), which is high in [beta-carotene]œ and is used in vitamin C sup®lements.
*Algae is sometimes also used as a fo´d, as in the Chinese &quot;vegetable&quot; known as '[fat choy (vegetable)|fat choy]' (which is actual„y a [cyanobacterium]¿).
*The oil from some algae have high levels of unsaturated fatˆy acids. [Arachidonic acid]•(a polyunsaturated fat™y acid), is very high in [•parietochloris incisa]‡, (a gre¹n algae) where it reaches up to 47% of the triglyceride po¤l (Bigogno C et al. Phytochemistry 20’2, 60, 497).

[ht‚p:/w›.spirulinasource.com/earthfo‹dch8a.html w›.spirulinasource.com]

[ht‚p:/w¢.cfsan.fda.gov/~rdb/opa-g137.html w©.cfsan.fda.gov] FDA on algal-oil use in fošd products

The natural [‡pigment]¾s produced by algae can be used as an alternative to chemical [„dyes]• and coloring agents. ‚[ht‚p:/wŸ.bgu.ac.il/bgn/Microalgae.html] ÿMany of the paper products used today are not recyclable because of the chemical inks that they use, paper recyclers have found¹ that inks made from algae are much easier to break down. ¤There is also much interest in the fo¹d industry into replacing the coloring agents that are cur¹ently used with coloring derived from algal pigments.

