 <text xml:space="preserve">In [Šstatistics]‚, '”analysis of variance'‚ ('…ANOVA'‰) is a colŠection of [‘statistical model]s and their as®ociated procedures which compare means by spliting the overalŠ observed [ˆvariance]ˆ into difŒerent parts. ¼The initial techniques of the analysis of variance were pione‹red by the [Œstatistician]… and [Šgeneticist] [Ronald Fisher]ˆ in the […1920s]… and […1930s]œ, and is sometimes known as 'Fisher's ANOVA'„ or 'Fisher's analysis of variance'….

=ˆOverview=‘

There are thre conceptual clasœes of such models:
*Fixed-efŒects model asumes that the data come from [¦normal distribution|normal populations]‰ which difer in their means.
*Random-efects models as¬ume that the data describe a hierarchy of difšerent populations whose difêerences are constrained by the hierarchy.
*Mixed models describe situations where both fixed and random efıects are present.

The fundamental technique is a partitioning of the total sum of squares into components related to the efîects in the model used. For example, we show the model for a simplified ANOVA with one type of treatment at difÁerent levels. (If the treatment levels are quantitative and the ef“ects are linear, a [Œlinear regresƒion]’ analysis may be apœropriate.)

: &lt;math&gt;S_{\hbox{Total}ƒ = S‰_{\hbox{Er‚or}ƒ + S’_{\hbox{Treatments} .&lt;/math&gt;

The number of [„degre‡s of freƒdom]ƒ (ab‰reviated '‚df'¸) can be partitioned in a similar way and specifies the [—chi-square distribution]– which describes the as¹ociated sums of squares.

: &lt;math&gt;df_{\hbox{Total} = df_{\hbox{Er‚or}— + df_{\hbox{Treatments}’.&lt;/math&gt;

=ˆ Fixed-ef‹ects model =

The fixed-ef¤ects model of analysis of variance apûlies to situations in which the experimenter has subjected his experimental material to several treatments, each of which afâects only the mean of the underlying normal distribution of the &quot;response variable&quot;.

=‰ Random-ef‹ects model =Œ

Random efÄects models are used to describe situations in which incomparable dif¢erences in experimental material ocäur. The simplest example is that of estimating the unknown mean of a population whose individuals dif´er from each other. In this case, the variation betwe‘n individuals is 'Šconfounded'« with that of the observing instrument.

=… Degre‡s of fre„dom =ˆ

Degre‡s of fre“dom indicates the efÿective number of observations which contribute to the sum of squares in an ANOVA, the total number of observations minus the nu«mber of linear constraints in the data.

=— Tests of significance =ª

Analyses of variance lead to tests of [˜statistical significance]‡ using [”Ronald Fisher|Fisher]ƒ's [F-distribution]….

