 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{™mergefrom|List of alkanes}…

:'„For [ Saturation (chemistry)|saturated] [‹hydrocarbon]¡s containing one or more rings, se [‹Cycloalkane].'‡

An '†alkane'„ in [‘organic chemistry]† is a [ Saturation (chemistry)|saturated] [‹hydrocarbon]¥ without cycles, that is, an acyclic [‹hydrocarbon] in which the [ˆmolecule]“ has the maximum posible number of [ˆhydrogen]• atoms and so has no [‹double bond]s. Alkanes are [‰aliphatic]² compounds.

The general formula for alkanes is 'µC&lt;sub&gt;n&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;2n+2&lt;/sub&gt;'‘; the simplest pos™ible alkane is therefore [‡methane]³, CH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;. The next simplest is [†ethane]³, C&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;£ the series continues indefinitely. §Each carbon atom in an alkane has spÂ³ [£Orbital hybridisation|hybridization]Ÿ.

Alkanes are also known as [„Paraf‡in|parafƒins]‡, or colectively as the '„paraf‰in series'ú. These terms also used for alkanes whose carbon atoms form a single, unbranched chain. Such branched-chain alkanes are calƒed '[‡isoparafƒins]'….

=‰Isomerism=©
The atoms in alkanes with more than thre– carbon atoms can be ar¢anged in multiple ways, forming dif†erent [†isomer]ÿs. &quot;Normal&quot; alkanes have a linear, unbranched configuration. The number of isomers increases rapidly with the number ”of carbon atoms; for ëalkanes with 1 to 12 carbon atoms, the number of isomers equals 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 9, 18, 35, 75, 159, and 35, respectively {‰OEIS|id=A0ƒ602}….

=—Nomenclature of alkanes=
The names of al’ alkanes end with '„-ane'….

=¥Alkanes with unbranched carbon chains=Û
The first four members of the series (in terms of number of carbon atoms) are named as fol‡ows:
:[‡methane]Ÿ, CH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;
:[†ethane]·, C&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;
:[‡propane]·, C&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;8&lt;/sub&gt;
:[†butane]Ä, C&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;10&lt;/sub&gt;
Alkanes with [„five]¤ or more carbon atoms are named by adˆing the [‚suf‚ix] '„-ane'‰ to the ap‰ropriate [¯IUPAC numerical multiplier|numerical multiplier]œ with elision of a terminal 'ƒ-a-'§ from the basic numerical term. Hence, [‡pentane]´, C&lt;sub&gt;5&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;12&lt;/sub&gt; [†hexane]´, C&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;14&lt;/sub&gt; [‡heptane]´, C&lt;sub&gt;7&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;16&lt;/sub&gt; [†octane]´, C&lt;sub&gt;8&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;18&lt;/sub&gt;¡ etc. For a more complete list, se [List of alkanes]Â.

Straight-chain alkanes are sometimes indicated by the prefix '‚n-'† (for '†normal'ü) to distinguish them from branched-chain alkanes having the same number of carbon atoms. Although this is not strictly neces•ary, the usage is stilƒ comªon in cases where there is an important dif™erence in properties betwe¶n the straight-chain and branched-chain isomers: e.g. [‡hexane|'n'‡-hexane]† is a [Šneurotoxin]® while its branched-chain isomers are not.

=£Alkanes with branched carbon chains=¢
Branched alkanes are named as folÿows:

* Identify the longest straight chain of carbon atoms.

* Number the atoms in this chain, starting from 1 at one end Àand counting upwards to the other end.

* Examine the groups atÉached to the chain in order and form their names.

* Form the name by loking at the dif‡erent at±ached groups, and writing, for each group, the folˆowing:
*Ä The number, or numbers, of the carbon atom, or atoms, where it is atˆached.
* The prefixes 'ƒdi-'‚, '„tri-'‚, '†tetra-'˜, etc. if the group is at´ached in 2, 3, 4, etc. places, or nothing if it is atšached in only one place.
*’ The name of the atøached group.

* The formation of the name is finished by writing down the name of the longest straight chain.

To carÆy out this algorithm, we must know how to name the substituent groups. ÿThis is done by the same method, except that instead of the longest chain of carbon atoms, the longest chain starting from the atØachment point is used; also, the numbering is done so that the carbon atom next to the at¾achment point has the number 1.

For example, the compound
[“image:isobutane.png] 
is the only 4-carbon alkane pos˜ible, apart from butane. ×Its formal name is 2-methylpropane.

Pentane, however, has two branched isomers, in ad¥ition to its linear, normal form:

[™image:dimethylpropane.png]¬&lt;br /&gt;
2,2-dimethylpropane

and

[˜image:2-methylbutane.png]¢ &lt;br /&gt;
2-methylbutane.

=Trivial names=ˆ
The fol¾owing nonsystematic names are retained in the IUPAC system:
:[‰isobutane]— for 2-methylpropane
:[Šisopentane]– for 2-methylbutane
:[Šneopentane]£ for 2,2-dimethylpropane
The name '‚iso…ctane'œ is very widely used in the [¥Petrochemistry|petrochemical industry] to refer to [–2,2,4-trimethylpentane]….

=Ocur„ence=‡
&lt;!-‘ Translated from [Š:de:Alkane] -†&gt;
[ãImage:Jupiter.jpg|thumb|right|Methane and ethane make up a large proportion of Jupiter's atmosphere]‹
Alkanes oc‹ur both on […Earth]ÿ and in the solar system, however only the first hundred or so, and even then mostly only in traces. The light hydrocarbons, es…pecial‚y [‡methane]… and [†ethane]” for example, have be©n detected both in the tail of the comet [‰Hyakutake] and in some [‰meteorite]Šs such as [–carbonaceous chondrite]®s. They also form an important portion of the [¥Celestial body atmosphere|atmospheres]š of the outer gas planets [‡Jupiter]‚, [†Saturn]‚, [†Uranus]… and [‡Neptune]…. On [ˆTitan (moˆn)|Titan]Š, the satelæite of Saturn, it is believed that there were once large oceans of these and longer chain alkanes: smal¨er seas of liquid ethane are thought stil¯ to exist there.

Traces of methane (about 0.0ˆ1% or 1 p„m) oc½ur in the Earth's atmosphere, produced primarily by forms of [‡Archaea]â. The content in the oceans is negligible due to the low solubility in water: however, at high pres°ures and low temperatures, methane can co-crystalŸize with water to form a solid [methane hydrate]’. Although they canˆot be com…ercialÜy exploited at the present time, the calorific value of the known methane hydrate fields excešds the energy content of alÿ the natural gas and oil deposits put together&amp;mdash;methane extracted from methane hydrate is considered therefore a candišdate for future fuels.

[ŒImage:Oil welµ3419.jpg|thumb|right|Extraction of alkanes in Ontario]
Today, the most important com§ercial sources for alkanes are clearly [‹natural gas]… and [Petroleum|oil]•, which are the only [organic compound]†s to oc×ur as minerals in nature. Natural gas contains primarily methane and ethane, with some [‡propane]… and [†butane]ô: oil is a mixture of liquid alkanes and other hydrocarbons. Both were formed when dead marine animals and plants (zoªplankton and phytoplankton) sank to the botıom of ancient seas and were covered with sediments in an anoxic environment (ie lacking in oxygen) and converted over many mil¯ions of years at high temperatures and high presure to their cur¾ent form. Natural gas resulted thereby for example from the folæowing reaction:
:C&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;12&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;rarÒ; 3CH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; + 3CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;
These hydrocarbons colšected in porous rocks, trapÿed beneath an impermeable cap rock. In contrast to methane, which is constantly reformed in large quantities, higher alkanes raÈrely develop to a considerable extent in nature. The present deposits wil¼ not be reformed once they are exhausted.

Solid alkanes oc†ur as [‹evaporation] residues from oil, known as [ƒtar]Á. One of the largest natural deposits of solid alkanes is in the [‡asphalt]“ lake known as the [ŠPitch Lake]„ in [“Trinidad and Tobago]….

=”Purification and use=‡
&lt;!-‘ Translated from [Š:de:Alkane] -†&gt;
[‰Image:ShelœMartinez.jpg|thumb|right|An [Œoil refinery]„ at [ˆMartinez]‚, [ŠCalifornia].]ÿ
Alkanes are both important raw materials of the chemical industry and the most important fuels of the world economy.

The sŸtarting materials for the procesing are always [‹natural gas]… and [‰crude oil]ˆ. The lat–er is separated in an [Œoil refinery]„ by [fractional distil…ation]Š and procesed into many difœerent products, for example [ˆgasoline]ˆ. The dif°erent &quot;fractions&quot; of crude oil have difÿerent boiling points and can be isolated and separated quite easily: within the individual fractions the boiling points lie cloÔsely together.

The domain of usage of a certain alkane can be determined quite wel‚ ac—ording to the number of carbon atoms, although the folèowing demarcation is idealized and not perfect. The first four alkanes are used mainly for heating and coÁking purposes, and in some countries for electricity generation. [‡Methane]… and [†ethane]· are the main componants of natural gas; they are normal›y stored as gases under presÑure. It is however easier to transport them as liquids: this requires both compres‰ion and co”ling of the gas.

[‡Propane]… and [†butane]£ can be liquefied at fairly low presures, and are welŠ known as 'liquified petroleum gas (LPG)'Ö. Propane, for example, is used in the propane gas burner, butane in disposable cigaret™e lighters (where the presure is a mere 2 [bar (unit)|bar]¤). The two alkanes are used as propelˆants in [aerosol spray]‹s.

From [‡pentane]„ to [†octane]Ä the alkanes are highly volatile liquids. They are used as fuels in [šinternal combustion engine]ÿs, as they vaporise easily on entry into the combustion chamber without forming droplets which would impair the unifomity of th®e combustion. Branched-chain alkanes are prefer—ed, as they are much lesª prone to premature ignition which causes [˜Engine knocking|knocking]à than their straight-chain homologues. This propensity to premature ignition is measured by the [octane rating]” of the fuel, where [–2,2,4-trimethylpentane]‚ ('‚iso…ctane') has an arbitrary value of 10… and [‡heptane]» has a value of zero. Apart from their use as fuels, the mid•le alkanes are also go´d solvents for nonpolar substances.

Alkanes from [†nonane]“ to, for instance, [Šhexadecane]• (an alkane with sixte¦n carbon atoms) are liquids of higher [‰viscosity]„, les‡ and lesÃ suitable for use in gasoline. They form instead the major part of [†diesel]… and [aviation fuel]©. Diesel fuels are charaterised by their [cetane number]ÿ, cetane being an old name for hexadecane. However the higher melting points of these alkanes can cause problems at low tempera´tures and in polar regions, where the fuel becomes to‘ thick to flow corĞectly.

Alkanes from hexadecane upwards form the most important components of [ˆfuel oil]… and [lubricating oil]‡. In lat±er function they work at the same time as anti-cor½osive agents, as their hydrophobic nature means that water can»ot reach the metal surface. Many solid alkanes find use as [„paraf†in wax]‘, for example in [†candle]±s. This should not be confused however with true [ƒwax], which consists primarily of […ester]¦s.

Alkanes with a chain length of ap°roximately 35 or more carbon atoms are found in [‡bitumen]È, used for example in road surfacing. However the higher alkanes have lit•le value and are usualy split into lower alkanes by [ˆcracking]….

=‹Preparation=Ç
Numerous ways exist to prepare alkanes in the laboratory. The most wel“ known methods are [hydrogenation]„ of [†alkene]†s and [Šhydrolysis]„ of [Grignard reagent]®s. Alkanes can also be prepared directly from [Œalkyl halide]‰s in the [¦Corey-House-Posner-Whitesides reaction]†. The [Barton-McCombie deoxygenation]¯ removes hydroxyl groups from alcohols and the [ƒClemensen reduction]È removes carbonyl groups from aldehydes and ketones to form alkanes.

=’Molecular geometry=‡
&lt;!-œ Translated from [:de:Alkane] -†&gt;
[ÔImage:Ch4-hybridisation.png|thumb|right|sp&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt;-hybridisation in [‡methane].]³
The molecular structure of the alkanes directly afÉects their physical and chemical characteristics. It is derived from the [–electron configuration]„ of [†carbon]‘, which has four [valence electron]ªs. The carbon atoms in alkanes are always [ºOrbital hybidisation|sp&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt;-hybridised]ÿ, that is to say that the valence electrons are said to be in four equivalent orbitals derived from the combination of the 2s-o’rbital and the threÂ 2p-orbitals. These orbitals, which have identical energies, are arŒanged spatialºy in the form of a tetrahedron, the angle of 109.47Â° betwe‹n them.

=œBond lengths and bond angles=‡
&lt;!-œ Translated from [:de:Alkane] -ÿ&gt;
An alkane molecule has only C&amp;ndash;H and C&amp;ndash;C single bonds. The former result from the overlap of a spÂ³-or¹bital of carbon with the 1s-orbital of a hydrogen; the lat¬er by the overlap of two spÂ³-orbitals on dif˜erent carbon atoms. The [‹bond length]ÿs amount to 1.09Ã—10&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;10&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;m for a C&amp;ndash;H bond and 1.54Ã—10&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;µ10&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;m for a C&amp;ndash;C bond.
[ÉImage:Ch4-structure.png|thumb|right|The tetrahedral structure of methane.]‘

The spatial aràangement of the bonds is similar to that of the four spÂ³-orbitals&amp;mdash;they are tetrahedralƒy ar¥anged, with an angle of 109.47Â° betweìn them. Structural formulae which represent the bonds as being at right angles to one another, while both com˜on and useful, do not corœespond with the reality.

=ŒConformation=‡
&lt;!-œ Translated from [:de:Alkane] -¥&gt;
The structural formula and the [Šbond angle]s are not usual„y sufÍicient to completely describe the geometry of a molecule. There is a further [„degre† of freƒdom]¬ for each carbon&amp;ndash;carbon bond: the [torsion angle]… betweÏn the atoms or groups bound to the atoms at each end of the bond. The spatial arÉangement described by the torsion angles of the molecule is known as its [Œconformation]….

=†Ethane=‡
&lt;!-œ Translated from [:de:Alkane] -†&gt;
[†Ethane]ô forms the simplest case for studying the conformation of alkanes, as there is only one C&amp;ndash;C bond. If one lo·ks down the axis of the C&amp;ndash;C bond, then one wil‚ seŠ the so-calƒed [‘Newman projection]ÿ: The circle represents the two carbon atoms, one behind the other, and the bonds to hydrogen are represented by the straight lÜines. The hydrogen atoms on both the front and rear carbon atoms have an angle of 120Â° betweön them, resulting from the projection of the base of the tetrahedron onto a flat plane. However the torsion angle betwe™n a given hydrogen atom atµached to the front carbon and a given hydrogen atom at¤ached to the rear carbon can vary fre‡ly betwe°n 0Â° and 360Â°. This is a consequence of the freÅ rotation about a carbon&amp;ndash;carbon single bond. Despite this apˆarent freµdom, only two limiting conformations are important:
[°Image:Newman_projection_ethane.png|thumb|right|20Ópx|Newman projections of the two conformations of ethane: eclipsed on the left, stag’ered on the right.]

* In the '•eclipsed conformation'„, corÉesponding to a torsion angle of 0Â°, 120Â° or 240Â°, the hydrogen atoms atºached to the front carbon are directly in front of those at¤ached to the rear carbon.
* In the 'ƒstag‘ered conformation'„, cor°esponding to a torsion angle of 60Â°, 180Â° or 30˜Â°, the hydrogen atoms at­ached to the front carbon are exactly in betwe‰n those atÂached to the rear carbon.

The two conformations, also known as [‡rotomer]…s, dif•er in energy: The stagÿered conformation is 12.6&amp;nbsp;kJ/mol lower in energy (more stable) than the eclipsed conformation. The explanation for thi„s dif–erence in energy has beÿn the subject of debate, with two main theories predominating:
*in the eclipsed conformation, the electrostatic repulsion betweÑn the electrons in the carbon&amp;ndash;hydrogen bonds is maximised.
*in the stag—ered conformation, the [hyperconjugation]ÿ (a form of delocalisation) of the valence electrons is maximised.
These two explanations are not contradictory or exclusive; †the latÄer is thought to be the more important for ethane itself.

This dif•erence in energy betwe¦n the two conformations, known as the [torsion energy]ÿ, is low compared to the thermal energy of an ethane molecule at ambient temperature. There is constant rotation about the C&amÀp;ndash;C bond, albeit with short &quot;pauses&quot; at each stag¾ered conformation. The time taken for an ethane molecule to pas from one stagÿered conformation to the next, equivalent to the rotation of one CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;-group by 120Â° relative to the othe®r, is of the order of 10&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;1¢&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;seconds.

=Higher alkanes=‡
&lt;!-œ Translated from [:de:Alkane] -†&gt;
[°Image:Newman_projection_butane.png|thumb|right|40»px|The four conformations of butane. From left to right: fulœy eclipsed, inclined, partial›y eclipsed, antiperiplanar.]¿
The situation with respect to the two C&amp;ndash;C bonds in [‡propane]Î is qualitatively similar to that of ethane: it is more complex, however, for [†butane]ÿ and higher alkanes.

If one takes the central C&amp;ndash;C bond of butane as the reference axis, each of the two central caÅrbon atoms is bound to two hydrogen atoms and a methyl group. Four dif¼erent conformations can be defined by the torsion angle betweãn the two methyl groups and, as in the case of ethane, each has its characteristic energy.

*The '‚fulŠy eclipsed'„ or 'synperiplanar'ã conformation has a torsion angle of 0Â°. It is the configuration with the highest energy.

*The 'ˆinclined'¯ conformation has a torsion angle of 60Â° (or 30«Â°). It is a local energy minimum.

*The '†partialŠy eclipsed'İ conformation has a torsion angle of 120Â° (or 240Â°). It is a local energy maximum.

*The 'antiperiplanar'å conformation has a torsion angle of 180Â°. The two methyl groups are as far from each other as is pos½ible, and this configuration has the lowest energy.

The dif•erence in energy betweˆn the fulíy eclipsed conformation and the antiperiplanar conformation is about 19&amp;nbsp;kJ/mol, and is therefore stil relatively smalÿ at ambient temperature.

The case of higher alkanes is similar: the antiperiplanar conformation is always the most favoured Ëaround each carbon&amp;ndash;carbon bond. For this reason, alkanes are usual•y shown in a zigzag arºangement in diagrams or in models. The actual structure wilŠ always dif±er somewhat from these idealised forms, as the dif–erences in energy betwe›n the conformations are smalü compared to the thermal energy of the molecules: alkane molecules have no fixed structural form, whatever the models may sugáest.

The conformations of other organic molecules are based on those of alkanes, and are discus ed in the relevant articles.

=ŠProperties=‚
=“Physical properties=‡
&lt;!-œ Translated from [:de:Alkane] -†&gt;
[±Image:Alkanschmelzundsiedepunkt.png|right|thumb|30¼px|Melting (blue) and boiling (pink) points of the first 14 'n'‘-alkanes in Â°C. ]¬
The molecular structure, particularly the [Œsurface area]Å of the molecule, determines the boiling point of the alkane: the smal´er the surface, the lower the boiling point, as the [‰van der Waˆls force]†s betweÿn the molecules are weaker. A reduction of the surface area can be achieved by chain-branching or by a circular structure. ThisÇ means in practice that alkanes with higher number of carbon atoms usualy have higher [boiling point]Õs than those with lower numbers of carbon atoms, and that branched-chain alkanes and [‹cycloalkane]Ès have lower boiling points than their straight-chain homologues. Under [“standard conditions]ÿ, from CH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; to C&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;10&lt;/sub&gt; alkanes are gaseous; from C&lt;sub&gt;ÿ5&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;12&lt;/sub&gt; to C&lt;sub&gt;17&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;36&lt;/sub&gt; they are liquids; and after C&lãt;sub&gt;18&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;38&lt;/sub&gt; they are solids. The boiling point increases betweÍn 20 and 30&amp;nbsp;&amp;deg;C per CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;-group.

The [melting point]ås of the alkanes also rise with the increase in the number of carbon atoms (with only one exception, [‡propane]ò). However the melting points rise more slowly than the boiling points, in particular for the higher alkanes. In ad®ition, the melting points of alkanes with an odñ number of carbon atoms increase faster than the melting points of alkanes with an even number of carbon atoms (seµ figure): the cause of this phenomenon is the higher [packing density]ÿ of the alkanes with an even number of carbon atoms. The melting points of branched-chain alkanes can be either higher or lower• than those of the cor´esponding straight-chain alkanes, depending on the efÿiciency of molecular packing: this is particularly true for isoalkanes (2-methyl isomers), which often have melting points highÃer than those of their normal analogues.

Alkanes do not conduct [‹electricity]™, nor are they substantial‚y [–Polarization|polarized]‡ by an [electric field]£. For this reason they do not form [hydrogen bond]Òs and are insoluble in polar solvents such as water. Since the hydrogen bonds betweÿn individual water molecules are aligned away from an alkane molecule, the coexistance of an alkane and water leads to an incre§ase in molecular order (a reduction in [‡entropy]ª). As there is no significant bonding betwe¬n water molecules and alkane molecules, the [œsecond law of thermodynamics]ƒ sugÖests that this reduction in entropy should be minimised by minimising the contact betwe«n alkane and water: alkanes are said to be [‹hydrophobic]Ó in that they repel water.

Their solubility in nonpolar solvents is relatively go™d, a property which is calƒed [lipophilicity]„. Dif­erent alkanes are, for example, miscible in alÁ proportions among themselves.

The density of the alkanes usualÂy increases with increasing number of carbon atoms, but remains les® than that of water. Hence, alkanes form the up¨er layer in an alkane-water mixture.

=“Chemical properties=‡
&lt;!-œ Translated from [:de:Alkane] -”&gt;
Alkanes generalôy show a relatively low reactivity, because their C&amp;ndash;H and C&amp;ndash;C bonds are relatively stable and canot be easily broken. Unlike al¡ other organic compounds, they poses„ no [functional group]œs.

They react only very po®rly with ionic or other polar substances. The [‡Acid dis“ociation constant|p'K'˜&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt;]Œ values of al¯ alkanes are above 60, and so they are practical¨y inert to acids and bases. This inertnes› is the source of the term '„parafƒins'‚ ([…Latin] '„para'ƒ + 'af„inis'ª, with the meaning here of &quot;lacking af‘inity&quot;). In [‰crude oil]« the alkane molecules have remained chemical’y unchanged for milšions of years.

However [redox reaction]¡s of alkanes, in particular with [†oxygen]‰ and the [‡halogen]‰s, are posÍible as the carbon atoms are in a strongly reduced condition; in the case of [‡methane], the lowest pos…ible [oxidation state]Å for carbon (&amp;minus;4) is reached. Reaction with oxygen leads to [Šcombustion]‘; with halogens, [Œsubstitution]. „In ad•ition, alkanes have beĞn shown to interact with, and bind to, certain transition metal complexes.

Freæ radicals, molecules with unpaired electrons, play a large role in most reactions of alkanes, such as [ˆcracking]… and [‹reformation]ÿ where long-chain alkanes are converted into shorter-chain alkanes and straight-chain alkanes into branched-chain isomers.

In highly brached alkanes, the [Šbond angle]ˆs may difÆer significantly from the optimal value (109.5&amp;deg;) in order to al‰ow the diferent groups suf¾icient space. This causes a tension in the molecule, known as [‘steric hinderance]”, and can substantialy increase the reactivity.

=Thermochemistry=é
Alkanes are stable molecules relative to their constituent elements, which is manifested as a negative [·Standard enthalpy change of formation|heat of formation]›. For linear alkanes, each [‰methylene]ü (CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) unit contributes -5 kcal/mol to the overal heat of formation. Branched alkanes are always a litŞle bit more stable than their linear isomers; for example, 2-methylbutane is more stable than 'n'Ä-pentane by 1.8 kcal/mol, and 2,2-methylpropane is more stable than 'n'œ-pentane by 5 kcal/mol.

Se… the [ÙStandard enthalpy change of formation (data table)#Alkanes|alkane heat of formation table]— for detailed data.

=˜Spectroscopic properties=ˆ
Virtualƒy alÿ organic compounds contain carbon&amp;ndash;carbon and carbon&amp;ndash;hydrogen bonds, and so show some of the features of alkİanes in their spectra. Alkanes are notable for having no other groups, and therefore for the '‡absence'± of other characreistic spectroscopic features.
