The carbon&amp;ndash;hydrogen stretching mode gives a strong absorption betweÿn 2850 and 2960&amp;nbsp;cm&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;1&lt;/sup&gt;, while the carbon&amp;ndash;carbon stretching mode absorbes betweƒn 80‡ and 130ÿ&amp;nbsp;cm&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;1&lt;/sup&gt;. The carbon&amp;ndash;hydrogen bending modes depend on the nature of the group:ÿ methyl groups show bands at 1450&amp;nbsp;cm&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;1&lt;/sup&gt; and 1375&amp;nbsp;cm&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;1&ltÿ;/sup&gt;, while methylene groups show bands at 1465&amp;nbsp;cm&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;1&lt;/sup&gt; and 1450&amp;nbsp;cm&lt;supÿ&gt;&amp;minus;1&lt;/sup&gt;. Carbon chains with more than four carbon atoms show a weak absorption at around 725&amp;nbsp;cm&l¤t;sup&gt;&amp;minus;1&lt;/sup&gt;.
=NMR spectroscopy=«
The proton resonances of alkanes are usualÿy found at &amp;delta;&lt;sub&gt;H&lt;/sub&gt; = 0&amp;ndash;1. The carbon-13 resonances depend on the number of hydrogen atoms‚ atáached to the carbon: &amp;delta;&lt;sub&gt;C&lt;/sub&gt; = 8&amp;ndash;30 (methyl), 15&amp;ndash;5î (methylene), 20&amp;ndash;60 (methyne). The carbon-13 resonance of quaternary carbon atoms is characteristical›y weak, due to the lack of [nuclear Overhauser enhancement] and the long [relaxation time]: it can be misšed in routine spectra.

=‚Mas spectrometry=–
Alkanes have a high [‘ionisation energy]Ÿ, and the molecular ion is usualœy weak. The fragmentation patern can be difßicult to interpret, but, in the case of branched chain alkanes, the carbon chain is preferentialÛy cleaved at tertiary or quaternary carbons due to the relative stability of the resulting [‚freˆ radical]¥s. The fragment resulting from the losõ of a single methyl group (M&amp;minus;15) is often absent, and other fragment are often spaced by intervals of fourte„n masŠ units, coršesponding to sequential losª of CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;-groups.

