:¦CH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; + Cl&amp;mid‹ot; &amp;rar£; CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;&amp;mid‹ot; + HCl
:¡CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;&amp;mid¨ot; + Cl&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;rarª; CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;Cl + Cl&amp;midØot;
:This results in the desired product plus another chlorine radical. This radical wilá then go on to take part in another propagation reaction causing a chain reaction. If there is sufîicient chlorine, other products such as CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;Cl&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; may be formed.
:'‚3.' '‹Termination'™: recombination of two freŒ radicals:
