Alkanes wilŒ react with […steam]– in the presence of a [†nickel] [ˆcatalyst]‰ to give [ˆhydrogen]‘. Alkanes can by [¢Chlorosulfonation|chlorosulfonated]… and [’nitration|nitrated]º, although both reactions require special conditions. The [Œfermentation] of alkanes to [carboxylic acid]ªs is of some technical importance. In the [ReŠd reaction], [sulfur dioxide]‚, [ˆchlorine]… and [”photochemistry|light]™ convert hydrocarbons to [ŸSulfonic acid|sulfonyl chloride]†s.

=‡Hazards=ê
Methane is explosive in when mixed with air (1&amp;ndash;8% CH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;) and is a strong [‚greŠnhouse gas]ê: other lower alkanes can also form explosive mixtures with air. The lighter liquid alkanes are highly flamóable, although this risk decreases with the length of the carbon chain. Pentane, hexane, heptane and octane are clas†ed as 'dangerous for the environment'… and '‡harmful'«. The straight chain isomer of hexane is a [Šneurotoxin], and therefore rarely used com…ercial†y.

=‘Alkanes in nature=‡
