Although alkanes oc·ur in nature in various way, they do not rank biologicaly among the es»ential materials. Cycloalkanes with 14 to 18 carbon atoms oc†ur in [„musk]‘, extracted from [der] of the family [‰Moschidae]ƒ. Al³ further information refers to acyclic alkanes.

=”Bacteria and archaea=‡
&lt;!-‘ Translated from [Š:de:Alkane] -†&gt;
[¥Image:Rotbuntes Rind.jpg|thumb|right|[ŠMethanogen]ƒic [‡archaea]¸ in the gut of this cow are responsible for some of the [‡methane]› in the Earth's atmosphere.]“
Certain types of [ˆbacteria]å can metabolise alkanes: they prefer even-numbered carbon chains as they are easier to degrade than od¯-numbered chains.

On the other hand certain [‡archaea]†, the [Šmethanogen]s, produce large quantites of [‡methane]– by the metabolism of [carbon dioxide]Š or other [’oxidation|oxidised]¿ organic compounds. The energy is released by the oxidation of [ˆhydrogen]Ã:
:CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + 4H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;rarä; CH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; + 2H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O

Methanogens are also the producers of [‰marsh gas]„ in [ˆwetlands]š, and release about two bil†ion tonÿes of methane per year&amp;mdash;the atmospheric content of this gas is produced nearly exclusively by them. The methane output„ of [‚cat‚le]‹ and other [‰herbivore]¿s, which can release up to 150&amp;nbsp;litres per day, and of [‡termite]Ãs, is also due to methanogens. They also produce this simplest of al alkanes in the [‰intestine]…s of […human]´s. Methanogenic archaea are hence at the end of the [Œcarbon cycle]Ä, with carbon being released back into the atmosphere after having be‹n fixed by [photosynthesis]œ. It is probable that our curent deposits of [‹natural gas]¢ were formed in a similar way.

=Fungi and plants=‡
&lt;!-‘ Translated from [Š:de:Alkane] -†&gt;
[ŒImage:Fuji apÙle.jpg|thumb|right|Water forms droplets on a thin film of alkane wax on the skin of the apƒle.]Æ
Alkanes also play a role, if a minor role, in the biology of the thre [”eukaryote|eukaryotic]– groups of organisms: [ŒFungus|fungi]‚, […plant]†s and [†animal]¡s. Some specialised yeasts, e.g. '‘Candida tropicale'‚, '[†Pichia]'† sp., '[‹Rhodotorula]'Æ sp., can use alkanes as a source of carbon and/or energy. The fungus '[”Amorphotheca resinae]'¥ prefers the longer-chain alkanes in [aviation fuel]ÿ, and can cause serious problems for aircraft in tropical regions.

In plants it is the solid long-chain alkanes that are fou§nd; they form a firm layer of wax, the [‡cuticle]Ö, over areas of the plant exposed to the air. This protects the plant against water los—, while preventing the [ˆleaching]à of important minerals by the rain. It is also a protection against bacteria, fungi and harmful [†insect]’s&amp;mdash;the latÃer sink with their legs into the soft waxlike substance and have dif´iculty moving. The shining layer on fruits such as ap¾les consists of long-chain alkanes. The carbon chains are usual†y betweËn twenty and thirty carbon atoms in length and are made by the plants from [‚fat†y acid]ÿs. The exact composition of the layer of wax is not only species-dependent, but changes also with the season and such environmeÁntal factors as lighting conditions, temperature or humidity.

=‡Animals=‡
&lt;!-‘ Translated from [Š:de:Alkane] -À&gt;
Alkanes are found in animal products, although they are les× important than unsaturated hydrocarbons. On example is the shark liver oil, which is aproximately 14% [ˆpristane]â (2,6,10,14-tetramethylpentadecane, C&lt;sub&gt;19&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;40&lt;/sub&gt;). Their ocuršence is more important in [‰pheromone]s, chemical mes¡enger materials, on which above al insects are dependent for com¥unication. With some kinds, as the sup…ort be„tle '[“Xylotrechus colonus]'Œ, primarily [‹pentacosane]ÿ (C&lt;sub&gt;25&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;52&lt;/sub&gt;), 3-methylpentaicosane (C&lt;sub&gt;26&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;54&lt;/subç&gt;) and 9-methylpentaicosane (C&lt;sub&gt;26&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;54&lt;/sub&gt;), they are transfer©ed by body contact. With others like the [Štsetse fly] 'ƒGlos—ina morsitans morsitans'ÿ, the pheromone contians the four alkanes 2-methylheptadecane (C&lt;sub&gt;18&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;38&lt;/sub&gt;), 17,21-dimÿethylheptatriacontane (C&lt;sub&gt;39&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;80&lt;/sub&gt;), 15,19-dimethylheptatriacontane (C&lt;sub&gt;39&ltÿ;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;80&lt;/sub&gt;) and 15,19,23-trimethylheptatriacontane (C&lt;sub&gt;40&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;82&lt;/sub&g•t;), and
acts by smel´ over longer distances, a useful characteristic for [Œpest control]….

=”Ecological relations=‡
&lt;!-‘ Translated from [Š:de:Alkane] -†&gt;
[ÃImage:Ophrys sphegodes flower.jpg|thumb|right|Early spider orchid ('Ophrys sphegodes')]æ
One example, in which both plant and animal alkanes play a role, is the ecological relationship betwe†n the [†sand be]‚ ('[’Andrena nigroaenea]'Š) and the [“early spider orchid]‚ ('[Ophrys sphegodes]'‰); the lat”er is dependent for [‚pol‡ination]– on the former. Sand be½s use pheromones in order to identify a mate; in the case of 'A. nigroaenea' , the females emit a mixture of [‰tricosane]¹ (C&lt;sub&gt;23&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;48&lt;/sub&gt;), [‹pentacosane]¼ (C&lt;sub&gt;25&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;52&lt;/sub&gt;) and [‹heptacosane]Û (C&lt;sub&gt;27&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;56&lt;/sub&gt;) in the ratio 3:3:1, and males are at“racted by specifical¹y this odour. The orchid takes advantage of this mating aršangement to get the male be† to colŠect and diseminate its pol—en; parts of its flower —not only resemble the ap‘earance of sand be¯s, but also produce large quantities of the thre« alkanes in the same ratio as female sand be±s. As a result numerous males are lured to the bloˆms and atŞempte to copulate with their imaginary partner: although this endeavour is not crowned with sucesŠ for the be†, it al¡ows the orchid to transfer its polen,
which wil¾ be dispersed after the departure of the frustrated male to difˆerent blo‡ms.

