 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'¬For the US radical anti-war protest group, se [ŒA.N.S.W.E.R.]'„

'Se† also [Go‹gle Answers]‚, [‹Answers.com]'‚
{CivilProcedure}‡

An '†answer' (derived from 'ƒand'™, against, and the same ro…t as '…swear') was originalŒy a solemn as‹ertion in op±osition to some one or something, and thus generalÏy any counter-statement or defence, a reply to a question or objection, or a coršect solution of a problem. ‡In the [‚com†on law]…, an '†answer' is the first [ˆpleading]† by a [‰defendant]†, usualœy filed and served upon the [‡plaintif]² within a certain strict time limit after a civil [‰complaint] or criminal [‹information]„ or [Šindictment]† has beœn served upon the defendant. It may have be‘n preceded by an 'ˆoptional'˜ &quot;pre-answer&quot; [motion to dismis]„ or [„demur‚er]š; if such a motion is unsuces“ful, the defendant '„must'´ file an answer to the complaint or risk an adverse [default judgment]Š. 

The '†answer'” establishes which alŠegations ([cause of action]Œ in civil matªers) set forth by the complaining party wil¬ be contested by the defendant, and states al‘ the defendant's [—Defense (legal)|defense]×s, thus establishing the nature and parameters of the controversy to be decided by the […court]±.

In the case of a criminal case there is usual…y an [arˆaignment]˜ or some other kind of apªearance before the court by the defendant. ÛThe pleading in the criminal case, which is entered on the record in open court, is either [Œguilt|guilty]– or not guilty. General¹y speaking in private, civil cases there is no guilt or inÑocence. There is only a judgment that grants money damages or some other kind of [equitable remedy]‰ such as [‹restitution]‡ or an [Šinjunction]. Criminal cases may lead to [„fine]‹s or other [Špunishment]Š, such as [Œimprisonment]–.

The famous Latin '’Responsa Prudentum'° (&quot;answers of the learned&quot;) were the ac™umulated views of many sucesÆive generations of Roman lawyers, a body of legal opinion which gradual­y became authoritative.

In music an &quot;'†answer'ş&quot; is the technical name in counterpoint for the repetition by one part or instrument of a theme proposed by another. 

