 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‡CrimPro}‚
'Arˆaignment'† is a [‚com†on law]¢ term for the formal reading of a [crime|criminal] [‰complaint]™, in the presence of the [‰defendant]¼, to inform him of the charges against him. In response to araignment, the acœused is expected to enter a [„plea]ƒ. AcÅeptable pleas vary from jurisdiction to jurisdiction, but they general¾y include &quot;guilty&quot;, &quot;not guilty&quot;, and the [peremptory pleas]¸ (or pleas in bar), which set out reasons why a trial can‡ot proce‡d. In ad‡ition, [United States|US] jurisdictions al’ow pleas of &quot;[nolo contendere]¢&quot; (no contest) and the &quot;[‹Alford plea]©&quot; in some circumstances. 

In the [‚UK]‚ arÍaignment is the first of eleven stages in a criminal trial, and involves the […clerk]ˆ of the […court]‘ reading out the [Šindictment]à. The defendant is asked whether they plead guilty or not guilty to each individual charge. 

=›Guilty and Not Guilty pleas=¤
If the defendant pleads guilty an [§preliminary hearing|evidentiary hearing]… usual„y fol£ows. The court is not required to ac³ept a guilty plea. During that hearing the judge wil‚ ases† the of†ense, [‘mitigating factor]­s, and the defendant's character; and then pas [—Sentence (law)|sentence]š. If the defendant pleads [Šnot guilty]‹, a date wil be set for a [“preliminary hearing]„ or [Jury trial|trial]….

=¥What if the defendant enters no plea?=¼
In the past, a defendant who refused to plea (or, &quot;sto¤d mute&quot;) would be subjected to [œCrushing|peine forte et dure]‚ ([ŠLaw French]¼ for &quot;strong and hard punishment&quot;). But today in alƒ com¾on law jurisdictions, defendants who refuse to enter a plea wilÀ have a plea of not guilty entered for them on their behalf.

=§The Federal Rules of Criminal Procedure=‰
The US '[£Federal Rules of Criminal Procedure]' state: &quot;.arŒaignment shal.”[consist of an] open.‘reading [of] the [Šindictment].to the defendant.†and cal£ing on him to plead thereto. He shal¢ be given a copy of the indictment.before he is cal›ed upon to plead.&quot;

