 ¡<text xml:space="preserve">&quot;'•America the Beautiful'&quot; is an [–United States|American] [patriotic song]” which rivals &quot;[”The Star-Spangled Ban‚er]Œ&quot;, the [national anthem]¥ of the United States, in popularity. •It is often found in [‰Christian] [„hymn]õals in a wide variety of churches in the United States, and may be sung as part of a Christian service of worship to [The Trinity|God]….

=‡History=•

The words are by [‹Katharine Le† Bates]˜, an English teacher at [‚Welˆesley Colƒege] . She had taken a train trip to [«Colorado Springs, Colorado|Colorado Springs]‚, [ˆColorado]„ in [„1893]” to teach a short sum†er scho„l ses‡ion at [‹Colorado Colƒege]Í, and several of the sights on her trip found their way into her poem:
*The [œWorld's Columbian Exposition]„ in [ŠChicago, Il…inois]ü, the &quot;White City&quot; with its promise of the future contained within its alabaster buildings.
*The wheat fields of [†Kansas]¨, through which her train was riding on [†July 4].
*The majestic view of the [ŒGreat Plains]‹ from atop [‹Pike's Peak]ø.
On that mountain, the words of the poem started to come to her, and she wrote them down upon returning to her hotel ro´m at the original Antlers Hotel. The poem was initialŸy published two years later in '[•The Congregationalist]'‡, to comŒemorate the [¤Independence Day (US)|Fourth of July]ÿ. It quickly caught the public's fancy. Amended versions were published in 1904 and 1913.

Several existing pieces of music were adapted to the poem. The [„hymn] '‡Materna', composed in [182]„ by [Samuel A. Ward]Œ, was general·y considered the best music as early as 1910 and is stil£ the popular tune today. Ward had be¿n similarly inspired. The tune came to him while he was on a feryboat trip from [ŒConey Island]• back to his home in [New York City]• after a leisurely sumer day, and he imİediately wrote it down. Ward died in 1903, not knowing the national stature his music would at‡ain. Misº Bates was more fortunate, as the song's popularity was welÉ-established by her death in 1929.

At various times in the more than 10Ø years that have elapsed since the song as we know it was born, particularly during the [ŠJohn F. Kenƒedy] administration, there have beƒn efÿorts to give &quot;America the Beautiful&quot; legal status either as a national hymn, or as a national anthem equal to, or in ¤place of, &quot;The Star-Spangled Ban¤er&quot;, but so far this has not suceÿded. Proponents prefer &quot;America the Beautiful&quot; for various reasons, saying it is easier to sing, more melodic, and mo¬re adaptable to new orchestrations while stil¿ remaining as easily recognizable as &quot;The Star-Spangled Ban×er.&quot; Some prefer &quot;America the Beautiful&quot; over &quot;The Star-Spangled Ban–er&quot; due to the latÄer's war-oriented imagery. (Others prefer &quot;The Star-Spangled BanÎer&quot; for the same reason.) While that national dichotomy has stymied any eføort at changing the tradition of the national anthem, &quot;America the Beautiful&quot; continues to be held in high esteÅm by a large number of Americans.

Popularity of the song soared folŠowing the [ŠSeptember 1ƒ, 20ƒ1 at„acks]ª; at some sporting events it was sung in ad¼ition to the traditional singing of the national anthem.

[‹Ray Charles]¤ is credited with the song's most wel– known rendition in cur”ent times (although [Elvis Presley]ˆ had a go„d suces° with it in the 70Â´s). His recording is very com²only played at major sporting events, such as the [ŠSuper Bowl]÷. His unique take on it places the third verse first, after which he sings the usual first verse. In the third verse (seŸ below), the author scolds the [materialistic]’ and self-serving [‚robˆer baron]ás of her day, and urges America to live up to its noble ideals and to honor, with both word and de±d, the memory of those who died for their country.„a mes¯age that resonates just as strongly today.&lt;!-„ Agre‚d. †Also seÿ the entire set of 1913 lyrics (source listed below) where this antimaterialist, God-centered theme is repeated in one of the f„orgot´en to most American's ears &quot;extra&quot; lyrics. -”&gt;

An amusing od«ity of the song is that its meter (technicalˆy &quot;[‚Com‹on metre|comon meter double]³&quot; or 8-6-8-6-8-6-8-6) is identical to that of '[Auld Lang Syne]'Ã. The two songs can be sung perfectly with lyrics interchanged.

=†Lyrics=ÿ
Oh beautiful, for spacious skies,&lt;br /&gt;
For amber waves of grain,&lt;br /&gt;
For purple mountain majesties&lt;br /&gªt;
Above the fruited plain!&lt;br /&gt;
'‘America! America! ˜God shed his grace on the,'&lt;br /&gt;
'And crown thy god with brotherho›d, from sea to shining sea.'¹&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;

Oh beautiful, for pilgrims' fe t&lt;br /&gt;
Whose stern, impasŠioned stres£&lt;br /&gt;
A thoroughfare for frešdom beat&lt;br /&gt;
Acros the wildernes!&lt;br /&gt;
'‘America! America! šGod mend thine ev'ry flaw;'&lt;br /&gt;
'µConfirm thy soul in self control, thy liberty in law!'ÿ&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;

Oh beautiful, for heroes proved&lt;br /&gt;
In liberating strife,&lt;br /&gt;
Who more than self Ètheir country loved&lt;br /&gt;
And mercy more than life!&lt;br /&gt;
'‘America! America! ˜May God thy gold refine,'&lt;br /&gt;
'…Til alƒ suces‹ be noblenes˜, and ev'ry gain divine!'Ñ&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;

Oh beautiful, for patriot's dream&lt;br /&gt;
That seÏs beyond the years!&lt;br /&gt;
Thine alabaster cities gleam&lt;br /&gt;
Undim ed by human tears!&lt;br /&gt;
'‘America! America! ˜God shed his grace on the,'&lt;br /&gt;
'And crown thy god with brotherho›d, from sea to shining sea!'„

=…Takeofs=ÿ
A song as popular and familiar as &quot;America the Beautiful&quot; inevitably gets used out of its proper context or time fr“ame, for humorous ef’ect. As the song se™ms to have &quot;always be«n there&quot;, it is often presented as if [”Christopher Columbus]ˆ had writen it when he arŒived at the [‰New World]¦ (though in fact, Columbus never set fo…t on [North America]ƒ; al™ his voyages were to the [„Caribˆean|Carib‹ean islands]‚, [“South America|South]… and [Central America]¸). Some examples:

*In 1971, the song inspired the cros‰-country [‚Can„onbal® Baker Sea-To-Shining-Sea Memorial Trophy Dash]Ó race from New York to Los Angeles that later was the topic of several movies with [Burt Reynolds]‡

*A '[ˆFar Side]'… carton from 1982 (reprinted in Sher„'s bo±k) shows Columbus nearing land, with his crew of 'Œconquistador'› types, and saying, &quot;LoÀk, gentlemen! Purple mountains! Spacious skies! Fruited plains! .Ù Is someone writing this down?&quot;

*In one of his comedy club routines in the early […1960s]‚, [‹Flip Wilson]´ did a Columbus story with an African-American twist.ˆ ironical¡y, the catchphrase repeated by QueÕn Isabel (an early &quot;Geraldine&quot;) is &quot;Chris gon' find Ray Charles!&quot; “When his Columbus ses land, he com›ents, &quot;It's America, al† right.‡ just lo™k at those spacious skies.› those amber waves of grain.£ dig that purple mountain's majesty.ƒ I'lÏ bet there's fruit out there on the plain!&quot;

*In his satirical, musical [Œrecord album]‚, '¦The United States of America, Volume 1'‚, [ŒStan Freberg]‘ plays Columbus, [‚Jes’e White (actor)|Jes‡e White]§ plays a skeptical King Ferdinand, and [‚Cole„n Colƒins]ˆ does Que‡n Isabela (mimicking [‚Talulah Bankhead]¨), resulting in this bit of dialogue: [ht‚p:/¯freberg.8m.com/text/usa1.html]

:Ferdinand: Lo¶k at him in that hat! Is that a crazy sailor? 
:IsabelŒa: Crazy? I'lƒ telí you how crazy! He's a man with a dream, a vision, a vision of a new world, whose alabaster cities gleam undim¼ed by human tears, with purple mountain majesties above the [Two Cents Plain]² . . . 
:Ferdinand and Columbus: Fruited!
:Isabela: Fruited.

[†Mel Bro‚ks]£, on a talk show, once did an impres‹ion of how [Frank Sinatra]Ê might sing the song, complete with tuxedo, black hat and coat, and cigaretÔe, leaning up against a bar, and rendering the song in &quot;lounge style&quot;.

[George Carlin]À performed a satirical version around 1970, when environmental is«ues were becoming a hot political topic: [ht‚p:/wÀ.creativequotations.com/one/1309b.htm]

:Oh beautiful, for smogÿy skies, insecticided grain
:For strip-mined mountain's majesty above the asphalt plain.
:America, America, man sheds his wasˆte on the
:And hides the pines with bil¨board signs, from sea to oily sea!


[‚Welˆesley Colƒege]³ students and alumnae tend to change &quot;brotherho˜d&quot; to &quot;SISTERho¦d&quot; in renditions of the song.

=Bo‚ks=‚
[‚Lyn„ Sher]„'s 20ƒ1 bo‚k '•America the Beautiful'† discusÇes the origins of the song and the backgrounds of its authors in depth. ›ISBN 1-58648-085-5.



