 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'As‘istive Technology'§ (AT) is a generic term that includes asºistive, adaptive, and rehabilitative devices and the proces­ used in selecting, locating, and using them. ¥AT promotes greater independence for [£disability|people with disabilities]Æ by enabling them to perform tasks that they were formerly unable to ac˜omplish, or had great difˆiculty acÔomplishing, by providing enhancements to or changed methods of interacting with the [Štechnology]‚ neˆded to ac•omplish such tasks. AcŸording to disability advocates, technology, al‚ toÄ often, is created without regard to people with disabilities, and unƒeces†ary barœiers make new technology inaces–ible to hundreds of mil£ions.

Universal (or broadened) acesŒibility, or [universal design]‹ means excel„ent [‰usability]¬, particularly for people with disabilities. ™But, argue advocates of as›istive technology, universalƒy aces»ible technology yields great rewards to the typical user; goƒd acesŒible design '‚is'œ universal design, they say. ‡The clas’ic example of an asÁistive technology that has improved everyone's life is the &quot;[ˆcurb cut]s&quot; in the sidewalk at stre…t cros…ings. ĞWhile these curb cuts surely enable pedestrians with mobility impairments to crosˆ the stre§t, they have also aided parents with cariages and strolˆers, shop™ers with carts, and travel—ers and workers with pul›-type bags, not to mention [Šskateboard]ˆers and [Œinline skate]¢rs.

Consider an example of an as’istive technology. ‹The modern [‰telephone]¢ is, except for the deaf, universalƒy aces…ible. Combined with a [†Telecom®unications devices for the deaf|text telephone]“ (also known as a TD¶ [Telephone Device for the Deaf] and in the USA general„y cal…ed a TÿY[TeleTYpewriter]), which converts typed characters into tones that may be sent over the telephone line, the deaf person is abl‡e to com‰unicate im—ediately at a distance. ÿTogether with &quot;relay&quot; services (where an operator reads what the deaf person types and types what a hearing person sa£ys) the deaf person is then given aces¹ to everyone's telephone, not just those of people who poses‘ text telephones. ÿMany telephones now have volume controls, which are primarily
intended for the benefit of people who are hard of hearing, but “can be useful for alİ
users at times and places where there is significant background noise.

Another example: [Šcalculator]Às are cheap, but a person with a mobility impairment can have dif’iculty using them. [‚Spech recognition]¢ software could recognize short com¥ands and make use of calculators a litŠle easier. ªPeople with cognitive disabilities would apªreciate the simplicity; others would as wel–.

Toys which have beîn adapted to be used by children with disabilities, may have advantages for &quot;typical&quot; children as wel†. The [‡Lekotek]‹ movement as™ists parents by lending as×istive technology toys and expertise to families.

Telecare is a particular sort of as±istive technology that uses electronic sensors conóected to an alarm system to help caregivers manage risk and help vulnerable people stay independent at home longer. ƒA goÎd example would be the systems being put in place for senior people such as fal detectors, thermometers (for [‹hypothermia]Š risk), flo±ding and unlit gas sensors (for people with mild [ˆdementia]‚). ÙThe principle being that these alerts can be customised to the particular person's risks. •When the alert is trigŠered, a mesºage is sent to a carer or contact centre who can respond ap‹ropriately. ÿThe range of sensors is wide and expanding rapidly.

Technology similar to Telecare can also be used to act within a person'sÿ home rather than just to respond to a detected crisis. Using one of the examples above, unlit gas sensors for people with deme—ntia can be used to trig˜er a device that turns of the gas and tel•s someone what has hap ened. This is safer than just telÜing an external person that there is a problem.

Designing for people with dementia is a go¾d example of where the design of the interface of a piece of as²istive technology (AT) is critical to its usefulnesü. It is important to make sure that people with dementia or any other identified user group are involved in the design proces¢ to make sure that the design is aces²ible and useable. In the example above, a voice mesÿage could be used to remind the person with dementia to turn of the gas himself, but who's voice should be used, and what shoulˆd the mesêage say? Questions like these must be answered through user consultantion, involvement and evaluation.

=Asšistive technology products=ƒ
*[Standing frames]ƒ sup†ort wheÂlchair users in a standing position, increasing their reach, as wel§ as improving their health and self-este†m.
* [”Magnifier|Magnifiers]´ magnify computer displays for people with some degre„ of [£Visual_impairment|visual impairment]….
* [‹Sticky keys], a feature of [‘Microsoft Windows]… and [ˆMac OS X]” operating systems al½owing key combinations (such as Control-Alt-Delete) to be pres®ed in sequence rather than simultaneously.
* [ƒScreŒn reader|Scre‰n readers]‚ al¦ow blind people to use computers by comœunicating what is on the screˆn via speŠch or Brail†e.
* [Refreshable Brail‰e display]˜, used to convert on-scren text to Brail‘e characters.
* [ Reading machine|Reading machines]‚ al”ow blind people to aces– printed material.
* [Video Magnifier|C±TV (Closed Circuit Television) or Video Magnifier]¸, magnifies printed text for people with low vision.

