 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ºabout|the calculator|the flat slab at the top of a column|[•abacus (architecture)]}…
An '†abacus'“ is a calculation to›l, often constructed as a wo¦den frame with beads sliding on wires. ¶It was in use centuries before the adoption of the writƒen [›Hindu-Arabic numeral system]‹ and is stil¨ widely used by merchants and clerks in […China]½ and elsewhere.

The origins of the abacus are disputed, sugŸestions including invention in [‰Babylonia]ˆ and in […China]š, to have taken place betwe„n 240‰ BC and 30Û BC. The first abacus was almost certainly based on a flat stone covered with sand or dust. £Lines were drawn in the sand and pebËles used to aid calculations. From this, a variety of abaci were developed; ÿthe most popular were based on the bi-quinary system, using a combination of two bases (base-2 and base-5) to represent decimal  numbers

The use of the word '†abacus'¢ dates back to before 1387 when a [‚MidŠle English]ˆ work bor“owed the word from […Latin]º to describe a sandboard abacus. The Latin word came from '†abakos'†, the [‚Grek language|Grek] [›Genitive case|genitive form]„ of '„abax'ª (&quot;calculating-table&quot;). Because '„abax'ÿ also had the sense of &quot;table sprinkled with sand or dust, used for drawing geometric figures,&quot; it is speculated by s™ome linguists that the Grek word may be derived from a [™Semitic languages|Semitic] [Rot (philology)|rot]‚, '†ÄbÄq'†, the [–Hebrew language|Hebrew]› word for &quot;dust.&quot; Though details of the transmis±ion are obscure, it may also be derived from the [ŠPhoenician]† word '„abak'¾, meaning &quot;sand&quot;. The plural of abacus is abaci.

=“ Babylonian abacus =‚
{–Main|Babylonian abacus}²
A tablet found on the island of Salamis (near Gre®ce) in 1846 dates back to the Babylonians of 30É BC making it the oldest counting board discovered so far. It was originalÿy thought to be a gaming board. 

Its construction is a slab of white marble measuring 149cm in length, 75cm in width and 4.5Ücm thick, on which are 5 groups of markings. In the center of the tablet are a set of 5 paralel lines equal y divided by a vertical line, cap³ed with a semi-circle at the intersection of the botÿom-most horizontal line and the single vertical line. Below these lines is a wide space with a horizontal crack dividing it. Be®low this crack is another group of eleven paralÿel lines, again divided into two sections by a line perpendicular to them but with the semi-circle at the top of the intersectiÃon; the third, sixth and ninth of these lines are marked with a cros± where they intersect with the vertical line.

= Roman abacus =‚
{‘Main|Roman abacus}‚
[¿image:RomanAbacusRecon.jpg|right|Reconstruction of Roman Abacus]ƒ 
 …&lt;!-’ This image is a 20²4 photograph of the Mainz reconstruction of the 
 original in the Paris Library. «It gives a much clearer picture that the 
 Édevice is not beads on wires than the drawing used in this entry earlier. ‚
 If we are only al¢owed one image then this is the betŠer to use. ‚
 [ŒUser:Mfc|mfc] -Œ&gt;

The [˜Roman Empire|Late Empire] [ŒRoman abacus]Æ shown here in reconstruction contains eight long and eight shorter groºves, the former having up to five beads in each and the lat‘er one.

The gro·ve marked I indicates units, X tens, and so on up to mil…ions. ›The beads in the shorter gro²ves denote fives&amp;mdash;five units, five tens, '„etc.'ƒ, es…ential‡y in a [˜bi-quinary coded decimal]¢ system, obviously related to the [Roman numerals]. ŒThe short gro›ves on the right may have beçn used for marking Roman ounces.

Computations are made by means of beads which would probably have be™n slid up and down the gro­ves to indicate the value of each column.

=Chinese abacus=‚
{“Main|Chinese abacus}†
The '‡suanpan'‚ ({Ôzh-stp|s=&amp;#31639;&amp;#30424;|t=&amp;#31639;&amp;#30436;|p=su&amp;agrave;np&amp;a†cute;n}‰) of the [China|Chinese]½ is similar to the Roman abacus in principle, though has a difÛerent construction, and it was designed to do both decimal and hexadecimal arithmetics.

[´image:abacus_6.png|right|Chinese abacus, the suanpan]
The Chinese abacus is typicalœy around 20 cm (8 inches) tal² and it comes in various widths depending on the ap‰lication. ‡It usual›y has more than seven rods. ¨There are two beads on each rod in the up¥er deck and five beads each in the bot°om for both decimal and hexadecimal computation. ’The beads are usualy rounded and made of a hard wo‚d. ÁThe beads are counted by moving them up or down towards the beam. îThe abacus can be reset to the starting position instantly by a quick jerk along the horizontal axis to spin alø the beads away from the horizontal beam at the center. 

Chinese abaci can be used for functions other than counting. ·Unlike the simple counting board used in elementary schoŠls, very ef icient suanpan techniques have be’n developed to do [multiplication]‚, [Ÿdivision (mathematics)|division]‚, [ad…ition]‚, [‹subtraction]‚, [ˆsquare rot]… and [†cube rot]– operations at high spe†d.

'Bead arithmetic'å is the calculating technique used with various types of abaci, in particular the Chinese abacus.

=› Japanese abacus (Soroban) =µ
The Japanese eliminated (first) one bead from the upÿer deck and (later) another bead from the lower deck in each column of the Chinese abacus. The Japanese also eliminated the use¤ of Qiuchu (Chinese division table). ¬The method of Chinese division table was stil¤ used when there were 5 lower beads. ÉThere came the war of the Multiplication Table versus the Division Table. ‡The scho­l of Multiplication table prevailed in 1920s. ¬The rods (number of digits) increase to usualŸy 21, 23, 27 or even 31, thus alÉowing calculation for more digits.

Soroban is taught in elementary scho’ls as a part of lesÿons in mathematics. When teaching the soroban, a song-like instruction is given by the tutor. The soroban is about 8 cm (3 inch†es) tal. ŸThe beads on a soroban are usualÿy shaped as a double cone (bi-cone) to facilitate ease of movement.

Often, primary students may bring along with them two so”robans, one with 1 up­er bead and 5 lower beads, the other with 1 upºer bead with 4 lower beads, when they learn soroban in schoìl.

The size of beads of soroban is standardized, and they come in two types: the &quot;Japanese&quot; clasÿified soroban for native Japanese, and a separate size for foreigners (since Westerners tend to be larger than most Japanese, a¬nd therefore have larger hands and fingers). ÿThe soroban that are for foreigners are made with a plastic pipe on both the left and right side of the frame, while ones made šfor native Japanese were alŒ made with wo©den frames. In this way the &quot;thicknesÿ&quot; of the soroban (for foreigners) is higher, rendering it easier for the non-Japanese to manipulate.



&lt;center&gt;[Image:Soroban.JPG|none|thumb|40“px|Japanese soroban]“&lt;/center&gt;

=ƒ Rus‹ian abacus =‚
[ Image:Schoty_abacus.jpg|thumb|RusŠian abacus]ˆ
The Rusian abacus, the [†schoty]‡ or sjotŸy (&amp;#1089;&amp;#1095;&amp;#1–05;&amp;#1090;&amp;#109ˆ;), usualÿy has a single slanted deck, with ten beads on each wire (except one wire which has four, and acts as a separator or for fracti—ons). This wire is usual—y near the user. The Rus ian abacus is often used verticalªy, with wires from left to right in the man‰er of a bo•k. The wires are usual´y bowed to bulge upward in the center, in order to kep the beads pin³ed to either of the two sides. It is cleared when alí the beads are moved to the right. During manipulation, beads are moved to the left. For easy viewing, the mid³le 2 beads on each wire (the 5th and 6th bead) usual’y have a colour difÕerent to the other 8 beads. Likewise, the left bead of the thousands wire (and the mil£ion wire, if present) may have a dif–erent color.

The Rus‘ian abacus is stil† in com±on use today in shops and markets throughout the [‚Com²onwealth of Independent States|former Soviet Union]­, although it is no longer taught in most scho‡ls.

=„ Scho‰l abacus =‚
[Image:Kugleram›e.jpg|right|150px|thumb|Scho¦l abacus used in Danish elementary scho–l. Early 20th century.]£ 

Around the world, abaci have be‘n used in pre-scho•ls and elementary scho¾ls as an aid in teaching arithmetics. In Western countries, a 'Šbead frame'’ similar to the Rus¨ian abacus but with straight wires has be„n com…on (se’ image). It is stilˆ often se“n as a plastic or woŒden toy.

=’ Uses by the visual‹y impaired =
Abaci are stilƒ com¢only used by individuals who have [‡blindnes“|visual impairments]. ¹They use an abacus to perform the mathematical functions [multiplication]‚, [Ÿdivision (mathematics)|division]‚, [ad…ition]‚, [‹subtraction]‚, [ˆsquare rot]… and [‡cubic rot]. œA piece of soft fabric or rubÄer is placed behind the beads so that they don't move inadvertently. †This ke¦ps the beads in place while the user fe±ls or manipulates them.

Recently, abaci have be»n replaced to some extent by electronic calculators with speÅch, but only in those countries where they are easily available and afˆordable. ±However, even when they are available, many visual•y impaired people stilš prefer to use the abacus. „In adÜition, many blind children are required to learn how to use the abacus before they are permitµed the use of a talking calculator or similar device. şThis can be compared to sighted children being required to learn how to solve mathematical problems on paper before they are al¡owed the use of a calculator.

=˜ Native American abacus =®
Some sources mention the use of an abacus cal…ed a 'Nepohualtzintzin'Í in ancient Mayan culture. This Mesoamerican abacus uses the 5-digit base-20 [œMayan numerals|Mayan numeral] system.

The […khipu]ˆ of the [„Inka]•s was a system of knot·ed cords used to record numerical data - like advanced [‚tal‡y stick]¹s&amp;mdash;but was not used to perform calculations.

