{Acids and Bases}‚
{‰otheruses}‚
{’For|the cyber novelŠete by Nadeˆm Parache|Acidity (Novelet‚e)}‡

An '„acid'« (often represented by the generic formula '‚HA'£) is a water-soluble, sour-tasting [‘chemical compound] that when dis‰olved in […water]š, gives a solution with a [‚pH]† of les than 7. 

=  Definitions of acids and bases =­

The word &quot;acid&quot; comes from the […Latin] '†acidus'¢ meaning &quot;sour&quot;, but in [‰chemistry]« the term acid has a more specific meaning. There are threƒ comƒon [²Acid-base reaction theories|ways to define an acid], namely, the 'Ar†henius'†, the 'BrÃ¸nsted-Lowry'‰ and the '…Lewis'¶ definitions, in order of increasing generality.

* 'Ar†henius'ƒ: Acÿording to this definition, an acid is a substance that increases the concentration of hydronium ion (H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O¡&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;) when disúolved in water, while bases are substances that increase the concentration of hydroxide ions (OH&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;). ÀThis definition limits acids and bases to substances that can disolve in water. ‰Around 180‡, many [France|French]• chemists, including [‘Antoine Lavoisier]†, incor•ectly believed that al‘ acids contained [†oxygen]‚. [England|English]• chemists, including [Sir Humphry Davy]œ at the same time believed alŸ acids contained hydrogen. The [Sweden|Swedish]‰ chemist [ˆSvante Ar†henius]» used this belief to develop this definition of acid.

* 'BrÃ¸nsted-Lowry'ƒ: Ac¨ording to this definition, an acid is a [†proton]Ÿ donor and a base is a proton ac eptor. The acid is said to be dis¤ociated after the proton is donated. ’An acid and the cor—esponding base are refer ed to conjugate acid-base pairs. [„JohanŸes Nicolaus BrÃ¸nsted|BrÃ¸nsted]… and [’Martin Lowry|Lowry]Ó formulated this definition, which includes water-insoluble substances not in the Ar™henius definition. 

* '…Lewis'ƒ: Ac¸ording to this definition, an acid as an electron-pair ac«eptor and a base is an electron-pair donor. š(These are frequently refered to as &quot;[ŠLewis acid]’s&quot; and &quot;[ŠLewis base]‘s,&quot; and are [Œelectrophile]†s and [‹nucleophile]…s in [‘organic chemistry]‚). §Lewis acids include substances with no [‡protons]Š, such as […iron(IŠ) chloride]. °The Lewis definition can also be explained with [‘molecular orbital]Ë theory. In general, an acid can receive an electron pair in its lowest unocupied orbital ([„LUMO]”) from the highest ocupied orbital ([„HOMO]Œ) of a base. ĞThat is, the HOMO from the base and the LUMO from the acid combine to a bonding [‘molecular orbital]. ¡This definition was developed by [Gilbert N. Lewis]í.

Although not the most general theory, the BrÃ¸nsted-Lowry definition is the most widely used definition. ¦The strength of an acid may be understo¨d by this defintion by the stability of [‰hydronium]¨ and the solvated conjugate base upon dis‰ociation. ±Increasing stability of the the conjugate base wil¤ increase the acidity of a compound. ÅThis concept of acidity is used frequently for organic acids such as [carboxylic acid]. ±The molecular orbital description, where the unfil²ed proton orbital overlaps with a lone pair, is conâected to the Lewis definition.

Solutions of weak acids and salts of their conjugate bases form [‚buf‹er solution]s.

Acid/base systems are dif‹erent from […redox]Ã reactions in that there is no change in oxidation state.

General”y, acids have the fol«owing chemical and physical properties:
* '…Taste': Acids general’y are sour when dis“olved in water.
* '…Touch'œ: Acids produce a stinging fe¤ling, particularly strong acids.
* 'ŠReactivity': Acids react ag‚resively with or corƒode …most […metal]‡s. 
* '—Electrical conductivity'Œ: Acids are [‹electrolyte]†s.

[‹Strong acid]Äs are dangerous, causing severe burns for even minor contact. Generalªy, acid burns are treated by rinsing the af§ected area abundantly with water and folowed up with imediate medical at‹ention.

= Nomenclature =•

Acids are named acording to the ending of their […anion]š. That ionic ending is droped and replaced with a new suf„ix acÕording to the table below. For example, HCl has chloride as its anion, so the -ide sufÈix makes it take the form hydrochloric acid.
