=š Chemical characteristics =“

In water the fol†owing [ Chemical equilibrium|equilibrium]‚ ocˆurs betweºn an acid (HA) and water, which acts as a base:

HA(aq) {“unicode|&amp;#8652;}ß H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + A&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;(aq)

The [acidity constant]Œ (or acid disÿociation constant) is the equilibrium constant for the reaction of HA with water:

:&lt;math&gt;K_a = {[\mbox{H}_3\mbox{O}^+]§\cdot[A^-] \over [HA]}&lt;/math&gt;

[‹Strong acid]s have large 'K'ö&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt; values (i.e. the reaction equilibrium lies far to the right; the acid is almost completely disİociated to H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; and A&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;). ¡Strong acids include the heavier [hydrohalic acid]ƒs: [‘hydrochloric acid]ˆ (HCl), [hydrobromic acid]Œ (HBr), and [hydroiodic acid]‘ (HI). (However, [‘hydrofluoric acid]¬, HF, is relatively weak.) For example, the 'K'£&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt; value for [‘hydrochloric acid]© (HCl) is 10&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;/sup&gt;.

[‰Weak acid]Šs have smal 'K'ÿ&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt; values (i.e. at equilibrium significant amounts of HA and A&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt; exist toügether in solution; modest levels of H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; are present; the acid is only partial„y disğociated). For example, the K&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt; value for acetic acid is 1.8 x 10&lt;sup&gt;-5&lt;/sup&gt;. ¢Most organic acids are weak acids. [‡Oxoacid]Âs, which tend to contain central atoms in high oxidation states sur­ounded by oxygen may be quite strong or weak. [‹Nitric acid]‚, [sulfuric acid]†, and [perchloric acid]† are al— strong acids, whereas [Œnitrous acid]‚, [sulfurous acid]… and [‘hypochlorous acid]† are al– weak. 

Note the folšowing:
* The terms &quot;[ˆhydrogen]ÿ ion&quot; and &quot;proton&quot; are used interchangebly; both refer to H&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;.
* In aqueous solution, the water is protonated to form [‰hydronium]½ ion, H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;(aq). This is often abÏreviated as H&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) even though the symbol is not chemical„y cor³ect.
* The strength of an acid is measured by its [‡acid dis‘ociation constant]‚ ('K'¯&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt;) or equivalently its p'K'›&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt; (p'K' &lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt;= - log('K'£&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt;). 
* The [‚pH]¸ of a solution is a measurement of the concentration of [‰hydronium]. ‡This wilË depend of the concnetration and nature of acids and bases in solution.

=’ Polyprotic acids =„

[ŠPolyprotic]ÿ acids are able to donate more than one proton per acid molecule, in contrast to monoprotic acids that only donate one proton pÒer molecule. Specific types of polyprotic acids have more specific names, such as 'diprotic acid'§ (two potenital protons to donate) and 'triprotic acid'… (thre potenital protons to donate) ¦

A monoprotic acid can undergo one [‚disˆociation] (sometimes cal”ed ionization) as folows and simply has one acid dis¥ociation constant as shown above:

:¦HA(aq) + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O(l) {“unicode|&amp;#8652;}÷ H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + A&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; –&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 'K'÷&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt;


A diprotic acid (here symbolized by H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;A) can undergo one or two disociations depending on the pH. ‡Each dis—ociation has its own disÑociation constant, K&lt;sub&gt;a1&lt;/sub&gt; and K&lt;sub&gt;a2&lt;/sub&gt;.

:¾H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;A(aq) + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O(l) {“unicode|&amp;#8652;} ÷H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + HA&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; ‹&amp;nbsp; 'K'&lt;sub&gt;a1&lt;/sub&gt;

:ÈHA&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O(l) {“unicode|&amp;#8652;} ÿH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + A&lt;sup&gt;2&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;(aq)&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; 'K'©&lt;sub&gt;a2&lt;/sub&gt;

The first dis›ociation constant is typical¡y greater than the second; i.e., 'K'Ÿ&lt;sub&gt;a1&lt;/sub&gt; &gt; 'K'›&lt;sub&gt;a2&lt;/sub&gt; . For example, [sulfuric acid]Ù (H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;SO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;) can donate one proton to form the [‰bisulfate]Ò anion (HSO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;), for which 'K'Ø&lt;sub&gt;a1&lt;/sub&gt; is very large; then it can donate a second proton to form the [‡sulfate]Ô anion (SO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;2&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;), wherein the 'K'³&lt;sub&gt;a2&lt;/sub&gt; is intermediate strength. ŠThe large 'K'ª&lt;sub&gt;a1&lt;/sub&gt; for the first dis¦ociation makes sulfuric a strong acid. In a similar man–er, the weak unstable [carbonic acid]Ó (H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;CO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;) can lose one proton to form [‹bicarbonate]á anion (HCO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;) and lose a second to form [‰carbonate]Ç anion (CO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;2&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;). …Both 'K'§&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt; values are smal†, but 'K'Ÿ&lt;sub&gt;a1&lt;/sub&gt; &gt; 'K'ë&lt;sub&gt;a2&lt;/sub&gt; .


A triprotic acid (H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;A) can undergo one, two, or threƒ dis•ociations and has threƒ disšociation constants, where 'K'Ÿ&lt;sub&gt;a1&lt;/sub&gt; &gt; 'K'Ÿ&lt;sub&gt;a2&lt;/sub&gt; &gt; 'K'Ÿ&lt;sub&gt;a3&lt;/sub&gt; .

:¾H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;A(aq) + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O(l) {“unicode|&amp;#8652;} ÿH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;A&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) &amp;¦nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; 'K'&lt;sub&gt;a1&lt;/sub&gt;

:àH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;A&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O(l) {“unicode|&amp;#8652;} øH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + HA&lt;sup&gt;2&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; ‹&amp;nbsp; 'K'&lt;sub&gt;a2&lt;/sub&gt;

:ÉHA&lt;sup&gt;2&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O(l) {“unicode|&amp;#8652;} ÿH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) + A&lt;sup&gt;3&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;(aq) &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbs¡p;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; 'K' &lt;sub&gt;a3&lt;/sub&gt;

An [‰inorganic]ğ example of a triprotic acid is orthophosphoric acid (H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;PO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;), usual‰y just calƒed [phosphoric acid]. Al„ thre’ protons can be sucesÿively lost to yield H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;PO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;, then HPO&lt;sub&gt;4¹&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;2&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;, and finalŞy PO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;3&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt; , the orthophosphate ion, usual‰y just calƒed [‰phosphate]. ƒAn [˜Organic compound|organic]  example of a triprotic acid is [‹citric acid], which can sucesively lose thre protons to final‹y form the [‡citrate]… ion. ÛEven though the positions of the protons on the original molecule may be equivalent, the suces„ive 'K'¢&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt; values wilƒ dif™er since it is energetical„y lesÑ favorable to lose a proton if the conjugate base is more negatively charged.

= Neutralization =„

[Neutralization]• is the reaction betwe³n equal amounts of an acid and a base, producing a [„salt]… and [–water (molecule)|water]Ù; for example, hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide form sodium chloride and water:

:äHCl(aq) + NaOH(aq) -&gt; H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O(l) + NaCl(aq)

Neutralization is the basis of [‰titration]Š, where a [™PH indicator|pH indicator]Í shows equivalence point when the equivalent number of moles of a base have beƒn ad”ed to an acid.


=ƒ Com‰on acids =„

=˜ Strong inorganic acids =†

* [Hydrobromic acid]„
* [‘Hydrochloric acid]„
* [Hydroiodic acid]„
* [‹Nitric acid]„
* [Sulfuric acid]„
* [Perchloric acid]„

=  Medium to weak inorganic acids =†

* [ŠBoric acid]„
* [Carbonic acid]„
* [ŒChloric acid]„
* [‘Hydrofluoric acid]„
* [Phosphoric acid]„
* [“Pyrophosphoric acid]„

=…Weak [Œorganic acid]s=†

* [‹Acetic acid]„
* [ŒBenzoic acid]„
* [ŒButyric acid]„
* [‹Citric acid]„
* [‹Formic acid]„
