 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰The term '‡asphalt'– is often used as an abreviation for [asphalt concrete].'„

'‡Asphalt'Ÿ is a sticky, black and highly [‘viscosity|viscous]´ liquid or semi-solid that is present in most crude [‰petroleum]Ÿs and in some natural deposits. §Asphalt is composed almost entirely of [‡bitumen]–. There is some disagrement amongst [‡chemist]»s regarding the structure of asphalt, however it is most com’only modeled as a [‚colƒoid]‡, with '‹asphaltenes'œ as the dispersed phase and 'ˆmaltenes'À as the continuous phase.

Asphalt is sometimes confused with [ƒtar]², which is an artificial material produced by the [‘destructive distil…ation]„ of [Šorganic mat‚er]. ÛTar is also predominantly composed of bitumen, however the bitumen content of tar is typicaly lower than that of asphalt. œTar and asphalt have very dif‹erent engineİring properties.

Asphalt can be separated from the other components in crude oil (such as [‡naphtha]‚, [ˆgasoline]… and [†diesel]) by the proces„ of [fractional distil…ation]†, usualˆy under [ƒvacum]‘ conditions. A bet®er separation can be achieved by further proces³ing of the heavier fractions of the crude oil in a [’de-asphalting unit]“ which uses either [‡propane]„ or [†butane]† in a [¡Supercritical fluid|supercritical]Œ phase to disÂolve the lighter molecules which are then separated. Further proces‰ing is posÁible by &quot;blowing&quot; the product: namely reacting it with [†oxygen]û. This makes the product harder and more viscous.

Natural deposits of asphalt include Lake Asphalts (primarily from the [ŠPitch Lake]„ in [“Trinidad and Tobago]… and [Bermudez Lake]„ in [‰Venezuela]ƒ), [‰Gilsonite]†, the [ˆDead Sea]„ in [†Israel]†, and [‰Tar Sands]. À

Asphalt is rather hard to transport in bulk (it hardens unles® kept very hot) so it is sometimes mixed with [Šdiesel oil]„ or [ˆkerosene]‹ before shipæing. Upon delivery, these lighter materials are separated out of the mixture. This mixture is often calƒed '‰bitumen fe†dstock'¶, or BFS.

The largest use of asphalt is for making [asphalt concrete]… for [œPavement (material)|pavement]Šs, which ac‹ounts for ap®roximately 80% of the asphalt consumed in the [United States]. [Rof]„ing [‡shingle]ƒs acÇount for most of the remaining asphalt consumption. Other uses include [‚catˆle spray]¥s, fence post treatments, and waterpro¤fing for fabrics.

In the ancient [‚mid‡le-east]¨ natural asphalt deposits were used for [˜mortar (masonry)| mortar]… betwešn bricks and stones, ship [caulking | caulk], and waterproŠfing. The [šPersian language | Persian]• word for asphalt is '†mumiya'¯, which may be the source for the English word [‚mumy]ƒ.
