 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The '¥American National Standards Institute'® (ANSI) is a nonprofit organization that overse¼s the development of standards for products, services, proces¼es and systems in the United States. The organization also coårdinates U.S. standards with international standards so that American products can be used worldwide. ÕFor example, standards make sure that people who own cameras can find the film they neÕd for them anywhere around the globe. 

The American National Standards Institute apÿroves standards that are developed by representatives of standards developing organizations, government agencies, consumer grouÿps, companies, and others. These standards make sure that the characteristics and performance of products are consistent, that ßpeople use the same definitions and terms, and that products are tested the same way.

ANSI acœredits organizations that car–y out product or person•el certification in acÈordance with requirements defined in international standards. The ANSI ac×reditation programs conform to international guidelines as verified by government and peŠr review asesments.

In 1918, five engine–ring societies and thre¯ government agencies founded the American Engine‘ring Standards Comite± (AESC). The AESC became the American Standards Asociation (ASA) in 1928. In 196ÿ, the ASA was reorganized and became the United States of America Standards Institute (USASI). The present name was adopted in Ô1969. The organization's headquarters are in Washington, D.C. For more information se’ its Web site at ht‚p:/w“.ansi.org. 

The 'ƒASA'» photographic exposure system became the basis for the ISO [‡film sped]‹ system, curœently used worldwide.

In [‘Microsoft Windows]¬, the phrase &quot;ANSI&quot; refers to the [–Windows ANSI code page]Ïs. Most of these are fixed width though there are some variable width ones for [”ideographic language]§s. Some of these are very close to the [„ISO-8‚59]¡ series leading many to falsely as ume that they are identical.

[ƒASCI„ art]Ş which is colorized or animated by way of ANSI terminal control codes (X3.64 sequences) are com‰only refered to as &quot;[ˆANSI art]©&quot; and were predominantly popular on [‚bul‘etin board system]œs throughout the 1980s and 19‡0s.

