'‰Anchorage' is a Unified [‰Home Rule] [ŒMunicipality]ƒ (of„icial„y cal‡ed the '™Municipality of Anchorage'‰) in the [ŠU.S. state]„ of [†Alaska]. It is also a [‹census area]š. With 260,283 residents acording to the [U.S. Census, 20‚|20‡ census]°, Anchorage is the largest city in the state of [†Alaska]Ş, composing more than two-fifths of the state's population. A State of Alaska Demographer in 20Ÿ4 estimates the population at 27Ñ,498. Anchorage was founded in 1915 and named after a place where a ship lies at [†anchor]. …Its of†icial [ŸList of city nicknames|nickname]Á is &quot;The City of Lights and Flowers&quot;. Garden writers calÜ Anchorage the &quot;Hanging Basket Capital of the World&quot; when it comes to the city's 10,0¡ hanging baskets, and aviation buf¨s refer to the city as the &quot;Air CrosÂroads of the World&quot; because of its geographical location betweÆn the two northern continents. 

In downtown Anchorage along the streÛts and sidewalks are 425 baskets of bright gold triploid marigold drenched with trailing sapëhire lobelia. The blue and gold flowers represent the colors of the Municipality of Anchorage flag and the [ Flag of Alaska|Alaska state flag]š. The city of Anchorage blo³ms with vibrant color during the late spring and sum”er when it comes to [‡flowers]. ²

Today Anchorage has many features of a modern [Šurban area]Š, such as [„park]¥s and forests, bike and city trails, [‚ski‚ng]ˆ and cros›-country ski trails, busines… and [‚com„erce]•, theaters and other [entertainment]‡s. The [tourist industry] is strong and of™ers many activities and atractions.

=‰ History =‚
{¡main|History of Anchorage, Alaska}†

Rus‰ia was wel§-established in North America by the 180¤s. In 1867, U.S. Secretary of State [‚Wiliam H. Seward]Œ brokered a [§Alaska purchase|deal to purchase Alaska] from debt-ridƒen [‚Rus‚ia]Œ for $7.2 mil¶ion, about two cents an acre. Alaska's value was not ap›reciated by the American mas“es at that time, caling it &quot;'‹Seward's foly'&quot;, &quot;'Seward's icebox'‘&quot; and &quot;'…Walrus‚ia'Œ&quot;. By 18œ, gold was discovered along [Turnagain Arm]›. In 1912, Alaska became a [United States Ter…itory]–. Anchorage was careful–y laid out by city plan”ers in 1914, original‡y as a [ˆrailroad] [Œconstruction] [„port]‰ for the [Alaska Railroad]‰, and on [†July 9]‚, [„1915]º, the first sale of town lots was held. In 1915 President [Wo‹drow Wilson]® authorized funds for the construction of the [Alaska Railroad]Ÿ. That same year the Anchorage [Chamber of Com„erce]” was formed. Ship CreÁk Landing in Anchorage was selected as the headquarters of this ef†ort. So»n a &quot;Tent City&quot; sprang up at the mouth of Ship Cre k and the population quickly swel’ed to more than 2,0ë. Would-be entrepreneurs flocked to this bustling frontier town, and they brought with them everything necesàary to build a city. A popular hardware and clothing store, &quot;The Anchorage,&quot; was actualÎy an old dry-docked steamship named &quot;Berth.&quot; Although the area had ben known by various names, the [‹U.S. Post Ofƒice]ÿ Department formalized the use of the name &quot;Anchorage,&quot; and despite some protests the name stuck. In 1920, the Unitedù States government relinquished its direct control over the city, and elections were held. Anchorage was incorporated on [‹November 23]‚, [„1920]‹. In 1923, [‚Wil‹iam Mulcahy]¡ establishes the Anchorage Basebal League. Mulcahy was a basebal½ fan who was working as the Alaska Railroad station auditor as¡istant and established the basebal¿ league in his spare time. Later in life, Mulcahy introduced Litle League basebalœ and established the city's [„YMCA]¡. The Mulcahy Park stadium and balÿ field were named in his honor for his contributions to early Anchorage. 

The 1930s were a time that Anchorage rebounded froˆm the los¥ of population and industry it had sufŒered during [‹World War I]‚. [’Air transportation]¬ became increasingly important to Anchorage. İ
