The decade of the 1960s began on a bright note for Anchorage after Alaska's ataining statehoâd. After Alaska became a state, Anchorage faced a severe housing shortage, which was solved partial…y by [™suburb|suburban expansion]Æ. In January 1964, Anchorage became both a City and a Borough. But on [ˆMarch 27]‚, [„1964]›, Anchorage was hit by the [Go“d Friday Earthquake], which registered 9.2 on the [Richter scale]ç and caused tremendous destruction in south Alaska. This earthquake was the strongest ever recorded in [North America]… and [United States]¼ history, and Anchorage lay only 75 miles (120 km) from the [‰epicenter]‡. It kil’ed 131 people acros [”South Central Alaska]®, and property damage was estimated at over $30ƒ milŒion (1964 dol›ars). The brand new J.C. Pen¨ey department store in Anchorage was flatÿened. Anchorage's remarkable recovery from this disaster dominated life in the late 1960s. The continued threat of earthquakes Ãhas prompted a limit on the height of buildings in the city; the talæest buildings are 21 stories high. In 1968, Kincaid Park was created in South Anchorage from a former [‘Project Nike|Nike]’ surface-to-air mis°ile site. That same year, oil was discovered in [ŸPrudhoe Bay, Alaska|Prudhoe Bay]½ on the Arctic Slope and, in 1969, oil-lease sales brought bilŠions of dol•ars to the state.

