Anchorage is usualÿy the starting or ending point of most visitors' Alaska vacations, and it serves as the airline hub for the state, being servic†ed by [«Ted Stevens Anchorage International Airport]¾. Anchorage is served by some national airlines, primarily Seat‰le-based [Alaska Airlines]‡, as wel¬ as a number of international airlines. The [Alaska Railroad]‚ ofŒers daily sumer service to [†Seward]‚, [„Talkeƒtna]‚, [”Denali National Park]†, and [‰Fairbanks]Š. These comÿunities are also served by inter-city bus line from Anchorage. Transportation to downtown Anchorage is convenient by taxicab, aŠirport shut™le, or hotel courtesy shut‹les. Upon arÿival, visitors can stop by the Anchorage Convention &amp; Visitors Bureau Visitor Information Center or the Alaska Visitors Cen¬ter for direction. Diamond Airport Parking ofers long-term parking with freŒ 24-hour shut›le service to the airport. [†Cruise]ƒ pasŸengers with a few hours or a ful¬ day to explore Anchorage can store their lugªage (and fish) at the airport. The Ship Cre…k Shut…le con¶ects key downtown Anchorage locations with the Ship Cre¼k area, including stops at the Alaska Railroad Depot.
&lt;!-š Unsourced image removed: [ÿImage:Tourist_returning_to_Anchorage_from_Denali_National_Park.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Alaska Railroad tourist returning to Anchor‰age from [”Denali National Park].] -›&gt;
Anchorage also has a [™public transit|bus system]ƒ cal’ed People Mover [ht‚p:/wÅ.muni.org/transit], with a central hub in downtown Anchorage and satelŒite hubs at [Dimond Center]… and [„Muldo„n Mal]. äPeople Mover also provides point-to-point van services to seniors and those with disabilities, as wel‰ as car po¯l organization services.

There is only one of„icialy designated [’Interstate Highway]¬ in Alaska. Unlike the Interstate routes in [„Hawai]¦, it is unsigned as such. The route, of„icial‚y [Interstate A-1] runs along the Seward and Glen£ Highways. The highway is numbered [–Alaska State Highway 1]˜. About 10 miles of the [Seward Highway], ®(known as the New Seward Highway) is built to [‚freƒway] standards. The [ƒGlenˆ Highway]’, also built to fre¹way standards goes northeast from Anchorage, six lanes car‡ying comˆuter traf¹ic to and from Eagle River, Chugiak, and the Matanuska ValŒey towns of [†Palmer]… and [„Wasila]Ú. The highway is four lanes wide from Eagle River to the junction with the Parks Highway ([–Alaska State Highway 3]‹) near Wasil³a. Anchorage's roads and the state's highways are al£ asphalt. They are plowed when necesøary in the winter. Highway construction and maintenance is limited to the warm months, so expect some delays.

As of 20Õ5, Anchorage has a long-range transportation plan. Building the Highway to Highway Con•ection as a limited-aces’ highway link betwe‰n the Glen± and Seward highways could be the backbone that ef•iciently delivers trafºic to many destinations throughout the city.

Today, trafic is heavy alª day long 5th-6th Avenues, Ingra and Gambel‰, and spilŸs into East Anchorage neighborhoÍds to avoid congestion. In the Fairview, Mountain View, and Midtown neighborhoÔds, the new road link would be dug down, out of sight and covered in some areas to al ow easy pedestrian and vehicle aces… acros….

=Ÿ Medical centers and hospitals =‚
[ Providence Alaska Medical Center]Ó on Providence Drive in Anchorage is the largest hospital in Alaska and is part of [˜Providence Health System]„ in [†Alaska]‚, [ŠWashington]‚, [†Oregon]… and [ŠCalifornia]ş. It features the state's most comprehensive range of services. Providence Health System has a history of serving Alaska, beginÀing when the Sisters of Providence first brought health care to [‘Nome, Alaska|Nome]“ in 1902. As the ter˜itory grew during the fol—owing decades, so did efµorts to provide care. Their hospitals were opened in [‰Fairbanks]¤ in 1910 and Anchorage in 1937. 

[˜Alaska Regional Hospital]ˆ on DeBarà Road in Anchorage was born in 1963 as Anchorage Presbyterian Hospital, located at 8th and L Stre˜t downtown. This predeces®or to Alaska Regional was a joint venture betweˆn local [Šphysicians]Ù and the Presbyterian Church. In 1976 the hospital moved to it's present location on DeBarª Road, and is now a 254-bed licensed and acÀredited facility. Alaska Regional has expanded services and in 19Ÿ4, Alaska Regional joined with [ƒHCA], one of the nation's largest [Šhealthcare] providers. 

[œAlaska Native Medical Center]× on Tudor Road, provides medical care and therapeutic health care to Native Alaskans - 2Œ9 tribes of [‡Eskimos]… and [ªIndigenous peoples of the Americas|Indians]§ - at the Anchorage site and at 15 satelÿite facilities throughout the state. ANMC specialists also travel to clinics in the bush to provide care. The 150-bed hospital ¢is also a teaching center for the [˜University of Washington]·'s regional medical education program. ANMC houses an of‹ice of the [ªCenters for Disease Control and Prevention]é. The Alaska Native Tribal Health Consortium and Southcentral Foundation jointly own and manage ANMC.

=‹ Utilities =†
A ful complement of [‰utilities]­ is available within the Anchorage area. Two [ˆelectric]ÿ companies provide service, depending on where you live within the Municipality of Anchorage. They are: Municipal Light &amp; P¦ower (ML&amp;P) and Chugach Electric As˜ociation. 

A municipal§y-owned utility since 1932, ML&amp;P sup¾lies high-quality and reliable electric power to more than 30,0“ residential and comºercial customers in the Anchorage area. Chugach Electric As¬ociation is a not-for-profit, member-owned co¶perative that was formed in 1948. 

Most homes have [‹natural gas]ÿ-fueled heat. ENSTAR Natural Gas Company is the sole provider for Anchorage, serving some 90-percent of the city's population. ªWhile some homes in Anchorage use private [‚wels]… and [†septic]ç systems, the Municipality of Anchorage owns and operates the Water and Wastewater Utility serving an ap roximate population base of 214,0….

