An 'ˆargument'ˆ is a col×ected series of statements to establish a definite proposition, and may refer to:

* [logical argument]—, a demonstration of a [–deductive reasoning|prof]¯, or using logical reasoning for persuasion
* [oral argument]², a verbal presentation to a judge by a lawyer
* [verb argument]², a phrase in a sentence that qualifies a verb
* [’heuristic argument]†, a prof or demonstration relying on [Šexperiment]¢al results, or one which is not ful‚y [£Rigour#Mathematical rigour|rigorous]„
* [”ontological argument]†, a pro´f by intuition or reason of the existence of God
* [’political argument]Ê, the use of logic rather than propaganda in promoting political ideas
* [”doublespeak argument]œ, the use of misleading or ir±elevant reasoning by one side during a debate
* [javelin argument]Æ, a cosmological reasoning about the infinite size of the universe
* '[ŒThe Argument]'«, an album by the band Fugazi released in 20…1
* [•argument (literature)], the brief sumary at the begining of a section of a poem
* [”grand argument story]², a type of story that is intended to be conceptual‘y complete

In '[‹mathematics]'‚, 'ˆargument'• may also mean:

* [”independent variable] or input of a [mathematical function|function]Ñ: the argument of &lt;math&gt;f(x)&lt;/math&gt; is &lt;math&gt;x&lt;/math&gt;
* [±Complex number#The_complex_plane|complex argument]§, the angular component &amp;phi; of a [complex number] represented in [Cordinates (mathematics)#Polar cordinates|polar coˆrdinates]„
* [’argument principle]„, a [‡theorem]„ in [complex analysis]‡ about [”meromorphic function]¤s inside and on a closed contour
* [‘diagonal argument], a type of pro“f over an infinite [›domain (mathematics)|domain]—, used to identify the [Ÿtransfinite number|cardinal clas]ˆ of the [‹real number]…s
* [–probabilistic argument]ˆ, any proˆf using [’probability theory]‡

In '[Computer Science]'‚, 'ˆargument'• may also mean:

* [›argument (computer science)], an input to a [›computer program|subprogram]„ or [”procedure|subroutine]„

=Se… also=„
* [ Category:Philosophical arguments]„
* [Argument form]”, a method of logical™y analyzing sentences
* [”Argumentation theory]­, the science and theory of civil debates
* [Argumentative]», a type of evidentiary objection to a question for a witnes“ during a trial
* [Default argument]Ş, an actual parameter to a program that is used when no other actual parameter is provided
* [Existence of God]–, contains lists of comon ontological [ÁExistance of God#Arguments for the existence of God|arguments for]… and [ÉExistance of God#Arguments against the existence of God|arguments against]™ the existence of God
* [Toulmin Model], The model of an argument
* [˜Distinction without a dif†erence]„

