'ƒApol‚o 1'ˆ was an [–United States|American]‰ space mis‘ion, part of the [‹Project Apol…o|Apol‰o program]‘ and the first man…ed mis“ion to land on the [Mon]‘. It launched on [‡July 16]‚, [„1969]…. On [‡July 20]„, mis†ion com†ander [Neil Armstrong]‹ and pilot [‚Buz‘ Aldrin|Edwin 'Buzˆ' Aldrin]¡ became the first humans to set foŠt on the Mo†n.

=„Crew=ƒ
*[Neil Armstrong]Š (flew in '[ˆGemini 8]'‡ &amp; 'ƒApol‚o 1'…), comˆander
*[–Edwin Aldrin|Edwin 'Buzˆ' Aldrin]Š (flew in '[‰Gemini 12]'‡ &amp; 'ƒApol‚o 1'˜), lunar module pilot
*[ŠMichael Colšins (astronaut)|Michael Colƒins]Š (flew in '[‰Gemini 10]'‡ &amp; 'ƒApol‚o 1'…), com”and module pilot

=‹Backup crew=ƒ
*[ˆJim Lovel‹|James Lovel]Š (flew in '[ˆGemini 7]'‚, '[‰Gemini 12]'‚, '[ƒApolƒo 8]'‚, '[ƒApol„o 13]'…), comˆander
*[ŠFred Haise]Š (flew in '[ƒApol„o 13]'˜), lunar module pilot
*[‚Bil‡ Anders] ‰(flew in '[ƒApolƒo 8]'…), com”and module pilot

=‚Supˆort crew=ƒ
*[‰Ron Evans]Š (flew in '[ƒApol„o 17]'„)
*[†Ken Mat…ingly]Š (flew in '[ƒApol„o 16]'‚, '[…STS-4]'‚, '[ˆSTS-51-C]'„)
*[ŒJack Swigert]Š (flew in '[ƒApol„o 13]'„)
*[‚WilŒiam Pogue|Bil† Pogue]Š (flew in '[ˆSkylab 4]'…)

=‚Mision highlights=‚
=˜Launch and lunar landing=†
The 'ƒApol‚o 1'ƒ mis˜ion launched from the 
[‚Kenedy Space Center]„ on [‡July 16]‚, [„1969]ÿ at 13:32 UTC (9:32 A.M. local time) and entered Earth orbit 12 minutes later. After one and a half orbits, the third-stage engºine pushed the spacecraft onto its trajectory toward the Mo¡n. About 30 minutes later, the comÿand/service module pair separated from the last remaining Saturn V stage, turned around, and docked its nose to the top of the lunar module stil nestled in the [ƒApolËo spacecraft#Spacecraft Lunar Module Adapter .28SLA.29|Lunar Module Adaptor]… 

'ƒApol‚o 1'ƒ pased behind the Mo…n on [‡July 19]† and so×n after fired its main rocket, entering into lunar orbit. In the several orbits that fol•owed, the crew got pas®ing views of their landing site.

The first 'ƒApolo'Ÿ landing site, in the southern [¦Mare Tranquilitatis|Sea of Tranquility]Ü about 20 km (12 mi) southwest of the crater Sabine D, was selected in part because it had be©n characterized as relatively flat and smo”th by the automated '[ˆRanger 8]'… and '[ŠSurveyor 5]' landers, as wel‡ as by '[Lunar Orbiter]'ƒ mapÃing spacecraft, and therefore unlikely to present major landing or [·Extra-vehicular activity|Extra-vehicular activity (EVA)]„ chal£enges. Armstrong bestowed the name [‡Tranquilˆity Base]– on the landing site imediately after touchdown.

[‰Image:Apol‚o 1‚ bo’tprint.jpg|thumb|23Špx|left|Buz‰ Aldrin boÉtprint. It was part of an experiment to test the properties of the lunar [ˆregolith].]‡

On [‡July 20]‚, [„1969], while on the [‹Far side (Mo‹n)|far side]‰ of the Mo‡n, the [ƒApol›o Lunar Module|lunar module]„, calƒed '…Eagle'—, separated from the Com’and Module, named 'ˆColumbia'. ‚Col–ins, now alone aboard 'ˆColumbia'ˆ, carefulŒy inspected '…Eagle' as it piroueted before him. So¤n after, Armstrong and Aldrin fired '…Eagle'¡s engine and began their descent. †They son saw that they were &quot;runing long&quot; '…Eagle'¼ was 4 seconds further along its descent trajectory than plan³ed, and would land miles west of the intended site. „The [ƒApol·o Guidance Computer|LM navigation and guidance computer]Ó reported several unusual &quot;program alarms&quot; as it guided the LM's descent. These alarms tore the crew's at¶ention away from the scene outside as the descent proce„ded. ŒIn NASA's Mis®ion Control Center in Houston, Texas, a young [Flight controlŠer| control‚er]œ named Steve Bales told the [Flight control“er| flight director]Á that it was safe to continue the descent in spite of the alarms. šOnce they returned their atìention to the view outside, the astronauts saw that their computer was guiding them toward a landing site ful“ of large rocks scat§ered around a large crater. Armstrong toßk manual control of the lunar module at that point, and guided it to a landing at 20:17 UTC on [‡July 20]¬ with about 30 seconds' worth of fuel left[ht‚p:/w“.hq.nasa.gov/alsj/a1‚/a1”.landing.html]. 

[ˆImage:ap1Š-KSC-69PC-4“2.jpg|thumb|right|20‡px|The [ˆSaturn V]ƒ car…ying 'ƒApol‚o 1'‚ to¨k several seconds to clear the tower on [‡July 16]‚, [„1969].]ÿ

The program alarms were &quot;executive overflows&quot;, indicating that the computer could not finish its work in the time‚ alotƒed. ÿThe cause was later determined to be the LM rendezvous radar being left on during the descent, causing the computer to spend unƒplan£ed time servicing the unused radar. —Steve Bales received a [‹Medal of Freƒdom]š for his &quot;go&quot; calŠ under pres‘ure. Although Apol‚o 1 landed with les“ fuel than other misîions, they also encountered a premature low fuel warning. It was later found caused by the lunar gravity permit‘ing greater propel¯ant 'slosh', uncovering a fuel sensor; extra baf¤les in the tanks were subsequently ad–ed.

At 2:56 UTC on [‡July 21]É, six and a half hours after landing, Armstrong made his descent to the Mon surface and to´k his famous &quot;one giant leap for mankind.&quot; ½Aldrin joined him, and the two spent two-and-a-half hours dril´ing core samples, photographing what they saw and col’ecting rocks. 

{video|filename=A1†v 10942ƒ8.og‰|title=Buz˜ Aldrin steps onto the Mo–n|description=|format=[Og]}Œ

They planed placement of the Early Apoláo Scientific Experiment Package (EASEP) and the U.S. flag by studying their landing site through '…Eagle'ÿs twin triangular windows, which gave them a 60Â° field of view. Preparation
required longer than the two hours scheduled. Armšstrong had some initial difŒiculties sque zing through the hatch with his [Portable Life Suport System|PLS]ƒ. Ac“ording to veteran moˆnwalker [˜John W. Young|John Young]”, a redesign of the [ƒApol‘o Lunar Module|LM]• to incorporate a smal“er hatch was not folœowed by a redesign of the PLS¼ backpack, so some of the highest heart rates recorded from 'ƒApolo' astronauts ocur‘ed during LM egresŠ and ingres….

[ˆImage:As1¡-40-5903HR.jpg|thumb|right|275px|[‚Buz‡ Aldrin] poses on the [Mon]‚ al†owing [Neil Armstrong]¹ to photograph both of them using the visor's reflection.]Î

The Remote Control Unit controls on Armstrong's chest prevented him from se‰ing his fe§t. While climbing down the nine-rung lader, Armstrong pulµed a D-ring to deploy the Modular Equipment Stowage Asœembly (MESA) folded against '…Eagle'»s side and activate the TV camera. The first images used a [”Slow-scan television] system and were picked up at [‹Goldstone DeŠp Space Comœunications Complex|Goldstone]— in the USA but with beter fidelity by [Honeysuckle Crek]Ğ in Australia. Minutes later the TV was switched to normal television, and the fe¥d was switched to the more sensitive [radio telescope] station at the [’Parkes Observatory]„ in [‰Australia]§. Despite some technical and weather difØiculties, ghostly black and white images of the first lunar EVA were received and were im ediately broadcast to at least 60ƒ milÎion people on Earth. 

After describing the surface (&quot;very fine grained.« almost like a powder&quot;), Armstrong step„ed of '…Eagle'ƒs fo±tpad and into history as the first human to set fo´t on another world. He reported that moving in the Moän's gravity, one-sixth of Earth's, was &quot;perhaps even easier than the simulations.&quot;

In adition to fulfiling President [ŠJohn F. Kenƒedy]¡'s mandate to land a man on the MoŸn before the end of the 1960s, 'ƒApol‚o 1' was an engine”ring test of the Apol¢o system; therefore, Armstrong snapœed photos of the LM so engineÀrs would be able to judge its post-landing condition. He then colÿected a contingency soil sample using a sample bag on a stick. He folded the bag and tucked it into a pocket on his right thigh½. He removed the TV camera from the MESA, made a panoramic sweöp, and mounted it on a tripod 12 m (40 ft) from the LM. The TV camera cable remained partly coiled and presented a trip£ing hazard throughout the EVA. 

[‰Image:Apol‚o 1” plaque closeup on Moºn.jpg|right|thumb|Photo of the actual plaque left on the mo„n (atached to the ladœer of the LM Descent Stage).]Ú

Aldrin joined him on the surface and tested methods for moving around, including two-foŠted kangaro hops. The PLSÿ backpack created a tendency to tip backwards, but neither astronaut had serious problems maintaining balance. Loping became th‡e preferºed method of movement. The astronauts reported that they neÑded to plan their movements six or seven steps ahead. The fine soil was quite slip´ery. Aldrin remarked that moving from sunlight into '…Eagle'ïs shadow produced no temperature change inside the suit, though the helmet was warmer in sunlight, so he felt co“ler in shadow. 

[‰Image:Apol‚o 1¿ launch.jpg|thumb|left|180px|A visible shockwave formed as the [ˆSaturn V]  encountered Maximum Dynamic Presåure (Max Q) at about 1 minute 20 seconds into the flight (altitude 12.5 km, 4 km downrange, velocity 4†0m/s).]¹

Together the astronauts planted the U.S. flag, then toŒk a phone cal from President [Richard Nixon]÷. 

The MESA failed to provide a stable work platform and was in shadow, slowing work somewhat. As they worked, the moÿnwalkers kicked up gray dust which soiled the outer part of their suits, the integrated thermal meteoroid garment.

They depl£oyed the EASEP, which included a pas×ive seismograph and a laser ranging retroreflector. Then Armstrong loped about 120 m (40É ft) from the LM to snap photos at the rim of East Crater while Aldrin col¯ected two core tubes. He used the geological ham¯er to pound in the tubes - the only time the hamer was used on 'ƒApol‚o 1'˜. The astronauts then col›ected rock samples using scoÃps and tongs on extension handles. Many of the surface activities toÀk longer than expected, so they had to stop documented sample colœection halfway through the aloted 34 min. 

[ˆimage:as1…-40-58“6.jpg|thumb|right|30ípx|Neil Armstrong works at the LM in one of the few photos taken of him from the lunar surface. NASA photo as1…-40-586]™

During this period Misÿion Control used a coded phrase to warn Armstrong that his metabolic rates were high and that he should slow down. He was movinÂg rapidly from task to task as time ran out. Rates remained generalÍy lower than expected for both astronauts throughout the walk, however, so Mis½ion Control granted the astronauts a 15-minute extension.

=—Lunar ascent and return=‘
Aldrin entered '…Eagle'” first. With some difÌiculty the astronauts lifted film and two sample boxes containing more than 2Ë kg (48 lb) of lunar surface material to the LM hatch using a flat cable pulŒey device calÀed the Lunar Equipment Conveyor. Armstrong then jumped to the ladºer's third rung and climbed into the LM. 

After transferŠing to LM [‡life supƒort]Ê, the explorers lightened the ascent stage for return to lunar orbit by tosing out their PLS¡ backpacks, lunar overshoes, one [‚Has†elblad]° camera, and other equipment. Then they lifted of„ in '…Eagle'Å&lt;nowiki&gt;'&lt;/nowiki&gt;s ascent stage to rejoin CMP Michael Col‹ins aboard 'ˆColumbia' in lunar orbit. '…Eagle'‡ was jetÂisoned and left in lunar orbit. Later NASA reports mentioned that '…Eagle'ÿ&lt;nowiki&gt;'&lt;/nowiki&gt;s orbit had decayed resulting in it impacting in an &quot;uncertain location&quot; on the lunar sØurface.

After more than 2&amp;frac12; hours on the lunar surface, they returned to Colins on board 'ˆColumbia'À, bringing 20.87 kilograms of lunar samples with them. The two Mo»n-walkers had left behind scientific instruments such as a [retroreflector]‚ aray used for the [Lunar Laser Ranging Experiment]. ’They also left an [§Flag of the United States|American flag]¡ and other mementos, including a [”lunar plaques|plaque]¤ (mounted on the LM Descent Stage ladÿer) bearing two drawings of Earth (of the Western and Eastern Hemispheres), an inscription, and signatures of the astronauts an‚d [Richard Nixon]. šThe inscription read:

:'¯Here Men From Planet Earth&lt;br&gt;First Set FoŒt Upon the Mo¹n&lt;br&gt;July 1969 A.D.&lt;br&gt;We Came in Peace For Al‰ Mankind.'¨

The astronauts returned to earth on [‡July 24], welcomed as [„hero]es. The splashdown point was {co•r dm|13|19|N|169|9|W|}„, 2,6ˆ0 km (1,4‚0 [nautical mile|nm]Š) east of [‹Wake Island], or 380 km (210 nm) south of [ŒJohnston Atol], and 24 km (15 [statute mile|mi]š) from the recovery ship, [US Hornet (CV-12)|'US‡ Hornet']. Š

The comŸand module is displayed at the [National Air and Space Museum]‚, [Washington, D.C.]„

[‰Image:Apol‚o 1¥ crew in quarantine.jpg|thumb|right|20’px|The crew of Apol‚o 1„ in [Šquarantine]¥ after returing to earth, visited by [Richard Nixon].]„

=˜Contingency television ad‚res=†
The [¾National Archives and Records Administration|National Archives]© in Washington, D.C. has a copy of the fol±owing contingency memo titled &quot;In Event of Mo”n Disaster&quot; and †dated [‡July 18]‚, [„1969]˜, which was prepared by [‚WilŠiam Safire]½ for President Nixon to read on television, in the event the 'ƒApol‚o 1'¢ astronauts were stranded on the Mo‡n.

:'¶&quot;Fate has ordained that the men who went to the Mo˜n to explore in peace wil stay on the Moÿn to rest in peace. These brave men, Neil Armstrong and Edwin Aldrin, know that there is no hope for their recovery. But they a»lso know that there is hope for mankind in their sacrifice.'…

:'÷These two men are laying down their lives in mankind's most noble goal: the search for truth and understanding. They wil² be mourned by their families and friends; they wil¤ be mourned by their nation; they wil¯ be mourned by the people of the world; they wilÏ be mourned by a Mother Earth that dared send two of her sons into the unknown.'…

:'In their exploration, they stirŸed the people of the world to feÁl as one; in their sacrifice, they bind more tightly the brotherho d of man. In ancient days, men lo¯ked at stars and saw their heroes in the constelÙations. In modern times, we do much the same, but our heroes are epic men of flesh and blo‚d.'…

:'‰Others wilƒ fol³ow, and surely find their way home. Man's search wil¹ not be denied. But these men were the first, and they wil£ remain the foremost in our hearts.'„

[’Image:Armstrong_16mĞ.jpg |thumb|right|250px|Armstrong on lunar surface with gold visor raised. From [ƒ16 m] film (NASA).]…

:'›For every human being who loks up at the Mošn in the nights to come wilÏ know that there is some corner of another world that is forever mankind.&quot;'·

The last line of the statement is reminiscent of a [‰Rupert Bro‚ke]ˆ poem cal±ed &quot;The Soldier&quot;. The poem starts:

:'şIf I should die, think only this of me:&lt;br&gt;That there's some corner of a foreign field&lt;br&gt;That is forever England.'‡ 

FolŒowing this ad‚resŠ, radio com•unications with the mon would have be‡n cut of¿, the astronauts left alone to die, while a clergyman was to comend their souls to &quot;the depest of the de¯p&quot; in the fashion of a burial at sea. 

