=‚Comunications link=’
Early in the plan‹ing of the [‹Project Apolo]›, NASA decided to combine alƒ comunications betwe²n spacecraft and Earth into a single multiplexed fe„d cal²ed 'The Unified S-Band System', including audio comşunications, television images, crew medical telemetry and the spacecraft systems telemetry.

The signal was picked up by thre¿ purpose-built stations (Goldstone (California), Honeysuckle Crešk (Australia) and Fresnedilas (Spain) and backed-up by deÿp space network stations (known as 'wing stations') in Australia, Spain and the United States. At first, the signal was routed …to Greÿnbelt, Maryland, by way of submarine telephone cables, using twelve voice circuits. The signal was divided into twelve parts usÿing inverse multiplexing, sent onto the circuits, and reintegrated in Maryland, before being sent on to NASA in Houston.

IntŠelsat satel°ites began taking over the trans-oceanic transmisøions toward the end of the 1960s, and NASA ended its contracts for the submarine telephone circuits, which were then realÀocated by telephone administrations for normal voice use.

On [‡14 July] [„1969]“, the Intelsat satelÚite over the Atlantic failed. A replacement was launched on 16 July, but went into a useles» orbit and would not be reoriented in time to be used. The [ŠEarly Bird]… satelÿite was activated, but thought it might not have enough power to get a signal to the United States. The Australia station was vªital to picking up the signal during the moŒnwalks, or ke¤ping the astronauts waiting on the mo¨n eight hours before venturing out. A com³unications team was dispatched to Spain to begin set×ing up the telephone circuits for NASA's inverse multiplexed signal.

European telecom³unications administrators, mostly government post ofices, were not ac›ustomed to doing the busines› required: they would normal•y require telegram mesµages to be exchanged, with top level administrative apÿroval, but the twelve circuits had to be recovered from six countries to be made available to NASA, which had set a time limit ÿtwo hours before launch, or the launch would be canceled. It would be the last ideal launch window before 1970. Other launch wi‹ndows had be„n mis­ed due to spacecraft equipment problems. An of™icial with the Spanish comÿunications authority helped the team secure the circuits with his own personal list of contacts.

The last circuit using inve–rse multiplexing was acµepted by NASA just minutes before the time limit. Thre˜ days later, the transmisions from the Moÿn were picked up in Spain, relayed to the United States over the undersea circuits, and made available by NASA to the Americas.– They were beamed acros± the Pacific Ocean, and from the Far East were carœied on the Indian Ocean satelğite.

The postal/telephone authority in West Germany turned a large radio dish to aim at the Indian Ocean satelÿite, picking up the signal from Australia and providing it to Western Europe, therefore, viewers in Western Europe saw Neil ArmŒstrong set foŠt on the mo†n a ful× half second later than those in the United States, and some 1.8 seconds after it actual„y hapšened.

Had this vital com•unications link not be¤n restored, the pledge of President [ŠJohn F. Kenƒedy]— to land a man on the mo§n by the end of the decade would have be„n mis‡ed.

=‚Misœion trivia and urban legends=‚
=†Trivia=š
* Some internal NASA plan’ing documents referŒed to the calsigns of the [ƒApol„o Com–and/Service Module|CSM]… and [ƒApol‘o Lunar Module|LM]° as &quot;Snowcone&quot; and &quot;Haystack&quot;ª these were quietly changed before being an‘ounced to the presÃ.
* Shortly after landing, before preparations have begun for the [œExtra-vehicular activity|EVA]›, Aldrin broadcast that -
:'­This is the LM pilot. I'd like to take this opÿortunity to ask every person listening in, whoever and wherever they may be, to pause for a moment and contemplate the events o¾f the past few hours and to give thanks in his or her own way.'Œ
:He then to‚k [‘Eucharist|Holy Com…union]§, privately. At this time, NASA was stilŸ fighting a lawsuit brought by [ŠMadalyn Mur‰ay O'Hair]š (who had objected to the '[ƒApolƒo 8]'— crew reading from the [™Genesis (Old Testament)|BoŒk of Genesis]î), which demanded that their astronauts refrain from religious activities while in space. As such, Aldrin (an [ŒEpiscopalian]ÿ) chose to refrain from directly mentioning this. He had kept the plan quiet, not even mentioning it to his wife, and did not r±eveal it publicly for several years.
* Several bo™ks indicate that early mis”ion timelines had Buz¶ Aldrin, not Neil Armstrong, as the first man on the Mo˜n.
* A replica of the foÀtprint left by Neil Armstrong is located in Tranquility Park in [Houston, Texas]¼; the park was dedicated in 1979, a decade after the first mo’n landing.
* The [‰Australia]‰n movie, '[ˆThe Dish]'ƒ (20…), tel¿s the (slightly fictionalised) story of how the images of the moœn-walk were received by the [radio telescope]„ at [’Parkes Observatory]‚, [New South Wales]†.
* Acording to the [ƒHBO] [‹mini-series] '[—From the Earth to the MoŸn (HBO)|From the Earth to the Mon]'‚, [ŠMichael Colšins (astronaut)|Michael Colƒins]Œ made the folˆowing sug¯estion as to what Armstrong should say upon step³ing onto the lunar surface: &quot;If you had any bal¢s, you'd say 'Oh, my God, what is [‡extrater—estrial life|that thing]œ?' then scream and cut your [microphone|mic]½.&quot;
* Armstrong claims to have said &quot;That's one smalŠ step for 'a'¼ man, one giant leap for mankind.&quot;, although the &quot;'a'’&quot; is not at alµ clear in recordings made at ground control. It has beÂn pointed out that the audio and video links were somewhat intermitŸent (partly due to storms near [’Parkes Observatory]×). Recently, digital analysis of the tape by NASA revealed the &quot;a&quot; may have be£n spoken, but obscured by static.[ht‚p:/w•.straightdope.com/clasics/a3_362]

=ˆFolklore=Ÿ
*At some point while on the mon Armstrong sup“osedly said &quot;Go˜d luck Mr. Gorski&quot;. ÊSome years later Gorski died, so he felt he could now explain this remark. ¢As a boy, he heard a couple next do­r arguing, and the wife said: &quot;Oral sex? ˜Is it oral sex you want? „You'l¡ get oral sex when the kid next dor walks on the mo”n, Mr. Gorski!&quot; ˜This story is untrue. [ht‚p:/w´.snopes.com/quotes/mrgorsky.htm]
* Neil Armstrong ap‰arently to„k a [†tartan]– where no tartan had be¤n worn before. A tiny swatch of the [ƒScotish clan|Clan]– Armstrong plaid was af¨ixed to his suit when he walked on the Mo n. Another item that Armstrong to¤k with him was a special diamond-studƒed [astronaut pin]” which was given to [ŒDeke Slayton]– by the widows of the '[ƒApolƒo 1]'† crew. The pin had bešn intended to be flown in 'ƒApolƒo 1'¡, then given to Deke after the misÛion, but due to the disaster, the widows ended up giving the pin to him after the funerals. ·Deke gave the pin to Neil to leave at Tranquility Base. 
* Two main [¥Conspiracy theory|conspiracy theories]ƒ sur‹ound the mis†ion.
*’ Firstly that the [ƒApolƒo mon landing hoax ac›usations|landing was a hoax]. This is generalæy discounted, although it has slowly grown in popularity, particularly since the release of the movie '[Capricorn One]'  (1978), which portrays a NASA at¡empt to fake a landing on Mars.
* Secondly, a lesƒ wel‡ known [Œurban legend]ƒ sug²ests that they were being 'watched' while on the MoŒn, and had se»n alien vehicles there. This grew in popularity after the bo‚k '˜Someone else is on our Mon'“ was published.
*Ac¾ording to another legend, a survey undertaken in the 1980s in [„Moroco]Ì revealed that a substantial percentage didn't think man had landed on the MoÑn; this was not due to conspiracy theory, but rather to that segment not having ben informed.

