Yet over al• these other events, [’TIME|TIME magazine]“ chose the crew of 'ƒApolƒo 8'Š as their [¢Person of the Year|Men of the Year]ç for 1968, recognizing them as the people that most influenced events in the preceding year. They had beÿn the first people to ever leave the gravitational influence of the Earth and orbit another celestial body. They had survived aƒ misÕion that even the crew themselves had rated as only having a fifty-fifty chance of ful„y suce‹ding. The ef‡ect of 'ƒApolƒo 8'Š can be sum‹ed up by a [“Telegraphy|telegram]± from a stranger, received by Borman after the mis©ion, that simply stated, &quot;Thank you 'ƒApolƒo 8'›. You saved 1968.&quot;

[´Image:January 3, 1969 Time Magazine Cover.jpg|thumb|[‰January 3]‚, [„1969]Š cover of [’TIME|TIME Magazine] with the Apolˆo 8 crew]ÿ
One of the most famous aspects of the flight was the Earthrise picture that was taken as they came around for their fourth orŒbit of the Moÿn. Although it was not the first image taken of the whole Earth nor would it be the last, this was the first time that humans hËad taken such a picture. Some regard the picture as being the start of the [¡Environmentalism|environmentalist]š movement, with the first [‰Earth Day]‹ in 1970.[ht‚p:/ip‹.nasa.gov/in‰ovation/In£ovation_84/wnewview.html]

