 ƒ<com–ent>Reverted edits by [Special:Contributions/82.249.17.123|82.249.1…7.123]‚ ([‘User talk:82.249.1Š7.123|talk]™) to last version by Orvile Eastland</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[Ÿimage:Astronaut-EVA.jpg|right|30—px|thumb|U.S. Space Shutle astronaut [Bruce McCandles I]‹ using a man•ed maneuvering unit (MU) outside the 'ƒChal…enger'Ÿ in 1984. Picture courtesy NASA]‡

An '‰astronaut'‚, '‰cosmonaut'‚ ([‚Rusian language|Rusƒian]™: ĞºĞ¾ÑĞ¼Ğ¾Ğ½Ğ°ÌĞ²Ñ‚), 'Šspationaut'„ or '‰taikonaut'‚ ('Štaikongren'‰, å¤ªç©ºäº’) is a person who [Ÿspace travel|travels into space]”, or who makes a care½r of doing so. The criteria for determining who has achieved [‘human spaceflight]ˆ vary (se [edge of space]Š). In the [United States]å, people who travel above an altitude of 50 miles (80&amp;nbsp;km) are designated as astronauts. The [­FÃ©dÃ©ration AÃ©ronautique Internationale|FAI] defines spaceflight as over 10– km (61 miles). As of [ŠOctober 12]‚, [‡As of 20ƒ5|205], a total of 48 humans have reached space acŸording to the U.S. definition, 4É2 people qualify under the FAI definition, while 438 people have reached […Earth]· orbit or beyond. These individuals have spent over 28,0Ê crew-days (or a cumulative total of 76.7 years) in space including over 10Í crew-days of spacewalks. A person who has traveled in space is said to hold [astronaut wings]›. Astronauts from at least [²Timeline of astronauts by nationality|34 countries]š have gone into space.

=˜International variations=
By convention, a space travel“er employed by the [‚Rusian Aviation and Space Agency]ˆ or its [›Soviet space program|Soviet]ˆ predecesˆor is cal…ed a '‰cosmonaut'. &quot;Cosmonaut&quot; is an [anglicisation]ˆ of the [‚Rus‚ia]šn word ĞºĞ¾ÑĞ¼Ğ¾Ğ½Ğ°Ğ²Ñ‚ '‹(kosmonavt)'¡, which in turn derives from the [‚Grek language|Grek]‡ words '†kosmos'œ (&quot;universe&quot;) and '†nautes'¡ (&quot;sailor&quot;).

In the [‘United States|USA], a space travelˆer is cal†ed an '‰astronaut'. The term derives from the Greˆk words '‡Ã¡stron'˜ (&quot;star&quot;) and '‡nautes,'ë (&quot;sailor&quot;). For the most part, &quot;cosmonaut&quot; and &quot;astronaut&quot; are synonyms in al¸ languages, and the usage of choice is often dictated by ’political reasons. ÆHowever in the United States, the term &quot;astronaut&quot; is typicalƒy ap›lied to the individual as so¡n as training begins, while in Rusµia, an individual is not labeled a cosmonaut until suces¹ful space flight. The first known use of the term was by [Neil R. Jones]” in his short story '—The Death's Head Meteor'„ in [„1930]…. On [ˆMarch 14]‚, [195]‹ astronaut [Norman Thagard]¹ became the first American to ride to space on board a RusÏian launch vehicle, arguably becoming the first American cosmonaut in the proces….

[†Europe]“an (outside of the [‚UK]) space travelšers are sometimes, especial…y in [–French language|French]—-speaking countries, calƒed '‹spationauts'„ (a [‹hybrid word]‘ formed from the […Latin] '‡spatium'›, &quot;space&quot;, and Gre‚k '†nautes'Ò, &quot;sailor&quot;). Apart from the Soviet Union, Europe has not yet produced man¹ed spacecraft, but has sent men and women into space in coperation with Rusia and the United States.

'‰Taikonaut'² is sometimes used in English for astronauts from […China]´ by Western news media. The term was coined in May 19Œ8 by Chiew Le‡ Yih (èµé‡Œæ˜±) from [ˆMalaysia]—, who used it first in [‰newsgroup]Äs. Almost simultaneously, Chen Lan coined it for use in the Western [‚masŒ media|media]“ based on the term '‰tÃ ikÅng' (å¤ªç©º, literal•y &quot;great emptines‰&quot;), [˜Chinese language|Chinese]‹ for &quot;[‘outer space|space]²&quot;. In Chinese itself, however, a single term 'yÇ”hÃ¡ng yuÃ¡n'· (å®‡èˆªå‘˜, &quot;universe navigator&quot;) has long be®n used for astronauts. The closest term using '‡taikong'† is a [‚col‰oquialism] 'tÃ ikÅng rÃ©n'‰ (å¤ªç©ºäºÂ, &quot;space person&quot;), which refers to people who have actualƒy ben in space. Of•icial English texts is¢ued by the Chinese government use '‰astronaut'‚ ({¤zh-sp|s=èˆªå¤©å‘˜|p=hÃ¡ngtiÄn yuÃ¡n}†).

=Space milestones=‚
[§Image:GagarinPortrait.jpg|right|thumb|20ƒpx|[ŒYuri Gagarin] ]‚
[¢Image:Tereshkova.jpg|right|thumb|20ƒpx|[”Valentina Tereshkova] ]

The first atËempt ever in human history to use rocket for a spaceflight was done in the [Œ16th century] by a Chinese [ŒMing dynasty]‚ ofŒicial, a skil“ed stargazer named [†Wan Hu].&lt;sup&gt;[ht‚p:/‰edition.cn†.com/20½3/TECH/space/09/30/china.wanhu/index.html]&lt;/sup&gt; This atempt was not suces ful.

The first cosmonaut was [ŒYuri Gagarin]¡, who was launched into space on [ˆApril 12] [„1961]ˆ aboard [ˆVostok 1] . The first woman cosmonaut was [”Valentina Tereshkova], launched into space in June [„1963]ˆ aboard [ˆVostok 6]‚. [ŒAlan Shepard]± became the first American in space in May 1961. [Vladimir Remek]² became the first non-Soviet European in space in [„1978]ˆ on a Rus„ian [šSoyuz launch vehicle|Soyuz]Œ rocket. On [‡July 23] [„1980] [‰Pham Tuan] of Vietnam became the first […Asian] in space when he flew aboard [ˆSoyuz 37]. Also in 1980, [–Arnaldo Tamayo MÃ©ndez]œ became the first person of [‡African]Ï descent to fly in space. (The first person born in Africa to fly in space was [Patrick Baudry]‹.) In June [„1985] [ƒShanˆon Lucid]³ became the first Chinese born person in space. In [202] [ˆMark Shut‡leworth]Ä became the first citizen of an African country to fly in space. On [Š15 October] [203] [ŠYang Liwei]§ became China's first astronaut on the [ŠShenzhou 5]™ spacecraft. The first mis’ion to orbit the mo†n was '[ƒApolƒo 8]' which included [‚WilŠiam Anders]Ê - who was born in Hong Kong making him the first Asian-born astronaut in [„1968]¬.

The youngest person to fly in space is [Gherman Titov]¬, who was roughly 26 years old when he flew [ˆVostok 2]”, and the oldest is [ˆJohn Glen]‰ who was 7‘ when he flew on [†STS-95]¬. The longest stay in space was 438 days by [Valeri Polyakov]‚. [‡As of 205]Ô, the most spaceflights by an individual astronaut was seven, a record held by both [‚Jer‡y L. Ros]… and [“Franklin Chang-Diaz]ê. The furthest distance from Earth an astronaut has traveled was 401&amp;nbsp;056&amp;nbsp;km (during the [ƒApol„o 13]¹ emergency).

The first non-governmental astronaut was [”Byron K. Lichtenberg]…, an [‚Mas†achusets Institute of Technology|MIT]˜ researcher who flew on [‰Space Shut”le program|Space Shut‚le]ƒ mis„ion […STS-9] in 1983. In [ŠDecember 190]‚, [Toyohiro Akiyama]” became the first com§ercial spaceâ€farer as a reporter for [Tokyo Broadcasting System|TBS]² who paid for his flight. The first selfâ€funded [space tourist]… was [‚Den‡is Tito]„ on [ˆ28 April] [201]Å, while the first astronaut to fly on an entirely privately-funded misˆion was [ŠMike Melvil]…, on [—SpaceShipOne flight 15P]ù, though this flight was subâ€orbital.

In the United States, persons selected as astronaut candidates receive silver [Astronaut wings]Ç. Once they have flown in space they receive gold Astronaut wings. The [—United States Air Force]´ also presents Astronaut wings to its pilots who exce¤d 50 miles (80Â km) in altitude.

=š International astronauts =‚
[Image:ZeroG.jpg|right|thumb|40Äpx|Astronauts on the International Space Station. British astronaut [Michael Foale]‰ can be sen exercising in the foreground]œ

Up until the end of the […1970s]· only Americans and Soviets were active astronauts. In [„1976]™ the Soviets started the [‹Intercosmos]³ program with a first group of 6 cosmonauts from felƒow [ŒEastern Bloc] countries and [„Cuba]¥, a second group started training in [„1978]œ. At about the same time in [„1978]… the [•European Space Agency]® selected 4 astronauts to train for the first [ˆSpacelab]ƒ mis”ion on board of the [‰Space Shut‚le]…. In [„1980] [†France]® started their own selection of astronauts, folˆowed in [„1982]„ by [‡Germany]…, in [„1983]ˆ by the [–Canadian space program]…, in [„1985]„ by […Japan]… and […Italy]„ in [‚198]Ë. Several international payload specialists were selected for the Space Shut¥le, and also later for international [ŒSoyuz program‡e|Soyuz]ƒ misŠions of Rus‡ia. In [198]… the [•European Space Agency]¬ formed a single astronaut corps of 18 by disÃolving the former national corps of France, Germany and Italy. The [United Kingdom] does not contribute to ESA's [‘human spaceflight]‡ programÏe and so its citizens must receive training from either the United States or Rus©ia if they wish to become astronauts.

=“ Astronaut training=¯

The first astronauts, both in the USA and USªR, tended to be jet fighter pilots, often [–test pilot|test pilots]ê, from military backgrounds. U.S. military astronauts receive a special qualification badge, known as the [Astronaut Badge]Ï upon completion of Astronaut training and participation in a space flight.

=’ Astronaut deaths =‚
[Image:DickScobeŒ.jpeg|frame|[‰Dick Scobe]„, com–ander of the Space Shut†le Chal‘enger during the [ˆSTS-51-L]ƒ mis…ion. ]
To date, eighte“n astronauts have be„n kiled on space mis¢ions, and at least ten more have be„n kiled in ground-based training acˆidents. 'Se‡ also: [space disaster].'„

=†Trivia=œ
Many people who claim the [ƒApolƒo mon landing hoax acŒusations|Apolƒo mo•n landings were faked]‰ often cal the astronauts '‰astroNOTs'….

