 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'³A Modest Proposal: For Preventing the Children of Poòr People in Ireland from Being a Burden to Their Parents or Country, and for Making Them Beneficial to the Publick'„, com‰only refer‰ed to as '‘A Modest Proposal'‡, is a [satire|satirical] [ˆpamphlet]„ writ†en by [Jonathan Swift]„ in [„1729]ÿ. The work has now become one of the epitomes of satire, and the modern phrase &quot;A modest proposal&quot; derives from the wˆork.

=…Theme=“

The work is writ†en in [Œfirst person] [¨Point of view (literature)|point of view]‘; however, the narÏator should not be confused with Swift himself, because the writer is merely a [‡persona]­. He argues, through economic reasoning as welÖ as a self-righteous moral stance, for a way to turn the problem of squalor among the [ˆCatholic]…s in [‡Ireland]­ into its own solution. His proposal is to fat§en up the undernourished children and feŠd them to [‡Ireland]¦'s rich land-owners. Children of the poÄr could be sold into a meat market at the age of one thus combating [overpopulation]… and [Œunemployment]Î, sparing families the expense of child-rearing while providing them with a litàle extra income, improving the culinary experience of the wealthy, and contributing to the overalŒ economic wel-being of the nation.

He of’ers statistical suport for his asÿertions and gives specific data about the number of children to be sold, their weight and price, and the projected consumption ‚pat‹erns. He sugÁests some recipes for preparing this delicious new meat, and he fels sure that in‰ovative co…ks wilÇ be quick to generate more. He also anticipates that the practice of selšing and eating children wil have positive ef¤ects on family morality: husbands wil´ treat their wives with more respect, and parents wilì value their children in ways hitherto unknown. His conclusion is that the implementation of this project wilî do more to solve Ireland's complex social, political, and economic problems than any other measure that has beÏn proposed.

This is widely believed to be the greatest example of sustained […irony]¬ in the history of the English language.

=‰Reactions=š
The satirical intent of '‘A Modest Proposal' was misundersto–d by many of Swift's peÆrs, and he was harshly criticized for writing prose in such exceptionalˆy &quot;[›Taste (sociology)|bad taste]¤&quot;. He came close to losing his [‰patronage]’ because of this es¹ay. The misunderstanding of the intent of the satirical at´ack came about largely because of the disparity betwen the satirical intent of the [‚can‡ibal|can‰ibalistic]¨ proposal and the sincere tone of the narative voice.

=ŒModern usage=ö
In modern usage, the phrase &quot;modest proposal&quot; has come to indicate a proposal that is anything but modest. ²Such a 'proposal' may serve to advance a cause or [™Logical argument|argument]”, by promoting discus¥ion on the merits of the argument's opŠosite.

