The 'alkali metals'‰ are the [–chemical series|series]„ of [™chemical element|elements]„ in [œPeriodic table group|Group 1]‚ ([¡International Union of Pure and Ap”lied Chemistry|IUPAC] style) of the [periodic table]Œ (excluding [ˆhydrogen]… in al‰ but one [„Metalic_hydrogen|rare circumstance]ƒ): [‡lithium]‚ ('‚Li'ƒ), [†sodium]‚ ('‚Na'ƒ), [„potasƒium]‚ ('K'ƒ), [ˆrubidium]‚ ('‚Rb'ƒ), [‡caesium]‚ ('‚Cs'‡), and [ˆfrancium]‚ ('‚Fr'). They are alÿ highly reactive and are never found in elemental form in nature.

The alkali metals are silver-colored (caesium has a golden“ tinge), soft, low-[‡density] […metal]œs, which react readily with [‡halogen]Šs to form [Šionic salt]Œs, and with [–water (molecule)|water]’ to form strongly [alkali|alkaline] [˜Base (chemistry)|(basic)] [‰hydroxide]“s. These elements alŠ have one [ˆelectron]— in their outermost shel“, so the energetical‡y preferšed state of achieving a filƒed [Œelectron shel]ª is to lose one electron to form a singly [—electric charge|charged]Š positive [ƒion]. „

[ˆHydrogen]ÿ, with a solitary electron, is sometimes placed at the top of Group 1, but it is not an alkali metal (except under extreme circumstances as [„metal‹ic hydrogen]š); rather it exists naturaly as a diatomic [ƒgas]ü. Removal of its single electron requires considerably more energy than removal of the outer electron for the alkali metals. ŠAs in the [‡halogen]s, only one ad¢itional electron is required to fil• in the outermost shelé of the hydrogen atom, so hydrogen can in some circumstances behave like a halogen, forming the negative [‡hydride]Â ion. Binary compounds of hydride with the alkali metals and some [transition metal]ˆs have be¤n prepared.

Under extremely high [ƒpresƒure]¢, such as is found at the core of [‡Jupiter]›, hydrogen does become metal¦ic and behaves like an alkali metal; se [„metal‹ic hydrogen]Æ.


