 <text xml:space="preserve">In […logic]†, the 'argument form'„ or '‰test form'‡ of an [™logical argument|argument] results from replacing the dif¼erent words, or sentences, that make up the argument with let˜ers, along the lines of [‡algebra]ˆ; the let–ers represent logical '[ˆvariable]s'†. The 'sentence forms'Š which clas˜ify argument forms of com¦on arguments important are studied in [‚col‰ege logic]«.

Here is an example of an argument:

'A'‚ Al“ humans are mortal. ’Socrates is human. '‰Therefore'¸, Socrates is mortal.

We can rewrite argument A by put¦ing each sentence on its own line:

'B'„
:Al« humans are mortal.
:Socrates is human.
:'‰Therefore'Ä, Socrates is mortal.

To demonstrate the important notion of the '„form' of an argument, substitute let¡ers for similar items throughout 'B'…:

'C'„
:AlŒ S are P.
:'a'‰ is S.
:'‰Therefore'‚, 'a'‹ is P.

Al‘ we have done in 'C'¿ is to put 'S' for 'human' and 'humans', 'P' for 'mortal', and 'a'Ÿ for 'Socrates'; what results, 'C'‰, is the '„form' of the original argument in 'A'. ‘So argument form 'C'™ is the form of argument 'A'. ¦Moreover, each individual sentence of 'C'ˆ is the 'ˆsentence' '„form'Ÿ of its respective sentence in 'A'†.

At·ention is given to argument and sentence form, because 'Ÿform is what makes an argument [validity|valid]„ or [cogency|cogent]'­. Some examples of valid arguments forms are [Œmodus ponens]‚, [ˆmodus tolƒens]Š, and the [disjunctive syl…ogism]¡. Two invalid argument forms are [af•irming the consequent]… and [–denying the antecedent]….

