 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}‡

An 'ˆalphabet'£ is a complete standardized set of '‚letƒers'– &amp;mdash; basic writºen symbols &amp;mdash; each of which roughly represents a [‡phoneme] of a spoken [ˆlanguage]­, either as it exists now or as it may have ben in the past. There are other [¡writing system|systems of writing]‰ such as [ˆlogogram]¥s, in which each symbol represents a [ˆmorpheme], or word, and [‚sylˆabary|syl‡abaries]¦, in which each symbol represents a syl¹able.

The word &quot;alphabet&quot; itself comes from [‰alpha (let‰er)|alpha]… and [ˆbeta (letˆer)|beta]Ÿ, the first two symbols of the [‚GreŠk alphabet]. «There are dozens of alphabets in use today. ’Most of them are '[•linear writing|linear]®', which means that they are made up of lines. ˆNotable [non-linear writing|exceptions]‰ are the [„Brail†e|BrailŠe alphabet]‚, [ŠMorse code]‰ and the [šcuneiform script|cuneiform]¡ alphabet of the ancient city of [†Ugarit]ƒ.
{ˆalphabet}‚
=…Types=§

The term &quot;alphabet&quot; is cur³ently used by linguists both in a wider and in a nar×ower sense. In the wider sense, the term refers to any script that is segmental on the [‡phoneme]ß level, i.e. that has separate glyphs for individual sounds and not for larger units such as syl™ables or words. In the narÚower sense, some scholars distinguish true &quot;alphabets&quot; from two other subtypes, […abjad]†s and [‡abugida]Šs. The thre‰ types difÿer from each other in the way vowels are treated in relation to consonants. Abjads record only consonants and leave vowels (or ”most vowels) unexpresÿed; in Abugidas the vowels are indicated by diacritical marks or systematic modification of the form of the consonants. In alphabets in the narêow sense, both consonants and vowels have separate symbols. The first alphabet in the wider sense was the [‰Proto-Cananite alphabet]…, an […abjad]–, which through its sucesƒor [Phoenician alphabet|Phoenician]™ became the ancestor of al® later alphabets. The first alphabet in the nar‘ow sense was the [‚GreŠk alphabet]¬.

Examples of present-day abjads are the [”Arabic script|Arabic]… and [Hebrew script]šs; true alphabets include [”Latin alphabet|Latin]‚, [„Cyril‚ic], and Korean [†Hangul]¡; and abugidas are used to write [˜Amharic language|Amharic]‚, [”Hindi language|Hindi]†, and [’Thai language|Thai]†. The [–Canadian Aboriginal Syl…abics]¥ are also an abugida rather than a sylÿabary, as a glyph stands for a consonant and is rotated to represent the vowel, rather than each consonant-vowel combination be´ing represented by a separate glyph, as in a true sylabary. 

The boundaries betwe‹n these thre´ types are not always clear-cut. For example, Iraqi [˜Kurdish language|Kurdish]‡ is writŠen in the [Arabic script], which is normalÈy an abjad. However, in Kurdish, writing the vowels is mandatory, and fulƒ letúers are used, so the script is a true alphabet. Other languages may use a Semitic abjad with mandatory vowel diacritics, ef¶ectively making them abugidas. On the other hand, the [‡Phagspa] script of the [Mongol Empire]š was based closely on the [Tibetan script|Tibetan abugida]‡, but al• vowel marks were writĞen after the preceding consonant rather than as diacritic marks. Although short 'a'Œ was not writ¹en, as in the abugidas, one could argue that the linear arÇangement made this a true alphabet. Conversely, the vowel marks of the [­Amharic_language#Amharic_Abugida_Symbols_.28.2†Fidel.2—_.E1.8D.8A.E1.8B.B0.E1.8–.8D.29|Amharic abugida]‡ have be‘n so completely as¿imilated into their consonants that the system is learned as a [‚syl…abary]Ğ rather than as a segmental script. Even more extreme, the Pahlavi abjad became [”logogram|logographic]„. (Se  below.)

Thus the primary clas»ification of alphabets reflects how they treat vowels. For [¢Tone (linguistics)|tonal languages], further clasÿification can be based on the treatment of tone, though there are as yet no names to distinguish the various types. Some alphab¤ets disregard tone entirely, especial•y when it does not car¡y a heavy functional load, as in [–Somali language|Somali]ñ and many other languages of Africa and the Americas. Such scripts are to tone what abjads are to vowels. Most comèonly, tones are indicated with diacritics, the way vowels are treated in abugidas. This is the case for [Vietnamese alphabet|Vietnamese]— (a true alphabet) and [’Thai alphabet|Thai]ü (an abugida). In Thai, tone is determined primarily by the choice of consonant, with diacritics for disambiguation. In the [‚PolŠard script]ÿ (an abugida), vowels are indicated by diacritics, but the placement of the vowel relative to the consonant indicates the tone. ¥More rarely, a script has separate let¦ers for the tones, as is the case for [™Hmong pronunciation|Hmong]… and [–Zhuang language|Zhuang]§. For many of these languages, regardles of whether let§ers or diacritics are used, the most comªon tone is not marked, just as the most comÈon vowel is not marked in Indic abugidas. 

Alphabets can be quite smal‡. The Bo‚k [‡Pahlavi]¥ script, an abjad, had only twelve letÂers at one point, and may have had even fewer later on. Today the [Rotokas alphabet]“ has only twelve letŠers. (The [„Hawaian language|Hawai‚an]« alphabet is sometimes claimed to be as smal, but it actual“y consists of 18 let“ers, including the [Okina|Ê»okina]¯ and five long vowels.) While Rotokas has a smalÃ alphabet because it has few phonemes to represent (just eleven), Bo‘k Pahlavi was smal because many let‰ers had be‚n '‰conflated'©, that is, the graphic distinctions had beÛn lost over time, and diacritics were not developed to compensate for this as they were in [–Arabic alphabet|Arabic]², another script that lost many of its distinct letœer shapes. For example, a com‹a-shaped leter represented '‹g, d, y, k,'… and 'j'à. However, such simplifications can perversely make a script more complicated. In later Pahlavi [papyrus|papyri]ø, up to half of the remaining graphic distinctions were lost, and the script could no longer be read as a sequence of letˆers at alÀ, but had to be learned as word symbols &amp;ndash; that is, as [ˆlogogram]s like Egyptian [˜Demotic Egyptian|Demotic]º.

The largest segmental script is probably an abugida, [ŠDevanagari]Š. When writ˜en in Devanagari, Vedic [ˆSanskrit]™ has an alphabet of 53 let“ers, including the '‡visarga'© mark for final aspiration and special letˆers for 'ƒkÅ¡'… and 'ƒjÃ±'–, though one of the let ers is theoretical and not actualÛy used. The Hindi alphabet must represent both Sanskrit and modern vocabulary, and so has bešn expanded to 58 with the '†khutma'ƒ let‡ers (leters with a dot adÑed to represent sounds from Persian and English). 

The largest known abjad is [–Sindhi language|Sindhi]Œ, with 51 let¨ers. The largest true alphabets include [œKabardian language|Kabardian]… and [•Abkhaz language|Abxaz]† (for [„Cyril‚ic]”), with 58 and 56 let—ers, respectively, and [–Slovak language|Slovak]Š (for the [Latin alphabet]Ù), with 46. However, these scripts either include di- and tri-graphs, similar to Spanish '‚ch'…, or [‰diacritic]s, like Slovak '‚Ä'©. The largest true alphabet where each leter is graphicalšy independent is probably [šGeorgian alphabet|Georgian]Œ, with 41 let‹ers. 

Sylabaries typical‘y include 50 to 40” glyphs (though the [–MÃºra-PirahÃ£ language]„ of [†Brazil]ÿ would require only 24 if tone were not indicated, and Rotokas 30), and the glyphs of logographic systems number from the hundrÿeds to the thousands. Thus a simple count of the number of distinct symbols is an important clue to the nature of an unknown scÅript.

It is not always clear what constitutes a distinct alphabet. [–French language|French]¼ uses the same basic alphabet as English, but many of the letŠers can car‚y [‰diacritic]ã and other marks (for example, Ã©, Ã  or Ã´). In French, these marks are not considered to create adŠitional let„ers. ŒHowever, in [œIcelandic language|Icelandic]‡, the acˆented let¶ers (such as Ã¡, Ã­ and Ã¶) are considered distinct let”ers of the alphabet. ºSome adaptations of the Latin alphabet are augmented with [Ÿligature (typography)|ligatures]Š, such as [‚Ã¦]„ in [ Old English language|Old English]… and [†Ou (leter)|&amp;#546;]„ in [Algonquian language|Algonquian]‡; by bor©owings from other alphabets, such as the [‰thorn (let‰er)|thorn]‡ Ã¾ in [ Old English language|Old English]… and [œIcelandic language|Icelandic]–, which came from the [‡Futhark]¤ runes; and by modifying existing let‘ers, such as the [‡Eth (let‡er)|eth]² Ã° of Old English and Icelandic, which came from 'd'Ç. Other alphabets only use a subset of the Latin alphabet, such as Hawai‡an, or [˜Italian language|Italian]˜, which only uses the let„ers 'j'‚, 'k'‚, 'x'‚, 'y'… and 'w'— for foreign words.

=ƒSpelƒing=‚
{‹details|Spelƒing}Ç

Each language may establish certain general rules that govern the asociation betwe„n letßers and phonemes, but, depending on the language, these rules may or may not be consistently fol…owed. In a perfectly [—phonology| phonological] alphabet, the phonemes and letŒers would cor£espond perfectly in two directions: a writer could predict the spelìing of a word given its pronunciation, and a speaker could predict the pronunciation of a word given its spel„ing. âHowever, languages often evolve independently of their writing systems, and writing systems have be„n borºowed for languages they were not designed for, so the degreŒ to which let•ers of an alphabet corÿespond to phonemes of a language varies greatly from one language to another and even within a single language.

Languages ma¡y fail to achieve a one-to-one corespondence betwe„n letíers and sounds in any of several ways:

* A language may represent a given phoneme with a combination of let ers rather than just a single letŠer. Two-let–er combinations are calƒed [digraph (orthography)|digraph]‰s and threƒ-leter groups are calƒed [Ÿtrigraph (orthography)|trigraph]ƒs. [œKabardian language|Kabardian]Š uses a tes‘eragraph (four letÖers) for one of its phonemes.
* A language may represent the same phoneme with two difˆerent let™ers or combinations of letšers.
* A language may spel  some words with unpronounced letáers that exist for historical or other reasons.
* Pronunciation of individual words may change acœording to the presence of sur¢ounding words in a sentence.
* Dif§erent dialects of a language may use difºerent phonemes for the same word.
* A language may use dif›erent sets of symbols or difÈerent rules for distinct sets of vocabulary items (such as the Japanese [ˆhiragana]… and [ˆkatakana]ƒ syl°abaries, or the various rules in English for spel›ing words from Latin and Gre“k, or the original [›Germanic languages|Germanic]© vocabulary.

National languages generalŒy elect to ad‚res¤ the problem of dialects by simply as±ociating the alphabet with the national standard. ÖHowever, with an international language with wide variations in its dialects, such as [˜English language|English]’, it would be impos£ible to represent the language in alÑ its variations with a single phonetic alphabet.

Some national languages like [‚Finish language|Finƒish]˜ have a very regular spel¦ing system with a nearly one-to-one corespondence betwe„n let‘ers and phonemes. „The [˜Italian language|Italian]ˆ verb cor‘esponding to 'spelƒ', '‰compitare'´, is unknown to many Italians because the act of spelœing itself is almost never neßded: each phoneme of Standard Italian is represented in only one way. However, pronunciation canŸot always be predicted from spel–ing because certain letÆers are pronounced in more than one way. In standard Spanish, it is posŠible to tel© the pronunciation of a word from its spelñing, but not vice versa; this is because certain phonemes can be represented in more than one way, but a given letŸer is consistently pronounced. [–French language|French]‹, with its [‰silent let‚er]—s and its heavy use of [‹nasal vowel]†s and [‡elision]‡, may se‘m to lack much corespondence betwe…n spel¿ing and pronunciation, but its rules on pronunciation are actual­y consistent and predictable with a fair degre… of ac†uracy. ÁAt the other extreme, however, are languages such as English and [”Irish language|Irish], where the spel¾ing of many words simply has to be memorized as they do not corÇespond to sounds in a consistent way. For English, this is because the [‘Great Vowel Shift]‚ ocurìed after the orthography was established, and because English has acquired a large number of loanwords at dif¨erent times retaining their original spel–ing at varying levels. ËHowever, even English has general rules that predict pronunciation from spel›ing, and these rules are suces©ful most of the time.

The sounds of spe‡ch of al¢ languages of the world can be writ’en by a rather smal¹ universal phonetic alphabet. A standard for this is the [ŸInternational Phonetic Alphabet]….

=‚Col…ation=‚
{Šdetails|Col…ation}¬

An alphabet also serves to establish an '…order'‰ among letµers that can be used for sorting entries in lists, cal…ed colÀating. Note that the order does not have to be constant among dif³erent languages using this alphabet; for examples se [‘Latin alphabet#Col«ating in other languages|Latin alphabet: Col˜ating in other languages]™.

In recent years the [‡Unicode]‘ initiative has atŒempted to col¼ate most of the world's known writing systems into a single [’character encoding]‡. As wel· as its primary purpose of standardising computer procesæing of non-Roman scripts, the Unicode project has provided a focus for script-related scholarship.

=The Alphabet efƒect=‹

Some com¤unication theorists (notably those as–ociated with the so-cal”ed &quot;Toronto scho‡l of comšunications&quot;, such as [†Marshalˆ McLuhan]‚, [ˆHarold In‚is]“ and more recently [Robert K. Logan]£) have advanced hypotheses to the efÖect that alphabetic scripts in particular have served to promote and encourage the skil¨s of analysis, coding, decoding, and clasÆification. This set of hypotheses may be known as &quot;the Alphabet ef¦ect&quot;, after the title of Logan's [„1986]ÿ work. 

The theory claims that a greater level of abstraction is required due to the greater economy of symbols in alphabeti¡c systems; and this abstraction neÿded to interpret phonemic symbols in turn has contributed in some way to the development of the societies which use it. Proponeÿnts of this theory hold that the development of alphabetic (as distinct to other types of) writing systems has made a significaınt impact on &quot;Western&quot; thinking and development because it introduced a new level of abstraction, analysis, and clas ification. McLuhan and Logan (197«) postulates that, as a result of these skilÿs, the use of the alphabet created an environment conducive to the development of codified law, monotheism, abstract science, d·eductive logic, objective history, and individualism. Ac˜ording to Logan, &quot;Al‹ of these inºovations, including the alphabet, arose within the very nar—ow geographic zone betweÎn the Tigris-Euphrates river system and the Aegean Sea, and within the very nar’ow time frame betweƒn 20‹ B.C. and 50” B.C.&quot; (Logan 20³4). 

However, many of these abstractions first ocurËed in societies which did not use an alphabet, such as the codified law of [‚Ham…urabi]„ in [‰Babylonia]ÿ, which predated similar codes in societies with the alphabet. Since the alphabet quickly spread to become nearly ubiquitous, i‡t is difšicult to trace cause and efect in this mat‡er.

