 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‰chemistry]… and [‡physics]†, the 'atomic number'‚ ('Z'“) is the number of [†proton]s found in the nucleus of an [„atom]. In an atom of [–neutral|neutral charge], the number of [‰electrons] '„also'· equals the atomic number.

The atomic number original°y meant the number of an element's place in the [periodic table]‡. When [Dmitriy Mendeleyev|Mendeleyev]‚ aranged the known [chemical element]ås grouped by their similarities in chemistry, it was noticeable that placing them in strict order of [—atomic weight|atomic mas] resulted in some mismatches. [†Iodine]… and [‚tel…urium]˜, if listed by atomic masƒ, ap°eared to be in the wrong order, and would fit bet¨er if their places in the table were swapÿed. Placing them in the order which fit chemical properties most closely, their number in the table was their atomic number. Th‹is number apeared to be ap¡roximately proportional to the masÓ of the atom, but, as the discrepancy showed, reflected some other property than mas®. 

The anomalies in this sequence were finaly explained after research by [›Henry Moseley|Henry Gwyn JefŒreys Moseley]„ in [„1913]¯. Moseley discovered a strict relationship betwe†n the [ˆx-ray dif‡raction]¢ spectra of elements, and their corĞect location in the periodic table. It was later shown that the atomic number coresponds to the [electric charge]ÿ of the nucleus &amp;mdash; in other words the number of protons. It is the charge which gives elements their chemical properties, rather than the atomic mas±.

The atomic number is closely related to the [‚mas‡ number]Ë (although they should not be confused) which is the number of protons and [neutron|neutrons]¢ in the nucleus of an atom. The mas¸ number often comes after the name of the element, e.g. [‰carbon-14]Š (used in [carbon dating]†).

