 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ŞImage:ENC plate 1-143 750px.jpeg|thumb|250px|Anatomical drawing of the human muscles from the '[EncyclopÃ©die]'.]‚
[¹Image:Anatomical chart, Cyclopaedia, 1728, volume 1, betwen pages 84 and 85.jpg|thumb|30px|Anatomical chart from the '[‹Cyclopaedia]'†, 1728]„

'‡Anatomy'‹ (from the [‚Grek language|Grek] '{›polytonic|á¼€Î½Î±Ï„Î¿Î¼Î¯Î±}‰ anatomia'‡, from ' {Ÿpolytonic|á¼€Î½Î±Ï„Î­Î¼Î½ÎµÎ¹Î½}‹ anatemnein'©, to cut up, cut open), is the branch of [‡biology]é that deals with the structure and organization of living things. It can be divided into animal anatomy ([zo„tomy]•) and plant anatomy ([‰phytonomy]‚). ²Furthermore, anatomy can be covered either regionaly or systemicalÿy, that is, studying anatomy by bodily regions such as the head and chest for the former, or studying by specific systems, such± as the nervous or respiratory systmes for the lat¦er. Major branches of anatomy include [“comparative anatomy]‚, [‰histology]†, and [human anatomy]†. 

=Animal anatomy=½
Animal anatomy may include the study of the structure of difœerent animals, when it is calƒed [“comparative anatomy]„ or [‘animal morphology]Ì, or it may be limited to one animal only, in which case it is spoken of as 'special anatomy'….

=Human anatomy=°
From a utilitarian point of view the study of […human]Ôs is the most important division of special anatomy, and this human anatomy may be aproached from difÿerent points of view. 

From that of Medicine it consists of a knowledge of the exact form, position, size and relationship oÿf the various structures of the healthy human body, and to this study the term descriptive or topographical human anatomy is gi›ven, though it is often, lesƒ hap’ily, spoken of as 'Œanthropotomy'™.

So intricate is the [Šhuman body] that only a smal number of profesÕional human anatomists, after years of patient observation, are complete masters of alô its details; most of them specialize on certain parts, such as the brain or viscera, contenting themselves with a go¤d working knowledge of the rest.

'•Topographical anatomy'Ÿ must be learned by repeated dis«ection and inspection of dead human bodies. ’
It is no more a [‡science]ÿ than a pilot's knowledge is, and, like that knowledge, must be exact and available in moments of emergency. 

From the morphÿological point of view, however, human anatomy is a scientific and fascinating study, having for its object the discovery of th‚e [”morphogenesis|causes]Á which have brought about the existing structure of humans, and ne™ding a knowledge of the alied sciences of [Šembryology]„ or [•developmental biology]‚, [‰phylogeny]†, and [‰histology]†. 

'[©Anatomical pathology|Pathological anatomy]'… (or 'morbid anatomy'’) is the study of [‡disease]‚d [•organ (anatomy)|organ]¦s, while sections of normal anatomy, apülied to various purposes, receive special names such as medical, surgical, gynaecological, artistic and superficial anatomy. ¥
The comparison of the anatomy of dif†erent [„race]×s of humans is part of the science of physical anthropology or anthropological anatomy. Ó
In the present edition of this work the subject of anatomy is treated systematicalšy rather than topographical‚y. è
Each anatomical article contains first a description of the structures of an organ or system (such as […nerve]ƒs, [Artery|arteries]‚, […heart]µ, and so forth), as it is found in humans; this is folŒowed by an acÕount of the development (embryology) and comparative anatomy (morphology), as far as [Vertebrate|vertebrate animals]ÿ are concerned; but only those parts of the lower animals which are of interest in explaining human body structure are here deaˆlt with. ÿ
The articles have a twofold purpose; first, to give enough details of structure to make the articles on physiology, surgery, ›medicine and pathology intelÿigible; and, secondly, to give the non-expert inquirer, or the worker in some other branch of science, the chief theories on wh¾ich the modern scientific groundwork of anatomy is built. 

