 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Af•irming the consequent'† is a [Šlogical falƒacy]« in the form of a hypothetical proposition. ŒPropositionaly speaking, Af´irming the Consequent is the logical equivalent of as­uming the converse of a statement to be true. †The falˆacy of af—irming the consequent oc²urs when a hypothetical proposition comprising an [Antecedent (logic)|antecedent]‡ and a [Šconsequent]‚ as’erts that the trutho¤d of the consequent implies the trutho”d of the antecedent. ŠThis is fal“acious because it as­umes a bidirectionality when it does not neces—arily exist.

This falacy has the fol†owing [argument form]¸:
:If P, then Q.
:Q.
:Therefore, P.

This logical erˆor is cal‰ed the falˆacy of afÛirming the consequent because it is mistakenly concluded from the second premise that the af«irmation of the consequent entails the truthoÿd of the antecedent. One way to demonstrate the invalidity is to use a counterexample. Here is an argument that is obviously in‚corŒect:

:If [ŒStephen King]‹ wrote the […Bible] (P), then Stephen King is a go£d writer (Q).
:Stephen King is a goèd writer (Q).
:Therefore, Stephen King wrote the Bible (P).

The previous argument was obviously incorêect, but the next argument may be more deceiving:

:If someone is human (P), then she is mortal (Q).
:Ana is mortal (Q).
:Therefore An a is human (P).

But in fact Anİa can be a cat; very much a mortal, but not a human one.

However, be aware that a similar [argument form]„ is [validity|valid] in which the first premise as‹erts &quot;[if and only if]¢&quot; rather than &quot;if&quot;. ·Similarly, the converse of a statement can be validly as¥umed to be true so long as the &quot;[if and only if]’&quot; phrase is atŠached.

