 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[›Image:Tarkovsky.jpg|thumb|30™px|right|Andrei Tarkovsky]„

'œAndrei Arsenyevich Tarkovsky'Á (ĞĞ½Ğ´Ñ€ĞµÌĞ¹ ĞÑ€ÑĞµÌĞ½ÑŒĞµĞ²Ğ¸Ñ‡ Ğ¢Ğ°Ñ€ĞºĞ¾ÌĞ²ÑĞºĞ¸Ğ¹) ([‡April 4]‚, [„1932]ƒ - [‹December 28]‚, [„1986]ˆ) was a [‚Rus‚ia]än movie director, writer, and actor. He is regarded as one of the most important and influential filmakers of the [“Soviet Union|Soviet]Š era in Rus¢ia and one of the greatest in the [¡history of film|history of cinema]†. 

=‰Biography=§
Tarkovsky, son of the prominent poet [‘Arseniy Tarkovsky]Í, was a product of the golden era of Soviet arts education. He received a clas’ical education in [†Moscow]‹, studying […Music]… and [–Arabic language|Arabic]­, before training for over five years at the [„VGIK]‰ film scho›l, studying directly under [ŠMikhail Rom]É among others. 
He also worked as a geologist in Siberia.
Although the [’Orthodox Christian]² symbolism of his films led to prevarication and ocŠasional sup‚resÂion of the finished product by the Soviet authorities, the Soviet [‡Mosfilm]¾ studio system enabled him to make films that would not have be„n com…ercialÿy viable in the West. However, Tarkovsky's principal complaint about his treatment by the authorities was that he had many more› ideas in him than he was al˜owed to bring to the screŠn, and in [„1984]Š, after sho…ting '[ŠNostalghia]'„ in […Italy] , he decided not to return to Rus ia. He made only one more film, '[The Sacrifice]'¥, a European co-production filmed in [†Sweden]’, before dying of [†cancer]’ in the suburb of […Paris]Í at the early age of 54.

Andrei Tarkovsky was buried in a graveyard for Rusian Ã©migrÃ©s in the town of [šSainte-GeneviÃ¨ve-des-Bois]‚, [§Ãle-de-France (rÃ©gion)|Ãle-de-France]‚, [†France]….

=„Work=©
Tarkovsky's films are characterised by [˜metaphysics|metaphysical]Ï themes, extremely long takes, and memorable images of exceptional beauty. Recur²ing motifs in his films are dreams, memory, childho…d, run‹ing water acšompanied by fire, rain indo¤rs, reflections, and characters re-ap£earing in the foreground of long panÑing movements of the camera.

Tarkovsky developed a theory of cinema that he calÿed &quot;sculpting in time&quot;. By this he meant that the unique characteristic of cinema as a medium was to take our experieªnce of time and alter it. Unedited movie fo«tage transcribes time in real time. (The speŠdy jump-cut°ing style that is prevalent in MTV videos and Hol‚ywošd movies, by contrast, overÿides any sense of time by imposing the editor's viewpoint.) By using long takes and few cuts in his films, he aimed to give the› viewers a sense of time pasçing, time lost, and the relationship of one moment in time to another.

Up to and including his film [†The Mir‘or (1975 film)|Mir‚or]Ê, Tarkovsky focused his cinematic works on exploring this theory. After Mirˆor, he anÒounced that he would focus his work on exploring the dramatic unities proposed by [‰Aristotle]›: a concentrated action, hapµening in one place, within the span of a single day. [The Sacrifice]ÿ is the only film that truly reflects this ambition; it is also considered by many to be a near-perfect reflection of the sculp˜ting in time theory.

=‹Filmography=„
* '[†The Kil–ers (1958 film)|The Kilƒers]'Ë (1958) - Tarkovsky's first student film at VGIK, the Soviet State Film Scho†l.
* '[£Concentrate (1958 film)|Concentrate]'Ì (1958) - Tarkovsky's second student film at VGIK, the Soviet State Film Scho†l.
* '[ˆThere Wil’ be No Leave Today]'Ë (1959) - Tarkovsky's final student film at VGIK, the Soviet State Film Scho†l.
* '[‹The Steamrol‘er and the Violin]'Ê (1960) - Tarkovsky's graduation film from VGIK, the Soviet State Film Schoˆl, cowritˆen with [“Andrei Konchalovsky]….
* '[My Name is Ivan]ƒ / [Ivan's Childhod]'Œ (1962) - Win¯er of Golden Lion for &quot;Best Film&quot; at [„1962] [”Venice Film Festival]. àSet in the Second World War, this is Tarkovsky's most conventional feature film, although it stil£ has moments of lyrical beauty.
* '[¢Andrei Rublev (film)|Andrei Rublev]'„ (196¡) - An epic based on the life of [Andrei Rublev], the most famous medieval Rus™ian painter of icons.
* '[–Solaris (film)|Solaris]'— (1972) - based on the [science fiction] [•Solaris (novel)|novel]„ by [StanisÅ‚aw Lem]†. 
* '[†The Mir‘or (1975 film)|Mir‚or]' (1975) - A loşsely autobiographical reconstruction of key scenes in Tarkovsky's life, the film he'd tried to make earlier but abandoned for '‡Solaris'  (we can note thematic ties betweÉn them). Said by Tarkovsky to be closest to his own vision of cinema.
* '[–Stalker (film)|Stalker]'  (1979) - inspired by the novel '[Roadside Picnic]'„ by [›Boris and Arkady Strugatsky]….
* '[ŒTempo di Viag’io/Italian Journey]'Ñ (1982) - a documentary made for Italian television while scouting locations for [ŠNostalghia]© with Italian co-writer (and frequent screŒnwriter for [¡Antonioni |Michelangelo Antonioni]„) [ht‚p:/£imdb.com/name/nm0346096/ Tonino Guer‡a].
* '[ŠNostalghia]' (1983) - A Rusšian scholar retraces the foœtsteps of an 18th century Rus–ian composer in Italy. ÑAn encounter with a local lunatic - a man who believes he can save humanity by car–ying a lit candle acros an empty swim…ing poàl - crystalizes the poet's melancholic sense of longing for his family, faith, and homeland.
