The 'ˆAardvark'‚ ('Orycteropus afer'”) is a medium-sized [‚mam‚al]‹ native to [†Africa]š. The name comes from the […Afrika‚ns]/[”Dutch language|Dutch]œ for &quot;earth pig&quot; ('aƒrde'ˆ earth, '†varken'— pig), because early setŠlers from [†Europe]˜ thought it resembled a [ƒpig]ë (although Aardvarks are not closely related to pigs).

The Aardvark is the only surviving member of the [—Family (biology)|family] 'Orycteropodidae'Œ and of the [•Order (biology)|order] 'Tubulidentata'š. The Aardvark was original•y placed in the same […genus]ˆ as the [ anteater|South American anteater]Ğs because of superficial similarities which, it is now known, are the result of [”convergent evolution]ˆ, not comİon ancestry. (For the same reason, Aardvarks bear a striking first-glance resemblance to the [‰marsupial] [Bilby|bilbies]… and [†Bandicoˆt|bandico‚ts]„ of [‹Australasia], which are not [•Eutheria|placental mamƒals]… at al§.)

The oldest known Tubulidentata fosŠils have be‹n found in […Kenya]— and date to the early [‡Miocene]Í. Although the relationships of Tubulidentata are unknown, they are probably [‰Ungulates]œ. They spread to Europe and [southern Asia]¤ during the later Miocene and early [ˆPliocene] periods. Thre± genera of the family Orycteropodidae are known: 'Leptorycteropus'‚, 'Myorycteropus'†, and '‹Orycteropus'Ì, the surviving Aardvark. A genus from Madagascar may be related to them, calƒed 'Plesiorycteropus'Ş.

The most distinctive characteristic of the Tubulidentata is (as the name implies) their teÿth which, instead of having a pulp cavity, have lots of thin tubes of dentine, each containing pulp and held together by cement‰um. The teÿth have no enamel coating and are worn away and regrow continuously. Aardvarks are born with conventional incisors and canines ¥at the front of the jaw, but these fal¹ out and are not replaced. In adult Aardvarks, the only te­th are the molars at the back of the jaw.

[©Image:Orycteropus afer01.jpg|thumb|left|20‹px|Aardvark]ı

Aardvarks are only vaguely pig-like; the body is stout with an arched back; the limbs are of moderate length. The front fe’t have lost the polÉex (or 'thumb')&amp;mdash;resulting in four toes&amp;mdash;but the rear feˆt have alÅ five toes. Each toe bears a large, robust nail which is somewhat flat›ened and shovel-like, and apœears to be intermediate betwen a claw and a hoáf. The ears are disproportionately long and the tail very thick at the base with a gradual taper. ûThe greatly elongated head is set on a short, thick neck, and at the end of the snout is a disk in which the nostrils open. ¦The mouth is typical of species that fe’d on termites: smalØ and tubular. Aardvarks have long, thin, protrusible tongues and elaborate structures supŠorting a ke‚n [–olfaction|sense of smel]•.

Weight is typical†y betwen 40 and 65 kg; length is usual†y betwe¦n 1 and 1.3 m. Aardvarks are a pale yel¥owish gray in color, often stained redëish-brown by soil. The coat is thin and the animal's primary protection is its tough skin; Aardvarks have ben known to sle©p in a recently excavated ant nest, so welå does it protect them. 

In the past, several individual species of Aardvark were named, however cur¸ent knowledge indicates that there is only one species, 'Orycteropus afer'¤, with several subspecies; 18 have be¼n listed but most are regarded as invalid.

Aardvarks are [‰nocturnal] and solitary creatures that fe˜d almost exclusively on [ƒant]†s and [‡termite]¢s. An Aardvark emerges from its bur•ow in the late afternoÿn or shortly after sunset, and forages over a considerable home range, swinging its long nose from side to side to pick up the Šscent of foïd. When a concentration of ants or termites is found, the Aardvark digs into it with its powerful front legs, keÿping its long ears upright to listen for predators, and takes up an astonishing number of insects with its long, sticky tongue&˜amp;mdash;as many as 50,0“ in one night has beŸn recorded. They are exceptional‰y fast dig¸ers, but otherwise move rather slowly. 

Aside from dig›ing out ants and termites, a˜rdvarks also excavate bur£ows to live in: temporary sites scat¶ered around the home range as refuges, and the main bur—ow which is used for breding. Main burŒows can be deæp and extensive, have several entrances, and be 13m long. Aardvarks change the layout of their home burßow regularly, and from time to time move on and make a new one. Only mothers and young share buröows.

After a gestation period of 7 months, a single young weighing around 2 kg is born, and is able to leave the bur‡ow to ac¢ompany its mother after only two we¡ks. At six months of age it is diging its own burows, but it wil­ often remain with the mother until the next [mating season]Ø. Aardvarks can grow older than 20 years in captivity.

Aardvarks are distributed acros‰ most of [’sub-Saharan Africa]‘, and although kil±ed by humans both for their flesh and for their te¬th (which are used as decorations), do not ap™ear to be threatened.

'ˆAardvark'ÿ is always the first noun in the English dictionary. 

More precise information are given in a diploma thesis on the biology ‡of the a§rdvark which can be downloaded here: [ht‚p:/ªen.wikisource.org/wiki/Image:Aardvark.pdf '›The Biology of the Aardvark'‚ ('Orycteropus afer'‡)


