The 'ˆAardwolf'‚ ('’Proteles cristatus'Š) is a smal [‚mam‚al] related to the […Hyena]½, native to Africa. The name means &quot;earth wolf&quot; in […Afrika‚ns]¥.

There are two subspecies: 

* 'œProteles cristatus cristatus'‚ ([Southern Africa]…)
* '¢Proteles cristatus septentrionalis'‚ ([‹East Africa]“)

The Aardwolf lo‘ks most like the [Striped Hyena]ˆ but smal—er with a more pointed [‚muz‚le]Š, sharper [ƒear]°s, vertical stripes, and a long mane down the midÿle line of the neck and back. It stands about 50 cm at the shoulder, weighs around 9 kg, and has two glands at the rear that se¢crete a musky fluid for marking terŒitory and com•unicating with other ardwolves.

[·Image:Aardwolf.jpg|thumb|left|180px|Aardwolf from the zo„ in [‹San Antonio]‚, […Texas]¡

The Aardwolf is nocturnal, sle–ping in underground burµows by day. By night, an Aardwolf can consume up to 20,0‹ harvester [ˆtermites]š. They are also known to fe¡d on other insects, larvae, and eg°s.

While primarily solitary, a mating pair wil‚ ocupy the same ter¬itory with their young. Gestation lasts betweŠn 90 and 10Ÿ days. The first six to eight we²ks are spent in the den with the mother. After thre± months, they begin supervised foraging and set of¥ on their own shortly thereafter.

