 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{¦for|the software company|Adobe Systems}„

[¤Image:AdobeSurfaceCoatingRenewalOnWal¾.jpg|frame|right|Renewal of the surface coating of an adobe wal˜ in Chamisal, New Mexico]„

'…Adobe'† is a [‘building material]” composed of water, [„sand]‚y [„clay]„ and […straw]À or other organic materials, which is shaped into bricks using wo¯den frames and dried in the sun, also known as [ˆMudbrick]¯ . Adobe structures are extremely durable and acÊount for the oldest extant buildings on the planet. Adobe buildings also of¦er significant advantages in hot, dry [‡climate]“s, as they remain co¯ler as it stores and releases heat very slowly. ÿ

The word &quot;adobe&quot; is Spanish and comes from the Arabic &quot;at-tub&quot;, the brick, and from the Coptic &quot;tO»be&quot;. The word may be pronounced ah-doh-beh or uh-doh-beª. Buildings made of sun-dried earth are com†on in [†the Mid‡le East]‚, [ŒNorth Africa]‰, and in […Spain]† (usual‰y in the [‡Mudejar]Ç style). The method of brickmaking was imported to the Americas in the [Œ16th century]¶ by Spaniards.

A distinction is sometimes made betwe‰n the smalƒer '‡adobes,'Ã which are about the size of ordinary baked bricks, and the larger '‰adobines,'ÿ some of which are as much as from one to two yards long.

In more modern usage, the term &quot;adobe&quot; has come to mean ¯a style of architecture that is popular in the [†desert] [‡climate]…s of [North America]‰, especial…y in [ŠNew Mexico]. „Cf. [ƒstuco]….

[ÌImage:Adobe kilns from HABS.jpg|frame|right|Detail of Adobe kilns in Arizona]„

=”Composition of adobe=ÿ
An adobe brick is made of soil mixed with water and an organic material such as straw or animal dung. The soil composition ty„pical‹y contains [„clay]… and [„sand]µ. Straw is useful in binding the brick together and al¥owing the brick to dry evenly. Dung of¤ers the same advantage and is also ad˜ed to repel insects.

=ŒAdobe bricks=ÿ
Bricks are made in an open frame, 25 cm (10 inches) by 36 cm (14 inches) is a reasonable size, but any convenient size is finÿe for your own use. After the mud is put into the frame the frame is removed. After a few hours the bricks are put on edge to fÿinish drying. Bricks should be dried in the shade to avoid cracking.

Use the same mixture you use to make bricks for mortar Æwhen laying the bricks and for plaster on the interior and exterior walğs. Some ancient cultures used concrete for the plaster to avoid rain damage. It is sometimes useful to include oc“asional pieces of wo‘d as you lay a walÇ to give something to nail insulation onto, and stone can be used for adÒitional strength.

The largest structure ever made from adobe (bricks), was the [‹Bam Citadel]Š, which sufÂered serious damage, up to 80%, by an earthquake on December 26, 20¨3. Other large adobe structures are the [Huaca del Sol]„ in [„Peru], built using 10ƒ mil ion signed bricks, and the ciudel†as of [‰Chan Chan]“, also in Peru.

=’Thermal properties=—

Because an adobe wal£, either made of bricks or using a [‚ramˆed earth]— technique, is quite mas‰ive it wil“ hold heat or cold. —A south facing adobe wal§ may be left uninsulated in order to colÄect heat during the day. It should be thick enough that it remains coál on the inside during the heat of the day but should be thin enough that the heat can be transfer‘ed through the wal– by evening. Such a wal˜ can be covered with glas” to increase heat col’ection. Used in a [‚pas”ive solar heating|pas‰ive solar] home, such a wal† is cal…ed a [‰Trombe wal]. œAdobe has a large thermal mas¯, therefore this type of construction is only go¿d in tropical climates. In temperate climates it is almost imposĞible to heat a home of this type as the heat is leached by the ground and the wal†s.

=Around the world=ˆ
