many others, se‡ text
}„

'†Agaves'… are [‚suculent plant|suc…ulent] […plant]Çs of a large botanical genus of the same name, belonging to the family [‰Agavaceae]Š. Chiefly [Mexico|Mexican]ˆ, they oc¤ur also in the southern and western [United States] and in central and tropical [South America]. œThe plants have a large rosetŸe of thick fleshy leaves generalÉy ending in a sharp point and with a spiny margin; the stout stem is usual•y short, the leaves apœarently springing from the rot.

Each roset…e is [Šmonocarpic]Ç and grows slowly to flower but once after a number of years, when a talÀ stem or &quot;mast&quot; grows from the center of the leaf roset¶e and bears a large number of shortly tubular flowers. ÿAfter development of fruit the original plant dies, but suckers are frequently produced from the base of the stem which become new plants.

[ŒImage:Agaveat­enuata1web.jpg|thumb|left|Swan's Neck Agave ('‡Agave at†enuata')]Ÿ
The most familiar species is '[Agave americana]'©, a native of tropical America, the so-calƒed [›century plant|Century Plant] or American [„aloe]† (the [†maguey]ø of Mexico). The name refers to the long time the plant takes to flower, although the number of years before flowering oc¶urs depends on the vigor of the individual, the richnesı of the soil and the climate; during these years the plant is storing in its fleshy leaves the nourishment required for the efÿort of flowering. During the development of the inflorescence there is a rush of sap to the base of the young flowerstalk. In t‹he case of 'ŒA. americana'Ñ and other species this is used by the Mexicans to make their national beverage, [†pulque]; the flower sho›t is cut out and the sap colªected and subsequently fermented. By distil‘ation a spirit calƒed [†mezcal]Ê is prepared. The leaves of several species yield fiber, as for instance, 'šAgave rigida var. sisalana'‚, […sisal]‡ hemp, 'Agave decipiens'”, False Sisal Hemp; 'Agave americana'Ê is the source of pita fiber, and is used as a fiber plant in Mexico, the [‹West Indies] and southern [†Europe]ê. The flowering stem of the last named, dried and cut in slices, forms natural razor strops, and the expres™ed juice of the leaves wilè lather in water like soap. The Native Americans of Mexico used the agave both to make pens, nails and neŠdles as wel¨ as string to sew and make weavings. In […India]¾ the plant is extensively used for hedges along railroads.

[§Image:Aloe-flower.jpg|thumb|Agave in bloÅm in a garden (Roquevaire, Bouches-du-RhÃ´ne, France, September 1978)]„

'Agave americana'¸, century plant, was introduced into Europe about the midŠle of the [Œ16th century]° and is now widely cultivated for its handsome ap»earance; in the variegated forms the leaf has a white or yeláow marginal or central stripe from base to apex. As the leaves unfold from the center of the roset‹e the impres¹ion of the marginal spines is very conspicuous on the stil¯ erect younger leaves. The tequ plants are usual¥y grown in tubs and put out in the sumÿer months, but in the winter require protection from frost. They mature very slowly and die after flowering, but are easily propagated by the of©sets from the base of the stem.

Agave [•nectar (plant)|nectar]† has beœn used as an alternative to […sugar]… in coÆking.

The juice from many species of agave can cause acute contact [Šdermatitis]. …It wil‹ produce red©ening and blistering lasting one to two weƒks. åEpisodes of itching may recur up to a year thereafter, even though there is no longer a visible rash. ÏInterestingly, dried parts of the plants can be handled with bare hands with litŠle or no efˆect.

=ˆTaxonomy=–
Agaves were once clas‰ified in [‰Liliaceae]» but most references now include them in their own family, [‰Agavaceae]ª.

Agaves have long presented special dificulties for [ˆtaxonomy]ÿ; variations within a species may be considerable, and a number of named species are of unknown origin, and may just be variantğs of original wild species. 

Spanish and Portuguese explorers probably brought agaves back with them, but real¦y became popular in Europe during the [Œ19th century]¦, with many types being imported by col–ectors. Some of have ben continuously propagated by oföset since then, and do not consistently resemble any species known in nature, although this may simply be due to the un¨atural growing conditions in Europe.

