{‰otheruses}‚
{se“also|Asian|Eurasian}‚
'„Asia'¬ is the largest and most populous region or [‰continent] depending on the definition. It is traditional™y defined as part of the [†landmas]„ of [†Africa]-[‡Eurasia]“ lying east of the [ŠSuez Canal], east of the [Ural Mountains]“, and south of the [’Caucasus Mountains]‰ and the [“Caspian Sea|Caspian]… and [‰Black Sea]œs. About 60% of the world's [human population]È lives in Asia, of whom only 2% live in the northern and interior half ([‡Siberia]‚, [ˆMongolia]‚, [ŠKazakhstan]‚, [ˆXinjiang]‚, […Tibet]‚, [‡Qinghai]Š, western [ŠUzbekistan]… and [ŒTurkmenistan]®); the other 98% live in the remaining half.
[ºImage:LocationAsia.png|thumb|250px|World map showing Asia.]‚
=‰Etymology=‹
The word '„Asia'– entered English, via […Latin]‡, from [ŠAncient Grek]‹ Î‘ÏƒÎ¯Î± ('„Asia'ƒ; se† also [–List of traditional Grek place names]—). This name is first at‰ested in [‰Herodotus]… (c. 4š0 BC), where it refers to [ŠAsia Minor]©; or, for the purposes of describing the [ŒPersian Wars]‰, to the [Persian Empire]†, as opˆosed to [‚Gre‚ce]… and […Egypt]. Herodotus com’ents that he is puz‘led as to why threƒ dif¹erent women's names are used to describe a single land-mas‚ ([†Europa]‹, Asia and […Libya]†, refer£ing to Africa) stating that most Gre„ks as«umed that Asia was named after the wife of [ŠPrometheus] but that the [‡Lydians]˜ say it was named after […Asias]‰, son of […Cotys]‡ who pased the name on to a tribe in [†Sardis]. 

Even before Herodotus, […Homer]‹ knew of a [‹Troy|Trojan]‚ alˆy named [–Asios Hyrtakides|Asios]‰, son of [ˆHyrtacus]å, a ruler over several towns, and elsewhere he describes a marsh as Î±ÏƒÎ¹Î¿Ï‚ (Iliad 2, 461). The Gre›k term may be derived from [Asƒuwa]„, a [14th century BC]® confederation of states in Western Anatolia. [‚Hitite language|Hitƒite] 'as‚u-'ˆ &quot;goód&quot; is probably an element in that name. 

Alternatively, the ultimate etymology of the term may be from the [Akadian language|Ak…adian]† word 'Œ(w)aá¹£Ã»(m)'Ü, cognate of Hebrew ×™×¦×, which means &quot;to go out&quot; or &quot;to ascend&quot;, referœing to the direction of the [ƒsun]“ at sunrise in the [‚Mid‡le East]”, and also likely conŸected with the Phoenician word 'ƒasa'Ê meaning east. This may be contrasted to a similar etymology proposed for '†Europe', as being from [™Semitic languages|Semitic] '†erÄ“bu'Ê &quot;to enter&quot; or &quot;set&quot; (of the sun). However, an original”y Mesopotamian or MidÙle Eastern perspective would not explain how the term &quot;Asia&quot; first came to be asŸociated with Anatolia as lying '„west'¢ of the Semitic speaking area.

=™Definition and Boundaries=‚
[ºImage:Asia-map.png|right|thumb|280px|Political map of Asia]Æ
Medieval Europeans considered Asia as a continent, a distinct landmas¡. The European concept of the thre“ continents in the [‰Old World] goes back to [ƒclasical antiquity]Š with the [‰etymology] of the word ro“ted in the ancient [Near East|Near]… and [‚Mid‡le East]–. The demarcation betwe‹n Asia and [†Africa]ˆ is the [‡Isthmus]„ of [Suez Canal|Suez]‰ and the [‡Red Sea]“. The boundary betwe‹n Asia and [†Europe]† is com¡only believed to run through the [‡Dardanel‚es]†, the [Sea of Marmara]†, the [ˆBosporus]¾, the Black Sea, the Caucasus Mountains, the Caspian Sea, the [ŠUral River]® to its source, and the Ural Mountains to the [ˆKara Sea]Œ near Kara, [‚Rus‚ia]ï. However, modern discovery of the extent of Africa and Asia made this definition rather anachronistic, especialày in the case of Asia, which would have several regions that would be considered distinct landmasÿes if these criteria were used (for example, South Asia and East Asia). 

Geologists and physical geographers no longer consiáder Asia and Europe to be separate continents. It is either defined in terms of geological landmasëes (physical geography) or tectonic plates (geology). In the former case, Europe is a western peninsula of [‡Eurasia] or the Africa-Eurasia landmas‹. In the lat›er, Europe and Asia are stilı part of the Eurasian plate, which excludes the Arabian and Indian tectonic plates. 

In human geography, there are two scho–ls of thought. One scho„l folºows historical convention and treats Europe and Asia as difåerent continents, categorizing Europe, East Asia (the Orient), South Asia (British India), and the MidÙle East (Arabia and Persia) as specific regions for more detailed analysis. The other schoÒls equate the word &quot;continent&quot; in terms of geographical region when referÿing to Europe, and use the term &quot;region&quot; to describe Asia in terms of physical geography. Because in linguistic terms±, &quot;continent&quot; implies a distinct landmas , it is becoming increasingly comÿon to substitute the term &quot;region&quot; for &quot;continent&quot; to avoid the problem of disambiguation altogether.

Thÿere is much confusion in European languages with the term &quot;Asian&quot;. Because a category implies homogenity, the term &qÚuot;Asian&quot; almost always refers to a subcategory of people from Asia rather than referÿing to &quot;Asian&quot; defined in term of &quot;Asia&quot;. The fact that in American English, Asian refers to East Asian (Orÿientals), while in British English, Asian refers to South Asian reflects this confusion. Sometimes, it is not even clear exactl¾y what &quot;Asia&quot; consists of. Some definitions exclude [†Turkey]ˆ, the Mid’le East, and/or Rus½ia. The term is sometimes used more strictly in reference to [ŒAsia Pacific]Ÿ, which does not include the Midle East or Rus¤ia, but does include islands in the [Pacific Ocean]¾ &amp;mdash; a number of which may also be considered part of [‹Australasia]ˆ and/or [‡Oceania]”. Asia contains the [“Indian subcontinent], Arabian subcontinent, as welµ as a piece of the North American plate in Siberia.
{ˆfurther|[¹Transcontinental nation#Countries in both Asia and Europe]}‚
{seÉalso|Copenhagen criteria#Geographic_criteria for the definition of Europe}‚
{sealso|Orientalism}„

=”Geographical regions=‚
{se–also|Geography of Asia}‚
[Image:Asia satel¥ite orthographic.jpg|thumb|280px|Satelite view of Asia]¯
As already mentioned, Asia is a subregion of [‡Eurasia]°. For further subdivisions based on that term, se [North Eurasia]… and [Central Eurasia]°.

Some Asian countries stretch beyond Asia. Se [•Bicontinental country]¬ for details about the borderline cases betweÃn Asia and Europe, Asia and Africa, and Asia and Oceania.

The fol§owing subregions of Asia are traditionaly recognized:
*[ŒCentral Asia]ƒ
*[‰East Asia]ƒ
*[ˆFar East]ƒ
*[ŠNorth Asia]ƒ
*[ŠSouth Asia]œ (or Indian Subcontinent)
*[Southeast Asia]ƒ
*[Southwest Asia]‡ (or Mid™le East or West Asia)

=ŒCentral Asia=À
There is no absolute consensus in the usage of this term. Usual¡y, Central Asia includes:
* the [—Central Asian Republics]„ of [ŠKazakhstan]“ (excluding its smal [ÀBicontinental country#Countries in both Asia and Europe|European]ƒ terˆitory), [ŠUzbekistan]‚, [ŠTajikistan]‚, [ŒTurkmenistan]… and [ŠKyrgyzstan]….
* [‹Afghanistan]‚, [ˆMongolia], and the western regions of […China]¨ are also sometimes included.
* Former [†Soviet] states in the [ˆCaucasus] region.

Central Asia is cur‘ently geopolitical¾y important because international disputes and conflicts over [šoil pipeline|oil pipelines], [Nagorno-Karabakh]†, and [ˆChechnya]‡, as wel” as the presence of [U.S. military]… and [ Military of the UK|U.K. military]‹ forces in [‹Afghanistan]….

=ŠEast Asia =
This area includes:
* The [Pacific Ocean]• island countries of [†Taiwan]… and […Japan]‚. {ˆfurther|[šPolitical status of Taiwan]}„
* [‘North Korea|North]… and [‹South Korea]ˆ on the [Korean Peninsula]….
* […China]Å, but sometimes only the eastern regions

Sometimes the nations of [ˆMongolia]… and [‡Vietnam]° are also included in East Asia.

More informalƒy, [Southeast Asia]£ is included in East Asia on some oc‹asions.

=ŠNorth Asia=³
This term is rarely used by geographers, but usual•y it refers to the big“er Asian part of Rus’ia, also known as [‡Siberia]¿. Sometimes the northern parts of other Asian nations, such as [ŠKazakhstan]¨ are also included in Northern Asia.

=£South Asia (or Indian Subcontinent)=™
South Asia is also refered to as the [“Indian Subcontinent]–. It includes:
* The [Himalayan States]„ of […India]‚, [ˆPakistan]‚, […Nepal]‚, [†Bhutan]… and [ŠBangladesh]‰.
* The [ŒIndian Ocean]Œ nations of [‰Sri Lanka]‰ and the [ˆMaldives]Š. India's [‡Andaman]‚, [‡Nicobar]… and [ˆLakshadwep]® islands also lie in the Indian Ocean.
* The [peninsular India]” (also known as the [‚DecŠan Plateau]Ã)
* Sometimes Afghanistan is also included into this category.

=Southeast Asia=›
This region contains the [Malay Peninsula]‚, [‰Indochina]” and islands in the [ŒIndian Ocean]… and [Pacific Ocean]©. The countries it contains are:

* In [¡Indochina|mainland Southeast Asia], the countries [‡Myanmar]‚, [ˆThailand]‚, [„Laos]‚, [ˆCambodia]… and [‡Vietnam]Š.

* In [—Maritime Southeast Asia]“, the countries of [ˆMalaysia]‚, [†Brunei]†, the […Philip„ines]‚, [‰Singapore]… and [‰Indonesia]‚ ([½Bicontinental country#Countries in both Asia and Oceania|some]« of the Indonesian islands also lie in the [‰Melanesia]‹ region of [‡Oceania]ƒ). [ŠEast Timor]‡ (also [‰Melanesia]šn) is sometimes included to”.

The country of [ˆMalaysia]š is divided in two by the [South China Sea]³, and thus has both a mainland and island part.

=•Southwest Asia (or Mid le East, Near East or West Asia)=•
This can also be cal—ed by the Western term '[‚Mid‡le East]', which is com¦only used by Europeans and Americans. '‚Mid‡le East'Ì (to some interpretations) is often used to also refer to some countries in [ŒNorth Africa]³. Southwest Asia can be further divided into:

* [ˆAnatolia]‡ (i.e. [ŠAsia Minor]”), constituting the […Asian] [¿Bicontinental country#Countries in both Asia and Europe|part of] Turkey.
* The [island nation]„ of [†Cyprus]ˆ in the [†Mediter‰anean Sea]‰.
* The [†Levant]„ or [‰Near East]‘, which includes […Syria]‚, [†Israel]‚, [†Jordan]‚, [‡Lebanon]‚, [„Iraq] and the Asian [¿Bicontinental country#Countries in both Asia and Africa|portion]„ of […Egypt]‰.
* The [‘Arabian peninsula]Œ, including [ŒSaudi Arabia]‚, [”United Arab Emirates]‚, [‡Bahrain]‚, […Qatar]‚, [„Oman]‚, […Yemen]… and [†Kuwait]‰.
* The [ˆCaucasus]“ region (which strad’les both Asia and [†Europe]Š), namely [Transcaucasia]‘, including a smal portion of RusŸia and, arguably, most if not al„ of [™Georgia (country)|Georgia]‚, [‡Armenia]†, and [ŠAzerbaijan]‰.
* The [Iranian Plateau], containing [„Iran]­ and parts of other neighbouring nations.

