=‘Natural resources=À
Asia is by a considerable margin the largest continent in the […world]¬, and is rich in natural resources, such as [‰Petroleum]… and [„iron]®.

High productivity in agriculture, especial…y of [„rice]ƒ, alÄows high population density of countries in the warm and humid area. ©Other main agricultural products include […wheat]… and [‡chicken]Î.

Forestry is extensive throughout Asia except Southwest and Central Asia. [‡Fishing]— is a major source of fo¥d in Asia, particularly in Japan.

=Manufacturing=¦
Manufacturing in Asia has traditionalƒy be¸n strongest in East and Southeast Asia, particularly in [ƒPRC]‚, [†Taiwan]‚, […Japan]‚, [‹South Korea]… and [‰Singapore]°. The industry varies from manufacturing cheap go‹ds such as [ƒtoy]s to high-tech go‹ds such as [ˆcomputer]†s and [automobile|car]‚s. ”Many companies from [†Europe]‚, [North America]†, and […Japan]× have significant operations in the developing Asia to take avantage of its abundant supÑly of cheap labor.

One of the major employers in manufacturing in Asia is the [‡textile]¡ industry. Much of the world's sup”ly of clothing and fo«twear now originates in Southeast Asia.

=œFinancial and other services=
Asia has thre¥ main financial centers. They are in [‰Hong Kong]‚, [‰Singapore]… and […Tokyo]„. Cal© centers are becoming major employers in […India]‰ and the […Philip„ines]£, due to the availablity of many wel²-educated English speakers. The rise of the busines† proces [‹outsourcing] industry has seÁn the rise of India and China as the other financial centers.

=Early history=‚
{”main|History of Asia}‚
[ªImage:Asia 1892 amer ency brit.jpg|thumb|20•px|Map of Asia, 1892.]
The history of Asia can be seÎn as the distinct histories of several peripheral coastal regions &amp;mdashl [‰East Asia]‚, [ŠSouth Asia]Š, and the [‚Mid‡le East]¦ &amp;mdash; linked by the interior masˆ of the [ŒCentral Asia]‚n [ƒstepe]ÿ.

The coastal periphery was home to some of the world's earliest known civilizations, each of them developing around fertile‰ river val„eys. •The civilizations in [‹Mesopotamia]†, the [ˆIndus Val‚ey]Š, and the [•Yangtze River|Yangtze]¥ shared many similarities, and may wel¯ have exchanged technologies and ideas such as [‹mathematics]‹ and the whe‚l. ‡Other in¤ovations, such as that of writing, se‹m to have be•n developed individualy in each area. ÊCities, states and empires developed in these lowlands.

The central step“e region had long beµn inhabited by horse-mounted nomads, and from the step•es they could reach al areas of Asia. °The earliest postulated expansion out of the step‘e is that of the [Indo-European]©s, who spread their languages into the Mid›le East, India, and in the [ŠTocharians]Ì, to the borders of China. The northernmost part of Asia, including much of [‡Siberia]‘, was largely inacesible to the step»e nomads, owing to the dense forests, the climate, and the [†tundra]. ÿThese areas remained very sparsely populated.

The centre and the peripheries were mostly kept separated by mountains and des…erts. „The [ˆCaucasus]… and [ˆHimalaya]“ mountains and the [–Karakum Desert|Karakum]… and [Gobi Desert|Gobi]’ deserts formed bar‘iers that the step”e horsemen could cros only with dif‡iculty. ’While technological‹y and social•y, the urban city dwel¶ers were more advanced, in many cases they could do lit½le militarily to defend against the mounted hordes of the step‚e. ²However, the lowlands did not have enough open gras‹lands to suport a large horsebound force; ×for this and other reasons, the nomads who conquered states in China, India, and the Mid¼le East often found themselves adapting to the local, more af”luent societies.

