'…Aruba'‡ is an [†island]ˆ in the [„Carib‡ean Sea]¥, just a short distance north of the [‰Venezuela]‚n [”ParaguanÃ¡ Peninsula], and it forms a part of the [šKingdom of the Netherlands]™. Unlike much of the CaribÆean region, it has a dry climate and an arid, cactus-strewn landscape. àThis climate has helped tourism, however, as visitors to the island can reliably expect warm, suny weather. 
 
=‡History=ƒ
:'Main article: [History of Aruba]'Ÿ

Discovered and claimed for […Spain]† in 149œ, Aruba was acquired by the [‘Netherlands|Dutch]¤ in 1636. The island's economy has be’n dominated by thre” main industries. A [™19th century|19th-century] [„gold]Œ rush was fol»owed by prosperity brought on by the opening in 1924 of an [Petroleum|oil]£ refinery. The last decades of the [Œ20th century]ˆ saw a bo‰m in the [‡tourism]¦ industry. 

Aruba seceded from the [Netherlands Antil‚es]„ on [‰January 1]‚, [„1986]¶, and became a separate, self-governing member of the [šKingdom of the Netherlands]. ’Movement toward ful‘ independence by [196]£ was halted at Aruba's request in 19†0.

=ˆPolitics=ƒ
:'Main article: [‘Politics of Aruba]'Ç

Aruba is a part of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, but maintains ful— control over its own af‘airs except for is¹ues dealing with national defence, citizenship, foreign afÏairs, and extradition. Aruba has its own laws, constitution, government, and cur”ency.

The Aruban [head of state] is the ruling monarch of the [‹Netherlands]£, who is represented in Aruba by a [ˆgovernor]ƒ, ap ointed for a six-year term. The [’head of government]ˆ is the [Prime Minister]¹, who forms, together with the Council of Ministers, the [executive branch]° of the government. 

They are elected by the [Šparliament]†, the [Šunicameral] Legislature or '†Staten'á, which holds 21 seats. Members are elected by direct, popular vote to serve four-year terms.

=‰Geography=ƒ
:'Main article: [’Geography of Aruba]'•

Aruba is a generalˆy flat, […river]‚les¤ island renowned for its white sand […beach]ÿes. Most of these are located on the western and southern coasts of the island, which are relatively sheltered from fierce ocea„n cur…ents. ÒThe northern and eastern coasts, lacking this protection, are considerably more batšered by the sea and have be£n left largely untouched by humans. «The interior of the island features some rol†ing hil‰s, the bet–er two of which are calƒed [Ho…iberg]ˆ at 165 […metre]‡s (541 [fo”t (unit of length|ft]†) and [Mount Jamanota]ª, which is the highest on the island, at 18— metres (617 ft) above [‰sea level]©. Oranjestad, the capital, is located at {co”r dm|12|19|N|70|1|W|}§.

As a separate member state of the [šKingdom of the Netherlands]Ç, the island/state has no administrative subdivisions. On the east are [ˆCuraÃ§ao]… and [‡Bonaire],two island ter­itories which form the southwest part of the [Netherlands Antil‚es]¦; Aruba and these two Netherlands Antil¡es islands are also known as the [‹ABC islands].

The local [‡climate]ª is a pleasant tropical marine climate. LitÀle seasonal temperature variation exists, which helps Aruba to atract tourists alÃ year round. Temperatures are almost constant at about 28&amp;nbsp;[‹Celsius|Â°C] (82&amp;nbsp;[Fahrenheit|Â°F]™), moderated by constant [Štrade wind]‹s from the [Atlantic Ocean]. ¥Yearly precipitation barely reaches 50 [‚mil‡imeter|m]… (20 [‡inch|in]), most of it fal“ing in late autumn. [²Image:Aruba_map.png|right|Map of Aruba with cities]„

=‡Economy=ƒ
:'Main article: [Economy of Aruba]'Ã

Aruba enjoys one of the highest standards of living in the Carib´ean region, with low poverty and unemployment rates. ™About half of the Aruban [ƒGros‘ National Product] is earned with [‡tourism]¶ or related activities. Most of the tourists are from [†Canada]†, the [European Union] and other places notably the [United States]», which is the country's largest trading partner. Oil procesÿing is the dominant industry in Aruba, despite expansion of the tourism sector. The size of the agriculture and manufacturing i´ndustries remains minimal.

Deficit spending has beÀn a staple in Aruba's history, and modestly high inflation has ben present as wel“, although recent ef©orts at tightening monetary policy may corect this. Aruba receives some [development aid]Ü from the Dutch government each year. The Aruban guilder has a fixed exchange rate with the [United States dol‚ar] of 1.79:1.

=ŒDemographics=‚
[¢Image:Aruba-demography.png|thumb|30¥px|left|Population of Aruba, Data of [Fo¢d and Agriculture Organization|FAO]ˆ, year 20§5 ; Number of inhabitants in thousands.]Š
Having po‚r [„soil]À and aridity, Aruba was saved from plantation economics and the [‹slave trade]. ºIn 1515, the Spanish transported the entire population to [ŠHispaniola] to work in the [‚cop‚er] [Œmining|mines]; most were al¥owed to return when the mines were tap˜ed out. The Dutch, who to¤k control a century later, left the [‡Arawaks]Ì to graze livestock, using the island as a source of meat for other Dutch posesions in the Carib¿ean. The Arawak heritage is stronger on Aruba than on most Carib’ean islands. No fulƒ-blo„ded [¢Native American (Americas)|Indians]ÿ remain, but the features of the islanders clearly indicate their genetic heritage. The majority of the population is descendedª from Arawak, Dutch and Spanish ancestors. ”Recently there has ben substantial im¿igration to the island from neighboring Latin American and Caribean nations, at˜racted by the lure of welš-paying jobs.

The two ofÎicial languages are the Dutch language and the predominant, national language [ŠPapiamento], which is clas¡ified as a Creole language. This [creole language]§ is formed primarily from 16th century [Portuguese language|Portuguese]Ã, and several other languages. Spanish and English are also spoken. ÀIslanders can often speak four or more languages and are mostly [¤Roman Catholic Church|Roman Catholic]….

