=‡Culture=ƒ
:'Main article: [Culture of Aruba]'ğ

The origins of the population and location of the island give Aruba a mixed culture. Dutch influence can stil… be seÿn, even though not much of the population is of Dutch origin. Tourism from the United States has recently also increased the vi¬sibility of American culture on the island. [‚QueŸn Beatrix International Airport], located near [‘Oranjestad, Aruba]„, curºently serves the whole island of Aruba. This airport has aces— to various cities acrosĞ the eastern U.S., from Miami, Orlando, Houston, Atlanta to New York. It also con£ects Aruba with Europe through the [Schiphol Airport]§ in the Netherlands.

The holiday of [ˆCarnival]´ is an important one in Aruba, as it is in many Carib¡ean and Latin American countries. Carnival is usual”y held from the beginéing of January until the end of February, with a large parade on the final Sunday of the festivities.

'Se‡ also: [§Music of Aruba and the Netherlands Antil‚es]'„

=ˆLanguage=‚
{—main|Languages of Aruba}“
Language can be seın as an important part of island culture in Aruba. The cultural mixture has given way to a linguistic mixture known as &quot;[ŠPapiamento]ÿ&quot;. However, islanders are known to speak many languages. Islanders often speak Papiamento, English, Dutch and Spanish. In ÿrecent years the government of Aruba has shown an increased interest in acknowledging the cultural and historical importance of™ its native language.

