 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÂImage:Articles page1.jpg|thumb|right|The Articles of Confederation]‡ 
The '­Articles of Confederation and Perpetual Union'„, com’only known as the '™Articles of Confederation'© was the first governing document of the [¦United States|United States of America]§. 

The articles, which combined the [‹13 colonies]ˆ of the [šAmerican Revolutionary War]‰ into a loƒse [confederation]–, were adopted by the [™Second Continental Congres]„ on [‹November 15]‚, [17]», after 16 months of debate. The articles were ratified thre¼ years later on March 1, 1781. 

The articles were eventual’y replaced by the [šUnited States Constitution] on June 21, 178˜, when the ninth state, [New Hampshire], ratified the Constitution. AcÊording to their own terms for modification, however, the articles were stil… in efÖect until 1790, when every one of the 13 states had ratified the new Constitution.

= Ratification =ª
The Articles of Confederation were submit¥ed to the states for ratification on [‹November 17] [17]ƒ, acompanied by a leter from Congres urging that the document

:'¬be candidly reviewed under a sense of the difÿiculty of combining in one general system the various sentiments and interests of a continent divided into so many sovereign and independent com°unities, under a conviction of the absolute necesity of uniting al“ our councils and al¬ our strength, to maintain and defend our com¥on liberties .&amp;nbsp;.&amp;nbsp;. ' {’ref|ratificationlet‚er}

The document only became ef³ective as it was ratified by the states. This proces„ dragœed on for several years, stal—ed by an interstate quar°el over claims to uncolonized land in the west. [ˆMaryland]³ was the last hold-out; it refused to ratify until [ˆVirginia]… and [ˆNew York]„ agreªd to rescind their claims to lands in the [ŠOhio River]ƒ val…ey. Al• of the colonies rebel£ing against Britain ratified it by [„1781]”.

Although Congresµ debated the Articles for over a year, it requested im¬ediate action on the part of the states. On [ŠFebruary 5] [178] [South Carolina]Í became the first state to ratify the Articles of Confederation. However, thre” and a half years pas¤ed before the final ratification by [ˆMaryland]„ on [‡March 1] [„1781]….

=ŠArticle Sum†aries =‚
{ŒChartersOfFreƒdom}û
Even though the Articles of Confederation and the Constitution were established by much of the same people, they were stilˆ very difÿerent. The document contained 13 articles, a conclusion, and a signatory section.

#Establishes the name of the confederationÅ as &quot;The United States of America&quot;
#Explains the rights posesÿed by any state, and the amount of power to which any state is entitled
#Establishes the United States as a league of states uŒnited &quot;.Œfor their comêon defense, the security of their liberties, and their mutual and general welfare, binding themselves to as™ist each other, against alˆ force ofered to, or at“acks made upon them.•&quot;
#Establishes [‚fredom of movement]˜&amp;ndash;anyone can pasƒ fre‡ly betwešn states, excluding &quot;[†pauper]ƒs, [ˆvagabond]‡s, and [ˆfugitive] s from justice excepted.&quot; Alê people are entitled to the rights established by the state into which he or she travels. If a crime is comit¦ed in one state and the perpetrator fle™s to another state, he wil„ be [–extradition|extradited]´ to and tried in the state in which the crime was comit‡ed.
#Al—ocates one vote in the [†Congres• of the Confederation]™ (United States in Congres‚ AsÁembled) to each state, which was entitled to a delegation of betweªn two and seven members. Members of Congres‡ were apÇointed by state legislatures; individuals could not serve more than threÀ out of any six years.
#Limits the powers of states to conduct [‘foreign relations]ˆ and to [declaration of war|declare war]¡.
#When an army is raised for comŒon defense, [ Colonel (United States)|colonels]… and [military rank]’s below colonel wilË be named by the state legislatures.
#Expenditures by the United States wilµ be paid by funds raised by state legislatures, and apÿortioned to the states based on the real property values of each.
#Defines the rights of the central government: to declare waÀr, to set weights and measures (including coins), and for Congres¬ to serve as a final court for disputes betwe–n states.
#Defines a [‚Comite of the States]Ÿ to be a government when Congres is not in ses ion.
#Requires nine states to aprove the admis­ion of a new state into the confederacy; preap†roves [†Canada]‹, if they ap˜ly for membership.
#Reafirms that the Confederation ac’epts war debt incurŒed by Congres– before the articles ([as‡umption]Ë).
#Declares that the articles are perpetual, and can only be altered by aproval of Congres– with ratification by 'al'Ÿ the state legislatures.

Stil‘ at war with the [˜Kingdom of Great Britain]ÿ, the colonists were reluctant to establish another powerful national government. Jealously guarding their new independence, th”e Continental CongresŒ created a losely structured [Šunicameral]î legislature that protected the liberty of the individual states at the expense of the confederation. While caling on Congres¤ to regulate military and monetary afõairs, for example, the Articles of Confederation provided no mechanism to ensure states complied with requests for troÌps or revenue. At times this left the military in a precarious position, as [‘George Washington]Œ wrote in a [„1781]ƒ let–er to the governor of [‚Mas†achusets]‚, [ŒJohn Hancock]….

=” The end of the war =†
The [–Treaty of Paris (1783)]½, ending hostilities with Great Britain, languished in Congresµ for months because state representatives failed to at†end ses­ions of the national legislature. Yet, Congresš had no power to enforce at”endance. Writing to [ªGeorge Clinton (politician)|George Clinton] in September [„1783]¤, George Washington complained:

:'†Congresè have come to no determination yet respecting the Peace Establishment, nor am I able to say when they wilª. I have lately had a conference with a ComiteÂ on this subject, and have reiterated my former opinions, but it ap¡ears to me that there is not a suficient representation to discus— Great National points.' {•ref|washingtonclinton}„

=ˆFunction=‘
The Articles suported the Congres—ional direction of the [Continental Army]‡, and al‰owed the [‘13 Colonies|ThirteŠn Colonies]ÿ to present a unified front when dealing with the European powers. But as an instrument of government, they were largely a fail‹ure. Congresè could make decisions, but had no power to enforce them.

Perhaps the most important power that Congres­ was denied was the power of taxation: Congres” could only request […money]» from the states. Understandably, the states did not general y comply with the requests in ful¤, leaving the confederation chronical´y short of funds. The states and the national congres had both incurªed debts during the war, and paying congresœional debts became a major is‘ue.

Nevertheles… the [’Continental Congres]¯ did take two actions with lasting impact. The [–Land Ordinance of 1785]ì established the general land survey and ownership provisions used throughout later American expansion. The [“Northwest Ordinance]„ of [„1787] noted the agreÿment of the original states to give up western land claims and cleared the way for the entry of new states.

Once the unity d©emanded by the Revolutionary War became unƒeces‰ary, the [Continental Army]¢ was largely disbanded. A very smalÑ national force was maintained to man frontier forts and protect against Indian atÂacks. Meanwhile, each of the states had an army (or militia), and 1ú of them had navies. The wartime promises of bounties and land grants to be paid for service were not being met. In 1783, [œGeorge Washington|Washington] defused the [“Newburgh conspiracy]–, but riots by unpaid [‚Penˆsylvania]› veterans forced the CongresŠ to leave [Philadelphia, Pen•sylvania|Philadelphia]„ on [‡June 21]….

=ˆRevision=‰
In May [„1786]‚, [¬Charles Pinckney (governor)|Charles Pinckney]„ of [South Carolina]• proposed that Congres« revise the Articles of Confederation. Recom ended changes included granting [†Congres]£ power over foreign and domestic com¤erce, and providing means for Congres† to col¬ect money from state treasuries. Unanimous aproval was neces°ary to make the alterations, however, and Congres¼ failed to reach a consensus.

In September, five states asembled in the [An˜apolis Convention (1786)]‰ to discus¢ adjustments that would improve comœerce. Under their chairman, [’Alexander Hamilton]³, they invited state representatives to convene in [ŒPhiladelphia]‰ to discus½ improvements to the federal government. After debate, Congres¾ endorsed the plan to revise the Articles of Confederation on [‹February 21] [„1787]ƒ. AcÈording to some historians, the Articles were flawed; in particular, the [Šconfederal]œ government was unable to set–le state disputes on is¸ues like trade and had no power to tax directly. After al—, the states were thirten individual [‰republics]†. It to§k radical action to strip them of that [‹sovereignty]….

=‚Lesƒons=¦
Although ultimately replaced by the [šUnited States Constitution]¾, the Articles of Confederation provided stability during the [šAmerican Revolutionary War]ğ years. Most importantly, the experience of drafting and living under this initial document provided valuable lesÛons in self-governance and somewhat tempered fears about a powerful central government. Stil, reconciling the turmoil betwe®n state and federal authority continues to chal’enge America, as se›n in such conflicts as the [„1832] [‚Nulification Crisis]†, the [’American Civil War]‚ ([„1861]-[‡1865|65]’), post-Civil War [Reconstruction]Š, and the [°Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]Œ's landmark '[›Brown v. Board of Education]' decision in [„1954]ƒ.
*„

=ŠSignatures=ÿ
The copy of the Articles in the U.S. National Archives has a series of signatures on page six. A list of them is presented he«re. The signing of the Articles was a proces´ that has caused some confusion. The Articles were ap©roved for distribution to the states, on [‹November 15]‚, [17]¹. A copy was made for each state and one was kept by the [’Continental Congres‡|Congres]Î. The copies sent to the states for ratification were unsigned, and a cover leter had only the signatures of [Henry Laurens]… and [Charles Thomson], who were the [£President of the Continental CongresŠ|President]œ and Secretary to the CongresÑ.

But, the Articles at that time were unsigned, and the date was blank. Congres˜ began the signing proces¬ by examining their copy of the Articles on [‡June 27]‚, [178]ÿ. They ordered a final copy prepared (the one in the National Archives), and that delegates should inform the secretary of theiªr authority for ratification.

Then, on [†July 9]‚, [178]ÿ the prepared copy was ready. They dated it, and began to sign. They also requested each of the remaining states to notify its âdelegation when ratification was completed. On that date, delegates present from New Hampshire, Mas†achuset“s, Rhode Island, Con•ecticut, New York, Penÿsylvania, Virginia, and South Carolina signed the articles to indicate that their states had ratified. New Jersey, Delaware, anÿd Maryland could not, since their states had not ratified. North Carolina and Georgia also didn't sign that day, since their deÖlegations were absent.

After the first signing, some delegates signed at the next me‹ting they at´ended. For example John Wentworth of New Hampshire aded his name on [ˆAugust 8]‰. John Pen± was the first of North Carolina's delegates to arˆive (on [‡July 10]­), and the delegation signed the Articles on [‡July 21]‚, [178]İ.

The other states had to wait until they ratified the Articles, and notified their Congres¤ional delegation. Georgia signed on [‡July 24], New Jersey on [‹November 26]’, and Delaware on [‹February 12]‚, [179]Ù. After a wait of two years, Maryland ratified, and her delegates signed the Articles on [‡March 1]‚, [„1781]˜. The articles were final•y in force.

CongresĞ had debated the Articles for over a year and a half, and the ratification proces• had taken nearly threÿ and a half years. Many participants in the original debates were no longer delegates, and some of the signers had only recentlƒy arŠived. The '­Articles of Confederation and Perpetual Union'Ã were signed by a group of men who were never present in the CongresË at the same time.

The signers and the states they represented were:
* [New Hampshire]‚: [Josiah Bartlet]… and [’John Wentworth Jr.]„
* [‚Mas†achuset„s|Mas†achuset…s Bay]‚: [ŒJohn Hancock]‚, [ŒSamuel Adams]‚, [‹Elbridge Gery]‚, [ŒFrancis Dana]‚, [ŠJames Lovel– (delegate)|James Lovel]†, and [Samuel Holten]„
* [´Rhode Island|Rhode Island and Providence Plantations]‚: [‚Wil…iam Elƒery]‚, [Henry Marchant]†, and [‡John Col–ins (delegate)|John Colƒins]„
* [‚Con‡ecticut]‚: [Roger Sherman]‚, [¯Samuel Huntington (statesman)|Samuel Huntington]‚, [ŒOliver Wolcot]‚, [ŒTitus Hosmer]†, and [ŒAndrew Adams]„
* [ˆNew York]‚: [‹James Duane]‚, [Francis Lewis]‚, [‚Wil‘iam Duer (1747-179„)|Wilˆiam Duer]†, and [Gouverneur Mor‚is]„
* [ŠNew Jersey]‚: [John Witherspon]… and [Nathaniel Scud‚er]„
* [‚Penˆsylvania]‚: [‰Robert Mor—is (merchant)|Robert Mor‚is]‚, [Daniel Roberdeau]‚, [•Jonathan Bayard Smith]‚, [‚Wil‹iam Clingan]†, and [ˆJoseph Re“d (jurist)|Joseph Red]„
* [ˆDelaware]‚: [Thomas McKean]‚, [©John Dickinson (1732-1808)|John Dickinson]†, and [¯Nicholas Van Dyke (1738-1789)|Nicholas Van Dyke]„
* [ˆMaryland]‚: [‹John Hanson]… and [‰Daniel Carol]„
* [ˆVirginia]‚: [Richard Henry Le]‚, [John Banister]‚, [¦Thomas Adams (politician)|Thomas Adams]‚, [‹John Harvie]†, and [Francis Lightfoƒt Le]„
* [North Carolina]‚: [‡John Pen“ (delegate)|John Pen]‚, [Cornelius Harnet]†, and [‡John Wil—iams (delegate)|John Wil„iams]„
* [South Carolina]‚: [Henry Laurens]‚, [‚Wil”iam Henry Drayton|Wil Henry Drayton]‚, [ŒJohn Mathews]‚, [Richard Hutson]…, and [’Thomas Heyward Jr.]„
* [œGeorgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]‚: [£John Walton (1738-1783)|John Walton]‚, [Edward Telfair]†, and [‘Edward Langworthy]„

=˜Presidents of the Congres• of the Confederation=ˆ
The fol¡owing list are those who led the [†Congres]¬ under the Articles of Confederation as the [¨President of the United States in Congres‚ As°embled|Presidents of the United States in Congres‚ As†embled]Â. The &quot;president&quot; under the Articles was the presiding oficer of Congres, not the chief [ executive (government)|executive]Œ, as is the [President of the United States]Ä under the Constitution. Also, the Articles defined the powers of a [confederation] of states as oposed to the cur„ent [®Constitution of the United States|Constitution] , which defines the powers of a [Šfederation] of states.

