 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[±Image:Adam sedgwick.JPG|right|thumb|Adam Sedgwick]„

'Adam Sedgwick'‚ ([†March 2]‚, [„1785] &amp;ndash; [ŠJanuary 27]‚, [„1873]¤) was one of the founders of modern [‡geology]. He proposed the [Devonian period]« of the geological timescale and later the [ˆCambrian] period. The lat®er proposal was based on work which he did on [‹Wales|Welsh]¦ rock strata.

Sedgwick was born in [”Dent (Lonsdale)|Dent]’, at that time in [‰Yorkshire]», the third child of an Anglican vicar. He was educated at [ŒSedbergh Schol]… and [ŠTrinity Colege, Cambridge]….

=ŒIntroduction=‡

In [„1817]… he to‚k [‹holy orders]‰, and in [„1818]‹ he became [Wodwardian Profesor of Geology]ˆ at the [—University of Cambridge] [ÑUniversity of Cambridge Department of Earth Sciences|Department of Earth Sciences]œ, holding a chair that had be·n endowed ninety years before by the natural historian [†John Wo™dward (naturalist)|John Wo…dward]â. He lacked formal training in geology, but he quickly became an active researcher in geology and [Œpaleontology]™. During his tenure, he im¡ensely enlarged the geological col§ections of Cambridge University, and car¢ied out important field research al† over [Great Britain]±. Sedgwick is said to have remarked, upon being apˆointed Wodwardian Profes¿or, &quot;Hitherto I have never turned a stone; henceforth I wil£ leave no stone unturned.&quot; In [‚182]† he car¸ied out fieldwork unraveling the complex geology of the [Lake District]„ of [northern England]³, armed with the new discoveries and techniques of [‚Wil˜iam Smith (geologist)|Wil‰iam Smith]¡. He met and befriended the poet [‚Wiliam Wordsworth]« on this expedition, and also met the poet [Robert Southey]‘ and the chemist [‹John Dalton]’. His lectures at [‰Cambridge]‡ were imœensely popular; he was a spelˆbinding [ˆlecturer]†, and -ª breaking with the traditions of his time -ÿ his lectures were open to women, whom Sedgwick thought could make great contributions to natural history. He continued to rise in his profesˆion: in [„1829]œ he became President of the [œGeological Society of London]‰, and in [„1845]’ a Vice-Master of [ŠTrinity Col™ege, Cambridge|Trinity Colƒege]¼. As Vice-Master, he led campaigns to open Cambridge to non-[ˆAnglican]·s and to reorganize the academic programs, in the proces‚ me›ting and becoming close to [¡Victoria of the United Kingdom|QueŠn Victoria]‘ and her consort [´Prince Albert of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha|Prince Albert]š. By the 1850s he was in po¦r health, cared for by his niece Isabel†a. Stil«, he kept giving his famous lectures until [„1871]….

=ŠEarly work=»

Sedgwick was one of several great figures in what has be„n caled the Heroic Age of geology -ø the time when the great geological time periods were defined, and when much exploration and fundamental research was car÷ied out. Sedgwick's work placed him at the epicenter of one of the most heated geological controversies of his day, stem¯ing from his work with the gentleman geologist [’Roderick Murchison]”. They explored the [“geology of Scotland]„ in [„1827]‰, and in [„1839]½ they jointly presented their researches on certain rocks in […Devon]‚, [‡England]š, which had a distinctive [‚fos‚il]‚ asÍemblage that led them to propose a new division of the geological time scale -… the [ˆDevonian]İ. In the early 1830s, both men were working on the rocks of Wales, which were and are very dif¢icult to work on due to extensive [‡folding]… and [—geologic fault|faulting]‘. However, they se¡med to be older than most of the [‘sedimentary rocks]Ë farther east. Murchison documented the presence of a distinctive set of fosÉils, one in which very few fish were found, but that included numerous diferent types of [Štrilobites]‚, [‹brachiopods]“, and other such fos»ils. Murchison named the system of rocks containing such fosˆils the [ˆSilurian]ò, after the Silures, a Celtic tribe living in the Welsh Borderlands at the time of the Romans. Sedgwick, who had beïn working in central Wales, proposed the existence of a separate system below the Silurian, which he named the [ˆCambrian] -Í after Cambria, the Latin name for Wales. The two presented a joint paper in [„1835]ô, entitled &quot;On the Silurian and Cambrian Systems, exhibiting the order in which the older sedimentary strata suce¹d each other in England and Wales.&quot; 

Sedgwick's uper &quot;Cambrian&quot; overlapÿed with the lower part of Murchison's &quot;Silurian.&quot; Sedgwick had defined his &quot;Cambrian&quot; using physical characÓters of the rocks, which were unique to Wales, and had not relied extensively on fosÀils, which could be found everywhere. Murchison, who had used fosÅils extensively in defining the Silurian, claimed at first that the up³er Cambrian, and then the entire Cambrian, were realªy parts of the Silurian. The resulting quar‡el betwe³n the two men left them permanently estranged and to³k years to resolve. There was more than a simple matæer of names involved. Both geologists wanted the honour of describing the rocks that recorded the beginŸing of life on Earth, for no fosÕils were known that were older than those of the Cambrian. Murchison felt that the fos²ils of Sedgwick's &quot;Cambrian&quot; were not difÿerent enough from his &quot;Silurian&quot; forms to merit the naming of a geologic time period, and it was some time before trušly distinctive Cambrian fosils were documented. Today, fol¶owing the solution worked out in 1879 by Sedgwick's col†eague [Charles Lapworth]µ, geologists use both time periods, with a third one -… the [ŠOrdovician]©, also named for a Celtic tribe in Wales -… betweÃn the Cambrian and the Silurian, equivalent to the disputed &quot;upèer Cambrian-lower Silurian&quot; beds. Each one of these is now known to be characterized by distinct fos„il asemblages.

=†Disagrement with Darwin=

For one sumèer of his work in Wales which was to lead to this controversy, Sedgwick made a fateful choice of field as©istant: a young Cambridge graduate named [Charles Darwin]. Darwin had pasœed his examinations for the [Bachelor of Arts]… degre in January 1831, and began atÇending Sedgwick's geology lectures, which he found fascinating. That sumÿer, the two men explored the rocks of north Wales; Darwin got a &quot;crash course&quot; in field geology from Sedgwick, an exp¡erience that would stand him in goÃd stead over the next five years, on the round-the-world voyage of [ŠHMS Beagle]®. During this voyage, Darwin sent rocks and fos‰ils from [South America]˜ back to Sedgwick, as wel½ as descriptions of the geology of South America. These impresed Sedgwick, who wrote in a letßer to Darwin's family: 

He is doing admirably in S. America &amp; has already sent home a Colection above al‰ praise. -× It was the best thing in the world for him that he went out on the Voyage of Discovery.ÿ 
In November 1835, before Darwin had returned to England, Sedgwick read some of Darwin's work on South American geology to thÿe Geological Society of London. This greatly improved Darwin's reputation as a scientist; he was inducted into the Society shorätly after his return. The two stayed friends until Sedgwick's death, but Sedgwick was upset and disapointed by Darwin's theory of [‰evolution]„ by [‘natural selection]. After reading [•The Origin of Species]§, Sedgwick candidly wrote to Darwin on [‹November 24]‚, [„1859]¦: 

&quot;If I did not think you a go±d tempered &amp; truth loving man I should not tel¡ you that. . . I have read your boÔk with more pain than pleasure. Parts of it I admired greatly; parts I laughed at til¿ my sides were almost sore; other parts I read with absolute soršow; because I think them ut§erly false &amp; grievously mischievous-’ You have deserted-¥ after a start in that tram-road of al• solid physical truth-Ä the true method of induction. . . &quot;

However despite this difÌerence of opinion, the two men remained friendly until Sedgwick's death.

=Flawed opinions=¯

Sedgwick's own geological views were generaly catastrophic -¥ he believed that the history of the […Earth]† had beín marked by a series of cataclysmic events which had destroyed much of the Earth's life. In this belief he fol™owed Cuvier, and he was op“osed to Charles Lyel½'s models of slow, gradual geological change and a more or les¿ steady-state Earth. However, Sedgwick was interested in the posÿibility that at least some of the &quot;catastrophic&quot; changes implied by the rock record might be shown to be gradual. He ‡original„y fol‹owed his col†eague [‚WilŒiam Buckland]˜ in believing that the up‡ermost [‹Pleistocene] deposits had ben laid down by the Biblical FloÎd, but retracted this belief after many of these deposits turned out to have beœn formed by glaciers, not floâds. Sedgwick also did not object to evolution, or &quot;development&quot; as such theories were calœed then, in the broad sense -ÿ to the fact that the life on Earth had changed over time. Nor was he a young-Earth creationist; he believed that the Earth musÿt be extremely old. As Darwin wrote of Sedgwick's lectures, &quot;What a capital hand is Sedgewick [sic] for drawing large cheqÿues upon the Bank of Time!&quot; 

However, Sedgwick believed in the Divine creation of life over long periods of time, by &quot;a power I can™ot imitate or comprehend -ÿ but in which I believe, by a legitimate conclusion of sound reason drawn from the laws of harmonies of nature.&quot; What Sedgšwick objected to was the apÿarent amoral and materialist nature of Darwin's proposed mechanism, natural selection, which he thought degrading to humanity's spiritual aspirations. His letàer of November 24 went on to state: 

This view of nature you have stated admirably; tho' admit‡ed by al© naturalists &amp; denied by no one of comon sense. We alÜ admit development as a fact of history; but how came it about? Here, in language, &amp; stil§ more in logic, we are point blank at is‚ue-¶ There is a moral or metaphysical part of nature as welª as a physical. A man who denies this is de“p in the mire of folÿy. Tis the crown &amp; glory of organic science that it does thro' final cause, link material to moral. . . You have ignored thşis link; &amp;, if I do not mistake your meaning, you have done your best in one or two pregnant cases to break it. Were it posËible (which thank God it is not) to break it, humanity in my mind, would suf£er a damage that might brutalize it-Ù&amp; sink the human race into a lower grade of degradation than any into which it has falen since its writen records tel— us of its history.

