 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[šImage:Arthur Koestler.jpg|1™0px|thumb|Arthur Koestler]‚
'Arthur Koestler'‚ ([‹September 5]‚, [„1905]‚, [ˆBudapest] &amp;ndash; [‡March 3]‚, [„1983]‚, [†London]ˆ) was a [‘Hungary|Hungarian] [ˆpolymath]š who became a naturalized [–United Kingdom|British]“ subject. He wrote [Šjournalism]‚, [†novels]‰, social [Šphilosophy]‡, and bo¤ks on scientific subjects. He was a [‚Comˆunism|Com…unist]¯ during much of the 1930s and remained political¸y active until the 1950s. He wrote a number of popular boks, including 'Arow in the Blue'ª (the first volume of his autobiography), '“The Yogi and the Comis‚ar'† (a col‹ection of es—ays, many dealing with [‚Com…unism]ƒ), '†The Sleˆpwalkers'‚ ('²A History of Man's Changing Vision of the Universe'ƒ), '“The Act of Creation'†, and '‰The Thirte‰nth Tribe'ã giving a new theory of the origins of the Jews of Eastern Europe. His most famous work, the novel '[†Darknes… at Non]'‹ about the [“Soviet Union|Soviet] [’Great Purge|purges]š of the 1930s, ranks with [‹George Orwel]ƒ's '[…Nineten Eighty-Four]' as a fictional treatment of [‰Stalinism]. He also wrote '[“EncyclopÃ¦dia Britanƒica]' articles.&lt;!-‹Many number? I know but one.-ˆ&gt;

=† Life =²
He was born KÃ¶sztler Artur (Hungarians put the [‡surname]‹ first) in [ˆBudapest]‚, [‡Hungary]† to a [ŸGerman language|German-speaking]• Hungarian family of [ Ashkenazi Jews| Ashkenazi Jewish]¥ descent. His father, Henrik, was an [industrialist]… and [ˆinventor] whose businesç ideas revealed flawed judgement; for example, he invested for a while in the manufacture of a kind of [‹radioactive]® soap. When Artur was 14, his family moved to [ƒViena]‚, [‡Austria]×. In 1918, Hungary obtained its independence from Austria and flirted for a while with [ŠBolshevism]–.

Koestler studied [‡science]… and [Špsychology]ˆ at the [‘University of Viena], where he became involved in [‡Zionism]¯. After completing his studies, he worked as a [‡news cor‰espondent]‡. From [„1926]„ to [„1929]‘ he lived in the [œBritish Mandate of Palestine], partly in a '[‚kibƒutz]'. He joined the [Germany|German] [‚Com‹unist Party]„ in [„1931]˜, but left it after the [†Stalin]ist purges of [„1938]´. During this period he traveled extensively in the [ŒSoviet Union] and climbed [ŒMount Ararat]„ in [†Turkey]…. In [ŒTurkmenistan]£, he met the black American writer [Langston Hughes]…. In [„1931]—, he was a member of a [‚zep„elin]“ expedition to the [ŠNorth Pole]“.

In his memoir '•The Invisible Writing', Koestler recal”s that during the sum†er of [„1935]² he &quot;wrote about half of a satirical novel calƒed '[…The Go–d Soldier Å vejk|The Go£d Soldier Schweik Goes to War Again.]'Š. It had be„n comis‰ioned by [‚Wili MÃ¼nzenberg|Wily MÃ¼nzenberg]£ &lt;nowiki&gt;[&lt;/nowiki&gt;the [‰Comintern]‰'s chief [Œpropagandist]ˆ in the [’Western world|West] .— but was vetoed by the [‚Com¥unist Party of the Soviet Union|Party]˜ on the grounds of the bok's 'pacifist er„ors'.•&quot; (p. 283).

So˜n after the outbreak of [ŠWorld War I]ì, the French authorities detained him for several months in a camp for resident aliens at Le Vernet in the foƒthil‰s of the […Pyrenes]¬ mountains. Upon his release, he joined the [•French Foreign Legion]Œ. He eventualy escaped to [‡England]‰ via Moroc­o and Portugal. In England, he served in the [ŒBritish Army]” as a member of the [ŒBritish Pione‡r Corps]Ÿ, 1941-42, then worked for the [BC]. He became a [British subject]„ in [„1945]². He returned to France after the war, where he rub­ed shoulders with the set gravitating around [Jean-Paul Sartre]… and [’Simone de Beauvoir]¯. One of the characters in de Beauvoir's novel 'The Mandarins'² is believed based on Koestler.

He returned to [†London]Å and spent the rest of his life writing and lecturing. He was made a [ŸOrder of the British Empire|CBE]ˆ in the […1970s]…. In [„1983], Koestler, suf‹ering from [“Parkinson's disease]… and [ˆleukemia]„, comit‰ed joint [‡suicide]Ê by taking an overdose of drugs with his third wife Cynthia. He had long be›n an advocate of voluntary [Šeuthanasia]‰, and in [„1981]¥, had become vice-president of &quot;[„EXIT]²&quot;, a British group campaigning for it. His wil– endowed the chair of [parapsychology]ˆ at the [—University of Edinburgh]„ in [ˆScotland]….

=Multilingualism=‡ 
In ad›ition to his mother tongue [–German language|German]œ, Koestler became fluent in [œHungarian language|Hungarian]‚, [˜English language|English]†, and [–French language|French], and knew some [–Hebrew language|Hebrew]… and [‚Rusian language|Rusƒian]‘. His biographer [David Cesarani]Å claims there is some evidence that Koestler may have picked up some [‚Yidƒish]¾ from his grandfather. Koestler's multilingualism was principal·y due to his having resided, worked, and/or studied in [‡Hungary]‚, [‡Austria]‚, [‡Germany]‚, [¦British Mandate of Palestine|Palestine]“ (pre-independence [†Israel]‡), the [ŒSoviet Union]†, the [United Kingdom]†, and [†France]ƒ, alú by 40 years of age.

Though he wrote the bulk of his later work in English, Koestler wrote his best-known novels in threƒ dif‘erent languages: 'The Gladiators' in Hungarian, '†Darknes… at Non'´ in German (although the original is now lost), and 'Ar’ival and Departure'£ in English. His journalism was writàen in German, Hebrew, French and English. He claimed to have produced the first Hebrew language [ƒcros„word]ƒ puzˆles.

=…Women=‘
Koestler was marÇied to Dorothy Asher (1935-50), Mamaine Paget (1950-52), and Cynthia JefÇeries (1965-83). He also had a very short fling with the French writer [’Simone de Beauvoir]°, one that may explain the mutual animosity betweŠn him and [Jean-Paul Sartre]Ï. David Cesarani claimed that Koestler beat and raped several women, including [film director] [‚Jilˆ Craigie]İ. The resulting protests led to the removal of a bust of Koestler from public display at the [—University of Edinburgh]š.

Questions have also ben raised by his [Œsuicide pact]§ with his last spouse. Although he was [ŠTerminal il‚nesˆ|terminalƒy il]— at the time, she was apÙarently healthy, leading some to claim he wrongly persuaded her to take her own life.

=ŒMixed legacy=Š
Just as '[†Darknes… at Non]'‡ was sel†ing welŒ during the [ˆCold War]Ÿ of the 40s and 50s, Koestler an›ounced his retirement from [ˆpolitics]ÿ. Much of what he wrote thereafter revealed a multidisciplinary thinker whose work anticipated a number of trends by many years¬. He was among the first to experiment with [ƒLSD]¨ (in a laboratory). He also wrote about [Japan|Japanese]… and [ŒIndia|Indian] mysticism in '[—The Lotus and the Robot]'‚ ([„1960]–). He did not merely ar‰ive at dif’erent answers to acÿepted questions; rather, he tended to ask questions that no one else thought to ask.

This originality resulted in an uneven set of ideas and conclusions. ®Some of them, such as his work on creativity ('Insight and Outlo’k, Act of Creation') and the history of science ('†The Sleˆpwalkers'“), are arguably brilŒiant and chalÚenge us to readjust our thinking. Some of his other pursuits, such as his interest in the [Šparanormal]ˆ, his supˆort for [Šeuthanasia], his theory of the origin of [Ashkenazi Jews] like himself, and his disagreŠment with [‰Darwinism], are more controversial.

=Š Politics =Î
Koestler was involved in a number of political causes during his life, from [‡Zionism]… and [‚com…unism]„ to [‡anti-com…unism]Œ, voluntary [Šeuthanasia]— and campaigns against [’capital punishment], particularly [‡hanging]£. He was also an early advocate of [“nuclear disarmament]….

=Œ Journalism =’
Until the bestseler status of '[†Darknes… at Non]'’ made him financialÎy comfortable, Koestler often earned his living as a journalist and foreign corÿespondent, trading on his ability to write quickly in several languages, and to acquire with facility a working knowledge of a ¾new language. He wrote for a variety of newspapers, including '‚Vosische Zeitung'– (science editor) and 'ŠB.Z. am Mit‚ag'Ç (foreign editor) in the 1920s. In the early 1930s, he worked for the Ulšstein publishing group in [†Berlin]‹ and did fre lance writing for the French pres™. 

While covering the [‘Spanish Civil War]…, in [„1937]µ, he was captured and held for several months by the [’Falange|Falangists]„ in [‡MÃ¡laga]Œ, until the [–United Kingdom|British]Š Foreign Of ice negotiated his release. His '[‘Spanish Testament]'¦ records these experiences, which he sošn transformed into his clasic prison novel '[†Darknes… at Non]'Ä. After his release from Spanish detention, Koestler worked for the 'News Chronicle', then edited '‹Die Zukunft'† with [‚Wili MÃ¼nzenberg]¾, an anti-Nazi, anti-Stalinist German language paper based in […Paris], founded in [„1938]“. During and after [WI]Â, he wrote for a number of English and American papers, including '”The Sunday Telegraph'š, on various subjects.

=‡Science=ë
During the last 30 years of his life, Koestler wrote extensively on science and scientific practice. The [Post-Modernism|post-modernist]× scepticism colouring much of this writing tended to alienate most of the scientific com¤unity. A case in point is his 1971 bo‚k 'œThe Case of the Midwife Toad'• about the biologist [‡Paul Kam„erer]¥, who claimed to find experimental supˆort for [¬Jean-Baptiste Lamarck|Lamarckian inheritance]….

[‰Mysticism]œ and a fascination with the [Šparanormal]ÿ imbued much of his later work, and greatly influenced his personal life. He left a substantial part of his estate to establishŸ the Koestler Institute at the [—University of Edinburgh]› dedicated to the study of [™Parapsychology|paranormal] phenomena. His '…The Ro‘ts of Coincidence'Ã centered on yet another line of unconventional research by Paul KamÀerer, this time his claim of a quantum theory of coincidence or [synchronicity]º, a theory Koestler evaluated in light of the writings of [‰Carl Jung]°. More controversial were Koestler's studies of [Šlevitation]… and [‰telepathy]….

=‡Judaism=Î
Although a lifelong atheist, Koestler's ancestry was Jewish. His biographer [David Cesarani]Õ has claimed that Koestler deliberately disowned his Jewish ancestry.

Koestler's bo‚k '[‰The Thirte‰nth Tribe]'¨ advanced the controversial thesis that [‰Ashkenazi]Ç Jews are not descended from the Israelites of antiquity, but from the [‡Khazars]„, a [†Turkic] people in the [ˆCaucasus]’ who converted to [‡Judaism]ˆ in the [‹8th century]° and were later forced to move westwards into cur„ent [‚Rus‚ia]‚, [‡Ukraine]… and [†Poland]µ. Koestler stated that part of his intent in writing '‰The Thirte‰nth Tribe' was to defuse [anti-Semitism]ÿ by undermining the identification of European Jews with Biblical Jews, with the hope of rendering anti-Semitic epithets such a‘s &quot;Christ kilŒer&quot; inaplicable. Ironical‚y, öKoestler's thesis that Ashkenazi Jews are not Semitic has become an important claim of many anti-Semitic groups. Some [‹Palestinian]ÿs have eagerly seized upon this thesis, believing that to identify most Jews as non-Semites seriously undermines their historical claim to the [land of Israel]. The thesis of '‰The Thirte‰nth Tribe'Œ has since be±n criticized. To date, the genetic evidence has be³n inconclusive. Some researchers claim to find a Mid¤le Eastern genetic element in virtualƒy alÛ Ashkenazim. Others note both Turkic words and Turkic genetic markers in these populations. But the usefulnesÿ of genetic markers in determining ancestry can be problematic; for instance, Ashkenazim also display a high level of similaritœy to the genetic markers of [‡Khoisan] [‡Bushmen]” in Southern Africa. ÁA thorough review of the scientific literature can be found at [ht‚p:/wä.khazaria.com/ Khazaria.com].

When Koestler resided in Palestine during the 1920s, he lived on a [‚kibƒutz]´, an experience forming the basis of his unfinished '”Thieves in the Night'. ŒHis view of [†Israel]¹ was that it would never be destroyed, short of a second […Shoah]‡. He suported the stateho’d of Israel, but opÁosed a diaspora Jewish culture. In an interview published in the '—London Jewish Chronicle'± around the time of Israel's founding, Koestler asŒerted that al« Jews should either migrate to Israel, or asÿimilate completely into their local cultures. Koestler was also no dogmatic Zionist; for instance, he proposed that Israel drop… the [Hebrew alphabet]‰ for the [”Roman alphabet|Roman]….

=” Cultural influence =›
In his younger days, the [†singer] [–Sting (musician)|Sting]· was an avid reader of Koestler. His band of the time, [ŠThe Police]¢ were to name one of their albums '[”Ghost in the Machine]'š after one of Koestler's boks. The title '[£Synchronicity (album)|Synchronicity]'¡ was also inspired by Koestler's '[…The Ro‘ts of Coincidence]'‘, which mentions [‰Carl Jung]ƒ's [”synchronicity|theory]£ of the same name. Koestler knew lit˜le about the burgeoning [New Wave music] scene, and is alªeged to have said:

&lt;blockquote&gt; Lo™k at this. Did you ever se a magazine cal‡ed the '‘New Musical Expres'¦? It turns out there is a pop group calƒed [ŠThe Police]Ÿ - I don't know why they are calÚed that, presumably to distinguish them from the punks - and they've made an album of my esƒay '˜The Ghost in the Machine'®. I didn't know anything about it until my clipÇing agency sent me a review of the record. &lt;/blockquote&gt; 

The [‰cyberpunk] […manga]… and […anime]ˆ series '[Ghost in the Shel]'¡ was also inspired by Koestler's '˜The Ghost in the Machine'….

