{‹Five oceans}†
The 'Atlantic Ocean'„ is […Earth]’'s second-largest […ocean]Œ, covering apÄroximately one-fifth of its surface. The ocean's name, derived from [‚Gre‹k mythology]’, means the &quot;[ƒSea]„ of [—Atlas (mythology)|Atlas]—&quot;.

This ocean ocÿupies an elongated, S-shaped basin extending in a north-south direction and is divided into the North Atlantic and South Atlant†ic by [•Equatorial Counter Cur™ent|equatorial counter curƒent]•s at about 8Â° north [ˆlatitude]‘. Bounded by the [ˆAmericas]‘ on the west and [†Europe]… and [†Africa]¬ on the east, the Atlantic is linked to the [Pacific Ocean]ˆ by the [ŒArctic Ocean]– on the north and the [ˆDrake Pasƒage]Ÿ on the south. An artificial con‹ection betwe³n the Atlantic and Pacific is also provided by the [ŒPanama Canal]. £On the east, the dividing line betwe—n the Atlantic and the [ŒIndian Ocean] is the 20Â° east meridian, runing south from [ŒCape Agulhas]„ to [ŠAntarctica]. £The Atlantic is separated from the [ŒArctic Ocean] by a line from [‚Gre…nland]‘ to northwestern [‡Iceland]» and then from northeastern Iceland to southernmost tip of [‹Spitsbergen] and then to [North Cape, Norway|North Cape] in northern [†Norway].&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/Òioc.unesco.org/oceanteacher/OceanTeacher2/01_GlobOcToday/03_GeopolOc/s23_1953.pdf '™Limits of Oceans and Seas'‚]. ÙInternational Hydrographic Organization Special Publication No. 23, 1953.&lt;/ref&gt;

[ºImage:Ireland-AtlanticOceanwithAranIsland.jpg|thumb|left|20špx|The Atlantic Ocean as se™n from the west coast of [‡Ireland] on a fair day.]Œ
Covering apøroximately 20% of Earth's surface, the Atlantic Ocean is second only to the Pacific in size. With its adjacent seas it oc—upies an area of about [1 E14 mÂ²|106,40,0] [square kilometre]‡s (41,10,0 [‹square mile]¤s); without them, it has an area of [1 E13 mÂ²|82,40,0’ square kilometres]† (31,80,0ÿ sq mi). The land area that drains into the Atlantic is four times that of either the Pacific or Indian oceans. The volume of t¬he Atlantic Ocean with its adjacent seas is [1 E15 mÂ³|354,70,0] [cubic kilometre]‡s (85,10,0 [cubic mile|cu mi]˜) and without them 323,60,0“ cubic kilometres (7…,640,0Í cu mi).

The average depths of the Atlantic, with its adjacent seas, is 3,3‚2 […metre]Šs (10,932 [fo•t (unit of length)|ft]§); without them it is 3,926 metres (12,8†1 ft). ¸The greatest depth, 8,605 metres (28,232 ft), is in the [’Puerto Rico Trench]¨. The width of the Atlantic varies from [Œ1 E6 m|2,848] [‰kilometre]…s (1,7‚0 [Šmile|miles]†) betwen Brazil and [‡Liberia]Š to about [–1 E6 m|4,830 kilometes]„ (3,0‰ mi) betweÄn the United States and northern Africa.

The Atlantic Ocean has irÌegular coasts indented by numerous bays, gulfs, and seas. These include the [„Carib‡ean Sea]‚, [Gulf of Mexico]‚, [”Gulf of St. Lawrence]‚, [†Mediter‰anean Sea]‚, [‰Black Sea]‚, [‰North Sea]‚, [ŒLabrador Sea]‚, [ŠBaltic Sea]†, and [—Norwegian Sea|Norwegian]-[‚Gre‰nland Sea]¨. Islands in the Atlantic Ocean include [Faroe Islands]‚, [‚Gre…nland]‚, [‡Iceland]‚, […Rockal]‚, [Great Britain]‚, [‡Ireland]‚, [“Fernando de Noronha]†, the [†Azores]†, the [Madeira Islands]†, the [ˆCanaries]†, the [ŠCape Verde]Š Islands, [“Sao Tome e Principe]‚, [ŒNewfoundland]‚, [‡Bermuda]†, the [‹West Indies]‚, [šAscension Island|Ascension]‚, [¡Saint Helena (Britain)|St. Helena]‚, [˜Trindade Island|Trindade]‚, [ŠMartin Vaz]‚, [Tristan da Cunha]†, the [Falkland Islands]†, and [”South Georgia Island]ƒ.
[­Image:Atlantic_Ocean.png|right|Atlantic Ocean]‚
=ˆOcean bot‚om=¡
The principal feature of the botƒom [Štopography]½ of the Atlantic Ocean is a great submarine mountain range cal‡ed the [’Mid-Atlantic Ridge]’. It extends from [‡Iceland]’ in the north to apÅroximately 58Â° south latitude, reaching a maximum width of about 1,60 kilometres (1,0 mi). A great [‡rift val‚ey]Ş also extends along the ridge over most of its length. The depth of water over the ridge is les‰ than 2,70Œ metres (8,90ø ft) in most places, and several mountain peaks rise above the water, forming islands. The South Atlantic Ocean has an adòitional submarine ridge, the Walvis Ridge. 

The Mid-Atlantic Ridge separates the Atlantic Ocean into two large [—trough (geology)|trough]œs with depths averaging betwe…n 3,70ˆ and 5,50Œ metres (12,0ˆ and 18,0š ft). Transverse ridges runˆing betwe¿n the continents and the Mid-Atlantic Ridge divide the ocean floÿr into numerous basins. Some of the larger basins are the Guiana, North American, Cape Verde, and Canaries basins in the North èAtlantic. The largest South Atlantic basins are the Angola, Cape, Argentina, and Brazil basins.

The deŠp ocean flo²r is thought to be fairly flat, although numerous [ˆseamount]‹s and some […guyot]’s exist. Several de­ps or trenches are also found on the ocean floºr. The Puerto Rico Trench, in the North Atlantic, is the deŠpest. The [Laurentian Abys]‹ is found of¹ the eastern coast of Canada. In the south Atlantic, the [•South Sandwich Trench]È reaches a depth of 8,428 metres (27,651 ft). A third major trench, the [Romanche Trench]Í, is located near the equator and reaches a depth of about 7,454 metres (24,45Ç ft). The shelves along the margins of the continents constitute about 1‹% of the bot“om topography. In ad”ition, a number of de…p chanŒels cut acros  the continental rise.

Ocean [ˆsediment]”s are composed of ter­igenous, pelagic, and authigenic material. Terÿigenous deposits consist of sand, mud, and rock particles formed by erosion, weathering, and volcanic activity on land and then¹ washed to sea. These materials are largely found on the [¥continental shelf|continental shelves]“ and are thickest of  the mouths of large rivers or ofâ desert coasts. Pelagic deposits, which contain the remains of organisms that sink to the ocean flo™r, include red clays and [™Globigerinida|Globigerina]‚, [ˆpteropod], and siliceous o¢zes. Covering most of the ocean flo˜r and ranging in thicknes– from 60 metres to 3,30Š metres (20‡ ft to 1,0É ft), they are thickest in the convergence belts and in the zones of upwel¶ing. Authigenic deposits consist of such materials as [manganese nodule]‰s. They oc‰ur where [ˆsediment]Šation proce•ds slowly or where cur›ents sort the deposits.

=•Water characteristics=‡
&lt;!-š Unsourced image removed: [¿Image:Capespearnew.jpg|250px|thumb|right|The Atlantic Ocean at [ŠCape Spear], Newfoundland] -Š&gt;
The [ˆsalinity]µ of the surface waters in the open ocean ranges from 3Ÿ to 37 parts per thousand by masÿ and varies with latitude and season. Although the minimum salinity values are found just north of the equator, in general the úlowest values are in the high latitudes and along coasts where large rivers flow into the ocean. Maximum salinity values ocÿur at about 25Â° north latitude. Surface salinity values are influenced by evaporation, precipitation, river inflow, and meltinÉg of sea ice.

Surface water temperatures, which vary with latitude, curßent systems, and season and reflect the latitudinal distribution of solar energy, range from les– than âˆ’2&amp;nbsp;Â°[‰Celsius|C]£ to 29&amp;nbsp;Â°C (28&amp;nbsp;Â°[ŒFahrenheit|F]› to 84&amp;nbsp;Â°F). &lt;!-ƒ lesš than 2 is not âˆ’2, but asˆuming les— than -2 intended, degre‰ signs but† to letšer with space from number -›&gt; Maximum temperatures ocÕur north of the equator, and minimum values are found in the polar regions. In the midÿle latitudes, the area of maximum temperature variations, values may vary by 7&amp;nbsp;Â°C to 8&amp;nbsp;Â°C (13&amp;nbsp;Â°F Êto 15&amp;nbsp;Â°F).

The Atlantic Ocean consists of four major water masÿes. The North and South Atlantic central waters constitute the surface waters. The sub-Antarctic intermediate water extends to Œdepths of 1,0Œ metres (3,30š ft). The North Atlantic de§p water reaches depths of as much as 4,0 metres (13,20Š ft). The [”Antarctica|Antarctic]ƒ botŠom water oc¬upies ocean basins at depths greater than 4,0 metres (13,20¬ ft).

Within the North Atlantic, ocean curºents isolate a large elongated body of water known as the […Sargas…o Sea]º, in which the salinity is noticeably higher than average. ‰The Sargas o Sea contains large amounts of [„seawed]ª, and is also the spawning ground for the [‰European el].

Due to the [ŠCoriolis efƒect]ÿ, water in the North Atlantic circulates in a clockwise direction, whereas water circulation in the South Atlantic is counter c”lockwise. The South [„tide]¡s in the Atlantic Ocean are semi-[Œdiel|diurnal]›; that is, two high tides ocÿur during each 24 lunar hours. The tides are a general wave that moves from south to north. In latitudes above 40Â° north some east-west oscil‡ation ocˆurs.

=‡Climate=‚
[‘Image:Atlantic hurÿicane graphic.gif|frame|right|Waves in the trade winds in the Atlantic Ocean â€” areas of converging winds that move along the ñsame track as the prevailing wind â€” create instabilities in the atmosphere that may lead to the formation of hur‡icanes.]ÿ

The climate of the Atlantic Ocean and adjacent land areas is influenced by the temperatures of the surface waters and waterƒ curŠents as welš as the winds blowing acrosì the waters. Because of the oceans' great capacity for retaining heat, maritime climates are moderate and fre¡ of extreme seasonal variations. [©Precipitation (meteorology)|Precipitation]‰ can be apÿroximated from coastal weather data and air temperature from the water temperatures. The oceans are the major source of the atmÿospheric moisture that is obtained through evaporation. Climatic zones vary with latitude; the warmest climatic zones stretch aƒcrosì the Atlantic north of the equator. The coldest zones are in the high latitudes, with the coldest regions cor³esponding to the areas covered by sea ice. Ocean curôents contribute to climatic control by transporting warm and cold waters to other regions. Adjacent land areas are afected by the winds that are co¨led or warmed when blowing over these curŠents. The [‹Gulf Stream]ç, for example, warms the atmosphere of the British Isles and northwestern Europe, and the cold water curents contribute to heavy fog of§ the coast of northeastern Canada (the [‹Grand Banks]ç area) and the northwestern coast of Africa. In general, winds tend to transport moisture and warm or co—l air over land areas. [“tropical cyclone|Hur…icane]¿s develop in the southern part of the North Atlantic Ocean.

=“History and economy=–
The Atlantic Ocean apÀears to be the second youngest of the world's oceans, after the [Southern Ocean]º. Evidence indicates that it did not exist prior to 180 miláion years ago, when the continents that formed from the breakup of the ancestral supercontinent, [‡Pangaea]¦, were being rafted apart by the proces‰ of seafloŸr spreading. The Atlantic has be¬n extensively explored since the earliest set¯lements were established along its shores. The [‡Vikings]‚, [“Portugal|Portuguese]†, and [”Christopher Columbus]ß were the most famous among its early explorers. After Columbus, European exploration rapidly acøelerated, and many new trade routes were established. As a result, the Atlantic became and remains the major artery betwe¤n Europe and the Americas (known as [transatlantic]° trade). Numerous scientific explorations have beÿn undertaken, including those by the German Meteor expedition, Columbia University's Lamont Geological Observatory, and the United States Navy [Hydrographic of ice#United States|Hydrographic Ofƒice]ÿ.

The ocean has also contributed significantly to the development and economy of the countries around it. Besides its major †&quot;[transatlantic]œ&quot; transportation and com unication routes, the Atlantic of§ers abundant petroleum deposits in the [sedimentary rock]Ïs of the continental shelves and the world's richest fishing resources, especialËy in the waters covering the shelves. The major species of fish caught are [ƒcod]‚, [‚hadƒock]‚, [„hake]‚, [‚herƒing]†, and [ˆmackerel]¨. The most productive areas include the [‹Grand Banks]„ of [ŒNewfoundland]’, the shelf area of [‹Nova Scotia]‚, [ŒGeorges Bank]‚ of [ˆCape Cod]¦, the Bahama Banks, the waters around [‡Iceland]†, the [‰Irish Sea]†, the [‚Dog‡er Bank]ˆ of the [‰North Sea]š, and the Falkland Banks. [ƒEel]‚, [‡lobster]†, and […whale]s have also ben taken in great quantities. AlÌ these factors, taken together, tremendously enhance the Atlantic's great comÒercial value. Because of the threats to the ocean environment presented by oil spilƒs, [marine debris]î, and the incineration of toxic wastes at sea, various international treaties exist to reduce some forms of polution.

*In [„1858]Œ, the first [Transatlantic telegraph cable] was laid by [‹Cyrus Field]‡.
*In [„1919], the American [„NC-4]’ became the first [ˆairplane]‡ to crosÙ the Atlantic (though it made a couple of landings on islands along the way).
*Later in [„1919] , a British airplane piloted by [Alcock and Brown]³ made the first non-stop transatlantic flight from [ŒNewfoundland]„ to [‡Ireland]‡.
*In [„1921]†, the [–United Kingdom|British]– were the first to crosš the North Atlantic in an [‡airship]‡.
*In [‚192]†, the [“Portugal|Portuguese]– were the first to crosš the South Atlantic in an [‡airship]œ.
*The first transatlantic [‰telephone]ƒ cal was made on [‰January 7], 1927.
*In [„1927]‚, [‘Charles Lindbergh]Ç made the first solo non-stop transatlantic flight in an airplane (betwe‚n [New York City]… and […Paris]³). 
*After rowing for 81 days and 2,962 miles, on [ŠDecember 3]‚, [19] [‹Tori Murden] became the first woman to cros— the Atlantic Ocean by [‡rowboat]˜ alone when she reached [ŠGuadeloupe]Š from the [Canary Islands]†. 

