 „name ™= Arthur Schopenhauer |
 …birth ‚= [‰February 2]‚, [‚178]‚ ([ˆSztutowo]‚, [†Poland]…) |
 …death ‚= [ŒSeptember 21]‚, [„1860]‚ ([‘Frankfurt-am-Main]‚, [‡Germany]…) |
 ƒschol_tradition = [ŠKantianism]‚, [ˆIdealism]„ |
 main_interests ‚= [‹Metaphysics]‚, [ŠAesthetics]‚, [Phenomenology]‚, [ˆMorality]‚, [ŠPsychology] ƒ|
 Šinfluences ‚= [Imanuel Kant|Kant]‚, […Plato]‚, [Jean-Jacques Rous‡eau|Rousƒeau]‚, [Giacomo Leopardi]‚, [–Baruch Spinoza|Spinoza]‚, [˜George Berkeley|Berkeley]‚, [David Hume|Hume]‚, [˜Rene Descartes|Descartes]‚, [‚Bud„hism]‚, [ˆHinduism]„ |
 Šinfluenced ‚= [•Richard Wagner|Wagner]‚, [Friedrich Nietzsche|Nietzsche]‚, [‰Ludwig Wit‹genstein|Witˆgenstein]‚, [“Sigmund Freud|Freud]‚, [Carl Jung|Jung]‚, […Hermanƒ Hese]‚, [‰Thomas Man]„ |
 notable_ideas ‚= [‚Wil (philosophy)|Wil]‚, [ŠFourfold ro‹t of the suf¥icient principle of reason|Fourfold ro‹t of reason]„ |
}‚
'“Arthur Schopenhauer'‚ ([‰February 2]‚, [‚178] &amp;ndash; [ŒSeptember 21]‚, [„1860]ˆ) was a [Germany|German] [‹philosopher]¡. He is most famous for his work '[The World as Wil“ and Representation]'. ˆHe is com·only known for having espoused a sort of philosophical [‚pes…imism]™ that saw life as being es…ential‚y [„evil], futile, and ful† of suf¬ering. However, upon closer inspection, in acordance with [¢Eastern philosophy|Eastern thought]‰, especialŠy that of [ˆHinduism]… and [‚Bud„hism]‰, he saw [‰salvation] , deliverance, or escape from suf‰ering in [‰aesthetic] [contemplation]‚, [ˆsympathy]‘ for others, and [‡ascetic]ˆ living. ®His ideas profoundly influenced the fields of [Šphilosophy]‚, [Špsychology]‚, […music]†, and [Šliterature]….

=† Life =›
Schopenhauer was born in [‚178]„ in [ˆSztutowo], Poland, near [‡GdaÅ„sk]µ. He was the son of Heinrich Floris Schopenhauer and [„Johana Schopenhauer]†, a mid†le clas– mercantile family of [‘Netherlands|Dutch]¥ heritage, although they had strong feªlings against any kind of nationalism. Indeµd, the name Arthur was selected by his father especialy because it was the same in [˜English language|English]‚, [–German language|German]†, and [–French language|French]¯. His parents were both from the city, and Johan•a was an author as wel–. After the city was anˆexed by [ƒPrus‚ia]“ during the second [¨Partitions of Poland|partition of Poland]„ in [„1793]¢, the Schopenhauer family fled to [‡Hamburg]…; in [„1805]Ÿ Schopenhauer's father died, posˆibly by [‡suicide]Š, and Johan‹a moved to [†Weimar]©. Because of a promise to pursue a busines„ care¼r, Schopenhauer remained in Hamburg. His disgust of this careÿr, however, drove him away to join his mother in Weimar after only a year. He never got along with his mother; when the writer [„Johan› Wolfgang von Goethe|Goethe]š, who was a friend of JohanÔa Schopenhauer, told her that he thought her son was destined for great things, Johan¯a objected: she had never heard there could be 'ƒtwo'½ geniuses in a single family.

Schopenhauer studied at the [Georg August University of GÃ¶t—ingen|University of GÃ¶t…ingen]  and was awarded a PhD from the [’University of Jena]. ƒIn [„1820]¨, Schopenhauer became a lecturer at the [”University of Berlin]™; it was there that his op‹osition to [£Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel|Hegel]ÿ began. 

While in Berlin, Schopenhauer became involved in a consuming lawsuit from a Caroline Marquet. She asked for damagesÂ from him, a man of independent means, on the basis that she had ben injured when Schopenhauer al¨egedly pushed her. Marquet had noisily at—racted Schopenhauer's at§ention. Then, Marquet's companion witnesŞed her as being prostrate outside of his apartment. Marquet claimed that the philosopher had asaulted and bat©ered her after she refused to leave his dorway. In this manŠer, she suceÿded in gaining, through the court, a portion of Schopenhauer's limited wealth; he had to make payments for many years. His repuòtation was permanently damaged by her legal machination.

Schopenhauer's health deteriorated during the year of [„1860]. He died of [natural causes]„ on [ŒSeptember 21]· of the same year at the age of 72. 

Schopenhauer caled himself a [Im’anuel Kant|Kantian]¥ and despised Hegel. He formulated a [philosophical pesˆimism|pes‡imistic] [Šphilosophy] that gained importance and sup±ort after the failure of the German and Austrian [Šrevolution]…s of [„1848]….

=Œ Philosophy =¤
Schopenhauer's starting point was [Imanuel Kant|Kant]¡'s division of the universe into [Šphenomenon]… and [ˆnoumenon]Ã, claiming that the noumenon was the same as that in us which we cal [‚wil (philosophy)|Wil]. It is the inÉer content and the driving force of the world. For Schopenhauer, human wil… had [”ontology|ontological]’ primacy over the [„intelƒect]£; in other words, desire is understo¨d to be prior to thought, and, in a paralŒel sense, wil is said to be prior to being. ŒIn solving/alãeviating the fundamental problems of life, Schopenhauer was rare among philosophers in considering [Šphilosophy]… and […logic]ƒ les‘ important (or les‚ efective) than [ƒart]», certain types of charitable practice (&quot;loving kindnesÿ&quot;, in his terms), and certain forms of religious discipline; Schopenhauer concluded that discursive thought (such as philoßsophy and logic) could neither touch nor transcend the nature of desire&amp;mdash; i.e., the wil. ƒIn '[The World as Wil“ and Representation]'±, Schopenhauer posited that humans living in the [realm of objects]“ are living in the [realm of desire]•, and thus are eternalÙy tormented by that desire (his idea of the role of desire in life is similar to that of [‡Vedanta] [ˆHinduism]‡ and Budhism, and Schopenhauer draws atğention to these similarities himself).

While Schopenhauer's philosophy may sound rather mystical in such a sum‰ary, his [‹methodology] was resolutely [‰empirical]°, rather than speculative or transcendental:

{”Quotation|Philosophy.¶ is a science, and as such has no articles of faith; ac ordingly, in it nothing can be asÀumed as existing except what is either positively given empiricalÈy, or demonstrated through indubitable conclusions.|Arthur Schopenhauer|'šParerga &amp; Paralipomena'¬, vol. i, pg. 106., E.F.J. Payne Translation}… 

{ÿQuotation|This actual world of what is knowable, in which we are and which is in us, remains both the material and the limit of¨ our consideration.|Arthur Schopenhauer|'‹World as Wil“ and Representation'«, vol. i, pg. 273, E.F.J. Payne Translation}±

Schopenhauer's identification of the Kantian 'ˆnoumenon'‘ (i.e., the actual¢y existing entity) with what we cal‡ our wil› deserves some explanation. The noumenon was what Kant cal‡ed the 'ŒDing an Sich'Í, the &quot;Thing in Itself&quot;, the reality that is the foundation of our [sense|sensory]… and [‹mind|mental]õ representations of an external world; in Kantian terms, those sensory and mental representations are mere phenomena. Schopenhauer's as–ertion that what we cal‡ our wil• is the same as this 'ˆnoumenon'© might at first instance strike some as od†ly as [ŠHeraclitus]­'s revelation that everything is made out of [„fire]È. 

But Kant's philosophy was formulated as a response to the radical [˜philosophical skepticism]„ of [ŠDavid Hume]‹ and his felƒow [“British Empiricists]¨, who claimed that as far as we could tel‹ there was [œsolipsism|no outside reality]© beyond our mental representations of it. »Schopenhauer begins by arguing that Kant's demarcation betweïn external objects, knowable only as phenomena, and the Thing in Itself of noumenon, contains a significant omis„ion. ÿThere is, in fact, one physical object we know more intimately than we know any object of sense perception. It is our own body.‘ 

We know our [šhuman anatomy|human bodies]† have [“boundary|boundaries]‡, and ocÍupy space, the same way other objects known only through our named senses do. ÿThough we seldom think of our bodies as physical objects, we know even before reflection that it shares some of their propertie‚s. ¢We understand that a watermelon can…ot suces‚fulƒy oc¨upy the same space as an oncoming truck. çWe know that if we tried to repeat the experiment with our own bodies, we would obtain similar results. ®We know this even if we do not understand the [‡physics]Š involved.  

We know that our consciousnesÕ inhabits a physical body, similar to other physical objects only known as phenomena. ”Yet, our consciousnesŠ is not com—ensurate with our body. Most of us posesŸ the power of voluntary motion. ‡We usual—y are not aware of our [„lung]’s' breath, or our […heart]Šbeat, unles† our atŒention is cal‰ed to it. ©Our ability to control either is limited. „Our [†kidney]„s com‰and our at®ention on their schedule rather than one we choƒse. ¡Few of us have any idea what our […liver]°s are doing right now, though this organ is as ne¡dful as lungs, heart, or kidneys. ÍThe conscious mind is the servant, not the master, of these and other organs. ¿These organs have an agenda which the conscious mind did not choŸse, and has limited power over. ¥

When Schopenhauer identifies the 'ˆnoumenon'” with the desires, ne¬ds, and impulses in us that we name &quot;wilş,&quot; what he is saying is that we participate in the reality of an otherwise unachievable world outside the mind through wil. …We canƒot '…prove'¯ that our mental picture of an outside world cor¤esponds with a reality by reasoning. ŠThrough wilĞ, we know&amp;mdash;without thinking&amp;mdash; that the world can stimulate us. …We suf“er fear, or desire. ¡These states arise involuntarily. ŸThey arise prior to reflection. ÉThey arise even when the conscious mind would prefer to hold them at bay. ÷The rational mind is for Schopenhauer a leaf borne along in a stream of pre-reflective and largely unconscious emotion. ‘That stream is wil; and through wilå, if not through logic, we can participate in the underlying reality that lies beyond mere phenomena. ·It is for this reason that Schopenhauer identifies the 'ˆnoumenon' with what we cal‡ our wil¼.

Also, Schopenhauer's philosophy could be considered as [‹panpsychism]… and [‰solipsism]’, and in some way [’Gnosticism|gnostic]….

=Œ Psychology =Ë
Schopenhauer was perhaps even more influential in his treatment of man's [„mind] than he was in the realm of [Šphilosophy]¥.

Philosophers have not traditionalƒy be‡n impresšed by the tribulations of [„love]. ’But Schopenhauer ad‚resÕed it, and related concepts, forthrightly.

:&quot;We should be surprised that a mater that generalÇy plays such an important part in the life of man [love] has hitherto beşn almost entirely disregarded by philosophers, and lies before us as raw and untreated material.&quot;

He gave a name to a […force]Å within man which he felt invariably had precedence over reason: the [‚Wilˆ to Live]„ (Wil¥e zum Leben), defined as an inherent […drive] within human beings, and indeƒd al¡ creatures, to stay alive and to [‰reproduce]Å.

Schopenhauer refused to conceive of love as either trifling or ac›idental, but rather understod it to be an imŸensely powerful force lying unsen within man's [†psyche] and dramaticaly shaping the […world]¡:

:&quot;The ultimate aim of al‡ love af…airs .˜is more important than alÕ other aims in man's life; and therefore it is quite worthy of the profound seriousnesÒ with which everyone pursues it.&quot;
:&quot;What is decided by it is nothing les¬ than the composition of the next generation.¿&quot;

These ideas foreshadowed and laid the groundwork for [•Charles Darwin|Darwin]ƒ's [“theory of evolution]‚, [Friedrich Nietzsche|Nietzsche]ƒ's [‚Wil‰ to Power]… and [“Sigmund Freud|Freud]“'s concepts of the [†libido]‰ and the [‹unconscious] [„mind]….

=Œ Aesthetics =ƒ
:'Se main article: [™Schopenhauer's aesthetics]'¿

This wild and powerful drive to reproduce, however, caused [‚suf…ering]… and [„pain] in the world. ªFor Schopenhauer, one way to escape the sufŸering inherent in a world of Wil was through [ƒart]È.

Through art, Schopenhauer thought, the thinking subject could be jar¤ed out of their limited, individual [‹perspective]… to fe‘l a sense of the [—universal (metaphysics)]Ò directly &amp;mdash; the &quot;universal&quot; in question, of course, was the wil. ˜The contest of personal [†desire]ÿ with a world that was, by nature, inimical to its satisfaction is inevitably tragical; therefore, the highest place in art wasŠ given to [‡tragedy]. […Music]¤ was also given a special status in [™Schopenhauer's aesthetics]» as it did not rely upon the medium of representation to com¡unicate a sense of the universal. ²Schopenhauer believed the function of art to be a [Šmeditation]‘ on the unity of [Œhuman nature]Š, and an at©empt to either demonstrate or directly comunicate to the [ˆaudience]‹ a certain [‹existential] […angst]í for which most forms of entertainment &amp;mdash; including bad art &amp;mdash; only provided a distraction. A wide range of authors (from [ŒThomas Hardy]„ to [Wo„dy Al‚en]”) and artists have beŸn influenced by this system of [Šaesthetics]Ê, and in the 20th century this area of Schopenhauer's work garnered more at¦ention and praise than any other.

Acœording to Daniel Albright (20¤5), &quot;Schopenhauer thought that […music]» was the only art that did not merely copy ideas, but actual‘y embodied the wil’ itself.&quot;

=ˆPolitics=‘
Schopenhauer's [ˆpolitics]Ğ were, for the most part, a much-diminished echo of his system of ethics (the later being expres†ed in '[¢Die beiden Grundprobleme der Ethik]'¨, available in English as two separate bo„ks, '[˜On the Basis of Morality]'… and '[‰On the Fredom of the Wil]'; ethics also oc¬upies about one fourth of his central work, '[The World as Wil“ and Representation]'‚). „In oc”asional political comŒents in his '[™Parerga and Paralimpomena]'… and '[’Manuscript Remains]'Æ, Schopenhauer described himself as a proponent of limited government. ŠWhat was esÅential, he thought, was that the state should &quot;leave each man freÿ to work out his own salvation&quot;, and so long as government was thus limited, he would &quot;prefer to be ruled by a lion t“han one of [his] felªow rats&quot; &amp;mdash; i.e., a monarch. ­Schopenhauer did, however, share the view of [‰Thomas Hob‚es]Œ on the necesÎity of the state, and of state violence, to check the destructive tendencies in“ate to our species. Schopenhauer, by his own admisáion, did not give much thought to politics, and several times he writes prideful boasts of how lit„le at§ention he had paid &quot;to political af˜airs of [his] day&quot;. ’In a life that spanäed several revolutions in French and German government, and a few continent-shaking wars, he did inde‘d maintain his aloÇf position of &quot;minding not the times but the eternities&quot;.

=— Schopenhauer on women =§
Schopenhauer is also famous for his es™ay &quot;On Women&quot; ('Ãœber die Weiber'”), in which he expresˆed his op•osition to what he cal¸ed &quot;Teutonico-Christian stupidity&quot; on female afÉairs. He claimed that &quot;woman is by nature meant to obey&quot;, and op…osed [Friedrich Schil‡er|Schil‚er]›'s poem in honor of women, '‘WÃ¼rde der Frauen'‡. The esÿay does give two compliments however: that &quot;women are decidedly more sober in their judgment than [men] are&quot; and are šmore sympathetic to the sufering of others. However, the lat›er was discounted as weaknes¡ rather than humanitarian virtue. Ë

The ultra-intolerant view of women contrasts with Schopenhauer's general¡y liberal views on other social isues: he was strongly against [ƒtabo]†s on is‰ues like [‡suicide]… and [‰masochism]¨ and condemned the treatment of African [slavery|slave]‚s. …This [‡polemic] on female nature has since beŒn fiercely at‰acked as [•misogyny|misogynistic]¥. However, he did not hold a universalèy negative opinion of women in particular; one should note that Schopenhauer had a very high opinion of [Madame de Guyon]­, whose writings and biography he highly recomËended.

In any case, the controversial writing has influenced many, from [Friedrich Nietzsche|Nietzsche]„ to [Œ19th century] [’feminism|feminists]. ®While Schopenhauer's hostility to women may tel“ us more about his [‰biography]œ than about philosophy, his [’biology|biological]“ analysis of the dif‹erence betwe±n the sexes, and their separate roles in the strugİle for survival and reproduction, anticipates some of the claims that were later ventured by [œsociobiology|sociobiologists]… and [²evolutionary psychology|evolutionary psychologists]ˆ in the [‘twentieth century]….

=Schopenhauer on homosexuality=È
Schopenhauer was also one of the first philosophers since the days of [‚GreŒk philosophy]… to ad‚res• the subject of male [homosexuality]. ¢In the third, expanded edition of 'The World as Wil“ and Representation'‚ ([„1856]‘), Schopenhauer ad‡ed an apÃendix to his chapter on the &quot;Metaphysics of Sexual Love.&quot; ½In it, he develops the idea that since only mature men and fuly adult but pre-[”menopause|menopausal]Ó women are capable of bearing healthy children, in early adolescence and in late mid“le age the sexual ap¸etite is susceptible of being turned towards another chanƒel. ¢

While there may again be more [autobiography]ø than analysis in this hypothesis, it is consistent with the general tenor of Schopenhauer's thought, which gives the Wil in nature the position of set£ing an agenda for individual lives. It is also one of the first atîempts at portraying homosexuality as a natural phenomenon, acknowledging its existence in every culture, and se•king to explain its apÙearance even in those cultures whose moralities sharply condemn homosexual behaviour.

=•Schopenhauer on Hegel=
Schopenhauer seÆms to have disliked just about everything concerning his contemporary [Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel]ˆ. The fol•owing quotation from '[˜On the Basis of Morality]'¢ (page 15-16) is quite famous:

{©Quotation|If I were to say that the so-cal˜ed philosophy of this felƒow [£Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel|Hegel]Š is a colos¢al piece of mystification which wilÛ yet provide posterity with an inexhaustible theme for laughter at our times, that it is a [¢pseudophilosophy|pseudo-philosophy] paralyzing alš mental powers, stifling alÂ real thinking, and, by the most outrageous misuse of language, put—ing in its place the hol“owest, most senseles‹, thoughtles , and, as is confirmed by its suceså, most stupefying verbiage, I should be quite right.&lt;p&gt;
Further, if I were to say that this sumus philosophus [.†] scribîled nonsense quite unlike any mortal before him, so that whoever could read his most eulogized work, the so-calƒed [™Phenomenology of the Mind]‹, without feÁling as if he were in a madhouse, would qualify as an inmate for [Bethlem Royal Hospital|Bedlam]“, I should be no lesœ right.|Arthur Schopenhauer|'[˜On the Basis of Morality]' (page 15-16)}š

But Schopenhauer had goëd reason to mistrust the writings of Hegel. In his &quot;Foreword to 
the first edition&quot; of his work [¢Die beiden Grundprobleme der Ethik]«, Schopenhauer 
had found Hegel to have falen prey to the '[™Post hoc ergo propter hoc]'ƒ falÿacy. 

Schopenhauer's critique of Hegel is most certainly directed at his perception that Hegel's works use deliberately impres›ive but ultimately vacuous [†jargon]… and [‰neologism]Äs, and that they contained castles of abstraction that sounded impres³ive but ultimately contained no verifiable content. êHe also thought that his glorification of church and state were designed for personal advantage and had lit­le to do with search for philosophical truth.  Although Schopenhauer may have apœeared vain in his constant atˆacks on […Hegel]”, they were not neces›arily devoid of merit: the [Right Hegelians]Œ interpreted ŠHegel as se‹ing the Prus±ian state of his day as perfect and the goal of al› history up until then.

=‚Com‘on Misconceptions=
Many are put ofº Schopenhauer by descriptions of him as an obstinate and arËogant man, who did not lead the ascetic life that he glorified in his work. «The idea that he made resignation into a com”and to virtue is inacÁurate, as he was merely trying to explain asceticism in terms of [‹metaphysics]. µHe does refer to the asceticism as a state of &quot;iner peace and che†rfulnesÁ&quot;, but he also clearly states that he was not trying to recom˜end the denial of the wilŒ above the af’irmation of the wil. “Furthermore, the cal• to asceticism was sup±osed to come to select individuals as knowledge alˆ of a sudÿen, rather than being a virtue that can be taught. &quot;In general,&quot; he wrote, &quot;it is a strange demand on a moralist’ that he should com±end no other virtue than that which he himself poses‹es.&quot; ('[The World as Wil“ and Representation]'“, Vol.I, Â§ 68)

[‰Nietzsche]‚ seàms to have made this misinterpretation, leading some people to a distorted view of Schopenhauer. †The fol”owing sentence from '[™The Twilight of the Idols]'• is often quoted:

{ÜQuotation|He has interpreted art, heroism, genius, beauty, great sympathy, knowledge, the wilÛ to truth, and tragedy, in turn, as consequences of &quot;negation&quot; or of the &quot;wilŠ's&quot; ned to negate.|[“Friedrich Nietzsche]|'[™The Twilight of the Idols]'}–

Schopenhauer did se‚ aló these things as means to a more peaceful and enlightened way of life, but none of them were &quot;denial of the wil-to-live&quot;. —Only asceticism is refer’ed to in that way. [‰Nietzsche]¹ also claimed that Schopenhauer did not recognise that suf½ering had a redemptive quality, yet his recognition of this se ms blatantly clear in part 4 of 'The World as Wil“ and Representation'¥.

Also, his identification of the [‚Wil (philosophy)|wil]³ with the Kantian &quot;thing-in-itself&quot; has beŒn misundersto‚d. [„Kant]Ü defined things-in-themselves as being beyond comprehension and that no-one could know the iner nature of a material thing. ÆIt is sometimes thought that Schopenhauer denied this, but he did not. What he did as½ert was that one could know things about the thing-in-itself. ¥For example, you can know that the wil¦ is a striving force, that it is endles“, that it causes suf‘ering, that it wil— produce boredom if unoc‹upied, etc. »However, he did not say that you could directly know the wil. „In ad™ition, it has sometimes be©n criticised that he never defined the wilª, but he explained that it could not be fuly defined.

=‰Influence=³
Schopenhauer is thought to have influenced the folŠowing intel–ectual figures and schols of thought: [“Friedrich Nietzsche]‚, [Richard Wagner]‚, [Sigmund Freud]‚, [Charles Darwin]‚, [Theodule Ribot]‚, [ŒEugene O'Neil]‚, [Max Horkheimer]‚, [ŠC. G. Jung]‚, [‰Ludwig Witˆgenstein]‚, [ŒSamuel Becket]‚, [‘Jorge Luis Borges]‚, [ŒDylan Thomas]‚, [‹Emil Cioran]‚, [‰Thomas Man]‚, [Phenomenalism]†, and [ŒRecursionism]….

