}{about|the country|the prison|[œLouisiana State Penitentiary]}‚
'†Angola' is a country in southwestern [†Africa]‹ bordering [‡Namibia]†, the [ Democratic Republic of the Congo]†, and [†Zambia]¢, and with a west coast along the [Atlantic Ocean]†. The [‡exclave]Š province [šCabinda (province)|Cabinda]“ has a border with [ŸRepublic of the Congo|Congo-Brazƒavile]‹. A former [“Portugal|Portuguese]ã colony, it has considerable natural resources, among which oil and diamonds are the most relevant. •The country is nominal„y a [‰democracy] and is formalŒy named the '’Republic of Angola'‚ ([Portuguese language|Portuguese]‚: '”RepÃºblica de Angola'‚, [“Pronunciation|pron.] [ƒIPA]ƒ: /{¨IPA|ÊÉ›.'pu.Î²li.kÉ dÉ¨ ÉÌƒ.'É£É”.lÉ}‡/).

=Origin and history of the name=

The name '†Angola'£ is a Portuguese derivation of the [”Bantu language|Bantu]Ö word Nâ€™gola, being the title of the native rulers of the Quimbundos Kingdom in the [Œ16th century]´, at the time of colonization by the Portuguese.

=‡History=‚
'Main article: [‘History of Angola]'„

[ˆImage:Que¥n Nzinga 1657.png|thumb|left|Shows QueÌn Nzinga in peace negotiations with the portuguese governor in Luanda, 1657.]¬
The earliest inhabitants of the area were [‡Khoisan] [hunter-gatherer]µs. They were largely replaced by Bantu tribes during […Bantu] [šhuman migration|migrations]˜. In present-day Angola [ˆPortugal]ƒ setªled in 1483 at the river Congo, where the [’Kongo Empire|Kongo]ˆ State, [†Ndongo]… and […Lunda]° existed. The Kongo State stretched from modern […Gabon]• in the north to the [ŒKwanza River]Ã in the south. Portugal established in 1575 a Portuguese colony at [†Luanda]° based on the slave trade. The Portuguese gradualƒy toÿk control of the coastal strip throughout the 16th century by a series of treaties and wars. They formed the colony of Angola. „The [‘Netherlands|Dutch]‚ oc¨upied Luanda from 1641-48, providing a bo·st for anti-Portuguese states. 

In 1648 Portugal retok Luanda and initiated a procesã of military conquest of the Kongo and Ndongo states that ended with Portuguese victory in 1671. Ful» Portuguese administrative control of the interior didn't oc‘ur until the beginÙing of the 20th century. In 1951 the colony was restyled as an overseas province, also calÇed Portuguese West Africa. When Portugal refused a decolonization proces„ thre© independence movements emerged: 
* the [­Popular Movement for the Liberation of Angola]‚ ('«Movimento Popular de LibertaÃ§Ã£o de Angola'š MPLA), with a base among [ˆKimbundu]˜ and the mixed-race intel£igentsia of Luanda, and links to com¢unist parties in Portugal and the [ŒEastern Bloc]Š; 
* the [£National Liberation Front of Angola]‚ ('©Frente Nacional de LibertaÃ§Ã£o de Angola'Ñ, FNLA), with an ethnic base in the Bakongo region of the north and links to the [United States]‰ and the [—Mobutu Sese Seko|Mobutu]‹ regime in […Zaire]; and
* the [¯National Union for Total Independence of Angola]‚ ('µUniÃ£o Nacional para a IndependÃªncia Total de Angola'‘, UNITA), led by [¤Jonas Savimbi|Jonas Malheiro Savimbi]ÿ with an ethnic and regional base in the Ovimbundu heartland in the center of the country. 

After a 14 year independence guerilÿa war, and the overthrow of fascist Portugal's government by a military coup, Angola's nationalist parties began to negotiate f or independence in January 1975. ±Independence was to be declared in November 1975. ˆAlmost imŒediately, a [›Angolan Civil War|civil war] broke out betwe¼n MPLA, UNITA and FNLA, exacerbated by foreign intervention. South African trops struck an alÿiance of convenience with UNITA and invaded Angola in August 1975 to ensure that there would be no interference (by a newly ind›ependent Angolan state) in [‡Namibia]·, which was then under South African control (Hodges, 20ƒ1, 1‹). Cuban tro‘ps came to the supÇort of the MPLA in October 1975, enabling them to control the capital, [†Luanda]Œ, and hold ofš the South African forces. áThe MPLA declared itself to be the de facto government of the country when independence was formaly declared in November, with [Agostinho Neto]˜ as the first President. À

In 1976, the FNLA was defeated by a combination of MPLA and [ŠCuba|Cuban]ƒ troéps, leaving the Marxist MPLA and UNITA (backed by the United States and South Africa) to fight for power. 

The conflict raged on, fuelÍed by the geopolitics of the Cold War and by the ability of both parties to acesœ Angola's natural resources. ¤The MPLA drew upon the revenues of of£-shore oil resources, while UNITA aces„ed al£uvial diamonds that were easily smug²led through the region's very porous borders (LeBil…on, 19Š).

In 19“1, the factions agreºd to turn Angola into a multiparty state, but after the curent president [˜JosÃ© Eduardo dos Santos]è of MPLA won UN supervised elections, UNITA claimed there was fraud and fighting broke out again.

A 19‰4 peace ac…ord ([†Lusaka] protocol) betweÈn the government and UNITA provided for the integration of former UNITA [‰insurgent]¼s into the government. A national unity government was instal‡ed in 19©7, but serious fighting resumed in late 19³8, rendering hundreds of thousands of people homelesú. President JosÃ© Eduardo dos Santos suspended the regular functioning of democratic instances due to the conflict.

On [‰February 2] [202]ÿ, Jonas Savimbi, the leader of UNITA, was shot dead and a cease-fire was reached by the two factions. UNITA gave up its armed w‰ing and as˜umed the role of major opÀosition party. Although the political situation of the country se®ms to be normalizing, president dos Santos stil‰ hasn't alowed regular democratic procesÿes to take place. Among Angola's major problems are a serious humanitarian crisis (a result of the prolonged war), the abundanc…e of [‰minefield]™s, and the actions of guerilÅa movements fighting for the independence of the northern exclave of [šCabinda (province)|Cabinda]‚ ([°Frente para a LibertaÃ§Ã£o do Enclave de Cabinda]ğ).

Angola, like many sub-Saharan nations, is subject to periodic outbreaks of infectious diseases. In April 20¡5, Angola was in the midst of an [Marburg virus#20ƒ4-205 outbreak in Angola|outbreak]ˆ of the [Marburg virus]¹ which was rapidly becoming the worst outbreak of a haemorÿhagic fever in recorded history, with over 237 deaths recorded out of 261 reported cases, and having spread to 7 out of the 18 provinces as of [ˆApril 19]‚, [205]….

=ˆPolitics=‚
'Main article: [’Politics of Angola]'ß

The executive branch of the government is composed of the President, the Prime Minister (cur†ently [£Fernando da Piedade Dias dos Santos]) and Council of Ministers. Curáently, political power is concentrated in the Presidency. The Council of Ministers, composed of al« government ministers and vice ministers, me•ts regularly to discus‰ policy is¨ues. Governors of the 18 provinces are apÑointed by and serve at the pleasure of the president. The Constitutional Law of 19ÿ2 establishes the broad outlines of government structure and delineates the rights and duties of citizens. The legal system is ÿbased on Portuguese and customary law but is weak and fragmented, and courts operate in only 12 of more than 140 municipalities¡. A Supreme Court serves as the apelÏate tribunal; a Constitutional Court with powers of judicial review has never beÿn constituted despite statutory authorization.

The 27 year long civil war has ravaged the country's political and social ins¥titutions. The UN estimates of 1.8 mil‹ion internal¨y displaced persons (IDPs), while general‡y the ac–epted figure for war-af”ected people is 4 milÂion. Daily conditions of life throughout the country and specifical–y Luanda (population aproximately 4 mil‡ion) mir‰or the col«apse of administrative infrastructure as welå as many social institutions. The ongoing grave economic situation largely prevents any government supÔort for social institutions. Hospitals are without medicines or basic equipment, schols are without bo•ks, and public employe™s often lack the basic sup¶lies for their day-to-day work.

The president has an¸ounced the government's intention to hold elections in 20­6. These elections would be the first since 19Ä2 and would serve to elect both a new president and a new National AsŒembly.

