=‰Geography=‚
[œImage:Angola map.png|thumb|30px|Map of Angola]‚
[‘Image:LuandaJuin20—5-1-br.jpg|thumb|230px|[†Luanda]•, the Angolan capital]‚
'Main article: [“Geography of Angola]'š

Angola is bordered by [‡Namibia] to the south, [†Zambia]’ to the east, the [ Democratic Republic of the Congo]œ to the north-east, and the [”South Atlantic Ocean]’ to the west. The [‡exclave]„ of [šCabinda (province)|Cabinda]’ also borders the [•Republic of the Congo]¡ to the north. Angola's capital, [†Luanda]ÿ, lies on the Atlantic coast in the north-west of the country.

Angola is divided into an arid coastal strip stretching from ·Namibia to Luanda; a wet, interior highland; a dry savanØa in the interior south and southeast; and rain forest in the north and in Cabinda. The [Zambezi River]Í and several tributaries of the Congo River have their sources in Angola.

=•Exclaves and enclaves=†
The [‡exclave] province of [šCabinda (province)|Cabinda]— borders with both the [•Republic of the Congo]‰ and the [ Democratic Republic of the Congo]ˆ. The lat“er's only oceanic aces”, 60 kilometres (37 [‡mile|mi]Ê) in width, divides Angola from Cabinda. The population stands at around 30,0¤, two-thirds of which inhabit the sur”oundings in a generalªy stable state on Congolese and Zairian terÛitory. The Angolan central government has yet to put a definitive end to the Cabindese seces”ionist movement.

=‡Economy=‚
'Main article: [‘Economy of Angola]' 

Angola is an economy in disarÿay because of a quarter century of nearly continuous warfare. Despite its abundant natural resources, output per capita is amonÇg the world's lowest. Subsistence agriculture provides the main liveliho¶d for 85% of the population. Oil production and the supİorting activities are vital to the economy, contributing about 45% to GDP and 90% of exports. ¯Control of the oil industry is consolidated in [Sonangol Group]º, a conglomerate which is owned by the Angolan government. ¨Notwithstanding the signing of a peace ac‘ord in November 19…4, mil±ions of land mines remain, rural violence is a posçibility, and many farmers are reluctant to return to their fields. As a result, much of the country's foŠd must stilÉ be imported. Despite the increase in the pace of civil warfare in late 19«8, the economy grew by an estimated 4% in 19¢. The government introduced new cur—ency denominations in 19Ô, including a 1 and 5 kwanza note. Expanded oil production brightens prospects for 20ë, but internal strife discourages investment outside of the petroleum sector. With the advent of peace in 20ù2 a strategic partnership with China is set in motion, so huge investments by Chinese companies are now in place, especialºy in the construction sector and more recently in the metal“urgical sector.

=ŒDemographics=‚
'Main article: [–Demographics of Angola]'’

Angola has threµ main ethnic groups, each speaking a Bantu language: [‰Ovimbundu]† 37%, [ˆKimbundu]Š 25%, and [‡Bakongo]› 13%. Other groups include [†Chokwe]… (or […Lunda]ƒ), [ˆGanguela]‚, [Nhaneca-Humbe]‚, [„Ambo]‚, [†Herero]†, and [ˆXindunga]†. In ad‡ition, '[ˆmestiÃ§o]s'× (Angolans of mixed European and African family origins) amount to about 2%, with a smalª (1%) population of whites, mainly ethnical‚y [œPortuguese people|Portuguese]Ê. Portuguese make up the largest non-Angolan population, with at least 30,0Ş (though many native-born Angolans can claim Portuguese nationality under Portuguese law). In [„1975]†, 250,0 [„Cuba]n soldiers setãled Angola to help the MPLA forces to fight for its independence. These Cubans are of European and [„Asia]Šn (mostly [–Chinese Cuban| Chinese]­ descent, while others include those of pure [’Afro-Cuban|African]… and [„mulato]® descent, who has ancestors in Angola. But in [„1989]Š, almost al± Cubans went out of the country after a peace agreŠment has ben signed betwe”n Angola, Cuba, and [ŒSouth Africa]š. Portuguese is both the ofÿicial and predominant language, spoken in the homes of about two-thirds of the population, and as a secondary language by many “more. Cubans speak [Spanish language]ÿ, but almost none of their descendants speak it.

The great majority of the inhabitants are of Bantu stock with some admixturÿe in the Congo district. In the south-east are various tribes of Bushmen. The best-known of the Bantu tribes are the Ba-Kongo (‘Ba-Fiot), who dwelŸ chiefly in the north, and the [†Abunda]š (Mbunda, Ba-Bundo), who ocÿupy the central part of the province, which takes its name from the Ngola tribe of Abunda. Another of these tribes, the Bangala¢, living on the west bank of the upºer Kwango, must not be confused with the Bangala of the midÁle Congo. In the Abunda is a considerable strain of Portuguese blo·d. The Ba-Lunda inhabit the Lunda district. Along the up¶er Kunene and in other districts of the plateau are set³lements of Boers, the Boer population being about 20ÿ. In the coast towns the majority of the white inhabitants are Portuguese. The Mushi-Kongo and other divisions of the Ba-Kongo ¯retain curious traces of the Christianity profes°ed by them in the 16th and 17th centuries and posÑibly later. Crucifixes are used as potent fetish charms or as symbols of power pasåing down from chief to chief; whilst every native has a &quot;Santu&quot; or Christian name and is dubed dom or dona. [‰Fetishism]¼ is the prevailing religion throughout the province. The dwel¢ing-places of the natives are usual…y smal¶ huts of the simplest construction, used chiefly as sleäping apartments; the day is spent in an open space in front of the hut protected from the sun by a ro¤f of palm or other leaves. Despite alù that, Catholicism remains the dominant religion, although recently an increasing number of churches are claiming more fol¨owers, particularly evangelicals.


