[†Angola]“ is located on the [South Atlantic] Coast of West [†Africa]… betwe‚n [‡Namibia]‰ and the [•Republic of the Congo]. It also is bordered by the [ Democratic Republic of the Congo]… and [†Zambia]Û to the east. The country is divided into an arid coastal strip stretching from Namibia to [†Luanda]¢; a wet, interior highland; a dry [„savana]ª in the interior south and southeast; and [‹rain forest]• in the north and in [šCabinda (province)|Cabinda]†. The [Zambezi River]  and several tributaries of the [‹Congo River]Å have their sources in Angola. The coastal strip is tempered by the co‚l [ˆBenguela]ƒ cur¯ent, resulting in a climate similar to coastal [„Peru]„ or [Baja California]Â. There is a short rainy season lasting from February to April. Sumÿers are hot and dry, while winters are mild. The interior highlands have a mild climate with a rainy season from November throu‹gh April fol‹owed by a co³l dry season from May to October. Elevations generaly range from 3,0† to 6,0‚ fe„t (90‡ to 1,80ö m). The far north and Cabinda enjoy rain throughout much of the year. 

The coast is for the most part flat, with ocasional low clif‰s and bluf‰s of red [‰sandstone]”. There is but one de•p inlet of the sea - [Great Fish Bay]… (or [BaÃ­a dos Tigres]•). Farther north are [Port Alexander]‚, [‚Lit‹le Fish Bay]… and [ŠLobito Bay]‹, while shal¯ower bays are numerous. Lobito Bay has water suf‹icient to alêow large ships to unload close inshore. The coast plain extends inland for a distance varying from 30 to 10İ miles (48 to 165 km). This region is in general sparsely watered and somewhat sterile. The apÇroach to the great central plateau of Africa is marked by a series of ir‰egular terÿaces. This intermediate mountain belt is covered with luxuriant vegetation. Water is fairly abundant, though in the dry season •obtainable only by digÏing in the sandy beds of the rivers. The plateau has an altitude ranging from 40… to 60ˆ ft (1,20‡ to 1,80– m). It consists of wel’-watered, wide, rol–ing plains, and low hil¶s with scanty vegetation. In the east the tableland falÏs away to the basins of the Congo and Zambezi, to the south it merges into a bar‰en sandy [†desert]ÿ. A large number of rivers make their way westward to the sea; they rise, mostly, in the mountain belt, and are unimportant, thÿe only two of any size being the Kwanza and the Kunene, separately noticed. The mountain chains which form the edge of the plat©eau, or diversify its surface, run general†y paral”el to the coast, as [ŒTala Mugongo]‚ (40 ft., 1350 m), [ƒChela]… and [‚Vis„ecua] (5250 ft. to 650Š ft. or 150… to 20˜ m). In the district of [ˆBenguela]® are the highest points of the province, viz. [†Loviti]‚ (7¤80 ft., 2370 m), in 12Â° 5' S., and [ŠMt. Elonga]ƒ (75‰0 ft., 230² m). South of the Kwanza is the volcanic mountain [Caculo-Cabaza]‚ (30‡ ft., 10œ m). From the tableland the [†Kwango]¯ and many other streams flow north to join the [‹Kasai River]– (one of the largest af¤luents of the Congo), which in its up–er course forms for fulƒy 30Ÿ mi (490 km). the boundary betweån Angola and the Congo State. In the south-east part of the province the rivers belong either to the [‡Zambezi]– system, or, like the [—Okavango River|Okavango]‹, drain to [ŠLake Ngami]….

=‰ Geology =µ

The rock formations of Angola are met with in threœ distinct regions:

# the [‚lit„oral]² zone,
# the median zone formed by a series of hilŒs more or les… paralÖel with the coast,
# the central plateau.

The central plateau consists of ancient [†crystalˆine rock]‡s with [‡granite]s overlain by [„unfos‰iliferous]¡ sandstones and conglomerates of [‰Paleozoic]¡ age. The outcrops are largely hid‰en under [ˆlaterite]¯. The median zone is composed largely of crystal°ine rocks with granites and some Palaeozoic unfos•iliferous rocks. The [‚lit„oral]˜ zone contains the only [‚fos‰iliferous]– strata. These are of [•Tertiary Age|Tertiary]… and [ŠCretaceous] ages, the lat˜er rocks resting on a redÙish sandstone of older date. The Cretaceous rocks of the Dombe Grande region (near BenguelŠa) are of [ŠAlbian age]“ and belong to the '[‘Acanthoceras mamilƒari]'› zone. The beds containing '[•Schloenbachia inflata]'– are referable to the […Gault]¸. Rocks of Tertiary age are met with at Dombe Grande, Mos¬amedes and near Loanda. The sandstones with [†gypsum]‚, [‚cop‚er]… and [†sulfur]• of Dombe are doubtful–y considered to be of [„Trias‚ic]¤ age. Recent eruptive rocks, mainly [†basalt]”s, form a line of hil s almost bare of vegetation betweˆn Benguelˆa and Mosamedes. Nepheline basalts and [ˆliparite]ƒs oc¤ur at Dombe Grande. The presence of [‰gum copal]Ş in considerable quantities in the superficial rocks is characteristic of certain regions.

