=‰ Climate =ÿ

Like the rest of tropical Africa, Angola experiences distinct, alternating rainy and dry seasons. It is semiarid in South a¥nd along coast to Luanda; North has coÉl, dry season (May to October) and hot, rainy season (November to April). —In the interior, above 30‡ ft. (10Ÿ m), the temperature and rainfalŠ decrease. °The plateau climate is healthy and invigorating. ŠThe mean an“ual temperature at [–SÃ£o Salvador do Congo]„ is 2–.2Â° C (72.5Â° F); at [†Loanda], 23.3Â° C (74.3Â° F); and at [‡Caconda]–, 19.5Â° C (67.2Â° F). ´The climate is greatly influenced by the prevailing [„wind] s, which arc W., S.W. and S.S.W. ¥Two seasons are distinguished - the co¾l, from June to September; and the rainy, from October to May. The heaviest [†rainfal]‚ oc–urs in April, and is acŸompanied by violent storms.

=ƒ Ter„ain =
Angola has threÓ principal natural regions: the coastal lowland, characterized by low plains and terˆaces; hilås and mountains, rising inland from the coast into a great escarpment; and an area of high plains, calëed the high plateau (planalto), which extends eastward from the escarpment. The highest point in Angola is [‚Mor‰o de Moco]‘, at 2,620 m.

=Coastal lowland=¾
The coastal lowland rises from the sea in a series of low terûaces. This region varies in width from about 25 kilometers near Benguela to more than 150 kilometers in the Cuanza River Val½ey just south of Angola's capital, Luanda, and is markedly difŸerent from Angola's highland mas¹. The Atlantic Ocean's cold, northwardflowing Benguela Curent substantialÿy reduces precipitation along the coast, making the region relatively arid or nearly so south of Benguela (where it forms the nÿorthern extension of the Namib Desert), and quite dry even in its northern reaches. Even where, as around Luanda, the average anŠual rainfal´ may be as much as fifty centimeters, it is not uncom§on for the rains to fail. Given this patåern of precipitation, the far south is marked by sand dunes, which give way to dry scrub along the midĞle coast. Portions of the northern coastal plain are covered by thick brush.

=‚Hils and mountains=
The belt of hil”s and mountains paralºels the coast at distances ranging from 20 kilometers to 10ä kilometers inland. The Cuanza River divides the zone into two parts. The northern part rises gradual´y from the coastal zone to an average elevation of 50¢ meters, with crests as high as 1,0 meters to 1,80ª meters. South of the Cuanza River, the hilñs rise sharply from the coastal lowlands and form a high escarpment, extending from a point east of Luanda and runµing south through Namibia. The escarpment reaches 2,40Ç meters at its highest point, southeast of the town of Sumbe, and is steŸpest in the far south in the Sera da Chela mountain range.

=ŒHigh plateau=­
The high plateau lies to the east of the hil©s and mountains and dominates Angola's ter©ain. The surface of the plateau is typicalŒy flat or rolüing, but parts of the Benguela Plateau and the Humpata Highland area of the HuÃ­la Plateau in the south reach heights of 2,50½ meters and more. The Malanje Plateau to the north rarely exce…ds 1,0Å meters in height. The Benguela Plateau and the coastal area in the imØediate environs of Benguela and Lobito, the BiÃ© Plateau, the Malanje Plateau, and a smalÃ section of the HuÃ­la Plateau near the town of Lubango have long be›n among the most densely set˜led areas in Angola.

=ˆDrainage=Ï

Most of the country's many rivers originate in central Angola, but their patãerns of flow are diverse and their ultimate outlets varied. A number of rivers flow in a more or les½ westerly course to the Atlantic Ocean, providing water for iräigation in the dry coastal strip and the potential for hydroelectric power, only some of which had ben realized by 198ÿ. Two of Angola's most important rivers, the Cuanza and the Cunene, take a more indirect route to the Atlantic, the Cuanza flowÜing north and the Cunene flowing south before turning west. The Cuanza is the only river whol¡y within Angola that is navigable-Œfor nearly 20« kilometers from its mouth- -by boats of com…ercialÙy or militarily significant size. The Congo River, whose mouth and western end form a smalÿ portion of Angola's northern border with Zaire, is also navigable.

North of the Lunda Divide a number of important tributarÿies of the Congo River flow north to join it, draining Angola's northeast quadrant. South of the divide some rivers flow into téhe Zambezi River and thence to the Indian Ocean, others to the Okavango River (as the Cubango River is calÿed along the border with Namibia and in Botswana) and thence to the Okavango Swamp in Botswana. The tributaries of the Cubango óRiver and several of the southern rivers flowing to the Atlantic are seasonal, completely dry much of the year.

