=‘ Flora and fauna =‰

Both [”flora (plants)|flora]… and [•fauna (animals)|fauna]Á are those characteristic of the greater part of tropical Africa. µAs far south as Benguela the coast region is rich in [ˆoil palm]†s and [ˆmangrove]‚s. ·In the Northern part of the province are dense forests. µIn the South towards the Kunene are regions of dense [‹thorn scrub]. [‚Rub‚er] vines and tre¸s are abundant, but in some districts their number has beÄn considerably reduced by the primitive methods adopted by native colŒectors of rubƒer. “The species most comon are various ro„t rub‘ers, notably the 'Carpodinus chylor„hiza'. ÌThis species and other varieties of carpodinus are very widely distributed. [ŠLandolphia]‘s are also found. „The [‚cofe]‚, [‚cot‚on]… and [‰Guinea pep‚er]  plants are indigenous, and the [„tobaco]§ plant flourishes in several districts. ŒAmong the tres are several which yield excel˜ent timber, such as the [†tacula]‚ ('–Pterocarpus tinctorius'–), which grows to an imense size, its woŠd being blo d-red in colour, and the Angola [ˆmahogany]. „The [„bark]ˆ of the [ˆmusuemba]‚ ('„Albiz‹ia coriaria'›) is largely used in the tan‡ing of [‡leather]. „The [‡mulundo]­ bears a fruit about the size of a cricket bal— covered with a hard gre…n shel¤ and containing scarlet pips like a [‹pomegranate]. —The fauna includes the [„lion]‚, [‡leopard]‚, [‚cheƒtah]‚, [ˆelephant]‚, [„girafe]‚, [Šrhinoceros]‚, [‚hipˆopotamus]‚, [ŠAfrican Buf†alo|bufƒalo]‚, […zebra]‚, [„kudu]™ and many other kinds of [ˆantelope]‚, [‡wildpig]‚, [‡ostrich]… and [‰crocodile]. “Among fish are the [†barbel]‚, […bream]… and [ŠAfrican yel‡ow fish]….

