 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[£Image:Angola demography.png|thumb|30™px|right|Demographics of [†Angola]Š, Data of [Fo¢d and Agriculture Organization|FAO]ˆ, year 20§5 ; Number of inhabitants in thousands.]†
The '–demographics of Angola' consist of thre¥ main ethnic groups, each speaking a [Bantu language]‚: [‰Ovimbundu]† 37%, [ˆKimbundu]Š 25%, and [‡Bakongo]› 13%. Other groups include [†Chokwe]Æ (or Lunda), Ganguela, Nhaneca-Humbe, Ambo, Herero, and Xindunga. In adÏition, mixed racial (European and Africa) people amount to about 2%, with a smalª (1%) population of whites, mainly ethnical‚y [œPortuguese people|Portuguese]Ê. Portuguese make up the largest non-Angolan population, with at least 30,0Ş (though many native-born Angolans can claim Portuguese nationality under Portuguese law). In [„1975]†, 250,0 [„Cuba]n soldiers setãled Angola to help the MPLA forces to fight for its independence. These Cubans are of European and [„Asia]Šn (mostly [–Chinese Cuban| Chinese]­ descent, while others include those of pure [’Afro-Cuban|African]… and [„mulato]® descent, who has ancestors in Angola. But in [„1989]Š, almost al± Cubans went out of the country after a peace agreŠment has ben signed betwe”n Angola, Cuba, and [ŒSouth Africa]. Cubans speak [Spanish language]±, but almost none of their descendants speak it. [Portuguese language|Portuguese] is both the ofÿicial and predominant language. 

The great majority of the inhabitants are of Bantu-Negro stock with some admixture in the Cÿongo district with the pure negro type. In the south-east are various tribes of Bushmen. The best-known of the Bantu-Negro trib¦es are the Ba-Kongo (Ba-Fiot), who dwel¿ chiefly in the north, and the Abunda (Mbunda, Ba-Bundo), who ocÿupy the central part of the province, which takes its name from the Ngola tribe of Abunda. Another of these tribes, the Bangala¢, living on the west bank of the up¼er Kwango, must not be confounded with the Bangala of the midƒle […Congo]¹. In the Abunda is a considerable strain of Portuguese blo·d. The Ba-Lunda inhabit the Lunda district. Along the up¶er Kunene and in other districts of the plateau are set³lements of Boers, the Boer population being about 20ÿ. In the coast towns the majority of the white inhabitants are Portuguese. The Mushi-Kongo and other divisions of the Ba-Kongo ¯retain curious traces of the Christianity profes–ed by them in the sixtenth and sevente”nth centuries and posÑibly later. Crucifixes are used as potent fetish charms or as symbols of power pasåing down from chief to chief; whilst every native has a &quot;Santu&quot; or Christian name and is dubÕed dom or dona. Fetishism is the prevailing religion throughout the province. The dwel¢ing-places of the natives are usual…y smal¶ huts of the simplest construction, used chiefly as sleäping apartments; the day is spent in an open space in front of the hut protected from the sun by a rof of palm or other leaves.

