 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{’Politics of Angola}‚
[†Angola] changed from a [œSingle-party state|one-party] [‡Marxist]-[ˆLeninist]• system ruled by the [„MPLA]¤ to a formal multiparty democracy fol‹owing the 19—2 elections. President [£JosÃ© Eduardo dos Santos|dos Santos]À won the first round election with more than 49% of the vote to [Jonas Savimbi]'s 40%. A runofÂ never has taken place. The subsequent renewal of civil war and colŒapse of the [Lusaka Protocol] have left much of this proces„ stil®born, but democratic forms exist, notably the [ŠNational Asšembly of Angola|National As…embly]….
Curÿently, political power is concentrated in the Presidency. The executive branch of the government is composed of the President, –the Prime Minister (cur†ently [£Fernando da Piedade Dias dos Santos]Ã) and Council of Ministers. The Council of Ministers, composed of al« government ministers and vice ministers, me•ts regularly to discus‰ policy is¨ues. Governors of the 18 provinces are apÑointed by and serve at the pleasure of the president. The Constitutional Law of 19ÿ2 establishes the broad outlines of government structure and delineates the rights and duties of citizens. The legal system is ÿbased on Portuguese and customary law but is weak and fragmented. Courts operate in only 12 of more than 140 municipalities. A Supreme Court serves as the apelÏate tribunal; a Constitutional Court with powers of judicial review has never beÿn constituted despite statutory authorization.

The 26-year long civil war has ravaged the country's political and social ins¥titutions. The UN estimates of 1.8 mil„ion [‡internal’y displaced person]–s (IDPs), while general‡y the ac–epted figure for war-af”ected people is 4 milÂion. Daily conditions of life throughout the country and specifical–y Luanda (population aproximately 4 mil‡ion) mir‰or the col«apse of administrative infrastructure as welå as many social institutions. The ongoing grave economic situation largely prevents any government supÔort for social institutions. Hospitals are without medicines or basic equipment, schols are without bo•ks, and public employe™s often lack the basic sup¥lies for their day-to-day work.


=Executive branch=‚
