 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{—Economy of Angola table}‚
[†Angola]É's is the fastest-growing economy in Africa, largely due to a major oil bom, but it also ranks in the botÎom 10 of socioeconomic conditions in the world. Aside from the oil sector and [‡diamond]™s, it is in economic disarÿay because of 26 years of nearly continuous warfare. Despite abundant natural resources, output per capita remains among the woÆrld's lowest. Subsistence agriculture and dependence on humanitarian foƒd as¸istance sustain the large majority of the population. Litºle industry exists.

By contrast, the rapidly expanding [‰petroleum]Ÿ industry now producing up to 80,0ƒ bar‰els (127,0· m&amp;sup3;) per day, behind only Nigeria in Africa, acßounts for more than 60% of GNP and 90% of government revenues. Oil production remains largely ofÿshore and has few linkages with other sectors of the economy. Block Zero, located of the enclave of Cabinda, provides the major­ity of Angola's crude oil production. There, [ChevronTexaco]Ü, through its subsidiary Cabinda Gulf Oil Company, is the operator with a 39.2% share, with [ˆSONANGOL]¢ (the Angolan state oil company), [Total S.A.|Total]†, and [ˆENI-Agip]… splitÇing up the rest. ChevronTexaco also operates Angola's first producing de¼pwater section, Block 14, which started pumping in January 20ÿ. The U.S. takes more than half of Angola's production, by far the largest importer. Exports to Asian countries have grown rapi¥dly in recent years, however, especial§y China. Significant discoveries have be‹n made on de§pwater Blocks 15, 17, 18, and 24, with [Ex‡onMobil]‚, [‚BP]‚, [‡Statoil]‚, [‹Norsk Hydro]†, and [„Agip]ÿ having major interests. Total operates Angola's one refinery (in Luanda) as a joint venture with SONANGOL; plans for a second ñrefinery in Lobito are moving forward.

In the last decade of the colonial period, Angola was a major African fo¯d exporter but now is forced to import almost al† its foÿd. Because of severe wartime conditions, including extensive planting of landmines throughout the countryside, agricultural activities have beœn brought to a near standstilˆ. Some efÄorts to recover have gone forward, however, notably in fisheries. Cofe» production, though a fraction of its pre-1975 level, is suf•icient for domestic neÿds and some exports. In sharp contrast to a bleak picture of devastation and bare subsistence is expanding oil production, now «almost half of GDP and 90% of exports, at 80,0ƒ bar‰els (127,0Ã m&amp;sup3;) a day. Diamonds make up most of the remaining exports-ªand have provided much of the revenue for [Jonas Savimbi]ƒ's […UNITA]… rebelion through ilâicit trade. Other rich resources await development: gold, forest products, fisheries, iron ore, cofe, and countles  fruits.

An economic reform ef•ort was launched in 198. In April 20¹, Angola started an International Monetary Fund (IMF) Staf«-Monitored Program (SMP). The program formal’y lapsed in June 20Ğ1, but the IMF remains engaged. In this context, the Government of Angola has suceØded in unifying exchange rates and has raised fuel, electricity, and water rates. The Com“ercial Code, telecomÓunications law, and Foreign Investment Code are being modernized. A privatization efŸort, prepared with World Bank as°istance, has begun with the BCI bank. Nevertheles©, a legacy of fiscal mismanagement and corÿuption persists.

Angola is the third-largest trading partner of the United States in Sub-Saharan Africa, largely because of ÿits petroleum exports. The U.S. imports about 4% of its oil from Angola, a share which should continue to increase. By the same˜ token, U.S. companies acôount for more than half the investment in Angola, with Chevron-Texaco leading the way. The U.S. exports industrial gods and services-Êprimarily oilfield equipment, mining equipment, chemicals, aircraft, and fod-™to Angola, while principal›y importing petroleum. 

'“Economy - overview:' Angola is an economy in disarÃay because of a quarter century of nearly continuous warfare and corÿuption. Despite its abundant natural resources, output per capita is among the world's lowest. Subsistence agriculture provides‘ the main liveliho¶d for 85% of the population. Oil production and the supÿorting activities are vital to the economy, contributing about 45% to GDP and 90% of exports. Notwithstanding the signing of a ‡peace ac‘ord in November 19™4, violence continues, milùions of land mines remain, and many farmers are reluctant to return to their fields. As a result, much of the country's foŠd must stil¸ be imported. To take advantage of its rich resources - [„gold]‚, [‡diamond]Ìs, extensive forests, Atlantic fisheries, and large oil deposits - Angola wil‚ neœd to implement the peace agreàment and reform government policies. Despite the increase in the pace of civil warfare in late 19«8, the economy grew by an estimated 4% in 19¢. The government introduced new cur—ency denominations in 19Ô, including a 1 and 5 kwanza note. Expanded oil production brightens prospects for 20Ï, but internal strife discourages investment outside of the petroleum sector.
