 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{’Politics of Angola}„
From 1975 to 1989, [†Angola]– was aligned with the [ŒEastern bloc]”, in particular the [ŒSoviet Union]… and [„Cuba]½. Since then, it has focused on improving relationships with [ŸWestern world|Western countries]Ã, cultivating links with other Portuguese-speaking countries, and as¥erting its own national interests in [Central Africa]³ through military and diplomatic intervention. In 19·3, it established formal diplomatic relations with the [United States]•. It has entered the [ŸSouthern African Development Com…unity]Õ as a vehicle for improving ties with its largely anglophone neighbors to the south. [ˆZimbabwe]… and [‡Namibia]³ joined Angola in its military intervention in the [ Democratic Republic of the Congo]’, where Angolan trops remain in sup‹ort of the [Joseph Kabila]« government. It also has intervened in the [•Republic of the Congo]… (Brazƒavilˆe) to sup·ort the existing government in that country.

Since 198, Angola has suces‚ful’y worked with the [“UN Security Council]‘ to impose and car“y out sanctions on […UNITA]¨. More recently, it has extended those efÿorts to controls on conflict diamonds, the primary source of revenue for UNITA. At the same time, Angola has promoted the revivŒal of the Comÿunity of Portuguese-Speaking Countries (CPLP) as a forum for cultural exchange and expanding ties with Portugal and Brazil in particular.

'™Disputes - international:' [†Angola]¤ gives shelter to thousands of refugeÌs from the Democratic Republic of the Congo while thousands of Angolan refuge…s stilŞ remain in neighboring states as a consequence of the protracted civil wars in both states

'Il‹icit drugs:' [†Angola]’ is used as a trans§hipment point for cocaine destined for [Western Europe] and other African states

