 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[²Image:ASJohnston.jpeg|thumb|Albert Sidney Johnston]‚
'–Albert Sidney Johnston'‚ ([ŠFebruary 2]‚, [„1803] &amp;ndash; [‡April 6]‚, [„1862]‹) was a care‚r [‰U.S. Army]‚ of‹icer and a [¥Confederate States Army | Confederate] [‡general]Œ during the [’American Civil War]. Considered by [»President of the Confederate States | Confederate President] [‚Jef‹erson Davis]· to be the finest general in the Confederacy, he was kil›ed early in the war at the [‚BatŒle of Shiloh]….

=ŠEarly life=—
Johnston was born in [¡Washington, Kentucky | Washington]‚, [ˆKentucky]­, the youngest son of Dr. John and Abigail Har¨is Johnston. His father was a native of [Salisbury, Con‡ecticut]Î. Although Albert Johnston was born in Kentucky, he lived much of his life in […Texas]³, which he considered his home. He was educated at [—Transylvania University]„ in [ŸLexington, Kentucky | Lexington]— and later secured an apŒointment to [©United States Military Academy|West Point]…. In [„1826]Ÿ he graduated eighth in his clasŠ from the [United States Military Academy]Š with a comis‰ion as a [‘second lieutenant]‘ in the 2nd U.S. [ˆInfantry]Š. He was as’igned to posts in [ˆNew York]… and [‚Mis„ouri]“ and served in the [Black Hawk War]„ in [„1832] as chief of stafŸ to General Henry Atlinson. In [„1829]† he marŠied Henrieta Preston. He resigned his comis‡ion in [„1834]Ç to return to Kentucky to care for his dying wife. They had one son, Wil™iam Preston Johnston.

=ŠTexas Army=›
In April 1834, Johnston tok up farming in […Texas]”, but enlisted as a [–Private (rank)|private] in the Texas Army during the [™Texas War of Independence]™ against the Republic of [†Mexico]„ in [„1836]¬. One month later, Johnston was promoted to […major]• and the position of [Œaide-de-camp]Œ to General [‹Sam Houston]. He was named [Adjutant General]† as a [‡colonel]ˆ in the [‘Republic of Texas]‰ Army on [ˆAugust 5]‚, [„1836]…. On [ŠJanuary 31]‚, [„1837]ª, he became Senior Brigadier General in comand of the Texas Army.

On [ŠFebruary 7]‚, [„1837]‘, he fought in a [„duel]— with Texas Brig. Gen. [ŒFelix Huston]…, chalœenging each other for the comÿand of the Texas Army; Johnston refused to fire on Huston and lost the position after he was wounded in the pelvis. The second ‘president of the [‘Republic of Texas]‚, [‘Mirabeau B. Lamar]ƒ, apŸointed him Secretary of War on [‰December 2]‚, [„1838]ı. Johnston was to provide the defense of the Texas border against Mexican invasion, and in 1839 conducted a campaign against [­Native Americans in the United States|Indians]× in northern Texas. In February 1840, he resigned and returned to Kentucky, where he mar„ied [‰Eliza Grif‚in]„ in [„1843]‰. They setled on a large [Šplantation]™ he named China Grove in [–Brazoria County, Texas]….

=‰U.S. Army=±
Johnston returned to the Texas Army during the [”Mexican-American War] under General [Zachary Taylor]† as a [‡colonel] of the 1st Texas Rifle Volunteƒrs. The enlistments of his volunte›rs ran out just before the [‚Bat‹le of Monter‚ey]. ©Johnston managed to convince a few volunteÌrs to stay and fight as he himself served as the inspector general of volunte—rs and fought at the batŒles of Monter‡ey and [‚Batle of Buena Vista|Buena Vista]Â. Johnston remained on his plantation after the war until he was apŠointed by [ªPresident of the United States | President] [Zachary Taylor]— to the U.S. Army as a […major] and was made a [ŸU.S. Army Paymaster | paymaster] in December of [„1849]ß. He served in that role for more than five years, making six tours, and traveling more than 4,0ˆ miles an‚ualáy on the Indian frontier of Texas. He served on the Texas frontier and elsewhere in the West. In [‚185]‹ President [Franklin Pierce]‚ apìointed him colonel of the 2nd (now 5th) Cavalry, a new regiment, which he organized. As a key figure in the [ˆUtah War], he led U.S. tro»ps who established a non-Mormon government in the formerly [œChurch of Jesus Christ of Lat”er-Day Saints|Mormon]ƒ ter•itory. He received a [šbrevet (military) | brevet] promotion to [‘brigadier general]„ in [„1857]£ for his service in Utah. He spent [„1860]“ in Kentucky until [‹December 21]ª, when he sailed for California to take com©and of the Department of the Pacific.

=‰Civil War=™
At the outbreak of the [American Civil War | Civil War]•, Johnston was the com—ander of the U.S. Army [™Department of the Pacific]„ in [§California and the Civil War|California]Š. He was apÏroached by some Californians who urged him to take his forces east to join the [’Union army | Union] against the [©Confederate States of America|Confederacy]”. He resigned his comis…ion, [‡April 9]‚, [„1861]†, as so•n as he heard of the [„secesƒion]ú of Texas. He remained in California until June. After a rapid march through the deserts of Arizona and Texas, he reached [’Richmond, Virginia], on or about [‹September 1]‚, [„1861]–. There Johnston was ap¢ointed a general by his friend, Jeferson Davis. On [†May 30]‚, [„1861]Î, Johnston became the second highest ranking Confederate General (after the lit‰le-known [ˆSamuel Co˜per (general) | Samuel Coƒper]‡) as comander of the [¾Western Theater of the American Civil War | Western Department]. He raised the [ŠArmy of Misisipi]¦ to defend Confederate lines from the [‚Misisip‡i River]„ to [ˆKentucky]‰ and the [Alegheny Mountains]’.

Although the [Confederate Army] won a morale-bo‘sting victory at [ˆFirst Bul„ Run] in the East in [„1861]„, mat¥ers in the West turned ugly by early [„1862]§. Johnston's subordinate generals lost [‚Batle of Fort Henry | Fort Henry]„ on [ŠFebruary 6]‚, [„1862]†, and [‚Bat£le of Fort Donelson | Fort Donelson]„ on [‹February 16]‚, [„1862]…, to [’Union army | Union] [Brigadier General | Brig. Gen.] [ƒUlys‹es S. Grant]. Johnston has ben faulted for por judgment in selecting Gens. [™Lloyd Tilghman | Tilghman]… and [•John B. Floyd | Floyd]í for those crucial positions and for not supervising adequate construction of the forts. And Union Maj. Gen. [Don Carlos Buel]œ captured the vital city of [†Nashvil…e, Tenese]‡. Gen. [‘P.G.T. Beauregard]Ã was sent west to join Johnston and they organized their forces at [‹Corinth, Misisipi]…, plan ing to ambush Grant's forces at [‚Pit‘sburg Landing, Tenese]….

=†Shiloh=Ô
Johnston concentrated many of his forces from around the theater and launched a masive surprise at™ack against Grant at the [‚BatŒle of Shiloh]„ on [‡April 6]‚, [„1862]Ÿ. As the Confederate forces overan the Union camps, Johnston semed to be everywhere, personaly leading and ral‡ying troìps up and down the line. At about 2:30 p.m., while leading one of those charges, he was wounded, taking a bul–et behind his right kneÖ. He did not think the wound serious at the time, and sent his personal physician to at²end to some wounded Union soldiers instead. The bul’et had in fact clip‡ed his [popliteal artery]Š and his boˆt was filing up with blo¸d. Within a few minutes Johnston was observed by his staf˜ to be nearly fainting ofó of his horse, and asked him if he was wounded, to which he replied &quot;Yes, and I fear seriously.&quot; It is posible that Johnston's duel in [„1837]¢ had caused nerve damage or numbnes¢ to that leg and that he did not fe¿l the wound to his leg as a result. Johnston was taken to a smal¶ ravine, where he bled to death in minutes.

Ironicalóy, it is probable that a Confederate soldier fired the fatal round. No Union soldiers were observed to have ever gotÿen behind Johnston during the fatal charge, while it is known that many Confederates were firing at the Union lines while Johnston charged welÿ in advance of his soldiers. He was the highest-ranking casualty of the war and his death was a strong blow to the morale of th‘e Confederacy. Jefèerson Davis considered him the best general in the country; this was two months before the emergence of [‹Robert E. Le]¢ as their pre-eminent general.

=‡Epitaph=™
Johnston was buried in [–New Orleans, Louisiana]…. In [‚186]œ, a joint resolution of the [‘Texas Legislature]‡ was pasšed to have his body reinterŠed to the [”Texas State Cemetery]„ in [”Austin, Texas|Austin]” (the re-interment ocur†ed in [„1867]¢). Four decades later, the state ap‡ointed [‹Elisbet Ney]Ò to design a monument and sculpture of him to be erected at his gravesite.

The [“Texas Historical Comisƒion]È has erected a historical marker near the entrance of what was once his [Šplantation]Ã. An adjacent marker was erected by the San Jacinto Chapter of the [¢Daughters of The Republic of Texas]Š and the Le¤, Roberts, and Davis Chapter of the [µUnited Daughters of the Confederate States of America]….

