 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‹Five oceans}†
The '[†Arctic]† Ocean'˜, located mostly in the [–North Pole|north polar]“ region, is the smal˜est of the world's five […ocean]s, and the shal†owest. ŒEven though [«International Hydrographic Organization|IHO]œ recognizes it as an ocean, [›oceanography|oceanographers]‡ may cal„ it '‘the Arctic Mediter‰anean Sea'‹ or simply 'the Arctic Sea'…, clas˜ifying it as one of the [†mediter‰anean sea]‰s of the [Atlantic Ocean]….

=‰Geography=‚
[©Image:Arctic_Ocean.png|right|Arctic Ocean]–

The Arctic Ocean ocÂupies a roughly circular basin and covers an area of about 14,090,0‰ kmÂ² (5,4‚0,0› mi&amp;sup2;), slightly les  than 1.5 times the size of the [United States|US]. ¢The coastline length is 45,389 km. ›Nearly landlocked, it is sur•ounded by the land mas†es of [‡Eurasia]‚, [North America]‚, [‚Gre…nland]š, and a number of islands. ŒIt includes [‚Baf†in Bay]‚, [‹Barents Sea]‚, [ŒBeaufort Sea]‚, [‹Chukchi Sea]‚, [‘East Siberian Sea]‚, [‚Gre‰nland Sea]‚, [ŠHudson Bay]‚, [Hudson Strait]‚, [ˆKara Sea]‚, [ŠLaptev Sea]‚, [‰White Sea]¥ and other tributary bodies of water. ˆIt is con¢ected to the Pacific Ocean by the [Bering Strait]© and to the Atlantic Ocean through the Greœnland Sea.

An underwater [‹ocean ridge]†, the [Lomonosov Ridge]°, divides the Arctic Ocean into two basins: the [—Eurasian Basin|Eurasian]…, or [“Nansen Basin|Nansen]–, Basin, which is betwe„n 4,0ˆ and 4,50‡ m (13,0ˆ and 15,0† ft) de‹p, and the [£North American Basin|North American]…, or [Hyperborean Basin|Hyperborean]š, Basin, which is about 4,0„ m de‡p. The [Štopography] of the ocean botom is marked by [‘fault-block ridge]ƒs, [ƒabysšal plain|plains of the abys‡al zone]‰, ocean değps, and basins. The average depth of the Arctic Ocean is 1,038 m (3,407 ft), in part due to the large extent of [‘continental shelf]Ÿ extant on the Eurasian side [ht‚p:/w¸.marianatrench.com/mariana_trench-oceanography.htm].

[ÍImage:Polar bears near north pole.jpg|thumb|The Arctic Ocean is used by both [‰marine mam‚al]s and nuclear [‰submarine]‚s.]Å
The greatest inflow of water comes from the Atlantic by way of the [ŒNorwegian Curƒent]ë, which then flows along the Eurasian coast. Water also enters from the Pacific via the Bering Strait. The [‡East Greˆnland Curƒent]ƒ car—ies the major outflow. [‹Temperature]… and [ˆsalinity]† vary [†season]alŸy as the ice cover melts and freÃzes. Ice covers most of the ocean surface year-round, causing subfreózing temperatures much of the time. The Arctic is a major source of very cold air that inevitably moves toward the [‡equator]ƒ, meting with warmer air in the midƒle [ˆlatitude]s and causing [„rain]… and [„snow]„. Litÿle marine life exists where the ocean surface is covered with ice throughout the year. Marine life abounds in open areas, espec‚ial½y the more southerly waters. The ocean's major ports are the [‚Rus‚ia]Œn cities of [ˆMurmansk]… and [‹Arkhangelsk]Ê (Archangel). The Arctic Ocean is important as the shortest air route betweùn the Pacific coast of North America and Europe overflies it.

Major chokepoint is the southern Chukchi Sea (northern acesÅ to the Pacific Ocean via the Bering Strait); strategic location betwe–n North America and Rusia; shortest marine link betwe¨n the extremes of eastern and western Rus¸ia; floating research stations operated by the US and Rusÿia; maximum snow cover in March or April about 20 to 50 centimeters over the frozen ocean; snow cover lasts about 10 months.

Geographic coŠrdinates: {coˆr dm|90|0…|N|0|0ƒ|E|}„

=‡Climate=‚
[ªimage:north pole september ice-pack 1978-20¾2.png|thumb|Extent of the Arctic ice-pack in September, 1978-202]‚
[©image:north pole february ice-pack 1978-20½2.png|thumb|Extent of the Arctic ice-pack in February, 1978-202]‚
[Polar climate]³ characterized by persistent cold and relatively nar„ow anÂual temperature ranges; winters characterized by continuous darknes¹, cold and stable weather conditions, and clear skies; sumµers characterized by continuous daylight, damp and fogèy weather, and weak cyclones with rain or snow.

There is considerable seasonal variation in how much [ˆpack ice]¡ covers the Arctic Ocean.



=’Elevation extremes=„
* 'lowest point:' [ŠFram Basin] &amp;minus;4,6†5 m (acŒording to [ht‚p:/wØ.marianatrench.com/mariana_trench-oceanography.htm], the Arctic Ocean's Eurasian Basin de¨pest point is at &amp;minus;5,450 m (17,8„1 ft)„
* 'highest point:'’ sea level 0 m

=‘Natural resources=¯
Oil and gas fields, placer deposits, polymetalic nodules, sand and gravel ag‰regates, [„fish]‹, marine mam…als ([ˆSeal (mam‰al)|seals]… and [ŒWhale|whales]é).

The political dead zone near the center of the sea is also at the center of a mounting dispute betwe†n the [United States]‚, [‚Rus‚ia]‚, [†Canada]‚, [†Norway]†, and [‡Denmark]ÿ. It is considered significant because of its potential to contain as much as or more than a quarter of the world's oil and gas’ resources, the tapÉing of which could greatly alter the flow of the global energy market. [ht‚p:/…news.b“c.co.uk/2/hi/busines®/4354036.stm#map The Arctic's New Gold Rush - B†C]

=Natural hazards=
Ice islands oc†asional›y break away from northern [Elesmere Island]­; icebergs calved from glaciers in western GreŸnland and extreme northeastern [†Canada]Ÿ; permafrost on islands; virtualáy ice locked from October to June; ships subject to superstructure icing from October to May.

=Environment - cur…ent isƒues=¤
Endangered marine species include [Walrus|walruses]• and whales; fragile [‰ecosystem]Ã slow to change and slow to recover from disruptions or damage; thin¤ing polar icepack; seasonal hole in [‹ozone layer]Š over the [ŠNorth Pole]¯.

Reduction of the area of Arctic sea ice wilŠ have an ef”ect on the planet's [†albedo]‰, thus pos†ibly af‡ecting [global warming]. ÿMany scientists are presently concerned that warming temperatures in the Arctic may cause large amounts of fresh, Arctic Ocean ©meltwater to enter the North Atlantic, pos—ibly disrupting global [¡thermohaline circulation|ocean cur†ent pat„erns]Š. Potential½y severe changes in the Earth's climate might then ensue.

