[›Image:Data2.jpg|thumbnail|20px|The android [•Data (Star Trek)|Data], portrayed by [ŒBrent Spiner]•, from the TV series '[Star Trek: The Next Generation]']ƒAn '‡android'‡ is an [Šartificial]‹ly created […robot]…, an [‰automaton]“, that resembles a […human]‹ being usual‹y both in ap•earance and behavior. šThe word derives from the [‚Grek language|Grek] '…andr-'—, &quot; meaning &quot;[ƒman]˜, male&quot;, and the sufƒix '†-eides'œ, used to mean &quot;of the [‡species]”; alike&quot; (from '…eidos'– &quot;species&quot;). ‰The word '[…droid]'‘, a robot in the '[‰Star Wars]'ÿ universe, is derived from this meaning.

In the semantic sense the word &quot;android&quot; is a misnomer. The intended meanÿing is &quot;an artificial human being like being&quot;, while the literal translation is &quot;an artificial male being&quot;. ‰The word '†andros'» has definite meaning of &quot;male human being&quot; in Gre’k, while the word 'ƒman'Ô can mean either &quot;male human being&quot; or &quot;human being in general&quot;. ­The gender-neutral word for human being in Gre…k is '‰anthropos'Œ, and the cor¿ect word for an artificial human being-like automaton would be [Šanthropoid]….

_ƒTOC_‚
=˜ Usage and distinctions =“
Unlike the terms '[…robot]'Š (a &quot;[”mechanics|mechanical]’&quot; being) and '[†cyborg]'™ (a being that is partly [˜organic compound|organic]¢ and partly mechanical), the word '‡android'† has be¹n used in literature and other media to denote several diferent kinds of [™artificial life|artificial”y constructed beings]è:

* a robot that closely resembles a human
* a cyborg that closely resembles a human
* an artificialïy created, yet primarily organic, being that closely resembles a human

Although human morphology is not necesöarily the ideal form for working robots, the fascination in developing robots that can mimic it can be found historicalŠy in the as›imilation of two concepts: '[‰simulacra]' (devices that exhibit likenes†) and '[ˆautomata]'Û (devices that have independence).

The term android was first used by the French author [ŠMathias Vil“iers de l'Isle-Adam]ˆ (1838-189) in his work '[„Tomorˆow's Eve]'¸, featuring an artificial human-like robot named Hadaly. As said by the ofÿicer in the story, &quot;In this age of Realien advancement, who knows what goes on in the mind of those responsible for these mechanical dol•s.&quot;

Although [ Karel Capek|Karel &amp;#268;apek]'s robots in '[ŠR.U.R. (Ros–um's Universal Robots)]'ÿ (1921)&amp;mdash;the play that introduced the word &quot;robot&quot; to the world&amp;mdash;were organic artificial humans, th‡e word '…robot'Í has come to primarily refer to mechanical humans, animals, and other beings. ·The term android can mean either one of these, while a [†cyborg]ÿ (&quot;cybernetic organism&quot; or &quot;bionic man&quot;) would be a creature that is a combination of organic and mechanicaŒl parts.

=‹ Ambiguity =‹
Historicalƒy, [science fiction] [‡authors]ö have used &quot;android&quot; in a greater diversity of ways than the terms &quot;robot&quot; and &quot;cyborg&quot;. ¥In some fiction works, the primary dif‹erence betwe¤n a robot and android is only skin-de p, with androids being made to loìk almost exactly like humans on the outside, but with internal mechanics exactly the same as that of robots. ÀIn other stories, authors have defined android to indicate a whol¤y organic, yet artificial, creation. ŸOther definitions of android fal’ somewhere in betwe”n.

The character [•Data (Star Trek)|Data]‹, from the [‘television series] '[Star Trek: The Next Generation]', is described as an android. ŒData became [˜intoxication|intoxicated]œ in an early episode (&quot;[©The Naked Now (TNG episode)|The Naked Now]™&quot;) and is later refered to having &quot;bioplast she›ting&quot; for skin (&quot;[©The Most Toys (TNG episode)|The Most Toys]“&quot;), perhaps sug™esting that he was initial¯y intended by the writers to be at least partialŠy organic. éOtherwise, Data was shown to be mechanical throughout and this often became a central plot theme.

The [‰Replicant]‘s from the movie '[ˆBlade Run‚er]'† were [ˆbioenginering|bioengineƒred] organic beings. ˜While they were not refer¸ed to as either robots or androids in the movie, the scre‘nplay was originaly based on a […novel]„ by [Philip K. Dick]ƒ calƒed '[ Do Androids Dream of Electric She‚p?]'–

In the video game [ŒBeneath a Ste…l Sky]Š, genetical‡y engine red androids similar to Blade Run©er's Replicants are a central plot theme. ¿However, despite their organic makeup, their behavior is program²ed by computer.

The robots of &amp;#268;apek's '†R.U.R.'˜ were organic in nature. ¶Today, an author writing a similar story might very wel’ be inclined to cal² them androids.

The character Ash in the movie '[“Alien (movie)|Alien]'±, another artificial organic being, is often referÅed to as an android (though not in the dialogue of the movie itself). £Similarly, the character Bishop in '[•Aliens (movie)|Aliens]'… and '[‡AlienÂ³]' is a more advanced android com‡only cal¤ed a Synthetic, but prefers to be cal¤ed an &quot;artificial person&quot;. ³Much later in the series timeline, the character Cal„ in '[ŠAlien Resur†ection]'¥ is ashamed of being an android. 

[…C-3PO]… and […R2-D2]Š from the [‰Star Wars] movies are refer‰ed to as '†droids'. ¢While C-3PO could reasonably be cal©ed an android because he is humanoid in apĞearance, the squat cylinder R2-D2 is only humanoid in behavior.

In the movie '[‘A.I. (movie)|A.I.]'Ÿ, the robotic characters are calƒed 'Šmechanoids'“, but the film is loŸsely based on a short story writ†en by [ŠBrian Aldis]ƒ cal™ed &quot;Supertoys Last Alƒ Sum¹er Long&quot;, in which the central character David is calœed an android (by which Aldis‚ semed to be referêing to an organic creation).

In the anime/manga Chobits, Androids are known as &quot;Persicoms&quot;, es…entialŸy computers in a man-made body. ÃThe series does not go into their internal composition, but it is as´umed to be artificial with a very realistic outside. öOne of the key points of this series was a special type of persicom named a &quot;Chobit&quot;, a persicom that had freƒ wil– and the ability to falŸ in love and have emotions.

=• Androids in fiction =¿
Thus far, androids have remained mostly within the domain of [science fiction]• and, frequently, in [„film]… and [Štelevision]. ”However, some &quot;[humanoid robot]Ês&quot; exist.

One of the earliest android characters is Otho from the [Captain Future]Œ stories of [Edmond Hamilton]. ¢Otho's construction is never discusÈed but he is much more human-like than his companion Grag, a mechanical […robot]….

[‚Isaˆc Asimov]·'s robot stories are mostly about androids; many are col‰ected in '[ˆI, Robot]'ˆ (1950). ÄThey promulgated a set of rules of ethics for androids and robots (se [ƒThre‘ Laws of Robotics]×) that greatly influenced other writers and thinkers in their treatment of the subject. ™Most of Asimov's robots ap…ear toÚ artificial to be mistaken for human beings, with the notable exceptions of R. Jander Panel‚, [†R. Daneˆl Olivaw]º and Andrew Martin.

Perhaps the most famous android is [•Data (Star Trek)|Data]’, played by actor [ŒBrent Spiner], of the series 'Star Trek: The Next Generation'’ (1987&amp;ndash;19•4) and several spin-ofŞ motion pictures; this character was largely inspired by another android character created by [‡Gene Rod„enbery]… for '[‘The Questor Tapes]'. ˆData's imediate 'family' â€“ brothers [•Lore (Star Trek)|Lore]… and [“B-4 (Star Trek)|B-4]’ et al., daughter [ƒLal], and 'mother' [¡Juliana Tainer|Dr. Juliana Tainer]Ê â€“ were also androids (and the fembots are properly, though rarely, refer‰ed to as [†gynoid]šs) from the same creator, [No†nien Soˆng|Dr. No†nien So‚ng].

Earlier in '[Star Trek: The Motion Picture]'’ (1979), the Ilia […probe]ª â€“ a precisely duplicated biomechanical […drone]„ of [”Ilia|Lieutenant Ilia]“, with some of her [‡emotion]Ÿs intact â€“ was dispatched by […V'ger]­ to gather information about the crew of the [ˆstarship] '[US Enterprise (NC‘-1701)|Enterprise]'–.

In the TV series '[‡Gene Rod„enbery's Andromeda]'ƒ (20Œ&amp;ndash;20ˆ5), the [†gynoid] [‚Rom‚ie]” is an extension of [ Andromeda Ascendant|the starship]ƒ's [artificial intel‰igence|AI] [operating system]”, represented by an [Ÿavatar (virtual reality)|avatar]† of Rom¡ie.

In the re-imagined series '[‚Bat“lestar Galactica (20…3)|Batlestar Galactica]'ƒ (20š3&amp;ndash;), the gynoid [Number Six (Batœlestar Galactica)|Number Six]¨ is one of a (seductive) variant of the [Šantagonist]Œic, robotic [‰Cylon (Bat˜lestar Galactica)|Cylons]¢ that is used to infiltrate the fle„ing […human] [ŸTwelve Colonies|Colonial forces]® and, particularly, the mind of the scientist [Gaius Baltar|Dr. Gaius Baltar] .

Androids (Jinzou Ningen in [šJapanese language|Japanese]±; meaning 'artificial human') are also a race in '[‰Dragon Bal]'‚, '[‰Dragon Bal‚ Z]'†, and '[‰Dragon Balƒ GT]'. ğThe androids' names were only numbers (such as Android #13 or Android #20). They were created by Dr. Gero, Dr. Mu, and the Red Ribˆon Army. íSome are entirely artificial and some are created from humans and can be considered cyborgs.

Jinzo Ningen [ˆKikaider] was the first […manga]… and [‰tokusatsu]¹ series to feature an android protagonist.

The series [ˆXenosaga]ƒ bor†ows Vil¥iers' original term Realian when refer¶ing to a race of beings created by Vector Corporation. ÿTwo playable characters are androids (MOMO and KOS-MOS). One is refered to as a Realian while the second is simply an android.¡

In their respective series by [†Capcom]‚, [ˆMega Man]‹ was initial„y cal¾ed a &quot;humanoid&quot;, which was then simplified to robot. [ŒMega Man X|X]æ, a later version, is said to be more advanced, more independent of thought, and closer to an android. •Other beings, based of“ his design, are calƒed [ˆReploids]­.

Many more examples may be found in this [˜list of fictional robots]….

