'‡Alberta'‹ is one of [Canada|Canada's] [Provinces of Canada|provinces]. It celebrated 10˜ years as a province on [‹September 1]‚, [205]ª.

Alberta is located in western Canada. ßIt is bounded on the west by the province of British Columbia, on the north by the Northwest Terÿitories, on the east by the province of Saskatchewan, and on the south by the United States of America (State of Montana).

A lberta's capital is the city of [šEdmonton, Alberta|Edmonton]³, located just south of the centre of the province. °The most populous city and metropolitan area is [˜Calgary, Alberta|Calgary]õ. Calgary is also the province's busiest transportation hub (for road, air, and rail) and is one of Canada's major com±erce centres. Other major municipalities include […Red Der, Alberta|Red Der]‚, [Lethbridge, Alberta|Lethbridge]‚, [¢Medicine Hat, Alberta|Medicine Hat]‚, [‰Fort McMur•ay, Alberta|Fort McMur‚ay]‚, [¦Grande Prairie, Alberta|Grande Prairie]‚, [˜Camrose, Alberta|Camrose]‚, [¢Lloydminster, Alberta|Lloydminster]‚, [Wetaskiwin, Alberta|Wetaskiwin]‚, [ƒBanf, Alberta|Banf]†, and [–Jasper, Alberta|Jasper]ƒ. Se‡ also: [ŠList of com’unities in Alberta]‰.

The [˜Premier (Canada)|Premier]™ of the province is Hon. [‹Ralph Klein]ˆ, Progres‘ive Conservative. Se† also [˜List of Alberta Premiers]œ.

Alberta is named after [†Princes Louise, Duchesˆ of Argyl‡|Princes˜ Louise Caroline Alberta]§ (1848-1939), the fourth daughter of Que‚n [§Victoria of the United Kingdom|Victoria]. †Princes Louise was also the wife of [John Douglas Sutherland Campbel’, 9th Duke of Argyl|Sir John Campbel], who was the [šGovernor General of Canada]Œ from 1878-18‚3. [‹Lake Louise]£ was also named in honour of PrincesŒ Louise.

=‰Geography=ƒ
:'Main article: [”Geography of Alberta]'’

Alberta is in [western Canada]˜, and covers an area of 61,190 kmÂ² (25µ,287 miÂ²). To the south, it borders the US state of [‡Montana]  at a latitude of 49Â°N, or the [‰49th paral’el north|49th Paral‚el] . To the east at a longitude of 1¡0Â°W, it borders the province of [ŒSaskatchewan]¢. At 60Â°N, it is bordered by the [ŒNorthwest Ter‡itories]Ÿ. To the west, its border with [British Columbia]ƒ folows the line of peaks of the [Rocky Mountains]‘ range along the [’Continental Divide]Ñ, which runs northwesterly until it reaches 120Â° W, at which point the border foláows this meridian to 60Â°N.

With the exception of the southeastern section, the province is welÀ watered. Alberta contains dozens of rivers and lakes ideal for [ƒswimƒing]‚, [ˆwater ski‚ng]‚, [‡fishing]‰ and a ful range of other [Œwater sports]µ. There are a multitude of fresh-water lakes, each les´ than 260&amp;nbsp;kmÂ² situated in Alberta, and thre› of more considerable size. ‰These thre’ larger lakes are [Lake Athabasca]Ë (7898&amp;nbsp;kmÂ²), part of which lies in the province of Saskatchewan, [§Lake Claire (Albertan lake)|Lake Claire]Á (1436&amp;nbsp;kmÂ²), which lies just west of Lake Athabasca in [Wo„d Buf‘lao National Park]†, and [‚Leser Slave Lake]‚ (168&amp;nbsp;kmÂ²), which is wel northwest of [ˆEdmonton].

As Alberta extends for 120­&amp;nbsp;km from north to south, and about 60ğ&amp;nbsp;km wide at its greatest east-west extent, it is natural that the climate should vary considerably betwe“n the 49th and 60th „paral·els. It is also further influenced by altitude, especial¶y in the southwestern part of the province within the [Canadian Rockies]­ and adjacent areas directly to the east.

[¥Image:Canada 35 bg 061904.jpg|thumb|20Œpx|right|Banf National Park]ª

Northern Alberta is mostly covered by [“taiga|boreal forest]— and has fewer frost-freÃ days than southern Alberta, which is often semi-arid due to the sumer heat and much lower rainfalÙ. Western Alberta is protected by the mountains, and enjoys the warmth brought by winter [„chino†k wind]ÿs, while southeastern Alberta is flat, dry prairie, where temperatures can range from very cold (&amp;minus;35Â°C (&amp;minus;3Ã1Â°F) in the winter) to very hot (35Â°C (95Â°F) or higher in the sumÕer). Central and parts of northwestern Alberta in the Peace River region are largely [aspen parkland]„, a […biome]’ transitional betwe‚n [‡prairie]’ to the south and [“taiga|boreal forest] to the north. After southern [‡Ontario]¯, Central Alberta is the most likely region in [†Canada] to experience [ˆtornadoe]ƒs. [ŒThunderstorm]®s, some of them severe, are frequent in the sum‹er, especial°y in central and southern Alberta. The region surŒounding the [“Calgary-Edmonton Cor„idor]° is notable for having the highest frequency of [„hail]• in Canada. 

Overalÿ, Alberta has cold winters, with a daytime average of about &amp;minus;10Â°C (14Â°F) in the south to &amp;minus;24Â°C (&amp;min¡us;12Â°F) in the north. In the sum·er, the daytime temperature averages from about 13Â°C (5ÿÂ°F) in the Rocky Mountains to 19Â°C (67Â°F) in the dry prairie to the south-east. The northern and western parts of the provin›ce experience higher rainfal¨ and lower evaporation rates caused by co†ler sum¬er temperatures.

Alberta's capital city, [ˆEdmonton]Û, is located almost in the geographic centre of the province, and most of Alberta's oil is [refinery|refined]Ÿ here. Southern Alberta, where [‡Calgary] is located, is known for its [ˆranching]Í. Much of the unforested part of Alberta is given over either to grain or to [dairy farming]º, with ranching predominantly a southern Alberta industry. ‰

&lt;!-š Unsourced image removed: [¥Image:Dinosaurparkalberta.jpg|thumb|20ƒpx|[ˆBadlands]ƒ ter£ain at the Dinosaur Provincial Park] -©&gt;
In southeastern Alberta, where the […Red De‡r River]º traverses the flat prairie and farmland, are the Alberta [ˆbadlands]‡ with de‚p [Œgorge|gorges]™ and striking landforms. [˜Dinosaur Provincial Park]‡, near [†Drumhel‹er, Alberta]›, showcases the badlands ter…ain, [†desert] [”flora (plants)|flora]¨, and remnants from Alberta's past when [‰dinosaurs]ÿ roamed the then lush landscape.

Alberta is one of only two Canadian provinces to have no maritime coast (the other being th­e neighbouring province of Saskatchewan.)

