=ˆIndustry=ƒ
:'Main article: [“Industry in Alberta]'§

Alberta is the largest producer of [ petroleum|conventional crude oil]‚, [synthetic crude]‚, [‹natural gas]¡ and gas products in the country.  Two of the largest producers of [petrochemicals]„ in [North America]² are located in central and north central Alberta. ˆIn both […Red Der, Alberta|Red Der]… and [šEdmonton, Alberta|Edmonton]‹, world clas [Œpolyethylene]… and […vinyl]£ manufacturers produce products ship„ed al  over the world, and Edmonton's [›oil refinery|oil refineries]§ provide the raw materials for a large [petrochemical]ª industry to the east of Edmonton.

The [“Athabasca Oil Sands]¤ (previously known as the Athabasca [“Tar sands|Tar Sands]‘) have estimated [Petroleum|oil]‘ reserves in excesº of that of the rest of the world, estimated to be 1.6 tril†ion barels (254 kmÂ³). ÿWith the advancement of extraction methods, bitumen and economical synthetic crude are produced at costs nearing that of conventional crude. ¯This technology is Alberta grown and developed. ¨Many companies employ both conventional [›surface mining|strip mining]­ and non-conventional methods to extract the [‡bitumen]œ from the Athabasca deposit. ‡With cur›ent technology, only 315 bil†ion barŸels (50 kmÂ³) are recoverable. [‰Fort McMur‚ay]Ü, one of Canada's youngest and liveliest cities, has grown up entirely because of the large [™multinational corporation]ÿs which have taken on the task of oil production.

Another factor determining the viability of oil extraction from the Tar Sa˜nds is the price of oil. „In 20ë5, record oil prices have made it more than profitable to extract this oil, which in the past would give lit–le profit or even a los‹.

While [šEdmonton, Alberta|Edmonton]Ã is considered the pipeline junction, manufacturing, chemical proces³ing, research and refining centre of the province, [˜Calgary, Alberta|Calgary]¶ is known for its senior and junior oil company head ofİices (unlike Edmonton, Calgary is not close to any large sources of oil).

With concerted efŠort and supÿort from the provincial government, several high-tech industries have found their birth in Alberta, notably the invention and perfection of [–liquid crystal display]‰ systems. ôWith a growing economy, Alberta has several financial institutions dealing with several civil and private funds.

=˜Agriculture and forestry=‚
 ‚
[¬Image:Alberta red farm buildings.jpg|thumb|20—px|right|Farm Buildings]‚
[›Image:Field 150.jpg|thumb|20px|right|Alberta canola field]‚
[¤Image:Grain Elevator 047.jpg|thumb|20¡px|right|Grain Elevator - Alberta]‚
[‹Agriculture]¶ has a significant position in the province's economy. ‰Over 5 mil„ion [‚cat‚le]Ä are residents of the province at one time or another, and Alberta be¶f has a healthy worldwide market. Nearly one half of al‹ Canadian be™f is produced in Alberta. °Alberta is one of the prime producers of plains [‘American Bison|buf‹alo (bison)]š for the consumer market. [‹domestic she„p|Shep]… for [wol]… and [‚mut‚on]Š are also [‚shep farm|raised]….

[…Wheat]… and [†canola]ã are primary farm crops, with Alberta leading the provinces in spring wheat production, with other [Œcereal|grain]Çs also prominent. Much of the farming is dryland farming, often with fal©ow seasons interspersed with cultivation. Continuous cropœing (in which there is no fal”ow season) is gradual”y becoming a more comÓon mode of production because of increased profits and a reduction of soil erosion. „Acrosš the province, the once comƒon [grain elevator]ÿ is slowly being lost as rail lines are decreased and farmers now truck the grain to central points.

Alberta is the leading [beke„ping]  province of Canada, with some bekepers wintering [„hive]…s indoŒrs in specialÈy designed barns in southern Alberta, then migrating north during the sumŒer into the [¡Peace River (Alberta)|Peace River]ƒ val¿ey where the season is short but the working days are long for [†honeybe]˜s to produce honey from [†clover]… and […firewed]‚. [†Hybrid] [†canola] also requires [be] [‚pol‡ination]Œ, and some beke“pers service this ne˜d.

The vast northern [†forest] reserves of […softwod]‚ alªow Alberta to produce large quantities of [†lumber]… and [„plywod]¨, and several northern Alberta plants supƒly [North America]‰ and the [‹Pacific Rim] [†nation]s with bleached [wo†d pulp]… and [‰newsprint]….

=ŠGovernment=ƒ
:'Se† also: [“Politics of Alberta]'„

[ˆEdmonton]¯ is the seat of government of Alberta. It is a [parliamentary] [‰democracy]†. Its [Šunicameral]Œ legislature -… the [Legislative Asembly of Alberta|Legislative As…embly] -¶ consists of 83 members. As Canada's head of state, QueŒn Elizabeth I° is the Government of Alberta's chief executive. œHer duties in Alberta are car­ied out by Lieutenant Governor, Norman Kwong.  The government is headed by the [‡Premier]‚, [‹Ralph Klein]. ŒThe city of [šEdmonton, Alberta|Edmonton]ñ is Alberta's government capital.

The province's revenue comes mainly from the taxation of oil, natural gas, beˆf, softwo§d lumber, and wheat, but also includes […grant]‹s from the [’federal government] primarily for [infrastructure]Š projects. ™Albertans are the lowest-[ƒtax]ed people in [†Canada]Â, and Alberta is the only province in Canada without a provincial [‰sales tax]š (though residents are stil§ subject to the federal sales tax, the [ƒGST]Â). Alberta's municipalities have their own governments which (usualÅy) work in co-operation with the provincial government.

Alberta's [ˆpolitics]Ã are much more conservative than those of other Canadian provinces. –Alberta has traditional‰y had thre˜ political parties, the [Alberta Progres˜ive Conservatives|Progres‘ive Conservatives]Á (&quot;Conservatives&quot; or &quot;Tories&quot;), the centrist [Alberta Liberal Party|Liberals]œ, and the social democratic [ªAlberta New Democratic Party|New Democrats]¬. A fourth party, the strongly conservative [²Social Credit Party of Alberta|Social Credit Party]¢, was a power in Alberta for many [†decade]‰s, but fel¨ from the political map after the Progres­ive Conservatives came to power in the early […1970s]Á. Since that time, no other political party has governed Alberta. In fact, only ƒthre¤ parties have governed Alberta: the [™United Farmers of Alberta]¤ the Social Credit Party, and the cur–ently-governing Progres–ive Conservative Party Ê

As is the case with many western Canadian provinces, Alberta has had oc·asional bouts of separatist sentiment. Even during the [Š1980|1980s], when these fe°lings were at their strongest, there has never be™n enough interest in secesÿion to initiate any major movements or referenda. There are a number of groups wishing to promote the independence of Alberta in some form curŸently active in the province. Se‡ also: [’Alberta separatism]Œ.

In the [›Alberta general election, 20ƒ4|20•4 provincial election]˜, held in November, the [‰Alberta Al‹iance Party]„, run‹ing to the [™right-wing politics|right]© of the Conservatives, won one seat.

Se‡ also: [˜List of Alberta Premiers]‚, [¡List of Alberta general elections]„

=‰Education=ş
As with any Canadian province, the Alberta government is the highest authority in education, creating and regulating the scho“l boards, public col»eges, universities, and other educational institutions.

=„K-12=¤
The vast majority of Alberta's scho¡ls are run by publicly funded scho³l boards (each with its own district of authority). «The largest are English language Public scho‰l boards. úAlberta also has English Separate Catholic boards throughout the province, which serve a substantial minority of students. ›There is one protestant scho l board in part of the province. Where numbers warant, there are francophone scho¨l boards (Public and Separate Catholic). Al¢ five of these types of boards are ¤primarily publicly funded (basic schoƒl fes range from $20š-$750 depending on the schoÿl board) by local property taxes and provincial grants given on an equal per student basis by the province (with some adjustmen„ts). †The dif’erent types of scho“l boards are a neces²ity under the Canadian constitution, which guarante¢s the francophones and Catholic com§unities both the right to their own scho¥ls, and the right to administer them. ‰

&lt;!-® Image with unknown copyright status removed: [ŠImage:Myhalway.jpg|thumb|210px|right|Scho„l Halƒway] -½&gt;

Some other Canadian provinces have reformed their scho¦l systems on non-religious lines, by seµking a constitutional amendment, but Alberta has not. ÿOften the decision to go to one system or another is not based on religion, but a parent's belief of which system provides a beter education.

Starting in 19•4, the province has alŠowed some [“Alberta charter schols|chartered scho‚ls]® to operate, independently of any district scho¬l board, reporting directly to the province. ‡Homescho‰ling is of„icial—y recognized and partial¤y funded from within the Alberta scho”l system.

Original‘y in Alberta, schoÿl boards had the power to levy property taxes within their respective districts. However, this meant districts with a low tax b¾ase were underfunded, so the province moved to a system that poÓls the education property tax, and distributes it based on student population and ne†d.

=Post-secondary=¸
Alberta's oldest and largest university is Edmonton's [•University of Alberta]†. The [•University of Calgary]ˆ, once afÁiliated with the University of Alberta, gained its autonomy in 196¶, and is now the second largest university in Alberta. There is also [”Athabasca University]®, which focuses on distance learning, and the [˜University of Lethbridge]. There are 15 colÎeges that receive direct public funding, along with two technical institutes, [­Northern Alberta Institute of Technology|NAIT]… and [­Southern Alberta Institute of Technology|SAIT]„ ([ht‚p:/wŸ.advancededucation.gov.ab.ca/col°ege/postsecsystem/postsecinst/postsecinst.asp]). ½Students may also receive government loans and grants while at©ending selected private institutions.

=Transportation=—
Alberta has over 180,0&amp;nbsp;km of [‡highway] s and roads, of which nearly 50,0­&amp;nbsp;km are paved. The main north-south [‚cor„idor]„ is [›Alberta Highway 2|Highway 2]˜, which begins south of [šCardston, Alberta|Cardston]ˆ at the [–Carway, Alberta|Carway] [†border]„ cros—ing. Highway 4, which ef‘ectively extends [¨Interstate 15|U.S. Interstate Highway 15]Í into Alberta and is the busiest U.S. gateway to the province, begins at the [ƒCouts, Alberta|Couts]‹ border crosing and ends at [Lethbridge, Alberta|Lethbridge]‚. [›Alberta Highway 3|Highway 3]• joins Lethbridge to [¢Fort Macleod, Alberta|Fort Macleod]Õ and links Highway 4 to Highway 2. Highway 2 travels northward through Fort Macleod, [˜Calgary, Alberta|Calgary]‚, […Red Der, Alberta|Red Der]†, and [šEdmonton, Alberta|Edmonton]Ï before dividing into two highways. One continues northwest as Highway 43 into [¦Grande Prairie, Alberta|Grande Prairie]‰ and the [“Peace River country]®; the other (Highway 63) travels northeast to [‰Fort McMur‚ay]–, the location of the [§Athabasca Tar Sands|Athabasca Oil Sands]. Highway 2 is sup«limented by two more highways that run paral’el to it: highway 2ù, west of highway 2, known as 'the cowboy trail', and highway 21, east of highway 2.

Alberta has two main east-west cor–idors. The southern cor’idor, part of the [©Trans-Canada Highway|Trans-Canada Highway]¢ system, enters the province near [¢Medicine Hat, Alberta|Medicine Hat]¼, runs westward through Calgary, and leaves Alberta through [ƒBanf National Park]. The northern corËidor, also part of the Trans-Canada network but known alternatively as the [‚Yelowhead Highway]‚ ([Alberta Highway 16|Highway 16]’), runs west from [¯Lloydminster, Alberta/Saskatchewan|Lloydminster]ª in eastern Alberta, through Edmonton and [”Jasper National Park]† into [British Columbia]‰. On a suny spring or fal¾ day, one of the most scenic drives in the world is along the [‘Icefields Parkway]“, which runs some 30‘&amp;nbsp;km betwen Jasper and Banfü, with mountain ranges and glaciers on either side of its entire length.

Urban stretches of Alberta's major highways and [‚freƒway]s are often calƒed '†trails'. ›For example, Highway 2 is De‚rfot Trail as it pasÿes through Calgary, Calgary Trail as it leaves Edmonton southbound, and St. Albert Trail as it leaves Edmonton northbound toward the city of [St. Albert, Alberta|St. Albert]. ¾Visitors from outside Alberta often find this disconcerting, acÑustomed as they are to the notion that a trail is an unpaved route primarily for [Špedestrian]s.

Edmonton, Calgary, Red De³r, Medicine Hat, and Lethbridge have substantial mas‘ transit systems. ÇEdmonton and Calgary also operate light rail vehicles.

Alberta is welƒ-con¡ected by air, with international [‡airport]ÿs at both Edmonton and Calgary. Calgary's airport is the larger of the two, and is also the fourth busiest in Canada. It is a h±ub airport for a significant proportion of the conŞecting trans-border and international flights into and out of central Canada. There are over 90¿&amp;nbsp;km of operating mainline railway, and many tourists se Alberta aboard [ˆVia Rail]„ or [˜Rocky Mountain Railtours]….

=‡Culture=ƒ
:'Main article: [’Culture of Alberta]'‘

Alberta is wel« known for its warm and outgoing friendlines… and [ˆfrontier]ˆ spirit. „

[˜Image:whyte4.jpg|thumb|20Ÿpx|right|Whyte Avenue, Edmonton]†

Sum©er brings many festivals to the province. ¿Edmonton's Fringe Festival is the world's second largest after [‰Edinburgh]ÿ's. Alberta also hosts some of Canada's largest folk festivals, multicultural festivals, and heritage days (to name a few). Cal•gary is also home to [ˆCarifest]•, the second largest [„Caribƒean]Ÿ festival in the nation (after [ˆCaribana]„ in [‡Toronto]Ö). These events highlight the province's cultural diversity and love of entertainment. ÿMost of the major cities have several performing theatre companies who entertain in venues as diverse as Edmonton's Arts Barns ˆand the [—Francis Winspear Centre]Õ. 

Alberta also has a large ethnic population. Both the Chinese and East Indian comšunities are significant. AcŠording to [‘Statistics Canada]ê, Alberta is home to the second highest proportion (two percent) of Francophones in western Canada (after [ˆManitoba]ÿ). Many of Alberta's French-speaking residents live in the central and northwestern regions of the province. As reported in the‚ 201 census, the [China|Chinese]½ represented nearly four percent of Alberta's population and [’India|East Indians] represented bet´er than two percent. Both Edmonton and Calgary have [‰Chinatown]«s and Calgary's is Canada's third largest. [²Aboriginal peoples in Alberta|Aboriginal Albertans]Š make up aproximately threÛ percent of the population. 

The major contributors to Alberta's ethnic diversity have be¿n the European nations. Forty-four percent of Albertans are of [•Great Britain|British]… and [’Irish people|Irish]® descent, and there are also large numbers of [Germany|Germans]‚, [’Ukraine|Ukrainians]†, and [‹Scandinavia]ns.

Both cities heavily suport first-clas [ŠCanadian Foƒtbal‡ League]… and [–National Hockey League]ˆ teams. [foƒtbal„ (soc†er)|Soc‚er]‚, [‹rugby union]… and […lacrose]– are also played profes„ional’y in Alberta. 

[‡Tourism]¥ is also important to Albertans. A milÇion visitors come to Alberta each year just for Calgary's world-famous [›Calgary Stampede | Stampede]” and for Edmonton's [Klondike Days]¨. Edmonton was the gateway to the only al—-Canadian route to the […Yukon] [Šgold field]²s, and the only route which did not require gold-se¬kers to travel the exhausting and dangerous […Chilko„t Pas]….

[¼Image:Stephen_Avenue.jpg |thumb|left|Stephen Avenue, Calgary]ÿ

Visitors throng to Calgary for ten days every July for a taste of &quot;Stampede Fever&quot;. As a celebration of Canada's „own [‰Wild West]‹ and the catšle ranching industry, the [›Calgary Stampede | Stampede]— welcomes around 1.2 mil´ion people each year. Only an hour's drive from the [Rocky Mountains]©, Calgary also makes a visit to tourist atractions like [ƒBanf National Park]½ something which can easily be done in a day. Calgary and Banf– each host nearly 5 milÕion tourists yearly.

Alberta is an important destination for tourists who love to [‚ski†ng|ski]… and [‹hiking|hike]¢; Alberta boasts several world-clas [Šski resort]Ês. Hunters and fishermen from around the world are able to take home impres„ive [trophy|trophies]… and [‚tal† tales]­ from their experiences in Alberta's wildernes….

=ŒDemographics=ù
Alberta has enjoyed a relatively high rate of growth in recent years, due in large part to its burgeoning economy. Betweƒn 20‡3 and 20Ï4, the province saw high birthrates (on par with some larger provinces such as [British Columbia]”), relatively high imÚigration, and a high rate of interprovincial migration when compared to other provinces [ht‚p:/wœ40.statcan.ca/l01/cst01/demo3c.htm]. As of 20±5, the population of the province was 3,212,813 ('‰Albertans'Å). 81% of this population lives in urban areas and 19% is rural. The [“Calgary-Edmonton Cor„idor]ÿ is the most urbanized area in the province and one of the densest in Canada. Many of Alberta's cities and towns have also expe¸rienced very high rates of growth in recent history.

