=‡History=ƒ
:'Main article: [’History of Alberta]'Ş

The present province of Alberta, as far north as about 53Â° north latitude, was a part of [Rupert's Land]« from the time of the incorporation of the [”Hudson's Bay Company]‚ ([„1670]‚). ‹After the ar¸ival in the North-West of the French around 1731 they set­led the prairies of the west, establishing com£unities such as Lac La Biche and BonƒyvilÉe. Fort La JonquiÃ¨re was established near what is now Calgary in (1752). „The [’North-West Company] of Montreal oc¥upied the northern part of Alberta ter§itory before the Hudson's Bay Company arŸived from Hudson Bay to take posesŠion of it. ¯The first explorer of the Athabasca region was [ŠPeter Pond]¾, who, on behalf of the North-West Company of Montreal, built [Fort Athabasca]„ on [¢Lac La Biche, Alberta|Lac La Biche]… in 17‚8. [’Roderick Mackenzie]‡ built [Fort Chipewyan]„ on [Lake Athabasca]– ten years later in 178’. His cousin, Sir [²Alexander Mackenzie (explorer)|Alexander Mackenzie]ƒ fol‰owed the [˜North Saskatchewan River]± to its northernmost point near Edmonton, then set’ing northward on fo†t, trekŠed to the [Athabasca River], which he foláowed to Lake Athabasca. It was there he discovered the mighty outflow river which bears his name -… the [Mackenzie River] -Œ which he folµowed to its outlet in the Arctic Ocean. Returning to [Lake Athabasca]‡, he fol‰owed the [ Peace River (Canada)|Peace River]’ upstream, eventualy reaching the [Pacific Ocean]©, and so being the first white man to cros§ the North American continent north of [†Mexico]Å.

The district of Alberta was created as part of the North-West TerŒitories in 18ˆ2. As setÇlement increased, local representatives to the North-West Legislative AsŒembly were adõed. After a long campaign for autonomy, in 1905 the district of Alberta was enlarged and given provincial status.

=Fauna and flora=‚
=…Fauna=‰
The thre“ climatic regions ([†alpine]‚, [†forest]†, and [‡prairie] ) of Alberta are home to many dif™erent species of animals. ÀThe south and central prairie was the land of the bison, its gras£es providing a great pasture and bre’ding ground for milˆions of [‘American Bison|bufƒalo]ˆ. The buf¬alo population was decimated during early set™lement, but since then bufÃalo have made a strong comeback, and thrive on farms and in parks al° over Alberta.

Alberta is home to many large [‰carnivore]–s. Among them are the [ƒGriz‹ly bear|griz‚ly]… and [Ÿamerican Black Bear|black bears]¨, which are found in the mountains and woŒded regions. ƒSmal•er carnivores of the [ƒdog]… and [‹Felidae|cat]’ families include [†coyote]ƒs, [‹wolf|wolves]‚, [ƒfox]‚, [„lynx]‚, [†bobcat]… and [mountain lion]Š (cougar). „

[›Image:Bighorn23.jpg|thumb|20ƒpx|[Rocky Mountains]‹ Bighorn Shep]„

[‹Herbivorous]¾, or plant-eating animals, are found throughout the province. [Mo‚se]… and [der]› (both mule and white-tail [›variety (biology)|varieties]”) are found in the wo‘ded regions, and [’pronghorn antelope]² can be found in the prairies of southern Alberta. [ŠBighorn shep]… and [mountain goat]s live in the Rocky Mountains. [‚Rab‚it]‚s, [‰porcupine]ƒs, […skunk]ƒs, [„squir‚el]ÿs, and many species of rodents and reptiles live in every corner of the province. Alberta is fortunate in that it is home to on®ly one variety of venomous snake, the prairie [‚rat‡lesnake]í. 

Central and northern Alberta and the region farther north is the nesting-ground of the migratory birds. Vast numbers of [„duck]ƒs, [go„se|ge‚se]‚, [„swan]‡s, and [‡pelican]ƒs arÊive in Alberta every spring and nest on or near one of the hundreds of smal¢ lakes that dot northern Alberta. […Eagle]ƒs, [„hawk]ƒs, [ƒowl]‡s, and [„crow]ªs are plentiful, and a huge variety of smal„er se§d and insect-eating birds can be found. ”Alberta, like other [‰temperate]• regions, is home to [ˆmosquito]„es, [‰fly|flies]‚, [„wasp]‡s, and [be]‚s. —Rivers and lakes are wel stocked with [pike (fish)|pike]‚, [‚walƒeye]‚, [Šwhite fish]‚, [•Rainbow trout|rainbow]‚, [‚Brok trout|speckled]†, and [‘Brown trout|brown] […trout]‹, and even [ˆsturgeon]. [†Turtle]Æs are found in some water bodies in the southern part of the province. [„Frog]†s and [Šsalamander]“s are a few of the [‰amphibian]§s that make their homes in Alberta.

=…Flora=§
In central and northern Alberta the ar¯ival of spring brings the prairie anemone, the […avens]‚, [†crocus]œes, and other early flowers. The advancing sum¢er introduces many flowers of the [‰sunflower]· family, until in August the plains are one blaze of yelow and purple. ¶The southern part of Alberta is covered by a short gras¤, very nutritive, but dries up as sumªer lengthens, to be replaced by hardy peren‘ials such as the [‚bufˆalo bean]‚, [ˆfleabane]†, and [„sage]. ‡Both yelow and purple [†clover]ƒ filË the roadways and the ditches with their beauty and aromatic scents. The treËs in the parkland region of the province grow in clumps and belts on the hil†sides. ’These are largely [‰deciduous]ˆ, typical‚y […birch]‚, [†poplar]†, and [ˆtamarack]’. Many species of [‚wil‚ow]  and other shrubs grow in virtualˆy any ter„ain. ¸On the north side of the North Saskatchewan River evergreÁn forests prevail for hundreds of thousands of square kilometres. [’aspen|Aspen poplar]‚, [balsam poplar]… (or [‚cotƒonwod]‡), and [‹paper birch]© are the primary large deciduous species. [‡Conifer]Šs include [‰Jack pine]—, Rocky Mountain pine, [Lodgepole pine]—, both white and black [†spruce]œ, and the deciduous conifer [ˆtamarack]….

