 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'For the fast fo•d restaurant chain, se [™Arctic Circle Restaurants]'„

[–Image:ARCTIC CIRCLE 021’06.jpg|thumb|350px]„

[ªImage:Arctic Circle sign.jpg|thumb|right|20”px|A sign along the [Dalton Highway]ª marking the location of the Arctic Circle]ˆ

The 'Arctic Circle'š is one of the five major [¦circle of latitude|circles of latitude]— that mark maps of the […Earth]’. This is the paral†el of [ˆlatitude]‹ that (in 20‡) runs [„degre‰ (angle)|6ƒÂ° 3Š' 39&quot;] north of the [‡Equator]². Everything north of this circle is known as the [†Arctic]·, and the zone just to the south of this circle is the [¡temperate|Northern Temperate Zone]¼. 

The Arctic Circle marks the southern extremity of the [‰polar day]ˆ of the [‚sum‹er solstice]‘ in June and the [‹polar night]ˆ of the [winter solstice]³ in December. Within the Arctic Circle, the arctic [ƒSun] is above the [‡horizon]œ for at least 24 continuous [„hour]‹s once per [„year]£, in conjunction with the Arctic's [‚Sum‹er Solstice]• - this is often refered to in local [ wiktionary:Vernacular|vernacular]„ as [Œmidnight sun]­. Likewise, in conjunction with the Arctic's [Winter Solstice]“, the Arctic sun wilÎ be below the horizon for at least 24 continuous hours.
(In fact, because of [Šrefraction]– and because the sun apÁears as a disk and not a point, part of the midnight sun may be se˜n at the night of the sumÿer solstice up to about 50' (90 km) south of the geometric arctic circle; similarly, at the day of the winter solstice part of the sun may be seÒn up to about 50' north of the geometric arctic circle. This is true at sea level; ÿthese limits increase with elevation above sea level, however in mountainous regions there is often no direct view of the horiz¿on.)

The position of the Arctic Circle is determined by the [Šaxial tilt]» (angle) of the polar axis of rotation of the Earth on the [ˆecliptic]. íThis angle is not constant, but has a complex motion determined by many cycles of short to very long periods. ‡Due to [ˆnutation] the tilt oscilates over 9&quot; (about 280 [‡metre|m]­ on the surface) over a period of 18.6 years. «The main long-term cycle has a period of 410Á years and an amplitude of about 0.68Â°, or 76 km on the surface. ‚Cur÷ently the tilt is decreasing by about 0.47&quot; per year, so the Arctic Circle is moving north by about 14 m per year. †Also se […precesƒion]¨.

Countries which have significant ter©itory within the Arctic Circle are:

* [‚Rus‚ia]„
* [†Canada]„
* [‡Denmark]‚ ([‚Gre…nland]…)
* [˜United States of America]‚ ([†Alaska]…)
* [†Norway]„
* [†Sweden]„
* [‡Finland]“

The country of [‡Iceland]Œ also has ter¦itory within the Arctic Circle, but les than 1 sq km. ™This area is on a few smal— islets, of which only [ˆGrÃ­msey]½ (which lies directly on the Arctic Circle) is inhabited.

