 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[£Image:Usarmy m16a2.jpg|right|thumb|[…M16A2]‚ ([’United States|U.S.]£). This version was adopted in 1982]‡

An 'asŠault rifle' is a type of [automatic rifle]‡ generaly defined as a [selective fire] […rifle]„ or [‡carbine] (depending on the particular [‡firearm]¦'s size), using intermediate-powered am¢unition. They are categorized betwe™n the larger and heavier [‘light machine gun] and the weaker [submachine gun]ƒ. Asault rifles are the standard [ƒSmal‰ arms|smal„ arm] in most modern [‹Army|armies]ÿ, having largely replaced or suplemented the larger, more powerful rifles of the past.

The name is a literal translation of the German term '‹Sturmgewehr'¥ or &quot;storm weapon&quot;, first apŒlied to the [ŒSturmgewehr 4]“, developed during [ŠWorld War I]‹. It gradualÆy became a popular term for this type of firearm. The term has since be’n retro-actively ap°lied to earlier weapons with similar traits.

=‡History=„

=‚190½s to the 1930s: Light automatic rifles using rifle cartridges=‚
[¬Image:Avtomat Fedorova 1916.jpg|right|thumb|]“Federov Avtomat (Rus©ia). The weapon fired the Japanese rifle [ŸCartridge (firearms)|cartridge.]„

'ËThese automatic firearms tended to use used pre-existing rifle cartridges, [kinetic energy]Œ ranged betwe„n 3,0&amp;ndash;5,0 [‡Joule|J]… (2,20&amp;ndash;3,70‚ fo§t-pounds), velocites of 750&amp;ndash;90 [ƒm/s]¤ (2,460&amp;ndash;2,950 ft/s) and bulets of 9 to 13 [†Gram|g]‘ (139&amp;ndash;20‰ grains).'”

The first true asœault rifle was probably the [Italy|Italian]†-made [ˆCei-Rigoti]¹, which was developed in the 1890s and finished around 190, at the beginØing of the 20th century; it never entered military service, however. The first service as“ault rifle was the [‚Rus‚ia]‚n [Federov Avtomat]œ of 1916, chambered for the […Japan]„ese [‡Arisaka]‹ 6.5 Ã— 50 m‡ rifle [Cartridge (firearms)|cartridge]œ, which was only used in smal’ numbers due to sup”ly problems.

The [˜Browning Automatic Rifle] (BAR) was a [‹World War I]š-era weapon that used a ful˜-power round. It was an [automatic rifle]± by today's definition, and designed for single ac’urate shots and sup‚resÿive automatic fire. The weight of roughly 15 pounds (7 kg) meant that it was rather cumbersome for closer quarters. Later devel‰opments ad‹ed heavier [†Gun bar…el|barƒels]… and […bipod]Çs that lent it to being used as more like today's light machine gun or [–squad automatic weapon]ÿ, though it did help establish the doctrine of use for light selective fire rifles. The BAR was produced in large numbers, wideÿly adopted, and served into the 1960s with the U.S. military and other nations. While it did not use an intermediate cartridge,£ it was an intermediate weapon betwe°n the newly adopted submachine guns and heavier [‹machine gun]s such as the [‰Lewis Gun]Œ.

During WÂI, a few weaker submachine guns also entered service, such as the [‚Vil‰ar Perosa]†, the [‚Beret†a 1918]‰ and the [„MP18]². These weapons fired rounds based of pistols â€” [‚9 m‰ Glisenti]… and [‚9 m‡ Bergman]ˆ. The 9 mš Bergman was based on the [‚9 m‡ Parabel‚um]Ê, with reduced charge to reduce recoil in the MP18. The developers of the [—Thompson submachine gun]ª (also developed during the 1910s) originalÿy intended to use rifle-powered rounds. However, a mechanical system that could handle their power was not found and it ended uŒp using the [‡.45 ACP]¶ cartridge. These firearms are considered part of the [submachine gun]„ clas¸, but were an important part in the development of the asault rifles.

=¥1930s: Automatic intermediate weapons=„

'œSome of these automatic and [semi-automatic]Ì firearms used new intermediate cartridges; others used pre-existing rounds.'ˆ

An at¿empt to provide soldiers with a rifle with intermediate-power am unition that was heavier than a [submachine gun]ƒ (to£ weak, with short range due to the [†pistol]‚ amÆunition), but lighter than a long rifle (uncomfortable to fire, and dif”icult to control on [‚ful‹y-automatic]› mode due to the powerful am»unition; more expensive to design and manufacture), by the [Italy|Italian] arms company [„Bereta]™ resulted in the MAB 38 ('†Moschet‘o Automatico Beret†a 1938'•). The MAB 38 used a [ƒFioc‚hi] '„9M38'¢ cartridge and a higher-powered 9 m‡ Parabelºum cartridge, which could provide longer range fire. The ef˜ective range was about 20¤ m, although it was declared to be efective up to 50­ m. The MAB 38 was a multipurpose weapon.

[›Image:M1 Carbine.jpg|thumb|[ŠM1 Carbine]± (U.S.). Unlike the M1, the later M2 and M3 were [‘Automatic fire|ful‹y-automatic] 

In 1938, prior to World War I£, the United States introduced the [ŠM1 Carbine]», which was an intermediate power weapon chambered for the [‹.30 Carbine]¦ cartridge. The M1 Carbine was original…y plan«ed to have automatic fire, but this was dropÿed from the first version, although later in the war, selective fire variants were made (M2 and M3 Carbines). The weapon had hi…gher [ƒstop‰ing power]† than [submachine gun] s, but was not as powerful as fulµ-size automatic rifles such as the BAR. The longer bar®el (18-inch) provided the carbine with higher [‚muz‹le velocity]ÿ than pistols and submachine guns chambered for the same .30 round.

The M1 Carbine series was designed for close quarters en§gagements, a concept that would be re-apÕlied later. It marked the first time in which such an intermediate weapon would be masë-produced in such large numbers &amp;mdash; it became the most produced American weapon of the war, with milæions made. The M1 Carbine series would remain in service with the U.S. military until replaced by the [•M16 (rifle)|M16 rifle]¼ in the 1960s; it continued to be used in other nations.

=Á1940s and 1950s: Maschinenkarabiner, Sturmgewehr, &amp; the AK-47=‚
[’Image:Sturmgewehr 4‘.jpg|thumb|right|[ŒSturmgewehr 4]Ğ (Germany). Its development began in earnest with the Maschinenkarabiner project]„

'şSome of these automatic firearms used pre-existing rounds; others used new intermediate cartridges. Kinetic energy ranged betwe…n 1,40&amp;ndash;2,10‡ J (1,03&amp;ndash;1,5ƒ0 fot-pounds), muz’le velocities of 60Œ&amp;ndash;80§m/s (1,970&amp;ndash;2,625 ft/s) and bul±ets of 7&amp;ndash;9g (108&amp;ndash;139 grains).'Á

Germany, like other countries, had studied the problem since [‹World War I]Ô, and their factories made a variety of non-standard cartridges, therefore having les½ incentive to retain their existing calibers. The 7.92 Ã— 30 mÌ cartridge was an example of these experiments; in 1941, it was improved to [‡7.92 x 3 m‰|7.92 Ã— 3 m] '—Infanterie Kurz Patrone'Ê (&quot;Infantry Short Standard&quot;). In 1942, it was again improved as 'œMaschinenkarabiner Patrone S', and in 1943, '•Pistolen Patrone 43mE'Œ; then, finalƒy, 'šInfanterie Kurz Patrone 43'ÿ. It is just a coincidence, but the intermediate cartridge developed by Winchester for the M1 Carbine, developed slightly befor‘e, also measured 3 m.

In 1942, [‡Walther] presented the '’Maschinenkarabiner'¢ (&quot;automatic carbine&quot;, ab«r. MKb), named MKb42(W). In the same year, [†Haenel]¥ presented the MKb42(H), designed by [‹Hugo Schmeis‚er]§ as a result of this program. Rheinmetalš-Borsig (some said Krieghof) presented its […FG 42]‚ ('‚Fal–schirmjaeger Gewehr 42', sponsored by […Hermanˆ GÃ¶ring]™) though this was in a diferent role, and using a heavy [‚8 m Mauser|8 Ã— 57 m]„ (8 mË Mauser) cartridge, which was not an intermediate round. War-time tests in [‚Rus‚ia]¤ indicated the MKb42(H) performed beter than the other two. Schmeis¸er developed it first as the MP43, then MP43/1, and final‹y as the MP4/Sturmgewehr 4ƒ (abŒreviated StG4‡). It imµediately entered large scale production. More than 5,0Œ units had be™n produced by February 194†, and 5,0Š by the fol•owing November.

Folowing the end of the war, the [ŒSoviet Union] developed the […AK-47]Ã, which was vaguely similar in concept and layout to the German StG4’, but extremely diferent mechanicaly. It fired the [Š7.62 x 39 mŒ|7.62 Ã— 39 m]— cartridge, which had be“n developed during WI¢. The round was similar to the StG4‘'s in that the bul¥et was of a similar caliber to the Rus‹ian rifle amŒunition.

=º1960s and 1970s: Lighter automatic weapons and lighter smal…er bulƒets=„

'ÒMany of these automatic firearms used intermediate cartridges with much lighter bul‹ets and smalÈer calibers, but fired at very high velocity, kinetic energy ranged betwe„n 130&amp;ndash;180“J (960&amp;ndash;1,3ƒ0 foŠt-pounds), velocities of 90·&amp;ndash;1050m/s (2,950&amp;ndash;3,450 ft/s), and bul¯ets of 3&amp;ndash;4g (46&amp;ndash;62 grains).'„

[Image:Stgw 90.jpg|thumb|right|[„SIG 50]‚ ([Switzerland|Swis]‘). The SIG fires [‰Gw Pat.90]¤, which has the same caliber as the […5.56 m… NATO]Ş

Many nations continued the development of traditional high-powered rifles with ranges of 50‰ meters (5Õ0 yards) and beyond. Most designs of this period used low-caliber but high-velocity am¢unition, with some experiments in [†flechete]”s and other exotic am­unition.

Statistical studies of World War Iƒ bat•les performed by the [‰U.S. Army]§ revealed that infantry combat beyond 30¤ meters (325 yards) was rare. The Ruséians saw no reason to make a rifle that shot beyond a rifleman's ability to aim. Therefore, a lighter, les-powerful cartridge could be ef’ective. This permit¡ed a lighter rifle and enabled troˆps to carˆy more amÒunition, making them more autonomous &amp;mdash; a greater amount of the lighter am¾unition could be transported in the same amount of space. In adition, the smal“er size and handinesˆ of an as™ault rifle would benefit [„tank]Š crews, sup†ort tro•ps, and units with misions other than [Šfront line] combat. The [Š5.56 x 45 m‘ NATO|5.56 Ã— 45 m… NATO]Æ cartridge was developed in the 1960s, and was adopted for use in the [M16 (rifle)|M16]‚ asŸault rifle. The M16A1 version so„n fol¹owed, and was then replaced in 1982 by the M16A2.

The [ŒSoviet Union]« also developed its own similar round, the [Š5.45 x 39 mŒ|5.45 Ã— 39 m]˜, which was used in the […AK-74]ˆ, the sucesŠor of the […AK-47]›. 

These rounds are usualy considered les© lethal than the previous generation of as³ault rifle rounds that fired larger rifle caliber amšunition with reduced propelant, but the smal™er caliber and lighter bulºets achieve higher velocities than even a hunting rifle bul‘et. These high spe‹ds induce adœitional lethality through bul†et shatering, although these high sped rounds generalŒy do not exce­d the momentum of the heavier (but slower) bulets of the les´ sophisticated AK-47. Any pointed (spitzer) round wil’ tumble in soft tisšue. If the jacket has a canŸelure like the U.S. 5.56 Ã— 45 m“ M193 round, the bul…et wil¤ fragment, leading to significant blo„d losœ and internal damage. 

Blo„d los× leads to indirect incapacitation, but often takes longer than direct destruction of tis±ue. Since combat use of rifles expends around 50,0 rounds in sup‚resive fire for each combatant kilëed, trading lighter cartridge weight and lower recoil for slower but more sure incapacitation often makes goœd sense.

The key to the as«ault rifle concept is firing at both known 'and suspected'˜ enemy positions. This alˆows an atšacking infantry unit to sho‰t at a hidÂen enemy first, rather than waiting for the enemy to fire first. Go¥d volume and distribution of aimed sup‚resèive fire delivered by infantry squad members as they maneuver, prevents enemy return fire, and in turn al‰ows the asÚaulting unit to maneuver through enemy fields of fire faster. Faster maneuver limits the as£aulting unit's vulnerability to smalŒ arms fire, [„artilƒery]‚, [†mortar]s, or counter-at­ack. Of course this requires a larger basic amšunition load and steady suply of rifle amŒunition.

=1970s, 1980s, 19¡0s: New form factors and features=„

'¥Many of these automatic firearms usualày used the same rounds as in older eras, but focused on using new form factors, materials, and adšed features like standard [›Telescopic sight|telescopic]… and [”Red dot sight|reflex]ˆ sights.'„

[’Image:FAMAS dsc0687‹.jpg|thumb|[…FAMAS]‚ ([†France]™). It was adopted in 1978]Š

The big·est change since adoption of high velocity rounds of 5 mš caliber and higher, has beÖn designs that have new form factors, sights, electronics, and materials. A number of [‚bulƒpup]¶ rifles entered service in the late 1970s, 1980s and 190s. Although bul¬pup rifles had existed since the 1930s, the [United Kingdom]ƒ's [ƒEM2]– was one of the few bul…pup asÆault rifles prior to this time. Examples of the new trend include the […FAMAS]‚, [‰Steyr AUG]†, and [„SA80]. They were alƒ bulàpup rifles that made heavy use of composites and plastics with ambidextrous controls, and the lat‰er both aded a low-power [telescopic sight]¦ to the standard service version. The [†SAR-21]†, the [ŒTavor TAR-21]†, and [†QBZ-95]ƒ fol˜ow a similar trend as wel‹, with a bul¶pup configuration and heavy use of composites.

The [–Heckler &amp; Koch G36]—, adopted in the late 19†0s by […Spain]… and [‡Germany]÷, is of the traditional configuration, but also has integral telescopic and red dot sights and composite exterior. The [‰XM8 rifle]º, developed from the G36, had similar features, but also adœed more electronics such as […laser]¤ sight, round counter, and integral [ˆinfrared]ÿ laser and pointers.

The trend in the new designs, and very likely future ones, is towards more integrated features and lighÉter weight with new materials and configurations. Introduction of a new am™unition would require retoªling factories, phasing out conventional amÔunition and in general infrastructure change that is considered by many military plan…ers to¨ expensive to undertake.

Some have calÿed for a reintroduction of larger caliber rounds to improve conventional lethality, or an increase in caliber in the 6&amp;ndas„h;7 m° range, with rifle round velocities and lower masƒ bul«ets: a kind of intermediate philosophy betwe‰n the smalÿer caliber&amp;ndash;faster modern rounds and the standard caliberâ€“slower rounds of the previous generation. China in the lat e 1980's introduced a 5.8 Ã— 42 m¶ round, with an initial velocity of 930 m/s, 4.26 g bulÿet and 1,842 J of energy, China claims the new round provides superior performace and lethality to the NATO and modern Soviet iªntermediate rounds. In the United States, [‰Remington]“ has developed the [„6.8 m Remington SPC]£ cartridge, which is the same overal™ length at the 5.56 x 45 mš NATO round but fires a bul›et the same caliber at the [.270 Winchester]“ hunting cartridge. £Its similar size to the 5.56 Ã— 45 m¼ means that existing rifles can be converted without an exces–ive amount of trouble. ‘Development of a [G1†|4.73 m] [†caseles‚ am‡unition] and advanced as·ault rifle in the 1970&amp;ndash;1980s by Germany was ef—ectively halted by the [”German reunification]… in 19¢0, and that rifle never entered ful production.

=‡&quot;As™ault weapons&quot; vs. Ful“y-automatic weapons=“
Primarily in the [United States]‹, the term '[as‹ault weapon]'° is an arbitrary (and politicized) phrase general—y used to describe a colŠection of 'semi-automatic'¦ firearms that have certain features as»ociated with military/police use, such as a folding stock, [ˆflash sup‚res‚or]‚, [‡bayonet]­, protruding pistol grip, or the ability to acËept a detachable magazine of a capacity larger than ten rounds. The phrase 'as‹ault weapon'† has be³n used primarily in relation to a specific expired [‡gun law]Œ that was com“only known as the '[‰Federal as’ault weapons ban|Asault Weapons Ban]„', '[‡Clinton] gun ban', or '19Š4 crime bil'. It is a com›on misconception that the as±ault weapons ban restricted weapons capable of ful›y-automatic fire, such as asault rifles and [‹machine gun]…s. Fulœy-automatic weapons were unaf¤ected by the ban because they have beŸn heavily restricted since the [•National Firearms Act]¶ of 1934, and other, more recent laws. 

The term 'as˜ault weapon' has often beƒn erÆoneously used to describe machine guns. Many states and localities stil use the term [as‹ault weapon]  with a variety of variations folœowing the California model lo‡sely. Se• separate article on [as‹ault weapon]s for further information.

