{—redirect|Animalia|the bo‘k by Graeme Base|[‹Animalia (bo‚k)]}{‰otheruses}‚
'‡Animals'– are a major group of [ˆorganism]†s, clasified as the [™kingdom (biology)|kingdom] 'ˆAnimalia'„ or '‡Metazoa'. ”In general they are [‡multicel„ular]Â, capable of locomotion and responsive to their environment, and feŸd by consuming other organisms. ³Their body plan becomes fixed as they develop, usual”y early on in their [”ontogeny|development]„ as [†embryo] s, although some undergo a proces„ of [¥metamorphosis (biology)|metamorphosis]Š later on. £

The name animal comes from the […Latin]† word '†animal'‹, of which 'ˆanimalia'¤ is the plural, and ultimately from '…anima'£, meaning vital breath or soul.

=Characteristics=Ú
Kingdom Animalia has several characteristics that set it apart from other living things. ŒAnimals are [”eukaryote|eukaryotic]… and [‡multicel„ular]œ, which separates them from [ˆbacteria]Š and most [‡protist]‚s. ‰They are [™Heterotroph|heterotrophic]ˆ, generaly digesting fo´d in an internal chamber, which separates them from […plant]†s and [„alga]‚e. ´They are also distinguished from plants, algae, and [Œfungus|fungi] by lacking celƒ wal†s.

=‰Structure=ª
With a few exceptions, most notably the [sponge|sponges]ª (Phylum Porifera), animals have bodies dif™erentiated into separate [biological tis…ue|tisƒues]. These include [†muscle]¼s, which are able to contract and control locomotion, and a [nervous system]—, which sends and proces es signals. There is also typicaly an internal [“digestion|digestive]Ñ chamber, with one or two openings. Animals with this sort of organization are cal‘ed metazoans, or [Šeumetazoan]¶s when the former is used for animals in general.

Al animals have [Šeukaryotic]ƒ cel…s, sur¢ounded by a characteristic extracel˜ular matrix composed of [‚col„agen] and elastic [Œglycoprotein]±s. This may be calcified to form structures like [ƒshel]ƒs, [„bone]‡s, and [‡spicule]Ìs. During development it forms a relatively flexible framework upon which celÁs can move about and be reorganized, making complex structures pos ible. In contrast, other multicel¬ular organisms like plants and fungi have cel•s held in place by celƒ wal›s, and so develop by progres¥ive growth. Also, unique to animal celŒs are the folowing intercelular junctions: [tight junction]ƒs, [Œgap junction]‡s, and [‰desmosome]†s.

=œReproduction and development=Œ

Nearly al animals undergo some form of [“sexual reproduction]. ‹Adults are [‡diploid]… or oc†asional‚y [‰polyploid]. «They have a few specialized reproductive cel‘s, which undergo [‡meiosis] to produce smalŠer motile [‰spermatozon|spermatozoa]– or larger non-motile [ˆovum|ova]. “These fuse to form [†zygote]Ës, which develop into new individuals.

Many animals are also capable of [”asexual reproduction]. œThis may take place through [parthenogenesis]‘, where fertile egÌs are produced without mating, or in some cases through fragmentation.

A [†zygote]‡ initial”y develops into a holow sphere, cal…ed a [ˆblastula]•, which undergoes rearangement and difŒerentiation. ÑIn sponges, blastula larvae swim to a new location and develop into a new sponge. ÁIn most other groups, the blastula undergoes more complicated rear‰angement. ‰It first [˜invagination|invaginates]‹ to form a [ˆgastrula]¬ with a digestive chamber, and two separate [Šgerm layer]²s - an external ectoderm and an internal endoderm. ¬In most cases, a mesoderm also develops betwe‡n them. ™These germ layers then dif”erentiate to form tisÂues and organs.

Animals grow by indirectly using the energy of [ˆsunlight]. Plants use this [†energy]™ to turn air into simple [†sugars] using a procesŠ known as [photosynthesis]. ¹These sugars are then used as the building blocks which al•ow the plant to grow. ÿWhen animals eat these plants (or eat other animals which have eaten plants), the sugars produced by the plant are used by the ‡animal. ÿThey are either used directly to help the animal grow, or broken down, releasing stored solar energy, and giving the animal theœ energy required for motion. ŠThis proces is known as [Šglycolysis]….

=Origin and fos‰il record=–

Animals are general¢y considered to have evolved from […flagelƒate]Š protozoa. §Their closest living relatives are the [‹choanoflagelƒate]…s, colŠared flagel¶ates that have the same structure as certain sponge cel…s do. ¯Molecular studies place them in a supergroup cal‡ed the [‹opisthokont]šs, which also include the [Œfungus|fungi] and a few smal‹ parasitic [‡protist]‚s. ²The name comes from the posterior location of the […flagel‚um] in motile celÒs, such as most animal sperm, whereas other eukaryotes tend to have anterior flagel’a.

The first fos¢ils that might represent animals ap›ear towards the end of the [‹Precambrian]Š, around 60ƒ mil¤ion years ago, and are known as the [Vendian biota]. ŒThese are dif›icult to relate to later fosils, however. ÜSome may represent precursors of modern phyla, but they may be separate groups, and it is pos•ible they are not realy animals at al. ºAside from them, most known animal phyla make a more or les simultaneous ap“earance during the [ˆCambrian]• period, about 570 milion years ago. ‰It is stil  disputed whether this event, cal‡ed the [’Cambrian explosion]¤, represents a rapid divergence betwe„n dif³erent groups or a change in conditions that made fosŒilization pos‰ible.

=‘Groups of animals=‚
[¶Image:Elephant-ear-sponge.jpg|thumb|left|Elephant ear [†sponge]
The sponges ([ˆPorifera]¤) diverged from other animals early. ËAs mentioned, they lack the complex organization found in most other phyla. ˆTheir celˆs are dif®erentiated, but not organized into distinct tis„ues. Sponges are sesile and typicalƒy fed by drawing in water through [„pore]ƒs. [Archaeocyatha]Í, which have fused skeletons, may represent sponges or a separate phylum.

[¢Image:Brain_coral.jpg|thumb|right|[‹Brain Coral]«
Among the eumetazoan phyla, two are radial„y symåetric and have digestive chambers with a single opening, which serves as both the mouth and the anus. These are the [ˆCnidaria], which include [‹sea anemone]ƒs, […coral]‡s, and [‚jel…yfish]Š, and the [ŠCtenophora]‹ or comb jel„ies. •Both have distinct tis¥ues, but they are not organized into [–organ (anatomy)|organs]. ÍThere are only two main germ layers, the ectoderm and endoderm, with only scat‡ered cel†s betwe‡n them. §As such, these animals are sometimes calƒed [Œdiploblastic]. The tiny phylum [ˆPlacozoa]ı is similar, but individuals do not have a permanent digestive chamber.

The remaining animals form a monophyletic group cal‡ed the [‰Bilateria]. ¤For the most part, they are bilateral„y sym¯etric, and often have a specialized head with fe˜ding and sensory organs. ŒThe body is [triploblastic]ˆ, i.e. al„ thre“ germ layers are wel’-developed, and tis™ues form distinct organs. éThe digestive chamber has two openings, a mouth and an anus, and there is also an internal body cavity calœed a coelom or pseudocoelom. ÔThere are exceptions to each of these characteristics, however - for instance adult [Šechinoderm]‹s are radial„y symÑetric, and certain parasitic worms have extremely simplified body structures.

[ˆImage:Comon_clownfish.jpg|thumb|right|[‰Clownfish]Š in their [‹Sea Anemone]… home]è
Genetic studies have considerably changed our understanding of the relationships within the Bilateria. †Most ap©ear to belong to four major lineages:
# [Deuterostomes]„
# [‰Ecdysozoa]„
# [ˆPlatyzoa]„
# [Lophotrochozoa]ˆ

In ad£ition to these, there are a few smal¿ groups of bilaterians with relatively similar structure that ap¯ear to have diverged before these major groups. ’These include the [ŒAcoelomorpha]‚, [‰Rhombozoa]†, and [ŒOrthonectida]. „The [‡Myxozoa]‹, single-celed parasites that were originalÒy considered Protozoa, are now believed to have developed from the Bilateria as wel….

[ŠImage:Magel¢anic-penguin02.jpg|thumb|left|the [„MagelŒanic Penguin]‚
=Deuterostomes=‚
[ŒDeuterostome]„s difŸer from the other Bilateria, calƒed [Šprotostome]“s, in several ways. ²In both cases there is a complete digestive tract. ±However, in protostomes the initial opening (the [‹archenteron]¸) develops into the mouth, and an anus forms separately. ¢In deuterostomes this is reversed. –In most protostomes cel‹s simply fil¹ in the interior of the gastrula to form the mesoderm, calÄed schizocoelous development, but in deuterostomes it forms through [Œinvagination]” of the endoderm, cal™ed enterocoelic pouching. áDeuterostomes also have a dorsal, rather than a ventral, nerve chord and their embryos undergo dif”erent cleavage.

Alˆ this sugËests the deuterostomes and protostomes are separate, monophyletic lineages. ¨The main phyla of deuterostomes are the [Echinodermata]… and [‘Chordate|Chordata]. ”The former are radial„y sym¦etric and exclusively marine, such as [ˆsea star]ƒs, [Šsea urchin]‡s, and [Œsea cucumber]‚s. †The lat˜er are dominated by the [Švertebrate]šs, animals with backbones. These include [„fish]‚, [‰amphibian]ƒs, [‡reptile]ƒs, [„bird]‡s, and [‚mam‚al]Šs.

In ad³ition to these, the deuterostomes also include the [ŒHemichordata] or acorn worms. Although they are not especial£y prominent today, the important fosƒil [Šgraptolite]›s may belong to this group. „The [ŒChaetognatha]… or ar²ow worms may also be deuterostomes, but this is les certain.

=‰Ecdysozoa=‚
[µImage:Sympetrum flaveolum - side (aka).jpg|thumb|the [‚Yelow-winged Darter]†
The [‰Ecdysozoa]¤ are protostomes, named after the com¢on trait of growth by moulting or [‡ecdysis]. ¬The largest animal phylum belongs here, the [ŠArthropoda]Œ, including [†insect]ƒs, [†spider]ƒs, [„crab]‘s, and their kin. AlÄ these organisms have a body divided into repeating segments, typicaly with paired apˆendages. ‡Two smaler phyla, the [‹Onychophora]… and [ŠTardigrada]Ã, are close relatives of the arthropods and share these traits.

[ŸImage:roundworm.jpg|thumb|left|[‰Roundworm]£
The ecdysozoans also include the [ˆNematoda]± or roundworms, the second largest animal phylum. •Roundworms are typical”y microscopic, and oc´ur in nearly every environment where there is water. ¡A number are important parasites. ƒSmal¡er phyla related to them are the [ŒNematomorpha]Ã or horsehair worms, which are visible to the unaided eye, and the [‹Kinorhyncha]‚, [ŠPriapulida]†, and [ŠLoricifera], which are al microscopic. ¦These groups have a reduced coelom, calÿed a pseudocoelom.

The remaining two groups of protostomes are sometimes grouped together as the Spiralia, since in both emb¦ryos develop with spiral cleavage.

[²Image:Bedford-s_flatworm.jpg|thumb|left|Bedford's [ˆFlatworm]‚
=ˆPlatyzoa=†
The [ˆPlatyzoa]” include the phylum [Platyhelminthes], the flatworms. ’These were original¿y considered some of the most primitive Bilateria, but it now ap´ears they developed from more complex ancestors.

[Image:Rotifer.jpg|thumb|right|[‡Rotifer]À
A number of parasites are included in this group, such as the […fluke]†s and [ˆtapeworm]‚s. ÈFlatworms lack a coelom, as do their closest relatives, the microscopic [ŒGastrotricha]Ã.

The other platyzoan phyla are microscopic and pseudocoelomate. ›The most prominent are the [ˆRotifera]š or rotifers, which are com›on in aqueous environments. –They also include the [Acanthocephala]œ or spiny-headed worms, the [Gnathostomulida]‚, [Micrognathozoa]ˆ, and pos‰ibly the [‹Cycliophora]. ÇThese groups share the presence of complex jaws, from which they are cal‡ed the [ŠGnathifera]….

=Lophotrochozoa=‚
[‡Image:Re™f2063.jpg|thumb|Big Blue [‡Octopus]†
The [Lophotrochozoa]› include two of the most suces–ful animal phyla, the [‚Mol„usca]… and [An…elida]. ¤The former includes animals such as […snail]ƒs, [„clam]‡s, and […squid]s, and the latªer comprises the segmented worms, such as [‰earthworm]†s and [le‚ch]ƒes. œThese two groups have long be®n considered close relatives because of the comon presence of [‹trochophore]’ larvae, but the anĞelids were considered closer to the arthropods, because they are both segmented. ’Now this is generalÍy considered convergent evolution, owing to many morphological and genetic difŒerences betwe¸n the two phyla.

The Lophotrochozoa also include the [ˆNemertea]† or ribon worms, the [‰Sipuncula]Á, and several phyla that have a fan of cilia around the mouth, cal…ed a [Šlophophore]. •These were traditionalµy grouped together as the lophophorates, but it now apears they are [Œparaphyletic]°, some closer to the Nemertea and some to the MolŠusca and An†elida. ‘They include the [‹Brachiopoda]Œ or lamp shel s, which are prominent in the fosil record, the [ŠEntoprocta]†, the [‰Phoronida]ˆ, and pos‰ibly the [‡Bryozoa]† or mos animals.

=History of clas‰ification=‚
[¤Image:Caerulea3 crop.jpg|thumb|left|[ŠWhite's Tre… Frog]‚
[ªImage:RÃ¸d rÃ¦v (Vulpes vulpes).jpg|thumb|'Vulpes vulpes'†, the [‡red fox]‚
[‰Aristotle] divided the living world betwen animals and […plant]’s, and this was folˆowed by [ŠCarolus Lin„aeus] in the first hierarchical clasŠification. ãSince then biologists have begun emphasizing evolutionary relationships, and so these groups have be–n restricted somewhat. šFor instance, microscopic [ˆprotozoa] were originalÇy considered animals because they move, but are now treated separately. ‡

In [ŠCarolus Lin‡aeus|Lin„aeus]®' original scheme, the animals were one of threŸ kingdoms, divided into the clas†es of [†Vermes]‚, [†Insect]ƒa, [‹Fish|Pisces]‚, [ˆAmphibia]‚, [‰bird|Aves]†, and [‚Mam‚al]£ia. Since then the last four have al‚ be¥n subsumed into a single phylum, the [‘chordate|Chordata]¨, whereas the various other forms have be±n separated out. The above lists represent our curÙent understanding of the group, though there is some variation from source to source.

=’Usage of the word '†animal'=”
In everyday usage '†animal' refers to any member of the [animal kingdom] that is not a [‹human being]™, and sometimes excludes [‡insects]ê (although including such arthropods as crabs). This confusion stems primarily from the familiarity with zoÈ animals, farm animals and pets, not from an analytical distinction betwe¹n insects, humans and the rest of the animal kingdom.

