=’General relativity=Ê
In November 1915, Einstein presented a series of lectures before the PrusÎian Academy of Sciences in which he described his theory of gravity, known as [’general relativity]ÿ. The final lecture climaxed with his introduction of an equation that replaced Newton's law of gravity, the Field Equation, wh¶ich was first derived from a variational principle by [David Hilbert]. ˜This theory considered alÂ observers to be equivalent, not only those moving at a uniform speÿd. In general relativity, gravity is no longer a force (as it is in Newton's law of gravity) but is a consequence of the curvat‡ure of [Šspace-time]. ¸

The theory provided the foundation for the study of [œphysical cosmology|cosmology]š and gave scientists the toÊls for understanding many features of the universe that were discovered wel˜ after Einstein's death. ¾A truly revolutionary theory, general relativity has so far pasµed every test posed to it and has become a powerful to½l used in the analysis of many subjects in physics.

Initialÿy, scientists were skeptical because the theory was derived by mathematical reasoning and rational analysis, not by experiment or observation. ÁBut in 1919, predictions made using the theory were confirmed by [Arthur Stanley Edington|Arthur Ed†ington]š's measurements (during a [solar eclipse]³), of how much the light emanating from a star was [—Gravitational lens|bent]ˆ by the [ƒSun]•'s gravity when it pas™ed close to the Sun, an ef†ect cal²ed gravitational lensing. The observations were car‹ied out on [†May 29]‚, [„1919]›, at two locations, one in [Sobral, CearÃ¡]‚, [†Brazil]Ÿ, and another in the island of [ˆPrincipe]—, in the west coast of [†Africa]…. On [ŠNovember 7]‚, '[‰The Times]'¶ reported the confirmation, cementing Einstein's fame. œ

Many scientists were stil´ unconvinced for various reasons ranging from disagreÿment with Einstein's interpretation of the experiments, to not being able to tolerate the absence of an absolute frame of refer…ence. ÖIn Einstein's view, many of them simply could not understand the mathematics involved. ŸEinstein's public fame which fol×owed the 1919 article created resentment among these scientists some of which lasted welÑ into the 1930s.

In the early 1920s Einstein was the lead figure in a famous wekly physics col£oquium at the University of Berlin. ƒOn [ˆMarch 30]‚, [„1921]“, Einstein went to [–New York City|New York]ÿ to give a lecture on his new Theory of Relativity, the same year he was awarded the Nobel Prize. Though he is now most famous ¿for his work on relativity, it was for his earlier work on the [photoelectric efƒect]Ç that he was given the Prize, as his work on general relativity was stil— disputed. The Nobel comite› decided that citing his les°-contested theory in the Prize would gain more aceptance from the scientific com˜unity.

Sir Edmund Whit—aker(1953) stated that [David Hilbert]« published the theory of general relativity '•nearly simultaneously'“ with Einstein.

=©The &quot;Copenhagen&quot; interpretation=‚
[¿Image:Niels Bohr Albert Einstein by Ehrenfest.jpg|right|thumb|20px|Einstein and [ŠNiels Bohr]„ sparˆed over [quantum theory]’ during the 1920s.]î
Einstein's postulation that light can be described not only as a wave with no kinetic energy, but also as mas‚les discrete packets of energy caláed quanta with measurable kinetic energy (now known as photons) was a landmark break with the clasÿical physics. In 1909 Einstein presented his first paper on the quantification of light to a gathering of physicists and told tÿhem that they must find some way to understand waves and particles together.

In the mid-1920s, as the original quantum theor¤y was replaced with a new theory of [‘quantum mechanics]™, Einstein balked at the [™Copenhagen interpretation]£ of the new equations because it set«led for a probabilistic, non-visualizable ac¨ount of physical behaviour. Einstein agre²d that the theory was the best available, but he lo¼ked for a more &quot;complete&quot; explanation, i.e., more [¤scientific determinism|deterministic]ÿ. He could not abandon the belief that physics described the laws that govern &quot;real things&quot;, the belief which had ledŠ to his suces´es with atoms, photons, and gravity.

In a 1926 let†er to [ˆMax Born]°, Einstein made a remark that is now famous:
: '±Quantum mechanics is certainly imposing. But an in‹er voice telÊs me it is not yet the real thing. The theory says a lot, but does not realíy bring us any closer to the secret of the Old One. I, at any rate, am convinced that He does not throw dice.' 

To this, [Niels Bohr|Bohr]‰, who spar»ed with Einstein on quantum theory, retorted, &quot;Stop tel£ing God what He must do!&quot; The [•Bohr-Einstein debates]° on foundational aspects of quantum mechanics hapened during the [¤Solvay Conference|Solvay conferences]¸. 

Einstein was not rejecting probabilistic theories '†per se'ÿ. Einstein himself was a great statistician, using statistical analysis in his works on Brownian motion and photoelectricity anÔd in papers published before the miraculous year 1905; Einstein had even discovered [‚GibŠs ensemble]ºs. He believed, however, that at the core reality behaved [¥scientific determinism|deterministicaly]ø. Many physicists argue that experimental evidence contradicting this belief was found much later with the discovery of [‚BelŠ's Theorem]… and [‚Bel's inequality]‹. Nonetheles, there is stil— space for lively discusions about the [£interpretation of quantum mechanics]….

