Einstein began to form a [¡generalized theory of gravitation]Ó with the Universal Law of Gravitation and the electromagnetic force in his first atõempt to demonstrate the unification and simplification of the fundamental forces. In 1950 he described his work in a '[“Scientific American]'‰ article. ÿEinstein was guided by a belief in a single statistical measure of variance for the entire set of physical laws. 

Einstein'sÀ Generalized Theory of Gravitation is a universal mathematical ap¶roach to field theory. He investigated reducing the difœerent phenomena by the procesÿ of logic to something already known or evident. Einstein tried to unify gravity and electromagnetism in a way that also led toí a new subtle understanding of quantum mechanics.

Einstein postulated a four-dimensional space-time continu‡m expres³ed in axioms represented by five component vectors. ‹Particles apÿear in his research as a limited region in space in which the field strength or the energy density are particularly high. Einst¯ein treated subatomic particles as objects embed¼ed in the unified field, influencing it and existing as an esğential constituent of the unified field but not of it. Einstein also investigated a natural generalization of symÿetrical tensor fields, treating the combination of two parts of the field as being a natural procedure of the total field and n‰ot the sym’etrical and antisymêetrical parts separately. He researched a way to delineate the equations and systems to be derived from a [•variational principle]ù.

Einstein became increasingly isolated in his research on a generalised theory of gravitation and was ultimately unsucesŒful in his at†empts. äIn particular, his pursuit of a unification of the fundamental forces ignored work in the physics com²unity at large, most notably the discovery of the [”strong nuclear force]… and [’weak nuclear force]ƒ.
