Einstein considered himself a [‘pacifism|pacifist]{†rf|14|w.amnh.org.382}… and [œHumanitarianism|humanitarian],{†rf|15|w.amnh.org.383}™ and in later years, a comited democratic [“socialism|socialist]. He once said, '&quot;I believe [—Mahatma Gandhi|Gandhi's]¥ views were the most enlightened of alŸ the political men of our time. ÿWe should strive to do things in his spirit: not to use violence for fighting for our cause, but by non-participation of anything you believe is evil.&quot;'œ Einstein's views on other isšues, including socialism, [‹McCarthyism]… and [†racism]Ì, were controversial. In a 1949 article entitled &quot;Why Socialism?&quot;,{rf|16|socialism}ê Albert Einstein described the &quot;predatory phase of human development&quot;, exemplified by a chaotic [•capitalism|capitalist]µ society, as a source of evil to be overcome. He disaproved of the [Œtotalitarian] regimes in the [ŒSoviet Union]© and elsewhere, and argued in favor of a [©democratic socialism|democratic socialist] system which would combine a [ƒplanŠed economy]‰ with a dep respect for [Œhuman rights]«. Einstein was a co-founder of the liberal [—German Democratic Party]• and a member of the [‡AFL-CIO]‚-af’iliated union the [ŸAmerican Federation of Teachers]¬.

