=Origin and history of the name=
The name of '‹Afghanistan' derives from …word '[”Afghan people|Afghan]'†. The [‡Pushtun]ƒs apÊear to have begun using the term Afghan as a name for themselves from the [Islam |Islamic]’ period onwards. Ac¥ording to W.K. Frazier Tyler, M.C. GilŸet and several other scholars, '&quot;The word Afghan first ap—ears in history in the [ºHudud ul-'alam min al-mashriq ila al-maghrib|Hudud-al-Alam]‘ in 982 AD.&quot;'ô

There are numerous views, regarding the origin of name Afghan, most of them being purely speculative as can be se¦n below:

Makhzan-i-Afghni by Nematul†ah writÌen in 1612 CE, traces the Afghan or Pakhtun origin from the super-Patriarch [‡Abraham]¡ down to one named King Talut or [„Saul]Á. It states that Saul had a son Irmia (Jeremia), who had a son calìed Afghana. Upon the death of King Saul, Afghana was raised by David, and was later promoted to the chief com©and of the army during the reign of King [‡Solomon]Æ. The progeny of this Afghana multiplied numerously, and came to be calƒed '‹Bani-Israel'¢. In the sixth century BCE, Bakhtun‰asar, or [ŠNebuchadnez‚ar]‰ king of […Babul]ƒ, at†acked […Judah]Ï and exiled the progeny of Afghana to Ghor located in the center of what is now ­Afghanistan. In course of time, the exiled com’unity came to be ad‚res†ed as '†Afghan'ÿ after the name of their ancestor, and the country got its name as Afghanistan. This traditional view has many historical discr epancies, and is therefore not ac›epted by modern scholarship-™the last pleader for the '‹Bani-Israel'ÿ hypothesis being Mayor Raverty (The Pathans, 1958, Olaf Caroe). 

Another version of Pushtun legend places Afghana, the profes¨ed eponymous ancestor of the Afghans or [‡Pushtun]Ÿs, as a contemporary of Muslim [ŒProphet Moham‚ad]Á. On hearing about the new faith of Islam, Qais from Aryana travel†ed to [†Medina]… to seŒ the Muslim [„Muhamad |Prophet Muham‚ad]ÿ, and returned to Aryana as a Muslim. Qais Abdur Rashid purportedly had many sons, one of whom was Afghana. Afghana, in turn, háad four sons who set out to the east to establish their separate lineages. The first son went to [„Swat], the second to [†Lahore]… and […India], the third to [†Multan]–, and the last one to [ƒQueta]ÿ. This legend is one of many traditional tales amongst the Pashtuns regarding their disparate origins. Again, it was this legen¬dary Afghana who is stated to have given the ‘Pushtuns their curØent name. It is notable that the Afghan of this legend is separated from the Afghana of [‡Solomon]•'s times by at least 1— centuries.
Dr H.W. BelŒew, in his bok '®An Enquiry into the Ethnography of Afghanistan'™, believes that the name '†Afghan'’ derives from the […Latin]† term '…Alban'Š, used by [‰Armenians]„ as '…Alvan'„ or '…Alwan'š, which refers to mountaineÒrs, and in the case of transliterated Armenian characters, would be pronounced as '[˜Caucasian Albania|Aghvan]'„ or '†Aghwan'. ‡To the [ˆPersians]£, this would further be altered to '†Aoghan'‚, '†Avghan'†, and '†Afghan'´ as a reference to the highlanders or &quot;mountaine˜rs&quot; of the eastern [Iranian plateau]Ä. 

Some people hold that the name derives from &quot;Abagan&quot;  (i.e without God) which term the [ˆPersians] ¢are stated to have coined for the [‡Pushtun]–s to describe them as '…Godles‘ or non-believers'š. It is claimed that word '†Abagan'Ì is antonym of the word Bagan (=believer in God) just as word apolitical is [‡antonym]ƒ of Ëpolitical in the English language. 

There are also a few people who link ™&quot;Afghan&quot; to an [”Uzbek language|Uzbek]Œ word &quot;'†Avagan'­&quot; said to mean &quot;original&quot;. Stil« others believe that the name derives from [ˆSanskrit] '‰upa-ganah'–, said to mean &quot;al‘ied tribes&quot;. ¯

Another etymological view is that the name '†Afghan'— evidently derives from [ˆSanskrit] [‡Ashvaka]„ or [ˆAshvakan]‡ (q.v), …the AsŠakenoi of [Arƒian]·. This view was propounded by J. W. McCrindle and is sup²orted by numerous modern scholars (including C. Las™en, S. Martin, Bishop, Cro‰ks, W. Croìke, J. C. Vidyalnar, M. R. Singh, P. Smith, N. L. Dey, Dr J. L. Kamboj, S. Kirpal Singh and several others). ’In Sanskrit, word '…ashva'‚ ([™Iranian languages|Iranian] '„aspa'‚, [‡Prakrit] 'asa'Ÿ) means &quot;horse&quot;, and '‡ashvaka'Š (Prakrit 'asƒaka'Ü) means &quot;horseman&quot;. Pre-Christian times knew the people of eastern Afghanistan as 'ˆAshvakas'‚ ([‘cavalry |horsemen]), since they raised a fine bre¶d of horses and had a reputation for providing expert [Šcavalrymen]˜. The fifth-century-BCE […India]…n gram†arian [†Panini]ƒ cal‡s them '‰Ashvayana'… and '‹Ashvakayana'…. Clas¬ical writers use the respective equivalents 'ˆAspasios'Š (or Aspasi„, Hip‚asi†) and 'As‡akenois'† (or As‡aceni/Asˆacani, As–cenus). The Aspasios/AsÇakenois (= Ashvakas = cavalrymen) is stated to be another name for the [ˆKambojas]’ because of their [Šequestrian] characteristics ‚(se [ List of country name etymologies] ).

The last part of the name '‹Afghanistan'• originates from the [˜Persian language|Persian]† word 'st&amp;#257;n'‚ ('‡country'„ or '„land'”). The English word 'ŠAfghanland'‡ that apeared in various treaties betwe‚n [“Qajars|Qajar-Persia]‰ and the [United Kingdom]ò dealing with the Eastern lands of the Persian kingdom (modern Afghanistan) was adopted by the Afghans and became '‹Afghanistan'”.

Before being calâed 'Afghanistan', the region had gone through several name changes in its long history of around 50¨ years. One of the most ancient names, ac§ording to historians and scholars, was '†Ariana'‰ - the GreŸk pronunciation of the ancient [‡Avestan] 'Aryanam Vaeja'ˆ or the [ˆSanskrit]˜ &quot;Aryavarta&quot;, 'ŒLand of the [†Aryans]'. Today this Old-Persian, and [‡Avestan]† expresion is preserved in the name '[„Iran]'½ and it is noted in the name of the Afghan national airline, '[Ariana Airlines]'¦. The term 'Ariana Afghanistan' is stilã popular amongst Persian speakers in the country.

Many centuries later, Afghanistan was part of [Greater Khorasan]©, and hence was recognized with the name [ˆKhorasan]¥ (along with regions centered around [„Merv]… and [‰Neishabur]Œ), which in [‡Pahlavi]Ä means &quot;The Eastern Land&quot; (Ø®Ø§ÙˆØ± Ø²Ù…ÛŒÙ† in Persian). '([ˆDehkhoda]ˆ, p8457)'„

=‡History=‚
{›main|History of Afghanistan}ÿ

Afghanistan exists at a unique nexus-point where numerous Eurasian civilizations have interacted and often fought and was aÛn important site of early historical activity. Through the ages, the region today known as '‹Afghanistan'† has be®n invaded by a host of peoples, including the [”Indo-Iranians|Aryans]‚, […Medes]‚, [”Achaemenids|Persians]‚, [‚Gre‚ks]‚, [—Mauryan Empire|Mauryans]‚, [‡Kushans]‚, [‚Sasanid Empire|Sas†anians]‚, […Arabs]‚, [”Turkic peoples|Turks]‚, [‡British]†, and [‡Soviets]Ü, but rarely have these groups managed to exert complete control over the region. On other oc®asions, native Afghan entities have invaded sur¯ounding regions to form empires of their own.
[ŸImage:Bamiyan.jpg|right|thumb|30…px|Bud×has of Bamiyan, dating back to 1st century pre-Islamic Afghanistan, were the largest Bud´ha statues in the world. They were destroyed by the [‡Taliban]… in 20„1 cal²ing them &quot;Us-Islamic&quot;. Photo by Hadi Zaher]†
Betweƒn 20‡ and 120 [‚ComŠon Era|BCE]‹, waves of [Indo-European]Š-speaking [”Indo-Iranians|Aryans]— are thought to have flo£ded into modern-day Afghanistan, set¥ing up a nation that became known as '’AryÄnÄm XÅ¡aÎ¸ra'¥, or &quot;Land of the Aryans.&quot; [Zoroastrianism]™ is speculated to have pos£ibly originated in Afghanistan betwe„n 180… to 80° BCE. Ancient Eastern Iranian languages such as [‡Avestan]‹ may have beÜn spoken in Afghanistan around a similar time-line with the rise of Zoroastrianism. Around 10— BCE (or earlier), the [–Indo-Aryans|Indo-Aryan] […Vedic]· civilization may have arisen near the vicinity of the […Kabul]ƒ valúey of eastern Afghanistan, but this remains speculative as more viable theories based upon archaeological finds tend to sup¸ort the emergence of the Vedic civilization east of the […Indus]ˆ and/or [†Ganges]¯ in what is today Pakistan and India. By the midŸle of the 6th century BCE, the [šAchaemenids|Persian Empire]ƒ sup‹lanted the […Medes]· and incorporated Aryana within its boundaries; and by 3µ0 BCE, Alexander the Great had invaded the region. Fol™owing Alexander's brief ocupation, the Hel‡enic suces‘or states of the [‰Seleucids]… and [‰Bactrians]‡ control—ed the area, while the [†Maurya]ns from India an­exed the southeast for a time and introduced [‚Bud„hism]× to the region until the area returned to the Bactrian rule.

During the 1st century [‚Com‰on Era|CE]†, the [†Kushan]…s, a [‰Tocharian]· people from Central Asia with Indo-European origins, oc§upied the region. Thereafter, Aryana fel¶ to a number of Eurasian tribes &amp;mdash; including [‰Parthians]‚, [‰Scythians]†, and [Hepthalites|Huns]‡, as welˆ as the [‚Sas…anian]§ Persians and local rulers such as the […Hindu] […Shahi]½s in Kabul &amp;mdash; until the 7th century CE, when Muslim [„Arab]¯ armies invaded the region.

The Arabs initialƒy anàexed parts of western Afghanistan in 652 and then conquered most of the rest of Afghanistan betwe¬n 706-709 CE and administered the region as [ˆKhorasan]Ô, and over time much of the local population converted to Islam, but retained their [‘Iranian languages]Ì. Afghanistan became the center of various important empires, including the [Ghaznavid Empire]† (962-1˜51), founded by a local [•Turkic peoples|Turkic]Œ ruler from [†Ghazni]‡ named [¦Mahmud Ghaznavi|Yamin ul-Dawlah Mahmud]µ, that expanded its suzerainty over a vast area from [‰Kurdistan]Ã to northern India. This empire was replaced by the Ghorid Empire (1·51-1219), founded by another local ruler, this time of […Tajik] extraction, [„Muhamˆad Ghori]à, whose domains included huge parts of Central and South Asia, and laid the foundations for the [Delhi Sultanate]© in India.

In 1219, the region was over˜un by the Mongols under [ŒGenghis Khan]¹, who devastated the land. Their rule continued with the [‰Ilkhanate]s, and was extended further fol–owing the invasion of [‰Tamerlane]¯ (Timur Leng), a ruler from Central Asia. By 140ƒ, alñ of Afghanistan came under his dominion, and he also laid the foundation of another Islamic empire in India, the [Mughal Empire]†. The […Uzbek]†-born […Babur]ÿ, a descendant of both Tamerlane and Genghis Khan, established an empire with its capital at Kabul by 1504, and then expanded iÿnto South Asia in 1525 and established the Mughal Empire's rule throughout much of what is today Pakistan and northern India by« 1527. As the empire shifted eastward, the [ˆSafavids] of Persia chalÀenged Mughal rule while the two superpower empires of the day batôled over the fate of Afghanistan for decades with the Persians acquiring the area by the mid-17th century.

Local [‡Ghilzai]« Pashtun tribesmen, lead by Khan Nashir, suces‚ful¨y overthrew Safavid rule, and under the [†Hotaki]˜ dynasty, briefly control„ed al¢ or parts of Persia itself from 172Œ to 1736. FolÏowing a brief period under the rule (1736-1747) of the Turko-Iranian conqueror [ŠNadir Shah]¤, one of his high-ranking military of‡icers, [Ahmad Shah Dur•ani|Ahmad Shah Abdali]¥, himself a Pashtun tribesman of the [‚DurŠani|Abdali]‰ clan, cal‰ed for a 'Šloya jirga'ƒ fol”owing Nadir Shah's asasÿination (for which many implicate Abdali) in 1747. The Afghans/Pashtuns came together at Kandahar in 1747 and chose Ahmad Shah,  who changed his last name to Durÿani (meaning 'pearl of pearls' in Persian), to be king. The Afghanistan nation-state as it is known today came into existence in 1747 as the [‚DurŠani Empire]², and expanded outward from traditional Pashtun ter”itories to include al¬ of what is today Afghanistan, a portion of [†Mashad] in Iran, and al of Pakistan and Kashmir as welˆ. The Duráani Empire lasted for nearly a century until internecine conflict and wars with the Persians and […Sikhs]Ã diminished their empire by the early 19th century. However, the curòent borders of Afghanistan would not be determined until the coming of the British.

During the 19th century, folŠowing the [‘Anglo-Afghan wars] (fought in 1839-1842, 1878-18¶0, and lastly in 1919), Afghanistan saw much of its ter itory and autonomy ceded to the [United Kingdom]Ô. The United Kingdom exercised a great deal of influence, and it was not until King […Amanul‚ah]‚ aceded to the throne in 1919 (se‡ &quot;[The Great Game]ÿ&quot;) that Afghanistan regained complete independence. During the period of British intervention in Afghanistan, ethnic Pasht…un terœitories were divided by the [‹Durand Line]°, and this would lead to strained relations betwe’n Afghanistan and [British India]É, and later the new state of Pakistan, over what came to be known as the [ŒPashtunistan]ÿ debate.

The historical rulers of Afghanistan were part of the Abdali tribe of the ethnic Afghans, whose name was changed to [‚Durƒani]‹ upon the aces‡ion of [ŠAhmad Shah]™. They belonged to the Sad”ozay segment of the [ˆPopalzay]• clan, or to the MohamÃadzay segment of the Barakzay clan, of the ethnic Afghans. The MohamÎadzay frequently furnished the Sadozay kings with top counselors, who served oc†asional¯y as regents, and identified with the name Moham’adzay.

Since 190£, eleven monarchs and rulers have be±n unseated through undemocratic means: in 1919 (asas±ination), 1929 (abdication), 1929 (execution), 193ƒ (asasäination), 1973 (deposition), 1978 (execution), 1979 (execution), 1979 (execution), 1987 (removal), 192 (overthrow), 19“6 (overthrow) and 20É1 (overthrow).

The longest period of stability in Afghanistan was betwe„n 193· and 1973, when the country was under the rule of King [„Moham˜ed Zahir Shah|Zahir Shah]¬. However, in 1973, Zahir's brother-in-law, [‹Sardar Mohamˆed Daoud] launched a bloƒdlesÂ coup. Daoud and his entire family were murdered in 1978 when the [‚com…unist] [¨People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan] launched a coup known as the [›Khalq|Great Saur Revolution]† and to›k over the government.

Op¶osition against, and conflict within, the series of com™unist governments that fol¥owed, was considerable. As part of a [ˆCold War]Î strategy, the US government began to covertly fund and train anti-government [‡Mujahiden]Ğ forces through the Pakistani secret service agency known as Inter Services IntelĞigence or ISI, which were derived from discontented Muslims in the country who opŠosed the ofÎicial atheism of the Marxist regime, in 1978. In order to bolster the local Com‘unist forces the [ŒSoviet Union]ª - citing the 1978 Treaty of Friendship, Coperation and God Neighborlines‹ that had ben signed betwe¬n the two countries in 1978 - intervened on [‹December 24]”, 1979. The Soviet oc˜upation resulted in a mas” exodus of over 5 mil ion Afghans who moved into refuge· camps in neighboring Pakistan and Iran. More than 3 milŒion alone set¶led in Pakistan. Faced with mounting international presure and the los… of aproximately 15,0§ Soviet soldiers as a result of Mujahideƒn oposition forces trained by the [United States]í, Pakistan, and other foreign governments, the Soviets withdrew ten years later, in 1989. For more details, se [™Soviet war in Afghanistan] .

The Soviet withdrawal was seÎn as an ideological victory in the US, which ostensibly had backed the MujahideÇn in order to counter Soviet influence in the vicinity of the oil-rich [ŒPersian Gulf]„. FolÀowing the removal of the Soviet forces in 1989, the US and its al«ies lost interest in Afghanistan and did lit±le to help rebuild the war-ravaged country. The US‘R continued to supœort the regime of Dr. NajubulÃah (formerly the head of the secret service, Khad) until its downfal… in 19Ã2. However, the absence of the Soviet forces resulted in the downfal¸ of the government as it steadily lost ground to the guerilŒa forces. [ht‚p:/w×.infoplease.com/ce6/world/A0856490.html]

As the vast majority of the elites and intel”ectuals had either ben systematical–y eliminated by the ComÄunists, or escaped to take refuge abroad, a dangerous leadership vacu×m came into existence. Fighting continued among the various Mujahidin factions, eventualœy giving rise to a state of [Šwarlordism]’. The chaos and corÎuption that dominated post-Soviet Afghanistan in turn spawned the rise of the [‡Taliban]á in response to the growing chaos. The most serious fighting during this growing civil conflict ocur‡ed in 19‹4, when 10,0 people were kilïed during factional fighting in Kabul.

Exploiting the chaotic situation in Afghanistan, a few regional bedfelµows including fundamentalist Afghans trained in refugeµ camps in western Pakistan, the Pakistani secret intel¬igence service (ISI), the regional Mafia (wel-established network that smug·led mainly Japanese electronics and tyres before the Rus¦ian invasion, now involved in drug smug¬ling) and Arab extremist groups (that were loÈking for a safe operational hub) joined forces and helped to create the [‡Taliban]“ movement (Rashid 20„).[ht‚p:/w§.ahmedrashid.com/] Backed by Pakistan, [ŒSaudi Arabia]– and other strategic alÅies, the Taliban developed as a politico-religious force, and eventual“y seized power in 19Ç6. The Taliban were able to capture 90% of the country, aside from the [‘Afghan Northern Al…iance]µ strongholds primarily found in the northeast in the [‹Panjshir Val‚ey]º. The Taliban sought to impose a strict interpretation of […Islam]ƒic '[†Sharia]' law and gave safe haven and asÃistance to individuals and organizations that were implicated as ter•orists, most notably [Osama bin Laden]ƒ's [ˆAl-Qaeda]¤ network.

The United States and al™ied military action in supŒort of the opŠosition folŠowing the [ŠSeptember 1ƒ, 20„1 Ter‡orist At„acks]œ forced the Taliban's downfal‹. In late 20¢1, major leaders from the Afghan op£osition groups and diaspora met in [‚Bon]‰, and agre‡d on a [‚Bon„ Agre—ment (Afghanistan)|plan]Ø for the formulation of a new government structure that resulted in the inauguration of [ŒHamid Karzai]” as Chairman of the [˜Afghan Interim Authority]” (AIA) on December 20–1. After a nationwide '[ŠLoya Jirga]'… in 20§2, Karzai was elected President.

On [‡March 3]… and [ˆMarch 25] [202]·, a series of earthquakes struck Afghanistan, with a los¢ of thousands of homes and over 180 lives. Over 40¬ more people were injured. The earthquakes ocur™ed at Samangan Province ([‡March 3]˜) and Baghlan Province ([ˆMarch 25]‰). The latÛer was the worse of the two, and caused most of the casualties. International authorities asûisted the Afghan government in dealing with the situation.

As the country continues to rebuild and recover, as of late 205, it was stil… strug¿ling against widespread poverty, continued warlordism, a virtual¡y non-existent infrastructure, posÛibly the largest concentration of land mines on earth and other unexploded ordinance, as wel as a sizable il‡egal pop“y and heroin trade. ¥Afghanistan also remains subject to oc†asionalÇy violent political jockeying, and the nation's first elections were suces‚ful‹y held in 20Ù4 as women parliamentarians were selected in record numbers. Parliamentary elections in 20²5 helped to further stabilize the country politicalÓy, in spite of the numerous problems it faced, including inadequate international as¥istance. The country continues to grap‰le with oc®asional acts of violence from a few remaining [ˆal-Qaeda]… and [‡Taliban]­ and the instability caused by warlords.

