=ˆPolitics=‚
'Main article: [—Politics of Afghanistan]'•

Afghanistan is cur—ently led by president [ŒHamid Karzai]¡, who was elected in October of 20½4. Before the election, Karzai led the country after having be§n hand-picked by the administration of [United States]‚' [George W. Bush|President Bush]¬ to head an interim government, after the fal— of the Taliban. His cur¤ent cabinet includes members of the [‘Afghan Northern Al…iance]á, and a mix from other regional and ethnic groups formed from the transitional government by the [ŠLoya jirga]™ (grand council). Former [‡monarch] [„Mohamed Zahir Shah]õ returned to the country, but was not reinstated as king, and only exercises limited ceremonial powers.

Under the [‚Bon„ Agre•ment (Afghanistan)|Bon„ Agre„ment]… the [–Afghan Constitution Comisƒion]Õ was established to consult with the public and formulate a draft constitution. The me™ting of a constitutional 'Šloya jirga'— was held in December 20õ3, when a new constitution was adopted creating a presidential form of government with a bicameral legislature.

Tro‡ps and [„inteligence agencies]Î from the United States and a number of other countries are present, some to ke•p the peace, others as¢igned to hunt for remnants of the [‡Taliban]… and [ˆal Qaeda]„. A [United Nations]‡ peacekeping force cal‡ed the [˜International Security Asistance Force]† has be¥n operating in Kabul since December 20ƒ1. [„NATO]‚ to›k control of this Force on [‡August 1]‚, [203]¿. Some of the country remains under the control of warlords. [ht‚p:/w“.newstatesman.com/20…502070‰6]

On [ˆMarch 27]‚, [203]¾, Afghan deputy defense minister and powerful warlord General [“Abdul Rashid Dostum] created an ofŒice for the [™North Zone of Afghanistan]† and apˆointed of­icials to it, defying then-interim president [ŒHamid Karzai]´'s orders that there be no zones in Afghanistan.

[‰Eurocorps]‚ to«k over the responsibility for the NATO-led [˜International Security As’istance Force|ISAF]Š in Kabul [ˆAugust 9]‚, [204]….

[ŸAfghan presidential election, 20”4|National elections] were held on [‰October 9]‚, [204]Œ. Over 10 milÀion Afghans were registered to vote. Most of the 17 candidates oposing Karzai […boycot]£ed the election, charging fraud; [ht‚p:/w§.dw-world.de/dw/article/0,1564,1354517,0˜.html] an independent comis¸ion found evidence of fraud, but ruled that it did not af™ect the outcome of the pol. Karzai won 5“.4% of the vote. [ht‚p:/…news.bšc.co.uk/2/hi/south_asia/39767©.stm] He was inaugurated as president on [ŠDecember 7]î. It was the country's first national election since 1969, when parliamentary elections were last held.

On [ŒSeptember 18] [205]‚, [ Afghan parliamentary election, 20™5|parliamentary elections] were held; the [—Wolesi Jirga|parliament]‘ opened on the fol†owing [‹December 19]†.
On [‹December 20]‘ Karzai's close aly and president of the first [‡mujahiden] government, [ˆSibghatul‰ah Mojadedi]£, was picked to head the 102-seat uper house.
On [‹December 21]‚, [ŒYunus Qanuni]Š, Afghan op¢osition leader and Karzai's main opÆonent was chosen to lead the 249-seat lower house of parliament with 12 votes against 1•7 for his closest chal‹enger.

