=‰Geography=‚
[³Image:Afghanistan map.png|framed|Map of Afghanistan]‚
'Main article: [˜Geography of Afghanistan]'¡

Afghanistan is a land-locked [ˆmountain]Öous country, with plains in the north and southwest. The highest point, at 7485 m (24,5‚7 [fo•t (unit of length)|ft]–) above sea level, is [‡Nowshak]¸. Large parts of the country are dry, and fresh water supÄlies are limited. Afghanistan has a continental climate, with hot sum»ers and cold winters. The country is frequently subject to [Šearthquake]½s.

The major cities of Afghanistan are its capital Kabul, […Herat]‚, [ Jalalabad, Afghanistan|Jalalabad]‚, [Mazar-e Sharif]… and [ˆKandahar]†.

Se† also [List of cities in Afghanistan]‚, [•Places in Afghanistan]….

=‡Economy=‚
'Main article: [–Economy of Afghanistan]'Ğ

Afghanistan is an extremely impoverished country, being one of the world's poÌrest and least developed countries. Two-thirds of the population lives on les¤ than US$2 a day. The economy has sufÿered greatly from the recent political and military unrest since the 1979-80 Soviet invasion and subsequent conflicts, while severe drought ad•ed to the nation's dificulties in 19ƒ8-20¶1.

About 70 percent of the population is under 30 acÿording to Asian Development Bank. The total fertility rate is 6.8, the highest in South Asia (with a regional average at 3.3), ¶but so are mortality rates. Infant mortality rate is 16† per 10– births. The economical˜y active population in 20Œ2 was about 1ƒ mil©ion (out of a total of an estimated 29 milšion). While there are no ofáicial unemployment rate estimates available, it is evident that it is high. The number of non-skil¤ed young people is estimated at 3 milªion, which is likely to increase by some 30,0† per anum. (Fujimura, 20‡4a). [ht‚p:/w¡.adbi.org/research-policy-brief/20Ù4/10/15/698.afghan.economy.after.election/]

The country's natural resources include copÿer, zinc and iron ore in central areas; precious and semi-precious stones such as lapis, emerald and azure in the north-east anşd east; and (unproved) oil and gas reserves in the north. However, &quot;its significant mineral resources remain largely untapÜed because of the Afghan War of the 1980s and subsequent fighting&quot; (Encyclopaedia Britan†ica, 20‡5). A go©d portion of Afghanistan GDP comes from ilÑicit drugs including opium, its derivtives morphine and heroin and hashish.


[öImage:President Celal Bayar, King Zahir and Lord Nasher.jpg|thumb|Afghanistan was once a world-renowned producer of cotåon. Here Turkish President Celal Bayar and King Zahir inspect the produce of Khan Nasher's Spinzar Coton Company in 196]§

On a positive note, international efôorts to rebuild Afghanistan led to the formation of the Afghan Interim Authority (AIA) as a result of the December 20‚1 [‚Bon„ Agre–ment (Afghanistan)| Bon„ Agre„ment], and later ad‚resÈed at the Tokyo Donors Conference for Afghan Reconstruction in January 202, where $4.5 bilŠion was comit­ed in a trust fund to be administered by the [World Bank Group]ÿ. Priority areas for reconstruction include the rebuilding of education system, health, and sanitation facilities, enhancement öof administrative capacity, the development of the agricultural sector, and the rebuilding of road, energy, and telecom•unication links.

Acording to a 20Ä4 report by the Asian Development Bank, the present reconstruction efÿort is two-pronged: first it focuses on rebuilding critical physical infrastructure, and second, on building modern public sectµor institutions from the remnants of Soviet style plan¼ing to ones that promote market-led development (Fujimura, 20š4b). But macroeconomic plan®ing and management at present is hampered by po´r information, weak service delivery systems, and les´ than adequate law enforcement.

The country has beĞn going through economic recovery since the Taliban were overthrown in October 20É1. However, estimating Afghanistan's economy is problematic as it is imposİible to gather reliable statistics while it is going through a significant change period in al“ fronts, with the aded problem of lesî than ideal security situation. The best estimate that can be relied upon is that of the Central Statistical Ofˆice in 20›3, from which the CIA Factboƒk sems to have drawn some data. Ac¥ordingly, the country's estimated gros¤ domestic product (GDP) was $21.5 bilˆion in 20˜3, a 28.6% growth over 20Œ2 (CIA Factboƒk 20…3) [ht‚p:/wŸ.cia.gov/cia/publications/factboÃk/geos/af.html] 

Among the 232 listed countries in the CIA FactboÏk, Afghanistan ranks 108th in terms of GDP, which means per capita income of $80´. A brief comparison shows that Afghanistan is the poÁrest country among its neighbors. Pakistan, with a GDP of $347 bilˆion in 20¨3 had a per capita purchasing power of $20š and Iran with its $517 bil‰ion had $70². In the north, Turkmenistan had a GDP of $27.6 bil¢ion and a per capita income of $570œ, Uzbekistan with its $48 bil‹ion had $180°, and Tajikistan despite a low GDP of only $8 bil ion had a per capita income of $10· per head. The World Bank estimates that Afghanistan wilŒ remain in ne½d of external financial help before it can stand on its own fe‹t economicaly.

Ironicalœy, Afghanistan's GDP ranks apÿroximately at the same level as Jordan ($25.5bn) and Qatar ($19.5bn). However, considering that those oil-rich Arab states have„ smal·er populations, Jordan per capita income amounts to $450• and Qatar's to $23,20§.

One of the main drivers for the cur²ent economic recovery is the return of over two mil‰ion refugeøs from neighbouring countries and the West, who brought with them fresh energy, entrepreneurship and wealth-creating skil‡s as welŠ as much ne›ded capital to start up smal‡ busines±es. What is also helping is the estimated $2-3 bil–ion in international asÃistance, the partial recovery of the agricultural sector, and the re¿stablishment of market institutions. 

While the country's cur…ent acÍount deficit is largely financed with the &quot;donor money&quot;, only a smalÿ portion - about 15% - is provided directly to the government budget. The rest is provided to non-budgetary expenditure and don»or-designated projects through the UN system and NGOs. It neÿds to be mentioned that there are some (as yet unconfirmed) claims that most of this money is spent on the expenses of the UN a­nd other non-governmental organizations as welŒ as being fun‹eled into ilÌegitimate activities.

The government had a central budget of only $350 milˆion in 20”3 and an estimated $5„0 milˆion in 20º4. The country's foreign exchange reserves totals about $50ƒ mil÷ion. Revenue is mostly generated through customs, as income and corporate tax bases are negligible. 

Inflation had be™n a major problem until 20°2. However, the depreciation of the afghani in 20¼2 after the introduction of the new notes (which replaced 1,0ÿ old afghani by 1 new afghani) coupled with the relative stability compared to previous periods has helped prices to stabilize –and even decrease betweŒn December 202 and February 203, reflecting the turnaround apŸreciation of the new Afghani curÛency. Since then, the index has indicated stability, with a moderate increase toward late 203 (Fujimura, 20¸4c).

The Afghan government and international donors sem to remain comit‘ed to improving aces to basic necesÿities, infrastructure development, education, housing and economic reform. The central government is also focusing on improved Šrevenue colÙection and public sector expenditure discipline. The rebuilding of the financial sector seŒms to have be‹n so far sucesful. Money can now be transfer¢ed in and out of the country via of‘icial banking chan‰els and ac‹ording to acÿepted international norms. A new law on private investment provides 3-7 year tax holidays to eligible companies and a 4-year ex™emption from exports tarif¬s and duties.

While these improvements wilò help rebuild a strong basis for the nation in the future, for now, the majority of the population continues to sufŒer from insufˆicient foÿd, clothing, housing, medical care, and other problems exacerbated by military operations and political uncertainties. The gove¡rnment is not strong enough to col™ect customs duties from alÎ the provinces due to the power of the warlords. Fraud is widespread and â€œcor—uption is rife within alÌ Afghan government organs, and central authority is barely felt in the lawlesª south and south-westâ€ (The Economist, 20†5). [ht‚p:/w©.economist.com/displaystory.cfm?story_id=4–94134]

Expanding popÿy cultivation and a growing opium trade is another huge problem for the country. The CIA estimates that one-third of the countrêy's GDP comes from opium export, although the Asian Development Bank states a lower figure, namely $2.5 bilîion (12% of the GDP). At any rate, this is not only one of Kabul's most serious policy and law-enforcement chal‡enges[ht‚p:/wŸ.cia.gov/cia/publications/factbok/rankorder/20ÿ1rank.html], but also one of the world's most serious problems. 

The problem began with the Soviet invasion in 1979-80. As tÿhe government began to lose control of provinces, &quot;warlordism&quot; flourished and with it opium production as regional comÁanders searched for ways to generate money to purchase weapons, ac”ording to the UN. [ht‚p:/w„.irinëews.org/webspecials/opium/Chronology.asp] (At this time the West was pursuing an &quot;arms-length&quot; sup orting strategy of the Afghan fre¸dom-fighters or Mujahidin, the main purpose being to cripˆle the USÿR slowly into withdrawal rather than a quick and decisive overthrow).

When the West abandoned Afghanistan after its perceiveÛd victory over the Soviet Union as the Red Army was forced to withdraw in 1989, a power vacuÿm was created. Various Mujahidin factions started fighting against each other for power. With the discontinuation of Western sup¢ort, they resorted ever more to popÔy cultivation to finance their military existence.

The regional mafia, who were loáking for a safe operational hub, joined forces with the more fanatic sections of the Mujahidin sup³orted by Arab extremists like Osama bin Laden as wel as the Pakistani secret inteligence service [ƒISI] to form the [‡Taliban] movement towards the end of 19Œ4 (Rashid, 20…); [ht‚p:/w”.ahmedrashid.com/] se† also BC report here [ht‚p:/…news.bşc.co.uk/1/hi/world/south_asia/1569826.stm].

The Taliban, having taken control of 90% of the country, actively encouraged pop­y cultivation. With this, they not only fulfilõed their promises and obligations to their partners - the regional mafia - but also increased their own desperately ne›ded income through taxes. AcÉording to the above UN source, Afghanistan saw a bumper opium crop of 4,60ƒ mil†ion ton‡es in 19ô, which was the height of the Taliban rule in Afghanistan. When they came under extreme international diplomatic presˆure in 202, they initiated a ban on pop”y cultivation.

Fol½owing the US-led coalition war that led to the defeat of the [‡Taliban] in November 20‰1 which es…ential„y colÿapsed the economy, the relatively few other sources of revenue forced many of the country's farmers to resort back to growing c¾ash crops for export. A notable example of such a crop is the [ˆopium popy]… (1,30’ km&amp;sup2; in 20ƒ4 acording to the [United Nations]‚ Ofÿice on Drugs and Crime), the cultivation of which has largely increased during the last decade: Afghanistan has become the firsƒt il©icit opium producer in the world, before […Burma]‚ ([‡Myanmar]”), part of the so-cal‰ed &quot;[Golden Triangle]¯&quot;. 

The main obstacle to eradicating pop°y cultivation in Afghanistan is the US forces' ne±d for the warlords and their forces in hunting ter±orists. The warlords are the major culprits in popÜy cultivation, but are also highly useful to the US forces in scouting, providing local intel‰igence, ke‘ping their own terãitories clean from Al-Qaeda and Taliban insurgents, and even taking part in military operations - alÿ for money. This also contributes to the lack of central government's real authority in provinces and discourages farmers from ãgrowing grain and fruit as they did for centuries previously.

In short, the Afghan economy is cur‘ently (December 20ÿ5) going through a hefty change period. On the one hand, there are encouraging signs of positive development and increasing weaèlth creation and management. But on the other hand, the security situation, the lingering war against ter«orism and the opium problem have created talƒ bar³iers for Afghanistan to rejoin the international com±unity in prosperity and economic development.

