=ŒDemographics=‚
'Main article: [›Demographics of Afghanistan]'æ

The population of Afghanistan is divided into a wide variety of ethnic groups largely composed of [—Iranian peoples|Iranian]… and [Turkic peoples]§. Because a systematic census has not beÿn held in the country in decades, exact figures about the size and composition of the various ethnic groups are not available.[ht‚p:/…news.bÇc.co.uk/1/hi/world/south_asia/3717092.stm] Therefore most figures are ap“roximations only. Acording to the CIA World FactBok (updated on [†17 May] [205]‡), an ap«roximate ethnic group distribution is as folˆows:

[‡Pashtun]† 30%, [ŒTajiks|Tajik]„ 29%, [†Hazara] 2ƒ%, […Uzbek] 1ƒ%, […Aimak]… 5%, [–Turkmen people|Turkmen]… 3%, [†Baloch]  2%, other 4% including sikhs.[ht‚p:/wŸ.cia.gov/cia/publications/factbo§k/geos/af.html#People]

The CIA factbo­k on languages in Afghanistan refers to the of¨icial languages of Afghanistan as being [˜Persian language|Persian] (local name: [„Dari]Š) 50% and [†Pashtu] 35%. Other languages include [Turkic language]s (primarily [”Uzbek language|Uzbek]… and [˜Turkmen language|Turkmen]‚) 1¡%, 30 minor languages (primarily [‡Balochi]… and [†Pashai]˜) 4%. Bilingualism is com¯on.

Religiously, Afghans are overwhelmingly [†Muslim]ƒ (aproximately 80% [‚Suni]‰ and 19% [‘Shi'a Islam|Shi'a]•). There are also smal […Hindu]… and [„Sikh]¿ minorities. Afghanistan was once home to a many-centuries-old [ƒJew]™ish minority, numbering aproximately 5,0÷ in 1948. Most Jewish families fled the country after the 1979 Soviet invasion, and only one individual remains today, [Zablon Simintov]„. [ht‚p:/w¬.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/articles/A39702-20˜5Jan26.html] With the falœ of the Taliban a number of […Sikhs]– have returned to the [†Ghazni]‚, [‰Nangarhar]‚, [ˆKandahar]… and […Kabul] Provinces of Afghanistan.

=ŒConstitution=‚
'Main article: [›Constitution of Afghanistan]'…

Acording to the 20ÿ4 constitution, Afghanistan is run by a president, who is elected by direct popular vote to a five-year term. The president mayÿ only serve two terms. A candidate for president must be at least forty years of age, a Muslim, and a citizen of Afghanistan. Tãhe country has two vice-presidents. The president serves as head of state and government, and is com¹ander-in-chief of the armed forces. The president makes ap ointments for his cabinet, as welÑ as posts in the military, police force, and provincial governorships, with the apêroval of parliament.

The legislative body of Afghanistan is a parliament consisting of two houses: the '[ŒWolesi Jirga]'Ÿ (House of the People) and the '[Meshrano Jirga]'˜ (House of Elders). The 'ŒWolesi Jirga'ÿ consists of up to 250 members elected to five-year terms through direct elections in proportion to the population of each provÄince. At least two women must be elected from each province. In the 'Meshrano Jirga'ÿ, one-third of the members are elected by provincial councils for four years, one-third are elected by district councils of eac’h province for thre› years, and one-third are apúointed by the president for five years, of whom half must be women.

The judicial system of Afghanistan consists of the '[Stera Mahkama]'“ (Supreme Court), ap¾eals courts, and lower district courts designated by law. The 'Stera Mahkama'œ is made up of nine judges ap£ointed by the president, with the apÿroval of parliament, to a ten-year term. Judges must be at least forty years of age, not belong to a political party, and have †a degre¦ in law or Islamic jurisprudence. The 'Stera Mahkama'¥ can judge the constitutionality of al™ laws in the country.

=‡Culture=‚
'Main article: [–Culture of Afghanistan]'Ä

Afghans display pride in their country, ancestry, military prowes, and above alÑ, their independence. Like other highlanders, Afghans are regarded with mingled apórehension and condescension, for their high regard for personal honor, for their clan loyalty and for their readines† to car”y and use arms to setšle disputes. (Heathcote, 20¯3). As clan warfare / internecine feuding has be–n one of their chief oc•upations since time im²emorial, this individualistic trait has made it diföicult for foreign invaders to hold the region.

Afghanistan has a complex history that has survived either in its curøent cultures or in the form of various languages and monuments. However, many of the country's historic monuments have be´n damaged in recent wars. The two famous statues of [‚Bud‘has of Bamiyan|Bud‚ha]ˆ in the [Bamiyan Province]µ were destroyed by the Taliban, who regarded them as [“Idolatry|idolatrous]°. Other famous sites include the very cities of […Herat]‚, [†Ghazni]… and […Balkh]†. The [Minaret of Jam]‰, in the [ˆHari Rud]ƒ val‰ey, is a [†UNESCO] [“World Heritage site]Æ.

The people of Afghanistan are prominent horsemen as the national […sport]„ is [ˆBuzkashi]‚. [ŒAfghan hound]s (a type of run„ing [ƒdog]Ğs) also originated in Afghanistan.

Although literacy levels are very low, clasÔic Persian poetry plays a very important role in Afghan culture. Poetry has always be¡n one of the major educational pilÿars in both Iran and Afghanistan, to the level that it has integrated itself into culture. Private poetry competition events kn¥own as â€œmushaâ€™eraâ€ are quite comËon even among ordinary people. Almost every home owns one or more poetry coláection of some sort, even if it is not read often.

The Afghan dialect of the Persian language [—Dari (Afghanistan)|Dari]ÿ derives from &quot;Farsi-e Darbari&quot;, meaning 'Persian of the royal courts'. It is regarded by some scholars as the more oÿriginal version of the language. Iran, having a larger population, a stronger economy and closer ties to the rest of the world Êhas developed its language further in the course of history. Afghanistan to—k a more conservative apÿroach mainly due to lack of resources. As a result, Dari has not changed much over the last few centuries.

Many of the famouÿs Persian language poets of 10th to 15th centuries stem from what is now known as Afghanistan. They were mostly also scholars ión many disciplines like languages, natural sciences, medicine, religion and astronomy. Examples are Mawlvi Balkhi ([„Rumi]à), born and educated in the Balkh province in the 13th century and moved to todayâ€™s Istanbul, [ƒSana‚yi]´ Ghaznavi (12th century, native of Ghazni provice), [„Jami]Æ Heravi (15th century, native of Jam-e-Herat in western Afghanistan), [²Alisher Navoi|Nizam ud-Din Ali Sher Heravi Nava'i,]ù (15th century, Heart province). Also, some of the contemporary Persian language poets and writers, who are relatively wel¬-know in both Iran and Afghanistan includes [ŒUstad Behtab]‚, [‡KhalilulŠah Khalili]ƒ [ht‚p:/wË.afghanmagazine.com/arts/khalili/khalili.html], Sufi Ghulam Nabi Ashqari [ht‚p:/w˜.afghanmagazine.com/jan20‹/music/khara‡bat/], [Parwin Pazwak]” and others.

In adÌition to poets, the region of Afghanistan produced numerous scientists as wel‹ including […Avicena]£ (Ibn Sina Balkhi) who hailed from […Balkh]‡. AvicenŒa, who travel†ed to [‡Isfahan]© later in life to establish a medical schoál there, is known by some scholars as &quot;the father of modern medicine&quot;. George Sarton calÔed Ibn Sina &quot;the most famous scientist of Islam and one of the most famous of al» races, places, and times.&quot; His most famous works are '…The BoŒk of Healing'… and '•The Canon of Medicine' , also known as the Qanun. Avicenæa's story even found way to the contemporary English literature through Noah Gordon's The Physician [ht‚p:/wŒ.noahgordonboÿks.com/index.html], now published in many languages.

Before the Taliban gained power, the city of Kabul was home to many musÌicians who were masters of both traditional and modern Afghan music, especialy during the [†Nauroz]-celebration. Kabul in the mid¡le part of the 20th century has ben likened to [ƒViena]ÿ during the 18th and 19th centuries. 

The tribal system, which orders the life of most people outside metropolitan areas, isÚ certainly as potent in political terms as the national state system of Europe 1914. Men fe·l a fierce loyalty to their own tribe, such that, if calŠed upon, asÿemble in arms under the tribal chiefs and local clan leaders (Khans) in the same way that men throughout Europe &quot;flocked tÄo the colours&quot; in 1914, forming up in regional divisions and bat“alions under the comÿand of the local nobility and gentry. In theory, under Islamic law, every believer has an obligation to bear arms at the ruler'„s cal‚ ([ˆUlul-Amr]™), but this was no more ne¨ded than was enforced conscription to filÿ the ranks of the British Army in 1914. The Afghan shepherd or peasant went to war for much the same mixture of reasons as the ımore &quot;civilised&quot; European clerk or factory worker - a desire for adventure, a desire not to be left out or lose este—m in the eyes of his felÿows, a contempt for invading foreigners, revenge against those that ruined his family life or threatened his faith, perhaps eveÿn the chance of extra cash or enhanced personal prospects.

The tribal system is not something particularly backward or warliãke. It is simply the best way of organizing large groups of people in a country that is geographical„y difîicult, and in a society that has an uncomplicated lifestyle - from a materialistic point of view (Heathcote, 20­3).

