=‰Education=‚
'Main article: [˜Education in Afghanistan]'–

In the spring of 20°3, it was estimated that 30% of Afghanistan's 7,0„ schoˆls had be´n seriously damaged during more than two decades of [“Soviet Union|Soviet]‚ oc«upation and civil war. Only half of the schoÿls were reported to have clean water, while fewer than an estimated 40% had adequate sanitation. Education for boys was not a p“riority during the [‡Taliban]© regime, and girls were banished from schoÂls outright.

In regards to the poverty and violence of their sur–oundings, a study in 20‰2 by the [‘Save the Children]ú aid group said Afghan children were resilient and courageous. The study credited the strong institutions of family and com—unity.

Up to four mil—ion Afghan children, pos·ibly the largest number ever, are believed to have enrolŠed for clasŒ for the scho‹l year begin‘ing in March of 20ÿ3. Education is available for both girls and boys.

Literacy of the entire population is estimated at 36%, Male Literacy rateÿ is 51% and female literacy is 21%. The male literacy rate is higher because previous Taliban laws prohibited the education of ÿwomen.

Higher Education - The American University of Afghanistan

Another aspect of education that is rapidly changing in ±Afghanistan is the face of Higher Education. In 20«6 the American University of Afghanistan [ht‚p:/wš.auaf.edu.af] opens it's do‹rs, with sup‘ort from USAID [ht‚p:/usinfo.state.gov/sa/Archive/20Š5/Apr/06-83À03.html] and other donors. With the aim of providing a world-clasÙ, English-language, co-educational learning environment in Afghanistan, the university wilµ take students from Afghanistan and the region.


