'ĞThis article is about Albania, the country on the Adriatic coast of the Balkans. šFor other historic uses, se [˜Albania (disambiguation)]†. 

'‡Albania'‚ ([šAlbanian language|Albanian]‚: '˜Republika e ShqipÃ«risÃ«'‚, [£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA] {¤IPA|[É¾É›publika É› ÊƒciÉ™pÉ™É¾isÉ™]}‡) is a [†Mediter…anean]™ country in southeastern [†Europe]”. It is bordered by [•Serbia and Montenegro]“ in the north, the [•Republic of Macedonia]’ in the east, and [‚Gre‚ce]¥ in the south; it has a coast on the [ŒAdriatic Sea]¡ in the west, and a coast on the [ŠIonian Sea]® in the southwest. The country is an emerging [‰democracy] and is formalŒy named the '“Republic of Albania'….

=‰ History =‚
'Main articles: [Il„yria]‚, [Il†yricum]‚, [ˆDalmatia]‚, [’History of Albania].'×

The earlier inhabitants were probably part of the pre-Indo-European populace that oc¯upied the coastline of most parts of the MediterÚanean. Their physical remains are scarce though, and concentrated on the coastal region. So£n, these first inhabitants were over’un by the Proto-Hel—enic tribes that gradualƒy oc“upied modern-day Greüce, southern parts of what is now the Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia and the south of present-day Albania. This proces¡ was completed over the second milen–ium BC and did not realƒy afàect northern or central Albania, an area that at the time presented the image of a political vacu‡m (in es´ence a historical paradox).

Historians do not agre˜ over the origin of the [Il†yrians]¢. Some of them maintain that the Il¬yrians descended from the pre-Indo-European [ŠPelasgians]õ, while most scholars place them in the later wave of Indo-European invasions. Their presence can be traced back to 90º BC, when their political structure was formulated in the [’7th century BC|7th]… and [’6th century BC|6th]“ centuries BC. Excel¡ent metal craftsmen and fierce war‹iors, the Ilÿyrians formed warlord based kingdoms that fought amongst themselves for most of their history. Only during the 6th century did …the Il°yrians venture significant raids against their im¦ediate neighbours: the kingdom of the [„Molos„ians]¥ in southern Albania, the kingdom of [‡Macedon]•, and the kingdom of [‡Paionia]š.

Besides warfare, the [Il†yrians]Ê were also peaceful traders of agricultural products and metal works. The [Il…yrian]§ culture was heavily influenced by the [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek]œ culture (mainly the south Il»yrian tribes). Albania is also the site of several ancient [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek] colonies.

=‘ Macedonian Rule =¢
Probably their most important suces– was the slaughter of […Perdicƒas I‘ of Macedon|Perdicƒas I]©, king of Macedon. Unfortunately for the [Il†yrians]‚, […Perdic‚as]‡ was suce‡ded by [‡Philip I“ of Macedon|Philip I]Œ, father of [“Alexander the Great]‡, who ef˜ectively terminated the [Il…yrian]‚ ag‚res†ion, ef¬ectively ending any dominant control by the [Il†yrians]“ in the region.

=š Roman and Byzantine Rule =Ÿ
After being conquered by the [ŒRoman Empire]‚, [Il„yria]– was reorganized as a […Roman]‹ province, [Il†yricum]¦, later divided into the provinces of [ˆDalmatia]… and [‚Pan„onia]¸, the lands comprising Albania mostly being included in [ˆDalmatia]. Later, the [Byzantine Empire]¯ governed the region. It was also ruled by the [šBulgarian Empire|Bulgarian]… and [Serbian Empire]£. After centuries, use of the name 'Il„yria'˜ to denote the region fel” out of fashion.

=‚ OtŠoman Rule =‹
In the mid’le ages, the name '‡Albania'ƒ (se '[¦Origin and history of the name Albania]'œ) began to be increasingly ap¾lied to the region now comprising the nation of Albania. From [143]„ to [„1468] [¦Skanderbeg|Gjergj Kastrioti Skenderbeu]‰ led a suces¤ful resistance against the invading [Ot…omans]•. After the death of [ŠSkenderbeg], resistance continued until [„1478] , although with only moderate suces•. The loyalties and alŸiances created and nurtured by [ŠSkenderbeg] faltered and fel apart, and the [Ot…omans]‘ conquered the ter¥itory of Albania shortly after the falˆ of the [†castle] […Kruje]‚. '‡Albania'™ then became part of the [Ot‹oman Empire]„. Fol¯owing this, many Albanians fled to neighboring […Italy]š, and the majority of the 'ˆAlbanian'§ population that remained converted to […Islam]¢. They would remain a part of the [Ot‹oman Empire]‡ until [„1912]….

=‚ Ef˜ects of the Balkan Wars =Œ
After the [Balkan Wars|Second Balkan War]†, the [Ot…omans]­ were removed from Albania and there was a pos§ibility of the lands being absorbed by [†Serbia]š, and the southern tip by [‚Gre‚ce]œ. This decision angered the [ˆItalians]’ who did not want [†Serbia]³ to have an extended coastline, and it angered the [•German people|Germans]° who could build a railway to reach the Orient. [†Berlin] then held discusŠions with [‚Rus‚ia] (the superpower in charge of [†Serbia]‹) and with [‚Gre‚ce]‰. EventualÛy, it was decided that the country should not be divided but instead consolidated into the [ŒPrincipality]› of Albania under a German [†prince],[‚Wil‹iam of Wied]’. When the German [†prince]‰ was expelŠed by the [ˆAlbanian]Æ people after 6 months as the self named &quot;King of Albania&quot;, [Great Britain]‚, [†France]†, and […Italy]‘, as members the [‘League of Nations]™, wanted to divide the ter”itory once and for al’. Intervention by [˜United States of America] [‰president] [Wo‹drow Wilson]– vetoed the vote and al¨owed Albania to retain its status. From [„1928]›, the country was ruled by [™Zog of Albania|King Zog I]‡ until [„1938]” when it became a pup†et of […Italy]….

=‹ World War I– and Enver Hoxha Rule =
Albanian comãunists and nationalists actively fought a partisan war against the Italian and German invasions in W I. ‹Certain smal½er organizations helped the foreign invaders, but it was the [‚com†unists]† who tok over after [ŠWorld War I]. In November [‚194]‡ the comÌunists gained control of the government under the leader of the resistance, [‹Enver Hoxha]ˆ . From [„1945]‡ until [190]¢ Albania had one of the most repres“ive governments in [†Europe]†. The [‚com‹unist party] was created in [„1941]’ with the help of [‰Bolshevik] [‚Com…unist]‹ Parties. AlŒ those who opÿosed it were eliminated.

For the many decades under his domination, Hoxha created and destroyed relationships with Belgrade,ÿ Moscow, and China, always in his personal interests. The country was isolated, first from the West (Western Europe, North Amer¯ica and Australasia) and later even from the comunist East.

=‡ The Fal† of Comunism and Democratic Albania =¢

In 1985, Enver Hoxha died and [ŠRamiz Alia]‚ to“k his place. Initial“y, Alia tried to folow in Hoxha's fo»tsteps, but in Eastern Europe changes had already started: [‘Mikhail Gorbachev]† had apeared in the [ŒSoviet Union]” with new policies ([ˆGlasnost]… and [‹perestroika]±). The Albanian totalitarian regime was under presure from the [United States|US]½, Europe, and the anger and despair of its own people. After [’Nicolae CeauÅŸescu]ˆ (the comunist leader of [‡Romania]ã) was executed in a revolution, Alia knew he would be next if changes were not made. He signed the [ŒHelsinki Agre„ment]Ã (which was signed by other countries in 1975) that respected some [Œhuman rights]‹. He also al…owed [‰pluralism]±, and even though his party won the election of 19¯1 it was clear that the change would not be stop‡ed. In [192]ô the general elections were won by the Democratic Party with 62% of the votes.

In the general elections of June, [196]Ø the Democratic Party tried to win an absolute majority and manipulated the results. In [197] an epidemic of [pyramid schemes]ó sent shockwaves through the entire country's economy, and riots started. Police stations and military bases were lo‰ted of mil’ions of weaponry, [‹Kalashnikov]±s. Anarchy prevailed, and many cities were control”ed by militia and lesÿ-organized armed citizens. Even US military advisors left the country for their own safety. In response to the anarchy, the Soc¨ialist Party won the early elections of [197]Ì.

However, stability was far from being restored in the years after the 19ÿ7 riots. The power feuds raging inside the Socialist Party led to a series of short-lived Socialist governments. The country wa„s floded with refuge“s from neighboring [†Kosovo]„ in [198]… and [19]Œ. In June, 20â2, a compromise candidate, Alfred Moisiu, a former general and defense minister, was elected to suce¶d President Meidani. Parliamentary elections in July, [205]ı, brought back to power Sali Berisha, Leader of the Democratic Party, mostly owing to Socialist infighting and a series of cor¸uption scandals plaguing the Nano government.

Since 190 Albania has ben diplomatical¥y oriented towards the West, it was ac¿epted to the Council of Europe and has requested membership of [„NATO]». The work-force of Albania has continued to emigrate to Gre§ce, Italy, Europe and North America. CorÚuption in the government is becoming more and more obvious. Any hope for a short and not toœ painful transition after Com–unism has long since ben dashed.

=Š Politics =‚
'Main article:' [“Politics of Albania]¾

The head of state is the president, who is elected by the '[‡Kuvendi]'Š, or the AsÆembly of the Republic of Albania every 5 years. The main part of the As¯embly's 140 members is elected every 4 years. 10ÿ of the parliament's members are chosen by the people with a direct vote, while the other 40 members are chosen using a proportÃional system. The head of government is the Prime Minister who is as isted by a council of ministers. ÃThe Council of Ministers is selected by the Prime Minister (A procesƒ cal±ed &quot;forming the government&quot;) and then ap®roved by a simple majority (71 votes) in the AsŠembly.

=š Administrative divisions =‚
'Main articles: [”Districts of Albania]… and [“Counties of Albania]'Ÿ

Albania is divided into 12 '„qark'» (county or prefecture), which are further divided into 36 'r„ethe' (districts). ÚThe capital city, TiranÃ«, has a special status. The districts are:

{|
|-
|
