=‹ Geography =‚
'Main article: [”Geography of Albania]'„

[ªImage:Albania map.png|frame|Map of Albania]Ÿ

Albania consists of mostly [‚hil]†y and [ˆmountain]†ous teræain, the highest mountain, Korab in the district of Dibra reaching up to 2,753 metres (9,032&amp;nbsp;[fo•t (unit of length)|ft]¨). The country mostly has a continental [‡climate]Œ, with cold [†winter]Šs and hot [‚sum‚er]›s.

Besides capital city [†Tirana]ˆ, with 80,0§ inhabitants, the principal cities are [‚DurƒÃ«s]‚, [‡Elbasan]‚, [ˆShkodÃ«r]‚, [ŒGjirokastÃ«r]‚, [†VlorÃ«]… and [‡KorÃ§Ã«]‘. In Albanian gramÁar a word can have indefinite and definite forms, and this also apÒlies to city names: so both TiranÃ« and Tirana, ShkodÃ«r and Shkodra are used.

=‰ Economy =‚
'Main article: [’Economy of Albania]'¥

In Albania, half of the economical—y-active population stilŒ engaged in [‹agriculture]Ú and a fifth works abroad.

The country has almost no exports, and imports most if its goŠds from GreÑce and Italy. Money for imports comes from financial aid and from the money that [im†igrant]¬s working abroad - mostly in neighbouring Gre¢ce - bring to Albania. This is a go‚d [Šstatus quo]‡ businesŒ for both GreÄce and Italy. 

Albania's coastline on the Ionian Sea, near the Gre”k tourist island of […Corfu]ä, is becoming increasingly popular with foreign visitors due to its relatively unspoilt nature and go¯d beaches. However, the tourist industry is stil§ in its infancy.

Growth was strong 20Î3-05 and inflation is not a problem.

