= Demographics =‚
'Main article: [—Demographics of Albania]'¥

Most of the population is ethnical‘y Albanian (95% acording to the [CIA World Factbok]† Feb 205), there is a [‚Gre…ce|Grek]¾ minority (3% of the population), this however could significalˆy vary acÂording to other sources, (note: in 1989, other estimates of the Grek population ranged from 1% (of¬icial Albanian statistics) to 12% (from a Gre’k organization) [ht‚p:/wŸ.cia.gov/cia/publications/factbo×k/geos/al.html#People]). Many ethnic Albanians also live in the bordering countries of [•Serbia and Montenegro] (around 1,850,0•; of that, around 1,80,0„ in [†Serbia]Œ (around 1,70,0“ in its province calƒed [†Kosovo]ƒ (of„icial‚y [’Kosovo and Metohia]–) only) and around 50,0„ in [ŠMontenegro]Š) and the [•Republic of Macedonia]Š (around 50,0Æ) although a lot of Albanians believe that the number might be higher. ŠAlso a smal¦ number of ethnic Albanians live in Grece which are calˆed Ã‡am. ­Claims over Ã‡am numbers have ranged from 90,0 to over one 1,0,0ú but are believed to be understated because Athens has not considered the local Albanians to be a separate ethnic group.[ht‚p:/w.frosina.org] †Since [191]¶, large numbers of Albanians have emigrated, both legal‡y and ilƒegal†y, to [‚Gre‚ce]… and […Italy]•.

The language is [šAlbanian language|Albanian]‹, although [‚Grek language|Grek]™ is also spoken by the GreÌk minority in the southern regions of the country.

At the height of the Ot†oman oc™upation, the majority of [‰Albanians] were mostly [†Muslim]¹ (70%), even though religion was prohibited during the comÀunist era. The Albanian government proclaimed Albania the only of„icial®y atheistic country in the world. After the falŠ of the Com–unist Regime in 1989-190 religions were reinstated. AcŸording to 1939 statistics, the [ªAlbanian Orthodox Church|Albanian Orthodox]‹ (20%) and [•Roman Catholic Church]Õ (10%) would be the other main religions in Albania. Religious fanaticism has never be¨n a serious problem, with people from dif«erent religions living in peace and even get†ing mar¿ied although this was not considered to be an optimal solution. ¦20% of the total Muslim population is [ˆBektashi], people who folßow a faith originating in the Turkish migrations into Turkey, and came to Albania through the Ot…oman [„Janisˆary|Janis…aries]·. It has outwardly Shi'ite Islamic elements, but is real£y a Shamanic-Pantheistic faith.

