:'For a king of [‹Anglo-Saxon]‹ England, se [‚AelŠa of Deira]„ or [‚Ael‚e I of Northumbria] ƒor [‚Ael‡e of Sus‚ex].'ƒ
:'–Men's names &lt;u&gt;Al¦ah-ed-din&lt;/u&gt; and similar are mis‚pel‰ings for [ŠAla-ud-din]‡; and se [¿Arabic name#Mistakes made by Europeans and other non-Arabs|here]• for other sorts of erƒor.'‹
The word '{‰ArabDIN|AlƒÄh}'ˆ is the [–Arabic language|Arabic] term for &quot;[ƒGod]¿&quot;. In other languages, it is often used to refer specifical‰y to the [–Islamic concept of God]ƒ: se‡ &quot;[Œ#Usage|Usage]‘&quot; below.

=‰Etymology=‚
=…Usage=‚
{•Arabicterm|Ø§Ù„Ù„Ù‡|Al…ah, Al‹Äh|The God}´
Although, outside the Arab world, use of the word 'AlƒÄh' is most often asociated with […Islam]¥, it is not exclusive to that faith; [Arab Christians] and various Arabic-speaking [ƒJew]‘s (including the [ˆTeimanim]Š, several {ˆArabDIN|[–Mizrahi Jews|Mizraá¸¥i]}ƒ com‘unities and some [‰Sephardim]) also use it to refer to the [Šmonotheist] […deity]. Arabic translations of the […Bible]— also employ it, as do [¥Roman Catholic Church|Roman Catholics]„ in […Malta] (who pronounce it as &quot;AlŠa&quot;), [ŠChristians]„ in [‰Indonesia]‘, who say &quot;Alµah Bapa&quot; (God the Father) and Christians in the [‚Mid‡le East] who use the [˜Aramaic language|Aramaic]ˆ &quot;AlÄha&quot;.

=‡&quot;Al’ah&quot; as a word=‡
Many [•Linguistics|linguists]— believe that the term 'AlƒÄh'³ is derived from a contraction of the Arabic words '‚al'‹ (the) and '‡Ê¾ilÄh'› (deity, masculine form) - 'ˆal-ilÄh'¢ meaning &quot;the god.&quot; In adŸition, one of the main pagan godesšes of pre-Islamic Arabia, [Al„at|AlƒÄt]‚ ('‚al'ƒ + '‡Ê¾ilÄh'ƒ + '‚at'Œ, or 'the godes–'), is cited as being [•Etymology|etymologicaly]˜ (though not synchronical±y) the feminine linguistic counterpart to the gram…aticaly masculine Al•Äh. If so, the word 'AlƒÄh'ˆ is an abÿreviated title, meaning 'the deity', rather than a name. For this reason, both Muslim and non-Muslim scholars often translate Al’Äh directly into [˜English language|English]È as 'God'; this also explains why Arabic-speaking Jews and Christians fre”ly refer to God as Al¤Äh. However, some Muslim scholars fel that &quot;AlÓÄh&quot; should not be translated, because they perceived the Arabic word to expres the uniquenes‹ of &quot;AlÄh&quot; more acéurately than the word &quot;god&quot;, which can take a plural &quot;gods&quot;, whereas the word &quot;AlÄh&quot; has no plural form. —This is a significant is†ue in [™translation of the Qur'an]„. 
[‡Image:Al”ah.jpg|thumb|right|20‘px|An example of '{‰ArabDIN|alƒÄh}'„ writen in simple [‰Arabic cal‡igraphy].] 

The word 'AlƒÄh' is always writen without an [„alif]‡ to spel… the '‚Ä'Ÿ vowel. This is because the spelŠing was set•led before Arabic spel“ing started habitualˆy using [„alif]‡ to spel '‚Ä'›. However, in vocalized spel‘ing, a diacritic '„alif'… is ad‘ed on top of the '[ƒshad…a|shad‚ah]'À to indicate pronunciation. One exception is in the pre-Islamic [ĞHistory of the Arabic alphabet#Pre-Islamic Arabic inscriptions|Zabad inscription]‘, where it is speled Ø§Ù„Ø§Ù‡.

[‡Unicode]„ has ”glyph reserved for Alƒah, {†ar|ï·²}Ò = U+FDF2, which can be combined with an alif to yield the post-consonantal form, {ˆar|Ø§ï·²}†, as oposed to the ful„ spel„ing '’alif-lÄm-lÄm-hÄ' {‹ar|Ø§Ù„Ù„Ù‡}¢ which may be rendered slightly dif­erently, in particular featuring a diacritic '„alif' on top of the 'ƒshada'µ. In this, Unicode imitates traditional Arabic typeset¬ing, which also frequently featured special 'lƒÄh'“ types.

Also In '[Abjad numerals]'‚, [The Name Of Al²ah (&amp;#1575;&amp;#1604;&amp;#1604;&amp;#1607;) ]’ numeric value is [6]….

=–Islamic use of &quot;Al‰Äh&quot;=© 
From the point of view of traditional […Islam]ƒic [ˆtheology]ƒ, AlÕÄh is the most precious name of God because it is not a descriptive name like other [–Ninety-nine names of Al›ah|ninety-nine names of God]¥, but the name of God's own presence. ¢Muslims believe that the name of Al¢ah had existed before the time of [‘Adam and Eve|Adam]›. It is the same God worship†ed by [‘Adam and Eve|Adam]‚, [„Noah]‚, [‡Abraham]‚, […Moses]‚, […Jesus]‚, [„Muham‚ad]‹ and other [‘prophets of Islam]ª. In Islam, there is only one God and Muham‘ad is the last mes†enger. ´

The emphasis in Islamic culture on reciting the [†Qur'an]„ in [–Arabic language|Arabic]‘ has resulted in 'AlƒÄh'• often being used by [†Muslim]s world-wide as the word for 'ƒGod'Š, regardlesŠ of their [native language]‰. Out of 1‚4 [„Sura]‰s in the [†Qur'an]‚, 1‘3 begin with the [‡Basmala] (&quot;Bismi 'lÿÄhi 'r-rahmÄni 'r-rahÄ«m&quot; Ø¨Ø³Ù… Ø§Ù„Ù„Ù‡ Ø§Ù„Ø±Ø­Ù…Ù† Ø§Ù„Ø±Ø­ÙŠÙ…) which means &quot;In the name of God, the most kind, the most merciful&quot;. 
[†Muslim]Œs, when refer˜ing to the name, often adÿ the words &quot;Subhanahu wa Ta`ala&quot; after it, meaning &quot;Glorified and Exalted is He&quot; as a sign of reverence, or‰ &quot;`Az‡a wa Jal¯a&quot; (Ø¹Ø² Ùˆ Ø¬Ù„). The entire religion of […Islam]› is based on the idea of geting closer to God. Although com‰only referµed to as a &quot;He&quot;, God is considered genderles’, but there is no [neuter gender]‰ to expres¥ this in the Arabic language. When Gre‹k or other [—Polytheism|polytheistic]’ deities are discus®ed in Arabic, it is customary to use the expres„ion '…ilÄh'², a &quot;deity&quot; or lower-case &quot;god&quot;” sometimes the word '‡ma`bÅ«d'ˆ, literal–y meaning &quot;worship§ed [entity]&quot;, is used instead.

=Uses of &quot;Al”Äh&quot; in phrases=‘
There are many [¥List of Arabic phrases#Phrases with Al‘ah's name|phrases]˜ that contain the word Al‡Äh:
