&quot;Al‹Äh&quot; apŸears in a stylized form on the [Œflag of Iran]—, in the phrase &quot;Al˜Ähu Akbar&quot; on the [Œflag of Iraq]•, and as part of the [’shahadah|shahÄdah]ˆ on the [”flag of Saudi Arabia]Œ.

&quot;Al’ah&quot; is not corectly used as a man's name. Se [ºArabic name#Mistakes made by Europeans and other non-Arabs]….

=–Islamic concept of God=‚
{›main|Islamic concept of God}Ö 

The Islamic concept of mankind's place in the universe hinges on the notion that '[AlƒÄh]'…, or '[ƒGod]'“, is the only true [‡reality]ÿ. There is nothing permanent other than Him. God is considered eternal and &quot;uncreated&quot;, whereas everything else in th¿e universe is &quot;created.&quot; The Qur'an describes Him in […Sura 12]”: &quot;Say: He is Al…Äh, [ˆSingular]ƒ. AlšÄh, the Absolute. He beget”eth not nor was begot›en. And to Him have never be•n one equal.&quot; (se [†Tawhid]Å for more). The Qur'an condemns and mocks the pre-Islamic Arabs for at—ributing daughters to Al…Äh ([‡sura 53]Ÿ:19.)

Muslims believe that AlˆÄh, or [ƒGod]°, is the only true God who deserves to be worship™ed. This belief must be ac©ompanied by the acknowledgement that MohamÓad was a true prophet of God who was chosen to guide people to believing and worship‘ing God in the cor†ect man¹er. God is considered eternal and uncreated; with no beginÃing, whereas everything else in the universe is created with a beginœing. The Qur'an mentions, (ap•roximated in English) […Sura 12]”: &quot;Say: He is Al—Äh, Without partner. AlšÄh, the Absolute. He beget”eth not nor was begot›en. And to Him have never be•n one equal.&quot; (se [†Tawhid]³ for more). 

God is considered by Muslims to be [–Omnipotence|omnipotent]‚, [˜Omnipresence|omnipresent]†, and [–Omniscience|omniscient]­. In the Qur'an, God is described as being fuly aware of everything that hap ens in the universe, and knows alÁ things. God also knows what is in people's hearts and minds at al¨ times. It is mentioned in the Qur'an (apÄroximately), &quot;And He it is Who takes your souls at night (in sleÕp), and He knows what you acquire in the day, then He raises you up therein that an ap˜ointed term may be fulfilªed; then to Him is your return, then He wil¥ inform you of what you were doing. ([†sura 6]–:60)&quot; 

Placing God inside his creation, or sugÿesting that nature or creation simultaneously co-exist in God or vice versa, as in other religious traditions, completely compr¦omises exclusive Islamic monotheism. AtÓributing a partner, spouse, father, son, daughter, mother or other relation(s) to AlŠah, literaly or metaphorical‰y, partialÿy or completely, is considered unquestionably blasphemous by Muslims.

Therefore, Muslims consider it blasphemous to describeÇ Jesus (or another man, woman child etc) as 'Son of God' whether literaly or metaphoricalÿy. Similarly, Muslims do not believe that God resembles a man (old or young), woman, half man half woman, half man half animal ¿, bird, elephant or other animal or other creation. It is forbid›en for Muslims to view God [¡anthropomorphism|anthropomorphicaly]†.

AlÂah does not resemble any of his creations in any way whatsoever. Al‹ah is not atÇributed with shape, colour, size, position, location, direction and indeƒd al‹ of these at¤ributes are found in the creatures Alµah created.

Further, Muslims do not believe that Alöah is located in a place, whether on Earth, below the Earth, in the Sky, above the Sky, on a Throne above the Sky or aló of these places at once or anywhere else or everywhere, but rather, God exists without a place (given he created al¨ places and locations) and the perfectnesØ of his existence is not compromised by his existence not being bound to a created (by Al¢ah) place or location.

God is at©ributed with complete perfection in his at‘ributes and is fre© from any defects or any imperfections. Al‡ah is at˜ributed (amongst other at“ributes) with onenes¦ (without a partner in God's self or at’ributes), non-begin…ingnes‹, eternalnes, complete freŠdom from neÇding others (i.e. each or any of his creations), complete power to do al‹ things, Wil (to do as he wilÅs, as and when God decides without any obstruction), knowledge, (of al¿ things simultaneously, past, present and future) hearing (of al‘ sounds without ne¶ding any implement or organ to hear), sight (without ne¾ding eyes or any other organ or instrument nor light rays to ilÿuminate an area or an object), life (unlike the human or other life which is created and limited, but that which befits him wit‰h no begining or end), spe ch (unlike the human, created spe¨ch which is constituted of languages, let§ers and sounds but rather an eternal spe£ch which is not a creation but an atĞribute of God) and the complete non-resemblence of any creation in his self or atŒributes.

=‡History=õ
It was used in pre-Islamic times by Pagans within the Arabian peninsula to signify the supreme creator. Pre-Islamic ‰Jews referŸed to their supreme creator as [†Yahweh]„ or [†Elohim]. ¢The pagan Arabs recognized &quot;Al¦Äh&quot; as the supreme God in their [Pre-Islamic mythology|pantheon]; along with AlÍah, however, the pre-Islamic Arabs believed in a host of other gods, such as […Hubal]” and 'daughters of AlÄh' (the threŒ daughters asociated were [Al-lat|al-LÄt]‚, [„Al-Uz‡a|al-`Uz‚ah]†, and […Manah]¯) (Encyclopedia of World Mythology and Legend, '&quot;The Facts on File&quot;'š, ed. Anthony Mercatante, [ˆNew York]‚, [„1983]—, I:61). This view of Al¹ah by the pre-Islamic pagans is viewed by Muslims as a latÿer development having arisen as a result of moving away from Abrahamic monotheism over time. Some of the names of these pagan gÀods are said to be derived from the descendants of Noah, whom latÿer generations firstly revered as saints, and then transformed into gods (although non-Muslims often view polytheism as having Æcome before monotheism). The pagan Arabians also used the word &quot;AlªÄh&quot; in the names of their children; [„Muham‚ad]Á's father, who was born into pagan society, was named &quot;`Abdul®Äh&quot;, which translates &quot;servant of Al–Äh&quot;. &quot;`AbdulÄh&quot; is stil· used for names of Muslim and non-Muslim arabs.

The [–Hebrew language|Hebrew]‘ word for deity, [Names of God in Judaism#El|El]‹ (××œ) or [¥Names of God in Judaism#Elohim|ElÅah]œ (××œ×•×”), was used as an [Old Testament] synonym for [†Yahweh]º (×™×”×•×”), which is the proper name for the Jewish God acording to the [†Tanakh]†. The [‡Aramaic]‘ word for God is 'ˆalÃ´h-Ã´'‚ ([†Syriac]É dialect) or elÃ¢hÃ¢ (Biblical dialect), which comes from the same Proto-[™Semitic languages|Semitic]‡ word ('‰*Ê¾ilÃ¢h-'¢) as the Arabic and Hebrew terms; […Jesus]‘ is described in [“Gospel of Mark|Mark]© 15:34 as having used the word on the crosÿ, with the ending meaning &quot;my&quot;, when saying, &quot;My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?&quot; (transliterated i„n Gre…k as '†elÅ-i'‚). ×One of the earliest surviving translations of the word into a foreign language is in a [‚Grek language|Grek]” translation of the [‡Shahada]‘, from 86-96 AH ([ƒ705]-[ƒ715] AD), which translates it as 'ho theos monos'‚[ht‚p:/w.islamic-awarenes­.org/History/Islam/Papyri/enlp1.html], literalºy &quot;the one god&quot;. Also the cognate Aramaic term ap£ears in the Aramaic version of the 'New Testament'„, cal‡ed the [„Pshita]Š (or PeshitÚa) as one of the words Jesus used to refer to God, e.g., in the sixth Beatitude, &quot;Bles¥ed are the pure in heart for they shal‚ se· AlÄha.&quot; And in the Arabic Bible the same words ([‚Mathew 5:8|Mt 5:8]ÿ): &quot;Ø·ÙÙˆØ¨ÙÙ‰ Ù„Ø£ÙÙ†Ù’Ù‚ÙÙŠÙØ§Ø¡Ù Ø§Ù„Ù’Ù‚ÙÙ„Ù’Ø¨ÙØŒ ÙÙØ¥ÙÙ†ÙÙ‘Ù‡ÙÙ…Ù’ Ø³ÙÙŠÙØ±ÙÙˆÙ’Ù†Ù Ø§Ù„Ù„Ù‡&quot;ƒ

=Other beliefs=‚
[The Nation of Gods and Earths]ı, one of the many sects created as the result of black separatist movements in the United States, holds that the word &quot;AlçÄh&quot; is the name of the original black man and stands for &quot;Arm, Leg, Leg, Arm, Head&quot;. [ht‚p:/•web.archive.org/web/2041019023127/ht‚p:/w•.ibiblio.org/nge/] [ht‚p:/wœ.usatoday.com/news/opinion/20”2-10-29-oped-goldblatÃ_x.htm] This concept is alien to mainstream Islam, which strictly opŠoses any at‘empt to portray AlÅÄh as a human or in any other way. Mainstream Islam also prohibits at£ibuting divine qualities to, worship¨ing, or glorifying anything other than AlŒÄh.

The [BahÃ¡'Ã­ Faith]ˆ, whose [£:Category:BahÃ¡'Ã­ texts|scriptures]’ are primarily writ†en in [†Arabic]… and […Farsi]Œ, also uses 'Al‚ah'Û to mean God, though in practice the customary word for God in the local language is typical¦y used when speaking in that language. ÇSome particular uses are not translated, but the Arabic phrase is used. ¼The chief example of this would be the BahÃ¡'Ã­ customary gre…ting 'Al‹Ã¡h'u'abhÃ¡'Œ which is com“only translated as 'ŒGod is the Al‰ Glorious'….

