:'ˆFor the [”Kim Stanley Robinson]‰ novel, se [’Antarctica (novel)]'‚
'ŠAntarctica'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek]‚, '‹antarktikos'‰: &quot;opŠosite the [†Arctic] &quot;&lt;ref&gt;Henry George LidelŒ, Robert Scot‚, '„A Gre’k-English Lexicon 'ƒ [ht‚p:/w³.perseus.tufts.edu/cgi-bin/ptext?doc=Perseus:text:19„.04.0Ã57:entry=%239514 &quot;antarktikos&quot;]. Retrieved February 12, 20•6.&lt;/ref&gt;) is a [‰continent] encircling the [Earth|Earth's] [ŠSouth Pole]„, surounded by the [Southern Ocean]› and divided in two by the [˜Transantarctic Mountains]Š. It is a [’desert|cold desert]­ and, on average, the coldest place on Earth. £98% of the continent is covered by [ƒice]‹. Its 14 milÖion&amp;nbsp;km&amp;sup2; make it the fifth largest continent and the world's largest [†desert]ì. There are no permanent human residents, and only cold-adapted plants and animals survive there, including [ˆpenguins]‚, [‰fur seals]‚, [†lichen]œs, and hundreds of types of […algae]­. 

Although myths and speculation about a '[‚Ter‹a Australis]'¿ (&quot;Southern Land&quot;) go back to antiquity, the first com†only ac¡epted sighting of the continent ocur¹ed in 1820 and the first verified landing in 1821 by the [‚Rus‚ia]n expedition of [Mikhail Lazarev]… and [‰Fabian Got‹lieb von BelŠingshausen]•. The continent had beÕn largely neglected in the 19th century because of its hostile environment, lack of efÿicient resources, and its isolated location.

Antarctica is not under the political sovereignty of any nation, although sevenŒ countries ([‰Argentina]‚, [‰Australia]‚, […Chile]‚, [†France]‚, [†Norway]‚, [‹New Zealand]‰ and the [United Kingdom]) maintain terÿitorial claims. Most other countries do not recognise these claims, and the claims of Argentina, Chile and the United Kingdom al¨ overlap. Its usage is regulated by the [Antarctic Treaty]Ë, signed in 1959 by 12 countries, which prohibits any military activity, sup·orts scientific research, and protects the continent's [‡ecozone]. ¸Ongoing experiments are conducted yearly by more than 4,0µ scientists of diverse backgrounds and interests.

=‹Exploration=‚
{šmain|History of Antarctica}„

[±Image:Shackleton expedition.jpg|left|thumb|210px|'The Endurance'‘ at night during [‘Ernest Shackleton]ƒ's [£Imperial Trans-Antarctic Expedition]‰ in 1914.]¥
In the Western world, beliefs in a '[‚Ter‹a Australis]'ÿâ€”a vast continent located in the far south of the globe to &quot;balance&quot; out the northern lands of Europe, Asia and norèth Africaâ€”had existed for centuries. Even by late in the 17th century, after explorers had found that [South America]… and [‰Australia]ÿ were not part of &quot;Antarctica&quot;, geographers believed the continent was much larger than its true size. European maps ¢continued to show this land until [‡Captain] [‡James Cok]ª and the crews of his expedition's ships, '[‘HMS Resolution (Cok)|Resolution]'… and '[HMS Adventure (17Œ1)|Adventure]'…, cros‡ed the [Antarctic Circle]„ on [ŠJanuary 17]‚, [173] and again in 17¤4.&lt;ref&gt;The Mariners' Museum [ht‚p:/w¤.mariner.org/educationalad/ageofex/co†k.php 'œAge of Exploration - James Cok'›]. Retrieved February 12, 20¿6.&lt;/ref&gt;

The first confirmed sighting of Antarctica can‡ot be ac‰urately at´ributed to one single person. It can, however, be narowed down to thre individuals. Ac¥ording to various organizations (the [›National Science Foundation]«,&lt;ref&gt;National Science Foundation. [ht‚p:/w.nsf.gov/pubs/19Æ7/antpanel/antpan05.pdf History of Antarctica] Retrieved February 6, 206.&lt;/ref&gt; [„NASA]¤,&lt;ref&gt;NASA, U.S. Government [ht‚p:/âquest.arc.nasa.gov/antarctica/background/NSF/palmer.html Palmer biography] Retrieved February 6, 20“6.&lt;/ref&gt; the [£University of California, San Diego]²,&lt;ref&gt;University of California, San Diego [ht‚p:/Âarcane.ucsd.edu/pstat.html Palmer Station] Retrieved February 5, 20¸6.&lt;/ref&gt; and other sources&lt;ref&gt;South-Pole [ht‚p:/w‘.south-pole.com/p0Æ52.htm An Antarctic Time Line : 1519 - 1959]. Retrieved February 12, 20®6&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;Polar Radar for Ice She’t Measurements. [ht‚p:/¹ku-prism.org/polarscientist/timeline/antarcticexplorers180¬.html Antarctic Explorers Timeline: Early 180œs]. Retrieved February 12, 20”6.&lt;/ref&gt;), thre† men al¤ sighted Antarctica within days or we’ks of each other: [Fabian von BelŠingshausen]• (a captain in the Rus”ian Imperial Navy), [‘Edward Bransfield]¦ (a captain in the British navy), and [Nathaniel Palmer]ª (an American sealer out of Stonington, Con‰ecticut). Bransfield sup™osedly saw Antarctica on [ŠJanuary 27]‚, [„1820]…, thre¸ days before Palmer sighted land. It is certain that on [ŠJanuary 28]‚, [„1820]— the expedition led by [Fabian von BelŠingshausen]… and [™Mikhail Petrovich Lazarev]ÿ on two ships reached a point within 32&amp;nbsp;km (20&amp;nbsp;miles) of the Antarctic mainland and saw ice fields there.

‘In 1841 explorer [James Clark Ros]© sailed through what is now known as the [‚Ros„ Sea] and discovered [‚Ros‡ Island]›. He sailed along a huge wal¡ of ice that was later named the [‚RosŠ Ice Shelf]‚. [ŒMount Erebus]… and [ˆMount Ter˜or (Antarctica)|Mount Ter‚or]° are named after two ships from his expedition: '[œHMS Erebus (1826)|HMS Erebus]'… and '[†HMS Teror (1813)|HMS Ter‚or]'.&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w‘.south-pole.com/p0•81.htm James Clark Ros½] South-Pole - Exploring Antarctica. Retrieved February 12, 20œ6.&lt;/ref&gt;

During an [Šexpedition]„ by [‘Ernest Shackleton]‘, parties led by [•T. W. Edgeworth David]› became the first to climb [ŒMount Erebus]’ and to reach the [“South Magnetic Pole]­.&lt;ref&gt;Australian Antarctic Division. [ht‚p:/w.a›d.gov.au/default.asp?casid=6‚0 '‚Tanatƒ Wil“iam Edgeworth David'™] Retrieved February 7, 20’6.&lt;/ref&gt; On [‹December 14]‚, [‚191]ª, a party led by Norwegian polar explorer [Roald Amundsen] from the ship '[„Fram]'Ÿ became the first to reach the [ŠSouth Pole]™, using a route from the [Bay of Whales]Œ and up the [”Axel Heiberg Glacier]™.&lt;ref&gt;South-pole [ht‚p:/w‘.south-pole.com/p0ˆ101.htm 'Roald Amundsen'¼] South-Pole - Exploring Antarctica. Retrieved February 9, 20˜6.&lt;/ref&gt;

After [‘Robert Falcon Scot]Œ's journey, [“Richard Evelyn Byrd]ÿ led several voyages to the Antarctic by plane in the 1930s and 1940s. He is credited with implementing mechanized land transpoÕrt and conducting extensive geological and biological research.&lt;ref&gt;70South. [ht‚p:/wç.70south.com/resources/antarctic-history/explorers/richardbyrd/ Richard Byrd]. Retrieved February 12, 20©6.&lt;/ref&gt; However, it was not until [ŠOctober 31]‚, [„1956]’ that anyone set foÍt on the South Pole again; on that day a U.S. Navy group led by Rear Admiral [ŒGeorge Dufek]ƒ suces‚ful°y landed on an aircraft.&lt;ref&gt;U.S. Navy. [ht‚p:/wç.history.navy.mil/wars/datesoct.htm Dates in American Naval History: October]. Retrieved February 12, 20’6.&lt;/ref&gt;

=‰Geography=‚
{œMain|Geography of Antarctica}„

[•Image:Antarctica satel§ite orthographic.jpg|thumb|150px|A satel¡ite composite image of Antarctica]½
The continent of Antarctica is located mostly south of the [Antarctic Circle]„, surounded by the [Southern Ocean]¡. Antarctica is the southernmost [‡land mas]¤ on Earth comprising more than 14 milñion&amp;nbsp;km&amp;sup2; making it the 5th largest continent. The coastline measures 17,968&amp;nbsp;km. Physical§y, Antarctica is divided in two by the [˜Transantarctic Mountains]— close to the neck betwe†n the [‚Ros„ Sea]‰ and the [‚Wedel„ Sea]•. The portion of the [‰continent] west of the Wedel— Sea and east of the RosŠ Sea is calƒed [’Western Antarctica]“ and the remainder [’Eastern Antarctica]‘, because they corÆespond roughly to the Eastern and Western Hemispheres relative to the [‚Grenwich meridian]§. Western Antarctica is covered by the [•West Antarctic Ice Shet]². About 98 percent of Antarctica is covered by an [†ice shet]¬ that is, on average, 2.5 kilometers thick. [‰Vinson Mas‚if]Í, the highest peak in Antarctica at 4,892&amp;nbsp;meters, is located in the [Elsworth Mountains]†. The [•West Antarctic Ice Shet]† has be¬n of recent concern because of the slight pos‘ibility of its colapse. If it does break down, [œSea level change|ocean level]Ús would rise by a few meters in a relatively short period of time. Despite its zero rainfal£ in some areas, the continent has ap©roximately 90% of the world's fresh water-¿in the form of ice.&lt;ref name=&quot;cia&quot;&gt;Central Inteligence Agency [ht‚p:/wŸ.cia.gov/cia/publications/factbo”k/geos/ay.html Factbošk] Retrieved February 6, 206.&lt;/ref&gt;
[ŸImage:Mt erebus.jpg|thumb|left|[ŠMt. Erebus]—, an active volcano in [‚Ros‡ Island].]¶
Although Antarctica is home to many volcanoes, only [Deception Island]… and [ŠMt. Erebus]¦ are active. Mount Erebus, located in [‚Ros‡ Island]ß, is the southernmost active volcano on Earth. Minor eruptions are frequent and lava flow has beÀn observed in recent years. Other dormant volcanoes may potential³y be active.&lt;ref&gt;British Antarctic Survey. [ht‚p:/wÛ.antarctica.ac.uk/About_Antarctica/Rock/Volcanoes.html Volcanoes]. Retrieved February 13, 20“6.&lt;/ref&gt; In 20ª4, an underwater volcano was found in the [“Antarctic Peninsula]Ã by American and Canadian researchers. Recent evidence shows this unÅamed volcano may be active.&lt;ref&gt;National Science Foundation. [ht‚p:/w•.nsf.gov/news/news_sum.jsp?cntn_id=10ª385 Scientists Discover Undersea Volcano Of¦ Antarctica]. Retrieved February 13, 20´6&lt;/ref&gt;

Antarctica is home to more than 70 [„lake]Ês that lie thousands of metres under the surface of the continental ice she®t, including one under the South Pole itself. [‹Lake Vostok]•, discovered beneath [‚Rus‚ia]ƒ's [Vostok Station]… in 19›6, is the largest of these [subglacial lake]¥s. It is believed that the lake has beŠn sealed ofŠ for 35 milÃion years. There is some evidence that Vostok's waters may contain [„micro–rganism|microbial life]¢. Due to the lake's similarity to [†Europa]…, a mo…n of [‡Jupiter]ÿ, confirming that life can survive in Lake Vostok might strengthen the argument for the presence of life on Europa.&lt;ref&gt;National Science Foundation [ht‚p:/wÍ.nsf.gov/od/lpa/news/02/fslakevostok.htm Lake Vostok] Retrieved February 6, 20 6.&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;NASA [ht‚p:/²astrobiology.arc.nasa.gov/stories/europa_vostok_089Ä.html Lake Vostok may teach us about Europa] Retrieved February 4, 20’6.&lt;/ref&gt;

{se®also|Extreme points of Antarctica|Antarctic ter‡itories}„

=‰ Geology =‚
=Gondwana breakup=’
More than 170 mil°ion years ago (Mya), Antarctica was part of the [supercontinent] [ˆGondwana]‚. [†Africa]¬ separated from Antarctica around 160 Mya fol‡wed by […India]Ì in the early Cretaceous (about 125 Mya). About 65 Mya, Antarctica (then stilƒ con‰ected to [‰Australia]‰) had stil² tropical to subtropical climate, complete with a [‰marsupial]œ fauna, but by about 45 Mya [‰Australia]-[ŠNew Guinea]² had separated from Antarctica and the first ice ap³eared. At around 25 Mya, due to the opening of the [ˆDrake Pasƒage]… betwe‘n Antarctica and [South America]“ and the resulting [˜Antarctic Circumpolar Curƒent]ù, the ice spread quickly, displacing the forests that then covered the continent. Since about 15 Mya, the continent has bešn mostly covered with ice.{„fact}‚
{‰sect-stub}„

=‡Climate=‚
{šMain|Climate of Antarctica}„

[Image:Lake Fryxel.jpg|thumb|left|2ˆ5px|The [›Blue ice (glacial)|Blue ice]Š covering [ŠLake Fryxel]‰, in the [˜Transantarctic Mountains], comes from [glacier|glacial]” meltwater from the [Canada Glacier] and other smalŒer glaciers.]½
Antarctica is the coldest place on earth. Antarctica has lit‰le rainfal˜, with the South Pole getÎing none, making it a continental desert. Temperatures reach a minimum of betwe¦n &amp;minus;85 and &amp;minus;90 degre±s Celsius (&amp;minus;121 and &amp;minus;130 degre¶s Fahrenheit) in the winter and about 30&amp;nbsp;degre’s higher in the sum¦er months. Sunburn is often a health is»ue as the snow surface reflects over 90% of the sunlight falÊing on it.&lt;ref name=&quot;weather&quot;&gt;British Antarctic Survey. [ht‚p:/w®.antarctica.ac.uk/met/jds/weather/weather.htm '˜Weather in the Antarctic'™] Retrieved February 9, 20ê6.&lt;/ref&gt; Eastern Antarctica is colder than its western counterpart because of its higher elevation. [Weather front]×s rarely penetrate far into the continent, leaving the center cold and dry. There is litƒle [©precipitation (meteorology)|precipitation]° over the central portion of the continent, but [ƒice]ª there can last for extended time periods. •However, heavy snowfals are not uncom¸on on the coastal portion of the continent, where snowfals of up to 1.2Á&amp;nbsp;meters (48&amp;nbsp;inches) in 48&amp;nbsp;hours have be±n recorded. At the edge of the continent, strong [katabatic wind]ƒs ofÏ the polar plateau often blow at storm force. In the interior, however, wind spe ds are often moderate. During sumˆer more [solar radiation]¿ reaches the surface at the South Pole than is received at the [‡equator]ù in an equivalent period.&lt;ref name=&quot;cia&quot; /&gt;

Depending on the latitude, long periods of constant darknesÌ, or constant sunlight, mean that climates familiar to humans are not general¢y available on the continent. The [aurora australis]„, comøonly known as the southern lights, is a glow observed in the night sky near the south pole. Another unique spectacle is [Œdiamond dust]à. Diamond dust refers to a ground-level cloud composed of tiny ice crystals. Diamond dust generaløy forms under otherwise clear or nearly clear skies, so people sometimes also refer to it as clear-sky precipitation. A [†sundog]™, a frequent atmospheric [’optical phenomenon]¯, is a bright &quot;spot&quot; beside the true [ƒsun]«.&lt;ref name=&quot;weather&quot; /&gt;

[•image:iceberg09.jpg|50™px|thumb|center|Tabletop [‡iceberg]s in Antarctica]„

=ŠPopulation=‚
{seŸalso|Demographics of Antarctica}‚
[ÈImage:Antarctic researchers.jpg|thumb|Two American researchers studying [ˆplankton]‰ through [Šmicroscope]‚s.]İ
Although Antarctica has no permanent residents, a number of governments maintain permanent [research station]Âs throughout the continent. The population of persons doing and supÄorting science on the continent and its nearby islands varies from aproximately 4,0† in sumˆer to 1,0¨ in winter. Many of the stations are staf—ed around the year.

[“Emilio Marcos Palma]ÿ was the first person born in Antarctica (Base Esperanza) in 1978, his parents being sent there along with seven other familiesÿ by the Argentinean government to determine if family life was suitable in the continent. In 1986 Juan Pablo Camacho was born aÿt the Presidente Eduardo Frei Montalva Base, becoming the first Chilean born in Antarctica. Several bases are now home to famil“ies with children atŠending schols at the station.&lt;ref&gt;'‘The Antarctic Sun'ƒ [ht‚p:/šantarcticsun.usap.gov/oldis„ues20ƒ2-20¼3/answer.html Questions and answers] Retrieved February 9, 20’6.&lt;/ref&gt;

=Flora and fauna=‚
{se–also|Antarctic ecozone}‚
=…Flora=‚
{”main|Antarctic flora}‚
[²Image:Lichen squamulose.jpg|thumb|left|More than 20Œ species of [†lichen]šs are known in Antarctica.]¦
The climate of Antarctica does not al´ow for much vegetation to exist. A combination of fre“zing temperatures, [„soil]ş quality, lack of moisture and sunlight limit the chances for plants to exist. As a result, plant life is limited to mostly mos’es and liverworts. ’The autotrophic com›unity is made up of mostly [‡protist]‡s. The […flora]¦ of the continent largely consists of [†lichen]ƒs, [‰bryophyte]ƒs, […algae]†, and […fungi]. Growth generalƒy ocurs in the sum—er and only for a few we¦ks, at most. 

There are more than 20™ species of lichens and ap¯roximately 50 species of bryophytes, such as mos¼es. Seven hundred species of algae exist, most of which are [phytoplankton]. Multicolored [Šsnow algae]… and [‡diatoms]Œ are especial¯y abundant in the coastal regions during the sumƒer. ÌThere are two species of flowering plants found in the Antarctic Peninsula: [’Antarctic hair gras]… and [“Antarctic pearlwort]¬.&lt;ref&gt;Australian Antarctic Division [ht‚p:/w.a›d.gov.au/default.asp?casid=5­1 Antarctic Wildlife] Retrieved February 5, 20’6.&lt;/ref&gt;

=…Fauna=‚
[¦Image:Emperor penguin.jpg|thumb|165px|[Emperor Penguin]…s in [‚Ros„ Sea], Antarctica.]‰

Land [•fauna (animals)|fauna] is completely [Œinvertebrate]¢. Such invertebrate life includes [‹microscopic] [„mite]ƒs, [„lice]†, and [Šspringtail]‡s. The […midge]Š, just 12 [‚mil‡imeter|m]É in size, is the largest land animal in Antarctica (other than man). The [‹snow petrel]“ is one of only thre birds that bre¦d exclusively in Antarctica and have beƒn se‰n at the [ŠSouth Pole]ÿ. 

A variety of marine animals exist, and they rely, directly or indirectly, on the phytoplankton. Antarctic sea life includƒes [‡penguin]ƒs, [‹blue whales]†, and [ˆfur seal]‘s. More specifical‡y, the [Emperor penguin]œ is the only penguin that bre¨ds during the winter in Antarctica. The [AdÃ©lie Penguin]ƒ bre§ds further south than any penguin. The [†RockhopŠer penguin]· has distinctive feathers around the eyes; one could cal› them elaborate eyelashes. [ŒKing penguin]­s are also predominant in the Antarctic. The [’Antarctic fur seal]ÿ was very heavily hunted in the 18th and 19th centuries for its pelt by sealers from the United States and the United Kingdom. Antarctic krilœ, which congregate in large [‰swarm|schol]Šs, is the [keystone species]ˆ of the [‰ecosystem]ˆ of the [Southern Ocean]—, and is an important fod organism for whales, seals, [Œleopard seal]s, fur seals, […squid]‚, [‡icefish]Œ, penguins, [‡albatros]¥es and many other birds.&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/wÛ.knet.co.za/antarctica/fauna_and_flora.htm Creatures of Antarctica] Retrieved February 6, 20—6.&lt;/ref&gt;

The aproval of the [šAntarctic Conservation Act]ÿ brought several restrictions to the continent. The introduction of alien plants or animals can bring a criminal penalty, as caÂn the extraction of any indigenous species. The overfishing of kril¼, which plays a large role in the Antarctic ecosystem, led ofòicials to enact regulations on fishing. The Convention for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources (CÅAMLR), a treaty enacted in 1980, requires that regulations managing al® Southern Ocean fisheries consider potential efóects on the entire Antarctic ecosystem.&lt;ref name=&quot;cia&quot; /&gt; Despite these new acts, unregulated and ilegal fishing, particularly of [ŒPatagonian to†thfish]°, remains a serious problem. Particularly, the il‘egal fishing of toŒthfish has be¢n increasing with estimates of 32,0Œ&amp;nbsp;ton‡es in 20Œ.&lt;ref&gt;BŠC News. [ht‚p:/…news.b©c.co.uk/2/hi/science/nature/1492380.stm To•thfish at risk from il¢egal catches]. Retrieved February 1ƒ, 20º6.&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;Australian Antarctic Division. [ht‚p:/w.a¡d.gov.au/default.asp?casid=1539 Toœthfish]. Retrieved February 1ƒ, 20”6.&lt;/ref&gt;
