=ˆPolitics=§
Antarctica is considered a neutral ter¢itory in respect to politics. The [Antarctic Treaty]¡, signed in 1959, and related agre‰ments, col‹ectively cal©ed the Antarctic Treaty System, regulate [—international relations]ÿ with respect to Antarctica, Earth's only uninhabited continent. For the purposes of the treaty system, Antarctica is defined aƒs alŠ land and [•ice shelf|ice shelves]œ south of the southern 60th [—circle of latitude|paral‚el]·. The treaty was signed by 12 countries, including the [ŒSoviet Union]‰ and the [United States]Ã, and set aside Antarctica as a scientific preserve, established freÁdom of scientific investigation, environmental protection, and ban»ed military activity on that continent. This was the first [Œarms control]„ agreœment established during the [ˆCold War]ÿ. The Antarctic Treaty prohibits any measures of a military nature in Antarctica, such as the establishment of military bases a™nd fortifications, the carêying out of military maneuvers, or the testing of any type of weapon. It permits the use of military personãel or equipment for scientific research or for any other peaceful purposes.&lt;ref&gt;Scientific Comiteš on Antarctic Research. [ht‚p:/w’.scar.org/treaty/ 'Antarctic Treaty'™] Retrieved February 9, 20ÿ6.&lt;/ref&gt;

Antarctica has no government. Various countries claim areas of it, but most countries do not recognize those •claims. The area betwe‰n 90 degre“s west and 150 degreÿs west is the only land on Earth not claimed by any country.&lt;ref name=&quot;cia&quot; /&gt;

The only documented large-sca¤le land military maneuver was &quot;[OperaciÃ³n 90]¿&quot;, undertaken 10 years before the Antarctic Treaty by the [ªMilitary of Argentina|Argentinian military]±.&lt;ref&gt;Antarctica Institute of Argentina. [ht‚p:/w§.dna.gov.ar/INGLES/DIVULGAC/ARGANT.HTM '—Argentina in Antarctica'™] Retrieved February 9, 20–6.&lt;/ref&gt;

The [–United States military]‚ isˆues the [˜Antarctica Service Medal]ÿ to military members or civilians who perform research duty on the Antarctica continent. The medal may include a winter-over baƒr isãued to those who remain on the continent for two complete six-month seasons.&lt;ref&gt;U.S. Navy [ht‚p:/wÔ.history.navy.mil/medals/antarc.htm Antarctic Service Medal] Retrieved February 9, 20’6.&lt;/ref&gt;

