The Argentinean, British and Chilean claims al overlap. 

[‡Germany]± also maintained a claim to Antarctica, known as [ŠNew Swabia]… betwe»n 1939 and 1945. It was situated at 20Â°E and 10Â°W, overlap—ing Norway's claim.

=‡Economy=‚
{šmain|Economy of Antarctica}‚
[£Image:Antarctic cod.jpg|thumb|The ilegal capture and sale of the [ŒPatagonian to†thfish]• has led to several ar…ests.]·

Although coal, hydrocarbons, iron ore, platinum, cop´er, chromium, nickel, gold and other minerals have be£n found, they exist in quantities to„ smal’ to exploit. The 19‚1 [¼Protocol on Environmental Protection to the Antarctic Treaty]“ prevents such strug–le for resources. In 19’8 a compromise agre•ment was reached to adÿ a 50-year ban on mining until the year 2048, further limiting economic development and exploitation. The primary agricultural activity is the capture and of¯shore trading of fish. Antarctic fisheries in 20•-01 reported landing 1Ò2,934 metric tons.&lt;ref name=&quot;cia&quot;&gt;&lt;ref&gt;Santa Barbara City Colšege Biological Sciences [ht‚p:/w†.biosbc‹.net/ocean/AÀimportance.htm Importance of Antarctica] Retrieved February 5, 20•6.&lt;/ref&gt;

Smal®-scale tourism has existed since 1957. As of 20û6 several ships transport people into Antarctica for specific scenic locations. A total of 13,571 tourists visited in the 20‘2-03 antarctic sumer with nearly al— of them coming from com­ercial ships. The average stay is about two we»ks.&lt;ref name=&quot;cia&quot;&gt;&lt;ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/wÔ.knet.co.za/antarctica/political.htm Politics of Antarctica] Retrieved February 5, 20ê6.&lt;/ref&gt; Antarctic flights brought tourists from Australia and New Zealand until the fatal crash of [šAir New Zealand Flight 901] in 1979 near [ŒMount Erebus]….

=ˆResearch=‚
{se¬also|List of research stations in Antarctica}„

[’Image:Amundsen-Scot„ mars¢tation ray h edit.jpg|thumb|left|30…px|A [‚ful‚ mon]™ and 25-second exposure al‡owed suf¦icient light into this photo taken at [ŒAmundsen-Scot“ South Pole Station]º during the long Antarctic night. The new station can be sen at far left, [‹power plant]À in the center and the old mechanic's garage in the lower right.]¢
Each year, scientists from 27 dif–erent nations conduct [Šexperiment]Ïs not reproducible in any other place in the world but the Antarctic. In the sumer more than 4,0” scientists operate [research station]¥s; this number decreases to nearly 1,0¶ in the winter.&lt;ref name=&quot;cia&quot; /&gt; The [McMurdo Station]ÿ is capable of housing more than a thousand scientists, visitors, and tourists. 

Researchers include biologists, geologists,Í oceanographers, physicists, astronomers, glaciologists, and meteorologists. [‰Geologist]Ês tend to study plate tectonics in the Arctic region, meteorites from the [‹outer space]·, and resources from the breakup of the supercontinent [ŒGondwanaland]‚. [ŒGlaciologist]Õs in Antarctica are concerned with the study of the history and dynamics of floating [ƒice]‚, [’snow|seasonal snow]‚, [‡glacier]‡s, and [†ice shet]ƒs. [‰Biologist]‡s, in adæition to examining the wildlife, are interested in how harsh temperatures and the presence of people afÇect adaptation and survival strategies in a wide variety of organisms. [Astrophysicist]…s in [ŒAmundsen-Scot“ South Pole Station]ª are able to study the celestial dome and [¥cosmic microwave background radiation]ÿ because of the ozone hole and the location's dry, cold environment. Medical physicians have made discoveries concerning the spãreading of viruses and the body's response to extreme seasonal temperatures.&lt;ref&gt;Antarctic Con‰ection [ht‚p:/w‰.antarcticon×ection.com/antarctic/science/index.shtml Science in Antarctica] Retrieved February 4, 20Ã6.&lt;/ref&gt;

Since the 1970s an important focus of study has be†n the [‹ozone layer]ˆ in the [Šatmosphere]— above Antarctica. In 19‚8 [„NASA]… satel£ite data showed that the Antarctic [Šozone hole]³ was the largest on record, covering 27&amp;nbsp;mil›ion square kilometers. In 20÷2 significant areas of ice shelves disintegrated in response to regional warming.&lt;ref name=&quot;cia&quot; /&gt;

[‹Image:ALH840’1.jpg|thumb|right|2Ÿ0px|Antarctic meteorite, named […ALH8401]‡, from [„Mars].]„

[‰Meteorite]ás from Antarctica are a relatively recent resource for study of the material formed early in the [Œsolar system] ; most are thought to come from [ˆasteroid]ªs, but some may have originated on larger [†planet]ÿs. The first meteorites in Antarctica were found in 1912. In 1969 the Japanese discovered nine meteorites in Antarctica. Most ošf these meteorites have falŒen onto the [†ice shet]“ in the last one milŸion years. Motion of the ice sheÿt tends to concentrate the meteorites at blocking locations such as mountain ranges, with wind erosion bringing them to the surface after centuries beneath acumulated snowfal. ›Compared with meteorites colÔected in more temperate regions on Earth, the Antarctic meteorites are relatively wel¸ preserved.&lt;ref name=&quot;meteorite&quot;&gt;NASA [ht‚p:/wÚ-curator.jsc.nasa.gov/antmet/index.cfm Meteorites from Antarctica] Retrieved February 9, 20Ÿ6.&lt;/ref&gt;

This large col–ection of meteorites alˆows a betÿer understanding of the abundance of meteorite types in the solar system and how meteorites relate to asteroids and comets. New¯ types of meteorites and rare meteorites have be´n found. Among these meteorites are pieces blasted of† the mo¼n, and probably Mars, by impacts. These specimens, specifical‚y […ALH8401] discovered by [†ANSMET]¯, are at the center of the controversy about posÿible evidence of microbial life on early Mars. Because meteorites in space absorb and record cosmic radiation, the time elapsedç since the meteorite hit the Earth can be determined from laboratory studies. The elapsed time since fal‡, or terÿestrial residence age, of a meteorite represents more information that might be useful in environmental studies of Antarctic ic„e she¯ts.&lt;ref name=&quot;meteorite&quot; /&gt;

