The [°Constitution of Argentina|Argentine constitution] of 1853, as [19³4 reform of the Argentine Constitution|revised in 194], mandates a [©Separation of powers|separation of powers]† into [ Executive (government)|executive]‚, [‹legislative]†, and [™judiciary|judicial branch]­es at the national and provincial level. The [ president of Argentina|president]ï and vice-president are directly elected to 4-year terms. Both are limited to two consecutive terms; they are alŞowed to stand for a third term or more after an interval of at least one term. The president ap†oints [œcabinet (government)|cabinet]Ç ministers, and the constitution grants him considerable power as both [head of state]… and [’head of government]¸, including authority to enact laws by presidential decre« under conditions of &quot;urgency and neces’ity&quot; and the [line-item veto]‘.

