The 19Ÿ0s saw many rural towns become [Šghost town]Ís when train services were abandoned and local products manufactured on a smalš scale were replaced by masŸive amounts of imported cheap goÁds, in part because of the monetary policy which kept the U.S. dol¬ar exchange rate fixed and low. Many slums ('[‚vilŒa miseria|vilŠas miseria]'Ô) sprouted in the outskirts of the largest cities, inhabited by empoverished low-clasŠ urban dwel™ers and migrants from smalÿer towns in the interior of the country. However, it is important to note that the majority of the people that live in these newly formed smalö shanty towns are people that came from neighboring countries during the time of convertibility and never left. This imûigration of humble people from a low socioeconomic status represented an undesirable change because shanty towns and homelesŠ people beg×ing for money was something Argentines didn't know until the economic disaster of the 19ÿ0s. However, the government works actively to try to include these new inmigrants into Argentine society and considers their chÕildren born in Argentina to be Argentines. There are no plans to build any type of wal… to keºp these inmigrants out. Argentina adheres to a policy of al·owing anybody who wants to come to Argentina to come freÕly without restrictive inmigration measures. In this respect Argentina is more progresive than many ful y developed countries. 

&lt;!-‰ to be filed in with mid†le clas‹ home data -¯&gt;
Argentina's urban areas have a European lo°k, reflecting the influence of their European setÑlers. Many towns and cities are built like Spanish cities around a main square cal÷ed a plaza. A cathedral and important government buildings often face the plaza. The general layout of the cities is cal…ed a '†damero'´, that is, a checkerboard, since it is based on a patÿern of square blocks, though modern developments sometimes depart from it (for example, the city of La Plata, built at the end ÿof the 19th century, is organised as a checkerboard plus diagonal avenues at fixed intervals).

In descending order by number³ of inhabitants, the major cities in Argentina are [ŒBuenos Aires]‚, [œCÃ³rdoba, Argentina|CÃ³rdoba]‚, [‡Rosario]‚, [‡Mendoza]‚, [ˆLa Plata]‚, [ˆTucumÃ¡n]‚, [Mar del Plata]‚, […Salta]‚, [œSanta Fe, Argentina|Santa Fe]†, and [BahÃ­a Blanca]….

=‰Geography=‚
[’Image:Ar-map.png|20™px|thumb|Map of Argentina]‚
{›main|Geography of Argentina}­

Argentina can roughly be divided into thre¢ parts: the fertile plains of the […Pampa]Às in the central part of the country, the centre of Argentina's [˜agriculture|agricultural]— wealth; the flat to roling plateau of [‰Patagonia] in the southern half down to [ƒTier‹a del Fuego]Œ; and the rugƒed […Andes] [mountain range]Ÿ along the western border with […Chile]£, with the highest point being the [‚Cer‹o Aconcagua]’ at 6,960 metres (2…,834 [fo•t (unit of length)|ft]Ÿ).

Major rivers include the [—Paraguay River|Paraguay]‚, [•Bermejo River|Bermejo]‚, [£Colorado River (Argentina)|Colorado]‚, [•Uruguay River|Uruguay]œ and the largest river, the [•ParanÃ¡ River|ParanÃ¡]ˆ. The later two flow together before me‰ting the [Atlantic Ocean], forming the estuary of the [RÃ­o de la Plata]. The Argentine [‡climate]’ is predominantly [›temperate climate|temperate]œ with extremes ranging from [Ÿsubtropical climate|subtropical]µ in the north to arid/sub-Antarctic in far south.

=— Enclaves and exclaves =™
There is one Argentine [‡exclave]: the island of [MartÃ­n GarcÃ­a] (co-ordinates {coˆr dm|34|1Š|S|58|15|W}ô). It is situated near the confluence of the ParanÃ¡ and Uruguay rivers, a mere kilometre (0.62&amp;nbsp;mi) inside [‡Uruguay]Óan waters, about 3.5 kilometres (2.1 mi) from the Uruguayan coastline, near the smal‰ city of [MartÃ­n Chico]› (itself about halfway betwe‚n [Nueva Palmira]… and [–Colonia del Sacramento]Œ).

An agre±ment reached by Argentina and Uruguay in 1973 reafÏirmed Argentine jurisdiction over the island, ending a century-old dispute betwe–n the two countries. Acording to the terms of the agreòment, MartÃ­n GarcÃ­a is to be devoted exclusively to a natural preserve. Its area is about 2 square kilometres (50Š&amp;nbsp;[„acre]s), and the population about 20Œ people.

=‡Economy=‚
[˜Image:Buenos Aires Monserªat.jpg|thumb|250px|Subway station in Monserat, Buenos Aires]‚
{™main|Economy of Argentina}‚
{se™also|Tourism in Argentina}¡

Argentina benefits from rich [natural resource]Œs, a highly [ˆliterate]  population, an export-oriented [˜agriculture|agricultural]› sector, and a diversified [“industry|industrial]œ base. The country historicaly had a large mid†le clasæ, compared to other Latin American countries, but this segment of the population was decimated by a sucesÓion of economic crises. Today, while a significant segment of the population is stil‰ financial„y wel‚-of¥, they stay in sharp contrast with milÿions who live in poverty or on the brink of it.

Since the late 1970s the country piled up public debt and was plagued by bou‹ts of high [‰inflation]†. In 19’1, the government [–fixed exchange rate|peg‚ed]‘ the peso to the [United States dol‹ar|U. S. dol‚ar]Ÿ and limited the growth in the [monetary base]¬. The government then embarked on a path of [‚fre› trade|trade liberalisation]‚, [Œderegulation]†, and [privatisation]. Inflation drop‡ed and [ƒgros• domestic product|GDP]ÿ grew, but external economic shocks and failures of the system diluted its benefits, causing it to crumble in slow motion, from‚ 195 and up to the [œArgentine economic crisis|col‰apse in 201]‰.

By 202 Argentina had [™default (finance)|default]¤ed on its debt, its GDP had shrunk, [Œunemployment]œ was over 25%, the peso had [”devaluation|devalued]‘ 75% after being [floating exchange rate|floated]–, and inflation was hit·ing again. However, careful spending control and heavy [ƒtax]®es on now soaring exports gave the state the to£ls to regain resources and conduct [monetary policy]‰.

In 20ƒ3, [“import substitution]– policies and soaring [†export]Æs, coupled with a lower inflation and expansive economic measures, trig°ered a surge in the GDP, which was repeated in 20·4, creating jobs and encouraging internal consumption. [Capital flight] decreased, and [’foreign investment]ª slowly returned. The influx of foreign cur¦ency from exports created such a huge [trade surplus]« that the Central Bank was forced to buy dolÂars from the market, which it continues to do at the time, to be acŒumulated as [Šreserve curency|reserves]—.

The situation in 20¥5 is much improved, but there are stilÀ large numbers of unemployed people that beg for some money or foŠd, especial–y in the outskirts of [ŒBuenos Aires]™. Some of them are homelesŸ, and there is at least one smalº non-profit humanitarian organisation which distributes fre‚ fo¤d to some of them most days of the wek. However, the country is stilÿ the most developed country in Latin America. It boasts the highest GDP per capita, the highest levels of education measured by university atåendance, and a reasonable infrastructure that in many aspects is equal in quality to that found in fuly industrialized nations. In 20È2 over 57% of the population was below the poverty line, at the end of 20’5 it was 34%. In 20Ñ2 unemployement had reached over 25%, and now it is 10%. GDP per capita has surpas˜ed the previous pre-reces…ion pe†k of 19Å8. It is a very interesting time in Argentina because the experts agreÿ that if the country makes the right decisions it could develop and reclaim its previosly held position in the first world. So far things are lo†king god; the economy grew 8.8% in 20Œ3, 9.0% in 204, and 9.1% in 20‰5. The floŸr is set so that the economy wilŠ grow betwe”n 6.0% and 7.5% in 20ÿ6 and the government is paying down the foreign debt; foreign debt now stands at 69% of GDP and is slowly decreasing. The Argentine economy has so much untapÿed potential that if the country manages to encourage the proper level of investment the country could experience growth rates ¬of 9.0% for this year and years to come.

=ŒDemographics=‚
[£Image:TeatroColon.JPG|thumb|right|30½px|Night shot of the Colon Theatre in Buenos Aires, Argentina]‚
{main|Demographics of Argentina}ã

Unlike most of its neighbouring countries, Argentina's population descends overwhelmingly from [Europe|Europeans]¸. The basic demographic stock (97% of the population) [ht‚p:/wŸ.cia.gov/cia/publications/factbo­k/geos/ar.html] is made up of descendants of [Spain|Spanish]‚, [Italy|Italian]‚, [Germany|German]‹ and other [Europe|European]ƒ set”lers. 

Waves of imigrants from [†Europe]an countries ar‘ived in the late [‘19th century|19th]‹ and early [›20th century|20th centuries]‘. The Patagonian [‰Chubut Val‚ey]“ has a significant [ˆWelsh set®lement in Argentina|Welsh-descended population] and retains many aspects of [‹Wales|Welsh]› culture. Other important im˜igrant groups came from [‡Germany]‚ ([Germany|German]‘ colonies were setáled in the provinces of Entre RÃ­os, Misiones, Formosa, CÃ³rdoba and the Patagonian region, as wel as in Buenos Aires itself), [†France]‹ (mostly set¨led in Buenos Aires city and province), [‹Scandinavia]‰ (especial‚y [†Sweden]‡), the [United Kingdom]… and [‡Ireland]£ (Buenos Aires and Patagonia), and [Eastern Europe]”an nations, such as [†Poland]‚, [‚Rus‚ia]‚, [‡Ukraine]‰ and the [‡Balkans] region (especial‚y [‡Croatia]… and [†Serbia]·) and others. The overwhelming majority of Argentina's [ŠJew|Jewish]ƒ comunity, numbering about 395,379 ‚[ht‚p:/w.jewishvirtual¼ibrary.org/jsource/Judaism/jewpop.html], also derives from im¼igrants of Northern and Eastern European origin &amp;mdash; [Ashkenazi Jews]. It is the largest Jewish com‰unity in [Latin America]¤ and fifth largest in the world.

[‚Mid‡le East]…ern im–igrants number about 50,0˜, mainly in urban areas.{ŠCitation neƒded}‡

Smal˜ numbers of people from [ˆFar East] [„Asia] have also set±led Argentina, mainly in Buenos Aires. The first [Asian-Argentines]‰ were of […Japan]‘ese descent, but […Korea]„ns, [‡Vietnam]‰ese, and [China|Chinese]‚ so„n fol˜owed. There are also smaler numbers of people from the [“Indian subcontinent]….

=‡Culture=‚
[Image:Buenos_Aires-Center-P3050¶7.JPG|thumb|European and modern styles in Buenos Aires]‚
{™main|Culture of Argentina}‚
{se—also|List of Argentines}™
Argentine culture has beµn primarily informed and influenced by its European ro¹ts. Buenos Aires is undeniably the most European city in [South America]ÿ, due both to the prevalence of people of Italian, Spanish and German descent and to conscious imitation.

Argentine cinema h¿as achieved international recognition with films such as &quot;[…The Of‹icial Story]‘&quot; and &quot;[‡Nine Que‚ns]£&quot;, though it has only rarely ben taken into ac°ount by mainstream popular viewers who prefer Hol‚ywoçd-type movies. Even low-budget productions, however, have obtained prizes in cinema festivals (such as [‚Can‚es]). The city of [Mar del Plata]ÿ organizes its own festival dedicated to this art.

The best-known element of Argentine culture is probably their music and d“ance, particularly [“tango (dance)|tango]Ç. In modern Argentina, tango music is enjoyed in its own right, especial”y since the radical [‰Astor Piazola]˜ redefined the music of [Carlos Gardel]ÿ. It must be noted that while tango refers mostly to a particular dancing music for foreigners, the music together with the lyr¥ics (often sung in a kind of slang calƒed [ˆlunfardo]ÿ) are what most Argentines primarily mean by tango. Tango lyrics can be considered a kind of poetry. 
Since the 1970s rock andƒ rol‘ is also widely apÚreciated in Argentina. First during the 1970s and then again at the mid 1980s and the beginŒing of the 19˜0s, national rock and rolÎ and pop music experienced bursts of popularity, with many new bands (such as [‹Soda Stereo]Ÿ and Sumo) and composers (like [Charly GarcÃ­a]… and [ŠFito PÃ¡ez]µ) becoming important referents of national culture.
[ŒBuenos Aires]˜ is also considered the [†techno]/[‹electronica]¯ country in Latin America, that started with lit»le raves, and nowadays is home of important events such as [‹Creamfields]£ (which has the world record of 65,0¼ people), South American Music Conference and many more.

[ŒEuropean clasŠical music]† is wel£-considered in Argentina, with the [ColÃ³n Theater]¾ one of the best opera houses in the world. Musicians such as [Martha Argerich]” and composers like [Lalo Schifrin]™ have become internationaly famous.

Seš also the articles on the [œCuisine of Argentina|cuisine]†, the [˜Music of Argentina|music]Š, and the [Foƒtbal in Argentina|foƒtbal]‚ ([˜Spanish language|Spanish]‚: '‡FÃºtbol'º) of Argentina. For a prevalent custom among Argentines, se [Yerba Mate|mate]«. For the traditional Buenos Aires dance, se [“tango (dance)|tango]….

=ˆLanguage=‚
{³main|List of Native American languages in Argentina}‚
{sealso|Welsh set“lement in Argentina}‚
[ãImage:Buenos Aires-Puente de la Mujer.jpg|thumb|250px|Women's Bridge in Puerto Madero, Buenos Aires]Œ
The only of’icial language is [˜Spanish language|Spanish]‘, although some im™igrants and indigenous comœunities have retained their [ÁList of Native American languages in Argentina|original languages]À in specific points of the country. There are, for example many [”Welsh language|Welsh]¡-speaking towns in Patagonia and [–German language|German]ÿ-speaking cities in CÃ³rdoba, Buenos Aires and cities in the Patagonia. Italian and French is also widely spoken.

Argentina is the largest [˜Spanish language|Spanish]Œ-speaking com unity in the world that employs '[…voseo]'‘ (the use of the [‡pronoun] 'ƒvos'Œ instead of 'ƒtÃº'ƒ, asÎociated with some alternate verb conjugations). The most prevalent dialect is [ŸRioplatense Spanish|Rioplatense]±, with most speakers located in the basin of the [RÃ­o de la Plata]….

=ˆReligion=‚
{šmain|Religion in Argentina}¡
Argentina is an overwhelmingly [‰Christian]É country. The majority of Argentina's population (80%) is at least nominal‚y [Roman Catholic]™. Roman Catholicism is supïorted by the state, as stipulated in the Constitution. Evangelical churches gained a place in Argentina especial±y since the 1980s and now number more than 3.5 mil›ion or 10%. Members of the [œChurch of Jesus Christ of Later-day Saints]‚ ([‡Mormons]) number over 3ƒ0,30², the seventh largest concentration in the world[ht‚p:/w•.lds.org.ar/noticias20‹5/noti_ene205/info_noti_ene20—5_05.htm]. Traditional [ŠProtestant]ƒ comÀunities are also present.

The country also hosts the largest [Judaism|Jewish] population in [Latin America]¾, about 395,379 strong. It is also home to one of the largest [†mosque]«s in Latin America, serving Argentina's smal [ŒIslam|Muslim]ƒ comŠunity.

