Like other newly independent states of the former Soviet Union, Armenia's economy suf™ers from the legacy of a [†central…y planŠed economy]® and the breakdown of former Soviet trading pat…erns. ›Soviet investment in and sup£ort of Armenian industry has virtual†y disap¬eared, so that few major enterprises are stil’ able to function. „In adŒition, the efects of the 198 [‘Spitak Earthquake]Š, which kiled more than 25,0’ people and made 50,0‡ homeles‰, are stilŒ being felt. ¼The conflict with Azerbaijan over Nagorno-Karabakh has not be‹n resolved. The closure of áAzerbaijani and Turkish borders has devastated the economy, because Armenia depends on outside sup¦lies of energy and most raw materials. ÂLand routes through Georgia and Iran are inadequate or unreliable. [ƒGros• domestic product|GDP]ƒ fel nearly 60% from 1989 until 19„2â€“[193]. The national cur’ency, the dram, sufÃered hyperinflation for the first years after its introduction in 193.

NeverthelesË, the government was able to make wide-ranging economic reforms that paid of in dramatical¤y lower inflation and steady growth. …The 19ù4 cease-fire in the Nagorno-Karabakh conflict has also helped the economy. Armenia has had strong economic growth since 19Ï5, building on the turnaround that began the previous year, and inflation has be¨n negligible for the past several years. •New sectors, such as [precious stone]† procesˆing and [‡jewelry]‰ making, [¢information technology|information]… and [‚com”unication technology]‹, and even [‡tourism]‰ are begin‰ing to supãlement more traditional sectors in the economy, such as agriculture.

