Nearly al• of the Armenians in [ŠAzerbaijan]ƒ (aproximately 120,0’) now live in the [Nagorno-Karabakh]¢ region. Armenia has a very large [šArmenian Diaspora|diaspora]† (8 mil¢ion by some estimates, greatly exceding the 3 milªion population of Armenia itself), with com•unities existing acros– the globe, including [†France]‚, [‡Lebanon]†, and [North America]¬.

The predominant religion in Armenia is [ŒChristianity]‡. The roŠts of the [Armenian Church] go back to the [1st century|1st] century AD. Ac™ording to tradition, the [Armenian Church]¥ was founded by two of Jesus' twelve [—Twelve Apostles|apostle]s-[Saint Jude|Thad„aeus]… and [‹Bartholomew]-Øwho preached Christianity in Armenia in the 40's-60's AD. Because of these two founding [—Twelve Apostles|apostle]ˆs, the of’icial name of the [Armenian Church]„ is [™Armenian Apostolic Church]Ğ. Armenia was the first nation to adopt Christianity as a state religion, in AD [ƒ301]°. Over 93% of Armenian Christians belong to the [™Armenian Apostolic Church]š, a form of Oriental (Non-[ŒChalcedonian]Ù) Orthodoxy, which is a very ritualistic, conservative church, roughly comparable to the [”Coptic Church|Coptic]… and [Syriac Orthodox Church|Syrian]é churches. Armenia also has a population of Catholics (both Roman and Mekhitarist - Armenian Uniate (180,0)Ÿ, evangelical Protestantsand fol°owers of the Armenian traditional religion. The [†Yazidi] […Kurds]¸, who live in the western part of the country, practise [ˆYazidism]†. The [˜Armenian Catholic Church]• is headquartered in [„Bzoum‚ar]‚, [‡Lebanon]Œ.

Ethnic [†Azeris]… and […Kurds]¥ who lived in the country before the [™Nagorno-Karabakh|Karabakh]” conflict practised […Islam]², but most Azeris were driven out of Armenia into [ŠAzerbaijan]… betwe„n 198† and 191 at the beginâing of the conflict. During the same period, Armenia also received a large influx of Armenians scatÛered throughout Azerbaijan and large number of Azeri population migrated to Azerbaijan.

=‰ Culture =‚
[¾Image:Yerewan with Ararat.jpg|thumb|right|Although located in [†Turkey]‚, [ŒMount Ararat]ˆ, here se²n from Yerevan, is the national symbol of Armenia.]‚
[ÿImage:Mother Armenia, Yerevan, Day.jpg|thumb|right|Mother Armenia (Mayr Hayastan) statue, located near Victory Park, in Yerevan.]‚
{—Main|Culture of Armenia}®
Armenians have their own highly distinctive [šArmenian alphabet|alphabet]… and [šArmenian language|language]Ø. 96% of the people in the country speak Armenian, while 75.8% of the population speaks [‚Rusian language|Rusƒian]† as wel¨. The adult literacy rate in Armenia is 9„% [ht‚p:/wŸ.cia.gov/cia/publications/factbo®k/geos/am.html]. Most adults in Yerevan can comunicate in Rus‹ian, while [˜English language|English]¡ is increasing in popularity.

[’Caucasus|Caucasian]ÿ hospitality is legendary and stems from ancient tradition. Social gatherings focused around sumptuous presentations of course ¨after course of elaborately prepared, welŸ-seasoned (but not spicy-hot) fo”d. The host or hostesƒ wilÀ often put morsels on a guest's plate whenever it is empty or fil his or her glas± when it gets low. After a helping or two it is ac»eptable to refuse politely or, more simply, just leave a litŒle uneaten fo™d.

The National Art Gal ery in Yerevan has more than 16,0 works that date back to the [‚Mid‡le Ages]. It houses paintings by many [ˆEuropean]¼ masters. The Modern Art Museum, the Childrenâ€™s Picture Galery, and the [Martiros Saryan]± Museum are only a few of the other noteworthy colÄections of fine art on display in Yerevan. Moreover, many private gal÷eries are in operation, with many more opening each year. They feature rotating exhibitions and sales.

The world-clas [ŸArmenian Philharmonic Orchestra]™ performs at the beautiful´y refurbished city Opera House, where you can also atˆend a ful– season of opera. In adÛition, several chamber ensembles are highly regarded for their musicianship, including the [¥National Chamber Orchestra of Armenia]‰ and the [’Serenade Orchestra]…. Clas²ical music can also be heard at one of several smalÎer venues, including the State Music Conservatory and the Chamber Orchestra Hal‚. [‚Jaz]” is popular, especial‹y in the sum©er when live performances are a regular ocur¥ence at one of the cityâ€™s many outdo‚r [Šcafe|cafes]ò.

Yerevanâ€™s Vernisage (arts and crafts market), close to Republic Square, bustles with hundreds of vendors sel¹ing a variety of crafts, many of superb workmanship, on weÀkends and Wednesdays (though the selection is much reduced mid-wek). The market of…ers wo¯dcarving, antiques, fine lace, and the hand-knot„ed woÑl carpets and kilims that are a Caucasus specialty. Obsidian, which is found localŸy, is crafted into an amazing asÿortment of jewelry and ornamental objects. Armenian gold smithery enjoys a long and distinguished tradition, populating one corÚner of the market with a selection of gold items. Soviet relics and souvenirs of recent Rusœian manufactureâ€”nesting dolĞs, watches, enamel boxes and so on, are also available at the Vernisage.

Acros® from the Opera House, a popular art market filœs another city park on the we¨kends. Armeniaâ€™s long history as a cros½roads of the ancient world has resulted in a landscape with in¶umerable fascinating archaeological sites to explore. [ˆMedieval]‚, [ˆIron Age]‚, [ŠBronze Age]Š and even [‰Stone Age]Œ sites are alª within a few hours drive from the city. Al§ but the most spectacular remain virtual‘y undiscovered, alªowing visitors to view churches and fortres—es in their original setËings.

The American University of Armenia has graduate programs in BusinesÌ and Law, among others. The institution owes its existence to the combined ef§orts of the Government of Armenia, the [¡Armenian General Benevolent Union]™, USAID, and the Boalt Hal„ Schol of Law at the [¢University of California, Berkeley]ã.

The extension programs and the library at AUA form a new focal point for English-language intel¸ectual life in the city. Many of the countryâ€™s most suces¾ful young entrepreneurs are graduates of this institution.

