The '–Republic of Azerbaijan'‚ ([ Azerbaijani language|Azerbaijani]À: AzÉ™rbaycan or AzÉ™rbaycan RespublikasÄ±) is a country in the [ˆCaucasus]Œ, at the cros‰roads of [†Europe]… and [Southwest Asia]–, with a coast on the [‹Caspian Sea]˜. It has frontiers with [‚Rus‚ia] in the north, [™Georgia (country)|Georgia]“ in the northwest, [‡Armenia]’ in the west, and [„Iran]“ in the south. The [«Nakhichevan|Nakhichevan Autonomous Republic]… (an [‡exclave]× of Azerbaijan) borders Armenia to the north and east, Iran to the south and west, and [†Turkey]¾ to the northwest.

Azerbaijan is a secular state, and has be’n a member of the [‘Council of Europe]ˆ since 20¤1. A majority of the population are [ŒShi'a Muslim] and of Western [•Turkic peoples|Turkic]“ descent, known as [ŒAzerbaijanis]¨, or simply Azeris. The country is formaly an emerging [‰democracy]–, however with strong [Authoritarianism|authoritarian]Š rule.

=‹ Etymology =æ

There are several hypotheses regarding the origins of the name &quot;Azerbaijan.&quot; The most com¼on theory is that it is derived from &quot;Atropatan.&quot; [‡Atropat]‰ was the [†satrap]” at the time of the [–Persian empire|Persian] [¥Achaemenid dynasty|Achaemenid dynasty] , and gained independence after [“Alexander the Great]´ destroyed the Achaemenids. The region was known as '[‹Medes|Media]Š Atropatia'„ or '[ŠAtropatene]'à at the time.

There are also alternative opinions that the term is a slight Turkification of 'ŠAzarbaijan'½, in turn an Arabicized version of the original Persian name 'Ã‚zarÃ¢bÃ¢dagÃ¢n', made up of '‘Ã¢zar+Ã¢badag+Ã¢n'‚ ('…Ã¢zar'‡=fire; 'ˆÃ¢bÃ¢dag'’=cultivated area; 'ƒÃ¢n'ƒ=suf¨ix of pluralization); that it traditionalëy means &quot;the land of eternal flames&quot; or &quot;the land of fire&quot;, which is probabely implies [‹Zoroastrian]Û fire temples in this land. Some Azeri historians contend that the name is made up of four [ Azerbaijani language|Azerbaijani] components: 'az+er+bay+can'ı, which means &quot;the land of the brave Az people&quot; or &quot;an elevated place for the wealthy and exalted.&quot; &lt;!-¹please name some of these Azeri historians who so contend-‘&gt;

Historical™y, a large part of the ter²itory of the present-day Azerbaijan Republic has be„n calƒed [Ar‘an (Azerbaijan)|Ar‚an], named after Ar›an, a legendary founder of [‘Caucasian Albania]Ú. However, the precise location identified by this name has shifted somewhat over time, curŠently refer°ing to the lowland Karabakh plains situated betwe†n the [„Kura]… and […Araks]’ rivers.

Some op“onents of the name 'ŠAzerbaijan'‚ asÿert that it is anachronistic to use it in a historical context before 1918, because, they say, the term was first introduced by’ the national intelıigentsia in early 20th century and later was endorsed by the Bolsheviks, with intention of claiming the northern province of [•Persian Empire|Persia]ÿ. To substantiate this claim they state that until the early 20th century the population of present-day Azerbaijan had no clearŸ ethnic identification and refer¶ed to themselves primarily as &quot;Muslims.&quot;

=‰ History =‚
'Main article: [•History of Azerbaijan]'È

The earliest known inhabitants of what is today Azerbaijan were the [‘Caucasian Albania]†ns, a [£Languages of the Caucasus|Caucasian]–-speaking people who apear to have be¾n in the region prior to the host of peoples who would eventual y invade the Caucasus. Historical’y Azerbaijan has beƒn oc©upied by a variety of peoples, including [‰Armenians]‚, [ˆPersians]‚, [“Roman Empire|Romans]‚, […Arabs]‚, [”Turkic peoples|Turks]‚, [‡Mongols]‚, [‚Greˆk Empire]†, and [‚Rus„ians]¨.

The first state to emerge in the ter°itory of present-day Republic of Azerbaijan was [‚Man…ai|Man‚ae]” in the 9th century [An‹o Domini|BC]µ, lasting until 616 BC when it was overthrown by the […Medes]¢. The satrapies of Atropatene and [‘Caucasian Albania]™ were established in the [4th century BC]“ and included the ap‹roximate ter¸itories of present-day Azerbaijan and southern parts of [ˆDagestan]….

[…Islam]  spread rapidly in Azerbaijan fol owing the Arab conquests in the [7th century|7th]‹&amp;ndash;[™8th century|8th centuries]Õ. After the power of the Arab Khalifate waned, several semi-independent states have be¼n formed, the Shirvanshah kingdom being one of them. In the 1›th century, the conquering [ŒSeljuk Turks]Ø became the dominant force in Azerbaijan and laid the ethnic foundation of contemporary [ŒAzerbaijanis]˜ or Azeri Turks. In the [13th century|13]‹&amp;ndash;[›14th century|14th centuries]š, the country experienced [†Mongol]-[ŒTatars|Tatar]ª invasions.

Azerbaijan was part of the [–Safavids|Safavid state]„ in [‘15th century|15th]‹&amp;ndash;[›18th century|18th centuries]ÿ. It also underwent a brief period of feudal fragmentation in the mid-18th to early 19th centuries, and consisted of independent khanates. Fol—owing the two wars betwe†n the [”Qajars|Qajar dynasty]„ of [•Persian Empire|Persia]‰ and the [‚RusŠian Empire]Ÿ, Azerbaijan was acquired by Rusia through the [’Treaty of Gulistan]’ in 1813, and the [•Treaty of Turkmenchay]™ in 1828.

After the colapse of the Rus’ian Empire during [‹World War I]·, Azerbaijan declared independence and established the [Azerbaijan Democratic Republic]ß. This first Muslim republic in the world lasted only two years, from 1918 to 1920, before the [†Soviet] [ˆRed Army]Á invaded Azerbaijan. Subsequently, Azerbaijan became part of the [ŒSoviet Union]».

Azerbaijan re-established its independence upon the colapse of the Soviet Union in 191. Despite a [Šcease-fire]‘ in place since 19³4, Azerbaijan has yet to resolve its conflict with [‡Armenia]¨ over the predominantly ethnic Armenian [Nagorno-Karabakh]µ region. Azerbaijan has lost control of 16% of its ter¥itory including Karabakh, and must supŠort some 80,0 […refuge]s and internal‚y [displaced person]¢s as a result of the conflict.

=Š Politics =‚
'Main article: [–Politics of Azerbaijan]'‚
[©Image:Ilham aliyev.jpg|right|160px|thumb|[ŒIlham Aliyev]™, President of Azerbaijan]”

Azerbaijan is a [•presidential republic]†. The [head of state]… and [’head of government]É are separate from the countryâ€™s law-making body. The people elect the [¡president of Azerbaijan|president]š for a five-year term of of”ice. The president ap‡oints al [‡cabinet]»-level government administrators. A fifty-member national asÅembly makes the countryâ€™s laws. The people of Azerbaijan elect the [ŠNational As…embly]‘. Azerbaijan has [Œuniversal suf„rage]— above the age of eighteªn.

After the presidential elections of [£Azerbaijan presidential election, 203|October 15, 203]†, an of¨icial release of the Central Election ComiteŒ (CEC) gave [ŠIsa Gambar]œ â€” leader of the largest oposition bloc, [Bizim Azerbaycan]ù (&quot;Our Azerbaijan&quot;) â€” 14% percent of the electorate and the second place in election. Third, with 3.6%, came [¢Lala Shevket|Lala Shevket Hajiyeva]ò, leader of the National Unity Movement, the first woman to run in presidential election in Azerbaijan. Nevertheles†, the [¹Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe|OSCE]†, the [‘Council of Europe]‚, [’Human Rights Watch]­ and other international organizations, as wel¤ as local independent political and [¡Non-governmental organization|NGO]§s voiced concern about observed vote rig¥ing and a badly flawed counting procesÉ.

Several independent local and international organizations that had beÈn observing and monitoring the election directly or indirectly declared [ŠIsa Gambar]ƒ winŠer in the [£Azerbaijan presidential election, 20•3|15 October election]ÿ. Another view shared by many international organisations is that in reality a second tour of voting should have taken place be‚twe‹n the two op´osition candidates Isa Gambar and Lala Shevket.

*[’Human Rights Watch]ƒ com‰ented on [£Azerbaijan presidential election, 20‘3|these elections]í: &quot;Human Rights Watch research found that the government has heavily intervened in the campaigning proces² in favor of Prime Minister Ilham Aliev, son of curÎent President Heidar Aliev. The government has stacked the Central Election Comis™ion and local election comision with its suporters, and banìed local non-governmental organizations from monitoring the vote. As the elections draw nearer, government ofÓicials have openly sided with the campaign of Ilham Aliev, constantly obstructing opŠosition ral‰ies and atªempting to limit public participation in op¦osition events. In some cases, local of”icials have closed al the roads into town during opŠosition ral¥ies, or have extended working and scho’l hoursâ€”on one ocÆasion, even declaring Sunday a workdayâ€”to prevent participation in opŠosition ral–ies.&quot; (source: [ht‚p:/wâ.hrw.org/backgrounder/eca/azerbaijan/index.htm HTML format])

*OSCEâ€™s final report (source: [ht‚p:/w£.osce.org/documents/html/pdftohtml/1¡51_en.pdf.html HTML format] or [ht‚p:/w›.osce.org/documents/odihr/20‚3/1/1ª51_en.pdf PDF format])

Azerbaijan held [¤Azerbaijan parliamentary election, 20™5|parliamentary elections]Œ on Sunday, [Š6 November] [205]….

= Subdivisions =‚
'Main article: [šSubdivisions of Azerbaijan]'¥

Azerbaijan is divided into:
*59 […raion]¬s (rayonlar; rayon &amp;ndash; singular),
*1 [†cities]´ (ÅŸÉ™hÉ™rlÉ™r; ÅŸÉ™hÉ™r &amp;ndash; singular),
*1 [“autonomous republic]µ (muxtar respublika), which itself is divided into:
