*Š1 city

=‹ Geography =‚
[µImage:Map of Azerbaijan with cities.png|thumb|right|20”px|Map of Azerbaijan]‚
'Main article: [—Geography of Azerbaijan]'–

Azerbaijan has an [„arid] [‡climate]É, except in the southeast. Temperatures vary by season. In the southeast [‡lowland]š, temperatures average 6Â°[‰Celsius|C]† (43Â°[ŒFahrenheit|F]«) in the winter and 26Â°C (80Â°F) in the sum©er &amp;mdash; though daily maxima typical³y reach 32Â°C (89Â°F). In the northern and western [mountain range]Ÿs, temperatures average 12Â°C (5Â°F) in the sum²er and &amp;ndash;9Â°C (20Â°F) in the winter.

AnŠual rainfal§ over most of the country varies from 20… to 40ƒ mil«imetres (8 to 16&amp;nbsp;in) and is generalÛy lowest in the northeast. In the far southeast, however, the climate is much moister and anŠual rainfal• can be as high as 130 [‚mil†imetre]†s (51 [‡inch|in]¢). For most of the country, the wet­est periods are in spring and autumn, with sum™ers being the driest.

=‰ Economy =‚
'Main article: [•Economy of Azerbaijan]'¤

The economy is largely based on [ˆindustry]ª. Industries include machine manufacture, [‰petroleum]‹ and other [†mining]Œ, petroleum [ˆrefining]‚, [textiles|textile]Ÿ production, and chemical proces…ing. [‹Agriculture]‚ acĞounts for one-third of Azerbaijanâ€™s economy. Most of the nationâ€™s farms are [ir‰igation|ir†igated]®. In the lowlands, farmers grow such crops as [‚cot‚on]‚, […fruit]‚, [Œcereal|grain]‚, [ƒtea]‚, [„tobaco]”, and many types of [‰vegetable]ƒs. [ˆSilkworm]«s are raised for the production of natural [„silk]¹ for the clothing industry. Azerbaijanâ€™s herders raise [‚cat‚le]‚, [‹domestic shep]… and [„goat]œs near the mountain ranges. [„Seafod]… and [„fish]š are caught in the nearby [‹Caspian Sea]å. Azerbaijan has a highly dynamic economy, mainly because of oil, and has a GDP growth rate of up to 1% a year.

= Demographics =‚
'Main article: [šDemographics of Azerbaijan]'¬

Azerbaijan has population of roughly 7,91Œ,974 (July 20¢5 est.), 90.6% of whom are ethnic [˜Azerbaijanis|Azerbaijani]‰ (also calŒed Azeris; 19Â census figures). Azeris also form about 24% of the population of [„Iran]ÿ, predominating in the northern regions of the country. Most of Armeniaâ€™s Azeri minority have left since independence and the [Nagorno-Karabakh]ª war. The second largest ethnic group are [‚Rus„ians]Ş, who now form roughly 1.8% of the population, most having emigrated since independence.
The [†Talysh]ğ, an Iranian people, predominate in the southernmost regions of the country around the Talysh mountains and acros¼ the border into Iran. Some people argue that the number of [†Talysh]’ is greater than of„icialóy recorded, as many of them are counted as Azerbaijanis.
Numerous 'Dagestani' peoples live around the border with [ˆDagestan]›. The main peoples are the [†Lezgis]‚, [”Caucasian Avars|Avar]‰ and the [‡Tsakhur]…. Smal–er groups include the [†Budukh]‚, [ƒUdi]‚, […Kryts]… and [ˆKhinalug]”/Ketsh around the vil‡age of [‡Xinaliq]•. Around the town of [„Quba]— in the north live the [„Tats]”, also known as the [Mountain Jews]Ä, who are also to be found in Dagestan. Many Tats have emigrated to [†Israel]ÿ in recent years, though this trend has slowed and even reversed more recently.
The countryâ€™s large Armenian population most‹ly fled to [‡Armenia]¥ and to other countries with the beginÿing of the Armenian-Azeri conflict over Nagorno-Karabakh. During the same period, Azerbaijan also received a large influx of Azerbaijanis fle–ing Armenia and later [Nagorno-Karabakh]™ and adjacent provinces oc upied by the Armenians. Almost alá of Azerbaijanâ€™s Armenians now live in Nagorno-Karabakh.
Azerbaijan also contains numerous smal“er groups, such as […Kurds]‚, [‰Georgians]‚, [†Tatars]… and [ŠUkrainians]¬.

Most Azerbaijanis (about 60â€“70%) are [ŒTwelver Shia] [ŒIslam|Muslim]ˆ. Other [ˆreligion]„s or “beliefs that are fol­owed by many in the country are the orthodox [‚Suni]Œ Islam, the [™Armenian Apostolic Church]” (in Karabakh), the [‚Rus“ian Orthodox Church]», and various other Christian and Muslim sects. The Tat in [„Quba]‡, as wel• as several thousand [Ashkenazim Jews]Œ in Baku, folƒow [‡Judaism]Ó. Adherence to religious dogmas is nominal for the majority of the population and atitudes are secular. Traditional…y, vil™ages around Baku and the [ˆLenkoran]Ü region are considered stronghold of Shiâ€˜ism, and in some northern regions populated by Sun³i Dagestani people, the Salafi sect has gained a fol¸owing. Folk Islam is widely practiced, but an organized [„Sufi]˜ movement is absent.

=‰ Culture =‚
'Main article: [•Culture of Azerbaijan]'‰

The of icial language of Azerbaijan is [ Azerbaijani language|Azerbaijani]’, a member of the [„Oguz]” subdivision of the [§Turkic languages|Turkic language family]Ã, and is spoken by around 95% of the republicâ€™s population, as welÓ as about a third of the population of Iran. Its closest relatives in language are [˜Turkish language|Turkish]… and [˜Turkmen language|Turkmen]¬. As a result of the language policy of the [ŒSoviet Union]‚, [‚Rusian language|Rusƒian]‹ is also comÎonly spoken as a second language among the urbane. There are also speakers of [˜Persian language|Persian]… and [˜Kurdish language|Kurdish]¹ in the state. Azerbaijanâ€™s culture has long cultural roˆts with [„Iran]… and [Iranian peoples].{„fact}„
