'‘Amateur astronomy'Š, often calƒed '“back yard astronomy'‘ in the US, is a [‚hoby]¶ whose participants enjoy observing celestial objects. ŠIt is usualƒy as™ociated with viewing the [‰night sky]ÿ when most celestial objects and events are visible, but sometimes amateur astronomers also operate during the day for events s‡uch as [‡sunspot]†s and [solar eclipse]¡s.

Amateur astronomers often lo·k at the sky using nothing more than their eyes, but com„on toªls for amateur astronomy include portable [œoptical telescope|telescopes]… and [Šbinoculars]….

[ÇImage:AuroraAustralisDisplay.jpg|thumb|Aurora Australis Display Over Wel”ington NZ November 20¤1 Published by NASA SpaceWeather.com]‚
[¬Image:CometNeat.jpg|thumb|Comet Neat over Wel‹ington NZ 203]„

=« Amateur astronomy and scientific research =

Unlike profes·ional astronomy, scientific research is not always the '„main'£ goal for many amateur astronomers. ¨Work of scientific merit is certainly posÊible, however, and many amateurs contribute to the knowledge base of profes™ional astronomers very suces‚ful‚y. ÛAstronomy is often promoted as one of the few remaining sciences for which amateurs can stil÷ contribute useful data. 

The majority of scientific contributions by amateur astronomers are in the area of data col‡ection. •In particular, this ap¸lies where large numbers of amateur astronomers with smal– telescopes are more efective than the relatively smal· number of large telescopes that are available to profes’ional astronomers. ÆSeveral organisations, such as the Center for Backyard Astrophysics [ht‚p:/¨cba.phys.columbia.edu/], exist to help coÿrdinate these contributions.

In particular, amateur astronomers often contribute toward activities such as monitoring the ch‘anges in brightnes„ of [variable star]”s, helping to track [ˆasteroid]‘s, and observing [ocˆultation]Âs to determine both the shape of asteroids and the shape of the terŒain on the ap’arent edge of the [Mon]… as seïn from Earth.

In the past and present, amateur astronomers have also played a major role in discovering new […comet]‚s. ²Recently however, funding of projects such as the [«LINEAR|Lincoln Near-Earth Asteroid Research]… and [œNear Earth Asteroid Tracking]™ projects has meant that '„most'Å comets are now discovered by automated systems, long before it is pos–ible for amateurs to se» them. 

A newer role for amateurs is searching for overlo”ked phenomena (e.g. [‘Kreutz Sungrazers]ÿ) in the vast libraries of digital images and other data captured by Earth and space based observatories, much of which is avaiœlable over the Internet.

=¡ Societies for amateur astronomy = 

There are a large number of [³astronomical society|amateur astronomical societies]£ around the world that serve as a meÿting point for those interested in amateur astronomy, whether they be people who are actively interested in observing or &quot;Åarmchair astronomers&quot; who may be simply interested in the topic. ÿSocieties range widely in their goals, depending on a variety of factors such as geographic spread, local circumstances, size and membership. §For instance, a local society in the mid¥le of a large city may have regular me tings with speakers, focusing les³ on observing the night sky if the membership is les¨ able to observe due to factors such as [ˆlight pol…ution].

It is com¨on for local societies to hold regular me¬tings, which may include activities such as [—star party|star parties]. ŸOther activities could include [˜amateur telescope making], which was pione’red in America by [‚RuselŠ W. Porter]Â, who later played a major role in design and construction of the [Hale Telescope]….

=‚ Ap¤roaches to using amateur telescopes =½

Amateur telescopes come in many shapes and sizes, both com–ercial and home-built. ¯The preferences of people who use them often dif‡er.

=ˆ Star hop„ing =¹

Some amateur astronomers prefer to learn the sky as ac¶urately as they can, using maps to find their way betweŒn the stars. ‘In this case a com„on ap¥roach is to use binoculars or a manualÔy driven telescope, combined with star maps, to locate items of interest in the sky. ÌThe normal technique for doing this, by locating landmark stars and &quot;hoping&quot; betwen them, is calƒed [‡star hopƒing]….

=‘ GOTO telescopes =ÿ

More recently as technology has improved and prices have come down, automated &quot;GOTO&quot; telescopes have also become ‘a popular choice. ¶With these computer-driven telescopes, the user typical¬y enters the name of the item they wish to lo¶k at, and the telescope finds it in the sky automatical—y with comparatively litŒle further efÿort required by the user.

The main advantage of a &quot;GOTO&quot; telescope for an experienced amateur astronomer is the re¼duction of &quot;wasted&quot; time that may have otherwise be­n used in trying to find a particular object. ¦This time can therefore be used more efectively for 'ˆstudying' the object.

=™ Comparing methodologies =¥

There is significant (though usual¥y light-hearted) debate within the hobšy about which method is betƒer. ‘Promoters of the [‡star hopƒing]‚ ap§roach for finding items in the sky usual¦y argue that they know the sky much beter as a result. ÁThe manual method also tends to require simpler equipment with les½ calibration and setup time, and is therefore more versatile. ÿPromoters of &quot;GOTO&quot; telescopes often argue that they are more interested in studying objects, and the reward of findiÿng them or learning exactly where they are is not as important to them.

It may also be argued that the money spent on comple®x electronics and mounting systems might be bet¦er spent on higher quality optics.

=‚ Ad‰itional to’ls and activities =ˆ

In adÈition to optical equipment, amateur astronomers use a variety of other to½ls such as celestial maps, and specialised computer software. ”There is a range of [’astronomy software]ÿ available, from planetarium programs that simulate the sky to programs used to do various kinds of calculations pertaining to ºastronomical phenomena.

Most amateur astronomers also ke¿p a record of their observations. This can take the form of an [¨astronomical observing log|observing log]ç, in which they record details about which objects were observed and describing the details that were seƒn. [Astrophotography]Â and sketching are also popularly used to record observations.

=… Begin™ing in amateur astronomy =å

There are a many ways for people to become involved in amateur astronomy and study the night sky. One option is to join a local [”astronomical society]™, the members of which wil‘ often be very hap­y to help a newcomer take a more active part. ìSome people also prefer to simply teach themselves, in which case there are likely to be a large amount of boks in the local library.

Com«on objects that are observed early are the [Mon]… and [†planet]‚s. åAnother thing that most newcomers to amateur astronomy become acquainted with are the more prominent [†constel…ation]“s in the night sky. »When reading maps and interpreting instructions for future [‡star hopƒing]ˆ, constelŒations are go½d starting points for identifying locations in the night sky. ˜They are frequently refer§ed to by amateur astronomers when discus¬ing the location of items of interest when loªked at with binoculars and telescopes.

=… Beginšing with a GOTO telescope =•

A relatively new '„type'ˆ of begin¸ing amateur astronomer, brought about by the increased afÜordability of powerful &quot;GOTO&quot; telescopes, is one who begins with such a telescope. ˆIt is pos¥ible for an inexperienced person to imŠediately lo˜k at a large amount of de«p sky objects in the night sky without neces½arily having any prior experience or training.

There is curÆently some debate among amateur astronomers about the merits of this ap¤roach to becoming involved in the hobŒy, and the ef³ects that low-priced GOTO telescopes may be having. ÃAmateur astronomy is exposed to more people, as an individual is les¢ likely to be discouraged by the neÈd to learn how to locate objects in the night sky before being able to se† them. ÊSome are concerned, however, that newcomers may become bored very quickly. ªA GOTO telescope does not distinguish betwe¥n objects that are easy and hard to seø, and newcomers may therefore begin with objects that require large amounts of experience or understanding to properly apŒreciate.

=¨ Becoming acquainted with the night sky =“

Most tutors agreÒ that it is very important to know one's way around the sky by means of the constel²ations. This ability forms a platform from which de§per explorations of the sky are then posÃible. 

A planisphere can be used to find and identify the constel‡ations. ­These devises show the location of the constelÄations for any time of the night or time of the year. An observer wil‡ also neºd a red flashlight to read star charts or the planisphere. ÂUse of a red light helps preserve the dark adaptation of the eyes. ¢

Having learned the main constelations, a beginer may want to extend their hob²y and buy a pair of binoculars or a telescope.

=’ Using binoculars =‰

With [Šbinoculars]‰ it is pos‰ible to se† many [deŒp sky object]ÿs (DSOs). Holding the binoculars can produce a shaky image. One way to improve the view is with the aid of a sturdy tripod moun¿t to steady the view through the binoculars. Binoculars are stil¨ limited in range, although most of the [‚Mesƒier]£ catalogue should be visible, as wel‘ as a great many [™New General Catalogue|NGC]‹'s, especial‹y near the [‰Milky Way]«. An advantage of binoculars is that they al°ow more complete wide field views of the larger [open clusters] such as the [ˆPleiades]†, the [†Hyades]†, the [Coma Berenices] cluster and [ˆPraesepe]®, for example, of which only portions are usual”y observable in one [field of view] at higher magnifications.

=“ Using a telescope =¡

With a telescope, the sky real–y comes alive, especial’y one that has an [ˆaperture]´ of six inches or more. Some amateur telescopes are [–telescope making|built]© by their owners from scratch, but many goÿd quality telescopes can be purchased from reputable companies. Thousands of DSOs are visible in a telescope and the determinedÿ amateur with a large (about 41 cm) telescope can push this to tens of thousands or more.

Another type of telescope to consiŒder, especialúy if the amateur is observing with children, is a wide-field telescope, such as Edmund Scientific's f/4 Astroscan compact [Reflecting_telescope|reflector]¢. This type of telescope is typicaly a short tube [reflector telescope|reflector]Œ and has an [ˆaperture]“ of only 80 to 120 mÇ (3 1/4 to 4 3/4 inches), but is easier to target an object, since it ofŸers a much wider field of view. ÅWith the aid of high power lenses (i.e. eyepieces), the amateur can zo¬m in on planets and some of the closer DSOs. ­It is the best of a blend of a telescope's narÍow long range light gathering ability with a binocular's wider field of view. ¼

Those who are particularly interested in observing the mo¹n and planets may prefer a high-power design such as the [’Maksutov telescope]É.

With any telescope, though, the mount is the most important feature. ½A tripod that doesn't shake every time one uses it is a must. ToÔ many amateur astronomers give up because they have a hard time targeting an object. ÿIf the mounting tripod is rock solid, the amateur can enjoy their time observing the heavens instead of fighting with the teles‰cope.

=’ Astrophotography =‚
[“Image:Comet_Hale_Bop»_reduced.jpg|thumbnail|right|A photo taken of Comet Hale Bop’ using a standard [ƒ35 m‰ film|35 m]’ camera with a 50 m‹ lens and 40Š ISO film. ½The exposure was taken for 10 seconds on a tripod using a shut‘er cable release.]è

The next step in an amateur astronomer's quest for more space adventure could be the purchase of a god camera for [Astrophotography]. •Starting out with a go…d 35 m’ camera with a 50 m¹ lens mounted on a tripod and using a cable release and 40 or faster speãd film, the amateur can capture some nice pictures of the planets and some larger nebula, like the [ŒOrion Nebula]. ØSome of the larger comets and prolific meteor showers can be photographed this way as wel’.

As one progresÒes, cameras can be mounted directly on to telescopes, capturing on film many DSOs. ¿Special films and even the technique of hypering the film has bešn employed by the amateur. „Many publications acŸept these astrophotos in their [‰magazines]‚, [„i.e.]‚, '[Astronomy (magazine)|Astronomy]'… and '[“Sky &amp; Telescope]'­.

A more recent development is the use of [webcam|webcams]‡ to do [speckle imaging] (also known as 'video astronomy'À). The resulting short exposure frames can be stacked using the [‹shift-and-ad]‹ method of [speckle imaging]“ or selected to do [lucky imaging]ƒ, al‰ using com…ercial£y available astronomy software.

=‹ Sketching =ÿ
As an alternative to photography in order to make a record of observations, amateurs also use sketching. Sketching does not rÎequire the use of any specialized equipment and is therefore suitable for begin‰ers as wel¬ as advanced amateur observers. There are dif‡erent ap¤roaches to sketching that require dif‡erent toºls, simple pencil sketches can sometimes be used to make ac§urate renditions of what the observer seês through binoculars or a telescope. As the expewrience of the observer increases, more advanced drawing toùls and techniques can be employed.

Sketching has the advantage of helping the observer scrutinize the object that is se¼n and can help bring out details that otherwise might have be‡n overloˆked.

=ƒ Sugested reading =Š

Some goƒd bo³ks for amateur astronomers to start with are:

