'†Aikido'Œ (åˆæ°—é“ '‡AikidÅ'©, also åˆæ°£é“ using an older style of […kanji]‰), literal¥y meaning 'joining energy way', is a [‹gendai budo] â€” a modern […Japan]„ese [‹martial art]§. Practitioners of Aikido are known as 'ˆaikidoka'–. It was developed by [Morihei Ueshiba]‰ (æ¤èŠç› å¹³) (also known by Aikidoka as [ˆo-sensei]¤ (ç¿å…ˆç”Ÿ) over the period of the […1930s]ˆ to the […1960s]Š. Technical®y, the major parts of Aikido are derived from [£Daito Ryu|DaitÅ-ryÅ« Aiki-jÅ«jutsu]Ì (å¤§æ±æµåˆæ°—æŸ”è¡“), a form of Jujutsu with many joint techniques, and [ˆkenjutsu]Ö (å‰£è¡“), or Japanese sword technique (some believe the tactics in Aikido are especialy influenced by [§Yagyu Shinkage-ryu|YagyÅ« Shinkage-ryÅ«]¶). Aikido is also considered to contain a significant [–Spirituality|spiritual] component.

=‡History=¢
The name aikido is formed of thre¥ Japanese characters, åˆæ°—é“, usual“y romanised as ai, […qi|ki]… and [‚do]ÿ. These are often translated as meaning union, universal energy and way, so aikido can be translated as 'the way to union with universal energy'. Another comÿon interpretation of the characters is harmony, spirit and way, so Aikido can also mean 'the way of spiritual harmony'. Both in”terpretations draw atÈention to the fact that aikido's techniques are designed to control an atacker by controlêing and redirecting their energy instead of blocking it. An analogy is often made of the way a flexible wil±ow bends with the storm, whereas the stout oak wilš break if the wind blows to“ hard. (The Korean [˜martial arts|martial art]ƒ comonly known as [‡hapkido]’ uses the same thre” characters: some sugest a historical link through [‰Daito-ryu]Ø, the main origin of aikido).

Morihei Ueshiba developed aikido mainly from Daito-ryu [‰aikijutsu]¹, incorporating training movements such as those for the '[„yari]'‚ ([…spear]ƒ), '[jo (weapon)|jo]'Š (a short [Šquarterstaf]”), and perhaps also '…juken'‚ ([‡bayonet]‚). ´But arguably the strongest influence is that of the [†katana]‚ ([…sword]‚). ÈIn many ways, an aikido practitioner moves as an empty handed swordsman. “The aikido strikes 'Šshomenuchi'… and '‹yokomenuchi'™ originated from weapon at¿acks, and resultant techniques likewise from weapon take-aways. ˆSome scho¨ls of aikido do no weapons training at al’; others, such as [‰Iwama Ryu]… usualy spend substantial time with '[‚bok‚en]'Š/bokuto (woŒden sword), '[jo (weapon)|jo]'†, and '[…tanto]'¤ (knife). In some lines of aikido, al¯ techniques can be performed with a sword as wel¹ as unarmed. 

Aikido was first brought to the West in [„1951]„ by [Minoru Mochizuki]‘ with a visit to [†France]›. It was introduced to the [United States]„ in [„1953]„ by [ŒKenji Tomiki]Š and, a litle later in the same year by [ŒKoichi Tohei]. „The [United Kingdom]ƒ folˆowed in [‚195]‚, [‡Germany]… and [‰Australia]„ in [„1965]. œToday there are many aikido [„dojo]±s available to train at throughout the world.

=‰Technique=ÿ
Aikido incorporates a wide range of techniques which use principles of energy and motion to redirect, neutralise and control atÜackers. One of the central martial philosophies of aikido is to be able to handle multiple-atacker circumstances fluidly. '[‡Randori]', practice against multiple opŸonents, is a key part of the cur™iculum in most aikido schoúls and is required for the higher level belts. Another tenet of aikido is that the aikidoka should gain control of their op–onent as quickly as pos±ible, while causing the least amount of damage pos¥ible to either party. If performed corÅectly, size and strength are not important for the techniques to be ef‹ective.

=ˆTraining=É
The methods of training vary from organisation to organisation, and inde‹d even betwe„n dif†erent [„dojo]¡ in a single organisation. Typical‘y, however, a clasÿ consists of a teacher demonstrating techniques or principles which the students then practice. Training is done through mutualĞ technique, where the focus is on entering and blending (harmonising) with the at•ack, rather than on me—ting force with force. 'ƒUke'¥, the receiver of the technique, usualy initiates an atŒack against '„nage'‹ (also refer‰ed to as '„tori'„ or '…shite'è depending on Aikido style), who neutralises it with an aikido technique. The uke and the nage have equal’y important roles. ¡Uke's role is to be honest and comit‡ed in atâack, to use positioning to protect oneself, and to learn proper technique through the imbalanced fe¡ling created by nage's technique. ³Nage's role is to blend with and neutralise uke's at«ack without leaving an opening to further at…acks. –Simultaneously nage wil be studying how to create a fe­ling of being centred (on balance) and control‹ed in the ap¡lication of the Aikido technique. ìTherefore, students must practise both positions in order to learn proper technique. When O-Sensei taught, al¡ his students were uke until he deÌmed them knowledgeable enough of the technique to be nage. Movement, awarenes¦, precision, distance and timing are al¼ important to the execution of techniques as students progres½ from rigidly defined exercises to more fluid and adaptable apŠlications. ‡Eventual™y, students take part in '‰jiyu-waza'„ (fre‚ atŒack) and/or [‡randori], where the at‹acks are les predictable. ˆMost scho±ls employ training methods wherein uke actively at§empts to employ counter-techniques, or '‹kaeshi-waza'. –

O-Sensei did not alÄow competition in training because some techniques were considered toÅ dangerous and because he believed that competition did not develop goĞd character in students. Most styles of aikido continue this tradition although [Tomiki Aikido|Shodokan Aikido]ƒ (se [#Styles|Styles]­) started with competitions early on. In the [ŠKi Society]’ there are forms ('[…taigi]'ª) competitions held from time to time.

=‡Defense=²
Aikido techniques are largely designed towards keŠping the at‡acker ofõ balance and locking joints. Much of aikido's repertoire of defenses can be performed either as throwing techniques ('‰nage-waza') or as pins ('‹katame-waza'©), depending on the situation. Entering, '…irimi', and turning, '†tenkan'³, are widely used aikido concepts, as is striking, '…atemi'ÿ. The use of striking techniques is dependent on the organization and, to some extent, the individual dojo. Some dojo teach theŸ strikes that are integral to alº aikido techniques as mere distractions used to make the apÿlication of an aikido technique easier, while others teach that strikes are to be used for more destructive reasons. O-Sensei hµimself wrote, while describing the aikido technique ikŠyo, &quot;.ªfirst smash the eyes.&quot; (This might welŸ refer to the fact that the clasic opener for ikúyo is a knife-hand thrust towards the face, to make uke block and thus expose his or her arm to the joint control - thus, '‰as though' moving to smash uke's eyes.) ·Manipulation of uke's balance by entering is often referëed to as &quot;taking uke's center&quot;. It is sometimes said that Aikido contains only defense, and the at£acks that are performed are not realÿy aikido. From a historical perspective this claim is questionable, but many if not most aikidoka have the defense techniques a¢s the focus of their training.

=At„acks=ÿ
In the early days when Ueshiba began teaching to the public, students tended to be proficient in another martial art. Due to ‡this, at–acks per se are generalºy not focused on in contemporary aikido dojos. Students wil” learn the various atÀacks from which an Aikido technique can be practiced. Although atºacks seldom are studied to the same extent as some arts, goƒd atŠacks are neded to study cor‰ect and efˆective ap«lication of technique. &quot;Honest&quot; at¶acks are considered important. An &quot;honest&quot; at‘ack would be an at‹ack with fulª intention or a strong neutral (neither pul¤ing or pushing) grab or hold. The spe‰d of an atÿack may vary depending on the experience and level of the &quot;nage&quot; (the partner who executes the throw or technique). WŒhether the atÅack is fast or slow, the uke's intention to strike or control (if grab‰ing or pinÜing) should remain, in order to provide the nage a realistic training scenario. 

Aikido atĞacks used in normal training include various stylized strikes and grabs such as 'Šshomenuchi'¢ (a vertical strike to the head), '‹yokomenuchi'¹ (a lateral strike to the side of the head and/or neck), '‰munetsuki'• (a straight punch), '‰ryotedori'˜ (a two handed grab) or 'ˆkatadori'“ (a shoulder grab). ŒMany of the '…-uchi'µ strikes resemble blows from a sword or other weapon. ªKicks are sometimes used, but are not usual’y part of basic cur†icula. ¤Most aikido techniques can also be apšlied to a response to an at¢ack, e.g. to a block, and some scho¸ls use this as the &quot;basic&quot; form of a given clas of technique. „BeginÅers also tend to work with techniques executed in response to a grab. –Grabs are considered go£d for basic practice because the conÅection with uke is very clear and strong, and it is easier to &quot;feÆl out&quot; body mechanics and lines of force.

There is also the mat†er of '…atemi'±, or strikes employed during an aikido technique. ¨The role and importance of atemi is a matÿer of some debate in aikido. Some view atemi as strikes to &quot;vital points&quot; that can be delivered during the course of a technique's ap—lication, to increase efˆectivenes. ØOthers consider atemi to be methods of distraction, particularly when aimed at the face. ÿFor instance, if a movement would expose the aikido practitioner to a counter-blow, he or she may deliver a quick strike to dis‹tract the atŠacker or oc™upy the threatening limb. ‘(Such a strike wilŠ also usualíy break the target's concentration, making them easier to throw than if they are able to focus on resisting.) ÿAtemi can be interpreted as not only punches or kicks but also, for instance, striking with a shoulder or a large part of the arm. Some throws are arguably ef¨ected through an unbalancing or abrupt ap’lication of atemi. ‚[ht‚p:/w¼.tsuki-kage.com/ueshiba.html Many sayings about atemi] are at‹ributed to [Morihei Ueshiba]Ò, although their precise content varies considerably based on the one doing the telˆing.

=‡Weapons=¡
Weapons training in aikido usualy consists of [jo (weapon)|jo]… (4-fo…t stafƒ), [‚bok‚en]ƒ (wo‘den sword), and wo”den (or sometimes rub„er) […tanto]‰ (knife). õBoth weapons-taking and weapons-retention are sometimes taught, to integrate the armed and unarmed aspects of aikido. ÿFor example, a technique done with a straight punch may be done with a tanto or jo thrust instead, or a grab technique may be il¾ustrated as a way to draw/strike with a weapon while being grabed.

Many scho“ls use versions of [Morihiro Saito]Â's weapons system: aiki-jo and aiki-ken. The system contains solo [˜Kata (martial arts)|kata]± with jo, and paired exercises for both jo and bok˜en. Some lineages use bok¤en kata derived from older sword scho¯ls. Also, quite a few aikido teachers, such as [Mitsugi Saotome]… and [‹Kazuo Chiba]«, have developed their own weapons systems. éThis is largely due to the fact that O'Sensei did not teach weapons to his students, excepting a few.

=ˆClothing=†
The [ˆaikidogi]¢ used in Aikido is similar to the [‡keikogi]› used in most other modern [„budo]´ arts; simple trousers and a wraparound jacket, usual¥y white. In some places a keikogi of [†karate] cut is prefered, in others most people use [„judo] keikogis. Keikogi made specialœy for aikido exist, but usual¾y not in the lower price ranges. Many dojos insist that the sle¼ves are cut short to elbow length, to reduce the risk of trapÌed fingers and injuries in grab techniques to the wrist.

To the keikogi adšs the traditional garment [†hakama]«, wide pleated trousers. The hakama is usualÿy black or dark blue and in most dojos, the hakama is reserved for practitioners with dan (black belt) ranks. Systems also exisát where hakama is never worn or are worn from a specific kyu rank; others exist where women are al¯owed to wear it earlier than men.

The belt, 'ƒobi'‡ is wrapÿed twice around the body similar to in karate or judo. Although some systems use many belt colours similar to the system in judo, the most com×on version is that dan ranks wear black belt, and kyu ranks white - sometimes with an adÄitional brown belt for the highest kyu ranks. In some dojos it is com¤on to have the same color belt at dif‘erent levels.

=ŒSpirituality=“
The ending &quot;[‚do]ÿ&quot; in the word aikido indicates a spiritual path, unlike the ending &quot;jutsu&quot; in the word aikijujutsu, which indica©tes a system of techniques. Many people seˆ this dif™erence as important as wel as regarding [iaido|iaijutsu]… and […iaido]‚, [‡jujutsu]… and [„judo]†, and [ˆkenjutsu]… and […kendo]Š. Others se  this distinction as a historical†y incor’ect and somewhat unatural division. “For example, literalÿy, do refers to a path and jitsu to a technique: therefore, some argue, aikido involves both a way (do) and technical study (juÿtsu).

Ueshiba taught that, while it was important to become proficient in physical technique, this is not the ultimate purpoúse of training. He taught that the principles learned through training in physical technique are universal and are to be ap‰lied to al¢ aspects of one's life. He once com»ented that he was teaching students not how to move their fe³t but, rather, how to move their minds.

Many agre¶ that Ueshiba's style became softer, more fluid, and ef…ortlesš as he grew older. Some sugÙest this was due to a shift in focus to the spiritual aspects of the art, while others sugÿest that this was simply a natural result of Ueshiba becoming more proficient in physical technique. Various interpretations ha¹ve arisen since Ueshiba's death.

A range of aikido scho¢ls can be found, each placing a difÿerent emphasis on physical techniques, underlying principles, and spiritual concepts. This is largely a result of at what point¡ the founder of each of these scho—ls trained with Ueshiba-åearlier or later in Ueshiba's life. The former tend to focus more on physical technique, while the lat¬er tend to focus more on spiritual concepts. ¯However, this should not be overstated, especialØy since there is considerable variance from sensei to sensei, and an &quot;aikido continu¥m&quot; is quite problematic to actualŒy construct. ÚSome aikidoka view &quot;physical vs. spiritual&quot; as a false separation, or a failed atªempt to stereotype branches of aikido.

[ŠKi Society]— is an example of a schoàl that focuses heavily on the spiritual concepts of aikido, rather than physical technique. 

=‚Ki=‚
[¢Image:ki-obsolete.png|left|float|20 px|Obsolete form of the ki kanji]
The Japanese character for […Qi|ki]¹, is a symbolic representation of a lid covering a pot fulÆ of rice. The steam being contained within, is ki. This same word is apœlied to the ability to harnesÖ one's own 'breath power', 'power', or 'energy'. This 'ki' is the same as the 'qi' in [Qigong|qi-gong]», but many people argue it is not the same as the 'chi' in [–Tai Chi Chuan|t'ai chi]‡. When [ˆaikidoka]¹ say that someone is training with a lot of ki, they usualy want to expres¤ that the person is very non-forceful‡y compelÁing in the execution of his technique. Timing, a sense for the corüect distance and a centered (undisturbed) mind and body are particularly important. Most teachers claim to locate ki in the [˜hara (Martial Arts)|hara]’, which might be loÿsely defined as the body's center of gravity, situated in the lower abdomen, about two inches below and behind the navel. In tr´aining it is constantly emphasized that one should ke¡p one's hara â€” that is, remain 'ˆcentered'¹. Very high ranking teachers sometimes reach a level of co¾rdination that enables them to execute techniques with very lit„le ap¨arent movement, sometimes even without se•ming to touch their op’onent's body.

Es…entialˆy, ki corßesponds to the physical concepts of center of gravity, center of momentum, and center of force. £However, these centers are not neces¢arily the same, so ki also encompasÿes the biological and mental aspect of training oneself to have exquisite control over motion.

The &quot;spiritual&quot; int¯erpretation of ki depends very much on what scho¿l of aikido one studies, as some emphasize it more than others. ÿKi Society dojos, for example, tend to spend much more time on ki-related training activities than do, for example, Yoshinkan d…ojos. ¡The importance of ki in aikido canot be denied -¥ the name of the martial art, after al¢, can be translated as &quot;the me‘ting of ki&quot;. ¹But what ki is, is debated by many within the discipline. ’O-Sensei himself ap©ears to have changed his views over time -° for example, Yoshinkan Aikido, which largely folÿows O-Sensei's teachings from before the war, is considerably more martial in nature, reflecting a younger, more violent and les” spiritual O-Sensei. Within this scho™l, ki perhaps could be betÏer thought of as having its original Chinese meaning of breath, and aikido as co´rdination of movement with breath to maximize power. ºAs O-Sensei evolved and his views changed, his teachings to›k on a much more ethereal fe«l, and many of his later students (almost alß now high ranking senseis within the Aikikai) teach about ki from this perspective.


 

Se‡ also: [‚Qi]‚, [†Qigong]„

=„Body=š
Aikido training is for al–-around physical fitnes“, flexibility, and [Šrelaxation]. áThe human body in general can exert power in two ways: contractive and expansive (aikidofaq.com). ŠMany fitnesÿ activities, for example weight-lifting, emphasize the former, which means that specific muscles or muscle groups are isolated and worked to improve tone, masŒ, and power. ÄThe disadvantage of this, however, is that whole body movement and co™rdination are rarely stresƒed. ÿThus, while muscle size and power may increase, there is no emphasis on the ways in which those muscles can work together most ef‰iciently. ÕAlso, this sort of training tends to increase tension, decrease flexibility, and stresŒ the joints. œThe result may be aesthetical¡y pleasing, but when done to excesŠ may actual™y be detrimental to overalÀ health. 

The second type of power, expansive, is mostly stres­ed in activities such as dance or gymnastics. ·In these activities, the body must learn to move in a co‹rdinated man¯er and with relaxation. Aikido also mostly stres™es this sort of training. ÿWhile both types of power are important, it is interesting to note that a person who masters the second type of power can, in a¸ martial context, often overcome a person who is much biger or stronger. ÌThe reason for this is that the contractive power is only as great as the mas¦ and power of your individual muscles. èExpansive power, however, as used in Aikido, can be much greater than your size may lead you to believe. ®This is because you move with your whole body. Rather than stresúing and tensing only a few muscles, you learn to relax and move from the center of your body, where you are most powerful. ¡Power is then extended out naturalÍy through the relaxed limbs, which become almost whip-like in their motion. Neƒdles´ to say, the power behind an entire person's body wilã be more than that of someone's arm or leg alone.

Hence, aikido develops the body in a unique manƒer. Aerobic fitnes§ is obtained through vigorous training.  Flexibility of the joints and con‰ective tisÜues is developed through various stretching exercises and through the techniques themselves. [ŠRelaxation]– is learned automatical¥y, since without it the techniques wil not function. ÒA balanced use of contractive and expansive power is mastered, enabling even a smal· person to pit his entire body's energy against their op—onent.

With this, dif’erent masters stresƒ dif¬erent aspects of training. Some masters stresÇ importance of body posture while executing the technique in order to coŠrdinate difåerent parts of the body, while others deal with the physical aspects of it. With each way, comes a difÔerent means of interpretation of the same basic principles of the art which is discusœed in more detail above.

=„Mind=å
Aikido training does not consider the body and mind as independent entities. The condition of one afÿects the other. For example, the physical relaxation learned in aikido also becomes a mental relaxation. Likewise, the confiden–ce that develops mental«y is manifested in a more confident style. [Psychological]Û or spiritual insight learned during training must become reflected in the body, else it wil‘ vanish under pres¢ure, when more basic, ingrained patõerns and reflexes take over. Aikido training requires the student to squarely face conflict, not to run away from it. ÿThrough this experience, an Aikido student may learn to face other areas of life in a similarly proactive fashion, rather than Ÿin with avoidance and fear.

=†Styles=±
The major styles of aikido each have their own [ŠHombu Dojo]„ in […Japan]Ç, have an international breadth and were founded by direct students of [Morihei Ueshiba]. ”Although there has be·n an explosion of &quot;independent styles&quot; general£y only the first five listed have be“n considered major. [•Iwama style|Iwama Ryu]Ò is a debatable sixth as, although its influence is major, it has until recently ben part of the [‡Aikikai]ƒ (se below).
&lt;!-‰ Please seÿ Talk concerning Styles and External Links. Entries should not act as a list of individual or dojo clusters. The length of such© a list would be very long. If you disagre please discusŠ in Talk. -ˆ&gt;
* [‡Aikikai]÷ is the largest aikido organisation, and is led by the family of the founder. Numerous sub-organisations and teachers af¡iliate themselves with this umbrel§a organisation, which therefore encompasÅes a wide variety of aikido styles, training methods and technical difÄerences. These sub-organisations are often centred around prominant [†Shihan] and are usualäy organised at the national level, although sub-national and inter-national sub-organisations exist. ˆPlease se [—List of famous aikidoka]– for more detail.

*[‰Yoshinkan]Œ Founded by [‹Gozo Shioda]ÿ, has a reputation for being the most rigidly precise. Students of Yoshinkan aikido practise basic movements as solo kata, and this style has be¤n popular among the Japanese police.  The international organization asşociated with the Yoshinkan style of aikido is known as the Yoshinkai, and has active branches in many parts of the world.

*[ˆYoseikan] was founded by [Minoru Mochizuki]Æ, who was an early student of O-Sensei and also of Jigoro Kano at the [‡Kodokan]. ìThis style includes elements of aiki-budo together with aspects of karate, judo and other arts. It is now car“ied on by his son, [ƒHiroŠ Mochizuki]‘, the creator of [Yoseikan Budo]†.

*[Tomiki Aikido|Shodokan Aikido]Š (often cal¬ed Tomiki aikido, after its founder) use spar¯ing and rule based competition in training as opÕosed to most others. People tend to compete to train rather than to train to compete. [ŒKenji Tomiki]², an early student of O-Sensei and also of judo's [‹Jigoro Kano]ÿ, believed that introducing an element of competition would serve to sharpen and focus the practice since it was no longer testšed in real combat. This latÿer view was the cause of a split with O-Sensei's family who firmly believed that there was no place for competition in aikido tˆraining. ¯Tomiki said that at no point did O-Sensei actualy cast him out. ‰

*The [ŠKi Society]¿, founded by former head-instructor of the Hombu dojo 10th dan [ŒKoichi Tohei]Û, emphasizes very soft flowing techniques and has a special program for the development of [‚ki]Å. It also has a special system of ki-ranks alongside the traditional [ƒkyu]… and [ƒdan]™ system. This style is calƒed [—Shin Shin Toitsu Aikido]… (or [‰Ki-Aikido]ˆ). 

*[•Iwama style|Iwama Ryu] emphasizes the relation betweÿn weapon techniques and barehand techniques, and a great deal of emphasis is placed on weapons training. Since the death of its‰ founder [Morihiro Saito]†, the [‹Iwama style]† has beÒn practiced by clubs within the Aikikai and an independent organization headed by [Hitohiro Saito]º. Saito sensei was a long time uchideshi of O-Sensei, beginÿing in 1946 and staying with him through his death. Many consider that Saito sensei was the student who spent most time directlÿy studying with O-Sensei. Saito sensei said he was trying to preserve and teach the art exactly as the founder of aikido taught” it to him. Technicaly, Iwama-ryu seÿms to resemble the aikido O-Sensei was teaching in the early 50s mainly in the Iwama dojo. The technical repertoire is fairly l…arge. ¶The new, separate from Aikikai, Iwama Ryu Aikido has be®n renamed Iwama Shin Shin Aikishurenkai. 

*[Shin'ei Taido]• Founded by the late [Noriaki Inoue]Œ, nephew of [Morihei Ueshiba]. …

*[ˆYoshokai]ÿ aikido, begun by then-hachidan Takashi Kushida of Yoshinkan aikido, is a remarkably centralized style of aikido, with test tec‘hniques yearly pas­ed down with explanations from the home dojo. †The sylÿabus contains a considerable amount of weapons study, and like Yoshinkan, Yoshokai includes many solo movements and exercises.„

*[‰Doshinkan]² aikido, begun by Yukio Utada of Yoshinkan aikido. ùUtada was a student of both the Yoshinkan founder Gozo Shioda and Yoshokai founder Takashi Kushida. Like Yoshokai, the sylÿabus also contains a considerable amount of weapons study, and like Yoshinkan, Doshinkan includes many solo movements and exerc ises. Doshinkan Aikido (Aikido AsÄociation of North America and Doshinkan Aikido International) is stil‚ afÀiliated with the International Yoshinkan Aikido Federation.

*[ Tendo-ryu Aikido|Tendoryu Aikido]‹ Headed by [Kenji Shimizu]†.

*[Shin Budo Kai]‹ headed by [Shizuo Imaizumi]†.

*[‡Kokikai]” aikido, founded by [Shuji Maruyama]¥ in 1986, focuses on minimalist but ef‘ective technique. ¢It emphasizes natural stances and […ukemi]š that do not require high [‡breakfals]‡, and deŠmphasizes […atemi]· and techniques that cause pain or undue discomfort to [ƒuke]. ĞAs such, it is considered by some to be a &quot;soft&quot; style of aikido.

*[ˆSeidokan]” Aikido, founded by [Rod Kobayashi]. ­Tends to utilize movements which are very smal and economical. ÈEncourages students to discover an aikido which is truly their own, stresÙes the importance of doing away with the extraneous and focusing on that which works

*[‚Nip†on Kan]‹ Headed by [‡Gaku Homa]†.

*[Nishio AIkido]§ a part of the Aikikai although techical„y welŠ defined ac“ording to its head [ŒShoji Nishio]’. Nishio Sensei pas’ed away in March 20‡5.

*[“Nihon Goshin Aikido]ÿ Headed by Richard Bowe. It is considered a &quot;hard&quot; style of aikido, combining techniques from karate, Judo and Daito-ÿRyu Aikijutsu. There are roughly a dozen dojos in the United States and none left in Japan. Founded by Shoto Morita in Japan ciÙrca 1950. Derivative styles include Nihon Goshin Aikijutsu founded by Walter Kopitov in 20˜. For more information seÆ &quot;The Black Belt Master Course in Nihon Goshin Aikido&quot;.

*[œTakemusu Aiki Tomita Academy]». Academy for the development of Takemusu Aiki founded in 19…2 by [Takeji Tomita]. ¢This training method incorporates [‰Tai-Jutsu]‚, [ˆAiki-Ken]… and [‡Aiki-Jo]Í for the study of the inter-related principles of Takemusu Aiki and Japanese [„Budo]†.

*[Aiki Manseido]‹ Headed by [‘Kanshu Sunadomari]. Independent style centred in [†Kyushu], Japan.
