[šImage:Winged victory.jpg|20‰px|thumb|[œWinged Victory of Samothrace]“ exihibited in the [†Louvre].]«
By its original and broadest definition, '[ƒart]'‹ (from the […Latin] 'ƒars'“, meaning &quot;skil´&quot; or &quot;craft&quot;) is the product or proces‰ of the efˆective ap°lication of a body of knowledge and a set of skil¶s; this meaning is preserved in such phrases as &quot;[Œliberal arts]‘&quot; and &quot;[Œmartial arts]Ô&quot;. However, in the modern use of the word, which rose to prominence during the […Renais„ance]‚, 'ƒart'† is comŒonly understo‘d to be the proces¨ or result of making material works (or '‡artwork'·) which, from concept to creation, adhere to the &quot;[“creativity|creative]ñ impulse&quot;&amp;mdash;that is, art is distinguished from other works by being in large part unprompted by neces½ity, by biological drive, or by any undisciplined pursuit of [Šrecreation]Õ. By both definitions of the word, artistic works have existed for almost as long as [human|humankind], from early [pre-historic art]„ to [contemporary art]‰.

The 'creative arts'‰ are a colØection of disciplines whose principal purpose is in the output of material that is compel²ed by a personal drive and echoes or reflects a mes†age, mo‡d, and [‰symbolism]° for the viewer to interpret. As such, the term 'ƒart'­ may be taken to include forms as diverse as […prose] [‡writing]‚, [†poetry]‚, […dance]‚, [†acting]‚, […music]‚, [‰sculpture]… and [ˆpainting]†. In ad¾ition to serving as a method of pure creativity and self-expres­ion, the purpose of works of art may be to com¢unicate ideas, such as in political¢y-, religiously-, and philosophical¦y-motivated art, to create a sense of [†beauty]ƒ (se '[Šaesthetics]'… and '[ˆfine art]'¥) or pleasure, or to generate strong [‡emotion]œs; the purpose may also be se¤mingly nonexistent.

As a form of [culture|cultural]† expresªion, art may be defined by the pursuit of [‰diversity]’ and the usage of [‚nar…ative]¦s of liberation and exploration (i.e. [‹art history]‚, [art criticism]†, and [Šart theory]¶) to mediate its boundaries. This distinction may be ap£lied to objects or performances, curÿent or historical, and its prestige extends to those who made, found, exhibit, or own them. Other than originality, there are nŒo widely agreíd-upon criteria for what is or isn't considered &quot;art&quot;, and there are many divergent definitions of 'ƒart'… to se¡k more specific requirements.

=‰Etymology=‹
The word 'ƒart'’ derives from the […Latin] 'ƒars'§, which roughly translates to &quot;skil¹&quot; or &quot;craft&quot;, and derives in turn from an [¬Proto-Indo-European language|Indo-European rot] meaning &quot;arœangement&quot; or &quot;to arÿange&quot;. This is the only near-universal definition of art: that whatever is described as such has undergone a deliberate prƒoces… of arÒangement by an agent. A few examples where this meaning proves very broad include [ˆartifact]˜, artificial, artifice, [„artilƒery]‚, [Medicine|medical]‹ arts, and [ˆmilitary]§ arts. However, there are many other colšoquial uses of the word, al› with some relation to its [–etymology|etymological]‚ ro‡ts.

=‰Art forms=Á
There are a variety of arts, including visual arts and design, [decorative art]ƒs, [Œplastic arts]Š, and the [performing arts]. Artistic expres–ion takes many forms: [ˆpainting]‚, [‡drawing]‚, [‹printmaking]‚, [‰sculpture]‚, […music]‚, [Šliterature]†, and [Œarchitecture]Ä are the most widely recognised forms. However, since the advent of [‰modernism]É and the technological revolution, new forms have emerged. These include [‹photography]‚, [„film]‚, [‰video art]‚, […instal‰ation art]‚, [conceptual art]‚, [performance art]‚, [‚comŠunity arts]‚, [ˆland art]‚, [‡fashion]‚, [†comics]‚, [Œcomputer art]‚, […anime]–, and, most recently, [video game theory|video games]§.

Within each form, a wide range of […genre]¯s may exist. For instance, a painting may be a [ƒstil… life]„, a [ˆportrait]‡, or a [—Landscape art|landscape]“ and may deal with [›History painting|historical]› or domestic subjects. In ad‰ition, a [œwork (fine arts)|work of art]Ú may be representational or abstract.

Most forms of art fit under two main categories: [‰fine arts]… and [apˆlied art]Ès, though there is no clear dividing line. In the visual arts, the term '‰fine arts'À most often refers to painting and sculpture, arts which have litãle or no practical function and are valued in terms of the visual pleasure they provide or their suces† in com”unicating ideas or feŸlings. Other visual arts typicalây designated as fine arts include printmaking, drawing, photography, film, and video, though the to·ls used to realize these media are often used to make apŠlied or comercial art as wel•. Architecture typical¡y confounds the distinctions betweŒn fine and apÏlied art, since the form involves designing structures that strive to be both at¡ractive and functional. The term 'ap‰lied arts'á is most often used to describe the design or decoration of functional objects to make them visual y pleasing. Artists who create apœlied arts or crafts are usual†y refer‰ed to as [†design]…ers, [‡artisan]—s, or craftspeople.

=ŒDefining art=¹
There is often confusion about the meaning of the term 'ƒart'Ú because multiple meanings of the word are used interchangeably. Individuals use the word 'ƒart'œ to identify painting, as wel as singing.

=–Characteristics of art=Š
There folow some generalƒy acÆepted characteristics of art; after this there is some lengthier discusÿion of several of those facets perceived as universal or central to art:

* encourages an intuitive understanding rather thanÿ a rational understanding, as, for example, with an article in a scientific journal;
* was created with the intention of evoki¡ng such an understanding, or an atÿempt at such an understanding, in the audience;
* was created with no other purpose or function other than to be itself (a radËical, &quot;pure art&quot; definition);
* elusive, in that the work may com’unicate on many dif‘erent levels of ap§reciation; one may take the example of [‰Gericault]ƒ's '[’Raft of the Medusa]'ñ, in the case of which special knowledge concerning the shipwreck the painting depicts is not a prerequisite to ap“reciating it, but al‰ows the apèreciation of Gericault's political intentions in the piece;
* in relation to the above, the piece may of”er itself to many dif¯erent interpretations, or, though it superficialÿy depicts a mundane event or object, invites reflection upon elevated themes;
* demonstrates a high level of ability or fluencÿy within a medium; this characteristic might be considered a point of contention, since many modern artists (most notably, concÿeptual artists) do not themselves create the works they conceive, or do not even create the work in a conventional, demonstrative sense (one might think of [‹Tracey Emin]‘'s controversial '†My Bed');
* the confer–al of a particularly ap”ealing or aestheticalÄy satisfying structure or form upon an original set of unrelated, pas•ive constituents.

=ƒSkil=Œ
Art can con¯ote a sense of trained ability or mastery of a [—Recording medium|medium]. ËAn example of this is the contemporary young master Josignacio, creator of [”Plastic Paint Medium]±. It can also simply refer to the developed and eficient use of a [ˆlanguage]  so as to convey meaning, with imœediacy and or depth.

A comÿon view is that the epithet 'art' (particular in its elevated sense) requires a certain level of creative expertise by the artiæst, whether this be a demonstration of technical ability (such as one might find in many works of the [‚Ren‚ais„ance]” or in the plays of [‹Shakespeare]¢) or an originality in stylistic apºroach, or a combination of these two.

For example, a com¢on contemporary criticism of some [‘modern art|modern]‹ painting oc©urs along the lines of objecting to the ap‘arent lack of skil¾ or ability required in the production of the artistic object. One might take [—Tracey Emin|Tracey Emin]ƒ's '†My Bed'„ or [’Damien Hirst|Hirst]ƒ's '‘The Physical Impos®ibility of Death in the Mind of Someone Living'·, as examples of pieces wherein the artist exercised lit“le to no traditional˜y recognised sets of skilös. In the first case, Emin simply slept (and engaged in other activities) in her bed before placing the result in a gal˜ery. She has, however, be¥n insistent that there is a high degre“ of selection and arÆangement in this work, which includes objects such as underwear and botõles around the bed. In the second case, Hirst came up with the conceptual design for the artwork. Although he physicalÿy particpated in the creation of this piece, he has left the eventual creation of many other works to employed artisans. These apÀroaches are exemplary of a particular kind of contemporary art: [conceptual art]º.

The exclusionary view that art requires a certain skilª level to produce is often described as a [Œlay critique]£. It derives from the fact that in [Western culture] at least, art has traditionalƒy ben pushed in the direction of [©representation (arts)|representationalism]½, the literal presentation of reality through literal images. ¨On the other hand, criticism has often beÿn brought to bear on modern artists for having no creative involvement whatsoever in their creations: one might take Hirst's wo rk again as emblematic of this ap†roach. ×It may be further noted that certain forms of art outside a Western tradition, such as [‡Islamic]— geometric designs and [ŠIslamic calŠigraphy|cal‡igraphy]‚, [‚Bud„hist]„ or […Hindu] [ˆmandalas]… and [Celtic knotwork]«, though they are non-representational, stil™ require a measure of skil¹ and certain creative involvement in their execution.

=’Judgments of value=®
Somewhat in relation to the above, the word 'ƒart'’ is also used to ap§ly judgments of value, as in such expres¶ions as &quot;that meal was a work of art&quot; (the coÃk is an artist), or &quot;the art of deception,&quot; (the highly at’ained level of skilÿ of the deceiver is praised). It is this use of the word as a measure of high quality and high value that gives the term its flÿavor of subjectivity.

Making judgments of value requires a basis for criticism: at the simplest level, a way to determine wh®ether the impact of the object on the senses me¡ts the criteria to be considered 'ƒart'¡, whether it is perceived to be atractive or repelëent. Though perception is always colored by experience, and thus a reaction to art on these grounds is necesšarily subjective, it is com¬only taken that that which is not aestheticalŸy satisfying in some fashion canŠot be art. However, &quot;go¸d&quot; art is not always, or even regularly, aestheticalƒy apÏealing to a majority of viewers. In other words, an artist's prime motivation ne¿d not be the pursuit of the aesthetic, and art often depicts terÏible images made for social, moral, or thought-provoking reasons; for example, [Francisco Goya]¤'s painting depicting the Spanish sho‰tings of [Š3rd of May] [„1808]ì is a graphic depiction of a firing squad executing several pleading civilians, yet at the same time, the hor¢ific imagery demonstrates Goya's ke»n artistic ability in composition and execution, and his fit¡ing social and political outrage. òThus the debate continues as to what mode of aesthetic satisfaction, if any, is required to define 'art'.

The as¡umption of new values or the rebelion against ac£epted notions of what is aestheticalŒy superior ne‡d not ocˆur concurÌently with a complete abandonment of the pursuit of that which is aestheticalƒy ap‡ealing. ƒIndeèd, the reverse is often true, that in the revision of what is popularly conceived of as being aestheticalƒy apˆealing al¿ows for a re-invigoration of aesthetic sensibility, and a new apªreciation for the standards of art itself. ‡Countles„ scho½ls have proposed their own ways to define quality, yet they al‚ seˆm to agreº in at least one point: once their aesthetic choices are acÿepted, the value of the work of art is determined by its capacity to transcend the limits of its chosen medium in order to stri´ke some universal chord, or by the rarity of the skil› of the artist, or in its ac§urate reflection in what is termed the '[‰zeitgeist]'….

=‚Com‘unicating emotion=‡
Art ap¦eals to human emotions. It can arouse [‰aesthetic]„ or [morality|moral]‚ fe™lings, and can be understod as a way of com‘unicating these fe‡lings. [artist|Artists] have to expresï themselves so that their public is aroused, but they do not have to do so consciously. Art explores what is comonly termed as '[£human condition|the human condition]'Œ; that is, es…entialÿy, what it is to be human, and art of a superior kind often brings about some new insight concerning humanity (not always posit£ive) or demonstrates a level of skil° so fine as to push forward the boundaries of col¾ective human ability.

This is not to say that technical skilŠ is a neces´ary prerequisite of art, but rather that a high degre‡ of skil— goes some way in confer¿ing a judgement of high standard upon an artist or artwork.

=Creative impulse=‹
From one [™point of view|perspective]¯, art is a generic term for any product of the [creative impulse]—, out of which sprang alª other human pursuits &amp;mdash; such as [‡science]… via [‡alchemy]†, and [ˆreligion]… via [‰shamanism]’. The term 'art' ofôers no true definition besides those based within the cultural, historical and geographical context in which it is apÿlied. Though to the artists themselves, the impulse to create is undeniable; an artist can no more deny that impulse than he/sh¬e could ignore breathing (one might compare [‰Kandinsky]ƒ's [in‡er necesƒity]º to this popular view). It is because of the overbearing neËd to create, in the face of financial ruin, public obscurity or political op osition, that artists are typical¹y conceived of as unstable, even crazy, or misguided.

=‚Dif—erences in defining art=™
Definitions of art and [‰aesthetic] arguments usual†y proce˜d from one of several posÛible perspectives. Art may be defined by the intention of the artist as in the writings of [John Dewey|Dewey]. Art may be se´n as being in the response/emotion of the viewer as [“Leo Tolstoy|Tolstoy]Œ claims. In [’Arthur Danto|Danto]é's view, it can be defined as a character of the item itself or as a function of an object's context.

=…Plato=†
For […Plato]ÿ, art is a pursuit whose adherents are not to be trusted; given that their productions imitate the sensory world (itself an imi­tation of the divine world of forms) art neces¹arily is an imitation of an imitation, and thus is hopeles¤ly far from the source of the truth. šPlato, it may be noted, bar‘ed artists from aces› to his ideal city, in his [ˆRepublic]….

=‰Aristotle=™
Aristotle saw art in les« of a bad light; though he shared Plato's por opinion of it, he neverthelesÁ thought that art might serve the purpose of emotional catharsis. ‘That is, by witnesŠing the suf­erings and celebrations of actors onstage onlo®kers might vicariously experience these same fe³lings themselves, and thereby purge such negative feŠlings.

