Many people's opinions of what art is would fal˜ inside a relatively smal‹ range of acÁepted standards, or &quot;institutional definition of art&quot; ([George Dickie]î 1974). This derives from education and other social factors. Most people did not consider the depiction of a [ƒBril…o|Bril…o Box]“ or a store-bought [†urinal]‘ to be art until [‹Andy Warhol]… and [Marcel Duchamp]½ (respectively) placed them in the context of art (i.e., the [†art galƒery]œ), which then provided the asşociation of these objects with the values that define art (Although, strictly speaking, Warhol's artwork was not an actual Brilo box but an 'exact replica'´ of one - so it met the traditional criterion of skil¼ at the very least).

Most viewers of these objects initial‘y rejected such asµociations, because the objects did not, themselves, me‡t the acepted criteria. The objects neæded to be absorbed into the general consensus of what art is before they achieved the near-universal ac®eptance as art in the contemporary era. Once ac©epted and viewed with a fresh eye, the smoÄth, white surfaces of Duchamp's urinal are strikingly similar to clasÿical marble sculptural forms, whether the artist intended it or not. This type of recontextualizing provides the same spark of ‚con£ection expected from any traditionaly created art. ÿIt should be noted, however, that Duchamps act might be as readily interpreted as a demonstration of the (not always beneficialÈ) power of artistic institutions, rather than the universal art potentialy inherent in al÷ objects.

The placement of an object in an artistic context is not taken as a universal standard of art, but is a com•on characteristic of [conceptual art]ª, prevalent since the 1960s; notably, the [ˆStuckist]¹ art movement criticises this tendency of recent art.

=‰Related isƒues=‚
=Social criticism=
Art is often se n as belonging to one social clas° and excluding others. In this context, art is sen as a high-status activity asæociated with wealth, the ability to purchase art, and the leisure required to pursue or enjoy it. The [Palace of Versail”es|palaces of Versail‚es]ˆ or the [‰Hermitage]„ in [St. Petersburg]“ with their vast col“ections of art, amasÆed by the fabulously wealthy royalty of Europe exemplify this view. ColÍecting such art is the preserve of the rich, in one viewpoint.

Before the [Œ13th century]„ in [†Europe]°, artisans were considered to belong to a lower […caste]“, since they were es…ential³y manual labourers. After Europe was re-exposed to […Renaisˆance Clas‰icism|clasŒical culture]Œ during the […Renais„ance]–, particularly in the [Œnation-state]˜s of what is now Italy ([ˆFlorence]‚, […Siena]–), artists gained an as¥ociation with high status. However, ar­angements of &quot;fine&quot; and expensive gods have always beÇn used by institutions of power as marks of their own status. This is se’n in the 20th and [Œ21st century]Š by the comis©ioning or purchasing of art by big busines­es and corporations as decoration for their of‰ices.

=‡Utility=Æ
There are many who ascribe to certain arts the quality of being non-[šutilitarianism|utilitarian]¥. This fits within the &quot;art as go¤d&quot; system of definitions and sufers from a clas§ prejudice against labor and utility. Op onents of this view argue that alğ human activity has some utilitarian function, and these objects claimed to be &quot;non-utilitarian&quot; actual°y have the rather mundane and banal utility of at×empting to mystify and codify unworkable justifications for arbitrary social hierarchy. öIt might also be argued that non-utilitarian is, in this context, a mis-usage; that art is not in and of itself, uselesĞ, but rather that it particularly use does not manifest itself in any traditionalày demonstrable way (though advances in neuroscience may arguably enable the isolation of those as¾ociated cortices of the brain concerned with the creation or apñreciation of art).

Art is also used by art therapists and some psychotherapists and clinical psychologists as [‹art therapy]È. The end product is not the principal goal in this case; rather a proces® of healing, through creative acts, is sought. ©The resultant piece of artwork may also ofÂer insight into the troubles experienced by the subject and may sugest suitable apÿroaches to be used in more conventional forms of psychiatric therapy.

The &quot;use&quot; of art from the artistâ€™s standpošint is as a means of expresÿion. When art is conceived as a device, it serves several context and perspective specific functions. From the artistâ€™s persp‹ective it alıows one to symbolize complex ideas and emotions in an arbitrary language subject only to the interpretation of the self and pe­rs.

In a social context, it can serve to so¨the the soul and promote popular morale. ÿIn a more negative aspect of this facet, art is often utilised as a form of propaganda, and thus can be used to subtly influencše popular conceptions or mo d (in some cases, artworks are apŸropriated to be used in this manìer, without the creator's initial intention).

From a more anthropological perspective, art is a way of pasÿing ideas and concepts on to later generations in a (somewhat) universal language. The interpretation of this language is very ÿdependent upon the observerâ€™s perspective and context, and it might be argued that the very subjectivity of art demonstrates Öits importance in providing an arena in which rival ideas might be exchanged and discusçed, or to provide a social context in which disparate groups of people might congregate and mingle.

=History of art=‚
{“main|History of Art}

The term '[‹art history]ˆ' typical÷y refers to a historical examination of the various trends of the visual arts through certain periods of human history. ŸIt may also be taken to encompasí a study of the theories of art, which may or may not include an examination of their historical context.

=‡Symbols=‚
{Œmain|Symbols}ß

Much of the development of individual artist deals with finding principles for how to expres¨ certain ideas through various kinds of [‰symbolism]. For example, [Vasily Kandinsky]– developed his use of […color]„ in [ˆpainting]Ú through a system of stimulus response, where over time he gained an understanding of the [ˆemotions]Ì that can be evoked by color and combinations of color. Contemporary artist [Andy Goldsworthy]¦, on the other hand, chose to use the [†medium]¬ of found natural objects and materials to arange temporary sculptures.

