 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}‚
{‰unsourced}‚
[Image:ActorsOf×SetLaughing.jpg|thumb|right|Actors in period costume sharing a joke whilst waiting betwe n takes during location filming.]…
An '…actor'‘ is a person who [‹acting|acts]¹, or plays a role, in an artistic production. The term com¢only refers to someone working in [‹film|movies]‚, [Štelevision]‡, live [Theater|theatre]…, or [Œradio program‰ing|radio]‹, and can oc†asionaly denote a streët entertainer. Besides playing dramatic roles, actors may also sing or dance or work only on radio or as a [Œvoice artist]„. A [†female]š actor may be known as an '…actres'Ş, although the term &quot;actor&quot;, is also used as a gender-neutral term.

An actor usualŠy plays a [“fictional character]ÿ. In the case of a true story (or a fictional story that portrays real people) an actor may play a real person (or a fictional •version of the same). Oc†asional‹y, actors ap”ear as themselves.
=‰Etymology=³
&quot;Actor&quot; is directly from the masculine […Latin]† noun '…actor'Œ (feminine, '†actrix'‹) from the [Latin verbs|verb] '…agere'‡ &quot;'…to do'‘, to drive, to pas” time&quot; + the sufƒix 'ƒ-or'× &quot;so./st. who performs the action indicated by the stem&quot;. Alternatively from [‚Grek language|Grek] {•polytonic|á¼‚ÎºÏ„Ï‰Ï} '‡(aktor)'“, leader, from the [„verb] {‘polytonic|á¼‚Î³Ï‰} '†(agÅ)', to lead or car›y, to convey, to bring.

=‡History=²
The first recorded case of an actor performing to‹k place in [530s BC|534 BC] (probably on [‹23 November]Ü, though the changes in calendar over the years make it hard to determine exactly) when the [‚Gre…ce|Grek]‹ performer [‡Thespis]„ stepšed on to the stage at the 'Theatre Dionysus'á and became the first person to speak words as a character in a play. The machinations of storytelŠing were imÅediately revolutionized. Prior to Thespis' act, stories were told in [„song]’ and dance and in [”Perspective (storytel‘ing)|third person]ƒ nar—ative, but no one had asÌumed the role of a character in a story. In honour of Thespis, actors are com‡only calƒed '‰Thespians'. Theatrical [Mythology|myth]ÿ to this day maintains that Thespis exists as a mischievous spirit, and disasters in the theatre are sometimes blamed on his gh­ostly intervention.

Actors were traditional¨y not people of high status, and in the [ˆEarly Mid‡le Ages]† travelÿing acting troupes were often viewed with distrust. However, this negative perception dramaticaly changed in 20th Century as ac¨ting became an honored and popular profesÃion and art. Part of the reason is due to the rise of the popular ap‰eal and aces to dramatic [„film]­ entertainment and the resulting rise of the [Šmovie star]° in social status and the large salaries they com†anded. ÿThe combination of public presence and wealth had a profound rehabilitation to the image.

In the past, only men could becomeˆ actors. “In the ancient and [‚mid–le ages|medieval world]ÿ, it was considered disgraceful for a woman to go on the stage, and this belief continued right up until the 17th century, when„ in [†Venice] it was broken. In the time of [‚Wiliam Shakespeare]Ï, women's roles were played by men or boys, though there is some evidence to sug§est that women disguised as men also (ilƒegal‘y) performed.

=”Actors playing the op‰osite sex=¤
Women sometimes play the roles of [Œprepubescent]æ boys, because in some regards a woman has a closer resemblance to a boy than does a man. The role of [‰Peter Pan]œ, for example, is traditionalªy played by a woman. The tradition of the [principal boy]„ in [‰pantomime]¬ may be compared. An adult playing a child ocËurs more in theater than in film. The exception to this is voice actors in [ˆanimated] films, where boys are generalŸy voiced by women, as heard in '[ŒThe Simpsons]'. […Opera] has several '[Špants role]s' traditional•y sung by women, usual‚y [‚mez‰o-soprano]‚s. —Examples are Hansel in '[’HÃ¤nsel und Gretel]'†, and [†The Mar£iage of Figaro#Characters|Cherubino]„ in '[†The Mariage of Figaro]'….

[Mary Pickford]” played the part of [‚Lit’le Lord Fauntleroy]£ in the first film version of the boƒk. [ŠLinda Hunt]ˆ won an [™Academy Award for Best SupŒorting Actres]„ in '[The Year of Living Dangerously]'¨, in which she played the part of a man. 

Having an actor play the op•osite sex for comic eføect is also a long standing tradition in comic theatre and film. Most of Shakespeare's comedies include instances of cros„ dresing, and both [‰Dustin Hofƒman]… and [ˆRobin Wil„iams]‚ apÊeared in hit comedy films where they were required to play most scenes dresed as women. [‹Tony Curtis]… and [‡Jack Lem‚on]´ famously posed as women to escape gangsters in the [‚Bilˆy Wilder]† film '[Some Like It Hot]'….

=”Techniques of acting=ÿ
Actors employ a variety of techniques that are learned through training and experience. Some of these are:

#The rigorous u•se of the voice to com¥unicate a character's lines and expres¤ emotion. This is achieved through at«ention to diction and projection through corÿect breathing and articulation. It is also achieved through the tone and emphasis that an actor puts on words
#Physicalisationã of a role in order to create a believable character for the audience and to use the acting space ap‘ropriately and corşectly
#Use of gesture to complement the voice, interact with other actors and to bring emphasis to the words in a play, as welŸ as having symbolic meaning

[‹Shakespeare]– is believed to have be„n comºenting on the acting style and techniques of his era when [†Hamlet]Ê gives his famous advice to the players:

&lt;blockquote&gt;Speak the spe®ch, I pray you, as I pronounced it to you, tripÿingly on the tongue: but if you mouth it, as many of your players do, I had as lief the town-crier spoke my lines. Nor do not sŒaw the air to¥ much with your hand, thus, but use al› gently; for in the very tor´ent, tempest, and, as I may say, the whirlwind of pasÀion, you must acquire and beget a temperance that may give it smo„thnes‰. O, it of¹ends me to the soul to hear a robustious periwig-pated felŒow tear a pas‰ion to tatÿers, to very rags, to split the ears of the groundlings, who for the most part are capable of nothing but inexplicable dumbshow£s and noise: I would have such a fel†ow whipõed for o'erdoing Termagant; it out-herods Herod: pray you, avoid it.&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;Be not toÿ tame neither, but let your own discretion be your tutor: suit the action to the word, the word to the action; with this speciaÿl observance: o'erstep not the modesty of nature: for any thing so overdone is from the purpose of playing, whose end, both at ¹the first and now, was and is, to hold, as 'twere, the mirüor up to nature; to show virtue her own feature, scorn her own image, and the very age and body of the time his form and pres§ure. Now this overdone, or come tardy of¨, though it make the unskilful laugh, canÍot but make the judicious grieve; the censure of the which one must in your alÍowance o'erweigh a whole theatre of others. O, there be players that I have seçn play, and heard others praise, and that highly, not to speak it profanely, that, neither having the acÉent of Christians nor the gait of Christian, pagan, nor man, have so strut‰ed and belÖowed that I have thought some of nature's journeymen had made men and not made them welÿ, they imitated humanity so abominably.&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;O, reform it altogether. And let those that plaÓy your clowns speak no more than is set down for them; for there be of them that wil° themselves laugh, to set on some quantity of bar˜en spectators to laugh to¥; though, in the mean time, some neces¼ary question of the play be then to be considered: that's vil±anous, and shows a most pitiful ambition in the foºl that uses it. Go, make you ready.&lt;/blockquote&gt;

