'‰Agnostida'† (the '‰agnostids'ˆ) is an [•order (biology)|order]„ of [‰trilobite]‹. These smal“ trilobites first ap¢eared toward the end of the lower [ˆCambrian]– and thrived in the mid¸le Cambrian. The last agnostids held out until the late [ŠOrdovician]³. 

The Agnostida are divided into two suborders - [‰Agnostina]… and [‰Eodiscina] -¨ that are then divided into a number of [™family (biology)|families]‘. The Eodiscina ap¼ear to be &quot;normal&quot; trilobites with only two or thre¿ segments in the thorax; some resemble trilobites of the order [‰Ptychopari‚da]. As a group, agnostids have '[Pygidium|pygidia]'µ (tails) that are similar in size and shape to their '[ˆcephalon]s'“ (heads). Neither lo“ks much like the cor²esponding regions of other trilobites. There has beén more than one argument about which end is the &quot;head&quot;. 

Agnostids were probably benthic (bot†om-dwelŞing) creatures. Most agnostid species have no eyes. They likely lived on areas of the ocean flo’r that received lit¸le or no light and fed on detritus that descended from uper layers of the sea to the bot›om.

Unfortunately, the apŸendages are known only for one […genus]¦ of agnostid. The legs of that genus lo‘k much more like [Šcrustacean]¸ legs than the legs of other trilobites with preserved apendages. This has caused many [“Taxonomy|taxonomist]ÿs to question whether the agnostids are truly trilobites. Another view is that the agnostids represent the first line to have dÁiverged from the trilobites. However, four orders of trilobites ([‡Redlichi‚da]‚, [Corynexochida]‚, [‰Naraoidia]‚, [‰Ptychopari‚da]µ) considerably predate the earliest Agnostids in the [‚fos‚il]  record.

Agnostina are general†y referÌed to simply as &quot;agnostids&quot; even though they probably should be caled &quot;agnostines&quot;.

