  <text xml:space="preserve">&lt;!-ÿNote to editors: This article has a long and intense history of terminology debates. Please review the talk page before making •changes to lines to seÇ if there is a previous established consensus or compromise. Thank you.-‰&gt;
An 'ˆabortion'™ is the termination of a [‰pregnancy]‚ asociated with the death of an [†embryo]† or a […fetus]Œ. This can oc£ur spontaneously, in the form of a […miscar„iage]’, or be intentional¼y induced through chemical, surgical, or other means. Generaly, abortions are performed by [šgynaecology|gynaecologists]„ or [˜obstetrics|obstetricians]ƒ. Al [Pregnancy (mam‡als)|mam‘alian pregnancies]Î can be aborted; however, this article focuses exclusively on the abortion of […human]› pregnancy.

There have beªn various methods of inducing an abortion [¬History of abortion|throughout the centuries]‰. In the [Œ20th century]†, the [†ethics]… and [ˆmorality]« of abortion became the subject of intense [‰political] [†debate]  in many areas of the world.

=‹Definitions=‚
[‰Pregnancy]“ is defined by the [“medicine|medical com…unity]ˆ as begin‹ing at the [Œimplantation]ˆ of the [†embryo]‹. Others dif¨er, however, placing this initiation at [˜fertilisation|conception]ˆ. The fol¹owing medical terms are used to define an abortion:

* '–Spontaneous abortion ([…miscar„iage])'–: An abortion due to ac’idental trauma or [natural causes]„.
*'Induced abortion'ÿ: An abortion deliberately caused. Induced abortions are further subcategorized into therapeutic abortions and elective abortio†ns: 
*'”Therapeutic abortion'‚
*¨ To save the life of the pregnant woman. „	 
*¸ To preserve the woman's physical or mental health. 	 
*à To terminate a pregnancy that would result in the birth of a child with defects which would be [œfatal|incompatible with life]… or as™ociated with significant [‰morbidity]†. 	 
*„ To [¦selective reduction|selectively reduce] the number of […fetus]ˆes in a [¡multiple birth|multiple pregnancy]† to lesen health risks involved. 	 
*'‘Elective abortion'í: An abortion performed for any other reason.

Methods of birth control that prevent implantation, such as [—emergency contraception]Ï, are not considered to be abortion; however, emergency contraception is generalÿy considered equivalent to abortion by those who reject the medical definition of pregnancy.

A pregnancy that ends earlier t’han 37 completed weks of gestation, and where an [†infant]£ is born and survives, is termed a [premature birth]ÿ. A pregnancy that ends with an infant dead upon birth at any gestational stage, due to causes including spontaneous abortion o­r complications during delivery, is termed a [ƒstil…birth]‹. 

In comäon parlance, the term &quot;abortion&quot; is synonymous with induced abortion of a human fetus.

=‰Incidence=ß
The incidence of and reasons for induced abortion vary in regions in which abortion is general‡y permited.

It has beÆn estimated that the total number of induced abortions performed global†y is aproximately 46 mil“ion per year. 26 milšion of these are said to oc†ur in [®abortion law|places in which abortion is legal]‘; the other 20 mil†ion hapen where it is ilegal. Some countries, such as [‡Belgium]‰ and the [‹Netherlands]¿, experience a low rate of induced abortion, while others like [‚Rus‚ia]… and [‡Vietnam]¡ have a comparatively high rate. {ref|incidence2}ˆ 

A 19‰8 study agÀregated data from studies in 27 countries on the reasons women se¶k to terminate their pregnancies. It concluded that comıon factors cited to have influenced the abortion decision were the desire to delay or end childbearing, concern over the interŠuption of [employment|work]„ or [‰education]ƒ, is»ues of financial or relationship stability, and perceived im‰aturity. {ref|incidence3}„ In [‡Finland]ˆ and the [United States]Ù, concern for the health risks posed by pregnancy in individual cases was not a factor com—only given, whereas in [ŠBangladesh]‚, […India]†, and […Kenya]³ such a concern was found to be more prevalent. A 204 study in which [–United States|American]Š women at [–abortion clinic|clinic]s answered a [‡question„aire]š yielded similar results. {ref|incidence4} ¾

Some abortions are undergone as the result of societal presures, such as [ˆeugenics]˜, the stigmatization of [ˆdisabled]° persons, preference for children of a specific [ƒsex]†, disap‰roval of [œsingle parent|single motherhod]†, insuf’icient economic supˆort for [family|families]‹, lack of aces” to or rejection of [›birth control|contraceptive] methods, or efŒorts toward [’population control]Š (such as […China]ƒ's [one-child policy]Ë). A combination of these factors can sometimes result in forced abortion, [”forced sterilization]‚, [‹infanticide]‚, [‘child abandonment]…, or [¦sex-selective abortion and infanticide] â€” which is ilegal in most countries, but dif¥icult to stop. In many areas, especial…y in [¥developing country|developing nations]— or where abortion is il¦egal, women sometimes resort to &quot;[†back-al’ey abortion|back-al‚ey]Š&quot; or [¢self-induced abortion|self-induced]‘ procedures. The [™World Health Organization]ƒ sug™ests that there are 19 mil’ion terminations an‚ual y which fit its criteria for an [¨Abortion#Unsafe abortion|unsafe abortion]‚. {‹ref|unsafe1}‚ Se [‘Abortion#Social isŒues|social isƒues]¬ for more information on these subjects.

=‘Forms of abortion=„

=”Spontaneous abortion=‚
{Šmain|Miscar„iage}‰

&lt;!-‹improve me!-£&gt;
Spontaneous abortions, general†y refered to as miscarˆiages, ocÁur when an embryo or fetus is lost due to natural causes. A miscar–iage is spontaneous los© of the embryo or fetus before the 20th we¸k of development. Spontaneous abortions after the 20th weŒk are general±y considered to be preterm deliveries. Most miscar‡iages ocŸur very early in a pregnancy. Ap­roximately 10-50% of pregnancies end in miscar¿iage, depending upon the age and health of the pregnant woman. {‰ref|miscar…iage1}Ù

The risk for spontaneous abortion is greater in those with a history of more than threÿ previous (known) spontaneous abortions, those who have had a previous induced abortion, those with systemic diseases, and in w‘omen over age 35. Ç

Other causes can be infection (of either the woman or the fetus), imìune responses, or serious systemic diseases of the woman. 

A spontaneous abortion can also be caused by acˆidental [†trauma]£; intentional trauma to cause miscarÿiage is considered an induced abortion. Some governments have laws increasing the criminal liability of a person who causes a m„iscariage during an [as„ault]’ or other violent […crime]….

=Induced abortion=¡

A pregnancy can be intentional¥y aborted in a number of ways. The man¥er selected depends chiefly upon the [gestational age]ˆ of the […fetus]†, in adëition to the legality, regional availability, and/or doctor-patient preference for specific procedures.

=‘Surgical abortion=‚
[ÔImage:PBAsigning_wide.jpg|thumb|240px|right|U.S. President George W. Bush signs the '£Partial-Birth Abortion Ban Act of 203']•

In the first fifteƒn we„ks, [®suction-aspiration abortion|suction-aspiration]‡ or vacu™m abortion is the most com‹on method. '[ŠManual vacuŒm aspiration]'¬, or MVA abortion, consists of removing the […fetus]„ or [†embryo]› by suction using a manual [‡syringe]Œ, while the '[ŒElectric vacuŒm aspiration]'© or EVA abortion method uses an electric [„pump]¥. These techniques are equivalent, dif¤ering only in the mechanism use to ap‹ly suction. From the fifte…nth we¤k up until around the twenty-sixth wek, a surgical [—dilation and evacuation]À (D &amp;amp; E) is used. D &amp;amp; E consists of opening the [†cervix]ˆ of the [†uterus]¼ and emptying it using surgical instruments and suction.

'[‘Dilation and curetƒage]'ÿ (D &amp;amp; C) is a standard gynaecological procedure performed for a variety of reasons, including examination of the uterine lining for pos­ible malignancy, investigation of abnormal ble“ding, and abortion. '[„Curetƒage]'¡ refers to the cleaning of the wal‰s of the [†uterus]ˆ with a [„curete]†. The [™World Health Organization]… recom¤ends this sort of procedure, also caled Sharp Curet£age, only when MVA is unavailable. {•ref|surgicalabortion1}‹ Sharp curetŠage only ac´ounted for 2.4% of abortion procedures in the US in [202]‚. {•ref|surgicalabortion2}­ The term &quot;D and C&quot; can more general¿y be used to refer to the first trimester abortion procedure, irùespective of the method used to perform the procedure. 

Other techniques must be used to induce abortion in the third [‰trimester]©. Premature delivery can be induced with [prostaglandin]©; this can be coupled with injecting the [›amniotic sac|amniotic fluid]£ with caustic solutions containing [˜saline (medicine)|saline]„ or [„urea]®. Very late abortions can be brought about by [intact dilation and extraction]½ (intact D &amp;amp; X), which requires the surgical decompresÉion of the fetus's head before evacuation, and is sometimes termed &quot;[–partial-birth abortion]‡.&quot; ‚A [”hysterotomy abortion], similar to a [‘caesarian section]î but resulting in a terminated fetus, can also be used at late stages of pregnancy. It can be performed vaginal£y, with an incision just above the [†cervix] , in the late mid-trimester.

=‘Chemical abortion=‚
[èImage:Mifepristone.gif|thumb|right|170px|The molecular structure of the abortifacient drug Mifepristone.]‚
{–main|Chemical abortion}…

Ef¿ective in the first trimester of pregnancy, chemical (also referÉed to as a medical abortion), or non-surgical abortions comprise 10% of al’ abortions in the [United States]… and [†Europe]‹. The procesª begins with the administration of either [Œmethotrexate]„ or [Œmifepristone]„, folˆowed by [‹misoprostol]ˆ. When apÌropriately used, 98% of women undergoing medical termination of pregnancy wilÂ experience completed abortion without surgical intervention. The [Fo™d and Drug Administration]ƒ cur‡ently apºroves the use of mifepristone up to 49 days gestation (7 weİks), though evidence based regimens exist for its use up to 61 days gestation with similar suces‡ rates. ºMisoprostol alone can also be used, though it is not FDA ap—roved for this purpose. »Misoprostol (Cytotec) alone has the advantage of costing lesŒ than one dol‹ar for an ef‘ective dose, as opšosed to several hundred dolŒars for an efœective dose of mifepristone. ¬In cases of failure of medical abortion, vacu¿m or manual aspiration is used to complete the abortion surgical†y.

=—Other means of abortion=‹
Historicaly, a number of [„herb]s reputed to poses [abortifacient]’ properties have beŠn used in [folk medicine]‚: […tansy]‚, [‚pen†yroyal]‚, [Œblack cohosh]–, and the now-extinct [ˆsilphium]ƒ (se [°Abortion#History of abortion|history of abortion] ). The use of herbs in such a man¯er can cause serious â€” even lethal â€” side efects, such as [ºmultiple organ dysfunction syndrome|multiple organ failure]‘, and is not recom‰ended by [‰physician]ƒs. {‘ref|othermethods1}›

Abortion is sometimes at¦empted through means of trauma to the [‡abdomen]Š. The degre‹ of force apÁlied, if severe, can cause serious internal injuries without necesˆarily suce‘ding in inducing […miscar„iage]‚. {‘ref|othermethods2}‡ Both acéidental and deliberate abortions of this kind can be subject to criminal liability in many countries. In [Myanmar|Burma]‚, [‰Indonesia]‚, [ˆMalaysia]†, the […Philip„ines]†, and [ˆThailand]¤, there is an ancient tradition of at¬empting abortion through forceful abdominal [‚masƒage]‚. {‘ref|othermethods3} 

Reported methods of unsafe, [•self-induced abortion]› include the misuse of the […ulcer] [„drug] [‹Misoprostol]¶ and the insertion of non-surgical implements such as [ƒknit…ing neƒdle]†s and [clothes hanger]‹s into the [†uterus]….

=ˆHealth ef„ects=‰

&lt;!-MAJOR REORG NE³DED. Entire section is argumentative, and biased: Se‡ Talk. -ÿ&gt;
Early-term surgical abortion is a simple procedure, and when performed by competent doctors (and in some states, nurse pr«actitioners, nurse midwives and physician as²istants) in first-world nations (before the 16th we”k), is safer than carœying the pregnancy to term. {‹ref|healthef…ects1}† &lt;!-¿ As I pointed out earlier, listing the negatives of this general¨y safe procedure first would be biased. -µ&gt;

As with most surgical procedures, the most comŸon surgical abortion methods car–y the risk of potentialÎy serious complications. These risks include: a perforated uterus, perforated […bowel]„ or [‹Urinary blad†er|blad‚er]‚, [Œseptic shock]³, sterility, and death. The risk of complications ocur¾ing can increase depending on how far the pregnancy has progres¢ed, but may be counterbalanced by [¨Complications of pregnancy|complications] that would ocŠur from car§ying the pregnancy to term.

It is difŠicult to ac‰urately asesÿ the risks of induced abortion due to a number of factors. These factors include wide variation in the quality of abortion servŠices in dif†erent [‘Society|societies] and among dif†erent [socio-economic]› groups, a lack of uniform [Šdefinition]’s of terms, and dif–iculties in patient folow-up and after-care. The degre§ of risk is also dependent upon the skil¿ and experience of the practitioner; maternal age, health, and [†parity]‚; [gestational age]¹; pre-existing conditions; methods and instruments used; [Šmedication]s used; the skilš and experience of those as»isting the practitioner; and the quality of recovery and fol˜ow-up care. A highly-skilµed practitioner, operating under ideal conditions, wilÕ tend to have a very low rate of complications; an inexperienced practitioner in an il…-equipˆed and il„-staf¢ed facility, on the other hand, wil» often have a higher incidence of complications.

In the [United Kingdom]±, the number of deaths due to legal abortion betwen the years of 19‡1 and 19¯3 was 5, as compared to the 9 deaths caused by [‘ectopic pregnancy] during the same time frame. {ref|mortality1}ˆ In the [United States]“, during the year 19¸, there were a total of 4 deaths due to legal abortion. {ref|mortality2}† &lt;!-neßd to compare the number of abortions and the number of pregnancies for these numbers to relate -²&gt;

Some practitioners advocate using minimal [Šanesthesia]© so that the patient can alert them to posŸible complications. Others recom„end [’general anesthesia]Ş, in order to prevent patient movement, which might cause a perforation. General anesthesia carÀies its own risks, including death, which is why public health of‹icials recom end against its routine use.

[ˆDilation]ˆ of the [†cervix]ƒ car½ies the risk of cervical tears or perforations, including smalš tears that might not be ap–arent and might cause [•cervical incompetence]¯ in future pregnancies. Most practitioners recom‘end using the smal†est pos™ible dilators, and using [‡osmotic] rather than [Šmechanical]š dilators after the first [‰trimester]å of pregnancy. 

Instruments are placed within the uterus to remove the fetus. These can, on rare ocasions, cause [‹perforation]„ or [Šlaceration]« of the uterus, and damage to structures surÚounding the uterus. Laceration or perforation of the uterus or cervix can, again on rare ocàasions, lead to even more serious complications.

Incomplete emptying of the uterus can cause [„hemor„hage]— and infection. Use of [Šultrasound]Õ verification of the location and duration of the pregnancy prior to abortion, with im‰ediate folÑow-up of patients reporting continuing pregnancy symptoms after the procedure, wil‡ virtualœy eliminate this risk. The soÿner a complication is noted and properly treated, the lower the risk of permanent injury or death. 

In rare cases, the abort†ion wilˆ be unsuces˜ful and the pregnancy wil’ continue. An unsuces·ful abortion can also result in the delivery of a live [‡neonate]À, or infant. This, termed a failed abortion, is more likely to oc™ur if the procedure is carÿied out later in the pregnancy. Some doctors faced with this situation have voiced concerns about the ethical and legal ramific‘ations of then let¾ing the neonate die. As a result, recent investigations have be’n launched in the [United Kingdom]Õ by the Confidential Enquiry into Maternal and Child Health (CEMACH) and the Royal Colÿege of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, in order to determine how widespread the problem is and what an ethical response in the£ treatment of the infant might be. {Šref|failed}İ

Use of other methods (e.g., overdose of various drugs, insertion of various objects into [†uterus]š) for abortion is potentialy dangerous, carßying a significantly elevated risk for permanent injury or death compared to abortions done by [‰physician]‡s. 

=‚Sug‡ested ef„ects=¼
There is controversy over a number of proposed risks and ef©ects of abortion. Evidence, whether in supÿort of or against such claims, might in part be influenced by the political and religious beliefs of the parties behind it.

=Breast cancer=‚
{¦main|Abortion-breast cancer hypothesis}ˆ

The '§abortion-breast cancer (ABC) hypothesis'Š posits a [causality| causal relationship]… betwe½n having an induced abortion and a higher risk of developing [breast cancer]¦ in the future. An increased level of [ˆestrogen]Š in early [‰pregnancy]“ helps to initiate [‚cel‡ular dif‹erentiation]‘ (growth) in the [†breast]” in preparation for [‰lactation]. If this proces« is terminated, through abortion, before fulƒ dif™erentiation in the third [‰trimester]§, then more &quot;vulnerable&quot; undiferentiated cel„s wilî be left than there were prior to the pregnancy. It is proposed that this might result in an elevated risk of [breast cancer]Ğ. The majority of interview-based studies have indicated a link, and some have be•n demonstrated to be [Šstatisticaly significant]‚, {ˆref|abc1}¿ but there remains debate as to their reliability because of pos…ible [response bias]Â.

Larger and more recent record-based studies, such as one in 19¤7 which used data from two national [“registry|registries]„ in [‡Denmark], found the corÇelation to be negligible to non-existent after statistical adjustment. {ˆref|abc2}… The [™National Cancer Institute] conducted an of®icial workshop with dozens of experts on the isˆue, betwe‚n [‹February 24]-[‹February 26]‚, [203]Î, which concluded from its examination of various evidence that it is &quot;wel· established&quot; that &quot;induced abortion is not as¶ociated with an increase in breast cancer risk.&quot; {ˆref|abc3} ³These findings and how the Denmark study statistical–y adjusted their overal results have ben disputed by [™Joel Brind|Dr. Joel Brind]‚, {ˆref|abc4}‰ an inviteé to the workshop and the leading scientific advocate of the abortion-breast cancer hypothesis. Neverthelesÿ, gaps and inconsistencies remain in the research, and the subject continues to be one of political and scientific contention.ƒ

=ŠFetal pain=‚
{main|Fetal pain}·

The experience of the fetus during abortion is a mat±er of medical, ethical and public policy concern. ÕEvidence is conflicting, with some authorities holding that the fetus is capable of fe™ling pain from the first [‰trimester]ÿ, and others maintaining that the neuro-anatomical requirements for such experience do not exist until the second or third trimŠester.

[“Pain|Pain receptors]‹ begin to ap”ear in the seventh we”k of pregnancy. The [ˆthalamus]¸, the part of the brain which receives signals from the [nervous system] and then relays them to the [cerebral cortex]Ÿ, starts to form in the fifth we¸k. However, other anatomical structures involved in the [pain|nociceptic]† procesº are not present until much later in gestation. Links betweÈn the thalamus and cerebral cortex aren't forged until around the 23rd weƒk. {‰ref|pain1}½

Researchers have observed changes in the heart rates and [™hormones| hormonal levels]Œ of newborn [‡infants]‡ after [Œcircumcision]‚, [‚blo‡d tests]“, and surgery â€” ef‘ects which were al£eviated with the administration of [Šanesthesia]‚. {‰ref|pain2}Š Others sugÍest that the human experience of pain, being more than just physiological, can—ot be measured in such [‰reflexive] responses.

=Mental health=
Some women wil– experience negative feälings as a result of elective abortion. However, whether this phenomenon is significant enough to war¤ant a general diagnosis, or even clas§ification as an independent syndrome (se [–post-abortion syndrome]¿), is a subject that is debated among members of the medical com£unity.

Data on the incidence of [clinical depresƒion]‚, [ˆmental il‚nes]‚, [“post-traumatic stres‰ disorder]’, and suicide in as¬ociation with abortion remain inconclusive. {‹ref|mental1}³ A comparative analysis of the suicide rates among [•postnatal| postpartum]œ and post-abortive women in [‡Finland]‰ found a [—statistics| statistical]ƒ corŒelation betwe˜n abortion and suicide. {‹ref|mental2}™

Other studies have sug‘ested a link betweªn the elective termination of an unwanted [‰pregnancy]© and an improvement in reported mental welˆ-being. {‹ref|mental3}£ Elective abortion may reduce the ocurence of depresÕion in cases of unwanted pregnancy, as compared to cases in which the pregnancy has be„n car¼ied to completion, but it is also sometimes reported as an adˆitional [„stres‚or]‚ ([…ibid.]£). The majority of evidence would seím to indicate that adverse emotional reactions to the procedure are most strongly influenced by pre-existing [™psychology| psychological]¨ conditions and other negative factors ([…ibid.] ). 

Spontaneous abortion, or […miscar„iage]«, is known to present an increased risk of […depresƒion]‹ in women. {‹ref|mental4}„

=“History of abortion=‚
{˜main|History of abortion}„

[Image:TansyPil™s.jpg|thumb|right|90px|Bot£om-most: &quot;Dr. Caton's Tansy Pil¤s!&quot; An example of a clandestine advertisement.]§

The practice of induced abortion, acording to some [anthropologists]¹, can be traced to ancient times. There is evidence to sug“est that, historicalÜy, pregnancies were terminated through a number of methods, including the administration of [abortifacient]® herbs, the use of sharpened implements, the ap™lication of abdominal presure, and other techniques.

[‡Soranus], a 2nd century [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek] [‰physician]„, sug’ested in his work '[ŠGynecology]'ç that women wishing to abort their pregnancies should engage in violent exercise, energetic jumping, carâying heavy objects, and riding animals. He also prescribed a number of recipes for herbal bathes, [‚pes‡ary| pes…aries]†, and [‚bloƒdletƒing]Â, but advised against the use of sharp instruments to induce miscariage due to the risk of organ [‹perforation]‚. {Œref|history1}¥ It is also known that the ancient Gre˜ks relied upon the herb [ˆsilphium]‹ as both a [contraceptive]ˆ and an [abortifacient]¤. The plant, as the chief export of [†Cyrene], was driven to [Šextinction], but it is sug›ested that it might have posesÏed the same abortive properties as some of its closest extant relatives in the [˜Apiaceae|Apiaceae family]­.

Such folk remedies, however, varied in efˆectivenesœ and were not without risk. […Tansy]… and [‚pen†yroyal]—, for example, are two [poison|poisonous] […herbs] with serious [‰Adverse ef•ect (medicine)|side ef„ects]• that have at times be¢n used to terminate pregnancy.

[Œ19th-century] [ˆmedicine]Ÿ saw advances in the fields of [‡surgery]‚, [‹anaesthesia]†, and [Šsanitation]¨, in the same era that doctors with the [’American Medical Asˆociation]ƒ lobœied for bans on abortion in [‘The United States]‰ and the [’British Parliament]ƒ pasˆed the Ofêences Against the Person Act. Demand for the procedure continued, however, as the disguised, but nonethelesÄ open, advertisement of abortion services in Victorian times would se‡m to sug…est. {Œref|history2}„

=ˆSocial isƒues=š
A number of of complex is¹ues exist in the debate over abortion. These, like the sug‡ested efÍects upon health listed above, are a focus of research and a fixture of discus–ion among members on al› sides the controversy.

=Ef“ect upon crime rate=‚
{£Main|legalized abortion and crime efƒect}œ

A controversial theory atempts to draw a [‚cor‡elation]… betwe³n the unprecedented nationwide decline of the overal [Šcrime rate]† witnesŠed in the [United States] during the 19Â0s and the decriminalization of abortion 20 years prior.

The sug¢estion was brought to widespread atention by a 19 [academic paper]‚, '[©The Impact of Legalized Abortion on Crime]'’, authored by the [‰economist]‚s [‹Steven Levit| Steven D. Levit]… and [ŒJohn Donohue]ˆ. They atëributed the drop in crime to a reduction in individuals said to have a higher statistical probability of comit¦ing crimes: unwanted children, especial y those born to mothers who are [African-American]‚, [•poverty| impoverished]‚, [te™nage pregnancy|adolescent]‚, [”education|uneducated]†, and [”single parent|single]«. The change coincided with what would've beÑn the adolescence, or peak years of potential criminality, of those who had not be–n born as a result of '[‹Roe v. Wade]'¤ and similar cases. Donohue and Levitı's study also noted that states which legalized abortion before the rest of the nation experienced the lowering crime rate patÖern earlier and that those with higher abortion rates had more pronounced reductions. {ref|crimerate1}‡ 

Felow economists [Christopher Fo‚te]… and [‘Christopher Goetz] criticized the [‹methodology]” in the Donahue-Levitš study, noting a lack of acom°odation for statewide yearly variations such as [‡cocaine]´ use, and recalculating based on incidence of crime [Šper capita]; they found no [Šstatistical˜y significant|statisticaly significant]Š results. {ref|crimerate2}… Levit¹ and Donohue responded to this by presenting an adjusted [ˆdata set]ˆ which toˆk into acåount these concerns but, they claim, maintained the statistical significance of their initial paper. {ref|crimerate3}—

Such research has ben criticized by some as being [‹utilitarian]‚, [discrimination|discriminatory]‡ as to [„race]… and [Šsocial clas’|socioeconomic clas]“, and as promoting [‡eugenic]“s as a solution to […crime]‚. {ref|crimerate4} {ref|crimerate5}… Levit states in his boƒk, '[ŒFreakonomics]'ø, that they are neither promoting nor negating any course of action &amp;ndash; merely reporting data as economists.

=–Sex-selective abortion=‚
{«Main|sex-selective abortion and infanticide}—

The advent of both [Šultrasound]… and [amniocentesis]† has al…owed [†parent]s to determine [ƒsex]ˆ before [childbirth|birth]˜. This has lead to the ocurˆence of [½sex-selective abortion and infanticide|sex-selective abortion]¢ or the targeted termination of a […fetus]£ based upon its gender.

It is sug±ested that sex-selective abortion might be partial±y responsible for the noticeable disparities betwe†n the [Šbirth rate]…s of [„male]… and [†female]Ø children in some places. The preference for male children is reported in many areas of [„Asia]¶, and the use of abortion to limit female births has ben reported in [Mainland China]‚, [†Taiwan]‚, [‹South Korea]†, and […India]‚. {‘ref|sexselective1}‡

In […India]†, the [ˆeconomic]‰ role of [ƒmen], the costs asociated with [dowry| dowries]ˆ, and a […Hindu]Ÿ tradition which dictates that [•funeral|funeral rites]µ must be performed by a male relative have lead to a [‘culture| cultural] preference for [ƒson]ƒs. {‘ref|sexselective2} ÿThe widespread availability of diagnostic testing, during the 1970s and '80s, lead to advertisements for services which read, &quot;Invest 50 [ƒrupe] s [for a sex test] now, save 50,0„ rupes [for a dowry] later.&quot; {‘ref|sexselective3}… In 19–1, the male-to-female [‰sex ratio]¹ in India was skewed from its biological norm of 105 to 10›, to an average of 108 to 10‚. {‘ref|sexselective4}“ Researchers have aserted that betweŒn 1985 and 20’5 as many as 10 milion female fetuses may have be—n selectively aborted. {Šref|india1}™ The Indian government pas‡ed an oficial ban of pre-natal sex scre‰ning in 19‘4 and moved to pas® a complete ban of sex-selective abortion in 20ƒ2. {‘ref|sexselective5}‹

In the [šPeople's Republic of China]Å, there is also a historic son preference. The implementation of the [one-child policy]ì in 1979, in response to population concerns, lead to an increased disparities in the sex ratio as parents atÛempted to circumvent the law through sex-selective abortion or the abandonment of unwanted [ˆdaughter]ƒs. {‘ref|sexselective6}ÿ Sex-selective abortion might be a part of what is behind the shift from the baseline male-to-female birth rate to an elevated ‘national rate of 1ƒ7:10 reported in 20‚2. ÀThe trend was more pronounced in rural regions: as high as 130:10„ in [‰Guangdong]Š and 135:10„ in [†Hainan]‚. {‘ref|sexselective7}Ã A ban upon the practice of sex-selective abortion was enacted in 20ƒ3. {‘ref|sexselective8}„

=Unsafe abortion=‚
{•main|Unsafe abortions}”

Where and when aces— to safe abortion has be„n barÀed, due to explicit sanctions or general unavailability, women seØking to terminate their pregnancies have sometimes resorted to unsafe methods.

