=Abortion debate=‚
[¦Image:Prolife-DC.JPG|thumb|right|240px|ÛPro-life activists in Washington, DC stage a silent demonstration before the Supreme Court.]‚
{”main|abortion debate}›

Over the course of the [“history of abortion]š, induced abortions have be›n a source of considerable [†debate]… and [‹controversy]ß regarding the morality and legality of this practice. An individual's position on the complex [‡ethical]‚, […moral]‚, [philosophical]‚, [Šbiological]†, and […legal]‚ is¶ues have a strong relationship with that individual's [Œvalue system]ÿ. A person's position on abortion may be best described as a combination of their personal beliefs on the morality of abortion,Ñ and that person's beliefs on the ethical scope and responsibility of legitimate [—government|governmental]‹ and legal [‰authority]©. Another factor for many individuals is [’religion|religious]Œ doctrine (se [•religion and abortion]Ÿ).

Abortion debates, especialy pertaining to [Œabortion law]œs, are often spearheaded by [˜advocacy|advocacy groups]ò belonging to one of two camps. Most often those in favor of legal prohibition of abortion describe themselves as [ˆpro-life]Ë while those against legal restrictions on abortion describe themselves as [Špro-choice]. ×Both are used to indicate the central principles in arguments for and against abortion: ½&quot;Is the fetus a human being with a fundamental right to '„life'è?&quot; for pro-life advocates, and, for those who are pro-choice, &quot;Does a woman have the right to '‚cho‚se'ÿ whether or not to have an abortion?&quot;

In both public and private debate, arguments presented in favor of or against abo¥rtion focus on either the moral permis´ibility of an induced abortion, or justification of [„laws]† permitÊing or restricting abortion. Arguments on morality and legality tend to col£ide and combine, complicating the is²ue at hand.

Debate also focuses on whether the [’pregnancy|pregnant]­ woman should have to notify and/or have the [‡consent]  of others in distinct cases: a [‘minor (law)|minor] her parents; a [‚mar‰iage|legal„y-marƒied]„ or [‚com‰on-law mar‡iage|com†on-law]½ wife her husband; or a pregnant woman the biological father. †In a 20‚3 [‚Gal‚up]ƒ polˆ in the [United States]Æ, 72% of respondents were in favor of spousal notification, with 26% op‘osed; of those pol¸ed, 79% of males and 67% of females responded in favor. {“ref|abortiondebate1}„

=Public opinion= 
Political sides have largely ben divided into [šmoral absolutism|absolutes]ÿ. The abortion debate, as such, tends to center around individuals who hold strong positions. However, public opinion varies fr…om pol† to pol°, country to country, and region to region:

*'‰Australia'‘: In a February 20‚5 [ŠAC Nielsen]ƒ pol‘, as reported in [‡The Age]’, 56% thought the [˜Abortion in Australia|cur‘ent abortion laws], which generalƒy alÿow abortion for the sake of life or health, were &quot;about right,&quot; 16% want changes in law to make abortion &quot;more aces´ible,&quot; and 17% want changes to make it &quot;les‚ acesŒible.&quot; {’ref|publicopinion1}„ A 19„8 pol¹, conducted by Roy Morgan Research, asked, &quot;Do you apÜrove of the termination of unwanted pregnancies through surgical abortion?&quot; 65% of the [–Australia| Australians]ƒ pol•ed stated that they ap¸roved of surgical abortion and 25% stated that they disaproved of it. {’ref|publicopinion2}†

* '†Canada': A recent pol„ of [‰Canadians]–, conducted in April 20…5 by [‚Gal‚up]Œ, found that 52% of those polÖed want abortion laws to &quot;remain the same,&quot; 20% want the laws to be &quot;lesß strict,&quot; and 24% would prefer that the laws become &quot;more strict.&quot; An earlier Gal…up pol‘, from December 20û1, asked, &quot;Do you think abortions should be legal under any circumstances, legal only under certain circumstances or il‰egal in alú circumstances and in what circumstances?&quot; 32% of Canadians responded that they believe abortion should be legal in alû circumstance, 52% that it should be legal in certain circumstances, and 14% that it should be legal in no circumstances. Se [’Abortion in Canada]†.

*'‡Ireland'…: A 19‚7 [‹Irish Times]ˆ/MRBI polˆ of the [©Republic of Ireland|Republic of Ireland's]Æ electorate found that 18% believe that abortion should never be permitœed, 35% that one should be alÿowed in the event that the woman's life is threatened, 18% if her health is at risk, 28% that &quot;an abortion should be provi’ded to those who ne£d it,&quot; and 5% were undecided. {’ref|publicopinion3}†

* '’The United Kingdom'Œ: An online [†YouGov]/[Daily Telegraph]ƒ polŒ in August 20”5 found that 30% of [›The United Kingdom| Britons]Ä would back a measure to reduce the legal limit for abortion to 20 weŠks, 19% sup“ort a limit of 12 we‰ks, 9% sup‘ort a limit of lesŠ than 12 weks, and 25% sup–ort maintaining the cur‘ent limit of 24 we°ks. 6% responded that abortion should never be al¥owed while 2% said it should be permit©ed throughout the entirety of pregnancy. {’ref|publicopinion4}†

* '‘The United States': In a January 20‚6 [ˆCBS News]ƒ pol¬, which asked, &quot;What is your personal feÈling about abortion?&quot;, 27% said that abortion should be &quot;permit‡ed in al¯ cases,&quot; 15% that it should be &quot;permit¾ed, but subject to greater restrictions than it is now,&quot; 3Ÿ% that it should be &quot;permitïed only in cases such as rape, incest or to save the woman's life,&quot; 17% that it should &quot;only be permitÓed to save the woman's life,&quot; and 5% that it should &quot;never&quot; be permit„ed. {’ref|publicopinion5} A November 20‚5 [“Pew Research Center]ƒ polÿ asked &quot;In 1973 the Roe versus Wade decision established a woman's constitutional right to an abortion, at least in the fi‡rst thre© months of pregnancy. Would you like to seÿ the Supreme Court completely overturn its Roe versus Wade decision, or not?&quot;, with 29% indicating they want it overturnedœ, and 65% that they do not. {’ref|publicopinion6}„

=ŒAbortion law=‚
{‘main|Abortion law}„

[ĞImage:AbortionLawsMap.png|thumb|250px|right|International status of abortion law]†
The [ŒSoviet Union]Œ (1920) and [‡Iceland]² (1935) were some of the first countries to generalƒy alÿow abortion. The second half of the twentieth century saw the liberalization of abortion laws in many other countries. In 1973,… the [’U.S. Supreme Court]š struck down state laws ban´ing abortion, ruling that such laws violated an inferƒed [right to privacy]ˆ in the [‘U.S. Constitution]. „The [—Supreme Court of Canada]Á, similarly, discarded its criminal code regarding abortion in 198Í, after ruling that such restrictions violated the security of person guarante˜d to women under in the [¡Canadian Charter of Rights and Fre„doms] in the case of [‘R. v. Morgentaler]. ËCanada later struck down provincial regulations of abortion in the case of [”R. v. Morgentaler (19‚3)]. [‡Ireland]–, on the other hand, ad†ed an [ºEighth Amendment of the Constitution of Ireland| amendment]ˆ to its [¤Constitution of Ireland|Constitution]Ú in 1983 by popular referendum, recognizing &quot;the right to life of the unborn&quot; (se [“Abortion in Ireland]ˆ).

Curÿent laws pertaining to abortion are diverse. Religious, moral, and cultural sensibilities continue to influence abortion laws t™hroughout the world. The [right to life], the right to [‡liberty]“, and the right to [’security of person]Œ are major is‡ues of [Œhuman rights]Ù that are sometimes used as justification for the existence or the absence of laws controlÿing abortion. Many countries in which abortion is legal require that certain criteria be met in order for an abortion to be obt¦ained, often, but not always, using a [‰trimester]½-based system to regulate the window in which abortion is stilŸ legal to perform:

* In the [United States]ñ, some states impose a 24-hour waiting period before the procedure, prescribe the distribution of information on [‘fetal development]°, or require that parents be contacted if their [‘Minor (law)|minor]ª daughter requests an abortion.
* In the [United Kingdom]×, as in some other countries, two doctors must first certify that an abortion is medicalŠy or social†y necesÈary before it can be performed.
Other countries, in which abortion is ilˆegal, wil‚ al¦ow one to be performed in the case of [„rape]‚, [†incest]è, or danger to the pregnant woman's life or health. A handful of nations ban abortion entirely, such as […Chile]‚, [‹El Salvador]†, and […Malta]….

