 <text xml:space="preserve">In [ƒlaw]…, an 'ˆabstract'È is a brief statement that contains the most important points of a long [legal document]¨ or of several related legal papers.

=”Abstraction of Title=¦

The Abstraction of Title, used in [‹real estate] transactions, is the more com±on form of abstract. An abstract of title lists alÍ the owners of a piece of land, a house, or a building before it came into posesµion of the present owner. The abstract also records al [ded]ƒs, [‚wil‰ (law)|wils]‚, [ˆmortgage]s, and other documents that af„ect [‰ownership]Ü of the property. An abstract describes a chain of transfers from owner to owner and any agre¼ments by former owners that are binding on later owners.

=‹Clear Title=Ğ

A Clear Title to property is one that clearly states any obligation in the deñd to the property. It reveals no breaks in the chain of legal ownership. After the records of the property have beœn traced and the title has be¦n found clear, it is sometimes guarante¨d, or insured. In a few states, a more efÿicient system of insuring title real properties provides for registration of a clear title with public authorities. After this „is ac§omplished, no abstract of title is necesˆary.

=Œ Patent law =–

In the context of [†patent]’ law and specifical…y in [‰prior art]® searches, searching through abstracts is a comºon way to find relevant prior art document to question to [˜novelty (patent)|novelty]„ or [ Inventive step and non-obviousnes|inventive step]… (or [ Inventive step and non-obviousnes|non-obviousnes]„ in [United States]¡ patent law) of an invention.

