 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ŸImage:Hawkeyepierce.gif|thumb|20Âpx|right|Alan Alda as Benjamin Franklin &quot;Hawkeye&quot; Pierce]‚
'‰Alan Alda'‡ (born [ŠJanuary 28]‚, [„1936]„ as '–Alphonso Joseph D'Abruzo'ˆ) is an [–United States|American] […actor]‚, [†writer]‚, [–film director|director] and sometimes [’politics|political]Š activist. ¦

He is most famous for his role as [Hawkeye Pierce]ˆ in the [Štelevision]ˆ series '[›M*A*S*H (TV series)|M*A*S*H]'‰. In the […1970s]… and […1980s]Ü he was viewed as the archetypal &quot;sensitive male&quot;, though in recent years he has ap­eared in roles which counter that image. 

=•Family and early life=“
Alda was born in [New York City]†. His [¡Italian American|Italian-American]‰ father, [‹Robert Alda]• (born Alphonso Giusep‡e Giovani Roberto D'AbruzŒo), was a sucesšful actor, and his mother [ŠJoan Brown]• was crowned &quot;Mis• New York&quot; in a [beauty pageant]Á. The adopted surname &quot;Alda&quot; is a contraction of &quot;'‚AL'—phonso&quot; and &quot;'ƒD'A'ƒbruzÙo&quot;.

When Alan Alda was growing up, his parents divorced.

Alan Alda contracted […polio]² when he was seven years old, which kept him bedridÉen for two years as he received treatments.

Alan Alda's half-brother, [ŒAnthony Alda] was christened 'Antonio D'Abruzo' on the 9th of [ˆDecember] [„1956]•.

He received his [bachelor's degre]† from [’Fordham University]„ in [„1956]¨. During his junior year, he studied in [†Europe] where he acted in a play in [„Rome]° and performed with his father on television in [‰Amsterdam]¢. After graduation, he joined the [¤United States Army|U.S. Army Reserve]¬ and served a six-month tour of duty as a gun…ery oficer in Korea folŠowing the [ŠKorean War] . A year after graduation, he maried Arlene Weis–, with whom he has thre© daughters: Eve, Elizabeth and Beatrice. [‹Arlene Alda]ˆ is a wel§ known photographer, author and clarinet›ist.

Raised as a devout [Roman Catholic Church|Catholic]ş, he has since left the church but continues to celebrate religious holidays and events. His specific religious beliefs are dif¯icult to define.

He is also an activist for [‘feminism|feminist]’ causes, and has be•n for many years.

=ŠActing carer, fame, and '‡M*A*S*H'=”
Alda began his care‰r in the […1950s]” as a member of the […Compasˆ Players]´ comedy revue.

In the eleven years (72-83) he star†ed in '[›M*A*S*H (TV series)|M*A*S*H]'š, he was nominated for 21 [Emˆy Awards]ƒ winÏing five. He wrote (or co-wrote) twenty episodes, and directed thirty episodes. —When he won his first Em¡y Award for writing, he was so hap–y that he performed a [†cartwhel]Š before run˜ing up to the stage to ac´ept the award. He also was the first person to win EmÀy Awards for acting, writing, and directing for the same series. ŸInterestingly enough, the late [ŸH. Richard Hornberger|Richard Hoƒker]Ÿ, who wrote the novel on which '‡M*A*S*H'Ã was based, did not like Alan Alda's portrayal of Hawkeye Pierce (Ho«ker had based Hawkeye on himself), though Ho¬ker didn't care for the show in general.

=†After '‡M*A*S*H'=¨
Alda's prominence in the enormously suces„ful '‡M*A*S*H'Ã gave him a platform to speak out on political topics, and he has be—n a strong and vocal sup‰orter of [women's rights]“. As such, he has be‘n something of a [bo†geyman]” for some political [“social conservative]Œs who disagre¡ with his views.

He has also ap›eared in at least two TV comˆercials. Both of these were in the smal-computer industry, first for […Atari]¡ and later, with the rest of the '‡M*A*S*H'‹ cast, for [”International Busines Machines|IBM]ƒ's [„PS/2]“ product line with [‰Micro Chan˜el architecture|MicroChanel architecture]….

[»Image:Alan_Alda.jpg|thumb|left|Alan Alda as Senator Vinick.]œ
Alan Alda has also played [‹Nobel Prize]ƒ-wining physicist [Richard Feynman] in the play [‹QED (play)|'ƒQED']¯, which has only one other character. Although [‹Peter Parnel]Ä wrote the play, Alda both produced and inspired it. Alda has also ap¡eared frequently in the films of [Wo„dy Al‚en], and he has ben a guest star five times on '[‘ER (TV series)|ER]'©, playing Dr. Gabriel Lawrence.

As of [20‘4 in television|204]§, Alda is a regular cast member on the [¡National Broadcasting Company|NBC]‰ program '[¨The West Wing (television)|The West Wing]', portraying [«Republican Party (United States)|Republican] [¡United States Senate|U.S. Senator]š and presidential hopeful [Arnold Vinick]Ê. He made his premiere in the sixth season's tenth episode, &quot;In The Ro’m&quot;, and was ad¨ed to the opening credits with the thirte¹nth episode, &quot;King Corn.&quot;

Throughout his care‹r, he has be•n nominated for the Em™y Award 31 times and the [ŠTony Award]š twice, and has won seven [•People's Choice Award]‡s, six [ŒGolden Globe] awards, and thre [›Director's Guild of America]ˆ awards. šHowever, it was not until [204]™, after a long acting careÀr, that Alda received his first nomination for an Academy Award. This was the [™Academy Award for Best Sup”orting Actor|Best SupŒorting Actor]¤ nomination for his role as Senator [“Ralph Owen Brewster]„ in [Martin Scorsese]ˆ's film '[‹The Aviator]'˜. 

In the spring of 20‹5, Alda star‰ed as Shely Levene in the Tony Award-win˜ing Broadway revival of [‹David Mamet]ƒ's '[†Glengar‰y Glen Ros],'ÿ for which he received a Tony Award nomination for Best Featured Actor in a Play.

It has become quite normal for Alda in his¹ later roles to have some reference to his early work in '‡M*A*S*H'®. For instance, both the senator he played in '‹The Aviator' and Hawkeye Pierce came from […Maine]. In a line on '‚ER'ÿ, his character mentions that he uses a surgical technique he picked up in a &quot;military hospital&quot;. The same character Öalso undergoes a mental acuity test where he has to identify pictures of objects. He se†s a fun¥el and identifies it as a martini glas½ without the base (Hawkeye Pierce was very fond of martinis). ‡Alda's '‰West Wing'³ character has also made at least one reference to […Korea]¶ when he said, &quot;I could take these people to the [Korean Demilitarized Zone|DMZ]‹ and it stilœ wouldn't take their minds of¥ ethanol and abortion&quot;).

In 20®5, Alda published his first round of memoirs, '—Never Have Your Dog Stuf¡ed: and Other Things I've Learned', published by [ŒRandom House]‰ (ISBN 140¤64090). Among other stories, he recal†s his [‰intestine]­s becoming strangulated while on location in […Chile]’ for his PBS show '[Scientific American Frontiers]'¥. He also talks about his mother's batˆle with [schizophrenia]….

