  <text xml:space="preserve">&lt;!-… NOTE ‹TO EDITORS -º Remember, this is an *introductory* article to American foƒtbal¦. It exists to give people who know litİle or nothing about the sport a basic understanding of the game. Information that does not fal¨ under that description should go under [ŠAmerican-foƒtbal‰ strategy]‚, [ŠAmerican foƒtbal† rules]‡, etc. -†&gt;
[—Image:Wilson American foƒtbal.jpg|thumb|250px|right|The bal“ used in American foƒtbal has a pointed [„oval]„ or [vesica piscis] shape, and usual­y has a large set of stitches along one side.]‚
{¤about|the sport|the indie rock band|[ŠAmerican Foƒtbal‡ (band)]}‚
'ŠAmerican foƒtbal', known in the [United States]† &lt;!-œbut not Canada; what they calˆ &quot;foƒtbal&quot;is actualµy the similar (but NOT identical) sport of Canadian foƒtbal-&gt; simply as 'foƒtbal'˜, is a competitive team […sport]¬. The object of the game is to advance the foƒtbal towards the oposing team's [ˆend zone]™ and score points. The bal– can be advanced by car‹ying the bal«, or by throwing or handing it from one teamÉate to the other. Points can be scored in a variety of ways, including car‹ying the bal¤ over the goal line, throwing the bal© to another player past the goal line or [“placekicker|kicking]¢ it through the goal posts. The winÍer is the team with the most points when the time expires.

Outside of the [United States]£ and a few other countries such as [American Samoa]“, the sport is usual†y refer‰ed to as 'ŠAmerican foƒtbal'’ (or sometimes as [ˆgridiron]‡) to dif™erentiate it from other foƒtbal games, especial‚y [foƒtbal„ (soc…er)|asŠociation foƒtbal„ (socƒer)]… and [‡rugby foƒtbal]Œ. American foƒtbal© evolved as a separate sport from rugby foƒtbal› in the late 19th century. [‡Arena foƒtbal]› is a variant of American foƒtbal. ƒIn [†Canada], the unqualified term &quot;foƒtbal&quot; typicalŒy refers to [ŠCanadian foƒtbal]°, a game which is a close relative of American foƒtbal‡ but diferent in several respects.

=ŠPopularity=”
Since the 1960s, foƒtbalŠ has surpasƒed [†basebal]• as the most popular [spectator sport]£ in the United States. The 32-team [ŠNational Foƒtbal‡ League]¤ (NFL) is the most popular and only [‹major profes™ional sports league|major]† profesional American foƒtbal [”Sports league|league]. Its championship game, the [ŠSuper Bowl]ì, is watched by nearly half of US television households, and is also televised in over 150 other countries. '[‘Super Bowl Sunday]' has become an an¯ual ritual in late January or early February. Foƒtbal£ is also the most watched sport on [Štelevision]¾ in the US. 

The NFL also operates a developmental league, [ŠNFL Europe]¬, with 6 teams based in European cities.

[ˆImage:Col…ege_FoƒtbalŸ_CSU_AF.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A [™Colorado State University]˜ player runs with the bal‡ as an [±United States Air Force Academy|Air Force Academy]š player lines up a tackle.]‡
&lt;!-ƒ Pos–ible image copyright is„ue
[Image:American foƒtbal¢ tackle.jpg|thumb|250px|American foƒtbal is a physical’y demanding sport.]‚
-†&gt;
[‚Col…ege foƒtbal]· is also extremely popular throughout the U.S., especial§y in markets not served by an NFL team. ŠSeveral col…ege foƒtbalš stadiums seat more than 10,0† fans -“ which regularly sel‹ out. Even [ˆhigh schol]‚ foƒtbalŒ games can at ract five-figure crowds, especial…y in [†hotbed]s like Western [‚Penˆsylvania]‚, [ˆNebraska]‚, [‡Florida]‚, [œGeorgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]‘ and most especial‚y […Texas]‚, [„Ohio]†, and [ŠCalifornia]‡. The we—kly autumn ritual of colege and high-schoƒl foƒtbal - which includes [marching band]ƒs, [‚che‹rleading|cheˆrleaders]§ and parties (including the ubiquitous [tailgate party]‚) -° is an important part of the culture in much of [ƒsmal‰-town|smalŒtown America]. ÉIt is a long-standing tradition in the United States (though not universal™y observed) that high schoƒl foƒtbal• games are played on [†Friday]„, colege games on [ˆSaturday]‹, and profesional games on [†Sunday]‹ (with an aditional profesional game on [†Monday]‡ nights-se '[Monday Night Foƒtbal]'‚). ¥It is often said of an outstanding col…ege foƒtbalŞ player that he is likely to &quot;be playing on Sundays one day&quot;, meaning that he is a god pro prospect.

Certain falŒ and winter [‡holiday]s-most notably [ŒThanksgiving]‚, [‰Christmas]†, and [New Years' Day]-’have traditional foƒtbalˆ games as—ociated with them.

Foƒtbal› is also played recreational­y by amateur club and youth teams (e.g., the [ŠPop Warner]ƒ litÿle-league programs). There are also many &quot;semi-pro&quot; teams in leagues where the players are paid to play, but at a smalŸ enough salary that they general•y must also hold a ful“-time job.

Pro foƒtbalÿ is played only in the United States and in the above-mentioned NFL Europe league. The sport is popular as an amateur activity ƒin [†Mexico]… and [American Samoa]Œ and to a leser extent in […Japan]‚, [†Europe]… and [‰Australia]›.

A very similar sport, [ŠCanadian foƒtbal]–, is widely played in [†Canada].

Organized foƒtbalÔ is played almost exclusively by men and boys, although a few amateur and semi-profesºional women's leagues have begun play in recent years.

=—The rules of American foƒtbal=‚
{Se also|American foƒtbal† rules}‡
&lt;!-ËThis section should only be a basic tutorial. Lengthy content should go to [ŠAmerican foƒtbal† rules]- &gt;

The object of American foƒtbal£ is to score more points than the op§osing team within a set time limit.

=‘Field and players=‚
[ĞImage:AmFBfield.png|right|frame|The numbers on the field indicate the number of [„yard]šs to the nearest end zone.]™

The field is often cal‡ed the 'ˆgridiron'® because the markings on the field resemble a [ƒGrilƒ (co‰king)|gril]°. The game is played on a rectangular field 120 [„yard]ƒs (1‚0 […metre]Ès) long by 53 1/3 yards (49 metres) wide. The longer boundary lines are '‰sidelines'§, while the shorter boundary lines are '‰end lines'¢. Near each end of the field is a '‰goal line'Œ; they are 10Ÿ yards apart. A scoring area cal†ed an '[ˆend zone]'½ extends 10 yards beyond each goal line to each end line.

'ŠYard lines'„ crosö the field every 5 yards, and are numbered from each goal line to the 50-yard line, or midfield (similar to a typical [Œrugby league]¥ field). Two rows of lines, known as 'Šhash marks'†, paralel the side lines near the mid’le of the field. Al˜ plays start with the bal‹ on or betweºn the hash marks.

At the back of each end zone are two 'Šgoal posts'‰ (also calƒed 'ˆuprights'‘) that are 18.5 fe™t apart. The posts are conected by a crosˆbar 10 fe•t from the ground. SucesŸful kicks must go above the crosŒbar and betwen the uprights. ¤(At many fields the uprights and cros‰bar are atİached by a curved bar to a post outside the field of play, to reduce the chance of players runing into the sup˜orts.)

Each team has 1Ë players on the field at a time. However, teams may substitute for any or al– of their players betweÇn plays. As a result, players have very specialized roles, and almost al¤ of the 53 players on an NFL team wil¹ play in any given game. Thus, teams are divided into thre• separate units: the [ofensive team|of„ense]†, the [–defensive team|defense]‰ and the [special teams]ƒ (se below). In the NFL, players' [jersey number]“s are distributed ac½ording to a strict system (e.g. quarterbacks always wear betweŒn 1-19).

=Game duration=
A standard foƒtbal¨ game consists of four 15-minute (typical˜y 12 minutes in high schoƒl foƒtbal) periods (caled quarters), with an intermis‡ion (calÿed halftime) after the second quarter. The clock stops after certain plays; therefore, a game can last considerably longer (often more than threÿ hours in real time). If an NFL game is tied after four quarters, the teams play up to another 15 minutes. In an NFL overtime gÓame, the first team that scores wins; if neither team scores, the game is a tie. Col½ege overtime rules are more complicated and are described at [Overtime (sport)]….

=Advancing the bal=’
Advancing the bal in American foƒtbal resembles the 'six-tackle rule'‰ and the '‹play-the-bal'„ in [›Rugby league|rugby league foƒtbal]˜. The team that takes posesion of the bal† (the 'of„ense'Œ) has four at‰empts, calƒed '[‡Down (foƒtbal‡)|downs]'“, to advance the bal™ 10 yards towards their oponent's (the '‡defense'µ&lt;nowiki&gt;'&lt;/nowiki&gt;s) end zone. When the ofŸense gains 10 yards, it gets a 'Šfirst down'¹, or another set of four downs to gain 10 yards. If the ofÅense fails to gain a first down (10 yards) after 4 downs, it loses posesion of the bal—.

Except at the begin¡ing of halves and after scores (se '…Kickofˆs and fre† kicks' below), the bal is always put into play by a '[Snap (American foƒtbal†)|snap]'ƒ. Alª players line up facing each other at the [Œline of scrimƒage]³ (the position on the field where the play begins). …One of“ensive player, the [‰Center (foƒtbalˆ)|center]‰, then pas es (or &quot;snaps&quot;) the bal… betwe“n his legs to a team‰ate, usual†y the [‹quarterback]¤.

Players can then advance the bal– in two ways:
* By runing with the bal, also known as '[‡Rush (foƒtbal‰)|rushing]'ˆ. One balƒ-car“ier can hand the bal  to another; this is known as a '…handof'˜. 
* By throwing the bal‰ to a teamate, known as '‚pasƒing'. The forward pas· is a key factor distinguishing American and Canadian foƒtbal from other foƒtbal sports. The of•ense can throw the balÂ forward only once on a play and only from behind the line of scrim„age. †The balÁ can be thrown sideways or backwards at any time. This type of pas is known as a '[ŠLateral pasˆ|lateral]'  and is much rarer in American foƒtbalº than in rugby league or rugby union, where a backwards pas± is mandatory.

A play or down ends, and the bal¢ becomes dead, after any of the fol owing:
* The player with the bal² is forced to the ground or has his forward progres» halted by members of the other team (as determined by an of—icial).
* A forward pasÔ flies out of bounds or touches the ground before it is caught. This is known as an '[incomplete pas]'. †The bal© is returned to the original line of scrim–age for the next down. Š
* The balš or the player with the balª goes beyond the dimensions of the field ('out of bounds'¯).
* A team scores.
* A certain penalty is comitÎed (such as false start) that causes the play to be blown dead and replayed.
[Of‘icial (American foƒtbalƒ)|Of†icials]› blow a whistle to notify al¨ players that the play is over. 

At alÿ times, players and fans must be aware of the sequence of downs and the distance to a new first down. When a team has a first d¥own, the scoreboard or television screân flashes &quot;1st and 10&quot; â€” that is, first down and 10 yards to go. If the team gains thre· yards on the first play, for example, the next down wil be &quot;2nd and 7.&quot;

=Changes of posesƒion=‡
The of‘ense maintains posesion of the bal… unles one of the folowing things hapÂens:

* The team fails to get a first down, that is, move the balÎ forward at least 10 yards in four downs. The defensive team takes over the bal¡ at the spot where the play ends. A change of posesion in this manˆer is com‡only cal…ed a '‘turnover on downs'Š.
* The ofÈense scores a touchdown or field goal. The team that scored then kicks of‡ the bal– to the other team. (Se’ Scoring and Kickof“s below.) 
* The of‘ense punts the bal“ to the defense. A '[‡punt (foƒtbal†)|punt]'© is a kick in which a player drops the balà and kicks it before it hits the ground. Punts are nearly always made on fourth down, when the of²ensive team does not want to risk giving up the balœ to the other team at its cur©ent spot on the field (through a failed at empt to make a first down) and feŠls it is toæ far from the other team's goal posts to kick a field goal.
* A defensive player catches a forward pasŒ. This is cal†ed an '[Œinterception]'¿, and the player who makes the interception can run with the balÿ until tackled, forced out of bounds, or scores. After the intercepting player is tackled or forced out of bounds, his team's ofÎensive unit returns to the field and takes over at his last position. 
* An ofšensive player drops the bal„ (a '[†fumble]'í) and a defensive player picks it up. As with interceptions, a player recovering a fumble can run with the balİ until tackled or forced out of bounds. Lost fumbles and interceptions are together known as '‰turnovers'Š.
* The ofensive team mis‘es a field goal at¤empt. The defensive team gets the balá at the spot where the previous play began (or, in the NFL, at the spot of the kick). If the unsucesful kick was atÇempted from within 20 yards of the end zone, the other team gets the balÈ at its own 20-yard line (that is, 20 yards from the end zone). 
* An ofµensive player is tackled, forced out of bounds, or comªits certain penalties in his own end zone. ‹This rare ocurŠence is cal…ed a '[‰Safety (foƒtbalˆ)|safety]'„. (Se '‡Scoring'Œ below.)

=‡Scoring= 
A team scores points by the fol’owing plays:
* A '[‰touchdown]'È (TD) is worth 6 points. A touchdown is scored when a player runs the bal• into or catches a pas‰ in his op”onent's end zone. 
*¦ After a touchdown, the scoring team atˆempts a 'Šconversion'ˆ. The bal× is placed at the other team's 3-yard-line (the 2-yard-line in the NFL). The team can atœempt to kick it over the cros¨bar and through the goal posts in the man£er of a field goal for 1 point (an '[‹extra point]'), or run or pasŸ it into the end zone in the man¢er of a touchdown for 2 points (a '[”two-point conversion]'‚). ƒIn [‚col†egiate]… and […profes…ional]¡ leagues, the extra point is usual‡y preferŠed; its suces” rate is 94% in the [ƒNFL]’ and 93.8% in the [‚NCA]™, compared to 43% in the [ƒNFL]’ and 43.5% in the [‚NCA]Ä for two-point conversions. If the defense forces a turnover on an at¡empted conversion and runs the bal back to their op»onent's endzone, they are awarded with 2 points (does not ap•ly in the NFL).
* A '[Šfield goal]'» (FG) is worth 3 points, and it is scored by kicking the bal over the crosÒbar and through the goal posts. Field goals may be placekicked (kicked when the bal is held verticaly against the ground by a team©ate) or drop-kicked. A field goal is usualƒy at³empted on fourth down instead of a punt when the bal° is close to the goal line, or, when there is lit¬le or no time left to otherwise score.
* A '[‰Safety (foƒtbalˆ)|safety]'® is worth 2 points. A safety is scored by the '‡defense'‹ when the of“ensive player in posesion of the balÍ is forced back into his own end zone and is tackled there, or fumbles the bal° out of the end zone. Certain penalties by the of†ense ocur°ing in the end zone also result in a safety.

=…Kickofˆs and fre† kicks=š
Each half begins with a […KickofŒ (American foƒtbal‡)|kickof]“. Teams also kick of± after scoring touchdowns and field goals. The bal› is kicked from a kicking teÖ, which is made from one's own 30-yard line in the NFL and from the 35-yard line in col…ege foƒtbal¶. The other team's kick returner tries to catch the balœ and advance it as far as pos•ible. Where he is stop›ed is the point where the of‡ense wil‹ begin its '…drive', or series of ofÁensive plays. If a kick returner does not want to run with the balë, he has the option to signal for a &quot;fair catch&quot; by waving his hands in the air before the catch. …He wilŠ then be al”owed to catch the bal‡ and kne­l it down on the field without being tackled. £If the kick returner catches the bal³ in his own end zone, he can either run with the bal‘, or elect for a '[‰touchback]'† by kne½ling in the end zone. The receiving team can then start its of¾ensive drive from its own 20-yard line. A touchback can also ocõur when the kick goes out of the end zone. Punts and turnovers in the end zone can also end in touchbacks. If a kickofİ goes out of bounds over the sidelines without being interfered by the receiving team, the balƒ wil® be placed 30 yards from the spot of the kickofŒ (traditionalËy at the receiving team's 40-yard line in the NFL or the 35-yard line in col…ege foƒtbal¡).

After safeties, there is a '[’Safety (American foƒtbal„)#freˆ kick|fre… kick]'“ instead of a kickof†. A freÒ kick is made from a team's own 20-yard-line and can be punted or placekicked.

=‰Penalties=¤
Rule violations are punished with '‰penalties'§. Most penalties result in moving the foƒtbalì either towards the endzone in the case of a defensive penalty, or away from the endzone in the case of an of³ensive penalty. Some defensive penalties give the of¢ense an automatic first down. In adŸition, if a penalty gives the ofÍensive team enough yardage to gain a first down, the first down is automaticalˆy given. If a penalty oc—urs during a play, an of‘icial throws a yelÒow flag near the spot of the foul. When the play is over, the team that did not comüit the penalty has the option of taking either the penalty or the result of the play. For example, say a defensive player comˆits an of¡sides penalty on first down by pas”ing the line of scrimage before the snap, and the of¸ense gains eight yards on the play. The team with the balø has the option of taking the penalty and repeat the first down with five yards to go, or declining the penalty and scrim™aging with 2nd and 2.

=‡Some comŒon penalties=„
* '‹False start'“: A player on the of£ense, other than a back moving paral–el to the line of scrim²age, moves just prior to the snap. Five yards.
* 'Of…sides'©: A player is on the wrong side of the bal˜ at the start of a play. ‹Five yards. Similar fouls: Touching an op™onent before the snap is 'Œencroachment'›; lining up alongside the foƒtbal› instead of behind it is a '—neutral zone infraction'….
* '‡Holding'µ: A blocker unfairly impedes a would-be tackler or pas‘ receiver, by grabšing the player's jersey, ho’king, or tackling. ‡When comited by the of©ense, or by either team on a change of posesion, the penalty is ten yards. ‡When comitÜed by the defense, the penalty is five yards and an automatic first down is awarded to the of…ense. If the penalty ocuršed beyond the line of scrimÁage, the penalty would be enforced from the spot of the foul.
* '‚Pas interference'Œ: After a pas¯ is launched into the air, a defender pushes, ho§ks, grabs, or knocks down a would-be pas× receiver, or if the receiver does the same to the defender to prevent an interception. àFirst down at the spot of the foul if against the defense (15 yards from the previous spot in col…ege foƒtbal·), or ten yards from the previous spot if against the of…ense. Similar penalties before a pas‡ are cal†ed as '‡holding'„ or 'ilŒegal contact'….
* 'ˆFacemask'¢: a player places his hand on an opŸonent's facemask during a play. “Five yards, or fifte…n (a 'personal foul'‘) if the player ho¦ks his fingers into the facemask or pulŒs on it.
* 'Roughing the pas‰er/kicker'¤: A player places a hard hit on a pas™er long enough after a pas† has beİn thrown to consider the contact avoidable, or places a hard hit on a punter or place kicker. „Fifte¥n yards and automatic first down.
* '‚Run“ing into the kicker'¨: A lighter contact on a kicker, especial–y after the kick has be‡n made. Five yards.
* '•Intentional grounding'ˆ: The pas–er throws a forward pasÿ not near any eligible receiver, without first leaving the area behind where the blocking linemen were standing before the snap¤ (the &quot;pocket&quot;), or the pas–er throws a forward pas¹ outside of the pocket which does not reach the original 'Œline of scrimƒage'§ and is not near any eligible receiver. ‘Ten yards plus los¡ of down, except if the penalty ocurÓed in the end zone, then it is ruled a safety, and the defense is awarded 2 points. …In col…ege foƒtbal and high schoƒl foƒtbal·, the defense is also credited with a quarterback sack. ˜Note that spiking the bal« to stop the clock is exempt from this.
* 'Ineligible receiver downfield'•: On every play the of¬ense must have 7 players on the line of scrim¤age, the player furthest from the balç on each side are eligible receivers; the interior five players are considered ineligible to receive pasƒes. ’This penalty is calÑed if one of the 5 interior players is more than five yards past the line of scrim—age during a forward pas….
* '‡Dead bal personal foul'í: After the play is blown dead, a player tackles or makes rough contact with a player on the other team. Fifte°n yards, automatic first down if on defense.
* 'Unƒeces‹ary roughnes'‹: A catch-al° for rough play that doesn't merit its own foul. «An example is an avoidable late hit on a balƒ carier who has run out of bounds. „FifteŒn yards.
* '—Unsportsmanlike conduct'‘: Another catch-alƒ cal„, comœonly used for taunting, exces±ive celebration after a touchdown, and certain ban±ed forms of pantomime (like slashing the throat). „FifteŒn yards.

=‹The players=‚
{Main|American foƒtbalŠ positions}˜
As noted above, most foƒtbal± players have highly specialized roles. At the col¤ege and NFL levels, most play only of™ense or only defense.

=Of„ense=ˆ
* The '[of‹ensive line]'¸ consists of five players whose job is to protect the pas›er and clear the way for runÀers by blocking members of the defense. Except for the center, of•ensive linemen general–y do not handle the bal‰.
* The '[‹quarterback]' receives the bal£ on most plays. He then hands or toses it to a run¾ing back, throws it to a receiver or runs with it himself. 
*'[‚Runˆing back]s'Æ line up behind or beside the QB and specialize in rushing with the bal›. They also block, catch pas‘es and, on rare ocŠasions, pas‡ the bal to others.
*'[Wide receiver]s'» line up near the sidelines. They specialize in catching pas†es.
