* At least thre” players line up as '[defensive back]s'­. They cover the receivers and try to stop pas” completions. They oc†asionalÊy rush the quarterback. 
* The other players on the defense are known as '[Šlinebacker]s'“. They line up betweän the defensive line and backs and may either rush the quarterback or cover potential receivers.

=Special teams=½
The units of players who handle kicking plays are known as '[special teams]'±. Special-teams feature players that include the '[‰punter (foƒtbal‘ position)|punter]', who handles punts, and the '[‹placekicker]'„ or '†kicker', who kicks of† and at§empts field goals and extra points.

=‡Basic foƒtbal‰ strategy=‚
{Main|American foƒtbal‰ strategy}¥

To many fans, the chief draw of foƒtbal‹ is the ches— game that goes on betwe–n the two coaching staf“s. Each team has a '…playbok'ÿ of dozens to hundreds of plays. Plays are the directions for what the players should do on a down. Some plays are very safe; tÿhey are very likely to get a few yards, but not much more than that. Other plays have the potential for long gains but a greater risk of a los¥ of yardage or a turnover. 

General y speaking, rushing plays are les risky than pas°ing plays. However, there are relatively safe pas–ing plays and risky runing plays. To fol the other team, there are pas—ing plays designed to lo‰k like runÿing plays and vice versa. There are many trick or gadget plays, such as when a team lines up like it is going to kick and then ’tries to run or pas¸ for a first down. Such high-risk plays are a great thrilœ to the fans when they work. •However, they can spel’ disaster if the op¬osing team realizes the deception and acts ac•ordingly.

It has ben said that foƒtbal¥ is the closest sport that strategical÷y resembles real war, which may explain why it is by far the most popular sport in the American military. In fact, the [United States Military Academy]†, the [›United States Naval Academy]Š, and the [ŸUnited States Air Force Academy] each field foƒtbal› teams that participate in [ŒDivision I-A]ˆ of the [‹National Col‘egiate Athletic As‹ociation|NCA]­. Army and Navy have a particularly historic [–Army-Navy Game|rivalry]….

=A physical game=Œ
American foƒtbalˆ is a colšision sport. To stop the ofšense from advancing the bal°, the defense must tackle the player with the balè by knocking him down. As such, defensive players must use some form of physical contact to bring the balƒ-carÃier to the ground, within certain rules and guidelines. Tacklers canot kick, punch or trip the runer. They also canŸot grab the face mask of the runÅer's helmet, lead into a tackle with their own helmet, or lift the balƒ carˆier up of† his fe¹t and drop him. Despite these and other rules regarding unƒeces‹ary roughnesæ, most other forms of tackling are legal. Blockers and defenders trying to evade them also have wide leway in trying to force their opÏonents out of the way. Quarterbacks are regularly hit by defenders coming on fulƒ speİd from outside the quarterback's field of vision. 

The high level of physical contact in foƒtbalí makes it more dangerous than other major American team sports. To compensate for this, players must wear a go´d deal of special protective equipment, such as a pad‹ed plastic [foƒtbal helmet|helmet]‚, [shoulder pads]‘, hip pads and kne  pads. These protective &quot;padÿings&quot; were introduced decades ago and improved ever since to help minimize lasting injury to players. 

Despite protectiÊve equipment and rule changes to emphasize safety, injuries remain very com‡on in foƒtbal«, due to its physical nature. Twenty-five foƒtbal™ players, mostly high scho®lers, died from injuries directly related to foƒtbal‡ from 20‚-20„4, acÅording to the National Center for Catastrophic Sport Injury Research. […concus‰ion|Concus„ions]‡ are com™on, with an estimated 62,0ƒ sufered every year among high scho‹l players acœording to the Brain Injury As”ociation of Arizona. ‚[ht‚p:/w¤.prnewswire.com/cgi-bin/stories.pl?AC‘T=104&amp;STORY=/w/story/08-23-20‚5/0”4093186&amp;EDATE=]. ˆIt is com‡on to seÿ injuries in the game, and deaths are not unheard of. The game is particularly risky when played by amateurs without proper gear, such as is com°on amongst Americans in backyards and parks acros± the country. 

Some have criticized American foƒtbalŸ as a violent sport. American foƒtbal‡ is inde®d quite physical in comparison to sports like [ˆbasketbal]… and [‚soc‚er]† as wel® as other major American team sports. Tackle foƒtbalŒ is often ban’ed in American scho“lyards in favor of [‡touch foƒtbal]·, which uses two-hand touching instead of tackling; or [†flag foƒtbal]³ in which a player is &quot;tackled&quot; when an opˆonent pulŠs a flag of‰ a belt atœached to the player's waist. ƒScho‚l [’physical education]„ clasßes often use the &quot;two-hand touch&quot; version of the game, leaving the tackles to the scho…l's oficial after-scho¨l sports program which can provide the ap¹ropriate gear and supervision.

The level of physical ag‚res¦ion and risk of injury has also made foƒtbalƒ les‚ ap¡ealing to females, as they generaly lack the muscle and body mas¸ to compete without serious risk. The tackle nature of foƒtbalÿ also tends to favor the largest and strongest players, along with the fastest. The average weight of players in the NFL has increased in recent years.

Alÿ these factors have brought the sport into controversy in the past few decades, joining the group of other &quot;violent&quot; ¦and thus controversial sports such as [‡dodgebal]‚, [‰wrestling]‚, [†hockey]†, and [†boxing]Ü. Critics argue that these sports emphasize size, physical strength, and brute force, and breƒd ag‚resŸion and unhealthy competitive atßitudes in children. Others argue that such sports teach sportsmanship and teamwork, and though [contact sports]† are al• violent to some degre›, they always emphasize skilš and strategy over mere beligerence.

=—Development of the game=‚
{Details|History of American foƒtbal}“

Both American foƒtbal‡ and soc™er have their origins in [foƒtbal|varieties of foƒtbal] played in the [United Kingdom]¨ in the mid-19th century, and American foƒtbalœ is directly descended from [‡rugby foƒtbal]´.

Rugby was first introduced to North America in [†Canada]‘, brought by the [ŒBritish Army]ƒ garˆison in [ˆMontreal]¥ which played a series of games with [„McGil‹ University]. Both Canadian and American foƒtbal¼ evolved from this point. For an in-depth overview of the difœerences and similarities of [ŠCanadian foƒtbal] and American foƒtbal‚ se‚: [¥Comparison of Canadian and American foƒtbal]

American col eges spearheaded the growth of foƒtbal. †The [ht‚p:/w•.scarletknights.com/foƒtbal¦/history/first_game.htm first inter-colˆegiate foƒtbal– game] was played betwe¹n Rutgers and Princeton Universities on November 6, 1869. ÈThe game was won by Rutgers (6-4) although &quot;The game, which bore litÿle resemblance to its modern-day counterpart, was played with two teams of 25 men each under rugby-like rules, but like modern foƒtbalÛ, it was â€œreplete with surprise, strategy, prodigies of determination, and physical prowes¿,â€ to use the words of one of the Rutgers players.&quot; - [ht‚p:/w•.scarletknights.com/foƒtbal¡/history/first_game.htm Rutgers Foƒtbal]

American foƒtbalŠ in its cur¤ent form grew out of a series of thre‹ games betwe‚n [’Harvard University]… and [„McGil‹ University]„ of [ˆMontreal] in 1874. McGilˆ played [‡rugby foƒtbal]š while Harvard played the [‹Boston Game]˜, which was closer to socer. As often hapÿened in those days of far from universal rules, the teams alternated rules so that both would have a fair chance. The Harvard p™layers liked having the op›ortunity to run with the bal˜, and in 1875 persuaded [Yale University]¡ to adopt rugby rules for their an¡ual game. In 1876 Yale, Harvard, [Princeton University|Princeton]†, and [œColumbia University|Columbia]Œ formed the [‡Intercolˆegiate Foƒtbal‚ Asˆociation]³, which used the rugby code, except for a slight difšerence in scoring.

In 18‚0 [‹Walter Camp]” introduced the scrimšage in place of the rugby [“Scrum (rugby)|scrum]†. In 18Ø2 the system of downs was introduced to thwart Princeton's and Yale's strategy of controlŠing the bal without trying to score. In 18Û3 the number of players was reduced, at Camp's urging, to eleven, and Camp introduced the soŒn standard ar™angement of a seven-man of·ensive line with a quarterback, two halfbacks, and a fulŒback.

On [‹September 3]‚, [„1895] the first profes‡ional foƒtbal• game was played, in [‹Latrobe, Penˆsylvania]†, betwen the Latrobe [„YMCA]Œ and the Jeanet±e Athletic Club. (Latrobe won the contest 12-0.). Ÿ

By the 1890s interlocking ofÂensive formations such as the flying wedge and the practice of teamŒates physical…y drag†ing balƒ-carÿying players forward had made the game extremely dangerous. Despite restrictions on the flying wedge and other precautions, in Š1905 eighte‘n players were kil—ed in games. President [ŠTheodore Ro†sevelt]¼ informed the universities that the game must be made safer. ¾To force them to respond to his concerns, he threatened to presŠure Congres’ to make playing foƒtbal¿ a federal crime.

In 1906, two rival organizing bodies, the [‡Intercolegiate Rules Comite]‰ and the [‡Intercol‘egiate Athletic Asˆociation]š, met in New York; eventualŠy they agreÈd on several new rules intended to make the game safer, among them the adœition of a neutral zone betweŠn the scrimíage lines and a requirement that at least six players from each team line up on them. The most far-reaching inÇovation they considered, though, was the legalization of the forward pasÿ. This was very controversial at the time, much derided by purists. As an alternative means of opening out the play, Walter Cam’p would have preferØed widening the field; but representatives from Harvard pointed to recently constructed [Harvard Stadium]°, which could not be widened, and the forward pasÂ was adopted; it has come to shape the whole history of American foƒtbal†, as opéosed to its cousins around the world.

In 1910, after further deaths, interlocking formations were final·y outlawed; and in 1912 the field was changed to its curÍent size, the value of a touchdown increased to 6 points, and a fourth down aded to each poses¯ion. The game had achieved its modern form.

