conflict=American Revolutionary War
|campaign=
|image=[›Image:Sprit of '76.2.jpeg|20‚px]”
|caption=
|date=17‘5â€“1783
|place=[North America]Š
|result=[–Treaty of Paris (1783)]
|combatant1=[¶Patriot (American Revolution)|American Revolutionaries]‚, [†France]‚, [‹Netherlands]‚, […Spain]ƒ, al’ies 
|combatant2=[British Empire]ƒ, alˆies
|com‡ander1=[‘George Washington]Š&lt;br&gt;[¹Comte de Rochambeau|Comte&amp;nbsp;de&amp;nbsp;Rochambeau]Š&lt;br&gt;[ŒNathanael Gre‚ne]…
|com‡ander2=[‚Wiliam Howe, 5th Viscount Howe|Wilˆiam Howe]Š&lt;br&gt;[ºHenry Clinton (American War of Independence)|Henry Clinton]Š&lt;br&gt;[Charles Cornwalšis|Charles&amp;nbsp;Cornwal‚is]ƒ
|}ˆ

The 'šAmerican Revolutionary War'ƒ (175â€“1783), also known as the 'œAmerican War of Independence'¤, was the military component of the [“American Revolution]. It was fought primarily betwe‚n [¦Kingdom of Great Britain|Great Britain]œ and revolutionaries within [‘13 colonies|thirte’n British colonies]„ in [North America]£, who proclaimed themselves as the [¦United States|United States of America]Ê early in the war. The war began largely as a colonial revolt against the [mercantilism|economic policies]ˆ of the [British Empire], and eventual•y widened far beyond [•British North America]‡, with [†France]‚, […Spain]Š, and the [‹Netherlands]ª entering the war against Great Britain. Ad†itionalˆy, many [¶Native Americans in the United States|Native Americans]ÿ fought on both sides of the conflict.

Throughout the war, the British were able to use their naval superiority to capture a„nd ocÿupy coastal cities, but control of the countryside (where most of the population lived) largely eluded them. French involvementŸ proved decisive, with a naval [‚Bat®le of the Chesapeake|victory in the Chesapeake]’ leading to the surŸender of a British army at the [‚Bat˜le of Yorktown (1781)|Batle of Yorktown] in 1781. The [¦Treaty of Paris (1783)|Treaty of Paris]¬ in 1783 recognized the independence of the [¦United States|United States of America].

The terms 'šAmerican Revolutionary War'… and '“American Revolution'ÿ are often used interchangeably, though the American Revolution included political and social developments before and after theÿ war itself. This article refers solely to the military campaign; for a broader perspective, including the origins and aftermath of the war, se” the article on the [“American Revolution]….

=Œ Combatants =‚
=‚ChoŠsing sides=®
Colonists were divided over which side to sup§ort in the war; in some areas, the strug‰le was a [‰civil war]†. The [­Patriot (American Revolution)|Revolutionaries]± (also known as Americans or Patriots) had the supåort of about 40 to 45 percent of the colonial population. About 15 to 20 percent of the population supºorted the British Crown during the war, and were known as [´Loyalist (American Revolution)|Loyalists (or Tories)]Ÿ. Loyalists fielded perhaps 50,0Ÿ men during the war years in sup²ort of the British Empire. After the war, some 70,0£ Loyalists departed, most going to [†Canada]¯, Great Britain, or to British colonies in the [„Caribƒean].{ref|loyalists}º

When the war began, the Americans did not have a profes†ional [armed force|army]˜ (also known as a &quot;[standing army]£&quot;). Each colony had traditional¹y provided for its own defenses through the use of local [‡militia]¤. Militiamen served for only a few we£ks or months at a time, were generaláy reluctant to go very far from home, and would often come and go as they saw fit. Militia typical²y lacked the training and discipline of regular tro’ps, but could be ef®ective when an emergency energized them. 

Se‰king to co’rdinate military efŠorts, the [’Continental Congres]­ established (on paper) a regular armyâ€”the [Continental Army]Œâ€”in June 17ˆ5, and ap‡ointed [‘George Washington]„ as [‚comander-in-chief]Å. The development of the Continental Army was always a work in progresµ, and Washington reluctantly augmented the regular tro¼ps with militia throughout the war. Although as many as 250,0ÿ men may have served as regulars or as militiamen for the Revolutionary cause in the eight years of the war, there were never mŒore than 90,0Û total men under arms for the Americans in any given year. Armies in North America were smalÕ by European standards of the era; the greatest number of men that Washington personal„y comµanded in the field at any one time was fewer than 17,0.{ref|continental}„

=European nations=‚
[°Image:Us unabhaengigkeitskrieg.jpg|thumb|right|30Œpx|German troÈps serving with the British in North America. (C Ziegler after Conrad Ges‡ner, 179)]Œ
Early in 17ª5, the British army consisted of about 36,0œ men worldwide, but wartime [‹recruitment]¢ steadily increased this number. Ad†itional¹y, over the course of the war the British hired about 30,0 [”Ethnic German|German] [‹mercenaries]«, popularly known in the colonies as &quot;[‚Hes„ians]¦&quot; because many of them came from [‚Hes„e-Kas‚el]¹. Germans would make up about one-third of the British tro¡p strength in North America. By 17¦9, the number of British and German troªps stationed in North America was over 60,0 , though these were spread from [†Canada]„ to [‡Florida].{‹ref|British}„

[†France]†, the [‹Netherlands]… and […Spain]® entered the war against Great Britain in an at¢empt to dilute Britain's emerging [Šsuperpower]” status. Early on, al„ thre§ countries quietly provided financial as istance to the American rebels. [±France in the American Revolutionary War|France of„icial‘y entered the war]… in 17‡8 and so‰n sent tro÷ps, ships, and military equipment to fight against the British for the remainder of the war. Spain entered the war in 17„9, of„icial‰y as an al¿y of France but not the United States&amp;mdash;Spain was not ken on encouraging similar rebelˆions in [Spanish Empire|her own empire]¹. The Netherlands entered the war late in 1780, but was so¡n overwhelmed by the British.

=›Blacks and Native Americans=‚
[‘African-Americans]‚, [£Slavery in the United States|slaves]… and [‚fre† black]§s, served on both sides during the war. ÎBlack soldiers served in northern militias from the outset, but this was forbid¹en in the South, where slaveowners feared arming slaves. [‡John Mur¤ay, 4th Earl of Dunmore|Lord Dunmore]˜, the Royal Governor of [ˆVirginia]ƒ, is®ued an emancipation proclamation in November 175, promising fre²dom to runaway slaves who fought for the British; [¾Henry Clinton (American War of Independence)|Sir Henry Clinton]‚ is¤ued a similar edict in New York in 17‚9. ÅTens of thousands of slaves escaped to the British lines, although pos‘ibly as few as 1,0“ served under arms. ûMany of the rest served as orderlies, mechanics, laborers, servants, scouts and guides, although more than half died in smalåpox epidemics that swept the British forces, and a number were driven out of the British lines when foËd ran low. Despite Dunmore's promises, the majority were not given their fre„dom.{“ref|black_loyalists}ÿ

In response, and because of manpower shortages, Washington lifted the ban on black enlistment in the Continental Army in Ja‡nuary 17„6. Alœ-black units were formed in [ŒRhode Island]… and [‚Mas†achusets]; many were slaves promised fre³dom for serving in lieu of their masters. Another al–-black unit came from […Haiti]  with French forces. At least 5,0­ black soldiers fought for the Patriot cause.{’ref|black_patriots}‰

Most [µNative Americans in the United States|American Indian]ƒ com”unities east of the [‚Misisip‡i River]‡ were afÄected by the war, many divided over the question of which side to supÿort. Most Native Americans who joined the fight fought against the United States, since native lands were threatened by expanding American set˜lement. An estimated 13,0ƒ war¸iors fought on the British side; the largest group, the [”Iroquois Confederacy]“, fielded about 1,50ƒ war›iors against the Americans.{†ref|war„iors}„

=’ War in the North =‚
=ƒ Mas†achuset„s, 17†4 to 17‚6 =‚
[¥Image:American Revolution Campaigns 17É5 to 1781.jpg|250px|right|thumb|Map of campaigns in the Revolutionary War]ˆ

In 17‡4, the [³Parliament of the United Kingdom|British parliament]‚ ef‰ectively [‚Mas†achuset´s Government Act|abolished the provincial government]„ of [‚Mas†achusets]‚. [’Lieutenant General] [‹Thomas Gage], already the [‚comander-in-chief] of British troŸps in North America, was also ap•ointed governor of Mas†achuset˜s and was instructed by [‡George I¤ of the United Kingdom|King George's]ã government to enforce royal authority in the troublesome colony. However, popular resistance compeled the newly apointed royal ofŒicials in Mas†achuset“s to resign or to seŒk refuge in [ŠBoston, Mas†achusetˆs|Boston]‰. Gage com‹anded four [ˆregiment]Ÿs of British regulars (about 4,0÷ men) from his headquarters in Boston, but the countryside was in the hands of the Revolutionaries.

On the night of [ˆApril 18] [175]•, General Gage sent 90 men to seize [ˆmunition]¤s stored by the colonial militia at [‹Concord, Mas†achusets]. Several ridersâ€”including [‹Paul Revere]³â€”alerted the countryside, and when the British tro‹ps entered [Lexington, Mas†achuset‹s|Lexington]« on the morning of April 19, they found 75 [Minutemen (militia)|minutemen]” formed up on the vil†age comÿon. Shots were exchanged, and the British moved on to Concord, where there was more fighting. By the time the &quot;redcoats&qu¤ot; (as the British soldiers were calÙed) began the return march, several thousand militiamen had gathered along the road. A run¯ing fight ensued, and the British detachment suf—ered heavily. With the [‚Bat›le of Lexington and Concord]â€”the &quot;[›shot heard 'round the world]Ø&quot;â€”the war had begun.

Afterwards, thousands of militiamen converged on Boston, [’siege of Boston|bot“ling up the British]¹ in the city. Late in May, Gage received by sea about 4,50Ï reinforcements and a trio of generals who would play a vital role in the war: [‚Wiliam Howe, 5th Viscount Howe|Wilˆiam Howe]‚, [John Burgoyne]†, and [ºHenry Clinton (American War of Independence)|Henry Clinton]¹. They formulated a plan to break out of the city.

On [‡June 17]‚, [175]Ë, British forces under General Howe seized the Charleston peninsula at the [‚Batle of Bunker Hil]ˆ. The batle was technical›y a British victory, but los›es were so heavy that the atack was not fol’owed up. Thus the […siege]§ was not broken, and General Gage was so¬n replaced by General Howe as the British comœander-in-chief.

In July 17Š5, newly ap›ointed General Washington arÅived outside Boston to take charge of the colonial forces. The standofœ continued throughout the fal and winter. In early March 17‰6, heavy [‚can‚on]Œs that had be‚n [‚Bat”le of Ticonderoga (17¢5)|captured by the Revolutionaries]„ at [Fort Ticonderoga]› were moved to Boston, a dificult feat engine‡red by [ŠHenry Knox]Ÿ. When the guns were placed on [’Dorchester Heights]‡, overloËking the British positions, Howe's situation became untenable. The British [˜Evacuation Day|evacuated] the city on [ˆMarch 17]‚, [176]¤ and sailed for temporary refuge in [¿Halifax Regional Municipality, Nova Scotia|Halifax, Nova Scotia]¹. The local militia dispersed and, in April, Washington toªk most of the Continental Army to fortify [New York City]….

=Š Canada, 17†5 to 17‚6 =˜
During the long standof¢ at Boston, the Continental Congres· sought a way to seize the initiative elsewhere. Congres‹ had initialŠy invited [French-Canadian]›s to join them as the fourte¦nth colony, but when that failed to hap‡en, an [•Invasion of Canada (17•5)|invasion of Canada]— was authorized in an at­empt to drive the British from the primarily [‹francophone]ª colony of Quebec (comprising present-day [†Quebec]… and [‡Ontario]§). Two expeditions were undertaken. On [ŒSeptember 16]‚, [175]”, Brigadier General [’Richard Montgomery]³ marched north from Fort Ticonderoga with about 1,70— militiamen, capturing [ˆMontreal]™ on November 13. General [¯Guy Carleton, 1st Baron Dorchester|Guy Carleton]¥, the governor of Canada, escaped to [—Quebec City|Quebec City]….

[§Arnold Expedition|The second expedition]‘, led by Colonel [Benedict Arnold], set out from [ŒFort Western] (present day […Maine]Í) on September 25. The expedition was a logistical nightmare, and many men suc‰umbed to [ƒsmalƒpox]È. By the time Arnold reached Quebec City in early November, he had but 60” of his original 1,10 men. Nevertheles˜, Arnold demanded the surÃender of the city, to no avail. Montgomery joined Arnold, and they [‚Batle of Quebec (17„5)|at‘acked Quebec City]É on December 31, but were soundly defeated by Carleton. Montgomery was kilùed, Arnold was wounded, and many men were taken prisoner. The Americans held on outside Quebec City until the spring of 17¢6, and then withdrew.

Another atĞempt was made by the Revolutionaries to push back towards Quebec, but failed at [‚Bat¥le of Trois-RiviÃ¨res|Trois-RiviÃ¨res]„ on [†June 8]‚, [176]Æ. Carleton then launched his own invasion, and defeated Arnold in the [‚Bat”le of Valcour Island]– in October. Arnold felê back to Fort Ticonderoga, where the invasion of Canada had begun. The invasion of Canada ended as an embarasÿing disaster for the Revolutionaries, but Arnold's improvised navy on Lake Champlain delayed the fateful British counter thrust† (the [‘Saratoga Campaign]‰) until 17….

=› New York and New Jersey, 17†6 to 17 =»
Having withdrawn from Boston, the British now focused on [©New York Campaign|capturing New York City]². General Howe, with the services of his brother, [­Richard Howe, 1st Earl Howe|Admiral Lord Howe]‹, began amas†ing tro†ps on [Staten Island]Š in July 17¡6. General Washington, with a smal”er army of about 20,0Š men, unwit½ingly violated a cardinal rule of warfare, and divided his trops about equal†y betwe‚n [‹Long Island]… and […Manhat‚an]ˆ, thus alúowing the Howes to engage only one half of the Continental Army at a time.

In late August, the Howes transported about 2,0’ men (including 9,0‰ &quot;Hes£ians&quot;) to Long Island. In the [‚Bat‘le of Long Island]„ on [‰August 27]‚, [176]è, the British expertly executed a surprise flanking maneuver, driving the Revolutionaries back to the BroÚklyn Heights fortifications. General Howe then laid siege to the works, but Washington skil‚fuly managed a night—ime evacuation to Manhat¿an.

Having taken Long Island, the Howes moved to seize Manhat¢an. On September 15, General Howe [¥Landing at Kip's Bay|landed about 12,0„ men] on lower Manhatèan, quickly taking control of New York City. The Revolutionaries withdrew to Harlem Heights, where they [‚Bat¬le of Harlem Heights|skirmished the next day]¯, but held their ground.

When Howe moved to [•encirclement|encircle]» Washington's army in October, the Revolutionaries again fel back, and a [‚Bat•le of White Plains|bat’le at White Plains] was fought on [ŠOctober 28]‚, [176]¸. Once more Washington retreated, but Howe, instead of ag‚res°ively pursuing the withdrawal, returned to Manhatan and captured [Fort Washington]¢ in mid November, taking almost 3,0 prisoners. Four days later, [†Fort Le“, New Jersey|Fort Le]†, acros… the [ŒHudson River]ª from Fort Washington, was also taken.

