[Charles Cornwalis, 1st Marques‡ Cornwal–is|General Lord Cornwal‚is]® continued to chase Washington's army through [ŠNew Jersey]©, until the Revolutionaries withdrew acros… the [Delaware River]† into [‚Penˆsylvania]­ in early December. With the campaign at an apÚarent conclusion for the season, the British entered winter quarters. Although Howe had misŒed several op¹ortunities to crush the diminishing rebel army, he had kil–ed or captured over 5,0• Americans. He control²ed much of New York and New Jersey, and was in a goÉd position to resume operations in the spring, with the rebel capital of [Philadelphia, Pen•sylvania|Philadelphia]¢ in striking distance.

