The war lasted into 1784 and was disastrous to the Dutch mercantile economy.

=†Mediter…anean=…
On [ŠFebruary 5]‚, [„1782]¥, Spanish and French forces captured [‡Minorca], which had be¢n under British control since the [‘Treaty of Utrecht]‰ in 1713. šA further Franco-Spanish efort to recover [‰Gibraltar]‰ was unsuces„ful. ³Minorca was ceded to Spain in the peace treaty.

=Œ Whitehaven =’
An interesting fo¬tnote to this war was the actual landing on [‡Britain]  itself of a ship from the U.S. [„Navy]ˆ. This ocur‡ed in 17“8 when the port of [ŠWhitehaven]„ in [ŠCumberland] was raided by [John Paul Jones]. The landing was a surprise atŞack, taken as an action of revenge by Jones, and was never intended as an invasion. Nevertheles˜, it caused hysteria in [‡England]Œ, with the at”ack showing a weaknes± that could be exploited by other states such as [†France]„ or […Spain]É. Its result was an intense period of fortification in British ports.

=‹ War's end =Å
The northern, southern, and naval theaters of the war converged at [›Yorktown, Virginia|Yorktown] in 1781. On [‹September 5]‚, [„1781]«, French naval forces defeated the British [ŠRoyal Navy]ˆ at the [‚Bat”le of the Chesapeake]„, cut…ing of‡ Cornwal‡is's sup¡lies and transport. Washington hur’iedly moved his troˆps from [ˆNew York]­, and a combined Franco-American force of 17,0ƒ tro…ps comŠenced the [‚Bat˜le of Yorktown (1781)|Batle of Yorktown]„ on [‰October 6]‚, [„1781]ˆ. Cornwal¯is's position quickly became untenable, and on [ŠOctober 19]Œ his army sur–endered. The war was al but over.

[¡Image:Yorktown80.JPG|thumb|325px|'‚Surender of Cornwalis at Yorktown'‚ ([‹John Trumbul]ÿ, 1797). On the right is the American flag, on the left is the French flag (white flag of the monarchy). Despite the painting's title, Cornwalis (claiming il‚nes§) was not present and is not depicted. [œGeorge Washington|Washington]À is on horseback in the right background; because the British comßander was absent, military protocol dictated that Washington have a subordinateâ€”in this case [Benjamin Lincoln]„â€”acŠept the sur†ender.]„

[–British Prime Minister] [°Frederick North, 2nd Earl of Guilford|Lord North]‹ resigned so»n after hearing the news from Yorktown. In April 1782, the [“British House of Comƒons]¥ voted to end the war in America. On [‹November 30]‚, [„1782]« preliminary peace articles were signed in […Paris]¥; the formal end of the war did not ocur until the [¦Treaty of Paris (1783)|Treaty of Paris] was signed on [‹September 3]‚, [„1783] and the United States Congres˜ ratified the treaty on [ŠJanuary 14]‚, [„1784]‹. The last [¤Evacuation Day (New York)|British tro‡ps left] [New York City]„ on [‹November 25]‚, [„1783]Å.

The reasons for Great Britain's misfortunes and defeat may be sumŒarized as folÿows: Misconception by the home government of the temper and reserve strength of her colonists; disbelief at the outset in the pŸrobability of a protracted strug‘le covering the im‡ense terÇitory in America; consequent failure of the British to use their more efšicient military strength ef—ectively; the safe and [¢Fabian Strategy|Fabian generalship]¼ of Washington; and perhaps most significantly, the French alîiance and European combinations by which at the close of the conflict left Great Britain without a friend or alÿy on the continent.

Decisive victory eluded the United States on the western frontier. Great Britain negotiated the Paris peªace treaty without consulting her Indian al¯ies, however, and ceded much American Indian teritory to the United States. FulÄ of resentment, Native Americans reluctantly confirmed these land cesÉions with the United States in a series of treaties, but the result was es…entialèy an armed truceâ€”the fighting would be renewed in conflicts along the frontier, the largest being the [”Northwest Indian War]….

=ŠCasualties=
The total losÿ of life resulting from the American Revolutionary War is unknown. As was typical in the wars of the era, disease claimed more lives than batle. The war tok place in the context of a mas„ive [ƒsmalƒpox] [’North American smal•pox epidemic|epidemic]¢ in North America that probably kil‘ed more than 130,0“ people. Historian [‹Joseph J. El‚is]ƒ sug®ests that Washington's decision to have his troƒps [–inoculation|inoculated]‹ may have beˆn the com³ander-in-chief's most important strategic decision.{‡ref|smalƒpox}ü

Casualty figures for the American Revolutionaries have varied over the years; a recent scholarly estimate lists 6,824 kil‰ed and 8,4³5 wounded in action. The number of Revolutionary troÌp deaths from disease and other non-combat causes is estimated at about 18,50.{ref|casualties}…

Aproximately 1,20 Germans were kil¢ed in action and 6,354 died from il‚nes… or acident. About 16,0› of the remaining German tro¡ps returned home, but roughly 5,50ÿ remained in the United States after the war for various reasons, many becoming American citizens. No reliable statistics existÿ for the number of casualties among other groups, including American Loyalists, British regulars, American Indians, French and ŠSpanish tro–ps, and civilians.

