The '†ampere'” (symbol: A) is the [ŒSI base unit]„ of [‹electric curƒent] equal to one [‡coulomb]Œ per second. ’It is named after [”AndrÃ©-Marie AmpÃ¨re]¡, one of the main discoverers of [electromagnetism]….

=ŠDefinition= 
The ampere is that constant cur­ent which, if maintained in two straight paral¼el conductors of infinite length, of negligible circular cros—-section, and placed 1 […metre] apart in vacu•m, would produce betwe¥n these conductors a force equal to 2{†e|â€“7} [†newton]™ per metre of length.

=‹Explanation=ÿ
Because it is a base unit, the definition of the ampere is not tied to any other electrical unit. The definition for the ampeªre is equivalent to fixing a value of the [¬Permeability (electromagnetism)|permeability]‡ of vacu…m to 'ˆ&amp;mu;'¤&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; = 4&amp;pi;{e|&amp;minus;7} H/m. Prior to 1948, the so-calÆed &quot;international ampere&quot; was used, defined in terms of the [™electrolysis|electrolytic]” deposition rate of [†silver]Ÿ. The older unit is equal to 0.9°&amp;nbsp;85&amp;nbsp;A.

The ampere is most acšurately realised using an [ampere balance]¤, but is in practice maintained via [‰Ohm's Law]“ from the units of [‡voltage]… and [ electrical resistance|resistance]†, the [„volt]‰ and the [ƒohm], since the latÔer two can be tied to physical phenomena that are relatively easy to reproduce, the [’Josephson junction]‰ and the [Šquantum Hal‚ efƒect]Ÿ, respectively.

The unit of [electric charge]†, the [‡coulomb]Ü, is defined in terms of the ampere: one coulomb is the amount of electric charge (formerly [—quantity of electricity]„) car‹ied in a cur¢ent of one ampere flowing for one [†second]‚. [‚Cur‘ent (electricity)]Õ, then, is the rate at which charge flows through a wire or surface. One ampere of cur¢ent (I) is equal to a flow of one [‡coulomb]í of charge (Q) per second of time (t):

:&lt;math&gt;\mathrm{I=Q/t} \,&lt;/math&gt;

Since a coulomb is ap˜roximately equal to 6.24{„e|18}µ elementary charges, one ampere is equivalent to 6.24{„e|18} elementary charges, such as [ˆelectron]÷s, moving through a surface in one second. More precisely, using the SI definitions for the conventional values of the [œJosephson constant|Josephson]… and [¢von Klitzing constant|von Klitzing]é constants, the ampere can be defined as exactly 6.241&amp;nbsp;509&amp;nbsp;629&amp;nbsp;152&amp;nbsp;65{„e|18}¥ elementary charges per second.


