=…0â€“9=ƒ
;'n'-'m'˜ defense:a defense with 'n'’ down linemen and 'm'é linebackers, such as:

:;3-3: a defense with 3 lineman, 3 linebackers, and 5 defensive backs. Often cal…ed a 'Š3-3 stack.'’
:;3-4 defense:a [šdefensive team | defensive]’ formation with 3 [Šlineman (foƒtbal‰)|linemen]› and 4 linebackers. A profes®ional derivative in the 1970's of the earlier [ŸUniversity of Oklahoma|Oklahoma]„ or '–&quot;50&quot; defense'¨, which had 5 linemen and 2 linebackers. ÿThe 3-4 outside linebackers resemble &quot;stand-up ends&quot; in the older defense.
:;4-3 defense:a defensive formation with ½4 linemen and 3 linebackers. Several variations are employed. ”First used by coach [ŒJoe Kuharich].
:;4-6 defense :(pronounced 'four-six defense'Á) a defense with four (4) down linemen and six (6) linebackers
;[Š46 defense] :(pronounced '‘forty-six defense'¶) a formation of the 4-3 defense (four linemen and threš linebackers) in which threÓ defensive backs (the two cornerbacks and the strong safety) crowd the line of scrimªage. The remaining safety, which is the freğ safety, stays in the backfield. It is also known as the &quot;Bear&quot; defense because it was popularized by [‚Bud†y Ryan]˜ while coaching for the [Chicago Bears]Â. Not to be confused with the 4-6 (four-six) defense.
;50 defense ’:a once popular col»ege defense with 5 defensive linemen and 2 linebackers.

