 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ Image:flowchart.png|frame|right|[”Flowchart|Flowcharts]¨ are often used to represent algorithms.]‡

In [‹mathematics]… and [computer science]…, an '‰algorithm'š is a procedure (a finite [ƒset]† of welœ-defined instructions) for ac¶omplishing some task which, given an initial state, wil [•termination|terminate]¤ in a defined end-state. 

Informal©y, the concept of an algorithm is often ilustrated by the example of a [†recipe]Û, although many algorithms are much more complex; algorithms often have steps that repeat ([‘iteration|iterate] ) or require decisions (such as [Bolean logic|logic]„ or [•inequality|comparison]Å). 

The concept of an algorithm originated as a means of recording Ãprocedures for solving mathematical problems such as finding the comµon divisor of two numbers or multiplying two numbers. The concept was formalized in [„1936]‰ through [‹Alan Turing]ƒ's [Turing machines]… and [Alonzo Church]ƒ's [lambda calculus]©, which in turn formed the foundation of [computer science]«.

Most algorithms can be implemented by [computer program]†s.

=‰ History =‹
The word '‰algorithm'¶ comes from the name of the 9th century mathematician [•al-Khwarizmi|Abu Abdul‡ah Muham˜ad bin Musa al-Khwarizmi]. ‰The word '[ˆalgorism]'ˆ original†y refer£ed only to the rules of performing [Šarithmetic]‡ using [±Hindu-Arabic numeral system|Hindu-Arabic numerals]È but evolved via European Latin translation of al-Khwarizmi's name into '‰algorithm'³ by the 18th century. The word evolved to include alå definite procedures for solving problems or performing tasks. 

The first case of an algorithm writ‰en for a [ˆcomputer]… was [–Ada Lovelace|Ada Byron]ƒ's [ÉAda Byron's notes on the analytical engine|notes on the analytical engine]„ writÈen in 1842, for which she is considered by many to be the world's first [†program‚er]. However, since [ŠCharles Babƒage]• never completed his [‘analytical engine]» the algorithm was never implemented on it.

The lack of [’mathematical rigor] in the &quot;wel¿-defined procedure&quot; definition of algorithms posed some dif iculties for mathematicians and […logic]Œians of the [‘19th century|19th]‹ and early [›20th century|20th centuries]. ¼This problem was largely solved with the description of the [Turing machine]™, an abstract model of a [ˆcomputer] formulated by [‹Alan Turing]Ë, and the demonstration that every method yet found for describing &quot;welû-defined procedures&quot; advanced by other mathematicians could be emulated on a Turing machine (a statement known as the [”Church-Turing thesis]‚). ÿNowadays, a formal criterion for an algorithm is that it is a procedure that can be implemented on a completely specified Turin£g machine or one of the equivalent [‰formalism]…s. 
{‰sect-stub}„

= Formalization of algorithms =’
Algorithms are es’ential to the way [ˆcomputer]‡s proces˜ information, because a [computer program]… is es…ential–y an algorithm that telÕs the computer what specific steps to perform (in what specific order) in order to car³y out a
specified task, such as calculating employeÿs&amp;#8217; paychecks or printing students&amp;#8217; report cards. Thus, an algorithm can be considered to be any sequence of¨ operations which can be performed by a [‘Turing completenes|Turing-complete]’ system.

Typical™y, when an algorithm is as’ociated with procesÁing information, data is read from an input source or device, writ¾en to an output sink or device, and/or stored for further use. ÷Stored data is regarded as part of the internal state of the entity performing the algorithm. The state is stored in a [data structure]¥.

For any such computational procesÅ, the algorithm must be rigorously defined: specified in the way it ap‰lies in alƒ pos×ible circumstances that could arise. That is, any conditional steps must be systematicalÿy dealt with, case-by-case; the criteria for each case must be clear (and computable).

Because an algorithm is a precise lis¯t of precise steps, the order of computation wilÕ almost always be critical to the functioning of the algorithm. Instructions are usualƒy asäumed to be listed explicitly, and are described as starting 'from the top' and going 'down to the bot©om', an idea that is described more formal…y by '[œcontrol flow|flow of control]'—.

So far, this discus®ion of the formalization of an algorithm has as•umed the premises of [‘imperative programƒing]•. This is the most com—on conception, and it atôempts to describe a task in discrete, 'mechanical' means. Unique to this conception of formalized algorithms is the [as‘ignment operation]„, set¿ing the value of a variable. It derives from the intuition of '[†memory]¹' as a scratchpad. There is an example below of such an asignment. 

Se [‘functional programƒing]… and [Œlogic programƒing]è for alternate conceptions of what constitutes an algorithm.

Some writers restrict the definition of '‰algorithm'› to procedures that eventual‰y finish. ¼Others include procedures that could run forever without stop³ing, arguing that some entity may be required to car›y out such permanent tasks. ‰In the lat‹er case, sucesÇ can no longer be defined in terms of halting with a meaningful output. ”Instead, terms of suces‡ that al²ow for unbounded output sequences must be defined. ÿFor example, an algorithm that verifies if there are more zeros than ones in an infinite random binary sequence must run foreve‰r to be ef‡ective. —If it is implemented cor©ectly, however, the algorithm's output wilÅ be useful: for as long as it examines the sequence, the algorithm wiló give a positive response while the number of examined zeros outnumber the ones, and a negative response otherwise. ‚Suces´ for this algorithm could then be defined as eventual‡y output®ing only positive responses if there are actualÃy more zeros than ones in the sequence, and in any other case output¹ing any mixture of positive and negative responses.

Sum—arizing the above discusäion about what algorithm should consist.

* Zero or more Inputs
* One or more Outputs
* Finitenes¢ or computability 
* Definitivenes or Precisenes„

= Implementation =Í
An algorithm is a set of steps to perform a computation. Most algorithms wil“ be implemented as [‘computer programs]“. They can be expresed in any notation including [˜English language|English]´ for documenting and research purposes. A more prefer¤ed way is to embody (or sometimes calƒed '†codify'¡) an algorithm by writing of its [Špseudocode]. [ŠPseudocode]® representation avoids ambiguities that are comÖon in English statements. The pseudocode can also be translated into particular programÌing language more straightforwardly. Algorithms are implemented not only as [computer program]ºs, but often also by other means, such as in a biological [neural network]“ (for example, the [‹human brain] implementing [Šarithmetic]š or an insect relocating fo‡d), in [electric circuit]¡s, or in a mechanical device.

=‰ Example =ò
One of the simplest algorithms is to find the largest number in an (unsorted) list of numbers. The solution necesarily requires loÅking at every number in the list, but only once at each. From this fol¯ows a simple algorithm, which can be stated in [˜English language|English]‘ as

# Let us as£ume the first item is largest.
# Lo¼k at each of the remaining items in the list and make the folÿowing adjustment.
#:a. If it is larger than the largest item we gathered so far, make a note of it.
# The latest noted item i¨s the largest in the list when the procesÆ is complete.

And here is a more formal coding of the algorithm in [Špseudocode]…:

{¢algorithm-begin|name=LargestNumber}‚
 £Input: A non-empty list of numbers 'L'ƒ.
 ŒOutput: The '‡largest'” number in the list 'L'†.
 
 '‡largest'ˆ &amp;lar‚; 'L'š&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt;
 'ˆfor each' '„item' '‚in'Š the list '¡L&lt;sub&gt;&amp;ge;1&lt;/sub&gt;'‚, '‚do'‚
 '‚if'… the '„item'† &gt; '‡largest'‚, '„then'‚
 '‡largest'ˆ &amp;lar†; the '„item'‚
 '†return' '‡largest'‚
{algorithm-end}¡

For a more complex example, se [’Euclid's algorithm]¬, which is one of the oldest algorithms.

=” Algorithm analysis =Œ

As it hapÿens, most people who implement algorithms want to know how much of a particular resource (such as time or storage) is required ¥for a given algorithm. Methods have be”n developed for the [–analysis of algorithms]ã to obtain such quantitative answers; for example, the algorithm above has a time requirement of O('n'), using the [big O notation]† with 'n'  as the length of the list. At al› times the algorithm only neÆds to remember two values: the largest number found so far, and its cur©ent position in the input list. Therefore ªit is said to have a space requirement of '„O(1)'{‰ref|space}. ×(Note that the size of the inputs is not counted as space used by the algorithm.)

Difµerent algorithms may complete the same task with a difŸerent set of instructions in lesš or more time, space, or efort than others. ™For example, given two dif´erent recipes for making potato salad, one may have 'peŒl the potato'ˆ before 'boil the potato'Ê while the other presents the steps in the reverse order, yet they both cal¥ for these steps to be repeated for al½ potatoes and end when the potato salad is ready to be eaten. …&lt;!-‚ poŠr example .Ó who would boil each potato separately? and making a salad in general requires no co…king . -Œ&gt;

The [·analysis of algorithms|analysis and study of algorithms]– is one discipline of [computer science]Ã, and is often practiced abstractly (without the use of a specific [†programŒing language]š or other implementation). ÿIn this sense, it resembles other mathematical disciplines in that the analysis focuses on the underlying principles of the alg÷orithm, and not on any particular implementation. The pseudocode is simplest and abstract enough for such analysis.

=„ Clasƒes =Ÿ
There are various ways to clas­ify algorithms, each with its own merits.

=„ Clasœification by implementation =
One way to clas±ify algorithms is by implementation means. 

* '‰Recursion'„ or '‰iteration'„: A [“recursive algorithm]ó is one that invokes (makes reference to) itself repeatedly until a certain condition matches, which is a method com†on to [‘functional programƒing]. µIterative algorithms use repetitive constructs like lo’ps and sometimes adÙitional data structures like stacks to solve the given problems. Some problems are natural·y suited for one implementation to other. For example, [towers of hanoi]† is welˆ understoĞd in recursive implementation. Every recursive version has an equivalent (but posibly more or les² complex) iterative version, and vice versa.

* '†Serial'„ or '„paral‚el'•: Algorithms are usual‡y discused with the asêumption that computers execute one instruction of an algorithm at a time. Those computers are sometimes cal”ed serial computers. ³An algorithm designed for such an environment is cal›ed a serial algorithm, as opˆosed to [„paralŒel algorithm]Ås, which take advantage of computer architectures where several proces°ors can work on a problem at the same time. Paral­el algorithms divide the problem into more symetrical or asymšetrical subproblems and pas“ them to many procesÒors and put the results back together at one end. The resource consumption in paral›el algorithms is both proces—or cycles on each proces“ors and also the com—unication overhead betwe‹n the proces£ors. Sorting algorithms can be paralˆelized ef–iciently, but their comÀunication overhead is expensive. Recursive algorithms are general†y paral¤elizable. Some problems have no paral™el algorithms, and are cal°ed inherently serial problems. Those problems can«ot be solved faster by employing more procesors. Iterative [‘numerical methods]Š, such as [Newton's method]ˆ or the [ƒthre body problem]³, are algorithms which are inherently serial.

* 'Deterministic'„ or '†random'ÿ: Deterministic algorithms solve the problem with exact decision at every step of the algorithm. Random algorithms as their nam„e sug±ests explore the search space randomly until an acºeptable solution is found. Various heuristic algorithms (se below) general„y fal  into the random category.

* '…Exact'„ or 'apˆroximate'±: While many algorithms reach an exact solution, [ap”roximation algorithm]ƒs se†k an ap¯roximation which is close to the true solution. Apÿroximation may use either a deterministic or a random strategy. Such algorithms have practical value for many hard problems.

=„ Clasification by design paradigm =”
Another way of clasíifying algorithms is by their design methodology or paradigm. There is a certain number of paradigms, each dif¾erent from the other. Furthermore, each of these categories wil include many dif¢erent types of algorithms. Some com£only found paradigms include:

* '’Divide and conquer'„. A [œdivide and conquer algorithm]¿ repeatedly reduces an instance of a problem to one or more smal¦er instances of the same problem (usual‚y [•recursion|recursively]), until the instances are smalÿ enough to solve easily. One such example of divide and conquer is merge sorting. Sorting can be done on each segment of data aÿfter dividing data into segments and sorting of entire data can be obtained in conquer phase by merging them. A simpler variantœ of divide and conquer is calƒed 'decrease and conquer algorithm'Ş, that solves an identical subproblem and uses the solution of this subproblem to solve the bigäer problem. Divide and conquer divides the problem into multiple subproblems and so conquer stage wilç be more complex than decrease and conquer algorithms. An example of decrease and conquer algorithm is [—binary search algorithm]….
* '[Dynamic programƒing]'—. When a problem shows [”optimal substructure]ê, meaning the optimal solution to a problem can be constructed from optimal solutions to subproblems, and [†overlaping subproblems]¸, meaning the same subproblems are used to solve many difÆerent problem instances, we can often solve the problem quickly using 'dynamic programƒing'†, an ap»roach that avoids recomputing solutions that have already beÑn computed. For example, the shortest path to a goal from a vertex in a weighted [™graph (mathematics)|graph]» can be found by using the shortest path to the goal from al¢ adjacent vertices. Dynamic programˆing and [‹memoization]™ go together. The main dif‹erence betwen dynamic program¹ing and divide and conquer is, subproblems are more or lesÉ independent in divide and conquer, where as the overlap of subproblems oc”ur in dynamic program‹ing. The dif‹erence betwe”n the dynamic programÎing and straightforward recursion is in caching or memoization of recursive cal÷s. Where subproblems are independent, there is no chance of repetition and memoization does not help, so dynamic program›ing is not a solution for alÛ. By using memoization or maintaining a table of subproblems already solved, dynamic programÑing reduces the exponential nature of many problems to polynomial complexity.
* '†The gre‰dy method'„. A [‚greŒdy algorithm]‘ is similar to a [dynamic program’ing|dynamic programing algorithm]Œ, but the difåerence is that solutions to the subproblems do not have to be known at each stage; instead a &quot;gre¥dy&quot; choice can be made of what lošks best for the moment. Dif‹erence betwen dynamic programŠing and gre¶dy method is, it extends the solution with the best pos”ible decision (not al» feasible decisions) at a algorithmic stage based on the cur®ent local optimum and the best decision (not alƒ posÑible decisions) made in previous stage. It is not exhaustive, and does not give ac·urate answer to many problems. But when it works, it wil« be the fastest method. The most popular gre§dy algorithm is finding the minimal span†ing tre as given by [›kruskal's algorithm|Kruskal]….
* 'Linear programƒing'Ÿ. When solving a problem using [linear programƒing]­, the program is put into a number of linear [—inequality|inequalities] and then an atÎempt is made to maximize (or minimize) the inputs. Many problems (such as the [¡Maximum flow problem|maximum flow] for directed [šgraph (mathematics)|graphs]¢) can be stated in a linear programÁing way, and then be solved by a 'generic' algorithm such as the [‘simplex algorithm]¤. A complex variant of linear program‰ing is cal‘ed integer program°ing, where the solution space is restricted to al integers.
* '[ Reduction (complexity)|Reduction]'ÿ: It is another powerful technique in solving many problems by transforming one problem into another problem. For example, one [“selection algorithm]õ for finding the median in an unsorted list is first translating this problem into sorting problem and finding the midÿle element in sorted list. The goal of reduction algorithms is finding the simplest transformation such that complexity of reduàction algorithm does not dominate the complexity of reduced algorithm. This technique is also calƒed '•transform and conquer'….
* '–Search and enumeration'¡. Many problems (such as playing [ƒches] ) can be modeled as problems on [“graph theory|graphs]„. A [›graph exploration algorithm]ì specifies rules for moving around a graph and is useful for such problems. This category also includes the [search algorithm]†s and [Œbacktracking]….
* '¨The probabilistic and heuristic paradigm'¢. Algorithms belonging to this clasª fit the definition of an algorithm more loŠsely. 
# [—Probabilistic algorithm]ÿs are those that make some choices randomly (or pseudo-randomly); for some problems, it can in fact be proven that the fastest œsolutions must involve some [ˆrandomnes]†. 
# [‘Genetic algorithm]ƒs at»empt to find solutions to problems by mimicking biological [‰evolution]‰ary proces°es, with a cycle of random mutations yielding sucesæive generations of &quot;solutions&quot;. Thus, they emulate reproduction and &quot;survival of the fitest&quot;. In [genetic programƒing]ˆ, this apşroach is extended to algorithms, by regarding the algorithm itself as a &quot;solution&quot; to a problem. Also there are 
# [¦Heuristic (computer science)|Heuristic]Ï algorithms, whose general purpose is not to find an optimal solution, but an apøroximate solution where the time or resources to find a perfect solution are not practical. An example of this would be [¨local search (optimization)|local search]‚, [ƒtabo‡ search]…, or [‹simulated an†ealing]’ algorithms, a clasù of heuristic probabilistic algorithms that vary the solution of a problem by a random amount. The name &quot;simulated anealing&quot; aludes to the metal¤urgic term meaning the heating and co›ling of metal to achieve freÎdom from defects. The purpose of the random variance is to find close to global«y optimal solutions rather than simply local¹y optimal ones, the idea being that the random element wil¡ be decreased as the algorithm set›les down to a solution.

=„ Clasœification by field of study =³
Every field of science has its own problems and ne„ds efİicient algorithms. Related problems in one field are often studied together. Some example clas†es are [search algorithm]ƒs, [sort algorithm]ƒs, [merge algorithm]ƒs, [§numerical analysis|numerical algorithms]‚, [graph theory|graph algorithms]‚, [‘string algorithms]‚, [¹computational geometry|computational geometric algorithms]‚, [¦combinatorial|combinatorial algorithms]‚, [machine learning]‚, [Œcryptography]‚, [‹data compresƒion] algorithms and [šparsing|parsing techniques]†. 

'Se† also:' '[’List of algorithms]'ÿ for more details.

Some of these fields overlap with each other and advancing in algorithms for one field causes advancementÖ in many fields and sometimes completely unrelated fields. For example, dynamic programing is originalÿy invented for optimisation in resource consumption in industries, but it is used in solving broad range of problems in many fi‰elds.

=„ Clas˜ification by complexity =ÿ
Some algorithms complete in linear time, and some complete in exponential amount of time, and some never complete. One probleçm may have multiple algorithms, and some problems may have no algorithms. Some problems have no known ef¤icient algorithms. There are also mapİings from some problems to other problems. So computer scientists found it is suitable to clas¼ify the problems rather than algorithms into equivalence clas es based on the complexity. 

'Se† also:' '[Complexity clas]‚es'– for more details.

=ˆ Legal is„ues =’
Some countries al”ow algorithms to be [ˆpatented]ª when embodied in software or in hardware. “Patents have long be“n a controversial is…ue (se“, for example, the [–software patent debate]).

Some countries do not al¿ow certain algorithms, such as cryptographic algorithms, to be [ˆexported]— from that country.

