 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'an‰ual plant'† is a […plant]Š that usualy germinates, [†flower]’s and dies in one [„year]‡. 

An—uals are often used in [†garden]s Ìto provide splashes of color, as they tend to produce more flowers than peren‹ials. Some [„perenial plant|peren„ials]… and [ƒbienial plant|bien„ials]š are grown in gardens as an¾uals for convenience, particularly if they are not considered [‡hardines (plants)|hardy]¤ for the local climate. Also, many fo d plants are, or are grown as, an¢uals, including most domesticated [ŒCereal|grain]œs.

The life-cycle of an an‰ual can oc¥ur in a period as short as two or thre […month]ÿs in some species, though most last a bit longer. Vegetables grown in apartment container gardens can last up to two years, if —they are maintained indo­rs during the winter months.

Examples of an“ual plants include [ƒpea]ƒs, [‹cauliflower]ƒs, […basil]†, and [ˆmarigold]†s.

