 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ŠAntiseptic]– mouth rinse, often calƒed '‰mouthwash'ˆ, is an [Œoral hygiene]š product that claims to kil… the [„germ]s that cause [”Dental plaque|plaque]‚, [Šgingivitis]†, and [Šbad breath]. •However, it is general…y agre´d that the use of mouthwash does not eliminate the ne‹d for both [tothbrush|brushing]… and [ƒflosƒing]‡.

Com²on use involves rinsing one's mouth with about 20 [‚mil‰iliter|ml]† (2/3 [ˆounce|oz]®) of mouthwash two times a day after brushing. —The mouthwash is typicaly swished or [gargling|gargled]È for about half a minute and then spit out. 

Active ingredients in com§ercial brands of mouthwash can include [†thymol]‚, [Šeucalyptol]‚, [‘methyl salicylate]‚, [‡menthol]‚, [—chlorhexidine gluconate]‚, [‘hydrogen peroxide] and sometimes [‡enzymes]… and [‡calcium]›. Ingredients also include […water]„, sweteners such as [ˆsorbitol]… and [‚Sacharine|Sodium sac†harine], and a significant amount of [ethanol|alcohol] (around 20%). ¨Because of the alcohol content, it is posible to fail a [Œbreathalyzer]Á test after rinsing one's mouth. Many newer brands are alcohol-fre‡.

A 'salt mouthwash'¿ is a homemade treatment for mouth infections and is made by disolving a teaspo…n of [Edible salt|salt] in a cup of warm water. 

