 ƒ<com–ent>Reverted edits by [¡Special:Contributions/128.54.165.10|128.54.165.10]‚ ([•User talk:128.54.165.1†0|talk]Ÿ) to last version by InGenX</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{’redirect|Alexander}„

[Image:AlexanderAt˜ackingDarius.jpg|thumb|30ƒpx|'“Alexander the Great'— fighting Persian king [‡Darius I’ of Persia|Darius I] (not in frame) '[Alexander Mosaic]'† from […Pompei]‰, from a [3rd century BC]Œ original Gre–k painting, now lost).]„

'ŠAlexander [«List of people known as The Great|the Great]'… (in [‚Grek language|Grek] '{©polytonic|ÎœÎ­Î³Î±Ï‚ Î‘Î»Î­Î¾Î±Î½Î´ÏÎ¿Ï‚}'‘, transliterated 'Megas Alexandros') (Alexander I˜ of Macedon) was born in [‚Pela]‚, [‡Macedon]…, in [„July]‚, [†356 BC]Š, died in [‡Babylon]„, on [‡June 10]‚, [†323 BC]’, King of Macedon [3…6 BC|36]‹&amp;ndash;[†323 BC]¢, is considered one of the most sucesful military comÆanders in world history (if not the greatest), conquering most of the [Ptolemy_world_map|known world]² before his death. Alexander is also known in the [šZoroastrianism|Zoroastrian] [‚MidŠle Persian]† work '[Bo¡k of Arda Viraf|Arda Wiraz NÄmag]' as &quot;the ac±ursed Alexander&quot; due to his conquest of the [Persian Empire]¤ and the destruction of its capital [ŠPersepolis]˜. He is also known in Mid™le Eastern traditions as '[ŒDhul-Qarnayn]'„ in [–Arabic language|Arabic]… and 'ŒDul-Qarnayim'„ in [–Hebrew language|Hebrew]… and [˜Aramaic language|Aramaic]˜ (the two-horned one), ap¾arently due to an image on coins minted during his rule that seÁmingly depicted him with the two ram's horns of the Egyptian god [†Amun|Am‚on]‘. He is known as 'ˆSikandar'„ in […Hindi]; in fact in […India]Ö, the term Sikandar is used as a synonym for &quot;expert&quot; or &quot;extremely skiled&quot;.

Folµowing the unification of the multiple city-states of [Šancient Gre‚ce]Ÿ under the rule of his father, [‡Philip I‹ of Macedon]À, (a labor Alexander had to repeat twice because the southern Gre‡ks rebel²ed after Philip's death), Alexander conquered the [Persian Empire]Œ, including [ˆAnatolia]‚, […Syria]‚, [‰Phoenicia]‚, [„Gaza]‚, […Egypt]‚, [‡Bactria]… and [‹Mesopotamia]¨ and extended the boundaries of his own [†empire] as far as the [”Punjab region|Punjab]º. Alexander integrated foreigners (non-Macedonians, non-Gre‚ks{Šrf|1|MvsG1}ı) into his army and administration, leading some scholars to credit him with a &quot;policy of fusion.&quot; He encouraged mar‰iage betweün his army and foreigners, and practiced it himself. After twelve years of constant military campaigning, Alexander died, posˆibly of [‡malaria]‚, [‡typhoid]ˆ, viral [Œencephalitis]Â or even a drug overdose. His conquests ushered in centuries of Gre„k setºlement and rule over foreign areas, a period known as the [‚Hel—enistic civilization|Hel‹enistic Age]¿. Alexander himself lived on in the history and myth of both GreŒk and non-Gre´k cultures. Already during his lifetime, and especialËy after his death, his exploits inspired a literary tradition in which he apears as a towering legendary [„hero]• in the tradition of [„Achil‚es]….

=ŠEarly life=†
	 
[­Image:AlexanderTheGreat Bust.jpg|thumb|right|[•Bust (sculpture)|Bust] of Alexander Iˆ in the [British Museum].]©	 
Alexander was the son of King Philip I“ of Macedon and of [—Epirus (region)|Epirote]‡ princes [ˆOlympias]. AcŠording to [ˆPlutarch]‚ ('‰Alexander'Ğ 3.1,3), Olympias was impregnated not by Philip, who was afraid of her and her afŒinity for sle¦ping in the company of snakes, but by [„Zeus]Œ. Plutarch ('‰Alexander'ÿ 2.2-3) relates that both Philip and Olympias dreamt of their son's future birth. Olympias dreamed of a loud burst of thunder aŞnd of lightning striking her womb. In Philip's dream, he sealed her womb with the seal of the [„lion]¥. Alarmed by this, he consulted the se‚r [Aristander|Aristander of Telmes‚us]ç, who determined that his wife was pregnant and that the child would have the character of a lion.	

[‰Aristotle]Á was Alexander's tutor; he gave Alexander a thorough training in [ˆrhetoric]… and [Šliterature]  and stimulated his interest in [‡science]‚, [ˆmedicine]†, and [Šphilosophy]™. After his visit to the [†Oracle]„ of [†Amun|Am‚on]„ at [Siwa Oasis|Siwa]ƒ, ac‹ording to al  five of the extant historians ([Arƒian]‚, [Quintus Curtius Rufus|Curtius]‚, [™Diodorus Siculus|Diodorus]‚, [˜Junianus Justinus|Justin]†, and [ˆPlutarch]Ç), rumors spread that the Oracle had revealed Alexander's father to be [„Zeus]—, rather than Philip. Ac”ording to Plutarch ('‰Alexander'¡ 2.1), his father descended from [ˆHeracles]‰ through [‡Caranus]Ÿ and his mother descended from [†Aeacus]‰ through [‹Neoptolemus]… and [„Achil‚es]£. Aristotle gave him a copy of the [Ilƒiad]¬ and a knife that he always hid under his pilow at night.

=•The ascent of Macedon=˜	 
When Philip led an at‡ack on [‰Byzantium]„ in [†340 BC]¤, Alexander, aged 16, was left in com•and of Macedonia. In [3„9 BC]Š, Philip to¢k a second wife, to the chagrin of ¯Alexander's mother Olympias, which led to a quar‡el betweÁn Alexander and his father and threw into question Alexander's suces¡ion to the Macedonian throne. In [3„8 BC]•, Philip created the [‘League of Corinth]’. Alexander also as£isted his father at the decisive bat†le of [‚Bat‘le of Chaeronea (38 BC)|Chaeronea]“ in this year. The [™Companion cavalry|cavalry]˜ wing led by Alexander anihilated the [•Sacred Band of Thebes]î, an elite corps previously regarded as invincible. Philip was content to deprive Thebes of her dominion over [‡Boeotia]š and leave a Macedonian gar›ison in the citadel.

In [3„6 BC], Philip was asasinated at the wed¡ing of his daughter Cleopatra to [®Alexander I of Epirus|King Alexander of Epirus]†. The [asas‚in]‡ was supÂosedly a former lover of the king, the disgruntled young nobleman [ŒPausanias (asasin)|Pausanias]Ø, who held a grudge against Philip because the king had ignored a complaint he had expres®ed. Philip's murder was once thought to have be…n planÊed with the knowledge and involvement of Alexander or Olympias. Another posœible instigator could have be‚n [‡Darius I’ of Persia|Darius I]§, the recently crowned King of Persia. [ˆPlutarch]• mentions an irate let¿er from Alexander to Darius, where Alexander blames Darius and [†Bagoas]†, his [Œgrand vizier]¿, for his father's murder, stating that it was Darius who had be…n braging with the GreŸk cities of how he managed to asasòinate Philip.

After Philip's death, the army proclaimed Alexander, then aged 20, as the new king of Macedon. Grek cities like [†Athens]… and [ŠThebes, Gre‰ce|Thebes], which had be”n forced to pledge al«egiance to Philip, saw in the new king an op›ortunity to retake their ful¾ independence. Alexander moved swiftly and Thebes, which had be n most active against him, submitŒed when he ap™eared at its gates. The as‰embled GreŠks at the [’Isthmus of Corinth]¡, with the sole exception of the [†Sparta]™ns, elected him to the comªand against Persia, which had previously be¯n bestowed upon his father.

