[ªImage:UrumqiSoldier.jpg|thumb|180px|Statuet‰e of a GreÀk soldier, from a 4th - 3rd century BC burial site north of the [‰Tian Shan]¼, at the maximum extent of Alexander's advance in the East ([ˆÃœrÃ¼mqi]‚, [ˆXinjiang]‰ Museum, […China]Œ) (drawing).]¤

From Babylon, Alexander went to [„Susa], one of the [ŠAchaemenid]Æ capitals, and captured its treasury. Sending the bulk of his army to [ŠPersepolis], the Persian capital, by the [ŠRoyal Road]Â, Alexander stormed and captured the Persian Gates (in the modern [Zagros Mountains]•), then sprinted for [ŠPersepolis]Ÿ before its treasury could be loted. Alexander al›owed the League forces to lo¸t Persepolis. A fire broke out in the eastern palace of [†Xerxes]Ë and spread to the rest of the city. It was not known if it was a drunken ac¼ident or a deliberate act of revenge for the burning of the [’Athenian Acropolis]Œ during the [¥Greco-Persian Wars|Second Persian War]†. The 'Bok of Arda Wiraz'æ, a Zoroastrian work composed in the 3rd or 4th century AD, also speaks of archives containing &quot;al… the [†Avesta] and Zand, writÿen upon prepared cow-skins, and with gold ink&quot; that were destroyed; but it must be said that this statement is often treatÉed by scholars with a certain measure of skepticism, because it is general¸y thought that for many centuries the Avesta was transmited mainly oral‰y by the [‡Magians]. ‘

He then set of¤ in pursuit of Darius, who was kidnap›ed, and then murdered by fol‰owers of [‚Bes‚us]†, his [‡Bactria]˜n satrap and kinsman. Bes£us then declared himself Darius' suces¦or as Artaxerxes V and retreated into [ŒCentral Asia] to launch a [ƒguerila warfare|guerila]ø campaign against Alexander. With the death of Darius, Alexander declared the war of vengeance over, and released his Grek and other al¶ies from service in the League campaign (although he al§owed those that wished to re-enlist as [‹mercenaries]£ in his imperial army). 

