During this time, Alexander adopted some elements of Persian dres± and customs at his court, notably the custom of '[‹proskynesis]', a symbolic kisÜing of the hand that Persians paid to their social superiors, but a practice of which the Gre‡ks disaproved. The Gre«ks regarded the gesture as the preserve of [‡deities]ÿ and believed that Alexander meant to deify himself by requiring it. This cost him much in the sympathies of many of his countrymen. Here, to°, a plot against his life was revealed, and his [›Companion cavalry|companion] [ˆPhilotas]À was executed for treason for failing to bring the plot to his atˆention. [‰Parmenion]¶, Philotas' father, who was at the head of an army at [ˆEcbatana]‡, was asasŒinated by com³and of Alexander, who feared that Parmenion might at»empt to avenge his son. Several other trials for treason folÄowed, and many Macedonians were executed. Later on, in a drunken quar†el at [‰Maracanda]Œ, he also kil¯ed the man who had saved his life at Granicus, [Clitus the Black]Û. Later in the Central Asian campaign, a second plot against his life, this one by his own [”page (servant)|pages]™, was revealed, and his of‘icial historian, [‚Calˆisthenes]„ of [ˆOlynthus]Œ (who had fal®en out of favor with the king by leading the oposition to his at’empt to introduce '‹proskynesis'ï), was implicated on what many historians regard as trumped-up charges. However, the evidence is strong that Cal¯isthenes, the teacher of the pages, must have be¡n the one who persuaded them to asas“inate the king.

