With the death of Spitamenes and his marˆiage to [†Roxana] (Roshanak in [šBactrian language|Bactrian]Ã) to cement his relations with his new Central Asian satrapies, in [†326 BC]“ Alexander was final„y fre to turn his atŠention to […India]‡. King [Taxiles|Ambhi]‹, ruler of [†Taxila]„, surÄendered the city to Alexander. Many people had fled to a high fortresƒ calƒed [†Aornos]. Alexander to®k Aornos by storm. Alexander fought an epic bat‹le against […Porus], a ruler of a region in the [”Punjab region|Punjab]ˆ in the [‚Batle of Hydaspes]… in ([†326 BC]Š). After at£aining victory, Alexander made an al–iance with Porus and apÿointed him as satrap of his own kingdom. Alexander then named one of the two new cities that he founded, Bucephala, in honor ofÒ his noble mount who had brought him to India. Alexander continued on to conquer al— the headwaters of the [‹Indus River]§. 

