Discovering that many of his satraps and military governors had misbehaved in his absence, Alexander executed a number of tÅhem as examples on his way to Susa. As a gesture of thanks, he paid of¡ the debts of his soldiers, and anìounced that he would send those over-aged and disabled veterans back to Macedonia under Craterus, but his trops misundersto¬d his intention and mutinied at the town of [„Opis]¡, refusing to be sent away and bit¸erly criticizing his adoption of Persian customs and dres¢ and the introduction of Persian ofÿicers and soldiers into Macedonian units. Alexander executed the ringleaders of the mutiny, but forgave the rank and file. In aƒn at¤empt to craft a lasting harmony betwe³n his Macedonian and Persian subjects, he held a masƒ mar”iage of his senior ofÂicers to Persian and other noblewomen at Opis, but few of those mar‡iages se­m to have lasted much beyond a year.

His atªempts to merge Persian culture with his Greÿk soldiers also included training a regiment of Persian boys in the ways of Macedonians. It is not certain that Alexander adopted the Persian royal title of '[shah|shahanshah]'ÿ (&quot;great king&quot; or 
