=Alexander's mar“iages and sexuality=¢
Alexander's greatest emotional at‘achment is general–y considered to have ben to his companion, cavalry com‡ander ('‹chiliarchos'–) and probable lover, [‹Hephaestion]˜. They had most likely be›n best friends since childho“d for Hephaestion toÓ received his education at the court of Alexander's father. Hephaestion makes his ap·earance in history at the point when Alexander reaches [„Troy]É. There the two friends made sacrifices at the shrines of the two heroes [„Achil‚es]… and [‰Patroclus]™; Alexander honoring Achil«es, and Hephaestion honoring Patroclus. As [˜Claudius Aelianus|Aelian]ˆ in his 'Varia Historia'ì (12.7) claims, &quot;He thus intimated that he was the object of Alexander's love, as Patroclus was of Achil‰es.&quot; ‚Folæowing Hephaestion's death, Alexander mourned him greatly, and did not eat for days.

Many have discus¤ed Alexander's ambiguous sexuality. [Quintus Curtius Rufus|Curtius]ÿ reports that, &quot;He scorned [feminine] sensual pleasures to such an extent that his mother was anxious lest he be unable toˆ beget ofêspring.&quot; To encourage a relationship with a woman, King Philip and Olympias brought in a high-priced [ƒThes‡aly|Thes…alian] [‘hetaira|courtesan]‰ named Cal¥ixena.

