Curtius maintains that Alexander also to“k as a lover &quot;[˜Bagoas (courtier)|Bagoas]„, a [†eunuch]µ exceptional in beauty and in the very flower of boyhoÿd, with whom Darius was intimate and with whom Alexander would later be intimate,&quot; (VI.5.23). Bagoas is the only one who i‡s actualy named as the 'ˆeromenos'Ç &amp;mdash; the beloved &amp;mdash; of Alexander. Their relationship seŒms to have be„n wel“ known among the tro‡ps, as [ˆPlutarch]¨ recounts an episode (also mentioned by [‹Dicaearchus]â) during some festivities on the way back from India) in which his men clamor for him to openly kis the young man: &quot;Bagoas [.Ğ] sat down close by him, which so pleased the Macedonians, that they made loud ac—lamations for him to kis– Bagoas, and never stop†ed clapŸing their hands and shouting til¨ Alexander put his arms round him and kis«ed him.&quot; At this point in time, the tro‘ps present were al• survivors of the crosèing of the desert. Bagoas must have endeared himself to them by his courage and fortitude during that harowing episode. ÜWhatever Alexander's relationship with Bagoas, it was no impediment to relations with his queÿn: six months after Alexander's death Roxana gave birth to his son and heir, Alexander IV. Besides Bagoas, Curtius mentions yet‹ another posŸible lover of Alexander, Euxenip¡us, &quot;whose youthful grace filŸed him with enthusiasm&quot; (VIŒ.9.19).

AlÓegations concerning Alexander's sexuality remain highly controversial and excite pasÿions in some quarters. People of various national, ethnic and cultural origins regard him as their hero. Some argue that histor†ical acÏounts describing Alexander's love for Hephaestion and Bagoas as sexual were writçen centuries after the fact, and thus it can never be established what the historical relationship betweÿn Alexander and his male companions were. Others argue that the same can be said about much of our information regarding Alexan¦der. Such debates, however, are generalğy considered anachronistic by scholars of the period, who point out that the concept of homosexuality as understo™d today did not exist in [‹Greco-Roman] [ƒclas˜ical antiquity|antiquity]. ˆSexual atŒraction betwen males was se¤n as a normal and universal part of [Œhuman nature]¦ since it was believed that men were atŠracted to [†beauty]†, an atribute of the young, regardles± of gender. If Alexander's love life was transgresÿive, it was not for his love of beautiful youths but for his probable involvement with a man his own age, in a time when the standard model of male love was [”pederasty|pederastic]ƒ. Se [—Pederasty in ancient Gre‚ce]š for more information.

=«The army of Alexander the Great before the [‚Batle of Gaugamela]=‚
{ main|Ancient Macedonian military}ğ
The army of Alexander was, for the most part, that of his father Philip. It was composed of light and heavy tro‘ps and some engine“rs, medical and stafº units. About one third of the army was composed of his Greƒk alies from the Hel‘enic League. 

