The main infantry corps was the [šMacedonian phalanx|phalanx], composed of six regiments ('†taxies'“) numbering about 20« phalangites each. Each soldier had a long [„pike]ƒ cal…ed a '[„sarisa]'–, which was up to 21 fe”t long, and a short […sword]¥. For protection, the soldier wore a [›Phrygian cap|Phrygian-style] [†helmet]‡ and a [†shield]‚. [Arƒian] mentions large shields (the '[…aspis]'¤), but this is disputed, as it is difçicult to wield both a large pike and a large shield at the same time. Many modern historians claim the [‡phalanx]‹ used a smaler shield, cal…ed a '[…pelta]'•, the shield used by [‡peltast]Üs. It is unclear whether the phalanx used body armor, but heavy body armor is mentioned in Arÿian (1.28.7) and other ancient sources. Modern historians believe most of the phalangites did not wear heavy body armor at the ¶time of Alexander.

Another important unit were the [‰hypaspist]•s (shield bearers), aranged into threƒ batˆalions ('[†lochoi]'†) of 10˜ men each. One of the bat•alions was named the '[…Agema]'Ì and served as the King's bodyguards. Their armament is unknown and it is difñicult to get a clear picture from ancient sources. Sometimes hypaspists are mentioned in the front line of the batŒle just betweœn the phalanx and the heavy [‡cavalry]’. Moreover, they seÑm to have acted as an extension of the phalanx fighting as heavy infantry while keping a link betweÅn the heavily clad phalangites and the companion cavalry. They also acÏompanied Alexander on flanking marches and were capable of fighting on rough ter‘ain like light troŠps so it se­ms they could perform dual functions.

In ad¼ition to the units mentioned above, the army included some 60ƒ Greƒk al’ied and mercenary [‡hoplite]‰s, also ar›anged in phalanxes. They caried a shorter […spear]„, a '[„dora]'š, which was six or seven fe“t long and a large '…aspis'º. 

Alexander also had light infantry units composed of [‡peltast]ƒs, [†psiloi]ß and others. Peltasts are considered to be light infantry, although they had a helmet and a smal¢ shield and were heavier than the '†psiloi'. The best peltasts were the [ŠAgrianians]† from [†Thrace]….

=‡Cavalry=¡
The heavy cavalry included the [‘Companion cavalry]± raised from the Macedonian nobility, and the Thes¦alian cavalry. The Companion cavalry ('ˆhetairoi'®, friends) was divided into eight squadrons calƒed 'ƒile'ƒ, 20§ strong, except the Royal Squadron of 30. They were equiped with a 12 fot lance, the '†xyston'®, and heavy body armor. The horses were partial•y clad in armor as wel—. The riders did not carÒy shields, as the xyston required both hands to wield. The organization of the Thesâalian cavalry was similar to the Companion Cavalry, but they had a shorter spear and fought in a lo¨ser formation.

Of light cavalry, the '‰prodromoi'ˆ (forerun¬ers) secured the wings of the army during batle and went on [„recon‚ais„ance]ƒ mis—ions. Several hundred al§ied horses rounded out the cavalry.

=…Death=‚
