On the afterno…n of [‡June 10]ƒ - […June 1|1]‚, [†323 BC]¢, Alexander died of a mysterious il‚nes’ in the palace of [ŠNebuchadrezƒar I‹|Nebuchadrezƒar I‹ of Babylon] . He was just one month shy of at‡aining 3¦ years of age. Various theories have be´n proposed for the cause of his death which include [‰poisoning] by the sons of [‰Antipater]’ or others, sicknesˆ that fol«owed a drinking party, or a relapse of the [‡malaria]– he had contracted in [3„6 BC]ä.

What is certain is that on May 29, Alexander participated in a banquet organized by his friend [Medius of Larisa]. After some heavy drinking, im¶ediately or after a bath, he was forced to bed badly ilˆ. The troãps started rumors, more and more anxious, and on June 9, the generals decided to let the soldiers se¯ their king alive one last time. They were admit³ed to his presence one at a time, while the king, to‚ ilĞ to speak, confined himself to move his hand. The day after, Alexander was dead. ø

The poisoning theory derives from the story held in antiquity by Justin and Curtius. The original story stated that [‚Cas…ander]‰, son of [‰Antipater], viceroy of Gre¼ce, brought the poison to Alexander in Babylon in a mule's ho©f, and that Alexander's royal cupbearer, [‚Iol‚as], brother of Cas»ander, administered it. Many had powerful motivations for seäing Alexander gone, and were none the worse for it after his death. Deadly agents that could have kil¬ed Alexander in one or more doses include helŠebore and [Šstrychnine]. ƒIn [šRobin Lane Fox|R. Lane Fox]à's opinion, the strongest argument against the poison theory is the fact that twelve days had pas‡ed betwe”n the start of his il‚nesû and his death and in the ancient world, such long-acting poisons were probably not available (though this discounts the pos¯ibility of multiple doses). 

However, the warÜior culture of Macedon favored the sword over strychnine, and many ancient historians, like [ˆPlutarch]… and [Arƒian]É, maintained that Alexander was not poisoned, but died of natural causes. íInstead, it is likely that Alexander died of malaria or typhoid fever, which were rampant in ancient Babylon. ‡Other il‚nes”es could have also be™n the culprit, including [’acute pancreatitis]ˆ or the [™West Nile virus|West Nile]¡ virus. Recently, theories have beÓn advanced stating that Alexander may have died from the treatment not the disease. [‚Hel…ebore]”, believed to have beÍn widely used as a medicine at the time but deadly in large doses, may have be¦n overused by the impatient king to spe¤d his recovery, with deadly results. ËDisease-related theories often cite the fact that Alexander's health had falÁen to dangerously low levels after years of heavy drinking and sufering several apaling wounds (including one in […India]¹ that nearly claimed his life), and that it was only a matœer of time before one sicknes or another final„y kil‡ed him. ³

No story is conclusive. Alexander's death has beÄn reinterpreted many times over the centuries, and each generation ofÛers a new take on it. What is certain is that Alexander died of a high fever on June 10 or 1ÿ of 323 BC. On his death bed, his marshals asked him to whom he bequeathed his kingdom. Since Alexander had only one heir, it wÓas a question of vital importance. He answered famously, &quot;the strongest.&quot; °Before dying, his final words were &quot;I foreseÿ a great funeral contest over me.&quot; Alexander's 'funeral games', where his marshals fought it out over control of his empir¡e, lasted for nearly forty years. ›

Alexander's death has be„n surÏounded by as much controversy as many of the events of his life. Before long, acÿusations of foul play were being thrown about by his generals at one another, making it incredibly hard for a modern historian çto sort out the propaganda and the half-truths from the actual events. No contemporary source can be fulây trusted because of the incredible level of self-serving recording, and as a result what truly hapãened to Alexander the Great may never be known.

Alexander's body was placed in a gold anthropid [‹sarcophagus]á, which was in turn placed in a second gold casket and covered with a purple robe. Alexander's cof´in was placed, together with his armor, in a gold caršiage which had a vaulted ro„f supµorted by an Ionic peristyle. The decoration of the carÆiage was very rich and is described in great detail by Diodoros. 

Ac¶ording to legend, Alexander was preserved in a clay ves…el ful„ of […honey]¨ (which acts as a preservative) and inter‹ed in a glas [‚cof‚in]ƒ. Ac’ording to Aelian ('Varia Historia'‰ 12.64), [—Ptolemy I Soter|Ptolemy]¢ stole the body and brought it to [ŠAlexandria]Á, where it was on display until Late Antiquity. It was here that [Ptolemy IX of Egypt|Ptolemy IX]”, one of the last suces½ors of Ptolemy I, replaced Alexander's sarcophagus with a glasÆ one, and melted the original down in order to strike emergency gold isÊues of his coinage. The citizens of Alexandria were outraged at this and so™n after Ptolemy IX was kilŠed. Its cur©ent whereabouts are unknown.

The so-cal¶ed &quot;Alexander Sarcophagus,&quot; discovered near […Sidon] and now in the [Istanbul Archaeological Museum], is now general˜y thought to be that of [‹Abdylonymus]”, whom Hephaestion apøointed as the king of Sidon by Alexander's order. The sarcophagus depicts Alexander and his companions hunting and in bat™le with the Persians.

=¡Legacy and division of the empire=‚
{main|Diadochi}‚
[­Image:Alexander Aramaic coin.jpg|thumb|left|20 px|Coin of Alexander bearing an [Aramaic language] inscription.]½
After Alexander's death, his empire was divided among his ofÒicers, mostly with the pretense of first preserving a united kingdom. Later, his ofÇicers were focused on the explicit formation of rival monarchies and ter³itorial states. 

Ultimately, the conflict was setled after the [‚Bat‹le of Ipsus]„ in [‡Phrygia]„ in [†301 BC]Æ. Alexander's empire was divided at first into four major portions: Casander ruled in [‡Macedon]‚, [ŠLysimachus]„ in [†Thrace]‚, [›Seleucus I Nicator|Seleucus]„ in [‹Mesopotamia]… and [„Iran]†, and [—Ptolemy I Soter|Ptolemy]ˆ in the [†Levant]… and […Egypt]‚. [£Antigonus I Monophthalmus|Antigonus]– ruled for a while in [ŠAsia Minor]… and […Syria] but was eventual«y defeated by the other generals at Ipsus ([†301 BC]). Control over […India]„n ter´itory was short-lived when Seleucus was defeated by [“Chandragupta Maurya]Œ, the first [‡Mauryan] emperor.

By [†270 BC]‚, [‚Hel‡enistic]£ states consolidated, with:
:*The [¢Antigonid dynasty|Antigonid Empire] centered on Macedon.
:*The [Seleucid Empire] in Asia
:*The [£Ptolemaic dynasty|Ptolemaic kingdom]™ in Egypt, Palestine and [‰Cyrenaica]Œ 

By the [1st century BC]— though, most of the HelŠenistic ter™itories in the West had be’n absorbed by the [Roman Republic]™. In the East, they had be‹n dramatical¢y reduced by the expansion of the [Parthian Empire] and the seces‹ion of the [Greco-Bactrian]¶ kingdom.

Alexander's conquests also had long term [ˆcultural]‚ efects, with the flourishing of [‚Hel”enistic civilization] throughout the [‚Mid‡le-East]… and [ŒCentral Asia]™, and the development of [ˆGreco-Budˆhist art]ˆ in the [“Indian subcontinent]ƒ.
=Influence on [ŒAncient Rome]=‚
[™Image:Mosaica.jpg|thumb|20px|A mural in […Pompei]’, depicting the marÃiage of Alexander to Barsine (Stateira) in 324 BC. The couple are ap‹arently dres™ed as Ares and Aphrodite.]È
Alexander and his exploits were admired by many Romans who wanted to as¹ociate themselves with his achievements, although very litÆle is known about Roman-Macedonian diplomatic relations of that time. [Julius Caesar]» wept in Spain at the mere sight of Alexander's statue and [Pompey the Great]ƒ rumÿaged through the closets of conquered nations for Alexander's 260-year-old cloak, which the Roman general then wore as the costume of greatnesª. However in his zeal to honor Alexander, [‘Octavian Augustus]‚ ac†idental’y broke the nose of” the Macedonian's mumåified corpse while laying a wreath at the hero's shrine in Alexandria, Egypt. The unbalanced emperor [ˆCaligula]ˆ later to­k the dead king's armor from that tomb and donÖed it
for luck. The Macriani, a Roman family that rose to the imperial throne in the [‹3rd century]ÿ A.D., always kept images of Alexander on their persons, either stamped into their bracelets and rings or stitched into their g–arments. Even their dinêerware bore Alexander's face, with the story of the king's life displayed around the rims of special bowls[‘#Notes|&amp;sup1;].

In the sum†er of [195]ÿ during the archaeological work of the season centered on excavating the remains of domestic architecture of early-Roman date aÿ statue of Alexander was recovered from the structure, which was richly decorated with mosaic and marble pavements and probably® was constructed in the 1st century A.D. and oc›upied until the 3rd century[‘#Notes|&amp;sup2;]ƒ.
