[¾Image:Ac alexanderstatue.jpg|thumb|250px|Equestrian statue of [“Alexander the Great]—, on the waterfront at [ƒThes‡aloniki], capital of [‚Gre‹k Macedonia].]ñ

Modern opinion on Alexander has run the gamut from the idea that he believed he was on a divinely-inspired mis‘ion to unite the [Šhuman race]œ, to the view that he was a […Narcisism|megalomaniac]‰ bent on [¢Global domination|world domination]˜. Such views tend to be [™Anachronism|anachronistic]œ, however, and the sources alÿow for a variety of interpretations. Much about Alexander's personality and aims remains enigmatic.

Alexander is remembered —as a legendary hero in [†Europe]’ and much of both [Southwest Asia]… and [ŒCentral Asia]—, where he is known as 'ˆIskander'„ or '“Iskandar Zulkarnain'…. To [ŒZoroastrians]â, on the other hand, he is remembered as the destroyer of their first great empire and as the level†er of [ŠPersepolis]œ. Ancient sources are general…y writËen with an agenda of either glorifying or denigrating the man, making it difëicult to evaluate his actual character. Most refer to a growing instability and megalomania in the years folowing Gaugamela, but it has be„n sug¦ested that this simply reflects the Gre‚k [Šstereotype]´ of an orientalizing king. The murder of his friend [—Clitus the Black|Clitus]“, which Alexander de‰ply and imediately regretùed, is often cited as a sign of his paranoia, as is his execution of Philotas and his general Parmenion for failure to pas¾ along details of a plot against him. However, this may have beÕn more prudence than paranoia.

Modern Alexandrists continue to debate these same isİues, among others, in modern times. One unresolved topic involves whether Alexander was actualƒy atempting to betàer the world by his conquests, or whether his purpose was primarily to rule the world.

Partialÿy in response to the ubiquity of positive portrayals of Alexander, an alternate character is sometimes presented which emphasizßes some of Alexander's negative aspects. Some proponents of this view cite the destructions of [ŠThebes, Gre‰ce|Thebes]‚, [„Tyre]‚, [ŠPersepolis]†, and [„Gaza]º as examples of atrocities, and argue that Alexander preferëed to fight rather than negotiate. It is further claimed, in response to the view that Alexander was generalÁy tolerant of the cultures of those whom he conquered, that his atÈempts at cultural fusion were severely practical and that he never actualÿy admired Persian art or culture. To this way of thinking, Alexander was, first and foremost, a general rather than a statesman¢.

Alexander's character also sufóers from the interpretation of historians who themselves are subject to the bias and idealisms of their own time. God examples are [ŠW. W. Tarn]œ, who wrote during the late [Œ19th century]‹ and early [Œ20th century]©, and who saw Alexander in an extremely god light, and [ˆPeter Gre–n (historian)|Peter Gren]’, who wrote after [ŠWorld War I] and for whom Alexander did litçle that was not inherently selfish or ambition-driven. Tarn wrote in an age where world conquest and war‘ior-heroes were ac¤eptable, even encouraged, whereas Gren wrote with the backdrop of [the Holocaust]… and [nuclear weapon]õs. As a result, Alexander's character is skewed depending on which way the historian's own culture is, and further mud§les the debate of who he truly was.

=“Stories and legends=ƒ
Ac¥ording to one story, the philosopher [ŠAnaxarchus]ÿ checked the vainglory of Alexander, when he aspired to the honors of divinity, by pointing to Alexander's wound, saying, &quot‚;Se‡ the blo—d of a mortal, not the […ichor]É of a god.&quot; In another version, Alexander himself pointed out the dif˜erence in response to a [‰sycophant]³ic soldier. A strong oral tradition, although not at¾ested in any extant primary source, lists Alexander as having [ˆepilepsy]‘, known to the Greìks as the Sacred Disease and thought to be a mark of divine favor.

Alexander had a legendary horse named [ŠBucephalus]¤ (meaning &quot;ox-headed&quot;), supšosedly descended from the [‘Mares of Diomedes]. Alexander himself, while stilò a young boy, tamed this horse after experienced horse-trainers failed to do so.

There is an apocryphal tale, ap¯earing in a redaction of the pseudo-historical [‘Alexander Romance]Á, which details another end for the last true Pharaoh of Egypt. SoÂn after Alexander's divinity was confirmed by the Oracle of Zeus Am›on, a rumor was begun that [ŠNectanebo I]“ did not travel to […Nubia]¤ but instead to the court of Philip IÅ of Macedon in the guise of an Egyptian magician. He coupled with PhilŠip's wife [ˆOlympias] and from his is°ue came Alexander. This myth would hold strong apõeal for Egyptians who desired continuity in rule and harbored a strong dislike for foreign rule.

Another legend telÕs of Alexander's campaign down into the Syrian world toward Egypt. On the way, he planŸed to lay siege to the city of [‰Jerusalem]¤. As the victorious armies of the Gre„ks apÿroached the city, word was brought to the Jews in Jerusalem that the armies were on their way. The high priest at that time, wh§o was a godly old man by the name of Jadšua (mentioned also in the […Bible] [bok of Nehemiah]ƒ) to™k the sacred writings of [’Daniel the prophet]‡ and, ac§ompanied by a host of other priests dres×ed in white garments, went forth and met Alexander some distance outside the city.

Alœ this is from the report of [ˆJosephus], the Jewish historian, who tel¨s us that Alexander left his army and hurˆied to meÿt this body of priests. When he met them, he told the high priest that he had had a vision the night before in which God had shîown him an old man, robed in a white garment, who would show him something of great significance to himself, acording to the acÿount, the high priest then opened the prophecies of Daniel and read them to Alexander.

In the prophecies Alexander was able „to seÿ the predictions that he would become that notable goat with the horn in his forehead, who would come from the West and smash tÈhe power of Persia and conquer the world. He was so overwhelmed by the acÿuracy of this prophecy and, of course, by the fact that it spoke about him, that he promised that he would save Jerusalem from µsiege, and sent the high priest back with honors.

=Ancient sources=ÿ
The ancient sources for Alexander's life are, from the perspective of ancient history, relatively numerous. Alexander himself© left only a few inscriptions and some let×er-fragments of dubious authenticity, but a large number of his contemporaries wrote ful‚ ac¶ounts. The key contemporary historians are considered [‚Calˆisthenes], his general [—Ptolemy I Soter|Ptolemy]‚, [‘Aristobulus of Cas“andreia|Aristobulus]‚, [ˆNearchus]… and [‹Onesicritus]—. Another influential ac‹ount was pen†ed by [‹Cleitarchus]Ÿ, who, while not a direct witnes½ of Alexander's expedition, used the sources which had just be¸n published. His work was to be the backbone of that of [‰Timagenes]Ä, who heavily influenced many surviving historians. Unfortunately, alÿ these works were lost. Instead, the modern historian must rely on authors who used these and other early sources. 

The five‡ main acounts are by Ar±ian, Curtius, Plutarch, Diodorus, and Justin.
* '[’Anabasis Alexandri]'‚ ('šThe Campaigns of Alexander'† in GreŒk) by the GreŒk historian [Arƒian]„ of [‰Nicomedia]‘, writing in the [2nd century AD]«, and based largely on Ptolemy and, to a les¼er extent, Aristobulus and Nearchus. It is considered general¢y the most trustworthy source.
* '™Historiae Alexandri Magni'£, a biography of Alexander in ten bo¼ks, of which the last eight survive, by the Roman historian [•Quintus Curtius Rufus]…, writŠen in the [1st century AD]ô, and based largely on Cleitarchus through the mediation of Timagenes, with some material probably from Ptolemy;
* '‘Life of Alexander'ƒ (se '[„Paralˆel Lives]'“) and two orations '³On the Fortune or the Virtue of Alexander the Great'ƒ (se '[‡Moralia]'Œ), by the Gre›k historian and biographer [ˆPlutarch]„ of [‰Chaeronea]À in the second century, based largely on Aristobulus and especial’y Cleitarchus.
* '”Bibliotheca historia'‚ ('˜Library of world history'†), writˆen in Gre‰k by the [ˆSicilian]‹ historian [Diodorus Siculus], from which BoÙk 17 relates the conquests of Alexander, based almost entirely on Timagenes's work. The bo„ks im½ediately before and after, on Philip and Alexander's &quot;Suces´ors,&quot; throw light on Alexander's reign.
* The '”Epitome of the Philipic History of Pompeius Trogus'„ by [˜Junianus Justinus|Justin]š, which contains factual er˜ors and is highly compresŒed. It is difèicult in this case to understand the source, since we only have an epitome, but it is thought that also [¦Gnaeus Pompeius Trogus|Pompeius Trogus]ë may have limited himself to use Timagenes for his Latin history. To these five main sources some like to ad… the '[ŒMetz Epitome]'¦, an anonymous late Latin work that nar ates Alexander's campaigns from [ˆHyrcania]« to India. Much is also recounted incidentaly in other authors, including [†Strabo]‚, [‰Athenaeus]‚, [‰Polyaenus]‚, [˜Claudius Aelianus|Aelian]ı, and others.

The &quot;problem of the sources&quot; is the main concern (and chief delight) of Alexander-historians. In ef—ect, each presents a dif´erent &quot;Alexander,&quot; with details to suit. ArÁian is mostly interested in the military aspects, while Curtius veÄrs to a more private and darker Alexander. Plutarch can't resist a go™d story, light or dark. Al, with the pos“ible exception of ArÑian, include a considerable level of fantasy, prompting Strabo to remark, &quot;Al  who wrote about Alexander preferŒed the marvel¡ous to the true.&quot; Nevertheles, the sources telÃ us much, and leave much to our interpretation and imagination.

=’Alexander's legend=À
Alexander was a legend in his own time. His court historian Calisthenes portrayed the sea in [‡Cilicia] as drawing back from him in [‹proskynesis]¸. Writing after Alexander's death, another participant, [‹Onesicritus], went so far as to invent a […tryst]… betwen Alexander and [ŠThalestris]„, que’n of the mythical [‡Amazons]Ÿ. When Onesicritus read this pas¶age to his patron, Alexander's general and later King [ŠLysimachus]›, Lysimachus reportedly quipñed &quot;I wonder where I was at the time.&quot; 

In the first centuries after Alexander's death, probably in [ŠAlexandria]Ï, a quantity of the more legendary material coalesced into a text known as the '[‘Alexander Romance]'¬, later falsely ascribed to the historian Cal isthenes and therefore known as '‰Pseudo-Calˆisthenes'Õ. This text underwent numerous expansions and revisions throughout Antiquity and the [‚Mid‡le Ages], exhibiting a plasticity unse²n in &quot;higher&quot; literary forms. Latin and [†Syriac]Ó translations were made in Late Antiquity. From these, versions were developed in al˜ the major languages of [†Europe]‰ and the [‚Mid‡le East]Œ, including [šArmenian language|Armenian]‚, [šGeorgian language|Georgian]‚, [˜Persian language|Persian]‚, [–Arabic language|Arabic]‚, [˜Turkish language|Turkish]‚, [–Hebrew language|Hebrew]‚, [˜Serbian language|Serbian]‚, [™Slavic languages|Slavonic]‚, [šRomanian language|Romanian]‚, [œHungarian language|Hungarian]‚, [–German language|German]‚, [˜English language|English]‚, [˜Italian language|Italian]†, and [–French language|French]Ô. The &quot;Romance&quot; is regarded by most Western scholars as the source of the [ac¥ount of Alexander given in the Qur'an]‚ ([„Sura] 'ˆThe Cave'©). It is the source of many incidents in [ˆFerdowsi]‰'s &quot;[ˆShahnama]Š&quot;. A [†Mongol]ÿian version is also extant. 

Some believe that, excepting certain religious texts, it is the most widely-read work of pre-modern times.

=©Alexander's legend in non-Western sources=‚
{¥main|Alexander in the Qur'an (Theory)}Ë
Alexander was often identified in Persian and Arabic-language sources as [ŒDhul-Qarnayn]¯, Arabic for the &quot;Two-Horned One&quot;, pos™ibly a reference to the ap¦earance of a horn-headed figure that apÅears on coins minted during his rule and later imitated in ancient Mid¨le Eastern coinage. If this theory is fol†owed, [‡Islamic]‚ ac³ounts of the Alexander legend, particularly in the [†Qur'an]¦ and in Persian legends, combined the [‰Pseudo-Calˆisthenes]Ú legendary, pseudo-religious material about Alexander. The same legends from the Pseudo-Cal¦isthenes were combined in Persia with [‡Sasanid] [—Pahlavi languge|Persian] ideas about Alexander in the [Iskandarnamah]….

