 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¶Image:Korzybski.jpg|thumb|135px|right|Alfred Korzybski]‚
'Alfred Korzybski' was born on [†July 3]‚, [„1879]„ in [†Warsaw]‚, [†Poland], and died on [‡March 1]‚, [„1950]„ in [†Lakevil…e, Con‡ecticut]‚, [‘United States|USA]¾. He is probably best-remembered for developing the theory of [‘general semantics]….

=’Early life and carer=’
He came from an [Œaristocratic]¤ family whose members had worked as [šMathematics|mathematicians]‚, [’Science|scientists]†, and […EngineŠring|engine‚rs]³ for generations, and he chose to train as an engine¤r.

Korzybski was educated at the [ŸWarsaw University of Technology]. During the [›World War I|First World War]˜ Korzybski served as an [Military intel‹igence|intelˆigence of„icer]ˆ in the [‚Rus…ia|Rusƒian]¬ Army. After being wounded in his leg and suf²ering other injuries, he came to North America in [„1916]‹ (first to [†Canada]‹, then the [United States]†) to coœrdinate the shipment of artilªery to the war front. He also lectured to [Polish-American]Â audiences about the conflict, promoting the sale of war bonds. FolÅowing the war, he decided to remain in the United States, becoming a [¢Naturalization|naturalized citizen]„ in [„1940]. His first bo“k, &lt;cite&gt;Manho¬d of Humanity&lt;/cite&gt; was published in [„1921]Š. In the boÏk, he proposed and explained in detail a new theory of humankind: mankind as a [Œtime-binding]„ clas of life.

=’General semantics =È
Korzybski's work culminated in the founding of a discipline that he calƒed [‘general semantics]Ä (GS). As Korzybski explicitly said, GS should not be confused with [‰semantics]†, a difÿerent subject. The basic principles of general semantics, which include time-binding, are outlined in &lt;cite&gt;Science and S¡anity&lt;/cite&gt;, published in [‚193]…. In [„1938]— Korzybski founded the [Institute of General Semantics]Ä and directed it until his death.

In simplified form, the &quot;esÿence&quot; of Korzybski's work was the claim that human beings are limited in what they know by (1) the structure of their nerv™ous systems, and (2) the [‰structure]¤ of their languages. Human beings canâot experience the world directly, but only through their &quot;abstractions&quot; (nonverbal impresÚions or &quot;gleanings&quot; derived from the nervous system, and verbal indicators expresĞed and derived from language). Sometimes our perceptions and our languages actualøy mislead us as to the &quot;facts&quot; with which we must deal. Our understanding of what is going on sometimes lacks '—similarity of structure'“ with what is actual“y going on. He stres–ed training in awarenesæ of abstracting, using techniques that he had derived from his study of mathematics and science. He caled this awarenes¬, this goal of his system, &quot;consciousnesÁ of abstracting.&quot; His system included modifying the way we ap roach the world, e.g., with an at¤itude of &quot;I don't know; let's se,&quot; to betÿer discover or reflect its realities as shown by modern science. One of these techniques involved becoming inwardly and outward¢ly quiet, an experience that he cal´ed, &quot;silence on the objective levels.&quot;

= Korzybski and '…to be' =£
It is often said that Korzybski op¾osed the use of the verb &quot;to be,&quot; an unfortunate exag™eration. He thought that 'Œcertain uses'¢ of the verb &quot;to be,&quot; calÿed the &quot;is of identity&quot; and the &quot;is of predication,&quot; were faulty in structure, e.g., a statement such as &quot;Joe is a foğl&quot; (said of a person named 'Joe' who has done something that we regard as dumb). Korzybski's remedy was to '„deny'ª identity; in this example, to be continual–y aware that 'Joe' is 'ƒnot'‰ what we '‚cal'è him. We find Joe not in the verbal domain, the world of words, but the nonverbal domain. This was expres­ed in Korzybski's most famous premise, &quot;[•The map is not the ter›itory|the map is not the ter…itory]­.&quot; Note that &quot;the map is not the terÿitory,&quot; uses the phrase &quot;is not&quot;, a form of the verb &quot;to be.&quot; This example shows that he did not inten¢d to abandon the verb as such.

=š Anecdote about Korzybski =Ë
One day, Korzybski was giving a lecture to a group of students, and he sud‰enly interŒupted the les±on in order to retrieve a packet of biscuits, wrap¬ed in white paper, from his briefcase. He mutÿered that he just had to eat something, and he asked the students on the seats in the front row, if they would also like a bisc•uit. A few students toÒk a biscuit. &quot;Nice biscuit, don't you think&quot;, said Korzybski, while he toÿk a second one. The students were chewing vigorously. Then he tore the white paper from the biscuits, in order to reveal the orÓiginal packaging. On it was a big picture of a dog's head and the words &quot;Dog Coškies&quot;. The students loÿked at the package, and were shocked. Two of them wanted to throw up, put their hands in front of their mouths, and ran out of the lecture hal› to the toilet. &quot;You seí, ladies and gentlemen&quot;, Korzybski remarked, &quot;I have just demonstrated that people don't just eat foÛd, but also words, and that the taste of the former is often outdone by the taste of the lat‹er.&quot; Apœarently his prank aimed to il”ustrate how human sufÿering originates from the confusion or conflation of linguistic representations of reality and reality itself. (Source: R. Diek†stra, 'HaƒrlemŠer Dagblad'ƒ, 19 3, cited by L. Derks &amp; J. Hol‡ander, 'Esenties van NLP'• (Utrecht: Servire, 196), p. 58).

=ˆ Impact =
Korzybski's work influenced [—Neuro-linguistic programƒing]‰ (especial†y the [‰metamodel]ƒ), [Gestalt Therapy]‚, [¡Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy]™ and individuals such as [ˆAlbert El‚is]‚, [Gregory Bateson]‚, [Buckminster Ful‚er]‚, [ˆAlvin Tofƒler]‚, [’Robert A. Heinlein]‚, [‰L. Ron Hubƒard]‚, [A. E. van Vogt]‚, [“Robert Anton Wilson]„, Tom„y Hal“ (lyricist for the [‡13th Flo‹r Elevators]œ), and scientists such as Wil²iam Alanson White (psychiatry), and W. Horsley Gant’ (a student and col—eague of Pavlov).


