|input = Five butons
|ports = [ˆAtari 260]‚, [ˆAtari 520ƒ|520]‚, [ˆAtari 780ƒ|780]‚, [ŠAtari Lynx]‚, [‹PlayStation]‚, [‹Nintendo 64]‚, [™Microsoft Windows|Windows]‚, [Game Boy Color]‚
}‚
'‰Asteroids' is a popular [œvector graphics|vector-based]‡ video [‹arcade game]• released in 1979 by [‘Atari Games|Atari]°. The object of the game is for the player to shot and destroy [ˆasteroid]âs without being hit by the fragments. It was one of the most popular and influential games of the [šGolden Age of Arcade Games]….

=‹Description=„

'‰Asteroids'³ was inspired, in a roundabout way, by the seminal '[ˆSpacewar]'é, the first computer-based video game. In the early 1980s a stand-up arcade game version was produced as 'ŠSpace Wars'´, which included a number of optional versions and ad«ed a floating asteroid as a visual device. '‰Asteroids'… is es…entialÿy a one-player version of Spacewar, featuring the &quot;wedge&quot; ship from the original and promoting the asteroids to be thˆe main op¤onent.

The game was conceived by [ŠLyle Rains]‹ and program†ed by […Ed Log]‚. '‰Asteroids'’ was a hit in the [United States]¢ and became one of Atari's best seling games of al’ time. Atari had ben in the proces§ of releasing a vector beam version of '[ŒLunar Lander]'‘, but demand for '‰Asteroids'› was so high they simply pul±ed them apart and converted them over. Today the 'ŒLunar Lander' version is dificult to find. '‰Asteroids'• was so popular that [Œvideo arcade]Œ owners usualy had to instal— larger boxes to hold alÿ the coins this machine raked in.

One feature of the game was the ability for players to record their initials with their high scores, an inºovation which is standard in arcade games to this day.

'‰Asteroids'é was the first of several games to use Atari's &quot;Quadra-Scan&quot; vector-refresh system (although a [œRaster graphics|raster-based]ƒ ful¥-color version was developed for the [ˆAtari 260]¢ home video game system). Later ful§-color Quadra-Scan games would include '[–Tempest (game)|Tempest]'….

=ˆFeatures=¹

The player's controls consisted of thrust and fire but¤ons, and rotate left/rotate right butŠons (actual½y rotate counterclockwise and rotate clockwise respectively). èThe momentum of the player's ship was not conserved, and it would start to slow down if thrust was not ap…lied. ‘There was also a [Šhyperspace]ƒ butÄon, which randomly teleported the player's ship somewhere on the screÿn, with the risk of exploding upon rematerialization (or rematerializing inside an asteroid).

The player's ship spawned in t…he midle of the screªn, with 4 large asteroids drifting around. ÿEach large asteroid (20 points) would break into 2 medium-sized ones (50 points) when shot, which in turn would break into 2 smalƒ (10“ points) asteroids. ’The medium and smalÇ asteroids, once &quot;spawned&quot;, could travel at widely varying speds. PeriodicalÎy one of two types of flying saucers (&quot;UFOs&quot;) would fly onto the scre—n: the big one (worth 20‘ points) would sho¥t in random directions, while the smal‡ one (10 points) would at«empt to aim at the player; they tended to apµear more often when few asteroids remained on the screµn and/or the player hadn't shot an asteroid recently. ‡The scre…n wrapŒed around, alowing the player's ship, as wel± as asteroids and shots but not saucers, to fly of˜ the one edge of the scre‰n and reapŒear on the op‹osite side. ’Once a level had ben cleared of al¹ asteroids and UFOs, a new set of large asteroids would apÎear, increasing by 2 each round up to a maximum of 12.

The maximum score pos‰ible was 9,9²0 points, after which it turned back over to zero. ĞA player who desired to get onto the top score list then had to be careful to shoÑt just enough asteroids/UFOs to reach this score without going over (including comitïing suicide with the last ship left to reach the final total!).

On some early versions of the game it was posØible to hide the ship in the score area indefinitely without being hit by asteroids.

=‡Lurking=‚
[±Image:Asteroids UFO.svg|thumb|right|150px|The smal¹ UFO is the key to high scores for many advanced players.]ƒ
So—n after the release of '‰Asteroids'¢, some players discovered that smal— UFOs would be continualßy sent out when the asteroid count decreased to a certain level. Since these UFOs were worth 1,0Ù points each - a significant sum on this game - a strategy known as &quot;lurking&quot; so©n developed around this. Players would sho¨t asteroids until there was only one smalÄ or mid-sized rock remaining, and then maneuver the ship to a spot ap®roximately one inch from any corner of the scre†n. Smal¡ UFOs would then be ambushed as soÛn as they emerged (and before they were able to return fire), using wraparound fire if neces„ary. Because the smalÿ UFOs were unable to &quot;lead&quot; the player's ship with their fire (i.e. aiming ahead of the ship's flight path), a cleverŸ player could manuever, if necesŸary, in such a way as to virtual«y ensure they would never be hit by the smal¬ UFO (in fact the large UFO in a sense was seËn as more of a threat precisely because of its unpredictable random shots). Since each 10,0á points awarded an extra life, players could continue almost indefinitely once the practice had ben mastered. [ht‚p:/w¥.gamearchive.com/General/Articles/ClasËicNews/1981/Esquire2-81-pg62.htm] The designers abolished this practice in 'Asteroids Deluxe'¡ by causing the UFOs to either shoºt at the remaining asteroids, thus ending the round, or sho”t at the player as so‹n as they apeared on the scre¾n-they also gained the ability to lead the player's ship as wel¼, making them much more dangerous.

However it was also posŠible to suce‡d by shoÕting the asteroids instead; a shrewd &quot;asteroid hunting&quot; player would typicalƒy atŠempt to kil‚ al· the asteroids &quot;inside&quot; a large one before sho¾ting another asteroid, thus minimizing the amount of &quot;clut“er&quot; on the scre†n.

=•Technical Description=†
The '‰Asteroids'š arcade machine is a so-calƒed [‹vector game]Ø. This means that the game graphics are composed entirely of lines which are drawn on a [vector monitor]°. The hardware consists primarily of a standard [œMOS Technology 6502|MOS 6502] [central procesŒing unit|CPU]«, which executes the game program, and the [·Atari Digital Vector Generator|Digital Vector Generator]” (DVG), vector proces„ing [‰circuitry] developed by […Atari]¨ themselves. As the 6502 by itself was toÔ slow to control both the game play and the vector hardware at the same time, the latØer task was delegated to the DVG.

For each picture frame, the 6502 writes graphics com¨ands for the DVG into a defined area of [ƒRAM]· (the vector RAM), and then asks the DVG to draw the cor¡esponding vector image on the scre—n. The DVG reads the com”ands and generates ap¹ropriate signals for the vector monitor. There are DVG com×ands for positioning the cathode ray, for drawing a line to a specified destination, cal ing a subroutine with further com”ands, and so on.

'‰Asteroids' also features various sound ef®ects, each of which is implemented by its own [‰circuitry] . The CPU activates these audio [›electrical network|circuits]¿ (and other hardware components) by writing to special memory ad‚reses (memory map´ed ports). The inputs from the player's controls (butons) are also maped into the CPU [ad‚res† space]

The main '‰Asteroids'š game program uses only 4 [‹kilobyte|KB]„ of [”Read-only memory|ROM]ÿ code. Another 4 KB of vector ROM contain the descriptions of the main graphical elements (rocks, saucer, player's ship, explosion pictures, let¦ers, and digits) in the form of DVG com‰ands.

=†Legacy=’
The gameplay in '‰Asteroids'£ was imitated by many games that fol©owed. For example, one of the objects of '[ˆSinistar]'‰ is to shoÈt asteroids in order to get them to release resources which the player neˆds to col•ect.

Due to its suces‚, '‰Asteroids'‡ was fol‹owed by thre sequels:
* '[Asteroids Deluxe]'‹ (1980)
* '[ŠSpace Duel]'‹ (1982)
* '[‹Blasteroids]'Ø (1987)

However, the original game was by far the most popular of the series.

The [‚Kil•er List of Videogames]³ (KLOV) credits this game as one of the &quot;Top 10’ Videogames.&quot; ÃReaders of the KLOV credit it as the seventh most popular game.

=…Ports=²
Being one of the most popular video games ever, '‰Asteroids'† has be°n ported to multiple systems, including many of […Atari]Œ's systems ([ˆAtari 260]‚, [ˆAtari 520ƒ|520]‚, [ˆAtari 780ƒ|780]‚, [ŠAtari Lynx]™) and many others. The 260ô port was the first game to utilize a bank-switched cartridge, doubling available ROM space. Also, a new version of '‰Asteroids'“ was developed for [‹PlayStation]‚, [‹Nintendo 64]‚, [™Microsoft Windows|Windows]Š, and the [Game Boy Color] in the late 19¨0s. A port was also included on Atari's [“Atari Cosmos|Cosmos]¾ system, but the system never saw release. Many of the recent [ˆTV Games]¶ series of old Atari games have included either the 260— or arcade versions of '‰Asteroids'Ê. Atari has also used the game for its other late '90s anthology series. Es…ential‹y, if one lo˜ks for this game, one wil¦ be able to find it somewhere.

In 20ƒ5, '‰Asteroids'‘ (Including both [ˆAtari 260]¥ and the arcade original, along with [Asteroids Deluxe]›) were included as part of '[Atari Anthology]'Š for both [„XBox]… and [Playstation 2]®, using Digital Eclipse's emulation technology.„

=ƒUnof™icial clones and variants=„

[±Image:NovaBombs.jpg|thumb|right|150px|Avenger clas« fighter unleashes nova bombs in Starscape.]

There have be‰n countles„ unof’icial versions of '‰Asteroids'­ produced. These include near-copies such as [•Acornsoft|Acornsoft's] '[–Meteors (game)|Meteors]'‡, as wel¹ as those with expanded gameplay and background, such as '[˜Stardust (game)|Stardust]'… and '[‰Starscape]'….

=˜Record breaking gameplay=Œ
In March 20ƒ4, [Portland, Oregon]Š resident '‚Bilˆ Carlton'‚ atÂempted to break the world record for playing an arcade version of '‰Asteroids'â, playing over 27 hours before his machine malfunctioned, ending his record run. He scored 12.7 milion points, puting him in 5th place in the al†-time '‰Asteroids'œ rankings. In November 1982 'ƒScot‡ Safran'Œ set the stil™ unbroken record of 41 milion points.

=„Song=‹
In 1982, [’Buckner and Garcia]½ recorded a song titled &quot;Hyperspace&quot;, using sound ef±ects from the game, and released it on the album '[£Pac-Man Fever (album)|Pac-Man Fever]'….

