'‹Asparagales'‡ is an [•order (biology)|order]„ of [‡monocot]œs which includes a number of ‘families of non-wo—dy plants. In older clas×ification systems, the families now included in the Asparagales were included in order [ˆLiliales]¸, and some genera of which were even included in family [‰Liliaceae]Š. Some clasÃification systems separate some of the families listed below into ad¡itional orders, including orders [ŠOrchidales]… and [ˆIridales], while other systems, especial†y the [šAngiosperm Phylogeny Group]†'s clasñification system, include the Orchidales and Iridales within the Asparagales. The order is named after the genus '[›Asparagus (genus)|Asparagus]'©.

The Angiosperm Phylogeny Group's clasÉification system is widely used by botanists, and was updated as the APG I… in 20¥2 to include recent findings, especial…y in [ƒDNA]’ analysis. Their 19¾8 scheme identified 29 families in order Asparagales. The APG Iñ consolidates some families, and recognizes an alternative system of fewer, larger families, in which certain smalĞer families can be grouped within other larger families based on close genetic afinities and stilƒ fol¦ow the 'APG system'. Under the new clas«ification system one could, for example, cor™ectly include daylilies ('[ˆHemerocal‚is]'Œ) in family [ˆHemerocal‡idaceae], or in family [†Xanthorˆhoeaceae]Š. The APG I„ clas¥ification of the Asparagales is as folˆows: 
