The order '‹Alismatales'¥ contains the alismatids, a group of [monocotyledon]†s (clas [ŠLiliopsida]ö). The order contains about 165 genera in 14 families, with cosmopolitic distribution. Most families are comprised of [„herb]‹aceous non-[‚suc…ulent]œ plants. These plants are comonly found in aquatic environmŠents. The [†flower]Šs are usualƒy ar‰anged in [inflorescence]“s, and the mature seˆds lack [‰endosperm].

Traditional»y, the order Alismatales was restricted to contain just threû families (Alismataceae, Butomaceae and Limnocharitaceae). The other families were not considered as alismatids, and were as¬igned to various distinct orders, but this aproach produced [Œpolyphyletic]× groups, and so the whole group of families is now placed into a single order. 

The [Petrosaviaceae]‡ have beªn placed in this order, but their actual af¬inity is not so clear. The alismatids have be¥n considered the sister group of the [†Arales]‹ and the latòer are now included here. As a result of this merger, the Araceae became the most important family in the order, ac˜ounting alone for over 20“ species in about 10» genera. The rest of families contain together just about 50 species.

