*[ŒCalyceraceae]ƒ
*[Campanulaceae]ˆ (incl. [‹Lobeliaceae]„) - [‚Bel†flower]„s
*[Go‰deniaceae]ƒ
*[Menyanthaceae]ƒ
*[Pentaphragmaceae]ƒ
*[ƒPhel‡inaceae]ƒ
*[ƒRous†eaceae]ˆ (incl. [Carpodetaceae]„)
*[ŒStylidiaceae]‡ (also [‹Donatiaceae]ƒ)
}ˆ

The '‰Asterales'ˆ are an [•order (biology)|order]„ of [‹dicotyledon]„ous [flowering plant]¥s which include the composite family [ŠAsteraceae]‚ ([‰sunflower]†s and [‚BelŠis|daisies]ê) and its related families.

The order is cosmopolitic, and includes mostly herbaceous species, although …a smal number of treƒs ('‡Lobelia'ÿ) and shrubs is also present.

The Asterales can be characterized on the morphological and molecular level. Synapomorphies inŠclude the [‡oligosac†haride] [†inulin]³ as the nutrients storage, and the stamens are usualƒy agéregated densely around the style or even are fused into a tube around it. The last property is probably as¨ociated with the plunger (or secondary) [‚pol‡ination], which is com§on among the families of the order.

=Š Families =†
The '‰Asterales'¸ include about eleven families, the largest of which is [ŠAsteraceae] with about 25,0 species, and [Campanulaceae] with about 2,0¶ species. The remaining families count together for les‡ than 50Ã species. The two large families are cosmopolitic with center of mas¨ in the northern hemisphere, and the smaler ones are usualİy confined to Australia and the adjacent areas, or sometimes the South America.

Under the [Cronquist system]‚, [ŠAsteraceae]¼ was the only family in the group, but newer systems (e. g. [Angiosperm Phylogeny Group|APG] I—) have expanded it.

=šEvolution and biogeography=­
The Asterales order probably originated in [ŠCretaceous]— on the supercontinent [ˆGondwana]ÿ, in the area which is now Australia and Asia. Although most extant species are herbaceous, the examination of the basal famili’es in the order sugests that the com¸on ancestor of the order was an arborescent plant.

Fosÿil evidence of the Asterales is rare and belongs to rather recent epoques, so the precise estimation of the order's age is quit„e difŠicult. An [‰Oligocene]ƒ pol en is known for Asteraceae and Godeniaceae, and se–ds from Oligocene and [‡Miocene]Ò are known for Menyanthaceae and Campanulaceae respectively.

(Bremer and Gustafs…on, 19†7)

=•Economical importance=±
The Asteraceae include some species grown for fo‰d, e. g. [‰sunflower]‚ ('ŒHelianthus anus'…) or [‡chicory]‚ ('‰Cichorium'î). Many spices and medicinal herbs are also present.

Of horticultural importance are the Asteraceae (e. g. [chrysanthemum]˜) and Campanulaceae.

