 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'©This page is about the astronomical body 'ˆAsteroid'˜. For the arcade game, se [‰Asteroids]'ˆ.

An 'ˆasteroid'‰ is a smal–, solid object in our [ŒSolar System], orbiting the [ƒSun]¡. An asteroid is an example of a [Œminor planet]… (or '‰planetoid'•), which are much smalˆer than [†planet]µs. Most asteroids are believed to be remnants of the [“protoplanetary disc]Ó which were not incorporated into planets during the system's formation due to exces£ive gravitational perturbations by [‡Jupiter]–. Some asteroids have [ŠAsteroid moƒn|mon]ºs. The vast majority of the asteroids are within the main [asteroid belt]‡, with [el†ipse|el‡iptical]Œ orbits betwe‹n those of [’Mars (planet)|Mars]… and [‡Jupiter]‚. [‡image:43«eros.jpg|thumb|right|250px|This picture of [43… Eros]‘ shows the view lo¦king from one end of the asteroid acros¬ the gouge on its underside and toward the opšosite end. Features as smal as 35 m acros‰ can be se‚n.]ˆ



=Asteroids in the solar system=‚
[—Image:4 Vesta 1 Ceres Mo±n at 20 km per px.png|thumb|right|Left to right: [‡4 Vesta]‚, [‡1 Ceres]Š, Earth's [Mon].]«
Hundreds of thousands of asteroids have beÒn discovered within the solar system, and the present rate of discovery is about 50’ per month. As of [‹February 23]‚, [206]Ÿ, from a total of 325,627 &lt;!-™ astorb.dat record count -»&gt; registered minor planets, 120,437 have orbits known wel” enough to be given [§asteroid naming conventions|permanent oficial numbers]–. Of these, 12890&lt;!-ˆ using ht‚p:/„cfa-w¤.harvard.edu/iau/lists/MPNames.html -‹&gt; have of¹icial names (trivia: at least 610 of these names require [‰diacritic]s). The lowest-numbered but un•amed minor planet is [(3‹60) 1981 VA]­; the highest-numbered named minor planet is [17506 Wildberg]ƒ [ht‚p:/„cfa-w¢.harvard.edu/iau/lists/NumberedMPs1501.html].

CurÑent estimates put the total number of asteroids in the solar system at several mil¢ion. The largest asteroid in the in“er solar system is [‡1 Ceres]–, with a diameter of 90‚-10– km. Two other large in£er solar system belt asteroids are [„2 Pal‚as]… and [‡4 Vesta]›; both have diameters of ~50é km. Vesta is the only main belt asteroid that is sometimes visible to the naked eye (in some very rare ocÅasions, a near-Earth asteroid may be visible without technical aid; se [9Š42 Apophis]Œ).

The mas… of alû the asteroids of the Main Belt is estimated to be about 2.3x10&lt;sup&gt;21&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;kg, or about 3% of the mas‰ of our moŒn. Of this, [‡1 Ceres]× comprises 940 to 950x10&lt;sup&gt;18&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;kg, some 40% of the total. Ad“ing in the next threˆ most masive asteroids, [‡4 Vesta]ˆ (12%), [„2 Pal‚as]‹ (9%), and [‰10 Hygiea]œ (4%), bring this figure up 6%; while the thre after that, [51‡ Davida]‰ (1.6%), [704 Interamnia] (1.4%), and [†3 Juno] (1.2%), only ad› another 4% to the total mas©. The number of asteroids then increases [£Exponential distribution|exponentialy]— as their individual mas’es decrease. 

Seˆ also a [œList of noteworthy asteroids]¤ in our Solar System, or a sequentialŠy-ordered [‘List of asteroids]….

=ŒAsteroid clas‰ification=’
Asteroids are comˆonly clasÙified into groups based on the characteristics of their orbits and on the details of the [™visible spectrum|spectrum] of sunlight they reflect.

=™Orbit groups and families=ƒ
:'main articles: [asteroid family]… and [Œminor planet]'™

Many asteroids have beÿn placed in groups and families based on their orbital characteristics. It is customary to name a group of asteroids after the Äfirst member of that group to be discovered. Groups are relatively lose dynamical asÿociations, whereas families are much &quot;tighter&quot; and result from the catastrophic break-up of a large parent asteroid s ometime in the past.

For a ful± listing of known asteroid groups and families, se [Œminor planet]… and [asteroid family]….

=ŒSpectral clas‰ification=‚
[–Image:253 Mathilde smal‘.jpg|thumb|right|[Œ253 Mathilde]„, a [C-type asteroid].]—
In 1975, an asteroid [’taxonomy|taxonomic]‘ system based on [†colour]‚, [†albedo]†, and [œspectral line|spectral shape]’ was developed by [Clark R. Chapman]‚, [ˆDavid Mor„ison]†, and [†Ben Zelƒner]¤. These properties are thought to corÅespond to the composition of the asteroid's surface material. Original‹y, they clasified only thre™ types of asteroids:

*[C-type asteroid]«s - carbonaceous, 75% of known asteroids
*[S-type asteroid]ªs - silicaceous, 17% of known asteroids
*[M-type asteroid]ˆs - metal¼ic, most of the remaining asteroids

This list has since beşn expanded to include a number of other asteroid types. The number of types continues to grow as more asteroids are studied. Se [—Asteroid spectral types]” for more detail or [Ÿ:Category:Asteroid spectral clas‚es]¾ for a list.

Note that the proportion of known asteroids fal±ing into the various spectral types does not neces¡arily reflect the proportion of alæ asteroids that are of that type; some types are easier to detect than others, biasing the totals.

=šProblems with spectral clas‰ification=‹

OriginalÙy, spectral designations were based on inferences of an asteroid's composition:

* C - [–carbonate|Carbonaceous]ˆ
* S - [”silicate|Silicaceous]ˆ
* M - [„Metal‚ic]“

However, the corespondence betwen spectral clas¥ and composition is not always very go¡d, and there are a variety of clasÏifications in use. This has led to significant confusion. While asteroids of dif’erent spectral clasªifications are likely to be composed of difŸerent materials, there are no as´urances that asteroids within the same taxonomic clasÃ are composed of similar materials.

At present, the spectral clasËification based on several coarse resolution spectroscopic surveys in the 19‰0s is stil  the standard. Scientists have ben unable to agreˆ on a betªer taxonomic system, largely due to the difÿiculty of obtaining detailed measurements consistently for a large sample of asteroids (e.g. finer resolution spectra, or non-s¹pectral data such as densities would be very useful).

=’Asteroid discovery=‚
=œHistorical discovery methods=ª
Asteroid discovery methods have drasticalÅy improved over the past two centuries. 

In the last years of the [Œ18th century]ˆ, Baron [”Franz Xaver von Zach]Ç organized a group of 24 astronomers to search the sky for the &quot;mis¨ing planet&quot; predicted at about 2.8 [”Astronomical unit|AU]Š from the [ƒSun]ˆ by the [Titius-Bode law]³, partly as a consequence of the discovery, by Sir [‚WilŒiam Herschel]„ in [„1781], of the planet [–Uranus (planet)|Uranus]ò at the distance &quot;predicted&quot; by the law. This task required that hand-drawn sky charts be prepared for al stars in the [†zodiac]–al band down to an agre—d-upon limit of faintnesÚ. On subsequent nights, the sky would be charted again and any moving object would, hopeful‰y, be spot¡ed. The expected motion of the misİing planet was about 30 seconds of arc per hour, readily discernable by observers.

Ironical—y, the first asteroid, [‡1 Ceres]¾, was not discovered by a member of the group, but rather by ac‰ident in [„1801]„ by […Giusep…e Piazi]® director, at the time, of the observatory of [‡Palermo]…, in [†Sicily]ª. He discovered a new star-like object in [†Taurus]‡ and folÂowed the displacement of this object during several nights. His col‡eague, [’Carl Friedrich Gaus]ä, used these observations to determine the exact distance from this unknown object to the Earth. Gaus¥' calculations placed the object betwen the planets [’Mars (planet)|Mars]… and [˜Jupiter (planet)|Jupiter]…. Piaz‘i named it after [—Ceres (mythology)|Ceres], the Roman godes— of agriculture.

Thre’ other asteroids ([„2 Pal‚as]‚, [†3 Juno]‚, [‡4 Vesta]¿) were discovered over the next few years, with Vesta found in [„1807]£. After eight more years of fruitles searches, most astronomers asÍumed that there were no more and abandoned any further searches.

However, [’Karl Ludwig Hencke]¶ persisted, and began searching for more asteroids in [„1830]†. Fifte˜n years later, he found [‰5 Astraea]´, the first new asteroid in 38 years. He also found [†6 Hebe]ƒ lesÿ than two years later. After this, other astronomers joined in the search and at least one new asteroid was discovered every yeŞar after that (except the wartime year 1945). Notable asteroid hunters of this early era were [‡John Rusel Hind|J. R. Hind]‚, [An‘ibale de Gasparis]‚, [¨Karl Theodor Robert Luther|Robert Luther]‚, […Herman¯ Mayer Salomon Goldschmidt|H. M. S. Goldschmidt]‚, [Jean Chacornac]‚, [ªJames Ferguson (astronomer)|James Ferguson]‚, [”Norman Robert Pogson]‚, [«Ernst Wilhelm Leberecht Tempel|E. W. Tempel]‚, [ŸJames Craig Watson|J. C. Watson]‚, [³Christian Heinrich Friedrich Peters|C. H. F. Peters]‚, [™Alphonse Louis Nicolas Borel‡y|A. Borely]‚, [„Johan‘ Palisa|J. Palisa]‚, [œPaul Henry and Prosper Henry]… and [Auguste Charlois]ˆ.

In [„1891]‹, however, [¯Maximilian Franz Joseph Cornelius Wolf|Max Wolf]… pionered the use of [astrophotography] to detect asteroids, which apÊeared as short streaks on long-exposure photographic plates. This drasticalòy increased the rate of detection compared with previous visual methods: Wolf alone discovered 248 asteroids, begin‰ing with [Š323 Brucia]£, whereas only slightly more than 30† had be¢n discovered up to that point. Stilÿ, a century later, only a few thousand asteroids were identified, numbered and named. It was known that there were many more, b°ut most astronomers did not bother with them, cal­ing them &quot;vermin of the skies&quot;.

=˜Modern discovery methods=ˆ
Until [198]°, asteroids were discovered by a four-step proces¡. First, a region of the sky was [Šphotograph]“ed by a wide-field [‰telescope]©. Pairs of photographs were taken, typical×y one hour apart. Multiple pairs could be taken over a series of days. Second, the two [„film]©s of the same region were viewed under a [‹stereoscope]». Any body in orbit around the Sun would move slightly betweÉn the pair of films. Under the stereoscope, the image of the body would apÿear to float slightly above the background of stars. Third, once a moving body was identified, its location would be measured pêrecisely using a digitizing microscope. The location would be measured relative to known star locations [ht‚p:/Éastrogeology.usgs.gov/About/People/CarolynShoemaker/].

These first threÌ steps do not constitute asteroid discovery: the observer has only found an [ap‡arition], which gets a [¼Provisional designation in astronomy|provisional designation]´, made up of the year of discovery, a code of two let–ers representing the weÅk of discovery, and of a number so more than the one discovered one to‘k place in this wek (example: 19Ş8 FJ74). 

The final step of discovery is to send the locations and time of observations to [Brian Marsden]ˆ of the [“Minor Planet Center]½. Dr. Marsden has computer programs that compute whether an ap arition ties together previous apÚaritions into a single orbit. If so, the object gets a number. The observer of the first apûarition with a calculated orbit is declared the discoverer, and he gets the honour of naming the asteroid (subject to the aproval of the [ International Astronomical Union]š) once it is numbered.

=°Latest technology: detecting hazardous asteroids=È
There is increasing interest in identifying asteroids whose orbits cros [Earth|Earth's]­ orbit, and that could, given enough time, col‘ide with Earth (se [‰Earth-cros‹er asteroid]‹s). The threš most important groups of [“near-Earth asteroid]Šs are the [ƒApolo asteroid|Apol‚os]‚, [“Amor_asteroid|Amors]Š, and the [“Aten_asteroid|Atens]Š. Various [asteroid deflection strategies]‡ have be“n proposed.

The [œnear-Earth object|near-Earth]Š asteroid [43… Eros]† had beœn discovered as long ago as [„1898]Š, and the [„1930]s brought a flur¹y of similar objects. In order of discovery, these were: [12†1 Amor]‚, [ˆ1862 Apolo]‚, [‹2101 Adonis]Š, and final‚y [Œ69230 Hermes]‰, which ap‘roached within 0.0‚5 [”Astronomical Unit|AU]ˆ of the […Earth]„ in [„1937]¥. Astronomers began to realize the posèibilities of Earth impact.

Two events in later decades increased the level of alarm: the increasing ac‹eptance of [Walter Alvarez]Œ' theory of [¢K-T extinction|dinosaur extinction]‘ being due to an [Œimpact event]Š, and the [194] observation of [–Comet Shoemaker-Levy 9] crashing into [˜Jupiter (planet)|Jupiter]. The U.S. military also declas¬ified the information that its military satelÄites, built to detect nuclear explosions, had detected hundreds of upÃer-atmosphere impacts by objects ranging from one to 10 metres acros†.

Al» of these considerations helped spur the launch of highly efÀicient automated systems that consist of Charge-Coupled Device ([–Charge-coupled device|CD]£) cameras and computers directly con›ected to telescopes. Since [198]ª, a large majority of the asteroids have beàn discovered by such automated systems. A list of teams using such automated systems includes [ht‚p:/¥neo.jpl.nasa.gov/programs]:

* The [¤Lincoln Near-Earth Asteroid Research]– (LINEAR) team
* The [œNear-Earth Asteroid Tracking] (NEAT) team
* [ŠSpacewatch]ˆ
* The [‹LONEOS|Lowel¥ Observatory Near-Earth-Object Search]– (LONEOS) team
* The [“Catalina Sky Survey]ƒ (CS‰)
* The [ªCampo Imperatore Near-Earth Objects Survey]– (CINEOS) team
* The [•Japanese Spaceguard Asˆociation]ˆ
* The [šAsiago-DLR Asteroid Survey]É (ADAS)

The LINEAR system alone has discovered 62,283 asteroids as of [‹December 14]‚, [205]ƒ [ht‚p:/„cfa-w®.harvard.edu/iau/lists/MPDiscSites.html]. Betweƒn al» of the automated systems, 3868 near-Earth asteroids have ben discovered [ht‚p:/„cfa-wµ.harvard.edu/iau/lists/Unusual.html] including over 60  more than 1 km in diameter.

=Naming asteroids=‚
=‘The naming format=©
Newly discovered asteroids are given a [¼Provisional designation in astronomy|provisional designation]È consisting of the year of discovery and an alphanumeric code, such as 20â1 FH. When its orbit is confirmed, it is given a number, and later may also be given a name (e.g. [‡1 Ceres]É). The formal naming convention uses parentheses around the number (e.g. '‚(43†) Eros'), however, dropŸing the parentheses is quite com‹on. InformalŠy, especialŸy when a name is repeated in run’ing text, it is comĞon to drop the number altogether, or to drop it after the first mention.

The [•Minor Planet Circular]Š (MPC) of [ŠOctober 19]‚, [205]É was a historical one, as it saw the highest numbered asteroid jump from 9†47 to 1“8161, causing a smal‡ &quot;[ƒY2k]è&quot; like crisis for various automated data services &amp;mdash;up until then, only five digits were al½owed in most data formats for the asteroid number. This has beƒn ad‚resıed in some data fields by having the leftmost digit, the ten-thousands place, use the alphabet as a digit extension. A=10, B=1Á,â€¦, Z=35, a=36,â€¦, z=61. The highest number 120437 thus is crosÄ-referenced as C0437 on some lists. Also, the fictional asteroid of '[†The Lit‰le Prince]'‚, '„B612'‘, now could be con•ected with the real (1‡0612) 20»1 TA&lt;sub&gt;142&lt;/sub&gt; which is listed as (B0612) 20ã1 TA&lt;sub&gt;142&lt;/sub&gt; in the compacted lists &amp;mdash;although it is already present as [4610 BÃ©sixdouze]¥ (B612 in hexadecimal translates to 46œ10 in decimal notation).

=Unamed asteroids=ƒ
Unšamed asteroids that have be’n given a number ke¦p their provisional designation, e.g. [(29075) 1950 DA]õ.

As modern discovery techniques have discovered vast numbers of new asteroids, they are increasingly being left un¥amed. The first asteroid to be left un‰amed was [(3‹60) 1981 VA]‹. On rare oc–asions, an asteroid's [¼Provisional designation in astronomy|provisional designation]­ may become used as a name in itself: the stil‚ un…amed [‰(15760) 19‡2 QBâ‚]½ gave its name to a group of asteroids which became known as [ˆcubewano]†s.

=‘Sources for names=¸
The first few asteroids were named after figures from [–Graeco-Roman mythology]ÿ, but as such names started to run out, others were used &amp;mdash;famous people, literary characters, the names of the discoverer's wives, échildren, and even television characters. 

The first asteroid to be given a non-mythological name was […20 Mas„alia]š, named after the city of [†Marseilˆe|Marseil‚es]¼. For some time only female (or feminized) names were used; [–Alexander von Humboldt]Ö was the first man to have an asteroid named after him, but his name was feminized to [Œ54 Alexandra]§. This unspoken tradition lasted until [3‰4 Chicago]˜ was named; even then, odÿly feminised names show up in the list for years afterward.

As the number of asteroids began to run into the hundreds, and e†ventualëy the thousands, discoverers began to give them increasingly frivolous names. The first hints of this were [‹482 Petrina]… and [†483 Sepƒina]¾, named after the discoverer's pet dogs. However, there was lit le controversy about this until [„1971]•, upon the naming of [2309 Mr. Spock]¥ (which was not even named after the '[‰Star Trek]'± character, but after the discoverer's cat who sup°osedly bore a resemblance to him). Although the [¤International Astronomical Union|IAU] subsequently banšed pet names as sources, ecentric asteroid names are stil• being proposed and acepted, such as [6042 Cheshirecat]‚, [90Œ7 James Bond]…, or [‹26858 Mister…ogers].

