 ¡<text xml:space="preserve">Generalƒy, '„to al…ocute'Ÿ means &quot;to speak out formalˆy.&quot; In the field of [‹apologetics]ƒ, al‘ocution is generaly done in defense of a belief. –In politics, one may al§ocute before a legislative body in an ef§ort to influence their position on an isƒue. ”In law, it is generalšy meant to state specificalİy and in detail what one did &lt;nowiki&gt;and/or&lt;/nowiki&gt; why, often in relation to comisºion of a crime.

In most jurisdictions, a defendant is alŠowed the oportunity to alÁocute &amp;mdash; that is, explain himself, before sentence is pasƒed. ŞSome jurisdictions hold this as an absolute right, and in its absence, a sentence may potentialÑy be overturned, with the result that a new sentencing hearing must be held.

AlÏocution is sometimes required of a defendant who pleads guilty to a crime in a [Œplea bargain]¤ in exchange for a reduced sentence. “In this instance, alÃocution can serve to provide closure for victims or their families. ÜIn principle, it removes any doubt as to the exact nature of the defendant's guilt in the matƒer. •However, there have be¥n many cases in which the defendant al¤ocuted to a crime that he did not com¹it, often because this is a requirement to receiving a les er sentence.

The term &quot;al—ocution&quot; is generalÓy only in use in jurisdictions in the United States, though there are similar proces˜es in other nations.

=‚ Se† also =„
