 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'af†idavit'© is a formal sworn statement of fact, writšen down, signed, and witnesÈed (as to the veracity of the signature) by a taker of oaths, such as a [notary public]. The name is [Medieval Latin]„ for '™he has declared upon oath'‘.

One use of afidavits is to al¥ow evidence to be gathered from witnesÓes or participants that may not be available to testify in person before the court. 

In American [jurisprudence]™, it is very unusual to alŠow an unsup‡orted afÀidavit to be entered into evidence (as the person sworn in the afœidavit is not subject to crosÎ-examination) with regard to material facts which may be dispositive of the matƒer [†at bar]. AfÇidavits from persons who are dead or otherwise incapacitated, or who canšot be located or made to apŒear may be acœepted by the court, but usualšy only in the presence of [‚cor’oborating evidence]. A formerly writ„en afœidavit, which reflected a betÙer grasp of the facts closer in time to the actual events, may be used to refresh a witnes†' recol‡ection. Materials used to refresh recolection are admis­ible as evidence.

Some types of motions wil‰ not be ac•epted by a court unles‚ acÃompanied by an independent sworn statement or other evidence, in supŒort of the ne‘d for the motion. œIn such a case, the court wil‚ acˆept an af˜idavit from the filing at‹orney in supŸort of the motion, as certain asšumptions are made, to wit: …The af¬idavit in place of sworn testimony promotes [judicial economy]. ‘The lawyer is an [of‘icer of the court]ï and knows that a false swearing by him, if found out, could be grounds for severe penalty up to and including [Šdisbarment]. The lawyer if calíed upon would be able to present independent and more detailed evidence to prove the facts set forth in his afidavit.



=— In the United Kingdom =ƒ
Af­idavits are made by writing &quot;I (state ful“ name) of (insert ad‚resµ)on this date (date in words) make oath and say as folƒows.‡&quot;. After this has be…n writ¶en, the facts to be sworn are listed, in pros or in bulŠet points.  The document is then taken to a [‚comisioner for oaths]‡ (most [Šsolicitors]Œ are also comis’ioners for oaths). ‡They wil¢ then ask you to swear on a holy boœk particular to your faith ([‘The New Testament]‚, [‘The Old Testament]†, the [†Qur'an]§, etc) and ask you to verify what has be‘n stated.

An Afµidavit is of equivalent value to sworn testimony.

