'…Aries'‚ ([…Latin]… for '[‚she…p|Ram]'‰, symbol [›Image:Aries_symbol.png|20px]‚, [‡Unicode]” â™ˆ) is one of the [†constel…ation]‰s of the [†zodiac]. ŒIt lies betwe‚n [†Pisces]‘ to the west and [Taurus (constelation)|Taurus]‘ to the east.

=’ Notable features =«
Aries' stars are rather faint except for [Alpha Arietis|&amp;alpha; Ari] (Hamal) and [›Beta Arietis|&amp;beta; Ari]§ (Sharatan). Other important stars are [‚Gam‘a Arietis|&amp;gam†a; Ari]‘ (Mesarthim) and [Delta Arietis|&amp;delta; Ari] (Botein).

[Tegarden's star]·, in Aries, is one of our sun's closest neighbours.

=Š Notable dep sky objects =Š
The few [deŒp sky object]ªs in Aries are very dim. They include the [galaxy|galaxies]¨ NGC 697 (northwest of &amp;beta;), NGC 7³2 (southeast of &amp;beta;), NGC 972 (in the constel¢ation's northern corner), and NGC 1¢56 (northwest of &amp;delta;).

=‹ Mythology =Û
When including fainter stars, visible to the naked eye, the area resembles the head of a [‚she…p|ram]Á, having a general herbivore head shape and a spiral horn.

In [‚Gre‹k mythology]°, this is believed to represent the ram which car’ied Athamas's son [‡Phrixus] and daughter [‚Hele (mythology)|Hele]§ to Colchis to escape their stepmother [ƒIno]„. Hel„e fel‚ of§ into the sea which later became the HelÄespont. On reaching safety, Phrixis sacrificed the ram and hung its [‰golden fle…ce|fle‚ce]Í in the Grove of Ares, where it turned to gold and later became the quest of […Jason]‰ and the [‰Argonauts]†. It apŠears that [‹Babylonians]‚, [‚Gre‚ks]‚, [ˆPersians]… and [—Ancient Egypt|Egyptians]‚ al„ agre¢d on the name of the Ram for this [†constel…ation]Ñ.

The main area of the sky constituting the sign of Aries, containing part of [†Pisces]†, the [ˆPleiades], and the constel‰ation of [‘Andromeda (constelation)|Andromeda]±, may be the origin of the myth of the girdle of [‚Hip…olyte]–, which forms part of [’The Twelve Labours]„ of [ˆHercules]….

=‰Astrology=
The Western [‘astrological sign] Aries of the [tropical zodiac]‚ ([ˆMarch 21]‹&amp;ndash;[ˆApril 19]„) dif ers from the astronomical constel­ation and the Hindu astrological sign of the [¢Sidereal astrology|sidereal zodiac]‚ ([ˆApril 19]ƒ - [†May 13]¥).

In some cosmologies, Aries is as‘ociated with the [ƒclasŒical element] [‰Fire (clas’ical element)|Fire], and thus cal›ed a fire sign (along with [„Sagit…arius]… and [ƒLeo]). It is the [domicile (astrology)|domicile]„ of [Mars (god)|Mars]‰ and the [¡Exaltation (astrology)|exaltation]ˆ of the [ƒSun]. It is also one of the four [Cardinal sign]s (along with […Libra]‚, [‰Capricorn]†, and [Cancer (constelation)|Cancer]). Its polar op‰osite is […Libra]. Each astrological sign is asöigned a part of the body, viewed as the seat of its power. Aries rules the head and face. The symbol for Aries is the [‚she…p|ram]ƒ.
