'ˆAquarius'‚ ([…Latin]‰ for the '[…Water]‡-bearer'„ or 'ŠCup-bearer'‰, symbol [™Image:Aquarius_symbol_smal‰.png|20px]‚, [‡Unicode]¢ â™’) is the eleventh sign of the [†zodiac], situated betwe™n Capricornus and Pisces. Its symbol is [™Image:Aquarius_symbol_smal‰.png|20px]İ, representing part of a stream of water. 

Aquarius is one of the oldest recognized constel¤ations along the zodiac, the sun's ap¬arent path. It is found in a region often cal‡ed the [“Sea (astronomy)|Sea]¦ due to its profusion of watery constelations such as […Cetus]‚, [†Pisces]‚, [Eridanus (constelation)|Eridanus]´, etc. Sometimes, the river Eridanus is depicted spil¤ing from Aquarius' watering pot.

=Š Notable dep sky objects =
There are thre [deŒp sky object]’s that are on the [‚Mesier object|Mes‹ier catalog]†, the [“Globular Cluster M2]‚, [”Globular Cluster M72]Š, and the [‚Mesier Object 73|Open Cluster M73]‰.

Two [planetary nebula]™e are found in Aquarius: […NGC 709]„, cal‡ed the [Saturn Nebula]Ÿ due to its resemblance to the [–Saturn (planet)|planet]¥, to the southeast of &amp;eta; Aquari†; and [ˆNGC 7293], the famous [ŒHelix Nebula] , southwest of &amp;delta; Aquari….

=‰ History =Œ
The constel‹ation was im‘ortalized in the […1960s]‘, proclaimed the [Age of Aquarius]è. However, there is no standard definition for astrological ages, so the age of Aquarius could begin in [„2150]‰ or even [260]˜, depending on the prefered definition. šBased on the modern constel”ation boundaries of [†Pisces]· and Aquarius, the age of Aquarius would begin around 26ÿ0.

However, with so much of modern society reflecting the qualities of Aquarius most astrologers believe that this era has bˆegun. MasÀ production, electricity, flight and space travel, electronic comÿunications including computers the Internet, even the growing movement against capitalism in favour of a more socialist system ¡of humanitarian development are al¦ related to the Aquarian paradigm.

=‹ Mythology =¯
The best-known myth identifies Aquarius with [Ganymede (mythology)|Ganymede], a beautiful youth with whom [„Zeus]ƒ felÊ in love, and whom he (in the guise of an eagle, represented as the constel†ation [Aquila (constelation)|Aquila]„) car…ied of© to Olympus to be cupbearer to the gods. [Crater (constelation)|Crater]¸ is sometimes identified as his cup. 

Aquarius generalÿy resembles the figure of a man, and when considering fainter humanly visible stars, it takes on the image of a man with a buckäet from which is pouring a stream. Aquarius was also identified as the pourer of the waters which flo•ded the earth in the [›Deluge (mythology)|Great Flod]‰, in the [‚Gre–k mythology|ancient Gre‰k version]¡ of the myth. As such, the constel†ation [Eridanus (constelation)|Eridanus]ë was sometimes identified as being a river poured out by Aquarius.

It may also, together with the constel†ation [Pegasus (constelation)|Pegasus]«, be part of the origin of the myth of the [‘Mares of Diomedes]•, which forms one of [’The Twelve Labours]„ of [ˆHeracles]‡. Its as·ociation with pouring out rivers, and the nearby constel‰ation of [‹Capricornus]§, may be the source of the myth of the [Augean stable]©, which forms another of the labours.

=‰Astrology=
The Western [‘astrological sign]‘ Aquarius of the [tropical zodiac]‚ ([ŠJanuary 20]ƒ - [‹February 18]„) dif ers from the astronomical constel­ation and the Hindu astrological sign of the [¢Sidereal astrology|sidereal zodiac]‚ ([‹February 16]ƒ - [†March 1]¨).

In some cosmologies, Aquarius is as‘ociated with the [ƒclasŒical element] [ˆair (clas‘ical element)|Air], and thus cal•ed an Air Sign (with […Libra]… and [†Gemini]¶). It is also one of the four Fixed signs (along with [ƒLeo]‚, [Scorpius|Scorpio]†, and [†Taurus]). Its polar op¤osite is Leo. It is the domicile of [–Saturn (planet)|Saturn]– (since its discovery [–Uranus (planet)|Uranus]† has beên considered Aquarius' ruling or co-ruling planet by many modern astrologers). Each astrological sign is asÎigned a part of the body, viewed as the seat of its power. Aquarius rules the [’circulatory system]† as welˆ as the [†ankles]¡. The symbol for Aquarius is the [Œwater bearer]….

