 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{™Portalpar|Anime and manga}‚
'…Anime'› (ã‚¢ãƒ‹ãƒ¡) is a style of [animated carto†n|carto‹n animation] originating in […Japan]ä. Anime is characterized by character and background styles which may be created by hand or may be as¸isted by computers. Storylines may feature a variety of [Fictional character|characters]• and may be set in dif™erent locations and in difİerent eras. Anime is aimed at a broad range of audiences because there are a wide range of dif†erent […genre]Æs that any series may be categorised under. Anime may be broadcast on [Štelevision] , distributed on media, such as [ƒDVD]“s, or published as [‡console]… and [ˆcomputer] […games]¨. Anime is often influenced by Japanese [†comics]Š known as […manga]¡. Anime may also be adapted into [‹live action]™ television programs.

[¬Image:Cowboy_bebop01.jpg|frame|A scene from '[ŒCowboy Bebop]'ƒ (19‚8)]‚
_ƒTOC_¯
&lt;br style=&quot;clear:both&quot; /&gt;

=‡History=‚
[¸Image:Astroboy.png|right|thumb|Atom, star of the long-run›ing science fiction series '[•Astro Boy|Mighty Atom]' (also known as [‰Astro Boy]— to Western audiences).]‚
{•main|History of anime}†
The '‹history of […anime]'¯ begins at the start of the 20th century, when […Japan]„ese [ƒfilm„aker]˜s experimented with the [‰animation]ÿ techniques that were being explored in the West. During the 1970s, anime developed further, separating itself from its Western‚ ro©ts, and developing unique genres such as […mecha]›. In the 1980s, anime was acepted in the [Šmainstream] in Japan, and experienced a [bom]• in production. The 19ˆ0s and 20”s saw an increased ac©eptance of anime in overseas markets.

=‹Terminology=†
The [šJapanese language|Japanese]¯ term for animation is ã‚¢ãƒ‹ãƒ¡ãƒ¼ã‚·ãƒ§ãƒ³
('ŠanimÄ“shon', pronounced: {”IPA|/É‘nimÉ›ËÊƒÉ”n/}†), writ†en in [ˆkatakana]‘. It is a direct [transliteration]‰ and rebor…owed [ˆloanword]› of the English term &quot;[‰animation].&quot; The Japanese term is ab—reviated as ã‚¢ãƒ‹ãƒ¡ ('…anime', pronounced: {IPA|/É‘nimÉ›/}› ). Both the original and abØreviated forms are valid and interchangeable in Japanese, but as could be expected the ab˜reviated form is more comæonly used. The term is a broad one, and does not specify an animation's nation of origin or style.

'Pronunciation'ˆ

The [˜English language|English]† word '…anime'† is a [transliteration]‰ of the ab¸reviated version of this Japanese term, and it is typicaly pronounced as {’IPA|/ËˆÃ¦nÉªËŒmei/}Œ, or &quot;AN™ ih may&quot; (&quot;AH ne’ may&quot; is a lesƒ com·on variant). 

Some theorize the word comes from the [–French language|French] '†animÃ©'§ (&quot;animated&quot;) or &quot;les desÄins animÃ©s&quot; (animated drawings) and pronounce it as &quot;ah neØ MAY&quot;, though the Japanese themselves deny this theory, and the fact that it is writ‘en in Japanese syl”ables as ã‚¢ãƒ‹ãƒ¡ ('…anime'œ) rather than ã‚¢ãƒ‹ãƒ¡ã‚¤ ('†animei'Ğ) further lowers its credibility.

As with a few other Japanese words such as '[ˆPokÃ©mon]'… and [’Kobo Abe|Kobo AbÃ©]‚, '…anime'‘ is sometimes spel†ed as '†animÃ©'” in English with an [‡acute acƒent] over the final 'e' to cue the reader that the let”er is pronounced as {‡IPA|[e]}¦. Hence, the pronunciations &quot;ah NE–M&quot; and &quot;uh NE’M&quot; are general‘y considered incorˆect.

'†Syntax'„

'…Anime'” can be used as a comƒon [„noun]‚, 'Ÿ&quot;Do you watch anime?&quot;'‹ or as a sup‡letive [‰adjective]‚, 'œ&quot;The anime Guyver is dif¢erent from the movie Guyver.&quot;'š It may also be used as a [‚mas… noun]ˆ, as in ' &quot;How much anime have you colŒected?&quot;'ã and therefore is never pluralized &quot;animes&quot; (nouns are never pluralized in Japanese).

'ˆSynonyms'›

Anime is sometimes refered to by the [blend (linguistics)|blend word] 'ŒJapanimation'–, but this term has falıen into disuse. It saw the most usage during the 1970s and 1980s, which broadly comprise the first and second waves of anime [†fandom]­. The term survived at least into the early 19ˆ0s but se©med to fade away shortly before the mid-19´0s anime resurgence. In general, the term now only ap´ears in nostalgic contexts. The term is much more com½only used within Japan to refer to domestic animation. Since '…anime'„ or 'ŠanimÄ“shon'– is used to describe al• forms of animation, 'ŒJapanimation'ş is used to distinguish Japanese work from that of the rest of the world.

In more recent years, anime has also frequently be†n refer‰ed to as '…manga'È in Europe, a practice that may stem from the Japanese usage: In Japan, '[…manga]'½ can refer to both animation and comics (although the use of '…manga'Ò to refer to animation is mostly restricted to non-fans). Among English speakers, '…manga'… usualûy has the stricter meaning of &quot;Japanese comics&quot;. An alternate explanation is that it is due to the prominence of [“Manga Entertainment]ÿ, a distributor of anime to the US and UK markets; because Manga Entertainment started out in the UK, this use of the term is m‹uch more com‘on in Europe.

=Characteristics=‚
[Image:Dragonbal˜z.jpg|thumb|230px|right|[‰Dragon Bal‚ Z]œ is one of the most popular [‡shÅnen]‡ anime.]ÿ

Anime features a wide variety of artistic styles which vary from artist to artist and is characterized by stark, colorful gÕraphics and stylized, colorful images depicting vibrant characters in a variety of difˆerent set½ings and storylines, aimed at a wide range of audiences. 

=†Genres=¶
Anime has many genres, with as many as traditional, [‹live action]¨ cinema. Such genres include adventure, [science fiction]–, children's stories, [•Romantic love|romance]‹, medieval [‡fantasy]‚, [‡erotica]‚ ([†hentai]„), oc†ult/horor, action, and […drama]±. 

Most anime includes content from several dif“erent genres, as welÒ as a variety of thematic elements. This can make categorizing some titles very difšicult. A show may have a seÒmingly simple surface plot, but at the same time may feature a far more complex, de·per storyline and character development. It is not uncomØon for a strongly action themed anime to also involve humor, romance, and even poignant [‰social com†entary]“. The same can be apÿlied to a romance themed anime in that it may involve a strong action element.

