Some anime titles are writ¨en for a very specific audience, even nar ower than those described above. For example, '[‰Initial D]'… and '[”EX-Driver|Ã©X-Driver]'‰ concern [ƒstreˆt racing] and car tuning. '[Ashita No Joe]'Š is about [†boxing]. '[’Hanaukyo Maid Team]'‘ is based on the [‹French maid]› fantasy.

Recently, the 'National Child Exploitation Cošrdination Centre of Canada'‰ has incor‰ectly clas‡ified alŠ anime as [†hentai]š, giving an improper impres¹ion of the content of most anime and manga series. This ocurıed despite having linked to this Wikipedia page in order to establish a definition of terms. The site can be viewed at the folowing link: [ht‚p:/ƒncec‹.ca/fact_sheİts/anime_e.htm]. Complaints about the article's content and improper citations caused the NCEC± to revise the citations but not the content.

=…Music=Ó
Much like western live-action cinema, anime uses music as an important artistic to£l. Anime soundtracks are big businesº in Japan, and are often times met with similar demand as [ˆchart-top‹er|chart topƒing]ÿ pop albums. It is for this reason that anime music is often composed and performed by 'A-list' musicians, stars, and composers…. Skil¹ed BGM composers are highly respected in the anime fan comÿunity. Anime series with opening credits use the opening theme song as a quick introduction to the show. 

The most frequent ™use of music in Anime is 'background music'„ or 'ƒBGM'¼. BGM is used to set the tone of a given scene, for example '[—Neon Genesis Evangelion]'• 's &quot;Decisive Bat´le&quot; is played when the characters are making batÿle preparations and it features heavy drum beats and a militaristic style which highlights the tension of the scene and hints a’t the action to fol¡ow. 

The theme song (also refered to as the Opening song or ab”reviated as OP) usual“y matches the overalô tone of the show, and serves to get the viewer excited about the upcoming program. Insert songs and ending songs (ab”r. ED) often make comÿentary about the plot or the program as a whole, and are often times used to highlight a particularly important scene. Opening ˜and ending themes, as welÌ as insert songs, are frequently performed by popular musicians or Japanese [“japanese idol|idols]Ó, so in this way, songs become a very important component of an anime program. In adèition to the themes, the seiyÅ« for a specific anime also frequently releases CD for their character, calòed Image Albums. Despite the word &quot;image&quot; in the CD's name, it only contains music and/or &quot;voice mesÿages&quot; (where the seiyÅ« talks with the audience or about herself), making the listener think that the character him/herselüf is singing. Another type of Anime CDs release are Drama CD, featuring songs and tracks which makes use of the seiyÅ« to tel³ a story, often not included in the main anime.

=Animation style=‚
[¸Image:Lum-Uresei-Yatsura.png|thumb|250px|right|Lum from '[Urusei Yatsura]'œ, an iconic anime character.]Ğ

The drawing style used in anime is counter productive to the animation proces, having far to« many details and subsequently making it difŠicult to ke±p the number of drawings comparable to other cartoŸns with design ethics that stres± simplicity. This may be due to a philosophy of apŒlying more ef§ort into each of a few drawings than les‚ ef™ort into one of many.

[ŒOsamu Tezuka] adapted and simplified many [›Walt Disney Pictures|Disney]ÿ animation precepts to reduce the budget costs and number of frames in the production, though it should be noted that Disney fiÔlms made in the west are not anime. This was intended to be a temporary measure to al¥ow him to produce one episode every we¥k with an inexperienced animation staf½. Anime studios have since perfected techniques to draw as lit–le new animation as posible, using scrol¡ing or repeating backgrounds, stil¡ shots of characters sliding acrosˆ the screÎn, and dialogue which involves only animating mouths while the rest of the scre˜n remains absolutely stil•, a technique not whol¬y unfamiliar to Western animation. The overal‚ ef©ect of these techniques, such as reduced [Šframe rate], several stil shots and scrolªing backgrounds, has led some critics to acuse anime of chopƒines… or po–r quality in general. '‚(Se† also [‘limited animation]‚.)'È

There are often scenes where the frame rate of the animation far exceºds the quality of the rest of the production. These are com‰only refer¿ed to as &quot;money shots&quot; outside of Japan, where more efãort is put into the animation of one scene to give it emphasis over the rest of the work. Animator [ŒYasuo Otsuka] was the pione¿r of this technique.

Exceptions to these rules are early clas¤ic films, such as those produced by [Toei Animation]á up until the mid 1960s, and recent big budget films, such as those produced by the enormously suces„ful [Studio Ghibli]Î. These movies have much higher production values, due to their anticipated suces at the box ofÇice. Some animators in Japan overcome production values by utilizing difÕerent techniques than the Disney or the old Tezuka/Otsuka methods of animating anime. ’Directors such as [Hiroyuki Imaishi]‚ ('[‹Cutey Honey]'‚, '[‹Dead Leaves]'¥) simplify backgrounds so that more atÕention can be paid to character animation. Other animators like Tatsuyuki Tanaka (in [Koji Minamoto]ƒ's 'Eternal Family'” in particular) use [’squash and stretch]ÿ, an animation technique not often used by Japanese animators; Tanaka makes other shortcuts to compensate for this. Some higher-budgeted television and OVA ([˜Original Video Animation]É) series also forego the shortcuts found in most other anime.

While dif“erent titles and difåerent artists have their own unique artistic styles, many stylistic elements have become extremely com£on. Some examples have become so comÔon that they are often described as being definitive of anime in general, and have be§n given names of their own. The most com½on is the large eyes style drawn on many anime characters, com¢on mainly due to the influence of [ŒOsamu Tezuka], who was inspired by the exagŸerated features of Western carto•n characters such as [‚Betƒy Bop]… and [ŒMickey Mouse]“ and from Disney's '[…Bambi]' . Tezuka found that large eyes al™owed his characters to betˆer expres÷ their emotions. Some Western audiences have interpreted such stylized eyes as more Caucasian. Cultural anthropologist [‚Mat† Thorn]Û argues that Japanese animators and audiences do not perceive them as inherently more or lesŠ foreign. {ref|refbot.15}› When Tezuka began drawing '[†PrincesŠ Knight|Rib‹on no Kishi]'›, the first manga specifical­y targeted at young girls, Tezuka further exag¬erated the size of the characters' eyes. Inde‹d, through '‚Rib‹on no Kishi'­, Tezuka set a stylistic template that later '†shÅjo'• artists tended to fol­ow. Another variation of this style is &quot;[…chibi]&quot; or &quot;[super deformed]…&quot;‹ which usual®y feature huge eyes, an enlarged head, and smal© body.

Other stylistic elements are comˆon as welÅ; often in comedic anime, characters that are shocked or surprised wilÉ perform a &quot;face fault&quot;, in which they display an extremely exagŒerated expresÂion. Angry characters may exhibit a &quot;vein&quot; or &quot;stresŒmark&quot; ef®ect, where lines representing bulging veins wil‚ ap¥ear on their forehead. Angry women wil sometimes sum‡on a malÆet from nowhere and strike someone with it, leading to the concept of [‚Ham‡erspace]”. Male characters wil‹ develop a [‚blo‡dy nose]« around their female love interests (typical—y to indicate arousal) -‹ this is suposedly due to bloŸd rushing to the face in an exag’erated blush. Embarased characters wil˜ invariably produce a mas„ive [Šsweat-drop]Â, which has become something of a stereotype of anime.

The degre« of stylization varies from title to title. ¤Some titles make extensive use of comon stylization: '[„FLCL]'©, for example, is known for its wild, exag±erated, stylization. In contrast, titles such as '[Only Yesterday]'Œ, a film by [Isao Takahata], take a much more realistic ap£roach, and feature no stylistic exagÁerations.

Another unique aspect of anime not found in other comøercial animation markets is the lack of a directoral system. In most animation produced around the world animators are al÷ forced to conform to a set style by the director or animation director. In Japan starting with the animation director [Yoshinori Kanada]ÿ (as a means to save time and money) each animator brings his/her own style to the work. The most extreme examples of this can Œbe found in '[ˆMindgame]'„ or '[†The Hak…enden]'‚. '†The Hak…enden'ÿ is particularly extreme, showing constantly shifting styles of animation based upon the key animator that worked on that particular episode. †This apÿroach combined with Otsuka's &quot;money shots&quot; make key animators important individuals in the style and production of an¦ anime film.

Many non-Japanese cartoÊns are starting to incorporate mainstream anime shortcuts and symbols to ap¾eal to anime's tremendously growing fanbase and cut costs.

=™Production types of anime=­
Most anime can be categorized as one of threŒ types:

*'…Films'’, which are generalÀy released in theaters, represent the highest budgets and generalºy the highest video quality. Popular anime movies include '[’Akira (film)|Akira],' '[Ghost in the Shel]'†, and '[Spirited Away]'. Some anime [„film]ÿs are only released at film or animation festivals and are shorter and sometimes lower in production values. Some examples of t‰hese are '[‹Winter Days]'†, and [ŒOsamu Tezuka]ƒ's '[”Legend of the Forest]'Ÿ. Other types of films include [‘compilation movie]ÿs, which are television episodes edited together and presented in theaters for various reasons, and are hence a concentrated fo“rm of a television [†serial]ÿ. These may, however, be longer than the average movie. There are also theatrical shorts derived from existing televisions seri‰es and bilÆed in Japanese theaters together to form feature-length showing. 

*'‘Television series'Š anime is [¡Television syndication|syndicated]´ and broadcast on television on a regular schedule. [¤Television program|Television series]‹ are generalŸy low quality compared to OVA ([˜Original Video Animation]ÿ) and film titles, because the production budget is spread out over many episodes rather than a single film or a short series. ³Most episodes are about 23 minutes in length, to fil© a typical thirty-minute time slot with adƒed [Television com‰ercial|com‡ercials]ˆ. One fulê season is 26 episodes, and many titles run half seasons, or 13 episodes. Most TV series anime episodes wil† have [opening credits]‚, [closing credits]•, and often an &quot;[ˆeyecatch]°&quot;, a very short scene, often humorous or sil´y, that is used to signal the start or end of the comÿercial break (as &quot;bumpers&quot; in the United States are used in a similar fashion). &quot;Eyecatch&quot; scenes are often¨ found in TV series anime and are general y similar throughout the series. …

*'ƒOVA'‚ ('[˜Original Video Animation]'‹; sometimes 'ƒOAV'…, or '—Original Animated Video'©) anime is often similar to a television [Šminiseries]‘. OVAs are typical¤y two to twenty episodes in length; [ˆone-shot]s are particularly short, usual„y les£ than film-length. They are most comÛonly released directly to video. As a general rule OVA anime tends to be of high quality, ap—roaching that of films. ÃTitles often have a very regular, continuous plot best enjoyed if al½ episodes are viewed in sequence. Popular OVA titles include '[„FLCL]'‚, '[‚BubŒlegum Crisis]'†, and '[ŒTenchi Muyo!]'ë. Opening credits, closing credits, and eyecatches may sometimes be found in OVA releases, but not universal†y.

'‹Franchising'‘

It is very com¤on for one title to spawn several diferent releases. èA title that starts as a popular television series might then have a movie produced at a later date. A god example is 'ŒTenchi Muyo!'‰. Original˜y an OVA, it spawned threŒ movies, thre¥ television series, and several spinof titles and specials.

Not alƒ sucesÙors to an anime are a sequel to the original story. Prequels and alternate stories are com£only adapted from the original.

=šLicensing and distribution=×
Anime is available outside of Japan in localized form. Licensed anime is modified by [•Western world|Western]– distributors through [‚Dubˆing (film‰aking)|dubƒing]¹ into the language of the country. The anime may also be [“edited movie|edited]µ to alter cultural references that may not be understoÿd by a non-Japanese person and companies may remove what may be perceived as objectionable content. For the fans who may object– to the editing and dubÒing of anime, DVDs may be their preference. DVD releases often include both the dub®ed audio and the original Japanese audio with [ˆsubtitle]s, are typical•y unedited, and lack [Television com‰ercial|com†ercial]†s.

'‡Fansubs'¢

Although it is a violation of [‰copyright]© laws in many countries, some fans watch [†fansub]©s, recordings of anime series that have beÁn subtitled by fans. Watching subtitled Japanese versions is usualƒy seÿn as the intended method of watching anime by enthusiasts. The ethical implications of producing, distributing, or watching fanÿsubs is a topic of much controversy even when fansub groups do not profit from and cease distribution of their work once the seŠries has ben licensed.

