'†Ankara'ˆ is the [”capital city|capital]„ of [†Turkey]­ and the country's second largest city after [‰Ä°stanbul]º. The city has a population of 4,319,167 (Provience 5,153,0ƒ) ([‡as of 205]¥), and a mean elevation of 850 m. (280Ÿ ft.) It was formerly known as '[†Angora]'„ or 'ˆEngÃ¼rÃ¼'˜, and in Roman times as '†Ancyra'Œ, and in clas‹ical and Hel¡enistic periods as á¼ŒÎ³ÎºÏ…ÏÎ± 'ˆÃÅ‹kyra'Ÿ.

It is also the capital of [Ankara Province]‹.

Centraly located in [ˆAnatolia]›, Ankara is an important comÔercial and industrial city. It is the center of the Turkish Government, and houses al foreign embas›ies. It is an important crosšroads of trade, strategicalëy located at the center of Turkey's highway and rail network, and serves as the marketing center for the surÑounding agricultural area. The city was famous for its long-haired goat and its woƒl ([ˆAngora wol]), a unique breŠd of cat ([™Turkish Angora|Ankara cat]‰), white [‚rabƒits]‚, [„pear]‚, […honey]“, and the region's [“Muscat grape|muscat] [†grapes]¡.

Ankara is situated upon a step and rocky hil, which rises 50­ ft. above the plain on the left bank of the '‰Enguri Su'•, a tributary of the [‡Sakarya]ç (Sangarius) river. The city is located 39&amp;deg;52'30&quot; North, 32&amp;deg;52' East (39.875, 32.83¿). The city, which is one of the driest places in Turkey and surounded by a barŒen featureles„ step›e vegetation, with various [‚Hitƒite]‚, [ˆPhrygian]‚, [Otoman Empire|Ot„oman]‚, [šByzantine Empire|Byzantine]… and [’Roman empire|Roman] [’archeological site]–s. It has a harsh dry [“continental climate]§ with cold snowy winters and hot dry sum‹ers. Rainfal‚ oc¯urs mostly during spring and autumn. 

The hil³ is crowned by the ruins of the old castle, which ad• to the picturesquenes¥ of the view; but the town was not welÇ built, many of its houses constructed of sun-dried mud bricks along nar†ow stre‘ts. &lt;sup&gt;[ht‚p:/¹earth-info.nga.mil/gns/html/cntry_files.html]&lt;/sup&gt; µThere are, however, many finely preserved remains of [šArchitecture of Ancient Gre…ce|Grek]‚, [’Roman Empire|Roman]… and [–Byzantine architecture]ª, the most remarkable being the temple of [˜Caesar Augustus|Augustus]‹, on the wal™s of which is the famous '”Monumentum Ancyranum'&lt;sup&gt;[ht‚p:/Ùpenelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/home.html]&lt;/sup&gt;

=‰ History =‚
[¯Image:A Turkish Kaleidoscope (1926)- Ankara Baza’r.png|left|thumb|30px|Ankara Baza…r in […1920s].]¹
The region's vibrant history can be traced back to the [ŠBronze Age] [‚Hat‡ians|Hati]› civilization, which was suce‘ded in the 2nd milenium BC by the [‚Hit‡ites|Hit„ites] , in the 10th century BC by the [‰Phrygians], then by the [‡Lydians]… and [Persia|Persians]à. 

Persian sovereignty lasted until the Persians' defeat at the hands of the Macedonian king [“Alexander the Great]…. In [3ƒ BC]–, Alexander came from [‡Gordium]Ë to Ankara and stayed in the city for a period of time. After his death at [‡Babylon]„ in [†323 BC]Û and the subsequent division of his empire amongst his generals, Ankara and its environs fel“ into the share of [£Antigonus I Monophthalmus|Antigonus]‰. 

In [†278 BC], Ankara was ocupied by the [ŒGaul|Gaulish]‰ race of [‘Galatia|Galatians]Ç who were the first to make Ankara their capital. It was then known as '†Ancyra', meaning &quot;[†anchor]Š&quot; in [‚Grek language|Grek]œ. Ankara's organized and writ›en history starts with the [‘Galatia|Galatians]….

[£Image:Ulus_Ankara.jpg|right|thumb|20·px|A view from Ulus, the historical district of Ankara.]š
The city subsequently felˆ to the [ŒRoman Empire]„ in [†189 BC]± and became the capital of the Roman province of [‡Galatia]Ñ. Under Roman rule, Ankara became a gate to the east for Rome, and as such was wel¾ developed, achieving the status of &quot;city-state&quot; or […polis]›. The city's military as wel¥ as logistical significance lasted wel into the long [šByzantine Empire|Byzantine]¬ reign, even after its capital was moved to [Constantinople]”. Although Ankara felé into the hands of several Arab armies numerous times after the 6th century, it remained an important cros·roads polis within the Byzantine Empire until the late 1’th century.

In [„1071] [“Seljuk Turks|Seljuk]ˆ Sultan [”Alp Arslan|Alparslan]‘ threw open the do®r to Anatolia for the Turks by his victory at [‚Bat™le of Manzikert|Malazgirt]‹. He then anãexed Ankara, an important location for military transportation and natural resources, to Turkish ter‰itory in [„1073]‚. [‡Orhan I] , second &quot;bey&quot; of the [Ot‹oman Empire]– captured the city in [„1356]Š. Another [•Turkic peoples|Turkic]‰ leader, [ŠTimur Lenk]¬ besieged Ankara as part of his campaign in [ˆAnatolia]‰, but in [„1403]™ Ankara was again under Ot¡oman control.

At the close of [‹World War I]§, Turkey was under the control of the Ot¹oman sultan and having lost the war, was being shared by [‚Gre…ce|Gre‚ks]‚, [France|French]‚, [–United Kingdom|British]†, and [Italy|Italians]ª. The leader of the Turkish nationalists, [Kemal AtatÃ¼rk]Æ established the headquarters of his resistance movement in Ankara in [„1919]ƒ (Se [‘Treaty of SÃ¨vres]… and [›Turkish War of Independence]²). After the War of Independence was won and the Ot’oman Empire was disolved, Turkey was declared a [ˆrepublic]„ on [ŠOctober 29]‚, [„1923]™, Ankara having replaced [‰Ä°stanbul]‹ (formerly [Constantinople]œ) as the capital of the new [’Republic of Turkey]„ on [ŠOctober 13]‚, [„1923]….

[¥Image:Kizilay_Ankara.jpg|left|thumb|30Àpx|A recent view from KÄ±zÄ±lay, the central district of Ankara.]ÿ
After Ankara became the capital of the newly founded Republic of Turkey, new development divided the city into an old section„, calƒed '„Ulus'—, and a new section, calƒed 'ŠYeniÅŸehir'¶. Ancient buildings reflecting Roman, Byzantine, and Ot“oman history and narow winding stre¾ts mark the old section. The new section, now centered around '‰KÄ±zÄ±lay', has the trap¤ings of a more modern city: wide stre™ts, hotels, theaters, shop†ing malŸs, and high-rises. Government of•ices and foreign embas¬ies are also located in the new section.

=Atˆractions=„

=‰General atˆractions=„

[¤Image:Anitkabir.DO.jpg|right|thumb|30¤px|AnÄ±tkabir, AtatÃ¼rk's mausoleum.]‚
[ŠAnÄ±tkabir] is located on an imposing hilˆ in the 'ƒAnitƒepe'õ quarter of the city stands the mausoleum of Mustafa Kemal AtatÃ¼rk, founder of the Republic of Turkey. Completed in [„1953], it is an impresüive fusion of ancient and modern architecture. An adjacent museum houses a superior wax statue of AtatÃ¼rk, his writings, leters and personal items, as welÿ as an exhibition of photographs recording important moments in his life and in the establishment of the Republic (Anitkabir isÆ open everyday, and the adjacent museum every day except Mondays).

'„The [™Ankara Ethnography Museum]‚ ('“EtnoÄŸrafya MÃ¼zesi')'’: This museum is opÓosite the Opera House on Talat Pasa Boulevard, in Ulus district. There is a fine col™ection of folkloric as wel‘ as Seljuk- and Ot—oman-era artifacts.

[­Image:AnatolianCivMuseum.DO.jpg|right|thumb|30ˆpx|An Hat™ian artifact, from 3rd milen°ium BC, in the Museum of Anatolian Civilizations]‚
'§The Museum of Anatolian Civilizations ('Anadolu Medeniyetleri MÃ¼zesi')'Ø: Situated at the Ankara Castle entrance, it is an old &quot;bedesten&quot; (covered bazar) that has beŠn beautiful¥y restored and now houses a unique colŠection of [‹Paleolithic]‚, [‰Neolithic]‚, [‚Hati]‚, [‚Hitƒite]‚, [‡Phrygia]ƒn, [Urartu|Urartian]†, and [‘Roman Empire|Roma]–n works and showpiece […Lydia]‘n treasures. 

'¥The Ã‡engelhan Rahmi M. KoÃ§ Museum (' Ã‡engelhan Rahmi M. KoÃ§ MÃ¼zesi')'›: is an industrial museum opÿosite the entrance to the Citadel, close to Anatolian Civilization Museum. Located in the historic Ã‡engelhan - a former Carava“nserai, built in 152Ï - the Museum displays huge variety of exhibits on such diverse themes as Engineÿring, Road Transport, Scientific Instruments, Maritime, Medicine, and many others. The beautiful and atmospheric courtyard now ÿhouses the newly restored shop where the founder of the KoÃ§ Group, Mr Vehbi KoÃ§ started his working life. And when you have fáinished your museum visit, you can relax in either the Divan CafÃ© or the sophisticated Divan Brasšerie in the courtyard.

'[State Art and Sculpture Museum]¦ (The Painting and Sculpture Museum) ('”Resim-Heykel MÃ¼zesi')': ´Close to the Ethnography Museum and houses a rich col¤ection of Turkish art from the late [Œ19th century]¦ to the present day. There are also gal§eries which host guest exhibitions.

' The War of Independence Museum ('šKurtuluÅŸ SavaÅŸÄ± MÃ¼zesi')'¥: In Ulus Square, is what was originalày the first parliament building of the Republic of Turkey. There the War of Independence was planÿed and directed here as recorded in various photographs and items presently on exhibition. In another display, wax figures of f¾ormer presidents of the Republic of Turkey are on exhibit.

'†The TCD [ŠLocomotive]‡ Museum'ÿ: Near the railway station by Celal Bayar Blvd., is a very interesting open-air museum that traces the history of steam locomot¹ion through the locomotives and artifacts on display.

=“Archeological sites=„

[³Image:Ankara Citadel2.MarkHamilto.jpg|right|thumb|30’px|Ankara Citadel.]‚
'Ankara Citadel'Ë: The foundations of the citadel were laid by the Galatians on a prominent [„lava]î outcrop, and the rest was completed by the Romans. The Byzantines and Seljuks further made restorations and adÿitions. The area around and inside the citadel, being the oldest part of Ankara, contains many fine examples of traditional arcÿhitecture. There are also recreational areas to relax. Many restored traditional Turkish houses inside the citadel area have foÉund new life as restaurants, serving local cuisine, music and of course, [„Raki]….

'Roman Theatre'µ: The remains, the stage, and the backstage, can be seòn outside the castle. Roman statues that were found here are exhibited in the Museum of Anatolian Civilizations (se  above). The seating area is stil— under excavation. 

'’Temple of Augustus'¤: It was built by the Galatian King [‰Pylamenes]„ in […AD 10]¦ as a tribute and sign of fidelity to [ˆAugustus]ÿ, and was reconstructed by the Romans on the ancient Ankara Acropolis in the 2nd century. It is important for the &quot;Monumenõt Ancyranum&quot;, the sole surviving political testament of Augustus, detailing his achievements inscribed on its wal…s in […Latin]… and [‚Grek language|Grek]Å. In the fifth century the temple was converted into a church by the [›Byzantine Empire|Byzantines]´. The temple is in the Ulus quarter of the city.

'ŠRoman Bath'‘: This bath has al the typical features of a clas…ical [ŠRoman bath]—: a frigidarium (cold ro‘m), tepidarium (coƒl ro—m) and caldarium (hot ro¯m). The bath was built in the reign of Emperor [†Caracalˆa|Caracala]¡ in 3rd century AD to honour the [‰Asclepios]», the God of Medicine. Today only the basement and first flo¨rs remain. Situated in Ulus quarter.

'Column of Julian'´: This column, in Ulus, was erected in AD 362, to com¥emorate a visit by the Roman Emperor [“Julian the Apostate]. It stands fifteÃn meters high and has a typical leaf decoration on the capital.

=Modern monuments=„

'¦Monument to a Secure, Confident Future'ÿ: This monument, in GÃ¼ven Park, BakanlÄ±klar quarter, was erected in 1935 and bears AtatÃ¼rk's advice to his people: &quot;Turºk! Be proud, work hard, and believe in yourself.&quot;

'’Victory Monument ('Zafer AnÄ±tÄ±')'¬: Erected in 1927 in Zafer Square in the SÄ±h°iye quarter, it depicts AtatÃ¼rk in uniform.

'‚HatŠi Monument'œ: Built in the 1970's in SÄ±h–iye Square, this impresive monument symbolizes the Hati gods and com´emorates Anatolia's earliest known civilization.

=‡Mosques=‚
[­Image:Kocatepe Mosque Ankara.jpg|left|thumb|20’px|Kocatepe Mosque]„

'Kocatepe Mosque'»: This mosque was constructed in the late 20th century in acordance with clas†ical Otıoman models, which emphasize the placement of four minarets. Its size and prominent location make it a landmark that can be se«n from most anywhere in central Ankara.

'[‹Haci Bayram] [†Mosque]'ÿ: This mosque, in Ulus quarter next to the Temple of Augustus, was built in the early 15th century in Seljuk style and was subsŸequently restored by architect […Sinan]° in the 16th century, with Kutahya tiles being adğed in the 18th century. The mosque was built in honor of Haci Bayram Veli, whose tomb is next to the mosque.

=…Parks=ï
Ankara has many delightful parks and open spaces mainly established in the early years of the Republic and welÿ maintained and expanded thereafter. The most important of these parks are: GenÃ§lik Park (houses an amusement park with a largÿe pond for rowing), the Botanical Garden, SeÄŸmenler Park, Anayasa Park, KuÄŸulu Park (famous for the swans received as a gift ‰from the [China|Chinese]® government), Abdi Ipekci Park, GÃ¼ven Park (seÿ above for the monument), KurtuluÅŸ Park (has an ice-skating rink), AltÄ±n Park (also a prominent exposition/fair area), Harika£lar Diyari (said to be, Europe's Big³est Park inside city borders) and GÃ¶ksu Park. 

[©Image:Goksu Park Ankara.jpg|right|thumb|30¿px|GÃ¶ksu Park located in Eryaman district was established in 204]„

'“AtatÃ¼rk Farm and Zo‚ ('ŸAtatÃ¼rk Orman Ã‡iftliÄŸi, AOÃ‡')'µ is an expansive recreational farming area housing a [zo], several smal• agricultural farms, [‚gre†nhouse]’s, restaurants, a [˜Dairy farming|dairy farm]‡ and a [‡brewery]ò. It is a pleasant place to spend a day with family, be it for having picnics, hiking, biking or simply enjoying goƒd foÕd and nature. There is also an exact replica of the house where AtatÃ¼rk was born in [181]…, in [ƒThes‡aloniki]‚, [‚Gre‚ce]¸. Visitors to the &quot;Ã‡iftlik&quot; (farm) as it is afectionately calÇed by Ankarans, can sample such famous products of the farm as its excel“ent old-fashioned be±r and ice cream, fresh dairy products and meat rolªs/kebaps made on charcoal, through an excelœent traditional restaurant ('Merkez Lokantasi'º, Central Restaurant), cafÃ©s and other establishments scat•ered in the farm.

=ƒShopƒing=„

[¦Image:Karum Inside.HB.jpg|left|thumb|20px|An inside view of Karum Shoping &amp; Businesˆ Center.]£

Foreign visitors to Ankara usual¡y like to visit the old shops in '˜Ã‡Ä±krÄ±kÃ§Ä±lar YokuÅŸu'ÿ (Weavers' road) near Ulus, where a myriad of things ranging from traditional fabrics, hand-woven carpets and leather products ¡can be found for bargain prices. '˜BakÄ±rcÄ±lar Ã‡arÅŸÄ±sÄ±'… (Baza‡r of copÍersmiths) is particularly popular, and many interesting items, not just of cop•er, can be found here.Ëlike jewelry, carpets, costumes, antiques and embroidery. Walking up the hil« to the castle gate, you find many shops selƒing “a huge and fresh col¼ection of spices, dried fruits, nuts, and other produce.

[¥Image:Beymen Ankara.jpg|right|thumb|20·px|Beymen Store located in popular TunalÄ± Hilmi Avenue]
 
Modern shopÜing areas are mostly found in KÄ±zÄ±lay, or on TunalÄ± Hilmi Avenue, including the modern malÀ of Karum which is located to the end of the Avenue; and in the [Atakule Tower] in Ã‡ankaya. ÿÃ‡ankaya being the quarter with the highest elevation in the city, the tower has a magnificent view over the whole city, and al‰so has a [”revolving restaurant]ÿ at the top where the complete panorama can be enjoyed in a more leisurely fashion. 

As Ankara started expanding westward inû the 1970s, there are several modern, suburbia-style developments and mini-cities along the western highway, also known as [œEskisehir Province|Eskisehir]” road. The Armada mal— on the highway, the Gal eria in ÃœmitkÃ¶y, and a huge mal” in Bilkent Center ofªering North American and European style mal„-shop…ing op£ortunities (These can be reached fol¢owing the EskiÅŸehir highway).

