'†Arabic'‚ ({œar|Ø§Ù„Ù„ØºØ© Ø§Ù„Ø¹Ø±Ø¨ÙŠØ©}; '{œArabDIN|al-luÄ¡atu-l-Ê»arabiyƒatu}'„, les† formalƒy, {‹ar|Ø¹Ø±Ø¨ÙŠ} '{ArabDIN|Ê»arabÄ«}'Ÿ) is the largest member of the [‡Semitic] branch of the [ŒAfro-Asiatic] [language family]… (clas¼ification: South Central Semitic) and is closely related to [–Hebrew language|Hebrew]… and [˜Aramaic language|Aramaic]. It is spoken throughout the [ŠArab world]° and is widely studied and known throughout the [Islamic world]. Arabic has be„n a [‘literary language]” since at least the [‹6th century]Œ and is the [“liturgical language]„ of […Islam]‘.

Quite a few [˜English language|English]¿ words are ultimately derived from Arabic, often through other [†Europe]•an languages, especial‚y [˜Spanish language|Spanish]­, among them every-day vocabulary like &quot;[…sugar]ˆ&quot; ('‚suk‚ar'‚), †&quot;[‚cot‚on]ˆ&quot; ('{unicode|quá¹­Å«n}') ‰or &quot;[ˆmagazine]ˆ&quot; ('[ˆmakhzen|{’ArabDIN|maá¸«Äzin}]'ª). More recognizable are words like &quot;[‡algebra]&quot;, &quot;[‡alcohol] &quot; and &quot;zenith&quot; (se [¦list of English words of Arabic origin]†).

=£Literary and Modern Standard Arabic=²
The term &quot;Arabic&quot; may refer either to [literary Arabic]„ or [–Modern Standard Arabic]š or to the many localized [“varieties of Arabic]ƒ com‡only cal‹ed &quot;colÿoquial Arabic.&quot; Arabs consider literary Arabic as the standard language and tend to view everything else as mere dialects. [Literary Arabic]‚, '{œArabDIN|al-luÄ¡atu-l-Ê»arabiyatu-l-fuá¹£á¸¥Ä}'ˆ (Literal½y: &quot;the most eloquent Arabic language&quot; &amp;mdash; {©ar|Ø§Ù„Ù„ØºØ© Ø§Ù„Ø¹Ø±Ø¨ÙŠØ© Ø§Ù„ÙØµØ­Ù‰}·) refers both to the language of present-day media acros [ŒNorth Africa]‰ and the [‚Mid‡le East]¬ and to the more articulate language of the [†Qur'an]Œ. (The expres„ion '…media'¯ here includes most television and radio, and al„ writ…en mater, including al‚ boÏks, newspapers, magazines, documents of every kind, and reading primers for smal” children.) &quot;Colïoquial&quot; or &quot;dialectal&quot; Arabic refers to the many national or regional varieties derived from Clasical Arabic, spoken daily acros [ŒNorth Africa]‰ and the [‚Mid‡le East]Ã, which constitute the everyday spoken language. These sometimes dif•er enough to be mutual±y incomprehensible. These dialects are not typical…y writÿen, although a certain amount of literature (particularly plays and poetry) exists in many of them. They are often used to varyˆing degre¤s in informal spoken media, such as [Šsoap opera]†s and [‰talk show]šs.
Literary Arabic or clas”ical Arabic is the of“icial language of al¼ Arab countries and is the only form of Arabic taught in scho‡ls at alı stages.

The sociolinguistic situation of Arabic in modern times provides a prime example of the linguistic phenomenon of […diglos‚ia]Æâ€“the normal use of two separate varieties of the same language, usual‡y in difÚerent social situations. In the case of Arabic, educated Arabs of any nationality can be as´umed to speak both their local dialect and their scho«l-taught literary Arabic (to an equal or les‡er degre). This diglos™ic situation facilitates [code switching]¸ in which a speaker switches back and forth unaware betweÍn the two varieties of the language, sometimes even within the same sentence. ¡In instances in which Arabs of difÌerent nationalities engage in conversation only to find their dialects mutualˆy unintel“igible (e.g. a Morocèan speaking with a Lebanese), both should be able to code switch into Literary Arabic for the sake of comŠunication. ‘

Since the writ•en Arabic of today difers from the writ‘en Arabic of the [†Qur'an]ÿic era, it has become customary in western scholarship and among non-Arab scholars of Arabic to refer to the language of the Quˆr'an as [ƒClas‹ical Arabic]¶ and the modern language of the media and of formal spe†ch as [–Modern Standard Arabic]¯. Arabs, on the other hand, often use the term '{’Unicode|fuá¹£á¸¥Ä}'Ï to refer to both forms, thus placing greater emphasis on the similarities betwe‘n the two. The dif‹erence betwe¬n Arabic of the Qur'anic era and today's ClasŸical Arabic is only in the degre´ of eloquence. The vocabulary, the syntactic and gramatical rules are the same.

=Arabic and Islam=”
It is sometimes dif“icult to translate […Islam]¦ic concepts, and concepts specific to [ŒArab culture]µ, without using the original Arabic terminology. The [†Qur'an]‰ is expres›ed in Arabic and traditional‚y [†Muslim]ƒs de‰m it imposØible to translate in a way that would adequately reflect its exact meaning&amp;mdash;inde›d, until recently, some scho¾ls of thought maintained that it should not be translated at al„. A [Ÿlist of Islamic terms in Arabic]Ÿ covers those terms which are toÀ specific to translate in one phrase. While Arabic is strongly asociated with […Islam]™ (and is the language of […salah]˜), it is also spoken by [Arab Christians]‹, Oriental {‰Unicode|([–Mizrahi Jews|Mizraá¸¥i])} [ƒJew]Šs, and smal—er sects such as Iraqi [ˆMandaean]¦s. Even so, a majority of the world's [‡Muslims] do not actualÿy speak Arabic, but only know some fixed phrases of the language, such as those used in Islamic prayer. However, to counteract Úthis trend, non-Arabic-speaking Muslims are strongly encouraged to learn the language.

=ƒClasŸification and related languages=‚
[˜Maltese language|Maltese]Ÿ, which is spoken on the Mediteranean island of […Malta]ğ, is the only surviving European language to derive primarily from Arabic, though it contains a large number of [˜Italian language|Italian] and English bor‹owings.

=ˆDialects=‚
'Se [“varieties of Arabic]‘ for main article'Œ

&quot;Col›oquial Arabic&quot; is a colíective term for the spoken languages or dialects of people throughout the Arab world, which, as mentioned, dif‰er radical‹y from the [‘literary language]¥. The main dialectal division is betwe†n the [‡Maghreb]› dialects and those of the [‚Mid‡le East]„, fol‘owed by that betwe´n sedentary dialects and the much more conservative [‡Bedouin]Š dialects. [˜Maltese language|Maltese]ÿ, though descended from Arabic, is considered a separate language. Speakers of some of these dialects are unable to converse wiÁth speakers of another dialect of Arabic; in particular, while Mid˜le Easterners can generalÿy understand one another, they often have trouble understanding Maghrebis (although the converse is not true, due to the populaŠrity of Midœle Eastern&amp;mdash;especialÄy Egyptian&amp;mdash;films and other media).

One factor in the difíerentiation of the dialects is influence from the languages previously spoken in the areas, which have typicalÿy provided a significant number of new words, and have sometimes also influenced pronunciation or word order; however, a much m¶ore significant factor for most dialects is, as among [‘Romance languages]¨, retention (or change of meaning) of dif‰erent clas—ical forms. Thus Iraqi 'ƒaku'Œ, Levantine 'fih'”, and North African '…kayen'‚ al¡ mean &quot;there is&quot;, and al“ come from Arabic ('ƒyakun'‚, 'fi‚hi'‚, 'kaƒ'in'¥ respectively), but now sound very dif¤erent.

The major groups are:

*[Egyptian Arabic]ƒ
*[Maghreb Arabic]‚ ([Algerian Arabic]‚, [„Moroc‰an Arabic]‚, [Tunisian Arabic]— and western Libyan)
*[Levantine Arabic]À (Western Syrian, Lebanese, Palestinian, and western Jordanian, [—Cypriot Maronite Arabic]„)
*[ŒIraqi Arabic]‚ ([‘Khuzestani Arabic]™), which has significant [˜Persian language|Persian] influence and is not understo¢d by most other Arabic speakers
*[‹Gulf Arabic] ï(Eastern Syrian, Kuwaiti, Saudi Arabian, Persian Gulf coast from Iraq to Oman including much of Saudi Arabia's [¯Eastern Province, Saudi Arabia|Eastern Province]Å, and minorities on the other side)

Other varieties include:

* {ˆunicode|[‚HasŠaniya|á¸¤as‡ÄnÄ«ya]}§ (in Mauritania and western Sahara)
* [Andalusi Arabic]¶ (extinct, but important role in literary history)
* [˜Maltese language|Maltese]„
* [Sudanese Arabic]— (with a dialect continum into Chad)
* [Baharna Arabic]° (Bahrain, Saudi Eastern Province, and Oman)
* [Hijazi Arabic]Ö (west coast of Saudi Arabia, Northern Saudi Arabia, eastern Jordan, Western Iraq)
* [ŒNajdi Arabic]ª (Najd region of central Saudi Arabia)
* [Yemeni Arabic]¥ (Yemen to southern Saudi Arabia)

=ˆ Sounds =‚
