The phonemes below reflect the pronunciation of Standard Arabic.

=†Vowels=’

Arabic has threµ vowels, with their long forms, plus two diphthongs: 'a' {ŠIPA|[É›Ìˆ]}‡ (open 'e' as in English 'ƒbed'”, but centralised), 'i' {ˆIPA|[Éª]}‚, 'u' {ˆIPA|[ÊŠ]}‚; '‚Ä' {ŠIPA|[Ã¦Ë]}‚, '‚Ä«' {‰IPA|[iË]}‚, '‚Å«' {‰IPA|[uË]}‚; '‚ai'‚ ('‚ay'‚) {ŒIPA|[É›ÌˆÉª]}‚, '‚au'‚ ('‚aw'‚) {ŒIPA|[É›ÌˆÊŠ]}‚. [Alˆophone|Alˆophonicaly]Ÿ, after velarized consonants (seƒ fol’owing), the vowel 'a' is pronounced {ˆIPA|[É‘]}‚, '‚Ä'„ as {ŠIPA|[É‘Ë]}’ (thus also after 'r'ƒ), '‚ai'„ as {ŠIPA|[É‘Éª]}… and '‚au'„ as {ŠIPA|[É‘ÊŠ]}….

