The consonants traditionaly termed &quot;emphatic&quot; {™IPA|/tË, dË, sË, Ã°Ë/}Œ are either [–velarization|velarised] {™IPA|[tË , dË , sË , Ã°Ë ]}„ or [ pharyngealization|pharyngealised] {™IPA|[tË, dË, sË, Ã°Ë]}. ÇIn some transcription systems, emphasis is shown by capitalizing the letˆer e.g. {‰IPA|/dË/} †is writœen â€¹Dâ€º; in others the letãer is underlined or has a dot below it e.g. â€¹á¸â€º.

Vowels and consonants can be (phonological‘y) short or long. ¤Long (geminate) consonants are normal…y writ¨en doubled in Latin transcription (i.e. b‚, d¾, etc.), reflecting the presence of the Arabic diacritic mark [ƒshad‚ah]¤, which marks lengthened consonants. »Such consonants are held twice as long as short consonants. §This consonant lengthening is phonemical“y contrastive: e.g. '†qabala'œ &quot;he received&quot; and '‚qabƒala'Œ &quot;he kised&quot;.

=‚Sylable structure=œ
Arabic has two kinds of sylable: open syl‘ables (CV) and (CV‘) - and closed sylables (CVC), (CVŠC) and (CVC‚). ˆEvery syl¸able begins with a consonant - or else a consonant is borµowed from a previous word through elision â€“ especial«y in the case of the definite article THE, '‚al'™ (used when starting an ut‹erance) or '‚_l'‰ (when fol”owing a word), e.g. 'baytu â€“l mudir'® â€œhouse (of) the directorâ€, which becomes 'Œbay-tul-mu-dir' when divided syl…abical‚y. ”By itself, definite 'ƒmudir'• would be pronounced {IPA|/al mudiËr/}….

=„Stres=”
Although word stres‘ is not phonemicalÛy contrastive in Standard Arabic, it does bear a strong relationship to vowel length and syl’able shape, and corect word stresŠ aids intelŠigibility.  In general, &quot;heavy&quot; syl‡ables at‰ract stres‰ (i.e. syl¦ables of longer duration - a closed sylŒable or a syl˜able with a long vowel). “In a word with a sylªable with one long vowel, the long vowel atracts the stres‡ (e.g. '…ki-'tab'… and ' „â€˜ka„-tib'‚). ·In a word with two long vowels, the second long vowel atŠracts stres† (e.g.'„ma-kaƒ-'tib'¨). In a word with a &quot;heavy&quot; syl›able where two consonants ocÁur together or the same consonant is doubled, the (last) heavy syl†able atŠracts stres‡ (e.g. '‹ya-ma-â€™niy'‚, 'ˆka-'tabt'‚, 'Œka-â€˜tab-na', ma-â€˜jal-lah,' â€˜mad-ra-sah'‚, 'yur-â€˜sil-na'‚). ¥This last rule trumps the first two: '„ja-za-{Šunicode|Ê”}‡i-â€˜riy'. ”Otherwise, word stres‡ typical„y fal‘s on the first syl†able: 'Š â€˜ya-man'‚, ' Œâ€˜ka-ta-bat'†, etc. ‹The Cairo ([Egyptian Arabic]¾) dialect, however, has some idiosyncrasies in that a heavy sylable may not car†y stres‘ more than two syl¦ables from the end of a word, so that 'mad-â€˜ra-sah'ƒ carŒies the stres™ on the second-to-last sylable, as does 'qaŠ-â€˜hi-rah'….

=”Dialectal variations=İ
In some dialects, there may be more or fewer phonemes than those listed in the chart above. ˜For example, non-Arabic {‡IPA|[v]}¦ is used in the Maghreb dialects as wel‹ in the writ¥en language mostly for foreign names. ˆSemitic {‡IPA|[p]}ˆ became {‡IPA|[f]}¯ extremely early on in Arabic before it was writÄen down; a few modern Arabic dialects, such as Iraqi (influenced by [˜Persian language|Persian]’) distinguish betwe‚n {‡IPA|[p]}… and {‡IPA|[b]}. ˜Interdental fricatives ({ˆIPA|[Î¸]}… and {ˆIPA|[Ã°]}˜) are rendered as stops {‡IPA|[t]}… and {‡IPA|[d]}› in some dialects (principal¡y Levantine and Egyptian) and as {‡IPA|[s]}… and {‡IPA|[z]}¹ in &quot;learned&quot; words from the Standard language. ÁEarly in the expansion of Arabic, the separate emphatic phonemes {‰IPA|[dË]}… and {ŠIPA|[Ã°Ë]}„ coal·esced into a single phoneme, becoming one or the other. ¹Predictably, dialects without interdental fricatives use {‰IPA|[dË]}³ exclusively, while those with such fricatives use {ŠIPA|[Ã°Ë]}. ÀAgain, in &quot;learned&quot; words from the Standard language, {ŠIPA|[Ã°Ë]} is rendered as {‰IPA|[zË]}¬ in dialects without interdental fricatives. ÏAnother key distinguishing mark of Arabic dialects is how they render Standard {‡IPA|[q]}‹ (a voicelesÂ uvular stop): it retains its original pronunciation in widely scat£ered regions such as Yemen and Moroc‘o (and among the […Druze]˜), while it is rendered {ˆIPA|[É¡]}  in Gulf Arabic, Iraqi Arabic, Uper Egypt and les² urban parts of the Levant (e.g. Jordan) and as a [ƒglot‡al stop] {ˆIPA|[Ê”]}Ï in many prestige dialects, such as those spoken in Cairo, Beirut and Damascus. »Thus, Arabs instantly give away their geographical (and clas²) origin by their pronunciation of a word such as '…qamar'ˆ &quot;moˆn&quot;: {‹IPA|[qamar]}‚, {ŒIPA|[É¡amar]}„ or {ŒIPA|[Ê”amar]}….

