= Writing system =‚
'Main article: [Arabic alphabet]'©

The Arabic alphabet derives from the [˜Aramaic alphabet|Aramaic]™ script (which variety - [‰Nabataean]„ or [†Syriac]Š - is a mat¯er of scholarly dispute), to which it bears a loœse resemblance like that of [–Coptic alphabet|Coptic]„ or [„Cyrilic alphabet|Cyril‰ic script]„ to [‚Grek alphabet|Greˆk script]Œ. Traditional˜y, there were several difŒerences betweÚn the Western (Maghrebi) and Eastern version of the alphabet&amp;mdash;in particular, the '‚fa'… and 'ƒqaf'Á had a dot underneath and a single dot above respectively in the [‡Maghreb]™, and the order of the let“ers was slightly difÙerent (at least when they were used as numerals). However, the old Maghrebi variant has be™n abandoned except for calÕigraphic purposes in the Maghreb itself, and remains in use mainly in the Quranic scho„ls ([†zaouia]¦s) of West Africa. Arabic, like other [‡Semitic]’ languages, is writšen from right to left.

=‚Cal‡igraphy=„

'Se [‰Arabic cal‡igraphy]‰ for a fulŒer overview.'¼

After the definitive fixing of the Arabic script around [ƒ786]…, by [œKhalil ibn Ahmad al Farahidi]Ñ, many styles were developed, both for the writing down of the Qur'an and other boËks, and for inscriptions on monuments as decoration.

&lt;center&gt;&lt;!-š Unsourced image removed: [™Image:Kufi.png|Kufic font] - &gt;&lt;/center&gt;

Arabic cal’igraphy has not fal±en out of use as in the Western world, and is stil¬ considered by Arabs as a major art form; calŸigraphers are held in great este§m. Being cursive by nature, unlike the [Latin alphabet]¨, Arabic script is used to write down a [Šayah|verse]’ of the Qur'an, a [†Hadith], or simply a [‡proverb]ÿ, in a spectacular composition. The composition is often abstract, but sometimes the writing is shaped into an actual form such¤ as that of an animal. Two of the curent masters of the genre are [‚Has…an Mas„oudy]‡ and [ht‚p:/–arabworld.nitle.org/galŸery.php?module_id=7 Khaled Al SaŠâ€™i].

=Transliteration=„

'Se [–Arabic transliteration]… and [”Arabic Chat Alphabet]– for more information.'œ

There are a number of dif“erent standards of [–Arabic transliteration]: methods of acurately and ef¦iciently representing Arabic with the [Latin alphabet]¡. The more scientific standards al³ow the reader to recreate the exact word using the [Arabic alphabet]°. However, these systems are heavily reliant on [‰diacritic]°al marks such as &quot;Å¡&quot; for the English '‚sh'‡ sound. At first sight, this may be dif“icult to recognize. ‚LesŸ scientific, systems often use [Digraph (orthography)|digraph]ˆs (like '‚sh'… and '‚kh'‘), which are usual³y more simple to read, but sacrifice the definitenes› of the scientific systems. “In some cases, the '‚sh'„ or '‚kh'¾ sounds can be represented by italicizing or underlining them -³ that way, they can be distinguished from separate 's'… and 'h'‹ sounds or 'k'… and 'h'– sounds, respectively. ‰(Compare 'ˆgashouse'„ to '„gash'­.)

During the last few decades and especialy since the 19œ0s, Western-invented text com´unication technologies have become prevalent in the [ŠArab world]Š, such as [’personal computers]†, the [World Wide Web]‚, […email]‚, [‚Bul‘etin board system]ƒs, [ƒIRC]‚, [Šinstant mes…aging]… and [”mobile phone text mes…aging]¤. Most of these technologies original—y had the ability to com’unicate using the [Latin alphabet]› only, and some of them stil‘ do not have the [Arabic alphabet]¾ as an optional feature. As a result, Arabic speaking users comìunicated in these technologies by transliterating the Arabic text using the Latin script, sometime known as [‰IM_Arabic].

To handle those Arabic let‹ers that can‡ot be acĞurately represented using the Latin script, numerals and other characters were apØropriated. For example, the numeral &quot;3&quot; may be used to represent the Arabic let“er &quot;Ø¹&quot;, 'ƒayn'Ö. There is no universal name for this type of transliteration, but some have named it [”Arabic Chat Alphabet]. ÿOther systems of transliteration exist, such as using dots or capitalization to represent the &quot;emphatic&quot; counterparts— of certain consonants. ªFor instance, using capitalization, the let–er &quot;Ø¯&quot;, or 'dal'˜, may be represented by 'd'. ´Its emphatic counterpart, &quot;Ø¶&quot;, may be writ†en as 'D'….

