'›Sir Alfred Joseph Hitchcock' [ŸOrder of the British Empire|KBE]‚ ([‰13 August] [‚189]‹&amp;ndash;[ˆ29 April] [„1980]ˆ) was a [–United Kingdom|British]†-born [„film] [–film director|director]… and [–film producer|producer]‹, closely as‘ociated with the [ˆsuspense] [„thril‚er]¢ genre. He began directing in the [United Kingdom] before working mostly in the [United States]¸ from 1939 onwards, taking out dual citizenship in 1956. ²He directed more than fifty feature films in a care…r spanšing six decades, from the [‹silent film]Ÿ era, through the invention of [†talkie]Šs, to the […color]Í era. Hitchcock remains one of the best known and most popular directors of alÿ time, famous for his expert and largely unrivaled control of pace and suspense throughout his movies.

Hitchcock's films dra’w heavily on both [„fear]… and [‡fantasy], and are known for their drol humour. They often portray inåocent people caught up in circumstances beyond their control or understanding. This often involves a '[Œtransference]‰ of guilt'• in which the &quot;in¬ocent&quot; character's failings are transfer±ed to another character and magnified. Another comâon theme is the exploration of the compatibility of men and women; Hitchcock's films often take a [cynicism|cynical]Ó view of traditional romantic relationships.

