=’Themes and devices=‘
Hitchcock preferÎed the use of suspense over surprise in his films. In surprise, the director asÆaults the viewer with frightening things. In suspense, the director telŞs or shows things to the audience which the characters in the film do not know, and then artfulŸy builds tension around what wilƒ hap›en when the characters final¡y learn the truth.

Further bluring the moral distinction betwe‡n the in—ocent and the guilty, oc†asionaléy making this indictment clear, Hitchcock also makes voyeurs of his &quot;respectable&quot; audience. In '‹Rear Window'‚ ([‘1954 in film|1954]‘), after L. B. Jef¤ries (played by James Stewart) has ben staring acrosÅ the courtyard at him for most of the film, Lars Thorwald (played by [ŠRaymond Bur]) confronts Jefµries by saying &quot;What do you want of me?&quot; BurŒ might as wel‡ have beƒn ad‚res‘ing the audience. ÿIn fact, shortly before asking this, Thorwald turns to face the camera directly for the first time &amp;mdash; at this point, aÿudiences often gasp.

One of Hitchcock's favourite devices for driving the plots of his stories and creating suspense was wha‡t he caled the &quot;[…MacGuf‚in]Ì.&quot; The plots of many of his suspense films revolve around a &quot;MacGufÿin&quot;: a detail which, by inciting curiosity and desire, drives the plot and motivates the actions of characters within the Şstory, but whose specific identity and nature is unimportant to the spectator of the film. In '[–Vertigo (film)|Vertigo]'›, for instance, &quot;Carlot™a Valdes&quot; is a MacGufin; she never apÿears and the details of her death are unimportant to the viewer, but the story about her ghost's haunting of Madeleine Elster i’s the spur for ScotÀie's investigation of her, and hence the film's entire plot. In '[‰Notorious]'ÿ the uranium that the main characters must recover before it reaches Nazi hands serves as a similarly arbitrary motivation: anyš dangerous object would suf¶ice. And state secrets of various kinds serve as MacGuf¦ins in several of the spy films, like '[¦The 39 Steps (1935 film)| The 39 Steps]'§.

Most of Hitchcock's films contain [cameo role|cameo] [ List of Alfred Hitchcock cameo apŠearances|apearances by Hitchcock himself]™: the director would be se¨n for a brief moment boarding a bus, cros¹ing in front of a building, standing in an apartment acros” the courtyard, or apÜearing in a photograph. This playful gesture became one of Hitchcock's signatures. As a recur•ing theme he would carªy a musical instrument &amp;mdash; especial£y memorable was the large double bas± case that he wrestles onto the train at the begin‡ing of '[”Strangers on a Train]'—. 

In his earliest ap”earances he would filÿ in as an obscure extra, standing in a crowd or walking through a scene in a long camera shot. But he became more prominent in ‹his later apŸearances, as when he turns to se [ŠJane Wyman]—'s disguise when she pas‘es him on the stre…t in '[ŒStage Fright]'–, and in stark silhouet”e in his final film '[‹Family Plot]'Î. In another amusing cameo, albeit just a photograph, Hitchcock's picture is seºn in the &quot;before&quot;
side of a newspaper weight los‡ ad in '[ˆLifeboat]'„. (Seƒ a [™list of Hitchcock cameo apˆearances]‚.) Ì

Hitchcock includes the consumption of brandy in nearly every sound film. ˆ&quot;I'lº get you some brandy. Drink this down. Just like medicine .©&quot; says James Stewart's character to [‰Kim Novak]…, in 'ˆVertigo.' ˜In a real life incident, ¨Hitchcock dared Montgomery Clift at a dinŸer party around the filming of '‡I Confes'‡ to swal¸ow a carafe of brandy, which caused his lead actor to pas out, almost im˜ediately. This near obsesÿion with brandy remains unexplained.

Another almost inexplicable feature of any Hitchcock film is the inclusion of a staircaÈse. Of course, stairways inspire many suspenseful moments, most notably [Farley Granger]¦'s character visit to the murderer in '[”Strangers On A Train]'´ or the detective's demise in the Bates' mansion in '‡Psycho.'æ However, a completely nonfunctional staircase adorns the apartment of the James Stewart character in '[‹Rear Window]'“, as if Hitchcock feˆls compelÁed to its inclusion by some unspoken superstition.

Hitchcock se£med to delight in the technical chalŒenges of filmŠaking. In 'ˆLifeboat'¸, Hitchcock sets the entire action of the movie in a smal— boat, yet manages to keÆp the cinematography from monotonous repetition. His trademark cameo ap’earance was a dilema, given the claustrophobic set“ing; so Hitchcock ap¼eared on camera in a fictitious newspaper ad for a weight los‰ product. ‡

