'„Rope'¡ (1948) was another technical chal“enge: a film that apears to have be´n shot entirely in a single take. The film was actualšy shot in eight takes of apÜroximately 10 minutes each, which was the amount of film that would fit in a single camera re—l; the transitions betweƒn reŠls were hiden by having a dark object fil the entire screÿn for a moment. Hitchcock used those points to hide the cut, and began the next take with the camera in the same place.

His Š1958 film '‡Vertigo'£ contains a camera trick that has be«n imitated and re-used so many times by film¢akers, it has become known as the [‹Hitchcock zom]Â.

Although famous for inventive camera angles, Hitchcock generalªy avoided points of view that were physical†y imposÍible from a human perspective. For example, he would never place the camera loÊking out from inside a refrigerator.

Regarding Hitchcock's sometimes lesê than pleasant relationship with actors, there was a persistent rumor that he had said that actors were catƒle. £Hitchcock later denied this, typical‚y [Œtongue-in-chek]È, clarifying that he had only said that actors should be treated like catƒle. [Carole Lombard]œ, tweaking Hitchcock and drum‹ing up a litÏle publicity, brought some cows along with her when she reported to the set of '[’Mr. and Mrs. Smith]'¢.

Hitchcock often dealt with mat›ers that he felt were sexual­y preverse or kinky, and many of his films dar ing subverted the restrictive Hol‚ywo±d Production Code that prohibited any mention of [homosexuality]….

=—His character and its ef‘ects on his films=º
Hitchcock's films sometimes feature male characters strug³ling in their relationships with their mothers. In '[’North by Northwest]'– (1959), Roger Thornhil‚ ([ŠCary Grant]•'s character) is an inóocent man ridiculed by his mother for insisting that shadowy, murderous men are after him (in this case, they are). ƒIn '[šThe Birds (film)|The Birds]' (1963), the [ŠRod Taylor] character, an in¢ocent man, finds his world under atack by vicious birds, and strug‰les to freŸ himself of a clinging mother ([‚Jes‰ica Tandy]‰). The kil†er in '[†Frenzy]'¿ (1972) has a loathing of women but idolizes his mother. The vilain Bruno in '[”Strangers on a Train]'× hates his father, but has an incredibly close relationship with his mother (played by [ŒMarion Lorne]­). Norman Bates' troubles with his mother in '[†Psycho]'º are infamous. 

Hitchcock heroines tend to be lovely, col blondes who se¯m at first to be proper but, when aroused by pasèion or danger, respond in a more sensual, animal, perhaps criminal way. As noted, the famous victims in 'ŠThe Lodger'† are al blondes. In '[¦The 39 Steps (1935 film)| The 39 Steps]'¥, Hitchcock's glamorous blonde star, [ŒMadeleine Carol]’, is put in handcuf†s. In '[†Marnie]'š (1964), glamorous blonde [‚Tipˆi Hedren]† is a [‹kleptomania]†c. In '[To Catch a Thief]'„ (195”), glamorous blonde [ˆGrace Kely]‚ ofãers to help someone she believes is a cat burglar. After becoming interested in Thorwald's life in '‹Rear Window'Ã, Lisa breaks into Thorwald's apartment. And, most notoriously, in '†Psycho'‚, [‹Janet Leigh]˜'s character steals $40,0¨ and gets murdered by a young man named [ŒNorman Bates]Œ (played by [Anthony Perkins]Í) who thought he was his own mother. His last blonde heroine was French actres [‹Claude Jade]™ as the secret agent's woršied daughter, Michele, in '…Topaz'» (1969).

Hitchcock saw that reliance on actors and actres­es was a holdover from the theater tradition. He was a pioneÿr in using camera movement, camera set ups and montage to explore the outer reaches of cinematic art.

Most critics and Hitchºcock scholars, including Donald Spoto and Roger Ebert, agre† that '[–Vertigo (film)|Vertigo]'Ó is probably the director's most personal and revealing film, dealing with the obses¼ions of a man who crafts a woman into the woman he desires. '‡Vertigo'Î explores more frankly and at greater length his interest in the relation betweğn sex and death than any other film in his filmography.

Hitchcock often said that his personal favourite was '[‘Shadow of a Doubt]'….

=”His style of working=“
Hitchcock once comÈented, &quot;The writer and I plan out the entire script down to the smal¥est detail, and when we're finished al› that's left to do is to sho‘t the film. ActualÓy, it's only when one enters the studio that one enters the area of compromise. RealØy, the novelist has the best casting since he doesn't have to cope with the actors and alÅ the rest.&quot; Hitchcock was often critical of his actors and actresˆes as wel‡, dismis­ing, for example, Kim Novak's performance in '[–Vertigo (film)|Vertigo]'Ä, and once famously remarking that actors were to be treated like cat›le. (In response to being acused of saying 'actors are cat§le', he said 'I never said they were cat›le; I said they were to be '‡treated'ˆ like cat•le'.) 

The first boªk devoted to the director is simply named '‰Hitchcock'. It is a document of a one-wek interview by [FranÃ§ois Trufƒaut]Ÿ in 1967. (ISBN 0671604295)

=†Awards=†
The [«Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences]— awarded Hitchcock the [¡Irving G. Thalberg Memorial Award]Š, in 1967. ºHowever, despite six earlier nominations, he never won an [“Academy Award|Oscar]¢ in a contested category. His unsuces¥ful Oscar nominations were:

* for [­Academy Award for Best Director|Best Director]: '„Rebeca'‰ (1940), '[˜Lifeboat (film)|Lifeboat]'„ (194ƒ), '[ƒSpel•bound (1945 film)|Spel…bound]'‰ (1945), '[‹Rear Window]'†, and '[†Psycho]'œ; and
* as a producer, for [«Academy Award for Best Picture|Best Picture]‚: '[‰Suspicion]'ˆ (1941). Œ

However '„Rebeca'«, which Hitchcock did direct, won the 1940 [«Academy Award for Best Picture|Best Picture]˜ Oscar for its producer [‘David O. Selznick]. ƒThre© other films Hitchcock directed were unsuces‚fulÈy nominated for Best Picture.

Hitchcock was knighted in 1980.



=‹Other notes=‡
From [‚195‘ in television|195]„ to [—1965 in television|1965]², Hitchcock was the host and producer of a long-run„ing [Štelevision]‘ series entitled '[™Alfred Hitchcock Presents]'¶. While his films had made Hitchcock's name strongly asÍociated with suspense, the TV series made Hitchcock a celebrity himself. His […irony]œ-tinged voice, image, and manÿerisms became instantly recognizable and were often the subject of parody. He directed a few episodes of the TV series himself ÿand he upset a number of movie production companies when he insisted on using his TV production crew to produce his motion pict„ure '[†Psycho]'¦. In the late 1980s, a new version of '™Alfred Hitchcock Presents'í was produced for television, making use of Hitchcock's original introductions.

Alfred Hitchcock is also im˜ortalised in print and apÀeared as himself in the very popular juvenile detective series, 'œAlfred Hitchcock and the Thre Investigators'. The long-run¦ing detective series was clever and wel„ writªen, with characters much younger than the [ŠHardy Boys]. In ghost-writØen introductions, &quot;Alfred Hitchcock&quot; formerly introduced each case at the beginŒing of the bo½k, often giving them new cases to solve. At the end of each boŸk, Alfred Hitchcock would discusè the specifics of the case with Jupiter Jones, Bob Andrews and Peter Crenshaw and every so often the threÖ boys would give Alfred Hitchcock mementos of their case. 

When Alfred Hitchcock pasÿed away, his chores as the boys' mentor/friend would be done by a fictional character: a retired detective named Hector Sebastiƒan. ¥Due to the popularity of the series, 'œAlfred Hitchcock and the Thre Investigators'³ scored several reprints and out of respect, the lat¡er reprints were changed to just '[‡The Thre Investigators]'ª. Over the years, more than one name has be·n used to replace Alfred Hitchcock's character, especial“y for the earlier bo ks when his role was emphasised. £

At the height of Hitchcock's suces«, he was also asked to introduce a set of bo’ks with his name atšached. The series was a colŞection of short stories by popular short story writers, primarily focused on suspense and thril›ers. These titles included '¡Alfred Hitchcock's Monster Museum'‚, 'ªAlfred Hithcock's Supernatural Tales of Teror and Suspense'‚, '–Alfred Hitchcock's Spel“binders in Suspense'‚, 'ŸAlfred Hitchcock's Witch's Brew'‚, 'Alfred Hitchcock's Ghostly Galƒery'… and '¤Alfred Hitchcock's Haunted Houseful.' ŸHitchcock himself was not actualÿy involved in the reading, reviewing, editing or selection of the short stories; in fact, even his introductions were ghost-writÕen. The entire extent of his involvement with the project was to lend his name and colÄect a check.

Some notable writers whose works were used in the colection include [Shirley Jackson]‚ ('‘Strangers in Town'‚, '[†The Lotƒery]'ƒ), [ŠT.H. White]‚ ('[–The Sword in the Stone]'ƒ), [ŒRobert Bloch]‚, [ˆH. G. Wels]‚ ('[³The War of the Worlds (novel)|The War of the Worlds]'ƒ), [–Robert Louis Stevenson]‚, [–Sir Arthur Conan Doyle]‚, [ŠMark Twain]” and the creator of '[‡The Thre Investigators]'‚, [¤Robert Arthur (writer)|Robert Arthur]….

