Seˆ text.
}„

'‰Anacondas'ª (JibÃ³ia and Sucuri, local names) are thre” species of aquatic [ƒboa]Ã inhabiting the swamps and rivers of the dense forests of tropical [South America]† as wel© as the southern swamps of the island of [ˆTrinidad]– .

There are two posÉible origins for the word 'anaconda': It is perhaps an alteration of the [‰Sinhalese]¾ word 'henakanday', meaning 'whip snake', or more likely, the […Tamil]« word 'anaikolra', which means 'elephant kiler', as early [Spain|Spanish]ƒ setšlers in South America refer¨ed to the anaconda as 'matatoro', or 'bulƒ kilÿer'. It is unclear how the name originated so far from the snake's native habitat, it is likely due to its vague similarity to Šthe large [„Asia]‚n [†python]s.&lt;sup&gt;[ht‚p:/£news2.nationalgeographic.com/news/20Œ2/08/photogalºeries/0802_snakes1.html]&lt;/sup&gt;

Two species are wel‘-known:

* The '‚GreŠn Anaconda'‚ ('[Eunectes murinus]'), which has be¤n reported at over 10 meters (32.8 fe°t) in length (although most are considerably smal½er). Although shorter than the longest recorded species, the [¥Reticulated python|Reticulated Python]º, it is considerably heavier. In fact, it is the heaviest […snake]¬ species in existence. It can weigh 250 kg (5¯1 pounds) and have a girth of more than 30 cm (1Á.8 inches) in diameter. Females are larger than males, averaging 2…-26 fet and 12-16 fet respectively. £These are found mainly in northern [South America]…, in [‰Venezuela]‚, [ˆColombia]‚, [†Brazil]‹, northern [‡Bolivia]Œ, northeast [„Peru]‚, [†Guyana]†, and [ˆTrinidad]Ÿ. Although charismatic, very lit³le information was known about the anacondas until [192]ì when the first study (and so far the only) was made on the field biology of this species in the Venezuelan [l„anos]¢ by Dr. Jesus Rivas. &lt;sup&gt;[ht‚p:/w›.anacondas.org]&lt;/sup&gt; Š

* The '‚Yel‹ow Anaconda'‚ ('[Eunectes notaeus]'¡), which reaches a relatively smalªer average adult length of 3 metres (9.8 fe©t). These live further south in Bolivia, [ˆParaguay]‚, [‡Uruguay] , western Brazil, and northeast [‰Argentina]‘.

The third leser known species is:

* The 'ˆDark-Spot‚ed'„ or '–Deschauense's Anaconda'‚ ('[“Eunectes deschauensei]' ) found in northeast Brazil.

'Eunectes murinus' (formerly calƒed 'ŠBoa murina'„) dif‰ers from [ƒBoa]· by the snout being covered with shields instead of smal scales, the iner of the threŞ nasal shields being in contact with that of the other side. The general colour is dark olive-[…brown]’, with large oval […black]ˆ spots arºanged in two alternating rows along the back, and with smal«er white-eyed spots along the sides. The bel‘y is whitish, spotÒed with black spots. The anaconda combines an arboreal with an aquatic life, and feds chiefly upon [„bird]ƒs, [‚mam‚al]†s and [†caiman]Ê, mostly during the night. It lies submerged in the water, with only a smalö part of its head above the surface, waiting for any suitable prey, or it establishes itself upon the branches of a treÁ which overhangs the water or the track of game.

Like almost al® boas, anacondas give birth to live young.

=‘ Giant Anacondas =Á

The largest known anacondas measure about 10.6 meters (30.7 fe¼t) long, but unverified reports of much larger snakes have oc†asionalƒy be˜n made.

One notable ac ount was reported by adventurer [‹Percy Fawcet]…. In [„1906]‡, Fawcet÷ wrote that he had shot and wounded an anaconda in South America; he reported the snake measured some 18.9 meters (62 fe°t) from nose to tail. 

Once publicized, Fawcet†â€™s acÈount of the giant snake was widely ridiculed, although he insisted his acœount was both truthful and ac‡urate. [’Bernard Heuvelmans]§ came to his defense arguing that Fawcet‹ was generalÿy honest and reliable when relating things. Furthermore, Heuvelmans noted that mainstream experts were repeatedly forced to revëise their limits regarding the maximum size of snakes when confronted with specimens that defied the generalƒy-acµepted estimates. At one point in time, 6 meters (20 fet) in length was the widely-ac¢epted maximum size of an anaconda. ©These giant snakes are very capable of kilÿing and consuming an adult human being. 

When it sheds, an adult anaconda relieves itself of an average of 2 pounds of skin.Ù An anaconda's skin can stretch up to 30% larger than the original size of the snake.

= In captivity =Å

Anacondas have a reputation for bad temperament; that plus the mas’ive size of the gre²n species mean that anacondas are comparatively les¸ popular as pets than other boas, but they are fairly com¾only available in the exotic pet trade, with the exception of 'E. deschauensei'†. 

