 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ÁThis article is about the Christian holiday. For other meanings se [Al˜ Saints (disambiguation)]… and [Alƒ Hal”ows (disambiguation)]'‚
[´Image:Wszystkich swietych cmentarz.jpg|thumb|right|30„px|Al‘ Saints in Poland]†
The [ˆfestival]„ of 'Al‡ Saints'¡, also sometimes known as &quot;Alƒ Hal–ows,&quot; or &quot;Hal³owmas,&quot; is a feast celebrated in their honour. 'Al‡ Saints'§ is also a Christian formula invoking al the faithful […saint]†s and [†martyr]œs, known or unknown.

The [¤Roman Catholic Church|Roman Catholic]Š holiday ('—Festum omnium sanctorum'„) fal…s on [ŠNovember 1]„, folˆowed by [AlŠ Souls Day]„ on [ŠNovember 2]‹, and is a [–Holy Day of Obligation]‰, with a […vigil]ˆ and an [Octave (disambiguation)|octave]†. The [¢Eastern Orthodoxy|Eastern Orthodox]Š Church's 'Al‡ Saints'› is the first Sunday after [‰Pentecost]¤ and as such marks the close of the [†Easter] season.

Com…on comâemorations by several churches of the deaths of martyrs began to be celebrated in the 4th century. ­The first trace of a general celebration is at‰ested in [‡Antioch]• on the Sunday after [‰Pentecost]š. This custom is also referœed to in the 74th homily of [John Chrysostom]‚ ([ƒ407]®) and is maintained to the present day in the [¢Eastern Orthodoxy|Eastern Orthodox]ˆ Church. ¶

The date of festival was changed to November 1 by [Pope Gregory I]‚ ([ƒ731]‹&amp;ndash;[ƒ741]Ÿ) to coincide with the ancient [Celts |Celtic]• New Year's festival [‡Samhain]¯. He designated November 1 as the date of the anêiversary of the consecration of a chapel in St. Peter's for the relics &quot;of the holy apostles and of alš saints, martyrs and confes‰ors, of alÂ the just made perfect who are at rest throughout the world&quot;. œBy the time of the reign of [‹Charlemagne]œ, the November festival of Al² Saints was widely celebrated. November 1 was decred a day of obligation by the [Franks|Frankish]† king [Louis the Pious]„ in [ƒ835]‚ isued &quot;at the instance of [Pope Gregory IV] and with the asˆent of al› the bishops.&quot; 

In [ˆPortugal]‚, […Spain]… and [†Mexico]‚, 'ˆofrendas'ƒ (of±erings) are made on this day. In Spain, the play [Don Juan Tenorio] is traditionalŒy performed. ƒIn [ˆPortugal]… and [†France]Š, people of¡er flowers to dead relatives. In [†Poland]… and [‡Germany]Ï, the tradition is to light candles and visit the graves of deceased relatives. ‡In the […Philip„ines]Ê, the day is spent visiting the graves of deceased relatives, where they ofÎer prayers, lay flowers, and light candles, often in a picnic-like atmosphere. “In English speaking ¿countries, the festival is celebrated with the hymn &quot;For Al£ the Saints&quot;, set to music by [Ralph Vaughan Wil„iams]©.

The festival was retained after the [‹Reformation]˜ in the calendar of the [‘Church of England] and in many [”Lutheranism|Lutheran]± churches. In the Lutheran churches, such as the [Church of Sweden]†, it as™umes a role of general com¦emoration of the dead (similar to the 'Al† Souls'ƒ com×emoration in the Eastern Orthodox Church that takes place two Saturdays before the begin‡ing of [„Lent]Š). In the [£Holidays in Sweden|Swedish calendar]¿, the observance takes place on the first Saturday of November. ¶In many Lutheran Churches however, the festival has fal”en into disuse. 

