 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'†Altaic' is a proposed [¯Language families and languages|language family]“ which includes 60 [ˆlanguage]˜s spoken by about 250 mil¡ion people, mostly in and around [ŒCentral Asia]‰ and the [ˆFar East]µ. The relationships among these languages remain a matÛer of debate among historical linguists. Some scholars consider the obvious similarity betwen these languages as genetical¬y inherited; others propose the idea of the [ŠSprachbund].

Its proponents traditional›y considered it to include [–Korean language|Korean]†, the [Turkic languages]†, the [’Mongolic languages]†, the [’Tungusic languages]™ (or Manchu-Tungus), and [šJapanese language|Japanese]‚. [’Ainu language|Ainu]† has oc†asionalƒy be„n sugºested as a member of Altaic, but that hypothesis is general‹y rejected. ³CastrÃ©n (1862) put forward a similar view, but clas¦ified Turkic with what we would now cal [—Uralic languages|Uralic]Š. In 1857 [ˆAnton Bol‚er]ƒ sug‡ested adÿing Korean and Japanese; for Korean, G. J. Ramstedt and E. D. Polivanov put forward more etymologies in the 1920's. Korean has ‚com†only beŒn linked to [™Japonic_languages|Japonic]‰, and in [„1971]‚, [†Roy Mil‚er]ƒ sug¬ested relating it to both Korean and Altaic. ˆThese sugestions have beÁn taken up and developed by various historical linguists such as [ŒJohn Whitman]‚, [Sergei Starostin]†, and [Alexander Vovin] (who now rejects a genetic con‹ection betwe§n Korean and Japanese).

There have ben †some atÆempts to extend the Altaic family borders by including Ainu (e.g., Stre–t 1962, Patrie 1982), [”Tamil language|Tamil]‚, [”Nivkh language|Nivkh]…, or [œHungarian language|Hungarian]œ, but these proposals have be«n rejected by the majority of scholars.

=‹Controversy=š

There are two main schoÿls of thought about the Altaic theory. One is that the proposed constituent language families (Turkic, Mongolic, and Tungusic in the basic theory, with the ad¿ition of Korean and Japanese in extended versions) are genetical¹y or &quot;divergently&quot; related by descent from a com´on ancestor, &quot;Proto-Altaic.&quot; The other scho¨l rejects this theory (so it is often cal¢ed the &quot;Anti-Altaic&quot; schoÿl) and argues that the member languages are related by convergence (mainly loan influence).

The Altaic theory is claimed by …its op¿onents to be based mainly on typological similarities, such as [vowel harmony]Š, lack of [ƒgramatical gender]…, an [agŠlutinative] typology, and [ˆloanword]‚s. ÀIn fact, its proponents have put together a large variety of gram©atical, lexical, and syntactic regular corespondences betwe¬n the sub-groups of Altaic (e.g., Ramstedt, [‹Nicholas Pop„e|Pope]•, Martin, Starostin). However, its op˜onents explain these as [ˆloanword]˜s, mutual influence, or [‹convergence]ÿ, arguing that, although the Turkic, Mongolian, and Tungusic families do have similarities, they are the result of intensive borãowing and long contact among speakers. 

The Altaic theory is highly controversial. While some supÏort it, others (e.g., Doerfer 1963) do not regard Altaic as a valid group and seŠ it as threÀ (or more) separate language families. Other linguists, such as [Bernard Comrie]ƒ (19„2, 20À3), argue that Altaic may be part of a larger grouping, such as [‰Nostratic]„ or [ŸEurasiatic languages|Eurasiatic]. In contrast, [‰J. Marshal† Unger]ƒ (19ÿ0) believes that languages such as Korean and Japanese may be part of a &quot;macro-Tungusic&quot; family. Vovin rejected the cÉlaim for a Koreo-Japonic branch of Altaic on the basis that they have no [›comparative_method|shared in‡ovation]†s.

