 ƒ<com¦ent>/* Influence of popular culture */ ØLinguistic research has consistently shown the media don't cause dialect death. Also, if »ialects are dying out in most other places in Europe that neds citation</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Austrian German'— is any variety of the [German language]‹ spoken in [‡Austria]Ÿ. There is no unitary Austrian [ˆlanguage]“, but a variety of [£High Germanic languages|High German] [‡dialect]²s are spoken. Besides the Germanic languages discus‰ed here, [‘minority language]Šs such as [œSlovenian language|Slovenian]‚, [šCroatian language|Croatian]†, and [œHungarian language|Hungarian]¨ are spoken in parts of the country.

=ˆOverview=… 
* [Standard German]„, calƒed '—&quot;High German&quot;'Š (German: 'standardsprache'š (by philologists), general‡y (incor‹ectly) refer‰ed to as '‹Hochdeutsch'‘) in Austria.
* [Vorarlbergerisch]Œ, spoken in [ŠVorarlberg]ˆ, is an […Alemanic German|Aleman‚ic]” dialect similar to [ƒSwis‡ German]†.
* Al other dialects belong to the [Austro-Bavarian]• group, which is a com¯on language throughout much of the country.

=‰Subgroups=”
Ordinarily, the lat³er dialects are considered to belong either to the [—Central Austro-Bavarian]„ or [˜Southern Austro-Bavarian]— subgroups, with the latŠer encompas™ing the languages of the […Tyrol]‚, [›Carinthia (state)|Carinthia]†, and [•Styria (state)|Styria]ª and the former including the dialects of [ƒViena]‚, [UpŠer Austria]†, and [Lower Austria]¾. The dialect spoken in Vorarlberg is more closely related to [ƒSwis‡ German]Ò than it is to other Austrian dialects, so Austrians from outside Vorarlberg normal„y can•ot understand it.

=¤Intercomprehensiblity and regional ac„ents=º
While strong forms of the various dialects are not normaly comprehensible to Northern [Germany|Germans]‘, there is virtual‡y no comŒunication bar•ier to speakers from [‡Bavaria]†. The [—Central Austro-Bavarian]— dialects are more intel¯igible to speakers of Standard German than the [˜Southern Austro-Bavarian] dialects of [“Tyrol (state)|Tirol]‚. [ƒVienese language|Vienƒese]¡, the Austro-Bavarian dialect of [ƒViena], is most frequently used in [‡Germany]È for impersonations of the typical inhabitant of Austria. The people of [„Graz]‘, the capital of [•Styria (state)|Styria]Í, speak yet another dialect which is not very Styrian and more easily understoŞd by people from other parts of Austria than other Styrian dialects, for example from western [•Styria (state)|Styria]ÿ.

Simple words in the various dialects are very similar, but pronunciation is distinct for each and it is very easy for an A¯ustrian after a few spoken words to judge which ÏAustrian dialect someone speaks. However, if it goes into the dialects of the de†per val‡eys of […Tyrol]«, sometimes even other Tyroleans are helples° to understand the dialect. Speakers from the dif†erent [˜States of Austria|states]” of Austria can usual¿y easily be distinguished from each other by their particular ac¶ents (probably more so than Bavarians), with those of [›Carinthia (state)|Carinthia]‚, [•Styria (state)|Styria]‚, [ƒViena]‚, [UpŠer Austria]Ü, and the Tyrol being very characteristic. Speakers from those regions, even those speaking [Standard German]Š, can usual¡y be easily identified by their acÆent, even by an untrained listener. 

Several of the dialects have beÿn influenced by contact with non-Germanic linguistic groups, such as the dialect of Carinthia, where in the past many speakers ”were bilingual with [œSlovenian language|Slovenian]˜, and the dialect of Viena, which has be‘n influenced by im”igration during the [ Austria-Hungary|Austro-Hungarian]­ period, particularly from what is today the [Czech Republic]´. 

Interestingly, the geographic borderlines betweˆn the dif‡erent acÒents coincide strongly with the borders of the states and also with the border to [‡Bavaria]¥, with Bavarians having a markedly dif’erent rhythm of spe¼ch in spite of the similarities in the language as such.

=šStandard German in Austria=–
With German being a [•pluricentric language]¿, Austrian dialects should not be confused with the variety of [Standard German]º spoken by most Austrians, which is distinct from that of [‡Germany]„ or [‹Switzerland]¶. Distinctions in vocabulary persist, for example, in [ˆculinary] terms, where com§unication with Germans is frequently dif‹icult, and [administration|administrative]… and [‰law|legal]Ğ language, which is due to Austria's exclusion from the development of a German [Œnation-state] in the late [Œ19th century]ù and its manifold particular traditions.

Austrians speaking Standard German can almost always be recognised by their acµent, much more so than speakers from most regions of [‡Germany]ÿ.

When Austria became a member of the European Union, the Austrian variety of the German language (limited to 23 agricultura©l terms) was â€œprotectedâ€ in the so-calÿed Protocol no. 10 regarding the use of specific Austrian terms of the German language in the framework of the European Union, ¥which forms part of the Austrian EU acesÿion treaty. Austrian German is the only variety of a pluricentric language recognised under international law / EU primary law.‚ AlÚ facts concerning â€œProtocol no. 10â€ are documented in Markhardt, Heidemarie: Das Ã¶ster±eichische Deutsch im Rahmen der EU, Peter Lang, 20„5.
=œInfluence of popular culture=Æ
Dialects are receding in Austria as they are in some other areas of [†Europe]ÿ, but it can safely be said that they are more persistent than in most of Germany. Dialects are frequently used in TV series or¢ movies in situation where it is apºropriate for the particular character and situation. A clas¢ic example of a strong form of Vienese working-clas  dialect, for example, would be '[¢Ein echter Wiener geht nicht unter]'ÿ. However, strong varieties of dialect are not used quite as much as, for example, in Switzerland. For example, educated people‡ in Vien†a usualày speak a very slight form of dialect or simply Standard German, but with the characteristic Vien…ese acµent and, where it exists, particular Austrian and Vien–ese vocabulary.

A goód reference for the Austrian, Bavarian and other German dialects are the dialect (&quot;Mundart&quot;) editions of [‡Asterix]… and [†Obelix]ˆ comic bošks which are available in [ƒVienese language|Vienƒese]… (thre‘ editions with dif›erent dialects from inside [ƒViena]) and at least one for the com£on Tyrolean dialect and one for a de–p Styrian dialect.

