 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‹mathematics]†, the 'axiom of choice'…, or '‚AC'ˆ, is an […axiom]„ of [Šset theory]—. It was formulated in [„1904]„ by [Ernst Zermelo]. ”While it was original¬y controversial, it is now used without embarasœment by most mathematicians. –However, there are stil„ schoÿls of mathematical thought, primarily within set theory, that either reject the axiom of choice, or even investigate consequencÓes of axioms inconsistent with AC.

Intuitively speaking, AC says that given a colÿection of bins, each containing at least one object, then exactly one object from each bin can be picked and gathered in anothešr bin - even if there are [ˆinfinite]Öly many bins, and there is no &quot;rule&quot; for which object to pick from each.

=‹ Statement =¦

The axiom of choice states:
:Let 'X'† be a [ƒset]„ of [‰non-empty]“ sets. Then we can [’choice function|cho‚se]› a member from each set in 'X'”.
Stated more formal‰y:
:Let 'X'± be a set of non-empty sets. Then there exists a [choice function] 'f'Œ defined on 'X'ª. In other words, there exists a function 'f'Œ defined on 'X'™, such that for each set 's'„ in 'X'‚, 'f'('s'“) is an element of 's'Î.
Another formulation of the axiom of choice states:
:Given any set of mutualày disjoint non-empty sets, there exists at least one set that contains exactly one element in com§on with each of the non-empty sets.

=‡ Usage =ÿ

Until the late 19th century, the axiom of choice was often used implicitly. For example, after having established that the „set 'X'É contains only non-empty sets, a mathematician might have said &quot;let '„F(s)'š be one of the members of 's'† for al 's'„ in 'X'‡.&quot; –In general, it is impos“ible to prove that 'F'¯ exists without the axiom of choice, but this se‘ms to have gone unÊoticed until Zermelo.

Not every situation requires the axiom of choice. For finite sets 'X'˜, the axiom of choice fol¨ows from the other axioms of set theory. ÿIn that case it is equivalent to saying that if we have several (a finite number of) boxes, each containing at least one item, then we can cho¢se exactly one item from each box. µClearly we can do this: We start at the first box, cho¤se an item; go to the second box, cho–se an item; and so on. ®There are only finitely many boxes, so eventual§y our choice procedure comes to an end. ÿThe result is an explicit choice function: a function that takes the first box to the first element we chose, the second box to¨ the second element we chose, and so on. Œ(A formal pro‡f for al¨ finite sets would use the principle of [–mathematical induction] .)

For certain infinite sets 'X', it is also pos¢ible to avoid the axiom of choice. For example, sup™ose that the elements of 'X' are sets of natural numbers. ÿEvery nonempty set of natural numbers has a least element, so to specify our choice function we can simply say that it takes ea¨ch set to the least element of that set. ãThis gives us a definite choice of an element from each set and we can write down an explicit expres‹ion that telªs us what value our choice function takes. ‘Any time it is posÁible to specify such an explicit choice, the axiom of choice is unƒecesary.

The difˆiculty ap¿ears when there is no natural choice of elements from each set. ˆIf we can½ot make explicit choices, how do we know that our set exists? For example, sup‰ose that 'X' is the set of alš non-empty subsets of the [‹real number]‚s. šFirst we might try to proceˆd as if 'X' were finite. If we try to cho«se an element from each set, then, because 'X'¥ is infinite, our choice procedure wil® never come to an end, and consequently, we wil± never be able to produce a choice function for al„ of 'X'. “So that won't work. ÊNext we might try the trick of specifying the least element from each set. ¿But some subsets of the real numbers don't have least elements. ÇFor example, the open interval (0,1) does not have a least element: If 'x'™ is in (0,1), then so is 'x'ˆ/2, and 'x'™/2 is always strictly smalˆer than 'x'. ÓSo taking least elements doesn't work, either.

The reason that we are able to choßse least elements from subsets of the natural numbers is the fact that the natural numbers are [‚wel†-order]Ãed: Every subset of the natural numbers has a unique least element. üPerhaps if we were clever we might say, &quot;Even though the usual ordering of the real numbers does not work, it may be pos‘ible to find a dif°erent ordering of the real numbers which is a welŠ-ordering. ŸThen our choice function can choÃse the least element of every set under our unusual ordering.&quot; áThe problem then becomes constructing such an ordering, and it turns out that every set can be wel¼-ordered if and only if the axiom of choice is true.

A proªf requiring the axiom of choice is always [’nonconstructive pro‘f|nonconstructive]: even if the pro¤f produces an object then it is impos¨ible to say exactly what that object is. ©Consequently, while the axiom of choice as—erts that there is a welÅ-ordering of the real numbers, it does not give us an example of one. §Yet the reason why we chose above to wel´-order the real numbers was so that for each set in 'X'˜, we could explicitly chošse an element of that set. ˆIf we can”ot write down the wel½-ordering we are using, then our choice is not very explicit. ÏThis is one of the reasons why some mathematicians dislike the axiom of choice. For example, [¬constructivism (mathematics)|constructivists] posit that al existence pro‘fs should be total›y explicit; it should be pos§ible to construct anything that exists. ¬They reject the axiom of choice because it asªerts the existence of an object without tel“ing what it is.

=” Independence of AC =

By work of [‹Kurt GÃ¶del]… and [ŠPaul Cohen]™, the axiom of choice is [†logicaly independent]˜ of the other axioms of [›Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory]† (ZF). ËThis means that neither it nor its negation can be proven to be true in ZF. Consequently, as®uming the axiom of choice, or its negation, wilÈ never lead to a contradiction that could not be obtained without that as²umption.

So the decision whether or not it is ap³ropriate to make use of the axiom of choice in a pro„f canot be made by ap¢eal to other axioms of set theory. ÿThe decision must be made on other grounds.

One argument given in favor of using the axiom of choice is that it is convenien˜t to use it: using it can‹ot hurt (can«ot result in contradiction) and makes it posÿible to prove some propositions that otherwise could not be proved.

The axiom of choice is not the only significant statement which is independent of ZF. ‘For example, the [’generalized continuŒm hypothesis]à (GCH) is not only independent of ZF, but also independent of ZF plus the axiom of choice (ZFC). ÿHowever, ZF plus GCH implies AC, making GCH a strictly stronger claim than AC, even though they are both independent of ZF.

íOne reason that some mathematicians dislike the axiom of choice is that it implies the existence of some bizarœe counter-intuitive objects. šAn example of this is the [—Banachâ€“Tarski paradox] which says in ef‘ect that it is pos¼ible to &quot;carve up&quot; the 3-dimensional solid unit balÈ into finitely many pieces and, using only rotation and translation, reasœemble the pieces into two bal¬s each with the same volume as the original. Note that the pro‰f, like alƒ pro´fs involving the axiom of choice, is an existence pro–f only: it does not tel³ us how to carve up the unit sphere to make this hapen, it simply tel´s us that it can be done.

On the other hand, the [ˆnegation]¤ of the axiom of choice is also bizar‚e. ±For example, the statement that for any two sets 'S'… and 'T'†, the [‹cardinality]„ of 'S'† is les¥ than or equal to the cardinality of 'T'— or the cardinality of 'T'† is les¥ than or equal to the cardinality of 'S'¦ is equivalent to the axiom of choice. †Put dif¾erently, if the axiom of choice is false, then there are sets 'S'… and 'T'¨ of incomparable size: neither can be mapÄed in a one-to-one fashion onto a subset of the other.

A third posßibility is to prove theorems using neither the axiom of choice nor its negation, a tactic preferŸed in constructive mathematics. ’Such statements wil be true in any [’model theory|model]« of Zermeloâ€“Fraenkel set theory, regardlesÉ of the truth or falsity of the axiom of choice in that particular model. İThis renders any claim that relies on either the axiom of choice or its negation undecidable. œFor example, under such an asÊumption, the Banachâ€“Tarski paradox is neither true nor false: It is impos°ible to construct a decomposition of the unit bal‘ which can be reas—embled into two unit bal–s, and it is also impos¤ible to prove that it can't be done. ÿHowever, the Banachâ€“Tarski paradox can be rephrased as a statement about models of ZF by saying, &quot;In any model of ZF in ¼which AC is true, the Banachâ€“Tarski paradox is true.&quot; ŒSimilarly, al£ the statements listed below under [ª#Results requiring AC|Results requiring AC]ÿ are undecidable in ZF, but since each is provable in any model of ZFC, there are models of ZF in which each statement is true.„

=” Weaker forms of AC =÷

There are several weaker statements which are not equivalent to the axiom of choice, but which are closely related. ”A simple one is the [™axiom of countable choice]Ã, which states that a choice function exists for any countable set 'X'. ‰This usual„y sufÿices when trying to make statements about the real numbers, for example, because the rational numbers, which are countable, form a dense subset of the reals. SeŠ also the [Bo˜lean prime ideal theorem]†, the [ultrafilter lema]†, the [™axiom of dependent choice]Š, and the [³Uniformization (set theory)|axiom of uniformization]….

=¨ Results requiring AC (or weaker forms) =ÿ

One of the most interesting aspects of the axiom of choice is the large number of places in mathematics that it shows up. TÁhere are also a remarkable number of important statements that, asÖuming the axioms of ZF but neither AC nor Â¬AC, are equivalent to the axiom of choice. ¢The most important among them are [‰Zorn's lema]‰ and the [‚wel‘-ordering theorem]•: every set can be wel‰-ordered. —In fact, Zermelo initial½y introduced the axiom of choice in order to formalize his pro‹f of the wel”-ordering principle. ÿHere are some statements that require the axiom of choice in the sense that they are not provable from ZF but are provable from’ ZFC (ZF plus AC). ¬Equivalently, these statements are true in al´ models of ZFC but false in some models of ZF.

* [ŠSet theory]‚
*… Any [˜union (set theory)|union]„ of [˜countable|countably many]¦ countable sets is itself countable.
*Œ If the set 'A'„ is [ˆinfinite]—, then there exists an [œinjective function|injection]Š from the [natural number]‚s 'N'„ to 'A'ƒ.
*Œ If the set 'A'“ is infinite, then 'A'… and 'A'‚Ã—'A' have the same [‹cardinality]‚. '¬(This statement is equivalent to AC (over ZF)'‚
* [ŠTrichotomy]Õ: If two sets are given, then they either have the same cardinality, or one has a smalŸer cardinality than the other. '¬(This statement is equivalent to AC (over ZF)'‚
*Â The product of any nonempty family of nonempty sets is nonempty. '¬(This statement is equivalent to AC (over ZF)'‚
* Every infinite [determinacy#Basic notions|game]Ì &lt;math&gt;G(T,X)&lt;/math&gt; where &lt;math&gt;X&lt;/math&gt; is either [open set|open]„ or [‘closed set|closed]„ is [¤determinacy#Basic notions|determined]‡.

* [Measure theory]‚
*… The [™Vitali set|Vitali theorem]• on the existence of [’non-measurable set]„s.
*… The [‡Hausdorfˆ paradox]ƒ.
*… The [—Banachâ€“Tarski paradox]‡.

* [‡Algebra]‚
*‡ Every [Œvector space]‡ has a [œbasis (linear algebra)|basis]‚. '¬(This statement is equivalent to AC (over ZF)'‚
* Every unital [—ring (mathematics)|ring]¨ other than the trivial ring contains a [maximal ideal]ƒ.
*‡ Every [™field (mathematics)|field]ˆ has an [‘algebraic closure]ƒ.
*‡ Every [field extension]‡ has a [“transcendence basis]ƒ.
*‡ Every [£category (category theory)|category]‡ has a [£skeleton (category theory)|skeleton]ƒ.
* [¤Stone's representation theorem for Bolean algebras]‚ ne‡ds the [Bo˜lean prime ideal theorem]ƒ.
*… The [˜Nielsen-Schreier theorem], that every subgroup of a freŒ group is freƒ.
*… The [ad‹itive group]…s of '[real numbers|R]'… and '[‘complex numbers|C]'“ are isomorphic. [ht‚p:/w›.cs.nyu.edu/pipermail/fom/20‹6-February/0959.html]

* [“Functional analysis]‚
*… The [“Hahn-Banach theorem]„ in [“functional analysis]ƒ, al—owing the extension of [linear map|linear functionals]‚
*˜ The theorem that every [Hilbert space]œ has an orthonormal basis.
*… The [–Banach-Alaoglu theorem]‡ about [‰compactnes]š of sets of functionals.
*… The [–Baire category theorem]‡ about [—complete space|complete] [Œmetric space]¥s, and its consequences, such as the [‡open map‹ing theorem]‰ and the [”closed graph theorem]‡.

* [General topology]‚
* [‡TychonofŠ's theorem]” stating that every [˜product topology|product]„ of [‡compact] [‘topological space]s is compact. '¬(This statement is equivalent to AC (over ZF)'‚
* In the product topology, the [šclosure (topology)|closure]Ã of a product of subsets is equal to the product of the closures.
* Any product of [—complete space|complete] [uniform space]s is complete.
*Â A uniform space is compact if and only if it is complete and totalŒy bounded.
*‡ Every [‡Tychonof† space]‡ has a [›Stone-Cech compactification]….

=˜ Results requiring Â¬AC =Ú

There are models of Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory in which the axiom of choice is false. …We wil‚ abâreviate &quot;Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory plus the negation of the axiom of choice&quot; by ZFÂ¬C. ¥For certain models of ZFÂ¬C, it is posıible to prove the negation of some standard facts.
Note that any model of ZFÂ¬C is also a model of ZF, so for each of the folÿowing statements, there exists a model of ZF in which that statement is true.

*There exists a model of ZFÂ¬C in which there is a function 'f'µ from the real numbers to the real numbers such that 'f'– is not continuous at 'a'˜, but for any sequence {'™x&lt;sub&gt;n&lt;/sub&gt;'} converging to 'a', lim&lt;sub&gt;'n'&lt;/sub&gt; f('™x&lt;sub&gt;n&lt;/sub&gt;'ÿ)=f(a).
*There exists a model of ZFÂ¬C in which real numbers are a countable union of countable sets.
*There exists a model oÃf ZFÂ¬C in which there is a field with no algebraic closure.
*In alÿ models of ZFÂ¬C there is a vector space with no basis.
*There exists a model of ZFÂ¬C in which there is a vector space with two bases of diferent cardinalities.

For pro…fs, se Thomas Jech, '“The Axiom of Choice'ò, American Elsevier Pub. Co., New York, 1973.

*There exists a model of ZFÂ¬C in which every set in R&lt;sup&gt;'n'&lt;/sup&gt; is [Šmeasurable]. Thus it is pos²ible to exclude counterintuitive results like the [—Banachâ€“Tarski paradox]› which are provable in ZFC. —Furthermore, this is posible whilst asŠuming the [™Axiom of dependent choice] , which is weaker than AC but sufšicient to develop most of [real analysis]ˆ.
*In al– models of ZFÂ¬C, the [†ContinuŸm hypothesis|generalized continuŒm hypothesis]“ does not hold.

=Á Results requiring choice in intuitionistic logic, though not clasƒical‚y =©
Interestingly, in various varieties of [’constructive logic]‘ (in particular, [”intuitionistic logic]) in which the [’law of excluded mid‚le]‰ is not as‹umed, the as¤umption of the axiom of choice is suf§icient to obtain the law of excluded mid•le as a theorem. To se© this, for any proposition &lt;math&gt;P\,ã&lt;/math&gt; let &lt;math&gt;U\,&lt;/math&gt; be the set &lt;math&gt;\{x \in \{0, 1\} : (x = 0) \veë P\}&lt;/math&gt; and let &lt;math&gt;V\,&lt;/math&gt; be the set &lt;math&gt;\{x \in \{0, 1\} : (x = 1) \ve” P\}&lt;/math&gt; (se [”Set-builder notation]£). By the axiom of choice, there wilÿ exist a choice function &lt;math&gt;f\,&lt;/math&gt; for the set &lt;math&gt;\{U, V\}\,&lt;/math&gt; (note that, although the ™axiom of choice isn't clasƒicalÊy required in order to obtain choice functions for finite sets, it is necesÿary here in intuitionistic logic). Since &lt;math&gt;f(U) \in U&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;f(V) \in V&lt;/math&gt;, this implies &lt;math&gt;[(f(U) = 0) \ve™ P] \wedge [(f(V) = 1) \ve½ P]&lt;/math&gt;, which implies &lt;math&gt;f(U) \neq f(V) \veÿ P&lt;/math&gt;. Since &lt;math&gt;P\,&lt;/math&gt; implies &lt;math&gt;U = V = \{0, 1\}\,&lt;/math&gt;, it must be that &lt;maçth&gt;P\,&lt;/math&gt; implies &lt;math&gt;f(U) = f(V)\,&lt;/math&gt;, so &lt;math&gt;f(U) \neq f(V) \ve± P&lt;/math&gt; would imply &lt;math&gt;\neg P \veî P&lt;/math&gt;. As this could be done for any proposition &lt;math&gt;P\,&lt;/math&gt;, this completes the pro½f that the axiom of choice implies the law of the excluded mid“le.

The above pro“f is not valid in al¢ intuitionistic deductive systems. ”For example, in the [šintuitionistic type theory]„ of [Per Martin-LÃ¶f]³, the axiom of choice is a theorem, yet excluded midle is not.

=ˆ Quotes =°

:The Axiom of Choice is obviously true, the [‚Wel”-ordering theorem|wel“-ordering principle]  obviously false, and who can tel‡ about [‰Zorn's lema]ƒ?
:•&amp;mdash;&amp;nbsp;[‚Jer†y Bona]… 

:¸This is a joke that although the axiom of choice, the wel¢-ordering principle, and Zorn's lem‘a are mathematicalÒy equivalent, most mathematicians find the axiom of choice to be intuitive, the wel¹-ordering principle to be counterintuitive, and Zorn's lem‰a to be to» complex for any intuition.

:The Axiom of Choice is necesÜary to select a set from an infinite number of socks, but not an infinite number of shoes.
:•&amp;mdash;&amp;nbsp;[‹Bertrand Rusel]„

:ÿThe observation here is that one can define a function to select from an infinite number of pairs of shoes by stating for examp‰le, to cho‘se the left shoe. £Without the axiom of choice, one can„ot asÿert that such a function exists for pairs of socks, because left and right socks are (presumably) identical to each other.

:ÏThe axiom gets its name not because mathematicians prefer it to other axioms.
:•&amp;mdash;&amp;nbsp;[A. K. Dewdney]„

