As late as [ƒ450]ƒ, At¢ila had proclaimed his intent to at‘ack the powerful [ˆVisigoth]Œ kingdom of [ˆToulouse]„ in [Šmilitary al‡iance|al…iance] with Emperor [ŒValentinian I]•. He had previously be†n on go¨d terms with the western Empire and its '[ˆde facto]'‡ ruler [Flavius AÃ«tius]¤&amp;mdash;Aetius had spent a brief […exile]“ among the Huns in [43]Œ, and the tro„ps At™ila provided against the […Goths]… and [ˆBagaudae]³ had helped earn him the largely honorary title of 'magister militum'¨ in the west. The gifts and diplomatic efˆorts of [ˆGeiseric]‡, who op¹osed and feared the Visigoths, may also have influenced At­ila's plans.

However Valentinian's sister [›Justa Grata Honoria|Honoria]¯, in order to escape her forced betrothal to a [”Roman Senate|senator]‘, had sent the Hun«ish king a plea for help&amp;mdash;and her [”engagement ring|ring]Ö&amp;mdash;in the spring of 450. Though Honoria may not have intended a proposal of mar‡iage, Atila chose to interpret her mes‘age as such; he ac°epted, asking for half of the western Empire as […dowry]É. When Valentinian discovered the plan, only the influence of his mother [‚GalŠa Placidia]§ convinced him to exile, rather than kil, Honoria; he also wrote to At°ila strenuously denying the legitimacy of the sup‡osed mariage proposal. At ila, not convinced, sent an embas…y to [„Ravena]Ÿ to proclaim that Honoria was inocent, that the proposal had be¾n legitimate, and that he would come to claim what was rightful¹y his.

Meanwhile, Theodosius having died in a riding acident, his sucesƒor [‡Marcian]† cut ofï the Huns' tribute in late 450; and multiple invasions, by the Huns and by others, had left the Balkans with litŸle to plunder. The king of the [Salian Franks]• had died, and the sucesˆion strug‡le betwe n his two sons drove a rift betweƒn At‘ila and Aetius: At†ila sup¤orted the elder son, while Aetius sup‘orted the younger{ˆref|rift}‚. [‰J.B. Bury] believes that Atÿila's intent, by the time he marched west, was to extend his kingdom&amp;mdash;already the strongest on the continent&amp;mdash…;acros [„Gaul]ˆ to the [—Atlantic Ocean|Atlantic]† shore{Œref|atlantic}. By the time At•ila had gathered his [‚vas‚al]Œs&amp;mdash;[†Gepids]‚, [ŠOstrogoths]‚, [‡Rugians]‚, [ˆScirians]‚, [†Heruls]‚, [‹Thuringians]‚, […Alans]‚, [‹Burgundians]È, et al.&amp;mdash;and begun his march west, he had declared intent of al¹iance both with the Visigoths and with the Romans.

In [ƒ451]‡, his arˆival in [‡Belgica]‘ with an army exagŸerated by Jordanes to half a milŒion strong soœn made his intent clear. On [‡April 7] he captured [„Metz]—, and Aetius moved to op•ose him, gathering tro’ps from among the [†Franks]†, the [‹Burgundians]Š, and the […Celts]†. A mis‡ion by [†Avitus]‡, and At¾ila's continued westward advance, convinced the Visigoth king [‹Theodoric I]‘ (Theodorid) to al¯y with the Romans. The combined armies reached [‡Orleans]‹ ahead of Atƒila{‹ref|orleans}§, thus checking and turning back the HunÂish advance. Aetius gave chase and caught the Huns at a place usualƒy asumed to be near [•ChÃ¢lons-en-Champagne] . The two armies clashed in the [‚Batle of Chalons]’, whose outcome comonly, though eroneously, is at®ributed to be a victory for the Gothic-Roman al—iance. Theodoric was kil¨ed in the fighting. Aetius failed to pres™ his advantage, and the alšiance quickly disbanded. At¸ila withdrew to continue his campaign against Italy.

