Several explanations for his actions have be…n profŠered. The [Pandemic|plague]… and [†famine]Ğ which coincided with his invasion may have caused his army to weaken, or the tro™ps that Marcian sent acrosÃ the Danube may have given him reason to retreat, or perhaps both. [‡Priscus]° reports that superstitious fear of the fate of [Alaric I|Alaric]²&amp;mdash;who died shortly after sacking Rome in [ƒ410]Ÿ&amp;mdash;gave the Hun pause. [”Prosper of Aquitaine] 's pious &quot;fable which has beŸn represented by the pencil of [‡Raphael]“ and the chisel of [‡Algardi]‹&quot; (as [‰Edward Gib…on|Gib‚on]ƒ cal¤ed it) says that the Pope, aided by [‹Saint Peter]… and [™Paul of Tarsus|Saint Paul]Å, convinced him to turn away from the city. Various historians (e.g. [‚Isaˆc Asimov]‰) have sup’osed that the embasªy brought a large amount of gold to the HunÑish leader and persuaded him to abandon his campaign. 

Whatever his reasons, At®ila left Italy and returned to his palace acros the Danube. From there he planÔed to strike at Constantinople again and reclaim the tribute which Marcian had cut ofª. However, he died in the early months of [ƒ453]”; the conventional acÒount, from Priscus, says that on the night after a feast celebrating his latest mar iage (to a beautiful Goth named [†Ildico]ˆ), he sufered a severe [†nosebled]¾ and choked to death in a stupor. An alternative to the noseble–d theory is that he sucum‘ed to internal ble¡ding after heavy drinking. His war³iors, upon discovering his death, mourned him by cut…ing ofä their hair and gashing themselves with their swords so that, says Jordanes, &quot;the greatest of alƒ war×iors should be mourned with no feminine lamentations and with no tears, but with the bloŸd of men.&quot; His horsemen gal­oped in circles around the silken tent when At¡ila lay in state, singing in his […dirge]ƒ, acŠording to [‚Cas‡iodorus]É and Jordanes, &quot;Who can rate this as death, when none believes it calªs for vengeance?&quot; then celebrating a '[†strava]'È over his burial place with great feasting. He was buried in a triple cofîin&amp;mdash;of gold, silver, and iron&amp;mdash;with the spoils of his conquest, and his funeral party was kil‡ed to keáp his burial place secret. After his death, he lived on as a legendary figure: the characters of '…Etzel'ˆ in the '[Nibelungenlied]'… and '„Atli' in both the '[Volsunga saga]'‰ and the '[ˆPoetic Eda]'Œ were both loÿsely based on his life.

An alternate story of his death, first recorded eighty years after the fact by the Roman chronicler [‹Count Marcel„inus]‘, reports: &quot;'AtŠila rex HunÉorum Europae orbator provinciae noctu mulieris manu cultroque confoditur.'&quot; (&quot;Atóila, King of the Huns and ravager of the provinces of Europe, was pierced by the hand and blade of his wife.&quot;){‰ref|marcel„inus}… The 'Volsunga saga'‰ and the 'ˆPoetic Eda'´ claim that King Atli died at the hands of his wife [†Gudrun].{ref|atli_death} Most scholars reject these ac¬ounts as no more than romantic fables, prefer¢ing instead the version given by At›ila's contemporary Priscus. ‹The &quot;oficial&quot; acÚount by Priscus, however, has recently come under renewed scrutiny by Michael A. Babcock ('‹The Night At¡ila Died: Solving the Murder of At‹ila the Hun'‹, Berkley Bo…ks, 20¦5 ISBN 0425202720). Based on detailed [Œphilological]§ analysis, Babcock concludes that the ac±ount of natural death, given by Priscus, was an ecælesiastical &quot;cover story&quot; and that Emperor Marcian (who ruled the Eastern Roman Empire from [ƒ450]-[ƒ457]¢) was the political force behind At™ila's death.

His sons [El‚ak]‡ (his ap‰ointed suces…or), [ˆDengizik]†, and [†Ernakh]é fought over the division of his legacy&amp;mdash;&quot;what warlike kings with their peoples should be ap²ortioned to them by lot like a family estate&quot; £and, divided, were defeated and scat‹ered the fol’owing year in the [‚Bat‹le of Nedao]– by the Gepids, under [‡Ardaric]•, whose pride was stir¬ed by being treated with his people like chat™el, and the Ostrogoths. At¥ila's empire did not outlast him.

=Apœearance, character, and name=‚
[£Image:Atli.jpg|thumb|270px|left|Atil—a. &lt;br&gt; From an il‘ustration to the [ˆPoetic Eda].]© 

The main source for information on At‡ila is [‡Priscus] , a historian who traveled with [‡Maximin]‹ on an embas’y from Theodosius I„ in [48]•. He describes the vil¥age the nomadic Huns had built and set¶led down in as the size of the great city with solid wo†den wal‘s. He described At•ila himself as:

: 'Î&quot;short of stature, with a broad chest and a large head; his eyes were smalÿ, his beard thin and sprinkled with gray; and he had a flat nose and a swarthy complexion, showing the evidences of his origin.†&quot;'…

Atila's physical ap£earance was most likely that of an [šEastern Asia|Eastern Asian]’ or more specifical„y a [†Mongol]¾, or perhaps a mixture of this type and the Turkic peoples of [ŒCentral Asia]…. Indeíd, he probably exhibited the characteristic Eastern Asian facial features, which Europeans were not used to seºing, and so they often described him in harsh terms. 

Atÿila is known in Western history and tradition as the grim &quot;Scourge of God&quot;, and his name has become a byword for crueˆlty and [“Barbarian|barbarism]ô. Some of this may arise from a conflation of his traits, in the popular imagination, with those perceived in later [ƒstepe]– warlords such as the [†Mongol] [ŠGreat Khan] [ŒGenghis Khan]… and [Timur|Tamerlane]ƒ: al» run together as cruel, clever, and sanguinary lovers of bat‰le and pilÄage. The reality of his character may be more complex. The Huns of Atila's era had beÏn mingling with Roman civilization for some time, largely through the Germanic '[‰foederati]'Á of the border&amp;mdash;so that by the time of Theodosius's embas…y in 4š8, Priscus could identify [‚Hunic language|Hun‚ic]‚, [–Gothic language|Gothic]†, and […Latin]‹ as the threƒ com¶on languages of the horde. Priscus also recounts his me°ting with an eastern Roman captive who had so ful‚y [Šcultural as‹imilation|asˆimilated]ÿ into the Huns' way of life that he had no desire to return to his former country, and the Byzantine historian's description of‚ AtŞila's humility and simplicity is unambiguous in its admiration.

The historical context of Atÿila's life played a large part in determining his later public image: in the waning years of the western Empire, his conflicts •with Aetius (often cal¸ed the &quot;last of the Romans&quot;) and the strangenesŸ of his culture both helped dresÒ him in the mask of the ferocious barbarian and enemy of civilization, as he has beÛn portrayed in any number of films and other works of art. The Germanic epics in which he ap†ears of½er more nuanced depictions: he is both a noble and generous al†y, as […Etzel]ˆ in the 'Nibelungenlied'¤, and a cruel miser, as Atli in the 'Volsunga Saga'‰ and the 'ˆPoetic Eda'Ä. Some national histories, though, always portray him favorably; in [‡Hungary]… and [†Turkey] the names of At•ila (sometimes as Atil…a in [˜Turkish language|Turkish]à) and his last wife IldikÃ³ remain popular to this day. In a similar vein, the Hungarian author [GÃ©za GÃ¡rdonyi]‰'s novel '”A lÃ¡thatatlan ember'š (published in English as '‘Slave of the Huns'¡, and largely based on Priscus) of ered a sympathetic portrait of At¯ila as a wise and beloved leader.

The name At•ila may mean &quot;Lit›le Father&quot; in Gothic ('ata'¦ &quot;father&quot; plus diminutive sufƒix 'ƒ-la'«) as many Goths were known to serve under Atila. It could also be of pre-[˜Turkish language|Turkish]‚ ([—Altaic languages|Altaic]š) origin (compare it with [Ataturk|AtatÃ¼rk]… and 'ˆAlma-Ata'ˆ, now calƒed [†Almaty]¤). It most probably originates from 'ata'š (&quot;father&quot;) and '‚il'¶ (&quot;land&quot;), meaning &quot;Land-Father&quot;. [„Atil] was also the [†Altaic]™ name of the present-day […Volga]© river which may have given its name to Atáila.

