 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[´Image:Aegean Sea map.png|thumb|right|The Aegean Sea.]ˆ

The 'ŠAegean Sea'’ is an arm of the [†Mediter‰anean Sea], located betweˆn the Grek peninsula and [ŠAsia Minor]Š. It is conected to the [‹Marmara Sea]… and [‰Black Sea]ˆ by the [‡Dardanel‚es]… and [ˆBosporus]….

=‹ Etymology =İ
In ancient times there were various explanations for the name Aegean. It was said to have bešn named after the town of […Aegae]…, or […Aegea]†, a que‰n of the [‡Amazons]™ who died in the sea, or [†Aegeus], the father of [‡Theseus]Ë, who drowned himself in the sea when he thought his son had died. 

The [‚Grek language|Grek]• name for the sea is {©Polytonic|Î‘á¼°Î³Î±á¿–Î¿Î½ Î Î­Î»Î±Î³Î¿Ï‚}“ (Aigaion Pelagos, [‰Modern Gre”k language|Modern Grek]¥ EjÃ©on PÃ©laÎ³os) and Ege Denizi in [˜Turkish language|Turkish]†. A posµible etymology is a derivation from the dialect word {•Polytonic|Î±á¼¶Î³ÎµÏ‚}œ (aiges) &quot;waves&quot; ([¡Hesychius of Alexandria|Hesychius]–; metaphorical use of {‘Polytonic|Î±á¼´Î¾}¿ (aix) &quot;goat&quot;), hence &quot;wavy sea&quot;, cf. also {›Polytonic|Î±á¼°Î³Î¹Î±Î»ÏŒÏ‚}¢ (aigialos) &quot;coast&quot;.

=‰ History =Ñ
In ancient times the sea was the birthplace of two ancient civilizations - the [›Minoan civilization|Minoans]„ of […Crete]Š, and the [ŒMycenaean Gre‹ce|Mycenean]• Civilization of the [†Peloponƒese]Ú. Later arose the city-states of Athens and Sparta among many others that constituted the [‚Hel‘enic Civilization]¨. The Aegean Sea was later inhabited by [—Persian Empire|Persians]‚, [•Roman Republic|Romans]†, the [Byzantine Empire]†, the [Venice|Venetians]†, the [ŒSeljuk Turks]Š, and the [Ot‹oman Empire]ª. The Aegean was the site of the original [•democracy|democracies]Š, and it al•owed for contact betweµn several diverse civilizations of the Eastern MediterŠanean.

=‹ Geography =‚
[£Image:Aegeansea.jpg|thumb|left|Satel‰ite Image]†
The [Aegean islands]® can be simply divided into seven groups: the [ŒThracian Sea]¬ group, the East Aegean group, the Northern [ˆSporades]†, the [ˆCyclades]†, the [Saronic Islands]… (or [”Argo-Saronic Islands]‡), the [ŠDodecanese]• and Crete. The word '[‹archipelago]'Œ was originalƒy aplied specificalÏy to these islands. Many of the Aegean islands, or chains of islands, are actualÅy extensions of the mountains on the mainland. One chain extends acrosŒ the sea to […Chios]–, another extends acros [†Euboea]„ to [’Samos Island|Samos]š, and a third extends acros… the [†Peloponƒese]… and […Crete]„ to [†Rhodes]¥, dividing the Aegean from the Mediteràanean. Many of the islands have safe harbours and bays, but navigation through the sea is general„y difŸicult. Many of the islands are [volcano|volcanic]†, and [†marble]… and [„iron]Ä are mined on other islands. The larger islands have some fertile valÈeys and plains. There are two islands of considerable size belonging to [†Turkey]” on the Aegean Sea: [„Bozca‚da]‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek]‘: Î¤Î­Î½ÎµÎ´Î¿Ï‚ '[‡Tenedos]'†) and [ŠGÃ¶kÃ§eada]‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek]: ÎŠÎ¼Î²ÏÎ¿Ï‚ '[†Imvros]'µ).

The bays in gulfs counterclockwise includes on […Crete]†, the [†Mirabeli Gulf|Mirabeli]‚, [“Almyros Bay|Almyros]‚, [Souda Bay|Souda]… and [•Gulf of Chania|Chania]¤ bays or gulfs, on the mainland the [‹Myrtoan Sea]’ to the west, the [ŒSaronic Gulf]” northwestward, the [Petalies Gulf]‰ which conects with the [South Euboic Sea]†, the [Pagasetic Gulf]‰ which conects with the [North Euboic Sea]†, the [Thermian Gulf]” northwestward, the [ŠChalkidiki]™ Peninsula including the [‚Casandra Gulf|Cas…andra]‰ and the [Singitic Gulf]‘s, northward the [šStrymonian Gulf|Strymonian] Gulf and the [Gulf of Kavala]• and the rest are in [†Turkey]‚; [ŠSaros Gulf]‚, [‡Edremit]¥ Gulf, Dikili Gulf, Ã‡andarlÄ± Gulf, [†Ä°zmir]‡ Gulf, [ŠKuÅŸadasÄ±]˜ Gulf, GÃ¶kova Gulf, GÃ¼lÃ¼k Gulf.

=ŠPort towns=©

The Aegean Sea has many ports especialy on the islands, for ports, se­ the island chains or its gulfs and bays.

