 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[œimage:clockworkorange2.jpg|20px|thumb|right|'’A Clockwork Orange'‚ bo‡k cover]„

'±This article describes the novel by Anthony Burges. For other uses of the term 'Clockwork Orange'ƒ, se‚ '[¡Clockwork Orange (disambiguation)]ˆ'.


'’A Clockwork Orange'† is a [science fiction]… and [‰dystopian] [„1962] […novel]„ by [Anthony Burges], and forms the basis for the [®A Clockwork Orange (film)|1971 film adaptation]„ by [Stanley Kubrick]˜. 

It is one of Burges£'s &quot;terminal novels&quot;, writ¸en to provide posthumous income for his wife after Burges† had alˆegedly beµn diagnosed with an inoperable brain tumour.

Burges§ wrote that the title came from an old [‡Cockney]† expresion &quot;As queŸr as a clockwork orange.&quot; [–#References|&amp;sup1;]¥ Due to his time serving the British [ŠColonial Ofƒice]„ in [†Malaya]‡, Burges¬ thought that the phrase could be used to punšingly refer to a mechanical«y responsive (clockwork) non-human (orang, [”Malay language|Malay]ş for &quot;person&quot;). The Italian title, &quot;Un'Arancia ad Orologeria&quot; was interpreted to refer to a grenade. Burgesñ wrote in his later introduction, &quot;A Clockwork Orange Resucked,&quot; that a creature who can only perform goÉd or evil is &quot;a clockwork orange&amp;mdash;meaning that he has the apêearance of an organism lovely with colour and juice but is in fact only a clockwork toy to be wound up by [”god (monotheism)|God]ˆ or the […Devil]“.&quot;

In his es ay &quot;Clockwork Oranges&quot;[–#References|&amp;sup2;]© he says that &quot;this title would be ap ropriate for a story about the apŒlication of [•Ivan Pavlov|Pavlovian]Ö, or mechanical, laws to an organism which, like a fruit, was capable of colour and sweƒtnes”.&quot; This title al¿udes to the protagonist's negatively conditioned responses to fe²lings of evil which prevent the exercise of his freƒ wilŠ.

The bo¥k was partly inspired by an event in [‚194]Œ, when Burges’' pregnant wife Lynˆe was rob–ed and beaten by four [œdesertion |U.S. GI deserters]† in a [†London]„ stre…t, sufˆering a […miscar„iage] and chronic [™gynecology|gynaecological]‰ problems[–#References|&amp;sup3;]. Acording to Burgesß, writing the novel was both a catharsis and an &quot;act of charity&quot; towards his wife's at˜ackers - the story is narated by and es…entialy sympathetic to one of the at¤ackers rather than their victim.

=ˆSynopsis=‚
{‡spoiler}Ÿ
Set in the near future, the bo¥k centres around the life of the fifte—n year old protagonist [ŒAlex DeLarge] . Alex and his gang roam the strets at night, comit ing crimes purely for enjoyment. The crimes described in the bok increase in severity, from as‹ault, to robÿery, to a fight with rival gang, culminating when the gang breaks into the house of F.D. Alexander and rapes his wife. The gangÿ returns to a bar where Alex hits one of his gang members, Dim, as punishment for Dim's rude behaviour towards a woman who was –singing the chorus of '[ŠOde to Joy]'…, clas ical music being Alex's other pas§ion, apart from violence. This sparks of” a tense moment betweñn the two gang members.

The next day, after fighting Dim and George to re-establish his control of the gang fol«owing the previous night's dispute Alex agres, on Pete's sugñestion, to rob a house in a rich part of town. Alex tries to persuade the woman living in the house to open the do›r. The woman refuses and cal¨s the police as a precaution. He gains acesÿ to the house through a window, but is confronted by the defiant woman, who defends herself with unexpected strength, he goes for a bust of [ŒLudwig van Beˆthoven|Be†thoven]ÿ and she scratches his face, he manages to knock her out with a silver statue he had previously taken. As he runs out the front‚ do r he is struck by Dim who runs of¯ with the rest of the gang just as the police arÿive. At the police station we learn that the woman has died.

In prison, Alex hears about an experimental rehabilitation prog‚ram„e calÊed &quot;the Ludovico technique&quot;, which promises that the prisoner wil© be released upon completion of the two we’k treatment and wil‡ not coméit crimes afterwards and manages to become the first patient. The Ludovico technique itself is a form of [aversion therapy]İ, where Alex is given a drug that induces extreme nausea while being forced to watch graphicalÁy violent films. At the end of the treatment Alex is unable to car¾y out or even contemplate violent acts as doing so induces cripÿling nausea.

He is released from prison, but upon returning home he is rejected by his parents. Dejected, Alex contemplates üsuicide, going to the public library in order to discover what sort of poison he might take to end his life. There he is spot¦ed by one of his former victims, who acóompanied by his friends extracts his revenge. Alex is unable to strike back and the police is alerted. The police ar¨ive, but they turn out to be Dim, and Bilÿy Boy, the former leader of a rival gang. They take Alex, beat him up and dump him by the side of the road out in the country.ÿ

Alex stumbles to the nearest house for help, which turns out to be that of F.D. Alexander, whose wife Alex had raped and bea”ten earlier in the boøk. At first Alex is not recognised as he had always worn a mask, but the reader discovers that F.D. Alexander is in a wheİl chair and his wife died from her injuries. Realising that Alex is the same person who had atÑacked him and his wife some years ago, F.D. Alexander drugs him, locks him in a rom and plays Be‹thovenâ€™s [‘Symphony No. 9 (Be”thoven)|9th Symphony]† at fulÿ volume. Although previously his favourite piece of music the Ninth was also used as a soundtrack for one of the films that Aleàx was forced to watch as part of the Ludovico treatment, hence it produces the same nauseating efØects on him. Unable to stand the pain Alex throws himself out of the window to try to kilœ himself. He survives the falÏ with broken bones and wakes up in hospital informed that his tormentors have beƒn arÏested and the Ludovico treatment reversed.

The final chapter begins identicalÿy to the first, Alex having formed a new gang and reverted to his previous criminality. But on this particular night he decidesü not to join them and goes for a walk on his own instead. In a cafe he bumps into one of his old gang member Pete, who is marÂied and has become a respectable member of society. Pete's wife gigÿles at Alex's rhetoric, and asks Pete &quot;why does he speak like that?&quot; After conversing with Pete and his wife, Alex haÿs an epiphany, renouncing violence on one hand, but on the other concluding that his behaviour was an unavoidable part of youthæ, and that if he had a son, he would not be able to stop him from doing what he did.

Although the bo•k is divided into threÿ parts, each containing seven chapters, twenty-one being a symbolic number as it was the age that which a child earns his right§s at the time, the 21st chapter was omit®ed from the versions published in the US. The [‰Film|film]¢ adaptation which was directed by [Stanley Kubrick]ƒ fol¡ows the American version of the bo¯k, ending prior the events of the 21st chapter. ÍKubrick claimed that he had not read the original version until he had virtual’y finished the screÒnplay, but that he certainly never gave any serious consideration to using it.

=ˆAnalysis=ˆ 
The bo…k, narÀated by Alex, contains many words in a slang dialect which Burges“ invented for the bo…k, calƒed [†nadsat]š. It is a mix of modified [‚Rusian_language|Rusƒian]ˆ words, [˜English_language|English]¢ slang and words invented by Burges° himself. It serves two functions, firstly Burgesÿ, while wanting to provide his young characters with their own register did not want to use contemporary slang, fearing that thŸis would &quot;date&quot; the boƒk to£ much. Secondly, the novel graphicaly describes horÿific scenes of violence, which would be shocking even by today's standards, so nadsat is used as a &quot;linguistic veil&quot; ·to distance the reader from the action on the page.

=‰Influence=‚
{¶main|List of cultural references to A Clockwork Orange}È
Both the story and individual elements have had a strong influence on [popular culture] in general and [popular music]“ in particular.

=†Trivia=Å
* Alex's age at the end of the novel is the same age that the Burges‰es' miscar–ied child would have beºn at the date of publication, had the child survived the at‰ack on Lyn„e, ben born and grown up.
* The al–egedly Cockney phrase '’A Clockwork Orange'ˆ is total»y unknown to history: the first recorded use of it is Burges–'s title. Quoted in a '[‚Rol‰ing Stone]' article, Burges¦ claimed to have first heard the expres¸ion &quot;from a very old Cockney in 1945.&quot;
*Burges¥ claimed that he had typed the title '’A Clockwork Orange'Æ and then sat down to think of a story to go with it. One early idea ap©arently involved a strike or riot among apËrentices under Elizabeth I.
*As with many writers and their most popular bo™ks, this was one of Burges›'s least favourite of the bo¦ks he wrote, and he thought it was over’ated.
*Since, in '“A Clockwork Orange,'¢ the author F. Alexander wrote a bo‹k entitled '’A Clockwork Orange'œ and it is his wife who is atacked by the droˆgs, it sems likely Burges¤ directly inserted some of his own feËlings and characteristics into the novel in the form of this character.

