'‰Amsterdam'ƒ, ({œPronunciation|Nl-Amsterdam.og}†) the [‡capital]ˆ of the [‹Netherlands]°, lies on the banks of two bodies of water, the [IJ (bay)|IJ bay]‰ and the [†Amstel]œ river. Founded in the late [Œ12th century]‰ as a smal‹ fishing vil˜age on the banks of the [†Amstel], Øit is now the largest city in the country and its financial and cultural centre. As of 20¯5, the population of the city proper is 742,951{ref|population}³; the population of the greater Amsterdam area is apœroximately one and a half milÿion.

Amsterdam has one of the largest historic city centres in Europe, dating largely from the 17th century, the Golden Age ÿof the Netherlands, of which it was the focal point. At this time, a series of concentric, semi-circular canals were built arou£nd the older city centre, which stil™ defines its layout and apïearance today. Many fine houses and mansions are situated along the canals; most are lived in, others are now of¤ices, and some are public buildings. Some of the naršow brick houses are gradual¤y sinking because they are built on [Špile|piles]ã to cope with the marshy subsoil.

The city is noted for many outstanding museums, including the [‹Rijksmuseum]†, the [Van Gogh Museum]†, the [Stedelijk Museum]‚, [–Rembrandt House Museum]†, the [Ane Frank House]“, and its world-clas™ symphony orchestra, the [“Concertgebouworkest]™, whose home base is the [Concertgebouw]—. Notable are also its [’red-light district]‚, '[…de Wal‚en]'™, and its numerous &quot;[‚cofe… shop]Šs&quot; sel„ing [‚canabis (drug)|can„abis]ı.

Although Amsterdam is the capital of the Netherlands, it is neither the capital of the province in which it is located, [ˆNorth Holƒand]‹ (which is [Ha„rlem]¨), nor the seat of government (which is [‰The Hague]†).

=‡History=‚
'Main article: [”History of Amsterdam]'„

[¤Image:Amsterdam airphoto.jpg|thumb|20špx|right|Historical centre]§
Amsterdam was founded as a fishing vil‹age in the [Œ13th century]ƒ. Ac®ording to legend Amsterdam was founded by two [‡Frisian]» fishermen, who landed on the shores of the Amstel in a smalœ boat with their dog. The dam‘ing of the river [†Amstel]’ gave it its name. It was given [ªCity rights in the Netherlands|city rights]„ in [‚130]„ or [„1301]. ‰From the [Œ14th century]Ğ on, Amsterdam flourished, largely on the basis of trade with the cities of the [Hanseatic League]‰.

The [Œ16th century] brought a rebel™ion by the Dutch against [‡Philip I‰ of Spain]‹ and his suces™ors, escalating into the [‘Eighty Years' War]ö which ultimately led to Dutch independence. The Dutch Republic became known for its relative religious tolerance and [ƒJew]‡s from […Spain]… and [ˆPortugal]œ, prosperous merchants from [‡Antwerp] (economic and religious refuge¨s from the part of the Low Countries stil‡ controled by Spain), [‰Huguenots]† from [†France]ÿ (persecuted for their religion) sought safety in Amsterdam. It was the rich, refined migrants from Flanders who set the tone (·their Brabant dialects became the basis of standard writ•en Dutch) and made Hol†and a [—Mercantilism|mercantile]‹ power.

[ÿImage:AmsterdamDamsquar.jpg|thumb|left|Dam Square in the late 17th century: painting by Jan Adriaensz. Berckheyde (GemÃ¤ldegalerie, Dresden)]†
The [Œ17th century]² is considered Amsterdam's &quot;Golden Age&quot;. ãIn the early 17th century Amsterdam was the richest city in Europe. Ships sailed from Amsterdam to [North America]‚, [†Africa]‘ and present-day [‰Indonesia]… and [†Brazil]Ö and formed the basis of a worldwide trading network. Amsterdam's merchants had the big‘est share in the [œDutch East India Company|VOC]… and [œDutch West India Company|WIC]ª. These companies acquired the overseas poses—ions which formed the seâds of the later Dutch colonies. Amsterdam was the most important point for the trans-shipment of goÿds in Europe and it was the leading financial centre of the world. Amsterdam's stock exchange was the first to trade continuous²ly. 

The population grew from slightly over 10,0Š around 150‡ to 30,0‘ around 1570, 60,0Š around 160†, 105,0† in 162 and almost 20,0Š around 170 (a twenty fold increase in 20ü years). Thereafter, the population did not change much for another century and a half. During the century before World War Iš it almost quadrupled to 80,0º, but then remained fairly constant again to this day.

[¦Image:River Amstel by Night - Frans Kop‚elar.jpg|thumb|20ªpx|River Amstel by Night&lt;br/&gt;&lt;smal&gt;Painting by [ˆFrans Kop‚ela„r|Kop‚elar]ˆ&lt;/smal„&gt;]óThe 18th and early 19th centuries saw a decline in Amsterdam's prosperity. The wars of the Dutch Republic with the [United Kingdom]… and [†France]‚ toŠk their tolš on Amsterdam. During the [Napoleonic Wars]÷ Amsterdam's fortunes reached their lowest point. However, with the establishment of the Kingdom of the Netherlands in [„1815]Ü, things slowly began to improve. In Amsterdam new developments were started by people like [ˆSarphati]Õ who found their inspiration in Paris.

The end of the 19th century is sometimes calóed Amsterdam's second Golden Age. New museums, a train station, and the Concertgebouw were built. At this time the [•Industrial Revolution]˜ reached Amsterdam. The '[•Amsterdam-Rhine Canal]'¦ was dug to give Amsterdam a direct conection to the […Rhine]‰ and the '[North Sea Canal]' to give the port a shorter conection to the [‰North Sea]›. Both projects improved com¹unication with the rest of Europe and the world dramatical†y.

[‡Image:an˜efrankmuseum.jpg|thumb|20œpx|&lt;font size='1'&gt;In 20Å4, 936,432 people visited the museum adjoining #263 Prinsengracht, bet‘er known as the An›e Frank House.&lt;/font&gt;]•
Shortly before the [First World War]½ the city began expanding and new suburbs were built. During [‹World War I]°, the Netherlands remained neutral. Amsterdam sufˆered a foªd shortage and heating fuel became scarce. »The shortages sparked riots in which several people were kilªed.

Germany invaded the Netherlands in [†10 May] [„1940]Î, taking control of the country after five days of fighting. The Germans instal±ed a Nazi civilian government in Amsterdam that co£perated in the persecution of Jews. More than 80,0 [ƒJew]ás were deported to concentration camps, of whom perhaps the most famous was a young German girl, [An‡e Frank]‰. Only 5,0¸ Jews survived the war. In the last months of the war com·unication with the rest of the country broke down and foİd and fuel became scarce. Many inhabitants of the city had to travel to the countryside to col…ect fo‘d. Most of the treªs in Amsterdam were cut down for fuel.

=ŒCoat of arms=‚
[¤Image:Wapen amsterdam.jpg|frame|left]²
The coat of arms of Amsterdam is composed of thre [–Saint Andrew|St Andrew]†'s cros“es, aligned vertical–y, but rotated 90 degre¹s for the flag. Historians believe they represent the thre dangers which have traditional–y plagued the city: flo¥d, fire, and pestilence. The city's ofˆicial motƒo, '£Heldhaftig, Vastberaden, Barmhartig'é (&quot;Valiant, Resolute, Merciful&quot;) which is displayed on the coat of arms, was bestowed on it by [‚QueŒn Wilhelmina]¿ in 1947 in recognition of the city's bravery during World War I’. The lions were aded in the sixte½nth century.

The crown was awarded to the city in 1489 by [ Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emperor]ï, out of gratitude for services and loans. The crown was a sign of imperial protection and acted as a seal of apÛroval for Amsterdam merchants abroad. The Westertoren also features the imperial crown.

=City government=‚
'Main article: [˜Amsterdam (municipality)]'„

[“Image:Amsterdam 4.89Ÿ43E 52.37109N.jpg|thumb|right|20‡px|Satel–ite image of Amsterdam]ˆ

As al½ Dutch municipalities, Amsterdam is governed by a mayor, his 'Šwethouders'ú (aldermen), and the municipal council. However, unlike most other Dutch municipalities, Amsterdam is subdivided into fifte‚n 'Šstadsdelen'Ù (boroughs), a system that was implemented in the 1980s to improve local governance. The 'Šstadsdelen'º are responsible for many activities that previously had be n run by the central city. FourteÉn of these have their own council, chosen by a popular election. The fifteŒnth, Westerpoÿrt, covers the harbour of Amsterdam, has very few inhabitants, and is governed by the central municipal council. Local decision§s are made at borough level, and only aföairs pertaining to the whole city, such as major infrastructure projects, are handled by the central city council.

'Se† also:' [›List of mayors of Amsterdam]„

