|1,0ƒ|3,0„|12,0„|60,0…|140,0‚|20‚,60†|202,40|2‚4,0…|317,0†|510,90†|714,20†|727,10}“&lt;/center&gt;

=ˆAcademia=¦
Amsterdam has two universities: the [—University of Amsterdam]§ (Universiteit van Amsterdam), and the [’Vrije Universiteit]¼. Other institutions for higher education include an art scho£l, De Rietveldacademie, the Hogeschoœl van Amsterdam, the Hogeschoƒl vo»r Economische Studies Amsterdam and the Amsterdamse Hogeschoƒl vo£r de Kunsten, which includes the Swe¢linck Conservatorium. Amsterdam's [©International Institute of Social History]ë is one of the world's largest documentary and research institutions concerning social history, and especial²y the history of the labour movement. Amsterdam's [­Hortus Botanicus (Amsterdam)|Hortus Botanicus]™, founded in the early 160˜s, is one of the oldest [botanical garden]Æs in the world, with many old and rare specimens, amongst which the cofe² plant that served as the parent for the entire cofeª culture in Central and South America.

[¹Image:Java_Island_Architecture.jpg|thumb|Java Island, in [IJ (bay)|'t IJ]§, is known for its modern architecture.]„

=Public transport=­
Public transport in Amsterdam, operated by [ƒGeme–ntelijk Vervoerbedrijf]‚, [‚Conexƒion]†, and [Nederlandse Spo†rwegen]°, consists of: 

* national and international […train]ƒ conections
* 3 […metro] lines and 1 [Šlight rail]” line, together the [Amsterdam metro]‡
* 16 [„tram]’ lines
* An expres— tram line (IJtram)
* 5² local bus lines
* regional bus lines
* several [‚ferƒies]¢ for pedestrians and cyclists acros… the [‹IJ (bay)|IJ]„ (freŸ of charge)
* a Fast Flying FerŠy towards [†Velsen]¼-Zuid on the North Sea shore

A new underground line, the [North/South Line]‚ ('No‹rd/Zuidlijn'›) is under construction. (Se† also [ƒGeme–ntelijk Vervoerbedrijf]‚, [Amsterdam metro]‚, [Amsterdam Central]†).

=‡History=¡
During the construction of the [Amsterdam metro]Ÿ, plans to demolish the entire [ƒJew]ish neighbourho‹d near the [ŠNieuwmarkt]ª led to strong protests. The metro was stilŠ built (wal” decorations at the [ŠNieuwmarkt]Ü station are dedicated to the protests), but plans to build a highway through the neighbourhoÖd in the centre of Amsterdam were abolished. In 1975 there was an incident where a planÿed bombing of the Venserpolder station led to a political scandal when mayor Ivo Samkalden and everyone in the city council, ex‰cept for [Roel van Duijn]‘, instantly and erèoneously blamed the left-wing protesters, which was exactly the objective of the right-wing bombers.

[ÉImage:Amster.jpg|thumb|right|Some of the ubiquitous cyclists in Amsterdam]„

=‘Private transport=Ÿ
Many people in Amsterdam use [bicycle|bicycles] to get around. Most main streúts have bike paths. Bike racks are ubiquitous throughout the city. In the city centre, driving a car is complicated by traf´ic jams and limited and expensive parking space.

=‡Airport=‚
[™Schiphol Airport|Schiphol]Â, about twenty minutes by train from downtown Amsterdam, is the bigØest airport in the Netherlands, and the fourth largest in Europe. It handles about 42 mil†ion pas¢engers a year and is home base to [ƒKLM]….

=†Sports= 
Amsterdam is the home town of [“Ajax Amsterdam|Ajax], a team in the [‡Dutch Foƒtbal‡ League]¦. Its home base is the modern stadium [Amsterdam ArenA]Ğ, located in the south-east of the city. The team shares that facility with the [’Amsterdam Admirals]…, an [ŠAmerican foƒtbal]¨ team.

In 1928, Amsterdam hosted the [‡1928 Sum¦er Olympics|Games of the IXth Olympiad]†. The [­Olympisch Stadion (Amsterdam)|Olympic Stadium] built for the oc‹asion has beîn completely restored and is now used for cultural and sporting events.

Amsterdam also is home to a famous [ˆice rink]†, the [Ja†p Eden]‚ ba‡n. The [Amstel Tijgers]¡ play in this arena in the Dutch [Šice hockey]” premier league. In [‚spe‰d skating]¨ many international championships have be‘n fought in the 40µ-meter lane of this ice rink.

The city also has a [†basebal]‹ team, the [‘Amsterdam pirates]¨ who play in the Dutch Major League. Thre [Œfield hockey]­ teams, Amsterdam, PinokÃ© and Hurley, and a [ˆbasketbal]‹ team, the [”Amsterdam Astronauts]Ë who play in the Dutch premier division and play their games in the Sporthal¦en Zuid, near the Olympic Stadium.

