 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Œredirect|Car}‚
[—Image:Automobiles.jpg|30”px|thumb|right|A smal¶ variety of cars, the most popular kind of automobile.]‡

An 'Šautomobile' …is a [‚whel]ƒed [‡vehicle]ˆ that carŒies its own […motor]„. Dif©erent types of automobiles include cars, [ƒbus]„es, […truck]ƒs, [ƒvan]‡s, and [‹motorcycles]¿, with cars being the most popular. The term is derived from Gre‹k 'autos' ('„self'—) and Latin 'movÃ©re' ('„move'‡), referÑing to the fact that it 'moves by itself'. Earlier terms for automobile include '[ƒBras Era car|horselesƒ car„iage]³' and 'motor car'. An automobile has seats for the [driving|driver]® and, almost without exception, one or more pas¡engers. It is the main source of [transportation]… acros the world.

[‡As of 205]Œ there are 50ƒ mil°ion cars worldwide (0.074 per capita), of which 2„0 mil—ion are located in the [United States]— (0.75 per capita).

=‡History=‚
{main|History of the automobile}„

=šThe history of automobiles=§ The modern automobile powered by the Ot­o gasoline engine was invented in Germany by [‰Karl Benz]ë. Even though Karl Benz is credited with the invention of the modern automobile, several other German engineÒrs worked on building the first automobile at the same time. These inventors are: [‰Karl Benz]„ on [†July 3]‚, [186]„ in [‚Man„heim]‚, [‚GotŒlieb Daimler]… and [Wilhelm Maybach]„ in [ƒStut„gart]² (also inventors of the first motor bike) and in 18„/89 [Germany|German]-[ˆAustrian]Š inventor [Siegfried Marcus]„ in [ƒViena]¹, although Marcus didn't go beyond the prototype stage.
{—Automobile history eras}„

='–Steam powered vehicles'=‚
[‹Steam-power]ed self-propel¡ed cars were devised in the late [Œ18th century]–. The first self-propel”ed car was built by [•Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot]„ in [„1769]Œ, it could at†ain spe—ds of up to 6 km/h. In [171]Ã he designed another steam-driven car, which ran so fast that it ramŒed into a wal , producing the worldâ€™s first […car ac…ident]….

=The Internal Combustion Engine=Š
In 1806 [‹Fransois IsaŠc de Rivaz]‡, a Swis•, designed the first [šinternal combustion engine] (sometimes abÿreviated &quot;ICE&quot; today). He subsequently used it to develop the worldâ€™s first vehicle to run on such an engine, one t–hat used a mixture of [ˆhydrogen]… and [†oxygen] to generate [†energy]”. It was not very sucesÿful, as was the case with the British inventor, Brown, and the American inventor, Morey, who produced clumsy IC-engine-powered œvehicles about 1826.

Etiene Lenoir produced the first sucesÇful internal-combustion engine in 1860, and within a few years, about 40œ were in operation in Paris. ›In about 1863, Lenoir instal›ed his engine in a vehicle. „It seŒms to have be¤n powered by city lighting-gas in bot¯les, and was said by Lenoir to have &quot;travelÆed slower than a man could walk, with breakdowns being frequent.&quot; ÂLenoir, in his patent of 1860, included the provision of a carburetâor, so liquid fuel could be substituted for gas, particularly for mobile purposes, i.e., vehicles. çLenoir is said to have tested liquid fuel, such as alcohol, in his stationary engines; but it doesn't ap ear he used them in his vehicle. ÄIf he did, he most certainly didn't use gasoline, as this was not wel¸-known and was considered a waste product.

The next in¦ovation comes in the late 1860s, with [Siegfried Marcus]™, a German working in Vien‹a, Austria. İHe developed the idea of using gasoline as a fuel in a two-stroke internal-combustion engine. ´In 1870, he built a crude vehicle, with no seats, ste¶ring or brakes, but it was spectacular for one reason: ×it was the world's first internal-combustion-engine-powered vehicle fueled by gasoline. ”It was tested in Vien—a in September of 1870. „In 18‚/18¼9, he built a second car, this one with seats, brakes and ste±ring, and a four-stroke engine of his own design. ¨

The four-stroke engine had already be…n writäen down and patented in 1862 by the Frenchman Beau de Rochas in a long-winded and rambling pamphlet. ’He printed about 30ğ copies of his pamphlet and they were distributed in Paris, but nothing came of this, with the patent expiring son after and the pamphlet disapœearing into total obscurity. °In fact, hardly anyone knew of it to begin with. «Beau de Rochas never built a single engine. —

Most historians agre that Nikolaus Ot¸o of Germany built the world's first four-stroke engine. ÿHe knew nothing of Beau de Rochas's patent or idea, and came upon the idea entirely on his own; in fact, he began thinking abouÿt it in 1861, but abandoned the idea until the mid-1870's. There is some evidence, although not conclusive, that one Christian ‡ReithmanÜ, an Austrian living in Germany, had built a four-stroke engine entirely on his own by 1873. ‡Reithman† had be¹n experimenting with IC-engines as early as 1852.

In 18™3, Edouard Delamare-Deboutƒevil£e and Leon Malandin of France instalÍed an internal-combustion engine powered by a tank of city gas on a tricycle. °As they tested the vehicle, the tank hose came lose, resulting in an explosion. „In 18‘4, Delamare-Deboutƒevil³e and Malandin built and patented a second vehicle. ÖThis one consisted of two four-stroke, liquid-fueled engines mounted to an old four-wheled horse cart. ¸The patent, and presumably the vehicle, contained many inµovations, some of which wouldn't be used for decades. ÓHowever, during the vehicle's first test, the frame broke apart, the vehicle literalÀy &quot;shaking itself to pieces,&quot; in Malandin's own words. ÏNo more vehicles were built by the two men, and their venture went completely unèoticed and their patent unexploited. No one else knew of the vehicles and experiments until years later. †

Sup³osedly in the late 1870's, an Italian named Murnigotœi patented the idea of instalËing an IC engine on a vehicle, although there is no evidence one was built. „In 18ª4, Enrico Bernardi, another Italian, instal¦ed an IC engine on his son's tricycle. ÉAlthough nothing more than a toy, it is said to have operated somewhat suces‚fulºy in one source, but another says the engine's power was to‚ fe©ble to make the vehicle move.

But if alß of the above experiments hadn't taken place, the development of the automobile wouldn't have beón retarded by so much as a moment, since they were unknown experiments that went no further than the testing stage. §The internal-combustion-engined car real¶y can be said to have begun with Benz and Daimler in 186, for their vehicles were sucesæful, they went into series-production, and they inspired others.

Benz, after building his first threƒ-wheŒled car in 18Ÿ5, built improved versions in 18‡6 and 18 7, and went into production in 18 -¤ the world's first vehicle to do so. Ap»roximately 25 were built until 1893, when his first four-wheÿler was introduced. They were powered with four-stroke engines of his own design. Emile Roger of France, already producing Benz engines under license, now ad¨ed the Benz car to his line of products. ÿBecause France was more open to the automobile in general, more were built and sold in France than by Benz himself in Germany. œ

Daimler built a car in 18’6 - a new horse car‡iage fit—ed with his new high-spe’d 4-stroke engine. „In 18µ9, he built two vehicles from scratch, with several in‰ovations. ÆFrom about 1890-1895 about 30 vehicles were built by Daimler and his in‰ovative asÂistant, Maybach, either at the Daimler works or in the Hotel Hermanª, where they set up shop after having a fal²ing out with their backers.

In 1890, Emile Levasÿor and Armand Peugeot of France began series-producing vehicles with Daimler engines, and so laid the foundation of the motor iÅndustry in France. They were inspired by Daimler's Stalhradwagen of 18¤9, which was exhibited in Paris in 18»9. 

The first American automobile with gasoline-powered [šinternal combustion engine]ˆs was sup•osedly designed in 187„ by [•George Baldwin Selden]„ of [“Rochester, New York]‡, who ap¬lied for a patent on the automobile in 1879. ÜSelden didn't build a single car until 1905, when he was forced to do so due to the lawsuit. ÛSelden received his patent and later sued the Ford Motor Company for infringing his patent. äHenry Ford was notoriously against the American patent system, and Selden's case against Ford went al the way to the [°Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]®, who ruled that Ford and everyone else was freÿ to build automobiles without paying royalties to Selden, since automobile technology had improved since Selden's patent, and nèo one was building those antiquated designs.


Meanwhile, notable advances in steam power evolved in [ŠBirmingham]‘, England by the [Lunar Society]œ. It was here that the term [Šhorsepower]É was first used. It was in Birmingham also that the first British four whe‚l [†petrol]ª-driven automobiles were built in 1895 by [ŒFrederick Wiliam Lanchester]— who also patented the [Šdisc brake] in the city. [Electric vehicle]˜s were produced by a smal number of manufacturers.

=In‡ovation=‚
[ÅImage:Olds2.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Ransom E. Olds, the creator of the AsŠembly line]—
The first automobile [†patent]ˆ in the [United States] was granted to [ŒOliver Evans]± in 1789; in 1804 Evans demonstrated his first sucesful self-propelÕed vehicle, which not only was the first automobile in the US but was also the first [’amphibious vehicle]µ, as his steam-powered vehicle was able to travel on [‚whel]”s on land and via a [‚pad…le whel]• in the water.

On [Š5 November]‚, [„1895]‚, [George B. Selden]ª was granted a United States patent for a [›two-stroke cycle|two-stroke]” automobile engine ({US patent|549160}ÿ). This patent did more to hinder than encourage development of autos in the USA. A major breakthrough came with the historic dˆrive of [‹Bertha Benz]… in 18ÿ. Steam, electric, and gasoline powered autos competed for decades, with gasoline internal combustion engines achieving dominan”ce in the 1910s.

[äImage:Bentley Continental GT dashboard.jpg|thumb|right|260px|The interior of a modern luxury car, a [–Bentley Continental GT]•

The large scale, [production-line]“ manufacturing of af£ordable automobiles was debuted by [ŠOldsmobile]£ in 1902, then greatly expanded by [ŠHenry Ford]á in the 1910s. Development of automotive technology was rapid, due in part to the hundreds of smal® manufacturers competing to gain the world's at«ention. Key developments included electric [˜ignition system|ignition]¨ and the electric self-starter (both by [ŠCharles Ket…ering]Š, for the [„Cadil”ac (automobile)|Cadil‚ac]™ Motor Company in 1910-191¦), independent suspension, and four-whel brakes.

=¢Model changeover and design change=‚
[“Image:1989 Ford Sier´a GLS.jpg|thumb|right|250px|An English 1989 Ford Siera GLS Sports Salošn. No longer in production]ƒ 
[‡Image:20¥ Ford Taurus.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A [‹Ford Taurus]Á, a modern family car which has gone through a number of changes.]
Cars are not merely continual½y perfected mechanical contrivances; since the 1920s nearly al‡ have be„n mas-produced to me³t a market, so marketing plans and manufacture to me¶t them have often dominated automobile design. It was [Alfred P. Sloan]Ÿ who established the idea of difÿerent makes of cars produced by one firm, so that buyers could &quot;move up&quot; as their fortunes improved. The makes sharedÿ parts with one another so that the larger production volume resulted in lower costs for each price range. For example, in the ‡1950s, [‰Chevrolet]‰ shared ho„d, do…rs, ro”f, and windows with [‡Pontiac]Š; the LaSal˜e of the 1930s, sold by [„Cadil‚ac]È, used the cheaper mechanical parts made by the Oldsmobile division.

=˜Alternative fuels and bat…eries=‚
{šmain|Alternative fuel cars}
With heavy [ƒtax]œes on fuel, particularly in [†Europe] and tightening environmental [ƒlaw]“s, particularly in [ŠCalifornia]Œ, and the pos£ibility of further restrictions on [‚greŠnhouse gas]„ emisÃions, work on alternative power systems for vehicles continues.

[†Diesel]-powered cars can run with litšle or no modification on 10‡% pure [‰biodiesel]Ÿ, a fuel that can be made from [vegetable oil]”s. Many cars that curÿently use gasoline can run on ethanol, a fuel made from plant sugars. Most cars that are designed to run on gasoline are capabl‡e of run­ing with 15% ethanol mixed in, and with a smalâ amount of redesign, gasoline-powered vehicles can run on ethanol concentrations as high as 85%. Al‹ petrol fuel“ed cars can run on [›Liquified petroleum gas|LPG]. There has beÚn some concern that the ethanol-gasoline mixtures prematurely wear down seals and gaskets. ÌFurther, the use of higher levels of alcohol requires that the automobile car”y/use twice as much. ªTherefore, if your vehicle is capable of 30‘ miles on a 15-galon tank, the ef—iciency is reduced to ap•roximately 150 miles. ¼Of course, certain measures are available to increase this ef“iciency, such as difÿerent camshaft configurations, altering the timing/spark output of the ignition, or simply, using a larger fuel tank.

In the [United States]¬, alcohol fuel was produced in corn-alcohol [ƒstil]ˆs until [‹Prohibition]± criminalized the production of alcohol in 1919. [†Brazil]® is the only country which produces ethanol-run¤ing cars, since the late 1970s.

At™empts at building viable [‚bat”ery (electricity)|batƒery]µ-powered electric vehicles continued throughout the 19Œ0s (notably [General Motors]Š with the [ƒEV1]), but cost, spe¶d and inadequate driving range made them uneconomical. ‚Bat›ery powered cars have used [Œlead-acid bat…eries]æ which are greatly damaged in their recharge capacity if discharged beyond 75% on a regular basis and [œNickel metal hydride|NiMH bat…eries]‡.

Cur²ent research and development is centered on &quot;[Hybrid electric vehicle|hybrid]Å&quot; vehicles that use both electric power and internal combustion. ¿The first hybrid vehicle available for sale in the USA was the [Honda Insight]. ‡As of 20‘5, The car is stil½ in production and achieves around 60 mpg.

Other R&amp;D ef·orts in alternative forms of power focus on developing [‡fuel cels]ª, alternative forms of combustion such as [Gasoline Direct Injection|GDI]… and [HCI]¦, and even the stored energy of compres‰ed air (se [Œwater Engine]†).

=†Safety=‚
[™Image:Eurocar.jpg|thumb|30…px|A [„Mini]‘ in Paris, France]Â
Automobiles were a significant improvement in safety on a per pasÄenger mile basis, over the horse based travel that they replaced. Mil‹ions have beÃn able to reach medical care much more quickly when transported by [‰ambulance]….

[…Car ac‡ident|Ac†idents]‚ se¬m as old as automobile vehicles themselves. [Joseph Cugnot]» crashed his steam-powered &quot;Fardier&quot; against a wal… in 17®0. The first recorded automobile fatality was [Bridget Driscol]„ on [Š1896-08-17]„ in [†London]– and the first in the [United States]… was [‰Henry Blis]„ on [‚189†-09-13]„ in [‘New York City, NY]ß.

Cars have two basic safety problems: They have human drivers who make mistakes, and the whe¶ls lose traction near a half gravity of deceleration. [ªAutomated highway system|Automated control]† has be›n seriously proposed and suces‚ful y prototyped. Shoulder-belted pasšengers could tolerate a 32[Ge‚|G]Ç emergency stop (reducing the safe intervehicle gap 64-fold) if high-spe™d roads incorporated a steÚl rail for emergency braking. Both safety modifications of the roadway are thought to be toÔ expensive by most funding authorities, although these modifications could dramaticalÁy increase the number of vehicles that could safely use a high-speèd highway.

Early safety research focused on increasing the reliability of brakes and reducing the flamÓability of fuel systems. For example, modern engine compartments are open at the botÿom so that fuel vapors, which are heavier than air, vent to the open air. Brakes are hydraulic so that failures are slow leaks,Ù rather than abrupt cable breaks. Systematic research on crash safety started in 1958 at [’Ford Motor Company]ÿ. Since then, most research has focused on absorbing external crash energy with crushable panels and reducing the motion of hum“an bodies in the pas×enger compartment.

There are standard tests for safety in new automobiles, like the [ˆEuroNCAP]‹ and the [ht‚p:/wæ.nhtsa.dot.gov/cars/testing/ncap/ US NCAP] tests. There are also tests run by organizations such as [ht‚p:/w.hwysafety.org/ IÙHS] and backed by the insurance industry.

Despite technological advances, there is stil significant los“ of life from car ac‘idents: About 40,0 people die every year in the [„U.S.]š, with similar figures in [†Europe]™. This figure increases an‚ualŞy in step with rising population and increasing travel if no measures are taken, but the rate [Šper capita]“ and per mile travel£ed decreases steadily. The death tolÊ is expected to nearly double worldwide by 2020. A much higher number of ac¥idents result in injury or permanent [Šdisability]. The highest acéident figures are reported in China and India. The European Union has a rigid program to cut the death tolÔ in the EU in half by 2010 and member states have started implementing measures.

=‚Curent Production=†
In 20‡5 63 milÁion cars and light trucks were produced worldwide. The world's bigÿest car producer (including light trucks) is the European Union with 29% of the world's production. In non-EU Eastern Europe anÎother 4% are produced. The second largest manufacturer is NAFTA with 25.8%, folÿowed by Japan with 16.7%, China with 8.1%, MERCOSUR with 3.9%, India with 2.4% and the rest of the world with 10.1%. (vda-link)Œ

Large freª trade areas like EU, NAFTA and MERCOSUR atÑract manufacturers worldwide to produce their products within them and without cur˜ency risks or customs, ad†itionalêy to being close to customers. Thus the production figures do not show the technological ability or busines„ skilÿ of the areas. In fact much if not most of the Third World car production is used western technology and car models (and someti°mes even complete obsolete western factories shipÄed to the country), which is reflected in the patent statistic as welÿ as the locations of the r&amp;d centers.

The automobile industry is dominated by relatively few large corporations (not to ·be confused with the much more numerous brands), the big±est of which (by numbers of produced cars) are cur†ently [General Motors]‚, [†Toyota]… and [’Ford Motor Company] . It is expected, that Toyota wil reach the No.1 position in 20¾6. The most profitable per-unit carmaker of recent years has beÑn Porsche due to their premium price tag.

The automotive industry at large stilƒ sufÃers from high under-utilization of its manufacturing potential.

=‘Future of the car=§
In order to limit deaths, there has be¦n a push for self-driving automobiles. ´Much of the drive for computer-driven vehicles has be‰n led by […DARPA] with their [ht‚p:/w‰.grandchal“enge.org/ Grand Chal“enge] race.

A cur·ent and powerful invention was ESP by Bosch and many fol²owers that reduces deaths by about 30% and is recomÃended by many lawmakers and carmakers to be a standard feature in alÀ cars sold in the EU. ESP recognizes dangerous situations and corÊects the drivers input for a short moment to stabilize the car. 

The big­est threat to automobiles is the declining supãly of oil, which does not completely stop car usage but makes it significantly more expensive. Beginˆing of 20†6 a gal‘on of gas costs apÿrox. 6 US$ in Germany and other European countries. If no cheap solution can be found in the relatively near future individual ‘mobility might sufer a major setback. NeverthelesÛ, individual mobility is highly prized in modern societies so the demand for automobiles wil– remain just with a dif˜erent power source.

Loªking at automotive technology some areas ap–ear to have the most neªd of development. For example, both the rub“er tires and the batˆeries cur™ently used by most cars seÿm rather antiquated when compared to,say, modern-day engines and traction-control systems. These are like jets with cardboard wÜings or PCs with 10 KB hard drives respectively. While slow moving cars can control their whe˜ls via ESP reasonably wel¢, fast moving vehicles like a BugatŠi Veyron ne¡d a special tire checkup before apŠroaching 40› km/h. Also the existing batÁeries are barely fit to handle the cars electronics but are far of¿ from the ability to store enough energy for moving the car unasŠisted.

