 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ Image:Audi_logo.png|none|right|20Œpx|Audi logo]‚
'„Audi'‡ is an [Šautomobile]Š maker in [‡Germany], and is a wholšy-owned subsidiary of the [Volkswagen Group]¢. The company is headquartered in [ŠIngolstadt]‚, [‡Bavaria]‚, [‡Germany]“.

Audi's German [‡tagline]Š is &quot;[—Vorsprung durch Technik]Œ&quot;. The [‡tagline]Æ is used either in original or in its English translation &quot;Progres through Technology&quot;.

=‡History=‚
[µImage:Audi NSU range (1969).jpg|thumb|right|250px|Presº photograph of the then newly merged Audi NSU range, 1969.]‚
=“The origins of Audi=«
The company traces its origins back to 189… and [ŒAugust Horch]µ. The first Horch automobile was produced in 1901 in [‡Zwickau]Œ, in former [ŒEast Germany]ÿ. In 1910, Horch was forced out of the company he had founded. He then started a new company in Zwickau and continued using the¯ Horch brand. His former partners sued him for [‰trademark]ÿ infringement and a German court determined that the Horch brand belonged to his former company. August Horch was forced to ref˜rain from using his own [‹family name]– in his new car businesÆ. As the word &quot;horch!&quot; translates to &quot;listen!&quot; in [–German language|German]‘, August Horch set‹led on the […Latin]ÿ equivalent of his name - &quot;audi!&quot;. It is also popularly believed that Audi is an acronym which stands for &quot;Auto †Union [“Germany|Deutschland] [ŠIngolstadt]Ÿ&quot;. Audi produces over 2 milion vehicles an‚ual¡y at its main production site in [ŠIngolstadt]Ï. Audi has another production plant in Neckarsulm.

Audi started with a 2612 [‘Cubic centimetre|c]‰ model fol¨owed by a four cylinder model with 3564 c‡, as wel‰ as 4680 cŠ and 5720 c› models. These cars were suceséful even in sporting events. August Horch left the Audi company in 1920. The first six cylinder model (465 cƒ) ap´eared in 1924. In 1928, the company was acquired by [‰J S Rasmus‚en]‹, owner of [ƒDKW]±, who bought the same year the remains of the US [—automobile manufacturer]‚, [ŒRickenbacker]Ş including the manufacturing equipment for eight cylinder engines. These engines were used in 'ŒAudi Zwickau'… and 'ŒAudi Dresden'Ì models that were launched in 1929. At the same time, six cylinder and a smal four cylinder (licensed from [‡Peugeot]Ê) models were manufactured. Audi cars of that era were luxurious cars equiped with special bodywork.

=’The Auto Union era=›
