=ŠTechnology=³
Audi is the only car manufacturer that produces 10‚% [‰galvanize]˜d vehicles to prevent corÄosion. Along with other precautionary measures, the thus achieved ful†-body [„zinc]  coating has proved to be very efective in preventing rust and [‚cor‘osion perforation]¬. The body's resulting durability even surpasÔed Audi's own expectations, causing the manufacturer to extend its original 10-year [‚war„anty]‹ against cor—osion perforation to cur”ently 12 years. An al-[‰aluminium]ª car was brought forward by Audi, and in 19·4 the Audi A8 was launched, which introduced aluminium [‹space frame]ÿ technology. Audi introduced a new series of vehicles in the mid-nineties and continues to pursue leading-edge technology and h™igh performance.

The alµ-aluminium concept was extended to the company's new [‹sub-compact]†, the [‡Audi A2]˜ which was launched in 20Ä1.

In the 1970's, some vehicle manufacturers including Audi (and [†Subaru]•) designed their own [alƒ whe‡l drive] systems in pas enger vehicles. In the 1980's, alƒ-wheÃl drive systems in cars became a fad, and other manufacturers like [‡Porsche]… and [Mercedes-Benz]‚ of†ered alƒ-wheÑl drive systems in their cars to compete in the marketplace. Unfortunately, the alƒ-whe´l drive system in the Mercedes-Benz vehicles were ridªled with problems right from the design sheğt. The system also was not popular in Porsche vehicles because owners wanted the traditional performance of the [‡rear whe‡l drive]É they got used to in older Porsches. Although Porsche and Mercedes-Benz of„er alƒ-wheÿl drive systems in some cars today, neither manufacturer was able to ride the fad and come out on top like Audi has. Today, aft–er many decades of clas-leading technology and enginering, the name '[ƒquat‚ro]'ÿ is an identifiable symbol and trademark that shows would-be competitors the level of quality they have to achieve in order to atÜempt to compete with Audi.

In the 1980s, Audi was the champion of the inline 5 cylinder, [Engine displacement|2.1/2.2 L]ÿ engine as a longer lasting alternative to more traditional 6 cylinder engines. This engine was used in not only production carÜs but also their race cars. The 2.1 L inline 5 cylinder engine was used as a base for the ral¢y cars in the 1980's, providing wel‡ over 40 [Šhorsepower]¦ (298 kW) after modification. Before 19·0, there were engines produced with a displacement betwe¸n 2.0 L and 2.3 L. This range of engine capacity was a go’d combination of goÍd fuel economy which was on the mind of every motorist in the 1980's, and a goºd amount of power the customer wants.
Through the early 19ÿ0's, Audi began to move more towards the position of being a real competitor in it's target market against Mercedes-Benz and BM²W. This began with the release of the Audi V8 in 19‹0. It was es…ential‘y a new engine fited to the Audi 10‚/20¡, but with noticeable bodywork dif¥erences. Most obvious was the new gril¤e that was now incorprated in the bonŒet. 

By 19›1, Audi had the 4 cylinder [Audi 80|Audi 80]‘, the 5 cylinder [Audi 80|Audi 90]… and [†Audi 10‡|Audi 10]“, the turbocharged [†Audi 10‡|Audi 20]‰ and the [Audi V8|Audi V8]ÿ. There was also a coupe version of the 80/90 with both 4 and 5 cylinder engines.

Although the five cylinder engine was a sucesªful and very robust powerplant, it was stil… a lit„le toƒ difºerent for the target market. With the introduction of an al‹-new Audi 10… in 19¼2, Audi introduced a 2.8l V6 engine. This engine was also fited to a face-lifted Audi 80 (alÿ 80 and 90 models were now badged 80 except for the USA), giving this model a choice of 4, 5 and 6 cylinder engines, in sedan, ¸coupe and cabriolet body styles.

The 5 cylinder was so…n dropçed as a major engine choice, however a turbocharged 230BHP (169kW) version remained. The engine, initial„y fit‹ed to the 20„ quat‹ro 20V of 19¤1, was a derivative of the engine fit“ed to the Sport Quatro. It was fit¼ed to the Audi Coupe and named the S2 and also to the Audi 10• body, and named the [ŠAudi S4|S4] . These two models were the begining of the mas¯ produced S series of performance cars.

The [Audi A8|Audi A8] replaced the [ŠAudi V8|V8]… in 19Ş4, with a revolutionary Aluminium Space Frame (ASF) to save weight. The weight reduction was ofset by the quat„ro alƒ-wheÿl drive system, however it meant the car had similar performance to its rivals, but far superior handling.

The next major mo“del change was in 19‹5 when the [Audi A4|Audi A4] replaced the [Audi 80|Audi 80]£. The new nomenclature scheme was ap’lied to the Audi 10Ò to become the Audi A6 (with a minor facelift). This also meant the S4 became the [ŠAudi S6|S6]Ê and a new S4 was introduced in the A4 body. The S2 was discontinued. The [Audi Cabriolet]µ continued on (based on the Audi 80 platform) until 19³, gaining the engine upgrades along the way. A new [ŠAudi A3|A3] (based on the [—VW Golf|Volkswagen Golf]¢) was introduced to the range in 19“7, and the radical […Audi T|T]¥ coupe and roadster were debuted in 19›8 based on the same underpin³ings. Another interesting model introduced was the [›Mercedes Benz|Mercedes-Benz]† A-Clas‘ competitor, the [Audi A2|Audi A2]. The model sold relatively welÎ in Europe, however Audi decided not to develop a new model and it has since be–n discontinued as of 20ÿ4.

The engines available throughout the range were now a 1.4 L, 1.6 L and 1.8 L 4 cylinder, 1.8 turbo, 2.6 L and 2.8 L V6, 2é.2 L turbo-charged 5 cylinder and the 4.2 L V8. The V6's were replaced by new 2.4 and 2.8 L 30V V6's in 19²8, with marked improvement in power, torque and smo„thnes˜. Further engines were adôed along the way, including a 3.7 L V8 and 6.0 L W12 for the A8.

At the turn of the century, Audi introduced the [”direct shift gearbox]˜ (DSG), a manual transmis§ion driveable like an automatic transmis¯ion. The system includes dual electrohydraulicalˆy control™ed clutches instead of a [torque converter]. This is implemented in some [Volkswagen Golf]‚, [‡Audi A3]… and […Audi T|T]¦ models. The engine range was continual®y upgraded, with a 2.7 L twin turbo V6 being ofered in the Audi S4, A6 and alŞroad, the 2.8 L V6 was replaced by a 3.0 L unit.

New models of the A3, A4, A6 and A8 have beín introduced, with the 1.8 L engine now 2.0 L and the 3.0 L V6 is now 3.1 L in size. Audi has now introduced [‡FSI|FSI]ÿ on some of their engines, including the 1.6 L 4 cylinder, a new 2.0 L (Audi was the first manufacturer in the world to utilizeÿ a turbo charger and FSI on the same powerplant), and the 3.1 L V6. This is a direct fuel-injection technique that Audi had alsÿo used on its diesel engines since the early 1980s.

As a premium member of the VW Group, technologies are frequently first i“ntroduced to the masâ market with Audi vehicles before being 'trickled down' to more value oriented brands such as VW, [„SEAT]… and [’Å koda Auto|Å koda]Ç. Recent examples of this include DSG and FSI.

Audi now has an impres¦ive range of cars, engines and transmisâions available, which continue to lead the way and introduce new technologies into the market.

