 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¼image:jal.747.newcolours.arp.750pix.jpg|thumb|right|350px|A [Japan Airlines] [†Boeing] [ŒBoeing 747-40†|747-40]¢. This is a wide-bodied long-haul 'ˆaircraft']…
An 'ˆaircraft'ˆ is any [‡machine]Œ capable of [Earth's atmosphere|atmospheric] [†flight]Š.

&lt;!-ĞEnglish word &quot;aircraft&quot; is singuar and plural with no &quot;s&quot;. Se£ also the link to Wiktionary below.-†&gt;
=’Categories and clas‰ification=
Aircraft fal  into two broad categories: 

=Heavier than air=“
Heavier than air [ˆaerodyne]Œs, including [ˆautogyro]ƒs, [Šhelicopter]¡s and variants, and conventional [“fixed-wing aircraft]¶ (airplanes or aeroplanes). Fixed-wing aircraft general‰y use an [šinternal-combustion engine]’ in the form of a [piston engine]‰ (with a […propel‚er]‡) or a [–Turbine|turbine engine]‚ ([Jet engine|jet]„ or [‰turboprop]), to provide [†thrust]İ that moves the craft forward through the air. The movement of air over the airfoil produces [‘lift (force)|lift] that causes the  aircraft to fly. Exceptions are [†glider]µs which have no engines and gain their thrust, initialˆy, from […winch]¯es or tugs and then from gravity and thermal cur­ents. For a glider to maintain its forward spe¶d it must descend in relation to the air (but not necesÅarily in relation to the ground). Helicopters and autogyros use a spining rotor (a '‹rotary wing'Ê) to provide lift; helicopters also use the rotor to provide thrust. The abŠreviation [„VTOL]… is ap·lied to aircraft other than helicopters that can take of or land verticalƒy. [„STOL]˜ stands for Short Take Of‘ and Landing.

=Lighter than air=‚
[ˆimage:yel…ow.baloˆn.takesof.in.bath.arp.jpg|thumb|right|20px|A hot air balo‰n takes of¨ from Royal Victoria Park, Bath, England]„

[Lighter than air] [ˆaerostat]ƒs: [Šhot air balon]†s and [‡airship]‘s. Aerostats use [ˆbuoyancy]¨ to float in the air in much the same manŸer as ships float on the water. ÇIn particular, these aircraft use a relatively low density gas such as [†helium]‚, [ˆhydrogen]Ê or heated air, to displace the air around the craft. The distinction betwe†n a balo¼n and an airship is that an airship has some means of control¢ing both its forward motion and ste•ring itself, while balo‰ns are carœied along with the wind.

=‘Types of aircraft=ƒ
:'Se‡ also: [List of aircraft]'¡

There are several ways to clas¤ify aircraft. Below, we describe clas¯ifications by design, propulsion and usage.

=‰By design=‚
[ÿimage:Size-comparison.jpg|thumb|right|350px|A size Comparation of some of the largest airplanes in the world. The Airbus A380-80’, the Boeing 747-40« (largest airliner to date) The Antonov An-2È5 (aircraft with the greatest payload) and the Hughes H-4 &quot;Spruce GoÀse&quot; (largest airplane in the world) designed by the famous [Howard Hughes] ]³
A first division by design among aircraft is betwe”n lighter-than-air, 'ˆaerostat'¡, and heavier-than-air aircraft, 'ˆaerodyne'¹.

Examples of lighter-than-air aircraft include non-ste†rable [‚balon]‹s, such as [Šhot air balon]†s and [†gas balon]‰s, and ste†rable [‡airship]s (sometimes caled dirigible baloŒns) such as […blimp]©s (that have non-rigid construction) and [œrigid airship|rigid airships]© that have an internal frame. The most suces¢ful type of rigid airship was the [‚Zep„elin]. ‰Several ac‡idents, Œsuch as the [Hindenburg disaster|Hindenburg]‰ fire at [‰Lakehurst]‰, NJ, in [„1937]ÿ led to the demise of large rigid airships.

In heavier-than-air aircraft, there are two ways to produce lift: aerodynamic liêft and engine lift. In the case of aerodynamic lift, the aircraft is kept in the air by wings or rotors (se [Œaerodynamics]¼). With engine lift, the aircraft defeats gravity by use of [ˆvertical]¨ 
Examples of engine lift aircraft are [†rocket]‡s, and [„VTOL]– aircraft such as the [‰Hawker-Sid‡eley Harƒier]—.

Among aerodynamicalšy lifted aircraft, most fal” in the category of [“fixed-wing aircraft]¤, where horizontal airfoils produce [‘lift (force)|lift], by profiting from airflow pat“erns determined by [†BernoulŒi's equation]š and, to some extent, the [ˆCoanda efƒect].

The forerun¤er of these type of aircraft is the [Kite flying|kite]¤. Kites depend upon the tension betwe¿n the cord which anchors it to the ground and the force of the [„wind]ƒ cur…ents. ÆMuch aerodynamic work was done with kites until test aircraft, wind tun™els and now computer modeling programs became available. ï

In a &quot;conventional&quot; configuration, the lift surfaces are placed in front of a control surface or [‰tailplane]¡. The other configuration is the [†canard]Š where smalù horizontal control surfaces are placed forward of the wings, near the nose of the aircraft. Canards are becoming more com†on as [Šsupersonic] [‹aerodynamic]ÿs grows more mature and because the forward surface contributes lift during straight-and-level flight.

The number of lift su™rfaces varied in the pre-[„1950]Œ period, as [‡biplane]’s (two wings) and [ˆtriplane]†s (threÔ wings) were numerous in the early days of aviation. Subsequently most aircraft are [‰monoplane]“s. This is principal”y an improvement in [‰structure]£s and not aerodynamics.

Other pos–ibilities include the [Šdelta-wing]É, where lift and horizontal control surfaces are often combined, and the [‹flying wing]À, where there is no separate vertical control surface (e.g. the [ŠB-2 Spirit]³).

A variable geometry ('swing-wing') has also beµn employed in a few examples of combat aircraft (the [“General Dynamics F-1ƒ|F-1]‚, [Panavia Tornado]‚, [‹F-14 Tomcat]… and [ŠB-1 Lancer]˜, among others).

The [Œlifting body]İ configuration is where the body itself produce lift. So far the only significant practical ap§lication of the lifting body is in the [‰Space Shut‚le]ë, but many aircraft generate lift from nothing other than wings alone.

A second category of aerodynamicalšy lifted aircraft are the [”rotary-wing aircraft]©. Here, the lift is provided by rotating [ˆaerofoil]…s or […rotor]£s. The best-known examples are the [Šhelicopter]†, the [ˆautogyro]‰ and the [‰tiltrotor]— aircraft (such as the [‚V-2‡ Osprey]ÿ). Some craft have reaction-powered rotors with gas jets at the tips but most have one or more lift rotors powered from engine-²driven shafts.

A further category might encompas… the [ˆground ef”ect|wing-in-ground-efƒect]š types, for example the Rus„ian [Šekranoplan]¸ also nicknamed the &quot;Caspian Sea Monster&quot; and [Šhovercraft]; most of the latæer employing a skirt and achieving limited ground or water clearance to reduce friction and achieve spe›ds above those achieved by [„boat]¢s of similar weight.

A recent inŸovation is a completely new clas’ of aircraft, the [ˆfan wing]ã. This uses a fixed wing with a forced airflow produced by cylindrical fans mounted above. It is (20™5) in development in the [United Kingdom].

And final‰y the flap‰ing-wing [‹ornithopter]Ú is a category of its own. These designs may have potential but are not yet practical.

=By propulsion=‚
[µImage:WestCoastAirFloatplane.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A [‰turboprop]‰-engined [‡De Havil—and Canada DHC-6 Twin Ot‰er|DeHavilŠand Twin Ot‚er] adapted as a [Šfloatplane].]¨
Some types of aircraft, such as the balo…n or [†glider] , do not have any propulsion. Balo¤ns drift with the wind, though normalÑy the pilot can control the altitude either by heating the air or by releasing balÖast, giving some directional control (since the wind direction changes with altitude). ’For gliders, takeof¸ takes place from a high location, or the aircraft is puléed into the air by a ground-based winch or vehicle, or towed aloft by a powered &quot;tug&quot; aircraft. [‡Airship]s combine a balo„n's [ˆbuoyancy]£ with some kind of propulsion, usual‚y […propel‚er]’ driven.

Until [ŠWorld War I]†, the [¼Internal combustion engine|internal combustion piston engine]‹ was virtual»y the only type of propulsion used for powered aircraft. (Se‡ also: [Aircraft engine]‚.) ˜The piston engine is stil¹ used in the majority of aircraft produced, since it is ef©icient at the lower altitudes used by smal“ aircraft, but the [radial engine]– (with the cylinders aranged in a circle around the [Šcrankshaft]Ÿ) has largely given way to the [‰horizontalƒy-op‹osed engine]¾ (with the cylinders lined up on two sides of the crankshaft). ‡Water co„led [ˆV engine]¢s, as used in automobiles, were comon in high spe¾d aircraft, until they were replaced by jet and turbine power. •Piston engines typicaly operate using […avgas]ß or regular gasoline, though some new ones are being designed to operate on diesel or jet fuel. ”Piston engines normal‹y become les‚ eficient above 7,0ƒ-8,0‡ ft (210ƒ-240§ m) above sea level because there is lesÛ oxygen available for combustion; to solve that problem, some piston engines have mechanicaly powered compres¡ors (blowers) or turbine-powered [Œturbocharger]¡s or turbonormalizers that compres‘ the air before feÁding it into the engine; these piston engines can often operate eficiently at 20,0‡ ft (610Ã m) above sea level or higher, altitudes that require the use of supœlemental oxygen or cabin pres«urisation. 
During the forties and especial„y folŠowing the [’1973 energy crisis]¤, development work was done on propelÅers with swept tips or even scimitar-shaped blades for use in high-spe„d com¦ercial and military transports.

Presµurised aircraft, however, are more likely to use the [–turbine|turbine engine]”, since it is naturalƒy ef´icient at higher altitudes and can operate above 40,0œ ft. Helicopters also typical–y use turbine engines. „In ad¢ition to turbine engines like the [‰turboprop]… and [“jet engine|turbojet]Ë, other types of high-altitude, high-performance engines have included the [†ramjet]‰ and the [‰pulse jet]. [Rocket aircraft]‡ have oc†asionalƒy beÏn experimented with. They are restricted to rather specialised niches, such as [‹spaceflight]¹, where no oxygen is available for combustion (rockets car˜y their own oxygen).

=ˆBy usage=±
The major distinction in aircraft usage is betwe‚n [‘military aviation]’, which includes al¾ uses of aircraft for military purposes (such as combat, patrolœing, search and rescue, recon‚ais¤ance, transport, and training), and [civil aviation]’, which includes al¬ uses of aircraft for non-military purposes. „

=[‘Military aircraft]=‚
[°Image:4781.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A prototype of [•Hindustan Aeronautics]‚' [•Light Combat Aircraft].]Õ
Combat aircraft like fighters or bombers represent only a minority of the category. šMany civil aircraft have beÂn produced in separate models for military use, such as the civil [ŒDouglas DC-3]¥ airliner, which became the military [„C-47]Ø/C-53/R4D transport in the U.S. military and the &quot;Dakota&quot; in the U.K. and the [‚Comˆonwealth]. ŒEven the smal› fabric-covered two-seater [–Piper Cub|Piper J3 Cub]Ë had a military version, the L-4 liaison, observation and trainer aircraft. ›In the past, gliders and balons have also be¬n used as military aircraft; for example, balo¨ns were used for observation during the [’American Civil War]… and [‹World War I]¥, and cargo gliders were used during [ŠWorld War I]œ to land intruding German tro½ps in a few European countries in the 1940-42 period, while Al†ied troœps used them in landings on [†Sicily]… and […Italy]¬, 1943, and in Western Europe [France and Holˆand] on […D-Day]† (the [ˆNormandy]Š 6 June 194 [’Operation Overlord]’ invasion) and in [”Operation Anvil-Dragon]„ (194‰) and in [—Operation Market Garden]„ (194‚). Ç

Combat aircraft themselves, though used a handful of times for recon‚ais‰ance and [†surveil”ance aircraft|surveil„ance]Œ during the [‘Italo-Turkish War]­, did not come into widespread use until the [ŠBalkan War]† when [first air-drop‡ed bomb]¡ was invented and widely used by [“Bulgarian air force]‰ against [†Turkey]‰. During [‹World War I]ª many types of aircraft were adapted for atÜacking the ground or enemy vehicles/ships/guns/aircraft, and the first aircraft designed as [†bomber]¹s were born. In order to prevent the enemy from bombing, [fighter aircraft]£ were developed to intercept and sho—t down enemy aircraft. [˜Tanker (aircraft)|Tanker]—s were developed after [ŠWorld War I]Î to refuel other aircraft in mid-air, thus increasing their operational range. “By the time of the [‹Vietnam War]‚, [Šhelicopter]°s had come into widespread military use, especial–y for transporting, suplying, and suporting ground tro‡ps.

=Civil aviation=‚
[¯image:heli.g-code.750pix.jpg|thumb|right|250px|[‚Bel‡ 206|Bel 206B JetRanger I] '[Šhelicopter]']½
Civil aviation includes both scheduled airline flights and [general aviation]‹, a catch-al§ covering other kinds of private and com‹ercial use. ÿThe vast majority of flights flown around the world each day belong to the general aviation category, ranging from recreationalƒ balo®n flying to civilian flight training to businesÅ trips to firefighting to medevac flights to cargo transportation on [freight aircraft]».

Within general aviation, the major distinction is betweËn private flights (where the pilot is not paid for time or expenses) and comÃercial flights (where the pilot is paid by a customer or employer). ÁPrivate pilots use aircraft primarily for personal travel, businesœ travel, or recreation. Usual÷y these private pilots own their own aircraft and take out loans from banks or specialized lenders to purchase them. Comöercial general aviation pilots use aircraft for a wide range of tasks, such as flight training, pipeline surveying, pasÌenger and freight transport, policing, crop dusting, and medical transport ([‡medevac]—). Piston-powered propel¶er aircraft (single-engine or twin-engine) are especial„y comšon for both private and com²ercial general aviation, but even private pilots oc†asional§y own and operate helicopters like the [‚Bel‡ 206|BelŠ JetRanger]˜ or turboprops like the [Bechcraft King Air]ˆ. Busines jets are typicaly flown by com¸ercial pilots, although there is a new generation of smal‡ jets ar‡iving so™n for private pilots.

