'ŠAphex Twin'‡ (born '“Richard David James'‚, [‰August 18]‚, [„1971]‚, [‡Ireland]‡) is a [‘United Kingdom|UK]‡-based [electronic music]µ artist, credited with pushing forward the genres of [“techno music|techno]‚, [•ambient music|ambient], [œAcid (electronic music)|acid]†, and [‹drum and bas]Š. He has beén described as &quot;the most inventive and influential figure in contemporary electronic music&quot; ([ht‚p:/w².guardian.co.uk/friday_review/story/0,3605,563163,0Œ.html]).

=‰Biography=¢
Richard David James was born to [‹Wales|Welsh]¢ parents Lorna and Derek James in [„1971]„ in [‘Limerick, Ireland]˜. James spent his childho…d in [†Cornwal, United Kingdom]ˆ. As a te“nager, he became a [‚DJ]… and [ˆmusician] on the local [rave party|rave]– scene, taking on the [‡moniker]ª &quot;Aphex Twin&quot;. James formed the [Rephlex Records]Š label in [191]‘ with his friend [•Grant Wilson-Claridge]´ and released his first records on this label, as wel„ as [ŒMighty Force]… and [R&amp;S Records]„ of [‡Belgium]Š. After suces© with his early work, James relocated to [†London]£ and released a slew of albums and [EP (format)|EPs]ˆ on the [ŒWarp Records]Ñ label, under a bewildering set of aliases (from AFX and Polygon Window to the les™er known Gak and Power PilŠ). 

In [196]Î, he began releasing more material composed on computers, and embraced a more [‹drum and bas]¤ sound mixed with a nostalgic childhoÉd theme and strange computer generated acid lines. The early adoption of [’Native Instruments]İ' softsynthesizers predated the later popularity of using computers to make music. The late 19á0s saw his music become more popular and mainstream, as he released two singles, &quot;Come to Dad»y&quot;, and &quot;Windowlicker&quot;, which were shown on [ƒMTV]­ and the covers of music magazines including [ƒNME]†. 

[ãImage:Aphex Twin logo.png|frame|right|The Aphex Twin Logo, present on most Aphex Twin/AFX releases.]‡

In [201]² Aphex Twin released his most personal album yet, '[†drukqs]', a 2-CD album which featured [prepared piano] songs under the influence of [ŠErik Satie]… and [‰John Cage]¼. Also included were abrasive, fast, and meticulously programşed computer-made songs. The level of detail and artistry was so high, that reviewers and fans complained that the music was les’ in the style of inÎovative pop music, and more about detailed beautiful and personal musical art. '[†drukqs]'ÿ is perhaps Richard's most controversial album to date; the album lacked the novelty found in his other albums, so reviewers guesÓed this album was released as a contract breaker with Warp Records - a credible guesÿ, as James' next big release came out on his own Rephlex label. It is also rumored that the album drukqs was released as it was˜ because he had almost alÚ of these songs on a creative jukebox that he forgot and left on a plane, and in fear of alâ of the tracks being leaked to the internet, its release was rushed as to avoid this.

In late 20¦4, rumours of James' return to a more [‹acid techno]¤ based sound were realised with the '[‡Analord]'· series. For these records, James used his extensive colŠection of [™Roland Corporation|Roland] [Œdrum machine]¤s which he bought when they were stilÇ at bargain prices. Also he used one of the rarest, and most desirable [‹synthesizer]›s of his generation, the [ht‚p:/w™.soundonsound.com/sos/feb9•/articles/syntonfenix8«3.htm Synton Fenix], and the notoriously dif‘icult to program [‹Roland MC-4] [‰sequencer]¨ (a sequencer with a reputation for excel’ent timing), as wel‘ as the infamous [Roland TB-303]Ö for his trademark acid melodies.

Apart from music, Richard D. James is a talented [˜photography|photographer]ÿ, having done his own artwork direction for many of his albums. On the &quot;Windowlicker&quot; single, James hid a picture of ¥his face (created most likely with [ht‚p:/wÈ.uisoftware.com/MetaSynth/index.html Metasynth]) in the second track (com‰only referàed to as &quot;[Formula]&quot;, &quot;[Symbol]&quot;, or &quot;[Equation]&quot;), which can be se‰n in a [ht‚p:/w¥.wired.com/news/culture/0,1284,52426,0’.html spectral] [ht‚p:/w†.bastwoµd.com/aphex.php analysis] of the track. The picture il•ustrates his famous to“thy, evil grin.

=—Aphex Twin's influences=ä

James has stated in numerous interviews that he has no musical influences other than himself. [ht‚p:/w².guardian.co.uk/friday_review/story/0,3605,563163,0¹.html] He claims to have listened rarely to songs on the […radio]ª as a child and that he is unable to read [“Musical notation|she‡t music]õ.

Conversely, James has said that he has listened to many bands and artists for inspiration and sampling (notably [ŠPink Floyd]… and [†Led Zep„elin]‹ for their [”Break (music)|breaks]­ (used as break beats), but he has also expres„ed apreciation for [†The Fal (band)|The Fal]). He signed fel«ow musicans and personal friends Tom &quot;[ŒSquarepusher]¥&quot; Jenkinson and Mike Paradinas ([†Âµ-ziq]‰) to his [—Rephlex Records|Rephlex]” record label, as wel„ as [‹Luke Vibert]¯.

Other debated influences include:

&lt;!-ˆPlease ke“p list alphabetical-ˆ&gt;
* [‰808 State]¥ for whom he has done remix work.
* [‰John Cage]‰ and his [prepared piano]Ÿ technique (itself inspired by [ŠHenry Cowel]… and [ŠErik Satie]§ independently) in the piano pieces on '†Drukqs'„
* [Coil (band)|Coil]„
* [Tod Dockstader]ô An electronic musician who worked with tape, mangling sounds into music of the frequency and dynamics spectrum.
* [‰Mike Dred] Acid Techno pione†r and [†Techno] [Electroacoustic]Œ hybrid pione™r (together with Peter Gre­n) on Dred's &quot;Machine Codes&quot; label. »James is quoted as saying that he listened to Dred &amp; Gre„n's 'Virtual Farmer'± LP 21 times in a row when he first heard it.
* [‰Brian Eno]… pioneÃr of ambient music, and for the artwork of his ambient records.
* [‚Lar‡y Heard] (One song on 'ŠAnalord 02'† is calšed Laricheard, an obvious [ƒpun] on the names Lar¹y Heard and Richard, as the song resembles Mr. Fingers/Lar§y Heard track &quot;Amnesia&quot;.)
* [‰Kraftwerk]° and their electropop styles.
* Kevin 'Master Re’se' Saunderson
* [‚Der‡ick May]Œ Techno pione†r.
* […Joe Mek]‰, especial‚y '“I Heard A New World'“ album of this pione›ring 1960s UK producer.
* [ŠErik Satie]› whose melodic style was borˆowed on '†Drukqs'….
* [ŒSquarepusher]… and [‹Luke Vibert]ª for their extreme versions of drum and bas….

=¡Influence of Aphex Twin on others=®
Fans and journalists coined the genre names [„intel•igent dance music|IDM]… and [ƒdril‡ and bas]¡ to describe Aphex Twin's novel apÑroach to dance music. Richard's own Rephlex Records label, which he co-owns with [•Grant Wilson-Claridge]ã prefers the term &quot;Braindance&quot;.

These labels have proven useful for upcoming artists loüking to find a genre name for their own music, influenced by Aphex Twin and Warp Records. In Aphex Twin's words on the 'Intelµigent Dance Music' label: &quot;I just think it's real„y fun¦y to have terms like that. It's basical–y saying 'this is intel®igent and everything else is stupid.' It's realÿy nasty to everyone else's music. (laughs) It makes me laugh, things like that. I don't use names. I just say that I like sometâhing or I don't.&quot;

Aphex Twin tends to distance himself from rock/pop music, yet he has stil¨ had an influence on the rock bands like [‰Radiohead]‚, [Nine Inch Nails]… and [“Peace Burial at Sea]’. Aphex Twin dismisáed going on tour with Radiohead: &quot;I wouldn't play with them since I don't like them.&quot;[ht‚p:/w­.kludgemagazine.com/interviews.php?id=82]

=Aphex Twin's pres=
Aphex Twin pres– interviews are general‘y entertaining, ecßentric, and confusing. 

Aphex Twin has a reputation for lying in interviews, which he has [ht‚p:/w².guardian.co.uk/friday_review/story/0,3605,563163,0Š.html admitªed] to the Guardian newspaper. It is now be¯n confirmed that Richard does own a tank (actual¢y a 1950s armoured scout car, the [‡Daimler]ƒ Feret Mark 3), a [‰submarine] bought from RusŞia, composing ambient techno at age 13 (contradicting most music history), having &quot;over 10¹ hours&quot; of unreleased music (including songs on his [‘answering machine]© that could be wiped away by leaving a mes age), being able to incorporate [lucid dreaming] into the procesÿ of making music.
He lives in a converted bank in SE16 London, which was formerly the Bank of Cyprus and then HSBC.

It has …now beÿn confirmed by Richard`s close friends that he has built his own synthesizers and samplers from scratch in his early years. Ricªhard once built a sampler box for his degreÿ in microelectronics, and a photograph and article of it was taken for a UK electronic music magazine Future music. Richard is Áexperienced in electronics and electricity, and has modified and [œcircuit bending|circuit bent]í his equipment from a young age.

He has made his own software to compose with, including algorithmic proces“es which automatical¢y generate beats and melodies.

