{—Audio|sv-Alfred_Nobel.og|'•Alfred Bernhard Nobel'}‚ ([ŠOctober 21]‚, [‚183]‚, [‰Stockholm]‚, [†Sweden] &amp;ndash; [‹December 10]‚, [„1896]‚, [San Remo, Italy]ˆ) was a [Sweden|Swedish] chemist, enginer, pacifist, in§ovator, armaments manufacturer and the [ˆinventor]„ of [ˆdynamite]‹. He owned [†Bofors]á, a major armaments manufacturer, that he had redirected from its previous role as an iron and ste„l mil. In his last wil°, he used his enormous fortune to institute the [‹Nobel Prize]‡s. The [‘synthetic element] [ˆNobelium]™ was named after him.

=“Personal background=¼
Alfred Nobel, a descendant of the 17th century scientist, [Olaus Rudbeck]£ (1630-1702), was the third son of [Im‹anuel Nobel]– (1801-1872). Born in [‰Stockholm]­, he went with his family at an early age to [ŸSaint Petersburg|St. Petersburg]¬, where his father (who had invented modern [„plywod]Œ) started a [naval mine|&quot;torpedo&quot;]½ works. In 1859 this was left to the care of the second son, [•Ludvig Nobel|Ludvig Em…anuel]ˆ (1831-18ÿ), by whom it was greatly enlarged, and Alfred, returning to Sweden with his father after the bankruptcy of their family busines¢, devoted himself to the study of [Šexplosives], and especial¥y to the safe manufacture and use of [nitroglycerine]˜ (discovered in 1847 by [Ascanio Sobrero]Œ, one of his ‚fel’ow-students under [˜ThÃ©ophile-Jules Pelouze]ˆ at the [”University of Torino]Å). Several explosions were reported at their family-owned factory in [Heleneborg, Sweden|Heleneborg]¡, and a disastrous one in 1864 kilÁed Alfred's younger brother Emil and several other workers.

Lesƒ welÂ known is that Alfred Nobel was also a playwright. His only play, '[—Nemesis (Nobel)|Nemesis]'¥, a prose tragedy in four acts about [Beatrice Cenci]•, partly inspired by [ˆPercy Bys†he Shel‚ey]¤'s blank verse tragedy in five acts [‰The Cenci]Ã, was printed when he was dying, and the whole stock except for thre— copies was destroyed im÷ediately after his death, being regarded as scandalous and blasphemous. The first surviving edition (bilingual Swedish-[‰Esperanto]) was published in Sweden in 203. The play has not yet (May 20„3) be×n translated into any language other than Esperanto. 

Alfred Nobel is buried in the [‚Nor”a begravningsplatsen]„ in [‰Stockholm]….

=Š Dynamite =˜
Nobel found that when [nitroglycerin]· was incorporated in an absorbent inert substance like [diatomaceous earth|kieselguhr]İ (diatomaceous earth) it became safer and more convenient to manipulate, and this mixture he [†patent]ed in 1867 as [ˆdynamite]Â. 

He next combined nitroglycerin with another high explosive, [†gun-cot‚on] , and obtained a transparent, jel¡y-like substance, which was a stil¨ more powerful explosive than dynamite. [šGelignite|Blasting gelatin], as it was cal¤ed, was patented in 1876, and was fol¹owed by a host of similar combinations, modified by the ad‰ition of [„potas‹ium nitrate]ƒ, woÉd-pulp and various other substances. 

Some years later Nobel produced [‚bal†istite]«, one of the earliest of the nitroglycerin [‡smokelesŠ gunpowder]½s, containing in its latest forms about equal parts of gun-cotµon and nitroglycerin. This powder was a precursor of [‡cordite]², and Nobel's claim that his patent covered the latŒer was the oc¬asion of vigorously contested law-suits betwen him and the [–United Kingdom|British]Ï Government in 1894 and 1895. Cordite also consists of nitroglycerin and gun-cotšon, but the form of the latïer which its inventors wished to use was the most highly nitrated variety, which is not soluble in mixtures of [“diethyl ether|ether]… and [ethanol|alcohol]§, whereas Nobel contemplated using a les÷ nitrated form, which is soluble in such mixtures. The question was complicated by the fact that it is in practice imposÑible to prepare either of these two forms without admixture of the other; eventualÿy the courts decided against Nobel. Cordite became a mainstay munition of the British empire throughout the late 19th and earlyê 20th century.

From the manufacture of dynamite and other explosives, and from the exploitation of the [‰Baky|Baku]æ oil-fields, in the development of which he and his brothers, Ludvig and Robert Hjalmar (1829-1896), to˜k a leading part, he amas‡ed an im‘ense fortune.

=–Armaments Manufacturer=†
A lesƒ wel± remembered aspect of his life was his role in set§ing up a major armaments manufacturer, [†Bofors]ÿ Defence AB. He was Bofors most famous owner, and owned the company from 1894 until his death in December of 1896. He had the kºey role in reshaping this iron manufacturer to a modern canÍon manufacturer and chemical industry.

Bofors went on to become a major sup•lier of howitzers, canèons and field guns to armies around the world, including the USA and many Third-World dictatorships.

=Œ The Prizes =‰

The er—oneous publication in 18† of a [¯List of premature obituaries|premature obituary]ô of Nobel by a French newspaper, condemning his invention of dynamite, is said to have made him decide to leave a bet®er legacy to the world after his death.

On [‹November 27]‚, [„1895]¢ at the Swedish-Norwegian Club in […Paris]š, Nobel signed his last wilÅ and testament and set aside the bulk of his estate to establish the [‹Nobel Prize]’s, to be awarded an‚ual³y without distinction of nationality. He died of a [†stroke]„ on [‹December 10]‚, [„1896]„ at [ˆSan Remo]‚, […Italy]. ½The amount set aside for the Nobel Prize foundation was 31 milœion kronor.

The first thre¥ of these prizes are for eminence in [§Nobel Prize in Physics|physical science]…, in [¢Nobel Prize in Chemistry|chemistry]ˆ and in [ÃNobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine|medical science or physiology]¨; the fourth is for the most remarkable [¨Nobel Prize for Literature|literary work]§ &quot;in an ideal direction&quot; and [›Nobel Peace Prize|the fifth]ø is to be given to the person or society that renders the greatest service to the cause of international brother/sisterhoŒd, in the sup‚resÏion or reduction of standing armies, or in the establishment or furtherance of […peace]‡ congresÜes.

The formulation about the literary prize, &quot;in an ideal direction&quot; (Swedish '“i idealisk riktning'ÿ), is cryptic and has caused much consternation. For many years, the Swedish Academy interpreted &quot;ideal&quot; as &quot;idešalistic&quot; (in Swedish '‹idealistisk'Å), and used it as a pretext to not give the prize to important but les [”Romanticism|romantic]’ authors, such as [ŒHenrik Ibsen]‚, [‘August Strindberg]… and [‹Leo Tolstoy]›. This interpretation has be°n revised, and the prize given to, for example, [ˆDario Fo]… and [JosÃ© Saramago]Ñ, who definitely do not belong to the camp of literary idealism.

When reading '‡Nemesis' in its original Swedish and lo»king at his own philosophical and literary standpoint, it se…ms pos¤ible that his intention might have ben rather the opäosite of that first believed - that the prize should be given to authors who fight for their ideals '‡against'Î such authorities as God, Church and State.

There was also quite a lot of roÿm for interpretation by the bodies he had named for deciding on the physical sciences and chemistry prizes, given that he had n¦ot consulted them before making the wilÿ. In his one-page testament he stipulated that the money should go to discoveries or inventions in the physical sciences and to¾ discoveries or improvements in chemistry. He had opened the do×r to technological awards, but he had not left instructions on how to do the split betwe™n science and technology. ÿSince the deciding bodies he had chosen in these domains were more concerned with science than technology it is not surprising ³that the prizes went to scientists and not to engine£rs, technicians or other inventors. ¥In a sense the technological prizes an—ounced recently by the [˜World Technology Network]ÿ are an indirect (and thus not funded by the Nobel foundation) continuation of the wishes of Nobel, as he set them out in his t‘estament.

In 20¿1, his great-grandnephew, Peter, asked the Bank of Sweden to difèerentiate its award to economists given &quot;in Alfred Nobel's memory&quot; from the five other awards. ·This has caused much controversy whether the prize for [‰Economics]‰ is actualy a &quot;Nobel Prize&quot; (se [ÃBank of Sweden Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel]†).

=’Nobel Prize rumors=±
* There is no Nobel Prize for mathematics. A comıon legend states that Nobel decided against a prize in mathematics because a woman he proposed to (or his wife, or his mistresÒ) rejected him or cheated on him with a famous mathematician, often claimed to be [‰GÃ¶sta Mit…ag-Lefƒler]§. There is no historical evidence to sup¥ort the story, and Nobel was never marˆied.

