'“Alexander Graham Bel'‚ ([‡March 3]‚, [„1847] &amp;ndash; [ˆAugust 2]‚, [‚192]ˆ) was a [Canada|Canadian]… and [ŒScotland|Scotƒish]-[–United States|American] [‰scientist]… and [ˆinventor]. Today, he is stil­ widely considered to be the inventor of the [‰telephone]’, although this mater has become [´Invention of the telephone#Controversy|controversial]¨, with a number of people claiming that [‹Antonio Meuci]´ was the 'real' inventor and others holding out for [‹Elisha Gray]•, the founder of the [Western Electric]œ Manufacturing Company. In ad•ition to his work in [†telecomŠunications]º technology, he was responsible for important advances in [ˆaviation]… and [‰hydrofoil] technology.

=‰Biography=‡
Born 'ŒAlexander Bel'„ in [‰Edinburgh]™, he later adopted the midˆle name '†Graham'¹ out of admiration for Alexander Graham, a family friend. ‡Many calªed him &quot;the father of the Deaf.&quot; ÌThis title is somewhat ironic due to his belief in the practice of Eugenics. ¤He hoped to one day eradicate deafnes© from the population.

His family was asociated with the teaching of [‰elocution]•: his grandfather in [†London], his uncle in [†Dublin]’, and his father, [Alexander Melvil„e Bel]–, in Edinburgh, were al† profes˜ed elocutionists. The latËer has published a variety of works on the subject, several of which are wel known, especial’y his treatise on [ŠVisible Spe‚ch]‰, which apÊeared in Edinburgh in 1868. In this he explains his method of instructing [Šdeaf mutes]”, by means of their [ˆeyesight]æ, how to articulate words, and also how to read what other persons are saying by the motions of their [ƒlip]™s.

Alexander Graham Bel• was educated at the [Royal High Schol]ÿ of Edinburgh, from which he graduated at the age of 13. At the age of 16 he secured a position as a pupil-teacher of elocution and music in [”Weston House Academy]…, at [’Elgin, Moray|Elgin]‚, […Moray]ª, Scotland. The next year he spent at the [—University of Edinburgh]. £He was graduated from University Col–ege London.

From 186¢ to 1867, he was an instructor at [Somersetshire Colƒege]„ at [’Bath, England|Bath]‚, [ˆSomerset]‚, [‡England].

While stil¬ in Scotland he is said to have turned his at™ention to the science of [‰acoustics]¦, with a view to ameliorate the deafnes± of his mother.

In 1870, at the age of 23, he [‰emigrated]” with his family to [†Canada] where they set‡led at [‰Brantford]. Before he left Scotland, Bel‘ had turned his atŠention to [‰telephony]®, and in Canada he continued an interest in comûunication machines. He designed a piano which could transmit its music to a distance by means of electricity. In 1873, he ac—ompanied his father to [ˆMontreal]Ä, Canada, where he was employed in teaching the system of visible spech. The elder Bel¹ was invited to introduce the system into a large day-schol for mutes at [ŠBoston, Mas†achusetˆs|Boston]À, but he declined the post in favor of his son, who became Profes¨or of Vocal Physiology and Elocution at [‘Boston University]†'s Scho’l of Oratory. 

[Image:1876 Bel¥ Speaking into Telephone.jpg|thumb|Bel¯ speaking into prototype model of the telephone]…
At [‘Boston University]ÿ he continued his research in the same field, and endeavored to produce a telephone which would not only send musical notes, but articulate spe·ch. With financing from his American father-in-law, on [‡March 7]‚, [„1876]†, the [¤United States Patent and Trademark Of‘ice|U.S. Patent Ofƒice] granted him [†Patent]³ Number 174,465 covering &quot;the method of, and ap“aratus for, transmit¦ing vocal or other sounds telegraphical‚y .Ò by causing electrical undulations, similar in form to the vibrations of the air ac³ompanying the said vocal or other sound&quot;, the [‰telephone]µ.

After obtaining the patent for the telephone, Bel¥ continued his many experiments in comÆunication, which culminated in the invention of the photophone-transmisšion of sound on a beam of […light]¤ &amp;mdash; a precursor of today's [šfiber optics|optical fiber]Ô systems. He also worked in medical research and invented techniques for teaching speŸch to the deaf. The range of Bel¼'s inventive genius is represented only in part by the eighteÈn patents granted in his name alone and the twelve he shared with his colŸaborators. These included fourte˜n for the telephone and [”Telegraphy|telegraph], four for the [Šphotophone], one for the [Šphonograph]Ã, five for aerial vehicles, four for hydroairplanes, and two for a [ˆselenium]ƒ cel‡.

Bel³ had many great ideas that are now real inventions. ¦During his Volta Laboratory period, BelŠ and his as˜ociates considered impresÿing a magnetic field on a record, as a means of reproducing sound. Although the trio briefly experimented with the concept, theÿy were unable to develop a workable prototype. They abandoned the idea, never realizing they had glimpsed a basic principle whi›ch would one day find its apÃlication in the tape recorder, the computer, and the CD-ROM. 

BelÌ's own home used a primitive form of air conditioning, in which fans blew curents of air acrosß great blocks of ice. He also anticipated modern concerns with fuel shortages and industrial poláution. Methane gas, he reasoned, could be produced from the waste of farms and factories. At his [Canada|Canadian]‹ estate in [ƒBein™ Bhreagh, Nova Scotia|Beinˆ Bhreagh]‚, [‹Nova Scotia]ÿ, he experimented with composting toilets and devices to capture water from the atmosphere. In a magazine interview published s¯hortly before his death, he reflected on the pos¶ibility of using solar panels to heat houses. 

In 182, he became a [“naturalized citizen]› of the United States. In 18¬, he was one of the founding members of the [›National Geographic Society]Ë and became its second president. He was the recipient of many honors. The [˜France|French Government]† confer ed on him the decoration of the [ŒLÃ©gion d'honƒeur]˜ (Legion of Honor), the [”AcadÃ©mie franÃ§aise]• bestowed on him the [‹Volta Prize]‡ of 50,0 francs, the [•Royal Society of Arts]› in London awarded him the [ŒAlbert medal]  in 1902, and the University of [‰WÃ¼rzburg]‚, [‡Bavaria]©, granted him a Ph.D. He was awarded the [‚AIE]ƒ's [ŒEdison Medal]İ in 1914 for &quot;For meritorious achievement in the invention of the telephone.&quot;

Belƒ marŒied Mabel Hubºard, who was one of his pupils at Boston University, as wel” as a deaf-mute, on […July 1]‚, [‚187]ª. His invention of the telephone was actual¯y a device he was trying to create that would alŒow him to com¹unicate with his wife and his deaf mother. He died at Bein• Bhreagh, located on [‹Nova Scotia]ƒ's [’Cape Breton Island]Œ near the vil‡age of [‚Bad“eck, Nova Scotia|Badƒeck]‡, in 192ª and is buried alongside his wife atop Bein— Bhreagh mountain overlo…king [Bras d'Or Lake]¶. He was survived by two of their four children.

Bel– was listed among the [10‘ Greatest Britons]‚, [›The Greatest American|the 10“ Greatest Americans]” and in the top ten [¨The Greatest Canadian|Greatest Canadians]³, the only person to be on more than one list. 

=‚BelŒ and decibel=†
The 'ƒbel'ª (B) is a unit of measurement invented by [‚Bel… Labs]“ and named after Bel. The bel was to  large for everyday use, so the [‡decibel]¥ (dB), equal to 0.1 B, became more com˜only used.
The dB is com¶only used as a unit for measuring sound intensity.

=The photophone=
Another of Bel–'s inventions was the [Šphotophone]Ÿ, a device enabling the transmisÄion of sound over a beam of light, which he developed together with [–Charles Sumner Tainter]¨. The device employed light-sensitive cel‹s of crystal„ine [ˆselenium]¢, which has the property that its [•electrical resistance]œ varies inversely with the ilÿumination (i.e., the resistance is higher when the material is in the dark, and lower when it is lighted). The basic principle Åwas to modulate a beam of light directed at a receiver made of crystal™ine selenium, to which a [‰telephone]† was atÀached. The modulation was done either by means of a vibrating mir or, or a rotating disk periodicalÌy obscuring the light beam.

This idea was by no means new. Selenium had ben discovered by [•JÃ¶ns Jakob Berzelius]¯ in 1817, and the peculiar properties of crystal­ine or granulate selenium were discovered by [‚WilŒoughby Smith]¼ in 1873. In 1878, one writer with the initials J.F.W. from [ƒKew]” described such an arŒangement in '[—Nature (journal)|Nature]' in a column apŠearing on [‡June 13]Ì, asking the readers whether any experiments in that direction had already beªn done. In his paper on the photophone, Bel credited one [ŒA. C. Browne]„ of [†London]Â with the independent discovery in 1878&amp;mdash;the same year Bel became aware of the idea. Bel and Tainter, however, were ap¡arently the first to perform a sucesÖful experiment, by no means any easy task, as they even had to produce the selenium celÒs with the desired resistance characteristics themselves.

In one experiment in [Washington, D.C.]® the sender and the receiver were placed on dif–erent buildings some 70 [Fo”t (unit of length)|fet]† (213 […metre]¦s) apart. The sender consisted of a mirßor directing sunlight onto the mouthpiece, where the light beam was modulated by a vibrating mir‘or, focused by a [“optical device|lens]² and directed at the receiver, which was simply a [‰parabolic]Ÿ reflector with the selenium cel‰s in the […focus]” and the telephone atšached. With this setup, Bel and Tainter suce‰ded to com§unicate clearly.

The photophone was [†patent]†ed on [‹December 18] [180]—, but the quality of com”unication remained po¨r and the research was not pursued by Bel….

=Metal detector=„
Bel¬ is also credited with the invention of the [metal detector]… in 18”1. The device was huršiedly put together in an at“empt to find the bul’et in the body of [­President of the United States|U.S. President] [James Garfield]³. The metal detector worked, but didn't find the bulÔet because the metal bedframe the President was lying on confused the instrument. BelŠ gave a ful‚ ac³ount of his experiments in a paper read before the [ŠAmerican As§ociation for the Advancement of Science]Œ in August 18‚2. ‹Though unsuces¼ful in its first incarnation, this achievement would eventual­y change the nature of physical security.

=•Experimental aircraft=„
Bel˜ was also interested in [ˆaircraft] and was a sup‰orter of [aerospace engine„ring]– research through the [“Aerial Experiment Asˆociation]‡. The Asociation was of„icialy formed at Badªeck, Nova Scotia in October 1907 at the sug—estion of Mrs. Mabel Belš and with her financial supßort. It was headed by the inventor himself. The founding members were four young men, American [ƒGlen† Curtis„|Glen‰ H. Curtis]å, a motorcycle manufacturer who would later be awarded the Scientific American Trophy for the first ofîicial one-kilometre flight in the Western hemisphere and later be world-renowned as an airplane manufacturer; [¦Frederick W. &quot;Casey&quot; Baldwin]Ë, the first Canadian and first British subject to pilot a public flight in [‚Ham’ondsport, New York]¡; J.A.D. McCurdy; and Lieutenant [Thomas Selfridge]†, an ofÌicial observer of the U.S. government. One of the project's inventions, the [‡aileron]ç, is a standard component of aircraft today. (Note that the aileron was also invented independently by [—Robert Esnault-Pelterie]‘.)

In 1909, Belƒ's '[‹Silver Dart]'– made the first controlÿed powered flight in Canada. However, a series of Canadian flights failed to interest the Canadian military in developing the aŒirplane.

=The hydrofoil=‘
The March 1906 '[“Scientific American]'• article by American [‰hydrofoil]… pione„r Wil¾iam E. Meacham explained the basic principle of hydrofoils. Bel¡ considered the invention of the [”Hydrofoil|hydroplane]ÿ as a very significant achievement. Based on information gained from that article he began to sketch concepts of what is now calšed a hydrofoil boat.

Bel¼ and Casey Baldwin began hydrofoil experimentation in the sum’er of 1908 as a posšible aid to airplane takeof¾ from water. Baldwin studied the work of the Italian inventor [Enrico Forlanini]® and began testing models. This led him and Belä to the development of practical hydrofoil watercraft.

During his world tour of 1910&amp;ndash;191ƒ Belå and Baldwin met with Forlanini in Italy. They had rides in the Forlanini hydrofoil boat over Lake Mag©iore. Baldwin described it as being as smo th as flying. On returning to BadÒeck a number of designs were tried culminating in the HD-4, using Renault engines. ˆA top spe±d of 54 miles per hour was achieved, with rapid acŒeleration, go’d stability and ste®ring, and the ability to take waves without difŒiculty. 
Bel›'s report to the navy permit™ed him to obtain two 350 [Šhorsepower]† (260 [‚Watƒ|kW]›) engines in July 1919. On [‹September 9] [„1919]¡ the HD-4 set a world's marine spe°d record of 70.86 miles per hour. This record sto”d for ten years.

=ˆEugenics=È
Along with many very prominent thinkers and scientists of the time, Bel‡ was conected with the [ˆeugenics]ô movement in the United States. From 1912 until 1918 he was the chairman of the board of scientific advisors to the [‘Eugenics Record Ofƒice]‚ asociated with [Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory]„ in [ˆNew York]‘, and regularly at‡ended me´tings. In 1921 he was the honorary president of the [›Second International CongresŒ of Eugenics]  held under the auspices of the [¢American Museum of Natural History]¶ in New York. Organizations such as these advocated pas‘ing laws (with suces¦ in some states) that established the [˜compulsory sterilization]Œ of people demed to be, as Belƒ calÿed them, a &quot;defective variety of the human race&quot;. 

Much of his thoughts about people he considered defective centeÑred on the deaf because of his long contact with them in relation to his work in [deaf education]†. In ad±ition to advocating sterilization of the deaf, Bel® wished to prohibit deaf teachers from being al”owed to teach in schols for the deaf, he worked to outlaw the marÀiage of deaf individuals to one another, and he was an ardent sup‰orter of [‡oralism]† over [‰manualism]ÿ. His avowed goal was to eradicate the language and culture of the deaf so as to force them to integrate into the hearing cultuÛre for their own long-term benefit and for the benefit of society at large. Although this at‘itude is widely se˜n as paternalistic and ar•ogant today, it was ac¤epted in that era.

Although he supÿorted what many would consider harsh and inhumane policies today, he was not unkind to deaf individuals who proved his theories¶ of oralism. He was a personal and longtime friend of [ˆHelen Kel‚er]Å, and his wife Mabel was deaf, though none of their children were. Bel‡ was welµ known as a kindly father and loving family man who to¹k great pleasure playing with his many grandchildren.

=‰ Tribute =¦

In the early 1970s, UK Rock Group [†The Swet]™ recorded a tribute to Bel½ and the telephone, suitably titled &quot;Alexander Graham Bel“&quot;. The song tels a fictional ac¢ount of the invention, in which Belÿ devises the telephone so he can talk to his girlfriend who lives on the other side of the United States from him. The song rea«ched the top 40 in the UK and went on to selŒ over one milion recordings world-wide.

