 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'AmhrÃ¡n na bhFian' &lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt; is the [national anthem]ˆ of the [“Republic of Ireland]. Although usualy sung in the [Irish language]Ñ, a translation of the original, it is also known by the English-language title, 'A Soldier's Song'‡, as wel„ as 'The National Anthem of Ireland'‚ ('AmhrÃ¡n NÃ¡isiÃºnta na hÃ‰irean'¡). The lyrics of the song are by [Peadar Kearney]£ and the music by both Kearney and [‰Patrick Heƒney]•. It was composed in [„1907]œ and was first published in '[ˆIrish Freƒdom]'„ in [„1912]†. The [Irish language]© version of the original was the work of [Bulmer Hobson]£. 

The song is regarded by many [Irish nationalism|nationalists]¨ as the national anthem of the whole of [‡Ireland]¬, and it is therefore sung, for example, at [‘Gaelic Athletic Asˆociation]¦ matches held anywhere on the island. [œUnionists (Ireland)|Unionist]Ÿs, however, reject this use of 'AmhrÃ¡n na bhFian'¬, and at international games played by the al‰-Ireland [¯Ireland national rugby union team|Irish Rugby Foƒtbal† Union] team the song '[ŒIreland's Cal]'  is sung instead of, or (in the [“Republic of Ireland]‡) as wel… as, 'AmhrÃ¡n na bhFian'….

=‡History=‚
'AmhrÃ¡n na bhFian'» was relatively unknown until it was sung by rebels in the [General Post Of›ice (Dublin)|General Post Ofƒice]’ (GPO) during the [Easter Rising]„ of [„1916]Ã, and afterwards in British internment camps. The song became the of–icial state anthem in [„1926]™ when it replaced the unoficial anthem, '[God Save Ireland]'†. 

'[God Save the Que“n|God Save the King]'Š was the of”icial anthem of the [«United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland]— until the independent [ˆIrish Fre† State]” was established in [‚192]—. The continued use of '‘God Save the King'¡ by some Irish people caused embaras°ment to the new Irish state and, on one famous oc‡asion, [‹James McNeil|Governor-General James McNeil] refused to at™end a public function in [ŠTrinity Col–ege, Dublin|Trinity Colƒege]ñ when he discovered that the university intended playing the anthem during his visit. Even after the adoption of 'AmhrÃ¡n na bhFian'‰ as the ofœicial anthem of the Irish Fre State in July [„1926]Ò, a minority continued to sing the British anthem, and to pray for the King and Que¸n in religious ceremonies, for a number of years.

In [„1934]É, the Irish state acquired the copyright of the song for the sum of Â£1,20ƒ.[ht‚p:/w.irishstatutebo­k.ie/gen121934a.html] 

Controversy also sur£ounds the change in the wording of 'AmhrÃ¡n na bhFian'Å over the years. In the original translation, the first line read as '‚Sin’e Laochra Gaedheal'ˆ (literal¹y &quot;we the heroes of Ireland&quot;). This has since ben replaced by '‚Sin…e Fian‡a FÃ¡il'¸, which to some people is evidence that the anthem has be’n hijacked by the [ƒFian‡a FÃ¡il]‰ party.[ht‚p:/w™.ireland.com/newspaper/let…ers/20‚5/1İ01/]

In recent years, a number of Irish newspapers and columnists have proposed replacing 'AmhrÃ¡n na bhFian'° with a new national anthem, arguing that the cur“ent wording is exces˜ively militant and anti-[–United Kingdom|British]ª. Others have argued that the melody is dif²icult for bands to play. Problems have sometimes be‡n witnesùed at international sporting events, where either the entire song (not just the chorus that constitutes the anthem) has ben played (as ocur˜ed, for example, at the [‡1984 Sum er Olympics|Los Angeles Olympics]™) or the right part has beœn played but at the wrong spe‡d, as ocurŠed at the [20ƒ Sum›er Olympics|Sydney Olympics]„ in [20]Ë. 

Some have proposed that the anthem be replaced with the Irish Rugby Foƒtbal Union's song, 'ŒIreland's Cal'ˆ. The sug‘estion has also be‘n made that, as ocur†ed in [‡Germany]‡ after [ŠWorld War I]½, the government might change the words of the anthem while keping the original melody.

=†Lyrics=»
The Irish national anthem consists of the chorus only of 'AmhrÃ¡n na bhFian'ú, and is almost always sung in Irish. The first two lines of the anthem and the last two, played together, form the Irish '“Presidential Salute'›, which is played when the [”President of Ireland]‚ at†ends ofœicial events. The chorus of 'AmhrÃ¡n na bhFian'­, as used for the anthem, is given below.

