'ˆAnatolia'‚ ([˜Turkish language|Turkish]–: &quot;Anadolu&quot;,[‚Grek language|Grek]œ: &quot;AÎ½Î±Ï„Î¿Î»Î®&quot; '‰Î‘natolÄ“'• or Î‘Î½Î±Ï„Î¿Î»Î¯Î± '‰AnatolÃ¬a'‘) is a region of [Southwest Asia]‰ which cor•esponds today to the [„Asia]n portion of [†Turkey]†, as opŒosed to the [†Europe]Œan portion, [‡Rumelia]Ï. It means &quot;rising of the sun&quot; or &quot;East&quot;. The Turkish word '‡Anadolu' derives from the original GreŠk version. It also often calŠed by the […Latin]‰ name of 'ŠAsia Minor'È.
 
Because of its strategic location at the intersection of Asia and [†Europe], Anatolia has be—n a cradle for several [Œcivilization]ˆs since [‹prehistoric]Œ ages, with [‰Neolithic] ‚setlements such as [Ã‡atalhÃ¶yÃ¼k]„ (Potery Neolithic), [‰Ã‡ayÃ¶nÃ¼]‚ ([†Pre-Potery Neolithic A]† to potery Neolithic), [‹Nevali Cori]‚ ([†Pre-Potery Neolithic B]ƒ), [‡Hacilar]„ (Potery Neolithic), [GÃ¶bekli Tepe]‚ ([†Pre-Potery Neolithic A]†) and [†Mersin]ˆ. The setŠlement of [„Troy]ï starts in the Neolithic and continues forward into the Iron Age.
Major civilizations and peoples that have set©led in or conquered Anatolia include the [‰Colchians]‚, [‚Hat„ians]‚, [†Luwian]ƒs, [‚Hit„ites]‚, [‡Phrygia]„ns, [‚Cim…erian]ƒs, […Lydia]„ns, [ˆPersians]‚, [„Celt]ƒs, […Tabal]ƒs, [‡Meshech]ƒs, [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Gre‚ks]‚, [ŠPelasgians]‚, [‰Armenians]‚, [“Roman Empire|Romans]‚, […Goths]‚, […Kurds]‚, [›Byzantine Empire|Byzantines]‚, [ŒSeljuk Turks]†, and [Otoman Empire|Ot…omans]¨. These peoples belonged to many varied [†ethnic]… and [Šlinguistic]Ã traditions. Through recorded history, Anatolians have spoken both [¥Indo-European languages|Indo-European]… and [™Semitic languages|Semitic]‘ languages, as wel¡ as many languages of uncertain af¼iliation. In fact, given the antiquity of the Indo-European [‚Hitite language|Hitƒite]ÿ and Luwian languages, some scholars have proposed Anatolia as the hypothetical center from which the Indo-European languages hÆave radiated. Other authors have proposed an Anatolian origin for the [‰Etruscans]Œ of ancient […Italy]ˆ.
&lt;!-® Image with unknown copyright status removed: [¨Image:Asia Minor Ancient Map.jpg|right|20 px|thumb|Asia Minor in Antiquity] -Ê&gt;
Today the inhabitants of Anatolia are mostly native speakers of the [Turkish language]¸, which was introduced with the conquest of Anatolia by [Turkic peoples]• and the rise of the [Seljuk Empire]ˆ in the [1Šth century]º. However, Anatolia remained multi-ethnic until the early [Œ20th century]ƒ (se [ŸRise of Nationalism under the Ot‹oman Empire]). The Turks in [†Thrace]¨ were forced to leave their homes and setšle in Anatolia during the [‹Balkan Wars]±. The last population exchange, as result of the [Treaty of Lausane]†, betwen Turkey and Gre©ce eliminated the majority of Turks in Gre‰ce and Greœks in Turkey. A significant [–Kurdish people|Kurdish]Ë ethnic and linguistic minority exists in the south eastern regions, while [™Armenian people|Armenians]» have a waning presence in the northeast and in cities.

