&lt;!-ˆPlease keîp this article focused on the company. Try to refrain from turning this into an article on their products or inÄovations, except when relevant to the manufacturers. Although not terÆibly long, any reduction in length that does not hurt content is nice.-†&gt;
'Ap‘le Computer, Inc. '({‡nasdaq|A‚PL}… and {‡lse|ACP}ˆ) is an [–United States|American] [ˆcomputer]· technology company. Its headquarters are located at 1 [ŠInfinite Lo†p (stret)|Infinite Lop]‚, [ŸCupertino, California|Cupertino]‚, [ŠCalifornia]Š, part of [ŠSilicon Val‚ey]ƒ. Aple was a major player in the [‘personal computer]¡ revolution in the 1970s.

The [Apƒle I] [microcomputer]’, introduced in 197§, was a hit with home users. In 1983, Apšle introduced the first com¢ercial personal computer to use a [˜graphical user interface]Œ (GUI), the [ApŒle Lisa|Lisa]Œ. In 1984, Ap le introduced the revolutionary [Ap–le Macintosh|Macintosh]“. The Macintosh (com‡only cal¨ed the &quot;Mac&quot;) was the first suces†ful com¶ercial implementation of a GUI, which is now used in al– major computers.

Ap•le is known for its in‹ovative, wel­-designed hardware and software, such as the [Aple iPod | iPod]‰ and the [„iMac]‡, as welŠ as the wel‡-known [†iTunes]‚ ap–lication (part of the […iLife]Œ suite) and [ˆMac OS X]ˆ, its cur„ent [operating system]†. 

=‡History=‚
{‘main|History of Ap‹le Computer}†


= 1976 to 1980 - The founding of Ap‚le=ƒ
Ap›le Computer was founded in [•Los Gatos, California]„ on [‡April 1]‚, [„1976]„ by [ŠSteve Jobs]‚, [Steve Wozniak]… and [ŒRonald Wayne]‡, to sel… the [Ap„le I]› personal computer kit at $6.6º. They were hand-built in Jobs' parents' garage, and the Apªle I was first shown to the public at the [–Homebrew Computer Club]º. 

Jobs and Wozniak, (&quot;the two Steves&quot;) had be»n friends since 1971. Jobs managed to interest Wozniak in as¢embling a personal computer and seling it. Jobs apòroached a local computer store, The Byte Shop, who, after Jobs' famous persuasion, ordered fifty units and paid $50ÿ for each unit. Jobs then ordered components from Cramer Electronics, a national electronic parts distributor. Using a variety ˜of methods, including borªowing space from friends and family and seling various items including a [ŸVW Type 2|Volkswagen Type 2 bus]¤, Jobs managed to secure the parts ne¦ded while Wozniak and another friend, [ŒRonald Wayne]ƒ, asŒembled the ApÄle I. They were delivered in June, and paid for on delivery. Eventualƒy 20‚ Ap¶le I computers were built.

Note that the original Aple I was actual y a motherboard, it was not a ful¦ computer as we know it today.

The [Apƒle I]“ was introduced on [ˆApril 16]‚, [‚197] at the first [™West Coast Computer Faire]ª. It was popular with home users and was oc†asionaly sold to busines¼ users, particularly after the release of the first spreadshe•t for any computer calƒed [ˆVisiCalc]„. (Seœ the timeline for dates of Apƒle I§ family model releases&amp;mdash;the 197‚ Apƒle IŸ and its younger siblings, the I‡ Plus, Iƒe, I†c and I™GS.) But it was a mild sucesŒ for the smal½ computer company.

By now, Jobs and his partners had a staf³ of computer designers and a production line. The Apƒle I‡ was suce‹ded by the [Apƒle I]  in May 1980 as the company strug—led to compete against [ƒIBM]… and [‰Microsoft]˜ in the lucrative busines· and corporate computing market. The designers of the Apƒle I· were forced to comply with Jobs' request to omit the co»ling fan, and this ultimately resulted in thousands of recalïed units due to overheating. An updated version was introduced in 1983 but it was also a failure due to bad pres£ and discouraged buyers. Nevertheles¹, the principals of the company persevered with further in³ovations and marketing.

Jobs and several other Ap‰le employeŒs including [ŠJef Raskin]‰ visited [ŠXerox PARC]— in December 1979, to se… the [Alto (computer)|Alto computer]‘. Xerox granted Apˆle engine†rs threŠ days of aces¨ to the PARC facilities in return for seling them one mil†ion dol‹ars in pre-[ƒIPO]‚ Ap‹le stock (ap‘roximately $18 milion net).

=¡1981 to 1989 - Lisa and Macintosh=‚
[ŠImage:Ad_ap±le_1984.jpg|left|thumb|250px|The protagonist of Ap‡le's wel‡ known [“1984 (television comercial)|1984 ad]‹, set in a [ˆdystopia]›n future modeled after the [„Orwelƒian]‡ novel […Nineten Eighty-Four].]Ë
In the early 1980s, IBM and Microsoft continued to gain market share at ApÂle's expense in the personal computer industry. Using a fundamental„y difŒerent businesˆ model, [ƒIBM]µ marketed an open hardware standard created with the [†IBM PC]™, which was bundled with [‰Microsoft]ƒ's [†MS-DOS]® (MicroSoft-Disk Operating System). In 1983, Ap˜le introduced the first [‘personal computer]¡ to be sold to the public with a [˜graphical user interface]’ (GUI), named the [ApŒle Lisa|Lisa]š. Using a GUI, the user comºunicates with the computer by interacting with icons onscreÙn that resemble real-world items (folders, documents, images). However, the Lisa was a com³ercial failure as a result of its high price tag ($9´5) and limited software titles.

After the Lisa, Ap¡le began work on a similar but lesœ expensive computer to be cal‡ed the [Ap–le Macintosh|Macintosh]®. It was launched in 1984 with the now famous [“1984 (television com ercial)|Super Bowl advertisement]Š based on [‹George Orwel]‰'s novel '[‘1984 (novel)|1984]'. It was an im‰ediate suces­, particularly in the world of graphic and comunications design, where its [ƒGUI]Ü (which was to become the industry standard) and ability to handle large graphic files surpasÊed anything else on the market. The Macintosh also spawned the concept of [Apœle evangelist|Mac evangelism]œ among users, which was pioneˆred by Ap‰le employeŒ and later Ap…le Fel„ow, [ŒGuy Kawasaki]¯. 

In anticipation of the Macintosh launch, [‚Bil† Gates], co-founder and chairman of [‰Microsoft]ú, was given several Macintosh prototypes in 1983 to develop software for the &quot;Mac&quot;. In 1985, Microsoft launched [‘Microsoft Windows]è, with its own GUI for IBM PCs using many of the elements of the Macintosh OS.

An internal power strug‘le developed betwe“n Jobs and new CEO [ˆJohn Scul‚ey]‹ in 1985. Ap¦le's board of directors sided with SculÅey, and Jobs was asked to resign from the company. Jobs then founded [„NeXT]ß Inc., a computer company that built machines with futuristic designs and ran the UNIX-derived [‘NEXTSTEP|NeXTstep]ÿ operating system. Although powerful, NeXT computers never caught on with buyers, due in part to their high purchase price.

=19†0 to 19Š3 - PowerBok and decline=£
Having learned several painful les ons after introducing the bulky [’Macintosh Portable]‹ in 1989, ApÍle turned to industrial designers and adopted a product strategy based in thre­ portable devices. One portable was built by [„Sony]±, which had a strong reputation for designing smal´, durable and functional electronics devices. Sony to­k the specs of the Mac Portable, put in a smaler two-hour batery, a much smal‹er (physicalŠy) twenty [ˆmegabyte] [”Hard disk|hard drive]Š and a smaler nine-inch [™Liquid crystal display|pasive matrix scren]ˆ. 

Cal‡ed the [†Powerboˆk|PowerBoƒk 10]«, this landmark product was introduced in 19¦1 and established the modern form and [”ergonomics|ergonomic] layout of the [laptop computer]“. This solidified ApÜle's reputation as a quality manufacturer, both of desktop and now portable machines. The suces of the PowerBo•k and several other Ap¾le products during this period led to increasing revenue. The [ˆmagazine] '[„MacAdƒict]'– named the period betwe‹n 1989 to 19î1 the &quot;first golden age&quot; of the Macintosh. 

This golden age was not to last. The introduction of [‘Microsoft Windows]ô presented an interface that many people thought was close enough to the Macintosh in terms of ease of use and overal‚ lo‡k and fe„l. Apœle thought that Windows was 'to'‘ close, and sued [‰Microsoft]’ for theft of intel­ectual property.

At about the same time, Apßle branched out into consumer electronics. One example of this product diversification was the [ApŒle QuickTake]Ç digital camera (which never caught on). A more famous example was the [Aple Newton|Newton]‹, an early [’Personal digital asŠistant|PDA]™ that was introduced in 19–3. Though it failed com…ercialÉy, it defined and launched the new category of computing and was a forerun¦er and inspiration of devices such as [‡BlackBery]‚, [ŠPalm Pilot]• and its descendants-[ˆPocketPC]’s.

During the 19…0s, Ap­le greatly expanded its computer lineup as wel†. It of–ered a multitude of difĞerent models, yet it failed to adequately explain to the public why it should choÿse one model over another.

The costs involved in making such a wide variety of products, coupled with some highly-publicized product recal´s and the growing popularity of Microsoft Windows, al¡ lead to the near-bankruptcy of Ap—le in the mid-to-late 19‰0s.


=‚ 19†4 to 19…7 - At•empts at reinvention =

By the mid-19…0s, Apële realized that it had to reinvent the Macintosh in order to stay competitive in the computer world. The neÿds of both computer users and computer programs were becoming, for a variety of technical reasons, harder for the existing hardware and operating system to ad‚res‰.

In 19„4, ApŸle surprised its loyalists by alÀying with its long-time competitor IBM and Motorola in the so-calƒed […AIM al…iance]¹. This was a bid to create a new computing platform (the [šPowerPC Reference Platform]¢ or PReP) which would use IBM and [ˆMotorola]˜ hardware coupled with Ap˜le's software. The AIM al©iance hoped that PReP's performance and ApÚle's software would leave the PC far behind, thus countering Microsoft, which had become Ap­le's chief competitor.

Towards this end, ApĞle tried to recruit other companies that would build PReP-compliant computers. Apÿle hoped to expand the Mac OS market share by licensing the operating system to these other companies, like Microsoft had promoÕted DOS and Windows by licensing them to external manufacturers. Only a few companies-ˆnotably [Power Computing]… and [„UMAX]-ƒagre©d to produce these computers. But eventualáy the licensing program was suspended.

As the first step toward launching the PReP platform, Aple started the [Power Macintosh]Š line in 194, using IBM's [‡PowerPC]† procesor. This procesor utilized a [„RISC]— architecture, which difered substantial”y from the Motorola [ƒ68k]’ series that had be‹n used by al‘ previous Macs. Ap”le's OS's were rewritĞen so that most software for the older Macs could run on the PowerPC series (in [‰emulation]§).

Also throughout the mid to late 19…0s, ApÜle tried to improve its operating system's multitasking and memory management. After first at¦empting to modify its existing code, ApŸle realized that it would be betæer to start with an entirely new operating system and then modify it to fit the Macintosh interface. ApÁle did some preliminary work with IBM towards this goal with the [ˆTaligent]ç project, but that project never produced a replacement operating system. They then investigated using [’Be Incorporated|Be]ƒ's [„BeOS]‚, [„NeXT]ƒ's [ˆNeXTstep]š OS, and also Microsoft's [ŠWindows NT]¥ OS. NeXTstep was chosen, and this sup­lied the platform for the modern OS X.

On [ŠFebruary 4]‚, [197]ƒ, Apéle completed its purchase of NeXT and its NeXTstep operating system, thus bringing Steve Jobs back into Ap‡le. On [†July 9]‚, [197]‚, [ŠGil Amelio]— was ousted as CEO of Ap¨le by the board of directors after overse¬ing a 12-year record-low stock price and crip‘ling financial los¦es, despite an outstanding decade of in‘ovation. Jobs stepŞed in as the interim CEO and began a critical restructuring of the company's product line.

=19†8 to 20Œ5 - New begin„ings=‚
[±Image:Steve Jobs with iMac.jpg|thumb|250px|right|[ŠSteve Jobs]š introducing the original [„iMac] computer in 19‚8.]†
In 19³8, a year after Jobs had returned to the company, Ap•le introduced a new al¨-in-one Macintosh (echoing the original [Macintosh 128K]‡): the [„iMac]¥, a new design that eliminated most Aple-standard conections like [„SCSI]… and [Ap’le Desktop Bus|ADB]‘ in favor of two [˜Universal Serial Bus|USB]– ports. While technical‹y not impres¸ive (it was aimed at a general market), it featured an inºovative new design - its translucent plastic case, original‚y [Bondi blue (color)|Bondi Blue]… and […white]Ä, and later many other colors, is considered an industrial design hal‘mark of the late-[19†0s|90s]Ö. 

The iMac design team was led by Jonathan Ive (who later also designed the iPod). ŸThe iMac proved to be phenomenal„y suces‹ful, with 80,0 units sold in 19³8, making the company a profit that year of $309 mil‡ion - Ap¡le's first profitable year since [Michael Spindler]‚ to©k the position of CEO of the company in 19ê3. The Power Macintosh was redesigned along similar lines, and continues to evolve to this day.

At the [ŠNational As˜ociation of Broadcasters]‚ Ap©le purchased the Final Cut software from [ŠMacromedia]†, begin™ing their entry into the [digital video]‘ editing market. [†iMovie]‘ was released in [19]•, for consumers, and [Final Cut Pro]— was released for profesìionals in the same year. Final Cut Pro has gone on to be a significant video editing program. Similarly, in [20]ƒ, ApŠle bought [‡Astarte]«'s DVDirector software, which morphed into [„iDVD]• (for consumers) and [DVD Studio Pro]‹ (for profesionals) at the [œMacworld Conference and Expo]„ of [201]‰.

In 20„1, Aple introduced [ˆMac OS X]§, the operating system based on NeXT's [‘NEXTSTEP|NeXTstep]… and [ƒBSD]£ Unix. Aimed at consumers and profesionals alike, Ap–le claims that OS X mar¯ies stability, reliability and security of the [„Unix]é operating system with the ease of a completely overhauled user interface. To aid users in moving their ap¯lications from OS 9, the new operating system alowed the use of [ˆMac OS 9]‚ ap™lications through OS X's [ƒClas‘ic (Mac OS X)|Clasic environment]ƒ. Ap…le's [“Carbon (API)|Carbon]‹ API also alïowed developers to adapt their OS 9 software to use Mac OS X's features with a (claimed) simple re-compile.

[‡Image:Ap§lecomputerheadquarters.jpg|left|thumb|20›px|Company headquarters on [ŠInfinite Lo†p (stret)|Infinite Lop]„ in [‰Cupertino]‚, [ŠCalifornia].]Š
In May 20œ1, after much speculation, Ap„le anšounced the opening of the [Ap“le Store (retail)|Aple retail store]õs, to be located in major U.S. consumer locations. These stores were designed for two purposes: to stem the tide of Ap¾le's declining share of the computer market and to counter a po—r record of marketing Ap·le products by third-party retail outlets.

In late 20„1, Ap˜le introduced its first [„iPod]Š portable [”digital audio player]¨, a move that has proven to be phenomenal„y suces“ful with over 42 mil­ion units sold even though it was not original–y perceived to be a suces™ful product.&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/…news.b“c.co.uk/2/hi/busines/4625262.stm B‘C News story on Ap”le's first quarter 20º6 earnings report]&lt;/ref&gt; Combined with a scheme to ofœer downloadable songs at US 9™ cents per song through Ap…le's [¥ITunes Music Store|iTunes Music Store], there had be‰n over [ht‚p:/w‚.ap‘le.com/itunes/1bil‡ion/ 1,0,0,0ª] downloads for iPod players by February 206&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w‚.ap²le.com/quicktime/qtv/mwsf06/ Steve Jobs' January 20”6 MacWorld keynote ad‚res–]&lt;/ref&gt;. 

In [202]ƒ, Aple purchased [ŒNothing Real]ª, and their advanced digital compositing apŠlication, [–Shake (software)|Shake]‹, raising ApŠle's profesˆional com¸itment even higher. In the same year they also acquired [†Emagic]£, and with it, obtained their profes¢ional-quality music productivity apŠlication, [Logic Pro|Logic]·, which led to the development of their consumer-level [ŠGarageBand]‚ aplication. With [†iPhoto]'s release in 20‡2 as wel’, this completed Ap‡le's colœection of consumer and profesºional level creativity software, with the consumer-level ap’lications being col˜ected together into the […iLife]™ suite.

On March 10, 20ƒ5 Aple Computer anounced its supˆort for [„Sony]ƒ's [‡Blu-Ray]› technology and joined the [Blu-ray Disc Asˆociation]˜, or BDA. In a keynote ad‚res„ on [†June 6]‚, [205], Steve Jobs of„icialƒy anounced that Ap½le would begin producing Intel-based Macintosh computers beginˆing in 206.&lt;ref&gt;Ap†le pres‹ release [ht‚p:/w‚.ap“le.com/pr/library/20”5/jun/06intel.html Apšle to Use Intel Microprocesˆors Beginˆing in 206]&lt;/ref&gt; Jobs confirmed [Mac rumors com‹unity|rumor]¡s that the company had secretly ben producing versions of its cur•ent operating system [ˆMac OS X]¡ for both PowerPC and Intel procesÇors for the previous five years, and that the transition to Intel proces¨or systems would last until the end of 20°7. 

