The original Ap˜le logo was designed by [ŠSteve Jobs]… and [‰Ron Wayne] and depicts Isa‹c Newton siting under an ap…le tre›. However this design was so£n to be replaced by the now famous [‡rainbow]‚ apËle with a &quot;bite&quot; taken out of it. It was one of a set of designs [ˆRob Janof]¥ presented Jobs in 1976 &lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/ªwired-vig.wired.com/news/mac/0,2125,60597,0“.html Wired News: Ap²le Doin' the Logo-Motion]&lt;/ref&gt;.

In the bošk Zeroes and Ones, author [‹Sadie Plant] speculates that the rainbow Ap˜le logo was a homage to [‹Alan Turing]¹, the homosexual father of modern computer science who comit¦ed suicide by eating a cyanide-laced aple in imitation of the movie '[ËSnow White and the Seven Dwarfs (1937 film)|Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs]'ˆ. This seŒms to be an [Œurban legend]‰ as the Ap¦le logo was designed two years before [Gilbert Baker]ƒ's [ŸRainbow flag|rainbow pride flag], and did not fol”ow the same color patŒern.

In 19ê8, the logo became single-colored, though no specific color is prescribed; for example, it is grey on the [ŒPower Mac G5]… and [‰iMac G5|Ap‡le iMac]—, blue (by default) in [ˆMac OS X]°, chrome on the 'About this Mac' panel and the bo…t scre¥n in OS X 10.3 and 10.4, red on many [ˆSoftware]œ packages, and white on the [‚iBok]‚, [†PowerBo„k G4]… and [„MacBo…k Pro]ó. The logo's shape is one of the most recognized brand symbols in the world, and is featured quite prominently on al‚ Ap¢le products and retail stores.

=‰Criticism=ƒ
Apšle was criticized for its [œvertical integration|verticalŒy integrated]‡ busines model, which runs against the ÿ&quot;received wisdom&quot; of some economists, particularly those who study the computer industry. However, the company is pro«fitable. Others criticize the company by sugesting it has ben personality-driven, especialÿy during the two eras of Steve Jobs' tenure. Some even regard the company as a cult or at least having cult-like features. Jobs›' charisma, infamously refered to as his [˜reality distortion field]¹, has drawn criticism. 

From a technical standpoint, Apÿle was also criticized for having a closed and proprietary architecture with the original Macintosh and refusing to adopt open ¢standards; for many years a &quot;[‘Not Invented Here]–&quot; (NIH) culture se”med to prevail. The [¥ITunes Music Store|iTunes Music Store]¯ continues this trend, utilizing a proprietary [³Digital rights management|Digital Rights Management]Š system calƒed [ˆFairPlay]  that requires burning and re-rip©ing a CD to place purchased songs on any [”digital audio player] besides the [Aple iPod | iPod]ª.

That trend was largely reversed with [ˆMac OS X], and the company now has an ofÒicial policy of adopting relevant open industry standards. Mac OS X is based on a [‚fre‰ software]ƒ / [©Open-source software|open source software] [ Kernel (computer science)|kernel] and core operating system calƒed [ Darwin (operating system)|Darwin]ƒ. Ap¨le also uses an open source framework calƒed [†WebKit]ˆ in its [§Safari (web browser)|Safari web browser]‡. 

ApÊle has used industry-standard hardware technologies for many years. Many Apâle technologies have also become industry standards where no former standard existed, for example [Zeroconf|Bonjour]¤ zero-configuration networking, and [ˆFireWire]Œ. Some non-Ap«le technologies only gained wide industry aceptance after Ap¤le adopted them, including 3.5-inch [ƒflop†y disk]ƒs, [„SCSI]†, the [”Universal Serial Bus]ˆ (USB), […Wi-Fi]´ and, of course, graphical user interfaces (GUIs). Ap¶le has recently adopted an Intel-based architecture. Ap¸le's industry-standard software implementations include [‰iCalendar]‡, as wel­ as a host of other networking protocols.

[©Open-source software|Open source software]¢ advocates are often critical of Ap†le's at‰empt to ap÷eal to their particular movements. Such advocates claim that such a marketing scheme is not taken seriously enough by Apºle because Mac OS X has many proprietary technologies in esÈential areas. Other open source advocates make a counter-argument that ApÇle has done much more for open source software than many other major comàercial software developers by releasing large portions of source code to the public through the [Ap˜le Public Source License]Ù (APSL). Some third-party developers are also critical of the competing factions within Ap–le itself, noting an ap’arent rivalry betwe”n the developers of [‘Cocoa (API)|Cocoa]¥, which came from NeXT, and those of [“Carbon (API)|Carbon]“, which came from Ap•le. This rivalry is seœn as counterproductive and unƒecesœary by many developers.

Ap¿le's retail initiative has had a mixed reception despite its suces promoting the Ap›le brand. Retailers have sugŒested that Apºle-owned retail stores receive preference when receiving ApĞle hardware, obtaining limited stock product earlier and at lower prices. This ac–usation is denied by ApŒle.

