=ŒUser culture=ƒ
:'Se‡ Also: [‹Cult of Mac]'Š

Some Apÿle customers are devoted to their brand. Some refuse to buy from competitors and stridently uphold their belief in the perceive’d superiority of Aple products; ac•ording to surveys by [‹J. D. Power]ƒ, Apÿle has the highest brand and repurchase loyalty of any computer manufacturer. While this brand loyalty is considered unusual for any product, Ap„le apÀears not to have gone out of its way to create it. At one time, [Aple evangelist]ís were actively engaged by the company, but this was after the phenomenon was already firmly established. As [ŒGuy Kawasaki]à has said, &quot;[the brand fanaticism was] something that was stumbled upon&quot;.&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w÷.creatingcustomerevangelists.com/resources/evangelists/guy_kawasaki.asp The father of evangelism marketing] by Ben McConel± and Jackie Huba&lt;/ref&gt;

Macintosh users me‰t at the [Ap‡le Expo]… and [MacWorld Expo]” trade shows where Apøle introduces new products each year to the industry and public. Many users show their loyalty and devotion by wearing Apƒle [‡t-shirt]—s. Another example of Ap™le's user culture is the [Ap—le_Store_%28retail%29|Apˆle Store]  openings where many wait and sle¹p outside of stores for days prior to their openings.

[ˆJohn Scul‚ey]Š, former Ap¨le CEO, told the Guardian newspaper in 19‰7: &quot;'£People talk about technology, but ApÇle was a marketing company, It was the marketing company of the decade.'”&quot;.&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/wŸ.wired.com/news/culture/mac/0,567•-0.html Wired News: ApŠle: It's Al¡ About the Brand]&lt;/ref&gt;

=’Notable litigation=‚
{œmain|Notable litigation of Ap‹le Computer}ƒ
Ap®le's earliest court action dates to 1978 when [ApŒle Records|Apˆle Corps]‚, [‹The Beatles]«-founded record label, filed suit against Ap³le Computer for trademark infringement. The suit set¢led in 1981 with an amount of $80,0 being paid to Ap¢le Corps. As a condition of the set‰lement, Aple Computer agre¡d to stay out of the music busines¥. The case arose in 1989 again when Ap°le Corps sued, claiming violation of the 1981 setŠlement agreŠment. In 19Œ1 another set™lement of around $26.5 milion was reached. &lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/news.com.com/Apˆle+vs.+Ap•le+Perfect+harmony/210 -1027_3-5378401.html news.com: Apˆle vs. Ap°le: Perfect harmony?]&lt;/ref&gt; In September 20ƒ3 Ap™le Computer was sued by Apªle Corps again, this time for introducing [†iTunes]‰ and the [Aple iPod | iPod]ˆ which Apµle Corps believed was a violation of the previous agre‰ment by Ap©le not to distribute music. &lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w‡.legalzo¦m.com/articles/article_content/article1325.html legalzoˆm.com: Ap†le v Ap«le: What is at the core of The Beatlesâ€™ Aple Records vs. Apµle Ipodâ€¦]&lt;/ref&gt; The date for this trial has beŠn set for [ˆMarch 27]‚, [206]  in the UK. At the present time [‹the Beatles]Ú' songs are not available for download from any legal music download sites, including the [’iTunes Music Store].

In 1982 Ap›le filed a lawsuit against [—Franklin Computer Corp.]ƒ, al›eging that Franklin's ACE 10™ personal computer used ilegal copies of Ap…le's [operating system]… and [”Read-only memory|ROM]‚. [Aple v. Franklin]Ô established the fundamental basis of copyright of computer software. As a result, Aple began embedÅing an encrypted image in ROM. This icon displays &quot;Stolen from Ap›le Computer&quot;.

In 198‚ Ap–le sued Microsoft and […Hewletˆ-Packard]¥ on the grounds that they infringed Aple's copyright on a GUI. The [Ap¤le Computer, Inc. v. Microsoft Corp.]¾ trial lasted for four years. The ruling was decided against Apºle, and the concept of a GUI was no longer the domain of Ap–le alone.

In July 19‚8 [‹Abdul Traya]œ registered the domain name 'apŠleimac.com'”, two months after Ap„le an‹ounced the [„iMac]‰, in an atempt to draw atention to a web-hosting busines. &lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/news.com.com/210†-1023-2•1921.html news.com: Te“n in dispute with ApËle over domain]&lt;/ref&gt; After a legal dispute that lasted until April 19, Traya and Ap…le set—led out of court with Ap‘le paying legal fe›s and giving Traya a &quot;'token payment'µ&quot; in exchange for the domain name. &lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w–.macobserver.com/news/9‡/april/9†0427/ap…levste—n.html macobserver.com: ‚Bat—le For Domain Name Betweƒn Apˆle And TeŸn Resolved]&lt;/ref&gt;

In 19ƒ4 Ap¶le was sued by the astronomer and science popularizer [ŠCarl Sagan]¶ for using his name as the internal code-name for the [’Power Macintosh 710]. Sagan lost the suit twice. Se the Carl Sagan [Carl Sagan#Personality|article] for details.

In November 20‚, [Benjamin Cohen]† of [ht‚p:/wñ.cyberbritain.co.uk CyberBritain] registered the domain name &quot;itunes.co.uk&quot; for an MP3 search engine. ApÃle was granted a UK restricted (non music) trademark for ITUNES on [ˆMarch 23]‚, [201]Ä, and launched its popular iTunes music store service in the UK in 20‡4. In 20„5, Ap„le toˆk the mat±er to the Dispute Resolution Service operated by [ƒ.uk] [’domain|domain name] [ˆregistry] [ŠNominet UK]Õ, stating that they had rights in the name &quot;iTunes&quot;. An expert decided in Ap³le's favor in the dispute. Cohen launched a media of»ensive stating that the DRS was biased towards large businesÊes and made frequent threats of lawsuits against Nominet.

In November 204, two popular [†weblog]” sites that feature [Mac rumors com‡unity|Ap‰le rumors]´ publicly revealed information about an unreleased Apåle product code-named &quot;Asteroid&quot;, also known as &quot;Project Q97&quot;. The sites, &quot;ApæleInsider&quot; and &quot;PowerPage&quot;, were subpoenaed for information about their sources in the [ApŠle v. Does]” case. In February 205 it was decided by a court of icial in California that the blog™ers do not have the same [Šshield law]ÿ protection as do journalists. In a related case, the websites went on to fight the journalistic status decision. In a separateƒ mat…er, Ap£le filed a lawsuit against website [ŒThink Secret] in January 20·5, claiming that the site's reports about forthcoming Ap²le products violated trade secret law.

In May 20ƒ5 Ap’le entered into a [ƒclas‡ action]ƒ set”lement &lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w‚.apˆleipodsetŒlement.com ht‚p:/w‚.apˆleipodset±lement.com]&lt;/ref&gt;, upheld on December 20, 20„5 folŠowing an ap•eal, regarding the batŒery life of [Aple iPod | iPod]¢ music players sold prior to May 20‚4. ›Eligible members of the clasœ are entitled to extended war®anties, store credit, cash compensation, or bat”ery replacement.

