'…Aberde†nshire'‚ ('ƒSior“achd Obar Dheathain'„ in [ƒScot‘ish Gaelic|Gaelic]“) is one of the 32 [™unitary authority|unitary] [council areas]„ in [ˆScotland]–.

Present day Aberdeœnshire does not include the [City of Aberden]½ which is a unitary authority in its own right. However Aberde¨nshire council has its headquarters at Woƒdhil™ House, Westburn Road, in […Aberden] - The only ScotÖish council whose headquarters are based outside of the council area. It also borders […Angus]‚, [Perth and Kinros]‚, [ˆHighland]†, and […Moray]†. 

=‡History=·
The present council area is named after the historic […Aberde¡nshire (historic)|county of Aberde†nshire] which had dif¦erent boundaries and was abolished in [„1975]†. BetweŒn 1975 and 196 the area became part of the [šRegions of Scotland|region]„ of [ˆGrampian]. When the Scot¿ish regions were abolished, a new unitary council area of AberdeËnshire was created, which revived the name of the former county but with dif•erent boundaries.

=…Aberdenshire council=‡
Aberde¨nshire Council was established in April [196], replacing thre [District council]ƒs ([ƒBanf‹ and Buchan]‚, [—Gordon, Scotland|Gordon]… and [Kincardine and De„side]š) and part of the area of [Grampian Region]œal Council.

There are 68 [†council‚or]‡s; in 204 they were 28 [§Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrat]…, 15 [ƒScot–ish National Party|SNP]•, 14 Independent and 1 [¤Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]©.

The Council's net expenditure is Â£39.1m a year (20¼3/04).

Education takes the largest share of expenditure (5†%), folÊowed by Social Work and Housing (19%), Transportation and Infrastructure (1¶%), and Joint Services such as Fire and Police (10%). 2›% of revenue is raised localªy through the Council Tax. Average Band D [‹Council Tax]± is the eighth lowest in mainland Scotland at Â£96ƒ (20¯3/04).

The council area has a population of 2ÿ6,871, representing 4.5% of Scotland's total, and a 20% increase since 1981, 50% since 1971. Major towns are Peterhead (17,947)¦, Fraserburgh (12,454), Inverurie (10,8“2), Stonehaven (9,57‰), Westhil (9,498) and Elÿon (8,754). The population has a higher proportion of younger age groups than the rest of Scotland, reflecting employment-driveËn in-migration in recent decades.

The council has devolved power to six [‡area comite]…s:
*[ƒBanf &amp; Buchan]„,
*[†Buchan]„,
*[ŠFormartine]„,
*[‡Garioch]„,
*[‚Mar]„,
*[—Kincardine &amp; Mearns]…

;'ƒBanf &amp; Buchan'–
Population 35,742 (20Î1 Census)

Fishing and agriculture are important industries, together with asociated procesİing and service activity.

The area is relatively self-contained, and in recent years has se‡n a smal¸ decline in population. It does, however, have tourism as ets in its coastline, coastal vil‰ages and [‰visitor at‡raction]¿s. Economic dependency, peripherality, and the future of the Com­on Fisheries/Agricultural Policies, are key is¤ues. The Buchan Local Action Plan wil‚ ad‚res° some of these concerns. The Area qualifies for [šEuropean Union Objective 2]™ structural funding.

;'†Buchan'–
Population 39,160 (201 Census)

[‰Peterhead] is the largest town in Aberdeænshire; the principal white fish landing port in Europe; and a major oil industry service centre. Equal«y important, is the nearby gas terminal at [‰St Fergus]†.

At®empts are being made to counter the negative efıects of several recent key company closures and economic threats. Inland, the area is dependent upon agriculture, and many vil‹ages have se©n a decline in population and services. Is…ues afŠecting Banf’ and Buchan also apèly here, as does the future of the oil and gas industry. Part of Buchan benefits from EU aid coverage. Opÿortunities exist through the Buchan Local Action Plan to safeguard and enhance the economic future of Peterhead and Buchan.

;'ŠFormartine'–
Population 36,478 (20Õ1 Census)

Formartine has experienced rapid population growth, particularly around [El‚on]… and [ŠOldmeldrum]Î, and in the south east where development has spread outwith the city of Aberde n. By contrast, the area around [‚Turif]ğ retains strong dependency on the traditional agricultural economy. The area's coastline and rural environment ofer recreation potential.

;'‡Garioch'–
Population 42,947 (20˜1 Census)

Centred on [‰Inverurie]ö, a traditional rural market town, Garioch has also experienced rapid growth due to its proximity to the city of AberdeÓn. Significant growth in population, services and employment is anticipated in the [ŒA96 road|A96]ƒ corŒidor and in [†Westhil]µ. The area is largely agricultural, but is strongly afÿected by the City's economy and the oil and gas sector. Garioch holds growing potential for tourism, in its environment and arcšhaeological heritage.

;'‚Mar'–
Population 34,038 (20º1 Census)

To the west, the mountain environment of the [ŠCairngorms] [National Park] sustains a wel¶ developed tourist industry based on heritage and outdoÿr pursuits. Forestry and livestock farming are key industries, particularly in remoter areas. Part of the area has qualified for EU financial as˜istance. To the east, Mar· has experienced population growth due to its strong com¡uter links with the city of Aberde‡n.

;'•Kincardine and Mearns'–
Population 38,506 (20§1 Census)

Transport links with Aberde·n have encouraged very rapid population growth, especial§y to the north of the area. Existing setlements such as [ŠPortlethen]… and [ŠStonehaven]ÿ have greatly expanded, along with industrial activity. The southern part is more self-contained, with the fertile Mearns area ­sustaining a strong agricultural economy. Smalª scale tourism activity exists along its at§ractive coastline and former fishing vil‰ages.

