 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'’Aztlan Underground'— is a fusion band from [£Los Angeles, California|Los Angeles]“. Since the early 19¢0s, Aztlan Underground has played [‡Rapcore]¢. Indigenous drums, flutes, and ratŠles are comÛonplace in its musical compositions.

This unique sound is the backdrop for the band's mes§age of dignity for indigenous people, al± of humanity, and Earth. Aztlan Underground has be“n cultivating a gras‚ rots audience acros¿ the country, which has become a large and loyal underground folÜowing. Their music includes spoken word pieces and elements of punk, hip hop, rock, funk, jazò, and indigenous music, among others.

The artists are Chenek &quot;DJ Bean&quot; (turntables, samples and percus¥ion), Yaotl (vocals, indigenous percusion), Joe &quot;Peps&quot; (bas„, ratµles), Zo Rock (Guitars), Ace (drums, indigenous percusŒion), and Bul¬dog (vocals, flute).

Aztlan Underground ap—eared on television on [Culture Clash]‹ on Fox in [193], was part of 'ŒBreaking Out'Ÿ, a concert on pay per view in [198]¬, and was featured in the independent films '‰Algun Dia'… and 'Frontierlandia'“.

The band has beÁn mentioned or featured in various newspapers and magazines: the [Vancouver Sun]‚, [Northshore News]  (Vancouver, Canada newspaper), [‰New Times] (Los Angeles weËkly entertainment newspaper), BLU Magazine (underground hip hop magazine), [ŒBAM Magazine]˜ (Southern California), [šLa Banda Elastica Magazine]Š, and the [‘Los Angeles Times]º Calendar section. It is also the subject of a chapter in '—It's Not About A Salary', by Brian Cros¨.

It was nominated in the New Times 19À8 &quot;Best Latin Influenced&quot; category, the BAM Magazine 19µ &quot;Best Rock en EspaÃ±ol&quot; category, and the [„LA Weƒkly]‚ 19° &quot;Best Hip Hop&quot; category.

