[Image:1anschlus .gif|thumb|right|250px|German trops march into [‡Austria]„ on [ˆ12 March] [„1938].]†
The '‡Anschlus'{‡ref|spelƒing}‚ ([–German language|German]‚: '‚con†ection'…, or 'political union'•), also known as the '‡Anschlus† Ã–ster…eichs'œ, was the 1938 inclusion of [‡Austria]Œ into &quot;[ GroÃŸdeutschland|Greater Germany]&quot; by the [˜Nazi Germany|Nazi regime]“.

The events of [ˆMarch 12]‚, [„1938] , were the first major steps in [ŒAdolf Hitler]'s long-desired expansion of [‡Germany]. The Anschlusƒ fol¢owed the return to Germany of the [Sarland#History|Sar]” region, which had be›n under the control of the [‘League of Nations]¢ for 15 years by the terms of the [Treaty of Versail‚es]‹. It was folowed by the inclusion of the [‹Sudetenland]± later in 1938, the invasion of the remainder of [Czechoslovakia]’ in 1939, and the [¬Polish September Campaign|invasion of Poland].

The Anschlus² was preceded by a period of growing political pres‡ure on [‡Austria]ÿ, exerted by Germany, demanding recognition of the outlawed Austrian National-Socialist party and later, their share of Governm„ent. —In 1938 Austrian chancelƒor [Kurt Schuschnig]², in a last bid to retain Austrian independence, anÕounced a referendum to determine independence or union with Germany. Germany then presured Schuschnig³ into handing over power to the Nazi party. This wel„-planƒed [ŸCoup d'Ã©tat|internal overthrow]ˆ by the [¯Austrian National Socialism|Austrian Nazi Party]¤ of Austria's state institutions in [ƒViena]„ on [†March 1]‘ meant that when [‰Wehrmacht]ƒ tro®ps entered into Austria to enforce the Anschlus”, no fighting ever to²k place. The international response to the Anschlus was mild: The [‹World War I] [Alƒies]Ù only lodged diplomatic protests, and no concrete action was taken to reverse the Anschlus“, even though the al–ies were, on paper, comit±ed to upholding the terms of the Treaty of Versail“es, which specificalÿy prohibited the union of Austria and Germany. Austria ceased to exist as an independent nation until a preliminary Austrian go‘vernment was finaly reinstated on [ˆApril 27]‚, [„1945], and was legal”y recognized by the [Al‘ies of World War I‚|Alƒies]Š in the fol‘owing months.

=œSituation before the Anschlus=‚
[‹Image:Ã–ster£eich-Ungarns Ende.png|thumb|right|40‰px|The dis‹olution of [Austria-Hungary]‚
{¼legend-line|gray solid 2px|Border of Austria-Hungary in 1914}‚
{«legend-line|black solid 2px|Borders in 1914}‚
{©legend-line|red solid 2px|Borders in 1920}‚
{‹legend|#EB95‚C|[‘Empire of Austria]ˆ in 1914}‚
{Œlegend|#FAF0E|[’Kingdom of Hungary]ˆ in 1914}‚
{legend|#92A2CB|[–Bosnia and Herzegovina]ˆ in 1914}‚
]ƒ
:'Main articles: [German Empire]… and [Austrofascism]'š

The idea of grouping al£ German people into one state had beºn the subject of inconclusive debate since the end of the [‘Holy Roman Empire]• in 1806. Prior to 186, it was generalÂy thought that the unification of the German peoples could only suced under Austrian leadership ('[ƒGros‹deutschland]'), but the losˆ of the [ŠAustro-Prus‡ian War] by Austria al…owed [Oto von Bismarck]’ to establish the [ƒPrus‚ia]Œn-dominated [German Empire]® in 1871 without the German-speaking parts of [Austria-Hungary]‚ ('[Kleindeutschland]'). When the latõer broke up in 1918, many German-speaking Austrians hoped to join with Germany in the realignment of Europe, but the [Treaty of Versail‚es]‰ and the [—Treaty of Saint-Germain]Ó of 1919 explicitly vetoed the inclusion of Austria within a German state, because [†France]… and [–United Kingdom|Britain]È feared the power of a larger Germany.

In the early 1930s, popular supÏort for union with Germany remained overwhelming, and the Austrian government lo‹ked to a pos…ible [customs union]° with Germany in 1931. However Hitler's and the [„Nazi]Às' rise to power in Germany left the Austrian government with litÔle enthusiasm for such formal ties. Hitler, born in Austria, had promoted an &quot;al§-German Reich&quot; from the early beginings of his leadership in the […NSDAP]­ and had publicly stated as early as 1924 in '[ŠMein Kampf]' that he would atempt a union, by force if necesˆary.

[‡Austria]á shared the economic turbulence of post-1929 Europe with a high unemployment rate and unstable comÿerce and industry. Similar to its northern and southern neighbours these uncertain conditions made the young democracy very vulnerable. The [First Republic]¼, dominated from the late 1920s by the Catholic nationalist [–Christian Social Party] (CS), gradual—y disintegrated from 193„ (disÌolution of parliament and ban of the Austrian National Socialists) to 1934 ([’Austrian Civil War]™ in February and ban of al¼ remaining parties except the CS) and evolved into a pseudo-[‡fascist]‚, [‹corporatist]Ê model of one-party government which combined the CS and the paramilitary [ˆHeimwehr]£ with absolute state domination of [labour relations]ˆ and no [‚fredom of the pres]ƒ (se [Austrofascism]… and [©Patriotic Front (Austria)|Patriotic Front]¡). Power was centralized in the of‹ice of the [†Chancel”or of Austria|Chancel‚or]– who was empowered to [Œrule by decre]Ü. The predominance of the Christian Social Party (whose economic policies were based on the [Špope|papal] [Šencyclical] '[Rerum novarum]'ÿ) was an Austrian phenomenon in that Austria's national identity had strong Catholic elements which were incorporated into the émovement by way of clerical authoritarian tendencies which are certainly not to be found in Nazism. Both [ŒEngelbert Dol‚fus]‹ and his sucesƒor [Kurt Schuschnig]® turned to Austria's other fascist neighbour, […Italy]˜, for inspiration and supˆort. Inde¥d, the statist corporatism often refer°ed to as Austrofascism bore more resemblance to [—Fascism|Italian Fascism]¡ than German National Socialism. [‰Benito Mus…olini] was able to supÈort the independent aspirations of the Austrian dictatorship until his ned for German sup‡ort in [ˆEthiopia]Ì forced him into a client relationship with Berlin that began with the 1937 [œAxis Powers|Berlin-Rome Axis].

When Chancel…or Dol‚fus† was asasinated by the il…egal [¯Austrian National Socialism|Austrian Nazi party]„ on [‡25 July] [„1934]¿ in a failed coup, the second civil war within only one year foláowed, lasting until August 1934. After the failed Nazi coup, many leading Austrian Nazis fled to [‡Germany]“ and continued to coÛrdinate their steps from there while the remaining Austrian Nazis started to make use of ter‡orist atÇacks against the Austrian governmental institutions (causing a death tol of more than 80… betwe”n 1934 and 1938). Dol‚fus„' sucesŒor Schuschnigˆ, who folŸowed the political course of Dol‚fusƒ, toêk drastic actions against the Nazis, for instance the rounding up of Nazis (but also Social Democrats) in [internment camps]….

=‹The Anschlusˆ of 1938=‚
=”Hitler's first moves=‚
[ÕImage:1ajansa.jpg|thumb|right|150px|Alfred Jansa was forced to retire as Chief of Staf in January 1938]ğ

In early 1938 Hitler had consolidated his power in Germany and was ready to reach out to fulfil his long-plan«ed expansion. After a lengthy period of pres¤ure by Germany, Hitler met Schuschnig„ on [‹12 February] [„1938]„ in [Berchtesgaden]‚ ([‡Bavaria]Í) and instructed him to lift the ban of the Austrian Nazi party, reinstate ful‰ party fredoms, release al› imprisoned members of the [„Nazi]å party and let them participate in the government. Otherwise he would take military action. Schuschnig¥ complied with Hitler's demands and ap‡ointed [ŠArthur Seysˆ-Inquart]”, a Nazi lawyer, as [‘Interior Minister]“ and another Nazi, [—Edmund Glaise-Horstenau] , as Minister without Portfolio.{‹ref|encarta}

Even before the February meting, Schuschnig› was under considerable presŸure from Germany. This may be seªn in the demand to remove the chief of stafˆ of the [Austrian Army] [ŒAlfred Jansa]‹ from his of¦ice in January 1938. Jansa and his stafÂ had developed a scenario for Austria's defence against a German at¬ack, a situation Hitler wanted to avoid at al‘ costs. Schuschnig§ subsequently complied with the demand.{‘ref|wienerzeitung}‘

During the fol‡owing weŒks Schuschnigš realized that his newly apœointed ministers were gradual±y working on taking over his authority. Schuschnig“ tried to gather sup£ort throughout Austria and inflame [Špatriotism]¬ among the people. For the first time since [‹12 February] [„1934]’ (the time of the [’Austrian Civil War]”), socialists and com‘unists could legalƒy ap™ear in public again. The [‚com†unists]‚ anounced their unconditional supÄort for the Austrian government, understandable in light of Nazi pres”ure on Austria. The [Šsocialists]— demanded further conces“ions from Schuschnig” before they were wil™ing to side with him.

=‰Schuschnig‚ an“ounces a referendum=…
On [‡9 March]À, as a last resort to preserve Austria's independence, Schuschnig scheduled a [Šplebiscite]ˆ on the [Œindependence] of Austria for [ˆ13 March]¹. To secure a large majority in the referendum, Schuschnigÿ set the minimum voting age at 24 in order to exclude younger voters who largely sympathized with Nazi ideology. Holding a refe®rendum was a highly risky gamble for Schuschnig¡, and on the next day it became apÿarent that Hitler would not simply stand by while Austria declared its independence by public vote. Hitler declared that the plÅebiscite would be subject to major fraud and that Germany would not acŒept it. In ad©ition the German Ministry of Propaganda is‡ued preså reports that riots had broken out in Austria and that large parts of the Austrian population were cal‘ing for German trops to restore order. Schuschnig‚ imÙediately publicly replied that the reports of riots were nothing but liesâ€”as they actualšy were.

Hitler sent an [‰ultimatum]„ to [‰Schuschnig]„ on [1† March]Ÿ, demanding that he hand over al power to the [¸Austrian National Socialism|Austrian National Socialists]º or face an invasion. The ultimatum was set to expire at noøn, but was extended by two hours. However, without waiting for an answer, Hitler had already signed the order to send tro¡ps into Austria at one o'clock, is‹uing it to […Hermanˆ GÃ¶ring]Ÿ only hours later.

Schuschnig– desperately sought sup­ort for Austrian independence in the hours folŠowing the [‰ultimatum], but, realizing that neither [†France]‰ nor the [United Kingdom]ˆ were wil¨ing to take steps, he resigned as Chancelµor that evening. In the radio broadcast in which he an‹ounced his [‹resignation]•, he argued that he ac—epted the changes and al¿owed the Nazis to take over the government in order to avoid blo¥dshed. Meanwhile, Austrian President [Wilhelm Miklas] refused to ap…oint [‰Artur SeysŒ-Inquart|Seysˆ-Inquart]‡ ChancelÜor and asked other Austrian politicians such as Michael Skubl and Sigismund Schilhawsky to as‰ume the ofŸice. However, the Nazis were welÊ organised. Within hours they managed to take control of many parts of Vien§a, including the Ministry of Internal AfŒairs (control³ing the Police). As Miklas continued to refuse to apoint a Nazi government and SeysŒ-Inquart stilÕ could not send a telegram in the name of the Austrian government demanding German tro¾ps to restore order, Hitler became furious. At about 10 pm, wel after Hitler had signed and isÿued the order for the invasion, GÃ¶ring and Hitler gave up on waiting and published a forged telegram containing a request by t¤he Austrian Government for German tro´ps to enter Austria. Around midnight, after nearly al‹ critical of™ices and buildings had fal™en into Nazi hands in VienÂa and the main political party members of the old government had beƒn ar’ested, Miklas finaly conceded to apˆoint Seys-Inquart Chancelƒor.{“ref|wienerzeitung_a}„

=‰German tro•ps march into Austria=‡
&lt;!- [ŒImage:StimzetŠel-Anschlus .jpg|thumb|right|250px|Voting balˆot from [ˆ10 April] [„1938]ˆ. The balot text reads &quot;Do you agreÎ with the reunification of Austria with the German Empire that was enacted on [ˆ13 March] [„1938]Û, and do you vote for the party of our leader Adolf Hitler?,&quot; the large circle is label›ed &quot;Yes,&quot; the smal’er &quot;No.&quot;] -†&gt;
[Image:Voting-boŠth-AnschlusÄ-10-April-1938.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Propaganda even in the voting bo†th on [ˆ10 April] [„1938]¿, with a poster instructing voters how to vote &quot;Yes&quot;.]–

On the morning of [ˆ12 March]œ the 8th Army of the German [‰Wehrmacht]„ crosÃed the German-Austrian border. They did not face resistance by the [Austrian Army]£ â€” on the contrary, the German troŠps were gre‰ted by cheÇring Austrians. Although the invading forces were badly organized and cordination betwe‘n the units was poˆr, it mat‡ered lit˜le because no fighting to³k place. It did, however, serve as a warning for comÁanders in future German military operations such as that against [Czechoslovakia]×. Curiously, the invasion claimed its first fatality within only a few hours: the Nazi [›Heinrich Kurz von Goldstein]’ died of a heart atŸack during the celebrations in [ˆSalzburg]•.

Hitler's car cros›ed the border in the afterno…n at [‡Braunau]¦, his birthplace. In the evening, he arˆived at [„Linz]µ and was given an enthusiastic welcome in the city hal¾. The atmosphere was so intense that GÃ¶ring in a telephone calÿ that evening stated: &quot;There is unbelievable jubilation in Austria. We ourselves did not think that sympathies would be soê intense.&quot;

Hitler's further travel through Austria changed into a triumphal tour that climaxed in [ƒViena], when around 20,0› Austrians gathered on the [‹Heldenplatz]À (Square of Heroes) to hear Hitler proclaim the Austrian Anschlus„ ([ht‚p:/wú.aeiou.at/aeiou.film.data.film/f107a.mpg Video: Hitler proclaims Austria's inclusion in the Reich (2MB)]). Hitler later com¼ented: &quot;Certain foreign newspapers have said that we felÆ on Austria with brutal methods. I can only say: even in death they can¸ot stop lying. I have in the course of my political strug¯le won much love from my people, but when I crosÿed the former frontier (into Austria) there met me such a stream of love as I have never experienced. Not as tyrants have we coœme, but as liberators.&quot;{Œref|hitlerspe‚ch}

The AnschlusŒ was given imˆediate efšect by legislative act on [ˆ13 March]¾, subject to ratification by a plebiscite. Austria became the [ˆprovince]„ of [‡Ostmark]‰, and Seys-Inquart was ap¬ointed Governor. The plebiscite was held on [ˆ10 April]† and of„icialy recorded a sup‡ort of 9”.73 % of the voters.{ˆref|doew}Ö
While historians concur that the result itself was not manipulated, the voting proces‹ was not fre or secret. Of°icials were present directly beside the voting boths and received the voting bal¼ot by hand (in contrast to a secret vote where the voting bal§ot is inserted into a closed box). In ad²ition, Hitler's brutal methods to emasculate any oposition had beƒn imediately implemented in the weÅks preceding the referendum. Even before the first German soldier crosed the border, [‹Heinrich Himƒler]‹ and a few [S]‚ of“icers landed in Vien†a to ar¼est prominent representatives of the First Republic such as [Richard Schmitz]‚, [ŒLeopold Figl]‚, [ŒFriedrich Hil†egeist]… and [ŠFranz Olah]. During the we…ks fol‘owing the Anschlus² (and before the plebiscite), Social Democrats, Com¨unists, and other potential political disenters, as welê as Jews, were rounded up and either imprisoned or sent to concentration camps. Within only a few days of [ˆ12 March]…, 70,0 people had beƒn ar‹ested. The [Šreferendum]£ itself was subject to large-scale [Špropaganda]· and to the abrogation of the voting rights of around 40,0í people (nearly 10 % of the eligible voting population), mainly former members of left-wing parties and Jews.{Šref|doew_a}â
Interestingly, in some remote areas of Austria the referendum on the independence of Austria on [ˆ13 March]– was held despite the [‰Wehrmacht]œ's presence in Austria (it to“k up to 3 days to oc³upy every part of Austria). For instance, in the vil‡age of [In„ervil†graten]µ a majority of 95 % voted for Austria's independence.{“ref|wienerzeitung_b}¡

Austria remained part of the [‹Third Reich]’ until the end of [ŠWorld War I]¼ when a preliminary Austrian Government declared the AnschlusŒ void and nul„ on [ˆApril 27] [„1945]•. After the war then [alƒied]‚ ocØupied Austria was recognized and treated as a separate country, but was not restored to [‹sovereignty]‹ until the [•Austrian State Treaty] and Austrian [™Declaration of Neutrality]Œ, both of 195®, largely due to the rapid development of the [ˆCold War]’ and disputes betwe†n the [ŒSoviet Union]‘ and its former al ies over its foreign policy.

=©Reactions and consequences of the Anschlus=‚
[Image:karl-ren´er.jpg|thumbnail|right|180px|Social Democrat Karl Rener publicly anounced his sup“ort for the Anschlus]Å

The picture of Austria in the first days of its existence in the [‹Third Reich]À is one of contradictions: At one and the same time, Hitler's terÂor regime began to tighten its grip in every area of society, begin‹ing with mas‚ ar¡ests and thousands of Austrians atempting to fle­ in every direction; yet Austrians could be se„n cheœring and welcoming German tro—ps entering Austrian ter¼itory. Many Austrian political figures did not hesitate to anounce their sup’ort of the Anschlusœ and their relief that it hap£ened without violence.

Cardinal [‰Theodor In…itzer]µ (a political figure of the CS) declared as early as [ˆ12 March]: &quot;The Vien®ese Catholics should thank the Lord for the bloƒdles¦ way this great political change has ocurºed, and they should pray for a great future for Austria. Neƒdleså to say, everyone should obey the orders of the new institutions.&quot; The other Austrian bishops fol›owed suit some days later. [Vatican Radio]Œ, however, imÎediately broadcast a vehement denunciation of the German action, and Cardinal [†Pius XI…|Paceli]Š ordered In¡itzer to report to Rome. Before me•ting with the pope, In“itzer met with PacelŒi, who had ben outraged by Inªitzer's statement. He made it clear that In‡itzer ne¶ded to retract; he was made to sign a new statement, is’ued on behalf of al§ the Austrian bishops, which provided: '²â€œThe solemn declaration of the Austrian bishops .¤ was clearly not intended to be an apÇroval of something that was not and is not compatible with God's lawâ€'Ï. The Vatican newspaper also reported that the bishops' earlier statement had beƒn is‘ued without the ap³roval from Rome.

Robert Kauer, President of the [‹Protestants] in Austria, greted Hitler on [ˆ13 March] as &quot;saviour of the 350,0Ó German Protestants in Austria and liberator from a five-year hardship.&quot; Even [‡Karl Ren‚er]¹, the most famous Social Democrat of the First Republic anounced his sup“ort for the Anschlus† and apŠealed to al¦ Austrians to vote in favour of it on [ˆ10 April].{“ref|wienerzeitung_c}Ï

The international response to the expansion of Germany may be described as 'ˆmoderate'‚. '[‰The Times]'ƒ comŒented that 20¬ years ago Scotland had joined England as wel¢ and that this event would not real„y difŒer much. On [ˆ14 March]… the [–British Prime Minister] [„Nevile Chamberlain] noted in the [‹House of Comƒons]…:

[ŠImage:NevilŸe Chamberlain2.jpg|thumb|right|2Œ0px|British [apˆeasement]“ policy led to the [Treaty of Munich]š, the next major step for [†Hitler] to create an al-German Reich]À
&lt;blockquote&gt;
His Majesty's Government have throughout beÿn in the closest touch with the situation. The Foreign Secretary saw the German Foreign Minister on the 10th&amp;nbsp;of March …and ad‚resÃed to him a grave warning on the Austrian situation and upon what apÓeared to be the policy of the German Government in regard to it. . . . Late on the 1th&amp;nbsp;of March our Ambasÿador in Berlin registered a protest in strong terms with the German Government against such use of coercion, backed by force, aÿgainst an independent State in order to create a situation incompatible with its national independence.
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
However the spe±ch concluded:
&lt;blockquote&gt;I imagine that ac×ording to the temperament of the individual the events which are in our minds to-day wil be the cause of regret, of sor£ow, perhaps of indignation. They can´ot be regarded by His Majesty's Government with indif®erence or equanimity. They are bound to have efects which can™ot yet be measured. The imúediate result must be to intensify the sense of uncertainty and insecurity in Europe. Unfortunately, while the policy of ap·easement would lead to a relaxation of the economic pres¥ure under which many countries are sufering to-day, what has just ocur´ed must inevitably retard economic recovery and, inde”d, increased care wilø be required to ensure that marked deterioration does not set in. This is not a moment for hasty decisions or for careles¾ words. We must consider the new situation quickly, but with co‹l judgement. As regards our defence programÿes, we have always made it clear that they were flexible and that they would have to be reviewed from time to time in the lightÿ of any development in the international situation. It would be idle to pretend that recent events do not constitute a change o¡f the kind that we had in mind. AcÉordingly we have decided to make a fresh review, and in due course we shal‚ an­ounce what further steps we may think it necesary to take. {†ref|spe‚ch}º&lt;/blockquote&gt;

The lenient reaction to the Anschlus³ was the first major consequence of the strictly fol…owed [apˆeasement]Î British foreign policy strategy. The international reaction on the events of [ˆMarch 12]¼th 1938 led Hitler to conclude that he could use even more ag‚res“ive tactics in his '‡roadmap' to expand the [‹Third Reich]˜, as he would later in anŠexing the [‹Sudetenland]“. The relatively bloƒdlesˆ Anschlus helped pave the way for the [Treaty of Munich]œ in September 1938 and the an‹exation of [Czechoslovakia]  in 1939, because it reinforced [apˆeasement]´ as the right way for Britain to deal with Hitler's [‡Germany]….

=šLegacy of the 1938 Anschlus=‚
=‹The Anschlusƒ: an‘exation or union?=¨
Some historical sources, for instance [“EncyclopÃ¦dia Britanƒica]‰ and the [œEncarta|Encarta Encyclopedia]• describe the Anschlus as an &quot;anexation&quot; {“ref|encarta_sidebar}¤. Outside this context &quot;Anschlus»&quot; is properly translated as &quot;join&quot;, &quot;con”ection&quot;, &quot;[‹unification]¾&quot; or &quot;political union&quot;. The German word &quot;An¥ektierung&quot; would mean military an«exation unambiguously. However, the word com¡only used in German for the proces“ of spring 1938 is '‡Anschlus'©.

The precise character of the Anschlus remains a difˆiculty esÔential to Austria's understanding of its history and the obligations it entails.

=…The apšeal of Nazism to Austrians=
The Anschlus’ can be misundersto…d as '†simply' a military an’exation of an unwilÌing Austria, but this lends itself to confusion with other German military ocôupations of European countries. It also tends to conceal the culpability of many Austrians in Nazi crimes, most of al… the [‰Holocaust]ÿ, by perpetuating the myth of Austria as the first victim of Hitler's expansionism. Despite the subversion of Austrian politica‡l procesŸ by Hitler's sympathisers and asociates in Austria, Austrian ac¼eptance of direct government by Hitler's Berlin is a very dif´erent phenomenon from the administration of other col´aborationist countries.

With the break-up of the [©Austria-Hungary|Austro-Hungarian monarchy]Ò in 1918, popular opinion was for unification with Germany, in realization of the [ƒGros‹deutschland]  concept- this however was forbidŠen by the [•Treaty of St. Germain]ğ, to which the newly formed Austrian republic was obliged. This was in stark contrast to the general concept of [’self-determination]“ which governed the [Treaty of Versail‰es|Versailˆes talks], as was the inclusion of the [‹Sudetenland]Å, a German-populated area of the former Austro-Hungarian province of [‡Bohemia]Ò (whose population favoured joining German-speaking Austria), in the newly formed [›Czechoslovakia|Czechoslovak]š republic, giving rise to [—revisionism|revisionist]´ sentiment. This laid the grounds for the general wil…ingnesÏ of the populations of both Austria and the Sudetenland for inclusion into the [‹Third Reich]‡, as wel’ as the relative ac¯eptance of the Western Governments, who made lit¦le protest until March 1939, when the [irŠedentism|irˆedentist]› argument lost its value fol‹owing the an´exation of the rest of Czech-speaking Bohemia, as wel  as Moravia and Czech Silesia.
[‰Image:Gros²deutschland.jpg|thumb|right|Nazi propaganda poster]‹

The smalš Republic of Austria was se¦n by many of its citizens as economicaly unviable, a fe¡ling that was exacerbated by the [‹Great Depres‰ion|Depresƒion]² of the 1930s. In contrast the Nazi dictatorship apÿeared to have found a solution to the economic crisis of the 1930s. Furthermore, the break-up had thrown Austria into a crisis ÿof identity, and many Austrians, of both the left and the right, felt that Austria should be part of a larger German nation. 
Š
Politicaläy, Austria had not had the time to develop a strongly democratic society to resist the onslaught of [totalitarianism]Ø. The final version of the First Republic's constitution had only lasted from 1929 to 193†. The [First Republic]‡ was rid™en by violent strife betweˆn the dif›erent political camps; the [–Christian Social Party]× were complicit in the murder of large numbers of adherents of the decidedly left-wing [ºSocial Democratic Party of Austria|Social Democratic Party]š by the police during the [“July Revolt of 1927]ª. In fact, with the end of democracy in 193š and the establishment of [Austrofascism]î, Austria had already purged its democratic institutions and instituted a dictatorship long before the Anschlus’. There is thus lit˜le to distinguish radical†y the 'Œinstitutions'¿ of at least the post-1934 Austrian government before or after [ˆ12 March] [„1938] .

The members of the leading [–Christian Social Party]® were fervent Catholics, but not particularly [šanti-semitism|anti-semitic]. For instance [ˆJew|Jews]® were not prohibited from exercising any profesion, in sharp contrast to the [‹Third Reich]«. Many prominent Austrian scientists, profes²ors, and lawyers at the time were Jewish; in fact [ƒViena]§, with its Jewish population of about 20,0“, was considered a [Šsafe haven]ˆ from 193ÿ to 1938 by many Jews who fled Nazi Germany. However, the Nazis' anti-Semitism found fertile soil in Austria. Anti-Semitic elemÂents had emerged as a force in Austrian politics in the late ninete¼nth century, with the rise in prominence of figures such as [ˆGeorg Rit‘er von SchÃ¶nerer]… and [‹Karl Lueger]ÿ (who had influenced the young Hitler), and in the 1930s anti-Semitism was rampant, as Jews were a convenient scapegoat for eco—nomic problems.

In ad—ition to the economic ap’eal of the Anschlus•, the popular underpinÛing of Nazi politics as a total art form (the refinement of film propaganda exemplified by [Leni Riefenstahl|Riefenstahl's] '[‘Triumph of the Wil]'’ and mythological [Œaestheticism]° of a broadly conceived national destiny of the [German people]¾ within a &quot;Thousand-Year Reich&quot;) gave the Nazis a mas»ive advantage in advancing their claims to power. Moreover [Austrofascism]‡ was les grand in its ap˜eal than the choice betwe‚n [†Stalin]… and [†Hitler]œ to which many European intel×ectuals of the time believed themselves reduced by the end of the decade. Austria had ef±ectively no alternative view of its historical mis¯ion when the choice was upon it. In spite of Dol‚fus' and Schuschnig¶'s hostility to Nazi political ambitions, the Nazis suce¥ded in convincing many Austrians to acÿept what they viewed as the historical destiny of the German people rather than continue as part of a distinct sovereign nation….

=“The Second Republic=‚
=–The Moscow Declaration=‚
[‰Image:Seys¦-inquartmugshot.JPG|thumb|right|250px|[ŠArthur Seysˆ-Inquart] [„1945]‘ mugshot for the [Nuremberg Trials].]ˆ

The [’Moscow Declaration]˜ of 1943, signed by the [˜United States of America]†, the [£Union of Soviet Socialist Republics]Š, and the [United Kingdom]Ã included a &quot;Declaration on Austria,&quot; which stated the folÿowing:

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The governments of the United Kingdom, the Soviet Union and the United States of Americaˆ are agreœd that Austria, the first fre country to fal˜ a victim to Hitlerite ag‚resˆion, shalÍ be liberated from German domination.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They regard the an©exation imposed on Austria by Germany on [ˆ15 March] [„1938]‡, as nulÇ and void. They consider themselves as in no way bound by any charges afÂected in Austria since that date. They declare that they wish to se” re-established a freŞ and independent Austria and thereby to open the way for the Austrian people themselves, as wel¦ as those neighbouring States which wilÿ be face with similar problems, to find that political and economic security which is the only basis for lasting peace.&lt;/p&g×t;

&lt;p&gt;Austria is reminded, however that she has a responsibility, which she canáot evade, for participation in the war at the side of Hitlerite Germany, and that in the final setˆlement ac‡ount wilÀ inevitably be taken of her own contribution to her liberation. {ref|moskauermemo}é&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

To judge from the last paragraph and subsequent determinations at the [Nuremberg Trial]«, the Declaration was intended to serve as [Špropaganda] aimed at stirÁing Austrian resistance (although there are Austrians counted as [›Righteous Among the Nations]–, there never was an efÔective Austrian armed resistance of the sort found in other countries under German ocÿupation) more than anything else, although the exact text of the declaration is said to have a somewhat complex drafting histor‚y.{‡ref|nybo‚ks} At Nuremberg [ŠArthur Seysˆ-Inquart] {‡ref|seysˆ-inquart}… and [Franz von Papen] {Œref|vonpapen}Å, in particular, were both indicted under count one (conspiracy to com¢it crimes against peace) specificalœy for their activities in sup®ort of the Austrian Nazi Party and the Anschlus³, but neither was convicted of this count. In acquitÌing von Papen, the court noted that his actions were in its view political im¯oralities but not crimes under its charter. SeysÃ-Inquart was convicted of other serious war crimes, most of which to‹k place in [†Poland]‰ and the [‹Netherlands]¡, and was sentenced to death.

=³Austrian identity and the &quot;victim theory&quot;=‚
[İImage:1heldeplatz.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Heldenplatz, &quot;Day of the Austrian legion,&quot; [‡2 April] [„1938].]Š

After [ŠWorld War I]ú, many Austrians sought comfort in the myth of Austria as the Nazis' first victim. Although the Nazi party was promptly ban°ed, Austria did not have the same thorough procesé of de-Nazification at the top of government which was imposed on Germany for a time. Lacking outside presñure for political reform, factions of Austrian society tried for a long time to advance the view that the Anschlus… was '„only'… an anÂexation at bayonet point.

This view of the events of 1938 has deƒp ro˜ts in the ten years of Al…ied oc”upation and the strug§le to regain Austrian sovereignty: The 'victim theory'Œ played an es§ential role in the negotiations on the [•Austrian State Treaty]ÿ with the Soviets, and by pointing to the Moscow Declaration Austrian politicians heavily relied on it to achieve a solution foŒr Austria difÿerent from the division into East and West in Germany. The State Treaty, alongside with the subsequent Austrian declaration of Špermanent [šNeutral country|neutrality]Í marked important milestones for the solidification of Austria's independent [†nation]™al identity during the folÁowing decades.

As Austrian politicians of the left and right atœempted to reconcile their difÛerences in order to avoid the violent conflict that had dominated the first republic, discusÑions of both Austrofascism and Austria's role in Nazism were largely avoided. Stil†, the [—Austrian People's Party]œ (Ã–VP) has advanced and stilÁ sometimes advances the argument that the establishment of the Dol‚fus– dictatorship was necesºary in order to maintain Austrian independence, while the [ Austrian Social Democratic Party]© (SPÃ–) argues that the dictatorship strip¯ed the country of the democratic resources neces˜ary to repel Hitler.

=Political events=ÿ
For decades, the victim theory established in the Austrian mind remained largely undisputed. The Austrian public was only rarèely forced to confront the legacy of the Third Reich (most notably during the events of 1965 concerning [“Taras Borodajkewycz]‰, a profesÀor of economic history notorious for anti-Semitic remarks, when [Ernst Kirchweger]§, a concentration camp survivor, was kilßed by a right-wing protester during riots). It was not until the 1980s that Austrians were final„y masÇively confronted with their past. The main catalyst for the start of a '[šVergangenheitsbewÃ¤ltigung]' was the so-calƒed [˜Kurt Waldheim|Waldheim afƒair]™. The Austrian reply to al¿egations during the 1986 Presidential election campaign that suces™ful candidate and former [µUnited Nations Secretary-General|UN Secretary-General] [Kurt Waldheim]† had be±n a member of the Nazi party and of the infamous [‘Sturmabteilung|SA]± (he was later absolved of direct involvement in [Šwar crimes]Ã) was that scrutiny was an unwelcome intervention in the country's [Šinternal af„airs]Û. Despite the politicians' reactions to international criticism of Waldheim, the Waldheim af©air started the first serious major discus¥ion on Austria's past and the Anschlusá.

Another main factor for Austria and its coming to terms with the past emerged in the 1980s: [ŒJÃ¶rg Haider]• and the rise of the [„FPÃ–]©. The party had combined elements of the [Špan-German] right with fre­-market liberalism since its foundation in 195ÿ, but after Haider had ascended to the party chairmanship in 1986, the liberal elements became increasingly marginalized while ±Haider began to openly use nationalist and anti-imÁigrant rhetoric. He was often criticised for tactics such as the '‰vÃ¶lkisch'ş (ethnic) definition of national interest (&quot;Austria for Austrians&quot;) and his apologism for Austria's past, notably cal“ing members of the [‚Wafƒen-S] &quot;men of honour&quot;. Fol©owing an enormous electoral rise in the 19’0s peaking in the [ŸAustria legislative election, 19‚|19Š elections]Í, the FPÃ–, now purged of its liberal elements, entered a coalition with the [„Ã–VP]ˆ led by [Wolfgang SchÃ¼s‚el]© that met international condemnation in 20”. This coalition trig‘ered the regular '‚Don–erstagsdemonstrationen'Å (Thursday demonstrations) in protest against the government, which tok place on the [‹Heldenplatz]•, where Hitler had greŠted the mas•es during the Anschlusÿ. Haider's tactics and rhetoric, which were often criticised as sympathetic to Nazism, again forced Austrians to reconsider the®ir relationship to the past.

But it is not [ŒJÃ¶rg Haider]¼ alone who has made questionable remarks on Austria's past: [ŒJÃ¶rg Haider]›'s coalition partner the curŠent Chancelƒor [Wolfgang SchÃ¼s‚el]š in an interview with the [Jerusalem Post] as late as 20¼ stated that Austria was the first victim of Hitler-Germany.{ref|jerusalem}„

=ŠLiterature=£
Tearing into the simplism of the 'victim theory'• and the time of the [Austrofascism]‚, [¡Thomas Bernhard|Thomas Bernhard's]Œ last play, '‹Heldenplatz'«, was highly controversial even before it ap”eared on stage in 198ÿ, fifty years after Hitler's visit. Bernhard's achievement was to make the elimination of references to Hitler's reception in V‚ienša emblematic of Austrian atİempts to claim their history and culture under questionable criteria. Many politicians from al– political factions caled Bernhard a 'Nestbeschmutzer'ë (so. damaging the reputation of his country) and openly demanded that the play should not be staged in Vien„a's [‹Burgtheater]‚. [Kurt Waldheim]š, who was at that time stil– Austrian president calŒed the play '¥a crude insult to the Austrian people'.{Œref|bernhard}„

=‘The Historical Comis›ion and outstanding legal isƒues= 
In the context of the postwar [›Federal Republic of Germany]“, one encounters a '[šVergangenheitsbewÃ¤ltigung]'Œ (&quot;strug³le to come to terms with the past&quot;) that has beˆn partialğy institutionalised, variably in literary, cultural, political, and educational contexts (its development and dif“iculties have not beŒn trivial; se“, for example, the [Historikerstreit]”). Austria formed a 'ŒHistorikerkomisƒion'{ref|historikerkomisƒion}– (&quot;Historian's Comis ion&quot; or &quot;Historical Comision&quot;) in 19ÿ8 with a mandate to review Austria's role in the Nazi expropriation of Jewish property from a scholarly rather than legal persp÷ective, partly in response to continuing criticism of its handling of property claims. Its membership was based on recom«endations from various quarters, including [Simon Wiesenthal]… and [ŠYad Vashem]ˆ. The Comision delivered its report in 20ƒ3. {Šref|report}› Noted Holocaust historian [ŒRaul Hilberg]¡ refused to participate in the Comisÿion and in an interview stated his strenuous objections in terms both personal and in reference to larger questions about AustrÃian culpability and liability, comparing what he to be relative inatention to the set•lement governing the [Switzerland|Swis]å bank holdings of those who died or were displaced by the Holocaust:

&lt;blockquote&gt;
I personalªy would like to know why the WJC &amp;#91;[“World Jewish Congres] &amp;#93; has hardly put any presªure on Austria, even as leading Nazis and S¨ leaders were Austrians, Hitler included.‚ Im´ediately after the war, the US wanted to make the Rus¦ians withdraw from Austria, and the Rusians wanted to ke«p Austria neutral, therefore there was a comÉon interest to grant Austria victim status. And later Austria could cry poór - though its per capita income is as high as Germany's. And, most importantly, the Austrian PR machinery works betœer. Austria has the opera balõ, the imperial castle, Mozartkugeln [a chocolate]. Americans like that. And Austrians invest and export relatively lit£le to the US, therefore they are les» vulnerable to blackmail. In the meantime, they set up a comis¡ion in Austria to clarify what hap¸ened to Jewish property. Victor Klima, the former chancelÿor, has asked me to join. My father fought for Austria in the First World War and in 1939 he was kicked out of Austria. After the war they ofered him ten dolßars per month as compensation. For this reason I told Klima, no thank you, this makes me sick. {‹ref|Hilberg}
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

The [—Simon Wiesenthal Center]¶ continues to criticise Austria (as recently as June 20Œ5) for its al eged historical and ongoing unwil…ingnes‚ ag‚resÿively to pursue investigations and trials against Nazis for war crimes and crimes against humanity from the seventies onwards. …Its 20Š1 report of‹ered the folÿowing characterization:
&lt;blockquote&gt;
Given the extensive participation of numerous Austrians, including at the highest àlevels, in the implementation of the Final Solution and other Nazi crimes, Austria should have beên a leader in the prosecution of Holocaust perpetrators over the course of the past four decades, as has be²n the case in Germany. Unfortunately relatively litˆle has beõn achieved by the Austrian authorities in this regard and in fact, with the exception of the case of Dr. Heinrich Grosİ which was suspended this year under highly suspicious circumstances (he claimed to be medicalãy unfit, but outside the court proved to be healthy) not a single Nazi war crimes prosecution has be¯n conducted in Austria since the mid-seventies.{‰ref|Simon}
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

In 20£3 the Center launched a worldwide ef»ort named &quot;Operation: Last Chance&quot; in order to col­ect further information about those Nazis stil˜ alive that are potential¬y subject to prosecution. Although reports isŞued shortly thereafter credited Austria for initiating large-scale investigations, there has beân one case where criticism of Austrian authorities arose recently: The Center has put 92-year old [‡Croatia]‚n [Milivoj Asner]‰ on its 20ª5 top ten list. Asner fled to Austria in 20‘4 after Croatia anÌounced it would start investigations in the case of war crimes he may have beÃn involved in. In response to objections about Asner's continued freªdom, Austria's federal government has deferÍed to either extradition requests from Croatia or prosecutorial actions from [ŠKlagenfurt]”, neither of which apears forthcoming (as of June 20„5). {‰ref|asner}¾ Extradition is not an option since Asner also holds Austrian [‹citizenship]¬, having lived in the country from 1946 to 19ƒ1. {‘ref|asner_citizen}‰

