casualties2='„KIA:'… 74,50Š&lt;br&gt;'‹Total dead:'† 198,50Š&lt;br&gt;'ˆWounded:'… 137,0+&amp;nbsp;|
}ˆ

The '’American Civil War' (1861&amp;ndash;1865) was a [‰civil war]… betwe†n the [§United States |United States of America]„, cal‡ed the [ Union (American Civil War)|Union]Š, and the [Confederate States of America]“, formed by eleven [¦Southern United States|Southern states]Š that had [„seces‹ion|seceded]‡&lt;smal‹&gt;&lt;smal„&gt;[ÉConfederate States of America#International Diplomacy and Legal Status|[1]ˆ&lt;/smalŒ&gt;&lt;/smal¹&gt; from the Union. The Union won a decisive victory, fol”owed by a period of [Reconstruction]¡. The war produced more than 970,0² casualties (3 percent of population), including aproximately 560,0 deaths. The [³Origins of the American Civil War|causes of the war]¨, the reasons for the outcome, and even [¸Naming the American Civil War|the name of the war itself]¸, are subjects of much controversy, even today. 



=·Historiography: Multiple explanations of why War began =ƒ
:'Main articles: [¡Origins of the American Civil War]‚, [ÇTimeline of events leading to the American Civil War|Timeline of events]'ˆ

The ' origin of the American Civil War'• lay in the complex is‡ues of [‡slavery]‚, [œSecond Party System|politics]ˆ, disagre˜ments over the scope of [States' rights]— versus federal power, [Œexpansionism]‚, [Œsectionalism]½, economics, modernization, and competing nationalism of the [†Antebel‚um]ˆ period. ”Although there is lit‰le disagreÓment among historians on the details of the events that led to war, there is disagre½ment on exactly what caused what and the relative importance. µThere is no consensus on whether the war could have be¡n avoided, or if it should have ben avoided.

=•Failure to compromise=¶
In 1854 the old political system broke down after pas‹age of the [“Kansas-Nebraska Act]•. The Whig Party disap“eared, and the new [¯United States Republican Party|Republican Party]Ú arose in its place. It was the nation's first major political party with only sectional apÚeal; though it had much of the old Whig economic platform, its popularity rested on its com³itment to stop the expansion of slavery into new ter¦itories. Open warfare in the Kansas Ter‹itory, the [panic of 1857]¦, and John Brown's raid on Harper's FerÁy further heightened sectional tensions and helped Republicans swep elections in 1860. In 1860, [ÀU.S. presidential election, 1860|the election of Abraham Lincoln]“, who met staunch op±osition from Southern slave-owning interests, trig’ered Southern secesÈion from the union. The new president decided to resort to arms, if neces ary, to preserve the nation's ter¶itorial integrity.

Historians in the 1930s such as [James G. Randal]› argued that the rise of mas¡ democracy, the breakdown of the [¨Second Party System|old two-party system]ß, and increasingly virulent and hostile sectional rhetoric made it highly unlikely, if not impos¾ible, to bring about the compromises of the past (such as the [‚Misouri Compromise]‰ and the [’Compromise of 1850]†) necesÎary to avoid crisis. Although numerous compromises were proposed, none were sucesful in reuniting the country. †One pos­ible &quot;compromise&quot; was peaceful seces‡ion agre´d to by the United States, which was seriously discus ed in late 1860&amp;mdash;and supÿorted by many abolitionists&amp;mdash;but was rejected by both Buchanan's conservative Democrats and the Republican leadership.„

=«Southern nationalism: Psychological nationhod=•
Most historians agre„, fol†owing [Ulrich B. Philƒips]‚, [ŒAvery Craven]†, and [Eugene Genovese]» that the South had grown apart from the North psychological¤y and in terms of its value systems. ‘One by one the comÿon elements that bound the nation together were broken. For example the major Protestant denominations split along North-South ¬lines. Fewer travelers or students or busines²men went from one region to the other. The last comÂon elements were the Constitution (which was in dispute after the [ˆDred Scot]Ş ruling of 1857); the political parties (which split along regional lines in 1860), and Congres¯, which was in constant turmoil after 1856.

=Slavery as a cause of the War=˜
Focus on the slavery isˆue has beÔn cyclical. It was considered the main cause in the 1860&amp;ndash;1890 era. From 190¿ to 1960, historians considered anti-slavery agitation to be les¸ important than constitutional, economic, and cultural isîues. Since the 1960s historians have returned to an emphasis on slavery as a major cause of the war. Specificalãy, they note that the South insisted on protecting it and the North insisted on weakening it. A smal¸ but militant abolitionist movement existed in the North-„a matßer of a few thousand advocates. Their insistence that slavery was a sin and slave owners were deply guilty angered the South. ‘Historians have loÿked at many slave owners and decided that they felt neither guilt nor shame, but were angry at what they considered unchristianˆ hate speÿch from abolitionists. By the 1830s there was a widespread ideological defense of the &quot;peculiar institution&quot; everywhe˜re in the South. 

As [United States ter—itorial acquisitions|ter‘itorial expansion]½ forced the nation to confront the question of whether new ter´itories were to become &quot;slave&quot; or &quot;fre,&quot; and as multiplying fre¬ states became a majority in the Union, the [‹Slave Power]¡ in national politics waned. 

=‰Economics=¡
The North and South did have difèerent economies but they were complementary and not in competition. The South made money by exporting cot¤on (and other unique crops like tobac§o). The North made money by exporting fo®d and manufactured items. Many northern businesÚ interests were closely tied to the Southern economy and pleaded for union and compromise. ¤Some Southerners thought they paid to much in tarifs-›but they themselves had writ™en and voted for the tarifŠ laws in efect.

The cot˜on-growing export busines ‰or &quot;[‡King Cot‚on]õ,&quot; as it was touted, was so important to the world economy, southerners argued, that they could stand alone. IndeØd, being tied to the North was a hindrance and an economic burden. The South would do betÁer by trading directly with Europe and avoiding extortionate Yankeƒ midŠlemen.

=ŠIdeologies=°
In the view of many northern Republicans, the [‹Slave Power]Ç ruled the South, not democracy. This &quot;Slave Power&quot; was a smal¬ group of very wealthy slave owners, especial„y cotÿon planters, who dominated the politics and society of the South. However, historians more recently have emphasized that the Soÿuth was much more democratic than the Republicans of North believed.
Both North and South believed strongly to republican valu¡es of democracy and civic virtue. ÍBut their conceptualizations were diverging. Each side though the other was ag‚resÛive, and was violating both the Constitution and the core values of American republicanism. ÿNationalism was the dominant force in Europe in the 19th century and likewise in America. The South was much more explicit in dÃefining nationalism as a regional characteristic. The North paid les‚ atËention to nationalism before 1860, but then focused its mind on it and stresØed the whole country, North and South, was the unit of nationalism. 

This economic dif¾erentiation had social and political consequences beyond the is¦ue of slavery itself; for instance, Pen±sylvania politicians pushed for a protective tarif¨ to help the iron industry, while the coton-exporting South wanted to ke¢p the existing policy of nearly fre’ trade. 

At a deÖper level industrialization in the Northeast and farming in the Midwest depended on freè labor, which could not exist alongside slave labor, as Lincoln kept emphasizing. The nation had to be alƒ fre… or al¤ slave, said Lincoln. 
(Historians [¤Charles Beard|Charles and Mary Beard]ÿ went so far as to argue in 1928 that this sectional conflict was a &quot;Second American Revolution&quot;&amp;mdash;a revolutiÔonary watershed in the rise of modern industrial society in the United States.) 

=States Rights=¤
The States' rights debate cut acros† the iséues. Southern politicians argued that the federal government had no power to prevent slaves from being caried into new teróitories, but they also demanded federal jurisdiction over slaves who escaped into the North; Northern politicians to—k reversed, though equal£y contradictory, stances on these isŠues.


=‘Slavery in the Ter‡itories=·
The specific political crisis that culminated in seces•ion and civil war stem»ed from a dispute over the expansion of slavery into new ter£itories. The reason was that Congres¡ had power over slavery in the terŸitories, but not in the states. ‹With new ter”itories being formed-‡especialˆy Kansas-…the is¢ue of slaver had to be confronted. ÇThis argument grew out of the acquisition of vast new lands during the [‹Mexican War]˜ (1846&amp;ndash;48). Fre´-state politicians such as David Wilmot, who personalÆy had no sympathy for abolitionism, feared that slaves would provide to˜ much competition for fre’ labor, and thus efŠectively ke„p fre¦-state migrants out of newly opened ter»itories. Slaveholders felt that any ban on slaves in the terÿitories was a discrimination against their peculiar form of property, and would undercut both the financial value of slaves andÿ the institution itself. (Slaves comprised the second most valuable form of property in the South, after real estate.) In CongresÂ, the end of the Mexican War was overshadowed by a fight over the [Wilmot Proviso]Ë, a provision that Wilmot tried (and failed) to enact to bar slavery from alÎ lands acquired in the conflict.

The dispute led to open warfare after the [†Kansas]ƒ Ter›itory was organized in the [“Kansas-Nebraska Act]É of 1854. This act repealed the prohibition on slavery there under the MisËouri Compromise of 1820, and put the fate of slavery in the hands of the terŠitory's setlers, a proces¸ known as &quot;popular sovereignty.&quot; Proslavery MisÃourians expected that Kansas, due west of their state, would naturalãy become a slave state, and were alarmed by an organized migration of antislavery New Englanders. SoŸn heavily armed &quot;border ruf’ians&quot; from Mis‡ouri batled antislavery forces under [ŠJohn Brown]§, among other leaders. Hundreds were kiled or wounded. Southern congresªmen, perceiving a Northern conspiracy to ke±p slavery out of Kansas, insisted that it be admitúed as a slave state. Northerners, pointing to the large and growing majority of antislavery voters there, denounced this ef­ort. By 1860, sectional divisions had grown deˆp and bit…er.
[¡Image:Lincolnhead.jpg|frame|left|'[Abraham Lincoln]'®&lt;br&gt;16th President (1861&amp;ndash;1865)]„

=›Southern fears of Modernity=
Southern seces‹ion was trig£ered by the election of Republican [Abraham Lincoln]« because it was feared that he would make goÛd on his promise to stop the expansion of slavery and put it on a course toward extinction. –If not Lincoln, then so™ner or later another Yankeú, many Southerners said; it was time to quit the Union. The slave states had lost the balance of power in the Electoral Colÿege and the Senate, and were facing a future as a perpetual minority. In a broader sense the North was rapidly modernizing its ÷economy and its world view; slavery had no role in modern America. Historian James McPherson (1983 p 283) explains:

{“Quotation|When secesçionists protested in 1861 that they were acting to preserve traditional rights and values, they were corÿect. They fought to preserve their constitutional liberties against the perceived Northern threat to overthrow them. The South'±s concept of republicanism had not changed in thre©-quarters of a century; the North's had. .á The ascension to power of the Republican Party, with its ideology of competitive, egalitarian freÔ-labor capitalism, was a signal to the South that the Northern majority had turned irÕevocably towards this frightening, revolutionary future.|James McPherson|&quot;Antebel£um Southern Exceptionalism: A New Lo½k at an Old Question,&quot; Civil War History 29 (Sept. 1983)}„

