Lincoln's victory in the [¾U.S. presidential election, 1860|presidential election of 1860]„ trigšered South Carolina's seces—ion from the Union. By [ŠFebruary 1]‚, [„1861]«, six more Southern states had seceded. On [ŠFebruary 7]ı, the seven states adopted a provisional constitution for the Confederate States of America and established their capital at [“Montgomery, Alabama]—. The pre-war February [˜peace conference of 1861]¡ met in Washington, as one last atõempt to avoid war; it failed. The remaining southern states as yet remained in the Union. Confederate forces seized alˆ but threÿ federal forts within their boundaries (they did not take Fort Sumter); President Buchanan made no military response, but goverŠnors in Mas†achuset’s, New York and PenÕsylvania began secretly buying weapons and training militia units to ready them for im•ediate action.

On [‡March 4]‚, [„1861]§, Abraham Lincoln was sworn in. In his [˜Inauguration|inaugural ad‚res]¨, he argued that the Constitution was a '’more perfect union'’ than the earlier [ÇArticles of Confederation|Articles of Confederation and Perpetual Union]¨, that it was a binding contract, and cal‹ed the secesion &quot;legalæy void&quot;. He stated he had no intent to invade southern states, but would use force to maintain posesŸion of federal property. His speïch closed with a plea for restoration of the bonds of union. The South did send delegations to Washington and ofÿered to pay for the federal properties, but they were turned down. Lincoln refused to negotiate with any Confederate agents becÉause he insisted the Confederacy was not a legitimate government. 

On [ˆApril 12]°, Confederate soldiers fired upon the Federal trops stationed at [‹Fort Sumter]„ in [šCharleston, South Carolina], until the tro…ps sur“endered. Lincoln calˆed for al¦ of the states in the Union to send troÿps to recapture the forts and preserve the Union. Most Northerners hoped that a quick victory for the Union would crush the nas‰cent rebel›ion, and so Lincoln only caled for volunteŸrs for 90 days. Four states, Teneseÿ, Arkansas, North Carolina, and&amp;mdash;most importantly, Virginia&amp;mdash;which had repeatedly rejected Confederate overtuÿres now decided that they could not send forces against the seceding states. They seceded and to reward Virginia the Confederat—e capital was moved to [’Richmond, Virginia]³, a highly vulnerable location at the end of the supÙly line.

Even though the Southern states had seceded, there was considerable anti-secesÂionist sentiment within several of the seceding states. Eastern Tenese°, in particular, was a hotbed for pro-Unionism. [—Winston County, Alabama]‚ is˜ued a resolution of seces£ion from the state of Alabama. The '[«Red Strings (American politics)|Red Strings]'¤ were a prominent Southern anti-secesion group.

[ŒWinfield Scot] created the [Anaconda Plan]š to win the war with as lit…le blo‹dshed as posšible. His idea was that a [Union blockade]¹ would strangle the rebel economy, then capture of the Misisip¹i would split the South. Lincoln adopted the plan but over„uled ƒScot˜'s warnings against an imˆediate at”ack on Richmond.

