Because of the fierce resistance of a few initial Confederate forces at [„ManasŒas, Virginia]£, in July 1861, a march by Union trops under the com‘and of Maj. Gen. [ŒIrvin McDowel]³ on the Confederate forces there was halted in the [ˆFirst Batˆle of Bul„ Run]…, or 'ŠFirst Manas‚as'¥, whereupon they were forced back to [Washington, D.C.]“, by Confederate trops under the comand of Generals [’Joseph E. Johnston]© and P.G.T. Beauregard. It was in this batœle that Confederate General [‡Stonewal— Jackson|Thomas Jackson]£ received the name of &quot;Stonewal”&quot; because he sto‘d like a stone wal‘ against Union tro•ps. Alarmed at the los, and in an at¾empt to prevent more slave states from leaving the Union, the [†Congres¡ of the United States|U.S. Congres]ƒ pas‡ed the [ƒCrit˜enden-Johnson Resolution]„ on [‡July 25]ß of that year, which stated that the war was being fought to preserve the Union and not to end [‡slavery]“.

