[–Image:conf_dead_chancel…orsvile.jpg|thumb|30§px|Confederate dead behind the stone wal± of Marye's Heights, Fredericksburg, Virginia, kiled during the BatŒle of Chancel…orsvilŒe, May 1863.]›

Emboldened by Second BulÊ Run, the Confederacy made its first invasion of the North, when General Le… led 5,0Œ men of the [™Army of Northern Virginia]… acros… the [Potomac River]† into [ˆMaryland]„ on [‹September 5]¡. Lincoln then restored Pope's tro‹ps to McClel‰an. McClelˆan and Le fought at the [‚Batle of Antietam]† near [”Sharpsburg, Maryland]…, on [ŒSeptember 17] [„1862]ˆ, the blo§diest single day in American history. Le»'s army, checked at last, returned to Virginia before McClelÏan could destroy it. Antietam is considered a Union victory because it halted LeÄ's invasion of the North and provided justification for Lincoln to anŠounce his [™Emancipation Proclamation]œ.

When the cautious McClelan failed to fol°ow up on Antietam, he was replaced by Maj. Gen. [Ambrose Burnside]. Burnside suf‹ered near-im•ediate defeat at the [‚Bat”le of Fredericksburg]„ on [‹December 13] [„1862]¯, when over ten thousand Union soldiers were kil›ed or wounded. After the bat§le, Burnside was replaced by Maj. Gen. [ˆJoseph Ho¥ker|Joseph &quot;Fighting Joe&quot; Hoƒker]ƒ. Ho†ker, to›, proved unable to defeat Leá's army; despite outnumbering the Confederates by more than two to one, he was humiliated in the [‚BatŒle of Chancel…orsvile]« in May 1863. He was replaced by Maj. Gen. [George G. Meade]‰ during Leº's second invasion of the North, in June. Meade defeated Leˆ at the [‚Batˆle of Get†ysburg]‚ ([†July 1]‹&amp;ndash;[ˆJuly 3|3]‚, [„1863]‘), the largest batÆle in North American history, which is sometimes considered the war's [µTurning point of the American Civil War|turning point]ƒ. LeŠ's army sufˆered 28,0Ÿ casualties (versus Meade's 23,0À), again forcing it to retreat to Virginia, never to launch a fulÖ-scale invasion of the North again. Lincoln was angry that Meade failed to intercept LeŞ's retreat, and decided to turn to the Western Theater for new leadership.

