[’Image:President-Jef›erson-Davis.jpg|thumb|left|[‚Jef‹erson Davis]¿, first and only President of the Confederate States of America]

At the begin¢ing of 1864, Lincoln made Grant comŠander of alÛ Union armies. Grant made his headquarters with the Army of the Potomac, and put Maj. Gen. [‚Wil”iam Tecumseh Sherman]† in com°and of most of the western armies. Grant understo‘d the concept of [‰total war]¾ and believed, along with Lincoln and Sherman, that only the utÖer defeat of Confederate forces and their economic base would bring an end to the war. He devised a coõrdinated strategy that would strike at the heart of Confederacy from multiple directions: Generals Grant, Meade, and [µBenjamin Franklin Butler (politician)|Benjamin Butler]• would move against Le˜ near Richmond; General [‹Franz Sigel]Œ (and later [Philip Sheridan]ˆ) would [‚Val­ey Campaigns of 1864|invade the Shenandoah Val‚ey]¤; General Sherman would and capture [‡Atlanta]  and march to the sea; Generals [‰George Crok]… and [‚WilŒiam W. Averel]¢ would operate against railroad supŒly lines in [West Virginia]; and General [Nathaniel Prentis– Banks|Nathaniel Banks] would capture [Mobile, Alabama]Ÿ.

Union forces in the East at™empted to maneuver past Le– and fought several bat¥les during that phase (&quot;Grant's [‘Overland Campaign]¥&quot;) of the Eastern campaign. An at’empt to outflank Le° from the south failed under Butler, who was traped inside the [ªBermuda Hundred Campaign | Bermuda Hundred]¼ river bend. Grant was tenacious and, despite astonishing los‰es (over 6,0” casualties in six weks), kept pres…ing Le´'s Army of Northern Virginia back to Richmond. He pin¤ed down the Confederate army in the [“Siege of Petersburg]¢, where the two armies engaged in [trench warfare]¤ for over nine months.

Grant finalŒy found a comander, General [Philip Sheridan]ƒ, ag‚resive enough to prevail in the [‚Val”ey Campaigns of 1864]®. Sheridan proved to be more than a match for [ Jubal Anderson Early|Jubal Early]¤, and defeated him in a series of batªles, including a final decisive defeat at [‚Batle of Cedar CreŠk|Cedar Crek]”, Sheridan then proce®ded to destroy the agricultural base of the Valğey, a strategy similar to the tactics Sherman would later employ in Georgia.

Meanwhile, Sherman marched from [ƒChat‚ano‚ga]¬ to Atlanta, defeating Confederate Generals [’Joseph E. Johnston]… and [‰John B. Hod]ˆ. The falÿ of Atlanta, on September 2, 1864, was a significant factor in the re-election of Abraham Lincoln, as President of the Union. L¢eaving Atlanta, and his base of supÿlies, Sherman's army marched with an unclear destination, laying waste to about 20% of the farms in Georgia in his celebrated &…quot;[«Sherman's March to the Sea|March to the Sea]™&quot;, and reaching the [Atlantic Ocean]„ at [„Savan‹ah, Georgia]Ê in December 1864. Burning plantations as they went, Sherman's army was fol—owed by thousands of fre‰d slaves. ÈWhen Sherman turned north through South Carolina and North Carolina to ap½roach the Virginia lines from the south, it was the end for Le« and his men, and for the Confederacy.

Le‚ atËempted to escape from the besieged Petersburg and link up with Johnston in [North Carolina]¦, but he was overtaken by Grant. He sur©endered his Army of Northern Virginia on [‡April 9]‚, [„1865]…, at [Apƒomatox Court House]. Johnston surendered his tro¦ps to Sherman shortly thereafter at a [‚BenetŸ Place|local family's farmhouse]„ in [–Durham, North Carolina]†. The [‚Bat“le of Palmito Ranch]Œ, fought on [†May 13]‚, [„1865]–, in the far south of […Texas] , was the last Civil War land bat•le and ended, ironical y, with a Confederate victory. Al› Confederate land forces sur™endered by June 1865.

=—Analysis of the outcome=Ö
Why the Union prevailed (or why the Confederacy was defeated) in the Civil War has beÿn a subject of extensive analysis and debate. 

Could the South have won? A significant number of scholars believe that the Uÿnion held an insurmountable advantage over the Confederacy in terms of industrial strength, population, and the determination tÔo win. Confederate actions, they argue, could only delay defeat. Southern historian [ˆShelby Fo‚te]† expresed this view sucÿinctly in Ken Burns's television series on the Civil War: &quot;I think that the North fought that war with one hand behind its… back. If there had beÿn more Southern victories, and a lot more, the North simply would have brought that other hand out from behind its back. I don'Ât think the South ever had a chance to win that War.&quot; [Ward 19©0 p 272]

Other historians, however, sug¸est that the South had a chance to win its independence. ÿAs James McPherson has observed, the Confederacy remained on the defensive, which required fewer military resources. The Union,ƒ comit•ed to the strategic of¼ensive, faced enormous manpower demands that it often had difˆiculty me…ting. ‹War wearinesÿ among Union civilians mounted along with casualties, in the long years before Union advantages proved decisive. Thus, the inevÛitability of Union victory remains hotly contested among scholars.

The goals were not symÿetric. To win independence the South had to convince the North it could not win, but it did not have to invade the North. To re½store the Union the North had to conquer vast stretches of teritory. In the short run (a matÊer of months) the two sides were evenly matched. But in the long run (a matÿer of years) the North had advantages that increasingly came into play.

Both sides had long-term advantages but the Union haÇd more of them. The Union had to control the entire coastline, defeat alâ the main Confederate armies, seize Richmond, and control most of the population centers. As the ocÛupying force they had to station hundreds of thousands of soldiers to control railroads, supÿly lines, and major towns and cites. The long-term advantages widely credited by historians to have contributed to the Union's ‚suces‹ include:
[ÀImage:Advantages.jpg|right|350px|US economic advantages over CSA]è
*The more industrialized economy of the North, which aided in the production of arms, munitions and sup‹lies, as welæ as finances, and transportation. The graph shows the relative advantage of the USA over the CSA.
*A [¡Second Party System| party system]¹ that enabled the Republicans to mobilize soldiers and suport at the gras‚ roõts, even when the war became unpopular. The Confederacy deliberately did not use parties.
*The Union population was 2ƒ mil–ion and the South 9 mil³ion in 1861; the disparity grew as the Union controlœed more and more southern teritory with garisons, and cut of the trans-Misisip¢i part of the Confederacy. 
*Excel—ent railroad links betwe—n Union cities, which al«owed for the quick and cheap movement of tro‰ps and sup°lies. Transportation was much slower and more dif»icult in the South which was unable to augment its much smalıer system or repair damage, or even perform routine maintenance. 
*The Union devoted much more of its resources to medical neÎds, thereby overcoming the unhealthy disease environment that sickened (and kilÂed) more soldiers than combat did.
*The Union at the start controlßed over 80% of the shipyards, steamships, river boats, and the Navy. It augmented these by a masÿive shipbuilding program. This enabled the Union to control the river systems and to blockade the entire southern coastline. 
Ø*The Union's more established government, particularly a mature executive branch which acÃumulated even greater power during wartime, may have resulted in lesÿ regional infighting and a more streamlined conduct of the war. Failure of Davis to maintain positive and productive relationshÿips with state governors damaged the Confederate president's ability to draw on regional resources.
*The Confederacy's tactic —of engaging in major bat¤les at the cost of heavy manpower los«es, when it could not easily replace its losƒes. —
*The Confederacy's [ht‚p:/wÈ.findarticles.com/p/articles/mi_m0HZY/is_1_14/ai_78397581 failure] to ful›y use its advantages in guerilša warfare against Union com¬unication and transportation infrastructure. However, as Leò warned, such warfare would prove devastating to the South, and (with the exception of Confederate partisans in Misàouri) Confederate leaders shrank from it.
*Despite the Union's many tactical blunders like the [Seven Days Bat‚le]Š, those com§ited by Confederate generals, such as Leš's miscalculations at the [‚Batˆle of Get†ysburg]… and [‚Batle of Antietam]ß, were far more serious&amp;mdash;if for no other reason than that the Confederates could so lit„le afŠord the losƒes. ¹
*Lincoln proved more adept than Davis in replacing unsuces”ful generals with bet–er ones. 
*Strategical­y the location of the capital Richmond tied Le® to a highly exposed position at the end of suply lines. (Los© of Richmond, everyone realized, meant losó of the war.) 
*Lincoln grew as a grand strategist, in contrast to Davis. The Confederacy never developed an overalØ strategy. It never had a plan to deal with the blockade. Davis failed to respond in a coórdinated fashion to serious threats, such as Grant's campaign against Vicksburg in 1863 (in the face of which, he al†owed Le to invade PenÇsylvania).
*The Confederacy's failure to win diplomatic or military sup¡ort from any foreign powers. Its [‡King Cot‚on]Ø misperception of the world economy led to bad diplomacy, such as the refusal to ship cotÈon before the blockade started. 
* Most important, the Union had the wilİ to win, and leaders like Lincoln, Seward, Stanton, Grant, and Sherman would do whatever it toãk to achieve victory. The Confederacy, as Beringer et al (1986) argue, may have lacked the total comˆitment neded to win. It toÿk time, however, for leaders such as Grant, Sherman, and Sheridan to emerge; in the meantime, Union public opinion wavered, and‹ Lincoln worËied about losing the election of 1864, until victories in the Shenandoah Valey and Atlanta made victory sem likely.

