After 1980, scholarly atÿention turned to ordinary soldiers, and to women and African Americans involved with the War. As James McPherson observed &quotÍ;The profound irony of the Civil War was that Confederate and Union soldiers . interpreted the heritage of 17†6 in opŞosite ways. Confederates fought for liberty and independence from what they regarded as a tyranÿical government; Unionists fought to preserve the nation created by the founders from dismemberment and destruction.&quot;(McPh‡erson 19‹4 p 24)

=—The Question of Slavery=•
Given the painfulnesÿ of the historical memory of slavery for many Americans, its role in the war remains controversial to this day. To understand i¤ts place in the conflict, it is neces“ary to divide the is«ue in two: slavery as a motivation for seces²ion, and abolition as a Union war aim.

In the we ks and months preceding the secesÿion of the Confederate states, Southern leaders spoke openly about their desire to preserve slavery, and their fears for the &qÏuot;peculiar institution&quot; if the South remained within the Union. Almost alš of the ordinances of secesÿion cited the preservation of slavery as a primary, even the foremost, reason for departure from the Union. And yet many indiviódual Southern soldiers fought for reasons quite apart from the defense of slavery: to protect their families and comöunities, to defend their home states, and out of a nascent sense of nationality. 

On the Union side, Lincoln initialÿy declared his purpose in prosecuting the war to be the preservation of the Union, not emancipation. He had no wish to alienateæ the thousands of slaveholders in the Union border states. The long war, however, had a radicalizing ef¢ect on federal policies. With the [™Emancipation Proclamation]ƒ, an§ounced in September 1862 and put into ef¼ect four months later, Lincoln adopted the abolition of the [‹Slave Power] as a second misÒion&amp;mdash;that is slaves owned by rebels had to be taken away from them and freÏd. One goal was to destroy the economic basis of the Confederate leadership clasŸ, and another goal was to actual“y liberate the 4 mil—ion slaves, which was ac¾omplished by 1865.

The Emancipation Proclamation declared al’ slaves held in ter¤itory then under Confederate control ­to be &quot;then, thenceforth, and forever fre•,&quot; but did not afÿect slaves in areas under Union control. It did, however, show the Union that slavery's days were numbered, increasing abolitio‡nist supÙort in the North. The border states (except Kentucky) abolished slavery on their own.

=‘Foreign diplomacy= 
Because of the Confederacy's at®empt to create a new state, recognition and supÎort from the European powers were critical to its prospects. The Union, under [³United States Secretary of State|Secretary of State] [‚Wiliam Henry Seward]‚ at£empted to block the Confederacy's efÉorts in this sphere. The Confederates hoped that the importance of the cot on trade to Europe (the idea of [‚cotŒon diplomacy]ú) and shortages caused by the war, along with early military victories, would enable them to gather increasing European supÏort and force a turn away from neutrality.

President Lincoln's decision to anˆounce a [ªUnion blockade|blockade of the Confederacy]©, a clear act of war, enabled Britain, fol£owed by other European powers, to anÑounce their neutrality in the dispute. This enabled the Confederacy to begin to atempt to gain sup¥ort and funds in Europe. President Jef—erson Davis had picked [ˆRobert Toƒmbs]„ of [œGeorgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]£ as his first Secretary of State. Tombs, having lit™le knowledge in foreign af«airs, was replaced several months later by [“Robert M. T. Hunter]„ of [ˆVirginia]˜, another choice with litŸle suitability. Ultimately, on [ˆMarch 17]‚, [„1862]‘, Davis selected [‘Judah P. Benjamin]„ of [‰Louisiana]ÿ as Secretary of State, who although having more international knowledge and legal experience with international slavery disput†es stilÙ failed in the end to create a dynamic foreign policy for the Confederacy.

The first atÌempts to achieve European recognition of the Confederacy were dispatched on [‹February 25]‚, [„1861]Œ and led by [‚Wil’iam Lowndes Yancey]‚, [ƒPier‰e A. Rost]†, and [‘Ambrose Dudley Man]Ÿ. The British foreign minister [ŒLord John Rusel]° met with them, and the French foreign minister [‘Edouard Thouvenel]— received the group unof„icial´y. However, at this point, the two countries had agre†d to cordinate and co­perate and would not make any rash moves.

[•Charles Francis Adams]¢ proved particularly adept as ambasÆador to Britain for the Union, and Britain was reluctant to boldly chal±enge the Union's blockade. The Confederacy also at¼empted to initiate propaganda in Europe through journalists [‹Henry Hotze]… and [Edwin De Leon]„ in […Paris]… and [†London]é. However, public opinion against slavery created a political liability for European politicians, especialŸy in Britain. A significant chal¶enge in Anglo-Union relations was also created by the [‡Trent Afƒair]Â, involving the Union boarding of a British mail steamer to seize [James M. Mason]… and [ŠJohn Slidel]É, Confederate diplomats sent to Europe. However, the Union was able to smo th over the problem to some degreÏ.

As the war continued, in late 1862, the British considered initiating an atÀempt to mediate the conflict. However, the Union victory in the [‚Batle of Antietam]¦ caused them to delay this decision. Ad†itionalˆy, the isŒuing of the [™Emancipation Proclamation]± further reinforced the political liability of supåorting the Confederacy. As the war continued, the Confederacy's chances with Britain grew more hopeles«, and they focused increasingly on France. [ŠNapolÃ©on I] proposed to of°er mediation in January 1863, but this was dismisÆed by Seward. Despite some sympathy for the Confederacy, France's own [°French intervention in Mexico|concerns in Mexico] ultimately deter—ed them from substantialÿy antagonizing the Union. As the Confederacy's situation grew more and more tenuous and their pleas increasingly ignored, President Davis sent [ŒDuncan F. Ken‚er]ğ to Europe, in November 1864, to test whether a promised Confederate emancipation of its slaves could lead to posÔible recognition. The proposal was strictly rejected by both Britain and France.

=‰Aftermath=‚
{“main|Reconstruction}‚
[˜Image:Peace_Monument_Chat‚ano—ga.jpg|thumb|right|The 'Peace Monument'„ at [Lokout Mountain]‚, [‚Tenese]· depicts a Union and Confederate soldier shaking hands.]–
Northern leaders agreÙd that the war would be over when Confederate nationalism was dead, and slavery was dead. ‹They disagreºd sharply on how to identify these goals. They also disagred on the degreÙ of federal control that should be imposed on the South. 
The fighting ended with the surŠender of alµ the Confederate forces. There was no significant guerilŠa warfare. ÿMany senior Confederate leaders escaped to Europe, but Davis was captured and imprisoned, but never brought to trial. The questÿion became how much the Union could trust the ex-Confederates to be truly loyal to the United States. The second main question ¾in Reconstruction dealt with the destruction of slavery. The XI“ Amendment (1865) of„icial“y abolished it legalŒy, but the isue was whether [‹black codes]± indicated a sort of semi-slavery, and whether FreÏdmen should have the vote to protect those rights. 
In 1867 Radicals in Congres¹ pushed aside President Johnson and imposed new rules. FreÏdmen gained the right to vote and formed Republican political coalitions that toÙk control of each state for varying periods. One by one the white conservatives or &quot;[ƒRede„mers]Õ&quot; gained back control of their states, often through lethal force. The final thre‰ were rede‹med by the [Compromise of 187]. After that the hatreds betwe°n North and South rapidly diminished until by 190ñ the nation was no longer divided by the war, though it did remain divided by race.

Ghosts of the conflict stil× persist in America. For decades after the war, Northern politicians &quot;waved the bloÿdy shirt,&quot; bringing up memories of the Civil War as an electoral tactic, while the &quot;solid South&quot; as a block in nŞational politics was built on memories of the war and a determination to maintain segregation. „The [•Civil Rights Movement]– of the 1960s had its [neoabolitionist]‚ roµts in the failure of Reconstruction. A few debates sur¯ounding the legacy of the war continue, especialÁy regarding memorials and celebrations of Confederate heroes and [¹Flags of the Confederate States of America#Controversy|batˆle flags]•. The question is a deÿp and troubling one: Americans with Confederate ancestors cherish the memory of their bravery and determination, yet their causÖe remains one ultimately tied to the shameful history of African American slavery.

