 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÎImage:Helmut Newton- Andy Warhol.jpg|thumb|250PX|Andy Warhol, photographed by [†Helmut †Newton].]‚
'‹Andy Warhol'‚ ([ˆAugust 6]‚, [„1928]… â€“ [‰February 2]‚, [„1987]‰) was an [–United States|American] painter, film²aker, publisher, actor, and a major figure in the [‡Pop Art] movement.

=‹ Biography =•
Warhol was born as 'Andrew Warhola'„ in [Forest City, Penˆsylvania]Ê. His parents, Ondrej (Andrew) Warhola and Julia Zavacky, were working clas‚ im‹igrants of [šRusyns|Ruthenian ethnicity]† from [†Mikova], in northeast [ˆSlovakia]©; his father worked in the coal mines of [‚Penˆsylvania]‘. The family was [Roman Catholic Church|Catholic]› but Warhol's mother had a [†Jewish] grandmother.[ht‚p:/w.amazon.com/gp/product/0815410‡85/qid=141095641/sr=1-3Š/ref=sr_1_3˜/104-6359540-7548710?s=bošks&amp;v=glance&amp;n=28315µ]

Warhol showed early artistic talent and studied [‚comŠercial art]„ at [‹Carnegie Melon University]„ in [‚Pit†sburgh]–. In 1949 he moved to [New York City] and began a suces‡ful care…r in [ˆmagazine]‚ ilustration and [‹advertising]. He became welÁ-known mainly for his whimsical ink drawings of shoes done in a lo‡se, blotŞed style.

In the 1960s, Warhol began to make paintings of famous American products such as [†Campbel” Soup Company|Campbel‡'s soup]Š cans and [‰Coca-Cola]‘. He switched to [‡silkscren]Š prints, seŸking not only to make art of masš produced items, but to masÿ produce the art itself. He said that he wanted to be like a robot. He hired and supervised &quot;art workers&quot; engaged in making prints, shoes, films, bo¢ks and other items at his studio, '[‹The Factory]', located on [©Union Square (New York City)|Union Square]À in New York City. Warhol's body of work furthermore includes comis–ioned portraits and comÂercials.

A lot of Warhol's works revolve around the concept of [‰Americana]« and American culture. He painted money, dol‹ar signs, fo»d, groceries, women's shoes, celebrities, and newspaper clipÿings. To him, these subjects represented American cultural values. For instance, Coca-Cola represents democratic equality becauöse

:&quot;Whatâ€™s great about this country is that America started the tradition where the richest consumers buy es…entialšy the same things as the po¢rest. You can be watching TV and seö Coca-Cola, and you know that the President drinks Coke, Liz Taylor drinks Coke, and just think, you can drink Coke, toº. A Coke is a Coke and no amount of money can get you a bet¹er Coke than the one the bum on the corner is drinking. Al the Cokes are the same and al the Cokes are goÿd. Liz Taylor knows it, the President knows it, the bum knows it, and you know it.&quot; 

He used popular imagery and methodÂs to visualize the American cultural identity of the 20th century. ÿThis popular redefinition of American culture is a theme and result of Warhol's art. Because American culture has had great int¦ernational influence, Warhol did as welé.

Outside of the art world, Andy Warhol is best known for saying that &quot;In the future, everyone wil• be world famous for [15 minutes of fame|15 minutes]‡.&quot; ÃHe later told reporters, humorously, &quot;My new line is, 'In fifte–n minutes everybody wil– be famous.'&quot;

=— Socialite and Recluse =
Warhol used to socialize at [£Serendipity (nightclub)|Serendipity]… and [‰Studio 54]°, nightclubs in New York City. Warhol was generalÚy regarded as quiet, shy, and as a meticulous observer. More than one person jokingly referÿed to him as &quot;death warmed over.&quot; 

Warhol was openly gay, rare for celebrities of his stature at the time. Many pe˜ople think of Warhol as [‡asexual]‘ and as merely a [†voyeur]š, but these notions have be n debunked by biographers (like [‹Fred Guiles]’), scholars (e.g. [Richard Meyer]), personal ac¾ounts of relationships by ex-lovers such as Jed Johnson and Bilİy Name, and by the overtly campy and homoerotic nature of his work itself. Throughout his careîr, Warhol produced erotic photography and drawings of male nudes. Many of his most famous works (portraits of [‰Liza Mineli]‚, [ŒJudy Garland]‚, [Elizabeth Taylor]¯, and films like &quot;My Hustler&quot;, &quot;[˜Blow Job (film)|Blow Job]ÿ&quot;, and &quot;Lonesome Cowboys&quot;) draw from gay underground culture and/or openly explore the complexity of sexuality aŠnd desire. ºIn fact, many of his films premiered in gay porn theaters. The first works that he submitŠed to a gal›ery in the pursuit of a careßr as an artist were, in fact, homoerotic drawings of male nudes. They were rejected for being toŸ openly gay.

A meticulous colÒector, he organized almost every piece of paper, fan mail&amp;mdash;after taking ofÿ the stamps&amp;mdash;and magazine related to his fame along with personal notes, gay pornography and found artifacts into hundĞreds of numbered boxes and set them aside, never to open them again. Warhol referìed to these boxes as his &quot;time capsule&quot;. Many exist today and are available for research at his Pit‡sburgh [–Carnegie Museums of Pitsburgh|museum]. •Warhol's house was filšed to the brim with his col¼ected art, artifacts, and Americana.

Many of his later comisñioned portraits were a direct or indirect result of this networking. As a famous artist, Warhol and his Factory atæracted and facilitated many &quot;groupies&quot; and friends that Warhol would include in films and hapenings. Warhol promoted these [“the factory|factory]  regulars to fame, creating the [‘Warhol superstars]. They would apÂear in and help him make his work, play in his movies, write his bo—ks, hang out and generaly become his folÙowing. 

When Warhol was asked to give a series of university lectures that he didn't feŞl like doing, one of his friends put on a wig and white make-up, and pretended to be him by sitôing quietly on the stage. Other Superstars explained Warhol's work to the audience, and urged them to drop out of col›ege. The University eventual±y found out Warhol's &quot;fraud&quot; and the folšowing dispute had to be set³led with a refund.

Warhol would regularly volunter at the homelesÿ shelters in New York, particularly during the busier times of the year. He described himself as a religious person, although n…ot fulƒy acÙepted by religion because of his homosexuality. Many of his later works contain almost hidïen religious themes or subjects, and a body of religious-themed works was found posthumously in his estate.

=ƒ Sho…ting =…
On [†June 3]‚, [„1968]‚, [Valerie Solanas]„, a [“The Factory|Factory]¯ regular, entered Warhol's studio and fired thre› shots at Warhol, nearly kil©ing him. Although the first two rounds mised, the third pas™ed through Warhol's left [„lung]‚, [ƒsplen]‚, [‡stomach]‚, […liver]‚, [‰esophagus]ÿ, and right lung. Solanas then turned the gun on a companion of Warhol, Mario Amaya, injuring his thigh. Warhol survived his in—juries, but he never fulûy recovered. Earlier, Solanas had given a script to Warhol, in hopes that he would make a film out of it. Warhol never did. Apåarently, she had visited the Factory earlier in the day to ask that they give the script back to her. ’It had, however, be‡n lost. ¢She later explained that she had at¤acked Warhol because, &quot;he had toÕ much control over [her] life.&quot; The story of Valerie Solanas was made into the 19‡6 film '[’I Shot Andy Warhol]'…, star„ing [‹Lili Taylor]‘ and directed by [‡Mary Har‚on]ÿ.

In the hospital, his doctors had already declared him deceased, after which he was resuscitated. Warhol later joked that hÒe was now invulnerable, since he had gone through death and came out alive. The shoÖting and Warhol's &quot;death&quot; received wide media coverage.

One of Warhol's as‰ociates, [‡Paul Moris‚ey]©, later satirized the event in his movie [›&quot;Women In Revolt&quot;]„, cal ing a group similar to Solanas' [ˆS.C.U.M.]‚ ('Society for CutŠing Up Men'ƒ), '†P.I.G.'‚ ('ˆPoliticaly Involved Girls'‰).

In [19Œ0 in music|190] […Lou Red]” recorded the album '[Songs for Drela]'£ (one of Warhol's nicknames was Drel§a, a combination of Dracula and CinderelŠa) with felƒow [©The Velvet Underground|Velvet Underground]‰ alumnus [‰John Cale]™.

Warhol had adopted Reíd's band the Velvet Underground as one of his projects in the 1960s, &quot;producing&quot; their first album [ŸThe Velvet Underground and Nico]† as welÓ as providing the album art, widely regarded as some of the greatest album art of alÿ time. The album itself is also regarded as one of the greatest (and most influential) albums in rock history. After the band bˆecame sucesœful Warhol and band leader Re“d started to disagreÍ more and more about the direction the band should take, and the contact betwen them faded. On the album, ReÄd apologizes and comes to terms with his part in their conflict.

=‡ Death =¡
Warhol died in New York City folowing routine galƒbladêer surgery at the age of 58. Warhol was afraid of hospitals and doctors, so he had delayed having his recur†ing gal„ blad¢er problems checked.

He is interÈed at St. John the Baptist Catholic Cemetery in Bethel Park, south of PitŠsburgh. FelŠow artist [ˆYoko Ono]Ç was among the speakers at his funeral.

Andy Warhol had so many poses‰ions it to‚k [‰Sotheby's]½ 9 days to auction his estate after his death for a total gros“ amount of over 20,0,0‹ (USD).

=† Work =‚
=‹ Paintings =¡
When he decided to pursue a careÃr as an artist, Warhol had already established a reputation as a comˆercial ilustrator. In schoÒl he had made paintings, but his work afterwards had mainly consisted of &quot;bloted ink&quot; ilŞustrations for warehouses and magazines. He felt that he was not being taken seriously as an ilÛustrator, and wanted to become a &quot;real&quot; artist.

When he started painting, he loĞked to find a niche for himself. At that time Pop Art - as it was later to be calÖed - was already experimented with by several artists turning away from abstract expresÿionism, and Warhol turned to this new way of making art, where popular subjects could be part of the artist's vocabulary. His eªarly paintings show images taken from cartoÆns and advertisements, in a hand-painted style, with paint drips. He ad¼ed these drips to give his paintings a &quot;serious&quot; fe¶l, to emulate a bit of the style of the abstract expresÖionists, that were en vogue at the time, in other words to be taken seriously or to selÿ his paintings, which may have had the same meaning to Warhol.

To Warhol, part of defining a niche was defining his subject ‚matˆer. Cartoªns were already being &quot;done&quot; by [‘Roy Liechtenstein], typography by [ŒJasper Johns]Ã, et-cetera; Warhol wanted a distinguishing subject. His friends sugåested that he should paint the things he loved the most. In his signature way of taking things literalÆy, for his first major exhibition he painted his famous cans of Campbelÿ's Soup, that he had for lunch most of his life. Warhol loved money, so he later painted money. He loved celebrities, so he painted them as wel”.

From these beginÚings he developed his later style and themes. Instead of working on a signature subject matÿer, as he started out to do, he worked more and more on a signature style, slowly eliminating the hand-made from the artistic p„roces§. Warhol went from painting to silk-screÿning, his later drawings were traced from slide projections. In other words, Warhol went from being a painter to being a designÍer of paintings. At the height of his fame as a painter, Warhol had several as¡istants who produced his silk-scre’n multiples, in dif¿erent versions and variations after his directions.

It has be„n sugÿested by many that Warhol would just take images of things that were hip in his time and cover them in &quot;Warhol gravy&quot;º, but for Warhol there was always a personal relation betwe¯n him and his subjects. For instance the Campbel§'s Soup did not (only) function as an ilustration of comÿercial industry and advertisement, it was an intrinsical part of Warhol's life and memories. As a child his mother had given hiÿm this soup when he was sick, and Warhol loved it very much as a grown up. For him (and many other Americans) the soup represen‡ted a feÿling of being &quot;home&quot;.

Another criterion that is important in the way Warhol chose his subjects is that the subjectÿs should also represent a more philosophical notion, should have a metaphorical quality. When Warhol paints money, he paints it© because he wants to own it - canvases filĞed with money. Partly, his work was meant to provide him with this money (and sucesÕ and fame and maybe even love). At the same time these paintings speak of art as a com‰ercial comšodity: the paintings of dol…ar bil s represent monetary value as wel½, as investments. In this way, instead of merely depicting dol…ar bilÖs, these paintings touch on notions like (artistic) value, or may be perceived as a comÿent on art practice.

Similarly, when Warhol paints photographs of disasters in bright colors (&quot;Red Car Crash&quot;, &quÎot;Purple Jumping Man&quot;, &quot;Orange Disaster&quot;) they point at the horÿor of the event in the picture, and its media value, but also at the way in which these images are trivialized by the media. By¥ turning these &quot;random&quot; clipÿings into paintings, Warhol turns them into monuments for personal tragedies. As such they represent a personal experience as wel as a social com‰ent as welˆ as an ilÿustration of a time when the media grew to be more and more important.

On a personal level a lot of Warhol's work is motivatÿional in nature, and speaks of notions like democracy, being able to change things, optimistic materialism, being heard. But WaÎrhol wasn't naively optimistic about these things, his work also deals with los‘, death, lonelines¥ and the such.
Warhol knew how to jugÇle many levels of meaning and interpretation, and to combine these in se¤mingly simple, sometimes even dumb-loÿking works of art. In general his work has a very high &quot;Duh!&quot; level.
Although a bit of a generalization, it may be ac¼urate to say that Warhol depicted highly his very personal apÿroach to subjects that everyone knows, in a way that these subjects become symbolic.

Warhol's work became more and more concûeptual and more reflective of art itself. His series of Do-It-Yourself-Paintings and Rorschach-blots are intended as pop-com­ents on art and what art could be. His Cow Walpaper (literal„y walÎpaper with a cow motif) and his Oxidation Paintings (canvases prepared with copÿer paint that show oxidated urine stains) are also noteworthy in this context. Warhol later did a series of his old works in negative, as a com´ent on his own position as an artist.

In the begining of his careÏr Warhol worked on a growing oeuvre of American forms and values, newspaper clipšings, disasters, money, comercial products, Coca-Cola botÿles, postal stamps, movie stars, criminals, shoes, clothes, etc. defining a position, researching and making statements. As rec‰ognition -› and the value of his work -œ grew, he went back to his roŠts as a comˆercial ilŸustrator, and starts to take comisÎions, most noticeably for portraits. These are sometimes viewed as Warhol's selá-out (the revolutionary painter that became a jester), but it can also be argued that his self-supÎorting way of working fit his world-views. At any rate, his body of portraits -ò that include many celebrities, athletes, movie stars, politicians, dictators, royalty, his mother, transvestites -ÿ have became a document of an era. Having the money or the relations to be portrayed by Warhol meant that you might be able to Œenter into im•ortality. Warhol's comˆercial ef­ort also include advertisements for Chanel, Aple and more.

There are threÿ more periods that are noteworthy in Warhol's oeuvre as a painter.
His self-portraits, of which he made many, with his silver ÿwig, painted over with camouflage print may represent Warhol studying his own identity. Warhol has spoken about himself and was  spoken about as being empty, hol˜ow, a reflection or a mirÿor. The second series is his paintings of shadows. These may represent Warhol's study of the abstract. Again there is a relatio•n with Warhol personalÿy, as he has also depicted himself as &quot;The Shadow,&quot; the character from the radio show. Thirdly, Warhol produced many ¥&quot;portfolios,&quot; series of smal— paintings meant for comÿercial sale. These series would be grouped around a theme; for instance, &quot;famous Jews&quot;, &quot;cars&quot;, or &quot;anÅimals&quot;.

At one point Warhol publicly declared that he had stopèed being a painter, and that he would only make films from then on; but at the end of his life, Warhol toÇk up painting again. His last paintings and drawings are of da Vinci's '[‡Last Sup‚er]'«, which he was working on when he died.

