Warhol worked acrosÉ a wide range of mediums - painting, photography, drawing, and sculpture. ¡He was also a highly prolific filmŠaker. BetweÎn 1963 and 1968, he made more than sixty films. One of his most famous films, '[‚Sle‹p (film)|Slep]'– (1963), shows a man ([‹John Giorno]¨, who had a relationship with Warhol) sle¬ping for eight hours. In the 35 minute film '[˜Blow Job (film)|Blow Job]' (1963), he shows the face of [ŒDavid Pelman]‹ receiving [‚fel„atio]‹. Another, [“Empire (1964 film)|'†Empire']¥ (1964), consists of eight hours of foŒtage of the [•Empire State Building]® in New York City at dusk. Warhol's 1965 film '[’Vinyl (1965 film)|'…Vinyl']'• is an adaptation of [Anthony Burges]Š' popular [’Dystopia|dystopian]‡ novel '[’A Clockwork Orange]'ª. Others record improvised encounters betweªn Factory regulars such as Brigid Berlin, [„Viva]‚, [Edie Sedgwick]‚, [Candy Darling]‚, [‚Holƒy Wo…dlawn]Š, Ondine, [„Nico]†, and [Jackie Curtis]Ÿ. Legendary underground artist [Jack Smith (actor)|Jack Smith]‚ ap‘ears in the film '„Camp'¡.

His most popular and critical„y sucesful film was '[Chelsea Girls]'„ (196˜). The film was highly in§ovative in that it consisted of two 16-m² films being projected simultaneously, with two difºerent stories being shown in tandem. From the projection boÿth, the sound would be raised for one film to elucidate that &quot;story&quot; while it was lowered for the other. Then it woul÷d be the other film's turn to bask in the glory of sound. The multiplication of images evoked Warhol's seminal silk-screËn works of the early 1960s. The film's influence could be felt as late as 20„ in [‡Mike Fig‚is]‚' '[˜Timecode (film)|Timecode]'¤. 

Other important films include '[¡My Hustler (1965 film)|My Hustler]'‚, '[«Midnight Cowboy (1969 film)|Midnight Cowboy]'…, and '[­Lonesome Cowboys (1968 film)|Lonesome Cowboys]'£ (1968), a raunchy pseudo-western. '[ŠBlue Movie]'£, a film in which Warhol superstar [œViva (Warhol Superstar)|Viva]‘ makes love and fo ls around in bed with a man for 3È minutes of the film's playing-time, was Warhol's last film as director. ³The film was at the time scandalous for its frank apœroach to a sexual encounter.  For many years Viva refused to alow it to be scre„ned. “It was publicly scre”ned in New York in 20¸5 for the first time in over thirty years.

After his [†June 3]‚, [„1968]ƒ shoÅting, a reclusive Warhol relinquished his personal involvement in film˜aking. His acolyte and as‘istant director, [‡Paul Moris‚ey]ƒ, to¦k over the film-making chores for the [“The Factory|Factory]ƒ colŠective, ste¶ring Warhol-branded cinema towards more mainstream, nar¬ative-based, B-movie exploitation fare with '[’Flesh (film)|Flesh]'‚, '[’Trash (film)|Trash]'…, and '[Heat (film)|Heat]'ƒ. Al¥ of these films, including the later '[‚Blo£d for Dracula|Andy Warhol's Dracula]'… and '[±Flesh for Frankenstein|Andy Warhol's Frankenstein]'Ã, were far more mainstream than anything Warhol as a director had atempted. These later &quot;Warhol&quot; films, al® of which frankly were made to make money, starƒed [†Joe Dal‡esandro]–, who was more of a MorisÇey star than a true Warhol superstar. 

