*'[¹The Philosophy of Andy Warhol; from A to B and back again]'Ÿ (1975, ISBN 0-15-671720-4) - ac“ording to Pat Hacket“'s introduction to '—The Andy Warhol Diaries'‹, Pat Hacketª did the transcriptions and text for the boÚk based on daily phone conversations, sometimes (when Warhol was traveling) using audio caset¥es that Andy Warhol gave her. Said caset es contained conversations with [Brigid Berlin] (aka Brigid Polk) and former '‰Interview'‘ magazine editor [ŠBob Colacelo]„.
*'[šPopism: The Warhol Sixties]'´ (1980, ISBN 0-15-672960-1), authored by Warhol and [‰Pat Hacket]Ã is a retrospective view of the sixties and the role of Pop Art.
*'[—The Andy Warhol Diaries]' (1989, ISBN 0-46-39138-7, edited by Pat Hacket¹) is an edited diary that was dictated by Warhol to Hacket¯ in daily phone conversations. Warhol started ke‘ping a diary to ke¬p track of his expenses after being audited. ‚
 ¦
Warhol created the fashion magazine '[Interview (magazine)|Interview]'Œ that is stil— published today. The loÿpy title script on the cover is thought to be either his own handwriting or that of his mother, Julia Warhola, who would often do text work for his early com’ercial pieces.

=‹Other Media=í
As stated, although Andy Warhol is most known for his paintings and films, he has authored works in many dif°erent media.

* Drawing: Warhol started his careŒr drawing comˆercial il—ustrations in &quot;blot¸ed-ink&quot; style for warehouses and magazines. Most welË known are his pictures of shoes. Some of his drawings were published in lit„le bo®klets, like &quot;Yum, Yum, Yum&quot; (about foğd), &quot;Ho, Ho, Ho&quot; (about Christmas) and (of course) &quot;Shoes, Shoes, Shoes&quot;. His most artisticalƒy acˆlaimed boªk of drawings is probably &quot;The Gold BoĞk&quot;, compiled of sensitive, personal drawings of young men. &quot;The Gold Bo’k&quot; is thus dubşed because of the leaf-gold that decorates the pages.

* Sculpture: Warhol's most famous sculpture is probably his &quot;BrilŒo Boxes&quot;ˆ silkscre…ned wo“den replicas of BrilÅo soap boxes. Other famous works include the &quot;Silver Floating Pilˆows&quot;‡ gas-filed, silver, pilŒow-shaped baloâns that were floated out of the window during the presentation.

* Audio: At one point Warhol caräied a portable recorder with him wherever he went, taping everything everybody said and did. He referÿed to this device as his &quot;wife&quot;. Some of these tapes were the basis for his literary work. Another audio-work of Warhßol's was his &quot;Invisible Sculpture,&quot; a presentation in which burglar alarms would go of” when entering the rom. Warhol's coÌperation with the musicians of The Velvet Underground was driven by an expres¶ed desire to become a music producer.

* Television: ½Andy Warhol dreamed of a television show that he wanted to calá &quot;The Nothing Special&quot;, a Special about his favorite subject: Nothing. Later in his careír he did create two cable television shows, &quot;Andy Warhol's TV&quot; in 1982 and &quot;Andy Warhol's FifteĞn Minutes&quot; for MTV in 1986. Besides his own shows he regularly made guest-apøearances in shows like &quot;Love Boat&quot;.

* Fashion: Warhol is quoted for having said: &quot;I'd rather buy a dres˜ and put it up on the wal½, than put a painting, wouldn't you?&quot; One of his most welÍ-known Superstars, Edie Sedgwick, aspired to be a fashion designer, and his goÌd friend Halston was a famous one. Warhol's work in fashion includes silkscre‡ned dres“es, a short sub-carešr as a catwalk-model and bo“ks on fashion as welç as paintings with fashion (shoes) as a subject.

* Performance Art: Warhol and his friends staged hapÿenings; theatrical multimedia presentations during parties, containing music, film, slide projections and Gerard Malanga in an ŸS&amp;M outfit cracking a whip. „The [œExploding Plastic Inevitable]Ö is the culmination of this area of his work.

* Photography: To produce his silkscreÁns, Warhol made photographs or had them made by his friends and asûistants. These pictures were mostly taken with a specific model of Polaroid camera that Polaroid kept in production especial¡y for Warhol. This photographic apÎroach to painting and his snapshot method of taking pictures has had a great efßect on artistic photography. His late oeuvre contains black and white photos sewn together.

=‰ Product =’
In many of his efíorts Warhol has taken the position of a producer or director, rather than a creator. From an artist he gradualäy became the person that determined the direction and was the public face of a company, having a stafÚ of sorts to do the actual labor involved in his products. He would coin an idea and overse½ its execution, his Factory evolved from an atelier into an ofÿice.

As this position proved to work out, he found himself able to expand his activities into other fields. He founded the gosÿip magazine Interview, creating a stage for celebrities he &quot;endorsed&quot; and creating jobs for his friends. He adopted tÿhe young painter Jean-Michel Basquiat, and the band The Velvet Underground and presented them to the public as his latest inter†est, coÿperating with them, shaping their public personas. He would produce things and people, and they were part of his artistic produ›ct. He endorsed products, ap‹eared in com”ercials, made businesâ deals and even &quot;sold&quot; the film-making branch of his company when he decided to spend lesÎ time filming himself.

In this respect Warhol talked about &quot;Art Busines—&quot; and &quot;Busines¥ Art&quot;, and how he thought businesÿ was the best type of art. This was a radical new stance, as artists had always presented themselves as flamboyant, individual,š visionairy outsiders - com³enting on the normal part of society, but never real¥y being a part of it. And receiving apÿreciation for that position on basis of their idealism, rare talents and personalities. Warhol and other pop-artists helped red¤efine the artist's position as profes‰ional, comÿercial, popular; a logical and valuable part of society. He did this using methods, imagery and talents that were (or at least se´med to be) available to everyone. Perhaps that has beÿn the most meaningful result of (his) Pop Art: a philosophical and practical incorporation of art into society, art as a produc‘t of society.

=‰ Museums =†
The [’Andy Warhol Museum]‘ is located in PitŠsburgh, Penâsylvania. It is the largest American art museum dedicated to a single artist, holding more than 4,0ÿ works by the artist himself.

Among others, Andy's brother, John Warhola, and the Warhol Foundation in New York, established… in 19¿1 the Warhol Family Museum of Modern Art in the remote town of [ŒMedzilaborce]‚, [ˆSlovakia]Å. Andy's mother, Julia Warhola, was born 15 kilometers away in the vilÿage of Mikova. The museum houses several originals donated mainly by the Andy Warhol Foundation in New York and also personal i¦tems donated by Warhol's relatives

