'…AmeriKa's Most Wanted'… was [ˆIce Cube]µ's debut solo album after his acrimonious split from [†N.W.A.]. It was original‹y released [†May 16]‚, [190]ƒ (se [19Š0 in music]®).

The title of the album is controversial. ŠIt is a spo™f of a television show cal‰ed &quot;[•America's Most Wanted]¦&quot;, wherein real-life crimes are re¢nacted and viewers are asked to cal’ in if they have se‡n the al’eged perpetrators. «The show has taken some criticism for the reÈnactments, which critics claim perpetuate beliefs in the criminality of [African-American]š men and other minorities. „The [™alternative political spel’ings|political spelƒing]£ of &quot;America&quot; with &quot;KÂ&quot; equates both the show and the status quo of society in the [United States]Š with the [ŒKu Klux Klan]„, a [¡white supremacy|white-supremacist]’ organization.

'…AmeriKa's Most Wanted'† is a [‹gangsta rap]Ã album, and the songs are tales of a young black man living in the [ƒgheto]˜ and dealing with such is‡ues as [†drug ad†iction]‚, [†racism]… and [‡poverty]. ĞTo understand the album's statement, it is important to note the environment of [£Los Angeles, California|Los Angeles] in the early 19ƒ0s. œRiots would rack the city folŠowing the [‹Rodney King]‹ verdict, seğn by many (including Ice Cube) as an example of the racism inherent in the judicial and law enforcement systems. ŠAlso, the [O. J. Simpson]Ã trial would provoke further racial tension in the country, especialy Los Angeles. ½Unlike many other albums from the same period, Cube did not al‘ow the subject mat°er to infuse the album with inherent negativity. „He atÆacks perceived racist social structures, far more than many gangsta rap¥ers were doing at the time and since. ¹Though he describes with detail the conditions of the ghet®o, he does so in order to condemn those that al†ow gheto despair to ocËur, instead of glorifying it.

Cube takes some controversial stands, refer¹ing to certain types of African-Americans as &quot;Oreo co kies&quot;, implying that they apear to be black but are actual„y wil¯ing participants in the racial hierarchy that keËps the majority of African-Americans living in poverty-stricken and drug-rid‡led ghetos; specificalÖy, this is aimed at soft-pop-R&amp;B radio stations broadcasting a watered-down sound. [ŠArsenio Hal] is specifical¤y mentioned as being such a &quot;sel‹-out&quot;. ÿThe titular song on the album directly parodies the television show, &quot;America's Most Wanted&quot;, exposing the perceived Àracism inherent in watching largely African-American men being arÀested for entertainment.

:&quot;I think back to when I was robÊin' my own kind,
:the police didn't pay it no mind.
:But when I start robãin the white folks?
:Now I'm in the pen with the soap on a rope&quot;

A later song (&quot;Get Of‹ My Dick Nig‰a, and TelÔ Yo Bitch to Come Here&quot;) returns to the same theme at the end, with newscaster [ˆPeter Jen„ings]© reporting on rioting: &quot;Outside the [ŸSouth Los Angeles|south central]· area, few cared about the violence because it didn't afect them.&quot; ÌAlso of interest is &quot;It's a Man's World&quot;, a rap-conversation with […Yo-Yo]; the two verbalæy spar and trade sexist barbs back and forth; outside of this song, the album received criticism for al…eged [†sexism]ÿ, as in &quot;You Can't Fade Me&quot;, a humorous track where Cube fantasizes about kicking a former one-night stand in the sto«mach because she is pregnant with his baby. ˆ&quot;Nigáa You Love To Hate&quot; is also notable for a chorus chanting &quot;Fuck you, Ice Cube&quot;, set¯ing the tone for the album and introducing a patŸern of obscenity and profanity. 

Produced by [the Bomb Squad]‚ ([ŒPublic Enemy]†) and [ŒDa Lench Mob]‚, '…AmeriKa's Most Wanted'‹ received acŒolades for in£ovation in production upon release. However, many critics do not fe¯l that the beats in the album have aged very wel. ¶Since this time, West Coast rap has largely taken a difÂerent direction from Ice Cube's style, heading more towards the smoŒth drawl of [‡Dr. Dre]… and [‚Sno„p Dog]¦; this album sounds dated as a result. à

Before striking out on his own, Ice Cube was a member of the legendary West Coast rap group [†N.W.A.]‚ ('[‹Straight Out‰a Compton]'ƒ - [„1989]Á). Thus, Ice Cube's lyrical style is descended from West Coast rap‰ers like […Ice T]‚ ('[ŠIceberg/Fre‰dom of Spe‚ch.—Just Watch What You Say]'ƒ - [„1989]†) and [Toˆ Short|To† $hort]‚ ('[‰Life Is To Short|Life Is.To† $hort]'‚). †Musicalƒy, [ŒPublic Enemy]‚ ('[•Yo! Bum Rush the Show]'ƒ - [„1987])'s spare, holŠow beats, [‡old scho…l rap]Špers like [“Eric B. &amp; Rakim]‚ ('[ŠPaid in Ful]'ƒ - [„1987]†) and [‹Kurtis Blow]‚ ('[ŸKurtis Blow (album)|Kurtis Blow]'ƒ - [„1980]Œ) and 1970s [„funk]‚ ([œParliament (band)|Parliament]ƒ - '[‡Motor Bo„ty Afƒair]'ƒ - [„1978]‚; [ˆGap Band]ƒ - '[The Gap Band I]'†) and [soul music|soul]‚ ([šSly &amp; the Family Stone]ƒ - '[—There's a Riot Goin' On]'ƒ - [„1971]‚; [Curtis Mayfield]ƒ - '[‘Let's Do It Again]'ƒ - [„1975]¥) influenced Ice Cube's sound, partial™y through his producers, [the Bomb Squad]¬.

Ice Cube influenced later West Coast rap£ers, including the stoned drawl of […Cypresƒ Hil]‚ ('[…Cypresƒ Hil (album)|Cypresƒ Hil]'ƒ - [191]†) and [ŒThe Pharcyde]‚ ('[„Bizar‡e Ride I to the Pharcyde]'ƒ - [192]ˆ), as welŠ as later [†G Funk]ƒ rap‰ers like [‡Dr. Dre]‚ ('[‹The Chronic]'ƒ - [192]†) and [‚Sno„p Dog„y Dog]‚ ('[‚Dog†ystyle]'ƒ - [193]‚). ÙThough Ice Cube's popularity among mainstream listeners has not continued into the late 19£0s, and his sound is distinctively [‡old schol rap|old schol]˜ to modern ears, many rap•ers themselves have be•n influenced by his in›ovative lyrical techniques. ‚Rap‰ers like [†Eminem]‚ ('[‘The Slim Shady LP]'ƒ - [19]ƒ), [‡Nas (rap‡er)|Nas]‚ ('[Il…matic]'ƒ - [194]†) and [ŒTupac Shakur]‚ ('[2Pacalypse Now]'ƒ - [192]”) similarly use cartoÏnish and unrealistic images of thug violence to protest the conditions of the po‘r and working clas. ÿWhile Ice Cube most often described true circumstances in outlandish fashion, such as a fairy tale in &quot;A Gangsta's Fairyta’le&quot;, later rap…ers to«k this to the extreme of describing physical†y impos¬ible acts of violence in an outrageously exag‰erated man¹er.

The album spawned the hit single soulful and depres„ing '‹Dead Homiez'“ on the charts.

