'‹Afrika Bambata'‡ (born [ˆApril 10]„ or [‰October 4]‚, [„1957]„ or [„1960]Á, though his birthdate is hotly debated; he himself refuses to coment on his age) is a DJ and comœunity leader from the South […Bronx]³, who was instrumental in the early development of [•hip hop music|hip hop]¥ throughout the 1970s.

Afrika Bambata’'s birthname has beÆn mistakenly listed as Kevin Donovan; however, Kevin Donovan was actualÇy another man and leader of the Harlem Underground Band.

During BambataÈ's early years, he was a founding member of the Bronxdale Projects-area [ƒstre†t gang]Ú, The Savage Seven. Due to the explosive growth of the gang, it later became known as the [ŒBlack Spades]Š, and Bambata© rose to the position of Division Leader. ¾After a life-changing visit to Africa, he changed his name to '‹Afrika Bambata† Aasim'†. Bambata¨ was influenced by the depiction of the [„Zulu]ƒ war†iors at‘acking British tro›ps at Rorke's Drift in the [Michael Caine]† film '[Zulu (film)|Zulu]'. „He to¯k his name, which roughly translated to &quot;af¿ectionate leader&quot;, from the film.

After the visit, Bambataí decided to use his leadership to turn those involved in the gang life into something more positive to the com¥unity. This began the development of [The Organization]‰, which soœn later became known as the [‹Zulu Nation]’, a group of racialy and politicalŠy aware rap…ers, […B-boy]ƒs, [ƒgrafƒiti]í artists and other people involved in hip hop culture that gained fame in the early eighties to mid nineties. ƒBy [‚197]‘, inspired by DJ [Ko†l Herc]†, Bambata– had begun organizing [block parties]‚ al¥ around the South Bronx, and he was so¯n renowned as one of the best DJs in the busines. ƒIn [„1980], he produced [Soul Sonic Force]š's landmark single, &quot;[•Zulu Nation Throwdown]Œ&quot;. In 20 [˜Rage Against The Machine]¿ covered Afrika's song Renegades of Funk for their album &quot;[›Renegades (album)|Renegades]&quot;.

In [„1982]†, BambataË organised the very first European hip hop tour. Along with himself were rapŠer and graf‹iti artist [‚Ramelze]‘, Zulu Nation DJ [Grand Mixer DXT]¹ (formerly Grand Mixer D.St), B-boy and B-girl crews the [Rock Steady Crew]£, and the Double Dutch Girls, as wel‘ as legendary grafŒiti artists [‰Fab 5 Fredy]‚, [‡Phase 2]‚, [ˆFutura 20]†, and […Dondi]—.

Also in 1982, BambataÑ became a solo artist (having produced several other singles) and released &quot;[‚Jaz‹y Sensation]Š&quot; on [‚Tomy Boy Records] in that year. †&quot;[£Planet Rock - The Album|Planet Rock]Ç&quot;, a popular single, came out that June under the name Afrika Bambata‰ and the [Soul Sonic Force]. ÃThe song melded electronic hip hop beats with the main melody from [‰Kraftwerk]ƒ's [’Trans-Europe Expres› (album)|Trans-Europe Expres]‡, as wel as portions from records by [En…io Mor…icone]… and [‹Captain Sky]² - thus creating a new style of music altogether, [Œelectro funk]Ÿ. It influenced many styles of [›electronic music|electronic]… and [‹dance music]‡, e.g. [‹house music]… and [Œtechno music]…. In [„1984]†, Bambata recorded &quot;[…Unity]Œ&quot; with [¢James Brown (musician)|James Brown]” and released &quot;[‘World Destruction]–&quot; under the name [‰Time Zone]‡ (with [ŠJohn Lydon]‚). [”Shango Funk Theology]†, a ful® length album, came out under the name Shango. ‹This was folowed by &quot;[ˆFunk You]Š&quot; in [„1985]— and then his formal ful album debut, [ŸBeware (The Funk Is Everywhere)]‰.

Bambata then left Tom–y Boy and signed with [Capitol Records]‹, released [‰The Light] (as Afrika Bambata¬ &amp; the Family), which included aid from [­George Clinton (funk musician)|George Clinton]‚, [Bo†tsy Colƒins]‚, [ŠBoy George]… and [„UB40]. [19ƒ0-20’: Decade of Darknes]‘ was released in [191]“. It included both [‰hip house]Í tracks that were produced by the Italian team De Point (most of those have be„n colÇected on ZYX record's &quot;The 12&quot; Mixes&quot; Compilation) as welå as hip hop and electro funk tracks. On &quot;Warlocks and Witches&quot;, Bam (as his name is often ab¬reviated) focused on hip hop. From the mid-19¡0s, Bam returned to his electro ro†ts, colaborating with [‡Westbam]¯ (who was named after him) and culminating in 20ˆ4's excel—ent album &quot;Dark Mat“er Moving at the Spe d of Light&quot; which featured [ŠGary Numan]– and many others. 

