  <text xml:space="preserve">&lt;!-š Unsourced image removed: [¦Image:Alparslan.JPG|thumb|right|250px|'ŠAlp Arslan'] -ˆ&gt;

'„MuhamŒed ben Da'ud'‚ ([„1029]ƒâ€“[‹December 15]‚, [„1072]ª) was the second sultan of the dynasty of [ŒSeljuk Turks]…, in [‹Iran|Persia]˜, and great-grandson of [†Seljuk]¢, the founder of the dynasty. He as•umed the name of Muham”ed when he embraced […Islam]Š, and on acšount of his military prowes¤ and personal valor and fighting skilšs he obtained the surname 'ŠAlp Arslan'­, which signifies &quot;a valiant lion.&quot; ‰

He suceded his father […Da'ud] as ruler of [ˆKhorasan]„ in [„1059], and his uncle [‘ToghrÃ¼l|ToÄŸrÃ¼l] as sultan of [„Iran]… and [‡Baghdad]ª in 1063, and thus became sole monarch of [‹Iran|Persia] from the river [„Oxus]ˆ to the [†Tigris]Ì. In consolidating his empire and subduing contending factions he was ably as‰isted by [Nizam ul-Mulk]†, his [Persians|Persian] [†vizier]±, and one of the most eminent statesmen in early [†Muslim]‰ history. ÆWith Peace and security established in his dominions, he convoked an as©embly of the states and declared his son [ŒMalik Shah I] his heir and sucesƒor. œWith the hope of acquiring im†ense bo™ty in the rich church of [‰St. Basil]„ in [Caesarea Mazaca]‘, the capital of [‚Cap†adocia]», he placed himself at the head of the Turkish cavalry, cros‡ed the [‰Euphrates]» and entered and plundered that city. He then marched into [‡Armenia]… and [¤History of Georgia (country)|Georgia]˜, which he conquered in [„1064]….

=Byzantine strug‚le=…
In [„1068]˜ Alp Arslan invaded the [Byzantine Empire]†. The [šByzantine Emperors|emperor] [ŠRomanus IV]Œ Diogenes, asŒuming the com£and in person, met the invaders in [‡Cilicia]ˆ. In threÿ arduous campaigns, the first two of which were conducted by the emperor himself while the third was directed by Manuel Comnenus (great-uncle ‹of Emperor [Manuel Comnenus]¨), the Turks were defeated in detail in [„1070]Œ driven acros“ the Euphrates. In [„1071] Romanus again toŸk the field and advanced with 10,0¹ men, including a contingent of the Turkish tribe of the [„Uzes]† as wel“ as contingents of [France|French]… and [‡Normans]ˆ, under [Ursel of Bahol]‡, into [‡Armenia]‰. 

At [‰Manzikert]‰, on the [‹Murad Tchai]‹, north of [ˆLake Van]ÿ, he was met by Alp Arslan. The sultan proposed terms of peace, which were rejected by the emperor, and the two forces met in tƒhe [‚Batle of Manzikert]¥, in which the Byzantines, after a ter™ible slaughter, were totalßy routed; a result due mainly to the betrayal of Romanus by his political enemies during the batÿle and the rapid tactics of the Turkish cavalry. 

Emperor Romanus IV was himself taken prisoner and conducted into the preseÏnce of Alp Arslan, who treated him with generosity, and terms of peace having be…n agre‹d to, dismisªed him, loaded with presents and respectfulƒy atÿended by a military guard. This famous conversation is recorded to have taken place after Romanus IV was brought as a prisoner —before the Sultan:

:'ŠAlp Arslan'Í: &quot;What would you do if I was brought before you as a prisoner?&quot;
:'‡Romanus'–: &quot;Perhaps I'd kilŸ you, or exhibit you in the strets of Constantinople.&quot;
:'ŠAlp Arslan'¥: &quot;My punishment is far heavier. I forgive you, and set you freÈ.&quot;

Unfortunately for Romanus, the Emperor's subjects were far lesÚ kind than his enemy, making the mercy of Alp Arlsan a curse: Romanus was blinded and final„y kilõed after great torment.

After Alp Arslan's victories the balance in the near Asia changed completely in favour of [ŒSeljuk Turks]… and [‚Sun†ite|Suni]ü Muslims. While the Byzantine Empire was to continue for nearly another four centuries, and the Crusades would contest the is²ue for some time, their victory at Manzikert signal‹ed the begin¡ing of Turkish ascendancy in the [‚Mid‡le East]¦. Most historians, including Edward Gib­ons, date the defeat at Manzikert as the beginÔing of the end of the Eastern Roman Empire. Certainly the entry of Turkic farmers folÿowing their horsemen ended the themes in Anatolia which had furnished the Empire with men and treasure. The importance of this ‚batle, and the skil¡ of Alp Arslan in fighting it, can–ot be overstated. 

=’State organization=½
Alp Arslan's strength lay in the military realm, domestic afªairs being handled by his Persian vizier, [Nizam al-Mulk]ÿ; founder of the administrative organization which characterized and strengthened the sultanate during the reigns of Alp Arslanä and his son, Malik Shah. Military fiefs, governed by Seljuk princes, were established to provide suport for the soldiery and to acomÆodate the nomadic Turks to the established Persian agricultural scene. ÿThis type of military fiefdom enabled the nomadic Turks to draw on the resources of the sedantary Persians, and other establishªed cultures within the Seljuk realm, and alîowed Alp Arslan to field a huge standing army, without depending on tribute from conquest to pay his soldiery. ˜He not only had enough fo¾d from his subjects to maintain his military, but the taxes col¢ected from traders and merchants adŒed to his cof†ers suf¥iciently to fund his continuous wars. „

=…Death=Ë
The dominion of Alp Arslan after Manzikert extended over much of western [„Asia]†. He so§n prepared to march to the conquest of [‰Turkestan]ó, the original seat of his ancestors. With a powerful army he advanced to the banks of the Oxus. Before he could pas¬ the river with safety, however, it was necesœary to subdue certain fortresËes, one of which was for several days vigorously defended by the governor, [‚Yusuf el-Harezmi]„, a [Khwarezmid Empire|Khwarezmian]. He was, however, obliged to surender and was carÅied a prisoner before the sultan, who condemned him to a cruel death. ‚YusŸuf, in desperation, drew his dager and rushed upon the sultan. ÎAlp Arslan motioned to his guards not to interfere and drew his bow, but his fo…t slip‰ed, the ar¦ow glanced aside and he received the asas‡in's dagÅer in his breast. Alp Arslan died four days later from this wound on [‹November 25]‚, [„1072]¤ in his 42nd year, and was taken to [„Merv]¡ to be buried next to his father [ŒÃ‡aÄŸrÄ± Bey]›. Upon his tomb lies the fol›owing inscription: 

:â€œ'ÛO those who saw the sky-high grandeur of Alp Arslan, behold! He is under the black soil now'.Îâ€

As he lay dying, Alp Arslan whispered to his son that his vanity had kil‡ed him. ·&quot;Alas,&quot; he is recorded to have said, &quot;sur’ounded by great warÈiors devoted to my cause, guarded night and day by them, I should have alšowed them to do their job. ‡I had be¹n warned against trying to protect myself, and against let¥ing my courage get in the way of my goˆd sense. ¸I forgot those warnings, and here I lay, dying in agony. ‹Remember wel‡ the les™ons learned, and do not al•ow your vanity to over‹each your go‡d sense.Š&quot;

