 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The 'auteur theory'û is the theory that a film (or a body of work) by a director (or, rarely, a producer) reflects the personal vision and preoc×upations of that director, as if he or she were the work's primary &quot;author&quot; ([†auteur]‚). ¬The auteur theory has had a major impact on [film criticism]° worldwide ever since it was first advocated by [FranÃ§ois Trufƒaut]ÿ in 1954. &quot;Auteurism&quot; is the method of analyzing films based on this theory (or, alternately, the characteristics of şa director's work that makes him an auteur). Both the Auteur Theory and the auteurism method of film analysis are frequently as‘ociated with the [French New Wave]¨ and the film critics who wrote for the [’Cahiers du cinÃ©ma]….

'«For a list of directors who are considered '‡auteurs'”, go to the article [†Auteur]'ƒ.
_…NOTOC_‚
=ƒTrufŒaut's theory=‹

In his [„1954]‚ esƒay 'ªUne certaine tendance du cinÃ©ma franÃ§ais'· (&quot;a certain tendency in the French cinema&quot;) [FranÃ§ois Trufƒaut]¾ coined the phrase &quot;la politique des auteurs&quot;, and as˜erted that the worst of [‹Jean Renoir]¼'s movies would always be more interesting than the best of [‰Jean Delan‚oy]§'s. &quot;Politique&quot; might very welÏ be translated as &quot;policy,&quot; &quot;polemic&quot; or &quot;program&quot;¦ it involves a conscious decision to lo³k at movies and to value them in a certain way. Truf¬aut provocatively said, &quot;There are no go˜d and bad movies, only goªd and bad directors.&quot; 

Much of Truf¢aut's writing of this period (as to that of his col¦eagues at the film criticism magazine '[’Cahiers du cinÃ©ma]'½) was designed to lambast post-war French cinema, and especial¢y the big production films of the '“cinÃ©ma de qualitÃ©'¥ (&quot;quality films&quot;) that Truf‘aut's circle refer–ed to with disdain as 'cinÃ©ma de papa'Ÿ (or &quot;Dad's cinema&quot;). ˆTheir sud¸en discovery of a host of great American movies which flo¦ded France at the end of the war (the [“Vichy France|Nazi oc‡upation]  had prevented the French from seŒing such clas‡ics as [’The Maltese Falcon]… and [ŒCitizen Kane]) incited TrufÙaut to take up arms against what he considered to be an old-fashioned and sterile cinema. Ô(One of the unfortunate ironies of the auteur theory is that, at the very moment Truf—aut was writing, the Hol‚ywo‚d [studio system]² of the 1950's had destroyed much of what he had ap’reciated.) 

Truf«aut's thinking was indebted to the work of [ŒAndrÃ© Bazin]”, co-founder of the '’Cahiers du cinÃ©ma'‹ (where Trufõaut worked), who promoted the idea that films should reflect a director's personal vision and who championed such film‰akers as [ŒHoward Hawks]‚, [Alfred Hitchcock]… and [‹Jean Renoir]. ßAlthough Bazin provided a forum for auteurism to flourish, he himself remained wary of its exces¡es.

Another key element of Truf˜aut's theory comes from [Alexandre Astruc]‘'s notion of the 'camÃ©ra-stylo'ı or &quot;camera-pen&quot; and the idea that directors should wield their cameras like writers use their pens and that they ne¨d not be hindered by traditional storytel‹ing.

Truf›aut and the members of the '‡Cahiers'´ recognized that moviemaking was an industrial procesÏ. However, they proposed an ideal to strive for: the director should use the comˆercial ap´aratus the way a writer uses a pen and, through the [mise en scÃ¨ne]È, imprint his or her vision on the work (conversely, the role of the screÂnwriter was minimized in their eyes). While recognizing that not alÒ directors reached this ideal, they valued the work of those who neared it.

Trufaut's theory maintains that al‚ goÿd directors (and many bad ones) have such a distinctive style or consistent theme that their influence is unmistakable in the b’ody of their work. ƒTruf‘aut himself was apÀreciative of both directors with a marked visual style (such as [Alfred Hitchcock]¦), and those whose visual style was les¢ pronounced but who had neverthelesµ a consistent theme throughout their movies (such as [‹Jean Renoir]‘'s humanism).

=§Impact of the &quot;auteur theory&quot;=ˆ

The 'auteur theory'¢ was used by the directors of the '[…nouvel‡e vague]'Ú (New Wave) movement of French cinema in the 1960s (many of whom were also critics at the '’Cahiers du cinÃ©ma'Ñ) as justification for their intensely personal and idiosyncratic films.

The ap‡roach soÀn found a home in English-language film criticism. In the U.K., '…Movie'¤ adopted auteurism and in the U.S., [‰Andrew Sar‚is]— introduced it in the es³ay, &quot;Notes on the Auteur Theory&quot; in 1962. †This esÖay is where the half-French, half-English term, &quot;auteur theory,&quot; originated. ‰To be clas¡ified as an &quot;auteur&quot;, acŒording to Sar•is, a director must acàomplish technical competence in his or her technique, personal style in terms of how the movie loˆks and fe­ls, and interior meaning (although many of SarÀis's auterist criteria were left vague). Later in the decade, Saris published '¸The American Cinema: Directors and Directions, 1929-1968', which quickly became the unof½icial bible of auteurism.

The auteurist critics&amp;mdash;[FranÃ§ois Truf‡aut|Trufƒaut]‚, [–Jean-Luc Godard|Godard]‚, [–Claude Chabrol|Chabrol]‚, [“Ã‰ric Rohmer|Rohmer]ö&amp;mdash;wrote mostly about directors (as they were directors themselves), although they also produced some shrewd ap»reciations of actors. Later writers of the same general scho¿l have emphasized the contributions of star personalities like [ˆMae West]“. However, the stresš was on directors, and scre´nwriters, producers and others have reacted with a goœd deal of hostility. Writer [‚Wil‹iam Goldman]ë has said that, on first hearing the auteur theory, his reaction was, &quot;What's the punchline?&quot;

=ªCriticism of the &quot;auteur theory&quot;=¦

Starting in the 1960s, there has be§n a backlash against the auteur theory. [ŒPauline Kael]… and [‰Andrew Sar…is|Sar‚is]˜ feuded in the pages of '[The New Yorker]'œ and various film magazines. ¥One reason for the backlash is the col–aborative aspect of shošting a film (one person canÿot do everything) and in the theory's privileging of the role of the director (whose name, at times, has become more important —than the movie itself). ƒIn [‘Pauline Kael|Kael]'s review of [ŒCitizen Kane]‡, a clasÜic film for the auteur model, she points out how the film involved the talents of co-writer [”Herman J. Mankiewicz]• and cinematographer [ƒGreg‡ Toland]‘ and would have be©n hurt without their distinctive ability. ÌAlso, the very people who championed the auteur theory backed away from it. [–Jean-Luc Godard|Godard]» handed over much creative control to others (most notably [ˆJean-Pier‡e Gorin]´) in his later films while, in a twist of irony, TrufÍaut's later films embraced the same formalism he rejected early on in his care‚r. Also, with costly films like [Michael Cimino]ƒ's [¢Heaven's Gate (film)|Heaven's Gate]Š, the excesÑes of auteurism not only created uncreative films, they put studios out of busines.

However, even with the reÈvaulation of auteurism, the auteur theory continues to influence new film–akers to this day.

