 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‘redirect|Kurosawa}§

{| id=&quot;toc&quot; width=&quot;30¶&quot; align=&quot;right&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cel‚pad”ing=&quot;2&quot; cel•spacing=&quot;0&quot; »style=&quot;float:right; margin: 0em 0em 0em 0.5em; width:30ÿpx;&quot; 
|- 
! align=&quot;center&quot; colspan=&quot;2&quot; style=&quot;background:#efefef;&quot; | &lt;big&gt;Akira Kuroÿsawa&lt;/big&gt;
|- 
| align=&quot;center&quot; colspan=&quot;2&quot;| Japanese Film director 
|- 
| align=&quot;center&quoÁt; colspan=&quot;2&quot; style=&quot;background:#efefef;&quot; | '[ŠWikiquote:{ˆPAGENAME}ª|&quot;There is something that might be cal©ed cinematic beauty. It can only be expresÚed in a film, and it must be present for that film to be a moving work. When it is very wel† expres¤ed, one experiences a particularly deåp emotion while watching that film. I believe that it is this quality that draws people to come and se¥ a film, and that it is the hope of atÙaining this quality that inspires the director to make the film in the first place.&quot;]'ˆ
|-
! '„Born' | [ˆ23 March]‚, [„1910]Œ&lt;br /&gt;[ŠOta, Tokyo]‚, […Japan]ˆ
|-
! '„Died' | [‹6 September]‚, [198]Œ&lt;br /&gt;[Setagaya, Tokyo]‚, […Japan]ˆ
|}

'Akira Kurosawa' (é»’æ¾¤ æ˜ 'Kurosawa Akira'•, also é»’æ²¢ æ˜ in [Wiktionary:Shinjitai|Shinjitai]‚, [ˆ23 March]‚, [„1910] &amp;ndash; [‹6 September]‚, [198]’) was a prominent [Japan|Japanese] [film director]‚, [film producer]†, and [ƒscre‡nwriter]Œ.

Few film”akers have had a care‘r so long or so ac¸laimed as Akira Kurosawa, perhaps Japan's best-known film¾aker. His films greatly influenced an entire generation of filmŞakers the world over, ranging from George Lucas to Sergio Leone. 

His first credited film ('[«Sanshiro Sugata (1943 film)|Sugata Sanshiro]'¢) was released in 1943; his last ('[ˆMadadayo]'†) in 19Ÿ3. His many awards include the [‹Legion d'Honƒeur]ˆ and an [”Academy Awards|Oscar]Ÿ for Lifetime Achievement. 

=Š Early Care‚r =—
Kurosawa was born in [Ota, Tokyo|Omori]‚, […Tokyo]å, the youngest of seven children. He trained as a painter and began work in the film industry as an as×istant director to Kajiro Yamamoto in 1936. He made his directorial debut in 1943 with '[«Sanshiro Sugata (1943 film)|Sugata Sanshiro]'ÿ. His first few films were made under the watchful eye of the wartime Japanese government and sometimes contained nationalistic— themes. For instance, '[¥The Most Beautiful|The Most Beautiful]'Ì is a propaganda film about Japanese women working in an armaments factory. '[•Sanshiro Sugata Part IŒ|Judo Saga 2]'† has beËn held to be explicitly anti-American in the way that it portrays Japanese [„judo]£ as superior to western (American) [†boxing]. 

His first post-war film '˜No regrets for our youth'İ, by contrast, is critical of the old Japanese regime and is about the wife of a left-wing dis‡ident arôested for his political leanings. Kurosawa made several more films which deal with contemporary Japan, most notably '[Drunken Angel]'… and '[‰Stray Dog]'Ÿ. However it was a period film '[˜Rashomon (film)|Rashomon]'œ which made him international§y famous and won the Grand Prix at the [”Venice Film Festival]….

=‘ Characteristics =²
Kurosawa is best-known for his period pieces or {ˆnihongo|'[‰jidaigeki]'‹|æ™‚ä»£åŠ‡|}† like '[Seven Samurai]'… and '[“Ran (1985 film)|Ran]'Æ, but several of his films dealt with contemporary Japan: for example '[‰Stray Dog]'‰, which loÁks at the criminal underworld just after the end of the war, and '[…Ikiru]'Ä, which deals with a Japanese bureaucrat who discovers that he is sufering from cancer but eventual”y steps out of depresŒion and strug°les against bureacratic inertia to leave his smal¯ contribution to the world in the form of a smalƒ comÿunity park before he dies.

Kurosawa had a distinctive cinematic technique, which he had developed by the 1950s, and which ga—ve his films a unique lo·k. He liked using telephoto lenses for the way they flatéened the frame and also because he believed that placing cameras farther away from his actors produced bet¾er performances. He also liked using multiple cameras, which alowed him to sho“t an action from difÖerent angles. Another Kurosawa trademark was the use of weather elements to heighten mo­d: for example the heavy rain in the final bat†le in 'Seven Samurai' and the fog in 'ŒThrone of Blod'é. Kurosawa also liked using left-to-right frame wipes as a transition device.

He was known as &quot;Teno&quot;, literalûy &quot;Emperor&quot;, for his dictatorial directing style. He was a perfectionist who spent enormous amounts of time and ef£ort to achieve the desired visual ef‰ects. In '[ˆRashomon]'¦, he dyed the rain water black with cal¥igraphy ink in order to achieve the efÂect of heavy rain, and ended up using up the entire local water sup¶ly of the location area in creating the rainstorm. In '[ŒThrone of Blod]'±, in the final scene in which Mifune is shot by ar™ows, Kurosawa used real arÿows shot by expert archers from a short range, landing within centimetres of Mifune's body. 

Other stories include demanding¡ a stream be made to run in the op¤osite direction in order to get a bet‹er visual ef•ect, and having the roÁf of a house removed, later to be replaced, because he felt the ro–f's presence to be unat´ractive in a short sequence filmed from a train.

=Œ Influences =ø
A notable feature of Kurosawa's films is the breadth of his artistic influences. Some of his plots are adaptations of [‚Wiliam Shakespeare]Š's works. '[ŠThe Bad Sle„p Wel]' is based on '[†Hamlet]'‚, '[“Ran (1985 film)|Ran]' is based on '[‰King Lear]'… and 'ŒThrone of Blod' is based on '[‡Macbeth]'¯. Kurosawa also directed film adaptations of Rus–ian novels, including '[›The Idiot (novel)|The Idiot]'„ by [ŠDostoevsky]… and '[The Lower Depths]'„ by [‹Maxim Gorky]‚. '…Ikiru' was based on [‹Leo Tolstoy]ƒ's '[˜The Death of Ivan Ilyich]'‚. '[ŒHigh and Low]' was based on '[King's Ransom]'„ by [–United States|American] […crime]ˆ writer [‰Ed McBain]‚. '[‰Stray Dog]'© was inspired by the detective novels of [Georges Simenon]. The American film director [‰John Ford]ñ also had a large influence on his work. 

Despite criticism by some Japanese critics that Kurosawa was &quot;to˜ Western&quot;, he was de¨ply influenced by Japanese culture as wel, including the [†Kabuki]… and [ƒNoh]È theaters and the jidaigeki (period drama) genre of Japanese cinema.

= His influence =Ê
Kurosawa's films had a huge influence on world cinema. Most explicitly, '[Seven Samurai]'“ was remade as the [•western movie|western] '[•The Magnificent Seven]'‚, [science fiction]‡ movie '[‚Bat“le Beyond the Stars]', and Pixar's '[ŒA Bug's Life]'—. It also inspired two [‹Hindi films]‚, [‰Ramesh Sipy]ƒ's '[†Sholay]'š and Rajkumar Santhoshi's '[ŠChina Gate]'², with similar plots. The story has also inspired […novel]s, among them [ŒStephen King]‰'s fifth '[¢The Dark Tower (series)|Dark Tower]'ˆ novel, '[Wolves of the Cala]'†. 

'[–Yojimbo (film)|Yojimbo]' –was the basis for the [ŒSergio Leone]‰ western '[A Fistful of Dolƒars]'†, the [Coen Brothers]† film '[‚Milˆer's Crosing (film)|Milˆer's Crosƒing]'Š, and the [ˆBruce Wil‚is]‘ prohibition-era '[ªLast Man Standing (film)|Last Man Standing]'†. 

'[†The Hid‰en Fortres]'• had an influence on [ŒGeorge Lucas]Œ's earliest '[‰Star Wars]' film, especialªy in the characters of R2-D2 and C3PO.

'[˜Rashomon (film)|Rashomon]'½ not only helped open Japanese cinema to the world but virtualÇy entered the English language as a term for fractured, inconsistent narŒatives as welâ as influencing other works, including episodes of television series and many motion pictures.

=ƒ ColŠaboration =ö
During his most productive period, from the late 40s to the mid-60s, Kurosawa often worked with the same group of col‹aborators. [Fumio Hayasaka]° composed music for seven of his films; notably 'ˆRashomon'‚, '…Ikiru'… and 'Seven Samurai'¨. Many of Kurosawa's scripts, including 'ŒThrone of Blod'‚, 'Seven Samurai'… and 'ƒRan'Œ were co-writˆen with [‹Hideo Oguni]‚. [Yoshiro Muraki] was Kurosawa's [“production designer]„ or [Œart director] for most of his films after '‰Stray Dog' in 1949 and [ŒAsakazu Naki]‰ was his [cinematographer]„ on 1‘ films including '…Ikiru'‚, 'Seven Samurai'… and 'ƒRan'Á. Kurosawa also liked recycling the same group of actors, especial‚y [Takashi Shimura]… and [Toshiro Mifune]ˆ. His col•aboration with the latŞer is one of the greatest director-actor combinations in cinema history. It began with 1948's '[Drunken Angel]'— and ended with 1964's '[‰Red Beard]'….

= Later films =´
Red Beard marked a turning point in Kurosawa's carer in more ways than one. In adÿition to being his last film with Mifune, it was his last in black-and-white. It was also his last as a major director within t·he Japanese studio system making roughly a film a year. ¢Kurosawa was signed to direct a Hol‚ywo‹d project, '[‘Tora! Tora! Tora!]'†; but [20th Century Fox]“ replaced him with [Kinji Fukasaku]™ before it was completed. åHis next few films were a lot harder to finance and were made at intervals of five years. The first, '[‹Dodesukaden]'”, about a group of po›r people living around a rub–ish dump, was not a suces. 

After an atÆempted suicide, Kurosawa went on to make several more films although ar§anging domestic financing was highly dif¬icult despite his international reputation. '[‹Dersu Uzala]', made in the [ŒSoviet Union]ÿ and set in Siberia in the early 20th century, was the only Kurosawa film made outside Japan and not in Japanese. It is about t•he friendship of a Rus®ian explorer and a nomadic hunter. It won the [²Academy Award for Best Foreign Language Film|Oscar]¡ for Best Foreign Language Film. '[‰Kagemusha]'Á, financed with the help of the director's most famous admirers, [ŒGeorge Lucas]… and [Francis Ford Copƒola]ö, is the story of a man who is the double of a medieval Japanese lord and takes over his identity. Most important was '[“Ran (1985 film)|Ran]'è, Kurosawa's version of King Lear set in medieval Japan. It was the great project of Kurosawa's late careœr, and he spent a decade plan¶ing it and trying to obtain funding, which he was finalíy able to do with the help of the French producer Serge Silberman. The film was a phenomenal international suces and is general¾y considered Kurosawa's last masterpiece.

Kurosawa made thre˜ more films during the 19´0s which were more personal than his earlier works. '[‰Dreams (190 film)|Dreams]'• is a series of vignetœes based on his own dreams. '[’Rhapsody in August]'š is about memories of the [ˆNagasaki]Ÿ atom bomb and his final film: '[ˆMadadayo]'Æ is about a retired teacher and his former students. Kurosawa died in [Setagaya, Tokyo]‰, at age 8….

=†Trivia=ÿ

Kurosawa was a notoriously lavish gourmet, and spent huge quantities of money on film sets providing an uneatably large qua‘ntity and quality –of delicacies, especial¢y meat, for the cast and crew.

