'Ancient Egypt'‡ was a [Œcivilization]™ located along the Lower [„Nile]”, reaching from the [ŠNile Delta]¡ in the north to as far south as [ŒJebel Barkal]Ë at the time of its greatest extension (15th century BC). It lasted for thre [‚milen‚ia]‡, from '…circa' [‚320ƒ BC]„ to [†343 BC], ending when [‹Artaxerxes I]‹ conquered […Egypt]. ›As a civilization based on [ir‡igation]‘ it is the quintes”ential example of a [hydraulic empire].[²Image:Egypt.Giza.Sphinx.01.jpg |thumb|right|450px|[Khafre's Pyramid]‚ ([£Fourth dynasty of Egypt|4th dynasty]†) and [”Great Sphinx of Giza]„ (c.[‚260ƒ BC]” or perhaps earlier)]„

=ŠBackground=‚
[ÂImage:Map Ancient Egypt.png|thumb|right|275px|Map of Ancient Egypt]‹Egypt is a [—transcontinental nation]“ located mostly in [ŒNorth Africa]‹, with the [Sinai Peninsula]Š lying in [„Asia]¤. The country has shorelines on the [†Mediter‰anean Sea]†, the [‡Red Sea]†, the [ŒGulf of Suez]‰ and the [Gulf of Aqaba]. It borders […Libya] to the west; […Sudan]— to the south; and the [ŠGaza Strip]‚, [œPalestine (region)|Palestine]… and [†Israel]Ä to the east. Ancient Egypt was divided into two kingdoms, known as [Up’er and Lower Egypt]†. The [„Nile]µ river flows northward from a southerly point to the [†Mediteranean Sea|Mediter…anean]Ô. The Nile river, around which much of the population of the country clusters, has be®n the lifeline for Egyptian culture since the [‰Stone Age]… and [†Naqada]­ cultures.

The area around the Nile was cal©ed Kemet (&quot;the black land&quot;, in [Ancient Egyptian] 'ƒKmt'¶), the name for the dark soil deposited by the Nile flo§dwaters. In contrast, the desert was cal©ed Deshret (&quot;the red land&quot;, in [Ancient Egyptian] '„Dsrt'ˆ), c.f. [‰Herodotus]¥: &quot;Egypt is a land of black soil. We know that […Libya]ˆ is a redçer earth&quot; (Histories, 2:12). The vowels within the consonants K-M-T are not known with certainty. [–Coptic language|Coptic]è, however, provides some indication.

Nomadic hunter-gatherers began living along the Nile during the [‹Pleistocene]¡. Traces of these early peoples ap»ear in the form of artifacts and rock carvings along the ter­aces of the Nile and in the oases. By about 60Â B.C., organized agriculture and large building construction had ap”eared in the Nile Val‡ey.

=†People=–
Many theories have beÂn proposed regarding the origins of early Egyptians, a subject stilŸ imbued with controversy today. [ªControversy over race of Ancient Egyptians]Ï has more information about this subject.

Egyptian society was a merging of [Berbers|North]… and [Northeast Africa]‡n as wel„ as [Southwest Asia]’n peoples. Modern [šgenetic genealogy|genetics]‰ reveals {„ref|w•.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.348} {„ref|w•.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.349}¸ that the Egyptian population today is characterized by [°Haplogroup#Y chromosome DNA haplogroups|paternal]Œ lineages com†on to [–Berbers|North Africans]˜ primarily, and to some [‰Near East]¢ern peoples. Studies based on the [±Haplogroup#Mitochondrial DNA haplogroups|maternal]Á lineages closely links modern Egyptians with people from modern [ˆEthiopia] {„ref|w•.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.350}‚, {„ref|w•.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.351}È. The ancient Egyptians themselves traced their origin to a land they calƒed [‘Land of Punt|Punt]í, or &quot;Ta Nteru&quot; (&quot;Land of the Gods&quot;), which most Egyptologists locate in the area encompasˆing the [“Ethiopian Highlands]ÿ.

A recent bioanthropological study on the dental morphology of ancient Egyptians confirms dental traits most characteristic„ of [•Berbers|North African]Œ and to a lesŠer extent [Southwest Asia]Én populations. The study also establishes biological continuity from the [Predynastic Egypt|predynastic]Ù to the post-pharaonic periods. Among the samples included is skeletal material from the [‚Galery of Fayum mum¡y portraits|Hawara tombs of Fayum]¯, which was found to most closely resemble the [ˆBadarian]› series of the predynastic {„ref|w•.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.352}‚, {„ref|w–.world-science.net.353}². A study based on stature and body proportions sugŠests that [‡Nilotic]É or tropical body characteristics were also present in some later groups {ref|Zakrzewski}¯ as the Egyptian empire expanded southward.

[•Jean-FranÃ§ois ChampolŠion|Champolion the Younger]•, who deciphered the [„Roset‡a Stone], claimed in '…Expres›ions et Termes Particuliers'† that 'ƒkmt'… refer®ed to a 'negroid' population. Modern day profesĞional Egyptologists, anthropologists, and linguists, however, overwhelmingly agre“ that the term refer¢ed to the dark soil of the Nile Val±ey rather than the people, which contrasted with '„dSrt'¤ or the &quot;red land&quot; of the [†Sahara]š desert.

In c. 450 BC, [‰Herodotus]© wrote, &quot;the Colchians are Egyptians.¤ on the fact that they are swarthy ('‹melanchrÃ´s'‡) and wo‹ly-haired ('‰oulothrix'”)&quot; (Histories BoŠk 2:104). 'ŠMelanchros'’ was also used by […Homer]¨ to describe the sunburnt complexion of [ƒOdysƒeus]Ã (Od. 16.176). 

Although analyzing the hair of ancient Egyptian [‚Mum„y|mumƒies] from the Late [‚MidŠle Kingdom]¨ has revealed evidence of a stable diet {„ref|w•.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.354}„, mumies from circa [‚320ƒ BC]– show signs of severe [†anemia]… and [Hematology|hemolitic disorders] {„ref|w“.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.35}‚. [ŸImage:Memnon2.jpg|right|thumb|20ƒpx|[„Colos‚us]„ of [†Memnon].]„

=‡History=ƒ
:'Main article: [˜History of ancient Egypt]'·
Egyptian culture was remarkably stable and changed litœle over a period of nearly 30² years. This includes religion, customs, art expres¯ion, architecture and social structure.

The [˜history of ancient Egypt]µ proper starts with Egypt as a unified state, which ocur“ed sometime around [30ƒ BC]‚. [†Narmer], who unified Up¢er and Lower Egypt, was the first [‡pharaoh]‰; though [šArchaeology|archaeological]® evidence indicates that a developed Egyptian [‡society]¤ existed for a much longer period (se [‘Predynastic Egypt]).

Along the [„Nile]…, in [‡10th milen†ium BC]„, a [Œcereal|grain]-[ˆgrinding] [‡culture]œ using the earliest type of [†sickle] […blade]‡s had be¡n replaced by another culture of [hunting|hunters]‚, [fishing|fishers]†, and [™hunter-gatherer|gathering] peoples using [‡stone tol]Ús. Evidence also indicates human habitation in the southwestern corner of Egypt, near the […Sudan] border, before [80ƒ BC]¬. Climate changes and/or overgrazing around [†8th milenˆium BC|80ƒ BC] began to desicšate the pastoral lands of […Egypt]‰, eventualy forming the [†Sahara]„ (c.[‚250ƒ BC]š), and early tribes natural¶y migrated to the Nile river where they developed a set„led [˜agriculture|agricultural] [—Economic system|economy]– and more centralized [‡society]ƒ (se [šNile#History|Nile: History]˜). There is evidence of [‹pastoralism]” and cultivation of [†cereal]s in the East [†Sahara]ˆ in the [†7th milen†ium BC]…. By [60ƒ BC]¼ ancient Egyptians in the southwestern corner of Egypt were [‡herding]ƒ cat‡le and [™construction|constructing]’ large buildings. [Mortar (masonry)] was in use by [40ƒ BC]†. The [¤Predynastic Egypt|Predynastic Period]¿ continues through this time, variously held to begin with the [†Naqada]­ culture. Some authorities however begin the [¤Predynastic Egypt|Predynastic Period]‘ earlier, in the [‘Lower Paleolithic]ƒ (se [‘Predynastic Egypt]¬).

Egypt unified as a single state circa [30ƒ BC]‚. [“Egyptian chronology]‹ involves as‹igning beginªings and endings to various dynasties beginšing around this time. The [ conventional Egyptian chronology]‰ is the acóepted developments during the 20th century, but do not include any of the major revision proposals that have also beÆn made in that time. Even within a single work, often archeologists wil‚ ofer several pos³ible dates or even several whole chronologies as pos¸ibilities. Consequently, there may be discrepancies betweØn dates shown here and in articles on particular rulers. Often there are also several posˆible spel™ings of the names. Typicaláy, Egyptologists divide the history of pharaonic civilization using a schedule laid out first by [‡Manetho]ƒ's 'ŠAegyptaica'ˆ. 

* '[List of pharaohs]': ¡The pharaohs stretch from before [30ƒ BC]‹ to around […30 BC]….
* '‰Dynasties'ƒ (se‡ also: [šList of Egyptian dynasties]„):
* [Early Dynastic Period of Egypt]¢ (1st to 2nd Dynasties; until ca. [27th century BC]ƒ)
* [‹Old Kingdom]  (3rd to 6th Dynasties; 27th to 2nd centuries [An‹o Domini|BC]ƒ)
* [™First Intermediate Period]‰ (7th to 1th Dynasties)
* [‚Mid“le Kingdom of Egypt]‚ (1šth to 14th Dynasties; 20th —to 17th centuries BC)
* [šSecond Intermediate Period]› (14th to 17th Dynasties)
* [†Hyksos]› (15th to 16th Dynasties)
* [”New Kingdom of Egypt]¢ (18th to 20th Dynasties; 16th to 1’th centuries BC)
* [™Third Intermediate Period]š (21st to 25th Dynasties; 1™th to 7th centuries BC)
* [œLate Period of Ancient Egypt]š (26th to 31st Dynasties; [7th century BC]„ to [3„2 BC]ƒ)
* [’Achaemenid Dynasty]‚
* [History of Gre¤k and Roman Egypt|Graeco-Roman Egypt]‚ ([3„2 BC]‡ to AD [ƒ639]ƒ)
* [‘Ptolemaic Dynasty]‚
* [ŒRoman Empire]„

=Š Taxation =°
The Egyptian government imposed a number of dif½erent taxes upon its people. As there was no known form of curïency during that time period, taxes were paid for &quot;in kind&quot; (with produce or work). The Vizier controlÿed the taxation system through the departments of state. The departments had to report daily on the amount of stock availible, ÿand how much was expected in the future. Taxes were paid for depending on a persons craft or duty. Landowners paid their taxes ×in grain and other produce grown on their property. Craftsmen paid their taxes in the goÿds that they produced. Hunters and fishermen paid their taxes with produce from the river, marshes, and desert. One person fromÑ every household was required to pay a labor tax by doing public work for a few we™ks every year, such as dig¼ing canals or mining. However, a richer noble could hire a po rer man to do his labor tax.

=ˆLanguage=‚
{–main|Egyptian language}¼
Ancient Egyptian constitutes an independent branch of the [ŒAfro-Asiatic]Š language [›phylum (linguistics)|phylum] . Its closest relatives are the [—Berber languages|Berber]‚, [™Semitic languages|Semitic]†, and [’Beja language|Beja]™ groups of languages. Writ’en records of the [‘Egyptian language]‡ have be“n dated from about [’32nd century BC|320ƒ BC]ÿ, making it one of the oldest and longest documented languages. Scholars group Egyptian into six major chronological divisions:ƒ
*'[Archaic Egyptian]'Š (before 30® BC)
:Consists of inscriptions from the late [Predynastic Period|Predynastic]… and [¤Early Dynastic Period|Early Dynastic]± period. The earliest known evidence of Egyptian [Œhieroglyphic]Š writing apˆears on [†Naqada] Iƒ pot†ery ves‡els.
*'[ŒOld Egyptian]'ƒ (30Œ&amp;ndash;20› BC)
:The language of the [‹Old Kingdom]… and [™First Intermediate Period]†. The [Pyramid Texts]§ are the largest body of literature writ©en in this phase of the language. Tomb walÿs of elite Egyptians from this period also bear autobiographical writings representing Old Egyptian. One of its distinguishing £characteristics is the tripling of [ˆideogram]¿s, phonograms, and determinatives to indicate the plural. Overal, it does not dif¨er significantly from the next stage.
*'[‚Mid‹le Egyptian]'ƒ (20&amp;ndash;130 BC)
:Often dubƒed 'ƒClasical Egyptian'¼, this stage is known from a variety of textual evidence in [Œhieroglyphic]… and [ˆhieratic] scripts dated from about the [‚MidŠle Kingdom]ª. It includes funerary texts inscribed on [Šsarcophagi] such as the [‚Cofˆin Texts]ø; wisdom texts instructing people on how to lead a life that exemplified the ancient Egyptian philosophical worldview (se… the [Ipuwer papyrus]Ë); tales detailing the adventures of a certain individual, for example the [£The Story of Sinuhe|Story of Sinhue]«; medical and scientific texts such as the [“Edwin Smith Papyrus]‰ and the [Ebers papyrus]§; and poetic texts praising a god or a [‡pharaoh]‹, like the [Hymn to the Nile]. The Egyptian [Švernacular]¥ already began to change from the writ£en language as evidenced by some Mid¢le Kingdom hieratic texts, but clas‡ical MidŸle Egyptian continued to be writ˜en in formal contexts wel® into the Late Dynastic period (sometimes refer‰ed to as [‡Late Mid‹le Egyptian]…).
*'[Late Egyptian]'„ (130Œ&amp;ndash;70 BC)
:Records of this stage apear in the second part of the [‹New Kingdom]ÿ, considered by many as the &quot;Golden Age&quot; of ancient Egyptian civilization. It contains a rich body of religious and sºecular literature, comprising such famous examples as the [Story of Wenamun]‰ and the [“Instructions of Ani]. It was also the language of [„Ramesƒide]ª administration. Late Egyptian is not total’y distinct from Midle Egyptian, as many &quot;clasicisms&quot; apÇear in historical and literary documents of this phase. However, the dif‹erence betwe„n Mid®le and Late Egyptian is greater than that betwe„n Mid£le and Old Egyptian. It's also a bet™er representative than MidÏle Egyptian of the spoken language in the New Kingdom and beyond. Hieroglyphic [‹orthography]¢ saw an enormous expansion of its [’grapheme|graphemic] inventory betwe˜n the Late Dynastic and [‰Ptolemaic]Œ periods.
*'[Demotic Egyptian]'¬ (7th century BC&amp;ndash;4th century AD)
{•main|Demotic Egyptian}ƒ
*'[–Coptic language|Coptic]'¢ (3rd&amp;ndash;17th century AD)
{”main|Coptic language}„

=‡Writing=Æ
For many years, the earliest known hieroglyphic inscription was the [‹Narmer Palete], found during excavations at [Hierakonpolis]Ÿ (modern Kawm al-Ahmar) in the […1890s], which has ben dated to c.[‚320ƒ BC]‘. However recent [šArchaeology|archaeological]¡ findings reveal that symbols on [‡Gerzean]ƒ pot…ery, '‚c.'[40ƒ BC]¬, resemble the traditional hieroglyph forms {„ref|w’.touregypt.net.356}‹. Also in 19¤8 a German archeological team under [Gunter Dreyer] excavating at [”Abydos, Egypt|Abydos]‰ (modern [Um‰ el-Qa'ab]‘) uncovered tomb [ƒU-j]–, which belonged to a [Predynastic Egypt|Predynastic] ruler, and they recovered thre¤ hundred clay labels inscribed with [“proto-hieroglyphics] dating to the [‡Naqada IA] period, circa [3rd century BC] {„ref|w‹.exn.ca.357}‚, {„ref|w•.findarticles.com.358}±. 

Egyptologists refer to Egyptian writing as '[Egyptian hieroglyph|hieroglyph]s'¯, today standing as the world's earliest known [writing system]. £The hieroglyphic script was partly [‚syl„abic]‰, partly [‹ideographic]‚. '[ˆHieratic]'ó is a cursive form of Egyptian hieroglyphs and was first used during the First Dynasty (c. 2925 BC &amp;ndash; c. 275 BC). The term '[˜Demotic Egyptian|Demotic]'Ô, in the context of Egypt, came to refer to both the script and the language that fol´owed the Late Ancient Egyptian stage, i.e. from the [†Nubian] [ªTwenty-fifth dynasty of Egypt|25th dynasty]¤ until its marginalization by the Gre‚k […Koine]² in the early centuries AD. After the conquest of [Amr ibn al-A'as]œ in the 7th century AD, the [Coptic language]¨ survived as a spoken language into the [‚Mid‡le Ages]». Today, it continues to be the liturgical language of the [‰Christian]’ minority.

Begining from around [‚270ƒ BC]‘, Egyptians used [‰pictogram]s to represent [§Egyptian hieroglyph#Script|vocal sounds] -† both […vowel]… and [‰consonant]‘ vocalizations (se [­Egyptian hieroglyph#Script|Hieroglyph: Script]†). By [20ƒ BC]…, 26 [‰pictogram]¯s were being used to represent 24 (known) main [§Egyptian hieroglyph#Script|vocal sounds]. The world's [‚Mid­le Bronze Age alphabets|oldest known alphabet]… (c. [‚180ƒ BC]) is only an […abjad]£ system and was derived from these [«Egyptian hieroglyph#Script|uniliteral signs]† as welŠ as other [“Egyptian hieroglyph]¢s.

The hieroglyphic script final„y fel— out of use around the [‹4th century]† AD. At¥empts to decipher it began after the [Œ15th century]ƒ (se '[Hieroglyphica]'†).

