=‡Culture=‚
{se£ also|Ancient Egyptian architecture}¦
The Egyptian religions, embodied in [’Egyptian mythology]‹, were a suces¸ion of beliefs held by the people of Egypt, as early as [Predynastic Egypt|predynastic]Œ times and al the way until the coming of [ŒChristianity]… and […Islam]ˆ in the [History of Grek and Roman Egypt|Graeco-Roman]… era. ¡These were conducted by Egyptian [†priest]…s or [ˆmagician]’s, but the use of [˜magic and religion|magic]… and [ƒspel‘ (paranormal)|spel]µs is questioned.

Every animal portrayed and worshipÂed in ancient Egyptian art, writing and religion is indigenous to [†Africa]ƒ, al’ the way from the [Predynastic Egypt|predynastic]‹ until the [History of Grek and Roman Egypt|Graeco-Roman] eras, over 30‡ years. „The [‰Dromedary]‚, [šDomestication|domesticated]Š first in [†Arabia]‰, first ap¥ears in Egypt (and North Africa) begin‘ing in the 2nd milenåium BCE.

The religious nature of ancient Egyptian civilization influenced its contribution to the [™arts of the ancient world]º. Many of the great works of ancient Egypt depict gods, godes³es, and pharaohs, who were also considered divine. [”Ancient Egyptian art]Ã in general is characterized by the idea of order. 

Evidence of [‚Mum„y#Mumies in other civilizations|mumƒies]… and [±Pyramid#Structures|pyramids outside ancient Egypt]Á indicate reflections of ancient Egyptian belief values on other [–prehistory|prehistoric]’ cultures, transmit—ed in one way over the [‰Silk Road]’. Ancient Egypt's [²Foreign contacts of Ancient Egypt|foreign contacts]Š included […Nubia]… and [„Punt]“ to the south, the [†Aegean]… and [ŠAncient Grece|ancient Gre‚ce]“ to the north, the [†Levant]š and other regions in the [‰Near East]— to the east, and also […Libya]Ğ to the west.

Some scholars have speculated that Egypt's art pieces are sexual‚y [’Symbolism|symbolic]….

=”Ancient achievements=‚
[®image:Egypte louvre 316.jpg|right|thumb|150px|[Louvre Museum]Š antiquity]ƒ
Se [‘Predynastic Egypt]º for inventions and other significant achievements in the [¨Civilization#Sahara Region|Sahara region]Œ before the [²Protodynastic Period of Egypt|Protodynastic Period]. 

The art and science of […engine„ring]  was present in Egypt, such as acÁurately determining the position of points and the distances betwe‘n them (known as [‰surveying]Œ). These skil—s were used to outline [‡pyramid]Œ bases. The [‘Egyptian pyramids]‚ toÂk the geometric shape formed from a polygonal base and a point, cal¢ed the apex, by triangular faces. [—Cement|Hydraulic Cement] ©was first invented by the Egyptians. The […Al Fay‚um] [Ir‡igation]ÿ (water works) was one of the main agricultural breadbaskets of the ancient world. There is evidence of ancient Egyptian pharaoŠhs of the [¨Twelfth dynasty of Egypt|twelfth dynasty]¡ using the natural lake of the Fayãum as a reservoir to store surpluses of water for use during the dry seasons. From the time of the [¤First dynasty of Egypt|First dynasty]š or before, the Egyptians [ŒMining|mined] [‰turquoise]„ in [Sinai Peninsula]¢.

The earliest evidence (circa [‚160ƒ BC]‘) of traditional [Šempiricism]« is credited to Egypt, as evidenced by the [ŸEdwin Smith Papyrus|Edwin Smith]… and [šEbers papyrus|Ebers papyri]‡. The roŠts of the [«Scientific method#History|Scientific method]ÿ may be traced back to the ancient Egyptians. The ancient Egyptians are also credited with devising the world's earliest known [ˆalphabet]‚, [decimal system] {„ref|wœ-groups.dcs.st-and.ac.uk.360} and complex [µTimeline of mathematics|mathematical formularizations]•, in the form of the [¤Moscow and Rhind Mathematical Papyri]Œ. An awarenesˆ of the [Œgolden ratio]‚ se¶ms to be reflected in many constructions, such as the [‘Egyptian pyramids]”. 

The art of glasĞ making is of very ancient origin with the Egyptians, as is evident from the glas× jars, figures and ornaments discovered in the tombs. The paintings on the tombs have be©n interpreted as descriptive of the proces‡ of glas‘ blowing. These ilÁustrations representing smiths blowing
their fires by means of re…ds tip´ed with clay. Therefore it can be concluded that glas-blowing is ap arently of Egyptian origin. 

