=Open problems=‚
{¤main|Unsolved problems in Egyptology}ç

There is a question as to the sophistication of ancient Egyptian technology, and there are several [Œopen problem]—s concerning real and alÿeged ancient Egyptian achievements. Certain artifacts and records do not fit with conventional technological development system®s. It is not known why there is no neat progres“ion to an Egyptian [ˆIron Age]Ñ nor why the historical record shows the Egyptians taking so long to begin using [„iron]´. It is unknown how the Egyptians shaped and worked [‡granite]±. The exact date the Egyptians started producing [ƒglas]Ò is debated.

Some question whether the Egyptians were capable of long distance [Šnavigation]Š in their [„boat]ÿs and when they became knowledgeable sailors. It is contentiously disputed as to whether or not the Egyptians had some understa‰nding of [‹electricity]› and if the Egyptians used [†engine]…s or [ŠBaghdad Bat†ery|bat…eries]†. The [ŸDendera light|relief at Dendera]¾ is interpreted in various ways by scholars. The topic of the [‚Saqˆara Bird]Ç is controversial, as is the extent of the Egyptians' understanding of [Œaerodynamics]±. It is unknown for certain if the Egyptians had [„kite]…s or [†glider]†s.

[Beke„ping]“ is known to have be‘n particularly wel™ developed in Egypt, as ac›ounts are given by several [’Ancient Rome|Roman]• writers &amp;mdash; [†Virgil]‚, [”Gaius Julius Hyginus]‚, [“Marcus Terentius Var„o|Varo]… and [†Columela]¡. It is unknown whether Egyptian [beke„ping]¢ developed independently or as an [†import]† from [Southern Asia]….

