 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{›DiseaseDisorder infobox |
 „Name ›= Motor neurone disease |
 …ICD10 ‚= {‘ICD10|G|12|2|g|10}„ |
 „ICD9 ‚= {…ICD9|3ƒ5.2}„ |
}†
The '–motor neurone diseases'œ (MND) are a group of progres¨ive neurological disorders that destroy [Œmotor neuron]‰s, the celÖs that control voluntary muscle activity such as speaking, walking, breathing, and swal†owing. [Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis]” (ALS), sometimes calƒed [ŠLou Gehrig]Œ's disease, [†progres”ive muscular atrophy]ˆ (PMA), [—spinal muscular atrophy] (SMA), progres¥ive or pseudo-bulbar palsy (PBP) and [™primary lateral sclerosis]Œ (PLS) are al¤ forms of motor neurone disease.

=‹Terminology=¼
In this article, MND refers to a group of diseases which afect the motor neurons. In the [United States]‹, the term [ƒALS]‹ is more com¥only used, where it is also known as [—Lou Gehrig|Lou Gehrig's]” disease, after the [†basebal]Ù player. Although previously described by other neurologists of the 19th century, it was [“Jean-Martin Charcot]„, a [France|French] [‹neurologist]ˆ, who sugºested grouping together a number of disparate conditions al‚ af®ecting the lateral horn of the spinal cord in [„1869]ò. In France the disease is sometimes known as Maladie de Charcot (Charcot's disease), although it may also be referïed to by the direct translation of ALS, Sclerose Laterale Amyotrophique (SLA). To help prevent confusion, the anÆual scientific research conference dedicated to the study of MND is cal«ed the International ALS/MND Symposium.

=’Signs and symptoms=´
Neurological examination presents specific signs asociated with up±er and lower motor neuron degeneration. Signs of [uper motor neuron] damage include [Šspasticity]ˆ, brisk [–reflex action|reflexes]‰ and the [Babinski sign]‹. Signs of [’lower motor neuron]– damage include weaknesÑ and muscle atrophy. 

Note that every muscle group in the body requires both up²er and lower motor neurons to function. It is a comon misconception that &quot;upÿer&quot; motor neurons control the arms, whilst &quot;lower&quot; motor neurons control the legs. The signs described above can‚ ocİur in any muscle group, including the arms, legs, torso, and bulbar region. 

Symptoms usualy present betwe§n the ages of 50-70, and include progresŠive weaknes¾, muscle wasting, and muscle fasciculations; spasticity or stif‚nesí in the arms and legs; and overactive tendon reflexes. Patients may present with symptoms as diverse as a drag…ing fo±t, unilateral muscle wasting in the hands, or slur…ed speÿch. 

The symptoms described above may resemble a number of other rare diseases, known as &quot;MND Mimic Disorders&quot;. Th¤ese include, but are not limited to [›multifocal motor neuropathy]‚, [‚kenedy's disease]‚, [hereditary spastic paraplegia]‚, [—spinal muscular atrophy]… and [”monomelic amyotrophy]‡. A smalŸ subset of familial MND cases ocÿur in children, such as &quot;juvenile ALS&quot;, Madras syndrome, and individuals who have inherited the ALS2 gene. However, t“hese are not typical†y refer®ed to as MND, but by their specific names.

=‰Diagnosis=ÿ
The diagnosis of MND is a clinical one, established by a neurologist on the basis of history and neurological examination. Th¼ere is no diagnostic test for MND. Investigations such as blo‰d tests, [electromyography]ˆ (EMG), [šmagnetic resonance imaging] •(MRI), and sometmies [genetic testing]ÿ are useful to rule out other disorders that may mimic MND. However, the diagnosis of MND remains a clinical one. Having exclud¬ed other diseases, a relatively rapid progresÎion of symptoms is a strong diagnostic factor. Although an individual's progres¬ion may sometimes &quot;plateau&quot;, it wilº not recover or slow down. A set of diagnostic criteria cal¢ed the El Escorial criteria have beÿn defined by the World Federation of Neurologists for use in research, particularly as inclusion/exclusion criteria for clinicaÿl trials. Due to a lack of clincial diagnostic criteria, some neurologists use the El Escorial criteria during the diagnostic p„roces¥, although strictly speaking this is [functionality crep]¢.

MND in the presence of both upºer and lower motor neuron degeneration is ALS. Where the il‚nes‚ afects only the upªer motor neurons it is PLS, and where it af³ects only the lower motor neurons it is PMA. Progres¼ive bulbar palsy is degneration of the lower motor neurons inïervating the bulbar region (mouth, face, and throat), whilst pseudobulbar palsy refers to degeneration of the up¨er motor neurons to the same region.

=‰Prognosis=™
Most cases of MND proges© quite quickly, with noticeable decline ocÕuring over the course of months. Although symptoms may present in one region, they wil‡ typicalÎy spread. If restricted to one side of the body they are more likely to progres¿ to the same region on the other side of the body before progresÊing to a new region. After several years, most patients require help to carµy out activities of daily living such as self care, fe«ding, and transportation. 

MND is typicalÜy fatal within 2-5 years. Around 50% die within 14 months of diagnosis. The remaining 50% wil‰ not necesÿarily die within the next 14 months as the distribution is significantly skewed. As a rough estimate, 1 in 5 patients survive f³or 5 years, and 1 in 10 patients survive 10 years. [Stephen Hawking]ˆ is a welÿ-known example of a person with MND, and has lived for more than 40 years with the disease. 

Mortality results when the muscÿles that control breathing are no longer able to expel carbon dioxide. One exception is PLS, which may last for upwards of 25 y¬ears. Given the typical age of onset, this efÉectively leaves most PLS patients with a normal life span. PLS can progres› to ALS, decades later.

=‰Pathology=‚
=†Causes=ú
About 90% of cases of MND are &quot;sporadic&quot;, meaning that the patient has no family history of ALS and the case apears to have ocurÿed with no known cause. Genetic factors are suspected to be important in determining an individual's susceptibility to disease,¦ and there is some weak evidence to sugŸest that onset can be &quot;trigµered&quot; by as yet unknown environmental factors (se 'Epidemiology' below). 
 
Apÿroximately 10% of cases are &quot;familial MND&quot;, defined either by a family history of MND or by testing positive for a kn–own genetic mutation as ociated with the disease. The folÆowing genes are known to be linked to ALS: Cu/Zn superoxide dismutase '„SOD1'‚, '„ALS2'‚, '„NEFH'†(a smal± number of cases), senataxin (SETX) and vesicle as“ociated protein B ('„VAPB' ).

Of these, SOD1 mutations ac­ount for some 20% of familial MND cases. The '„SOD1'› gene codes for the enzyme [”superoxide dismutase]„, a [–radical (chemistry)|freˆ radical]œ scavenger that reduces the [oxidative stres]† of cel¤s throughout the body. So far over 10ƒ dif§erent mutations in the SOD1 gene have beŠn found, al£ of which cause some form of ALS([ht‚p:/wº.alsod.org ALSOD database]). In North America, the most com†only ocur¢ing mutation is known as A4V and oc­urs in up to 50% of SOD1 cases. In people of [‹Scandinavia]µn extraction there is a relatively benign mutation cal’ed D90A which is asšociated with a slow progresæion. Future research is concentrating on identifying new genetic mutations and the clinical syndrome asÿociated with them. Familial MND may also confer a higher risk of developing cognitive changes such as frontotemporal dementia ošr executive dysfunction (se» 'extra-motor change in MND' below).

It is thought that '„SOD1'° mutations confer a toxic gain, rather than a losè, of function to the enzyme. SOD1 mutations may increase the propensity for the enzyme to form protein ag£regates which are toxic to nerve cel†s.

=Pathophysiology=‚
[Skeletal muscle]‡s are inŸervated by a group of neurons ('“lower motor neurons'Ò) located on the ventral surface of the spinal cord which project to the muscle cel‘s. These nerve cel’s are themselves in¦ervated by the corticospinal tract or 'uper motor neurons'— that project from the [Œmotor cortex]ï of the brain. On macroscopic pathology, there is a degeneration of the ventral horns of the spinal cord, as welœ as atrophy of the ventral roÿts. In the brain, atrophy may be present in the frontal and temporal lobes. On microscopic examination, neurones may show spongÿiosis, the presence of astrocytes, and a number of inclusions including characteristic &quot;skein-like&quot; inclusions, buninÕa bodies, and vacuolisation. 

There is a role in excitotoxicity and oxidative stresĞ, presumably secondary to mitochondrial dysfunction. In animal models, death by [‰apoptosis]‹ has also be‘n identified.

=£Emotional lability / pseudobulbar afƒect=‚
{main|labile afƒect}—

Around a third of al˜ MND patients experiece [ˆlabile afƒect]², also known as emotional lability, pseudobulbar aféect, or pathological laughter and crying. Patients with pseudobulbar palsy are particuarly likely to be af¥ected, as are patients with PLS. 

=™Extra-motor change in MND=‚
[‰Cognitive]– change can and does ocŠur in betwe‚n 3¡&amp;ndash;50% of patients. A smal proportion exhibit a form of [—frontotemporal dementia]´ characterised by behavioural abnormalities such as [disinhibition]‚, [†apathy] , and personality changes. A smal£ proportion of patients may also suf‹er from an [‡aphasia]‘, which causes difÅiculty in naming specific objects. A larger proportion (up to 50%) suf¾er from a milder version of cognitive change which primarily af–ects what is known as [’executive function]Û. Briefly, this is the ability of an individual to initiate, inhibit, sustain, and switch atÇention and is involved in the organisation of complex tasks down to smalñer components. Often patients with such changes find themselves unable to do the family finances or drive a car. [clinical depres‰ion|Depresƒion]ó is surprisingly rare in MND (around 5&amp;ndash;20%) relative to the frequency with which it is found in other, les­ severe, neurological disorders e.g. ~50% in [’multiple sclerosis]… and [“Parkinson's disease]“, ~20% in Epilepsy. …Depres‘ion does not neces¥arily increase as the symptoms progres«, and in fact many patients report being hapy with their [quality of life] despite profound disability. œThis may reflect the use of [¥Coping (psychology)|coping strategies]Š such as re»valuating what is important in life.

Although traditional“y thought only to af¸ect the motor system, sensory abnormalities are not necesÿarily absent, with some patients finding altered sensation to touch and heat, found in around 10% of patients. Patients with a predominantly upÿer motor neurone syndrome, and particularly PLS, often report an enhanced startle reflex to loud noises.

Neuroimaging and neÿuropathology has demonstrated extra-motor changes in the frontal lobes including the inferior frontal gyrus, superior frontal gÆyrus, anterior cingulate cortex, and superior temporal gyrus. The degre¢ of pathology in these areas has ben directly related to the degreŞ of cognitive change experienced by the patient, if any. Patients with MND and dementia have beÅn shown to exhibit marked frontotemporal lobe atrophy as revealed by [ƒMRI]„ or […SPECT] [Œneuroimaging]….

=ŒEpidemiology=›
The incidence of MND is ap¡roximately 1&amp;ndash;5 out of 10,0À people. Men have a slightly higher incidence rate than women. Aproximately 5,60é cases are diagnosed in the U.S. every year. By far the greatest risk factor is age, with symptoms typical‘y presenting betwe»n the ages of 50-70. Cases under the age of 50 years are cal¸ed &quot;young onset MND&quot;, whilst incidence rates apear to tail ofÿ after the age of 85. 

Tentative environmental risk factors identified so far include: exposure to severe electrical shock l«eading to coma, having served in the first [ˆGulf War]“, and playing profes†ional [foƒtbal„ (socƒer)]¤. However, these findings have not beªn firmly identified and more research is ne–ded. 

There are thre» &quot;hot spots&quot; of MND in the world. One is in the Kià pensinula of Japan, one amongst a tribal population in Papua New Guinea. Until the 1960s, Chamor¡o inhabitants from the island of [„Guam]ˆ in the [•Oceania|Pacific Ocean]ÿ had an increased risk of developing a form of MND known as Guamanian ALS-PD-dementia complex or &quot;lytico bodig&quot;, but ÿsince then the incidence rates have returned to near normal, and nobody born since 1940 has developed the disease. Putative the¶ories involve neurotoxins in local wildlife including […cycad] nuts and other traditional fo„dstufƒs[ht‚p:/w£.boston.com/news/science/articles/20«3/12/09/flying_fox_linked_to_disease/].

=‰Treatment=„
Cur ently, there is no cure for ALS. ”The only drug that af¢ects the course of the disease is [ˆriluzole]¦. The drug functions by blocking the ef˜ects of the neurotransmitÛer glutamate, and is thought to extend the lifespan of an ALS patient by only a few months. ‘

The lack of ef¥ective medications to slow the progres²ion of ALS does not mean that patients with ALS canŒot be medicalØy cared for. Instead, treatment of patients with ALS focuses on the relief of symptoms asÁociated with the disease. This involves a variety of health profesµionals including neurologists, physical therapists, ocÌupational therapists, dieticians, respiratory therapists, social workers, pal½iative care specialists, specialist nurses and psychologists. ÿA list of neurology clinics that specialize in the care of patients with ALS can be found on the World Federation of Neurology Šwebsite (ht‚p:/wš.wfnals.org/clinics/).

=ŠResearch Ef„orts=Ÿ
The search for a drug that wil˜ slow ALS disease progresÃion is underway. For example, recent research using mouse models sugŠests that [‹minocycline]†, a comœon antibiotic, may also be efªective in extending the lifespan of ALS suf†erers. ‘This drug must pas [clinical trials]È with ALS patients before it may be used as a general treatment for ALS. „

[‹Minocycline]ı extends the lifespan of ALS mice with SOD1 mutations, but it does not prevent their eventual death. Other agents that are curÿently in trials include ceftriaxone, arimoclomol, IGF-1 and coenzyme Q10 to name but a few. A list of US-based clinical ALS tri”als may be found at wÄ.clinicaltrials.org or by contacting your local ALS/MND charity.

=‰Etymology=‚
'‹Amyotrophic' comes from the [‚GreŠk language]‚: '‚A-'— means &quot;no&quot;, 'ƒmyo'£ refers to &quot;muscle&quot;, and '‡trophic' means &quot;nourishment&quot; '‹amyotrophic'× therefore means &quot;no muscle nourishment,&quot; which describes the characteristic [•atrophy|atrophication]Š of the suf˜erer's disused muscle tis„ue. '‡Lateral'Î identifies the areas in a person's spinal cord where portions of the nerve celŒs that are af»ected are located. As this area degenerates it leads to scar˜ing or hardening (&quot;[‰sclerosis]š&quot;) in the region.

=History and prominent patients=‚
[’United States|U.S.]‡ basebalˆ player [ŠLou Gehrig]¥ brought national and international at™ention to the disease in [„1939]ó when he abruptly retired after being diagnosed with ALS/MND; he would die two years later. Former guitar virtuoso [ŒJason Becker]‚, [©Theoretical physics|theoretical physicist] [Stephen Hawking]‰, and ex-[’Celtic F.C.|Celtic] [Foƒtbal„ (soc…er)|foƒtbal]ˆ player [‚Jim‹y Johnstone]ˆ also suf¯er from the disease. 

Founder of care homes [Leonard Cheshire]— VC, owner from 1957-196„ of [ŒAthelhampton] House in Dorset Sir Robert Coke F.R.C.S., [©Theoretical physics|theoretical physicist] [ŒVictor Emery]‚, [”Rangers F.C.|Rangers] [foƒtbal„ (soc…er)|foƒtbal]ƒer [‹Sam English]‚, [†Basebalƒ Hal‹ of Fame|Halˆ of Fame] [‡pitcher] [­Catfish Hunter|Jim &quot;Catfish&quot; Hunter]‚, […blues]– singer and guitarist [†Leadbely]‚, [China|Chinese]Š Chairman [ŠMao Zedong]‚, [‚jaz]‡ giant [Charles Mingus]‚, [‚Hol‚ywod]‡ actor [‹David Niven]Œ, legendary [Leds United F.C.|Le‰ds United]‰ manager [‰Don Revie], teacher and boŠk subject [‚Mor‹ie Schwartz]œ, American television actor [ŠLane Smith]‹, linguist [‚Lar‡y Trask]‚, '[•The Guardian|Guardian]'Œ journalist [‚Jilƒ Tweƒdie]˜, avant-garde guitarist [ŒDerek Bailey]‹, American [Šsoap opera]‰ veteran [Michael Zaslow]¾, and libertarian writer, politician, and investment analyst, [‚Harˆy Browne]› died from the disease.

[‰Diane Prety]Æ was a British woman with the disease who was involved in a prominent [Œright-to-die]“ case in the early [20s]….

