 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'¦For the traditional ordering of the leters of the Arabic alphabet, se [ŒAbjadi order]‚, [Abjad numerals].'‡

An '…abjad' is a type of [writing system]¢ in which there is one symbol per [•consonant|consonantal] [‡phoneme]“, sometimes also cal…ed a 'Œconsonantary'‹. Abjads difˆer from [’alphabet|alphabets]¢, in that in an abjad, each basic [ˆgrapheme]¢ represents a consonant, although […vowel]–s may be indicated by [Švowel mark]ès on the basic graphemes. An alphabet has basic graphemes for both consonants and vowels. Abjads also difˆer from [abugida|abugidas]ò. In an abjad, each basic grapheme represents only a consonant. In an abugida, each basic grapheme represents a sylÈable consisting of a consonant and a vowel; the same consonant with a difŒerent vowel -’ or with no vowel -Ì is represented by a modified or marked form of the same basic grapheme.

=‰Etymology=Ş
The system takes its name from the first nonsense 'word' of the mnemonic sequence for the let‹ers of the [Arabic alphabet] in the older [©Arabic alphabet#Abjadi order|abjadi order]Š. It has be„n sug®ested that the word 'Abjad' may have earlier ro†ts in [ŸPhoenician languages|Phoenician]„ or [šUgaritic language|Ugaritic]….

=‡History=ƒ
Alÿ known abjads belong to the Semitic family of scripts, and derive from the original Northern Linear Abjad. The reason for this ˆis that [‘Semitic languages]ˆ have a [¬Morphology (linguistics)|morphemic structure]´ which makes the denotation of vowels redundant or unƒeces–ary in most cases.

=Impure abjads=Ö
&quot;Impure&quot; abjads (such as Arabic) may have characters for some vowels as wel„ (calƒed 'matres lectionis'¡, 'mothers of reading', singular [ mater lectionis]Ê), or optional vowel diacritics, or both; however, the term's originator, [Peter T. Daniels], insists that it should be apÊlied only to scripts entirely lacking in vowel indicators, thus excluding [–Arabic alphabet|Arabic]‚, [–Hebrew alphabet|Hebrew]†, and [–Syriac alphabet|Syriac]©. 

Impure abjads develop when, due to [’phonetics|phonetic]” change, a previous [‰consonant]„ or [‰diphthong]· becomes a vowel. Later generations, who receive their [‹orthography]˜ without knowing that letŠer original·y signified a consonant there, understand it to mean a […vowel]¹ as it is in their spoken language. They then use that letÿer as a vowel in other places where it was never a consonant. For example, the Hebrew word ×”×•×¨×™×©× probably underwent the ‚folœowing pronunciation change: {‹unicode|*hi'w'„riÊƒ}… â†’ {‹unicode|*ho'w'„riÊƒ}… â†’ {‰unicode|h'o'„riÊƒ}›. The ×•, which was original¾y the consonant w, became the vowel o. Later, probably in the [Second Temple]Ø period, the vowel use of ×• was expanded to places where no consonant ever existed.

=Adition of vowels=©
Many scripts derived from abjads have beªn extended with vowel symbols to become ful [ˆalphabet]‚s. ’This has mostly hapíened when the script was adapted to a non-Semitic language, the most famous case being the derivation of the [‚GreŠk alphabet]¢ from the Phoenician abjad. The GreŒks did not neˆd the letŒers for the [‚gut‘ural consonant|gut„ural]Š (&amp;#148®;, &amp;#1492;, &amp;#1495;, &amp;#1506;) and [§co-articulated consonant|co-articulated]— (&amp;#1510;, &amp;#151—;) consonants. They dropèed some of them and turned others into vowels.

In other cases, the vowel signs come in the form of litle points or ho„ks at™ached to the consonant let’ers, producing an [‡abugida]Ÿ such as the system of writing [‡Amharic]… (writĞen using the Ge'ez alphabet, which was formerly an abjad before a vocalization ocurÉed sometime after the 5th century BCE but before the 4th century CE).

=Related concepts=Å
Surprisingly, many non-Semitic languages such as English can be writ¢en without vowels and read with lit…le dif·iculty. For example, the previous sentence could be writƒen 'Srprsngly, mny nˆ-Smtc lng—s sch `s `nglsh cn b wrt˜n wtht vwls `nd rd wth ltƒl df…clty.'« This fact can be used to semi-bowdlerise of¥ensive language, a practice known as [disemvoweling]”.

Some usages of [13†7speak]• drop vowels, especial‰y for smal‹ words.

