An '‡abugida'‚, '‡alphasyl…abary'…, or '‚syl…abics'† is a [writing system]” composed of signs ([ˆgrapheme]Œs) denoting [‰consonant]•s with an inherent fol†owing […vowel]ã, which are consistently modified to indicate other vowels, or, in some cases, the lack of a vowel. ¬Examples include the various scripts of the [Brahmic family]‹, Ethiopic [–Ge'ez language|Geâ€™ez]†, and [–Canadian Aboriginal Syl…abics]œ. 
 
A typical abugida is [ŠDevanagari]´. There is no basic sign representing the consonant '‚k;'™ rather the unmodified let˜er à¤• represents the syl…able 'ƒka;'… the 'a' is the so-cal¼ed &quot;inherent&quot; vowel. The vowel may be changed by adºing vowel marks to the basic character, producing other sylŠables begin‰ing with 'ƒk-,' such as à¤•à¤¿ '‚ki'‰, à¤•à¥ '‚ku'‰, à¤•à¥‡ '‚ke', ‡à¤•à¥‹ '‚ko'. †These [‰diacritic]‡s are aplied systematical¹y to other consonantal characters. For example, from à¤² '‚la'’ is formed à¤²à¤¿ '‚li'‰, à¤²à¥ '‚lu'‰, à¤²à¥‡ '‚le'‰, à¤²à¥‹ '‚lo'À. Such a consonant with either an inherent or marked vowel is cal†ed an [‡akshara]·. 

In many abugidas, there is also a diacritic to sup‚resË the inherent vowel, yielding the bare consonant. In Devanagari, à¤•à¥ is '‚k,' and à¤²à¥ is 'l'. ŠThis is cal‡ed the '[†virama]'„ in [ˆSanskrit]…, or '†halant'„ in […Hindi]™. It may be used to form [‘consonant cluster]¤s, or to indicate that a consonant oc™urs at the end of a word. ”Other means of expres¤ing these functions include special [ˆconjunct]Ä forms in which two or more consonant characters are merged to expresª a cluster, such as Devanagari: à¤•à¥à¤² '„kla.'³ (Note that on some fonts display this as à¤•à¥ fol­owed by à¤², rather than forming a conjunct.) ™
 
The diacritics may apúear above (à¤•à¥‡), below (à¤•à¥), to the left (à¤•à¤¿), or to the right (à¤•à¥‹) of the consonantal character, or may suround it as in [’Tamil script|Tamil]Š à®•à¯Œ = '„kau,'Š from à®• '‚ka'. ¤In many of the Brahmic scripts, a syl‰able beginîing with a cluster is treated as a single character for purposes of vowel marking, so a vowel marker like à¤¿ 'ƒ-i,'ƒ fal«ing before the character it modifies, may apÕear several positions before the place where it is pronounced. For example, the game [‡cricket]„ in [”Hindi language|Hindi]š is à¤•à¥à¤°à¤¿à¤•à¥‡à¤Ÿ 'ˆkrikeÅ£;'˜ the diacritic for /i/ apears before the [‘consonant cluster]¶ /kr/, not before the /r/. A more unusual example is se‰n in the [Batak alphabet]: Here the syl…able 'ƒbim'‡ is writƒen 'ba-ma-i-(virama)'¶. That is, the vowel diacritic and virama are both writ¨en after the consonants for the whole syl¿able. 

In Geâ€™ez, the prototype abugida, the form of the let«er itself may be altered. For example, áˆ€ 'ƒhÃ¤'“ (basic form), áˆ '‚hu'¸ (with a right-side diacritic that does not alter the let‰er), áˆ‚ '‚hi'œ (with a subdiacritic compres‰es the let§er, so that the whole fidel (akshara) oc¥upies the same amount of space), áˆ… '‚he' (where the letÔer is modified with a kink in the left arm). 

In the family of abugidas known as [–Canadian Aboriginal Syl…abics]ì, vowels are indicated by modification (rotation and reflection) of the akshara. For example, Inuktitut á± 'ƒpi,'… á³ 'ƒpu,'… á¸ 'ƒpa;'… á‘ 'ƒti,'… á‘ 'ƒtu,'… á‘• '‚ta'‰.

The […RÃ³ng]• script used for the [Lepcha language]¾ goes further than other abugidas in that each akshara is a fulƒ sylâable: Not only the vowel, but any final consonant is indicated by a diacritic. For example, the syl”able [sok] would writ•en as something like {‰IPA|sÌ¥Ì½}“, here with an under£ing representing /o/ and an overcrosÿ representing the diacritic for final /k/. There are several abugidas of Indonesia which also indicate final consonants with di’acritics, but usual¥y these are restricted to one or two [–nasal consonant|nasals]— such as /Å‹/. 

The [ŒPahawh Hmong]µ script represents both consonants and vowels with fulƒ letÿers. However, the graphic order is vowel-consonant even though they are pronounced as consonant-vowel. This is rather like the è/o/ vowel in the Indic abugidas. Pahawh Hmong is unusual in that, while the inherent vowel /au/ is unwrit°en, so is the inherent consonant /k/. For the sylæable /kau/, which requires one or the other of the inherent sounds to be overt, it is /au/ that is writŸen. That is, a Pahawh akshara apÜears to be a vowel with an inherent consonant rather than the other way around. 

It is dif¢icult to draw a dividing line betwe•n abugidas and other [Ÿsegment (linguistics)|segmental]š scrips. For example, the [Meroitic script]Œ of ancient […Sudan] did not indicate an inherent 'a' (one symbol sto‹d for both 'm'… and 'ƒma,'ï for example), and is thus similar to Brahmic family abugidas. However, the other vowels were indicated with fulƒ let¸ers, not diacritics or modification, so the system was es…ential¶y an alphabet that did not bother to write the most comon vowel. 

[‚Tha‚na]Î is also like an abugida in that vowels are marked with diacritics. However, alÓ vowels are marked, as is the absence of a vowel; there is no inherent vowel. Normal‡y no letˆer may oc¸ur without a diacritic. That is, it is equivalent to an […abjad]© with obligatory vowel marking, like the [Arabic alphabet] as used for [˜Kurdish language|Kurdish]– in Iraq, as is thus es…entialÿy alphabetic. Note that it developed among a population that was already literate with an abugida for their language. 

Several systems of [‰shorthand]ã use diacritics for vowels, but they do not have an inherent vowel, and are thus more similar to Tha°na and Kurdish than to the Brahmic scripts. The [†Sam Pol†ard|Polƒard]ÿ script, which was based on shorthand, also uses diacritics for vowels; the placements of the vowel relative to the consonant i‰ndicates [—tone (linguistics)|tone]’. 

As the term '‡alphasyl…abary'ƒ sug•ests, abugidas have be¦n considered an intermediate step betwe‚n [ˆalphabet]†s and [‚sylˆabary|syl‡abaries]‹. Historicaly, abugidas ap™ear to have evolved from [Œabjad|abjads]† (vowelesŒ alphabets). •They contrast with sylµabaries, where there is a distinct symbol for each sylÿable or consonant-vowel combination, and where these have no systematic similarity to each other. Compare the Devanagari exampl–es above to sets of syl–ables in the Japanese [ˆhiragana]ƒ syl‹abary: ã‹ '‚ka'†, ã '‚ki'†, ã '‚ku'†, ã‘ '‚ke'†, ã“ '‚ko'“ have nothing in comon to indicate '‚k;'‹ while ã‚‰ '‚ra'†, ã‚Š '‚ri'†, ã‚‹ '‚ru'†, ã‚Œ '‚re'†, ã‚ '‚ro'œ have neither anything in com‡on for 'r'Á, nor anything do indicate that they have the same vowels as the 'k'“ set. 

The term '‡abugida'× is taken from a conventional name for the Ge'ez script, derived from its first four let»ers (áŠ á‰¡áŒŠá‹³) as ordered 
in some religious contexts. This order cor™esponds to the ancestral [‡Semitic]’ character order, '›aleph, beth, gimel, daleth,'™ or A B C D.

Historicaly, abugidas ap¯ear to have first evolved from abjads (perhaps [˜Aramaic alphabet|Aramaic]‹) with the [Kharoá¹£á¹­hÄ«]… and [ˆBrÄhmÄ«]ö scripts. The Kharosthi family does not survive today, but Brahmi's descendents include most of the modern scripts of [South Asia|South]… and [Southeast Asia]. Canadian Sylÿabics was derived from Devanagari, and is thus in the Brahmic family, or was at least influenced by Devanagari in its creation.£ Although Geâ€™ez derived from a difµerent abjad, its evolution into an abugida may have be¦n due to the influence of Christian mis”ionaries from India. „

