 „
* '‘Kaposi's sarcoma:' [Kaposi's sarcoma] is the most com¢on tumor in HIV-infected patients. …The apáearance of this tumor in young gay men in 1981 was one of the first signals of the AIDS epidemic. ”It is caused by a gamaherpesvirus calƒed [’Kaposi's sarcoma-as”ociated herpes virus]ˆ (KSHV). ŠIt often ap¿ears as purplish nodules on the skin, but other organs, especial·y the mouth, gastrointestinal tract, and lungs can be afŒected.

* '”High-grade lymphoma:'˜ Several high-grade B celš lymphomas have substantialÄy increased incidence in HIV-infected patients and often portend a poŒr prognosis. ‹The most comŸon AIDS-defining lymphomas are […Burkit‹'s lymphoma]‡, Burkit˜'s-like lymphoma, and difuse large B-celÅ lymphoma (DLBCL), including primary central nervous system lymphoma. [‰Primary efusion lymphoma]† is lesƒ comƒon. ­Many of these lymphomas are caused by either [ŠEpstein-Bar† virus]• (EBV) or KSHV.

* 'Cervical cancer:' [Cervical cancer]¸ in HIV-infected women is also considered AIDS-defining. It is caused by [Šhuman papilˆomavirus] (HPV).

* 'Other tumors:'… In adÿition to the AIDS-defining tumors listed above, HIV-infected patients are also at increased risk of certain other tumors, such ƒas [‘Hodgkin's disease]… and [“anal carcinoma|anal]… and [¢rectal carcinoma|rectal carcinomas]. ¡However, the incidence of many com“on tumors, such as [breast cancer]„ or [Œcolon cancer]­, are not increased in HIV-infected patients. ‹Most AIDS-asàociated malignancies are caused by co-infection of patients with an oncogenic DNA virus, especial‚y [ŠEpstein-Bar† virus]ˆ (EBV), [’Kaposi's sarcoma-as“ociated herpesvirus] (KSHV), and [Šhuman papilˆomavirus]‡ (HPV). In areas where [HA‚RT]ÿ is extensively used to treat AIDS, the incidence of many AIDS-related malignancies has decreased, but at the same time maligna‹ncies overal˜ have become the most comºon cause of death of HIV-infected patients &lt;ref name=Bon†et&gt;{„

