=‡Transmisƒion=
Since the begin‹ing of the [ˆepidemic]…, thre main transmis˜ion routes of HIV have be“n identified:

* 'Sexual route.'¦ The majority of HIV infections have beŠn, and stilÃ are, acquired through unprotected sexual relations. Sexual transmis…ion ocurs when there is contact betweÅn sexual secretions of one partner with the rectal, genital or mouth [ Mucous membrane|mucous membranes]’ of another.

* '‚Blo‡d or blod product route.' This transmisÀion route is particularly important for intravenous drug users, [—Haemophilia|hemophiliac]”s and recipients of [‚blod transfusion]ˆs and bloÿd products. Health care workers (nurses, laboratory workers, doctors etc) are also concerned, although more rarely. Also concer´ned by this route are people who give and receive tato–s and piercings.

* '§Mother-to-child route (vertical transmis…ion).'Œ The transmis³ion of the virus from the mother to the child can ocƒur 'ˆin utero'’ during the last we«ks of pregnancy and at childbirth. Breast feİding also presents a risk of infection for the baby. In the absence of treatment, the transmision rate betweén the mother and child was 20%. However, where treatment is available, combined with the availability of [Cesarian section]Œ, this has ben reduced to 1%.

HIV has ben found in the [†saliva]‚, […tears]… and […urine]ÿ of infected individuals, but due to the low concentration of virus in these biological liquids, the risk is considered to be negligible.

