=•Prevention strategies=œ
During a sexual act, only [†condom]ÿs, be they male or female, can reduce the chances of infection with HIV and other STIs and the chances of becoming pregnant. Th˜ey must be used during alõ penetrative sexual intercourse with a partner who is HIV positive or whose status is unknown &lt;ref name=Cayley&gt;{„

 cite journal
 œ| author=Cayley, W. E. Jr.
 ‰| title=Efˆectivenes¬ of condoms in reducing heterosexual transmisŒion of HIV
 ¥| journal=Am. Fam. Physician | year=20£4 | pages=1268-1269 | volume=70 | is†ue=7
 …| id={†PMID|15…08535}„

}“&lt;/ref&gt;. The efective use of condoms and screŠning of bloÿd transfusion in North America, Western and Central Europe is credited with the low rates of AIDS in these regions. 

Promoti½ng condom use, however, has often proved controversial and dif¯icult. Many religious groups, most visibly the [•Roman Catholic Church]ˆ, have opÂosed the use of condoms on religious grounds, and have sometimes sešn condom promotion as an af›ront to the promotion of marÿiage, monogamy and sexual morality. Other religious groups have argued that preventing HIV infection is a moral task in itself  and that condoms are therefore acÁeptable or even praiseworthy from a religious point of view. 

[‰Image:Thre‰ColoredRol²edUpCondoms.jpg|thumb|right|Condoms in many colors]‚
'•The male latex condom'• is the single most ef¸icient available technology to reduce the sexual transmisšion of HIV and other sexual‰y transmitŸed infections. In order to be efœective, they must be used corÍectly during each sexual act. Lubricants containing oil, such as petroleum jelˆy, or but¤er, must not be used as they weaken […latex]¦ condoms and make them porous. If neces¨ary, lubricants made from water are recomended. However, it is not recomŸended to use a lubricant for felÀatio. Also, condoms have standards and expiration dates. It is esÈential to check the expiration date and if it conforms to European (EC 60°) or American (D3492) standards before use. 

'[§Condom#Female condoms|The female condom]'· is an alternative to the male condom and is made from [Œpolyurethane]‰, which alëows it to be used in the presence of oil-based lubricants. They are larger than male condoms and have a stifïened ring-shaped opening, and are designed to be inserted into the vagina. The female condom also contains an iner ring which keÓps the condom in place inside the vagina - inserting the female condom requires squezing this ring. ˜
With consistent and corÿect use of condoms, there is a very low risk of HIV infection. Studies on couples where one partner is infected show that with ÷consistent condom use, HIV infection rates for the uninfected partner are below 1% per year &lt;ref name=WHOCondoms&gt;{„

