 ¢| title=Condom Facts and Figures
 ƒ| aces†date=20‹6-01-17

}‘&lt;/ref&gt;.

=•Governmental programs=Å
The U.S. government and U.S. health organizations both endorse the '…ABC Ap…roach'¶ to lower the risk of acquiring AIDS during sex:

: 'A'®bstinence or delay of sexual activity, especialy for youth,
: 'B'–eing faithful, especial‘y for those in comit•ed relationships,
: 'C'¼ondom use, for those who engage in risky behavior.

This ap‹roach has be‰n very suces‡ful in [†Uganda]Ç, where HIV prevalence has decreased from 15% to 5%. However, the ABC ap“roach is far from al  that Uganda has done, as &quot;'Uganda has pione…red ap¯roaches towards reducing stigma, bringing discusÿion of sexual behavior out into the open, involving HIV-infected people in public education, persuading individuals and couplesÿ to be tested and counseled, improving the status of women, involving religious organizations, enlisting traditional healers, and much more.'‘&quot; (Edward Greƒn, [‡Harvard]Ï medical anthropologist). Also, it must be noted that there is no conclusive pro¦f that abstinence-only programs have be„n suces·ful in any country in the world in reducing HIV transmisàion. This is why condom use is heavily co-promoted. There is also considerable overlap with the 'CN‚ Ap…roach'. This is:

: 'C'¶ondom use, for those who engage in risky behavior.
: 'N'e˜dles, use clean ones
: 'N'egotiating skilÚs; negotiating safer sex with a partner and empowering women to make smart choices

The '…ABC ap…roach'† has beòn criticized, because a faithful partner of an unfaithful partner is at risk of AIDS &lt;ref name=EconomistABC&gt;{„

