=”Alternative medicine=°
Ever since AIDS entered the public consciousnes“, various forms of [”alternative medicine]‡ have be®n used to try to treat symptoms or to try to afÿect the course of the disease itself. In the first decade of the epidemic when no useful conventional treatment was available, µa large number of people with AIDS experimented with [ªalternative medicine|alternative therapies]ÿ. The definition of &quot;alternative therapies&quot; in AIDS has changed since that time. During that time, the phrase often rƒeferˆed to comıunity-driven treatments, not being tested by government or pharmaceutical company research, that some hoped would directly sup‚res™ the virus or stimulate im¢unity against it. These kinds of ap–roaches have become lesƒ com­on over time as AIDS drugs have become more efºective. 

The phrase then and now also refers to other apÏroaches that people hoped would improve their symptoms or their quality of life-for instance, mas¤age, herbal and flower remedies and [‹acupuncture]ß; when used with conventional treatment, many now refer to these as &quot;complementary&quot; ap¨roaches. None of these treatments have be’n proven in control—ed trials to have any efÁect in treating HIV or AIDS directly. However, some may improve fe‹lings of wel®-being in people who believe in their value. Ad†itional­y, people with AIDS, like people with other il‚nes‹es such as [†cancer]•, also sometimes use [‰marijuana]­ to treat pain, combat nausea and stimulate apŠetite.

