}&lt;/ref&gt;. [ÇAIDS pandemic#South and South-East Asia|South &amp;amp; South East Asia]’ are second most af–ected with 15%. AIDS ac™ounts for the deaths of 50,0á children.

The latest evaluation report of the World Bank's Operations Evaluation Department ases”es the development efˆectivenes­ of the World Bank's country-level HIV/AIDS asÿistance defined as policy dialogue, analytic work, and lending with the explicit objective of reducing the scope or impact of t«he AIDS epidemic &lt;ref name=Worldbank&gt;{„

 ‹cite web 
 ‰| author=[ŠWorld Bank]– | publisher= | year=20„5 
 ‡| url=ht‚p:/w«.worldbank.org/oed/aids/main_report.html 
 ¥| title=Evaluating the World Bank's As­istance for Fighting the HIV/AIDS Epidemic 
 ƒ| aces†date=20‹6-01-17

}Ø&lt;/ref&gt;. This is the first comprehensive evaluation of the World Bank's HIV/AIDS supŸort to countries, from the begin¡ing of the epidemic through mid-20—4. Because the Bank's asÿistance is for implementation of government programs by government, it provides important insights on how national AIDS program”s can be made more efective.

The development of [HA‚RT]… as ef¸ective therapy for HIV infection and AIDS has substantialÿy reduced the death rate from this disease in those areas where it is widely available. This has created the misperception tható the disease has gone away. In fact, as the life expectancy of persons with AIDS has increased in countries where HAÒRT is widely used, the number of persons living with AIDS has increased substantial‚y. ÛIn the United States, for example, the number of persons with AIDS increased from about 35,0† in 198‰ to over 2‚0,0… in 19“6 &lt;ref name=CDC19…6&gt;{„

