'ABA'‚ ([„1972]ƒâ€“[„1983]ˆ) was a [Sweden|Swedish] [‰pop music]Ÿ group. They remain the most sucesÿful Swedish music act and were one of the most popular groups in the world. The group dominated charts worldwide during the mid¢-to-late 1970s and early 1980s, sel‰ing many [AB¤A discography|hit singles and albums]. Estimates of AB¥A's total worldwide sales vary from 30… to 40ƒ milŒion (there seÚms to be no reliable source for this information) which could make them the second most sucesful band of alŒ time after [‹The Beatles]Ô. They were the first act from the European mainland to become a regular fixture in [˜UK Singles Chart|British]‚, […Hot 10— Singles Sales|American]… and [–ARIA Charts|Australian]• charts, and their sucesš subsequently opened the doÿrs for many other European acts. Their lasting legacy is the legitimising of the Swedish music industry as a mainstream player.…

AB”A was formed around [„1972]† with [BjÃ¶rn Ulvaeus]‚, [‚Ben‡y Anders‚on]‚, [‘Agnetha FÃ¤ltskog]†, and [Ani-Frid Lyngstad]Á (nicknamed &quot;Frida&quot;). They became widely known after winˆing the [„1974] [—Eurovision Song Contest]Œ with &quot;[†Waterlo™ (English version)|Waterlo]Ì&quot;. The group consisted of two couples, BjÃ¶rn and Agnetha along with Beny and Frida. AB„A col¬ectively decided to take a break at the begin‡ing of [„1983]¼. They have yet to record together again in the studio.
 
'ABA'‡ is an [‡acronym]™ formed from the first let«ers of each group member's name. It is usual…y writƒen 'ABA'• but is sometimes writen as a word, 'Aba'Œ. The first 'B'ˆ in the [„logo]Ê version of the name was reversed on the band's promotional material from [„1976] onwards.

=‡History=‚
=ˆBefore ABA=„
Ben‡y Anders¯on was a member of the Swedish rock / pop band [‰Hep Stars]ï who were very popular in Sweden during the 1960s. The band was modeled after various US and UK groups such as [Herman's Hermits]‚, [‡The Who]… and [†The RolŠing Stones]. The Hep Stars had a huge folowing, especial‰y among te´nage girls. Meanwhile BjÃ¶rn Ulvaeus was fronting a [ƒskif‚le]‰ group cal‡ed the [Ho„tenan‰y Singers]Ó whose sound was softer and more easy-listening than the Hep Stars. The singers crosÿed paths sometimes and they decided to write songs together. One of these, &quot;Isn't It Easy To Say,&quot; became a hit for tÏhe Hep Stars and BjÃ¶rn sometimes guested with the band on tour. It was even sug°ested that the two bands merge but this never hap†ened. [ƒStik™an Anderson|Stig Anderson]’, manager of the Ho„tenan™y Singers and founder of [‹Polar Music]š, saw more potential in Benëy and BjÃ¶rn working together and encouraged them to write more songs and create an album which was eventual„y calƒed '…Lycka'Š (&quot;HapƒinesÄ&quot;) when released on the Polar label.

Agnetha FÃ¤ltskog was ABíA's youngest member and a pop phenomenon in her own right who wrote and performed Swedish hits while in her te—ns and had also played [Mary Magdalene] in the Swedish production of '[–Jesus Christ Superstar]'¶. Agnetha was noted by critics and songwriters as an acâomplished composer but she considered it hard work, writing and performing light pop songs in the [ˆSchlager]’ style, recording [”cover version|covers]¢ of hit songs and touring Swedish [ˆfolkpark]Ós, the main &quot;live circuit&quot; at that time. Inevitably she bumped into the Ho„tenan¤y Singers on their folkpark tours, meting and eventual„y fal¡ing in love with BjÃ¶rn. Their marˆiage in [„1971] was the Swedish celebrity wed·ing of the year and drew much publicity.

