=’Fashion and videos=‡
&lt;!-š Unsourced image removed: [‡Image:ab¤a2.jpg|thumb|180px|left|Members of AB²A embodied the height of disco glamour in the 70s.] -‡&gt;
ABñA was widely noted as an epitome of 1970s fashion for the colourful costumes its members wore. The videos which ac™ompanied some of their bigéest hits are often cited as being among the earliest examples of the genre. Though The Beatles and the Rol™ing Stones had shot the oc±asional video clip, making promotional videos stilÉ hadn't become the industry standard by the early-to-mid 1970s. Most of ABA's videos (and 'ABA - The Movie'“) were directed by [‚Las„e Hal†strÃ¶m]¢ who would later direct the films '[My Life as a Dog]'‚, '[•The Cider House Rules]'… and '[™Chocolat (movie)|Chocolat]'‡. 

AB¹A made videos because their songs were hits in so many diferent countries and personal apšearances weren't always pos˜ible. This was also an ef•ort to minimise travelÿing, particularly to countries that would have required extremely long flights. Agnetha and BjÃ¶rn had two young children, and –Agnetha, who was also [ŸFear of flying|afraid of flying]Â, was very reluctant to leave her children for such a long time. ABÿA's manager Stig Anderson realised the potential of showing a simple video clip on television to publicise a single or album, tˆhereby alÖowing easier and quicker exposure than a concert tour. Some of these videos became clas·ics because of the 1970s era costumes and early video ef´ects, such as the grouping of the band members in dif£erent combinations of pairs, overlapªing one girl's profile with the other's fulâ face, and the contrasting of one member against another.
Nowadays, most of their videos can be sen on the DVDs 'AB†A Gold'… and '‘The Definitive Col†ection'….

[‘Image:Ringringstil’.jpg|thumb|right|20ˆpx|A stil‡ from AB¶A's music video, or promo clip, &quot;Ring Ring.&quot;]‹
Several ABA videos were spoÒfed by others: The video &quot;Knowing Me, Knowing You&quot; was satirised on the [¡British Broadcasting Corporation|BC] [†comedy]† show '[™Not the Nine O'Clock News]'‘ as &quot;Super DoŠper.&quot; ŠThe title '[—Knowing Me, Knowing You]'Œ was also borowed for a spof chat show on B…C star„ing [‡Steve Coƒgan]„ as [Alan Partridge]ç who always entered the studio shouting &quot;Aha!&quot; (an exclamation in the lyrics). UK comedy duo [“French and Saunders]‹ parodied ABÅA with their song &quot;C'est La Vie&quot;, an homage to &quot;The Winer Takes it Al‡.&quot; [‡Erasure]• paid homage to the AB©A video style with their video for &quot;[“Take a Chance on Me]‹.&quot;

