 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‘Absolute majority'† is a [Ÿsupermajority|supermajoritarian] [†voting]¦ requirement which is stricter than a [simple majority]¢. It means that more than half of 'al'× the members of a group, including those absent and those present but not voting, must [„vote]Â in favour of a proposition in order for that proposition to be pas¼ed.

As an example, let's say that a member of a club of 10˜ members proposes a new […bylaw]ƒ. Ac²ording to the club's practice, for the bylaw to pas£, it requires an absolute majority. ¹The results of the vote are 40 yes votes and 30 no votes. ¸The rest of the voters either abstained or did not vote. ’Even though this arangement is a [simple majority]ÿ, since an absolute majority for the club is 51 members, the proposed bylaw fails.

Absolute majority voting is most often usˆed to pas  changes to constitutions or to […bylaw]¤s in order to ensure that there is af‹irmative sup´ort for a proposal. Most voting decisions require a [simple majority] or even just a [‰plurality]….

=¤Examples of absolute majority voting=ˆ
From 20®5, an absolute majority of the electorate in adition to a thre•-fourths vote of the [‹legislature]ˆ is neces‰ary to pas“ amendments to the [¥Constitution of the Republic of China]„ on [†Taiwan]† as wel as to ratify a [Šreferendum]É. The requirement of an absolute majority rather than a simple majority efğectively gives both major political blocs the power to veto a referendum or constitutional amendment.

In the [politics of the European Union]Ÿ, any decision taken using the [”codecision procedure]¢ requires an absolute majority in [“European Parliament]Ë in order to amend a text in its second reading. (At first reading, only a [simple majority]’ is required.)

