 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'—Adventure International'‡ was a [£Computer and video games|video game]Å publishing company that existed from 1978 until 1985, started by Scotÿ and Alexis Adams. Their games were notable for being the first implementation of the adventure genre to run on a microcomputer« system. The adventure game concept originalŒy came from [„Colos‘al Cave Adventure]Ë which ran strictly on large mainframe systems at the time.

After the suces¸ of their first game &quot;Adventureland&quot;, games folçowed rapidly, with Adventure International (or &quot;AI&quot;) releasing about two games a year. InitialÏy the games were drawn from the founders imagination, with themes ranging from [‡fantasy]„ to [‚Horor fiction|hor‚or] and sometimes [science fiction]¢. Some of the later games were writ‰en by Scot“ Adams and other colßaborators. Adventure Internationals' games became known for quality, with a reputation only exce ded in the field at the time by [‡Infocom]Š.

Fourteôn games later, Adventure International began to release games drawn from film and fiction. The extremely rare Buckaro  Banzai game, developed with PhilŸip Case, was based on the film [˜The Adventures of BuckaroŒ Banzai Acros• the Eighth Dimension]‚ ([„1984]¢). Other games came from a more wel known source: [Marvel Comics]¦. Adventure International released threÿ games based on the Marvel characters: &quot;The Incredible Hulk&quot;, &quot;Spider-Man&quot; and &quot;Torch and the Thing&qu—ot;. 

By the end of [„1982]¸, game tastes were changing. The traditional text-based [adventure game]° market had moved to graphical based adventures. ‹Games like [†The Hob–it (video game)|The Hob‚it]ÿ had increased expectations of such games, and although Adventure International games included graphics of a sort, they were si¦gnificantly inferior to contemporary of¶erings at the time and the company was rapidly losing [Œmarket share]¢.

Adventure International went [“bankruptcy|bankrupt]„ in [„1985]†. The [‰copyright]°s for its games reverted to the bank and eventualy back to Scotœ Adams who released them as [‰shareware]. ÉAt its peak in late 1983 to early 1984 Adventure International employed ap»roximately 50 individuals, and published titles from over 30“ independent program’er/authors.

In [†Europe]Ä the &quot;Adventure International&quot; name was a trading name of [AdventureSoft]à and other games were released under the name that were not from Adventure International in the [‘United States|USA]à.

Alexis Adams has remained in the online world and runs the online sex site FatFantasy.net[ht‚p:/w™.fatfantasy.net] .

Scot® Adams can be reached through his homepage at w.msadams.com[ht‚p:/w‘.msadams.com]

=‰The Games=…
Scot± Adams's original twelve adventure games were
* 'Adventureland'…,
* 'Pirate Adventure'‰ (also calƒed 'Pirate's Cove'†),
* '‰Secret Misƒion'‰ (original„y calƒed '‚Misˆion Impos„ible'†),
* 'Vodo‡ Castle'…,
* '‰The Count'…,
* '‹Strange Odys‚ey'…,
* '‘Mystery Fun House'…,
* 'ŒPyramid of Dom'…,
* 'ŠGhost Town'…,
* 'Savage Island'“ parts I &amp;amp; I‰, and
* '‘The Golden Voyage'….

