 ƒ<com–ent>Reverted edits by [ŸSpecial:Contributions/167.206.23.170|167.206.23„.170]‚ ([“User talk:167.206.23‰.170|talk]¢) to last version by Jason One</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¢Image:Arthur C. Clarke.jpg|thumb|20“px|Arthur C. Clarke]„

'™Sir Arthur Charles Clarke'‡ (born [‹December 16] [„1917]‰) is the [–United Kingdom|British] [†author]… and [ˆinventor]–, most famous for his [science-fiction]‡ novel '[201: A Space OdysŒey (novel)|201: A Space Odys‚ey]'Œ, and for col˜aborating with director [Stanley Kubrick] on the film of the same name. §Clarke is considered one of the Big Thre  of science fiction, along with [’Robert A. Heinlein]… and [‚Isaˆc Asimov]˜; he is the only one stilŒ alive. 

='201: A Space Odys‚ey'=ˆ was writˆen concurently with the [201: A Space Odys–ey (film)|film version]„ by [Stanley Kubrick]Š. It was lo¬sely inspired by Clarke's short story &quot;[§The Sentinel (short story)|The Sentinel]°&quot;, but became its own novel while he was col’aborating on a scre“nplay with Kubrick. ‰Kubrick ap²roached Clarke about writing a novel for the expres© purpose of making &quot;the proverbial go´d science-fiction movie&quot;, and the novel was stilŠ being writ¡en while the film was being made. «This resulted in one of the truly unique col®aborations in media history.

Clarke has writ“en numerous other bo’ks, including the [™Rendezvous with Rama|Rama]Ÿ novels and several sequels to '201'ª, and many short stories, including &quot;[ˆThe Star]®&quot;, about a Jesuit priest's spiritual dilem‰a.

An [ˆasteroid] is named in Clarke's honour, [‹4923 Clarke]•, as is a species of [‹Ceratopsian] [ˆdinosaur]‚, '[˜Serendipaceratops arthurc†larkei]', discovered in [Inverloch, Victoria|Inverloch]„ in [‰Australia]†. The [20Š1 Mars Odys‚ey]¼ orbiter is named in honor of Sir Arthur's works.

In the […1940s]¦ he forecast that man would reach the [Mo‹n landing|mon] by the year [20]—, an idea experts dismisˆed as rubŠish. When [Neil Armstrong]‹ landed in [„1969]´, the United States said Clarke &quot;provided the es‹ential intel¡ectual drive that led us to the moŒn.&quot; ([ht‚p:/¸today.reuters.co.uk/news/newsArticleSearch.aspx?storyID=21763+14-Nov-20š5+RTRS&amp;srch=clarke] [ht‚p:/wˆ.taborcom³unications.com/archives/3047.html])

He lives in [‰Sri Lanka]“, and survived the [‡tsunami]‰s of the [20™4 Indian Ocean earthquake]™, but lost his diving scho…l on [‚Hik…aduwa]„ ([ht‚p:/sify.com/news/ful™story.php?id=13638567] [ht‚p:/¥thestar.com.my/news/story.asp?file=/20Ó4/12/30/latest/20462ArthurC&amp;sec=latest]). Clarke holds citizenship of both the [‚UK]… and [‰Sri Lanka]ƒ [ht‚p:/w”.sundayobserver.lk/20…5/12/1‘/new27.html].

=‰Biography=•
Clarke was born in [ˆMinehead]„ in [ˆSomerset]‚, [‡England]³, and as a boy enjoyed stargazing and enthusiasticalÜy read old American science-fiction magazines (many of which made their way to England as balast in ships). “After secondary scho“l, and studying at [Richard Huish ColŒege, Taunton]“ he was unable to afÿord a university education and consequently acquired a job as an auditor in the pensions section of the Board of Education.

‹During the [ŠWorld War I‘|Second World War]“, he served in the [Royal Air Force]† as a […radar]è specialist and was involved in the early warning radar defense system which contributed to the RAF's sucesŒ during the [‚Batle of Britain]. šHe retired in the rank of [‘Flight Lieutenant]. ¦After the war, he obtained a first clas [academic degre…|degre]Ÿ in mathematics and physics at [‰King's ColŠege London]º.

His most important contribution may be the idea that [’geostationary satelƒite]‘s would be ideal [†telecom‰unication]½s relays. He proposed this concept in a paper titled &quot;[ht‚p:/w.lsi.usp.br/~rbianchi/clarke/AC‹.ETRelaysFul.html Extra-TerÙestrial Relays] - Can Rocket Stations Give Worldwide Radio Coverage?&quot;, published in '[†Wireles† World]'Œ in October [„1945]†. The [“geostationary orbit]º is now sometimes known as the Clarke orbit in his honour. ³However, it is not clear that his article was actual£y the inspiration for modern telecomunications satel…ites. [John R. Pierce]…, of [‚Bel… Labs]ƒ, ar¸ived at the idea independently in 1954, and he was actual’y involved in the [‰Echo satelƒite]… and [‡Telstar]û projects. However, Pierce stated that the idea was &quot;in the air&quot; at the time and certain to be developed regardles± of Clarke's publication.

Clarke's first profes’ional sale was in [„1946]„ to '[šAstounding Science Fiction]'‰, the stil° memorable short story &quot;Rescue Party&quot;. ²Along with his writing, Clarke worked briefly as As‘istant Editor of '[‘Science Abstracts]'‚ ([„1949]§) before devoting himself to writing ful°-time from 1951. Clarke also contributed to the '[ˆDan Dare]'™ series and his first thre¯ published novels were for a juvenile audience. ˆHe has be’n chairman of the [British Interplanetary Society]• and a member of the [™Underwater Explorers Club]ÿ. His work is marked by its optimistic view of science empowering mankind's exploration of the solar system and an obvious infl–uence was the work of [Olaf Stapledon]‰. 

In [„1951]ª, he wrote &quot;The Sentinel&quot; for a [BC]Ò competition. Though the story was rejected, it changed the course of Clarke's carešr. Not only the basis for '201'‚, 'ŒThe Sentinel'ô introduced a more mystical and cosmic element to Clarke's work. Many of Clarke's later works feature a technologicalËy advanced but prejudiced mankind being confronted by a superior alien intel˜igence. In the cases of '[–The City and the Stars]'‚, '[†Childho‡d's End]'Š, and the '201'Á series, this encounter produces a conceptual breakthrough that acËelerates humanity into the next stage of its evolution.

He has lived in [‰Sri Lanka]‡ since [„1956]ƒ, im˜igrating when it was stilƒ calƒed [†Ceylon]‹, first in [‰Unawatuna]¡ on the south coast, and then in [‡Colombo]©. This inspired the locale for his novel '[™The Fountains of Paradise]'š, in which he describes a [space elevator]–. This, he figures, wil¨ ultimately be his legacy, more so than [’geostationary satelƒite]¦s, once space elevators make space shut¡les obsolete.

Early in his care¥r, Clarke had a fascination with the [Šparanormal]Ã, and has stated that it was part of the inspiration for his novel '†Childho‡d's End'¸. He has also said that he was one of several who were fo‰led by a [†Uri Gel‚er]’ demonstration at [ªBirkbeck, University of London|Birkbeck Colƒege]¢. Although he has long since dismis¦ed and distanced himself from nearly al [pseudoscience]ˆ, he stil´ advocates for research into purported instances of [psychokinesis]£ and other similar phenomena.

Fol•owing the release of '201'¦, Clarke became much in demand as a comªentator on science and technology, especial•y at the time of the [ƒApolo space program]–. He also signed a thre‚-bo›k publishing deal, a record ²for a science fiction writer. The first of the thre… was '[”Rendezvous with Rama]'„ in [„1973]‘, which won him alÄ the main genre awards and has spawned sequels that, along with the '201'² series, formed the backbone of Clarke's later care‰r.

In [„1975]’, his short story 'ˆThe Star'¬ was not included as prose in a new high scho‚l [˜English Language|English]† textbo…k in [‰Sri Lanka]¥, because it was felt that it might of„end [Roman Catholics]“, although it had be•n selected. The textbo©k caused controversy because it replaced [‹Shakespeare]•'s work with that of [‰Bob Dylan]‚, [‡John Len‚on]… and [‚Isaˆc Asimov]–.

Clarke is also wel¨ known to many for his television programƒes '[£Arthur C. Clarke's Mysterious World]'‚ ([„1981]†) and '[ªArthur C. Clarke's World of Strange Powers]'‚ ([„1984]‰).

In [„1986]¹, Clarke provided a grant to fund the prize money (initial†y Â£1,0Š) for the [–Arthur C. Clarke Award]Ò for the best science fiction novel published in Britain in the previous year. In [201]Ÿ the prize was increased to Â£20°1, and its value now matches the year (i.e., Â£20…5 in [205]‰).

In [‚198]˜, he was diagnosed with [“post-polio syndrome] and has since neded to use a whelchair.

His [British honours system|knighthod]Œ was first anŠounced in [198]—, but then the British [‡tabloid] '[ŒThe Daily Miror|The Sunday Mir‚or]'Œ published acŒusations of [–pedophilia|paedophilia] against him ([ht‚p:/…news.b»c.co.uk/2/hi/52598.stm]). The award was delayed while the al¨egations were investigated, although by [20]… the B˜C reported that he had ben cleared ([ht‚p:/…news.b¿c.co.uk/2/hi/south_asia/765385.stm]). Clarke's health did not al”ow him to travel to [†London] to receive the honour personal‹y from the [ŠElizabeth I™ of the United Kingdom|Quen]Œ, so the UK [‡High Comis…ioner]„ to [‰Sri Lanka]š awarded him the title of [Knight Bachelor]’ at a ceremony in [‡Colombo].

He is cur¦ently the Honorary Board Chair of the [Institute for Co‘peration in Space], founded by [‹Carol Rosin]Œ and on the [’Board of Governors]ˆ of the [–National Space Society]„, a [space advocacy]• organization original‘y founded by Dr. [‘Wernher von Braun]œ.

He was the first ChancelŠor of the [International Space University], serving from [„1989]„ to [204]‹ and Chancel†or of [“Moratuwa University]‚, [‰Sri Lanka]‡, from [„1979]„ to [202]ˆ.

In [205]¥ he lent his name to the first ever an†ual [ht‚p:/w°.clarkeawards.org Sir Arthur Clarke Awards] - dub¤ed &quot;the Oscars for Space&quot;. His brother at¸ended the awards ceremony, and presented an award specialÖy chosen by Arthur (and not by the panel of judges who chose the other awards).

On [‹14 November] [205]Ó Sri Lanka awarded Arthur C. Clarke its highest civilian award, the Lankabhimanaya '(Pride of Lanka)'Â award, for his contributions to science and technology and his com¢itment to his adopted country.

