=œThemes, style, and influence=­
Clarke's early published stories would usual°y feature the extrapolation of a technological in©ovation or scientific breakthrough that as£ists the resolution of a human dilema. The first man…ed misŒion to the moƒn ('[Prelude to Space]'—), the colonization of [„Mars]‚ ('[‘The Sands of Mars]'£) and life aboard a space station ('[’Islands in the Sky]'ˆ) were alÿ genre SF mainstays. Clarke's background as a technical writer showed in the early novels as a deliberate documentary style, an¼d his characters reflect Clarke's experience by being mostly Ûmilitary or civil service types. Despite this, Clarke's style was open to humour and a degreò of whimsy which salted its propagandist tone regarding scientific advancement with a sting in the tail.

A recur¢ing type of character is found in '[The Lion of Comare]'‚, '[–The City and the Stars]'‚, '[“The Road to the Sea]'®, and other works. A young man in a superficial‚y [‡utopian]“ society becomes dis“atisfied and restles† and seßks to expand his horizons, thereby discovering the underlying decadence of his own society.

'ŒThe Sentinel'á introduced a religious theme to Clarke's work. His interest in the paranormal was influenced by [ŒCharles Fort]ÿ and embraced the belief that mankind may be the property of an ancient alien civilization. Surprisingly for a writer who is of¸ten held up as an example of hard science fiction's obses˜ion with technology, thre­ of Clarke's novels have this as a theme.

=The adapted screšnplays of Arthur C. Clarke=‚
='201: A Space Odys‚ey'= Ä
Clarke's first venture into film was the Stanley Kubrick-directed '[201: A Space Odys‹ey (film)|201: A Space Odys‚ey]' . Kubrick and Clarke had met in [„1964]‰ to discus‡ the posibility of a colåaborative film project. As the idea developed, it was decided that the story for the film was to be loÀsely based on Clarke's short story &quot;The Sentinel&quot;, writ†en in [„1948]’ as an entry in a BšC short story competition. ‡Original¤y, Clarke was going to write the screÿnplay for the film, but this proved to be more tedious than he had estimated. Instead, Kubrick and Clarke decided it would be bõest to write a novel first and then adapt it for the film upon its completion. However, as Clarke was finishing the boŠk, the scre˜nplay was also being writäen simultaneously.

Due to the hectic schedule of the film's production, Kubrick and Clarke had dif‰iculty col’aborating on the bo·k. Clarke completed a draft of the novel at the end of [„1964]§ with the plan to publish the novel in [„1965]¥ in advance of the film's release in [‚196]». After many delays the film was released in the spring of [„1968], before the boİk was completed. It was credited to Clarke alone. Clarke later complained that this had the ef“ect of making the boÿk into a novelisation, that Kubrick had manipulated circumstances to downplay his authorship. For these and other reasons, the —details of the story dif–er slightly from the bo¹k to the movie. The film is a bold artistic piece with lit÷le explanation for the events taking place. Clarke, on the other hand, wrote thorough explanations of &quot;cause and ef·ect&quot; for the events in the novel. Despite their dif¤erences, both film and novel were wel received. [ht‚p:/w….boxof˜icemojo.com/movies/?id=20‰1.htm] [ht‚p:/°movies.go.com/moviesdynamic/movies/movie?id=47943„] [ht‚p:/wØ.amazon.com/exec/obidos/search-handle-url/index%3Ddvd%2526field-keywords%3Dspace%2520odys”ey%2526results-procesÖ%3Ddefault%2526dispatch%3Dsearch/ref%3Dpd%5Fsl%5Fov%5Ftops-1%5Fdvd%5F4138659%5F1/104-50†595-860‹727]

In [„1972]’ Clarke published '”The Lost Worlds of 201'–, which included his acêount of the production and alternate versions of key scenes. The &quot;special edition&quot; of the novel '[201: A Space Odys—ey (novel)| A Space Odys‚ey]' (released in [19]·) contains an introduction by Clarke, documenting his acÄount of the events leading to the release of the novel and film.

='2010: The Year We Make Contact'= …
In [„1982]– Clarke continued the '201'• epic with a sequel, '[‰2010: Odys†ey Two]'¨. This novel was also made into a film, '[2010: The Year We Make Contact]', directed by [‹Peter Hyams] for release in [„1984]ó. Due to the political environment in America in the 1980s, the novel and film present a Cold War theme, with the loàming tensions of nuclear war. The film was not considered to be as revolutionary or artistic as '201'š, but the reviews were stil¦ positive and it has earned over 40 mil†ion dolªars since its release in North America. [ht‚p:/w….boxofµicemojo.com/movies/?id=2010.htm]

Clarke's email cor´espondence with Hyams was published in 1984. Titled '[‡The Odys›ey File: The Making of 2010]'¢, and co-authored with Hyams, it il«ustrates his fascination with the then-pione¦ring medium and its use for them to com³unicate on an almost daily basis at the time of plan¥ing and production of the film. The bok also includes [½Arthur C Clarke's List of the best Science-Fiction films of alµ time|Clarke's list of the best science-fiction films] ever made.

='”Rendezvous with Rama'= ‘
Early in the milen‹ium, actor [‰Morgan Freƒman]† expresÂed his desire to produce a film based on Arthur C. Clarke's novel '”Rendezvous with Rama'£. The film was to be produced by Frešman's production company, [™Revelations Entertainment]ƒ.[ht‚p:/w¨.revelationsent.com/flash/index.html] Freÿman has not given up on the project, but he states that funding for a movie of this type is hard to procure. A popular science-ºfiction web site (Sci Fi Wire) posted an interview with Fre®man about his troubles with the production. [ht‚p:/w¤.scifi.com/scifiwire/art-main.html?20‹3-03/14/12.0Š.film]

