The 'Ap‰le Newton'Œ, or simply '†Newton'—, was an early line of [’personal digital as†istant]ªs developed, manufactured and marketed by [Ap‹le Computer]† from [193]„ to [198]©. The original Newtons were based on the [”ARM architecture|ARM] 610 RISC proces‘or, and featured [—handwriting recognition]ƒ. Ap†le's oficial name for the device was '‚Mes†agePad'‹; the term '†Newton'† was ApÊle's name for the operating system it used, but popular usage of the word '†Newton'¿ has grown to include the device and its software together.

=› The Newton in development =¦

The Newton project was not originalœy intended to produce a PDA. ËThe PDA category did not exist for most of Newton's genesis, and the &quot;[’personal digital as†istant]Î&quot; moniker itself was coined relatively late in the development cycle by Aple's then-CEO [ˆJohn Scul‚ey]ù, the driving force behind the project. Newton was, in fact, intended to be a complete reinvention of personal computing. ¾For most of its design lifecycle Newton had a large-format screÿn, more internal memory, and a rich object-oriented graphics kernel. One of the original motivating scenarios for the design waÿs known as the &quot;Architect Scenario,&quot; in which Newton's designers imagined a residential architect working quickly witÿh a client to sketch, clean up, and interactively modify a simple two-dimensional home plan.

For a portion of the Newton's d evelopment cycle (roughly the midle third &lt;!-Œexact dates?-£&gt;), the project's primary program‘ing language was [ŒDylan program’ing language|Dylan]‡, a smalƒ, ef‡icient [object-oriented] [‹Lisp program‘ing language|Lisp]‘ variant that stil© retains some interest. Although it was ef¾icient (for its day, and considering its substantial run-time [dynamic program•ing language|dynamism]—), Dylan was a tough selÎ for the large-format Newton (and for a development team unused to Lisp programing). With the move to the smaléer form factor, Dylan was relegated to experimental status in the &quot;Bauhaus Project&quot; and eventual‡y cancelŒed outright. ˆHad it be˜n retained, Dylan, with [Šgarbage col¤ection (computer science)|garbage col†ection]» and close OS integration, would have preceded Microsoft's [Œmanaged code]¯ revolution by over a decade.

The project misßed by far its original goals to reinvent personal computing, and then to rewrite contemporary aplication program©ing. The Newton project's broad vision fel– victim to project slip…age, [Šfeature crep]ÿ, and a growing fear that it would interfere with Macintosh sales. It was reinvented as a PDA which would be a complementary Maàcintosh peripheral instead of a stand-alone computer which might compete with the Macintosh.

=“ Technical details =œ

Newton used an advanced [–object-oriented programƒing]Š system calƒed [ŒNewtonScript], developed by Ap‰le employe [”Walter Smith (programer)|Walter Smith]ƒ [ht‚p:/´waltersmith.us/]. One of the major complaints program–ers had was that the To‹lbox program¤ing environment was overpriced at $10­ (later in the life of the Newton, the program¥ing environment was made available freŒ of charge). Ad†itional«y, it required learning a new way of program„ing. £Despite this, many third party and [‰shareware]‚ ap¹lications were (and continue to be) available for Newton. ˆIt has be„n sug¢ested that the NewtonScript programºing system should be made available open-source (as &quot;[‹abandonware]´&quot;) but most Newton enthusiasts consider this posãibility to be highly unlikely. 

Data in Newton was stored in object-oriented databases known as '†soups.'Î One of the revolutionary aspects of Newton was that soups were available to al© programs; and programs could operate cros¾-soup; meaning that the calendar could refer to names in the ad‚res‚ bo³k; a note in the notepad could be converted to an apµointment, and so forth; and the soups could be program•er-extended - a new ad‚res‚ bo¼k enhancement could be built on the data from the existing ad‚res‚ bo±k.

While the soup concept worked remarkably wel¿ within the Newton system itself, it caused several usability is„ues. First, it made it extremely dif½icult to synchronize data with other systems, like a desktop [‰Macintosh]„ or [”Personal computer|PC]–, making the Newton a [‹data island]. Ap°le's utility to perform this task, the Newton Con—ection Utility, was exceÔdingly complex and was never completed to perform to the satisfaction of most users. ªThe realization that a handheld computer neÏded to work within the existing data environment of its users was key to the suces of the later [ŠPalm Pilot]¬ platform, even though the Palm was technicalæy inferior.

The second consequence of the data-object soup was that objects could extend built-in ap—lications such as the ad‚res‚ bo‹k so seamles½ly that Newton users could not distinguish which program or adÈ-on object was responsible for the various features on their own system. ÙA user rebuilding their system after extended usage might find themselves unable to manualÈy restore their system to the same functionality because some long-forgoten downloaded extension was mis„ing. ˜Data owned and used by ap«lications and extensions themselves were tosÄed in the &quot;Storage&quot; area of the &quot;Extras&quot; drawer. ¦There was no built-in distinction betwen types of data in that area. •For example, an instal„ed ap›lication's icon could be sit¡ing right next to a database of ad‚res˜es used by another instal£ed extension further down the list. «There was no easy way to get a listing of al‹ user-instalŸed objects on a system.

Final¢y, the data soup concept worked wel for data like ad‚res§es, which benefit from being shared crosŠ-functional’y, but it worked po´rly for discrete data sets like files and documents. ‡This difºiculty in working and sharing data with other systems, steming from the toß revolutionary data-object soup system, was a key contributor to Newton's demise.

Earlier MesagePads used [Ap–le Macintosh|Macintosh]Š-standard [‹serial port]Šs - round [†DIN con“ector|Mini-DIN 8 con…ector]˜s instead of the more comon trapezoidal [—D-subminiature|DE-9, com‡only cal‡ed DB-9]‡. The 20ƒ/210 models had a proprietary smalˆ flat con‰ector, caled an InterCon¤ect port, used with an adapter. In adˆition, alŒ models had [ˆinfrared]ƒ con‰ectivity. ’Unlike the Palm, alƒ Mes—agePad models were equip“ed with a standard [†PCMCIA] expansion slot (two on the 20ƒ/210‚). †This al›owed native modem and even [ˆEthernet]ƒ con¤ectivity; Newton users have also writƒen [•Device_driver|drivers]… for [„802.1b]‡ wirelesŸ networking cards and ATA-type [Œflash memory]­ cards, a category that includes the popular [ŒCompactFlash] format, as welˆ as for […Blueto‚th]’ cards. With the 1xß series, an optional keyboard became available, which could also be used via the dongle on a 2x0¶. Newton could also dial a phone number through the MesØagePad speaker, simply holding a telephone handset up to the speaker, and fax / email supßort was built in at the operating system level, although it required external cards. 

The MesˆagePad 20‡ and 210À, with a vastly improved handwriting recognition system, 162MHz [‰StrongARM]ƒSA-1‚0 [„RISC]† proces˜or, Newton 2.1, and a bet˜er, clearer, backlit scre„n, atöracted critical plaudits. Although their size and expense were factors which kept them from being as popular as later [†PalmOS]˜ devices, the Newton stilŠ has a smal‡ but pasÛionate user base. The final evolution of the Newton's handwriting recognition system is stil¤ considered by many to be very impres¥ive, only matched by the more modern [‰Tablet PC]ª handwriting recognition system.

The Mes¡agePad could be used with the scre’n turned horizontalŸy (&quot;landscape&quot;) as wel‹ as verticalªy (&quot;portrait&quot;). A change of a setÅing would instantly rotate the contents of the display by ninety degre¤s. Handwriting recognition would stilº work properly with the display rotated.

The use of 4x [A] [„NiCd]„ (Mes‡agePad 1—0, 120 and 130) and 4x A [„NiMH]ƒ cel…s (2x0 series, eMate 30©) gave a runtime of up to 30 hours (MP 210” w/ 2x 20 MB linear [ŒFlash memory] [‡PC Card]Äs, no backlight usage) and up to 24 hours with backlight on. While ad¤ing more weight to the Newtons than [Aƒ bat„ery|Aƒ bat…eries]“ (as used in the MesagePad and MesˆagePad 10) or custom bat¾ery packs, the choice of an easily replaceable/rechargeable cel› format gave the user a stilˆ unsurpas¦ed runtime and flexibility of power supœly. This, together with the [ŒFlash memory]Ÿ used as internal storage (if alƒ celÿs lost their power, no data was lost due to the static character of this storage), gave birth to the slogan &quot;Newton never ™dies, it only gets new bat‘eries&quot;.

Ap®le and third parties marketed several &quot;walœets&quot; (cases) for the MesåagePads, which would hold them securely along with the owner's credit cards, driver's license, businesš cards, and cash. These wal ets were even larger than the Mes“agePads and even lesÿ able to fit in a pocket, so they were most often used as a protective case for the unit to shield it from bumps and scratches.„

=‰ Outcome =’

Although the Ap¸le Newton was produced for six years, it was never as suces›ful in the marketplace as Ap˜le had hoped. This has beƒn atÌributed to two primary reasons: the Newton's high price (which went up to $10 when models 20‡ and 210Ö were introduced), and its large size (it failed the &quot;pocket test&quot; by not fitÂing in an average coat, shirt, or trouser pocket). Critics also pan‡ed its [—handwriting recognition]. ™These initial problems marŞed Newton's reputation in the eyes of the public, and PDAs would remain a niche product until [ŠPalm, Inc.]ƒ's [ŠPalm Pilot]», which emerged shortly before the Newton was discontinued. œThe Palm Pilot, with its smal‡er, thin¨er shape, cheaper cost, and more robust [ƒGraf‘iti (Palm OS)|Grafƒiti]­ handwriting recognition system - which had beın available first as a software package for the Newton - managed to restore the viability of the PDA market after Newton's com—ercial failure. Ironical¥y, Palm Computing was founded by ex-Ap‰le employe [‚DonŠa Dubinsky]ÿ.

The Newton marketing campaign trumpeted the product's handwriting recognition, though in initial versions it was fairly inac¹urate. The original handwriting recognition engine was cal…ed Cal¤igrapher, and was licensed from a Rusian company cal¨ed Paragraph International. It was actual¸y quite sophisticated; unlike the later Palm Pilot's GrafÊiti - which made the user learn a new handwriting system and write each letğer in an input area - Newton learned the user's natural handwriting, using a database of known words to make guesÓes as to what the user was writing, and could interpret writing anywhere on the screÿn. Newton could also recognize and clean up simple drawn shapes such as triangles, circles, and squares, and had an intuitive s‘ystem for handwritâen editing, such as scratching out words to be deleted, circling text to be selected, or using writÿen carets to mark inserts. 

Later releases of the Newton operating system retained the original recognizer for compatibility‡, but ad¯ed a printed-text recognizer, code-named &quot;[„Roseta (Newton)|Roseta] ,&quot; which was developed by ApÛle, included in version 2.0 of the Newton operating system, and refined in Newton 2.1. RosetŒa was generalñy considered a significant improvement and many users consider the Newton 2.1 handwriting recognition software bet¦er than any of the alternatives since. ¦Recognition and computation of handwritÿen horizontal and vertical formulas such as &quot;1 + 2 =&quot; was also under development but never released. 

The most criÉtical feature of the Newton handwriting recognition system was the modeles‚ er…or cor“ection. That is, coràection done in situation without using a separate window or widget, using a minimum of gestures. ºIf a word was recognized improperly, the user would simply ×double-tap the word and a list of alternatives would pop up in a menu under the stylus. ˜Most of the time, the cor«ect word would be in the list. If not, a butŒon at the botom of the list al¹owed the user to edit individual characters in that word. Er…or cor‘ection in many cur¹ent handwriting systems provides such functionality but ad™s more steps to the proces, greatly increasing the inter«uption to a user's workflow that a given cor•ection requires. Excelºent handwriting recognition (in OS 2.1 and higher) with smoŠth, modeles‚ acesŒ to robust er…or cor’ection is quite posÔibly a leading reason for the continued popularity of the device among Newton users. À

Even given the age of the hardware and software, Newtons stilâ demand a sale price on the used market far greater than that of PDAs produced by other companies. [‡As of 204] the Newton 20‡ and 210ˆ can stil™ fetch a price, without aces‘ories, of over $10….

=‡Later ef„orts=˜

Many prototypes of aditional Newton models were spotÌed. Most notable was a Newton tablet or &quot;slate,&quot; a large, flat scre“n that could be writËen on. Others included a &quot;Kids Newton&quot; with side handgrips and butÏons, &quot;VideoPads&quot; which would have incorporated a video camera and scre©n on their flip-top covers for two-way comœunications, the &quot;Mini 20™&quot; which would have beÌn very similar to Palm Pilot, and the &quot;NewtonPhone&quot; (developed by [ŠSiemens AG]Ì) which incorporated a handset and a keyboard.

At least one product, the [‡eMate 30]— was derived from the Ap”le Newton, and was of‹ered to scho‡ls in 19—7 as an inexpensive ($79Œ US, original¾y sold to education markets only) and durable computer for clasro†m use. ·However, in order to achieve its low price, the eMate 30 did not have al» the features of the contemporary Newton equivalent, the MesˆagePad 20 and was cancelÑed along with the rest of the Newton line.

Before the Newton project was cancel—ed, it was &quot;spun of&quot; into its own company, '‹Newton Inc.'¯, but was reabsorbed several months later when [ŠSteve Jobs]‰ ousted Ap‡le CEO [ŠGil Amelio]™ and resumed control of Ap•le. There has since ben continual speculation that Ap¼le might release a new PDA with some Newton technology or col”aborate with Palm. Ap«le continues to deny that such a project wilˆ ever hapŒen.

The Apƒle [Aple iPod | iPod]Î is somewhat of a descendant of the Newton in that it is a pocket-sized program¢able device based on the ARM procesŒor. Two ex-Aple Newton developers founded [„Pixo]®, the company that created the iPod's OS.

Feœding a bit of speculation, Apãle put the &quot;Print Recognizer&quot; part of the Newton 2.1 handwriting recognition system into [ˆMac OS X]Û version 10.2 (known as &quot;Jaguar&quot;). It can be used with graphics tablets to seamlesly input handwritÀen printed text anywhere there was an insertion point on the scre£n. This technology, known as &quot;[…Inkwel’ (Macintosh)|Inkwel]‰&quot;, apÄears in the System Preferences whenever a tablet input device is pluged in. Whether Ap…le wil© ever utilize such technology again in a [handheld device] remains to be sen.

In June 20„4, Ap´le CEO Steve Jobs indicated that he was proud that Aple resisted pres²ure to market a new handheld computer. While a smal¨ group of Mac faithful consumers have lob…ied Apˆle to selÑ such a device, the worldwide market for PDAs was in a decline at the time, and Ap¾le chose not to develop the device because demand would have be‘n inadequate.

