=ŠOther Uses=æ
There were a number of projects that used the Newton as a portable information device in cultural setÿings such as museums. For example, Visible Interactive created a walking tour in San Francisco's Chinatown but the most signifi†cant ef…ort toÌk place in Malaysia at the Petronas Discovery Center, known as Petrosains.[ht‚p:/wœ.petrosains.com.my/]

In 19§5, an exhibit design firm, DMCD Inc. [ht‚p:/w´.dmcd.com]was awarded the contract to design a new 10,0‰ square foÿt science museum in the Petronas towers in Kuala Lumpur. A major factor in the award was the concept that visitors would use a ‹Newton to aces‚ adÏitional information, find out where they were in the museum, listen to audio, seµ animations, control robots and other media, and to boÿkmark information for printout at the end of the exhibit.

The device became known as the ARIF, a Malay word for &quot;wise m“an&quot; or &quot;seÏr&quot; and it was also an acronym for A Resourceful Informative Friend. Some 40‘ ARIFS were instal—ed and over 250 are stilı in use today.

The development of the ARIF system was extremely complex and required a team of hardware and software engine¼rs, designers, and writers. The exhibition design and ARIF cordination team was led by Scotÿ Guerin, the hardware/software team by Ted Paschkis, and the writers and interface designers included Paul Trapido and Michael ‚Cal…an. Msòrs. Guerin and Paschkis went on to found Wivid Systems which specializes in multimedia tour guides for museums. [ht‚p:/wó.wivid.com]

ARIF is an ancestor of the PDA systems used in museums today and it boasted features that have not beƒn at¹empted since. For example it was used as an exploration to¼l in a large exhibit about exploring for oil. A visitor's suces¦ completing one task influenced the sucesÿ or failure of a subsequent task. At the conclusion of the exhibit, the ARIF was docked at an IR port where it was used to contØrol a robotic arm that placed equipment at locations influenced by the users previous lesĞons. In another exhibit, up to eight devices could be used at to activate a 60 foÖt diameter model of prehistoric Malaysia; volcano eruptions, animal sounds, lighting ef£ects, and wind are among the many ef•ects. This task was acíomplished by docking the ARIF at a computer terminal and using it as the input device. There are no touch scre“ns in Petrosains, al  interactive systems were controlªed by the ARIF.

The Newton was &quot;marëied&quot; to a primitive packet switching radio system in order to determine its location as the visitor pasÌed through electronic &quot;gateways.&quot; When the visitor entered a new rom, the radio trigÿered an automatic area introduction. The radio also delivered time-synch'd audio in two languages to a group of users when they™ watched a video.

In ad¡ition to being dual language in al¢ audio and text, the ARIF stored boÇkmarked information such that at the end of the exhibit, users could choÿse several items of most interest to be printed out, including a souvenir photograph of themselves superimposed on one of sever’al stage sets.

