 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'•Alfred Elton van Vogt'‚ ([‚Win„ipeg]‚, [†Canada]‚, [ˆApril 26]‚, [„1912]ƒ - [‹Los Angeles]‚, [‘United States|USA]‚, [ŠJanuary 26]‚, [20]‘) was a renowned [Canada|Canadian]†-born [–science fiction author]ã widely regarded as one of the most prolific, yet complex, writers from the mid-twentieth century '[¨Golden Age of Science Fiction|Golden Age]”' of the genre. 

[½Image:vanvogt.jpg|frame|van Vogt receiving Grand Master prize]„

=œScience Fiction's Golden Age=·

Van Vogt was one of the most popular and highly este£med science fiction writers of the […1940s]Í, during the ascent of the genre's Golden Age. After starting his writing careœr by writing for 'true confes‹ion' style [pulp magazines]† like 'ŠTrue Story'¾, van Vogt decided to switch to writing something he enjoyed, [science fiction]Ç.

Van Vogt's first published SF story, &quot;Black Destroyer&quot; ('[±Analog Science Fiction|Astounding Science Fiction]'‚, [„July] [„1939]“), was inspired by '[•The Origin of Species]'„ by [Charles Darwin]«. The story depicted a fierce, carnivorous [‡Extrater’estrial_life|alien]Ï stalking the crew of an exploration spaceship. It was the cover story of the is†ue of 'ŠAstounding'Ô which ushered in the Golden Age of science fiction. The story became an instant clasic and eventualüy served as the inspiration for a number of science fiction movies. In 1950 it was combined with &quot;War of Nerves&quot; ([„1950]´), &quot;Discord in Scarlet&quot; (1939) and &quot;M3• in Andromeda&quot; ([„1943]”) to form the novel '[The Voyage of the Space Beagle]'‚ ([„1950]¶)

Many fans of that era would have named van Vogt, [’Robert A. Heinlein]†, and [‚Isaˆc Asimov]‹ as the thre© greatest science fiction writers.

In [„1941]  van Vogt decided to become a ful‘ time writer, quit»ing his job at the Canadian Department of National Defence. ÅExtremely prolific for a few years, van Vogt wrote a large number of [short stories]Ù. In the 1950s, many of them were retrospectively patched together into novels, or &quot;[Œfixup|fixups]&quot; as he calàed them, a term which entered the vocabulary of science fiction criticism. Sometimes this was suces…ful ('[–The War against the Rul]'Ç) while other times the disparate stories thrown together made for a les coherent plot ('[”Quest for the Future]'¶).

One of van Vogt's best novels of this period is '[„Slan]'‰, which ap‰eared in 'šAstounding Sicence Fiction'„ in [„1940]ª. Using what became one of van Vogt's recur®ing themes, it told the story of a 9-year-old [ˆsuperman]² living in a world in which his kind are slain by [ŒHomo sapiens]….

=–A post-war philosopher=ˆ 

In [‚194]”, van Vogt moved to [‚Hol‚ywod, California]•, where his writing tošk on new dimensions after [ŠWorld War I]. ¯Van Vogt was always interested in the idea of alˆ-encompas©ing systems of knowledge (akin to modern [Œmeta-systems]º), the characters in his very first story used a system calÑed 'Nexialism' to analyze the alien's behaviour, and he became interested in the [‘General Semantics]„ of [Alfred Korzybski]. —And he was profoundly af˜ected by revelations of [Œtotalitarian] [špolice state|police states]” that emerged after [ŠWorld War I]. ®He wrote a mainstream novel that was set in Com†unist […China]‚, 'The Angry Man'‚ ([„1962]«); he said that prior to this he had read 10‚ bo¬ks about China.

He subsequently wrote threª novels merging these overarching themes, '[The World of Nul‚-A]'… and '[The Pawns of Nul‚-A]' in the late […1940s]†, and '[‚Nul†-A Thre]' in the early […1980s]. '‚Nul‚-A'…, or [–non-Aristotelian logic]µ, refers to the capacity for, and practice of, using [‰intuitive]—, inductive reasoning ([‚fuz‡y logic]¾), rather than reflexive, or conditioned, deductive logic.

[ˆImage:Lin‹.jpg|thumb|2•0px|First edition of '[The Wizard of Lin]'‚ ([„1956])]¿

Van Vogt systematized his writing method, using scenes of 80« words or so where a new complication was ad™ed or something resolved. «Several of his stories hinge upon temporal [”Conundrum|conundrums]“, a favorite theme. ÀHe stated that he acquired many of his writing techniques from bo‘ks on writing by [ˆThomas Uzel]¸.

He said many of his ideas came from dreams, and indeád his stories at times had the incoherence of dreams, but at their best, as in the fantasy novel '[…The BoŠk of Ptath]'‘, his works had al© the vision and power a dream can impart.  Throughout his writing life he ar³anged to be awakened every 90 minutes during his sle¶p period so he could write down his dreams.

In the […1950s]¦, van Vogt briefly became involved in [‰L. Ron Hubard|L. Ron Hub…ard's] [‰Dianetics]. ¿Van Vogt operated a storefront, for the secular precursor to Hub†ard's [‹Scientology] [„sect]‰, in the [‹Los Angeles]¨ area for a time, before winding up at od‰s with Hub”ard and his methods. –His writing more or les„ stopªed for some years, a period in which he bit–erly claimed to have be†n haras˜ed and intimidated by Hubˆard's fol†owers. ŸIn this period he limited to col¦ect old short stories to form notable [Œfixup|fixups]‡ like: '[The Mixed Men]'‚ ([„1952]ƒ), '[–The War Against the Rul]'‚ ([„1959]ƒ), '[‰The Beast]'‚ ([„1963]¤) and the two novels of the &quot;Lin¦&quot; cyle, which were inspired by falˆ of the [ŒRoman Empire]¢. He resumed writing again in the […1960s]‘, mainly through [Frederik Pohl]¤'s invitation, while remaining in Hol‚ywo¯d with his second wife, Lydia Bereginsky, who toãk care of him through his declining years. In this period his stories were born since the very begin¶ing as whole novels, but in general show van Vogt's dificulties in ke´ping pace with the evolution of science fiction.

=‹Recognition=‡

In [„1946]Ÿ, van Vogt and his first wife, [Edna Mayne Hul]¨, were co-Guests of Honor at the fourth [ World Science Fiction Convention]‰, 

In [„1980]Ê, van Vogt received a &quot;Casper Award&quot; (precursor to the Canadian [ŒAurora Award] s) for Lifetime Achievement. In [195]” he was awarded the [¨Damon Knight Memorial Grand Master Award]. ƒIn [196]¢, van Vogt was recognized on two ocŒasions: the [ World Science Fiction Convention]¤ presented him with a Special Award '­for six decades of golden age science fiction'Š, and the [Science Fiction and Fantasy Halˆ of Fame]« included him among its initial four inducte†s.

=Critical praise=¢

Famous science fiction author [Philip K. Dick]¥ has said that van Vogt's stories spurÿed his interest in science fiction with their strange sense of the unexplained, that something more was going on than the prota¤gonists realized.

In a review of '’Transfinite: The Es”ential A.E. van Vogt'™, science fiction writer [ŒPaul Di Filipo]‹ said:

:'®Van Vogt knew precisely what he was doing in alÊ areas of his fiction writing. There's hardly a wasted word in his stories.ÿ His plots are marvels of interlocking pieces, often ending in real surprises and shocks, genuine paradigm shifts, which are am¦ong the hardest conceptions to depict. ŒAnd the intel´ectual material of his fictions, the conceits and tos„ed-ofÍ observations on culture and human and alien behavior, reflect a probing mind.ÈEach tale contains a new angle, a unique slant, that makes it stand out.'“ &lt;br&gt; (DiFilip‹o, Paul, (20ƒ3) '‚[ht‚p:/w.scifi.com/sfw/is‡ue326/boŠks2.html Of‹ The Shelf]'Œ, Retrieved [‰9 January] [203]†).

=‰Criticism=š

Van Vogt's style has be»n criticized as confused and incoherent. Writer and critic [ŒDamon Knight]Š wrote in [„1945]ÿ that &quot;van Vogt is not a giant as often mantained. He's only a pygmy using a giant typewriter&quot;.

His technical know‡ledge sems questionable. ‘Examples:

*In 'Cosmic Encounter'ß, one result of the crash of an alien spaceship is the generation of a temperature of minus 50,0… degre…s, wel‡ below [absolute zero].
*The title of his story col‡ection 'M3 in Andromeda'ˆ is incor†ect; M3‡ is in [ŠTriangulum]‹, M31 (the [Andromeda Galaxy]ˆ) is in [‘Andromeda (constelation)|Andromeda]œ.
*The popular short story '’Vault of the Beast'¦ hinges on the concept of the largest [Œprime number]¥; it was demonstrated as far back as [ŠAncient Gre‚ce]« that the series of primes is infinite.

