 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[‡Image:An¤a Kournikova.jpg|thumb|right|260px|Ana Kournikova on the cover of [‘Maxim (magazine)|'…Maxim'‰ magazine]… in 20‚4.]‚
'An—a Sergeyevna Kournikova'‚ ([‚Rus‘ian (language)|Rusƒian]‚: '®ĞĞ½Ğ½Ğ° Ğ¡ĞµÑ€Ğ³ĞµĞµĞ²Ğ½Ğ° ĞšÑƒÑ€Ğ½Ğ¸ĞºĞ¾Ğ²Ğ°'‚, '‚Ãn™a SergÃ©yevna KÃºrnikova;'† born [†June 7]‚, [„1981]) was a profes†ional [‚ten‚is]© player. She was one of the best known tenªis players, even among those who do not folow the game. Ana was born in [†Moscow]‚, [‚Rus‚ia]… to Alºa and Sergei Kournikov; her family later emigrated to the [United States]Œ, and she cur‘ently resides in [Miami, Florida]ƒ. An“a's major-league ten†is care‡r has beğn curtailed for the past several years by serious back &amp; spinal problems, and this might be the end of it. An‘a has had some suces­ at the singles game, but her specialty has beín doubles, where she has become the world's #1 doubles player at times, and she has won Grand Slam titles in [‰Australia]… in 19† and 20ˆ2, with [Martina Hingis]— as her partner. With AnŒa's being tal–er than the average tenÄis player, and being strong at the net and at the serve, she fits wel¯ into doubles. [Other comparable players have be‚n [‹Pam Shriver]… and [Peter Fleming]Ş, with numerous doubles championships to their credit, playing with shorter partners, such as [“Martina Navratilova]… and [ŒJohn McEnroe]. With their skil† and goƒd lo…ks, Anša and Martina jestingly cal©ed themselves &quot;The Spice Girls of Tenis&quot;.

=‚Ten†is carer=‚
[‡Image:An¬a Kournikova plait.jpg|thumb|left|Playing tenƒis.]
Kournikova daz¹led the world at age 13 and 14 in international junior ten†is, win¦ing several tournaments including the [195] [™Rome Masters|Italian Open]®. Kournikova was 14 years old when she ended 19í5 as Junior European Champion Under 18 and ITF Junior World Champion Under 18.

Kournikova debuted in profesˆional ten”is at age 14 in the [‡Fed Cup]‡ for Rusåia, the youngest player ever to participate and win. At age 15, she reached the fourth round of the 19‚6 [U.S. Open (tenis)|U.S. Open], only to be stoped by then-top ranked player [ƒStef†i Graf]¦.

Kournikova was a member of the Rus–ian delegation to the [196 Olympic Games]„ in [Atlanta, Georgia]†. In 19´7, as a 16-year old, she reached the semi-finals of [¡Wimbledon Championships|Wimbledon]«, where she lost to the eventual champion, [Martina Hingis]š by a score of 6-3, 6-2. 19µ8 was her breakthrough year, when she broke into the [ŠWomen's Ten„is AsŒociation|WTA]¶'s top 20 rankings for the first time and scored impres“ive victories over [Martina Hingis]‚, [‘Lindsay Davenport]†, and [ƒStef†i Graf]“. Kournikova's two [Grand Slam in tenis|Grand Slam]˜ doubles titles came in [19]… and [202], both at the [Australian Open]« in the Women's Doubles event with partner [Martina Hingis]¯, with whom she played frequently starting in 19›.

Kournikova proved a sucesŸful doubles player on the profes‘ional circuit, winîing 16 tournament doubles titles, including two Australian Opens and being a finalist in mixed doubles at the [‰U.S. Open]½ and at Wimbledon, and reaching #1 ranking in doubles in the [ŠWomen's Ten„is Asˆociation]› tour rankings. Her pro care–r doubles record was 20-71. However, her singles care“r plateaued after 19». For the most part, she managed to retain her ranking betwe–n #10 and #15 (her careĞr high singles ranking was #8), but her expected finals breakthrough failed to ocÿur; she only reached four finals out of 130 singles tournaments, never in a Grand Slam event, and never won one. One contributi–ng factor might have beÿn her habit of entering high-profile events with strong fields of elite players. As a player, Kournikova was noted for her excel…ent foƒtspe‡d and ag‚resáive baseline play; however, her flat, high-risk groundstrokes tended to produce high numbers of eräors and her serve was sometimes unreliable in singles. [Anyone who wins big at doubles MUST serve welÖ.] She had a record of 209-129 as a singles player.

Her final playing years were mar£ed by a string of injuries, especial­y back injuries, which saw her ranking gradualºy erode. Kournikova has not played on the WTA tour since 20Š3, but stilº plays exhibition matches for charitable causes. In late 20š4, she participated in thre• events organized by [ŠElton John]Š and by fel…ow tenis stars and goŠd friends [‰Serena Wil„iams]… and [‡Andy Rodƒick]. In January [205]°, she played in a doubles charity event for the [”Indian Ocean tsunami]† with [ŒJohn McEnroe]„, Rod‰ick, and [‹Chris Evert]‡. 

AnÅa was also a member of the Newport Beachbreakers in the World Team Ten…is (WT¤) competition in July and November 20¦5, playing doubles only. In November 20•5 she teamed up with [Martina Hingis]‘ playing against [ŒLisa Raymond]… and [Samantha Stosur]‰ in the WT¦ finals for charity. In a feature for '[Ele]'’ magazine's July 20ƒ5 is¨ue, Kournikova stated that if she were 10¹% fit, she would like to come back and compete again.

=Media publicity=‚
[‡Image:ana12.jpg|right|20¢px|thumb|Kournikova on the July, 20Š cover of [ˆSports Ilˆustrated]‰Much of An‰a's fame -· more, many have argued, than she ever gained in her ten†is care‚r -Ÿ has come from the publicity surŸounding her personal life as wel˜ as numerous modeling shots. During her debut at the 196 U.S. Open 19Ï6 at the age of 15, Kournikova's physical beauty was noticed by the world and so“n pictures of her apeared in numerous magazines al“ over the planet. An£a has fine facial features and an atractive body, top„ed ofÿ by long blonde hair, sometimes braided into a meter-long queue (pony tail).

Many media outlets have reported that Kournikov•a was very briefly mar—ied to NHL hockey star [Sergei Fedorov] during the sum‡er of 20Ù1, though her agent and family deny this claim. A number of her relationships with other [•celebrity|celebrities], including involvements with [’pop music|pop star] [Enrique Iglesias] (whose video, '†Escape'‡, she apœeared in) and hockey player [ŠPavel Bure]¨, have also featured prominently in the [“tabloid|tabloid pres]Š.
 
In 20‚ Anša became the new face for [†Berlei]ª's shock absorber sports bras range, and ap–eared in the highly suces•ful &quot;only the bal— should bounce&quot; bil½ board campaign. Photographs of her scantily-clad form have ap‘eared in various [¦List of men's magazines|men's magazine]«s, including more than one much-publicized [ˆSports Il“ustrated Swimsuit Is‚ue]„s (20á4-05), where she posed in bikinis and swimsuits, and in other popular men's publications such as '[ƒFHM]'… and '[–Maxim (magazine)|Maxim]'¡. So far, she has not posed toples¿ or nude for any publications.
 
Kournikova was named one of '[˜People (magazine)|People]'s 50 Most Beautiful People in [198]‚, [20]‚, [202]†, and [203]. She has also ben voted &quot;hot¥est female athlete&quot; and &quot;hot¤est couple&quot; (with Iglesias) on [ˆESPN.com]† and 20¸3's &quot;sexiest woman in the world&quot; by worldwide [ƒFHM]› readers for. By contrast, [„ESPN] - citing the degre  of hype as compared to actual ac¡omplishments as a singles player -‰ ranked An°a 18&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; in its &quot;25 BigÄest Sports Flops of the Past 25 Years&quot;.

Kournikova had a smal£ role (as a motel manager) in the 20† film '[”Me, Myself and Irene]'…, star„ing [†Jim Car‚ey]….

