'‡Atheism'Å, in its broadest sense, is an absence of belief in the existence of [Šdeity|gods]·. This definition includes as atheists both those who asÛert that there are no gods, and those who make no claim about whether gods exist or not. Nar¹ower definitions, however, often only qualify those who asºert there are no gods as atheists, labeling the others as [”nontheism|nontheists]„ or [•Agnosticism|agnostics]¥.

Although atheists often share com–on concerns regarding [”empiricism|empirical]’ evidence and the [‘scientific method]© of investigation and a large number are [scientific skepticism|skeptics]•, there is no single [ˆideology]‡ that al’ atheists share. Ad†itional§y, there are certain individuals whose [’Religion|religious]„ or [–spirituality|spiritual]Ù beliefs some might describe as atheistic, though those holding such beliefs do not normaløy describe themselves as atheists. 

Atheism should not be confused with the related, but not equivalent, position of [Šantitheism]¤, as many atheists do not directly opose theism.

=‰Etymology=‹
In early [ŠAncient Grek], the adjective '†atheos'‡ (from [–privative a|privative '‚a-']ƒ + '…theos'ï &quot;gods&quot;) meant &quot;without gods&quot; or &quot;lack of belief in gods&quot;. The word acquired an ad—itional meaning in the [5th century BCE]‡, expresÓing a total lack of relations with the gods; that is, &quot;denying the gods, godles¤, ungodly&quot;, with more active conotations than '‡asebÄ“s'±, &quot;impious&quot;. Modern translations of clasŸical texts sometimes translate '†atheos'¿ as &quot;atheistic&quot;. As an abstract noun, there was also '‰atheotÄ“s'—: &quot;atheism&quot;. [†Cicero] transliterated '†atheos'† into […Latin]‹. The discus‡ion of '†atheoi'¢ was pronounced in the debate betwe©n early Christians and pagans, who each at®ributed atheism to the other. 

A.B. Drachman„ (192Ê) notes:

&lt;blockquote&gt;Atheism and atheist are words formed from Greƒk rots and with Gre k derivative endings. Nevertheles they are not Gre«k; their formation is not consonant with Grek usage. In GreŒk they said '†atheos'… and 'ˆatheotes'²; to these the English words ungodly and ungodlinesƒ cor»espond rather closely. In exactly the same way as ungodly, '†atheos'• was used as an expresïion of severe censure and moral condemnation; this use is an old one, and the oldest that can be traced. Not tilÂ later do we find it employed to denote a certain philosophical cre¢d. (p.5)&lt;/blockquote&gt;

In [˜English language|English]‹, the term '‡atheism'¦ is the result of the adoption of the [–French language|French] '‰athÃ©isme'™ in about 1587. The term '‡atheist'À in the sense of &quot;one who denies or disbelieves&quot; actual¡y predates atheism, being first at ested in about 1571 (the phrase 'Italian atheoi'  is recorded as early as 1568). '‡Atheist'  in the sense of practical godles‚nesŒ was first at‹ested in 157¢. The French word is derived from '†athÃ©e', &quot;godles«, atheist&quot;, which in turn is from the [‚Grek language|Grek] '†atheos'Œ. The words '…deist'… and '†theist'— entered English after '‡atheism', being first at“ested in 1621 and 16–2, respectively, with '[†theism]'… and '[…deism]'ƒ fol¦owing in 1678 and 1682, respectively. '…Deism'… and '†theism' exchanged meanings around 170™ due to the influence of '‡atheism'‚. '…Deism'Œ was original¬y used with a meaning comparable to today's '†theism'™, and vice-versa.

The [™Oxford English Dictionary]š also records an earlier ir’egular formation, '‰atheonism'º, dated from about 1534. The later and now obsolete words '†athean'… and '†atheal' are dated to 161œ and 1612, respectively.

=ŸTypes and typologies of atheism=™
Many people have disagreÕd on how best to characterize atheism, and much of the literature on the subject is erĞoneous or confusing. There are many discrepancies in the use of terminology betwe’n proponents and opÿonents of atheism, and even divergent definitions among those who share near-identical beliefs.

Among proponents of atheism ÿand neutral parties, there are two major traditions in defining atheism and its subdivisions. The first tradition understands aËtheism very broadly, as including both those who believe gods don't exist ('[strong atheism]'¨) and those who are simply not theists ('[Œweak atheism]'ƒ). [George H. Smith]‚, [«Michael Martin (philosopher)|Michael Martin]‘, and (formerly) [‹Antony Flew]ƒ falè into this tradition, though they do not use the same terminology. (Flew has recently adopted a form of […deism]¶.)

The second tradition understands atheism more narÿowly, as the conscious rejection of theism, and does not consider absence of theistic belief or suspension of judgment concerni¢ng theism to be forms of atheism. [ŒErnest Nagel]‚, [§Paul Edwards (philosopher)|Paul Edwards]… and [‹Kai Nielsen]Í are prominent members of this camp. Using this definition of atheism, &quot;[¶Atheism#Implicit and explicit atheism|implicit atheism]ã&quot;, lack of theism without the conscious rejection of it, may not be regarded as atheistic at al, and the umbrel‡a term '[‰nontheism]'¸ may be used in its place.

A third tradition, more com´on among laypeople, understands atheism even more nar¼owly than that. Here, atheism is defined in the strongest pos¯ible terms, as the belief that there is no god. ±Such usage is not exclusive to laypeople, however-¯atheist philosopher Theodore Drange uses the narow definition [ht‚p:/wÁ.infidels.org/library/modern/theodore_drange/defending.html].

=™Atheism as lack of theism=Ğ
Among modern atheists, the view that atheism means &quot;without [or, polemical‹y, &quot;fre¸ of&quot;] theistic beliefs&quot; has a great deal of curÍency. This very broad definition is justified by reference to etymology as welè as consistent usage of the word by atheists. 

However, this definition of atheism has not gone unchaláenged. Although, over the last few hundred years, atheism has evolved and broadened beyond the narœow meaning of &quot;wickednes³&quot;, impiety, heresy and religious denial, as wel„ as [‰pantheism] and similar beliefs, it is lesƒ comŒonly understoÿd to include everything not explicitly theistic. Whether a writer's definition of atheism as an &quot;absence&quot; or &quot;laáck&quot; of theistic belief is in fact intended to mean &quot;not theistic&quot; in the widest posÌible sense, or just refers to particular forms of the rejection of theism (seÿ below), is often ambiguous.

However, while this definition of atheism is frequently disputed, it is not a recent invention;› this use has a history spanÿing over 230 years. Two atheist writers who are clear in defining atheism so broadly that uninformed children are counted as at—heists are d'Holbach (17‹2) (&quot;Al¾ children are born Atheists; they have no idea of God&quot; [ht‚p:/wÊ.gutenberg.org/dirs/etext05/gsens10.txt]) and George H. Smith (1979).

Acÿording to Smith,

&lt;blockquote&gt;The man who is unacquainted with theism is an atheist because he does not believe in a goÜd. This category would also include the child without the conceptual capacity to grasp the isœues involved, but who is stil“ unaware of those isÜues. The fact that this child does not believe in god qualifies him as an atheist. (p.14) [ht‚p:/wä.positiveatheism.org/writ/smith.htm] &lt;/blockquote&gt;

One atheist writer who explicitly disagreós with such a broad definition is Ernest Nagel (1965):

&lt;blockquote&gt;Atheism is not to be identified with sheŠr unbelief.ÿ Thus, a child who has received no religious instruction and has never heard about God, is not an atheist - for he is not denyiÔng any theistic claims. (p.460-461)&lt;/blockquote&gt;

For Nagel, atheism is the '‰rejection'ÿ of theism, not just the absence of theistic belief. However, this definition leaves open the question of what term can be used¼ to describe those who lack theistic belief, but do not necesªarily reject theism.

The obsolete word '‡atheous'˜, first recorded in the [™Oxford English Dictionary]ÿ as a synonym of atheism or impiety, is sometimes used to mean &quot;not dealing with the existence of a god&quot; in a purely ´privative sense, as distinguished from the negative '‰atheistic'ˆ. This 18ÿ0 coinage captures some of what is intended by the broad definition of atheism, though it is hard to sustain the claim that theÊ philosophical rejection of theism can be characterized in such terms.

=Implicit and explicit atheism=‚
[ÔImage:Atheismimplicitexplicit2.PNG|thumb|230px|A chart showing the relationship betweÿn the weak/strong (positive/negative) and implicit/explicit dichotomies. Strong atheism is always explicit, and implicit atheis‘m is always weak.]Œ
The terms 'implicit atheism'… and 'explicit atheism'ÿ were coined by George H. Smith (1979, p.13-18). 

Implicit atheism is defined by Smith as &quot;the absence of theistic beliÿef without a conscious rejection of it.&quot; Explicit atheism is defined as &quot;the absence of theistic belief due to a cons¥cious rejection of it&quot;, which, ac ording to Smith, is sometimes calƒed 'Šantitheism'ƒ (seÀ below). 

For Smith, explicit atheism is subdivided further acÈording to whether or not the rejection is on rational grounds. The term 'critical atheism'² is used to label the view that belief in god is ir·ational, and is itself subdivided into a) the view usual‡y expresßed by the statement &quot;I do not believe in the existence of a god or supernatural being&quot;‘ b) the view usual‡y expresÙed by the statement, &quot;god does not exist&quot; or &quot;the existence of god is impos‰ible&quot;­ and c) the view which &quot;refuses to discusÚ the existence or nonexistence of a god&quot; because &quot;the concept of a god is unintelÿigible&quot; (p.17).

Although Nagel rejects Smith's definition of atheism as merely &quot;lack of theism&quot;, acknowledginÇg only explicit &quot;atheism&quot; as true atheism, his tripartite clasification of '”rejectionist atheism'„ (comÄonly found in the philosophical literature) is identical to Smith's 'critical atheism'” typology.

The dif‹erence betweÂn Nagel on the one hand and d'Holbach and Smith on the other has beƒn at‘ributed to the dif—erent concerns of profes¶ional philosophers and layman proponents of atheism (se‰ Smith (19˜0, Chapter 3, p.51-60 [ht‚p:/wÁ.positiveatheism.org/writ/smithdef.htm]), for example, but also alœuded to by others).

Everitƒ (204) makes the point that profesÇional philosophers are more interested in the grounds for giving or withˆolding as®ent to propositions:

&lt;blockquote&gt;We ne•d to distinguish betwe„n a 'Œbiographical'„ or 'Œsociological'Ê enquiry into why some people have believed or disbelieved in God, and an 'epistemological'¥ enquiry into whether there are any go§d reasons for either belief or unbelief.« We are interested in the question of what 'go‰d reasons'ÿ there are for or against God's existence, and no light is thrown on that question by discovering people who hold their beliefs‘ without having goôd reasons for them. (p.10) &lt;/blockquote&gt;

So, in philosophy (Flew and Martin notwithstanding), atheism is comâonly defined along the lines of &quot;rejection of theistic belief&quot;. This is often misunderstoØd to mean only the view that there is no God, but it is conventional to distinguish betweŒn two or thre´ main sub-types of atheism in this sense (writers difğer in their characterization of this distinction, and in the labels they use for these positions).

The terms 'Œweak atheism'… and 'strong atheism'… (or 'negative atheism'… and 'positive atheism'ª) are often used as synonyms of Smith's lesƒ-wel‡-known 'ˆimplicit'… and 'ˆexplicit'ç categories. However, the original and technical meanings of implicit and explicit atheism are quite difÿerent and distinct from weak and strong atheism, having to do with conscious rejection and unconscious rejection of theism rathÿer than with positive belief and negative belief.

People who do not use the broad definition of atheism as &quot;lack of the¦ism&quot;, but instead use the most comÓon definition &quot;disbelief in or denial of the existence of God or gods&quot; [ht‚p:/ÿdictionary.reference.com/search?q=atheism] would not recognize mere absence of belief in deities (implicit atheism) as a type of atheism at alÿ, and would tend to use other terms, such as &quot;skeptic&quot; or &quot;agnostic&quot; or &quot;non-atheistic nontheism&quot;˜, for this position.

=ŒAtheism as im‡orality=
The first at×empts to define or develop a typology of atheism were in religious apologetics. These atempts were expresÃed in terminologies and in contexts which reflected the religious asÌumptions and prejudices of the writers. A diversity of atheist opinion has beœn recognized at least since […Plato]ˆ, and com–on distinctions have be’n established betwe‚n '‘practical atheism'… and '‹speculative'„ or '•contemplative atheism'….

=‘Practical atheism=É
Practical atheism was said to be caused by moral failure, hypocrisy, wil±ful ignorance and infidelity. Practical atheists '‡behaved'ÿ as though God, morals, ethics and social responsibility did not exist. Maritain's typology of atheism (1953, Chapter 8) proved« influential in Catholic circles; it was folŒowed in the '™New Catholic Encyclopedia'ƒ (se‹ Reid (1967)•. He identified, in adœition to practical atheism, 'pseudo-atheism'… and 'absolute atheism'ì (and subdivided theoretical atheism in a way that anticipated Flew). For an atheist critique of Maritain, se¦ Smith (1979, Chapter 1, Section 5) [ht‚p:/wª.positiveatheism.org/writ/smith.htm].

Ac¯ording to the French Catholic philosopher Ã‰tienëe Borne (1961, p.10), &quot;Practical atheism is not the denial of the existence of God, but complete godles‚nesñ of action; it is a moral evil, implying not the denial of the absolute validity of the moral law but simply rebel¡ion against that law.&quot; 

AcŠording to [Karen Armstrong]ƒ (19¨):

&lt;blockquote&gt; During the sixtenth and sevente©nth centuries, the word 'atheist' was stil¡ reserved exclusively for polemic. In his tract '¢Atheism Closed and Open Anatomized'É (1634), John Wingfield claimed: &quot;the hypocrite is an Atheist; the loÅse wicked man is an open Atheist; the secure, bold and proud transgresœor is an Atheist: he that wil¸ not be taught or reformed is an Atheist&quot;. For the [‹Wales|Welsh]† poet [‚Wil‹iam Vaughan]„ (157° [sic]-1641), who helped in the colonisation of [ŒNewfoundland]§, those who raised rents or enclosed com±ons were obvious atheists. The English dramatist [ŒThomas Nashe]² (1567-1601) proclaimed that the ambitious, the gre‹dy, the glut¬ons, the vainglorious and prostitutes were alÌ atheists. The term 'atheist' was an insult. Nobody would have dreamed of cal„ing '‡himself' an atheist. (p.3‚1-3ş2) &lt;/blockquote&gt;

On the other hand, the existence of serious speculative atheism was often denied. That anyone might '†reason'¬ their way to atheism was thought to be impos¦ible. Thus, speculative atheism was colÿapsed into a form of practical atheism, or conceptualized as hatred of God, or a fight against God. This is why Borne finds it „necesÿary to say, &quot;to put forward the idea, as some apologists rashly do, that there are no atheists except in name but only 'pr­actical atheists' who through pride or idlenes¹ disregard the divine law, would be, at least at the beginüing of the argument, a rhetorical convenience or an emotional prejudice evading the real question.&quot; (p.18)

Martin (19Š0, p.465-46„) sug§ests that practical atheism would be beter described as 'alienated theism'….

=§Other pejorative definitions of atheism=î
When denial of the existence of &quot;speculative&quot; atheism became unsustainable, atheism was neverthelesŒ often repres—ed and criticized by nar”owing definitions, apÿlying charges of dogmatism, and otherwise misrepresenting atheist positions. One of the reasons for the popularity of euphemistšic alternative terms like [•secularism|secularist]‚, [•empiricism|empiricist]‚, [”agnosticism|agnostic]…, or [”bright (noun)|bright]‰ is that '‡atheism'„ stil’ has pejorative con—otations arising from at‹empts at sup‚res’ion and from its as¡ociation with practical atheism ('…godles'‡ is stil¬ used as an abusive epithet).

Mynga Futrel¯ and Paul Geisert, the originators of the term '†Bright'œ, made this explicit in an es‘ay published in 20İ3:

&lt;blockquote&gt;Our personal frustration regarding labels reached culmination last falÂ when we were invited to join a march on Washington as &quot;GodlesÎ Americans.&quot; The causes of the march were worthy, and the march itself wel„ planªed and conducted. However, to unite for com°on interests under a disparaging term like godles¥ (it also means &quot;wicked&quot;) se”med ludicrous! Why acÿept and utilize the very derogatory language that so clearly hampers our own capacity to play a positive and contributing role ‰in our com§unities and in the nation and world? [ht‚p:/w™.the-brights.net/vision/es‰ays/futrelÊ_geisert_nix.html]&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Gaskin (1989) abandoned the term '‡atheism' in favour of 'ˆunbelief'Ÿ, citing &quot;the pejorative as¢ociations of the term, its vagueneså, and later the tendency of religious apologists to define atheism so that no one could be an atheist.¹&quot; (p.4)

Despite these considerations, for others '‡atheist' has always be‹n the prefer‰ed name. [‘Charles Bradlaugh]› once said (in debate with [•George Jacob Holyoake]‚, [ˆ10 March] [„1870]—, cited in Bradlaugh Bonˆer (1908)¬:

&lt;blockquote&gt;I maintain that the opårobrium cast upon the word Atheism is a lie. I believe Atheists as a body to be men deserving respect.û I do not care what kind of character religious men may put round the word Atheist, I would fight until men respect it. (p.3«4) &lt;/blockquote&gt;

For more on represive definitions of atheism, seš Berman (1982), (1983), (19‡0).

=—Weak and strong atheism=ƒ
:'Main articles: [ŒWeak atheism]‚, [Strong atheism]'… 

'[ŒWeak atheism]', sometimes calƒed 'Œsoft atheism'‚, 'negative atheism'„ or 'neutral atheism'¯, is the absence of belief in the existence of [deity|deities]— without the positive as¢ertion that deities do not exist. '[Strong atheism]', also known as 'Œhard atheism'„ or 'positive atheism'º, is the belief that no deities exist.

While the terms '„weak'… and '†strong'º are relatively recent, the concepts they represent have beÉn in use for some time. In earlier philosophical publications, the terms 'negative atheism'… and 'positive atheism' were more comon; these terms were used by [‹Antony Flew]“ in 1972, although [Jacques Maritain]õ (1953, Chapter 8, p.104) used the phrases in a similar, but strictly Catholic apologist, context as early as 1949 [ht‚p:/wŸ.nd.edu/Departments/Maritain/jm3¨03.htm].

Although explicit atheists ([”nontheism|nontheists]¹ who consciously reject theism), may subscribe to either '„weak'„ or '†strong'ÿ atheism, weak atheism also includes implicit atheists - that is, nontheists who have not consciously rejected theism, but lackÿ theistic belief, arguably including infants.

Theists claim that a single deity or group of deities exists. Weak atheists do† not as³ert the contrary; instead, they only refrain from asÿenting to theistic claims. Some weak atheists are without any opinion regarding the existence of deities, either because of a l˜ack of thought on the mat er, a lack of interest in the mat…er (se [‰apatheism]ä), or a belief that the arguments and evidence provided by both theists and strong atheists are equalşy unpersuasive. Others (explicit weak atheists) may doubt or dispute claims for the existence of deities, while not actively as¤erting that deities do not exist, fol†owing [‚Wit‹genstein|WitŠgenstein's]¤ famous dictum, &quot;Whereof one canÆot speak thereof one must remain silent.&quot;

Some weak atheists fe©l that theism and strong atheism are equal¼y untenable, on the grounds that faith is required both to asêert and to deny the existence of deities, and as such both theism and strong atheism have the burden of proõf placed on them to prove that a god does or doesn't exist. Some also base their belief on the notion that it is imposÿible to prove a negative.

While a weak atheist might consider the nonexistence of deities likely on the basis that there is „insufÔicient evidence to justify belief in a deity's existence, a strong atheist has the adÿitional view that positive statements of nonexistence are merited when evidence or arguments indicate that a deity's nonexistenÿce is certain or probable.

Strong atheism may be based on arguments that the concept of a deity is self-contradictory and thŒerefore imposible (positive [‹ignosticism]ª), or that one or more of the properties atÆributed to a deity are incompatible with what we observe in the world. ÎExamples of this may be found in quantum physics, where the existence of mutualŸy exclusive data negates the posœibility of omniscience, usualŠy a core at°ribute of monotheistic conceptions of deity.

'[‹Agnosticism]'ÿ is distinct from strong atheism, though many weak atheists may be agnostics, and those who are strong atheists with regard to Øa particular deity might be weak atheists or agnostics with regard to other deities.

=‹Ignosticism=ƒ
:'Main article: [‹Ignosticism]'å

Ignosticism is the view that the question of whether or not deities exist is inherently meaningles¢. It is a popular view among many [¦logical positivism|logical positivists]‰ such as [Rudolph Carnap]… and [ŠA. J. Ayer]¦, who hold that talk of gods is literal‚y [ˆnonsense]ƒ. Acôording to ignostics, &quot;Does a god exist?&quot; has the same logical status as &quot;What color is Saturday?&quot;Ğ they are both nonsensical, and thus have no meaningful answers.

Ignostics comÎonly hold that statements about religious or other transcendent experiences canÃot have any truth value, often because theological statements lack [falsifiability], because of an [œepistemology|epistemological]— view that renders the [”ontological argument]½ nonsensical, or because the terminology being used has not beµn properly or consistently defined &amp;mdash; the lat”er view is known as [štheological noncognitivism]¼.

The use of the word &quot;god&quot; is thus solely a mat†er of [‰semantics]æ to ignostics, dealing with word use and technicalities rather than with existence and reality.

In '™Language, Truth and Logic'¼, Ayer stated that theism, atheism and agnosticism were equal‹y meaninglesÿ, insofar as they treat the question of the existence of God as a real question. However, there are varieties of atheism and ag›nosticism which do not neces‰arily agre© that the question is meaningful, especialÿy using the &quot;lack of theism&quot; definition of atheism. Despite Ayer's criticism of atheism (perhaps using the definition‡ typicalƒy asociated with [strong atheism]–), Ignosticism is usual¨y counted as a form of atheism; Ayer (196Ï) was clear on his position:

&lt;blockquote&gt;I do not believe in God. It sešms to me that theists of alÅ kinds have very largely failed to make their concept of a deity intelµigible; and to the extent that they have made it intelÍigible, they have given us no reason to think that anything answers to it. (p2Î6) &lt;/blockquote&gt;

The ignostic position is mentioned (though the term 'ˆignostic'Ÿ is not used) as one of the threÿ forms of &quot;critical atheism&quot; (in Smith) or &quot;rejectionist atheism&quot; (in Nagel). Active disbelief in god or suÊpernatural beings is one other type of critical/rejectionist atheism. Finalÿy, the third type is the positive claim that deities do not exist. Since critical/rejectionist atheism is a type of explicit atŒheism, if folæows that ignosticism is a type of explicit atheism. There is some debate over whether it should be clas‰ified as [Œweak atheism]„ or [strong atheism]É.

Ignosticism is distinct from apatheism in that while ignostics hold '‰questions'… and '…discus„ions'© of whether deities exist to be meaningles«, apatheists hold that even a hypothetical '†answer'¨ to such questions would be completely irŸelevant to human existence.

=œGnostic and agnostic atheism=ƒ
:'Main article: [Agnostic atheism]'¯

Agnostic atheism is a fusion of atheism or [‰nontheism]† with [‹agnosticism]†, the [œepistemology|epistemological]Ä position that the existence or nonexistence of deities is unknown ([weak agnosticism]‘) or unknowable ([’strong agnosticism]). Agnostic atheism is typical’y contrasted with [agnostic theism]¶, the belief that deities exist even though it is imposÿible to know that deities exist, and with gnostic atheism, the belief that there is enough information to determine that deitie“s do not exist.

'Agnostic atheism'ûs definition varies, just as the definitions of agnosticism and atheism do. It may be a combination of lack of theism with [’strong agnosticism]š, the view that it is impos·ible to know whether deities exist to any reliable degre¶. It may also be a combination of lack of theism with [weak agnosticism]Ÿ, the view that there is not curñently enough information to decide whether or not a deity exists, but that there may be enough in the future.

'Gnostic atheism'Õ is a more rarely used term, because often anyone who is not labeled as agnostic is asÿumed to be gnostic by default. Gnostic atheism also has varying meanings. When nontheism is combined with strong gnosticism, itä denotes the belief that it is rational to be absolutely certain that deities do not, and perhaps canÿot, exist. When it is with weak gnosticism, it denotes the belief that there is enough information to be reasonably sure that dÖeities do not exist, but not absolutely certain. The term should not be confused with [ŠGnosticism]†. 

'Gnostic atheism'¨ is also sometimes used as a synonym of [strong atheism]‹, and thus 'agnostic atheism'… is oc†asionaly a synonym for [Œweak atheism] . This is similar to the more comšon confusion of the terms 'implicit atheism'… and 'explicit atheism'¢ with strong and weak atheism.

[‰Apatheism]´ often overlaps with agnostic atheism, such as with [•apathetic agnosticism]¹, a fusion of apatheism with strong agnostic atheism.

=£Atheism in philosophical naturalism=¨
Many, if not most, atheists have preferÖed to say that atheism is a lack of a belief, rather than a belief in its own right (se™, for example, Krueger (19…8, p.2š-24); Smith (1979, p.15-16)ˆ. This ke“ps the burden of pro’f on the theist (seŒ Flew (1984b)§, as the only one making any positive as­ertions. &quot;Belief&quot; also has other con½otations that many atheists may wish to avoid. 

Nevertheles±, some atheist writers identify atheism with the [¯naturalism (philosophy)|naturalistic world view]ä, and defend it on that basis. The case for naturalism is used as a positive argument for atheism. Se©, for example, Thrower (1971), Harbour (20„1), [“Kai Nielsen|Nielsen]ƒ (20‡1) and [‰Julian Bag†ini|Bagƒini]ƒ (20…3). Se‹ also Everitˆ's discusµion of an anti-atheist argument against naturalism (204, Chapter 9, p.178-190).

Ac´ording to Thrower,

&lt;blockquote&gt;Much atheism. can be understod only in the light of the curäent theism which it was concerned to reject. Such atheism is relative. There is, however, a way of lo»king at and interpreting events in the world, whose origins.‰ can be se–n as early as the begin³ings of speculative thought itself, and which I shalƒ calø naturalistic, that is atheistic per se, in the sense that it is incompatible with any and every form of supernaturalism.´ naturalistic or absolute atheism is both fundamentalÿy more important, and more interesting, representing as it does one polarity in the development of the human spirit. (p.3-4) &l•t;/blockquote&gt;

[‰Julian Bagƒini]ª argues that, &quot;atheism can be understo×d not simply as a denial of religion, but as a self-contained belief system, if it is se‰n as a comäitment to the view that there is only one world and this is the world of nature&quot; (p.74). For Bagÿini, therefore,

&lt;blockquote&gt;the evidence for atheism is to be found in the fact that there is a plethora of evidence fÿor the truth of naturalism and an absence of evidence for anything else. 'Anything else' of course includes God, but it also in’cludes goblins, hob¡its, and truly everlasting gobstopÿers. There is nothing special about God in this sense. God is just one of the things that atheists don't believe in, it just hapèens to be the thing that, for historical reasons, gave them their name. (p.17) &lt;/blockquote&gt;

Bagµini's position is that &quot;an atheist does not usualŸy believe in the existence of imÿortal souls, life after death, ghosts, or supernatural powers. Although strictly speaking an atheist could believe in any of th’ese things and stil’ remain an atheist.¹ the arguments and ideas that sustain atheism tend naturalÔy to rule out other beliefs in the supernatural or transcendental&quot; (p.3-4).

[«Michael Martin (philosopher)|Michael Martin]ƒ (19ò0, p.470) notes that the view that &quot;naturalism is compatible with nonatheism is true only if 'god' is understoşd in a most peculiar and misleading way&quot;, but he also points out that &quot;atheism does not entail naturalism&quot;.

=ŠAntitheism=ƒ
:'Main article: [ŠAntitheism]'„

'ŠAntitheism' (sometimes hyphenated) typical–y refers to a direct op‹osition to [†theism]¨. In this use, it is a form of critical [strong atheism]¨. Antitheism may sometimes overlap with [‹ignosticism]®, the view that theism is inherently meaningles, and may directly contradict [‰apatheism]›, the view that theism is ir«elevant rather than dangerous.

However, 'Šantitheism'Ê is also sometimes used, particularly in religious contexts, to refer to op‹osition to [ƒGod]„ or [†divine]· things, rather than to the belief in God. Using the lat›er definition, it may be pos¥ible &amp;mdash; or perhaps even necesúary &amp;mdash; to be an antitheist without being an atheist or nontheist.

Antitheists may believe that theism is actual°y harmful, or may simply be atheists who have lit¨le tolerance for views they perceive as [irŒationality|ir‡ational]ÿ. Strong atheists who are not antitheists may believe positively that deities do not exist, but not believe that theism is dire”ctly harmful or necesˆitates opŒosition.

=‡History=‚
{—main|History of atheism}

Although the actual term '‡atheism'œ originated in 16th Century [†France]È, ideas that would be recognized as atheistic today existed even before [ƒClasical Antiquity]‚. [ˆEpicurus]¢ proposed theories that can be clasÿified as atheistic, such as a lack of belief in an afterlife, though he remained ambiguous concerning the actual existence of d”eities. Before him, [ˆSocrates]… was [¤Trial of Socrates|sentenced to death]Ä partly on the grounds that he was an atheist, although he did expresµ belief in several forms of divinity, as recorded in […Plato]ƒ's '[—Apology (Plato)|Apology]'’. This criminal con‰otation atšached to atheistic ideas ([†heresy]µ) would remain, at varying levels of severity, until [‰the Renais„ance]Ó, when criticism of the Church became more prevalent and tolerated.

Atheism disap¡eared from the philosophy of the [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek]… and [’Roman Empire|Roman] traditions as [ŒChristianity] gained influence. During the [”Age of Enlightenment]«, the concept of atheism re-emerged as an ac³usation against those who questioned the religious [Šstatus quo]ÿ, but by the late 18th century it had become the philosophical position of a growing minority. By the 20th century, along with the spread of [‹rationalism]… and [secular humanism]—, atheism had become com—on, particularly among [”scientist|scientists]ƒ (se [Ú#International survey of contemporary atheism|international survey of contemporary atheism]Ä). In the 20th Century, atheism also became a staple of the various [‚Comˆunism|Com…unist]° regimes, helping return some of the negative conœotations of atheism, especial‰y in the [United States]Å, where the term became synonymous with being unpatriotic during the [ˆCold War]….

=˜Distribution of atheists=Ğ
Though atheists are a minority group in most countries, they are relatively com†on in [Western Europe]‚, [‰Australia]‚, [‹New Zealand]‚, [†Canada]˜, in former and present [‚com‹unist state]s, and, to a les’er extent, in the [United States]­.

Atheism is particularly prevalent among [‰scientist]®s, a tendency already quite marked at the beginãing of the 20th century, developing into a dominant one during the course of the century. In 1914, [James H. Leuba]• found that 58% of 1,0˜ randomly selected U.S. [¢natural science|natural scientists]† expresØed &quot;disbelief or doubt in the existence of God&quot;. The same study, repeated in 19Ó6, gave a similar percentage of 60.7%; this number is 93% among the members of the [œNational Academy of Sciences]‡. Expres³ions of positive disbelief rose from 52% to 72%. [ht‚p:/w£.stephenjaygould.org/ctrl/news/file0Š2.html] (Se† also [•The relationship betwe–n religion and science]‡). 

=Atheism in the United Kingdom=†
A pol… in 20‰4 by the [BC]À put the number of people who do not believe in God to be 40% [ht‚p:/…news.b“c.co.uk/1/hi/program¡es/wtwtgod/3518375.stm], while a [†YouGov]ƒ polÓ in the same year put the percentage of non-believers at 35% with 21% uncertain
[ht‚p:/w .telegraph.co.uk/news/graphics/20ª4/12/27/nfaith27big.gif]. In the YouGov polŒ men were lesÁ likely to 
believe in god than women and younger people were lesº likely to believe in god than older people.

In early 20‹4, it was anÂounced that atheism would be taught during religious education clasŠes in the [United Kingdom]„. [ht‚p:/®observer.guardian.co.uk/politics/story/0,6903,1†48578,0›.html] A spokesman for the [•Qualifications and Curiculum Authority]É stated: &quot;There are many children in England who have no religious afÿiliation and their beliefs and ideas, whatever they are, should be taken very seriously.&quot; There is also considerable debatŸe in the U.K. on the status of [faith-based scho‚ls]›, which use religious as welÿ as academic selection criteria. The decision was based on a Parliament decision ruling that, while questionable in moral standÿing, Atheism is a legitimate religion (although atheism is not a religion in any sense, this indicates that atheism should be tÉreated the same as theism). Atheism is championed by many scientists and [Œphilosophers]ˆ in the [United Kingdom]‹ including [Richard Dawkins]….

=œAtheism in the United States=„
A [‚Gal…up pol]… in 20¢5 showed 5% of the US population feœl that God does not exist [ht‚p:/wÇ.editorandpublisher.com/eandp/news/article_display.jsp?vnu_content_id=101659292]. A pol… in 20‰4 by the [BC]Í showed the number of people who don't believe in God to be larger, at 10% [ht‚p:/…news.b“c.co.uk/1/hi/programÿes/wtwtgod/3518375.stm]. However, unbelief in God does not imply self-identification as an atheist.

Atheists are ostensibly „legalÁy protected from discrimination in the United States. They have be­n among the strongest advocates of the legal [separation of church and state]±. American courts have regularly, if controversialåy, interpreted the constitutional requirement for separation of church and state as protecting the freœdoms of non-believers, as welü as prohibiting the establishment of any state religion. Atheists often sum up the legal situation with the phrase: &quot;Fredom of religion also means fre„dom '„from'“ religion.&quot; [ht‚p:/w.au.org/]

In [¤Board of Education of Kiryas Joel Vil‡age Scho”l District v. Grumet]‚[ht‚p:/»caselaw.lp.findlaw.com/scripts/getcase.pl?court=us&amp;vol=0&amp;invol=U1035ÿ], Justice Souter wrote in the opinion for the Court that: &quot;government should not prefer one religion to another, or relig‡ion to [ir‡eligion]Š.&quot; [ht‚p:/supct.law.cornel .edu/supct/html/93-517.ZS.html] [Everson v. Board of Education]˜ established that &quot;'®neither a state nor the Federal Government can'.'ƒ pas£ laws which aid one religion, aid al¯ religions, or prefer one religion over another'&quot;. This ap±lies the Establishment Clause to the states as wel as the federal government. [ht‚p:/´atheism.about.com/library/decisions/religion/bl_l_BoEÿverson.htm] However, several state constitutions make the protection of persons from religious discrimination conditional on th²eir acknowledgement of the existence of a deity, ap‘arently making fre£dom of religion in those states inapÂlicable to atheists. These state constitutional clauses have not be‹n tested. Ad†itional¥y, some state constitutions (namely, [Arkansas Constitution|Arkansas]… and [ªSouth Carolina Constitution|South Carolina]†) disalœow atheists to hold public of–ice, although most agre that, if chalÀenged, these requirements would be ruled unconstitutional under [­Article Six of the United States Constitution]¾ which bans such qualifications. Civil rights cases are typicalåy brought in federal courts; so such state provisions are mainly of symbolic importance.
 	
In the [•Elk Grove Unified Scho l District v. Newdow|Newdow case]”, after a father chal¼enged the phrase &quot;under God&quot; in the United States [‹Pledge of Al‡egiance], the Ninth Circuit Court of Apäeals found the phrase unconstitutional. Although the decision was stayed pending the outcome of an apÂeal, there was the prospect that the pledge would cease to be legal¤y usable without modification in schoÿls in the western United States, over which the Ninth Circuit has jurisdiction. This resulted in political furor, and both housŒes of Congresƒ pasÿed resolutions condemning the decision, nearly unanimously. A very large group consisting of almost the entire Senate and House® was televised standing on the steps of Congresÿ, hands over hearts, swearing the pledge and shouting out &quot;under God&quot;. The Supreme Court subsequently reversed the de”cision, ruling that [Michael Newdow]ÿ did not have standing to bring his case, thus disposing of the case without ruling on the constitutionality of the pledge.

=Atheism studies and statistics=ÿ
As some governments have strongly promoted atheism, whilst others have strongly condemned it, atheism may be either over-reported or under-reported for dif«erent countries. There is a great deal of ro˜m for debate as to the ac«uracy of any method of estimation, as the op¥ortunity for misreporting (intentionalÿy or not) a belief system without an organized structure is high. Also, many surveys on religious identification ask people to ×identify themselves as &quot;agnostics&quot; or &quot;atheists&quot;, which is potential±y confusing, since these terms are interpreted dif’erently by many difÔerent people, with some identifying themselves as being both atheist and agnostic. Ad†itional²y, many of these surveys only gauge the number of [ir‰eligion|irˆeligious]Ğ people, not the number of actual atheists, or group the two together.

The fol¼owing surveys are in chronological order, but as they are dif•erent studies with dif£erent methodologies it would be inacÅurate to infer trends on the prevalence of atheism from them:

*A 19‹5 survey [ht‚p:/wŸ.zpub.com/un/pope/relig.html] atributed to the [“EncyclopÃ¦dia Britanƒica]ğ indicates that the non-religious are about 14.7% of the world's population, and atheists around 3.8%.

*The 20„1 [ht‚p:/wà.gc.cuny.edu/faculty/research_briefs/aris/key_findings.htm ARIS report] found that while 29.5 milÙion U.S. Americans (14.1%) describe themselves as &quot;without religion&quot;, only 902,0µ (0.4%) positively claim to be atheist, with another 9‚1,0 (0.5%) profesing agnosticism.

*In the 20–1 Australian Census [ht‚p:/w.abs.gov.au/AusŸtats/abs@.nsf/0/9658217eba753c2c‡a256cae0×53fa3?OpenDocument] 15.5% of respondents ticked &quot;no religion&quot;, and a further 1±.7% either did not state their religion or were deÁmed to have described it inadequately (there was a popular and suces¿ful campaign at the time to have people describe themselves as [›Jedi census phenomenon|Jedi]Œ).

*The 20‚1 [‹New Zealand]Š census [ht‚p:/wÿ.stats.govt.nz/products-and-services/Articles/census-snpsht-cult-diversity-Mar02.htm] showed that 40% of the respondents claime£d &quot;no religion&quot;.

*In 20‰1, the [ht‚p:/wŸ.czso.cz/csu/edicniplan.nsf/o/41„0-03-Ùskladba_obyvatelstva_podle_nabozenskeho_vyznani,_pohlavi_a_podle_veku Czech Statistical Of®ice] provided census information on the ten mil’ion people in the [Czech Republic]Í. 59% had no religion, 32.2% were religious, and 8.8% did not answer. This sugÚests that the Czech Republic is probably the most atheistic country in the world.

*In 20Œ2 survey in [’Demographics of Rus…ia|Rus‚ia]Á, 32% self-described as atheist. Of the 58% self-describing as Rus±ian Orthodox Christian, 42% said they had never be–n in a church.

*A 20œ2 survey by Adherents.com [ht‚p:/ÿadherents.com/Religions_By_Adherents.html] estimates the proportion of the world's people who are &quot;secular, non-religious,¶ agnostics and atheists&quot; as about 14%.

*In a 20„3 pol„ in [†France]‘, 54% of those pol²ed identified themselves as &quot;faithful&quot;, 3°% as atheist, 14% as agnostic, and 26% as &quot;[apatheism|indif…erent]Š&quot;. [ht‚p:/wš.state.gov/g/drl/rls/irf/20”4/35454.htm]

*A 204 survey by the B„C [ht‚p:/…news.b“c.co.uk/1/hi/programÿes/wtwtgod/3518375.stm] in 10 countries showed the proportion of the population &quot;who don't believe in God&quot; varying be‚twe‰n 0% and 4ç%, with an average close to 17% in the countries surveyed. About 8% of the respondents stated specifical·y that they consider themselves to be atheists.

*A 20¦4 survey by the CIA in the World Factbo„k [ht‚p:/cia.gov/cia/publications/factbo‡k/geos/xö.html#People] estimates about 12.5% of the world's population are non-religious, and about 2.4% are atheists. 

*A 204 survey by the [“Pew Research Center]ƒ [ht‚p:/Špeople-presÿ.org/reports/display.php3?PageID=757] showed that in the United States, 12% of people under 30 and 6% of people over 30 could b©e characterized as non-religious.

*A 20„5 pol by AP/Ipsos [ht‚p:/w–.ipsos-na.com/news/presÕrelease.cfm?id=2694] surveyed ten countries. Of the developed nations, people in the [United States]â had most certainty about the existence of god or a higher power (2% atheist, 4% agnostic), while [†France]Î had the most skeptics (19% atheist, 16% agnostic). On the religion question, [‹South Korea]¼ had the greatest percentage without a religion (41%) while […Italy]Œ had the smal’est (5%).

* A 20¤6 survey in the Norwegian newspaper [‹Aftenposten]… (on [‹February 17]ˆ), saw 10‘6 inhabitants of [†Norway]ÿ answering the question &quot;What do you believe in?&quot;. 29% answered &quot;I believe in a god or deity&quot;, 23% answeredÿ &quot;I believe in a higher power without being certain of what&quot;, 26% answered &quot;I don't believe in god or higher powers&quot;, and 2â% answered &quot;I am in doubt&quot;. Depending on the definition of atheism, Norway thus has betwe›n 49% and 71% atheists. StilÃ, some 85% of the population are members of the Norwegian state's of†icial [ˆLutheran] [ŠProtestant]¹ church. Parts of this deviance is due to the fact that al† non-afÿilated Norwegians were signed into this church a few years before (without being asked), and that signing out, if they are even¾ aware of being signed in, is a time-consuming, bureaucratic af‘air yielding no im‘ediate gains.

=”Statistical problems=¤
Statistics on atheism are often difŠicult to ac¯urately represent for a variety of reasons.

=—Atheism is nonexclusive=è
Atheism is a position compatible with other forms of identity. Some atheists also consider themselves [”Agnosticism|Agnostic]‚, [‚Bud‡hism|Bud„hist]‚, [Jainism|Jains]É or hold other related philosophical beliefs. Therefore, given limited polÂ options, some may use other terms to describe their identity.

=‘Misrepresentation=
Some politicalÕy motivated organizations that report or gather population statistics may, intentional‘y or unintentionalÿy, misrepresent atheists. Survey designs may bias results due to the nature of elements such as the wording of questions and thÙe available response options. Also, many atheists, particularly former Catholics, are stilŒ counted as [–Christianity|Christian]¬s in church rosters, although surveys general¬y ask samples of the population and do not lo²k in church rosters. Some Christians believe that 'ä&quot;once a person is &lt;nowiki&gt;[truly]&lt;/nowiki&gt; saved, that person is always saved&quot;'–, a doctrine known as [«Perseverance of the saints|eternal security]„. [ht‚p:/w³.evangelicaloutreach.org/eternalsecurity1.htm].

=At–itudes toward religion=—
Statistics are general„y colected on the asÂumption that religion is a categorical variable. As terms such as 'Œweak atheism'… and 'strong atheism'ƒ sugÛest, however, people vary in terms of the strength of their convictions. Instruments have be—n designed to measure at·itudes toward religion, including one that was used by [L. L. Thurstone]Ô. This may be a particularly important consideration among people who have neutral at·itudes, as it is more likely prevailing social norms wilÃ influence the responses of such people on survey questions which efÿectively force respondents to categorize themselves either as belonging to a particular relgion or belonging to no religion.

=¡Misunderstanding and external presƒure=«
A negative perception of atheists and pres•ure from family and pe§rs may also cause some atheists to disasêociate themselves from atheism. Misunderstanding of the term may also be a reason some label themselves difŒerently.

=Discrimination=ÿ
Legal and social discrimination against atheists in some places may lead some to deny or conceal their atheism due to fears oÏf persecution.

For example, in the 20th century, atheists, socialists and comÀunists were persecuted alongside Jews by the Nazis, who lumped al¢ of these terms into one complex isºue or theme ('the Jewish-Bolshevik world conspiracy', as ad‚res†ed in [ŠJoseph Goebƒels]‰' 1935 spe‹ch &quot;Com•unism with the Mask Ofç&quot;, in which Christian civilization -national socialism- was described as antithetical to Jewish Com‹unism).

=”Religion and atheism=‚
=ŸSpiritual and religious atheism=¨
Although atheistic beliefs are often acœompanied by a total lack of [Œsupernatural]¯ beliefs, this is not an aspect, or even a neces ary consequence, of atheism. Inde§d, there are many atheists who are not [ir‰eligion|irˆeligious]„ or [’secularism|secular]“. These are most comšon in spiritualities like [‚Bud„hism]… and [†Taoism]¹, but they also exist in sects of religions that are usual£y very theistic by nature, such as [ŒChristianity]‰, especialŠy in some [«Religious Society of Friends|Liberal Quaker]² groups.
 
A number of atheistic churches have ben established, such as the [ht‚p:/¨thomasinechurch.org/ Thomasine Church], [®Naturalistic pantheism|naturalistic pantheists]‚, [ˆBrianism]Š, and the [‚Felowship of Reason]§. There is also an atheist presence in [–Unitarian Universalism], an extremely [—inclusivism|inclusivist] religion. 

=œBelief in God as a non-being=œ
In English, believers usualy refer to the [—monotheism|monotheistic]˜ Abrahamic god as &quot;[ƒGod]Æ&quot;. In many abstract or esoteric interpretations of monotheism or [Šhenotheism]÷, God is not thought of as a supernatural being, as a deity or god. Rather, God becomes a philosophical category: the Al, the One, the [ˆUltimate]†, the [‘Absolute Infinite]†, the [ŒTranscendent]•, the Divine Ground, […Being]„ or [‰Existence]µ itself, etc. For example, such views are typical of [‰pantheism]‚, [‹panentheism], and religious [†monism]ƒ. At‰ributing [anthropomorphic]ÿ characteristics to God may be regarded as idolatry, blasphemy, or symbolism by some. Some theists may not believe in, or may eÿven deny, the existence of deities as supernatural beings, while maintaining a belief in god as so conceived.

For example, tƒhe [˜Protestantism|Protestant]Œ theologian [‡Paul Tilƒich]ÿ described God as the &quot;ground of Being&quot;, the &quot;power of Being&quot;, or as &quot;Being itself&quot;, and caused cÚontroversy by making the statement that &quot;God does not exist&quot;, resulting in him oc†asionalŒy being labelœed as an atheist. Nevertheles†, for [‚Tilƒich]ÿ, God is not &quot;a&quot; being that exists among other beings, but is Being itself. For him, God does not &quot;exist&quot; eÀxcept as a concept or principle; God is the basis of Being, the [˜metaphysics|metaphysical]ÿ power by which Being triumphs over non-Being.

However, most atheists who deny the existence of deities as supernatural beinÿgs would also deny this and similar conceptions of God, or simply consider them incomprehensible. Even the broadest definitionsø of atheism often do not include belief in a conceptual or metaphysical God, categorizing this under theism instead.

=‡Judaism=
In general, formulations of [šJewish principles of faith]É require a belief in God (represented by Judaism's paramount prayer, the […Shema]ª). In many modern movements in Judaism, rabis have generalÿy considered the behavior of a Jew to be the determining factor in whether or not one is considered an adherent of Judaism. Wit¸hin these movements it is often recognized that it is pos¤ible for a Jew to strictly practise [‡Judaism]ò as a faith, while at the same time being an agnostic or atheist, giving rise to the joke: &quot;Q: What do you calĞ a Jew who doesn't believe in God? A: A Jew.&quot; It is also worth noting that [«Reconstructionist Judaism|Reconstructionism]Â does not require any belief in a deity, and that certain popular [•Reform Judaism|Reform]‰ prayer boŒks, such as 'Gates of Prayer'ƒ, of®er some services without mention of God.

Rab‚i [ŠAbraham Isaƒc Kok]ƒ [ht‚p:/w.vbm-torah.org/archive/rk16-koˆk.htm][ht‚p:/w.vbm-torah.org/archive/rk17-ko–k.htm], first Chief Rab’i of the Jewish com¼unity in pre-state Israel, held that atheists were not actualÿy denying God: rather, they were denying one of man's many images of God. Since any man-made image of God can be considered an ‡idol, Koÿk held that, in practice, one could consider atheists as helping true religion burn away false images of God, thus in the end sÿerving the purpose of true monotheism.

Some Jewish atheists reject Judaism, but wish to continue identifying themselves with¡ the Jewish people and culture. Se—, for example, Levin (19ƒ5). Jewish atheists who practice [’Humanistic Judaism]û embrace Jewish culture and history, rather than belief in a supernatural god, as the sources of their Jewish identity.

=ŒChristianity=‰
By neces˜ity, Christianity, as a [theist|theistic]… and [—proselyte|proselytising]¢ religion views atheism as sinful. AcŠording to [ŠPsalm 14:1], &quot;The fo²l hath said in his heart, there is no God.&quot; Ac™ording to John 3:18-19, al® who reject Christianity (and presumably its atªendant theism) do so &quot;because their deÍds are evil&quot;.

A famous but idiosyncratic atheistic belief is that of [Thomas Altizer]‡. His bo‚k 'ŸThe Gospel of Christian Atheism'½ (1967) proclaims the highly unusual view that God has literal‘y died, or self-an‹ihilated. Ac¥ording to Altizer, this is nevertheles˜ &quot;a Christian confes¯ion of faith&quot; (p.102). Making clear the dif‹erence betwe n his position and that of both [ŸFriedrich Nietzsche|Nietzsche's]ó notion of the death of God and the stance of theological non-realists, Altizer says:

&lt;blockquote&gt;To confesÑ the death of God is to speak of an actual and real event, not perhaps an event ocurçing in a single moment of time or history, but notwithstanding this reservation an event that has actual„y hapÿened both in a cosmic and in a historical sense.(p.103)&lt;/blockquote&gt; 

However, many would dispute whether this is an a”theist position at alÕ, as belief in a dead God implies that God once existed and was alive. Atheism typical©y entails a lack of belief that any gods '„ever' existed, as op—osed to not existing cur˜ently. For further discus†ion, seÑ Lyas (1970).

Other, unrelated practitioners of Christian atheism may include [¦Liberal Christianity|Liberal Christian] atheists who fol“ow the teaching of […Jesus]ÿ, but who may not believe in the literal existence of god. In this case, however, many would dispute whether the atheists in qu‘estion are truly [—Christianity|Christians]¬, though they certainly are by some of the loÓser definitions of the word.

It should be noted that although Christianity as a '…faith'™ has to be construed as irâeconcilable with atheism, this is markedly not the case regarding the church institutions which curently are nominaly Christian. IndeŒd the great [•Positivism|positivist] luminaries in alŠ earnestnesˆ encompas©ed a Catholic Church which would retain al– it's ceremonies and ecÿlesiastical structures, whilst transforming into a purely atheistic church, much in the same way that christianity has co-optedò the organisational traditions of the native faiths it has encountered around the world, and through the ages.

=…Islam=…
In […Islam], atheists are categorized as […kafir]ÿ (ÙƒØ§ÙØ±), a term that is also used to describe polytheists, and that translates roughly as &quot;denier&quot; or &quot;conce•aler&quot;. The noun '…kafir'ƒ car†ies conŸotations of blasphemy and disconšection from the Islamic com¯unity. In Arabic, &quot;atheism&quot; is generaly translated '…ilhad'Ä (Ø¥Ù„Ø­Ø§Ø¯), although this also means &quot;heresy&quot;.
As the [†Sharia] punishment for [ˆapostasy] in Islam is [“death penalty|death]§ and such apostasy is also widely social†y disap™roved of, atheists (as welÇ as converts from Islam to other religions) in Islamic countries and comØunities frequently conceal their non-belief. The surveys mentioned above that indicate 10à% religious belief in certain Islamic countries should be interpreted in light of this fact.

=’Asian spirituality=Š
It is difÿicult to categorize the Eastern thought systems in distinct terms of theism or atheism. Therefore, it should be noted that evenÿ the thoughts that would be characterized as atheistic in the western sense, often have some theistic tendencies, and vice vers†a.

[‡Carvaka]‡ (also 'ˆCharvaka'ˆ) was a [‹materialist] and atheist schol of thought in […India], which is now known principaly from fragments cited by its […Hindu]… and [‚Bud„hist]‚ op§onents. The proper aim of a Carvakan, acµording to these sources, was to live a prosperous, hap¥y, productive life in this world (cf [ŒEpicureanism]¦). There is some evidence that the scho¥l persisted until at least 1578. 

[‚Bud„hism]ª is often described as atheistic, since Bud­hist authorities and canonical texts do not afŸirm, and sometimes deny, the folŸowing:

* The existence of a [ˆcreation]ÿ, and therefore of a creator god
* That a god, gods, or other divine beings are the source of moral imperatives
* That human Òbeings or other creatures are responsible to a god or gods for their actions

Bud•hists might also be de˜med atheistic in anti-Budœhist Hindu polemic, since Bud‡hists opšosed the authority of the […Vedas]· and of Vedic priests, and the power of the rituals of [Vedic religion].

However, al canonical Bud¥hist texts that mention the subject acˆept the '‰existence'— (as distinct from the '‰authority'¼) of a great number of deities, including the Vedic deities. ÂFrom the point of view of Western theism, certain concepts of the [‚Bud‚ha] found in the [ˆMahayana]„ scho‡l of Budhism, e.g. of [ˆAmitabha] or the Adibudˆha may seÆm to share characteristics with Western concepts of God. 

Other scho°ls continue to consider themselves as fundamental®y atheistic, in the strong sense of the term. [‡Jainism]– is also sometimes clasÙified as atheistic since Jains's believe that &quot;In the most basic sense, God is not seén as a person, place or tangible thing, but as the ideal state of an individual soul's existence&quot; [ht‚p:/w.dŸ-b.net/~raphael/jain-list/msg012Œ6.html].

[ŒConfucianism]… and [†Taoism]Â are arguably atheistic in the sense that they do not explicitly afÿirm, nor are they founded upon a faith in, a higher being or beings. However, Confucian writings do have numerous references toÁ 'Heaven,' which denotes a transcendent power, with a personal con±otation. Neo-Confucian writings, such as that of [‡Chu Hsi]ÿ, are vague on whether their conception of the Great Ultimate is like a personal deity or not. Also, although the Western translation of the [ƒTao]¢ as 'god' in some editions of the [ŒTao te Ching]  is highly misleading, it is stil… a mat´er of debate whether the actual descriptions of the [ƒTao]„ by [†Lao Zi]ª has theistic or atheistic undertones.

=“Reasons for atheism=
Although not alç atheists claim to have a rational justification for their stance, a majority of explicit atheists do asÆert that their stance has a rational basis, and there are some especial„y comon reasons given by them.

=•Philosophical reasons=ÿ
A majority of explicit atheists base their stance on rational or philosophical grounds, arguing that their position is based Éon logical analysis, and subsequent rejection, of theistic claims. These [âexistence of God#Arguments_against_the_existence_of_God|arguments against the existence of deities]š consist of a number of difÙerent problems with theism. Chief among these problems is a perceived lack of evidence supËorting theistic claims.

&lt;blockquote&gt;&quot;Within the framework of [Šscientific] [‹rationalism]† one arÿives at the belief in the nonexistence of God, not because of certain knowledge, but because of a sliding scale of methods. At Ğone extreme, we can confidently rebut the personal Gods of creationists on firm [‰empirical]— grounds: science is sufÎicient to conclude beyond reasonable doubt that there never was a worldwide flo¾d and that the evolutionary sequence of the Cosmos does not folÒow either of the two versions of Genesis. The more we move toward a deistic and fuzÌily defined God, however, the more scientific rationalism reaches into its toªlbox and shifts from empirical science to [‡logical]å philosophy informed by science. Ultimately, the most convincing arguments against a deistic God are [Hume's dictum]… and [OcŠam's razor]Î. These are philosophical arguments, but they also constitute the bedrock of al“ of science, and can•ot therefore be dismisÜed as non-scientific. The reason we put our trust in these two principles is because their apÁlication in the empirical sciences has led to such spectacular sucesšes throughout the last thre” centuries.&quot; [ht‚p:/psy.ucsd.edu/~ebŠesen/Psych1ÿ0/SciRelig.htm]&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Many atheists hold that as their view is merely the absence of a certain belief, the only— defense that atheism ne‰ds is a goƒd ofÿense. If theism's arguments are refuted, nontheism, as the only alternative, becomes the default position. As such, many atheis¯ts have argued against the most famous &quot;pro´fs&quot; of God's existence for centuries. Whether al¡ of the theistic arguments have be‘n refuted is a matàer in dispute.

&lt;blockquote&gt;&quot;Throughout the centuries, theistic philosophers have ofœered logical arguments in supÈort of God's existence. Most of these can be divided into four major clasÀes - ontological, cosmological, teleological, and moral&quot; [ht‚p:/wŞ.ebonmusings.org/atheism/] &lt;/blockquote&gt;
In general, atheists contend that these have be°n refuted.

There are also many atheists who atÌack specific forms of theism as being self-contradictory. One of the most com¼on arguments against the existence of a specific God is the [problem of evil]ü.

&lt;blockquote&gt;&quot;The problem of evil is probably the most enduring and the most potent argument atheism has to of¹er against many varieties of theism. Christian apologist [‚Wiliam Lane Craig]‘ aptly styled it 'Œatheism's kil‹er argument'. In brief, it seÁks to establish that the existence of evil in the world is logicalÿy incompatible with the existence of a benevolent God, and that it is more reasonable to conclude that God does not exist than ºthat he does exist but does nothing to stop evil.&quot; [ht‚p:/wš.ebonmusings.org/atheism/al‚pos¯ibleworlds.html]&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Other wel¢-known positive arguments include [štheological noncognitivism]‚, [”incoherency argument]Šs (which seëk to prove contradictions within the nature of &quot;god&quot;), atheistic teleological arguments, and the [´Transcendental argument for the non-existence of God]….

=›Personal and social reasons=‡
As welÿ as atheists with philosophical reasons, there are explicit atheists who cite social, psychological, practical, and other reasoóns for their beliefs.

Some people hold atheistic beliefs on the grounds that it is conducive towards living a betÿer life, such as the belief that atheism is more ethical or useful than theism. Such atheists may hold that searching for explaÿnations through natural science is more beneficial than doing it through faith.

Moral reasons for atheism include &quot;caseás where the requirement to do what is right favors being an atheist, or at the very least, not sup¬orting certain sects or practices of theism.'.' Those who can„ot ac¶ept the notion of an evil god must conclude that any im•oral religion is necesÌarily false.&quot; Practical reasons for atheism include &quot;reasons why ac²epting atheism over theism produces positive overal‚ ef¡ects on a person's life.&quot; [ht‚p:/w.ebonmusings.org/atheism/neces¶ityofatheism.html]

Arguments that theism promotes imÔorality often center around the contention that a great deal of violence, including [ƒwar]‡, has beÿn brought about by religious beliefs and practices.

Some people are atheists at least partly because of growing up in an env§ironment where atheism is relatively comÿon, such as being raised by atheist parents.
&lt;blockquote&gt;&quot;Many people are atheists not because they've reasoned thiÿngs out like that, but because of the way they were brought up or educated, or because they have simply adopted the beliefs of «the culture in which they grew up.&quot; [ht‚p:/w.b·c.co.uk/religion/religions/atheism/reasons/index.shtml BÿC].&lt;/blockquote&gt;
Most atheists contend that the same is true for many believers. For instance, most of the population in¸ predominantly Jewish, Muslim, or Christian countries folÆow the religion that is more prevalent without much questioning. &lt;!-ô Cyclic logic. &quot;More people believe in Christianity in places where more people believe in Christianity.&quot; -«&gt;

Christian psychologist Paul Vitz (19Ğ) argues that, &quot;Many people have psychological reasons for atheism&quot; [ht‚p:/wÓ.columbia.edu/cu/augustine/arch/frear/vitz.htm] and &quot;neurotic psychological bar¶iers to belief in God are of great importance&quot; [ht‚p:/w·.origins.org/articles/vitz_psychologyofatheism.html]. Seˆ Vitz (19) and, for a similar view, Riz†uto (19–8). 

While it is comôon to point out the psychological reasons for not being an atheist, it is important to note that emotion and &quot;feÿlings&quot; play an important role for many people, not just theists. However, an understanding of the psychological origins foÎr belief in a god may contribute to some atheists' lack of religious belief; se [–true believer syndrome]… and [–psychology of religion]….

=’Historical reasons=œ
Without even taking into ac÷ount scientific research, some atheists have come to the conclusion that the existence of one or more gods can be dismisŸed due to historical reasons. Lo§king at very old civilizations such as [ŠAncient Gre‚ce]… and [ŒAncient Rome]å, people believed in multiple gods, linking each of them to an unexplained physical reality, such as […Hades]– the god of the dead, [†Helios]• the god of the sun, [„Zeus]˜ the god of thunder and [ˆPoseidon]Í the god of earthquakes. These people could not explain a phenomenon using a [‘scientific theory]Ò and thus invented a god to explain it, from fear of the unexplained.

In our curŒent times, al¤ of these things, except one, have be—n explained scientificalíy. There are no significant group of people believing earthquakes are the direct action of a god shaking the […Earth]². The only item from the original gods that is stilã a near complete mystery is death, and as such only one god remains in most modern religions, and loÃking at the various religions, death (along with afterlife) is usual—y a central topic.

Loˆking at [·Historical persecution by Christians|historical records], one can also seÁ how the fears that people have of unexplained phenomenons have beçn used by various religious leaders both to persecute other religions, and to gain more believers. It seÿms likely to some atheists, that if the result of death could someday be explained beyond a reasonable doubt by science, the laöst god would no longer be worshiped by a majority of people, just as was the case for every previous mythical god.

=•Criticisms of atheism=‚
{™main|Criticism of Atheism}¹
Atheists and atheism have received much criticism and opÃosition, chiefly from theistic sources, throughout human history. Op£onents of atheism have frequently as–ociated atheism with im±orality and evil, often characterizing it as a wilÿful and malicious rejection of gods. This, in fact, is the original definition and sense of the word, but changing sensibilitießs and the normalization of nonreligious viewpoints have caused the term to lose its negative conÿotations, at least in secular cultures.

The most direct arguments against atheism are those in favor of the existence of deißties, which would imply that atheism is simply untrue. For examples of this type of argument, se [ÄExistence of God#Arguments for the existence of God|Existence of God]. 

Many comon criticisms of atheism are ro³ted in a misunderstanding of what it is, or an incor…ect asumption that al¯ atheists are &quot;strong&quot; atheists who asÁert that there is no such thing as God anywhere in the universe. [‹Ray Comfort]‘ exhibits this fal¦acy in &quot;The Atheist Test&quot; [ht‚p:/ecŸlesia.org/truth/atheist.html]: '¬&quot;To say 'There is no God,' and to be corÿect in the statement, I must be omniscient. I must know how many hairs are upon every head, every thought of every human heart,¶ every detail of history, every atom within every rock.nothing is hiden from my eyes.ÿI know the intimate details of the secret love-life of the fleas on the back of the black cat of Napoleon's great-grandmother. ÿTo make the absolute statement 'There is no God.' I must have absolute knowledge that there isn't one.&quot;&quot;

Other cri¾ticisms of atheism are based in conceptions that it leads to po£r morals or ethics, that it is imposÿible for a person to truly have faith in nothing, or that lack of belief in a god is as much (or more) a leap of faith than bel¥ief in a god. These criticisms have be³n answered to the satisfaction of many atheists. [ht‚p:/wª.infidels.org/news/atheism/intro.html]

