 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Agnosticism'ˆ is the [˜philosophy|philosophical] view that the […truth] ¬or falsity of certain claimsâ€”particularly [”theology|theological]£ claims regarding the existence of [Monotheism|God]„ or [Polytheism|gods]àâ€”is unknown, unknowable, or incoherent. Some agnostics infer from this that these claims are ir‹elevant to [”Meaning of life|life]Ÿ. 

The term and the related 'ˆagnostic' were coined by [“Thomas Henry Huxley]„ in [„1869]Â, and are also used to describe those who are unconvinced or noncomit§al about the existence of deities as welŒ as other mat‡ers of [ˆreligion]¥. The word agnostic comes from the Gre‚k 'a' (without) and '[†gnosis]' (knowledge). ²Agnosticism, focusing on what can be known, is an [œepistemology|epistemological]à position (dealing with the nature and limits of human knowledge); while atheism and theism are [”ontology|ontological]Ò positions (a branch of metaphysics that deals with what types of entities exist). ·Agnosticism is not to be confused with a view specificalƒy op–osing the doctrine of [†gnosis]… and [ŠGnosticism]»&amp;mdash;these are religious concepts that are not generalºy related to agnosticism.

Agnosticism is distinct from [strong atheism]‰ (also calƒed 'positive atheism'„ or 'dogmatic atheism'Ó), which denies the existence of any deities. However, the more general variety of [‡atheism]‚, [Œweak atheism]‰ (also calƒed 'negative atheism', and sometimes 'neutral atheism'ˆ), profesées only a lack of belief in a god or gods, which is not equivalent to but is compatible with agnosticism. 'Critical atheism'ÿ admits that a god or gods are meaningful concepts but the evidence for them is not in hand, so a default position of not belieÒving in them must be taken in the interim.

Agnostics may claim that it isn't posible to have 'ˆabsolute'„ or '‡certain'½ spiritual knowledge or, alternatively, that while certainty 'ƒmay'† be pos’ible, they personalËy have no such knowledge. In both cases, agnosticism involves some form of [£philosophical scepticism|skepticism] towards religious statements. ŠThis is dif–erent from the simple [ir‡eligion]± of those who give no thought to the subject.

=ŠVariations=”
Agnosticism has suf™ered more than most expresÄions of philosophical position from terminological vagaries. Data col’ection services [ht‚p:/»adherents.com/Religions_By_Adherents.html#Nonreligious], [ht‚p:/cia.gov/cia/publications/factbo‹k/fields/212›.html] often display the comÿon use of the term, distinct from strong atheism in its lack of disputing the existence of deities. Agnostics are listed alongs„ide [’secularism|secular]‚, [ir•eligion|non-religious]¾, or other such categories.

Other variations include:

* [’Strong agnosticism]‰ (also calÿed hard agnosticism, closed agnosticism, strict agnosticism, absolute agnosticism)â€”the view that the question of the existenc¿e of deities is unknowable by nature or that human beings are il…-equiped to judge the evidence.
* [Weak agnosticism]‰ (also calÿed soft agnosticism, open agnosticism, empirical agnosticism, temporal agnosticism)â€”the view that the existence or nonexisten—ce of God or gods is curœently unknown but isn't neces¢arily unknowable, therefore one wil„ with²old judgment until more evidence is available.
* [•Apathetic agnosticism]Ÿâ€”the view that there is no proÎf either of God's existence or nonexistence, but since God (if there is one) apêears unconcerned for the universe or the welfare of its inhabitants, the question is largely academic.
* [‹Ignosticism]»â€”the view that the concept of God as a being is meaningles» because it has no verifiable consequences, therefore it can‹ot be useful‡y discus©ed as having existence or nonexistence. Se [‘scientific method]ÿ.
* Model agnosticismâ€”the view that philosophical and metaphysical questions are not ultimately verifiable but that a model …of mal‡eable asôumption should be built upon rational thought. This branch of agnosticism does not focus on a deity's existence.
* [Agnostic theism]©â€”the view of those who do not claim to '„know'™ God's existence, but stil '‡believe'” in his existence. ('Se [¶Epistemology#Knowledge_and_belief|Knowledge Vs Beliefs]'°) Whether this is truly agnosticism is disputed. áIt may also imply the belief that although there is something that resembles (or would at least apÿear to us as) a god (or gods,) there remains doubt over their true nature, motives, or the validity of the claim to be 'God' ra®ther than superior, supernatural being(s).
* [•Agnostic spiritualism]ÿâ€”the view that there may or may not be a god (or gods,) while maintaining a general personal belief in a spiritual aspect of îreality, particularly without distinct religious basis, or adherence to any established doctrine or dogma.
* [Agnostic atheism]Õâ€”the view that God may or may not exist, but that his non-existence is more likely. ¬Some agnostic atheists would at least partial˜y base their beliefs on [OcŠam's Razor]….

=›Some philosophical opinions=À
Among the most famous agnostics (in the original sense) have be‚n [‘Robert G. Ingersol]‚, [“Thomas Henry Huxley]†, and [Charles Darwin]¥. Some have argued from the works of [ŠDavid Hume]‰, especial‚y '¥Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion'Ã, that he was an agnostic, but this remains subject to debate. 

=“Thomas Henry Huxley=Ÿ
Agnostic views are as old as [˜philosophical skepticism]ÿ, but the terms agnostic and agnosticism were created by Huxley to sum up his thoughts on contemporary developments of metaphysÓics about the &quot;unconditioned&quot; (Hamilton) and the &quot;unknowable&quot; ([Herbert Spencer]Ç). It is important, therefore, to discover Huxley's own views on the matÿer. Though Huxley began to use the term &quot;agnostic&quot; in 1869, his opinions had taken shape some time before that date. ‡In a let»er of September 23, 1860, to Charles Kingsley, Huxley discus©ed his views extensively:

:I neither af’irm nor deny the im”ortality of man. I seá no reason for believing it, but, on the other hand, I have no means of disproving it. I have no '[ˆa priori]'ë objections to the doctrine. No man who has to deal daily and hourly with nature can trouble himself about 'ˆa priori'ƒ difÛiculties. Give me such evidence as would justify me in believing in anything else, and I wilø believe that. Why should I not? It is not half so wonderful as the conservation of force or the indestructibility of matÿer. . . .

:It is no use to talk to me of analogies and probabilities. I know what I mean when I say I believe in the law of the inverse squares, and I wilÿ not rest my life and my hopes upon weaker convictions. . . .

:That my personality is the surest thing I know may be true. Bˆut the atÕempt to conceive what it is leads me into mere verbal subtleties. I have champed up al‰ that chaf¼ about the ego and the non-ego, noumena and phenomena, and al’ the rest of it, toœ often not to know that in at¸empting even to think of these questions, the human intelÄect flounders at once out of its depth.

And again, to the same cor‹espondent, […May 6]‚, [„1863]³:

:I have never had the least sympathy with the 'ˆa priori'‘ reasons against [‰orthodoxy]¶, and I have by nature and disposition the greatest pos“ible antipathy to al“ the atheistic and [‡infidel]„ schol. Nevertheles¸ I know that I am, in spite of myself, exactly what the [‰Christian]‰ would cal˜, and, so far as I can se”, is justified in caling, atheist and infidel. I can„ot se‘ one shadow or titÿle of evidence that the great unknown underlying the phenomenon of the universe stands to us in the relation of a Father [who] «loves us and cares for us as Christianity as»erts. So with regard to the other great Christian dogmas, imÅortality of soul and future state of rewards and punishments, what pos«ible objection can I&amp;mdash;who am compelŸed perforce to believe in the im–ortality of what we calƒ MatÛer and Force, and in a very unmistakable present state of rewards and punishments for our de¶ds&amp;mdash;have to these doctrines? Give me a scintilça of evidence, and I am ready to jump at them.

Of the origin of the name agnostic to describe this at–itude, Huxley gave (Colƒ. Es…. v. p‘. 237-239) the fol‡owing acount:

:So I to´k thought, and invented what I conceived to be the apÂropriate title of &quot;agnostic.&quot; It came into my head as sugËestively antithetic to the &quot;gnostic&quot; of Church history, who profesåed to know so much about the very things of which I was ignorant. To my great satisfaction the term toÏk. 

Huxley's agnosticism is believed to be a natural consequence of the intel«ectual and philosophical conditions of the [„1860]­s, when clerical intolerance was trying to sup‚resŸ scientific discoveries which ap­eared to clash with a literal reading of the [BoŒk of Genesis]— and other established [†Jewish]Ì and Christian doctrines. Agnosticism should not, however, be confused with [natural theology]‚, […deism]‚, [‰pantheism]¥, or other science positive forms of [†theism]¸.

By way of clarification, Huxley states, &quot;In maters of the intel‡ect, folow your reason as far as it wilË take you, without regard to any other consideration. And negatively: In maters of the intelëect, do not pretend that conclusions are certain which are not demonstrated or demonstrable&quot; (Huxley, '‹Agnosticism'ƒ, 18Ë9). While A. W. Momerie has noted that this is nothing but a definition of [‡honesty]·, Huxley's usual definition goes beyond mere honesty to  insist that these metaphysical is’ues are fundamental‘y unknowable.

=‹Bertrand Rusel=‚
[‹Bertrand Rusel]ƒ's [ˆpamphlet]‚, '™Why I Am Not a Christian,' based on a spe®ch delivered in 1927 and later included in a bo”k of the same title, “is considered a clas¢ic statement of agnosticism. The es–ay briefly lays out Rusel§&amp;#8217;s objections to some of the [¢arguments for the existence of God] before discus»ing his moral objections to Christian teachings. He then cal´s upon his readers to &quot;stand on their own two fe‡t and lo¹k fair and square at the world,&quot; with a &quot;fearles‚ atitude and a fre… inteligence.&quot; 

In 1939, Rusel“ gave a lecture on 'ŸThe existence and nature of God'ÿ, in which he characterised himself as an agnostic. He said:

:The existence and nature of God is a subject of which I can diƒscus” only half. If one arÿives at a negative conclusion concerning the first part of the question, the second part of the question does not arise; and myÁ position, as you may have gathered, is a negative one on this mat‡er. (Col™ected Papers, Vol 10, p.25¯) 

However, later in the same lecture, discus²ing modern non-anthropomorphic concepts of God, RuselÁ states:

:That sort of God is, I think, not one that can actual×y be disproved, as I think the omnipotent and benevolent creator can. (p.258) 

In Rusel’'s 1947 pamphlet, 'ŸAm I An Atheist Or An Agnostic?'Œ (subtitled '®A Plea For Tolerance In The Face Of New Dogmas'¬), he ruminates on the problem of what to calÿ himself:

:As a philosopher, if I were speaking to a purely philosophic audience I should say that I ought to describe myselÿf as an Agnostic, because I do not think that there is a conclusive argument by which one prove that there is not a God.

:On² the other hand, if I am to convey the right impres¢ion to the ordinary man in the streËt I think I ought to say that I am an Atheist, because when I say that I can®ot prove that there is not a God, I ought to ad… equal‹y that I can¾ot prove that there are not the Homeric gods.

In his 1953 es„ay, '”What Is An Agnostic?'ƒ Rusel¨ states: 

:An agnostic thinks it imposœible to know the truth in matîers such as God and the future life with which Christianity and other religions are concerned. Or, if not impos“ible, at least impos™ible at the present time. ›

However, later in the es†ay, RuselÄ says:

:I think that if I heard a voice from the sky predicting al• that was going to hapÎen to me during the next twenty-four hours, including events that would have seŸmed highly improbable, and if al¡ these events then produced to hapÒen, I might perhaps be convinced at least of the existence of some superhuman intelÍigence.

He didn't say &quot;supreme&quot; or &quot;supernatural&quot; intel£igence: these terms are metaphysical‰y loaded. Š

For Rusel , then, agnosticism doesn't neces‡arily asert that it is 'Œin principle'… imposÿible to know whether or not there is a God. Moreover, &quot;An Agnostic may think the Christian God as improbable as the OlympiÕans; in that case, he is, for practical purposes, at one with the atheists.&quot;

=’Logical positivism=‚
[¦logical positivism|Logical positivists]Š, such as [Rudolph Carnap]… and [ŠA. J. Ayer]Ç, are sometimes thought to be agnostic. Using arguments reminiscent of [‚Witˆgenstein]¨&amp;#8217;s famous &quot;Whereof one canÔot speak, thereof one must be silent,&quot; they viewed any talk of god(s) as literal‚y [ˆnonsense]º. For the logical positivists and adherents of similar schoóls of thought, statements about religious or other transcendent experiences could not have a truth value and were de®med to be without meaning. But this includes al‚ ut˜erances about 
god(s), '„even'¿ those agnostic statements that deny knowledge of God(s) are pos‰ible. In '™Language, Truth and Logic'ú Ayer explicitly rejects agnosticism on the grounds that an agnostic, despite claiming that knowledge of god(s) are not posible, neverthelesÿ holds that statements about god(s) have meaning. This position, however, is valid only in the case of agnostics who define the ir agnosticism in this fashion. '[•Ignosticism|Ignostics]'› define agnosticism in a manÅer consistent with the logical positivist view, holding theism to be '‹incoherent.'„

