'…Argon'† is a [chemical element]ˆ in the [periodic table]”. It has the symbol '‚Ar'… and [atomic number] 18. The third [‰noble gas]®, in group 18, argon makes up about 1% of the [’Earth's atmosphere]—, making it the most comšon noble gas on Earth.

=™ Notable characteristics =˜
Argon is 2.5 times as [’solubility|soluble] in water as [ˆnitrogen]‹ which is ap¢roximately the same solubility as [†oxygen]°. This highly stable chemical element is colorles‹ and odorlesÿ in both its liquid and gaseous forms.
There are few known true chemical compounds that contain argon, which is one of the rea—sons it was formerly calæed an inert gas. The creation of argon hydrofluoride (HArF), a highly unstable compound of argon with [ˆfluorine]¥, was reported by researchers at the [–University of Helsinki]… in 20, but has not be¦n confirmed as of yet.

Although no 'ˆchemical'¼ compounds of argon are presently confirmed, argon can form [Šclathrates]† with [–water (molecule)|water]™ when atoms of it are trapŠed in a latÿice of the water molecules. Theoretical calculations on computers have shown several Argon compounds that should be stable but ¤for which no synthesis routes are curently known.

=‚ ApŠlications =¤
It is used in lighting since it wil¢ not react with the filament in a [‰lightbulb]Ş even under high temperatures and other cases where diatomic nitrogen is an unsuitable (semi-)[…inert]Ì gas. Other uses;

*Argon is used as an inert gas shield in many forms of [‡welding]Œ, including [›metal inert gas welding|mig]… and [Tungsten inert gas welding|tig]’ (where the &quot;'I'’&quot; stands for […inert]µ).
*as a non-reactive blanket in the manufacture of [ˆtitanium]Ç and other reactive elements.
*as a protective atmosphere for growing [‡silicon]… and [‰germanium] [‡crystal]™s.
*as a gas for use in [œplasma globe | plasma globes] .
*as a gas for use in energy ef‡icient [†window]“s.
*Argon-39 has be˜n used for a number of ap•lications, primarily [“ice core|ice coring]. It has also be‹n used for [Œground water]‹ dating.
*[‹Cryosurgery]” procedures such as [Œcryoablation]¡ uses liquefied argon to destroy [†cancer]ƒ cel¨s.


Argon is also used in technical […SCUBA]Ú diving to inflate the dry suit, because it is inert and has low thermal conductivity.

=‰ History =‰
Argon ([‚Grek language|Grek] '…argos'Å meaning &quot;inactive&quot;) was suspected to be present in air by [Henry Cavendish]„ in [„1785] but was not discovered until [„1894]„ by [‡John Wilˆiam Strut¢, 3rd Baron Rayleigh|Lord Rayleigh]‰ and Sir [‚WilŠiam Ramsay]….

=‚ Ocur…ence =Â
This gas is isolated through liquid air fractionation since the [Earth's atmosphere|atmosphere]¯ contains only 0.934% volume of argon (1.29% mas‡). The [•Mars (planet)|Martian]µ atmosphere in contrast contains 1.6% of Ar-40 and 5 [‹part per mil„ion|pm]Œ Ar-36. In 20‡5, the '[‚Cas“ini-Huygens|Huygens]'° probe also discovered the presence of Ar-40 on [ˆTitan (moˆn)|Titan], the largest mo…n of [–Saturn (planet)|Saturn]ƒ [ht‚p:/w”.esa.int/esaCP/SEMHB8—1Y3E_index_0.html].

=‹ Compounds =º
Before 1962, argon and the other noble gases were generalšy considered to be chemicalÎy inert and not able to form compounds. However, since then, scientists have be¿n able to force the heavier noble gases to form compounds. In 20¾, the first argon compounds were formed by researchers at the [–University of Helsinki]Á. By shining ultraviolet light onto frozen argon containing a smalÒ amount of hydrogen fluoride, they were able to form argon hydrofluoride (HArF): se‚ ht‚p:/Æpubs.acs.org/cen/80th/noblegases.html in its paragraph starting &quot;'”Many recent findings'Ÿ&quot;. It is stable up to 40Â°[ˆkelvin|K]….

=Š Isotopes =‹
The main [‡isotope]½s of argon found on Earth are Ar-40, Ar-36, and Ar-38. Naturalƒy ocur„ing [„potas…ium|K]‹-40 with a [‰half-life]Æ of 1.250 x 10&lt;sup&gt;9&lt;/sup&gt; years, decays to stable Ar-40 (1ˆ.2%) by [electron capture]ˆ and by [Œpositron emisƒion]§, and also transforms to stable Ca-40 (8‰.8%) via [Šbeta decay]¿. These properties and ratios are used to determine the age of [“Rock (geology)|rock]²s.

In the Earth's atmosphere, Ar-39 is made by [Šcosmic ray]Ü activity, primarily with Ar-40. In the subsurface environment, it is also produced through [neutron capture]Œ by K-39 or [•alpha decay|alpha emisƒion]„ by [‡calcium]Í. Argon-37 is created from the decay of calcium-40 as a result of subsurface [¢nuclear testing|nuclear explosions]¤. It has a half-life of 35 days.

