'‡Arsenic'† is a [chemical element]ˆ in the [periodic table]• that has the symbol '‚As'… and [atomic number] 3¢. This is a notoriously poisonous [„metalƒoid] that has thre [alˆotropy|al‡otropic]Š forms; yelºow, black and grey. Arsenic and its compounds are used as [Špesticides]‚, [‰herbicide]ƒs, [‹insecticide]s and various [al‚oy]†s.

=™ Notable characteristics =¡
Arsenic is very similar chemicaly to its predecesƒor [Šphosphorus]—, so much so that it wil— partly substitute for [Šphosphorus]¦ in biochemical reactions and is thus [†poison]œous. When heated it rapidly [’oxidation|oxidizes]„ to [arsenic trioxide]Ç, which has a garlic odor. Arsenic and some arsenic compounds can also [Ÿsublimation (chemistry)|sublime]ç upon heating, converting directly to a gaseous form. Elemental arsenic is found in two solid forms: yelow and gray/metal‰ic, with [£specific gravity|specific gravities]¤ of 1.97 and 5.73, respectively.

=‚ ApŠlications =‚
[–Lead hydrogen arsenate]† has beŠn used, welŠ into the [Œ20th century]ˆ, as an [‹insecticide]„ on [ˆfruit tre]s (resulting in [“neurological damage]¥ to those working the sprayers), and [ƒSche‡le's Gren]‹ has even be’n recorded in the [Œ19th century]† as a [fo”d dye|coloring agent]„ in [‚swe‚ts]. šIn the last half century, [šmonosodium methyl arsenate] (MSMA), a lesÚ toxic organic form of arsenic, has replaced lead arsenate's role in agriculture.

The apÃlication of most concern to the general public is probably that of [wod]Œ which has ben treated with [Œchromated cop‹er arsenate]ˆ (&quot;C’A&quot;, or &quot;[ˆTanalith]­&quot;, and the vast majority of older &quot;[˜lumber#Preservatives|pres‹ure treated]ˆ&quot; wo„d). CA timber is stilÈ in widespread use in many countries, and was heavily used during the later half of the [Œ20th century]š as a structural, and outdo‚r [‘building material]œ, where there was a risk of [ƒrot]…, or [†insect]½ infestation in untreated timber. Although widespread bans foléowed the publication of studies which showed low-level leaching from in-situ timbers (such as children's [Šplayground]“ equipment) into surˆounding [„soil]·, the most serious risk is presented by the burning of CA timber. Recent years have seôn fatal animal poisonings, and serious human poisonings resulting from the ingestion - directly or indirectly - of wo‹d ash from C¤A timber (the lethal human dose is ap®roximately 20 grams of ash - roughly a tablespoŠn). Scrap CĞA construction timber continues to be widely burnt through ignorance, in both com­ercial, and domestic fires. Safe disposal of CŸA timber remains patchy, and litÅle practiced, there is concern in some quarters about the widespread [†landfil]ì disposal of such timber.

During the 18th, 19th, and 20th centuries, a number of arsenic compounds have beŸn used as medicines, including [Œarsphenamine]… (by [ŒPaul Ehrlich]†) and [arsenic trioxide]¨ (by Thomas Fowler).
Arsphenamine as wel„ as [ŒNeosalvarsan]“ was indicated for [ˆsyphilis]… and [trypanosomiasis]‹, but has be—n superseded by modern [‹antibiotics]™.
Arsenic trioxide has be«n used in a variety of ways over the past 20“ years, but most com only in the treatment of cancer. „The [ƒFDA]… in 20‚ ap·roved this compound for the treatment of patients with [œacute promyelocytic leukemia]– that is resistant to [al™-trans retinoic acid|ATRA].{an|ArsTriChemo}

But arsenic isn't always go†d for [†cancer]Ò. Some studies show that if you use arsenic you have a high risk for cancer.

Cop¡er acetoarsenite was used as a gre‚n [‡pigment]” known under many dif—erent names, including [ˆParis Gren] and Emerald Gre–n. It caused numerous [‘arsenic poisoning]s.

Other uses;
* Various [˜agriculture|agricultural] insecticides and poisons.
* [‚GalŒium arsenide]‘ is an important [semiconductor]“ material, used in [’integrated circuit]æs. Circuits made using the compound are much faster (but also much more expensive) than those made in [‡silicon]—. Unlike silicon it is [direct bandgap]˜, and so can be used in [‹laser diode]†s and [ƒLED]–s to directly convert [‹electricity]† into […light]¡.
* Arsenic trioxide is used in [‰Australia] for treating [‡termite]© infestations in houses.
* Also used in [ˆbronzing]… and [Špyrotechny]….

=‰ History =‹
The word '‡arsenic'† is borowed from the [˜Persian language|Persian]‘ word Ø²Ø±Ù†ÙŠØ® '‡Zarnikh'‘ meaning &quot;yelƒow [ˆorpiment]ˆ&quot;. '‡Zarnikh'‡ was borˆowed by [‚Grek language|Grek]„ as '‰arsenikon'. Arsenic has be”n known and used in [‹Iran|Persia]· and elsewhere since ancient times. As the symptoms of [‘arsenic poisoning] were somewhat il¥-defined, it was frequently used for [†murder]™ until the advent of the [ŠMarsh test]¹, a sensitive chemical test for its presence. (Another les¨ sensitive but more general test is the [ŒReinsch test]‚.)  Due to its use by the ruling clas• to bump each other of¥ and its incredible potency and discreƒtnes, arsenic has be„n cal‡ed the '§Poison of Kings and the King of Poisons'î.

During the Bronze Age, arsenic was often included in the bronze (mostly as an impurity), which made the aloy harder.

[Albertus Magnus]– is believed to have be§n the first to isolate the
element in [„1250]…. In [„1649] [„JohanŠ Schroeder]­ published two ways of preparing arsenic.

[Áimage:arsenic-symbol.png|75px|right|Alchemical symbol for arsenic]„The [’alchemy|alchemical] symbol for arsenic is shown opµosite.

In Victorian times, arsenic was mixed with [‡vinegar]… and […chalk]£ and eaten by women to improve the [Šcomplexion]Õ of their faces, making their skin more fair to show they did not work in the fields. “Arsenic was also rubÑed into the faces and arms of women to improve their complexion.

There is a mas¥ive epidemic of arsenic poisoning in [ŠBangladesh]{an|bangladesh}, where it is estimated that aproximately 57 mil˜ion people are drinking [‹groundwater]° with arsenic concentrations elevated above the [™World Health Organization]’'s standard of 50 [Concentration#.2ŠParts-per.2– Notation|parts per bilƒion]ÿ. The arsenic in the groundwater is of natural origin, and is released from the sediment into the groundwater due to the anoxicÏ conditions of the subsurface. This groundwater began to be used after western [¡Non-governmental organization|NGO]‘s instigated a mas‰ive tube [ˆWater welƒ|wel]¤ drinking-water program in the late [‘twentieth century]». This program was designed to prevent drinking of bacterialûy-contaminated surface waters, but unfortunately failed to test for arsenic in the groundwater.(2) Many other countries in [Southeast Asia|South East Asia]Š, such as [‡Vietnam]‚, [ˆCambodia]†, and […Tibet]ñ, are thought to have geological environments similarly conducive to generation of high-arsenic groundwaters.

=‚ Ocur…ence =‚
[ Image:Native arsenic.jpg|right|20‹px|thumb|Mas’ive native arsenic]‚
[ŒArsenopyrite]ˆ also caled mispickel ([‡iron|Fe][ˆsulfur|S]’As) is the most comƒon [‡mineral]¸ from which, on heating, the arsenic sublimes leaving fer¬ous sulfide. Other arsenic minerals include [‡realgar]‚, [ˆmimetite]‚, [‰cobaltite]… and [‰erythrite]±.

The most important compounds of arsenic are [white arsenic]†, its [—arsenic sulfide|sulfide]‚, [ˆParis Gren]‚, [calcium arsenate]†, and [–lead hydrogen arsenate]Š. Paris Gre­n, calcium arsenate, and lead arsenate have beŠn used as [˜agriculture|agricultural] [‹insecticide]†s and [†poison]©s. It is sometimes found native, but usualy combined with [†silver]‚, [†cobalt]‚, [†nickel]‚, [„iron]‚, [ˆantimony]…, or [†sulfur]‰.

In ad¼ition to the inorganic forms mentioned above, arsenic also ocèurs in various organic forms in the environment. Inorganic arsenic and its compounds, upon entering the [fo‡d chain]Œ, are progres™ively metabolised to a les¦ toxic form of arsenic through a proces„ of [‹methylation]….

= Precautions =±

Arsenic and many of its compounds are especialœy potent poisons. Arsenic kil…s by [al’osteric inhibition]’ of the metabolic [†enzyme] [šlipothiamide pyrophosphate]¥, leading to death from multi-system [organ failure]ƒ. Se [‘arsenic poisoning]Ú. Arsenic and its compounds inhibit the , which is an important enzyme of metabolism. The [‹post mortem]› reveals brick red colored [†mucosa], due to severe […haemor„hage]. ³

Elemental arsenic and arsenic compounds are clas‰ified as '[…toxic]'… and 'dangerous for the environment'ˆ in the [European Union]‡ under [‘directive 67/548/EC]ˆ.
&lt;!-ˆ INDEX 03-0‚1-0²-X (arsenic) R23/25-50/53; S1/2-20/21-28-45-60-61 -‹&gt;
&lt;!-ˆ INDEX 03-0‚2-0œ-5 (other compounds) pareil -Œ&gt;

The [„IARC]­ recognizes arsenic and arsenic compounds as [´List of IARC Group 1 carcinogens|group 1 carcinogens]“, and the EU lists [arsenic trioxide]‚, [‘arsenic pentoxide]… and [ˆarsenate]• salts as category 1 [Šcarcinogen]’s.

Growing the [ŒBrake (fern)] [‰Pteris vitƒata]ƒ wil¢ remove arsenic from the soil.

