'ˆAntimony'† is a [chemical element]ˆ in the [periodic table]• that has the symbol '‚Sb'‚ ([“Latin (language)|L.] '‡Stibium'†) and [atomic number]‡ 51. A [„metalƒoid]•, antimony has four alÄotropic forms. The stable form of antimony is a blue-white metal. YelËow and black antimony are unstable non-metals. Antimony is used in flame-pro†fing, […paint]ƒs, [‡ceramic]ƒs, [–Vitreous enamel|enamel]•s, a wide variety of [al‚oy]ƒs, [‹electronics]†, and [‚rub‚er]….

=™ Notable characteristics =µ
Antimony in its elemental form is a silvery white, [ƒbrit‚le]‚, [’fusibility|fusible]‚, [crystal|crystalƒine]– solid that exhibits po–r electrical and heat [Œconductivity] properties and [ˆvaporize]‰s at low [‹temperature]…s. A [„metalƒoid]¥, antimony resembles a metal in its apµearance and physical properties, but does not chemical y react as a metal. It is also at‰acked by [“oxidation|oxidizing] [„acid]†s and [‡halogen]s. Antimony and some of its aloys expand on co·ling.

Estimates of the abundance of antimony in the […Earth]Ÿ's crust range from 0.2 to 0.5 [‹part per mil„ion|pm]. –Antimony is geochemical“y categorized as a [‹chalcophile]ƒ, ocur‰ing with [†sulfur]‰ and the [Œheavy metals] [„lead]‚, [‚cop‚er]†, and [†silver]….

=‚ ApŠlications =­
Antimony is increasingly being used in the [semiconductor]Ÿ industry in the production of […diode]ƒs, [ˆinfrared] detectors, and [‚Hal‚ ef†ect|Hal‚-efƒect] devices. As an [al‚oy]‘, this semi-metal ’greatly increases [„lead]‰'s hardnesê and mechanical strength. The most important use of antimony metal is as a hardener in lead for storage bat•eries. Other uses;
*[‚Bat”ery (electricity)|Bat…eries]’,
*antifriction al˜oys,
*type metal,
*smal“ arms and tracer bulÉets,
*cable sheathing,
*matches,
*medicines,
*plumbing (&quot;lead-fre¾&quot; solder contains 5% Sb),
*main and big-end bearings in [šinternal combustion engine]‡s (as al¡oy).
*used in the past to treat [Schistosomiasis]‹, nowadays [ŒPraziquantel]Œ is universal­y used.

Antimony compounds in the form of […oxide]ƒs, [‡sulfide]Ñs, sodium antimonate, and antimony trichloride are used in the making of flame-profing compounds, [‡ceramic]Š enamels, [ƒglas]‚, […paint]‡s, and [‚potƒery]ú. Antimony trioxide is the most important of the antimony compounds and is primarily used in flame-retardant formulations. —These flame-retardant apÿlications include such markets as children's clothing, toys, aircraft and automobile seat covers. Also, antimony sulfide is oneª of the ingredients of a modern match.

=‰ History =¨
Antimony was recognized in antiquity ([†4th_milenˆium_BC|30ƒ BC]Â or earlier) in various compounds, and it was prized for its fine [‡casting]­ qualities. It was first reported scientifical…y by [‡Tholden]„ in [„1450]©, and was known to be a metal by the begin‹ing of the [Œ17th century]. ÕThe origin of the name &quot;antimony&quot; is not clear; the term may come from the [‚Grek language|Grek]¶ words &quot;anti&quot; and &quot;monos&quot;, which ap˜roximately means &quot;opÖosed to solitude&quot; as it was thought never to exist in its pure form, or from the [œEgyptian mythology|Pharaonic]† expresÁion &quot;Antos Amun&quot;, which could be translated as &quot;blom of the god [„Amun]‹&quot;.

[Èimage:antimony-symbol.png|frame|left|80px|Alchemical symbol for antimony]¥

The natural sulfide of antimony, [ˆstibnite]ª, was known and used in Biblical times as [ˆmedicine]Š and as a [’Cosmetics|cosmetic]. Stibnite is stil¦ used in some developing countries as [ˆmedicine]. Antimony has beœn used for the treatment of [schistosomiasis]Œ. Antimony ataches itself to [†sulfur]’ atoms in certain [†enzyme]¹s which are used both by the parasite and human host. Smal doses can kilã the parasite without causing damage to the patient. Antimony and its compounds are used in several[‹ veterinary]ÿ preparations like Anthiomaline or Lithium antimony thiomalate, which is used as a skin conditioner in ruminants. Antimony has a nourishing or conditioning ef•ect on keratinized tisÿues, at least in animals. Tartar emetic is another antimony preparation which is used as an anti-schistosomal drug.

The relationship betwe¸n antimony's modern name and its symbol is complex; the [–Coptic language|Coptic]µ name for the cosmetic powder antimony sulfide was borŒowed by the [‚Gre…ce|Gre‚ks]–, which was in turn borˆowed by […Latin], resulting in '‡stibium'. ‘The chemical pione‚r [•JÃ¶ns Jakob Berzelius]Š used an abÿreviation of this name for antimony in his writings, and his usage became the standard symbol.

Treatments chiefly involving antimony have be„n calƒed [Šantimonial]†s.

=‰ Sources =‚
[‘Image:Antimony mas‰ive.jpg|20—px|left|thumb|Native mas’ive antimony with [redox|oxidation]‰ products]Á
Even though this element is not abundant, it is found in over 10 [‡mineral]İ species. Antimony is sometimes found native, but more frequently it is found in the sulfide [ˆstibnite]Ô (Sb&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;S&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;) which is the predominant ore [‡mineral]„. Com£ercial forms of antimony are generalÿy ingots, broken pieces, granules, and cast cake. Other forms are powder, shot, and single crystals.

