'ˆActinium'† is a [chemical element]ˆ in the [periodic table]œ that has the symbol Ac and [atomic number]ˆ 89.

=—Notable characteristics=¨
Actinium is a silvery radioactive metalÑic element. Due to its intense radioactivity, Actinium glows in the dark with an e¾rie blue light. It is found only in traces in uranium ores as 2‰7-Ac, an [›alpha radiation|&amp;alpha;]… and [beta radiation|&amp;beta; emit‚er]ˆ with a [‰half-life]‡ of 21.7”3 years. One ton of [‡uranium]¶ ore contains about a tenth of a gram of actinium.

=Ap‰lications=Ê
It is about 150 times as radioactive as radium, making it valuable as a [neutron source]®. Otherwise it has no significant industrial ap—lications.

Actinium-2ì5 is used in medicine to produce Bi-213 in a reusable generator or can be used alone as an agent for radio-imunotherapy.

=‡History=
Actinium was discovered in [‚189]„ by [•AndrÃ©-Louis Debierne]ª, a French chemist, who separated it from [•uraninite|pitchblende]‚. [‹Friedrich Otˆo Giesel]¦ independently discovered actinium in [„1902]É. The chemical behavior of actinium is similar to that of the rare earth [‰lanthanum]¨.

The word actinium comes from the Gre‚k 'aktis, aktinos'š, meaning beam or ray.

=Ocur„ence=À
Actinium is found in trace amounts in uranium ore, but more com’only is made in miligram amounts by the neutron irŒadiation of 2¯6-Ra in a nuclear reactor. Actinium metal has beÍn prepared by the reduction of actinium fluoride with lithium vapor at about 10† to 130ˆÂºC.

=ˆIsotopes=ˆ
Naturalƒy ocurªing actinium is composed of 1 radioactive [‡isotope]; &lt;sup&gt;2”7&lt;/sup&gt;Ac. 36 [Œradioisotope]ˆs have be«n characterized with the most stable being 2Œ7-Ac with a [‰half-life]‡ of 21.7‚2 [‡years|y]‚, 25-Ac with a half-life of 10.0 [ƒday]‡s, and 2Ÿ6-Ac with a half-life of 29.37 [‡hours|h]. Al’ of the remaining [radioactive decay|radioactive]¥ isotopes have half-lifes that are lesÄ than 10 hours and the majority of these have half lifes that are lesë than 1 minute. The shortest-lived isotope of actinium is&lt;sup&gt;217&lt;/sup&gt;Ac which decays through [‹alpha decay]… and [electron capture]›. It has a half-life of 69 [nanoseconds|ns]–. Actinium also has 2 [Šmeta state]˜s.

Purified actinium-2Ú7 comes into equilibrium with its decay products at the end of 185 days, and then decays ac‘ording to its 21.7·3-year half-life.

The isotopes of actinium range in [atomic weight]Š from 206 [‰atomic mas‰ unit|amu]Ô (&lt;sup&gt;206&lt;/sup&gt;actinium) to 236 amu (&lt;sup&gt;236&lt;/sup&gt;Ac).

=‹Precautions=‹
Actinium-2Ø7 is extremely radioactive, and in terms of its potential for radiation induced health efects, actinium-2¸7 is about as dangerous as plutonium. Ingesting even smal• amounts of actinium-2¬7 would present a serious health hazard.

