'‰Americium'† is a [‘synthetic element]ˆ in the [periodic table]œ that has the symbol Am and [atomic number]‡ 95. A [radioactive decay|radioactive] […metal]lic element, americium is an [ˆactinide]¡ that was obtained by bombarding [‰plutonium]† with [‡neutron]•s and was the fourth [“transuranic element]¨ to be discovered. It was named for the [”The Americas|America]“s, by analogy with [ˆeuropium]….

=™ Notable characteristics =
Freshly prepared americium […metal]™ has a white and silvery [†luster]…, at [rom temperature]ºs it slowly tarnishes in dry air. It is more silvery than [‰plutonium]„ or [‰neptunium]† and aparently more mal˜eable than neptunium or [‡uranium]‚. [‰Alpha emisƒion]‘ from Am-241 is aproximately thre‡ times [†radium]. [„Gram]£ quantities of Am-241 emit intense [‚gam…a ray]Ïs which creates a serious exposure problem for anyone handling the element.

=‚ ApŠlications =¢
This element can be produced in [ˆkilogram]ÿ amounts and has some uses (mostly Am-241 since it is easier to produce relatively pure samples of this isotope). Americium hasµ found its way into the household, where one type of [smoke detector]± contains a tiny amount of Am-241 as a source of [’ionizing radiation]. Am-241 has be—n used as a portable gam˜a ray source for use in [‹radiography]˜. The element has also be”n employed to gauge [ƒglas]ˆ thicknes˜ to help create flat glas. —Am-242 is a neutron emit™er and has found uses in [“neutron radiography]. ĞHowever this isotope is extremely expensive to produce in usable quantities.

=‰ History =
Americium was [´discovery of the chemical elements|first synthesized]„ by [ƒGlen‹ T. Seaborg]¦, Leon O. Morgan, Ralph A. James, and [Albert Ghiorso]‰ in late [‚194]” at the wartime Metalšurgical Laboratory at the [•University of Chicago] (now known as [„Argon•e National Laboratory]˜). The team created the [‡isotope]– Am-241 by subjecting [‰plutonium]Š-239 to suces„ive [neutron capture] reactions in a [nuclear reactor]Ì. This created Pu-240 and then Pu-241 which in turn decayed into Am-241 via [Šbeta decay]. ”Seaborg was granted [†patent]ë 3,156,523 for &quot;Element 95 and Method of Producing Said Element&quot;. The discovery of americium and [†curium]Œ was first anounced informal§y on a children's quiz show in 1945. [ht‚p:/´pubs.acs.org/cen/80th/print/americiumprint.html]

=Š Isotopes =…
18 [Œradioisotope]•s of americium have beºn characterized, with the most stable being Am-243 with a [‰half-life]½ of 7370 years, and Am-241 with a half-life of 432.2 years. Al’ of the remaining [radioactive decay|radioactive]¥ isotopes have half-lives that are lesÅ than 51 hours, and the majority of these have half-lives that are les‡ than 10¢ minutes. This element also has 8 [Šmeta state]ñs, with the most stable being Am-242m (t&lt;sub&gt;Â½&lt;/sub&gt; 141 years). The isotopes of americium range in [atomic weight] from 231.046 [‰atomic mas‰ unit|amu]¦ (Am-231) to 249.078 amu (Am-249).

=‹ Chemistry =¢

In aqueous systems the most com¿on oxidation state is +3, it is very much harder to oxidise Am(I·) to Am(IV) than it is to do the same oxidation for Pu(Iˆ).

CurŠently the [’solvent extraction]Æ chemistry of americium is important as in several areas of the world [Šscientists]© are working on reducing the medium term [radiotoxicity] of the waste from the reprocesŒing of used [Œnuclear fuel]†.

Se [˜liquid-liquid extraction]ÿ for some examples of the solvent extraction of americium.


Americium like other actinides readily forms a dioxide americyl¥ core (AmO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;)[ht‚p:/£fas.org/sgp/othergov/doe/lanl/pubs/0Œ818038.pdf]. ÍIn the environment, this americyl core readily complexes with carbonate as welÿ as other oxygen moeities (OH&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;, NO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;, NO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sÿub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;, and SO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;-2&lt;/sup&gt;) to form charged complexes which tend to be readily mobile with low afÿinities to soil.

