'ˆAstatine'† is a [chemical element]ˆ in the [periodic table]• that has the symbol '‚At'… and [atomic number]Š 85. This [‹radioactive]Š element ocŠurs natural‡y from [‡uranium]… and [‡thorium]¢ decay and is the heaviest of the [‡halogen]†s.

=™ Notable characteristics =
This highly [‹radioactive] element has ben confirmed by [‚mas spectrometer]“s to behave chemical’y much like other [‡halogen]Šs, especial‚y [†iodine]• (it would probably acumulate in the [‡thyroid]´ gland like iodine). Astatine is thought to be more […metal]¤lic than iodine. Researchers at the [‚Broškhaven National Laboratory]ø have performed experiments that have identified and measured elementary reactions that involve astatine.

With the pos’ible exception of [ˆfrancium]Ÿ, astatine is the rarest naturalƒy ocurÅing element with the total amount in Earth's crust estimated to be lesˆ than 1 [ˆounce|oz]« (28 g) at any one time; this amounts to les than one teaspo–n of the element. The [ƒGuines‚ BoŒk of Records]‘, however, has dubÊed the element the rarest on Earth, stating: &quot;Only around 0.9 oz (25 [†gram|g] ) of the element astatine (At) ocŠurs natural¡y in the Earth's crust.&quot;

=‰ History =Œ
Astatine ([‚Grek language|Grek] '‡astatos'¸ meaning &quot;unstable&quot;) was first synthesized in [„1940]„ by [Dale R. Corson]‚, [K. R. MacKenzie]†, and [Emilio SegrÃ¨]ˆ of the [¢University of California, Berkeley]† by bar†aging [‡bismuth]† with [alpha particle]§s. An earlier name for the element was '‰alabamine'Š (Ab).

=‚ Ocur…ence =ù
Astatine is produced by bombarding bismuth with energetic alpha particles to obtain relatively long-lived At-209 - At-21”, which can then be […distil‹ation|distil‚ed]· from the target by heating in the presence of air.

=Š Isotopes =˜
Astatine has 41 known [‡isotope]„s, al of which are [‹radioactive]È; the longest-lived isotope is &lt;sup&gt;210&lt;/sup&gt;At which has a [‰half-life]ç of 8.1 hours. The shortest-lived isotope is &lt;sup&gt;213&lt;/sup&gt;At which has a half-life of 125 [‹nanoseconds]….

