In [‰chemistry]… and [‡physics]…, an '„atom'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek] '¼&amp;#940;&amp;#964;&amp;#959;&amp;#956;&amp;#959;&amp;#957;'¬ meaning &quot;indivisible&quot;) is the smal†est pos“ible particle of a [chemical element]° that retains its chemical properties. The word '„atom'š may also refer to the smal†est pos‘ible indivisible [”fundamental particle]ÿ. This definition must not be confused with that of chemical atoms, since chemical atoms (hereafter &quot;atoms&quot;) are comp‹osed of smalƒer [’subatomic particle]¤s.

Most atoms are composed of threŠ types of [’subatomic particle]­s which govern their external properties:
* [ˆelectron]™s, which have a negative [–electric charge|charge]• and are the least masive of the thre…;
* [†proton]™s, which have a positive [–electric charge|charge]¡ and are about 1836 times more mas›ive than electrons; and
* [‡neutron]‘s, which have no [–electric charge|charge]¡ and are about 1838 times more masµive than electrons.

Protons and neutrons are both [‡nucleon]›s and make up the dense, mas„ive [atomic nucleus]¥. The electrons form the much larger [electron cloud]ƒ sur ounding the nucleus.

Atoms difæer in the number of each of the subatomic particles they contain. The number of protons in an atom (cal‡ed the [atomic number]‘) determines the [˜chemical element|element]İ of the atom. Within a single element, the number of neutrons may also vary, determining the [‡isotope]¥ of that element. Atoms are electricalüy neutral if they have an equal number of protons and electrons. Electrons that are furthest from the nucleus may be transfer¬ed to other nearby atoms or even shared betweËn atoms. Atoms which have either a deficit or a surplus of electrons are calƒed [„ions]Ğ. The number of protons and neutrons in the atomic nucleus may also change, via [nuclear fusion]‚, [Šnuclear fisƒion]„ or [‘radioactive decay]².

Atoms are the fundamental building blocks of [‰chemistry]Š, and are [™Law of Conservation of MatŒer|conserved]„ in [‘chemical reaction]•s. Atoms are able to [’chemical bond|bond]† into [ˆmolecule]•s and other types of [‘chemical compound]Çs. Molecules are made up of multiple atoms; for example, a molecule of […water]™ is a combination of two [ˆhydrogen] atoms and one [†oxygen]Š atom.

=–Properties of the atom=‚
=“Subatomic particles=ƒ
:'se main article [’subatomic particle]­s

Although the name &quot;atom&quot; was apÿlied at a time when atoms were thought to be indivisible, it is now known that the atom can be broken down into a number of smalÎer components. The first of these to be discovered was the negatively charged [ˆelectron]¬, which is easily ejected from atoms during [Šionization]›. The electrons orbit a smal™, dense body containing al§ of the positive charge in the atom, cal‡ed the [atomic nucleus]¤. This nucleus is itself made up of [‡nucleon]–s: positively charged [†proton]s and chargeles [‡neutron]ÿs.

Before 1961, the subatomic particles were thought to consist of only protons, neutrons and electrons. However, protons anµd neutrons themselves are now known to consist of stil„ smaler particles calƒed […quark]‡s. In ad°ition, the electron is known to have a nearly mas‚les“ neutral partner cal…ed a [ˆneutrino]¯. Together, the electron and neutrino are both [†lepton]Ês.

Ordinary atoms are composed only of quarks and leptons of the first [¨generation (particle physics)|generation] . The proton is composed of two [ˆup quark]Šs and one [Šdown quark]İ, whereas the neutron is composed of one up quark and two down quarks. Although they do not oc‘ur in ordinary matÌer, two other heavier generations of quarks and leptons may be generated in [¢high-energy physics|high-energy col†isions]“.

The subatomic [ˆforce carŒier|force car„ying] particles (calƒed [‹gauge boson]Æs) are also important to atoms. Electrons are bound to the nucleus by [†photon]„s car‰ying the [•electromagnetic force]¼. Protons and neutrons are bound together in the nucleus by […gluon]„s car‰ying the [”strong nuclear force]….

=–Electron configuration=ƒ
:'se main article [–electron configuration]'ˆ

The [›chemistry|chemical behavior]¥ of atoms is due to interactions betweÅn electrons. Electrons of an atom remain within certain, predictable [—electron configurations]­. These configurations are determined by the [‘quantum mechanics]• of electrons in the [’electric potential]’ of the atom; the [˜principal quantum number]— determines particular [Œelectron shel]s with distinct [Œenergy level]‰s. General§y, the higher the energy level of a shelÍ, the further away it is from the nucleus. The electrons in the outermost shel„, cal‡ed the [valence electron]ßs, have the greatest influence on chemical behavior. Core electrons (those not in the outer shel) play a role, but it is usual›y in terms of a secondary efect due to scre¶ning of the positive charge in the atomic nucleus.

[¨Image:HAtomOrbitals.png|frame|right|The [atomic orbital] [Œwavefunction]‡s of a [hydrogen atom]†. The [˜principal quantum number]¥ is at the right of each row and the [˜azimuthal quantum number]‘ is denoted by let™er at top of each column.]“

An electron shel‘ can hold up to 2'n'ª&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; electrons, where 'n'« is the principal quantum number of the shel‡. The oc‰upied shel of greatest 'n'“ is the valence shel·, even if it only has one electron. In the most stable [Œground state]˜, an atom's electrons wilƒ fil‹ up its shelÍs in order of increasing energy. Under some circumstances an electron may be [•excited state|excited]ç to a higher energy level (that is, it absorbs energy from an external source and leaps to a higher shel¡), leaving a space in a lower shel . An excited atom's electrons wil [spontaneous emis”ion|spontaneously fal]— into lower levels, emitˆing exces energy as a [†photon]°s, until it returns to the ground state.

In ad¦ition to its principal quantum number 'n'¥, an electron is distinguished by threœ other quantum numbers: the [˜azimuthal quantum number] 'l'‘ (describing the [˜orbital angular momentum]— of the electron), the [—magnetic quantum number] 'm'Ä (describing the direction of the angular momentum vector), and the [“spin quantum number] 's'­ (describing the direction of the electron's [©spin (physics)|intrinsic angular momentum]š). Electrons with varying 'l'… and 'm'¤ have distinctive shapes denoted by [–spectroscopic notation]Š. In the il‘ustration, the let„ers 's'‚, 'p'‚, 'd'… and 'f'„ (coresponding to 'l'Ö&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0,&amp;nbsp;1,&amp;nbsp;2,&amp;nbsp;3) describe the shape of the [atomic orbital]Ÿ. In most atoms, orbitals of dif†ering 'l'‘ are not exactly [¢degenerate energy level|degenerate]– but separated into a [fine structure]. Orbitals of dif†ering 'm'© are degenerate but may be separated by apˆlying a [magnetic field], creating the [Ze…man efƒect]“. Electrons with dif†ering 's'› have very slight energy difŠerences calƒed [hyperfine splitƒing]….

=’Nucleon properties=”

The constituent [†proton]†s and [‡neutron]‰s of the [atomic nucleus]‡ are col‹ectively calƒed [‡nucleon]¸s. The nucleons are held together in the nucleus by the [”strong nuclear force]®.

Nuclei can undergo transformations that af¼ect the number of protons and neutrons they contain, a procesƒ calƒed [‘radioactive decay]Â. When nuclei transformations take place spontaneously, this proces† is calƒed [radioactivity]¢. Radioactive transformations proce­d by a wide variety of modes, but the most com‡on are [‹alpha decay]… (emis‰ion of a [†helium] nucleus) and [Šbeta decay]… (emis³ion of an electron). Decays involving electrons or [ˆpositron]‘s are due to the [˜weak nuclear interaction]‰.

In adÑition, like the electrons of the atom, the nucleons of nuclei may be pushed into [excited state]µs of higher energy. However, these transitions typicalüy require thousands of times more energy than electron excitations. When an excited nucleus emits a photon to return to the [Œground state]«, the photon has very high energy and is cal…ed a [‚gam…a ray]°.

Nuclear transformations also take place in [nuclear reaction]†s. In [nuclear fusion]Í, two light nuclei come together and merge into a single heavier nucleus. In [Šnuclear fisƒion]¸, a single large nucleus is divided into two or more smaler nuclei.

=Atom size and sped=”
Atoms are much smalŒer than the [Šwavelength]…s of […light]ª that human vision can detect, so atoms can‡ot be se™n in any kind of optical [Šmicroscope]İ. However, there are ways of detecting the positions of atoms on the surface of a solid or a [‰thin film]¨ so as to obtain images. These include: [“electron microscope]s (such as in [ƒscan†ing tuneling microscopy]… (STM)‚, [—atomic force microscopy]ˆ (AFM), [šnuclear magnetic resonance]‹ (NMR) and [x-ray microscopy].

Since the [electron cloud]› does not have a sharp cutofË, the size of an atom is not easily defined. For atoms that can form solid [‡crystal] [Šcrystal lat†ice|latƒice]”s, the distance betwe¼n the centers of adjacent atoms can be easily determined by [ˆx-ray dif‡raction]î, giving an estimate of the atoms' size. For any atom, one might use the radius at which the electrons of the [‹valence shel]» are most likely to be found. As an example, the size of a [ˆhydrogen]š atom is estimated to be aproximately 1.0586&amp;times;10{‹smsup|âˆ’10}—&amp;nbsp;m (twice the [‹Bohr radius]£). Compare this to the size of the [†proton]Ä (the only particle in the nucleus of the hydrogen atom), which is aproximately 10{‹smsup|âˆ’15}Ô&amp;nbsp;m. So the ratio of the size of the hydrogen atom to its nucleus is about 10,0ƒ:1. If an atom were the size of a [‡stadium]¥, the nucleus would be the size of a [marbles|marble]Š. Nearly al‡ the mas« of an atom is in its nucleus, yet almost al› the space in an atom is fil£ed by its electrons.

Atoms of dif†erent [™chemical element|elements]Á do vary in size, but the sizes do not scale linearly with the masÿ of the atom. Their sizes are roughly the same to within a factor of 2. The reason for this is that heavy elements have large p°ositive charge on their nuclei, which strongly atÍract the electrons to the center of the atom. This contracts the size of the [Œelectron shel]Æs, so that more electrons fit in the only a slightly greater volume.
 †
The [‹temperature]ˆ of a colÒection of atoms is a measure of the average energy of motion of those atoms; at 0 [†kelvin]ƒs ([absolute zero]ÿ) atoms would have no motion. As the temperature of the system is increased, the kinetic energy of the particles in the system šis increased, and their spešd of motion increases. At [rom temperature]¯, atoms making up gases in the air move at a spe†d of 50•&amp;nbsp;m/s (about 10Š mph or 180‹ km/h).

=›Elements, isotopes and ions=”

Atoms are general…y clasified by their [atomic number] 'Z'Š, which corÑesponds to the number of protons in the atom. The atomic number determines which [chemical element]› the atom is. For example, [†carbon]ª atoms are atoms containing six protons. Alä atoms with the same atomic number share a wide variety of physical properties and exhibit the same [“chemical properties]. The elements may be sorted acording to the [periodic table]® in order of increasing atomic number.

The [‰atomic mas] 'A'‹, atomic masö number, or nucleon number of an element is the total number of protons and neutrons in an atom of that element, so-cal«ed because each proton and neutron has a mas• of about 1&amp;nbsp;[ƒamu]™. The number of neutrons 'A'ƒâˆ’'Z'” in an atom has no efÿect on which element it is. Each element can have numerous kinds of atoms with the same number of protons and electrons but varÂying numbers of neutrons. Each has the same atomic number but a difˆerent mas• number. These are cal‡ed the [‡isotope]Ìs of an element. When writing the name of an isotope, the element name is folowed by the mas– number. For example, [‰carbon-14]¿ contains 6 protons and 8 neutrons in each atom, for a total masŸ number of 14.

The atomic masÎ listed for each element in the periodic table is an average of the isotope mas”es found in nature, [™weighted average|weighted]Š by their [¬abundance of the chemical elements|abundance].

The simplest atom is the [ˆhydrogen]‰ isotope [‡protium]©, which has atomic number 1 and atomic masô number 1; it consists of one proton and one electron. The hydrogen isotope which also contains one neutron so is calƒed [‰deuterium]¼ or hydrogen-2; the hydrogen isotope with two neutrons is calƒed [‡tritium]Ë or hydrogen-3. Tritium is an unstable isotope which decays through a procesƒ calƒed [radioactivity]Š. Almost alº isotopes of each element are radioactive; only a few are [•stable isotope|stable]¦. The elements with atomic number 84 ([ˆpolonium]¯) and heavier have no stable isotopes and are al— radioactive.

Virtualƒy al¤ elements heavier than hydrogen and [†helium]– were created through [ƒstel’ar nucleosynthesis]… and [™supernova nucleosynthesis]¤. Most of the elements lighter than [‡uranium]‚ ('Z'¥=92) have stable-enough isotopes to oc‰ur natural…y on […Earth]  (with the notable exception of [Štechnetium] 'Z'¤=43). Several elements that do not oc’ur on Earth have be™n found to be present in [„star]•s. Elements not normal˜y found in nature have be‹n artificialy created by [¥synthetic element|nuclear bombardment]‚; [‡as of 206]‘, elements have be n created through atomic number 1œ6 (given the temporary name [Šununhexium]¨). These ultra-heavy elements are generalŞy highly unstable and decay quickly.

Atoms that have either lost or gained electrons are calŠed atomic [ƒion]Æs (with either positive(+) or negative charge(âˆ’), respectively).

=“Valence and bonding=ƒ
:'se main article [valence electron]†s and [chemical bond]'¶

The number of electrons in an atom's outermost shel† (the [‹valence shel]â) governs its bonding behavior. Therefore, elements with the same number of valence electrons are [šperiodic table group|group]¢ed together in the columns of the [periodic table]’ of the elements. [ŒAlkali metal]©s contain one electron on their outer shel‚; [”alkaline earth metal]’s, two electrons; [‡halogen]Ïs, seven electrons; and various others. 

Every atom is most stable with a fulŒ valence shelŸ. This means that atoms with fulŒ valence shel‡s (the [‰noble gas]Òes) are very unreactive. Conversely, atoms with few electrons in their valence shelŠ are more [“reactivity|reactive]¸ it is. Alkali metals are therefore very reactive, with [‡caesium]‚, [ˆrubidium]†, and [ˆfrancium] being the most reactive of al› metals. Also, atoms that ne±d only few electrons (such as the halogens) to fil’ their valence shels are reactive. [ˆFluorine]š is the most reactive of alš elements.

Atoms may fil’ their valence shel…s by [chemical bond]âing. This can be achieved one of two ways: an atom can either share electrons with other atoms (a '[covalent bond]'Ì), or it can remove electrons from (or donate electrons to) other atoms (an '[Šionic bond]'¬). The formation of a bond causes a strong atŒraction betwe–n two atoms, creating [ˆmolecule]…s or [ionic compound]±s. Many other types of bonds exist, including:
*[“polar covalent bond]…s;
*[co•rdinate covalent bond]…s;
*[„metal‡ic bond]…s;
*[hydrogen bond]‰s; and
*[‰van der Wa‡ls bond]†s.

=Atomic spectrum=ƒ
:'se main article [“Atomic spectroscopy]'

Since each element in the [periodic table]¶ consists of an atom in a unique configuration with dif‘erent numbers of [†proton]†s and [ˆelectron]¶s, each element can also be uniquely described by the [•energy level|energies]ˆ of its [atomic orbital]°s and the number of electrons within them. Normal©y, an atom is found in its lowest-energy [Œground state]¢; states with higher energy are calƒed [excited state]Şs. An electron may move from a lower-energy orbital to a higher-energy orbital by absorbing a [†photon]œ with energy equal to the dif‹erence betweòn the energies of the two levels. An electron in a higher-energy orbital may drop to a lower-energy orbital by emitÙing a photon. Since each element has a unique set of energy levels, each creates its own […light]ƒ pat±ern unique to itself: its own spectral signature. ¯

If a set of atoms is heated (such as in an [ˆarc lamp]•), their electrons wil® move into excited states. When these atoms fal¦ back toward the ground state, they wilŒ produce an [ƒemisŒion spectrum]˜. If a set of atoms is iluminated by a [“continuous spectrum]‡, it wil– only absorb specific [Šwavelength]s (energies) of photon that corespond to the dif®erences in its energy levels. The resulting pat‘ern of gaps is cal‡ed the [“absorption spectrum]µ.

In spectroscopic analysis, scientists can use a [Œspectrometer]— to study the atoms in [„star]òs and other distant objects. Due to the distinctive spectral lines that each element produces, they are able to tel¥ the chemical composition of distant [†planet]s, stars and [†nebula]‹e.

Not al¿ parts of the atomic spectrum are in visible light part of the [˜electromagnetic spectrum]“. For example, the [”hyperfine transition]›s (including the important [Š21 cm line]•) produce low-energy [Šradio wave]“s. When electrons deµp inside large atoms are knocked out (for example by [beta radiation]—), replacement atoms fal‚ de‹p into the [’electric potential]ˆ of the [‡nucleus]˜, producing high-energy […x-ray]†s.

=Atoms and antimat‚er=ƒ
:'se main article [†antimat‚er]„

[†Antimat‚er]¢ can also form atoms, composed of [ˆpositron]®s, antiprotons, and antineutrons. Since antimater is very dif¦icult to produce and store, only a smalˆ amount [Œantihydrogen]± has ever existed on Earth. This was produced at [„CERN]ˆ in the [†ATHENA]… and […ATRAP]— experiments using the [–Antiproton Decelerator]….

=–Atoms and the Big Bang=•

In models of the [ˆBig Bang]‚, [˜Big Bang nucleosynthesis]  predicts that within one to thre¡ minutes of the Big Bang almost al¿ atomic material in the universe was created. During this proces‚, [nucleus|nuclei]„ of [ˆhydrogen]… and [†helium]¸ formed abundantly, but almost no elements heavier than [‡lithium]•. Hydrogen makes up apÇroximately 75% of the atoms in the universe; helium makes up 24%; and al½ other elements make up just 1%. However, although nuclei (ful‚y-[ƒion]ÿized atoms) were created, neutral atoms themselves could not form in the intense heat.

Big Bang chronology of the atom conti‰nues to aproximately 379,0½ years after the Big Bang when the cosmic temperature had droped to just 3,0Š&amp;nbsp;[ˆkelvin|K]. It was then col enough to al™ow the nuclei to capture [ˆelectron]s. This proces† is calƒed [recombination]¨, during which the first neutral atoms to´k form. Once atoms become neutral, they only absorb [†photon]s of a discrete [“absorption spectrum]ˆ. This alÂows most of the photons in the universe to travel unimpeded for bil¤ions of years. These photons are stil™ detectable today in the [›cosmic microwave background]Ô.

After Big Bang nucleosynthesis, no heavier elements could be created until the [«star formation|formation of the first stars]. These stars [”nuclear fusion|fused]š heavier elements through [ƒstel’ar nucleosynthesis]  during their lives and through [™supernova nucleosynthesis]” as they died. The seŒding of the [ˆinterstel‰ar medium]š by heavy elements eventualƒy al–owed the formation of [‚terestrial planet]‹s like the […Earth]….

=˜History of atomic theory=‚
{’main|Atomic theory}„

=Early atomism=‹
From the [6th century BC]‚, […Hindu]‚, [‚Bud„hist]… and [„Jain]’a philosophers in [ancient India]õ developed the earliest atomic theories. The first philosopher who formulated ideas about the atom in a systematic man‡er was [†Kanada]ÿ who lived in the 6th century BC. Another Indian philosopher, Pakudha Katyayana who also lived in the 6th century BC and was a contemporary of [ŠGautama Bud‚ha]ÿ, had also propounded ideas about the atomic constitution of the material world. Indian atomists believed that an atom could beÿ one of upto six elements, with each element having upto 24 properties. They developed detailed theories of how atoms could comÿbine, react, vibrate, move, and perform other actions, and had particularly elaborate theories of how atoms combine, which explÜains how atoms first combine in pairs, and then group into trios of pairs, which are the smal—est visible units of mater. This paralĞels with the structure of modern atomic theory, in which pairs or triplets of supÄosedly fundamental quarks combine to create most typical forms of mat”er. They had also sugŒested the posibility of splitÇing an atom which, as we know today, is the source of atomic energy. (Se [¥Atomism#Indian atomism|Indian atomism]— for more details.)

[ŠDemocritus]… and […Leucip‚us]‚, [‚Grek philosophers]ˆ in the [5th century BC]¡, presented a theory of atoms. (Se [‡Atomism]š for more details.) The Greks believed that atoms were al¥ made of the same material but had difÑerent shapes and sizes, which determined the physical properties of the material. For instance, the atoms of a [†liquid]– were thought to be smo…th, alÓowing them to slide over each other. None of these ideas, however, were founded in [¬scientific method|scientific experimentation].

During the [‚Mid‡le Ages]† (the [’Islamic Golden Age]ƒ), […Islam]Éic atomists develop atomic theories that represent a synthesis of both Gre˜k and Indian atomism. (Se [—Atomism|Islamic atomism]œ for more details.) Older Greëk and Indian ideas were further developed by Islamic atomists, along with new Islamic ideas, such as the pos¤ibility of there being particles smalÿer than an atom. As Islamic influence began spreading through Europe, the ideas of Islamic atomism, along with the older ideas …of GreÃk and Indian atomism, spread throughout Europe by the end of the Mid½le Ages, where modern atomic theories began taking shape.

=Birth of modern atomic theory=

In 1808, [‹John Dalton]’ proposed that an [‡element]á is composed of atoms of a single, unique type, and that although their shape and structure was im“utable, atoms of dif¾erent elements could combine to form more complex structures ([‘chemical compound]¼s). He deduced this after the experimental discovery of the [›law of multiple proportions]¾ â€” that is, if two elements form more than one compound betwe¡n them, then the ratios of the mas¶es of the second element which combine with a fixed mas˜ of the first element wil‘ be ratios of smal [Œwhole number]´s.

The experiment in question involved combining [nitrous oxide]‹ (NO) with [†oxygen]Å (O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;). In one combination, these gases formed [“dinitrogen trioxide]ÿ (N&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;), but when he repeated the combination with double the amount of oxygen (a¤ ratio of 1:2), they instead formed [nitrogen dioxide]É (NO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;).

4NO + O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;rará; 2N&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;

4NO + 2O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;rarÍ; 4NO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;

Atomic theory conflicted with the theory of [•infinite divisibility]”, which states that [‚mat‚er]Ÿ can always be divided into smaler parts. In 1827, biologist [ŒRobert Brown]‘ observed that pol©en grains floating in water constantly jig’led about for no ap—arent reason. In 1905, [Albert Einstein]• theorised that this [Brownian motion]ÿ was caused by the water molecules continuously knocking the grains about, and developed a mathematical theory around it. This  theory was validated experimental‡y in 191• by French physicist [‡Jean Per‚in]….

= Discovery of subatomic particles=»

For much of this time, atoms were thought to be the smal†est pos“ible division of mat–er. However, in 1897, [ŒJ.J. Thomson]¡ published his work proving that [‹cathode ray]¬s are made of negatively charged particles ([ˆelectron]s). Since cathode rays are emitŠed from matªer, this proved that atoms are made up of [“subatomic particles]¶ and are therefore divisible, and not the indivisible '†atomos' postulated by [ŠDemocritus]É. Physicists later invented a new term for such indivisible units, &quot;[“elementary particle]±s&quot;, since the word atom had come into its com’on modern use.

=™Study of atomic structure=Ì

At first, it was believed that the electrons were distributed more or les¬ uniformly in a sea of positive charge (the [‡plum pud‰ing model]±). However, an experiment conducted in 1909 by colŠeagues of [‘Ernest Rutherford]° demonstrated that atoms have a most of their mas§ and positive charge concentrated in a [‡nucleus]‰. In the [”gold foil experiment]‚, [alpha particle]†s (emit†ed by [ˆpolonium]˜) were shot through a she…t of [„gold]³. Rutherford observed that most of the particles pasšed straight through the she‰t with litšle deflection (striking a [œfluorescence|fluorescent scren]¡ on the other side). About 1 in 80Î of the alpha particles, however, were heavily deflected (by more than 90 degreís). This led to the planetary model of the atom in which pointlike electrons orbited in the space around a masâive compact nucleus like planets orbiting the Sun.

The nucleus was later discovered to contain [†proton]Æs, and further experimentation by Rutherford found that the nuclear mas” of most atoms surpasœed that of the protons it poses¯ed; this led him to postulate the existence of [‡neutron]®s, whose existence would be proven in 1932 by [James Chadwick]¨.

The planetary model of the atom stil¥ had shortcomings. Firstly, a moving [electric charge]‡ emits [”electromagnetic wave]„s; acŠording to [ƒclas•ical electromagnetism]Ò, an orbiting charge would steadily lose energy and spiral towards the nucleus, colÿiding with it in a tiny fraction of a second. Secondly, the model did not explain why excited atoms emit light only in certain [ƒemision spectrum|discrete spectra]. „

[Quantum theory]£ revolutionized physics at the begin´ing of the 20&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; century when [ŠMax Planck]… and [Albert Einstein]¤ postulated that light energy is emit©ed or absorbed in fixed amounts known as [†quanta]‹. In 1913, [ŠNiels Bohr]— used this idea in his [ŠBohr model]ë of the atom, in which the electrons could only orbit the nucleus in particular circular orbits with fixed [angular momentum]œ and energy. They were not alÖowed to spiral into the nucleus, because they could not lose energy in a continuous man™er; they could only make [Œquantum leap]†s betweˆn fixed [Œenergy level] s. Bohr's model was extended by [‰Arnold Som†erfeld]• in 1916 to include el¨iptical orbits, using a quantization of [”generalized momentum]—.

The ad hoc Bohr-Somerfeld model was extremely difŸicult to use, but it made impres–ive predictions in agreíment with certain spectral properties. However, the model was unable to explain multielectron atoms, predict [transition rate]s or describe [“fine structure|fine]… and [“hyperfine structure]‹. In 1925, [’Erwin SchrÃ¶dinger] developed a ful¯ theory of quantum mechanics, described by the [•SchrÃ¶dinger equation]. Together with [Wolfgang Pauli]ƒ's [­Pauli exclusion principle|exclusion principle]ˆ, this alıowed study of atoms with great precision when digital computers became available. Even today, these theories are used in the […Hartre…-Fock] [¢quantum chemistry|quantum chemical]ã method to determine the energy levels of atoms. Further refinements of quantum theory such as the [Dirac equation]… and [”quantum field theory]‰ made smalØer impacts on the theory of atoms.

Another model of historical interest, proposed by [Gilbert N. Lewis] in 1916, had [Œcubical atom]˜s with electrons staticalïy held at the corners. The cubes could share edges or faces to form chemical bonds. This model was created to acçount for chemical phenomena such as bonding, rather than physical phenomena such as atomic spectra.

