 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[image:040719_172_dorset_marnhul‹.jpg|thumb|2¤0px|Modern arable agriculture typicaly uses large [šfield (agriculture)|fields]’ like this one in [†Dorset]‚, [‡England].]…
In [‰geography]‚, '‹arable land'‡ (from […Latin] '…arare'…, to [†plough] ) is a form of [˜agriculture|agricultural] [ˆland use]Š, meaning [•land (economics)|land]§ that can be (and is) used for growing [‘agriculture|crops]. [David Ricardo]« incorporated the idea of arable land into [ˆeconomic] [†theory]™.

Of the earth's 57 mil–ion square miles (148,0,0°&amp;nbsp;km&amp;sup2;) of land, more than 12 mil•ion square miles (31,0,0ÿ&amp;nbsp;km&amp;sup2;) are arable. 

Most of the arable land on earth is around the largest rivers on earth. Some examples aˆre: the [„Nile]Œ River, the [†Tigris]… and [‰Euphrates] Rivers, the [‚Yelˆow River]†, the [ŒAmazon River]†, the [†Ganges]‰ and the […Rhine]— River. These rivers flo”d regularly, overspilœing their banks. When the flo²d is over, the rivers recede, leaving behind rich [„silt]“. This silt is excel“ent fertilizer for […crops]§. Even if the land is overfarmed, and al… the [ˆnutrient]Ìs are depleted from the soil, the land renews its fertility when the next flo½d comes. Rivers and streams can make desert land arable. 

=Unarable land=¬

On unarable land, farming is nearly impos‰ible unlesš more advanced methods of [‹agriculture] are used. Unarable land usual®y has no source of fresh water, and is often to hot (desert), to‘ cold (arctic), to‰ rocky, to mountainous, to‰ salty, to‰ rainy, toŒ snowy, or to« cloudy. Clouds block the sunlight plants ne†d for [photosynthesis]˜ (making sunlight into fo¥d). The plants starve without light. [ŠStarvation]… and […nomad]¿ism often exist on unarable land. Unarable land is sometimes cal¡ed 'wastes', 'badlands', 'worthlesî' or 'no man's land'.

Sometimes, unarable land can be turned into arable land. New arable land makes more fo“d, and can prevent [Šstarvation]±, saving lives. This also makes the country more [‡self-suf†icient] and political¸y independent, because the country doesn't have to buy foÅd from other countries. Making unarable land arable often involves dig‰ing new ir™igation canals and new wels, aquaducts, [Œdesalination]” plants, planting tre›s for shade in the desert, [Šhydroponic]¤s, fertilizer, nitrogen fertilizer, [‰pesticide]ƒs, [reverse osmosis]Œ water proces…ors, […mylar]¹ insulation or other insulation against heat and cold, dig’ing ditches and hil©s for protection against the wind, and greÿnhouses with internal light and heat for protection against the cold outside and to provide light in cloudy areas.

Some exam’ples of infertile 'ˆunarable'  land being turned into fertile '†arable'© land are: 
* Aran Island: This island of¹ the west coast of Ireland, (not to be confused with the [‰Isle of Ar‚an]„ in [ˆScotland]ƒ's [Firth of Clyde] ), was unarable because it was to° rocky. The people covered the island with a shalow layer of seaweÑd and sand from the ocean. This made it arable. Today, they grow crops there.
* [†Israel]ª: Israel was mostly unarable desert until [Œdesalination]ÿ plants were built on the coast. The plants turn salt water into fresh water for farming, drinking, and washing. They created t¿heir own large fresh water source.

Some examples of fertile '†arable'¢ land being turned into infertile 'ˆunarable'¢ land are: 
* Droughts like the '[‰dust bowl]‰' of the [‹Great Depresƒion]® in the U.S. turned farmland into desert. 
* [ŠRainforest]Ñ Deforestation: The fertile tropical forests turn into infertile desert land.
* [”Roman Republic|Roman]’s' destruction of [ˆCarthage]”: At the end of the [‰Punic War]ÿs, the victorious Romans sowed the earth with salt, to symbolize total victory. The Roman symbol meant that Carthage would neveºr grow back - their civilization ended. Crops won't generalÿy grow in salty soil. This is why salt water from the ocean can't be used to water crops. 
* Each year more arable land is los™t to desertification and [‡erosion]¤ from human industrial activities. Ir¨igation of farm land also increases the [†sodium]‚, [‡calcium]†, and [‰magnesium]˜ in the soil. This procesì steadily concentrates salt in the ground, decreasing productivity for crops that are not salt-tolerant.
* [ŒUrban sprawl]¤: In the United States, about 2.2 milion acres (8,90” kmÂ²) of land was ad–ed to urban areas betweƒn 19‡2 and 20¦2, much of it farm land now paved.

