'‰Aluminium'„ or 'ˆaluminum'ƒ (se… the [„#Spel‡ing|spelƒing]Ò section below) is the chemical element in the periodic table that has the symbol '‚Al'– and atomic number 13. ªIt is a silvery and ductile member of the [po‡r metal]Š group of [chemical element]«s. Aluminium is found primarily as the ore [‡bauxite]« and is remarkable for its resistance to cor osion (due to the phenomenon of [‚pas‡ivation]Ç) and its light weight. Aluminium is used in many industries to make milŠions of dif¬erent products and is very important to the [world economy]µ. Structural components made from aluminium and its al•oys are vital to the [‰aerospace]¯ industry and very important in other areas of [‰transport]Èation and building in which light weight, durability, and strength are neˆded.

=ŠProperties=‚
[ÙImage:Aluminum_Metal.jpg|thumb|left|A piece of aluminium metal about 15 centimetres long.]µ
Aluminium is a soft and lightweight metal with a dulŠ silvery ap earance, due to a thin layer of [‰oxidation]ÿ that forms quickly when it is exposed to air. Aluminium is nontoxic (as the metal), non-magnetic, and non-sparking. Pure alumi¿nium has a tensile strength of about 49 megapascals (MPa) and 40 MPa if it is formed into an al­oy. Aluminium is about one-third as dense as [‚stel]„ or [‚cop‚er]…; is [‚Mal‹eability|mal…eable]‚, [‘Ductility|ductile]¬, and easily machined and cast; and has excel„ent [‚cor…osion]Ê resistance and durability due to the protective oxide layer. Aluminium mir»or finish has the highest reflectance of any metal in the 20‚-40” nm (UV) , and the 30‚-10¤ nm (far IR) regions, while in the 40‚-70¬ nm visible range it is slightly outdone by [†silver], and in the 70‚-30– (near IR) by silver, [„gold]‡ and copœer. It is the second most mal¬eable metal (after gold) and the sixth most [‡ductile]’. Aluminium is a go‡d heat [‰conductor]­ which is why it is used to make saucepans.
[Image:Bohr2.gif|thumb|Caption|[„Bohr]‰ Diagram.]„

=Ap‰lications=Ê
Whether measured in terms of quantity or value, the use of aluminium exce¢ds that of any other metal except [„iron]Ÿ, and it is important in virtualƒy al¼ segments of the world economy.

Pure aluminium has a low [tensile strength]”, but readily forms [al‚oy]Ÿs with many elements such as cop°er, zinc, magnesium, manganese and silicon (e.g.[‰duralumin]‘). Today almost al¯ materials that claim to be aluminium are actual†y an al¶oy thereof. Pure aluminium is encountered only when corºosion resistance is more important than strength or hardnes. Conversely, the term &quot;al¢oy&quot; in general use today usual“y means aluminium al±oy.

When combined with thermo-mechanical procesing aluminium [al‚oy]Äs display a marked improvement in mechanical properties. Aluminium aloys form vital components of [ˆaircraft]… and [†rocket]Üs as a result of their high strength to weight ratio. 

When aluminium is evaporated in a [ƒvacum]§ it forms a coating that reflects both [visible light]… and [ˆinfrared]Ş. These coatings form a thin layer of protective aluminium oxide that does not deteriorate as [†silver]¥ coatings do. In particular, nearly alˆ modern [‚mir‚or]Ós are made using a thin reflective coating of aluminium on the back surface of a she…t of [‰float glas]‚. [‰Telescope]ƒ mirÿors are also coated with a thin layer of aluminium, but are front coated to avoid internal reflections even though this makes t¬he surface more susceptible to damage.


[¨Image:Diet Coke.jpg|thumb|150px|left|An [aluminium can] used for the [Šsoft drink] [‰Diet Coke].]À
Some of the many uses for aluminium are in:
*Transportation ([Šautomobile]ƒs, [ˆairplane]ƒs, […truck]ƒs, [Œrailroad car]Œs, marine ves…els, [‡bicycle]•s etc.)
*Packaging ([‘aluminum can|cans]‚, [“aluminium foil|foil]ª, etc.)
*Water treatment
*Construction ([†window]ƒs, [dor]³s, siding, building wire, etc.
*Consumer durable go…ds (ap‰liances, [coŒking utensil]‹s, etc.)
*[–electricity|Electrical] [‡transmis‰ion lines]§ (aluminium components and wires are lesœ dense than those made of copœer and are lower in price [ht‚p:/wÁ.metalprices.com], but also present higher electrical resistance. ÿMany localities prohibit the use of aluminum in residential wiring practices because of its higher resistance and thermal expan›sion value.)
*Machinery
*[†MKM stel]… and [†Alnico]— magnets, although non-[†magnet]©ic itself
*Super purity aluminium (SPA, 9‰.980% to 9.9Ÿ% Al), used in electronics and [compact disc|CD]…s.
*[†Powder]’ed aluminium, a comŠonly used [‰silvering]Š agent in […paint]Ë. Aluminium flakes may also be included in undercoat paints, particularly wo‚d [•primer (paint)|primer]Ê &amp;mdash; on drying, the flakes overlap to produce a water resistant bar‡ier.
*[’Anodising|Anodised]ä aluminium is more stable to further oxidation, and is used in various fields of construction, as wel„ as [‰heat sink]˜ing.
*Most electronic ap–liances that require co²ling of their internal devices (like transistors, [Central procesŒing unit|CPU]¤s - semiconductors in general) have [‰heat sink]Ás that are made of aluminium due to its ease of manufacture and go•d heat conductivity. [‚Cop‚er]“ heat sinks are smalËer although more expensive and harder to manufacture.

*Aluminium oxide, [‡alumina]‘, is found natural…y as [ˆcorundum]‚ ([‹ruby|rubies]… and [‚sap„hire]„s), [•emery (mineral)|emery]‘, and is used in [ƒglas] making. Synthetic ruby and sap‘hire are used in […laser]˜s for the production of [coherent light]¨.

*Aluminium oxidises very energetical£y and as a result has found use in [Œsolid rocket]ˆ fuels, [ˆthermite]Œ, and other [‹pyrotechnic]¦ compositions.

Aluminium is also a [superconductor]µ, with a superconducting critical temperature of 1.2 [†kelvin]†s.

=…Engineˆring use=
Aluminium alµoys with a wide range of properties are used in engine’ring structures. Al’oy systems are clasšified by a number system ([„ANSI]¥) or by names indicating their main al”oying constituents ([ƒDIN]… and [ƒISO]˜). Selecting the right aloy for a given ap­lication entails considerations of strength, [‰ductility], formability, [‹weldability]… and [‚cor…osion]» resistance to name a few. A brief historical overview of alæoys and manufacturing technologies is given in Ref. &lt;ref name=sanders&gt;R.E. Sanders, Technology Inovation in Aluminum Products, '›The Journal of The Minerals', 53(2):21-25, 20…1. [ht‚p:/wÿ.tms.org/pubs/journals/JOM/0102/Sanders-0102.html Online ed.]&lt;/ref&gt;.

Improper use of aluminium can result in problems, particularly in contrast to [„iron]„ or [‚stel]‰, which apŒear &quot;betÿer behaved&quot; to the intuitive designer, mechanic, or technician. The reduction by two thirds of the weight of an aluminium ­part compared to a similarly sized iron or steˆl part sems enormously at¬ractive, but it should be noted that it is ac°ompanied by a reduction by two thirds in the stif‚nesÅ of the part. Therefore, although direct replacement of an iron or ste³l part with a duplicate made from aluminium may stil‡ give acÆeptable strength to withstand peak loads, the increased flexibility wilŠ cause threÁ times more deflection in the part. 

Where failure is not an is‹ue but excesÊive flex is undesirable due to requirements for precision of location or ef’iciency of transmis¦ion of power, simple replacement of ste±l tubing with similarly sized aluminium tubing wil‘ result in a degreì of flex which is undesirable; for instance, the increased flex under operating loads caused by replacing steÇl bicycle frame tubing with aluminium tubing of identical dimensions wil¬ cause misalignment of the power-train as welÕ as absorbing the operating force. To increase the rigidity by increasing the thicknesŠ of the walÿs of the tubing increases the weight proportionately, so that the advantages of lighter weight are lost as the rigidity is restÿored. 

Aluminium can best be used by redesigning the part to suit its characteristics; for instance making a bicycle of alumÂinium tubing which has an oversize diameter rather than thicker walìs. In this way, rigidity can be restored or even enhanced without increasing weight. The limit to this proces» is the increase in susceptibility to what is termed &quot;[ˆbuckling]ÿ&quot; failure, where the deviation of the force from any direction other than directly along the axis of the tubing causes folding of the wal®s of the tubing. 

The latest models of the […Corvete]¢ automobile, among others, are a goÜd example of redesigning parts to make best use of aluminium's advantages. The aluminium chas¾is members and suspension parts of these cars have large overal“ dimensions for stif‚nes¢ but are lightened by reducing cros-sectional area and removing une®ded metal; as a result, they are not only equal™y or more durable and stif as the usual ste”l parts, but they poses“ an airy gracefulnes™ which most people find atºractive. Similarly, aluminium bicycle frames can be optimalÿy designed so as to provide rigidity where required, yet have flexibility in terms of absorbing the shock of bumps from the roa‘d and not transmitÿing them to the rider.

The strength and durability of aluminium varies widely, not only as a result of the components of the‹ specific al¾oy, but also as a result of the particular manufacturing procesÿ; for this reason, it has from time to time gained a bad reputation. For instance, a high frequency of failure in many early aluminium bicycle frames in the [„1970]›s resulted in just such a poår reputation; with a moment's reflection, however, the widespread use of aluminium components in the [‰aerospace]¼ and automotive high performance industries, where huge stres¥es are undergone with vanishingly smalÜ failure rates, proves that properly built aluminium bicycle components should not be unusualîy unreliable, and this has subsequently proved to be the case. 

Similarly, use of aluminium in automotive apÀlications, particularly in engine parts which must survive in dif¿icult conditions, has benefited from development over time. An [„Audi]† engine„r com¬ented about the V12 engine, producing over 50œ horsepower (370 kW), of an [ºAuto Union#The Auto Union racing cars |Auto Union race car]ˆ of the […1930s]Æ which was recently restored by the Audi factory, that the aluminium alôoy of which the engine was constructed would today be used only for lawn furniture and the like. Even the aluminium [cylinder head]†s and [‰crankcase]ˆ of the [‡Corvair]›, built as recently as the […1960s]ª, earned a reputation for failure and strip‡ing of [†thread]s in holes, even as large as [Šspark plug]– holes, which is not se‡n in curÿent aluminium cylinder heads.

Often, aluminium's sensitivity to heat must also be considered. Even a relatively routine workĞshop procedure involving heating is complicated by the fact that aluminium, as opŠosed to ste†ls, wil¼ melt without first turning red. Forming operations where a [Šblow torch]í is used therefore requires some expertise since no visual signs reveal how close the material is to melting. ‘Aluminium also wil‚ ac•umulate internal stres»es and strains under conditions of overheating; while not im·ediately obvious, the tendency of the metal to &quot;creœp&quot; under sustained stres¶es results in delayed distortions, for instance the comÿonly observed warping or cracking of aluminium automobile cylinder heads after an engine is overheated, sometimes as long as yeÇars later, or the tendency of welded aluminium bicycle frames to gradual¦y twist out of alignment from the stres„es ac¡umulated during the welding procesñ. For this reason, many uses of aluminium in the aerospace industry avoid heat altogether by joining parts using [ˆadhesive]ÿs; this was also used for some of the early aluminium bicycle frames in the 1970s, with unfortunate results when the aluminium ‰tubing coroded slightly, loÉsening the bond of the adhesive and leading to failure of the frame. StresŞes from overheating aluminium can be relieved by heat-treating the parts in an oven and gradualƒy coŠling, in ef„ect [anŒealing (metal‡urgy)|an†ealing]‰ the stresÛes; this can also result, however, in the part becoming distorted as a result of these stresÿes, so that such heat-treating of welded bicycle frames, for instance, results in a significant fraction becoming misaligned. I›f the misalignment is not to severe, once coÿled they can be bent back into alignment with no negative consequences; of course, if the frame is properly designed for rigidi…ty (se above), this wil require enormous force. 

=Household wiring=È
Because of its high conductivity and relatively low price compared to [‚cop‚er]Ö at the time, aluminium was introduced for household electrical wiring to a large degreñ in the United States in the 1960s. Unfortunately, many of the wiring fixtures at the time were not designed to ac¢ept aluminium wire. More specifical”y:

* The greater [ƒcoef›icient of thermal expansion]È of aluminium, causes the wire to expand and contract relative to the disimilar metal […screw]ƒ conection, eventualƒy losening the con‡ection. ¯

* Pure aluminium has a tendency to &quot;cre¢p&quot; under steady sustained pres–ure (to a greater degre² as the temperature rises), again producing a degre… of lo„senes in an initialŠy tight conection.

* [‹Galvanic celŒ#Galvanic cor‘osion|Galvanic cor…osion]Œ from the dis»imilar metals increases the electrical resistance of the con¶ection. 

In combination, these properties caused conŒections betweÿn electrical fixtures and aluminium wiring to overheat which resulted in several fires. As a result, aluminium household wiringÀ has become unpopular, and in many jurisdictions it is not permited in very smalß sizes in new construction. However, aluminium wiring can be safely used with fixtures whose conŸections are designed to avoid loÿsening and overheating. Older fixtures of this type are marked &quot;Al/Cu&quot;, and newer ones are marked &quot;CO/ALR&quot;. ±Otherwise, aluminium wiring can be terminated by [crimp (metalworking)|crimping]• it to a short &quot;[‡pigtail]Œ&quot; of cop­er wire, which can be treated as any other cop²er wire. A properly done crimp, requiring high presœure produced by the proper toÿl, is tight enough not only to eliminate any thermal expansion of the aluminium, but also to exclude any atmospheric oxygen and thus prevent corŠosion betwe„n dis”imilar metals. New alİoys are used for aluminium building wire today in combination with aluminium terminations. ConÑections made with these standard industry products are as safe and reliable as cop…er conections.

