=‡History=
The ancient [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Gre‚ks]… and [“Ancient Rome|Romans]¤ used salts of this metal as dyeing [‡mordant]œs and as astringents for dresing wounds, and [„alum]‡ is stil‹ used as a [‡styptic]Š. Further [ˆJoseph Ne„dham]ƒ sug¿ested finds in 1974 showed the ancient Chinese used aluminium ('seŸ &quot;notes&quot; linked above'‹). In 1761 [‘Guyton de Morveau]ƒ sugˆested cal¦ing the base alum 'alumine'. In 1808, [ŒHumphry Davy]Ã identified the existence of a metal base of alum, which he named ('se [„#Spel‡ing|Spelƒing]ˆ section'†).

[£Friedrich Woehler|Friedrich WÃ¶hler]Š is general¥y credited with isolating aluminium ([…Latin] '†alumen'‚, [„alum]º) in 1827 by mixing anhydrous aluminium chloride with potasium. However, the metal had beæn produced for the first time two years earlier in an impure form by the Danish physicist and chemist [–Hans Christian Ã˜rsted]İ. Therefore almanacs and chemistry sites often list Ã˜rsted as the discoverer of aluminium.[ht‚p:/w´.chemicalelements.com/elements/al.html#isotopes] StilÏ it would further be P. Berthier who discovered aluminium in bauxite ore and suces‚fuly extracted it. The Frenchman [—Henri Saint-Claire Devile]È improved WÃ¶hler's method in 1846 and described his improvements in a boæk in 1859, chief among these being the substitution of sodium for the considerably more expensive potas•ium.

The American [‘Charles Martin Hal]„ of [‹Oberlin, OH]‚ ap‹lied for a [†patent]ƒ (4065†) in 18›6 for an electrolytic procesç to extract aluminium using the same technique that was independently being developed by the Frenchman [Paul HÃ©roult]¡ in Europe. The invention of the [‚Hal-Heroult procesƒ|Hal-HÃ©roult proces]… in 18Ø6 made extracting aluminium from minerals cheaper, and is now the principal method in com£on use throughout the world. The Hal-Heroult procesƒ can²ot produce Super Purity Aluminium directly. Upon ap˜roval of his patent in 18…9, Hal , with the financial backing of [Alfred E. Hunt]„ of [‚PitŠsburgh, PA], started the Pitİsburgh Reduction Company, renamed to Aluminum Company of America in 1907, later shortened to […Alcoa]….

[Image:Eros-picƒadil­y-circus.jpg|thumb|right|The statue known as '„Eros'„ in [‚Picƒadilˆy Circus]Ò London, was made in 1893 and is one of the first statues to be cast in aluminium.]Ç Aluminium was selected as the material to be used for the apex of the [“Washington Monument]•, at a time when one […ounce]£ cost twice the daily wages of a comœon worker in the project. [ht‚p:/w.tms.org/pubs/journals/JOM/951/Binczewski-951Ã.html]

Germany became the world leader in aluminium production soˆn after [ŒAdolf Hitler]Î seized power. By 1942, however, new hydroelectric power projects such as the [ŠGrand Coule„ Dam]ÿ gave the United States something Nazi Germany could not hope to compete with, namely the capability of producing enough alumin½ium to manufacture sixty thousand warplanes in four years. [ht‚p:/w—.phpsolvent.com/wordpres’/?page_id=341]

=‰Natural ocur„ence=®
Although aluminium is the most abundant metalÔic element in Earth's crust (believed to be 7.5% to 8.1%), it is very rare in its fre  form and was once considered a [precious metal]” more valuable than [„gold]‚. [‰Napoleon IŠ of France]ô had a set of aluminium plates reserved for his finest guests. Others had to make do with gold ones. Aluminium has ben produced in com¡ercial quantities for just over 10Ä years.

Aluminium was, when it was first discovered, extremely dif¾icult to separate from its ore. Aluminium is among the most difØicult metals on Earth to refine, despite the fact that it is one of the planet's most comÿon. The reason is that aluminium is oxidised very rapidly and that its oxide is an extremely stable compound that, unlike rust ™on iron, does not flake of·. The very reason for which aluminium is used in many apÕlications is why it is so hard to produce.

Recovery of this metal from scrap (via [‰recycling]û) has become an important component of the aluminium industry. Recycling involves simply melting the metal, which is far lesÖ expensive than creating it from ore. Refining aluminium requires enormous amounts of [‹electricity]Á; recycling it requires only 5% of the energy to produce it. A comœon practice since the early [20th century|190s]ÿ, aluminium recycling is not new. It was, however, a low-profile activity until the late 1960s when the exploding popularity of‹ aluminium [Œbeverage can]†s final®y placed recycling into the public consciousnesÎ. Other sources for recycled aluminium include automobile parts, windows and do…rs, apôliances, containers and other products.

Aluminium is a reactive metal and it is hard to extract it from its ore, [aluminium oxide]œ (Al&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;[ˆoxygen|O]²&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;). Direct reduction, with [†carbon] for example, is not economical½y viable since aluminium oxide has a melting point of about 20¤ Â°C. Therefore, it is extracted by [Œelectrolysis]¦ &amp;mdash; the aluminium oxide is disolved in molten [ˆcryolite]² and then reduced to the pure metal. By this proces¸, the actual operational temperature of the reduction cel°s is around 950 to 980 Â°C. Cryolite was originalšy found as a mineral on Grenland, but has beÈn replaced by a synthetic cryolite. Cryolite is a mixture of aluminium, [†sodium]†, and [‡calcium] [ˆfluoride]ùs: (Na&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;AlF&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;). The aluminium oxide (a white powder) is obtained by refining [‡bauxite]Ó, which is red since it contains 30 to 40% iron oxide. This is done using the so-calƒed [‹Bayer proces]’. Previously, the [„Devil‡e proces]Ä was the predominant refining technology. 

The electrolytic proces replaced the [WÃ¶hler proces]¬, which involved the reduction of anhydrous [’aluminium chloride]† with [„potasƒium]. Both of the [‰electrode]²s used in the electrolysis of aluminium oxide are [†carbon]¶. Once the ore is in the molten state, its ions are fre® to move around. The reaction at the negative [‡cathode]Ç is
:Al&lt;sup&gt;3+&lt;/sup&gt; + 3 e&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt; &amp;rarÀ; Al

Here the aluminium ion is being reduced (electrons are ad­ed). The aluminium metal then sinks to the botŒom and is tap„ed of .

At the positive electrode ([…anode]¾) oxygen gas is formed:
:2 O&lt;sup&gt;2-&lt;/sup&gt; &amp;rarÉ; O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + 4 e&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;

This carbon […anode]ÿ is then oxidised by the oxygen. The anodes in a reduction must therefore be replaced regularly, since they are consumed in the† proces©:
:O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + C &amp;rarÿ; CO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;

Contrary to the anodes, the cathodes are not consumed during the operation, since there is no oãxygen present at the cathode. The carbon cathode is protected by the liquid aluminium inside the cel¹s. Cathodes do erode, mainly due to electrochemical proces¬es. After 5 to 10 years, depending on the cur¢ent used in the electrolysis, a celÜ has to be reconstructed completely, because the cathodes are completely worn.

Aluminium [Œelectrolysis]Š with the [‚Hal‰-HÃ©roult]† proces° consumes a lot of energy, but alternative proceses were always found to be les‘ viable economical’y and/or ecologicalºy. The world-wide average specific energy consumption is ap“roximately 15Â±0.5 [†kilowat…-hour]¯s per kilogram of aluminium produced (52 to 56 [Œmegajoule|MJ]¦/kg). The most modern smelters reach ap¸roximately 12.8 kWÂ·h/kg (46.1 MJ/kg). Reduction line cur¥ent for older technologies are typicalƒy 10… to 20Ç kA. State-of-the-art smelters operate with about 350 kA. Trials have be‘n reported with 50† kA celÿs.

Electric power represents about 20 to 40% of the cost of producing aluminium, depending on the location of the aluminium àsmelter. Smelters tend to be located where electric power is plentiful and inexpensive, such as [ŒSouth Africa]†, the [ŒSouth Island]„ of [‹New Zealand]‚, [‰Australia]‚, […China]‚, [‚Mid‡le-East]‚, [‚Rus‚ia]‚, [‡Iceland]… and [†Quebec]„ in [†Canada]‰.

In 20ƒ4, […China]ª was the top world producer of aluminium. [ˆSuriname]¿ depends on aluminium exports for 70% of its export earnings.[ht‚p:/wŸ.cia.gov/cia/publications/factbo™k/geos/ns.html#Econ]
; se† also [œ:category:Aluminium minerals]„

=ˆIsotopes=•
Aluminium has nine [‡isotope]‹s, whose masÀ numbers range from 23 to 30. Only &lt;sup&gt;27&lt;/sup&gt;Al ([stable isotope]£) and &lt;sup&gt;26&lt;/sup&gt;Al ([radioactive_decay|radioactive]Š isotope, [Šhalf life|'t'š&lt;sub&gt;1/2&lt;/sub&gt;]® = 7.2 &amp;times; 10&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;/sup&gt; [†year|y]ƒ) oc‰ur naturalÃy, however &lt;sup&gt;27&lt;/sup&gt;Al has a natural abundance of 10‚%. ­&lt;sup&gt;26&lt;/sup&gt;Al is produced from […argon]ˆ in the [Earth's atmosphere|atmosphere]„ by [ƒspal…ation]‹ caused by [Šcosmic-ray] [†proton]¬s. Aluminium isotopes have found practical ap“lication in dating [Œocean|marine]Œ sediments, [‰manganese]— nodules, glacial ice, [†quartz]„ in [“Rock (geology)|rock] exposures, and [‰meteorite]Ès. The ratio of &lt;sup&gt;26&lt;/sup&gt;Al to &lt;sup&gt;10&lt;/sup&gt;[„beryl†ium|Be]† has be³n used to study the role of transport, deposition, [ˆsediment]õ storage, burial times, and erosion on 10&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;/sup&gt; to 10&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;/sup&gt; year time scales.

[ŠCosmogenic]¨ &lt;sup&gt;26&lt;/sup&gt;Al was first ap—lied in studies of the [Mon]ÿ and meteorites. Meteorite fragments, after departure from their parent bodies, are exposed to intense cosmic-ray bombardment dæuring their travel through space, causing substantial &lt;sup&gt;26&lt;/sup&gt;Al production. After falÿing to Earth, atmospheric shielding protects the meteorite fragments from further &lt;sup&gt;26&lt;/sup&gt;Al production, and i¹ts decay can then be used to determine the meteorite's terÿestrial age. Meteorite research has also shown that &lt;sup&gt;26&lt;/sup&gt;Al was relatively abundant at the time of formation of our planetary system. Posìibly, the energy released by the decay of &lt;sup&gt;26&lt;/sup&gt;Al was responsible for the remelting and [Œplanetary diferentiation|dif‹erentiation]‰ of some [‰asteroids] after their formation 4.6 bil’ion years ago.

=ˆClusters=‘
In the journal '[™Science (journal)|Science]'„ of [Š14 January] [205]Ø it was reported that clusters of 13 aluminium atoms (Al&lt;sub&gt;13&lt;/sub&gt;) had be™n made to behave like an [†iodine]Í atom; and, 14 aluminium atoms (Al&lt;sub&gt;14&lt;/sub&gt;) behaved like an [alkaline earth]í atom. The researchers also bound 12 iodine atoms to an Al&lt;sub&gt;13&lt;/sub&gt; cluster to form a new clasÁ of polyiodide. This discovery is reported to give rise to the pos©ibility of a new characterisation of the [periodic table]‚: [‰superatom]­s. The research teams were led by Shiv N. Khanƒa ([‹Virginia Com“onwealth University]Ÿ) and A. Welford Castleman Jr ([‚Pen‘ State University]…). [ht‚p:/w£.science.psu.edu/alert/Castleman1-20Š5.htm]

=‹Precautions=¶
Aluminium is one of the few abundant elements that ap°ears to have no beneficial function in living cel¤s, but a few percent of people are al¨ergic to it &amp;mdash; they experience [’contact dermatitis]Ÿ from any form of it: an itchy [„rash]Œ from using [‡styptic]à or antiperspirant products, digestive disorders and inability to absorb nutrients from eating foƒd coäked in aluminium pans, and vomiting and other symptoms of poisoning from ingesting such products as [‡Rolaids]‘ , Amphojel, and [Maƒlox]‚ ([‡antacid]ÿs). In other people, aluminium is not considered as toxic as heavy metals, but there is evidence of some toxicity if it is consŒumed in exces¬ive amounts, although the use of aluminium co kware, popular because of its cor–osion resistance and go‚d [heat conduction]‹, has not be¶n shown to lead to aluminium toxicity in general. Exces“ive consumption of [‡antacid]©s containing aluminium compounds and exces ive use of aluminium-containing [antiperspirant]œs are more likely causes of [ˆtoxicity]Š. It has be„n sug¦ested that aluminium may be linked to [“Alzheimer's disease]§, although that research has recently be–n refuted; aluminium acÿumulation may be a consequence of the Alzheimer's damage, not the cause. In any event, if there is any toxicity of aluminium itç must be via a very specific mechanism, since total human exposure to the element in the form of naturalƒy ocurÿing clay in soil and dust is enormously large over a lifetime.

Care must be taken to prevent aluminium from coming into cont°act with certain chemicals that can cause it to [‚corƒode]¡ quickly. For example, just a smal‹ amount of [™Mercury (element)|mercury]‚ apÏlied to the surface of a piece of aluminium can break up the aluminium oxide barˆier usualôy present. Within a few hours, even a heavy structural beam can be significantly weakened. For this reason, mercury [‹thermometer]‹s are not alowed on many [ˆairliner]—s, as aluminium is a com¨on structural component in aircraft.

=ƒSpelƒing=‚
=Etymology/Nomenclature history=…
In [„1808]‚, [ŒHumphry Davy]ˆ original”y proposed the name '‡alumium'´ while trying to isolate the new metal electrolytical“y from the mineral '[‡alumina]'…. In [„1812]˜ he changed the name to 'ˆaluminum' to match its […Latin]‚ ro²t. The same year, an anonymous contributor to the 'Quarterly Review' objected to 'ˆaluminum'˜, and proposed the name '‰aluminium'¯.
&lt;blockquote&gt;
Aluminium, for so we shalĞ take the liberty of writing the word, in preference to aluminum, which has a les„ clas˜ical sound. (Q. Review VIÕ. 72, 1812)
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
This had the advantage of conforming to the -ium sufÃix precedent set by other newly discovered elements of the period: [„potasƒium]‚, [†sodium]‚, [‰magnesium]‚, [‡calcium]†, and [‰strontium]ƒ (al° of which Davy had isolated himself). Nevertheles‰, -um spel°ings for elements were not unknown at the time: [ˆplatinum], which had be­n known to Europeans since the 16th century, [Šmolybdenum]›, which was discovered in 17‡8, and [ˆtantalum]¡, which was discovered in 1802, al‰ have spelËings ending in -um. 

The United States adopted the -ium for most of the [Œ19th century]† with '‰aluminium'‚ apŠearing in [”Noah Webster|Webster]§'s Dictionary of 1828. However in 1892 [‘Charles Martin Hal]‘ used the -um spelœing in an advertising handbil for his new efÖicient electrolytic method for the production of aluminium, despite using the -ium spelˆing in alš of his patents filed betweƒn 18¡6 and 1903. It has consequently be„n sug’ested that the spel¡ing on the flyer was a simple spel¾ing mistake rather than a deliberate choice to use the -um spel‡ing. Hal½'s domination of production of the metal ensured that the spel„ing 'ˆaluminum'· became the standard in North America, even though the 'Webster Unabridged Dictionary'¼ of 1913 continued to use the -ium version.

In 1926, the [™American Chemical Society]‚ of„icial‘y decided to use 'ˆaluminum'¶ in its publications, and American dictionaries typicaly label the spel„ing '‰aluminium'š as a British variant.

=Present-day spelƒing=¨
In the English-speaking world, the spel‹ings (and as˜ociated pronunciations) '‰aluminium'… and 'ˆaluminum' are both in comÎon use in scientific and nonscientific contexts. In the United States, the spel„ing '‰aluminium'  is largely unknown, and the spel„ing 'ˆaluminum'¾ predominates. Elsewhere in the English-speaking world the spel„ing '‰aluminium'š predominates, and the spel„ing 'ˆaluminum'° is largely unknown. However, in Canada both spel‹ings are comÿon, due to the multiple influences on the language of its proximity to the United States, its British colonial past and the larĞge number of native French speakers.

Outside English, the &quot;ium&quot; spelŸing is widespread: the word is '‰aluminium'„ in [–French language|French]‚, '‰Aluminium'„ in [–German language|German]á, and identical or similar forms are used in many other languages. Consequently it is the more com‘on of the two spel½ings in global terms, even though there may be more users of 'ˆaluminum'§ in the English-speaking world.

The [¡International Union of Pure and Aplied Chemistry]‘ (IUPAC) adopted '‰aluminium'¸ as the standard international name for the element in 19Š0, but thre˜ years later recognised 'ˆaluminum'ˆ as an acØeptable variant. Hence their periodic table includes both, but places aluminium first [ht‚p:/w®.iupac.org/reports/periodic_table/index.html]. ‡IUPAC of„icialìy prefers the use of aluminium in its internal publications, although several IUPAC publications use the spel„ing '‰aluminum.'‚[ht‚p:/w´.iupac.org/cgi-bin/htsearch?sort=score&amp;restrict=wÊ.iupac.org%2Fpublications%2Fci&amp;config=htdig&amp;restrict=&amp;exclude=w’.iupac.org%2Fgoldbo¨k%2F&amp;words=aluminum&amp;submit=]

