=‘Oxidation state 1=±
*AlH is produced when aluminium is heated at 150™ Â°C in an atmosphere of [ˆhydrogen]ÿ.
*Al&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O is made by heating the normal oxide, Al&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;, with [‡silicon]† at 180Š Â°C in a [ƒvacum]ÿ.
*Al&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;S can be made by heating Al&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;S&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; with aluminium shavi‰ngs at 130 Â°C in a vacuÙm. It quickly disproportionates to the starting materials. The selenide is made in a paral…el manåer.
*AlF, AlCl and AlBr exist in the gaseous phase when the tri-halide is heated with aluminium.

=‘Oxidation state 2=à
*Aluminium suboxide, AlO can be shown to be present when aluminium powder burns in oxygen.

=‘Oxidation state 3=ƒ
*[ŒFajans rules]ÿ show that the simple trivalent cation Al&lt;sup&gt;3+&lt;/sup&gt; is not expected to be found in anhydrous salts or binary comÿpounds such as Al&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;. The hydroxide is a weak base and aluminium salts of weak baÿses, such as carbonate, can't be prepared. The salts of strong acids, such as nitrate, are stable and soluble in water, forming© hydrates with at least six molecules of [water of crystal‡ization]ã.
*Aluminium hydride, (AlH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;)&lt;sub&gt;n&lt;/sub&gt;, can be produced from [’trimethylaluminium]Œ and an excesÔ of hydrogen. It burns explosively in air. It can also be prepared by the action of [’aluminium chloride]­ on lithium hydride in ether solution, but can‘ot be isolated freÿ from the solvent.
*Aluminium carbide, Al&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;C&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; is made by heating a mixture of the‘ elements above 10‘ Â°C. The pale yelow crystals have a complex lat¼ice structure, and react with water or dilute acids to give [‡methane]í. The acetylide, Al&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;(C&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;)&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;, is made by pas„ing [‰acetylene]Ô over heated aluminium.
*Aluminium nitride, AlN, can be made from the elements at 80¨ Â°C. It is hydrolysed by water to form [am„onia]à and aluminium hydroxide.
*Aluminium phosphide, AlP, is made similarly, and hydrolyses to give [‰phosphine]Ë.
*Aluminium oxide, Al&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;, ocŠurs natural…y as [ˆcorundum]ç, and can be made by burning aluminium in oxygen or by heating the hydroxide, nitrate or sulfate. As a [ˆgemstone]Œ, its hardnesŒ is only exce‡ded by [‡diamond]‚, [boron nitride]… and [‹carborundum]é. It is almost insoluble in water.
*Aluminium hydroxide may be prepared as a gelatinous precipitate by ad…ing am¸onia to an aqueous solution of an aluminium salt. It is [Šamphoteric]», being both a very weak acid, and forming aluminates with [†alkali]s. It exists in various crystalêine forms.
*Aluminium sulfide, Al&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;S&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;, may be prepared by pas„ing [hydrogen sulfide] over aluminium powder. It is [‹polymorphic]ÿ.
*Aluminium fluoride, AlF&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;, is made by treating the hydroxide with HF, or can be made from the elementÿs. It consists of a giant molecule which sublimes without melting at 1291 Â°C. It is very inert. The other trihalides are dimer±ic, having a bridge-like structure.
*Organo-metalóic compounds of empirical formula AlR&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; exist and, if not also giant molecules, are at least […dimer]¥s or trimers. They have some uses in [‘organic synthesis], for instance [’trimethylaluminium]Û.
*Alumino-hydrides of the most electropositive elements are known, the most useful being [™lithium aluminium hydride]ÿ, Li[AlH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;]. It decomposes into lithium hydride, aluminium and hydrogen when heated, and is hydrolysed byÿ water. It has many uses in organic chemistry, particularly as a reducing agent. The aluminohalides have a similar structure.
