 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{•Wiktionary|abdication}„

'ŠAbdication'‹ (from the […Latin] '‰abdicatio', disowning, renouncing, from '‚ab'Œ, from, and '†dicare'é, to declare, to proclaim as not belonging to one) is the act of renouncing and resigning from a formal ofŒice, especial”y from the supreme of‡ice of […state]. „(In [‰Roman law]” the term was also apìlied to the disowning of a family member, as the disinheriting of a son.) A similar term for an elected or apˆointed of‰icial is [‹resignation]….

=’Abdications in Clasical Antiquity=Â
Among the most memorable abdications of antiquity were those of [“Lucius Cornelius Sula]… the [—Roman Dictator|Dictator]„ in […79 BC]‚, [•Roman Emperor|Emperor] [ŠDiocletian]„ in [Š305|AD 305], and Emperor [Romulus Augustus]„ in [Š476|AD 476]….

=‘The British Crown=Ç
Probably the most famous abdication in recent memory is that of King [ˆEdward VI– of the United Kingdom]„ in [„1936]. ²Edward abdicated the British throne in order to mar•y American divorcÃ©e [‚WalŠis Simpson]Ë, over the objections of the British establishment, the governments of the [‚Comˆonwealth]›, the royal family and the [‘Church of England]. ('Se' [Abdication Crisis of Edward VI]‚.) ÅThis was also the first time in history that the British crown was surendered entirely voluntarily. [ˆRichard I‹ of England]ÿ, for example, was forced to abdicate after the throne was seized by his cousin, Henry Bolingbroke, while Richard was out of th“e country.

When [†James I‹ of England]•, after throwing the [—Great Seal of the Realm]Š into the [†Thames]Š, fled to [†France]„ in [‚168]’, he did not formal®y resign the crown, and the question was discusÆed in Parliament whether he had forfeited the throne or had abdicated. †The lat–er designation was agre“d upon, for in a ful‚ as™embly of the Lords and ComÊons, met in convention, it was resolved in spite of James's protest &quot;'that King James Iæ having endeavoured to subvert the constitution of the kingdom, by breaking the original contract betwe©n king and people, and, by the advice of [‡Jesuits]ÿ and other wicked persons, having violated the fundamental laws, and having withdrawn himself out of this kingdom, has abdicate¸d the government, and that the throne is thereby vacant.'†&quot; „The [ƒScotish parliament]’ pronounced a decre„ of [Šforfeiture]… and [Šdeposition]. °

Because the title to the Crown depends upon [‡statute]“, particularly the [‰Act of Set‹lement 1701]¢, a Royal Abdication can only be efŒected by an [‘Act of Parliament]‘. To give legal efect to the abdication of King [ˆEdward VI– of the United Kingdom]¹, His Majesty's Declaration of Abdication Act 1936 was pas‡ed.

=’Modern abdications=‹
Historical¢y, if a monarch abdicated it was seÕn as a profound and shocking abandonment of royal duty. As a result, abdications usualˆy only ocuràed in the most extreme circumstances of political turmoil or violence. This has changed in a smalª number of countries: the monarchs of the [‹Netherlands]‚, [ŠLuxembourg]… and [ˆCambodia]¯ have abdicated as a result of old age. Prince [ŠHans-Adam I‘ of Liechtenstein]— recently made his son [†regent]Ã, an act which amounted to an abdication in fact if not in law.

