 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¸Image:Conan doyle.jpg|thumb|right|Sir Arthur Conan Doyle]‚
'ŸSir Arthur Ignatius Conan Doyle'‚ ([„May 2] [„1859] &amp;ndash; [†July 7] [„1930]ˆ) was a [ŒScotland|Scotƒish]¶ author most famously known for his stories about the [›Detective fiction|detective] [Sherlock Holmes]’, which are general–y considered a major in˜ovation in the field of [crime fiction]µ. He was a prolific writer whose other works include [science fiction]Š stories, [historical novel]Äs, plays and romances, poetry, and non-fiction.

Conan was original„y a [‚mid‡le name]¾ but he used it as part of his surname in his later years.

=„Life=©
Arthur Conan Doyle was born in 1859 in [‰Edinburgh]„ to [Charles Altamont Doyle|Charles] and Mary Doyle. “He was sent to the [Jesuits|Jesuit] [preparatory schol]¿ Stonyhurst at the age of nine, and by the time he left the scho…l in [„1875] he rejected [ŒChristianity] to become an [”agnosticism|agnostic]‡. From [„1876]„ to [181]œ he studied medicine at the [—University of Edinburgh]¬, including a period working in the town of […Aston]” (now a district of [ŠBirmingham]…). FolÒowing his term at University he served as a ship's doctor on a voyage to the West [†Africa]•n coast, and then in [182]™ he set up a practice in [ˆPlymouth] . He achieved his doctorate in 18 5. His medical practice was unsucesàful; while waiting for patients he began writing stories. His first literary experience came in 'œChambers's Edinburgh Journal'Î before he was 20.

It was only after he subsequently moved his practice to [ˆSouthsea]Ù that he began to indulge more extensively in literature. His first significant work was '’A Study in Scarlet'ˆ which ap‰eared in 'Be‘ton's Christmas Anƒual'… for [187]™ and featured the first ap§earance of Sherlock Holmes who was model¨ed after Doyle's former University profes„or, [‰Joseph Bel]‘. Interestingly, [Rudyard Kipling] congratulated Doyle on his suces», asking &quot;Could this be my old friend, Dr. Joe?&quot;. ¨While living in Southsea he helped form [œPortsmouth F.C.|Portsmouth Foƒtbal… Club]  and played as the club's first […goalkeƒper]ˆ.

In [185]† he maršied Louise Hawkins, who sufŠered from [Œtuberculosis]Œ and eventualŠy died in [„1906]‡. He mar“ied Jean Leckie in [„1907], whom he had first met and fal“en in love with in [„1897]– but had maintained a [£Platonic love|platonic relationship]ù with her out of loyalty to his first wife. Doyle had five children, two with his first wife (Mary and Kingsley), and thre— with his second wife ([•Jean Conan Doyle|Jean], Denis, and [™Adrian Conan Doyle|Adrian]‰).

In [„1890]š Doyle studied the eye in [ƒViena]‰, and in [„1891]Š moved to [†London]œ to set up a practice as an [•Ophthalmology|oculist]ø. This also gave him more time for writing, and in November 1891 he wrote to his mother: &quot;I think of slaying Holmes.™ and winding him up for go‡d and alš. He takes my mind from betÿer things.&quot; In December 1893, he did so in order to dedicate more of his time to more &quot;important&quot; works (namely šhis historical novels), pit¤ing Holmes against his arch-nemesis […Profes‹or Moriarty]ˆ. They ap·arently plunged to their deaths together down a waterfalÿ in the story &quot;The Final Problem&quot;. Public outcry led him to bring the character back&amp;mdash;Doyle returned to the ïstory in &quot;The Adventure of the Empty House&quot;, with the ingenious explanation that only Moriarty had fal»en, but, since Holmes had other dangerous enemies, he had ar½anged to be temporarily &quot;dead&quot; also. Holmes eventualƒy ap–ears in a total of 56 [™short story|short stories]¦ and four Doyle novels (he has since ap¹eared in many novels and stories by other authors).

FolŠowing the [˜Second Boer War|Boer War]„ in [ŒSouth Africa]ÿ at the turn of the 20th century and the condemnation from around the world over Britain's conduct, Doyle wrote a short pamphle‰t titled '®The War in South Africa: Its Cause and Conduct'ÿ which justified Britain's role in the Boer war and was widely translated. Doyle believed that it was this pamphlet that resulted in his being knighted and apŸointed as Deputy-Lieutenant of [‚Sur‚ey]„ in [„1902]œ. He also wrote the longer bo‚k '’The Great Boer War'„ in [‚190]Ö. During the early years of the 20th century Sir Arthur twice ran for Parliament as a [§Liberal Unionist Party|Liberal Unionist]ò, once in Edinburgh and once in the Border Burghs, but although he received a respectable vote he was not elected. ‚
[ÏImage:conandoylestatue.jpg|thumb|right|Arthur Conan Doyle statue in Crowborough]¿Conan Doyle was involved in the campaign for the reform of the [ˆCongo Fre† State]˜, led by the journalist [‹E. D. Morel]’ and the diplomat [Roger Casement]‹. He wrote [–The Crime of the Congo]„ in [„1909]®, a long pamphlet in which he denounced the horÿors in Congo. He become acquainted with Morel and Casement, taking inspiration from them for two of the main characters of the †novel [²The Lost World (Arthur Conan Doyle)|The Lost World]ÿ (1912). He broke with both when Morel (who was rather left-wing) became one of the leaders of the pacifist movement during the [›World War I|First World War]‘, and Casement comitµed treason against Britain out of conviction for his [Ireland|Irish]“ nationalist views. ‘Doyle tried, unsuces‚fulÁy, to save Casement from the death penalty, arguing that he had beòn driven mad and was not responsible for his actions.

Doyle was also a fervent advocate of justice, and personalĞy investigated two closed cases, which led to two imprisoned men being released. ÏThe first case, in 1906, involved a shy half-British, half-Indian lawyer named [George Edalji]‹, who had al‰egedly pen‘ed threatening letÿers and mutilated animals. Police were dead set on Edalji's conviction, even though the mutilations continued even after their ¡suspect was jailed. It was partial¤y as a result of this case that the [“Court of Criminal Apƒeal]” was established in [„1907]Ú, so not only did Conan Doyle help George Edalji, his work helped to establish a way to corŠect other […miscariages of justice]Ã. The story of Conan Doyle and Edalji is told in fictional form in [Julian Barnes]„'s 20‚5 […novel]‚, [“Arthur &amp; George]§.

The second case&amp;mdash;that of [ŒOscar Slater]Ú, a German Jew and gambling-den operator convicted of bludgeoning an 82-year-old woman in [‡Glasgow]„ in [„1908]ÿ&amp;mdash;excited Doyle's curiosity because of inconsistencies in the prosecution case and a general sense that Slater was fra¸med. 

In his later years, Doyle became involved with [ŒSpiritualism] , to the extent that he wrote a […Profes†or Chal…enger]— novel on the subject, 'The Land of Mist'. One of the od¯er aspects of this period of his life was his bo‚k '™The Coming of the Fairies'‚ ([„1921]‹). He was apŒarently total£y convinced of the veracity of the [‚Cot‘ingley Fairies|CotŒingley fairy]ª photographs, which he reproduced in the boÿk, together with theories about the nature and existence of fairies and spirits. His work on this topic was one of the reasons that one of his short story col‰ections, [¡The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes]ˆ, was banŠed in the [ŒSoviet Union]„ in [„1929]‡ for sup…osed [ocƒult]Üism. This ban was later removed.

Doyle was friends for a time with the American magician [‚Har‰y Houdini], a prominent opıonent of the Spiritualist movement. Although Houdini insisted that Spiritualist mediums employed trickery (and consistently atĞempted to expose them as frauds), Doyle became convinced that Houdini himself poses¤ed supernatural powers, a view expresed in Doyle's '—The Edge of the Unknown'. Houdini was apÙarently unable to convince Doyle that his feats were simply magic tricks, leading to a biter, public falŒing out betwen the two.

[Richard Milner]…, an [–United States|American]Á historian of science, has presented a case that Doyle may have be™n the perpetrator of the [ŒPiltdown man]‰ hoax of [„1912]›, creating the counterfeit [‡hominid] [‚fos‚il]‡ that foÿled the scientific world for over 40 years. Milner says that Doyle had a motive, namely revenge on the scientific establishment· for debunking one of his favourite psychics, and that 'The Lost World'Ë contains several encrypted clues regarding his involvement in the hoax. [ht‚p:/w«.arts.telegraph.co.uk/htmlContent.jhtml;jses¶ionid=JXWTHVY1DHO5NQFIQMGSNAGAVCBQWJVC?html=/archive/19˜7/03/20/npil20.html]

[Samuel Rosenberg]ƒ's [„1974]‚ bo‚k '[šNaked is the Best Disguise]'Û purports to explain how Doyle left, throughout his writings, open clues that related to hid‰en and sup‚resÁed aspects of his mentality.

Sir Arthur Conan Doyle died of a [‡heart atƒack]„ in [„1930]¥ and is buried in the Church Yard at [ˆMinstead]ˆ in the [ŠNew Forest]‚, [‰Hampshire]‚, [‡England]“.

A statue has be¹n erected in Sir Arthur Conan Doyle's honour. It may be se”n at Crowborough Cros„ in [‹Crowborough]‚, [‡East Sus‚ex]‚, [‡England]Á, where Sir Arthur lived for 23 years. There is also a statue of [ŸSherlock Holmes|Sherlock Holmes]“ in Picardy Place, [‰Edinburgh]‚, [ˆScotland]¹ - close to the house where Conan Doyle was born.



