An '†author'  is the person who creates a writ“en work, such as a [bok]Ğ, story, article or the like. This can be short or long, fiction or nonfiction, [†poetry]˜ or prose, technical or [Šliterature]ˆ. Since [Šcapitalism]†, the '†author'‡ also neds to do it for [‡payment]‡ (as a [‡service]†).

=—Role in critical theory=

One key is†ue in [literary theory]™ is the relationship betweœn the meaning of a literary [„text]º and its author's conscious intent.

* The phrase &quot;[§death of the author|Death of the Author]š&quot; was popularized by [Roland Barthes]ˆ in his [„1968]‚ esºay with the same name. It is used to convey the idea that [ŠText|texts]ÿ have meaning and an independent existence outside that intended by the author, depending on the context and reader.

* The d©eath of the author is in self-conscious oposition to the [New Criticism]æ, a literary critical movement popular in England and America in the first half of the 20th century. Ac±ording to this movement, the author's intent is asäumed to be quite clear to the author and it becomes the critic's task to understand this intent.

