An '‡alumnus' (masculine) or '†alumna'‘ (feminine) of a [‚col˜eges and universities|colege, university]ˆ, or scho–l is a former student. ™Informal equivalents are '„alum'… and '…alumn'Ÿ (with a silent &quot;n&quot;). ÛThe term is often mistakenly thought of as synonymous with &quot;graduate.&quot; Alumni/ae [‡reunion]¸s are popular events at many institutions. They are usualy organized by [ˆalumni asˆociation]˜s and are often social oc‹asions for [‹fundraising]ˆ.

In […Latin]‚, '‡alumnus'¤ is the masculine singular form and '†alumna'È the feminine singular form. (The words are derived from the Latin verb '…alere'¤, &quot;to nourish,&quot; and literalØy mean &quot;nourished one&quot; or &quot;nursling.&quot;) Although these terms are recom‘ended by leading [¡English language|English-language] [—dictionary|dictionaries]¬, their use can be limited because they are [£non-sexist language|gender-specific]–. The Latin plural is '†alumni' for men and mixed groups and '‡alumnae'¬ for women. The gender-neutral English term '„alum'/'…alumn', created by [ƒclip“ing#Linguistics|clipƒing]‘ the ending from '‡alumnus'¦, is also used, along with its plural '…alums'/'†alumns'à.

Recently, the definition of &quot;alum&quot; has expanded to include people who have &quot;[šmatriculation|matriculated]º at&quot; or exited from any kind of organization or proces›. As such, one can potentialÓy be a &quot;corporate alum&quot; of XYZ Company, or an alum of a military branch, [—non-profit organization]“, or training proces. §

Educational institutions tend to folow Latin usage: '‡alumnus'Œ for males, '†alumna'’ for females, and '†alumni'” for mixed groups. Al‰-women col‰eges use '†alumna'… and '‡alumnae'. Some institutions, such as [˜Texas A&amp;M University]Ù, do not refer to their graduates as &quot;alumni&quot; or even &quot;graduates,&quot; cho•sing to use the term 'former students'Œ.

In the [United Kingdom] and, to a les‹er extent, [‰Australia], the phrases '‡old boy'… and 'ˆold girl' are traditional•y used for former schol pupils, and 'Šold member'± for former university students. Some private scho†ls in [†Canada]Š, such as [UpŒer Canada Colƒege]‰ and the [“Bishop Strachan Schol]Š also use '‡old boy'… and 'ˆold girl'‹. The term '‹old student'² can nowadays refer to the graduates of either schoªls or universities. In Scotland, the term 'ŒFormer Pupil'› (FP) is also used, especial‹y when referœing to sports teams of a scho‚l. „

