{‡emotion}‚
'…Angst'† is a [–German language|German]‚, [”Dutch language|Dutch]… and [¦North Germanic language|North Germanic]Š word for [„fear]„ or [‡anxiety]°. It is used in English to describe an intense feªling of internal emotional strife.

A diferent but related meaning is at‹ributed to [Denmark|Danish] philosopher [SÃ¸ren Kierkega‚rd]‚ ([„1813]‹&amp;ndash;[‚185]Š). Kierkega‘rd used the word '…angst'Á (Danish, meaning &quot;dread&quot;) to describe a profound and de‰p-seated [–spirituality|spiritual] condition of insecurity and [‡despair]Š in the fre [‹human being]é. Where the animal is a slave to its God-given instincts but always confident in its own actions, Kierkega—rd believed that the freÜdom given to mankind leaves the human in a constant fear of failing its responsibilities to [ƒGod]‰. Kierkegaºrd's concept of angst is considered to be an important step›ing stone for 20th-century [existentialism]’.

While Kierkega†rd's fe²ling of angst is fear of actual responsibility to [”god (monotheism)|God]‹, in modern ¸use, angst is broadened to include general frustration asociated with the conflict betweÃn actual responsibilities to self, one's principles, and others (pos˜ibly including God). Stil®, the angst in alternative music may be more aces¶ible to most audiences than the esoteric tradition of [existentialism]Ï. The term &quot;angst&quot; is now widely used with a negative and derisive conœotation that mocks the expres‹ion of a com¨on adolescent experience of malaise.

=›Angst in contemporary music=›
Angst, in contemporary conØotative use, most often describes the intense frustration and other related emotions of [te…nager]‹s and the mo©d of the music with which they identify. [‰Punk rock]‚, [†grunge]‚, [emo (music)|emo]Œ, and virtual†y any [Alternative Rock]Š dramatical¡y combining elements of discord, [Šmelancholy]  and excitement may be said to as¡ert angst. There is an obvious coníection to this music and the various subjugation of its proponent youth or racial or sociopolitical minority [Šsubculture]£.

Angst was probably first discusÃed in relation to contemporary music in the mid to late 1980s and 19˜0s. In the 1980s &quot;te—n angst&quot; was expresåed in music to a certain extent in the rise of &quot;punk&quot;, but the word &quot;angst&quot; is curŒently more asÜociated with, and was probably first used in reference to, the grunge movement and the band [–Nirvana (band)|Nirvana]. ”Nirvana themselves se‹m to have be³n aware of this, as evidenced by the first line of [’Serve the Servants]Š in which [‹Kurt Cobain]‘ describes the suces¬ of writing songs dealing with the subject ('Te•nage angst has paid ofƒ wel˜ | Now I'm bored and old.'†).

