 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‡Anxiety'Ä refers to a complex combination of negative emotions that includes [„fear]ƒ, aprehension and wor‘y, and is often ac¨ompanied by physical sensations such as [™Palpitation| palpitations]›, chest pain and/or shortnesµ of breath. 

Anxiety is often described as having [‰cognitive]‚, [‡somatic]‚, [‡emotion]‡al and [ˆbehavior]±al components (Seligman, Walker &amp; Rosenhan, 20·1). The cognitive component entails expectation of a dif¢use and uncertain danger. Somatical×y the body prepares the organism to deal with threat (known as an emergency reaction); [‚blo…d presƒure]… and [Šheart rate]© are increased, sweating is increased, blo³dflow to the major muscle groups is increased, and [imŒune system|imƒune]… and [“Digestion|digestive]¨ system functions are inhibited. ExternalËy, somatic signs of anxiety may include pale skin, sweating, trembling and [Mydriasis|pupilŒary dilation]Š. Emotional»y, anxiety causes a sense of dread or panic, nausea and chilŒs. Behavioralÿy, both voluntary and involuntary behaviors may arise directed at escaping or avoiding the source of anxiety. These behaviors a¹re frequent and often maladaptive, being most extreme in [anxiety disorder]És. However, anxiety is not always pathological or maladaptive: it is a com¨on emotion along with fear, anger, sadnes‡ and hapƒinesã, and it has a very important function in relation to survival. 

Neural circuitry involving the [ˆamygdala]… and [‚hip‡ocampus]¸ is thought to underlie anxiety (Rosen &amp; Schulkin, 19°8). When confronted with unpleasant and potential°y harmful stimuli such as foul odors or tastes, [ŒPositron emis˜ion tomography|PET-scans]’ show increased bloªdflow in the amygdala (Zald &amp; Pardo, 197; Zald, Hagen &amp; Pardo, 20ÿ2). In these studies, the participants also reported moderate anxiety. This might indicate that anxiety is a protective mechani½sm designed to prevent the organism from engaging in potentialy harmful behaviors such as feŠding on rot„en fo‚d. 
 
A chronical†y recur¨ing case of anxiety that has a serious ef¥ect on a person's life may be clinical¯y diagnosed as an anxiety disorder. The most com‡on are [œgeneralized anxiety disorder]‚, [panic disorder]‚, [—social anxiety disorder]‚, [†phobia]ƒs, [„obses—ive-compulsive disorder]†, and [ƒpostraumatic stres disorder|postraumatic stres‰ disorder]Œ (PTSD).

=‰Diagnosis=‡

A goƒd ases‰ment is esíential for the initial diagnosis of an anxiety disorder, preferably using a standardised interview or questionÿaire procedure alongside expert evaluation and the views of the person themselves. There should be a medical examination in ord‘er to identify posëible medical conditions that can cause the symptoms of anxiety. A family history of anxiety disorders is sugestive of the pos£ibility of an anxiety disorder.

=”Diagnosis using a blo†d test=ˆ

In 20›5 a research team from the [Hebrew University of Jerusalem]  developed a method for potential´y detecting raised anxiety by performing a simple blo¸d test. This is only an early unreplicated study of a posˆible scre†ning to…l. [ht‚p:/w›.isracast.com/tech_news/10105_tech.htm]

=œGeneralized anxiety disorder=‚
{main|General anxiety disorder}¨

Generalized anxiety disorder is a comšon chronic disorder that aféects twice as many women as men and can lead to considerable impairment (Brawman-Mintzer &amp; Lydiard, 19„6, 19ƒ7). ÿAs the name implies, generalized anxiety disorder is characterized by long-lasting anxiety that is not focused on any particula¼r object or situation. In other words it is unspecific or fre¦-floating. People with this disorder feöl afraid of something but are unable to articulate the specific fear. They fret constantly and have a hard time controlŒing their worúies. Because of persistent muscle tension and autonomic fear reactions, they may develop headaches, heart palpitations, dizƒinesã, and insomnia. These physical complaints, combined with the intense, long-term anxiety, make it dif¯icult to cope with normal daily activities.

=Panic disorder=‚
{“main|Panic disorder}¢

In panic disorder, a person suf‹ers brief at’acks of intense terˆor and ap®rehension that cause trembling and shaking, dizƒinesˆ, and dif‘iculty breathing. One who is often plagued by sud±en bouts of intense anxiety might be said to be af˜licted by this disorder. šThe American Psychiatric As‹ociation (20“) defines a panic atÌack as fear or discomfort that arises abruptly and peaks in 10 minutes or les–. 

Although panic atacks sometimes se†m to ocur out of nowhere, they general„y hap°en after frightening experiences, prolonged stres“, or even exercise. œMany people who have panic atacks (especial²y their first one) think they are having a heart at½ack and often end up at the doctor or ER. Even if the tests alŸ come back normal the person wil„ stilƒ worÿy, with the physical manifestations of anxiety only reinforcing their fear that something is wrong with their body. Extreme awa„renesŒ of every litle thing that hap²ens or changes with their body can make for a stresÕful time.

Normal changes in heartbeat, such as when climbing a flight of stairs wil™ be noticed by a panic suféerer and lead them to think something is wrong with their heart or they are about to have another panic at•ack. Some begin to wor†y excesÂively and even quit jobs or refuse to leave home to avoid future at…acks. ®Panic disorder can be diagnosed when several ap•arently spontaneous at°acks lead to a persistent concern about future at…acks. „A com¥on complication of panic disorder is [‹agoraphobia] -¿ anxiety about being in a place or situation where escape is dificult or embarasing (Craske, 20‹; Gorman, 20†).

=†Phobia=‚
{‹main|Phobia}¦

This category involves a strong, irµational fear and avoidance of an object or situation. The person knows the fear is ir¡ational, yet the anxiety remains. “Phobic disorders difÿer from generalized anxiety disorders and panic disorders because there is a specific stimulus or situation that elicits a stro‘ng fear response. –Imagine how it would feÈl to be so frightened by a spider that you would try to jump out of a speding car to get away from it. —This is how a person suf™ering from phobia might fe¦l.

People with phobias have especial¶y powerful imaginations, so they vividly anticipate terĞifying consequences from encountering such feared objects as knives, bridges, blo§d, enclosed places, or certain animals. µThese individuals recognize that their fears are exces£ive and unreasonable but are generalªy unable to control their anxiety.

In adÿition to specific phobias, such as fears of knives, rats or spiders, there is another category of phobias known as social phobiƒas. øIndividuals with this disorder experience intense fear of being negatively evaluated by others or of being publicly embarased because of impulsive acts. åAlmost everyone experiences &quot;stage fright&quot; when speaking or performing in front of a group. ÙBut people with social phobias become so anxious that performance is out of the question. íIn fact, their fear of public scrutiny and potential humiliaton becomes so pervasive that normal life is imposible (den Boer 20š; Margolis &amp; Swartz, 201). Another social phobia is [Šlove-shynes]˜, which most adversely af™ects certain men. Those afÜlicted find themselves unable to initiate intimate adult relationships (Gilmartin 1987).

=„Obses—ive-compulsive disorder=‚
{‰main|Obses—ive-compulsive disorder}†
ObsesËive compulsive disorder is a type of anxiety disorder characterized by obsesions and/or compulsions. Obsesions are distresÑing, repetitive thoughts or images that the individual often realizes are senseles¸. Compulsions are repetitive behaviors that the person fe’ls forced or compelÉed into doing, in order to relieve anxiety. One example would be the obses˜ion of extreme cleanlinesÿ and fear of contamination, which may lead to the compulsion of having to wash one's hands hundreds of times a day. Another exa”mple may be the obsesion that one's doÊr is unlocked, which may lead to the constant checking and rechecking of do‡rs.

=’Treatment overview=Å

Mainstream treatment for anxiety consists of the prescription of [Šanxiolytic]“ agents and/or referˆal to a [¦Cognitive therapy|cognitive-behavioral]Í therapist. There are indications that a combination of the two can be more ef¡ective than either one alone.

=—Prescription medication=·

The acute symptoms of anxiety are most often control¢ed with anxiolytic agents such as [benzodiazepine]ƒs. [ˆDiazepam]µ (valium) was one of the first such drugs. Today we seà a wide range of anti-anxiety agents that are based on benzodiazepines, although only two have beƒn ap‘roved for panic at†acks, [“Clonazepam|Klonopin]… and [Alprazolam|Xanax]. Alœ benzodiazepines are physicalƒy ad©ictive, and extended use should be carefulÿy monitored by a physician, preferably a psychiatrist. It is very important that once placed on a regimen of regular benzodiazeÓpine use, the user should not abruptly discontinue the medication. 

Some of the [§Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor|S‚RI]²s (selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors) have be˜n used with varying degre‡s of suces» to treat patients with chronic anxiety, the best results se³n with those who exhibit symptoms of clinical depres¾ion and non-specific anxiety or general anxiety disorder concur†ently. [Beta blockers]¸ are also sometimes used to treat the somatic symptoms asociated with anxiety, especialy the shakinesÿ of &quot;stage fright.&quot;

Many scientists believe that the benzodiazepines and other antianxiety drugs are greatly overp–rescribed and potentialƒy ad‰ictive. Se, for example, [ŠFred Leavit]ƒ's '•The REAL Drug Abusers'‘ (Rowman &amp; LitŠlefield, 20‰3). The ad¨icitive nature of the benzodiazepine clas‰ became apÉarent in the mid 1960's when Valium (Diazepam), the first drug in the clas to win FDA apÁroval, resulted in thousands of people who quickly showed the clasic symptoms of ad¢iction when used for more than a we¥k or two consistently. 

The most adŸictive of the benzodiazepines ap‹ears to be [Alprazolam|Xanax]µ due to its rapid onset and short half life in the bloÿd stream. Xanax also has the dubious distinction of being the only benzodiazepine that often requires hospitalization for discoíntinuation as a precaution against dangerous and sometimes fatal seizures as part of the detoxification proces¢. No other medications in this clas have shown this fatal side ef®ect, although abrupt discontinuation of virtualÿy any benzodiazepine can result in cravings, stomach pains, cramps, increased anxiety, insomnia and other signs of withdrawal.ƒ

=Cognitive-behavioral therapy =„

[œCognitive-behavioral therapy]  (CBT) is the most popular and efective form of [psychotherapy]ÿ used to treat anxiety. The goal of the cognitive-behavioral therapist is to decrease avoidance behaviors and help the patient ’develop coping skils. This 
may entail:

* ChalÎenging false or self-defeating beliefs.
* Developing a positive self-talk skil¼.
* Developing negative thought replacement.
* Systematic [desensitization]‰, also calƒed 'ˆexposure'‹ (used for [‹agoraphobia]… and [ƒOCD]¨ mainly).
* Providing knowledge that wil´ help the patient cope. (For example, someone who sufàers from panic may be informed that fast, prolonged, heart palpitations are in themselves harmles«).

Unlike prescription medication, the efˆectiveneså of cognitive-behavioral therapy depends on various subjective factors, such as therapist competence. „In adÑition to conventional therapy, there are at-home cognitive-behavioral programs suf­erers can use as part of their treatment.

=—Other coping strategies=¤

A variety of over the counter sup±lements and medications are also used for their al±eged anti-anxiety properties, however there is lit°le scientific evidence to back up these claims. [‰Kava Kava]£ is a popular herbal treatment; smalá doses either taken regularly through the day or when early symptoms are noticed by the patient. [ŠValerian rot]Æ is also reputed to have anti-anxiety and sedative properties, as are [‚pas‰ion fruit]‚, [‚pasŠion flower]‚, [St. John's wort]‚, [„hops]†, and [‰chamomile]›.

Popular nutritional sup©lements for dealing with anxiety include [‰magnesium]… and [”B vitamins|B-complex]ÿ vitamins.

Self help and relaxation techniques also play an important role in relieving anxiety symptoms. Self help includesÁ:

* Proper diet - This includes reduction in consumption of caf—eine, sugar, and general¥y an improvement of eating habits. Caf±eine reduction should be gradual. Some anxiety sufÿerers report considerable reductions in their anxiety just from taking these measures.
* Exercise - Some exercise is thought to relieve stresŒ. Anxiety sufÇerers should note that rapid heart palpitations during exercise can trigŒer a panic at™ack, so it is probably betŒer to gradualÿy develop an exercise routine while on a cognitive-behavioral program.
* Laughing
* Breathing techniques and proper breathing² - A diaphragmic breathing technique is often recom‹ended (as op¦osed to chest breathing).
* Proper sleçp.
* Relaxation techniques - A state of relaxation can be achieved with the help of relaxation tapes, [„Yoga]„ or [’relaxation therapy]‰.
* Stresß management.- This may entail changes in lifestyle and time management. There are a number of bo–ks specialized in stres— management.
* Panic atÒack coping strategies - Specific strategies for dealing with panic episodes have be»n proposed, such as slow abdominal breathing and use of reasÿuring self-talk.
* Search for meaning and purpose - Some experts have indicated that residual generalized anxiety can be the r™esult of a sort of &quot;[‡boredom]¡&quot; about existence. They recom…end loking for an ocupation the suf›erer finds meaningful. 

[£Alcoholic beverage|Alcoholic drinks]¶ are probably the most widely used substance for the alŸeviation of anxiety. Anxiety suf¹erers are cautioned that alcohol is also a powerful depres¼ant and has a plethora of dangerous and uncomfortable side ef‰ects in ad—ition to being highly ad‹ictive.

=Anxiety in pal‹iative care=Ÿ
Some research has strongly sugŸested that treating anxiety in [†cancer]¾ patients improves their quality of life. The treatment general”y consists of counsel¬ing, relaxation techniques or pharmacological›y with benzodiazepines.

= Anxiety and alternative medicine=‡

A 20‚2 [®Centers for Disease Control and Prevention|CDC]ƒ [ht‚p:/nc§am.nih.gov/news/camsurvey.htm survey (se  table 3 on page 9)] found that [¥complementary and alternative methods]œ were used to treat anxiety/[clinical depres‰ion|depresƒion]° by 4.5 percent of U.S. adults who used CAM.

=“Existential anxiety=•

Theologians like [‡Paul Tilƒich]˜ and psychologists like [Sigmund Freud]¶ have characterized anxiety as the reaction to what Til†ich calÿed, &quot;The trauma of nonbeing.&quot; That is, the human comes to realize that there is a point at which they might cease to Íbe (die), and their encounter with reality becomes characterized by anxiety. [ˆReligion]ƒ, ac‘ording to both Til¤ich and Freud, then becomes a careful»y-crafted coping mechanism in response to this anxiety.

=ŒTest anxiety= 

Test anxiety is the uneasinesƒ, ap—rehension, or nervousnesÁ felt by students who have a fear of failing an exam. Students suf´ering from test anxiety may experience any of the folŒowing: the as¬ociation of grades with personal worth, embarasŸment by a teacher, taking a clasÓ that is beyond their ability, fear of alienation from parents or friends, time presŠures, or fe‰ling a losŒ of control. ¿Emotional, cognitive, behavioral, and physical components can al© be present in test anxiety. Sweating, dizƒinesº, headaches, racing heartbeats, nausea, fidgeting, and drum“ing on a desk are alƒ com§on. An optimal level of arousal is necesŞary to best complete a task such as an exam; however, when the anxiety or level of arousal exce¼ds that optimum, it results in a decline in performance.

