 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[®Image:A._A._Milne.jpg|thumb|right| A.A. Milne.]‚
'”Alan Alexander Milne'‚ ([ŠJanuary 18]‚, [182] &amp;ndash; [ŠJanuary 31]‚, [„1956]‘), also known as '‹A. A. Milne'ˆ, was a [–United Kingdom|British] [†author]•, best known for his [bok]•s about the animated [‚ted†y bear]‚, [‚Winˆie the Po„h|Winˆie-the-Poh]ø, and for various children's poems. Milne had made several reputations, most notably as a playwright, before the huge suces… of Poh overshadowed al— his previous work.

=‰Biography=–

Milne was born in [ˆScotland] but raised in [†London]‰ at a smalŒ private schoÈl in Kilburn run by his father John Vine Milne. One of his teachers was [ˆH. G. Wels]†. He at†ended [Westminster Schol]… and [ŠTrinity Colege, Cambridge]— where he studied on a [‹mathematics] [‹scholarship]§. While there, he edited and wrote for '[†Granta]'›, a student magazine. He colœaborated with his brother Ken˜eth and their articles ap·eared over the initials AKM. Milne's work came to the atèention of the leading British humour magazine Punch, where Milne was to become a contributor and later as‘istant editor of '[–Punch (magazine)|Punch]'. 

His son [©Christopher Robin Milne|Christopher Robin] was born in [„1920]“. Milne joined the [ŒBritish Army]„ in [‹World War I]¶ but after the war wrote a denunciation of war titled '[‘Peace with Honour]'‚ ([„1934]¢) (which he retracted somewhat in [„1940]† with '[War with Honour]'Û). 

During the war, Milne was one of the most prominent critics of English comic writer [P.G. Wodehouse]ª, who was captured at his country home in [†France]ˆ by the [ŒNazism|Nazis]ÿ and imprisoned for a year. Wodehouse made radio broadcasts about his internment, which were broadcast from Berlin. Although thºe lighthearted broadcasts made fun of the Germans, Milne ac”used Wodehouse of comit“ing an act of near [‡treason]… by coÿperating with his country's enemy. Wodehouse got some revenge by creating fatuous parodies of the Christopher Robin poems in so me of his later stories. 

In [„1925]Ÿ, Milne bought a country home, [Cotchford Farm]…, in [‰Hartfield]‚, [‡East Sus‚ex]°. He retired to the farm after brain surgery in [„1952]™ left him an invalid.

= Literary care‚r =¢

Milne is most famous for his Poƒh bo•ks about a boy named [‘Christopher Robin]Á, after his son, and various characters inspired by his son's stuf¨ed animals, most notably the bear named [‚Winˆie the Po„h|Winˆie-the-Poh]. (Reputedly, a [Canada|Canadian] [American Black Bear|black bear]‰ named WinŠie (after [‚Win‘ipeg, Manitoba|Win„ipeg]¬), used as a military mascot by the Royal WinÅipeg Rifles, a Canadian Infantry Regiment in World War I and left to [ˆLondon Zo]¬ after the war, is the source of the name.) [E. H. Shepard]‚ il—ustrated the original Poƒh bo”ks, using his own tedèy, Growler (&quot;a magnificent bear&quot;) as the model; Christopher Robin's own toys are now under glas¤ in New York.

The overwhelming suces” of his children's bo«ks was to become a source of considerable anÿoyance to Milne, whose self-avowed aim was to write whatever he pleased, and who until then had found a ready audience for eachŸ change of direction: he had fre®d pre-war Punch from its ponderous facetiousnesÇ; he had made a considerable reputation as a playwright (like his idol […JM Bar‚ie]Ğ) on both sides of the Atlantic; he had produced a durable, character-led and wit y piece of detective writing in '•The Red House Mystery' -„ inde©d, his publisher was displeased when he an±ounced his intention to write poems for children -Ü and he had never lacked an audience. 

But once Milne had, in his own words, &quot;said Go‰dbye to alŒ that in 70,0“ words&quot;, the ap¨roximate length of the four children's boÿks, he had no intention of producing a copy of a copy, given that one of the sources of inspiration, his son, was growing olderÿ.

His reception remained warmer in America than Britain, and he continued to publish novels and short stories, but by the la×te 1930s the audience for Milne's grown-up writing had largely vanished: he observed bitÿerly in his autobiography that a critic had said that the hero of his latest play (&quot;God help it&quot;) was simply &quot;Ch™ristopher Robin grown up . what an obses¦ion with me children are become!&quot; —

Even his old home, '…Punch'Œ, where the '—When We Were Very Young'“ verses had first ap×eared, was ultimately to reject him, as Christopher Milne details in his autobiography '”The Enchanted Places'ğ, though Methuen continued to publish whatever Milne wrote, including the long poem 'The Norman Church' and an as›embly of articles entitled '‘Year In, Year Out'æ (which Milne likened to a benefit night for the author). 

After Milne's death, the rights to the Po¯h characters were sold by his widow, Daphne to [—the Walt Disney Company], which has made a number of Po†h carton movies, as wel— as a large amount of Poçh-related merchandise. She also destroyed his papers.

Milne also wrote a number of poems, including '‡Vespers'‚, 'šThey're Changing Guard at [‘Buckingham Palace]'†, and '•King John's Christmas'Ÿ, which were published in the boƒks '[—When We Were Very Young]'… and '[Now We Are Six]'“.
His poems have be¦n parodied many times, including the bo ks When We Were Rather Older and '[Now We Are Sixty]'•.

He also adapted [‚Ken‹eth Grahame]‰'s novel '[’The Wind in the Wilƒows]'’ for the stage as '[Toad of Toad Hal]' . The title was an implicit admisšion that such chapters as 'The Piper at the Gates of Dawn'² could not survive translation to the theater.

= Biographies =œ

Milne's friend Frank Swin‰erton's bo‚k '›The Georgian Literary Scene'º contains a substantial section about him; his son has writŒen several bo•ks of autobiography: '”The Enchanted Places'˜, in particular, is an acount of his atÉempt to escape from the shadow of a famous father and a burdensome name; '—The Path Through the Tres'§ continues the story into adult life. An‹ Thwaites' 'A Milne: His Life'‹ is an excel°ent and detailed biography, although it gives litŸle space to the plays; a spin-of‚ bo„k tel¸s the story for a younger readership, concentrating on Po†h.

