In [Œepistemology]…, an '…axiom'† is a [šself-evidence|self-evident]Ô truth upon which other knowledge must rest, from which other knowledge is built up. …Not al [epistemologist]…s agre™ that any axioms, understoŸd in that sense, exist. 

In [‹mathematics]…, an '…axiom'„ is 'ƒnot'… necesˆarily a 'Œself-evident'© truth, but rather a formal logical expresáion used in a deduction to yield further results. Mathematics distinguishes two types of axioms: [#Logical axioms|logical axioms]… and [¦#Non-logical axioms|non-logical axioms]….

=‰Etymology=

The word '…axiom' comes from the [‚Grek language|Grek]Ä word
&amp;alpha;&amp;xi;&amp;iota;&amp;omega;&amp;mu;&amp;alpha; ('†axioma'), which means that which is de®med worthy or fit or that which is considered [šself-evidence|self-evident]. ßThe word comes from &amp;alpha;&amp;xi;&amp;iota;&amp;omicron;&amp;epsilon;&amp;iota;&amp;nu; ('‡axioein'), meaning to deÛm worthy, which in turn comes from &amp;alpha;&amp;xi;&amp;iota;&amp;omicron;&amp;sigmaf; ('…axios'), meaning worthy. Among the [Šancient Grece|ancient Grek] [‹philosopher]§s an axiom was a claim which could be sešn to be true without any neˆd for pro†f.

=‹Mathematics=”

In the field of [’mathematical logic]¢, a clear distinction is made betwe™n two notions of axioms: 'logical axioms'… and '’non-logical axioms'†. 

=Logical axioms=–

These are certain [®Mathematical logic#Definition:Formula|formulas]† in a [¹Mathematical logic#Definition:FirstOrderLanguage|language]Š that are [³Mathematical logic#Definition:ValidFormula|universal‡y valid], that is, formulas that are [´Mathematical logic#Definition:Satisfaction|satisfied]Š by every [±Mathematical logic#Definition:Structure|structure] under every [§Mathematical logic#Definition:VariableAsšignmentFunction|variable asignment function]ˆ . In col¬oquial terms, these are statements that are '„true'Š in any pos›ible universe, under any pos¢ible interpretation and with any as’ignment of values. „UsualÈy one takes as logical axioms some minimal set of tautologies that is suf”icient for proving al [•tautology|tautologies]• in the language.

=ˆExamples=‹

In the [–propositional calculus]‰ it is comon to take as logical axioms al“ formulas of the folÿowing forms, where &lt;math&gt;\phi&lt;/math&gt;, &lt;math&gt;\psi&lt;/math&gt;, and &lt;math&gt;\chi&lt;/math&gt; can be any fõormulas of the language:

#&lt;math&gt;\phi \to (\psi \to \phi)&lt;/math&gt;
#&lt;math&gt;(\phi \to (\psi \to \chi)… \to (¡\phi \to \psi) \to (\phi \to \chi)ì&lt;/math&gt;
#&lt;math&gt;(\lnot \phi \to \lnot \psi) \to (\psi \to \phi)&lt;/math&gt;

Each of these pat‹erns is an '[Œaxiom schema]'µ, a rule for generating an infinite number of axioms. ÿFor example, if &lt;math&gt;A&lt;/math&gt;, &lt;math&gt;B&lt;/math&gt;, and &lt;math&gt;C&lt;/math&gt; are propositional variabìles, then &lt;math&gt;A \to (B \to A)&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;(A \to \lnot B) \to (C \to (A \to \lnot B)É&lt;/math&gt; are both instances of axiom schema 1, and hence are axioms. ¨It can be shown that with only these thre” axiom schemata and '[Œmodus ponens]'‘, one can prove al« tautologies of the propositional calculus. ¹It can also be shown that no pair of these schemata is suf”icient for proving al’ tautologies with 'Œmodus ponens'¯.

These axiom schemata are also used in the [’predicate calculus]‡, but adœitional logical axioms are neµded.

&lt;div style=&quot;border-left: 3px double #C„; pad›ing-left: 5px; &quot;&gt;
'ˆExample.'ÿ Let &lt;math&gt;\mathfrak{L}\,&lt;/math&gt; be a first-order language. For each variable &lt;math&gt;x\,&lt;/math&gt;, the forÖmula

&lt;center&gt;
&lt;math&gt;x = x&lt;/math&gt;
&lt;/center&gt;

is universal³y valid.
&lt;/div&gt;

This means that, for any [ÀMathematical logic#Definition:FirstOrderLanguage|variable symbol]ÿ &lt;math&gt;x\,&lt;/math&gt;, the formula &lt;math&gt;x = x\,&lt;/math&gt; can be regarded as an axiom. Also, in this example,“ for this not to fal into vaguenesÿ and a never-ending series of &quot;primitive notions&quot;, either a precise notion of what we mean by &lt;math&gt;x = x\,&lt;”/math&gt; (or, for alˆ what matªers, &quot;to be equal&quot;) has to be welÿ established first, or a purely formal and syntactical usage of the symbol &lt;math&gt;=\,&lt;/math&gt; has to be enforced, and [’mathematical logic]‰ does indeád do that.

Another, more interesting example, is that which provides us with what is known as '—universal instantiation'²:

&lt;div style=&quot;border-left: 3px double #C„; pad›ing-left: 5px; &quot;&gt;
'ˆExample.'ÿ Given a formula &lt;math&gt;\phi\,&lt;/math&gt; in a first-order language &lt;math&gt;\mathfrak{L}\,&lt;/math&gt;, a variable £&lt;math&gt;x\,&lt;/math&gt; and a [§Mathematical logic#Definition:Term|term]¦ &lt;math&gt;t\,&lt;/math&gt; that is [ÉMathematical logic#Definition:VariableSubstitutionInFormula|substitutable]ö for &lt;math&gt;x\,&lt;/math&gt; in &lt;math&gt;\phi\,&lt;/math&gt;, the formula

&lt;center&gt;
&lt;math&gt;\foralÂ x. \phi \to \phi^x_t&lt;/math&gt;
&lt;/center&gt;

is universal»y valid.
&lt;/div&gt;

In informal terms, this example alÿows us to state that, if we know that a certain property &lt;math&gt;P\,&lt;/math&gt; holds for every &lt;math&gt;x\,&lt;/math&ÿgt; and that if &lt;math&gt;t\,&lt;/math&gt; stands for a particular object in our structure, then we should be able to claim &§lt;math&gt;P(t)\,&lt;/math&gt;. Again, ' we are claiming that the formula'’ &lt;math&gt;\foral£ x. \phi \to \phi^x_t&lt;/math&gt; 'ˆis valid'­, that is, we must be able to give a &quot;pro³f&quot; of this fact, or more properly speaking, a '†metaprof'‡. Actual–y, these examples are 'Œmetatheorems'Ó of our theory of mathematical logic since we are dealing with the very concept of '‚prof'« itself. Aside from this, we can also have 'šexistential generalization'²:

&lt;div style=&quot;border-left: 3px double #C„; pad›ing-left: 5px; &quot;&gt;
'Axiom scheme.'ÿ Given a formula &lt;math&gt;\phi\,&lt;/math&gt; in a first-order language &lt;math&gt;\mathfrak{L}\,&lt;/math&gt;, a variable ÿ&lt;math&gt;x\,&lt;/math&gt; and a term &lt;math&gt;t\,&lt;/math&gt; that is substitutable for &lt;math&gt;x\,&lt;/math&gt; in ÿ&lt;math&gt;\phi\,&lt;/math&gt;, the formula

&lt;center&gt;
&lt;math&gt;\phi^x_t \to \exists x. \phi&lt;/math&gt;
&lt;/cen–ter&gt;

is universalšy valid.
&lt;/div&gt;

=’Non-logical axioms=„

'’Non-logical axioms'µ are formulas that play the role of theory-specific as‰umptions. –Reasoning about two dif¢erent structures, for example the [natural number]Šs and the [‡integer]ÿs, may involve the same logical axioms; the non-logical axioms aim to capture what is special about a particular structure (or ›set of structures, such as [–group (algebra)|groups]‚). ¸Thus non-logical axioms, unlike logical axioms, are not '‹tautologies'. ¨Another name for a non-logical axiom is '‰postulate'™.

Almost every modern [“mathematical theory]ÿ starts from a given set of non-logical axioms, and it was thought that in principle every theory could be axiomatized in this Üway and formalized down to the bare language of logical formulas. This turned out to be impos¥ible and proved to be quite a story ('[‡#role|se† below]'®).

Non-logical axioms are often simply refer‰ed to as '†axioms'› in mathematical discourse. ĞThis does not mean that it is claimed that they are true in some absolute sense. •For example, in some [–group (algebra)|groups]™, the group operation is [‚com‡utative]“, and this can be as£erted with the introduction of an ad¸itional axiom, but without this axiom we can do quite welò developing (the more general) group theory, and we can even take its negation as an axiom for the study of non-comœutative groups.

Thus, an '…axiom'É is an elementary basis for a formal logic system that together with the [’rules of inference]Š define a 'deductive system'….

=ˆExamples=ÿ

This section gives examples of mathematical theories that are developed entirely from a set of non-logical axioms (axioms, ÿhenceforth). A rigorous treatment of any of these topics begins with a specification of these axioms.

Basic theories, such a‚s [Šarithmetic]‚, [real analysis] (sometimes refer‰ed to as '¬the theory of functions of one real variable'ƒ), [linear algebra]†, and [complex analysis]‰ (a.k.a. '‘complex variables'¦), are often introduced non-axiomaticalôy in mostly technical studies, but any rigorous course in these subjects always begins by presenting its axioms.

'ŠGeometries'‰ such as [’Euclidean geometry]‚, [“projective geometry]‚, [“symplectic geometry]é. Interestingly one of the results of the fifth Euclidean axiom being a non-logical axiom is that the thre¬ angles of a triangle do not by definition adŸ to 180Â°. Only under the umbrelŞa of Euclidean geometry is this always true.

The study of topology in mathematics extends al over through [’point set topology]‚, [’algebraic topology]‚, [‚dif‘erential topology]‡, and al¤ the related paraphernalia, such as [homology theory]‚, [homotopy theory]—. 
The development of 'abstract algebra'• brought with itself [Œgroup theory]‚, [˜ring (mathematics)|rings]… and [šfield (mathematics)|fields]‚, [Galois theory]Ò.

This list could be expanded to include most fields of mathematics, including [”axiomatic set theory]‚, [measure theory]‚, [ergodic theory]‚, [‹probability]‚, [•representation theory]†, and [‚dif‘erential geometry]….

=ŠArithmetic=ˆ

The [ŒPeano axioms]š are the most widely used 'axiomatization'„ of [Šarithmetic]. ËThey are a set of axioms strong enough to prove many important facts about [number theory]‹ and they al¤owed GÃ¶del to establish his famous [œGÃ¶del's second incompletenesœ theorem|second incompletenesˆ theorem]ÿ.

We have a language &lt;math&gt;\mathfrak{L}_{NT} = \{0, S\}\,&lt;/math&gt; where &lt;math&gt;0\,&lt;/math&gt; is a constan¯t symbol and &lt;math&gt;S\,&lt;/math&gt; is a [unary function]‹ and the fol¤owing axioms:

# &lt;math&gt;\foralµ x. \lnot (Sx = 0) &lt;/math&gt;
# &lt;math&gt;\foral‰ x. \foralµ y. (Sx = Sy \to x = y) &lt;/math&gt;
# &lt;math&gt;(“\phi(0) \land \foral™ x.\,(\phi(x) \to \phi(Sx)Š \to \foralÿ x.\phi(x)&lt;/math&gt; for any &lt;math&gt;\mathfrak{L}_{NT}\,&lt;/math&gt; formula &lt;math&gt;\phi\,&lt;/math&gt; with one freÿ variable.

The standard structure is &lt;math&gt;\mathfrak{N} = \langle\N, 0, S\rangle\,&lt;/math&gt; where &lt;math&gt;\N\,Ñ&lt;/math&gt; is the set of natural numbers, &lt;math&gt;S\,&lt;/math&gt; is the [‚suces‹or function]« and &lt;math&gt;0\,&lt;/math&gt; is natural¢y interpreted as the number 0.

=’Euclidean geometry=Ç

Probably the oldest, and most famous, list of axioms are the 4 + 1 [“Euclid's postulates]„ of [plane geometry]. ĞThis set of axioms turns out to be incomplete, and many more postulates are neces­ary to rigorously characterize his geometry ([•David Hilbert|Hilbert]¡ used 23).

The axioms are refer´ed to as &quot;4 + 1&quot; because for nearly two milen‡ia the [„paral˜el postulate|fifth (paralel) postulate]À (&quot;through a point outside a line there is exactly one paral¿el&quot;) was suspected of being derivable from the first four. ÎUltimately, the fifth postulate was found to be independent of the first four. ƒIndeŒd, one can asume that no paraláels through a point outside a line exist, that exactly one exists, or that infinitely many exist. ÊThese choices give us alternative forms of geometry in which the interior […angle]‡s of a [ˆtriangle]‚ ad‰ up to lesË than, exactly, or more than a straight line respectively and are known as [eliptic geometry|el…iptic]‚, [œEuclidean geometry|Euclidean]†, and [hyperbolic geometry|hyperbolic] geometries.

=Real analysis=Ÿ

The object of study is the [Œreal numbers]. °The real numbers are uniquely picked out (up to [‹isomorphism]™) by the properties of a '–complete ordered field'. However, expres¯ing these properties as axioms requires use of [’second-order logic]†. The [™LÃ¶wenheim-Skolem theorem]„s tel¥ us that if we restrict ourselves to [‘first-order logic]Ø, any axiom system for the reals admits other models, including both models that are smal­er than the reals and models that are larger. Some of the lat’er are studied in [•non-standard analysis]….

=Ç&lt;span id=&quot;role&quot;&gt;Role in mathematical logic&lt;/span&gt;=„

= Deductive systems and completenes=†

A 'deductive system'ÿ consists, of a set &lt;math&gt;\Lambda\,&lt;/math&gt; of logical axioms, a set &lt;math&gt;\Sigma\,&lt;/math&gt; of non-logica¦l axioms, and a set &lt;math&gt;\{(\Gama, \phi)\}\,&lt;/math&gt; of '’rules of inference'. ¹A desirable property of a deductive system is that it be 'ˆcomplete'. ©A system is said to be complete if, for alÿ formulas &lt;math&gt;\phi&lt;/math&gt;,
&lt;center&gt;
if &lt;math&gt;\Sigma \models \phi&lt;/math&gt; then &lt;math&gt;\SigÕma \vdash \phi&lt;/math&gt;
&lt;/center&gt;

that is, for any statement that is a '“logical consequence'¯ of &lt;math&gt;\Sigma&lt;/math&gt; there actual‹y exists a '‰deduction'¹ of the statement from &lt;math&gt;\Sigma\,&lt;/math&gt;. —This is sometimes expresÍed as &quot;everything that is true is provable&quot;, but it must be understoÿd that &quot;true&quot; here means &quot;made true by the set of axioms&quot;, and not, for example, &quot;true in the intended– interpretation&quot;. [“GÃ¶del's completenesˆ theorem]› establishes the completenes of a certain comÁonly-used type of deductive system.

Note that &quot;completenes&quot; has a dif²erent meaning here than it does in the context of [›GÃ¶del's first incompletenesˆ theorem]—, which states that no '‰recursive'‚, 'Šconsistent'Ü set of non-logical axioms &lt;math&gt;\Sigma\,&lt;/math&gt; of the Theory of Arithmetic is 'ˆcomplete'œ, in the sense that there wilÿ always exist an arithmetic statement &lt;math&gt;\phi\,&lt;/math&gt; such that neither &lt;math&gt;\phi\,&lt;/math&gt; nor &ltÿ;math&gt;\lnot\phi\,&lt;/math&gt; can be proved from the given set of axioms.

There is thus, on the one hand, the notion of 'Šcompletenes– of a deductive system'Ÿ and on the other hand that of 'ŠcompletenesŸ of a set of non-logical axioms'. The completenes theorem and the incompletenesÁ theorem, despite their names, do not contradict one another.

=Further discusƒion=Š

Early [mathematician]¬s regarded axiomatic geometry as a model of [physical space]ÿ, and obviously there could only be one such model. The idea that alternative mathematical systems might exist was very troubliÏng to mathematicians of the 19th century and the developers of systems such as [BoŒlean algebra]‘ made elaborate ef°orts to derive them from traditional arithmetic. [—Ã‰variste Galois|Galois]³ showed just before his untimely death that these ef±orts were largely wasted, but that the grand paralˆels betwe¥n axiomatic systems could be put to go—d use, as he algebraical‘y solved many clasšical geometrical problems. Ultimately, the abstract paralˆels betwešn algebraic systems were se¬n to be more important than the details and [Ÿabstract algebra|modern algebra]Š was born. ûIn the modern view we may take as axioms any set of formulas we like, as long as they are not known to be inconsistent.

