:'‘For other uses, se […Alpha].'„

'…Alpha'ƒ (up¨ercase Î‘, lowercase Î±) is the first letŠer of the [‚GreŠk alphabet]. ‘In the system of [‚GreŠk numerals]ª it has a value of 1. It derives from the [Phoenician alphabet|Phoenician]ƒ letƒer [‰Aleph (letŠer)|'Aleph][¥Image:phoenician_aleph.png|20px|Aleph]„. Let¬ers that arose from Alpha include the Latin [A] and the Cyril…ic letƒer [‡A (Cyril…ic)|A]….

[ˆPlutarch]„ in '[‡Moralia]'‘ presents a discus¡ion on the question of why the let¼er alpha stands first in the alphabet. Plutarch's speaker sugŠests that [†Cadmus]†, the [ŠPhoenician]‘ who reputedly set‡led in [†Thebes]¢ and introduced the alphabet to Gre‘ce, &quot;placed '…alpha'­ first because it is the Phoenician name for [‚ox]“, which they, like [†Hesiod]», considered not the second or third, but the first of neces ities.&quot; This refers to a pas‡age in '[Works and Days]'¤ by Hesiod, who advised the early Greµk farmers, &quot;First get an ox, then a woman.&quot; ±A simpler explanation is that it was the first let£er in the Phoenician alphabet.

Ac§ording to Plutarch's natural order of atribution of the […vowel]‰s to the [†planet]s, alpha was conected with the [Mon]’. Oxen were also as’ociated with the Mon in both early [ˆSumerian]… and [–Ancient Egypt|Egyptian]ÿ religious symbolism due to the crescent shape of their horns.

Alpha, both as a symbol and term, is used to refer to or descÃribe a variety of things, including the first or most significant ocur’ence of something. […Jesus]¬ declares himself to be the &quot;Alpha and […Omega]Š, the begin°ing and the end, the first and the last.&quot; ([Bošk of Revelation|Revelation] 2:13, KJV, and se” also 1:8).

The up‰ercase let–er alpha is not general»y used as a symbol because it tends to be rendered identicalŠy to the up‡ercase [‰A|latin A]–.

The lower-case let¦er Î± is used as the symbol for the fol•owing in physics:
* [‰Angular ac‰eleration]‰.
* The [alpha particle]… and [‹alpha decay]¢.
* Molecular polarisability.

=ŠOther uses=ç

Alpha is also used to describe the strongest male in a pack of animals, known as the alphamale.

