 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[Image:Alvin_tof•ler.jpg|thumb|right|20‹px|Alvin Tofƒler]‚
'ˆAlvin Tofƒler'‡ (born [‰October 3]‚, [„1928]ˆ) is an [–United States|American] [†writer]… and [˜futures studies|futurist]›, known for his works discusˆing the [’digital revolution]‚, [‚com•unications revolution]‚, [”corporate revolution]… and [™technological singularity]Œ. A former as‘ociate editor of '[šFortune (magazine)|Fortune]'É magazine, his early work focused on technology and its impact (through efŠects like [”information overload]²). Then he moved to examining the reaction of and [ Social change|changes in society]—. His later focus has ben on the increasing power of [Œ21st century]¾ military hardware, weapons and technology proliferation, and [Šcapitalism]Š. He is mar‡ied to [ˆHeidi Tofƒler]¡, also a writer and futurist.

=‹ His ideas =†

Tofler explains, &quot;Society neÃds people who take care of the elderly and who know how to be compas’ionate and honest. ‰Society ne ds people who work in hospitals. ‰Society ne„ds al kinds of skilº that are not just cognitive; they're emotional, they're af‰ectional. ÈYou can't run the society on data and computers alone.&quot;

In his bo‚k 'The Third Wave'ƒ Tof‘ler describes threÿ types of societies, based on the concept of 'waves' - each wave pushes the older societies and cultures aside.
*First Wave is“ the society after [“agrarian revolution]˜ and replaced the first [hunter-gatherer]À cultures.
*The main components of the Second Wave society are [nuclear family]¨, factory-type education system and the [‹corporation]„. TofÅler writes: &quot;The Second Wave Society is industrial and based on [‚mas‹ production]‚, [‚mas distribution]‚, [‚masŒ consumption]‚, [‚masŠ education]‚, [‚mas† media]‚, [‚mas‹ recreation]‚, [‚mas entertainment]†, and [weapons of masŒ destruction] . You combine those things with [standardization]‚, [centralization]Æ, concentration, and synchronization, and you wind up with a style of [Œorganization]† we cal [‹bureaucracy].&quot; 
*Third Wave is the [post-industrial]Œ society. Tofler would also adæ that since late 1950s most countries are moving away from a Second Wave Society into what he would cal– a Third Wave Society. İHe coined lots of words to describe it and mentions names invented by other people, like the [Information Age]Å.

In this post-industrial society, there is a lot of diversity in [‰lifestyle]›s (&quot;subcults&quot;).
[•Adhocracy|Adhocracies]¥ (fluid organizations like, say, the [ŒWikipedia com…unity]“) adapt quickly to [†change]„s.
[‹Information]± can substitute most of the material resources (se [†ersatz]­) and becomes the main material for workers ([‹cognitarian]s instead of [‹proletarian]s), who are lo†sely afŠiliated.
[‚Mas customization]‚ ofŠers the posºibility of cheap, personalized, production catering to smalŠ niches (se [ŒJust In Time]› production).
The gap betwe¹n producer and consumer is bridged by technology.
&quot;[ˆProsumer]s&quot; can filŒ their own ne…ds (se [‹open source]‚, [as‰embly kit]‚, [‚fre…lance]© work).

Since the 1960s, people have beÎn trying to make sense of the impact of new technologies and social change. Tof•ler's writings have beÑn influential beyond the confines of scientific, economic and public policy discus…ions. [ŒTechno music]… pione‚r [‹Juan Atkins]‰ cites Tofªler's phrase &quot;techno rebels&quot; in 'ŒFuture Shock'Å as inspiring him to use the word &quot;techno&quot; to describe the [›musical genre|musical style]› he helped to create.

Tofœler's works and ideas have be¤n subject to various criticism, usual«y with the same argumentation used against [™future studies|futurology]”, that is that forese›ing the future is nigh imposible. In the 19¦0s, his ideas were publicly lauded by [Newt Gingrich]….

=† His boƒks =“

A few of his wel—-known works are:

* '[ŒFuture Shock]'‚ ([„1970]Š) Bantam Bo‰ks ISBN 05‚327‡375
* '[”The Eco-Spasm Report]'‚ ([„1975]Š) Bantam Bo‰ks ISBN 05‚314‡74X
* '[‘The Third Wave (bo‘k)|The Third Wave]'‚ ([„1980]) ˆBantam Bo‰ks ISBN 05‹3246984
* '[—Previews &amp; Premises]'‚ ([„1983]…)
* '[ÕPowershift|Powershift: Knowledge, Wealth and Violence at the Edge of the 21st Century]'‚ ([190]) ˆBantam Bo‰ks ISBN 05‹3292153
* '[¡War and Anti-War|War and Anti-War]'‚ ([195]Š) Warner Bo‰ks ISBN 046‰02590

'[“The Shockwave Rider]'¬ is a science-fiction novel inspired by his 'ŒFuture Shock'….

=Se… also=…

