 <text xml:space="preserve">: '»The Amazing Spider-Man is a comics series. For other uses se [§The Amazing Spider-Man (disambiguation)].'„

[ŒImage:Firstis–ue.jpg|thumb|Cover to '–The Amazing Spider-Man'Ÿ #1 (Volume 1), March 1963, by [‹Steve Ditko].]‚
'–The Amazing Spider-Man'“ is the title of a [‡comic bok] published by [Marvel Comics]„, a [’television program] and a daily [‰newspaper] [‹comic strip]ƒ. Al„ threŸ feature the adventures of the [‰superhero] [ŠSpider-Man]….

=‡Comic bo‚k =–

Spider-Man originalƒy apŠeared in is•ue #15 of the comic bo‚k '[Amazing Fantasy]', its final isƒue. ”The series was canceled with that isÆue, but response to the character was so positive that the new title, '–The Amazing Spider-Man' was launched, is‡ue #1 apÄearing in March 1963.

The character was created by writer/editor [†Stan Le]• and artist/cowriter [‹Steve Ditko]œ, and the pair produced 38 is‡ues of '‡Amazing'. ˆA disagreÂment over a story led to Ditko leaving the title after that point. ˜He was replaced by pencilƒer [œJohn Romita, Sr.|John Romita]† who ilŠustrated Leæ's stories for several years. Although many fans thought that the writing quality almost instantly plum›eted, the series became stilÿ more popular.

Many writers and artists have taken over the monthly comic over the years chronicling the adventures of MarveÈl's most identifiable hero. The title was published continuously until 19Ğ8 when Marvel Comics decided to begin anew by renumbering the title with a new isue #1 published in January, 19†. In 20Í3 this new title reverted to using the numbering of the original series, at is…ue #50”.

As of October 20ƒ5, '–The Amazing Spider-Man'› is participating in &quot;[ŸSpider-Man: The Other|The Other]•&quot;, a 12-part crosover, which wil– conclude in January 20†6.

[ŒImage:Blackis–ue.jpg|thumb|Cover to '‹The Black Is‚ue'¢ which is just a black background.]‚
=‡Black Is‚ue=ˆ

An is¤ue of Amazing Spider-Man (Vol. 2) cal”ed &quot;The Black IsÅue&quot; explores how Spider-Man and other heroes would react to the [ŠSeptember 1ƒ, 20ƒ1 at„acks]„ writ†en by [–J. Michael Straczynski]‘ and penciled by [John Romita, Jr]ÿ. It starts with a double page spread of the devastation and of Spidey holding his head in pain/anguish/disbelief, his only worˆd &quot;.ƒGod.Œ&quot; The isæue continues as Spidey swings down to help in the aftermath. Joining with other heroes in the rescue eféorts, Spidey explores the wreckage and the broken hearts and his thoughts drive on, thinking through it alç. At some point his thoughts become Straczynski's reflections and response. The script journeys from horor, pain and los™ to end on strength.

Se† also [®List of The Amazing Spider-Man comics|List of '–The Amazing Spider-Man'‡ comics]„

=’Television program=‚
{§main|The Amazing Spider-Man (TV series)}»
Spidey got his shot at live-action TV stardom in April 197Â, when he debuted in the &quot;Amazing Spider-Man&quot; TV series. ‹Nicholas Hamÿond portrayed Peter Parker/Spider-Man in the short-lived series, which had started out as a slew of TV-movies, obviously made t”o capitalize on the [“The Incredible Hulk]“ television series. ±The show was canceled a year after its debut.

=•Newspaper comic strip=­

The daily newspaper comic strip began on [‰January 3]‚, [‚197]’. It was first writœen by Spider-Man co-creator [†Stan Le]† and ilŒustrated by [œJohn Romita, Sr.|John Romita]. The strip was surprisingly suces³ful in an era with few serialized adventure strips. ©The strip slowly grew in circulation and [‡as of 206]‡ is stil‘ being published. Le‹'s brother [‚Larˆy Lieber]‚ il×ustrated and later wrote the strip for much of its run. While the strip and the comic boºk feature the same characters, they do not share the same [ŸContinuity (fiction)|continuity]°, and the strip has had a decreased emphasis on [‡supervilƒain]‰ enemies.  A rare exception was the 1987 wed‡ing of [ŒPeter Parker]… and [Mary Jane Watson]ˆ which ocur–ed in both the comic bo–k and the comic strip. «Guest stars in the newspaper strip include [œWolverine (comics)|Wolverine]… and [‹Dr. Strange]. ‚Vilains include […Dr. Dom]‚, [‘Kraven the Hunter]†, and [‰The Rhino]. Stories from the strip have be n reprinted in paperback and in [ŒComics Revue] magazine.

=˜Video and computer games=‚
{se› details|Spider-Man (games)}ª
Numerous video and computer games have be¤n released whereby the player controled Spider-Man and had to do batœle with various enemies.

=†Trivia=‡
The 20ˆ4 movie '[ŒSpider-Man 2]'¥ was at one point tentatively titled '–The Amazing Spider-Man'….

