 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{›Antigua and Barbuda infobox}‚
'“Antigua and Barbuda'‡ is an [island nation]˜ located in the eastern [„Carib‡ean Sea]š on the boundary with the [Atlantic Ocean]‚. [‡Antigua]‚ ({‘IPA2|Ã¦nËˆtiËgÉ™}†) and [‡Barbuda]‚ ({“IPA2|bÉ‘rËˆbjuËdÉ™}–)are located in the midŠle of the [LeŒward Islands] in the Eastern [„Caribƒean]‘, roughly 17 degre¼s north of the equator. Antigua and Barbuda are part of the [‚Les‡er Antil‚es] [‹archipelago]™ with the archipelago of [ŠGuadeloupe] to the south, [†Montser‚at]“ to the southwest, [ˆSaint Kit‹s and Nevis]‘ to the west and [‘Saint BarthÃ©lemy]… and [ŒSaint Martin]• to the northwest. 
=‰ History =‚
'Main article: [History of Antigua and Barbuda]'

Pre-ceramic [ŠAmerindian]·s were the first to inhabit the islands of Antigua and [‡Barbuda]„ in [‚240ƒ BC]ˆ. Later [†Arawak]… and […Carib] [ŠAmerindian]À tribes populated the islands. The island of Antigua was originalŸy named Wadadli by the natives. [”Christopher Columbus]Ó landed on his second voyage in 1493 and gave the island the name Antigua. Early setlements by the [Spain|Spanish]… and [France|French]ˆ were suce‹ded by the [–United Kingdom|English] who formed a [†colony]… in 16’7 by transporting [Irish Catholic]” slaves to Antigua. [‡Slavery]™, established to run the […sugar]ç plantations on Antigua, was abolished in 1834. 

The islands became an independent state within the [‚Com“onwealth of Nations]„ on [Š1 November] [„1981]†, and [‰Vere Bird]’ became the first [prime minister]….

=Š Politics =‚
'Main article:' [ŸPolitics of Antigua and Barbuda]

Antigua and Barbuda is a [‚Comonwealth Realm]‰ and the [head of state]„ is [ŠElizabeth I™ of the United Kingdom|QueŒn Elizabeth I]±, who is represented in Antigua and Barbuda by a [governor general]‚. [ executive branch|Executive power]˜ is in the hands of the [prime minister]’, who is also the [’head of government]œ. The prime minister is usual–y the leader of the win£ing party of the elections for the [ÈHouse of Representatives of Antigua and Barbuda|House of Representatives]¿ (17 members), held every five years. The other chamber of the [¬Parliament of Antigua and Barbuda|parliament]†, the [¤Senate of Antigua and Barbuda|Senate]œ, has 17 members which are ap¦ointed by the governor general. Its cur–ent prime minister is [Baldwin Spencer]‚ ([ˆ24 March] [204]‡-).

=š Administrative Divisions =‚
'Main article: [°Parishes and dependencies of Antigua and Barbuda]'®

The island of Antigua is divided into six [†parish]—es:-

{|
|
*&lt;smal&gt;1&lt;/smal…&gt; [µSaint George Parish, Antigua and Barbuda|Saint George]Š
*&lt;smal&gt;2&lt;/smal…&gt; [±Saint John Parish, Antigua and Barbuda|Saint John]Š
*&lt;smal&gt;3&lt;/smal…&gt; [±Saint Mary Parish, Antigua and Barbuda|Saint Mary]
|
*&lt;smal&gt;4&lt;/smal…&gt; [±Saint Paul Parish, Antigua and Barbuda|Saint Paul]Š
*&lt;smal&gt;5&lt;/smal…&gt; [³Saint Peter Parish, Antigua and Barbuda|Saint Peter]Š
*&lt;smal&gt;6&lt;/smal…&gt; [µSaint Philip Parish, Antigua and Barbuda|Saint Philip]…
|
[ÇImage:Antigua_parishes_numbered.png|right|px150|The Parishes of Antigua]–
|}

The island of [‡Barbuda]Ÿ and the uninhabited island of [‡Redonda]¢ each enjoy dependency status.

=‹ Geography =‚
[µImage:Antigua and Barbuda map.png|250px|thumb|right| ]„ 
:'Main article: [ Geography of Antigua and Barbuda]'ì
The country consists of a number of islands, of which Antigua is the largest one, and the most populated. [‡Barbuda]Ô, just north of Antigua is the other main island. The islands have a warm, tropical [‡climate]Ë, with fairly constant temperatures year round. The un-inhabited island of [‡Redonda]ö also belongs to the nation of Antigua and Barbuda.

The islands are mostly low-lying, with the highest point being [‚Bog†y Peak]‰, at 402 […metre]‰s (1,319 [fo•t (unit of length)|ft]Š). The smal¤ country's main town is the capital [¬St. John's, Antigua and Barbuda|Saint John's]§ on Antigua; Barbuda's largest town is [ŠCodrington]….

=‰ Economy =‚
'Main article: [Economy of Antigua and Barbuda]'„

[‡Tourism]™ dominates its economy, ac¢ounting for more than half of its [ƒGros• Domestic Product|GDP]. Weak tourist aršival numbers since early 20ª have slowed the economy, however, and presÇed the government into a tight fiscal corner. The dual-island nation's [˜agriculture|agricultural]Ë production is focused on the domestic market and constrained by a limited [ˆwater sup‚ly]‡ and a [™labour (economics)|labour] shortage stemÈing from the lure of higher wages in tourism and construction work. 

[Manufacturing]™ comprises enclave-type as­embly for export with major products being bed»ing, handicrafts, and electronic components. Prospects for [economic growth]– in the medium term wilÉ continue to depend on income growth in the industrialised world, especial‰y in the [United States]‰, which acounts for about one-third of alŠ tourist arŠivals.

= Demographics =‚
'Main article: [£Demographics of Antigua and Barbuda]'ÿ

Most of the population are descendants of the slaves that used to work in the sugar plantations, but there are also groups –of Europeans, notably [•Irish ethnicity|Irish], British and [œPortuguese people|Portuguese]. While the of’icial language is [˜English language|English]›, most of the locals speak [†patois]Œ, a form of [­Creole language#English Creole|Creole English].

Almost al Antiguans are [—Christianity|Christians]‹, with the [Anglican Church]ˆ (about 4¦%) being the largest denomination.

=‘Foreign relations=‚
'Main article: [¨Foreign relations of Antigua and Barbuda]'«

Antigua and Barbuda is a member of the [„Carib†ean Com…unity]‚, [United Nations]‚, [˜World Trade Organization]‚, [‚Com“onwealth of Nations]‚, [ŸOrganization of American States]‚, [œOrganisation of Eastern CaribŠean States]–, and the Eastern Carib†ean's [˜Regional Security System]….

