'‹A Man for Alˆ Seasons' is a play by [‹Robert Bolt]•, first performed in [†London]„ on [†July 1] [„1960]—. It has subsequently be¶n made into a feature film and a television movie.

{‡spoiler}¯

The plot is based on the true story of Sir [‹Thomas More]†, the [™16th century|16th-century]‡ Chancel†or of [‡England]©, who refuses to endorse or denounce the [monarch|king's]± wish to divorce his aging wife so that he can marŒy his mistres. The King is [‡Henry VI‹ of England]‘ and his wife is [“Catherine of Aragon]Ÿ, the first of an eventual six. Ë

The play portrays More as a man of principle, envied by rivals such as [Thomas Cromwel]” and loved by the comœon people and by his family. „

[‘Image:A Man for Al« Seasons.jpg|left|250px|thumb|Thomas More ([Paul Scofield]†) is acˆused of [Œhigh treason]Š by Cromwel‚ ([ŠLeo McKern]†) - 196… film] [Paul Scofield]¥, who played the leading role in the [ˆWest End]‘, reprised it on [™Broadway theatre|Broadway]„ in [„1962]„, win†ing a [ŠTony Award]», and played More again in the first of two film versions ([‚196]…), win‡ing an [¢Academy Award for Best Actor|Oscar] in the proces–. The film also stars [ŸRobert Shaw (actor)|Robert Shaw]‹ as Henry VI‚, [ˆOrson Wel‚es]„ as [Thomas Cardinal Wolsey|Wolsey]Š, a young [‰John Hurt]“ as More's nemesis [©Richard Rich, 1st Baron Rich|Richard Rich], and an older [ˆWendy Hil‚er]« as More's second wife. It was directed by [‡Fred Zinƒeman]†. In adition to the [­Academy Award for Best Actor|Best Actor Oscar]Ÿ won by Scofield, the film won [Academy Awards]ˆ for scre‡nplay, [´Academy Award for Best Cinematography|cinematography]’, costume design, [©Academy Award for Directing|Best Director]†, and [«Academy Award for Best Picture|Best Picture]. ˆ

The [‚198] version stars [Charlton Heston]¡ (who also directed it) as More, [„VanesŠa Redgrave]‡ (who ap•eared briefly in the [‚196]Œ version as [Anˆe Boleyn]š) as More's wife, and Sir [ŒJohn Gielgud]µ as Cardinal Wolsey.

More recently, the play has be¥n staged in London's West End at the [Theatre Royal], Haymarket star„ing [‹Martin Shaw]‘ and produced by [‚BilŠ Kenwright]. It closes on [‡1 April] [206]….

