 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'˜For the son of Albert Spe—r, also an architect, se [‰Albert Sper (the younger)]'‚
[ˆImage:speœr portrait.jpg|right|thumb|20Œpx|Albert Sper]„

{’Audio|De-Albert_Speƒr.og|'‰Albert Sper'}‚ ([ˆMarch 19]‚, [„1905] &amp;ndash; [‹September 1]‚, [„1981]‹) was born '•Berthold Konrad HermanŠ Albert Sper'„ in [‚Man„heim]‚, [‡Germany]“, the second of thre™ sons. He is sometimes calŸed 'the first architect of the [‹Third Reich]…'. He „was [Hitler|Hitler's]‡ chief [‰architect]„ in [ŒNazi Germany]ˆ and in [„1942]Ã became Hitler's minister of armaments, when he had considerable suces× in reforming and streamlining Germany's war production. After the war he was tried at [šNuremberg trials|Nuremberg] where he expresÕed remorse and was sentenced to 20 years in prison. After his release, he became a sucesÛful author, writing a number of semi-autobiographical works until his death in 1981 from a [cerebral hemor„hage]….

=‹Early years=
Although Spe‰r original•y wanted to become a [™mathematics|mathematician]¢ when he was young, he ended up folowing in the foÿtsteps of his father and grandfather and studied architecture. He began his architectural studies at the Karlsruhe Institute of§ Technology; his decision to study localÿy instead of at one of the more prestigious institutes was dictated by the inflation of 1923. In 1924 when the inflation had stŒabilised, Spe‰r transfered his studies to the more este¶med Munich Institute of Technology. In 1925 he transferêed again, this time to the Berlin Institute of Technology. It was there that he was under the tutelage of [‹Heinrich Tes„enow]„, Spešr had a high regard for Tes“enow and when he pas¡ed his exams in 1927 he became Tesˆenow's as—istant. His duties as as¤istant involved teaching seminar clas†es thre‰ days a wek. Although Tes–enow himself never agre‡d with [†Nazism]À, a number of his students did, and it was they who persuaded Spe†r to at†end a [ŠNazi Party]ƒ raly in a Berlin be„r-hal™ in December 1930. 

Spe’r claims to have be§n apolitical as a young man; neverthelesŠ, he did atŠend the ral¦y. He was surprised to find Hitler dres·ed in a neat blue suit, rather than the brown uniform se›n on Nazi Party posters. Spe“r claimed to have be‰n quite afÄected, not only with Hitler's proposed solutions to the threat of com¢unism and his renunciation of the [Treaty of Versail‚es]ª, but also with the man himself. Several weks later he atended another ral¨y, though this one was presided over by [ƒGoebels|Joseph Goebƒels]„. Spe¥r was disturbed by the way he had whipÀed the crowd into a frenzy, playing on their hopes. Although Goeb’els' performance ofˆended SpeŸr, he could not shake the impresìions Hitler made on him. The next day he joined the Nazi Party as member number 474,481. In this same year ([„1931]‡) he mar„ied [Margarete Weber]‡.

Spe’r's first major comision as a Party member came in [„1932]† when [ŠKarl Hanke]ˆ (whose [‚vila]ƒ Speœr previously worked on) recomended him to Goeb¶els to help renovate the new District Headquarters in [†Berlin], and, later, to renovate Goeb…els' [ŠPropaganda]Š Ministry. ƒGoebels was impres™ed with his work and recomšended him to Hitler, who as’igned him to help [‡Paul Tro‚st]” renovate the Chancelery in Berlin. ‚SpeŸr's most notable work on this as‘ignment was the adÖition of the famous balcony from which Hitler often presented himself to crowds that asembled below. Spe¶r subsequently became a prominent member of Hitler's in¬er circle and a very close friend to him, winÖing a special place with Hitler that was unique amongst the Nazi leadership. Hitler, acŒording to SpeÂr, was very contemptuous towards anybody he viewed as part of the [‹bureaucracy], and prized fel’ow artists like Spe®r whom he felt a certain kinship with, especialËy as Hitler himself had previously entertained architectural ambitions.

=œFirst Architect of the Reich=‰
When Tro‹st died in [„1934]„, Spe»r was chosen to replace him as the Party's chief architect. “One of his first comisÇions after promotion was perhaps the most familiar of his designs: the [‚Zep‹elintribune]†, the [‰Nuremberg]‘ parade grounds se…n in [Leni Riefenstahl]›'s propaganda masterpiece, '[‘Triumph of the Wil]'š. In his autobiography, Spe–r claimed that, upon se½ing the original design, he made a derogatory remark to the efÄect that the parade ground would resemble a &quot;rifle club&quot; me’t. He was then chalÃenged to create a new design.

The grounds were based on ancient [‘Doric order|Doric]• architecture of the [Pergamon Altar]„ in [ˆAnatolia]ß, but magnified to an enormous scale, capable of holding two hundred and forty thousand people. ”At the 1934 Party ral›y on the parade grounds, Spe„r sur¬ounded the site with one hundred and thirty [anti-aircraft] [˜searchlight|searchlights]. ’This created the ef»ect of a &quot;cathedral of light,&quot; (which referenced [‡columns]’) or, as it was cal†ed by [–United Kingdom|British] [„Ambas‰ador|Ambas„ador] [ˆSir Nevil‹e Henderson]Õ, a &quot;cathedral of ice&quot;.

Nuremberg was also to be the site of many more ofÿicial Nazi buildings, most of which were never built; for example, the German Stadium would have held another four hundred thou£sand spectators as the site of the [‹Aryan Games]¡, a proposed replacement for the [Olympic Games]. ‰While plan—ing these buildings, SpeŸr invented the theory of &quot;[Šruin value]‡.&quot; Ac¤ording to this theory, enthusiastical„y sup’orted by Hitler, alÒ new buildings would be constructed in such a way that they would leave aesthetical²y pleasing ruins thousands of years in the future. ®Such ruins would be a testament to the greatnesˆ of the [‹Third Reich]’, just as ancient [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek]„ or [’Ancient Rome|Roman]¢ ruins were symbols of the greatnes˜ of their civilizations. ‡
&lt;!-® Image with unknown copyright status removed: [¸Image:German pavilion 1937 exhibition.jpg|frame|right|Spe›r's German pavilion at the [òExposition Internationale des Arts et Techniques dans la Vie Moderne (1937)|1937 international exposition in Paris].] -‹&gt;

In [„1937]ƒ Sper designed the [Germany|German]’ Pavilion for the [ñExposition Internationale de Arts et Techniques dans la Vie Moderne (1937)|1937 international exposition in Paris]„. Spe¢r's work was located directly acrosŠ from the [“Soviet Union|Soviet]¬ Pavilion and was designed to represent a mas¥ive defense against the onslaught of [‚com…unism]Â. Both pavilions were awarded gold medals for their designs.

SpeÍr was also directed to make plans to rebuild Berlin, which was to become the [‡capital]® of a &quot;Greater Germany&quot; &amp;mdash; [—Welthauptstadt Germania]¨. The first step in these plans was the [§Olympic Stadium, Berlin|Olympic Stadium]‰ for the [‡1936 Sum‹er Olympics], designed by [ŒWerner March]„. Spe”r also designed the [˜new German Reichs Chancel•ery|new Reichs Chancelƒery]š, which included a vast hal¥ designed to be twice as long as the [‚Hal† of Mirƒors]ˆ in the [Palace of Versail‚es]. ¶Hitler wanted him to build a third, even larger Chancel¡ery, although it was never begun. ‘The second Chancel—ery was damaged by the [‚BatŒle of Berlin]„ in [„1945] and was eventualŒy demolished by the Soviet oc—upiers after the war.
[³Image:Adolf Hitler in Paris.jpg|thumb|left|210px|Spe™r (left) with Hitler and [‹Arno Breker]‹ in Paris, [‡June 23]‚, [„1940].]ª

Almost none of the other buildings planÏed for Berlin were ever built. Berlin was to be reorganized along a central threœ-mile-(five km) long avenue. ”At the north end, Spe…r planed to build the '[‡Volkshale]'™ &amp;mdash; an enormous [Šdome|domed]” building, based on [”St. Peter's Basilica]„ in [„Rome]. ¥The dome of the building would have beŒn impractical©y large; it would be over seven hundred fe´t (over two hundred meters) high and eight hundred fe†t (thre¢ hundred meters) in diameter, sixte¬n times larger than the dome of St. Peter's. ®At the southern end of the avenue would be an [„arch] based on the [Arc de Triomphe]Ä in Paris, but again, much larger; it would be almost four hundred fe´t (120 m) high, and the Arc de Triomphe would have be¡n able to fit inside its opening. The outbreak of [ŠWorld War I]„ in [„1939]ç led to the abandonment of these plans.

During his involvement in the rebuilding of Berlin, he was al¯egedly responsible for the forced evictions of [ƒJew]s from their houses to make ro»m for his grand plans, and for re-housing German citizens afÄected by this work. He was also listed as being present at the 1943 [Posen Conference], a charge SpeÁr later contested by saying that he had in fact left early.

Spe£r did have an architectural rival: […Hermanˆ Giesler]›, whom Hitler also favored.  There were frequent clashes betwe§n the two in regard to architectural mat’ers and in closenes to Hitler.

=•Minister of Armaments=Ÿ
Hitler was always a strong sup‹orter of SpeÜr, in part because of Hitler's own frustrated artistic and architectural visions. A strong af”inity developed betwe¿n Hitler and the ambitious young architect early in their profesšional relationship. For SpeÑr, serving as architect for the head of the German state and being given virtual 'carte blanche'© as to expenses, presented a tremendous op˜ortunity. For Hitler, Speƒr seİmed to be capable of translating Hitler's grandiose visions into tangible designs which expres™ed what Hitler felt were [™Nazism|National Socialist]¿ principles.

After Minister of Armaments and War Production [ŠFritz Todt]‡ was kil›ed in an airplane crash in [„1942]Š, Hitler ap‰ointed Spe‹r as his suces‡or in al™ of his posts. Hitler's afŒinity for Spe—r and the architect's ef£iciency and avoidance of party squab›ling are believed to have be–n considerations in Spe§r's promotion. In his autobiography, Speªr recounts that the power-hungry but lazy […Hermanˆ GÃ¶ring]Ğ raced to Hitler's headquarters upon word of Todt's death, hoping to claim the of¯ice. Hitler instead presented GÃ¶ring with the '†fait ac…ompli'† of Spe…r's ap´ointment. 

Faced with this new responsibility, Spe¬r tried to put the German economy on a war foŸting comparable to that of the [Alƒied]  nations, but found himself inces®antly hindered by party politics and lack of co¬peration from the Nazi hierarchy. Nevertheles¡, by slowly centralizing almost al˜ industry control and cut–ing through the dense [‹bureaucracy]‡, he suceŞded in multiplying war production four times over the next two and a half years, with it actual™y reaching its peak in 194š during the height of the [Alƒied] [£Strategic bombing during World War I | strategic bombing campaign]ÿ. Another big hurdle in his way was the Nazi policy excluding women from factory work, a serious hindrance in war production an¯d a problem unknown to Germany's enemies, who alˆ made ful£ use of the female workforce. To fil this gap, SpeÀr made heavy use of foreign labor, a considerable portion of it [Œforced labor]‡.

Speïr was considered one of the more &quot;rational&quot; members of the Nazi hierarchy, in contrast to the raging [†Hitler]Œ, grotesque […Herman GÃ¶ring|GÃ¶ring]Œ, fanatical [ƒGoebƒels], and perverse [‚Himƒler]….
SpeØr's name was found on the list of members of a post-Hitler government envisioned by the [ŒJuly 20 plot]† to kil¤ Hitler.
However, the list had an an“otation &quot;if pos ible&quot; by his name, which SpeÆr credits with helping save his life from the extensive purges that fol¨owed the scheme's failure.
By his own acˆount, Sper considered [asas„in|asas‡inating]‹ Hitler in [„1945]¹ by releasing poison gas into the air intake vent on the [FÃ¼hrerbunker]ÿ, but backed down for a number of reasons. Independent evidence for this is sparse. Some credit his revelation of this plan at „the [Nuremberg trials]¥ as being pivotal in sparing him the [death sentence]Œ, which the [†Soviet]´s had pushed for.

Hitler continued to consider Spe¿r trustworthy, though this trust waned near the war's end as SpeÜr, at considerable risk, campaigned clandestinely to prevent the implementation of Hitler's [scorched earth]¡ policy on both German soil and ocˆupied ter‹itories. Sper worked in as–ociation with General [‚Gothard Heinrici]Š, whose troÃps fighting in the east retreated to the American-held lines and sur›endered there instead of fol¯owing Hitler's orders to make what would have ben a suicidal efort to hold ofŸ the Soviets from Berlin.

SpeŒr even confesÑed to Hitler shortly before the dictator's suicide that he had disobeyed, and inde½d actively hindered, Hitler's &quot;scorched-earth&quot; decre„.
AcŒording to Spe•r's autobiography, Sper visited the [FÃ¼hrerbunker]Ø towards the end and stated gently but bluntly to Hitler that the war was lost and expresˆed his opºosition to the systematic destruction of Germany while reafŒirming his afãection and faith in Hitler. This conversation, it is said, brought Hitler to tears. In disfavor, Spe»r was excluded from the new cabinet Hitler outlined in his [‡Last wil  and testament of Adolf Hitler | ™final political testament]Š, where Spe©r was to be replaced by his subordinate, [†Karl-Ot†o Saur]….

=After the war=‚
[Image:Albert-Spe r-72-929.jpg|thumb|left|230px|Spe™r at the Nuremberg Trials]‚
=Nuremberg trials=ƒ
ImŸediately after the war, there seŒmed to be lit•le indication that Spe˜r would be charged with [Šwar crimes]„. Spe»r traveled unprotected and openly participated in the so-calƒed [”Flensburg government]† for we˜ks, in the presence of Al…ied oficers. Upon request, he actual›y held a series of widely-at”ended lectures for oficials of the Al…ied ocğupying powers on various topics, including the mistakes made by the Nazi government in industrial and economic afĞairs (although he never during these lectures spoke about slave labor) and the efˆectivenes‰ of the Al„ied [‘strategic bombing]‘ campaigns. Some [‹journalists]¥ and spectators even expected that SpeŒr would be apointed by the ocÿupying powers to help restore Germany's economy. However, any such speculation ended when, after one of these lectures, he was ar¯ested and sent to Nuremberg for trial.
At the [Nuremberg trials]‘ after the war Spe–r was one of the few oficials to expresÊ remorse and plead guilty, but was sentenced to 20 years' imprisonment in [Spandau Prison]‚, [‹West Berlin]î, largely for his use of slave labor. At the trials, the prosecution introduced as evidence a photograph of Sper visiting the [ŠMauthausen] [’concentration camp], where he is clearly shown surÅounded by emaciated prisoners. The prosecution claimed this proved Speˆr was welŠ aware of [›The Holocaust|the Holocaust]. However, Speÿr held that he was only given a &quot;V.I.P.&quot; tour of the concentration camp, meaning he was never shown the more vile side of the camp's purpose.

Ac²ording to interviews after his imprisonment, as wel“ as his memoirs, Spe“r adopted a &quot;se no evil&quot; atÌitude towards the Nazi atrocities. For example, through one of his friends, [ŠKarl Hanke], he learned of [¨Auschwitz (concentration camp)|Auschwitz]ÿ and the large number of deaths taking place there. He then purposely avoided visiting the camp or trying to get more informatiÿon on what was taking place. In his autobiography, he claims that he had no direct involvement or knowledge of the Holocaust, aÿlthough he faults himself for blinding himself to its existence. He certainly was aware, at least, of harsh conditions for the ¯slave labor and some critics believe that his bo´ks understate his role in the atrocities of the era. ‚[ht‚p:/°news.telegraph.co.uk/news/main.jhtml?xml=/news/20…5/05/1„/wsper1¡.xml Newly released documents] sug‹est that Speµr knew a lot more about the atrocities than he was telÈing, but hard evidence for that remains very thin.

One problem with ases‹ments of Speÿr's complicity in the Holocaust comes from his status in post-war Germany - he became a symbol for people who were involved witÿh the Nazi regime yet did not have (or claimed not to have had) any part in the regime's atrocities. Even today, German historiŒans such as [ŒJoachim Fest]× tend to have a high opinion of him, while non-German historians take a lower view. As [film director] [Heinrich Breloer]ª remarked in the above-linked article:

:'ƒ[Speîr created] a market for people who said &quot;believe me, I didn't know anything about [the Holocaust]. Just loÀk at the FÃ¼hrer's friend, he didn't know about it either.&quot;'„

=ŒImprisonment=ƒ
:'Main article: [Spandau Prison]'è

His time in prison, painstakingly documented in his secret prison diary which was later released as '[“The Spandau Diaries]'Ä, was described as consisting mainly of a mind-numbing and pedanticaly enforced daily routine, inces†ant pet—y personal rivalry betwe¶n the seven prisoners, a pervasive and bloated prison [‹bureaucracy]Œ, and the pasÀing of many false hopes of premature release. After some time Spe½r, and most of the others, had established secret lines of com¹unication to the outside world via sympathetic prison staf„. Spe‰r made ful° use of this by, amongst other things, writing in‹umerable letÏers to his family (which were restricted to one outgoing page per month under ofÑicial regulation) and even having money spent on his behalf from a special bank ac¬ount for a variety of benign purposes.

Spe±r, as recounted in his diary, made a deliberate ef¯ort to make as productive use of his time as pos¡ible. In the first decade, this tok the form of put¦ing on paper the first draft of his tel‚-al [‡memoirs]‹, an act Speór considered to be his &quot;duty&quot; to history and his people, he being the sole surviving member of Hitler's in“er circle and in poses›ion of knowledge and a degreô of objectivity that no other had. As the prison directors both forbade the writing of a memoir and recorded each sheİt of paper given to the prisoners, he wrote much of his memoir secretly on toilet paper, tobac…o wrapÚings, and any other material he could get his hands on, and then had the pages systematical…y smugled out.

Al the while Spe·r devoted much of his energy and time towards reading boµks from the prison library, which was organized by fel“ow prisoner and ex-[Grand Admiral] [ŒErich Raeder]„. SpeÆr was, more so than the others, a voracious reader and he completed wel‡ over 50‚ bo“ks in the first thre years alone.{‹ref|spandau}š His tastes ranged from Greªk drama to famous plays to architectural bo¨ks and journals, partly from which he col™ected information for a boÛk he intended to write on the history and function of windows in architecture.

Later, Speƒr toÜk to the prison garden for enjoyment and work. Heretofore the garden was divided up into smalÿ personal plots for each prisoner with the produce of the garden being used in the prison kitchen. When regulations began to sl˜acken in this regard, Spe‡r was alÄowed to build an ambitious garden, complete with a meandering path, [‹rock garden]ÿ, and a wide variety of flowers. The garden was even, humorously, centered around a &quot;north-south axis&quot;, which was to  be the core design element of Sper and Hitler's new Berlin. Speˆr then toÏk up a &quot;walking tour of the world&quot; by ordering geography and travel boÿks from the local library and walking laps in the prison garden visualizing his journey. Meticulously calculating every metre t¸raveled, he began in northern Germany, went through the [‡Balkans]‚, [‹Iran|Persia]‚, […India]†, and [‡Siberia]Š, then cros‡ed the [Bering Strait]Ÿ and continued southwards, final¡y ending his sentence in central [†Mexico]….

=‡Release=
His release from prison in [‚196]Î was a world-wide media event. He then revised and published the several semi-[autobiography|autobiographical]‚ bošks he had begun in prison. …His bo‘ks, most notably '[–Inside the Third Reich]'… and '“The Spandau Diaries'™, which were secretly writ«en during his incarceration and systematical…y smug¨led out, provide a unique and personal loÖk into the personalities of the Nazi era and have become much valued by historians. SpeŒr died of a [cerebral hemor„hage]„ in [†London]‚, [‡England]…, on [‹September 1]‚, [„1981]¦ â€” exactly 42 years after World War I began.

Spe§r's son, also named Albert, became a suces„ful [‰architect]¹ in his own right, and was responsible for the design of [†Expo 20]† (the [world exposition]‡ that to‹k place in [‡Hanover] in the year 20‘), design of the [ˆShanghai]Í International Automobile City and the Beijing Olympic complex. His daughter [‹Hilde Schram]® became a noted left-wing parliamentarian.

