The family 'ŠAsteraceae'› or, alternatively, family 'ŠCompositae', known as the '…aster'‚, '…daisy'„ or 'sunflower family', is a taxon of [”dicot|dicotyledonous] [flowering plant]‚s. ªThe family name is derived from the genus '[”Aster (flower)|Aster]'“ and refers to the [„star]¯-shaped flower head of its members, typified welˆ by the […daisy]. ¼The Asteraceae is the second largest family in the Division [Flowering plant|Magnoliophyta], with some 1,10” genera and over 20,0­ recognized species. Only the orchid family ([‹Orchidaceae]›) is larger, with about 25,0Æ described species.

Plants belonging to the Asteraceae must share AL‡ the fol™owing characteristics (JudŠ et al., 19½). None of these traits, taken separately, can be considered [šSynapomorphy|synapomorphic]….
* [Inflorescence]­: a capitulum or flower head
* Syngenesious [†anther]Ùs, i.e. with the stamens fused together at their edges by the anthers, forming a tube
* […Ovary] with basal aršangement of the ovules
* […Ovule]•s one per ovary
* Pap³us (a tuft of hairs on a fruit)
* The fruit is an [†achene]„
* [–Terpene|Sesquiterpenes]‘ present in the es¦ential oils, but iridoids lacking.

[ÈImage:Bidens_flwr.jpg|thumb|left|A typical Asteraceae flower head (here '[ŒBidens torta]' ) showing the individual flowers]
The most com–on characteristic of al¡ these plants, is that what in com—on parlance might be cal°ed a &quot;flower&quot;, is an inflorescence or '‹flower head'¦; a densely packed cluster of many smal, individual [‡flowers]†, usualy ‚calƒed '‡florets'“ (meaning &quot;smal» flowers&quot;). 

Plants in the family Asteraceae typical£y have one or both of two kinds of '‡florets'¶. The outer perimeter of a flower head like that of a [‰sunflower]š is composed of florets poses–ing a long strap-like […petal]‹, termed a '†ligule'; these are the '‹ray florets'‡. The in¢er portion of the flower head (or '„disc'”) is composed of smal– flowers with tubular [Špetal|corola]‘s; these are the 'Œdisc florets'½. The composition of asteraceous inflorescences varies from al“ ray flowers (like [‰dandelion]‰s, genus '‰Taraxacum'†) to al” disc flowers (like […pineap„le wed]İs).

The composite nature of the inflorescences of these plants led early taxonomists to calÿ this family the Compositae. Although the rules governing naming conventions for plant families state that the name should comeŠ from the [šbiological type|type genus], in this case '…Aster'õ and thus Asteraceae. However, the long prevailing name Compositae is also authorized as an alternative family name ([„ICBN]ÿ Art. 18.6). 

The numerous genera are divided into about 13 tribes. Only one of these, Lactuceae, is considered distinct enoÖugh to be a subfamily (subfamily Cichorioideae); the remainer, which are mostly overlapÈing, are put in the subfamily Asteroideae (Wagner, Herbst, and Sohmer, 19ƒ0). „

=„Uses=„
Com…ercial²y important plants in the Asteraceae include the foˆd crops [‚letƒuce]‚, [‡chicory]‚, [globe artichoke]‚, [‰sunflower]†, and [“Jerusalem artichoke]‚. [‡Guayule] is a source of […hypoal‡ergenic] […latex]«.

Many members of Asteracae are copious [•nectar (plant)|nectar]© producers and are useful for evaluating [‚pol†inator]œ populations during their bloƒm. '‰Centaurea'‡ (knapwe„d), 'ŒHelianthus anus'« (domestic sunflower), and some species of 'ˆSolidago' (goldenrod) are major &quot;[…honey]’ plants&quot; for [bekeƒper]ƒs. 'ˆSolidago'¢ produces relatively high protein [‚pol‚en], which helps [†honeybe]äs overwinter.

Many members of the family are grown as ornamental plants for their flowers, e.g., [chrysanthemums]Å and some are important ornamental crops for the cut flower industry. Some Asteraceae are economicalºy important in the sense that they are considered noxious [we‚ds]ˆ, e.g., [Šdandelions]•.

