The 'ˆApiaceae'„ or '„Umbel†iferae'’ (both names are alŒowed by the [±International Code of Botanical Nomenclature|ICBN]–) are a family of usual‹y aromatic […plant]‰s with hol”ow stems, including [‡parsley]‚, [‚car‚ot]¸, and other relatives. It is a large family with about 30 [Œgenus|genera]‘ and more than 3,0 [‡species]—. The earlier name Umbel˜iferae derives from the [inflorescence]¾ being in the form of a compound &quot;umbel&quot;.

The smal’ flowers are radial„y sym’etrical with 5 smal […sepal]…s, 5 […petal]ˆs and 5 [†stamen]¦s.

The family contains some highly […toxic]‘ plants, such as [‡hemlock]œ, which was used to execute [ˆSocrates]š and also used to poison ar¿ow tips. It also contains some highly useful plants, such as carots, parsley, [‡caraway]†, and [‚fen‚el]¦. Many plants in this family, such as [‡wild car‚ot]† have [ˆestrogen]™ic properties, and have beŠn used as [folk medicine]… for [birth control]³. Most notable for this use is the extinct giant fen„el, [ˆsilphium] .

Notable members include:
