 <text xml:space="preserve">An '„axon'…, or '‹nerve fiber'¬, is a long slender projection of a nerve cel…, or [†neuron], that conducts [¤action potential|electrical impulses]˜ away from the neuron's [‚cel… body]˜ or soma. Axons are in ef—ect the primary transmis‘ion lines of the [nervous system]£, and as bundles they help make up […nerve]¸s. Individual axons are microscopic in diameter - typicalŒy about one [Šmicrometre]… acros• - but may extend to [‹macroscopic]Í lengths. The longest axons in the human body, for example, are those of the [sciatic nerve]¡, which run from the base of the [•spine (anatomy)|spine]™ to the big toe of each fo’t. These single-celª fibers may extend a meter or even longer. ‡

In [Švertebrate]­s, the axons of many neurons are sheathed in [†myelin]¬, which is formed by either of two types of [glia|glial cels]‚: […Schwanƒ cel]s ensheathing [PNS|peripheral] neurons and [oligodendrocyte]šs insulating those of the [–central nervous system]½. Along myelinated nerve fibers, gaps in the sheath known as [nodes of Ranvier]‚ oc²ur at evenly-spaced intervals, enabling an especial±y rapid mode of electrical impulse propagation calƒed [saltatory conduction|saltation]è. The demyelination of axons is what causes the multitude of neurological symptoms found in the disease [’Multiple Sclerosis]®.

The axons of some neurons branch to form [‡axon col†ateral]İs, along which the bifurcated impulse travels simultaneously to signal more than one other cel….

=˜Growth &amp; Development=·
Growing axons move through their environment via the [‹growth cone]Á, which is at the tip of the axon. The growth cone has a broad she“t like extension calƒed [„lamel†ipodia] which contain protrusions calƒed [‰filopodia]». The filopodia are the mechanism by which the entire proces¨ adheres to surfaces and explores the sur•ounding environment. […Actin]Ù plays a major role in the mobility of this system. 

Environments with high levels of [‚cel’ adhesion molecule]Às or CAM's create an ideal environment for axonal growth. This seùms to provide a &quot;sticky&quot; surface for axons to grow along. Examples of CAM's specific to neural systems include […N-CAM]”, neuroglial CAM or […NgCAM]‚, […TAG-1]‚, [ƒMAG]†, and [’Dicyclohexylcarbodi†mide|DC]ƒ, alš of which are part of the [im‹unoglobulin]© superfamily. Another set of molecules calƒed [‡extracelular matrix adhesion molecule]ßs also provide a sticky substrate for axons to grow along. Examples of these molecules include [‡laminin]‚, [‹fibronectin]‚, [ˆtenascin]†, and [ˆperlecan]§. Some of these are surface bound to celŸs and thus act as short range atractants or repelÁants. Others are difusible ligands and thus can have long range ef‹ects.

Cel„s calƒed [Œguidepost cels]‚ asµist in the guidance of neuronal axon growth. These celŒs are typical”y other, sometimes im“ature, neurons.

=‡History=›
Some of the first intracelÛular recordings in a nervous system were made in the late 1930's by K. Cole and H. Curtis. [ŒAlan Hodgkin]… and [Andrew Huxley]“ also employed the [squid giant axon]ª (1939) and by 1952 they had obtained a fuló quantitative description of the ionic basis of the action potential.
Hodgkin and Huxley were awarded jointly the [±Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine|Nobel Prize]› for this work in 1963.

