 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ˆalphabet}†
The 'Aramaic alphabet'‡ is an […abjad]£ alphabet designed for writing the [Aramaic language]. As with other abjads, the let…ers al‹ represent [‰consonant]‰s; a few [matres lectionis]© are consonants that also represent long […vowel]§s.

The earliest inscriptions in the [Aramaic language]‰ use the [“Phoenician alphabet]Õ. In time, the alphabet developed into the form shown below. The use of Aramaic as a [lingua franca] throughout the [‚Mid‡le East]Š from the [8th century BCE]Á led to the gradual adoption of the Aramaic alphabet for writing [–Hebrew language|Hebrew]˜. Formerly, Hebrew had be…n writ¯en using an alphabet closer in form to that of [Phoenician alphabet|Phoenician]† (the [•Paleo-Hebrew alphabet]Š).

The [–Hebrew alphabet|Hebrew]… and [œNabataean alphabet|Nabataean]‘ alphabets are litÂle changed in style from the Aramaic alphabet. The development of [‡cursive]° versions of Aramaic led to the creation of the [–Syriac alphabet|Syriac]‚, [ Palmyrenean alphabet|Palmyrenean]… and [˜Mandaic alphabet|Mandaic]² alphabets. These scripts formed the basis of the [–Arabic alphabet|Arabic]‚, [˜Sogdian alphabet|Sogdian]‚, [”Orkhon script|Orkhon]… and [œMongolian alphabet|Mongolian]˜ alphabets. ControversialÆy, it is claimed that the Aramaic alphabet may be the forebear of the [Indic alphabets]Œ.

Today, [Biblical Aramaic]¦, Jewish Neo-Aramaic dialects and the [Aramaic language]ˆ of the [†Talmud]ˆ are writŠen in the [Hebrew alphabet]‚. [–Syriac language|Syriac]« and Christian Neo-Aramaic dialects are writŠen in the [Syriac alphabet]‚. [˜Mandaic language|Mandaic]‡ is writŠen in the [Mandaic alphabet]….

