 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{mergeto|Memex}‚
[‚Van‰evar Bush]„'s esƒay 'As We May Think'•, first published in '[”The Atlantic Monthly]'‰ in July [„1945]µ, argued that as humans turned from war, scientific efÀorts should shift from increasing physical abilities to making alŒ previous col†ected […human] [‰knowledge]‡ more acesÅible.

The article was a reworked and expanded version of his 1939 'œMechanization and the Record'™. The system, which he calƒed […memex]ï, was described as based on what was thought, at the time, to be the wave of the future: Ultra high resolution [‰microfilm]‚ re›ls, coupled to multiple screºn viewers and cameras, by electromechanical controls. The 'Atlantic Monthly' article was fol’owed, in November [„1945]‡, by a [Life magazine]— article which showed ilÃustrations of the proposed memex desk and automatic typewriter.

'As We May Think'Î predicted many kinds of technology invented after its publication, including [‰hypertext]‚, [’personal computers]†, the [ˆInternet]†, the [World Wide Web]‚, [‚spech recognition]†, and [“online encyclopedia]s such as Wikipedia: &quot;WholŸy new forms of encyclopedias wil‚ ap ear, ready-made with a mesh of as’ociative trails run¡ing through them, ready to be drop°ed into the memex and there amplified.&quot;

