'ˆAcute disšeminated encephalomyelitis'‚ ('„ADEM'‰) is an imune mediated [‡disease]„ of [‘human brain|brain]‰. It usualƒy oc†urs folˆowing a [•virus (biology)|viral] [‰infection]„ or [‚vac‡ination]“, but it may also ap®ear spontaneously.

There are multiple inflamˆatory cel¸ deposits in the brain, particularly in the section 
calƒed [ˆwhite mat‚er]. Although it ocˆurs in al¯ ages, most reported cases are in children and [—Adolescence|adolescents]Â.

It has an abrupt onset and a monophasic course. Symptoms usualy begins 1-3 we˜ks after infection or vacœination. Major symptoms are […fever]‚, [ˆheadache]Š, drowsines‚, [‡seizure]†s and [„coma]‘. Although initial—y the symptoms are usualÿy mild, later in the course of the disease patients may even die, if they are not treated properly. Some patients recover complÛetely, while others have permanent neurological impairments.

The first treatment is usual‚y [‡steroid]†s and [intensive care]— is often required.

