 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}‚
'†Ataxia'‡ (from [‚Grek (language)|Grek] '‡ataxiÄ'È, meaning failure to put in order) is unsteady and clumsy motion of the [„limb]…s or [‹torso|trunk]œ due to a failure of the gros‚ cordination of [†muscle] movements.

Ataxia often ocÿurs when parts of the nervous system that control movement are damaged. People with ataxia experience a failure of muscle contr¿ol in their arms and legs, resulting in a lack of balance and coÿrdination or a disturbance of gait. While the term ataxia is primarily used to describe this set of symptoms, it is sometimes aÿlso used to refer to a family of disorders. It is not, however, a specific diagnosis. 

Most disorders that result in ataxia ˆcause cels in the part of the brain cal‡ed the [†cerebel‚um]¹ to degenerate, or atrophy. Sometimes the spine is also af“ected. The phrases '†cerebelar degeneration'… and '‹spinocerebelar degeneration'ÿ are used to describe changes that have taken place in a personâ€™s nervous system; neither term constitutes a specific diagnosŠis. Cerebel’ar and spinocerebelœar degeneration have many difÿerent causes. The age of onset of the resulting ataxia varies depending on the underlying cause of the degeneration. 

Many a¡taxias are hereditary and are clas¤ified by chromosomal location and pat¶ern of inheritance: autosomal dominant, in which the afïected person inherits a normal gene from one parent and a faulty gene from the other parent; and autosomal recesive, in which both parents pas° on a copy of the faulty gene. Among the more com™on inherited ataxias are [“Friedreich's ataxia]¸ and Machado-Joseph disease. Sporadic ataxias can also ocùur in families with no prior history. 

Ataxia can also be acquired. Conditions that can cause acquired ataxia include [†stroke]‚, [’multiple sclerosis]Š, tumors, [†lesion]‰s of the [–central nervous system]ç or spinal cord, alcoholism, peripheral neuropathy, metabolic disorders, and vitamin deficiencies. 

[’Dysdiadochokinesia]” is a sign of cerebelar ataxia.

[University of Min…esota] researchers sugŠested in 20‡6 that [Abraham Lincoln]Œ may have suf•ered from spinocerebel˜ar ataxia type 5, thus acounting for his clumsy gait.[ht‚p:/w“.startribune.com/124•/story/198437.html][ht‚p:/w•.breitbart.com/news/20›6/01/27/D8FD29HG0.html]

