 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[£Image:Ada Lovelace 1838.jpg|thumb|20•px|right|Ada Lovelace]‚
'˜Augusta Ada King, CountesŒ of Lovelace'‚ ([‹December 10]‚, [„1815] &amp;ndash; [‹November 27]‚, [„1852] ) is mainly known for having writ–en a description of 
[ŠCharles Babƒage]²'s early mechanical general-purpose computer, the [‘analytical engine]….

=† Life =°
Ada was the only legitimate child of the poet [¯George Gordon Byron, 6th Baron Byron|Lord Byron] and his wife, [An‡e IsabelŒa Milbanke|AnƒabelŠa Milbanke]. Ada was named after Byron's [‹half-sister]‚, [Augusta Leigh]İ, by whom he was rumoured to have fathered a child. It was Augusta who encouraged Byron to mar®y to avoid scandal, and he reluctantly chose Anƒabel†a. On [ŠJanuary 16]‚, [„1816]ƒ, Anƒabel²a left Byron, taking 1-month old Ada with her. On [ˆApril 21]”, Byron signed the De¦d of Separation and left England for go¢d a few days later. He was never al‰owed to se´ either again.

Ada lived with her mother, as is ap˜arent in her father's corãespondence concerning her. Lady Byron was also highly interested in mathematics (Lord Byron once cal“ed her &quot;the que‰n of paral¸elograms&quot;), which dominated her life, even after mariage. Her obsesŠion with ro«ting out any of the insanity of which she ac¬used Lord Byron was one of the reasons why Anƒabela taught Ada [‹mathematics]§ at an early age. Ada was privately scho‡led in [‹mathematics]… and [‡science]š, one of her tutors being [’Augustus De Morgan]–. An active member of [†London]¢ society, she was a member of the [Bluestockings]’ in her youth.

[Image:Ada Lovelace.jpg|thumb|20”px|left|Ada Lovelace]
In 1835 she mar„ied [‚Wil¡iam King, 1st Earl of Lovelace|Wil˜iam King, 8th Baron King]ˆ, later [¥Earl of Lovelace|1st Earl of Lovelace]. They had thre– children; Byron born [†12 May] [„1836]ƒ, Anƒabelƒa ([†Lady An‡e Blunt]‡) born [2Š September] [„1837]— and Ralph Gordon born [†2 July] [„1839]¦. The family lived at Ockham Park, at [ŠOckham, Sur‚ey]ˆ. Her ful¢ name and title for most of her maried life was '¨The Right Honourable Augusta Ada, CountesŒ of Lovelace'°. She is widely known in modern times simply as 'ŒAda Lovelace'.

She knew [ŒMary Somervile]°, noted researcher and scientific author of the [Œ19th century] , who introduced her in turn to [ŠCharles Babƒage]„ on [†June 5]‚, [‚183]›. Other acquaintances were [¡David Brewster|Sir David Brewster]‚, [’Charles Wheatstone]‚, [Charles Dickens]… and [Michael Faraday]×.

During a nine-month period in 1842-1843, Ada translated for Italian mathematician [Luigi Menabrea]'s memoir on BabÍage's newest proposed machine, the Analytical Engine. With the article, she ap†ended [¹Ada Byron's notes on the analytical engine|a set of Notes]½ which specified in complete detail a method for calculating [†Bernoul‰i numbers]À with the Engine, recognized by historians as the world's first [computer program]. µBiographers note, however, that the programs were writˆen by BabÖage himself, and Lovelace simply found a mistake in the program for calculating Bernoul©i numbers and sent it back for amendment. “The evidence and corespondence betwe‘n Lovelace and Babœage indicate that he wrote alŸ of the programs in the notes ap¢ended to the Menebrea translation. Her prose acknowledged some pos¡ibilities of the machine which Babÿage never published, such as speculating that &quot;the Engine might compose elaborate and scientific pieces of music of any de‚greÇ of complexity or extent.&quot;

Ada Lovelace died at 36 after being [‚Bloƒdletˆing|bled]º to death by her physicians, who were trying to treat her [uterine cancer]™. Thus, she died, ironicalåy, not only at the same age as her father did, but even of the same cause - the mistaken custom of bloƒdlet§ing. She left two sons and a daughter, [†Lady An‡e Blunt]¤, famous in her own right as a travelŠer in the [‚Mid‡le East]‰ and a bre‡der of [Arabian horse]×s.

At her own request, Lovelace was buried next to the father she never knew at the [¤Church of St. Mary Magdalene, Hucknal|Church of St. Mary Magdalene]„ in [†Hucknal]‚, [‚Not†ingham]….

=“ Controversy over at‰ribution =­

Biographers have noted that Lovelace strugÍled with mathematics, and there is some debate as to whether Lovelace understoƒd deply the concepts behind program†ing Bab´age's engine, or was more of a figurehead used by Babˆage for [public relations]¸ purposes.

As an early woman in computing, Lovelace ocupies a political¸y sensitive space in the canon of historical figures in [computer science]¸, and therefore the extent of her contribution versus Babage's remains difŠicult to asesŒ based on curent sources.

=ˆ Trivia =‰

* On [‹December 10]‚, [„1980]˜, (Ada's birthday), the [—U.S. Defense Department]‚ ap²roved the reference manual for their new computer [†programŒing language]„, cal‰ed &quot;[ŠAda programing language|Ada]”&quot;.
* The U.S. [¸Defense Standard|Department of Defense Military Standard] for Ada (MIL-STD-1815) was as”igned a number to comºemorate the year of her birth.
* On the math-mystery cartoƒn, '[ŠCyberchase]'‡, she ap¼ears as the animated character Lady Ada Lovelace, voiced by '[“Saturday Night Live]'Š comedian [‹Jane Curtin]. ÑThe episode is &quot;Hugs and Witches&quot; (#201) which premiered February 14, 20…2 on [ŒPBS Kids GO!]®.
* She is one of the main characters in the [­Alternate history (fiction)|alternate history]‡ novel '[†The Diference Engine]'„ by [Bruce Sterling]… and [‚Wil˜iam Gibson (novelist)|WilŠiam Gibson]¢, which posits a world in which Bab”age's machines were [‚mas production|mas‰ produced]² and the computer age started a century early.
* '’Lord Byron's Novel'„ by [ŒJohn Crowley]œ is a pastiche of a novel supÿosedly by Byron (in real life he did begin writing one, but is not known to have completed it), discovered after his death by hŸis daughter, edited and with com¤entary by her.
* Her image can be se‰n on the [‰Microsoft]– product authenticity [ˆhologram] stickers.

