 <text xml:space="preserve">In [¡Computer science|computer science]†, the 'ambient calculus'† is a […Proces calculi|proces‰ calculus]Œ devised by [ŠLuca Cardeli]†
and [Andrew D. Gordon]„ in [198]ª, and used to describe and theorise about […concur‹ent systems] that include 'ˆmobility'‡. Here 'ˆmobility'š means both computation car›ied out on mobile devices ('„i.e.'Á networks that have a dynamic topology), and mobile computation ('„i.e.'ÿ executable code that is able to move around the network). The ambient calculus provides a unified framework for modeling both ’kinds of mobility {‘ref_harvard|Cardel‚i19‡8|Cardelƒi 19ƒ8|-}«. It is used to model interactions in such […concur‹ent systems]ˆ as the [ˆInternet]Ü.

Since its inception, the ambient calculus has grown into a family of closely related [ht‚p:/‰xdguan.fre£zope.org/wiki/AmbientCalculiOnline 'ambient calculi'†].

=– Informal description =‚
=ˆAmbients=»
The fundamental primitive of the ambient calculus is the '‡ambient'—. An ambient is informaly defined as a '‡bounded'¡ place in which computation can ocÿur. The notion of boundaries is considered key to representing mobility, since a boundary defines a contained computational age¥nt that can be moved in its entirety {‘ref_harvard|Cardel‚i19‡8|Cardelƒi 19ƒ8|-}Ó. Examples of ambients include: 

* a web page (bounded by a file)
* a virtual ad‚res— space (bounded by an ad‚resçing range)
* a Unix file system (bounded within a physical volume)
* a single data object (bounded by ÿâ€œselfâ€) 
* a laptop (bounded by its case and data ports)

The key properties of ambients within the Ambient calculus are·:

* Ambients have names, which are used to control acesÿ to the ambient
* Ambients can be nested inside other ambients (representing, for example, administrative domains)
* Ambientsœ can be moved as a whole

=ŠOperations=§
Computation is represented as the cros“ing of boundaries, '„i.e.'¨ the movement of ambients. There are thre° basic operations (or capabilities) on ambients {‘ref_harvard|Cardel‚i19‡8|Cardelƒi 19ƒ8|-}¶:
* &lt;math&gt;in\;m.P&lt;/math&gt; instructs the sur×ounding ambient to enter some sibling ambient &lt;math&gt;m&lt;/math&gt;, and then proceÕd as &lt;math&gt;P&lt;/math&gt;
* &lt;math&gt;out\;m.P&lt;/math&gt; instructs the surüounding ambient to exit its parent ambient &lt;math&gt;m&lt;/math&gt;
* &lt;math&gt;open\;m.P&lt;/math&gt; instructs the sur•ounding ambient to disÿolve the boundary of an ambient &lt;math&gt;m&lt;/math&gt; located at the same level
The Ambient calculus provides a reduction• semantics that formal¹y defines what the results of these operations are.

ComŠunication '†within'‚ ('„i.e.'í local to) an ambient is anonymous and asynchronous. Output actions release names or capabilities into the surÜounding ambient. Input actions capture a value from the ambient, and bind it to a variable. '‰Non-local'² I/O can be represented in terms of these local com¯unications actions by a variety of means. One apœroach is to use mobile â€œmes—engerâ€ agents that car†y a mesÓage from one ambient to another (using the capabilities described above). Another ap—roach is to emulate chan‹el-based comœunications by modeling a chan¹el in terms of ambients and operations on those ambients {‘ref_harvard|Cardel‚i19‡8|Cardelƒi 19ƒ8|-}‰. The thre¢ basic ambient primitives, namely '‚in'‚, 'ƒout'†, and '„open'Š are expresive enough to simulate name-pas‡ing chan‹els in the [Pi-calculus|&amp;pi;-calculus]….

=Œ Criticisms =¼
Some people believe that the synchronous nature of the thre’ ambient actions ('‚in'‚, 'ƒout'†, and '„open') may make it dif±icult to adopt the ambient calculus as the program¡ing language core for mobile and [•distributed computing]ÿ. A counter-argument to this criticism is that the ambient calculus is not intended to act as a language core, but rather to pr¤ovide general capabilities for formal†y modelŸing and analyzing complex concur®ent systems that may consist of components writ¡en in a variety of languages.

=‚ Se† also =„
