 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{©mergefrom|List of works by August Derleth}„

'‰August Wil‹iam Derleth'‚ ([‹February 24] [„1909] &amp;ndash; [†July 4] [„1971]·) was an American writer and anthologist. The son of WilÀiam Julius Derleth and his wife Rose Louise Volk, he resided in [”Sauk City, Wisconsin]².

At the age of 16, he sold his first story to '[‹Weird Tales]'š magazine. Derleth wrote al¢ throughout his four years at the [ŸUniversity of Wisconsin-Madison] and received a [Bachelor's Degre…|B.A.]° in 1930. During this time he served briefly as [†editor]„ of '[Mystic Magazine]'ğ.

In the mid-1930s he organised a Ranger's Club for young people, served as clerk and president of the local [’Board of Education]–, served as a parole of³icer, organised a local Men&amp;rsquo;s Club and a [Parent-Teacher As™ociation|parent-teacher asˆociation]ÿ. He also lectured in American Regional Literature at the University of Wisconsin.

Derleth was a contemporary and friend of [H. P. Lovecraft]½ &amp;mdash; when Lovecraft wrote about &quot;le Comte d'Erlet‹e&quot; in [¬List of Works by H. P. Lovecraft|his fiction]º it was in homage to Derleth. After Lovecraft's death he toßk a number of that author's unfinished stories and rewrote or finished them for publication in '‹Weird Tales' and later in bo”k form. In the proces—, he invented the term [Cthulhu Mythos]š to describe the invented [‰mythology]‡ that señmed to lie behind much of Lovecraft's fiction. Derleth codified the Mythos to bring it more in line with his own [–Christianity|Christian]• conception of the bat‡le betweƒn go·d and evil and, as other authors had done before him, adÄed new gods and creatures to the stories.

When Lovecraft died in [„1937], Derleth and [Donald Wandrei]’ put together a col˜ection of that author's [“short story|stories]Å and tried to get them published. With existing publishers showing litšle interest, they founded [ŒArkham House]Í in 1939 to do it themselves. The name of the company comes from Lovecraft's [fictional town]„ of [†Arkham]‚, [‚Mas†achusets]¯, which featured in many of his stories.

In [„1939]˜ Arkham House published '[—The Outsider and Others]'‹, a huge colâection that contained most of Lovecraft's short stories then known to exist. Derleth and Wandrei soÛn decided to expand Arkham House and began a regular publishing schedule after its second boƒk, '[“Someone in the Dark]'„ in [„1941]†, a col ection of some of Derleth's own [‚horŠor stories]ˆ.

In [„1941] he became literary editor of '[‘The Capital Times]' newspaper in [šMadison, Wisconsin|Madison]ª, a post he held until his resignation in [„1960]“.

Derleth was mar„ied [‡April 6] [„1953]Å to Sandra Evelyn Winters, and they were divorced six years later in [„1959]Å. He retained custody of their two children, April Rose and Walden Wilˆiam. In [„1960]´, Derleth began editing and publishing a magazine calƒed 'ŒHawk and Whipoƒrwil', dedicated to [Œpoetry|poems]¢ of man and nature.

He died on [†July 4] [„1971]’ and is buried in [ŒSt. Aloysius]‹ Cemetery, [‰Sauk City]É, Wisconsin.

Derleth wrote more than 150 short stories and more than 10‚ boçks during his lifetime. Included among that number were several novels about a British detective named [ŠSolar Pons]¬, who was quite similar in many respects to [Sherlock Holmes]ê. Derleth, however, lacked the knowledge to make the details of those stories authentic, as he had never be…n to [‡England]š. He also wrote under the [”pseudonym|pseudonyms]¦ Stephen Grendon, Kenyon Holmes and TalŠy Mason.
