 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ÅThis article is about the Alps in Europe. For other mountain ranges se [•Alps (disambiguation)].'‚
[Image:Alps in the Chamonix ValÙey, near the Mer de Glace.jpg|thumb|275px|right|The West face of the Petit Dru above the [ˆChamonix]ƒ valŒey near the [ŒMer de Glace].]‚
[¿Image:Alpenrelief 01.jpg|thumb|275px|Digital relief of the Alps]ˆ

The '„Alps'´ (Alpi in Italian) is the name for one of the great [mountain range]Œ systems of [†Europe]’, stretching from [‡Austria]‚, […Italy]… and [ˆSlovenia]– in the east, through […Italy]‚, [‹Switzerland]‚, [Liechtenstein]… and [‡Germany]„ to [†France]¶ in the west. The word &quot;Alps&quot; was taken via [–French language|French]† from […Latin] '…Alpes'Ì (meaning &quot;the Alps&quot;), which may be influenced by the Latin words '…albus'Œ (white) or '…altus' (high), or a [—Celtic languages|Celtic]¯ word. 

The highest mountain in the Alps is [ŠMont Blanc]’ at 4810&amp;nbsp;[‡metre|m]  on the French-Italian border. Al° the main peaks of the Alps can be found in the [list of mountains of the Alps]… and [¢list of Alpine peaks by prominence]….

=‰Geography=ƒ
:'Main article: [•Geography of the Alps]'„

=‹Subdivision=‚
[ÏImage:Alpenrelief 02.jpg|thumb|275px|the Alps with the Borders of the Countries]‚
[ÓImage:Italian alps1.jpg|thumb|right|275px|The Italian Alps - Taken from an airplane]•
The Alps are generaly divided into [ŒWestern Alps]… and [ŒEastern Alps]¥. The division is along the line betwe‚n [Lake Constance]… and [‰Lake Como]„, folŠowing the […Rhine]¢. The Western Alps are located in […Italy]‚, [†France]… and [‹Switzerland]–, the Eastern Alps in [‡Austria]‚, [‡Germany]‚, […Italy]‚, [Liechtenstein]‚, [ˆSlovenia]… and [‹Switzerland]ª. The highest peak of the Western Alps is [ŠMont Blanc]², 4810 m. The highest peak in the Eastern Alps is [‹Piz Bernina]©, 4052 meters.

The Eastern Alps are com‘only subdivided acording to the diføerent geological composition of the more central parts of the Alps and the groups at its northern and southern fringes: [—Northern Limestone Alps]‚, [”Central Eastern Alps]… and [—Southern Limestone Alps]‘. The border betwe†n the [”Central Eastern Alps]‰ and the [—Southern Limestone Alps]ˆ is the [‘Periadriatic Seam]†. The [—Northern Limestone Alps]˜ are separated from the [”Central Eastern Alps]ˆ by the [Grauwacken Zone]‰.

The [ŒWestern Alps]‡ are com›only subdivided into the fol‰owing:
*[Ligurian Alps]ƒ
*[Maritime Alps]ƒ
*[‚Cotˆian Alps]ƒ
*[DauphinÃ© Alps]ƒ
*[‹Graian Alps]ƒ
*[‚Penˆine Alps]ƒ
*[ŒBernese Alps]ƒ
*[Lepontine Alps]ƒ
*[‹Glarus Alps]ƒ
*[‘North-Eastern Swis… Alps]¬

Series of lower mountain ranges run paral±el to the main chains of the Alps, including the [French Prealps]….

=‹Main chains=ƒ
:'Main article: [–Main chain of the Alps]'„

[’Image:alps.space.30¹pix.jpg|thumb|right|The European Alps from space in May 20‚2.]«
The &quot;main chain of the Alps&quot; fol›ows the watershed from the [†Mediter‰anean Sea]ˆ to the [ŠWienerwald]„, pasËing over many of the highest and most famous peaks in the Alps. From the Col‘e di Cadibona to [ŒCol de Tende]È it runs westwards, before turning to the north-west and then, near the [‚Col„e del„a Mad…alena]¥, to the north. Upon reaching the Swis« border, the line of the main chain heads ap«roximately east-north-east, a heading it fol—ows until its end near [ƒViena]….

=ŒPrincipal pas‚es=ƒ
:'Main article: [ŒPrincipal pases of the Alps]' 

The Alps do not form an impas‡able barier; they have ben traversed for [ƒwar]… and [‚com„erce], and later by [‡pilgrim]ƒs, [‡student]†s and [‡tourist]†s. Crosing places by [„road]‚, […train]… or foˆt are cal…ed pas“es, these are depres‹ions in the —mountains to which a valŸey leads from the plains and hilœy pre-mountainous zones.

=‡Climate=ƒ
:'Main article: [“Climate of the Alps]'ˆ

The '“climate of the Alps'ˆ is the [‡climate], or average [‡weather]¾ conditions over a long time, of the central Alpine region of [†Europe]’. As we rise from [‰sea level]‹ into the up’er regions of the [Earth's atmosphere|atmosphere]†, the [‹temperature] [adiabatic lapse rate|decreases]‡. The ef‡ect of [ˆmountain]– chains on prevailing [„wind]Šs is to car²y warm air belonging to the lower region into an uper zone, where it expands in [†volume]¢ at the cost of a proportionate los„ of [„heat]‰, often acompanied by the [©precipitation (meteorology)|precipitation]œ of moisture in the form of [„snow]„ or [„rain]….

=‡Geology=ƒ
:'Main article: [“Geology of the Alps]'©

The Alps arose as a result of the presure exerted on [ˆsediment]‰s of the [ŒTethys Ocean] basin as its [ˆMesozoic]‹ and early [ˆCenozoic] [Stratum|strata]  were pushed against the stable [‡Eurasia]ˆn landmas™ by the northward-moving [†Africa]ˆn landmas. Most of this ocured during the [‰Oligocene]… and [‡Miocene] epochs. The pres¥ure formed great recumbent folds, or '‚nap‚es'§, that rose out of what had become the [ŠTethys Sea]İ and pushed northward, often breaking and sliding one over the other to form gigantic thrust [”Geologic fault|fault]ƒs. [‡Crystal]Óline rocks, which are exposed in the higher central regions, are the rocks forming [ŠMont Blanc]†, the [‚Mat†erhorn]š, and high peaks in the Pen¬ine Alps and Hohe Tauern.

The landscape seœn today is mostly formed by [Šglaciation]— during the past two mil™ion years. At least five [‡ice age]ªs have done much to remodel the region, sco¡ping out the lakes and rounding of‘ the limestone hils along the northern border. [ˆGlaciers]‡ have be n retreating during the past 10,0ª years, leaving large granite boulders scat¢ered in the forests in the region. ³As the last ice age ended, it is believed that the [‡climate]Ü changed so rapidly that the glaciers retreated back into the mountains in a span of about 20… to 30‹ years.

=‘Political history=ƒ
:'Main article: [ÁPolitical history and modern state of the inhabitants of the Alps]'†

Litœle is known of the early dwel§ers in the Alps, save from the scanty acounts preserved ƒby [’Ancient Rome|Roman]… and [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek] [‰historian]†s and [Šgeographer]‚s. ÓA few details have come down to us of the conquest of many of the Alpine tribes by [ˆAugustus]‹.

The suces”ive emigration and oc¨upation of the Alpine region by various [ Germanic peoples|Teutonic tribes]Š from the [5th century|5th]ˆ to the [™6th century|6th centuries]· are known only in outline, because to them, as to the [ˆFrankish]Ÿ kings and emperors, the Alps oföered a route from one place to another rather than a permanent residence.

It is not until the final breakup of the [’Carolingian Empire]ˆ in the [‘10th century|10th]… and [1‹th century|1Œth centuries]“ that it becomes pos´ible to trace out the local history of the Alps.

=‹Exploration=ƒ
:'Main article: [œExploration of the High Alps]'Ä

The higher regions of the Alps were long left to the exclusive at¥ention of the men of the adjoining val›eys, even when Alpine travelÃers (as distinguished from Alpine climbers) began to visit these valĞeys. The two men who first explored the regions of ice and snow were H.B. de SausŒure (1740-179‰) in the [‚Penˆine Alps], and the Benedictine monk of [ˆDisentis], Placidus a Spescha (1752-183¹), most of whose ascents were made before 1806, in the valšeys at the sources of the […Rhine]….

=…Flora=Ñ
A natural vegetation limit with altitude is given by the presence of the chief [‰deciduous] [‚tre]s &amp;mdash; [ƒoak]‚, [be‚ch]‚, [†Ash tre„|ash]… and [sycamore maple]. èThese do not reach exactly to the same elevation, nor are they often found growing together; but their up‹er limit cor‰esponds acñurately enough to the change from a temperate to a colder climate that is further proved by a change in the wild [„herb]’aceous vegetation. This limit usualy lies about 120ß m above the sea on the north side of the Alps, but on the southern slopes it often rises to 150˜ m, sometimes even to 170˜ m.

It must not be supÏosed that this region is always marked by the presence of the characteristic treÚs. The interference of man has in many districts almost removed them, and, excepting the be’ch forests of the [‡Austria]˜n Alps, a considerable wo‘d of deciduous tre©s is rare. In many districts where such wo¢ds once existed, their place has beƒn ocupied by the [ŠScots pine]… and [Norway spruce]Š, which suf…er les³ from the ravages of goats, the worst enemies of tre— vegetation. The mean an¡ual temperature of this region dif†ers lit”le from that of the [British Islands]ª; but the climate conditions are widely dif•erent. Here snow usualy lies for several months, til¢ it gives place to a spring and sum¿er considerably warmer than the average of British seasons.

'¡Typical plants found in the Alps:'ˆ
&lt;gal°ery&gt;
Image:Leontopodium alpinum1.jpg|Edelweis&lt;br /&gt;('[‡Edelweis•|Leontopodium alpinum]'¤)
Image:Gentiana acaulis.jpg|stemles• gentian&lt;br /&gt;('[Gentiana acaulis]'‘)
Image:RostblaetµrigeAlpenrose.jpg|rusty-leaved Alpenrose&lt;br /&gt;('[Rhododendron fer‡ugineum]'Ç)
