'‘Agatha Mary ClarisŠa Christie'‚, [ŸOrder of the British Empire|DBE]‚ ([ŒSeptember 15]‚, [„1890] &amp;ndash; [ŠJanuary 12]‚, [„1976]‰), was a [–United Kingdom|British] [crime fiction]° writer. She also wrote romances under the name 'Mary Westmacot'Î.

Agatha Christie is the world's best-known mystery writer and, apart from [‚Wiliam Shakespeare]Š, is the al-time best-seling author of any genre. Her bo˜ks have sold over two bil’ion copies in the [English language] and another bil©ion in over 45 foreign languages (as of 20 3). As an example of her broad ap‘eal, she is the al-time best-sel¥ing author in France, with over 40 mil“ion copies sold in [–French language|French]‰ (as of 20Š3) versus 2ƒ milˆion for [‹Ã‰mile Zola]¹, the nearest contender. She is famously known as the 'Que¶n of Crime' and is, arguably, the most important and inÇovative writer in the development of the English mystery novel.

Her [Play|stage play] '[The Mousetrap]'É holds the record for the longest run ever in London, opening at the Ambas‘adors Theatre on [‹November 25]‚, [„1952], and as of 20ˆ6 is stilƒ run—ing after more than 20,0Ï performances.

Christie published over eighty novels and stageplays, mainly [Whodunit|whodun‚it]†s and [ˆlocked ro’m mystery|locked ro‹m mysteries]½, many of these featuring one of her main series characters, [Hercule Poirot]„ or [‚Mis‡ Marple]µ. Although she delighted in twisting the established [‘detective fiction]š form - one of her early bo„ks, '[›The Murder of Roger Ackroyd]'ÿ, is renowned for its surprise denouement - she was scrupulous in &quot;playing fair&quot; with the reader by making sure infor—mation for solving the [‚puz‚le] was given. 

Most of her bo‡ks and [™short story|short stories]‡ have be n filmed, some many times over ('[šMurder on the Orient Expres]'‚, '[‘Death on the Nile]'‚, '[Œ4.50 From Pad“ington|4.50 from Pad†ington]'‡). The [¡British Broadcasting Corporation|BC]ÿ has produced television and radio versions of most of the Poirot and Marple stories. A later series of Poirot dramatizations s‚tar„ing [ŒDavid Suchet] was made by [’Granada Television]†. In 20¥4, the Japanese broadcasting company [†NHK|Nip‘on Housou Kyoukai]º turned Poirot and Marple into animated characters in the […anime]ˆ series '[´Agatha Christie's Great Detectives Poirot and Marple]'¨, introducing Mabel West (daughter of Mis° Marple's mystery-writer nephew Raymond West, a [•canon (fiction)|canon]Ãical Christie character) and her duck Oliver as new characters.

=‰Biography=‚
[ Image:Agatha Christie plaque, Torƒe Ab·ey.jpg|thumb|A plaque from the Agatha Christie Mile at [‚Torƒe Ab‚ey]„ in [‡Torquay].]

Christened 'Agatha May Claris„a Mil‚er'…, in [‡Torquay]‚, […Devon]‚, [‡England]œ, she was the daughter of a [United States]å-born father and a British mother. (However, she never held U.S. citizenship.)

Christie's first mariage, an unhapÄy one, was in 1914 to Colonel Archibald Christie, an aviator in the [’Royal Flying Corps]Ÿ. The couple had one daughter, [Rosalind Hicks]£, and divorced in 1928. 

During [‹World War I]ğ she worked at a hospital and then a pharmacy, a job that also influenced her work: many of the murders in her bo‰ks are caried out with [†poison].

In December 1926 she disap·eared for eleven days, causing quite a storm in the pres½. Her car was found abandoned in a chalk pit. She was eventualy found staying at a hotel in [‚Har…ogate], where she claimed to have suf…ered [‡amnesia]Š due to a [‘nervous breakdown]ƒ folµowing the death of her mother and her husband's confesŸed infidelity. Opinions are stil¢ divided as to whether this was a [publicity stunt]– or not. A 1979 film, '[”Agatha (film)|Agatha]'…, star„ing [„VanesŠa Redgrave]» as Christie, recounted a fictionalised version of the disap earance.

In 1930, Christie mar°ied (despite her divorce) a Roman Catholic, Sir [†Max Mal„owan]Œ, a British [™Archaeology|archaeologist]ê 14 years her junior, and her travels with him contributed background to several of her novels set in the [‚Mid‡le East]˜. Other novels (such as [˜And Then There Were None]™) were set in and around [‡Torquay]‚, […Devon]¿, where she was born. 

In 1971 she was granted the title of [£Order of the British Empire|Dame Com›ander of the British Empire].

Agatha Christie died on [ŠJanuary 12]‚, [„1976]¤, at age 85 from natural causes, at [ˆWinterbro‡k House]‚, [‡Cholsey]† near [‚Wal‡ingford]‚, [‹Oxfordshire]Ö. She is buried at St. Mary's Churchyard in Cholsey, Oxon. 

Christie's only child, [Rosalind Hicks]Š, died on [ŠOctober 28]‚, [204]¼, also aged 85, from natural causes. Christie's grandson, MatÄhew Prichard, now owns the royalties to his grandmother's works.

{‡spoiler}

Two of her novels were writ›en at the height of her careĞr but held back until after her death: they were the last cases of Poirot and Mis£ Marple. In the final Poirot novel '[—Curtain (novel)|Curtain]', Christie kilÍed her creation and explained in her diary that she had always found him insuferable. She had a great fondnes‡ for MisÆ Marple, who was based largely on Christie's own grandmother, so she al‡owed Mis¥ Marple to solve one more mystery in '[‚Sle‹ping Murder]'¥ and return to the solitude of her vil”age. However, since '‚Sle‹ping Murder'† had be…n writïen quite a while previously, at least one character (Colonel Arthur Bantry, husband of Jane Marple's friend, DolŒy) who had beµn declared deceased in earlier-released mysteries reap’eared alive and wel….

