 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'‘For other uses, se [Plague|Plague (disambiguation)].'„

'ŠThe Plague'‚ ('[’French language|fr].' 'ˆLa Peste'‡) is a […novel]„ by [ŒAlbert Camus], published in [„1947]‰, that telÈs the story of medical workers finding solidarity in their labor as the [Algeria|Algerian]‰ city of [„Oran] is swept by a [pandemic|plague]ò. It asks a number of questions relating to the nature of destiny and the human condition. The characters in the bo¶k, ranging from doctors to vacationers to fugitives, al“ help to show the ef¦ects the plague has on a populace.

{‡spoiler}Š

General©y taken as a metaphoric treatment of the [‘French resistance]„ to [„Nazi]‚ ocupation during [ŠWorld War I]‚, 'ŠThe Plague'Ï is interpreted to mean much more. Camus uses extreme hardships (e.g., pain, sufĞering, and death) to represent our human world. The story is told through the narÅative of the main character, Dr. Rieux, whose decidedly existential acÿount of events in the story is not only helpful in exploring the philosophy of existentialism, but also in making this a metaphŸor of the nature of life and suf•ering. Although his aproach in the boÊk is severe, he emphasizes the ideas that we ultimately have no control, ir²ationality of life is inevitable, and he further il¶ustrates the human reaction towards the â€˜absurdâ€™. 'ŠThe Plague'Å represents how the world deals with the philosophical notion of the [Absurdism|Absurd]´, a theory which Camus himself helped to define.

