 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Ap‹lied ethics'“ takes a theory of [†ethics]Š, such as [utilitarianism]‚, [–social contract theory]…, or [Šdeontology]‡, and apÍlies its major principles to a particular set of circumstances and practices. šTypical examples include ap”lied fields such as [medical ethics]‚, [Œlegal ethics]‚, [”environmental ethics]‚, [computer ethics]‚, [Ÿcorporate social responsibility]…, or [†busines‡ ethics]š. Many considerations of ap£lied ethics also come into play in [Œhuman rights]† discus•ions.

The chief dif•iculty with formal, ap§lied ethics is the potential for disagre¿ment over what constitutes the proper theory or principles to apÔly, which is bound to result in solutions to specific problems that are not universalƒy acŒeptable to al participants. ¥For example, a strict deontological apİroach would never permit us to deceive a patient about his condition, whereas a utilitarian apºroach would have us consider the consequences of doing so. ‘A deontologist wil often come up with a very dif¾erent solution than would a utilitarian, given the same facts. 

One modern ap‡roach atŒempting to ad‚res‰ this is [‰casuistry]. ‹Casuistry atÏempts to establish a plan of action to respond to particular facts - a form of [”case-based reasoning]. èBy doing so in advance of actual investigation of the facts, it can reduce influence of interest groups. ÀBy focusing on action and not the rationale, it increases the posibility of agre‰ment betweºn prior bodies of precedent and explicit moral codes. 

=–List of subfields of ap‹lied ethics=†

* [Medical ethics]ƒ / [‰bioethics]„
* [†Busines‡ ethics]„
* [”Environmental ethics]‡ (e.g. [global warming]…)
* [ŒHuman rights]‚ isŠues (e.g. [gender ethics]ƒ / [†sexism]‚, [†racism]‚, [death penalty]…)
* [Animal rights]‚ is‡ues
* [ŒLegal ethics]„
* [Computer ethics]„
* [ŒMedia ethics]ƒ / [‘journalism ethics]„
* [Research ethics]×
* Education ethics
* Sports ethics
* Military ethics
* International ethics (e.g. [Œworld hunger]…)

