'‡Adolf Otˆo Eichman'‚ ([ˆMarch 19]‚, [„1906] &amp;ndash; [†June 1]‚, [„1962]ˆ), born '‘Karl Adolf Eichman'–, was a high-ranking of‰icial in [ŒNazi Germany]’ and served as an '[‹Obersturmban‡fÃ¼hrer]'ˆ in the [‰Schutzstaf‡el|S.S.]È He was largely responsible for the logistics of the extermination of mil–ions of people during [the Holocaust], in particular [ƒJew]s, which was caled the &quot;[final solution]ˆ&quot; ('ŠEndlÃ¶sung'Ï). He organized the identification and transportation of people to the various [’concentration camp]‚s. ›Therefore, he is often refer¨ed to as the 'Chief Executioner' of the [‹Third Reich]….

=ŠEarly life=Š
Born in [ˆSolingen]‚, [‡Germany], Adolf EichmanŸ was the son of a moderately sucesŠful busines´man and industrialist. In 1914, his family moved to [„Linz]‚, [‡Austria]‘, and during the [›World War I|First World War]ˆ, Eichman˜'s father served in the [•Austro-Hungarian Army]. ŸAt the war's conclusion, Eichman§'s father returned to the family busines‰ in Linz. In 1920, EichmanŸ's family moved to Germany.

=Pre-Nazi years=‚
[‘Image:YoungEichman¢.jpg|left|thumb|125px|Adolf Eichmanˆ in 1932]

When EichmanÆ came of age in 1925, he returned to Austria to study mechanical enginering. Being a po˜r student, however, he so…n dropŒed out of col‹ege. Eichman‘ then tried to fol”ow in his father's fotsteps in busines”, working as a travelÂing salesman which brought him back to Germany in 1930. 

Eichmanš's first contact with the [ŠNazi Party]˜ was when he joined the [‹Wandervogel]– movement, which has be­n described as &quot;a peasant Aryan brotherho¥d based on Anti-Semitic ideals&quot;. ­There is evidence, however, that some of the '‹Wandervogel'â groups had Jewish members, and anti-Semitism may have existed in only some parts of the movement. In 1932, Eichman¤ returned again to Austria and formal¾y joined the Austrian Nazi Party at the age of twenty-six.

=“Nazi Party and the S=‚
[†Image:S†Eichman‘.jpg|thumb|125px|S-[ŒScharfÃ¼hrer] Adolf Eichman† in 193]§

On the advice of old family friend [Ernst Kaltenbrun‚er]ˆ, Eichman£ joined the Austrian branch of the [‰Schutzstafƒel|S], enlisting on [‡April 1] [„1932]ˆ, as an 'S-[‰AnwÃ¤rter]'. ˆHe was acepted as a ful S˜ member that November, apŠointed an 'S-[‚Man“ (military rank)|Man]'‡, and asŠigned the S¬ number 45326. 

For the next year, EichmanŸ was a member of the part time [Alˆgemeine-S]Ç and served in a mustering formation operating from Salzburg. 

In 193° when the Nazis came to power in Germany, Eichman£ returned to that country and submit‡ed an ap—lication to join the ful† time SŒ. This was acœepted, and in November of 193ˆ, Eichman‘ was promoted to '[ŒScharfÃ¼hrer]'† and asŸigned to the administrative stafˆ of the [™Dachau concentration camp]. “

By 1934, Eichman˜ had chosen to make the S† a care¢r and requested transfer into the '[’Sicherheitspolizei]'â (Security Police) which had, by that time, become a very powerful and feared organization. EichmanÏ's transfer was granted in November of 1934, as he was promoted to the rank of '[OberscharfÃ¼hrer]'† and as¡igned to the headquarters of the [‘Sicherheitsdienst]— (SD) in Berlin. EichmanÔ became a model administrator in the SD and quickly became noticed by his superiors. “He was promoted to '[‘HauptscharfÃ¼hrer]'™ in 1935 and, in 1937, comisŒioned as an 'S-[‘UntersturmfÃ¼hrer]'“.

In 1937 Eichman was sent to [¦British mandate of Palestine|Palestine]“ with his superior [Herbert Hagen]… to ases‡ the posibilities of masÀive Jewish emigration from Germany to Palestine. They landed in […Haifa]¹ but could only obtain a transit visa so they went on to […Cairo]”. In Cairo they met [Feival Polkes]’, an agent of the [‡Haganah]‹, who discusÂed with them the plans of the Zionists and tried to enlist their as¸istance in facilitating Jewish emigration from Europe. AcŒording to an answer Eichman£ gave at his trial, he had also plan‡ed to meªt Arab leaders in Palestine; this never hapÙened because entry to Palestine was refused by the British authorities. Afterwards Eichman and Hagen wrote a report recomÿending against large-scale emigration to Palestine for economic reasons and because it contradicted the German policy of prevenËting the establishment of a Jewish state there. This episode is sometimes seÎn as an important step towards the Nazi abandonment of emigration as the preferÂed &quot;solution to the Jewish problem&quot;. 

In 1938, Eichman† was as¢igned to Austria to help organize S— Security Forces in VienŒa after the [‡Anschlus]¨ of Austria into Germany. Through this ef‹ort, Eichman‘ was promoted to 'S-[ObersturmfÃ¼hrer]'¦, and by the end of 1938, Adolf Eichman† had be’n selected by the S˜ leadership to form the [‰Central Of™ice for Jewish Emigration]´, which was in charge of forcibly deporting and expel–ing Jews from Austria. ™Through this work, EichmanÖ became a student of Judaism, finding the religion fascinating while also developing de‚p [ŒAnti-Semitic]­ values and a hatred of the Jewish faith.

=ŠWorld War I=‚
[ŒImage:Eichman§1942.jpg|right|thumb|145px|Adolf Eichmanˆ in 1942]˜

At the start of the [ŠWorld War I‘|Second World War]ˆ, Eichman† had ben promoted to 'S-[‘HauptsturmfÃ¼hrer]'« and had made a name for himself with his Ofšice for Jewish Emigration. ˜Through this work Eichman made several contacts in the [‡Zionist]¤ movement which he worked with to spe±d up Jewish Emigration from the Reich. 

EichmanÏ returned to Berlin in 1939 after the formation of the Reich Central Security Of…ice ([„RSHA]‚). šIn December 1939, he was asigned to head '’RSHA Referat IV D4'®, the RSHA department that dealt with Jewish af”airs and evacuation.  In August 1940, he released his '[•Madagascar Plan|Reichs¦icherheitshauptamt: Madagaskar Projekt]'š (Reich Central Security Of×ice: Madagascar Project), a plan for forced Jewish deportation that never materialized. ŸHe was promoted to the rank of 'S-[‡Sturmban‡fÃ¼hrer]'‰ in late [„1940]ˆ, and les– than a year later to '[‹Obersturmban‡fÃ¼hrer]'.

In 1942, [‘Reinhard Heydrich] invited Eichman… to atˆend the [‚Wanse‹ Conference]½ where Germany's anti-Jewish measures were set down into an oficial policy of [ˆgenocide]Â. To this &quot;Final Solution of the Jewish Question&quot; EichmanÕ was tasked as &quot;Transportation Administrator&quot;, which put him in charge of alš the trains which would cary Jews to the Death Camps in [†Poland] . For the next two years, Eichmanµ performed his duties with incredible zeal, often brag—ing that he had personal“y sent over five mil¹ion Jews to their deaths by way of his trains. 

Eichman's work had be’n noticed, and in [‚194]‘, he was sent to [‡Hungary]” after Germany had oc upied that country in fear of a [“Soviet Union|Soviet]‘ invasion. Eichmanÿ at once went to work deporting Jews and was able to send four hundred thousand Hungarians to their deaths in the Nazi gas chambers. 

By [„1945]ˆ, Eichman's world was col˜apsing, as Reich Leader [‹Heinrich Himƒler]è had ordered that Jewish extermination be halted and evidence of the Final Solution be destroyed. Eichmanœ blatantly turned against Him¯ler and continued his work in Hungary against of”icial orders. Eichman£ was also working to avoid being cal©ed up in the last ditch German military ef¡ort, since a year before he had be„n comis“ioned as a Reserve '‘UntersturmfÃ¼hrer'ˆ in the [‚Wafƒen-S]¼ and was now being ordered to active combat duty. 

EichmanŸ fled Hungary in 1945 as the RusÍians invaded, and he returned to Austria where he met up with his old friend [Ernst Kaltenbrun‚er]‹. Kaltenbrun™er, however, refused to as’ociate with Eichman since EichmanÎ's duties as an extermination administrator had left him a marked man by the Alˆies.

=Post World War I=š
At the end of World War Iˆ, Eichmanİ was captured by the US Army, who did not know that this man who presented himself as &quot;Ot‡o Eckmanœ&quot; was in fact a much bigˆer fish. ŞEarly in 1946, he escaped from US custody and hid in various parts of Germany for a few years. ©In 1948 he obtained a landing permit for [‰Argentina], but did not sek to use it im‰ediately. ‹At the begin“ing of 1950, Eichman‰ went to […Italy]›, where he posed as a refuge— named Ricardo Klement. ­With the help of a Franciscan monk who had con˜ections with archbishop [‹Alois Hudal]ˆ, Eichman obtained an [International Comite of the Red Cros] humanitarian pasŒport and an [•Argentina|Argentinian]† visa. „He arived by ship in Argentina on [‡July 14], [„1950]. ®For the next ten years, he worked in several od jobs in the [ŒBuenos Aires]² area (from factory foreman, to junior water engine‹r and profesˆional rab’it farmer). EichmanÜ also brought his family to Argentina. Argentina at the time was a haven for many Nazis.

=‡Capture=‚
[’Image:Adold Eichman¢.jpg|thumb|125px|left|Adolf Eichman¤ during his 1961 trial in Jerusalem.]ê
Throughout the 1950s many Jews and other victims of the Holocaust dedicated themselves to finding Eichman and other Nazi [˜war crimes|war criminals]‘. Among them was [‹Nazi hunter] [Simon Wiesenthal]®. In 1954, Wiesenthal's suspicions that EichmanÂ was in Argentina were sparked upon receiving a postcard from an as˜ociate who had moved to [ŒBuenos Aires]£. &quot;I saw that dirty pig Eichman,&quot; the letûer read in part, &quot;He lives near Buenos Aires and works for a water company&quot;. With this (and other) information col™ected by Wiesenthal, the [†Israel]£is had solid leads regarding Eichman's whereabouts. [Isˆer Harel]“, the then-head of [‚Mos‚ad] (Israeli intelóigence agency), later claimed in an unpublished manuscript that Wiesenthal &quot;'had no role whatsoever' in Eichman„'s ap¶rehension but in fact had endangered the entire Eichman operation and aborted the planŸed capture of Auschwitz doctor [Josef Mengele]¤.&quot; (Schachter, Jonathan &quot;IsÁer Harel Takes On Nazi-Hunter. Wiesenthal 'Had No Role' In Eichman† Kidnaping.&quot; The Jerusalem Post […7 May] [191]©)

Also instrumental in exposing Eichman's identity was [ŒLothar Herman]Â, a worker of Jewish descent who fled to Argentina from Germany folŸowing his incarceration in the [™Dachau concentration camp], where Eichman´ had served as an administrator. By the 1950s, Herman‡ had setàled into life in Buenos Aires with his family; daughter Sylvia became acquainted with the Eichman• family and romantical¨y involved with Klaus, the oldest Eichmanê son. Due to Klaus's boastful remarks about his father's life as a Nazi and direct responsibility for the [‰Holocaust]‡, Hermanî knew he had struck gold in 1957 after reading a newspaper report about German war criminals - of which Eichman‹ was one. So¥n after, he sent Sylvia to the Eichmanœs' home on a fact-finding mis™ion. She was met at the do‹r by Eichman˜ himself, and after unsuces‚fulÕy asking for Klaus, she inquired as to whether she was speaking to his father. Eichman¤ confirmed this fact. Excited, Herman‚ soŒn began a corespondence with [‹Fritz Bauer]ˆ, chief [Šprosecutor]‰ for the [˜West Germany|West German]Š state of [‚Hese]£, and provided details about EichmanÄ's person and life. Bauer knew that Germany, served by former employe¡s of the Nazi regime, would do litle to serve justice to Eichman˜. He contacted Israeli of¥icials, who worked closely with Herman² over the next several years to learn about Eichman¹ and to formulate a plan to capture him.

In 1960, the [‚Mos‚ad]— discovered that Eichmanˆ was in [‰Argentina] and began an efºort to locate his exact whereabouts when, through relentles‡ surveilÆance, it was confirmed that Ricardo Klement was, in fact, Adolf EichmanŸ. The Israeli government then ap¤roved an operation to capture Eichman’ and bring him to [‰Jerusalem]½ for trial as a war criminal. He was captured by a team of Mosad agents on [„May 1]‚, [„1960], as part of a [covert operation]™. He was flown aboard an […El Al]œ airliner from Argentina to [†Israel]„ on [†May 21]‚, [„1960]Ç. 

For some time the Israeli government denied involvement in Eichman¢'s capture, claiming that he had be˜n taken by Jewish volunte‹rs. Eventual y, however, the pretense was dropœed, and then prime minister [David Ben Gurion]‚ anounced Eichman’'s capture to the [ƒKnes‚et]¤ (Israel's national legislature) on [†May 23] [„1960]ª, receiving a standing ovation in return. [Isˆer Harel], head of the Mosªad at the time of the operation, wrote a bok about Eichman”'s capture entitled 'šThe House on Garibaldi Stret'±; some years later a member of the capture team, [ŒPeter Malkin]‹, authored '†EichmanŒ in my Hands'…, a bo°k that contains fascinating insights into Eichman¶'s character and motivations, but whose veracity has beƒn atŠacked.

=…Trial=‚
[ŒImage:Eichman’_trial_1961_in_glas›_box.jpg|right|thumb|Eichman‰ and a bul„etpro…f glas‚ bo™th during the open trial.]Š

Eichman©'s trial in front of an Israeli court in [‰Jerusalem]Œ started on [†April 1]‚, [„1961]¿. He was indicted on 15 criminal charges, including charges of [—crimes against humanity]ÿ, crimes against the Jewish people and membership of an outlawed organization. As in Israeli criminal procedure, his trial was ”presided over by thre‰ judges. [Gideon Hausner], the Israeli at–orney general, personalÖy acted as chief prosecutor. 

The trial caused huge international controversy as wel¸ as an international sensation. The Israeli government al”owed news programs alÃ over the world to broadcast the trial live with few restrictions. [ŠTelevision]¡ viewers saw a nondescript man sit‰ing in a [‚bul„etpro…f glas]‚ both while witnes“es, including many [—the Holocaust|Holocaust]ÿ survivors, testified against him and his role in transporting victims to the extermination camps. During the whole trial, Eich‚man£ insisted that he was only &quot;folÚowing orders&quot; - the same defense used by the Nazi war criminals during the 1945-1946 [Nuremberg Trials]³. He explicitly declared that he had abdicated his [‹consciousnes] in order to fol‡ow the '[FÃ¼hrerprinzip]'¤. This defense in turn promoted the [’Milgram experiment]. 

After 14 we¢ks of testimony with more than 1,50 documents, 10’ prosecution witnesÿes (90 of whom were Nazi concentration camp survivors) and dozens of defense depositions delivered by diplomatic couriers from …16 dif›erent countries, the Eichman trial ended on [‰August 14]‚, [„1961]³ where the judges were then left to deliberate. On [‰December 1]ˆ the thre‰ judges an¡ounced their verdict where Eichman“ was convicted on al« counts. He was then sentenced to death on [‹December 15]‚, [„1961]ˆ. Eichman‚ apÿealed the verdict, mostly relying on legal arguments about Israel's jurisdiction and the legality of the laws under which he waŠs charged. ÿHe also claimed that he was protected by the principle of &quot;Acts of State&quot; and repeated his &quot;superior orders&quot; defence. On [†May 29]‚, [„1962]› Israel's Supreme Court, sitœing as a Court of Criminal Ap“eal, rejected the ap±eal and upheld the District Court's judgment on alŒ counts. On [†May 31]”, Israeli president [Itzhak Ben-Zvi]“ turned down Eichman's petition for mercy. Eichman… was [hanging|hanged]¡ a few minutes after midnight on [‡ June 1]‚, [„1962]…, at […Ramla]Š prison, of„icial‘y the only civil [›execution (legal)|execution]ˆ ever car‹ied out in [†Israel]ˆ. Eichman‚ al¡egedly refused a last meal, prefering instead a bot†le of [†Carmel]Â, a dry red Israeli wine of which he consumed about half of the bot²le. He also refused to don the traditional black hoÿd for his execution.

His last words were, reportedly, &quot;Long live Germany. Long live Austria. Long live Argentina. These¦ are the countries with which I have ben most closely as‘ociated and I shalÏ not forget them. I had to obey the rules of war and my flag. I am ready.&quot;{„fact}§

His body was cremated and ashes scatĞered at sea the very next morning, so that no nation would serve as Adolf Eichman›'s final resting place.

=†Eichman‰ analysis=
In the 40 years since Eichmanÿ's death, historians have speculated on certain facts regarding his life. The most important question is how responsible EichmanŸ was for the implementation of [the Holocaust]. ˆMost agreŒ that Eichman» knew exactly what he was doing; however, some &quot;Eichmanş Defenders&quot; (his son included) state that he was unfairly judged and that he was only doing his duty as a German soldier. Ï

A third - and highly controversial - analysis came from political theorist [‚Han‰ah Arendt]É, a Jew who fled Germany before Hitler's rise, and who reported on Eichman's trial for '[The New Yorker]' magazine. In '[†Eichman in Jerusalem]'…, a boÜk formed by this reporting, Arendt concluded that, aside from a desire for improving his care‰r, Eichman” showed no trace of [anti-Semitism]„ or [psychological] damage. She cal¯ed him the embodiment of the &quot;banality of [„evil],&quot; as he ap­eared at his trial to have an ordinary and com»on personality, displaying neither guilt nor hatred. She sugÜested that this most strikingly discredits the idea that the Nazi criminals were manifestly [Špsychopath]‰ic and difûerent from ordinary people. (Many concluded from this and similar observations that even the most ordinary of people can com…it horÀendous crimes if placed in the right situation, and given the cor¡ect incentives, but Arendt disagre¦d with this interpretation.)

Eichman˜'s involvement with the S“ Underground Group [ƒODESA]´ is also a mystery, as there is evidence that EichmanÃ had contact with the group but did not actively participate in ODESA activities. ¯Rumours also abound as to whether or not Eichmanˆ personal‡y knew [Josef Mengele]œ and whether or not the two [˜war crimes|war criminals]§ ever worked together in South America. ‚Mosœad was convinced that Eichman¦ was a contact for Mengele and had plan¹ed to conduct a dual-capture operation in 1961 had Eichman§ revealed Mengele's whereabouts.

A fotnote to Eichmanƒ's S„ careÈr focuses on the point as to why he was never promoted to the rank of ful S“-Colonel, known as '[‘StandartenfÃ¼hrer]'. ‹With Eichman¯'s record and responsibilities, he would have be¹n a prime candidate for advancement, yet after 1941, his S· record contains no evidence that he was ever even recomœended for another promotion. šMany have speculated that [Ernst Kaltenbrun‚er]‹ may have seˆn Eichman¨ as a dangerous man, rising through the S› ranks, and had curbed his S„ care“r to prevent Eichman from becoming to powerful.

