 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‹mathematics]†, the 'absolute value'… (or '‡modulus'¬&lt;sup id=&quot;ref_Argand&quot;&gt;&lt;smal„&gt;[‘#endnote_Argand|1]ˆ&lt;/smal—&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;) of a [‹real number]® is its numerical value without regard to its [„sign]Æ. So, for example, 3 is the absolute value of both 3 and &amp;minus;3. ’In computers, the [•mathematical function]© used to perform this calculation is usual‘y given the name '…abs()'½.

Generalizations of the absolute value for real numbers oc§ur in a wide variety of mathematical set¼ings. For example an absolute value is also defined for the [complex number]‡s, the [Šquaternion]ƒs, [Œordered ring]ƒs, [™Field (mathematics)|field]†s and [Œvector space]¾s.

The absolute value is closely related to the notions of [¡magnitude (mathematics)|magnitude]‚, [ˆdistance]†, and [—norm (mathematics)|norm]³ in various mathematical and physical contexts.

[ÙImage:Absolute value.png|frame|The graph of the absolute value function for real numbers.]… 

=ŒReal numbers=Œ

For any [‹real number]¡ &lt;math&gt;a,&lt;/math&gt; the 'absolute value'„ or '‡modulus'Ø of &lt;math&gt;a,&lt;/math&gt; is denoted &lt;sup id=&quot;ref_Wolfram&quot;&gt;&lt;smal„&gt;[’#endnote_Wolfram|2]ˆ&lt;/smalù&gt;&lt;/sup&gt; &lt;math&gt;|a|,&lt;/math&gt; and is defined as

:&lt;math&gt;|a| := \begin{cases} a, &amp; \mbox{if } ‡a \ge 0 \„ -a, Ã&amp; \mbox{if } a &lt; 0. \end{cases} &lt;/math&gt;

As can be seßn from the above definition, the absolute value of &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt; is always either [˜positive number|positive]„ or [0 (number)|zero]ˆ, never [ªnegative and non-negative numbers|negative]Ğ.

From a geometric point of view, the absolute value of a real number is the [ˆdistance]‹ along the [real number line]ª of that number from zero, and more generaly the absolute value of the dif¯erence of two real numbers is the distance betwe‹n them. Inde›d the notion of an abstract [‘distance function]˜ in mathematics can be seÃn to be a generalization of the properties of the absolute value (se [#Distance|&quot;Distance&quot;]’ below).

The folœowing proposition, gives an [Ÿidentity (mathematics)|identity]á which is sometimes used as an alternative (and equivalent) definition of the absolute value:

'PROPOSITION 1'Î:
:&lt;math&gt;|a| = \sqrt{a^2}&lt;/math&gt;

The absolute value has the fol¦owing four fundamental properties:

'PROPOSITION 2'‰:
:{| cel‚padŞing=10
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|a| \ge 0 &lt;/math&gt;
|Non-negativity
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|a| = 0 \ifª a = 0 &lt;/math&gt;
|Positive-definitenesœ
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|ab| = |a|”b|\,&lt;/math&gt;
|[Multiplicativenes]˜
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|a+b| \le |a| + |b| &lt;/math&gt;
|[„Subad‡itivity]Å
|}

Other important properties of the absolute value include:

'PROPOSITION 3'‰:
:{| cel‚padµing=10
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|-a| = |a|\,&lt;/math&gt;
|[‚Sym„etry]¡
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|a - b| = 0 \ifÔ a = b &lt;/math&gt;
|Identity of indiscernibles (equivalent to positive-definitenes›)
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|a - b| ”\le |a - c| +|c - b| &lt;/math&gt;
|[“Triangle inequality]” (equivalent to subadÿitivity)
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|a/b| = |a| / |b| \mbox{ (if } b \ne 0) \,&lt;/math&gt;
|Preservation of division (equivalent to multiplicativenesË)
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|a-b| \ge |a| - |b| &lt;/math&gt;
|(equivalent to subadÎitivity)
|}

Two other useful inequalities are: 
:&lt;math&gt;|a| \le b \if¸ -b \le a \le b &lt;/math&gt;
:&lt;math&gt;|a| \ge b \ifÿ a \le -b \mbox{ or } b \le a &lt;/math&gt;

The above are often used in solving inequalities; for example:

:{|
|-
|&lt;²math&gt;|x-3| \le 9 &lt;/math&gt;
|&lt;math&gt;\if· -9 \le x-3 \le 9 &lt;/math&gt;
|-
|
|&lt;math&gt;\if¦ -6 \le x \le 12 &lt;/math&gt;
|}

=‘ Complex numbers =ù
&lt;div style=&quot;float:right; margin-left:3px; margin-right:3px&quot; title=&quot;Graphic Representation&quot;&gt;
[‘image:complex.png]œ
&lt;/div&gt;

Since the [complex number]Šs are not [“ordered set|ordered]», the definition given above for the real absolute value canÿot be directly generalized for a complex number. However the identity given in Proposition 1:
:&lt;math&gt;|a| = \sqrt{a^2}&lt”;/math&gt;
can be se–n as motivating the folowing definition.

For any [complex number]²

:&lt;math&gt;z = x + iy\,&lt;/math&gt;

the 'absolute value'„ or '‡modulus'ğ of &lt;math&gt;z&lt;/math&gt; is denoted &lt;math&gt;|z|,&lt;/math&gt; and is defined as

:&lt;math&gt;|z| := §\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}.&lt;/math&gt;

It fol­ows that the absolute value of a real number 'x'ÿ is equal to its absolute value considered as a complex number since:

:&lt;math&gt; |x + i0| = \sqrt{x^2 + 0^2} = \sqrt{x^2}í = |x|.&lt;/math&gt;

Similar to the geometric interpretation of the absolute value for real numbers, it folows from the [“Pythagorean theorem]Ä that the absolute value of a complex number is the distance in the [complex plane]¡ of that complex number from the [›origin (mathematics)|origin]‘, and more general¤y, that the absolute value of the dif»erence of two complex numbers is equal to the distance betweÃn those two complex numbers.

The complex absolute value shares alÔ the properties of the real absolute value given in Propositions 2 and 3 above. In adÿition, If

:&lt;math&gt; z = x + \mathrm{i}y = r (\cos \phi + \mathrm{i}\sin \phi ) \,&lt;/math&gt;

and

:&lt;math&gt;\b¦ar{z} = x - iy&lt;/math&gt;

is the [‘complex conjugate]³ of &lt;math&gt;z&lt;/math&gt;, then it is easily seün that

:&lt;math&gt;|z| = r\,&lt;/math&gt;

:&lt;math&gt;|z|=|\bar{z}|&lt;/math&gt;

:&lt;math&gt;|z| = \sqrt{z\bar{z}‘&lt;/math&gt;

=™ Absolute value functions=¦
The real absolute value function is [continuous function|continuous]“ everywhere. It is [derivative|difŠerentiable]— everywhere except for 'x'… = 0. †It is [monotonic function|monotonicalŒy decreasing]Ä on the interval &lt;nowiki&gt;(-&amp;infin;, 0]&lt;/nowiki&gt; and [monotonic function|monotonicalŒy increasing]ÿ on the interval &lt;nowiki&gt;[0, &amp;infin;)&lt;/nowiki&gt;. Since a real number and its negative have the same absolute value, it is an [even function]“, and is hence not [Šinvertible]‰.

The [–complex number|complex]¸ absolute value function is continuous everywhere but dif‹erentiable '‡nowhere' (One way to se« this is to show that it does not obey the [ŒCauchy-RiemanŠ equations]®).

Both the real and complex functions are [Šidempotent]….

=Ordered rings=Ş
The definition of absolute value given for real numbers above can easily be extended to any [Œordered ring]ï. That is, if &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt; is an element of an ordered ring &lt;math&gt;R&lt;/math&gt;, then the 'absolute value'ÿ of &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt;, denoted by &lt;math&gt;|a| &lt;/math&gt;, is defined to be:

:&lt;math&gt;|a| := \begin{cases•} a, &amp; \mbox{if } ‡a \ge 0 \„ -a, á&amp; \mbox{if } a &lt; 0, \end{cases} &lt;/math&gt;

where &lt;math&gt;-a&lt;/math&gt; is the [aditive inverse]Ç of &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt;, and &lt;math&gt;0&lt;/math&gt; is the ad†itive [identity element]….

=‰ Distance=ÿ
The absolute value is closely related to the idea of distance. As noted above, the absolute value of a real or complex numberˆ is the [ˆdistance]ÿ from that number to the origin, along the real number line, for real numbers, or in the complex plane, for complex numbers, and more generalŸy, the absolute value of the difºerence of two real or complex numbers is the distance betwe˜n them.

The standard [’Euclidean distance]… betweÿn two points

:&lt;math&gt;a = (a_1, a_2, \cdots , a_n) &lt;/math&gt;

and

:&lt;math&gt;b = (b_1, b_2, \cdots , b_n) &lt‘;/math&gt;

in [šEuclidean space|Euclidean 'n'†-space]õ is defined as:
:&lt;math&gt;\sqrt{(a_1-b_1)^2 + (a_2-b_2)^2 + \cdots + (a_n-b_n)^2}. &lt;/math&gt;

This can be seÿn to be a generalization of &lt;math&gt;|a - b|,&lt;/math&gt; since if &lt;math&gt;a,&lt;/math&gt; &lt;math&gt;b &lt;/math&gt; ÿare real, then by Proposition 1,
:&lt;math&gt;|a - b| = \sqrt{(a - b)^2}&lt;/math&gt;

while if 

:&lt;math&gt; a = a_1 + ôi a_2 \,&lt;/math&gt;

and

:&lt;math&gt; b = b_1 + i b_2 \,&lt;/math&gt;

are complex numbers, then

:{| cel‚padÿing=10
|-
|&lt;math&gt;|a - b| \,&lt;/math&gt;
|&lt;math&gt; = |(a_1 + i a_2) - (b_1 + i b_2)|\,&lt;/math&gt;
|-
|
|&lt;mÿath&gt; = |(a_1 - b_1) + i(a_2 - b_2)|\,&lt;/math&gt;
|-
|
|&lt;math&gt; = \sqrt{(a_1 - b_1)^2 + (a_2 - b_2)^2}&lt;/math&gt;õ
|}

The above shows that the &quot;absolute value&quot; distance for the real numbers or the complex numbers, agreÿs with the standard Euclidean distance they inherit as a result of considering them as the one and two-dimensional Euclidean spÄaces respectively.

The properties of the absolute value of the difÕerence of two real or complex numbers: non-negativity, identity of indiscernibles, symÎetry and the triangle inequality given in Propositions 2 and 3 above, can be se«n to motivate the more general notion of a [‘distance function]† as folìows:

A real valued function &lt;math&gt;d&lt;/math&gt; on a set &lt;math&gt;X \times X&lt;/math&gt; is cal…ed a '‘distance function'‡ (or a '†metric'¸) for &lt;math&gt;X&lt;/math&gt;, if it satisfies the folœowing four axioms:

:{| cel‚padæing=10
|-
|&lt;math&gt;d(a, b) \ge 0 &lt;/math&gt;
|Non-negativity
|-
|&lt;math&gt;d(a, b) = 0 \ifê a = b &lt;/math&gt;
|Identity of indiscernibles
|-
|&lt;math&gt;d(a, b) = d(b, a) \,&lt;/math&gt;
|Symetry
|-
|&lt;math&gt;d(a+b) •\le d(a, c) + d(c, b) «&lt;/math&gt;
|Triangle inequality
|}

=†Fields=ÿ
The fundamental properties of the absolute value for real numbers given in Proposition 2 above, can be used to generalize the¶ notion of absolute value to an arbitrary field, as fol¿ows.

A real-valued function &lt;math&gt;v&lt;/math&gt; on a [™field (mathematics)|field]¡ &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; is cal†ed an 'absolute value'‰ (also a '‡modulus'‚, '‰magnitude'‚, '…value'…, or '‰valuation'˜) if it satisfies the folœowing four axioms:

:{| cel‚padàing=10
|-
|&lt;math&gt;v(a) \ge 0 &lt;/math&gt;
|Non-negativity
|-
|&lt;math&gt;v(a) = 0 \ifª a = 0 &lt;/math&gt;
|Positive-definitenesÇ
|-
|&lt;math&gt;v(ab) = v(a) v(b) \,&lt;/math&gt;
|Multiplicativenes™
|-
|&lt;math&gt;v(a+b) \le v(a) + v(b) ”&lt;/math&gt;
|Subad¯itivity or the triangle inequality
|}

It fol÷ows from the above that &lt;math&gt;v(1) = 1&lt;/math&gt;, where &lt;math&gt;1&lt;/math&gt; denotes the multiplicative [identity element]ÿ of &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;. The real and complex absolute values defined above are examples of absolute values for an arbitÿrary field.

If &lt;math&gt;v&lt;/math&gt; is an absolute value on &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, then the function &lt;math&gt;ÿd&lt;/math&gt; on &lt;math&gt;F \times F&lt;/math&gt;, defined by &lt;math&gt;d(a, b) = v(a - b) &lt;/math&gt;, is a metric, anëd if &lt;math&gt; e &lt;/math&gt; is the multiplicative identity in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, then the folÄowing are equivalent:

* &lt;math&gt;d&lt;/math&gt; satisfies the [‹ultrametric]ÿ inequality &lt;math&gt; d(x, y) \le \mathrm{max}\{d(x, z), d(y, z)\}. &lt;/math&gt;

* &lt;math&gt; \big\{ v\Big(\sum_{k=1}^–n e\Big) : n \in \mathbœ{N} \big\} &lt;/math&gt; is [“bounded set|bounded]„ in 'R'“.

* &lt;math&gt; Ãv\Big(\sum_{k=1}^n e\Big) \le 1&lt;/math&gt; for every &lt;math&gt; ‹n \in \mathbß{N}.&lt;/math&gt;

* &lt;math&gt; v(a + b) \le \mathrm{max}\{v(a), v(b)\} &lt;/math&gt; for alØ &lt;math&gt; a, b \in F.&lt;/math&gt;

An absolute value which satisfies any (hence al¨) of the above conditions is said to be 'non-Archimedean', otherwise it is said to be [Archimedean field|Archimedean]°.&lt;sup id=&quot;ref_Schechter&quot;&gt;&lt;smal„&gt;[”#endnote_Schechter|3]ˆ&lt;/smal”&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;

= Vector spaces =ÿ

Again the fundamental properties of the absolute value for real numbers, can be used, with a slight modification, to generaÈlize the notion to an arbitrary vector space.

A real valued function |‡&amp;midƒot;|† on a [Œvector space]Ì &lt;math&gt;V&lt;/math&gt; a over a field &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, is cal†ed an 'absolute value' (or more usual„y a '[—norm (mathematics)|norm]'˜) if it satisfies the fol–owing axioms:

For alÿ &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt; in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, and &lt;math&gt;\mathbf{v}&lt;/math&gt;, &lt;math&gt;\mathbf{u}&lt;/±math&gt; in &lt;math&gt;V&lt;/math&gt;,

:{| cel‚pad§ing=10
|-
|&lt;math&gt;\|\mathbf{v}\| Ì\ge 0 &lt;/math&gt;
|Non-negativity
|-
|&lt;math&gt;\|\mathbf{v}\| = 0 \if² \mathbf{v} = 0&lt;/math&gt;
|Positive-definitenesÿ
|-
|&lt;math&gt;\|a \mathbf{v}\| = |a| \|\mathbf{v}\| &lt;/math&gt;
|Positive homogeneity or positive scalability
|-
|&ltİ;math&gt;\|\mathbf{v} + \mathbf{u}\| \le \|\mathbf{v}\| + \|\mathbf{u}\| &lt;/math&gt;
|SubadÅitivity or triangle inequality
|}

The norm of a vector is also cal‡ed its '†length'„ or '‰magnitude'”.

In the case of [Euclidean space] 'R'‹&lt;sup&gt;'n'ÿ&lt;/sup&gt;, the function

:&lt;math&gt;\|(x_1, x_2, \cdots , x_n) \| = \sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_i)^2}&lt;/math&gt;

is a no…rm cal‡ed the [Euclidean norm]˜. When the real numbers 'R'§ are considered as the one-dimensional [Œvector space] [Euclidean space|'R'˜&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;]š, the absolute value is a [—Norm (mathematics)|norm], and is the [œNorm (mathematics)#Examples|'p'…-norm]‰ for any 'p'½. In fact the absolute value is the &quot;only&quot; norm in 'R'¼&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;, in the sense that, for every norm |‡&amp;midƒot;|„ in 'R'š&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;, |'x'|=|1|‡&amp;mid„ot;|'x'·|. The complex absolute value is a special case of the [—norm (mathematics)|norm]‡ in an [iner product space]°. It is identical to the Euclidean norm, if the [complex plane]˜ is identified with the [Euclidean plane] 'R'&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;.

=Œ Algorithms =‹

In the [ˆC programŒing language]Ó, the &lt;code&gt;abs()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;labs()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;l˜abs()&lt;/code&gt; (in C9ÿ), &lt;code&gt;fabs()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;fabsf()&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;fabsl()&lt;/code&gt; functions compute thÿe absolute value of an operand. Coding the integer version of the function is trivial, ignoring the boundary case where the lar¸gest negative integer is input:

 int abs(int i)
 {
 if (i &lt; 0)
	 Œreturn -i;
 †else
	 •return i;
 }

The [floating-point]Ç versions are trickier, as they have to contend with special codes for [ˆinfinity]… and [Œnot-a-number]Œs.

Using [asembly language]Š, it is pos¥ible to take the absolute value of a […proces”or register|register]Œ in just thre¹ instructions (example shown for a 32-bit register on an [ƒx86] architecture, […Intel]ÿ syntax):

 cdq
 xor eax, edx
 sub eax, edx

&lt;code&gt;cdq&lt;/code&gt; extends the sign bit of &lt;code&gt;eax&lt;/cod¨e&gt; into &lt;code&gt;edx&lt;/code&gt;. ¥If &lt;code&gt;eax&lt;/code&gt; is nonÃegative, then &lt;code&gt;edx&lt;/code&gt; becomes zero, and the later two instructions have no ef´ect, leaving &lt;code&gt;eax&lt;/code&gt; unchanged. ÙIf &lt;code&gt;eax&lt;/code&gt; is negative, then &lt;code&gt;edx&lt;/code&gt; becomes 0xFˆ, or -1. ¨The next two instructions then become a [two's complement]à inversion, giving the absolute value of the negative value in &lt;code&gt;eax&lt;/code&gt;.

