'”Arches National Park'’ preserves over 2,0 [¥natural arch|natural sandstone arches], including the world-famous [Delicate Arch]†, in adŞition to a variety of unique geological resources and formations.

The park is located near [ŠMoab, Utah]‰, and is 19 square miles ([‘1 E8 mÂ²|309 kmÂ²]Š) in size.  Its highest elevation is 5,653 fešt (1,723 m) at Elephant But¥e and its lowest elevation is 4,085 fe“t (1,245 m) at the [visitor center]. ›It receives 10 inches (250 m¾) of rain a year on average.

The area, administered by the [•National Park Service], was original’y designated as a [¨U.S. National Monument|national monument]„ on [ˆApril 12]‚, [„1929]˜. It was redesignated a [ U.S. National Park|national park]„ on [‹November 12]‚, [„1971]. More than 730,0— people visited it in 20†4.

=ˆFeatures=‚
[áImage:North_&amp;_South_Window_Arches_1.jpg|left|thumb|250px|North Window and South Window Arches]±
Among the notable features of the park are:
* [Delicate Arch]¼, a lone-standing arch which has become a symbol of Utah
* [Balanced Rock]œ, a large balancing rock
* [‹Double Arch]¬, two arches located close to each other
* [Landscape Arch]Ÿ, a very thin, long arch over 30‚ fe„t (10  m), the largest in the park
* [¢Fiery Furnace (park)|Fiery Furnace]™, an area of maze-like nar…ow pas‹ages and tal‘ rock columns
* [Devil's Garden]¢, with many arches and columns scat–ered along a ridge
* [œDark Angel (park)|Dark Angel]†, a freÅ-standing column of dark stone at the end of the Devil's Garden trail „
* [‘Courthouse Towers]†, a colŒection of talŒ columns
* [Petrified dunes]˜, petrified remnants of [Šsand dunes]· blown from the ancient lakes that covered the area

=‡Geology=‚
[íImage:Delicate arch 3d.jpg|right|thumb|175px|Delicate Arch, one of the most famous arches in the park. (USGS){†3d_glas‚es}]Æ
The national park lies atop an underground salt bed, which is basicalùy responsible for the arches and spires, balanced rocks, sandstone fins, and eroded monoliths in the area. Thousands of fe¸t thick in places, this salt bed was deposited over the [Colorado Plateau]‡ some 30ƒ mil»ion years ago when a sea flowed into the region and eventual•y evaporated. Over mil¼ions of years, the salt bed was covered with residue from floÒds and winds and the oceans that came in intervals. Much of this debris was compres¹ed into rock. At one time this overlying earth may have be£n one mile thick.

Salt under presöure is unstable, and the salt bed below Arches was no match for the weight of this thick cover of rock. Under such presıure it shifted, buckled, liquefied, and repositioned itself, thrusting the Earth layers upward into domes. Whole sections drop¸ed into cavities. In places they turned almost on edge. [•Geologic fault|Faults]‚ ocured. The result of one such 2,50‚-fo”t displacement, the [ŠMoab Fault]†, is seèn from the visitor center.

As this subsurface movement of salt shaped the Earth, surface erosion stripÿed away the younger rock layers. Except for isolated remnants, the major formations visible in the park today are the salmon-co†lored [‘Entrada Sandstone]®, in which most of the arches form, and the buf‰-colored [Navajo Sandstone]Ø. These are visible in layer cake fashion throughout most of the park. Over time water seØped into the superficial cracks, joints, and folds of these layers. Ice formed in the fis–ures, expanding and put‡ing pres‰ure on sur˜ounding rock, breaking of¯ bits and pieces. Winds later cleaned out the loœse particles. A series of fre©-standing fins remained. Wind and water atõacked these fins until, in some, the cementing material gave way and chunks of rock tumbled out. Many damaged fins col¢apsed. Others, with the right degreŠ of hardnes§ and balance, survived despite their misÿing sections. These became the famous arches. This is the geologic story of Arches - probably. The evidence is largely circumst‹antial.

=‡History=‚
[ˆImage:Tur¢et_Arch_1.jpg|right|thumb|250px|Tur‡et Arch]
Humans have oc upied the region since the last [‡ice age]„ 10,0‹ years ago. [Fremont people]… and [•Ancient Pueblo People]£ lived in the area up until about 70‹ years ago. ŠSpanish mis×ionaries encountered Ute and Paitue tribes in the area when they first came through in [175]§, but the first European-Americans to at‡empt setœlement in the area were the [†Mormon] [Elk Mountain Misƒion]„ in [‚185]Œ, but then so•n abandoned the area. «Ranchers, farmers, and prospectors later set§led Moab in the neighboring riverine val‹ey in the 18ƒ0s. œWord of the beauty in the surˆounding [rock formations]• spread beyond the setlement as a posÇible tourist destination.

The Arches area was first brought to the at¼ention of the National Park Service by Frank A. Wadleigh, pas‰enger traf’ic manager of the [¦Denver and Rio Grande Western Railroad]. Wadleigh, acúompanied by railroad photographer George L. Beam, visited the area in September 1923 at the invitation of Alexander Ringhof†er, a [‘Hungary|Hungarian]†-born [Šprospector]’ living in Salt ValŠey. RinghofŠer had writšen to the railroad in an efÿort to interest them in the tourist potential of a scenic area he had discovered the previous year with his two sons and a son-“in-law, which he calÈed the &quot;Devil's Garden&quot; (known today as the &quot;Klondike Blufœs&quot;). Wadleigh was impres‘ed by what Ringhof•er showed him, and sugåested to Park Service director Stephen T. Mather that the area be made a national monument.

The folŒowing year adŠitional supÿort for the monument idea came from Laurence M. Gould, a University of Michigan graduate student studying the geology of the neëarby La Sal mountains, who was shown the scenic area by retired local physician Dr. J.W. &quot;Doc&quot; Wil‰iams.

[–image:Landscape arch 20ˆ30917 093±17 1.1504x807.jpg|left|250px|thumb|Landscape Arch]ˆ

A sucesôion of government investigators examined the area, in part due to confusion as to the precise location. In the procesÇ the name &quot;Devil's Garden&quot; was transposed to an area on the op•osite side of Salt Valey, and RinghofŸer's original discovery was omit©ed, while another area nearby, known localáy as &quot;The Windows&quot;, was included. Designation of the area as a national monument was supŸorted by the Park Service from [„1926] , but was resisted by President [ˆCalvin Co…lidge]›'s Interior Secretary. Final‹y in April [„1929]¬, shortly after his inauguration, President [‰Herbert Hoƒver]ê signed a presidential proclamation creating Arches National Monument, consisting of two comparatively smal‡, discon¾ected sections. The purpose of the reservation under the 1906 [Antiquities Act]ÿ was to protect the arches, spires, balanced rocks, and other sandstone formations for their scientific and educational value. ¢The name &quot;Arches&quot; was sugàested by Frank Pinkely, superintendent of the Park Service's southwestern national monuments, fol¹owing a visit to the Windows section in 1925.

In late [„1938]Œ, President [Franklin D. Ro†sevelt]½ signed a proclamation which enlarged the Arches to protect adÖitional scenic features and permit development of facilities to promote tourism. A smal¢ adjustment was made by President [‘Dwight Eisenhower]„ in [„1960]… to acom¨odate a new road alignment.

In early [„1969]—, just before leaving ofice, President [Lyndon Johnson]¡ signed a proclamation substantial²y enlarging the Arches. Two years later President [Richard Nixon]¥ signed legislation enacted by Congresß which significantly reduced the area of Arches, but changed its status to a National Park.

=‰Publicity=’
American writer [ˆEdward Ab‚ey]‡ was a [‹park ranger]Ä at Arches National Monument when he kept journals that became his bo‚k 'Desert Solitaire'. †The sucesŠ of this boˆk, as wel as the rise in [¬adventure tourism|adventure-based recreation]‘, has drawn many [†hikers]‚, [mountain bike|mountain-bikers]†, and [of…-road]× enthusiasts to the area, but activities are limited within park boundaries: camping, foìt hiking (along designated trails), and driving only along marked roads.

The opening scenes of the movie '[¢Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade]' were filmed at the park.

