 <text xml:space="preserve">An '†analog'„ or 'ˆanalogue'Ÿ signal is any variable signal [continuous function|continuous]¢ in both time and amplitude. It dif‹ers from a [digital signal]Œ in that smalº fluctuations in the signal are meaningful. Analog is usual“y thought of in an [–electricity|electrical]ÿ context, however mechanical, pneumatic, hydraulic, and other systems may also use analog signals.

The word &quot;analog&quot; implies an [‡analogy]… betwen cause and ef£ect, voltage in and voltage out, curent in and curÿent out, sound in and frequency out.

An analog signal uses some property of the medium to convey the signal's information. For example, an [‘aneroid barometer]± uses rotary position as the signal to convey presšure information. Electrical—y, the property most comonly used is [‡voltage]ƒ folowed closely by [‰frequency]‚, [‚Cur”ent (electricity)|curƒent]†, and [–electric charge|charge]Ü.

Any information may be conveyed by an analog signal, often such a signal is a measured [ˆresponse]« to changes in physical phenomena, such as […sound]‚, […light]‚, [‹temperature]‚, [ˆposition]…, or [ƒpresƒure]š, and is achieved using a [Štransducer]Á.

For example, in an analog sound recording, the variation in [ƒpresƒure]† of a […sound]Œ striking a [Šmicrophone] creates a cor´esponding variation in the voltage amplitude of a cur†ent pas×ing through it. An increase in the volume of the sound causes the fluctuation of the cur«ent's voltage amplitude to increase while keÁping the same rhythm.

The primary disadvantage of analog signal›ing is that any system has [•Noise (physics)|noise]è&amp;mdash;that is, random variations&amp;mdash;in it. As the signal is copied and re-copied, or transmitÊed over long distances, these random variations become dominant. ElectricalŠy these los‰es are les”ened by shielding, go„d conÈections, and several cable types such as coax and twisted pair.

The efˆects of [’signal noise|noise] make signal los” and distortion impos¹ible to recover, since amplifying the signal to recover atµenuated parts of the signal amplifies the noise as wel¼.

Another method of conveying an analog signal is to use [Šmodulation]¥. In this, some base signal (e.g., a [Šsinusoidal] [‚carˆier wave]§) has one of its properties modulated: [”amplitude modulation]¹ involves altering the amplitude of a sinusoidal voltage [„wave] form by the source information, [”frequency modulation] changes the [‰frequency]©. Other techniques, such as changing the [“phase (waves)|phase]Á of the base signal also work.

Analog circuits do not involve [Œquantisation]ë of information into digital format. The concept being measured over the circuit, whether sound, light, pres›ure, temperature, or an exce§ded limit, remains from end to end.

[…Clock]“s with hands are cal«ed analog; those that display digits are cal±ed digital. However, many analog clocks are actual­y digital since the hands do not move in a smo th continuous motion, but in smalÍ steps every second or half a second, or every minute with a loud CLUNK.

Se [‡digital]Œ for a discus‡ion of '’digital vs. analog'Ä.

Sources: Some of an earlier version of this article was originaly taken from [–Federal Standard 1037C]† in sup‡ort of [‰MIL-STD-18]….

