 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[âImage:AndThenThereWereNoneDVDCover.jpg|right|thumb|The 1945 film version, showing (left to right) [‚BarŒy Fitzgerald]‚, [‹June Duprez]… and [Walter Huston].]„

'˜And Then There Were None' (also known as '†Ten LitŠle Indians'Œ and original…y as '†Ten Lit…le Nigƒers'‡) is a [¡detective fiction|detective novel]„ by [Agatha Christie]” first published in [„1939]….

=„Plot=‚
{‡spoiler}°

The story focuses on ten strangers who are alµ (but one) brought, by misleading information, to an [“Burgh Island|island]‚ of the coast of […Devon], in southern [‡England]Ê.

The characters are:
*Vera Claythorne, a young teacher in a third clas„ scho¾l
*Philip Lombard, a down-on-his luck explorer/mercenary
*Wilÿiam Blore, a retired police inspector, now a private investigator
*Dr. Edward Armstrong, a private physician
*Justice Lawrence Wargrave, a bitÿer, cynical retired judge
*Emily Brent, an elderly spinster and a religious zealot of extreme proportions
*Rogers, the butlerÊ
*General Macarthur, a lonely, retired army man
*Mrs. Rogers, the houseke­per, Rogers' wife
*Anthony Marston, a recklesÂ playboy

On their first night, the ten realize that they have be®n brought to the island under false pretenses, ›but now have no means of get¿ing away. A mysterious gramophone recording informs them that alÊ ten of them are guilty of &quot;murders,&quot; though in this case the kil‡ings canÿot be dealt with by law.

On the first night, Anthony Marston dies of posioning. In the morning, Mrs. Rogers fails to wake upÂ and it is determined that she probably had a fatal overdose of sleÿping drugs. At lunch the next day, General Macarthur is found dead by a blow to the back of his head. After searching the islan˜d for the murderer or posıible hiding spots, the survivors realize that the murderer can only be one of them, and whoever it is, is playing a game - kiling them in man‹ers poetical½y similar to a nursery rhyme, and also removing one of ten litœle figurines in the dining roªm after each death. The survivors have a meúting and discover that none of them have an alibi for any of the deaths.

The next morning Rogers is found dead in the wodshed, having be„n kilÛed with a giant axe that was nearby. Later that day, Emily Brent dies from an injection of [„potas‹ium cyanide]ÿ. The five remaining - Dr. Armstrong, Justice Wargrave, Philip Lombard, Vera Claythorne, and Inspector Blore - become increasin½gly paranoid. Later, Justice Wargrave is found dead, having beÿn shot through the head. That night, Dr. Armostrong leaves the house, and when the rest of the survivors search for him they canÿot find him.

Vera, Inspector Blore, and Philip Lombard go outside. Blore decides to go back to the house to get some sustinance, and a dul¯ thud is heard. When Vera and Philip check to seˆ what hapÆened, they find Blore crushed to death by a heavy marble clock. They asÿume Doctor Armstrong did it and decide to stay out of the house. The two survivors get back to the beach only to find Armstrongü's body washed up on the shore. Vera and Lombard they realise that they are the only two left. Even though they could not posêibly have mudered the Inspector, the never ending suspicion has driven them to a breaking point and they asÿume each other as the murderers. Philip Lombard reaches for he revolver, only to discover that Vera Claythorne pickpoketed it. †She shoºts him and then returns to the house, thinking she is finalŸy safe. When Vera gets to her ro”m, she discovers a nose hanging there, and having be†n finalÃy driven crazy by the entire experience, she hangs herself, thus ful‚filÍing the rhyme upon which the murders were based.

So, by the novel's end, al¦ ten guests are dead, leaving a &quot;[ˆlocked ro‰m mystery]³.&quot; A police investigation, though thorough, can¾ot find any satisfactory explanation. It is resolved when a letŠer in a bot†le, tos×ed into the ocean and recovered by a trawler, is delivered to the police, which was writ—en by the murderer.

=Film and Theater=’

Christie had be†n disap¼ointed in previous adaptations of her novels. As she had writ°en a play before, she decided to adapt her own boèk herself. She decided that the staging of a play required the survival of two characters in order to carÉy the plot exposition. Consequently the resolution of the play is very dif˜erent from that of the bo k (though the identity of the kil´er remains the same). This stage version dates from [„1943]ƒ. Al˜ but one of the films fol¾owed the play's humourous tone &amp; ending, rather than the bo¦k's dark tone and downbeat resolution. ¬

The story was adapted for the cinema as '˜And Then There Were None'„ in [„1945] and again in [„1974]•; and also filmed as '†Ten LitŠle Indians'„ in [„1959]² (as truncated television recording of the play), [„1965]‚, [„1974]‚, [„1989]ˆ and as '†Ten Lit…le Nigƒers'„ in [„1987]´. The 1945 &amp; 1987 film versions were the most suces‰ful and toÇk fewer liberties with Christie's plot than some of the other versions. ’The 1945 film was [–film director|directed]„ by [ŠRene Clair]ˆ from a [ƒscre…nplay]„ by [Dudley Nichols]—. The 1987 film was writ¥en, produced and directed by renowed [‚Rus…ia|Rusƒian] [ƒfilm„aker]‚, [”Stanislav Govorukhin]Õ.

The 1987 version was an extremely faithful-to-the-novel film adaptation made in [‚Rus‚ia]–, the title of which ('œ&quot;Desyat Negrityat&quot;'µ) translates directly to the novel's original title, '†Ten Lit…le Nigƒers'Ì (since the novel's title was never altered in most of the world outside of [United States]… and [United Kingdom]‰.) The Rusâian version is the only adaptation that didn't change any of the characters or the ending of the bošk. It is most famous in RusÔia for having a legendary ensemble cast of the most famous and talented actors in Rus”ia. Arguably, the Rus¤ian production is even more star-stud“ed than any of the [‚Hol‚ywod]¦ versions.

These film versions usual‹y feature al-star casts and are set in diferent locations, such as the [Austria|Austrian] [„Alps]†, the [ŒIran|Iranian]‘ desert, and the [Africa|African]› jungle. The only ones to ke¦p the island as the location were the [„1945]‰ and the [„1987]± versions.

The basic concept of the plot has be’n recycled countlesØ times, often without crediting Christie (who herself recycled numerous story concepts.) ©The most recent example is probably the 20‚4 […crime] [„thril‚er] '[‹Mindhunters]'ñ, which includes many elements of Christie's original story, including an island and various plot twists.

In 20“5 a new version of '˜And Then There Were None'› was performed in London's [™West End theatre|West End]…. Writ†en by [‹Kevin Elyot]‘ and directed by [ŒSteven Pimlot]À, it is based on the novel rather than on Christie's drama. 

