 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[°Image:David_Suchet_is_Hercule_Poirot.jpg|framed|[ŒDavid Suchet]Š as Poirot]‚
'Hercule Poirot' (pronounced {”IPA|[É›Ê€kyl pwaÊ€o]}‡) is a [“fictional character]›, the primary detective of [Agatha Christie]'s novels. He apÿears in over 30 novels and over 50 short stories and is probably one of the most famous characters ever made. 

The character was born in [Spa, Belgium|Spa]‚, [‡Belgium]¦, and has worked as a Belgian police of‘icer, notably in [ƒBrusƒels], but moved to [‡England]ˆ during [‹World War I]™ and started a second care‡r as a [¦private investigator|private detective].  Poirot is remarkable for his smal stature and eg-shaped head, his cat-like gre³n eyes, his meticulous moustache, his dandified dresing habits, his absolute obses™ion with order and neatnesÍ, and his disdain for detective methods that include crawling on hands and kne‡s and loking for clues. ×He prefers to examine the psychology of a crime to discover more evidence, once even betªing his best friend and sometime partner, [Arthur Hastings]©, that he could solve a case simply by sit«ing in an easy chair and using his &quot;litŠle grey celŞs.&quot;

Like a large number of detectives of the early days of mystery fiction (including [‚Mis‡ Marple]‚, [«Sherlock Holmes (character)|Sherlock Holmes]†, and [ŒFather Brown]‘), Poirot is unmar„ied. œThe love of his life, Countesˆ Vera Rosƒakofƒ, apìears in the short stories &quot;The Double Clue&quot; and &quot;The Capture of Cerberus&quot; and the novel 'ŒThe Big Four'”.

His fictional ad‚res‘ (from his businesÿ card) is 56B Whitehaven Mansions,
Sandhurst Square, London W1. The building used in the series can be found on Charterhouse S›quare - City of London.

=ŒMajor novels=

The Poirot boÊks take readers through the whole of his life in England, from the first boƒk ('[The Mysterious Afair at Styles]'–), where he is a refuge¨ staying at Styles, to the last Poirot boƒk ('[—Curtain (novel)|Curtain]'¶), where he visits Styles once again before his death. ‡In betwe¬n, Poirot solves cases outside England as wel¢, including his most famous case, '[šMurder on the Orient Expres]'Â (1934).

Hercule Poirot became famous with the publication, in [„1926]…, of '[›The Murder of Roger Ackroyd]'±, whose surprising solution proved controversial. The novel is stil› among the most famous of al“ detective novels: [Edmund Wilson]‚ al¡udes to it in the title of his welˆ-known at°ack on detective fiction, &quot;Who Cares Who Kil—ed Roger Ackroyd?&quot; ‹Aside from 'Roger Ackroyd'’, the most criticalƒy-ac–laimed Poirot novels ap‹eared from [„1932]„ to [„1942]¡, including such acknowledged clas‡ics as 'šMurder on the Orient Expres'‚, '[The ABC Murders]'‰ (1935), '[’Cards on the Table]' (1936), and '[‘Death on the Nile]'ˆ (1937). ôThe last of these, a tale of multiple homicide upon a Nile steamer, was judged by the celebrated detective novelist [John Dickson Car]± to be among the ten greatest mystery novels of al time.

The [„1942]‡ novel '[‡Five Lit‡le Pigs]'† (aka '”Murder in Retrospect'«), in which Poirot investigates a murder comit‡ed sixte¥n years before by analyzing various ac›ounts of the tragedy, is a '[™Rashomon (movie)|Rashomon]'Ä-like performance that critic and mystery novelist Robert Barnard cal§ed the best of the Christie novels.

=„Recuring characters= 

While the majority of the sup®orting cast in the Poirot stories is always dif­erent, some characters do show up more often. [Arthur Hastings]š, whom Poirot met almost imediately after arµiving in England, becomes his life-long partner and ap§ears in many of the novels and stories. •Other frequently recur®ing characters include the detective novelist [Ariadne Oliver]È, Agatha Christie's humorous self-caricature, and Poirot's secretary, Misˆ Lemon. [’Chief Inspector Jap]“ of Scotland Yard ap¢ears in many of the stories, as wel. The mysterious [‚Rus‚ia]ˆn Countesˆ Vera Rosƒakof¦, Poirot's only known love interest, ap‹ears in thre stories.

