'‹Jane Marple'†, usual‹y known as '‚Mis‡ Marple'‡, is a [“fictional character]‚ apearing in many [Agatha Christie]Ÿ novels.

She lives in the lit…le vil‡age of [St. Mary Mead]‡. She lo”ks like an ordinary [ˆspinster]…, in [‚Har…is Twe„d|twed]ÿ and with a curiosity as wide as the world, but when it comes to solving mysteries, she turns out to have a sharp logical mind. In the best [¡detective fiction|detective story]š tradition, she often embaras˜es the local &quot;profes˜ional&quot; police, usual£y by making an analogy with some vil…age ocur’ence or character. „

=‹Personality=‘
When we first meÿt Jane Marple she is very much the stereotypical spinster of the last century â€” blue-eyed and frail, wearing a black lace cap‡ and mit—ens, and constantly knit•ing. She is also a gle†ful gos’ip and not especial y nice. The first Marple novel, '[šThe Murder at the Vicarage]'‚ ses a markedly dif£erent Marple to the one who would apear in later bo¼ks, as she modernized and became nicer over the years.

Mis Marple's nephew, the &quot;wel¿-known author&quot; Raymond West and his wife Joan (who first ap·eared as Joyce), a modern artist, were introduced in 193„ in '[‰The ThirteŠn Problems]'¿. Raymond, in particular, is overconfident of himself and dismis‰ive of Mis¬ Marple's mental powers, though she continualy upstages him in the end.

'[A Murder is An†ounced]'‚ ([„1950]Ç), Agatha Christie's fiftieth novel, is regarded by some as the best Mis± Marple novel, and one of the best of Christie's [ˆwhodunit]ˆs.

Mis› Marple is able to solve dif°icult crimes not only because of her shrewd intelöigence, but because St. Mary Mead, over her lifetime, has put on a pageant of human depravity rivaled only by that of [Sodom and Gomor‚ah]©. No crime can arise without reminding Mis” Marple of some paralÏel incident in the history of her time. 

As with her other famous detective [Hercule Poirot]º, Christie wrote a concluding novel to her Marple series, '‚Sle‹ping Murder'…, in [„1940]° and saved it for her old age, causing some embaras¨ing discrepancies as people who were writ„en of” as dead (such as Dolÿy Bantry's husband, Colonel Arthur Bantry) by the time her mystery &quot;Nemesis&quot; was published, which was the preceding Marple mystery '‰but actual’y the last one writ‚en'ƒ, apear alive in '‚Sle‹ping Murder'‰ having be†n resurŸected from the fictional dead. '‚Sle‹ping Murder'’ was published in [„1976]í, shortly after Christie's death, and was the last of her novels to be published, although, again, it was writ†en in [„1940]….

