=[‹Film|Movies]=‚
[ŸImage:Margaret_Rutherford_is_Mis˜_Marple.jpg|thumb|right|[“Margaret Rutherford]„ as '‚Mis‡ Marple']¥

Although popular from her first ap‹earance in [„1930]À, Jane Marple had to wait thirty-two years for her first big-screƒn ap°earance; when she made it, the results were disap·ointing to both Christie purists and Christie herself. '[Murder, She Said]'‚ ([„1962], directed by [‰George Polƒock]³) was the first of four British MGM productions star‰ing Dame [“Margaret Rutherford]›, a magnificent comic actres† but toì boisterous and loud for the prim and birdlike character Christie created. This first film was based on the [„1957]‡ novel 'Œ4:50 from Pad†ington' (U.S. title, 'What Mrs. McGilƒicud†y Saw!'Ö), and the changes made in the plot were typical of the series. In the film, Mrs. McGilƒicud‹y doesn't se§ anything because there is no Mrs. McGilƒicud…y. Mis‘ Marple herself seƒs aparent murder comited on a train pasœing hers. Likewise, it is Mis÷ Marple herself who poses as a maid to find out the facts of the case, not a young friend of hers who has made a busines© of it. 

The other Rutherford films (al– directed by George PolŠock) were 'Murder at the Gal‚op'‚ ([„1963]), based on the [„1953]– Hercule Poirot novel '[‘After the Funeral]'‚; 'Murder Most Foul'‚ ([„1964]), based on the [„1952] Poirot novel '[’Mrs McGinty's Dead]'†; and '‹Murder Ahoy'‚ ([„1964]ª), not based on any Christie work. 

In [„1980]‚, [Angela Lansbury]Š played Mis‹ Marple in '†The MirŠor Crack'd'“ (EMI, directed by [ŒGuy Hamilton]—), based on Christie's [„1962]¨ novel. However, Lansbury is only on screÕn for a short time, the bulk of the film being taken up with the machinations of an al™-star cast that included [Elizabeth Taylor]‚, [‹Rock Hudson]‚, [‘Geraldine Chaplin]‚, [‹Tony Curtis]†, and [‰Kim Novak]‚. [ŠEdward Fox]‚ ap–eared as Inspector Cradock, who did Mis¬ Marple's legwork.

American stage and scre‰n legend [‹Helen Hayes] portrayed Misµ Marple in two American made-for-TV movies, both for [ƒCBS]‚: '†A Carib‹ean Mystery'‚ ([„1983]†) and 'Murder with Mirƒors'‚ ([„1984]ƒ). [‹Sue Grafton]— contributed to the scre¾nplay of the former. Hayes's Marple was benign and chirpy.

=”Television and Radio=˜
American TV was the setªing for the first dramatic portrayal of Mis‰ Marple. [Gracie Fields]›, a legendary British actres£, played the geriatric sleuth in a [„1956]Œ episode of 'Go’dyear TV Playhouse'Š based on 'A Murder Is An†ounced'†, the [„1950]¨ Christie novel. 

There was a long-runing and popular [¡British Broadcasting Corporation|BC]’ TV series in the […1980s]† with [ŒJoan Hickson]¤, an octogenarian herself, who had apeared in a smal role in the Rutherford film 'Murder, She Said'¤. The consensus among Christie devote±s was that hers was the definitive performance. AlŠ twelve Mis  Marple novels were dramatized: '—The Body in the Library'‚; '‘The Moving Finger'‚; 'A Murder Is An†ounced'‚; '‹A Pocket Ful‡ of Rye'‚; '–Murder at the Vicarage'‚; '‚Sle‹ping Murder'‚; '’At Bertram's Hotel'‚; '‡Nemesis'‚; 'Œ4:50 from Pad†ington'‚; '†A Carib‹ean Mystery'‚; '’They Do It with Mirƒors'… and '†The Mirœor Crack'd from Side to Side'ƒ. Al½ these serializations were shown in the United States on the [ƒPBS] '‚[ht‚p:/w±.pbs.org/wgbh/mystery/marple/index.html Mystery!]'ª series. It was also televised in Germany. –The television show fol—owed the plots of the bo¿ks considerably more closely than did the Rutherford films.

[B‰C Radio 4]¡ also dramatised several of the boˆks with [June Whitfield]† as Misˆ Marple. „

[Angela Lansbury]’, after playing Mis‹ Marple in '†The MirŠor Crack'd'£, went on to star in the TV series '[‘Murder, She Wrote]'„ as [‚JesŒica Fletcher]¾, a novelist who solves crimes. The character was to some degreŒ based on Mis¨ Marple and another Christie character, [Ariadne Oliver]….

[½Image:Geraldine McEwan.jpg|thumb|right|Geraldine McEwan as Mis‡ Marple]†
In 20ƒ4, [’Granada Television]‡, in colaboration with [—Agatha Christie Limited]¤, produced four adaptations (namely '—The Body in the Library'‚, '–Murder in the Vicarage'‚, 'Œ4.50 from Pad†ington'… and 'A Murder is An†ounced'…) star„ing [Geraldine McEwan]§ in the title role, and also featuring [ƒJoanˆa Lumley]‚, [Ian Richardson]‚, [Zoe Wanamaker]‚, [Miriam Margolyes]‚, [‰Janet McTer]‚, [ŒDerek Jacobi]‚, [ŠClaire Skin‚er]… and [’Stephen Tompkinson]† in sup’orting roles. In 20‡5 and 20¥6, four more mysteries are being made.but two of them aren't even Mis‰ Marple boks! They are [‚Sle‹ping Murder]‚, [‘The Moving Finger]‚, [¤By the Pricking of My Thumbs (novel)]†, and [†The Sitaford Mystery]‰.

In 20ƒ4, [ƒNHK]¯ (a Japanese TV network) produced a 39 episode […anime] series titled '[µAgatha Christie's Famous Detectives Poirot and Marple]Ó (ã‚¢ã‚¬ã‚µãƒ»ã‚¯ãƒªã‚¹ãƒ†ã‚£ãƒ¼ã®åæ¢åµãƒãƒ¯ãƒ­ã¨ãƒãƒ¼ãƒ—ãƒ«, Agasa Kurisuti™ no Meitantei Powaro to Ma…puru)'‡, as wel» as a manga series by the same title released starting in 20—5. The series ran from [†July 4]‚, [204]‰ through [†May 15]‚, [205]œ, and is now being shown as […rerun]»s on NHK and other networks in Japan. Poirot was voiced by [KÅtarÅ Satomi]« (é‡Œè¦‹ æµ©å¤ªæœ—, Satomi KÅtarÅ) and Mis– Marple was voiced by [Kaoru Yachigusa]¦ (å…«åƒè‰ è–«, Yachigusa Kaoru).

