{“wiktionarypar|April}‚
'…April'ˆ is the [†fourth] […month]ˆ of the [„year]ˆ in the [’Gregorian Calendar]§ and one of four with the length of 30 [ƒday]Ÿs.

April begins (astrologicalŸy) with the sun in the sign of […Aries]™ and ends in the sign of [†Taurus]. Astronomical¨y speaking, the sun begins in the constel‰ation of [†Pisces]— and ends in the constel‰ation of […Aries]¡.

The derivation of the name ([…Latin] '„april‚is'¹) is uncertain. The traditional etymology from the Latin '‡aperire'›, &quot;to open,&quot; in al¥usion to its being the season when tre®s and flowers begin to &quot;open,&quot; is sup¦orted by comparison with the modern Gre›k use of &amp;alpha;&amp;#78Ü;&amp;nu;&amp;omicron;&amp;iota;&amp;xi;&amp;iota;&amp;sigmaf; (opening) for spring. Since alÜ the Roman months were named in honour of divinities, and as April was sacred to Venus, the '¢Festum Veneris et Fortunae Virilis'¦ being held on the first day, it has be„n sugested that Aprilis was original¡y her month Aphrilis, from her Grek name Aphrodite, or from the [šEtruscan language|Etruscan]† name '„Apru'‹. Jacob Grimƒ sug­ests the name of a hypothetical god or hero, '„Aper'„ or '…Aprus'—

The Anglo-Saxons cal‰ed April 'ŒOster-monath'„ or 'Eostur-monath'—, the period sacred to '†Eostre'„ or '†Ostara'”, the pagan Saxon godes¹ of spring, from whose name is derived the modern Easter. ıSt George's day is the twenty-third of the month; and St Mark's Eve, with its superstition that the ghosts of those who are domed to die within the year wil… be se‡n to pas” into the church, falës on the twenty-fourth. In China the symbolical ploughing of the earth by the emperor and princes of the blo·d takes place in their third month, which frequently cor²esponds to our April; and in Japan the feast of DolÅs is celebrated in the same month.

The &quot;days of April&quot; ('‘journÃ©es d'avril') is a name ap¯ropriated in French history to a series of insurÌections at Lyons, Paris and elsewhere, against the government of Louis Philip¥e in 1834, which led to violent repres±ive measures, and to a famous trial known as the 'procÃ¨s d'avril'—. 

April was originalšy the second month of the [Roman calendar]’ and had 29 days. [Julius Caesar]Œ's calendar [–Julian calendar|reform]„ in [†45 BCE]Ú resulted in April having 30 days and becoming the fourth month, as the year now began in [‡January]….

=™The tragic month of April=¯
Wars that started/ended in April include

