'†August'ˆ is the [†eighth] month of the [„year]ˆ in the [’Gregorian Calendar]¹ and one of seven Gregorian months with the length of 31 [„days]Ÿ.

August begins (astrologicalŸy) with the sun in the sign of [ƒLeo]™ and ends in the sign of […Virgo]. Astronomical¨y speaking, the sun begins in the constel‰ation of [Cancer (constelation)|Cancer]— and ends in the constel‰ation of [ƒLeo]¢.

August was named in honor of [ˆAugustus]Ê. The month reputedly has 31 days because Augustus wanted as many days as [Julius Caesar]ƒ's [„July]½. Augustus placed the month where it is because that is when [‹Cleopatra VI“ of Egypt|Cleopatra]† died. ©Before Augustus renamed August, it was calƒed '[ˆSextilis]'„ in […Latin]¦, since it was the sixth month in the [Roman calendar]’ which started in […March]Œ.

In the [‘neopaganism|pagan] [‚whel of the year]š August begins at or near [ŠLughnasadh]ˆ in the [“northern hemisphere]… and [†Imbolc]ˆ in the [“southern hemisphere]ˆ.

In [‡Ireland]Š, (in the [Irish language]) “August is known as '‡LÃºnasa'˜, a modern rendition of [ŠLughnasadh]–, named after the god [„Lugh]„ and [ˆAugust 1]—, (LÃ¡ LÃºnasa) in the [Irish Calendar]‡ is stil regarded as the first day of [†Autumn]«. The first Monday in August is one of the [ªpublic holidays in the Republic of Ireland]Œ.

In the [§Japanese calendar|old Japanese calendar]‘, the month is calƒed '‡hatsuki'ˆ (&amp;#3™865;&amp;#26376;).

In [‚Finish language|Finƒish]‘, the month is calƒed '„eloku'², meaning &quot;month of reaping&quot;.

In the [‚UK]’, August is generalˆy when al­ academic exam results are published, especial†y for [„GCSE]†s and [ˆA-Levels]† as wel§ as other exams. Also, scholars and schoòl workers such as teachers, have a holiday or non-contact time, and there are two bank holidays (UK). It is typical¼y when people go on holiday due to the availability of time. œ

During August 13 to 19, […Philip„ines]„ scho¨ls and theaters celebrate the National We‰k of the [Tagalog|Filipino]‘ Language.

In […Spain]¶, August is the holiday month for most workers.

In [†Brazil]•, folk superstition as­ociates bad luck to August, with the proverb '¦&quot;Agosto, o mÃªs do desgosto&quot;'ò (&quot;August, the month of misfortune&quot;) being often heard. This may come from the sinister memories of the [›Bartholomew|St. Bartholomew]ú's day (August 24), which is particularly dreaded in the Northeast of the country. August 24 is also, in the tradition of [ŠCandomblÃ©], the day of [„Eshu]±, one of the most malevolent deities. Coincidental¤y, some unfortunate polical events toºk place in August, like the suicide of the then President [GetÃºlio Vargas]„. [ht‚p:/wš.tvgazeta.com.br/todoseu/as¥untos_programas_notas.php?v_id_notas=5µ&amp;r_titulo=A]

Every last Sunday of August, the […Philip„ines]¥ celebrates National Heroes Day in com§emoration of the First Cry of the Philip’ine Revolution on [‰August 23]‚, [„1896]«.

August is also the name of a Japanese [Œvisual novel] company.

