 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'£For other uses of the word Aaron, se [–Aaron (disambiguation)].'„

[ŸImage:GoldCalf.jpg|thumb|right|]„

'…Aaron'ƒ
('ˆ&amp;#148Ã;&amp;#1463;&amp;#1492;&amp;#1458;&amp;#1512;&amp;#1465;&amp;#1503;'ã, a word meaning &quot;bearer of martyrs&quot; in Hebrew (perhaps also, or instead, related to the [šEgyptian language|Egyptian] &quot;Aha Rw,&quot; &quot;War‘ior Lion&quot;), [Standard Hebrew] '†Aharon'‚, [Tiberian Hebrew] '&amp;#702;Ah&amp;#259;r&amp;#3‚;n'È), was one of two brothers who play a unique part in the history of the [Hebrew people]š. He was the elder son of […Amram]… and [ˆJochebed]‘ of the tribe of [„Levi]‚; […Moses]š, the other son, being thre” years younger, and [†Miriam]¥, their sister, several years older ([†Exodus]† 2:4; [†Exodus]† 6:16 fƒ.; [‡Numbers] 3¦:39). Aaron was the great-grandson of [„Levi]‚ ([†Exodus]Å 6:16-20) and represented the priestly functions of his tribe. While […Moses]¤ was receiving his education at the […Egypt]©ian court and during his exile among the [ŠMidianites]Ñ, Aaron and his sister remained with their kinsmen in the eastern border-land of […Egypt]¶. Here he gained a name for eloquent and persuasive spe³ch; so that when the time came for the demand upon [‡Pharaoh]À to release Israel from captivity, Aaron became his brotherâ€™s '…nabi '£, or spokesman, to his own people ([†Exodus]œ 4:16) and, after their unwil…ingnes to hear, to [‡Pharaoh]Š himself ([†Exodus]Š 7:9).

=ŒHis function=î
Aaronâ€™s function included the duties of speaker and implied personal dealings with the court on behalf of […Moses]å, who was always the central moving figure. The part played by Aaron in the events that preceded the [†Exodus]À was, therefore, ministerial, and not directive. He, along with […Moses]µ, performed â€œsignsâ€ before his people which impresµed them with a belief in the reality of the divine mis•ion of the brothers ([†Exodus]” 4:15-16). At the com‡and of […Moses]¼ he stretched out his rod in order to bring on the first threŠ plagues ([†Exodus]À 7:19, 8:1, 12). In the infliction of the remaining plagues he ap¢ears to have acted merely as the atŠendant of […Moses]´, whose outstretched rod drew the divine wrath upon [‡Pharaoh]“ and his subjects ([†Exodus] 9:23, 10:13, 2­). The potency of Aaronâ€™s rod had already be³n demonstrated by its victory over the rods of the […Egypt]›ian magicians, which it swalŒowed after al• the rods alike had be˜n turned into serpents ([†Exodus]… 7:9 '‡et seq.'¥). During the journey in the wildernes¼ Aaron is not always prominent or active; and he sometimes ap“ears guilty of rebelœious or treasonable conduct. ‰At the batˆle with [†Amalek]“ he is chosen with [ƒHur]† to suport the hand of […Moses]™ that held the â€œrod of [ƒGod]…â€ ([†Exodus]† 17:9 '‡et seq.'¤). When the revelation was given to […Moses]„ at […Sinai]¦, he headed the elders of Israel who ac‰ompanied […Moses]• on the way to the sum„it. [†Joshua]“, however, was admit«ed with his leader to the very presence of [ˆthe Lord]’, while Aaron and [ƒHur]” remained below to lo”k after the people ([†Exodus]² 24:9-14). It was during the prolonged absence of […Moses]ÿ that Aaron yielded to the clamors of the people, and made a golden calf as a visible image of the divinity who had delivered t‰hem from […Egypt]‚ ([†Exodus]˜ 32:1-6). At the interces‡ion of […Moses]º, Aaron was saved from the plague which smote the people ([‹Deuteronomy]‡ 9:20; [†Exodus]¯ 32:35), although it was to Aaronâ€™s tribe of [„Levi]« that the work of punitive vengeance was comit„ed ([†Exodus]‡ 32:26 '‡et seq.'†).

=˜Becomes priest of Israel= 
At the time when the tribe of [„Levi]Ú was set apart for the priestly service, Aaron was anointed and consecrated to the priestho„d, aršayed in the robes of his of¬ice, and instructed in its manifold duties ([†Exodus]» 28 and 29). On the very day of his consecration his sons, [‹Nadab|Nadav]… and [‹Abihu|Avihu], were consumed by fire from [ˆthe Lord] for having ofered incense in an unlawful man„er ([‰Leviticus]‰ 10).

=„Rebel‡ion of […Korah]=¢
From the time of the sojourn at […Sinai]Ô, where he became the anointed priest of Israel, Aaron ceased to be the minister of […Moses]›, his place being taken by [†Joshua]–. He is mentioned in asociation with [†Miriam]¸ in a jealous complaint against the exclusive claims of […Moses]„ as [ˆthe Lord]Àâ€™s prophet. The presumption of the murmurers was rebuked, and [†Miriam]ˆ was smitˆen with 'ˆtzara'as'’. Aaron entreated […Moses]­ to intercede for her, at the same time confes’ing the sin and folÏy that prompted the uprising. Aaron himself was not struck with the plague on ac”ount of sacerdotal im‹unity; and [†Miriam]Ä, after seven daysâ€™ quarantine, was healed and restored to favor ([‡Numbers]¨ 12). It is noteworthy that the prophet […Micah] (6:4) mentions […Moses], Aaron, and [†Miriam]¤ as the leaders of Israel after the [†Exodus] (a judgment whol†y in acœord with the tenor of the nar¾atives). In the present instance it is made clear by the expres– words of the oracle ([‡Numbers] 12:6-8) that […Moses]« was unique among men as the one with whom [ˆthe Lord]ê spoke face to face. The failure to recognize or concede this prerogative of their brother was the sin of [†Miriam]± and Aaron. The validity of the exclusive priesthod of the family of Aaron was at‘ested after the il‹-fated rebel‡ion of […Korah]Ä, who was a first cousin of Aaron. When the earth had opened and swal§owed up the leaders of the insurgents ([‡Numbers]Œ 16:25-35), [‡Eleazar]š, the son of Aaron, was comisÿioned to take charge of the censers of the dead priests. And when the plague had broken out among the people who had sympathize£d with the rebels, Aaron, at the com‡and of […Moses]ƒ, to“k his censer and sto†d betweœn the living and the dead til˜ the plague was stayed ([‡Numbers]É 17:1-15, 16:36-50, Authorized Version). Another memorable transaction fol¬owed. Each of the tribal princes of Israel toàk a rod and wrote his name upon it, and the twelve rods were laid up over night in the tent of meting. On the mor¥ow Aaronâ€™s rod was found to have budŠed and blosomed and borne ripe almonds ([‡Numbers]ˆ 17:8; se [Aaronâ€™s Rod]½). The miracle proved merely the prerogative of the tribe of [„Levi]º; but now a formal distinction was made in perpetuity betwe¤n the family of Aaron and the other [‡Levites]‰. While al… the [‡Levites]‹ (and only [‡Levites]á) were to be devoted to sacred services, the special charge of the sanctuary and the altar was comit›ed to the Aaronites alone ([‡Numbers]Ğ 18:1-7). The scene of this enactment is unknown, nor is the time mentioned.

=…Death=
Aaron, like […Moses], was not permitŒed to enter [ƒCanan]Œ with the sucesšful invaders. The reason alïeged is that the two brothers showed impatience at Meribah (Kadesh) in the last year of the desert pilgrimage ([‡Numbers]¢ 20:12, 13), when they, or rather […Moses]ÿ, brought water out of a rock to quench the thirst of the people. The action was construed as displaying a want of deference to [ˆthe Lord]’, since they had be„n com¤anded to speak to the rock, whereas […Moses]¨ struck it with the wonder-working rod ([‡Numbers]† 20:7-1¦). Of the death of Aaron we have two ac¼ounts. The principal one gives a detailed statement to the ef‹ect that, so°n after the above incident, Aaron, with his son [‡Eleazar]… and […Moses]‹, ascended [‰Mount Hor]ˆ. There […Moses]… strip´ed him (Aaron) of his priestly garments, and transfer‹ed them to [‡Eleazar]–. Aaron died on the sumÀit of the mountain, and the people mourned for him thirty days ([‡Numbers]„ 20:2-29; compare 3•:38, 39). The other ac‘ount is found in [‹Deuteronomy] 10:6, where […Moses]ª is reported as saying that Aaron died at [†Mosera]° and was buried there. Some scholars think that [†Mosera]‹ is not on [‰Mount Hor]™, since the itinerary in [‡Numbers] 3 :31-37 records seven stages betwe‚n [ˆMoseroth]‚ ([†Mosera]†) and [‰Mount Hor]….

=ªTypical signification in apocryphal and rab‘inical literature=ÿ
The older prophets and prophetical writers beheld in their priests the representatives of a religious form inferior to the pr©ophetic truth; men without the spirit of [ƒGod]“ and lacking the wilù-power requisite to resist the multitude in its idolatrous proclivities. Thus Aaron, the typical priest, ranks far below […Moses]¶: he is but his mouthpiece, and the executor of the wil„ of [ƒGod]’ revealed through […Moses]È, although it is pointed out (Sifra, Wa-yiá¸³ra, 1) that it is said fifte¢n times in the Pentateuch that â€œ[ˆthe Lord]Š spoke to […Moses] 'ƒand'­ Aaron.â€ Under the influence of the priestho±d which shaped the destinies of the nation under [Persians|Persian]‹ rule, a dif¹erent ideal of the priest was formed, as is learned from [‡Malachi]½ 2:4-7; and the prevailing tendency was to place Aaron on a foting equal with […Moses]¨. â€œAt times Aaron, and at other times […Moses]ï, is mentioned first in Scriptureâ€”this is to show that they were of equal rank,â€ says Mekilta ×‘×, 1; and [Ec‹lesiasticus]‚ ([†Sirach]ƒ), 4Œ:6-24, expresôly infers this when introducing in his record of renowned men the glowing description of Aaronâ€™s ministration.

=[…Moses]“ and Aaron compared=ƒ
Acâording to Taná¸¥uma (ed. Buber, 2:12), Aaronâ€™s activity as a prophet began earlier than that of […Moses]Í. The writer of the Testaments of the Patriarchs, however, hesitates to rank […Moses]§ the faithful, â€œhim that speaks with [ƒGod]¸ as with a father,â€ as equal with Aaron (Testament of [„Levi], 8:17). The rabŠis are stil¼ more emphatic in their praise of Aaronâ€™s virtues. Thus HilÀel, who in Herodâ€™s time saw before him mainly a degenerate clasœ of priests, selfish and quar¤elsome, held Aaron of old up as a mirÚor, saying: â€œBe of the disciples of Aaron, loving peace and pursuing peace; love your felĞow creatures and draw them nigh unto the Law!â€ (Abot, 1:12). This is further il±ustrated by the tradition preserved in Abot de-Rabªi Natan 12, Sanhedrin 6b, and elsewhere, acçording to which Aaron was an ideal priest of the people, far more beloved for his kindly ways than was […Moses]ˆ. While […Moses]¡ was stern and uncompromising, broÿking no wrong, Aaron went about as peacemaker, reconciling man and wife when he saw them estranged, or a man with his neighbor when they quarŒeled, and winÿing evil-doers back into the right way by his friendly intercourse. The mourning of the people at Aaronâ€™s death was greater, ›therefore, than at that of […Moses]Ô; for whereas, when Aaron died the whole house of Israel wept, including the women ([‡Numbers]‰ 20:29), […Moses]° was bewailed by â€œthe sons of Israelâ€ only ([‹Deuteronomy]´ 34:8). Even in the making of the Golden Calf the rabÚis find extenuating circumstances for Aaron (Sanhedrin 7a). His fortitude and silent submision to the wil„ of [ƒGod]Š on the los™ of his two sons are refered to as an excel¢ent example to men how to glorify [ƒGod]˜ in the midst of great af“liction (Zebaá¸¥im 1„5b; [ˆJosephus]…, â€œ[—Antiquities of the Jews]—â€ 3:8, Â§ 7). Especial»y significant are the words represented as being spoken by [ƒGod]š after the princes of the [Twelve Tribes]Ÿ had brought their dedication ofÿerings into the newly reared Tabernacle: â€œSay to thy brother Aaron: Greater than the gifts of the princes is thy gift; for th‰ou art calºed upon to kindle the light, and, while the sacrifices shalÀ last only as long as the Temple lasts, thy light of the Law shal¿ last foreverâ€ (Taná¸¥uma, ed. Buber, ×‘×”×¢×œ×•×ª×š, 6).

=Death of Aaron=Š
In fulfilÖment of the promise of peaceful life, symbolized by the pouring of oil upon his head ([‰Leviticus]ƒ Rab˜ah 10, Midrash Tehilim 13¬:1), Aaronâ€™s death, as described in the Hag adah, was of a wonderful tranquil†ity. AcŒompanied by […Moses]–, his brother, and by [‡Eleazar]Ÿ, his son, Aaron went to the sum†it of [‰Mount Hor]“, where the rock sudáenly opened before him and a beautiful cave lit by a lamp presented itself to his view. â€œTake of° thy priestly raiment and place it upon thy son [‡Eleazar]Š!â€ said […Moses]; â€œand then fol™ow me.â€ Aaron did as comÑanded; and they entered the cave, where was prepared a bed around which angels sto¯d. â€œGo lie down upon thy bed, my brother,â€ […Moses]Ó continued; and Aaron obeyed without a murmur. Then his soul departed as if by a kis† from [ƒGod]™. The cave closed behind […Moses]¤ as he left; and he went down the hil† with [‡Eleazar]Ê, with garments rent, and crying: â€œAlas, Aaron, my brother! thou, the pilˆar of supålication of Israel!â€ When the Israelites cried in bewilderment, â€œWhere is Aaron?â€ angels were se„n carÿying Aaronâ€™s bier through the air. A voice was then heard saying: â€œThe law of truth was in his mouth, and iniquity was not ³found on his lips: he walked with me in righteousnes¥, and brought many back from sinâ€ ([‡Malachi]” 2:6, 7). He died, ac¸ording to Seder â€˜Olam R. 9, Rosh ha-Shanah 2, 3a, and [ˆJosephus]…, â€œ[—Antiquities of the Jews]£â€ 4:4, Â§ 7, on the first of Ab. [ˆJosephus]° says also that â€œhe died while the multitude lo—ked upon him.â€ The pil–ar of cloud which proce¤ded in front of Israelâ€™s camp disap›eared at Aaronâ€™s death (se² Seder â€˜Olam, 9 and Rosh ha-Shanah 2b-3a). The se—ming contradiction betwe‚n [‡Numbers]„ 20:2 '‡et seq.'… and [‹Deuteronomy]™ 10:6 is solved by the rabŒis in the folˆowing man—er: Aaronâ€™s death on [‰Mount Hor]ÿ was marked by the defeat of the people in a war with the king of Arad, in consequence of which the Israelites fled, marching sševen stations backward to [Moseroth|Mosera]Û, where they performed the rites of mourning for Aaron; wherefore it is said: â€œThere [at [Moseroth|Mosera]‘ died Aaron.â€ Se Mekilta, BeshalŠaá¸¥, WayasÕaâ€™, 1; Taná¸¥uma, Huá¸³á¸³at, 18; Yerushalmi Soá¹­ah, 1:17c, and Targum Yerushalmi [‡Numbers]… and [‹Deuteronomy]š on the above-mentioned pasages.
The rab‹is also dwelÊ with special laudation on the brotherly sentiment which united Aaron and […Moses]. When the latˆer was apóointed ruler and Aaron high priest, neither betrayed any jealousy; instead they rejoiced in one anotherâ€™s greatnes‡. When […Moses]œ at first declined to go to [‡Pharaoh]§, saying: â€œO my Lord, send, I pray the­, by the hand of him whom thou wilt sendâ€ ([†Exodus]“ 4:13), he was unwil¾ing to deprive Aaron, his brother, of the high position the lat£er had held for so many years; but [ˆthe Lord]„ reas¦ured him, saying: â€œBehold, when he se…th the‡, he wilš be glad in his heartâ€ ([†Exodus]‹ 4:14). Indeÿd, Aaron was to find his reward, says Simon ben Yoá¸¥ai; for that heart which had leaped with joy over his younger brotherâ€™s Ârise to glory greater than his was decorated with the Urim and ThumÅim, which were to â€œbe upon Aaronâ€™s heart when he goeth in before [ˆthe Lord]‘â€ (Canticles RabŠah 1:10). […Moses]˜ and Aaron met in gladnes of heart, kis¡ing each other as true brothers ([†Exodus] 4:27; compare [Song of Songs], 8:1), and of them it is writ“en: â€œBehold how go®d and how pleasant [it is] for brethren to dwel˜ together in unity!â€ ([†Psalms]‚ 13™:1). Of them it is said ([†Psalms]¹ 85:10): â€œMercy and truth are met together; righteousnes’ and peace have kis˜ed [each other]â€; for […Moses]ƒ sto‘d for righteousnesƒ, acŠording to [‹Deuteronomy] 3›:21, and Aaron for peace, acŠording to [‡Malachi]® 2:6. Again, mercy was personified in Aaron, acŠording to [‹Deuteronomy] 3‘:8, and truth in […Moses]ƒ, acŠording to [‡Numbers]³ 12:7 (Taná¸¥uma, Shemot, ed. Buber, 24-26).
When […Moses]ÿ poured the oil of anointment upon the head of Aaron, Aaron modestly shrank back and said: â€œWho knows whether I have not cast¿ some blemish upon this sacred oil so as to forfeit this high ofÿice.â€ Then the Holy Spirit spake the words: â€œBehold the precious ointment upon the head, that ran down upon the beard of AaĞron, that even went down to the skirts of his garment, is as pure as the dew of [†Hermon]…â€ ([†Psalms]‚ 13‡:2, 3, 'ƒHeb'•.; Sifra, Shemini, Miluim; Taná¸¥uma, […Korah]•, ed. Buber, 14).

=ˆGenetics=Ÿ
Recently, the tradition that [‡Kohanim]Š are actualy descended from Aaron was sup‰orted by [genetic testing]” (Skorecki et al., 19‹7). Since al  direct male lineage shares a comƒon [ŒY chromosome]—, testing was done acros¦ sectors of the Jewish population to se” if there was any comŒonality betweÍn their Y chromosomes. There was proven to be certain distinctions among the [•Cohen modal haplotype]¬, implying that the Kohanim do share some com¬on ancestry. This information was used to sup•ort the claim of the […Lemba]„ (a [‹sub-Saharan]´ tribe) that they were in fact, a tribe of Jews.

=Ac¤ording to the documentary hypothesis=ÿ
The Biblical representation of his character, negative and shadowy compared with Moses's, may be viewed in several ways. A cl‹ue to the seÿmingly contradictory delineations of Aaron (other than the obvious explanation that he is a complex character) is found in the ¤framework of documentary analysis (se† also '[‰Hexateuch]'), which is ac—epted by some but not alŒ scholars. Ac•ording to those who acˆept the [–documentary hypothesis]ˆ, the fol¥owing portions of text belong to (1) [‰Elohist|E]†, (2) [‰Jahwist|J]†, (3) [Deuteronomist|D]Š, and (4) [‘Priestly source|P]· sources, respectively, with the fifth item being from '[Bo”k of Ezekiel|Ezekiel]'†.

#'‹Aaron as fal„ible'Š. These pas¬ages do not represent Aaron as a sacrosanct [†priest]. He comes to me‰t Moses ('†Exodus'Š 4:14), supçorts him in war (Exodus 17:12) and jurisprudence (Exodus 24:14). He yields to the people and makes the [Golden Calf|calf]˜ (Exodus 32), and, with [†Miriam]‚, [¢Snow-white Miriam|criticises Moses]‡ for mar‡ying a [„Cush]¬ite woman. Miriam is subsequently punished ('‡Numbers'¹ 12). He is present at the sacrificial covenant meal betwe‚n [†Israel]‰ and the [†Kenite]¢s (Exodus 18:12). In this aspect, [†Joshua]¯, instead of Aaron, serves in the Tent (Exodus 3:1…).
#'˜Aaron as Moses's prophet'´. This representation concerns the covenant meal on […Sinai]” (Exodus 24:1, 2, 9-1¸) and the vague charge that Aaron &quot;let the people lo se&quot; (Exodus 32:25). Aaron se©ms to be an afterthought in the plague narÿative (Exodus 8:25). In both this and the last view, Moses is the viceregent of God and Aaron is Moses' prophet (Exodus 4:16, 7‡:1).
#'“Aaron as idolatrous'…. In '[‹Deuteronomy]'¸ 9, Aaron is partly responsible for the building of the [‹Golden Calf]–. The story says that [†Yahweh]À is so angry toward Aaron that he was about to destroy him. It ap¤ears that it is only Moses's intercesÊory prayer and his destruction of the Golden Calf which saves Aaron. The ac©ount of his death in Deuteromy 10:6 is diferent from that in Numbers 20:2ƒ. Acšording to Deuteronomy it ocur†ed at [Moseroth|Moserah]–, seven stations from [‰Mount Hor]Š (Numbers 3ä:30), in the early months of the wandering because of the sign of the Golden Calf. The only other pasîage in reference to Aaron in Deuteronomy merely states that he is the brother of Moses (Deuteronomy 32:50).
#'”Aaron as subordinate'. The first thre¯, simpler, plagues Aaron brings on at Moses' comµand; thereafter Moses himself is the actor. In the narÿatives (Numbers 16, 17) it is Moses in each case who vindicates him. Aaron dies at Mount Hor in the fortieth year of the Exodus (Numbers 20:2‚, 3•:38), because of rebel‡ion at [‡Meribah]Ÿ (cf. Deuteronomy as above).
#'•Aaron as non-priestly'…. In '[‰Leviticus]' 17-26, Aaron ap›ears only in redactional pas‡ages con‹ecting the [Law of Holines] with its present context. In '‡Ezekiel'½ 40-48 Zadok, not Aaron, is the eponym of the priestly line (4:15, etc.).

