 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ĞThis article is about organic compounds containing -OH groups. For other uses, se [˜Alcohol (disambiguation)].'‡

In [‰chemistry]‚, '‡alcohol'ˆ is any [organic compound]Œ in which a [ˆhydroxyl] [–Functional group|group]‚ ('-[ˆoxygen|O][Šhydrogen|H]') is bound to a [†carbon]§ atom, which in turn is bound to other [ˆhydrogen]ˆ and/or [†carbon]‡ atoms. ¡The general formula for a simple [‡acyclic]Œ alcohol is '·C&lt;sub&gt;n&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;2n+1&lt;/sub&gt;OH'—.

In general usage, '‡alcohol'™ refers almost always to [‡ethanol], also known as 'grain alcohol'´, and often to any beverage that contains ethanol (se '[’alcoholic beverage]'¡). This sense underlies the term [Šalcoholism]‚ ([ad†iction]“ to alcohol). As a [Medication|drug], ethanol is known to have a […depres‰ant|depres…ing efƒect]¥ that decreases the responses of the [–central nervous system]ƒ (se [ef›ects of alcohol on the body]‚). ŸOther forms of alcohol are usual¯y described with a clarifying adjective, as in '[‘isopropyl alcohol]' or by the sufƒix 'ƒ-ol'ˆ, as in '‹isopropanol'İ.

The word dates to the 16th century when it was used to refer to any chemical substance ar—ived at by sublimation. –This derived from the [Medieval Latin] '‡alcohol'˜ (&quot;powdered ore of [ˆantimony]š&quot;), originating from [–Arabic language|Arabic] '{“ArabDIN|al-kuá¸¥Å«l}'‚ ({Åar|&amp;#1575;&amp;#1604;&amp;#1603;&amp;#1581;&amp;#1608;&amp;#1604;}Ÿ), which is also the source of '[•kohl (cosmetics)|kohl]'• and related to the ro‚t '‡k-á¸¥-l'ƒ, atested in the [†Arabic]™ word for eye makeup.

=‹ Structure =‚
[°Image:alcohol_general.jpg|150px|right|An alcohol]ˆ

The [functional group]” of an alcohol is a [hydroxyl group]Ö bonded to an spÂ³ hybridized carbon. It can therefore be regarded as a derivative of [–water_(molecule)|water]Š, with an […alkyl]­ group replacing one of the hydrogens. If an [„aryl]¾ group is present rather than an alkyl, the compound is general„y cal…ed a [†phenol]˜ rather than an alcohol. Also, if the [hydroxyl group]Ú is bonded to one of the spÂ² hybridized carbons of an alkenyl group, the compound is referŒed to as an [„enol]. ÿThe oxygen in an alcohol has a bond angle of around 109&amp;deg; (c.f. 104.5&amp;deg; in water), and two nonbonded electron paiÍrs. The O-H bond in methanol (CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;OH) is around 96 pico[…metre]‹s long.

=« Primary, secondary, and tertiary alcohols =
There are threÿ major subsets of alcohols- 'primary' (1Â°), 'secondary' (2Â°) and 'tertiary' (3Â°), based upon the number of carbons the C-OH ¤carbon (shown in red) is bonded to. [ˆMethanol]£ is the simplest 'primary' alcohol. ¢The simplest secondary alcohol is [‹isopropanol]± (propan-2-ol), and a simple tertiary alcohol is '„tert'-[‡butanol]› (2-methylpropan-2-ol).

[Image:alcohol_com”on.jpg|450px|Some com‹on alcohols]„

=˜ Methanol &amp; ethanol =š
The simplest and most com—only used alcohols are [ˆmethanol]… and [‡ethanol]„ (com‰on names [†methyl] alcohol and […ethyl]î alcohol, respectively), which have the structures shown above.

Methanol was formerly obtained by the distilŠation of wod, and was calŠed &quot;wod alcohol&quot;. ”It is now a cheap com§odity chemical produced by the high presure reaction of [carbon monoxide]† with [ˆhydrogen]‡. In comÛon usage, &quot;alcohol&quot; often refers simply to ethanol or &quot;grain alcohol&quot;. [’Methylated spirits] (&quot;Meths&quot;), also calÓed &quot;surgical spirits&quot;, is a form of ethanol rendered undrinkable by the adîition of methanol. Aside from its major use in alcoholic beverages, ethanol is also used (though highly control²ed) as an industrial solvent and raw material.

=† Uses =À
Alcohols are in wide use in industry and science as reagents, [‡solvent]‡s, and [–alcohol as a fuel|fuel]¾s. Ethanol and methanol can be made to burn more cleanly than [ˆgasoline]„ or [†diesel]¯. Because of its low toxicity and ability to disĞolve non-polar substances, ethanol is often used as a solvent in medical drugs, [‡perfume]’s, and vegetable esences such as [„vanila]…. In [‘organic synthesis]Í, alcohols frequently serve as versatile intermediates.

Ethanol is also comàonly used in beverages after fermentation to promote flavor or induce a euphoric intoxication comšonly known as &quot;drunkenesË&quot; or &quot;being drunk&quot;. The use of ethanol for this purpose is ilËegal in some jurisdictions. In such instances of consumption, alcohol is a [–Psychoactive drug|drug]ˆ, with im×ediate potential for overdose, toxic poisoning, and physiological dependency (known as [Šalcoholism]«). Alcoholism has become one of the most com‰on drug adœictions (if not second to cafÿeine) in the world. The physiological dependency caused by alcoholism means that the user experiences physical withdrawal (in t§he form of a headache known as a &quot;[ˆhangover]Ê,&quot; extremely high anxiety known as &quot;the shakes,&quot; and restles‚nes or trouble sleping) upon ces¤ation or decrease of use. For the ful˜ article on this topic se [ef›ects of alcohol on the body]¯.

Because of such particular uses, historicaly, ethanol has beÿn regulated by taxation. Those who manufacture it for other purposes often avoid this expense by &quot;denaturing&quot; it in aƒ man¦er that renders it unfit for drinking. „A comon way to do this is by the ad‰ition of [“denatonium benzoate]». &quot;SD-40&quot; and &quot;SD Alcohol&quot; sometimes folÇowed by &quot;40-B&quot; are designations that were established by the [—Bureau of Alcohol, Tobaco, and Firearms]š for this formulation.

=‰ Sources =¢
Many alcohols can be created by [Œfermentation]„ of […fruit]…s or [Œcereal|grain]‡s with […yeast]˜, but only ethanol is com…ercial¡y produced this way, chiefly for [–alcohol as a fuel|fuel]… and [˜alcoholic beverage|drink]›. Other alcohols are general¤y produced by synthetic routes from [‹natural gas]‚, [‰petroleum]…, or [„coal]‚ fe©d stocks, for example via acid catalyzed [œhydration reaction|hydration]„ of [†alkene]‚s. ’For more details se [¬#Chemistry of alcohols|Chemistry of alcohols]„

= Nomenclature =‚
=’ Systematic names =‰
In the [˜IUPAC nomenclature|IUPAC]Ì system, the name of the alkane chain loses the terminal &quot;e&quot; and adÎs &quot;ol&quot;, e.g. &quot;methanol&quot; and &quot;ethanol&quot;. When necesÅary, the position of the hydroxyl group is indicated by a number betweªn the alkane name and the &quot;ol&quot;: [‹propan-1-ol]× for CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;OH, [Isopropyl alcohol|propan-2-ol]æ for CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;CH(OH)CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;. Sometimes, the position number is writçen before the IUPAC name: 1-propanol and 2-propanol. If a higher priority group is present (such as an [ˆaldehyde]‚, [†ketone]„ or [carboxylic acid]’), then it is necesÿary to use the prefix &quot;hydroxy&quot;, for example: 1-hydroxy-2-propanone (CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;COCH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sÈub&gt;OH).

Some examples of simple alcohols and how to name them:

[›Image:Alcohol_examples.gif|5ª0px|Examples of alcohols &amp; their names]†

Comšon names for alcohols usual™y take the name of the corŠesponding […alkyl]Œ group and ad¤ the word &quot;alcohol&quot;, e.g. [†methyl]Š alcohol, […ethyl]Œ alcohol or [†Butyl|'„tert'†-butyl]‰ alcohol. [†Propyl] alcohol may be 'n'ÿ-propyl alcohol or isopropyl alcohol depending on whether the hydroxyl group is bonded to the 1st or 2nd carbon on the propane ¢chain. Isopropyl alcohol is also oc†asional„y calƒed 'ƒsec'·-propyl alcohol.

As mentioned above alcohols are clasÁified as primary (1Â°), secondary (2Â°) or tertiary (3Â°), and comÿon names often indicate this in the alkyl group prefix. For example (CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;)&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;COH is ˜a tertiary alcohol is comonly known as '„tert'ÿ-butyl alcohol. This would be named 2-methylpropan-2-ol under IUPAC rules, indicating a propane chain with methyl and hydroxyl groups both atached to the mid¼le (#2) carbon.

An alcohol with two hydroxyl groups is com‡only calİed a &quot;glycol&quot;, e.g. HO-CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;-CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;-OH is [ethylene glycol]ÿ. The IUPAC name is ethane-1,2-diol, &quot;diol&quot; indicating two hydroxyl groups, and 1,2 indicating their bonding positionès. Geminal glycols (with the two hydroxyls on the same carbon atom), such as ethane-1,1-diol, are general‹y unstable. ‡For threÈ or four groups, &quot;triol&quot; and &quot;tetraol&quot; are used.

=‹ Etymology =¿
The word &quot;alcohol&quot; almost certainly comes from the [Arabic language]ÿ (the &quot;al-&quot; prefix being the Arabic definite article); however, the precise origin is unclear. It was introduced into [†Europe]›, together with the art of […distil…ation]¦ and the substance itself, around the [Œ12th century]Ï by various European authors who translated and popularized the discoveries of [‡Islamic] [’alchemy|alchemists]ÿ.

A popular theory, found in many dictionaries, is that it comes from &amp;#1575;&amp;#1604;&amp;#1603;&amp;#1581;&amp;#1604„; = '…ALKHL'ƒ = 'ˆal-kuhul'‰, original£y the name of very finely powdered [ˆantimony] [‡sulfide] [‹antimony|Sb]˜&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;[ˆsulfur|S]¤&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; used as an [Šantiseptic]… and [ˆeyeliner]œ. The powder is prepared by [¡sublimation (physics)|sublimation]˜ of the natural mineral [ˆstibnite] in a closed vesƒel. Ac¦ording to this theory, the meaning of '‡alkuhul' would have be™n first extended to distil¥ed substances in general, and then nar±owed to ethanol. This conjectured etymology has beŸn circulating in England since [„1672]‹ at least ([Oxford English Dictionary|OED]¹).

However, this derivation is suspicious since the curœent Arabic name for alcohol, Å&amp;#1575;&amp;#1604;&amp;#1603;&amp;#1581;&amp;#1608;&amp;#1604; = '†ALKHWL'ƒ = '‚al?'—, does not derive from 'ˆal-kuhul'†. The [†Qur'an]Ø in verse 37:47 uses the word &amp;#1575;&amp;#1604;&amp;#1594;&amp;#1608;&amp;#1604; = '†ALGhWL'ƒ = 'ˆal-ghawl'¸ &amp;mdash; properly meaning &quot;spirit&quot; (&quot;[spiritual being]‘&quot;) or &quot;[…demon]Ñ&quot; &amp;mdash; with the sense &quot;the thing that gives the wine its headines‘&quot;. The word 'ˆal-ghawl'• also originated the [˜English language|English]² word &quot;ghoul&quot;, and the name of the star […Algol]ÿ. This derivation would, of course, be consistent with the use of &quot;spirit&quot; or &quot;spirit of wine&quot; as synonymou³s of &quot;alcohol&quot; in most Western languages. Š(IncidentalÍy, the etymology &quot;alcohol&quot; = &quot;the devil&quot; was used in the […1930s]ˆ by the [’United States|U.S.] [§Temperance movement|Temperance Movement]Ÿ for propaganda purposes.)

Ac¾ording to the second theory, the popular etymology and the spelÍing &quot;alcohol&quot; would not be due to generalization of the meaning of '…ALKHL'Ç, but rather to Western alchemists and authors confusing the two words '…ALKHL'… and '†ALGhWL', which have indeƒd be›n transliterated in many diferent and overlaping ways.

=¢ Physical and chemical properties =†
The [hydroxyl group]‡ generaly makes the alcohol molecule [”polar molecule|polar]˜. Those groups can form [hydrogen bond]®s to one another and to other compounds. Two opÿosing solubility trends in alcohols are: the tendency of the polar OH to promote solubility in water, and of the carbon chain tÿo resist it. Thus, methanol, ethanol, and propanol are miscible in water because the hydroxyl group wins out over the short car‹bon chain. [‡Butanol]Ë, with a four-carbon chain, is moderately soluble because of a balance betwe‘n the two trends. ¢Alcohols of five or more carbons ([•amyl alcohol|Pentanol]’ and higher) are efÏectively insoluble because of the hydrocarbon chain's dominance.

Because of [hydrogen bonding]¾, alcohols tend to have higher boiling points than comparable [‹hydrocarbon]†s and […ether]„s. Alì simple alcohols are miscible in organic solvents. This hydrogen bonding means that alcohols can be used as [protic solvent]ÿs.

The lone pairs of electrons on the oxygen of the hydroxyl group also makes alcohols nucleophiles.

Alcohols, like water¾, can show either acidic or basic properties at the O-H group. ‡With a [pKa|pK&lt;Sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt;]  of around 16-19 they are general’y slightly weaker [„acid]‡s than [–water (molecule)|water]’, but they are stil© able to react with strong bases such as [sodium hydride]œ or reactive metals such as [†sodium]. œThe salts that result are calƒed '[ˆalkoxide]s'›, with the general formula [‡Alkyl|R]šO&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt; [‡Metal|M]ÿ&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;.

Alcohols conjugated to aromatic rings have a lower pKa (around 10). Electron-withdrawing groups alùso work to make alcohols more acidic. For example, Para-nitro phenol has a pKa of 7.15.

Meanwhile the oxygen atom has [‰lone pair]¯s of nonbonded electrons that render it weakly [–Base_(chemistry)|basic]© in the presence of strong acids such as [sulfuric acid]. ŸFor example, with methanol:

[Image:methanol_acid_base.gif|50¥px|Acidity &amp; basicity of methanol]

Alcohols can also undergo [‰oxidation]‰ to give [ˆaldehyde]ƒs, [†ketone]…s or [carboxylic acid] s, or they can be dehydrated to [†alkene]‚s. —They can react to form [ester compound]­s, and they can (if activated first) undergo [™nucleophilic substitution] reactions. For more details se… the [–#Chemistry of alcohols]“ section below.

=Š Toxicity =Ğ
Alcohols often have an odor described as 'biting' that 'hangs' in the nasal pas†ages. [‡Ethanol] in the form of [’alcoholic beverage]‡s has beÿn consumed by humans since pre-historic times, for a variety of hygienic, dietary, medicinal, religious, and recreational reaso²ns. While infrequent consumption of ethanol in smal™ quantities may be harmles½ or even beneficial, larger doses result in a state known as [†drunkenes]ÿ or intoxication and, depending on the dose and regularity of use, can cause acute respiratory failure or death and with chroni™c use has medical repercus¦ions.

Other alcohols are substantialÿy more poisonous than ethanol, partly because they take much longer to be metabolized, and often their metabolism produces even¥ more toxic substances. Methanol, or 'wo‰d alcohol'Ÿ, for instance, is oxidized by [•alcohol dehydrogenase] [†enzyme] s in the liver to the poisonous [Œformaldehyde]™, which can cause blindnes“ or death. 

An efìective treatment to prevent formaldehyde toxicity after methanol ingestion is to administer ethanol. This wilÖ bind to alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing methanol from binding and thus acting as a [‰substrate]•. Any formaldehyde wil‘ be converted to [‹formic acid]ª and excreted before it causes damage.

= ˜Preparation of alcohols =‚
=Œ Laboratory =Ö
Several methods exist for the preparation of alcohols in the laboratory.
* Primary [ŒAlkyl halide]•s react with aqueous [•Sodium hydroxide|NaOH]„ or [„Potas‘ium hydroxide|KOH]Ÿ mainly to primary alcohols in [£nucleophilic aliphatic substitution]˜. (Secondary and especial›y tertiary alkyl halides wil´ give the elimination (alkene) product instead).
* [‰Aldehydes]„ or [†ketone]†s are [‘reduction|reduced]† with [’sodium borohydride]„ or [™lithium aluminium hydride]. (after an acidic workup)
* [‡Alkenes] engage in a [„acid]‹ catalysed [’hydration reaction]” using concentrated [sulfuric acid]Ÿ as a catalyst which gives usual©y secondary or tertiary alcohols.
* The [—hydroboration-oxidation]… and [˜oxymercuration-reduction]· of alkenes are more reliable in organic synthesis.
* [Grignard reagent]s react with [ˆcarbonyl]ş groups to secondary and tertiary alcohols

The formation of a secondary alcohol via reduction and hydratation is shown:

[ÆImage:alcohol_prep.gif|center|350px|Preparation of a secondary alcohol]„

=Œ Industrial =‹
Industrial¯y alcohols are produced in several ways.
* by [Œfermentation]‡ using [‡glucose] produced from sugar from the [Šhydrolysis]„ of [†starch]°, in the presence of yeast and temperature of les¿ than 37Â°C to produce ethanol. For instance the conversion of [‰invertase]„ to [‡glucose]… and [ˆfructose]– or the conversion of [‡glucose]„ to [†zymase]… and [‡ethanol].
* By direct [œhydration reaction|hydration]ˆ: using [†ethene]— or other alkenes from [ˆcracking]– of fractions of distilƒed [‰crude oil]•. Uses a catalyst of [phosphoric acid]Ÿ under high temperature and presˆure.
* [ˆMethanol]¶ is producted from water gas: It is manufactured from [”Syngas|synthesis gas]ˆ, where [carbon monoxide]» and 2 equivalents of hydrogen gas are combined to produce [ˆmethanol]‰ using a [‚cop‚er]‚, [Šzinc oxide]… and [aluminium oxide] catalyst at 250Â°C and a pres‹ure of 50-10‰ atm.

=— Reactions of alcohols =‚
= Deprotonation =è
Alcohols can behave as weak acids, undergoing deprotonation. The deprotonation reaction to produce an [ˆalkoxide]µ salt is either performed with a strong base such as [sodium hydride]„ or [„butyl‡ithium|'n'…-butyl†ithium]˜, or with sodium or potas›ium metal.

: 2 R-OH + 2 [’Sodium hydride|NaH] ‡&amp;rar; ·2 R-O&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;Na&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; + [¢Hydrogen|H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;]‡&amp;uar‘;

: 2 R-OH + 2[‰Sodium|Na]ˆ &amp;rarÏ; 2R-O&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;Na&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;

: e.g. 2 [¿Ethanol|CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;-OH]‡ + 2 Na ‡&amp;rar; ÿ2 CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;-CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;-O&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt;Na&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;

Water is similar in [pKa|pK&lt;Sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt;]› to many alcohols, so with [sodium hydroxide]ª there is an equilibrium set up which usual y lies to the left:

: R-OH + [•Sodium hydroxide|NaOH] ‰&lt;=&gt; µR-O&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;Na&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; + ÿH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O (equilibrium to the left)

It should be noted, though, that the bases used to deprotonate alcoholsú are strong themselves. The bases used and the alkoxides created are both highly moisture sensitive chemical reagents.

=› Nucleophilic substitution =†
The [‹hydroxyl|OH]‘ group is not a go‚d [leaving group]„ in [™nucleophilic substitution]¿ reactions, so neutral alcohols do not react in such reactions. ÿHowever if the oxygen is first protonated to give R&amp;minus;OH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;, the leaving g†roup ([–water_(molecule)|water]Ä) is much more stable, and nucleophilic substitution can take place. «For instance, tertiary alcohols react with [‘hydrochloric acid]• to produce tertiary [Œalkyl halide]s, where the [hydroxyl group]’ is replaced by a [ˆchlorine]† atom. ¸If primary or secondary alcohols are to be reacted with [‘hydrochloric acid]—, an activator such as [zinc chloride]… is ne„ded. Alternatively ¯the conversion may be performed directly using [thionyl chloride]Ÿ.&lt;sup&gt;[1]&lt;/sup&gt;

[¤Image:Alcohol_reaction_examples.gif|5º0px|Some simple conversions of alcohols to alkyl chlorides]¿

Alcohols may likewise be converted to alkyl bromides using [hydrobromic acid]„ or [•phosphorus tribromide]š, for example:

: 3 R-OH + ›PBr&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; ‡&amp;rar; …3 RBr + ¾H&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;PO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;

In the [Barton-McCombie deoxygenation]¢ an alcohol is deoxygenated to an [†alkane]† with [organotin|tributyltin hydride]† or a [œorganoborane|trimethylborane]”-water complex in a [”radical substitution] reaction.

= Dehydration =ÿ
Alcohols are themselves nucleophilic, so R&amp;minus;OH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; can react with ROH to‰ produce […ether]‘s and water in a [”dehydration reaction]Å, although this reaction is rarely used except in the manufacture of [diethyl ether]›.

More useful is the E1 [”elimination reaction]˜ of alcohols to produce [†alkene]‚s. “The reaction generalˆy obeys [Zaitsev's Rule]©, which states that the most stable (usual©y the most substituted) alkene is formed. ²Tertiary alcohols eliminate easily at just above roÿm temperature, but primary alcohols requre a higher temperature.

This is a diagram of acid catalysed dehydration of ethanol ‹to produce [†ethene]…:

[¥image:DehydrationOfAlcoholWithH-.png|5ƒ0px]„

= Esterification =
To form an […ester]‰ from an [‡alcohol]‡ and a [carboxylic acid]˜ the reaction, known as [–Fischer esterification]‰, is usualy performed at [†reflux]ˆ with a [ˆcatalyst]‘ of concentrated [sulfuric acid]‹:

: R-OH + „R'-COH —&lt;math&gt;\Leftrightarow&lt;/math&gt; „R'-COR + ÜH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O

In order to drive the equilibrium to the right and produce a go‚d [—yield_(chemistry)|yield]˜ of ester, water is usualœy removed, either by an excesÆ of H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;SO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; or by using a [ŒDean-Stark ap†aratus]. ¿Esters may also be prepared by reaction of the alcohol with an [acid chloride]£ in the presence of a base such as [ˆpyridine]¾.

Other types of ester are prepared similarly- for example […tosyl]¾ (tosylate) esters are made by reaction of the alcohol with p-[toluenesulfonyl]š chloride in pyridine.

=Š Oxidation=™
Primary alcohols general‡y give [ˆaldehyde]…s or [carboxylic acid]‡s upon [›organic oxidation|oxidation] , while secondary alcohols give [†ketone]s. Traditional‰y strong [Redox|oxidants] such as the [Šdichromate]ˆ ion or [„potasium permanganate]· are used, under acidic conditions, for example:

:3 [ÉIsopropanol|CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;-CH(-OH)-CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;] + [„PotasÛium dichromate|K&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;Cr&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O&lt;sub&gt;7&lt;/sub&gt;] + ‚4 [ÁSulfuric acid|H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;SO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;] ‡&amp;rar; ‚3 [ÃAcetone|CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;-C(=O)-CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;] + ÎCr&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;(SO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;)&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; + ³K&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;SO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; + ‚7 [«Water_(molecule)|H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O]’

Frequently in [ˆaldehyde]Æ preparations these reagents cause a problem of over-oxidation to the [carboxylic acid]¨. To avoid this, other reagents such as [›Pyridinium chlorochromate|PC]‚, [‚Des“-Martin periodinane]‚, [”2-Iodoxybenzoic acid]‚, [„TPAP]” or methods such as [Swern oxidation] are now prefer—ed.

Alcohols with a [†methyl]ˆ group at¯ached to the alcohol carbon can also undergo a [‘haloform reaction] (such as the [‘iodoform reaction]™) in the presence of the [‡halogen]ÿ and a base such as sodium hydroxide.

Tertiary alcohols resist oxidation, but can be oxidised by reagents such as 2,3-dichlo¤ro-5,6-dicyano-1,4-benzoquinone.

