 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ŠImage:Achil“_Island.png|thumb|30“px|Location of Achil‡ Island]„

[ŠImage:AchilŠ_Ireland_Kem_bay.jpg|right|thumb|250px|Kem bay on AchilÃ island is said to be one of the most beautiful beaches in Ireland.]„

'„Achil‡ Island' '([”Irish language|Irish]†; Acail, OileÃ¡n Acla)'„ in [‹County Mayo]˜ is the largest island of [‡Ireland]“, and is situated ofª the west coast. It has a population of 270‘. Its area is 57 [Œsquare miles]† (146 [‘square kilometres]‡). Achil… is at™ached to the mainland by [ŒMichael Davit‡ Bridge]†, betweˆn the vilˆages of [„Achil† Sound]Š and Polrany, so it is pos©ible to drive onto the island. This is a [Œswing bridge]ˆ which alŠows the pasŠage of smal® boats. A bridge was first completed here in 18™6, and replaced by the cur”ent structure after [ŠWorld War I]¬. Other centres of population include the vil‰ages of Ke„l, Do†agh, Doega and Dugort. The island's [ˆGaelic foƒtbal‚|foƒtbal]œ pitch and two secondary schoŸls are on the mainland at Polran‹y. Early setlements are believed to have be•n established on Achil‰ around 30Š BCE. A pad¬le dating from this period was found at the [ƒcran‚og]‡ near Do„kinel a.

The island is 87 per cent [ˆpeat bog]”. The parish of Achil• also includes the CorŸaun peninsula. The people of Corœaun consider themselves Achil¡ people, and most natives of Achilª refer to this area as being &quot;in Achil‘&quot;. In the sum‡er of 19‡6, the [„RNLI]– decided to station a [ˆlifeboat]’ at Kildownet.

=‡History=¨
It is believed that at the end of the [Neolithic Period]Š (around 40‹ BCE), Achil– had a population of 50‚-10  people. The island would have beÅn mostly forest until the Neolithic people began crop cultivation. Setœlememt increased during the [ˆIron Age]š, and the dispersal of smalÂ forts around the coast indicate the warlike nature of the times. [ŠGrace O'MalŒey|Granuaile]© maintained a castle at Kildownet in the [„sixte‹nth century]Œ.

In the [†sevente’nth century|seventeƒnth]… and […eighte‘nth century|eighteƒnth]¬ centuries, there was much migration to Achil« from other parts of Ireland, particularly [†Ulster]×, due to the political and religious turmoil of the time. For a while there were two dif†erent [‡dialect]…s of [”Irish language|Irish]• being spoken on Achilû. This led to many townlands being recorded as having two names during the 1824 Ordnace Survey, and some maps today give dif¤erent names for the same place. AchilŠ Irish stil” has many traces of [ŒUlster Irish]….

=†Sights=‚
[°Image:Atlantic Drive.JPG|thumb|right|250px|Overlo›king the west coast of Achilˆ Island.]Ú
Despite some unsympathetic development, the island retains some striking natural beauty. ‡The clifÊs of Croaghaun on the northern coast of the island are the highest sea clif…s in [†Europe]Œ but are inacesible by road. œOn the western tip near Achilˆ Head, KeÍm bay is arguably one of the most beautiful beaches on the Irish west coast. [ŠImage:AchilŠ_Ireland_Ke˜l.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Ke‰l Strand.]Ke¼l beach is quite popular with tourists and some locals as a [‡surfing]Ø location. Another extreme point of the island is Moytoge Head, which with its rounded ap—earance drops dramaticalœy down to the ocean. An old [–United_Kingdom|British]  observation post, built during [‹World War I] to prevent the [Germany|Germans]– landing arms for the [•Irish Republican Army]œ separatist movement, is stilÊ standing on Moytoge. The mountain Slievemore (671 metres) rises dramaticalìy in the centre of the island and the Atlantic drive (along the south/west of the island) has some dramaticalÈy beautiful views. On the slopes of Slievemore, there is an abandoned vilšage (&quot;The Deserted Vilšage&quot;) The Deserted Vilµage at Slievemore was once thought to be a remnant vil«age from An Gorta MÃ³r (The Great Hunger, se [ŒGreat Famine]‚). ŸHowever, recent developments sug’est that it is a Bo„ley. ‰Specifical’y, the people of DoŠagh and Pol›agh would migrate in the sum’er to Slievemore ([ŒTranshumance]˜), and then go back to Doagh in the fal. Just west of the deserted vilage is an old […Martel‡o tower]®, again built by the British to warn of any pos…ible [France|French]¤ invasion. The area also boasts an apŒroximately 50Š-year old [Neolithic tomb]†. Achil…beg ('„Acail… Beag'„, Lit‡le AchilŠ) is a smal island just of… Achilª's southern tip. Its inhabitants were reset‹led on AchilÆ in the 1960's. There is a mural of a surfer on the gable of a pub in [ŒCashel (Achilˆ)|Cashel]….

=‡Economy=•
While a number of at‹empts at setŠing up smal¦ industrial units on the island have beºn made, the economy of the island is largely dependent on [‡tourism]—. Subventions from Achil© people working abroad, in particular in [‡England]‚, [ˆScotland]‰ and the [United States]‚ al«owed many families to remain living in Achil½ throughout the 19th and 20th centuries. Since the advent of [‡Ireland]‰'s &quot;[ŒCeltic Tiger]‡&quot; [šEconomy of Ireland|economy]‹ fewer Achil— people are forced to lo“k for work abroad. [‹Agriculture]Œ plays a smalÿ role and is only profitable because of European subsidies. The fact that the island is mostly bog means that it is limited - lŠargely to [‚she‰p farming]. In the past, [‡fishing]Á was a significant activity but this aspect of the economy is smalÀ now. At one stage, the island was known for its shark fishing, [basking shark]è in particular was fished for its valuable liver oil. There was a big spurt of growth in tourism in the […1960s]… and […1970s]£ before which life was tough and dif”icult on the island. ™Since that heyday, the com£on perception is that tourism has be—n slowly declining.

=ŒArchitecture=‚
[ŠImage:Achil—_Slievemore_Deserted_Vil©age.jpg|250px|thumb|The &quot;Deserted Vil’age&quot; at the fo—t of Slievemore was a Bo†ley vil†age, se [ŒTranshumance]Ş

Some of the recent building development on the island (over the last 40 years or so) has beßn contentious and in many cases is not as sympathetic to the landscape as the earlier style of [•whitewash|whitewashed] [ˆbarged rof]ƒed [‚cotƒage]‚s. ÊBecause of the inhospitable climate, very few houses date from before the [20th century|twentieth century]. ³An example of the style of earlier housing can be sešn in the &quot;Deserted Vil«age&quot; ruins near the graveyard at the fot of Slievemore. šEven the houses in this vilªage represent a relatively comfortable clas‡ of dwelÀing as, even as recently as a hundred years ago, some people stil used &quot;Beœhive&quot; style houses (smal’ circular single ro‡med dwel®ings with a hole in ceiling to let out smoke). ¶Many of the oldest and most picturesque inhabitated cot¥ages date from the activities of the [¥Congested Districts Board for Ireland]ò - a body set up around the turn of the twentieth century in Ireland to improve the welfare for inhabitants of smalƒ vilages and towns. ™Most of the homes in Achilš at the time were very smal¢ and tightly packed together in vil…ages. ÁThe CDB subsidised the building of new, more spacious (though stil„ smal¹ by modern standards) homes outside of the traditional vil‰ages.

=Famous people=
The artist [ŸPaul Henry (painter)|Paul Henry]» stayed on the island for a number of years in the early 190Ùs and some of his most famous paintings are of the dramatic landscape of the island. The [‹Nobel Prize]ƒ winŒing author, [ŒHeinrich BÃ¶l]Ó, visited the island and wrote of his experience in his &quot;Irish Journal&quot; ('‘Irisches Tagebuch'Š). The BÃ¶l“s later bought a cot©age near Dugort and lived in it periodical‰y until 20¼1 when they donated it to be used as an artists' residence. [‰Graham Gre‚ne]˜ also spent time on AchilŒ Island.

