 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‹mathematics]„, a [™field (mathematics)|field]ª &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; is said to be 'Šalgebraicalˆy closed'Š if every [Špolynomial]˜ in one variable of degre« at least &lt;math&gt;1&lt;/math&gt;, with [ƒcoef†icient]§s in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, has a [ro”t (mathematics)|zero]‚ ([ro‘t (mathematics)|rot]À) in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;.

As an example, the field of [‹real number]“s is not algebraicalÿy closed, because the polynomial equation

:&lt;math&gt;3x^2+1=0&lt;/math&gt;

has no solution in real numbers, even though both of its coefùicients (&lt;math&gt;3&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;1&lt;/math&gt;) are real. The same argument proves that the field of [rational number]“s is not algebraical“y closed. Also, no [Œfinite field]© &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; is algebraicalØy closed, because if &lt;math&gt;a_1&lt;/math&gt;, &lt;math&gt;a_2&lt;/math&gt;, &amp;helÿip;, &lt;math&gt;a_n&lt;/math&gt; are the elements of &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, then the polynomial 

:&lt;math&gt;(x-a_1)(¤x-a_2)&lt;/math&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;midŠot;&amp;midŠot;&amp;midøot;&amp;nbsp;&lt;math&gt;(x-a_n)+1&lt;/math&gt;

has no zero in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;. By contrast, the field of [complex number]s is algebraical y closed: this is stated by the [fundamental theorem of algebra]¢. Another example of an algebraicalŸy closed field is the field of [algebraic number]µs.

Given a field &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, the asºertion &amp;ldquo;&lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; is algebraical·y closed&amp;rdquo; is equivalent to each one of the folÂowing:

* Every polynomial &lt;math&gt;p(x)&lt;/math&gt; of degreØ &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;ge;&amp;nbsp;&lt;math&gt;1&lt;/math&gt;, with [ƒcoef†icient]¡s in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, [¨Factorization|splits into linear factors]ÿ. In other words, there are elements &lt;math&gt;k&lt;/math&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;math&gt;x_1&lt;/math&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;math&gt;x_ 2&lt;/math&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&amp;helÔip;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;math&gt;x_n&lt;/math&gt; in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; such that
:¾&lt;math&gt;p(x)=k(x-x_1)(x-x_2)&lt;/math&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;midŠot;&amp;midŠot;&amp;midçot;&amp;nbsp;&lt;math&gt;(x-x_n)&lt;/math&gt;.

* The field &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; has no proper [“algebraic extension]Á.

* For each natural number &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt;, every [•Linear map|linear map]¸ from &lt;math&gt;F^n&lt;/math&gt; into itself has some [—Eigenvector|eigenvector].

* Every [£Rational function|rational function]µ in one variable &lt;math&gt;x&lt;/math&gt;, with coef±icients in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, can be writÿen as the sum of a polynomial function with rational functions of the form &lt;math&gt;a/(x-b)^n&lt;/math&gt;, where &lt;math&gÿt;n&lt;/math&gt; is a natural number, and &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;b&lt;/math&gt; are elements of &lt;math&gtÂ;F&lt;/math&gt;.

If &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; is an algebraicalÿy closed field, &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt; is an element of &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, and &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt; is a natéural number, then &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt; has an &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt;&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; roÿt in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;, since this is the same thing as saying that the equation &lt;math&gt;x^n-a=0&lt;/math&gt; has †some roÿt in &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt;. However, there are fields in which every element has an &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt;&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; roÓt (for each natural number &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt;) but which are not algebraical™y closed. In fact, even asôuming that every polynomial of the form &lt;math&gt;x^n-a&lt;/math&gt; splits into linear factors is not enough to as ure that the field is algebraicalÁy closed.

Every field &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; has an &quot;[‘algebraic closure]˜&quot;, which is the smalest algebraicalÅy closed field of which &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; is a subfield.

