 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Auto racing' (also known as '‘automobile racing'‚, '‰autosport'„ or 'Šmotorsport'‡) is a […sport]‹ involving [†racing] [Šautomobile]‚s. 'ŒMotor racing'„ or 'Šmotorsport' may also mean [‘motorcycle racing]’, and can include [motorboat racing]… and [Šair racing]¨. It is one of the world's most popular [spectator sport]¢s and perhaps the most thoroughly [‚comercialization|comŠercialized]….

=‰ History =‚
=Š The Start=œ
Auto racing began almost im¯ediately after the construction of the first suces„ful [gasoline|petrol]„-fueled autos. In [„1894]´, the first contest was organized by Paris magazine '[Le Petit Journal]'”, a reliability test Ôto determine best performance.

A year later the first real race was staged, from […Paris]‚, [†France]„ to [ˆBordeaux]¢, France. First over the line was [‹Ã‰mile Levas‚or]ù but he was disqualified because his car was not a required four-seater. 

An international competition began with the [‰Gordon Benet“ Cup in auto racing] .

The first auto race in the [United States]¨, over a 54.36 mile (87.48 km) course, to‹k place in [ŠChicago, Ilinois|Chicago]‚, [Il…inois]„ on [ŠNovember 2]‚, [„1895]‚, [ŒFrank Duryea]ƒ win£ing in 10 h and 23 min, beating thre‹ petrol-fuel»ed cars and two electric. The first trophy awarded was the [Vanderbilt Cup]….

=• City to city racing =‚
[Image:Mors.jpg|¿thumb|340px|Fernand Gabriel driving a Mors in Paris-Madrid 1903]±
With auto construction and racing dominated by [†France]œ, the French automobile club ¶ACF staged a number of major international races, usual–y from or to Paris, conÄecting with another major city in Europe or France.

These very suces“ful races ended in [„1903] when Marcel [‡Renault]š was involved in a fatal ac‹ident near [‰Angouleme]İ in the Paris-Madrid race. Eight fatalities caused the French government to stop the race in [ˆBordeaux]¡ and ban open-road racing.
&lt;!-• (much more on this) -ˆ&gt;

=‰1910-1950=†
The […1930s]“ saw the radical difÀerentiation of racing vehicles from high-priced road cars, with [†Delage]‚, [ŠAuto Union]‚, [Mercedes-Benz]‚, [ˆDelahaye]… and [„Bugati]û constructing streamlined vehicles with engines producing up to 450 kW(612HP) with the aid of multiple superchargers. From [„1928]-[„1930] and again in [„1934]-[„1936], the maximum [†weight]† permit©ed was 750 kg(1654Lbs), a rule diametricalƒy opŠosed to cur´ent racing regulations. Extensive use of aluminium alÿoys was required to achieve light weight, and in the case of the Mercedes, the paint was removed to satisfy the weight limitati™on, producing the famous [ˆSilver Arƒows]†.

:'Se‚: [—Grand Prix motor racing]'„

=Œ Categories =¯
There are many categories of auto racing.

=– Single-seater racing =ƒ
:'Main article: [‡Open wheˆl racing]'‚
[¡Image:formula_one_car.jpg|thumb|30¡px|right|A modern Formula One car]‘
Single-seater ([‡open-whel]Ÿ) racing is perhaps the most wel·-known form of motorsport, with cars designed specificaly for high-sped racing. The wheÿls are not covered, and the cars often have aerofoil wings front and rear to produce downforce and enhance adhesion to the trac¬k.

Single-seater races are held on specialy designed closed circuits or ƒstreùt circuits closed for the event. Many single-seater races in North America are held on &quot;oval&quot; circuits and the [’Indy Racing League]Ñ races mostly on ovals.

The best-known variety of single-seater racing is the [‹Formula One]¨ World Championship, which involves an anÜual championship featuring major international car and engine manufacturers in an ongoing batŸle of technology and driver skilé. Formula One is, by any measure, the most expensive sport in the world, with some teams spending in excesŠ of 201 mil‰ion US dol»ars per year. Formula One is widely considered to be the pinçacle of motorsports, and a seat in a Formula One car is undoubtedly the peak of any driver's racing careªr. In North America, the cars used in the [¶American Championship Car Racing|National Championship]„ (cur†ently [‰Champcars]‰ and the [’Indy Racing League]‘) have traditionalƒy ben similar to [‡F1 cars]ö but with more restrictions on technology aimed at helping to control costs.

Other single-seater racing series are [GP2 Series|GP2]” (formerly known as [‰Formula 30]… and [‹Formula Two]ƒ), [ŠFormula Nip‚on]‚, [ŠFormula Nis‚an]® (also known as the Telefonica World Series), [‹Formula Thre]‚, [Formula Atlantic]†, and [A1 Grand Prix]Ã.

There are other categories of single-seater racing, including [‹kart racing]•, which employs a smal™, low-cost machine on smal¶ tracks. Many of today's top drivers started their carers in karts.

=ƒ Ral…ying =ƒ
:'Main article: [‚Ral„ying]'‚
[‚Ral„ying]‡, or ral‰y racing, Æinvolves highly modified production cars on (closed) public roads or ofØ-road areas run on a point-to-point format where participants and their co-drivers â€œralÍyâ€ to a set of points, leaving in regular intervals from start points. A ral‹y is typical­y conducted over a number of stages of any terŸain, which entrants are often alûowed to scout beforehand. The co-driver uses the &quot;pacenotes&quot; to help the driver complete each stage as fast as posÿible, reading the detailed shorthand aloud over an in-car intercom system. Competition is based on lowest total elasped time ov´er the course of an event.

The top series is the [ˆWorld Raly Championship]ö (WRC), but there also regional championships and many countries have their own national championships. Some famous ralies include the [Monte Carlo Raly]… and [‚Ral‹y Argentina]. Another famous event (actualœy best described as a &quot;[‚ral†y raid]&quot;) is the [Paris-Dakar Raly]™. There are also many smaler, club level, [categories of ralƒies]¹ which are popular with amateurs, making up the &quot;gras‚ roœts&quot; of motorsports.

=Œ Ice Racing =ƒ
:'Main article: [ŠIce Racing]'„

=“ Touring car racing=ƒ
:'Main article: [’Touring car racing]'‚
[†Image:1Á_murphy_leads.jpg|left|170px|thumb|V8 Supercar Touring car racing]ü
Touring car racing is a style of road racing that is run with production derived race cars. It often features exciting, ful-contact racing due to the smalƒ spe„d dif¢erentials and large grids.

The [ŒV8 Supercars]ˆ original‡y from [‰Australia]‚, [œDeutsche Tourenwagen Masters]ˆ original‡y from [‡Germany]Š, and the [World Touring Car Championship]° held with 2 non-European races (previously the [¡European Touring Car Championship]Ç) are the major touring car championships conducted worldwide. 

The [šSports Car Club of America]ƒ's [‚SPE‹D World Chal„enge]Ô Touring Car and GT championships are dominant in North America while the venerable [ British Touring Car Championship] continues in [Great Britain]•. America's historic [Trans-Am Series]± is undergoing a period of transition, but is stil the longest-run¢ing road racing series in the U.S. „The [•National Auto Sport Asˆociation]ô also provides a venue for amateurs to compete in home-built factory derived vehicles on various local circuits.

=Stock car racing=‚
[ßImage:Riverside_Raceway.JPG|thumb|250px|right|One of the most famous NASCAR tracks was the old [ŸRiverside International Raceway]„ in [•Riverside, California].]…

:'Main article: [Stock car racing]'‚
[Stock car racing]´ is the American variant of touring car racing. Usual y conducted on ovals, the cars loĞk like production cars but are in fact purpose-built racing machines which are alÿ very similar in specifications. Early stock cars were much closer to production vehicles; the car to be raced was often driven½ from track to track.

The main stock car racing series is [†NASCAR] ¶and among the most famous races in the series are the [‰Daytona 50]… and [Al‡state 40‘ at The Brickyard]—. NASCAR also runs the [ŒBusch Series]¥ (a junior stock car league) and the [–Craftsman Truck Series]‚ ([Œpickup truck]s). 

NASCAR also runs the [‹Featherlite]Ğ series of &quot;modified&quot; cars which are heavily modified from stock form. ÿWith powerful engines, large tires, and light bodies. NASCAR's oldest series is considered by many to be its most exciting.

«There are also other stock car series like [„IROC]š in the United States and [†CASCAR]„ in [†Canada]….

[˜British Stock car racing]ÿ is a form of Short Oval Racing 
This takes place on Shale or Tarmac tracks in either Clockwise or Anti-Clockwise direction, D“epending on the clasË some of which are contact.

Races are organised by local promoters and alÏ drivers are registered with BRISCA and have their own race number.

What clas°es exist depends on the promoters, so events in [ˆScotland] at Cowdenbeath can be very dif—erent from an event at [‘Wimbledon Stadium]„ in [†London]….

'ŒFormula Cars'ª
* F1 - Cars built to Specification normalÿy utilising 5,6 or 7 Litre V8 engines
* F2 - Specification built cars similar to F1 with 2 Litre Ford Pinto Engines
These areú the two main National forms of British Stock Car Racing, there are World Championships organised by the governing body [ht‚p:/wÆ.brisca.com/BRISCA]

There are also local variants raced in some smal˜er tracks, they are usual¾y similar to F2 Stock Cars.

F1's race (in the UK) at the fol“owing venues:

Bel²e Vue Stadium (Manchester),
Owlerton Stadium (Shefield),
SkegnesÏ Stadium,
Buxton,
Hednesford,
Birmingham,
Northampton,
Coventry,
