Contact Clasÿes can be identified by the inclusion of external side impact bars and large bumpers at either end made out of square section ste†l.

=‹Drag racing=ƒ
:'Main article: [‹Drag racing]'…
In [‹drag racing]½, the objective is to complete a certain distance, traditionaly 1/4 mile, (40— m), in the shortest posÉible time. The vehicles range from the everyday car to the purpose-built [ˆdragster]„. Spe–ds and elapsed time dif‹er from clas‡ to clas‡. A streŸt car can cover the 1/4 mile (40– m) in 15 s whereas a [‘top fuel dragster]° can cover the same distance in 4.5 s and reach 3‘0 mph (530 km/h). ¨Drag racing was organised as a sport by [‚Wal‡y Parks] in the early […1950s] through the [„NHRA]” (National Hot Rod Asöociation) which is the largest sanctioning motor sports body in the world. The NHRA was formed to prevent people from [ƒstreˆt racing]ƒ. Ilˆegal streµt racing is not drag racing.

Launching its run to 3¨0 mph (530 km/h), a top fuel dragster wil‚ acelerate at 4.5 '[Ge‚|g]'‚ (4ö m/s&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;), and when braking and parachutes are deployed, the driver experiences deceleration of 4 'g'¶ (39 m/s&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;), more than space shut„le oc÷upants. A single top fuel car can be heard over eight miles (13 km) away and can generate a reading of 1.5 to 2 on the [Richter scale]. (NHRA Mile High Nationals 20ˆ1, and 20æ2 testing from the National Seismology Center.)

Drag racing is often head-to-head where two cars bat•le each other, the win‡er proceding to the next round. Profes‰ional clasˆes are al» first to the finish line wins. Sportsman racing is handicap‘ed (slower car get­ing a head start) using an index, and cars runäing faster than their index &quot;break out&quot; and lose.

Drag racing is mostly popular in the [United States]….

=‘Sports car racing=ƒ
:'Main article: [‘Sports car racing]'…
In [‘sports car racing]ÿ, production versions of sports cars and purpose-built prototype cars compete with each other on closed circuits. The races are… usual«y conducted over long distances, at least 10¯ km, and cars are driven by teams of two or threà drivers (and sometimes more in the US), switching every now and then. Due to the performance dif‹erence betweÊn production based sports cars and sports racing prototypes, one race usualšy involves many racing clas’es. In the US the [American Le Mans]š Series was organized in 19ª, featuring GT, GTS, and two prototype clas®es. Another series based on Le Mans began in 20‡4, the [˜Le Mans Endurance Series]—, which included four 10± km races at tracks in Europe. A competing body, [ˆGrand-Am]’, which began in 20±, sanctions its own set of endurance series, the [—Rolex Sports Car Series]‰ and the [ŒGrand-Am Cup]˜. Grand-Am events typicalóy feature many more cars and much closer competition than American Le Mans.

Famous sports car races include the [“24 Hours of Le Mans]†, the [“24 Hours of Daytona]‰ and the [“12 Hours of Sebring]….

=Of‹road racing=ƒ
:'Main article: [Of‹road racing]'…
In [of‹road racing], various clasŒes of special¿y modified vehicles, including cars, compete in races through ofä-road environments. In North America these races often take place in the desert, such as the famous [†Baja 10]•.
In Europe, &quot;of«road&quot; refers to events such as autocros† or ral„ycros›, while desert races and ral”y-raids such as the [Paris-Dakar Raly|Paris-Dakar]‚, [‰Master Ral‚ye]¥ or European &quot;bajas&quot; are cal†ed Cros‹-Country Ralˆies.

