=‹Kart racing=ƒ
:'Main article: [‹Kart racing]'’
Although often se¸n as the entry point for serious racers into the sport, [‹kart racing]Ô, or karting, can be an economic way to try your luck at motorsport and is also a fulÍy fledged international sport in its own right. World-famous F1-drivers like [šMichael Schumacher|Michael]… and [Ralf Schumacher]¿ and most of the typical starting grid of a modern Grand Prix toÉk up the sport at around the age of eight, with some testing from age threÌ. Several former motorcycle champions have also taken up the sport, notably [ŒWayne Rainey]¡, who was paralysed in a racing ac¡ident and now races a hand-control¿ed kart. As one of the cheapest ways to go racing, karting is seÍing its popularity grow worldwide.

Go-karts, or just &quot;karts&quot; - seÃm very distant from normal road cars, with dimunitive frames and whels, but a smalã engine combined with very light weight make for a quick machine. The tracks are also on a much smal£er scale, making kart racing more aces—ible to the people.

