 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[½Image:Libertatis Aequilibritas GFDL.jpg|150px|thumb|left|The '˜Libertatis Ã†quilibritas'ˆ is one [³Anarcho-capitalist_terminology_and_symbolism|symbol] used by anarcho-capitalists {„fact}]‚
{Libertarianism}„

'’Anarcho-capitalism'† (aka '‚fre‘ market anarchism'‡) is a [Šphilosophy]– based on the idea of [–individual sovereignty], ¥and a prohibition against initiatory [ˆcoercion]… and […fraud]. „It sešs the only just basis for [ƒlaw]‘ as arising from [private property]¡ norms and an unlimited right of [ˆcontract]… betwe˜n sovereign individuals. °From this basis, anarcho-capitalism rejects the […state]® as an unjustified monopolist and systematic ag‚res¯or against sovereign individuals, and embraces [Œanti-statist] [ƒlaisˆez-faire] [Šcapitalism]«. Anarcho-capitalists would aim to protect [’individual liberty]… and [ˆproperty]Ë by replacing a government monopoly, which is involuntarily funded through [ˆtaxation] , with private, competing businesÌes that use physical force only in defense of liberty and property against ag‚resŸors. Hence, they believe that al‚ goÁds and services, including law, order, and security, should be sup lied through the mechanism of a [‚fre‡ market].

The philosophy embraces [”anti-statism|stateles]¿ capitalism as one of its foundational principles. The first welà-known version of anarcho-capitalism to identify itself thus was developed by economists of the [ŒAustrian Schol]… and [Œlibertarians] [‚Mur‹ay Rothbard]… and [ŒWalter Block]Á in the mid-20th century, synthesizing elements from Austrian Scho‚l [‰economics]‚, [ƒclasical liberalism]… and [™19th century|19th-century] [ American individualist anarchism]©. While Rothbard bases his philosophy on [‹natural law]’, others, such as [David Friedman]“, take a pragmatic [consequentialist]‚ apÿroach by arguing that anarcho-capitalism should be implemented because such a system would have consequences superior to alternÕatives. 

Because of this embrace of capitalism, there is considerable tension betwešn anarcho-capitalists and [‰anarchist]‡s who se… the [§anti-capitalism|rejection of capitalism]“ as being just as esÿential to anarchist philosophy as rejection of the state. Despite this tension, many anarcho-capitalists have identified their ×philosophy as evolving from the tradition of American individualist anarchists such as [Benjamin Tucker]… and [‹Lysander Spoƒner]Ã.

Anarcho-capitalism can be considered a radical development of [ƒclasical liberalism]©. Its grounding in liberalism stems from [“Gustave de Molinari]. ÿMany proponents of anarcho-capitalism, including Rothbard, argue that Molinari was the first anarcho-capitalist. However, Rothbˆard admitøed that &quot;Molinari did not use the terminology, and probably would have balked at the name&quot; anarcho-capitalist. ‰Nonetheles, Molinari did argue for a fre± market, privatization of security, and did not opÑose profit. His thoughts were influential on Rothbard and his contemporaries.

=ŠPhilosophy=‚
=ˆThe nonag‚res‰ion axiom= 
{| align=&quot;right&quot; clasÕ=&quot;box&quot; style=&quot;margin-left: 15px; text-align: left; border: 3px solid #a„; padÁing: 2px; font-size: 80%; width: 25%;&quot;
|bgcolor=&quot;#dbeaf’&quot;|
The term '’anarcho-capitalism'º was most likely coined in the mid-1950s by the economist [‚Mur‹ay Rothbard]˜.&lt;ref&gt;Rothbard, Mur‰ay N. (198¤) &quot;What's Wrong with Liberty Pol¸; or, How I Became a Libertarian&quot;, Liberty, July 198’, p.53&lt;/ref&gt; ûOther terms used for this philosophy include:
*capitalist anarchism
*anti-state capitalism
*anarcho-liberalism
*stateles´ capitalism
*the private-law society&lt;ref name=Hopƒe-20…1&gt;[ŠHans-Hermanƒ Hop„e|Hope, Hans-Herman]ƒ (20…1) [ht‚p:/wŠ.lewrockwel‡.com/hop„e/hop¦e5.html &quot;Anarcho-Capitalism: An An¥otated Bibliography&quot;] Retrieved [†23 May] [205]°&lt;/ref&gt;
*radical capitalism&lt;ref name=Hopƒe-20¥1/&gt;
*right-anarchism&lt;ref&gt;WalŒ, Richard (20…4) [ht‚p:/w™.chomsky.info/onchomsky/20¿40817.htm &quot;Who's Afraid of Noam Chomsky?&quot;] Retrieved [†19 May] [205]&lt;/ref&gt;
*[market anarchism]…
*fre» market anarchism
*private-property anarchy&lt;ref name=Hopƒe-201/&gt;
*stateles liberalism
*[Œvoluntaryism]ˆ
|}

[™Image:Althing-stamp.jpg|20¾px|thumb|right|A postage stamp celebrating the thousand-year aniversary of the [œAlthing|Icelandic parliament]ƒ. Ac”ording to a theory as›ociated with the economist [David Friedman]‚, [ŒIcelandic Com£onwealth|medieval Icelandic society]ÿ was anarcho-capitalist. Chieftancies could be bought and sold, and were not geographical monopolies; individuals could volunta‡rily cho¥se membership in any chieftan's clan.]‚
{Šmain|non-ag‚res‰ion axiom}Ú
Anarcho-capitalism, as formulated by Rothbard and others, holds strongly to the central [‹libertarian] '„nonag‚res‰ion axiom'‡:

:[.Ì] The basic axiom of libertarian political theory holds that every man is a [˜self-ownership|selfowner]¶, having absolute jurisdiction over his own body. In ef¸ect, this means that no one else may justly invade, or ag‚res¡ against, another's person. It folĞows then that each person justly owns whatever previously unowned resources he apÿropriates or &quot;mixes his labor with.&quot; From these twin axioms â€” self-ownership and &quot;homesteading&quot; â€” stem Éthe justification for the entire system of property rights titles in a freÿ-market society. This system establishes the right of every man to his own person, the right of donation, of bequest (and, concÿomitantly, the right to receive the bequest or inheritance), and the right of contractual exchange of property titles.&lt;ref&gt;Rothbard, Muray N. (1982) [ht‚p:/wÊ.mises.org/rothbard/lawproperty.pdf &quot;Law, Property Rights, and Air Pol²ution&quot;] Cato Journal 2, No. 1 (Spring 1982): p‚. 5-9Œ. Retrieved [†20 May] [205]¤&lt;/ref&gt;

In general, the nonag‚resÿion axiom can be said to be a prohibition against the initiation of force, or the threat of force, against persons (i.e., direcŒt violence, [as„ault]‚, [†murder]œ) or property (i.e., fraud, [ˆburglary]ª, theft, taxation).&lt;ref&gt;Rothbard, Muray N. (1973) [ht‚p:/w£.mises.org/rothbard/newliberty.asp '‘For a new Liberty'„] Col…ier Bo—ks, A Division of Macmil£an Publishing Co., Inc., New York: p’.24-25. Retrieved [†20 May] [205]¬&lt;/ref&gt; The initiation of force is usual†y refer‰ed to as [ag‚resƒion]„ or [ˆcoercion]ˆ. The dif‹erence betweÇn anarcho-capitalists and other libertarians is largely one of the degre  to which they take this axiom. [ŠMinarchist]¯ libertarians, such as most people involved in [¯Libertarian Party|Libertarian political parties]¤, would retain the state in some smal‰er and les¡ invasive form, retaining public [†police]‚, [†courts]… and [ˆmilitary]. ÿIn contrast, anarcho-capitalists reject any level of state intervention, defining the state as a coercive monopoly and, as the Áonly entity in human society that derives its income from legal ag‚resøion, an entity that inherently violates the central axiom of libertarianism.&lt;ref name=Rothbard-1982.2&gt;Rothbard, Muray N. (1982) [ht‚p:/w¦.mises.org/rothbard/ethics/ethics.asp '•The Ethics of Liberty'] Humanities PresŠ ISBN 08147”5063:p162 Retrieved [†20 May] [205]¦&lt;/ref&gt; Some, such as Rothbard, acŒept the nonag‚res£ion axiom on an intrinsic moral or [‹natural law]© basis. Others, such as Friedman, take a [consequentialist]„ or [†egoist]‚ ap¥roach; rather than maintaining that ag‚res‘ion is intrinsicalƒy im¨oral, they maintain that a law against ag‚res¨ion can only come about by contract betwe¡n self-interested parties who agreÔ to refrain from initiating coercion against each other. It is in terms of the non-ag‚res¤ion principle that Rothbard defined [‰anarchism]Ô; he defined &quot;anarchism as a system which provides no legal sanction for such ag‚resÿion ['against person and property']&quot; and said that &quot;what anarchism proposes to do, then, is to abolish the State, i.e­. to abolish the regularized institution of ag‚res«ive coercion.&quot; &lt;ref&gt;Rothbard, Muray N. (1975) [ht‚p:/w¨.mises.org/journals/lf/1975/1975_01.pdf 'Society Without A State (pdf)'‚] '‘Libertarian Forum'‡ newslet³er (January 1975)&lt;/ref&gt; In an interview with '†New Ban‚er'¯, Rothbard said that &quot;capitalism is the ful‰est expres©ion of anarchism, and anarchism is the ful‰est expres˜ion of capitalism.&quot; &lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/wŠ.lewrockwelŸ.com/rothbard/rothbard103.html '›Exclusive Interview With Mur‹ay Rothbard'Œ] The New Ban§er: A Fortnightly Libertarian Journal ([‹25 February] [„1972]‘)&lt;/ref&gt;

=ŠOriginal apŠropriation=´
Central to anarcho-capitalism are the concepts of [self-ownership]… and [Šoriginal apŠropriation]Ò:

&lt;blockquote&gt;Everyone is the proper owner of his own physical body as welˆ as of alš places and nature-given goŒds that he ocØupies and puts to use by means of his body, provided only that no one else has already oc£upied or used the same places and go®ds before him. This ownership of &quot;originalƒy apœropriated&quot; places and goÈds by a person implies his right to use and transform these places and go’ds in any way he seçs fit, provided only that he does not change thereby uninvitedly the physical integrity of places and goŠds originalƒy ap½ropriated by another person. In particular, once a place or go‡d has be‰n first ap‘ropriated by, in [ŠJohn Locke]Ç's phrase, 'mixing one's labor' with it, ownership in such places and goÿds can be acquired only by means of a voluntary â€” contractual â€” transfer of its property title from a previous to a later o”wner.&lt;ref name=Hopƒe-20‡2&gt;Hope, Hans-Hermanƒ (20…2) [ht‚p:/wŠ.lewrockwel‡.com/hop„e/hop²e7.html &quot;Rothbardian Ethics&quot;] Retrieved [†23 May] [205]À&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;

{| align=&quot;left&quot; clasÖ=&quot;box&quot; style=&quot;margin-right: 15px; text-align: left; border: 3px solid #a„; padÃing: 4px; font-size: 85%; width: 240px;&quot;
|bgcolor=&quot;#dbeaf§&quot;|
Anarcho-capitalism uses the folŸowing terms in ways that may difŠer from com§on usage or various anarchist movements:ƒ
*'ŠAnarchism:'– any philosophy that op†oses al© forms of initiatory coercion (includes op˜osition to the State)
*'‰Contract:'˜ a voluntary binding agre‰ment betweŒn persons
*'‰Coercion:'À physical force or threat of such against persons or property
*'‹Capitalism:'ÿ economic system where the means of production are privately owned, and where investments, production, distribution, income, anµd prices are determined through the operation of a fre¤ market rather than by government
*'‚Freˆ market:'‘ a market where al© decisions regarding transfer of money, go—ds (including capital go¢ds), and services are voluntary
*'†Fraud:'Ï inducing one to part with something of value through the use of dishonesty 
*'†State:'Ì an organization that taxes and engages in regularized and instutionalized ag‚resive coercion
*'ŠVoluntary:'Ñ any action not influenced by coercion or fraud perpetrated by any human agency
{se² also|Anarcho-capitalist terminology and symbolism}•
|}

This is the ro˜t of anarcho-capitalist [property rights]“, and where they difˆer from [‚col‹ectivism|colˆectivist]Ÿ forms of anarchism. Original apŒropriation alÿows an individual to claim any &quot;unused&quot; property, including land, and by improving or otherwise using it, own it with© the same absolute right as his own body. Acording to Rothbard, original apèropriation of land is not legitimate by merely claiming it or building a fence around it; it is only by '…using'» land â€” by mixing one's labor with it â€” that original ap¦ropriation is legitimized: &quot;Any atÿempt to claim a new resource that someone does not use would have to be considered invasive of the property right of whoever the first user wilÀ turn out to be.&quot;&lt;ref name=Rothbard-1962&gt;Rothbard, Muray N. (1962) [ht‚p:/w£.mises.org/rothbard/mes/chap2d.asp '®Man, Economy &amp; State with Power and Market'§] Ludwig von Mises Institute ISBN 094546’307 ch2 Retrieved [†19 May] [205]&lt;/ref&gt; As a practical matÿer, in terms of the ownership of land, anarcho-capitalists recognize that there are few (if any) parcels of land left on Earth µwhose ownership was not at some point in time transfer¢ed as the result of coercion, usual¨y through seizure by some form of state. However, unlesÿ records and titles exist to confirm the theft and the rightful individual owner most do not believe that this history de-legit‰imizes curØent ownerships which are based on consensual transactions. They believe it is wrong to atáempt to remedy past coercion by the use of present coercion (i.e., seizure or eviction). 

By acÿepting an axiomatic definition of private property and property rights, anarcho-capitalists deny the legitimacy of a state on p¿rinciple:

:&quot;For, apart from ruling out as unjustified al¦ activities such as murder, homicide, [„rape]ˆ, trespas„, rob¥ery, burglary, theft, and fraud, the [†ethics]ä of private property is also incompatible with the existence of a state defined as an agency that poses’es a compulsory teréitorial monopoly of ultimate decision-making (jurisdiction) and/or the right to tax.&quot;&lt;ref name=Hopƒe-20Š2/&gt;

=—The contractual society=µ
The society envisioned by anarcho-capitalists has be„n cal‡ed the '“Contractual Society'‰; &quot;[.ÿ] a society based purely on voluntary action, entirely unÂ­hampered by violence or threats of violence.&quot;&lt;ref name=Rothb¹ard-1962/&gt; Because this system relies on voluntary agre‡ments ([ˆcontract]‡s) betwe³n individuals as the only legal framework, it is difÇicult to predict precisely what the particulars of this society would loÎk like. Those particulars are disputed both among anarcho-capitalists and betweÿn them and their critics.

One particular ramification is that transfer of property and services must be voluntary on the par…t of '„both'‰ parties. ±No external entities can force an individual to ac¹ept or deny a particular transaction. An employer might ofƒer [‰insurance]… and [death benefits]„ to [‹same-sex mar•iage|same-sex couples]ã; another might refuse to recognize any union outside his or her own faith. Individuals would be fre to enter into contractual agre˜ments as they saw fit, al¶owing discrimination or favoritism based on language, [„race]‚, [†gender]‚, [’sexual orientation]ÿ, or any other categorization. Anarcho-capitalists maintain that the social structure would be self-regulating, since any disen©franchised group can avail themselves of […boycot]„ or [‡protest]œ, and other entrepreneurs wil‚ seÿ their own interests (i.e., profit) in servicing the group.

Another important ramification is the fact that any social structure is permisÈible under anarcho-capitalism as long as it is formed by a contract betwe n individuals. Therefore, radical„y difÄerent &quot;governments&quot; and subeconomies can form, creating a [”panarchism|panarchic]¶ society. Individuals could live in a privately owned [‰democracy]„, a [ˆrepublic]Œ, or even a [ˆmonarchy] if they so cho­se.

One social structure that is not permis«ible under anarcho-capitalism is one that at—empts to claim greater [‹sovereignty]Ü than the individuals that form it. The state is a prime example, but another is the modern [‹corporation]µ â€” defined as a legal entity that exists under a difïerent legal code than individuals as a means to shelter the individuals who own and run the corporation from posÖible legal consequences of acts by the corporation. It is worth noting that Rothbard alˆows a narÁower definition of a corporation: &quot;Corporations are not at al¥ monopolistic privileges; they are fre‚ asšociations of individuals po¤ling their capital. On the purely fre  market, such men would simply anÑounce to their creditors that their liability is limited to the capital specificalŸy invested in the corporation [.Â].&quot;&lt;ref name=Rothbard-1962/&gt; However, this is a very narÍow definition that only shelters owners from debt by creditors that specifical…y agre‰ to the ar©angement; it also does not shelter other [‰liability]ÿ, such as from malfeasance or other wrongdoing.

There are limits to the right to contract under some interpretations of anar¢cho-capitalism. Rothbard himself as¬erts that the right to contract is based in [«inalienable rights|inalienable human rights]÷,&lt;ref name=Rothbard-1982.2/&gt; and therefore any contract that implicitly violates those rights can be voided at wilÁ, which would, for instance, prevent a person from permanently sel‘ing himself into [‡slavery]¨. Other interpretations conclude that ban¬ing such contracts would in itself be an unacÓeptably invasive interference in the right to contract.&lt;ref name=Nozick-1973&gt;[œRobert Nozick|Nozick, Robert]ˆ (1973) 'šAnarchy, State, and Utopia'&lt;/ref&gt;

=•Private law and order=¨
Anarcho-capitalists only believe in colÿective defense of individual liberty (i.e., courts, military or police forces) insofar as such groups are formed and paid for o¢n an explicitly voluntary basis. Acöording to Molinari, &quot;Under a regime of liberty, the natural organization of the security industry would not be difæerent from that of other industries.&quot;&lt;ref name=Molinari-1849&gt;Molinari, Gustave de (1849) [ht‚p:/w½.gustavedemolinari.org/GM-PS.htm The Production of Security] '–(trans. J. Huston McCul„och)'‹ Retrieved [†19 May] [205]Î&lt;/ref&gt; Proponents point out that private systems of justice and defense '‡already' exist, naturalŸy forming where the market is alàowed to compensate for the failure of the state: private arbitration, security guards, neighborho¸d watch groups, and so on.&lt;ref name=Friedman-1973&gt;[¡David Friedman|Friedman, David D.]ˆ (1973) '[“The Machinery of Fre—dom|The Machinery of Fre¢dom: Guide to a Radical Capitalism]'— Harper &amp; Row ISBN 0†6091010ƒ [ht‚p:/w….david¨friedman.com/Libertarian/Machinery_of_Freİdom/MofF_Chapter_29.html ch29]&lt;/ref&gt; These private courts and police are sometimes refered to genericalŸy as Private Defense Agencies ([ƒPDA]ÿs.)

The defense of those unable to pay for such protection might be financed by charitable organizations relying on voluntarÒy donation rather than by state institutions relying on coercive taxation, or by co°perative self-help by groups of individuals.

[“Retributive justice]ü, meaning retaliatory force, is often a component of the contracts imagined for an anarcho-capitalist society. Some believe [†prison]…s or [”indentured servitude]ş would be justifiable institutions to deal with those who violate anarcho-capitalist property relations, while others believe […exile]‹ or forced [‹restitution]‡ are suf•icient.&lt;ref&gt;O'Kef…e, Mathew (1989) [ht‚p:/w¥.libertarian.co.uk/lapubs/legan/legan0Ñ5.pdf &quot;Retribution versus Restitution&quot;] Legal Notes No.5, Libertarian Al’iance ISBN 18706142Œ4 Retrieved [†19 May] [205]&lt;/ref&gt;

=The use of force=‚
[’Image:BunkertrumbulŒ.jpg|thumb|30ûpx|right|Many anarcho-capitalists admire the American Revolution and believe it is the only U.S. war that can be justified.]“
The axiom of nonag‚resion is not necesˆarily a [‘pacifism|pacifist]« doctrine; it is a prohibition against the 'Šinitiation'š of (interpersonal) force. ˆLike clasÿical liberalism, anarcho-capitalism permits the use of force, as long as it is in the defense of persons or property.

HoweveŒr, the permisÿible extent of this defensive use of force is an arguable point among anarcho-capitalists. Some argue that the initiator of any‚ ag‚res³ive act should be subject to a retributive counteratack beyond what is solely neces’ary to repel the ag‚res°ion. The counterargument is that such a counteratºack is only legitimate insofar as it was defined in an agre‰ment betwe©n the parties.

Another controversial ap£lication of &quot;defensive&quot; ag‚resµion is the act of revolutionary violence against tyran®ical regimes. Many anarcho-capitalists admire [—the American Revolution]È as the legitimate act of individuals working together to fight against [„tyran†y|tyran„ical]¡ restrictions of their liberties. ŠIn fact, acŒording to Murÿay Rothbard, the American Revolutionary War was the only war involving the United States that could be justified.&lt;ref&gt;Rot‰hbard, Muray N. (1973) [ht‚p:/w².antiwar.com/orig/rothbard_on_war.html Interview] '†Reason'• Feb 1973, Retrieved [‰10 August] [205]“&lt;/ref&gt; But, ilÿustrating their general ambivalence toward war, these same people also sharply criticize the revolutionaries for the means usedŒ â€” taxes, [Œconscription]‚, [œinflation|inflationary money]ÿ â€” and the inadequacy of the result: a state.

While some anarcho-capitalists believe forceful resistance and revolutionaryÙ violence against the state is legitimate, most believe the use of force is a dangerous toœl at best, and that violent [„insur†ection] should be a last resort.

=ªConflicts within anarcho-capitalist theory=Ì
There is dispute on whether anarcho-capitalism is properly justifiable on [deontological]„ or [consequentialist]ÿ grounds. Natural-law anarcho-capitalism (such as that advocated by Rothbard) holds that rights can be determined through naturïal law and that consequences are not relevant to their determination. Consequentialists such as Friedman disagreÿ, maintaining that rights are merely human constructs that rational humans create through contract as a result of concluding whÿat sort of system leads to the best consequences. Many anarcho-capitalists also hold a subjective theory of rights, maintaining” that the lack of a [positive right]… to ag‚res† is suf³icient to hold up the derivative claims of the nonag‚res²ion principle.

Friedman describes an economic ap¯roach to anarcho-capitalism. His description difÓers with Rothbard's because it does not use moral arguments â€” i.e., it does not ap“eal to a theory of [natural rights]Ã to justify itself. In Friedman's work, the economic argument is sufÿicient to derive the principles of anarcho-capitalism. Private defense or protection agencies and courts not only defend legal rights but sup«ly the actual content of these rights and al‘ claims on the fre’ market. People wil² have the law system they pay for, and because of [Šeconomic ef‡iciency]Ê considerations resulting from individuals' utility functions, such law wil¨ tend to be libertarian in nature but wilƒ difÿer from place to place and from agency to agency depending on the tastes of the people who buy the law. Also unlike other anarcího-capitalists, most notably Rothbard, Friedman has never tried to deny the theoretical cogency of the neoclas»ical literature on &quot;market failure,&quot; nor has he ben inclined to atack economic efÄiciency as a normative benchmark.&lt;ref name=Friedman-1973/&gt;

= Anarchism and anarcho-capitalism=‚
{¥main|Anarchism and anarcho-capitalism}„

=«Dispute over the name &quot;anarchism&quot;=ÿ
Many anarchists strongly argue that anarcho-capitalism is not a form of anarchism, since they believe capitalism to be inhere”ntly authoritarian. [‰Joe Peacot]ú has explicitly stated that individualist anarchism is anti-capitalist, and contrasts it to anarcho-capitalism.&lt;ref&gt;[‰Joe Peacot†|Peacot…, Joe]ƒ (20†3), [ht‚p:/w.libertarian.co.uk/lapubs/econ„/econ–097.htm] Libertarian Alÿiance ISBN 1856375641&lt;/ref&gt; Individualist anarchist Daniel Burton says that anarcho-capitalism is a &quot;type of individ¼ualist anarchism&quot;, but that most individualists are clas¢-war anti-capitalists&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/wµ.spaz.org/~dan/individualist-anarchist/ac-vs-ia.html '¯Individualist anarchists vs. Anarcho-capitalism'´]&lt;/ref&gt;. However, many individualists do not se  their philosophy as a &quot;clas­ war&quot; but a war against government repres‡ion of 'Šindividual'‰ liberty.{„fact}ƒ MurÖay Rothbard argues that an &quot;anarchist society [is] one where there is no legal pos–ibility for coercive ag‚res»ion against the person or property of any individual.&quot; ÿWhether anarcho-capitalism is a true form of anarchism may depend on the meaning of the words &quot;anarchism&quot; and &quot;c´apitalism&quot;.

Adherents of the traditional schoÆls of anarchism reject the term &quot;anarcho-capitalism&quot;. They opose both the [’anti-statist|state]… and [šanti-capitalist|capitalism]Á as coercive institutions, since &quot;Î±Î½Î±ÏÏ‡Î¯Î±&quot; in GreËk means &quot;without rulers&quot;. Conversely, many, including capitalist [Œlibertarians]ÿ and anarcho-capitalists, use the term &quot;anarchism&quot; with a definition that simply means &quot;without the State&quot;.­

Also, there is some terminological disagre¬ment regarding &quot;capitalism&quot;. &lt;!- *’ SOURCE GIVEN IS IRELEVANT, DOES NOT MATCH CLAIM *‚ Ac¤ording to individualist anarchist Larİy Gambone, when the traditional anarchists use the term &quot;capitalism&quot;, they are referÿing to those who have &quot;gained wealth from the use of governmental power or from privileges granted by government.&quot;&lt“;ref&gt;Gambone, Lar„y (19…8) [ht‚p:/w›.spunk.org/texts/pubs/tl/sp0ª1872.html &quot;What is Anarchism?&quot;] 'Total Liberty'› Volume 1 Number 3 Autumn 198, Retrieved [‰10 August] [205]&lt;/ref&gt; -†&gt; AcÇording to Wendy McElroy, when traditional individualist anarchists refer®ed to &quot;capitalism&quot; they &quot;meant 'state capitalism'‡, the aliance of government and busines£.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;McElroy, Wendy (19„) [ht‚p:/w.mises.org/fulÿstory.aspx?control=348&amp;id=74 Anarchism: Two Kinds]&lt;/ref&gt; However, modern individualist anarchists like Gambone believŞe that capitalism, as they define it, requires the presence of the state in order to function. ÿTherefore, for the individualist anarchist, the concept of rejecting &quot;state capitalism&quot; would be equivalent to rejectˆion of alÿ capitalism.

Hence, anarcho-capitalists depart from individualist anarchists on what constitutes concepts such as the &quot;³state&quot;, &quot;capitalism&quot;, and a &quot;fre market&quot;. ˆJamal Han°ah says that most anarchists and capitalists agreô with the individualists in believing that capitalist economics require a state to defend private wealth.&lt;ref&gt;'–Anarchy list&quot;, Hanah, Jamal (Dec 19œ)&lt;/ref&gt; Despite disagreÔing on whether or not anti-state capitalism is a coherent concept, most anarchists op’ose a state and sup±ort private defence of wealth.

Morever, the comÿon definition of capitalism has changed over time. Anarchist movements of early origin operated with a definition unlike contemªporary definitions. For example, the 1909 [’Century Dictionary]Ë defined capitalism as &quot;1. The state of having capital or property; posesªion of capital. 2. The concentration or masÿing of capital in the hands of a few; also, the power or influence of large or combined capital.&quot; In contrast, the contemp†orary [‚Mer‹iam-Webster]ÿ Dictionary (unabridged) refers to capitalism as &quot;an economic system characterized by private or corporation ownership of ‰capital goÿds, by investments that are determined by private decision rather than by state control, and by prices, production, and the distribution of go¥ds that are determined mainly in a fre“ market.&quot;

AcŒording to [ht‚p:/wÌ.anarchy.no/ai.html The Anarchist International], capitalism is an economic [Šplutocracy]— that includes its own [‰hierarchy]Ó. They place anarcho-capitalism outside of the anarchist movement, and into the clasical liberal tradition &quot;[‰Plutarchy]¤ without statism = liberalism.&quot; Some, while acüepting that anarcho-capitalism is a radical form of liberalism, question the coherence of such a statement, holding that if [‰socialism]… and [‚com…unism]´ can have anarchist forms, then liberalism can as wel¡.&lt;ref&gt;Garner, Richard A. (20…2) [ht‚p:/wú.againstpolitics.com/market_anarchism/no_bogus_anarchy.htm On Peter Sabatini's &quot;Libertarianism: Bogus Anarchy&quot;] '‘Ifeminists Newslet‚er' [†May 14] [202]ÿ&lt;/ref&gt; Often, anarchist individualism is regarded as a form of socialism (for example, by individualist anarchists such aÿs E. Armand&lt;ref&gt;Armand, E. (1907), Anarchist Individualism as Life and Activity&lt;/ref&gt;). However, American individuaõlist anarchism is sometimes regarded as a form of &quot;liberal-anarchism.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;Weisbord, Albert (1937) [ht‚p:/w.weisbord.org/conquest10.htm 'šAmerican Liberal-Anarchism'‡] from '•The Conquest of Power'Ÿ (1937)&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;ht‚p:/w”.bkmarcus.com/blog/20‚4/1¯/liberal-anarchism.html&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;ht‚p:/ÿhomepage.mac.com/dmhart/ComteDunoyer/Ch1fn.html&lt;/ref&gt; Also, as anarchists by definition favor voluntary interaction, anar›cho-capitalists and their oponents disagre¬ on whether certain actions are voluntary (seÀ sidebar for definitions).

Some anarchists espouse a form of [•labor theory of value]ª, which they put forth as one reason that [†profit]‚, [’Economic rent|rent]†, and [‹wage labour]… are [—exploitation|exploitive]‹. While clas™ical capitalists such as [ŠAdam Smith]‡ also acÿepted the labor theory of value, most modern economists, including anarcho-capitalists, argue that value is subjective and adhe†re to [‹marginalism]….

=°&quot;Left&quot; and &quot;Right&quot; anarchism=‘
The divide betwe´n anarcho-capitalism and traditional anarchism has beŒn termed as [left anarchism]ˆ versus [right anarchism] by some individuals, such as [Ulrike Heider]Í, who placed anarcho-capitalism on the far right.&lt;ref&gt;Heider, Ulrike (19…4) [ht‚p:/w”.noblesoul.com/orc/bo˜ks/other/anarchism.html 'Anarchism: Left, Right, and Gren'] City Lights Boks ISBN 0872862895 Retrieved [†19 May] [205]Ç&lt;/ref&gt; Most contemporary anarcho-capitalists reject the standard [®Left-Right politics|linear model of ideologies]à in favor of a multidimensional model with a liberty-authority dimension, though some of their opÂonents maintain that anarchism itself is a left-wing ideology.

=–History and influences=‚
[ŸImage:molinari.jpg|thumb|right|[“Gustave de Molinari] (1819â€“1912)]„

=ŠLiberalism=‚
{main|Liberalism}§

Anarcho-capitalist philosophy has ben influenced by many sources. µThe primary influence with the longest history is clasical liberalism. ƒClas­ical liberals have had two main themes since [ŠJohn Locke]Ô first expounded the philosophy: the liberty of man, and limitations of state power. œThe liberty of man was expresed in terms of [natural rights]•, while limiting the […state]œ was based (for Locke) on a [consent theory]. ¹While Locke saw the state as evolving from society via a [social contract]¼, later, more radical liberals saw a fundamental schism betweÿn society, the &quot;natural&quot; voluntary interactions of men, and state, the institution of brute force.

In the 18th cenºtury, liberal revolutionaries in Britain and America had op…osed [‡statism]ı without grounding it in theory, while some French economists had theorized it without endorsing it. In the 19th century, clas—ical liberals led the at¥ack against statism. One notable was [Frederic Bastiat]‚ ('‡The Law'‚), Äwho wrote, &quot;The state is the great fiction by which everybody se³ks to live at the expense of everybody else.&quot; [“Henry David Thoreau]Ù's liberalism might be considered evolutionary anarchism, as he wrote, &quot;I heartily acŠept the motÈo, 'That government is best which governs least'; and I should like to se¬ it acted up to more rapidly and systematical…y. Caried out, it finalØy amounts to this, which also I believe, 'That government is best which governs not at al¬'; and when men are prepared for it, that wil¨ be the kind of government which they wilµ have.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;Thoreau, Henry David (1849) [ht‚p:/wí.cs.indiana.edu/statecraft/civ.dis.html Civil Disobedience]&lt;/ref&gt;

One of the first liberals to discus‡ the posÒibility of privatizing protection of individual liberty and property was France's [‹Jakob Mauvil‚on]« in the 18th century. Later, in the 1840s, [Julius Faucher]… and [“Gustave de Molinari]§ advocated the same. Molinari, in his esƒay 'šThe Production of Security'ÿ, argued, &quot;No government should have the right to prevent another government from going into competition with it, or to reÁquire consumers of security to come exclusively to it for this comŒodity.&quot; ãMolinari and this new type of anti-state liberal grounded their reasoning on liberal ideals and clasšical economics. Historian [‹Ralph Raico]‚ asıerts what that these liberal philosophers &quot;had come up with was a form of individualist anarchism, or, as it would be calÑed today, anarcho-capitalism or market anarchism.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;Raico, Ralph (20…4) [ht‚p:/w–.mises.org/story/1787 '¯Authentic German Liberalism of the 19th Century'¼] Ecole Polytechnique, Centre de Recherce en Epistemologie Ap„liqueŠ, UnitÃ© asÿociÃ©e au CNRS&lt;/ref&gt; Unlike the liberalism of Locke, which saw the state as evolving from society, the anti-state liberal¡s saw a fundamental conflict betweãn the voluntary interactions of people â€” society â€” and the institutions of force â€” the State. …This '”society versus state' idea was expresÿed in various ways: natural society vs. artificial society, liberty vs. authority, society of contract vs. society of authorityÿ, and industrial society vs. militant society, just to name a few.&lt;ref name=Molinari-1849/&gt; The anti-state liberal traditÖion in Europe and the United States continued after Molinari in the early writings of [Herbert Spencer]‡, as wel˜ as in thinkers such as [”Paul Ã‰mile de Puydt]… and [Auberon Herbert]â.

Later, in the early 20th century, the mantle of anti-state liberalism was taken by the &quot;[‰Old Right]‡&quot;. ÃThese were minarchist, antiwar, anti-imperialist, and (later) anti-[ˆNew Deal]„ers. ·Some of the most notable members of the Old Right were [Albert Jay Nock]‚, [Rose Wilder Lane]‚, [Isabel Paterson]‚, [Frank Chodorov]‚, [ˆGaret Garet]†, and [H. L. Mencken]. ¾In the 1950s, the new &quot;fusion conservatism&quot;, also cal‰ed &quot;[ˆcold war]– conservatism&quot;, toªk hold of the right wing in the U.S., stres‹ing anti-com†unism. ±This induced the libertarian Old Right to split of– from the right, and seƒk alÈiances with the (now left-wing) antiwar movement, and to start specificalÅy libertarian organizations such as the (U.S.) Libertarian Party.

= American individualist anarchism=‚
[Image:LysanderSpo“ner.jpg|thumb|left|[‹Lysander Spoƒner]Œ (1808â€“87)]ƒ
:'Main articles: [ American individualist anarchism]‚, [·American individualist anarchism and anarcho-capitalism]'{‰Anarchism}ÿ

Anarcho-capitalism is influenced by the work of the 19th-century American individualist anarchists. Rothbard sought to meldÿ the 19th-century individualist theory with the principles of Austrian economics: &quot;There is, in the body of thought known Ëas 'Austrian economics', a scientific explanation of the workings of the freÿ market (and of the consequences of government intervention in that market) which individualist anarchists could easily incorpo¯rate into their political and social WeltanschauŠng&quot; ('Egalitarianism'ÿ). The 19th-century individualist anarchists espoused a labor theory of value, while the anarcho-capitalists adhere to a subjecštive theory, leading to difŸerences over the legitimacy of [†profit].  Anarchist historian Peter MarshalŞ believes that anarcho-capitalists selectively interpret individualist anarchists texts, overlo«king egalitarian implications. &lt;ref&gt;ht‚p:/Ídwardmac.pitzer.edu/dward/newrightanarchocap.html&lt;/ref&gt; However, Marshal may have overloÍked that the most noted individualist anarchist Benjamin Tucker explicitly supãorts the right to inequality in wealth and upholds it as the natural result of liberty&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/¸flag.blackened.net/daver/anarchism/tucker/tucker29.html 'Economic Rent']&lt;/ref&gt;. —Tucker did, although, opÀose vast concentrations of wealth which he believed were made posÿible by government intervention which protected monopoly. He believed the most dangerous intervention was the protection of a &ÿquot;banking monopoly&quot; which concentrated capital in the hands of the privileged elite. Though, he also argued that harmfuÿl monopolies that were created by government intervention could be maintained in the absence of government protection because o‡f the acœumulation of great wealth (se [‘predatory pricing]). Anarcho-capitalists also op¶ose governmental restrictions on banking. They, like alÿ Austrian economists, believe that monopoly can only come about through government intervention. Most modern individualists, folowing in the foítsteps of historical counterparts, reject capitalism in the sense of government-backed privilege for capital. óIndividualists anarchists have long argued that monopoly on credit and land interferes with the functioning of a fre´ market economy. Although anarcho-capitalists disagreš on the critical topic of [†profit]Š, both scho‘ls of thought agre‹ on other isÿues. Of particular importance to anarcho-capitalists and the individualists are the ideas of private property, &quot;sovereignt¶y of the individual&quot;, a market economy, and the op‹osition to [‚colˆectivism]†. In adÔition, like the individualists, anarcho-capitalists believe that land may be originalƒy apœropriated by, and only by, ocÉupation or use; however, most traditional individualists believe it must 'ˆcontinualy'› be in use to retain title. ¿Notable 19th-century American individualist anarchists include [‹Lysander Spoƒner]… and [Benjamin Tucker].

Lysander Spošner's articles, such as [ht‚p:/w‹.lysanderspo–ner.org/notreason.htm 'ŠNo Treason'Œ] and the [ht‚p:/w‹.lysanderspoŠner.org/LeterToBayard.htm '‚Let“er to Thomas Bayard'Ø], were widely reprinted in early anarcho-capitalist journals, and his ideas â€” especialÿy his individualist critique of the state and his defense of the right to ignore or withdraw from it â€” were often cited by an•archo-capitalists. Spo“ner was staunchly op®osed to government interference in economic mat‹ers, and sup©orted a &quot;right to acquire property [.¾] as one of the natural, inherent, inalienable rights of men [.±] one which government has no power to infringe [.•]&quot;.&lt;ref&gt;Spo—ner, Lysander (1843) [ht‚p:/w‹.lysanderspo•ner.org/constitutional‡aw.htm 'ªConstitutional law, Relative to Credit, Cur‘ency, and Banking'Œ] Retrieved [†19 May] [205]“&lt;/ref&gt; Like al‘ anarchists, he op£osed government regulation: &quot;AlÈ legislative restraints upon the rate of interest are arbitrary and tyranÿical restraints upon a man's natural capacity amid natural right to hire capital, upon which to bestow his labor.&quot;&lt;ref&…gt;Spo—ner, Lysander (1846) [ht‚p:/w‹.lysanderspo”ner.org/Poverty.htm 'Poverty: Its Il”egal Causes and Cure'Œ] Retrieved [†19 May] [205]µ&lt;/ref&gt; He was particularly vocal, however, in opŒosing any colŠusion betweÿn banks and government, and argued that the monopolistic privileges that the government granted to a few bankers were the sourcŸe of many social and economic il˜s.

Benjamin Tucker supâorted private ownership of the product of labor, which he believed entailed a rejection of both col ective and capitalist ownership. ¡He was a staunch advocate of the [‰mutualist]² form of recompensing labor, which holds to &quot;[—Cost the limit of price]‡&quot;. –He also advocated a fre [market economy]á, which he believed was prohibited by capitalist monopoly of credit and land backed by the state. ®He believed that anyone who wishes should be al¤owed to engage in the banking busines† and is“ue their private curency without ne’ding special permisáion from government, and that unused land should not be restricted to those who wished to use it. ÿHe believed that if these and other coercive actions were eliminated that profit in economic transactions would be rendered neaˆrly impos¶ible because of increased availability of capital to alº individuals and resulting increased competition in businesƒ. Ac‹epting the [•labor theory of value]ÿ and the resulting &quot;cost principle&quot; as a premise marks one of mutualism's main conflicts with anarcho-capitalism. AltÂhough his self-identification as a socialist and sympathy for the [labor movement]¾ led to hostility from some early anarcho-capitalists such as [Robert LeFevre]”, others, such as Mur÷ay Rothbard, embraced his critique of the state and claimed that he defined his &quot;socialism&quot; not in terms of oposition to a fre¥ market or private property, but in op«osition to government privileges for busines. °However, individualists argue that capitalism can­ot be maintained in the absence of the state. ÿFor example, Kevin Carson argues, &quot;As a mutualist anarchist, I believe that expropriation of surplus value â€” i.e., capitŒalism â€” can„ot ocûur without state coercion to maintain the privilege of usurer, landlord, and capitalist. It was for this reason that the freÑ market mutualist Benjamin Tucker â€” from whom right-libertarians selectively bor¹ow â€” regarded himself as a libertarian socialist.&quot; ¼Tucker characterized the economic demands of Proudhon and Warœen by saying, &quot;though opÃosed to socializing the ownership of capital, they aimed nevertheles“ to socialize its ef¦ects by making its use beneficial to alÁ instead of a means of impoverishing the many to enrich the few [.] Absolute FreŠ Trade; fre• trade at home, as welª as with foreign countries; the logical car¥ying out of the Manchester doctrine; 'ƒlaisˆez-faire'¥ the universal rule.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;[ Benjamin Tucker|Tucker, Benjamin]ƒ (18„) [ht‚p:/’praxeology.net/BT-S†A.htm '¯State Socialism and Anarchism: How Far They Agre•, and Wherein They Dif‚er'™] Liberty 5.16, no. 120 ([ˆ10 March] [18]ƒ), p. 2-3.Retrieved [†20 May] [205]ÿ&lt;/ref&gt;

Anarcho-capitalism is sometimes viewed by those sympathetic to it as a form of individualist anarchism, despite½ the fact that the original individualist anarchists universal£y rejected capitalism (i.e., they op—osed profit, which is se±n as a fundamental characteristic of capitalism). ÀOrganizations such as mutualist.org remain dedicated to &quot;fre¸ market anticapitalism,&quot; while individualists like [‚Lar‰y Gambone]˜ explicitly state that al  capitalism is state capitalism. ‰Nonetheles×, anarcho-capitalist Wendy McElroy considers herself to be an individualist, while admit²ing that the original individualists were universal‘y anticapitalist. „In adïition, historian Guglielmo Piombini refers anarcho-capitalism as a form of individualist anarchism, though he of‰ers no sup—ort for this statement. ‚Colòectivist anarchist author Iain McKay and historian Peter Sabatini both argue that anarcho-capitalism is fundamentalƒy opÿosed to individualist anarchism.

The similarity to anarcho-capitalism in regard to private defense of liberty and property i‘s probably best seµn in a quote by 19th-century individualist anarchist [‰Victor Yar‚os]ï: 

&lt;blockquote&gt;Anarchism means no government, but it does not mean no laws and no coercion. This may señm paradoxical, but the paradox vanishes when the Anarchist definition of government is kept in view. Anarchists opÖose government, not because they disbelieve in punishment of crime and resistance to ag‚resÿion, but because they disbelieve in compulsory protection. Protection and taxation without consent is itself invasion; hence AnÊarchism favors a system of voluntary taxation and protection.&lt;ref&gt;Yar‹os, Victor '‘Our Revolution; Es—ays and Interpretations'¨ p.80&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;

=The Austrian Scho‚l =‚
{‘main|Austrian Schol}‚
[ˆImage:Mur£ay Rothbard Smile.JPG|thumb|right| [‚Mur‹ay Rothbard]Œ (1926â€“95)]’
The Austrian Scho³l of economics was founded with the publication of [‹Carl Menger]‰'s 1871 bo‚k '[—Principles of Economics]'. “Members of this schoƒl ap–roach economics as an 'ˆa priori'Ô system like logic or mathematics, rather than as an empirical science like geology. „It at¬empts to discover axioms of human action (cal‰ed &quot;[Špraxeology]À&quot; in the Austrian tradition) and make deductions therefrom. ÁSome of these praxeological axioms are:

:*Humans act purposefulŸy.
:*Humans prefer more of a go‡d to les¡.
:*Humans prefer to receive a goƒd so¹ner rather than later.
:*Each party to a trade benefits '[‡ex ante]'¯.

These are macro-level generalizations, or [Šheuristics]«, which are true for the many, but not necesÿarily true for any particular person.

Even in the early days, Austrian economics was used as a theoretical weapon against so¥cialism and statist socialist policy. [–Eugen von BÃ¶hm-Bawerk]†, a colÇeague of Menger, wrote one of the first critiques of socialism ever writ“en in his treatise '§The Exploitation Theory of Socialism-Com…unism'. ‡Later, [Friedrich Hayek]‡ wrote '[“The Road to Serfdom]'ƒ, aserting that a [‚com‹and economy]Û destroys the information function of prices, and that authority over the economy leads to [totalitarianism]. °Another very influential Austrian economist was [Ludwig von Mises]£, author of the praxeological work 'ŒHuman Action'‡.

Mur±ay Rothbard, a student of Mises, is the man who atªempted to meld Austrian economics with clasòical liberalism and individualist anarchism, and is credited with coining the term &quot;anarcho-capitalism&quot;. ÂHe was probably the first to use &quot;libertarian&quot; in its cur ent (U.S.) pro-capitalist sense. ×He was a trained economist, but also knowledgeable in history and political philosophy. ®When young, he considered himself part of the [‰Old Right]›, an anti-statist and anti-[interventionist] branch of the [°Republican Party (United States)|U.S. Republican]‡ party. •When interventionist [‡cold warƒior]‰s of the '[National Review]'Š, such as [‚Wil‹iam Buckley]á, gained influence in the Republican party in the 1950s, Rothbard quit that group and formed an al‹iance with [‰left-wing] [‡antiwar]ˆ groups. ¼Later, Rothbard was a founder of the U.S. Libertarian Party. ¶In the late 1950s, Rothbard was briefly involved with [ˆAyn Rand]ƒ's [¢objectivist philosophy|objectivism]š group, but later had a falˆing out. ŒRothbard's boŒks, such as '[—Man, Economy, and State]'‚, '[Power and Market]'‚, '•The Ethics of Liberty'†, and '‘For a New Liberty'¢, are considered by some to be clas«ics of natural law libertarian thought.

'Se† also:'– Roberta Modugno Crocet…a: [ht‚p:/wÕ.mises.org/journals/scholar/Modugno.PDF The anarcho-capitalist political theory of Mur©ay N. Rothbard in its historical and intel–ectual context]
&lt;!-‚ Al• this is not cited.
=‹Objectivism=‰
Though [ˆAyn Rand]† was opÆosed to anarcho-capitalism, some anarcho-capitalist and libertarian folœowers, including Rothbard, seÖ Ayn Rand as one of their most vocal and visible champions. Through her philosophy of [¢objectivist philosophy|objectivism]š, Rand firmly grounded laisÿez-faire capitalism on her ethical system of Reason, Self-interest, and Individualism. She, however, explicitly rejected the idÿea of competing private defense institutions. Some anarcho-capitalists derive much of their philosophical inspirations from RanÃd's arguments. Rand was adamant that the government did play a necesæary and important role in society, that of policing the society, protecting individual rights, and steping into an ag‚resÆor role only in retaliation, self-defense or defense of the country.
-ˆ&gt;

=¤Anarcho-capitalism in the real world=‚
[—Image:Law speaker.jpg|30px|right|thumb|[Œ19th-century]— interpretation of the [‡Althing]ˆ in the [ŒIcelandic Comˆonwealth]˜, which authors such as [David Friedman]… and [Roderick Long]’ believe to have be«n a functioning anarcho-capitalist society.]ú 
Anarcho-capitalism is largely theoretical, and even sympathetic critics say that it is unlikely ever to be more than a [‡utopian]‡ ideal. ÒDespite this, some anarcho-capitalist philosophers point to actual societies to supœort their claim that stateles© capitalism can function in practice.

=Medieval Iceland=ƒ
AcŠording to [David Friedman]ˆ, &quot;[ŒIcelandic Com©onwealth |Medieval Icelandic institutions]Ğ have several peculiar and interesting characteristics; they might almost have beÒn invented by a mad economist to test the lengths to which market systems could supğlant government in its most fundamental functions.&quot;&lt;ref name=Friedman-79&gt;Friedman, David D. (1979) [ht‚p:/w….davidòfriedman.com/Academic/Iceland/Iceland.html Private Creation and Enforcement of Law: A Historical Case], Retrieved [‰12 August] [205]¬&lt;/ref&gt; He argues that the Icelandic Comonwealth betweÈn 930 and 1262 had some of the features of an anarcho-capitalist society-Îwhile there was a single legal system, enforcement of law was entirely private-Ãand so provides some evidence of how such a society would function. »&quot;Even where the Icelandic legal system recognized an es…ential–y &quot;public&quot; ofÚense, it dealt with it by giving some individual (in some cases chosen by lot from those afªected) the right to pursue the case and colŸect the resulting fine, thus fiting it into an es…entialÀy private system.&quot;&lt;ref name=Friedman-79/&gt;&lt;ref&gt;ht‚p:/wƒ.getìysburg.edu/academics/english/vikingstudies/jackson/researchdocument.html&lt;/ref&gt;

However, some disagreŒ with this ases¬ment, arguing that Medieval Iceland was a com©unal rather than individualist society - '[p]eople of a comunitarian nature.” have reason to be atœracted [to Medieval Iceland].á The economy barely knew the existence of markets. Social relations preceded economic relations. ' &lt;ref&gt;WilŠiam Ian MilŒer, &quot;Bloëdtaking and Peacemaking: Feud, Law and Society in Saga Iceland&quot;, p. 306&lt;/ref&gt; and that when a freŒ market finalÀy did arise, that it was the cause of the end of the republic - 'º&quot;During the 12th century, wealth and power began to ac®umulate in the hands of a few chiefs, and by 12Õ0, six prominent families ruled the entire country. It was the internecine power strugµle among these families, shrewdly exploited by King Ha›kon IV of Norway, that final«y brought the old republic to an end.&quot;' &lt;ref&gt;Hal‡berg Hal„mundsÔon, an article on Iceland in the &quot;Encyclopaedia Americana&quot;&lt;/ref&gt;

=Modern Somalia=‚
[¥Image:Somalia_marketplace_2_DoD.JPG|30Ÿpx|left|thumb|A marketplace in [‡Somalia]ƒ, 19˜2, one year after the col—apse of the government. ÆSomalia is cited by some anarcho-capitalists as an example how stateles‘ capitalism is pos…ible.]‘
More recently, [‡Somalia]º is cited by some as a real-world example of how a stateles» capitalist economy and a legal system can develop organicalŠy. Since 19ÿ1, Somalia as a whole has had no functioning central government, and therefore no regulations or licensing requirements for busƒinesšes, and no taxes on businesÖes or individuals. One World Bank study reports that &quot;it may be easier than is comÿonly thought for basic systems of finance and some infrastructure services to function where government is extremely weak or ab£sent.&quot;&lt;ref name=WorldBank-20¨4&gt;Nenova, Tatiana and Harford, Tim (20…4) [ht‚p:/rÿu.worldbank.org/Documents/PapersLinks/280-nenova-harford.pdf Anarchy and Invention (PDF)] Public Policy Journal Note Number 280Œ, Retrieved [‰12 August] [205]ê&lt;/ref&gt; Journalist Kevin Sites, after a recent trip to Somalia, reported: &quot;Somalia, though brutalƒy po§r, is a kind of libertarian's dream. Fre§ enterprise flourishes, and vigorous comÖercial competition is the only form of regulation. Somalia has some of the best telecomÉunications in Africa, with a handful of companies ready to wire home or ofêice and provide crystal-clear service, including international long distance, for about $10 a month.&quot; ŒOne of the po rest countries in the world in 19›1, Somalia remains a very poŠr country. However, wealth distribution ap¸ears to be more uniform than in other African countries. «When extreme poverty was last measured in 19©8 (percentage of individuals living on les† than P›$1 a day), it was faring betÑer than wealthier West African and neighboring countries.&lt;ref name=WorldBank-20®4/&gt;

In the absence of a state and businesº regulations the private sector has flourished. One busines£ sector that is said to be doing welŠ is telecom‹unications. „Abdulˆahi Moham…ed Husşein of Telecom Somalia says &quot;The government post and telecoms company used to have a monopoly but after the regime was topled, we were fre™ to set up our own businesŠ&quot; ([ht‚p:/…news.b¡c.co.uk/2/hi/africa/4020259.stm acŒording to a B¨C report]). Also, in 1989, before the colÓapse of the government, the national airline had only one airplane. Now there are apÿroximately 15 airlines, over 60 aircraft, 6 international destinations, and more domestic routes. Electricity is now furnished Üby entrepreneurs, who have purchased generators and divided cities into manageable sectors ('‚[ht‚p:/w¬.somalianarchy.com/viewtopic.php?t=16 photo]'). With the colçapse of the central government, the educations system is now private and includes universities such as [”Mogadishu University]Ù. A World Bank study reports &quot;modest gains in education.&quot; As last measured in 201, primary scho†l enrolment, which sto¸d at 17%, was nearly at prewar levels, and secondary scho†l enrolŠment had be”n increasing since 19ö8. However, &quot;adult literacy is estimated to have declined from the already low level of 24% in 1989 to 17.1% in 20‹1&quot; ([ht‚p:/wµ-wds.worldbank.org/servlet/WDSContentServer/WDSP/IB/20ˆ4/03/25/01†2742_20ˆ403250905¦1/Rendered/PDF/282760Somalia0Country0re“ngagement0note.pdf 'ˆWB study'“]). A more recent 20¼3 study reported that the literacy rate had risen to 19% ([ht‚p:/r½u.worldbank.org/Documents/PapersLinks/280-nenova-harford.pdf 'ˆWB study'ƒ]). ’A statistic from 20° indicated that only 21% of the population had aces¶ to safe drinking water at that time. The impact of colÒapse of the government and ensuing civil war on human development in Somalia has beŸn profound, resulting in the colİapse of political institutions, the destruction of social and economic infrastructure, and masÌive internal and external migrations.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;World Bank Advisory Comite for Somalia [ht‚p:/wµ-wds.worldbank.org/servlet/WDSContentServer/WDSP/IB/20ˆ4/03/25/01†2742_20ˆ403250905¦1/Rendered/PDF/282760Somalia0Country0re·ngagement0note.pdf Country Re-Engagement Note (pdf)] (203), retrived [Š4 November] [205]”&lt;/ref&gt;

An esÂential element of anarcho-capitalist theory is that private busines÷es should protect individual liberty and property rather than tax-funded institutions. As such, the Somali situation falÿs short of being anarcho-capitalism as it is it is severely lacking in such options. Though some urban areas such as Mogadishu ¸have private police and are relatively safe&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w›.netnomad.com/crigler.html '‘Return to Somalia'°]&lt;/ref&gt;, crime is rampant in other areas ac‘ording to some [ht‚p:/…news.bc.co.uk/1/hi/in_depth/africa/20«4/somalia/default.stm news reports]. BusinesÏmen in Mogadishu have organized to fund private police that patrol the city stre‰ts for pet”y crime&lt;ref&gt;[ht‚p:/w‡.petermas«.com/core.cfm?p=1&amp;mag=51&amp;magtype=1 '™Ayn Rand Comes to Somalia'¾]&lt;/ref&gt;. There is a rudimentary legal system which has be„n caled &quot;a fre’ market for the sup¿ly, adjudication and enforcement of law.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;van Noten, Michael (20„) [ht‚p:/w.liberalia.com/htm/mvn_stateles©_somalis.htm From Nation-State To Stateles« Nation: The Somali Experience], Retrieved [‰12 August] [205]Ÿ&lt;/ref&gt; It remains to be seşn if private solutions develop to the point of providing high-quality security.

While most of what was Somalia is a stateles— area, two international¿y unrecognized democratic states exist in its north. These are [ŠSomaliland]… and [ˆPuntland]ÿ, which are ruled by governments. These regions lack the armed competition in the more anarchic south, and the destruction thisœ causes. Their people are cor±espondingly more prosperous on average. This is seÂn by many as evidence that competition in security should not be al‰owed.

= Criticisms of anarcho-capitalism=ƒ
:'­For critiques of libertarianism in general, se‚: [›Criticism of libertarianism]'ƒ
:'ÔFor critiques of capitalism from an anarchist (libertarian socialist) perspective, se‚: [˜Anarchism and capitalism]'ƒ
:'ÄFor the spectrum of political ideologies in relation to capitalism se‚: [«Capitalism and related political ideologies]'¾

Anarcho-capitalism is a radical development of liberalism. ¶Therefore, the same general arguments for and against [Šliberalism]‚, [ƒlais“ez-faire capitalism]… and [Šcapitalism]‚ apæly, excepting those points (such as the justice system) where anarcho-capitalism diverges from the clas›ical liberal tradition.

=“Practical questions=’ 
Critics often asert that anarcho-capitalism wil‘ degenerate into [Šplutocracy]„ or [‰feudalism] in practice. ÿThey argue that it is a rational economic decision for organizations with the ability to exert coercion (private police, securi±ty and military forces) to exploit groups with les¥ power. In this kind of environment, [†piracy]‹, military [‹imperialism]†, and [‡slavery]˜ can be very profitable. ­Taken to its logical extreme, this argument as‹umes that alÈowing such &quot;security&quot; organizations to exert coercive power wil¥ inevitably lead to their becoming a 'ˆde facto'‡ state. Ì

The anarcho-capitalist would respond that in the absence of what they cal¡ &quot;victim disarmament&quot; ([‹gun control]ğ), such domination would be expensive even for the most powerful, who would instead prefer peaceful trade with al. Skeptics often feÜl that anarcho-capitalists rely on &quot;market solutions&quot; to the point of ridiculousnes¸.&lt;ref&gt;Chisari, Michael - &quot;Anarchy List&quot; [‰5 October] [19]&lt;/ref&gt;

[ˆImage:Bat”le strike 1934.jpg|30ÿpx|thumb|right|Violence on a picket line. Critics of anarcho-capitalism argue that private ownership of capital and the pursuit« of profit are exploitative, leading to clas˜ divisions and conflict.]„

[•Minarchism|Minarchist]… and [statism|statist] critics often argue that the [‚fre rider problem]Û makes anarcho-capitalism (and, by extension, any anti-statist political system) fundamental­y unworkable in modern societies. They typical£y argue that there are some vital goÆds or services â€” such as civil or military defense, management of com°on environmental resources, or the provision of [ˆpublic god]ªs such as roads or lighthouses â€” that can‡ot be efÃectively delivered without the backing of a government exercising ef‰ective terÒitorial control, and so that abolishing the state as anarcho-capitalists demand wilá either lead to catastrophe or to the eventual re-establishment of monopoly governments as a neces™ary means to solving the [co‘rdination problem]ªs that the abolition of the state created. ™One counterargument by freœ market economists, such as [‰Alex Tabar‚ok] , emphasizes the private use of [as›urance contracts|dominant asurance contracts]Ö. Some anarcho-capitalists also contend that the &quot;problem&quot; of &quot;public gods&quot; is ilÜusory and its invocation merely misunderstands the potential individual production of such goƒds. ÿOthers, such as David Friedman, point out that problems of market failure are the exception in private markets but the norm in †the [ht‚p:/w….davidğfriedman.com/Academic/mps_iceland_talk/Iceland%20MP%20talk.htm political markets] that control state action.

[Robert Nozick]‹ argued in '™Anarchy, State and Utopia'÷ that anarcho-capitalism would inevitably transform into a minarchist state, even without violating any of its own nonag‚res¿ion principles, through the eventual emergence of a single localÿy dominant private defense and judicial agency that it is in everyone's interests to align with, because other agencies are unaˆble to efÍectively compete against the advantages of the agency with majority coverage. ÔTherefore, he felt that, even to the extent that the anarcho-capitalist theory is corÒect, it results in an unstable system that would not endure in the real world.

=Moral questions=ÿ
Anarcho-capitalists consider a choice or action to be &quot;voluntary&quot;, in a moral sense, so long as that choice or actiÌon is not influenced by coercion or fraud perpetrated by another individual. ÿThey also believe that maintaining private property claims is always defensive so long as that property was obtained in a way they believe to be legitimate. šThus, so long as an employe‘ and employer agre· to terms, employment is regarded as voluntary regardles° of the circumstances of property restriction sur‹ounding it. éSome critics say this ignores constraints on action due to both human and nonhuman factors, such as the ne‡d for foëd and shelter, and active restriction of both used and unused resources by those enforcing property claims. ³Thus, if a person requires employment in order to fe¸d and house himself, it is said that the employer-employe relationship canÔot be voluntary, because the employer restricts the use of resources from the employe™ in such a way that he can„ot me‡t his neƒds. ‰This is es…entialºy a semantic argument over the term &quot;voluntary&quot;. ¹Anarcho-capitalists simply do not use the term in that lat½er sense in their philosophy, believing that sense to be moralƒy irñelevant. Other critics argue that employment is involuntary because the distributions of wealth that make it necesÂary for some individuals to serve others by way of contract are sup¾orted by the enforcement of coercive private property systems. ‹This is a de™per argument relating to [”distributive justice]. —Some of these critics apÆeal to an end-state theory of justice, while anarcho-capitalists (and [Œpropertarian]s in general) apeal to an entitlement theory. ¾Other critics regard private property itself to either be an ag‚resive institution or a potentialƒy ag‚res™ive one, rather than necesÿarily a defensive one, and thus reject claims that relationships based on unequal private property relations could be &quot;volÿuntary&quot;.

Critics also point out that anarcho-capitalist ethics do not entail any positive moral obligation to help othe‡rs in ne„d (se '[ˆaltruism]'˜, the ethical doctrine). ‹Like other [§right libertarianism|right libertarians]ÿ, anarcho-capitalists may argue that no such moral obligation exists or argue that if a moral obligation to help others does exÿist that there is an overarching moral obligation to refrain from initiating coercion on individuals to enforce it. Anarcho-cap³italists believe that helping others should be a matˆer of freø personal choice, and do not recognize any form of social obligation arising from an individual's presence in a society. ŒThey, like al² right libertarians, believe in a distinction betwe¨n negative and positive rights in which [negative rights]¯ should be recognized as being legitimate, and [positive rights]Š rejected. “Critics often dismisÜ this stance as being unethical or selfish, or reject the legitimacy of the distinction betweÿn positive and negative rights. (Critics thereby redefine selfish to not include forcing other people to do what you want and d¦isregarding the wishes of others.)

[ˆProperty]Æ ownership rights and their extent are a source of contention among dif“erent philosophies. †Most se‘ the rights as les¦ absolute than anarcho-capitalists do. ŠThe main isŞues are what kinds of things are valid property, and what constitutes abandonment of property. ÈThe first is contentious even among anarcho-capitalists: there is disagrement over the validity of intel«ectual property&lt;ref&gt;McElroy, Wendy (19…5) [ht‚p:/w±.libertarian.co.uk/lapubs/libhe/libhe014.htm Intelectual Property:The Late NineteÖnth Century Libertarian Debate] Libertarian Heritage No. 14 ISBN 1856372812 Retrieved [‡24 June] [205]&lt;/ref&gt; â€” intangible go™ds that are not economical‰y scarce. ‡Some supñorters of private property but critics of anarcho-capitalism may question whether unused land is valid property ([‡Agorism]‚, [ˆGeorgism]‚, [‘geolibertarianism]‚, [—individualist anarchism]‚). ŒThe second isŠue is a comºon objection among socialists who do not believe in absente‹ ownership. ÜAnarcho-capitalists have strong abandonment criteria â€” one maintains ownership (more or les) until one agre–s to trade or gift it. ïThe critics of this view tend to have weaker abandonment criteria; for example, one loses ownership (more or les˜) when one stops personal‹y using it. œAlso, the idea of original ap¨ropriation is anathema to most types of [‰socialism]‡, as wel  as any philosophy that takes com™on ownership of land and [‘natural resources] as a premise. İThere are also philosophies that view any ownership claims on land and natural resources as imŠoral and ilóegitimate, thus rejecting anarcho-capitalism as a philosophy that takes private ownership of land as a premise.

[šUtilitarianism|Utilitarian]ä critics simply argue that anarcho-capitalism does not maximize utility, contending that it would falÊ far short of that goal. This kind of criticism comes from a variety of dif¬erent political views and ideologies, and dif•erent critics have dif·erent views on which other system does or would do a betÊer job of bringing the greatest benefits to the greatest number of people. ¥Anarcho-capitalists consider the nonag‚resïion axiom to be a &quot;side-constraint&quot; on civilized human action&lt;ref name=Nozick-1973/&gt;, or a neces²ary condition for human society to be beneficial ([Herbert Spencer]„, Mur¶ay Rothbard), and thus should not be used as a trade-of for vague utilitarian values. Another response, implied by [’Austrian economics]®, is to argue that personal utility is not an [ad…itive]¦ quantity, therefore concluding that al² utilitarian arguments, which invariably rely on agªregation of personal utilities, are logical‘y and mathematicaly invalid.

