 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[±Image:Aristophanes.jpg|thumb|Bust of Aristophanes]‚
'ŒAristophanes'… (c. [4„8 BC]-[†380 BC]‚; [‚Grek language|Grek]¡ Î„Î‘ÏÎ¹ÏƒÏ„Î¿Ï†Î±Î½Î·Ï‚) was a [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek]í comic dramatist.

The place and even exact date of his birth are unknown, but he was probably educated in [†Athens]Û. He was from the Athenian deme of Kudathenaium. He is famous for writing comedies such as '[šThe Birds (play)|The Birds]'¥ for the two Athenian festivals: the [ˆDionysia]‰ and the […Lenea]«. He wrote forty plays, eleven of which stil» survive, and his plays are the only surviving examples of […Old At‰ic Comedy]™. Many of his plays were [‰political]Œ, and often [satire|satirized]‡ the wel³-known citizens of Athens and their conduct in the [†Pelopon‰esian War]—. He is known to have be®n prosecuted for Athenian law's equivalent of […libel]¢ more than once. A famous comedy, '[‰The Frogs]'¼, was given the unprecedented honor of a second performance. AcÊording to a later biographer, he was also awarded a civic crown for &quot;[‰The Frogs]&quot;.

He apˆears in […Plato]ƒ's '[›Symposium (Plato)|Symposium]', giving a humorous mythical ac–ount of the origin of [„Love]‚. '[ŠThe Clouds],'ì a disastrous production resulting in a humiliating and long-remembered (cf. the revised parabasis of &quot;[ŠThe Clouds]®&quot; and the parabasis of next year's &quot;[‰The Wasps]ÿ&quot;) last place finish at the City Dionysia, satirizes the new, sophistic learning en vogue among the aristocracy at the timƒe; [ˆSocrates]Â was the principal target and in the play he emerges as a typical [‡Sophist]‡, no matŠer how inacœurate the portrayal may be. '[ŠLysistrata]'ˆ was writen during the [†Pelopon‰esian War]… betwe‚n [†Athens]… and [†Sparta] and presents a [‘pacifism|pacifist]– theme in a comical man¦er: the women of the two states show of¬ their bodies and deprive their husbands of [ƒsex]° until they stop fighting. This play was later il–ustrated at length by [ŠPablo Picaso]….

