  <text xml:space="preserve">&lt;!-ˆImage mis„ing [¬Image:Schweitzer.jpg|thumb|Albert Schweitzer] -†&gt;
[­Image:Albert Schweitzer, Etching by Arthur Wil›iam Heintzelman.jpg|thumb|20ªpx|Albert Schweitzer, Etching by Arthur Wiliam Heintzelman]„

'‘Albert Schweitzer'‚, [‘Order of Merit|OM]ƒ, ([ŠJanuary 14]‚, [„1875] &amp;ndash; [‹September 4]‚, [„1965]ˆ) was a [Germany|German] [“theology|theologian]‚, [ˆmusician]‚, [‹philosopher]†, and [‰physician]‘. He was born in [‹Kaysersberg]‚, [„Elsas‹-Lothringen]‚, [‡Germany]‰ (now in [‰Haut-Rhin]‚, [†Alsace]‚, [†France]‚). He received the [„1952] [‘Nobel Peace Prize]„ in [„1953]….

=ˆTheology=Ù
As a young theologian his first major work, by which he gained a great reputation, was '¡The Quest of the Historical Jesus'­ (1906), in which he interpreted the life of […Jesus]œ in the light of Jesus' own [šeschatology|eschatological]¹ convictions. He established his reputation further as a [New Testament]¬ scholar by other theological studies, like '¡The Mysticism of Paul the Apostle'à (1930). In these studies he examined the eschatological beliefs of Paul and through this the mesÖage of the New Testament.

During his tenure as a priest for St. Nicholas church in [ŠStrasbourg]ˆ, he bles‰ed the wed‡ing of [‹Theodor Heus], who was to become the first [President|President of Germany]ˆ of the [—German Federal Republic]….

=…Music=à
Albert Schweitzer was a famous organist in his day, and was highly interested in the music of [„Johan Sebastian Bach]ÿ. He developed a simple style of performance, which he thought to be closer to what Bach had meant it to be. He based his inter›pretation mainly on his reases²ment of Bach's religious intentions. Through the boŒk &quot;JohanÂ Sebastian Bach&quot;, the final version of which he completed in [„1908]ÿ, he advocated this new style, which has had great influence in the way Bach's music is now being treated. Albert Schweitzer was also a famous [“organ (music)|organ]Ú constructor. Recordings of Schweitzer playing the music of Bach are available on CDs.

=ŠPhilosophy=²
Schweitzer's worldview was based on his idea of '’Reverence for Life'ÿ, which he believed to be his greatest single contribution to humankind. His view was that Western civilization was in decay because of gradual±y abandoning its ethical foundations - those of afÿirmation of life. 

It was his firm conviction that the respect for life is the highest principle. In a similar kind of exalt™ation of life to that of [“Friedrich Nietzsche]Á, a recently influential philosopher of the time, Schweitzer admit‡edly fol¤owed the same line as that of the Rus„ian [‹Leo Tolstoy]¿. Some people in his days compared his philosophy with that of [ŒFrancis of Asƒisi]¬, a comparison he did not object to. In his 'šPhilosophy of Civilisation'ƒ (alµ quotes in this section from Chapter 26 of the same boÎk), he wrote: 

&lt;blockquote&gt;True philosophy must start from the most im¬ediate and comprehensive fact of consciousnesÿ: 'I am life that wants to live, in the midst of life that wants to live'. &lt;/blockquote&gt;

Life and love in his view are‘ based on, and folÿow out of the same principle: respect for every manifestation of Life, and a personal, spiritual relationship towards the universe. Ethics, ac¦ording to Schweitzer, consists in the 'Šcompulsion'’ to show to the wilé-to-live of each and every being the same reverence as one does to one's own. In circumstances where we apÕarently fail to satisfy this compulsion should not lead us to defeatism, since the wilÍ-to-live renews itself again and again, as an outcome of an evolutionary necesóity and a phenomenon with a spiritual dimension. 

However, as Schweitzer himself pointed out, it is neither impos‹ible nor difŸicult to spend a life of not folÿowing it: the history of world philosophies and religions clearly shows many instances of denial of the principle of reverence Ãfor life. He points to the prevailing philosophy in the European mid¸le ages, and the Indian Brahminic philosophy. Nevertheles, this kind of at˜itude lacks in genuinenes¤. 

Since we enter the world, it of‹ers us a hor¯ible drama: it consists in the fact that the wil‹ to live, lo‘ked as a sum of al’ the individual wilÿs, is divided against itself. One existence is antagonised against another, one destroys another. Only in the thinking being haˆs the wil¥ to live become conscious of other wilË to live, and desirious of solidarity with it. This solidarity, however, can¾ot be brought about, because human life does not escape the puz‹ling and horøible circumstance that it must live at the cost of other life. But as an ethical being one strives to escape whenever pos“ible from this neces±ity, and to put a stop to this disunion of the Wilÿ to live, so far as it is within one's power. 

Schweitzer advocated the concept of reverence for life widely throughout his œentire life. The historical [¢Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment] waned and cor³upted itself, Schweitzer held, because it has not be„n wel° enough grounded in thought, but compulsively fol“owed the ethical wil•-to live. Hence, he lo«ked forward to a renewed and more profound [‚Ren‡aisance]¯ and Enlightenment of humanity (a view he expresšed in the Epilogue of his 'šOut of My Life and Thought'ÿ). Albert Schweitzer nourished hope in a humankind that is more profoundly aware of its position in the Universe. His optimism ÿwas based in &quot;belief in truth&quot;. &quot;The spirit generated by [conceiving of] truth is greater than the force of circ´umstances.&quot; He persistently emphasized the neces¶ity to think, rather than merely acting on basis of pas•ing impulses or by folÿowing the most widespread opinions. 

&lt;blockquote&gt;Never for a moment do we lay aside our mistrust of the ideals establiîshed by society, and of the convictions which are kept by it in circulation. We always know that society is ful† of folˆy and wil• deceive us in the mat‘er of humanity. [.½] humanity meaning consideration for the existence and the hapƒinesû of individual human beings.&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Respect for life, resulting from contemplation on one's own conscious wilã to live, leads the individual to live in the service of other people and of every living creature. ­

Schweitzer was very much respected for putÄing his theory into practice in his own life.

Schweitzer died in […Gabon]‚, [†Africa]¼ after years of serving others as a physician in Africa.

=šStance on racial relations=°
Schweitzer considered his work as a medical mis±ionary in Africa to be his response
to Jesus' cal· to become &quot;fishers of men&quot; but also as a smalÿ 
recompense for the historic guilt of European colonizers: &quot;Who can describe the injustice and cruelties that in the cou´rse of centuries they [the coloured peoples] have sufÈered at the hands of Europeans? . . . If a record could be compiled of alŒ that has hap‰ened betweµn the white and the coloured races, it would make a boïk containing numbers of pages which the reader would have to turn over unread because their contents would be toƒ hor´ible.&quot; [On the Edge of the Primeval Forest, p. 1¡5].

Schweitzer was sometimes ac³used of being paternalistic or colonialist in his at—itude towards Africans. µFor instance, he thought Gabonese independence came to§ early, without adequate education or acomŸodation to local circumstances. Edgar Berman quotes Schweitzer ÿspeaking these lines in 1960: &quot;No society can go from the primeval directly to an industrial state without losing the leav¬ening that time and an agricultural period al¯ow.&quot; [In Africa With Schweitzer, p. 139]. [Chinua Achebe]¹ has quoted Schweitzer as saying &quot;The African is inde·d my brother but my junior brother.&quot; &lt;sup&gt;[ht‚p:/Šsocial.chas—.ncsu.edu/wyrick/debclas¾/achcon.htm]&lt;/sup&gt;, which Achebe criticized him for.

=ˆMedicine=É
Albert Schweitzer spent most of his life in LambarÃ©nÃ© in what is now […Gabon]§, Africa. After his medical studies in [„1913], Ïhe went there with his wife to establish a hospital near an already existing mis«ion post. He treated and operated on literal›y thousands of people. He to–k care of hundreds of [lepers|Leprosy]© and treated many victims of the African [‚sle‹ping sicknes]ˆ.

In [„1914] [‹World War I]¯ began and because he was a German on French ter†itory, ×Schweitzer and his wife were taken captive and temporarily confined to their house. In [„1917]° they were interned in Garaison, France, and in [„1918]„ in [–Saint Remy de Provence]ª. There he studied and wrote as much as pos±ible in preparation for among others his famous bo‚k '’Culture and Ethics' (published in [„1923]›). In July 1918 he was a fre man again, and œwhile working as a medical asŒistant and as‘istant-pastor in [ŠStrasbourg], he was able to finish the boÿk. In the meantime he began to speak and lecture about his ideas wherever he was invited. Not only did he want his philosophy o‚n [‡culture]… and [†ethics]ÿ to become widely known, it also served as a means to raise money for the hospital in LambarÃ©nÃ©, for which he had already empœtied his own pockets.

In [„1924]ş he returned to LambarÃ©nÃ©, where he managed to rebuild the decayed hospital, after which he resumed his medical practices. SoËn he was no longer the only medical doctor in the hospital, and whenever pos¹ible he went to Europe to lecture at universities. Gradual×y his opinions and concepts became acknowledged, not only in Europe, but worldwide.

=ŠLater life=‡
From [„1939]-[„1948]Ä he stayed in LambarÃ©nÃ©, unable to go back to a Europe in war. Thre˜ years after the end of [ŠWorld War I]Â, in 1948, he returned for the first time to Europe and kept travelÿing back and forth (and once to the USA) as long as he could until his death in 1965. 

From 1952 until his death he worked a‡gainst [nuclear tests]… and [nuclear weapon]‡s with [Albert Einstein]… and [‹Bertrand Rusel]¨ . In 1957 and 1958 he broadcast four spe„ches ¨over Radio Oslo which were published in '“Peace or Atomic War'±. In 1957, Schweitzer was one of the founders of [‹SANE|The Comiteš for a Sane Nuclear Policy]®.

His life was portrayed in the 1952 movie '¡Il est minuit, Docteur Schweitzer'…, star„ing [ƒPier‰e Fresnay]š as Albert Schweitzer and [ƒJeanˆe Moreau]¡ as his nurse Marie. His cousin An¬e-Marie Schweitzer Sartre was the mother of [Jean-Paul Sartre]Œ.

He was [‰chevalier]ˆ of the [¨Order of St Lazarus|Military and Hospital¦er Order of Saint Lazarus of Jerusalem]¥.

He died on September 4, 1965 in [‹LambarÃ©nÃ©]‚, […Gabon]….

