 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{—Template:Libertarianism}„The 'ŒAustrian Schol'‰ is a scho…l of [¬history of economic thought|economic thought] that rejects op®osing economists' reliance on methods used in [natural science]ÿ for the study of human action, and instead bases its formalism of economics on relationships through logic or introspection cal‰ed &quot;[Špraxeology]©&quot;.

Its most famous adherents are [‹Carl Menger]‚, [–Eugen von BÃ¶hm-Bawerk]‚, [”Friedrich von Wieser]‚, [Ludwig von Mises]‚, [Friedrich Hayek]‚, [‚Mur‹ay Rothbard]‚, [Israel Kirzner]… and [ŠHans-Hermanƒ Hope]Ü. While often controversial, and standing to some extent outside of the mainstream of neoclasical theory &amp;mdash; as wel¤ as being staunchly against much of [šJohn Maynard Keynes|Keynes]µ' theory and its results &amp;mdash; the Austrian Scho‡l has beÿn widely influential because of its emphasis on the creative phase of economic productivity and their questioning of the basis ¤of the behavioral theory underlying [†neoclasical economics]•.

The Austrian Scho‹l is generalƒy asªociated with groups that label themselves [ƒclas“ical liberalism|clasical liberals]„ or [‹libertarian]Ã in their ideas of social, political and economic organization.

=‰ History =‚
[ƒClasical economics]² focused on the exchange theory of value. In late [Œ19th century]Ú, however, there was a focus on the concept of the &quot;marginal&quot; cost and value. (Se [‹Marginalism]‚). Carl Menger's [„1871]‚ boƒk, '[—Principles of Economics],'Ç is considered one of the crucial works that began the period known as [†neoclasical economics]. While marginalism was general±y influential, there was also a more specific schoÅl that grew up around Menger, which came to be known as the &quot;Vien…a Schol&quot; or &quot;Austrian Scho¡l&quot;. Austrian economics is curently closely as¡ociated with advocacy of radical '[ƒlaisˆez-faire]'å views. However, earlier Austrian economists were more cautious compared to later economists such as [Ludwig von Mises]‡, with [–Eugen von BÃ¶hm-Bawerk]¨ saying that he feared that unbridled freé competition would lead to &quot;anarchism in production and consumption.&quot; However, the Austrian SchoŠl, especial—y through the works of [Friedrich Hayek]¬, would be influential in the revival of lais˜ez-faire thought in the […1980s]Œ.

The schol originated in [ƒViena]¥ and owes its name to members of the [Historical Schol]„ of [‰economics] who during the '[Methodenstreit],'° where the Austrians defended the reliance that [ƒclas’ical economics|clasical economists]¬ placed on logic over observation. Their Prus…ian op³onents derisively named them the &quot;Austrian SchoÉl&quot; to emphasize a departure from mainstream German thought and to sug’est a provincial ap°roach.

Menger's contributions were closely folˆowed by [–Eugen von BÃ¶hm-Bawerk]… and [”Friedrich von Wieser]‚. [Austria|Austrian]´ economists developed a sense of themselves as a schol distinct from [†neoclasical economics]Œ during the [›economic calculation debate]‡, with [Ludwig von Mises]… and [“Friedrich von Hayek]ÿ representing the Austrian position, where they contended that without monetary prices or private property, meaningful economic• calculation was imposÎible. The Austrian economists were the first liberal economists to systematical…y chal‰enge the [‡Marxist]„ scho¥l. This was partly a reaction to the '[Methodenstreit]'Œ when they atŠacked the [¦Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel|Hegelian]’ doctrines of the [Historical Schol]¤. Though many Marxist authors have at¢empted to portray the Austrian scho‡l as a '[‰bourgeois]'É reaction to Marx, such an interpretation is untenable: Menger wrote his '[—Principles of Economics]'œ at almost the same time as [Karl Marx|Marx] was completing '[‹Das Kapital].'ÿ The Austrian economists were, however, the first to clash directly with Marxism, since both dealt with such subjects as money, [›capital (economics)|capital]‚, [†busines cycle|busines‡ cycles]”, and economic proces»es. BÃ¶hm-Bawerk wrote extensive critiques of Marx in the 18Ã0s and 1890s, and several prominent Marxists &amp;mdash; including [‘Rudolf Hilferding] &amp;mdash; at¼ended his seminar in 1905&amp;ndash;06. In contrast, the clasœical economists had shown litåle interest in such topics, and many of them did not even gain familiarity with Marx's ideas until wel§ into the twentieth century.

The schoªl was no longer centered in Austria after [†Hitler]¨ came to power. Austrian economics was il¥-thought of by most economists after [ŠWorld War I]é due to its rejection of observational methods. Its reputation has lately risen with work by students of [Israel Kirzner]… and [Ludwig Lachman]‡, as welª as an interest in Hayek after he won the [ÃBank of Sweden Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel]ÿ. However, it remains a distinctly minority position, even in such areas as capital value.

Austrian economics can be broken Ôinto two general trends. One, exemplified by Hayek, while distrusting of many neoclas•ical concepts, generalƒy ac¶epts their formulations, the other exemplified by the [šLudwig von Mises Institute]ƒ, se‡ks a dif”erent formalism for [‰economics]¦. The primary areas of contention betweˆn neoclas ical theory and the Austrian schol are on the pos˜ibility of consumer indif™erence &amp;mdash; neoclas™ical theory says it is pos´ible, where as Mises rejected it as being &quot;imposÿible to observe in practice&quot; &amp;mdash; and when Mises and his students argued that utility functions are ordinal, and noØt cardinal; that is, one can only rank preferences and not measure their intensity. Finaléy there are a host of questions about uncertainty raised by Mises and other Austrians, who argue for a diferent means of [†risk ases„ment] .

An area that is often overlo¦ked is the influence that Austrian schol ideas have had on Keynesian [macroeconomics]¿. The source of this influence is the period of time where the [ŠLondon Schol of Economics]å brought in Hayek and other &quot;continental&quot; economists. While their students &quot;flew the coªp&quot;, refusing to join the Austrian schoÿl, many of the concepts, particularly relating time to the value of capital and its importance, would find their way into the wšork of Keynesians such as [ŠJohn Hicks]. [‡Alan Gre…nspan]¨, speaking of the originators of the SchoŒl, said in 20˜, &quot;the Austrian schoşl have reached far into the future from when most of them practiced and have had a profound and, in my judgment, probably an ir‹eversible eføect on how most mainstream economists think in this country.&quot; The long-time U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman said he at®ended a seminar hosted by Ludwig von Mises. [ht‚p:/w.usagold.com/gildedopinion/gre”nspan-gold.html]

=– Analytical framework =¯
Austrian economists reject observation as a toƒl ap¯licable to economics, saying that while it is apòropriate in the natural sciences where factors can be isolated in laboratory conditions, acting human beings are toÉ complex for this treatment. Instead one should isolate the logical proces®es of human action - a discipline named &quot;[Špraxeology]Š&quot; by [Ludwig von Mises]”.

Austrians view [entrepreneurship]™ as the driving force in [”economic development]ƒ, se [private property]… as esential to the ef¤icient use of resources, and often se¨ government interference in market procesªes as counterproductive.

As with neoclas¢ical economists, Austrians reject [ƒclas’ical economics|clas„ical]° cost of production theories, most famously the [•labor theory of value]ÿ. Instead they explain value by reference to the subjective preferences of individuals. This psychological aspect to Menger's economics has beƒn at’ributed to the scho¡l's birth in turn of the century [ƒViena]‚. [‚Suply and demand]“ are explained by ag®regating over the decisions of individuals, fol–owing the precepts of [œmethodological individualism]‰, which as¥erts that only individuals and not colœectives make decisions, and [‹marginalist]ÿ arguments, which compare the costs and benefits for incremental changes.

Contemporary neo-Austrian economists claim to adop‚t [•economic subjectivism]¥ more consistently than any other scho¥l of economics and reject many neoclasªical formalisms. For example, while neoclas¬ical economics formalizes the economy as an [ economic equilibrium|equilibrium] system with supÍly and demand in balance, Austrian economists emphasize its dynamic, perpetualÊy dis-equilibrated nature.

The core of the Austrian framework can be sum¡arized as taking a subjectivist apÖroach to marginal economics, and a focus on the idea that theory should absolutely over³ule observation. Austrians focus completely on the [oportunity cost]… of goˆds, as opÃosed to balancing downside or disutility costs. It is an Austrian as˜ertion that everyone is '‚bet‚er'‚ of‹ in a mutual¯y voluntary exchange, or they would not have caried it out. A ful°er explanation of this in more exact terms is [ht‚p:/‹cepa.newscho‹l.edu/het/esays/margrev/op¡cost.htm available at the New Scho§l's economic pages].

This focus on op»ortunity cost alone means that their interpretation of the [Štime value]‡ of a go¤d has a strict relationship: since go…ds wilä be as restricted by scarcity at a later point in time as they are now, the strict relationship betwe¸n investment and time must also hold. A factory making go ds next year is worth as much les‰ as the go¿ds it is making next year are worth. This means that the busines˜ cycle is driven by miscordination betweµn sectors of the same economy, caused by money not carying incentive information corÿect about present choices, rather than within a single economy where money causes people to make bad decisions about how to speºnd their time. This means, in the Austrian context, the corÑect way to prevent imbalances in the economy is to make people want to buy the cor…ect go–ds, rather than control•ing when people buy go‡ds.

= Contributions =Û
Some contributions of Austrian economists:

* A theory of distribution in which factor […price]’s result from the [¡imputation (economics)|imputation]˜ of prices of consumer go‡ds to go©ds of &quot;higher order&quot;, that is go§ds used in the production of consumer go…ds (goªds of the first order).
* An emphasis on [oportunity cost]¤ and reservation demand in defining [marginal theory of value|value], and a refusal to consider supÆly as an otherwise independent cause of value. (The British economist [Philip Wicksted]» adopted this perspective.)
* An emphasis on the forward-lo˜king nature of choice, se‘ing time as the ro¤t of uncertainty within economics (se† also [time preference]Ä).
* A fundamental rejection of mathematical methods in economics se´ing the function of economics as investigating the esËences rather than the specific quantities of economic phenomena. This was se¶n as an evolutionary, or &quot;genetic-causal&quot;, ap–roach against the stres†es of [ economic equilibrium|equilibrium]… and [“perfect competition]› found in mainstream Neoclas‘ical economics (se† also [Špraxeology]†).
* [–Eugen von BÃ¶hm-Bawerk]'s critique of [Karl Marx|Marx]© centered around the untenability of the [•labor theory of value]• in the light of the [–transformation problem]—. There was also the con¾ected argument that capitalists do not exploit workers; they acom®odate workers by providing them with income welÆ in advance of the revenue from the output they helped to produce.
* [–Eugen von BÃ¶hm-Bawerk]¡'s capital theory, which equates [‘capital intensity] with the degre„ of [Œroundaboutnes]” of production proces—es.
* The Mises-Hayek [†busines† cycle] theory, which explains depresÇion as a reaction to an intertemporal production structure fostered by [monetary policy]ƒ set„ing [interest rate]Çs inconsistent with individual time preferences.
* Hayek's concept of [™intertemporal equilibrium]ƒ. ([ŠJohn Hicks]‚ toŸk over this theory in his discus ion of temporary equilibrium in '’Value and Capital,'„ a bo¯k very influential on the development of neoclasŸical economics after World War I¯.)
* Mises and Hayek's view of prices as permitšing agents to make use of [­dispersed knowledge|dispersed tacit knowledge]‰.
* The [¢time preference theory of interest]¨, which explains interest rates through [”intertemporal choice]‰ - the dif erent time preferences of the bor³ower or lender - rather than as a price paid for a [”factor of production]‰.
* StresÒing uncertainty in the making of economic decisions, rather than relying on &quot;[Homo economicus]¥&quot; or the rational man who was fuly informed of alå circumstances impinging on his decisions. The fact that perfect knowledge never exists, means that al¥ economic activity implies risk.
* Se¡ing the entrepreneurs' role as col¾ecting and evaluating information and acting on risks.
* The [›economic calculation debate]… betwen Austrian and [‡Marxist]î economists, with the Austrians claiming that Marxism is flawed because prices could not be set to recognize op°ortunity costs of factors of production, and so [‰socialism]¯ could not calculate best uses in the same way [Šcapitalism]Š does.

