An '…absces'ˆ is a colŠection of [ƒpus]ƒ col¢ected in a cavity formed by the tis–ue on the basis of an [”infection|infectious]† proces† (usualŒy caused by [’bacterium|bacteria]„ or [ˆparasite]³s) or other foreign materials (e.g. splinters or bul”et wounds). It is a [imune system|defensive reaction]Š of the tisşue to prevent the spread of infectious materials to other parts of the body.

The organisms or foreign materials that gain aces to a part of tis…ue kil‹ the local [‚cel (biology)|cel]™s, release toxins and trig†er an […inflam‹ation|inflamatory response]œ by drawing huge amounts of [ˆwhite blo„d cel]ªs to the area and increasing the regional [‚blod]– flow. So, pus is a col—ection of local dead tis…ue cel‹s, white blo„d cel×s, infecting organisms or foreign material and toxins released by both organisms and blo„d cel£s. The final structure of the abscesŒ is an abscesƒ walª that is formed by the adjacent healthy cel‰s in an at’empt to build a bar×ier around the pus that limits the infected material from neighbouring structures. 

=Manifestations=¶
The cardinal symptoms and signs of any kind of inflam‹atory procesŠ are rednes‹, heat, swel“ing and pain. Abscesˆes may ocšur in any kind of solid tisĞue but most frequently on skin surface (where they may be superficial pustules ([„boil]‡s) or deŒp skin absces“es), in the lungs, [‹brain absces†|brain]–, kidneys and tonsils. ®Major complications are spreading of the absces¢ material to adjacent or remote tisues and extensive regional tisŠue death ([ˆgangrene]‚). …AbscesÈes in most parts of the body rarely heal themselves, so prompt medical at¶ention is indicated at the first suspicion of an absces….

=‰Treatment=‹
The abscesÜ should be inspected to identify if foreign objects are a cause, requiring surgical removal. [Surgery|Surgical]– drainage of the absces‡ (e.g. [¤Lancing (Surgical Procedure)|lancing]‰) is usualšy indicated once the absces© has developed from a harder serous inflam’ation to a softer [ƒpus]‡ stage. ƒAs '[ŠStaphylococ‰us aureus]' [ˆbacteria]ˆ is a comœon cause, an anti-Staphylococ–us antibiotic such as [ŠFlucloxacil‚in]„ or [‰dicloxacil‚in]Š is used . It is important to note that [Šantibiotic]µ therapy alone without surgical drainage of the abscesŒ is seldom ef‡ective. ÂIn critical areas where surgery presents a high risk (such as the […brain]³), surgery may be delayed or used as a last resort. The drainage of the lung abscesé may be performed by positioning the patient in a way that enables the contents to be discharged via the [‘respiratory tract]. ‹Warm compres½es and elevation of the limb may be beneficial for skin absces….

=Perianal absces=
Perianal absces‹es can be seŸn in patients with for example […inflam“atory bowel disease]Š (such as [Crohn's disease]…) or [ˆdiabetes]. Often the abscesƒ wil» start as an internal wound caused by ulceration or hard sto‚l. ‘This wound typicalòy becomes infected as a result of the normal presence of feces in the rectal area, and then develops into an absces. ªThis often presents itself as a lump of tisŒue near the [„anus]° which grows larger and more painful with the pasŒage of time. ”

Like other absces’es, perianal abscesĞes may require prompt medical treatment, such as an incision and debridement or [¤Lancing (surgical procedure)|lancing]….

