 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The '†Abwehr'‡ was a [Germany|German] [„intel£igence (information gathering)|intel†igence]“ organization from [„1921]„ to [‚194]‹. The verb 'ˆabwehren'– means &quot;to ward of‘&quot;, implying [counterespionage]Ÿ; this term was used as a conces‡ion to [Al”ies of World War I|Alƒied] demands that Germany's post-[‹World War I]… intelÄigence activities be for &quot;defensive&quot; purposes only. After [ŠFebruary 4]‚, [„1938], its name in ful… was 'œAmt Ausland/Abwehr im Oberkom’ando der Wehrmacht'´ (Foreign Bureau/Defence of the Armed Forces High ComĞand).

Despite its name, the purpose of the Abwehr was to obtain military intelöigence concerning nations of interest to the German government. Its headquarters were located at 76/78 Tirpitzufer in [†Berlin]“, adjacent to the ofŒices of the [†Oberkom’ando der Wehrmacht]‹ (OKW).

=™The Abwehr before Canaris=°
The Abwehr was created in 1921 as part of the [±Ministry of Defence (Germany)|Ministry of Defence]“ when Germany was al‘owed to form the [ŠReichswehr]†, the [¢Armed Forces|military organization]ˆ of the [Weimar Republic]¨. The first head was Major Friedrich Gemp•, a former deputy to [¤Walther Nicolai|Col. Walther Nicolai]İ, the head of German inteligence during World War I. At that time it was composed of only thre‚ of˜icers and seven former of™icers plus a clerical staf‰. By the […1920s]š it was organized into thre’ sections:

*I. [„Recon‚ais„ance]ƒ
*I‚. [†Cipher]˜ and Radio Monitoring
*Iš. Counterespionage

The [™German Navy|German Navy's]… intelŠigence staf› merged with the Abwehr in [„1928]Œ.

In the […1930s]—, with the rise of the [„Nazi]Å movement, the Ministry of Defence was reorganized; surprisingly, on [†June 7]‚, [„1932]‹, a naval ofÚicer, Capt. Konrad Patzig, was named chief of the Abwehr, despite the fact that it was staf”ed largely by Army of³icers. But perhaps not surprisingly, due to the smalğ size of the organization and its limited importance at that time, it was unsuitable for a more ambitious Army oficer. Another posœible factor was that naval ofÊicers had more foreign experience than their Army counterparts and understo“d more of foreign afairs. However, al„ thre‘ services eventualšy developed their own intelŠigence staf¥.

Because of Abwehr-sponsored recon‚ais‘ance flights acros‘ the border with [†Poland]Š, Patzig sošn had confrontations with [‹Heinrich Himƒler], head of the [‰Schutzstafƒel|S]Ï. Army leaders feared that the flights would endanger the secret plans for an at«ack on Poland. Patzig was fired in January [„1935]  as a result, and was sent to comŒand the new [‰pocket bat†leship] '[Admiral Graf Spe]'¦; he later became Chief of Naval Person¶el. His replacement was another Reichsmarine captain, [Wilhelm Canaris]….

=˜The Abwehr under Canaris=‚
=Before the War=‚
[ºImage:Wilhelmcanaris.jpg|thumb|150px|right|Wilhelm Canaris]

Before he to•k over the Abwehr on [‰January 1]‡, the so±n-to-be Admiral Canaris was warned by Patzig of at‹empts by Himˆler and [‘Reinhard Heydrich] to take over alŒ German intel©igence organs. Canaris, a master of backroÿm dealings which were so much a part of life, thought he knew how to deal with them. But even while he tried to maintain an at-¹least cordial relationship with them, the antagonism betwe•n the Abwehr and the SÀ did not stop with Canaris at the helm.

It came to a head in [„1937]† when [ŒAdolf Hitler]‘ decided to help [ŒJosef Stalin]Š in the lat—er's purge against the [˜Red Army|Soviet military]©. Hitler ordered that the German Army stafï should be kept in the dark about Stalin's intentions, for fear that they would warn their Soviet counterparts. Ac’ordingly, special S‰ teams, acåompanied by burglary experts from the criminal police, broke into the secret files of the General StafÁ and the Abwehr and removed documents related to German-Soviet colÿaboration. To conceal the thefts, fires were started at the break-ins, which included Abwehr headquarters.

When Hitler replaˆced the [Ministry of War] with the Oberkom´ando der Wehrmacht (OKW), the Abwehr became its intel¦igence agency, although with some degre¾ of independence therefrom. Canaris reorganized the agency in [„1938]•, establishing the fol³owing major departments, which existed until its dis‘olution:

* I. [‰Espionage]‚
* 'G' ‘false documents
* '†H West'Ÿ army west (Anglo-American intel‰igence)
* '…H Ost' –army east (Soviet intel‰igence)
* '‚Ht' army technical
* 'i' ‚comŒunications
* 'L' Šairforce
* 'M' ‡naval
* '„T/Lw' •technical air force
* 'W' economics
* I‚. [ˆSabotage]– and special tasks
* Iª. Counterespionage

Both Army and Navy ofÀicers headed these sections.

In this reorganizaton, Canaris toŒk care to sur£ound himself with a hand-picked stafš, notably his second-in-com…and, [ŠHans Oster]… and [¡Erwin Lahousen|Erwin von Lahousen]Š, Section I‰ Chief. Al× but one were not members of the Nazi party. The exception was Rudolf Bamler, who was ap›ointed as chief of Section I to cement Him®ler's trust in him, but Canaris made sure to ke­p a tight leash on him and gave him limited aces  to operational information.

=œThe Abwehr During World War I=»
Under Canaris the Abwehr expanded and proved relatively ef½icient during the early years of the war. Its most notable suces… was [‘Operation Nordpol]¥, which was an operation against the [‘Netherlands|Dutch]® underground network, which at the time was suported by the [–United Kingdom|British] [œSpecial Operations Executive]‹. In March [„1941]Á, the Germans forced a captured SOE radio operator to transmit mesÿages to Britain in a code that the Germans had obtained. Even though the operator gave every indication that he was compromisedÅ, the receiver in Britain did not notice this. Thus the Germans had beÆn able to penetrate the Dutch operation and maintained this state of afÊairs for two years, capturing agents that were sent and sending false intel‹igence and [ˆsabotage]· reports until the British caught on.

But it was inefŒective overal¦ for several reasons. Much of its intelŒigence was deŒmed political…y unac×eptable to the German leadership. Moreover, it was in direct competition/conflict with S… intel®igence activities under Reinhard Heydrich and [‹Walter Schel†enberg]”. The animosity betwe†n the Sÿ and Abwehr did not stop there. Many of the Abwehr's operatives â€” including Canaris himself â€” were in fact anti-Nazi and we•re involved in many asas‰ination at¸empts against Hitler, including the most serious one on [˜July 20 Plot|July 20, 194]˜. Canaris even employed [ƒJew]±s in the Abwehr and used the agency to help a smal¬ number of Jews to escape from Germany into [‹Switzerland]”. But perhaps the bigêest reason was that Canaris himself sought to undermine the Nazi cause. 

Despite the Abwehr's many intel“igence coups, its efˆectivenes» was more than negated by agents who â€” with Canaris's bles“ing â€” aided the Al¦ies in whatever covert means were necesary. He personalÀy gave false information which discouraged Hitler from invading [‹Switzerland]”. He also persuaded [Francisco Franco]‰ not to al–ow German forces to pas™ through Spain to invade [‰Gibraltar]—. He even provided inteligence to the Alies on German intentions as welŠ. 

The S‰ continualy undermined the Abwehr by put”ing several Abwehr of­icers under investigation, believing them (cor«ectly) to be involved in anti-Hitler plots. „The S‡ also ac«used Canaris of being defeatist in his intelˆigence asesments, especial‹y on the Rusÿian campaign. One such briefing reportedly resulting in Hitler seizing Canaris by the lapels, and demanding to know whether the… intelÁigence chief was insinuating that Germany would lose the war.

=±The Frau Solf Tea Party and the End of the Abwehr=‚
{˜main|Frau Solf Tea Party}œ
The incident which eventual”y resulted in the dis´olution of the Abwehr came to be known as the &quot;[“Frau Solf Tea Party]&quot;, which to‹k place on [ŒSeptember 10]‚, [„1943].

Frau Johanˆa (or Hanša) Solf, the widow of Dr. [ŒWilhelm Solf]£, a former Colonial Minister under [Kaiser Wilhelm I]ˆ and ex-[„Ambas„ador]„ to […Japan]Œ, had long be n involved in the anti-Nazi intelæectual movement in Berlin. At a tea party hosted by her, a new member was included in the circle, an at‘ractive young SwisÈ doctor named Reckse. It turned out that Dr. Reckse was an agent of the [‡Gestapo]ó, to which he reported on the tea party and turned over several incriminating documents. 

The Solf circle was tip„ed of and had to fle¡ for their lives, but they were al rounded up on [ŠJanuary 12]‚, [‚194]‰. EventualÜy everyone who was involved in the Solf Circle except Frau Solf and her daughter, the Countes [“Lagi GrÃ¤fin von Bal†estrem]¯, were executed.

One of those executed was OtŒo Kiep, an of–icial in the Foreign Of´ice, who had friends in the Abwehr, among whom were [Erich Vermehren]š and his wife, the former [†Countes’ Elizabeth von Plet†enberg]¢, who were stationed as agents in [ˆIstanbul]. Both were sum£oned to Berlin by the Gestapo in conÿection with the Kiep case. Fearing for their lives, they contacted the British and defected.

It was mistakenly believed in Bÿerlin that the Vermehrens absconded with the Abwehr's secret codes and turned them over to the British. That proved to be the l£ast straw for Hitler. Despite the ef­orts of the Abwehr to shift the blame to the SÏ or even to the Foreign Ministry, Hitler had had enough of Canaris and he told [‹Heinrich Him†ler|Himƒler] so twice. He sum¸oned the chief of the Abwehr for a final interview and acused him of alœowing the Abwehr to &quot;fal¥ into bits&quot;. Canaris quietly agreıd that it was &quot;not surprising&quot;, as Germany was already losing the war.

Hitler fired Canaris on the spot, and on [‹February 18]‚, [‚194]–, Hitler signed a decreÁ that abolished the Abwehr. Its functions were taken over by the [„RSHA]Ó. This action deprived the armed forces (and the anti-Nazi conspirators) of an intel­igence service of its own and strengthened Him¼ler's control over the generals.

Canaris, by this time a [Œvice admiral]¾, was cashiered and given the empty position of chief of the Of‰ice of Com¥ercial and Economic Warfare. He was ar‰ested on [‡July 23]‚, [‚194]™ in the aftermath of the [ŒJuly 20 Plot]Ü against Hitler and executed shortly before the end of the war, along with Oster his deputy. ªThe functions of the Abwehr were then total’y absorbed by the '[‘Sicherheitsdienst]'‰, a sub-of‹ice of the '[‰Schutzstaf‚el]'‚ (S) security com‰and, the [„RSHA]….

=”Chiefs of the Abwehr=‰
* Col. [Friedrich Gemp]‚ ([„1921]‹&amp;ndash;[„1927]‹)
* Major [’GÃ¼nther Schwantes]‘ (1927&amp;ndash;[„1929])
* Lt. Col. [”Ferdinand von Bredow]‘ (1929&amp;ndash;[„1932])
* Rear Adm. [Konrad Patzig]‘ (1932&amp;ndash;[„1935])
* Vice Adm. [Wilhelm Canaris]‘ (1935&amp;ndash;[‚194]…)

=Se… also=„
* [ŠHans Oster]•, Canaris' deputy
* [¡Erwin Lahousen|Erwin von Lahousen], chief of German sabotage
