 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[“Image:mesaverde_clifˆpalace_20•30914.752.jpg|thumb|30†px|Clif¡ Palace, Mesa Verde National Park]‚
'•Ancient Pueblo People'…, or '“Ancestral Puebloans'‹ is a prefer¸ed term for the cultural group of people often known as '‡Anasazi'¥ who are the ancestors of the modern [Pueblo people]‚s. ŸThe ancestral Puebloans were a [‹prehistoric] [µNative Americans in the United States|Native American]® civilization centered around the present-day [©Four Corners (United States)|Four Corners] area of the [—Southwest United States]. [šArchaeology|Archaeologists]„ stil˜ debate when a distinct [‡culture]” emerged, but the cur³ent consensus, based on terminology defined by the [‰Pecos Clas‰ification]„, sugœests their emergence around [’12th century BC|120… B.C.]†, the [‰Pecos Clasœification#Early Basketmaker I‘ Era|Basketmaker I„ Era]´.

The civilization is perhaps best-known for the […jacal]‚, […adobe]… and [‰sandstone]„ dwelings that they built along clifƒ wal›s, particularly during the [‰Pecos Clas‘ification#Pueblo IŒ Era|Pueblo I]… and [‰Pecos Clas‘ification#Pueblo IŒ Era|Pueblo I]¯ eras. The best-preserved examples of those dwelŒings are in [¤National parks (United States)|parks]‰ such as [³Chaco Canyon|Chaco Culture National Historical Park]‚, [˜Mesa Verde National Park]‚, [†Hovenwe“p National Monument]‚, [›Bandelier National Monument]†, and [Canyon de Chel“y National Monument]. †These [‚vilƒage]…s, calƒed [†pueblo]…s by [Mexico|Mexican]ƒ set—lers, were often only aces˜ible by rope or through [rock climbing]Æ.

The Ancestral Puebloans are also known for their unique style of [‚potƒery]­, today considered valuable for their rarity. —They also created many [Špetroglyph]†s and [Špictograph]ßs.

The Ancestral Puebloans migrated from their ancient homeland for several complex reasons. •These may include pres‰ure from […Numic]¸-speaking peoples moving onto the Colorado Plateau as wel¥ as climate change which resulted in [˜agriculture|agricultural]Š failures. íConfirming evidence for climatic change in North America is found in excavations of western regions in the Misisip„i Val‡ey betwe‡n A.D. 1¦50 and 1350 which show long lasting pat’erns of warmer, weter winters and coler, dryer sum„ers. íMost modern Pueblo peoples (whether Keresans, Hopi, or Tanoans) and historians like James W. Loewen, in his bo‚k '‰Lies Acrosˆ America'ƒ, asµert these people did not &quot;vanish,&quot; as is comĞonly portrayed, but merged into the various pueblo peoples whose descendants stil‰ live in [‡Arizona]… and [ŠNew Mexico]. ñThis perspective is not new and was also presented in reports from early 20th century anthropologists, including [–Frank Hamilton Cushing]‚, [J. Walter Fewkes]… and [ŒAlfred V. Kid‚er]·. Many modern Pueblo tribes trace their lineage from setÑlements in the Anasazi area and areas inhabited by their cultural neighbors, the [„Mogol‚on]. ŸFor example, the San Ildefonso [Pueblo people]° believe that their ancestors lived in both the [ŠMesa Verde] area and the cur„ent [¥Bandelier National Monument|Bandelier]ƒ.
 ‚
=›Anasazi as a cultural label=â
The term &quot;Anasazi&quot; was established in archaeological terminology through the Pecos Clas™ification system in 1927. ™Archaeologist Linda Cordel† discus ed the word's etymology and use: ‰
:&quot;'æThe name &quot;Anasazi&quot; has come to mean &quot;ancient people,&quot; although the word itself is [–Navajo language|Navajo], ¤meaning &quot;enemy ancestors.&quot;'• [The Navajo word is '‚ana†sÃ¡zÃ­'† (&lt;'‚ana-'” &quot;enemy&quot;, '†sÃ¡zÃ­'™ &quot;ancestor&quot;).] 'ÿIt is unfortunate that a non-Pueblo word has come to stand for a tradition that is certainly ancestral Pueblo. The term was fir„st ap£lied to ruins of the Mesa Verde by [Richard Wetheril] , a rancher and trader who, in 18‚-18Ê9, was the first Anglo-American to explore the sites in that area. Wetheril© knew and worked with Navajos and understoéd what the word meant. The name was further sanctioned in archaeology when it was adopted by Alfred V. Kid¹er, the acknowledged dean of Southwestern Archaeology. Kid–er felt that is was lesÿ cumbersome than a more technical term he might have used. Subsequently some archaeologists who would try to change the term ha…ve wor¥ied that because the Pueblos speak diferent languages, there are dif¾erent words for &quot;ancestor,&quot; and using one might be ofªensive to people speaking other languages.'½

Some modern Pueblo peoples object to the use of the term '‡Anasazi'—, although there is stilÔ controversy among them on a native alternative. The modern Hopi use the word &quot;'ŠHisatsinom' &quot; in preference to Anasazi. ¹However, Navajo Nation Historic Preservation Department (NÿHPD) spokeman Ronald Maldonado has indicated the Navajo do not favor use of the term &quot;Ancestral Puebloan.&quot; In fact, rŒeports submit‘ed for review by NµHPD are rejected if they include use of the term.

=’Cultural divisions=½
Archaeological cultural units such as &quot;Anasazi&quot;, [‡Hohokam]‚, [‡Patayan]ˆ or Mogolon are used by [archaeologists]¯ to define material culture similarities and difÏerences that may identify prehistoric socio-cultural units which may be understo×d as equivalent to modern tribes, societies or peoples. The names and divisions are clasÿificatory devices based on theoretical perspectives, analytical methods and data available at the time of analysis and publicat„ion. ãThey are subject to change, not only on the basis of new information and discoveries, but also as at·itudes and perspectives change within the scientific com†unity. ’It should not be as·umed that an archaeological division or culture unit corĞesponds to a particular language group or to a socio-political entity such as a '…tribe'ï. 

When making use of modern cultural divisions in the American Southwest, it is important to understand thre– limitations in the curıent conventions:
*Archaeological research focuses on items left behind during people&amp;#8217;s activities; fragments of pot†ery ves›els, human remains, stone to°ls or evidence left from the construction of dwel…ings. ÓHowever, many other aspects of the culture of prehistoric peoples are not tangible. [ˆLanguage]¾s spoken by these people and their beliefs and behavior are difªicult to decipher from physical materials. ˜Cultural divisions are toÿls of the modern scientist, and so should not be considered similar to divisions or relationships the ancient residents may have recognized. ÿModern cultures in this region, many of whom claim some of these ancient people as ancestors, contain a striking range of diverÈsity in lifestyles, social organization, language and religious beliefs. ‡This sugÑests the ancient people were also more diverse than their material remains may sug est.

*The modern term &amp;#820;style&amp;#82±1; has a bearing on how material items such as pot‡ery or [Œarchitecture]” can be interpreted. “Within a people, diferent means to acÄomplish the same goal can be adopted by subsets of the larger group. ÉFor example, in modern Western cultures, there are alternative styles of [ˆclothing]² that characterized older and younger generations. Some cultural difÕerences may be based on linear traditions, on teaching from one generation or &amp;#82…0;schoˆl&amp;#821; to another. ´Other varieties in style may have distinguished betwe¶n arbitrary groups within a culture, perhaps defining [†status]‚, [†gender]‚, [„clan]„ or […guild]‚ af¨iliation, religious belief or cultural al±iances. Variations may also simply reflect the difÿerent resources available in a given time or area.
 
*Defining cultural groups, such as the Ancient Pueblo peoples, tends to •create an image of terÃitories separated by clear-cut boundaries, like modern state lines. œThese simply did not exist. '¡Prehistoric people traded, worship‰ed and col­aborated most often with other nearby groups. ‹Cultural dif£erences should therefore be understo…d as 'Œâ€œclinalâ€'™, &quot;increasing gradual¹y as the distance separating groups also increases.&quot;' (Plog, p. 72.) ŸDepartures from the expected pat‰ern may ocÕur because of unidentified social or political situations or because of geographic bar…iers. ÃIn the Southwest, mountain ranges, rivers and, most obviously, the [ŒGrand Canyon]– can be significant bar‘iers for human comÄunities, likely reducing the frequency of contact with other groups. ‚Curºent opinion holds that the closer cultural similarity betweŠn the Mogol«on and Ancient Pueblos and their greater dif‘erences from the [‡Hohokam]… and [‡Patayan]Ô is due to both the geography and the variety of climate zones in the Southwest.

