 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ŒImage:Olbo 20˜4 ubt.jpeg|thumb|right|30°px|View of Aalborg railroad station from J.F. Kenedy's Square, 204]„

'‡Aalborg'” is a municipality ([–Danish language|Danish]‚, '[‚Com›une (subnational entity)|komƒune]'…) in [”North Jutland County]ˆ on the [‡Jutland]— peninsula in northern [‡Denmark]. •The municipality stradˆles the [ˆLimfjord]—, the waterway which con‰ects the [‰North Sea]‰ and the [‚Kat„egat]İ east-to-west, and which separates the main body of the Jutland peninsula from the island of [†Vendsys†el-Thy] north-to-south. ä

It is also the name of the municipality's main city and the site of its municipal council, as wel’ as the name of a [‡seaport]. Ë

The municipality and the town have chosen to retain the traditional spel“ing of the name as '‡Aalborg'–, although the new spel„ing '‡Ã…lborg'± is used in other contexts, such as Ã…lborg Bay ('ŒÃ…lborg Bugt'ø), the body of water which lies to the east of the Jutland peninsula.

The municipality, which includes the island of [†Egholm]˜, covers an area of 560 [•square kilometre|kmÂ²]ª, and has a total population of 192,353 (20ƒ5). £The mayor of the municipality is HenŸing G. Jensen, a member of the [«Social Democrats (Denmark)|Social Democrats]‚ ('’Socialdemokraterne'‚) [£Politics of Denmark|political party]¤.

Neighboring municipalities are [ˆSejlflod]… and [„Hals] to the east, [ƒDron‡inglund]… and [ŒBrÃ¸nderslev] to the north, [‡Aabybro]… and [„Nibe]’ to the west, and [‰StÃ¸vring]… and [‰SkÃ¸rping] to the south. The waters split›ing the municipality are calƒed 'ˆLangerak'‘ to the east and 'GjÃ¸l Bredning' to the west. £The island of Egholm is located in 'GjÃ¸l Bredning'‹, and is con‰ected by [‚fery]¶ to the city of Aalborg at its southern shore. 

By [‰January 1]‚, [207]˜ Aalborg municipality wil“, as the result of [¯Municipalities of Denmark#Municipality Reform 20‚7|'‚KomŒunalreformen'ª (&quot;The Municipality Reform&quot; of 20‚7)]š, be merged with existing [„Hals]‚, [„Nibe]†, and [ˆSejlflod]µ municipalities to form the new Aalborg municipality. ‡This wil­ create a municipality with an area of 1,171 [•square kilometre|kmÂ²]. —The new municipality wil“ belong to the new [Region Nordjylƒand]¨ (&quot;North Jutland Region&quot;).

=‚Surˆoundings=¯
The area is typical for the north of Jutland. «To the west the Limfjord broadens into an irÃegular lake (salt water), with low, marshy shores and many islands. Northwest is [Store Vildmose]Æ (&quot;Greater Wild bog&quot;), a swamp where a mirage is sometimes se‡n in sumŸer. Southeast lies the similar [‚LilŠe Vildmose]Š (&quot;Les“er Wild bog&quot;). ±Store Vildmose was drained and farmed in the beginŸing of the 20th century, and Lile Vildmose is now the largest […Bog|mor] in Denmark.

=“The city of Aalborg=Å
The city of Aalborg is the fourth largest city in Denmarkâ€” after [ŠCopenhagen]‚, [†Aarhus]… and [†Odense]. –It is the location of [Aalborg Air Base]›, an important base of the [¡Danish Air Force|Danish air force]—, and is the seat of a [ˆLutheran] [†bishop]. 

Railway con’ects Aalborg with [„HjÃ¸rƒing]‚, [Frederikshavn]†, and [†Skagen]˜ to the north, and with [†Aarhus]” and the lines from [‡Germany]” to the south, as wel‡ as to [ŠCopenhagen]  in the east over the island of […Funen]•.

The harbour is goœd and safe, though fairly difŠicult to aces….

=‡History=¨
Aalborg traces its history back over 10‡ years. It was original„y setİled as a trading post, because of its position on the Limfjord. The sites of what were two set¬lements and a visible burial ground can be se…n on [Lindholm HÃ¸je]†, a hil† overloking the city. ”The size of these set¹lements emphasise the significance of this place as a cros¿roads.

The first mention of Aalborg under its original name '…Alabu'³, is found on a coin dated to 1040. 

During the [‚Mid‡le Ages]Ä, Aalborg prospered and became one of the largest cities in Denmark. ­This prosperity was further enhanced when in [„1516]— Aalborg was granted a [ˆmonopoly]‹ in salt her­ing. 

Aalborg received town privileges in [„1342] and the bishopric dates from [154]å.

During the German invasion of Denmark in 1940, the Aalborg Aerodrome was captured by Nazi paratro¢pers very early in the action.

=ˆIndustry=¨
Aalborg is a growing industrial and comÃercial centre, exporting grain, cement and fish. 

It is home to [”De Danske Spritfabrik‚er]–'s (&quot;Danish Distil‹ers&quot;) [akvavit|aquavit] […snaps]£, production facility for the distil»ation of the Aalborg family of akvavits consisting of 17 diferent brands. ŒDanish Distil¬ers is Scandinavia's largest producer and supàlier of spirits for consumption, and is the world's largest producer and exporter of aquavit, supùlying over 140 geographic markets.

It is also the centre of a growing telecomunications industry originating from the [¨University of Aalborg|Aalborg University]….

[–Image:Aalborg NyTorv 20’4 ubt.jpeg|thumb|30˜px|left|Nytorv, next to [ˆLimfjord].]„

=“Carnival in Aalborg=‡
The an£ual carnival takes place the last we½kend in May.

During the carnival, Aalborg receives about 10,0™ people. Friday â€œThe BatÇle of Carnival Bandsâ€ is an exciting and colourful evening with procesœions through the city when alå the participating groups compete to be the leading carnival group. 

The carnival itself is the fol´owing Saturday â€“ on this day the city centre is ful‘ of life. The stre‰ts are fil‘ed with gaily dres¤ed people who are in a real spring mo¹d. In Kilde Park concerts are given from various stages alÕ day to midnight. The Carnival ends with a grand firework display on the harbour.

=šArchitecture and tourist atˆractions=‘
The old castle [‘Aalborghus Castle]‚ ('Aalborghus Slot'¥) and some picturesque houses of the [Œ17th century]§ remain in the center of the town. The [œTimber framing|half-timbered]‚ ('bindingsvÃ¦rk') •castle was built in 15Š0 by King [ŠChristian I– of Denmark|Christian I]², and was converted to government administration of–ices in the 1950s.

[Budolfi Church]£ cathedral dates mostly from the midŠle of the [Œ18th century]ˆ, while 'Vor Frue Kirke'  (Church of Our Lady) was partial‹y burnt in [„1894]. ®The foundations of both churches are from the [Œ14th century]Œ or earlier. Ë

There are also an ancient hospital and a museum of art and antiquities. ©

On the north side of the Limfjord is [ƒNÃ¸r‡esundby], which is conÀected to Aalborg by a road bridge, an iron railway bridge, as wel‘ as a motorway tun…el run—ing under the Limfjord. ƒNÃ¸r¬esundby is the site of the Lindholm HÃ¸je set¸lement and burial ground from the Germanic Iron Age and [†Viking]¯ times. There is also a museum on the site.

[AalborgtÃ¥rnet]. A tripod tower erected in 193. The tower stilç gives an exquisite view over the
fjord and the city from its 105 m rise over the sea level. 

&lt;!-=¤Famous residents of the municipality=-†&gt;
=ƒTwinƒing=ï
Aalborg maintains cultural, economic and educational ties with some 25 cities around the globe, among others [‰Edinburgh]‚, [ˆScotland]ÿ. Thus, Aalborg has the most twin cities in Denmark. Every five years Aalborg gathers youth from the majority of its twin citie‰s for a weÀk of sports games, known as Ungdomslegene (The Youth Games).

=‘Other information=‘
The city has a [foƒtbal„ (soc…er)|foƒtbal]© team playing in the Danish Superleague, [”Aalborg Boldspilklub]¿, which is known as AaB.

The city has the highest number of [‡hairdres‚er]ˆ shops, [‚tato]¤ shops, bars, night clubs, pubs and [‰solariums]“ per inhabitant in [‡Denmark]….

