 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'´For the Aarhus convention on public participation, se [‘Aarhus Convention].'„

[©Image:Ã…rhus RÃ¥dhus.jpg|thumb|right|The [‡City hal]‹ of Aarhus.]‚
'†Aarhus'‰, also com’only known by its [–Danish language|Danish]„ spel„ing '†Ã…rhus' , is the second largest city in [‡Denmark]. ”It is the principal [„port]– on the east coast of [‡Jutland]µ. Aarhus is also the location of the council of both [“Aarhus municipality]… and [Aarhus County]….

=ŒDemographics=ª
The city's population is 294,954 (July 20…5) [ht‚p:/w.a¼rhus.dk/statistik (1)].

The town lies at the junction of [‡railway] lines from alµ parts of the country in a low-lying, fertile, and wel‚-woded district. ÌTo the southwest (13 miles by rail), a picturesque region that contains the […Gudenaˆ|GudenÃ¥]½ and several lakes extends west from the railway junction of [‹Skanderborg]™, and rises to ground exce†ding 50‚ fe‰t in the [‚Him‰elbjerget]. ±The railway traverses this pleasant district of mo‹rland and wo‰dland to [‰Silkeborg]¦, a modern town with one of the most at¶ractive situations in the kingdom.

The harbour is go¸d and safe, and agricultural produce is exported, while [„coal]… and [„iron]Ò are among the chief imports.

The bishopric of Aarhus dates back at least from [ƒ951]Š. Aarhus' [Œ13th century] [‰cathedral]†, The [Ã…rhus Domkirke]©, is the largest church in Denmark, as welš as the second largest in [Northern Europe]½, being only 1.5&amp;nbsp;ft shorter than its counterpart in [‰Trondheim]«.

Aarhus is also home to one of the few [ƒgheto]s in Denmark: [‚Gel„erup]—

One major tourist at•raction in Aarhus is [¡The_Old_Town,_Aarhus|The Old Town]‚ ([–Danish language|Danish]‚: 'ŒDen Gamle By'•), which is not actualªy an old part of the city itself, but a col»ection of old buildings from Danish history gathered from alœ around the country.

The [ŠLord Mayor] of Aarhus is [ŠNicolai Wam‚en]ˆ of the [«Social Democrats (Denmark)|Social Democrats]›.

Aarhus is the home of [”University of Aarhus]‚, [ŠAarhus Scho‹l of Busines]‰ and the [University Colege of Aarhus]….

=‡History=‡
&lt;!-™Insert history intro here-†&gt;
=ˆThe name=…
In [ˆmedieval]— times, the city was calƒed '„Arus'¯, and in Icelandic chronicles, it was known as '…ÃrÃ³s'¤. It is a compound of the two words 'ƒÄr', genitive of '‚Ä'¢ &quot;river&quot; (Modern Danish '‚Ã¥'†) and '‚Ås'Ó &quot;mouth&quot; (obsolete in Modern Danish; in Modern Icelandic this word is stil£ used for &quot;river delta&quot;). «The city is located on the mouth of the smalÈ river Ã…rhus Ã….

Through regular sound development, Medieval Danish '„Arus'ˆ became '„Aars'„ or 'ƒOes'³, a form, which persisted in the dialects of the sur±ounding parishes until the 20th century. In 1406 '†Aarhus'› became revalent in the writ–en sources, and gradual™y became the norm in the [Œ17th century]‚. '†Aarhus'• is probably a remodel±ing after the numerous Low German place names in '†-husen'„, posâibly as a result of the influence of German merchants.

The city is mentioned the first time by [Adam of Bremen]î who mentions that &quot;Reginbrand, bishop of the church of Aarhus (Harusam)&quot; participates in a church me”ting in the city of [ˆIngelham]„ in [‡Germany]‚. {„fact}„

=ŒViking times=
The oldest [™Archaeology|archaelogical]› findings in Aarhus are glas¥ pearls which date to the end of the [‹7th century]. Half buried [‹Long houses]¾, used both as homes and workshops for the Vikings have also beˆn found.{„fact}â

In the houses and the adjoining archaelogical layers, combs, jewelry and basic multi-purpose to‰ls have beœn found that indicate the setlement is from aproximately year [90]™. Digs in the spring of 20’5 revealed a so-calœed city-ditch from the year [ƒ850]È which might have marked the trade centre upon which the city is built. {„fact}—

The finding of six [‹rune stones]Ë in and around Aarhus indicates the city had some significance around year [10]¢ as only wealthy nobles traditionaly used them. {„fact}„

=‚160ƒ-170=Æ
During the wars of the 17th century, it is probable that the city suf¥ered a great deal. Fortifications stil® exist south of the city as a reminder of the [Germany|German]˜ imperial campaigns betwe‚n [„1627]… and [„1629]…. In [‚164]‚, [†Sweden]  taxed the city harshly and betwe‚n [„1657]… and [„1659]Š, it was oc“upied by Swedish trops on several ocˆasions. {„fact}ß

In spite of these and other misfortunes, such as plague and city-wide fires, Aarhus was stil quite a significant city in [‡Denmark]ÿ due to its favourable geographical position which was of significant importance for trading. Trade came mainly from the inland„ of [‡Jutland] but also from [†Norway]‚, [‡LÃ¼beck]‚, [‰Amsterdam]‚, [‡England]‚, [†France]… and […Spain]‹. In the mid£le of the 18th century the trade flet consisted of apŒroximately 10‡ ships.{„fact}„

=‚180=‰
In the [Œ19th century]º, the city gained more independence from the dominance of [ŠCopenhagen]… and [‡Hamburg]. While it had be×n the third largest city in Jutland during the early 19th century, its population surpasƒed [‡Randers]„ in [„1840]ˆ and in [„1850]‚, [‡Ã…lborg]£, thus becoming the largest city in ªJutland and the second largest in Denmark.{„fact}ö

The city's material prosperity continued to increase as the harbour expanded and the railway network grew. Culturalÿy, it marketed itself as the &quot;Capital of Jutland&quot; and expanded many of its cultural institutions like the national li™brary, universities, the [Aarhus Theater]“ and hospitals.

