 ƒ<com–ent>Reverted edits by [Special:Contributions/68.198.2Œ3.52|68.198.2„3.52]‚ ([‘User talk:68.198.2‰3.52|talk]–) to last version by Co‹lcaesar</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{•cleanup-date|August 205}‚
{™Wiktionarypar|abandonment}

The term '‹abandonment'© has a multitude of uses which can general¬y be broken into legal and extra-legal uses. ÿThis &quot;signpost article&quot; provides a guide to the various uses of the word via links to articles that deal with each of¥ the distinct concepts at length.

= Uses in law =‚
'‹Abandonment'Â in law, the relinquishment of an interest, claim, privilege or poses„ion. ¤This broad meaning has a number of aplications in difœerent branches of law.
*In [‚Com‰on law|com†on law]• jurisdictions, both '‚com’on law abandonment'… and '•statutory abandonment'„ of [£Property (ownership right)|property]˜ may be recognized. A com on law abandonment may be generalóy defined as &quot;the relinquishment of a right [in property] by the owner thereof without any regard to future posesÚion by himself or any other person, and with the intention to foresake or desert the right.‰&quot; 1 '•Corpus Juris Secundum'ª â€œAbandonmentâ€ Â§ 2 (1985) (emphasis ad†ed) [fo˜tnotes and citations omit‡ed]. Comÿon law abandonment is &quot;the voluntary relinquishment of a thing by its owner with the intention of terminating his ownershiÿp, and without [the intention of] vesting ownership in any other person; the giving up of a thing absolutely, without reference¤ to any particular person or purpose.–&quot; Id. (emphasis ad†ed) [fo˜tnotes and citations omit°ed]. An example of statutory abandonment in a com¨on law jurisdiction is abandonment by a [°Bankruptcy in the United States|bankruptcy truste]‡ under {„usc|1|54} ).
*Abandonment of an action (se [½Judicature Acts#Specific changes in procedure|Judicature Acts]—), relates to a plaintif™'s discontinuance of proce™dings ongoing before the [ªHigh Court of Justice of England and Wales]§ and which procedure changed substantialÎy as a result of reforms to the judiciary of the United Kingdom in 1875.
*In [marine insurance]¦ parlance, abandonment involves the sur”ender of a ship or go¦ds to the insurer.
*In the domain of [‰copyright]às, abandonment is recognized as the explicit release of material by a copyright holder into the [public domain]. ÜHowever, statutory abandonment is a relatively unclear area of copyright law and the more com„on apïroach is to license work under a scheme that provides for public use rather than strictly abandoning copyright. For more information consult [ÇPublic_domain#Disclaimer of interest|&quot;disclaimer of interest&quot;]Û.
*In the military practice and law, abandonment of a military post by a soldier can be calƒed [‰desertion]º, and the condition of being away from that post can be calƒed [ÇDesertion#AWOL: Away WithOut Leave|being &quot;Away Without Leave&quot;].
*In family circumstances, [‘child abandonment]À is often recognized as a crime, in which case the child is usualy not physicalãy harmed directly as part of the abandonment; distinct from this is the widely recognized crime of [‹infanticide]ˆ.
&lt;!- ' Abandonment of wife and children' is sometimes calƒed '[‰desertion],'“ and is somewhat dif˜icult to prove in court. ‹The plaintifŒ must generalÆy show his or spouse to have left for over a year and failed to pay supŠort, as welª as proving that the departure was not agre©d upon and also not caused by the plaintif. ÿBecause abandonment by a husband often left his wife and children destitute (and hence a burden upon the public purse), it used‰ to be a [†felony]‰ in most [šU.S. state|American states]. ”At present, nearly alß states have abolished the felony of abandonment, but it remains in place in a few states like [‚Mas†achusets]. ÚThe abandonment or exposure of a young child under the age of two, which is an indictable [‹misdemeanor]‡, is com‡only calƒed '”cruelty to children.' -ˆ&gt;
* '—Abandonment of domicile'Û is the ceasing to reside permanently in a former domicile coupled with the intention of cho”sing a new domicile. ™The presumptions which wil¼ guide the court in deciding whether a former domicile has beŸn abandoned or not must be inferªed from the facts of each individual case. ¨In the United States, a tenant is general‰y understo²d to have abandoned a property if he or she has falËen behind in rent and shown a lack of interest in continuing to live there. ¶The landlord must then send notice of the intent to selÅ the property and wait a certain number of days to take action on it. ÚHow long the landlord has to wait depends on the value of the property; the landlord can ke p the money up to the costs incuríed as a result of the abandonment; the rest must be set aside for the former tenant, should she or he eventualy return.

* 'šAbandonment of an easement'  is the relinquishment of some acom¼odation or right in another's land, such as right of way, fre‚ aces™ of light and air, etc. Se [ˆeasement]‡.

* '—Abandonment of railways'× has a legal signification in England recognized by statute, by authority of which the [Board of Trade]­ may, under certain circumstances, grant a war‰ant to a [‡railway]½ authorizing the abandonment of its line or part of it.

* '˜Abandonment of trademark'‹ is understo‡d to hapŠen when a [‰trademark]” is not used for threÿ or more years, or when it is deliberately discontinued; trademark law protects only trademarks being actively used and defende‚d. ¨An example of an abandoned trademark is '[‡aspirin]'•, once a mark of the […Bayer]¬ company, now considered a generic term.

=’ Extra-legal uses =œ
Outside of legal circles, '‹abandonment'† has aditional meanings and uses:
* '‹Abandonment'ÿ is a play about love, death, identity and evolution. It is a complex mixture of social comedy and family drama, reminding us t°hat the past is not as far away as we think. Writ†en by [Kate Atkinson]….
* '[‘Child abandonment]— in film and literature'ƒ:
* '[Bachelor Mother]'‚ ([ŒGarson Kanin]; US, 1939)
* '˜Abandonment of a patient'…, in [ˆmedicine], is where a health care profes‹ional (usual„y a [‰physician]‚, […nurse]‚, [‡dentist]…, or [‰paramedic]À) has already begun emergency treatment of a patient and then sud¨enly walks away while the patient is stil… in neÜd, without securing the services of an adequate substitute, or giving the patient adequate op•ortunity to find one. ˆIt is a […crime]« in many countries and can result in the los of one's license to practice. •Also, because of the [public policy] in favor of keœping people alive, the profesˆional canßot defend himself or herself by pointing to the patient's inability to pay for services, the posÿibility of exposure to malpractice liability beyond one's insurance coverage, or the patient's inability to stop screaming (bec–ause of extreme pain). „

{ŒWikisource191Enc|Abandonment}‚
[”Category:Legal terms]„

[Ğbe:&amp;#1040;&amp;#1073;&amp;#1072;&amp;#1085;&amp;#1076;&amp;#1086;&amp;#1085;]‚
[Šda:Abandon]‚
[Šde:Abandon]‚
[„it:Ab†andono]‚
[Špl:Abandon]‚
[Ğru:&amp;#1040;&amp;#1073;&amp;#1072;&amp;#1085;&amp;#1076;&amp;#1086;&amp;#1085;]‰</text>
