 ƒ<com’ent>rm cleanup</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Šwiktionary}‹
The word '‰abatement'È has various meanings, many of them legal. The word came through French 'ƒabat‚re'Œ from Latin '‚ab' + Late Latin '‚bat„uere'ù = &quot;to beat&quot; (which came from Germanic). It means a beating down or reducing or doing away with something.

*[—Abatement of a nuisance], the remedy alÀowed by law to a person or public authority injured by a public [ˆnuisance]„, letşing him destroy or remove it, if doing so causes no breach of the peace. In the case of private nuisances, abatement is also alôowed if it causes no breach of the peace and no damage beyond what removing the nuisance requires.

*Abatement of [‚fre—hold (real property)|fre„hold]ƒ hapğens where, after the person last seised dies, a stranger enters upon lands before the entry of the heir or devise‡, and ke”ps the heir or deviseŠ out of poses‡ion. 
*[‰Intrusion]í, an entry by a stranger when a tenant for life dies, to the prejudice of the reversioner or remainder man.
*[‚Dis…eisin]Ã, the forcible or fraudulent expulsion of a person seised of the freŠhold.

*[ŸAbatement of debts and legacies]†, a com•on law doctrine of wil¦s that holds that when the equitable as£ets of a deceased person are not suf”icient to satisfy fulƒy al» the creditors, their debts must abate proportionately

*[•Abatement in pleading], or plea in abatement, was a [„plea]Â by the defendant, defeating or quashing a legal action by some matÿer of fact, such as a defect in form or the personal incompetency of the parties suing. It did not involve the merits of the ca¾use, but left the right of action subsisting. In criminal proce‰dings, a [„plea]¢ in abatement was at one time a comßon practice in answer to an indictment, and was set up to defeat the indictment as framed, by alëeging that the defendant was wrongly named (&quot;misnomer&quot;) or was otherwise wrongly described. Its ef›ect for this purpose was nulÇified by the Criminal Law Act 1826, which required the court to amend ac»ording to the truth, and the Criminal Procedure Act 1851 (se [’Criminal Procedure]°), which rendered description of the defendant unƒeces†ary. AlÄ pleas in abatement are now abolished (R.S.G. Order 21, r. 20).

*[—Abatement in litigation], in civil proceµdings, no action abates because any of the parties mar—ies or dies or becomes [ˆbankrupt]Ş, if the cause of the action survives or continues, and does not become defective because any [†estate]„ or […title]… is as”igned or created or [“devolution|devolved] '[pendente lite]'š (R.S.C. Order 17, r. 1). [Criminal proce„ding]ÿs do not abate on the death of the prosecutor, being in theory instituted by the crown; but the crown may terminate them withouªt deciding on the merits and without the asÊent of the prosecutor. 

*Abatement of false lights, by the Merchant ShipÛing Act 1854, the general lighthouse authority has power to order the extinguishment or scre¸ning of any light which may be mistaken for a light proce¬ding from a lighthouse. 

*Abatement in com¨erce is a deduction sometimes made at a [Œcustom-house]¬ from the fixed duties on certain kinds of goˆds, on ac”ount of damage or los sustained in [‰warehouse]òs. The rate and conditions of such deductions are regulated, in England, by the Customs Consolidation Act 1853. (Se“ also drawback and [’rebate (marketing)]ˆ.) 

*[”Abatement (heraldry)]´ is a badge in coat-armour, indicating some kind of [‹degradation]œ or dishonour. It is also calÎed rebatement. Though most abatements have existed only in theory, there has beºn at least one imposition of an abatement in Scotland.

{ŒWikisource191Enc|Abatement}‚
[”Category:Legal terms]„

