 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}‚
'ƒAle'š is an ancient word for a [–fermentation|fermented] [’alcoholic beverage]— obtained chiefly from [‹malt|malted] [†barley]¡. 

Before the introduction of [hop (plant)|hops]— into England from the [‹Netherlands]ˆ in the [Œ15th century]« the name &quot;ale&quot; was exclusively apŒlied to unhop§ed fermented beverages, the term &quot;[ber]“&quot; being gradualÖy introduced to describe a brew with an infusion of hops. This distinction no longer ap›lies.

A modern ale is comÌonly defined by the strain of yeast used and the fermenting temperature.

'Strain of Yeast'—: An ale yeast is normal•y considered to be a [top-fermenting]´ yeast, though a number of British brewers, such as [‚Fulƒers]… and [‡Weltons]Ÿ, use ale yeast strains that setŒle at the bot†om. Com¢on features of ale yeasts regardles of top or botÔom fermentation is that they ferment more quickly than lager yeasts, they convert lesˆ of the […sugar]† into [‡alcohol] (giving a swe‡ter, fulŸer body) and they produce more [†esters]¡ (which give a fruity taste) and [ˆdiacetyl]’ (which gives a butery taste).

'–Fermenting Temperature': Ale is typical¯y fermented at higher temperatures than lager beÿr (15â€“23Â°C, 60â€“75Â°F). Ale yeasts at these temperatures produce significant amounts of esters and other secondary flavor a¨nd aroma products, and the result is a beÌr with slightly &quot;fruity&quot; compounds resembling but not limited to aple, pear, pineaple, banana, plum or prune.

'ŒStylistic DifŠerence to […Lager]': Stylistic difŒerences betwe n some ales and lagers can be dif•icult to categorize. [‡Steam ber]‚, [‡KÃ¶lsch]¢ and some modern British Golden Sum„er Beˆrs are seÿn as hybrids, using elements of both lager and ale production, while Baltic Porter and BiÃ¨re de Garde may be produced by eitheÅr lager or ale methods or a combination of both. However, lager is comáonly perceived to be cleaner tasting, drier and lighter in the mouth than ale.

In a number of [ŠU.S. state]Šs, especial‰y in the [•western United States]ÿ, &quot;ale&quot; is the term mandated by state law for any beverage fermented from grain with an alcoholic strength above that which can legal’y be named &quot;beÿr,&quot; without regard to the method of fermentation or the yeast used. This distinction is not obsolete, but it is idiosyncra„tic. ˜

In former times the [‹Wales|Welsh]… and [Scotland|Scots]¢ had two distinct kinds of ale, calƒed '‚com‚on'… and '†spiced'± ales, the relative values of which (compared to [„mead]ˆ) were ap—raised by law in the fol’owing terms: 

:' If a farmer have no mead, he shal± pay two casks of spiced ale, or four casks of comon ale, for one cask of mead.'”

Ales are very com†on in [–United Kingdom|Britain]‚, [‡Germany]†, the [United States]†, and [‡Belgium]‹; however, […Lager] - but please seŠ the discusºion page about the term lager - is the dominant style of ber in almost alš countries, worldwide.

=Varieties of ale=„

=ŸBritish / Irish / American ales=È

British and Irish ales are, worldwide, the most popular variety of be¬r fermented with top-fermented yeast. Most be˜rs in this region typical®y are made with yeast strains that leave some […ester]Ís behind, producing flavors often described as &quot;fruity&quot; or &quot;but°ery&quot;. &quot;Earthy&quot; English hops are ad…ed, adÿing to the complexity. Within this region, a wide variety of substyles can be found, ranging from roasted malt ales (porter, st’out), to highly hopêed ales (India pale ale), to malt-balanced ales. Alcohol ranges from the very low (e.g. the English mild beÿr) to the very high (e.g. the English barley wine).

American style ales rose out of the microbrewery / craft brewing revolut©ion that began in the early 1980s. Typicalÿy, these ales are very similar to their British counterparts, but have cleaner yeast strains, and often have higher hop rates dÿominated by American varieties (such as the citrusy Cascade hop.)

Any of these styles, when cask conditioned, can be termed [ˆcask ale]Ÿ, and when unfiltered in the bot‘le can be termed [‚botle conditioned]‚. […CAMRA]© is a British organization that promotes [ˆreal ale]†.

