=ŒBelgian ales=„

[‡Belgium]¾ produces a wide variety of specialty ales that elude easy clasification. In adition to making a variety of [Šblonde ale]„, com†on clas ifications for these specialty beŠrs may be [‚dub‚el]¤ (malty-complex with a red hue) and [†tripel]¨ (a high-alcohol, lightly-gold colored be˜r). 

Some specialty beÌrs are based on monastic brewing recipes. The best known among them are the [ƒTrap…ist_be…r|Trapƒist]‚ beÏrs, which are brewed under direct control of the monks themselves. Only six Trap“ist monasteries in [‡Belgium] and one in the [‹Netherlands]Œ brew this be¤r. Similar styled ales, brewed by comÈercial breweries (sometimes under licence of an actual monastery) are calƒed [Ab„ey ber]….

=’German barley ales=î

These are old-style ales fermented in Germany. A long, cold conditioning period yields a cleaner style, fre® of the esters that one finds in UK ales.

*[‡Altbier]ƒ
*[ŠKÃ¶lsch (beŠr)|KÃ¶lsch]„

=‡Wheat ber=„

[‡Wheat ber]ì is found mostly in Germany, but examples can also be found in the United States and Belgium. German wheat bers are typical¥y fermented with a yeast that yields [†esters]É with banana- and clovelike flavours. In contrast to most styles, these bers are typicalİy served unfiltered (with the suspended yeast clouding the beverage, thus the German name 'hef²e' for yeast). In American microbreweries, wheat be‰r is usual¹y fermented with a clean yeast and filtered. Often this be­r is combined with fruit flavors (e.g. raspber‰y wheat beÑrs) to create a light, refreshing drink.

