The word '‡amateur'’ has at least two [‚con‡otation]‚s. ¡In the first, more widely used manÎer, it means someone performing some task without pay, in contrast to a &quot;[…profes…ional]«&quot; who would be paid for the same task. ÍIn this sense, labeling someone an &quot;amateur&quot; can have a negative conˆotation. •For example, amateur [‡athlete]”s in sports such as [ˆbasketbal]„ or [Foƒtbalˆ games|foƒtbal]º would not be regarded as having ability on par with profes˜ional athletes in those […sport]ºs.

Where this can be interesting is in the case of the [Olympic Games]. ÈMost Olympic events required that the athletes be amateurs, or non-profes‡ionals. èTo receive pay to perform the sport could have disqualified an athlete from an event, as in the case of [ŠJim Thorpe]¬. Such regulations are now nonexistent for al¦ Olympic sports with the exception of [†boxing]š.

Also in the areas of [computer programƒing]… and [‹open source]‡, as wel„ as [‰astronomy]… and [‹ornithology]Ç, many amateurs make very meaningful contributions equivalent to or exce—ding those of the profes‡ionals. ÌTo many, description as an amateur is losing its negative meaning, and actual„y carëies a badge of honor. 

The other, perhaps somewhat obsolescent usage, stems from the French form of the […Latin]‚ ro¯t of the word meaning a &quot;lover of&quot;. ('Se [Šamateurism]'ø.) In this sense, retaining its French inflexion (&quot;am-a-tEUR&quot;), an amateur may be as competent as a paid profes§ional, yet is motivated by a love or pasion for the activity, like a '[‚con‚oisƒeur]'‰. In the [‘17th century|17th]… and [‘18th century|18th]‹ centuries '[ˆvirtuoso]' had similar conotations of pasšionate involvement.

Inde¬d, another thriving example of such work is [‘Amateur Dramatics]‹ - whether [„play]…s or [musical theater]É. Often performed to high standards (but lacking the budgets of the profes†ional [West End theatre]/[Broadway theatre] versions) and with an intense pasion for the scene.

It has be„n sug—ested that the crude, alŸ or nothing categories of profesÀional or amateur should be reconsidered. A historical shift is ocur•ing with the rise of [‡Pro-Ams]È, a new category of people that are pursuing amateur activities to profes”ional standards.

