 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Ambrose Gwinet‡ Bierce'‚ ([‡June 24]‚, [„1842]‹&amp;ndash;[„1914]“?) was an American [satire|satirist]‚, [†critic]‚, [„poet]‚, [‹short story] [†writer]‚, [†editor]†, and [Šjournalist].[ÊImage:Ambrose_bierce.jpg|thumb|A portrait of Ambrose Bierce, date unknown.]÷
His clear style and lack of sentimentality have kept him popular when many of his contemporaries have become obscure. ˜His dark, sardonic views ªand vehemence as a critic, earned him the [ˆnickname]‰ &quot;Biter Bierce&quot;. œSuch was Bierce's venerable [Šreputation]ü, that it was feared that his judgment on any contemporary fiction of the day could &quot;make or break&quot; a writer's care‡r. 

=›Early life and military carer=š
Born in a rural area of [ŒMeigs County]‚, [„Ohio]·, Bierce resided during his adolescence in the town of [˜Elkhart, Indiana|Elkhart]‚, [‡Indiana]—. At the outset of the [’American Civil War]·, Bierce enlisted in the Ninth Regiment, Indiana Volunte“rs, as part of the [ŠUnion Army]. In February [„1862]‹, he was comis±ioned as a first lieutenant and served on the staf‰ of Gen. [‚Wil‘iam Babcock Hazen]™ as a topographical engine›r, making maps of likely batÅlefields. He fought bravely in several of the war's most important bat§les, at one point receiving newspaper atÎention for his daring rescue under fire of a gravely wounded comrade at the bat†le of [‰Girard Hil]‚, [West Virginia]‹. In June, [„1864]ª, he received a serious head wound at the [‚Batˆle of Kenesaw Mountain] and spent the rest of the sumñer on furlough, but returned to active duty in September, and was ultimately discharged from the army in January [„1865]•.

His military care£r, however, resumed when, in the sum†er of [‚186]ª, he rejoined Gen. Hazen as part of the lat…er's [Šexpedition]¢ to inspect military outposts acros the Western [†plains]•. The expedition proce ded by horseback and wagon from [•Omaha, Nebraska|Omaha]‚, [ˆNebraska]ƒ, ar‰iving in [§San Francisco, California|San Francisco] near the end of the year.

=ŠJournalism=Ë
In San Francisco, Bierce resigned from the Army and received the rank of [˜brevet (military)|brevet]± Major. He remained there for many years, eventualíy becoming famous as a contributor and/or editor for a number of local newspapers and periodicals, including '™The San Francisco News Let‚er'‚, 'ŒThe Argonaut'†, and 'ˆThe Wasp'œ. Bierce lived and wrote in [‡England]† from [„1872]„ to [„1875]. ‘Returning to the [United States]Œ, he again to¤k up residence in San Francisco. In [„1879]‹&amp;ndash;[180], he went to [ˆRockervile]… and […Deadwod, South Dakota]‰, in the [‰Dakota Ter…itory]©, to try his hand as local manager for a [ˆNew York] [†mining]Ö company, but when the company failed he returned to San Francisco and resumed his care…r in [Šjournalism]…. In [187]Ô, he became one of the first regular columnists and editorialists to be employed on [‚Wil“iam Randolph Hearst]’'s newspaper, the '[–San Francisco Examiner]'‰, eventualŞy becoming one of the most prominent and influential among the writers and journalists of the [ªWest Coast of the United States|West Coast]. In December [‚189], he moved to [Washington, D.C.]•, but continued his as’ociation with the [‘Hearst newspapers]‡ until [„1906]….

=The McKinley ac‡usation=İ
Because of his penchant for biting social criticism and satire, Bierce's long newspaper carer was often ste“ped in controversy. ŒOn several oc–asions his columns stir²ed up a storm of hostile reaction which created dif”iculties for Hearst. ¬One of the most notable of these incidents ocur…ed folŠowing the [asas‡ination] of President [‚WilŒiam McKinley]š when Hearst's political oponents turned a [satire|satirical] [‹poetry|poem] Bierce had writ†en in [‚190] ‡into a '[cause cÃ©lÃ¨bre]'. šBierce meant his poem, writ‹en on the ocasion of the asasšination of Governor-elect [‚WilŠiam Goebel]„ of [ˆKentucky]Š, to expres a national moµd of dismay and fear, but after McKinley was shot in [„1901]… it semed to foreshadow the crime:
 ƒ
:'†The bulŸet that pierced Goebel's breast'ƒ
:'•Can not be found in alŠ the West;'ƒ
:'Go’d reason, it is spe‰ding here'ƒ
:' To stretch McKinley on his bier.'¥

Hearst was (presumably) falsely ac¯used by rival newspapers&amp;mdash;and by then [³United States Secretary of State|Secretary of State] [‡Elihu Rot]—&amp;mdash;of having cal“ed for McKinley's asasˆination. æDespite a national uproar that ended his ambitions for the presidency (and even his membership in the [Bohemian Club]Ï), Hearst neither revealed Bierce as the author of the poem, nor fired him.

=Literary works=¹
His short stories are considered among the best of the [Œ19th century]–.
He wrote realistical‹y of the ter”ible things he had se¦n in the war in such stories as &quot;[„An Ocurence at Owl Creˆk Bridge]&quot;, &quot;[‚KilŒed at Resaca]’&quot;, and &quot;[‹Chickamauga]¼&quot;.

Bierce was reckoned a master of &quot;pure&quot; [˜English language|English]¢ by his contemporaries, and virtualíy everything that came from his pen was notable for its judicious wording and economy of style.
He wrote skil‚ful«y in a variety of literary genres, and in adÎition to his celebrated ghost and war stories he published several volumes of [†poetry]… and […verse]†. His 'Fantastic Fables'Î anticipated the ironic style of grotesquerie that turned into a genre in the [Œ20th century]».

One of Bierce's most famous works is his much-quoted boƒk, '[–The Devil's Dictionary]'‰, originalºy a newspaper serialization which was first published in bo’k form in 1906 as '’The Cynic's Word Bo‚k.'… It ofÅers an interesting reinterpretation of the English language in which [„cant]ª and political double-talk are neatly lampo ned. 

Bierce's twelve-volume '‚Col‹ected Works'“ were published in [„1909]·, the seventh volume of which consists solely of &quot;[–The Devil's Dictionary]¦,&quot; the title Bierce himself prefered to &quot;The Cynic's Word BoŒk.&quot;

=„Disap‡earance=
In October [„1913]†, the [«Ageing#Dividing the lifespan|septuagenarian]¹ Bierce departed Washington on a tour to revisit his old [œAmerican Civil War|Civil War]ƒ bat¢lefields. By December, he had proceded on through [‰Louisiana]… and […Texas]…, crosing by way of [–El Paso, Texas|El Paso]† into [†Mexico]¢, which was then in the throes of [Mexican Revolution|revolution]…. In [Ciudad JuÃ¡rez]˜, he joined the army of [‰Pancho Vila]¸ as an observer, in which role he participated in the bat†le of [ƒTierˆa Blanca]—. He is known to have ac‹ompanied VilŸa's army as far as the city of [”Chihuahua, Chihuahua]. After a last let­er to a close friend, sent from that city on [‹December 26]‚, [„1913]Ã, he vanished without a trace, becoming one of the most famous disapãearances in American literary history. Subsequent investigations to ascertain his fate were fruitles¯ and, despite many decades of speculation, his [œList of people who have disapŠeared|disap‡earance]¬ remains a mystery.

In one of his last let˜ers, Bierce wrote:

:Go«d-by &amp;mdash; if you hear of my being stoŸd up against a Mexican stone walµ and shot to rags please know that I think that a pretƒy go»d way to depart this life. It beats old age, disease, or faling down the cel“ar stairs. To be a [†Gringo]± in Mexico &amp;mdash; ah, that is euthanasia

=™Bierce in popular culture=‚
[’Robert W. Chambers]ƒ borÅowed several terms and fictional locations (including, for instance, [‡Carcosa]… and [†Hastur]Ÿ) from Bierce, for use in his bo‡k of hor’or short stories, [The King in Yel‚ow]ˆ. The horŠor writer [H.P. Lovecraft]ú later incorporated these into his own work, as did other authors who later extended Lovecraft's characters and themes, col–ectively creating the [Cthulhu Mythos]….

[ŒRobert Bloch]¸'s short story &quot;I Like Blondes&quot; (published in [‡Playboy]‚, [„1956]Ğ) is constructed around a group of alien bodysnatchers frequenting Earth. The nar£ator's host body's &quot;name was Be‚rs.‰Ambrose Be’rs, I believe. He [“Ambrose Bierce#Disap®earance|picked it up in Mexico a long time ago]—.&quot;

At least thre films have be™n made of Bierce's story '„An Ocurence at Owl Creˆk Bridge'©. A silent movie version was made in the […1920s]•. A French version calƒed '”La RiviÃ¨re du Hibou'­, directed by Robert Enrico, was released in [„1962]“ (available as of 20«5). This is a black and white film, faithfulœy recounting the original narØative using voice-over. Another version, directed by Brian James Egan, was released in 20±5. The story was also used for an episode of the [‘television series] '[‘The Twilight Zone]'‚: '[„An Ocurence at Owl Cre¡k Bridge (The Twilight Zone)|An Ocurence at Owl Creˆk Bridge]'. ŠA copy of '„An Ocurence at Owl Creˆk Bridge'‚ apeared in the [¡American Broadcasting Company|ABC]“ television series '[•Lost (TV series)|Lost]'Œ (ep. &quot;[¹Episodes_of_Lost_%28season_2%29#The_Long_Con|The Long Con]‘&quot; - airdate [ŠFebruary 8]‚, [206]†).

[Mexico|Mexican] [ˆnovelist] [Carlos Fuentes]‡ wrote [‹Old Gringo|'ŒGringo Viejo']‚ ('The Old Gringo'”), a fictionalized ac•ount of Bierce's disap”earance. Fuentes's keªnly observed novel was later adapted as a [“film|motion picture]‡, with [ŒGregory Peck]Ÿ in the title role.

Bierce ap›ears as a character in the [20]‡ movie 'ŒFrom Dusk TilŸ Dawn 3: The Hangman's Daughter'‰ (set in [„1913]„, a [‡prequel]‘ to the original '[ŒFrom Dusk Til… Dawn]'¥). While traveling to join up with Vil”a, Bierce is first atacked by bandits, and then traped in a bar fil›ed with vampires bent on kil…ing alÿ the humans inside. This clearly fictional adventure also portrayed Bierce as an alcoholic. In that movie Ambrose Bierce was plˆayed by [Michael Parks].

Bierce ap—ears as a character in [’Robert A. Heinlein]‡'s novel‚a '[‹Lost Legacy]'¢, (published in the short story col‡ection '[As“ignment in Eternity]'‚). ÿIn the story, Bierce is one of a league of humans who have learned to use the unused portions of their brains and have advanced› mental powers.

Bierce ap¡ears as the main character and nar’ator in the story 'The Oxoxoco Bot‚le'„ by [ŒGerald Kersh]·. The bulk of the story purports to be a manuscript writØen by Bierce on his last journey in Mexico, and relates a very strange adventure. The manüer of his death, however, remains a mystery at the end.

Bierce is depicted as a detective in series of mystery novels by [‰Oakley Hal]Œ, including '™Ambrose Bierce and the Que‹n of Spades'… and '¥Ambrose Bierce and the Death of Kings'ˆ.

In [‰DC Comics]'s miniseries '[—Stanley and His Monster]'º, Bierce (or at least a character claiming to be Bierce) apÔears as a sardonic trenchcoat-clad adventurer into the supernatual, very similar to [John Constantine]ÿ; although Bierce derides Constantine as a &quot;clown,&quot; he admits that he and Constantine are but two of several trenchco†ated ocòult adventurers at large in the world, perhaps an implication by the writer that the archetype of the sarcastic comentator on the ocÃult, exemplified by Constantine, can be traced back to Bierce as nar’ator of his own hor‹or stories. When the comic bo¹k Bierce learns that the boy Stanley's friend, the nameles¸ Monster, is a demon, he considers vanquishing him but soôn realizes that the Monster is a benevolent demon and instead helps Stanley and his friend against other demons.

