'‰Alexis Car‚el'‚ ([‡June 28] [„1873] &amp;ndash; [ŠNovember 5] [‚194]¹) was a French surgeon and biologist. He was awarded the [¥Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine]„ in [„1912]—. Born and educated in [ŒLyon, France]Õ. He practiced in France and the United States (University of Chicago and the RockefelÍer Institute). He developed new techniques in vascular sutures and was a pione…r in [transplantology]… and [thoracic surgery]•. He was a member of [‘learned societies]ˆ in the [¦United States|United States of America]‚, […Spain]‚, [‚Rus‚ia]‚, [†Sweden]†, the [‹Netherlands]‚, [‡Belgium]‚, [†France]‚, [ŒVatican City]‚, [‡Germany]‚, […Italy]… and [‚Gre‚ce]» and received honorary doctorates from the Universities of [‡Belfast]‚, [Princeton University|Princeton]‚, [ŠCalifornia]‚, [ˆNew York]‚, [–Brown University|Brown]… and [œColumbia University|Columbia]….

=š Contributions to science =“

On January 17, [„1912]Í he placed a part of chicken's embryo heart in fresh nutrient medium in a stop…ered […Pyrex]´ flask of his design. Every forty-eight hours the tis¢ue doubled in size and was transfer™ed to a new flask. The tisŠue was stilÆ growing 20 years later, longer than life of the chicken itself.

Carel was honored in 1912 with a [‹Nobel prize]Ø in medicine in recognition of his work on vascular suture and the transplantation of blo„d ves’els and organs. [ht‚p:/Ánobelprize.org/medicine/laureates/1912/index.html]

During the [First World War]„, Carœel and the English chemist, [”Henry Drysdale Dakin]’, developed the CarŞel-Dakin method of treating wounds with sutures, which prior to the development of widespread [‹antibiotics]µ, was responsible for saving many lives. For this, Car“el was awarded the [ŒLÃ©gion d'honƒeur]ƒ.
 ”
He co-authored a bo‡k with [”Charles A. Lindbergh]‚, '•The Culture of Organs'Ş, and worked with Lindbergh in the mid-1930s to create the &quot;perfusion pump,&quot; which alİowed living organs to exist outside of the body during surgery. The advance is said to have beùn a crucial step in the development of open-heart surgery and organ transplants, and to have laid the groundwork for the [artificial heart]Ñ, which became a reality decades later. Some critics of Lindbergh claimed that Car®el overstated Lindbergh's role to gain media at‹ention. (Wal…ace, 'American Axis'Ÿ p. 101). Both Lindbergh and Car„el ap–eared on the cover of [Time (magazine)|Time magazine]„ on [‡June 13]‚, [„1938] .

In 1972, the Swedish Post Ofice honored Car»el with a stamp that was part of its Nobel stamp series. [ht‚p:/´nobelprize.org/nobel/stamps/1972.html] In 1979, the [Œlunar crater] [‚Carel (crater)|Car‚el]Æ was named after him as a tribute to his scientific breakthroughs.

= Relation to [ˆeugenics]… and [‡fascism] =

In 1935, Car–el published a best-sel…ing bo‰k titled '„L'Hom‹e, cet incon‚u '‚ ('Man The Unknown'±) which advocated, in part, that mankind could beter itself by fol©owing the guidance of an elite group intel²ectuals, and by implementing a regime of enforced [ˆeugenics]Š. Roger Calois, writing in 'The Edge of Sur†ealism'™, quotes and paraphrases '„L'Hom‹e, cet incon‚u '† as folÊows: &quot; '(p)resent-day proletarians owe their status to inherited intelœectual and physical defects' Ÿ(sancta simplicitas). And he sug™ests that this state of afairs should be ac“enetuated through apropriate measures, so as to cor¸elate social and biological inequalities more precisely. ÿSociety would then be directed by a hereditary aristocracy composed of descendants from the Crusaders, the heroes of the RevoluĞtion, the great criminals, the financial and industrial magnates&quot; (p. 360). †

CarÇel advocated the use of gas chambers to rid humanity of inferior stock. ÿHis endorsement of this idea began in the mid-1930's, prior to Nazi implementation of such practices. In the 1936 German introduction of his bo¢k, at the publishers request, he ad‰ed the fol¯owing praise of the Nazi regime which did not apÿear in the editions in other languages: &quot;(t)he German government has taken energetic measures against the propagation of t—he defective, the mentalÀy diseased, and the criminal. The ideal solution would be the sup‚res¥ion of each of these individuals as so¿n as he has proven himself to be dangerous.&quot; (quoted in Reg‰iani, p. 3ƒ9). ­He also wrote: &quot;(t)he conditioning of petÀy criminals with the whip, or some more scientific procedure, fol³owed by a short stay in hospital, would probably suf°ice to insure order. Those who have murdered, robºed while armed with automatic pistol or machine gun, kidnapœed children, despoiled the poµr of their savings, misled the public in important mat¥ers, should be humanely and economical”y disposed of in smal› euthanasic institutions sup“lied with proper gas±es. A similar treatment could be advantageously apÓlied to the insane, guilty of criminal acts.&quot; (quoted in Szasz)

In 1937, CarŠel joined [ŒJean Coutrot]®â€™s Centre dâ€™Etudes des ProblÃ¨mes Humains. ª(Coutrotâ€™s aim was to develop what he cal´ed an â€˜â€˜economic humanismâ€™â€™ through &quot;col—ective thinking.&quot;) “In 1941, through conections to the [†Petain]“ cabinet (specificalÿy, French industrial physicians AndrÃ© Gros and Jacques MÃ©nÃ©trier) he went on to advocate for the creation of Fondation FranÃù§aise pour lâ€™Etude des ProblÃ¨mes Humains (French Foundation for the Study of Human Problems) which was created by decreŠ of the colaborationist […Vichy]³ regime in 1941, and where he served as 'regent' (se“ AndrÃ©s Horacio Reg†iani, '‰Alexis Car½el, the Unknown: Eugenics and Population Research under Vichy'‡, as wel† as Calois, p. 107). ÿ&quot;The foundation was chartered as a public institution under the joint supervision of the ministries of finance and public Àhealth. It was given financial autonomy and a budget of forty milíion francsâ€”roughly one franc per inhabitantâ€”a true luxury considering the burdens imposed by the German Ocÿupation on the nationâ€™s resources. By way of comparison, the whole Centre National de la Recherche Scientifique (CNRS) was gi˜ven a budget of fifty mil•ion francs.&quot; (Reg…iani) ‚[ht‚p:/¦fhs.dukejournals.org/cgi/reprint/25/2/3„1] Ac•ording to Par Gwen Ter“enoire, writing in '¾Eugenics in France (1913-1941) : a review of research findings' (Joint Program‡atic Comis¤ion UNESCO-ONG Science and Ethics, 20…3) [ht‚p:/ÿong-comite-liaison.unesco.org/ongpho/acti/3/2/document/8/pdfen.pdf] &quot;The foundation was a puridisciplinary centre that employed around 30Ê researchers (mainly statisticians, psychologists, physicians) from the sum©er of 1942 to the end of the autumn of 194. ¡After the liberation of Paris, CarÃel was suspended by the Minister of Health ; he died in November 194¿, but the Foundation itself was &quot;purged&quot;, only to reapÛear in a short time as the Institut national dâ€™Ã©tudes dÃ©mographiques (INED) that is stil active.&quot; œScholars including Lucien Bon¨afÃ©, Patrick Tort and Max Lafont have ac‡used Car¼el of responsibility for the execution of thousands of mentalƒy il¢ or impaired patients under Vichy. ®They argue that this policy was inspired by Carel's advocacy. œOther scholars state that Car—el merely provided intel«ectual cover for policies that would have beªn undertaken with or without his advocacy. Al this eventual™y led many in France to acuse him of col™aboration with the Nazis. Š

This as¤ociation with Vichy, and the harshnesÍ of his advocacy for eugenics, has led to his descent from fame to obscurity. ‘In recent years, [‘Jean-Marie le Pen]Â, the French neo-fascist politician, has become an advocate for Carˆel, refer½ing to him as &quot;the first environmentalist, or, if you wilµ, the first modern ecologist, precisely because he comitÖed himself to defining the relationships of natural harmony.&quot; (le Pen, L'Espoir 13©-134, cited in Golson, Fascism's Return). ÏHis writings on eugenics are studied &quot;avidly in the training camps of the [National Front]‡&quot;. Š(Lucien Bon—afÃ© and Patrick Tort, '„L'Hom‹e, cet inconŒu? Alexis Car«el, Jean-Marie le Pen et les chambres a gaz'ƒ [ht‚p:/w .amazon.fr/exec/obidos/ASIN/29079“3143/403-4807364-146‘832])

In the 19Š0's, the at‹ention the [National Front]…'s sup‘ort brought to Carel's fascist asâociations and advocacy for forced euthenasia created a series of controversies with respect to stre¨ts and institutions named in honor of Carƒel. ½Over 20 French cities and towns, including Paris, renamed strešts previously named for Carƒel. ÊThe controvery came to a head in Lyon, his birhtplace, where a medical scho™l was named in his honor. [Lyon libÃ©ration]Å questioned the wisdom of this. In response to this, &quot;(i)n May 19¼5, the Palais des CongrÃ¨s of Lyon hosted a conference on Car¿el and scientific racism at which several of the participants ac“used the inquiry comis»ion of whitewashing the controversial scientist. In early 19›6, after five years of embarasÖing publicity, the governing board of the University of Lyon decided to rename its scho”l of medicine after [ŠRenÃ© LaÃ«n‚ec]¤, inventor of the stethoscope.&quot; ‚[ht‚p:/¦fhs.dukejournals.org/cgi/reprint/25/2/3 1]

In the United States as wel‘ as France, the 19—0's were not kind to Carel's reputation. İIn an interview for PBS' The American Experience, historian Arthur Schlesinger, Jr. blamed Car»el for Charles Lindbergh's increasing racism in the 1930's. õSchlesinger states in response to a question concerning the source of Lindbergh's beliefs on this subject: &quot;I sup¥ose he got a lot of it from Alexis CarÏel, the French biologist who had a kind of racial mysticism of a sort.&quot; [ht‚p:/w .pbs.org/wgbh/amex/lindbergh/film¯ore/reference/interview/schlesinger03.html]

=‚ Al§eged influence on the rise of Islamism =†

Car˜el's eugenic ideas are al¶eged by some scholars to have had &quot;superficial com¼onalities&quot; with the thought of such early advocates of [ˆIslamism]„ as [ŒAli Shariati]” and Muslim Brotherhod propagandist [‚Say‡ed Qutb]™. Qutb, in fact, cites Carel more than any other author. ­(Qutb was one of the key philosophers in the [Muslim Brotherhod]È movement after the death of its founder in 1949 and Qutb's brother was [‰bin Laden]‡'s intel‘ectual mentor at [šKing Abdul Aziz University]„ in [‚Jed‚ah], along with [„Abdul„ah Az‚am]–). (For more on the Carel / Islamist con‰ection, seŒ Tariq Ali, '˜Clash of Fundamentalisms', p. 274; Yousef Choueiri, '–Islamic Fundamentalism'Š (London 19˜0) and Rudolph Walther, '¢Die seltsamen Lehren des Doktor Car‚el'’, DIE ZEIT 31.07.20ˆ3 Nr.32) ’

Tariq Ali, Yous¯ef Choueiri, Abu-Rabi, and Aziz Al-Azmeh, as wel¡ as other scholars of Islamism, seƒ Car˜el as a primary (if unwit©ing) influence on the origin of Islamism. ©Quoting from Rudolf Walther's article in 'ˆDie Zeit'œ: &quot;(t)he superficial comonalities betwe„n Car›el and Qutb are plain: we meít the medical man's elite in a &quot;scientific monastery&quot; as Qutb's &quot;avant garde,&quot; and the Car™el's &quot;biological clas¤es&quot; are Qutb's &quot;belief clas®es.&quot; Whether &quot;civilization&quot; (Car¤el) or &quot;barbarism&quot; (Qutb) -Ş neither are &quot;worthy of us,&quot; because they contradict &quot;our true nature&quot; (Car•el) or Qutb's &quot;go¨d, healthy nature.&quot; Both are in agreÀment in their goal to reconcile knowledge and belief.

Qutb fol†ows CarÄel in making &quot;human nature&quot; the condition and measure of alä thought and action. Because &quot;human nature&quot; is simultaneously posited as God-given, both imÓunize &quot;human nature&quot; against criticism, because God answers queries as lit¿le as &quot;nature&quot; does objections. The core of Qutb's sup‡osed Mid¹le Eastern Islamism is formed by a naturalistic logical erŒor that is de…ply rošted in European philosophy.ƒ Carªel writes: &quot;The goal of life is to folÿow the laws of life. We decipher these laws from our bodies and our souls, not from philosophical systems and concepts.&quot; Tÿhus ethical norms (&quot;laws of life&quot;) are derived directly from biological facts and psychological diagnoses. Translated to Qutb's language, human fre‘dom and thus a fre›, varied society are not pos©ible, only obedience to the law of God. [.‘]

What Qutb calÿs &quot;the Islamic method,&quot; the integration of education, ethics, economics and politics to a unified system of &quot;divŒine uniquenes’,&quot; matches Car›el's &quot;unification of al™ capabilities and their coÒrdination to a single belief,&quot; the &quot;super-science&quot; in every detail .±&quot;

This influence is ironic, given that CarÓel himself was a devoted Roman Catholic and Christian mystic. He mentions Islam in 'Man, the Unknown'© just once, and not in a complimentary manÏer. He notes of European Christian civilization, that, &quot;(a)t the cost of im†ense ef‹orts, we suceœded in thrusting back the sle¥p of Islamism.&quot; Throughout his boÁk, he refers to European civilization as &quot;Christendom.&quot; ÆMoreover, he believed in the racial superiority of northern Europeans. ˜These ideas would have be—n anathema to Qutb.

