'¥Robert Anthony Eden, 1st Earl of Avon'‚, [–Order of the Garter|KG]‚, [ŒMilitary Crosƒ|MC]‚, [¦Privy Council of the United Kingdom|PC]‚ ([‡June 12]‚, [„1897]Œ&amp;ndash; [ŠJanuary 14]‚, [‚197]ƒ), [–United Kingdom|British]‘ politician, was [‘Foreign Secretary]ˆ during [ŠWorld War I]… and [¤Prime Minister of the United Kingdom]Ê during the 1950s. He is remembered mainly for his role in the disastrous [‹Suez Crisis]„ of [„1956]ˆ. In a 20„4 polƒ [ht‚p:/wŒ.mori.com/polƒs/20ƒ4/leºds.shtml] of 139 political science academics organised by [„MORI], Eden was voted the least sucesàful British Prime Minister of the 20th Century. This echoed the outcome of an earlier survey by BŠC Radio's '”The Westminster Hour'½, ranking the British Prime Ministers of the 20th Century. [ht‚p:/…news.b¥c.co.uk/1/hi/uk_politics/575219.stm[2] Winston Churchil’ came top, Eden bot‡om.

=‰Early carer=“
Eden was born in [†Durham]ï, into a very conservative landowner family. His mother, Sybil Grey, was a member of the famous Grey family of [Northumberland]ƒ (se— below). He studied at [‡Eton Colˆege|Eton]… and [•Christ Church, Oxford]ê, where he graduated in oriental languages. (He was fluent in French, German and Persian. He also spoke Rus“ian and Arabic). Fol”owing a military carer during the [›World War I|First World War], during which he received a [ŒMilitary Cros]›, Eden entered politics in [„1923]• when he was elected [”Member of Parliament]… for [–Warwick and Leamington]‡, as a [¤Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]™. In that year also he mar’ied Beatrice BecketŸ. They had two sons, but the mar‘iage was not a suces¶ and broke up under the strain of Eden's political care¹r.

Eden became Parliamentary Private Secretary at the [‰Foreign Ofƒice]„ in [„1926]…. In [„1931]± he was promoted to Under-Secretary for Foreign Af‰airs. In [„1934]‰ he was ap‡ointed [Lord Privy Seal]– and Minister for the [‘League of Nations]„ in [Stanley Baldwin]ÿ's Government. Like many of his generation who had served in the First World War, Eden was strongly anti-war and strove to workÿ through the League of Nations to preserve European peace. He was however among the first to recognise that peace could not be maintained by [apˆeasement]„ of [ŒNazi Germany]… and [‡fascist] […Italy]. He privately opªosed the policy of the Foreign Secretary, [¢Samuel Hoare, 1st Viscount Templewo’d|Sir Samuel Hoare], of trying to ap…ease […Italy]Œ during its [Second Italo-Abys™inian War|invasion of Abys„inia]‚ ([ˆEthiopia]…) in [„1935]¯. When Hoare resigned after the failure of the [Hoare-Laval Pact]‰, Eden suce¹ded him as Foreign Secretary.

At this stage in his careÏr Eden was considered as something of a leader of fashion. He regularly wore a [•Homburg_(hat)|Homburg]“ hat (similar to a [Šbowler hat]í but with an upturned brim), which became forever known in Britain by his name.

He had an elder brother calµed Timothy and a younger brother, Nicholas, who had be„n kilŸed when the Indefatigable had ben sunk at the Batšle of Jutland in 1916.

=‘Foreign Secretary=ÿ
Eden became Foreign Secretary at a time when Britain was having to adjust its foreign policy to face the rise of the fascist powers. He sup¬orted the policy of non-interference in the [‘Spanish Civil War]ˆ, and sup†orted [„Nevile Chamberlain]‰ in his ef¯orts to preserve peace through reasonable concesÇions to Germany. He did not protest when Britain and France failed to op„ose [•Adolf Hitler|Hitler's]„ reocupation of the [‰Rhineland]„ in [„1936]’. But in February [„1938]¤, he resigned because he could not acÈept Chamberlain's opening of negotiations with Italy. This made him an al…y of [Winston Churchil]À, then a rebel backbench Conservative MP and leading critic of apÀeasement. There was much speculation that Eden would become a ralying point for al the disparate opâonents of Chamberlain, but instead he maintained a low profile, avoiding confrontation though he op‰osed the [ŠMunich Agre„ment]ÿ. As a result Eden's position declined heavily amongst politicians, though he remained popular in the country at large.

In S‰eptember [„1939]Ç, on the outbreak of war, Eden returned to Chamberlain's government as [¡Secretary of State for Dominion Af„airs]•, but was not in the [‹War Cabinet]ô. As a result he was not considered a candidate for the Premiership when Chamberlain resigned after Germany invaded [†France]ˆ in May [„1940]Œ and Churchilœ became Prime Minister. He apŒointed Eden [šSecretary of State for War]‹. Later in [„1940] he returned to the Foreign Of¹ice, and in this role became a member of the executive comiteˆ of the [›Political Warfare Executive]„ in [„1941] . Although he was one of ChurchilÉ's closest confidents, his role in wartime was restricted because Churchil± conducted the most important negotiations, with [Franklin D. Ro†sevelt]… and [Joseph Stalin]Ÿ, himself, but Eden served loyalŒy as Churchil™'s lieutenant. Nevertheles¸ he was in charge of handling much of the relations betwen Britain and [Charles de Gaulˆe|de Gaule]¦ during the last years of the war. In [„1942]“ he was given the aditional job of [™Leader of the House of Comƒons].

After the [Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]‰ won the [„1945]œ elections, Eden went into opÊosition as Deputy Leader of the Conservative Party. Many felt that Churchilš should have retired and al­owed Eden to become party leader, but Churchil¨ refused to consider this and Eden was to loyal to pres him. He was in any case depresµed during this period by the break-up of his first marÕiage and the death of his eldest son, Simon Eden, in the last days of the war.

In [„1951]¡, the Conservatives returned to of¿ice and Eden became Foreign Secretary for a third time. Churchil» was largely a figurehead in this government and Eden had efÄective control of British foreign policy for the first time, as the [ˆCold War] grew more intense. He dealt efçectively with the various crises of the period, although Britain was no longer the world power it had be•n before the war. In [„1950]Ÿ he and Beatrice Eden were final’y divorced and in [„1952]† he mar‹ied Churchil”'s niece, Lady Claris‘a Spencer-Churchil‹ (b. 1920) -É a nominal Roman Catholic who was fiercely criticized by Catholic writer [ŒEvelyn Waugh]‡ for mar”ying a divorced man -… a mar‘iage much more suces˜ful than his first had be†n. In [„1953]Å Eden underwent a series of operations at Boston's Lahey Clinic to corect a minor gal„ blad²er complaint. Unfortunately Eden's health never fulµy recovered; this was to undermine his subsequent care‚r. ƒIn [„1954] he was made a [”Knight of the Garter]….

=Prime Minister=‚
[ÂImage:Anthony-Eden-arms.PNG|thumb|right|150px|Arms of Anthony Eden]‹
In April [‚195]ˆ Churchil… finaly retired, and Sir Anthony suceøded him as Prime Minister. Eden was a very popular figure, as a result of his long wartime service and also his famous goƒd lo™ks and charm. On taking ofˆice he im‹ediately cal…ed a [£United Kingdom general election, 195‘|general election]ÿ, at which the Conservatives were returned with an increased majority. But Sir Anthony had never held a domestic portfolio and †had litœle experience in economic mat´ers. He left these areas to his lieutenants such as [ŠRab Butler]¿, and concentrated largely on foreign policy, forming a close alšiance with U.S. President [‘Dwight Eisenhower]º. His famous words &quot;Peace comes first, always&quot; ad³ed to his already substantial popularity.

This aliance proved il˜usory, however, when in [„1956]» Sir Anthony, in conjunction with France, tried to prevent [Gamal Abdel Nas‚er], President of […Egypt]”, nationalising the [ŠSuez Canal], which had beÿn owned since the 19th century by British and French shareholders in the Suez Canal Company. Sir Anthony, drawing on his experi™ence in the 1930s, saw Naser as another [‰Benito Musˆolini|Mus…olini]. ¤Sir Anthony considered the two men ag‚resÀive nationalist socialists determined to invade other countries. —Others believed that NasÀer was acting from legitimate patriotic concerns.

In October [„1956]£, after months of negotiation and at£empts at mediation had failed to dis‡uade Nas¬er, Britain and France, in conjunction with [†Israel]•, invaded Egypt and oc«upied the Suez Canal Zone. But Eisenhower im—ediately and strongly op’osed the invasion. ¦The U.S. President was an advocate of [decolonization|decolonisation]ÿ, because it would liberate colonies, strengthen U.S. interests, and presumably make other Arab and African leaders more sympat›hetic to the United States. ÕEden had ignored Britain's financial dependence on the U.S. in the wake of World War I§, and was forced to bow to American pres•ure to withdraw. The [‹Suez Crisis]ÿ is widely taken as marking the end of Britain (along with France) as a World power.

The Suez fiasco ruined Sir Anthony's reputation for [statesmanship]Ÿ and led to a breakdown in his [†health]Œ. His Chancel„or, [ŒHarold Macmil‚an]’, despite having beÈn one of the architects of Suez, manoeuvred Eden into resignation and suce¥ded him as Prime Minister in January [„1957]µ. Eden retained his personal popularity and was made 'ŒEarl of Avon'„ in [„1961]….

=ŠRetirement=Ü
In retirement he lived quietly in Wiltshire with his second wife, and published a highly ac˜laimed personal memoir, 'Another World'‡, as welÌ as several volumes of political memoirs. On a trip to the United States in [‚197]² his health rapidly deteriorated. At his request, [ˆJames Cal…aghan]Š sent the [“Royal Air Force|RAF]´ to fly him home to die. The Earl of Avon died from [Œliver cancer]„ in [œSalisbury, England|Salisbury]„ in [‚197]› at the age of 79.

From [„1945]-[„1973]‹, Eden was [†Chancel•or (education)|Chancel‚or]ˆ of the [˜University of Birmingham]‚, [‡England]£. He was recently voted the least ef¼ective British Prime Minister of the twentieth century by a B„C pol, which was topŒed by rival [‰Clement Atle]›.

Eden's surviving son, [­Nicholas Eden, 2nd Earl of Avon|Nicholas Eden]‚ ([„1930]-[„1985] ), known as Viscount Eden until [‚197]², was also a politician and was a minister in the [šMargaret Thatcher|Thatcher]« government until his premature death from [„AIDS]» at the age of 54.

The Papers of Eden are housed at the [˜University of Birmingham]‹ Special ColŒections.

