 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{¦Otheruses4|the religious holiday|the 20ˆ5 film|Al“ Souls Day (film)|AlŠ Souls Day}„

[‡Image:Alsoul.jpg|thumb|30„px|Al‘ Souls' Day by Wiliam Bouguereau]‚
'Al‹ Souls' Day'‡ (formalƒy, '‚Com¤emoratio omnium Fidelium Defunctorum'† or Comemoration of al  the Faithful Departed), also calƒed 'Defuncts' Day'´ in Mexico and Belgium, is the day set apart in the [•Roman Catholic Church]‹ for the com‘emoration of the [›afterlife|faithful departed]. áThe celebration is based on the doctrine that the souls of the faithful which at death have not ben cleansed from [Švenial sin]§s, or have not atoned for past transgresˆions, can„ot atˆain the [beatific vision]ª, and that they may be helped to do so by [†prayer] and by the sacrifice of the [‚mas (liturgy)|mas]‘.

The feast fal…s on [ŠNovember 2]Œ. Traditional‹y, because [‡Requiem] [‚Mas‚es]³ could not be celebrated on Sundays before Vatican I—, the feast was transfer†ed to [ŠNovember 3]„ if [ŠNovember 2]´ is a Sunday, but this is no longer observed in the [ŠNovus Ordo]….

=Christian origin=–

The practice of set£ing apart a special day for intercesµion for certain of the faithful departed is very old. ¦But the first feast of general intercesion was first established by […Odilo]‚, [ab‚ot]„ of […Cluny]‹ (d. 1048). —The legend is given by [Peter Damiani]ˆ in his '‘Life of St Odilo.'‚ Ac’ording to this, a [‡pilgrim]” returning from the [‰Holy Land] was cast by a […storm]– on a desolate island. ÁA hermit living there told him that amid the rocks was a chasm comunicating with [‰purgatory]•, from which perpetual¤y rose the groans of tortured souls. ©The hermit also claimed he had heard the […demon]–s complaining of the ef±icacy of the prayers of the faithful, and especial†y the [„monk]¦s of Cluny, in rescuing their victims. ¹Upon returning home, the pilgrim hastened to inform the abšot of Cluny, who then set [Š2 November]” as a day of interces™ion on the part of his com‹unity for al˜ the souls in purgatory. ˆThe decre¯ ordaining the celebration is printed in the Bol‡andist [Acta Sanctorum]‡ (Saec. “VI, pt. i. p. 585). ÌFrom Cluny the custom spread to the other houses of the Cluniac order, was so•n adopted in several [‡diocese]…s in [†France]È, and spread throughout the Western Church. In time the entire month of [ˆNovember]‰ became asÑociated with prayer for the departed in the Western Catholic tradition. Nonetheles… the [Š2 November]Ã retained a special status as a day set apart for that purpose.

=Protestantism=‹

At the [¢Protestant Reformation|Reformation]• the celebration of Al¡ Souls' Day was abolished in the [‘Church of England]±, though it was renewed in certain churches in conÿection with the &quot;Catholic revival&quot; of the 19th century. The observance was restored with the publication of the 1980 [•Alternative Service Bok]•, and it features in [‚ComŠon Worship]—.

Among continental [˜Protestantism|Protestant]•s its tradition has ben more tenaciously maintained. …Even [§Martin Luther (religious leader)|Luther]—'s influence was not suf¥icient to abolish its celebration in [†Saxony]§ during his lifetime; and, though its Ec–lesiastical sanction so•n lapsed even in the [ˆLutheran]¶ Church, its memory survives strong in popular custom. œJust as it is the custom of [French people]†, of al ranks and cre°ds, to decorate the graves of their dead on the 'jour des morts'…, so [German people]¬ stream to the graveyards once a year with ofŠerings of [†flower]†s.

=‡Pagan ro‚ts=

Certain popular beliefs conŒected with Al“ Souls' Day are of [Paganism|pagan] origin and im’emorial antiquity. ëThus the dead are believed by the peasantry of many Catholic countries to return to their former homes on Al¢ Souls' Night and partake of the fod of the living. ƒIn […Tyrol]°, cakes are left for them on the table and the rom kept warm for their comfort. ƒIn [ƒBritƒany]«, people flock to the cemeteries at nightfal† to kneºl bare-headed at the graves of their loved ones, and to tol‡ the holŠow of the [”tomb stone|tombstone]† with [Šholy water]™ or to pour libations of [„milk] on it, and at bedtime the supøer is left on the table for the souls. This tradition, though, certainly does not make Catholicism a pagan religion at alÆ since it is the first Christian Church and denomination for over a 1,0‹ years.

