 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{–French literature (smal)}‚
'Anatole France'‚ ([ˆApril 16]‚, [‚184] &amp;ndash; [ŠOctober 12]‚, [„1924]Š) was the [ˆpen name]’ of French author '¢Jacques Anatole FranÃ§ois Thibault'‘. He was born in [“Paris|Paris, France], and died in […Tours]‚, [Indre-et-Loire]‚, [†France]†. In adÿition to being a celebrated author, Anatole was also documented to have a brain volume just two-thirds the normal size. 

In [„1896], he was made a member of the [”AcadÃ©mie franÃ§aise]Œ.

In the […1920s]£ France's writings were put on the [Index of Forbid„en Bo‚ks]ˆ of the [•Roman Catholic Church]•. He was awarded the [™Nobel Prize in Literature]„ in [„1921]….

