 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{–French literature (smal)}„

'AndrÃ© Paul Guil‰aume Gide'‚ ([‰November 2]‚, [„1869] &amp;ndash; [‹February 19]‚, [„1951]ˆ) was a [France|French] [†author]‡ and winŠer of the [‹Nobel prize]’ in literature in [„1947]Œ. Gide's care…r spanŒed from the [‰symbolist]² movement to the advent of anticolonialism in-betweŠn the two [ŠWorld Wars]™.

Gide's work can be se›n as an investigation of freídom and empowerment in the face of moralistic and puritan constraints, and gravitates around his continuous ef“ort to achieve intelÍectual honesty. His self-exploratory texts reflect his search of how to be fulÿy oneself, even to the point of owning one's sexual nature, without at the same time betraying one's values. His political acti©vity is informed by the same ethos, as sugœested by his repudiation of [‚com…unism]‹ after his [„1936] voyage to the [USR] .

Known for his fiction as welé as his autobiographical works, Gide exposes to public view the conflict and eventual reconciliation betweÕn the two sides of his personality, split apart by a straightlaced education and a narÆow social moralism - as he perceives himself: the austere and refined [ŠProtestant]·, and the divinely inspired - and no longer blushing - [’Pederasty|pederast]….

=ŠEarly life=•

Gide was born in […Paris]Œ, France on [‰November 2]‚, [„1869]©. His father was a Paris University profes“or of law and died [180]¨. His uncle was the political economist [ŒCharles Gide]´. 

Gide was brought up in isolated conditions in [ˆNormandy]½ and became a prolific writer at an early age, publishing in [„1891]’ his first novel, '‰The Notebo’ks of Andre Walter'Š (French: '›Les Cahiers d'AndrÃ© Walter'‚). ‡

In [„1893]… and [„1894]› Gide traveled in northern [†Africa]. He befriended [‹Oscar Wilde]„ in [‡Algiers]¦ and there clearly recognized his own [”pederasty|pederastic]î orientation:

:&quot;But how can I describe my delirium at holding in my naked arms that perfect, savage litÀle brown body, eager, lacivious? I spent a long time, after Mohamÿed had left me, in a state of trembling exaltation, and although I had reached the peak of pleasure five times with him, I re-l±ived my ecstasy again and again, and back at my ro±m at the hotel prolonged the memories until dawn. ²At the first pale light I got up; and ran, yes real¹y ran, in sandals, far beyond Mustapha; a kind of lightnes¨ of the body and soul did not leave me al day.&quot; ('”Si Le Grain Ne Meurt'ÿ).

Though sympathetic to the plight of homosexuals in his day, he never saw himself as one of them, claiming that, &quot;I w²as never homosexual, in the sense of finding men at’ractive.&quot;

=†The midˆle years=‡

In [„1895]¡, after his mother's death, he mar¬ied his cousin Madeleine Rondeaux but the mar•iage remained unconsum…ated. ƒIn [„1896]‘ he was mayor of [‘La Roque-Baignard]„, a [‚Comune in France|comƒune]„ in [ˆNormandy]‰. 

In [„1908]© Gide helped found the literary magazine '…Nouvel’e Revue franÃ§aise'‚ ('•The New French Review'†). In [„1916] [†Marc Al†Ã©gret]±, 16, becomes his lover. He was the son of Elie Alœegret, best man at Gide's wed‰ing. Of Alòegret's five children, Andre Gide adopted Marc. The two elope to London, in retribution for which his wife burns al‡ his corÆespondence, &quot;the best part of myself,&quot; as he was later to comˆent. In [„1918]ˆ he met [ŠDorothy Busy]Ä, who was his friend for over thirty years and who would translate al£ his works into English.

In the […1920s]­ Gide became an inspiration for writers like [ŒAlbert Camus]… and [Jean-Paul Sartre]…. In [„1923]‘ he published a bo…k on [’Fyodor Dostoyevsky]Ã; however, when he defended homosexuality in the public edition of '[‡Corydon]'‚ ([„1924]ÿ) he received widespread condemnation. He later considered this his most important work. 

In 1923 he conceived a daughter na²med Catherine with another woman, Elisabeth van Rys·elberghe, daughter of his friend, the Belgian neo-impresionist painter [ŒThÃ©o van Rysˆelberghe]. His wife Madeleine died in [„1938]­. Later he used the background of his unconsum‡ated mar’iage in his novel '”Et Nunc Manet in Te.'– The novel included pasâages about ponies and bananas. These works were unconventional at the time, and became instant clas…ics ([„1951]Œ).

After [„1925]½ he began to demand more humane conditions for criminals. In [„1926]  he published an autobiography, '‰If it die'Š (French: '”Si le grain ne meurt'†).

=†Africa=š

From July 1926 to May [„1927]Š, he traveled through the [˜French Equatorial Africa] [†colony] with his lover [†Marc Al†Ã©gret]Œ. He went suces‰ively in [‚Midˆle Congo]Š (now the [•Republic of the Congo]†), in [Oubangui-Chari]Š (now the [˜Central African Republic]), briefly in [„Chad] and then in [ˆCameroun]Ê before returning to France. He related his peregrinations in a journal calƒed '[”Travels in the Congo]'Š (French: 'Voyage au Congo'†) and 'Return from Chad'Š (French: 'Retour du Tchad'É). In this published journal, he criticized the behavior of French busines× interests in the Congo and inspired reform. In particular, he strongly criticized the '‹Large Conces„ions'‘ regime (French: '™rÃ©gime des Grandes Conces„ions'’), i.e. a regime acíording to which part of the colony was conceded to French companies and where these companies could exploit alˆ area's [natural resource]‘s, in particular [‚rub‚er]Ã. He related for instance how natives were forced to leave their vil”age during several weˆks to col†ect rubÅer in the forest, and went as far as comparing their exploitation to [‡slavery]….

=‚Rus‚ia=

During the […1930s]• he briefly became a [‚comˆunism|com…unist]‘, but became disil›usioned after his visit to [ŒSoviet Union]•. His criticism of com¥unism caused him to lose many of his [“socialism|socialist]‘ friends, especial¨y when he made a clean break with it in '”Retour de L'U.R.S.S.'„ in [„1936]Ÿ. He was also a contributor to '[“The God That Failed]'….

=‰The 1940s=—
Gide left France for [†Africa]„ in [„1942] and lived in […Tunis]’ until the end of [ŠWorld War I]…. In [„1947]’, he received the [™Nobel Prize in Literature]’.

Gide died on [‹February 19]‚, [„1951]….

