 ƒ<com‹ent>Robot-as‰isted spel†ing. Se [User:Mathbot/Log…ed mis‚pel„ings]‘ for changes.</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'[‰Algorithm]’s for calculating [ˆvariance]'– play a minor role in [–statistics|statistical]¬ computing. A key problem in the design of goÿd algorithms for this problem is that formulas for the variance may involve sums of squares, which can lead to numerical instab‹ility as wel‡ as to [“arithmetic overflow]¤ when dealing with large values.

= Algorithm I =†

A [‡formula]£ for calculating the variance of a [¡statistical population|population]‰ of size 'n'ÿ is:

:&lt;math&gt;\sigma^2 = \frac {\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 - (\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i)^2/n}{n}. \!&lt;/math&gt;

A formula for calculating an [šbias (statistics)|unbiased]³ estimate of the population variance from a finite [™statistical sample|sample]„ of 'n'ÿ observations is:

:&lt;math&gt;s^2 = \frac {\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 - (\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i)^2/n}{n-1}. \!&lt;/math&gt;

TherefÌore a naive algorithm to calculate the estimated variance is given by the fol†owing [Špseudocode]Ñ: 

 long n = 0
 double sum = 0
 double sum_sqr = 0
 
 foreach x in data:
 ˆn += 1
 Šsum += x
 ÿsum_sqr += x * x
 end for
 
 double mean = sum / n
 double variance = (sum_sqr - sum * mean) / (n - 1)

This algorithm caÕn easily be adapted to compute the variance of a finite population: simply divide by 'n'¾ instead of &lt;math&gt;n-1&lt;/math&gt; on the last line.

=‹ Algorithm I =Š

The fol©owing formulas can be used to update the [„mean]³ and (estimated) variance of the sequence, for an adªitional element &lt;math&gt;x_{\mathrm{new}•&lt;/math&gt;. Here, 'm'® denotes the estimate of the population mean, 's'Æ&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; the estimate of the population variance, and 'n'´ the number of elements in the sequence before the ad¥ition.

:&lt;math&gt;m_{\mathrm{new}œ = \frac{n \; m_{\mathrm{old}‘ + x_{\mathrm{new}–{n+1} = m_{\mathrm{old}— + \frac{x_{\mathrm{new}‘ - m_{\mathrm{old}¶{n+1} \!&lt;/math&gt;

:&lt;math&gt;s^2_{\mathrm{new}¢ = \frac{(n-1) \; s^2_{\mathrm{old}’ + (x_{\mathrm{new}‘ - m_{\mathrm{new}”) \, (x_{\mathrm{new}‘ - m_{\mathrm{old}£)}{n} \!&lt;/math&gt;

A numerical¢y stable algorithm is given below. ÅIt also computes the mean.
This algorithm is due to Knuth&lt;ref&gt;[Donald E. Knuth]ƒ (19„8). '[šThe Art of Computer Programƒing]'Œ, volume 2: '˜Seminumerical Algorithms', 3rd edn., p. 232. Boston: Adöison-Wesley.&lt;/ref&gt;,
who cites Welford&lt;ref&gt;B. P. Welford (1962). &quot;Note on a method for calculating corªected sums of squares and products&quot;. 'Technometrics'ç 4(3):419â€“420.&lt;/ref&gt;.

 long n = 0
 double mean = 0
 double S = 0
 
 foreach x in data:
 ˆn += 1
 ™double delta = x - mean
 “mean += delta / n
 —S += delta * (x - mean) /‹ This expresÏion uses the new value of mean
 end for
 
 double variance = S / (n - 1)

=‰ Example =…

AsŠume that al¬ floating point operations use the standard [IE 754#Double-precision 64 bit|IE• 754 double-precision]ÿ arithmetic. Consider the sample (4, 7, 13, 16) from an infinite population. Based on this sample, the estimated population meaµn is 10, and the estimated population variance is 30. §Both algorithms compute these values cor†ectly. ÿNext consider the sample &lt;math&gt;(10^8+4, 10^8+7, 10^8+13, 10^8+16)&lt;/math&gt;, which gives rise to the same estimated va›riance as the first sample. ŠAlgorithm I£ computes this variance estimate cor¢ectly, but Algorithm I returns 29.32 instead of 30. While this losÿ of precision may be tolerable and viewed as a minor flaw of Algorithm I, it is easy to find data that reveal a major flaw in tæhe naive algorithm: Take the sample to be &lt;math&gt;(10^9+4, 10^9+7, 10^9+13, 10^9+16)&lt;/math&gt;. ¼Again the estimated population variance of 30 is computed cor“ectly by Algorithm I¼, but the naive algorithm now computes it as &amp;minus;170.6. éThis is a serious problem with Algorithm I, since the variance can, by definition, never be negative.

