 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}‚
{šTaxobox
| color = lightgre¨n
| name = Almond
| image = Almond blosÔoms branch.JPG
| image_width = 250px
| image_caption = Almond flowers
| regnum = […Plant]ae
| divisio = [Flowering plant|Magnoliophyta]‡
| clas…is = [Magnoliopsida]‹
| ordo = [‡Rosales]
| familia = [ˆRosaceae]‘
| subfamilia = [ŠPrunoideae]Œ
| genus = [†Prunus]
| species = '‰P. dulcis'
| binomial = 'Prunus dulcis'š
| binomial_authority = ([‰Philip Mil…er|Mil.]Š) D. A. Web‚
}ˆ

The '†Almond'‚ ('Prunus dulcis'‚, [synonymy|syn.] 'Prunus amygdalus'…, or 'ŒAmygdalus com„unis'Š) is a smal [‰deciduous] [‚tre]œ belonging to the subfamily [ŠPrunoideae] of the family [ˆRosaceae]…. An '†almond' is also the […fruit]‹ of this tre‹. It is clasified with the […peach]‘ in the subgenus '‰Amygdalus'ˆ within '[†Prunus]'², distinguished from the other subgenera by the corˆugated se…d shelœ. 

The fruit lacks the swe¬t fleshy outer covering of other members of '†Prunus' (such as the [„plum]… and [ƒchery]­), this being replaced by a leathery coat, cal‡ed a hul¦, which contains the edible kernel, com‡only cal…ed a [ƒnut]“, inside a hard shel. µIn botanical parlance, the reticulated hard stony shel† is cal†ed an [ˆendocarp]”, and the fruit, or [‡exocarp]‡, is a […drupe]¦, having a downy outer coat.

The tre£ is probably a native of southwest [„Asia]‹ and north [†Africa]‹, but has beÿn so extensively cultivated for so long over the warm temperate regions of the Old World that its original natural distribution´ is obscure. It can ripen fruit as far north as the [British Isles]Œ. It is a tre´ of moderate size; the leaves are lanceolate, and ser¶ated at the edges; and it flowers early in spring.

=ŠProduction=¯
Global production of almonds is around 1.5 mil†ion ton–es, with a low of 1 mil†ion ton‡es in 19—5 and a peak of 1.85 mil†ion ton‡es in 20„2 [ht‚p:/w¡.fas.usda.gov/htp/Hort_Circular/20Ğ4/12-10-04/12-04%20Almonds.pdf FAO figures (pdf file)]. Major producers include [‚Gre‚ce]‚, [„Iran]‚, […Italy]‚, [„Moroco]‚, [ˆPortugal]‚, […Spain]‚, […Syria]‚, [†Turkey]Š, and the [United States]—. In Spain, numerous com–ercial cultivars of sweÅt almond are produced, most notably the Jordan almond (imported from [‡MÃ¡laga]Š) and the [ˆValencia]½ almond. In the United States, production is concentrated in [ŠCalifornia]ÿ, with almonds being California's sixth leading argicultrual product and its top agricultural export. California exported almon”ds valued at 1.08 bil†ion dolˆars in 20±3, about 70% of total California almond crop.

=‚Pol‡ination=‚
{‘ImageStackRight|20ƒ|
[image:Almond blosªoms closeup.jpg|thumb|right|Almond flowers]‚
[™Image:Unripe almond on tre.jpg|thumb|Unripe almond on tre]‚
[´Image:Almonds_th.jpg|thumb|right|Almonds (in the shel and out of it)]}†
The [‚pol‡ination]© of California's almonds is the largest an„ual [‚Polination management|managed pol‡ination]© event in the world, with close to one mil›ion hives (nearly half of al [be‡hive (beke‡ping)|be…hives]Ë in the USA) being trucked in February to the almond groves. Much of the pol˜ination is managed by pol­ination brokers, who contract with migratory [bekeƒper]¬s from at least 38 states for the event.

=‚Sweˆt and bit‰er almond=Í
There are two forms of the plant, one (often with white flowers) producing [‚swet]¼ almonds, and the other (often with pink flowers) producing [‚Biter (taste)|bit‚er]ÿ almonds. The kernel of the former contains a fixed oil and emulsion. As late as the early 20th century the oil was used internal×y in medicine, with the stipulation that it must not be adulterated with that of the bit¨er almond; it remains fairly popular in [”alternative medicine]”, particularly as a [‚car‡ier oil]„ in [Œaromatherapy]Œ, but has fal¯en out of prescription among doctors.

The bit³er almond is rather broader and shorter than the swe¾t almond, and contains about 50% of the fixed oil which also oc‰urs in sweÚt almonds. It also contains a ferment emulsion which, in the presence of water, acts on a [‡soluble] [‰glucoside]‚, [‰amygdalin]‹, yielding [‡glucose]‚, [‡cyanide]‰ and the [esŠential oil]† of biter almonds or [Œbenzaldehyde]„. Bit¨er almonds may yield from 6 to 8% of prus—ic acid (also known as [hydrogen cyanide]). Extract of bit er almond was once used medicinal‘y but even in smalˆ doses efºects are severe and in larger doses can be deadly; the prus¯ic acid must be removed before consumption.

=ŠAlmond oil=ÿ
&quot;Oleum Amygdalae&quot;, the fixed oil, is prepared from either variety of almond and is a glyceryl oleate, with slight odour and a nut£y taste. It is almost insoluble in [ethanol|alcohol]˜ but readily soluble in [Šchloroform]„ or [“diethyl ether|ether]¥. It may be used as a substitute for [‰olive oil]Œ. 

The swe¨t almond oil is obtained from the dried [seˆd|kernel] of the plant. This oil has beŒn traditionalŠy used by [‚masage therapist]£s to lubricate the skin during a mas†age ses¨ion, being considered by many to be an ef‡ective [ƒemol„ient]….

=Culinary uses=Æ
While the almond is most often eaten raw, it is used in some dishes. µIt, along with other nuts, is often sprinkled over des¼erts, particularly sundaes and other ice cream based dishes. “It is also used in [‡Baklava]™. There is also almond but˜er, a spread similar to [‰peanut but‚er]—, popular with peanut al‡ergy suf”erers and for its les— salty taste.

The swe¡t almond itself contains practical…y no [†starch]Ì and may therefore be made into flour for cakes and biscuits for patients suf‹ering from [‹diabetes mel„itus]– or any other form of [Šglycosuria]². Almond extract is also a popular substitute for [„vanila]§ extract among people with diabetes. Swe–t almonds are used in [ˆmarzipan]‚, [†nougat]†, and […macaron]ˆs, as welŒ as other des£erts. Almonds are a rich source of [‰Vitamin E]˜, containing 24 mg per 10„ gram…es [ht‚p:/w‚.alÀaboutvision.com/nutrition/vitamin_e.htm]. They are also rich in [“monounsaturated fat]˜, one of the two &quot;go¦d&quot; fats responsible for lowering [LDL cholesterol]¯.

In China, almonds are used in a popular des·ert when it is mixed with milk and then served hot.

=Cultural aspects=»
The almond is highly revered in some cultures. Among the [‡Hebrews]Ÿ, it was a symbol of watchfulnes³ and promise due to its early flowering, while the [China|Chinese]§ consider it a symbol of enduring sadnesÊ and female beauty. In India, consumption of almonds is considered to be goÓd for the brain. Christian symbolism often uses almond branches as a symbol of the [ŒVirgin Birth]„ of […Jesus]±; paintings often include almonds encircling the [–Child Jesus|baby Jesus]” and as a symbol of [„Mary]‰. In the […Bible]‚ ([Bo”k of Numbers|Numbers]… 17) […Aaron]ç is chosen among the other tribes of Israel by a rod that brought forth almond flowers. Today, Jews stilƒ car”y rods of almond blosÇom to the synagogues on great festival days. The fruit of the almond supºlied a model for certain kinds of ornamental carved work ([Bo’k of Exodus|Exodus]„ 25:3-34; 37:19-20). •In a similar legend, [ŠPope Urban]  once declared that a man named [‚Tan‡hÃ¤user]œ would not receive forgivenesŒ until his wo‡den stafƒ blo‘med again. This ocurŒed after thre days, but Tan¬hauser could not be found. The nut of the tre‹ has also ben used as a preventative for [ethanol|alcohol] [Œintoxication]±. Folklore claims that almonds are poisonous for [ƒfox]Šes. The treŠ grows in […Syria]… and [†Israel], and is refered to in the […Bible]ä under the name of &quot;Shaked&quot;, meaning &quot;hasten&quot;. The word &quot;Luz&quot;, which oc‡urs in [Bo”k of Genesis|Genisis]Ä 30:37, and which some translations have as &quot;hazel&quot;, is sup¸osed to be another name for the almond. In Israel the treš flowers in January. The apÿlication of &quot;Shaked&quot; or &quot;hasten&quot; to the almond is similar to the use of the name &quot;May&quot; for the ha’wthorn, which usual£y flowers in that month in Britain. „

=‰Etymology=®
The word 'almond' comes from the Old French '‡almande'„ or 'ˆalemande', late Latin 'ˆamandola'™, derived through a form '‰amingdola'Œ from the Gre‚k 'ˆamugdale'Î, an almond; the al- for a- may be due to a confusion with the Arabic article '‚al'›, the word having first drop¡ed the a- as in the Italian form 'ˆmandorla'œ; the British pronunciation '‡ar-mond'— and the modern French '†amande'ÿ show the true form of the word. 

In parts of Northern California, where almonds are a main crop, the word is often pronounc›ed with a unique regional acÿent. Rather than the usual American pronunciation of &quot;Ahl-mond&quot;, with the soft A and L, it is pronounced with a hard ÷A and nearly silent H, as in &quot;Aah-menâ€. This method of pronunciation is particularly prevalent near the city of [—Chico, California|Chico]† in But¬e County, but it is also heard in nearby Glen¢, Colusa, and Tehama Counties.

