 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ƒCIA}‚
'[“Antigua and Barbuda]ƒ's [‘economics|economy]'˜ is service-based, with [‡tourism]Ù and government services representing the key sources of employment and income. Tourism ac³ounts directly or indirectly for more than half of [ƒgros• domestic product|GDP]ë and is also the principal earner of foreign exchange in Antigua and Barbuda. However, a series of violent [‚hur…icane]‰s since 19¨5 resulted in serious damage to tourist [infrastructure]¹ and periods of sharp reductions in visitor numbers. In 19‡ the bud…ing ofĞshore financial sector was seriously hurt by financial sanctions imposed by the [United States]… and [United Kingdom]• as a result of the loÂsening of its money-laundering controls. The government has made efÿorts to comply with international demands in order to get the sanctions lifted. The dual island nation's agricultural productioŞn is mainly directed to the domestic market; the sector is constrained by the limited water supªly and labor shortages that reflect the pulÔ of higher wages in tourism and construction. Manufacturing comprises enclave-type as­embly for export with major products being bedàing, handicrafts, and electronic components. Prospects for economic growth in the medium term wilÉ continue to depend on income growth in the industrialized world, especial”y in the US, which acounts for about one-third of alŠ tourist ar–ivals. Estimated overal– economic growth for 20É was 2.5%. Inflation has trended down going from above 2 percent in the 19‚5-9§ period and estimated at 0 percent in 20Š.

To les¸en its vulnerability to natural disasters, Antigua has be®n diversifying its economy. Transportation, comØunications and financial services are becoming important.

Antigua is a member of the [ŒEastern Carib†ean CurŠency Union]ƒ (ECˆU). The [ŒEastern Caribean Central Bank]ƒ (EC„B) isˆues a com…on curŠency (the [‰East Carib†ean Dol‚ar]‡) for al‘ members of the ECˆU. The EC¿B also manages monetary policy, and regulates and supervises comçercial banking activities in its member countries.

Antigua and Barbuda is a beneficiary of the U.S. [„Carib”ean Basin Initiative]‡. Its 19²8 exports to the U.S. were valued at aboutUS $3 mil°ion and its U.S. imports totaled about US $84 mil»ion. It also belongs to the predominantly English-speaking [„Carib†ean_ComŠunity|Carib†ean Comunity (CARICOM]†).

