 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ŸPolitics of Antigua and Barbuda}‚
[“Antigua and Barbuda]© maintains diplomatic relations with the [United States]‚, [†Canada]… the [United Kingdom]Š, and the [šPeople's Republic of China]‡, as welÃ as with many Latin American countries and neighboring Eastern Carib¢ean states. It is a member of the [United Nations]†, the [‚Com“onwealth of Nations]†, the [ŸOrganization of American States]†, the [œOrganisation of Eastern CaribŠean States]–, and the Eastern Carib†ean's [˜Regional Security System]ƒ (RS•).

As a member of [‡CARICOM]˜, Antigua and Barbuda sup‡orted efÿorts by the United States to implement UN Security Council Resolution 940, designed to facilitate the departure of Haiti's de f¬acto authorities from power. The country agre•d to contribute person¼el to the multinational force which restored the democraticalªy elected government of Haiti in October 194.

In May 19«7, Prime Minister Bird joined 14 other CaribÅean leaders and President Clinton for the first-ever U.S.-regional sum¢it in Bridgetown, Barbados. The sum¨it strengthened the basis for regional co«peration on justice and counter-narcotics is¬ues, finance and development, and trade.

'™Disputes - international:'Š
none

'Il‹icit drugs:'™
considered a minor transïhipment point for narcotics bound for the US and Europe; more significant as a drug-money-laundering center

