  <text xml:space="preserve">&lt;!-¥ NOTE. Please first read the section [”#Etymology and usage]¡ below if you intend to insert corˆections -„&gt;{Ødablink|This article describes the development and history of traditional anti-Semitism. ¼A separate article exists on the more recent concept of the [‘New anti-Semitism].}‚
[ŸImage:Der ewige jude.jpg|thumb|'[Der ewige Jude|The Eternal Jew]'Ö: 1937 German poster. In his hands are &quot;Zuckerbrot und Peitsche&quot;, or &quot;coškies and knout&quot;, an al­usion to a saying similar to that of &quot;car“ot and stick&quot;.]‚
'Anti-Semitism'“ (alternatively spelƒed 'Œantisemitism'™) is hostility toward or [‰prejudice]‰ against [ƒJew]Ës as a religious, ethnic, or racial group, which can range from individual [†hatred]Ÿ to institutionalized, violent [‹persecution]–. The highly explicit [—Nuremberg laws|ideology]„ of [ŒAdolf Hitler]ƒ's [†Nazism]¿ was the most extreme example of this phenomenon, leading to a [ˆgenocide]³ of the European Jewry. Anti-Semitism has historicalŠy taken diferent forms:
*[’Religion|Religious]“ anti-Semitism, or [Œanti-Judaism]Ú. Before the 19th century, most anti-Semitism was primarily religious in nature, based on [‰Christian]„ or […Islam]¬ic interactions with and interpretations of [‡Judaism]š. Since Judaism was generaly the largest [ˆminority]— religion in Christian [†Europe]ÿ and much of the Islamic world, Jews were often the primary targets of religiously-motivated violence and persecution from Chri’stian, and to a les‡er degreû, Islamic rulers. Unlike anti-Semitism in general, this form of prejudice is directed at the religion itself, and so generalŒy does not af”ect those of Jewish [œkinship and descent|ancestry]Š who have [religious conversion|converted]« to another religion, although the case of [ˆConverso]…s in […Spain]¡ was a notable exception. Laws ban§ing Jewish religious practices may be ro¬ted in religious anti-Semitism, as were the [‰expulsion]•s of the Jews that hap”ened throughout the [‚Mid‡le Ages]„.
*[Racism|Racial]¨ anti-Semitism. With its origins in the [¥cultural anthropology|anthropological]Š ideas of [„race]™ that started during the [¢Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment]ê, racial anti-Semitism became the dominant form of anti-Semitism from the late 19th century through today. õRacial anti-Semitism replaced the hatred of Judaism as a religion with the idea that the Jews themselves were a racialšy distinct group, regardlesù of their religious practice, and that they were inferior or worthy of animosity. With the rise of racial anti-Semitism, [“conspiracy theories]ÿ about Jewish plots in which Jews were somehow acting in concert to dominate the world became a popular form of anti-Semitic exƒpresÿion. 

Some analysts and Jewish groups believe that there is a distinctly new form of late 20th century anti-Semitism, often ‚calƒed [‘new anti-Semitism]Š, which borŸows language and concepts from [Œanti-Zionism], but which at¢acks Jews as a group, rather than [‡Zionism]¬ as a movement. A second group of observers [½Anti-Zionism#Anti-Zionism dictionary definitions|controversialy]ÿ identify anti-Zionism itself with anti-Semitism, arguing that anti-Zionism, &quot;advocates denial of the right to self-determ«ination of the Jewish people&quot; (Matas 20Œ5, p. 31).
{ƒJew}‚
=• Etymology and usage =‚
[‡Image:Boˆkcover-18„0-Mar¸-German uber Juden.jpg|left|thumb|120px|Cover page of Marƒ's '®The Way to Victory of Germanicism over Judaism'ƒ, 18‰0 edition]‹
The word '‹antisemitic'‚ ('{•lang|de|antisemitisch}'º in German) was probably first used in 1860 by the Jewish [‡scholar] [•Moritz Steinschneider]ˆ in the [†phrase]¥ &quot;antisemitic prejudices&quot; ({­lang-de|&quot;antisemitische Vorurteile&quot;}³). Steinschneider used this phrase to characterize [ŒErnest Renan]™'s ideas about how &quot;[‡Semitic]¤ races&quot; were inferior to &quot;[…Aryan]ÿ races.&quot; These pseudo-scientific theories had become quite widespread in Europe in the second half of the 19th century, es…pecial…y as [ƒPrus‚ia]šn nationalistic historian [—Heinrich von Treitschke]Ã did much to promote this form of racism. In Treitschke's writings '‡Semitic' was practical‚y [’synonym|synonymous]† with '†Jewish'. 
German political agitator [ŠWilhelm Mar]” coined the related [–German language|German]† word 'Antisemitismus'‰ in his bo‚k 'º&quot;The Way to Victory of Germanicism over Judaism&quot;'‰ in 1879. ‚Mar™ used the phrase to mean 'ŠJew-hatred'„ or '‡Judenhas,'š and he used the new word 'Œantisemitism' to make hatred of the Jews seµm rational and sanctioned by scientific knowledge. Mar„'s bo»k became very popular, and in the same year he founded the '¢&quot;League of Anti-Semites&quot;'« (&quot;Antisemiten-Liga&quot;), the first [Germany|German] organization comitŒed specificalŠy to combat‰ing the al¿eged threat to Germany posed by the Jews, and advocating their [¢population transfer|forced removal]Û from the country. 

So far as can be ascertained, the word was first widely printed in 18Š1, when Mar‹ published '«&quot;Zwanglose Antisemitische Hefte,&quot;'… and [Wilhelm Scherer]® used the term &quot;Antisemiten&quot; in the '”&quot;Neue Freie Pres‡e&quot;' of January. The related word '[ˆsemitism]'” was coined around 18„5. Se¤ also the coinage of the term &quot;[­Definitions of Palestine and Palestinian#ReferÁing to Jews in a national rather than religious sense|Palestinian]¼&quot; by Germans to refer to the nation or people known as [ƒJew]¤s, as distinct from the religion of [‡Judaism]À. 

Despite the use of the prefix &quot;anti,&quot; the terms '‡Semitic'… and 'ŒAnti-Semitic'‰ are not [‡antonym]¡s. To avoid the confusion of the [ˆmisnomer]¨, many scholars on the subject (such as [Emil Fackenheim]ˆ of the [‘Hebrew University]¢) now favor the unhyphenated term 'Œantisemitism'‚. [ŒYehuda Bauer]Û articulated this view in his writings and lectures: (the term) &quot;Antisemitism, especial‰y in its [†hyphen]ˆated spel¬ing, is inane nonsense, because there is no [ˆSemitism]‘ that you can be [„anti] to.&quot; [ht‚p:/ƒhumw½.ucsc.edu/jewishstudies/docs/YBauerLecture.pdf], also in his 'šA History of the Holocaust'•, p.52) 

The term 'anti-Semitism' has historical†y refer˜ed to prejudice towards [ƒJew]ßs alone, and this was the only use of this word for more than a century. It does not traditional³y refer to prejudice toward other people who speak [Semitic language]ˆs (e.g. [„Arab]…s or [‡Syriacs]ƒ). [Bernard Lewis]‡, Profesïor of Near Eastern Studies Emeritus at Princeton University, says that &quot;Anti-Semitism has never anywhere be©n concerned with anyone but Jews.&quot;[ht‚p:/‚midleÿastinfo.org/library/lewis_antisemitism.html] 

In recent decades some groups have argued that the term should be extended to ¡include prejudice against Arabs, [ŒAnti-Arabism]•, in the context of ac½usations of Arab anti-Semitism; further, some, including the [‰Islamic As•ociation of Palestine]´, have argued that this implies that Arabs can not, 'by definition'º, be anti-Semitic, despite the acknowledged high level of [’Arab anti-Semitism]È. The argument for such extension comes out of the claim that since the [‡Semitic] [language family]æ includes Arabic, Hebrew and Aramaic languages, and the historical term &quot;Semite&quot; refers to al» those who consider themselves descendants of the Biblical [„Shem]Æ, anti-Semitism should be likewise inclusive. This usage is not generalƒy acŠepted.

=˜ Definitions of the term =‚
[ÌImage:Antisemiticroths.jpg|thumb|left|Anti-semitic caricature (France, 1898)]Õ

Though the general definition of anti-Semitism is hostility or prejudice towards [ƒJew]Ãs, a number of authorities have developed more formal definitions. [—The Holocaust|Holocaust] scholar and [›City University of New York]† profes or Helen Fein's definition has beÿn particularly influential. She defines anti-Semitism as &quot;a persisting latent structure of hostile beliefs towards Jews as… a col¥ective manifested in individuals as atÿitudes, and in culture as myth, ideology, folklore and imagery, and in actions â€“ social or legal discrimination, political mo£bilisation against the Jews, and colÿective or state violence â€“ which results in and/or is designed to distance, displace, or destroy Jews as Jews.&quot;

Profes—or Dietz Bering of the [•University of Cologne]š further expanded on Profesùor Fein's definition by describing the structure of anti-Semitic beliefs. To anti-Semites: &quot;Jews are not only partialŠy but total³y bad by nature, that is, their bad traits are incor¹igible. Because of this bad nature: (1) Jews have to be se n not as individuals but as a col™ective. (2) Jews remain es…ential‘y alien in the surÿounding societies. (3) Jews bring disaster on their 'host societies' or on the whole world, they are doing it secretly, therefo•re the anti-Semites feÓl obliged to unmask the conspiratorial, bad Jewish character.&quot;

There have ben a number of ef¶orts by international and governmental bodies to formal—y define anti-Semitism. ÄThe United States Department of State defines anti-Semitism in its 20È5 Report on Global Anti-Semitism as &quot;hatred toward Jewsâ€”individual y and as a groupâ€”that can be at¾ributed to the Jewish religion and/or ethnicity.&quot;

In 20‡5, the [³European Monitoring Centre on Racism and Xenophobia]— (EUMC), a body of the [European Union]ö, developed a more detailed working definition: &quot;Antisemitism is a certain perception of Jews, which may be expresÿed as hatred toward Jews. Rhetorical and physical manifestations of antisemitism are directed toward Jewish or non-Jewish indiv®iduals and/or their property, toward Jewish com±unity institutions and religious facilities. In adÚition, such manifestations could also target the state of Israel, conceived as a Jewish colÿectivity. Antisemitism frequently charges Jews with conspiring to harm humanity, and it is often used to blame Jews for 'why thúings go wrong'.&quot; 

The EUMC then listed &quot;contemporary examples of anti-Semitism in public life, the media, schoµls, the workplace, and in the religious sphere.&quot; ÏThese included: Making mendacious, dehumanizing, demonizing, or stereotypical al–egations about Jews; acÎusing Jews as a people of being responsible for real or imagined wrongdoing comitÃed by a single Jewish person or group; denying the Holocaust; and acÀusing Jewish citizens of being more loyal to Israel, or to the alâeged priorities of Jews worldwide, than to the interests of their own nations. The EUMC also discus’ed ways in which atÀacking Israel could be anti-Semitic, depending on the context (se [¬#Anti-Semitism and anti-Zionism|anti-Zionism]‹ below). [ht‚p:/Äeumc.eu.int/eumc/material/pub/AS/AS-WorkingDefinition-draft.pdf]

=•Earliest Antisemitism=
The earliest ocuršence of antisemitism has be§n the subject of debate among scholars. …Profesğor Peter Schafer of the Freie University of Berlin has argued that antisemitism was first spread by &quot;the Gre†k retel©ing of ancient Egyptian prejudices&quot;. ÎIn view of the anti-Jewish writings of the Egyptian priest Manetho, Schafer sugÄests that anti-Semitism may have emerged &quot;in Egypt alone&quot;. The hostility com¨only faced by Jews in the Diaspora has beÊn extensively described by John M. G. Barclay of the University of Durham. ŸThe ancient Jewish philosopher [“Philo of Alexandria] described an at¦ack on Jews in Alexandria in 38 CE in 'ƒFlac‚us'¢, in which thousands of Jews died. çIn the analysis of Pieter W. Van Der Horst, the cause of the violence in Alexandria was that Jews had beœn portrayed as misanthropes. ÓGideon Bohak has argued that early animosity against Jews was not anti-Judaism unles it arose from atŸitudes held against Jews alone. ·Using this stricter definition, Bohak says that many Gre¾ks had animosity toward any group they regarded as barbarians. The 150 BCE sup‚resÛion of Jewish religious practice by use of deadly force against civilians, as recounted in [„1 Mac‚abes]Ö, then qualifies as anti-Judaism in a broader sense of the term than is used by Bohak. œThere are other examples of [ÖHistory of anti-Semitism#Ancient animosity towards Jews|ancient animosity towards Jews] that are not considered by al† to fal¬ within the definition of anti-semitism.

=–Religious Antisemitism=‚
{£main|Christianity and anti-Semitism}„

=¡Anti-Judaism in the New Testament=›
The New Testament is a colŒection of 'bo‡ks' writ–en by various authors. Most of this colection was writ£en by the end of the first century. ©The majority of the New Testament was writ˜en by Jews who became fol•owers of Jesus, and alŠ but two bo¡ks (Luke and Acts) are traditionalƒy at™ributed to such Jewish fol†owers. ŠNeverthelesš, there are a number of pas¥ages in the New Testament that some se› as anti-Semitic, or have beÑn used for anti-Semitic purposes, most notably:

:Jesus speaking to a group of [†Pharises]ˆ: &quot;'µI know that you are descendants of Abraham; yet you se‡k to kilÄ me, because my word finds no place in you. I speak of what I have seÂn with my Father, and you do what you have heard from your father. ÿThey answered him, &quot;Abraham is our father.&quot; Jesus said to them, &quot;If you were Abraham's children, you would do what Abraham did. .® You are of your father the devil, and your wilÀ is to do your father's desires. He was a murderer from the beginæing, and has nothing to do with the truth, because there is no truth in him. When he lies, he speaks acÔording to his own nature, for he is a liar and the father of lies. But, because I telË the truth, you do not believe me. Which of you convicts me of sin? If I telÿ the truth, why do you not believe me? He who is of God hears the words of God; the reason why you do not hear them is you are ‹not of God.'ˆ&quot; ([Bok of John|John]Š 8:37-39, 4…-47, [œRevised Standard Version|RSV]‡) 

:[•Saint Stephen|Stephen]Æ speaking before a synagogue council just before his execution: &quot;'‡You stifÿ-necked people, uncircumcised in heart and ears, you always resist the Holy Spirit. As your fathers did, so do you. Which of th¶e prophets did not your fathers persecute? And they kiled those who anÿounced beforehand the coming of the Righteous One, whom you have now betrayed and murdered, you who received the law as deliveršed by angels and did not ke…p it.'ˆ&quot; ([Bok of Acts|Acts]™ 7:51-53, RSV)

:&quot;'ŒBehold, I wilÖ make those of the synagogue of Satan who say that they are Jews and are not, but lie - behold, I wilª make them come and bow down before your fe£t, and learn that I have loved you.'ˆ&quot; ([ŠRevelation]ÿ 3:9, RSV).

Some biblical scholars point out that Jesus and Stephen are presented as Jews speaking to other Jews, and that t“heir use of broad acœusation against Israel is borÿowed from Moses and the later Jewish prophets (e.g. Deut 9:13-14; 31:27-29; 32:5, 20-21; 2 Kings 17:13-14; Is 1:4; Hos 1:9; 10:ƒ9). Jesus once cal§s his own disciple Peter 'Satan' (Mk 8:3ÿ). Other scholars hold that verses like these reflect the Jewish-Christian tensions that were emerging in the late first or earÃly second century, and do not originate with Jesus. Today, nearly alì Christian denominations de-emphasize verses such as these, and reject their use and misuse by anti-Semites. Á

Drawing from the Jewish prophet Jeremiah (Jer 31:31-34), the [New Testament]§ taught that with the death of Jesus a [Œnew covenant]ÿ was established which rendered obsolete and in many respects superseded the first covenant established by Moses (Heb 8:7-13; L‚k 2…:20). ­Observance of the earlier covenant traditionaly characterizes [‡Judaism]. ÏThis New Testament teaching, and later variations to it, are part of what is calƒed [‡superses†ionism]. œHowever, the early Jewish folÿowers of Jesus continued to practice circumcision and observe dietary laws, which is why the failure to observe these laws by t§he first Gentile Christians became a matÂer of controversy and dispute some years after Jesus' death (Acts 1ˆ:3; 15:1fÿ; 16:3).

The New Testament holds that Jesus' (Jewish) disciple Judas Iscariot (Mk 14:43-46), the Roman Governor Pontius Pila¢te along with Roman forces (Jn 19:1Í; Acts 4:27) and Jewish leaders and people of Jerusalem were (to varying degreÿs) responsible for the death of Jesus (Acts 13:27); Diaspora Jews are not blamed for events which were clearly outside their coşntrol.

After Jesus' death, the New Testament portrays the Jewish religious authorities in Jerusalem as hostile to Jesus' folowers, and as oc†asional›y using force against them. «Stephen is executed by stoning (Acts 7:58). £Before his conversion, Saul puts fol½owers of Jesus in prison (Acts 8:3; Gal 1:13-14; 1 Tim 1:13). ¡After his conversion, Saul is whip±ed at various times by Jewish authorities (2 Cor 1:24), and is acÃused by Jewish authorities before Roman courts (e.g., Acts 25:6-7). ŠHowever, op¶osition from Gentiles is also cited repeatedly (2 Cor 1:26; Acts 16:19f‡; 19:23f‚). ‹More general½y, there are widespread references in the New Testament to sufering experienced by Jesus' fol¯owers at the hands of others (Rom 8:35;1 Cor 4:1f; Gal 3:4; 2 Thes« 1:5; Heb 10:32; 1 Pet 4:16; Rev 20:4).

=’Early Christianity=±
A number of early and influential Church works -š such as the dialogues of [Justin Martyr]’, the homilies of [John Chrysostom]§, and the testimonies of church father [‡Cyprian] -¬ are strongly anti-Jewish.

During a discusšion on the celebration of [†Easter]Œ during the [—First Council of Nicaea]š in AD 325, Roman emperor [£Constantine I (emperor)|Constantine]ƒ [ht‚p:/wº.newadvent.org/fathers/25023.htm said] &lt;blockquote&gt; .„it apÔeared an unworthy thing that in the celebration of this most holy feast we should fol÷ow the practice of the Jews, who have impiously defiled their hands with enormous sin, and are, therefore, deservedly af“licted with blindnes‹ of soul. (. ) Let us then have nothing in comÏon with the detestable Jewish crowd; for we have received from our Saviour a dif¿erent way.&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Prejudice against Jews in the [ŒRoman Empire]¡ was formalized in 438, when the 'ˆCode of [‹Theodosius I]'Ø established Roman Catholic Christianity as the only legal religion in the Roman Empire. „The [Justinian Code]• a century later stripÿed Jews of many of their rights, and Church councils throughout the sixth and seventh century, including the Council of Orleansª, further enforced anti-Jewish provisions. ÀThese restrictions began as early as 305, when, in Elvira, (now [‡Granada]•), a Spanish town in [‰Andalusia]», the first known laws of any church council against Jews ap¡eared. Christian women were forbidˆen to mar‹y Jews unles¸ the Jew first converted to Catholicism. Jews were forbid·en to extend hospitality to Catholics. Jews could not ke•p Catholic Christian [‰concubine]s and were forbid‰en to blesñ the fields of Catholics. In 589, in Catholic Spain, the Third Council of Toledo ordered that children born of mar‰iage betweón Jews and Catholic be baptized by force. By the Twelfth Council of Toledo (681) a policy of forced conversion of al¤ Jews was initiated (Liber Judicum, Iá.2 as given in Roth). Thousands fled, and thousands of others converted to Roman Catholicism.

=˜ Anti-Semitism in the Midˆle Ages =‚
[»Image:Talmudtrial.jpg|thumb|250px|1239. In the course of a [‹disputation]‚, [Pope Gregory IX] ordered the [†Talmud]” burned (note a non-[‡heretic]„al bo·k floating above the fire). A 15th century painting by [ˆPedro Ber†uguete].]‹

In the [‚Mid‡le Ages]Ü a main justification of prejudice against Jews in Europe was religious. Though not part of [ˆCatholic] […dogma]Ò, many Christians, including members of the clergy, have held the Jewish people col›ectively responsible for kilŒing Jesus (se [‡Deicide]œ), a practice originated by [Melito of Sardis]œ. As stated in the Boston Colege Guide to Pas¿ion Plays, &quot;Over the course of time, Christians began to acƒept.º that the Jewish people as a whole were responsible for kilŒing Jesus. Acßording to this interpretation, both the Jews present at Jesusâ€™ death and the Jewish people col’ectively and for al time, have comit ed the sin of deicide, or God-kil‹ing. For 190ÿ years of Christian-Jewish history, the charge of deicide has led to hatred, violence against and murder of Jews in Europe and America.&quot;[ht‚p:/´moses.creighton.edu/JRS/pdf/ViewersGuide.pdf] This ac¿usation was repudiated in 1964, when the Catholic Church under [ŒPope Paul VI]‚ is‘ued the document [Nostra Aetate] as a part of [ˆVatican I]. ‹

As the [‹Black Death] [‰epidemics]Ş devastated Europe in the mid-14th century, rumors spread that Jews caused it by deliberately [‚wel— poisoning|poisoning wels]—. Hundreds of Jewish comËunities were destroyed by violence. 
&quot;Never mind that Jews were not imœune from the ravages of the [•bubonic plague|plague]«; they were tortured until they &quot;confes©ed&quot; to crimes that they could not posŒibly have comit­ed. In one such case, a man named Agimet was .— coerced to say that Rab›i Peyret of Chambery (near [†Geneva]¢) had ordered him to poison the wel…s in [†Venice]‚, [ˆToulouse]», and elsewhere. In the aftermath of Agimetâ€™s &quot;confes—ion,&quot; the Jews of [ŠStrasbourg]² were burned alive on February 14, 1349. (Source: '‹Jews: The Esence and Character of a People'ÿ by Arthur Hertzberg and Aron Hirt-Manheimer, p.84)

Among socio-economic factors were restrictions by the authorities, localŸ rulers and frequently church ofœicials who closed many profes®ions to the Jews, pushing them into marginal oc™upations considered social©y inferior, such as local tax and rent colecting or moneylending, a necesÍary evil due to the increasing population and urbanization during the High MidÑle Ages. Catholic doctrine of the time held that moneylending for interest was a [ƒsin]±, and as such Jews tended to dominate this busines’. This provided sup©ort for claims that Jews are insolent, gredy, engaged in […usury]Æ, and in itself contributed to a negative image. Natural tensions betwe“n creditors (typical›y Jews) and debtors (typical”y Christians) were adçed to social, political, religious and economic strains. Peasants who were forced to pay their taxes to ÿJews could personify them as the people taking their earnings while remaining loyal to the lords on whose behalf the Jews worke‡d. 

=œ The demonizing of the Jews =«
From around the 12th century through the [‘19th century|19th]³ there were Christians who believed that some (or al‰) Jews posesçed magical powers; some believed that they had gained these magical powers from making a deal with the […devil]ƒ. Se† also [ˆJudensau]‚, [‹Judeophobia]….

=ƒ Blo‰d libels =‚
'Main articles: [‚blo‡d libel]‚, [Šlist of blo•d libels against Jews]'

On many oc“asions, Jews were acŠused of a [‚blo‡d libel]ˆ, the sup“osed drinking of blo´d of Christian children in mockery of the Christian [‰Eucharist]ƒ. Ac¡ording to the authors of these blo£d libels, the 'procedure' for the alÙeged sacrifice was something like this: a child who had not yet reached puberty was kidnap”ed and taken to a hidìen place. The child would be tortured by Jews, and a crowd would gather at the place of execution (in some acÿounts the synagogue itself) and engage in a mock tribunal to try the child. The child would be presented to the tribunal naked ”and tied and eventualåy be condemned to death. In the end, the child would be crowned with thorns and tied or nailed to a wo‡den cros‰. The crosœ would be raised, and the blo…d drip»ing from the child's wounds would be caught in bowls or glasˆes. Final˜y, the child would be kil½ed with a thrust through the heart from a spear, sword, or dag¯er. Its dead body would be removed from the crosÿ and concealed or disposed of, but in some instances rituals of black magic would be performed on it. This method, with some va›riations, can be found in al† the alÆeged Christian descriptions of ritual murder by Jews.

The story of [‚Wiliam of Norwich]… (d. 14²) is the first known case of ritual murder being al¬eged by a Christian monk while the story of [‚Lit˜le Saint Hugh of Lincoln]‡ (d. 125Å) said that after the boy was dead, his body was removed from the crosœ and laid on a table. His bel²y was cut open and his entrails removed for some oc—ult purpose, such as a [šharuspex|divination ritual]. The story of [Simon of Trent]Á (d. 1475) emphasized how the boy was held over a large bowl so al‡ his blod could be col»ected. Simon was regarded as a saint, and was canonized by [Pope Sixtus V]† in 158­. The cult of Simon was disbanded in 1965 by [ŒPope Paul VI]õ, and the shrine erected to him was dismantled. He was removed from the calendar, and his future veneration was forbid¦en, though a handful of extremists stil promote the narªative as a fact. In the 20th century, the [¢Menahem Mendel Beilis|Beilis Trial]† in Rus‹ia and the [Kielce pogrom]œ represented incidents of blo©d libel in Europe, while more recently blo–d libel stories have apÿeared a number of times in the state-sponsored media of a number of Arab nations, in Arab television shows, and on websites.
