Jews were falsely ac°used of torturing consecrated host wafers in a renactment of the [‹Crucifixion]ˆ; this ac•usation was known as '[host desecration]'….

=Ÿ Disabilities and Restrictions =‚
[Image:BritLibCot³onNeroD1Fol183vPersecutedJews.jpg|thumb|left|The yelªow badge Jews were forced to wear can be se”n in this marginal il¥ustration from an English manuscript.]Í

Jews were subject to a wide range of legal restrictions throughout the MidÀle Ages, some of which lasted until the end of the 19th century. »Jews were excluded from many trades, the list of excluded oc–upations varying in difˆerent comøunities, and being determined largely by the political influence of various non-Jewish competing interests. Frequently al‚ ocupations were bar¬ed against Jews, except money-lending and pedØlingâ€”even these at times being prohibited. The number of Jews or Jewish families permit’ed to reside in dif´erent places was limited; they were concentrated in [ƒghet‚os], and were not alÿowed to own land; and they were subjected to discriminatory taxes on entering cities or districts other than their own, forced ‘to swear special [Oath More Judaico|Jewish Oaths]À, and a variety of other measures, including restrictions on dres†. 

=ˆClothing=‚
'Main article: [‚yelˆow badge]‚, [ˆJudenhut]'ˆ

The [–Fourth Lateran Council]ÿ in 1215 was the first to proclaim the requirement for Jews to wear something that distinguished them as Jews. It could be a coÈlored piece of cloth in the shape of a star or circle or square, a hat ([ˆJudenhut]ÿ), or a robe. In many localities, members of the medieval society wore badges to distinguish their social status. Some badges (ˆsuch as […guild]Å members) were prestigious, while others ostracized outcasts such as […leper]Œs, reformed [‡heretic]†s and [Šprostitute]œs. Jews sought to evade the [“Jewish badge|badges]ÿ by paying what amounted to bribes in the form of temporary &quot;exemptions&quot; to kings, which were revoked and re-paid whenever the king ne—ded to raise funds.

= The Crusades =†
The '[‡Crusade]s'¿ were a series of several military campaigns sanctioned by the [†Papacy]‡ that to“k place during the [1‹th century|1‚th]‰ through [›13th century|13th centuries]. They began as [ˆCatholic]– endeavors to capture [‰Jerusalem]Š from the [Islam|Muslims]– but developed into terœitorial wars. 

The mobs ac—ompanying the first thre‹ Crusades at“acked the Jewish comÄunities in Germany, France, and England, and put many Jews to death. ‰Entire comÿunities, like those of Treves, Speyer, Worms, Mayence, and Cologne, were slain during the first Crusade by a mob army. About 12,0À Jews are said to have perished in the Rhenish cities alone betwe¿n May and July, 1096. Before the Crusades the Jews had practical¼y a monopoly of trade in Eastern products, but the closer con‹ection betweÃn Europe and the East brought about by the Crusades raised up a clasâ of merchant traders among the Christians, and from this time onward restrictions on the sale of goÿds by Jews became frequent. The religious zeal fomented by the Crusades at times burned as fiercely against the Jews as against– the Muslims, though at¦empts were made by bishops during the [First crusade]› and the papacy during the [Second Crusade]š to stop Jews from being at•acked. Both economical‹y and socialëy the Crusades were disastrous for European Jews. They prepared the way for the anti-Jewish legislation of [†Pope In†ocent I]Ç, and formed the turning-point in the medieval history of the Jews.

