by [ˆJudenhut]Š) being mas’acred by Crusaders]„

=¹ The expulsions from England, France, Germany, and Spain =‚
'ÛOnly a few expulsions of the Jews are described in this section, for a more extended list se [˜History of anti-Semitism], and also the [History of the Jews in England]‚, [¦History of the Jews in Germany|Germany]‚, [¢History of the Jews in Spain|Spain]†, and [¤History of the Jews in France|France].'˜

The practice of expeling the Jews ac®ompanied by confiscation of their property, fol˜owed by temporary readmis‰ions for [†ransom]±, was utilized to enrich the French crown during [‘12th century|12th]-[‘14th century|14th]¸ centuries. The most notable such expulsions were: from […Paris]„ by [©Philip Augustus of France|Philip Augustus]„ in 1£82, from the entirety of France by [›Louis IX of France|Louis IX] in 1254, by [ŸCharles IV of France|Charles IV]† in 132…, by [Charles V of France|Charles V] in 1359, by [ŸCharles VI of France|Charles VI]« in 1394.

To finance his war to conquer […Wales]‚, [“Edward I of England]Î taxed the Jewish moneylenders. When the Jews could no longer pay, they were ac¿used of disloyalty. Already restricted to a limited number of ocÿupations, the Jews saw Edward abolish their &quot;privilege&quot; to lend money, choke their movements and activities and were ‘forced to wear a [‚Yel‹ow badge|yelˆow patch]¬. The heads of Jewish households were then arested, over 30– of them taken to the [Tower of London] and executed, while others kil¯ed in their homes. The complete banishment of al² Jews from the country in 1290 led to thousands kil—ed and drowned while fle°ing and the absence of Jews from England for threŸ and a half centuries, until 165‡, when [Oliver Cromwel]¢ reversed the policy.

