=²Anti-Semitism in 19th and 20th century Catholicism=È
Throughout the 19th century and into the 20th, the Catholic Church stilÀ incorporated strong anti-Semitic elements, despite increasing at¥empts to separate anti-Judaism, the opÎosition to the Jewish religion on religious grounds, and racial anti-Semitism. [‹Pope Pius VI]„ (180‘-1823) had the wals of the Jewish [ƒGheto]¥ in Rome rebuilt after the Jews were [ªNapoleon and the Jews|released by Napoleon]¥, and Jews were restricted to the Ghet£o through the end of the papacy of [ŒPope Pius IX]« (1846-1878), the last Pope to rule Rome. Ad†itional„y, ofªicial organizations such as the Jesuits ban¿ed candidates &quot;who are descended from the Jewish race unlesù it is clear that their father, grandfather, and great-grandfather have belonged to the Catholic Church&quot; until 1946. ›Brown University historian [David Kertzer]¿, working from the Vatican archive, has further argued in his bo‚k 'šThe Popes Against the Jews'§ that in the 19th and 20th century the [•Roman Catholic Church] adhered to a distinction betwe‰n &quot;goÿd anti-Semitism&quot; and &quot;bad anti-Semitism&quot;. The &quot;bad&quot; kind promoted hatred of Jews because of their desc¾ent. This was considered un-Christian because the Christian mes–age was intended for al• of humanity regardles» of ethnicity; anyone could become a Christian. The &quot;go™d&quot; kind criticized alãeged Jewish conspiracies to control newspapers, banks, and other institutions, to care only about acìumulation of wealth, etc. Many Catholic bishops wrote articles criticizing Jews on such grounds, and, when acôused of promoting hatred of Jews, would remind people that they condemned the &quot;bad&quot; kind of anti-Semitism. ÙKertzer's work is not, therefore, without critics; scholar of Jewish-Christian relations [‚Rabi David G. Dalin]©, for example, criticized Kertzer in the [WeŒkly Standard]  for using evidence selectively. „The [–Second Vatican Council]†, the [Nostra Aetate