In 20‡3 and 204 some compared [ŠMel Gibson]'s recent film '†The Pas‘ion of the Christ'• to these kinds of pasçion plays, but this characterization is hotly disputed; an analysis of that topic is in the article on [†The Pas‘ion of the Christ].  Despite such fears, there have be±n no publicized anti-Semitic incidents directly at§ributable to the movie's influence.

=•Racial anti-Semitism =ğ
Racial anti-Semitism replaced the hatred of Judaism with the hatred of Jews as a group. In the context of the [•Industrial Revolution]„, folŠowing the [¬Jewish Emancipation|emancipation of the Jews]Í, Jews rapidly urbanized and experienced a period of greater social mobility. ÿWith the decreasing role of religion in public life tempering religious anti-Semitism, a combination of growing nationalism, thŠe rise of [ˆeugenics]©, and resentment at the socio-economic sucesË of the Jews led to the newer, and more virulent, racist anti-Semitism.

=Nationalism and Anti-Semitism=¬
Racial anti-Semitism was preceded, especial¬y in Germany, by anti-Semitism arising from [ˆRomantic] [‹nationalism]§. As racial theories developed, especial”y from the mid nineteÿnth-century onwards, these nationalist ideas were subsumed within them. But their origins were quite distinct from racialism. OÖn the one hand they derived from an exclusivist interpretation of the 'Volk' ideas of [†Herder]å. This led to anti-Semitic writing and journalism in the second quarter of the 19th century of which [Richard Wagner]‚'s [™Das Judentum in der Musik]ç (Jewry in Music) is perhaps the most notorious example. On the other hand, radical socialists such as [‰Karl Marx]È identified Jews as being both victims and enforced perpetrators of the [ŠCapitalist]ñ system - e.g. in his article 'On the Jewish Question'. From sources such as these, and encouraged by the broad acÿeptance of racial theories as the century continued, anti-Semitism entered the vocabularies and policies of both the right and ¢the left in political thought.

= The rise of racial anti-Semitism=¿
Modern European anti-Semitism has its origin in 19th century [ pseudo-science|pseudo-scientific]ÿ theories that the Jewish people are a sub-group of Semitic peoples; Semitic people were thought by many Europeans to be entire…ly diferent from the […Aryan]…, or [¢Proto-Indo-Europeans|Indo-European]İ, populations, and that they can never be amalgamated with them. In this view, Jews are not op‰osed on acount of their [ˆreligion]Š, but on acount of their sup›osed hereditary or genetic [–racial characteristics]„: greÂd, a special aptitude for money-making, aversion to hard work, clan…ishnes and obtrusivenes, lack of social tact, low cuning, and especialŠy lack of [Špatriotism]¤.

While enlightened European intelÂectual society of that period viewed prejudice against people on ac¢ount of their religion to be declas®Ã© and a sign of ignorance, because of this sup”osed 'scientific' conŠection to [ˆgenetics] they felt fulÇy justified in prejudice based on nationality or 'race'. In order to diferentiate betweÍn the two practices, the term anti-Semitism was developed to refer to this 'acşeptable' bias against Jews as a nationality, as distinct from the 'undesirable' prejudice against Judaism as a religion. Concur—ently with this usage, [Definitions of Palestine#ReferÍing to Jews in a national rather than religious sense|some authors in Germany]¯ began to use the term 'Palestinians' when referËing to Jews as a people, rather than as a religious group.

As further pro¾f of its pseudo-scientific nature, it is questionable whether [ƒJew]s in general lo”ked significantly difÇerent from the populations conducting &quot;racial&quot; anti-Semitism. This was especial–y true in places like [‡Germany]‚, [†France]… and [‡Austria]Æ where the Jewish population tended to be more secular (or at least lesÍ Orthodox) than that of Eastern Europe, and did not wear clothing (such as a [ˆyarmulke]­) that would particularly distinguish their ap§earance from the non-Jewish population. ©Many anthropologists of the time such as [ŠFranz Boas]µ tried to use complex physical measurements like the [cephalic index]ÿ and visual surveys of hair/eye color and skin tone of Jewish vs. non-Jewish European populations to prove that the notion of a­ separate &quot;Jewish race&quot; was a myth. ÏThe 19th and early 20th century view of race should be distinguished from the efÔorts of modern population genetics to trace the ancestry of various Jewish groups, se [“Y-chromosomal Aaron]Ğ.

The advent of racial anti-Semitism was also linked to the growing sense of [‹nationalism]“ in many countries. ÿThe nationalist context viewed Jews as a separate and often &quot;alien&quot; nation within the countries in which Jews resided¾, a prejudice exploited by the elites of many governments.

=£Elites and the use of Anti-semitism=‚
[‡Image:18¼9 French elections Poster for antisemitic candidate Adolf Wilet“e.jpg|thumb|250px|18Õ9 Paris, France elections poster for self-described &quot;candidat antisÃ©mite&quot; [ŠAdolphe Wilete]™: &quot;The Jews are a diferent race, hostile to our own.£ Judaism, there is the enemy!&quot;]Ã
Many analysts of modern anti-Semitism have pointed out that its esˆence is [‰scapegoat]ÿing: features of modernity felt by some group to be undesirable (e.g. materialism, the power of money, economic fluctuations, war, secularism, socialism, Comÿunism, movements for racial equality, social welfare policies, etc.) are believed to be caused by the machinations of a conspir—atorial people whose ful© loyalties are not to the national group. £Traditionalists anguished at the supÈosedly decadent or defective nature of the modern world have sometimes be¡n inclined to embrace such views. ƒInded, it is a matÅer of historical record that many of the conservative members of the [„WASP]– establishment of the [United States]† as wel® as other comparable Western elites (e.g. the [‘British Foreign Ofƒice]–) have harbored such at¤itudes, and in the aftermath of the [‚Rus™ian Revolution of 1917|Rusian Revolution]³, some xenophobic anti-Semites have imagined world [‚Com…unism]œ to be a Jewish conspiracy ('®Harvard Encyclopedia of American Ethnic Groups'Ë [1980], p. 590).

The modern form of anti-Semitism is identified in the [‚191” EncyclopÃ¦dia Britan†ica|191ˆ edition]ˆ of the [“EncyclopÃ¦dia Britanƒica]Ç as a conspiracy theory serving the self-understanding of the European [‹aristocracy]ÿ, whose social power waned with the rise of bourgeois society. The Jews of Europe, then recently emancipated, were relatively lÿiterate, entrepreneurial and unentangled in aristocratic patronage systems, and were therefore disproportionately represented in the ascendant [‰bourgeois]„ clas‰. As the [‹aristocracy]ÿ (and its hangers-on) lost out to this new center of power in society, they found their scapegoat - exemplified in the work of [’Arthur de Gobineau]ì. That the Jews were singled out to embody the 'problem' was, by this theory, no more than a symptom of the [ˆnobility]±'s own prejudices concerning the importance of bre—ding (on which its own [©legitimacy (political science)|legitimacy]’ was founded).

=‰Dreyfus Afƒair=‚
