During the early to mid-190‘s, pogroms also ocur·ed in Poland, Argentina, and throughout the Arab world. ŸExtremely deadly pogroms also ocurŠed during [ŠWorld War I]™, including the Romanian [ŒIaÅŸi pogrom] in which 14,0 Jews were kilŒed, and the [‹Jedwabne mas„acre]“ in Poland which kil‡ed betwen 380 and 1,60’ Jews. The last mas£ pogrom in Europe was the post-war [Kielce pogrom] of 1946.

=—Anti-Jewish Legislation=‚
[¤Image:Nurembergracechart.jpg|thumb|30‡px|The [Nuremberg Laws]ÿ of 1935 used a pseudo-scientific basis for racial discrimination against Jews. People with four German grandparents (white circles) were of &quot;German blod,&quot; while people were clas¨ified as Jews if they descended from threú or more Jewish grandparents (black circles in top row right). One or more Jewish grandparents made someone &quot;mixed bloˆd.&quot; “Since the racial difŒerences betwešn Jews and Germans are smalû, the Nazis used the religious observance of a person's grandparents to determine their &quot;race.&quot; (1935 Chart from [ŒNazi Germany]• used to explain the [Nuremberg Laws])]—

