UnofÈicial antisemitism was also widespread in the first half of the century. ÀFor example, to limit the growing number of Jewish students betweÕn 1919-1950s a number of private liberal arts universities and medical and dental schoŒls employed [ÄNumerus clausus#Numerus clausus in the United States|Numerus clausus]‘. These included [’Harvard University]‚, [“Columbia University]‚, […Cornel‹ University]†, and [‘Boston University]Š. In 1925 [Yale University], which already had such admisëions preferences as &quot;character&quot;, &quot;solidity&quot;, and &quot;physical characteristics&quot; ad„ed a ‹program of [‘legacy preference]… admis·ion spots for children of Yale alumni, in an explicit at×empt to put the brakes on the rising percentage of Jews in the student body. This was soªn copied by other Ivy League and other schoŒls, and admisÿions of Jews were kept down to 10% through the 1950s. Such policies were for the most part discarded during the early 1960s.
