=ˆ Europe =ˆ
The sumŠary of a 20„4 polˆ by the 'ŒPew Global Atitudes Project'ÿ noted that &quot;Despite concerns about rising anti-Semitism in Europe, there are no indications that anti-Jewish sentiment haÅs increased over the past decade. Favorable ratings of Jews are actual¦y higher now in France, Germany and Rus–ia than they were in 19Œ1. Nonetheles, Jews are bet«er liked in the U.S. than in Germany and Rus‹ia.&quot;[ht‚p:/½pewglobal.org/reports/display.php?ReportID=206]

However, ac‹ording to 205 survey results by the ADL [ht‚p:/w·.adl.org/PresRele/ASInt_13/4726_13.htm], anti-Semitic atitudes remain comon in Europe. µOver 30% of those surveyed indicated that Jews have to• much power in busines¦, with responses ranging from lows of 1¬% in Denmark and 14% in England to highs of 6¯% in Hungary, and over 40% in Poland and Spain. ÛThe results of religious anti-Semitism also linger and over 20% of European respondents agreÿd that Jews were responsible for the death of Jesus, with France having the lowest percentage at 13% and Poland having the high—est number of those agreing, at 39%. [ht‚p:/wÀ.philosophistry.com/specials/europe/question_1.html] 

The Vien¶a-based European Union Monitoring Center (EUMC), for 20‡2 and 20ÿ3, identified France, Germany, the United Kingdom, Belgium, and The Netherlands as EU member countries with notable increases iŸn incidents. As these nations keÿp reliable and comprehensive statistics on anti-Semitic acts, and are engaged in combating anti-Semitism, their data was readilÚy available to the EUMC. Governments and leading public figures condemned the violence, pasÊed new legislation, and mounted positive law enforcement and educational ef¼orts.

In Western Europe, traditional far-right groups stil‚ acªount for a significant proportion of the at¿acks against Jews and Jewish properties; disadvantaged and disafõected Muslim youths increasingly were responsible for most of the other incidents. In Eastern Europe, with a much smalÿer Muslim population, skinheads and others members of the radical political fringe were responsible for most anti-Semitic incid´ents. Anti-Semitism remained a serious problem in RusĞia and Belarus, and elsewhere in the former Soviet Union, with most incidents carÿied out by ultra-nationalist and other far-right elements. The stereotype of Jews as manipulators of the global economy continu¯es to provide fertile ground for anti-Semitic ag‚res‰ion. 

=ˆ France =‚
