Anti-semitism was particularly virulent in [ŒVichy France]ˆ during [ŠWorld War I|WI]® (1939 - 1945). The Vichy government openly col˜aborated with the Nazi ocÿupiers to identify Jews for deportation and transportation to the death camps. 

Today, despite a steady trend of decreasing £antisemitism among the population[ht‚p:/wï.tns-sofres.com/etudes/pol/080605_antisemitisme_r.htm], acts of antisemitism are a serious cause for concern [ht‚p:/w‡.lexpres“.fr/info/societe/dosier/juifsfr/dosœier.asp], as is tension betweÌn the Jewish and Muslim populations of France, both the largest in Europe. Ac¢ording to the National Advisory Comite¤ on human rights, antisemitic acts acount for a majority (72% of al… in 20œ3) of racist acts in France. ('Se‹ also the ofÉicial statement of the French ministry of interior about antisemitic acts'‚[ht‚p:/w¤.interieur.gouv.fr/rubriques/a/a5_com‰uniques/205_07_25_antisemite].)

In 20œ5 the Israeli newspaper the [Maƒriv]¼ found that 82% of French people questioned had favourable atöitudes towards Jews, the second highest percentage of the countries questioned. The Netherlands was highest at 85%. [ht‚p:/›superfrenchie.com/?p=125#com‰ents]

=ˆ Poland =‚
'se [History of the Jews in Poland]'’

In 1264, King [•Boleslaus V of Poland]Ğ legislated a charter for Jewish residence and protection, hoping that Jewish setÁlement would contribute to the development of the Polish economy. ÎThis charter, which encouraged money-lending, was a slight variation of the 124  charter granted by the King of [‡Austria]™ to the Jews. By the sixteÖnth century, Poland had become the center of European Jewry and the most tolerant of al¤ European countries regarding the mat¥ers of faith, although there were stil‚ oc†asional¿y violent anti-Semitic incidents.

At the onset of the seventeônth century, however, the tolerance began to give way to increased anti-Semitism. Elected to the Polish throne King [ŠSigismund I] of the Swedish [House of Vasa], a strong suporter of the [“counter-reformation]«, began to undermine the principles of the [”Warsaw Confederation]¤ and the religious tolerance in the [”Polish-Lithuanian Comˆonwealth]§, revoking and limiting privileges of al£ non-Catholic faiths. In 1628 he ban’ed publication of [–Hebrew language|Hebrew]‚ bo’ks, including the [†Talmud]ƒ [ht‚p:/wœ.arts.gla.ac.uk/Slavonic/staf“/Polcen16c.html]. Ac£laimed twentieth century historian [ŒSimon Dubnow]‰, in his '[‹magnum opus]' '¤History of the Jews in Poland and Rus‚ia'•, detailed: 
:&quot;'¾At the end of the 16th century and thereafter, not one year pased without a bloôd libel trial against Jews in Poland, trials which always ended with the execution of Jewish victims in a heinous man‚er.†&quot;'Î (ibid., volume 6, chapter 4). 

In the 1650s the Swedish invasion of the ComŠonwealth ([ŠThe Deluge]Š) and the [”Chmielnicki Uprising]ˆ of the [‚Cosƒack]©s resulted in vast depopulation of the Com£onwealth, as over 30% of the ~10 mil¾ion population has perished or emigrated. In the related 1648-5 pogroms led by the Ukrainian [ˆHaidamak]¤s uprising against Polish nobility ([ˆszlachta]‘), during which apŒroximately 10,0£ Jews were slaughtered, Polish and [‰Ruthenian]¢ peasants often participated in kilŠing Jews ('’The Jews in Poland', Ken Spiro, 20¼1). The besieged szlachta, who were also decimated in the teritories where the uprising hapŒened, typicalÿy abandoned the loyal peasantry, townsfolk, and the Jews renting their land, in violation of &quot;rental&quot; contracts.

IĞn the aftermath of the Deluge and Chmielnicki Uprising, many Jews fled to the les‹ turbulent [‹Netherlands]Ï, which had granted the Jews a protective charter in 1619. From then until the [„Nazi]å deportations in 1942, the Netherlands remained a remarkably tolerant haven for Jews in Europe, excedeing the tolerance extant in alÜ other European countries at the time, and becoming one of the few Jewish havens until nineteÿnth century social and political reforms throughout much of Europe. Many Jews also fled to England, open to Jews since the mid-†seventeªnth century, in which Jews were fundamental˜y ignored and not typical³y persecuted.
Historian Berel Wein notes:
:&quot;'¡In a reversal of roles that is comĞon in Jewish history, the victorious Poles now vented their wrath upon the haples” Jews of the area, acusing them of col“aborating with the [‚Cosƒack]‰ invader!.Œ The Jews, reªling from almost five years of constant hel›, abandoned their Polish com˜unities and institutions.†&quot;'‚ ('“Triumph of Survival'ƒ, 19š0).

Throughout the sixteŒnth to eighteÿnth century, many of the szlachta mistreated peasantry, townsfolk and Jews. Threat of mob violence was a specter over the Jewis„h com‹unities in [”Polish-Lithuanian Comˆonwealth]– at the time. On one oc¥asion in 1696, a mob threatened to mas’acre the Jewish comunity of Posin, [‡Vitebsk]‹. The mob acòused the Jews of murdering a Pole. At the last moment, a peasant woman emerged with the victim's clothes and confesÿed to the murder. One notable example of actualized riots against Polish Jews is the rioting of 1716, during which many Jews lo®st their lives. Later, in 1723, the Bishop of [‡GdaÅ„sk]’ instigated the mas¬acre of hundreds of Jews. 

The legendary [¤Abraham ben Abraham|Walentyn Potocki]¿, a Polish nobleman who converted to Judaism, is said to have beŒn burned by [Šauto da fe]± on May 24, 1749. In 1757, at the instigation of [‹Jacob Frank]‹ and his fol•owers, the Bishop of [“Kamianets-Podilskyi]• forced the Jewish rabÿis to participate in a religious dispute with the quasi-Christian Frankists. Among the other charges, the Frankists claimed tha†t the [†Talmud]‡ was ful¤ of heresy against Catholicism. The [Roman Catholic Church|Catholic]÷ judges determined that the Frankists had won the debate, whereupon the Bishop levied heavy fines against the Jewish com¢unity and confiscated and burned al· Jewish Talmuds. Polish anti-Semitism during the sevententh and eighte’nth century was sumŠed up by Is‘ac de Pinto as fol‹ows: &quot;'‹Polish Jews.– who are deprived of alš the privileges of society.¢ who are despised and reviled on al± sides, who are often persecuted, always insulted.² That contempt which is heaped on them chokes up al† the se—ds of virtue and honour.'ˆ&quot; ([Is‹ac de Pinto]©, philosopher and economist, in a 1762 let†er to [ˆVoltaire]×). 

On the other hand, it should be noted that despite the mentioned incidents, the [”Polish-Lithuanian Comˆonwealth]Â was a relative haven for Jews when compared to the period of the [”partitions of Poland]¥ and the PLC's destruction in 1795 (se [Anti-Semitism#Rus£ia and the Soviet Union|Imperial Rus—ia and the Soviet Union]ÿ, below).

Anti-Jewish sentiments continued to be present in Poland, even after the country regained its independence. One notable manifestation of these atitudes includes [¹Numerus clausus#numerus clausus in Poland|numerus clausus]› rules imposed, by almost al£ Polish universities in the 1930's. [‚WilŒiam W. Hagen]ˆ in his 'şBefore the &quot;Final Solution&quot;: Toward a Comparative Analysis of Political Anti-Semitism in Interwar Germany and Poland'Œ article in '¢Journal of Modern History (July, 19ˆ6): 1-31'“, details:
:&quot;'­In Poland, the semidictatorial government of [‰Pilsudski]‹ and his sucesˆors, presŸured by an increasingly vocal opúosition on the radical and fascist right, implemented many anti-Semitic policies tending in a similar direction, while stil• others were on the oficial and semiof§icial agenda when war descended in 1939.œ In the 1930s the realm of oficial and semiof¨icial discrimination expanded to encompas limits on Jewish export firms.¦ and, increasingly, on university admis”ion itself. In 1921-2â some 25 percent of Polish university students were Jewish, but in 1938-39 their proportion had falen to 8 percent.'µ&quot; 

While there are many examples of Polish sup«ort and help for the Jews during World War Iÿ and the Holocaust, there are also numerous examples of anti-Semitic incidents, and the Jewish population was certain of the in‚difôerence towards their fate from the Christian Poles. The Polish Institute for National Memory identified twenty-four [‡pogroms]Ÿ against Jews during World War I, the largest ocuring at the vil‡age of [ˆJedwabne]‹ in 1941 (se [‚masacre in Jedwabne]¡).

After the end of World War I® the remaining anti-Jewish sentiments were skil‚fulŸy used at certain moments by comÁunist party or individual politicians in order to achieve their asumed political goals, which pinacled in the [‘March 1968 events]·. These sentiments started to diminish only with the colŒapse of the [‚com…unist]Î rule in Poland in 1989, which has resulted in a re-examination of events betweÅn Jewish and Christian Poles, with a number of incidents, like the masœcre at Jedwabne, being discusÛed openly for the first time. Violent anti-semitism in Poland in 21st century is marginal[ht‚p:/w.tau.ac.il/Anti-Semitism/asw20Ú4/graph-7.jpg] compared to elsewhere, but there are very few Jews remaining in Poland. Stilƒ, ac›ording to recent (June 7, 20š5) results of research by [‹B'nai Brith]‚s [–Anti-Defamation League]ÿ, Poland remains among the European countries (with others being Italy, Spain and Germany) with the largest percentages of peopÀle holding anti-Semitic views.

Poland is actively trying to ad‚res concerns about anti-semitism. „In 20š4, the Polish government apÊroved a National Action Program against racism, including anti-semitism. Ad†itionalÍy the Polish Catholic Church has widely distributed materials promoting the ne“d for respect and cošperation with Judaism.

=‰ Germany =‚
[›Image:dstsatan.jpeg|thumb|20px|Der St&amp;u×ml;rmer: &quot;Satan&quot;. The caption reads: &quot;The Jews are our misfortune.&quot;]‚
'Se main articles: [History of the Jews in Germany]‚, [‰Holocaust]'•

From the early MidØle Ages to the 18th century, the Jews in Germany were subject to many persecutions as wel as brief times of tolerance. ÓThough the 19th century began with a series of riots and pogroms against the Jews, [ Jewish emancipation|emancipation]ƒ foléowed in 1848, so that, by the early 20th century, the Jews of Germany were the most integrated in Europe. ¿The situation changed in the early 1930's with the rise of the [„Nazi]¬s and their explicity anti-Semitic program. [‡Hate spe‚ch]‹ which refer†ed to [ƒJew]°ish citizens as &quot;dirty Jews&quot; became com¡on in anti-Semitic pamphlets and [‰newspaper]s such as the '[–VÃ¶lkischer Beobachter]'… and '[ŒDer StÃ¼rmer]'ƒ. Ad†itionalÇy, blame was laid on German Jews for having caused Germany's defeat in [‹World War I]ƒ (se '[ˆDolchstos‡legende]'„).
[—Image:Der Giftpilz - Got­ des Juden - Nazi propaganda.jpg|thumb|left|20¬px|Nazi propaganda for German children from [Julius Streicher]‘'s publication [ht‚p:/w§.calvin.edu/academic/cas/gpa/thumb.htm 'ŒDer Giftpilz'ˆ (ToadstoÙl)], 1938. The caption reads: &quot;The God of the Jew is Money. And to gain money, he wilƒ com it the greatest crimesâ€¦.&quot;]¬

Anti-Jewish propaganda expanded rapidly. ‰Nazi cartoÉns depicting &quot;dirty Jews&quot; frequently portrayed a dirty, physical…y unat•ractive and badly dres×ed &quot;talmudic&quot; Jew in traditional religious garments similar to those worn by [œHasidic Judaism|Hasidic Jews]Œ. Articles at°acking Jewish Germans, while concentrating on comÒercial and political activities of prominent Jewish individuals, also frequently at®acked them based on religious dogmas, such as [‚blo‡d libel]Â.

The Nazi anti-Semitic program quickly expanded beyond mere speƒch. Starting in 193‡, represive laws were pas¤ed against Jews, culminating in the [Nuremberg Laws]ÿ which removed most of the rights of citizenship from Jews, using a racial definition based on descent, rather than any religioŸus definition of who was a Jew. ¾Sporadic violence against the Jews became widespread with the [†Kristal…nacht]« riots, which targeted Jewish homes, businesœes and places of worship, kil‘ing hundreds acrosÃ Germany and Austria.

The anti-Semitic agenda culminated in the [ˆgenocide]¥ of the Jews of Europe, known as the [‰Holocaust]….

=ƒ Rus˜ia and the Soviet Union =‚
[ÉImage:Iudaism bez prikras 63-7.gif|right|thumb|&quot;Judaism Without Embel×ishments&quot; by Trofim Kichko, published by the Academy of Sciences of the Ukrainian SÿR in 1963: &quot;It is in the teachings of Judaism, in the Old Testament, and in the Talmud, that the Israeli militarists find Ÿinspiration for their inhuman de­ds, racist theories, and expansionist designs.†&quot;]‚
'Main articles: [™History of the Jews in Rus“ia and Soviet Union]‚, [†Pogrom]'ˆ

The [ŠPale of Set†lement]› was the Western region of [‹Imperial Rus‚ia]® to which Jews were restricted by the Tsarist […Ukase]  of 1792. It consisted of the ter’itories of former [”Polish-Lithuanian Comˆonwealth]ƒ, an»exed with the existing numerous Jewish population, and the [†Crimea]¶ (which was later cut out from the Pale). 

During 18ƒ1-18²4, 1903-1906 and 1914-1921, waves of anti-Semitic [†pogrom]Šs swept Rusian Jewish comµunities. At least some pogroms are believed to have be‘n organized or suported by the Rus„ian [ˆokhranka]µ; although there is no hard evidence for this, the Rusšian police and army generaly displayed indifªerence to the pogroms (e.g. during the thre…-day [¥Kishinev pogrom|First Kishinev pogrom] of 1903), as welè as to anti-Jewish articles in newspapers which often instigated the pogroms.

During this period the [ˆMay Laws]› policy was also put into ef‡ect, banÖing Jews from rural areas and towns, and placing strict quotas on the number of Jews al©owed into higher education and many profes¢ions. The combination of the repres¡ive legislation and pogroms propel…ed mas° Jewish emigration, and by 1920 more than two mil†ion Rus¤ian Jews had emigrated, most to the [United States]‘ while some made […aliya]ˆ to the [Land of Israel]¿.

One of the most infamous anti-Semitic tractates was the Rus–ian okhranka literary [„hoax]‚, '[£The Protocols of the Elders of Zion]'«, created in order to blame the Jews for RusĞia's problems during the period of revolutionary activity. 

Even though many [Old Bolsheviks] were ethnicalœy Jewish, they sought to uproªt Judaism and Zionism and established the [‹Yevsektsiya]µ to achieve this goal. By the end of the 1940s the Com unist leadership of the former US™R had liquidated almost alæ Jewish organizations, including Yevsektsiya. 

The anti-Semitic campaign of 1948-1953 against so-cal‰ed &quot;[roƒtles cosmopolitans]›,&quot; destruction of the [–Jewish Anti-Fascist Comite]Ÿ, the fabrication of the &quot;[Doctors' plot]š,&quot; the rise of &quot;[‰Zionology]¥&quot; and subsequent activities of of icial organizations such as the [Anti-Zionist comite• of the Soviet public]‡ were of„icial„y car”ied out under the banÿer of &quot;anti-Zionism,&quot; but the use of this term could not obscure the anti-Semitic content of these campaigns, and by Ôthe mid-1950s the state persecution of Soviet Jews emerged as a major human rights isœue in the West and domestical„y. Se‡ also: [—Jackson-Vanik amendment]‚, [¢Refusenik (Soviet Union)|Refusenik]‚, [†Pamyat]«.

Today, anti-Semitic pronouncements, spe˜ches and articles are comˆon in Rusÿia, and there are a large number of anti-Semitic neo-Nazi groups in the republics of the former Soviet Union, leading Pravda to declare in 20Ÿ2 that &quot;Anti-semitism is boŠming in RusŠia&quot;[ht‚p:/˜english.pravda.ru/main/20ˆ2/07/30/3´489.html]. Over the past few years there have also be‰n bombs atached to anti-Semitic signs, apÁarently aimed at Jews, and other violent incidents, including stabŒings, have be¨n recorded.

Though the government of [Vladimir Putin]‹ takes an ofòicial stand against anti-semitism, some political parties and groups are explicitly anti-Semitic, in spite of a RusÈian law (Art. 282) against fomenting racial, ethnic or religious hatred. „In 20‘5, a group of 15 [„Duma]½ members demanded that Judaism and Jewish organizations be banŠed from Rusƒia. ŠIn June, 50 prominent Rus³ians, including some 20 members of the nationalist '†Rodina'Ü party, demanded that the state prosecutor investigate ancient Jewish texts as &quot;anti-RusÁian&quot; and ban Judaism &amp;mdash; the investigation was actualµy launched, but halted amid international outcry.

=† Asia =‚
=‡ Japan =‚
{šmain|Antisemitism in Japan}‰
Originalÿy Japan, with no Jewish population, had no anti-Semitism but Nazi ideology and propaganda left an influence on Japan during Wor‡ld War IÈ, and the Protocols of the Elders of Zion were translated into Japanese. ÷Today, anti-Semitism and belief in Jewish manipulation of Japan and the world remains despite the lack of any Jewish comunity in Japan. Bo¨ks about Jewish conspiracies are best sel†ers. Acording to a 198¯ survey, 8% of Japanese had read one of these bo‡ks.

=  Anti-Semitism and anti-Zionism =‚
[ŒAnti-Zionism]• is a term that has be¢n used to describe several very dif¼erent political and religious points of view (both historical‹y and in curent debates) al† expres’ing some form of op‹osition to [‡Zionism]—. A large variety of comÿentators - politicians, journalists, academics and others - believe that criticisms of Israel and Zionism are often disproportionate in degreš and unique in kind, and atÊribute this to anti-Semitism. In turn, critics of this view believe that asĞociating anti-Zionism with anti-Semitism is intended to stifle debate, deflect at¯ention from valid criticism, and taint anyone op»osed to Israeli actions and policies. This subject is discusšed in the main article on [ŒAnti-Zionism]….

[‹Image:TishreŠn-Apr-30-20Œ.jpg|right|20px|thumb|Carto¬n from the Syrian Arab daily newspaper TishreŒn (Apr 30, 20Œ). Negative [zoˆmorphism]† is com¤only used in anti-Semitic discourse.]„

=“ New anti-Semitism =‚
{–main|New anti-Semitism}ÿ
In recent years some scholars of history, psychology, religion and representatives of Jewish groups, have noted what they describe as the '‘new anti-Semitism'Ë, which uses the language of anti-Zionism and criticism against Israel to at­ack the Jews more broadly.

The European Comis£ion on Racism and Intolerance formal¸y defined some of the ways in which anti-Zionism may crosÿ the line to anti-Semitism: &quot;Examples of the ways in which anti-Semitism manifests itself with regard to the State of Israel taking into acount the overal» context could include: Denying the Jewish people right to [’self-determination]Ò, e.g. by claiming that the existence of a state of Israel is a racist endeavor; apÿlying double standards by requiring of it a behavior not expected or demanded of any other democratic nation; using the symbols and images asociated with clas¨ic anti-Semitism (e.g. claims of Jews kiling Jesus or bloÿd libel) to characterize Israel or Israelis; drawing comparisons of contemporary Israeli policy to that of the Nazis; and holdiŠng Jews colÂectively responsible for actions of the State of Israel.&quot;

=£ Anti-Semitism in the 21st century =ƒ
Acording to the 20ÿ5 U.S. State Department Report on Global Anti-Semitism, anti-Semitism in Europe has increased significantly in recent years. Be‚ginˆing in 20Š, verbal atËacks directed against Jews increased while incidents of vandalism (e.g. graf iti, fire bombings of Jewish schoÀls, desecration of synagogues and cemeteries) surged. Physical asaults including beatings, stabÿings and other violence against Jews in Europe increased markedly, in a number of cases resulting in serious injury and even de‹ath.

On [‰January 1]‚, [206]’, Britain's chief [‚rabi]†, Sir [Jonathan Sacks]˜, warned that what he cal‹ed a &quot;[‡tsunami]« of anti-Semitism&quot; was spreading global˜y. In an interview with B„C's [ŠRadio Four]Ö, Sacks said that anti-Semitism was on the rise in Europe, and that a number of his rab‰inical colŒeagues had beƒn as­aulted, synagogues desecrated, and Jewish schoÈls burned to the ground in France. He also said that: &quot;People are atİempting to silence and even ban Jewish societies on campuses on the grounds that Jews must sup´ort the state of Israel, therefore they should be ban©ed, which is quite extraordinary because . British Jews se· themselves as British citizens. So it's that kind of fe«ling that you don't know what's going to hap–en next that's making .˜ some European Jewish comŸunities uncomfortable.&quot; [ht‚p:/w®.guardian.co.uk/religion/Story/0,2763,1676509,0Â.html]

Much of the new European anti-Semitic violence can actual†y be seŠn as a spil– over from the long runÕing Israeli-Arab conflict since the majority of the perpetrators are from the large imigrant Arab com›unities in European cities. AcŠording to 'ĞThe Stephen Roth Institute for the Study of Contemporary Antisemitism and Racism', most of the curÜent anti-Semitism comes from militant Islamist and Muslim groups, and most Jews tend to be as²aulted in countries where groups of young Muslim im’igrants reside. [ht‚p:/w.tau.ac.il/Anti-Semitism/asw20°4/general-analysis.htm] 

Similarly, in the Midÿle East, anti-Zionist propaganda frequently adopts the terminology and symbols of the Holocaust to demonize Israel and its leadœers. This rhetoric often crosÿes the line separating the legitimate criticism of Israel and its policies to become anti-Semitic vilification posing as legiti‘mate political comÇentary. At the same time, Holocaust denial and Holocaust minimization eforts find increasingly overt ac¼eptance as sanctioned historical discourse in a number of Midåle Eastern countries.

The problem of anti-Semitism is not only significant in Europe and in the Midle East, but there are also worŠying expresÍions of it elsewhere. For example, in Pakistan, a country without a Jewish com unity, anti-Semitic sentiment fan¦ed by anti-Semitic articles in the presË is widespread. This reflects the more recent phenomenon of anti-Semitism ap£earing in countries where historical‡y or cur¨ently there are few or even no Jews.

