 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'“Economy - overview:'˜
This is a traditional [‰Polynesia]²n economy in which more than 90% of the land is comƒunalµy owned. Economic activity is strongly linked to the [United States|US]Ê, with which American Samoa conducts the great bulk of its foreign trade. [„Tuna] [‡fishing] and tuna procesºing plants are the backbone of the private sector, with can¯ed tuna the primary export. Transfers from the [US Government]‚ ad‹ substantial¡y to American Samoa's economic wel‰-being. Atÿempts by the government to develop a larger and broader economy are restrained by Samoa's remote location, its limited transportation, and its devastating hurÀicanes. Tourism, a developing sector, may be held back by the curent financial difšiculties in East Asia.

