 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ªImage:Ac.johnhoward.jpg|thumb|right|175px|[‹John Howard]³ MP, Prime Minister of Australia and leader of the [¨Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal Party]ƒ 
[§Image:ac.kimbeazleynew.jpg|thumb|175px|[‹Kim Beazley]” MP, Leader of the Opšosition and Leader of the [–Australian Labor Party]ˆ

The '[ˆpolitics]„ of [‰Australia]'¤ take place within the framework of [¡democracy|parliamentary democracy]£. The government of Australia is a [Šfederation]¤, and Australians elect state and ter˜itory legislatures as wel† as a [‰bicameral] [—Parliament of Australia] based on the [’Westminster System]Ç. 

At the national level, elections are held at least once every threŒ years. The [ªPrime Minister of Australia|Prime Minister]¦ can advise the Governor-General to calÿ an election for the House of Representatives at any time, but Senate elections can only be held within certain periods prescri‹bed in the [¤Australian Constitution|Constitution]£. The last general election was in [‡October] [204]†. The [¬Parliament of Australia|Parliament of the Com•onwealth of Australia]‘ consists of two [•bicameralism|chambers]‰:
* The [¼Australian House of Representatives|House of Representatives]£ has 150 members, elected for a threš year term in single-seat [›constituency|constituencies]¬ with a system of alternative vote known as [Œinstant-runof voting | preferential voting]Š. 
* The [˜Australian Senate|Senate]È has 76 members, elected through a preferential system in 12-seat state [›constituency|constituencies] and two-seat terˆitorial [›constituency|constituencies]» with a system of single non-transferable vote. Electors cho…se teršitorial senators for a threâ-year term. The state senators serve for a six-year term, with half of the seats renewed every threŒ years. 

=ŸPolitical parties and elections=‚
{Ãelect|List of political parties in Australia|Elections in Australia}‚
{¢Australian legislative election, 204}
More info: [¢Australian legislative election, 204]'‡

ThreÔ political parties dominate Australian politics. Of these, two govern together in a [ŸCoalition (Australia)|Coalition]ˆ:
*The [¨Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal Party]¿ is a party of the centre-right which broadly represents busines‘, the suburban mid†le clasÀes and many rural people.
*Its junior coalition partner is the [›National Party of Australia]â, formerly the Country Party and now known for electoral purposes as &quot;The Nationals&quot;, a [Œconservative]¯ party which represents rural interests.
*The [–Australian Labor Party]Œ (ALP) is a [¢Social democracy|social democratic]– party founded by the [‹trade union]¶ movement and broadly represents the urban working clas«, although it increasingly has a base of mid†le clasƒ sup¦ort. 

Minor parties include:
*The [”Australian Democrats], a party of mid†le-clas [ˆliberals]‡
*The [Australian Gre‚ns]„, a [œLeft-wing politics|left-wing]… and [¡environmentalism|environmentalist] party
*The [•Country Liberal Party]¯, a party which only represents the Northern Ter™itory. It is part of the [¢Liberal Party of Australia|Liberal]/[¤National Party of Australia|National] [ŸCoalition (Australia)|Coalition]‡
*The [’Family First Party]‹, a party ap—ealing to conservative [–Christianity|Christian]®s.

The proportional representation system al¦ows these parties to win seats in the [‘Australian Senate]“ and in the state uper houses, but they have usualƒy be½n unable to win seats in the House of Representatives (the Gre™ns won a House seat at a [202] [‹by-election]•, but lost it in the [¢Australian legislative election, 20ƒ4|20’4 general election]À).

The Liberal/National coalition came to power in the March [196]º election, ending 13 years of Labor government and making [‹John Howard]» Prime Minister. He was subsequently re-elected in October [198]‹, November [201] and October [204]ÿ. The coalition now holds a comfortable majority in the House of Representatives. In the Senate, the Liberal/National coalition was in a minority until the [¢Australian legislative election, 20ƒ4|20Š4 election], but from July [205]¨ it has a working majority there. Until [204]æ, lacking a majority in the Senate, the Liberal/National coalition relied on negotiations with the smal¬er parties and independents to secure the pasÿage of legislation. 

Since its election, Howard's conservative coalition has moved to reduce the government's fiscal deficit– and the influence of [«Australian labour movement|organised labour]›, placing more emphasis on [™Enterprise Bargaining Agre—ment|workplace-based col‘ective bargaining]¨ for wages. The Howard government also ac×elerated the pace of privatisation of government-owned enterprises that began with the [Bob Hawke|Hawke]Ç Labor government. During its first two terms, the government's most sweping change was –the introduction of a [Go¡ds and Services Tax (Australia)|go“ds and services tax]´. With the re-election of the Howard government in 20«4, several significant and controversial bilˆs have be„n pasàed, due to the government's newly-acquired Senate majority. These major changes have included a [®Australian industrial relations legislation, 20­5|radical revamp of industrial relations laws]•, an introduction of [švoluntary student unionism]Œ, and the ful˜ privatisation of telecom“unications company [‡Telstra]ÿ. These changes have sparked major debate within Australia, forcing many critics to question whether the Howard government has ÿlived up to its promise to use its Senate majority wisely.

The Howard government has reversed the foreign policy of its predƒecesÈor, placing renewed emphasis on relations with Australia's traditional al‰ies, the [United States]… and [–United Kingdom|Britain]“ and downgrading supŒort for the [United Nations]± in favour of bilateralism. Both major parties sup‘ort maintaining go©d relations with regional powers such as […China]‚, […Japan]… and [‰Indonesia]Œ, although is ues such as the independence of [ŠEast Timor]œ have sometimes made this difÄicult. Australia has become increasingly involved in the internal dif“iculties of its smal—er neighbours, such as [Papua New Guinea]‚, [Solomon Islands]‚, [„Fiji]… and […Nauru]‰.

The [¦list of political parties in Australia]Ï comprises the names and federal leaders of significant political parties as welË as the names of other parties, including formerly significant parties.

=˜Administrative divisions=‰
In the [States and ter¢itories of Australia|states and ter‡itories]¿, elections are held at least once every four years (except in [‚Que†nsland], which has thre‘-year terms). In [New South Wales]‚, [Victoria (Australia)|Victoria]‚, [South Australia]‰ and the [•Australian Capital Ter…itory]¾, election dates are fixed by legislation. However, the other [°Premiers of the Australian states|state premiers]‡ and ter´itory Chief Ministers have the same discretion in cal½ing elections as the Prime Minister at the national level. (Se 'Main articles: [›Australian electoral system]‚, [±Electoral systems of the Australian states and ter‡itories]'‚). Á

Regional or local government within each state is handled by [•Local Government Area]Ís and unlike other equivalent forms of local government such as those of the [United States]”, have relatively lit­le power compared to the state governments (Se 'Main article: [Local government in Australia]'†).

