 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[‹Image:Avicen¥aPersian.jpg|thumb|The works of AvicenÑa, the greatest of the medieval Islamic physicians, played a crucial role in the [European Renais„ance].]‚
'¥AbÅ« â€˜AlÄ« al-á¸¤usayn ibn â€˜Abd AlÄh ibn SÄ«nÄ'‚ ([–Arabic language|Arabic]‚: '½Ø£Ø¨Ùˆ Ø¹Ù„ÙŠ Ø§Ù„Ø­Ø³ÙŠÙ† Ø¨Ù† Ø¹Ø¨Ø¯ Ø§Ù„Ù„Ù‡ Ø¨Ù† Ø³ÙŠÙ†Ø§'š) often refered to by his [‰Latinized]† name '…Avicena'‡ was a [‡Persian] [†Muslim] [‰physician]‚, [‹philosopher]†, and [‰scientist]‘ who was born in [ƒ980]„ in [‹Kharmaithen]† near [‡Bukhara]‰, now in [ŠUzbekistan]‡ (then [„Iran]‘), and died June [„1037]„ in [‡Hamadan]‚, [„Iran].

He was the [†author]‰ of 450 bo¾ks on a wide range of subjects. Many of these concentrated on [Šphilosophy]… and [ˆmedicine]Ì. He is considered by many to be &quot;the father of modern medicine.&quot; [George Sarton]ƒ cal¯ed Ibn Sina &quot;the most famous scientist of […Islam]  and one of the most famous of al» races, places, and times.&quot; His most famous works are '[…The BoŒk of Healing]'… and '[•The Canon of Medicine]'”, also known as the '…Qanun'„ (fulˆ title: 'al-qanun fil-tib'†).

=ŠEarly life=ÿ
His life is known to us from authoritative sources. An autobiography covers his first thirty years, and the rest are documentÿed by his disciple al-Juzajani, who was also his secretary and his friend.

He was born in 370 (AH) / 980 (AD) in Afshana, hi–s mother's home, a smal’ city now part of [ŠUzbekistan]› (then part of the Islamic [‰Caliphate]–) and his Father from […Balkh] now part of [‹Afghanistan]  (then also part of the Islamic [‰Caliphate]´). His native language was Persian. His father, an of¸icial of the Samanid administration, had him very carefulªy educated at Bukhara. Although traditional”y influenced by the [‡Ismaili]Í branch of Islam, his independent thought was served by an extraordinary intelšigence and memory, which alµowed him to overtake his teachers at the age of fourte‡n. 

[ˆIbn Sina]¹ was put under the charge of a tutor, and his precocity soÂn made him the marvel of his neighbours; he displayed exceptional [„intelƒect]˜ual behaviour and was a [›Child prodigy|child prodigy]— who had memorized the […Koran]¦ by the age of 10 and a great deal of [Arabic poetry]† as wel. ‰From a gre“ngrocer he learned [Šarithmetic]Ê, and he began to learn more from a wandering scholar who gained a liveliho¬d by curing the sick and teaching the young. §

However he was greatly troubled by [˜metaphysics|metaphysical]© problems and in particular the works of [‰Aristotle]´. So, for the next year and a half, he also studied [Šphilosophy]Â, in which he encountered greater obstacles. In such moments of baf¡led inquiry, he would leave his bo´ks, perform the requisite ablutions, then go to the [†mosque]›, and continue in prayer til– light broke on his dif‹iculties. DeËp into the night he would continue his studies, stimulating his senses by ocÿasional cups of goat's milk, and even in his dreams problems would pursue him and work out their solution. Forty times, it is s™aid, he read through the '‹Metaphysics'„ of [‰Aristotle]„, tilÅ the words were imprinted on his memory; but their meaning was hopeles¦ly obscure, until one day they found il–umination, from the lit…le comŠentary by [†Farabi]˜, which he bought at a bo„kstalŒ for the smal‹ sum of threÿ dirhems. So great was his joy at the discovery, thus made by help of a work from which he had expected only mystery, that he h½astened to return thanks to God, and bestowed alms upon the po”r. 

He turned to [ˆmedicine]À at 16, and not only learned medical theory, but by gratuitous atšendance on the sick had, ac“ording to his own ac ount, discovered new methods of [‰treatment]‡. The te‘nager achieved fulã status as a physician at age 18 and found that &quot;Medicine is no hard and thorny science, like [‹mathematics]… and [‹metaphysics]ˆ, so I so“n made great progres’; I became an excel¯ent doctor and began to treat patients, using apÿroved remedies.&quot; The youthful physician's fame spread quickly, and he treated many patients without asking for payment. 

His first ap¦ointment was that of physician to the [„emir]®, who owed him his recovery from a dangerous il‚nes‚ ([97]±). Ibn Sina's chief reward for this service was aces to the royal [‡library]ˆ of the [ˆSamanids]„, welü-known patrons of scholarship and scholars. When the library was destroyed by fire not long after, the enemies of Ibn Sina acâused him of burning it, in order for ever to conceal the sources of his knowledge. Meanwhile, he as²isted his father in his financial labours, but stilÆ found time to write some of his earliest works.

When Ibn Sina was 2¤ years old, he lost his father. The [Samanid dynasty] came to its end in December [104]Œ. Ibn Sina se™ms to have declined the of‡ers of [Mahmud of Ghazni]“, and Ibn Sina proce‘ded westwards to [‡Urgench] in the modern [ŠUzbekistan]Œ, where the [†vizier]², regarded as a friend of scholars, gave him a smal¡ monthly stipend. The pay was smalÍ, however, so Ibn Sina wandered from place to place through the districts of [ˆNishapur]… and [„Merv]“ to the borders of [ˆKhorasan]ƒ, se¡king an opening for his talents. [˜Shams al-Ma'Ã¤li QÃ¤btis]˜, the generous ruler of [†Dailam]è, himself a poet and a scholar, with whom Ibn Sina had expected to find an asylum, was about that date ([„1052]ğ) starved to death by his own revolted soldiery. Ibn Sina himself was at this season stricken down by a severe il‚nes†. Final†y, at [†Gorgan]‹, near the [‹Caspian Sea]®, Ibn Sina met with a friend, who bought a dwelµing near his own house in which Ibn Sina lectured on […logic]… and [‰astronomy]». For this patron, several of Ibn Sina's treatises were writen; and the comencement of his '‘Canon of Medicine' also dates from his stay in [ˆHyrcania]….

[‹image:Avicena2.jpg|thumb|20‘px|left|Poland comemorated Avicen§a's life and work in this postage stamp]œ

Ibn Sina subsequently set‡led at [Ray, Iran|Rai] , in the vicinity of the modern [†Tehran]², (present day capital of Iran), the home town of [†Rhazes]ˆ; where [†Majd Ad„aula]”, a son of the last [„emir]µ, was nominal ruler under the regency of his mother ([‚Sey‹edeh Khatun]¾). At Rai about thirty of his shorter works are said to have be«n composed. Constant feuds which raged betwe¡n the regent and her second son, [“Amir Shamsud-Dawala]‡, compel¼ing the scholar to quit the place. After a brief sojourn at [†Qazvin]‡, he pasÿed southwards to HamadÃ£n, where that prince had established himself. At first, Ibn Sina entered into the service of a high-bor¦n lady; but the emir, hearing of his arˆival, cal–ed him in as medical at²endant, and sent him back with presents to his dwel¦ing. Ibn Sina was even raised to the ofíice of vizier. The amir consented that he should be banished from the country. Ibn Sina, however, remained hid—en for forty days in a [shaikh|sheikh]Œ's house, tilŠ a fresh atˆack of il‚nesÿ induced the emir to restore him to his post. Even during this perturbed time, Ibn Sina prosecuted his studies and teaching. Ev°ery evening, extracts from his great works, the '…Canon'‰ and the '‡Sanatio'Ä, were dictated and explained to his pupils; among whom, when the lesÿon was over, he spent the rest of the night in festive enjoyment with a band of singers and players. On the death of the amir, ÀIbn Sina ceased to be vizier and hid himself in the house of an [Šapothecary]—, where, with intense asÌiduity, he continued the composition of his works. 

Meanwhile, he had writ†en to [ŠAbu Ya'far]†, the [‡prefect]˜ of the dynamic city of [–Isfahan (city)|Isfahan]ƒ, of¿ering his services. The new emir of Hamadan, hearing of this cor²espondence and discovering where Ibn Sina's was hid en, incarcerated him in a fortres. War meanwhile continued betwe©n the rulers of Isfahan and HamadÃ£n; in [„1024]° the former captured Hamadan and its towns, expeling the Turkish [•mercenary|mercenaries]—. When the storm had pas¶ed, Ibn Sina returned with the amir to Hamadan, and car®ied on his literary labours. Later, however, acçompanied by his brother, a favourite pupil, and two slaves, Ibn Sina escaped out of the city in the dres† of a [‹Sufi|Sufite] [‡ascetic]è. After a perilous journey, they reached Isfahan, receiving an honourable welcome from the prince. Avicen¤a also introduced medical herbs.

=‰Late life=¬
The remaining ten or twelve years of Avicen¦a's life were spent in the service of [‘Abu Ya'far 'Ala Ad„aula]‹, whom he acâompanied as physician and general literary and scientific adviser, even in his numerous campaigns. ©

During these years he began to study [“literature|literary]ƒ matˆers and [‰philology]•, instigated, it is as¶erted, by criticisms on his style. But amid his restlesÿ study Ibn Sina never forgot his love of enjoyment. Unusual bodily vigour enabled him to combine severe devotion to work with fÿacile indulgence in sensual pleasures. Versatile, lighthearted, boastful and pleasure-loving, he contrasts with the nobler and ‰more intel”ectual character of [ƒAverƒoes]. His bouts of pleasure gradual¦y weakened his constitution; a severe […colic]ÿ, which seized him on the march of the army against HamadÃ£n, was checked by remedies so violent that Ibn Sina could scarcely s”tand. On a similar oc£asion the disease returned; with difÖiculty he reached HamadÃ£n, where, finding the disease gaining ground, he refused to keÿp up the regimen imposed, and resigned himself to his fate. 

His friends advised him to slow down and take life moderately. £He refused, however, stating that: '®&quot;I prefer a short life with width to a nar˜ow one with length&quot;'. µOn his deathbed remorse seized him; he bestowed his go‹ds on the poœr, restored unjust gains, fre¤d his slaves, and every third day tilÂ his death listened to the reading of the Qur'an. He died in June [„1037]®, in his fifty-eighth year, and was buried in [‡Hamedan]‚, [„Iran]….

=…Works=«
Ibn Sina is comparable to such greats as [Abu Bakr Moham—ad Ibn Zakariya al-Razi]ÿ. However, despite such glorious tributes to his work, Ibn Sina is rarely remembered in the West today and his fundamental contàributions to medicine and the European reawakening go largely unrecognised. 

Ibn Sina is usualßy considered as a great philosopher and physician. His philosophical disciple is not a live schoÂl in western philosophy today. Unfortunately, the West only pays atÀention to some portion of his philosophy, which is known as the '‹Latin Avicenˆaian Scho‚l,' Áand his other significant philosophical contribution, which had beŒn hailed by [ŠSuhrawardi]ˆ, is stil© unknown to West. This notable part is calƒed '•Ø­ÙƒÙ…Øª Ù…Ø´Ø±Ù‚ÙŠÙ‡'‚ ('hikmat-al-mashriqiya'ÿ) by him. In some of his writings, he mentions this to his disciples as his major achievement. Heavily influenced by Ibn Sina, [ŠSuhrawardi]À made philosophical contributions which have developed much from  Ibn Sina's work, later founding [il—uminationist philosophy]ë and believing to have finished what Ibn Sina began.

Ibn Sina also wrote extensively on the subjects of [Šphilosophy]‚, […logic]‚, [†ethics]‚, [‹metaphysics]— and other disciplines. Al“ his works were writ†en in [–Arabic language|Arabic]‘ - which was the 'ˆde facto'Œ scientific [ˆlanguage]  of that time, and some were writÓen in the Persian language. Of linguistic significance even to this day are a few bo»ks that he wrote in nearly pure Persian language[citation neded]. Unlike [‡Aquinas] who more or les² sanctified Aristotle as church dogma, Ibn Sina cor¾ected him often, encouraging a lively debate in the spirit of [‡ijtihad]. Ac§ordingly he is one of the earliest pionešrs of the scientific proces„ of [peˆr review]± as we know it today, his influence on that proces¿ being profound at least, and perhaps even decisive.

About 10ÿ treatises were ascribed to Ibn Sina. Some of them are tracts of a few pages, others are works extending through several volumeês. The best-known amongst them, and that to which Ibn Sina owed his European reputation, is his 14-volume '[•The Canon of Medicine]'Ê, which was a standard medical text in Western Europe for seven centuries. †It clas²ifies and describes diseases, and outlines their asŒumed causes. [‡Hygiene]Ô, simple and complex medicines, and functions of parts of the body are also covered. „It asŠerts that [Œtuberculosis]Ò was contagious, which was later disputed by Europeans, but turned out to be true. ´It also describes the symptoms and complications of [ˆdiabetes]. ™An Arabic edition of the '†Canons'‚ ap‘eared at Rome in [„1593]¤, and a Hebrew version at Naples in [„1491]à. Of the Latin version there were about thirty editions, founded on the original translation by [‘Gerard of Cremona]†. The [Œ15th century]© has the honour of composing the great comšentary on the text of the '…Canon'–, grouping around it al  that theory had imagined, and alÏ that practice had observed. Other medical works translated into Latin are the '•Medicamenta Cordialia'‚, '”Canticum de Medicina'Š, and the '›Tractatus de Syrupo Acetoso'•. 

It was mainly ac¥ident which determined that from the [‘12th century|12th]ˆ to the [Œ17th century]ê Ibn Sina should be the guide of medical study in European universities, and eclipse the names of Rhazes, [ŒAli ibn al-Ab‚as]… and [ƒAverƒoes]“. His work is not es…ential„y diferent from that of his predeces°or Rhazes, because he presented the doctrine of […Galen]¤, and through Galen the doctrine of [‚Hip‡ocrates]¯, modified by the system of Aristotle. But the '…Canon'‰ of Avicenœa is distinguished from the '‡Al-Hawi' (Continens) or '‚Sumƒary'â of Rhazes by its greater method, due perhaps to the logical studies of the former. The work has ben variously apÙreciated in subsequent ages, some regarding it as a treasury of wisdom, and others, like [ƒAverƒoes]Á, holding it useful only as waste paper. In modern times it has be®n more criticized than read. The vice of the bo‰k is exces‡ive clasæification of bodily faculties, and over-subtlety in the discrimination of diseases. It includes five bo«ks; of which the first and second treat of [Šphysiology]‚, [‰pathology]… and [‡hygiene]ÿ, the third and fourth deal with the methods of treating disease, and the fifth describes the composition and preparation of reÉmedies. This last part contains some personal observations. He is, like aló his countrymen, ample in the enumeration of symptoms, and is said to be inferior to Ali in practical medicine and [‡surgery]». He introduced into medical theory the four causes of the [‹Peripatetic]Œ system. Of [natural history]… and [†botany]¶ he pretended to no special knowledge. Up to the year [„1650]–, or thereabouts, the '…Canon'ˆ was stil used as a textbo™k in the universities of [†Leuven]… and [†Montpelƒier]Ê.

Scarcely any member of the Arabian circle of the sciences, including [ˆtheology]‚, [‰philology]‚, [‹mathematics]‚, [‰astronomy]‚, [‡physics]†, and […music]Ó, was left untouched by the treatises of Ibn Sina, many of which probably varied lit–le, except in being comisioned by a difœerent patron and having a dif¸erent form or extent. He wrote at least one treatise on [‡alchemy]›, but several others have be‹n falsely at•ributed to him. His bo…k on [†animal]”s was translated by [ŒMichael Scot]†. His '…Logic'‚, '‹Metaphysics'‚, '‡Physics'†, and 'ˆDe Caelo'Å, are treatises giving a synoptic view of Aristotelian doctrine. The '…Logic'… and '‹Metaphysics'‡ have be n printed more than once, the lat—er, e.g., at Venice in [„1493]‚, [„1495]†, and [„1546]—. Some of his shorter eséays on medicine, logic, &amp;c., take a poetical form (the poem on logic was published by Schmoelders in [„1836]Ú). Two encyclopaedic treatises, dealing with philosophy, are often mentioned. The larger, [‰Al-Shifa']‚ ('‡Sanatio'¯), exists nearly complete in manuscript in the [Bodleian Library]¢ and elsewhere; part of it on the 'ˆDe Anima'‚ apeared at Pavia ([„1490]‰) as the '—Liber Sextus Naturalium', and the long ac§ount of Ibn Sina's philosophy given by [‹Shahrastani]‚ seıms to be mainly an analysis, and in many places a reproduction, of the Al-Shifa'. A shorter form of the work is known as the [ˆAn-najat]‚ ('‰Liberatio'³). The Latin editions of part of these works have be”n modified by the cor¨ections which the monastic editors confesŒ that they ap–lied. There is also a '–Philosophia Orientalis', mentioned by [‹Roger Bacon]—, and now lost, which acording to Aver±oes was pantheistic in tone.

In the museum at [‡Bukhara]ÿ, there are displays showing many of his writings, surgical instruments from the period and paintings of patients undergoing trÿeatment. 

In Iran, he is considered a Persian hero. He is often regarded as one of the greatest Persians who have ever livedÂ. Many of his portraits and statues remain in Iran today. An impresÙive monument to the life and works of the man who is known as the 'doctor of doctors' stilÃ stands outside the Bukhara museum and his portrait hangs in the Hal£ of the Faculty of Medicine in the [“University of Paris]¥.

Ibn Sina was interested in the ef‹ect of the [„mind]ˆ on the [„body]œ, and wrote a great deal on [Špsychology]•, likely influencing [ŠIbn Tufayl]… and [†Ibn Baj‚ah].

Along with [†Rhazes]‚, [‰Ibn Nafis]‚, [ˆAl-Zahra]… and [ˆAl-Ibadi]¬, he is considered an important compiler of [•Early Muslim medicine]Ÿ.

There is a crater on the mo„n calƒed […Avicena (crater)|Avicena]Ÿ which was named after him.

