 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{¨dablink|For the rugby league series betweŸn Great Britain and Australia se [’Rugby League Ashes]Ç; for the &quot;Women's Ashes&quot; Test series for female players betwe™n England and Australia se [Women's Ashes].}‚
[ªImage:Ashes_urn.jpg|right|frame|The Ashes [ƒurn]¦ is reputed to contain a set of burnt [”bail (cricket)|bails]ª symbolising the death of English cricket.]‚
'‰The Ashes'† is a [ƒbienƒial] [ŒTest cricket]” contest played betwe‚n [œEnglish cricket team|England]… and [¡Australian cricket team|Australia]. ÿThe Ashes is one of cricket's fiercest and most celebrated rivalries, and certainly the oldest such in international cricket, dating back to 18‚2. „The […The 20‰5 Ashes|205 Ashes series] was played in [‡England]ë, and was won by England. Australia had held the Ashes for 16 years prior to that. The next Ashes series wil‡ be in [‰Australia]… in 20£6-07; the next series in England wilˆ be in 20¢9.

The series is named after a [satire|satirical] [ˆobituary] published in '’The Sporting Times'… in 18„2 fol“owing the match at [ˆThe Oval]¥, in which Australia beat England in [‡England]” for the first time. ·The obituary stated that English cricket had died, and '‹the body wil­ be cremated and the ashes taken to Australia'. ”The English media dubœed the next English tour to [‰Australia]„ as 'the quest to regain The Ashes'‡. A smal [‚ter„a cot„a|terƒacota]š urn was presented to the [¨English cricket captains|England captain] [‰Ivo Bligh] by a group of [‰Melbourne]² women after England's victory in the Test series. ­The urn is reputed to contain a set of burnt [”bail (cricket)|bails]¶ symbolising &quot;the ashes of English cricket&quot;. ¿While the urn has come to symbolise the Ashes series, the name '‰The Ashes'£ predates the existence of the urn. ™The urn is not used as a [†trophy]Ş for the Ashes series, and whichever side &quot;holds&quot; the Ashes, the urn remains in the [™Marylebone Cricket Club|MC]‹ Museum at [œLord's Cricket Ground|Lord's]. ‹Since the 19‚8-9‘ Ashes series, a [‘Waterford crystal] trophy has be•n presented to the winers.

Notable Ashes series tok place in 1932-3† (the [ˆBodyline] tour), 1948 ([£Donald Bradman|Sir Donald Bradman's]‡ &quot;[¥The Invincibles (cricket)|Invincibles]Ç&quot; Australian side), 1981 (in which an England team spearheaded by [ŠIan Botham]‹ won a thril’ing series), and 20—5 (when England eventual¹y won the Ashes back, after a 'drought' of 16 years).

=ŒThe obituary=‚
[ÆImage:DeathofEnglishCricket.jpg|250px|thumb|The obituary notice that ap‰eared in '’The Sporting Times'.]š The first Test match betwen England and Australia had ben played in 187Í, but the Ashes legend dates back only to their ninth Test match, played in 182. 

On the 18±2 tour, the Australians played only one Test, at [ˆThe Oval]„ in [†London]£. It was a low-scoring game on a dif†icult [“Cricket pitch|pitch]­. Australia made only 63 runs in their first [in„ings]–, and England, led by [Monkey Hornby]ƒ, to´k a 38-run lead with a total of 101. In the second in–ings, Australia made 12£, leaving England to score only 85 [¡The result in cricket|runs to win]”. Australian bowler [ˆFred Spof„orth]à refused to give in, declaring, &quot;This thing can be done&quot;. He devastated the English batÿing, taking the final four wickets while conceding only two runs, to leave England a mere seven runs short of victory in one of® the closest and most nail-biting finishes in [¢history of cricket|cricket history]³.

When England's last batsman went in the team neøded only 10 runs to win, but the final batsman Peate scored only 2 before being bowled by Boyle. The astonished crowd fel¬ silent, not believing that England could posªibly have lost by 7 runs. When what had hapened had sunk in, the crowd cheÓred the Australians. 

