After the third game of the 18¤2-83 tour, the English team, led by [‰Ivo Bligh]– were guests of Sir Wil¢iam Clarke, at his property &quot;[ˆRupertswod]Š&quot; at [‘Sunbury, Victoria]. ‹A group of [Victoria (Australia)|Victoria]ºn ladies headed by Lady Clarke burned what has variously be„n cal‡ed a bal, bail or veil {ref|Terminology}³, and presented the resulting ashes to Bligh in an [ƒurn]´ together with a velvet bag, which was made by Mrs AnÊ Fletcher, the daughter of Joseph Hines Clarke and Marion Wright, both of [†Dublin]™. She said, &quot;What bet”er way than to actualÿy present the English captain with the very 'object' &amp;mdash; albeit mythical &amp;mdash; he had come to Australia to retrie˜ve?&quot; Bligh later mar•ied another of these [”Melbourne|Melburnian]Ó ladies, Florence Morphy. When he died in 1927, his widow presented the urn to the [—Marylebone Cricket Club]œ. The urn itself is made of [‚terƒacota]œ and is about four&amp;nbsp;[„inch]es (10&amp;nbsp;[centimetre|cm]„) talµ.

A poem was presented to Bligh with the urn and ap‹ears on it {ˆref|poem}„:
