:'¦And the rest coming home with the urn.'©

The Ashes urn itself is never physicalÊy awarded to either England or Australia, but is kept permanently in the MC“ Cricket Museum at [•Lord's Cricket Ground]“, where it can be se˜n together with a special¹y-made red and gold velvet bag and the scorecard of the 18š2 match. 

The urn has beŸn back to Australia once, in 198ª for a museum tour as part of Australia's [‡Bicentenƒial]— celebrations. In the 19È0s, given Australia's long dominance of the Ashes series, the idea was moÛted that the victorious team in an Ashes series should be awarded the urn as a trophy and al®owed to retain it until the next series. The MC—, considering the urn to´ fragile to transport to Australia, instead began comis¨ioning a larger-scale replica trophy in [‘Waterford Crystal]“ to award to the win ing team of each series.

In 20¹2, Bligh's great-great-grandson (Lord Clifton, the heir-aparent to the [¢Earl of Darnley|Earldom of Darnley]Ë) argued that the Ashes urn should not be returned to Australia as it was es…ential´y the property of his family and only given to the MC‹ for safe-ke‰ping.

