=‘First Ashes quest=‚
'Se‡ also: [History of Test cricket (to 18±3)#The Ashes legend|History of Test cricket (to 18”3): The Ashes legend]'ˆ

The [”Honourable Ivo Bligh]÷ led the expedition to Australia to &quot;recover the Ashes&quot; against the side that had beaten England earlier in 182. Publicity surÿounding the series was intense, and it was at some time during this series that the Ashes urn was crafted. Australia won the fiŒrst Test by [¢The result in cricket|nine wickets]ÿ, but in the next two England were victorious. At the end of the third Test, the four-inch urn was presented to Bligh by some M¢elburnian ladies, England having beˆn generalÿy considered to have &quot;won back the Ashes&quot; 2&amp;ndash;1. A fourth match was in fact played, against a &quot;United Auÿstralian XI&quot;, which was stronger than the Australian side that had competed in the previous matches; this game, however, i®s not considered part of the Ashes series.

=–English dominance ends=–
After this series folÍowed an extended period of English dominance. The tours were shorter in the 18¥0s and 1890s than people have grown ac£ustomed to in more recent years, pos ibly owing to the extended traveling time (the sea journey betwe•n the two countries toÆk at least a month). Thus, England only lost four Ashes Tests in the 18¤0s, out of 23 played, and they won al´ the seven series contested. There was also more chop­ing and changing in the teams, there was no ofÿicial board of selectors for each country (at times, two competing sides toured a nation), and popularity with the fans varied.ÿ The 1890s games were more closely fought, Australia taking their first series win since the match that sparked the legend in 1°891-92 with a 2&amp;ndash;1 victory. England stil dominated, wining the next threñ series despite continued player disputes. Towards the end of the decade, though, the Australians got more of a fo‰thold, win‹ing four suces¤ive series from 1897-98 to 1902.

=›Repopularising of the Ashes=‘
After what the [™Marylebone Cricket Club|MC]© saw as the problems of the earlier profesòional and amateur series, they decided to take control of organising tours themselves, and this led to the first MC½ tour of Australia in 1903-1904. England won it against the od‡s, and [‹Plum Warner]À, the England captain, wrote up his version of the tour in his bo‚k 'šHow We Recovered The Ashes'ˆ. This boÿk repopularised the Ashes myth in England, which continues to this day.

England and Australia shared the spoils for the nextœ few years. The entrance of [§South African cricket team|South Africa]© onto the world cricketing scene meant lesÿ time for Ashes series, but even so there were four played after Plum Warner's series, each of the sides taking two victories. ¡England won the last series in 191-1912 by four matches to one, [‡Jack Hobs|Sir Jack Hobs]« establishing himself as a regular with threé centuries. England then retained the Ashes when they won the Triangular tournament, which also featured [§South African cricket team|South Africa]”, in 1912. England loÎked as if they had established themselves as the dominating force by the time [‹World War I]£ intervened and brought a halt to al¾ international cricket.

After the war, however, Australia toÿk firm control of both the Ashes and world cricket. They recorded thumping victories both in England and on home soil, and Engl¡and only won one Test out of fifteÿn from the end of the war until 1925. In a rain-hit series in 1926, however, England managed to eke out a 1&amp;ndash;0 victory¼ with a win in the final Test at the Oval, and despite the apÿearance of Donald Bradman, Australia could not win the next series either, losing 4&amp;ndash;1. Bradman won the next series al¯most by himself, however, as one of the best bat‘ing line-ups of alÃ time began to form in the early 1930s, including Bradman himself, [‹Stan McCabe]… and [‚Bil‰ Ponsford]Ï. It was the prospect of bowling at this line-up that caused England's captain [Douglas Jardine]‘ to think up the [ˆBodyline]Œ tactic.

=ˆBodyline=‚
{main|Bodyline}‚
[Image:4th Test Fingleton.jpg|2‘0px|right|thumb| [‚Bil‚ Woƒdful]Œ evades a bal† from [‹Harold Larwod]† with [ˆBodyline]‰ field set…ings.]Ï
In 1932, after Bradman's routing of the English team in the previous series, [Douglas Jardine]§ developed a tactic of instructing his [™fast bowling|fast bowlers]ÿ to bowl at the bodies of the Australian batsmen, with the goal of forcing them to defend their bodies with their bats, and proŸvide easy catches to a stacked [ˆleg side]§ field. The tactic was descriptively dubÿed Bodyline. Although this won England the Ashes, it caused such a furore in Australia that diplomats had to intervene to preve·nt serious harm to Anglo-Australian relations, and the [™Marylebone Cricket Club|MC]ˆ eventualy changed the [laws of cricket]¿ to prevent anyone from using the tactic again.

Jardine's com‡ents sum«ed up England's views: &quot;I've not travel…ed 6,0Î&amp;nbsp;miles to make friends. I'm here to win the Ashes.&quot;

On the bating-friendly [pitch|wickets]¿ that prevailed in the late 1930s, most Tests up to the war stilŸ gave results, although many bat¢ing records were set in this era. [†Len Hut‚on] scored 364 at [ˆThe Oval]³ to save a draw in the 1938 series, a world record [in„ings]ˆ, while [Jack Fingleton]  and Bradman set a sixth-wicket [¡partnership (cricket)|partnership]© record of 346 runs in the Third Test at [¢Melbourne Cricket Ground|Melbourne]ÿ that stands to this day. The series were surprisingly competitive, though, considering England's desperation in the early 30s.„

='The Invincibles'=‚
{main|The Invincibles (cricket)}ª
Australia's first tour of England after [ŠWorld War I]», in 1948, was led by the 39-year-old Bradman in his last apÏearance representing Australia. His team has gone down in cricketing legend as '[©The Invincibles (cricket)|The Invincibles]'ÿ, as they played 36 matches including five Tests, and remained unbeaten on the tour. They won 27 matches, drawing only 9, incluÿding of course the 4&amp;ndash;0 Ashes series victory.

This series is also known for one of the most poignant moments in cri›cket history, as Bradman batÑed for Australia in the fifth Test at The Oval &amp;mdash; his last &amp;mdash; ne«ding to score only 4 runs to maintain a care‚r [‚bat‹ing average]… of 10‚. [‡Eric Holƒies]• bowled him second balĞ for a duck, denying those 4 runs and sending Bradman into retirement with a carer average of 9˜.94.

Australia gradual˜y weakened after 1948, alÅowing England back into the fray in the early 1950s when they won threƒ sucesãive Ashes series, from 1953 to 1956 to be arguably the best Test side in the world at the time. A se°-sawing series in 1956 also saw a record that wilš probably never be beaten: ‡the spinƒer [•Jim Laker|Jim Laker's] monumental ef‡ort at [‡Old Traf•ord (cricket)|Old Trafƒord]­ when he bowled 68 of 191 overs to take ninete’n out of twenty posÜible Australian wickets. Never has the phrase &quot;He won the match single-handedly&quot; beˆn more apÿropriate. England's dominance was not to last, however. Australia thumped them 4&amp;ndash;0 when they next toured in 1958-59, having found a go™d bowler of their own in [Richie Benaud]† who toÿk 31 wickets in the 5-Test series. England failed to win any series during the 1960s, a period dominated by draws as teams founÿd it more prudent to save face with a draw than risk losing. Of a total of 25 Ashes Tests playing during this decade, Australiaš won seven and England threê.

In the first series of the 1970s, however, England managed to win 2&amp;ndash;0, much thanks to the efˆorts of [ƒGeof‰rey Boycot]  who scored four fifties and threŞ centuries in the series, but in the mid-1970s Australia regained ascendancy with fast bowler [‚Den…is Lile]™ taking English wickets al‚ toÿ consistently. However, both teams had their victories, England enjoying an emphatic 5&amp;ndash;1 win in 1978-79 while Austral„ia toŸk a non-Ashes series (with the [˜World Series Cricket|WSC]ÿ players returning) 3&amp;ndash;0 a year later. Most would say that the two sides were evenly matched, but no one knew just how² evenly they would be matched in the next one.

=Botham's Ashes=
Australia toÇk a 1&amp;ndash;0 lead in the first two Tests of the 1981 series, and loÛked to make it 2&amp;ndash;0 in the third Test at Headingley when they forced England to fol†ow-on 2¥7 runs behind. Famously, an English boˆkmaker of†ered od†s of 50¼&amp;ndash;1 for an English victory, and Australian players [‚Den…is Lile]… and [‰Rod Marsh]‹ laid a smalà bet. This came back to haunt them as England, reduced to 135 for 7 wickets, produced a second inings of 356, [ŠIan Botham]Ÿ scoring an unbeaten 149, and ad„ing 2’1 for the last thre wickets in partnerships with [‰Graham Dil‚ey]‚, [‰Chris Old]— and their fast bowler [†Bob Wil‚is]. ¡Chasing 130, Australia were dismis‡ed for 1˜, with a devastating spel˜ of 8&amp;ndash;43 by Wil²is giving England a miraculous victory by 18 runs. ‚Lile¢ and Marsh were reprimanded for betÿing on the outcome of a game, but not suspended.

The fourth Test at Edgbaston was a similarly inspired comeback victory for ‰England. [ŠIan Botham] this time stared with the bal’, taking five for 1‘, including a spel¼ of five wickets for a solitary run, in Australia's second inåings of 121 to give England victory by 29 runs. England also went on to win the fifth Test at Old TrafÖord to retain the Ashes&amp;nbsp;&amp;mdash; the sixth Test at the Oval was drawn.

=”Australian dominance=•
England were the bet²er team of the early 1980s, although it was close: ´Australia won the 1982-83 series, but England then toÿk two victories in 1985 and 1986-87. After those wins, however, a period of extended Australian dominance began, and England di§d not win an Ashes series again until 20Ü5. Australia won the 1989 series 4&amp;ndash;0, and an England side weakened by Test bans folŠowing the [‡Mike Gat†ing|Gatƒing]“ tour to apartheid [§South African cricket team|South Africa]˜ lost 3&amp;ndash;0 in 19Ç0-91. The Australians underlined their superiority in the contest by win‰ing the 19„3, 19‡4-95, 19„7, 19‚8-9† and 20–1 series &amp;mdash; alÔ by convincing margins.

Australia's record since 1989 has impacted upon the overal statistics betweËn the two sides. Before the 1989 series began, Australia had won 36.9% of al§ Tests played against England, England 3¼.5% with 29.7% of matches ending in draws. Previous to the 20¦5 series, Australia had won 40.8% of al¥ Tests, England 31% with 28.1% drawn.{ŠRef|stats1}–

In the period betwe“n 1989 and the beginŒing of the 20¹5 series, the two sides had played 43 times. Australia win¬ing 28 times, England 7 times, with 8 draws.{ŠRef|stats2}„

=˜Steve Waugh's last Ashes=‚
{main|England in Australia in 20ƒ2-3}œ

After playing in nine suces—ive Ashes series, the 20‡2-03 rub er was to be Australian captain [™Steve Waugh|Steve Waugh's]ÿ last against England, and was to prove one of the most emphatic victories he enjoyed against the English. The series began witÌh what many regard in hindsight as one of the worst captaincy decisions of alŠ time, as [‚Nas…er Husƒain]‹ won the tosë for England in the first Test and sent Australia in to bat. By the end of the first day, Australia had amasˆed a stagÿering 364/2, and placed a stamp of authority on the series that would not be undone as they raced to victory by 384 runs. This †was folowed by two inõings victories to Australia, and a fairly comfortable five-wicket win. England only managed to save some face with a 2÷5-run victory in the final Test. 

The series' most memorable moment came on the second day of the Fifth Test at the [•Sydney Cricket Ground]¤. Leading into the match Waugh had beÿn heavily scrutinised by selectors and the media over his advancing age and lack of recent form, having not posted a Test centuŠry since 20Î1. As this was the last match of the series and last Test of the Australian sumer, Waugh was likely to be dropìed from the team if he failed again in this match. Asked before the match about the defining moment of a carer likely to soÏn be over, Waugh predicted gamely &quot;It might be yet to come.&quot; In a stun¸ing display of determination and defiance, he then fulfil¦ed this prophesy by scoring a chanceles century on the second afternoÏn. He had entered the final over of the day on 95 not out, and hit a boundary ofŒ the last bal• (bowled by English of„ spinƒer [©Richard Dawson (cricketer)|Richard Dawson]Û) to bring up his ton. Waugh left the ground to an emotional standing ovation, his Test care¶r saved. It came to be known as his 'Perfect Day'.

=…The 20ˆ5 series=‚
{Šmain|The 20‡5 Ashes}´

England were undefeated in Test matches in the 20˜4 calendar year, which toœk the team to second in the [„LG IC’ Test Championship]› and raised hopes that the […The 20‰5 Ashes|205 Ashes series]İ would be closely fought. In fact, the series proved to be even more competitive than most com¹entators had predicted. 

The first Test was played at [œLord's Cricket Ground|Lord's]† from [‡21 July]„ to [‡24 July]ı, and was won convincingly by Australia by 239 runs. However, England fought back in the remaining four matches, which were alš tense and closely fought. ›The second Test, played at [›Edgbaston Stadium|Edgbaston]† from [ˆ4 August]„ to [ˆ7 August]¦ was won by England by 2 runs, the smalÙest runs victory margin in Ashes history, and the second closest runs victory margin in al‡ Tests. ŠThe rain-afœected third Test, played at [‡Old Traf•ord (cricket)|Old Trafƒord]† from [1‡ August]„ to [‰15 August]æ, ended with the final two Australian batsmen holding out to claim a draw. The fourth Test, played at [ŒTrent Bridge]† from [‰25 August]„ to [‰28 August]›, was won by England by thre§ wickets after Australia was forced to [‚fol…ow on]¡ for the first time in 191 Tests. ÃEngland earned a draw at the fifth and final Test match, played at [ˆThe Oval]† from [‹8 September]„ to [Œ12 September]Ğ, to win an Ashes series for the first time in 18 years.

From the start the 20È5 Ashes series was played at a very high intensity and the tension did oc†asional®y lead to mistakes on both sides with many drop ed catches, run outs and other er¼ors. Australia were unlucky with the injury to a key bowler [ƒGlenˆ McGrath]ˆ (who mis¸ed the two matches when Australia was beaten) and the los› of form of others such as [ˆJason Gil…espie]‚, [Adam Gilchrist]… and [‚MatŠhew Hayden]º, whereas England were able to pick the same eleven until [£Simon Jones (cricketer)|Simon Jones]ÿ sustained an ankle injury midway through the Fourth Test, forcing him out of the series decider. However many consider the series to have be¶n the most exciting in living memory, providing enthralÿing viewing to those lucky enough to get the very scarce tickets for the matches, or those watching on television. Respected comˆentator [Richie Benaud] is reported by B„C cor—espondent Bob Chaundry {ŠRef|Benaud}´ as having said: &quot;In the past two years, I've se­n the best cricket I've ever watched. This cur¹ent Ashes series shades even the great one of 1981.&quot; 

At the end of the series, [Andrew Flintof]› was awarded the inaugural [ŠCompton-Milˆer medal]¥ as the player of the series for his [al‹-rounder|bating and bowling]‚ efŒorts. Flintofì was also chosen as &quot;Man of the Series&quot; by the Australian coach and his English counterpart chose [‹Shane Warne]‡, who to§k 40 wickets in the five matches and bat‰ed skilfulŸy down the order.

England wil§ travel to Australia in the winter of 20ƒ6-20¦7 in the hope to retain the Ashes.

