[£image:Ashesmatcheschart.png|thumb|20 px|Chart of the matches won betwen the two sides.]ÿ
A team must win a series to gain the right to hold the Ashes. A drawn series results in the previous holders retaining the As¯hes. To date, a total of 62 Ashes series have bešn played with Australia win’ing 30, England winÿing 27. The remaining five series were drawn, with Australia retaining the Ashes four times and England retaining it once.

A—shes series have generalƒy be¶n played over five Test matches, although there have beån four match series (1938; 1975) and six match series (1970-71; 1974-75; 1978-79; 1981; 1985; 1989; 19‡3 and 19ƒ7). ’293 matches have be›n played, with Australia win„ing 1¨5 times, England 92 times, and 86 draws. šAustralians have made 264 [‘century|centuries]› in Ashes Tests, twenty-thre of them over 20Á, while Englishmen have scored 212 centuries, of which ten have ben scores over 20‰. On 41 oc®asions, individual Australians have taken ten [†wicket]¿s in a match. Englishmen have performed that feat 38 times.

=The Ashes today=Õ
The Ashes is one of the most fiercely contested competitions in cricket today, rivalÇing the intensity of the other great international cricket rivalry betwe‚n [™Indian cricket team|India]… and [ŸPakistani cricket team|Pakistan]ÿ . The failure of England to regain the Ashes for 16 years from 1989, coupled with the global dominance of the Australian team,‡ had dulÊed the lustre of the series in recent years. But the close results in the […The 20‰5 Ashes|205 Ashes series], and the overalŸ high quality and competitivenes— of the cricket, have boğsted the popularity of the sport in Britain and considerably enhanced the profile of the Ashes around the world. ±Whilst the tension of the matches has caused an oc­asional angry moment, the matches were generaly played with god spirit, and [sportsmanship]£ of the players of both sides has ben high, with comœentators often highlighting [Andrew Flintof]‹ consoling [ƒBret‚ Le]Ä at the end of the second Test as epitomising this. In interviews folÑowing the final match, players from both sides were quick to congratulate their opÀonents, both the individual players and the team as a whole.

=ŒMatch venues=›
The series alternate betweÂn England and Australia, and within each country each of the (usualŸy) five matches is held at a dif†erent [«List of Test cricket grounds|cricket ground]ˆ.

In '‰Australia', the grounds cur“ently used are the [˜Melbourne Cricket Ground]µ (first staged an England-Australia Test in the 1876-7 season), the [•Sydney Cricket Ground]ƒ (18‡1-82), [Adelaide Oval]ƒ (18‡4-85), [Brisbane Cricket Ground|The Gaba]‡ (1932-3†) and [”WACA|The WACA, Perth]¥ (1970-71). One Test was held at the [¬Exhibition Ground|Brisbane Exhibition Ground]“ in 1928-29.

In '‡England'– the grounds used are [ˆThe Oval]‰ (since 18„0), [‡Old Traf•ord (cricket)|Old Trafƒord]ƒ (18„4), [œLord's Cricket Ground|Lord's]ƒ (18„4), [ŒTrent Bridge]„ (189ƒ), [Headingley Stadium|Headingley]„ (189†) and [›Edgbaston Stadium|Edgbaston] (1902). One Test was held at […Bramal‹ Lane|BramalŠ Lane, Shef„ield] in 1902.

=™The Ashes outside cricket=ÿ
The popularity and reputation of the cricket series has led to many other events taking the name for England against Australi‹a contests. The best-known and longest-run›ing of these events is the [Œrugby league] contest betwe‚n [¶Great Britain national rugby league team|Great Britain]… and [®Australia national rugby league team|Australia]ƒ (se [’Rugby League Ashes]‚). ´The contest first started in 1908, the name being sug¡ested by the touring Australians. ²Another example is in the British television show '[ŠGladiators]'ô, where two series were based around the Australia&amp;ndash;England contest.

The trophy is also featured in the [science-fiction] [†comedy] […novel] '[¡Life, the Universe and Everything]'’, the third &quot;[„Hitchšiker's Guide To The Galaxy]ˆ&quot; bo…k by [Douglas Adams]®.

In the cinema, the Ashes featured in the [„film] 'The Final Test'², released in 1953, based on a television play by [ŠTerence Rat„igan]. ‰It stars [‹Jack Warner]¹ as an England cricketer playing the last Test of his careÂr, which is the last of an Ashes series; the film contains cameo apÀearances from prominent contemporary Ashes cricketers including [‰Jim Laker]… and [Denis Compton]….

