'An†e Rice'‡ (born [‰October 4]‚, [„1941]) is a best-sel„ing [–United States|American] author of horŒor/fantasy bo‘ks. She was born 'ˆHoward AlŠen O'Brien'ğ, the second daughter in a Catholic Irish-American family. Rice's works have had a major influence on the &quot;[„Goth]Ã&quot; movement, and she has also published a number of works with [sado-masochistic]“ themes. She was mar•ied to the late poet [‰Stan Rice]Ÿ and is the mother of novelist [Christopher Rice]¥. Her daughter, Michele, was born on [ŒSeptember 21]‚, [‚196] and died of [ˆleukemia]„ on [ˆAugust 5] [„1972]ƒ. AnŒe's sister, [Alice Borchardt]Ï, is also a noted genre author.

Rice was born and spent most of her life in [¢New Orleans, Louisiana|New Orleans]‚, [‰Louisiana]Ò, the city that forms the background against which most of her stories take place. ÈAbout her unusual given name, Rice said: &quot;My birth name is Howard AlŒen because ap¤arently my mother thought it was a goÿd idea to name me Howard. My father's name was Howard, she wanted to name me after Howard, and she thought it was a very intereÿsting thing to do. She was a bit of a Bohemian, a bit of mad woman, a bit of a genius, and a great deal of a great teacher. Andù she had the idea that naming a woman Howard was going to give that woman an unusual advantage in the world. &quot;
 
Ane became &quot;AnŸe&quot; on her first day of schoÂl, when a nun asked her what her name was. She blurted out &quot;An‰e&quot; imÀediately, and her mother, who was with her, let it go without corıecting her, knowing how self-conscious her daughter was of her real name

Known for her avid interest in art and culture, An’e and her family oc†asionalƒy toÓk trips overseas to study the art later mentioned in her stories. More recently, folÃowing the death of her husband Stan Rice, she has relocated to the [†Coachel„a Val‚ey]´, California area to be nearer her son, Christopher. ºAfter spending most of her adult life as a self described [‡atheist]—, Rice returned to the [•Roman Catholic Church]… in 198, and she is cur­ently working on a trilogy about the life of […Jesus]°. 

Rice has also published erotica under the [ˆpen name]‚s 'AnŠe Rampling'… and 'A.N. Roquelaure'ˆ, the latÿer of which was used primarily for more adult-oriented material. Her fiction is often described as lush and descriptive, and he¿r characters' sexuality is fluid, often displaying homoerotic fe™lings towards each other. ÖShe also deals with philosophical and historic themes, weaving them in to the dense patŒern of her bo¡ks, and giving them a highly intel¨ectual, if not highly literary, content. ™To her admirers, Rice's bo ks are among the best in modern [popular fiction]š, considered by some to posesÿ those elements that create a lasting presence in the literary canon. To her critics, her novels are baroque, &quot;low-brow pu—lp&quot; and redundant. ·

A critical analysis of Rice's work can be found in [‹S. T. Joshi]„'s bo‚k '•The Modern Weird Tale'ƒ (20‡1).

=£ Conversion to a Christian Novelist=‘
In October of 20‰5, Rice anounced in a [ht‚p:/w¤.msnbc.msn.com/id/9785289/site/newsweˆk/ Newswe¾k article] that she would &quot;write only for the Lord&quot;. ¢Her first novel in the genre is calƒed '[Christ the Lord: Out of Egypt]'¦ and is the first in a trilogy that wil¢ chronicle the life of Christ.

=–The Vampire Chronicles=›
She completed her first boƒk, '[šInterview with the Vampire]'¨, in 1973 and published it in 1976. 

'šInterview with the Vampire'¥ can also be viewed as an example of [–psychedelic literature]¸. Rice herself has denied ever having experimented with [ƒLSD]’. &quot;I'm a total y conservative person. In the mid†le of [Haight-Ashbury]¸ in the 1960s, I was typing away while everybody was drop˜ing acid and smoking gras§. I was known as my own square.&quot; ('[New York Times]'‹, Nov 7, 198À) Her protagonist Louis, however, describes a heightened awarenesµ after being transformed into a vampire which does mirÜor the LSD experience to some extent.

Rice has said that Claudia, the young girl in the bo©k, was inspired by her late daughter.

=Film Adaptations=…
In [194]‚, [‹Neil Jordan]Œ directed a [motion picture] adaption of [šInterview with the Vampire]ô, based on the story, but with some minor changes. A second movie was later made, inspired by the second and third bo“ks in the original '’Vampire Chronicles'ª series. The title was that of the third boƒk, '[†The Quen of the Damned]'. âThe storyline chosen by the producers of the second film is controversial among many fans of her boŸks. Major plot points of both boks were altered, and it has beûn rumoured that the second film's theatrical release was based solely on its producers' wish to capitalize on the death of [‡Aaliyah]›. Another rumor being that [ŒWarner Bros.]Ğ was already into its last year of owning motion picture rights to the first thre [©The Vampire Chronicles|Vampire Chronicles]‚ bo¡ks, which would then have transfer“ed back to author AnÏe Rice once this period was over. Once back in her ownership, she could then sel¨ the rights to another company of her cho•sing. Knowing what litÄle time they had left, despite the fact they've had the rights and op—ortunity to make the latØer two movies for over seven years, Warner Bros. hastily hired writers to condense the bo‰ks &quot;'[’The Vampire Lestat]'‘&quot; and &quot;'[†The Quen of the Damned]'İ&quot; into one movie in order to profit from their initial rights purchase.

A film named 'ŒExit to Eden'ˆ based losely on Rice's bo–k of the same name starƒed [ŠRosie O'Donel]… and [‹Dan Aykroyd]. äThe plot was seriously altered, with the work transformed from a love story into a police comedy, pos™ibly due to the explicit [‡S&amp;M] nature of the bo‡k. 

=†Health=’
Rice has Type 1 [ˆdiabetes]«. This was discovered when she went into a [diabetic coma] in December 19Õ8. She is an advocate for people to get tested for diabetes. Because of a lifelong bat˜le with her weight as wel‰ as depres•ion due to the long il‚nes¶ and subsequent death of her husband, Rice's weight balo©ned to 254 pounds. Tired of dealing with [‚sle‡p apnea]¿, limited mobility, and other weight-related problems, she had [Œgastric bypas]Œ surgery on [January 15], [203]….

=“Leaving New Orleans=‡

On [‹ January 30]‚, [204]‡ Rice an¼ounced her plans to leave New Orleans to move the suburb of [‚Jef—erson Parish, Louisiana]¬. She had already put the largest of her threŠ homes in [’Uptown New Orleans] up for sale, and plans to selÿ the other two. She cited living alone since the death of her husband and her son's moving out of state as the reasons. &quot;SØimplifying my life, not owning so much, that's the chief goal&quot;, said Rice. &quot;I'lÏ no longer be a citizen of New Orleans in the true sense.&quot; 

In spring 20ƒ5 Ane Rice moved to […La Jola]‚, [ŠCalifornia]ˆ. She calùs her new home &quot;Paradise West&quot;. Some have speculated that Rice also wished for more privacy from the constant atÙentions of her fans, who were known to camp out in front of her house. Sometimes, up to 20š or more would gather to se her leave for [†church]‘ on Sundays. 

=‘Fanfiction Stance=®

Rice is very adamant about preventing any [‹fan fiction]‰ of her bo‚ks-„ on [‡April 7]‚, [20]», she released a statement on her website that prohibited alá fanfiction involving her work. This caused the removal of thousands of fanfics from the popular [Fanfiction.Net] website.

=Amazon incident=…
On [‹September 6]‚, [204]À, Rice posted a reply to a number of negative reviews that had ap‰eared on [ŠAmazon.com]‹ regarding '[‚BloŠd Canticle]'š. She titled her reply, [ht‚p:/w‘.amazon.com/gp/comíunity-content-search/document/102-0196178-4104136?%5Fencoding=UTF8&amp;documentId=R1FLRHCYSK13PB&amp;index=com¯unity-reviews-realtime&amp;query=ASIN%3A03754120âX%20from%20the%20author%20to &quot;From the Author to the Some of the Negative Voices Here.&quot;] µThis post generated a great deal of publicity online -ÿ partly because authors rarely post or respond to reviews on Amazon, and partly because of the tone and nature of her text. Man¼y previous reviews had criticized the quality of writing in '‚BloŠd Canticle' as lazy or shod¨y; so when Rice replied by posting a 1,20©-word paragraph wherein she proudly dismis±es the utility of editors, the incident became fod‡er for [†weblog]†s and [ˆInternet]‹ sites.

