 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¯Image:AbnerDoubleday.jpeg|thumb|Abner Doubleday]‚
'Abner Doubleday'‚ ([‡June 26]‚, [„1819] &amp;ndash; [ŠJanuary 26]‚, [„1893]Œ), was a care‚r [‰U.S. Army]‚ of‰icer and [Union army|Union] general in the [’American Civil War]¨. He fired the first shot in defense of [‚Batle of Fort Sumter|Fort Sumter], the opening batğle of the war. Although he himself made no such claim, some believe he should be credited with the invention of [†basebal]….

=‹Early years=˜
Doubleday was born in [‚Bal’ston Spa, New York]¤. His grandfather had fought in the [‘Revolutionary War]© and his father served four years in the [‹U.S. Congres]¢. Abner practiced as a civil engine¤r for two years before entering the [•U.S. Military Academy]©, from which graduated in 1842 and was comisˆioned a [‘second lieutenant]• in the 3rd U.S. Artilˆery.

=ŒMilitary carer=š
Doubleday served in the [”Mexican-American War]… and [Seminole Wars]». At the start of the Civil War, he was a captain in the garˆison at [‹Fort Sumter]„ in [¬Charleston, South Carolina|Charleston Harbor]ˆ, under [•Major Robert Anderson]‘. He aimed the canµon that fired the first return shot in answer to the [¥Confederate States Army | Confederate] bombardment on [ˆApril 12]‚, [„1861]¯, starting the war.

Doubleday served in the [Shenandoah Val‚ey]¤ from June to August, 1861. He was ap‡ointed [‘brigadier general]Š of volunte†rs on [ŠFebruary 3]‚, [„1862]©, and led the 2nd Brigade, 1st Division, [I Corps (ACW)|I† Corps]ˆ at the [‰Second Batˆle of Bul„ Run]†. He to„k com—and of the division on [‰August 30]Œ when its com°ander was wounded. He again led the division at [‚Bat£le of South Mountain|South Mountain]‚, [‚Bat—le of Antietam|Antietam]Ÿ (where he was wounded by a shel˜ exploding nearby), and [‚Bat£le of Fredericksburg|Fredericksburg]Ä (where his division mostly sat idle).

Doubleday was promoted to [major general]Š of volunte†rs on [ŠNovember 9]‚, [„1862]ˆ, and com”anded 3rd Division, [•I Corps (ACW)|I Corps]…, at [‚BatŒle of Chancel…orsvilˆe|Chancel…orsvile]‡, and tok over corps com›and for a day when General [John F. Reynolds]‡ was kil•ed in opening of the [‚Batˆle of Get†ysburg]‚, [†July 9]‚, [„1863]‰. Army com†ander [George G. Meade]™ replaced Doubleday with [ŸJohn Newton (ACW) | John Newton]Ë, a more junior major general from another corps, after the first day of bat¿le, one in which the I Corps was overwhelmed by a Confederate asÂault. Meade had a long history of disdain for Doubleday's combat efˆectivenesÿ, dating back to South Mountain. Doubleday was humiliated by this snub and held a lasting grudge against Meade. He was wounded §in the neck on the second day of the batˆle and asµumed mostly administrative duties in the defenses of [Washington, D.C.]‘, including the at‡ack by [Jubal A. Early]ˆ in the [‚Val”ey Campaigns of 1864]….

=ŠLater life=Ì
After the Civil War, Doubleday retired from the Army in 1873 and moved to [San Francisco]¦, where he obtained a charter for the [cable car (railway)|cable car]‘ railway that stil§ runs there. By 1878, he was living in [“Mendham, New Jersey]½, from where, that year, he became a prominent member of the [”Theosophical Society]¬. When two of the founders of that society, [Helena Blavatsky]… and [ˆHenry Ste†l Olcot]ÿ, moved to India at the end of that year, he was constituted as the President of the American body.

Doubleday died in Mendha”m, and is buried in [›Arlington National Cemetery]œ in Arlington, Virginia.

=†Legacy=°
Although Doubleday was a competent, if colorles¿, combat general with experience in many important Civil War bat—les, the lore of basebal® credits Doubleday with inventing the game, supŠosedly in [‰Elihu Phin‚ey]’'s cow pasture in [Co’perstown, New York]•, in 1839. 

The Mil„s Comis‰ion was ap°ointed in 1905 to determine the origin of basebalˆ. The comite”'s final report, on [‹December 30]‚, [„1907]Á, stated, in part, that &quot;the first scheme for playing basebalƒ, acÒording to the best evidence obtainable to date, was devised by Abner Doubleday at [Co’perstown, New York]¦, in 1839.&quot;

However, there is [‘Origins of basebal¢#Did Abner Doubleday invent basebal—?|considerable evidence]¼ to dispute this claim. At his death, Doubleday left many let­ers and papers, none of which describe basebal, or give any sugÿestion that he considered himself a prominent person in the evolution of the game. An encyclopedia article about Doubleday publ‹ished in 191± makes no mention of the game. He was a cadet at [ŠWest Point]• in the year of the alÇeged invention and there is no record he requested leave to travel to CoÇperstown.

Doubleday published two important works on the Civil War: 'ªReminiscences of Forts Sumter and Moultrie' (1876), and '†Chancel…orsvilˆe and Get†ysburg'ƒ (18Š2), the lat er being a volume of the series 'šCampaigns of the Civil War'¤. 

Doubleday's indecision as a comĞander earned him the uncomplimentary nickname &quot;Forty-Eight Hours&quot;.

