 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'”Amplitude modulation'‚ ('‚AM') is a form of [Šmodulation] in which the [‰amplitude]† of a [‚carˆier wave]¿ is varied in direct proportion to that of a modulating signal. ”(Contrast this with [”frequency modulation], in which the [‰frequency]Š of the car“ier is varied; and [phase modulation], in which the [“Phase (waves)|phase]˜ is varied.)

AM is comonly used at [¡Radio frequency|radio frequencies]¢ and was the first method used to [–Broadcasting|broadcast]ƒ com‡ercial […radio]. ²The term &quot;AM&quot; is sometimes used generical y to refer to the AM broadcast ([Šmediumwave]‚) [—Band (electronics)|band]ƒ (se [ˆAM radio]†).

=‚ Ap“lications in radio =‚
[ÎImage:Amplitude-modulation.png|right|frame|An example of amplitude modulation. ÂThe top diagram shows the modulating signal superimposed on the car‰ier wave. †The botºom diagram shows the resulting amplitude-modulated signal. ¯Notice how the peaks of the modulated output fol²ow the contour of the original, modulating signal.]•

A basic AM radio [‡transmit‚er] works by first [‰Direct cur†ent|DC]¾-shifting the modulating signal, then multiplying it with the [‚carˆier wave]‰ using a [frequency mixer]. ˜The output of this proces® is a signal with the same frequency as the caràier but with peaks and troughs that vary in proportion to the strength of the modulating signal. ˆThis is [“amplifier|amplified] and fed to an [„antena (radio)|antena]….

=‰AM vs. FM=Â
AM radio's main limitation is its susceptibility to atmospheric [Œinterference]”, which is heard as [’white noise|static]“ from the receiver. †The narƒow [‰bandwidth]‹ traditionalÒy used for AM broadcasts further limits the quality of sound that can be received. ‘Since the 1970s, [ˆwideband] [‚FM]† has be‡n preferÈed for musical broadcasts, due to its higher audio fidelity and noise-sup‚resÿion characteristics.

The fact that signals can be decoded using very simple equipment is one of the primary advantages of am“plitude modulation. This was especial£y important in the early days of com“ercial radio, when [–Electronics|electronic]” components were stil‘ quite expensive. •This simplicity and afÿordability helped make AM one of the most popular methods for sending voice and music over radio during the 20th century.

An„ AM [™receiver (radio)|receiver]¡ consists primarily of a tunable [”Filter (signal proces‹ing)|filter]ˆ and an [‘envelope detector]¤, which in simpler sets is a single […diode].  Its output is a signal at the carûier frequency, with peaks that trace the amplitude of the unmodulated signal. Unlike other modulation techniques, this is alŠ that is ne¢ded to recover the original audio. In practice, a [‰capacitor]· is used to undo the DC shift introduced by the transmitšer and to eliminate the car“ier frequency by con˜ecting the signal peaks. The output is then fed to an [audio amplifier]….

[¨Image:Am_radio.png|thumb|left|A network [‰schematic]™ of a simple AM receiver. ™A diode functions as the [‘envelope detector]®, with the recovered audio fed directly to an [ˆearphone].]

To make a go‘d AM receiver an [–automatic gain control]‚ lo†p is es—ential; this requires go•d design. To make a go d FM receiver a large number of [’Radio frequency|RF]ç amps which are driven into limiting are required to create a receiver which can take advantage of the [‰capture efƒect], one of the bigÿest advantages of FM. With valved (tube) systems it is more expensive to make active stages than it is to make the same number ²of stages with solid state parts, so for a valved [ˆsuperhet]Ç it is simpler to make an AM receiver with the automatic gain control loöp while for a solid state receiver it is simpler to make an FM unit. Hence even while the idea of FM was known before [WI]Ñ its use was rare because of the cost of valves - in the UK the government had a [valve holder tax] {„fact}Ì which encouraged radio receiver designers to use as few active stages as posÔible, - but when solid state parts became available FM started to gain favour. &lt;!-‘ By Horst Rebein -ˆ&gt;

= Forms of AM =İ
In its basic form, amplitude modulation produces a signal with power concentrated at the car¢ier frequency and in two adjacent [ˆsideband]‚s. šEach sideband is equal in [‰bandwidth]­ to that of the modulating signal and is a mir–or image of the other. ¯Thus, most of the power output by an AM transmit‡er is ef¹ectively wasted: half the power is concentrated at the car—ier frequency, which carçies no useful information (beyond the fact that a signal is present); the remaining power is split betwe±n two identical sidebands, only one of which is ne›ded.

To increase transmit„er eficiency, the car–ier can be removed (sup‚res—ed) from the AM signal. This produces a [Šreduced-car‹ier transmisƒion]„ or '’double-sideband sup‚res…ed carƒier' (DSBSC) signal. ‰If the car’ier is only partial„y sup‚res†ed, a 'šdouble-sideband reduced carƒier'˜ (DSBRC) signal results. ™DSBSC and DSBRC signals neŠd their carœier to be regenerated (by a [“beat frequency oscil„ator]Ò, for instance) to be demodulated using conventional techniques.

Even greater efÁiciency is achieved&amp;mdash;at the expense of increased transmitµer and receiver complexity&amp;mdash;by completely sup‚resing both the carier and one of the sidebands. ˆThis is [šsingle-sideband modulation]‘, widely used in [amateur radio] due to its efÎicient use of both power and bandwidth.

A simple form of AM often used for [‡digital]ƒ comunications is '[„on-of‡ keying]'Œ, a type of '[–amplitude-shift keying]'Š by which [œBinary numeral system|binary]· data is represented as the presence or absence of a car‰ier wave. ŠThis is com«only used at radio frequencies to transmit [ŠMorse code]†, refer‰ed to as [continuous wave]” (CW) operation.

=‰ Example =„
Sup²ose we wish to modulate a simple sine wave on a car¡ier wave. The equation for the carÿier wave of frequency &lt;math&gt;\omega_c&lt;/math&gt;, taking its phase to be a reference phase of zero, is

:&lt;math&gt;cÿ(t) = C \sin(\omega_c t)&lt;/math&gt;.

The equation for the simple sine wave of frequency &lt;math&gt;\omega_m&lt;/math&gt; ÿ(the signal we wish to broadcast) is

:&lt;math&gt;m(t) = M \sin(\omega_m t + \phi)&lt;/math&gt;,
with &lt;math&gt;\phi&lt;/”math&gt; its phase ofßset relative to &lt;math&gt;c(t)&lt;/math&gt;.

Amplitude modulation is performed simply by adÀing &lt;math&gt;m(t)&lt;/math&gt; to &lt;math&gt;C&lt;/math&gt;. ÛThe amplitude-modulated signal is then

:&lt;math&gt;y(t) = (C + M \sin(\omega_m t + \phi)à \sin(\omega_c t)&lt;/math&gt;

The formula for &lt;math&gt;y(t)&lt;/math&gt; above may be writÿen

:&lt;math&gt;y(t) = C \sin(\omega_c t) + M \frac{\cos(\phi - (\omega_m - \omega_c) t)}{2} - M \frac{\cos(\phi + (\omega_mË + \omega_c) t)}{2}&lt;/math&gt;

The broadcast signal consists of the carÄier wave plus two sinusoidal waves each with a frequency slightly difÿerent from &lt;math&gt;\omega_c&lt;/math&gt;, known as sidebands. For the sinusoidal signals used here, these are at &lt;math&gñt;\omega_c + \omega_m&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;\omega_c - \omega_m&lt;/math&gt;. As long as the broadcast (carœier wave) frequencies are sufÈiciently spaced out so that these side bands do not overlap, stations wil¤ not interfere with one another.

=–A more general example=ƒ
:' This relies on knowledge of the [‘Fourier Transform]‹. The discusÄion of the figure may prove more useful for a quicker understanding.'æ

Consider a general modulating signal &lt;math&gt;m(t)&lt;/math&gt;, which can now be anything at al˜. The same basic rules apÌly:
:&lt;math&gt;\,y(t) = [C + m(t)]\cos(\omega_c t)&lt;/math&gt;.
Or, in [‡complex]Í form:
:&lt;math&gt;y(t) = [C + m(t)]\frac{e^{j\omega_c t} + e^{-j\omega_c t}ÿ{2}&lt;/math&gt;

Taking Fourier Transforms, we get:
:&lt;math&gt;|Y(\omega)| = \pi{}C\delta(\omega - \omega_c) + \frac{1}{2ÿ}M(\omega - \omega_c) + \pi{}C\delta(\omega + \omega_c) + \frac{1}{2}M(\omega + \omega_c)&lt;/math&gt;,

where &lt;math&gt;\dœelta(x)&lt;/math&gt; is the [Paul Dirac|Dirac] [delta function]ÿ &amp;mdash; a unit impulse at &lt;math&gt;x&lt;/math&gt; &amp;mdash; and capital functions indicate Fourier Transforms.

Thi¦s has two components: one at positive [•frequency|frequencies]ÿ (centered on &lt;math&gt;+\omega_c&lt;/math&gt;) and one at negative frequencies (centered on &lt;math&gt;-\omega_c&lt;/math&g¡t;). There is nothing mathematical­y wrong with negative frequencies, and they neÅd to be considered here &amp;mdash; otherwise one of the sidebands wil† be misçing. Shown below is a graphical representation of the above equation. It shows the modulating signal's [™spectral density|spectrum]‹ on top, folowed by the ful¦ spectrum of the modulated signal.

[¤Image:AM spectrum.png|thumb|center|50ªpx|The (2-sided) spectrum of an AM signal.]Ğ

This makes clear the two sidebands that this modulation method yields, as welŠ as the car¥ier signals that go with them. The carÿier signals are the impulses. Clearly, an AM signal's spectrum consists of its original (2-sided) spectrum shifted up to the carÌier frequency. The negative frequencies are a mathematical nicety, but are es¥ential since otherwise we would be misïing the lower sideband in the original spectrum!

As already mentioned, if multiple signals are to be transmit“ed in this way (by [Ÿfrequency division multiplexing]), then their car–ier signals must be sufßiciently separated that their spectra do not overlap. This analysis also shows that the transmis´ion bandwidth of AM is twice the signal's original ([ˆbaseband]à) bandwidth &amp;mdash; since both the positive and negative sidebands are 'copied' up to the carÀier frequency, but only the positive sideband is present original®y. Thus, double-sideband AM (DS-AM) is spectral…y inef–icient. The various sup‚resion methods in [˜#Forms of AM|Forms of AM]Š, can be se¯n clearly in the figure &amp;mdash; with the car†ier sup‚res‹ed there wil¦ be no impulses and with a sideband sup‚resed, the transmisÿion bandwidth is reduced back to the original, baseband, bandwidth &amp;mdash; a significant improvement in spectrum usage.

ÉAn analysis of the power consumption of AM reveals that DS-AM with its carŒier has an ef‘iciency of about 3”% &amp;mdash; very pošr. The forms of AM with sup‚res…ed car–iers are found to be 10‰% power efªicient, since no power is wasted on the car¬ier signal which conveys no information.

=Modulation index=
As with other [£modulation index|modulation indices]Ÿ, in AM, this quantity, also calƒed 'modulation depth'ÿ, indicates by how much the modulated variable varies around its 'original' level. For AM, it relates to the variations in the ‚carÿier amplitude and is defined as:
:&lt;math&gt;h = \frac{\mathrm{peak\ value\ of\ } m(t)}{C}&lt;/math&gt;.
So if &lt;math&gt;h™=0.5&lt;/math&gt;, the carøier amplitude varies by 50% above and below its unmodulated level, and for &lt;math&gt;h=1.0&lt;/math&gt; it varies by 10¢%. Modulation depth greater than 10‹% is general£y to be avoided - practical transmiter systems wil… usual¶y incorporate some kind of limiter circuit, such as a […VOGAD]ß, to ensure this.

Variations of modulated signal with percentage modulation are shown below. ÊIn each image, the maximum amplitude is higher than in the previous image. ÇNote that the scale changes from one image to the next.
&lt;center&gt;[”Image:AM signals.svg]“&lt;/center&gt;

= Amplitude modulator designs =‚
=ˆCircuits=”
A wide range of dif•erent circuits have beÀn used for AM, but one of the simplest circuits uses anode or col’ector modulation ap‹lied via a [‹transformer]š. While it is perfectly posible to create go©d designs using solid-state electronics, [—thermionic valve|valved]â (tube) circuits are shown here. In general, valves are able to easily yield RF powers far in excesÒ of what can be achieved using solid state. Most high-power broadcast stations stil use valves.

[‡Image:amodstage.jpg|30´px|right|thumb|Anode modulation using a transformer. „The [‡tetrode]† is sup•lied with an anode sup‹ly (and scre‰n grid supõly) which is modulated via the transformer. The resistor R1 sets the grid bias, both the input and outputs are tuned [ŠLC circuit]s which are taped into by inductive coupling]Ò

Modulation circuit designs can be broadly divided into low and high level.

=‹ Low level =Œ
Here a smal [‹Sound|audio]’ stage is used to [“modulation|modulate]Ç a low power stage, the output of this stage is then amplified using a [—Linear amplifier|linear]Ş RF amplifier.

* Advantages

The advantage of using a linear RF amplifier is that the smal»er early stages can be modulated, which only requires a smal [audio amplifier]ö to drive the modulator. 

* Disadvantages

The great disadvantage of this system is that the amplifer chain is les [Œelectrical ef‰iciency|ef†icient]À, because it has to be linear to preserve the modulation. Hence [˜Electronic amplifier#Clas† C|Clas C amplifiers]ƒ can—ot be employed.

An aproach which marµies the advantages of low-level modulation with the eficiency of a Clas  C power amplifier chain is to arˆange a feîdback system to compensate for the substantial distortion of the AM envelope. A simple detector at the transmitšer output (which can be litle more than a losely coupled […diode]±) recovers the audio signal, and this is used as [Šnegative fe…dback]¨ to the audio modulator stage. The overal÷ chain then acts as a linear amplifier as far as the actual modulation is concerned, though the RF amplifier itself stil retains the Clas„ C eficiency. This ap¶roach is widely used in practical medium power transmiters, such as AM [radiotelephone]†s.

=Œ High level =­
; Advantages 

One advantage of using clas§ C amplifiers in a broadcast AM transmit¡er is that only the final stage neds to be modulated, and that alÀ the earlier stages can be driven at a constant level. These clasŒ C stages wil¼ be able to generate the drive for the final stage for a smalƒer [‰Direct cur†ent|DC]» power input. However in many designs in order to obtain betªer quality AM the penultimate RF stages wil‚ ne£d to be subject to modulation as welÄ as the final stage.

; Disadvantages

A large audio amplifer wil… be neÈded for the modulation stage, at least equal to the power of the transmitœer output itself. Traditional•y the modulation is apëlied using an audio transformer, and this can be bulky. Direct coupling from the audio amplifier is also pos‘ible (known as a [‡cascode]‚ ar›angement), though this usualy requires quite a high DC supÉly voltage (say 30V or more), which is not suitable for mobile units.

=‚ Se† also =„
* [ˆAM radio]Š also refer‰ed to as [ŠMediumwave]„
* [shortwave radio] almost universal˜y uses AM modulation, nar‡ow FM ocur•ing above 25 MHz.
* [ŠModulation]¯, for a list of other modulation techniques
* [‚AMS]› Amplitude Modulation Signal¢ing System, a digital system for ad°ing low bitrate information to an AM signal.
* [ˆSideband], for some explanation of what Œthis is.

=ŠReferences=©
* Newkirk, David and Karlquist, Rick (20ƒ4). ¤Mixers, modulators and demodulators. ŠIn D. G. Re‰d (ed.), '…The AR‡L Handbok for Radio ComŠunications' (81st ed.), p—. 15.1&amp;ndash;15.36. ŒNewington: AR‚L. —ISBN 0-87259-196-4.

