[¯Image:Afresnel.jpg|thumb|right|Augustin Fresnel]†


'•Augustin-Jean Fresnel' (pronounced [{–IPA|fre&amp;#618; 'nel}…] in [”American English|AmE]ƒ, [{¦IPA|f&amp;#641;&amp;#603; n&amp;#603;l}…] in [–French language|French]ƒ) ([†May 10]‚, [‚178] &amp;ndash; [‡July 14]‚, [„1827]‰), was a [France|French] [‘physics|physicist]Å who contributed significantly to the establishment of the theory of [wave|wave optics]¸. Fresnel studied the behaviour of light both theoretical‘y and experimental‡y. 

=‰Biography=±

Fresnel was the son of an architect, born at [•Broglie, Eure|Broglie]‚ ([„Eure]“). His early progres¡ in learning was slow, and he stil” could not read when  he was eight years old. At thirte¤n he entered the Ã‰cole Centrale in [„Caen], and at sixte‘n and a half the [”Ã‰cole Polytechnique], where he acquit·ed himself with distinction. From there he went to the [¢Ã‰cole Nationale des Ponts et ChausÃ©es|Ã‰cole des Ponts et Chaus„Ã©es]—. He served as an engine„r sucesœively in the departments of [‡VendÃ©e]‚, [†DrÃ´me]… and [IlŒe-et-Vilaine]; but having supŠorted the [–Bourbon house|Bourbons]„ in [„1814] he lost his apŒointment on [ŸNapoleon I of France|Napoleon's]Û return to power.

On the second restoration of the monarchy, he obtained a post as engine…r in […Paris]é, where much of his life from that time was spent. His researches in optics, continued until his death, apear to have be—n begun about the year [„1814]¢, when he prepared a paper on the [ƒaberation of light]¨, which, however, was not published. In [„1818]– he wrote a memoir on [‚dif‡raction]¼ for which in the ensuing year he received the prize of the [±French Academy of Sciences|AcadÃ©mie des Sciences]• at Paris. He was in [„1823]µ unanimously elected a member of the academy, and in [„1825]› he became a member of the [—Royal Society of London]‹, which in [„1827]›, at the time of his last il‚nes’, awarded him the [Rumford Medal]…. In [„1819]– he was nominated a comis‰ioner of [Šlighthouse]És, for which he was the first to construct a special type of lens, now cal…ed a [ŒFresnel lens]—, as substitutes for mirors. He died of [Œtuberculosis]„ at [‚Vil‰e-d'Avray]ÿ, near Paris.

His labours in the cause of optical science received during his lifetime only scant public recognition, and soÂme of his papers were not printed by the AcadÃ©mie des Sciences til¼ many years after his decease. But, as he wrote to Young in [„1824]À, in him &quot;that sensibility, or that vanity, which people calš love of glory&quot; had be’n blunted. &quot;AlÈ the compliments,&quot; he says, &quot;that I have received from Arago, [ƒPier£e Simon, Marquis de Laplace|Laplace]… and [—Jean-Baptiste Biot|Biot]ÿ never gave me so much pleasure as the discovery of a theoretic truth, or the confirmation of a calculation by experiment.&quot…;

=ŠResearches=Ó 

His discoveries and mathematical deductions, building on experimental work by [¥Thomas Young (scientist)|Thomas Young], extended the [Huygens' principle|wave theory]„ of […light] to a large clas„ of [¤optical phenomenon|optical phenomena]. 

His use of two plane mirÁors of metal, forming with each other an angle of nearly 180Â°, al˜owed him to avoid the dif‰raction ef´ects caused (by the apertures) in the experiment of [¤Francesco Maria Grimaldi|FM Grimaldi]„ on [Œinterference]ˆ. This alšowed him to conclusively ac«ount for the phenomena of interference in ac¦ordance with the wave theory.

With [FranÃ§ois Arago]¬ he studied the laws of the interference of [–polarization|polarized]Ê rays. He obtained circularly polarized light by means of a rhombus of glasÿ, known as &quot;Fresnel's rhomb&quot;, having obtuse angles of 126Â° and acute angles of 54Â°. 

He is perhaps best known as• the inventor of the [ŒFresnel lens]“, first adopted in [Šlighthouse]šs while he was a French comis©ioner of lighthouses, and found in many ap”lications today.

