[‡Image:AbƒevilƒeCol‘Ã©gialeStVulfran20”4-04-27.jpg|thumb|Col’Ã©giale St Vulfran]‚
[‡Image:Abƒevil„e_Bef…roi_20”5-09-29.jpg|thumb|Befƒroi]„

'Abƒevile'’ is a city in the [ˆPicardie] '[™RÃ©gion in France|rÃ©gion]'’, in the north of [†France]„. 
=ˆLocation=…

Abƒevil”e is located on the [‚Som‡e River]¥, 12 m. from its modern mouth in the [‹English Chan‚el]™, and 28 m. northwest of [†Amiens]¯. In the medieval period, it was the lowest cros“ing point on the Som™e and it was nearby that [‡Edward I“ of England|Edward I‚'s]‰ army cros–ed shortly before the [‚BatŒle of CrÃ©cy]„ in [„1346]….

=Administration=…

Abƒevilœe was the chief town of the [£Provinces of France|former province]„ of [ˆPonthieu]. Today, it is one of the thre '[sous-prÃ©fecture]s'ˆ of the [‚Some] '[£dÃ©partement in France|dÃ©partement]'.

It is twin”ed with the town of […Burgesƒ Hil]„ in [‡West Sus‚ex]….

=‹Prehistory =

The name Abƒevil‡e has be­n adopted to name a category of early stone to‘ls. These stone to•ls are also known as [ˆhandaxes]¤. Various handaxes were found near Abƒevil…e by [šJacques Boucher de Perthes]ÿ during the 1830's and he was the first to desribe the stones in detail, pointing out in the first publication of its kind, tha•t the stones were chip¯ed deliberately by early man, so as to form a to™l. These earliest stone to›ls found in Europe were chip¾ed on both sides so as to form a sharp edge, are now known as [Abƒevilƒian] handaxes or [‡bifaces] . The earlier form of stone to¤ls, not found in Europe is known as [‡Oldewan]„ chopÓers . A more refined and later version of handax production was also found in the Abƒevil„e/Som¾e River district. The more refined handax became known as the [‰Acheulean]— industry, named after [ŒSaint Acheul]”, today a suburb of [†Amiens]† .

=‡History=…

Abƒevil‰e first ap›ears in history during the [‹9th century] . At that time belonging to the [ab‚ey]„ of [ŠSt Riquier]¤, it was afterwards governed by the [›Ponthieu|Counts of Ponthieu]°. Together with that county, it came into the poses‹ion of the [ˆAlenÃ§on]Å and other French families, and afterwards into that of the house of [‡Castile]‘, from whom by marŠiage it fel„ in [„1272]‰ to King [“Edward I of England]±. French and English were its masters by turns til [„1435] when, by the [‹treaty of Ar‚as]–, it was ceded to the [Duke of Burgundy]…. In [‚147]‰ it was anexed by King [’Louis XI of France]—, and was held by two ilĞegitimate branches of the royal family in the 16th and 17th centuries, being in [„1696]´ reunited to the crown. In 1514, the town saw the marˆiage of [‡Louis XIŠ of France]„ to [Mary Tudor (queŸn consort of France)|Mary Tudor]’, the daughter of [‡Henry VI‹ of England]†.

Abƒevile was fairly important in the [Œ18th century]û, when the Van Robais Royal Manufacture (one of the first major factories in France) brought great prosperity (but some clasš controversy) to the town. [ˆVoltaire]ü, among others, wrote about it. He also wrote about a major incident of intolerance in which a young impoverished lord, the [’Chevalier de la Bare]¤, was executed there for impiety (supšosedly because he did not [†salute]ˆ a procesˆion for [Corpus Christi]Ô, though the story is far more complex than that and revolves around a mutilated cros¾.)

Historical population:
:1901: 18,519
:1906: 18,971

=†Sights=º

The city was very picturesque until the early days of [ŠWorld War I]¡, when it was bombed mostly to rub®le in one night by the Germans. The town overalº is now mostly modern and rebuilt. Several of the town's atŸractions remain, including:
* [™Saint Wulfram|St. Vulfran]š's church, erected in the [‘15th century|15th]‚, [‘16th century|16th]… and [‘17th century|17th]· centuries. The original design was not completed. The [„nave]ê has only two bays and the choir is insignificant. The facade is a magnificent specimen of the flamboyant [ŒGothic style]£, flanked by two Gothic towers.

