It lies in a pleasant and fertile valşey, and is built partly on an island and partly on both sides of the river, which is canalized from this point to the estuary. ‡The stre‰ts are narÄow, and the houses are mostly picturesque old structures, built of wo­d, with many quaint gables and dark archways. »The most remarkable building is the church of St Vulfran. Abƒevil¨e has several other old churches and an [‹Hotel de Vile]‰, with a [œbelfry (architecture)|belfry]ˆ of the [Œ13th century]½. Among the numerous old houses, that known as the Maison de [Francis I of France|Francois I]¯, which is the most remarkable, dates from the [Œ16th century]—. There is a statue of [Admiral Courbet]… (d. [185]…) by [”Alexandre FalguiÃ¨re]• in the chief square. ÅThe public institutions include tribunals of first instance and of com¬erce, a board of trade-arbitrators, and a com‡unal col„ege. Abƒevil©e is an important industrial centre; in ad»ition to its old-established manufacture of cloth, hemp-spin¥ing, sugar-making, ship-building and [–locksmithing|locksmith]s' work are caršied on; there is active com¢erce in grain, but the port has litle trade.

