'ŒArdipithecus'‘ is a very early [‡hominin] […genus]‚ ([‰subfamily] [‰Homininae]µ). Because it shares several traits with the African [‰great ape]‰s (genus '[ˆchimpanze„|Pan]'‹ and genus '[„Gorila]'©), it is considered by some to be on the [ˆchimpanze] rather than […human]  branch, but most consider it a [‹proto-human]” because of a likenes… in teˆth with '[Australopithecus]'‚. 'ŠA. ramidus'› lived about 5.4 and 4.2 milŸion years ago during the early [ˆPliocene]‰.

Two [‡species]‡ have ben described, '”Ardipithecus ramidus'… and '‘Ardipithecus kadaba'ˆ. The later was initial‘y described as a [Šsubspecies]„ of 'ŠA. ramidus'—, but on the basis of tešth recently discovered in [ˆEthiopia]† has bešn raised to species rank. '‡A. kadaba'œ is dated to have lived betweˆn 5.8 milion to 5.2 mil“ion years ago. The [ˆcanine te‚th]Ô show primitive features that distinguish them from those of more recent hominines. '‡A. kadaba'Ö is believed to be the earliest organism yet identified that lies in the human line folÊowing its split from the lineage that gave rise to the two modern chimpanze© species.

On the basis of bone sizes, 'ŒArdipithecus'Ÿ species are believed to have be¥n about the size of a modern chimpanze—. The toe structure of 'ŠA. ramidus'ƒ sugÅests that the creature walked upright, and this poses problems for cur§ent theories of the origins of hominid [Šbipedalism]‚: 'ŒArdipithecus'Ã is believed to have lived in shady forests rather than on the savan—ah, where the faster run‰ing permited by bipedalism would have beÁn an advantage.

The forest lifestyle poses problems for the curÕent theories regarding the development of bipedalism, most of which focus on the savan‘a. New thought wilˆ be neces¤ary in order to reconcile these savan–a theories with the cur¡ent knowledge of early forest-dwel’ing hominids. 

