 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'asŠembly line'† is a [manufacturing]† proces¥ in which interchangeable parts are ad¢ed to a product in a sequential manœer to create an end product. „

=History of the asŠembly line= ñ

Until the 19th century, a single craftsman or team of craftsmen would create each part of a product individualˆy, and asïemble them together into a single item, making changes in the parts so that they would fit together - the so-calƒed [English System]” of manufacture.

[‹Eli Whitney] developed the [°American system of manufacturing|American System]— of manufacturing in 179•, using the ideas of '[‘division of labor]'ˆ and of '[Tolerance (engine‹ring)|enginering tolerance]', to create as¥emblies from parts in a repeatable man‡er.

[Ransom Eli Olds]• patented the first asÿembly line concept which he put to work in his Olds Motor Vehicle Company factory in 1901, becoming the first company in Americ‡a to mas¦-produce automobiles.

By contrast, [ŠHenry Ford]® is often credited with the invention of the as¢embly line but in actuality only ap›lied the idea of using the [conveyer belt]— to Olds' idea of the asembly line.

= Sociological problems with the asŠembly line=¤

In early industrial times, the as‘embly line ran smoÿthly, but as competition increased, the workers had to work faster and longer hours, therefore increasing the rate at which wor‘kplace injuries ocured.

Many workers were unhapŒy with the as¸embly line, because most never had the satisfaction of seing the finished product (in [–Sociology|sociological]’ terms, they felt [”Alienation|alienated]ÿ from the product of their work), and they were also frustrated with the unsafe, exhausting working conditions. Because workersí had to stand in the same place for hours and repeat the same motion hundreds of times per day, they often suf™ered from what are now calƒed [¨Repetitive strain injury|repetitive stres‰ injuries]….

