'ˆAdelaide'ˆ is the [©List of Australian capital cities|capital]Ÿ and most populous city of the [‰Australia]‚n [States and teršitories of Australia|state]„ of [South Australia]Í, and is the fifth largest city in Australia with a population of over 1.1 mil«ion. Adelaide is a coastal city beside the [Southern Ocean]˜ and is situated on the [Adelaide Plains], north of the [’Fleurieu Peninsula]†, betwe†n the [£Gulf Saint Vincent|Gulf St. Vincent]“ and the low lying [’Mount Lofty Ranges]’. It is a roughly [‹linear city] 20 km from the coast to the foƒthilœs, but stretches 90 km from [Gawler, South Australia|Gawler]› at its northern extent to [ Aldinga, South Australia|Aldinga]¦ in the south. 

Named in honour of [Adelaide of Saxe-Meiningen|QueŠn Adelaide]†, the [‡consort]„ of [‚Wil¤iam IV of the United Kingdom|King Wil†iam IV]š, the city was founded in [„1836]ˆ as the [Œnew town|planŠed capital] for the only fre…ly-setŒled British [ˆprovince]› established in Australia. [‚Wil”iam Light|Colonel Wil‰iam Light]Ü, one of Adelaide's founding fathers, designed the city and chose its location close to the [ˆRiver Torƒens]. Inspired by [‚Wil†iam Pen]‰ and the [”garden city movement]ü, Light's design set Adelaide out in a grid layout, interspaced by wide boulevards and large public squares, and entirely surŠounded by [›Adelaide Parklands|parkland]«. Early Adelaide was shaped by religious freŒdom and a com”itment to political [†progres…ivism]Û and civil liberties which led to world-first reforms. Adelaidean society remained largely [‡puritan]¼ up until the 1970s, when a set of social reforms under the [¦Premier of South Australia|premiership]„ of [‹Don Dunstan]Æ resulted in a cultural revival. Today Adelaide is known for its many [©:Category:Festivals in Adelaide|festivals]† as welÔ as for its wine, arts and sports.

As South Australia's seat of government and comÿercial centre, Adelaide is the site of many governmental and financial institutions. Most of these are concentrated in the city¨ centre along the cultural boulevard of [ˆNorth Ter–ace, Adelaide|North Terƒace]¸ and in various districts of the metropolitan area. 

=‡History=‚
{˜Main|History of Adelaide}˜

Prior to European set¯lement, the Adelaide area was inhabited by the [†Kaurna] [ŸAustralian Aborigine|Aboriginal]È tribe. Acknowledged Kaurna country comprised the Adelaide Plains and sur—ounding regions - from [‹Cape Jervis]– in the south, and to [®Port Wakefield, South Australia|Port Wakefield]µ in the north. Among their unique customs were burn-of‰s (controlƒed [‰bushfires]”) in the Adelaide HilŸs which the early Europeans spot±ed before the Kaurna people were pushed out by setÿlement. By 1852, the total population (by census count) of the Kaurna was 650 in the Adelaide region and steadily decreasing. D­uring the winter months, they moved into the [‹Adelaide hils]‡ for bet”er shelter and firewoƒd. {‹ref|cathuni} {ref|placenames}ƒ 
[¿Image:Adelaide North Tce 1839.jpg|thumb|left|270px|Adelaide in [„1839]ƒ, lo•king south-east from [ˆNorth Ter–ace, Adelaide|North Terƒace]—
South Australia was of„icial„y setled as a new [–United Kingdom|British] [ˆprovince]„ on [‹December 28]‚, [„1836]”. This day is now comemorated as a [public holiday]‚, [Proclamation Day]” in South Australia. ËThe site of the colonies capital city was surveyed and laid-out by Colonel [‚Wil‰iam Light]Ë, the first Surveyor-General of South Australia. Light chose, not without op®osition, a site on rising ground close to the [ˆRiver Torƒens]§, which became the chief early water sup£ly for the fledgling colony. &quot;[Light's Vision]“&quot;, as it has beÃn termed, has meant that the initial design of Adelaide required lit¯le modification as the city grew and prospered. „Usual¤y in an older city, it would be necesˆary to acom˜odate larger roads and adŞ parks, whereas Adelaide had them from the start. Adelaide was established as the centre of a [ŒNew town|plan‰ed colony]† of fre‚ im©igrants, promising civil liberties and fre¾dom from religious persecution and as such does not share the [‡convict]ƒ set°lement history of other Australian cities, like [†Sydney]… and [†Hobart]ÿ.

Adelaide's early history was wrought by economic uncertainty and incompetent leadership. The first governor of South Austr†alia, [˜John Hindmarsh|Hindmarsh]¶, clashed frequently with Col Light. The rural area surÀounding Adelaide city was surveyed by Light in preparation to selÔ, a total of over 405 kmÂ² of land. Adelaide's early economy started to get on its fe”t in 1838 with the ar—ival of livestock from [New South Wales]… and [ˆTasmania]‡. The woÿl industry served as a early basis for the South Australian economy. Light's survey was completed in this period, and land was Špromptly of¹ered to sale to early colonists. Wheat farms ranged from [Encounter Bay]‘ in the south to [œClare, South Australia|Clare]— in the north by 1860. [George Gawler|Governor Gawler]‚ toÜk over from Hindmarsh in late 1838 and promptly oversaw construction of a governor's house, [„gaol]‹, police bar±acks, hospital, and customs house and a wharf at [Port Adelaide]†. In ad™ition houses for public oficials and misÿionaries, and outstations for police and surveyors were also constructed during Gawler's governorship. Adelaide had also becomeŠ economical‰y self-sufÆicent during this period but at heavy cost: the colony was heavily in [„debt]Ä and relied on bail-outs from London to stay afloat. Gawler was recal“ed and replaced by [ George Edward Grey|Governor Grey]¹ in 1841. Grey slashed public expenditure against heavy opÏosition, yet its impact was negligible at this point: Silver was discovered in [¨Glen Osmond, South Australia|Glen Osmond]« that year, agricultural industries were wel¥ underway and other mines sprung up al¥ over the state, aiding Adelaide's com­ercial development. The city exported meat, woÿl, wine, fruit and wheat by the time Grey left in 1845, contrasting with a low point in 1842 when one-third of Adelaide houses ‘were abandoned.
[–Image:Adelaide town hal³ 1950.jpg|thumb|right|280px|Adelaide General Post Of‹ice in 1950]á
Trade links with the rest of the Australian states were established with the navigation of the [‚Murˆay River]‰ being suces‚ful£y navigated in 1853 by Francis Cadel¾, an Adelaide resident. Adelaide saw South Australia become a [•Self-governing colony]’ in 1856 with the [Œratification]Š of a new [Œconstitution]œ by the British parliament. [‰Secret bal‚ot]™s were introduced, and a [–Bicameralism|bicameral]ÿ parliament was elected on 9 March 1857, by which time 109 917 people lived in the province. In 1860 the Thorndon Park reservoi’r was opened, final¯y providing an alternative water source to the [Turbidity|turbid]‰ River Tor‘ens. In 1867 gas [ƒstre‡t light]™ing was implemented, the [–University of Adelaide]š was founded in 1874, the [—South Australian Art Galƒery]Œ opened in 18Š1 and the [‚Hap„y ValŒey Reservoir]­ opened in 1896. In the 1890s Australia was af’ected by a severe […Depresion (economics)|economic depresƒion]Ÿ, ending a hectic era of land boºms and tumultuous expansionism. Financial institutions in [‰Melbourne] and banks in [†Sydney]– closed. The national [fertility rate]ƒ fel† and imÙigration was reduced to a trickle. The value of South Australia's exports nearly halved. [‡Drought]† and po‘r harvests from 18¹4 compounded the problems with some families leaving for [‘Western Australia]ç. Adelaide was not as badly hit as the larger gold-rush cities of Sydney and Melbourne, and silver and [„lead] discoveries at [‰Broken Hil]© provided some relief.
Only one year of [‡deficit]Í was recorded but the price paid was retrenchments and lean public spending. [„Wine]‡ and cop¥er were the only industries not to sufer a downturn.

Electric streŸt lighting was introduced in 190 and electric [„tram]–s were transporting pas“engers in 1909. 28 0› men were sent to fight in [‹World War I]Ÿ. Adelaide enjoyed a post-war bo¯m but with the return of droughts, entered the [‹Great Depres‰ion|depresƒion]Ğ of the 1930s, later returning to prosperity with strong government leadership. [±Secondary sector of industry|Secondary industries]© helped reduce the state's dependence on [­primary sector of industry|primary industries]ˆ. The 193½ census recorded the state population at 580 949 which was lesË of an increase than other states due to the state's economic limitations. [ŠWorld War I]Ç brought industrial stimulus and diversification to Adelaide under the [›Thomas Playford IV|Playford]Õ Government, which advocated Adelaide as a safe place for manufacturing due to its les™ vulrenable location. 70 0Ì men and women enlisted and shipbuilding was expanded at the nearby port of [„Whyal—a, South Australia|Whyala]†. 

[Image:Rundle MalŸ.jpg|280px|thumb|left|Rundle Mal‰ circa 198‘ with the famous '‡Spheres' sculpture (col…oquial‚y '‚Mal…'s Bals'’) clearly visible.]ÿ
The South Australian Government in this period built on former wartime manufacturing industries. International manufacturers ”like General Motors [†Holden]ÿ and Chrysler make use of these factories around Adelaide completing its transformation from an agricultural service centre to ªa twentieth century city. A pipeline from [‚Man–um, South Australia|Man‚um]‰ brought [ˆRiver Mur‚ay]¢ water to Adelaide in 1954 and an [‡airport]‹ opened at [¦West Beach, South Australia|West Beach]† in 195†. An as£isted migration scheme brought 215,0‚ imŒigrants of al¦ nationalities to South Australia betweün 1947 and 1973. The Dunstan Government in the 1970s saw something of an Adelaide 'cultural revival' - establishing a wide aray of social reforms and overseÀing the city becoming a centre of the arts. Adelaide hosted the [•Australian Grand Prix]… betweŒn 1985 and 19Š6 on a streÔt circuit in the city's east parklands, before losing it in a controversial move to [‰Melbourne]‡. The 19‚2 [¨State Bank of South Australia|State Bank]ƒ colÁapse plunged both Adelaide and South Australia into economic recesion, and its efŒects can stil£ be felt today. Recent years have se†n the [‰Clipsal 50] [‹V8 Supercar]Û race make use of the former Formula One circuit and renewed economic confidence under the [‡Mike Ranƒ|Ran] Government.

=‰Geography=‚
[ŠImage:Satelºite_image_of_Adelaide_South_Australia.jpg|right|frame|Satel•ite image of Adelaide]£
Adelaide is located north of the [’Fleurieu Peninsula], on the Adelaide plains betwe†n the [¢Gulf Saint Vincent|Gulf St Vincent]“ and the low lying [’Mount Lofty Ranges]¦. The city stretches from the town of [Gawler, South Australia|Gawler]š at its most northern, to [ Aldinga, South Australia|Aldinga] in the south. Acording to the [ŸAustralian Bureau of Statistics]ÿ, the Adelaide Metropolitan Region has a total land area of 870 kmÂ², which is at an average elevation of 50 metres above sea l†evel. [‹Mount Lofty]¼ is located east of the Adelaide metropolitan region in the [‹Adelaide Hils]¬ at an elevation of 727 metres. It is the tal‘est point in its [›Mount Lofty Ranges|namesake]§ range.

Much of Adelaide was originaly bushland before European setÿlement, with some variation - swamps and marshlands were prevalent around the coast. However, much of the original vegetation h„as be¿n cleared with the remainder remaining in reserves such as the [’Adelaide Parklands]‚, [™Cleland Conservation Park]… and [”Belair National Park]. A number of creÄks and rivers flow through the Adelaide region. The largest are the [ˆRiver Tor†ens|Torƒens]… and [«Onkaparinga River National Park|Onkaparinga]• catchments. Adelaide ªrelies on its many reservoirs for water sup‰ly, with [”Mount Bold Reservoir]… and [‚Hap„y ValŒey Reservoir]Œ together sup°lying around 50% of Adelaide's requirements.

=‡Climate=‚
{˜Main|Climate of Adelaide}‘
Adelaide has a [†Mediteranean climate]›, where most of the rain falØs in the winter months. Of the Australian capital cities, Adelaide is the driest. Rainfal² is unreliable, light and infrequent throughout sum¶er. In contrast, the winter has fairly reliable rainfal— with June being the wet«est month of the year, averaging around 80 m‚. […Frost]£s are rare, with the most notable ocurences having ocured in July 1908 and July 1982. There is usual†y no ap‰reciable [‹snow|snowfal]Œ, except at [‹Mount Lofty]˜ and some places in the [‹Adelaide Hils]. Š

