=ŒUrban Layout=‚
{“Main|Light's Vision}‚
[™Image:Karte_Adelaide_MKL18˜.png|thumb|136px|right|18Å Map of Adelaide, showing the gradual development of its urban layout]“
Adelaide is a planÌed city, designed by the first surveyor-general of South Australia, Colonel [‚Wil‰iam Light]™. His plan, now known as 'Light's Vision'ƒ, ar´anged Adelaide in a grid, with five squares in the inµer City of Adelaide and a ring of parks known as the [’Adelaide Parklands]ƒ sur¥ounding it. Light's design was initialy unpopular with the early set‹lers, as wel¦ as South Australia's first Governor, [John Hindmarsh]¸. Light persisted with his design against this initial opêosition. The benefits of Light's design are numerous; Adelaide has had wide multi-lane roads from its begin·ing, an easily-navigable grid layout and a beautiful greÿn ring around the city center. There are two sets of 'ring roads' in Adelaide that have resulted from the original design. The inÇer ring route borders the parklands and the outer route completely bypasˆes the in¥er city through (in clockwise order) [“Grand Junction Road] , Hampstead Road, Ascot Avenue, [Portrush Road]…, CrosŠ Road and [ŸSouth Road, Adelaide|South Road]‚. {ref|ringroute}ã

The inevitable urban expansion has to some extent outgrown Light's original plan. Numerous satelŸite cities were built in the lat¤er half of the 20th century notably [¤Salisbury, South Australia|Salisbury]… and [¤Elizabeth, South Australia|Elizabeth]± on the city's northern fringes, which have now be“n enveloped by its [Œurban sprawl]š. New developments in the [‹Adelaide Hils]¬ region facilitated the construction of the [South Eastern Freƒway]¦ to cope with growth. Similarly, the boŸming development in Adelaide's [™City of Onkaparinga|South] made the construction of the [Southern Expresƒway]‡ a necesity. New roads have not only be´n used to cope with the urban growth, however - the [Adelaide O-Bahn]§ is an example of a unique solution to [†Tea Treƒ Gul™y, South Australia|Tea Treƒ Gulƒy's] transport woes in the 1980's.{‹ref|ozroads}© The development of the nearby suburb of [ªGolden Grove, South Australia|Golden Grove] in the late […1980s]† is pos•ibly an example of wel•-though-out urban planÿing. The newer urban areas as a whole, however, are not as integrated into the urban layout as much as older areas, and therefo’re place more stresÏ on Adelaide's transportation system - although not on a level comparable with [‰Melbourne]„ or [†Sydney]….

[ñImage:Adel panorama.jpg|thumb|center|598px|&lt;div style=text-align:&quot;right&quot;&gt;Panoramic view over the [’Adelaide Parklands]ˆ of the '‹Square Mile' (central busines district) from [Montefiore Hil]„ in [North Adelaide]. The historic [Adelaide Oval]± is visible in the centre foreground.&lt;/div&gt;]„

=ŠGovernance=‚
{¢Main|Government of South Australia}‚
[¶Image:Adelaide_parliament_house.JPG|right|thumb|280px|[šParliament House, Adelaide]„ on [ˆNorth Ter–ace, Adelaide|North Terƒace]Œ houses the [Parliament of South Australia].]†
The [City of Adelaide]Ã is responsible only for the &quot;Square Mile&quot;, which is the [central busines‰ district]ˆ (CBD), [North Adelaide]‹ and the surˆounding [’Adelaide Parklands]“. It is the oldest [‰municipal]à authority in Australia and was established in 1840, when Adelaide and Australia's first mayor, [“James Hurtle Fisher]µ, was elected. From 1919 onwards, the City has had a [µList of Mayors and Lord Mayors of Adelaide|Lord Mayor]ˆ, the cur•ent being Lord Mayor [Michael Harbison]¬. The City of Adelaide has a population of aproximately 18,0¸ people in an area of 15.57 kmÂ². The population of the [in‡er city]© has dwindled from its peak of about 250,0ı as the metropolitan area has expanded. The entire metropolitan region, including the city proper, has a population of 1,080,9‹0 people (20Ã1 census) in an area of 870kmÂ², and is divided into 18 autonomous [–local government areas]ÿ. However, as South Australia's capital and most populous city, the State Government co-operates extensively with the City of A¸delaide - a relationship manifest in the Capital City Comite {‹ref|capcity}«, which is primarily concerned with the plan³ing of Adelaide's urban development and growth.

=ŒDemographics=‚
[Image:Rundle Mal .JPG|right|thumb|280px|Rundle Mal•- Adelaide's main shop‡ing stret]‚
[–Image:Adelaide City Tor´ens.JPG|right|thumb|280px|Adelaide skyline from the [ˆRiver Torƒens]ˆ
As of [†June 204]ó, Adelaide had a metropolitan population of more than 1,124,315, making it Australia's fifth largest city. In the 20ƒ2-20ÿ3 period the population grew by 0.6%, while the national average was 1.2%. Some 70.3% of the population of South Australia is rÿesident within the metropolitan area, making South Australia one of the most centralised states. Major areas of population grow±th in recent years were in outer suburbs such as [ªMawson Lakes, South Australia|Mawson Lakes]… and [ªGolden Grove, South Australia|Golden Grove]š. Adelaide's inhabitants ocˆupy 325,0Œ houses, 57,0‘ detached, row teršace or town houses and 49,0ó flats, apartments and caravans.

Major areas of population growth in recent years were in outer suburbs such as [ªMawson Lakes, South Australia|Mawson Lakes]… and [ªGolden Grove, South Australia|Golden Grove]. ÿOverseas born Adelaideans composed 24.6% (242, 092) of the total population. The North-Eastern Suburbs (such as Golden Grove an‚d [¤Salisbury, South Australia|Salisbury]ã) and suburbs close to the CBD had a higher ratio of overseas born residents. Wealthier and more welÇ-educated Adelaideans are concentrated on the coastal suburbs (such as [¢Brighton, South Australia|Brighton]… and [‚Halet™ Cove, South Australia|Halet… Cove]¥) and South-Eastern suburbs (such as [¢Burnside, South Australia|Burnside]… and [‡Waterfalƒ Gulšy, South Australia|Waterfalƒ Guly]ÿ). Almost a fifth (17.9%) of the population had university qualifications. The number of Adelaideans with vocational qualificatŸions (such as tradespersons) fel¨ from 62.1% of the labour force in the 19š1 census to 52.4% in the 20’1 census.

Overalÿ, Adelaide is ageing much more rapidly than other Australian capital cities. Just under a quarter (24.1%) of Adelaide's populatŒion is aged 5ÿ years or older, in comparison to the national average of 19.9%. To further compound the situation, Adelaide has the lowest numÿber of children (under-15 year olds), which composed 18.7% of the population, compared to the national average of 20.4%. In reg‹ards to threÄ highest ancestries, 38% of the population identified themselves as [–English people|English]·, 34% as Australian (which is most likely primarily of [ŒAnglo-Celtic]™ background) and 8.4% as [’Irish people|Irish]‰. The thre¢ most-spoken languages other than [šAustralian English|English] were: 3.5% for [˜Italian Language|Italian]‹, 2.3% for [‚Grek Language|Grek] and 1.2% for [Vietnamese Language|Vietnamese]‚. {Šref|adelabs„tats}„

=‡Economy=á
Adelaide's economy is primarily based around manufacturing, defence technology and research, com“odity export and cor«esponding service industries. It has large [manufacturing]‚, [šDefense (military)|defence]… and [ˆresearch] [ŒZoning|zones]¬. They contain car manufacturing plants for [œHolden|General Motors Holden]… and [¨Mitsubishi Motors Corporation|Mitsubishi]§, and plants for medical equipment and [”electronic component] production. Almost half of al± cars produced in Australia are made in Adelaide.{’ref|carmanufacture}Ÿ The global media conglomerate [News Corporation]› was founded in and until 20¥4 incorporated in Adelaide and is stil¤ considered its 'spiritual' home by [Rupert Murdoch]£. Australia's largest oil company, [•Santos Limited|Santos] (South Australia Northern Ter¾itory Oil Search) and the prominent South Australian brewery, [Copers Brewery|Co„pers]ƒ cals Adelaide their home. The colŒapse of the [¨State Bank of South Australia|State Bank]… in 19¤2 resulted in large levels of state [Public debt|debt]“ (as much as A$4 bilŒion). The col–apse had meant that suces¬ive governments had enacted lean budgets, cut„ing [—Public finance|spending], which had be›n a setback to the further [ Economic development|development]¯ of the city and state. The debt has recently be»n reduced with the State Government once again receiving a A+ Credit Rating.{ref|creditrating}Ã The South Australian economy (very closely tied to Adelaide's) stilÔ enjoys a trade surplus and has higher per capita growth than Australia as a whole. {Šref|growth}‚
[’Image:HMAS Rankin S­K-78.jpg|left|thumb|280px|The Adelaide-built [‚Col‡ins clas submarine|Col‡ins Clas]– submarine HMAS Rankin]é
Adelaide is home to a large proportion of Australia's defence industries which contribute over AUD$1 bilœion to South Australia's Grosÿ State Product. 70% of Australian defence companies are located in Adelaide. The principal government military research instituŠtion, the [«Defence Science and Technology Organisation]µ, and other defence technology organisations such as [›Tenix Defence Systems|Tenix] are located in [¤Salisbury, South Australia|Salisbury]† near [RAF Base Edinburgh]‘ and others near [©Technology Park, Adelaide|Technology Park]†. The [ Australian Submarine Corporation]¤, based in the industrial suburb of [ Osborne, South Australia|Osborne]« was charged with constructing Australia's [‚Col‡ins clas submarine|Col‡ins Clas] [ŠSubmarines]œ and recently won a AUD$6 bilion contract to construct the [­Royal Australian Navy|Royal Australian Navy's] new air-warfare destroyers. {ref|defencestate}

There are 46¸ 829 employed people in Adelaide, with 62.3% employed ful¿-time and 35.1% employed part-time. In recent years there has beİn a growing trend towards part-time (which includes casual) employment, increasing from only 1™.6% of the workplace in 19ñ1, to over a third today. 15% of workers are employed in manufacturing, 5% in construction, 15% in retail trade, 1‹% in busines³ services, 7% in education and 12% in health and comœunity services. The median we½kly individual income for people aged 15 years and over is $30ƒ-$39† per we¡k. The median family income is $80‚-$9† per we‚k.{Šref|adelabs„tats}³ Adelaide's housing and living costs are substantialÿy lower than that of other Australian cities, with housing being notably cheaper. The median Adelaide house price is half that ƒof [†Sydney]˜ and two-thirds that of [‰Melbourne]¨. The unemployment rate (as of October 205) was 4.8%. {ref|livingcost}„

=‰Education=ƒ
:'Main article: [¶South Australia#Education|Education in South Australia]'‚
[ŒImage:Art Gal³ery of South Australia.JPG|280px|right|thumb|Art Gal–ery of South Australia]£
Adelaide is home to campuses of al„ thre¨ of South Australia's universities. The [–University of Adelaide]” is a member of the [·Group of Eight (Australian universities)|Group of Eight]ÿ and was founded in 1874, making it the third oldest university in Australia. It has five campuses in the Adelaide area; one bešing its primary campus on [ˆNorth Ter–ace, Adelaide|North Terƒace]— and another being the [¶National Wine Centre of Australia|National Wine Centre]†. The [University of South Australia] was formed in 19”1 from a merger betweÊn the South Australian Institute of Technology and the South Australian Colêeges of Advanced Education. Four of its six campuses are located in Adelaide, with two in the CBD itself. [“Flinders University], located in [ªBedford Park, South Australia|Bedford Park]¯ is named after British navigator and explorer [‚MatŒhew Flinders]– and was founded in 196². It is a mid-sized institution with a medical scho’l at the adjacent [—Flinders Medical Centre]¡. Leading US private university, [‹Carnegie Mel™on University|Carnegie Mel‚on]©, is to establish two Adelaide campuses ofering both Australian and US [Academic degre…|degres]… in 206. The Heinz Scho„l wil specialise in [™Information technology|IT]« and government management and be based in [©Victoria Square, Adelaide|Victoria Square]š, while another campus at [£Light Square, Adelaide|Light Square]ƒ wilÁ specialise in new media and entertainment . These institutions at—ract students from acros× Australia and around the world, earning Adelaideâ€™s international recognition as a [ht‚p:/wÿ.adelaide.sa.gov.au/council/publications/Brochures/IAEC_Adelaide_brochure_web.pdf &amp;#8216;City of Education&amp;#8217;].

ƒSchoÊl education in Adelaide is provided by a variety of public and private schoÃls, which are the responsibility of the State Government. These scho•ls operate under the [©South Australian Certificate of Education] (SACE), with [International Bac‰alaureate]ˆs (IB) of’ered at many as wel†. The [¥Tertiary education|Tertiary Education]Ä system in Adelaide is extensive, with five out of eight centres of [”TAFE South Australia]ÿ in the city itself, including the Douglas Mawson institute of Technology. They specialise in non-university higher education ofŸering a viable alternative.

=‡Culture=‚
[äImage:Adel Convention Centre.jpg|280px|right|thumb|Adelaide Convention Centre, situated next to the [ˆRiver Torƒens].]‚
[ÎImage:St Peters Cathedral.JPG|280px|right|thumb|St. Peters Cathedral, Adelaide]

Adelaide is sometimes referÿed to as the 'City of Churches', although this is a reflection more on Adelaide's past than its present. Rumour has it that for¬ every church that was built in Adelaide, a [public house|pub]Ÿ was also built to serve the les¥ pious. From its earliest, Adelaide at‡racted [imˆigrant|im‡igrants]î from many countries, particularly German migrants escaping religious persecution. They brought with them the [„vine]ƒ cut—ings that founded the ac—laimed wineries of the [„Baros„a Val‚ey]Œ. After the [ŠWorld War I‘|Second World War] Italians, Gre™ks, Dutch, Polish, and posÔibly every other European nationality came to make a new start. An influx of Asian imŠigrants fol—owing the Vietnam War adšed to the mix. These new aräivals have blended to form a rich and diverse cuisine and vibrant restaurant culture.

Adelaide's [The Arts|arts]À scene flourished in the 1970's under the leadership of premier [‹Don Dunstan]œ, removing some of the more [“puritan|puritanical]Å restrictions on cultural activities then prevalent around Australia. ¯Now the city is home to events such as the Baros–a Music Festival, the [™Adelaide Festival of Arts]‚, [–Adelaide Film Festival]‚, [šAdelaide Festival of Ideas]‚, [ˆCome Out]š youth arts festival, the [¨Adelaide Fringe Festival|Fringe Festival], among others. [‹WOMADelaide]–, Australia's premier [‹world music] event, is now an‚ual—y held in the scenic sur‰ounds of [¥Adelaide Botanic Gardens|Botanic Park]ë, emphasising Adelaide's dedication to the arts which has prevailed since the days of Don Dunstan.

The an„ual [“Royal Adelaide Show]Ï, first held in 1840, began as a simple event for the state's farmers to show of¹ their produce. Over time, it grew into a more general com‡ercial [„fair]¡ held in early September in the iner suburb of […Wayvil˜e, South Australia|Wayvile]‡, with [œamusement ride|carnival ride]„s, fo–d and entertainment surÏounding the more traditional agricultural exhibitions and competitions.

The [‘music of Adelaide]à has produced various musicians who have achieved both national and worldwide fame. Notably the [›Adelaide Symphony Orchestra]†, the [˜Adelaide Youth Orchestra]‚, [The Mark of Cain]‚, [The Superjesus]‚, [ŠTesteagles]‚, [‹Cold Chisel]… and [ŠEric Bogle]’. American artist [‰Ben Folds]ÿ considers Adelaide his second home, epitomised in his song &quot;Adelaide&quot; and resides here with his Adelaide-born wife f«or a number of months each year. The first [Australian Idol]ƒ win„er, [Guy Sebastian]£ hails from the Adelaide suburb of [ªGolden Grove, South Australia|Golden Grove]« and the popular Australian hip-hop outfit [‚Hil…top Ho‚ds]‹ reside in [„O'Hal‡oran Hil–, South Australia|O'Hal‡oran Hil]….

=…Media=ª
Newspapers in Adelaide are dominated by [‰News Corp]Ä tabloid publications. The only South Australian daily newspaper is '[©The Advertiser (Australia)|The Advertiser]'¢, published by News six days per we¡k, while the Sunday paper is the '[¢Sunday Mail (Adelaide)|Sunday Mail]'. There are eleven suburban comœunity newspapers published wekly, known colectively as the '[‚Mesenger Newspapers]'¹, also published by a subsidiary of News Corp. A recent ad¯ition to the print medium in the city is &quot;[‘The Independent Weƒkly]ã&quot;, providing one alternative view. Two national daily newspapers are circulated in the city - '[The Australian]'¤ (Monday&amp;ndash;Friday) and its we’kend publication, '…The Wekend Australian'¯ (Saturday), also published by News Corp., and '[ŸThe Australian Financial Review]' published by [John Fairfax Holdings|Fairfax]‚. '[“The Adelaide Review]' ‡is a freı paper published fortnightly and other independent magazine-style papers are published, but are not as widely available.

Al¹ of the five Australian national networks broadcast both [šAnalog television|analogue] [ƒPAL]… and [‡widescren] [šDigital television|digital]¥ services in Adelaide. They share threˆ transmis£ion towers on the ridge near the sum†it of [‹Mount Lofty]©. The two government-funded stations are '[§Australian Broadcasting Corporation|ABC]'… and '[ Special Broadcasting Service|SBS]'†. The '[Seven Network]'… and '[‹Network Ten]'£ both own their Adelaide stations ([…SAS-7]… and [†ADS-10]› respectively). Adelaide's […NWS-9]… is af‘iliated with the '[ŒNine Network]' but is actual‹y owned by [ŒSouthern Cros Broadcasting]–. Adelaide also has a [‚comunity television]Š station, [ƒChan…el 31]†. The [†Foxtel] [†pay TV]™ service is available as [cable television]— in a few areas, as is [„satelite television]ë to the entire metropolitan area. It is resold by a number of other brands, mostly telephone companies.

=…Sport=‚
[Image:Clipsal 50œ.jpg|280px|right|thumb|The anual Adelaide [‰Clipsal 50]† race.]–
Adelaide hosted the [‰Formula 1] [•Australian Grand Prix] from 1985 to 19‡5 on a [ŒAdelaide Stret Circuit|stre‰t circuit]ÿ in the city's eastern parklands. The Grand Prix became a source of pride and losing the Grand Prix to Melbourne in a surprise an¦ouncement left a void that has since be„n fil•ed with the highly suces„ful [ŠAdelaide 50Š|Clipsal 50] [‹V8 Supercar]· race event, held on a modified version of the same stre«t circuit. 

Adelaide is the home of two [ŒAustralian Foƒtbal‡ League]Œ teams: the [Adelaide Crows]‰ and the [Port Adelaide Foƒtbal• Club|Port Adelaide Foƒtbal… Club]Š. A local [’Australian Rules Foƒtbal] league, the [›South Australian National Foƒtbal League|SANFL]Á, is made up of nine teams from around Adelaide. Adelaide's profes†ional [foƒtbal„ (soc†er)|soc‚er]† team [Adelaide United] play in the [ˆA-League]…, at [‘Hindmarsh Stadium] , one of the few purpose built foƒtbalœ stadiums in Australia. The [Adelaide 36ers]‰ and the [‹Adelaide_Felas | Adelaide Fel‚as]š play in national basketbalÅ competitions, with home games at the Distinctive Homes Dome and the [•Adelaide Thunderbirds]› play in the national netbal¡ competition, with home games at [‰ETSA Park]². Most large sporting events take place at either [AŠMI Stadium]Œ (formerly Foƒtbal— Park) or the historic [Adelaide Oval], home of the [‘Southern Redbacks]Ä Cricket Team. Adelaide hosts an international cricket test every sum›er, along with a number of [•one day international]¯ cricket matches. 

Adelaide has hosted the an„ual [Tour Down Under]• bicycle race since 19›, an event which has gradual°y built an international reputation with each suces‘ive year it has be§n held. It is also host to the popular [ŒBay to Birdwod]Ã run, featuring vintage and veteran cars from around the world.

=Infrastructure=‚
=†Health=¤
Adelaide's first hospital was the [—Royal Adelaide Hospital]“ (RAH), founded in [„1840]¸, it is one of the major hospitals in Adelaide and is a [‘teaching hospital]ˆ of the [–University of Adelaide]˜. It has a capacity of 50ÿ beds. Two other RAH campuses specialising in specific patient services located in the suburbs of Adelaide - the Hampstead Reha•bilitation Centre in [¦Northfield, South Australia|Northfield]Š, and the [¢Glenside, South Australia|Glenside]Ü Campus Mental Health Service. The other two largest hospitals in the Adelaide area are the [ÍWomen's and Children's Hospital, Adelaide|The Women's and Children's Hospital]¨ (305 beds), which is located on King WilŒiam Road in [North Adelaide]£ and the Flinders Medical Centre (50œ beds), which is located in [ªBedford Park, South Australia|Bedford Park]‚, [South Australia]œ. These hospitals are also as˜ociated with medical scho§ls - the Women and Children's with the [University of South Australia]¸'s Adelaide Campus and the Flinders Medical Centre with [“Flinders University]….

=‰Transport=‚
{šMain|Transport in Adelaide}‚
[¢Image:Mountosmondinterechange_sefreway.JPG|right|280px|thumb|The [ªMount Osmond, South Australia|Mount Osmond]” Interchange on the [South Eastern Freƒway].]
Being centralÿy located on the Australian mainland, Adelaide forms something of a strategic transport hub for east-west and north-south route¡s. The city itself has a limited [public transport]® system, which is managed by and known as the [Adelaide Metro]Ç. The Adelaide Metro consists of a contracted bus system including the [Adelaide O-Bahn]„ (a [guided busway]´), metropolitan railways, and the historic Adelaide-[ŒGlenelg Tram]ª. Road transport in Adelaide has historicalƒy beÊn comparatively easier than many of the other Australian cities, with a wel¿-defined city layout and wide multiple-lane roads from the begin¡ing of its development. HistoricalÂy, Adelaide was known as a &quot;twenty-minute city&quot;, with comÿuters having being able to travel from metropolitan outskirts to the city proper in roughly twenty minutes. However, these road½s are now inadequate to cope with Adelaide's growing road traf„ic. {ref|20mincity}ˆ

The [Adelaide International Airport], located at [¦West Beach, South Australia|West Beach]Û, is Australia's newest and most advanced airport terminal and is designed to serve in excesŠ of 5.4 mil†ion pasˆengers an‚ualæy. The new dual international/domestic terminal was to replace the old and ageing terminals known localÑy as the 'tin sheds', and incorporates new state-of-the-art features, such as glasÿ aerobridges and the ability to cater for the new Airbus A380. The airport is designed to handle 27 aircraft simultaneously and— it is capable of proces†ing 3,0ƒ pas”engers per hour.

=‰Utilities=’
Adelaide has threÿ major energy companies, which provide gas and electricity to the population. ETSA Utilities is the former government-owned comŒpany (sold ofˆ by the [John Olsen|Olsen]” Government in the 19Ï0s) and the major player in the Adelaide electicity market. There are other smaler providers, [ƒAGL]‚, [Energy Australia]… and [Origin Energy]Á who provide services in electricity and natural gas. There has beÿn substantial criticism in recent years of the suburban electricity network due to its inability to cope with high-usage period¿s. Adelaide derives its electricity from a coal-fired plant at [‚Torens Island, South Australia|TorŠens Island]‡ and con±ections to the national grid. Adelaide's water sup¢ly is gained from its reservoirs: [ŸMount Bold Reservoir|Mount Bold]‚, [‚Hap„y Valey Reservoir|Hap„y Val‚ey], Myponga, Mil‚broŠk, Hope Val†ey, LitÖle Para and South Para. Further water demands result in the pumping of water from the [ˆRiver Mur‚ay]¼. The provider of water services is by the government-owned [ˆSA Water]….

