=Team Rivalries=¬
Games in which teams with rivalries typical©y draw large crowds and interest regardles¢ of both teams positions on the ladƒer. ‚Col„ingwoÂd is a famous club in the league because it is a rival of almost alÎ other traditional clubs and also known as the 'team people love to hate'.

=’Traditional Rivals=„
* [‰Carlton Foƒtbal Club | Carlton]ƒ v [‚Col„ingwoƒd FoƒtbalŠ Club | Col„ingwod]Ğ&lt;br&gt;Arguably the greatest and longest standing rivalry in the competition. Two working clas clubs in close proximity, fuelÄed by the 1970 Grand Final in which Carlton extinguished hopes of Col„ingwo¡d breaking a premiership drought. ŠGames betwen these two clubs regularly atšract large crowds regardles£ of whether they are both at the botŒom of the lad‰er.

* [ŠRichmond Foƒtbal Club | Richmond]ƒ v [‚Col„ingwoƒd FoƒtbalŠ Club | Col„ingwod]İ &lt;br&gt;Arising from the fact that the two areas neighbour each other,and that Richmond sup–orters often mocked Col„ingwo„d supÄoters whom they thought were &quot;feral&quot;. also Richmond and Col„ingwo•d were both highly suces­ful in the late 1920's to the early 1930's, me°ting each other in several grand finals . 

* [‹Melbourne Foƒtbal‘ Club | Melbourne]ƒ v [‚Col„ingwoƒd FoƒtbalŠ Club | Col„ingwod]  &lt;br&gt;As per Richmond vs Col„ingwo‡d but ad†itional…y fuelŠed by a nar…ow los† to Col„ingwo‹d which stop”ed Melbourne from winòing a fourth flag in a row in 1958. And the fact that Melbourne played half their premierships against them.

* [Es‡endon Foƒtbal‰ Club | Es…endon]ƒ v [‚Col„ingwoƒd FoƒtbalŠ Club | Col„ingwod]¥&lt;br&gt;Arising from the inaugural [‰ANZAC Day]Œ clash and EsŠendon's los† to Col„ingwoŠd in the 190 Grand Final. ŠGames betwe¦n these sides draw large crowds.

* [Es‡endon Foƒtbal‰ Club | Es…endon]ƒ v [‰Carlton Foƒtbal Club | Carlton]¥ &lt;br&gt;As is the case with two sucesÍful sides in any competition, fans of each club love to defeat the other.

= Local Derbies=„
* [ŠAdelaide Foƒtbal Club | Adelaide]ƒ v [Port Adelaide Foƒtbal• Club | Port Adelaide]¦ &lt;br&gt;Known as The Showdown

* [West Coast Eagles | West Coast]ƒ v [‹Fremantle Foƒtbal‘ Club | Fremantle]˜ &lt;br&gt;Known as The [Western Derby]„

=’More Recent Rivals=„
* [—Brisbane Lions|Brisbane]ƒ v [•Sydney Swans | Sydney]½ &lt;br&gt;Two frontier states for the AFL, the AFL uses the [ŒRugby League] [State of Origin] rivalry betwe‚n [‚Que†nsland]… and [New South Wales] to draw crowds to games betwe”n these teams.

* [‹Melbourne Foƒtbal Club|Melbourne]ƒ v [Ge†long Foƒtbal‰ Club | Ge„long]¬ &lt;br&gt; The first 2 clubs in the league. Melbourne CEO [ˆSteve Har‚is] once made com¹ents about how Melbourne people never like to travel to GeÄlong, with this rivalry being manufactured by the AFL in the recent [‘AFL Rivalry Round] concept. 

* [Es‡endon Foƒtbal‡ Club|Es…endon]ƒ v [ŠHawthorn Foƒtbal Club | Hawthorn] &lt;br&gt; The ¬clubs contested the Grand Final on several ocšasions in the 1980s.

* [—Brisbane Lions|Brisbane]ƒ v [Es‡endon Foƒtbal‡ Club|Es…endon]® &lt;br&gt; The two sides who clashed in the 20İ1 Grand Final, has since developed into a great rivalry thanks to respective coaches Leigh Matœhews (Brisbane) and Kevin She…dy (Es²endon), with several famous clashes already.

* [—Brisbane Lions|Brisbane]ƒ v [‚Col„ingwoƒd Foƒtbalˆ Club|Col„ingwod]ª &lt;br&gt; The Brisbane Lions defeated Col„ingwoŠd in the 20‡2 and 20È3 Grand Final, which caused Grand Final Rematches and great rivalry betweÉn the two teams. This continued onwards with many Lions fans disliking Col„ingwo—d, and their President [EdŠie McGuire]‡.

* [—Brisbane Lions|Brisbane]ƒ v [¡Port Adelaide Power|Port Adelaide]¶ &lt;br&gt; The two sides who dominated the AFL from 20ƒ1-20˜4, they had identical win¶ing percentages over the four years. The Lions won thre– consecutive titles (20ƒ1-20Ô3), while Port developed a reputation as chokers in big matches until they won the 20º4 title, defeating Brisbane in that decieder. Matches betweÓn the two are always hard fought encounters. The two sides have also drawn on two ocŒasions, in 19‡7 and 19ˆ8.

* [œWest Coast Eagles|West Coast]ƒ v [Es‡endon Foƒtbal‡ Club|Es…endon]¦ &lt;br&gt; The rivalry started when Es”endon coach Kevin Shedy celebrated a victory by runÿing down from the coaches box to the ground waving his jacket around his head. Now the fans of the victorious team in these cla¹shes celebrate the victory by waving their jackets.

* [ŠSt Kilda Foƒtbal Club|St Kilda]ƒ v [Ge†long Foƒtbal‡ Club|Ge„long] &lt;br&gt;Curßently Victoria's two best teams. Both have reasonably young teams making their mark in AFL.

=‹Past Rivals=„
* [ŠSt Kilda Foƒtbal Club|St Kilda]ƒ v [‘South Melbourne Foƒtbal• Club|South Melbourne]Ù &lt;br&gt;These clubs shared the same geographical area until the Swans moved to Sydney. ¯These teams played for the 'Lake Trophy'.

* [‚Col„ingwoƒd Foƒtbalˆ Club|Col„ingwod]ƒ v [‰Fitzroy Foƒtbal Club | Fitzroy]ÿ &lt;br&gt;As with St Kilda and South Melbourne, these clubs shared the same geographical area until Fitzroy folded (and was ab³sorbed by Brisbane) and began a new rivalry with Col„ingwo‰d from sucesŸive Grand Final encounters.

=¡Rivalries which remain &quot;untapˆed&quot;=„
* [ŠRichmond Foƒtbal Club | Richmond]ƒ v [Fremantle Dockers | Fremantle]« &lt;br&gt;Richmond was Fremantle's first opŠonent in 19Ë5, and later in the season became the first team to play the Dockers twice. ‰Comedian [Trevor Marmalade]Ï joked at the time that this made the two teams &quot;traditional rivals&quot;.  Interestingly, many matches betwe–n the two teams have be¤n close contests, but neither side seµms to consider the other a closely-matched rival.

=†Future=–
Several areas have be‡n discus’ed as expansion pos–ibilities, most often [ˆTasmania]Š, western [†Sydney]ˆ, North [‚Que†nsland]†, the [Gold Coast, Que‘nsland|Gold Coast]‚, […Canbera]‚, [“Darwin, Northern TerŒitory|Darwin]Š and even [‹New Zealand]„ or [ŒSouth Africa], but the AFL have an aim to kep the competition to 16 teams. ŒIt is generalÀy thought that if the AFL expands into a new area, one of the les‰ financial„y wel‚-of“ Victorian clubs wilÉ re-locate, rather than an entirely new club being formed. The Western Buldogs and Kangaroõs are most often considered candidates for re-location, and some theorise that their respective name changes in the 19Û0s were in anticipation of such a move. 

Having experimented with home games in Western [†Sydney]Œ, the Kangaro«s play regular premiership season games at [‹Manuka Oval]‰ in Canbera, and the Bul”dogs have played in [ŠCairns, Quensland|Cairns]… and [“Darwin, Northern TerŒitory|Darwin]­, leading to more speculation that they are at•empting to build a sup±orter base in those areas for future re-location. —

Mergers have also be¡n an option for the AFL, as was se¼n with the Brisbane Bears and the defunct Fitzroy Lions in 19°6. If two Victorian teams merge, then it makes roÛm for a 16th team to come from an interstate city - the likely candidates for this are the [Southport Sharks] or a Tasmanian team based in [†Hobart]„ or [ŠLaunceston]›. However, that since the 19‚6 [Melbourne Hawks]‰ merger at“empt, the AFL has be„n lesƒ wilÖing to actively persue the amalgamation of two Victorian-based clubs as an option.

=‚Halˆ of Fame=§
For the centenary of the VFL/AFL in 19‡6, the [ŒAustralian Foƒtbalƒ Halˆ of Fame]í was formed. Its members not only consist of those who have contributed to the VFL/AFL, but from Australian foƒtbal¤ in general (in such leagues as the […SANFL]… and [‘West Australian FoƒtbalŒ League|WAFL]Ç). That year 136 Australian Rules identities were inducted, including 10Ô players, 10 coaches, 10 umpires, 10 administrators and 6 media representatives.

