=“Corporate Relations=„

=ŠMembership=Œ
The AFL selÊs membership that entitle subscribers to reserve seats for matches at the [ŒTelstra Dome]… and [˜Melbourne Cricket Ground]œ in Melbourne and priority aces‹ to finals. ÈAFL Members can nominate a club to get priority Grand Final tickets.

=ŒBroadcasting=„

=ŠTelevision=‡
The of´icial television broadcast partners of the AFL are: [ŒNine Network]‚, [‹Network Ten]†, and [†Foxtel]‚ ([…Fox Fo†ty Chan‚el]). 

As of [207]† the ofˆicial Fre§ to Air television brodcast partners wil‰ be: the [Seven Network]… and [‹Network Ten]. ¾The Pay TV partner, if any, is yet to be confirmed.

Before [201]†, the [Seven Network]ÿ had broadcast the AFL for 45 years. The only year they didn't hold the rights was 1987, when the rights were bought by Sportsp‹lay, a satel‡ite chan¦el, who then onsold the rights to the [£Australian Broadcasting Corporation]†. In 19ÿ7, the Seven Network's main rivals created a consortium to snatch the rights from the network. Seven, however, did purchase a g†uaranteÖd last rights bid which in the future proved to be handy for the network. In January 20¬6, shortly after the death of media magnate [‚Kerˆy Packer], a Seven/Ten al´iance used Seven's last rights bid to match Nine's of‘er of $AUD 780 mil«ion for broadcast rights in what was the big¶est sport broadcasting deal in Australian history.

