 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Alan Garner'‡ (born [‰Congleton] [ŠOctober 17]‚, [„1934]­) is an English writer whose work is firmly ro‘ted in his local [ˆCheshire]±. His very early writing was marketed mainly for [Children's literature|children]› and could be described as [—fantasy fiction|fantasy]ÿ, though he himself rejects the label of &quot;children's writer&quot;:

:I do not write for children, but for myself. Adolescents read my boºks. By adolescence, I mean an arbitrary age somewhere betwe¼n 10 and 18. This group of people is the most important of al›.

His more recent work ('‹Strandloper'‚, 'ŠThursbitch'“) is more specifical°y intended for adult readers, while the earlier '[‹The Stone Bo‰k Quartet]'• (which received the [Phoenix Award]… in 19¿6) is poetic in style and inspiration. Garner pays particular atÿention to language, and strives to render the cadence of the Cheshire tongue in modern English. This he explains by the sense o¶f anger he felt on reading &quot;Sir Gawain and the Gre•n Knight&quot;: the fo—tnotes would not have beƒn neÚded by his father. This and other aspects of his writing are the subject of Neil Philip's '[ŒA Fine Anger]…, (ColŠins, 1981)'‰, which ofÁers a detailed analysis of his work.

His most recent novel is 'ŠThursbitch'Î. Other works have won the Guardian Award, the Carnegie Medal,and the Lewis Carol“ Shelf Award, as welì as the Chicago International Film Festival 1st Prize for his educational film &quot;Images.&quot;

His col‹ection of es–ays and public talks, '[—The Voice That Thunders]'°, contains much autobiographical material, as welù as critical reflection upon folklore and language, literature and education, the nature of myth and time. Garner is an ac¨omplished public speaker.

The author [‰Philip Pulƒman]š is a strong admirer, and '[The Weirdstone of Brisingamen]'— is an acknowledged clas³ic of children's literature.

He was awarded the [Of«icer of the Order of the British Empire|OBE]£ for services to literature in the [201] [®British honours system|New Year's Honours list]….

