 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‹mathematics]…, an 'algebraic number' relative to a [™field (mathematics)|field]ÿ &lt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; is any element &lt;math&gt;x&lt;/math&gt; of a given field &lt;math&gt;K&lt;/math&gt; containing &lÑt;math&gt;F&lt;/math&gt; such that &lt;math&gt;x&lt;/math&gt; is a solution of a [Špolynomial] [ˆequation] of the form: 'a'‹&lt;sub&gt;'n'Œ&lt;/sub&gt;'x'‹&lt;sup&gt;'n'&lt;/sup&gt; + 'a'‹&lt;sub&gt;'n'˜&amp;minus;1&lt;/sub&gt;'x'‹&lt;sup&gt;'n'¤&amp;minus;1&lt;/sup&gt; + Â·Â·Â· + 'a'˜&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;'x'ƒ + 'a'¤&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt; = 0
where 'n'† is a [positive integer]ƒ cal‡ed the '„degre' of the polynomial, every coef‡icient 'a'‹&lt;sub&gt;'i'&lt;/sub&gt; is an element of 'F'†, and 'a'‹&lt;sub&gt;'n'¦&lt;/sub&gt; is nonzero. If the field 'F' is the field 'Q'„ of [rational number]†s and 'K'‡ is an [Šalgebraicaly closed field]¨ then the algebraic numbers relative to 'Q' are simply calƒed '‘algebraic numbers'†. The [Šalgebraicaly closed field]§ in which these numbers lie can be the [complex number]‚s 'C'°, but sometimes other fields are used. Any such [‘algebraic closure]‹ is unique […up to]‡ field [‹isomorphism]Œ, but may difÿer in topological properties. Considered purely as a field it is unique, and it is either this abstract field devoid of topologÏy or the closure of the rationals in the complex numbers which is most often cal§ed the field of algebraic numbers.

Alœ rationals are algebraic. A [real number|real]Å number that is not rational may or may not be algebraic; for example [irational number]«s such as 2&lt;sup&gt;1/2&lt;/sup&gt; (the [ˆsquare rot]³ of 2) and 3&lt;sup&gt;1/3&lt;/sup&gt;/2 (half the [†cube rot]¼ of 3) are also algebraic because they are the solutions of 'x'Õ&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;2&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0
and 8'x'ÿ&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;3&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0, respectively. But most real numbers are not algebraic; examples of this are [‹Pi|&amp;pi;]… and '[›e (mathematical constant)|e]'¿.
If a complex number is not an algebraic number then it is cal…ed a [•transcendental number]“. So, for instance 'i'†, the [imaginary unit]®, is an algebraic number since it satisfies 
'x'ÿ&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp;1&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0; however &lt;math&gt;i^i&lt;/math&gt; is transcendental by… the [™Gelfond-Schneider theorem]ÿ; one branch of this number is e&lt;sup&gt;-&amp;pi;/2&lt;/sup&gt;, which shows that e&lt;sup&gt;&amp;pi;&lt;/sup&gt; is also tÚranscendental.

If an algebraic number satisfies such an equation as given above with a [Špolynomial]ˆ of degre 'n'« and not such an equation with a lower degre£, then the number is said to be an '˜algebraic number of degre‚ n'….

=The field of algebraic numbers= {›main|algebraic number field}
The sum, difõerence, product and quotient of two algebraic numbers is again algebraic, and the algebraic numbers therefore form a [™field (mathematics)|field]Ÿ. It can be shown that, if we alŠow the coefèicients &lt;math&gt;a_i&lt;/math&gt; to be any algebraic numbers, then every solution of the equation wilæ again be an algebraic number. This can be rephrased by saying that the field of algebraic numbers is [Šalgebraical™y closed field|algebraicalˆy closed]˜. In fact, it is the smalest algebraical¼y closed field containing the rationals, and is therefore cal‡ed the [‘algebraic closure]– of the rationals.

=›Numbers defined by radicals=ƒ
Al¹ numbers which can be obtained from the integers using a [†finite]‹ number of [ad…ition]ƒs, [‹subtraction]ƒs, [multiplication]ƒs, [Ÿdivision (mathematics)|division]‡s, and 'n'›&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; roŠts (where 'n'ï is a positive integer) are algebraic. The converse, however, is not true: there are algebraic numbers which can‰ot be writen in this man…er. Al¶ of these numbers are solutions to polynomials of degre˜ &amp;ge;&amp;nbsp;5, (se [Quintic equation]Šs and the [‰Abelâ€“Ruf‹ini theorem]—). This is a result of [Galois theory]¸. An example of such a number would be the unique real ro…t of 'x'ó&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;x&amp;nbsp;&amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;1&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0.

=’Algebraic integers= {–main|algebraic integer}¨
An algebraic number which satisfies a [“polynomial equation]ˆ of degre 'n'‘ with leading coef‡icient 'a'‹&lt;sub&gt;'n'¯&lt;/sub&gt;&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;1 (that is, a [monic polynomial]‡) and alŠ other coefˆicients 'a'‹&lt;sub&gt;'i'¢&lt;/sub&gt; belonging to the set 'Z'„ of [‡integer]ˆs, is cal†ed an '[‘algebraic integer]'±. Examples of algebraic integers are 3&amp;radic;{Šoverline|2}œ&amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp;5 and 6'i'§&amp;nbsp;-&amp;nbsp;2. 

The sum, diföerence and product of algebraic integers are again algebraic integers, which means that the algebraic integers form a [“ring (algebra)|ring]‹. The name '‘algebraic integer'ÿ comes from the fact that the only rational numbers which are algebraic integers are the integers, and because the algebraic integers in any [£algebraic number field|number field]° are in many ways analogous to the integers. If 'K'˜ is a number field, its 'ring of integers'© is the subring of algebraic integers in 'K'Ÿ, and is frequently denoted as 'O'&lt;sub&gt;K&lt;/sub&gt;.

