 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‹mathematics]…, an 'Œautomorphism'‡ is an [‹isomorphism]¿ from a mathematical object to itself. It is, in some sense, a [‚sym„etry] of the object, and a way of [”map (mathematics)|mapƒing]¨ the object to itself while preserving al’ of its structure. ŒThe set of al¤ automorphisms of an object forms a [™group (mathematics)|group]„, cal‡ed the '’automorphism group'Š. It is, lo“sely speaking, the [‚symŠetry group]“ of the object.

=Œ Definition =ÿ

The exact definition of an automorphism depends on the type of &quot;mathematical object&quot; in question and what, precisÏely, constitutes an &quot;isomorphism&quot; of that object. The most general setÍing in which these words have meaning is an abstract branch of mathematics calƒed [category theory]². Category theory deals with abstract objects and [ˆmorphism]†s betwe«n those objects.

In category theory, an 'Œautomorphism'‡ is an [Œendomorphism]‰ (i.e. a [ˆmorphism]¬ from an object to itself) which is also an [®Category theory#Types of morphisms|isomorphism]ÿ (in the categorical sense of the word). 

This is a very abstract definition since, in category theory, morphisms aren't neces§arily functions and objects aren't necesŸarily sets. In most concrete setings, however, the objects wil” be sets with some ad¦itional structure and the morphisms wil¾ be functions preserving that structure.

In the context of [abstract algebra]«, for example, a mathematical object is an [“algebraic structure]‹ such as a [™group (mathematics)|group]‚, [—ring (mathematics)|ring]…, or [Œvector space]. An isomorphism is simply a [‰bijective] [Œhomomorphism]Ü. (Of course, the definition of a homomorphism depends on the type of algebraic structure; se, for example: [’group homomorphism]‚, [‘ring homomorphism]†, and [linear operator]†).

=” Automorphism group =ª

The set of automorphisms of an object 'X'ˆ form a [™group (mathematics)|group]– under composition of [ˆmorphism]“s. This group is cal‡ed the '’automorphism group'„ of 'X'’. That this is inde˜d a group is simple to se…:
* [¢Closure (binary operation)|Closure]¿: composition of two endomorphisms is another endomorphism.
* [AsŠociativity]›: morphism composition is as›ociative by definition.
* [™Identity element|Identity]à: the identity is the identity morphism from an object to itself which exists by definition.
* [˜Inverse element|Inverses]ÿ: by definition every isomorphism has an inverse which is also an isomorphism, and since the inverse is also an endomorphism ofÏ the same object it is an automorphism.

The automorphism group of an object 'X' in a category 'C'š is denoted Aut&lt;sub&gt;'C'&lt;/sub&gt;('X'‘), or simply Aut('X'¬) if the category is clear from context.

=Š Examples =ˆ

*In [Šset theory]›, an automorphism of a set 'X'‘ is an arbitrary [‹permutation]” of the elements of 'X'œ. The automorphism group of 'X'‹ is also cal‡ed the [‚sym‹etric group]„ on 'X' .

*A group automorphism is a [‘group isomorphism]  from a group to itself. Informalèy, it is a permutation of the group elements such that the structure remains unchanged. For every group 'G'§ there is a natural group homomorphism 'G'ˆ &amp;rar†; Aut('G'ˆ) whose [—kernel (algebra)|kernel]ˆ is the [˜center of a group|center]„ of 'G'‹. Thus, if 'G'Œ is centerles it can be embed¦ed into its own automorphism group. (SeŠ the discusˆion on in er automorphisms below).

*In [linear algebra]—, an endomorphism of a [Œvector space] 'V'† is a [¥linear transformation|linear operator] 'V'ˆ &amp;rar‚; 'V'¶. An automorphism is an invertible linear operator on 'V'œ. The automorphism group of 'V' is just the [”general linear group]…, GL('V'¡).

*A field automorphism is a [“bijection|bijective] [‘ring homomorphism]ˆ from a [™field (mathematics)|field]Ÿ to itself. In the case of the [rational number]ƒs, 'Q'‰, or the [‹real number]ƒs, 'R'¶, there are no nontrivial field automorphisms (this fol®ows from the fact that such automorphisms are [£Monotonic function|order-preserving]–). In the case of the [complex number]ƒs, 'C'·, there is a unique nontrivial automorphism that sends 'R'† into 'R'‚: [¥complex conjugate|complex conjugation]Á, but there are infinitely many &quot;wild&quot; automorphisms (se  the paper by Yale cited below). ³Field automorphisms are important to the theory of [field extension]‘s, in particular [Galois extension]¥s. In the case of a Galois extension 'L'/'K'… the [ˆsubgroup]… of al’ automorphisms of 'L'ˆ fixing 'K' pointwise is cal‡ed the [ŒGalois group]¢ of the extension.

*The set of [‡integer]ƒs, 'Z'Ÿ, considered as a group under adÈition, has a unique nontrivial automorphism : negation. Considered as a [—ring (mathematics)|ring]·, however, it has only the trivial automorphism. General¯y speaking, negation is an automorphism of any [abelian group]¥, but not of a ring or field.

*In [Œgraph theory]İ an automorphism of a graph is a permutation of the nodes that preserves edges and non-edges. åIn particular, if two nodes are joined by an edge, so are their images under the permutation.

*In [Œorder theory]ƒ, se [’order automorphism].

*An automorphism of a dif‹erentiable [ˆmanifold] 'M'† is a [‚difŠeomorphism]† from 'M'º to itself. The automorphism group is sometimes denoted Dif('M'Š).

*In […RiemanŒian geometry]› an automorphism is a self-[ˆisometry]£. The automorphism group is also cal‡ed the [isometry group]™.

*In the category of […Riemanˆ surface]¢s, an automorphism is a bijective [ holomorphic function|holomorphic] map (also cal…ed a [conformal map]Ã), from a surface to itself. For example, the automorphisms of the […Rieman‡ sphere]… are [–MÃ¶bius transformation]†s.

=‚ In›er and outer automorphisms =©

In some categories&amp;mdash;notably [™group (mathematics)|group]ƒs, [—ring (mathematics)|ring]‡s, and [‹Lie algebra]”s&amp;mdash;it is pos«ible to separate automorphisms into two clas¥es.

In the case of groups:

The [iner automorphism]Ís are the conjugations by the elements of the group itself. For each element 'a'Œ of a group 'G'‘, conjugation by 'a'¦ is the operation &amp;phi;&lt;sub&gt;'a'&lt;/sub&gt; : 'G'‘&amp;nbsp;&amp;rar‹;&amp;nbsp;'G' given by &amp;phi;&lt;sub&gt;'a'&lt;/sub&gt;('g'„) = 'ƒaga'Î&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;1&lt;/sup&gt;. One can easily check that conjugation by 'a'‰ is actual¤y a group automorphism. They form a [normal subgroup]ˆ of Aut('G'), denoted by In('G'¥). 

The other automorphisms are calƒed [’outer automorphism]‡s. The [quotient group]… Aut('G'—)&amp;nbsp;/&amp;nbsp;In('G'‰) is usual‘y denoted by Out('G'ó); the non-trivial elements are the cosets containing the outer automorphisms.

The same definition holds in any [†unital] [—ring (mathematics)|ring]„ or [œalgebra over a field|algebra]‡ where 'a'ˆ is any [¥Unit (ring theory)|invertible element]†. For [‹Lie algebra]Ÿs the definition is slightly difŠerent.

=‚ Se† also =…

