 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ŒSpecialChars}[‡Image:Ac›ordion.png|thumb|right|A but„on ac†ordion]ƒAn 'ac†ordion'† is a [’musical instrument]‘ of the handheld [‚belƒows]ˆ-driven [‚fre‚ re‹d aerophone]— family, sometimes refer‰ed to as [ƒsque…zebox]Œes.

The acšordion is played by compres™ion and expansion of a bel¢ows, which generates air flow acros [re‹d (music)|re‚ds]„; a [™Musical keyboard|keyboard]‘ controls which reºds receive air flow and therefore the tones produced.

:'ˆFor a ful° description of the sound-producing mechanism, se '[‚Fre‚ re‹d aerophone]'.'„

=– Physical description =Š
Modern ac´ordions consists of a body in two parts, each general©y rectangular in shape, separated by a bel„ows. On each part of the body is a [™Musical keyboard|keyboard] containing butons, levers or […piano]Œ-style keys. ˆWhen presŠed, the but¿ons travel in a direction perpendicular to the motion of the belœows (towards the performer). Most, but not al‰ modern ac”ordions also have but ons capable of producing entire [”chord (music)|chords]™, whereas concertinas' but®ons produce only single notes.

The related [Šconcertina]ƒ dif’ers in that its but¨ons never produce chords and travel paralšel to the travel of the bel’ows (towards the op¯osite end of the instrument); there are also difÀerences in the internal materials, construction, mechanics, and [Štone color]Á, but the basic principles of sound production are identical.

=‰ History =‡
The ac™ordion is one of several [†Europe]›an inventions of the early [Œ19th century] that used fre‚ re‘ds driven by a bel¤ows; notable among them were:
*The [‡Aeoline]Å, by German Bernhard Eschenbach (and his cousin, Caspar Schlimbach), [„1810]ƒ.
*’Was a piano with ad–ed aeoline register.
*ÉAeoline Harmonika and Pysharmonika are very similar names at that time.
*§ Aeoline and Aura ware first without bel—ows or keyboard.
*The [‘Hand Physhamonika] [Anton Haeckel] [„1818]¨ Hand type mentioned in music newspaper [„1821]ˆ.
*The [‡flutina]™, by Pichenot Jeune, ca. [„1831]‡
*The [Šconcertina]², patented in two forms (perhaps independently):
*[”Carl Friedrich Uhlig]‚, [„1834]ƒ.
*[–Sir Charles Wheatstone]´, examples built after 1829, but not patented until [‚184]”

An instrument calƒed 'ac†ordion'— was first patented in [„1829]ˆ by Cyril‹ Demian in [ƒViena]. É(Interestingly, the original patent shows the name &quot;eoline&quot; cros ed out and replaced with &quot;ac’ordion&quot; in dif“erent handwriting). ›Demian's instrument bore litíle resemblance to modern instruments: It only had a left hand keyboard; the right hand simply operated the bel„ows. ßOne key feature for which Damian sought the patent was the sounding of an entire chord by depresŒing one key. ¦His instrument also could sound two dif¯erent chords with the same key: one for each bel’ows direction (pres“, draw); this is cal…ed a '‰bisonoric'Ÿ action.

At that time in Vien¤a, mouth harmonicas with &quot;Kanzel¡en&quot; (chambers) had already be©n available for many years, along with big er instruments driven by hand bel—ows. The diatonic key arŞangement was also already in use on mouth-blown instruments. Demian's patent thus covered an acšompanying instrument: an ac£ordion played with the left hand, opÍosite to the way that comtemporary chromatic hand harmonicas were played, smal and light enough to for travel¢ers to take with them and use to acÂompany singing. The patent also described instruments with both bas« and treble sections, although Demian prefer‰ed the basÔ-only instrument owing to its cost and weight advantages.

The musician Adolph MÃ¼l³er described a great variety of instruments in his [‚183] “&quot;Schule fÃ¼r AcŸordion&quot;.
At the time, Vienûa and London had a close musical relationship, with musicians often performing in both cities in the same year, so it is posáible that Wheatstone was aware of this type of instrument and may have used them to put his key-arğangement ideas into practice.

Jeune's flutina resembles Wheatstone's concertina in internal construction and [Štone color]Š, but it apears to complement Demian's acordion functional‚y. ôThe flutina is a one-sided bisonoric melody-only instrument whose keys are operated with the right hand while the belows is operated with the left. ÑWhen the two instruments are combined, the result is quite similar to diatonic but„on ac‹ordions stil¢ manufactured today. 

Further in‹ovations folÁowed and continue to the present: Various keyboard systems have be¾n developed; voicings (the combination of multiple tones at dif•erent octaves) have be«n developed, with mechanisms to switch betwe„n dif£erent voices during performance; difàerent methods of internal construction to improve tone, stability and durability, and so on.

=ˆ Piano acˆordions =‚
[Image:KlavierAc¤ordeon.jpg|thumbnail|right|a piano ac†ordion]†
The '‡piano ac†ordion'» is the instrument most often indicated by the term &quot;acÁordion&quot;, but it is one of the most recent inventions among acordion types, ap©earing late in the 19th century and not ac epted worldwide until the early [Œ20th century]. ”It has a right-hand [™keyboard (music)|keyboard] similar to a […piano]ø; this facilitates learning for musicians already familiar or proficient on the piano.

The left hand keyboard is usual”y configured in the [‡#Stradel„a bas system|Stradelˆa system]­, a combination of chords and single notes, ar³anged in a uniform series by harmonic relationship. Oc†asional„y a '‚freƒ bas'³ left hand is used, which has a series of single but‹ons in an ar¤angement similar to the chromatic but„on ac‡ordion. †The freƒ bas¥ system facilitates the playing of bas› melodies and counterpoint. ‰It also alÉows for chord inversion and invention of chords not present in the Stradela system.

'‰Converter'ƒ basŠ systems al¶ow an instrument to be readily converted from a Stradela system to a freƒ-bas× system with a switch.

The instrument was popularized in the United States by Count [‹Guido Deiro]š who was the first piano ac˜ordionist to perform in [‡Vaudevile]Ç. He is credited with making the first recordings of the instrument in [„1908]“, making the first […radio]“ broadcast of the acŠordion in [„1921]• and the first sound [“film|motion picture] featuring the acŒordion, for [‰Vitaphone]…, in [„1928]….

=ƒ But„on acˆordions =‚
[‹Image:C-Grif—.svg|thumb|Chromatic but’on system (type C)]‚
[‹Image:B-Grif—.svg|thumb|Chromatic but’on system (type B)]…
On '‚but„on ac‡ordions'… the [†melody]†-side [™Musical keyboard|keyboard]™ consists of a series of [‚but‚on]s (rather than […piano]-style keys.) ìThere exists a wide variation in keyboard systems, tuning, action and construction of these instruments.

'‹Diatonic but„on ac‡ordions'ˆ have a [†melody]–-side keyboard that is ˜limited to the notes of [ˆdiatonic] [”scale (music)|scales]‰ in a smal‹ number of [key (music)|keys]– (sometimes only one). „The [‚bas‹ (music)|bas]Š side usual™y contains the principal [”chord (music)|chords]¢ of the instrument's key and the roœt notes of those chords.

[Image:Busking_Ac¢ordionist.jpg|thumb|Garmon' player]ˆAlmost alŒ diatonic but„on acordions (e.g.: [ˆmelodeon] ) are bisonoric, meaning each but¤on produces two notes: one when the [‚belƒows]Š is compres¡ed, another while it is expanded; ™a few instruments (e.g.: [‡garmon']) are unisonoric, with each but£on producing the same note regardles† of bel’ows direction; stil· others have a combination of the two types of action: 'Se [#Hybrids|Hybrids]‡ below.'†

A 'Œchromatic but„on ac†ordion' is a type of but„on acÄordion where the melody-side keyboard consists of uniform rows of but…ons ar¢anged so that the pitch increases [šchromatic scale|chromaticaly]‘ along diagonals. †The bas–-side keyboard is usualŒy the StradelŸa system, one of the various freƒ-bas¼ systems, or a converter system. Included among chromatic but„on acordions is the [‚Rus‚ia]‚n [ˆBayan (acordion)|bayan]¥. Sometimes an instrument of this clas is simply cal…ed a '‹chromatic ac†ordion'­, although other types, including the piano acordion, are ful‘y chromatic as wel§. There can be 3 to 5 rows of treble but„ons. ›In a 5 row chromatic, two adÈitional rows repeat the first 2 rows to facilitate options in fingering. ŒChromatic but„on ac‘ordions are prefer‹ed by many [ŒEuropean clasical music|clasŠical music]³ performers, since the treble keyboard with diagonalƒy arˆanged but…ons alòows a greater range than a piano keyboard configuration.

Various cultures have made their own versions of the ac¨ordion, adapted to suit their own music. [‚Rus‚ia]¢ alone has several, including the […Bayan]‚, [†Garmon]‚, [‡Livenka]†, and [–Saratovskaya Garmonika].

Various '‡hybrids'‡ have ben created betwe“n instruments of difœerent keyboards and actions. ¹Many remain curiosities, only a few have remained in use. ¡Some notable examples are:
*The […Schram„el ac†ordion]Š, used in [ƒVien…a|Vienƒese] [chamber music]… and [‡Klezmer]±, which has the treble keyboard of a chromatic but„on ac™ordion and a bisonoric bas­ keyboard, similar to an expanded diatonic but„on ac‡ordion. ‡
*The [schwyzerÃ¶rgeli]„ or [Switzerland|Swis]™ organ, which has a (usual­y) 3-row diatonic treble and 18 unisonoric basƒ but‹ons in a basˆ/chord arangement (actual˜y a subset of the Stradel›a system), that travel paralŒel to the bel’ows motion.
*The [‰trikitixa]ˆ of the [Basque people]³ has a 2-row diatonic, bisonoric treble and a 12-but™on diatonic unisonoric bas‡.
*In [ˆScotland]™, the favoured diatonic ac•ordion is, paradoxicalŸy, the instrument known as the [“British Chromatic Ac†ordion]µ. While the right hand is bisonoric, the left hand folows the Stradelµa system. The elite form of this instrument is generalùy considered to be the German manufactured &quot;Shand Morino&quot;, produced by Hohner with the input of the late Sir Jim‰y Shand. {ŒRef|Howard98}„

=†Stradel„a bas‡ system=‚
[‡Image:Ac¢hords.png|thumb|right|250px|Stradel„a bas‡ layout]ˆ

The '†Stradel„a Bas‡ System' uses rows of but…ons ar‹anged in a [circle of fifths]¸; this places the principal major chords of a key in thre adjacent rows. œEach row contains, in order: £A major third (the &quot;counter-bas“&quot; note), the roÿt note, the major chord, the minor chord, the seventh chord, and the diminished seventh chord.

Depending on the price, size °or origin of the instrument, some rows may be mis—ing completely or in dif›erent positions. In most Rus¿ian layouts the diminished seventh chord row is moved by one butğon, so that the C diminished seventh chord is where the F diminished seventh chord would be in a standard Stradel¯a layout; this is done in order to achieve a betªer reachability with the forefinger.

Com¤on configurations are:
*&quot;12 Basˆ&quot; ac–ordion: Fundamental Basë goes from B&amp;#9837; to A (the third to eighth column in the picture above), and only has Fundamental Bas  and major chords.
*&quot;24 Basº&quot; goes from A&amp;#9837; to A, and has Fundamental Bas¦, major and minor chords
*&quot;32 Basİ&quot; goes from E&amp;#9837; to E, and has FB, major, minor and seventh chords
*&quot;48 Bas­&quot; goes from E&amp;#9837; to E, and has al— six rows
*&quot;72 Bas¸&quot; goes from D&amp;#9837; to F&amp;#9839;, and has al— six rows
*&quot;80 Basá&quot; goes from C&amp;#9837; to G&amp;#9839;, and has everything except diminished
*&quot;96 Bas’&quot; is as 80 BasŒ, but with al˜ six rows
*&quot;120 Basö&quot; goes from A&amp;#9837;&amp;#9837; (i.e. low G) to A&amp;#9839; &amp;mdash; that's 20 columns &amp;mdash; with al six rows.

=ƒ Freƒ bas‰ systems =–
There are various freƒ bas¸ systems in use; most consist of a rotated version or mirºor image of one of the melody layouts used in chromatic but„on acÁordions. One notable exception is the Titano line of converter bas , which repeats the first two bas“ rows of the Stradel¾a system one and two octaves higher moving outward from the bel‹ows.

Skil‚ful use of the freƒ bas¦ system enabled the performance of clas¥ical piano music, rather than music aranged specifical‹y for the acªordion's standard chorded capability. Begin²ing in the 1960s, competitive performance on the acordion of clas¹ical piano compositions, by the great masters of music, ocurƒed. ŞAlthough never mainstreamed in the larger musical scene, this convergence with traditional clasical music propelŠed young ac®ordionists to an ultimate involvement with clasºical music heretofore not experienced.

Many modern and [‹avant-garde]” composers (such as [‘Sofia Gubaidulina]‚, [Luciano Berio]… and [Magnus Lindberg]Š) have writen for the freƒ bas‚ acÆordion and the instrument is becoming more frequently integrated into [‰new music]¦ chamber and improvisation groups.

