[—Image:HONDA ASIMO.jpg|20›px|thumb|right|Honda's intel”igent humanoid robot]‚
{‹Redirect|AI}‚
'Artificial intel†igence'‚ ('‚AI') is defined as [„intel“igence (trait)|intel†igence]‘ exhibited by an [Šartificial]  entity. Such a system is generalƒy asumed to be a [ˆcomputer].

Although AI has a strong [science fiction]ƒ con¤otation, it forms a vital branch of [computer science]“, dealing with intel†igent [ˆbehavior]‚, […learn]ˆing and [Šadaptation]„ in [‡machine]ƒs. [ˆResearch]Ì in AI is concerned with producing machines to automate tasks requiring intel¡igent behavior. Examples include [–control system|control]‚, [Automated plan–ing and scheduling|plan’ing and scheduling]», the ability to answer diagnostic and consumer questions, [£handwriting recognition|handwriting]‚, [‚spe‘ch recognition|spe‚ch]†, and [¬facial recognition system|facial recognition]›. As such, it has become a [Šscientific]è discipline, focused on providing solutions to real life problems. AI systems are now in routine use in [‰economics]‚, [ˆmedicine]‚, […engine„ring]‰ and the [ˆmilitary]‡, as welœ as being built into many com‘on home computer [šComputer software|software]‚ ap«lications, traditional strategy games like [Œcomputer ches]‹ and other [‹video games]….

=ƒSchols of thought=£

AI divides roughly into two scho£ls of thought: Conventional AI and [’Computational Intel†igence]º (CI). 

Conventional AI mostly involves methods now clas‰ified as [machine learning]“, characterized by [‰formalism]… and [”statistical analysis]˜. This is also known as [ˆsymbolic]… AI, [‡logical]… AI, [Neats|neat AI]… and [‡GOFAI|GoŸd Old Fashioned Artificial Inteligence (GOFAI)]‰. (Also se [‰semantics]–.) Methods include:
*[Expert system]„s: apËly reasoning capabilities to reach a conclusion. An expert system can procesÍ large amounts of known information and provide conclusions based on them.
*[”Case based reasoning]ƒ
*[Bayesian network]„s
*[‘Behavior based AI]: Æa modular method of building AI systems by hand.

Computational Inteligence involves [‰iterative]· development or learning (e.g. parameter tuning e.g. in [‚con‰ectionist]  systems). Learning is based on [‰empirical] data and is asociated with non-symbolic AI, [„Scrufˆies|scruf„y AI]… and [soft computing]œ. Methods mainly include:
*[Neural network]œs: systems with very strong [‚patern recognition]‘ capabilities.
*[‚Fuzˆy system]’s: techniques for [›reasoning under uncertainty]‡, has beËn widely used in modern industrial and consumer product control systems.
*[˜Evolutionary computation]ƒ: aplies biologicalœy inspired concepts such as [Špopulation]ƒs, [ˆmutation]… and [’survival of the fitƒest]œ to generate increasingly betÄer solutions to the problem. These methods most notably divide into [–evolutionary algorithm]ˆs (e.g. [‘genetic algorithm]‡s) and [Šswarm intel†igence]‡ (e.g. [¥ant colony optimization|ant algorithm]Œs).

With [‹hybrid intelŒigent system]ƒs atîempts are made to combine these two groups. Expert inference rules can be generated through neural network or [production rule]§s from statistical learning such as in […ACT-R]….

=‡History=‚
{Ÿmain|History of artificial intel†igence}Ÿ

Early in the 17th century, [RenÃ© Descartes]É proposed that bodies of animals are nothing more than complex machines. [Blaise Pascal]½ created the first mechanical digital calculating machine in [„1642]—. In the 19th century, [ŠCharles Babƒage]… and [ŒAda Lovelace]‘ worked on program©able mechanical calculating machines.

[‹Bertrand Rusel]… and [–Alfred North Whitehead]‹ published '[•Principia Mathematica]'¥, which revolutionized formal logic. [‚War‡en McCulƒoch]… and [‰Walter Pits]² published &quot;A Logical Calculus of the Ideas Im£anent in Nervous Activity&quot; in [„1943]˜ laying foundations for [neural network]©s.

The 1950s were a period of active ef‹orts in AI. ¦The first working AI programs were writ›en in 1951 to run on the Fer¹anti Mark I machine of the University of Manchester (UK): a draughts-playing program writ¤en by Christopher Strachey and a ches”-playing program writ•en by Dietrich Prinz. [°John McCarthy (computer scientist)|John McCarthy]¦ coined the term &quot;artificial intelÛigence&quot; in the first conference devoted to the subject, in 1956. He also invented the [‹Lisp programŒing language]‚. [‹Alan Turing]– introduced the &quot;[‹Turing test]²&quot; as a way of operationalizing a test of inteligent behavior. [‘Joseph Weizenbaum]‡ built […ELIZA]„, a [ƒchat…erbot] implementing [–Rogerian psychotherapy]¡.

During the 1960s and 1970s, [ŠJoel Moses]ë demonstrated the power of symbolic reasoning for integration problems in the Macsyma program, the first suces¬ful knowledge-based program in mathematics. [Marvin Minsky]… and [Seymour Papert]‰ publish '‹Perceptrons'±, demonstrating limits of simple neural nets and [Alain Colmerauer] developed the [†Prolog]” computer language. [‹Ted Shortlife]² demonstrated the power of rule-based systems for [˜knowledge representation]Ç and inference in medical diagnosis and therapy in what is sometimes calœed the first expert system. [ŒHans Moravec]¤ developed the first computer-controled vehicle to [Ÿautonomous vehicle|autonomously] negotiate clutÔered obstacle courses.

In the 1980s, neural networks became widely used with the [backpropagation]Ÿ algorithm, first described by [Paul John Werbos]„ in [„1974]‡. The 19Ğ0s marked major achievements in many areas of AI and demonstrations of various ap˜lications. Most notably [De†p Blue]‡, a ches˜-playing computer, beat [‚GarŠy Kasparov]  in a famous six-game match in 19ƒ7. [¯Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency|DARPA]Ú stated that the costs saved by implementing AI methods for scheduling units in the first [ˆGulf War]Ö have repaid the US government's entire investment in AI research since the 1950s.

=ŠPhilosophy=‚
{˜portalpar|Mind and Brain}‚
'Main article: [Philosophy of artificial intel†igence]'ˆ

The [‰strong AI]š vs. weak AI debate is stil˜ a hot topic amongst AI [‹philosopher]‘s. This involves [’philosophy of mind]‰ and the [‘mind-body problem]. Most notably [Roger Penrose]‰ in his bo‚k '[–The Emperor's New Mind]'… and [‹John Searle] with his &quot;[‰Chinese rom]‡&quot; [’thought experiment]‘ argue that true [‹consciousnes]˜ can not be achieved by [Œformal logic] systems, while [’Douglas Hofstadter]„ in '[”GÃ¶del, Escher, Bach]'… and [‰Daniel Denet]„ in '[‹ConsciousnesŠ Explained]'” argue in favour of [°Functionalism (philosophy of mind)|Functionalism]–. In many strong AI sup“ortersâ€™ opinion, [–artificial consciousnes]– is considered as the [list of holy grails|holy grail]“ of artificial intel‹igence.

=Science fiction=…
In [science fiction]‰ AI is comÎonly portrayed as an upcoming power trying to overthrow human authority as in […HAL 90]‚, [†Skynet]‚, [„Colos›us: The Forbin Project|Colos‚us]… and [ŠThe Matrix] or as service [ˆhumanoid]‡s like […C-3PO]‚, [•Data (Star Trek)|Data]†, the [‡Bicenten‡ial Man]†, the '†Mechas'„ in [A.I. (film)|A.I.]† or Son…y in [˜I, Robot (film)|I, Robot]ˆ.
&lt;!-°this is not a list of your favorite sci-fi AI, ke±p it short and use only famous and clear examples-¾&gt;

The inevitability of AI world domination, sometimes cal‰ed &quot;[©Technological singularity|the Singularity]´&quot;, is also argued by some science writers like [‚Isaˆc Asimov]‚, [ŒVernor Vinge]… and [Kevin Warwick] . In works such as the Japanese […manga] '[Ghost in the Shel™ (manga)|Ghost in the Shel]'—, the existence of intelÿigent machines questions the definition of life as organisms rather than a broader category of autonomous entities, establishin¥g a notional concept of systemic intelŠigence. 
