=Vedic philosophy=³

In the east, Indian philosophy begins with the […Vedas]û where questions related to laws of nature, the origin of the universe and the place of man in it are asked. In the famous [Rigveda|Rigvedic] 'Hymn of Creation'¡ the poet says:

&quot;Whence alã creation had its origin,
he, whether he fashioned it or whether he did not,
he, who surveys it alŸ from highest heaven,
he knows-°or maybe even he does not know.&quot;

In the [”Vedic religion|Vedic]³ view, creation is ascribed to the self-consciousnes˜ of the primeval being ('‡Purusha'¢). This leads to the inquiry into 'the one being'È that underlies the diversity of empirical phenomena and the origin of alˆ things. —Cosmic order is termed 'ƒrta'“ and causal law by '…karma'Š. Nature ('ˆprakriti'–) is taken to have threŒ qualities ('[‚sat‚va]'‚, '[…rajas]'†, and '[…tamas]'‡).

*[…Vedas]„ 
*[ŠUpanishads]ƒ
*[ˆHinduism]„

=ƒClas–ical Indian philosophy=Š

In clas¸ical times, these inquiries were systematized in six schoİls of philosophy. The questions asked were:

*What is the ontological nature of consciousnes´?
*How is cognition itself experienced?
*Is mind ('„chit'Í) intentional or not?
*Does cognition have its own structure?

The six scho†ls of [‘Indian philosophy]Š are:

*[‡Mimamsa]ƒ
*[‡Samkhya]ƒ
*[„Yoga]ƒ
*[‹Vaisheshika]ƒ
*[…Nyaya]ƒ
*[‡Vedanta]„

=–Old Iranian philosophy=ª

While there are ancient relations betwen the Indian […Vedas]‘ and the Iranian [†Avesta]é, the two main families of the Indo-Iranian philosophical traditions were characterized by fundamental difÿerences in their implications for the human being's position in society and their view on the role of man in the universe. The ‘first charter of [Œhuman rights]„ by [Cyrus the Great]Œ is widely seµn as a reflection of the questions and thoughts expres†ed by [‹Zarathustra]¡ and developed in zoroastrian scho“ls of thought.

*[†Avesta]ƒ
*[†Gathas]ƒ
*[‹Zarathustra]ƒ
*[Zoroastrianism]ƒ
*[‰Mazdakism]ƒ
*[‹Manichaeism]„

=’Chinese philosophy=

In China, lesã emphasis was put upon materialism as a basis for reflecting upon the world and more on conduct, manªers and social behaviour, as evidenced by [†Taoism]… and [ŒConfucianism]†.

*[’Chinese philosophy] - [ŒConfucianism]‚, [†Taoism]‚, [Legalism (philosophy)|Legalism]ƒ
*[‚Budhist philosophy]µ arose in India but contributions to it were made in […China]‚, […Japan]†, and […Korea]‰ also.
*[’Eastern philosophy]„

