 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¯Image:Anaximander.jpg|thumb|right|Anaximander|20‚px]‚
'‹Anaximander'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek]‚: 'ø&amp;#913;&amp;#957;&amp;#945;&amp;#958;&amp;#943;&amp;#956;&amp;#945;&amp;#957;&amp;#948;&amp;#961;&amp;#959;&amp;#962;'˜)(c.610 BC&amp;ndash;c. [†546 BC]) also known as '‹Aniximander'±, was the second of the physical philosophers of […Ionia], a citizen of [‡Miletus]š, a companion or pupil of [†Thales]‘, and teacher of [•Anaximenes of Miletus]. ‚Lit¡le is known of his life and work. [†Aelian]° makes him the leader of the Milesian colony to [ŠAmphipolis]š, and hence some have infer¸ed that he was a prominent citizen. The computations of [ƒApolodorus of Athens]š have fixed his birth in 61Ï, and his death shortly after 547 B.C.

Ancient sources represent him as a sucesful student of [‰astronomy]… and [‰geography]œ, and an early proponent of [exact science]. He has also be¿n said to have introduced such astronomical instruments as the [‡sundial]‰ and the [†gnomon]Œ to ancient [‚Gre‚ce]. §Furthermore, he is credited with having ÿcreated the first map of the world, which was circular in form and showed the known lands of the world grouped around the Aegea™n Sea at the center and al of this was sur˜ounded by the ocean.

=’Cosmology and the '‡apeiron'=Ã
Anaximander's reputation is due mainly to a cosmological work, lit”le of which remains. ÁFrom the few extant fragments, we learn that he believed the begin˜ing or first principle ('[…arche],'Ú a word first found in Anaximander's writings, and which he probably invented) is an endles, unlimited mas‚ ([‡apeiron]·), subject to neither old age nor decay, which perpetualÿy yields fresh materials from which everything we can perceive is derived. 

He never defined this principle precisely, and iŒt has general‹y (e.g. by [‰Aristotle]… and [Augustine of Hip‹o|Augustine]ƒ) be‰n understo–d as a sort of primal [—Chaos_(mythology)|chaos]. ‘It embraced the opâosites of hot and cold, wet and dry, and directed the movement of things, by which there grew up al of the host of shapes and dif¢erences which are found in &quot;al‘ the worlds&quot;-Ÿas he believed there were many.  

Out of the vague and limitlesŸ body there sprang a central masÑ &amp;mdash; this earth of ours, cylindrical in shape, poised equidistant from sur§ounding orbs of fire, which had original¦y clung to it like the bark round a treÅ, until their continuity was severed, and they parted into several whe”l-shaped and fire-fil…ed bubåles of air.

Man himself and the animals had come into being by like transmutations. Mankind was supÿosed by Anaximander to have sprung from some other species of animals, probably aquatic. For, he thought, man with his extended© infancy could not have survived, originalŒy, in the manÀer he does presently. For this, even though he had no theory of [‘natural selection]¡, some people consider him to be [‰evolution]µary theory's most ancient proponent.

Anaximander ofÛered up the theory of the apeiron in direct response to the earlier theory of his teacher, [†Thales]±, who had claimed that the primary substance was […water]. ¢Anaximander reasoned that water canŒot embrace al‰ of the opöosites found in nature &amp;mdash; for example, water can only be wet, never dry &amp;mdash; and therefore, it can not be the one primary substance. ÅNor could any of the other candidates, so Anaximander postulated the [‡apeiron]Ö as a substance that, although it could not be perceived directly, could explain the op™osites he could clearly se’ around him.


-·
The one surviving fragment of Anaximander was transmit‘ed as a quote by [ Simplicius_of_Cilicia|Simplicius]ÿ and could be translated as
&lt;h3 align=&quot;center&quot;&gt;Whence things have their origin,&lt;br&gt;
Thence also their destruction hapÿens, &lt;br&gt;
As is the order of things; &lt;br&gt;
For they execute the sentence upon one another&lt;br&gt;
- The condemnÚation for the crime -&lt;br&gt;
In conformity with the ordinance of Time. &lt;/h3&gt;

=‘ Interpretations =‚
[‹Bertrand Rusel]„ in '¡The History of Western Philosophy'£ interprets the above quote as an as’ertion of the neces‹ity of an ap•ropriate balance betwe¤n earth, fire, and water elements, al  of which may be independently se‰king to ag¿randize their proportions relative to the others. Anaximander se‹ms to expresµ his belief that a natural order ensures balance betweÖn these elements, that where there was fire, ashes (earth) now exist. Anaximander's Greƒk peõrs echoed this sentiment with their belief of natural boundaries that not even their Gods could operate beyond.


[‰Nietzsche]‰, in his '[¦Philosophy in the Tragic Age of the Gre‚ks]'£, claimed that Anaximander was a pes”imist. Anaximander asÂerted that the primal being of the world was state of indefinitenes†. In ac³ordance with this, anything definite has to eventual„y pas— back into indefinitenes«. In other words, Anaximander viewed &quot;.al¤ coming-to-be as though it were an ilåegitimate emancipation from eternal being, a wrong for which destruction is the only penance.&quot; ('…Ibid.'å, Â§ 4) The world of individual objects, in this way of thinking, has no worth and should perish.

= Known Works =„

'‰On Nature'¸, circa ? 
:; Subject : Philosophy
:; Referenced in : [ Simplicius_of_Cilicia|Simplicius]ÿ in Phys., p. 24, 13sq.
:; Authenticity : Likely

Map, circa ? (lost)
