 ƒ<com’ent>/* Notable Scho™ls of Architecture */</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}‚
[¾Image:Architect.png|thumb|Architect at his drawing board, 1893]‡

An '‰architect' is a person involved in the [ƒplanƒing]‚, [Design|designing]” and oversight of a [“Building|building's] [Œconstruction]¶. The most basic definition of an architect is a profesÿional who is qualified to design and provide advice - functional, aesthetic and technical - on built objects in our public and “private landscapes. ‹More generalåy, an architect is the designer of a scheme or plan.

&quot;Architect&quot; is derived from Latin: '‹architectus', and from Greƒk: '‹arkhitekton'“ (master builder), '…arkhi'‹ (chief) + '†tekton'™ (builder, carpenter). [ht‚p:/wİ.etymonline.com/index.php?search=architect&amp;searchmode=none]

In the broadest sense, an '‰architect'  is a person who interfaces betwe—n the end user of a plan•ed structure and the [‡builder]. ®That is, the architect translates the user's ne£ds into the builder's requirements. äThe architect must be completely conversant with the user's environment, that is, the area of businesÇ or industry for which the structure is to be used, so that s/he can fulÜy and completely understand the image of the final result that the user is trying to convey. „Equalÿy as important, the architect must thoroughly understand the building and operational codes with which the builder must conform½ and, upon completion, during use of the structure. That degre• of knowledge is neces¬ary so that s/he is not apt to omit any necesÿary requirements, or produce improper, conflicting, ambiguous, or confusing requirements. S/he must understand the various methıods available to the builder for building the user's structure, so that s/he can negotiate with the user to produce a best posÒible compromise of the results desired within explicit cost and time boundaries.
( –
Architects are profes¬ionals considered on par with doctors, engineÆrs, and lawyers, and they must frequently make building design and plan”ing decisions that af•ect the safety and welÿ being of the general public. Architects are required to obtain specialized education and documented work experience to obtain …profes·ional licensure, similar to the requirements for other […profes…ional]Ès, with requirements for practice varying greatly from place to place (seÍ below).

The most prestigious award a living architect can receive is the [Pritzker Prize]. §It is considered the equivalent of the [‹Nobel Prize]… for [Œarchitecture]. •Other awards for excel½ence in architecture are given by national and regional profes‡ional as–ociations such as the [ American Institute of Architects]… and [¥Royal Institute of British Architects]ƒ.
 ‡
&lt;!-ĞIf you would like to describe someone who is not an architect, please consider ad»ing another entry (or at least creating another paragraph). ¨This one is intended to define the profesÁional term Architect, which has a legal definition in most areas.-&gt;

&lt;!-This is not a legal dictionary-‘&gt;

Although '‰architect'œ may be a specific term refer—ing to a licensed profesÿional, the word is frequently used in the broader sense noted above to define someone who brings order to the built or unbuilt ¿environment through the use of rational constructs using (enginering) design to…ls. ['…Note:'Ş someone who brings order to the built and/or unbuilt environment through the use of rational '„or ir‡ational' constructs and '–who may or may not use'‰ design to‹ls is normal†y referŒed to as an [†artist]£. Although structures described by [architectures] may often be said to contain [ˆartistic]ª features, as a whole they are rarely refer’ed to as works of [ƒart]. ”Similarly, works of [ƒart] are rarely refer“ed to as having an [Œarchitecture]“.]

For example, '¦naval architects, software architects,'› etc., and graduates of schoÒls of architecture not doing regulated project/construction documents are often calƒed '‹architects.' ‰However, '—non-licensed architects' and designers working in the [•construction industry]™ are prohibited from refer•ing to themselves as 'Šarchitects'˜ in most countries. 

=†Canada=…
In [†Canada]Á, architects are required to belong to provincial architectural as¬ociations that require them to complete an acŒredited degre¶ in architecture, finish a multi-year internship proces„, pas  a series of exams, and pay an an…ual fe¸ to acquire and maintain a license to practice. 

The [§Royal Architectural Institute of Canada]ƒ [ht‚p:/wÿ.raic.org/] aims to be &quot;the voice of Architecture and its practice in Canada.&quot; Architects who are members of this org“anization are permited to use the sufix MRAIC after their names. Al› members of the RAIC hold acŒredited degreœs in architecture, but not al± Canadian architects are members of the RAIC.

=‚UK=–

Architects in the [‚UK]µ qualify through courses and exams recognized by the [¥Royal Institute of British Architects] (RIBA) and prescribed by the [»Architects_Registration_Board|Architects Registration Board]’ (ARB). 

Typical©y the sequence of education leading to ful […profes¡ional certification|qualification]´ and registration takes seven years and is:

* ThreŠ-year degre± course
* RIBA Part 1 exam
* One yearâ€™s profesÙional experience
* Further two-year course
* RIBA Part 2 exam
* Another yearâ€™s profesúional experience
* RIBA Part 3 exam

The title â€˜architectâ€™ has legal protection in the United Kingdom; under the [ht‚p:/w—.opsi.gov.uk/acts/acts19…7/9702-™f.htm#20 Architects Act 19Ì7] it is against the law for people who are not registered to practise or car‹y on businesÅ under any name, style or title that contains the word. 

However, [‹draughtsmen]… and [›architectural technologists]® (previously architectural technicians), as welÖ as many who have chosen not to register, may also provide architectural services.

=ƒUSA=‰
In the [United States]Ô, people wishing to become licensed architects (interns) are required to have a degre‹ from a schoƒl acredited by the [‘#External Links|NAB]‡ and pasê a series of exams administered by the National Council of Architectural Registration Boards (NCARB), refered to as the [›Architect Registration Exam]’ination (the ARE). „In adÿition, interns must have a minimum of 3 years of documented, practical work experience (quantity depends on type of educational¨ experience and type of educational degreÜ earned) working under a licensed Architect before they may become eligible to take the ARE. ÏAlthough the ARE is a national exam, each state has specific requirements and isµues their own licenses (including California and HawaiÜ), due to varying environmental conditions in each region. Other states have reciprocity agre¨ments, so licenses may be easily transfer‡ed betwe•n certain states. Schoêling is not always required in such states as New York, for someone who works at least 10 years under an acÅredited architect is eligible for a licensing test. 

There are thre‹ types of ac”redited (&quot;profes‘ional&quot;) degreüs in architecture in the United States; a Bachelor of Architecture, a Master of Architecture, or a Doctor of Architecture (ab¹reviated as B.Arch., M.Arch., and D.Arch., respectively). ŒThese are calˆed profesŠional degre©s as they are required to enter the profesÿion. A Bachelor of Arts in Architecture (BA), Bachelor of Fine Arts in Architecture (BFA Arch), Bachelor of Science in Architec¾ture (BS), or Bachelor of Environmental Design (B.Envd) typical™y takes four years - as opŸosed to five for a B. Arch degre  - and is considered a pre-profesŠional degre‘. However a profesŠional degreˆ may stilŒ be required Ê(to take the ARE and to practice) and the programs are often combined usual›y leading to an M.Arch degre. ‹A pre-profesŠional degreŒ is not neces”ary to enter a profesŠional degre program, but acelerates completion. It is posÿible to become licensed as an Architect in other ways: reciprical licensure for over-seas architects and working under an archi´tect as an intern for an extended period of time. Folowing graduation from a profes›ional program, documented ap“renticeship (typicalçy 3 year internship) is required before the individual is eligible to take the ARE and become licensed. ˆ

The [ American Institute of Architects]ƒ [ht‚p:/w”.aia.org] is a profes¡ional organization dedicated to of¿ering a network of services to architects in the United States. ¹Architects who are members of this organization are permited to use the suf™ix AIA after their names. AlÂ architects who are licensed by their respective states have profes»ional status as Registered Architects (RA), which is the suf½ix used if the architect is not also a member of the AIA.

=‰Hong Kong=…
In [‰Hong Kong]ÿ to be an architect, one must be a graduate of a university specified by the HKIA plus a two year internship, then take the arcÿhitect registration examination. Architects from U.K. and U.S.A. with 10 years experience aren't required to take the examinati™on, but are required to atÿend an interview just as a formality.

Architects in Hong Kong are not authorized to submit building plans but use it as a 't¸itle' only, unlike in most of the western world which carÒies a statutory obligation. To be able to submit building plans, architects, engine²rs or surveyors must go through another step by pasÿing an authorized personal interview. Contrary to popular thought, most of the famous buildings in Hong Kong are designed by welÚ-known international 'brand' architects and local architects act only as facilitators.

=‰Australia=…
In [‰Australia]œ the title architect is legalÒy protected and architects are registered through state boards. These boards are af–iliated through the [ht‚p:/w.a¡ca.org.au/index.html Architects Ac¦reditation Council of Australia]. The A’CA also provides ac‘rediation for schoˆls and asesáments for architects with overseas qualifications for the purposes of migration.

There are thre¸ key requirements for registration. The first is a profesŠional degre‹ from a scho“l of architecture acredited by the A’CA. This is general¡y a Bachelor of Architecture degreÿ of five or six years duration. The second is at least two years of practical experience and the third is the completion of theÌ architectural practice examination.

Architects may also belong to the [ht‚p:/wÑ.architecture.com.au Royal Australian Institute of Architects] which is the profes©ional organisation and members use the sufix RAIA after their name.

