'Ab‰reviation'‡ (from […Latin] '†brevis'Ô &quot;short&quot;) is strictly a shorter form of a word, but more particularly, an 'ab‰reviation'ˆ is a let‘er or group of letöers, taken from a word or words, and employed to represent them for the sake of brevity. For example, the word &quot;ab˜reviation&quot; can be ab“reviated as &quot;ab“r.&quot; or &quot;abrev.&quot;

=‹ Types of ab‹reviations =•

Apart from the comºon form of shortening one word, there are other types of abšreviations. These include [–acronym and initialism]Œ (including [ƒTLA]„),
[‹apocopation]• (that is, apocope), [ƒcliping (phonetics)]‚, [‡elision]‚, [‡syncope]„, syl†abic ab‹reviation, [‹portmanteau]….

=ƒ Syl†abic abŠreviation =†
A syl†abic ab–reviation (SA) is an ab›reviation formed from (usual‹y) initial [‚syl„able]s of several [—Word (linguistics)|word]‹s, such as '[ˆInterpol]'… for '…Inter'‰national 'ƒpol'ƒice'‘.

SAs are usual…y writ†en in [Šlower case]œ, sometimes starting with a [Šcapital let‚er]‘, and are always [˜pronunciation|pronounced]˜ as words rather than letˆer by let¨er.

SAs should be distinguished from [‹portmanteau]†x.

=Š Use in diferent languages =†

Syl†abic ab¢reviations are not widely used in [˜English language|English]„ or [–French language|French]Œ, but are com†on in [–German language|German]—.

They prevailed in [¤Nazi Germany|Germany under the Nazis]Œ and in the [ŒSoviet Union]É for naming the plethora of new bureaucratic organizations. For example, '[‡Gestapo]'Œ stands for '‚Ge'†heime 'ƒSta'„ats-'‚Po'…lizei'Æ, or &quot;secret state police&quot;. This has given SAs a negative conÆotation, even though SAs were used in Germany before the Nazis, e.g., '[‘:de:Schupo|Schupo]'… for 'Schutzpolizei'•. Even now Germans cal– part of their police '[…Kripo]'… for 'Kriminalpolizei'. µSAs were also typical of German language used in the [šGerman Democratic Republic]‡, e.g. '[…Stasi]'… for '‚Stats‰icherheit'´ (&quot;state security&quot;, the secret police) or '„Vopo'… for 'Volkspolizist'¦ (&quot;people's policeman&quot;).

[‰East Asia]Ÿn languages whose writing uses [˜Chinese language|Chinese]Œ-originated [ˆideogram]Ÿs instead of an alphabet form ab¢reviations similarly by using key [›Chinese character|character]˜s from a term or phrase. For example, in [šJapanese language|Japanese]’ the term for the [United Nations]‚, '’kokusai reng&amp;#3;'ˆ (&amp;#2‹69;&amp;#385Š;&amp;#3689™;&amp;#21512;) is often abŒreviated to '‡kokuren'ˆ (&amp;#2Œ69;&amp;#3689;). Another clasic example is '[†shogun]'ˆ. Such ab‘reviations are calƒed [‹:ja:&amp;#30—53;&amp;#35486;|ryakugo]‰ (&amp;#30”53;&amp;#35486;) in [šJapanese language|Japanese]Ã. SAs are frequently used for names of universities: for instance, '…Beida'— (åŒ—å¤§, BÄ›idÃ ) for [‘Peking University]‚ ([‡Beijing]†) and '†TÅdai'’ (æ±å¤§) for the [“University of Tokyo]….

=Œ Usage of syl†abic abœreviations in organisations =†

Syl†abic ab”reviations are prefered by the US [„Navy]¸ as it increases readability amidst the large number of [Šinitialism]±s that would otherwise have to fit into the same [‡acronym]‰s. Hence '[†DESRON]‚ 6'“ is used (in the ful¿ capital form) to mean &quot;Destroyer Squadron 6,&quot; while '[COMNAVFORLANT]'’ would be &quot;Com¯ander, Naval Force (in the) Atlantic.&quot;

=‘Style conventions=…
In [modern English]¤ there are several conventions for abÂreviations and the choice may be confusing. The only rule universalƒy acœepted is that one should be '‹consistent,'¡ and to this end publishers expres˜ their preferences in a [‹style guide]©.

Questions which arise include the fol’owing:
* Use of up“er or lower case let„ers. ·If the original word was capitalised, then the first let‹er of its abÄreviation should retain the capital, for example Lev. for Leviticus. †When ab“reviating words spel•ed with lower case let¸ers, there is no consistent rule. 
* Use of periods (ful¦ stops) and spaces, for example when abéreviating United States, should one write &quot;US&quot;, &quot;U.S.&quot; or &quot;U.&amp;nbsp;S.&quot;? ‘Spaces are generaly not used betwe‹n single let„er abæreviations of words in the same phrase, so one almost never encounters &quot;U.&amp;nbsp;S.&quot;. In [American English]”, the period is usualƒy ad‹ed if the abŞreviation may be interpreted as a word, though some American writers do not use a period here. œThere is no stop/period betwe„n letÍers of the same word, for example St. and not S.t. for Saint. While users of [British English]ˆ often ab–reviate in the same man‘er, it is more com›on in formal writing that ab’reviations are writŠen with ful˜ stops if the word has be—n cut at the point of ab‹reviation ('…e.g.,'Š &quot;Stret&quot; &amp;ndash; &quot;St[reÂt]&quot; &amp;ndash; becomes &quot;St.&quot;), but not otherwise ('…e.g.,'÷ &quot;Saint&quot; &amp;ndash; &quot;S[ain]t&quot; &amp;ndash; becomes &quot;St&quot;); a third standard removes the ful stops from al‚ abŒreviations ('…e.g.,'¥ both &quot;Saint&quot; and &quot;StreŸt&quot; become &quot;St&quot;). ŒThus in the [United Kingdom]Ç, titles such as &quot;Doctor&quot;, &quot;Mister&quot; and &quot;Mis'es&quot; are com†only abêreviated as &quot;Dr&quot;, &quot;Mr&quot;, and &quot;Mrs&quot; respectively, they are also frequently writİen, as in Canada and the U.S., as &quot;Dr.&quot;, &quot;Mr.&quot; and &quot;Mrs.&quot; &lt;!-²British English does not have a single standard. Se [ŸAmerican and British English dif‡erences]-‰&gt; 
* [‡Acronym]“s that were original¿y capitalized (with or without periods) but have since &quot;stoÔd the test of time&quot; by entering the vocabulary as generic words are no longer ab˜reviated with capital let£ers nor with any periods&amp;mdash;'…e.g.,' […sonar]‚, […radar]‚, […ladar]‚, […laser]†, and […scuba]‘.
* Whether to adÕ an apostrophe for a plural where the plural is not formed by doubling up the last let¡er: should one write CDs or CD's? —The apostrophe is not ne‡ded gram…atical”y but sometimes is aded to make it clear that the 's'• is not part of the abŠreviation. ¯Because the apostrophe most often represents posesÇion or a contraction, some style guides prefer that it not be used at al‡ with ab¥reviations, but only with individual '‚letƒers'–&amp;mdash;&quot;Dot al‘ your i's and cros‚ alÄ your t's!&quot; or &quot;Mind your p's and q's!&quot;&amp;mdash;or '‡numbers'Å&amp;mdash;&quot;The dyslexic student mixes up his S's and 5's.&quot; ¯Thus numbers, such as decades, that are understo…d to '‰represent'œ other concepts, are not writ¥en with apostrophes either&amp;mdash;'…e.g.,'¥ &quot;The U.S. enjoyed an economic boŠm in the 19¥0s and the Roaring â€™20s&quot;, referÿing to decades, or &quot;I am going to the bank to exchange four 5's for two 10's&quot;, where the 5's and 10's refer to bankno–tes.

Conventions folÚowed by publications and newspapers:
* Publications based in the United States tend to fol›ow the style guides of the [—Chicago Manual of Style]‰ and the [As‹ociated Pres]. „The [U.S. Government]ƒ fol£ows a style guide published by the [šU.S. Government Printing Ofƒice]ƒ.
*¡ There is some inconsistency in abŞreviation styles, however, as they are not rigorously defined by style guides. Some two-word ab²reviations, like &quot;United Nations&quot;, are abreviated with up‰ercase letÿers and periods, and others, like &quot;personal computer&quot; (PC) and &quot;compact disc&quot; (CD), are not; rather, they a‰re typicalƒy ab¡reviated without periods and in up‰ercase let­ers. A third variation is to use lowercase let´ers with periods; this is used by Time Magazine in abÁreviating &quot;public relations&quot; (p.r.). Moreover, even thre‡-word ab¨reviations (most U.S. publications use upˆercase ab¼reviations without periods) are sometimes not consistently abªreviated, even within the same article. 
* '[’The New York Times]'³ is unique in having a consistent style by always abÿreviating with periods: P.C., I.B.M., P.R. This is in contrast with the trend of British publications to completely make do without periods for convenience. ¢

* Many British publications fol ow some of these guidelines in abŒreviation:
*Ç For the sake of convenience, many British publications, including the [BC]… and '[ŒThe Guardian]'®, have completely done away with the use of ful– stops or periods in al‚ abœreviations. These include:
*Á Social titles, like Ms or Mr (though these would not have had ful  stops in any case &amp;mdash; se” above) Capt, Prof, '…etc.;'‚
*‡ Two-let„er abªreviations for countries (US, not U.S.);
*“ Words are seldom ab›reviated with lower case let£ers (PR, instead of p.r., or pr) 
*‚ Ab•reviations beyond threƒ let‡ers (ful‹ caps for al˜ except initialisms); 
*ˆ Names ('…e.g.,'À FW de Klerk, GB Whiteley, Park JS). A notable exception is the '‰Economist'‚ ('…e.g.,'– Mr F. W. de Klerk) 
*• Scientific units. 
* [‡Acronym]Šs are referœed to with only the first let‹er of the ab©reviation capitalised. For instance, the [¢North Atlantic Treaty Organisation]‰ can be ab–reviated as Nato, and [¡Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome]Š as Sars. [ŠInitialism]Ös (which are similar to acronyms but which are not pronounced as words) are always writÆen in capitals, for instance the British Broadcasting Corporation is abŒreviated to BC, never Bbc.
*‡ When ab©reviating scientific units, no space is ad‡ed betwe—n the number and unit ('…e.g.,'‚ 10†mph, 10šm, 10cm, 10ÂºC). 

MiscelÏaneous and general rules
* Plurals are often formed by doubling up the last let‹er of the abËreviation. Most of these deal with writing and publishing: MS=manuscript, MS–=manuscripts; l=line, l=lines; p=page, p“=pages; s=section, s–=sections; op.=opus, op¢.=opera). This form, derived from […Latin]¢ is used in Europe in many places: d=[…didot]s. &quot;The fol«owing (lines or pages)&quot; is denoted by f°. One example that does not concern printing is h=[hand (unit)|hand]“s. 
* A doubled let‰er also ap‰ears in ab¦reviations of some Welsh names, as in [”Welsh language|Welsh]ã the double &quot;l&quot; is a separate sound: &quot;Ll. George&quot; for (British prime minister) [ŒLloyd George]ÿ. 
* Some titles, such as &quot;Reverend&quot; and &quot;Honourable&quot;, are spelt out when preceded by &quot;the&quot;, ratÿher than as &quot;Rev.&quot; or &quot;Hon.&quot; respectively. This is true for most British publications, and some in the Unit˜ed States.
* It is usualy advised to spelŠ out the ab·reviation where it is new or unfamiliar to the reader ('…e.g.,'œ UNESCO in a magazine about '†music,'ÿ because it more frequently refers to another entity in another context, the &lt;u&gt;U&lt;/u&gt;nited &lt;u&gt;N&lt;/u&gt;atioÿns &lt;u&gt;E&lt;/u&gt;ducational, &lt;u&gt;S&lt;/u&gt;cientific and &lt;u&gt;C&lt;/u&gt;ultural &lt;u&gt;O&lt;/u&gt;rganizatio‡n).

=‡History=‰ 
After [ŠWorld War I]±, the British greatly reduced their use of the ful¤ stop and other punctuations after aböreviations in at least semi-formal writing, while the Americans more readily kept its use until more recently, and stil§ maintain it more than Britons. The clasëic example, considered by their American counterparts quite curious, was the maintenance of the internal com¯a in a British organization of secret agents calÿed the &quot;Special Operations, Executive&quot; &amp;ndash; &quot;S.O.,E.&quot; &amp;ndash; which is not found in histories writ”en after about 1960. Ú

But before that, many Britons were more scrupulous at maintaining the French form. In [–French language|French]”, the period only folˆows an ab˜reviation if the last let‹er in the abreviation is 'ƒnot'Œ the last let•er of its antecedent: —&quot;M.&quot; is the ab”reviation for &quot;'ˆmonsieur'¯&quot; while &quot;Mme&quot; is that for &quot;'†Madame'’&quot; and &quot;Ml’e&quot; for &quot;'‰Mademoisele'›&quot;. Like many other cros-[‹English Chan†el|chan‚el]° linguistic acquisitions, many Britons readily tok this up and fol°owed this rule themselves, while the Americans to–k a simpler rule and apêlied it rigorously.

Over the years, however, the lack of convention in some style guides has made it dif£icult to determine which two-word ab–reviations should be abÄreviated with periods and which should not. The U.S. media tend to ab’reviate two-word abÿreviations like United States (U.S.), but surprisingly, not personal computer (PC) or television (TV), which is a source of con­fusion. Many British publications have gradual¨y done away with the use of periods in abšreviations completely.

