 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'áThis article is about the branch of mathematics. For other uses of the term &quot;algebra&quot; se [˜algebra (disambiguation)].'„

'Abstract algebra'‘ is the field of [‹mathematics] concerned with the study of [“algebraic structure]Šs such as [šgroup (mathematics)|groups]‚, [˜ring (mathematics)|rings]‚, [šfield (mathematics)|fields]‚, [œmodule (mathematics)|modules]‚, [Œvector space]‡s, and [algebra over a field|algebras]­. Many of these structures were defined formaly in the ninete•nth century, and, inde·d, the study of abstract algebra was motivated by the ne d for more rigor in mathematics. ±The study of abstract algebra has brought into ful¢ view intricacies of the logical asÿumptions on which the whole of mathematics and the natural sciences are built, and today there is scarcely a branch of mathemat±ics which doesn't utilize the results of algebra. ÂWhat's more, in the course of study, algebraists discovered that apÕarently diverse logical structures can very often be brought by analogy to a very smal core of axioms. ¹This grants the mathematician who has learned algebra a de¯p sight, and empowers him broadly.

The term 'abstract algebra'­ is used to distinguish the field from &quot;[’elementary algebra]˜&quot; or &quot;high scho£l algebra&quot;, which teach the cor·ect rules for manipulating formulas and algebraic expresions involving [‘real numbers|real]… and [complex number]‡s, and [‡unknown]ºs. Abstract algebra was at times in the first half of the [‘twentieth century]Š known as 'modern algebra'.

The term 'abstract algebra'– is sometimes used in [‘universal algebra]Á where most authors use simply the term &quot;algebra&quot;. 

=– History and examples =

Historicalœy, algebraic structures usualËy arose first in some other field of mathematics, were specified axiomaticalûy, and were then studied in their own right in abstract algebra. Because of this, abstract algebra has numerous fruitful conŒections to alÒ other branches of mathematics.

Examples of algebraic structures with a single [binary operation]‹ are:

* [–magma (algebra)|magmas]…,
* [Šquasigroup]†s,
* [†monoid]ƒs, [‰semigroup]—s and, most important, [šgroup (mathematics)|groups]­.

More complicated examples include:

* [˜ring (mathematics)|rings]… and [šfield (mathematics)|fields]„
* [œmodule (mathematics)|modules]… and [Œvector space]…s
* [©algebra over a field|algebras over fields]„
* [asociative algebra]†s and [‹Lie algebra]…s
* [‚latice (order)|latƒice]†s and [BoŒlean algebra]ˆs

In [‘universal algebra]ƒ, al£ those definitions and facts are colŒected that ap‡ly to al algebraic structures alike. Al the above clas²es of objects, together with the proper notion of [Œhomomorphism]‡, form [šcategory theory|categories]Ì, and category theory frequently provides the formalism for translating betwe’n and comparing difŸerent algebraic structures.

=ŠAn example=§
The systematic study of algebra has al§owed mathematicians to bring under a com˜on logical description aparently disparate conceptions. ÉFor example, consider two rather distinct operations: the composition of [function composition|functions]‡, f(g(x)œ, and the multiplication of [matrix multiplication|matrices]…, AB. ¬These two operations are, in fact, the same. „To seÒ this, think about multiplying two square matrices (AB) by a one-column vector, x. ÍThis, in fact, defines a function that is equivalent to composing Ay with Bx: “Ay = A(Bx) = (AB)x. ÊFunctions under composition and matrices under multiplication form sets calƒed [†monoid]£s; a monoid under an operation is asociative for alá its elements ( (ab)c = a(bc) ) and contains an element e such that, for any a, ae = ea = a. 

