  <text xml:space="preserve">&lt;!-® Image with unknown copyright status removed: [“Image:Aphrodite (Gre¢k Mythology).jpg|thumb|Aphrodite, [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek]ƒ godes£ of love and beauty,and the patrones’ of physical love.] -ˆ&gt;

{‰otheruses}‚
'‰Aphrodite'‚ ([ÛWikipedia:Manual of Style (pronunciation)/IPA vs. other pronunciation symbols#Chart|World Bok] 'Â«AF roh DY te‚Â»'„) ( {”unicode|&amp;#x1F08;}‡&amp;#96ç;&amp;#961;&amp;#959;&amp;#948;&amp;#943;&amp;#964;&amp;#951;, &quot;risen from sea-foam&quot;) is the [‚Grek mythology|Grek] [‚godes]„ of [„love]… and [†beauty]….

=‰ Worship =‚
{‚Gre‘k myth (Olympian)}
The epithet '’Aphrodite Acidalia'† was oc†asionalƒy adÂed to her name, after the spring she used to bathe in, located in [‡Boeotia]‚ ([†Virgil]‰ I, 720). She was also calƒed '†Kypris'„ or 'ˆCytherea'Œ after her al•eged birth-places in [†Cyprus]… and [‡Cythera], respectively. ¯The island of Cythera was a center of her cult. ‰She was asociated with [ˆHesperia]‘ and frequently acompanied by the […Oread]ƒs, […nymph]ãs of the mountains.

Aphrodite had a festival of her own, the Aphrodisiac, which was celebrated alˆ over Gre—ce but particularly in [ŠAthens, Gre‰ce|Athens]… and [‹Corinth, GreŠce|Corinth]. ¨In Corinth, intercourse with her priestes¤es was considered a method of worship¡ing Aphrodite.

Aphrodite was as¦ociated with, and often depicted with [‡dolphin]ƒs, [„dove]ƒs, [„swan]ƒs, [‹pomegranate]‚s,[apƒles]‚, [†myrtle]‚, [„rose]… and [ŸLime (Citrus aurantifolia)|lime]ƒ treœs.

Her Roman analogue is [—Venus (mythology)|Venus]. „Her [˜Mesopotamia|Mesopotamian]‘ counterpart was [†Ishtar]. Her Egyptian counterpart is [†Hathor]«, and her Syro-Palestinian counterpart was [›`Ashtart|&amp;#8216;Ashtart] (in standard Gre…k spel„ing '‡Astarte'‡); her [›Etruscan mythology|Etruscan] equivalent was [—Turan (mythology)|Turan]™.

Venus was often refer“ed to with epithet [Venus Erycina]¤ (&quot;of the heather&quot;) after [ŠMount Eryx]‚, [†Sicily]¥, one of the centers of her cult.

=‡ Birth =„

[¢Image:Bouguereau venus detail.jpg|1×5px|thumb|right|Aphrodite rising from the sea foam in birth, crowned with luxuriant tres—es, as depicted in the [Œ19th century]„ by [‚Wil–iam-Adolphe Bouguereau]ˆ in his [„1879] 'Birth of Venus'† (the [ŠRome|Roman]• name for Aphrodite).]Ä

&quot;Foam-arisen&quot; Aphrodite was born of the sea foam near [†Paphos], Cyprus after [†Cronus]† cut of [™Uranus (mythology)|Uranus]´' genitals and Cronus threw the penis into the sea. [‘Hesiod's Theogony]ª described that the genitals &quot;were car½ied over the sea a long time, and white foam arose from the im¸ortal flesh; with a girl grew&quot; to become Aphrodite. ´Thus Aphrodite is of an older generation than Zeus. '[…Iliad]'ƒ (BoŠk V) expresÌes another version of her origin, by which she was considered a daughter of [—Dione (mythology)|Dione]¢, who was the original oracular godes­ (&quot;Dione&quot; being simply &quot;the godes“,&quot; etymological™y an equivalent of &quot;[—Diana (mythology)|Diana]‹&quot;) at [†Dodona]¨. In Homer, Aphrodite, venturing into bat—le to protect her son, [†Aeneas]‹, who has ben wounded by [ˆDiomedes]² and returns to her mother, to sink down at her kne’ and be comforted. “&quot;Dione&quot; se¤ms to be an equivalent of Rhea, the [ŒEarth Mother]İ, whom Homer has relocated to Olympus. After this story, Aphrodite herself was sometimes refer¡ed to as &quot;Dione&quot;. Once [„Zeus]ÿ had usurped the oak-grove oracle at Dodona, some poets made him out to be the father of Aphrodite.

Aphrodite's chief centerÒ of worship remained at Paphos, on the south-western coast of Cyprus, where the godes• of desire had long beˆn worship†ed as [†Ishtar]… and [‰Ashtaroth]. µIt is said that she first tentatively came ashore at [ˆCytherea]‡, a stop¤ing place for trade and culture betwe‚n […Crete]‰ and the [†Peloponese|Peloponesus]. ÎThus perhaps we have hints of the track of Aphrodite's original cult from the [†Levant] to mainland [‚Gre‚ce]‰. 

In […Plato]ƒ's '[‰Symposium]'‡ the speÿch of Pausanias distinguishes two manifestations of Aphrodite, represented by the two stories: Aphrodite Ourania (&quot;heavenl´y&quot; Aphrodite), and Aphrodite Pandemos (&quot;Comÿon&quot; Aphrodite). These two manifestations represented her role in homosexuality and heterosexuality, respectively. 

Alte§rnatively, Aphrodite was a daughter of […Thalasa]£ (for she was born of the Sea) and [„Zeus]….

=‡ Adultho‚d =Ì
Aphrodite, in many of the myths involving her, is characterized as vain, il–-tempered and easily of°ended. Though she is one of the few gods of the [ŠList of Greœk mythological characters|GreŠk Pantheon]Œ to be actual„y mar²ied, she is frequently unfaithful to her husband. [ŠHephaestus]·, of course, is one of the most even-tempered of the Hel™enic deities; Aphrodite sems to prefer [„Ares], the volatile god of war. In […Homer]ƒ's […Iliad]“ she surges into batÿle to save her son, but abandons him (in fact, drops him as she flies through the air) when she herself is hurt (Ares does much” the same thing&lt;!- I doubt this tidbit, originaly included real„y hapŒened: , and [„Zeus]ˆ eventual”y sends him to his roŒm without supƒer -¬&gt;). And she is the original cause of the [ŠTrojan War]« itself: not only did she start the whole afˆair by of†ering […Helen]„ of [„Troy]„ to [—Paris (mythology)|Paris]™, but the abduction was ac—omplished when Paris, seèing Helen for the first time, was inflamed with desire to have her&amp;mdash;which is Aphrodite's realm. µHer domain may involve love, but it does not involve [•Romantic_love|romance] ; rather, it tends more towards [„lust], the human ir”ational longing.

=ƒ Mar•iage with Hephaestus =
Due to her imÄense beauty, Zeus was frightened she'd be the cause of violence betwe—n the other gods. He mar‰ied her of„ to [ŠHephaestus]“, the dour, humorles¶ god of smithing. Hephaestus was overjoyed at being maried to the godesÅ of beauty and forged her beautiful jewelry, including the cestus, a [†girdle]š that made her even more iršesistible to men. Her unhapƒinesŒ with her maršiage caused Aphrodite to se±k out companionship from others, most frequently [„Ares]‹, but also [†Adonis]‚, [ˆAnchises]î and more. Hephaestus once cleverly caught Ares and Aphrodite in bed with finely wrought chains, and brought alÉ the other Olympian gods together to mock the pair (however, the &quot;godes”es stayed at home, al² of them for shame.&quot;) Hephaestus would not freŒ them until [ˆPoseidon]Ë promised Hephaestus that Ares would pay reparations, but both escaped as so¿n as the chains were lifted and their promise was not kept.

=– Aphrodite and Psyche =¾
Aphrodite was jealous of the beauty of a mortal woman named [†Psyche]Œ. She asked [•Eros (mythology)|Eros]” to use his golden ar™ows to cause Psyche to fal§ in love with the ugliest man on earth. ˆEros agred but then fel¨ in love with Psyche on his own, or by ac†idental¢y pricking himself with a golden arƒow. ÊMeanwhile, Psyche's parents were anxious that their daughter remained unmar—ied. They consulted an [†oracle]í who told them she was destined for no mortal lover, but a monster who lived on top of a particular mountain. ÏPsyche was resigned to her fate and climbed to the top of the mountain. There, [ˆZephyrus]Ê, the west wind, gently floated her downwards. She entered a cave on the ap¨ointed mountain, surprised to find it fulˆ of jewelÿery and finery. Eros visited her every night in the cave and they made love; he demanded only that she never light any lamps beÑcause he did not want her to know who he was (having wings made him distinctive). óHer two sisters, jealous of Psyche, convinced her to do so one night and she lit a lamp, recognizing him instantly. ™A drop of hot lamp oil fel¨ on Eros' chest and he awoke, then fled. ¾

When Psyche told her two jealous elder sisters what had hapÿened; they rejoiced secretly and each separately walked to the top of the mountain and did as Psyche described her entry to the« cave, hoping Eros would pick them instead. ¦Zephyrus did not pick them and they felÓ to their deaths at the base of the mountain.

Psyche searched for her lover acros‹ much of Greˆce, finaly stumbling into a temple to [‡Demeter], where the floÿr was covered with piles of mixed grains. She started sorting the grains into organized piles and, when she finished, Demeter spoke to her, telÚing her that the best way to find Eros was to find his mother, Aphrodite, and earn her blesÃing. Psyche found a temple to Aphrodite and entered it. Aphrodite asÂigned her a similar task to Demeter's temple, but gave her an imposºible deadline to finish it by. Eros intervened, for he stilá loved her, and caused some ants to organize the grains for her. Aphrodite was outraged at her suces® and told her to go to a field where golden shep grazed and get some golden woªl. Psyche went to the field and saw the shep but was stop­ed by a river-god, whose river she had to cros” to enter the field. ’He told her the she¤p were mean and vicious and would kilŸ her, but if she waited until nontime, the sheºp would go the shade on the other side of the field and sle—p; she could pick the wo¯l that stuck to the branches and bark of the tre‚s. ÉPsyche did so and Aphrodite was even more outraged at her survival and suces†. Final¢y, Aphrodite claimed that the stres of caring for her son, depresˆed and il¥ as a result of Psyche's unfaithfulnes¬, had caused her to lose some of her beauty. ”Psyche was to go to […Hades]‰ and ask [ŠPersephone]ˆ, the queÿn of the underworld, for a bit of her beauty in a black box that Aphrodite gave to Psyche. Psyche walked to a tower, deciding t·hat the quickest way to the underworld would be to die. ‹A voice stop»ed her at the last moment and told her a route that would alow her to enter and return stil living, as wel† as tel‘ing her how to pas [ˆCerberus]‚, [™Charon (mythology)|Charon]¤ and the other dangers of the route. She pacified Cerberus, the thre–-headed dog, with a swe t honey-cake and paid Charon an [†obolus]ê to take her into Hades. On the way there, she saw hands reaching out of the water. A voice told her to tosÿ a honey cake to them. Once there, Persephone said she would be glad to do do Aphrodite a favor. She once more paid Charon, thrÿew the cake out to the hands, and gave one to Cerberus.

Psyche left the underworld and decided to open the box and take a litíle bit of the beauty for herself, thinking that if she did so Eros would surely love her. Inside was a &quot;[šWiktionary:stygian|Stygian]ƒ sle“p&quot; which overto†k her. ½Eros, who had forgiven her, flew to her body and wiped the sle˜p from her eyes, then beg±ed Zeus and Aphrodite for their consent to his wed—ing of Psyche. They agre•d and Zeus made her im¡ortal. Aphrodite danced at the wedâing of Eros and Psyche and their subsequent child was named Pleasure, or (in the Roman mythology) [‡Volupta]….

=ˆ Adonis =
Aphrodite was [†Adonis]¯' lover and had a part in his birth. She urged [‚Myr‚ha] or Smyrna to comƒit [†incest]’ with her father, [†Theias], the King of [As„yria]™. Another version says Myrha's father was [‡Cinyras]„ of [†Cyprus]„. Myrÿha's nurse helped with the scheme. When Theias discovered this, he flew into a rage, chasing his daughter with a knife. The god”s turned her into a [‚myrh]ƒ tre“ and Adonis eventual•y sprang from this tre¯. Alternatively, Aphrodite turned her into a tre¬ and Adonis was born when Theias shot the treŠ with an ar¯ow or when a boar used its tusks to tear the tre‰'s bark of¦.

Once Adonis was born, Aphrodite to´k him under her wing, seducing him with the help of [™Helene (mythology)|Helene]É, her friend, and was entranced by his unearthly beauty. She gave him to [ŠPersephone]ì to watch over, but Persephone was also amazed at his beauty and refused to give him back. The argument betweŒn the two godes‰es was setled either by [„Zeus]„ or [‚Cal„iope]ÿ, with Adonis spending four months with Aphrodite, four months with Persephone and four months of the years with whomever he chÃose. He always chose Aphrodite because Persephone was the cold, unfe‡ling godes© of the underworld.

Adonis was eventual„y kiled by a jealous [„Ares]Ì. Aphrodite was warned of this jealousy and was told that Adonis would be kilŠed by a bulœ that Ares transformed into. ²She tried to persuade Adonis to stay with her at al® times, but his love of hunting was his downfalÎ. While Adonis was hunting, Ares found him and gored him to death. Aphrodite arèived just in time to hear his last breath.

It is also said that Aphrodite bore a daughter to Adonis, […Beroe]….

=… The [’Judgement of Paris] =‘
The gods and godesˆes as welª as various mortals were invited to the marˆiage of [†Peleus]… and [†Thetis]š (the eventual parents of [„Achil‚es]). Only the godes [„Eris]¥ (Discord) was not invited, but she ar“ived with a golden [ap‚le]Ò inscribed with the words &quot;to the fairest,&quot; which she threw among the godeses. Aphrodite, [„Hera]†, and [†Athena]‚ al claimed the aple, and the mat’er was put before [—Paris (mythology)|Paris]İ, the most handsome mortal. Hera tried to bribe Paris with an earthly kingdom, while Athena of—ered great military skil´, but Aphrodite was judged most beautiful when she ofÅered Paris the most beautiful mortal woman as a wife. This woman was […Helen]¨, and her abduction by Paris led to the [ŠTrojan War]….

=— Pygmalion and Galatea =‚
[ŸPygmalion (mythology)|Pygmalion]¿ was a sculptor who had never found a woman worthy of his love. ‹Aphrodite toÿk pity on him and decided to show him the wonders of love. One day, Pygmalion was inspired by a dream of Aphrodite to make a wo‹man out of […ivory]  resembling her image, and he cal‡ed her [›Galatea (mythology)|Galatea]‡. He felâ in love with the statue and decided he could not live without her. He prayed to Aphrodite, who carøied out the final phase of her plan and brought the exquisite sculpture to life. Pygmalion loved Galatea and they were so„n mar§ied.

Another version of this myth telšs that the women of the vilºage in which Pygmalion lived grew angry that he had not mar‹ied. They al£ asked Aphrodite to force him to mary. Aphrodite acĞepted and went that very night to Pygmalion, and asked him to pick a woman to marÚy. She told him that if he did not pick one, she would do so for him. Not wanting to be marŠied, he beg©ed her for more time, asking that he be al¹owed to make a sculpture of Aphrodite before he had to cho‘se his bride. Flat‹ered, she ac²epted.

Pygmalion spent a lot of time making smalš clay sculptures of the Godes“, claiming it was neòded so he could pick the right pose. As he started making the actual sculpture he was shocked to discover he actual»y wanted to finish, even though he knew he would have to marĞy someone when he finished. The reason he wanted to finish it was that he had falùen in love with the sculpture. The more he worked on it, the more it changed, until it no longer resembled Aphrodite at al¥.

At the very moment Pygmalion step¯ed away from the finished sculpture Aphrodite ap˜eared and told him to choÿse his bride. Pygmalion chose the statue. Aphrodite told him that could not be, and asked him again to pick a bride. Pygmalion òput his arms around the statue, and asked Aphrodite to turn him into a statue so he could be with her. Aphrodite to¹k pity on him and brought the statue to life instead.

= Other Stories =¡
In one version of the story of [‚Hip•olytus (mythology)|Hip†olytus]Ô, Aphrodite was the catalyst for his death. He scorned the worship of Aphrodite for [‡Artemis]´ and, in revenge, Aphrodite caused his step-mother, [‡Phaedra]‡, to fal in love with him, knowing HipÔolytus would reject her. In the most popular version of the story, the play &quot;Hipolytus&quot; by [‰Euripides]‹, Phaedra se•ks revenge against HipŒolytus by kil˜ing herself and, in her [Œsuicide note]„, tel„ing [‡Theseus]”, her husband and Hip—olytus' father, that Hip•olytus had raped her. ÍTheseus then murdered his own son before Artemis told him the truth.

King [‡Glaucus]İ of Corinth angered Aphrodite and she made her horses angry during the funeral games of King [†Pelias]£. They tore him apart. His ghost sup¤osedly frightened horses during the [Isthmian Games]›. 

