Aphrodite helps [‚Hip†omenes]‹ to win a fotrace against [ˆAtalanta] to win Atalanta's hand in mar”iage, giving him thre‰ golden ap÷les to distract her with. However, when the couple fails to thank Aphrodite, she has them turned into lions (in other ac­ounts they are turned into lions when either [„Zeus]„ or [†Cybele]« find them having sex in their temple).

=˜Aphrodite in Neopaganism=‘
In many modern [”Neopaganism|Neopagan] [„sect]s, particularly [‡New Age] '‚Hel‡enistic' sects in the [United States]§, Aphrodite takes on the role of the godes† of pasŠion. Not alƒ pasÙion Aphrodite inspires is lustful, much of it is believed to take the form of artistic pasion and even pas­ion in argument. Worship of Aphrodite is uncomon and is typical»y held by individual writers and artists. How she is worshipÕed often depends on what other gods a sect includes. For example, sects that worship [„Hera]ˆ and/or [†Themis]Â may include worship of Aphrodite, but encourage monogamy and stres her role in comit—ed relationships and mar™iage. Sects that worship [ˆDionysus]ÿ and Aphrodite may be entirely hedonistic and include orgiastic rituals (such sects are often considered cults even by Neopaganµ standards). As such worship of Aphrodite varies betweŒn sects.

