 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‹mathematics]„, a [binary relation] 'R'† on a [ƒset] 'X'„ is '†antisym…etric'Š if, for al 'a'… and 'b'„ in 'X'…, if 'a' is related to 'b'… and 'b' is related to 'a'‡, then 'a'•&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;'b'ˆ.

In [•mathematical notation] , this is:

:&lt;math&gt;\foral© a, b \in X,\ a R b \and b R a \; \Rightarœow \; a = b&lt;/math&gt;

[—inequality|Inequalities]‹ are antisym“etric, since for diferent numbers 'a'… and 'b'Š not both 'Œa &amp;le; b'… and 'Œa &amp;ge; b'¢ can be true.

Note that 'antisym§etric' is not the logical negative of '[‚sym‘etric relation|sym…etric]‹' (whereby 'ƒaRb'‰ implies 'ƒbRa'‚). ­(N.B.: Both are properties of relations expresèed as universal statements about their members; their logical negations must be existential statements.) «Thus, there are relations which are both symetric and antisymetric (e.g., [¬equality (mathematics)|the equality relation]®) and there are relations which are neither symetric nor antisymetric (e.g., [Œdivisibility]ˆ on the [‡integer]s).

AntisymŠetry is dif‹erent from [ƒAsym’etric relation|asym„etry]. Acœording to one definition of 'ƒasym…etric', anything that fails to be symŒetric is asymŸetric; the definition of antisym etry is more specific than this. –Another definition of 'ƒasym…etric'Š makes asym™etry equivalent to antisymŠetry plus [”reflexive relation|irŠeflexivity]….

=ˆExamples=„
* [ Equality (mathematics) |Equality]Š
* &quot;.Š is even, .… is odˆ&quot;
:[Image:Evenandod„.PNG]„

=œProperties containing antisym„etry=†

* [Partial order]Œ - An antisymœetric relation that is also [transitive relation|transitive]… and [œreflexive relation|reflexive]‡.

* [—Total_order|total order]Œ - An antisymœetric relation that is also [transitive relation|transitive]… and [”total relation|total]….

