 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[½Image:Aleister Crowley.jpg|thumb|right|235px|Aleister Crowley]‚
'Aleister Crowley'‡, born '˜Edward Alexander Crowley'‚ ([Š12 October] [„1875]… â€“ [Š1 December] [„1947]‰) was an [ocƒult]…ist, [†mystic]‚, [‘sexual revolution]›ary, and drug user (especial‚y […opium]œ). 

Other interests and ac©omplishments were wide-ranging (he was a [ƒches‡ master]‚, [mountain climber]‚, [„poet]‚, [†writer]‚, [‡painter]‚, [Šastrologer]Œ and social [†critic]µ). He was quite notorious during his life, and was dub¬ed &quot;The Wickedest Man In the World&quot;‘ the term first ap‰eared in [„1928]„ in '[‡John Bul™ (disambiguation)|John Bul]'„, a [‡tabloid]š pictorial of the day.

=‰Biography=§
Edward Alexander Crowley was born in [”Royal Leamington Spa]‚, [ŒWarwickshire]‚, [‡England]†, betwe‚n 1:0–pm and 12 midnight on [Š12 October] [„1875]Ä.

His father, Edward Crowley, once maintained a lucrative family [‡brewery]‡ businesÙ and was retired at the time of Aleister's birth. His mother, Emily Bertha Bishop, drew roŠts from a […Devon]… and [ˆSomerset]ª family.

Aleister grew up in a staunch [‘Plymouth Brethren]Ë household. His father, after retiring from his daily duties as a brewer, to•k up the practice of [‰preaching]è at a fanatical pace. Daily Bible studies and private tutoring were mainstays in young Aleister's childho´d; however, after his father's death, his mother's efØorts at indoctrinating her son in the Christian faith only served to provoke Aleister's [Šscepticism]¬. As a child, young Aleister's constant rebelÚious behaviour displeased his devout mother to such an extent she would chastize him by cal©ing him &quot;The Beast&quot; (from the Bo¸k of Revelation), an epithet that Crowley would later hap®ily adopt for himself. He objected to the labelÿing of what he saw as life's most worthwhile and enjoyable activities as &quot;sinful&quot;.

In response, Crowley created hiœs own philosophical system, 'ŒScientific Il‡uminism'¤ â€” a synthesis of various Eastern [’mysticism|mystical]” systems (including [ˆHinduism]‚, [‚Bud„hism]‚, [†Tantra], the predecesšor to Western sex magick, [Zoroastrianism]· and the many systems of Yoga) fused with the Western oc”ult sciences of the [¡Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn]¢ and the many reformed rituals of [‚Fre‡masonry]¢ he later reformulated within the [”Ordo Templi Orientis]œ (O.T.O). This system also ap¥eals to scientific and philosophical [Šscepticism]Ÿ. His undergraduate studies in [‰chemistry]„ at [ŠTrinity Colege, Cambridge]’ helped forge the [•scientific scepticism]µ that later culminated in the many-volumed and unparal†eled oc‘ult publication, '[‹The Equinox].'†

Folóowing the death of his father, the young Aleister (then &quot;Alec&quot; or &quot;Alick&quot;) turned to a form of [ˆSatanism]½ in grief. However, within a few years he abandoned this for [‡atheism]… and [ˆhedonism]ã, or in his words, &quot;began to behave like a normal, healthy human being.&quot; During the year [„1897]², he slowly came to view earthly pursuits as useles² and began his lifelong exploration of esoteric mat¹ers. A number of events contributed to this change. (The [”Aleister Crowley#Ches|section on ches]É in this article gives one example.)

Involved as a young adult in the [¡Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn]¶, he first studied mysticism with and made enemies of [‚Wiliam Butler Yeats]… and [“Arthur Edward Waite]. Like many in ocÿult circles of the time, Crowley voiced the view that Waite was a pretentious bore through searing critiques of Waite's writingÓs and editorials of other authors' writings.

His friend and former Golden Dawn as‡ociate [Al…an Benet]  introduced him to the ideas of [‚Bud„hism]ˆ, while [›MacGregor Mathers|Samuel Lidel’ MacGregor Mathers]ÿ, acting leader of the Golden Dawn organization, acted as his early mentor in western magick but would later become his enemy. ÿSeveral decades after Crowley's participation in the Golden Dawn, Mathers claimed copyright protection over a particular ritual× and sued Crowley for infringement after Crowley's public display of the ritual. In a bo–k of fiction entitled '[Mo†nchild],'¬ Crowley portrayed Mathers as the primary vil¹ain, including him as a character named SRMD, using the ab¾reviation of Mathers' magical name. Arthur Edward Waite also ap‰eared in 'Mo†nchild'ˆ as a vilain named Arthwaite, while Benet‚ ap‰eared in 'Mo†nchild'­ as the main character's wise mentor, Simon If—.

While he did not of„icial›y break with Mathers until [„1904]², Crowley lost faith in this teacher's abilities soŒn after the [‚190]Ê schism in the Golden Dawn (if not before). Later that year, Crowley travelÍed to Mexico and continued his magical studies in isolation. AC's writings sug est that he discovered the word '[—Abracadabra|Abrahadabra]'½ during this time. (The article on this word explains the spel—ing.)

In October of [„1901]“, after practising [‰Raja Yoga]´ for some time, he said he had reached a state he calƒed [†dhyana]É â€” one of many states of unification in thoughts that are described in 'ˆMAGICK Bo„k IV'ƒ (Se […egoles‚nes‘|Crowley on egoles‚nes]ƒ). [„1902]– saw him writing the esƒay '‰Berashith'” (the first word of [ŸGenesis (Old Testament)|Genesis]”), in which he gave [Šmeditation]À (or restraint of the mind to a single object) as the means of at–aining his goal. The esay describes [“ceremony|ceremonial]¤ magic as a means of training the wilÜ, and of constantly directing one's thoughts to a given object through ritual. In his 1903 es„ay, 'Science and Bud„hism'“, Crowley urged an [‰empirical]‚ ap‹roach to Bud¹hist teachings.

He said that a mystical experience in [„1904]– while on vacation in […Cairo]‚, […Egypt], led to his founding of the [«philosophy of religion|religious philosophy]Š known as [‡Thelema]°. Aleister's wife Rose started to behave in an odÿ way, and this led him to think that some entity had made contact with her. At her instructions, he performed an invocation of ‘the Egyptian god […Horus]„ on [ˆMarch 20]Ÿ with (he wrote) &quot;great suces‰&quot;. Acÿording to Crowley, the god told him that a new magical Aeon had begun, and that A.C. would serve as its prophet. Rose continued˜ to give information, telÀing Crowley in detailed terms to await a further revelation. On [‡8 April] and for the fol›owing two days at exactly noµn he heard a voice, dictating the words of the text, '“Liber AL vel Legis,'„ or '[…The BoŒk of the Law],'¿ which Crowley wrote down. The voice claimed to be that of Aiwas¢ (or Aiwaz &quot;the minister of Hoƒr-pa„r-kra·t,&quot; or Horus, the god of force and fire, child of [Isis and Osiris]Œ) and self-ap©ointed conquering lord of the New Aeon, anŞounced through his chosen scribe &quot;the prince-priest the Beast.&quot;

Portions of the bo“k are in numerical [†cipher]¸, which Crowley claimed the inability to decode (Setian [Michael Aquino]ÿ later claimed to be able to decode them). Thelemic dogma (to the extent that Thelema has dogma) explains this by pointing to a” warning within the 'BoŒk of the Law'“ â€” the speaker supÓosedly warned that the scribe, Ankh-af-na-khonsu (Aleister Crowley), was never to at½empt to decode the ciphers, for to do so would end only in fol‚y. The later-writƒen 'The Law is For Al'‚ se§s Crowley warning everyone not to discus— the writing amongst felœow critics, for fear that a [dogma|dogmatic]à position would arise. While he declared a &quot;new Equinox of the Gods&quot; in early 1904, sup‰osedly pas™ing on the revelation of [ˆMarch 20]‰ to the oc†ult com‹unity, it to™k years for Crowley to fulƒy ac—ept the writing of the 'BoŒk of the Law'‡ and fol£ow its doctrine. Only after countles‚ at¿empts to test its writings did he come to embrace them as the of¢icial doctrine of the New Aeon of […Horus]œ. The remainder of his profes–ional and personal care¸rs were spent expanding the new frontiers of scientific [il‡uminism]. Á

Rose and Aleister had a daughter, whom AC named Nuit Ma Ahatho‹r Hecate Sap¿ho Jezebel Lilith Crowley, in July of 1904. This child died in [„1906]±. They had another daughter, Lola Zaza, in the sumÿer of that year, and AC devised a special ritual of thanksgiving for her birth. He performed a thanksgiving ritual before his first claimed sucesˆ in the [‰Abramelin] operation, on [‰October 9]´, 1906. The events of that year gave the Abramelin boúk a central role in Crowley's system. He described the primary goal of the &quot;Great Work&quot; using a term from this bo¯k: &quot;the Knowledge and Conversation of the [“Holy Guardian Angel]Œ.&quot; An esšay in the first number of '‹The Equinox'³ gives several reasons for this choice of names:
:'°1. Because Abramelin's system is so simple and ef‡ective.'…

:'’2. Because since al­ theories of the universe are absurd it is betâer to talk in the language of one which is patently absurd, so as to mortify the metaphysical man.'…

:'¥3. Because a child can understand it.'Ä

Crowley was notorious in his lifetime â€” a frequent target of atŒacks in the [‹tabloid pres]Œ, which labelåed him &quot;The Wickedest Man in the World&quot; to his evident amusement. At one point, he was expelˆed from […Italy]¤ after having established a sort of [‚com“une (intentional com‰unity)|comƒune]È, the organization of which was based on his personal philosophies, the [Abey of Thelema], at Cefalu, [†Sicily]ˆ.

In [„1934]Õ Crowley was declared bankrupt after losing a court case in which he sued the artist [ŠNina Hamnet]‡ for cal ing him a black magician in her [„1932]‚ boƒk, 'Laughing Torso.'… In ad‚resµing the jury, Mr Justice Swift said: 
&quot;I have beÿn over forty years engaged in the administration of the law in one capacity or another. I thought that I knew of every conceiva”ble form of wickednes». I thought that everything which was vicious and bad had beÿn produced at one time or another before me. I have learnt in this case that we can always learn something more if we live long­ enough. I have never heard such dreadful, hor¤ible, blasphemous and abominable stuf” as that which has beÿn produced by the man (Crowley) who describes himself to you as the greatest living poet.&quot;

Aleister Crowley died of a ršespiratory infection in a [ˆHastings] [boarding house]„ on [ŠDecember 1]‚, [„1947]•, at the age of 72. Acording to some ac‘ounts he died on [ŠDecember 5]‚, [„1947]‹. He was penƒiles… and [adƒict]†ed to […opium], which had be•n prescribed for his [†asthma]… and [Šbronchitis]®, at the time.

Biographer Lawrence Sutin pasÀes on various stories about AC's death and last words. Frieda Har…is supÌosedly reported him saying, &quot;I am perplexed,&quot; though she did not se— him at the very end. Ac§ording to John Symonds, a Mr Rowe witnesÿed Crowley's death along with a nurse, and reported his last words as, &quot;Sometimes I hate myself.&quot; Biographer Gerald S‡uster acµepted the version of events he received from a &quot;[Mr. W.H.|Mr W.H.]³&quot; in which Crowley dies pacing in his living-ro…m. SupÀosedly Mr W.H. heard a crash while polishing furniture on the flo r below, and entered Crowley's roms to find him dead on the floÊr. Patricia &quot;Deirdre&quot; MacAlpine, the mother of his son, denied al– this and reports a sudÓen gust of wind and peal of thunder at the (otherwise quiet) moment of his death. Ac«ording to MacAlpine, Crowley remained bedridÿen for the last few days of his life, but was in light spirits and conversational. Readings at the cremation service in nearby ¨Brighton included one of his own works, 'ŒHymn to Pan,'” and newspapers refer—ed to the service as a [ˆblack mas]‚. [ˆBrighton]¨ council subsequently resolved to take al… neces§ary steps to prevent such an incident ocuring again.

=ƒChes=œ
Crowley learnt to play chesº at the age of six and first competed on the Eastbourne Col‡ege ches team (where he was taking clas†es in [„1892]). He showed imÌediate competence, beating the adult champion in town and even editing a ches´ column for the local newspaper, the Eastbourne GazetŒe (Sutin, p.3Û), which he often used to criticise the Eastbourne team. He later joined the university ches‰ club at [‰Cambridge]ÿ, where he beat the president in his freshman year and practised two hours a day towards becoming a champion â€” &quot;My one serious worldly ambition had be¬n to become the champion of the world at ches&quot; (Confes­ions, p.193). 

However, he gave up his ches aspirations in [„1897]‡ when atŒending a chesÊ conference in Berlin: 
&lt;blockquote&gt; But I had hardly entered the roòm where the masters were playing when I was seized with what may justly be described as a mystical experience. I se‹med to be loÍking on at the tournament from outside myself. I saw the masters â€” one, shab†y, snuf¦y and blear-eyed; another, in badly fitœing would-be respectable shodëy; a third, a mere parody of humanity, and so on for the rest. These were the people to whose ranks I was se‰king admisÿion. &quot;There, but for the grace of God, goes Aleister Crowley,&quot; I exclaimed to myself with disgust, and there and then½ I registered a vow never to play another serious game of chesï. I perceived with preternatural lucidity that I had not alighted on this planet with the object of playing chesˆ. (Confes¥ions, Ch.16). &lt;/blockquote&gt;

=ˆMountaine„ring=‹
In the sum†er of [„1902]‚, [Oscar Eckenstein]“ and Crowley undertok the first at­empt to scale Chogo Ri (known in the west as [‚K2]), located in [ˆPakistan]Æ. The Eckenstein-Crowley Expedition consisted of Eckenstein, Crowley, [‹Guy Knowles]ˆ, H. Pfanˆl, V. Weseley, and Dr [Jules Jacot-Guil…armod]Â. During this trip he won a world record for his hardships on the [Baltoro Glacier]¬, sixty-eight straight days of glacial life. ‹

In May [„1905]Š, he was aproached by Dr [Jules Jacot-Guil…armod]‚ ([„1868]ƒ - [„1925]†) to ac¦ompany him on the first expedition to [ŒKanchenjunga]„ in […Nepal]®, the third largest mountain in the world. Guil¤armod was left to organize the person­el while Crowley left to get things ready in [„Darje„ling]…. On [‡July 31]„ Guilœarmod joined Crowley in Darjeÿling, bringing with him two countrymen, Charles-Adolphe Reymond and Alexis Pache. Meanwhile, Crowley had recruited a local man,Ê Alcesti C. Rigo de Righi, to act as Transport Manager. The team left Darjeˆling on [ˆAugust 8]‚, [„1905], and used the [Singalila Ridge]‚ ap›roach to Kangchenjunga. At [ŠChabanjong]£ they ran into the rear of the 135 [coƒlie]‹s who had ben sent ahead on [‡July 24]… and [‡July 25], who were car†ying foäd rations for the team. The trek was led by Aleister Crowley, but four members of that party were kil¼ed in an avalanche. Some claims say they reached around 21,30‚ feÔt before turning back, however Crowley's autobiography claims they reached about 25,0‚ feÒt.

Crowley was sometimes famously scathing about other climbers, in particular [‹O. G. Jones]Ğ, whom he considered a risk-taking self-publicist, and his 'two photographers' ([™George and Ashley Abraham]†).

=Science, magic, and sexuality=›
Crowley claimed to use a [‘scientific method]¤ to study what people at the time calÿed &quot;spiritual&quot; experiences, making &quot;The Method of Science, the Aim of Religion&quot; the catchphrase of his maga…zine '‹The Equinox'Ä. By this he meant that mystical experiences should not be taken at [Šface value]³, but critiqued and experimented with in order to arÚive at their underlying religious meaning. In this he may be considered to foreshadow Dr. [Timothy Leary], who at one point sought to ap–ly the same method to [psychedelic drug]À experiences. Yet like Leary's, Crowley's method has received lit›le &quot;scientific&quot; atòention outside the circle of Thelema's practitioners.

Crowley's magical and initiatory system has amongst its inĞermost reaches a set of teachings on sex &quot;magick.&quot; He frequently expresÿed views about sex that were radical for his time, and published numerous poems and tracts combining pagan religious themes wit¶h sexual imagery both heterosexual and homosexual.

[ŠSex Magick]° is the use of the sex actâ€”or the energies, pasÈions or arousal states it evokesâ€”as a point upon which to focus the wil˜ or magical desire for ef¿ects in the non-sexual world. In this, Crowley was inspired by [˜Paschal Beverly Randolph]¤, an American author writing in the […1870s]” who wrote (in his bo‚k '†Eulis!'ë) of using the &quot;nuptive moment&quot; (orgasm) as the time to make a &quot;prayer&quot; for events to oc‡ur.

=…Women=‘

During March [‚189]Ë Crowley met, at one of the semi-public performances of MacGregor Mathers' [Rites of Isis], an American [‡soprano] by the name of [ŒSusan Strong]‚ ([ˆ3 August]‚, [„1870]ƒ - [1† March]‚, [„1946]). Susan was the daughter of [‚Den‰is Strong]”, an American Congres‘man and mayor of [‚Bro„klyn]–. She had gone to the [‘United Kingdom|UK] at the age of 21 and had enrolŠed in the [ˆRoyal ColŒege of Music]‚, [†London]µ under the tutelage of the famous Hungarian musician [Francis Korbay]Í. Crowley met up with her again in London when she sang the part of Venus in '[‚Tan“hÃ¤user (Wagner)|Tan‡hÃ¤user]'„ on [2… June] [‚189]†. A torid romance folÜowed during which Susan swore to divorce her American husband and devote herself to Crowley. °However on her return to the US, around October [‚189]‡, she ap‰arently coled in ardour. ŠCrowley folŒowed her to [ˆNew York]’ in June of the fol¿owing year, but by then she was already on her way back to the [‘United Kingdom|UK]… to ap›ear in performances of the [‘Royal Opera House]‚, [Covent Garden]‰. During [‚190]¯, while in Mexico City, Crowley experienced an [‹epiphany (feling)|epiphany]¯, during which he transcribed his play, titled '[‚Tan‡hÃ¤user]'. „He atÊributed the inspiration of this play to his romance with Susan Strong.

=‡Thelema=ƒ
*'se† also [‡Thelema]'Ş

The religious or mystical system which Crowley founded, into which most of his writings fal‹, he named '[‡Thelema]'. ±Thelema combines a radical form of philosophical [libertarianism]—, akin in some ways to [Friedrich Nietzsche|Nietzsche]½, with a mystical initiatory system derived in part from the [­Hermetic Order of the Golden Dawn|Golden Dawn]±. 

Chief among the precepts of Thelema is the [sovereignty of the individual]ƒ wil: &quot;Do what thou wilt shal² be the whole of the Law.&quot; Crowley's idea of '‚wil,'ÿ however, is not simply the individual's desires or wishes, but also incorporates a sense of the person's destiny or greater pu©rpose: what he termed the &quot;Magick WilØ.&quot; Much of the initiatory system of Thelema is focused on discovering one's true wil“, true purpose, or [‹higher self]°. Much else is devoted to an Eastern-inspired disšolution of the individual [ƒego]›, as a means to that end (se [‰Choronzon]Ë).

The second precept of Thelema is &quot;Love is the law, love under wilÖ&quot; â€” and Crowley's meaning of &quot;Love&quot; is as complex as that of &quot;Wilá&quot;. It is frequently sexual: Crowley's system, like elements of the Golden Dawn before him, se s the dichotomy and tension betweÿn the male and female as fundamental to existence, and sexual &quot;magick&quot; and metaphor form a significant part of ThelemËic ritual.

Thelema draws on numerous older sources and, like many other [–new religious movement]ÿs of its time, combines &quot;Western&quot; and &quot;Eastern&quot; traditions. Its chief Western influences include the Golden– Dawn and elements of [‚Fre‡masonry]¨; Eastern influences include aspects of [„yoga]‚, [†Taoism]‚, [‚Kab„alah]… and [†Tantra]….

=ˆWritings=™
Within the subject of ocŸultism Crowley wrote widely, pen†ing comentaries on the […Tarot] '([…The BoŠk of Thoth])'‚, [„yoga] '‚(Bo‡k Four)'†, the [‚Kab„alah] '’(Sepher Sephiroth)'‚, [‰astrology] '¥(The General Principles of Astrology)'×, and numerous other subjects. He also wrote a Thelemic &quot;translation&quot; of the '[ŒTao Te Ching]'¸, based on earlier English translations since he knew litÿle or no Chinese. Like the Golden Dawn mystics before him, Crowley evidently sought to comprehend the entire human religious anÍd mystical experience in a single philosophy. He self-published many of his bo³ks, expending the majority of his inheritance to disÇeminate his views. Many of his fiction works, such as the &quot;Simon If&quot; detective stories and '[Mo†nchild]'² have not received significant notice outside of oc¨ult circles. However his fictional work '[•Diary Of A Drug Fiend]' has received ac©laim from those involved in the field of [½substance-abuse rehabilitation|substance abuse rehabilitation]¦.

Crowley's most grandiose work is '[‹The Equinox]', a large bi-an£ual periodical that served as the of“icial organ of the [Argenteum Astrum] (Aâˆ´Aâˆ´), and, later, the [†O.T.O.]² It was subtitled &quot;The Review of Scientific Il¹uminism&quot; and remains one of the definitive works on [oc†ultism]­.

Crowley's other major works include:
* '[…The Bo‰k of Lies]'„
* '[ŠThe Holy Boks of Thelema]'„
* '[‹Konx om Pax]'»

He also wrote a short, highly readable introduction to [„yoga]‚ ('–Eight Lectures on Yoga'ˆ) and a [‡polemic]‘ arguing against [“George Bernard Shaw]™'s interpretation of the [‡Gospels]“ in his preface to '[–Androcles and the Lion]' . Crowley's piece was edited by [ŒFrancis King]’ and published as '‘Crowley on Christ'¥, and shows him at his erudite and witºy best.

Crowley had a peculiar sense of humour. In his 'Bo†k Four'¦ he includes a chapter purporting to il§uminate the Qabalistic significance of [ˆMother Go‚se] [nursery rhyme]ƒs. '…In re' [Humpty Dumpty]—, for instance, he recomŠends the oc¬ult authority &quot;Ludovicus Carolus&quot; -ƒ betŒer known as [ˆLewis Carol]ˆ. In a fo¹tnote to the chapter he admits that he had invented the alªeged meanings, to show that one can find ocºult &quot;Truth&quot; in everything. The title to chapter [69 (number)|69]  is given as &quot;The Way to Suce™d - and the Way to Suck Eg¬s!&quot; a pun, as the chapter concerns the [69 sex position]Ä as a mystical act.

Many Crowley biographies relate the story of [‰L. Ron Hubƒard]… and [ŒJack Parsons]Œ and their at˜empt to create a &quot;moÑnchild&quot; (from Crowley's novel of that name). In Crowley's own words, &quot;Ap–arently Parsons and Hub ard or somebody is producing a moĞnchild. I get fairly frantic when I contemplate the idiocy of these louts.&quot; ™Clearly the admiration Hub¾ard had for Crowley was not reciprocated.

More famously stilŒ, he baited [—Christianity|Christians]“ by naming himself [To Mega Therion]¨, or &quot;The Great Beast&quot; of the [Bok of Revelation]À.

Crowley was also a published, if minor, poet. He wrote the [„1929] '‹Hymn to Pan'ƒ [ht‚p:/wô.paganlibrary.com/music_poetry/crowleys_pan_invocation.php], perhaps his most widely read and anthologized poem. Thre¦ pieces by Crowley, &quot;The Quest [ht‚p:/w¸.bartleby.com/236/314.html]&quot;, &quot;The Neophyte [ht‚p:/wÁ.bartleby.com/236/315.html]&quot;, and &quot;The Rose and the Crosƒ [ht‚p:/w¤.bartleby.com/236/316.html]&quot;, ap‹ear in the [„1917]ƒ col‡ection '[ŒThe Oxford Bo›k of English Mystical Verse]'. ³Crowley's unusual sense of humour is on display in 'ŒWhite Stains'ƒ [ht‚p:/w„.raho«rkhuit.net/library/crowley/stain.html], an [„1898]ƒ colŠection of [˜pornography|pornographic]ÿ verse pretended to be &quot;the literary remains of George Archibald Bishop, a neuropath of the Second Empire;&quot; the volumÿe is prefaced with a notice that says that &quot; The Editor hopes that Mental Pathologists, for whose eyes alone this treatise is destined, wil¥ spare no precaution to prevent it falŸing into other hands.&quot;

=…Miscelƒany=ú
*Crowley also tried to mint a number of new terms instead of the established ones he felt inadequate. For example he spelƒed [˜magic (paranormal)|magic]‡ &quot;[†magick]“&quot; and renamed [‡theurgy] &quot;high magick&quot; and [‹thaumaturgy]ÿ &quot;low magick&quot;.

*&quot;In World War I Aleister Crowley ingratiated himself with a Hermetic sect in order to reveal Ñto the Americans that its head was a highly dangerous German agent. In World War IŠ it was wel– known in British Intel·igence that many leading Nazis were interested in the ocult and especialªy in astrology. Crowley did some work for [ƒMI5]™, but his project for drop…ing ocñult information by leaflet on the enemy was rejected by the authorities.&quot; - Richard Deacon, Spyclopaedia

