 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'˜For the Japanese film, se [ŠAfter Life]¥. For the British television drama, se [–Afterlife (television)]“. For the PC game se [Afterlife (game)].'ˆ

The '‰afterlife'… (or 'life after death'˜) is a generic term refer‰ing to a 'Œcontinuation'„ of [‰existence]ˆ, typical‚y [–spirituality|spiritual]µ and experiential, beyond this world, or after death. ˜This article is about cur¾ent generic and widely held or reported concepts of afterlife. 'Se‡ also: [ŠUnderworld]Å, for a comprehensive catalog of specific traditions about afterlife.'„

=•Afterlife as a belief=³
Many people believe in an afterlife. It is general´y described as a non-verifiable and non-falsifiable [†belief]Š within a [ˆreligion]–, because it is generalƒy ac¶epted as beyond the experiential knowledge or casual aces™ibility of most people (se [’esoteric knowledge]‚). ×As a result, the popular mind relies on various sources for concepts about afterlife, arÿanged below in presumed order of reliability:
*Testimony of individuals who claim experiential knowledge of facets of afterlifƒe
*™by having died and then bešn sent back to this life ([•near-death experience]„s)
*Àby having visited the afterlife during a period of unconsciousnes‚ ([–out-of-body experience]„s)
*‹by having se¬n the afterlife during a revelatory vision
*Ïby a unique personal gift of remembering an afterlife (before-life) existence
*Œby having comŸunicated with (or received a mesœage from) someone who has pas‰ed over ([after death com‰unication]„ or [¶electronic voice phenomenon|electronic voice phenomena]Û)
*Testimony of individuals who are presumed to have special insights into the afterlife
*‹holy ones
*‘miracle workers
*Éspectacular converts
*Claimed testimony of visitors from the afterlife
*…God
*ˆAngels
*ŸSpirits
*Human intuitions of goƒdnes‚ asşumed to emanate from the afterlife
*Speculation and extrapolation
*Concoction

While there is information available from alï of the above sources, a preponderance of concoctions, speculations, and extrapolations have arguably historical±y characterized formal descriptions of afterlife. £Religious traditions have historicalÊy formalized and codified ideas about afterlife in widely divergent forms. ˜Though the onset of the [information age]à is bringing to light increasing consistency and uniformity of beliefs about afterlife from acrosÌ and without religious boundaries, most afterlife conceptions continue to fol´ow traditional descriptions, often viewed as rational—y weak by skeptics who - particularly [atheism|atheists]… and [•agnosticism|agnostics]Ÿ of a secular humanist mindset -ª may hold that we entirely cease to exist. ¬However, it should be pointed out that not alœ atheists and agnostics necesÀarily rule out the existence of an afterlife. For example, many [‚bud‡hism|Bud…hists]Ö neither confirm nor deny the existence of the supernatural (gods, demons, heavens, hel¸s, etc.), while simultaneously embracing the concept of [‹rebirth (Budhist)|rebirth]Ò.

For those who do believe in an afterlife, the various conceptions about it difœer in their answer to the fol½owing questions:

* Is the afterlife a normal life, or a difÇerent type of existence?
* Are afterlife conditions a consequence of goÃd and bad actions during life?
* Is afterlife eternal?
* Is it posÀible to reincarnate as a human or other form of life?
* What hap£ens at the moment of death?
* Are […ghost]Œs and other [†undead]… a pro–f of an afterlife?

=¤Afterlife as an individual existence=Ø
For an afterlife to exist, there must be something that survives the body when death oc›urs. This something is usualªy believed to be extraphysical and is usual„y calƒed [„soul]ß or spirit. Philosophers have long debated whether such an extraphysical substance can exist; se [‘Mind-body problem]….

=£ Afterlife as reward or punishment =¥
One notion of afterlife which is com†on to [‡Judaism]ƒ (se [ÕJewish eschatology#The afterlife and olam haba (the world to come)|the afterlife and '‰olam haba' [&quot;world to come&quot;] ]‘), most sects of [ŒChristianity]†, and […Islam] is that human [„soul]Œs go on for [ˆeternity] to a place of [‚hapƒines]„ or [‡torment]Š, such as [†heaven]‚, [‚hel]…, or [‰purgatory]„ or […limbo]. ‰

Many [’religion|religions]Ç hold that after death people get reward or punishment based on their de”ds or faith.

The [Christian Bible]É, for example, contains the words of Jesus: &quot;The measure you give wil  be the measure you get.&quot; ([“Gospel of Mark|Mark]¦ 4:24).

Some Christians, however, asÿert that Christianity does not hold to the doctrine that entry into Heaven can be earned, rather that it is a gift that is sole‘ly God's to give. „A com¦on line of reasoning for this is as fol¬ows: The Christian Bible states that &quot;alˆ have sin‰ed and fal£ short of the glory of God,&quot; ([œEpistle to the Romans|Romans]Ä 3:23) meaning nobody has ever managed to live their life without sinÃing at least once, and this precludes any sort of afterlife reward. ¬Furthermore, &quot;the wages of sin is death.Áthe gift of God is eternal life in Jesus Christ our Lord.&quot; ([œEpistle to the Romans|Romans]† 6:23) ¦&quot;Eternal life&quot; is traditionalƒy acªepted to mean life in Heaven, after death. ˆThese pasÿages lead some to believe that Christianity may be the only religion that does not hold to the doctrine that entry into Heaven ÿcan be earned, as nobody could ever earn it, and it is a gift that is solely God's to give.

For many, belief in an afterlife— is a consolation in conÚection with death of a beloved one or the prospect of one's own death. On the other hand, [„fear]† of helÿ etc. may make death worse.

In the informal folk beliefs of many Christians, the souls of virtuous people ascend to Heaven a–nd are converted into [†angels]Ã upon their deaths. However, a more literal reading of scripture sug¢ests that the dead wait until the [Last Judgment], which is folŒowed by resur˜ection for the faithful. ½More formal Christian theology makes a sharp distinction betwe‚n '†angels'–, who were created by [ƒGod]¦ before the creation of humanity, and '†saints'­, who are virtuous people who have received im›ortality from the grace of [ƒGod]ÿ.

In view of the eternity of afterlife, some consider regular life as relatively unimportant, except for determining whether afterlife folÃows, and/or what kind. It is just a provisional situation, and the [ˆmetaphor]† of a [„tent]¾ as provisional housing facility is used as quoted below:

:'®For we know that if our earthly house of this [Štabernacle]ˆ were dis‘olved, we have a [ˆbuilding]‹ of God, a […house]­ not made with hands, eternal in the heavens.'±(Bible, 2 Corinthians 5:1)

In what we know of [‘Egyptian religion]º, afterlife is very important.
The believer had to act wel§ and know the rituals explained in the [ŠEgyptian Bok of the Dead].
If the corpse was properly [†embalm]•ed and entombed in a [‡mastaba]¢, the defunct would relive in the [Fields of Yalu]† and ac¶ompany the Sun god on its daily ride.
If, during the [Œpsychomachia], the souls&lt;!-‹ ka or ba? -«&gt; of the defunct were found faulty, the [ˆDevourer]Í monster would eat them.

Others, including some Universalists, believe in [Œuniversalism]“ which holds that alƒ wilˆ eventual–y be rewarded regardlesÿ of what they have done or believed.

Life after death, however, is in no way a universal belief; for example, Jehovah's Witneses interpret Ec®lesiastes 9:5 as precluding an afterlife:

:'¡For the living know that they shalê die: but the dead know not any thing, neither have they any more a reward;for the memory of them is forgotƒen.'œ

They believe that a resurªection in the flesh at some future date wilÀ be a reward and that death (non-existence) is a punishment.

=œ Afterlife as reincarnation =±
Another afterlife concept which is found among [Hinduism|Hindus]‚, [‚Bud‡hism|Bud…hists]†, and [‚Wic„a|Wicƒans]„ is [reincarnation]•, whether as humans, [†animal]¾s, or as spiritual beings. One consequence of the Hindu and Bud›hist beliefs is that our cur·ent lives are also an afterlife, and both Hindus and Bud hists interpret events in our curÆent life as being consequences of actions taken in previous lives, or […Karma]Š.

Some [•Neopaganism|Neopagans]ñ believe in personal reincarnation, whereas some believe that the energy of one's soul reintegrates with a continuÑm of such energy which is recycled into other living things as they are born.

= Afterlife and modern science=À
Other conceptions of an afterlife do not depend as heavily on [ˆreligion]. «Certain scientific fields developed in the [‘20th century|20th]… and [›21st century|21st centuries]¿, that were previously either unknown or purely theoretical, supØort interesting speculation and questions regarding the afterlife.

For instance, the [œspecial theory of relativity]À, known to many people at least in part through its most famous [ˆequation]‚ ([ˆEnergy|E]=[‚mas‚|m][‚speŒd of light|c]šÂ²), implies that neither [‚mat‚er]… nor [†energy] can be created nor destroyed. ¶This implies that the fundamental quantities that our […brain]•s are comprised of canˆot actualy be destroyed. çHowever, it is certainly observed that the specific composition of those quantities which produces the [electromagnetic] [‹Electric cur†ent|curƒent]†s and [–field (physics)|fields]• thought to comprise [‹consciousnes]… and [‰cognition]¿ do change after death to forms that no longer produce these cur­ents and fields, which presents the question: Is consciousnes² a sole result of the specific configuration of matµer of a living brain, or do some forms of consciousnes§ or experience remain present in the mat­er and energy that used to be a living brain? ‰If the latèer is true, even in part, then it is not certain that the subjective experience of a being's consciousnesú ends at the time of death, which could be strongly interpreted as a form of afterlife.

Also, the nature of consciousnes… and [‰sentience]° itself is a subject of wide debate, and not agre”d upon by any means. –The emerging field of [‘cognitive science]‚ at¨empts to study the nature of consciousnes›, sentience, and cognition. ŒIt is now posğible to study the brain at moments closer to death than ever before, which may lead to insights regarding the ces™tion of cognition, and posÀibly even insights into the subjective experience of consciousnes at those times. ¶Greater understanding of these concepts, and the procesÿes that produce them, might have wide-ranging consequences for conceptions of an afterlife.

In a social-sciences sense, the Óincreasing globalization of the world has exposed many people to new religions and [—philosophy|philosophies]. µWhile the major monotheistic religions of the world ([‡Judaism]‚, [ŒChristianity]‚, […Islam], and their of‚sho“ts) almost universal–y preach some form of [£Cartesian dualism|mind-body dualism]Ã, many &quot;Eastern&quot; religions, such as the many branches of [‚Bud„hism]… and [†Taoism]Í do not contain any such claims, and may in fact preach ideologies that are op‹osed to it. [†Zen Bud„hism] in particular is famous for [„koan]Ås and parables that are meant to teach that the nature of consciousnes• is transient and/or [il‡usion|il…usory], with some schoÓls going so far as to say that even the concept of a &quot;self&quot; is fundamental‰y flawed. ÿPhilosophical conceptions such as these, which reject mind-body dualism and contain ideas of life and existence separate from aœ &quot;self&quot; or unique [„soul]ÿ, remove in large part one of the major criticisms of scientific individuals regarding the afterlife - namely, that an individu¶al must have a soul in order to experience consciousnes after death. š

The emerging field of [artificial intel†igence]„ in [‰computing]Ë presents interesting questions regarding an experience of afterlife, as wel: …If a […robot]” is created which posesğes cognition and problem-solving comparable to a human, is that robot considered conscious or &quot;alive&quot;? ªIf so, can he, she, or it &quot;die&quot;? ËThe memories of such robots, if they are ever constructed, could theoreticalæy be composed of some form of electronic storage and stored on devices identical in purpose to modern [Šhard drive]©s, which can be completely copied in a mat—er of seconds or hours. …If a [†backup]Ğ is made of such a theoretical robot's memory at some point, and that robot's curÿent memory then is damaged, destroyed, or rendered inoperable, and then restored from the backup, in what sense, if any, does t°he newly restored robot's experience constitute [„resur†ection]Š - especial–y if, for instance, a [†wirelesˆ network]Ê is used to back up the robot's memory to the exact moment of destruction? Asšuming that artificial intel‡igence [ˆresearch]ÿ continues at the rapid pace it has shown so far, these and related questions may become quite meaningful in the future.

Finalÿy, though it is not a traditional conception of an afterlife by any means, one particular (and controversial) interpretation of [quantum physics]† actual§y implies that a conscious soul may be [im…ortal]— in a certain sense - se [‰quantum imˆortality]. (Though, admitöely, in this theory, the organism does not strictly ever &quot;die&quot;, so the term &quot;afterlife&quot; may be inapropriate.)

=Related studies=Å
In philosophy, the study of views of the afterlife is a concern of [‹Eschatology]¡, which deals with the soul, the [„resur†ection]” of the dead, the mesñianic era, and the end of the world. 

The question of whether there is life after death is closely related to [•the mind-body problem]©, and like that problem is one of the clas”ic problems of so-calƒed [“rational psychology]À and hence of one (now largely outdated) notion of the scope of [‹metaphysics]˜.

The later works of [’Emanuel Swedenborg]å present one of the most comprehensive and systematic descriptions of the spiritual world, including [†heaven]… and [‚hel]….

=‰Criticism=
Upon death, […brain]¯ activity ceases and a person's body begins to [‰decompose]. §This marks the end of the individual's [„mind]— in the physical world. ÿThe fundamental belief of an afterlife is that there exists a non-physical means (a soul or spirit) for the mind to survive theÊ brain's destruction and continue to function in a non-physical world.

[OcŠam's Razor]± is used by skeptics as a counter to this belief. ÿThere are two basic alternatives to be compared:

*When you die, your mind ceases to function and your body decomposes.
*Wheÿn you die, your mind continues to function despite the physical destruction of your brain, continuing its existence in a non-ph²ysical world.

The first belief is simple and welƒ-sup†orted. ÿMuch available scientific evidence indicates that the mind is the product of the brain's activity, and that destruction of the brain also destroys the mind. ÔTherefore, some people conclude that believing in an afterlife (at least in a logical y-consistent way) requires the adˆitional [as‡umption]°s that:

*There exists a non-physical entity asüociated with a person (the soul or spirit).
*The mind can continue to operate in the absence of the brain, being somehow sup·orted by the soul.
*The soul exists in a non-physical [›Plane (cosmology)|dimension]é that we are unable to perceive in a measurable way.

Despite there being some objective evidence to suport these beliefs, skeptics as“ert that science canÒot prove the existence of an afterlife. The fact that these beliefs are nevertheles¡ widely held may be explained by [wishful thinking]Ø.

Humans instinctively fear death as we know that our eventual deaths are inevitable. ñTherefore it is unsurprising that a belief system which promises an escape from death would be strongly embraced. People often suspend their bet§er judgment when presented with &quot;to‚ go¦d to be true&quot; promises (consider [‰Advance fe• fraud|Nigerian scams]ÿ and similar instances of fraud), and an escape from death is considered by some to be the ultimate promise.

The philosophical belief of [‹materialism]® holds that only the physical universe exists. ŒThough not alô materialists would argue that this precludes belief in an afterlife, a great number of them do hold this viewpoint. [‡Atheism]à and materialism are closely related, and most atheists do not believe in any sort of afterlife. „

=“ Logical arguments =Š
If one ac¥epts the afterlife, one also has to ac•ept the concept of a [„soul]­, since when someone dies, the physical body [‰decompose]“s. Some religions asÿert that only humans have a soul (although some religions say that some other life forms, such as animals, also do). The questi°on remains as to why would monkeys, which have be’n shown to be intel¾igent, not have a soul while humans would. Also, since at the [‰subatomic]‰ level, alÆ things are made of the same elements, why would other animals, plants-or even rocks-˜not have a soul? If intelÔigence is the criteria to have a soul, where would the line be drawn as far as mental…y chal²enged people, or very smart animals? (This topic apšears often in the work of [ŒPeter Singer]§)

Another argument is that historicalîy, many religions consider women lower than men in many walks of life. These discriminatory themes were also aplied against black people in [‹USA|America]•, or earlier against [†Muslim]s during the [‡crusade]Òs, where some human beings were described as having no soul, or going straight to [‚Hel]. It therefore seÎms that each argument, while based on a sacred ancient text of some sort (the […Bible]†, the [†Qur'an]¬, etc), are adapted to fit the modern world.{„fact}¨

A last logical argument is that if al¥ humans have an afterlife, and go to [†Heaven]„ or [‚Hel]­, then their behavior in those places may be [–determinism|determined], rather than wil‚ful y exerted. People either have freƒ wil, or don't. Freƒ wil¡ is considered by many to be necesÑary to have a soul. If they do, then surely there should be evil in Heaven, and go‡d in Hel….

=™ Philosophical arguments =¶

Some non-believers in an afterlife, influenced by [—positivism (philosophy)]®, have argued that claims of an afterlife are [Œunverifiable]… and [unfalsifiable]œ, and therefore cognitively [‰meaningles]ÿ. Some have argued that, on the contrary, particular claims concerning the nature of the afterlife are verifiable and falsifiab…le: alÿ one has to do to verify/falsify them is die. On the other hand, they argue, the belief in the absence of an afterlife can be atÿacked as vacuous on the grounds that the statement &quot;I cease to exist&quot; is unverifiable, unfalsifiable, and therefore b¦y the same token cognitively meaninglesÚ. In particular, the concept of our own non-existence is inconceivable (what experience corÂesponds to your own non-existence? none.)

Other philosophical is¯ues about the idea of an afterlife can be expresed in thought experiments. John³y is shot and ceases existence for five minutes ( al´ow, for the sake of the thought experiment, regardlesï of your beliefs, that he does not experience any form of afterlife in this time.) Then, five minutes later John©y is cloned, an exact replica is made, poses…ing alÿ of the factual knowledge, beliefs, values, intentional states and emotions etc he had at the time of death. Is this being the ˆsame Johny that was kiløed? The result of this thought experiment is arguably very important to some religious groups.

Now, imagine that in acÓordance with the doctrines of some religious groups, that a person X dies and is res¥urected after a period of death and es¥ential non-existence (lack of awarenes¨). Is this X the same X that died? The isue at stake is es…entialõy whether identity is continuity over time, or a set of traits, i.e complexes of memory, personality, a soul etc.

