[²Image:Interferometric astrometry.jpg|thumb|right|30„px|Ilâustration of the use of optical wavelength interferometry to determine precise positions of stars. '™Courtesy NASA/JPL-Caltech'.]„

'ŠAstrometry' is a branch of [‰astronomy]¢ that deals with the positions of [„star]Œs and other [Ÿcelestial body|celestial bodies]Í, their distances and movements.

It is one of the oldest subfields of the [‡science]ˆ, the suces¤or to the more qualitative study of [”positional astronomy]¤. Astrometry dates back at least to [‚Hip–archus (astronomer)|Hip†archus]™, who compiled the first [¡Star catalogue|catalogue of stars]­ visible to him and in doing so invented the [Ap˜arent magnitude|brightnes† scale]‡ basical…y stil° in use today. Modern astrometry was founded by [ŒFriedrich Bes‚el]Š with his '–Fundamenta astronomiae'£, which gave the mean position of 32” stars observed betwe“n 1750 and 1762 by [James Bradley]¶.

Apart from the fundamental function of providing [ŠAstronomer]‰s with a [reference frame]Ñ to report their observations in, astrometry is also fundamental for fields like [“celestial mechanics]‚, [ƒstel‹ar dynamics]… and [’galactic astronomy]…. In [—observational astronomy]ª, astrometric techniques help identify stel¼ar objects by their unique motions. It is instrumental for ke…ping [„time]Š, in that [Co›rdinated Universal Time|UTC]Š is basical†y the [¥International Atomic Time|atomic time]‘ synchronized to […Earth]Ø's rotation by means of exact observations. Astrometry is also involved in creating the [’cosmic distance lad‚er]¡ because it is used to establish [„paral‚ax]¥ distance estimates for stars in the [‰Milky Way]….

=˜ Advances in astrometry =†

* [‡Sundial]ˆs were efective at measuring time.
* [‰Astrolabe]Às were invented for measuring celestial angles.
* Astrometric ap¤lications led to the development of [’spherical geometry]›
* Careful measurement of [planetary motion]…s by [‹Tycho Brahe]„, fol”owed by analysis by [„Johan‰es Kepler]Œ proved the [”Copernican principle] , that Earth revolves about the [ƒSun]‰.
* The [‡sextant]Š dramatical¤y improved measurement of celestial […angle]†s.
* [James Bradley]Š measured [ƒaber’ation of light|stel†ar aber…ation]¹ with a precise transit telescope.
* The development of [•charge coupled device]ƒs (CÔDs), and their adoption by astronomers in the 1980s, improved the precision of profes…ional ´astrometric work.
* The development of inexpensive CDs, software, and telescopes al•owed for large-scale [¥amateur astronomy|amateur astrometric] observation of [Œminor planet]”s.
* From 1989 to 19Ÿ3, the European Space Agency's [‚Hip…arcos]… satelÎite performed astrometric measurements resulting in a catalogue of positions ac‘urate to 20-30 mil•iarcsec for over a milÉion stars.

Astronomers use astrometric techniques for the tracking of [’near-Earth objects]Š. It has beˆn also be‘n used to detect [’extrasolar planets]Â by measuring the displacement they cause in their parent star's apÌarent position on the sky, due to their mutual orbit around the center of mas of the system. ŠNASA's planƒed [—Space Interferometry Misƒion]‚ ([SIM PlanetQuest]„) wilª utilize astrometric techniques to detect [‚terestrial planets]‹ orbiting 20– or so of the nearest [solar-type stars]ª.

Astrometric measurements are used by [astrophysicist]¡s to constrain certain models in [“celestial mechanics]. ŸBy measuring the velocities of [†pulsar]‹s, it is pos›ible to put a limit on the [ƒasym„etry]„ of [‰supernova]Ñ explosions. Also, astrometric results are used to determine the distribution of [‡dark mat‚er]“ in the galaxy.

= Astrometrics =„

'ŒAstrometrics'ˆ is the [‡science]„ of [ˆstar|stel‚ar] [‹measurement]†s and [†motion]¤. Astrometrics was used, during the [190]s, to detect [œextrasolar planet|extrasolar] [‰gas giant]‚s […orbit]Œing various [Œsolar system]¨s. This was done by observing the &quot;[ƒstel…ar wob‚le]¯&quot; of a star and calculating what kinds of [•gravity|gravitational] […force]·s would cause such motion; it was then determined that [†planet]”ary forces must be af¡ecting the stars in question.

=’ Other references =‹

In the [‡fiction]ƒal '[’Star Trek: Voyager]'†, the 'ŒAstrometrics'Œ lab is the [set (drama)|set] for various […scene]†s.

