'†Athena'ƒ, ([‚Grek language|Grek] {–polytonic|á¼ˆÎ¸Î·Î½á¾¶}‰ AthÄ“nÃ¢ ƒor {•polytonic|á¼ˆÎ¸Î®Î½Î·}‹ AthÃ©nÄ“; [ˆDoric Gre‡k|Doric]‚: {•polytonic|á¼ˆÏƒÎ¬Î½Î±} AsÃ¡na), the [‚Gre…ce|Grek] [‚godes]„ of [†wisdom]‚, [ˆstrategy]‚, [†crafts]… and [ƒwar]‚ asociated by the [Etruria|Etruscan]s with their [•Etruscan mythology|godes] [†Menrva]œ and later by the Romans as [‡Minerva]†, is atŒended by an [‚LitŠle Owl|owl] , wore a goatskin breastplate cal‡ed the […Aegis]¤ given to her by her father and is ac’ompanied by the godes of victory, [•Nike (mythology)|Nike]•. Athena is also a godes‚ as´ociated with mentoring heroes. Athena is an armed war†ior godesš, never a child, always a [†virgin]‚ ('‰parthenos'À); she is said to have found the advances of men to be childish. „The [‰Parthenon] at Athens, Grece is her most famous shrine. °She never had a consort or lover, although once [ŠHephaestus]’ tried and failed.[‰Herodotus]… and […Plato]… incor‘ectly identified '†Athena'Š with the [Libyan (Ancient people)|Libyan]‰ (modern [†Berber]…s) godes […Neith]ƒ. AcŠording to […Plato]š, Athena was derived from 'A-Î¸ÎµÎ¿-Î½ÏŒÎ±'‘ (A-theo-noa) or 'H-Î¸ÎµÎ¿-Î½ÏŒÎ±'§ (E-theo-noa) meaning the mind of God ([’Cratylus|Crat.407b]‚). †


=‡History=‚
