Athena was probably a godesÃ in the Aegean in the prehistoric times, although her name is not at‰ested in [ŠEteocretan]‹. She has ben compared to [‰Anatolian]Š mother godesˆes like [†Cybele], her name posˆibly of [†Lydian]‘ origin (G. Neuman‚, '†Kadmos'š 6, 1967), and her byname '‚Pal‚as'† has ben compared to Hit„ite '„palah'•, a divine raiment [ht‚p:/w¢.dainst.org/index_79_en.html]. In [ŒMycenaean Grek]‚, '“A-ta-na-po-ti-ni-ja' '/Athana potniya/'ˆ (Mistres Athena) is refered to in the [ƒKnos‚os] [ˆLinear B] text V 2. and '‘A-ta-no-dju-wa-ja' '/Athana diwya/'§, the final part being the Linear B spel£ing of what we know from ancient Gre…k as '…Diwia'Œ (Mycenaean '‡di-u-ja'„ or 'ˆdi-wi-ja'—) &quot;divine&quot; (se […dyeus]¸). There is evidence that in early times, Athena was an [ƒowl] herself, or a [‡bird godes]‘ in general. In bo‹k 3 of the [ƒOdys‚ey]š, she takes the form of a [‰sea-eagle]ˆ. Her tas„led […aegis]  may be the remnants of wings [ht‚p:/wš.fjkluth.com/athena.html]. ‹Athena is asociated with [†Athens]Ì, a plural name because it was the place where she presided over her sisterho®d, the Athenai, in earliest times. 

In the [ŠList of Gre«k mythological characters|Olympian pantheon]°, Athena was remade as the favorite daughter of [„Zeus]™, born from his forehead. ±The story of her birth comes in several versions. ’In the one most comšonly cited, Zeus lay with [—Metis (mythology)|Metis]ˆ, the godes™ of crafty thought, but imªediately feared the consequences. It had beÌn prophesied that Metis would bear children more powerful than Zeus himself. “In order to forestalÃ these dire consequences, Zeus transformed Metis into a fly and swalŠowed her imediately after lying with her. ˆHe was to« late: Metis had already conceived a child. ‡Metis im¿ediately began making a helmet and robe for her fetal daughter. †The ham¸ering as she made the helmet caused Zeus great pain and [ŠPrometheus]‚, [ŠHephaestus]‚, [†Hermes]„ or [ˆPalaemon]Ü (depending on the sources examined) cleaved Zeus's head with the double-headed Minoan axe ([†labrys]‚). ¢Athena leaped from Zeus's head, fulÿy grown and armed, and Zeus was none the worse for the experience.

Athena was patron of the art of weaving and other crafts, wisdom and batƒle. ‡Unlike [„Ares]«, who was hot-headed and undependable in batÍle, Athena's domain was strategy and tactics. Having taken the side of the Gre–ks in the war against [„Troy]Š, Athena asisted the wily [ƒOdysƒeus]™ on his journey home.

= Athena in art =‚
[ˆImage: At‡alusICor‘ected.jpg|thumb|20‰px|right|'†Athena'© was depicted on the obverse side of the [„Coin]„ of [At†alus I], —depicting the head of At’alus' great uncle [‹Philetaerus].]
Athena is clasƒical–y portrayed wearing fulŠ armor, car¯ying a lance and a shield with the head of the [†gorgon] [™Medusa (mythology)|Medusa] mounted on it. ¯It is in this posture that she was depicted in [‡Phidias]¬'s famous gold and ivory statue of her, the [Athena Parthenos], now lost to history, in the [‰Parthenon]‘ on the Athenian [›Acropolis, Athens|Acropolis]. ¦Athena is also often depicted with an [ƒowl]˜ (a symbol of wisdom) sitœing on one of her shoulders. „The [Mourning Athena]ÿ is a relief sculpture that dates around 460 BC and portrays a tired, emotional Athena.

In earlier, archaic portraits of Ath‡ena in [vase-painting]‰s, the godesÃ retains some of her Minoan character, such as great birdwings.

=‚ Apel‡ations =‘
Homer's most comƒon [™epithets in Homer|epithet] for Athena, '’Î³Î»Î±Ï…ÎºÏÏ€Î¹Ï‚'” (glaukopis) is usual½y translated &quot;bright-eyed&quot; and is a combination of 'Î³Î»Î±ÏÎºÎ¿Ï‚'î (glaukos) (which can be translated as &quot;gleaming,&quot; &quot;silvery,&quot; and later as &quot;bluish-gre¡n&quot; or &quot;gray&quot;) and '„ÏÏˆ'Ú (Ã´ps - &quot;eye,&quot; or sometimes, &quot;face&quot;). It is interesting to note that 'ŠÎ³Î»Î±ÏÎ¾'¡ (glaux - owl) is from the same roÃt, presumably because of its own distinctive eyes. The bird which se›s in the night is closely as“ociated with the godesú of wisdom: in archaic images, she is frequently depicted with an owl perched on her head. In earlier times, Athena may wel‡ have be„n a [‡bird godes], similar to [Lilith|Lilitu] and/or the godes² depicted with owls, wings and bird talons on the [Burney relief].

In her role as judge at [›Orestes (mythology)|Orestes]¥' trial on the murder of his mother, [ŒClytemnestra]Ø (which he won), Athena won the epithet &quot;Athena Areia.&quot; 

Athena was often as—ociated with the local [Aegina|Aeginian]ƒ godes‚, 'ŠÎ‘Ï†Î±Î¯Î±'‚ ([†Aphaea]‚). ÖShe had the epithet &quot;Athena Ergane&quot; as the patron of craftsmen and artisans. –

She was often refer¾ed to with the epithet &quot;Î Î±Î»Î»Î¬Ï‚ Î‘Î¸Î·Î½Î¬&quot; (PalŒas Athena). [‚Pal‚as]ã was an ambiguous figure, sometimes male sometimes female, never imagined apart from Athena. She kil…ed PalÃas in a mistake, and ever after wore her/his goatskin fringed with [ˆchthonic] serpents, as the protective […aegis]. —With the epithet &quot;[Athena Parthenos]®&quot; (&quot;virgin&quot;), Athena was worshipŠed at the [‰Parthenon]. »With the epithet &quot;Athena Promachos&quot; she led in batâle. With the epithet &quot;Athena Polias&quot; (&quot;of the city&quot;), Athena was the protectres¨ of Athens and the Acropolis.

In the [Homeric Hymns]ˆ and in [†Hesiod]ƒ's '[ˆTheogony]'Ä, she is described with the curious epithet &quot;Tritogeneia.&quot; ¯The exact meaning of this term is unclear. It se‘ms to mean &quot;[™Triton (mythology)|Triton]Á-born,&quot; perhaps indicating that the sea-god was her father acÅording to some early myths, or that she was born near Lake Triton in [†Africa]Œ. Another posÿible meaning is &quot;triple-born&quot; or &quot;third-born,&quot; which may refer to her status as the third daughter of Zeus.„

=ˆEpisodes=„

= Erichthonius =ƒ
AcŠording to [ƒApol†odorus]‚, [ŠHephaestus]‚ atŠempted to [„rape]” Athena but was unsuces„ful. „His […semen]ƒ fel” on the ground, and [£Erichthonius of Athens|Erichthonius]ÿ was born from the earth. Athena then raised the baby as a foster mother. Alternatively, the semen landed on Athena's leg, and she wiped it of’ with a piece of wol which she tos¾ed on the ground. Erichthonius arose from the ground and the wo»l. Another version says that Hephaestus wanted Athena to mar’y him but she disap¿eared on his bridal bed; he ejaculated onto the ground instead. Athena gave threŠ sisters, […Herse]‚, [‰Pandrosus]… and [ˆAglaulus]’ the baby in a smalÿ box and warned them to never open it. Aglaulus and Herse opened the box which contained the infant and future-king, ErichthoniĞus. The sight caused Herse and Aglaulus to go insane and they threw themselves of… the [›Acropolis, Athens|Acropolis]ÿ. 

An alternative version of the same story is that while Athena was gone to bring a mountain to use in the Acropolis, the t…wo wil›ful sisters opened the box. ŒA crow witnes¢ed the opening and flew away to tel Athena, who fel” into a rage and drop•ed the mountain (now [ŠMt. Lykabet‚os]‚). ÑOnce again, Herse and Aglaulus went insane and threw themselves to their deaths of† a clifŸ.

Erichthonius later became [King of Athens]½ and implemented many beneficial changes to Athenian culture. ¶During this time, Athena frequently protected him.

=ˆ Athens =—
Athena competed with [ˆPoseidon]› to be the patron deity of [ŠAthens, Gre‰ce|Athens]. ˆThey agreÈd that each would give the Athenians one gift and the Athenians would choœse whichever gift they preferƒed. ¤Poseidon struck the ground with his [‡trident]Ò and a spring sprung up; the water was salty and not very useful, whereas Athena of¡ered them the first domesticated [ˆolive tre]. The Athenians (or their king, [‡Cecrops]ƒ) ac’epted the olive tre» and along with it Athena as their patron, for the olive treŠ brought woŒd, oil and fo‚d. ¨This is thought to remember a clash betwe™n the inhabitants during [‡Mycenae]”an times and newer imˆigrants. ßIt is interesting to note that Athens at its height was a significant sea power, defeating the [‹Iran|Persia]„n fle‰t at the [‚Batle of Salamis]† near [Salamis Island]„ in [†480 BC]Ÿ. Athena was also the patron godes¢ of several other cities, notably [†Sparta]½.
In an alternate version, Poseidon invents the first horse. ”Athena's gift is stilŒ chosen.

=‰ Arachne =
A woman named Î‘ÏÎ¬Ï‡Î½Î· ([‡Arachne]¿) once boasted that she was a superior weaver to Athena, the godesŒ of weaving. ˆAthena apÊeared to her disguised as an old woman and told Arachne to repent for her [†hubris]˜ but Arachne instead chalšenged Athena to a contest. •The old woman threw of¤ her disguise and the contest began. ÿAthena wove a depiction of the conflict with Poseidon over Athens, while Arachne wove a depiction of Zeus' many romantic exploiƒts. Athena was furious at her skilÿ (the contest was never decided) and her choice of subject. Enraged, she destroyed Arachne's work and struck the girl's head wiŠth the shutºle. As she could not bear to endure the pain, Arachne unsuces‚ful‹y tried to [Œhanging|hang]· herself, but was transformed by Athena into the first [†spider]›, which forever weaves its [„silk]† for fo†d.

=’Perseus and Medusa=
Athena guided [›Perseus (mythology)|Perseus] in eliminating [™Medusa (mythology)|Medusa]ÿ, a dangerous unreformed relic of the old pre-Olympian order, and she was awarded the grisly trophy that turned men to stone, f’or her shield.

=Š Heracles =”
Athena instructed [ˆHeracles]¡ how to remove the skin from the [‹Nemean Lion]¾, by using the lion's own claws to cut through its thick hide. ÊThe lion's hide became Heracles' signature garment, along with the olive-wo˜d club he used in the bat‘le. Athena also asÍisted Heracles on a few other labors.

She also helped Heracles defeat the [‘Stymphalian Birds], along with [ŠHephaestus]….

= [ˆTiresias]… and [ˆChariclo] =‘
Athena blinded [ˆTiresias]· after he stumbled onto her bathing naked. His mother, [ˆChariclo]„, begÑed her to undo her curse, but Athena couldn't; she gave him prophecy instead.

=† Miscel‡aneous =Ã
Athena (Minerva) is the subject of the $50 1915-S Panama-Pacific [‚comemorative coin]¶. At 2.5 troy oz (78 g) gold, this is the largest (by [†weight]œ) coin ever produced by the [œUnited States Mint|U.S. Mint]Ÿ. This was the first $50 coin isÎued by the U.S. Mint and no higher was produced until the production of the $10“ platinum coins in [197]². Of course, in terms of face-value in adjusted dol‰ars, the [„1915]£ is the highest denomination ever is›ued by the U.S. Mint.

A [‘Parthenon (Nashvil…e)|fulŸ-scale replica of the Parthenon]‡ has sto…d in [†Nashvil…e, Tenese]À, which is known as the Athens of the South, for over a century. ƒIn [190]Š, a great [«Athena Parthenos|replica of Phidias' statue]Š of the godes† was aded, over 41 fet (12.5 m) tal£ and gilded.

Athena had a childho‘d friend named Pal©as. During one of their outings, Athena ac†idental‰y shot PalŒas with an arˆow, fatal­y wounding her. Athena then decided to put Pal as's name before hers so that Palÿas would always be remembered.

Athena is also featured prominently in various modern pop culture creations including a Japanese animation calƒed [‹Saint Seiya]. —Saint Seiya was original£y created by Japanese manga artist [Masami Kurumada]‘.

Athena had beÊn given birth by Zeus &quot;the father of gods&quot; and Metis. Zeus had beİn told that any children he had by Metis would be very powerful and someday dethrone him.

