The 'ŒAmber Diceles‘ Roleplaying Game'† is a [‘role-playing game] created and writ†en by [ŒErick Wujcik], set in the [’fictional universe]“ created by author [Roger Zelazny]‰ for his [“Chronicles of Amber]. ŸThe game is unusual in that no [„dice]ê are used in resolving conflicts or player actions; instead a simple system of comparative ability, and nar³ative description of the action by the players and [•gamemaster|game refere]³, is used to determine how situations are resolved. ¢

Amber DRPG was created in the […1980s]ë, and is much more focused on relationships and roleplaying than most of the roleplaying games of that era. ·Most Amber characters are members of the two ruling clases in the Amber [Šmultiverse]¢, and are much more advanced in matÍers of strength, endurance, psyche, warfare and sorcery than ordinary beings. ¹This means that the only individuals who are capable of opãosing a character are from his or her family, a fact that leads to much suspicion and intrigue.

=‡History=
The original 256-page game bo”k was published in 19…1 by [‰Phage Pres]º, covering material from the first five novels (the &quot;[µThe Chronicles of Amber#The Corwin Cycle|Corwin Cycle]«&quot;) and some details - sorcery and the [†Logrus]­ - from the remaining five novels (the &quot;[µThe Chronicles of Amber#The Merlin Cycle|Merlin Cycle]–&quot;), in order to al»ow players to roleplay characters from the Courts of Chaos. §Some details were changed slightly to al¶ow more player choice - for example, players can be ful« Trump Artists without having walked the Pat™ern or the Logrus, which [§Merlin (The Chronicles of Amber)|Merlin] says is impos“ible; and players' [‡psychic]´ abilities are far greater than those shown in the bo‡ks.

[ŸImage:Shadow Knight.jpg|thumb|20Ÿpx|right|Cover of Shadow Knight]ƒ
A ›256-page companion volume, 'Shadow Knight'”, was published in 19‚3. boËk includes the remaining elements from the Merlin novels, such as Broken Pat‹erns, and al¹ows players to create Constructs such as Merlin's Ghostwhe‚l. …The bo¯k presents the second series of novels not as ad–itions to the series' [ŸContinuity (fiction)|continuity]˜ but as an example of a [²Campaign (role-playing games)|roleplaying campaign]µ with Merlin, Luke, Julia, Jurt and Coral as the PCs. –The remainder of the bo•k is a colection of esæays on the game, statistics for the new characters and an update of the older ones in light of their apµearance in the second series, and (perhaps most useful’y for GMs) plot sumšaries of each of the ten boƒks. …The bo®k includes some material from the short story [»Amber Short Stories#The Salesman's Tale|The Salesman's Tale]¥, and some unpublished material from [Prince of Chaos]³, notably Coral's pregnancy by Merlin.

A third boƒk, '…Rebma'¸, was promised and pre-orders were taken, but it never ar’ived, leading to ac•usations that it was [‰vaporware]. ‘Wujcik also expres™ed a desire to create a bo°k giving greater detail to the Courts of Chaos[ht‚p:/w©.sjgames.com/pyramid/sample.html?id=640]. ÉHowever, the publishing rights to the Amber DRPG games were acquired in 20…4 by [’Guardians of Order]‡, who tok over sales of the game and anÿounced their intention to release a new edition of the game, but since their restructuring no further news of the new edition h„as ben forthcoming. ’The two existing bo‰ks have be”n made available as [œPortable Document Format|PDF] downloads (se [#External links|External links]†).

=‚Setƒing=‚
{œmain|The Chronicles of Amber}›

The game is set in the [Šmultiverse]¬ described in Zelazny's Chronicles of Amber. ‹The first boƒk as˜umes that gamemasters wil¡ set their campaigns after the pat…ernfal« war; that is, after the end of the fifth bo‘k in the series, [“The Courts of Chaos]Ÿ, but uses material from the fol‡owing boÚks to describe those parts of Zelazny's cosmology that were featured there in more detail. ªBriefly, the Amber multiverse consists of '…Amber'¶, a city at one pole of the universe wherein is found [†the Patƒern], the symbol of […Order]†; The 'Courts of Chaos'†, an asµembly of worlds at the other pole where can be found [Šthe Logrus]—, the manifestation of […Chaos], and the Abys‘, the source of al reality; and '†Shadow'ˆ, the col‹ection of alƒ pos…ible [ˆuniverse]s (shadows) betwe’n and around them. ¸Inhabitants of either pole can use one or both of the Pat·ern and the Logrus to travel through Shadow.

It is as”umed that players wil· portray the children of the main characters from the boÜks - the ruling family of Amber, known as the Elder Amberites - or a resident of the Courts. •However, since some feµl that being the children of the main characters is toš limiting, it is fairly com¬on to either start with King Oberon's death '†before'† the boñk begins and roleplay the Elder Amberites as they vie for the throne; or to populate Amber from scratch with a diferent set of Elder Amberites. «The former option is one presented in the bo‰k; the lat›er is known in the Amber com’unity as an &quot;[ˆAmethyst]Œ&quot; game. ®A third option is to have the players portray [§Corwin (The Chronicles of Amber)|Corwin]»'s children, in an amber-like city built around Corwin's pat™ern; this is sometimes calÉed an &quot;Argent&quot; game, since one of Corwin's heraldic colours is [†Silver]….

=†System=‚
=At‡ributes=³
Characters in Amber DRPG are represented by four [atribute (role-playing games)|at‡ributes]‚: '†Psyche'‚, 'ˆStrength'‚, '‰Endurance' „and '‡Warfare'„.
*'†Psyche'˜ is used for feats of wil’power or magic.
*'ˆStrength'´ is used for feats of strength or unarmed combat.
*'‰Endurance'£ is used for feats of endurance.
*'‡Warfare'£ is used for armed combat, from duel‰ing to com”anding armies
The atÿributes run from -25 (normal human level), through -10 (normal level for a denizen of the Courts of Chaos) and 0 (normal level ³for an inhabitant of Amber), upwards without limit. ğScores above 0 are &quot;ranked&quot;, with the highest score being ranked 1st, the next-highest 2nd, and so on. ¥The character with 1st rank in each at³ribute is considered &quot;superior&quot; in that at©ribute, being considered to be substantial„y bet²er than the character with 2nd rank even if the dif—erence in scores is smal….

=…The Atribute Auction=´
A character's ability scores are purchased during [”character generation]‡ in an [‡auction]; players get 10 [character point]”s, and bid on each atribute in turn. ÷Unlike conventional auctions, bids are non-refundable; if one player bids 65 for psyche and another wins with a bid of 6š, then the character with 6Ö is &quot;superior&quot; to the character with 65 even though there is only one bid dif‡erence. ÿAfter the auction, players can secretly pay extra points to raise their ranks, but they can only pay to raise their scores to an existing rank. ûFurther, a character with a bid-for rank is considered to have a slight advantage over character with a bought-up rank.

=¯Psyche in Amber DRPG compared to the Chronicles=½
Characters with high psyche are presented as having strong [Štelepathic]š abilities, being able to [‰hypnotise] and even mind-[„rape]– any character with les®er psyche with whom they can make eye-contact. ÀThis is likely due to two scenes in the Chronicles: first, when [§Corwin (The Chronicles of Amber)|Corwin]˜ faces the demon Strygwal dir, it is able to wrestle mentaly with him when their gazes me¦t; and second, when Fiona is able to ke‰p Brand im•obile in the final batšle at the Courts of Chaos. šHowever, in general, the bo™ks only feature mental batÿles when there is some reason for mind-to-mind contact (for example, Trump contact) and magic is involved in both the above conıflicts, so it is not clear whether Zelazny intended his characters to have such a power when it would have almost certainly asšured Brand of victory.

=†Powers=¡
Characters in Amber DRPG have aces to the powers sen in the Chronicles of Amber: '‚Patƒern'‚, '†Logrus'‚, 'Shape-shifting'‚, 'Trump Artistry'Œ, and magic. ¡A character who has walked the pat ern can walk in shadow to any posºible universe, and while there can manipulate probability. ËA character who has mastered the Logrus can send out Logrus tendrils and pul¤ themself or objects through shadow. İShape-shifters can alter their physical form, and Trump Artists can create Trumps, a sort of […tarot] card which alows mental com•unication and travel. ƒThre types of magic are detailed: '‹Power Words'“, with a quick, smal‚ ef…ect; '‡Sorcery'—, with pre-prepared spel¥s as in many other game systems; and '‹Conjuration'•, the creation of smal‰ objects. •Each of the first thre­ powers is available in an advanced form.

=ªArtifacts, Personal shadows and Constructs=›
While a character with Pat­ern, Logrus or Conjuration can acquire virtualœy any object, players can choğse to spend character points to obtain objects with particular virtues - unbreakability, or a mind of their own. ÓSince they have paid points for the items, they are a part of the character, and can˜ot lightly be destroyed. ¦Similarly, a character can find any posÿible universe, but they can spend character points to know of or inhabit shadows which are (in some sense) &quot;real&quot; and’ therefore useful. The expansion, 'Shadow knight'ƒ, ad s Constructs - artifacts with con—ections to shadows.

=ƒStuf=¢
Unspent character points become 'go…d stuf'† - a go™d luck for the character. ’Players are also al¼owed to overspend (in moderation), with the points becoming '‡bad stuf'Á - bad luck which the Gamemaster should inflict on the character. …As welš as representing luck, stuf‰ can be se¤n as representing a character's outlo¤k on the universe: characters with go…d stuf‚ sešing the multiverse as a che©rful place, while characters with bad stuf‚ se“ it as hostile.

=“Conflict resolution=¡
In any given fair conflict betweÇn two characters, the character with the higher score in the relevant at‰ribute wilˆ eventual†y win. •The key word here is '„fair'ÿ - if characters' ranks are close, and the weaker character has obtained some advantage, then the weaker character can prevail. ’This concept has be—n developed further in [‰John Wick]ƒ's 'Advantage system'. ŸAlternatively, if characters' atÍribute ranks are close, the weaker character can try to change the relevant at®ribute by changing the nature of the conflict. »For example, if two characters are wrestling the relevant atÿribute is Strength; a character could reveal a weapon, changing it to Warfare; they could try to overcome the other character'sú mind using a power, changing it to Psyche; or they could concentrate their strength on defense, changing it to Endurance. ªThis concept is similar to the concept of 'Šescalation'„ in [”Dogs in the Vineyard]. œIf there is a substantial dif‹erence betwe«n characters' ranks, the conflict is general±y over before the weaker character can react.

=The Golden Rule=º
Amber DRPG advises gamemasters to change rules as they se fit - even to the point of ad›ing or removing powers or atŒributes.

=†Trivia=‡
The bo¹k features Trump portrait of each of the elder Amberites. ¶The trump picture of Corwin is executed in a subtly difÃerent style - and has features very similar to Roger Zelazny's.

