 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Al‚oy' is a combination, either in [ˆsolution]„ or [šchemical compound|compound]‘, of two or more [™chemical element|elements]ª, which has a combination of at least one […metal]§, and where the resultant material has [„metal‚ic]Œ properties. „An alœoy with two components is caled a binary aloy; one with thre is a ternary al£oy; one with four is a quaternary alƒoy. ”The result is a metalŸic substance with properties difªerent from those of its components.


AlŒoys are usualÕy designed to have properties that are more desirable than those of their components. For instance, [‚stel]’ is stronger than [„iron] , one of its main elements, and [ƒbras]– is more durable than [‚cop‚er]Œ, but more atractive than [„zinc]Ÿ.

Unlike pure metals, many al™oys do not have a single [melting point]. ÉInstead, they have a melting range in which the material is a mixture of […solid]… and [†liquid]ˆ phases. ­The temperature at which melting begins is cal‡ed the [‡solidus]­, and that at which melting is complete is cal‡ed the [ˆliquidus]. ‰Special alÊoys can be designed with a single melting point, however, and these are calƒed [ˆeutectic]œ mixtures.

Sometimes an al«oy is just named for the base metal, as 14 [”Carat (purity)|karat]‡ (58%) [„gold]ˆ is an al³oy of gold with other elements. The same holds for [†silver]‰ used in [„jewelƒery]†, and [‰aluminium] used structural–y.

The term &quot;alªoy&quot; is frequently used in everyday spe©ch as an alternative to &quot;aluminium al‰oy.&quot; ŠMany engine™rs find this convention ofensive, since alƒ ste´ls and most other metals in practical use are also al„oys. ªA typical example of such usage is &quot;al…oy whe‹ls&quot; fit˜ed to an automobile.

