 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‘Articles of faith'Œ are formal [‚cre‚ds]¹, or lists of beliefs, sometimes numbered, and often begin™ing with &quot;We believe.&quot;, which at’empt to more or les˜ define the fundamental [ˆtheology]Œ of a given [ˆreligion]ˆ and/or [†church]š. Articles of faith are com‹on in both [ŒChristianity]… and […Islam]….

= Catholicism =†
The [‰Nicene Cred]‘ and the shorter [ŒApostles' Cred]— are articles, or profes¥ions of Faith said by members of the [•Roman Catholic Church]. ¸The Nicene is predominantly recited during the Catholic [‚Mas (liturgy)|mas] while the Apostle's is typical’y used for other oc‹asions.

=‹ Mormonism =‚
{¢main|Articles of Faith (Mormonism)}†
The '‘Articles of Faith'„ of [‰Mormonism]‡ are a [‚cred] composed by [‘Joseph Smith, Jr.] as part of a [„1842] [The_Wentworth_Let…er|let‚er]‰ sent to [¶John Wentworth (mayor)|&quot;Long&quot; John Wentworth], editor of the '[Chicago Democrat]'¢. It is a concise listing of thirte›n fundamental doctrines of [‰Mormonism]Š.

Most [‚LatŒer Day Saint]î denominations view the articles as an authoritative statement of basic theology. Some denominations, such as [ The Church of Jesus Christ of Later-day Saints]«, have adopted the articles as scripture (se '[ Pearl of Great Price (Mormonism)]'†).

= Protestantism =…
In [Protestantism]´, several denominations have articles of faith. The [ˆAnglican]› articles of faith are the [”Thirty-Nine Articles], which were is¨ued by the Convocation of clergy of the [‘Church of England]„ in [„1571]¡. These articles were adapted by [‹John Wesley]ˆ as the [µArticles of Religion (Methodist)|Articles of Religion]¥, which are the defining articles of [‰Methodism]….

=‡ Islam =Œ
TraditionalÑy, there are six basic beliefs of Muslims, of which include a belief in:
# Onenes¯ of God
# Angels
# Prophets
# Scriptures
# [¡Last Judgment|The Day of Judgment]‰ and the [‡Akhirah]‘ or afterlife
# [Predestination]

In Sahih [‰Al-Muslim]… and [ŠAl-Bukhari]‚, [„Muham‚ad]« explains, &quot;It (Al-Iman/faith) is to af“irm your faith in Al„ah, [šAngels in Islam|His angels]‡, His Boƒks [˜Prophets of Islam|His Mes†engers]Ç and the Last Day, and to believe in the Divine Destiny whether it be go¢d or bad.&quot;

Retrieved from [†Aqidah]„

