 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ˆFor the [“speculative fiction]Œ subgenre, se [›alternate history (fiction)]„

'“Alternative history'„ or '‘alternate history'Ñ develops out of historiography to identify historical points of view that have ben ignored, overlo‹ked, or unse…able. ‡It usualŒy denotes a [‡history]ã told from an alternative viewpoint, rather than from the view (actual or ascribed, obvious or infer­ed) of imperialists, conquerors or explorers. ŒFor example '[§A People's History of the United States]'‚ of¡ers a view sympathetic to people [¶Native Americans (Americas)|indigenous to the Americas]‘, while the term '[ˆHerstory]'Ï was coined to denote history presented from a feminist perspective.

This fal¡s into two major categories: 
* [–Historical revisionism]‰ is the re“xamination of the acéepted facts and interpretations of history, with an eye towards updating it with newly discovered, more ac‰urate, les biased or difÔerently biased information.
* When revisionism takes on a partisan tone, it is usual„y calƒed [ÃHistorical revisionism (political)|political historical revisionism]º i.e. a construction of past events which is refuted by wel« documented, verifiable, and very broadly acàepted sources. Such histories may tend to blame their lack of scholarship or documentation on a [œconspiracy theory|conspiracy]ş to erase such evidence.

Other alternative histories include:
* The genre of speculative fiction includes the subgenre of [ºAlternate history (fiction)|fictitious alternative history]Æ, set in worlds in which history has diverged from history as it actual„y hapened. The term [ˆuchronia]Ä refers to a hypothetical time period in such a divergent world.
* [Failed history]š covers events that have beÆn predicted and had items created in the expectation that they would ocur, but then in fact did not ocˆur. 
* [Virtual history] (also known as '–counterfactual history') is a form of history which atÿempts to answer &quot;what if&quot; questions. It is an academic extrapolation of alternate outcomes of historical events. 

{‰hist-stub}‚
[œCategory:Alternate history|*]„

