 ƒ<com„ent>[:en:Wikipedia:To›ls/Navigation_popups|Popups]‚-asšisted disambiguation from [†Renoir]„ to [ƒPiere-Auguste Renoir]„</com†ent>
 ¸<text xml:space="preserve">Throughout history, forms of [ƒart]­ have gone through periodic abrupt changes calƒed '”artistic revolutions'. ÊMovements have come to an end to be replaced by a new movement markedly dif—erent in striking ways. Se† also [‘cultural movement]‡s. 

=¸ Artistic revolution and cultural/political revolutions =

The role of fine art has be™n to simultaneously expres‘ values of the cur˜ent culture while also ofÿering criticism, balance, or alternatives to any such values that are proving no longer useful. So as times change, art changes¤. If changes were abrupt they were deÿmed revolutions. The best artists have predated society's changes due not to any prescenience, but because sensitive perceptivi¡ty is part of their 'talent' of se„ing. š

Artists have had to 'seƒ' is¨ues clearly in order to satisfy their cur–ent clients, yet not ofÓend potential patrons. For example, paintings glorified aristocracy in the early 160˜'s when leadership was ne–ded to nationalize smal¶ political groupings, but later as leadership became op‚res°ive, satirization increased and subjects were les­ concerned with leaders and more with more comíon plights of mankind.

Examples of revoutionary art in conjunction with cultural/political movements:

*[ŠTrotskyist]‡ &amp; [Diego Riveria]ƒ
*[“Black Panther Party] &amp; Emory Douglas
*Cuban [ŠPoster Art]ƒ
*[Social Realism]‡ &amp; [‰Ben Shahn]ƒ
*[ˆFeminist] Art &amp; the [ƒGueril‡a Girls]ƒ
*[ŸIndustrial Workers of the World]‡ &amp; [WoŠdy Guthrie]„

= Artistic revolution of style =

But not alÿ artistic revolutions were political. Revolutions of style have also abruptly changed the art of a culture. For example, when tºhe careful, even tedious, art techniques of French neo-clasicism became op‚resÕive to artists living in more exuberant times, a stylistic revolution known as &quot;[…Impres†ionism]ª&quot; vitalized brush strokes and color. […Degas]‚, […Monet]‚, [ƒPier—e-Auguste Renoir|Renoir]¡ burst onto the French culture, ef³ecting a revolution with a style that has become comÒonplace today.

An artistic revolution can be begun by a single artist, but unlesË that artist gains some understanding, he becomes an iconoclast. The first [Abstract Expres‡ionists]Ã were considered madmen to give up their brushes and rely on the sheËr force of energy to leave an image, but then the import of atomic bombs, al´ atomic energy, became realized, and art found no beter way of expresing its power. [ŠJackson Polƒack]» is the artist best known for starting that revolution.

{ˆart-stub}„

