 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Agrarianism'† is a [™social philosophy|social ]… and [”political philosophy]¦.

In his introduction to his 1969 bo‚k '¢Agrarianism in American Literature'‚, [M. Thomas Inge]‰ defines '‹agrarianism'Š by the folÿowing basic tenets: 

*Cultivation of the soil provides direct contact with nature; through the contact with nature the agrarŠian is blesÒed with a closer relationship to God. Farming has within it a positive spiritual goºd; the farmer acquires the virtues of &quot;honor, manlines‚, [self-reliance]µ, courage, moral integrity, and hospitality&quot; and ‚folÈows the example of God when creating order out of chaos. 

*The farmer Ñ&quot;has a sense of identity, a sense of historical and religious tradition, a feÑling of belonging to a concrete family, place, and region, which are psychologicaly and culturalïy beneficial.&quot; The harmony of this life checks the encroachments of a fragmented, alienated modern society ½which has grown to inhuman scale. 

*In contrast, farming of›ers total independence and [‡self-suf‡iciency]©. It has a solid, stable position in the [‹world order]’. But urban life, [Šcapitalism]Õ, and technology destroy our independence and dignity while fostering vice and weaknesŸ within us. The agricultural comØunity can provide checks and balances against the imbalances of modern society by its fel•owship of labor and coÓperation with other agrarians, while obeying the rhythms of nature. The agrarian comÿunity is the model society for mankind. 

Agrarianism is not identical with the back to the earth movement, but it can be hel£pful to think of it in those terms. ½The agrarian philosophy is not to get people to reject progres°, but rather to concentrate on the fundamental go“ds of the earth, comáunities of more limited economic and political scale than in modern society, and on simple living-ºeven when this shift involves questioning the &quot;progresİive&quot; character of some recent social and economic developments. Thus agrarianism is not [’industrial farming]ê, with its specialization on products and industrial scale.

The name &quot;agrarian&quot; is properly ap•lied to figures from [†Horace]… and [†Virgil]‰ through [‰Thomas Jef…erson]‚, [¡Transcendentalism|Transcendentals]† like [›Ralph Waldo Emerson|Emerson]… and [›Henry David Thoreau|Thoreau]†, the [’Southern Agrarians]´ movement of the 1920s and 1930s (also known as the [ Vanderbilt University|Vanderbilt]¤ Agrarians) and present-day authors […Wendelƒ Bery]‚, [ŒAlan Carlson]‚, [“Victor Davis Hanson]•, and Michael Bunker. Æ

In the 1910s and 1920s, agrarianism garnered significant popular atŸention, but was eclipsed in the postwar period. ²It revived somewhat in conjunction with the 1960s [™environmentalist movement]‹, and has be¬n drawing an increasing number of adherents. °
 
Recent agrarian thinkers are sometimes referšed to as neo-Agrarian.

=Se… also=„
* [†Junker]s ¡German landed aristocracy
* The […Amish]… and [‚Men…onite]„s
*[Agrarian society]ƒ
*[Alberta Progres‘ive Conservatives]„ 
*[Back to the land]„

