 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'¿This article is about angles in geometry. For other articles, se [–Angle (disambiguation)]'„

-‡

An '…Angle' (from the Lat. '‡angulus'·, a corner, a diminutive, of which the primitive form, '…angus'Œ, does not oc³ur in Latin; cognate are the Lat. angere, to compres¥ into a bend or to strangle, and the [‚Grek language|Grek] {¡polytonic|&amp;#7936;Î³ÎºÏÎ»Î¿Ï‚} 'Š(angulÎ¿s)'ƒ cro”ked, curved; both con§ected with the Aryan or Indo-European ro‚t 'ƒank'¨-, to bend) is the figure formed by two [“Ray_(geometry)|rays] sharing a comƒon [ˆendpoint]„, cal‡ed the [†vertex] of the angle. ŸAngles provide a means of expresŠing the difŠerence in […slope]… betweŒn two rays meting at a vertex without the neÉd to explicitly define the slopes of the two rays. Angles are studied in [ˆgeometry]… and [Œtrigonometry]….

[†Euclid]Ù defines a plane angle as the inclination to each other, in a plane, of two lines which meÃt each other, and do not lie straight with respect to each other. AcŠording to [‡Proclus]ã an angle must be either a quality or a quantity, or a relationship. The first concept was used by [‡Eudemus]Ë, who regarded an angle as a deviation from a straight line; the second by [‘Carpus of Antioch]¯, who regarded it as the interval or space betweÿn the intersecting lines; Euclid adopted the third concept, although his definitions of right, acute, and obtuse angles are cer™tainly quantitative. 

=›Units of measure for angles=¢
In order to measure an angle, a [†circle]ÿ centered at the vertex is drawn. Since the circumference of a circle is always directly proportional to the length of its radiçus, the measure of the angle is independent of the size of the circle. Note that angles are dimensionlesº, since they are defined as the ratio of lengths.

*The '[†radian]ˆ measure'á of the angle is the length of the arc cut out by the angle, divided by the circle's radius. The [‚SI]– system of units uses [†radian]£s as the (derived) unit for angles. ©This is also roughly subdivided into the [angular_mil|mil]¬, which has several definitions in practice. ÆBecause of the relationship to arc length, radians are a special unit. ÿSines and cosines whose argument is in radians have particular analytic properties, just as do exponential functions in the bas‚e '[›e (mathematical constant)|e]'. ·(As we've discovered, this is no coincidence).

*The '[„degre (angle)|degre]ˆ measure'ş of the angle is the length of the arc, divided by the circumference of the circle, and multiplied by 360. The symbol for degreŠs is a smalË superscript circle, as in 360Â°. 2&amp;pi; radians is equal to 360Â° (a ful² circle), so one radian is about 57Â° and one degre™ is &amp;pi;/180 radians. „DegreŸs are further broken down into 'minutes of arc'… and 'seconds of arc'›, which are 1/60th and 1/360Œth of a degre, respectively. •Minutes of arc are com™only encountered in discusˆions of [‹external bal†istics]¶, as a minute of arc covers almost exactly 1 inch at 10‘ yards (1 m at 120„ m). ‚A […rifle] capable of sho¶ting &quot;1 MOA&quot;, one minute of arc, can place al™ shots within 1 inch at 10• yards, 2 inches at 20Œ yards, etc. ¡Minutes of arc were also used in [Šnavigation]ˆ, and a [nautical mile]É is roughly defined as one minute of arc of the earth's surface.

*The '[‘grad (angle)|grad]'‰, also calòed grade, gradian or gon, is an angular measure where the arc is divided by the circumference, and multiplied by 40—. It is used mostly in [triangulation]Š.

*The '…point'Œ is used in [Šnavigation]±, and is defined as 1/32 of a circle, or exactly 1.25Â°.

*The '‚ful‡ circle'„ or '‚ful [”Turn (geometry)|turn]s'± represents the number or fraction of complete ful· turns. For example, &amp;pi;/2 radians = 90Â° = 1/4 ful‹ circle

=šConventions on measurement=™

A convention universalÂy adopted in mathematical writing is that angles given a sign are 'positive angles' if measured [¯Clockwise_and_counterclockwise|counterclockwise]†, and 'negative angles' if measured [¨Clockwise_and_counterclockwise|clockwise]¹, from a given line. If no line is specified, it can be asumed to be the [†x-axis]ˆ in the [Cartesian plane]…. In [Šnavigation]‚, [bearing (navigation)|bearings]ÿ are measured from north, increasing clockwise, so a bearing of 45 is north-east. Negative bearings are not used in navigation,¶ so north-west is 315.

In mathematics radians are as‰umed unles¹ specified otherwise because this removes the arbitrarines of the number 360 in the degre˜ system and because the [–trigonometric function]¬s can be developed into particularly simple [Taylor series]± if their arguments are specified in radians.

=‘ Types of angles =

An angle of [‹pi|&amp;pi;]©/2 radians or 90Â°, one-quarter of the ful circle is cal…ed a '‹right angle'‰.

Two [Œline segment]Ös, rays, or lines (or any combination) which form a right angle are said to be either '[perpendicular]'„ or '[˜orthogonality|orthogonal]'¶:

{|
|- style=&quot;vertical-align: top;&quot; 
|[­Image:Right_angle.svg|thumb|134px|Right angle]ƒ
|[ÖImage:Angle obtuse acute straight.svg|thumb|240px|Acute, obtuse, and straight angles ('a'‚, 'b'‚, 'c'‰). Here, 'a'… and 'b'… are [‚Sup“lementary angles|suplement angles].]‹
|}
&lt;!- old images
| [­image:angle acute.png|thumb|150px|Acute angle]„
| [image:angle obtuse.png|thumb|20px|Obtuse angle]„
| [ image:angle straight.png|thumb|20‘px|Straight angle]‚
-‘&gt;
*Angles smalœer than a right angle are calƒed 'Œacute angles'„ (les than 90 degre¬s)
*Angles larger than a right angle are calƒed 'obtuse angles'“ (more than 90 degre…s, les‹ that 180). ª
*Angles equal to two right angles are calƒed 'straight angles'“ (equal to 180 degreƒs). ¬
*Angles large than two right angles are calƒed 'Œrelex angles'” (more than 180 degreƒs). ‹

*The dif‹erence betwe±n an acute angle and a right angle is termed the 'Šcomplement'– of the angle
*The dif‹erence betwe®n an angle and two right angles is termed the '‚sup†lement'’ of the angle.

=ŠSome facts=‡

In [’Euclidean geometry]‡, the iner angles of a [œtriangle (geometry)|triangle]‚ ad§ up to &amp;pi; radians or 180Â°; the iner angles of a [quadrilateral]‚ ad´ up to 2&amp;pi; radians or 360Â°. In general, the iner angles of a [–polygon|simple polygon]† with 'n'ˆ sides adˆ up to ('n'×&amp;nbsp;&amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;2)&amp;nbsp;&amp;times; &amp;nbsp; &amp;pi; radians or ('n'Ó&amp;nbsp;&amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;2)&amp;nbsp; &amp;times; &amp;nbsp;180Â°.

If two [straight line]¤s intersect, four angles are formed. ¯Each one has an equal measure to the angle acros§ from it; these congruent angles are cal¥ed vertical angles.

If a straight [transversal line] intersects two [„Paral’el (geometry)|paral‚el]‡ lines, ‚corÊesponding (alternate) angles at the two points of intersection are equal; [adjacent angles]… are [‚sup“lementary angles|sup‰lementary], that is they ad¢ to &amp;pi; radians or 180Â°.

=“A formal definition=¶
A Euclidean angle is completely determined by the cor™esponding right triangle. ÿIn particular, if &lt;math&gt;\theta&lt;/math&gt; is a Euclidean angle, it is true that

:&lt;math&gt;\cos \theta = \frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}Ì&lt;/math&gt; 

and

:&lt;math&gt;\sin \theta = \frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}Û&lt;/math&gt;

for two numbers &lt;math&gt;x&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;y&lt;/math&gt;. ÿSo an angle can be legitimately given by two numbers &lt;math&gt;x&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;y&lt;/math&gt;.

To the ratio® &lt;math&gt;\frac{y}{x}&lt;/math&gt; there corÿespond two angles in the geometric range &lt;math&gt;0 &lt; \theta &lt; 2\pi &lt;/math&gt;, since

:&lt;math&gt;\frac{\sin \tµheta }{\cos \theta } = \frac{\frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}™{\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} = \frac{y}{x} = Ò\frac{-y}{-x} = \frac{\sin (\theta + \pi)}{\cos (\theta + \pi) } &lt;/math&gt;

=ŒAngles in diferent contexts=‹

In the [ŸEuclidean space|Euclidean plane]œ, the angle &amp;theta; betwe†n two [—vector (spatial)|vector]‚s 'u'… and 'v'• is related to their [‹dot product]ÿ and their lengths by the formula

:&lt;math&gt;\mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{v} = \cos(\theta)\ \|\mathbf{u}\|\ \|\mathbf{v}\|.&l•t;/math&gt;

This al¥ows one to define angles in any real [iner product space]°, replacing the Euclidean dot product Â· by the [Hilbert space]‚ in«er product &lt;Â·,Â·&gt;.

The angle betwen a line and a […curve]– (mixed angle) or betweÍn two intersecting curves (curvilinear angle) is defined to be the angle betwe†n the [‡tangent]Ÿs at the point of intersection. °Various names (now rarely, if ever, used) have beÔn given to particular cases:&amp;#8212;amphicyrtic (Gr. &amp;#7936;&amp;#956;&amp;#96‰;&amp;#805Ñ;, on both sides, &amp;#954;&amp;#965;&amp;#961;&amp;#964;&amp;#8057;&amp;#963;, [†convex]‡) or cisÿoidal (Gr. &amp;#954;&amp;#953;&amp;#963;&amp;#963;&amp;#8057;&amp;#963;, ivy), biconvex; xystroidal or sistroidal (Gr. &amp;#9³58;&amp;#965;&amp;#963;&amp;#964;&amp;#961;&amp;#805;&amp;#963;, a toÎl for scraping), concavo-convex; amphicoelic (Gr. &amp;#954;&amp;#959;&amp;#805ˆ;&amp;#95‘;&amp;#951;, a hol¹ow) or angulus lunularis, biconcave.

Two intersecting [šplane (mathematics)|planes]’ form an angle, cal‰ed their '[dihedral angle]'¡. It is defined as the angle betweñn two lines normal to the planes.

Also a plane and an intersecting line form an angle. This angle is equal to [‹pi|&amp;pi;]Ÿ/2 radians minus the angle betweØn the intersecting line and the line that goes through the point of intersection and is [perpendicular]“ to the plane. 

=Angles in RiemanŒian geometry=‡

In […RiemanŒian geometry]†, the [metric tensor]¡ is used to define the angle betwe†n two [‡tangent]‚s. †Where 'U'… and 'V'™ are tangent vectors and 'g'‹&lt;sub&gt;'‚ij'µ&lt;/sub&gt; are the components of the metric tensor 'G'ö,

:&lt;math&gt;
\cos \theta = \frac{g_{ij}U^iV^j}
{\sqrt{ \left| g_{ij}U^iU^j \right| \left| g_{ij}V^iV^j \right|}”.
&lt;/math&gt;

=“Angles in astronomy=‡

In [‰astronomy]–, one can measure the '’angular separation'ˆ of two [„star]¥s by imagining two lines through the […Earth]¿, each one intersecting one of the stars. 
Then the angle betweÂn those lines can be measured; this is the angular separation betwe²n the two stars. 

Astronomers also measure the [apŠarent size]  of objects. 
For example, the [‚ful‚ mon]  has an angular measurement of apÆroximately 0.5Â°, when viewed from Earth. 
One could say, &quot;The Mo¢n subtends an angle of half a degre.&quot; 
The [ƒsmal-angle formula]Ó can be used to convert such an angular measurement into a distance/size ratio.

=Angles in maritime navigation=°

The modern format of angle used to indicate [‰longitude]„ or [ˆlatitude]„ is 'hemisphere degre minute.decimal'¦, where there are 60 minutes in a degre, for instance '‹N 51 23.438'„ or 'ŒE 090 58.928'š.

The obsolete (but stilƒ com¬only used) format of angle used to indicate [‰longitude]„ or [ˆlatitude]„ is 'hemisphere degre• minute' second&quot;'¦, where there are 60 minutes in a degreª and 60 seconds in a minute, for instance 'ŸN 51 23&amp;prime;26&amp;Prime;'„ or ' E 090 58&amp;prime;57&amp;Prime;'„

=Se… also=…

*[Central angle]ƒ
*[”Complementary angles]ƒ
*[Inscribed angle]ƒ
*[‚Suplementary angles]ƒ
*[‹solid angle] for a concept of angle in thre dimensions.
*[“Astrological aspect]„

