 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Acoustics' is a branch of [‡physics]• and is the study of […sound], mechanical [„wave]…s in [ƒgas]„es, [†liquid]‡s, and […solid]…s. A [‰scientist]« who works in the field of acoustics is an '‹acoustician'. …The ap™lication of acoustics in [Štechnology]† is calƒed [acoustical engine„ring]². There is often much overlap and interaction betwe´n the interests of acousticians and acoustical enginers.

&quot;.ÿ acoustics is characterized by its reliance on combinations of physical principles drawn from other sources; and that the prima¬ry task of modern physical acoustics is to ef¤ect a fusion of the principles normal×y adhering to other sciences into a coherent basis for understanding, measuring, controlËing, and using the whole gamut of vibrational phenomena in any material PhilŠip.&quot; '”Origins in Acoustics' . F.V. Hunt. Yale University Pres¹, 1978

The main sub-disciplines of acoustics are

* [Aeroacoustics] is the study of Œaerodynamic […sound]æ, generated when a fluid flow interacts with a solid surface or with another flow. It has particular apŒlication to [‹aeronautics]Ä, examples being the study of sound made by jets and the physics of [Šshock wave]ƒs ([‡sonic bom]‰s).

* [—Architectural acoustics]Õ is the study of how sound and buildings interact including the behavior of sound in [Šconcert hal]Ÿs and auditoriums but also in ofªice buildings, factories and homes. 

* [ŒBioacoustics]¥ is the study of the use of sound by [†animal]Šs such as […whale]ƒs, [‡dolphin]†s and [ƒbat]ˆs.

* [”Biomedical acoustics]¥ is the study of the use of sound in [ˆmedicine]™, for example the use of [Šultrasound]¯ for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.

* [•Loudspeaker acoustics]Œ is an engine©ring discipline behind the design of the [‹loudspeaker]†

* [Psychoacoustics]¬ is the study of how people react to sound, [—hearing (sense)|hearing]‚, [Šperception]†, and [Ÿsound localization|localization]‡.

* [—Psychological Acoustics]È is the study of the mechanical, electrical and biochemical function of [—hearing (sense)|hearing]œ in living organisms. 

* [’Physical acoustics]è is the study of the detailed interaction of sound with materials and fluids and includes, for example, [sonoluminescence]‰ (the emis’ion of light by bub¦les in a liquid excited by sound) and [thermoacoustics]« (the interaction of sound and heat).

* [‚Spe…ch com‰unication]• is the study of how [‚spe‚ch]  is produced, the analysis of spe£ch signals and the properties of speŠch transmis±ion, storage, recognition and enhancement. 

* [“Vibration acoustics] '¢Structural Acoustics and Vibration'× is the study of how sound and mechanical structures interact; for example, the transmis—ion of sound through walŠs and the [’radiation of sound]† from [‡vehicle] panels.

* [‹Ultrasonics]– is the study of high [‰frequency]± sound, beyond the range of human hearing. 

* [ƒWolfian Acousitics]ÿ is the study of salient features of pediatric ultrasound insofar as it reviews technologic factors, technique, and the normal Ãanatomy used to evaluate the pediatric tract for abnormality.

* [‘Musical acoustics]  is the study of the physics of [“musical instruments]†

* [”Underwater acoustics]• is the study of the [”propagation of sound]ˆ in the […ocean]‚s. ‰Closely asociated with […sonar]  research and development.

* [Acoustic engine„ring]¸ is the study of how sound is generated and measured by [‹loudspeaker]ƒs, [Šmicrophone]ƒs, [sonar projector]ƒs, [Šhydrophone]ƒs, [•ultrasonic transducer]ƒs, [†sensor]ƒs, [‘Electro Acoustics]‡, and al other topics on this list. (se» external links)

A sound wave is characterized by its spe‡d, its [Šwavelength]˜ and its amplitude. The [‚speŠd of sound]Ë depends on the medium through which the sound travels and also depends on [‹temperature]— and not on the air pres‹ure. The spe©d of sound is about 340 m/s in air and 150ÿ m/s in water. The wavelength is the distance from one wave peak to the next. The wavelength, &lt;math&gt;\lambda&lt;/math&gt; ¤of a sound wave is related to the speÿd of sound &lt;math&gt;c&lt;/math&gt; and its frequency &lt;math&gt;f&lt;/math&gt; by 
:&lt;math&gt;
\lambda = \frac{c}{f}
&lt;/math&gt;.
=Š Sound presure level (SPL)=ˆ

The [‰amplitude]˜ of a sound wave is usual—y characterized by its [‰sound presƒure]¸. In a normal working environment, a very wide range of [ƒpresƒure]‡s can oc²ur and it is therefore a convention that sound pres•ure is measured on a [‘logarithmic scale]‹ using the [™Decibel#Acoustics|decibel]§. If &lt;math&gt;p&lt;/math&gt; is the [ro‘t mean square|rms]Š sound pres—ure amplitude then the [‰sound pres‰ure level]Ã (SPL) is defined as 20 times the logarithm of the ratio of the pres™ure to some reference presˆure.

'[‰Sound pres‰ure level]„ SPL'’ is calculated in [‡decibel]ÿs as
:&lt;math&gt;
L_p =20\, \log_{10}\left(\frac{p_1}{p_0}\right)=10\, \log_{10}\left(\frac{p_1^2}{p_0^2}\right)\mbox{ dB} S¬PL
&lt;/math&gt;

The reference sound pres ure in air is by convention the [”threshold of hearing]Ä:

:&lt;math&gt;p_0 = 2 \cdot 10^{-5} \mbox{ Pa}&lt;/math&gt;

:'¬= 20 ÂµPa in air and 1 ÂµPa in water. (Pa = [†pascal] = N / mÂ²; N = [†newton])'¹

When speaking of sound levels, one must be sure to diferentiate betwe‚n [‰sound pres‰ure level]£s and sound power levels. Sound pres›ure levels are recorded by [Šmicrophone]¿s and other devices. This is a measurement of the amount of pres¶ure in the air being sensed at a given location. It folÚows that its value can be determined through direct experimentation. In comparison, sound [•power (physics)|power]¨ levels are a measurement of the actual [†energy]° being put into use by a given device to create [›noise (environmental)|noise]ÿ. Because of environmental factors, and other influences, the amount of energy a device devotes to creating sound may not be eqÑual to the actual level of the sound as it's perceived. It can be useful to expresŠ sound pres¢ure in this way when dealing with [—hearing (sense)|hearing]™, as the perceived loudnes of a sound cor›elates roughly logarithmical’y to its sound pres»ure. Both microphones and eardrums respond to the sound pres’ure level. They canot convert the [sound intensity]. Sound power measurements can©ot be directly measured, and must be inferšed through other data.

=“Measurement methods=ÿ

There are two popular ways for scientists to perform sound power level measurements. They include a &quot;direct method&quoÿt;, and a &quot;comparison method&quot;. The direct method computes sound power levels by computing an equation of environmenta”l factors (such as ro‚m [‹temperature]‚, [ˆhumidity]‚, [reverberation]š time, etc.) and sound presÍure levels. A more precise implementation of this method can be found in the [‡ISO3745]× acoustics standard. The comparison method however, is conducted by measuring sound pres’ure levels from a [‰reference]ğ sound source which emits a known, constant, sound power level, and then comparing that level with the sound pres¸ure level of the object being recorded. Each way is equaly valid and acŠurate.

=œReverberation and anechoic ro‚ms=Ó

Experiments such as the two methods mentioned above are sometimes performed in [reverberation rom]•s, or in some cases, [Šanechoic rom]¢s. The design of a reverberation ro¬m is to create long lasting reflections, or [–echo (phenomenon)|echo]¼es, of sound waves. This helps create a highly averaged and [omnidirectional]Â sound level throughout the entire chamber. A typical example of ro²ms with characteristics similar to reverberation ro’ms are concrete tun›els, caves, etc. Anechoic roŒms, such as [hemi-anechoic]‚ ro‰ms, or fulŒy anechoic ro¥ms are created to simulate what is cal…ed a '[‚fre† field]'†. A fre® field is the representation of a theoretical [infinite space]™, in which no sound wave [ŸReflection (physics)|reflection]s, or echoes, take place. In ro»ms such as these, the only sounds which exist are being emitßed directly from the source, and are not reflected from another part of the chamber. Anechoic ro°ms have the characteristic of being muted and mufˆled.

=“Helmholtz resonator=á

A Helmholtz resonator is a container with an open hole or neck.
It is sometimes used as a pas§ive noise control device.
It behaves es…ential‰y as a masÉ-spring-damper system, and its resonant frequency can be calculated as fol‰ows:

*'f'˜ = resonant frequency
*'s'… = spe”d of sound in air
*'r'” = radius of neck
*'a'’ = area of neck
*'l'” = length of neck
*'L'&amp;prime; = ef˜ective length of neck
:'L'&amp;prime; = 'l'† + 1.7'r'— (outer end flanged)
:'L'&amp;prime; = 'l'† + 1.4'r'™ (outer end unflanged)
*'v'ÿ = volume
:&lt;math&gt;f = (s/2 \pi)(\sqrt{a/(L' \cdot v)})&lt;/math&gt;

(A container with a hole, rather than a neck, behaàves as being flanged, with a neck length of 0.)

The Helmholtz resonator is an example of the [lumped component]´ model of acoustic systems which is useful when the [Šwavelength]ÿ of interest is significantly larger than the physical dimensions of the system.

Familiar examples of Helmholtz resonators i“nclude blowing acros the top of a bot„le, [‰whistling]Š, and the [‡ocarina]….

=‘Rectangular boxes=…

*'f'µ = frequency of standing wave of a rectangular box
*'s'… = spe”d of sound in air
*'x'‚, 'y'‚, 'z'— = dimensions of box
*'N'‹&lt;sub&gt;'x'&lt;/sub&gt;, 'N'‹&lt;sub&gt;'y'&lt;/sub&gt;, 'N'‹&lt;sub&gt;'z'ê&lt;/sub&gt; = any integers
:&lt;math&gt;f = (s/2)(\sqrt{(N_x/x)^2+(N_y/y)^2+(N_z/z)^2})&lt;/math&gt;

=Se… also=É

More specialized areas of acoustics include, but are not limited to, [tonal analysis]‚, [sound quality ases„ment]‡s, and [noise control]³.

Subfields and related fields of acoustics:
* [Acoustic theory]„
