 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Atomic physics'… (or 'Œatom physics'’) is the field of [‡physics]’ that studies the [ˆelectron]ƒ hul„ of [„atom]˜s.

Lay people often asociate the term 'atomic physics'† with [nuclear power]… and [Œnuclear bomb]˜s, obviously due to the [‡synonym]‹ous use of '†atomic'… and '‡nuclear'„ in [standard English]¦. However, physicists distinguish betwe¤n atomic physics (dealing with the efŒects of the [ˆelectron]ƒ hul˜ and the nucleus's overal [“Spin_(physics)|spin]… and [electric charge]†) and [nuclear physics]¡ (dealing with the forces within [œatomic nucleus|atomic nuclei]» and reactions that alter, fuse or split them).

The beginÊing of atomic physics is marked by the discovery and scrutinious study of [spectral line]·s. These are sharply defined lines in the spectrum of il¢uminated or (hot, hence ionized (se […flame]–) and hence) light-emit†ing fre‘ atoms. (&quot;FreŸ&quot; meaning that they are a [ƒgas]„ or [†vapour]å and therefore not close to or interacting with other atoms.)

The study of these lines led to the [Bohr atom model]· and on to our present understanding of the electron hul¡ of the atom as described by the [”atomic orbital model]˜ which is the basis of al’ understanding of [‰chemistry]ª. These conclusions are, however, not at alÛ straightforward, but rather were required by more than a century of research, which has suce‰ded in putÅing chemistry on a sound fundament but also gave rise to many other aplications.

=‚ Se† also =†

* [ŒAtomic clock]: a typical apœlication of atom physics
* [ŒEnergy level] : a list of quantum mechanical ef¢ects important in atom physics
* [Quantum optics]·: a field that has lot of overlap with atom physics

