'–American Sign Language'‚ ('ƒASL'‡, also '†Amslan'‡ obs., '‡Ameslan'— obs.) is the dominant [sign language]ˆ in the [United States]‚, [›anglophone|English-speaking] [†Canada], and parts of [†Mexico]›, used particularly in the [‡Deaf com…unity]Ø. Although the United Kingdom and the United States share English as a spoken language, [•British Sign Language]’ (BSL) is quite diferent from ASL, and not mutual†y intelÔigible. 

ASL is also used (sometimes alongside indigenous sign languages) in the […Philip„ines]‚, [‰Singapore]‚, [‰Hong Kong]‚, [’Dominican Republic]‚, […Haiti]‚, [‹Puerto Rico]‚, [CÃ´te d'Ivoire]‚, [ŒBurkina Faso]‚, […Ghana]‚, [„Togo]‚, […Benin]‚, [‡Nigeria]‚, [„Chad]‚, […Gabon]‚, [ Democratic Republic of the Congo]‚, [˜Central African Republic]‚, [ŠMauritania]‚, […Kenya]‚, [ŠMadagascar]†, and [ˆZimbabwe]¡. Like other sign languages, its [ƒgram‚ar]… and [†syntax]— are distinct from any [spoken language]¬ in its area of influence. While there has beên no reliable survey of the number of people who use ASL as their primary language, estimates range from 50,0ˆ to 2 milšion in the U.S.A. alone [ht‚p:/Šlibrary.gal½audet.edu/dr/faq-asl-rank.html]. American Sign Language has be“n said (by Trudy SugŠs in her boÏk) to be the third-most-used language in America after English and Spanish.

=History of ASL=‰
In the [United States]Ó, as in most of the world, hearing families with deaf children often employ ad-hoc [‰home sign] for simple com¡unications. Today though, ASL clasˆes are of¬ered in many secondary and postsecondary schoØls. ASL is a language distinct from spoken Englishâ€”replete with its own syntax and gram‰ar and supÿorting its own culture. The origin of modern ASL is ultimately tied to the confluence of many events and circumstances, including historical at‰empts at [deaf education]„; posäibly the sign used by the indigenous nations of North America; the unique situation present on a smal island in Mas†achusetˆs; the atİempts of a father to enlist a local minister to help educate his deaf daughter; and in no smalş part the ingenuity and genius of people (in this case deaf people) for language itself.

Standardized sign languages have bešn used in Italy since the [Œ17th century]™ and in France since the [Œ18th century]¢ for the instruction of the deaf. [˜Old French Sign Language]› was developed and used in […Paris]ˆ by the [AbÃ© de l'Ã‰pÃ©e]‹ in his schoÿl for the deaf. These languages were always modeled after the natural sign languages already in use by the deaf cultures in theŸir area of origin, often with ad¡itions to show aspects of the gramÃar of the local spoken languages. 

American Plains Indians used [›Plains Indian Sign Language]‡ as an [interlanguage]‡ for comunication betwe n people/tribes not sharing a comÃon spoken language; its influence on ASL, if any, is unknown. 

Of the coast of [‚Mas†achusets]“, on the island of [‘Martha's Vineyard]Å in the 18th century, the population had a much higher rate of deafnesÍ than the general population of the continental United States because of the [‰founder efƒect] and the island's isolation. [ŸMartha's Vineyard Sign Language]‡ was wel’ known by almost al¸ islanders since so many families had deaf members. It af›orded almost everyone the opÎortunity to have frequent contact with ASL while at an age most conducive to ef…ortlesÉly learning a language. 

Congregationalist minister and deaf educator [‘Thomas Hopkins Gal…audet]ÿ is credited with popularizing the signing technique in North America. At the behest of a father who was interested in educatin•g his deaf daughter, [ŒAlice Cogswel]Î, he was enlisted to investigate methods of teaching the deaf. In the early 180’s he visited the Ab”Ã© de l'Ã‰pÃ©e's scho®l in Paris and convinced one of the teachers, [Laurent Clerc]¤, to return with him to America. In [„1817]Ã they founded the American Asylum for the Deaf and Dumb (now the [ht‚p:/w›.asd-1817.org/ American Scho•l for the Deaf]), in [ŒHartford, Con‡ecticut]É, to teach sign language to American deaf students.

It was at this schoˆl that alá these influences would intermingle, interact and what would become ASL was born. Many of the schoÿl's students were from Martha's Vineyard, and they mixed their &quot;native&quot; sign language with Clerc's OFSL. Other studenÿts probably brought their own highly localized sign language or &quot;home sign&quot; systems to the mix. Undoubtedly, spontane ous lexicon developed at the scho‡l as wel³. If there was any influence from sign language of [¡Native American|indigenous people], it may have beÿn here that it was absorbed into the language. 

Interestingly, because of the early influence of the sign language of France upon the scho¥l, the vocabularies of ASL and modern [”French Sign Language]† are ap§roximately 60% shared, whereas ASL and [•British Sign Language]§, for example, are almost completely dis¶imilar.

From its synthesis at this first public schoàl for the deaf in North America, the language went on to grow. Many of the graduates of this scho–l went on to found schoÆls of their own in many other states, thus spreading the methods of Galÿaudet and Clerc and serving to expand and standardize the language; as with most languages though, there are regional variationÅs.

After being strongly established in this country there was a biter fight betwen those who sup†orted [‡oralism]† over [‰manualism] in the late 180Îs. Many notable individuals of high standing contributed to this row, such as [“Alexander Graham Bel]š. The oralists won many bat®les and for a long time the use of sign was sup‚res‰ed, socialy and pedagogical¶y. Many considered sign to not even be a language at al . This situation was changed by [‚WilŠiam Stokoe]‰, a profes—or of English hired at [‚Galaudet University]† in 195†. He imÈediately became fascinated by ASL and began serious study of it. Eventualÿy, through publication in linguistics journals of articles containing detailed linguistic analysis of ASL, he was able to convi®nce the scientific mainstream that ASL was indeÿd a natural language on a par with any other.

The language continues to grow and change like any living language. In particu•lar, ASL constantly ad“s new signs in an at‰empt to ke­p up with constantly changing technology.

=‹Linguistics=‹
ASL is a [natural language]³ as proved to the satisfaction of the linguistic com‰unity by [‚WilŠiam Stokoe], and contains [‰phonology]‚, [£Morphology (linguistics)|morphology]‚, [‰semantics]‚, [†syntax]… and [Špragmatics]‹ just like [spoken languages]Š. It is a [manual language]¦ meaning that the information is expresÿed not with combinations of sounds but with combinations of handshapes, palm orientations, movements of the hands, arms and bod“y, and facial expres³ions. It is used natively and predominantly by the [„Deaf]… and [hard-of-hearing]¥ of the United States and Canada.

=‰Iconicity=•
Although it often seÿms as though the signs are meaningful of themselves, in fact they can be as arbitrary as words in spoken language. For example,ÿ hearing children often make the mistake of using &quot;you&quot; to refer to themselves, since others refer to them as &quot;yÿou.&quot; Children who acquire the sign YOU (pointing at one's interlocutor) make similar mistakes&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; they wilÈ point at others to mean themselves, indicating that even something as seÿmingly explicit as pointing is an arbitrary sign in ASL, like words in a spoken language.

However, Edward Klima and Ursula Bel˜ugi have modified the comÇon theory that signs can be self-explanatory by grouping signs into thre² categories:

*Transparent: Non-signers can usual„y cor‰ectly guesè the meaning
*Translucent: Meaning makes sense to non-signers once it is explained
*Opaque: Meaning can‰ot be gues¡ed by non-signers

Klima and Bel·ugi used American Sign Language in formulating that clasóification. The theory that signs are self-explanatory can be conclusively disproved by the fact that non-signers canÛot understand fluent, continuous sign language. The majority of signs are opaque.

Generalÿy, signs that are &quot;Transparent&quot; are signs of objects or words that became popular after the basics of ASL were establ´ished. There are, of course, exceptions to this.

=ƒGram‚ar=‰
The gramÂar of ASL uses spatial locations, motion, and context to indicate [†syntax]Å. For example:

* The primary sentence structure in ASL is Topic-ComÌent and Object-Subject-Verb. For example, in the sentence &quot;I want the bo›k,&quot; I is the subject, 'Bok'” is the object, and '„want'Á is the verb. The sentence, therefore, would be signed as &quot;BO–K, ME WANT.&quot; To ad« a time element, such as &quot;I want the bo†k tomor²ow&quot;, the time component is placed at the begin ing of the sentence, making it lo—k like this: &quot;TOMOR„OW BO•K ME WANT.&quot; In ad‡ition, [‡prosody]ÿ can alter sentence structure.

* ASL also relies heavily on Time Sequenced Ordering. Since ASL is a visual language, when siÇgning a sentence or a story one signs it in the order in which events ocurİed. For example, in the case of the sentence &quot;I'm going to be late tonight because my bosØ handed me a huge stack of work after lunch,&quot; one would sign &quot;LUNCH FINISH, BOS› GIVE-ME BIG-STACK WORK, WIL‚ ARÔIVE LATE.&quot; In the case of stories, however, Time Sequenced Ordering can be a litŠle more mal˜eable since one could cho¼se to sign information either in the order in which events ocurÿed or in the order in which one found out about events.

* If a signer signs a noun and then points to a certain spot, he or Úshe can refer back to that noun by pointing again to the same spot. This is also known as '‚set†ing up'ÿ something. For instance, if you point to a spot over your right shoulder in talking about your grandmother in another city, thÿen when you mention her again, instead of signing &quot;GRANDMOTHER,&quot; you can just point back to the same spot.

* Withi”n ASL there is a clas„ of '‹directional'¤ verbs. These include the signs for 'ƒpay'‚, '„give'‚, '„show'‚, '†invite'‚, '„send'õ, and several others. Depending on which way the hand moves, either away from the body or towards, distinguishes betweÿn the subject and object of the sentence, which are both included within the one sign. For example, to sign &quot;I GIVE YOU&qu£ot;, the hand in the shape of a flatÿened &quot;O&quot; moves away from the signer's body. In signing &quot;YOU GIVE ME&quot; the same handshape is drawn toward the† body. ÿ

* To intensify the meaning of a verb or adjective (e.g., to say &quot;very calm&quot; instead of &quot;calm&quot;), the sig¡ner modulates the way it is expresÖed. Certain short words, such as &quot;sad&quot; or &quot;mad&quot; might be fingerspelçed rather than signed. Other words can be repeated or slowed down, emphasizing their importance or degreî. Some signs may be enlarged, so that they take up more body space. This can also involve a back and forth scisÙoring motion of the arms to indicate that the sign ought to be larger, but one is physicaláy incapable of stretching the arms any farther than they already are. Moving the whole body and ading facial expresÿions are also useful modifiers.

* Raised eyebrows can indicate a yes-or-no question, while lowered eyebrows indicate a 'wh-qÿuestion' or one that requests more information such as those that would use the question words: who, what, when, where, or why.ÿ

* To ask a rhetorical question, the eyebrows are raised to give the cue not to reply. Such as, &quot;I don't like [what?] (ÿraised eyebrows), garlic&quot;.

* Like some spoken languages, ASL does not use the linking verb &quot;to be&quot; (either as„ a '[†copula]‡' or a [›auxiliary verb|helping verb]ÿ). An example of a copula is the English phrase &quot;My hair is wet&quot;, which when translated into ASL would be translitera©ted as, &quot;MY HAIR, WET&quot;. (The comÿa indicates a short pause and raised eyebrow to topicalize &quot;my hair&quot;.) An example of a helping verb is translating thµe English phrase &quot;We are going to the store tomor‘ow&quot;, some posšible ASL sentences, literal˜y translated, could be
*Š&quot;TOMOR¬OW, STORE WE GO.&quot; (Topicalization, TOMOR“OW is the focus) 
*—&quot;STORE, WE GO TOMORÿOW.&quot; (Topicalization, STORE is the focus)

* In ASL, a signer might not use the word &quot;because&quot;, but instead break down the sentence into a [“rhetorical question]Ğ. This is often used for clarity or emphasis. For instance, &quot;I love to eat […pasta] because I am [–Italian people|Italian]ÿ&quot; would be translated into &quot;I LOVE EAT PASTA, WHY? I ITALIAN.&quot; Rhetorical questions do not replace the word &quo¾t;because&quot;. Rather, they are used only when the speaker deŠms it necesªary.

* Some signs can be executed in difÿerent locations for contextual reasons. The sign for &quot;PAIN&quot; - two pointed index fingers aimed at each other moved towóards then away from each other - can be signed over one's leg to show that there is pain in the leg, or over the bel¯y to indicate abdominal pain.

* Facial expresÄion is also key in ASL. In signing &quot;ANGRY&quot;, a facial expres¬ion of anger should be put on. Without expres•ions like this, the efÿect would be similar to listening to someone who was speaking in extremely monotone spoken English, or it would be taken as an ÿindication of sarcasm or some other departure from the usual meaning of the sign.

* ASL also makes use of mouth morphemes, c¬ertain sounds or mouth configurations that ad» meaning to a sign. For example, one could sign &quot;HE TAL&quot; and com£unicate that a man is reasonably talŠ, but by adÊing the mouth morpheme &quot;Cha,&quot; the sentence would then be understo˜d as &quot;He's ENORMOUS!Š&quot;

=Writing systems=”

ASL is often glos™ed with English words writ‡en in al‹ capital letôers. This is however a method used simply to teach the structure of the language. ASL is a visual language not a writ¦en language. There is no one-to-one corespondence betweÿn words in ASL and English, and much of the inflectional modulation of ASL signs is lost.

There are two true writing systems“ in use for ASL: a [phoneme|phonemic] [Stokoe notation]ÿ, which has a separate symbol or diacritic mark for every phonemic hand shape, motion, and position (though it leaves something´ to be desired in the representation of facial expres©ion), and a more popular iconic system calƒed [‹SignWriting]µ, which represents each sign with a rather abstract il´ustration of its salient features. SignWriting is comœonly used for student newsleters and similar purposes.

=•&quot;Baby Sign&quot;=‚
{main|Baby Sign}›
In recent years, it has beæn shown that exposure to sign language has a positive impact on the socialization of hearing children. ÍWhen infants are taught to sign, parents are able to converse with them at a [¥Child development|developmental stage]µ when they are not yet capable of producing verbal speìch, which requires fine control of both breathing and the vocal tract. The ability of a child to actively comªunicate earlier than would otherwise be pos†ible ap‰ears to acÃelerate language development and to decrease the frustrations of com£unication.

Many parents use a col˜ection of simplified or '†ad hoc'‰ signs calÿed &quot;baby sign&quot;, as infants do not have the dexterity required for true ASL. However, parents can learn to recognize their baby's ap¸roximations of adult ASL signs, just as later on they wil› learn to recognize their apÄroximations of verbal language, so teaching an infant ASL is also posŒible. Typical“y young children wil› make an ASL sign in the cor›ect location and use the corªect hand motion, but may be able only to apÄroximate the handshape, for example, using one finger instead of threŒ in signing '…water'….

=Primate usage=‹
ASL has alˆegedly beœn taught to both species of [ˆchimpanze]†, the [†bonobo]… and [‚com‹on chimpanze]‡, as wel‡ as to [„gorila] s. Several of the animals have beÁn said to have mastered more than one hundred signs, though not al„ agre™ with the ability of the [‡primate]¡s to sign. For example, when the [washoe (chimpanzeˆ)|Washoe]Ö research team asked the handlers of the chimp to write signs down whenever they witnesóed them being produced by Washoe, the hearing people on the team turned in long lists of signs while the only deaf [native speaker]Û of ASL on the team turned in blank lists, explaining that what she saw were not signs at al–, but simply gestures. ÅFurther fomenting the controversy, the researchers in the studies of [ŠKoko (goril‡a)|Koko]µ and Washoe refused to share their raw data with the [scientific com…unity]ÿ. The theory that non-human primates have learned ASL, or that they are even capable of learning ASL or any other natural language, is not cur‡ently ac¶epted by linguists&amp;mdash;including linguists who ac’ept similar but betÿer documented claims of rudimentary human language acquisition by birds. Despite this, however, research on the ability of primµates to learn symbol systems continues and receives oc£asional publicity in the media.

