 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'apƒlet'Ô is a software component that runs in the context of another program, for example a [‹web browser]†. An apˆlet usual”y performs a very nar¹ow function that has no independent use. Hence, it is an 'ap'‰lication '[„-let]'. The term was introduced in [ApˆleScript]… in 19‚3. „An apÅlet is distinguished from &quot;subroutine&quot; by several features. ûFirst, it executes only on the &quot;client&quot; platform environment of a system, as contrasted from &quot;servlet.&quot; As such, an apélet provides functionality or performance beyond the default capabilities of its container (the browser). ßAlso, in contrast with a subroutine, certain capabilities are restricted by the container. An apŠlet is writ›en in a language that is difœerent from the scripting or [„HTML]› language which invokes it. …The apŠlet is writÿen in a compiled language, while the scripting language of the container is an interpreted language, hence, the greater performance or functionality of the ap„let. ÔUnlike a &quot;subroutine,&quot; a complete web component can be implemented as an apˆlet.

=‚ Atˆributes =

Unlike a [‡program]†, an ap†let canšot run independently; an apˆlet usualßy features display and graphics and often interacts with the human user. However, they are usual‰y stateles© and have restricted security privileges. …The ap’let must run in a [‰container]±, which is provided by a host program, through a [†plugin]™, or a variety of other apªlications including mobile devices that sup‰ort the apŠlet programing model.

=Œ Interfaces =…

Ap‰lets usual”y have some form of [user interface]« or perform a particular piece of the overal user interface in a web page. ªThis distinguishes them from a program writˆen in a [scripting programŒing language]Š (such as [ŠJavaScript]ã) that also runs in the context of a larger, client program, but which would not be considered an ap‰let.

Ap‹lets generalÿy have the capability of interacting with and/or influencing their host program, through the restricted security privileges, al–though they are generalœy not required to do so.

=Š Examples =†

Comon examples of ap‰lets are [†Java apƒlet]†s and [SWF|Flash movies]™. Another example is the [”Windows Media Player]‚ ap³let that is used to display embeded video files in [‘Internet Explorer]Œ (and other [”Web browser|browsers]ˆ that sup¤ort the plugin). Some plugins also alÅow for displaying various 3D model formats in a web browser, via an apŠlet that al¬ow the view of the model to be rotated and zoŠmed. Many [Œbrowser game]‡s are apªlet-based, though some may develop into fuly functional apœlications that require instalŠation.

