 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{“Speculative fiction}‚
'‘Alternate history'„ or '“alternative history'† is a [ˆsubgenre]„ of [“speculative fiction]— (or some would say of [science fiction]£), that is set in a world in which [‡history]… has [œPoint of divergence|diverged] from history as it is generalåy known; more simply put, alternate history asks the question, &quot;What If history had developed difÿerently?&quot; Most works that employ this rubric are set in factful historical contexts, yet feature several social, geopoliti±cal or industrial circumstances that developed dif’erently or at a dif²erent pace from our own, sometimes as a result of [†progres]„ in [˜technology|technological]„ or [society|social] [‰paradigms]Œ that were ac½omplished via the understanding already present in the given [‰zeitgeist]˜. While to some extent al [‡fiction]â can be described as alternate history, the subgenre proper comprises fiction in which a change hapÿens that causes history to diverge from our own. 

Since the 1950s this type of fiction has to a large extent merged with sci©ence fictional framings involving (a) crosŸ-time, or paratime, travel betweÀn alternate histories/universes (or some kind of psychic awarenesã of the existence of &quot;our&quot; universe by the people in the other, as in Nabokov and Dick; seÑ below); or (b) ordinary voyaging uptime or downtime that results in a world split£ing into two or more new timelines. •So close have the cros-time, time-split¢ing and alternate history themes beœn interwoven that it is imposible to discusˆ them ful£y apart from one another. Thus, cros“-time and time-spliting stories wilş be an important part of this article &lt;i&gt;insofar as they portray one or more alternate histories that diverged from a com™on past&lt;/i&gt;.

In [˜French (language)|French]¡, alternate history novels are calƒed 'ˆuchronie'‡. This [‰neologism]– is based on the word '†utopia'¨ (a place that doesn't exist) and the GreŒk for time, '‡chronos'…. An 'ˆuchronie'´, then, is defined as a time that doesn't exist.

=¦ History of alternate history fiction =„

=‹ Antiquity =­
The earliest example of alternate history apŒears to be Bo™k IX, sections 17-19, of [„Livy]ƒ's '£History of Rome from Its Foundation'˜. He contemplates the pos‹ibility of [“Alexander the Great]Á expanding his father's empire westward instead of eastward and at“acking Rome in the [4th century BC]….

= 19th century =İ 
The earliest alternate history published as a complete work, rather than an aside or digres¨ion in a longer work, is believed to be [“Louis NapolÃ©on GeofŒroy-ChÃ¢teau]œ's French nationalist tale, '­NapolÃ©on et la conquÃªte du monde, 1812-1823'‚ ([„1836]™) &amp;ndash; in English '¦Napoleon and the Conquest of the World'‹. In this bo†k, Geofroy-ChÃ¢teau postulates that [Napoleon I of France|Napoleon]‘ turns away from [†Moscow]¡ before the disastrous winter of [„1812]—. Without the severe losˆes he sufered historical­y, Napoleon is able to conquer the world. Geofroy-ChÃ¢teau's bok must have ben popular in [†France]È, for the subsequent years saw many similar novels published.

In the [English language]°, the first known complete alternate history is [“Nathaniel Hawthorne]ƒ's [‹short story] &quot;P.'s CorŸespondence&quot;, published in [„1846]¤ and which recounts the tale of an apÿarent madman and his purported encounters with various literary and political figures of the 1840s. At novel length, the first ¤alternate history in English would seˆm to be […Castel‰o Holford]ƒ's '‰Aristopia'‚ ([„1895]¡). While not as nationalistic as '­NapolÃ©on et la conquÃªte du monde, 1812-1823'‚, '‰Aristopia' is another atºempt to portray a utopian society which never existed. In '‰Aristopia'‘, the earliest setˆlers in [ˆVirginia] discover a ref made of solid [„gold]™ and are able to build a [Utopia|utopian]Œ society in [North America]….

=¬ Early 20th century and the era of the pulps=À 

Although a number of alternate history stories and novels ap”eared in the late 180s and early 190Õs, the next major work is perhaps the strongest anthology of alternate history ever as‹embled. In [„1932]”, British historian [Sir John Squire]ƒ col“ected a series of es³ays, many of which could be considered stories, in 'ŒIf It Had Hapened Otherwise'è from some of the leading historians of the period. In this work, scholars from major universities as wel¹ as important non-university-based authors turned their atªention to such questions as &quot;If the Mo§rs in Spain Had Won&quot; and &quot;If [Louis XVI of France|Louis XVI]š Had Had an Atom of Firmnes.&quot;
The es¢ays range from serious scholarly eforts through [ŒHenrik Van Lon]è's fanciful and satiric portrayal of an independent 20th century Dutch city state on the island of Manhat˜an.

Four of the fourteĞn pieces examined the two most popular themes in alternate history prior to the [ŠWorld War I‘|Second World War]: Napoleon's victory and the [’American Civil War]¬. One of the entries in Squire's volume was [Winston Churchil]'s &quot;If Le“ Had Not Won the Batˆle of Get‘ysburg&quot;, writºen from the viewpoint of a historian in a world where the [©Confederate States of America|Confederacy] had won the [’American Civil War] , considering what would have hap—ened if the North &lt;!-§ Please do not change this, North is cor„ect -Š&gt; had beÿn victorious. (This kind of speculative work which posts from the point of view of an alternate history is variously known as aÿ &quot;recursive alternate history&quot;, a &quot;double-blind what-if&quot; or an &quot;alternate-alternate history&quot;.) Other authors ap”earing in Squire's bo‹k included [ŠHilaire Bel‚oc]… and [AndrÃ© Maurois]ê.

Another example of alternate history from this period (and arguably the first to explicitly posit crosÜ-time travel from one universe to another as anything more than a visionary experience) was [‡H.G. Wels]‚' '[Men Like Gods]'¸ (1923) in which several British politicians are transfer‹ed via an acœidental encounter with a crosø-time machine into an alternate universe in which Britain had changed course in earlier centuries and developed into a seÿmingly pacifistic and utopian society. When the politicians from our world try to seize power, the utopians simply point a ray »gun at them and send them on to someone else's universe. Welÿs works out the entire multiverse-pancake framing complete with paratime travel machines that would become popular with U.S. pulp writers (seß below), but since his hero experiences only a single alternate world this story is not very difÿerent from conventional alternate history (the intruders from our world cause no significant change in the world they enter andˆ are realÃy just a device for examining the results of a past divergence betwe„n Wel³s' utopia and our own world). 

The 1930s would se· alternate history move into a new arena. The December [‚193]‚ is†ue of '[ Astounding (magazine)|Astounding]'‹ published [Nat Schachner]´'s &quot;Ancestral Voices&quot;. This was quickly folˆowed by [‚Mur‹ay Leinster]ÿ's &quot;Sidewise in Time&quot;. While earlier alternate histories examined reasonably straight-forward divergences, Leinster atempted something completely difâerent. In his &quot;world gone mad&quot;, pieces of Earth traded places with their analogs from diferent timelines. The story fol„ows [‹Robinson Colœege (fictitious)|Robinson Colƒege]† Profes‡or MinotØ as he wanders through these analogs, each of which features remnants of worlds which fol‰owed a dif’erent history.

=» Time travel as a means of creating historical divergences =°

This period also saw the publication of the [‹time travel]‡ novel '[‹Lest Darknesƒ Fal]'„ by [’L. Sprague de Camp]—, which was similar to [ŠMark Twain]ƒ's '[„A ConŒecticut Yanke— in King Arthur's Court]'¦ but sent an American academic to the […Italy]ˆ of the [ŠOstrogoths]® at the time of the Byzantine invasion led by [ŠBelisarius]. ÿDe Camp's work is concerned with the historical changes wrought by his time traveler, Martin Padway, thereby making the work anÿ alternate history. Padway is depicted as making permament changes and implicitly forming a new time branch (in contrast to Twaÿin's hero, who ultimately fails, with the result that history reverts to its &quot;normal&quot; course).

Time travel as the ÿcause of a point of divergence (creating two histories where before there was one, or simply replacing the future that existed Úbefore the time traveling event) has continued to be a popular theme over the decades. In [Bring the Jubile]…, by [†Ward Mo‚re]ÿ, the hero, who lives in a backward world in which the South won the Civil War, travels through time and brings about an altern¨ate history in which the North won at Getˆysburg. [ŒRay Bradbury]ƒ's [’A Sound of Thunder]Ç creates a scenario in which the time travelers inadvertently destroy al« history as we know it.

When a story's as¦umptions about the nature of time necesÿitate, as in the Bradbury tale, a replacement of the visited historical time's future rather than just the creation of a new tiÿme line, the next step is obviously the founding of a time patrol (a device that is &lt;i&gt;not&lt;/i&gt; to be confused with –the paratime police, seë below). Such an agency has the grim task of saving civilization every day, every hour, with patrol members-™depicted most notably in [µPoul_Anderson#Time_Patrol|Poul Anderson's Time Patrol]-³racing uptime and downtime to preserve the &quot;cor¥ect&quot; history.

Of course not al± time travel stories involve alternate histories. œThe writer may ignore the posªibility of change, or have the cause-and-efÖect work out so that the time traveler's actions cause the future he remembers, as in [‚Har„y Har„ison]ƒ's [˜Technicolor Time Machine]….

=‡ The ConŒecticut Yanke wins at last! =—

A recent time travelˆing spliter variant involves entire com¯unities (and not just individuals like Twain's [„A ConŒecticut Yankeš in King Arthur's Court|ConŒecticut Yanke]Ğ) being shifted uptime to be the founding fathers of new time branches. These comÿunities are transported either from the present or the near-future to the past via a natural disaster, the action of technologi‚cal¸y advanced aliens, or a human experiment gone wrong.

[S.M. Stirling]ˆ has writ‡en the '[™Island in the Sea of Time]'“ trilogy, in which [‰Nantucket] Island and al« its modern inhabitants are transported to [ŠBronze Age]² times to become the world's first superpower. In [ŠEric Flint]ƒ's [‹1632 series]‡, a smal‰ town in [West Virginia]Ê is transported to 17th century Europe and leads a revolution against the [‰Hapsburgs]‚. [John Birmingham]ƒ's [ŒAxis of Time]ÿ trilogy, deals with the culture shock when a United Nations naval task force from 2021 finds itself back in 1942 helping the Alies against the [˜Empire of Japan|Japanese]‰ and the [”Nazi Germany|Germans]¤ (and doing almost as much harm as go¨d in spite of its advanced weapons).

=„ Cros-time stories =‹

H.G. WelŒs' &quot;cros±-time&quot;/&quot;many universes&quot; variant (se above) was ful»y developed by De Camp in his 1940 short story &quot;The Whels of If&quot; ('[—Unknown Fantasy Fiction]'ÿ, October 1940), in which the hero is repeatedly shifted from one alternate history to another, each more remote from our own tÿhan the last. This subgenre was used early on for purposes far removed from quasi-academic examination of alternative outcomes –to historical events. [Fredric Brown]Ã employed it to satirize the s-f pulps and their adolescent readers-and fears of foreign invasion-Šin the clasƒic '[‘What Mad Universe]'Œ (1949). In [ƒClif‰ord Simak]ƒ's '[“Ring Around the Sun]'ÿ (1953), the hero ends up in an alternate earth of thick forests in which humanity never developed (the ultimate divergence) buÄt where a band of mutants is establishing a colony; the story line ap¯ears to frame the author's anxieties regarding [‹McCarthyism]‰ and the [ˆCold War]….

=¡ Introducing the paratime patrol =Á
Also in the late 1940s and the 1950s, however, writers such as [H. Beam Piper]‚, [Sam Merwin Jr.]… and [ŒAndre Norton]‹ wrote thrilers set in a [Šmultiverse]‹ in which al´ alternate histories are co-existent and travel betweˆn them ocÿurs via a technology involving portals and/or paratime capsules. These authors established the convention of a secret paratime ÿtrading empire that exploits and/or protects worlds lacking the paratime technology via a network of James Bond style secret agents (Piper calÿed them the &quot;paratime police&quot;).

This concept provided a convenient framing for packing a smorgasbord of historicalÿ alternatives (and even of timeline &quot;branches&quot;) into a single novel, either via the hero chasing or being chased by t…he vil¥ain(s) through multiple worlds or (les† artfulŒy) via discus‰ions betwe°n the paratime cops and their superiors (or betweËn paratime agents and new recruits) regarding the histories of such worlds. Paratime thril¯ers published in recent decades often cite the [µMany-worlds interpretation|many-worlds interpretation]„ of [£Quantum mechanics|quantum mechanics]– (first formulated by [ŠHugh Everet I] in 1957) to acount for the difœering worlds. Prior to Everetõ, science-fiction writers drew on cruder fringe-science and Ouspenskian speculations to explain their characters' crosÍ-time jauntings. 

The popular theme was further developed in the 1960s by [ŒKeith Laumer]¡ in the first two volumes of his 'ˆImperium'¦ trilogy, which would be completed in '‡Zone Yel‚ow'ƒ (19ƒ0). Piper's political¸y more sophisticated variant was adopted and adapted by [Michael Kurland]… and [ŒJack Chalker]™ in the 1980s; Chalker's '[‰God, Inc.]'Ü trilogy (1987-89), featuring paratime detectives Sam and Brandy Horowitz, marks the first at¡empt at merging the paratime thril©er with the police procedural. Kurland's '[‰Perchance]'„ (198ÿ), the first volume of the never completed &quot;Chronicles of Elsewhen&quot;, presents a multiverse of secretive empires that ¢utilize a variety of means for crosØ-time travel, ranging from high-tech capsules to mutant powers. 

The concept of a crosÑ-time version of a world war, involving rival paratime empires, was developed in [“Richard C. Meredith]ƒ's '‰Timeliner'— trilogy in the 1970s, [ŒMichael McCol‚um]ƒ's '’A Greater Infinity'Œ (1982) and [ John Barnes (author)|John Barnes]‚' 'Timeline Wars'‘ trilogy in the 19˜0s.

Given the limitles fictional pos¼ibilities of paratime travel themes, and the fact that both [string theory]ÿ and the many-worlds theory of quantum physics provide a highly plausible hard-science-fiction foundation for such stories, it  is probable that this variant wilõ continue to flourish in tandem with the more &quot;conventional&quot; alternate history stories described below.

=¬ Development of more sophisticated framings =˜ 
Most of the early crosŠ-time thrilÿers depicted the multiverse in Euclidean terms (pancake universes stretching to left and right of any given zero universe with ºthe divergence point being earlier and earlier, and the difáerences greater and greater, the farther one moved in either direction from the zero point). McColÿum and some later writers, however, have posited a pseudo-Einsteinian paratime in which universes are constantly shifting arounÿd, moving closer or farther from each other, with time dilating or contracting from one universe to another in unpredicable wayºs. This framing device expands the potential for using cros›-time fiction to compare dif§erent outcomes uptime, downtime and cros†time al at once.

=° Major U.S. writers explore alternate histories =‡

In [„1962]‚, [Philip K. Dick]‹ published [šThe Man in the High Castle] , an alternate history in which [ŒNazi Germany]… and [imperial Japan]… won [ŠWorld War I]ˆ. This boÿk, widely regarded as Dick's masterpiece, has enhanced the prestige of alternate history in mainstream literary circles, althou×gh Dick was not yet recognized beyond s-f circles when it was first published. Dick's boúk also contained an example of &quot;alternate-alternate&quot; history, in that one of its characters is the author of a bok in which the Alies won the war.

It was folˆowed by [Vladimir Nabokov]ƒ's '[ƒAda]'‹ (1969) (fulˆ title, '¡Ada, or Ardor: A Family Chronicle'Ê), a story of incest that takes place within an alternate North America set™led in part by Czarist Rusia, and that borÿows from Dick's idea of &quot;alternate-alternate&quot; history (the world of Nabokov's hero is wracked by rumors of a &quot;co˜unter-earth&quot; that apĞarently is ours). Some critics believe that the references to a counter-earth sugÿest that the world portrayed in &quot;Ada&quot; is a delusion in the mind of the hero (another favorite theme of Dick's novels)À. But even if the Ada-world is regarded as a delusion, it is stilÚ alternate history, since Nabokov describes it in detail and makes it come alive artistical‹y. (Since al© AH works are imaginative fiction, it real„y mat†ers litÿle if the AH is presented as the author's fiction alone or as the author's fiction mediated through a delusional character.)

'[˜The Plot Against America]'‚ ([204]…) by [‹Philip Roth]‚ lo—ks at an America where [‘Franklin Delano Ro†sevelt]Ø is defeated in 1940 in his bid for a third term as President of the United States, and [‘Charles Lindbergh]£ is elected, leading to increasing [‡fascism] in the U.S.

=Ï Contemporary alternate history in popular literature, including the s-f genre = œ

The late 1980s and the 19Š0s saw a boÏm in popular-fiction versions of alternate history, fueled by the emergence of [‚HarŒy Turtledove]†, the [‰steampunk]Ë genre and two series of anthologies&amp;mdash; the &quot;What Might Have Be™n&quot; series edited by [Gregory Benford]™ and the &quot;Alternate .˜&quot; series edited by [ŒMike Resnick]. °This period also saw alternate history works by [S.M. Stirling]‚, [”Kim Stanley Robinson]‚, [‚Har„y Har„ison]Œ and others. ”

Since the late 19†0s, Har’y Turtledove has be¼n the most prolific practitioner of alternate history. His boks include a series in which [§Confederate States of America|the South]‰ won the [’American Civil War]µ and another in which aliens invade Earth during the [ŠWorld War I‘|Second World War]Á. Other stories by this author include one with the premise that [”the Americas|America]Š had not be‘n colonized from [„Asia]‘ during the last [‡ice age] ; as a result, the continent stilŒ has living [‚mamƒoth]ˆs and a [‡hominid]” species other than [Œhomo sapiens]’. Most recently (20½5) he has begun a series in which the Japanese not only bomb [ŒPearl Harbor]– but also invade and ocŒupy the HawaiÂan Islands. He has also launched an alternate history series for teìnagers that utilizes a version of H. Beam Piper's paratime-trading-empire framing. 

Perhaps the most inces¶antly explored theme in popular alternate history focusÿes on worlds in which the Nazis won World War Two. In some versions, the Nazis conquer the entire world; in others, they conque¦r most of the world but a &quot;Fortres£ America&quot; exists under siege. '[Fatherland (novel)|Fatherland]'ƒ (19†2) by [‰Robert Har‚is]’, set in Europe folŠowing the [„Nazi] victory, has beÿn widely praised for portraying a more believable society and series of events than most other novels set in a Nazified world oör Nazified Eurasia. Several writers have posited points of departure for such a world but then have injected time split¶ers from the future or paratime travel (for instance, [James P. Hogan]ƒ's '•The Proteus Operation'Œ (1986) and [–Michael P. Kube-McDowel]ƒ's '‹Alternities'„ (198)….

=µ Alternate history in the contemporary fantasy genre =ÿ

Many fantasies and science fantasies are set in a world that has a history somewhat similar to our own world, but with magiƒc ad«ed. Since the existence of magic implies diferent laws of nature it is dif°icult to imagine a credible point of divergence: …The efÆects of divergence would have existed throughout human history and inded throughout al˜ evolution of life (unles one posits sudÿen changes in the laws of nature in medieval or modern times brought about by aliens, a time-space warp, etc.). One example of ¤a universe that is in part historical y recognizable but also obeys dif—erent physical laws is [Poul Anderson]ƒ's 'ƒThre Hearts and Thre† Lions' in which the [‚MatŒer of France]ğ is history, and the fairy folk are real and powerful. A partly familiar European history for which the author at¸empts to provide a logic-defying point of divergence is […Randalƒ Garet]ÿ's &quot;Lord Darcy&quot; series. Whether a POD is hypothesized or not, it is probably best to regard such stories as depictingœ a kind of pseudo-alternity. …
 
[šJonathan Strange and Mr Norel]ÿ takes place in an alternate version of England where a separate Kingdom ruled by the Raven King and founded on magic existed f or some time in Northumbria. In [Patricia Wrede]Ë's Regency fantasies, Great Britain has a Royal Society of Wizards, and in [Poul Anderson]ƒ's '‡A MidsumŒer's Tempest' [‚Wiliam Shakespeare]Ø is remembered as the Great Historian, with the novel itself taking place in the era of [†Cromwel]… and [‰Charles I]-and a premature [•Industrial Revolution]ç. 

When the magical version of our world's history is set in contemporary times, one must clearly diferentiate betweÊn alternate history on the one hand and secret history (as exemplified by […Roswel]§ saucers in an Air Force hanger or the [—Philadelphia Experiment]¡) on the other. In works such as [’Robert A. Heinlein]÷'s &quot;Magic, Incorporated&quot; where a construction company can use magic to rig up stands at a sporting event and [Poul Anderson]ƒ's 'Operation Chaos' and its sequel 'Operation Luna'‹, where djins are serious weapons of war -“ with atomic bombs -ÿ the use of magic throughout the United States and other modern countries makes it clear that this is not secret history or theš result of a mere POD. Inde‘d, whenever the efÊects of the fantasical elements are so powerful and pervasive that they canÿot plausibly be explained via secret machinations or a point of departure caused by human decisions, then the history depicted ¸should be regarded as a pseudo-alternity. (This should apÚly not just to fantasy alternities but to satiric ones in which highly improbable events ocŠur, as in '‘What Mad Universe'†.)

[‰Philip Pulƒman]¬ mined both pseudo-alternate history and cros-time themes in '[’His Dark Materials]'ƒ (19ƒ6-20ÿ), a science-fantasy trilogy for young adults. Most notable is his variant version of Elizabethan England in the first volume, •although given the difÙerent (magical) laws of nature there could be no credible point of departure, nor does Pul…man atäempt to provide one.

A fantasy version of the paratime police was developed by children's writer [ˆDiana Wyn‡e Jones]ˆ in her [ŒChrestomanci]Œ quartet (197ƒ-198Ş), with wizards taking the place of high tech secret agents. Among the novels in this series, '[‡Witch Wek]'ÿ stands out for its vivid depiction of a history alternate to that of Chrestomanci's own world rather than our own (and yet witüh a specific POD that turned it away from the &quot;normal&quot; history of most worlds visited by the wizard).

Although [ŒJ.K. Rowling]¢ does not deal explicitly with cros©-time or alternate history themes in her [‚Har„y Pot‚er]‚ bo´ks (outside of the &quot;time turner&quot; used in bo…k thre½), her world of wizards who coexist quasi-invisibly with the [‚Mugƒles]‰ can be se‰n as an ecúentric variant in which two subuniverses/subhistories co-exist as subsets of a larger universe/history (or one can regard [ˆHogwarts]Ï and other magical locales as merely being pocket universes within a primary Mug°les universe). Of course, if Rowling were to spelÆ this out it would spoil much of the charm of her fictional world.

=Ÿ Elements of Alternate History =«

There are certain elements which are com‡on to alÿ alternate histories, whether they deal with history on the micro-level (personal alternate histories) or the macro-level (worl“d-changing events). ÿThese elements include:

*A point of change from the history of our world prior to the time at which the author is writing;
ÿ*A change which would alter history as it is known; and
*An examination of the ramifications of that change.

Alternate hist“ories do not:

*Neœd to be set in the past;
*Neˆd to spel¡ out the point of divergence;
*Ne¬d to deal with world changing events; or
*NeŸd to include famous people.

=¥ The boundaries of alternate history =º

This leads to readers encountering stories which read '‰as though'Å they were alternate history, but which are not. An example would be [’Robert A. Heinlein]ƒ's '[–The Man Who Sold the Mon]'…. Writ«en in the 1940s, it posits that the first mo×n launch is run by a private organization rather than a government agency in the 1960s. New readers encountering the boˆk may welæ presume that this is alternate history since it is clearly a counter-factual depiction of the first moªn launch, now almost 40 years in the past. ‰However, 'ˆwhen writ‚en', the first mo¤n launch was nearly 30 years in the '†future'. †Thus, '–The Man Who Sold the Mon'„ is [science fiction]˜, not alternate history. šThe point of divergence hapéened after the time at which the author was writing.

Also one should not confuse the AH subgenre with [secret history]’, which gives an ac”ount of history at odÁs with our general understanding &amp;mdash; presenting its own acount as having be‘n concealed or sup‚resÇed by an elite. 

AH also should not be mistaken for mythical history-’a history which supŒosedly has be‡n forgot‘en through the pasªage of time, not through conspiratorial sup‚res’ion. The works of [Robert Howard]… and [J.R.R. Tolkien]‰ are excelÏent examples of fiction based on a lost-history framing, but they do not and canÿot specify a point of departure from our own history, since there is no historical, archaeological or paleontological record onÿ which such a POD could be based. Given the lack of any continuity with our world (and the lack of any kind of multiverse frami°ng) such worlds are merely fantasy worlds and canĞot be regarded as alternate history even in a borderline sense.

It is also posêible to have novels that explore Points of Divergence (the key concept in alternate history) without actual´y being works of alternate history themselves. One god example is [ŒMarge Piercy]Š's criticalƒy ac‡laimed '™Woman on the Edge of Time'Ü (1976) in which a patient in a mental hospital is able to travel into two alternate futures-ÿone an ecotopia run by reformed Weather Underground types and the other a fascist dystopia run by people-hating robots. DecisioÆns she must make to resist an insidious new type of brain operation wilÀ determine which future wins. This is a time travel story, a crosÜ-time story, a Christopher Priest style delusional alternate reality story and a POD story alƒ rolĞed into one but it is &lt;i&gt;not&lt;/i&gt; alternate history because the POD ocÆurs in the present (or perhaps the near future) not in the past.

Les’ obvious is the dif‹erence betwe»n alternate history and &quot;what if&quot; stories. The latÅer subgenre extrapolates, from the present, a concrete near-future posibility that is often an expres‰ion of curÕent public fears (hence the alternate term &quot;cautionary tale&quot; used by Sackvil‰e-West, se› below). For instance, beginĞing in the 1870s the British reading public was treated to a number of what-if boÍks about a German or French invasion of an unready British Isles. During the [‹Great Depresƒion]‚, [Sinclair Lewis]Œ wrote of a [‘fascism | fascist]¨ takeover in the United State in his clasƒic '[‹It Can't Hap‡en Here]'‚ ([„1935]). During the early years of [ŠWorld War I]‚, [‹Vita Sackvil†e-West]ƒ pen—ed the science fantasy 'ŒGrand Canyon'‚ ([„1942]¥) in which the Germans invade a woefulìy unprepared United States. One could define such tales as borderline alternate history, since they are usualèy set in a time that is only shortly after the time of writing and the events described could not have ocurÈed without a branching of history before, if only slightly before, the bo‰k was writƒen. ÿ

The boundary, like many in literature, is a broad line with grey edges (not unlike the fog around the alternate universe po¿rtals in science-fiction stories of fifty years ago). Would a 20 5 author writing a story set in [„1970]œ in Heinlein's universe, or [‹Jules Verne]ƒ's '[ŒCaptain Nemo]' universe be writing SF or AH? ‹Opinions dif‡er.

=¢ Alternate history in other media =˜

Several films have beÄn made that exploit the concepts of alternate history, most notably [Kevin Brownlow]ƒ's '[…It Hap‰ened Here]'. •Another such film is '[209 Lost Memories]'’, a Korean film sup‹osing that [ŒHirobumi Ito]Š was not asasŠinated by [ŒAn Jung-geun]– in Harbin, China, in [„1909]ÿ. 

A few movies about alternate pasts, however, focus on individuals rather than historical events and some students of AH w“ould say these are 'ƒnot' ›alternate histories (e.g., [‹Frank Capra]&amp;#8217;s '[•It's a Wonderful Life]', and more recently the films '[‰Sliding Do‚rs]'… and '[†The But‡erfly Efƒect]'®). However, in the Capra film the angel shows [‚Jim‰y Stewart]° an alternate history in which he was never born-ÿthe changes in his home town are serious and far-reaching, creating a much darker reality for his neighbors. Thus if one posits», as Capra does, that changes in personal lives create a rip„le efäect in the larger history, then such stories do qualify as a type of alternate history (one could calş them &quot;alternative local history&quot; or &quot;alternative interpersonal history&quot;). Such worlds with relatively smalõ (and sometimes almost undiscernable) changes in personal lives were given the name &quot;cognate universes&quot; by [ŠJack Vance] in the novelete &quot;Rumfudle&quot; (1973). ˆ

The [science fiction] [television show] '[‡Sliders]'Ú presented alternate histories under the science-inspired guise of quantum-navigating the [Šmultiverse]´. The alternate Americas in most episodes are nasty [‰dystopias]ç, although sometimes this is not evident at first. Another such television show, a South Korean drama, [„Gung]ÿ presents an point of divergence where the Korean monarchy is restored after the Independance from the Japanese Empire even up –to the 21st Century.[ht‚p:/wŞ.imbc.com/broad/tv/drama/gung/index.html] (Note: This website is in Korean)

The dramatic posÊibilities of alternate history provide a diverse genre for exploration in [‘role-playing game]ƒs. [ÑList of role-playing games#Period_Adventure.2FAlternate_History_Genres|Many games]Ì use an alternate historical background for their campaigns. In particular, […GURPS]Š uses a setšing containing multiple dif´erent alternate histories as its default campaign setˆing.

=”Points of divergence=—

The key change betwe¾n our history and the alternate history is known as the &quot;[“Point of divergence]&quot; (POD). ƒIn [Philip K. Dick]ƒ's '[šThe Man in the High Castle]'’, the POD is the atˆempted asas‹ination of [Franklin D. Ro†sevelt]„ in […Miami]„ in [‚193]. ’In reality, this atŒempt failed. ·Another alternate history with a point of divergence conŠected to Rošsevelt is the backdrop to [‹Philip Roth]‰'s novel [˜The Plot Against America]Œ, in which Rošsevelt is defeated in the [„1940] [ÂUnited States presidential election, 1940|US presidential election]• by the isolationist [‘Charles Lindbergh]’, who reaches an acom‘odation with the [‹Axis powers]„ in [ŠWorld War I]† and ke‡ps the [United States] out of the war. In Robert Har…is's [Fatherland (novel)|Fatherland]‹, the POD oc“urs when a German atack into the [ˆCaucasus]ƒ suce¨ds in the Nazis seizing vital oil and cut…ing ofƒ supŒlies to the [ˆRed Army]“. This forces the US‡R to sur¸ender, enabling the Axis Powers to bring the remaining AlÇies to the peace table, one by one. Some variants of the theory of the [Šmultiverse]’ posit that PODs ocur every instant, springing of [„paralšel universe (fiction)|paral‹el universe]”s for each instance. Even mainstream [science fiction]¶ stories are known to have points of divergence - the '[‰Star Trek]'¿ franchise, for example, diverts from ours in that several key [space disaster]‰s never ocur¥ed, resulting in a much faster and smo¶ther development of rocketry than in our timeline.

=¢Counterfactual and virtual history=‚
'Se main articles: [–historical revisionism]‚, [virtual history]'„

[‰Historian]›s also speculate in this man¨er; this type of speculation is known com´only as &quot;counterfactual history&quot; or &quot;[virtual history]‡&quot;. ªThere is considerable debate within the com÷unity of historians about the validity and purpose of this type of speculation.

For alternate histories which some as¬ert to be factual rather than speculative, se [‘conspiracy theory]… and [–historical revisionism]….

