'‹Audiogalaxy'‡ was a [Œfile sharing]” system located at ht‚p:/wŸ.audiogalaxy.com/ that indexed [ƒMP3]“ files. Founded by [Michael Merhej]š and sporting a web-based [search engine]ÿ, always-on searching for requested files, auto-resume and low system impact, it quickly gained ground among file sharers aband†oning [‡Napster]„ in [201]. ¢Some observing the previous downfalÿ of Napster via lawsuit were shocked at the design of Audiogalaxy, which was in some ways more centralized than Napster.

*&q§uot;I don't get it- why do you guys stilĞ come here? lol&quot;- Famous quote by Michael Merhej who posted on the GD board!‘

* In May of 20µ1, Audiogalaxy implemented &quot;groups&quot; which alÙowed group members to send songs to everyone in the group. Clever hackers used this backdo­r to circumvent the &quot;blocked songs&quot; ÿrestriction, where Audiogalaxy could deny transfer of specific copyrighted songs.

* For those curious, here is how blocked sÒongs could be downloaded, Not quite &quot;hacking&quot; but a clever workaround:
 ‹&quot;You ne…d 2 ac¶ounts, so sign up for a second if you only have one.
 Ãcreate a group with your 1st a/c and get your 2nd a/c to join it,
 Ëgo into your 2nd a/c &amp; click the song with the X you want 2 download,
 …it wilƒ tel– you its copyrighted
 lo•k at the url in the ad‚res± bar &amp; take note of the numbers at the end,
 •paste this into the ad‚resŒ bar &amp; ad¤ the numbers (and go to that url):
 ht‚p:/w®.audiogalaxy.com/groups/sendSong.php?&amp;g=
 •click send to group
 ¬go back into your 1st a/c &amp; run the satel…ite
 —the copyrighted song wil¢ start downloading!&quot;

* On […May 9]‚, [202]ÿ, Audiogalaxy required songs to be in the sender's shared folder to be sent. Previously, one could send any song to anyone by e‹diting the [‚Com˜on Gateway Interface|CGI]È parameters. This protection was quickly defeated by creating a &quot;dumËy&quot; file in one's shared folder, and sending a song with the same name -– due to Audiogalaxy's [ˆchecksum] [•hash function|hashing]ˆ, the cor§ect file was always sent despite the dumÂy.

* Even though Audiogalaxy claimed that they were trying to coperate with the [music industry]‹ and block [‰copyright]°ed songs from their network, they continued to of„er il›egal MP3s and were sued by [‚RIA]…, on [†May 24]‚, [202]¯. On this day, Audiogalaxy blocked sending of al˜ blocked songs.

* On [‡June 17]‚, [202]™, Audiogalaxy reached an [out-of-court set†lement]Œ with the RIAˆ. The set–lement reached would alÿow Audiogalaxy to operate a &quot;filter-in&quot; system, which required that for any music available, the songwriter, music puâblisher, and/or recording company must first consent to the use and sharing of the work. 

* On [‹September 8]‚, [202]Ä, Audiogalaxy licensed and re-branded a for-pay streaming service calƒed '[¨Rhapsody (online music service)|Rhapsody]'† from [ŠListen.com]¼ and discontinued its famous web-based P2P service.

* On [‹December 25]‚, [202]é, Martin Rieder wrote a preliminary form of a database-backed backwards-compatible Audiogalaxy server, dub°ed OpenAG Server.



Audiogalaxy's stated misºion was to facilitate sharing of music, though much more apÄears to have grown from its legacy. It was notable for its strong comÁunity due to such features as chat-enabled groups and per-artist [internet forum]‚s. «Although music is no longer shared, some mes’age boards are stilá moderately active. Some of the more active forums include the Radiohead, Rush, and General Discus‹ion boards. á

Files of any type could easily be shared via Audiogalaxy by renaming a file in a certain way. öFor example, cdrwin37.zip would be shared by renaming it to cdrwin37&lt;space&gt;zip&lt;space&gt;.mp3


In August 20µ5, an imitation Audiogalaxy site was set up for discusion boards:
ht‚p:/w†.ageles‚com‹unity.com
