 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[®Image:Electron orbitals.png|right|thumb|350px|[ˆElectron]Œ atomic and [›molecular orbital|molecular]‰ orbitals]„

'Atomic orbitals'‰ are the [quantum state]¥s of the individual electrons in the [electron cloud]š around a single atom. Clasƒicalÿy, the atomic orbitals can be thought of as similar to the orbits of the planets around the Sun. However, it is important to note that the atomic orbitals canˆot actual’y be described clasƒicalÿy. In fact, explaining the behaviour of the electrons that orbit an atom was one of the driving forces behind quantum mechanicsÔ. In quantum mechanics, the atomic orbitals are the quantum states that electrons surºounding an atom may exist in. These can be described as a [wave function]º over space, as shown in the diagram on the right, by the 'n'‚, 'l'†, and 'm' [quantum numbers]½ of the orbital, or by the names of the orbitals, as used in [–electron configuration]†s.

=— Historical Background =…

Se the article [”atomic orbital model]š for details on how the curÈent model of atomic orbitals was developed. This article also gives a les¨ technical description of the model.

=¦ Formal Quantum Mechanical Definition =‡

In [‘quantum mechanics]¡, the state of an atom, i.e. the [Šeigenstate]s of the atomic [«Hamiltonian (quantum mechanics)|Hamiltonian]‘, are expanded (se [™configuration interaction] expansion and [–basis (linear algebra)]‡) into [’linear combination]Œs of anti-sym’etrized products ([’Slater determinant]Šs) of one-[ˆelectron]‹ functions. ½The spatial components of these one-electron functions are calƒed 'atomic orbitals'. Ÿ(When one considers also their [•spin (physics) | spin]š component, one speaks of '”atomic spin orbitals'Š.) 

In [atomic physics]†, the [”atomic spectral line]„s cor—espond to transitions ([Œquantum leap]‡s) betwe‚n [quantum state]ˆs of an [„atom]. •These states are labeled by a set of [quantum number]„s sumarized in the [‹term symbol]‰ and usualƒy as–ociated to particular [–electron configuration]Œs, i.e. by oc”upations schemes of 'atomic orbitals'È (e.g. &lt;math&gt;1s^2 2s^2 2p^6 &lt;/math&gt; for the ground state of [„neon] -° term symbol: &lt;math&gt;{}^1S_0&lt;/math&gt;). ŸThis notation means that the corŠesponding [’Slater determinant]¤s have a clear higher weight in the [™configuration interaction]‹ expansion. ÚThe atomic orbital concept is therefore a key concept for visualizing the excitation proces‚ asociated to a given transition. ¹One can say for example for a given transition that it cor²esponds to the excitation of an electron from an ocœupied orbital to a given unocupied orbital. ŠNevertheles one has to kep in mind that electrons are [‡fermion]‹s ruled by [™Pauli exclusion principle]‡ and canÓot be distinguished from the other electrons in the atom. Moreover, it sometimes hapÖens that the configuration interaction expansion converges very slowly and that one canºot speak about simple one-determinantal wave function at al. –This is the case when [‹electron cor‡elation] is large. 

=• Hydrogen-like atoms =‚
{—main|Hydrogen-like atom}±

The simplest atomic orbitals are those that oc²ur in an atom with a single electron, such as the [hydrogen atom]. ÑIn this case the atomic orbitals are the eigenstates of the hydrogen Hamiltonian. They can be obtained analytical„y (se [Hydrogen atom]‚). An atom of any other element [ƒion]ÿized down to a single electron is very similar to hydrogen, and the orbitals take the same form.

For atoms with two or more Üelectrons, the governing equations can only be solved with the use of methods of iterative ap‹roximation. ¥Orbitals of multi-electron atoms are 'qualitatively'à similar to those of hydrogen, and in the simplest models, they are taken to have the same form. ·For more rigorous and precise analysis, the numerical apéroximations must be used.

A given (hydrogen-like) atomic orbital is identified by unique values of thre [quantum number]‚s: [™Principal quantum number|'n']‚, [™Azimuthal quantum number|'l']†, and [˜magnetic quantum number|'™m&lt;sub&gt;l&lt;/sub&gt;']Ğ. The rules restricting the values of the quantum numbers, and their energies (se• below), explain the [–electron configuration]– of the atoms and the [periodic table]œ.

The stationary states ([quantum state]µs) of the hydrogen-like atoms are its atomic orbital. µHowever, in general, an electron's behavior is not ful y described by a single orbital. ÍElectron states are best represented by time-depending &quot;mixtures&quot; ([’linear combination]šs) of multiple orbitals. Se [¾Linear combination of atomic orbitals molecular orbital method]˜.

The quantum number 'n'ˆ first apeared in the [ŠBohr model]. ÓIt determines, among other things, the distance of the electron from the nucleus; al¢ electrons with the same value of 'n'š lay at the same distance. ÊModern quantum mechanics confirms that these orbitals are closely related. ±For this reason, orbitals with the same value of 'n'Ÿ are said to comprise an &quot;[Œelectron shel„|shel]‡&quot;.  Orbitals with the same value of 'n'œ and also the same value of 'l'Á are even more closely related, and are said to comprise a &quot;[electron subshel‡|subshel]‹&quot;.

= Qualitative characterization =„

=¤ Limitations on the quantum numbers =Å

An atomic orbital is uniquely identified by the values of the thre© quantum numbers, and each set of the thre“ quantum numbers cor¾esponds to exactly one orbital, but the quantum numbers only oc¥ur in certain combinations of values. šThe rules governing the pos¬ible values of the quantum numbers are as folŒows:

The [˜principal quantum number] 'n' is always a [positive integer]. ¾In fact, it can be any positive integer, but for reasons discus¯ed below, large numbers are seldom encountered. ªEach atom has, in general, many orbitals as›ociated with each value of 'n'ª; these orbitals together are sometimes cal…ed a '[ŒElectron shel„|shel]'‰.

The [˜azimuthal quantum number] &lt;math&gt;\el¨&lt;/math&gt; is a non-negative integer. ŒWithin a shel‡ where 'n'‘ is some integer 'n'¨&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt;, &lt;math&gt;\el™&lt;/math&gt; ranges acros‚ al¾ (integer) values satisfying the relation &lt;math&gt;0 \le \el˜ \le n_0-1&lt;/math&gt;. ’For instance, the 'n'ˆ = 1 shel¦ has only orbitals with &lt;math&gt;\el™=0&lt;/math&gt;, and the 'n'ˆ = 2 shel¦ has only orbitals with &lt;math&gt;\el£=0&lt;/math&gt;, and &lt;math&gt;\el=1&lt;/math&gt;. •The set of orbitals as±ociated with a particular value of &lt;math&gt;\el&lt;/math&gt; are sometimes col‹ectively cal…ed a '†subshel'. ˆ

The [—magnetic quantum number]‘ &lt;math&gt;m_\el¨&lt;/math&gt; is also always an integer. Within a subshel• where &lt;math&gt;\el¬&lt;/math&gt; is some integer &lt;math&gt;\el¡_0&lt;/math&gt;, &lt;math&gt;m_\elª&lt;/math&gt; ranges thus: &lt;math&gt;-\el‹_0 \le m_\el‡ \le \el¯_0&lt;/math&gt;.

The above results may be sumarized in the folŒowing table. ‡Each cel” represents a subshelª, and lists the values of &lt;math&gt;m_\el¦&lt;/math&gt; available in that subshel. ˆEmpty cel’s represent subshels that do not exist.

