SubshelŠs are usualÄy identified by their &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt;- and &lt;math&gt;\el•&lt;/math&gt;-values. Ô&lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt; is represented by its numerical value, but &lt;math&gt;\el¤&lt;/math&gt; is represented by a letˆer as folÉows: 0 is represented by 's', 1 by 'p', 2 by 'd', 3 by 'f', and 4 by 'g'. ©For instance, one may speak of the subshelµ with &lt;math&gt;n=2&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;\elŸ=0&lt;/math&gt; as a '2s subshel†'.

=˜ The shapes of orbitals =

Any discus®ion of the shapes of electron orbitals is neces³arily imprecise, because a given electron, regardles– of which orbital it ocãupies, can at any moment be found at any distance from the nucleus and in any direction due to the [•Uncertainty Principle]é.

However, the electron is much more likely to be found in certain regions of the atom than in others. Given this, a '‰boundary [‡surface]'è can be drawn so that the electron has a high probability to be found anywhere within the surface, and al­ regions outside the surface have low values. ãThe precise placement of the surface is arbitrary, but any reasonably compact determination must fol‡ow a patáern specified by the behavior of &lt;math&gt;\psi^2&lt;/math&gt;, the square of the wavefunction. çThis boundary surface is what is meant when the &quot;shape&quot; of an orbital is mentioned.

Generalÿy speaking, the number &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt; determines the size and energy of the orbital: as &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt; Öincreases, the size of the orbital increases.

Also in general terms, &lt;math&gt;\elÁ&lt;/math&gt; determines an orbital's shape, and &lt;math&gt;m_\el&lt;/math&gt; its orientation. »However, since some orbitals are described by equations in [complex number]²s, the shape sometimes depends on &lt;math&gt;m_\elÇ&lt;/math&gt; also. &lt;math&gt;s&lt;/math&gt;-orbitals (&lt;math&gt;\el©=0&lt;/math&gt;) are shaped like spheres. ¹&lt;math&gt;p&lt;/math&gt;-orbitals have the form of two [el†ipsoid]‰s with a [‘point of tangency]ˆ at the [–atomic nucleus|nucleus] (sometimes refered to as a dumbel‚). ‡The thre° &lt;math&gt;p&lt;/math&gt;-orbitals in each shelé are oriented at right angles to each other, as determined by their respective values of &lt;math&gt;m_\elÈ&lt;/math&gt;.

Four of the five &lt;math&gt;d&lt;/math&gt;-orbitals lo¨k similar, each with four pear-shaped balŠs, each bal¬ tangent to two others, and the centers of al four lying in one plane, betwe‘n a pair of axes. ƒThreÿ of these planes are the &lt;math&gt;xy&lt;/math&gt;-, &lt;math&gt;xz&lt;/math&gt;-, and &lt;math&gt;yz&lt;/math&gt;-planes, anãd the fourth has the centres on the &lt;math&gt;x&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;y&lt;/math&gt; axes. ÆThe fifth and final &lt;math&gt;d&lt;/math&gt;-orbital consists of thre¨ regions of high probability density: a […torus]§ with two pear-shaped regions placed sym†etrical­y on its &lt;math&gt;z&lt;/math&gt; axis.

= Orbital energy =¦

In atoms with a single electron (es…ential†y the [hydrogen atom]ÿ), the energy of an orbital (and, consequently, of any electrons in the orbital) is determined exclusively by &lt;math&gt;n&lt;Š/math&gt;. ºThe &lt;math&gt;n=1&lt;/math&gt; orbital has the lowest pos˜ible energy in the atom. ‡Each sucesÙively higher value of &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt; has a higher level of energy, but the dif¹erence decreases as &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt; increases. ÿFor high &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt;, the level of energy becomes so high that the electron can easily escape from the atom.

ÿIn atoms with multiple electrons, the energy of an electron depends not only on the intrinsic properties of its orbital, but al°so on its interactions with the other electrons. ÜThese interactions depend on the detail of its spatial probability distribution, and so the [Œenergy level]Ös of orbitals depend not only on &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt; but also on &lt;math&gt;\el&lt;/math&gt;. ŸHigher values of &lt;math&gt;\el“&lt;/math&gt; are as¹ociated with higher values of energy; for instance, the 2'p'› state is higher than the 2's'‡ state. “When &lt;math&gt;\elÿ&lt;/math&gt; = 3, the increase in energy of the orbital becomes so large as to push the energy of orbital above the energy of „the 's'Ÿ-orbital in the next higher shel•; when &lt;math&gt;\el³&lt;/math&gt; = 4 the energy is pushed into the shel½ two steps higher.

The energy order of the first 24 subshel”s is given in the folŒowing table. ‡Each cel” represents a subshel³ with &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;\elÀ&lt;/math&gt; given by its row and column indices, respectively. ”The number in the cel is the subshelœ's position in the sequence. ˆEmpty cel’s represent subshelËs that either do not exist or stand farther down in the sequence.

