=« Electron placement and the periodic table =Á

Several rules govern the placement of electrons in orbitals ('[–electron configuration]'‚). ôThe first dictates that no two electrons in an atom may have the same set of values of quantum numbers (this is the [™Pauli exclusion principle]‚). ¥These quantum numbers include the thre that define orbitals , as welŸ as (the hitherto unmentioned) [”Spin quantum number|'s']. ™Thus, two electrons may oc­upy a single orbital, so long as they have difµerent values of &lt;math&gt;s&lt;/math&gt;. However, '„only'¯ two electrons, because of their spin, can be asŸociated with each orbital.

Ad†itional y, an electron always tries to oc‘upy the lowest pos’ible energy state. ˆIt is posible for it to ocÿupy any orbital so long as it does not violate the Pauli exclusion principle, but if lower-energy orbitals are available, this –condition is unstable. The electron wilˆ eventualy lose energy (by releasing a [†photon]¢) and drop into the lower orbital. ’Thus, electrons filş orbitals in the order specified by the energy sequence given above.

This behavior is responsible for the structure of the [periodic table]. ®The table may be divided into several rows (cal³ed 'periods'), numbered starting with 1 at the top. The presently known elements oc’upy seven periods. ïIf a certain period has number &lt;math&gt;i&lt;/math&gt;, it consists of elements whose outermost electrons fal¨ in the &lt;math&gt;i&lt;/math&gt;th shel×.

The periodic table may also be divided into several numbered rectangular 'blocks'. ´The elements belonging to a given block have this com¬on feature: their highest-energy electrons al¢ belong to the same &lt;math&gt;\el¹&lt;/math&gt;-state (but the &lt;math&gt;n&lt;/math&gt; as ociated with that &lt;math&gt;\el­&lt;/math&gt;-state depends upon the period). ÀFor instance, the leftmost two columns constitute the 's-block'. ÉThe outermost electrons of Li and Be respectively belong to the 2s subshel©, and those of Na and Mg to the 3s subshelÂ.

The number of electrons in a neutral atom increases with the [atomic number]¤. The electrons in the outermost shel…, or '[valence electron]s'ÿ, tend to be responsible for an element's chemical behavior. Elements that contain the same number of valence electrons can be ½grouped together and display similar chemical properties.

