 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‰chemistry]…, an 'Šamino acid'ˆ is any [ˆmolecule]” that contains both […amino]… and [carboxylic acid] [¢functional group|functional groups]…. In [Œbiochemistry]ÿ, this shorter and more general term is frequently used to refer to alpha amino acids: those amino acids in which the amino and¢ carboxylate functionalities are at’ached to the same [†carbon]‹, the so-calƒed [©alpha carbon|&amp;alpha;&amp;ndash;carbon]ˆ.

An '’amino acid residue'µ is what is left of an amino acid once a molecule of […water]† has be‹n lost (an [¦hydrogen ion|H&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;]¢ from the nitrogenous side and an [§hydroxyl ion|OH&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;]± from the carboxylic side) in the formation of a [Œpeptide bond]….

=ˆOverview=¹
Amino acids are the basic structural building units of [‡protein]“s. They form short [‡polymer]Š chains calƒed [‡peptide]…s or [Œpolypeptides]¡ which in turn form structures calƒed [‡protein]Œs. The procesŸ of such formation is known as [¡translation (biology)|translation]“, which is part of [‘protein synthesis]….

[šImage:Phe-stick.png|thumb|[Phenylalanine]¤ is one of the standard amino acids.]³

Twenty amino acids are encoded by the standard [Œgenetic code]‹ and are calƒed [proteinogenic]„ or '”standard amino acids'Â. 
At least two others are also coded by DNA in a non-standard manˆer as folŠows:

* [Selenocysteine]­ is incorporated into some proteins at a UGA […codon], which is normal“y a stop codon.
* [‚Pyr‡olysine]‘ is used by some [Šmethanogen]…s in [†enzyme]›s that they use to produce [‡methane]. ÿIt is coded for similarly to selenocysteine but with the codon UAG instead.

Other amino acids contained in proteins are usualŒy formed by [Ÿpost-translational modification]¿, which is modification after translation in protein synthesis. ŸThese modifications are often es¬ential for the function of the protein. 

[‡Proline]ÿ is the only proteinogenic amino acid whose side group is cyclic and links to the a-amino group, forming a secondary amino grou¨p. Formerly, proline was misleadingly cal†ed an [Šimino acid]©. 

Over one hundred amino acids have be’n found in nature. ”Some of these have ben detected in [‰meteorite]Šs, especial•y in a type known as [–carbonaceous chondrite]‚s. [„Micro‡rganism]†s and […plant]™s often produce very uncom¯on amino acids, which can be found in peptidic [‹antibiotics]ˆ (e.g., […nisin]„ or [‹alamethicin]‚). [‹Lanthionine]Á is a sulfide-bridged alanine dimer which is found together with [¢saturation (chemistry)|unsaturated] amino acids in [Œlantibiotics]¬ (antibiotic peptides of microbial origin). [Î1-Aminocyclopropane-1-carboxylic acid|1-Aminocyclopropane-1-carboxylic acid (AC)]‰ is a smal× disubstituted cyclic amino acid and a key intermediate in the production of the plant [‡hormone] [ˆethylene]‰.

In ad¼ition to protein synthesis, amino acids have other biological“y-important roles. [‡Glycine]… and [‰glutamate]… are [Œneurotransmit‚er]‡s as wel¥ as standard amino acids in proteins. ÉMany amino acids are used to synthesize other molecules, for example:
* [Štryptophan]£ is a precursor of the neurotransmitƒer [‰serotonin]„
* [‡glycine]­ is one of the reactants in the synthesis of [Šporphyrins]‰ such as [„heme]. ¸

Numerous non-standard amino acids are also biologicaly-important: [‚Gam“a-aminobutyric acid]˜ is another neurotransmit„er, [‰carnitine]Œ is used in […lipid]” transport within a [‚cel (biology)|cel]‚, [‰ornithine]‚, […citrulƒine]‚, [Œhomocysteine]‚, [hydroxyproline]‚, [hydroxylysine]†, and [‰sarcosine]¯.

Some of the 20 standard amino acids are calƒed [es‘ential amino acid]‘s because they can†ot be [Šsynthesize]‰d by the [human body|body]Œ from other [šchemical compound|compound]Šs through [‘chemical reaction]¦s, but instead must be taken in with fo‚d. ƒIn […human]ˆs, the es—ential amino acids are [†lysine]‚, [‡leucine]‚, [Šisoleucine]‚, [Šmethionine]‚, [phenylalanine]‚, [‰threonine]‚, [Štryptophan]‚, [†valine]‚. [‰Histidine]… and [ˆarginine]‹ are generaly considered esÿential only in children, because of their inability to synthesise them given their undeveloped metabolisms.

The phrase &quotş;branched-chain amino acids&quot; is sometimes used to refer to the aliphatic amino acids: leucine, isoleucine and valine.

=‘General structure=Ä

The general structure of proteinogenic alpha amino acids is:

