Where &lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt; represents a 'Šside chain' specific to each amino acid. ”Amino acids are usual…y clasified by the [œchemical property|properties]Ğ of the side chain into four groups. The side chain can make them behave like a [weak acid|weak] [„acid]„, a [weak base|weak] [–basic (chemistry)|base]„, a [Šhydrophile], if they are [”polar molecule|polar]†, and [Šhydrophobe] if they are [ˆnonpolar]….

=‰Isomerism=
Except for [‡glycine]¯, where &lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt; = H, amino acids ocŒur in two pos…ible [¡optical isomerism|optical isomers]„, cal‹ed D and L. Using the newer [¡Cahn Ingold Prelog priority rules]Ë for designating the configuration of optical isomers, the L isomer would asŒigned the let er S and the D isomer would be asŒigned the let…er R. ÍThe L (or S) amino acids represent the vast majority of amino acids found in [‡protein]‚s. ÊD (or R) amino acids are found in some proteins produced by exotic sea-dwel—ing organisms, such as [Šcone snail]‚s. ©They are also abundant components of the [‚celƒ wal]…s of [’bacterium|bacteria]ÿ.

The L- and D- conventions for amino acid do not refer to their own optical activity, but rather to the optical activity of² glyceraldehyde as an analogue of the amino acids. ãS-glyceraldehyde is levorotary, and R-glyceraldehyde is dexterorotary, and so S- amino acids are calÕed &quot;L-&quot; even if they are not levorotary, and R- amino acids are likewise cal¹ed &quot;D-&quot; even if they are not dexterorotary.

=‰Reactions=š
Proteins are created by [polymerization]“ of amino acids by [Œpeptide bond]Œs in a procesƒ calƒed [¡translation (biology)|translation]. …This [Œcondensation]Ë reaction yields the newly formed peptide bond and a molecule of water.

