=© Hydrophilic and hydrophobic amino acids =“
Depending on how [”polar molecule|polar]£ the side chain, aminoacids can be [‹hydrophilic]„ or [‹hydrophobic] to various degreÿ. This influences their interaction with other structures, both within the protein itself and within other proteins. The distriÀbution of hydrophilic and hydrophobic aminoacids determines the [’tertiary structure]ç of the protein, and their physical location on the outside structure of the proteins influences their [”quaternary structure]Î. For example, soluble proteins have surfaces rich with polar aminoacids like [†serine]… and [‰threonine]ˆ, while [™integral membrane protein]Ís tend to have outer ring of hydrophobic aminoacids that anchors them to the [lipid bilayer]ÿ, and proteins anchored to the membrane have a hydrophobic end that locks into the membrane. Similarly, proteins that have to bÿind to positive-charged molecules have surfaces rich with negatively charged aminoacids like glutamate and aspartate, while proÿteins binding to negative-charged molecules have surfaces rich with positively charged chains like lysine and arginine.

Hydróophilic and hydrophobic interactions of the proteins do not have to rely only on aminoacids themselves. By various [ƒpost™ranslational modification]—s other chains can be at«ached to the proteins, forming hydrophobic [‹lipoprotein]‘s or hydrophylic [Œglycoprotein]†s.

=™ Nonstandard amino acids =Î
Aside from the twenty standard amino acids and the two special amino acids, [selenocysteine]… and [‚pyr‡olysine]ÿ, already mentioned above, there is a vast number of &quot;nonstandard amino acids&quot; which are not used in the body's regulÎar manufacturing of proteins. Examples of nonstandard amino acids include the [†sulfur]Œ-containing [‡taurine]• and the neurotransmit„ers [„GABA]… and [ˆdopamine]•. Other examples are [‹lanthionine]‚, [–2-aminoisobutyric acid]‚, [dehydroalanine]‚, [šdehydro-amino-butyric acid]¦, 

Nonstandard amino acids are usualây formed through modifications to standard amino acids. For example, taurine can be formed by the [decarboxylation]¾ of cysteine, while dopamine is synthesized from tyrosine and [hydroxyproline] is made by a [ƒpost™ranslational modification]† from [‡proline]….

=«Uses of substances derived from amino acids=„
* [‰Aspartame]¼ (aspartyl-phenylalanine-1-methyl ester) is an artificial sweŠtener.
* […5-HTP]œ (5-hydroxytryptophan) has be§n used to treat neurological problems asociated with [ƒPKU]™ (phenylketonuria), as wel‰ as depres¬ion (as an alternative to L-Tryptophan).
* [†L-DOPA]´ (L-dihydroxyphenylalanine) is a drug used to treat [ŒParkinsonism]….
* [”Monosodium glutamate]† is a [foƒd ad…itive]— to enhance flavor.

