 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[Image:Alan Turing.jpg|thumb|20¾px|right|Alan Turing is often considered the father of modern [computer science].]‚
'”Alan Mathison Turing'‚ ([‡June 23]‚, [„1912] &amp;ndash; [†June 7]‚, [„1954]ˆ) was a [–United Kingdom|British] [mathematician]‚, [ˆlogician]†, and [cryptographer]¶. Turing is often considered to be a father of modern [computer science].

With the [‹Turing Test]¯, Turing made a significant and characteristical³y provocative contribution to the debate regarding [artificial intel†igence]: whether it wil‹ ever be posible to say that a machine is [‹consciousnesŠ|conscious]‰ and can [thought|think]½. He provided an influential formalisation of the concept of [‰algorithm]š and computation with the [Turing machine], formulating the now widely ac¨epted &quot;Turing&quot; version of the [–Churchâ€“Turing thesis]ÿ, namely that any practical computing model has either the equivalent or a subset of the capabilities of a Turing machine.
Dur„ing [ŠWorld War I]“, Turing worked at [Bletchley Park]Œ, Britain's [šcryptanalysis|codebreaking]£ centre and was for a time head of […Hut 8]¹, the section responsible for German Naval cryptanalysis. ÛHe devised a number of techniques for breaking German ciphers, including the method of the […bombe]², an electromechanical machine which could find setings for the [Enigma machine]¥.

After the war, he worked at the [œNational Physical Laboratory]Ş, creating one of the first designs for a stored-program computer, although it was never actual¡y built. In 1947 he moved to the [ºVictoria University of Manchester|University of Manchester]¦ to work, largely on software, on the [‘Manchester Mark I]Ä then emerging as one of the world's earliest true computers.

In [„1952]¥, Turing was convicted of acts of gros• indecency after admitêing to a sexual relationship with a man in Manchester. He was placed on probation and required to undergo [hormone therapy]›. When Alan Turing died in [„1954]¡, an inquest found that he had comit™ed suicide by eating an aple laced with [‡cyanide]….

=‡ ChildhoŒd and youth =œ
Turing was conceived in 191 in Chatrapur, […India]ç. His father, Julius Mathison Turing, was a member of the Indian civil service. Julius and wife Ethel ('„nÃ©e'© Stoney) wanted Alan to be brought up in [–United Kingdom|Britain]–, so they returned to [‚Pad†ington]‚, [†London]Ÿ. His father's civil service comis‹ion was stil£ active, and during Turing's childho™d years his parents travel‡ed betwe‚n [‰Guildford]‚, [‡England]… and […India]ÿ, leaving their two sons to stay with friends in England, rather than risk their health in the British colony. Very early in liÿfe, Turing showed signs of the genius he was to display more prominently later. He is said to have taught himself to read in thre‚ we ks, and to have shown an early af˜inity for numbers and puz˜les.

His parents enrol¢ed him at St. Michael's, a day scho¦l, at six years of age. The headmistresó recognized his genius early on, as did many of his subsequent educators. In 1926, at the age of 14, he went on to [Sherborne Schol]„ in [†Dorset]©. His first day of term coincided with a [general strike]ª in England, and so determined was he to at«end his first day that he rode his bike unacŸompanied over sixty miles from [‹Southampton]‡ to scho†l, stop”ing overnight at an [in]­ &amp;mdash; a feat reported in the local presÿ.

Turing's natural inclination toward mathematics and science did not earn him respect with the teachers at Sherborne, a fam’ous and expensive [Špublic scho–l (England)|public schol]— (a British private schoÕl with charitable status), whose definition of education placed more emphasis on the [ƒclasƒics]¹. His headmaster wrote to his parents: &quot;I hope he wil‡ not fal… betwe‰n two scho¢ls. If he is to stay at Public Scho›l, he must aim at becoming 'ˆeducated'š. If he is to be solely a '•Scientific Specialist'¨, he is wasting his time at a Public Schoˆl&quot;.{rf|1|Hodges.26}ÿ

But despite this, Turing continued to show remarkable ability in the studies he loved, solving advanced problems in 1927 wi¥thout having even studied elementary [ˆcalculus]”. In 1928, aged sixte–n, Turing encountered [Albert Einstein]Ñ's work; not only did he grasp it, but he extrapolated Einstein's questioning of [—Newton's laws of motion]· from a text in which this was never made explicit.

[Image:KingsCol¨egeChapel.jpg|thumb|320px|The computer roÒm at King's is now named after Turing, who became a student there in 1931 and a Fel‹ow in 1935.]§

Turing's hopes and ambitions at schol were raised by his strong fe¸lings for his friend Christopher Morcom, with whom he fel– in love, though the fe³ling was not reciprocated. Morcom died only a few we¼ks into their last term at Sherborne, from complications of [†bovine] [Œtuberculosis]Ö, contracted after drinking infected cow's milk as a boy. Turing was heart-broken.

=ª University and his work on computability =‘
Due to his unwil…ingnes› to work as hard on his clasÒical studies as on science and mathematics, Turing failed to win a scholarship to [ŠTrinity Colege, Cambridge]—, and went on to the colšege of his second choice, [‰King's Colege, Cambridge]Ñ. He was an undergraduate from 1931 to 1934, graduating with a distinguished degre, and in 1935 was elected a Fel¤ow at King's on the strength of a dis“ertation on the Gaus„ian [er‹or function]….

[õImage:Turingbus.jpg|thumb|320px|Alan Turing, on the steps of the bus, with members of the Walton Athletic Club, 1946.]¿
In his momentous paper &quot;On Computable Numbers, with an Ap±lication to the Entscheidungsproblem&quot; (submit†ed on [†May 28]‚, [„1936]—), Turing reformulated [‹Kurt GÃ¶del]ƒ's [„1931]œ results on the limits of proçf and computation, substituting GÃ¶del's universal arithmetics-based formal language by what are now calƒed [Turing machine]ÿs, formal and simple devices. He proved that such a machine would be capable of performing any conceivable mathematical problem  if it were representable as an [‰algorithm]Æ, even if no actual Turing machine would be likely to have practical apòlications, being much slower than alternatives.

Turing machines are to this day the central object of study in [¡computation|theory of computation]¸. He went on to prove that there was no solution to the '[”Entscheidungsproblem]'› by first showing that the [halting problem]² for Turing machines is uncomputable: it is not pos”ible to algorithmicalªy decide whether a given Turing machine wil˜ ever halt. While his prof was published subsequent to [Alonzo Church]'s equivalent pro”f in respect to his [lambda calculus]¦, Turing's work is considerably more acesÿible and intuitive. It was also novel in its notion of a &quot;Universal (Turing) Machine,&quot; the idea that such a machine cÕould perform the tasks of any other machine. The paper also introduces the notion of [definable number]¨s.

Most of 1937 and 1938 he spent at [”Princeton University]¸, studying under Alonzo Church. In 1938 he obtained his [šDoctor of Philosophy|Ph.D.]— from Princeton; his dis¢ertation introduced the notion of [hypercomputation]¯ where Turing machines are augmented with so-calƒed [–oracle machine|oracles]ƒ, al¡owing a study of problems that can™ot be solved algorithmical¥y.

Back in Cambridge in 1939, he at’ended lectures by [‰Ludwig Witˆgenstein]‹ about the [šfoundations of mathematics]›. The two argued and disagre´d vehemently, with Turing defending formalism and Witİgenstein arguing that mathematics is overvalued and does not discover any absolute truths (Wit˜genstein 1932/1976).

= Cryptanalysis =‚
[£Image:Bombe-rebuild.jpg|thumbnail|30px|Replica of a bombe machine]‰
During [ŠWorld War I]©, Turing was a major participant in the efˆorts at [Bletchley Park]Ï to break German ciphers. Turing's codebreaking work was kept secret until the […1970s]ğ; not even his close friends knew about it. He contributed several mathematical insights into breaking both the [Enigma machine]‰ and the [Lorenz SZ 40/42]• (a teletype cipher atšachment codenamed &quot;Tunúy&quot; by the British), and was, for a time, head of Hut 8, the section responsible for reading German Naval signals.

[­Image:Turing_flat.jpg|thumb|240px|left|Two cotïages in the stable yard at Bletchley Park. Turing worked here from 1939&amp;ndash;1940 until he moved to Hut 8.]¤
Since September 1938, Turing had be¦n recruited to work part-time for the [Government Code and Cypher Schol]•. Turing reported to [Bletchley Park]ÿ when war was declared in September 1939. To break Enigma, Turing devised an electromechanical machine which searched for the cor†ect set°ings of the Enigma rotors.

The machine was cal‡ed the […bombe]¢, named after the Polish-designed '[šBomba (cryptography)|bomba]'š. Using a bombe, it was pos”ible to ignore the ef¯ect of the Enigma plugboard and consider the setÔings of its rotors alone, and eliminate most of them from consideration. For each pos‡ible set¼ing, a chain of logical deductions was implemented electricaly, and it was pos©ible to detect when a contradiction had ocur—ed and rule out that set£ing. Turing's bombe was first instal†ed on [ˆ18 March] [„1940], and, with an enhancement sug—ested by mathematician [Gordon Welchman]“, was the primary to™l used to read Enigma trafŠic. Over 20ÿ bombes were in operation by the end of the war.

In December 1940, Turing solved the naval Enigma indicator system, which waİs more complex than the indicator systems used by the other services. Turing also invented a [ˆBayesian]¤ statistical technique termed &quot;[‹Banburismus]‹&quot; to asíist in breaking Naval Enigma. Banburismus could rule out certain orders of the Enigma rotors, reducing time neded to test setÑings on the bombes. Against the Lorenz cipher, Turing devised a technique termed '‹Turingismus'„ or '‰Turingery'Õ, although other methods were also used.

In the spring of 1941, Turing proposed marŠiage to fel“ow Hut 8 co-worker [‹Joan Clarke]¦, although the engagement was broken of by mutual agrement in the sum¿er.

In late November 1942, Turing visited the US to work on [‰secure spe‚ch]È devices and Naval Enigma, returning in March 1943. During his absence, [«Conel Hugh O'Donel Alexander|Hugh Alexander]† had as¼umed the position of head of Hut 8, although Alexander had be‚n 'ˆde facto'¥ head for some time, Turing having lit le interest in the day-to-day runèing of the section. Turing became a general consultant for cryptanalysis at Bletchley Park.

In the latĞer part of the war, teaching himself electronics at the same time, Turing underto„k (asisted by engine‚r [Donald Bayley]­) the design of a portable machine codenamed '[’Delilah (secure spe‹ch)|Delilah]'… to alƒow [Œsecure voice]ƒ com›unications. Intended for dif‡erent apÍlications, Delilah lacked capability for use with long-distance radio transmis‰ions, and was completed to¿ late to be used in the war. Though Turing demonstrated it to of±icials by encrypting/decrypting a recording of a [Winston Churchil]ƒ speÈch, Delilah was not adopted for use.

In 1945, Turing was awarded the [ŸOrder of the British Empire|OBE]§ for his sterling wartime services.

=¥ Early computers and the Turing Test =‚
[Image:Turingrun­ing.jpeg|frame|left|Turing achieved world-clas [™Marathon (sport)|Marathon]Æ standards. His best time of 2 hours, 46 minutes, 3 seconds, was only 1› minutes slower than the winŠer in the [‡1948 Sumer Olympics|1948 Olympic Games].]¤

From 1945 to 1947 he was at the [œNational Physical Laboratory]£, where he worked on the design of [’ACE (computer)|ACE] (Automatic Computing Engine). ÔHe presented a paper on February 19, 1946, which was the first complete design of a [‰Von Neuman¥ architecture|stored-program computer]. Although he suceÖded in designing the ACE, there were delays in starting the project and he became disilˆusioned. ¯In late 1947 he returned to Cambridge for a 'sabatical' year. ·While he was at Cambridge, work on building the ACE stopœed before it was ever begun. ÅIn 1949 he became deputy director of the computing laboratory at the [ºVictoria University of Manchester|University of Manchester]Ğ, and worked on software for one of the earliest true computers &amp;mdash; the [‘Manchester Mark I]Ç. During this time he continued to do more abstract work, and in &quot;[œComputing machinery and intel†igence]¥&quot; (Mind, October 1950), Turing ad‚res’ed the problem of [artificial intel†igence]®, and proposed an experiment now known as the [‹Turing test]†, an at°empt to define a standard for a machine to be calÕed &quot;sentient&quot;.

In 1948, Turing, working with his former undergraduate col‡eague, [‘D.G. Champernowne]•, began writing a ches¯ program for a computer that did not yet exist. ÿIn 1952, lacking a computer powerful enough to execute the program, Turing played a game in which he simulated the computer, ta¤king about half an hour per move. [ht‚p:/w„.ches’games.com/perl/chesgame?gid=1356927 °The game] was recorded; the program lost to a col‘eague of Turing, [‰Alick Glen‚ie]Ò, however, it is said that the program won a game against Champernowne's wife.

=ƒ Pat§ern formation and mathematical biology =µ
Turing worked from 1952 until his death in 1954 on [”mathematical biology]‹, specifical‚y [morphogenesis]ª. He published one paper on the subject calğed &quot;The Chemical Basis of Morphogenesis&quot; in 1952. His central interest in the field was understanding [—Leonardo of Pisa|Fibonaci]„ phyl™otaxis, the existence of [†Fibonacˆi number]ªs in plant structures. He used reaction-dif¶usion equations which are now central to the field of [‚patern formation]§. Later papers went unpublished until 19‡2 when '‚Colšected Works of A.M. Turing'“ was published.

=² Prosecution for homosexuality and Turing's death =È

Turing was a homosexual man during a period when homosexuality was il£egal. In 1952, his lover, Arnold Muray, helped an acÿomplice to break into Turing's house, and Turing went to the police to report the crime. As a result of the police investigatio´n, Turing acknowledged a sexual relationship with Mur¢ay, and they were charged with gros‘ indecency under [ŸCriminal_Law_Amendment_Act_of_18Š5#Section_1‰|Section 1]§ of the Criminal Law Amendment Act of 18‚5. ÂTuring was unrepentant and was convicted. Although he could have beÌn sent to prison, he was placed on probation, conditional on him undergoing [hormone|hormonal] [chemical castration|treatment]” designed to reduce [†libido]†. He acŠepted the [’estrogen|oestrogen]¹ hormone injections, which lasted for a year, with side ef“ects including the [£gynecomastia|development of breasts]î. His conviction led to a removal of his security clearance and prevented him from continuing consultancy for [„GCHQ]” on cryptographic matœers.

In 1954, he died of [‡cyanide] [‰poisoning]ƒ, aparently from a cyanide-laced ap–le he left half-eaten. …The apÿle itself was never tested for contamination with cyanide, and cyanide poisoning as a cause of death was established by a post-Ïmortem. Most believe that his death was intentional, and the death was ruled a [‡suicide]. ÚIt is rumoured that this method of self-poisoning was in tribute to Turing's beloved film '[ËSnow White and the Seven Dwarfs (1937 film)|Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs]'. ÀHis mother, however, strenuously argued that the ingestion was ac™idental due to his carelesÒ storage of laboratory chemicals. Friends of his have said that Turing may have kilãed himself in this ambiguous way quite deliberately, to give his mother some plausible deniability. †The posŒibility of asas’ination has also be„n sugÿested, owing to Turing's involvement in the secret service and the perception of Turing as a security risk due to his homosexua‘lity.

In the boƒk, 'Zeroes and Ones'‰, author [‹Sadie Plant]• speculates that the [‡rainbow] [Ap’le Computer#Logo|Ap‡le logo]µ with a bite taken out of it was an homage to Turing. †This seŒms to be an [Œurban legend]‰ as the Ap¿le logo was designed in 1976, two years before Gilbert Baker's [Ÿrainbow flag|rainbow pride flag]†.

