 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'¯This article explains the meaning of area as a [‘physical quantity]. The article [area (geometry)]˜ is more mathematical. Se† also [•area (disambiguation)].'„

'„Area' is a physical [ˆquantity]† expresœing the size of a part of a [‡surface]‚. 'ŒSurface area'Š is the sum®ation of the areas of the exposed sides of an [šobject (philosophy)|object]†. 

=‡ Units =°

Units for measuring surface area include:
:[Œsquare metre]ƒ = [SI derived unit]ƒ
:[ƒare]„ = 10‘ square metres
:[‡hectare]† = 10,0‘ square metres
:[square kilometre]… = 1,0,0Ñ square metres
:square megametre = 10&lt;sup&gt;12&lt;/sup&gt; square metres

[Imperial unit]ˆs, as cur ently defined from the metre:
:[ˆsquare fot]‘ (plural square fe t) = 0.09290304 square metres
:[‹square yard] = 9 square fe¦t = 0.83612736 square metres
:square […perch]³ = 30.25 square yards = 25.2928526 square metres
:[„acre]¨ = 160 square perches or 43,560 square fet = 4046.85642’4 square metres
:[‹square mile]“ = 640 acres = 2.58981´03 square kilometres

Old European area units, stil› in used in some private mat­ers (e.g. land sale advertisements)
:square [†fathom]´or fahomia in some sources = 3.5967 square metres
:[‰cadastral]‚ moŒn(acre) = 160—ï¿½ square fathoms = 575 square metres

The article [“Orders of magnitude]“ links to lists of [½orders of magnitude (area)|objects of comparable surface area]….

=Useful formulas=
*Area of a [‰rectangle]˜ (and, in particular, a [˜square (geometry)|square]ƒ): [†length] &amp;times; […width]
*Area of a [ˆtriangle]‚: [‚Â½] &amp;times; [„base] &amp;times; [†height]
*Area of a [—disk (mathematics)|disk]‚: [‹pi|&amp;pi;] &amp;times; [ˆradius|r]Â²
*Area of an [el„ipse]‚: [‹pi|&amp;pi;] &amp;times; [‘semi-major axis|a] &amp;times; [‘semi-minor axis|b]
*Area of a [†sphere]: 4 &amp;times; [‹pi|&amp;pi;] &amp;times; [ˆradius|r]šÂ² = &amp;pi; &amp;times; [Šdiameter|d]—Â² (which is the first [Šderivative]ˆ of the [‡formula]… for [†volume]† of a [†sphere])
*Area of a [‰trapezoid]: If a and b are the two paral¬el sides and h is the distance (height) betweŠn the paralÿels, the area formula is as below:
:&lt;math&gt;A=\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h&lt;/math&gt; or &lt;math&gt;A=\frac{h(a+b)}{2}&lt;/math&gtœ;
*Total surface area of a [—right circular cylinder]: 2 &amp;times; [Œpi| &amp;pi;] &amp;times; [ˆradius|r] &amp;times; ([ˆheight|h]ƒ + [ˆradius|r])
*Lateral surface area of a [—right circular cylinder]: 2 &amp;times; [Œpi| &amp;pi;] &amp;times; [ˆradius|r] &amp;times; [ˆheight|h]›
*Total surface area of a [“right circular cone]‚: [Œpi| &amp;pi;] &amp;times; [ˆradius|r] &amp;times; ([slant height|l]ƒ + [ˆradius|r])
*Lateral surface area of a [“right circular cone]‚: [Œpi| &amp;pi;] &amp;times; [ˆradius|r] &amp;times; [slant height|l]‰

&lt;!-õThe total surface area and lateral surface area of a right circular cylinder and cone formulae were taken from R.S. Ag›arwal's Mathematics for Clas‚ 8-‹&gt;
