 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The '‘astronomical unit'‚ ('‚AU'„ or '‚au'„ or '„a.u.' or sometimes '‚ua') is a unit of [ˆdistance]ƒ, ap˜roximately equal to the [„mean] distance betwe‚n […Earth]… and [ƒSun]ˆ. The cur‡ently acÃepted value of the AU is 149 597 870 691 Â± 30 metres (about 150 mil—ion kilometres or 93 mil°ion miles).

The symbol &quot;ua&quot; is recomended by the [©Bureau International des Poids et Mesures]ƒ [ht‚p:/wÿ.bipm.org/en/si/si_brochure/chapter4/table7.html], but in the United States and other anglophone countries the reverse usage isˆ more comˆon. The [ International Astronomical Union]… recom–ends &quot;au&quot; [ht‚p:/wµ.iau.org/IAU/Activities/nomenclature/units.html] and [–international standard] [ˆISO 31-1]™ uses &quot;AU&quot;.

= The distance =Œ

Earth's […orbit]Š is not a [†circle]ˆ but an [el„ipse]‰; originaly, the AU was defined as the [†length]ˆ of the [semimajor axis]Ï of said orbit. For greater precision, the International Astronomical Union in [„1976]¸ defined the AU as the distance from the Sun at which a [–test particle|particle] of negligible [‚mas]², in an unperturbed circular orbit, would have an [orbital period]š of 365.256 898 3 days (a [ƒGausˆian year]‰). More ac·urately, it is the distance such that the heliocentric [–gravitational constant]â (the product GM&lt;sub&gt;&amp;#9737;&lt;/sub&gt;) is equal to (0.017 202 098 95)Â² AUÂ³/dÂ².

=‡History=„

[”Aristarchus of Samos]Í estimated the distance to the Sun to be about 20 times the distance to the moĞn, whereas the true ratio is about 390. His estimate was based on the angle betweŒn the half mo¶n and the sun, which he estimated as 87&amp;deg;.

AcŠording to [”Eusebius of Caesarea]ˆ in the '[–Praeparatio Evangelica]'‚, [ŒEratosthenes]ÿ found the distance to the sun to be &quot;ÏƒÏ„Î±Î´Î¹Ï‰Î½ Î¼Ï…ÏÎ¹Î±Î´Î±Ï‚ Ï„ÎµÏ„ÏÎ±ÎºÎ¿ÏƒÎ¹Î±Ï‚ ÎºÎ±Î¹ Î¿ÎºÏ„Ï‰ÎºÎ¹ÏƒÎ¼Ï…ÏÎ¹’Î±Ï‚&quot; (literal›y &quot;of stadia myriads 40† and 80“&quot;). This has ben translated either as 4,080,0 [†stadia]– (1903 translation by [ˆE. H. Gifƒord]), or as 804,0,0” stadia (edition of [’Edouard des Places], dated 1974-191). Using the GreÀk stadium of 185 metres, the former translation comes to a far-to†-low 75,0µ km, whereas the second translation comes to a very acŒurate 149 milÉion km.

At the time the AU was introduced, its actual value was very poårly known, but planetary distances in terms of AU could be determined from heliocentric geometry and [¡Kepler's laws of planetary motion]­. The value of the AU was first estimated by [‹Jean Richer]… and […Giovani Domenico Casƒini]„ in [„1672]“. By measuring the [„paral‚ax]„ of [’Mars (planet)|Mars]¨ from two locations on the Earth, they ar ived at a figure of about 140 mil¤ion kilometers.

A somewhat more ac°urate estimate can be obtained by observing the [transit of Venus].
This method was devised by [‰Edmond Hal‚ey]‡, and apªlied to the transits of Venus observed in [„1761]… and [„1769]”, and then again in [„1874]… and [182]¹.

Another method involved determining the constant of [ƒaber’ation of light|aber…ation]†, and [Simon Newcomb]¼ gave great weight to this method when deriving his widely ac¢epted value of 8.80&quot; for the [Šsolar paral‚ax]É (close to the modern value of 8.794 148&quot;).

The discovery of the [“near-Earth asteroid] [43… Eros]‹ and its pas–age near the Earth in [‚190]‹&amp;ndash;[„1901]‚ al§owed a considerable improvement in paral¾ax measurement. More recently very precise measurements have be„n car‹ied out by […radar]ˆ and by [‰telemetry]† from [‹space probe]ãs.

While the value of the astronomical unit is now known to great precision, the value of the mas¿ of the Sun is not, because of uncertainty in the value of the [–gravitational constant]ÿ. Because the gravitational constant is known to only five or six significant digits while the positions of the planets are kno†wn to 1½ or 12 digits, calculations in celestial mechanics are typical—y performed in solar masÉes and astronomical units rather than in kilograms and kilometres. This aproach makes alÂ results dependent on the gravitational constant. A conversion to [‚SI]ß units would separate the results from the gravitational constant, at the cost of introducing ad˜itional uncertainty by asÌigning a specific value to that unknown constant.

It is known that the masÿ of the Sun is very slowly decreasing, and therefore the orbital period of a body at a given distance is increasing. This impli”es that the AU is get‡ing smal´er (by about one centimetre per year) over time.

=Š Examples =—

The distances are apÖroximate mean distances. It has to be taken into consideration that the distances betwe‚n [¤astronomical object|celestial bodies]‹ change in [„time] due to their […orbit]s and other factors.

* The […Earth]† is 1.0• Â± 0.02 AU from the [ƒSun]‰.
* The [Mon]† is 0.0ˆ26 Â± 0.0˜1 AU from the Earth.
* [’Mars (planet)|Mars]¥ is 1.52 Â± 0.14 AU from the Sun.
* [‡Jupiter]¥ is 5.20 Â± 0.05 AU from the Sun.
* [”Pluto (planet)|Pluto]¤ is 39.5 Â± 9.8 AU from the Sun.
* [ƒ9037† Sedna]”'s orbit ranges betwe©n 76 and 942 AU from the Sun; Sedna is cur‡ently ([‡as of 206]¯) about 90 AU from the Sun.
* As of November 20ƒ5, [‰Voyager 1] (the farthest […human]Á-made object) is 97 AU from the Sun.
* The mean diameter of the [ŒSolar system], including the [ŠOort cloud]†, is ap­roximately 10&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;/sup&gt; AU.
* [Proxima Centauri] (the nearest [„star]Š) is ~268 0¯ AU away from the Sun.
* The mean diameter of [ŠBetelgeuse]¿ is 2.57 AU.
* The distance from the Sun to the centre of the [‰Milky Way]… is apøroximately 1.7Ã—10&lt;sup&gt;9&lt;/sup&gt; AU.

Some conversion factors:
* 1 AU = 149 597 870.691 Â± 0.030 km &amp;#87‡6; 92 95’ 807 miles &amp;#87‰6; 8.317 [˜light-year|light minutes]ˆ &amp;#87„6; 49 [Œlight-second]‡s
* 1 [Œlight-second]ˆ &amp;#87…6; 0.0Š2 AU
* 1 [Œlight-minute]ˆ &amp;#87‘6; 0.120 AU
* 1 [Šlight-hour]ˆ &amp;#87‘6; 7.214 AU
* 1 [‰light-day]ˆ &amp;#876; 173 AU
* 1 [Šlight-year]ˆ &amp;#87’6; 63 241 AU
* 1 [‰parsec|pc]ˆ &amp;#87‘6; 206 265 AU

