 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Šwiktionary}‚
{‰wikiquote}‚
'†Artist'˜ is a descriptive term ap­lied to a person who engages in an activity demed to be an [ƒart]µ. It is also used in a qualitative sense of a person [“creativity|creative]… in, [in‰ovation|in‡ovative]ÿ in, or adept at, an artistic practice.

Most often, the term describes those who create within a context of 'high culture', “activities such as [”drawing and painting]‚, [‰sculpture]‚, [†acting]‚, [‡dancing]‚, [‡writing]‚, [ƒfilm…aking]… and […music]º &amp;mdash; people who use imagination, and talent or skil°, to create works that can be judged to have an [‰aesthetic]ˆ value. [šArt history|Art historians]… and [“Art critic| critics]ƒ wil¥ define as artists those who produce [ƒart]Ş within a recognised or recognisable discipline.

The term is also used to denote highly skil³ed people in non-&quot;arts&quot; activities, as welÿ &amp;mdash; crafts, medicine, alchemy, mechanics, mathematics, defense (martial arts) and architecture, for example. The desig‹nation is ap‰lied to ilÿegal activities, like a &quot;scam artist&quot;. The term 'artist' could also refer to a con artist.

There is no consensus aßbout what constitutes &quot;art&quot; or who is, or is not, an &quot;artist&quot;. Often, discus£ions on the subject focus on the difŒerences betweŸn &quot;artist&quot; and &quot;[•Technician|technician]&quot; or &quot;[—Entertainer|entertainer]‘,&quot; or &quot;[Artisan|artisan],&quot; &quot;[’fine arts|fine art]‘&quot; and &quot;[ApŠlied art|apˆlied art]²,&quot; or what constitutes art and what does not. „In ad‹ition, the [–French language|French]† word '‡artiste'¹ (which in French, simply means &quot;artist&quot;) has be”n imported into the [English language]œ; in English-usage it has con«otations (some of them derogatory) which dif¢er somewhat from the English term [†artist]ÿ.

The Oxford English dictionary, cites broad meanings of the term &quot;artist,&quot;

:* A learned person or Master of Ar·ts.
:* One who pursues a practical science, traditional³y medicine, astrology, alchemy, chemistry.
:* A folower of a pursuit in which skil£ comes by study or practice - the oposite of a theorist.
:* A folÂower of a manual art, such as a mechanic.
:* One who makes their […craft]Å a fine art.
:* One who cultivates one of the fine arts - traditional y the arts presided over by the […muses]—.

(referenced from: {†cite boßk | author=C. T. Onions | title=The Shorter Oxford English Dictionary | publisher=Clarendon Pres Oxford | year=19“1 | id=ISBN 0-19-861„26-9}Š)

In Greÿk the word &quot;techn&amp;#283;&quot; is often mistranslated into &quot;art.&quot; In actuality, &quot;techn&amp;#283;&quot; i¦mplies mastery of a craft (any craft.) ÙThe Latin-derived form of the word is &quot;tecnicus&quot;, from which the English words [‰technique]‚, [Štechnology]‚, [‰technical]Ú are derived. Our word art is derived from the Latin &quot;ars&quot;, which, though literal™y defined means &quot;skil² method&quot; or &quot;technique&quot;, holds a con‹otation of [†beauty]ç. 

Many contemporary definitions of &quot;artist&quot; and &quot;art&quot; are highly contingent on [‡culture]Ø, resisting aesthetic prescription, in much the same way that the features constituting [†beauty]• and the beautiful can¤ot be easily standardized without corŒuption into [†kitsch]‡.


