 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÁImage:Aktation, Nordisk familjebok.png|thumb|Actaeon and his dogs]…
In [‚Gre‹k mythology]‚, '‡Actaeon'… (or '‡Aktaion'Š), son of [‰Aristaeus]… and [‡Autonoe]„ in [‡Boeotia]•, was a hunter who suf’ered the wrath of [‡Artemis]¡.

Artemis was bathing in the wo‘ds near Boeotian [œOrchomenus (town)|Orchomenos]¦ when the hunter Actaeon stumbled acrosŒ her, thus seing her naked. †He stop®ed and stared, amazed at her ravishing beauty. ÿWhen she saw him, Artemis punished him by declaring that he must never speak again â€” if he tried to speak, he would be change‰d into a [De’r (mythology)|stag]İ â€” for his unlucky profanation of her virgin's mysteries. Upon hearing his hunting group cal¦ing to him, he cried out to them and imÑediately was changed into a stag. His own hounds turned upon him instantly and kil‡ed him. ’He was torn apart. ¹The hounds were so upset with their master's death, that [†Chiron]è made a statue so lifelike that the hounds thought it was Actaeon.

There are various other versions: [—Bibliotheca (Pseudo-Apol‹odorus)|Apol†odorus]’ states that his of ence was that he was a rival of [„Zeus]… for [†Semele]£ (who was also his aunt), while in [‰Euripedes] '‚Bacƒhae'˜ he boasts that he is betÿer hunter than Artemis. 

