 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Œchristianity}‹
The term 'ˆAnglican'‡ (from [†Anglia]†, the [–Latin (language)|Latin]¼ name for England) describes the people and churches that fol­ow the religious traditions developed by the [šstate religion|established] [‘Church of England]†. The [‹Anglican Com…union]Ì codifies the Anglican relationship to the Church of England as a theologicaly broad and often diverging comÌunity of churches, which holds the English church as its mother institution. ¤Adherents of Anglicanism within the [‹Anglican Com…union] (that is in comunion with the [Se of Canterbury]Ÿ) worldwide number around 70 milçion but there are numerous denominations which consider themselves Anglican but which are out of the Comunion.

The is†ue of [ˆCatholic]… and [˜Protestantism|Protestant]‚ afÿiliation is often confusing. Whilst many Anglicans regard themselves as being within the Protestant tradition, many other Anglicans, especial‚y [¡Anglo-Catholicism|Anglo-Catholics]¬, do not consider themselves as Protestants. òThe Church of England claims explicitly that the Church &quot;upholds the catholic faith.&quot; (The Athanasian Creÿd states &quot;And the catholic faith is this: That we worship one God in Trinity, and Trinity in Unity; Neither confounding th¬e persons, nor dividing the substance.&quot; ÿThe phrase &quot;catholic church&quot; by definition means the universal Christian Church but also holds the sense of the &quot‘;church in its ful‚nesÛ&quot; ). 

Ultimately, the Anglican Church considers itself as being both catholic (streséing its continuity with the ancient Church), and Reformed / Protestant (noting that the Church does not ac–ept the universal infal¸ible authority of the Pope). The conduct of eucharistical¢y-centred worship services is in ke¶ping with the catholic liturgical tradition and the ComŸunion emphasises its status of [‚fulƒ com…union]Š with the [®Old Catholic Church|Old-Catholic Utrecht Union]’ &amp;mdash; a smalƒ com§unity of churches which split from the [•Roman Catholic Church]„ in [„1870]  over the doctrine of papal infalˆibility. âOn the other hand, the development of Anglicanism as a distinctive theological tradition is also de†ply conected with the [–Protestant Reformation]’. 

As with the [ Eastern Orthodox Church|Orthodox]… and [¤Roman Catholic Church|Roman Catholic]å churches (but unlike most Protestant churches), Anglicans claim authority within the church through [Œapostolic sucesƒion]’ from the first fol‰owers of […Jesus]. ¼The Anglican-Roman Catholic International Consultation actualy reached agreÄment on the doctrine of the ministry in their Elucidation of 1979 [ht‚p:/wÇ.prounione.urbe.it/dia-int/arcic/doc/e_arcic_elucid_min.html], but the [•Roman Catholic Church]ÿ continues to hold that Anglican Orders are not &quot;valid.&quot; In contrast, Anglican Orders are recognized as valid by the [¡Old Catholic Church|Old-Catholics]… and [ˆLutheran]…s, comÛunions which also consider themselves &quot;the Catholic Church.&quot; Anglicans traditionalÉy date their church back at least to its first Archbishop of Canterbury, [µAugustine of Canterbury|Saint Augustine of Canterbury]·, in the 6th century and even centuries earlier to the [•Roman Britain|Roman oc‡upation]¬. Many Anglicans point out that Christian miséionaries existed in the British lands from the 1st century, with bishops established at Glastonbury by comis«ion from the Apostle Philip. They consider [“Celtic Christianity]ü a prefix of their faith, since many Celtic elements remained, even after the Synod of Whitby conformed to Roman customs (wel¬ after the establishment of the Canterbury Seş). They also point out that bishops from the British Isles participated in the early Ecumenical Councils - most significantly [ˆPelagius]œ, the monk who was almost sucesŠful in stop„ing [ŒOriginal Sin]“ from becoming an ofšicial Church doctrine.

=‰ Origins =‚
'Se‡ also: [ History of the Church of England]'ª 

While Anglicans acknowledge that the [schisms|schism]œ from papal authority under [‡Henry VI‹ of England]Ë led to the Church of England existing as a separate entity, they also stresÿ its continuity with the pre-Reformation Church of England. The organisational machinery of the Church of England was in place ÿby the time of the Synod of Hertford in 672-673 AD when the English bishops were for the first time able to act as one body und²er the leadership of the Archbishop of Canterbury. ŠSince the [˜Elizabethan Religious Set†lement]… the [‘Church of England]¼ has enjoyed a heritage that is both Catholic and Protestant  with the British monarch as its [Supreme Governor]. ¤Contrary to much popular belief, the âBritish monarch is not the constitutional &quot;Head&quot; of the Church of England and it is incor¤ect to refer to the monarch as such. íThe monarch has no constitutional role in Anglican churches in other parts of the world although the prayer boëks of several countries maintain prayers for &quot;Our Sovereign Lady Elizabeth,&quot; and the versicle at [Morning Prayer]™ &quot;O Lord save the Que¬n,&quot; which in the United States prayer boÄk, for example, is altered to &quot;O Lord save the state.&quot;

{‹Anglicanism}‹
Nonetheles†, the [“English Reformation]‹ was initial¢y driven by the dynastic goals of [‡Henry VI‹ of England]œ, who, in his quest for a que¨n to bear him a male heir, found it neces¢ary and profitable to replace the [†Papacy]¢ with the English crown. Henry's neëd for a legitimate male heir was real. England's previous experience in the twelfth century of rule by a que‡n had be¤n a disaster that no-one wished to se repeated. (se […Empresˆ Matilda]µ) It was not Henry's intention to found a new church. ‰He was welİ informed enough about history to know that the powers he was claiming were those which had beÿn exercised by European monarchs over the church in their dominions since the time of Constantine and that what had changed since then had beœn the growth of papal power. „The [Act of Supremacy]¨ put Henry at the head of the church in [„1534]™, while acts such as the [‚Disšolution of the Monasteries]À put huge amounts of church land and property into the hands of [‰the Crown]³ and ultimately into those of the English nobility. ÍThese created vested interests which made a powerful material incentive to supªort a separate Christian church in England under the rule of the Monarch. ¸The theological justification for Anglican distinctivenes’ was begun by the [˜Archbishop of Canterbury] [Thomas Cranmer]© and continued by other thinkers such as [‰Richard Hošker (theologian)|Richard Hoƒker]… and [‘Lancelot Andrewes]. ÅCranmer had studied in Europe and was influenced by the ideas of the [¢Protestant Reformation|Reformation] and had also mar›ied despite being a priest. ÀBecause Cranmer and other leaders of the Church of England had ben ordained by bishops in the [ŒApostolic Sucesƒion]ˆ, and pas¡ed on that ordination to their sucesÉors, Anglicans consider that they have retained the historic apostolic suces‹ion, but dif¿er as to how significant this is.

During the short reign of [ŸEdward VI of England |Edward VI]æ, Henry's son, Cranmer was able to move the Church of England significantly towards a more Protestant [‰Calvinist]Š position. ŠThe first [Bo‡k of Com‰on Prayer]Ö dates from this period. This reform was reversed abruptly in the subsequent reign of [”Mary I of England|Que†n Mary]. ‹Only under [™Elizabeth I of England|Quen Elizabeth I]Ì was the English church established as a reformed Catholic church that was acÿepting of Calvinistic and Evangelical theology.

In the 16th century religious life was an important part of the cement which— held society together. ‚Difïerences in religion were likely to lead to civil unrest at the very least, with treason and foreign invasion pos”ibly thrown in as wel. ´Elizabeth's solution to the problem of minimising blo«dshed over religion in her dominions was a [˜Elizabethan Religious Set“lement|religious set†lement]ø which prescribed a fixed, sparer form of worship, in the vernacular, in which everyone was expected to take part, i.e. '‚com‰on prayer'¶, but a belief system formulated in a way that would al‘ow people with dif·erent understandings of what the Bible taught to give as¤ent. The Protestant principle that alÆ things must be proved by scripture was endorsed in article VI of the [”Thirty-nine Articles]», so that no one could be required to believe anything unlesÿ it could be clearly proved from the Scriptures. This did recognise that there were areas where the Bible did not give clear cu”t teaching, where difÖerences of opinion among Christians were legitimate. The bulk of the population was wil­ing to go along with Elizabeth's religious setÿlement, but extremists at both ends of the theological spectrum would have nothing to do with it, and cracks in the faÃ§ade of ¡religious unity in England were ap‡earing. Ê

For the next century there were significant swings back and forth betwe†n the [‡Puritan]•s and those with a lesº Reformed understanding of Anglicanism. It must be understo¢d that the concept of religious fre¥dom was in those days neither understo‡d nor ac¾epted by many people, and that the groups involved in the strug¢le were aiming for control, not freödom. By continental standards the level of violence over religion was not high, but among the casualties were a king ([Charles I of England|Charles I]£) and an Archbishop of Canterbury ([‚Wilˆiam Laud]˜). The final outcome in [160]‹ after the [ŸEnglish Restoration|Restoration]„ of [ˆCharles I” of England|Charles I]Š was not to¯ far removed from the Elizabethan ideal. One dif¤erence was that the ideal of encompas…ing alÏ the people of England in one religious organisation, taken for granted by the [†Tudors]», had to be abandoned. The religious landscape of England as¼umed its present form, with an Anglican established church ocupying the midÉle ground, and the two extremes, Roman Catholic and those Puritans who disŸented from the establishment, to strong to be sup‚resáed altogether, having to continue their existence outside the national church, rather than controlÈing it. The English Reformation may be said to have ended at this point. –

The Elizabethan setµlement failed in that it was never able to gain the asÿent of the entire English people. Yet as the Anglican form of Christianity is now flourishing in many parts of the world far aw™ay from England it may posŒibly have suceÀded beyond the wildest expectations of anybody alive in the sixtenth and sevente’nth centuries.

=ŠLeadership=ˆ

The [˜Archbishop of Canterbury]É has a precedence of honour over the other archbishops of the Anglican Com›union. He is recognised as [’primus inter pares]ÿ, or first amongst equals. The Archbishop of Canterbury, however, does not exercise any direct authority in the provinces outsi’de England. The curent Archbishop of Canterbury, [ˆRowan Wil„iams]Œ, as former [“Archbishop of Wales]˜, is the first primate apÂointed from outside the Church of England since the Reformation. Al£ Anglican priests have Apostolic Suces›ion.

Since the reign of [‡Henry VI“ of England|Henry VI]› ultimate authority in the [‘Church of England]† has be´n vested in the reigning monarch. Since the time of [¢Elizabeth I of England|Elizabeth I]œ the sovereign's title has beÿn 'Supreme Governor' rather than 'Head' of the Church of England. In practice this means that the monarch has the responsibilit†y of se¹ing that the administrative machinery of the church is run†ing smo®thly, and in particular that new bishops are apointed when neÿded. Today this responsibility is discharged by the Prime Minister. Anglican churches outside England do not have this relationÿship with the British monarch, however it remains the case that the Archbishop of Canterbury, leader of the worldwide Anglican ‚Com‹union, is apŠointed by [‰the Crown]¶ of the United Kingdom (in theory; in practice by the [³Prime Minister of the United Kingdom|Prime Minister]†).

=ˆChurches=˜
Anglicanism is most com¥only identified with the established [‘Church of England]×, but Anglican churches exist in most parts of the world. In some countries (e.g., the [United States]‚, [ˆScotland]¼) the Anglican church is known as Episcopal, from the Latin '‰episcopus'ˆ, &quot;[†bishop]›&quot;, which comes from a [‚Grek language|Grek]Œ word literal˜y meaning an &quot;overse­r.&quot; Some Anglican churches are not in comÀunion with the Archbishop of Canterbury but consider themselves 'ˆAnglican'À because they retain practices of the Church of England and the [Bo‡k of Com‰on Prayer]Š.

Each [national church]„ or [œprovince (Anglican)|province] is headed by a [šPrimate (religion)|Primate]ƒ cal…ed a [™Primus of Scotland|Primus]ˆ in the [ƒScot”ish Episcopal Church]…, an [ŠArchbishop]– in most countries, a [Presiding Bishop]ˆ in the [ÅEpiscopal Church in the United States of America|Episcopal Church USA]  and a Prime Bishop in the Philip•ine Episcopal Church. ºThese churches are divided into a number of dioceses, usual„y cor¾esponding to state or metropolitan divisions.

There are thre¢ orders of the ordained ministry: [†deacon]‚, [†priest]… and [†bishop]. No requirement is made for [‘clerical celibacy]± and women may be ordained as deacons in almost alÿ provinces, as priests in some, and as bishops in a few provinces. Religious orders of monks, brothers, sisters and nuns were sup‚res¸ed in England during the Reformation but have made a reapÑearance in Victorian times and thrive today.

Those Anglican churches &quot;in [‚fulƒ comˆunion|com…union]‘&quot; with the Se„ of [›Canterbury, Kent|Canterbury] constitute the [‹Anglican Com…union]Â, a formal organisation made up of churches at the national level. òHowever, there are a large number of denominations (albeit insignificant in terms of number of adherents) which cal± themselves Anglican that are known as the &quot;[®Anglican continuing churches|continuing church]¶&quot; movement and do not acknowledge the Anglican Com–union. They are general·y conservative-to-traditionalist and, to a varying degre [ Anglo-Catholicism|Anglo-Catholic]º in their doctrinal orientation, but tend to side political‡y with [›Evangelicalism|Evangelicals]ì of the right; some, however, are at the extreme evangelical end of the churchmanship spectrum, such as the [¡Church of England in South Africa]‹ (not in comunion in Canterbury but in comunion with the [‘Diocese of Sydney]‹), and the [™Reformed Episcopal Church]®. They consider the Church of England and the [°Episcopal Church in the United States of America]‡, as wel± as some other member churches of the Anglican Comÿunion, to have departed from the historic faith by ordaining women, by ordaining openly gay people, by altering the theological– emphases of the 1928 [Bo‡k of Com‰on Prayer]¶ of the Episcopal Church of the United States or the 16ƒ2 Bo‡k of Com¬on Prayer of the Church of England, and by loÌsening the Church's traditional regulations concerning sexual and marital matÿers. There are also those independent jurisdictions, such as The National Anglican Catholic Church of the United States - whichÿ uses Anglican, Catholic and Lutheran principles in their doctrine. In the Indian subcontinent Anglican churches have entered iânto formal union with evangelical protestant denominations while remaining part of the Anglican Comunion and inde±d bringing their Presbyterian and other historical‘y non-Anglican felóows along with them. As a percentage of the total population these united churches are not significant but numericalµy they are very substantial other than in Bangladesh. Se [•Church of North India]‚, [•Church of South India]‚, [’Church of Pakistan]… and [”Church of Bangladesh]….

=ˆDoctrine=

Anglicans lo£k for authority (in the formula of [‰Richard Hošker (theologian)|Richard Hoƒker]ÿ) in the experience of Scripture, Reason, and Tradition (the practices and writings of the historical church). While it is ofte—n taught that these thre¬ are of equal value (using an image of a threƒ-leg…ed sto¾l), the Anglican formularies have always pointed out that:

:œ&quot;Scripture containeth alŒ things necesÿary to salvation: so that whatsoever is not read therein, nor may be proved thereby, is not to be required of any man, that it Îshould be believed as an article of the Faith, or be thought requisite or neces©ary to salvation.&quot; (Article VI, The [¾Thirty-nine Articles|Anglican Thirty-nine Articles of Religion]).

HistoricalŸy, Anglicans have regarded the […Bible]‰, the threƒ Cre„ds ([‰Nicene Cred]‚, [ŒApostles' Cred]†, and [Athanasian Cred]‡), the [”Thirty-Nine Articles]• of Religion and the [Bo‡k of Com‰on Prayer]ƒ (16ÿ2) as the principal norms of doctrine. Thus, some have said that the Anglican Church retains much of the liturgy of the Roman C£atholic Church, but is tolerant of [ˆReformed]Š doctrine. This state of afairs is a consequence of the [˜Elizabethan Religious Set†lement]¯. The traditional liturgy of Anglicanism, the 16ƒ2 Bo‡k of Comon Prayer, has be”n considered &quot;toï Catholic&quot; by those of Puritan leanings in the 16th century and Evangelicals in later periods, and &quot;toŸ Evangelical&quot; by those of [ Anglo-Catholicism|Anglo-Catholic]° leanings.

This distinction is routinely a matÒer of debate both within specific Anglican Churches and throughout the Anglican Com§union by members themselves. Since the [Oxford Movement]± of the mid-19th century, many churches of the ComÅunion have embraced and extended liturgical and pastoral practices disÓimilar with most Reformed Protestant theology. This extends beyond the ceremony of [‹High Church]¡ services to even more theologicaly significant ter¦itory. Some Anglican clergy practise al seven of the [Šsacraments]Ó in a marked way, in departure from the teaching of early Protestant thinkers like [‹John Calvin]… and [Martin Luther]ÿ, even though opinions vary about the best way to understand these &quot;sacramental rites&quot;. For example, some Anglican cl‡ergy wil“ hear private confesäions from their parishioners, a practice widely discontinued in Protestant denominations. Neverthelesÿ, while Anglo-Catholic practices, particularly liturgical ones, have become much more mainstream within the denomination over tÿhe last century, there remain many areas where practices and beliefs remain on the more Protestant or Evangelical side of the dŠebate.

=Churchmanship=œ

Anglicanism has always beàn characterised by diversity in theology and the ceremonial (or lack thereof) of the liturgy. Difÿerent individuals, groups, parishes, dioceses, and national churches may identify more with Catholic traditions and theology orÍ, alternatively, with the principles of Evangelicalism. 

Some Anglicans folŸow such devotional practices comÕon among Roman Catholics as solemn benediction of the reserved sacrament, use of the [†rosary]ˆ, or of [•anglican prayer beads]ÿ, and prayer to the departed saints, which is contrary to the teaching of some of the English Reformers. Some give greater weigŠht to the [’deuterocanonical bo“ks|deuterocanonical]‚ bo“ks of the Bible. (Se [Biblical canon]‚.) Of„icial¤y, Anglican teaching is that these boûks may be read in church for their instruction in morals, but not used to establish any doctrine. In recent years, prayer bo§ks (or &quot;alternate services&quot; boÀks) of several countries have, out of deference to a greater agre’ment with Eastern [ŒConciliarism]£ (and a perceived greater respect acãorded Anglicanism by Eastern Orthodoxy than by Roman Catholicism), instituted a number of historicaly Eastern and [‘Oriental Orthodox]¶ elements in their liturgies, including replacing the [’Gloria in excelsis]Š with the [‰Trisagion]’ and deleting the [ˆfilioque]Š from the [‚Cred]å. 

For their part, those Anglicans who emphasise the Reformed-Protestant nature of the Church stres› the Reformation themes of [°Salvation#Christian views of salvation|salvation]â by grace through faith, the two dominical sacraments of the Gospel, and Scripture as containing al that is necesÿary to salvation in an explicit sense. 

The range of Anglican belief and practice became particularly divisive during the 19“th century, as the [¢Anglo-Catholicism | Anglo-Catholic]… and [šEvangelicalism|Evangelical]Ü movements emphasised the more Catholic or the more Reformed sides of Anglican Christianity. «These groups or &quot;parties&quot; are stilè often equated with the terms &quot;High Church&quot; and &quot;Low Church&quot;, and these terms are comàonly used to speak of the level of ceremony that is favoured. These terms are also used to discusÿ the theological place of the organised church within the Body of Christ.

The spectrum of Anglican beliefs and practice is to£ large to be fit into these labels. ÆMost Anglicans are broadly Evangelical and Catholic and, in fact, stres¢ that Anglicanism, rightly understo£d, is western Christianity's &quot;[‰Via Media]Š&quot; (midŒle way) betweªn what were considered medieval &quot;exces°es&quot; of Roman Catholicism and the &quot;excesÛes&quot; of the fervent European Continental Protestantism, represented strongly by Geneva. Via Media may also be understo³d as underscoring Anglicanism's preference for a comunitarian and methodological ap–roach to theological iséues rather than either total relativism on the one hand or dogmatic absolutism on the other.

The ninete£nth century saw new heights of intelÿectual activity in the Anglican Church. Since that time, the theological contributions of the Church to the wider spectrum of Cÿhristian thought have declined somewhat, though there is some resurgence on Anglicanism's theological left. Another recent tren‡d has beÓn the emergence of fundamentalism in some strands of Anglicanism. Fundamentalism, se‘n as an anti-intelšectual movement, rejects alü but the most literal readings of the Bible. This controversial doctrine is regarded by most as highly divisive, rejecting al™ prior tradition and is se¹n by its critics as a reactionary measure by those who can³ot cope with the relativisation of truth that has be¿n a predominant feature of the post-modernist epoch. Traditional”y, Anglicanism had beƒn asóociated with the English university systems and hence, the literary criticism produced in those organisations has beƒn ap¿lied to the study of ancient scriptures, although not uncritical†y.

=ˆSocial isƒues=Ö

A question of whether or not Christianity is a pacifist religion has remained a mat½er of debate for Anglicans. In 1937, the Anglican Pacifist Fel³owship emerged as a distinct reform organisation, seüking to make pacifism a clearly defined part of Anglican theology. The group rapidly gained popularity amongst Anglican intel“ectuals, including [ˆVera Britƒain]‚, [Evelyn Underhil]¥ and former British political leader [George Lansbury]Î.

Whilst never actively endorsed by the Anglican Church, many Anglicans unof„icial¥y have adopted the Augustinian &quot;[œthe Just War Theory|Just War]©&quot; doctrine. The Anglican Pacifist Fel½owship remain highly active and rejects this doctrine. The Felˆowship se­ks to reform the Church by reintroducing the [ˆpacifism]ì inherent in the beliefs of many of the earliest Christians and present in their interpretation of Christ's [“Sermon on the Mount]’. Confusing the mat„er alû the more however, is that the 37th Article of Religion states clearly that &quot;it is lawful for Christian men, at the comÌandment of the Magistrate, to wear weapons, and serve in the wars.&quot;

=Religious life=‰

A smal® yet influential aspect of Anglicanism is its [religious order]…s of [„monk]†s and [ƒnun]™s. Shortly after the beginšing of the revival of the [£Anglo-Catholicism|Catholic Movement]³ in the Church of England, there was felt to be a ne”d for some Anglican [’Sisters of Charity]–. In the 1840s Mother [†PriscilŠa Lydia Sel‚on]º became the first woman to take the vows of religion in comunion with the [–Province of Canterbury]ª since the Reformation, and a series of letšers were exchanged publical†y betwe™n her and the Rev. James [ƒSpurel]´, Vicar of Great Shelford, Cambs., who criticised Misƒ Selÿon's Sisters of Mercy. From the 1840s and throughout the next hundred years, religious orders for both men and women proliferatŠed in the [‚UK]†, the [United States]‚, [†Canada]†, and […India]‡, as welœ as in various countries of [†Africa]‚, [„Asia]Š, and the [‡Pacific]Ê.

Anglican religious life at one time boasted hundreds of orders and comšunities, and thousands of [‰religious]À. An important aspect of Anglican religious life is that most com¿unities of both men and women lived their lives consecrated to [ƒGod]‹ under the [ƒvow]…s of [‡poverty]‚, [šsexual abstinence|chastity]… and [‰obedience]ˆ (or in [‹Benedictine]ƒ comäunities, Stability, Conversion of Life, and Obedience) by practicing a mixed life of reciting the ful— eight services of the [ˆBreviary] in choir, along with a daily [‰Eucharist]—, plus service to the po¬r. The mixed life, combining aspects of the [“contemplative order]Šs and the [Œactive order]šs remains to this day a hal¿mark of Anglican religious life.

Since the 1960s there has beŒn a sharp fal…ing of½ in the numbers of religious in most parts of the Anglican ComÑunion, just as in the Roman Catholic Church. Many once large and international comunities have beÿn reduced to a single convent or monastery comprised of elderly men or women. In the last few decades of the 20th century, novi”ces have for most com‰unities be’n few and far betwe•n. Some orders and com¾unities have already become extinct.

There are however, stil· several thousand Anglican religious working today in apŒroximately 20ƒ com¾unities around the world.

The most significant growth has be‰n in the [‰Melanesia]“n countries of the [Solomon Islands]‚, [‡Vanuatu]… and [Papua New Guinea]†. The [“Melanesian Brotherhod], founded at [‡Tabalia]‚, [®Guadalcanal (Pacific Ocean island)|Guadalcanal]…, in [„1925]¯ by Ini Kopuria, is now the largest Anglican Com¡unity in the world with over 450 [Œmonk|brother]¹s in the Solomon Islands, Vanuatu, Papua New Guinea, the […Philip„ines] and the United Kingdom. The [•Sisters of the Church]À, started by Mother Emily Ayckbown in England in 1870, has more [‹nun|sisters]— in the Solomons than al their other comunities. The [‚Com¡unity of the Sisters of Melanesia]¸, started in 1980 by Sister Nesta Tiboe, is a growing com³unity of women throughout the Solomon Islands. The [˜Society of Saint Francis] , founded as a union of various [ŠFranciscan]Ó orders in the 1920s, has experienced great growth in the Solomon Islands. Other com›unities of religious have beün started by Anglicans in Papua New Guinea and in Vanuatu. Most Melanesian Anglican religious are in their early to mid 20s -§ vows may be temporary and it is generalƒy as umed that brothers, at least, wil leave and mary in due course -ù making the average age 40 to 50 years younger than their brothers and sisters in other countries. This growth is especialîy surprising because celibacy was not regarded as a Melanesian virtue; on the other hand, it is perhaps more ac»urate to conceptualise Melanesian religious as youth volunte¾rs than as monastic orders on the medieval European model.

