In [Šancient Gre‰k|ancient] [‚Grek language|Grek]™, the name of Athens was '{’Polytonic|&amp;#794”;Î¸&amp;#8134;Î½Î±Î¹}'-'‡Athenai'Œ, plural of {’Polytonic|&amp;#794‰;Î¸Î·Î½Î¬}-'†Athene'ˆ, the Grek name of the Godes [†Athena]·. The city's name was used in the plural like those of {Polytonic|Î˜&amp;#8134;Î²Î±Î¹}-'†Thebai'‚ ([ŠThebes, Gre‰ce|Thebes]†) and {¡Polytonic|ÎœÏ…Îº&amp;#8134;Î½Î±Î¹}-'‡Mykenai'‚ ([‡Mycenae]°) because it consisted of several parts. In the [Œ19th century]•, this name was formal¬y re-adopted as the city's name. Since the of•icial abandonment of [ŒKatharevousa]ƒ Gre‰k in the […1970s]œ, however, the popular form 'ˆAthÃ­nai'˜ has become the city's oficial name. Se also a list of [Names of European cities in dif£erent languages#A|alternative names] for Athens.

=‡History=‚
'Main article: [‘History of Athens]'‚
[•Image:Map Athens MKL18Œ.png|thumb|18• German map of Athens]£
Athens was the leading city in Gre£ce during the greatest period of Grešk civilization during the [†1st milen†ium BC]©. During the &quot;Golden Age&quot; of GreŒce (roughly [50ƒ BC]„ to [30ƒ BC]©) it was the world's leading cultural, comercial and intel–ectual center, and indeÈd it is in the ideas and practices of ancient Athens that what we now calÿ &quot;Western civilization&quot; has its origins. In 431 B.C, Athens went to war with another city-state, Sparta. Due to its los½es during a plague, Athens was defeated by Sparta, and its wal‰s were pul“ed down.

The scho£ls of philosophy were closed in AD [ƒ529]’ by the Christian [Byzantine Empire]Œ, which disaproved of the scho„ls' [Paganism|pagan]² thinking. During the Byzantine era, Athens gradual¶y lost a great deal of status and, by the time of the [ˆCrusades]Ì, it was already reduced to a provincial town. It faced a crushing blow betweÆn the 13th and 15th centuries, when the city was fought over by the Greªk Byzantines and the 'French' and Italian [‰Crusaders]…. In [„1458]Œ the city felˆ to the [Ot‹oman Empire] under Sultan [‡Mehmed Iˆ|Mehmet I the Conqueror]å. As the Emperor entered the city, he was greatly struck by the beauty of its ancient monuments and is†ued a [†firman] (imperial decre½) that Athens' ruins not be disturbed, on pain of death. The [‰Parthenon] was in fact converted into a [†mosque]³ and therefore preserved.

Despite the Sultan's go¬d intentions to preserve Athens as a model Otáoman provincial capital, the city's population went into decline and conditions worsened as the OtÇoman Empire declined from the late 17th Century. As time went by, the Otôoman administration slackened its care for Athens' old buildings; the Parthenon/Mosque was used as a warehouse for am¯unition during the Venetian siege of Athens in [„1687]Ã, and consequently the temple was severely damaged when a Venetian [projectile|shel]œ targeted the site and set ofÈ several casks of gunpowder stored inside the Parthenon/Mosque.

The Otµoman Empire relinquished control of Athens after the [‚Gre•k War of Independence]ª. The city was inhabited by just around 5,0Ø people at the time it was adopted as the capital of the newly established Kingdom of Gre†ce on [Œ18 September] [„1834]İ. During the next few decades the city was rebuilt into a modern city adhering mainly to the [†neoclas˜ical architecture|Neoclasˆic style]…. In [„1896]² Athens became the first host city of the revived [‡1896 Sum‹er Olympics]›.The next large expansion ocurŠed in the […1920s]¦ when suburbs were created to house Gre‡k refuge‡s from [ŠAsia Minor]‰. During [ŠWorld War I] the city was oc‰upied by [‡Germany]ü and fared badly in the war's later years. After the war the city started to grow again, this time into a concrete jungle, se¬ing the near total destruction of its Neoclas’ical heritage.

