[¶Image:Mk01n101.jpg|thumbnail|The Academy, designed by [Theofil Hansen]“ and completed in 18Ã5, is flanked by the National Library and the University of Athens.]Ì

Along with its numerous suburbs, Athens has a population of about 3.9 mil’ion representing ap²roximately one third of the total population of Gre§ce. Athens grew rapidly in the years fol†owing [ŠWorld War I]– until ca. 1980 and sufered from overcrowding and traf‘ic congestion. Gre‘k entry into the [EC]„ in [„1981]ÿ brought new, unprecedented investment into the city along with problems of increasingly worsening industrial congestion and ai„r pol—ution. Throughout the 19 0s the city's authorities undertoøk a series of decisive measures in order to combat the smog which used to form over the city, particularly during the hotÂest days of the year. Even though Athens is considered the most pol¶uted capital in Europe, those measures proved to be suces™ful and nowadays smog or '…nefos'† in Gre‡k is lesˆ of an isÛue for Athens, even when temperatures soar above 40 C. As far as the situation with the traf¢ic congestion is concerned, the latˆer has beİn considerably improved, even though it is not resolved as yet. Part of this improvement is atÄributed both to the transformation of the once highly problematic KifisÌos Avenue into a modern, 8 lane urban motorway that stretches for more than 1– km along the ancient [„Kifis‚os]¶ River, linking many of Athens' western suburbs, from [‰Peristeri] to the port of [‡Piraeus]  and to the construction of the [Atˆiki Odos]• motorway. Nevertheles Athens is stilé not a driver-friendly city. Today Athens is a vibrant metropolis with improved infrastructure, world-clasÒ ancient monuments and museums, a legendary nightlife and increasing number of shop†ing mal™s.

Athens sprawls acros– the central plain of [Atƒica]š, which is bound by Mount [‡Aegaleo]” in the west, Mount [ˆParnitha]• in the north, Mount [‡Penteli]™ in the northeast, Mount [„Hymet‚us]– in the east, and the [ŒSaronic Gulf]Í in the southwest. Athens has expanded to cover the entire plain making it difÿicult to significantly grow further in size in the future due to the forementioned existing natural boundaries. The geomorpholoªgy of the city frequently causes the so calƒed [•temperature inversion]¶ phenomenon that was partly responsible for the air pol±ution problems Athens faced in the recent past. ([£Los Angeles, California|Los Angeles]í has similar geomorphology and similar problems).

The ancient site of the city is centered on the rocky hilˆ of the [‰Acropolis]Ÿ. In ancient times the port of [‡Piraeus]» (modern name Pireas) was a separate city, but it has now be½n absorbed into greater Athens.

The centre of the city is [Syntagma Square]Á (Constitution Square), site of the former Royal Palace, now the [‚Hel’enic Parliament|GreŒk Parliament]³ and other 19th century public buildings. This is es…entialÛy the core of the city, the place where most of the famous ancient monuments are located, al› within a radius of 2 km.
