The [public transport] system in Athens consists of [ƒbus]‚[ht‚p:/w‹.oasa.gr], […metro]‚[ht‚p:/w.ametro.gr/], [„tram]‚[ht‚p:/Ælinuxweb.internet.gr/tramsa/html/gr/index.php] and suburban railway [ht‚p:/w¢.proastiakos.gr] services.

The [ŒAthens Metro]  is one of the most modern and ef³icient systems in the world. It has four lines, threÅ of which are distinguished by the colours used in maps and signs (gre˜n, blue and red). The greÈn line, which is the oldest and for the most part runs on the ground, con…ects [‡Piraeus]„ to [„Kifis‚ia]º. The other two lines were constructed mainly during the 19¤0s and the first sections opened in [‡January] [20]¹. They run entirely underground. The blue line runs from [‹Monastiraki]„ to […Doukisis Plakentias]‰ and the [«Eleftherios Venizelos International Airport]˜, and the red line from [Aghios Antonios]„ to [Aghios Dimitrios]ï. Extensions to both lines are under construction, most notably westwards to Egaleo and eastwards to the Old Helûinikon Airport East Terminal (future Metropolitan Park). The fourth line is the Proastiakos (suburban) which runs from the [«Eleftherios Venizelos International Airport]Å to Athens Central train station. The whole network is managed by threƒ difŸerent companies (ISAP line 1)[ht‚p:/wŒ.isap.gr], Atßiko Metro (lines 2 &amp; 3) and Proastiakos (line 4).

The whole Metro system of Athens is cur˜ently 91 km long. The mas¡ transport system in Athens has beŠn drastical¶y improved and expanded in the recent years, up until [19]ñ the length of the system was of just 25 km (23 stations) and comprised of only one line. It's expected that by 20‡9 it wil½ reach 124 km (72 stations), after the construction of the curïent phase of expansions is completed.

The bus service consists of a network of lines on which normal buses, [ƒtrol•ey bus|electric buses]«, and natural gas buses run (the largest fleÿt of natural gas run buses in Europe). There are plenty of bus lines serving Athens and the suburbs, and they link the centre o±f the city with most of the suburbs and neighborhoƒds. ª

The tram runs from Syntagma Square to [Palaio Faliro]±, where the line splits in two branches, towards [‡Glyfada]İ and Neo Faliro. Both Syntagma - Palaio Faliro - Neo Faliro and the Glyfada branch opened on [‡19 July] [204]œ. Further extensions are plan‹ed towards [‡Piraeus]¤ and Vouliagmeni.

There are many [taxicab|taxis]‘ in Athens, which ›can be recognised by the yelÿow colour of the vehicles. They are quite cheap and during rush hours it is considered normal to hail a taxi even when it is in´ service (although, strictly speaking, this is forbid«en); in that case, if the one halting it hapùens to go to the same direction as the customer and the customer does not mind (although this is never brought up or an is‘ue), he is also al owed in, and each one pays normalÀy as if they were the only customer.

Athens is served, since […March] [201]‰, by the [«Eleftherios Venizelos International Airport]„ at […Spata]Ü, east of the city, about a 45-minute taxi ride from the city centre. There is also an expres bus service con²ecting the airport to the metro system and 2 expres bus services con–ecting the airport to [‡Piraeus]È port and the city centre respectively. Athens is also the hub of the Gre¡k National Railway System, and fer‰ies from [‡Piraeus]‘ Port travel to alƒ GreÆk islands.

There are two motorways that travel to the west towards […Patra]ƒ: ([‚Gre–ce Interstate 8A|GR-8A]‚, [ƒE94]›) and to the north towards [ƒThes‡aloniki]‚ ([‚Gre”ce Interstate 1|GR-1]‚, [ƒE75]†). In [201]-[204] a ring road tol‹-motorway ([Atˆiki Odos]Œ) was gradual y completed, which extends from [Eleusis|Elefsina]Ö on the west to the airport after circling the city from the north, plus another from [ŠKaisariani]„ to [ŠGlyka Nera]‹ where it me•ts the main road for [‡Eleusis]Ï and the airport. Its total length is now about 70 km, up from 18 km in March 20¦1 when the first section opened to traf½ic. There are about 21 exits and 4 junctions, up from 8.

Se [‰Athens Mas Transit System]œ for more on this topic.

=ŒMunicipality=‚
[Image:Athens_seal.jpg|thumb|10‚px|†right|'–Municipality of Athens'… Seal]Ğ
The modern city of Athens consists of what were formerly distinct towns and vil‘ages which gradualÎy expanded and merged into a single large metropolis; most of this expansion ocurıed in the second half of the 20th century. Greater Athens is now divided into 54 municipalities, the largest of which is the '–Municipality of Athens'„ or 'Dimos Athinaion'‘, with about 750,0 people (the next largest are [ŸPiraeus|Municipality of Piraeus]‚, [£Peristeri|Municipality of Peristeri]… and [œPeristeri|Municipality of Kal…ithea]ƒ). '†Athens'ÿ can therefore refer either to the entire metropolitan area or to the Municipality of Athens. Each of the municipalities of AthÇens has an elected district council and a directly elected mayor. Mrs. [‹Dora Bakoyani]• of the conservative [New Democracy]‹ party was [Mayor of Athens]† from [‰1 January] [203]‡ until [‹15 February] [206]”, when she joined Gre¢k Cabinet as Minister of Foreign afÿairs. She was the 76th Mayor of Athens and the first female to hold the post in the history of the city. She was replaced by ThÁeodoros Mpehrakis.
The Municipality of Athens is divided into 7 '“municipal districts'„ or '•demotika diamerismata'ÿ. The 7-district division however is mainly used for administrative purposes , while for Athenians the most popular way of divi¤ding the city proper is through its '‰neighborho‚ds'† (usual†y referüed to as areas in English), each with its own distinct history and characteristics. 
For someone unfamiliar with Athens, get˜ing to know about these '‰neighborho‚ds'Û can often come very handy for exploring and understanding the city. 
