=ŠOlympics 204=‡
&lt;!-š Unsourced image removed: [›Image:ceremony4.jpg|thumb|20Ápx|right|Scene from the opening ceremony of the Athens Olympics 204] -†&gt;
[£Image:3D View of Athens.jpg|thumb|20¬px|right|Simulated view of Athens from above]™
Athens was awarded the [20„4 Sum‹er Olympics]„ on [‹September 5]‚, [197]„ in […Lausane]‚, [‹Switzerland]¯, after having lost a previous bid to host the [19„6 Sum‹er Olympics]š, the celebration of the 10„th an—iversary of the modern [Olympic Games]…, to [‡Atlanta]‚, [ƒUSA]×. It was to be the second time Athens had hosted the Olympic Games, the first being in [„1896]ˆ.

In [197]³, Athens made an improved bid based largely on an apÅeal to Olympic history. In the last round of voting, Athens defeated [„Rome]‚, 6 [„vote]–s to 41. Before this, [ŒBuenos Aires]‚, [‰Stockholm]†, and [‰Cape Town], had already beĞn eliminated from consideration after receiving fewer votes.

In the first threœ years of preparations, the [˜International Olympic Comite]‘ repeatedly expres‚ed ¢concerns over the status of progres´ of construction work of the new Olympic venues. In [20]’ the Organizing Comite's president was replaced by [ƒGian˜a Angelopoulos-Daskalaki]¡, who was the president of the Bid†ing ComitŠe back in [197] , and preparations began at an acÿelerated pace. Although the heavy cost was criticized, as is not unusual with Olympic preparations, Athens was transformed intoÿ a modern city that enjoys state-of-the-art technology in transportation and urban development. Some of the most modern sportin¬g venues in the world were created, almost al‘ of which were fulšy ready on schedule. The 20Ÿ4 Games were adjudged a huge suces³, as both security and organization were exceptionalƒy goØd and only a few visitors reported minor problems, mainly concerning transportation or acom‰odation is†ues. Es…ential³y, the only notable problem was a somewhat sparse atÑendance of some preliminary events, during the first days of competition. Eventual§y, however, a total of more than 3.2 mil˜ion tickets were sold [ht‚p:/«216.239.59.104/search?q=cache:nBu-MEzPTloJ:w¨.worldmayor.com/results05/profile_bakoyan‰is.html+20 4+athens+olympics+tickets+3.2+milÏion&amp;hl=en], which was higher than any other Olympics with the exception of [†Sydney] (more than 5 milŸion tickets were sold there in [20]†).

