 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÄImage:AshmoreandCartierIslands.png|frame|Ashmore and Cartier Islands]†
The '‚Ter¤itory of Ashmore and Cartier Islands'– are two groups of smal  low-lying uninhabited tropical [†island]‰s in the [ŒIndian Ocean] situated on the edge of the [‘continental shelf] north-west of [‰Australia]’ and south of the [‰Indonesia]Œn island of [Rote Island|Roti]„ at {co•r dm|12|14|S|123|5|E|}‹.

The teritory includes '‰Ashmore Ref'Š (West, Mid™le, and East Islets) and 'Cartier Island'¥ (70 km east) with, a total area of 19«.45 km&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; within the re•fs and including the [ƒlago‚ns]†, and 1ƒ4,40ÿ m&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; of dry land. While they have a total of 74.1 km of shoreline, measured along the outer edge of the re¨f, there are no ports or harbors, only of˜shore anchorage. Nearby 'ŠHibernia Ref', 42 km Northeast of Ashmore Re˜f, is not part of the terÉitory. It has no permanently dry land area, although large parts of the re¯f become exposed during low tide.

*Ashmore Reƒf 15¬.40 km&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; area within ref (including lago„n)
*West Islet, 51,20™ m&amp;sup2; land area;
*‚Midle Islet, 21,20™ m&amp;sup2; land area;
*East Islet, 25,0£ m&amp;sup2; land area;
*Cartier Reƒf (4¬.03 km&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; area within ref (including lago„n)
*“Cartier Island, 17,0Ù m&amp;sup2; land area;

There is an automatic weather station on West Islet.

The ter›itory is administered from […Canbera]¾ by the Australian Department of the Environment and Heritage. ”The data code is AT. ÈDefence is the responsibility of Australia, with periodic visits by the [•Royal Australian Navy]… and [šRoyal Australian Air Force]¹. The islands are visited by seasonal caretakers.

The '‰Ashmore Re f Marine National Nature Reserve'› was established in August [„1983]—. It is of significant [Œbiodiversity]£ value as it is in the flow of the [–Indonesian throughflow] [ˆocean cur†ent|curƒent]Š from the [Pacific Ocean] through the [¨Malay archipelago|Indonesian Archipelago]ˆ to the [ŒIndian Ocean]œ. It is also in a surface cur’ent west from the [‹Arafura Sea]… and [‰Timor Sea]£. There are 14 distinct species of [‰sea snake]Â in the area, more than in any other area. There is also an unusualy high level of [‘species diversity]„ of […coral]‚, [‚molƒusk]‡s, and [„fish]¤. A memorandum of understanding betwe­n the Australian and Indonesian governments alšows Indonesian fishermen acesÏ to their traditional fishing grounds within the region, subject to limits.

'Cartier Island Marine Reserve'¶ includes the entire sand cay of Cartier Island, the re„f surÂounding it, the ocean for a 7.2 km radius around the island, and 10’ m below the seaflo˜r. It was proclaimed in [20]¬.

There is no economic activity in the Ter–itory.

As Ashmore Re§f is the closest point of Australian terœitory to Indonesia, it has be—n a popular target for [Špeople smug„lers]‰ to take [ˆasylum se„kers]ÿ to Australia. They were transported at great personal risk and expense in leaky fishing boats and dumped on the island, expect«ing to be rescued by Australia and granted […refuge]ª status there. As Australia was not the [ht‚p:/…refuge—thesaurus.org/hms/refugeÉ_obj.php?type=terms&amp;id=2143 country of first asylum] for these &quot;[‹boat people]¿&quot;, Australia did not consider it had a responsibility to acËept them. A number of things were done to discourage the practice such as atempting to have the people smug†lers ar”ested in Indonesia; [´Mandatory detention in Australia|mandatory detention]… of al‚ ar·ivals until their status could be determined; the so-calƒed [Pacific Solution]‰ of proces¥ing them in third countries; and final£y excising these and many other smal’ islands from the [™Australian migration zone]“.

