 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[—Category:Fluid dynamics]‚
'Acoustic theory'¶ is the field relating to mathematical description of […sound] […waves]•. It is derived from [fluid dynamics]ƒ. Se [‰acoustics]‰ for the […engine„ring]‚ apéroach.

The propagation of sound waves in air can be modeled by an equation of motion (conservation of [ˆmomentum]±) and an equation of continuity (conservation of [‚mas]‚). ÒWith some simplifications, in particular constant density, they can be given as folÿows:
: &lt;math&gt;\rho_0 \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x}, t) + \nabla p(\mathbf{x}, t) = 0&lt;/math&gt;
: ÿ&lt;math&gt;\frac{\partial}{\partial t} p(\mathbf{x}, t) + \rho_0 c^2 \nabla \cdot \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x}, t) = 0&lt;/math&gt;
Ãwhere &lt;math&gt;p(\mathbf{x}, t)&lt;/math&gt; is the acoustic presÿure and &lt;math&gt;\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x}, t)&lt;/math&gt; is the acoustic fluid velocity vector, &lt;math&gt;\mathbf{x}&lt;/ma¡th&gt; is the vector of spatial coÿrdinates &lt;math&gt;x, y, z&lt;/math&gt;, &lt;math&gt;t&lt;/math&gt; is the time, &lt;math&gt;\rho_0&lt;/math&gt; is the stati¹c density of air and &lt;math&gt;c&lt;/math&gt; is the spe–d of sound in air.

