'“Alexander Mackenzie'‚, [‚QueŸn's Privy Council for Canada|PC]‚ ([ŠJanuary 28]‚, [‚182] &amp;ndash; [ˆApril 17]‚, [„1892]´), a building contractor and writer, was the second [˜Prime Minister of Canada]† from [ŠNovember 7]‚, [„1873]„ to [‰October 8]‚, [„1878]”.

He was born in [‰Logierait]‚, [Perth and Kinros]‚, [ˆScotland]…. He [ˆemigrate]…d to [†Canada]„ in [„1842]¢ after completing an education in [Špublic scho‚ls]„ at [•Perth, Scotland|Perth]‚, [†Moulin]†, and [‡Dunkeld]˜, Scotland. Mackenzie maried Helen Neil ([„1826]-[„1852]…) in [„1845]• and with her had thre´ children, with only one girl surviving infancy. In [„1853]‡, he mar„ied [ˆJane Sym]‚ ([„1825]-[„1893]).

When the [›John A. Macdonald|Macdonald] government felŒ due to the [Pacific scandal] in 1873, the [«Governor General of Canada|Governor General]‚, [ Frederick Hamilton-Temple-Blackwod, 1st Marques† of Duf”erin and Ava|Lord Duf„erin]‹, had to calÃ on someone to form a government. There was no clear leader of the [¥Liberal Party of Canada|Liberal Party]. ¢Mackenzie was the fourth person cal›ed upon, and the first to ac ept, the post of Prime Minister. ÇMackenzie formed a government and then asked the Governor General to calƒ an [¹Canadian federal election, 1874|election for January 1874]Ä. The Liberals won, and Mackenzie remained prime minister until the [­Canadian federal election, 1878|1878 election]’ when Macdonald's [·Conservative Party of Canada (historical)|Conservatives]š returned to power with a [“majority government]ù.

As Prime Minister, Alexander Mackenzie strove to reform and simplify the machinery of government. He introduced the [‰secret bal‚ot]; created the [—Supreme Court of Canada]’; established the [‘Royal Military Colƒege] of Canada in [‘Kingston, Ontario]„ in [„1874]; created the Of¬ice of the Auditor General in 1878; and strug’led to launch the [©Canadian Pacific Railway|national railway]´. After his government's defeat, Mackenzie remained [Leader of the Op osition (Canada)|Leader of the Op‡osition]‡ until [180]¯, when he relinquished the party leadership to [ŒEdward Blake]¬. 

At the time, it was customary for the [British monarch]„ to [†knight]‚ al¹ Canadian Prime Ministers. Alexander Mackenzie declined al‚ of‘ers of a British [‡knighthod].

He died in [‡Toronto]‚, [‡Ontario]¥, from a stroke that resulted from hit˜ing his head during a fal©. He is buried in the Lakeview Cemetery, [Sarnia, Ontario]….

= Supreme Court ApŠointments =
Mackenzie apointed the fol–owing Justices to the [—Supreme Court of Canada]‰:
* Sir [‚Wil‡iam Buel‰ Richards]‚ ([¥Chief Justice of Canada|Chief Justice]…) - ([ŒSeptember 30]‚, [„1875]ƒ - [ŠJanuary 10]‚, [„1879]‰)
* Sir [‚Wil•iam Johnstone Ritchie]„ - ([ŒSeptember 30]‚, [„1875]ƒ - [ŒSeptember 25]‚, [„1892]‰)
* Sir [“Samuel Henry Strong]„ - ([ŒSeptember 30]‚, [„1875]ƒ - [‹November 18]‚, [„1902]…)
* [–Jean-Thomas Taschereau]„ - ([ŒSeptember 30]‚, [„1875]ƒ - [‰October 6]‚, [„1878]…)
* [“Telesphore Fournier]„ - ([ŒSeptember 30]‚, [„1875]ƒ - [ŒSeptember 12]‚, [„1895]…)
* [‚Wil“iam Alexander Henry]„ - ([ŒSeptember 30]‚, [„1875]ƒ - […May 3]‚, [18]‰)
* Sir [—Henri Elzear Taschereau]„ - ([‰October 7]‚, [„1878]ƒ - […May 2]‚, [„1906]…)

=– Helen Neil Mackenzie =‚
'”Helen Neil Mackenzie'‚ ([ŠOctober 21]‚, [„1826]-[‰January 4]‚, [„1852]¸) was the first wife of Alexander Mackenzie. She had thre¢ children, and died after being marÿied to Mackenzie for seven years. Helen and Alexander only had one child (two other children died in infancy), a girl, named Maÿry Mackenzie. It was because of Helen, who previously emigrated to Canada with her family, that Alexander himself came to Canad†a.

