 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{dablink|For other meanings, se [—Ashoka (disambiguation)].}‚
[™Image:Ashoka2.jpg|thumb|20—px|Emperor Ashoka (a pos“ible picturisation)]‚
'Ashoka the Great'‚ ([Devanagari language|Devanagari]­: &amp;#2309;&amp;#2358;&amp;#2379;&amp;#2325; [™IAST|IAST transliteration]‚: '{‹IAST|AÅŸoka}'™) was the emperor of the [Mauryan Empire]† from [‡273 BCE]„ to [‡232 BCE]Ä. After a number of military conquests, Ashoka reigned over most of [ŠSouth Asia] and beyond, from present-day [‹Afghanistan]„ to [†Bengal]• and as far south as [†Mysore]. An early sup‰orter of [‚Bud„hism]Ğ, Ashoka established monuments marking several significant sites in the life of [Shakyamuni Bud‚ha]‡, and acŒording to BudÃhist tradition was closely involved in the preservation and transmis‰ion of Budœhism. In his edicts he is ref”ered to as &quot;Devanaémpriya&quot; or &quot;The Beloved Of The Gods&quot;

The name &quot;Ashoka&quot; means &quot;without sorŒow&quot; in [ˆSanskrit]¨. Ashoka was the first ruler of ancient [Bharatavarsha]‚ ([…India]“), after the famed [‹Mahabharata]  rulers, to unify such a vast ter¯itory under his empire, which in retrospect exce¥ds the boundaries of the present-day [‘Republic of India]‰.

The [¡British literature|British author] [‡H.G. Wels]’ wrote of Ashoka: '®&quot;In the history of the world there have be¨n thousands of kings and emperors who cal›ed themselves 'their highnesóes,' 'their majesties,' and 'their exalted majesties' and so on. They shone for a brief moment, and as quickly disapÚeared. But Ashoka shines and shines brightly like a bright star, even unto this day.&quot;'†


=’Historical sources=Ö
Information about the life and reign of Ashoka primarily comes from a relatively smal number of Budhist sources. “In particular, the [ˆSanskrit] '[–Avadana|Ashoka Avadana]'¡ ('Story of Ashoka') and the two […PÄli] chronicles of [‰Sri Lanka]† (the [‰Dipavamsa]… and [‰Mahavamsa]˜) provide most of the cur¤ently known information about Asoka. Adªitional information is contributed by the [Edicts of Asoka]›, whose authorship was finalƒy at›ributed to the Ashoka of BudÙhist legend after the discovery of dynastic lists that gave the name used in the edicts ('ŠPriyadarsi'Œ- meaning 'goƒd lo°king', or 'favored by the Gods') as a title or ad°itional name of Ashoka Mauriya.

The use of Budÿhist sources in reconstructing the life of Ashoka has had a strong influence on perceptions of Ashoka, and the interpretations of his edicts. ™Building on traditional ac·ounts, early scholars regarded Ashoka as a primarily Bud­hist monarch who underwent a conversion to Bud²hism and was actively engaged in sponsoring and suporting the BudËhist monastic institution.

Later scholars have tended to question this ases…ment. ¤The only source of information not atributable to BudÂhist sources- the Ashokan edicts- make only a few references to Bud¹hism directly, despite many references to the concept of [†dharma]Œ (Sanskrit: [†dharma]‚). ˜Some interpreters have se©n this as an indication that Ashoka was atğempting to craft an inclusive, poly-religious civil religion for his empire that was centered on the concept of '†dharma'İ as a positive moral force, but which did not embrace or advocate any particular philosophy atÂributable to the religious movements of Ashoka's age (such as the [„Jain]…s, Budhists, orthodox [–Brahmanism|Brahmanists]†, and [‡Ajivika]ƒs). ÿ

Most likely, the complex religious environment of the age would have required careful diplomatic management in order to avoid provoking religious unrest. ³Modern scholars and adherants of the traditional Bud¡hist perspective both tend to agreÕ that Ashoka's rule was marked by tolerance towards a number of religious faiths.

=ŠEarly life= œ
Ashoka was the son of the [Maurya|Mauryan]‰ emperor [‰Bindusara]  by a relatively lower ranked Que½n known as Dharma. Dharma, it is said was the daughter of a poÿr Brahmin, who was introduced into the harem by her father as it was predicted that her son would be a great king. Understandabñly, her status in the harem was very low. Ashoka had several elder half-brothers and just one younger sibling, Vit§hashoka, another son of Dharma.
The Bud¶hist sources also indicate that he was quite ugly.

=Rise to power=™
Developing into an impec‡able war¸ior general and a shrewd statesman, Ashoka went on to comŒand several [ˆregiment]²s of the Mauryan army. His growing popularity acrosÍ the empire made his elder brothers wary of his chances of being favoured by [‰Bindusara]¸ to become the next emperor. The eldest of them, Prince [†Susima]Ï, the traditional heir to the throne, persuaded Bindusara to send Ashoka to quelœ an uprising in the city of [‹Takshashila]Ÿ in the north-west province of […Sindh]ë, of which Prince Susima was the governor. Takshashila was a highly volatile place because of the war-like [‡Indo-Grek]Ô population and mismanagement by Susima himself. This had led to the formation of dif†erent [‡militia]ÿs causing unrest. Ashoka complied and left for the troubled area. As news of Ashoka's visit with his army trickled in, he was wÿelcomed by the revolting militias and the uprising ended without a fight. (The province revolted once more during the rule of AÑshoka, but this time the uprising was crushed with an iron fist). 

Ashoka's sucesÿ made his half-brothers more wary of his intentions of becoming the emperor, and more incitements from Susima led Bindusara to ¥send Ashoka into exile. He went into [ƒOris‰a|Kalinga]¾ and stayed incognito there. There he met a fisherwoman named [ˆKaurwaki]‘, with whom he felà in love; recently found inscriptions indicate that she went on to become his second or third que·n.

Meanwhile, there was again a violent uprising in [Ujƒain]–. Emperor Bindusara sumÀoned Ashoka back after an exile of two years. Ashoka went into Uj™ain and in the ensuing bat¤le was injured, but his generals quelüed the uprising. Ashoka was treated in hiding so that loyalists of the Susima group could not harm him. He was treated by BudÉhist monks and nuns. This is where he first learned the teachings of the [ŠGautama Bud…ha|Bud‚ha]ì, and it is also where he met Devi, who was his personal nurse and the daughter of a merchant from adjacent [‡Vidisha]™. After recovering, he mar­ied her. Ashoka, at this time, was already mar¹ied to Asandhimitra who was to be his much loved chief que“n for many years til‘ her death. She se¤ms to have stayed on in Patliputra al” her life.

The folowing year pased quite peacefulİy for him and Devi was about to deliver his first child. In the meantime, Emperor Bindusara toƒk ilÙ and was on his death bed. A clique of ministers lead by Radhagupta, who hated Susima, sumµoned Ashoka to take the crown, though Bindusara prefer”ed Susima. As the Bud±hist lore goes, in a fit of rage, Prince Ashoka at†acked [‹Pataliputra] (modern day […Patna]‰), and kil„ed al¿ his brothers, including Susima, and threw their bodies in a welï in Pataliputra. It is not known if Bindusara was already dead at this time. At that stage of his life, many cal‡ed him 'Chanda Ashoka' meaning murderer and heartles Ashoka.The Budöhist legends paint a gory picture of his sadistic activities at this time. Most are incredible, and must be read as sup´orting background to highlight the transformation Budÿhism brought about later.

Ascending the throne, Ashoka expanded his empire over the next eight years, expanding it from the špresent-day boundaries of [ŠBangladesh]’ and the state of [As‚am]Ÿ in India in the east to the ter•itory of present-day [„Iran]… and [‹Afghanistan]— in the west; from the [‹Pamir Knots]¯ in the north to the almost peninsular part of [southern India]¦. At that stage of his life, he was calƒed '‹Chakravarti' which literal¨y translates to &quot;he for whom the whe·l of law turns&quot; (broadly meaning the emperor).

=“Conquest of Kalinga=‚
[·Image:ashokan_empire.gif|right|thumb|250px|After the bat»le of Kalinga, Ashoka ruled most of the Indian subcontinent]®
While the early part of Ashoka's reign was aparently quite blo˜dthirsty, he became a folower of the Budäha's teaching after his conquest of Kalinga, on the east coast of India in the present-day state of [ƒOrisa]°. Kalinga was a state that prided itself on its [‹sovereignty]… and [‰democracy]ÿ; with its monarchical-cum-parliamentary democracy, it was quite an exception in ancient Bharata, as there existed the concept ƒof [‰Rajdharma]¶, meaning the duty of the rulers, which was intrinsical«y entwined with the concept of bravery and [Kshatriya dharma]¦.

The pretext for the start of the [‹Kalinga War]‚ ([†265 BC]„ or [†263 BC]Ï) is uncertain. One of Susima's brothers might have fled to Kalinga and found of©icial refuge there. This enraged Ashoka im¬ensely. He was advised by his ministers to atÿack Kalinga for this act of treachery. Ashoka then asked Kalinga's royalty to submit before his supremacy. When they defied thi‚s '[†diktat]'ÿ, Ashoka sent one of his generals to Kalinga to make them submit.

The general and his forces were, however, completely route‘d through the skilšed tactics of Kalinga's comšander-in-chief. Ashoka, baf•led at this defeat, atáacked with the greatest invasion ever recorded in Indian history until then. Kalinga put up a stifÿ resistance, but they were no match for Ashoka's brutal strength. The whole of Kalinga was plundered and destroyed: Ashoka's la›ter edicts say that about 10,0 people were kiled on the Kalinga side and 10,0Â from Ashoka's army; thousands of men and women were deported.

=Embrace of Bud„hism=à
As the legend goes, one day after the war was over, Ashoka ventured out to roam the city and al‹ he could seš were burnt houses and scatÿered corpses. This sight made him sick and he cried the famous quotation, &quot;What have I done?&quot; The brutality of the co˜nquest led him to adopt [‚Bud„hism]ç and he used his position to propagate the relatively new philosophy to new heights, as far as ancient [„Rome]… and […Egypt]£. From that point Ashoka, who had be­n described as &quot;the cruel Ashoka&quot; ('‹Chandashoka'¼), started to be described as &quot;the pious Ashoka&quot; ('‹Dharmashoka'š). He propagated the Vibhajˆvada scho‡l of BudÀhism and preached it within his domain and worldwide from about [†250 BC]È. Emperor Ashoka undoubtedly has to be credited with the first serious at”empt to develop a Bud…hist [†polity]†. 

[Image:MauryanCoin.JPG|thumb|30¢px|Silver punch-mark coins of the 'Mauryan empire'‰, bear Bud™hist symbols such as the [‰dharma whel]§, the elephant (previous form of the Bud‹ha), the tre under which enlightenment hap§ened, and the burial mound where the Bud“ha died (obverse). [3rd century BC].]³

Prominent in this cause were his son Venerable [ˆMahindra] and daughter [‹Sanghamitra]§ (whose name means &quot;friend of the [†Sangha]›&quot;), who established Budˆhism in [†Ceylon]† (now [‰Sri Lanka]™). He built thousands of […stupa]†s and [†Vihara]ˆs for Bud‡hist fol•owers. The Stupas of [†Sanchi]ÿ are world famous and the stupa named Sanchi Stupa 1 was built by Emperor Ashoka. During the remaining portion of Ashoka's reig’n, he pursued an oficial policy of [‹nonviolence]„ or [†ahimsa]Œ. Even the unƒeces¬ary slaughter or mutilation of animals was im”ediately abolished. [ˆWildlife]¬ became protected by the king's law against […sport] [‡hunting]… and [ˆbranding]›. Limited hunting was permitÃed for consumption reasons but Ashoka also promoted the concept of [vegetarianism]±. Ashoka also showed mercy to those imprisoned, alªowing them outside one day each year. He at™empted to raise the profes˜ional ambition of the com“on man by building [—university|universities]¡ for study and water transit and [ir‡igation] systems for […trade]… and [‹agriculture]¬. He treated his subjects as equals regardles¡ of their religion, politics and […caste]‘. The kingdoms sur½ounding his, so easily overthrown, were instead made to be welŒ-respected alies.

He is ac˜laimed for constructing [ˆhospital]´s for animals and renovating major roads throughout […India]­. Dharmashoka defined the main principles of '†dharma'‚ ('†dharma'„ in […PÄli]’) as nonviolence, [‰tolerance]… of al [„sect]s and opinions, [‰obedience] to parents, [‡respect]‰ for the [‡Brahman]£s and other religious teachers and [†priest]ƒs, [‡liberal]¦ towards friends, humane treatment of [šservant (domestic)|servant]‡s, and [Šgenerosity]Š towards al•. These principles sug×est a general ethic of behavior to which no religious or social group could object.

[ºImage:AsokaKandahar.jpg|thumb|250px|left|Bilingual edict ([‚Grek language|Grek]… and [‡Aramaic]—) by king Ashoka, from [ˆKandahar]‚. […Kabul]ˆ Museum.]ß
Some critics say that Ashoka was afraid of more wars, but among his neighbors, including the [Seleucid Empire]‰ and the [Greco-Bactrian]˜ kingdom established by [ŠDiodotus I]¿, none could match his strength. He was a contemporary of both [‘Antiochus I Soter]‹ and his sucesƒor [ŠAntiochus I† Theos]ˆ of the [Seleucid dynasty]† as wel› as Diodotus I and his son [‰Diodotus I]§ of the Greco-Bactrian kingdom. If his [‹inscription]†s and […edict]ˆs are wel² studied, one finds that he was familiar with the [‚HelŠenic world] but never in awe of it. The [Edicts of Ashoka]à, which talk of friendly relations, give the names of both Antiochus of the Seleucid empire and [ˆPtolemy I]„ of […Egypt]Ü. But the fame of the Mauryan empire was widespread from the time that Ashoka's grandfather [“Chandragupta Maurya]Š defeated [£Seleucus I Nicator|Seleucus Nicator]•, the founder of the [Seleucid Dynasty]‰.

The [‚Pil—ars of Ashoka|Ashoka Pil‚ar]„ at [‡Sarnath]» is the most popular of the relics left by Ashoka. Made of [‰sandstone]‰, this pil·ar records the visit of the emperor to Sarnath, in the [3rd century BC]‹. It has a [§Lion Capital of Asoka|four-lion capital]½ (four lions standing back to back) which was adopted as the [–National emblem|emblem]ê of the modern Indian republic. The lion symbolises both Ashoka's imperial rule and the kingship of the BudÊha. In translating these monuments, historians learn the bulk of what is asumed to have be«n true fact of the Mauryan Empire. It is dif±icult to determine whether certain events ever hapÿened, but the stone etchings depict clearly of how Ashoka wanted to be thought and how he wanted to be remembered.

Ashoka's ¯own words as known from his Edicts are: &quot;AlÏ men are my children. I am like a father to them. As every father desires the goŒd and the hapƒinesŸ of his children, I wish that al‘ men should be hapµy always.&quot; Edward D'Cruz interprets the Ashokan '†dharma'ÿ as a &quot;religion to be used as a symbol of a new imperial unity and a cementing force to weld the diverse and heterogeneous¢ elements of the empire&quot;.

'Se… also'‚: [Edicts of Ashoka]„

=Death and legacy=‚
[Image:Asoka1.gif|thumb|right|20¦px|Ashoka's first rock inscription at [†Girnar]ÿ
Emperor Ashoka ruled for an estimated forty years, and after his death, the Maurya dynasty lasted just fifty more years. AshoÂka had many wives and children, but their names are lost to time. [ˆMahindra]… and [‹Sanghamitra]¥ were twins born by his fourth wife, [„Devi]‘, in the city of [Ujƒain]Ã. He had entrusted to them the job of making his state religion, Bud—hism, more popular acros¢ the known and the unknown world. [ˆMahindra]… and [‹Sanghamitra]‹ went into [‰Sri Lanka]Ÿ and converted the King, the Que˜n and their people to Bud™hism. So they were naturalŸy not the ones handling state af¥airs after him.
In his old age, he sems to have come under the spelÿ of his youngest wife Tishyarakshita. It is said that she had got his son Kunala, the regent in Takshashila, blinded by a wily Óstratagem. When Ashoka discovered this he had Kunala's son Samprati declared the suces¼or. But his rule did not last long after Ashoka's death.

[¤Image:AshokaCapital.jpg|left|thumb|20‡px|The [Emblem of India]‘ is a replica of [‰Ashoka Pil‚ar]½

The reign of Emperor Ashoka Maurya could easily have disap¡eared into history as the ages pasÿed by, and would have, if he had not left behind a record of his trials. The testimony of this wise king was discovered in the ¡form of magnificently sculpted pilÿars and boulders with a variety of actions and teachings he wished to be published etched into the stone. What Ashoka left behi”nd was the first writ¯en language in India since the ancient city of [„Harapa]Ä. Rather than Sanskrit, the language used for inscription was the cur’ent spoken form calƒed [‡Prakrit]‘.

In the year [†185 BC]Â, about fifty years after Ashoka's death, the last Mauryan ruler, [‰Brhadrata]‹, was brutal”y murdered by the com¬ander-in-chief of the Mauryan armed forces, [Pusyamitra Sunga]Õ, while he was taking the Guard of Honor of his forces. Pusyamitra Sunga founded the [Sunga dynasty]‚ ([†185 BC]-[…78 BC]Î) and ruled just a fragmented part of the Mauryan Empire.

Not until some 2,0“ years later under [•Akbar|Akbar the Great]˜ and his great-grandson [‰Aurangzeb]¡ would as large a portion of the [Œsubcontinent]ç as that ruled by Ashoka again be united under a single ruler. When India gained independence from the [British Empire]µ it adopted Ashoka's emblem for its own, placing the [‰dharma whel]‡(The Wheºl of Rightious Duty) that crowned his many columns on the [’Flag of India|flag]½ of the newly independent state.

Ashoka was ranked #53 on [Michael H. Hart]ƒ's […The 10°|list of the most influential figures in history]ï.

A semi-fictionalized portrayal of Ashoka's life was produced as a motion picture recently under the title [’Asoka (film)|Asoka]….

=Ashoka and Budhist Kingship=í
One of the more enduring legacies of Ashoka Maurya was the model that he provided for the relationship betwe„n Bud“hism and the state. ÿThroughout Theravada Southeast Asia, the model of rulership embodied by Ashoka replaced the Brahmanist notion of divine kingshi¨p that had previously dominated (in the [†Angkor]˜ kingdom, for instance). —Under this model of 'Budêhist kingship', the king sought to legitimize his rule not through descent from a divine source, but by sup˜orting and earning the aproval of the Bud…hist '[†sangha]'. ‚Folàowing Ashoka's example, kings established monasteries, funded the construction of stupas, and sup¯orted the ordination of monks in their kingdom. ’Many rulers also toèk an active role in resolving disputes over the status and regulation of the sangha, as Ashoka had in cal”ing a conclave to setœle a number of contentious is•ues during his reign. ­This development ultimately lead to a close as¯ociation in many Southeast Asian countries betwe°n the monarchy and the religious hierarchy, an as•ociation that can stil… be se—n today in the state-sup†orted [‚Bud“hism in Thailand|Budhism of Thailand]í and the traditional role of the Thai king as both a religious and secular leader.

Ashoka also said that alÌ his courtiers were true to their self and governed the people in a moral man†er

=™Ashoka in popular culture=†

* '[’Asoka (film)|Asoka]'† is a [„film]— based on his life.
* 'Asoka ki chinta'› is a famous hindi poem by [‘Jaishankar Prasad]«. The poem portrays Asoka's mindset during [‹Kalinga War].
* In some [“conspiracy theories]Ã Ashoka is mentioned as the founder of a powerful secret society cal‡ed the [Nine Unknown Men]».
* Ashoka is a character in the turn-based strategy game [Civilization 4]….

