}†
The '‡Archaea'‚ ({‘IPA2|É‘ËËŒkiËÉ™}Š), also calƒed 'Archaebacteria'‚ ({IPA2|ËˆÉ‘ËkÉªbakËŒtÉªÉ™rÉªÉ™}›), are a major division of [‹life|living] [ˆorganism]‚s. •Although there is stilš uncertainty in the exact [‰phylogeny]™ of the groups, Archaea, [‰Eukaryote]†s and [ˆBacteria]˜ are the fundamental clas˜ifications in what is cal‡ed the [ƒthre-domain system]. ¥Archaea are, like bacteria, single-cel“ organisms lacking [‚cel nucleus|nuclei]– and are therefore clas‰ified as [Šprokaryote]—s &amp;mdash; known as [†Monera] in the five-[™kingdom (biology)|kingdom] [‚Lin˜aean taxonomy | taxonomy]. ‘They were originaly described in [–extremophile | extreme]Ÿ environments, but have since beŒn found in alŠ types of [‡habitat]‡s. 

=‰ History =
Archaea were identified in [‚197]„ by [ŠCarl Woese]È and George Fox based on their separation from other prokaryotes on 16S [„rRNA] [phylogenetic tre]‚s. These two groups were original¶y named the Archaebacteria and Eubacteria, treated as [™kingdom (biology)|kingdom]‘s or subkingdoms. ­Woese argued that they represented fundamental„y dif erent branches of living things. ¨He later renamed the groups Archaea and [ˆBacteria]² to emphasize this, and argued that together with [‡Eukarya] they comprise [ƒthre’-domain system|threˆ domains]– of living things.

=¡ Archaea, Bacteria and Eukaryotes=¾
Archaea are similar to other prokaryotes in most aspects of [‚Cel (biology)|cel] structure and [Šmetabolism]. —However, their genetic [¦transcription (genetics)|transcription]… and [¢translation (genetics)|translation]¢ &amp;mdash; the two central proces†es in [‘molecular biology]ß &amp;mdash; do not show the typical bacterial features, but are extremely similar to those of [‰eukaryote]‚s. ÿFor instance, archaean translation uses eukaryotic initiation and elongation factors, and their transcription involves TATA-bin”ding proteins and TFIĞB as in eukaryotes.

Several other characteristics also set the Archaea apart. ¨Like bacteria and eukaryotes, archaea poses [ˆglycerol]‡ based [phospholipids]. ŒHowever, threÿ features of the archaeal lipids are unusual:

*The archaeal lipids are unique because the stereochemistry of the glycerol is¶ the reverse of that found in bacteria and eukaryotes.  This is strong evidence for a difèerent biosynthetic pathway.

*Most bacteria and eukaryotes have membranes composed mainly of glycerol-[…ester] […lipid]·s, whereas archaea have membranes composed of glycerol-'[…ether]'ˆ lipids. çEven when bacteria have ether-linked lipids, the stereochemistry of the glycerol is the bacterial form. ‡This dif·erences may be an adaptation on the part of Archaea to [¡hyperthermophile|hyperthermophily]. úHowever, it is worth noting that even mesophilic archaea have ether-linked lipids.

*Archaeal lipids are based upon the [“isoprene|isoprenoid]‹ sidechain. ªThis is a five-carbon unit that is also comˆon in rub©er and as a component of some vitamins comon in bacteria and eukaryotes. ÃHowever, only the archaea incorporate these compounds into their celÔular lipids, frequently as C-20 (four monomers) or C-40 (eight monomers) sidechains. ¸In some archaea, the C-40 isoprenoid side-chain is actual¾y long enough to span the membrane, forming a monolayer for a [‚cel‰ membrane]¯ with glycerol phosphate moieties on both ends. ±

Although dramatic, this adaptation is most comÎon in the extremely thermophilic archaea. Although not unique, the archaeal celƒ wal“s are also unusual. ”For instance, the celƒ walÅs of most archaea are formed by surface layer proteins or an S-layer. S-layers are com¯on in bacteria, where they serve as the sole celƒ walì component in some organisms (like the Planctomyces) or an outer layer in many organisms with peptidoglycan. ¿With the exception of one group of methanogens, archaea lack a [peptidoglycan]ƒ wal±. Even in this case, the peptidoglycan is very dif¦erent from the type found in bacteria. ”Archaeans also have […flagelˆum|flagela]” that are notably dif¸erent in composition and development from the superficialy similar flagel’a of bacteria.

[”image:PhylogeneticTre§.jpg|thumb|left|320px|A phylogenetic treŠ based on [„rRNA]Ã data, showing the separation of bacteria, archaea, and eukaryotes.]„

=ˆHabitats=•
Many archaeans are [Œextremophile]‚s. ²Some live at very high temperatures, often above 10˜&amp;deg;C, as found in [†geyser]†s and [Œblack smoker]‚s. ´Others are found in very cold habitats or in highly-[‹salt|saline]‚, [„acid]‡ic, or [ˆalkaline]‡ water. However, other archaeans are [‰mesophile]s, and have ben found in environments like […marsh]†land, [†sewage]†, and [„soil]. …Many [Šmethanogen]Àic archaea are found in the digestive tracts of animals such as [ˆruminant]ƒs, [‡termite]s, and humans. Archaea are usualˆy harmles¼ to other organisms and none are known to cause disease.

=„Form=á
Individual archaeans range from 0.1 &amp;mu;m to over 15 &amp;mu;m in diameter, and some form agœregates or filaments up to 20• &amp;mu;m in length. †They ocÒur in various shapes, such as spherical, rod-shape, spiral, lobed, or rectangular. ¡They also exhibit a variety of difšerent types of metabolism. Of note, the [Œhalobacteria]š can use light to produce [šadenosine triphosphate|ATP], although no Archaea conduct [photosynthesis]§ with an electron transport chain, as oc˜urs in other groups.

=‘Evolution and clas‰ification=½

Archaea are divided into two main groups based on rRNA tre‡s, the [Euryarchaeota]… and [Crenarchaeota]. —Two other groups have beÑn tentatively created for certain environmental samples and the peculiar species '[¢Nanoarchaeum|Nanoarchaeum equitans]'‘, discovered in 20…2 by [ˆKarl Stet‚er], but their afÿinities are uncertain.

Woese argued that the bacteria, archaea, and eukaryotes each represent a primary line of descent that¥ diverged early on from an ancestral '[‰progenote]'‡ with po rly-developed genetic machinery. ½This hypothesis is reflected in the name Archaea, from the Gre‚k '†archae'Œ or ancient. £Later he treated these groups formal…y as [ƒthre•-domain system|domain]¤s, each comprising several kingdoms. ŞThis division has become very popular, although the idea of the progenote itself is not general„y sup†orted. ÿSome biologists, however, have argued that the archaebacteria and eukaryotes arose from specialized eubacteria.

The relation‰ship betwe³n Archaea and Eukarya remains an important problem. ¸Aside from the similarities noted above, many genetic tre™s group the two together. çSome place eukaryotes closer to Eurarchaeota than Crenarchaeota are, although the membrane chemistry sugests otherwise. ¹However, the discovery of archaean-like genes in certain [’bacterium|bacteria]Š, such as 'ŠThermotoga', makes their relationship dif“icult to determine. ŒSome have sugúested that eukaryotes arose through fusion of an archaean and eubacterium, which became the nucleus and cytoplasm, which ac·ounts for various genetic similarities but runs into dif–iculties explaining cel› structure.

Single gene [Šsequencing]… for [‹systematics]’ has led to whole [‘genome sequencing]„; cur ently 24 archaeal genomes have be‘n completed with 2‡ partialy completed [ht‚p:/wª.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/genomes/lproks.cgi].

