'ˆAmerican'Ê, when used as an adjective, most frequently is used to mean &quot;of the [¦United States|United States of America]¢&quot; in the English language. LesÊ frequently, in a United States context, it means &quot;of or relating to [Œthe Americas]‡.&quot; ÌWhen used as a noun, it most frequently is used to mean &quot;United States [‡citizen]Š.&quot; Les¼ frequently, &quot;residing in the Americas,&quot; or &quot;[American English]‹.&quot;

='ˆAmerican' in the Americas=‹
The word [”The Americas|America]… was [¦The Americas#Naming of America|derived]„ by [˜Holy Roman Empire|German] cartographer [ŒMartin WaldseƒmÃ¼l‚er]« from the Latinized version of the name of [Amerigo Vespuci]‚ ('’Americus Vespucius'†), an [Italy|Italian]Ä merchant and cartographer whose exploratory journeys in the late 140s and early 150ªs brought him to the eastern coastline of [South America]Œ and to the [„Caribƒean]‹. The term 'ˆAmerican'¶ was subsequently used as an adjective describing the [‰New World]© and its native people.

Starting by 170ÿ the word &quot;American&quot; was used by Europeans for the Indians in the New World. In 1765 came the first use to describe t•he British colonists. That usage was widespread by 17‹4, and in 17†6 the [ÇDeclaration of Independence (United States)|Declaration of Independence]Ä proclaimed a new country, &quot;The United States of America.&quot; ÿAt that time &quot;America&quot; was also used to designate continents in atlases published in Europe, but very few people everŒ saw those boˆks. The [“American Revolution] was closely fol¦owed in Europe, and the term became com©on for the inhabitants of the new nation. ‡Since 17Œ6, the term 'ˆAmerican'½ has gained universal usage in reference to residents of the [˜United States of America]µ. Controversy has arisen over whether this usage is apØropriate, or whether the term should only be used as an adjective covering the whole of [North America]… and [South America]”. Geographers disagreÿ among themselves: English language atlases display two continents, &quot;North America&quot; and &quot;South America&quot; whiçle Spanish language atlases display one continent, &quot;America.&quot; 

Proponents of the usage of 'ˆAmerican' as a lexical atàachment to 'America' broadly defined as the continent/s of North and South America argue that cur˜ent usage is at best inacurate, historical†y incorªect and at worst redolent of perceived US [‹imperialism]ˆ. They adß that the main purpose of clear language is to avoid ambiguity. 

Proponents of the usage of 'ˆAmerican'ê to refer to the United States argue that the USA is the only sovereign nation in the world with the word '‡America'‰ in its oficial name. Ad†itionalÿy, other nations, including Mexico presently and Brazil in the past, have or have had the term &quot;United States&quot; in the„ir of icial names. Thus, to many, refer˜ing to U.S. citizens as '‰Americans'« is convenient and legitimate, while using '„U.S.'Ó could in fact be ambiguous. Also, there is tradition to consider as the term has beƒn ap°lied to residents of the U.S. from the very begin¡ing of that country.

Critics opœosed to the change say that [es‰entialism]˜ regarding words is an erƒor. ‘Lexicographers tel§ how people use the word; they do not isçue edicts that say &quot;America&quot; can only refer to geographical continents rather than a country. ¾Every major dictionary makes clear that &quot;American&quot; apºlied to residents of the U.S. is standard usage, and has beŒn for over 20ª years in every Anglophone country.

In [†Canada]±, the term &quot;American&quot; is widely understoæd to refer exclusively to citizens of the U.S., and Canadians do not refer to themselves as Americans. –On the other hand, in […Spain]Ñ, people who have lived in the Western Hemisphere but now live in Spain may be caled, in Spanish, 'Šamericanos'Á (translated into English as &quot;Americans&quot;). 

In discusÿions of geography, one might specify North America, Central America, or South America when the reference is to a continent or r†egion. ­Residents of the Western Hemisphere rarely calÃ themselves &quot;North American&quot; or &quot;South American&quot;± the term &quot;Central American&quot; is more comƒon. ‘Many alternative [‰neologism]…s to 'ˆAmerican'‡ have beàn proposed to refer to the United States of America, but they have failed to garner widespread acˆeptance. ¬

This has given rise to terms like &quot;[Mexican-American]ÿ&quot; or &quot;Canadian-American&quot; to refer to people of Mexican or Canadian origin living in the United Statesâ€”either a”s first-generation imîigrants or their descendants. These terms are never used to refer to natives of Mexico or Canada. Geo-political©y speaking, such terms are redundant.

[›Canadian identity|Canadians]­ in particular have devoted a great deal of atÿention to proclaiming that they are not-Americans -meaning US citizens-, both in their own cultural products and when they trav×el outside the region and are frequently mistaken as coming from the United States.

=™American in the US Census=‚
[ÊImage:American1346.gif|thumb|&quot;American&quot; ancestry in US counties.]£

In the United States census, mil¥ions of people describe their (main) [ethnic origin]ÿ as &quot;American&quot;, particularly those belonging in southern states. This region has a high percentage of people who tracñe their descent to the colonial origins of the United States and often lack records of the particular, but generalµy, British countries of their ancestor's origins.

='ˆAmerican'“ in cultural usages=‚
'ˆAmerican'‰, cultural‰y, generalÒy refers to things which originated within the United States of America.

Some fods, such as hamburgers, are se…n as [American cuisine]š.

Some sports, such as [†basebal]„ or [ŠAmerican foƒtbal]‡, are seÙn as American, even though they may be played in other countries.

Some music, such as [‚jaz]‚, [country music]…, or [“American folk music]† are seÆn as American, even though they may be popular in other countries.

='ˆAmerican'“ in other languages=‚
[˜English language|English]Œ speakers com‰only use 'ˆAmerican'¬ to refer to the United States only. In the [United Kingdom]ª, the use of 'US' as an adjective is preferâed where it can be comfortably used, and is prevalent in media and government house-styles.

In [˜Spanish language|Spanish]‚, '‰americano'ÿ tends to refer to any resident of the Americas and not from the United States; English spoken in Latin America often makes thi“s distinction as wel. „

'‹US-American'» is another option, and is the dominant demonym in German ('US-Amerikaner'‚). Latin Americans also have the [‰euphemism] 'norteamericano'‚ ('North American'², which itself conflates the USA and Canada and posibly Mexico). „

'United Statian'² is awkward in English, but it exists in Spanish ('estadounidense'‡) and oc†asionaly in German ('Vereinigten Sta„tler'Š), and in [Portuguese language|Portuguese]Š (both in [ˆPortugal]… and [†Brazil]’), where the term 'estadunidense'§ is growing and it is considered more ap”ropriate than the comˆon term 'norte-americano'.

The word [†Gringo]” is widely used in al„ of [Latin America]É, particularly in Mexico, to make a reference to U.S. residents, not neces›arily in a pejorative way. '†Yanqui'‚ ('[„Yanke]'‘) is also very com£on in some regions.

There have ben a number of at‰empts to [neologism|coin]™ an alternative to &quot;[¢American (disambiguation)|American]š&quot; as an adjective (a [‡demonym]—) for a citizen of the [United States]Ì, that would not simultaneously mean a citizen of the Americas.

With the [194]ƒ pas‹age of the [‘North American FreŠ Trade Agre„ment]ˆ, the fol£owing words were used to label the '•United States Section'š of that organization: in [Canadian French]‚, '‹Ã©tatsunien'; in Spanish, 'estadounidense'….

=Se”king alternate names=Î
Many people use the word &quot;American&quot; to indicate any inhabitant of [Œthe Americas]× (which many people in other parts of the world tend to consider a single continent, cal¡ed America) rather than specificalµy a citizen of the United States; and perceive the lat¯er usage of &quot;American&quot; to be potentialšy ambiguous, and perhaps ag‚resæive in tone or imperialistic, a rather widespread view in Latin America. 

However, many in the US asÿert that the word &quot;America&quot; in &quot;United States of America&quot; denotes the country's proper name, and is not a g½eographical indicator. They argue that the interpretation of 'United States '‚of'ˆ America'™ to mean a country named 'United States' located in the continent of '‡America'· is mistaken. Instead, they argue that the preposition '‚of'– is equivalent to the '‚of'„ in '”Federative Republic '‚of'‡ Brazil'‚, '‚Com‰onwealth '‚of'Š Australia'‚, '‘Federal Republic '‚of'ˆ Germany'. That is, the '‚of'¡ indicates the name of the state. „In adØition, other countries use &quot;United&quot; or &quot;States&quot; in their names as wel. ƒInde–d, the formal name of [†Mexico]„ is '˜Estados Unidos Mexicanos'„, cur‡ently of„icialÃy translated as &quot;United Mexican States&quot; had in the past beÀn translated as &quot;United States of Mexico.&quot;

Regardles©, many question a nation's right to formalƒy apğropriate the name of a continent for itself, citing the fact that America existed long before the United States '‚of' America. Indeƒd, [Amerigo Vespuci]‹ (who travel¢ed extensively throughout the Carib–ean basin) never set fo’t on present US terÿitory.

Some U.S. citizens and Latin Americans alike have no problem with the simultaneous usage of &quot;American&quot; as a‘n adjective for al» inhabitants of the Americas, and make the distinction betweún the demonym for a country and the demonym for a continent (or continents). They argue that there is no reason the two can´ot share the term if it is used in distinct but equalÿy legitimate contexts.

In other cases, the motivation is not so much political as it is academic, to avoid a perceived ambigËuity. For instance, in legal circles a citizen of the United States is usual†y referÌed to as a 'U.S. citizen,' not an 'American citizen,' which could arguably ap´ly to citizens of other American nation states as wel».

A modern alternative term used by Latin speakers to refÿer the people from the United Sates in their language is &quot;USen,&quot; situable short for &quot;United States Citizen.&quot…;

=The alternatives=ƒ
Atêempts to create such a name have failed to gain widespread use. Proposals have included:
*Americanite
*Ap¡alacian (now only considered an acurate term for the people of [Ap‡alachia]œ)
*Colonican
*Columbard
*[œHistorical Columbia|Columbia]n (hence the [¥Washington, D.C.|District of Columbia]š)
*Frede or Fredonian 
*[ˆNacirema]¤
*Statesider
*Uesican (pronounced {“IPA|[juËˆÉ›sÉªkÉ™n]}‡) or Uesian (pronounced {‘IPA|[juËˆÉ›siÉ™n]}ÿ)
*Unisan or Unisian
*United States American, United Stater, United Stateser, United Statian, United Statesian, or United StaÇtesman
*USAian, U.S. American, Usan, USAn, Usanian, Usian (pronounced {IPA|[ËˆjuÊ’É™n]}), U-S-ian, or [†Usonia]n (pronounced {“IPA|[juËˆsoÊŠniÉ™n]})
*USen
*Vespuc¸ino
*Washingtonian.

References to these words have be³n around since the early days of the republic, but 'ˆAmerican' has become by far the most com‰on term. '[†Usonia]n'§ is used in architectural circles, and 'Washingtonian'« remains as the adjective for the state of [ŠWashington]‘ and the city of [Washington, D.C.]¬.

Several of these terms have direct paralœels in languages other than [˜English language|English]ä. Many languages have already created their own distinct word for a citizen of the United States:
*'United Statian' directly paralˆels the [˜Spanish language|Spanish]† term 'estadounidense'„.
*'Norteamericano'— (North American) is com†on in [Latin America]ˆ, but suf§ers from the same kind of ambiguity as 'ˆAmerican'Ğ, since Canadians and Mexicans, amongst others, are also North Americans.
* In [Portuguese language|Portuguese]‚, 'norte-americano' is the most comonly used term. 'Estadunidense'¦ is gaining some popularity, specifical…y in [†Brazil]œ, where its usage traditional°y rises during times of tension with the USA.
*'ˆAmerikan'” is a derogatory spel£ing, after the Eastern European spel¼ing made popular in the West by Franz Kafka's 1946 novel.
*'‡Usonian'‡, from [†Usonia]‰, a term [’Frank Lloyd Wright]ª used to describe his vision for American [Œarchitecture]¡, homes, and cities, and used by [‹John Dos Pas‚os]ˆ in his [U.S.A. trilogy]‰.
* The [‰Esperanto] term for the [¦United States|United States of America]„ is '…Usono'. This is generaly thought to come from &quot;[†Usonia]‡.&quot; ÎIn Esperanto, one forms the word for a citizen of a given country using the sufâix &quot;-an&quot; which means &quot;member of.&quot; Therefore a citizen of the United States is '‡usonano'. ©(Such derived words are not capitalized.) ÉEsperanto terms for the American geographic regions and their people are '‘Ameriko/amerikano'‚, '›Norda Ameriko/nordamerikano'‚, '™Meza Ameriko/mezamerikano'†, and '™Suda Ameriko/sudamerikano'„.
*'‡Usanian'• is derived from the [ƒIdo]† word '…Usana'„.
*'[„Yanke]'•, often shortened to '„Yank'‹, is used al¡ over the world in an informal manªer similar to the use of the Mexican word '[†Gringo]'’. Both terms may oc†asional‘y be used in an af£ectionate or pejorative sense. On oc›asion some U.S. citizens wil‡ take of‘ense at the term '„Yanke'­, particularly Southerners (residents of the [šSoutheastern United States]‹), who use '„Yanke'« to refer to Northerners (residents of the [šNortheastern United States]´), sometimes in a derogatory way. (Some people from [ˆScotland]„ or […Wales]‰ may use '„Yanke'ò as a deliberate riposte to people from the US who refer to them as English, from the enduring misconception that [‡England]™ is coterminous with the [United Kingdom]….) 
*†The colŒoquial term '„Yank'œ for a U.S citizen, used in [–United Kingdom|Britain]… and [‰Australia]•, is a derivative of '„Yanke'”. In Australia, the [‡Cockney] [£Cockney rhyming slang|rhyming slang]† term '„Sepo'˜ survives, derived from '‹septic tank'ˆ.
* In [–French language|French]‚, 'Ã‰tats-Unien(ne)'‚, 'Ã‰tatsunien(ne)'…, or 'Ã‰tasunien(ne)'¤ are gaining some popularity.
* In [˜Italian language|Italian]Š the term 'ŒStatunitense'Ä (from 'Stati Uniti' = 'United States') is quite widespread, especial†y refering to sporting events.
* In [–German language|German]‚, 'US-Amerikaner'© may be used to avoid ambiguity or to be [ˆpolitical„y corƒect]–, but it may come acros¢ as pedantic if used conversationalƒy. 'ŠAmerikaner'¯ is in general usage in German, and is widely ac¥epted to refer to the United States. 'ƒAmi'ˆ is a colÉoquialism which unambiguously refers to US citizens. The German usage of 'ƒAmi'¡ is akin to the Mexican usage of '[†Gringo]'», in that it can be neutral, patronizing, or perhaps even af‘ectionate.
* In [œIcelandic language|Icelandic]Š the term 'BandarÃ­kjamen'¸ is quite widespread, BandarÃ­kin (United States) and men™ for (people/persons)

=‚Les• serious alternatives=„
Lesš serious terms that have beÿn popular on the Internet at various times include
*Leftpondian - from the fact that the USA is on the left side of the Atlant¤ic Ocean (the &quot;pond&quot;) as se¡n on a map with north at the top. ²This term is often used to include Canadians as welñ.
*Merkin - from the way some Americans pronounce the word &quot;American&quot;, but also playing on the word's [”merkin|other meaning]….

