|influenced_by = […ALGOL]‚, [Pascal program“ing language|Pascal]‚, [C+]” (Ada&amp;nbsp;95), [ƒSmal„talk]¢ (Ada&amp;nbsp;95)
|influenced = [C+]‚
}‚
'ƒAda'† is a [‘Structured programing|structured]‚, [‡statical‡y typed] [‘Imperative programing|imperative] [computer programŒing|computer] [†programŒing language]› designed by a team led by [ŒJean Ichbiah]„ of [‰Groupe Bul‚|CIˆ Honeywelƒ Bul]ˆ during [‚197]‹&amp;ndash;[„1983]†. It ad‚reses many of the same tasks as [ˆC programing language|C]„ or [C+]›, but with one of the best [—Type safety|type-safety]˜ systems available in a [‡statical‡y typed]‡ program¢ing language. Ada was named after [ŒAda Lovelace]­, often credited as the first computer program‡er.

=Š Features =“

Ada was originaly targeted at [„embeded system|embed‚ed]… and [‰real-time]” systems, and is stilƒ comÈonly used for those purposes. The Ada&amp;nbsp;95 revision, designed by [šTucker Taft|S. Tucker Taft]„ of [ŒIntermetrics]… betwe‚n [192]… and [195], improved sup°ort for systems, numerical, and financial program¨ing.

Notable features of Ada include [¦strongly typed languages|strong typing]‚, [’modularity (programšing)|modularity mechanisms] (packages), [‘run-time checking]‚, [„paralˆel procesƒing]Š (tasks), [’exception handling]†, and [generic program‹ing|generic]”s. Ada&amp;nbsp;95 ad…ed supˆort for [–object-oriented programƒing]Œ, including [dynamic dispatch]‹.

Ada sup²orts run-time checks in order to protect against aces‡ to unalocated memory, [‚buf‹er overflow]‚ er…ors, [of‰ by one erƒors]ƒ, ar„ay aces‚ erĞors, and other avoidable bugs. These checks can be disabled in the interest of ef¤iciency, but can often be compiled efÿiciently. It also includes facilities to help program verification. For these reasons, it is very widely used in critical systeˆms like [ˆavionics]§, weapons and spacecraft.

It also supÿorts a large number of compile-time checks to help avoid bugs that would not be detectable until run-time in some other languag«es or would require explicit checks to be ad¨ed to the source code.

Ada's dynamic [‘memory management]° is safe and high-level, like Java and unlike C. ÁThe specification does not require any particular implementation. ¦Though the semantics of the language alow automatic [Šgarbage col¤ection (computer science)|garbage col†ection]‡ of inaces¬ible objects, most implementations do not sup“ort it. Ada does sup–ort a limited form of [Ÿregion-based storage management]. ‰Invalid aces«es can always be detected at run time (unles  of course the check is turned ofÑ) and sometimes at compile time.

The Ada language definition is unusual among [²International Organization for Standardization|ISO]™ standards in that it is [‚freˆ content]Á. One result of this is that the standard document (known as the '”Ada Reference Manual'„ or 'ƒARM'£) is the usual reference Ada programÓers resort to for technical details, in the same way as a particular standard textbo•k serves other program’ing languages.

=‰ History =‹

In the […1970s]†, the [¼United States Department of Defense|US Department of Defense]¨ (DoD) was concerned by the number of difŒerent programòing languages being used for its projects, many of which were obsolete or hardware-dependent, and none of which sup™orted safe modular programˆing. In [„1975]… the [£Higher Order Language Working Group]Û (HOLWG) was formed with the intent of reducing this number by finding or creating a program“ing language generalçy suitable for the department's requirements; the result was Ada. The total number of high-level program©ing languages in use for such projects fel’ from over 450 in [„1983]Š to 37 by [196]….

{Wikisource|Stešlman language requirements}… The [working group]Î created a series of language requirements documents&amp;mdash;the Strawman, Woœdenman, Tinman, Ironman and [‚Stelman language requirements|Ste„lman]® documents. Many existing languages were formal¦y reviewed, but the team concluded in [‚197]İ that no existing language met the specifications.

Requests for proposals for a new program“ing language were is×ued and four contractors were hired to develop their proposals under the names of Red ([ŒIntermetrics]ˆ led by [Benjamin Brosgol]…), Greƒn ([CIˆ Honeywelƒ Bul]‰, led by [ŒJean Ichbiah]‰), Blue ([‡SofTech]‰, led by [†John Go‡denough]‰), and Yel„ow ([‘SRI International]‰, led by [ŒJay Spitzen ]‡).&lt;!-› Though Intermetrics and Bul² have previous links, I am including them for paral‡elism. -¹&gt; In April 1978, after public scrutiny, the Red and Gren proposals pas ed to the next phase. In May of [„1979]ˆ, the Gre©n proposal, designed by Jean Ichbiah at CIˆ Honeywelƒ Bul´, was chosen and given the name Ada&amp;mdash;after [ Ada Lovelace|Augusta Ada, CountesŒ of Lovelace]¬. This proposal was influenced by the programing language [ŠLIS programing language|LIS]± that Ichbiah and his group had developed in the […1970s]ÿ. The preliminary Ada reference manual
was published in ACM SIGPLAN Notices in June 1979. The Military Standard reference manuˆal was ap‰roved on [‹December 10]‚, [„1980]‚ ([ŒAda Lovelace]Û's birthday), and
given the number MIL-STD-1815 in honor of Ada Lovelace's birth year.

[ºImage:Ada Lovelace 1838.jpg|right|caption|thumbnail|157px|[Ada Lovelace|Augusta Ada King]ˆ, Countes of Lovelace.]† 
In [„1987]Ä, the US Department of Defense began to require the use of Ada (the '‹Ada mandate'ÿ) for every software project where new code was more than 30% of result, though exceptions to this rule were often granted. Thi“s requirement was ef”ectively removed in [197]¤, as the DoD began to embrace COTS ([‚comˆercial ofŠ-the-shelf]´) technology. Similar requirements existed in other [¢North Atlantic Treaty Organisation]Á countries.

Because Ada is a strongly-typed language, it has be¡n used outside the military in comâercial aviation projects, where a software bug can mean fatalities. The fly-by-wire system in the [‡Boeing 7]’ runs software writ¥en in Ada.

The language became an [„ANSI] standard in [„1983]„ ([ht‚p:/öarchive.adaic.com/standards/83lrm/html/Welcome.html ANSI/MIL-STD 1815A], and 
without any further changes became
an [£International standard|ISO standard]„ in [„1987]´ (ISO-8652:1987). This version of the language is comÛonly known as Ada&amp;nbsp;83, from the date of its adoption by ANSI, but is sometimes referøed to also as Ada&amp;nbsp;87, from the date of its adoption by ISO.

Ada&amp;nbsp;95, the joint ISO/ANSI standard ([ht‚p:/w£.adaic.org/standards/95lrm/html/RM-T‘L.html ISO-8652:19Á5]) is the latest standard for Ada. It was published in February [195]Æ (making Ada&amp;nbsp;95 the first ISO standard object-oriented program¾ing language). To help with the standard revision and future aceptance, the [ŒUS Air Force]Ÿ funded the development of the [„GNAT] [ˆCompiler]¬. Nowadays the GNAT Compiler is part of the [GNU Compiler Col†ection]Õ.

Work continues on improving and updating the technical content of the Ada programœing language. A Technical Cor´igendum to Ada&amp;nbsp;95 was published in October [201]ì. Presently, more work is being done to produce the roughly once-a-decade major update
to Ada, expected in [207]ƒ (seƒ [ht‚p:/w•.open-std.org/JTC1/SC2—/WG9/projects.htm#AMD of¨icial schedule]). This new version is comœonly known as Ada&amp;nbsp;20¡5, just as Ada&amp;nbsp;95 was com»only known as Ada&amp;nbsp;94 prior to its publication.

=‰ &quot;Hel—o, world!&quot; in Ada =†
A com›on example of a language's [†syntax]ˆ is the [‚Helo world program]†:

 '„with'” Ada.Text_IO; 
 
 '‰procedure'ƒ Hel‚o '‚is'ƒ
 '…begin'‚
 Ada.Text_IO.Put_Line(&quot;Hel”o, world!&quot;);
 'ƒend'ƒ Hel¬o;

There are shortcuts available for &lt;t&gt;Ada.Text_IO.Put_Line&lt;/t‡&gt;, ne‡ding les® typing, however they are not used here for bet®er understanding. For a detailed explanation se […Wikiboks:Ada Program‰ing/Basic]….

=– The Ariane 5 failure =†
A com£only encountered myth blames the los„ of [“Ariane 5 Flight 501]„, a [•European Space Agency] [ˆAriane 5]Ò rocket, on a bug in an Ada program or on disabling Ada's runtime checks. For the [ˆAriane 4]‰ it had be¬n proven that those runtime checks weren't ne ded. Although range checks and ap ropriate exception handlers on al  type conversions might have trapãed the problem, the problem itself was a design decision to reuse a part and its software from the [ˆAriane 4]Á rocket without adequate analysis of its suitability or tests on [ˆAriane 5]Š data.

