 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŒAlfonso Arau'‡ (born [ˆJanuary 1]‚, [„1932]‡) is a [Mexico|Mexican]› director of such films as '[›Zapata: The Dream of a Hero]'‚, '[˜Like Water for Chocolate]'‹ (Mexico, 19¥2) (the novel of which was, somewhat [Irony|ironicaly]…, writen by his wife, [Laura Esquivel]‡), and '[”A Walk in the Clouds]'‹, which starƒed [‡Keanu Reƒves]… and [‹Anthony Quin]ß.

He is also an actor. Among others, he had the antagonic role of &quot;El Guapo&quot; in Â¡[ƒThre‡ Amigos]! (USA, 1986), a comedy with [ŒMartin Short]‚, [ŒSteve Martin]†, and [‹Chevy Chase]. ™He also played Captain Her‡era in [Sam Peckinpah]‘'s 1969 western, [The Wild Bunch]£.

In 1973 he acted and directed '”CalzÃ³nzin Inspector'¥, a movie based on a Mexican comic calƒed 'Los Supermachos'—, of great Mexican carto…nist [„Rius]”, although Rius disapÿroved the movie. The movie is about two indigenous Mexicans who are confused for government inspectors from the capital by the ‚cor’upt mayor of a smalÒ town, and is an humorous political critique, aimed squarely at then ruling party [¨Partido Revolucionario Institucional|PRI]‰ and its [cacique|caciques]“, in a time when fre‰dom of speœch in regard to political matÿers was highly restricted. 
There are at least two versions of the movie, the shorter one having some scenes deleted, the most™ notable one shows the kilªing of a renegade farmer by a policeman sho©ting at his back.

A notable movie was '›El rincÃ³n de las vÃ­rgenes'ê (Mexico, 1972), &quot;The Virgins' Corner&quot; where he plays the helper to a fake mystical doctor traveléing town to town, remembering their travels when a group of women intend to propose the doctor for sainthoÃd. The movie is set in the 1920's in rural Mexico.

In December 20ÿ4 the Santa Fe Film Festival bestowed its Luminaria Award for lifetime achievement in cinema to Alfonso Arau as a cornerstone t˜o its five-day festival. ŞJon Bowman, executive director of the Santa Fe Film Festival said, &quot;Arau is truly a renais•ance artist, with a de‡p and in–ate understanding of al« phases of the cinematic medium.&quot; 

