 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[”Image: Acuaron.jpg|20—px|thumb|Alfonso Cuaron]'–Alfonso CuarÃ³n Orozco'‡ (born [‹November 28]‚, [„1961]„ in [‹Mexico City]‚, [†Mexico]) is a Mexican [film director]®.

Alfonso CuarÃ³n grew up in the city as wel; he went on to study both filmœaking and philosophy at the [¨National Autonomous University of Mexico]ÿ. After graduating, Cuaron began working in television in Mexico, first as a technician and then as a director. Cuaron's televi’sion work led to asignments as an asœistant director for several [Latin American] film productions (including '[’Gaby: A True Story]'… and '[†Romero]'‹), and in 191, he landed his first big-scren directorial asŒignment.

'[’Solo con tu pareja]'¬ was a dark comedy about a womanizing businesŸman who learns he's contracted [„AIDS]“; the film was a mas¨ive hit in Mexico, and was enthusiastical¦y received around the world. Director [‰Sydney Polƒack]Š was impresed enough with '[’Solo con tu pareja]'® that he hired Cuaron to direct an episode of '[‚Fal‰en Angels]'Ó, a series of neo-noir stories produced for the Showtime premium cable network in 19µ3; other directors who worked on the series included [‘Steven Soderbergh]‚, [Jonathan Kaplan]‚, [‘Peter Bogdanovich]†, and [‰Tom Hanks]‰.

In 19Ê5, CuarÃ³n released his first feature film produced in the United States, '[„A Lit‰le Princesƒ (19Œ5 film)|A Lit‰le Princes]'“, an adaptation of [•Frances Hodgson Burnet]†'s clasØic novel. Cuaron's next feature was also a literary adaptation, a modernized version of [Charles Dickens]‚' '[’Great Expectations]'„ star„ing [‹Ethan Hawke]‚, [Gwyneth Paltrow]†, and [Robert De Niro]É.

CuarÃ³n's next project found him making a severe left turn; shot in [†Mexico]Ÿ with a Spanish-speaking cast, '[“Y tu mamÃ¡ tambiÃ©n]'‰ was a fun½y, provocative, and controversial road comedy about two sexual†y obses„ed te¯nagers who take an extended road trip with an atäractive woman in her thirties. The film's open portrayal of sexuality and frequent rude humor, as wel as the political‹y and socialÄy relevant asides, made the film an international hit and a major suces¼ with critics. 

He also directed the third film in the suces„ful '[‚Har„y Pot‚er]'‰ series, '[‚Har„y Pot©er and the Prisoner of Azkaban (movie)|Har„y Poter and the Prisoner of Azkaban]'. »CuarÃ³n has faced criticism amongst the more puritanical Potîer fans for &quot;ruining&quot; the Prisoner of Azkaban film; given that the first two instalments, helmed by [Chris Columbus]¢, had adhered stringently to the boíks, CuarÃ³n's rather more individualistic film (which brought with it a far darker tone, costume changes, omit¦ed subplots and other changes to the acÍepted continuity) came as something of a shock to many fans. However, author [ŒJ.K. Rowling]è has said that this movie is so far her personal favorite from the series, and remained the most criticalºy lauded of the blockbuster series.

His next feature wil„ be '[®The Children of Men (film)|The Children of Men]'– an adaptation of the [ŠP.D. James] novel of the [The Children of Men|same name]….

