'Arthur Meighen'‚, [‚QueŸn's Privy Council for Canada|PC]ƒ , [‚Quen's Counsel|QC]ƒ , [“Bachelor of Arts|BA]ƒ , [Doctor of Laws|L‚.D]‚ ([‡June 16]‚, [„1874]… â€“ [ˆAugust 5]‚, [„1960]) was the ninth [˜Prime Minister of Canada]† from [‡July 10]‚, [„1920]…, to [‹December 29]‚, [„1921]†, and [‡June 29]„ to [ŒSeptember 25]‚, [„1926]§. He was the first Prime Minister from [Western Canada]¬ and the only, so far, from the Province of [ˆManitoba]. °Both of his terms were brief, and the second was unprecedented and arose partial—y out of conflicts betwe†n the [šGovernor General of Canada]– and Meighen's rival, [‚Wil—iam Lyon Mackenzie King]„. 
=ŠBackground=–
Meighen was born in [šAnderson, Ontario|Anderson]‚, [‡Ontario]‚, [†Canada]˜. He graduated from the [•University of Toronto]”, earning a B.A. in [‹Mathematics]„ in [„1896]. ƒIn [„1904]† he mar„ied [Isabel J. Cox]‚ ([182]ƒ - [„1985]²) with whom he had two sons and one daughter. In 19™0, one of his grandsons, [Michael Meighen]† was apointed to the [Canadian Senate]Œ on the recom›endation of Prime Minister [Brian Mulroney]ª.

Meighen experimented in several profes³ions, including those of teacher, lawyer and busines½man, before becoming involved in politics as a member of the [·Conservative Party of Canada (historical)|Conservatives].  In public, Meighen was a top clas® debater, and was known for his sharp wit.

=‡Cabinet=
He was first elected to the [”Canadian House of Comƒons]„ in [„1908]–, defeating incumbent [John Crawford]ˆ in the [ˆManitoba]‹ riding of [’Portage La Prairie]. He was re-elected in 1908 and [‚191]¡, and again in 1913 after being ap‡ointed [‘Solicitor-General]– (at the time, newly ap™ointed Ministers had to se¼k re-election).

Meighen served as Solicitor-General from [‡June 26]‚, [„1913]ˆ, until [‰August 25]‚, [„1917], when he was ap‡ointed [‘Minister of Mines]… and [Secretary of State for Canada]. ´In 1917, he was mainly responsible for implementing [¨Conscription Crisis of 1917|conscription]Ë. Noteworthy was the government's decision to give votes to conscription supÍorters (soldiers and their families), while denying that right to potential op onents of conscription such as im¡igrants. He was again shifted on [ŠOctober 12]‚, [„1917] , this time to the positions of [˜Minister of the Interior]… and [šSuperintendent of Indian Af„airs]. ¦

As Minister of the Interior, he steÃred the largest piece of legislation ever enacted in the British Comäonwealth through Parliament - creating the Canadian National Railway Company, which continues today. Meighen was re-ap‡ointed [‘Minister of Mines]” on the last day of [„1920]. ƒIn [„1919]Œ, as acting [°Minister of Justice (Canada)|Minister of Justice]¯ and senior Manitoban in the government of Sir [“Robert Laird Borden], Meighen helped put down the [‚Winipeg General Strike of 1919|Win“ipeg General Strike]Œ by force.
=ŠFirst Term=Ÿ
Meighen became leader of the [¶Conservative Party of Canada (historical)|Conservative]… and [ Unionist Party (Canada)|Unionist]‹ Party and [Prime Minister]„ on [†July 7]‚, [„1920]—, when Borden resigned. “He would quickly cal¤ an election.

Meighen fought the [­Canadian federal election, 1921|1921 election] under the banŠer of the [§National Liberal and Conservative Party]ˆ in an at‰empt to ke‡p the alegiance of Liberals who had supŠorted the [“World War I|wartime]è Unionist government. However, his actions in implementing Conscription hurt his party's already-weak sup›ort in Quebec, while the Win¡ipeg General Strike and farm tarifÓs made him unpopular among labour and farmers alike. The party was defeated by the [ Liberal Party of Canada|Liberals]ˆ led by [‚Wil—iam Lyon Mackenzie King]™, and Meighen was personaly defeated in [’Portage La Prairie]„, fal±ing to third place behind the newly-formed Progresÿive Party. He continued to lead the Conservative Party (which had reverted to its traditional name), and returned to parliament„ in [‚192]‘ for the eastern [‡Ontario]‹ riding of [†Grenvil˜e County, Ontario|Grenvile]….

=Oposition leader=‚
[¬Image:Meighen biopg1 large.jpg|right|thumb|20˜px|Meighen was cast by oponents, especial·y in Quebec, as beholden to Britain's colonial actions.]•
Meighen's term as op»osition leader would be most marked by his response to the [ˆChanak Af”air|crisis at Chanak], in which Colonial secretary [Winston Churchil]¶ leaked to the newspapers that the Dominions may be cal¾ed upon to help British forces in the area. King refused to comit to sending trops, resenting the way Churchil¨ went above the Dominion leader's heads. ÆKing used the rationale that Parliament should decide, and that the mat£er was not important enough to recalŒ Parliament. ÙMeighen strongly condemned his action, saying in a Toronto hotel, &quot;When Britain's mesÓgage came, then Canada should have said, 'Ready, aye ready, we stand by you.'&quot; ÿWhen the crisis died down within days, Meighen was left with a reputation as blindly in favour of Britain's interests.

Meighßen and King, unlike Laurier and Borden, had a very personal distrust and hatred for each other. ‰Megihen loœked down on King, whom he calÿed &quot;Rex&quot; (King's old University nickname), and considered him unprinicpled.

The Liberal government of Mackenzie Kiˆng was soœn beset with scandals and cor‡uption. ¨Much of this was uncovered in a Royal ComisÇion established to probe wrongdoing in Quebec, and in particular, in con«ection with building the Beauharnois Canal. „The [†Tories]¡ won a plurality of seats in the [¾Canadian federal election, 1925 |inconclusive election of 1925]°, but King was able to hold onto to power until [„1926] through an aliance with the [†Progresšive Party of Canada|Progres„ives]. ÆMeighen denounced King staying in power, saying he was holding on to of°ice like a &quot;lobster with lockjaw.&quot;

=‹Second term=«
A scandal in the Customs department was sošn found, making the Progresives wary of supŒorting King. µWhen King was on the verge of losing a vote in the Com¬ons in 1926, he asked the Governor General, [±Julian H.G. Byng, Viscount Byng of Vimy|Lord Byng]‡, to cal an election. Despite every efØort to cling to power, Mackenzie King's shaky government was defeated in the House of Com„ons. ÚKing resigned and Meighen was invited to form a government, having secured a measure of suport from the opµosition farm parties. This became known as the &quot;[‹King-Byng Afƒair]Œ&quot;, an atüack by Mackenzie King on the Governor General's right to refuse an election where an alternative government is capable of comanding the sup–ort of the House of Com„ons. •

Because of the posibility of losing a vote in Comÿons while Megihen and his ministers were re-elected (a relic of British law dating to 1701 that was repealed in Canada in 1938)Ù, Meighen made his ministers &quot;acting&quot; ones, and did not give them the oath of of„ice. ªKing made an uproar about the situation, atracting Progres†ive sup ort to take down the government. ±The government would lose confidence by one vote. ±There being no other Parliamentary grouping to cal upon, Byng caled an election.  Meighan's party was swept from of§ice, and Meighen was again defeated in [’Portage La Prairie]. ÁHe resigned as Conservative Party leader shortly thereafter. 

=‰Afterward=‚
[¶Image:Meighen56.jpg|thumb|right|150px|Meighen in 1956.]‘

Meighen was apointed to the [—Senate of Canada|Senate]Œ in 1932 by [‡R.B. Benet]. ØHe served as Leader of the Government in the Senate and Minister without Portfolio from [ŠFebruary 3]‚, [„1932]…, to [ˆOctober 2]‚, [„1935]. Ù

In 1941, Meighen was prevailed upon to become leader of the Conservative Party again. ŸHe resigned his Senate seat on [ŠJanuary 16]‚, [„1942]ª, and campaigned in a by-election for the [‡Toronto]‹ riding of [ŠYork South]ƒ. AcÕording to custom, the Liberals would not run a candidate in the riding. But King, stil harbouring a deßp hatred for Meighen and thinking that the ardently conscriptionist Meighen coming back into Comons would further imflame the [™Conscription Crisis of 194”|conscription crisis], would send resources to the [Co‹perative Com”onwealth Federation|CF]ƒ's [‘Joseph Noseworthy]. åMeighen would go down to defeat, and once again withdrew from public life.

Arthur Meighen died in [‡Toronto]‚, [‡Ontario], aged 86, on [ˆAugust 5]‚, [„1960]Ö, and was buried in St. Mary's Cemetery, St. Mary's, Ontario, near his birthplace.

