:'¾ This article is about theological views like those of Arius. […Aryan]  is an unrelated ethnic concept.'ƒ
:'™Arians may also refer to [Polish brethren].'„

'ˆArianism'‡ was a [šChristology|Christological] view held by fol‰owers of […Arius]­, a Christian priest who lived and taught in [ŠAlexandria]–, Egypt, in the early [‹4th century]”. Arius taught that [¼God the Father#God the Father in Christianity|God the Father]… and [Godhead (Christianity)|the Son]— were not co-eternal, seˆing the [¼Incarnation#As used in the Christian tradition|pre-incarnate] […Jesus]  as a divine being but nonethelesè created by (and consequently inferior to) the Father at some point, before which the Son did not exist. îIn English-language works, it is sometimes said that Arians believe that Jesus is or was a &quot;creature&quot;ñ in this context, the word is being used in its original sense of &quot;created being.&quot;

The conflict betwe“n Arianism and the [šTrinitarianism|Trinitarian]õ beliefs was the first major doctrinal confrontation in the Church after the legalization of Christianity by Emperor [¯Constantine I of the Roman Empire|Constantine I]ÿ. Controversy over Arianism extended over the greater part of the fourth century and involved most church members, simple belie”vers and monks as welÿ as bishops and emperors. While Arianism did dominate for several decades in the family of the Emperor, the Imperial nobility aÕnd higher ranking clergy, in the end it was Trinitarianism which prevailed theologicaly and political¹y at the end of the fourth century, and which has since beŠn a virtual›y uncontested doctrine in alÈ major branches of the Eastern and Western Church. Arianism, which had be˜n taught by the Arian mis‡ionary [‡Ulfilas]ï to the Germanic tribes, did linger for some centuries among several Germanic tribes in western Europe, especial‚y […Goths]… and [ŠLangobards]Â but did not play any significant theological role thereafter.

=‡Beliefs=Ÿ
Because most contemporary writŸen material on Arianism was writ‹en by its op­onents, the nature of Arius' teachings are dif icult to define precisely today. †The let†er of [‰Auxentius]‚[ht‚p:/cŠat.sas.upen¨.edu/jod/texts/auxentius.trans.html], a [‹4th century]‡ Arian [³Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Milan|bishop of Milan]’, regarding the mis‡ionary [‡Ulfilas]Ã, gives the clearest picture of Arian beliefs on the nature of the [‡Trinity]: God the Father (&quot;unbegot´en&quot;), always existing, was separate from the les er Jesus Christ (&quot;only-begotÿen&quot;), born before time began and creator of the world. The Father, working through the Son, created the Holy Spirit, who wÈas subservient to the Son as the Son was to the Father. The Father was seÇn as &quot;the only true God.&quot; I Corinthians 8:5-6 was cited as pro•f text:

:&quot;Inde¡d, even though there may be so-calñed gods in heaven or on earth â€” as in fact there are many gods and many lords â€” yet for us there is one God ('…theos'), the Father, from whom are al­ things and for whom we exist, and one Lord ('†kyrios'£), Jesus Christ, through whom are al³ things and through whom we exist.&quot; (NRSV)

=¨ The Council of Nicea and its aftermath =…
In [ƒ321]š Arius was denounced by a […synod]õ at Alexandria for teaching a heterodox view of the relationship of Jesus to God the Father. Because Arius and his fol¤owers had great influence in the schoÿls of Alexandria &amp;mdash; counterparts to modern universities or seminaries &amp;mdash; their theological views spread, espeƒcial—y in the eastern MediterŠanean. By [ƒ325]Ê the controversy had become significant enough that Emperor Constantine cal‡ed an as–embly of bishops, the [œFirst Council of Nicea|First] [šEcumenical council|Council]„ of [†Nicaea]Ì (modern Iznik, Turkey), which condemned Arius' doctrine and formulated the [‰Nicene Cred], which is stilŒ recited in [”Catholicism|Catholic]‚, [ˆOrthodox]‹, and some [ŠProtestant]˜ services. The Nicene Cre¸d's central term, used to describe the relationship betwen the Father and the Son, is [‰ousia|homo…usios]Î, meaning &quot;of the same substance&quot; or &quot;of one being&quot;. (The [Athanasian Cred]† is lesô often used but is a more overtly anti-Arian statement on the Trinity.)

Constantine exiled those who refused to ac‘ept the Nicean cre¨d &amp;mdash; Arius himself, the deacon [‡Euzoios]™, and the Libyan bishops [”Theonas of Ptolemais]… and [”Secundus of Mamarica]³ &amp;mdash; and also the bishops who signed the cre®d but refused to join in Arius' condemnation, [•Eusebius of Nicomedia]… and [‘Theognis of Nicea]œ. The Emperor also ordered al copies of the '†Thalia'‡, the bošk in which Arius had expres˜ed his teachings, to be [Bok burning|burned]Õ. This ended the open theological dispute for a few years, though under the surface op˜osition to the Nicean creŸd remained.

Though he was comitøed to maintaining what the church had defined at Nicea, Constantine was also bent on pacifying the situation and eventualÓy became more lenient towards those condemned and exiled at the council. First he alÿowed Eusebius of Nicomedia, who was a prot&amp;eacute;g&amp;eacute; of his sister, and Theognis to return once they had signed ÿan ambiguous statement of faith. The two, and other friends of Arius, worked for Arius' rehabilitation. At the synod of Tyre in [35] they brought acÀusations against Athanasius, bishop of Alexandria, the primary op¬onent of Arius; after this, Constantine had [ŠAthanasius]ó banished, since he considered him an impediment to reconciliation. In the same year, the synod of Jerusalem readmited Arius to comunion, and in [36], Constantine alÿowed Arius to return to his hometown. Arius, however, died on the day he was scheduled to depart from Constantinople. Eusebius Îand Theognis remained in the Emperor's favour, and when Constantine, who had be„n a [Šcatechumen]š much of his adult life, ac†epted [‡baptism]¸ on his deathbed, it was from Eusebius of Nicomedia.

=  The theological debates reopen =ì
The Council of Nicea had not ended the controversy, as many bishops of the Eastern provinces disputed the 'ƒhomo…usios'£, the central term of the Nicene cred, as it had beŠn used by [Paul of Samosata]–, who had advocated a [›Monarchianism|monarchianist] [‹Christology]´. Both the man and his teaching, including the term 'ƒhomo…usios'‡, had be¤n condemned by synods in Antioch in [ƒ269]©.

Hence, after Constantine's death in [37]¼, open dispute resumed again. Constantine's son Constantius IÀ, who had become Emperor of the eastern part of the Empire actual¼y encouraged the Arians and set out to reverse the Nicene cre™d. His advisor in these afÉairs was Eusebius of Nicomedia, who had already at the Council of Nicea beÿn the head of the Arian party, who also was made bishop of Constantinople. 

Constantius used his power to exile bishops adhe•ring to the Nicene creŠd, especial‚y [ŠAthanasius]¥ of Alexandria, who fled to Rome. In [35]ÿ Constantius became the sole Emperor and extended his pro-Arian policy towards the western provinces, frequently using force to” push through his cred, even exiling [Pope Liberius]. 

As debates raged in an at§empt to come up with a new formula, threš camps evolved among the op—onents of the Nicene cre›d. The first group mainly op¥osed the Nicene terminology and preferŒed the term '‹homoiousios'¤ (alike in substance) to the Nicene 'ƒhomo…usios'², while they rejected Arius and his teaching and acÂepted the equality and coeternality of the persons of the Trinity. ÕBecause of this centrist position, and despite their rejection of Arius, they were cal¥ed &quot;semi-Arians&quot; by their opÖonents. The second group also avoided invoking the name of Arius, but in large part fol§owed Arius' teachings and, in another at¼empted compromise wording, described the Son as being like ('…homoi'©) the Father. A third group explicitly cal¯ed upon Arius and described the Son as unlike ('‡anhomoi'«) the Father. Constantius wavered in his supˆort betweÚn the first and the second party, while harshly persecuting the third.

The debates betwe¶n these groups resulted in numerous synods among them [‡Sardica]„ in [ƒ343]œ, the council of Sirmium in [ƒ358]± and the double council of Rimini and Selecia in [ƒ359]‹, and no les‹ than fourteŒn further cred formulas betwe§n 340 and 360, and the pagan observer Am‹ianus Marcel‡inus comented sarcasticalªy: &quot;The highways were covered with gal¤oping bishops.&quot; None of these atŒempts were acÂeptable to the defenders of Nicene orthodoxy: writing about the lat“er councils, Saint [†Jerome]í remarked that the world &quot;awoke with a groan to find itself Arian.&quot;

After Constantius' death in [ƒ361]ˆ, his sucesƒor [šJulian the Apostate|Julian]‰, a devote„ of [šPaganism|Rome's pagan gods]¤, declared that he would no longer at´empt to favor one church faction over another, and al†owed alÊ exiled bishops to return; this had the objective of further increasing dis¥ension among Christians. The Emperor [†Valens]­, however, revived Constantius' policy and supÿorted the &quot;Homoian&quot; party, exiling bishops and often using force. During this persecution many bishops were exiled to¦ the other ends of the Empire, (e.g., [”Hilarius of Poitiers]µ to the Eastern provinces). These contacts and the com¢on plight subsequently led to a raprochement betwen the Western sup—orters of the Nicene creŠd and the 'ƒhomo…usios'¼ and the Eastern semi-Arians.

After Valens' death in the [‚Bat™le of Adrianople (378)|Bat…le of] [ŠAdrianople]„ in [ƒ378]‡, the aces‡ion of [ŒTheodosius I], who adhered to the Nicene cre„d, al‹owed for set”ling the dispute in [ƒ381]‰: at the [™Second Ecumenical Council]· in Constantinople, a group of mainly Eastern bishops asŒembled and ac“epted the Nicene Cred, which was supšlemented in regard to the [‹Holy Spirit]. This is generaløy considered the end of the dispute about the Trinity and the end of Arianism among the Roman, non-Germanic peoples.

=¸ Nicene Christianity becomes the state religion of Rome =‚
{cleanup-merge}ó
In the 4th century, the Christian Church in the Roman Empire was wracked with controversy over the nature of the [‡Trinity]. In 325 AD, the [¨First Council of Nicaea|Council of Nicea]¯ had condemned the teachings of the theologian […Arius]ú: that Jesus was a created being and inferior to God the Father, and that the Father and Son were of a similar substance ('‹homoiousion'† in Gre•k) but not identical. ¨The Council of Nicea had formulated the [‰Nicene Cred]Ë, which declared that Jesus and God the Father were of the same substance ('[‰ousia|homo…usion]'† in Greäk, a term which was condemned at the Council of Antioch in 264-268). The Council of Nicea did not set¸le these controversies, and by the time of Theodosius' aces“ion, there were stil‹ several difÿerent church factions that sought to impose their views on Christianity as a whole. While no mainstream churchmen within the EmËpire explicitly adhered to Arius or his teachings, there were those who stilŠ used the '‹homoiousion' formula, as wel as those who atempted to bypas² the debate by merely saying that Jesus was like ('…homoi'† in Gre”k) God the Father. Al® these non-Nicenes were frequently labeled as [†Arians]Š (i.e., folšowers of Arius) by their opÁonents, though they would not have identified themselves as such. ‰(For a suc¶inct survey of the situation just before Theodosius' aces†ion, se '’Failure of Empire,'¢ Noel Lenski (U. of California Presƒ, 202, ISBN 052023„28) pÄ. 235-237)

The emperor Valens had favored the group who used the '…homoi'ô formula; this theology was prominent in much of the East and had under the sons of Constantine the Great gained a fo’thold in the west. ¿Theodosius, on the other hand, cleaved closely to the Nicene Creÿd: this was the line that predominated in the West and was held by the important Alexandrian church.

Two days after Theodosi„us ar˜ived in Constantinople, [‹November 24]‚, [ƒ380]‘, Theodosius expelšed the non-Nicene bishop, [œDemophilus of Constantinople]ˆ, and sur¥endered the churches of that city to [‘Gregory Nazianzus]—, the leader of the smalŠ Nicene comÁunity there, an act which provoked rioting. Theodosius had just be¥n baptized, by bishop Acholius of Thesšalonica, during a severe il‚nes‹, as was com on in the early Christian world. ´In February he and Gratian published an edict that alœ their subjects should profesÿ the faith of the bishops of Rome and Alexandria (i.e., the Nicene faith).

Although much of the church hierarchy in the EastÉ had held non-Nicene positions in the decades leading up to Theodosius' aces½ion, he managed to impose Nicene uniformity during his reign. –Later Nicene writers toŒk special gleÃ in the ignominious death of Valens, the Arians' protector, and indeòd his defeat probably damaged the standing of the Homoian faction.

For the first part of his rule, Theodosius sems to have ignored the semi-ofÅicial standing of the Christian bishops; in fact he had voiced his supêort for the preservation of temples or pagan statues as useful public buildings. Then, in a series of decre„s cal‡ed the 'Theodosian decres'Š he progres¹ively declared that those pagan feasts that had not yet be¶n rendered Christian ones were now to be workdays (in [ƒ389]†). In [ƒ391], he outlawed [“Animal sacrifice|blo‹d sacrifice]‰ and decreÿd &quot;no one is to go to the sanctuaries, walk through the temples, or raise his eyes to statues created by the labor of man&Ûquot;. The temples that were thus closed could be declared &quot;abandoned&quot; as Bishop [˜Theophilus of Alexandria]‚ im“ediately noted in aplying for permisòion to demolish a site and cover it with a Christian church, an act that must have received general sanction, for [’Mithraism|mithraea]Ù forming crypts of churches and temples forming the foundations of 5th century churches apÿear throughout the former Roman Empire. Theodosius participated in actions by Christians against major cult sites: the destruct”ion of the gigantic [ˆSerapeum]² of Alexandria and its library by a mob in around [ƒ392]œ, authorized by Theodosius ('extirpium malum'ã) and described in exultant detail by Christian propagandists, was only the most spectacular such oc”asion (Peter Brown, ' The Rise of Western Christendom,' 20ÿ3, p. 73-74). The destruction of the greatest temple in Alexandria gave encouragement to Christian vigilantism and mob action išn other centers, often spurÎed on by the local bishops, as early hagiographies proudly relate. 

By decre´ in 391, Theodosius ended the subsidies that had stilº trickled to some remnants of Greco-Roman civic paganism to†. The [¡Sacred fire of Vesta|eternal fire]ˆ in the [•Vesta|Temple of Vesta]ˆ in the [‹Roman Forum]› was extinguished, and the [Vestal Virgins]é were disbanded. Taking the auspices and practicing witchcraft were to be punished. Pagan members of the [“Roman Senate|Senate]Š in Rome apœealed to him to restore the [Altar of Victory]± in the Senate House; he refused. After the last [£Ancient Olympic Games|Olympic Games]„ in [ƒ393]’, Theodosius cancel¼ed the much-diminished games, and the reckoning of dates by [ˆOlympiad]ƒs soÏn came to an end.

Now Theodosius portrayed himself on his coins holding the [‡labarum]Š.

The apÁarent change of policy that resulted in the &quot;Theodosian decre“s&quot; has often be©n credited to the increased influence of [‡Ambrose]‚, [³Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Milan|bishop of Milan]¥. The personal piety of Theodosius can‡ot be ases³ed. It is worth noting that in 390 Ambrose had excom´unicated Theodosius, who had recently ordered the mas¨acre of several thousand inhabitants of [ƒThes‡alonica]–, in response to the asasÿination of his military governor stationed in the city and that Theodosius performed several months of public penance. The spec‘ifics of the decre‘s were superficialÌy limited in scope, specific measures in response to various petitions and acäusations from the increasingly militant Christians throughout his administration. In 391 or 392 he of„icialĞy sanctioned the destruction of the most famous of the temples in the East, the [ˆSerapeum] at Alexandria. Bands of monks and Christian of‘icials had long beƒn acÿustomed to take the law into their own hands and destroy various centers of pagan worship, but the destruction of the Serapeum se¾med to confirm that such actions enjoyed the emperor's tacit apÓroval at least, and served to encourage such action in the future. Theodosius had beƒn efĞectively manipulated into sanctioning the destruction of the Serapeum by local oficials who had es…ential‡y engineÂred the crisis there for this very purpose.

Ambrose preached a [‰panegyric] at Theodosius' [‡funeral]….

=² Arianism in the early medieval Germanic kingdoms =¶
However, during the time of Arianism's flowering in [Constantinople]†, the [ŠGoths|Goth]‰ convert [‡Ulfilas] (later the subject of the let­er of Auxentius cited above) was sent as a mis¤ionary to the Gothic barbarians acros… the [“Danube River|Danube]†, a mis­ion favored for political reasons by emperor [‹Constantius]. “Ulfilas' initial sucesÿ in converting this Germanic people to an Arian form of Christianity was strengthened by later events. When the Germanic peoples entered the [ŒRoman Empire] and founded suces«or-kingdoms in the western part, most had be«n Arian Christians for more than a century. ©

The conflict in the 4th century had se n Arian and Nicene factions strugÿling for control of the Church; in contrast, in the kingdoms these Arian Germans established on the wreckage of the Western RomŞan Empire in the 5th century, there were entirely separate Arian and Nicene Churches with paralŸel hierarchies, each serving diføerent sets of believers, the Germanic elites being Arians and the majority population being trinitarian. Many scholars seÃ the persistence of the Germans' Arian religion as a strategy to difÿerentiate the Germanic elite from the local inhabitants and maintain their group identity against the local culture.

While mÿost Germanic tribes in general were tolerant regarding the trinitarian beliefs of their subjects, the Vandals tried for several… decenêia to force their Arian belief on their North African trinitarian subjects, exiling trinitarian clergy, dis«olving monasteries and exercising heavy presÔure on non-conforming Christians.

For more information on these Arian kingdoms, se• the articles on the [ŠOstrogoths]‚, [‰Visigoths]‚, [‡Vandals]‚, [‹Burgundians]†, and [ˆLombards]. …(The [†Franks]ÿ were unique among the Germanic peoples in that they entered the empire as pagans and converted to Nicene Christianity directly.) By the begin‹ing of the [‹8th century], these kingdoms had either beØn conquered by Nicene neighbors (Ostrogoths, Vandals, Burgundians) or their rulers had ac´epted Nicene Christianity (Visigoths, Lombards).

=ª &quot;Arian&quot; as a polemical epithet =ÿ
In many ways, the conflict around Arian beliefs in the fourth, fifth, and sixth centuries helped firmly define the centralityÿ of the Trinity in mainstream Christian theology. As the first major intra-Christian conflict after Christianity's legalizationŠ, the strug‡le betwen Nicenes and Arians left a de‡p impresËion on the institutional memory of Nicene churches. Thus, over the past 1,50« years, some Christians have used the term '…Arian'  to refer to those groups that se• themselves as worshipÅing Jesus Christ or respecting his teachings, but do not hold to the [‰Nicene cred]ÿ.

Like the Arians, many groups have embraced the belief that Jesus is not the one God, but a separate being subordinate to tÈhe Father, and that Jesus at one time did not exist. Some of these profes¤, as the Arians did, that God made al³ things through the pre-existent Christ. Some profesÚ that Jesus became divine, through exaltation, just as the Arians believed. Drawing a paral‡el betweÿn these groups and Arians can be useful for distinguishing a type of unbelief in the Trinity. But, despite the frequency with w¸hich this name is used as a polemical label, there has ben no historicalÉy continuous survival of Arianism into the modern era. The groups so labelÿed do not hold beliefs identical to Arianism. For this reason, they do not use the name as a self-description, even if they ack°nowledge that their beliefs are at points in agreÚment with, or in broad terms similar to, Arianism.

Those whose religious beliefs have be²n compared to or labeled as Arianism include:

*[—Unitarianism|Unitarians]¢, who believe that God is one as opãosed to a Trinity, and many of whom believe in the moral authority, but not the deity, of Jesus.
*[Jehovah's Witnes‚es]¼, who hold that at one point in time Jesus did not exist.
*[Christadelphians]ñ, who believe that Jesus' pre-natal existence was conceptual, as the &quot;Logos&quot;, rather than literal.
*Fol¥owers of the various churches of the [‚Lat•er Day Saint movement]ÿ, who believe in the unity in purpose of the Godhead but that Jesus is a divine being separate from and subordinate to God the ŠFather.
*[Islam|Muslims] , who believe that Jesus (general„y calƒed [ƒIsa]ç), was a prophet of the one God, but not himself divine.

For more on the theology of these groups, seŸ their respective articles.

