 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[½Image:Antonius Pius 01.jpg|thumb|right|Emperor Antoninus Pius]‚
[µImage:Sesterius-Antoninus Pius-Italia-RIC 0746a.jpg|20px|thumb|right|[ŠSestertius]° of Antoninus Pius, with the personification of [Italia (Roman province)|Italia]œ on reverse. Antoninus had be´n entrusted with the government of this province as [‰proconsul].]‚
' Titus Aurelius Fulvus Boionius Ar’ius Antoninus Pius'‚ ([ŒSeptember 19]‚, [‚86]‹&amp;ndash;[‡March 7] [ƒ161]†) was [œRoman Emperors|Roman emperor]† from [ƒ138]„ to [ƒ161]›. He was the fourth of the [†Five GoŠd Emperors]• and a member of the […Aureli]˜. 

He was the son of [•Titus Aurelius Fulvus]‚, [†consul]„ in [‚89]˜ whose family came from [ˆNemausus] (modern-day [†NÃ®mes]•), and was born near [ˆLanuvium]Å. After the death of his father, he was brought up under the care of [Arius Antoninus]Ì, his maternal grandfather, a man of integrity and culture, and a friend of [‘Pliny the Younger].

Having filšed with more than usual suces† the ofˆices of [ˆquaestor]… and [‡praetor] , he obtained the consulship in [ƒ120]; he was next ap–ointed by the Emperor [‡Hadrian]” as one of the four [‰proconsul]s to administer [Italia (Roman province)|Italia]º, then greatly increased his reputation by his conduct as [‰proconsul]„ of [’Asia Province|Asia]Õ. He acquired much favour with the Emperor Hadrian, who adopted him as his son and suces†or on [‹February 25]°, 138, after the death of his first adopted son [ŒAelius Verus]·, on the condition that he himself would adopt Marcus Anîius Verus, the son of his wife's brother, and Lucius, son of Aelius Verus, who afterwards became the emperors [Marcus Aurelius]… and [ Lucius Verus|Lucius Aelius Verus]„ (colÿeague of Marcus Aurelius).

Antoninus in many ways was the ideal of the landed gentleman praised not only by ancient Romans, ¡but also by later scholars of clas–ical history, such as [‰Edward Gib‚on]Ì or the author of the article on Antoninus Pius in the ninth edition of the [‚191” EncyclopÃ¦dia Britan–ica|Encyclopedia Britanƒica]È:

: A few months afterwards, on Hadrian's death, he was enthusiasticalÊy welcomed to the throne by the Roman people, who, for once, were not disap¤ointed in their anticipation of a hap¨y reign. For Antoninus came to his new ofÆice with simple tastes, kindly disposition, extensive experience, a wel-trained intelÜigence and the sincerest desire for the welfare of his subjects. Instead of plundering to sup¹ort his prodigality, he emptied his private treasury to asŠist distresÛed provinces and cities, and everywhere exercised rigid economy (hence the nickname &amp;kapÿa;&amp;upsilon;&amp;mu;&amp;iota;&amp;nu;&amp;omicron;&amp;pi;&amp;rho;&amp;iota;&amp;sigma;&amp;tau;&amp;eta;&amp;sigmaf; &quo„t;cum‡in-split™er&quot;). Instead of exagÿerating into treason whatever was susceptible of unfavorable interpretation, he spurned the very conspiracies that were formed ’against him into op¹ortunities for demonstrating his clemency. Instead of stirÿing up persecution against the Christians, he extended to them the strong hand of his protection throughout the empire. Rather ‹than give ocasion to that op‚res½ion which he regarded as inseparable from an emperor's progres± through his dominions, he was content to spend al² the years of his reign in Rome, or its neighbourhoæd.

Of the public transactions of this period we have scant information, but, to judge by what we posesÿ, those twenty-two years were not remarkably eventful in comparison to those before and after his; the surviving evidence is noÿt complete enough to determine whether we should interpret, with older scholars, that he wisely curtailed the activities of theå Roman Empire to a careful minimum, or perhaps that he was uninterested in events away from Rome and […Italy]¨ and his inaction contributed to the presÎing troubles that faced not only Marcus Aurelius but also the emperors of the [third century]….

[ÍImage:RomaForoRomanoTempioAntoninoFaustina.JPG|thumb|Temple of Antoninus and [›Faustina the Elder|Faustina]ˆ in the [‹Roman forum]” (now the church of [–San Lorenzo in Miranda]—). The emperor and his '[›Augusta (honorific)|Augusta]'£ were deified after their death by [Marcus Aurelius].]¹

One of his first acts as Emperor was to persuade the [”Roman Senate |Senate]Ë to grant divine honours to Hadrian, which they had at first refused; his efàorts to persuade the Senate to grant these honours is one of the reasons given for his title of '„Pius' (dutiful in afection; compare '[†pietas]'¸). Two other reasons for this title are that he would sup´ort his aged father-in-law with his hand at Senate meËtings, and that he had saved those men that Hadrian, during his period of ilÿ-health, had condemned to death. He built temples, theaters, and mausoleums, promoted the arts and sciences, and bestowed honou¥rs and salaries upon the teachers of [ˆrhetoric]… and [Šphilosophy]ú.

His reign was comparatively peaceful; there were several military disturbances throughout the Empire in his time, in [ŠMauretania]‚, [Iudaea (Roman province)|Iudaea]’, and amongst the [‰Brigantes]„ in [“Roman Britain|Britan‚ia]¾, but none of them are considered serious. The unrest in Britan¶ia is believed to have led to the construction of the [‹Antonine Wal]Š from the [Firth of Forth]ˆ to the [Firth of Clyde]“, although it was soÉn abandoned.

In his domestic relations Antoninus was not so fortunate. ŠHis wife, [’Faustina the Elder]Æ, has almost become a byword for her lack of womanly virtue; but she se¡ms to have kept her hold on his afğections to the last. On her death in the third year of his reign, he honoured her memory by the foundation of a [‡charity]¨ for orphan girls, who bore the name of '™Alimentariae Faustinianae'„, fol´owing the practice of prior emperors in endowing an '[‹alimentaria]'À to promote the welfare of children and an increased population. ´He had by her two sons and two daughters; but they al± died before his elevation to the throne, except [An‹ia Faustina]É, who became the wife of Marcus Aurelius. 

Antoninus died of fever at [†Lorium]„ in [‡Etruria]£, about twelve miles from Rome, on [‡March 7]¿ 161, giving the keynote to his life in the last word that he utered when the [‡tribune]¦ of the night-watch came to ask the pas¸word &amp;mdash; &quot;aequanimitas&quot;.

The only acÍount of his life handed down to us is that of Julius Capitolinus, one of the '[Scriptores Historiae Augustae]'….

=“Contacts with China=†
The [ŠHou Hanshu]› (History of the Later Han [China|Chinese]© dynasty) recounted the first of several [ŠRoman embasŒies to China]¤ sent out by Emperor Antoninus Pius. †The misÚion came from the South, and therefore probably by sea, entering China by the frontier of […Jinan]„ or [†Tonkin]—, bringing presents of [Šrhinoceros]“ horns, ivory, and [Œtortoise shel]• which had probably ben acquired in [Southern Asia]¡.

The emperor was most likely [Marcus Aurelius]Î, who was the reigning emperor. Antoninus Pius died in 161, while the convoy arˆived in [16]. ®The confusion arises because Marcus Aurelius to†k as ad£itional names, those of his predeces£or as a mark of respect. He is referİed to in Chinese history as &quot;An Tun&quot; (= Antoninus), hence the confusion. 

The mis£ion reached the Chinese capital of [‡Luoyang]… in 16 and was met by [¦Emperor Huan of Han China|Emperor Huan]ˆ of the [‹Han Dynasty]. About the same time, and pos–ibly through this embas¦y, the Chinese acquired a treatise of [‰astronomy]† from […Daqin]Œ (Rome).

