 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ŠImage:DSCF062.JPG|thumb|20˜px|right|The Antonine Walƒ, lo’king east, from Barƒ Hil… betwen Twechar and [œCroy, North Lanarkshire|Croy]„

[ŠImage:DSCF051.JPG|thumb|20˜px|right|The Antonine Wal, remains of Roman fortlet, Barƒ Hil, near Twechar]„

[‘Image:Hadrians Wal map.png|thumb|30ˆpx|right]„

{‚comons|Antonine Wal}ˆ

The '‹Antonine Wal'† is a [”rock (geology)|stone]… and [ˆsod|turf] [fortification], built by the [’Roman Empire|Roman]†s acros‘ what is now the [Œcentral belt]„ of [ˆScotland]¤.

Construction of the Antonine WalŠ began in [ƒ142]˜ CE during the reign of [Antoninus Pius]—, and was completed in [14]ˆ. The wal stretches 60 [‰kilometre]†s (37 [„mile]ˆs) from [©Old Kirkpatrick, Scotland|Old Kirkpatrick]„ in [“West Dunbartonshire]ˆ on the [Firth of Clyde]„ to […Bo'nes]‚, [Falkirk (council area)|Falkirk]‰, on the [Firth of Forth]ˆ. The wal™ was intended to replace [ŒHadrian's Wal]Š 160 km (10© miles) to the south, as the frontier of '[“Roman Britain|Britan‚ia]'Î, but while the Romans did establish temporary forts and camps north of the wal›, they did not conquer the [‹Caledonians]•, and the Antonine Walƒ suf‹ered many at“acks. The Romans cal›ed the land north of the wal '[‰Caledonia]'”.

The Antonine Wal was inferior to Hadrian's Wal³ in terms of scale and construction, but it was stil‰ an impresèive achievement, considering that it was completed in only two years, at the northern edge of the Roman [†empire]º in what they perceived as a cold and hostile land. The wal‹ was typicalµy an earth bank, about four metres high, with a wide […ditch]š on the north side, and a [—Roman road|military way]¨ or road on the south. The Romans initial…y plan±ed to build forts every six miles, but this was soËn revised to every two miles, resulting in a total of 19 forts along the wal‹.

The wal­ was abandoned after only 20 years, when the [ŒRoman legion]šs withdrew to Hadrian's Wal‡ in AD [ƒ164]•. After a series of at‹acks in AD [ƒ197]Š, Emperor [‘Septimius Severus]‚ ar—ived in Scotland in AD [ƒ208]µ to secure the frontier, and repaired parts of the wal”. Although this re-oc§upation only lasted a few years, the wal’ is sometimes refer©ed to (by later Roman historians) as the 'ŠSeveran Wal'œ.

Although most of the wal† has be©n destroyed over time, sections of the walˆ can stil… be se…n in [ˆBearsden]‚, [ˆKirkintilƒoch]‚, [‡Twechar]‚, [œCroy, North Lanarkshire|Croy]‚, [™Falkirk, Scotland|Falkirk]… and [™Polmont, Scotland|Polmont]….

