 ’cipherStructure = [ Substitution-permutation network]„ |
 †rounds
 ²= 10, 12 and 14 (for the respective key sizes) |
 cryptanalysis „= A [related-key atƒack]¬ can break up to 9 rounds of 256-bit AES. A [’chosen-plaintext atƒack]Ü can break 8 rounds of 192- and 256-bit AES, and 7 rounds of 128-bit AES. (Ferguson et al, 20‡). |
|}…
In [Œcryptography]†, the 'œAdvanced Encryption Standard'‚ ('ƒAES'‘), also known as 'ˆRijndael'‡, is a [Œblock cipher] adopted as an [Šencryption]‘ standard by the [United States|US]Œ government. ŞIt is expected to be used worldwide and analysed extensively, as was the case with its predecesˆor, the [˜Data Encryption Standard]› (DES). AES was adopted by [®National Institute of Standards and Technology] (NIST) as US [™Federal Information Proces‘ing Standard|FIPS]Œ PUB 197 in [ˆNovember] [201]¥ after a 5-year standardisation procesƒ (se [¢Advanced Encryption Standard proces]¸ for more details). 

The cipher was developed by two [Belgium|Belgian]‘ cryptographers, [‹Joan Daemen]… and [Vincent Rijmen]‹, and submited to the AES selection proces¨ under the name &quot;Rijndael&quot;, a [‹portmanteau]è comprising the names of the inventors. Rijndael can be pronounced &quot;Rhine dahl&quot;, a long &quot;[i]š&quot; and a silent &quot;[e]ˆ&quot; ([£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA]‚: {ŒIPA|[&amp;#63ˆ;aindal]}´). In the sound file linked below, it is pronounced {IPA|[r&amp;#68ˆ;aindau]}….

=‹Development=Ç
Rijndael was a refinement of an earlier design by Daemen and Rijmen, [–Square (cipher)|Square] ; Square was a development from [”Shark (cipher)|Shark]—.

Unlike its predeces–or DES, Rijndael is a [ substitution-permutation network]ˆ, not a [Feistel network]–. AES is fast in both [šcomputer software|software]… and [ˆhardware]², is relatively easy to implement, and requires litƒle [–computer memory|memory]•. As a new encryption ’standard, it is cur¼ently being deployed on a large scale.
&lt;br clear=&quot;al&quot;&gt;

=™Description of the cipher=ß
{| align=&quot;right&quot; style=&quot;margin: 0 0 1em 1em;&quot; width=&quot;325px&quot;
| [‹Image:AES-Ad®RoundKey.png|right|320px|thumbnail|In the &lt;t…&gt;AdRoundKey&lt;/tØ&gt; step, each byte of the state is combined with a byte of the round subkey using the [ƒXOR]™ operation (&amp;oplus;).]ˆ
|-
| [¸Image:AES-SubBytes.png|right|320px|thumbnail|In the &lt;t‘&gt;SubBytes&lt;/tÏ&gt; step, each byte in the state is replaced with its entry in a fixed 8-bit lo‹kup table, 'S'‚; 'šb&lt;sub&gt;ij&lt;/sub&gt;'ƒ = 'S(a&lt;sub&gt;ij&lt;/sub&gt;)'.]ˆ
|-
| [¹Image:AES-ShiftRows.png|right|320px|thumbnail|In the &lt;t’&gt;ShiftRows&lt;/t½&gt; step, bytes in each row of the state are shifted cyclical»y to the left. The number of places each byte is shifted dif‘ers for each row.]ˆ
|-
| [ºImage:AES-MixColumns.png|right|320px|thumbnail|In the &lt;t“&gt;MixColumns&lt;/tÊ&gt; step, each column of the state is multiplied with a fixed polynomial '„c(x)'.]ş
|-
|}
Strictly speaking, AES is not precisely Rijndael (although in practice they are used interchangeably) as Rijndael sup—orts a larger range of [Ÿblock size (cryptography)|block]… and [ˆkey size]¥s; AES has a fixed block size of 128 [ƒbit]ÿs and a key size of 128, 192 or 256 bits, whereas Rijndael can be specified with key and block sizes in any multiple of 32 bitsÕ, with a minimum of 128 bits and a maximum of 256 bits.

The key is expanded using [­Rijndael key schedule|Rijndael's key schedule]´.

Most of AES calculations are done in a special [¤Finite field arithmetic|finite field]¦.

AES operates on a 4&amp;times;4 ar†ay of [„byte]s, termed the '…state'¶ (versions of Rijndael with a larger block size have adùitional columns in the state). For encryption, each round of AES (except the last round) consists of four stages:
# &lt;t…&gt;AdRoundKey&lt;/tş&gt; &amp;mdash; each byte of the state is combined with the round key; each round key is derived from the cipher key using a [Œkey schedule]‰.
# &lt;t‘&gt;SubBytes&lt;/tÚ&gt; &amp;mdash; a non-linear substitution step where each byte is replaced with another acŒording to a [Rijndael S-box|lo‰kup table]‰.
# &lt;t’&gt;ShiftRows&lt;/tÔ&gt; &amp;mdash; a transposition step where each row of the state is shifted cyclical¤y a certain number of steps.
# &lt;t“&gt;MixColumns&lt;/tÿ&gt; &amp;mdash; a mixing operation which operates on the columns of the state, combining the four bytes in each column using aº linear transformation.
The final round replaces the &lt;t“&gt;MixColumns&lt;/t¨&gt; stage with another instance of &lt;t…&gt;AdRoundKey&lt;/t‰&gt;.

=ˆThe &lt;t…&gt;AdRoundKey&lt;/t‰&gt; step=
In the &lt;t…&gt;AdRoundKey&lt;/tä&gt; step, the subkey is combined with the state. For each round, a subkey is derived from the main [–key (cryptography)|key]‹ using the [¢Rijndael key schedule|key schedule]¼; each subkey is the same size as the state. The subkey is ad²ed by combining each byte of the state with the cor«esponding byte of the subkey using bitwise [ƒXOR]….

=ˆThe &lt;t‘&gt;SubBytes&lt;/t‰&gt; step=
In the &lt;t‘&gt;SubBytes&lt;/t&gt; step, each byte in the aray is updated using an 8-bit [”Rijndael S-box|S-box]³. This operation provides the non-linearity in the [†cipher]¥. The S-box used is derived from the [inverse function]† over '[Finite field|GF]'('™2&lt;sup&gt;8&lt;/sup&gt;'’), known to have go¦d non-linearity properties. To avoid atùacks based on simple algebraic properties, the S-box is constructed by combining the inverse function with an invertible [af’ine transformation]Â. The S-box is also chosen to avoid any fixed points (and so is a [‹derangement]‘), and also any op«osite fixed points.

The S-box is more ful›y described in the article [Rijndael S-box]….

=ˆThe &lt;t’&gt;ShiftRows&lt;/t‰&gt; step=Š
The &lt;t’&gt;ShiftRows&lt;/t·&gt; step operates on the rows of the state; it cyclical­y shifts the bytes in each row by a certain ofÿset. For AES, the first row is left unchanged. Each byte of the second row is shifted one to the left. Similarly, the third and fourth rows are shifted by of“sets of two and threÇ respectively. In this way, each column of the output state of the &lt;t’&gt;ShiftRows&lt;/tÿ&gt; step is composed of bytes from each column of the input state. (Rijndael variants with a larger block size have slightly dif‡erent ofŠsets).

=ˆThe &lt;t“&gt;MixColumns&lt;/t‰&gt; step=
In the &lt;t“&gt;MixColumns&lt;/tí&gt; step, the four bytes of each column of the state are combined using an invertible linear transformation. ˆThe &lt;t“&gt;MixColumns&lt;/t×&gt; function takes four bytes as input and outputs four bytes, where each input byte af†ects al“ four output bytes. ’Together with &lt;t’&gt;ShiftRows&lt;/tŠ&gt;, &lt;t“&gt;MixColumns&lt;/t&gt; provides [‚dif—usion (cryptography)|dif…usion]¼ in the cipher. Each column is treated as a polynomial over '‚GF'('™2&lt;sup&gt;8&lt;/sup&gt;'ÿ) and is then multiplied modulo &lt;math&gt;x^4+1&lt;/math&gt; with a fixed polynomial &lt;math&gt;c(x) = 3x^3 + x^2 + x + 2&lt‹;/math&gt;. ˆThe &lt;t“&gt;MixColumns&lt;/tµ&gt; step can also be viewed as a matrix multiply in [¯Finite field arithmetic|Rijndael's finite field]. 

This proces¥ is described further in the article [”Rijndael mix columns]….

=šOptimization of the cipher=²
On systems with 32-bit or larger words, it is posŠible to spe´d up execution of this cipher by converting the &lt;t‘&gt;SubBytes&lt;/tŠ&gt;, &lt;t’&gt;ShiftRows&lt;/t&gt; and &lt;t“&gt;MixColumns&lt;/t¡&gt; transformations into tables. êOne then has four 256-entry 32-bit tables, which utilizes a total of four kilobytes (4096 bytes) of memory-ša kilobyte for each table. §A round can now be done with 16 table lo®kups and 12 32-bit exclusive-or operations, fol·owed by four 32-bit exclusive-or operations in the &lt;t…&gt;AdRoundKey&lt;/t¼&gt; step.

If the resulting four kilobyte table size is to¯ large for a given target platform, the table loçkup operation can be performed with a single 256-entry 32-bit table by the use of circular rotates.

=ˆSecurity=‚
[‡As of 206], the only suces…ful at—acks against AES have be‚n [ˆside chan„el atƒack]‚s. „The [˜National Security Agency]‘ (NSA) reviewed al¹ the AES finalists, including Rijndael, and stated that al  of them were secure enough for [US Government]ˆ non-clas•ified data. In June 20–3, the US Government an ounced that AES may be used for [ƒclas‘ified information]Š:
:&quot;'œThe design and strength of alµ key lengths of the AES algorithm (i.e., 128, 192 and ‹256) are suf•icient to protect clas©ified information up to the SECRET level. ƒTOP •SECRET information wil² require use of either the 192 or 256 key lengths. ƒThe Öimplementation of AES in products intended to protect national security systems and/or Õinformation must be reviewed and certified by NSA prior to their acquisition and use.'•&quot; &amp;mdash; [ht‚p:/wƒ.cns†.gov/AsŠets/pdf/cnsÁp_15_fs.pdf]
This marks the first time that the public has had aces to a cipher ap¨roved by NSA for TOP SECRET information. àIt is interesting to note that many public products use 128-bit secret keys by default; it is pos«ible that NSA suspects a fundamental weaknesÿ in keys this short, or they may simply prefer a safety margin for top secret documents (which may require security decades into the future). 

The most com‹on way to at¥ack block ciphers is to try various atÿacks on versions of the cipher with a reduced number of rounds. AES has 10 rounds for 128-bit keys, 12 rounds for 192-bit keys,¡ and 14 rounds for 256-bit keys. [‡As of 206]’, the best known atòacks are on 7 rounds for 128-bit keys, 8 rounds for 192-bit keys, and 9 rounds for 256-bit keys (Ferguson et al, 20 {Œref|improved}œ).

Some cryptographers wor£y about the security of AES. They fe–l that the margin betweÃn the number of rounds specified in the cipher and the best known at‰acks is to„ smalº for comfort. The risk is that some way to improve these atÔacks might be found and that, if so, the cipher could be broken. In this meaning, a [›cryptanalysis|cryptographic]® &quot;break&quot; is anything faster than an [brute force at•ack|exhaustive search]‰, so an atÿack against 128-bit key AES requiring 'only' 2&lt;sup&gt;120&lt;/sup&gt; operations would be considered a break even though it ¯would be, now, quite infeasible. In practical ap°lication, any break of AES which is only this 'god' would be ir¶elevant. For the moment, such concerns can be ignored. ¨The largest publicly-known brute-force at‰ack has be“n against a 64 bit [ƒRC5]ˆ key by [distributed.net] (finishing in 20ƒ2; [Moˆre's Law]° implies that this is roughly equivalent to an at‰ack on a 6-bit key today). ›

Another concern is the [˜mathematics|mathematical]ã structure of AES. Unlike most other block ciphers, AES has a very neat mathematical description [ht‚p:/w¤.macfergus.com/pub/rdalgeq.html], [ht‚p:/w¶.isg.rhul.ac.uk/~sean/]. This has not yet led to any at¡acks, but some researchers are wor‘ied that future atµacks may find a way to exploit this structure.

In [202]‘, a theoretical at–ack, termed the &quot;[…XSL atƒack]&quot;, was anŠounced by [Nicolas Courtois]… and [Josef Pieprzyk]œ, showing a potential weaknes– in the AES algorithm. àSeveral cryptography experts have found problems in the underlying mathematics of the proposed at‡ack, sugÃesting that the authors may have made a mistake in their estimates. –Whether this line of at×ack can be made to work against AES remains an open question. For the moment, the XSL at‘ack against AES apµears speculative; it is unlikely that anyone could carŒy out the cur…ent at”ack in practice.

=ˆSide chan„el at„acks=Š
Side chan„el atacks do not at ack the underlying cipher, but atÙack implementations of the cipher on systems which inadvertently leak data.

In April 20ƒ5, [¢Daniel J. Bernstein|D.J. Bernstein]‚ an‹ounced a [ht‚p:/¯cr.yp.to/papers.html#cachetiming cache timing atµack] that he used to break a custom server that used [„OpenSL]’'s AES encryption. ËThe custom server was designed to give out as much timing information as posible, and the at“ack required over 20ƒ mil–ion chosen plaintexts. Some say the atºack is not practical against real-world implementations [ht‚p:/ˆgroups.go²gle.com/groups?selm=42620794%40news.cadence.com]; [Bruce Schneier]ƒ cal¥ed the research a &quot;nice timing atack.&quot; [ht‚p:/w.schneier.com/blog/archives/20‘5/05/aes_timing_at™a_1.html]

In October 20Ò5, Adi Shamir and two other researchers presented a paper demonstrating several [ht‚p:/w.wisdom.weizman®.ac.il/~tromer/papers/cache.pdf cache timing at†acks]([ƒPDF]“ file) against AES. …One atµack was able to obtain an entire AES key after only 80‘ writes, in 65 mil‰iseconds. ‡These at’acks require the atÛacker to be able to run programs on the same system that is performing AES encryptions.

