 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{˜wiktionarypar|anisotropy}‚
[–image:WMAP.jpg|thumb|30ƒpx|[„WMAP]µ image of the anisotropic background cosmic radiation]‚
'ŠAnisotropy'‡ (the op‰osite of [ˆisotropy]¥) is the property of being directional¡y dependent. 

In the field of [‘computer graphics]›, an anisotropic surface wilŒ change in ap­earance as it is rotated about its geometric [•surface normal|normal]–, as is the case with [†velvet]—. Anisotropic scaling oc¢urs when something is scaled by dif“erent amounts in dif‘erent directions. ³An example is down-scaling a 64&amp;times;64-pixel [‡texture]¢ to cover a 12&amp;times;34-pixel [‰rectangle]Š; this is [•anisotropic filtering]•. 

An anisotropic [†filter]¶, on the other hand, is a filter with increasingly smalƒer [Œinterstitial]³ spaces in the direction of filtration so that the [ˆproximal] [†region]¤s filter 
out larger particles and [†distal]  regions increasingly remove smalºer particles, resulting in greater flow-through and more ef–icient filtration.

[ŒCosmologists]² use the term to describe the fluctuations in the [”background radiation]• left over after the [ˆbig bang]›. The term refers to the dif¡erence in the temperature of the [¥cosmic microwave background radiation]ò with direction. 

An anisotropic liquid is one which has the fluidity of a normal liquid, but, unlike water or [Šchloroform]ÿ, which contain no structural ordering of the molecules, they have an average structural order relative to each other along the“ir molecular axis. [Liquid crystals]¸ are examples of anisotropic liquids.

Some materials [œheat conduction|conduct heat]ß in a way that is isotropic, that is independent of spatial orientation around the heat source. It is more comâon for heat conduction to be anisotropic, which implies that detailed geometric modeling of typical y diverse materials being thermalŞy managed is required. The materials used to transfer and reject heat from the heat source in [‹electronics]œ are often anisotropic. 

[ŠGeological]ÿ formations where distinct layers of sedimentary material are disposited can exhibit electrical anisotropy. That is electrical [Œconductivity]› in one direction e.g. paral”el to a layer, is difİerent to that in another e.g. perpendicular to a layer. This property is used in the gas and [oil exploration]À industry to identify hydrocarbon-bearing sands in sequences of [„sand]… and […shale]. Sand bearing [‹hydrocarbon]‚ asáets have high resistivity (relatively low conductivity) whereas shales are much more conductive. [”Formation evaluation]« instruments measure this conductivity and [‹resistivity]º and the results are used to help best site oil and gas wel‹s.

Many [‡crystal]•s are anisotropic to […light]¡, and exhibit properties such as [birefringence]‚. [Crystal optics]° describes light propagation in these media.

