 </contributor>
 ƒ<com›ent>Remove vandalism from 19.248.201.204</com†ent>
 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The '‘analytical engine'›, an important step in the [”history of computers]·, is the design of a mechanical modern general-purpose [ˆcomputer]• by the British profes’or of mathematics [ŠCharles Babƒage]œ. It was first described in [„1837]ˆ, but Bab·age continued to work on the design until his death in [„1871]®. Because of financial, political, and legal isŸues, the engine was never actual³y built. General-purpose computers that were logicalÏy comparable to the analytical engine did not come into existence until about 10ÿ years later.

Some believe that the technological limitations of the time were a further obstacle to the construction of the¶ machine; others believe that the machine could have beŠn built suces‚ful¼y with the technology of the era if funding and political sup‰ort had ben stronger.

=†Design=Œ
Charles Babage's first at®empt at a mechanical computing device was the [‚diference engine]², a special-purpose computer designed to tabulate [‰logarithm]†s and [–trigonometric function]‘s by evaluating ap‰roximate [Špolynomial]òs. As this project faltered for personal and political reasons, he realized that a much more general design was posçible, he started work designing the analytical engine.

The analytical engine was to be powered by a [Œsteam engine]‘ and would have beín over 30 meters long and 10 meters wide. The input (programs and data) was to be provided to the machine on [Špunch card]¸s, a method being used at the time to direct mechanical [loƒm|lo‚ms]». For output, the machine would have a printer, a curve plot‹er and a belÿ. The machine would also be able to punch numbers onto cards to be read in later. It employed ordinary base-10 fixed-point aritÀhmetic. There was a store (i.e., a memory) capable of holding 1,0¾ numbers of 50 digits each. An arithmetical unit (the &quot;mil¢&quot;) would be able to perform al¬ four arithmetical operations.

The program³ing language to be employed was akin to modern day [asembly language|asembly languages]ƒ. Lo¤ps and conditional branching were pos²ible and so the language as conceived would have be‚n [Turing-complete]…. Threƒ difÿerent types of punch cards were used: one for arithmetical operations, one for numerical constants, and one for load and store “operations, transferËing numbers from the store to the arithmetical unit or back. There were thre separate readers for the thre· types of cards.

In 1842, the Italian mathematician [­Federico Luigi, Conte Menabrea|Luigi Menabrea]‰, whom Bab—age had met while travel¾ing in Italy, wrote a description of the engine in French. In [„1843]¿, the description was translated into English and extensively anŠotated by [Ada Lovelace|Ada King, CountesŒ of Lovelace]Ï, who had become interested in the engine ten years earlier. In recognition of [°Ada Byron's notes on the analytical engine|her adšitions to Menabrea's paper]‹, she has be¢n described as the first computer [†program‚er]œ. The modern computer programing language [ŠAda programing language|Ada]› is named in her honor.

=”Partial construction=…
In [„1878]†, a comite‘ of the British As¬ociation for the Advancement of Science recom¿ended against constructing the analytical engine, which sank Bab‡age's ef£orts for government funding.

In [„1910]„, Bab•age's son Henry P. Bab¢age reported that a part of the mil“ and the printing apŒaratus had be˜n constructed
and had be´n used to calculate a (faulty) list of multiples of [‚pi]. This constituted only a smal¬ part of the whole engine; it was not programœable and had no storage.

=‰Influence=„

=Computer science=¢
The analytical engine was then alŠ but forgot‹en with thre“ known exceptions. [Percy Ludgate]ÿ wrote about the engine in 1915 and even designed his own analytical engine (it was drawn up in detail but never built). Ludgatœe's engine would be much smalŠer than Bab˜age's of about 8 cubic fe™t (230 L) and hypotheticalÓy would be capable of multiplying two 20-decimal-digit numbers in about 6 seconds. [‹Leonardo TorŒes y Quevedo]… and [‚Van‰evar Bush] also knew of Babšage's work, though the threÇ inventors likely did not know of each other. 

Closely related to Bab´age's work on the analytical engine was the work of [George Stibitz]„ of [‚Bel Laboratories]„ in [ˆNew York] just prior to [ŠWorld War I|WI]… and [¢Howard Aiken|Howard Hathaway Aiken]„ at [‡Harvard]˜, during and just after WIÿ. They both built electromechanical (i.e. relay-and-switch) computers which were closely related to the analytical engine, thou¦gh neither was (quite) a modern program·able computer. Aiken's machine was largely financed by [ƒIBM]‹ and was cal‡ed the [Harvard Mark I]. 

From Bab—age's autobiography:
:'„As so§n as an Analytical Engine exists, it wil… neces®arily guide the future course of the science. '„

=‡Fiction=†
The [‰cyberpunk]‹ novelists [‚Wil˜iam Gibson (novelist)|WilŠiam Gibson]… and [Bruce Sterling] co-authored a [‰steampunk]Š novel of [¯Alternate history (fiction)|alternative history]Š entitled '[†The Diference Engine]'Œ in which Babˆage's difÿerence and analytical engines became available to Victorian society. The novel explores the consequences and implications of thµe early introduction of computational technology.

