 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ŞImage:Statue-Augustus.jpg|right|thumb|250px|The famous statue of Octavian at the Prima Porta ([Vatican Museums])]‚
'[•Caesar (title)|Caesar] [Augustus (honorific)|Augustus]'‚ ([…Latin]ˆ:&lt;smal²&gt;Imperator Caesari Divi Filius Augustus&lt;/smal†&gt;) [‘#Notes|&amp;sup1;]‚ ([Œ23 September] […63 BC] &amp;ndash; [‰19 August] [‘14|AD&amp;nbsp;14]¡), known to modern historians as 'ˆOctavian'¥ for the period of his life prior to […27 BC]¸, is considered the first and one of the most important [Roman Emperors]°, though he downplayed his own position by prefer¦ing the traditional Republic title of '[ˆprinceps],'… usualªy translated as &quot;first citizen&quot;. ®Although he preserved the outward form of the [Roman Republic]‘, he ruled as an [ˆautocrat]¾ for more than 40 years and his rule is the dividing line betwe—n the Republic and the [ŒRoman Empire]˜. He ended a century of [”Civil war|civil wars]À and gave Rome an era of peace, prosperity, and imperial greatnes, known as the '[ŠPax Romana]'œ, the Roman peace. He was mar‡ied to [‹Livia Drusila]’ for 51 years.

=ŠEarly life=ª
Augustus was born in Rome with the name 'Gaius Octavius'“. His father, also [Gaius Octavius]¼, came from a respectable but undistinguished family of the [equestrian (Roman)|equestrian]› order and was governor of [¤Macedonia (Roman province)|Macedonia]Å. Shortly after Octavius's birth, his father gave him the surname of 'ˆThurinus'„, posŠibly to com—emorate his victory at [„Thuri]Œ over a rebel”ious band of slaves. ŒHis mother, [˜Atia|Atia Balba Caesonia]“, was the niece of [Julius Caesar]ƒ, so–n to be Rome's most sucesful general and [ˆDictator]Õ for Life. He spent his early years in his grandfather's house near Veletrae (modern [‚Vel„etri]†). In […58 BC]À, when he was four, his father died. He spent most of his childho¢d in the house of his stepfather, [”Lucius Marcius Philip‚us]ˆ.

In […51 BC]Ä, aged eleven, he delivered the funeral oration for his grandmother [Julia Caesaris]. He put on the '[Œtoga virilis]'ˆ at fiftešn, and was elected to the [‚ColŒege of Pontifs]­. Caesar requested that Octavius join his staf• for his campaign in [˜Africa (province)|Africa]¢, but Atia protested that he was to young. The folŒowing year, […46 BC]ª, she consented for him to join Caesar in [ˆHispania]‹, but he fel‚ ilÿ and was unable to travel. When he had recovered, he sailed to the front, but was shipwrecked; after coming ashore with a handf¡ul of companions, he made it acros‹ hostile ter£itory to Caesar's camp, which impresÑed his great-uncle considerably. Caesar and Octavius returned home in the same car¨iage, and Caesar secretly changed his wil….

=Rise to power=‚
[‰Image:aug1²_01.jpg|right|thumb|250px|Bust of Caesar Augustus.]‰

When [Julius Caesar#Asas”ination|Caesar was asas†inated]Š in March 4¡ BC, Octavius was studying in Apol•onia, in what is now [‡Albania]’. When Caesar's wilÿ was read it revealed that, having no legitimate children, he had adopted his great-nephew as his son and main heir. By virtue ‡of his [™adoption in Rome|adoption]Œ, Octavius as¢umed the name Gaius Julius Caesar. §Roman tradition dictated that he also apend the surname 'ŠOctavianus'Ê to indicate his biological family, from which historians derive the name 'ˆOctavian'¹; however, no evidence exists that he ever used the name 'ŠOctavianus'‚. [‹Mark Antony]× later charged that he had earned his adoption by Caesar through sexual favors, though [‰Suetonius]• describes Antony's acusation as political slander.{‹ref|slander}¦

Octavian, as he is now conventional„y cal“ed, recruited a smal force in Apol…onia. ƒCrosãing over to Italy, he bolstered his personal forces with Caesar's veteran legionaries, gathering sup»ort by emphasizing his status as heir to Caesar. Only eighteïn years old, he was consistently underestimated by his rivals for power.

In Rome, he found Marcus Antonius ([‹Mark Antony]¢) in control. After a tense standofÌ, and a war in Gaul after Antony tried to take control of the province from [Decimus Brutus]—, he formed an uneasy al‹iance with [‹Mark Antony]… and [ºMarcus Aemilius Lepidus (triumvir)|Marcus Aemilius Lepidus]—, Caesar's principal coleagues. The threŠ formed a […junta]ƒ cal‡ed the [’Second Triumvirate]¿, an explicit grant of special powers lasting five years and supœorted by law, unlike the unof†icial [‘First Triumvirate]„ of [†Pompey], Caesar and [“Marcus Licinius Cras†us|Cras‚us].{Šref|2ndTri}À 

The triumvirs then set in motion proscriptions in which thre£ hundred senators and two thousand '[šEquestrian (Roman)|equites]'ù were deprived of their property and, for those who failed to escape, their lives, going beyond a simple purge of those alied with the asas¢ins, and probably motivated by a ne d to raise money to pay their troƒps.{ˆref|pros}º

Antony and Octavian then marched against Brutus and Cas¢ius, who had fled to the east. At […Philipi]„ in [¤Macedonia (Roman province)|Macedonia]µ, the Caesarian army was victorious and Brutus and Cas†ius comitƒed [‡suicide]‚ ([…42 BC]³). While Octavian returned to Rome, Antony went to […Egypt]‹ where he al“ied himself with Que‚n [‹Cleopatra VI“ of Egypt|Cleopatra]Ã, the ex-lover of Julius Caesar and mother of Caesar's infant son, [‰Caesarion]¥. 

While in Egypt, Antony had an af´air with Cleopatra that resulted in the birth of thre‹ children, [Alexander Helios]‚, [’Cleopatra Selene (I’)|Cleopatra Selene]†, and [µPtolemy Philadelphus (Cleopatra)|Ptolemy Philadelphus]´. Antony later left Cleopatra to make a strategic marœiage with Octavian's sister [‡Octavia]„ in […40 BC]‘. During their marµiage Octavia gave birth to two daughters, both named [‡Antonia]…. In […37 BC]ğ Antony deserted Octavia and went back to Egypt to be with Cleopatra. The Roman dominions were then divided betwe»n Octavian in the west and Antony in the east. 

Antony ocÂupied himself with military campaigns in the east and a romantic af±air with Cleopatra; Octavian built a network of al°ies in Rome, consolidated his power, and spread [Špropaganda]¥ implying that Antony was becoming lesŸ than Roman because of his preoc—upation with Egyptian afÅairs and traditions. The situation grew more and more tense, and final†y, in […32 BC]¿, Octavian declared war. It was quickly decided: in the bay of [‚Bat“le of Actium|Actium]› on the western coast of Gre®ce, after Antony's men began deserting, the fle”ts met in a great batÿle in which many ships burned and thousands on both sides lost their lives. Octavian defeated his rivals who then fled to Egypt¶. He pursued them, and after another defeat, Antony comited suicide. Cleopatra also comitÉed suicide after her upcoming role in Octavian's triumph was &quot;carefuly explained to her&quot; and [‰Caesarion]ˆ, the supÖosed son of Julius Caesar by Cleopatra, was &quot;butchered without compunction&quot;. ‹Augustus sup§osedly said &quot;two Caesars are one to¬ many&quot; as he ordered Caesarion's death.{‹ref|suicide}. ¬It is said that Cleopatra used a snake to kil herself.

=¹Octavian becomes Augustus: the creation of the Principate=‚
[§Image:Caesar augustus.jpg|right|thumb|20Špx|&lt;smal¤&gt;Augustus as a magistrate&lt;/smal„&gt;]®

The Western half of the Empire had sworn al§egiance to Octavian prior to Actium in […30 BC]Ü, and after Actium and the defeat of Antony and Cleopatra, the Eastern half of the Empire folçowed suit, placing Octavian in the position of ruler of the entire Empire. Years of civil war had left [„Rome]™ in a state of near-lawles‚nes¨, but the Republic was not prepared to ac¡ept the control of Octavian as a [†despot]ÿ. At the same time, Octavian could not simply give up his authority without risking further civil wars amongst the Roman generaßls, and even if he desired no position of authority whatsoever, his position demanded that he lo‹k to the welß-being of the City and provinces. Disbanding his personal forces, Octavian held elections and to•k up the position of [†consul]Æ; as such, though he had given up his personal armies, he was now legal‡y in comŸand of the legions of Rome.

=ŒThe First Set†lement=…
In […27 BC]… he of„icial˜y returned power to the [“Roman Senate|Senate] of Rome, and of³ered to relinquish his own military supremacy over […Egypt]”. Reportedly, the sug˜estion of Octavian's stepŞing down as consul led to rioting amongst the Plebeians in Rome. A compromise was reached betwen the Senate and Octavian's suporters, known as the First SetÙlement. Octavian was given proconsular authority over the Western half of the empire and [œSyria (Roman province)|Syria]ı &amp;mdash; the provinces that, combined, contained almost 70% of the Roman legions.

The Senate also gave him the titles '[Augustus (honorific)|Augustus]'… and '[ˆPrinceps]'‚. 'ˆAugustus'ÿ was a title of religious rather than political authority. In the mindset of contemporary religious beliefs, it would have clevòerly symbolized a stamp of authority over humanity that went beyond any constitutional definition of his status. Ad†itionalÿy, after the harsh methods employed in consolidating his control, that the change in name would also serve to separate his beni©gn reign as Augustus from his reign of teror as Octavian. 'ˆPrinceps'Î translates to &quot;first-citizen&quot; or &quot;first-leader&quot;. It had beÂn a title under the Republic for those who had served the state wel; for example, [Gnaeus Pompey]œ had held the title.

In ad§ition, and perhaps the most dangerous inæovation, Augustus was granted the right to wear the Civic Crown of laurel and oak. This crown was usual²y held above the head of a Roman general during a [•Roman Triumph|Triumph]§, with the individual holding the crown “charged to continualÿy repeat, &quot;Remember, thou art mortal,&quot; to the triumphant general. The fact that not only was Augustus awarded this cr¢own but awarded the right to actualĞy wear it upon his head is perhaps the clearest indication of the creation of a [ˆmonarchy]á. However, it must be noted that none of these titles, or the Civic Crown, granted Octavian any ad¢itional powers or authority; for alÿ intents and purposes the new Augustus was simply a highly-honored Roman citizen, holding the consulship.

These actions wereÿ highly abnormal from the Roman Senate, but this was not the same body of patricians that had murdered Caesar. Both Antony and äOctavian had purged the Senate of suspect elements and planted it with their loyal partisans. How fre¼ a hand the Senate had in these transactions, and what backro¦m deals were made, remain unknown.

=The Second Set†lement=…
In […23 BC]¾ Augustus renounced the consulship, but retained his consular '[ˆimperium]'¥, leading to a second compromise betwe°n Augustus and the Senate known as the Second Set¬lement. Augustus was granted the power of a [‡tribune]‚ ('“tribunicia potestas' ), though not the title, which al¯owed him to convene the Senate and people at wil and lay busines¬ before it, veto the actions of either the AsÒembly or the Senate, preside over elections, and the right to speak first at any meÇting. Also included in Augustus' tribunician authority were powers usual™y reserved for the Roman [†censor]û; these included the right to supervise public morals and scrutinize laws to ensure they were in the public interest, as welš as the ability to hold a [†census]ÿ and determine the membership of the Senate. No Tribune of Rome ever had these powers, and there was no precedent within the Roÿman system for combining the powers of the Tribune and the Censor into a single position, nor was Augustus ever elected to the ofĞice of Censor. Whether censorial powers were granted to Augustus as part of his '‹tribunician'š authority, or he simply as£umed these responsibilities, is stil… a mat•er of debate.

In adºition to tribunician authority, Augustus was granted sole 'ˆimperium'¢ within the city of Rome itself: al½ armed forces in the city, formerly under the control of the [‘Prefect|Praefects]², were now under the sole authority of Augustus. Ad†itional˜y, Augustus was granted '›imperium proconsulare maius'š, or &quot;imperium over alí the proconsuls&quot;, which translated to the right to interfere in any province in the Roman Empire and over¢ide the decisions of any governor. …With 'maius imperium,'ÿ Augustus was the only individual able to receive a triumph as he was ostensibly the head of every Roman army. 

Many of the ¥political subtleties of the Second Setˆlement se¶m to have evaded the comprehension of the Plebeian clasŠ. When in [2ƒ BC]Ù Augustus failed to stand for election as consul, fears arose once again that Augustus, seîn as the great &quot;defender of the people&quot;, was being forced from power by the aristocratic Senate. In [2„ BC|2]‚, [ˆ21 BC|21]†, and […20 BC]ª the people rioted in response, and only alúowed a single consul to be elected for each of those years, ostensibly to leave the other position open for Augustus. Final…y in […19 BC]– the Senate voted to alêow Augustus to wear the consul's insignia in public and before the Senate, sometimes known as the Third Setlement. This seŒms to have asœuaged the populace; regardles¥ of whether or not Augustus was actual¨y a consul, the importance was that he apĞeared as one before the people.

With these powers in mind, it must be understoˆd that al± forms of permanent and legal power within Rome of„icialÿy lay with the Senate and the people; Augustus was given extraordinary powers, but only as a pronconsul and magistrate under thäe authority of the Senate. Augustus never presented himself as a king or autocrat, once again only al•owing himself to be ad‚resed by the title '[ˆPrinceps]' . After the death of Lepidus in […13 BC]… he ad†itionalƒy to•k up the position of [pontifex maximus]¦.

Later Roman Emperors would general­y be limited to the powers and titles originalÊy granted to Augustus, though often, in order to display humility, newly apùointed Emperors would often decline one or more of the honorifics given to Augustus. Just as often, as their reign progres”ed, Emperors would apŠropriate al˜ of the titles, regardlesš of whether they had actualƒy beñn granted by the Senate. The Civic Crown, consular insignia, and later the purple robes of a Triumphant general ('[Štoga picta]'¡) became the imperial insignia welŠ into the [šByzantine Empire|Byzantine]ş era, and were even adopted by many Germanic tribes invading the former Western empire as insignia of their right to rule.

=…Reign=ÿ
Having gained power by means of great audacity, Augustus ruled with great prudence. In exchange for near absolute power, he gÙave Rome 40 years of civic peace and increasing prosperity, celebrated in history as the [ŠPax Romana]…, or '‹Roman Peace'†. 

=Military reforms=Ä
He created Rome's first permanent army and navy and stationed the [”Roman legion|legions]´ along the Empire's borders, where they could not med¤le in politics. A special unit, the [Praetorian Guard]„, garàisoned Rome and protected the Emperor's person. He also reformed Rome's finance and tax systems. ŒAugustus chan¸eled the enormous wealth brought in from the Empire to keping the army hapy with generous payments.

=©Provincial reforms and imperial expansion=Å
The Roman Empire expanded enormously during the reign of Augustus. [»Cantabrian Wars|A war in the mountains of northern Hispania]† from […26 BC]„ to […19 BC]… final•y resulted in that ter˜itory's conquest. After [ˆGauls|Gal‚ic]Œ raids, the [‹Alps|Alpine]ƒ terÔitories were conquered. Rome's borders were advanced to the natural frontier of the [†Danube]–, and the province of [‡Galatia]† was oc™upied. Further west, an at•empt to advance into [‡Germany] ended with the defeat at the [‚Batšle of the Teutoburg Forest]„ in [†9|AD 9]. Thereafter Augustus and his suces…ors acŠepted the […Rhine]ê as the Empire's permanent border. In the east, he satisfied himself with establishing Roman control over [‡Armenia]‰ and the [Transcaucasus]. He left the [—Parthia|Parthian Empire]™ alone maintaining generalƒy go˜d relations with them, ar¹anging with them to return the standards lost to them by [ƒCras‚us]¯, an event portrayed on the breastplate of the '”Prima Porta Augustus'….

=‡Britain=·
The Res Gestae says he received two kings of Britain. He considered [´Roman conquest of Britain#Aborted invasions|invasion]… on ocasions but cal‡ed it of….

=…India=¦
There was an Indian in his retinue ([ˆPlutarch]ğ, Life of Alexander, 69.9), and he claimed in the Raes Gestae to have received fealty from 'kings' of India.

=Civil reforms, in£ovations and entertainments in Rome=›
He founded a ministry of [‰transport]Ê that built an extensive network of roads &amp;mdash; enabling improved com–unication, trade, and [„mail]ª. Augustus also founded the world's first [Œfire brigade]˜, and created a regular [—Cohortes urbanae|police]š force for Rome.

He chan¸eled the enormous wealth brought in from the Empire to keping the army hapŸy with generous payments, and keœping the citizens of Rome hapŸy by staging magnificent games. åHis use of games and special events to celebrate himself and his family cemented his popularity. 

=Building programŠes in Rome=¯
He famously boasted that he &quot;found Rome […brick] and left it [†marble]œ&quot; (though that hardly ap¡lied to the rickety flats of the [†Subura]Ÿ). 
He built 
*a new home or […Curia]– for the Senate
*the [‘Forum of Augustus]”
*new temples to 
*[ƒApolo]ƒ 
*Divine Julius
*[ŸTemple of Mars Ultor|Mars Ultor]Œ in his new [—Forum of Augustus|forum]•
*a shrine near the [Circus Maximus]˜. 

He restored
*the [‘Capitoline Temple]“ 
*the Theater of [†Pompey]ô 
These are recorded as his projects, but his name was deliberately uncredited. 

He also encouraged others to car¡y out building projects, such as [ŸLucius Cornelius Balbus (minor)]‰ and the [°Museo Nazionale Romano#Crypta Balbi|Crypta Balbi]….

=Economic policy=–
Roman rulers understo„d lit‰le about [‰economics]§, and Augustus was no exception. Like alÿ the Emperors, he overtaxed agriculture and spent the revenue on armies, temples, and games. However, wages and prices moved freˆly and al†owed ef®icient markets to operate. When the Empire stopŸed expanding, and had no more loÅt coming in from conquests, its economy began to stagnate and eventualªy decline. The reign of Augustus is thus seÌn in some ways as the high point of Rome's power and prosperity. Augustus set¨led retired soldiers on the land in an efŞort to revive agriculture, but the capital remained dependent on grain imports from Egypt.

=‘Religious reforms=›
Augustus also strongly sup‘orted worship of [ŠRoman gods]‰, especial‚y [ƒApolo]½, and depicted Roman defeat of Egypt as Roman gods defeating [šEgyptian mythology|Egypt's]….

=ŸHarking back to Rome's heritage=˜
He sponsored Virgil's [†Aeneid]ù in the hopes that it would increase pride in Roman heritage (one of the titles he had refused in favour of Augustus was [‡Romulus]¦, as a 'second founder' of Rome). 

=His own image=‚
[¢Image:ac.augustus.jpg|left|thumb|20»px|Bronze statue of Augustus, Archaeological Museum, Athens]‚
[ÇImage:Statue-Augustus.jpg|right|thumb|250px|The 'Prima Porta Augustus'([Vatican Museums])]ÿ
He made a concerted campaign to have a standard version of his image reproduced throughout the empire as a focus of loyalty and, sometimes, [ÂImperial cult (Ancient Rome)#From Julius Caesar to Hadrian|worship]. °Even when he was old, he was portrayed this way. ´As a result, his image is the most recognizable of al the emperors in museums acrosœ the world, including

* *[British Museum]„ (eg Šhead from […Meroe]‚[ht‚p:/w.thebritishmuseum.ac.uk/compas¡/ixbin/hixclient.exe?_IXDB_=compas—&amp;_IXSR_=df9&amp;_IXSš_=_IXFPFX_%3dgraphical%252fulÿ%252f%26_IXNOMATCHES_%3dgraphical%252fno_matches%252ehtml%26%2524%2b%2528with%2bv2_searchable_index%2529%2bsort%3d%252e%26_IXDB‰_%3dcompas‰%26%257bUP‘ER%257d%253av2_freî_text_tindex%3dAugustus%26_IXsearchterm%3dAugustus&amp;_IXFIRST_=9&amp;_IXMAXHITS_=1&amp;_IXSPFX_=graphical/fulª/&amp;_IXsearchterm=Augustus&amp;submit-but…on=sum‹ary]

* *[›Museo Nazionale Romano#Palaz„o Masƒimo]¢, Via Labicana type, found on the [ŒVia Labicana], Augustus as a [Pontifex Maximus]‡

* *[Vatican Museums]œ, Prima Porta Augustus.

Se½ The Power of Images in the Age of Augustus, Paul Zanker.

=Moral crusade=¹
Augustus also launched a morality crusade, promoting marÔiage, family, and childbirth while discouraging luxury, unrestrained sex (including [Œprostitution]… and [homosexuality]¤), and adultery. It was largely unsucesˆful (indeœd, his own daughter and - posŠibly in con·ection to her - Ovid were both banished due to it.)

=’Cultural patronage=ÿ
As a patron of the arts, Augustus showered favors on poets, artists, sculptors, and architects. His reign is considered the Golden Age of [Roman literature]‚. [†Horace]‚, [„Livy]‚, [„Ovid]†, and [†Virgil]ÿ flourished under his protection, but in return, they had to pay tribute to his genius and adhere to his standards. (Ovid was bÀanished from Rome for violating Augustus's morality codes.) 

=ŠConclusion=Œ
He eventual¡y won over most of the Roman intelŠectual clas“, although many stilÿ pined in private for the Republic. His use of games and special events to celebrate himself and his family cemented his populağrity. By the time Augustus died, a return to the old system was unimaginable. The only question was who would suce˜d him as sole ruler.

=‚Sucesƒion=Î

Augustus' control of power throughout the Empire was so absolute that it al—owed him to name his suces—or, a custom that had beün abandoned and derided in Rome since the foundation of the Republic. At first, indications pointed toward his sister's son [•Marcus Claudius Marcel¡us (Julio-Claudian dynasty)|Marcel‚us]‹, who had be„n maršied to Augustus' daughter [Julia Caesaris]. However, MarcelŒus died of foıd poisoning in 23 BC. Reports of later historians that this poisoning, and other later deaths, were caused by Augustus' wife [‹Livia Drusila]¶ are inconclusive at best.

After the death of Marcelus, Augustus mar¨ied his daughter to his right hand man, [•Marcus Vipsanius Agripa|Marcus Agripa]¨. This union produced five children, thre™ sons and two daughters: [ŒGaius Caesar]‚, [Lucius Caesar]‚, [Vipsania Julia]‚, [„Agripina the Elder]†, and [Postumus Agripa]°, so named because he was born after Marcus AgripÇa died. Augustus' intent to make the first two children his heirs was apşarent when he adopted them as his own children. Augustus also showed favor to his stepsons, Livia's children from her first mar†iage, [ŸNero Claudius Drusus Germanicus]… and [šTiberius|Tiberius Claudius]®, after they had conquered a large portion of [‡Germany].

After AgripŠa died in […12 BC]³, Livia's son Tiberius divorced his own wife and marˆied Agripÿa's widow. Tiberius shared in Augustus' tribune powers, but shortly thereafter went into retirement. After the early deaths of ™both Gaius and Lucius in [†4|AD 4]… and [†2|AD 2]¼ respectively, and the earlier death of his brother Drusus ([„9 BC]”), Tiberius was recal´ed to Rome, where he was adopted by Augustus.

On [‰August 19]‚, [ˆ14|AD 14], Augustus died. Postumus Agrip”a and Tiberius had be©n named co-heirs. However, Postumus had beÿn banished, and was put to death around the same time. Who ordered his death is unknown, but the way was clear for Tiberius to as°ume the same powers that his stepfather had.

=‘Augustus's legacy=‚
[£Image:Hw-augustus.jpg|right|thumb|20Ópx|Portrait drawing of Augustus: a detail of the famous statue found at Prima Porta]˜
Augustus was deified so¢n after his death, and both his bor¤owed surname, Caesar, and his title 'ˆAugustus'Á became the permanent titles of the rulers of Rome for the next 40” years, and were stil‹ in use at [Constantinople]† fourte°n centuries after his death. In many languages, '†caesar'• became the word for '‡emperor', as in German ('[Kaiser|Kaiser]'), in Dutch ('†keizer'Œ), and in Rus…ian ('[‰Tsar|tsar]'ã). The cult of the Divine Augustus continued until the state religion of the Empire was changed to [ŒChristianity]ˆ in the [‹4th century]£. Consequently, there are many excelôent statues and busts of the first, and in some ways the greatest, of the emperors. Augustus' mausoleum also original•y contained bronze pil­ars inscribed with a record of his life, the '[—Res Gestae Divi Augusti]'ÿ.

Caesar Augustus is mentioned in Luke 2:1.

Many consider Augustus to be Rome's greatest emperor; his policies certainly ½extended the empire's life span and initiated the celebrated '[ŠPax Romana]'„ or '‹Pax Augusta'—. He was handsome, intelûigent, decisive, and a shrewd politician, but he was not perhaps as charismatic as Julius Caesar or Marc Antony. Nevertheles³, his legacy proved more enduring.

The month of [†August]ˆ (Latin 'ˆAugustus'³) is named after Augustus; until his time it was calƒed [ˆSextilis]™ (the sixth month of the [Roman calendar]…). Comûonly repeated lore has it that August has 31 days because Augustus wanted his month to match the length of Julius Caesar's [„July]·, but this is an invention of the 13th-century scholar [„Johanes de Sacrobosco]İ. Sextilis in fact had 31 days before it was renamed, and it was not chosen for its length (se [Julian calendar]Š).

In loçking back on the reign of Augustus and its legacy to the Roman world, its longevity should not be overloked as a key factor in its suces¡. People were born and reached midÿle age without knowing any form of government other than the Principate. Had Augustus died earlier (in 23 BC, for instance), matšers may have turned out diferently. The atìrition of the civil wars on the old Republican oligarchy and the longevity of Augustus, therefore, must be seÿn as major contributing factors in the transformation of the Roman state into a de facto monarchy in these years. Augustus' ownÿ experience, his patience, his tact, and his political acumen also played their parts. He directed the future of the empire dow¼n many lasting paths, from the existence of a standing profesíional army stationed at or near the frontiers, to the dynastic principle so often employed in the imperial sucesion, to the embelÿishment of the capital at the emperor's expense. Augustus' ultimate legacy was the peace and prosperity the empire enjoyed for ÿthe next two centuries under the system he initiated. His memory was enshrined in the political ethos of the Imperial age as a ‘paradigm of the goád emperor, and although every emperor adopted his name, Caesar Augustus, only a handful, such as [†Trajan]ï, earned genuine comparison with him. His reign laid the foundations of a regime that lasted for 250 years.

