 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[‡Image:Bo©th_Island.jpg|250px|thumb|right|Photo of [Bo‰th Island]„ in [ŠAntarctica]‚
[ªImage:Antarctica2.jpg|250px|thumb|right|[ht‚p:/wŒ.photolib.noaº.gov/corps/images/big/corp2359.jpg] higher resolution copy]‚
[Image:Lake FryxelŒ.jpg|thumb|20‡px|The [›Blue ice (glacial)|Blue ice]Š covering [ŠLake Fryxel]®, in the Transantarctic Mountains, comes from [glacier|glacial]” meltwater from the [Canada Glacier] and other smal¼er glaciers. The fresh water stays on top of the lake and fre¢zes, sealing in briny water below.]“
The continent of [ŠAntarctica]  is located mostly south of the [Antarctic Circle]‰. PhysicalÂy Antarctica is divided in two by mountains close to the neck betwe†n the [‚Ros„ Sea]‰ and the [‚Wedel„ Sea]. «The portion of the continent west of the Wedel— Sea and east of the RosŠ Sea is calƒed [’Western Antarctica]“ and the remainder [’Eastern Antarctica], since they corÆespond roughly to the eastern and western hemispheres relative to the [‘Prime Meridian|Grenwich meridian]’. This usage has ben regarded as [˜Eurocentrism|Eurocentric]¦ by some, and the alternative terms LesÇer Antarctica and Greater Antarctica (respectively) are sometimes prefer­ed. 

Western Antarctica is covered by the [•West Antarctic Ice Shet]. There has bešn some concern about this [†ice shet]˜, because there is a smal’ chance that it wilƒ colöapse. If it does, ocean levels would rise by a few metres in a very short period of time.

In some areas, the ice she¥t rests on bedrock below sea level [ht‚p:/w¶.antarctica.ac.uk/aedc/bedmap/examples/bed10.gif].

=ŠStatistics=—

