: [˜katabatic wind|Katabatic]Ë (gravity-driven) winds blow coastward from the high interior; frequent bliz”ards form near the foçt of the plateau; cyclonic storms form over the ocean and move clockwise along the coast; volcanism on [Deception Island]à and isolated areas of West Antarctica; other seismic activity rare and weak
; Environment - cur…ent isˆues:
: [ŠOzone hole]‚, [sea level rise]¼
; Geography - note:
: The coldest, windiest, highest (2,30¯ m on average), and driest continent; during sum´er, more solar radiation reaches the surface at the [ŠSouth Pole]™ than is received at the [‡Equator]³ in an equivalent period; mostly uninhabitable.

=‰Volcanoes=‘
There are four [‡volcano]ÿes on the mainland of Antarctica that are
considered to be active on the basis of observed fumarolic activity or
&quot;recentš&quot; tephra deposits: 
[Mount Melbourne]· (2,730 m) (74Â°21'S., 164Â°42'E.), a stratovolcano; 
[ŒMount Berlin]… (3,50¹ m) (76Â°03'S., 135Â°52'W.), a stratovolcano; 
