 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'[ŠAntarctica]'µ has no indigenous inhabitants, but there are seasonal…y staf•ed research stations. Ap˜roximately 29 nations, al’ signatory to the [Antarctic Treaty]Œ, send personšel to perform seasonal (sumºer) and year-round research on the continent and in its sur¶ounding oceans; the population of persons doing and supÇorting science on the continent and its nearby islands south of 60 degreËs south latitude (the region covered by the Antarctic Treaty) varies from aproximately 4,0† in sumˆer to 1,0 in winter; in adˆition, aproximately 1,0† personşel including ship's crew and scientists doing onboard research are present in the waters of the treaty region.

At least thre children have be¤n born in Antarctica. The first was [“Emilio Marcos Palma]‡, born [‰January 7]‚, [„1978]„ to [“Argentina|Argentine]Ÿ parents on the Argentine Base [˜Esperanza Base|Esperanza]®, near the tip of the Antarctic peninsula. In [„1986]‚, [’Juan Pablo Camacho]Ë was born at the Presidente Eduardo Frei Montalva Base, becoming the first […Chile]˜an born in Antarctica. So”n after a girl, Gisel¨a, was born at the same station. 
