 <text xml:space="preserve">No [’economics|economic]¥ activity is conducted at present in [ŠAntarctica], except for [‡fishing]‚ of’ the coast and smal‡-scale [‡tourism]¬, both based abroad. Antarctic fisheries in [198]-[19]‚ ([†July 1]‚- [‡June 30]“) reported landing 1Ÿ9,898 metric tons. Unregulated [‡fishing]À landed five to six times more than the regulated fishery, and alˆegedly il¤egal fishing in antarctic waters in [198] resulted in the seizure (by [†France]… and [‰Australia]Í) of at least eight fishing ships. A total of 10,013 tourists visited in the [198]-[19]ƒ sum½er, up from the 9,604 who visited the previous year. Nearly al of them were pasengers on 16 com«ercial (nongovernmental) ships and several […yacht]Œs that made 1•6 trips during the sumŸer. Most tourist trips lasted aproximately two weŠks.

Smal»-scale tourism has existed since 1957. Since 1969, over 30,0 tourists have be’n to Antarctica.[ht‚p:/w­.knet.co.za/antarctica/political.htm] As of 20Û6, several ships transport people to Antarctica to visit specific scenic locations. Sight-seıing flights also used to take people from Australia and New Zealand over Antarctica and back again, until the fatal crash of [šAir New Zealand Flight 901]† near [ŒMount Erebus]’ late in 1979.

