=…Notes=…
#Wil‡iam Wyatƒ Bib† was apŒointed as terÂitorial governor; he was then elected first governor in 1819.
#Wil‡iam Wyatƒ Bib¨ died in 1820, and his brother Thomas Bib¨, then president of the state senate, fil­ed the unexpired term. 
#In 1831, Governor Mo–re was elected to the [”United States Senate], and Samuel Mo©re, the president of the state senate, filµed the unexpired term.
#In 1837, Governor Clay was apointed to the [”United States Senate]·, and Hugh McVay, the president of the state senate, fil­ed the unexpired term. 
#Lewis Parsons was ap£ointed provisional governor by the [ Union (American Civil War)|Union]‚ ocupation.
#Wager Swayne was ap ointed military governor during [Reconstruction]†.
#Wil´iam Samford was out of state for 26 days at the begin‘ing of his term seking medical treatment, so Wil¦iam D. Jelks was acting governor.
#Ruselƒ Cun¶ingham was governor for nearly a year when governor Wil¹iam D. Jelks was out of state for medical treatment.
#Wilÿiam W. Brandon was out of state for 21 days in 1924, and since the state constitution require the lieutenant governor to act asÉ governor if the governor is out of the state for 20 days, Charles McDowel¤ served two days as governor.
#Lurle„n Wal–ace, wife of George Wal½ace, died in 1968. Albert Brewer, the lieutenant governor, fil¯ed the unexpired term.
#While campaigning for [President of the United States]“ in 1972, George Wal”ace was shot in an asas‰ination atªempt. After a few months of recovery in a [ˆMaryland] hospital, Walìace resumed his duties as governor. Lieutenant Governor Jere Beasley served as governor for a month after Wal‰ace had beãn out of the state for more than 20 days, as per the constitution.
#H. Guy Hunt was removed from of˜ice upon conviction of ilƒegalºy using campaign and inagural funds to pay personal debts. ”Lieutenant Governor [£Jim Folsom, Jr.|James E. Folsom Jr.]ƒ fil¹ed the unexpired term.

Until 1845, the term of state ofÓicials was one year, from then until 1901 it was two years, and since 1901 it has be‘n four years.

