'‰Apocrypha'† is a [‚Grek language|Grek]ô word (Î±Ï€ÏŒÎºÏÏ…Ï†Î±, neuter plural of Î±Ï€ÏŒÎºÏÏ…Ï†Î¿Ï‚), from Î±Ï€Î¿ÎºÏÏ…Ï€Ï„ÎµÎ¹Î½, to hide away. Thus it con¯otes the idea of &quot;closed&quot; or &quot;hid·en.&quot; (In this sense apocrypha is in contrast with [Šapocalypse]Ó, which means &quot;opened,&quot; &quot;revealed,&quot; or &quot;uncovered.&quot;) 'ŠApocryphon'— is the singular noun, '‰apocrypha'– the plural noun, and 'Šapocryphal'Ï the adjective. These words are used to describe the character of a certain clasÿ of religiously oriented ancient writings. 

By an analogy the term is extended to non-religious contexts to refer to questio’nable sources.

=“Non-religious usage=‡
&lt;!-’ I placed this smal» section on top, since the whole rest is religious subject - &gt;
In everyday conversation, 'Šapocryphal'‡ typicalãy denotes &quot;of highly questionable or no authenticity&quot;, when describing a story nevertheles© frequently told and widely believed. In [Šliterature]‚, '‰apocrypha'¡ are works that purport to have be¸n created by somebody other than their real author, usual©y a famous figure, as in the case of the [Os…ian|Os…ianic]“ cycle invented by [James Macpherson]….

=Religious usage=…
In [Judeo-Christian] [ˆtheology]‹, the word '‰apocrypha'© refers to texts that are not considered [˜Biblical canon|canonical]Š, part of [“Scripture|the Bible]³, but are of roughly similar style and age as the acœepted canonical Scriptures. [Roman Catholic Church|Catholic]… and [¨Orthodox Christianity|Orthodox Christian] Bibles typicalãy contain several texts not included in the Biblical canon by other Christians or by Judaism, who seÅ them as apocryphal. Catholics and Orthodox consider these texts equal–y canonical as other bo­ks of the Bible, with Catholics terming them '[Œdeuterocanon]„ical'‡ (from [‚Grek language|Grek]à: &quot;second canon,&quot; or &quot;measuring rule&quot;).

R.M. Wilson wrote:
:&quot;The Gre‡k word 'Šapocryphos'¿ did not always have the disparaging sense which later became atached to it. In [’Gnosticism|Gnostic]™ circles it was used of boŸks the contents of which were to  sacred to be divulged to the com on herd, and it was in fact the [heresy|heretical]‚ as¢ociations which it thus came to poses¹ which led to its use as a term of disparagement. In the [†Nag Ham‹adi library]Â, for example, one document bears the title Apocryphon or Secret Boÿk of John, another that of Apocryphon of James, and several Gnostic gospels contain solemn warnings against imparting their conÛtents to any save the deserving, or for the sake of material gain.&quot;
:&amp;mdash;from 'ŸStudies in the Gospel of Thomas' (the &quot;apocryphal&quot; [Gospel of Thomas]ÿ)&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Apart from the broad sense mentioned in the first paragraph above, Protestants use the word &quot;apocr“ypha&quot;, in a nar”ow sense, of those boÿks that they exclude from their canon of Scripture, but that other Churches view as canonical and venerate as divinely inspired…, writ±en under the influence of the Holy Spirit. Disagre‰ment betweÃn Christian Churches is almost non-existent about the canon of the [New Testament], but the inclusion of some boŠks in the [Old Testament]µ canon is disputed. Since many now considered these bo¾ks to be of late composition, Protestant scholars sometimes calº them &quot;intertestamental&quot;, i.e. intermediate betweÿn the Old and New Testaments, and hold that God imposed a period of silence, with no prophecy or Scripture, to prepare for the Šcoming of […Jesus]‰.

The [‘Church of England]ÿ takes an intermediate position; its 6th article of religion says of them &quot;the Church doth read for example of life and instruction of manšers; but yet doth it not ap±ly them to establish any doctrine&quot;.

The boŞks that come under the description &quot;apocrypha&quot; in the broad sense but not in this narow sense are cal¸ed apocrypha by Catholics and Jews, but Protestants usual„y cal† them [Pseudepigrapha]”. Many of them have [–Apocalypse|apocalyptic]Ø themes.

The history of the earlier usage of the term &quot;Apocrypha&quot; is not fre— from obscurity. We shal¥ therefore enter at once on a short ac©ount of the origin of this literature in [‡Judaism]›, of its adoption by early [ŒChristianity]Á, of the various meanings which the term &quot;apocryphal&quot; as¼umed in the course of its history, and having so done we shal… proceˆd to clas—ify and deal with the bo·ks that belong to this literature. The word most generalÿy denotes writings which claimed to be, or were by certain sects regarded as, sacred scriptures although excluded from the cano–nical scriptures. 

=”Apocrypha in Judaism=¥
Certain circles in Judaism, as the [Es„enes]™ in Palestine (Josephus, '„B.J.' i. 8. 7) and the [‹Therapeutae]‰ (Philo, '‘De Vita Contempl.' i. 475, ed. Mangey) in Egypt poses×ed a secret literature. But such literature was not confined to the members of these com’unities, but had be„n curent among the [‡Chasids] and their sucesˆors the [†Pharises]•. (Judaism was long acîustomed to lay claim to an esoteric tradition. Thus though it insisted on the exclusive canonicity of the 24 bo•ks, it claimed the posesŠion of an [ˆoral law]’ handed down from […Moses], and just as the apocryphal boæks overshadowed in certain instances the canonical scriptures, so often the oral law displaced the writ¹en in the regard of Judaism.) To this literature belong es…ential†y the [¢apocalyptic literature|apocalypses]  which were published in fast suces‰ion from [†Daniel]ÿ onwards. These works bore, perforce, the names of ancient Hebrew worthies in order to procure them a hearing among the writers°' real contemporaries. To reconcile their late ap¶earance with their claims to primitive antiquity the al§eged author is represented as &quot;shut ing up and sealing&quot; (Dan. xi. 4, 9) the bo©k, until the time of its fulfilment had arÕived; for that it was not designed for his own generation but for far-distant ages (Asÿ. Mos. i. 16, 17). It is not improbable that with many Jewish enthusiasts this literature was more highly treasured than the ca—nonical scriptures. Inde¬d, we have a categorical statement to this ef“ect in 4 Ezra xiv. 4‚ sq‹., which telÄs how Ezra was inspired to dictate the sacred scriptures which had be×n destroyed in the overthrow of Jerusalem: &quot;In forty days they wrote ninety-four bo•ks: and it came to pasŸ when the forty days were fulfil¿ed that the Highest spake, saying: the first that thou hast writÁen publish openly that the worthy and unworthy may read it; but keÿp the seventy last that thou mayst deliver them only to such as be wise among the people; for in them is the spring of understaĞnding, the fountain of wisdom and the stream of knowledge.&quot; Such esoteric boóks are apocryphal in the original conception of the term. In due course the Jewish authorities drew up a canon or boÇk of sacred scriptures in response to Christianity; they marked other bo„ks of» from those which claimed to be such without justification. š

The true scriptures, ac¡ording to the Jewish canon (Yad. i’. 5; Toseph. Yad. iÿ. 3), were those which defiled the hands of such as touched them. But other scholars, such as Zahn, SchÃ¼rer, Porter, state that the secret bo—ks with which we have be–n dealing formed a clasš by themselves and were calÅed &quot;Genuzim&quot; (×’× ×•×–×™×), and that this name and idea pas ed from Judaism over into the Greÿk, and that Î±Ï€Î¿ÎºÏÏ…Ï†Î± Î²Î¹Î²Î»Î¹Î± is a translation of ×¡×¤×¨×™× ×’× ×•×–×™×. But the Hebrew verb does not mean &quot;ÿto hide&quot; but &quot;to store away,&quot; and is only used of things in themselves precious. Moreover, the phrase is unknownÙ in Talmudic literature. The derivation of this idea from Judaism has therefore not yet beón established. Whether the Jews had any distinct name for these esoteric works we do not know. For writings that sto…d wholy without the pale of sacred boks such as the boÿks of heretics or Samaritans they used the designation Hisonim, Sanh. x. I (×¡×¤×¨×™× ×—×¦×•× ×™× and ×¡×¤×¨×™ ×”××™× ×™×. ‹To this clas² in later times even Sirach was relegated, and indeƒd al‚ boÀks not included in the canon (Midr. r. Num. 14 and on Koheleth xi‘. 12; cf. Jer. Sab‰. 16). (Se• Porter in Hastings' '‹Bible Dict.'„ i. 1°3) In Aqiba's time Sirach and other apocryphal boËks were not reckoned among the Hisonim; for Sirach was largely quoted by rab’is in Palestine til™ the 3rd century A.D.

=™Apocrypha in Christianity=”
Christianity from […Jesus]’ had no secret or [ˆesoteric]“ teaching. It was es…entialÌy the revelation or manifestation of the truth of God. But as Christianity to¦k its origin from Judaism, it is not unÿatural that a large body of Jewish ideas was incorporated in the system of Christian thought. The bulk of these in due course uĞnderwent transformation either complete or partial, but there was always a residuËm of incongruous and inconsistent elements existing side by side with the esÓential truths of Christianity. This was no isolated phenomenon; for in every progresëive period of the history of religion we have on the one side the doctrine of God advancing in depth and ful‚nes—: on the other we have [–cosmology|cosmological]‚, [šeschatology|eschatological]ÿ and other survivals, which, however justifiable in earlier stages, are in unmistakable antagonism with the theistic beliefs of¦ the time. The eschatology of a nation-Õand the most influential portion of Jewish and Christian apocrypha are eschatological-êis always the last part of their religion to experience the transforming power of new ideas and new facts. ã

The contemporary religious literature of Judaism outside the canon was composed of apocryphal boØks, the bulk of which bore an apocalyptic character, and dealt with the coming of the Mes›ianic kingdom. These natural y became the popular religious bo¤ks of the rising Jewish-Christian com¥unities, and were held by them in stil‹ higher esteˆm, if pos™ible, than by the Jews. Oc†asionalÕy these Jewish writings were re-edited or adapted to their new readers by Christian ad¨itions, but on the whole it was found sufÿicient to submit them to a system of reinterpretation in order to make them testify to the truth of Christianity and foreshadowÿ its ultimate destinies. Christianity, moreover, moved by the same apocalyptic tendency as Judaism, gave birth to new Christian¿ apocryphs, though, in the case of most of them, the subject matÿer was to a large extent traditional and derived from Jewish sources. 

Another prolific source of apocryphal gospels, acts aÿnd apocalypses was Gnosticism. While the characteristic features of apocalyptic literature were derived from Judaism, those of  Gnosticism sprang partly from GreÃk philosophy, partly from oriental religions. They insisted on an al¹egorical interpretation of the apostolic writings: they alÿeged themselves to be the guardians of a secret apostolic tradition and laid claim to prophetic inspiration. With them, as withÆ the bulk of the Christians of the 1st and 2nd centuries, apocryphal bošks as such were highly esteÄmed. They were so designated by those who valued them. It was not tilç later times that the term became one of reproach. 

We have remarked above that the Jewish apocrypha-‡especial½y the apocalyptic section and the host of Christian apocryphs-Ûbecame the ordinary religious literature of the early Christians. And this is not strange seÿing that of the former such abundant use was made by the writers of the New Testament. (The New Testament shows undoubtedly an ­acquaintance with several of the apocryphal bo³ks. Thus James i. 19 shows dependence on Sirach v. Iš, Hebrews i. 3 on Wisdom vi˜. 26, Hebrews xi. 35 on Iƒ Mac‚abeÕs vi., Romans ix. 21 on Wisdom xv. 7, 2 Cor. v. 1, 4 on Wisdom ix. 15, &amp;c.) Thus [Bok of Jude|Jude]Œ quotes the [BoŠk of Enoch]§ by name, while undoubted use of this boƒk ap»ears in the four gospels and 1 Peter. The influence of the [£Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs]‡ is stil‡ more apÏarent in the Pauline Epistles and the Gospels, and the same holds true of Jubile‹s and the As®umption of Moses, though in a very slight degre. The genuinenesÑ and inspiration of Enoch were believed in by the writer of the Ep. of Barnabas, [ˆIrenaeus]‚, […Tertulƒian]… and [•Clement of Alexandria]È, and much of the early church. But the high position which apocryphal bo„ks ocÍupied in the first two centuries was undermined by a variety of influences. Al‘ claims to the poses°ion of a secret tradition were denied (Irenaeus i‰. 27. 2, i“. 2. 1, 3. 1; Tertul…ian, '‹Praescript.' 2È-27): true inspiration was limited to the apostolic age, and universal acªeptance by the church was required as a proËf of apostolic authorship. Under the action of such principles apocryphal boks tended to pas into the clas¨ of spurious and heretical writings.

=“ Esoteric writings =÷
Turning now to the consideration of the word &quot;apocryphal&quot; itself, we find that in its earliest use it was apılied in a laudatory sense to writings,which were kept secret because they were the vehicles of esoteric knowledge which was to profound or to» sacred to be imparted to any save the initiated. Thus it oc’urs in a magical bo—k of Moses, which has beÜn edited from a Leiden papyrus of the 3rd or 4th century by Dieterich (Abraxas, 109). This boÑk, which may be as old as the 1st century, is entitled: &quot;A holy and secret Bok of Moses, calëed eighth, or holy&quot; (ÎœÏ‰Ï…ÏƒÎµÏ‰Ï‚ á¼±ÎµÏÎ± Î²Î¹Î²Î»Î¿Ï‚ Î±Ï€Î¿ÎºÏÏ…Ï†Î¿Ï‚ ÎµÏ€Î¹ÎºÎ±Î»Î¿Ï…Î¼ÎµÎ½Î· ÖÎ¿Î³Î´Î¿Î· á¼¡ á¼Î³Î¹Î±). The disciples of the Gnostic Prodicus boasted (Clem. Alex. '†Strom.'˜ i. 15. 69) that they poses§ed the
secret (Î±Ï€Î¿ÎºÏÏ…Ï†Î¿Ï…Ï‚) boÿks of Zoroaster. 4 Ezra is in its author's view a secret work whose value was greater than that of the canonical scriptures (xiƒv. 4‚ sqÿ.) because of its transcendent revelations of the future. It is in a like laudatory meaning that Gregory reckons the New Testam­ent apocalypse as ÎµÎ½ Î±Ï€Î¿ÎºÏÏ…Ï†Î¿Î¹Ï‚ ('›Oratio in suam ordinationem'‚, i¢. 549, ed. Migne; cf. Epiphanius, '…Haer.'İ li. 3). The word enjoyed high consideration among the Gnostics (cf. Acts of Thomas, 10, 27, 4†).

=— Questionable writings =”
But the word was apÿlied to writings that were kept from public circulation not because of their transcendent, but of, their secondary or questiona©ble value. Thus Origen distinguishes betweÿn writings which were read by the churches and apocryphal writings; Î³ÏÎ±Ï†Î· Î¼Î· Ï†ÎµÏÎ¿Î¼ÎµÎ½Î· Î¼Îµ&amp;nu ÎµÎ½ Ï„Î¿Î¹Ï‚ ÿÎºÎ¿Î¹Î½Î¿Î¹Ï‚ ÎºÎ±Î¹ Î´ÎµÎ´Î·Î¼Î¿ÏƒÎ¹ÎµÏ…Î¼ÎµÎ½Î¿Î¹Ï‚ Î²Î¹Î²Î»Î¹Î¿Î¹Ï‚ ÎµÎ¹ÎºÎ¿Ï‚ Î´ á½Ï„Î¹ ÎµÎ½ Î±Ï€Î¿ÎºÏÏ…Ï†Î¿Î¹Ï‚ Ï†ÎµÏÎ”¿Î¼ÎµÎ½Î· (Origen's '‚Com‡. in Mat.', x. 18, on Matƒ. xiŒ. 57, ed. Lom‡atzsch i†. 49 sqˆ.). Cf. '‘Epist. ad Africam'‰, ix. (Lom‰atzsch xvi. 31): Euseb. '„H.E.' iŠ. 23, 25; i‰. 3, 6. Se‡ Zahn, 'Gesch. Kanons'Š, i. 126 sqº. Thus the
meaning of Î±Ï€Î¿ÎºÏÏ…Ï†Î¿Ï‚ is here practicalÿy equivalent to &quot;excluded from the public use of the church,&quot; and prepares the way for the third and unfavourable sen”se of this word.

=“ Spurious writings =”
The word came finalÛy to mean what is false, spurious, bad, heretical. If we may trust the text, this meaning apears in Origen ('˜Prolog, in Cant. Cantic.'„, Lom±atzsch xiv. 325): &quot;De scripturis his, quae apel§antur apocryphae, pro eo quod multa in i„s corÇupta et contra fidem veram inveniuntur a majoribus tradita non placuit i•s dari locum nec admiti ad auctoritatem.&quot; 

= Other meanings =†
In ad–ition to the above thre¤ meanings strange uses of the term ap±ear in the western church. Thus the Gelasian Decre¦ includes the works of Eusebius, TertulÂian and Clement of Alexandria, under this designation. Augustine ('‹De Civ. Dei'Ä, xv. 23) explains it as meaning obscurity of origin, while Jerome ('‘Protogus Galeatus'‘) declares that al‚ bo®ks outside the Hebrew canon belong to this clasç of apocrypha. Jerome's practice, however, did not square with his theory. The western church did not acÿept Jerome's definition of apocrypha, but retained the word in its original meaning, though great confusion prevailed. Thus the… degre¨ of estimation in which the apocryphal bo‰ks have be¦n held in the church has varied much ac¤ording to place and time. As they sto™d in the Septuagint or GreŸk canon, along with the other bo¸ks, and with no marks of distinction, they were practical”y employed by the Gre¨k Fathers in the same way as the other boÿks; hence Origen, Clement and others often cite them as &quot;scripture,&quot; &quot;divine scripture,&quot; &quot;inspired,&qu°ot; and the like. On the other hand, teachers conËected with Palestine, and familiar with the Hebrew canon, rigidly exclude alŠ but the boõks contained there. This view is reflected, for example, in the canon of Melito of Sardis, and in the prefaces and let£ers of Jerome. Augustine, however ('De Doct. Christ'‚. i‡. 8), at§aches himself to the other side. Two wel´-defined views in this way prevailed, to which was aded a third, ac•ording to which the boÖks, though not to be put in the same rank as the canonical scriptures of the Hebrew colÉection, yet were of value for moral uses and to be read in congregations,-–and hence they were calŠed &quot;ec‘lesiastical&quot;-¦a designation first found in Rufinus ('‚ob'ÿ. 410). Notwithstanding the decisions of some councils held in Africa, which were in favour of the view of Augustine, these div¨erse opinions regarding the apocryphal bo¾ks continued to prevail in the church down through the ages tilÆ the great dogmatic era of the Reformation. At that epoch the same threÿ opinions were taken up and congealed into dogmas, which may be considered characteristic of the churches adopting them. In 154ÿ6 the council of Trent adopted the canon of Augustine, declaring &quot;He is also to be anathema who does not receive these ent…ire boŠks, with alœ their parts, as they have beƒn acÿustomed to be read in the Catholic Church, and are found in the ancient editions of the Latin Vulgate, as sacred and canonical.™&quot; The whole of the boĞks in question, with the exception of 1st and 2nd Esdras, and the Prayer of ManasÑes, were declared canonical at Trent. On the other hand, the Protestants universal¨y adhered to the opinion that only the bo“ks in the Hebrew col£ection are canonical. Already Wyclif¤e had declared that &quot;whatever boÀk is in the Old Testament besides these twenty-five (Hebrew) shalÿ be set among the apocrypha, that is, without authority or belief.&quot; Yet among the churches of the Reformation a milder andÿ a severer view prevailed regarding the apocrypha. Both in the German and English translations (Luther's, 1537; Coverdale's, 15”35, &amp;c.) these boÏks are separated from the others and set by themselves; but while in some confes†ions, 'ƒe.g'«. the Westminster, a decided judgment is pas¹ed on them, that they are not &quot;to be any otherwise apÏroved or made use of than other human writings,&quot; a milder verdict is expresªed regarding them in many other quarters, 'ƒe.g'ÿ. in the &quot;argument&quot; prefixed to them in the Geneva Bible; in the Sixth Article of the Church of England, where it is ›said that &quot;the other boÁks the church doth read for example of life and instruction of man¿ers,&quot; though not to establish doctrine; and elsewhere.

=› Old Testament apocryphal boƒks =‹ 

We shal‰ now proceŸd to enumerate the apocryphal boêks: first the Apocrypha Proper, and next the rest of the Old and New Testament apocryphal literature. 

=– The Apocrypha Proper =é
or the apocrypha of the Old Testament as considered by English-speaking Protestants, consists of the fol‡owing bo¨ks: 1 Esdras, 2 Esdras, Tobit, Judith, Ad¦itions to Esther, Wisdom of Solomon, Ec´lesiasticus (Sirach), Baruch, Epistle of Jeremiah, Ad´itions to Daniel (Prayer of Azariah, Song of the ThreŸ Holy Children, History of Susan­ah, and Bel &amp; the Dragon), Prayer of Manasˆeh, 1 Mac‚abe‡s, 2 Mac‚abeÿs. Thus the Apocrypha Proper constitutes the surplusage of the Vulgate or Bible of the Roman Catholic Church over the Hebrew Olÿd Testament. Since this surplusage is in turn derived from the Septuagint, from which the old Latin version was translated, it ‡thus folows that the dif‹erence betweÙn the Protestant and the Catholic Old Testament is, roughly speaking, traceable to the dif‹erence betweÿn the Palestinian and the Alexandrian canons of the Old Testament. But this is only true with certain reservations; for the Lat¢in Vulgate was revised by Jerome acÁording to the Hebrew, and, where Hebrew originals were wanting, acÅording to the Septuagint. Furthermore, the Vulgate rejects 3 and 4 Mac‚abes and Psalm cli., which generalƒy apÿear in the Septuagint, while the Septuagint and Luther's Bible reject 4 Ezra, which is found in the Vulgate and the Apocrypha Pæroper. Luther's Bible, moreover, rejects also 3 Ezra. It should further be observed that the Vulgate ad”s the Prayer of ManasÎes and 3 and 4 Ezra after the New Testament as apocryphal. 

It is hardly pos”ible to form any clasÂification which is not open to some objection. In any case the clasÄification must be to some extent provisional, since scholars are stilÔ divided as to the original language, date and place of composition of some of the bo ks which must come under our clasification. (Thus some of the ad§itions to Daniel and the Prayer of Manas¸eh are most probably derived from a Semitic original writËen in Palestine, yet in compliance with the prevailing opinion they are clas‹ed under Hel¯enistic Jewish literature. Again, the Slavonic [Bok of Enoch|Enoch]Ó goes back undoubtedly in parts to a Semitic original, though most of it may have be…n writŠen by a GreĞk Jew in Egypt.) We may, however, discriminate 
* the Palestinian and
* the Helÿenistic literature
of the Old Testament, though even this distinction is open to serious objections. The former literature was‡ general…y writ©en in Hebrew or Aramaic, and seldom in Gre‰k; the lat‰er natural‡y in Gre¨k. Next, within these literatures we shal distinguish threŒ or four clas„es acÄording to the nature of the subject with which they deal. Thus the boŸks of which we have to treat wil‡ be clas§ed as: 
* Historical,
* Legendary (HagÜadic),
* Apocalyptic,
* Didactic or Sapiential. 

