[®Martin Luther (religious leader)|Martin Luther] considered the [Epistle of James]ÿ apocryphal, because he highly doubted its authorship by any of the several New Testament figures named James, and because it cšontains a statement that se£med to contradict his teachings of [°Salvation#Christian views of salvation|Salvation]Ó by faith alone: &quot;Faith without works is dead&quot; (2:26). He had a similar feling about the [–Epistle to the Hebrews]†, the [Epistle of Jude]‰ and the [Bošk of Revelation|Revelation]œ, and relegated those four boŠks to an apšendix in his Bible. Later [‰Lutherans]‘ included these boˆks as fulµ parts in their New Testament, but kept them behind alŒ the other bo¬ks. The Lutheran New Testament (at least in [–German language|German]‹) is thus ar‘anged slightly difËerently from that of most other Churches.

