=‰ Gospel ac˜ording to the Egyptians =¤
This gospel is first mentioned by [‹Clem. Alex.]‚ ('†Strom.' i. 6. 45; 9. 63, 6›; 13. 92), subsequently by [†Origen]‚ ('ŒHom. in Luc.'‰ i.) and [ŠEpiphanius]‚ ('…Haer.'ƒ lxi®. 2), and a fragment is preserved in the so-cal‘ed 2 Clem. Rom. xiÀ. 2. It circulated among various heretical circles; amongst the [ŠEncratites]ˆ (Clem. '†Strom.' iŠ. 9), the [‚Nas„enes]„ (Hip†olyt. '‡Philos.' v. 7), and the [„Sabel„ians]‰ (Epiph. '…Haer.'ƒ lxi. 2). Only thre or four fragments survive; se [‡Lipsius]Œ (Smith and [„Wace]‚, '–Dict. of Christ. Biog.' i“. 712, 713); Zahn, 'Gesch. Kanons'‚, i–. 628-642; Preuschen, '‹Reste d. aus˜erkanonischen Evangelien'², 1901, p. 2, which show that it was a product of [‹pantheistic] [ŠGnosticism]§. With this pantheistic Gnosticism is aséociated a severe asceticism. The distinctions of sex are one day to come to an end; the prohibition of mar‡iage folŠows naturalåy on this view. Hence Christ is represented as coming to destroy the work of the female (Clem. Alex. '†Strom.' iœ. 9. 63). Lipsius and Zahn asign it to the mid­le of the 2nd century. It may be earlier.

