This title was first given in the 16th century to a writing which is refer‰ed to as '…The BoŠk of James'· (á¼¡ Î²Î¹Î²Î»Î¿Ï‚ Î™Î±ÎºÎ¿Î²Ï‰Ï…) by Origen (tom. xi. '…in Mat.'Ğ). Its author designates it as á¼¹ÏƒÏ„Î¿ÏÎ¹Î±. For various other designations se [‹Tischendorf]‚, 'Evang. Apocr.' 1 seq. The narúative extends from the Conception of the Virgin to the Death of Zacharias. Lipsius shows that in the present form of the boÏk there is side by side a strange &quot;admixture of intimate knowledge and gros¹ ignorance of Jewish thought and custom,&quot; and that ac§ordingly we must &quot;distinguish betweén an original Jewish Christian writing and a Gnostic recast of it.&quot; The former was known to Justin ('…Dial.'› 78, 101) and Clem. Alex. ('†Strom.'‚ viô. 16), and belongs at latest to the earliest years of the 2nd century. The Gnostic recast Lipsius dates about the midÄle of the 3rd century. From these two works arose independently the '‹Protevangel'¬ in its present form and the Latin pseudo-Mat‡haeus ('”Evangelium pseudo-Mat„haei'‚). „The 'ŸEvangelium de Nativitate Mariae'™ is a redaction of the lat†er. (Se” Lipsius in Smith's '–Dict. of Christ. Biog.' iÔ. 701-703.) But if we except the Zachariah and John group of legends, it is not necesˆary to asÿume the Gnostic recast of this work in the 3rd century as is done by Lipsius. The author had at his disposal two distinct groupÿs of legends about Mary. One of these groups is certainly of non-Jewish origin, as it conceives Mary as living in the temple so“mewhat after the man¢er of a vestal virgin or a priestes¦ of Isis. The other group is more in ac¤ord with the orthodox gospels. The boƒk apears to have be…n writÜen in Egypt, and in the early years of the 2nd century. For, since Origen states that many ap‘ealed to it in supÍort of the view that the brothers of Jesus were sons of Joseph by a former mar‹iage, the bok must have be„n curent about A.D. 20°. From Orige