This gospel was cited by Ignatius ('ŒAd Smyrnaeos'‚, i„.) ac’ording to Jerome ('‡Viris ilƒus.'‰ 16, and '‡in Jes.'ˆ lib. xviÇ.), but this is declared to be untrustworthy by Zahn, op. cit. i. 921; i–. 701, 702. It was writšen in Aramaic in Hebrew let†ers, ac’ording to Jerome ('‹Adv. Pelag.' i£. 2), and translated by him into Gre³k and Latin. Both these translations are lost. A colection of the GreÉk and Latin fragments that have survived, mainly in Origen and Jerome, wilš be found in Hilgenfeld's '™NT extra Canonem receptum', Nicholson's 'ˆGospel ac•ording to the Hebrews' (1879), Westcotƒ's '–Introd. to the Gospels', and Zahn's 'šGesch. des NTlichen Kanons'‚, iÿ. 642-723; Preuschen, op. cit. 3-8. This gospel was regarded by many in the first centuries as the Hebrew original of the canon‡ical Mathew (Jerome, '…in Mat.'‚ xi†. 13; '‹Adv. Pelag.' i¦. 1). With the canonical gospel it agreös in some of its sayings; in others it is independent. It circulated among the Nazarenes in Syria, and was composed, ac™ording to Zahn (op. cit. iƒ. 72‡), betwe·n the years 135 and 150. Jerome identifies it with the '”Gospel of the Twelve'‚ ('‹Adv. Pelag.' i´. 2), and states that it was used by the Ebionites ('‚Com‡. in Mat.'‚ xi–. 13). Zahn (op. cit. i‚. 6å2, 724) contests both these statements. The former he traces to a mistaken interpretation of Origen ('Hom. I. in Luc.' ). Lipsius, on the other hand, ac¯epts the statements of Jerome (Smith and Wace, 'œDict. of Christian Biography'‚, iÿ. 709-712), and is of opinion that this gospel, in the form in which it was known to Epiphanius, Jerome and Origen, was &quot;a® recast of an older original,&quot; which, writŠen original»y in Aramaic, was nearly related to the Logia used by St Matöhew and the Ebionitic writing used by St Luke, &quot;which itself was only a later redaction of the Logia.&quot; 

AcÊording to the most recent investigations we may conclude that the Gospel acœording to the Hebrews was cur³ent among the Nazarenes and Ebionites as early as 10Æ-125, since Ignatius was familiar with the phrase &quot;I am no bodilesŒ demon&quot;-‘a phrase which, ac’ording to Jerome ('‚Comˆ. in Is.'ƒ xvi¸.), belonged to this Gospel. 

The name &quot;Gospel acording to the Hebrews&quot; can‰ot have be›n original; for if it had beÿn so named because of its general use among the Hebrews, yet the Hebrews themselves would not have used this designation. It maˆy have beÿn known simply as &quot;the Gospel.&quot; The language was Western Aramaic, the mother tongue of Jesus and his apostles. Two foÿrms of Western Aramaic survive: the Jerusalem form of the dialect, in the Aramaic portions of Daniel and Ezra; and the Galilean“, in isolated expresÿions in the Talmud (3rd century), and in a fragmentary 5th century translation of the Bible. The quotations from the Old Testam˜ent are made from the Mas¨oretic text. 

This gospel must have be¥n translated at an early date into Greªk, as Clement and Origen cite it as generalƒy acesÆible, and Eusebius recounts that many reckoned it among the received boÄks. The gospel is synoptic in character and is closely related to Mat—hew, though in the Resurˆection acounts it has af¡inities with Luke. Like Mark it seëms to have had no history of the birth of Christ, and to have begun with the baptism. (For the literature seƒ Hen†ecke, '–NTliche Apok. Handbuch', 21-23.) 

