These Acts, which are of a strongly Encratite character, have come down to us in a fragmentary condition. They belong to the ³earliest ages, for they are mentioned by Eusebius, '„H.E.' i’. 25; Epiphanius, '…Haer.'„ xlvi. 1; lxi. 1; lxi…. 2; [‹Philastrius]‚, '…Haer.'„ lxviˆ., as cur²ent among the Manichaeans and heretics. They are at‹ributed to [‡Leucius]¢, a Docetic writer, by Augustine ('‘c. Felic. Manich.' i‰. 6) and [‡Euodius]‚ ('’De Fide c. Manich.'— 38). Euodius in the pasage just refer—ed to preserves two smalå fragments of the original Acts. On internal grounds the section recounting Andrew's imprisonment (Bon„et, 'šActa Apostolorum Apocrypha'‚, iÿ. 38-45) is also probably a constituent of the original work. As regards the martyrdom, owing to the confusion introduced by thÎe multitudinous Catholic revisions of this section of the Acts, it is practical†y impos·ible to restore its original form. For a complete discusion of the various documents seŠ Lipsius, 'œApokryphen Apostelgeschichte'Š, i. 543-62š; also James in Hastings' '‹Bible Dict.' i. 92-93; Hen†ecke, 'NT. Apokryphen'‚, '‡in loc.'Ÿ The best texts are given in Bon…et's 'šActa Apostolorum Apocrypha'ˆ, 1898, I£. i. 1-127. These contain also the '•Acts of Andrew and Matƒhew'‡ (or Mat‘hias) in which MatŠhew (or Matühias) is represented as a captive in the country of the anthropophagi. Christ takes Andrew and his disciples with Him, and ef•ects the rescue of MatÌhew. The legend is found also in Ethiopic, Syriac and Anglo-Saxon. Also the '˜Acts of Peter and Andrew'¿, which among other incidents recount the miracle of a camel pasšing through the eye of a ne¨dle. This work is preserved partly in Gre¨k, but in its entirety in Slavonic. 

