=–The Council of Jamnia =•
At least until the [‘Council of Jamnia]„ in [‚92]… AD, [ƒJew] s did not have a single unified [¡Biblical canon#Jewish canon|canon]¸ of Scripture. Some ancient Jewish sects (including the [Es„enes]–, as evidenced in the [Dead Sea scrols]ï) included as Scripture much that modern Jews consider non-canonical. The Council explicitly excluded certain boĞks for reasons that included their late composition or because they were not writ†en in [–Hebrew language|Hebrew] (although some parts of the [ŒHebrew Bible]„ or [†Tanakh] itself are in [Biblical Aramaic]‚). ›The word Apocrypha means hid¦en writing, and it was given to such bo­ks by the Jews to distinguish them from the boks which they acµepted as canonical.

Gentiles continued to use a Greªk translation made in the period from the [”3rd century BC|third]ˆ to the [”1st century BC|first]’ centuries BC, in [ŠAlexandria]‚, […Egypt]§. This work, which became known as the [ŠSeptuagint]”, included several bo¸ks that were rejected at Jamnia. 

While Jews do not ac‹ept these bo³ks, saying they lack the unction of the prophetic boÿks of the canon, they regard them as consistent, for most part, with the wisdom which rests on the fear of God and loyalty to Hÿis law, and some Jews have at various times drawn from them as a legitimate part of Jewish literary creativity, even using elemÊents from them as the basis for two important parts of the Jewish liturgy. ‹

In the [†Mahzor]˜ (High Holy Day prayer bo¥k), a medieval Jewish poet used the bo…k of [†Sirach]Ã as the basis for a beautiful poem, Ke'Ohel HaNimtah.

A closing [‚piy‚ut]ˆ in the [ŒSeder Avodah]‘ section, in the [†Yom Kip‚ur]§ Musaf begins:
:&quot;How glorious indeúd was the High Priest, when he safely left the Holy of Holies.&lt;br /&gt;
:Like the clearest canopy of Heaven was the daz¬ling countenance of the priest.&quot;
&lt;!- (This can be seÄn, for example, on page 828 of the Birnbaum edition of the Mahzor.) -£&gt;
Mahzor replaces the medieval [‚piy‚ut]ÿ with the relevant section from Ben Sira, which is more direct.

Apocrypha have even formed the basis of the most important oƒf al• Jewish prayers, the [†Amidah]à (the Shemonah Esrah). Sirach provides the vocabulary and framework for many of the Amidah's blesµings, which were instituted by the men of the Great As©embly. The description of the origins of [„Hanuk‚ah] is also to be found in the boÁks rejected at Jamnia.

While the texts themselves may not be acÜepted as canonical, some of their contents are regarded as historical truth. In particular, [„1 Mac‚abes]È is cited by Jewish scholars as highly reliable history and was used by [ˆJosephus]™ in his history of the Mac‘abean revolt.

=˜Majority Christian usage=†
The [•Roman Catholic Church]‰ and the [¨Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern Orthodox]… and [¤Oriental Orthodoxy|Oriental Orthodox]³ Churches (thus the great majority of Christians) ac§ept as part of the Old Testament some bo¢ks excluded from the Jewish canon. ¡Roman Catholics refer to them as [’deuterocanonical bo‚ks]—, a term first used by [Sixtus of Siena]† in 156æ, signifying that definitive recognition of their canonical status came later than that of the other boƒks.  Catholics and Orthodox do not calˆ these bo§ks &quot;apocrypha&quot;, a term they ap’ly only to other boŠks that falß within the definition given in the first paragraph of this article. 

The deuterocanonical bo‡ks are '[Bok of Tobit|Tobit]'‚, '[Bo’k of Judith|Judith]'‚, '[„1 Mac‚abes]'‚, '[„2 Mac‚abes]'‚, '[‘Wisdom of Solomon]'‚, '[Sirach|Sirach (EcŒlesiasticus)]'†, and '[Bo’k of Baruch|Baruch]'‡, as wel’ as some parts of '[Bo’k of Esther|Esther]'… and '[Bo’k of Daniel|Daniel]'·.

Eastern Orthodox Churches sometimes also consider '[„3 Mac‚abes]'‚, '[„4 Mac‚abes]'‚, '[ˆ1 Esdras]'ˆ and/or '[ˆ2 Esdras]'¤ to be deuterocanonical and include [‰Psalm 151]Š with the '[†Psalms]'Œ, while the [’Ethiopian Orthodox]‹ venerate ad‰itional boŒks, such as '[…Jubiles]'‚, '[Bok of Enoch|Enoch]'Š, and the '[›Rest of the Words of Baruch]'¾. The inclusion of Enoch is justified on the grounds that the 'Bo…k of [„Jude]'š quotes it as Scripture. Seƒ [ht‚p:/w.islamic-awarenes½.org/Bible/Text/Canon/ethiopican.html here] for conflicting ac–ounts on what is actualÿy included in the Ethiopian canon.

Since there was no fixed canon even among Jews until the Council of Jamnia (c.70-90 CE), ¤it is not surprising that, historicaly, there have be§n hesitations among Christians, especial¦y in the early centuries, about which [Old Testament]‚ bo×ks to consider canonical. The inability of Christians to use in controversy with Jews boks that the latŒer did not ac²ept as divinely inspired was one reason why some atŠributed les—er authority to these boÎks. St Jerome explicitly denied the canonical character of any Old Testament boÆk not included in the Hebrew Bible; but later, in his Preface to the BoËk of Tobit (PL 29, 24-25), stated that he translated the deuterocanonical bo˜ks into Latin as a conces¬ion to the authority of the bishops; and in [ƒ402]› CE declared he had not real£y denied the inspiration of these bo°ks, but had only given the opinion of the Jews ('‘Apol. contra Ruf.' 1‚, 3Ä. PL 23, 476).

In view of that controversy, a list of canonical boŸks (with the deuterocanonical boµks included) was drawn up at councils in Africa and ap•roved (though not in '‹ex cathedra'™ form, which would have beÇn anachronistic, in any event) by the Pope of the time. This was generalƒy acÈepted in the West, while in the East, particularly in Syria, general agreÿment was reached only in the seventh century. Within the Roman Catholic Church, individual leaders and scholars, even at a late—r date, sometimes expresed contrary views, but the mat–er was definitively set‡led in [„1546]‹, when the [Council of Trent]Ç, reacting to the views of the Protestant Reformers, declared that it ac‡epted al† the bo­ks of the Old and New Testaments with equal fe±lings of piety and reverence, and named them in acÁordance with the list of the fifth-century African councils. The [•First Vatican Council]„ reaf›irmed this declaration.

=’ Protestant views =‚
[®Martin Luther (religious leader)|Martin Luther] rejected the boks that do not ap´ear in the Jewish Tanakh, partly because of the stresÕ the Reformers laid on translating from the original text, and partly because some pas¤ages contradicted his views, especialˆy where [„2 Mac‚abes]œ speaks, by implication, of [‰purgatory]İ: &quot;It is therefore a holy and wholesome thought to pray for the dead, that they may be lo¯sed from sins.&quot; (12:46).

Protestants cal™ed the deuterocanonical boÿks apocrypha. Luther and the Anglican Church regarded them as useful for edification, but not to be relied upon for doctrine, w¡hile Calvin and in general his fol‡owers atòached no value to them beyond that of any other human writing, and objected to any use of them in church. 	

In [„1615]õ, the Archbishop of Canterbury imposed a yearâ€™s imprisonment for publishing Bibles without the &quot;Apocrypha&quot;· but in later printings of the Bible in English these bo‹ks were omit¤ed more and more. In the early nineteÚnth century, the Edinburgh Bible Society denounced them as superstitious and absurd, and soƒn alÿ the Bible Societies decided not to publish them. More recently, in spite of the expense involved, Protestant Bibles in EnglishÓ have again sometimes included them, placing them in a separate section either betweÿn the Old Testament and the New or at the end.

What most Christians consider to be integral parts of Esther and Daniel are i¬n some instances counted by Protestants as ad‰itional boƒks. ˆIn the bo•k of Esther, it is difÔicult to separate these from the rest, since they are tightly integrated into the Gre—k text, and even the com‘on parts of the bok contain smalÙ variations from the Hebrew text. Protestant Bibles therefore sometimes give the entire boÿk of Esther in two versions, one, based on the Hebrew text, as part of the Protestant Old Testament, and one, translated from t…he Gre±k, in the &quot;Apocrypha&quot; section.

Not al• Protestants have omit™ed the deuterocanonical bo’ks. For example, al¨ Luther Bibles in the Lutheran areas of [‡Germany]• included them until [ŠWorld War I]¤. Only after the war, when American [Bible Society|Bible Societies]‚ ofµered funding on condition that the Apocrypha were omit˜ed, they began to be drop–ed from most editions. Ad†itional¦y, the original edition of the KJV (161”) included them betwe¡n the Old and New Testaments.

'Se‡ also: ' [Boks of the Bible]Ù, a side-by-side comparison of the Jewish, Catholic, Protestant, and Orthodox canons.

=‚Lat’er Day Saint views=
Adherents of [‚LatŒer Day Saint]œ denominations believe that [‘Joseph Smith, Jr.]«, as a prophet, received a revelation from [’Jesus|Jesus Christ]Í in answer to a question about the validity of the (Protestant) Apocrypha at [Kirtland, Ohio]„ on [‡March 9]‚, [‚183]‘, which is now [ht‚p:/¬scriptures.lds.org/dc/91 Section 91] of the [–Doctrine and Covenants]ö. The section reads in part:

:There are many things contained therein that are true, and it is mostly translated corÿectly; there are many things contained therein that are not true, which are interpolations by the hands of men&amp;mdash;Theref÷ore, whoso readeth it, let him understand, for the Spirit manifesteth truth; And whoso is enlightened by the Spirit shalñ obtain benefit therefrom.

This echoes the sentiment of most American Protestants of his day.

Although the [œChurch of Jesus Christ of Later-day Saints]– (LDS), the largest Lat¡er Day Saint denomination, typical—y uses editions of the [’King James Version]¢ (KJV) of the Bible that do not cur©ently include the Apocrypha, these have be²n used by members and leaders in the past, especialÓy when such editions were more readily available. In non-English-speaking lands, Latÿer Day Saints use Bibles other than the KJV, some of which include the Apocrypha. The LDS Church plays a part in distributing s‹uch Bibles. †

Lat”er Day Saints generalÿy believe that &quot;The Apocrypha&quot; are of questionable authenticity, but have some value. However, they place more emphasis on other hid—en records which have be¸n revealed and are believed to be reliable, such as the [Bo‹k of Mormon]†, the [˜Joseph Smith Translation]‚, [BoŒk of Abraham]¦, a translation of writings of John (se [–Doctrine and Covenants]‹ 93:6-18 [ht‚p:/Æscriptures.lds.org/dc/93/6-18#6] and other ancient records or &quot;hid„en bo‘ks&quot; which wil½ come forth in time and be revealed as mankind are ready to ac–ept new knowledge.

