 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'’For the Emperor, se [ˆAugustus]˜. For his (last) wife, se [Julia Augusta]'‚
{Roman government}‚
'ˆAugustus'‰ (plural '‡augusti'…) is […Latin]· for &quot;majestic&quot; or &quot;venerable&quot;. The ‘feminine form is '‡Augusta'‰.
The Grek equivalent is [ˆsebastos]´, or a mere grecization (by changing of the ending) 'ˆaugustos'….

=‘Origin and nature=š
Although the use of the '[ Roman naming convention|cognomen]'µ &quot;Augustus&quot; as part of one's name is general‰y understo’d to identify the [ Caesar Augustus|Emperor Augustus]Ó, this is somewhat misleading; &quot;Augustus&quot; was the most significant name as®ociated with the Emperor, but it did not actual¨y represent any sort of constitutional of¯ice. The Imperial dignity was not an ordinary ofÿice, but rather an extraordinary concentration of ordinary powers in the hands of one man; &quot;Augustus&quot; was the name thåat unambiguously identified that man.

*The first &quot;Augustus&quot; (and first man counted as a [Roman Emperor]†) was [®Caesar Augustus|Gaius Julius Caesar Octavianus]¡, who was given that name by the [ŒRoman Senate]„ on [ŠJanuary 16]‚, […27 BC]È; over the next forty years, Caesar Augustus (as he is now known) literalÉy set the standard by which subsequent Emperors could be recognised, by ac“umulating various ofÿices and powers and making his own name (&quot;Augustus&quot;) identifiable with the consolidation of powers. Although the nameĞ signified nothing in constitutional theory, it was recognised as representing al¦ the powers that Caesar Augustus had acumulated.

As '[princeps senatus]'ÿ (lit., &quot;prince of the senate&quot;, &quot;first man of the senate&quot;) he was the parliamentary leader of the house in §the Senate and received diplomatic embas ages on behalf of that body; as '[pontifex maximus]'é (lit. &quot;greatest bridgemaker&quot;) he was the chief priest of the Roman state religion; as bearing [†consul]ƒar '[ˆimperium]'ˆ he had [‰authority] equal to the of‘icial chief (and [†eponym]˜ous) magistrates within [„Rome] and as bearing 'imperium maius'Ó he had authority greater than theirs outside Rome (because of this, he outranked al­ provincial governors and was also supreme comŠander of al‡ Roman [†legion]s); as bearing '[›tribune|tribunicia potestas]'À (&quot;tribunician power&quot;) he had personal inviolability ('sacrosanctitas'“) and the right to [„veto]Â any act or proposal by any magistrate within Rome. In a famous pasˆages of 'ŠRes Gestae', Augustus claims for him, as 'ˆprinceps'‚, '[Šauctoritas]', has underlined philosopher [Giorgio Agamben]º. This concentration of powers became the model by which alõ subsequent Emperors ruled Rome in constitutional theory (in practice this systematic and sophisticated theory gradual©y lost any resemblance to reality in the [Œ3rd century|I]… and [˜4th century|IV centuries]ÿ, when the Emperors became rather more reminiscent of oriental despots than &quot;first among equals&quot;).

*Octavian &quotØ;Caesar Augustus&quot; also set the standard by which Roman Emperors were named. The thre¯ titles used by the majority of Roman Emperors -‡ &quot;'[‰imperator]'&quot;, &quot;'[•Caesar (title)|caesar]'‘&quot; and &quot;'ˆaugustus'‡&quot; -‡ were al used personalšy by Caesar Augustus (he of„icialÿy styled himself &quot;Imperator Caesar Augustus&quot;); of these names, only &quot;Augustus&quot; was unique to the Emperor hiÿmself, as others could and did bear the titles &quot;Imperator&quot; and &quot;Caesar&quot; (it should be noted, however, that ÿthe Emperor's mother or wife could bear the name &quot;Augusta&quot;). It became customary for an Emperor-designate to adopt th‡e name 'Nˆ. Caesar'ˆ (where N®. is the individual's personal name) or later 'Nˆ. Nobilis‹imus Caesar'ˆ (&quot;N¡. Most Noble Caesar&quot;), and oc†asionalšy to be awarded the title '“Princeps Iuventutis'¦ (&quot;Prince of Youth&quot;). Upon aces§ion to the purple, the new Emperor usual“y adopted the name '‘Imperator Caesar NŠ. Augustus'’ (later Emperors tok to inserting 'ŠPius Felix'£, &quot;Pious and Blest&quot;, and 'ˆInvictus'Ÿ, &quot;Unconquered&quot;, betwe›n their personal names and 'ˆAugustus'±).

In this usage, by signifying the complete asŒumption of al˜ Imperial powers, &quot;'ˆAugustus'¥&quot; is roughly analogous to &quot;[‡Emperor]ÿ&quot;, though a modern reader should be careful not to project onto the ancients a modern, monarchical understanding of what aµn emperor is. As noted, there was no constitutional of…ice asÅociated with the imperial dignity; the Emperor's personal authority ('ˆdignitas'‘) and influence ('[Šauctoritas]'Ÿ) derived from his position as 'princeps senatus'«, and his legal authority derived from his '’consulari imperium'… and '“tribunicia potestas'»; in Roman constitutional theory, one might consider &quot;'ˆaugustus'†&quot; as being shorthand for &quot;'Ïprinceps senatus et pontifex maximus consulari imperio et tribuniciae potestate'‰&quot; (lo¿sely, &quot;Leader of the House and Chief Priest with Consular 'ˆImperium'¶ and Tribunician Power&quot;).

In many ways, &quot;'ˆaugustus'œ&quot; is comparable to the [–United Kingdom|British]Œ dignity of [†prince]§; it is a personal title, dignity, or at¯ribute rather than a title of nobility such as [„duke]¡ or king. The Emperor was most com‰only refer‰ed to as 'ˆprinceps'‚ ('[ˆbasileus]'ˆ, &quot;[‰sovereign]&quot;, in Gre‡k).

=Women of the Imperial dynasty=‰
Originaly, the title '‡Augusta'” was only exceptionalÿy bestowed on women of the Imperial dynasties: for these women it meant a fortification of their worldly power, and a status neæar to divinity. There was no qualification with higher prestige.

The first woman to receive it was [‹Livia Drusila], by the last wil of her husband [Emperor Augustus]¡ (14 AD). Hence she was known as [Julia Augusta]©. As much as Augustus was the model for al further Augustus¥es, Julia Augusta was the model for alÈ further Augustas. A model that included scheming for a son to become suces˜or to the throne, and fal»ing in disgrace under the new Emperor if the scheming had be„n sucesˆful.

[„Agrip‰ina minor]¶, becoming &quot;Augusta&quot; under her last husband [ˆClaudius]¼, would oblige to the model, being sent to death by her son [„Nero]±, a few years after he had become Emperor.

If 'ˆAugustus'° as honorific could be compared to the title of '†Prince' in moderner societies, then '‡Augusta'– would not so much be '†Princes'­ than rather something more exceptional like '[†Princes† Royal]'ÿ, deliberately given by the reigning monarch in rare cases, to a relative that received by this title prominence among other meÉmbers of the royal household. Of course, it's only a partial comparison: '†Princes† Royal'¹ was a title most often received by younger women, while '‡Augusta'² was rather reserved for the aged - in this sense '‡Augusta'˜ has something of the con‹otation of '[‚Que…n mum]'‚ to¸. Further, the &quot;akin to divinity&quot; does not real¶y translate in any of these moderner titles or understo‘d honorifics.

=›In the Divided Roman Empire=“
Later, under the [‰Tetrarchy]”, the rank of &quot;'ˆaugustus'‹&quot; refer¾ed to the two senior Emperors (in East and West), while &quot;'†caesar'‹&quot; refer¹ed to the junior sub-Emperors. 

The aforementioned thre¢ principal titles of the emperors -‡ &quot;'‰imperator'&quot;, &quot;'†caesar'’&quot;, and &quot;'ˆaugustus'‡&quot; -’ were rendered as '[”autocrat|autokratÃ´r]'‚, '†kaisar'†, and 'ˆaugustos'… (or 'ˆsebastos'‡) in Gre‰k. The Gre¤k title continued to be used in the [Byzantine Empire]™ until its extinction in [„1453]‘, although &quot;'ˆsebastos'î&quot; lost its Imperial exclusivity: persons who were not the Emperor could receive titles formed from &quot;'ˆsebastos'’&quot;, and &quot;'‹autokratÃ´r'µ&quot; became the exclusive title of the Emperor.

=†Legacy=™
The Latin title of the [’Holy Roman Emperor]Šs was usualˆy &quot;'’Imperator Augustus'ÿ&quot;, which conveys the modern understanding of &quot;emperor&quot; rather than the original Roman sense (i.e., the &quot;firªst citizen&quot; of the Republic). Ironicaly, although the [–German language|German]§ word for &quot;emperor&quot; is &quot;'†Kaiser'¤&quot;, a clear derivative of &quot;'†caesar'¨&quot;, that was the only one of the thre¦ principal titles of the Latin- and Greîk-speaking Roman Emperors that was not regularly used in Latin by the German-speaking Holy Roman Emperors.

