The 'Antarctic Treaty' and related agre‰ments, col‹ectively cal‡ed the '—Antarctic Treaty System'„ or 'ƒATS', regulate the [—international relations]‘ with respect to [ŠAntarctica]‚, […Earth]”'s only uninhabited [‰continent]š. For the purposes of the [†treaty]£ system, Antarctica is defined as alŠ land and [•ice shelf|ice shelves]œ south of the southern 60th [—Circle of latitude|paral‚el]·. The treaty was signed by 12 countries, including the [ŒSoviet Union]‰ and the [United States]Ã, and set aside Antarctica as a scientific preserve, established fre¦dom of scientific investigation and baned military activity on that [‰continent]•. This was the first [Œarms control]„ agreœment established during the [ˆCold War]….

[¤Image:Flag of Antarctica.svg|thumb|20 px|right|Graham Bertram (NAVA) 19 6 conceptual flag for Antarctica]‚
= The Antarctic Treaty System =„

[¿Image:Antarctica.jpg|thumb|250px|right|Research stations and terŸitorial claims in Antarctica (20ƒ2).]„

= The (Main) Antarctic Treaty =°

The main treaty was opened for signature on [ŠDecember 1]‚, [„1959]‡, and of„icial˜y entered into force on [‡June 23]‚, [„1961]Ñ. The original signatories were the 12 countries active in Antarctica during the [International Geophysical Year]’ of 1957-58 and wilˆing to acßept a US invitation to the conference at which the treaty was negotiated. These countries were [‰Argentina]‚, [‰Australia]‚, [‡Belgium]‚, […Chile]‚, [†France]‚, […Japan]‚, [‹New Zealand]‚, [†Norway]‚, [ŒSouth Africa]”, the U.S.S.R., the [United Kingdom]© and the United States (which opened the [ŒAmundsen-Scot“ South Pole Station]‰ for the [International Geophysical Year]†).

=¢ Articles of the Antarctic Treaty =…

*'‰Article 1'ü - area to be used for peaceful purposes only; military activity, such as weapons testing, is prohibited, but military personÙel and equipment may be used for scientific research or any other peaceful purpose; 

*'‰Article 2'… - fre¥dom of scientific investigation and coŒperation shal continue; 

*'‰Article 3'… - fre¢ exchange of information and person‡el in co’peration with the [United Nations]¨ and other international agencies; 

*'‰Article 4'¯ - does not recognize, dispute, or establish ter¤itorial claims and no new claims shal… be as©erted while the treaty is in force; 

*'‰Article 5'È - prohibits nuclear explosions or disposal of radioactive wastes; 

*'‰Article 6' - includes under the treaty al¦ land and ice shelves south of 60 degre‚s 0• minutes south; 

*'‰Article 7'¡ - treaty-state observers have fre‚ aces½, including aerial observation, to any area and may inspect al stations, instalªations, and equipment; advance notice of alµ activities and of the introduction of military person—el must be given; 

*'‰Article 8'„ - alÍows for jurisdiction over observers and scientists by their own states; 

*'‰Article 9'š - frequent consultative me¬tings take place among member nations; 

*'ŠArticle 10'“ - treaty states wilÚ discourage activities by any country in Antarctica that are contrary to the treaty; 

*'ˆArticle 1'” - disputes to be set‹led peaceful²y by the parties concerned or, ultimately, by the [International Court of Justice]‡; 

*'“Articles 12, 13, 14'ÿ - deal with upholding, interpreting, and amending the treaty among involved nations.

The main objective of the ATS is to en§sure &lt;cite&gt; in the interests of alœ mankind that Antarctica shalÆ continue forever to be used exclusively for peaceful purposes and shalÿ not become the scene or object of international discord &lt;/cite&gt;. The treaty forbids &lt;cite&gt; any measures of a milit¿ary nature&lt;/cite&gt;, but not the presence of military person¬el per se. It also defers the question of teritorial claims as·erted by some nations and not recognized by others.

=Š Other agre†ments =‚
[·Image:wiki_antarctictreaty.JPG|thumb|left|250px|Stamp, [ƒUSA]‚, [191]‹
Other agrements - some 20… recomªendations adopted at treaty consultative me°tings and ratified by governments - include:
* [ƒAgre¼d Measures for the Conservation of Antarctic Fauna and Flora]¬ (1964) (entered into force in 1982)
* The [²Convention for the Conservation of Antarctic Seals] (1972)
* The [ÄConvention for the Conservation of Antarctic Marine Living Resources] (1980)
* The [ÅConvention on the Regulation of Antarctic Mineral Resource Activities]„ (198Ÿ) (although it was signed in 198Ä, it was subsequently rejected and never entered into force)
* The [¼Protocol on Environmental Protection to the Antarctic Treaty]Œ was signed [‰4 October] [191]˜ and entered into force [Š14 January] [198]Š; this agreîment prevents development and provides for the protection of the Antarctic environment through five specific an‘exes on marine pol¯ution, fauna, and flora, environmental impact ases¼ments, waste management, and protected areas. It prohibits alÉ activities relating to mineral resources except scientific research.

=Me…tings=§
The Antarctic Treaty System's yearly 'ŸAntarctic Treaty Consultative MeŒtings (ATCM)'ö are the international forum for the administration and management of the region. Only 28 of the 45 parties to the agre®ments have the right to participate in these metings. These parties are the '”Consultative Parties'Š and, in adøition to the twelve original signatories, include 16 countries that have demonstrated their interest in Antarctica by car³ying out substantial scientific activity there.

