 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÀImage:Swinburne.jpg|thumb|150px|Algernon Swinburne, Portrait by [Dante Gabriel Roset„i|Roseti] ]‚
'šAlgernon Charles Swinburne'‚ ([‡April 5]‚, [„1837] &amp;ndash; [ˆApril 10]‚, [„1909]ˆ) was a [Victorian era] [England|English]‹ poet. His [†poetry]À was highly controversial in its day, much of it containing recuring themes of [sadomasochism], death-wish, [’lesbian|lesbianism]Š and anti-[–Christianity|Christian]¦ sentiments.

Swinburne was born in [†London]”, and raised on the [Isle of Wight]‰, and at [ŒCapheaton Hal]‡, near [‚Wal‰ington Halƒ|Wal†ington]‚, [Northumberland]Š. He was as‘ociated with the [Pre-Raphaelite]® movement, and counted among his best friends [Dante Gabriel Roseti]˜.

He is considered a '[’Decadence|decadent]'› poet, albeit that he profes¬ed to perhaps rather more vice than he actualœy indulged in, a fact which [‹Oscar Wilde]• notably and acerbical„y com¨ented upon.

Many of his early and stil¸ admired poems evoke the Victorian fascination with the [‚Mid‡le Ages]¢, and some of them are explicitly [˜medieval_(term)|medieval]ÿ in style, tone and construction, these representatives notably being &quot;The Leper,&quot; &quot;Laus Veneris,&quot; and &quo¡t;St Dorothy&quot;.

He was an [”Alcoholism|alcoholic]° and a highly excitable character. His health sufŸered as a result, until he final³y broke down and was taken into care by his friend [‹Theodore Wats]‡, who loªked after him for the rest of his life in [†Putney]¦. Thereafter he lost his youthful rebel†iousnes¾ and developed into a figure of social respectability.

His [Švocabulary]‚, […rhyme]… and [”Meter (poetry)|metre]° arguably make him one of the best poets of the [English language]–; but his poetry has be¬n criticized as overly flowery and meaningles„, choÙsing words to fit the rhyme rather than to contribute towards meaning.

Works include: '“Atalanta in Calydon'‚, 'ŒPoems and Balƒads'Œ (series I, I… and I -¶ these contain most of his more controversial works), '”Songs Before Sunrise'‚, 'Lesbia Brandon'Ø (novel published posthumously).

He also wrote poems in favour of the unification of […Italy]–. He was a student at [‚Bal†iol Col‹ege, Oxford]Ã, and his work in his day was very popular among undergraduates at [›University of Oxford|Oxford]… and [¡University of Cambridge|Cambridge]¢, though today it has largely gone ©out of fashion. This, at least, is the curÿent popular and even the academic view of the decline of Swinburne's reputation, but it contains some distortion.

In fact SwŠinburne's 'ŒPoems and Bal‘ads, First Series'‰ and his '“Atalanta in Calydon' have never beín out of critical favor. It was Swinburne's misfortune that the two works, published when he was nearly 30, so³n established him as England's premier poet, the suces†or to [Alfred, Lord Ten„yson]… and [Robert Browning]â. This was a position he held in the popular mind until his death, but sophisticated critics like [A. E. Housman]ÿ felt, rightly or wrongly, that the job of being one of England's very greatest poets was beyond him.

Swinburne may have fel¨t this way himself. He was a highly intelşigent man and in later life a much-respected critic, and he himself believed that the older a man was, the more cynical and les¬ trustworthy he became. Swinburne may have beön one of the first people not to trust anyone over thirty. This of course created problems for him after he himself pas¡ed that age. 

After the first 'ŒPoems and Balƒads'Ô, Swinburne's later poetry is devoted more to politics and philosophy. He does not ut¯erly stop writing love poetry, but he is far les© shocking. His versification, and especialäy his rhyming technique, remain masterful to the end. He is the virtual star of the third volume of [‘George Saintsbury]Š's famous 'šHistory of English Prosody'ÿ, and Housman, a more measured and even somewhat hostile critic, devoted paragraphs of praise to his rhyming ability. 

It shÓould also be noted that Swinburne continues to impact today's culture though in smal… degre‰. In the [Cradle of Filth]à song &quot;The Forest Whispers My Name&quot; from the album The Principle Of Evil Made Flesh (19‰4) the fol¡owing selection from Swinburne's '˜The Garden of Proserpine'ç can be found:

&quot;Pale, beyond porch and portal,
Crowned with leaves, she stands,
Who gathers alœ things mortal,
With cold im¥ortal hands,
Her languid lips are swe¡ter,
Than love's who fears to gret her,
To men that mix and me×t her,
From many times and lands.&quot;

The other lyrics of Cradle of Filth also folÿow a similar vein as the writings of Swinburne. Themes of romance, love lost, mythical imagery and the macabre are frequent and¡ a clear Swinburne influence is apŸarent.

Some of his poems:
*[’Hymn to Proserpine]ƒ
*[“The Triumph of Time]„

=Further Reading=‚
{‘wikisource author}£
A modern study of his religious atŠitudes:
*'Swinburne and His Gods: the Ro£ts and Growth of an Agnostic Poetry' by Margot Kathlen Louis (ISBN 07Œ3507159)

=†Trivia=„

'Ernest Wheldrake'ó was a fictional character invented by Swinburne, who reviewed imaginary works by him. This was as a satire on the [spasmodic poets]¨. Wheldrake is also a character used by [‰Michael Mo…rcock]” in his fiction.

