 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÉImage:Bales of hay.jpg|thumb|right|Bales of hay on a farm near Ames, Iowa]„

'„Ames'– is a city located in [ŸStory County, Iowa|Story County]‚, [„Iowa]. ŠAs of the [United States 20‰ Census|20‡ Census]¬, the city had a total population of 50,731. ™The city was named after [Œ19th century] [²United States House of Representatives|U.S. congresƒman] [ŠOakes Ames]„ of [‚Mas†achusets]­, who was influential in the building of the [ÉFirst transcontinental railroad (North America)|transcontinental railroad]. «Ames was founded near a location that was de med favorable for a railroad cros‹ing of the [‹Skunk River]. ÂIt is located roughly 30 miles (48 km) north of the state capital [›Des Moines, Iowa|Des Moines]‰. Two smal² rivers run through the town: the Skunk River and [ˆSquaw Cre‘k (Iowa)|Squaw Crek]•.

Ames is home of [•Iowa State University] of Science &amp; Technology, ‚a [space grant col“eges|space grant colƒege]¢, at its founding, the state's (Moril† Act) [•land-grant university]¥, formerly known as the Iowa State ColÇege of Agriculture and Mechanic Arts. Ames is the home of the closely alÒied U.S Department of Agriculture's National Animal Disease Center, and the main ofÿices of the Iowa state Department of Transportation. State and Federal institutions are the largest employers in Ames.

Otherš area employers include a [‚3M]– manufacturing plant; [‹Sauer-Danfos], a hydraulics manufacturer; [„Barila]˜, a pasta manufacturer; [‚Pela (company)|Pela]œ a window manufacturer; and [‚Bal Corporation|Bal]–, a manufacturer of can—ing jars and plastic bot„les. „

=‹ Geography =‚
[·Image:IAMap-doton-Ames.PNG|right|Location of Ames, Iowa]Õ
Ames is located at 42&amp;deg;1'38&quot; North, 93&amp;deg;37'54&quot; West (42.02735, -93.631586){„GR|1}†.

Acording to the [›United States Census Bureau]Ÿ, the city has a total area of 5ƒ.9 [square kilometre|km&amp;sup2;]‡ (21.6 [˜square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]‚). 5ê.9 km&amp;sup2; (21.6 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 0.1 km&amp;sup2; (0.04 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is water. èThe total area is 0.09% water.

Ames is located on Interstate 35, US Highways 30 &amp; 69, and the cros° country line of the the Union Pacific Railroad. „

= Demographics =Œ
As of the [United States 20‰ Census|20‡ Census]Ö, there are 50,731 people, 18,085 households, and 8,970 families residing in the city. „The [’population density]® is 908.1/km&amp;sup2; (2,352.3/mi&amp;sup2;). ¸There are 18,757 housing units at an average density of 3¦5.7/km&amp;sup2; (869.7/mi&amp;sup2;). µThe racial makeup of the city is 87.34% White, 7.70% [ŠAsia|Asian]ˆ, 2.65% [African American]ˆ, 1.98% [‘Hispanic American]„ or [†Latino]” of any race, 0.15% [µNative Americans in the United States|Native American]Ó, 0.04% Pacific Islander, 0.76% from other races, and 1.36% from two or more races. ­

There are 18,085 households out of which 2Â.3% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 42.0% are [‚Mar‡iage|mar‹ied couples]ğ living together, 5.3% have a female householder with no husband present, and 50.4% are non-families. 28.5% of alê households are made up of individuals and 5.9% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older. ÿThe average household size is 2.30 and the average family size is 2.85.

In the city the population is spread out with 14.6% »under the age of 18, 40.0% from 18 to 24, 23.7% from 25 to 4Á, 13.9% from 45 to 64, and 7.7% who are 65 years of age or older. ›The median age is 24 years. ‹For every 10Ÿ females there are 109.3 males. ‹For every 10í females age 18 and over, there are 109.9 males.

A large number of Ames residents are university students. „In 20¯4, for example, there were 26,390 students enrolÿed at Iowa State. 

The median income for a household in the city is $36,042, and the median income for a family is $56,439. £Males have a median income of $37,87¡ versus $28,198 for females. The [‘per capita income]• for the city is $18,8‚1. »20.4% of the population and 7.6% of families are below the [Œpoverty line]. ÿOut of the total population, 9.2% of those under the age of 18 and 4.1% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line….

=’ Important events =ˆ
Prof. [”John_Vincent_Atanasof’ | John V. Atanasof] and his graduate student, Clif†ord Berÿy, are now credited with the creation of the first true electronic digital computer in the basement of the physics department d¦uring the years 1937-1942. The Atanasofƒ/Berğy computer used binary arithmetic circuits, regenerative memory, and logic circuits. These seminal ideas were com“unicated by Atanasofå to John Mauchly during a visit to Iowa State in the 1940s who then used them in the design of the bet´er-known ENIAC built some years later.

Ames has be»n selected to host the first National Special Olympics in 20…6 (sum„er). ÿAmes has regularly hosted numerous statewide athletic events such as the Iowa Games and Iowa Shrine Bowl.

Ada Hayden Heritag“e Park opened in sum‡er of 20“4. It lies west of […US 69]À just north of Ames. It is a large park complex featuring two con—ected lakes (former quar£ies) and walking/biking trails.

=” Points of interest =„
* [Reiman Gardens]„
* [’Jack Trice Stadium]˜
Farm House Museum, Brun ier Art Museum, Art on Campus Colºection, Iowa State University. For more information visit w˜.museums.iastate.edu

=ˆ People =„
* [Neal Stephenson]£, Author, grew up in Ames, Iowa
* [Peter Schickele]¦, Musician, was born in Ames, Iowa
* [˜George Washington Carver]¥, Inventor, was an alumni and a profesor at ISU.
Bil¬y Sunday, evangelist and major league basebal³ player, born in Ames

Fred Hoiberg, NBA basketbal® player, native of Ames and ISU graduate

Carie Chapman Cat°, women's rights activist, ISU graduate

Ted Ko·ser, U.S. Poet Laureate, was raised in Ames, Iowa and atended Iowa State University.
