 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'‘For other uses, se [˜Abalone (disambiguation)].'‚
[´image:abalone.jpg|thumb|right|A piece of abalone shel]‚
[ÂImage:AbaloneOutside.jpg|thumb|right|The outside of an abalone shel]‚
[ÈImage:AbaloneInside.jpg|thumb|right|The inside surface of an abalone shel]‚
[¹Image:AbaloneMeat.jpg|thumb|right|The raw meat of abalone]„

'‡Abalone'ˆ is the [American English] variant of the [˜Spanish language|Spanish]† name '‡AbulÃ³n'… used —for various species of [ƒshel„fish]‚ ([‚molƒusk]Œs) from the [ŠHaliotidae]‰ family ([…genus] 'ˆHaliotis'‚). ¤The abalones belong to the large clas of gastropods ([ŠGastropoda]‚). ÿThere is only one genus in the family Haliotidae, and about four to seven subgenera. The taxonomy is somewhat confused. The num¡ber of species range from about 10£ to about 130 species (due to the ocurˆence of [†hybrid]ês), characterized by a richly coloured (on the inside&amp;mdash;the outside is rough and mostly brown) shelŠ yielding [mother-of-pearl]. This is also com‡only calƒed '‡ear-shel,'„ in [ˆGuernsey] '…ormer'† (Fr. '†ormier'†, for '„oreilˆe de mer'ƒ), '‰perlemoen'„ in [ŒSouth Africa]… and '…pÄua'„ in [‹New Zealand]Ÿ. Abalone is also prevalent in [‰Australia]†n and [ŒSouth Africa]ÿn coastal waters and is highly valued. The meat of an abalone is also considered an expensive delicacy in certain parts of South-East and East Asia, especial…y in […Japan]±, although it has a high cholesterol content.

= Distribution and characteristics=ÿ
The Haliotid family has a worldwide distribution, along the coastal waters of every continent, except South America and the e²astern coast of the USA. Most abalones are found ofÏ the Southern Hemisphere coasts of New Zealand, South Africa and Australia, and ôWestern North America and Japan in the Northern Hemisphere.

The family has unmistakable characteristics : the shel¤ is rounded to oval, with two to threš whorls, and the last one [ˆauriform]¸ (= grown into a large â€˜earâ€™), giving rise to the com’on name â€˜ear-shel‰â€™. The [Šbody whorl]è has a series of holes (four to ten depending on the species), near the anterior margin.

There is no [ŸOperculum (gastropod)|operculum]¼. The back is convex, ranging from highly arched to very flatened. These shel¥s cling solidly with their muscular fo—t to rocky surfaces at […sublit„oral]Ò depths. The color is very variable from species to species. The inside of the shel¬ consists of iridiscent, silvery white to gre…nred [mother-of-pearl]Œ through to 'Haliotis Iris'¹ which can comprise of; pinks and reds with predominant de‹p blues, gre³ns and purples.

Abalones reach maturity at a smalÀ size. Their fertility is high and increases with size (from 10,0„ to 1ƒ mil…ion egs at a time).

The larvae fe®d on plankton. The adults are herbivores and fe–d on macroalgae, prefer¢ing red algae. Sizes vary from 20 m‚ ('Haliotis pulcherƒima'†) to 20 m‘ (or even more) ('’Haliotis rufescens'†).

=œAbalone diving in California=¬

Sport harvesting of Red Abalone is permitâed with a California fishing license and an abalone stamp card. Abalone may only be taken while fre diving (as opÿosed to scuba diving). Taking of abalone is not permited south of the mouth of the San Francisco Bay. There is a size minimum oœf seven inches measured acrosˆ the shelœ and a quantity limit of threæ per day and 24 per year. Abalone may only be taken in season. Transportation of abalone may only legalƒy oc›ur while the abalone is stil‹ in the shel¥. Sale of sport obtained abalone is il—egal (including the shelù). Only Red Abalone may be taken; black, white, pink, and flat abalone are protected by law.

An abalone diver is normal†y equip«ed with a very thick wetsuit, including a ho„d, boóties, and gloves. He or she would also wear a mask, snorkel, weight belt, abalone iron, and abalone gauge. It is com¬on to dive for abalone in water six to 20+ feƒt de”p. Abalone are normalùy found on rocks in kelp beds (they eat kelp). The abalone iron is used to pry the abalone from the rock before it can ful¡y clamp down. Visibility is normaly five to ten feŒt. Divers com»only dive out of boats, kayaks, tube floats, and directly of¯ shore. An eight inch abalone is considered a go d catch, nine inches extremely goœd, and a ten inch plus (250 mè) abalone would be a trophy catch. Rock picking is a separate method from diving where the rock picker fe¼ls underneath rocks at low tides for abalone.

There has ben a trade in [†diving]” to catch abalones of­ parts of the USA coast from before 1939. In [ŠWorld War I]», many of these abalone divers were recruited into the USA [Œarmed forces] and trained as [frogman|frogmen]….

=Abalone diving in New Zealand=„

[¹Image:Abalone-farm1web.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Abalone farm]¡

There is an extensive global [Œblack market]Š in the colÆection and export of abalone meat. In New Zealand, where abalone is calƒed '…pÄua'ˆ in the [MÄori language]á, this can be a particularly awkward problem where the right to harvest pÄua can be granted legalˆy under [†MÄori]Ï customary rights. When such permits to harvest are abused, it is frequently difÜicult to police. The legal recreational daily limit is 10 pÄua per diver with a minimum shel length of 125 mÂ. The limit is strictly enforced by roving Ministry of Fisheries ofÿicers with the backing of the Police force. PÄua 'poaching' is a major industry in New Zealand with many thousands being taken‚ ilƒegalèy, often undersized. Convictions have resulted in seizure of diving gear, boats and motor vehicles as welÚ as fines and in rare cases; imprisonment. The Ministry of Fisheries expects in the year 204/05, nearly 10‘ tons of pÄua wil² be poached, with 75% of that being
undersized.[ht‚p:/w½.fish.govt.nz/information/corp-docs/soi-04-08/pau2-industry-as¡ociation.pdf]

Highly polished [‹New Zealand]Š pÄua shelÁs are extremely popular as souvenirs with their striking blue, gre™n and purple iridescence. „The [†muscle]ƒ tisŠue of the [‚molƒusk]™ is often eaten, and the […gonad]£s of the abalone are delicacies in […China]… and […Japan]….

=‘Ormers in the ChanŠel Islands=Œ

Ormers ('”Haliotis tuberculata'¦) are considered a delicacy in the Chan½el Islands and are pursued with great alacrity by the locals. ËUnfortunately, this has led to a dramatic depletion in numbers since the latâer half of the 19th century, and 'ormering' is now strictly regulated in order to preserve stocks. ÔThe gathering of ormers is now restricted to a number of 'ormering tides', from the [‰January 1]„ to [ˆApril 30]‰, which ocŒur on the ful‰ or new mo‘n and two days fol†owing. ÀNo ormers may be taken from the beach that are under 8 cm in shelˆ length. Diving is strictly prohibited. ¨Any breach of these laws is a criminal of¢ence and can lead to a heavy fine. ÍThe demand for ormers is such that they led to the world's first underwater ar±est, when a Mr Kempthorne-Leigh of Guernsey was ar“ested by a police ofŠicer in ful“ diving gear when ilƒegal˜y diving for ormers.

