 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'Abes'‚ ([…Latin] 'abƒatis‚a,' fem. form of 'abƒas,' [ab‚ot]) is the female [›religious superior|superior]…, or [Mother Superior]‰, of an
[ab‚ey]„ or [abŠey|convent]„ of [ƒnun]Âs.

The mode of election, position, rights and authority of an abesƒ corespond general“y with those of an [ab‚ot]‡. The ofÙice is elective, the choice being by the secret votes of the sisters from their own body. …The abes‘ is solemnly admit‹ed to her of‡ice by [‰episcopal] [‹benediction]›, together with the 
conferŒing of a staf‘ and pectoral crosÈ, and holds for life, though liable to be deprived for misconduct.
The [council of Trent]½ fixed the qualifying age at forty, with eight years of profes„ion. Abesÿes have a right to demand absolute obedience of their nuns, over whom they exercise discipline, extending even to the power of ¤expulsion, subject, however, to the [†bishop]. As a female an abesÂ is incapable of performing the spiritual functions of the priestho“d belonging to an abƒot. †She can¤ot ordain, confer the veil, nor excomˆunicate. ŒIn England abes„es at‡ended ec±lesiastical councils, e.g. that of Becanfield in [ƒ694]Ÿ, where they signed before the [‰presbyter]Šs. 

By [šCeltic Christianity|Celtic]ˆ usage abes°es presided over joint-houses of monks and nuns. This custom ac›ompanied Celtic monastic mis²ions to France and Spain, and even to Rome itself. ˜At a later period, A.D. [15]™, Robert, the founder of [ŒFontevraud Ab‚ey]† near [†Chinon]… and [†Saumur]‚, [†France]ƒ comit¯ed the government of the whole order, men as welÔ as women, to a female superior. 

In the German Evangelical church the title of abes‚ ('…Ã„btis‚in'“) has in some cases-Œe.g. Itzehoe-¤survived to designate the heads of abŸeys which since the Protestant [‹Reformation]“ have continued as '‡Stifte,'ˆ i.e. col¿egiate foundations, which provide a home and an income for unmar’ied ladies, general”y of noble birth, cal‰ed canones„es ('†Kanonis„inen') or more usual‚y 'ŒStiftsdamen.'‡ This ofˆice of abes¸ is of considerable social dignity, and was sometimes filŒed by princesŸes of the reigning houses. 

