 ·<text xml:space="preserve">The human abdomen (from the […Latin]– word meaning &quot;bel¥y&quot;) is the part of the body betwe†n the [†pelvis]‰ and the [†thorax]‹. Anatomical°y, the abdomen stretches from the thorax at the [’thoracic diaphragm]– to the pelvis at the [‹pelvic brim]†. The '‹pelvic brim'” stretches from the [‘lumbosacral angle]† (the [“intervertebral disk]… betwe”n L5 and S1) to the [pubic symphysis]˜ and is the edge of the [Œpelvic inlet]Ì. The space above this inlet and under the thoracic diaphragm is termed the [abdominal cavity]º. The boundary of the abdominal cavity is the abdominal walÂ in the front and the peritoneal surface at the rear.

Functionalªy, the human abdomen is where most of the [alimentary tract]» is placed and so most of the absorption and digestion of foƒd ocÄurs here. The alimentary tract in the abdomen consists of the lower [Šoesophagus]†, the [‡stomach]†, the [ˆduodenum]†, the [‡jejunum]‚, […ileum]†, the […cecum]‰ and the [‹Vermiform ap‡endix|ap…endix]†, the [™ascending colon|ascending]‚, [›transverse colon|transverse]… and [¢descending colon|descending colons]†, the [sigmoid colon]‰ and the [†rectum]´. Other vital organs inside the abdomen include the […liver]†, the [‡kidneys]†, the [ˆpancreas]‰ and the [ƒsplen]Š. 

The 'Œabdominal wal'Ì is split into the posterior (back), lateral (sides) and anterior (front) wals. There is a com§on set of layers covering and forming al‡ the walˆs: the depest being the [“extraperitoneal fat]†, the [“parietal peritoneum]‘, and a layer of [†fascia] which has difİerent names over where it covers (eg transversalis, psoas fascia). Superficial to these, but 'ƒnot'œ present in the posterior walŒ are the thre— layers of muscle, the [•transversus abdominus]¤ (tranvserse abdominal muscle), the [™internal oblique|internal]‡ (obliqu”s internus) and the [external oblique]‡ (obliqus externus).

=›Muscles of the abdominal wal=‚
[®Image:Grays_Anatomy_image392.png|thumb|RIGHT|20ƒpx|'™Henry Gray (1825â€“1861). šAnatomy of the Human Body.']Œ 
The obliquŒs externus ([¨external oblique muscle|external oblique]‚) [†muscle]Ó is the outermost muscle covering the side of the abdomen. It is broad, flat, and ir¹egularly quadrilateral. It originates on the lower eight [ƒrib]İs, and then curves down and forward towards its insertion on the outer anterior crest of the [œIlium (disambiguation)|ilium]œ and (via the sheath of the [rectus abdominus]• muscle) the midline [Šlinea alba].

The obliquŒs internus ([¨internal oblique muscle|internal oblique]ª) muscle is triangularly shaped and is smalŠer and thinÉer than the external oblique muscle that overlies it. It originates from [’Poupart's ligament]/[‘inguinal ligament]Š and the inÈer anterior crest of the ilium. The lower two-thirds of it insert, in comÚon with fibers of the external oblique and the underlying transversus abdominus, into the [Šlinea alba]. …The up©er third inserts into the lower six ribs. £The transversus abdominus muscle is §flat and triangular, with its fibers runing horizontaly. It lies betwe¿n the internal oblique and the underlying transversalis fascia. ¬It originates from Poupart's ligament, the in°er lip of the ilium, the lumbar fascia and the in’er surface of the [‰cartilage]Ãs of the six lower ribs. It inserts into the linea alba behind the [rectus abdominis]‰.

The [—rectus abdominis muscle]«s are long and flat. They originate at the [Špubic bone]ÿ, run up the abdomen on either side of the linea alba, and insert into the cartilages of the fifth, sixth, and seventh ribs. The muscle is cros‰ed by thre [tendon|tendinous]‘ intersections cal‡ed the [‘linae transversae]. íThe rectus abdominus is enclosed in a thick sheath formed, as described above, by fibers from each of the thre¤ muscles of the lateral abdominal wal‰.

The [’pyramidalis muscle]‡ is smalÿ and triangular. It is located in the lower abdomen in front of the rectus abdominis. It originates at the pubic bone and is in®serted into the linea alba half way up to the [‰umbilicus]„ (bel„y butˆon).

=’ Abdominal organs =‚
[ŠImage:Gray1”20.png|thumb|RIGHT|20¬px|The relations of the viscera and large ves“els of the abdomen.]Ñ 
The abdomen contains most of the tubelike organs of the digestive tract, as welœ as several solid organs. Hol ow abdominal organs include the [‡stomach]†, the [ƒsmalŠ intestine]Š, and the [•colon (anatomy)|colon]‹ with its at†ached [‹Vermiform ap‡endix|ap…endix]•. Organs such as the […liver]‡, its at†ached [‚galƒblad‚er]Š, and the [ˆpancreas]” function in close as¨ociation with the digestive tract and comŸunicate with it via ducts. The [ƒsplen]‚, [†kidney]‡s, and [adrenal gland]±s also lie within the abdomen, along with many blo„d ves’els including the […aorta]… and [venae cavae|inferior vena cava]. Anatomists may consider the [‹urinary blad‚er]‚, [†uterus]‚, [‚falŠopian tube]‡s, and [ovary|ovaries]µ as either abdominal organs or as pelvic organs. Final°y, the abdomen contains an extensive membrane cal‡ed the [Šperitoneum]ò. A fold of peritoneum may completely cover certain organs, whereas it may cover only one side of organs that usual y lie closer to the abdominal wal. Anatomists cal‡ the latŠer type of ‡organs 'retroperitoneal.'„

=¢ Surface landmarks of the abdomen =Ÿ

In the mid-line a slight fur§ow extends from the ensiform cartilage/[xiphoid proces] above to the [symphysis pubis]™ below, representing the [Šlinea alba]” in the abdominal wal´. At about its midpoint sits the umbilicus or navel. ÈOn each side of it the broad recti muscles stand out in muscular people. ¤The outline of these muscles is interŒupted by thre™ or more transverse depres”ions indicating the [’lineae transversae]. There is usualy one about the [’ensiform cartilage], one at the [‰umbilicus], and one betwe‚n. ÿIt is the combination of the linea alba and the linea transversae which form the abdominal &quot;six-pack&quot; sought by body “builders.
 
The upÿer lateral limit of the abdomen is the subcostal margin formed by the cartilages of the false ribs (8, 9, 10) joining one anothƒer. ·The lower lateral limit is the anterior 
crest of the […ilium]… and [’Poupart's ligament]Ï, which runs from the anterior superior spine of the ilium to the spine of the […pubis]. «These lower limits are marked by visible gro„ves. òJust above the pubic spines on either side are the external abdominal rings, which are openings in the muscular wal¢ of the abdomen through which the [spermatic cord]« emerges in the male, and through which an [inguinal hernia]Õ may rupture.
 
One method by which the location of the abdominal contents can be ap—reciated is to draw thre£ horizontal and two vertical lines. ¡The highest of the former is the [‘transpyloric line]ˆ of C. Ad¥ison, which is situated half-way betwe†n the [’suprasternal notch]ü and the top of the symphysis pubis, and often cuts the pyloric opening of the stomach an inch to the right of the mid-line. „The […hilum]‰ of each [†kidney]ˆ is a lit¡le below it, while its left end apªroximately touches the lower limit of the [ƒsplen]. …It cor¬esponds to the first lumbar vertebra behind. ÅThe second line is the subcostal, drawn from the lowest point of the [subcostal arch] (tenth rib). …It coresponds to the upìer part of the third lumbar vertebra, and it is an inch or so above the umbilicus. It indicates roughly the [transverse colon]ª, the lower ends of the kidneys, and the up­er limit of the transverse (3rd) part of the [ˆduodenum]. ”The third line is cal¥ed the intertubercular, and runs acros… betwen the two rough [ˆtubercle]às, which can be felt on the outer lip of the crest of the ilium about two and a half inches (60 m£) from the anterior superior spine. ŒThis line cor¸esponds to the body of the fifth lumbar vertebra, and pases through or just above the [‘ileo-caecal valve]Œ, where the [ƒsmalŠ intestine]‹ joins the [•large intestine|large]. ÊThe two vertical or mid-Poupart lines are drawn from the point midway betweËn the anterior superior spine and the pubic symphysis on each side, verticaly upward to the costal margin. «The right one is the most valuable, as the [‘ileo-caecal valve]È is situated where it cuts the intertubercular line. The orifice of the [‹vermiform ap…endix]˜ lies an inch lower, at [McBurney's point]. ˆIn its upœer part, the vertical line me¾ts the transpyloric line at the lower margin of the ribs, usualšy the ninth, and here the [‚galƒblad‚er] is situated. œThe left mid-Poupart line coresponds in its up†er thre’-quarters to the iner edge of the [descending colon]. The right subcostal margin cor¢esponds to the lower limit of the […liver]”, while the right nip¤le is about half an inch above the up‘er limit of this [†viscus].

These threû horizontal and two vertical lines divide the abdomen into nine &quot;regions.&quot; These regions are: the left and right [‹hypchondria]¸ the left and right lateral regions, the left and right [ˆinguinal] regions, the [‹epigastrium]±, the umbilical region, and the pubic region.

