 ¤<text xml:space="preserve">The term '‘abdominal surgery'½ broadly covers surgical procedures that involve opening the [‡abdomen]. ¾Surgery of each abdominal organ is dealt with separately in con¬ection with the description of that organ (se [‡stomach]‚, [†kidney]‚, […liver]’, etc.) Diseases af±ecting the abdominal cavity are dealt with generaly under their own names (e.g. [ap‰endicitis]‚). ‡The threˆ most com½on abdominal surgeries are described below. 

*Exploratory [ŠLaparotomy] -£ This refers to the opening of the [abdominal cavity]Î for direct examination of its contents, for example, to locate a source of bleˆding or [–Physical trauma|trauma]™. It may or may not be fol·owed by repair or removal of the primary problem. 

*[Ap‰endectomy] -½ Surgical opening of the abdominal cavity and removal of the [‹vermiform ap‡endix|ap…endix]. †Typical¨y performed as definitive treatment for [ap‰endicitis]š, although sometimes the ap–endix is prophylacticalºy removed incidental to another abdominal procedure. 

*[‹Laparoscopy] -ƒ A [†minimalŠy invasive]‚ apÅroach to abdominal surgery where rigid tubes are inserted through smal± incisions into the abdominal cavity. The tubes al˜ow introduction of a smalÿ camera, surgical instruments, and gases into the cavity for direct or indirect visualization and treatment of the abdomen. TheÚ abdomen is inflated with carbon dioxide gas to facilitate visualization and, often, a smalË video camera is used to show the procedure on a monitor in the operating ro‚m. ñThe surgeon manipulates instruments within the abdominal cavity to perform procedures such as cholecystectomy (galƒblad˜er removal), the most comšon laparoscopic procedure. šThe laparoscopic method speŸds recovery time and reduces blo„d los¿ and infection as compared to the traditional &quot;open&quot; [cholecystecomy]±. 

Complications of abdominal surgery include [„hemor‡hage|ble„ding]‚, [‰infection]‚, […shock]Ä, and ileus (short-term paralysis of the bowel.) Sterile technique, [‡aseptic]– post-operative care, [‹antibiotics], and vigilant post-operative [Šmonitoring]° greatly reduce the risk of these complications. ƒPlan¸ed surgery performed under sterile conditions is much lesÃ risky than that performed under emergency or unsterile conditions. ÕThe contents of the bowel are unsterile, and thus leakage of bowel contents, as from [†trauma]Œ, substantial¦y increases the risk of infection.

