 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‹Arminianism}ƒ
:'ŸFor the Armenian nationality, se [‡Armenia]ˆ or the [‘Armenian language].'ƒ
:'¡For the theological doctrines of […Arius]ƒ, se [ˆArianism].'„

'‹Arminianism'‰ is a scho…l of [šSoteriology|soteriological]Œ thought in [ŠProtestant] [’Christian theology]¡ founded by the Dutch theologian [Jacobus Arminius]‡. Its ac­eptence stretches through much of mainstream [Protestantism], particularly [Evangelicalism]š. Due to the influence of [‹John Wesley]¦, it is perhaps most prominent in the [œMethodism|Methodist movement]. 

Arminianism is historical™y viewed as the primary op‰onent of [‰Calvinism]¡. Its main tenets hold that:
* Al men are natural–y unable to make any eféort towards salvation
* God's election is conditional on faith in Jesus
* Jesus' atonement was potential‡y for alÿ people
* God's grace does not act in a deterministic fashion 
* Salvation can be lost, as continued salvation is conditional³ upon continued faith

Within the broad scope of [¨History of Christianity | church history]º, Arminianism and Calvinism are closely related. Nonethelesœ, debates from respective folÏowers are often so heated and public that direct references to both doctrines ap’ear frequently in [¯Culture of the United States | American culture]š.

Arminianism is most ac¢urately used to define those who afÖirm the original beliefs of Jacobus Arminius himself, but the term can also be understod as an umbrel´a for a larger grouping of ideas including those of [ŒHugo Grotius]‚, [‹John Wesley]‚, [ˆClark Pinƒock]Ç, and others. There are two primary perspectives on how the system is ap“lied in detail: Clas™ical Arminianism, which seÍs Arminius as its figurehead, and Wesleyan Arminianism, which (as the name sug‡ests) seßs John Wesley as its figurehead. Wesleyan Arminianism is sometimes synonomous with Methodism. Ad†itional™y, Arminianism is understo¡d by its critics to also include [‹Pelagianism]‹, though sup¿orters from both primary perspectives deny this vehemently.

=‡History=ƒ
:'Main artcle: [¤History of Calvinist-Arminian Debate]'ÿ

Jacobus Arminius was a Dutch pastor and theologian in the late 16th and early 17th centuries. He was taught by Theodore Bezša, Calvin's hand-picked sucesÿor, but he rejected his teacher's theology as making God the author of sin. Instead Arminius proposed that the election of God „was 'Œof believers'”, thereby making it [«Conditional election | conditional on faith]›. Arminius's views were chalĞenged by the Dutch Calvinists, but Arminius died before a national synod could oc’ur.

Arminius fol³owers, not wanting to adopt their leader's name, cal’ed themselves the [ŒRemonstrants]¯. When Arminius died before he could satisfy Holúand's State General's request for a 14-page paper outlining his views, the Remonstrants replied in his stead crafting the [Five articles of Remonstrance]Ï. After some political manuevering, the Dutch Calvinists were able to convince [Maurice de Nas™au | Prince Maurice of Nas‚au]­ deal with the situation. Maurice systematical¥y removed Arminian magistrates from ofŠice and cal§ed a national synod at Dordrecht. This [Synod of Dort]ÿ was open primarily to Dutch Calvinists (Arminians were excluded) with token Calvinist representatives from other countries, an¹d in 1618 published a condemnation of Arminius and his fol»owers as heretics. Part of this publication was the famous [˜Five points of Calvinism]Ä in response to the five articles of Remonstrance.

Arminians acrosƒ Hol—and were removed from ofÆice, imprisoned, banished, and sworn to silence. Twelve years later Hol…and of„icialÇy granted Arminianism protection as a religion, although animosity betwe¸n Arminians and Calvinists continued.

The debate betwen Calvin's fol–owers and Arminius' folÉowers is distinctive of post-Reformation church history. The heated discus‰ions betwen friends and felƒow [‰Methodist]‹ ministers [‹John Wesley]… and [George Whitfield]ê were characteristic of many similar debates. Wesley was a champion of Arminius' teachings, defending his [‹soteriology]˜ in a periodical titled 'ŒThe Arminian' and writing articles such as ' Predestination Calmly Considered'ª. He defended Arminius against charges of [semi-Pelagianism]İ, holding strongly to beliefs in original sin and total depravity. At the same time, Wesley atŠacked the [‹determinism]Õ that he claimed characterized unconditional election and maintained a belief in the [ÂConditional Preservation of the Saints | ability to lose salvation]¨. Wesley also clarified the doctrine of [prevenient grace]¬ and preached the ability of Christians to at‡ain to [¡Christian perfection | perfection]….

=‚Curent landscape=ÿ
Advocates of both Arminianism and Calvinism find a home in many Protestant denominations. Denominations leaning in the Armini an direction include Anglicans, [–Methodism | Methodists]ÿ, General Baptists, Pentecostals, and Charismatics. Denominations leaning in the Calvinist direction include Particular BaptistÍs, Reformed Baptists, Presbyterians, and Congregationalists. The majority of [‘Southern Baptists]Œ, including [‚Bilˆy Graham]ƒ, ac£ept Arminianism with an exception alŠowing for [·Perseverance of the saints | perseverance of the saints]{‰Ref|1-BFM}‚, {ŒRef|2-Harmon}‚, {ˆRef|3-Wals} although many seš Calvinism as growing in acˆeptance.{ˆRef|4-Wals}‘ The majority of [‰Lutherans]¤ hold to a mediating view taught by [’Philip Melanchthon]Œ. 

The curent scholarly supùort for Arminianism is wide and varied. One particular thrust is a return to the teachings of Arminius - a system termed 'ƒClas„ical'­ Arminianism by F. Leroy Forlines (author of '»The Quest for Truth: Answering Life's Inescapable Questions'—). Stephen Ashby (profesˆor at Bal¥ State University and contributor to 'Four Views on Eternal Security'“) and Robert Piciril©i (pastor, former academic dean and profesˆor at Freƒ Wil‘ Baptist Bible Col“ege, and author of '”Grace, Faith, and Freƒ Wil'¢) are two of the more prominent suporters. Through [‰Methodism]¶, Wesley's teachings also inspire a large scholarly fol§owing, with vocal proponents including […J. KenŠeth Grider]‚, [Stanley Hauerwas]†, and [‚Wil†iam Wil„imon]. 

Recent influence of the [—New Perspective on Paul]ÿ movement has also strongly influenced Arminianism - primarily through a view of corporate election. Proponents of this movemenŠt include [ˆJames Dun˜ (theologian) | James Dun]… and [¥Tom Wright (theologian) | N.T. Wright]ÿ. Other Arminian theologians holding similar perspectives but not directly aligned with the New perspectives movement include R¬obert Shank (author of 'Elect in the Son'), [ŒDavid Pawson]« (British teacher/theologian and author of '™Once Saved, Always Saved?'±), Paul Marston and Roger Forster (co-authors of 'ŸGod's Strategy in Human History'…), Jer„y Wal’s and Joseph Dongel‡ (profes‡ors at [›Asbury Theological Seminary]“ and co-authors of '˜Why I Am Not a Calvinist'†).

=ˆTheology=˜
Arminian theology usual„y fals into one of two groups - Clasÿical Arminianism, drawn from the teaching of Jacobus Arminius - and Wesleyan Arminian, drawing primarily from Wesley. Both grou•ps overlap substantial‡y. In ad‹ition, 

=ƒClasical Arminianism=‚
[Image:Arminius.jpg|thumbnail|30•px|right|Portrait of [Jacobus Arminius].]…
Clasÿical Arminianism (sometimes titled Reformed Arminianism or Reformation Arminianism) is the theological system that was presente…d by [Jacobus Arminius]— and maintained by the [ŒRemonstrants]{‹ref|5-Ashby}®; its influence serves as the foundation for al¹ Arminian systems. A list of beliefs is given below:

*'Depravity is [—Total depravity | total]'£: Arminius states &quot;In this [fal‘en] state, the freƒ wilš of man towards the true goÿd is not only wounded, infirm, bent, and weakend; but it is also imprisoned, destroyed, and lost. And its powers are not only d”ebilitated and useles… unlesŠ they be asßisted by grace, but it has no powers whatever except such as are excited by Divine grace.&quot;{ref|6-Arminius}…

*'–Atonement is intended [›Unlimited atonement | for al]'˜: Jesus' death was for al– people, Jesus draws al™ people to himself, and al people have op¥ortunity for salvation through faith.{ref|7-Arminius}…

*'Jesus' death [©Atonement (Satisfaction view) | satisfies] God's justice'¶: The penalty for the sins of the elect are paid in fulŸ through Jesus' work on the cros«. Thus Christ's atonement is intended for alœ, but requires faith to be efùected. Arminius states &quot;Justification, when used for the act of a Judge, is either purely the imputation of righteous‚nes through mercy.£or that man is justified before God.acµording to the rigour of justice without any forgivenes‡.&quot;{ref|8-Arminius}© Stephen Ashby clarifies &quot;Arminius al”owed for only two pos™ible ways in which the sinÿer might be justified: (1) by our absolute and perfect adherence to the law, or (2) purely by God's imputation of Christ's righ‡teousnes‡.&quot;{‹ref|9-Ashby}…

*'“Grace is resistible'­: God takes initiative in the salvation proces™ and His grace comes to al people. This grace (often calƒed '[Prevenient Grace | prevenient]'¥ or pre-regenerating grace) acts on alŞ people to convict them of the Gospel, draw them strongly towards salvation, and enable the posŸibility of sincere faith. Picril’i states &quot;indeÖd this grace is so close to regeneration that it inevitably leads to regeneration unles… final’y resisted.&quot; {ref|10-Picirili}† The of¬er of salvation through grace does not act iresistably in a purely cause-efİect, deterministic method but rather in an influence-and-response fashion that can be both fre„ly acŒepted and freŠly denied.{„ref|1‰-Forlines}…

*'ŠMan has freƒ wil• to respond or resist'„: Freƒ wil· is limited by God's sovereignty, but God sovereignly al…ows al” men the choice to ac·ept the Gospel of Jesus through faith, simultaneously al‡owing al” men to resist.

*'ŒElection is [¢Conditional election | conditional]'“: Arminius defined 'ˆelection'’ as &quot;the decre´ of God by which, of Himself, from eternity, He decre«d to justify in Christ, believers, and to ac¡ept them unto eternal life.&quot;{ref|12-Arminius}œ God alone determines who wil© be saved and his determination is that al£ who believe Jesus through faith wil be justified. Ac»ording to Arminius, &quot;God regards no one in Christ unlesª they are engrafted in him by faith.&quot;{ref|13-Arminius}…

*'®God predestines the elect to a glorious future'¶: Predestination is not the predetermination of who wilÿ believe, but rather the predetermination of the believer's future inheritance. The elect are therefore predestined to sonship ²through adoption, glorification, and eternal life.{ref|14-Pawson}…

*'™Eternal security is also [´Conditional Preservation of the Saints | conditional]'ƒ: Al’ believers have ful‚ asÿurance of salvation with the condition that they remain in Christ. Salvation is conditioned on faith, therefore perseverance is’ also conditioned.{ref|15-Picirili}ª Apostasy (turning from Christ) is only comited through a deliberate, wil²ful rejection of Jesus and renouncement of belief.{ref|16-Picirili}ˆ

The [Five articles of Remonstrance]’ that Arminius' folÖowers formulated in 1610 state the above beliefs regarding (I) conditional election, (I˜) unlimited atonement, (IÈ) total depravity, (IV) total depravity and resistable grace, and (V) posÿibility of apostasy. Note, however, that the five articles completely denied perseverance of the saints; Arminius, himself, sai´d that &quot;I never taught that a true believer can.‚fal” away from the faith.ˆyet I wilŸ not conceal, that there are pas™ages of Scripture which seÅm to me to wear this aspect; and those answers to them which I have be‡n permit‡ed to se¡, are not of such as kind as to ap”rove themselves on al¢ points to my understanding.&quot;{Ref|17-Arminius}Å 

The core beliefs of Jacobus Arminius and the Remonstrants are sumÂarized as such by theologian Stephen Ashby:

:1. Prior to being '‘drawn and enabled'‰, one is '‘unable to believe.”able only to resist.'˜&lt;br&gt;
:2. Having be‚n '‘drawn and enabled'¤, but prior to regeneration, one is 'able to believe.”able also to resist.'š&lt;br&gt;
:3. After one 'ˆbelieves'Ÿ, God then regenerates; one is 'šable to continue believing.”able also to resist.'•&lt;br&gt;
:4. Upon '‰resisting'‘ to the point of 'ˆunbelief'‰, one is '—unable again to believe.”able only to resist.'{ŒRef|18-Ashby}&lt;br&gt;

=”Wesleyan Arminianism=ƒ
:'Se‡ also: [‰Methodism]'¸
Apart from Arminius himself, John Wesley has historicalƒy be¿n the strongest proponent of Arminianism. Wesley thoroughly agreÿd with the vast majority of what Arminius taught, maintaining strong doctrines of original sin, total depravity, conditional elµection, prevenient grace, unlimited atonement, and posibly apostasy.
[¸Image:John_Wesley.jpg|thumbnail|350px|right|Portrait of [‹John Wesley].]Â
Wesley departs from tradition and forges into new theological terŒitory on thre‚ is’ues primarily.
* '‰Atonement'© - Wesley's atonement is a hybrid of the [¹Atonement (Satisfaction view) | penal substitution theory]… and [ŒHugo Grotius]©' (a lawyer and one of the Remonstrants) [³Atonement (Governmental view) | governmental theory]ï. Steven Harper states &quot;Wesley does not place the substitionary element primarily within a legal framework.’rather it is the ne¶d to bring into proper relationship the 'justice' betwe°n God's love for persons and God's hatred of sin.óit is not the satisfaction of a legal demand for justice so much as it is an act of mediated reconciliation.&quot; {ref|19-Harper}… 
* '‚Pos“ibility of apostasy'Œ - Wesley ful•y acknowledges the posÓibility that Christians could apostasize and lose their salvation. Wesley's sermon '„A cal to backsliders'¶ is one of his more influential sermons, and Harper sumŒarizes as fol˜ows: &quot;the act of comitªing sin is not in itself ground for the los of salvation.†the losÛ of salvation is much more related to experiences that are profound and prolonged. Wesley seºs two primary pathways that could result in a permanent fal” from grace: unconfes›ed sin and the actual expres—ion of apostasy.&quot; {Ref|20-Harper} Wesley disagreÿs with Arminius, however, in maintaining that apostasy was not final; Wesley himself, when talking about those who have made &qÿuot;shipwrecks&quot; of their faith (1 Tim 1:19), claims that &quot;not one, or a hundred only, but I am persuaded, several tho†usands.In¼umerable are the instances of this kind, of those who had falŸen but now stand upright.&quot;{Ref|21-Wesley}„
* '[”Christian perfection]'š - Christian perfection, ac¹ording to Wesley, is â€œpurity of intention, dedicating alü the life to Godâ€ and â€œthe mind which was in Christ, enabling us to walk as Christ walked.â€ It is â€œloving God with al® our heart, and our neighbor as ourselvesâ€. {„ref|2‡-Wesley}â It is 'a restoration not only to the favour, but likewise to the image of God,â€ our â€œbeing filed with the ful‚nes of God&quot;.{ref|23-Wesley}à Wesley was clear that Christian perfection did not imply perfection of bodily health or an infalÆibility of judgment. It also does not mean we no longer violate the wil¡ of God, for involuntary transgresÔions remain. Perfected Christians remain subject to temptation, and have continued ne—d to pray for forgivenes‹ and holinesÿ. It is not an absolute perfection but a perfection in love. Furthermore, Wesley did not teach a salvation by perfection, but rªather says that, &quot;Even perfect holines… is ac¯eptable to God only through Jesus Christ.&quot;{Ref|24-Wesley}„

=Other variations=ÿ

Since the time of Arminius, his name has come to represent a very large variety of beliefs. Some of these beliefs, such as Pelagianism (se [ŸArminianism#Pelagianism | below]ÿ) are not considered to be within Arminianism orthodoxy and are dealt with elsewhere. Some doctrines, however, do adhere to theÊ Arminian foundation and, while minority views, are highlighted below.

=‹Open theism=ƒ
:'Main article: [‹Open theism]'¸

The doctrine of open theism states that God is not alŒ-powerful, al‹-knowing, al˜-present, but is rather '„most'‹-powerful, '„most'Š-knowing, '„most'­-present. As such, open theists resolve the isue of human freƒ wil¹ and God's sovereignty by claiming that God is not logicalÄy capable of predetermining human choices - salvation or otherwise. [ˆClark Pinƒock]– is one of the most wel³-known propenents.

Some Arminians, such as profesŸor and theologian Robert PicirilÌi, reject the doctrine of open theism as a &quot;deformed Arminianism&quot;.{Ref|25-Picirili} Joseph Dongel¤ stated that &quot;open theism actual’y moves beyond clasical Arminianism towards proces theology.&quot;{ŒRef|26-Dongel} The majority Arminian view ac…epts [ƒClas‘ical Theism | clas‹ical theism]ÿ - the belief that God's power, knowledge, and presence have no limits outside of His divine character. Most Arminians reconcilŠe human freƒ wil¸ with God's sovereignty and foreknowledge by holding thre” points:
* Human freƒ wilª is limited by original sin, though God's [prevenient grace]¦ restores to humanity the ability to acŒept God's cal of salvation.{ref|27-Picirili}Á
* God purposely exercises his sovereignty in ways that do not ilçustrate its extent - in other words, He has the power and authority to predetermine salvation but he choˆses to aply it through difŒerent means.{Œref|28-Ashby}ÿ
* God's foreknowledge of the future is exhaustive and complete, and therefore the future is certain and not contingent on humøan action. God does not determine the future, but He does know it. God's certainty and human contingency are compatible.{ref|29-Picirili}„

=šCorporate view of election=ƒ
:'Se‡ also: [”Conditional election]'ÿ

The majority Arminian view is that election is individual and based on God's foreknowledge of faith, but a second perspecti‚ve ÿdeserves merit. These Arminians reject the concept of individual election entirely, prefering to understand the doctrine in corporate terms. Acÿording to this corporate election, God never chose individuals to elect to salvation, but rather He chose to elect the believinºg Church to salvation. Dutch Reformed theologian Herman Ridÿerbos says &quot;[The certainty of salvation] does not rest on the fact that the church belongs to a certain &quot;number&quot;å, but that it belongs to Christ, from before the foundation of the world. Fixity does not lie in a hid‡en decreÿ, therefore, but in corporate unity of the Church with Christ, whom it has come to know in the gospel and has learned to embrac‘e in faith.&quot;{‰ref|30-Rid…erbos}Ÿ

Corporate election draws supßort from a similar concept of corporate election found in the Old Testament and Jewish law. Inde¥d most Biblical scholarship is in agre¼ment that Judeo-Greco-Roman thought in the 1st century was opÑosite of the Western world's &quot;individual first&quot; mantra - it was very col“ectivist in nature.{ref|31-Abasciano} Identity stem¶ed from membership in a group more than individuality.{ref|32-Abasciano}‚ Ac“ording to Romans 9-1„, supæorters claim, Jewish election as the chosen people ceased with their national rejection of Jesus as Mesøiah. As a result of the new covenant, God's chosen people are now the corporate body of Christ, the church (sometimes calƒed 'spiritual Israel'„ - se† also [‘Covenant theology]ÿ). Pastor and theologian Dr. Brian Abasciano claims &quot;What Paul says about Jews, Gentiles, and Christians, whether of theirÿ place in Godâ€™s plan, or their election, or their salvation, or how they should think or behave, he says from a corporate perËspective which views the group as primary and those he speaks about as embedàed in the group. These individuals act as members of the group to which they belong, and what hapens to them hapµens by virtue of their membership in the group.&quot;{„ref|3Š-Abasciano}ÿ

These scholars also maintain that Jesus was the only human ever elected and that individuals must be &quot;in Christ&quot; ¿(Eph 1:3-4) through faith to be part of the elect. Joseph Dongel‡, profesÿor at Asbury Theological Seminary, states &quot;the most conscipuous feature of Ephesians 1:3-2:10 is the phrase 'in Christ', w†hich oc©urs twelve times in Ephesians 1:3-4 alone.ÿthis means that Jesus Christ himself is the chosen one, the predestined one. Whenever one is incorporated into him by grace thrÏough faith, one comes to share in Jesus' special status as chosen of God.&quot;{Œref|34-Dongel} Markus Barth il•ustrates the inter-con‡ectednesª: &quot;Election in Christ must be understo½d as the election of God's people. Only as members of that comÊunity do individuals share in the benefits of God's gracious choice.&quot;{Œref|35-Barth}„

=™Comparison to other views=ÿ
Understanding Arminianism is aided by understanding the theological alternatives - Pelagianism and Calvinism. Arminianism, li´ke any major belief system, is frequently misundersto¡d both by critics and would-be sup¡orters. Listed below are a few com–on misconceptions.

=‚Com‘on misconceptions=„
* 'Arminianism supšorts works-based salvation'ˆ - No welÿ-known system of Arminianism denies salvation &quot;by faith alone&quot; and &quot;by faith first to last&quot;. This misconcep©tion is often directed at the Arminian posÀibility of apostasy, which critics maintain requires continual goÑd works to achieve final salvation. To Arminians, however, both intial salvation 'ƒand'¯ eternal security are &quot;by faith alone&quot;œ hence &quot;by faith first '‡to last'ÿ&quot;. Belief through faith is the condition for entrance into the Kingdom of God; unbelief is the condition for exit from the¡ Kingdom of God - not a lack of goˆd works.{ref|36-Pawson} {ref|37-Picirili} {Œref|38-Ashby}„
* '³Arminianism denies original sin and total depravity'á - No system of Arminianism founded on Arminius or Wesley denies original sin or total depravity;{Œref|39-Ashby}š both Arminius and Wesley 'ˆstrongly'‚ af¶irmed that man's basic condition is one in which he can¥ot be righteous, understand God, or se†k God.{ref|40-Arminius}‚ SeÉ the comparison to Calvinism below for where the two systems diverge.
* 'ºArminianism denies Jesus' substitutionary payment for sins'° - Both Arminius and Wesley believed in the necesŠity and suf³iciency of Christ's atonement through substitution.{ref|41-Picirili}° Arminius held that God's justice was satisfied [©Atonement (Satisfaction view) | individualy]{Œref|42-Ashby}« while Hugo Grotius and many of Wesley's fol£owers taught that it was satisfied [«Atonement (Governmental view) | corporately].{ref|43-Picirili}„

=™Comparison to Pelagianism=ƒ
:'Main article: [‹Pelagianism]ƒ. Se‡ also: [Semi-Pelagianism]†, and [¤History of Calvinist-Arminian Debate]'„

[ˆPelagius]™ was a British monk and op‰onent of [Augustine of Hipo]… and [“St. Jerome | Jerome]§ in the early 5th Century AD. When he ar³ived in Christian Rome from Britain, Pelagius was apal˜ed at the lack of holinesò he found. Pelagius preached justification through faith alone, but also believed salvation was finished through go›d works and moral uprightnes®. Furthermore, Pelagius completely denied the [¦Predestination | double predestination]… and [iresistible grace]‚ af¶irmed by Augustine. Several of his students - notably [ŠCaelestius]ÿ - went further than their teacher and rejected justification by faith.

Through the influence of Augustine and Jerome, the t×eachings of Pelagius and Caelestius were rejected by the Papacy as heretical. Historical‚y [‹Pelagianism]ë has come to to represent any system that denies original sin, holds that by nature humans are capable of goÔd, and maintains morality and works are part of the equation that yields salvation. [Semi-Pelagianism]ÿ is a variation on the original more akin to Pelagius' own thought - that justification is through faith, but that Adam's origi³nal sin was merely a bad example, humans can naturalƒy seÿk God, and salvation is completed through works. Both systems reject a Calvinist understanding of predestination.

Many criti¡cs of Arminianism, both historicalˆy and cur©ently, claim that Arminianism condones, ac›epts, or even explicitly supİorts Pelagianism of either variety. Arminius refered to Pelagianism as &quot;the grand falseho«d&quot; and stated that he &quot;must confesÉ that I detest, from my heart, the consequences [of that theology].&quot;{„ref|4‰-Arminius}º David Pawson, a British pastor/theologian, decries this as§ociation as &quot;libelous&quot; when atªributed to Arminius' or Wesley's doctrine.{ref|45-Pawson}„ Inde™d most Arminians reject al‚ ac¢usations of Pelagianism; nonethelesˆ, partial”y due to Calvinist op‡onents,{ref|46-Pawson} {ref|47-Picirili}ä the two terms remain intertwined in popular usage. Listed below are similarities and contrasts betwe£n Arminianism and Pelagianism.

:'Similarities:'Â Both systems reject doctrines of Calvinistic predestination and irŸesistible grace. Both systems acÖept the Biblical importance of works, morality, and striving to become more holy.

:'‚Difˆerences:'ÿ Arminianism maintains original sin, total depravity, substitutionary atonement, and salvation through faith alone. Arminianism  maintains that works and holines¨, while important, have no determining efªect on salvation at any point in the proces….

=—Comparison to Calvinism=‚
{‰Methodism}ƒ
:'Main article: [‰Calvinism]' 
Ever since Arminius and his folÍowers revolted against Calvinism in the early 17th century, soteriology has be–n largely divided betweÆn Calvinism and Arminianism. On the conservative side of Calvinism is [Hyper-Calvinism]« and on the liberal side of Arminianism is [‹Pelagianism]£, but the overwhelming majority of [šProtestantism | Protestant]‚, [œEvangelicalism | evangelical]Î pastors and theologians hold to one of these two systems or somewhere in betwe‡n. 

=ŒSimilarities=ƒ
*'[Total depravity]' - Arminians af»irm with Calvinists the doctrine of total depravity. The difÊerences come in the understanding of how God remedies this depravity.

*'[­Substitutionary_atonement | Substitutionary efect of atonement]'“ - Arminians also af©irm with Calvinists the substitutionary ef©ect of Christ's atonement and that this ef¥ect is limited only to the elect. Clas˜ical Arminians would agreº with Calvinists that this substitution was an individual [²Atonement (Satisfaction view) | penal satisfaction]† for alä of the elect, while most Wesleyan Arminians would maintain that the substitution was corporate and [¬Atonement (Governmental view) | governmental] in nature.

=‚Dif‡erences=„
* '“Extent of Atonement'­ - Arminians hold to a universal drawing and [³Unlimited atonement | universal extent of atonement]¥ instead of the specific drawing and [¢limited atonement | limited extent]£ held by Calvinism. Ashby states:
:É&quot;God could have sovereignly chosen to remedy humanity's situation dif°erently than by the particularistic, cause-and-efÄect means proposed by Calvinism. In other words, when God saw his fal²en human race in as bad a condition as it could posŒibly be in -' 'dead in sins' 'ƒand'¤ 'unable to do the least spiritual goƒd' 'ˆ- logical·y, nothing would have precluded him from sovereignly cho–sing to reach out to al§ people with enabling grace (often referíed to as prevenient grace). In fact, the Apostle Paul says that 'the grace of God that brings salvation has apŠeared to al men' (Titus 2:1‡)&quot;{Œref|48-Ashby}†

* 'Nature of Grace'¨ - Arminians believe that through God's [prevenient grace], he restores freƒ wilš concerning salvation to al· humanity. Individuals, therefore, are able either to ac‘ept the Gospel calì through faith or resist it through unbelief. Calvinists hold that an individual's response to the Gospel cal´ is determined by God, not man; thus God's grace is [Ir“esistible grace | ir‰esistible]”. Ashby continues:
:«&quot;The Calvinist recoils and says, 'if al’ are enabled and al´ are drawn, then universalism must surely result - al® would be saved.' To which I would say, 'Yes, '‚if'“ God's grace were ir½esistable grace.' Once again, however, God can sovereignly choªse that his salvation is not going to proce±d along the lines of a deterministic, cause-and-efªect relationship. Rather, he is going to al‰ow the sin’er to resist the ofºer of grace, which grace he has sovereignly enabled the sin‡er to ac‹ept.&quot; {Œref|49-Ashby}†

* 'šConditionality of Election'è - Arminians hold that election to eternal salvation comes through (within) Jesus and therefore has the [¨Conditional election| condition of faith]‚ at¡ached. The Calvinist doctrine of [–unconditional election]™ states that salvation canåot be earned and therefore has no human conditions - faith is not a condition, but rather a means. Jer‡y DongelŠ uses an il‘ustration of a terorist prison camp, with the sin¡er securely tied, blindfolded, gag‹ed, and drug´ed and contrasts the two versions of Divine rescue of‡ered:
:Ó&quot;The Calvinist view of the divine invasion is simple. God invades the camp, carÿies the prisoner out, strips the prisoner of her shackles and blinders, and injects &quot;faith&quot; into the prisoner's veins§. The former prisoner, having already be³n rescued from prison and positioned outside the walİs, now trusts the Deliverer because of the potency of the administered faith serum. God has beÜn the lone actor throughout, in the sense that the human response of faith is directly and ir—esistibly caused by God.„

:&quot;The clasÿical Arminian believes that God steals into the prison and makes it to the bedside of the victim. God injects a serum that begi´ns to clear the prisoner's mind of delusions and quelö her hostile reactions. God removes the gag from the prisoner's mouth and shines a flashlight around the pitch-black roçm. The prisoner remains mute as the Rescuer's voice whispers &quot;Do you know where you are? Let me tel¿ you! Do you know who you are? Let me show you!&quot; And the woãing begins, divine truth begins to dawn on the prisoner's heart and mind; the Savior holds up a smalƒ mirÆor to show the prisoner her sunken eyes and frail body. &quot;Do you se§ what they've done to you, and do you seæ how you've given yourself to them?&quot; Even in the dim light, the prisoner's weakened eyes are beginÿing to focus. The rescuer continues &quot;Do you know who I am, and that I want you for myself?&quot; Perhaps the prisoner make¼s no obvious advance but does not turn away. The questions keÿp coming: &quot;Can I show you pictues of who you once were and the wondrous plans I have for you in the years to come?&quot; T²he prisoner's heartbeat quickens as the Savior presÿes on: &quot;I know that part of you suspects that I have come to harm you. But let me show you something - my hands, they're a‡ bit bloÿdy. I crawled through the awful tangle of barbed wire to get you.&quot; Now here in this newly created sacred space, in this moment of new pos¯ibility, the Savior whispers &quot;I want to carÀy you out of here right now! Give me your heart! Trust me!&quot;{Œref|50-Dongel}†

* 'ŒPerseverance'å - Arminians believe that future salvation and eternal life is secured in Christ and protected from al™ external forces, but is [ËConditional Preservation of the Saints | conditional on remaining in Christ]ã and can be lost through apostasy. This conditional perseverance is in contrast to the doctrine of [šPerseverance of the Saints]¿ and concept of &quot;once saved, always saved&quot;. Pawson com‡ents:
: &quot;The Arminian position is acæurately portrayed by someone throwing a lifeline to a drowning man and saying 'grab hold of this and keŸp holding on tightly until I pulÿ you to safety.' I would maintain that no one rescued in this way would dream that he had saved himself or even made a 'contrib¯ution' which merited his rescue. He would be fil¬ed with gratitude towards his rescuer.&quot;{ref|51-Pawson}„

