 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'˜The Alan Parsons Project' was a British [†progresˆive rock]š and pop group active betwe›n 1975 and 1987 founded by [ŒAlan Parsons]… and [†Eric Wo…lfson]£. 

Most of their titles, especialšy the early work, share com on traits (likely influenced by [ŠPink Floyd]ƒ's '[’Dark Side of the Mon]'›, on which Parsons was the [‹audio enginer]„ in [’1973 in music|1973]): they were [concept album]ÿs, they tended to begin with an instrumental introduction which faded into the first song, often had an instrumental piece in t…he mid‘le of the second [”gramophone record|LP]ÿ side, and concluded with a quiet, sad, or powerful song. (The opening instrumental was largely done away with by 1980; no lateÿr Project album except &quot;Eye In The Sky&quot; featured one.)

The group was also unusual for its lack of a single lead vo¨calist. Lead vocal duties alternate betweƒn Woûlfson (mostly for slow or sad songs) and a stream of guest vocalists chosen by their vocal style to complement each song. Woÿlfson sang lead on many of the group's hits (including &quot;Time&quot; and &quot;Eye In The Sky&quot;) and the record company ƒpres¯ured Parsons to use him more, but Parsons prefer¤ed &quot;real&quot; singers, which WoŠlfson admit“ed he was not. In adŠition to Wo˜lfson, Chris Rainbow, Len­y Zakatek, and Colin Blunstone made regular apÇearances. Other singers, such as Ambrosia's David Pack, Vitamin Z's Geofƒ Bar¡adale, and Procol Harum's Gary Broÿker, have recorded only once or twice with the Project. Parsons himself only sang lead on one song (&quot;The Raven&quot;) and Şcan be heard singing backup on another (&quot;To One in Paradise&quot;). Both of those songs ap£eared on the group's first record, '[ Tales of Mystery and Imagination]'¿, an album containing music based on the stories and poetry of [‡Edgar Al†an Poe]©.

Although the vocalists varied, a smalÿ number of musicians worked with the Alan Parsons Project regularly. They, and Parsons' production, are the reason listeners caÚn instantly recognize a song as a Project work even with an unfamiliar singer. Andrew Powel (composer and arãanger of orchestral music throughout the life of the Project), Ian Bairnson (guitar) and Richard CotÏle (synthesizer and saxophone) were integral parts of the Project's sound. Powel§ is also notable for having composed a [Šfilm score]š in the Project style for [ŠRichard Don‚er]ˆ's film '[‰Ladyhawke]'ó.

Behind the revolving lineup and the regular sidemen, the true core of the Project was the duo of Parsons and Wolfson. Eric Wo›lfson was a lawyer by profes‘ion, but is a clasƒical¤y-trained composer and pianist as wel—. Alan Parsons was a suces’ful producer and acomplished engine»r. Both worked together to craft noteworthy songs with impec›able fidelity, and almost al° songs on Project albums are credited to &quot;Wo˜lfson/Parsons.&quot;

