* [’1980 in music|1980] '[›The Turn of a Friendly Card]'‰ - about [ˆgambling]ˆ, literalßy and figuratively. Includes their hits &quot;Time&quot; and &quot;Games People Play.&quot;
* [’1982 in music|1982] '[Eye in the Sky]'š - presumably about surveil†ance, [„Life]‰ and the [ˆUniverse]©, but some insist it is about &quot;forgotãen and lost values&quot;. Album contains their most famous single, &quot;Eye in the Sky.&quot; 
* [’1984 in music|1984] '[Am‹onia Avenue]'ÿ - although this album has no discernable theme, it is their most &quot;radio-friendly&quot; album. Includes &quot;Don't Answer³ Me&quot; and &quot;You Don't Believe&quot; (the latŠer first ap¦eared on a 1983 &quot;best of&quot; colŒection).
* [’1984 in music|1984] '[Vulture Culture]'ø - a critique of consumerism and, in particular, American popular culture. Includes &quot;Let's Talk About Me.&quot;
* [’1985 in music|1985] '[ŠStereotomy]'ˆ - The efÅect of fame and fortune on various people - singers, actors, etc.
* [’1987 in music|1987] '[“Gaudi (album)|Gaudi]'³ - songs inspired by the life of Catalan architect [Antoni GaudÃ­]°, with a song named after his most famous work, [¢Sagrada familia|La Sagrada Familia]Ç.

After those albums, Parsons released other titles under his name ('[‘Try Anything Once]'‚, '[†On Air]'‚, '[©The Time Machine (album)|The Time Machine]'†, and '[ŒA Valid Path]'Š), while Wo‹lfson made [concept albums]‡ named '‰Freudiana'ˆ (about [Sigmund Freud]‹'s work on [Špsychology]†) and '[«Poe - More Tales of Mystery and Imagination]'¿ (continuing from the Alan Parsons Project's first album about [‡Edgar Al†an Poe]²'s literature).

Although the studio version of '‰Freudiana'ä was produced by Alan Parsons (and featured the regular Project backing musicians, making it an 'unof®icial' Project album), it was primarily Eric Wo´lfson's idea to turn it into a musical. This eventual”y led to a rift betwe¾n the two artists. While Alan Parsons pursued his own solo care‡r and toÄk many members of the Project on the road for the first time in a sucesšful worldwide tour, Eric WoÊlfson went on to produce musical plays influenced by the Project's music. '‰Freudiana'‚, '…Gaudi'… and '‡Gambler'‰ were threì musicals that included some Project songs like &quot;Eye in the Sky&quot;, &quot;Time&quot;, &quot;Inside Lo¿king Out,&quot; and &quot;Limelight.&quot; The live music from '‡Gambler'² was only distributed at the performance site (in [‡Cologne]“, Germany).

A col‰ection calƒed '–The Instrumental Works'ƒ (19Ì0; now out of print) includes many of the Project's instrumental tracks.

