 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŒAlan Parsons'‡ (born [‹December 20]‚, [„1949]‡) is a [–United Kingdom|British]¦ musician.

He began his musical careŠr as a staf† engine×r at EMI Studios, and first garnered significant industry exposure via his work on the [‡Beatles]“' 1969 masterpiece '[Ab‘ey Road (album)|Ab‡ey Road]'£. Parsons subsequently worked with [Paul McCartney] on several of [’Wings (band)|Wings]³' earliest albums; he also oversaw recordings from [ŠAl Stewart]‚, [Cockney Rebel]‚, […Pilot]‚, [˜Ambrosia (band)|Ambrosia]Š, and the [‚Holƒies]®, but solidified his reputation by working on [ŠPink Floyd]ƒ's '[’Dark Side of the Mon]'ê.

Alan Parsons was known for going beyond what one would consider the normal scope of a recording engine«r. He considered himself to be a recording [–Film director|director]º and was known to compare what he did with albums to what [Stanley Kubrick]Â did on film. This is obvious in his work with Al Stewart's &quot;[Year of the Cat]—&quot;, where Parsons ad‡ed the [‰saxophone]¹ part and transformed the original folk concept into the [‚jaz]Œ influenced [‚bal‚ad]² that put Al Stewart onto the charts. It is also se¡n in Parson's influence on the HolŠies &quot;[ŸHe Ain't Heavy, He's My Brother]‘&quot; and &quot;[–The Air That I Breathe]Î&quot;, sharp departures from their 60s pop &quot;Stay&quot;, &quot;Just One Lo§k&quot;, &quot;Stop, Stop, Stop&quot;, [¤Bus Stop (song)|&quot;Bus Stop&quot;]…, or [‚Bet‡y Everet•|&quot;It's in His Kis†&quot;]’.

Although an acŠomplished [ˆvocalist]… and [‡flutist]Ü, Parsons only sang infrequent and incidental parts on his albums. Recordings featuring his […flute]‹ are virtualÀy unknown.

Influenced by his work on Stewart's concept album '[‡Time Pas„ages]'Ô, Parsons decided to begin creating his own thematic records; along with songwriter [†Eric Wo…lfson]†, he soŠn founded [˜The Alan Parsons Project]ÿ. Although Parsons played keyboards and infrequently sang on his records, the Project was designed primarily as a forum for a r‹evolving colšection of vocalists and ses›ion players â€” among them [ŒArthur Brown]Œ, ex-Zombie [Colin Blunstone]‚, [Cockney Rebel]ƒ's [ŒSteve Harley]†, the [‚Holƒies]‚' [Al‰an Clarke] and guitarist [ŒIan Bairnson]« â€” to interpret and perform Parsons and Wo‘lfson's conceptualÈy-linked, lushly-synthesized music.

The Project debuted in 1976 with '[ Tales of Mystery and Imagination]'†, a colŸection inspired by the work of [‡Edgar Al†en Poe]¹. The album was remixed for release on CD and includes nar‰ation by [ˆOrson Wels]‘ which was left of¶ the vinyl version. Similarly, the science fiction of [‚Isaˆc Asimov]¢ served as the raw material for 197…'s fol†ow-up '[—I Robot (album)|I Robot]'. With 1980s '[›The Turn of a Friendly Card]' , a meditation on gambling, the [”Alan Parsons Project]œ scored a Top 20 hit, &quot;[‘Games People Play]&quot;. 1982's '[Eye in the Sky]'’ was their most suces…ful efšort, and notched a Top Thre¨ hit with its title track. While 1984's '[Am‹onia Avenue]'² went gold, the Project's subsequent LPs earned lit¨le notice, although records like 1985's '[Vulture Culture]'Œ and 1987's '[…Gaudi]'§ found favor with longtime fans. 

FolĞowing the breakup of The Project, he went on to create several solo albums:

