 ƒ<comÒent>Note there are an infinite number of primes (to contrast with usage of &quot;al but finitely many&quot;)</com†ent>
 <text xml:space="preserve">In [‹mathematics], the phrase 'ˆalmost al'´ has a number of specialised uses.

&quot;Almost al²&quot; is sometimes used synonymously with &quot;al… but [†finite]Œly many&quot;‚ se [†almost]ˆ.

In [number theory]…, if 'P'('n'œ) is a property of positive [‡integer]Šs, and if 'p'('N'ª) denotes the number of positive integers 'n'ƒ les† than 'N'‹ for which 'P'('n'•) holds, and if 

:'p'('N'‚)/'N'ˆ &amp;rar‡; 1 as 'N'ˆ &amp;rar“; &amp;infin;

(se […limit]š), then we say that &quot;'P'('n'”) holds for almost al“ positive integers 'n'¥&quot; and write
:&lt;math&gt;(\foral³^\infty n) P(n)&lt;/math&gt;. 

For example, the [”prime number theorem]› states that the number of [prime numbers]ƒ les’ than or equal to 'N' is asymptotical‹y equal to 'N'„/ln 'N'½. Therefore the proportion of prime integers is roughly 1/ln 'N'š, which tends to 0. Thus, 'ˆalmost al'· positive integers are composite, however there are stil£ an infinite number of primes.

Oc†asional‘y, &quot;almost al¥&quot; is used in the sense of &quot;[‘almost everywhere]Š&quot; in [measure theory]«, or in the closely related sense of &quot;[almost surely]Š&quot; in [’probability theory]….

