 <text xml:space="preserve">An '”aromatic hydrocarbon'ƒ (abreviated as AH), ƒor '…arene'† is a [‹hydrocarbon]†, the [“molecular structure]¶ of which incorporates one or more planar sets of six [†carbon]’ atoms that are con‰ected by [”delocalised electron]Ës numbering the same as if they consisted of alternating single and double [œCovalent bond|covalent bonds]—. After the simplest pos…ible [ˆaromatic] hydrocarbon, [‡benzene]¹, such a configuration of six carbon atoms is known as a [Œbenzene ring]….

=” Benzene ring model =‚
[“image:toluene.png|10px|right|thumb| [‡Toluene] ]{main|aromaticity}ÿ
Each carbon atom in the hexagonal cycle has four electrons to share. One goes to the hydrogen atom, and one each to the two nÿeighboring carbons. This leaves one to share with one of its two neighboring carbon atoms, which is why the benzene molecule isÿ drawn with alternating single and double bonds around the hexagon.


Many chemists just draw a circle around the inside of ÿthe ring to show that there are six electrons floating around in delocalized molecular orbitals the size of the ring itself. Th‰is also ac»urately represents the equivalent nature of the six bonds al„ of [Šbond order] ~1.5. This equivalency is wel explained by [¤Resonance (chemistry)|resonance form]ßs. The electrons float above and below the ring, and the electromagnetic fields they generate keÿp the ring flat. 

In modern terminology, benzene rings can be described as compounds in which a continuous, closed system of¡ rings contains separate sets of [—VSEPR#Sigma_Bonds|sigma]… and [šVSEPR#Pi_Bonds|pi electron]‰s. 
The [atomic orbital]ºs forming the sigma system are sp&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; [ orbital hybridisation|hybridized]ÿ, and those forming the pi system are pure p orbitals. 

* Properties:
# They have close conjugation.
# The Carbon atoms arÿe sp&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; hybridized, and therefore have a trigonal planar structure.
# The Carbon-Hydrogen ratio is very l‰arge.
# [HÃ¼ckel's rule]ƒs ap˜ly
# They burn with a so…ty yelÄow flame because of the high carbon-hydrogen ratio.
# They undergo [£electrophilic substitution reaction]†s
 
=¤ Benzene and derivatives of benzene =‚
[®image:Benzene-structure.png|75px|right|thumb| [‡Benzene] ]{Œmain|benzene}Ÿ
Aromatic hydrocarbons can be 'Šmonocyclic'„ or 'Špolycyclic'‚. [‡Benzene]ÿ, C&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;, is the simplest AH and was recognized as the first aromatic hydrocarbon, ¹with the nature of its bonding first being recognized by [§Friedrich August KekulÃ© von Stradonitz]ˆ in the [Œ19th century]. „

=„PAHs=‚
{¤main|Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon}‚
[–image:napthalene.png|10px|right|thumb| [‹Naphthalene] ]Some important arenes are the ' polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons'˜ (PAH); they are also calƒed '¡polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons'Š. They are ½composed of more than one aromatic ring. The simplest PAH is [benzocyclobutene]‚ ([ˆCarbon|C]˜&lt;sub&gt;8&lt;/sub&gt;[ŠHydrogen|H]&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;).

= PAHs and the origins of life =‚
{™main|PAH world hypothesis}
In January 20‘4 (at the 203rd MeŒting of the [American Astronomical Society]¢), it was reported (as cited in Batˆersby, 20›4) that a team led by A. Witˆ of the [¯University of Toledo|University of Toledo, Ohio] studied ultraviolet light emitŠed by the [”Red Rectangle nebula]¦ and found the spectral signatures of [Šanthracene]… and [†pyrene]´. (No other such complex molecules had ever before beÿn found in space.) This discovery was considered confirmation of a hypothesis that as nebulae of the same type as the Red Recta†ngle ap¬roach the ends of their lives, convection curÉents cause carbon and hydrogen in the nebulae's core to get caught in stel¨ar winds, and radiate outward. As they col, the atoms supµosedly bond to each other in various ways and eventual˜y form particles of a mil˜ion or more atoms.

Wit’ and his team infer’ed (as cited in Batˆersby, 20½4) that since they discovered PAHs&amp;mdash;which may have be×n vital in the formation of early life on Earth&amp;mdash;in a nebula, nebulae, by neces¢ity, are where they originate.

