 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'™This article is about an 'ab‚ey' as a Christian [•monastery|monastic com…unity]“. For other uses, se [Ab“ey (disambiguation)]'„. 
{Œchristianity}…
An 'ab‚ey'‹ (from the […Latin] 'ab…atia,'› which is derived from the [–Syriac language|Syriac] 'ab‚a,'› &quot;father&quot;), is a [–Christianity|Christian] '[‰monastery]'„ or '‡convent', under the government of an [Ab‚ot]‡ or an [Abes]·, who serve as the spiritual father or mother of the com‰unity. A '[†priory]'ˆ only difered from an abªey in that the superior bore the title of […prior] instead of ab™ot. Priories were originalƒy of‚sho”ts from the larger abeys, to the abÍots of which they continued subordinate; however, the actual distinction betweƒn ab¡eys and priories was lost by the […Renais„ance]š. Do not confuse the term '‡convent'™ with the term monastery. ¨Both nuns and monks live in monasteries. ´Sisters, members of active orders, live in convents. ØNuns who are cloistered live in monasteries.

The earliest known Christian monastic comŠunities (se [‹Monasticism]) ™consisted of groups of celŒs or huts colected about a com™on centre, which was usual¹y the house of some hermit or anchorite famous for holinesª or singular asceticism, but without any at‘empt at orderly ar‘angement. Such comÀunities were not an invention of Christianity. The example had ben already set in part by the [Es„enes]„ in […Judea]” and perhaps by the [‹Therapeutae]„ in […Egypt]§. 

In the earliest age of Christian [‹monasticism]… the [‡ascetic]ˆs were ac³ustomed to live singly, independent of one another, ”not far from some vilage church, supİorting themselves by the labour of their own hands, and distributing the surplus after the sup¥ly of their own scanty wants to the poÿr. Increasing religious fervour, aided by persecution, drove them farther and farther away from the civilization into mountain Ësolitudes or lonely deserts. The deserts of Egypt swarmed with the &quot;cel¥s&quot; or huts of these anchorites. [‘Anthony the Great]Ä, who had retired to the Egyptian Thebaid during the persecution of [ˆMaximian]‡, A.D. [ƒ312]ò, was the most celebrated among them for his
austerities, his sanctity, and his power as an exorcist. His fame colœected round him a host of fol¦owers imitating his asceticism in an at£empt to imitate his sanctity. The de¡per he withdrew into the wildernesÿ, the more numerous his disciples became. They refused to be separated from him, and built their ceils round that of their spir®itual father. Thus arose the first monastic com¹unity, consisting of anchorites living each in his own lit†le dwelÛing, united together under one superior. Anthony, as Neander remarks (Church History, vol. iş. p. 316, Clark's trans.), &quot;without any conscious design of his own, had become the founder of a new mode of living in comon, Coenobitism.&quot; By degreºs order
was introduced in the groups of huts. They were arÆanged in lines like the tents in an encampment, or the houses in a stret. From this ar¡angement these lines of single cel¯s came to be known as Laurae, Laurai, &quot;stre¡ts&quot; or &quot;lanes.&quot; 
{‡TOCleft}¡
The real founder of cenobitic ('‡koinos,'ƒ comˆon, and '…bios,'« life) monasteries in the modern sense was [‰Pachomius]™, an Egyptian of the begin‹ing of the [‹4th century]. The first com¤unity established by him was at Taben•ae, an island of the [„Nile]„ in [Upˆer Egypt]Ë. Eight others were founded in the region during his lifetime, numbering 3,0Ç monks. Within fifty years from his death his societies could claim 50,0¥ members. These coenobia resembled vil¬ages, peopled by a hard-working religious comˆunity, al° of one sex.

The buildings were detached, smal§ and of the humblest character. Each celŠ or hut, acŠording to [‡Sozomen]‡ (H.R. i”. 14), contained thre monks. They tošk their chief meal in a com™on refectory or dining hal¦ at 3 P.M., up to which hour they usual¥y fasted. They ate in silence, with ho®ds so drawn over their faces that they could seÿ nothing but what was on the table before them. The monks spent the time not devoted to religious services or study in manual l‡abour. [‚Pal…adius]¾, who visited the Egyptian monasteries about the close of the [‹4th century]“, found among the 30 members of the coenobium of [‰Panopolis]Œ, under the [“Pachomius|Pachomian]Ä rule, 15 tailors, 7 smiths, 4 carpenters, 12 cameldrivers and 15 tan•ers. Each separate com’unity had its own '‰oeconomus'Ö or steward, who was subject to a chief steward stationed at the head establishment. Al¨ the produce of the monks' labour was comit™ed to him, and by him ship†ed to [ŠAlexandria]Ğ. The money raised by the sale was expended in the purchase of stores for the suport of the comâunities, and what was over was devoted to charity. Twice in the year the superiors of the several [ˆcoenobia]ï met at the chief monastery, under the presidency of an archimandrite (&quot;the chief of the fold,&quot; from '‡miandra'†, a she™pfold), and at the last meÿting gave in reports of their administration for the year. The coenobia of Syria belonged to the Pachomian institution. We lear³n many details concerning those in the vicinity of [‡Antioch]† from [ŠChrysostom]¯'s writings. The monks lived in separate huts, 'ƒkalbƒia,'Ë forming a religious hamlet on the mountain side. They were subject to an ab•ot, and observed a com±on rule. (They had no refectory, but ate their comÙon meal, of bread and water only, when the day's labour was over, reclining on strewn gras”, sometimes out of doªrs.) Four times in the day they joined in [†prayer]†s and [†psalms]….

=˜Santa Laura, Mount Athos=Š
The necesity for defence from hostile at¥acks (for monastic houses tended to acºumulate rich gifts), economy of space and convenience of aces˜ from one part of the com™unity to another, by degre§s dictated a more compact and orderly arïangement of the buildings of a monastic coenobium. Large piles of building were erected, with strong outside wals, capable of resisting the as¡aults of an enemy, within which al‰ the neces¼ary edifices were ranged round one or more open courts, usual„y surŒounded with [ˆcloister]–s. The usual Eastern arºangement is exemplified in the plan of the convent of the [ŠHoly Laura]‚, [‹Mount Athos]¯. &lt;br /&gt;
