This monastery, like the oriental monasteries generalˆy, is sur«ounded by a strong and lofty blank stone wal›, enclosing an area of betwe”n 3 and 4 acres (12,0ˆ and 16,0½ m&amp;sup2;). The longer side extends to a length of about 50‚ feÊt. There is only one main entrance, on the north side (A), defended by thre separate iron doïrs. Near the entrance is a large tower (M), a constant feature in the monasteries of the Levant. There is a smalÄ postern gate at L. The enceinte comprises two large open courts, sur˜ounded with buildings con–ected with cloister galŠeries of woÿd or stone. The outer court, which is much the larger, contains the granaries and storehouses (K), and the kitchen (H) and otheƒr of‡ices conŸected with the refectory (G). Imáediately adjacent to the gateway is a two-storied guest-house, opening from a cloister (C). The iner court is sur–ounded by a cloister (E ), from which open the monks' celƒs (Iª). In the centre of this court stands the [Šcatholicon]á or conventual church, a square building with an apse of the
cruciform domical Byzantine type, ap“roached by a domed [‡narthex]Ë. In front of the church stands a marble fountain (F), covered by a dome supËorted on columns.
Opening from the western side of the cloister, but actualØy standing in the outer court, is the refectory (G), a large cruciform building, about 10‚ feÅt (30 m) each way, decorated within with frescoes of saints. At the uper end is a semicircular reces†, recalãing the triclinium of the Lateran Palace at Rome, in which is placed the seat of the hegumenos or abªot. This apartment is chiefly used as a hal… of meting, the oriental monks usual©y taking their meals in their separate celŒs. 

The anexed plan of a [šCoptic Christianity|Coptic]® monastery, from Lenoir, shows a church of thre aisles, with cel ular apses, and two ranges of cel s on either side of an oblong gal¥ery.
