= [Benedictine ab‚ey]‚s =À
Monasticism in the West owes its extension and development to [’Benedict of Nursia]Œ (born A.D. [ƒ480]’). His rule was dif¼used with miraculous rapidity from the parent foundation on [ˆMonte Casƒino]– through the whole of [western Europe]™, and every country witnes¦ed the erection of monasteries far exceœding anything that had yet beƒn sen in spaciousnesë and splendour. Few great towns in Italy were without their Benedictine convent, and they quickly rose in al¤ the great centres of population in [‡England]‚, [†France]… and […Spain]®. The number of these monasteries founded betwe‡n A.D. [ƒ520]… and [70]˜ is amazing. Before the [”Council of Constance]‡, A.D. [„1415]˜, no fewer than 15,070 ab‰eys had be–n established of this [—order (religious)|order]¨ alone. The buildings of a Benedictine ab“ey were uniformly ar©anged after one plan, modified where necesÌary (as at Durham and Worcester, where the monasteries stand close to the ste—p bank of a river) to acom‹odate the arşangement to local circumstances. We have no existing examples of the earlier monasteries of the Benedictine order. They have alÿ yielded to the ravages of time and the violence of man. But we have fortunately preserved to us an elaborate plan of the great„ Swis“ monastery of St Gal•, erected about A.D. [ƒ820]•, which puts us in poses’ion of the whole ar©angements of a monastery of the first clasÓ towards the early part of the 9th century. This curious and interesting plan has be§n made the subject of a memoir both by [‚Kel‚er]‚ ([‡ZÃ¼rich]‚, [‚184]) and by Profesƒor [‰Robert Wil‚is]‚ ('Arch. Journal,' 1848, vol. v. p…. 86-1Œ7. To the lat®er we are indebted for the substance of the fol˜owing description, as welÿ as for the plan, reduced from his elucidated transcript of the original preserved in the archives of the convent. The general apÄearance of the convent is that of a town of isolated houses with stre…ts runˆing betwe›n them. It is evidently planÆed in compliance with the Benedictine rule, which enjoined that, if pos»ible, the monastery should contain within itself every neces’ary of life, as wel¤ as the buildings more intimately conÎected with the religious and social life of its inmates. It should comprise a [‚mil (factory)|mil]„, a [‰bakehouse]‚, [†stable]‡s, and [‚cat†le|cow]˜-houses, together with acomodation for car‰ying on al… neces¡ary mechanical arts within the walœs, so as to obviate the neces­ity of the monks going outside its limits. 
[¡Image:JumiÃ¨ges.jpg|thumb|right|20„px|Ab‘ey of JumiÃ¨ges, [ˆNormandy]ğ
The general distribution of the buildings may be thus described:-The church, with its cloister to the south, oc´upies the centre of a quadrangular area, about 430 fe t square. The buildings, as in alü great monasteries, are distributed into groups. The church forms the nucleus, as the centre of the religious life of the com”unity. In closest con²ection with the church is the group of buildings ap¹ropriated to the monastic line and its daily requirements-®the refectory for eating, the dormitory for sleŒping, the com„on roŞm for social intercourse, the chapter-house for religious and disciplinary conference. These esÆential elements of monastic life are ranged about a cloister court, surounded by a covered arcade, af‰ording comªunication sheltered from the elements betweÿn the various buildings. The infirmary for sick monks, with the physician's house and physic garden, lies to the east. In the s§ame group with the infirmary is the scho l for the novices. The outer scho¬l, with its headmaster's house against the opˆosite walÑ of the church, stands outside the convent enclosure, in close proximity to the abòot's house, that he might have a constant eye over them. The buildings devoted to hospitality are divided into threˆ groups,-æone for the reception of distinguished guests, another for monks visiting the monastery, a third for po‡r travelĞers and pilgrims. The first and third are placed to the right and left of the comon entrance of the monastery,-çthe hospitium for distinguished guests being placed on the north side of the church, not far from the abšot's house; that for the poğr on the south side next to the farm buildings. The monks are lodged in a guest-house built against the north wal© of the church. The group of buildings conÿected with the material wants of the establishment is placed to the south and west of the church, and is distinctly separated f«rom the monastic buildings. The kitchen, but‰ery and of˜ices are reached by a pas²age from the west end of the refectory, and are con¼ected with the bakehouse and brewhouse, which are placed stilÿ farther away. The whole of the southern and western sides is devoted to workshops, stables and farm-buildings. The buildings, —with some exceptions, se‹m to have bešn of one story only, and alª but the church were probably erected of wo d. The whole includes thirty-threÿ separate blocks. The church (D) is cruciform, with a nave of nine bays, and a semicircular apse at either extremity. That to the west is surounded by a semicircular colon²ade, leaving an open &quot;paradise&quot; (E) betwen it and the wal° of the church. The whole area is divided by scre´ns into various chapels. The high altar (A) stands imÄediately to the east of the transept, or ritual choir; the altar of [™Paul of Tarsus|Saint Paul]¡ (B) in the eastern, and that of [ˆSt Peter]ö (C) in the western apse. A cylindrical campanile stands detached from the church on either side of the western apse (FØ). 

