church has ošn its east side the &quot;[‡pisalis]&quot; or &quot;[‹calefactory]“&quot;, (H), the com…on sit…ing-ro±m of the brethren, warmed by flues beneath the flo¼r. On this side in later monasteries we invariably find the [chapter house]À, the absence of which in this plan is somewhat surprising. It apÿears, however, from the inscriptions on the plan itself, that the north walk of the cloisters served for the purposes of a chap•ter-house, and was fitÉed up with benches on the long sides. Above the calefactory is the &quot;[‰dormitory]Î&quot; opening into the south transept of the church, to enable the monks to at§end the nocturnal services with readines†. A pas¨age at the other end leads to the &quot;[„neces…arium]º&quot; (I), a portion of the monastic buildings always plan¬ed with extreme care. The southern side is ocÿupied by the &quot;refectory&quot; (K), from the west end of which by a vestibule the kitchen (L) is reached. This is separated´ from the main buildings of the monastery, and is con’ected by a long pasËage with a building containing the bake house and brewhouse (M), and the sle†ping-ro™ms of the servants. The upÿer story of the refectory is the &quot;vestiarium,&quot; where the ordinary clothes of the brethren were kept. On the western s½ide of the cloister is another two story building (N). The cel•ar is below, and the [†larder]Œ and store-rom above. Betwe°n this building and the church, opening by one doÿr into the cloisters, and by another to the outer part of the monastery area, is the &quot;parlour&quot; for interviews with viÛsitors from the external world (O). On the eastern side of the north transept is the &quot;[‹scriptorium]“&quot; or writing-roÿm (P1), with the library above. 

To the east of the church stands a group of buildings comprising two miniature conventual eÆstablishments, each complete in itself. Each has a covered cloister surÿounded by the usual buildings, i.e. refectory, dormitory, etc., and a church or chapel on one side, placed back to back. A detaäched building belonging to each contains a bath and a kitchen. One of these diminutive convents is ap—ropriated to the &quot;[†oblati]¿&quot; or novices (Q), the other to the sick monks as an &quot;[‰infirmary]ÿ&quot; (R). 

The &quot;residence of the physicians&quot; (S) stands contiguous to the infirmary, and the physic garden (T) a¹t the north-east corner of the monastery. Besides other roËms, it contains a drug store, and a chamber for those who are dangerously il˜. The &quot;house for bloƒdletÉing and purging&quot; adjoins it on the west (U). 

The &quot;outer scho¿l,&quot; to the north of the convent area, contains a large scho‚lrom divided acros‡ the mid‹le by a scre–n or partition, and surounded by fourte„n lit„le ro’ms, termed the dwel¶ings of the scholars. The head-master's house (W) is op osite, built against the side wal of the church. The two &quot;[ˆhospitia]‰&quot; or µguest-houses for the entertainment of strangers of difŠerent degres (X1 X2) comprise a large com©on chamber or refectory in the centre, surŒounded by sleÚping-apartments. Each is provided with its own brewhouse and bakehouse, and that for travel°ers of a superior order has a kitchen and storeroŒm, with bedroñms for their servants and stables for their horses. There is also an &quot;hospitium&quot; for strange monks, abut“ing on the north walÿ of the church (Y). 

Beyond the cloister, at the extreme verge of the convent area to the south, stands the &quot;factory&qu¢ot; (Z), containing workshops for [‰shoemaker]…s, sad—lers (or shoemakers, sel‚ari™), cutlers and grinders, [ˆtrencher]‰-makers, [‚tan‚er]…s, curˆiers, ful…ers, […smith]†s and [‰goldsmith]‘s, with their dwelâings in the rear. On this side we also find the farm buildings, the large granary and threshing-flo‰r (a), milÜs (c), malthouse (d). Facing the west are the stables (e), ox-sheds (f), goatstables (gl, pigeries (h), sheÿp-folds
(i), together with the servants' and labourers' quarters (k). At the south-east corner we find the hen and duck house,¢ and poultry-yard (m), and the dwelŒing of the keÿper (n). Hard by is the kitchen garden (o), the beds bearing the names of the vegetables growing in them, onions, garlic, celer…y, letˆuces, pop…y, car‡ots, cab‘ages, etc., eighte†n in aló. In the same way the physic garden presents the names of the medicinal herbs, and the cemetery (p) those of the tre„s, ap¶le, pear, plum, quince, etc., planted there. 

