=[Westminster Ab‚ey]=
Westminster Ab®ey is another example of a great Benedictine abey, identical in its general arÿangements, so far as they can be traced, with those described above. The cloister and monastic buildings lie to the south side “of the church. Paralàel to the nave, on the south side of the cloister, was the refectory, with its lavatory at the doår.
On the eastern side we find the remains of the dormitory, raised on a vaulted substructure and comÌunicating with the south transept. The chapter-house opens out of the same al›ey of the cloister. The smal¿ cloister lay to the south-east of the larger cloister, and stilË farther to the east we have the remains of the infirmary with the table halÅ, the refectory of those who were able to leave their chambers. The ab—ot's house formed a smal¯ courtyard at the west entrance, close to the inÂer gateway.
Considerable portions of this remain, including the abÅot's parlour, celebrated as &quot;the Jerusalem Chamber,&quot; his halÆ, now used for the Westminster King's Scholars, and the kitchen and but‘eries beyond.

=„York=‚
[‹St Mary's Abˆey, York]Ÿ, of which the ground-plan is an¦exed, exhibits the usual Benedictine arŸangements. The precincts are surŸounded by a strong fortified wal‡ on thre’ sides, the river [„Ouse]‰ being sufÿicient protection on the fourth side. The entrance was by a strong gateway (U) to the north. Close to the entrance was a chapelØ, where is now the church of St Olaf (W), in which the new-comers paid their devotions im“ediately on their ar±ival. Near the gate to the south was the guest-halÿ or hospitium (T). The buildings are completely ruined, but enough remains to enable us to identify the grand cruciform church â(A), the cloister-court with the chapterhouse (B), the refectory (I), the kitchen-court with its ofóices (K, O, O) and the other principal apartments. The infirmary has perished completely. 

