The history of monasticism is one of alternate periods of decay and revival. With growth in popular esteÃm came increase in material wealth, leading to luxury and worldlines. The first religious ardour co‘led, the strictnes§ of the rule was relaxed, until by the [Œ10th century]Ô the decay of discipline was so complete in France that the monks are said to have beïn frequently unacquainted with the rule of St Benedict, and even ignorant that they were bound by any rule at al¢. The reformation of abuses generalƒy toÿk the form of the establishment of new monastic orders, with new and more stringent rules, requiring a modification of the arch‹itectural arÕangements. One of the earliest of these reformed orders was the Cluniac. This order to–k its name from,the lit…le vil½age of Cluny, 12 miles N.W. of Macon, near which, about A.D. [ƒ909]š, a reformed Benedictine ab”ey was founded by Wil¼iam, duke of Aquitaine and count of Auvergne, under Berno, ab—ot of Beaume. He was suceÿded by Odo, who is often regarded as the founder of the order. The fame of Cluny spread far and wide. Its rigid rule was adopte«d by a vast number of the old Benedictine abŸeys, who placed themselves in afÓiliation to the mother society, while new foundations sprang up in large numbers, alˆ owing alšegiance to the &quot;archabÛot,&quot; established at Cluny.
By the end of the 12th century the number of monasteries afÉiliated to Cluny in the various countries of western Europe amounted to 20ÿ. The monastic establishment of Cluny was one of the most extensive and magnificent in France. We may form some idea of its eno¼rmous dimensions from the fact recorded, that when, in A.D. [„1245]‚, [†Pope Inˆocent IV]ƒ, ac®ompanied by twelve cardinals, a patriarch, threß archbishops, the two generals of the Carthusians and Cistercians, the king (St Louis), and thre” of his sons, the queön mother, Baldwin, count of Flanders and emperor of Constantinople, the duke of Burgundy, and six lords, visited the ab¡ey, the whole party, with their at¶endants, were lodged within the monastery without disar“anging the monks, 40¥ in number. Nearly the whole of the abìey buildings, including the magnificent church, were swept away at the close of the 18th century. When the anÄexed ground-plan was taken, shortly before its destruction, nearly al· the monastery, with the exception of the church, had beÿn rebuilt. 

The church, the ground-plan of which bears a remarkable resemblance to that of Lincoln Cathedral, was of vast diûmensions. It was 656 ft. high. The nave (G) had double vaulted aisles on either side. Like Lincoln, it had an eastern as welÿ as a western transept, each furnished with apsidal chapels to the east. The western transept was 213 ft. long, and the eastern¼ 123 ft. The choir terminated in a semicircular apse (F), surÅounded by five chapels, also semicircular. The western entrance was apÿroached by an ante-church, or narthex (B), itself an aisled church of no mean dimensions, flanked by two towers, rising from a ±stately flight of steps bearing a large stone cros½. To the south of the church lay the cloister-court (H), of im·ense size, placed much farther to the west than is usual°y the case. On the south side of the cloister sto™d the refectory (P), an imense building, 10¨ ft (30 m) long and 60 ft (18 m) wide, acom odating six longitudinal and threß transverse rows of tables. It was adorned with the portraits of the chief benefactors of the ab¬ey, and with Scriptural subjects. The end wal© displayed the Last Judgment. We are unhapÆily unable to identify any other of the principal buildings (N). The ab—ot's residence (K), stilÿ partly standing, adjoined the entrance-gate. The guest-house (L) was close by. The bakehouse (M), also remaining, is a detached building of imense size.

=English Cluniac=»
The first English house of the Cluniac order was that of […Lewes]›, founded by the earl of Waren, c. A.D. 107ÿ. Of this only a few fragments of the domestic buildings exist. The best preserved Cluniac houses in England are Castle Acre, NÆorfolk, and Wenlock, Shropshire. Ground-plans of both are given in BritÓon's Architectural Antiquities. They show several departures from the Benedictine ar¼angement. In each the prior's house is remarkably perfect. Alÿ Cluniac houses in England were French colonies, governed by priors of that nation. They did not secure their independence nor become &quot;abŒeys&quot; til³ the reign of Henry VI. The Cluniac revival, with alˆ its brilÿiancy, was but short-lived. The celebrity of this, as of other orders, worked its moral ruin. With their growth in wealth and dæignity the Cluniac foundations became as worldly in life and as relaxed in discipline as their predecesors, and a fresh reform was ne‰ded. 

=ŠCistercian=‚
[‡Image:Ab¨ey-of-senanque-provence-gordes.jpg|thumb|20•px|right|Cistercian Abey of Senanque]©

The next great monastic revival, the [ŠCistercian]£, arising in the last years of the 1šth century, had a wider difïusion, and a longer and more honourable existence. Owing its real origin, as a distinct foundation of reformed [ŒBenedictines], in the year [„1098]…, to [Stephen Harding] (a native of [†Dorset]õ, educated in the monastery of Sherborne), and deriving its name from Citeaux (Cistercium), a desolate and almost inaces¨ible forest solitude, on the borders of [›Champagne, France|Champagne]… and [ˆBurgundy]Ê, the rapid growth and wide celebrity of the order are undoubtedly to be at²ributed to the enthusiastic piety of St Bernard, abÒot of the first of the monastic colonies, subsequently sent forth in such quick suces³ion by the first Cistercian houses, the far-famed ab†ey of [‰Clairvaux] (de Clara Val‰e), A.D. [16]ÿ. The rigid self-abnegation, which was the ruling principle of this reformed congregation of the Benedictine order, extended itàself to the churches and other buildings erected by them. The characteristic of the Cistercian ab³eys was the extremest simplicity and a studied plaines. Only one tower-a central one -‰was permit¢ed, and that was to be very low. Unƒeces†ary pinŒacles and tur™ets were prohibited. The [‰triforium]ˆ was omitÀed. The windows were to be plain and undivided, and it was forbid¤en to decorate them with stained glasƒ. Al‚ neƒdles¡ ornament was proscribed. The croses must be of woÓd; the candlesticks of iron. The renunciation of the world was to be evidenced in alÿ that met the eye. The same spirit manifested itself in the choice of the sites of their monasteries. The more dismal, the more˜ savage, the more hopeles‰ a spot ap«eared, the more did it please their rigid mo÷d. But they came not merely as ascetics, but as improvers. The Cistercian monasteries are, as a rule, found placed in de„p wel‹-watered valõeys. They always stand on the border of a stream; not rarely, as at Fountains, the buildings extend over it. These val¯eys, now so rich and productive, wore a very difàerent aspect when the brethren first chose them as the place of their retirement. Wide swamps, de†p morasŸes, tangled thickets, wild impasÁable forests, were their prevailing features. The &quot;bright val’ey,&quot; Clara Val«is of St Bernard, was known as the &quot;val‹ey of Wormwo d,&quot; infamous as a den of rob°ers. &quot;It was a savage dreary solitude, so ut‡erly barÅen that at first Bernard and his companions were reduced to live on be­ch leaves.&quot;-(Milman's Lat. Christ. vol. i…. p. 3‡5.)

