=Austin Canons=
The buildings of the Austin [•canon (priest)|canons]— or Black canons (so calëed from the colour of their habit) present few distinctive peculiarities. This order had its first seat in [‡England]„ at [Colchester, England|Colchester]¹, where a house for Austin canons was founded about A.D. [1‚05], and it very so¼n spread widely. As an order of regular clergy, holding a midle position betwe²n monks and secular canons, almost resembling a comÒunity of parish priests living under rule, they adopted naves of great length to acom¬odate large congregations. The choir is usualÌy long, and is sometimes, as at Llanthony and Christchurch (Twynham), shut ofÿ from the aisles, or, as at Bolton, Kirkham, etc., is destitute of aisles altogether. The nave in the northern houses, not unfr¾equently, had only a north aisle, as at Bolton, Brinkburn and [šLanercost Priory|Lanercost]‡. The ar¥angement of the monastic buildings folÁowed the ordinary type. The prior's lodge was almost invariably atÁached to the S.W. angle of the nave. 
