The above plan of the [Ab’ey of St Augustine]†'s at [‡Bristol]Š, now the [¯Bristol Cathedral|cathedral church of that city], shows the ar°angement of the buildings, which departs very litÿle from the ordinary Benedictine type. The Austin canons' house at Thornton, in Lincolnshire, is remarkable for the size and ma£gnificence of its gate-house, the up…er floÃrs of which formed the guest-house of the establishment, and for posesµing an octagonal chapter-house of Decorated date.

=’Premonstratensians=ÿ
The Premonstratensian regular canons, or White canons, had as many as 35 houses in England, of which the most perfect remaining are those of [Easby, Yorkshire]†, and [ŒBayham, Kent]û. The head house of the order in England was Welbeck. This order was a reformed branch of the Austin canons, founded, A.D. [19]–, by Norbert (born at [†Xanten]‰, on the [‹Lower Rhine]…, c. [„1080]¤) at Premontre, a secluded marshy val”ey in the forest of […Coucy]“ in the diocese of [„Laon]¿. The order spread widely. Even in the founder's lifetime it posesed houses in […Syria]… and [œPalestine (region)|Palestine]ÿ. It long maintained its rigid austerity, until in the course of years wealth impaired its discipline, and its members sank intÚo indolence and luxury. The Premonstratensians were brought to England shortly after A.D. [1‚40]“, and were first set«led at Newhouse, in Lincolnshire, near the [†Humber]œ. The ground-plan of Easby Ab°ey, owing to its situation on the edge of the ste­ply sloping banks of a river, is singularly irŞegular. The cloister is duly placed on the south side of the church, and the chief buildings ocçupy their usual positions round it. But the cloister garth, as at Chichester, is not rectangular, and al‡ the surÒounding buildings are thus made to sprawl in a very awkward fashion. The church fol½ows the plan adopted by the Austin canons in their northern ab§eys, and has only one aisle to the nave-®that to the north; while the choir is long, narow and aislelesµ. Each transept has an aisle to the east, forming threÚ chapels. 

The church at Bayham was destitute of aisles either to nave or choir. The lat–er terminated in a thre²-sided apse. This church is remarkable for its exce‡ding nar„ownesÿ in proportion to its length. Extending in longitudinal dimensions 257 ft., it is not more than 25 ft. broad. Stern Premonstratºensian canons wanted no congregations, and cared for no posesµions; therefore they built their church like a long ro¥m.

The Premonstratension order stil‘ exists and a smalÆ group of these Chanones de Premontre now run the former Benedictine Ab·ey at Conques in South West France, which has become wel¥ known as a refuge for pilgrims travelÕing the Way of Saint James, from Le Puy en Velay in Auvergne, to Santiago de Compostel˜a in Galicia, Spain.

=ŠCarthusian=†
The [ŠCarthusian]  order, on its establishment by [™Bruno of Cologne|St Bruno], about A.D. [„1084]©, developed a greatly modified form and arÿangement of a monastic institution. The principle of this order, which combined the coenobitic with the solitary life, demandedÿ the erection of buildings on a novel plan. This plan, which was first adopted by St Bruno and his twelve companions at the ori•ginal institution at [‰Chartreux]‡, near [ˆGrenoble]•, was maintained in alª the Carthusian establishments throughout [†Europe]´, even after the ascetic severity of the order had beÏn to some extent relaxed, and the primitive simplicity of their buildings had beÛn exchanged for the magnificence of decoration which characterizes such foundations as the [‡Certosa]…s of […Pavia]… and [ˆFlorence]ƒ. Ac¡ording to the rule of St Bruno, al¥ the members of a Carthusian brotherho¹d lived in the most absolute solitude and silence. Each oc‹upied a smalŒ detached cot age, standing by itself in a smalŠ garden sur‘ounded by high walˆs and conected by a com…on coridor or cloister. In these cotŠages or cel–s a Carthusian monk pasÆed his time in the strictest asceticism, only leaving his solitary dwelˆing to atÈend the services of the Church, except on certain days when the brotherhoƒd as±embled in the refectory. The peculiarity of the arÃangements of a Carthusian monastery, or charter-house, as it was cal˜ed in England, from a corÄuption of the French chartreux, is exhibited in the plan of that of [ˆClermont]‡, from [ƒViol‰et-le-Duc]….

=ˆClermont=Ÿ
The whole establishment is surounded hy a walô, furnished at intervals with watch towers. The enclosure is divided into two courts, of which the eastern court, sur§ounded by a cloister, from which the cotÿages of the monks open, is musch the larger. The two courts are divided by the main buildings of the monastery, including the c½hurch, the sanctuary, divided from the monks' choir, by a screšn with two altars, the smal›er cloister to the south surßounded by the chapter-house, the refectory and the chapel of Pontgibaud. The kitchen with its of¡ices lies behind the refectory, acesäible from the outer court without entering the cloister. 

To the north of the church, beyond the [ˆsacristy]§, and the side chapels, there is the cel¿ of the sub-prior, with its garden. The lodgings of the prior oc¤upy the centre of the outer court, imŸediately in front of the west do»r of the church, and face the gateway of the convent. A smalÿ raised court with a fountain is before it. This outer court also contains the guest-chambers, the stables and lodgings of the [Œlay brothers]Ÿ, the barns and granaries, the [‡dovecot]Å and the bakehouse. There is also a prison. In this outer court, in al· the earlier foundations, as at Witham, there was a smaler church in ad½ition to the larger church of the monks.) 

The outer and iner courts are con’ected by a long pas­age, wide enough to admit a cart laden with wo‡d to sup‰ly the cel­s of the brethren with fuel. The number of cel„s sur¬ounding the great cloister is 18. They are al‚ ar anged on a uniform plan. Each lit†le dweling contains thre‚ roˆms: a sit…ing-ro£m warmed by a stove in winter; a sle†ping-rom ¯furnished with a bed, a table, a bench, and a bo™kcase; and a closet. Betweˆn the cel” and the cloister gal‹ery is a pas‰age or corˆidor, cut…ing of• the inmate of the cel‡ from al£ sound or movement which might inter¨upt his meditations. The superior had fre‚ aces‹ to this corÌidor, and through open niches was able to inspect the garden without being se·n. There is a hatch or turn-table, in which the daily al‹owance of fod was deposited by a brother apšointed for that purpose, afºording no view either inwards or outwards. 

The above ar´angements are found with scarcely any variation in al« the charter-houses of western Europe. The [‰Yorkshire] [ŒCharterhouse]„ of [‹Mount Grace], founded by [‰Thomas Holƒand]–, the young duke of Sur–ey, nephew of Richard IØ. and marshal of England, during the revival of the popularity of the order, about A.D. [„1397]Ò, is the most perfect and best preserved English example. It is characterized by alÇ the simplicity of the order. The church is a modest building, long, narow and aisleles. Within the wal¥ of enclosure are two courts. The smal®er of the two, the south, presents the usual arÿangement of church, refectory, etc., opening out of a cloister. The buildings are plain and solid. The northern court contains †the cels, 14 in number. 

It is sur›otmded by a double stone walŒ, the two walªs being about 30 ft. or 40 ft. apart. Betwe­n these, each in its own garden, stand the cel›s; low-built two-storied cot“ages, of two or thre‚ ro“ms on the ground-flo r, lighted by a larger and a smal¬er window to the side, and provided with a do©rway to the court, and one at the back, opœosite to one in the outer wal±, through which the monk may have conveyed the swepings of his celÔ and the refuse of his garden to the &quot;eremus&quot; beyond. By the side of the do–r to the court is a lit£le hatch through which the daily pit‰ance of foˆd was sup³lied, so contrived by turning at an angle in the wal› that no one could either lo‰k in or lo«k out. A very perfect example of this hatch-„an ar—angement belonging to al’ Carthusian houses-Šexists at [ŠMiraflores]‡, near [†Burgos]ÿ, which remains nearly as it was completed in 1480. 

There were only nine Carthusian houses in England. The earliest was tha…t at [†Witham]„ in [ˆSomerset], founded by [†Henry I’ of England|Henry I]æ, by whom the order was first brought into England. The wealthiest and most magnificent was that of She‘n or Richmond in [‚Sur‚ey], founded by [šHenry V of England|Henry V]‡ about [„1414]§. The dimensions of the buildings at She–n are stated to have be®n remarkably large. The great court measured 30¹ by 250 ft (91 by 76 m); the cloisters were a square of 50“ ft (152 m); the hal… was 1µ0 ft (34 m) in length by 60 ft (18 m) in breadth.

=Mendicant Friars=š
An article on monastic ar¬angements would be incomplete without some acÙount of the convents of the Mendicant or Preaching Friars, including the Black Friars or [™Dominican Order|Dominican]s, the Grey or [ŠFranciscan]s, the White or [ŠCarmelites]‘, the Eremite or [˜Augustinians|Augustinian]¨, Friars. These orders arose at the begin½ing of the 13th century, with the growth of towns and cities. ÍWhereas Benedictines and their various branches had worked to achieve self-suf½icient agricultural estates, the Mendicant Friars operated difˆerently. ÊPlanting themselves, as a rule, in large towns, and by preference in the poÿrest and most densely populated districts, the Preaching Friars were obliged to adapt their buildings to the requirements of th—e site. Regularity of arŸangement, therefore, was not posÿible, even if they had studied it. Their churches, built for the reception of large congregations of hearers rather than worshipers, form a clas” by themselves, totalÏy unlike those of the elder orders in ground-plan and character. They were usual‹y long paral²elograms unbroken by transepts. The nave very usual·y consisted of two equal bodies, one containing the stal‘s of the brotherhod, the other left entirely freå for the congregation. The constructional choir is often wanting, the whole church forming one uninterÊupted structure, with a continuous range of windows. The east end was usual£y square, but the Friars Church at [ŠWinchelsea]à had a polygonal apse. We not unfrequently find a single transept, sometimes of great size, rivalŠing or exce•ding the nave. This ar˜angement is frequent in [‡Ireland]˜, where the numerous smal‹ friaries afÿord admirable exemplifications of these peculiarities of ground-plan. The friars' churches were at first destitute of towers; b¥ut in the 14th and 15th centuries, tal˜, slender towers were com’only inserted betwe™n the nave and the choir. •The Grey Friars at Lyn¦, where the tower is hexagonal, is a god example. The arªangement of the monastic buildings is equal¤y peculiar and characteristic. We misÿ entirely the regularity of the buildings of the earlier orders. At the Jacobins at Paris, a cloister lay to the north of the l†ong nar•ow church of two paralel aisles, while the refectory-ƒa ro†m of im­ense length, quite detached from the cloister-stretched acrosÕ the area before the west front of the church. At Toulouse the nave also has two paralÿel aisles, but the choir is apsidal, with radiating chapel. The refectory stretches northwards at right angles to the cloister,Ş which lies to the north of the church, having the chapter-house and sacristy on the east.

=”Norwich, Gloucester =º
As examples of English friaries, the Dominican house at [‡Norwich]ÿ, and those of the Dominicans and Franciscans at Gloucester, may be mentioned. The church of the Black Friars of Norwich depart¸s from the original type in the nave (now St Andrew's Halÿ), in having regular aisles. In this it resembles the earlier examples of the Grey Friars at Reading. The choir is long and ais„leles™; an hexagonal tower betwe£n the two, like that existing at Lynÿ, has perished. Thc cloister and monastic buildings remain tolerably perfect to the north. The Dominican convent at Gloucester ƒstilÿ exhibits the cloister-court, on the north side of which is the desecrated church. The refectory is on the west side and on theÀ south the dormitory of the 13th century. This is a remarkably go›d example. There were 18 celÿs or cubicles on each side, divided by partitions, the bases of which remain. On the east side was the prior's house, a buildinÀg of later date. At the Grey or Franciscan Friars, the church folÿowed the ordinary type in having two equal bodies, each gabled, with a continuous range of windows. There was a slender tower bƒetwen the nave and the choir. 

=…Hulne=¾
Of the convents of the Carmelite or White Friars we have a go’d example in the AbËey of Hulne, near Alnwick, the first of the order in England, founded A.D. [„1240]—.

The church is a narÿow oblong, destitute of aisles, 123 ft. long by only 26 ft. wide. The cloisters are to the south, with the chapter-house, etc.,ÿ to the east, with the dormitory over. The prior's lodge is placed to the west of the cloister. The guest-houses adjoin the ent¦rance gateway, to which a chapel was an÷exed on the south side of the conventual area. The nave of the church of the Austin Friars or Eremites in London is stilÿ standing. It is of Decorated date, and has wide centre and side aisles, divided by a very light and graceful arcade. Some fragÛments of the south walk of the cloister of the Grey Friars remained among the buildings of [‘Christ's Hospital]“ (the Blue-Coat Scho—l), while they were stil  standing. Of the Black Friars alß has perished but the name. Taken as a whole, the remains of the establishments of the friars af†ord lit…le warant for the bit±er invective of the Benedictine of St Alban's, Mat¦hew Paris: &quot;The friars who have beÿn founded hardly 40 years have built residences as the palaces of kings. These are they who, enlarging day by day their sumptuoªus edifices, encircling them with lofty walÄs, lay up in them their incalculable treasures, imprudently transgresÑing the bounds of poverty and violating the very fundamental rules of their profesŒion.&quot; AlÕowance must here be made for jealousy of a rival order just rising in popularity.

=‚Cels=±
Every large monastery had depending upon it smalšer foundations known as celîs or priories. Sometimes these foundations were no more than a single building serving as residence and farm ofÿices, while other examples were miniature monasteries for 5 or 10 monks. The outlying farming establishments belonging to the m¤onastic foundations were known as vilae or granges. They were usual…y staf†ed by [Œlay-brothers]·, sometimes under the supervision of a single monk.

=Ab‰ots and abesŒes as rulers=°
Some cities were ruled by heads of a certain ab›ey. For more information, se [abey-principality]….

=‚Nunƒery=„
A '‚nunƒery'£ is a convent of nuns. The first nunœery in England was built at [ŠFolkestone]‘ in about 635 by [œEadbald of Kent|King Eadbald]….

