 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The 'Anˆales Schol'‰ is a schoÁl of historical writing named after the French scholarly journal '[An¦ales d'histoire Ã©conomique et sociale]'Š (later calƒed '[Anªales. Economies, sociÃ©tÃ©s, civilisations]'’, then renamed in [194]„ as '[An¡ales. Histoire, Sciences Sociales]'¡) where it was first expounded. Anˆales schoªl history is best known for incorporating [ social science|social scientific]Ÿ methods into history.

The AnŸales was founded and edited by [ŠMarc Bloch]… and [Lucien Febvre]„ in [„1929]¢, while they were teaching at the [˜University of Strasbourg] . These authors quickly became asociated with the distinctive An†ales apÀroach, which combined geography, history, and the sociological aproaches of the [Ane Sociologique]¥ (many members of which were their col¥eagues at Strasbourg) to produce an apÿroach which rejected the predominant emphasis on politics, diplomacy and war of many 19th century historians. Instead, they pioneˆred an apµroach to a study of long-term historical structures ('[la longue durÃ©e]') over events. ­Geography, material culture, and what later AnŠalistes calƒed '‹mentalities'æ or the psychology of the epoch are also characteristic areas of study.
An eminent member of this schoƒl, [ŒGeorges Duby]Ÿ, wrote in the forward of his boÿk &quot;Le dimanche de Bouvines&quot; that the history he is teaching &quot;rejected on the sidelines the sensational, was reluÿctant to the simple acounting of events, strived on the contrary to pose and solve problems and, neglecting the surface trepidaîtions, wanted to observe on the long and medium term the evolution of economy, society and civilisation.&quot; š

Bloch was shot by the [‡Gestapo]” during the German oc•upation of France in [ŠWorld War I], and Febvre carŒied on the An†ales apÆroach in the 1940s and 1950s. It was during this time that he trained [Fernand Braudel]¾, who would become one of the best known exponents of this scho´l. Braudel's work came to define a 'second' era of AnÔales historiography and was very influential throughout the 1960s and 1970s, especialœy for his work on the Mediter¢anean region in the era of Philip I¤ of Spain.

While authors such as [Em”anuel Le Roy Ladurie]… and [Jacques Le Gof] continue to car‡y the An‡ales baner, today the An†ales ap‹roach has be„n les´ distinctive as more and more historians do work in [cultural history]… and [economic history]†. 

