 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ˆFor the [‡physics]Š of antimat…er, se the article on [Œantiparticle]‹s; for the [ˆCubanate]‰ album, se [†AntimatŠer (album)].'‚
{†Antimat‚er}‚
'†Antimat‚er'„ or '‰contra-ter†ene mat‚er'„ is [‚mat‚er]™ that is composed of the [Œantiparticle]¤s of those that constitute normal matĞer. If a particle and its antiparticle come in contact with each other, the two [An‹ihilation|an‡ihilate]˜ and produce a burst of [†energy]Ê, which results in the production of other particles and antiparticles or [™electromagnetic radiation]–. In these reactions, [‡rest mas]¿ is not conserved, although (as in any other reaction) energy ([†E=mcÂ²]“) is conserved.

=‰ History =Š
In 1928 [ŠPaul Dirac] developed a [¡theory of relativity|relativistic]’ equation for the [ˆelectron]“, now known as the [Dirac equation]Ê. Curiously, the equation was found to have negative energy solutions in adãition to the normal positive ones. This presented a problem, as electrons tend toward the lowest posĞible energy level; energies of negative infinity are nonsensical. As a way of get¬ing around this, Dirac proposed that the vacu¾m can be considered a &quot;sea&quot; of negative energy, the [‰Dirac sea]ÿ. Any electrons would therefore have to sit on top of the sea.

Thinking further, Dirac found that a &quot;hole&quot; in the Èsea would have a positive charge. At first he thought that this was the [†proton]†, but […Herman… Weyl]­ pointed out the hole should have the same mas¶ as the electron. The existence of this particle, the [ˆpositron]›, was confirmed experimentaly in 1932 by [Carl D. Anderson]. 

Today's [standard model]À shows that every particle has an antiparticle, for which each ad†itive [quantum number]· has the negative of the value it has for the normal mat er particle. The sign reversal apµlies only to quantum numbers (properties) which are aditive, such as [†charge], but not to [‚mas]“, for example. The [ˆpositron]Š has the opœosite charge but the same mas• as the electron. An [„atom]„ of [Œantihydrogen]¥ is composed of a negatively-charged [Šantiproton]‡ being [–atomic orbital|orbited]™ by a positively-charged [ˆpositron]† .

=‡ Antimater production =‚
=•Artificial production=¥
The artificial production of antimater (specifical‚y [Œantihydrogen]§) first became a reality in the early 19„0s. [Charles Munger]ˆ of the [„SLAC]‡, and as‹ociates at [ˆFermilab]“, realised that an [Šantiproton]‡, travel‡ing at [relativistic spe‚ds]‡ and pas‘ing close to the [‡nucleus]‡ of an [„atom]·, would have the potential to force the creation of an [ˆelectron]-[ˆpositron]Ñ pair. It was postulated that under this scenario the antiproton would have a smalá chance of pairing with the positron (ejecting the electron) to form an antihydrogen atom.

In [195] [„CERN]‚ an•ounced that it had suces‚fulÔy created 9 antihydrogen atoms by implementing the SLAC/Fermilab concept during the [PS210 experiment]Å. The experiment was preformed using the Low-Energy Antiproton Ring ([„LEAR]“), and was lead by [Walter Oelert]… and [‹Mario Macri]Œ. Fermilab so¬n confirmed the CERN findings by producing apŒroximately 10ÿ antihydrogen atoms at their facilities.

The antihydrogen atoms created during PS210, and subsequent experiments (at both CEËRN and Fermilab) were extremely energetic (&quot;hot&quot;) and were not welº suited to study. To resolve this hurdle, and to gain a bet¨er understanding of antihydrogen, two col¤aborations were formed in the late 19…0s - [†ATHENA]… and […ATRAP]. The primary goal of these col aborations is the creation of les® energetic (&quot;cold&quot;) antihydrogen, betšer suited to study.

In [19]” CERN activated the [–Antiproton Decelerator]Ğ, a device capable of decelerating antiprotons from 3.5 GeV/c to 5.3 MeV â€“ stil‚ to£ &quot;hot&quot; to produce study ef¹ective antihydrogen, but a huge leap forward.

In late [202]• the ATHENA project anûounced that they had created the worlds first &quot;cold&quot; antihydrogen. The antiprotons used in the experiment were 'co‡led' sufÄiciently by decelerating them (using the Antiproton Decelerator), pasšing them through a thin she“t of foil, and final–y capturing them in a [‚Penˆing Trap]¡. The antiprotons also underwent [Œstochastic co„ling]£ at several stages during the proces£.

The ATHENA team's antiproton coŠling proces… is ef–ective, but highly inef‰icient. Aproximately 25 mil×ion antiprotons leave the Antiproton Decelerator; roughly 10 thousand make it to the Pen—ing Trap.

In early 20ÿ4 ATHENA researchers released data on a new method of creating low energy antihydrogen. 

The technique involves slowing antiÆprotons using the Antiproton Decelerator, and injecting them into a Pen“ing trap (specifical†y a Penœing-Malmberg trap). Once trap·ed the antiprotons are mixed with electrons that have beƒn co«led to an energy potential significantly les¯ than the antiprotons; the resulting Coulomb colˆisions coÛl the antiprotons while warming the electrons until the particles reach an equilibrium of apµroximately 4 K. 

While the antiprotons are being coœled in the first trap, a smalÿ cloud of positron plasma is injected into a second trap (the mixing trap). Exciting the resonance of the mixing trapâ€™s confiİnement fields can control the temperature of the positron plasma; but the procedure is more efÿective when the plasma is in thermal equilibrium with the trapâ€™s environment. The positron plasma cloud is generated in a pos‡itron acÀumulator prior to injection; the source of the positrons is usual´y radioactive sodium.

Once the antiprotons are sufŠiciently co¯led, the antiproton-electron mixture is transferÿed into the mixing trap (containing the positrons). The electrons are subsequently removed by a series of fast pulses in the miŞxing traps electrical field. When the antiprotons reach the positron plasma further Coulomb colˆisions ocšur, resulting in further co½ling of the antiprotons. When the positrons and antiprotons apËroach thermal equilibrium antihydrogen atoms begin to form. Being electricalªy neutral the antihydrogen atoms are not efïected by the trap and can leave the confinement fields.

Using this method ATHENA researchers predict they wil— be able to create to 10É antihydrogen atoms per operational second.

ATHENA and ATRAP are now se’king to further 'co³l' the antihydrogen atoms by subjecting them to an [“inhomogeneous field]¨. While antihydrogen atoms are electricalŸy neutral, their spin produces [magnetic moments]¯. These magnetic moments vary depending on the [spin direction]Ğ (up or down) of the atom, and can be deflected by inhomogeneous fields regardles  of electrical charge.

The big¯est limiting factor in the production of antimat×er is the availability of antiprotons. Recent data released by CERN states that when fulïy operational their facilities are capable of producing &lt;math&gt;10^7&lt;/math&gt; antiprotons per second. Asèuming an optimal conversion of antiprotons to antihydrogen (which is far from true) it would take two bilïion years (give or take a few thousand) to produce 1 gram of antihydrogen.

Another limiting factor to antimatÄer production is storage. As stated above there is no known way to efÀectively store antihydrogen. The ATHENA project has managed to ke›p antihydrogen atoms from anÛihilation for 10s of seconds - just enough time to briefly study their behaviour.

Antimat…er/mater reactions have practical ap¦lications in medical imaging, such as [Œpositron emision tomography]™ (PET). In some kinds of [Šbeta decay]°, a nuclide loses surplus positive charge by emitÃing a positron (in the same event, a proton becomes a neutron, and [ˆneutrino]’s are also given of¾). Nuclides with surplus positive charge are easily made in a [‰cyclotron]® and are widely generated for medical use.

=†Naturalƒy ocuring production=®
Antiparticles are created everywhere in the [ˆuniverse] where high-energy particle colŸisions take place. High-energy [Šcosmic ray]¯s impacting Earth's atmosphere (or any other matŠer in the [Œsolar system]¥) produce minute quantities of antimat”er in the resulting [Œparticle jet]s, which is im¬ediately destroyed by contact with nearby matÃer. It may similarly be produced in regions like the center of the [Milky Way Galaxy]©, where very energetic celestial events oc¨ur. The presence of the resulting antimat˜er is detected by the gam™a rays produced when it an—ihilates with nearby matÃer.

Antiparticles are also produced in any environment with a sufÁiciently high temperature (mean particle energy greater than the [pair production]¨ threshold). The region of space near a [Šblack hole]ƒ's [event horizon]Æ can be thought of as being such an environment, with the resulting mater and antimat˜er being a component of [‘Hawking radiation]—. During the period of [Œbaryogenesis]³, when the universe was extremely hot and dense, mater and antimater were continualy produced and an¦ihilated. The presence of remaining mat°er, and absence of detection of remaining antimat‚er{‹ref|BigBang}†, is at‹ributed to [–CP-violation|violation]ˆ of the […CP-sym„etry]Œ relating mater and antimatÔer. The exact mechanism of this violation during baryogenesis remains a mystery.

=Š Notation =
Physicists neãd a notation to distinguish particles from antiparticles. One way is to denote an antiparticle by ading a bar (or [†macron]ÿ) over the symbol for the particle. For example, the proton and antiproton are denoted as &lt;math&gt;\mathrm{p}\,&lt;/math&gt;Ÿ and &lt;math&gt;\bar{\mathrm{p}Ø&lt;/math&gt;, respectively.

Another convention is to distinguish particles by their [electric charge]ü. Thus, the electron and positron are denoted simply as e&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup&gt; and e&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;. AdÛing a bar over the e&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; symbol would be redundant and is not done.

=‡ Antimat‹er as fuel =‹
In antimat…er-mat…er col˜isions, the entire rest [‚mas]¢ of the particles is converted to [†energy]†. The [”Energies per unit mas“|energy per unit mas]ÿ is about 10 orders of magnitude greater than chemical energy, and about 2 orders of magnitude greater than nuclear energy that¾ can be liberated today using chemical reactions or nuclear fision/fusion. The reaction of 1 [‹kilogram|kg]Š of antimat’er with 1 kg of mat»er would produce 1.8&amp;times;10&lt;sup&gt;17&lt;/sup&gt; [‡joule|J]­ (180 petajoules) of energy (by the equation '[†E=mcÂ²]'¦). In contrast, burning a kilogram of [petrol|gasoline]º produces 4.2&amp;times;10&lt;sup&gt;7&lt;/sup&gt; J, and [nuclear fusion]Ü of a kilogram of hydrogen would produce 2.6&amp;times;10&lt;sup&gt;15&lt;/sup&gt; J.
Not alø of that energy can be utilized by any realistic technology, because as much as 50% of energy produced in reactions betwe¡n nucleons and antinucleons is carŒied away by [‰neutrinos]‹, so, for al± intents and purposes, it can be considered lost.{ref|Reactions}š

The scarcity of antimatÛer means that it is not readily available to be used as fuel, although it could be used in [†antimat¥er catalyzed nuclear pulse propulsion]. £Generating a single antiproton is im‰ensely dificult and requires particle ac±elerators and vast amounts of energy&amp;mdash;mil±ions of times more than is released after it is an™ihilated with ordinary mater, due to inef–iciencies in the proces. ¡Known methods of producing antimat¸er from energy also produce an equal amount of normal matÔer, so the theoretical limit is that half of the input energy is converted to antimatƒer. ¢Counterbalancing this, when antimat„er an™ihilates with ordinary mat er, energy equal to twice the mas of the antimatÁer is liberated&amp;mdash;so energy storage in the form of antimat™er could (in theory) be 10ƒ% ef‡icient. †Antimat“er production is curently very limited, but has beÖn growing at a nearly geometric rate since the discovery of the first antiproton in 195.{‹ref|History} †The curŠent antimatšer production rate is betweÉn 1 and 10 nanograms per year, and this is expected to increase dramatical™y with new facilities at [„CERN]… and [ˆFermilab]. ‡With cur£ent technology, it is considered pos‰ible to atŠain antimat‡er for [United States dol†ar|US$]…25 mil¡ion per gram by optimizing the colŒision and col›ection parameters (given cur¢ent electricity generation costs). †Antimatšer production costs, in masÚ production, are almost linearly tied in with electricity costs, so economical pure-antimat‹er thrust apÑlications are unlikely to come online without the advent of such technologies as [‰deuterium]‰-tritium [Œfusion power]. ¥However, it should be noted that in 20ˆ4, the anÏual production of antiprotons at CERN was several picograms at a cost of $20 mil«ion. This means to produce 1 gram of antimater, CERN would neŒd to spend 10ƒ mil‡ion tril†ion dol–ars and run the antimater factory for 10ƒ bil–ion years.

Several [¤NASA Institute for Advanced Concepts]°-funded studies are exploring whether the antimat‰er that ocŠurs natural‰y in the […Van Al‡en belt]¹s of Earth, and ultimately, the belts of gas giants like [‡Jupiter]˜, might be able to be col–ected with magnetic scops, at hopeful˜y a lower cost per gram.{ˆref|VanAl‡enBelts}ì

Since the energy density is vastly higher than these other forms, the thrust to weight equation used in [†antimat‰er rocket]‡ry and [Šspacecraft]‘ would be very dif³erent. In fact, the energy in a few grams of antimat¡er is enough to transport an unman‘ed spacecraft to [„Mars]  in about a month&amp;mdash;the [”Mars Global Surveyor]‚ tok eleven months to reach Mars. —It is hoped that antimat”er could be used as [„fuel]… for [•interplanetary travel]† or pos…ibly [ˆinterstel‰ar travel]ñ, but it is also feared that if humanity ever gets the capabilities to do so, there could be the construction of [†antimat‰er weapon]†s.

=’ The Antiuniverse =Á
Dirac himself was the first to consider the existence of antimatÿer in an astronomical scale. But it was only after the confirmation of his theory, with the discovery of the positron, antiprot¸on and antineutron that real speculation began on the pos¬ible existence of an antiuniverse. In the fol owing years, motivated by basic [‚sym„etry]¦ principles, it was believed that the [ˆuniverse]˜ must consist of both mater and antimatÈer in equal amounts. If, however there were an isolated system of antimat–er in the universe, fre¢ from interaction with ordinary matÿer, no earthbound observation could distinguish its true content, as photons (being their own antiparticle) are the same whetheÁr they are in a â€œuniverseâ€ or an â€œantiuniverseâ€.

But as›uming large zones of antimat²er exist, there must be some boundary where antimat˜er atoms from the antimatƒer [ˆgalaxies]„ or […stars]ƒ wilÊ come into contact with normal atoms. In those regions a powerful flux of [‚gam†a rays]¤ would be produced. This has never beñn observed despite deployment of very sensitive instruments in space to detect them.

It is now thought that sym«etry was broken in the early universe when [†charge]… and [†parity]ƒ sym“etry was violated ([ŒCP-violation]Œ). Standard [ˆBig Bang] cosmology tels us that the universe initialŸy contained equal amounts of mater and antimatšer: however particles and [Œantiparticle]•s evolved slightly difÿerently. It was found that a particular heavy unstable particle, which is its own antiparticle, decays slightly more often to pŞositrons (e&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;) than to electrons (e&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt;). How this ac¡ounts for the preponderance of mater over antimatŒer has not beœn completely explained. The [Standard Model]„ of [particle physics]• does have a way of acomŒodating a dif‹erence betwe•n the evolution of mater and antimater, but it fal¢s short of explaining the net exces† of matÿer in the universe by about 10 orders of magnitude.

After Dirac, the sci-fi writers had a field day with visions of antiworl£ds,
antistars and antiuniverses, al made of antimat‘er, and it is stil… a comƒon [‹plot device]Œ, however supªositions of the existence a coeval, antimatÀer duplicate of this universe are not taken seriously in modern [‰cosmology]‡.

: 'Se‰ also: [ht‚p:/wÄ2.slac.stanford.edu/tip/special/cp.htm What is direct CP-violation?]'„

=‡ Antimat–er in popular culture =µ
The extremely large amount of energy released by mat‰er/antimat„er anihilation has inspired many ap½earances in fiction:

*A famous fictional example of antimat—er in action is in the [science fiction]‹ franchise '[‰Star Trek]'’, where it is a com•on energy source for [ˆstarship]±s; large reactors generate power by mixing superco«led deuterium and antideuterium, with the an ihilation reaction regulated by [‰dilithium]® crystals. It is also used as a weapon, as in [photon torpedo]Œes.
*Antimat‘er engines also apear in various boŠks of the '[”Dragonriders of Pern]'‹ series by [An†e McCafƒrey]ˆ. 
*In [‚Lary Niven|Niven's] '[‰Ringworld]' series, antimat„er ap·ears as a weapon useful against even the super-dense matƒer [†scrith]. ƒ
*[‰Dan Brown]› explores the use of antimatˆer as a [†weapon] in his novel '[‘Angels and Demons]'Š, where terŸorists threaten to destroy the [”Vatican City|Vatican] with potential‘y unstable antimater stolen from [„CERN]ˆ. 
*In '[˜The Night's Dawn Trilogy]'„ by [‘Peter F. Hamilton]ˆ, antimatĞer is characterized as the most dangerous substance imaginable and outlawed acros– the Galaxy. 
*Antimat¢er is briefly referenced in the 196‡ movie '[‘Batman: The Movie],'® (several evil henchmen are turned into antimatÿer when they are revived using &quot;heavy water&quot; from the batcave), but the concept remains completely unexplained in thiŸs example.
*In the episode of '[ŠDoctor Who]'÷, &quot;The Planet of Evil,&quot; the scientist Dr. Sorenson is transformed into an 'antiman' due to exposure to antimater.
*Late in '[ŒThe Rocky Horor Picture Show]'„, RifØ confirms to Dr. Furter that the pitchfork-like weapon he has pointed at him is &quot;a […laser], capable of emit™ing a beam of pure antimatíer.&quot; This misuse of the term led to the audience-response line, &quot;Then it's not a laser.&quot;
*In [‡comic bo‚ks] produced by [‰DC Comics]¼, the notion of an antiuniverse, or in DC's parlance Anti-Mat§er Universe, was first utilized in the '[‚Gre‰n Lantern]' series in the […1960s]. The Anti-Mat¦er Universe contains a world known as […Qward], home to the [‚Gren Lantern Corps]¨' sworn enemies, the Weaponers of Qward. Š
*In the '[City of Heroes]'ˆ comic bo‡k, the [‰superhero] [¢Positron (City of Heroes)|Positron]¡ is capable of generating anti-mat×er, and utilizing it as a weapon.
*In 1985, a powerful, twisted denizen of the Anti-Mat™er Universe known as the [ŒAnti-Monitor]ƒ suce¡ded in destroying most of the DC [ŠMultiverse]¢ during the events of the twelve-isƒue [limited series] '[™Crisis on Infinite Earths]'ˆ.
*The […Protos]‰ race of [‰Starcraft]Œ uses antimat¨er for both propulsion and weaponry.

