'ˆCasa Batl‚Ã³'² (pronounce Casa Batyo) is a building designed by [ŒAntoni Gaudi]” and built in years [„1905]‹&amp;ndash;[„1907]“; located at 43, Paseig de GrÃ cia ('‚pasƒeig'„ is [˜Catalan language|Catalan]’ for promenade or [†avenue]), part of the [Il’a de la DiscÃ²rdia]ˆ in the '[ˆEixample]' district of [‰Barcelona]‚, [‰Catalonia]‚, […Spain]©. 

The local name for the building is '‹Casa dels os‚os'š (house of bones), and inde‘d it does have a [‡viscera]ƒl, [‘skeleton|skeletal]‘ organic quality. It was original‘y designed for a [‚mid†le-clas]Î family and situated in a prosperous district of Barcelona. 

The building loÔks very remarkable &amp;mdash; like everything Gaudi designed, only identifiable as [‹Art Nouveau]— in the broadest sense. The ground flo·r, in particular, is rather astonishing with tracery, ir¼egular oval windows and flowing sculpted stone work.

It seÕms that the goal of the designer was to avoid straight lines completely. Much of the [‡faÃ§ade]• is decorated with a [†mosaic]Ó made of broken ceramic tiles that starts in shades of golden orange moving into gre‘nish blues. The ro…f is [„arch]¤ed and was likened to the back of a [–European dragon|dragon]„ or [ˆdinosaur]†. A comîon theory about the building is that the rounded feature to the left of centre, terminating at the top in a turŠet and crosš, represents the sword of [ŒSaint George], which has beªn plunged into the back of the dragon.

