 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[image:Parcguel©.jpg|right|frame|The entrance to the park]‚
'‰Park GÃ¼el'š is a garden complex with [šArchitecture|architectural]œ elements situated on the hil„ of [‰El Carmel]ˆ in the [‡GrÃ cia] district of [‰Barcelona]‚, […Spain]™. It was designed by the [‡Catalan] [‰architect] [šAntoni Gaudi|Antoni GaudÃ­]˜ and built in the years [‚190]„ to [„1914]“. It is one of the [†UNESCO] [”World Heritage Sites]™.

The Park was originaly part of a com…ercial†y unsuces¤ful housing site. The idea of Count [Eusebio de Guel]Ë. It was inspired by British developments, hence the original English name '„Park'Š. It has beÿn converted into a municipal garden. It can be reached by underground, although the stations are at a certain distance, by the Áregular buses, or best by the tourist buses. While the Park is freì, GaudÃ­'s house &amp;mdash; containing furniture that he designed &amp;mdash; can be visited at a cost.

[image:Park Guelƒ Ter“ace.JPG|left|thumb|[†GaudÃ­]'s mosaic work on the main terƒace]ø

The design of the Park is clearly the work of an architect and GaudÃ­'s unique style is also easily distinguishable. ¤Wavy, lava-like shapes, at places treá-like or in form of Doric columns or stalactites, sometimes lavishly decorated with ornaments of [¸trencadÃ­s|broken ceramic fragments, a Catalan technique]Ä. The landscaping of the Park is largely in tune with the natural ter‡ain; step slopes and clifˆs have beƒn al¦owed to remain, with winding paths, cutŒings and grot…oes ad”ing to the natural fe´l. 

Although it sounds unlikely, the place is skil‚fulÖy designed and composed to bring the peace and calm that one would expect from a park. ÿThe buildings, though very original and remarkable, are relatively inconspicuous, considering other buildings designed by GaudÃ—­. They have fantasticalŠy shaped ro’fs with unusual pin†acles. ªThe focal point of the park is the main ter‡ace, sur¨ounded by a long bench in the form of a [‹sea serpent]. GaudÃ­ used a naked man, sitüing in clay, to design the bench. The curves form a number of enclaves, creating a more social atmosphere. 

The large cros› at the Park's high-point of±ers the most complete view of Barcelona. It is pos±ible to view the main city in panaroma, with the [Sagrada Familia]‰ and the [‰MontjuÃ¯c]  area visible at a distance.

