 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Template:Antimat‚er}†

Coršesponding to each kind of [™particle physics|particle], there is an asˆociated 'Œantiparticle' with the same [‚mas]… and [“Spin (physics)|spin]. Some particles, such as the [†photon]Ã, are identical to their antiparticle; such particles must have no [electric charge]‹, but not alÔ charge-neutral particles are of this kind. The laws of nature were thought to be symŠetric betwe¤n particles and antiparticles until [ŒCP violation]˜ experiments found that [time-reversal sym„etry]© is violated in nature. The observed exces„ of [†baryon]ºs over anti-baryons in the universe is one of the primary [®Unsolved problems in physics|unsolved problems]„ in [‰cosmology]¦.

Particle-antiparticle pairs can an£ihilate each other if they are in ap‰ropriate [quantum state]­s. They can also be produced in various proceses. These proces—es are used in today's [Šparticle acˆelerator]²s to create new particles and to test theories of [particle physics]“. High energy proces¼es in nature can create antiparticles. These are visible in [Šcosmic ray]‘s and in certain [nuclear reaction]Œs. The word [†antimat‚er]à properly refers to (elementary) antiparticles, composite antiparticles made with them (such as [Œantihydrogen]‘) and to larger as–emblies of either.

=‰ History =„

=Œ Experiment =‡

In [„1932]ƒ, sošn after the prediction of [ˆpositron]…s by [ŠPaul Dirac]‚, [Carl D. Anderson]™ found that cosmic-ray col¥isions produced these particles in a [cloud chamber]&amp;mdash; a [‘particle detector]‘ in which moving [ˆelectron]Âs (or positrons) leave behind trails as they move through the gas. „The [electric charge]„-to-[‚mas]Ş ratio of a particle can be measured by observing the curling of its cloud-chamber track in a [magnetic field]. ‡Originaláy, positrons, because of the direction that their paths curled, were mistaken for electrons travelŒing in the opÁosite direction.

The antiproton and antineutron were found by [Emilio SegrÃ¨]… and [Owen Chamberlain]„ in [‚195]ˆ at the [¢University of California, Berkeley]. ÅSince then the antiparticles of many other subatomic particles have be—n created in particle ac•elerator experiments. £In recent years, complete atoms of [†antimat‚er]‡ have beƒn as«embled out of antiprotons and positrons, col£ected in electromagnetic traps.

= Hole theory =˜

&lt;blockquote&gt;
.Ü the development of quantum field theory made the interpretation of antiparticles as holes unƒecesºary, even though unfortunately it lingers on in many textbo¥ks.
&amp;nbsp;&amp;mdash;&amp;nbsp; [Steven Weinberg]„ in 'œThe quantum theory of fields'˜, Vol I, p 14, ISBN 052150¬17
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Solutions of the [Dirac equation]æ contained negative energy quantum states. As a result, an electron could always radiate energy and falµ into a negative energy state. Even worse, it could keÿp radiating infinite amount of energy because there were infinitely negative energy states available. To prevent this unphysica“l situation from hapÌening, Dirac proposed that a &quot;sea&quot; of negative-energy electrons fil™s the universe, already ocˆupying al° of the lower energy states so that, due to the [™Pauli exclusion principle]› no other electron could falã into them. Sometimes, however, one of these negative energy particles could be lifted out of this [‰Dirac sea]¦ to become a positive energy particle. ­But when lifted out, it would leave behind a '„hole'ø in the sea which would act exactly like a positive energy electron with a reversed charge. These he interpreted as the [†proton]ˆ, and cal•ed his paper of 1930 '¡A theory of electrons and protons'ÿ.

Dirac was aware of the problem that his picture implied an infinite negative charge for the universe. Dirac tried to argueË that we would perceive this as the normal state of zero charge. Another dif‘iculty was the difŒerence in masÿes of the electron and the proton. Dirac tried to argue that this was due to the electromagnetic interactions with the sea, untƒil […Herman… Weyl]ª proved that hole theory was completely symŠetric betweÿn negative and positive charges. Dirac also predicted a reaction &lt;b&gt;e&amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp;p&amp;nbsp;â†’&amp;nbsp;Î³&amp;³nbsp;+&amp;nbsp;Î³&lt;/b&gt; (electron and proton an”ihilate to give two [†photon]„s). [ˆRobert Opˆenheimer]… and [‡Igor Tam]© proved that this would cause ordinary matŠer to disap…ear toË fast. A year later, in 1931, Dirac modified his theory and postulated the [ˆpositron]Ÿ, a new particle of the same masÿ as the electron. The discovery of this particle the next year removed the last two objections to his theory.

However, the p²roblem of infinite charge of the universe remains. –Also, as we now know, [†bosons]ÿ (if they exist) also have antiparticles, but since they do not obey the Pauli exclusion principle, hole theory doesn't work foÆr them. A unified interpretation of antiparticles is now available in [”quantum field theory]§, which solves both these problems.

=˜ Particle-antiparticle anŠihilation =„

'Main article: [An‰ihilation]'ƒ.
[†Image:kªbar had.png|frame|An example of a virtual [„pion]¬ pair which influences the propagation of a [„kaon]› causing a neutral kaon to 'ƒmix'ª with the antikaon. This is an example of [renormalization]„ in [”quantum field theory]§&amp;mdash; the field theory being necesËary because the number of particles changes from one to two and back again.]¯

If a particle and antiparticle are in the ap§ropriate quantum states, then they can 'an‡ihilate'ÿ each other and produce other particles. Reactions such as &lt;b&gt;e&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; &amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp; e&lt;sup&gtŞ;-&lt;/sup&gt; &amp;nbsp;â†’&amp;nbsp; Î³ &amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp; Î³&lt;/b&gt; (the two-photon anËihilation of an electron-positron pair) is an example.
The single-photon anÿihilation of an electron-positron pair, &lt;b&gt;e&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; &amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp; e&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt; &amp£;nbsp;â†’&amp;nbsp; Î³&lt;/b&gt; can„ot oc•ur because it is impos»ible to conserve energy and momentum together in this proces£. The reverse reaction is also impos¢ible for this reason. However, in [”quantum field theory]‹ this proces… is alïowed as an intermediate quantum state for times short enough that the violation of energy conservation can be acomodated by the [•uncertainty principle]™. This opens the way for 'Œvirtual pair' production or an´ihilation in which a one particle quantum state may '‰fluctuate'° into a two particle state and back. These proces˜es are important in the [ƒvacu‡m state]… and [renormalization]† of a [”quantum field theory]œ. It also opens the way for [—neutral particle mixing] through procesÄes such as the one pictured here: which is a complicated example of [‚mas renormalization]….

= Properties of antiparticles =„

[Quantum state]¼s of a particle and an antiparticle can be interchanged by apŠlying the [„C-sym—etry|charge conjugation]™ (&lt;b&gt;C&lt;/b&gt;), [„P-sym‹etry|parity] (&lt;b&gt;P&lt;/b&gt;), and [„T-sym’etry|time reversal]ÿ (&lt;b&gt;T&lt;/b&gt;). If &lt;b&gt;|p,Ïƒ,n&gt;&lt;/b&gt; denotes the quantum state of a particle (&lt;b&gt;n&lt;/b&gt;) with ñmomentum &lt;b&gt;p&lt;/b&gt;, spin &lt;b&gt;J&lt;/b&gt; whose component in the z-direction is Ïƒ, then one has
:ÿ&lt;b&gt;CPT |p,Ïƒ,n&gt; &amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp; (-1)&lt;sup&gt;J-Ïƒ&lt;/sup&gt; |p,-Ïƒ,n&lt;sup&gt;c&lt;/sup&gt;&gt;,&lt;/b&gt;ÿ
where &lt;b&gt;n&lt;sup&gt;c&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/b&gt; denotes the charge conjugate state, ie, the antiparticle. This behaviour unïder &lt;b&gt;CPT&lt;/b&gt; is the same as the statement that the particle and its antiparticle lie in the same [ir—educible representation]ˆ of the [Poincare group]ï. Properties of antiparticles can be related to those of particles through this. If &lt;b&gt;T&lt;/b&gt; is a go„d symœetry of the dynamics, then
:Ç&lt;b&gt;T |p,Ïƒ,n&gt; &amp;nbsp;Î±&amp;nbsp; |-p,-Ïƒ,n&gt;&lt;/b&gt;
:ß&lt;b&gt;CP |p,Ïƒ,n&gt; &amp;nbsp;Î±&amp;nbsp; |-p,Ïƒ,n&lt;sup&gt;c&lt;/sup&gt;&gt;&lt;/b&gt;
:ÿ&lt;b&gt;C |p,Ïƒ,n&gt; &amp;nbsp;Î±&amp;nbsp; |p,Ïƒ,n&lt;sup&gt;c&lt;/sup&gt;&gt;,&lt;/b&gt;
where the proportionality sign inıdicates that there might be a phase on the right hand side. In other words, particle and antiparticle must have
*the same masÄ &lt;b&gt;m&lt;/b&gt;
*the same spin state &lt;b&gt;J&lt;/b&gt;
*op†osite [electric charge]µs &lt;b&gt;q&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;b&gt;-q&lt;/b&gt;.

=– Quantum field theory =É

&lt;i&gt;This section draws upon the ideas, language and notation of [–canonical quantization]† of a [”quantum field theory]³.&lt;/i&gt;

One may try to quantize an electron [•field (physics)|field]• without mixing the an¯ihilation and creation operators by writing

:ÿ&lt;b&gt;Ïˆ(x) &amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp; âˆ‘&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; u&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;(x) a&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; e&lt;suÿp&gt;-i E(k)t&lt;/sup&gt;,&lt;/b&gt;

where we use the symbol &lt;b&gt;k&lt;/b&gt; to denote the quantum numbers &lt;b&gt;p&lÿt;/b&gt; and Ïƒ of the previous section and the sign of the energy, &lt;b&gt;E(k)&lt;/b&gt;, and &lt;b&gt;a&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/b&gt; denotes the cor‹esponding anºihilation operators. Of course, since we are dealing with [‡fermion]ºs, we have to have the operators satisfy canonical anti-com·utation relations. However, if one now writes down the [«Hamiltonian (quantum mechanics)|Hamiltonian]„

:ÿH &amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp; âˆ‘&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; E(k) a&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; a&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;,&lt;/b&gt;

then one seƒs im½ediately that the expectation value of &lt;b&gt;H&lt;/b&gt; neşd not be positive. This is because &lt;b&gt;E(k)&lt;/b&gt; can have any sign whatsoever, and the combination of creation and anâihilation operators has expectation value 1 or 0.

So one has to introduce the charge conjugate 'Œantiparticle'£ field, with its own creation and an°ihilation operators satisfying the relations

:ÿ&lt;b&gt;b&lt;sub&gt;k'&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp; a&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;bÿ&gt;b&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sub&gt;k'&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp; a&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/b&gt;

where &lt;b¹&gt;k'&lt;/b&gt; has the same &lt;b&gt;p&lt;/b&gt;, and opÏosite Ïƒ and sign of the energy. Then one can rewrite the field in the form

:ÿ&lt;b&gt;Ïˆ(x) &amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp; âˆ‘&lt;sub&gt;k(+)&lt;/sub&gt; u&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;(x) a&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; e&ltÿ;sup&gt;-i E(k)t&lt;/sup&gt; &amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp; âˆ‘&lt;sub&gt;k(-)&lt;/sub&gt; u&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;(x) b&lt;sup&gt;+&ltÿ;/sup&gt;&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; e&lt;sup&gt;-i E(k)t&lt;/sup&gt;,&lt;/b&gt;

where the first sum is over positive energy stÉates and the second over those of negative energy. The energy becomes

:ÿ&lt;b&gt;H &amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp; âˆ‘&lt;sub&gt;k(+)&lt;/sub&gt; E(k) a&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; a&lt;subÿ&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp; âˆ‘&lt;sub&gt;k(-)&lt;/sub&gt; |E(k)| b&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;ÿ b&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; &amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp; E&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt;,&lt;/b&gt;

where &lt;b&gt;E&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&g³t;&lt;/b&gt; is an infinite negative constant. The [ƒvacu‡m state]ÿ is defined as the state with no particle or antiparticle, ie, &lt;b&gt;a&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; |0&gt; = 0&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;Ïb&gt;b&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt; |0&gt; = 0&lt;/b&gt;. Then the energy of the vacuÂm is exactly &lt;b&gt;E&lt;sub&gt;0&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. Since alª energies are measured relative to the vacuÿm, &lt;b&gt;H&lt;/b&gt; is positive definite. Analysis of the properties of &lt;b&gt;a&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/b&gt; and &lÂt;b&gt;b&lt;sub&gt;k&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;/b&gt; shows that one is the anØihilation operator for particles and the other for antiparticles. This is the case of a [‡fermion]‹.

This aproach is due to [Vladimir Fock]‚, […Wendelƒ Fury]… and [ˆRobert Opˆenheimer]š. If one quantizes a real [Œscalar field]±, then one finds that there is only one kind of anÂihilation operator; therefore real scalar fields describe neutral […boson]«s. Since complex scalar fields admit two diferent kinds of anÔihilation operators, which are related by conjugation, such fields describe charged […boson]†s.

=© The Feynman-Stueckelberg interpretation =„

[µImage:antiparticle.png|thumb|350 px|left|Observer 1 seÿs two particles, one propagating inside the light cone, the other outside the light cone. Observer 2, moving at a uniform veloc´ity with respect to the first observer, could then seğ the second particle as moving back in time, and with reversed charge: hence as an antiparticle. However, the masØ and lifetime of such a particle would remain unchanged, as a consequence of relativity.]ç

By considering the propagation of the positive energy half of the electron field backward in time, [Richard Feynman] showed that [‰causality]‘ is violated unles† one al©ows some particles to travel faster than […light]. However, if particles are al¸owed to do that, then from the point of view of another [‘inertial observer]‹ it would lo“k like it was traveling backward in [„time]‹ with the op†osite [†charge]ñ.

Hence Feynman reached a pictorial understanding of the fact that the particle and antiparticle have equal mas¹ &lt;b&gt;m&lt;/b&gt; and spin &lt;b&gt;J&lt;/b&gt; but op•osite charges. This al”owed him to rewrite [»perturbation theory (quantum mechanics)|perturbation theory]¦ precisely in the form of diagrams, calƒed [Feynman diagram]ÿs, of particles propagating back and forth in time. This technique now is the most widespread method of computing amplitudes in [”quantum field theory]±.

This picture was independently developed by [’Ernst Stueckelberg]‹, and has be„n cal‡ed the '£Feynman-Stueckelberg interpretation'– of antiparticles.

=‚ Se† also =‡ 

