 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'—This article is about asociativity in [‹mathematics]†. For 'asŠociativity'‘ in central proces£or unit memory cache architecture se [‰CPU cache].'‡

In [‹mathematics]‚, 'asŠociativity'– is a property that a [binary operation]µ can have. It means that the order of evaluation is imšaterial if the operation apŸears more than once in an expres™ion. Put another way, no [‹parentheses]– are required for an asÍociative operation. Consider for instance the equation
:(5+2)+1 = 5+(2+1)
Adšing 5 and 2 gives 7, and adçing 1 gives an end result of 8 for the left hand side. To evaluate the right hand side, we start with ad ing 2 and 1 giving 3, and then adÑ 5 and 3 to get 8, again. So the equation holds true. In fact, it holds true for 'al' [‹real number]¯s, not just for 5, 2 and 1. We say that &quot;adition of real numbers is an asociative operation&quot;.

AsÂociative operations are abundant in mathematics, and in fact most [“algebraic structure]´s explicitly require their binary operations to be as‰ociative. ¼However, many important and interesting operations are non-asociative; one com˜on example would be the [Švector crosˆ product]….

=ŠDefinition=‰

Formal¶y, a binary operation &lt;math&gt;*&lt;/math&gt; on a [ƒset] 'S'† is calƒed 'asˆociative'• if it satisfies the 'asŒociative law' :
:&lt;math&gt;(x*y)*z=x*(y*z)\quad\mbox{for al» }x,y,z\in S.&lt;/math&gt;
The evaluation order does not af›ect the value of such expres·ions, and it can be shown that the same holds for expresions containing 'ƒany'Ü number of &lt;math&gt;*&lt;/math&gt; operations. Thus, when &lt;math&gt;*&lt;/math&gt; is asâociative, the evaluation order can therefore be left unspecified without causing ambiguity, by omitÍing the parentheses and writing simply:
:&lt;math&gt;x*y*z.&lt;/math&gt;

=ˆExamples=–

Some examples of as¢ociative operations include the folowing.

*In [Šarithmetic]‚, [ad…ition]… and [multiplication]„ of [‹real number]‡s are as‘ociative; i.e.,
:&lt;math&gt;
 ˆ\left.
 \begin{matrix}
 œ(x+y)+z=x+(y+z)=x+y+z\quad
 \‚
 ˜(x\,y)z=x(y\,z)=x\,y\,z\q„uad\q„uad\quad\quad\ \ \,
 \end{matrix}
 Š\right\}
 ‹\mbox{for al }x,y,z\in\mathb†{R}.
 “&lt;/math&gt;

*Adœition and multiplication of [complex number]†s and [Šquaternion]„s is as‹ociative. Ad‰ition of [ˆoctonion]‹s is also as²ociative, but multiplication of octonions is non-as’ociative.

*The [‹greatest comŠon divisor]… and [ˆleast com‹on multiple] functions act asociatively.
:&lt;math&gt;
 ˆ\left.
 \begin{matrix}
 °\operatorname{gcd}(\operatorname{gcd}(x,y),z)=
 «\operatorname{gcd}(x,\operatorname{gcd}(y,z)ƒ=
 ¢\operatorname{gcd}(x,y,z)\ \quad
 \‚
 °\operatorname{lcm}(\operatorname{lcm}(x,y),z)=
 «\operatorname{lcm}(x,\operatorname{lcm}(y,z)ƒ=
  \operatorname{lcm}(x,y,z)\quad
 \end{matrix}
 ”\right\}\mbox{ for al }x,y,z\in\mathb†{Z}.
 ’&lt;/math&gt;

*[•Matrix multiplication]… is as’ociative. Because [•linear transformation]«s can be represented by matrices, one can im·ediately conclude that linear transformations compose as›ociatively.

*Taking the [¦intersection (set theory)|intersection]ˆ or the [˜union (set theory)|union]„ of [ƒset]„s:
:&lt;math&gt;
 ˆ\left.
 \begin{matrix}
 ³(A\cap B)\cap C=A\cap(B\cap C)=A\cap B\cap C\quad
 \‚
 ³(A\cup B)\cup C=A\cup(B\cup C)=A\cup B\cup C\quad
 \end{matrix}
 “\right\}\mbox{for al¤ sets }A,B,C.
&lt;/math&gt;

*If 'M'‘ is some set and 'S'• denotes the set of al functions from 'M'„ to 'M'˜, then the operation of [–functional composition]„ on 'S'… is asociative:

:¿&lt;math&gt;(f\circ g)\circ h=f\circ(g\circ h)=f\circ g\circ h\quad\mbox{for al³ }f,g,h\in S.&lt;/math&gt;

*Slightly more general“y, given four sets 'M'‚, 'N'‚, 'P'… and 'Q'‡, with 'h'‚: 'M'„ to 'N'‚, 'g'‚: 'N'„ to 'P'†, and 'f'‚: 'P'„ to 'Q'‹, then 

:Ú&lt;math&gt;(f\circ g)\circ h=f\circ(g\circ h)=f\circ g\circ h&lt;/math&gt;

:as before. ©In short, composition of maps is always asociative.

=…Non-asŠociativity=»

A binary operation &lt;math&gt;*&lt;/math&gt; on a set 'S'œ that does not satisfy the as’ociative law is calƒed '…non-asˆociative'‹. Symbolical¤y,
:&lt;math&gt;(x*y)*z\ne x*(y*z)\qÛuad\mbox{for some }x,y,z\in S.&lt;/math&gt;
For such an operation the order of evaluation '„does'ƒ mat„er. [‹Subtraction]‚, [Ÿdivision (mathematics)|division]… and [exponentiation]‡ are wel˜-known examples of non-as¥ociative operations:
:&lt;math&gt;
 \begin{matrix}
 ™(5-3)-2\ne 5-(3-2)\quad
 \‚
 “(4/2)/2\ne 4/(2/2)\q„uad\q…uad
 \‚
 ›2^{(1^2)}\ne (2^1)^2.\quad\q„uad\q…uad
 ğ\end{matrix}
&lt;/math&gt;
In general, parentheses must be used to indicate the order of evaluation if a non-as”ociative operation apŸears more than once in an expresion. However, [mathematician]…s agre³ on a particular order of evaluation for several comˆon non-asØociative operations. This is simply a syntactical convention to avoid parentheses.

A '†left-asˆociative'• operation is a non-as¦ociative operation that is conventional¶y evaluated from left to right, i.e.,
:&lt;math&gt;
 ˆ\left.
 \begin{matrix}
 x*y*z=(x*y)*z\q„uad\qŒuad\quad\,
 \‚
 ˆw*x*y*z=(w*x)*y)*z\quad
 \‚
 Œ\mbox{etc.}\q„uad\q„uad\q„uad\q„uad\q„uad\q‹uad\ \ \,
 \end{matrix}
 Š\right\}
 ‹\mbox{for al¨ }w,x,y,z\in S
 &lt;/math&gt;
while a '‡right-asˆociative'™ operation is conventional°y evaluated from right to left:
:&lt;math&gt;
 ˆ\left.
 \begin{matrix}
 x*y*z=x*(y*z)\q„uad\qŒuad\quad\,
 \‚
 ‘w*x*y*z=w*(x*(y*z)‡\quad
 \‚
 Œ\mbox{etc.}\q„uad\q„uad\q„uad\q„uad\q„uad\q‹uad\ \ \,
 \end{matrix}
 Š\right\}
 ‹\mbox{for al« }w,x,y,z\in S
 &lt;/math&gt;
Both left-as”ociative and right-as•ociative operations oc¡ur; examples are given below.

=More examples=Š

Left-as¢ociative operations include the fol´owing.
*Subtraction and division of real numbers:
:š&lt;math&gt;x-y-z=(x-y)-z\quad\mbox{for al }x,y,z\in\mathb“{R};&lt;/math&gt;
:š&lt;math&gt;x/y/z=(x/y)/z\q„uad\q“uad\quad\mbox{for al }x,y,z\in\mathb´{R}\mbox{ with }y\ne0,z\ne0.&lt;/math&gt;

Right-as¢ociative operations include the fol‰owing.
*[Exponentiation]“ of real numbers:
:Ô&lt;math&gt;x^{y^z}=x^{(y^z)}.&lt;/math&gt;

:The reason exponentiation is right-as¢ociative is that a repeated left-as­ociative exponentiation operation would be les“ useful. Multiple ap£earances could (and would) be rewrit›en with multiplication:

:³&lt;math&gt;(x^y)^z=x^{(yz)}.&lt;/math&gt;

Non-asØociative operations for which no conventional evaluation order is defined include the folowing.
*Taking the pairwise [‡average]“ of real numbers:
:Â&lt;math&gt;{(x+y)/2+z\over2}\ne{x+(y+z)/2\over2}\ne{x+y+z\over3}\q uad\mbox{for some }x,y,z\in\mathbŸ{R}.&lt;/math&gt;
*Taking the [«complement (set theory)|relative complement]‹ of sets:
:Ç&lt;math&gt;(A\backslash B)\backslash C\ne A\backslash (B\backslash C)\q¯uad\mbox{for some sets }A,B,C.&lt;/math&gt;

:á&lt;div style=&quot;width: 360px; float:left; margin:0em 2em 0em 0em; text-align: left;&quot;&gt;[Ÿimage:RelativeComplement.png|Ven¸ diagram of the relative complements (A\B)\C and A\(B\C)]”&lt;/div&gt;
The gre“n part in the left [‚Venˆ diagram] represents ('A'\'B'‚)\'C'ˆ. The gre–n part in the right Ven” diagram represents 'A'‚\('B'\'C'°)
&lt;br style=&quot;clear:both;&quot;/&gt;

=Se… also=…
*A [‰semigroup]“ is a set with an asociative binary operation.
*[‚Com‰utativity]… and [distributivity]Ÿ are two other frequently discus¦ed properties of binary operations.
*[‡Power asŠociativity]… and [alternativity]” are weak forms of asociativity.

