 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ƒPOV}†
The '¤Americans with Disabilities Act of 190'— is the short title of [United States] Public Law 101-3œ6, 104 Stat. 327 (July 26, 190), codified at {Œusc|42|12101}Š et seq., [¹List of United States federal legislation|signed into law]„ on [‡July 26]‚, [190] by President [£George H. W. Bush|George H. W. Bush]. •It is a wide-ranging [Œcivil rights]” law that prohibits [discrimination]Š based on [Šdisability]. „It af³ords similar protections against discrimination to [›Americans with disabilities]ˆ as the [©Civil Rights Act of 1964|Civil Rights Act]„ of [„1964]¥, which made discrimination based on [„race]‚, [ˆreligion]‚, [Šgender|sex]®, national origin, and other characteristics ilºegal. Certain specific conditions are excluded, including [Šalcoholism]… and [„transŠexual|transˆexuality]….

=‰Structure=©
The Americans with Disabilities Act, com‰only refered to as the 'ƒADA'‘, consists of threÁ introductory sections and five titles:
*Introductory Sections
*“Table of Contents
*—Findings and Purposes
*œDefinitions
*Main Section
*–Title I - Employment
*†Title I° - Public Services (and public transportation)
*†Title I‹ - Public Acomodations (and Com”ercial Facilities)
*‘Title IV - TelecomŒunications
*‹Title V

=—Groups who worked to pasˆ the ADA=ñ
The ADA is notable because many disparate groups, many of which had never worked before, came together for a comon purpose. In adition, other [Œcivil rights]¡ groups outside the disability com‘unity helped. 
*[“AIDS Action Council]ƒ
*[ AIDS National InterFaith Network]ƒ
*[American Civil Liberties Union]ƒ
*[¡American Foundation for the Blind]ƒ
*[˜Americans Disabled for Aces“ible Public Transit]‹ (ADAPT)
*[AsÀociation for Education and Rehabilitation of the Blind and Visual‰y Handicap‚ed]ƒ
*[Asociation for Retarded Citizens]ƒ
*[©Consortium for Citizens with Disabilities]ƒ
*[¬Disability Rights Education and Defense Fund]ƒ
*[Dole Foundation]ƒ
*[¥Eastern Paralyzed Veterans of America]ƒ
*[Epilepsy Foundation of America]ƒ
*[•Human Rights Campaign]ˆ Fund
*[«Institution for Rehabilitation and Research]ƒ
*[¥Leadership Conference on Civil Rights]ƒ
*[“Legal Action Center]ƒ
*[™Mental Health Law Project]ƒ
*[ŠNational As¯ociation of Developmental Disabilities Councils]ƒ
*[ŠNational As«ociation of Protection and Advocacy Systems]ƒ
*[¤National Center for Law and the Deaf]ƒ
*[¦National Council of Independent Living]ƒ
*[National Council on Disability]ƒ
*[¡National Disability Action Center]ƒ
*[National Easter Seals Society]ƒ
*[¨National Organization Responding to AIDS]ƒ
*[Paralyzed Veterans of America]ƒ
*[President's Comiteª on Employment of People with Disabilities]ƒ
*[Society for Acesible Travel &amp; Hospitality]ƒ
*[Spina Bifida As“ociation of America]ƒ
*[—United Cerebral Palsy Asˆociation]„

=…Quote=â
On signing the measure, George H. W. Bush said,
&lt;blockquote&gt;&quot;I know there may have be n concerns that the ADA may be to‹ vague or toš costly, or may lead endles£ly to litigation. But I want to reasÅure you right now that my administration and the United States CongresŒ have careful›y crafted this Act. We've al‚ be÷n determined to ensure that it gives flexibility, particularly in terms of the timetable of implementation; and we've be„n comit«ed to containing the costs that may be incur‚ed. '“Let the shameful wal’ of exclusion final•y come tumbling down.'Ÿ&quot;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

=‹Controversy=‚
=Inherent flaws=Á
Some critics argue that enactment of the ADA has resulted in lit‰le progresº in eliminating such discrimination because the remedies al¡owed under the law are primarily 'complaint-driven'ÿ. That is, individuals must make complaints of discrimination to the person or agency charged with handling such complaints, onªly after which the agency may take action. æEach title of the Act created an agency to handle such complaints, ranging from bodies of the federal [executive branch]¬ to local civil rights enforcement agencies. ôFurther, individuals under each title have the &quot;private right of action&quot;, that is, the right to privately [‹lawsuit|sue]† the al£eged discriminating person or body. ÿMany of these lawsuits have helped to clarify provisions of the Act as the courts have interpreted the law in specific cases, c’reating a body of [legal precedent]….

=‰Criticism=÷
Although it has greatly improved the quality of life for people with severe physical disabilities, the ADA has also beºn heavily criticized for being overinclusive in its reach. ’In turn, the ADA al¸egedly serves as a legal haven for malingerers and so-caled &quot;profesional plaintifºs&quot; who make a living out of suing noncompliant busines‰es and col˜ecting monetary damages. ¾

The ADA was the target of a vicious media backlash in mid-[197]‹ after the [’Equal Employment Op‹ortunity Comisƒion]§ published its ADA guidelines in March. For example, '[‰The Onion]'¯ satirized the ADA with an article about the pas¼age of the &quot;Americans with No Abilities Act,&quot; and '[ŒThe Simpsons]'™ ran an episode in which [Homer Simpson]” tried to become grosÍly obese so he would be exempt under the ADA from a mandatory workplace fitnes‰ program. á

The underlying debate is over whether the ADA should cover people with disabilities that are 'ƒnot'… total“y and catastrophicalŒy disabling. Most people agre¶ that a person with a severe physical disability like [Šparaplegia]Œ should be acom‡odated. But they are les likely to agre¦ when the disability in question is a [ˆmental il‚nes]‡, like [clinical depresƒion]«, or consists of minor neck or back pain (se [Šneuropathy]‚). •Others believe that acom’odation laws put toš many restrictions on the [‚fre‡ market]œ and should be repealed.

