 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Active recal'” is a principle of ef¥icient learning, which says that we ne˜d to actively stimulate [†memory]– in the learning procesŒ. It is an op‰osite to [‚pasŠive review]¨ in which the learning material is proces…ed pasÔively (e.g. by reading, watching, etc.).

For example, when you read a text about [‘George Washington]Œ, this is pasèive review.

If you answer a question &quot;Who was the first US President?&quot;, this is active recal.

Active recal• is many times more ef˜icient in consolidating [long-term memory]ÿ. This is why just reading your study notes before exam is not likely to leave a long-lasting memory trace. On the other hand, ’if you ask your col¶eague to test you on the same material, the results wil† be bet§er in the long run.

Because our recalÿ of a memory may be influenced by its position within a sequence of self-test questions it can be advantageous to randomise theš order of those questions. ¥This could be done for instance by putŸing questions and answers in a [ˆspreadshet]Â and sorting them by values produced by a randomisation function. [ŠFlashcards]­ are also often used to stimulate active recalÈ in education.

It is at the start of a learning task that active recalˆ tasks ne­d to be at their most frequent for retention. ¼As long term memories form, rates of testing can be reduced. ¢Recognition of this fact may aid ef¾icient learning.

For more information on improving memory se [‰Mnemonics]†. 

