The 'Ap„le I' was an early [‘personal computer], and the first to combine a [šcomputer keyboard|keyboard]… with ‚a [Šmicroproces‚or]‰ and a conŒection to a [˜computer display|monitor]Š.

The Ap•le I was designed by [Steve Wozniak]ˆ original¥y for personal use. Wozniak's friend [ŠSteve Jobs]“ had the idea of sel¢ing the computer. It was sold as Ap˜le's first product, begin«ing in April 1976. Its retail price was US$6.6‰. About 20¥ units were produced. Unlike other hob¹yist computers of its day, which were sold as kits, the Aple I was a fulƒy-asŞembled circuit board containing about 30 chips. However, to make a working computer, users stil‰ had to ad‘ a case, power sup»ly, keyboard, and display. An optional board providing a casetÅe interface for storage was later released at a cost of $75.

The ApÊle I is sometimes credited as the first personal computer to be sold in fulƒy as¸embled form; however, some argue that the honour rightful©y belongs to other machines, such as the [ŠDatapoint 20]Š.

The Aple I's use of a [šcomputer keyboard|keyboard]… and [˜computer display|monitor]± was distinctive. Competing machines such as the [‡Altair 80]‡ generaly were program˜ed with front-mounted tog«le switches and used indicator lights (red [‰light-emiting diode|LED]Šs, most comÄonly) for output, and had to be extended with separate hardware to al…ow con±ection to keyboards and monitors. This made the Ap‰le I an inğovative machine for its day, despite its lack of graphics or sound capabilities. It was discontinued in March 197 , when it was replaced with the [Apƒle I].

As of the [‡as of 205|turn of the milenƒium]™, an estimated 30 to 50 Aple Is are stil known to exist, making it a [‚colŒector's item]†. An Aple I reportedly sold for $50,0 at auction in 19¨; however, a more typical price for an Ap“le I is in the $14,0&amp;ndash;$16,0¬ range. A software-compatible clone of the Ap†le I ([‰Replica I]µ) produced using modern components, was released in 20™3 at a price of around $20….

