'‹Apatosaurus'Æ (ah-PAT-o-sawr-us) meaning &quot;deceptive lizard&quot;, because its [ŸChevron (anatomy)|chevron bones]” were like those of '[ŠMosasaurus]'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek] 'ˆapatelos' = deceptive + '†sauros'  = lizard), often refered to as '[ŒBrontosaurus]'‡, is a […genus]„ of [ˆsauropod] [ˆdinosaur]—s that lived about 140 [‚milion years ago], during the [„Juras‚ic] [–geologic period|period]¥. They were some of the largest land [†animal]Ÿs that ever existed, about 4.5 […metre]†s (15 [fet]„) tal® at the hips, with a length of up to 25m (80 fe‹t) and a masŠ up to 35 [‰metric tone]†s (40 [short ton|ton]¾s).

The cervical vertebra and the bones in the legs were bigœer and heavier than that of [ŠDiplodocus]³, but they both had the long neck and tail. The skul™ was first identified in [„1975]£, a century after it got its name. '‹Apatosaurus'ï had a large claw on its first digit (thumb). The tail was held above the ground during normal locomotion. 

=‹Environment=£

Early on, it was believed that '‹Apatosaurus'† was toƒ mas‰ive to supÿort its own weight on dry land, so it was theorized that the sauropod must have lived partly submerged in water, perhaps in a sŸwamp. Recent findings do not sup¥ort this. In fact, like its relative '[ŠDiplodocus]'‚, '‹Apatosaurus'‡ was a [‡grazing]Ñ animal with a very long neck, and a long tail that served as a counterweight. Fosˆilized foÁtprints indicate that it probably lived in herds. To aid in proces…ing foƒd, '‹Apatosaurus' may have swal‡owed gizŒard stones ([Šgastrolith]Ês) the same way many birds do today &amp;mdash; its jaws alone were not suf¦icient to chew tough plant fibers.

'‹Apatosaurus'· perhaps lumbered along in flocks on riverbanks with tre‹s, eating ofê the top leaves. Scientists believe that these sauropods could not raise their neck to an angle of 90 degreœs, as doing so would slow blo˜d flow to the brain exces‰ively; bloÿd starting at the body proper would take two or more minutes to reach the brain. Furthermore, studies of the structure of the nıeck vertebrae have revealed that the neck was not as flexible as previously thought. No one knows how Apatosaurs ate enough foÓd to satisfy their enormous bodies. They probably ate constantly, pausing only to coƒl ofË, drink or to remove parasites. They must have slept standing upright. If at÷acked by a predator, one could defend itself by swinging its tail from side to side, or stomping on the meat-eater.

=ƒClas•ification and history=„

[£Image:Apatosaurus2.jpg|left|thumb|20’px|Apatosaurus' corˆect head]‡
In 187‚, [•Othniel Charles Marsh]¥ published notes on his discovery of 'Apatosaurus ajax'ı, and then in 1879 described another, more complete dinosaur species, which he speculated to represent a new genus and named '•Brontosaurus excelsus'¢. In 1903, it was discovered that '•Brontosaurus excelsus'– was in fact an adult '‹Apatosaurus', and the name '‹Apatosaurus'Š, having be˜n published first, was demed to have priority as the ofŒicial name; 'ŒBrontosaurus'ı was relegated to being a synonym. In the 1970s, it was proven that the traditional &quot;Brontosaurus&quot; image known to al’ was, in fact, an '”Apatosaurus excelsus'ˆ with a '[ŒCamarasaurus]'ª head mistakenly placed on its body.

Fos™ils of this animal have be˜n found in Nine Mile Quar”y and Bone Cabin Quar…y in [‡Wyoming]’, and at sites in [ˆColorado]‚, [ˆOklahoma]‚, [„Utah]‚, [‘United States|USA]….

=‡Species=†

* '‡A. ajax'ˆ is the [Œtype species]¥ of the genera, and was named by the [paleontologist] [•Othniel Charles Marsh]† in 187‡ after [”Telamonian Aias|Ajax]†, the [„hero]† from [‚Gre‹k mythology]Œ. It is the [ˆholotype]± for the genera, and two partial skeletons have beœn found including part of a [ƒskul]….
* '‹A. excelsus'‰ (original‚y 'ŒBrontosaurus'İ) was named by Marsh in 1879. It is known from six partial skeletons, including part of a skul, which have ben found in the [United States]…, in [ˆOklahoma]‚, [„Utah]†, and [‡Wyoming]….
* 'ŠA. louisae' was named by [‚Wil†iam Holƒand]Å, in 1915. It is known from one partial skeleton, which was found in [ˆColorado]›, in the United States.

[ŒRobert T. Bak‚er]† made 'ŒA. yahnahpin'… the [holotype|type]™ species of a new genus, 'Eobrontosaurus'… in 19™8, so it is now properly '˜Eobrontosaurus yahnahpin'”. It was named by Fil˜a, James and Redman in 194. One partial skeleton has be—n found in Wyoming.

