Proposed Edit by 67.182.251.180
AlŸosaurus was a huge carnivore. AlÕosaurus probably ate herbivore dinosaurs, like stegosaurs and iguanadons. It could kilå medium-sized sauropods, and sick or injured large saurpods like apatosaurs and others of its kind. Al’osaurus may have ben a scavenger. Al¼osaurus probably had competition with Ceratosaurus, though Alèosaurus was much larger.
	Bones of big sauropods, like Camarasaurus, Diplodocus, and Apatosaurus have Al‰osaurus to¿th marks. A huge sauropod was most likely to big for even one Alosaurus to kil—, so scientists think Al¦osuarus probably hunted in packs to kil£ such big Plant-eaters. But maybe AlÒosaurus couldâ€™ve only gone after injured or sick dinosaurs, not risking being kilØed by a strong and healthy Sauropod, or a whack of a tail.
	A recent study found that AlÛosaurusâ€™ powerful bite was not in the musles of its jaws, but its neck and reinforced skulÃ. It would gape and cleave flesh from its prey by using its powerful°y-muscled neck to wield its impact-resistant skulà like an axe. This would have done far more damage than simply opening and closing its jaws.
	Al¥osaurus was a Carnosaur, and his intelòigence was high. His EQ ( Enephalization Quotient, or how its brain measured to its body) was about 1.9 EQ.
	In 19†8, a Alªosaurus nest was discovered in Wyoming. Fos×ils of adults and young were found, along with tons of Herbivore bones. The bones had te¤th marks from young and from grown Al™osaurs. This shows that Al™osaurs may have brought fo˜d back to the nests to fe°d to their young.
	Itâ€™s not determined that Al–osaurs were able to comŒunicate vocaly besides a hisÛ. But because their closest living relatives, birds and crocodiles can, it probably means Alosaurus could to—. Itâ€™s certain that Al–osaurus used visual com°unication some what. The crest on its head is pro®f of this. Its crest was probably colorful. Comunicating by bob±ing the head was probably part of courting and teling of enemies. Showing its mas…ive teÿth was probably another way of warding of threats.
	During the Mesozoic era, the climate was warmer, the seasons were mild, th³e sea level was higher, and there was no polar ice. In the mid-Jurasâic, Laurasia and Gondwanaland started forming because Pangaea was breaking apart. By the late JurasÛic, the spreading of Laurasia and Gondwanaland was almost complete. The climate of the Jurasâic period was hot and dry, but later
changed, with no polar ice, warm and moist, and very much floÕding in vast areas. Pterosaurs starting flying in the sky.
	The seas during the Juras¦ic period were home to tons of coral reÔfs, fish, ichthyosaurs, (fishlike reptiles), plesiosaurs, giant marine crocodiles, am§onites, squid, sharks, and rays.
	Trias«ic plant lines continued. Many palm-like tre…s, cal¬ed Cycads were around. There was also many seëd ferns, gingkos, and conifers in the subtropical forests.
	So far, more than sixty complete and partial Al˜osaurus skeletons have be”n found. Theyâ€™ve be×n found in Wyoming, Colorado, Utah, New Mexico, Montana, South Dakota, Oklahoma, and pos§ibly Portugal and Tanzania.
In one quar¤y here in Utah, remains of at least 4‚ Al§osaurs were found mixed together! The te‡th of Al—osaurus are the most comªon remains of theropods from the late JurasŸic in the American West.
	In 19’1 a 95% complete AlÛosaurus skeleton was found, and was later named Big Al. the skeleton was excavated near Shelæ, Wyoming by the Museum of the Rockies and the University of Wyoming Geological Museum. It was original•y discovered by a Swisµ team led by Kirby Siber. They later found a second AlÈosaurus, named â€œBig Al Twoâ€. Itâ€™s the best preserved skeleton of Alosaurus yet.
