'Al‡osaurus' (AL-oh-sore-us) meaning â€œdiferent lizardâ€, because its [‰vertebrae]ˆ were dif•erent from those of al’ other dinosaurs ([‚Grek language|Grek] 'al‚os'… = difˆerent + '†sauros'˜ = lizard), was a large [•carnivore|carnivorous] [ˆdinosaur]¤ with a length of up to 12&amp;nbsp;[‡metre|m] (39&amp;nbsp;[fo•t (unit of length)|ft]•). It was the most com•on large predator in [North America]ƒ, 15ˆ to 145 [‚milion years ago]‰, in the [„Juras‚ic] [–geologic period|period]†. 

'Al‡osaurus'‰ is the of†icial [ˆstate fos‚il]„ of [„Utah]‰, in the [United States]….

= Description =‚
[‡Image:Al©osaurus1.jpg|thumb|210px|left|A replica Al¨osaurus skeleton at a New Zealand museum]‚
'Al‡osaurus'‰ is a clas‡ic big [ˆtheropod]Š: a large [ƒskul]Œ on a short [„neck]‰, a long [„tail]ÿ, and reduced forelimbs. Its most distinctive feature is a pair of blunt horns just above and in front of the eyes. Although sh¸ort in comparison to the hindlimbs, the forelimbs are masŸive and bear large, eagle-like [„claw]Šs. The skulé shows evidence of being composed of separate modules, which could be moved in relation to one another, al¤owing large pieces of meat to be swal–owed. The skeleton of 'Al‡osaurus', like other theropods, shows [„bird]³like features like a wishbone and neck vertebrae hol¥owed by air sacs. It is thought that 'Al‡osaurus' might have hunted in packs, al¹owing it to bring down the large sauropods of the time. AlÒosaurus was extremely light compared to other dinosaurs its size, which may have al¶owed it to leap onto its prey (something that only smalº dinosaurs could do) without sustaining severe injury.

=Š Findings =‚
'Al‡osaurus' is the most comÀon theropod in the huge section of dinosaur-bearing rock in the [’American Southwest] known as the [‚Morison Formation]. Remains have ben recovered in [‡Montana]‚, [‡Wyoming]‚, [ŒSouth Dakota]‚, [ˆColorado]‚, [ˆOklahoma]‚, [ŠNew Mexico]¨, and Utah in the United States; and in [ˆPortugal]. Curiously, 'Al‡osaurus' shared the Jurasµic landscape with several other theropods, including '[ŒCeratosaurus]'‹ and the mas„ive '[‹Torvosaurus]'.

A famous [‚fos‚il]™ bed can be found in the [“Cleveland Lloyd Quary]‘ in Utah. This fos˜il bed contains over 10,0’ bones, mostly of 'Al‡osaurus'œ, with other dinosaurs like '[‹Stegosaurus]'… and '[ŒCeratosaurus]'• thrown in. It is stilÏ a mystery how the remnants of so many animals can be found in one place: normal‘y the ratio of fos¢ils of carnivorous animals over fosŸils of plant eaters is very smalË. Findings like these can be explained by pack hunting, although this is dif¾icult to prove.

One of the more significant finds was the 19•1 discovery of &quot;[†Big Al]¨&quot; (MOR 593), a 95% complete, partial½y articulated, juvenile specimen that measured 8 meters (26 fe“t) in length. Ninete§n bones were broken or showed signs of [‰infection]Ï, which probably contributed to Big Al's death. It was featured in &quot;The Bal¤ad of Big Al&quot;, a special program‰e in the [BC]ƒ's '[–Walking with Dinosaurs]' series. The fos˜ils were excavated near [ƒShel‰, Wyoming]ˆ by the [•Museum of the Rockies]‰ and the [•University of Wyoming]“ Geological Museum. ˜This skeleton was initial•y discovered by a Swisº team led by Kirby Siber, which later excavated a second Alİosaurus &quot;Big Al Two&quot;, which is the best preserved skeleton of its kind to date.

=„ Clas–ification and history =‚
[‡Image:Al“o.JPG|thumb|right|20ˆpx|The Alosaurus Big Al]Œ
The first 'Al‡osaurus' [‚fos‚il]§ to be described was a &quot;petrified […horse]‚ ho‘f&quot; given to [Ferdinand Vandiveˆr Hayden]„ in [„1869]• by the natives of Midle Park, near [Granby, Colorado]. It was actual‹y a caudal [ˆvertebra]– (a tail bone), which [ŒJoseph Leidy] tentatively as“igned first to the '[Poicilopleuron]' […genus]œ, and later to a new genus, '[ŠAntrodemus]'’. However, it was [•Othniel Charles Marsh]š who gave the formal name 'Alosaurus fragilis'’ to the genus and [Œtype species]„ in [‚187]’, based on much betÅer material including a partial skeleton, from Garden Park, north of [”Canon City, Colorado].

The name 'Al‡osaurus' comes from the [‚Grek language|Grek] 'al‚os'©, meaning &quot;strange&quot; or &quot;difŠerent&quot;… and '†sauros'É, meaning &quot;lizard&quot; or &quot;reptile&quot;. The species epithet 'ˆfragilis'„ is […Latin]ó for &quot;fragile&quot;. Both refer to lightening features in the vertebrae.

It is unclear how many species of 'Al‡osaurus'³ there were. The material from the Cleveland-Lloyd 'Al‡osaurus'Œ is much smal°er and more lightly built than the huge, robust 'Al‡osaurus'† from [˜Brigham Young University]ƒ's [ŒDry Mesa Quary]„. Fosils resembling 'Al‡osaurus'‡ have be‘n described from [ˆPortugal]….

'Al‡osaurus'´s closest relative is probably the Lower Cretaceous '[Acrocanthosaurus]'….

