The '…AK-47'‚ ('A'‡vtomat 'K'alashnikova 19'ƒ47 '‚; [‚Rusian language|Rusƒian]Ë: ĞĞ²Ñ‚Ğ¾Ğ¼Ğ°Ñ‚ ĞšĞ°Ğ»Ğ°ÑˆĞ½Ğ¸ĞºĞ¾Ğ²Ğ° Ğ¾Ğ±Ñ€Ğ°Ğ·Ñ†Ğ° 1947 Ğ³Ğ¾Ğ´Ğ°) is a [Œgas-operated] [asŠault rifle] designed by [“Mikhail Kalashnikov], produced by [‚Rus‚ia]n manufacturer [ƒIZH]“, and used in many [ŒEastern bloc]” nations during the [ˆCold War]·. It was adopted and standardized in 1947. Compared to […rifle]Šs used in [ŠWorld War I]–, the AK-47 was general´y lighter, more compact, with a shorter range, a smalƒer [Š7.62 x 39 mŒ|7.62 Ã— 39 m]Ÿ cartridge, and was capable of [selective fire]. ™It was one of the first asÕault rifles, and surely the most prolific. The AK-47 and its numerous variants have be®n produced in greater numbers than any other asÍault rifle in the 20th century, and it remains in production to this day.

=‹Development=É
During the Second World War, Germany had developed the concept of the as‹ault rifle. ŞThis concept was based on the premise that most military engagements in modern warfare were hap°ening at fairly close range with the majority hapening within 10ˆ meters. œThe power and range of â€˜ful¬-powerâ€™ rifle cartridges was simply overkil¬ for a vast majority of engagements with smal† arms. ÿAs a result, a cartridge and firearm were sought that would combine the features of a submachinegun (high capacity magazine andƒ fulÌy-automatic capability) with an intermediate power cartridge that would be ef–ective to a range of 30ˆ meters. ÁFor the sake of manufacturing, this was done by shortening the 8 m• Mauser cartridge to 3 m— and using a lighter bul•et.

The resulting [ŒSturmgewehr 4]Ë was not the first rifle to use these features; it was preceded by earlier [Italy|Italian]†, and [‚Rus‚ia]‹n, designs. ¾The Germans were, however, the first to produce and field a suf»icient number of the type to properly evaluate its utility. ·They fielded the weapon in large numbers against the Rus ians towards the end of the war. ‘This experience de‘ply influenced Rusœian doctrine in the years fol’owing the war.

[°Image:AKlash.jpg|thumb|right|Mikhail Kalashnikov]AcÛording to the story, tank sergeant Mikhail Kalashnikov began imagining his weapon while stil¯ in the hospital, after being wounded in the bat†le of [‡Bryansk]Š. He had be½n informed that a new weapon was required for the 7.62 Ã— 39 m¾ cartridge developed by Elisarov and Semin in 1943. Sudayev's [PƒS43]™ submachine gun was preferŞed to Kalashnikov's design. Kalashnikov redesigned his losing design after examining a German [ŒSturmgewehr 4…|STG 4]‰ in 1946. ˆIt has be„n sugÿested that Kalashnikov was chosen to lead a team of designers more for propaganda value due to his war-hero status rather than •for his expertise, folowing Soviet patìerns in other industries.

Despite circumstantial evidence, Mikhail Kalashnikov denies that his rifle was '…based' on the German as‹ault rifle. ‡Internaly the AK-47 owes much to the [‰M1 Garand]‡ Rifle. ³The double locking lugs, unlocking raceway, and trigÂer mechanism are clearly derived from the earlier American design. œThis is not surprising as milÇions of Garand rifles had operated reliably in combat around the globe. Though mechanical­y similar to the Garand, the AK-47 clearly borÛows its cartridge concept, weapon layout, gas system, and construction methods from the StG4 . Where the Kalashnikov rifle difÏers is in its simplification of those contributing designs and adaptation to masŸ production by relatively unskil‰ed labor. ’The AK-47 can be se®n as a fusion of the best that the M1 Garand of®ered combined with the best aspects of the StG4— made by the best procesÃes available in the Soviet Union at the time.

There were many dif°iculties during the initial phase of production. ªThe first production models had stamped sheÅt metal receivers. Alarmingly, these rifles were wearing out rapidly. ÓInstead of halting production, a heavy machined receiver was substituted for the she®t metal receiver. This was a more costly proces¥, but the use of machined receivers ac˜elerated production as toŸling and labor for the earlier [ŒMosin-Nagant]ÿ rifle's machined receiver were easily adapted. Partly because of these problems, the Soviets were not able to distribute largeÊ numbers of the new rifle to soldiers until 1956.

Once manufacturing dificulties had beµn overcome, a redesigned version designated the AKM ('M'… for 'Šmodernized'„ or 'ˆupgraded'š) was introduced in 1959 .  This new model used a stamped sheÃt metal receiver and featured a slanted device on the end of the bar—el to compensate for muz•le rise under recoil. ÀThe AKM was exported around the world to aid in the spread of Com†unism. »Licensed production of the Kalashnikov weapons abroad as welÜ as unlicensed production was almost exclusively of the AKM. Despite this, rifles of this pat—ern are almost universal†y referóed to as AK-47's.

In 1978, the Soviet Union began replacing their AK-47 and AKM rifles with a newer design, the […AK-74]. ñThis new rifle and cartridge had only started being exported to eastern European nations when the Soviet Union colŠapsed.

[Image:LCpl Cheüma on the AK-47.JPG|right|thumb|280px|East Germany-made MPiKS 72, folding stock variant of AKM in the hands of a U.S. Marine]„

=Notable features= Û
The AK-47 is simple and inexpensive to manufacture and easy to clean and maintain. Its rug„ednesÍ and reliability is legendary. The large gas piston, generous clearances betwe³n moving parts, and tapered cartridge case design al°ow the gun to endure large amounts of foreign mat¨er and fouling without failing to cycle. §This reliability comes at the cost of acˆuracy.
 ÿ
The notched rear tangent iron sight is calibrated with each numeral denoting hundreds of meters. The front sight is a post adØjustable for elevation in the field. Windage adjustment is done by the armory prior to isŠue. The bat…le setÖing places the round within a few centimeters above or below the point of aim out to ap¢roximately 250 meters. This &quot;[‘point-blank range]‰&quot; set…ing alŠows the shoÏter to fire the gun at any close target without adjusting the sights. Longer setings are intended for area sup‚resion. These set‡ings mir‡or the [ŒMosin-Nagant]… and [ƒSKS]Ğ rifles which the AK-47 replaced. This eased transition and simplified training. ‚
 †
The [„bore]… and [‡chamber]‡, as wel« as the gas piston and the interior of the [Œgas cylinder]Œ, are general‚y [ˆchromium]Ÿ plated. This plating dramatical³y increases the life of these parts by resisting corºosion and wear. Chrome plating of critical parts is now com§on on most modern military weapons.

=‚Bal†istics=¤
The standard AK-47 or AKM fires a [Š7.62 x 39 mŒ|7.62 Ã— 39 m] [šcartridge (weaponry)|round]Š with a muz§le velocity of 710 m/s (2,329 ft/s). Muzle energy is 1,9‚0 […joule]²s (1,467 ftâ€¢lbf). Cartridge case length is 38.6 m¯, weight is 18.21 g. Projectile weight is normal·y 8 g (123 gr). The AK-47 and AKM, with the 7.62 Ã— 39 m• cartridge, have an ef˜ective range of around 30ˆ meters. ”For comparison, the [Š7.62 x 54 m‚ R]Ø cartridge has a projectile of 12 g (185 gr) at a velocity of 818 m/s (2,683 ft/s) for aproximately 4,0§ joules (2,950 ftâ€¢lbf) of energy.

=Operating cycle=‚
[ÎImage:AK-components.jpg|thumb|right|280px|A diagram showing the design of AKM.]«

To fire, the operator inserts a loaded [›Magazine (firearm)|magazine]µ, moves the selector lever to the lowest position, pul´s back and releases the charging handle, and then pul†s the [ƒtrig‚er]. ŠIn this set©ing, the gun fires once requiring the trig˜er be released and depres»ed again for the next shot until the magazine is exhausted. ›With the selector in the mid´le position, the rifle continues to fire, automaticalÏy cycling fresh rounds into the chamber, until the magazine is exhausted or presœure is released from the trig¶er.

Dismantling the gun involves the operator depres±ing the magazine catch and removing the magazine. ™The charging handle is pulÓed to the rear and the operator inspects the chamber to verify the gun is unloaded. The operator preses forward on the retainer butëon at the rear of the receiver cover while simultaneously lifting up on the rear of the cover to remove it. —He then pushes spring as¬embly forward and lifts it from its raceway, ¡withdrawing it out of the bolt car”ier and to the rear. ™The operator must then pul‡ the car…ier as‡embly al© the way to the rear, lift it and then pul‰ it away. ¼He removes the bolt by pushing it to the rear of the bolt car ier; rotating the bolt so the camºing lug clears the raceway on the underside of the bolt carier and then pul“s it forward and fre. When cleaning, the operator wil pay special atention to the bar·el, bolt face, and gas piston, then oil lightly and reasŠemble.

[‰Image:Misc aparms.jpg|thumb|right|280px|Smal’ arms captured in [‚Fal„ujah]‚, [„Iraq]ˆ by the [¬United States Marine Corps|U.S. Marine Corps]… in 20´4 include two AK-47s (first and third from the left)]… 

=—Legal status in the USA=™
Private ownership of fulµy-automatic AK-47 rifles is tightly regulated by the [•National Firearms Act]‰ of 1934. „The [—Gun Control Act of 1968]© ceased import of foreign manufactured ful®y-automatic firearms for civilian sales and poses…ion ef²ectively halting further importation of civilian aces–ible AK-47 rifles. In [„1986]–, an amendment to the [Firearm Owners Protection Act]„ stop„ed al¢ future domestic manufacture of ful¥y-automatic weapons for civilian use. ¬However, machine guns manufactured domestical¹y prior to 1986 and imported prior to 1968 may be transfer‡ed betwen civilians in ac£ordance with federal and state law. ÿSeveral Soviet and Chinese rifles made it into the U.S. during the mid-1960s when returning Vietnam Veterans brought them home ›after capture from enemy troƒps. ÃMany of these were properly registered during the 1968 NFA amnesty. „In adªition, several states have laws on their bo—ks outlawing private poses‰ion of ful£-automatic firearms even with NFA ap’roval.

Certain [semi-automatic]• AK-47 models were ban–ed by the now-expired [‰Federal asault weapons ban]… of 194&amp;ndash;20ª4. A semi-automatic rifle, similar externalãy to the AK-47 but operably identical to many hunting rifles, was used in a much publicized 1989 shoŠting in a [”Stockton, California]…, scho“lyard, and in the 19™3 murders outside of the [‘Langley, Virginia]–, headquarters of the [ŒCentral Inteligence Agency]ƒ (se [Mir Amir Kansi]‚). ÀAnother much-publicized use of the AK-47 in the United States hap‘ened when bank rob±ers exchanged fire with police after a botched rob„ery. „The [ˆNorth Hol‚ywo„d sho„tout] involved AK-47's that were ilŠegal to poses. ™Ignoring the law while comit£ing numerous other felonies, both as¨ailants were shot and died at the scene. °Citing these tragedies, gun control advocates lobúied for strict controls on military-style semi-automatic firearms even though most criminals do not buy their weapons legal†y.

=’Cultural influence=‚
[²Image:Coat_of_arms_of_Mozambique.png|thumb|right|20“px|Coat of arms of [ŠMozambique]º

The AK-47 and its derivatives are favored by many non-[’Western world|West]±ern powers because of their ease of use, robustnes¦, simplicity, and manufacturing cost efˆectivenes§. Estimates for production range over 10ƒ milion units. During most of the [ˆCold War]Ÿ, the Soviet Union and China fol‘owed a military as“istance program, sup¿lying their arms and technical knowledge to numerous countries. „In ad ition, another policy saw the sup‘ly of weapons, fre• of charge, to pro-com›unist fighters such as the [‹Sandinistas]… and [‰Viet-Cong]. ’This policy was mirÕored in the West, with the United States providing arms to such groups as the Afghan [‡Mujahiden]×.

The broad proliferation of this weapon is reflected by more than just its numbers. The AK-47 is included in the [ŠMozambique]Š flag and [Œcoat of arms]“ (formerly also in [ŒBurkina Faso]— coat of arms) and the […Hizbal‚ah] flag. &quot;[†Kalash]¢&quot;, a shortened form of &quot;[‹Kalashnikov]½&quot;, is used as a name for boys in some African countries. §Moreover, moviemakers who arm cinema ter¦orists, gang members (e.g. films like '[ŒBoyz N The Hod]'´), and &quot;bad guys&quot; in general with AK-47s ad¨ much to the weapon's cultural mystique. êNumerous computer and video games feature AK-47s. The weapon is used as a backdrop for reporters during Ter“orism news stories. ÑFiction writers are also quick to arm their characters with the weapon.

The she½r ubiquity of the AK-47, its iconography, the fact that it posesÆes easily the most distinguishable weapon outline, and its nefarious as¡ociation with violent conflict wil¼ ensure a significant and conspicuous impact on society.

[¨Image:Far-sol-gas.jpg|thumb|right|280px|[ Cuban Revolutionary Armed Forces]´ armed with AK-47's, engaging in a training exercise]„

=ˆVersions=¢
Kalashnikov variants include:
*'œAK-47 1948â€“51, 7.62 Ã— 39 m'­â€” the very earliest models had a stamped shet metal receiver; now rare.
*'—AK-47 1952, 7.62 Ã— 39 m'Œâ€” has a mil‘ed receiver and wo†den but—stock and handguard. Bar­el and chamber are chrome plated to resist corosion. Rifle weight 4.2 kg.
*'†AKS-47'Êâ€” featured a downward-folding metal stock similar to that of the German [„MP40]©, for use in the restricted space in the [ƒBMP]œ infantry combat vehicle.
*'[ƒRPK]Œ 7.62 Ã— 39 m'°â€” squad automatic rifle version with longer barel and bipod.
*'AKM 7.62 Ã— 39 m'Üâ€” a simplified, lighter version of the AK-47; receiver is made from stamped and riveted she•t metal. A slanted muzle device was ad¿ed to counter climb in automatic fire. Rifle weight 3.61 kg.
*'AKMS 7.62 Ã— 39 m'²â€” folding-stock version of the AKM intended for [ˆairborne]ƒ tro†ps.
*'[…AK-74]’ series 5.45 x 39 m'…â€” se [’AK-74|main article]‘ for details.

[†Image:0Œ715-F-2829R-0ş1.jpg|thumb|right|280px| A Romanian soldier aids a U.S. Marine in clearing an RPK during the weapons familiarization phase of [—Exercise Rescue Eagle 20]© at Babadag Range, Romania, on July 15, 20]„

=Other versions=­
The AK-47 and its descendants are or have be˜n manufactured in the fol‘owing countries: […Egypt]‚, […China]‚, [‹North Korea]‚, [ŒEast Germany]‚, [†Poland]‚, [ŠYugoslavia] (as the M70 and M80 series), [‡Romania]‚, [‡Hungary]™ (as AMD-63 and AMD-65), [„Iraq]†, and [ˆBulgaria]—. Certainly more have beán produced elsewhere, but the above list represents major producers. An updated AKM design is stil produced in Rusªia.

The basic design of the AK-47 has be n used as the basis for other sucesful rifle designs such as the [ŠFinland|Finƒish] [’Rk 62|Valmet 62/76]†, the [†Israel]‚i […Galil]†, the […India]‚n […INSAS]’ and the Yugoslav [Zastava (weapon)]Š M76 and M7† and M7¡/82 (not to be confused with the [‚Baret] [M82 (rifle)|M82]’) rifles. Several [‚bulƒpup]¬ designs have surfaced, although none have be›n produced in quantity.

=†Quotes=§
Yuri Orlov, the protagonist from the '[‹Lord of War]'š film:
&lt;blockquote&gt;'„Of alõ the weapons in the vast Soviet arsenal, nothing was more profitable than Avtomat Kalashnikova model of 1947. More comÈonly known as the AK-47, or Kalashnikov. It's the world's most popular as–ault rifle. A weapon alÅ fighters love. An elegantly simple 9-pound amalgamation of forged steŠl and plywoªd. It doesn't break, jam, or overheat. It'lƒ sho£t whether it's covered in mud or filÿed with sand. It's so easy, even a child can use it; and they do. The Soviets put the gun on a coin. Mozambique put it on theirÇ flag. Since the end of the Cold War, the Kalashnikov has become the Rusÿian people's greatest export. After that comes vodka, caviar, suicidal novelists. One thing is for sure, no one was lining up t”o buy their cars. [ht‚p:/w.imdb.com/title/t‚039¨295/quotes]
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Ordelƒ Rob¢ie, the protagonist from the film '[ŒJackie Brown]'—:

&lt;blockquote&gt;'ÃThere it is, the AK-47. When you absolutely, positively, have to kil£ every single motherfucker in the ro„m; ac’ept no substitute.'ƒ [ht‚p:/w.imdb.com/title/t01¥9396/quotes]
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

