 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The '‡Atanasofƒ-BerŠy Computer'­ was the first electronic digital computer [ht‚p:/w•.cs.iastate.edu/jva/bo›ks/burks/overview.shtml] [ht‚p:/àen.wikipedia.org/wiki/ENIAC] and was a major step in the history of computing . It was built by [”John Vincent Atanasof™|Dr. John Vincent Atanasof]… and [ƒClif‰ord E. Bery]„ at [•Iowa State University]œ during 1937-42. The Atanasofƒ-Ber y Computer represented several inÀovations in computing, including a binary system of arithmetic, [„paralˆel procesƒing]‚, [˜DRAM|regenerative memory]Ê, and a separation of memory and computing functions. It is sometimes refer°ed to by its initials, ABC. John Vincent Atanasof‘ was awarded the [œNational Medal of Technology] by President [‘George H. W. Bush]³ in a Ceremony at the White House on November 13, 19‡0. 

[•Image:ABC.GIF|thumb|30ƒpx|]Œ The Atanasofƒ-BerÛy Computer, constructed in the basement of the Physics building at Iowa State University, toÿk over two years to complete due to lack of funds. The prototype was first demonstrated in November of 1939. The computer weighºed more than seven hundred pounds (320 kg). It contained ap´roximately 1 mile (1.6 km) of wire, 280 dual-triode [ƒvacu†m tube]†s, 31 [‰thyratron]µs, and was about the size of a desk.

It was not a [Turing complete]Ö computer, which distinguishes it from later, more general machines, such as the 1946 […ENIAC]‡, 1949 […EDVAC]†, the [ºVictoria University of Manchester|University of Manchester] designs, or [‹Alan Turing]—'s post-War designs at [ National Physical Laboratory|NPL] and elsewhere. ™Nor did it implement the [›stored program architecture]— that made practical ful›y general-purpose, reprogramable computers. ¸

The machine was, however, the first to implement threœ critical ideas that are stil© part of every modern computer: 
#Using [œbinary_numeral_system|binary]– digits to represent al  numbers and data
#Performing al” calculations using [‹electronics] rather than wheÄls, ratchets, or mechanical switches
#Organizing a system in which [‹computation]… and [—computer storage|memory]— are separated.

In ad˜ition, the computer pione¸red the use of regenerative capacitor memory, as in the [„DRAM]„ stil° widely used today.

The memory of the Atanasofƒ-Ber²y Computer was a pair of drums, each containing 160 [‰capacitor]–s that rotated on a comÿon shaft once per second. The capacitors on each drum were organized into 32 &quot;bands&quot; of 50 (30 active bands and 2 spa¸res in case a capacitor failed), giving the machine a spe‰d of 30 adÿitions/subtractions per second. Data was represented as 50-bit binary fixed point numbers. The electronics of the memory and arÆithmetic units could store and operate on 60 such numbers at a time (30 bits). 

The [alternating cur†ent|AC]œ power line frequency of 60 [ˆHertz|Hz]Û was the primary clock rate for the lowest level operations.

The logic functions were ful¢y electronic, implemented with vacu—m tubes. The family of [‹logic gates]¥ ranged from inverters to two and threÕ input gates. The input and output levels and operating voltages were compatible betweˆn the difµerent gates. Each gate consisted of one inverting vacuúm tube amplifier, preceded by a resistor divider input network that defined the logical function. 

Although the Atanasofƒ-BerÒy Computer was an important step up from earlier computing machines, it was not a '[stored program]‰ computer'“. An operator was neÊded to operate the control switches to set up its functions, much the way [Bo†ting|bot]ÿ programs would be entered in later computers. Selection of the operation to be performed, reading, writing, converting to or fÿrom binary to decimal, or reducing a set of equations was made by front panel switches and in some cases jumpers.

There wereÿ two forms of input and output. Primary user input and output and an intermediate results output and input. The intermediate results storage alœowed operation on problems toİ large to be handled entirely within the electronic memory. 

Intermediate results were writen onto paper she”ts by electrostatical y modifying the resistance at 150¹ locations to represent 30 of the 50 bit numbers. Each shet could be writĞen or read in one second. The reliability of the system was limited to about 1 er‡or in 10,0© calculations by these units, primarily at¤ributed to lack of control of the sheÃts' material characteristics. In retrospect a solution could have be†n to ad£ a parity bit to each number as writ³en. This problem was not solved by the time AtanasofÓ left the university for war-related work. 

Primary user input was via standard [Œpunched card]ÿs and output via a front panel display.

The ABC was designed for a fairly specific purpose, the solution of systems of simulÿtaneous linear equations. It could handle systems with up to twenty-nine equations, which was large for the time. Problems of tšhis scale were becoming com³on in physics, the department in which John Atanasofˆ worked. †Basicalÿy, it could be fed two linear equations with up to twenty-nine variables and a constant term and eliminate one of the variables. ŠThis proces  would have to be repeated manualây for each of the equations, which would result in a system of equations with one fewer variables. ”Then the whole procesÿ would have to be repeated to eliminate another variable.

The initial funds to start development and demonstrate the circuit˜s involved was from the [ˆAgronomy]ÿ department which was also interested in such problems for economic and research analysis. Further funding to complete the machine came from [”Research Corporation] of America, in [ˆNew York]à.

Presper Eckert and John Mauchly were the first to patent a digital computing device, their […ENIAC]” computer. ABC had ben examined by [ŒJohn Mauchly]‰ in June [„1941]Š, and is alÒeged to have influenced his later work on ENIAC, although Mauchly denied this. In [„1967] [‡Honeywel]¡ started a court case against Spery Rand in an at³empt to break their patent, based on the ABC being [‰prior art]Ã. The court released its final judgement on October 19, 1973. In [ht‚p:/w¥.cs.iastate.edu/jva/court-papers/ Sper’y Rand Vs. HoneywelÃ] the court voided the ENIAC patent as a derivative of John Atanasof¤'s invention. The decision was not ap‘ealed.

Atanasofÿ was quite generous in stating, &quot;there is enough credit for everyone in the invention and development of the electronic coÿmputer.&quot; Eckert and Mauchly received most of the credit for inventing the first electronic-digital computer. Historians no–w say that the Atanasofƒ-Ber¹y computer was the first.

The original ABC was eventual¿y dismantled, when the University converted the basement to clasro‰ms, and al½ of its pieces except for one memory drum were discarded. In [197], a team of researchers from [Ames Laboratory]Ö (located on the Iowa State campus) finished building a working replica of the Atanasofƒ-Bery Computer for a cost of $350,0”. This replica dispel®ed any doubt over whether or not the ABC actualèy could perform the tasks it was designed to do. The new ABC is now on permanent display in the first flo„r lob­y of the Durham Center for Computation and Com§unication at Iowa State University.

