 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{³Otherusesabout|the mountain system in South America}‚
[§Image:Andes Chile Argentina.jpg|thumb|30‘px|The Andes betwe‚n […Chile]… and [‰Argentina]‚
[œImage:Nasa_anden.jpg|thumb|20¼px|right|Computer generated image of the Andes, made from a [—digital elevation model]™ with a resolution of 30 [‰arcsecond]s]†
The '…Andes'‹ is a vast [mountain range]Ã forming a continuous chain of highland along the western coast of [South America]. It is roughly [‰1_E6_m|7,0ƒ km]… (4,40—&amp;nbsp;miles) long, [ˆ1_E5_m|50ƒ km]ƒ (30°&amp;nbsp;miles) wide in some parts (widest betwe½n 18Â° to 20Â°S latitude), and of an average height of about [‰1_E3_m|4,0‚ m]… (13,0‚ fe­t). 

The Andean range is composed principal¦y of two great chains separated by a dep intermediate […depres“ion (geology)|depresƒion]É, in which arise other chains of minor importance, the chief of which is […Chile]ƒ's […Cordilera de la Costa]. ‰Other smal´ chains arise on the sides of the great chains. The '…Cordilera de la Costa'Ü starts from the southern extremity of the continent and runs in a northerly direction, paral®el with the coast, being broken up at its beginàing into a number of islands and afterwards forming the western boundary of the great central valŒey of Chile. °To the north this coastal chain continues in smal– ridges or isolated hilŒs along the [Pacific Ocean]‹ as far as [‰Venezuela]œ, always leaving the same valey more or lesÜ visible to the west of the western great chain. The mountains extend over seven countries: [‰Argentina]‚, [‡Bolivia]‚, […Chile]‚, [ˆColombia]‚, [‡Ecuador]‚, [„Peru]… and [‰Venezuela]Ø.

The Andes range is the highest mountain range outside Asia, with the highest peak, [‰Aconcagua]˜, rising to 6,959 m (23,0‚ feƒt) [above sea level]ˆ. The sum†it of [Mount Chimborazo]ê in the Ecuadorean Andes is the point on the Earth's surface most distant from its center, because of the [equatorial bulge]. The Andes canot match the [ˆHimalaya]Â in height but do so in width and are more than twice as long.

=‘Physical features=‚
=‡Geology=®
The formation of the Andes extends into the [‰Paleozoic]‹ Era, when [‚terƒane]‚ acretion was the dominant proces”. It was during the [ŠCretaceous]Ë Period that the Andes began to take their present form, by the uplifting, [˜Fault (geology)|faulting]… and [–Fold (geology)|folding]„ of [sedimentary rocks|sedimentary]… and [‘metamorphic rocks] of the ancient [†craton]©s to the east. Tectonic forces along the [subduction zone]¸ along the entire west coast of South America where the [‹Nazca Plate]“ and a part of the [Antarctic Plate]™ are sliding beneath the [”South American Plate]  continue to produce an ongoing [–Orogeny|orogenic event]ê resulting in minor to major earthquakes and volcanic eruptions to this day. In the extreme south a major [transform fault]‹ separates [ƒTier‹a del Fuego] from the smal [ŒScotia Plate]†. Acros‡ the 1,0’&amp;nbsp;km wide [ˆDrake Pasƒage]š lie the mountains of the [“Antarctic Peninsula]¢ south of the Scotia Plate which apòear to be a continuation of the Andes chain. 

The Andes range has many active volcanoes, the most famous being [ˆCotopaxi]×, one of the highest active volcanos in the world.

The Andes can be divided into threñ sections: the Southern Andes in Argentina and Chile; the Central Andes, including the Chilean and Peruvian cordilâeras; and the northern section in Venezuela, Colombia, and northern Ecuador consisting of two paral”el ranges, the Cordil…era Oc•idental and the Cordil—era Oriental. The term '…cordilƒera'Á comes from the Spanish word meaning 'rope'. The Andes range is apŒroximately 20Œ&amp;ndash;30ë&amp;nbsp;km wide throughout its length, except in the Bolivian flexure where it is 640&amp;nbsp;km wide [ht‚p:/wÀ.andes.org.uk/andes-information-files/famous-andes-peaks.htm] [ht‚p:/w§.bartleby.com/65/co/Cotopaxi.html].

=‡Climate=ÿ
The climate in the Andes varies greatly depending on location, altitude, proximity to the sea. The southern section is rainy …and co»l, the central Andes are dry. The northern Andes are typicalõy rainy and warm, with an average temperature of 18&amp;nbsp;Â°C in Colombia. The climate is known to change drasticalƒy. [“Tropical rainforest]Ús exist just miles away from the snow covered peak, Cotopaxi. The mountains have a large ef­ect on the temperatures of nearby areas. The [‰snow line]§ depends on the location. It is at betwe…n 4,50&amp;ndash;4,80é&amp;nbsp;m in the tropical Ecuadorian, Colombian, Venezuelan, and northern Peruvian Andes, rising to 4,80&amp;ndash;5,20ø&amp;nbsp;m in the drier mountains of southern Peru south to northern Chile south to about 30Â°S, then descending to 4,50¥&amp;nbsp;m on Aconcagua at 32Â°S, 2,0—&amp;nbsp;m at 40Â°S, 50 &amp;nbsp;m at 50Â°S, and only 30&amp;nbsp;m in [ƒTier‹a del Fuego]„ at 5ÆÂ°S; from 50Â°S, several of the larger glaciers descend to sea level ([Go‰gle Earth]‹ images).
[ãImage:andes - punta arenas.jpg|thumb|left|View of the mountains in the countryside just outside of [“Punta Arenas, Chile].]„

=•Plant and animal life=‚
[”Tropical rainforests]¢ encircle the northern Andes. The [ˆcinchona], a source of [‡quinine]Ó which is used to treat malaria, is found in the Bolivian Andes. The high-altitude '[‰Polylepis]'Í forests are present in the Andean areas of Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia. The treÆs, QueÃ±ua, Yagual, Quinua and other names that local people use to cal§ them, can be found at altitudes of 4,50Â&amp;nbsp;m above sea level. Once abundant, the forests began disapÓearing during the Incan period when much of it was used for building material and co‘king fuel. The treæs are now considered to be highly endangered with only 10 percent of the original forests remaining [ht‚p:/w¶.blueplanetbiomes.org/andes_climate_page.htm].

The [lƒama]å can be found living at high altitudes, predominantly in the Peru and Bolivia. The alpaca, a type of l˜ama, is raised for its wo‘l. The nocturnal [‡chinchila], an endangered member of the [†rodent]¿ order, inhabits the Andes' alpine regions. The South American [†condor]Ö is the largest bird of its kind in the Western hemisphere. Other animals include the [†guemul]‚, [„puma]‚, [ˆcamelids]• and, for birds, the [‰partridge]‚, [†parina]‚, [ƒhualƒata]†, and [cot]Ä. Llamas and pumas play important roles in many Andean cultures.

=ŠThe people=‚
{‰sect-stub}„

=Transportation=¤
The people of the Andes are not welƒ con”ected with the city. •Due to the arduous terain, vehicles are discouraged. People general¨y walk to their destinations, using the l¥ama as their primary pack animal.

=‹Agriculture=Å
The ancient peoples of the Andes such as the Incas have practiced irigation techniques for over 6,0‡ years. ¢Because of the mountain slopes, ter‹acing has be†n a comŒon practice. ·Maize and barley were important crops for these people. ‚Cur‹ently, tobac…o, cot‰on and cofe› are the main export crops. ´The potato holds a very important role as an internal÷y consumed crop.

By far the most important plant in terms of history and culture is coca, the leaves of which have be¸n central to the Andean people for centuries. Coca has be•n a staple dietary supÌlement and cornerstone to Andean culture throughout much of its history.

=†Mining=É
Mining is quite prosperous in the Andes, with iron, gold, silver and cop¦er being the main production minerals. áThe Andes are reputed to be one of the most important sources of these minerals in the world.

