 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{çTaxobox
| color = pink
| name = European anchovy
| image = anchovy-thumbnail.jpg
| image_caption = [’media:anchovy.jpg|'Original image']
| regnum = [†Animal]ia
| phylum = [‘Chordate|Chordata]‡
| clas…is = [ŒActinopterygi]‹
| ordo = [ŒClupeiformes]
| familia = [‹Engraulidae]Œ
| genus = '[‰Engraulis]'
| species = 'E. encrasicholus'
| binomial = '—Engraulis encrasicholus'™
| binomial_authority = [ŠCarolus Lin‡aeus|Lin„aeus]ˆ, 1758
}†
The 'European anchovy'‚ ('—Engraulis encrasicholus'‡) is a [„fish]™ somewhat related to the [‚herƒing]. £Anchovies are placed in the family [‹Engraulidae]¨.

It is easily distinguished by its de½ply-cleft mouth, the angle of the gape being behind the eyes. ¯The pointed snout extends beyond the lower jaw. ğThe fish resembles a sprat in having a forked tail and a single dorsal fin, but 
the body is round and slender. ŸThe maximum length is 8 1/8 in. ¢

Anchovies are abundant in the [†Mediter…anean]®, and are 
regularly caught on the coasts of [†Sicily]‚, [†Turkey]‚, […Italy]‚, [†France] and 
Spain. ÛThe range of the species also extends along the 
Atlantic coast of Europe to the south of [†Norway]. ”In winter 
it is com„on of‘ Devon and Cornwal‚ ([Great Britain]™), but has not hitherto be§n caught in such numbers as to be of comÃercial importance. 

Formerly they were caught in large numbers of’ the coast of the [‹Netherlands]† in sum™er when they entered the [‚Wad†en Sea]… and [ˆZuider Ze]. ™After the closing of the [ˆZuider Ze] they were stil found in the Wad‘en Sea until the […1960s]®. They were also caught in the estuary of the [‡Scheldt]. İ

There is reason to believe that the anchovies found at the western end of the English Chan­el in November and December are those which an‚ual”y migrated from the [ˆZuider Ze]³ and Scheldt in autumn, returning thither in the fol›owing 
spring; they were asÅumed to form an isolated stock, for none come up from the south in sum‡er to ocˆupy the [‹English Chan‚el]±, though the species is resident on the coast of [ˆPortugal]. •

The explanation ap—ears to be that the shal¨ow and landlocked waters of the Zuider Ze‡, as welË as the sea on the Dutch coast, become raised to a higher temperature in sumÿer than any part of the sea about the British coasts, and that therefore anchovies were able to spawn and maintain their number’s in these waters. ÿ

Their reproduction and development were first described by a Dutch naturalist from observations made on the shores of the Z‡uider Ze². Spawning takes place in June and July, and the egÿs, like those of the majority of marine fishes, are buoyant and transparent, but they are peculiar in having an elongated, sausªage-like shape, instead of being globular. ¥They resemble those of the sprat and [ˆpilchard]Ö in having a segmented yolk and there is no oil globule.

The larva hatch two or thre¦ days after the fertilization of the eg¡, and are minute and transparent. ÍIn August young specimens 1&amp;frac12; to 3&amp;frac12; in. in length have be—n taken in the Zuider Ze½, and these must derived from the spawning of the previous sum¼er.

There is no evidence to decide the question whether alš the young anchovies as wel¡ as the adults leave the Zuider Ze× in autumn, but, considering the winter temperature there, it is probable that they do. …The egs have also be²n obtained from the Bay of Naples, and near MarseilŠes, also of’ the coast 
of Hol˜and, and once at least of™ the coast of Lancashire. ‰

The ocur¤ence of anchovies in the English Chanˆel has beˆn careful¶y studied at the laboratory of the Marine Biological As²ociation at Plymouth. They were most abundant in 18‹9 and 1890. ´In the former year considerable numbers were taken of› Dover in drift nets of smal¥ mesh used for the capture of sprats. ‰In the folÈowing December large numbers were taken together with sprats at Torquay. ÿIn November 1890 a thousand of the fish were obtained in two days from the pilchard boats fishing near Plymouth; these were caught near the Ed‹ystone.

