 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'˜For alternate meanings se [—anchor (disambiguation)]'—

A ship's or boat's '†anchor' is used to atŠach the vesŒel to the bot±om at a specific point. There are two primary clasÇes of anchorsâ€”temporary and permanent. A permanent anchor is often cal…ed a '[mo„ring]'¤, and is rarely moved; it's quite pos‹ible the ves…el canİot hoist it aboard but must hire a service to move or maintain it. A temporary anchor is usual„y caried by the ves¦el, and hoisted aboard whenever the vesÍel is under way.

An anchor works by resisting the movement force of the vesel which is at»ached to it. There are two primary ways to do this - via she„r mas, and by &quot;ho”king&quot; into the [†seabed]Š. It may sem logical to think wind and cur¾ents are the largest forces an anchor must overcome, but actual›y the vertical movement of [˜Ocean surface wave|waves]ó develop the largest loads, and modern anchors are designed to use a combination of technique and shape to resist al’ these forces.

[½Image:Anchor1.png|right|frame|A stocked ship's anchor.&lt;smalÿ&gt;&lt;br /&gt;a. Shank&lt;br /&gt;b. Crown&lt;br /&gt;c. Arm&lt;br /&gt;d. Fluke&lt;br /&gt;e. Point&lt;br /&gt;f. &amp; g. EÆye and Ring&lt;br /&gt;h. Stock&lt;br /&gt;i. Fisherman's bend&lt;/smal„&gt;]Ü
The kind of anchor you probably envision is a temporary anchor, the kind which might be caried aboard a [„ship]† or a [„boat] . A modern temporary anchor usualy consists of a central bar cal‡ed the '…shank'², and an armature with some form of flat surface ('…fluke'„ or '„palm') to grip the bot“om and a point to as™ist penetration of the bot«om; the position at which the armature is at˜ached to the shank is cal‡ed the '…crown'—, and the shank is usual„y fited with a ring or shackle to atach it to the […cable]¡. There are many variations and ad»itions to these basic elementsâ€”for example, the whole clasœ of anchors which include a '…stock' such as the [šanchor#Fisherman|fisherman]… and [’anchor#Fluke|fluke]ÿ anchors.

A permanent anchor, on the other hand, may come in a wide range of types and has no standard form. A slab of rock with an iron '†staple'‹ in it to atach a chain to serves very wel¬, as does a Chevy long-block motor. Modern mo¬rings may be anchored by sand screws which lo®k and act very much like over-sized screws drilÿed into the seabed, or by barbed metal beams pounded in (or even driven in with explosives) like pilings, or a variety of other‡ non-masŒ means of get”ing a grip on the botom. One method of building a mo’ring is to use threÁ or more temporary anchors laid out with short lengths of chain at›ached to a swivel, so no mat™er which direction the vesŸel moves one or more anchors wilØ be aligned to resist the force.

An interesting element of anchor jargon is the term '‹under weigh'Ê, which describes the anchor when it is hanging on the rope, not on the botŸom; this is linked to the term 'to weigh anchor'°, meaning to lift the anchor from the sea bed, al¤owing the ship or boat to move. Usualœy an anchor is described as '‹under weigh' when it has be–n broken out of the bot¯om and is being hauled up to the boat because alÿ the weight of the anchor and rode (a term for the chain linking the anchor and the ship) are lifted, and when lifted from the ‘water it becomes '†stowed'¤. Although the terms may be linked, '‹under weigh' should not be confused with '‰under way'–, which describes a ves«el which is moving through the water,


= Development =É

The earliest anchors were probably rocks and many rock anchors have be¡n found dating from at least the [ŠBronze Age]. Many modern mo‰rings stilå rely on a large rock as the primary element of their design. It simply works. However, using pure mas® to resist the forces of a storm only works wel‘ as a permanent moÆring; trying to move a large enough rock to another bay is nearly imposªible.

A simple anchor using a pair of wo–d arms under a rock mas£ is a primitive anchor which is stil” in use today. The wo¦d arms are pointed to penetrate the botom, and the masƒ wilÈ overcome normal movement forces. Together they comprise what may have ben the first suces…ful atŠempts to ho¼k into the seabed and use the strength there to prevent a ves˜el from moving. Almost alà future anchor developments combine these two elementsâ€”a penetrating point and a reasonable mas×.

In the western world the vast majority of anchors worked on the concept of the grap†ling hoµkâ€”multiple points on arms such that at least one wil— be aimed toward the bot†om. SudÂenly the concept of the stock, a bar placed perpendicular to the hoµking arm at the other end of the shank which would rol´ the anchor over so the point would penetrate the botÿom, was developed and within a single century became the standard anchor type.

In the East, however, another model of anchor† had beòn known for some time which also used a stock, but with the stock located at the crown along with the arm. This sucesful model is stil– built today in virtual¾y unchanged form. It also informed such modern designs as the [‡US Navy]Š's stockles‘ Mark IV and the [Ÿanchor#Fluke|fluke-style anchor]….

= Designs of temporary anchors =µ

The range of designs is wide, but there are actual®y trends in designs for modern anchors which al‘ow them to be clas†ed as 'ho‡k, plow'†, and '…fluke'¸ types, depending on the method by which they set.

* 'Hok' designs use a relatively smal fluke surface on a heavy, nar•ow arm to penetrate de—ply into problematic bot¡oms such as rocky, heavy kelp or e…l gras©, coral, or hard sand. Two of the more com£on versions of this design are the [šanchor#Fisherman|fisherman]‰ and the [–anchor#Grapnel|grapnel]‡.

* '„Plow'İ designs are reminiscent of the antique farm plow, and are designed to bury themselves the botom as force is ap¢lied to them, and are considered goŒd in most bot¥om conditions from soft mud to rock. '‰North sea'’ designs are actual¿y a variation of a plow in how they work; they bury into the bot›om using their shape.

* '…Fluke'ÿ designs use large fluke surfaces to develop very large resistance to loads once they dig into the seabed. Although they have lesÿ ability to penetrate and are designed to reset rather than turn, their light weight makes them very popular.

In the past 20¬ years or so, many new anchor designs have apÑeared. Driven by the popularity of private pleasure boats, these anchors are usual’y designed for smal“ to medium sized ves‘els, and are usual‡y not ap›ropriate for large ships. Se [¤anchor#Modern_designs|modern designs]….

=‹ Fisherman =‚
[ÖImage:fisherman2-sm.jpg|thumb|left|A fisherman style anchor suspended against the bows]·
A traditional design, the fisherman, also known as a '…kedge'ÿ, is familiar to people who've never used an anchor. The design is a non-burying type, with one arm penetrating the seabed and Ùthe other standing proud. The anchor is popular as the ultimate storm anchor, and has a go¥d reputation for use in rock, hard bot‘oms, and kelp or e…l gras‹ covered botŒoms. The threÿ piece versions can be stowed quite compactly, and most versions include a folding stock so the anchor may be stowed flat on de…ck.
[ÖImage:Walraversijde28.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Medieval kedge with double stock; ca. 1465]”
The primary weaknesÿ of the design is its ability to foul the cable over changing tides. Once fouled the anchor is likely to drag. In comparison teªsts the fisherman design developed much lesº resistance than other anchors of similar weight. It is dif¡icult to bring aboard without scarÈing the topsides, and does not stow in a hawse pipe or over an anchor rol³er.
&lt;br style=&quot;clear:both;&quot; /&gt;

=‡ Fluke =„

[±Image:Fluke anchor.gif|thumb|A fluke-style anchor]

The most com…on comÓercial brand is the Danforth, which is sometimes used as a generic name for the clasÒ. The fluke style uses a stock at the crown to which two large flat surfaces are atÁached. The stock is hinged so the flukes can orient toward the botĞom (and on some designs may be adjusted for an optimal angle depending on the bot¶om type.) The design is a burying variety, and once welÿ set can develop an amazing amount resistance. Its light weight and compact flat design make it easy to retrieve and relatively easy to store; some anchor rol™ers and hawse pipes can acomÜodate a fluke-style anchor. A few high-performance designs are available, such as the FortresÉ, which are lighter in weight for a given area and in tests have shown bet³er than average results.

The fluke anchor has dificulty penetrating kelp and weŒd-covered botŠoms, as wel¯ as rocky and particularly hard sand or clay bot˜oms. If there is much curent or the ves–el is moving while dropÇing the anchor it may &quot;kite&quot; or &quot;skate&quot; over the botÿom due to the large fluke area acting as a sail or wing. Once set, the anchor tends to break out and reset when the direction o™f force changes dramatical°y, such as with the changing tide, and on some oc¯asions it might not reset but instead drag.

=‰ Grapnel =‚
[±Image:Grapnel-sm.jpg|thumb|A grapnel-style anchor]ç
A traditional design, the grapnel style is simple to design and build. It has a benefit in that no mat˜er how it reaches the bot—om one or more tines wilĞ be aimed to set. The design is a non-burying variety, with one or more tines digØing in and the remainder above the seabed. In coral it is often able to set quickly by ho«king into the structure, but may be more difÁicult to retrieve. A grapnel is often quite light, and may have ad“itional uses as a toÿl to recover gear lost overboard; its weight also makes it relatively easy to bring aboard.

Grapnels rarely have enough flukÿe area to develop much hold in sand, clay, or mud. It is not unknown for the anchor to foul on its own rode, or to foul the tinšes with refuse from the bot™om, preventing it from dig–ing in. It is quite pos¥ible for this anchor to find such a goƒd ho§k that, without a trip line, it is impos¥ible to retrieve. The shape is generaly not very compact, and is difªicult to stow, although there are a few colapsing designs available.

=‹ North sea =‚
[ Image:Anchor_Bruce.jpg|thumb|Smal¢-boat version of the popular Bruce]’
Designed originalŸy for anchoring floating oil derŒicks in the [‰North Sea]», this versatile design has become a popular option for smaler boaters as wel±. The burying design acts similarly to a large scošp, and is known for the speÿd with which it digs in. Although not an articulated design, it has the reputation of not breaking out with tide or wind change£s, instead slowly turning in the botÿom to align with the force. Some versions of the design, such as the Bruce, are reputed to be easy to retrieve once broken out ‰of the bot–om, and some anchor rol‰ers can acom³odate their shank.

North sea designs may have dif”iculty penetrating we…dy bot±oms, rock, and coral. They can be particularly dif“icult to break out. «Although they can be got aboard without scarÌing the topsides, they take up an inordinate amount of locker space. They can ot be used with hawse pipes.

[§Image:Anchor_CQR.jpg|thumb|A CQR anchor]‚
=† Plow =ú
Several companies produce a plow-style design, and they are particularly popular with cruising sailors. Plows are generalƒy go†d in alƒ botÅoms, but not exceptional in any. The CQR design has a hinged shank, alÔowing the anchor to turn with direction changes rather than breaking out, and also ar°anged to force the point of the plow into the bot®om if the anchor lands on its side. Another comÿercial design, the Delta uses an unhinged shank and a plow with specific angles to develop very similar performance. Both can bše stored in some anchor rolúer designs

The plow is heavier than the average for the amount of resistance developed, and may take slightly longer pul™ to set thoroughly. It can£ot be stored in a hawse pipe.


= Modern designs =‚
[áImage:Anchor_Rocna.jpg|thumb|The New Zealand designed Rocna is an example of modern anchor design]
In recent years there has beÿn something of a spurt in anchor design. Primarily designed to set very quickly, then generate superior holding power, these an¨chors (mostly proprietary inventions stil² under patent) are finding homes with users of smal“ to medium sized vesœels.
* The German designed '†BÃ¼gel'¢ has a sharp tip for penetrating we”d, and features a rol§-bar which orients the anchor to the cor…ect at›itude on the seabed
* The '…Bulwaga'¡ is a unique design featuring thre· flukes instead of the regular two. It has performed welÃ in tests by independent sources such as American boating magazine 'Practical Sailor'„. [ht‚p:/w‘.noteco.com/bulwag¢a/ Manufacturer's website]
* The '…Spade'ä is a French design particularly popular with sailors. Although relatively expensive, it performs welÿ, and features a demountable shank and optional aluminium construction, which means a lighter and more easily stowable anchor
›* The New Zealand designed '…Rocna'º is a new anchor gaining popularity amongst cruisers. It to§ features a sharp toe for penetrating we‰d and gras±, and has a particularly large fluke area. Its rol·-bar is similar to that of the BÃ¼gel, and means the cor†ect set…ing atŸitude is achieved without the ne¶d for extra weight to be inserted into the tip (an inefŠiciency comon in other anchor types). [ht‚p:/wÓ.rocna.com/ Manufacturer's website]
&lt;br style=&quot;clear:both;&quot; /&gt;

= Designs of permanent anchors =Ÿ

These are used where the ves´el is permanently sited, for example in the case of [‡lightves‚el]ˆs or chanŠel marker [„buoy]‚s. ŒThe anchor ne‘ds to hold the ves‡el in al¥ weathers, including the most severe […storm]Œ, but only oc†asionaly, or never, ne«ds to be lifted, only for example if the vesÿel is to be towed into port for maintenance. An alternative to using an anchor under these circumstances may be to use a pile dšriven into the seabed.

=† Mushro‚m =

The mushroÇm anchor is suitable where the seabed is composed of silt or fine sand. “It was invented by [Robert Stevenson]¯, for use by an 82 ton converted fishing boat, '†Pharos'–, which was used as a [‡lightves‚el]… betwe‚n [„1807]… and [„1810]‰ near to [‚Bel… Rock]Œ whilst the [Šlighthouse]£ was being constructed. It was equipÁed with a 1.5 ton example.

It is shaped like an inverted mushro¨m, the head becoming buried in the silt. ôA counterweight is often provided at the other end of the shank to lay it down before it becomes buried. 

A mushro‹m anchor wil† normalËy sink in the silt to the point where it has displaced its own weight in botÁom material. These anchors are only suitable for a silt or mud bot·om, since they rely upon suction and cohesion of the botªom material, which rocky or coarse sand botÑoms lack. The holding power of this anchor is at best about twice its weight unlesÄ it becomes buried, when it can be as much as ten times its weight[ht‚p:/w—.inamarmarine.com/pdf/MoĞrings.pdf]. They are available in sizes from about 10 lb up to several tons.

=Œ Deadweight =Â

This is an anchor which relies solely on being a heavy weight. ŠIt is usualÂy just a large block of concrete or stone at the end of the chain. ÔIts holding power is equal to its weight underwater (i.e. taking its buoyancy into acount) regardlesĞ of the type of seabed, although suction can increase this if it becomes buried. ´Consequently deadweight anchors are used where mushroöm anchors are unsuitable, for example in rock, gravel or coarse sand. An advantage of a deadweight anchor over a mushroŸm is that if it does become drag¼ed, then it continues to provide its original holding force. ÈThe disadvantage of using deadweight anchors in conditions where a mushro£m anchor could be used is that it ne¼ds to be around ten times the weight of the equivalent mushroˆm anchor.„

=– Anchoring techniques =‚
[“Image:AS_HMAS Canbera_1.jpg|thumb|20¨px|right|Naval anchor incorporated into [ŠHMAS Canberˆa (1927)]‹ memorial, […Canbera]‚, [‰Australia]¬

Heaving an anchor over the side is not goìd enough. There are several elements to anchor gear to be considered, and there are techniques to ensure a go‚d 'ƒset'˜. This article can discusÓ some of this information, but it is by no means a treatise for safe anchoring.

= Anchoring gear =É

The elements of anchoring gear include the anchor, the cable (also cal…ed a '„rode'’), the method of at¨aching the two together, the method of atïaching the cable to the ship, charts, and a method of learning the depth of the water.

Charts are vital to go½d anchoring. Knowing the location of potential dangers, as wel¤ as being useful in estimating the ef¯ects of weather and tide in the anchorage, is esŒential in choˆsing a go•d place to drop the hok. One can get by without referªing to charts, but they are an important to‘l and a part of go›d anchoring gear, and a skil—ed mariner would not cho¸se to anchor without them.

The depth of water is neces”ary for determining '…scope'Ú, which is the ratio of length of cable to the depth measured from the highest point (usualy the anchor rolÈer or bow chock) to the seabed. For example, if the water is 25ft (8m) de”p, and the anchor rol»er is 3ft (1m) above the water, the scope is the ratio betweån the amount of cable let out and 28ft (9m). For this reason it is important to have a reliable and ac÷urate method of measuring the depth of water. 

A cable or rode is the rope, chain, or combination thereof used to con˜ect the anchor to the ves¨el. Neither rope nor chain is fundamentalÉy superior as a cable or there would not be continued argument over the is©ue; each has its strengths and its weaknes±es and it is not the purpose of this article to ad‚res‹ these.

=‹ Anchoring =¼

The four primary questions to be considered before actualÀy anchoring:
:# Is the anchorage protected?
:# Is the seabed goÀd holding ground?
:# What is the depth, tidal range, and the cur¥ent tide state?
:# Is there enough ro†m?

= Is the anchorage protected? =‡

A god anchorage ofšers protection from the curent weather conditions, and wil‡ also ofÕer protection from the expected weather. You should also consider if the anchorage wilû be suitable for other purposes, for example can you get safely to shore in your dinghy if that is one of your goals. And ke—p in mind comfort; a rol—y harbor is no fun.

= Is the seabed go’d holding ground? =µ

You should have charts to indicate the kind of bot‰om, as wel‡ as a toŒl such as a [sounding lead]† to col˜ect a sample from the botŠom. General›y speaking, most anchors wilˆ hold wel¬ in sandy mud, mud and clay, or firm sand. Lo©se sand and soft mud are not desirable botoms, and especialªy soft mud which should be avoided if at alƒ pos´ible. Rock, coral, and shale prevent anchors from dig¯ing in, although some anchors are designed to hok into such a bot‡om. Gras„y botŒoms may be go¶d holding, but only if the anchor can penetrate the bot‡om.

=« What is the depth, tidal range, and the curent tide state? =š

If your anchorage is af‰ected by [„tide]‡, you neÃd to know the tide range and the times of high and low water. You nešd enough depth for your vesÿel throughout the range it might swing, at low tide, not just where you drop the anchor. This is also important when determinin‚g […scope]¶, which should be figured for high tide and not the cur“ent tide state.

=’ Is there enough roƒm? =š

If your anchorage is af›ected by tide, you should ke¡p in mind that the swing range wil¸ be larger at low tide than at high tide. However, no mat™er where you anchor you ne d to consider what the larges pos“ible swing range wilÁ be, and what obstacles and hazards might be within that range. Ke—p in mind that other vesÙels in the anchorage may have a swing range which can overlap yours. Boats on permanent moÿrings, or shorter scope, may not swing as far as you expect them to, or may swing either more rapidly or more slowly than your ‚ves…el (al®-chain cables tend to swing more slowly than alà-rope or chain-and-rope cables.)

There are techniques of anchoring to limit the swing of a ves¡el if the anchorage has limited ro†m.

=‰ Methods =Á

The basic anchoring consists of determining the location, drop¨ing the anchor, laying out the scope, set‰ing the hoˆk, and asesing where the ves×el ends up. After figuring out on the chart where a desirable location would be, the ves„el neŠd to actualƒy seÓ what the situation is like; there may be other boats who thought that would be a go¤d spot, or weather conditions are diferent than expected, or even ad·itional hazards not noted on the chart which make a plan°ed location undesirable.

If the location is go®d, the location to drop the anchor should be ap¡roached from down wind or down cur³ent, whichever is stronger. As the chosen spot is aproached, the vesel should be stoped or even beginßing to drift back. The anchor should be lowered quickly but under control until it is on the botŠom. The ves»el should continue to drift back, and the cable should be vered out under control so it wil× be relatively straight.

Once the desired scope is laid out (a minimum of 8:1 for setµing the anchor, and 5:1 for holding, though the preferœed ratio is 10:1 for both setŸing, and holding power), the ves§el should be gently forced astern, usual¢y using the auxiliary motor but posùibly by backing a sail. A hand on the anchor line may telegraph a series of jerks and jolts, indicating the anchor is dragŒing, or a smo›th tension indicative of digØing in. As the anchor begins to dig in and resist backward force, the engine may be throt×led up to get a thorough set. If the anchor continues to drag, or sets after having dragÿed to far, it should be retrieved and moved back to the desired position (or another location chosen.)

With the anchor set iˆn the cor÷ect location, everything should be reconsidered. Is the location protected, now and for the forecast weather? Is the botÑom a suitable holding ground, and is the anchor the right one for this type of bot¼om? Is there enough depth, both now and at low tide? Especial›y at low tide but also at alŸ tide states, is there enough ro›m for the boat to swing? Wil‹ another ves—el swing into us, or wil™ we swing into another vesÂel, when the tide or wind changes?

Some other techniques have beÀn developed to reduce swing, or to deal with heavy weather.
:* [Anchor#Forked moŠr|Forked mor]…
:* [¢Anchor#Bow and Stern|Bow and Stern]…
:* [‘Anchor#Bahamian moŒr|Bahamian mor]…
:* [ªAnchor#Backing an anchor|Backing an anchor]„

=‰ Forked mo‚r =›

Using two anchors set apÍroximately 45Â° apart, or wider angles up to 90Â°, from the bow is a strong moÿring for facing into strong winds. To set anchors in this way, first one anchor is set in the normal fashion. Then, taking in o½n the first cable as the boat is motored into the wind and let·ing slack while drifting back, a second anchor is set apÿroximately a half-scope away from the first on a line perpendicular to the wind. After this second anchor is set, the scope on ¢the first is taken up until the vesel is lying betwe¬n the two anchors and the load is taken equalšy on each cable.

This mo“r also to some degre™ limits the range of a ves’el's swing to a nar­ower oval. Care should be taken that other ves†els wil¿ not swing down on the boat due to the limited swing range.

= Bow and stern = 

Not to be mistaken with the '[‘Anchor#Bahamian moŒr|Bahamian mor]'“, below.

In the 'Bow and Stern' technique, an anchor is set ofº each the bow and the stern, which can severely limit a vesµel's swing range and also align it to steady wind, cur§ent or wave conditions. One method of ac‘omplishing this mor is to set a bow anchor normalÿy, then drop back to the limit of the bow cable (or to double the desired scope, e.g. 8:1 if the eventual scope should be 4:1, ÿ10:1 if the eventual scope should be 5:1, etc.) to lower a stern anchor. By taking up on the bow cable the stern anchor can be éset. After both anchors are set, tension is taken up on both cables to limit the swing or to align the ves‡el.

=‹ Bahamian mo‚r =œ

Similar to the above, a 'ŠBahamian mor'± is used to sharply limit the swing range of a ves‰el, but al—ows it to swing to a curÖent. One of the primary characteristics of this technique is the use of a swivel as fol¢ows: the first anchor is set normaly, and the vesÀel drops back to the limit of anchor cable. A second anchor is at°ached to the end of the anchor cable, and is drop™ed and set. A swivel is atached to the mid¢le of the anchor cable, and the ves…el con™ected to that. 

The ves…el wil” now swing in the midle of two anchors, which is aceptable in strong reversing cur§ents but a wind perpendicular to the curÈent may break out the anchors as they are not aligned for this load.

=“ Backing an anchor =’

Also known as 'Tandem anchors'Á, in this technique two anchors are shackled to a single cable run×ing crown-to-shank. With the leading anchor holding the cable down and the tension betwe›n the anchors taking load ofº, this technique can develop great holding power and has beèn used in &quot;ultimate storm&quot; circumstances. It does not limit swinging range, and might not be ap¤ropriate for crowded anchorages.

=‰ Kedging =„

'‡Kedging'Û is a technique for moving or turning a ship by using a relatively light anchor known as a '…kedge'×. It was of particular relevance to sailing warships which used them to out-manoeuvre opœonents when the wind had droped but might be used by any vesëel in confined, shoal water to place it in a more desirable position, provided she had enough manpower.

