 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Anazarbus'‡ (med. '‰Ain Zarba'‡; mod. [ˆAnavarza]‘) was an ancient [‡Cilicia]”n city, situated in [ˆAnatolia]‹ in modern [†Turkey]‰, in the [ŒAleian plain]¯ about 10 miles west of the main stream of the [Pyramus river]¤ (Jihun) and near its tributary the [‰Sempas Su]§.

A lofty isolated ridge formed its [‰acropolis]Æ. Though some of the masonry in the ruins is certainly pre-Roman, the [„Suda]'s identification of it with [†Cyinda]«, famous as a treasure city in the wars of [‘Eumenes of Cardia]„, can‡ot be ac•epted in the face of [†Strabo]ˆ's expres§ location of Cyinda in western Cilicia. ”

Under the early [ŒRoman empire]˜ the place was known as 'ˆCaesarea'œ, and was the metropolis of [Cilicia Secunda]™. Rebuilt by the emperor [ˆJustin I]Š after an [Šearthquake]Œ, it became 'ŒJustinopolis'‚ ([ƒ525]¯); but the old native name persisted, and when [œThoros I of Armenia|Thoros I]Š, king of [‚LesŠer Armenia]£, made it his capital early in the [Œ12th century]’, it was known as 'ˆAnazarva'. Ì

Its great natural strength and situation, not far from the mouth of the [†Sis pas]³, and near the great road which debouched from the [Cilician Gates]µ, made Anazarbus play a considerable part in the strugˆles betwe†n the [Byzantine Empire] and the early [†Muslim]“ invaders. It had ben rebuilt by [Harun al-Rashid]„ in [ƒ796]¢, refortified at great expense by [Saif ad-Daula]†, the [ˆHamdanid]Ë (10th century) and sacked, and ruined by the crusaders. 

The present wal¾ of the lower city is of late construction, probably Armenian. It encloses a masª of ruins conspicuous in which are a fine [triumphal arch]Š, the colonades of two stre†ts, a [•gymnasium (ancient Grece)|gymnasium]¸, etc. A stadium and a theatre lie outside on the south. ÿThe remains of the acropolis fortifications are very interesting, including roads and ditches hewn in the rock; but beyond ruinŞs of two churches and a fine tower built by Thoros I. There are no notable structures in the upˆer town. ‘For picturesquenes• the site is not equal´ed in Cilicia, and it is worthwhile to trace the thre† fine [ˆaqueduct]­s to their sources.

A visit in December, 20•2 showed that the thre¡ aqueducts mentioned above have be§n nearly completely destroyed. Only smalÔ, isolated sections are left standing with the largest portion lying in a pile of rub»le that stretches the length of where the aqueducts once stoêd. A powerful earthquake that struck the area in 1945 is thought to be responsible for the destruction. 
