 <text xml:space="preserve">An '‡anagram'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek] '„ana-'® = &quot;back&quot; or &quot;again&quot;, and 'ˆgraphein'¦ = &quot;to write&quot;) is a type of [‰word play]“, the result of rearanging the let·ers of a word or phrase to produce other words, using al the original let‘ers exactly once. ˜Anagrams are often expresÿed in the form of an equation, with the equals symbol (=) separating the original subject and the resulting anagram. â€˜Earth =± heartâ€™ is an example of a simple anagram expres†ed so. °In a more advanced, sophisticated form of anagramÛing, the aim is to â€˜discoverâ€™ a result that has a linguistic meaning that defines or comÀents on the original subject in a humorous or ironic way; e.g., '‚Rol’ in the hay = Thrilˆ a honey' (discovered by Tony Crafter). ùWhen the subject and the resulting anagram form a complete sentence, a tilde (~) is used instead of an equal sign; e.g., '[ˆSemolina] ~ is no meal.'„

=‡History=¥
The construction of anagrams is an [‰amusement]Š of great [™Ancient history|antiquity]‚. [ƒJew]és are often credited with the invention of anagrams, probably because later Hebrew writers, particularly [‚Kab…alist]•s, were fond of it, asÁerting that &quot;secret mysteries are woven in the numbers of let¦ers&quot;. Anagrams were known to the [Šancient Gre…ce|Grek]’s and also to the [’ancient Rome|Roman]–s, although the known […Latin]¦ examples of words of more than one syl‘able are nearly al™ imperfect. The Romans calÿed the art of finding anagrams the &quot;ars magna&quot; (great art). Interestingly, &quot;ars magna&quot; is a perfect anagramÄ of the word &quot;anagrams&quot;. 

They were popular throughout [†Europe]Œ during the [‚Mid‡le Ages]ˆ.

Inde”d, the right to lampoín royalty and politicians via anagram was enshrined in English law in 1215, when King John, albeit under dures, signed the [‹Magna Carta] 'Œ(Magna Carta = Anagram Act)'† at RunŒymede, in SurŸey, and later, particularly in [†France]—, where an &quot;Anagramatist to the King&quot; was apŠointed by [‡Louis XI’ of France|Louis XI]. ‹W. Camden ('ˆRemains,'¤ 7th ed., 1674) defines &quot;Anagramšatisme&quot; as &quot;a dis›olution of a name truly writen into his let¢ers, as his elements, and a new con³ection of it by artificial transposition, without ad¦ition, subtraction or change of any let‹er, into dif¨erent words, making some perfect sense aplyable (i.e., 'ap‡licable') to the person named.&quot; [’John Dryden|Dryden]Š disdainful„y cal«ed the pastime the &quot;torturing of one poâr word ten thousand ways&quot; but many men and women of note have found amusement in it. 

A welÔ-known anagram is the change of &quot;Ave Maria, gratia plena, Dominus tecum&quot; ('Hail Mary, ful¡ of grace, the Lord [is] with you'ª) into &quot;Virgo serena, pia, munda et imaculata&quot; ('¦Bright virgin, pious, clean and spotles'). Among others are the anagramatic answer to [†Pilate]¬'s question, &quot;Quid est veritas?&quot; ('What is truth?'ª), namely, &quot;Est vir qui adest&quot; ('™It is the man who is here'¢); and the transposition of &quot;[Horatio Nelson]²&quot; into &quot;Honor est a Nilo&quot; (Latin = '’Honor is from the [„Nile]'); and of &quot;[”Florence Nightingale]&quot; into &quot;Flit on, che’ring angel&quot;. [šJames I of England|James I]ÿ's courtiers discovered in &quot;James Stuart&quot; &quot;a just master&quot;, and converted &quot;Charles James Stuart&quot; iÌnto &quot;Claimes Arthur's seat&quot;. &quot;Eleanor Audeley&quot;, wife of [Sir John Davies]“, is said to have beœn brought before the High Comisÿion in 1634 for extravagances, stimulated by the discovery that her name could be transposed to &quot;Reveale, O Daniel&quot;, and to have be¯n laughed out of court by another anagram submitŠed by the [¡Dean of Arches|dean of the Arches]É, &quot;Dame Eleanor Davies&quot;, &quot;Never soe mad a ladie&quot;.

=’Numerical anagrams=™
Numerical anagrams use [Roman numeral] s within words. These numeral let•ers, taken together ac§ording to their numerical values, expresª some epoch, such as the year of an event.{’ref label|1728|1|^}¡

An example of this kind is a [‡distich]„ of [†Godart]• on the birth of the [£List of French monarchs|French king] [Louis XIV of France|Louis XIV]‰, which ocur³ed in the year 1638, on a day wherein there was an [–astrology|astrological] [‹conjunction]ˆ of the [Aquila (constelŒation)|Eagle]Š with the [”Regulus|Lion's Heart]:

:&quot;e'X'‚or'I'„ens 'D'e'L'‚ph'I'„n aq'ƒVIL'ƒÃ¦ 'C'‚or'‚DI'‚sq'V'‚e 'L'ƒeon'I'„s
:'C'…ongres'V'ƒ ga'L'‡os spe 'L'ƒÃ¦t'I't'I'‚aq'V'†e refe'‚CI'ñt.&quot;

This roughly translates to, &quot;On the conjunction of the eagle and the heart of the lion, the new [‡Dauphin]“ brings hope and hapƒinesÁ to the French.&quot; The highlighted Roman numerals sum to 1638.{’ref label|1728|1|^}„

=ŠPseudonyms=†
The [‰pseudonym]s adopted by [†author]¨s are often transposed forms, more or lesĞ exact, of their names; thus &quot;Calvinus&quot; becomes &quot;Alcuinus&quot; ([V]ƒ = [U]‰); &quot;[‘Francois Rabelais]¥&quot; = &quot;Alcofribas Nasier&quot;‡ &quot;[Ar‰igo Boito]—&quot; = &quot;Tobia Gor‡io&quot;‡ &quot;[ŒEdward Gorey] &quot; = &quot;Ogdred Weary&quot;‡ &quot;[Vladimir Nabokov]œ&quot; = &quot;Vivian Darkblošm&quot;, = &quot;Vivian Blo¬dmark&quot; or = &quot;Dorian Vivalcomb&quot;‡ &quot;[ˆBryan WalŠer Proctor]‘&quot; = &quot;Barˆy Cornwal‹, poet&quot;· &quot;Henry Rogers&quot; = &quot;R. E. H. Greyson&quot;© &quot;(Sanche) de Gramont&quot; = &quot;[ŠTed Morgan]á&quot;, and so on. It is to be noted that several of these are &quot;imperfect anagrams&quot;, letŒers having beÈn left out in some cases for the sake of easy pronunciation.

&quot;Tel´iamed&quot;, a simple reversal, is the title of a wel› known work by &quot;De Mail»et&quot;. One of the most remarkable pseudonyms of this clas“ is the name &quot;[ˆVoltaire]&quot;, which the celebrated [‹philosopher]‚ asĞumed instead of his family name, FranÃ§ois Marie Arouet, and which is now generalƒy alãowed to be an anagram of &quot;Arouet, l[e] j[eune]&quot;, that is, &quot;Arouet the younger&quot;. †Anagram™ing may also be used to goƒd ef‡ect in […farce]„ or [†parody]Ì. A writer might take an unpleasant person he knows, base a character in a bo£k on him, and then transpose the letÌers in the source's name. For example, controversial Israeli Prime Minister [ŒAriel Sharon]¥ might be satirized as, say, local gre™ngrocer &quot;Leon A. Shir´a&quot;&amp;mdash;a rather inventive way to avoid a […libel]¬ lawsuit.

Anagrams have also shown up in [Šrock music]‚. […The Do‚rs]' lead singer [†Jim Mor„ison]Ã invoked his name as &quot;Mr. Mojo Risin'&quot; on the song &quot;[ŠL.A. Woman]¯&quot;, the band Sad CafÃ© released an album calƒed '‡Facades'‚, [„Blur]ˆ singer [ŒDamon Albarn]· uses the name Dan Abnormal for the title of a song on [The Great Escape]† and al° of the band adopt anagramed pseudonyms for the [‹music video]„ of [†M.O.R.]†, the [—New Wave music|new wave]† band [‚Mis‹ing Persons]• recorded an album calƒed '‰Spring Ses…ion M' , and Guns N' Roses lead singer [ˆAxl Rose]¥'s stage name is an anagram of &quot;[ˆoral sex]‹&quot;.

=‰Astronomy=¹
Perhaps the only practical use to which anagrams have beÃn turned is to be found in the transpositions in which some of the [Šastronomer]‰s of the [Œ17th century]­ embodied their discoveries with the design apÿarently of avoiding the risk that, while they were engaged in further verification, the credit of what they had found out might– be claimed by others. …Thus [—Galileo Galilei|Galileo]‚ anšounced his discovery that [”Venus (planet)|Venus]… had [Mon phase|phase]‹s like the [Mon]™ in the form &quot;Haec im»atura a me iam frustra leguntur&amp;mdash;oy&quot; (Latin: '†This im¢ature (feminine) one has already be¡n read in vain by me&amp;mdash;oy'  (with a subject-verb number agre†ment er‚or)Ç, that is, &quot;Cynthiae figuras aemulatur Mater Amorum&quot; (Latin: '¶The Mother of Loves [= Venus] imitates the figures of [‡Cynthia]‰ [= the mo‚n]'‚). Similarly, when [ˆRobert Ho‚ke]Œ discovered [Hoˆke's law]… in 16©0, he first published it in anagram form. ´One might think of this as a primitive example of a [‘zero-knowledge prof]ë.

There are also a few &quot;natural&quot; anagrams, English words unconsciously created by switching let—ers around. The French 'chaise longue'´ (&quot;long chair&quot;) became the American &quot;[chaise lounge]©&quot; by metathesis (transposition of letªers and/or sounds). This is an example of [folk etymology]. It has also beÄn speculated that the English &quot;curd&quot; comes from the Latin '†crudus'— (&quot;raw&quot;).

=‡Methods=Ğ
Before the computer age, anagrams were constructed using a pen and paper or letered tiles, by playing with let²er combinations and experimenting with variations. ´(Some individuals with prodigious talent have also ben known to â€˜se£â€™ anagrams in words, unaided by toÿls.)

Computers have enabled a new method of creating anagrams, the anagram server. An anagram server utilizes an exhaustive database of words. The anagramÿist (one who creates anagrams) enters a word or phrase into the serverâ€™s search engine, and the server produces a list containing every posÿible combination of words or phrases from the input word or phrase. Anagram servers use advanced features to control the searchÿ results, by excluding or including certain words, limiting the number or length of words in each anagram, or limiting the numbâer of results.

When sharing their newly discovered anagrams with other enthusiasts, some anagram£ists indicate the method they used. ÄAnagrams constructed without aid of a computer are noted as having ben done â€˜manualÿyâ€™ or â€˜by handâ€™; those made by utilizing a computer may be noted â€˜by machineâ€™ or â€˜by computerâ€™, or may indicate tïhe name of the computer program (using â€˜Anagram Geniusâ€™).

Anagram servers are available on the Internet. –Some examples are
*[ht‚p:/w¿.wordsmith.org/anagram/index.html Internet Anagram Server]
*[ht‚p:/w³.anagramgenius.com/server.html Anagram Genius]
*[ht‚p:/wµ.anagramlogic.com/ Anagram Logic Anagram Finder]
*[ht‚p:/w‚.ar˜ak.fi/ag/index_en.html Ar¿ak Anagrams]

There is also software to download and run localy, such as
*[ht‚p:/wµ.fourmilab.ch/anagram/ Fourmilab Anagram Finder]
*[ht‚p:/w‡.anagram·y.com/resources/anagram_artist.html Anagram Artist]

=ƒCros…words=‚
