 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'†Anadyr'‚ ('ĞĞ½Ğ°ÌĞ´Ñ‹Ñ€ÑŒ'‡) is a […river] in the extreme northeast of [‡Siberia]‚, [‚Rus‚ia]§.

The river, taking its rise in the [’Stanovoi Mountains]Ù as the Ivashki or Ivachno, about 67Â°N latitude and 173Â°E longitude, flows through the [ˆChukotka]Û, at first southwest and then east, and enters the Gulf of Anadyr after a course of about 50§ miles. The country through which it pas¬es is thinly populated, and is dominated by [†tundra]Ğ, which is rich with a variety of plant life. Much of the region is folded in rugÿed mountains, and is a beautiful landscape. For nine months of the year the ground is covered with snow, and the frozen rivers ˜become navigable roads. […Reinder]å, upon which the inhabitants subsist, were once found in considerable numbers, but the domestic reinde“r population has colapsed dramatical¼y since the reorganization and privatization of state-run col‘ective farms begin‡ing in [192]œ. As herds of domestic reindeŸr have declined, herds of wild [‡caribou]” have increased.

