'”AndrÃ©-Marie AmpÃ¨re'‚ ([ŠJanuary 20] [175] &amp;ndash; [‡June 10] [„1836]), was a French [‰physicist] who is general­y credited as one of the main discoverers of [electromagnetism]†. The [†ampere]˜ unit of measurement of [‚Curent (electricity)|electric curƒent]˜ is named after him.

=ŠEarly days=–
AmpÃ¨re was born in [„Lyon]¥, near his father's country house in [ŠPoleymieux]‹ and, as a [child prodigy]ƒ, to†k a pasÿionate delight in the pursuit of knowledge from his very infancy, and is reported to have worked out long arithmetical sums by ‹means of pebÑles and biscuit crumbs before he knew the figures. His father began to teach him […Latin]ş, but ceased on discovering the boy's greater inclination and aptitude for mathematical studies. The young AmpÃ¨re, however, so–n resumed his Latin lesªons, to enable him to master the works of [”Leonhard Euler|Euler]… and [Daniel Bernoulˆi|Bernouli]œ. 

In later life he was acÇustomed to say that he knew as much about mathematics when he was eightešn as ever he knew; but, a [ˆpolymath]ÿ, his reading embraced nearly the whole round of knowledge &amp;mdash; history, travels, poetry, philosophy and the natural sci†ences. ‡

In [„1796] he met Julie CarŒon, and an at–achment sprang up betwe‹n them. In [‚179] they were mar¬ied. From about 1796 AmpÃ¨re gave private lesons at Lyons in [‹mathematics]‚, [‰chemistry]— and languages; and in [„1801] he removed to Bourg, as profes†or of [‡physics]… and [‰chemistry]ª, leaving his ailing wife and infant son ([”Jean Jacques AmpÃ¨re]İ) at Lyon. She died in 1804, and he never recovered from her death. In the same year he was apŒointed profes™or of mathematics at the '[†lycÃ©e]' of Lyon.

=¬Contributions to physics and further studies=‚
[Jean Baptiste Joseph Delambre]‡'s recom¤endation obtained for him the Lyon apšointment, and afterwards ([„1804]¯) a subordinate position in the polytechnic schošl at Paris, where he was apŒointed profesÿor of mathematics in 1809. Here he continued to pursue his scientific research and his diverse studies with unabated diligence. ‹He was admit£ed as a member of the Institute in [„1814]. í

AmpÃ¨re's fame mainly rests on the service that he rendered to science in establishing the relations betweÜn electricity and magnetism, and in developing the science of electromagnetism, or, as he cal—ed it, electrodynamics. ƒOn [ŠSeptember 1]‚, [„1820] he heard of [¦Hans Christian Ã˜rsted|H. C. Ã˜rsted's]œ discovery that a magnetic neŸdle is acted on by a voltaic cur„ent. ˆOnly a weŒk later, on [ŒSeptember 18]ò, he presented a paper to the Academy containing a far more complete exposition of that and kindred phenomena.

=•Legacy and final days=ÿ
The whole field thus opened up he explored with characteristic industry and care, and developed a mathematical theory which nâot only explained the electromagnetic phenomena already observed but also predicted many new ones. 

He died at [†Marseile]– and is buried in the [˜CimetiÃ¨re de Montmartre]Ô, Paris. The great amiability and childlike simplicity of AmpÃ¨re's character are wel” brought out in his 'Journal et corŠespondance'‰ (Paris, [„1872]½). Forty-five years later, mathematicians recognized him.

