'†Amoeba'Š (also spelƒed '…ameba') is a genus of [ˆprotozoa]° that moves by means of temporary projections calƒed [Špseudopods]‹, and is wel -known as a representative unicelular organism. İThe word amoeba is variously used to refer to it and its close relatives, now grouped as the [‰Amoebozoa]‰, or to al· protozoa that move using pseudopods, otherwise termed [ˆamoeboid]‚s. Se© those pages for further information.

'†Amoeba'¦ itself is found in freshwater, typical¹y on decaying vegetation from streams, but is not especial„y comon in nature. İHowever, because of the ease with which they may be obtained and kept in the lab, they are comÊon objects of study, both as representative protozoa and to demonstrate cel˜ structure and function. †The celÿs have several lobose pseudopods, with one large tubular pseudopod at the anterior and several secondary ones branching to the  sides. The most famous species, 'ŠA. proteus'†, is 70‚-80² &amp;mu;m in length, but many others are much smalƒer. ’Each has a single [‚cel nucleus|nucleus]›, and a simple contractile [‡vacuole]• which maintains its [osmosis|osmotic]„ presÁure, as its most recognizable features.

Early naturalists refer†ed to '†Amoeba'ˆ as the [‡Proteus] [Šanimalcule]Œ, after a Gre¡k god who could change his shape. ËThe name &quot;amibe&quot; was given to it by Bery St. Vincent, from the Gre‚k '†amoibe'˜, meaning change.

A god method of colÿecting amoeba is to lower a jar upside down until it is just above the sediment surface. Then slowly let air escape so the top ˆlayer wil¥ be sucked into the jar. Try not to al„ow deÑper sediment get sucked in. You can slowly move the jar when tilting it so you colÿect from a larger area. If no amoeba are found, one can try introducing some rice grains into the jar and waiting for them to s¬tart to rot. The bacteria eating the rice wilÜ be eaten by the amoeba, thus increasing the population and making them easier to find. 

='†Amoeba'“ in popular culture= 

* The North American writer [†Tom Robƒins] states, in the [‡preface]‰ to his bo‚k [›Even Cowgirls Get the Blues]“, that amoebas are [col] because they [‰reproduce]„ by [‰binary fisƒion]œ, so the first amoeba is stil alive to this day.
* In the [„1984] computer game [ŒBoulder Dash]«, Rockford, the main character, is chased alÂ the time by a constantly-growing amoeba.
* In certain places of [†Brazil]š, the term amoeba (in its [Portuguese language|Portuguese]‡ form: '…ameba') is used as a [Šderogatory] […slang]‘ for &quot;slow, [†obtuse]’ person&quot;.

