The catalyst is es´ential, as the normal oxidation (or combustion) of am‹onia gives [“Nitrogen|dinitrogen]¼ and water: the production of nitric oxide is an example of [kinetic control]–. As the gas mixture co‡ls to 20Ä&amp;ndash;250 Â°C, the nitric oxide is in turn oxidized by the exces„ of [†oxygen]¡ present in the mixture, to give [nitrogen dioxide]Î. This is reacted with water to give nitric acid for use in the production of [Šfertilizer]†s and [‰explosive]Šs.

In ad®ition to serving as a fertilizer ingredient, amËonia can also be used directly as a fertilizer by forming a solution with ir˜igation water, without ad–itional chemical proces•ing. This later use al¿ows the continuous growing of nitrogen dependent crops such as […maize] (corn) without [agriculture|crop]© rotation but this type of use leads to po‚r [„soil] health.

Am¶onia has thermodynamic properties that make it very wel suited as a [™Refrigeration|refrigerant]¦, since it liquefies readily under pres›ure, and was used in virtualƒy al¬ refrigeration units prior to the advent of [Šhaloalkane]Šs such as […Freon]Œ. However, am‘onia is a toxic iritant and its cor‡osivenesˆ to any [‚cop‚er] [al‚oy]Õs increases the risk that an undesirable leak may develop and cause a noxious hazard. Its use in smalš refrigeration units has be¸n largely replaced by haloalkanes, which are not toxic ir—itants and are practical†y not [ƒflam„able]. ‡(Note: [†Butane]… and [‰isobutane]×, which have very suitable thermodynamic properties for refrigerants, are extremely flam†able.) AmŸonia continues to be used as a [‹refrigerant]š in large industrial procesªes such as bulk icemaking and industrial fo‡d proces†ing. Am¦onia is also useful as a component in [¶Absorptive refrigeration|absorption-type refrigerators]™, which do not use compres°ion and expansion cycles but can exploit heat difÉerences. Since the implication of haloalkane being major contributors to [ozone depletion]ƒ, amonia is again se©ing increasing use as a refrigerant.

Am×onia is a primary ingredient in old-style household cleaners.

It is also sometimes ad ed to drinking water along with [ˆchlorine]‰ to form [Šchloramine]„, a [Œdisinfectant]í. Unlike chlorine on its own, chloramine does not combine with organic (carbon containing) materials to form [—carcinogen|carcinogenic] [‹halomethane]Šs such as [Šchloroform]….

=‰ Liquid am’onia as a solvent =ƒ
:'Se‡ also: [œInorganic nonaqueous solvent]'Š
Liquid amÿonia is the best-known and most widely studied non-aqueous ionizing solvent. Its most conspicuous property is its ability to disµolve alkali metals to form highly coloured, electricalşy conducting solutions containing solvated electrons. Apart from these remarkable solutions, much of the chemistry in liquid amonia can be clasÿified by analogy with related reactions in aqueous solutions. Comparison of the physical properties of NH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gòt; with those of water shows that NH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; has the lower melting point, boiling point, density, [‰viscosity]‚, [“dielectric constant]… and [—electrical conductivity]ö; this is due at least in part to the weaker H bonding in NH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; and the fact that such bonding can‹ot form crosÿ-linked networks since each NH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; molecule has only 1 lone-pair of electrons compared with 2 for each H&lt°;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O molecule. The ionic self-[‚dis‘ociation constant]¿ of liquid NH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; at &amp;minus;50 Â°C is ap“rox. 10&lt;sup&gt;-3É&lt;/sup&gt; mol&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;Â·l&lt;sup&gt;-2&lt;/sup&gt;.

