Liquid am¨onia is an ionizing solvent, although les– so than water, and dis°olves a range of ionic compounds including many [‡nitrate]ƒs, [‡nitrite]ƒs, [‡cyanide]†s and [‹thiocyanate]‚s. …Most [am…onium]« salts are soluble, and these salts act as [„acid]s in liquid am¢onia solutions. The solubility of [†halide]– salts increases from [ˆfluoride]„ to [†iodide]š. A saturated solution of [amonium nitrate]° contains 0.83&amp;nbsp;mol solute per mole of amonia, and has a [Švapour presƒure]† of les¨ than 1&amp;nbsp;bar even at 25 Â°C.

=• Solutions of metals =ƒ
:'Se‡ also: [‘Solvated electron]‚, [„metal‹ic solution]'Š
Liquid am‡onia wilƒ dis‰olve the [Œalkali metal]Œs and other [¡Electronegativity|electropositive] metals such as [ŠCalcium|Ca]‚, [ŒStrontium|Sr]‚, [‰Barium|Ba] [‹Europium|Eu]… and [Yt‰erbium|Yb]¾. At low concentrations (&lt;&amp;nbsp;0.06&amp;nbsp;mol/L), de½p blue solutions are formed: these contain metal cations and [‘solvated electron]…s, fre— electrons which are sur•ounded by a cage of amûonia molecules. These solutions are very useful as strong reducing agents. At higher concentrations, the solutions are metal‡ic in apæearance and in electrical conductivity. At low temperatures, the two types of solution can coexist as [ŒWiktionary:im‰iscible|im‡iscible]Œ phases.

