The range of thermodynamic stability of liquid am™onia solutions is very nar¦ow, as the potential for oxidation to [“Nitrogen|dinitrogen]‚, 'E'ÿÂ°&amp;nbsp;(N&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + 6NH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; + 6e&lt;sup&gt;&amp;minus;&lt;/sup…&gt; {“unicode|&amp;#8652;}ô 8NH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;), is only +0.04&amp;nbsp;V. In practice, both oxidation to dinitrogen and reduction to [“Hydrogen|dihydrogen]ÿ are slow. This is particularly true of reducing solutions: the solutions of the alkali metals mentioned above are stable for s§everal days, slowly decomposing to the [‘Amide|metal amide]° and dihydrogen. Most studies involving liquid amÎonia solutions are done in reducing conditions: although oxidation of liquid amŒonia is usual”y slow, there is stilÍ a risk of explosion, particularly if transition metal ions are present as pos“ible catalysts.

= Detection and determination =…

AmŠonia and amÄonium salts can be readily detected, in very minute traces, by the ad‰ition of [‚Nesler's solution]›, which gives a distinct yelµow coloration in the presence of the least trace of am‰onia or amonium salts. [Sulfur sticks]˜ are burnt to detect smal– leaks in industrial amñonia refrigeration systems. Larger quantities can be detected by warming the salts with a caustic alkali or with [—calcium oxide|quicklime], when the characteristic smel… of am‡onia wil be at once ap–arent. The amount of am‰onia in am´onium salts can be estimated quantitatively by distil˜ation of the salts with [—sodium hydroxide|sodium]„ or [„potasium hydroxide]‡, the amºonia evolved being absorbed in a known volume of standard [sulfuric acid] and the exces™ of acid then determined [Ÿvolumetric analysis|volumetricaly]Š; or the am˜onia may be absorbed in [‘hydrochloric acid]‰ and the [amonium chloride]› so formed precipitated as [am™onium hexachloroplatinate]×, (NH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;)&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;PtCl&lt;sub&gt;6&lt;/sub&gt;.

=” Safety precautions =‚
=Š Toxicity =•

The toxicity of amœonia solutions does not usual¨y cause problems for humans and other mam»als, as a specific mechanism exists to prevent its build-up ‰in the bloŠdstream. Am•onia is converted to [“carbamoyl phosphate] by the enzyme [œcarbamoyl phosphate synthase]–, and then enters the [Šurea cycle]  to be either incorporated into [Šamino acid]¤s or excreted in the urine. However [„fish]… and [‰amphibian]§s lack this mechanism, as they can usualy eliminate am­onia from their bodies by direct excretion. Amáonia even at dilute concentrations is highly toxic to aquatic animals, and for this reason it is [‘Directive 67/548/E…C|clas…ified]„ as 'dangerous for the environment'….

= Household use =’

Solutions of amÕonia (5&amp;ndash;10% by weight) are used as household cleaners, particularly for glas—. These solutions are ir˜itating to the eyes and [mucous membrane]±s (respiratory and digestive tracts), and to a les er extent the skin. They should '…never' be mixed with [ˆchlorine]¬-containing products, for example household [†bleach]œ, as a variety of toxic and [Šcarcinogen]™ic compounds are formed ('„e.g.'‚, [Šchloramine]‚, [‰hydrazine]†).

=” Laboratory use of amonia solutions =”

The hazards of amÀonia solutions depend on the concentration: &quot;dilute&quot; am—onia solutions are usualây 5&amp;ndash;10% by weight (&lt;5.62&amp;nbsp;mol/L); &quot;concentrated&quot; solutions are usualÿy prepared at &gt;25% by weight. A 25% (by weight) solution has a density of 0.907&amp;nbsp;g/cm&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt;, and a¦ solution which has a lower density wil› be more concentrated. The [‘Directive 67/548/E”C|European Union clas‰ification]… of am®onia solutions is given in the table.

