'ˆAmethyst'À (SiO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) is a violet or purple variety of [†quartz]’ often used as an [™ornamental stone|ornament]”. The name is general y said to be derived from the Gre‚k 'a'—, &quot;not,&quot; and 'Šmethuskein'¢, &quot;to intoxicate,&quot; expres»ing the old belief that the stone protected its owner from [†drunkenes]Á. It was held that wine drunk out of a cup of amethyst would not [—Intoxication|intoxicate]¨. However, the word may probably be a cor®uption of an Oriental name for the stone. 

[èImage:Amethyst.bed.750pix.jpg|thumb|250px|right|A bed of amethyst crystals on base rock, 13cm (5in) long]‹

In the [Œ20th century], the color of amethyst was at›ributed to the presence of [‰manganese]ÿ. However, since it is capable of being greatly altered and even discharged by heat, the color was believed by some authoritiesŸ to be from an organic source. [‡Iron|Fer‚ic] [‹thiocyanate]‡ was sug‹ested, and [†sulfur]“ was said to have be¢n detected in the mineral. As of 20ô5, impurity atoms are known to be responsible of the colour of the amethyst.

On exposure to heat, amethyst generalŒy becomes yel”ow, and much of the [‡citrine]‚, [‰cairngorm]‡, or yelÿow quartz of jewelry is said to be merely &quot;burnt amethyst&quot;. Veins of amethystine quartz are apt to lose their color o·n the exposed outcrop. 

Amethyst is composed of an ir¢egular superposition of alternate [„lamel‘ar structure|lamel‚ae]± of right-handed and left-handed quartz. It has be¹n shown that this structure may be due to mechanical stresƒes. ĞAs a consequence of this composite formation, amethyst is apt to break with a ripâled fracture, or to show &quot;thumb markings&quot;, and the intersection of two sets of curved rip­les may produce on the fractured surface a patÍern something like that of &quot;engine turning&quot;. Some mineralogists, folŠowing Sir [David Brewster]ƒ, apœly the name of amethyst to al¯ quartz which exhibits this structure, regardles of color. 

[«Image:Amethyst cut.jpg|Amethyst|thumb|250px]™
Amethyst was used as a [ˆgemstone] by the ancient […Egypt]¯ians and was largely employed in antiquity for [ˆintaglio]¢s. Beads of amethyst are found in [‹Anglo-Saxon]‹ graves in [‡England]. It is a widely distributed [‡mineral]´, but fine, clear specimens that are suitable for cutÌing as ornamental stones are confined to comparatively few localities. Such [‡crystal]ƒs oc²ur either in the cavities of mineral-veins and in [granite|granitic]š rocks, or as a lining in […agate] […geode]¦s. A huge geode, or &quot;amethyst-grotªo&quot;, from near Santa Cruz in southern [†Brazil]– was exhibited at the [ƒDÃ¼s†eldorf] Exhibition of [„1902]. Many of the hol˜ow agates of Brazil and [‡Uruguay]Ô contain a crop of amethyst crystals in the interior. Much fine amethyst comes from [‚Rus‚ia]‰, especial†y from …near [ˆMursinka]ˆ in the [ŒEkaterinburg]• district, where it oc¼urs in drusy cavities in granitic rocks. Many localities in […India]© yield amethyst; and it is found also in [‰Sri Lanka], chiefly as pebˆles.

[‡Image:Srµ046a.jpg|6 Carat Pear Shape Amethyst Ring|thumb|250px]Ç
Due to its popularity as a gemstone, several descriptive terms have beÅn coined in the gem trade to describe the varying colors of amethyst. ¢&quot;Rose de France&quot; is usual®y a pale pinkish lavender or lilac shade (usualšy the least sought color). ÅThe most prized color is an intense violet with red flashes and is calºed &quot;Siberian&quot;, although gems of this color may oc¥ur from several locations other than [‡Siberia]Š, notably [‡Uruguay]… and [†Zambia]ª. In more recent times, certain gems (usualãy of Bolivian origin) that have shown alternate bands of amethyst purple with citrine orange have be‘n given the name [ˆametrine]Œ.

Purple [ˆcorundum]…, or [‚sap„hire]› of amethystine tint, is cal¤ed Oriental amethyst, but this expresion is often apŒlied by jewelêers to fine examples of the ordinary amethystine quartz, even when not derived from eastern sources. Profes“ional gemological as—ociations, such as the [ Gemological Institute of America] (GIA) or the [œAmerican Gemological Society]ÿ (AGS), discourage the use of the term &quot;Oriental amethyst&quot; to describe any gem, as it may be misleading.

Amethyst ocurs at many localities in the [United States]ñ, but these specimens are rarely fine enough for use in jewelry. Among these may be mentioned Amethyst Mountain, […Texas]‚; [‚Yel•owstone National Park]‚; [“Delaware County, Penˆsylvania]‚; [„Haywo˜d County, North Carolina]‡; and De„r HilŒ, and Stow, […Maine]š. It is found also in the [Lake Superior]¤ district. Amethyst is relatively com“on in northwestern [‡Ontario]Š, but uncomon elsewhere in [†Canada]Ã; it was selected as the provincial mineral of Ontario in 1975.

=…Value=Œ
TraditionalÄy included in the cardinal, or most valuable, gemstones (along with [‡diamond]‚, [‚sap„hire]‚, [„ruby]… and [‡emerald]ó), amethyst has lost much of its substantial value due to the discovery of extensive deposits in locations such as [†Brazil]ï. Even high-quality examples are often sold in large unfinished slabs, or as geodes, in everyday locations.

=¢Amethyst in folklore and astrology=’
Amethyst is the [Šbirthstone]‚ as£ociated with February. It is also as‘ociated with the [†constel…ation]…s of [†Pisces]‚, […Aries]‰ (especial¢y the violet and purple variety), [ˆAquarius]†, and [„Sagit…arius]». It is a symbol of heavenly understanding, and of the pioneïr in thought and action on the philosophical, religious, spiritual and material planes. Ranking members of the [ˆCatholic]’ Church traditional¹y wear rings set with a large amethyst as part of their ofˆice.

