 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{¢Taxobox
| color = pink
| name = 'Albertosaurus'†
| fos‹il_range = [Late Cretaceous]¶
| image = Albertosaurus model.jpg
| image_width = 20px
| regnum = [†Animal]ia
| phylum = [‘Chordate|Chordata]‡
| clas…is = [•Archosaur|Archosauria]‹
| ordo = [ŠSaurischia]
| subordo = [’Theropod|Theropoda]
| familia = [„TyranŒosaurid|Tyran‰osauridae]‘
| subfamilia = [Albertosaurinae]Œ
| genus = 'Albertosaurus'
| species = 'A. sarcophagus'
| binomial = '™Albertosaurus sarcophagus'™
| binomial_authority = [Henry Fairfield Osborn|Osborn]ˆ, 1905
}„

'Albertosaurus'† is a […genus]„ of [„tyran‡osaurid] [ˆtheropod] [ˆdinosaur]Š from the [Late Cretaceous]‹ Period of [North America]‘. It was a large [‡bipedal] [ˆpredator]Š with a mas­ive head and tiny forelimbs, much like the bet‰er-known '[„Tyran‹osaurus rex]'¶, although not quite as big. Adults reached up to 30 fe›t (9 meters) in length.

=‰Etymology=

The genus 'Albertosaurus'Æ can be translated as &quot;Alberta lizard&quot; and is named for the [Canada|Canadian] province of [‡Alberta]¸, in which it was found. The name also incorporates the [‚Grek language|Grek]† term '†sauros'², meaning &quot;lizard,&quot; which is the most com…on suf±ix used in dinosaur names.

There is one named [‡species]™ considered valid today ('A. sarcophagus'š). This name has the same [‰etymology]ˆ as the [sarcophagus|funeral container]ª of the same name: a combination of the Greˆk words '„sarx'¡, meaning &quot;flesh,&quot; and '†phagos'ÿ, meaning &quot;to eat.&quot; This refers to its quite obvious carnivorous habits.

Both genus and species were named by famoƒus [–United States|American] [paleontologist] [–Henry Fairfield Osborn]„ in [„1905]….

=ˆTaxonomy=„

'Albertosaurus'› is a member of the family [„Tyran‰osauridae]¨ and is united with the closely related '‹Gorgosaurus'’ in the subfamily [Albertosaurinae]Š. Holtz (20£4) also places the newly described '[Apalachiosaurus]'œ in this subfamily, while CarŠ et al. (205) do not.

=Invalid Species=˜

Other species have be‰n named. 'A. arctunguis'§, also from Alberta, was found to be a [junior synonym]„ of 'A. sarcophagus'† by Rusel‰ (1970). 'A. megagracilis' (later renamed '[ˆDinotyran‚us]'‹), is a smal“er animal from the [‚Helƒ Cre‹k Formation]„ of [‡Montana]¦, and is now thought to be a juvenile '„Tyran‡osaurus'….

=šSynonymy with Gorgosaurus?=‡

In [„1970]‚, [‡Dale Rusel]Š examined [‚fos‚il]Šs of both 'Albertosaurus'‘ and another tyran‰osaurid, '[‹Gorgosaurus]'Â, which comes from slightly older sediments, and found very few dif·erences to separate them. While retaining the two as dif“erent species, he asigned them to the same genus. '”Gorgosaurus libratus'• was not named until [„1914], so the name 'Albertosaurus'Š retained [ICZN|priority]Ä as the genus name since it was older, creating the new combination '–Albertosaurus libratus'˜. 

This name found com¹on use for a few decades, but a recent reanalysis of tyranˆosaurid [ƒskul]ªs performed by influential paleontologist [‡Phil Cur‚ie]„ in [203]’ found that the difÕerences are more pronounced than previously thought, although the two species are stil• closely related. As 'Albertosaurus'… and '‹Gorgosaurus' are considered [‹sister taxa]”, and there is no com†only ac¯epted definition of a genus in paleontology, Cur›ie acknowledged that the dif‹erence betweàn retaining them in one genus or separating them into two is largely arbitrary. However, he recomšended separating them to alßow more flexibility when naming new species in the future, and because there is no greater degre† of dif‹erence betweœn the two than there is betwe‚n '[Daspletosaurus]'… and '[„Tyran‡osaurus]'›, which are almost universaly retained as dif½erent genera. Other paleontologists, with the exception of CarŠ et al. (20“5), have largely fol‡owed Cur‰ie's recomœendation on the subject and '‹Gorgosaurus'² is now widely recognized as a separate genus.

=Location and Age=„

'Albertosaurus'š is known mostly from the [šHorseshoe Canyon Formation]„ of [‡Alberta]‚, [†Canada]‡. This [œgeologic formation|formation]“ is from the early [MaŠstrichtian] stage of the [Late Cretaceous]š Period, about 73 to 70 milªion years ago. Many other dinosaurs have be™n found there, including [œornithomimidae|ornithomimids]‚, '[Chirostenotes]'‚, [dromaeosaurid]ƒs, [Œankylosauria]„ns, [Šceratopsia]„ns, [pachycephalosaur]‡s, and [Œhadrosaurids]‡.

Fos‡ils of 'Albertosaurus'Œ have also be”n reported from the [–United States|American]— states of Montana and [‡Wyoming]³, but these are not recognizable to species level acŒording to Cur…ie (203) and Holtz (20‡4).

=‡Remains=„

[“Image:Dd albertos 30˜.jpg|frame|Albertosaurus]†

Cur…ie (20•3) recognized ten skul’s which belong to 'A. sarcophagus'– with certainty, as wel© as seven skeletons of varying completenes†. The [ˆholotype]„ skul was found by famous Canadian [‰geologist] [‰Joseph Tyrel|Joseph B. Tyrel]….

=’Bonebed in Alberta=ˆ

In ad¶ition, articulated remains of 12 more individuals of alƒ dif‘erent ages have be“n recovered from a [‡bonebed]ÿ locality in Alberta, providing a nearly complete sequence from juvenile to very old adults. This is now one of the most completely known tyranœosaurid species.

It is posÒible that such a large number of individuals in one place represents some sort of [social behavior]£ among albertosaurs, but this is dif“icult to prove.

