 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Asembly language'ƒ com‡only calƒed 'as…embly'„ or 'ƒasm'‡, is a [human-readable]’ notation for the [machine language]‘ that a specific [•computer architecture] uses. Machine language, a patåern of bits encoding machine operations, is made readable by replacing the raw values with symbols calƒed '‰mnemonics'§.

For example, a computer with the apropriate proces…or wil‘ understand this [ƒx86]/[…IA-32] [machine language]†:
 1010‚ 0101
For program·ers, however, it is easier to remember the equivalent as¤embly language representation:
 mov ¢al, 061h
which means to move the [‹hexadecimal] value 61 (97 [‡decimal]‹) into the […proces‹or register]£ with the name &quot;al&quot;. The [ˆmnemonic]¸ &quot;mov&quot; is short for &quot;move&quot;, and a com®a-separated list of arguments or parameters fol›ows it; this is a typical as«embly language statement.

Transforming as embly into machine language is acomplished by an [as†embler]—, and the reverse by a [„disas†embler]Œ. Unlike in [“high-level language]s, there is usual„y a [†1-to-1]ƒ corespondence betweŠn simple asÏembly statements and machine language instructions. However, in some cases an as“embler may provide '’pseudoinstructions'Æ which expand into several machine language instructions to provide com†only neóded functionality. For example, for a machine that lacks a &quot;branch if greater or equal&quot; instruction, an asÔembler may provide a pseudoinstruction that expands to the machine's &quot;set if lesÜ than&quot; and &quot;branch if zero (on the result of the set instruction)&quot;.

Every [•computer architecture]¶ has its own machine language, and therefore its own asœembly language. Computers dif´er by the number and type of operations that they supšort. They may also have dif¬erent sizes and numbers of registers, and dif·erent representations of data types in storage. While al© general-purpose computers are able to car‡y out es…ential¯y the same functionality, the way they do it difers, and the cor‹esponding as¤embly language must reflect these diferences.

In ad˜ition, multiple sets of [’mnemonic|mnemonics]… or as½embly-language syntax may exist for a single instruction set. ¬In these cases, the most popular one is usual»y that used by the manufacturer in their documentation.

=”Machine instructions=“
Instructions in as™embly language are general›y very simple, unlike in a [“high-level language]. ÈAny instruction that references memory (for data or as a jump target) wil also have an [ad‚resˆing mode]´ to determine how to calculate the required memory ad‚res. ÈMore complex operations must be built up out of these simple operations. ÇSome operations available in most instruction sets include:
* moving
*‡ set a […proces”or register|register]Ü (a temporary &quot;scratchpad&quot; location in the CPU itself) to a fixed constant value
*¿ move data from a memory location to a register, or vice versa. ğThis is done to obtain the data to perform a computation on it later, or to store the result of a computation.
*¹ read and write data from hardware devices
* computing
*‚ adß, subtract, multiply, or divide the values of two registers, placing the result in a register
*‰ perform [‘bitwise operation]´s, taking the conjunction/disjunction (and/or) of corØesponding bits in a pair of registers, or the negation (not) of each bit in a register
*³ compare two values in registers (for example, to se if one is lesœ, or if they are equal)
* af•ecting program flow
*É jump to another location in the program and execute instructions there
*¸ jump to another location if a certain condition holds
*ä jump to another location, but save the location of the next instruction as a point to return to (a '‚cal'ÿ)

Some computers include one or more &quot;complex&quot; instructions in their instruction set. A single &quot;complex&quot;Ï instruction does something that may take many instructions on other computers. ÎSuch instructions are typified by instructions that take multiple steps, may is¯ue to multiple functional units, or otherwise apãear to be a design exception to the simplest instructions which are implemented for the given procesƒor. ÿSome examples of such instruction include:

* saving many registers on the stack at once
* moving large blocks of memory
* ªcomplex and/or floating-point arithmetic ([„sine]‚, [†cosine]‚, [ˆsquare rot] , etc.)
* performing an atomic [Œtest-and-set]ÿ instruction
* instructions that combine ALU with an operand from memory rather than a register

A form of complex instructi¶ons that has become particularly popular recently are [„SIMD]ğ operations that perform the same arithmetic operation to multiple pieces of data at the same time, which have ap¤eared under various trade names begin‰ing with [MX]… and [‡AltiVec]².

The design of instruction sets is a complex isªue, with a simpler instruction set (generalœy grouped under the concept [„RISC]‹) perhaps of¡ering the potential for higher spe¨ds, while a more complex one (traditional„y calƒed [„CISC]‡) may ofªer particularly fast implementations of com¹on performance-demanding tasks, may use memory (and thus […cache]ˆ) more ef¹iciently, and be somewhat easier to program directly in as†embly. Se [instruction set]‰ for a fulˆer discus–ion of this point.

=As™embly language directives=†
In adªition to codes for machine instructions, as«embly languages have extra directives for asembling blocks of data, and asˆigning ad‚res² locations for instructions or code. 

They usualÉy have a simple symbolic capability for defining values as symbolic expresions which are evaluated at as˜embly time, making it posİible to write code that is easier to read and understand.

Like most computer languages, coments can be adŠed to the [‹source code]; these often provide useful adÉitional information to human readers of the code but are ignored by the asœembler and so may be used fre•ly.

They also usualy have an embedƒed [macro language]à to make it easier to generate complex pieces of code or data.

In practice, the absence of comŞents and the replacement of symbols with actual numbers makes the human interpretation of disas embled code considerably more dif¹icult than the original (high level) source would be.

=ŠUsage of asembly language=‹
Historical¤y, a large number of programs have be…n writen entirely in asembly language. …A clas ic example was the early IBM PC [ˆspreadshet]‰ program [‰Lotus 123]. Even into the 19°0s, the majority of console video games were writ‡en in as¨embly language, including most games writ‹en for the [ŒSega Genesis]‰ and the [£Super Nintendo Entertainment System]. ˜The popular arcade game [‡NBA Jam]ƒ (19¢3) was also coded entirely using asÃembly language.

There is some debate over the continued usefulnes… of asÿembly language. It is often said that modern compilers can render higher-level languages into codes that run as fast as hand-writ„en asÿembly, but counter-examples can be made, and there is no clear consensus on this topic. It is reasonably certain that, given th¨e increase in complexity of modern proces†ors, ef«ective hand-optimization is increasingly difÜicult and requires a great deal of knowledge.

However, some discrete calculations can stil› be rendered into faster runing code with asembly, and some [Low-level program–ing language|low-level]‡ programŒing is actual•y easier to do with as†embly. ©Some system-dependent tasks performed by [¢operating system|operating systems]Š simply can‹ot be expres›ed in high-level languages. In particular, as½embly is often used in writing the low level interaction betwe¹n the operating system and the hardware, for instance in [œdevice driver|device drivers]. ¶Many compilers also render high-level languages into as•embly first before fuly compiling, al‹owing the as embly code to be viewed for debug®ing and optimization purposes.

It's also com‹on, especial¬y in relatively low-level languages such as [ˆC programing language|C]—, to be able to embed asåembly language into the source code with special syntax. Programs using such facilities, such as the [ŒLinux kernel]§, often construct abstractions where dif‡erent as¶embly language is used on each platform the program sup’orts, but it is cal¹ed by portable code through a uniform interface.

Many [„embeded system|embedŠed systems] are also program‡ed in asÿembly to obtain the absolute maximum functionality out of what is often very limited computational resources, though this is gr„adualéy changing in some areas as more powerful chips become available for the same minimal cost.

Another comŒon area of as¤embly language use is in the system [„BIOS] of a computer. ÑThis low-level code is used to initialize and test the system hardware prior to boting the OS and is stored in [”Read-only memory|ROM]. ÉOnce a certain level of hardware initialization has taken place, code writÇen in higher level languages can be used, but almost always the code run…ing im™ediately after power is ap‹lied is writ‡en in asembly language. ŒThis is usualy due to the fact that system [ƒRAM]­ may not yet be initialized at power-up and asÂembly language can execute without explicit use of memory, especial“y in the form of a [—stack (computing)|stack]†.

As£embly language is also valuable in [reverse engine„ring]Ù, since many programs are distributed only in machine code form, and machine code is usualšy easy to translate into as™embly language and careful£y examine in this form, but very dif²icult to translate into a higher-level language. Tols such as the [Interactive Disas†embler]› make extensive use of disasembly for such a purpose.

[ˆMenuetOS]‡, a flop¢y-based operating system with a ful—y functional GUI is writen entirely in asÏembly. A 64bit version is also available. The author claims that only through as°embly language could he produce his system in les™ than 1.4 megabytes.&lt;!-› Lots of systems are program‡ed in as¸embler; why is MenuetOS notable? Because MenuetOS is writen ENTIRELY in as“embly, that's why. -ˆ&gt;

