 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Otheruses|the fo–d or drink of the gods}

In ancient [‚Gre•k mythology|mythology]‚, 'ˆAmbrosia'„ (Gre‚k {ìlang|el|&amp;#945;&amp;#787;&amp;#956;&amp;#946;&amp;#961;&amp;#959;&amp;#963;&amp;#953;&amp;#769;&amp;#945;}”) is sometimes the fo¡d, 
sometimes the drink, of the [„gods]. “The word has generalƒy be‘n derived from Gre‚k '‚a-'— (&quot;not&quot;) and '‡mbrotos'¢ (&quot;mortal&quot;); hence the fo“d or drink of the im‡ortals. [†Thetis]• anointed the infant [„Achil‚es]• with ambrosia and pas«ed the child through the fire to make him im›ortal&amp;mdash;a familiar [‰Phoenicia]—n custom&amp;mdash;but [†Peleus]ƒ, apal‡ed, stop‹ed her. In '[…Iliad]'† xvi, [ƒApolo]” washed the black blo•d from the corpse of [ˆSarpedon]Ö and anointed it with ambrosia, readied for its dreamlike return to Sarpedon's native […Lycia]‰. The clasical scholar [ˆArthur Wol†gar Veral]Ä, however, denied that there is any clear example in which the word '‰ambrosios'… necesŒarily means 'im…ortal'‹, and preferôed to explain it as &quot;fragrant,&quot; a sense which is always suitable. If so, the word may be derived from the [™Semitic languages|Semitic] 'ƒMBR'£ (&quot;amber&quot;, compare &quot;[‰ambergris]¢&quot;) to which Eastern nations at·ribute miraculous properties. In Europe, honey-colored […amber]Å, sometimes far from its natural source, was already a grave gift in [‰Neolithic]’ times and was stil worn in the [7th century CE]’ as a talisman by [Druidry|druidic] [Frisia|Frisians], though St. [‡Eligius]É warned &quot;No woman should presume to hang amber from her neck.&quot; [W. H. Roscher]’ thinks that both '†nectar'œ and ambrosia were kinds of […honey]¤, in which case their power of confer…ing imŸortality would be due to the supâosed healing and cleansing power of honey, which is in fact aseptic, and because fermented honey ([„mead]‹) preceded [„wine]‡ as an [‰entheogen]¢ in the Aegean world: the Great Godes„ of […Crete]œ on some Minoan seals had a [be] face: compare [†Merope]… and [„Melisa]ƒ. Se† also […Ichor].

One of the impieties of [ˆTantalus]ƒ, acŠording to [†Pindar], was that he of®ered to his guests the ambrosia of the DeathlesŸ Ones, a theft akin to that of [ŠPrometheus]‚, [ŒKarl Kerenyi]‹ noted (in 'Heroes of the Gre‚ks'ƒ). […Circe] mentioned to [ƒOdysƒeus]Ó that a flock of doves brought the ambrosia to Olympus. 

Derivatively, the word 'ˆAmbrosia'½ (neuter plural) was given to certain festivals in honour of [ˆDionysus]¸, probably because of the predominance of feasting in con½ection with them. 

&quot;Ambrosia&quot; is related to the [Hinduism|Hindu] [†amrita]•, a drink which confer„ed imœortality on the gods.

In [‚Gre‹k mythology], one of the [™Hyades (mythology)|Hyades]§.

Many modern scholars, including Dan¢y Staples, relate ambrosia to the [Psychedelics, disociatives and deliriants | halˆucinogen]ˆic mushro‚m '[Amanita muscaria].' „

