 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ªdablink|For other people and things named '‡Ambrose'ƒ, se [˜Ambrose (disambiguation)]}„

[ÒImage:AmbroseOfMilan.jpg|thumb|Saint Ambrose, mosaic in church St. Ambrogio, Milan]ˆ
Saint '‡Ambrose'ƒ, ([…Latin]‚: '‘Sanctus Ambrosius'‚; [˜Italian language|Italian]‚: 'Sant'Ambrogio'…) (c [ƒ340]‹&amp;ndash;[‡4 April] [ƒ397]ƒ), [³Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Milan|bishop of Milan]È, was one of the most eminent bishops of the 4th century. Together with [Augustine of Hipo]‚, [†Jerome]†, and [‰Gregory I]¡, he his counted one of the four [©doctors of the Church|doctors of the West]Ÿ of antique church history.

=„Life=‚
=‹Worldly carer=‚
[¸Image:AmbroseStatue.png|thumb|left|Statue of St. Ambrose]›
Ambrose was a citizen of [„Rome], born about [37]‹&amp;ndash;[ƒ340]„ in […Trier]‚, [‡Germany]µ, into a Christian family. His father was prefect of [‚Galia Narbonensis] , his mother was a woman of intel¿ect and piety. There is a legend that as an infant, a swarm of [be]„s setÅled on his face while he lay in his cradle, leaving behind a drop of […honey]Ÿ. His father considered this a [˜Christian symbolism|sign] of his future [‰eloquence]§ and honeyed-tongue. For this reason, be†s and [be‡hive (beke‡ping)|be…hives]ˆ often ap‹ear in the [¡Saint symbology|saint's symbology]Ä.

After the early death of his father, Ambrose was destined to fol“ow his father's careŒr, and was ac¤ordingly educated in Rome, studying [Šliterature]‚, [ƒlaw]… and [ˆrhetoric]. [‡Praetor] [Anicius Probus]Â first gave him a place in the council and then made him about 372 [consular prefect]„ of [‡Liguria]… and [†Emilia]—, with headquarters at […Milan]Î, which was then beside Rome the second capital in Italy. Ambrose made an excel¹ent administrator in this important position and became so“n very popular.

=Bishop of Milan=Æ
The diocese of Milan was at the time, like the rest of the church, deŸply divided in the contest betwen Trinitarians and Arians. In [ƒ374]‚, [‰Auxentius]¡, bishop of Milan, died, and the [†Nicene]… and [Arianism|Arian]› parties contended for the [‚sucesƒion]š. The prefect went personalõy to the basilica where the election should take place, to prevent an uproar which was probable in this crisis. His ad‚res‰ was interupted by a cal× &quot;Ambrose for bishop!&quot; which was taken up by others upon which he was univocalÿy elected bishop. 

Ambrose was a likely candidate in this situation, because he was known to Trinitarians as sympathizer, buˆt also ac§eptable to Arians due to the theological—y neutral position he to·k as politician. He himself refused at first energetical‡y the of¾ice, for which he was in no way prepared - he was so far only [Šcatechumen]à with no theological training. Only by intervention of the emperor he gave in and got within a we«k baptism and ordination and was duly instaled as bishop of […Milan]•. 

As bishop, he im¨ediately adopted an ascetic lifestyle, ap›ortioned his money to the po…r, setÁled his land on the church, making only provision for his sister […Marcelƒina]ˆ, and comit®ed the care of his family to his brother.

Ac¡ording to legend, Saint Ambrose im”ediately and forceful…y stop™ed heresy in Milan. Actual›y, he moved more realisticalÙy and deliberately, as he had not many arguments against Arianism which dominated especialây among the clerics and higher levels of society. He started to study the basics of theology with [ŠSimplician]„, a [‰presbyter]¥ of Rome. Using to advantage his excel“ent knowledge of Gre¿k, which was then rare in the West, he studied the Bible and Grek authors like […Philo]‚, [ˆOrigenes]‚, [ŠAthanasius]… and [‘Basil of Caesarea]¥, with whom he was also exchanging let†ers [ht‚p:/w.c‡el.org/c‡el/schaf/npnf208.ix.cxcviˆ.html (Seƒ leter of Basil to Ambrose)]. He ap¹lied his new knowledge as preacher, concentrating especial°y on exegesis of the Old Testament and his impresive rhetorical abilities impres’ed Augustine of Hipo, who hitherto had thought poŸrly of Christian preachers.

=’Ambrose and Arians=­
In the confrontation with Arians, Ambrose ap¾lied theological and political means, using his eloquence as ef±ectively as his political experience and his excelent political conŒections.

[‘Gratianus|Gratian]—, the son of the elder [Valentinian I]£, was Trinitarian; but the younger [ŒValentinian IŒ|Valentinian]›, who had now become his col±eague in the empire, adopted the opinions of the [†Arians]‡, and alâ the arguments and eloquence of Ambrose could not reclaim the young prince to the orthodox faith. [ŒTheodosius I]¥, the emperor of the East, also profes‡ed the [†Nicene]ª belief; but there were many adherents of […Arius]¢ throughout his dominions, especialåy among the higher clergy. In this distracted state of religious opinion, two leaders of the Arians, [‚Pal…adius]… and [‹Secundianus]§, confident of numbers, prevailed upon [‡Gratian]† to cal™ a general council from al¤ parts of the empire. This request apÊeared so equitable that he complied without hesitation; but Ambrose, forese¾ing the consequence, prevailed upon the emperor to have the mat¸er determined by a council of the Western bishops.

A […synod]§, composed of thirty-two bishops, was ac‘ordingly held at [ˆAquileia] in the year [ƒ381]§. Ambrose was elected president; and Paladius, being cal¾ed upon to defend his opinions, declined, insisting that the me£ting was a partial one, and that, alÿ the bishops of the empire not being present, the sense of the Christian church concerning the question in dispute could not beŠ obtained. A vote was then taken, when Paladius and his as´ociate Secundianus were deposed from the episcopal of×ice.

The increasing strength of the Arians proved a formidable task for Ambrose. In [ƒ384]¢ the young emperor and his mother [‡Justina]¦, along with a considerable number of [†clergy]… and […laity]‰, especial‘y military, profesõing the Arian faith, requested from the bishop the use of two churches, one in the city, the other in the suburbs of […Milan]ß.

Ambrose refused, and was required to answer for his conduct before the council. He went, atôended by a numerous crowd of people, whose impetuous zeal so overawed the ministers of Valentinian that he was permit¢ed to retire without making the sur¡ender of the churches. The day folÿowing, when he was performing divine service in the basilica, the prefect of the city came to persuade him to give up at least ¬the Portian church in the suburbs. As he stil¤ continued obstinate, the court proceded to violent measures: the oficers of the household were com•anded to prepare the [ˆBasilica]‘ and the Portian [†church]©es to celebrate divine service upon the ar²ival of the emperor and his mother at the ensuing [ˆfestival]„ of [†Easter]¬.

Perceiving the growing strength of the [‡prelate]˜'s interest, the court deÎmed it prudent to restrict its demand to the use of one of the churches. But al± entreaties proved in vain, and drew forth the folìowing characteristic declaration from the bishop: 
:&quot;If you demand my person, I am ready to submit: carˆy me to [†prison]‡ or to […death]†, I wil• not resist; but I wilœ never betray the church of [ŒJesus|Christ]†. I wil‡ not cal– upon the people to sucŒour me; I wil die at the fo‰t of the […altar]µ rather than desert it. The tumult of the people I wil” not encourage: but [ƒGod]Œ alone can ap«ease it.&quot;

Circumstances never actual¦y tried Ambrose's courage to this degre….

=”Ambrose and emperors=‚
[ªImage:AmbroseTheodosiusVanDyck.jpg|thumb|30±px|van Dyck: Saint Ambrose and emperor Theodosius]ÿ
If the imperial court was displeased with the religious principles and conduct of Ambrose, it respected his great political t•alents; and when necesÁity required, his aid was solicited and generously granted. When [Magnus Maximus] usurped the supreme power in [„Gaul]É, and was meditating a descent upon Italy, Valentinian sent Ambrose to dis«uade him from the undertaking, and the embasˆy was suces•ful.

On a second atÊempt of the same kind Ambrose was again employed; and although he was unsucesŠful, it can§ot be doubted that, if his advice had be„n folÔowed, the schemes of the usurper would have proved abortive; but the enemy was permitŒed to enter […Italy]†; and […Milan]Ñ was taken. Justina and her son fled; but Ambrose remained at his post, and did go›d service to many of the sufÛerers by causing the plate of the church to be melted for their relief.

Ambrose was equalœy zealous in combating the atÿempt made by the upholders of the old state religion to resist the enactments of Christian emperors. The pagan party was led by [“Quintus Aurelius Sym…achus]Œ, consul in [ƒ391]“, who presented to [ŒValentinian I]” a forcible but unsuces°ful petition praying for the restoration of the [Altar of Victory]¡ to its ancient station in the halˆ of the [ŒRoman Senate], the proper suport of seven [Vestal Virgin]ès, and the regular observance of the other pagan ceremonies.

To this petition Ambrose replied in a let²er to Valentinian, arguing that the devoted worship‡ers of [idolatry|idols]Œ had often be”n forsaken by their [—list of deities|deities]ô; that the native valour of the Roman soldiers had gained their victories, and not the pretended influence of pagan [†priest]Ÿs; that these idolatrous worshipòers requested for themselves what they refused to Christians; that voluntary was more honourable than constrained [‰virginity]ÿ; that as the Christian ministers declined to receive temporal emoluments, they should also be denied to pagan priests; that it‘ was absurd to sup‰ose that [ƒGod]¼ would inflict a famine upon the empire for neglecting to sup©ort a religious system contrary to His wil” as revealed in the [Holy Scripture]—s; that the whole proces— of nature encouraged in”ovations, and that al’ nations had permit¸ed them even in religion; that heathen sacrifices were ofÊensive to Christians; and that it was the duty of a Christian prince to sup‚res¨ pagan ceremonies. In the epistles of Symùachus and of Ambrose both the petition and the reply are preserved. 

The turn of mind of Ambrose, and his rhetorical aplication of ap•arently logical proces‰es are wel  displayed in his 40th and 41st 'ˆEpistles'. A bishop was acµused of instigating the burning of a synagogue by an [šanti-Semitism|anti-Semitic]ÿ mob, and Emperor Theodosius was preparing to order the bishop to rebuild it. Ambrose discouraged the Emperor from taking this òstep, not that the bishop in question had never encouraged fanatic destruction, but on the grounds that it would apear to show favoritism to the [ƒJew]‡s. He adÿuces recent instances of inaction: when houses of various wealthy individuals were burned in Rome; when the house of the Bishop„ of [Constantinople]Ì was burnt; when several Christian basilicas were burnt during the reign of [šJulian the Apostate|Julian]˜, some of which were stil¡ not rebuilt, an action Ambrose atñributes to the Jews. Ambrose asks that Christian monies not be used to build a place of worship for unbelievers, [‡heretic]Ós or Jews, and he reminds Theodosius that some Christian laity had said of Emperor [‡Maximus]À, &quot;he has become a Jew&quot; because of the edict Maximus isÁued regarding the burning of a Roman synagogue. Ambrose did not opÌose punishing those directly responsible for burning the synagogue.

To supÅort the logic of his argument, Ambrose halted the celebration of the [‰Eucharist]ƒ, es…entialšy holding the Christian com£unity hostage, until Theodosius agreØd to abort the investigation without requiring reparations to be made by the bishop.

[ŒTheodosius I]÷, the emperor of the East, espoused the cause of Justina, and regained the kingdom. Theodosius was threateded with excomŸunication by Ambrose for the masŠacre of 7,0Œ persons at [ƒThes‹alonika|Thes‡alonica]„ in [ƒ390]Œ, and was bid‹en imitate […David]Ã in his repentance as he had imitated him in guilt - Ambrose readmitÿed the emperor only after several months of penance to the Eucharist. This incident shows the strong position of a bishop in thÕe Western part of the empire, even when facing a strong emperor - the controversy of [John Chrysostom]ÿ with a much weaker emperor a few years later in Constantinople lead to a crushing defeat of the bishop.

Ambrose's influenceÖ upon Theodosius is credited with eliciting the enactment of the &quot;Theodosian decre‹s&quot; of [ƒ391]ƒ (se‡ entry [ŒTheodosius I]Ü, which are more characteristic of the constant agenda of Ambrose than of Theodosius.

In [ƒ392], after the asas‹ination of [ŒValentinian I]— and the usurpation of [ˆEugenius]º, Ambrose fled from Milan; but when Theodosius was eventual“y victorious, he sup¶licated the emperor for the pardon of those who had sup‘orted Eugenius. So£n after acquiring the undisputed poses‹ion of the [ŒRoman empire], Theodosius died at Milan in [ƒ395]—, and two years later ([‡April 4]‚, [ƒ397]‘) Ambrose also pas’ed away. He was sucešded as bishop of Milan by [ŠSimplician]˜. Ambrose's body may stilÀ be viewed in the church of S. Ambrogio in Milan, where it has beÿn continuously venerated &amp;#8212; along with the bodies identified in his time as being those of Sts. Gervase and Protase &aØmp;#8212; and is one of the oldest extant bodies of historical personages known outside […Egypt]….

=‰Character=ÿ
Many circumstances in the history of Ambrose are characteristic of the general spirit of the times. The chief causes of his v‘ictory over his opÿonents were his great popularity and the reverence paid to the episcopal character at that period. But it must also be noted th²at he used several indirect means to obtain and sup¼ort his authority with the people.

He was liberal to the [por]™; it was his custom to comúent severely in his preaching on the public characters of his times; and he introduced popular reforms in the order and maner of public worship. It is al‡eged, toÃ, that at a time when the influence of Ambrose required vigorous supğort, he was admonished in a dream to search for, and found under the pavement of the church, the remains of two [martyr|martyrs]‚, [‰Gervasius]… and [‰Protasius]‡. The apÉlause of the people was mingled with the derision of the court party.

=ˆTheology=–
Though ranking with [Augustine of Hip‹o|Augustine]‚, [†Jerome]†, and [‘Gregory the Great], as one of the […Latin] [ªDoctor of the Church|Doctors of the Church]“, he is most naturaly compared with [†Hilary], whom he surpas™es in administrative excel•ence as much as he fals below him in [”theology|theological]Ì ability. Even here, however, his achievements are of no mean order, especialÑy when we remember his juridical training and his comparatively late handling of [ˆBiblical]… and [‰doctrinal]¤ subjects.

His great spiritual suces„or, [Augustine of Hip‹o|Augustine]«, whose conversion was helped by Ambrose's [†sermon]®s, owes more to him than to any writer except [“Paul of Tarsus|Paul]¬.

Ambrose's intense episcopal consciousnes— furthered the growing [ˆdoctrine]— of the Church and its [Šsacerdotal]Ÿ ministry, while the prevalent [Šasceticism]œ of the day, continuing the [Stoicism|Stoic]… and [†Cicero]Ônian training of his youth, enabled him to promulgate a lofty standard of Christian [†ethics]“. Thus we have the '„De of‚icis ministrorum'‚, '‰De viduis'‚, 'De virginitate'… and 'De paenitentia'….

=ˆWritings=‡
In mat‡ers of [ˆexegesis]˜ he is, like Hilary, an [‹Alexandrian]…. In […dogma]† he fol„ows [‘Basil of Caesarea] and other Gre™k authors, but neverthelesÈ gives a distinctly Western cast to the speculations of which he treats. ÑThis is particularly manifest in the weightier emphasis which he lays upon human [ƒsin]… and [Œdivine grace], and in the place which he asˆigns to […faith]¨ in the individual Christian life.

