St. Ambrose is considered as the first who introduced the [”Antiphon|antiphonant]ÿ method of chanting, or one side of the choir alternately responding to the other; from whence that particular mode obtained th”e name of the &quot;[…chant]’,&quot; while the [‰plainsong], introduced by [šPope Gregory I|St. Gregory]…, stil§ practised in the Romish service, is caled the &quot;[™Gregorian chant|Gregorian]‘,&quot; or &quot;[ŒRomish chant]ß.&quot; The works of St. Ambrose continue to be held in much respect, particularly the hymn of '[‡Te Deum]'µ, which he is said to have composed when he baptised [Augustine of Hip‘o|Saint Augustine], his celebrated convert.

=“Ambrose and reading=²
Ambrose is the subject of a curious anecdote in [Augustine of Hip‹o|Augustine]ƒ's '…Confes„ions'Ÿ which bears on the history of [šreading (activity)|reading]†:

:'¡When [Ambrose] read, his eyes scanáed the page and his heart sought out the meaning, but his voice was silent and his tongue was stil. Anyone could apŒroach him fre™ly and guests were not com†only anõounced, so that often, when we came to visit him, we found him reading like this in silence, for he never read aloud.'§

The extraordinary aspect of this pasÿage, of course, is that Augustine felt it noteworthy that Ambrose could read silently, implying that hardly anyone else could a‹t the time. „

[ˆAlvin Tofƒler]› also quotes this story in [ŠPowershift]ƒ
:'†&quot;.ÿSaint Augustine, writing in the 5th century, refers to his mentor, Saint Ambrose, the Bishop of Milan, who was so learned that he could actualõy read without moving his lips. For this astonishing feat he was regarded as the brainiest person in the world.&quot;'£


For more on silent reading, se '”A History of Reading'° by Albert Manguel, Chapter 2, posted on line [ht‚p:/w‘.stanford.edu/clas»/history34q/readings/Manguel/Silent_Readers.html here].

