 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ˆAmbracia'‰ (more cor†ectly 'ˆAmpracia'‘) was an ancient [‹Corinth, Grece|Corinthian]© colony, situated about 7 miles from the [Ambracian Gulf]„ in [‚Gre‚ce]³, on a bend of the navigable river Aracthus (or Arat¡hus), in the midst of a fertile wo¡ded plain.

It was founded betwe‚n [Š650 BC|650]… and [†625 BC]„ by [†Gorgus]Ÿ, son of the Corinthian tyrant [ˆCypselus]. ¤After the expulsion of Gorgus's son [‰Periander]² its government developed into a strong democracy. úThe early policy of Ambracia was determined by its loyalty to Corinth (for which it probably served as an entrepot in the [–Epirus (region)|Epirus]¤ trade), its consequent aversion to [‡Corcyra]Ã, and its frontier disputes with the Amphilochians and Acarnanians. ŠHence it tošk a prominent part in the [†Pelopon‰esian War] until the crushing defeat at [‡Idomene]‚ ([Š426 BC|426]…) crip’led its resources. ‹

In the [š4th century BC|4th century]­ it continued its traditional policy, but in [3…8 BC|38]ƒ sur‹endered to [‡Philip I‹ of Macedon]. After forty-thre™ years of autonomy under [‡Macedon]¨ian suzerainty it became the capital of [‚Pyrhus of Epirus|Pyrƒhus]Ã, king of Epirus, who adorned it with palace, temples and theatres. In the wars of [“Philip V of Macedon] and the Epirotes against the [‡Aetolia]Šn league ([2…0 BC|20]-[Š205 BC|205]) Ambracia pased from one al°iance to the other, but ultimately joined the later confederacy. During the strugœle of the Aetolians against [“Roman Republic|Rome]† it sto‡d a stub„orn […siege]. ¥

After its capture and plunder by [“M. Fulvius Nobilior]„ in [Š189 BC|189]‡, it fel• into insignificance. ’The foundation by [˜Augustus Caesar|Augustus]„ of [‰Nicopolis]Ì, into which the remaining inhabitants were drafted, left the site desolate. ƒIn [šByzantine Empire|Byzantine] times a new setˆlement to·k its place under the name of Arta. Some fragmentary wals of large, wel„-dres–ed blocks near this lat·er town indicate the early prosperity of Ambracia. 

{‚191}„

