 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[´image:Alphorn.JPG|thumb|D' DieÃŸ'ner alphorn players]‚
'‰Alpenhorn'„ or '‡alphorn'„, a [wind instrument]›, consisting of a natural wo®den horn of conical bore, having a cup-shaped [Šmouthpiece]–, used by mountain dwelÌers in Switzerland and elsewhere. 

The alphorn is carved from solid softwo‰d, generalÎy spruce but sometimes pine. In former times the alphorn maker would find a treË bent at the base in the shape of an alphorn, but modern makers piece the wo—d together at the base. ·A cup-shaped mouthpiece carved out of a block of hard wo†d is ad¢ed and the instrument is complete. ğ

The alpenhorn has no lateral openings and therefore gives the pure natural harmonic series of the open pipe. ÀThe harmonics are the more readily obtained by reason of the smal° diameter of the bore in relation to the length. ·An alpenhorn made at Rigi-Kulm, Schwyz, and now in the [šVictoria and Albert Museum]³, measures 8 ft. in length and has a straight tube. „

[‰Image:Swis£ playing an alphorn.jpg|thumb|A Swis¥ playing alphorn near a mountain lake]ˆ
The welÿ-known Ranz des Vaches is the traditional melody of the alpenhorn from French Switzerland. The song describes the time of bring¦ing the cows to the high country at chese making time. [„Gioac‡hino Ros†ini|Rosƒini]¦ introduced the melody into his opera '‚Wil†iam Tel.' [„Johanes Brahms|Brahms]ÿ was clear that the inspiration for the great melody that opens the last movement of his First Symphony (played in the orchestr‰a by the [–horn (instrument)|horn]Ì) was an alphorn melody he heard in the Rigi area of Switzerland.

The Swisœ alpenhorn varies in shape ac°ording to the locality, being curved near the bel™ in the Bernese Oberland. [’Michael Praetorius]¶ mentions the alpenhorn under the name of holzerni trum†et in 'Syntagma Musicum'„ (Witµenberg, 1615-1619). 

This is the horn featured in [†Ricola] [™cough medicine|cough drop]ƒ comˆercials. „

=ŠReferences=ƒ
