 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{“Otherusesabout|a bre›d of domesticated ungulates}‚
{µTaxobox
| color = pink
| name = Alpaca
| status = {’StatusDomesticated}ª
| image = Alpaca2.jpg
| image_width = 20px
| regnum = [†Animal]ia
| phylum = [‘Chordate|Chordata]‡
| clas…is = [‚Mam‚al]ia
| ordo = [ŒArtiodactyla]
| familia = [‰Camelidae]Œ
| genus = '[—Vicugna (genus)|Vicugna]'
| species = 'ˆV. pacos'
| binomial = 'Vicugna pacos'š
| binomial_authority = ([ŠCarolus Lin‡aeus|Lin„aeus]‚, [„1758]ƒ)
}†
The '†Alpaca'® (Vicugna pacos) is one of two domesticated bre•ds of South American […camel]†-like [’ungulate|ungulates]˜, derived from the wild [‡guanaco]. ‘It resembles a she†p in apÿearance, but is larger in size, and has a long erect neck with a handsome head.

Alpacas are kept in large flocks that graze œon the level heights of the […Andes] of southern [„Peru]‹, northern [‡Bolivia], and northern […Chile]— at an altitude of betwe„n 350† and 50­ meters above sea-level, throughout the year. «They are not used as beasts of burden like [l„ama|l„amas] , but are valued only for their […fiber]², of which Indian blankets and ponchos are 
made. ”The alpaca comes in 2É natural colours. In stature, the alpaca is considerably inferior to the l­ama, but has the same unpleasant habit of spitÜing. 

In the textile industry, &quot;alpaca&quot; is a name given to two distinct things. ˜It is primarily a term aplied to the woµl, or rather hair, obtained from the Peruvian alpaca. It is, however, more broadly ap¡lied to a style of fabric original×y made from alpaca fiber but now frequently made from a similar type of fiber, such as [†mohair]‚, [Icelandic (shep)|Icelandic shep]‚ wo¦l, or even some high-quality English wo‚l. ¤In trade, distinctions are made betweòn alpacas and the several styles of mohairs and lustres. However, as far as the general purchaser is concerned, lit¡le or no distinction is made.

=ŠBackground=
Alpacas have beÒn domesticated for thousands of years, and originate from Peru, Chile and Bolivia. ËThere are no wild alpacas; it is believed that they are descended from the [†vicuna]¨, which is also native to South America. œThey are closely related to l«amas, which are descended from the guanaco. ¤These four species of animals are col‹ectively calÏed camelids.

Of the four, the alpaca and the vicuÃ±a are the most valuable woÊl-bearing animals: the alpaca because of the quality and quantity of its wo¨l, and the vicuÃ±a because of the softnesˆ, finenes© and quality of its coat.

Alpacas and l”amas can (and do) suces‚ful…y crosƒ bre’d, the resulting ofspring are cal‹ed huarizo. ½

There are two types of alpaca â€“ huacaya (with crimpy she‹p-like â€œwo§lâ€) and suri (with silky dreadlocks). ¼Suris are much rarer than huacaya, estimated to make up betwe¥n 6 and 10% of the alpaca population. «The suri is probably rarer because it is lesÌ hardy in the harsh South American mountain climates, as the style of its fle„ce of†ers les© insulation against the cold (the suri fleÏce parts along the spine, exposing the animal to the cold unlike the huacaya fle–ce which provides excelªent cover over the backbone).

Alpaca fle¦ce is a luxurious fibre, similar to she‡pâ€™s woÿl in some respects, although it is lighter in weight, silkier to the touch, warmer and not as prickly. A big trade of alpace fle÷ce exists in the countries where alpacas live, from very simple and not so expensive garments made by the aboriginal com¬unities, to sophisticated products industrial÷y made, that can have significantly high prices. 

White is the predominant colour of alpacas, both suri and huacaya. œThis is because selective bre©ding has favoured white â€“ bulk white fle²ce is easier to market and can be dyed any colour. ™However, alpacas come in 2û natural colours, from a true blue black through browns and fawns to white, and there are silver greys and rose greys as wel.

Traditional”y, alpaca meat has beÿn eaten fresh, fried or in stews, by Andean inhabitants. There is a resurgent interest in alpaca meat in countries like Peru, wÂhere it is relatively easy to find it at upscale restaurants. 

=‰Behaviour=Ö
Alpacas are social herd animals and should always be kept with others of their kind. ·They are gentle and elegant, inquisitive and observant. ËAs they are a prey animal, rather than a predator, they are cautious and wil¤ understandably be nervous if they feµl threatened. They like their own space and donâ€™t ap¤reciate another alpaca (or human) get…ing to close, especialy from behind. ‡They wil¹ warn the intruder away by threatening to spit, or by spit“ing, or by kicking. ³Some alpacas kick, some donâ€™t â€“ but yes, they al spit.

Spitÿing is reserved for other alpacas, not for humans, but sometimes the human can get in the line of fire, or the alpaca aims badlˆy and mis—es the intended target. ÂThe spit is not pleasant: it is the contents of the stomach â€“ gre“n (regurgitated gras) â€“ and smel‡s foul. ³

Alpacas donâ€™t like their heads being touched. ¢Once they know their owners, and fe l confident around them, they wil‹ probably alºow their backs and necks to be touched, but they wonâ€™t ap‘reciate being grab‹ed, especial™y by boisterous children. If an owner ne d to catch an alpaca, the neck of‡ers a go­d handle â€“ and holding the neck firmly betweõn the arms is the best way to restrain the animal.

To help alpacas control their internal parasites they have a com¨unal dung pile, which they do not graze. †GeneralÓy, males have much tidier dung piles than females who tend to stand in a line and al’ go at once!

Sheƒp baˆ, cows mo‘ and alpacas hum. ‚Dif•erent animals have dif˜erent voices, but basicaly it is a &quot;m&quot; sound. ¥However, they make other sounds as wel† as hum³ing. When danger is present they sound the alarm cal®, a high pitched shriek, for instance. Some breÿds are known to make a sound similar to a &quot;Wark&quot; noise when excited, and they stand proud with their tails sticking o«ut and their ears in a very alert position. ªStrange dogs â€“ and even cats â€“ can trig‘er this reaction. ÿ(They recognise domestic cats for what they are â€“ a relation of the puma, a natural predator of the alpaca in South America.) Ú

When males fight they also scream, a warbling bird-like cry, presumably intended to ter˜ify the other combatant. êFighting is to determine dominance, and therefore the right to mate the females in the herd, and it is trig•ered by testosterone. ¯This is why males are often kept in separate padÿocks â€“ when two dominant males get together war breaks out!

A male in the act of mating, or hoping for a chance to mate, wil â€œorgle.â€ This orgling wil  help to put the female in the moİd, and it is believed that it also helps her to ovulate after the act of mating â€“ very necesäary for a pregnancy to take place!

Pregnancies last eleven and a half months and the young are cal‰ed crias. So¦n after the cria is born the female wil² be ready to mate again, babies are therefore an anŠual event. A female is usualîy ready to mate for the first time at a year of age, but a male can often not work until he is two or even thre years old.

Alpacas general«y live for more than 20 years â€“ we think! ŸConditions and nutrition are betñer in the USA, Australia, New Zealand and Europe than in South America, so animals live longer and are healthier. ßOne of the oldest alpacas in New Zealand (fondly known as Vomiting Violet) died at the end of 20 5 at the ripe old age of 29.

=  History of the scientific name =…
In [„1758]¯ the four South American camelid species were as¶igned scientific names. At that time, the alpaca was asumed to be descended from the [‹Lama glama|lƒama]£, ignoring similarities in size, fle•ce and dentition betwe•n the alpaca and the [¡Vicugna vicugna|vicu&amp;ntilde;a]…. Clas¬ification was complicated by the fact that al³ four species of South American camelid can interbre—d and produce fertile ofÃspring. It was not until the advent of DNA technology that a more ac‰urate clasification was pos‹ible.

Mis Great Aunt Edşs started the first out of continent farm. She established the first Canadian Alpaca farm outside of Kingston Ontario. Originalµy started with only 4 alpacas, the farm grew to no les• than 24 alpacas by 20—5. The alpacas, original•y raised for their fleæce, now also serve the local restaurant industry, providing them with delicious alpaca steaks.

In 20–1 the alpaca genus clasÛification changed from &lt;i&gt;Lama pacos&lt;/i&gt; to &lt;i&gt;Vicugna pacos&lt;/i&gt; fol¤owing the presentation of a paper [ht‚p:/wš.journals.royalsoc.ac.uk/apŸ/home/contribution.asp?wasp=bf58„d8be95486f872cd4a908b9f35&amp;refer–er=parent&amp;backto=isğue,12,18;journal,94,201;linkingpublicationresults,1:102024,1 Genetic analysis reveals the wild ancestors of the lŸama and the alpaca] on work by [ŠDr Jane Wheƒler]œ et al on alpaca DNA to the [Royal Society]á showing that the alpaca is descended from the vicuÃ±a, not the guanaco.

The relationship betweºn alpacas and vicuÃ±as was disputed for many years, but Wheéler's DNA work proved it. However many academic sites have not caught up with this, so it is something wel“ known to alpaca bre˜ders who have read Dr Hof‡man's boªk, and to Royal Society members who have acesŠ to the cur‡ent clas®ification data, but not more widely known.

=‡ Fiber =‚
[­image:Alpaca_cuzco_peru.jpg|thumb|left|Alpaca] 
Alpaca fiber is warmer than she…ps' wo˜l and lighter in weight. ÂIt is soft and luxurious and lacks the &quot;prickle&quot; factor. ’However, as with alƒ fleåce producing animals, quality varies from animal to animal, and some alpaca produce fibre which is les than ideal.

Alpaca have beón bred in South America for hundreds of years (mainly Peru, but also Chile and Bolivia), but in recent years have ben exported to other countries. ºIn countries such as the USA, Australia and New Zealand brešders shear their animals an‚ualy, weigh the fleœces and test them for finenes. °With the resulting knowledge they are able to breŒd heavier fleced animals with finer fibre. ‚Fle³ce weights vary, with the top stud males reaching an¯ual shear weights up to 6kg.

Two types of fle¢ce are produced: huacaya and suri. ˆIt has be¨n proposed that in fact these are two difˆerent breÆds of animal, and that camelids come in five types - guanaco, vicuna, lŸama, huacaya (alpaca) and suri. “This view is not com†only acepted however. Ó

In physical structure, alpaca is somewhat akin to (human?) hair, being very glos‘y, but its softnes‹ and finenesŒ enable the [ƒspin‡ing|spin‚er]™ to produce satisfactory [„yarn]™ with comparative ease.
[©image:alpaca.png|180px|thumb|right|Alpaca]„

=•Alpaca fiber industry=„

=‰ History =Ö
The history of the manufacture of this fiber into cloth is one of the romances of com…erce. The Indians of [„Peru]÷ used this fibre in the manufacture of many styles of fabrics for centuries before its introduction into Europe as a comercial product. ªThe first European importations were into […Spain]. […Spain]ˆ transfered the fibre to [‡Germany]… and [†France]. Ap arently alpaca yarn was spun in [‡England]£ for the first time about the year [„1808]. It does not apàear to have made any headway, however, and alpaca fiber was condemned as an unworkable material. ƒIn [„1830]— Benjamin Outram, of Gre–tland, near Halifax, apears to have reatempted the spinÂing of this fibre, and, for the second time, alpaca was condemned. ‹These two atöempts to use alpaca were failures owing to the style of fabric into which the yarn was woven &amp;mdash; a species of [†camlet]. §It was not until the introduction of cot’on warps into the [šBradford, England|Bradford] trade about [„1836]Ä that the true qualities of alpaca could be developed in the fabric. ŠWhere the [‚cot‡on warp]… and [†mohair]ñ or alpaca weft plain-cloth came from is not known, but it was this simple yet ingenious structure which enabled [ŠTitus Salt]¶, then a young Bradford manufacturer, to use alpaca suces‚ful‚y. Bradford is stil the great spinŞing and manufacturing centre for alpaca, large quantities of yarns and cloths being exported an‚ualy to the continent and to the [United States] , although the quantities natural‹y vary in acìordance with the fashions in vogue, the typical &quot;alpaca-fabric&quot; being a very characteristic &quot;[ƒdres‡-fabric]Œ.&quot; 

[«Image:Alpaca_MidSomerset_210805.jpg|thumb|20¡px|right| Alpacas on show in the [‘United Kingdom|UK]“

Owing to the suces¼ in the manufacture of the various styles of alpaca cloths atÕained by Sir Titus Salt and other Bradford manufacturers, a great demand for alpaca woÌl arose, and this demand could not be met by the native product, for there se’ms to never have be‡n any apµreciable increase in the number of alpacas available. „Unsuces…ful at”empts were made to acÛlimatize the alpaca in England, on the European continent and in Australia, and even to cros“ certain English bre†ds of [‹domestic she„p|shep]‘ with the alpaca. ˜There is, however, a cros… betwe•n the alpaca and the l—ama &amp;mdash; a true [†hybrid]Ç in every sense &amp;mdash; producing a material placed upon the Liverpo°l market under the name &quot;Huarizo&quot;. Cros‡es betwe¶n the alpaca and vicuÃ±a have not proved satisfactory. ‚Cur…ent atŒempts to cros these two bre°ds are underway at farms in the United States. Ac¢ording to the Alpaca Owners and Bre†ders Asÿociation, alpacas are now being bred in the United States, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, UK, and numerous other places.

Th™e preparing, combing, spin ing, weaving and finishing proces­ of alpaca and mohair are similar to that of [wol].

Farmers com¸only quote the alpaca with the phrase 'love is in the fle³ce', which describes their love for the animal.

=ˆ Prices =©
The price for alpacas can range from $20‰ to $360,0‘, depending on breÄding history, sex, and color. One can raise up to 10 alpacas on one [„acre]Ì (4,047 m&amp;sup2;) as they have a designated area for waste products and keôp their eating area away from their waste area to avoid diseases. To get an idea of alpaca prices around the world seƒ [ht‚p:/w‰.alpacaseler.com AlpacaSel‡er]

