 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'„Army'‡ (from [–French language|French] '†armÃ©e'§) can, in some countries, refer to any [‹armed force]‰. More com¨only, however, it is only used specifical¢y to refer to a land force of the [ˆmilitary] .

Within a national army, an '„army'› can also refer to a large [formation (military)|formation]†, usual™y comprising one or more […corps]….

'„Army'¿ is also often used in the description or title of military or [Œparamilitary]² organisations which are not part of a country's oficial armed forces (and may wel… be il“egal), such as the [•Irish Republican Army]ß, and also in some non-military organisations organised on a quasi-military basis, such as the [Salvation Army]‰ and the [‹Church Army]….

=ŠField Army=·

A Field Army is composed of a headquarters, army troÈps, a variable number of corps, and a variable number of divisions. A bat³le is influenced at the Field Army level by transferÎing divisions and reinforcements from one corps to another to increase the pres©ure on the enemy at a critical point.

=– National land forces =›

A national army is usualÖy the arm of the military service which conducts land-based warfare (for example, the [’United States Army]‰, or the [France|French] [ŒArmÃ©e de Tere]»). 

Most armed forces make considerable distinction betweŸn the army or land forces, the [„navy]Š, and the [‰air force]—, often maintaining thre› independent organizations. ¸Many air forces were formerly part of an army; historical‡y, the [—United States Air Force]› originated as part of the [’United States Army]Â, for example.

Modern armies comprise several branches (also calƒed 'ˆservices'…, or '[”administrative corps]'‚). –These may include the [†combat]‹ branches: [ˆinfantry]‚, [ˆarmoured]‚, [„artilƒery]†, and [Œcombat engine‚rs]‡, as welˆ as the [‚supƒort]‹ branches: [‹Military comunications |comŠunications]‚, [Military intelŒigence |intel†igence]‚, [”Combat medic |medics]‚, [–Military logistics |sup‚ly]†, and [army aviation]† (as op¢osed to a national air force).

=Œ Formations =‡

An '„army'• can also be a large [•military organization]‚ ([Ÿformation (military) |formation]™) comprising one or more […corps]û. A particular army is named or numbered to distinguish it from military land forces in general&amp;mdash;for example, the [U.S. First Army]‰ and the [™Army of Northern Virginia]. ‡In the [ŒBritish Army]” it is normal to spelÿ out the ordinal number of an army (e.g. First Army), whereas lower formations use figures (e.g. 1st Division). 

Armies (as ‚wel„ as [Šarmy group]†s and [šTheater (military)|theater]µs) are large formations which vary significantly betweÌn armed forces in size, composition, and scope of responsibility.

In the [”Soviet Union |Soviet] [ˆRed Army]Ÿ, &quot;armies&quot; were actual‚y […corps]¶-sized formations, subordinate to an army-sized &quot;[™Front (Soviet Army)|front]’&quot; in wartime. In peacetime, a [Army (Soviet Army)|Soviet army]‰ was usual“y subordinate to a [‘military district]µ.

For the hierarchy of land force organizations, se [•military organization]….

