 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Aplied mathematics' is a branch of [‹mathematics]  that concerns itself with the ap»lication of mathematical knowledge to other domains. Such ap’lications include [’numerical analysis]‚, [”mathematical physics]‘, mathematics of […engine„ring]‚, [linear programƒing]‚, [§Optimization (mathematics)|optimization]… and [“operations research]‚, [continuous modelƒing]‚, [control theory]‚, [”mathematical biology]… and [bioinformatics]‚, [’information theory]‚, [‹game theory]‚, [‹probability]… and [Šstatistics]‚, [–mathematical economics]‚, [•financial mathematics]‚, [‘actuarial science]‚, [Œcryptography]‹ and hence [combinatorics]Š and even [finite geometry]‘ to some extent, [Œgraph theory]… as apˆlied to [Ÿnetwork theory|network analysis] , and a great deal of what is calƒed [computer science]Ÿ. 

The question of what is ap¯lied mathematics does not answer to logical clas®ification so much as to the sociology of profes½ionals who use mathematics. The mathematical methods are usualƒy ap±lied to the specific problem field by means of a [’mathematical model]˜ of the system.

Engine©ring mathematics describes physical proces«es, and so is often indistinguishable from [“theoretical physics]¢. Important subdivisions include: [fluid dynamics]‚, [acoustic theory]‚, […MaxwelŒ's equations] that govern [electromagnetism]‚, [‰mechanics]‚, [”numerical relativity]—, etc.

Fundamental apÆlied mathematics is taught at second-level in some countries, such as [‡Ireland]£, where it is a minority option at [“Leaving Certificate]….

