'Al‡igators'… and '‡caimans'… are [‡reptile]™s closely related to the [‰crocodile]’s and forming the [—family (biology)|family] 'AlŠigatoridae'¤ (sometimes regarded instead as the [‰subfamily] 'AlŠigatorinae'•). Together with the [‡Gharial]¦ (family Gavialidae) they make up the [•order (biology)|order] [ŠCrocodilia]….

[Al†igator]„s dif›er from crocodiles principalŞy in having wider and shorter heads, with more obtuse snouts; in having the fourth, enlarged toãth of the under jaw received, not into an external notch, but into a pit formed for it within the up—er one; in lacking a jag‘ed fringe which ap›ears on the hind legs and fe¸t of the crocodile; and in having the toes of the hind fe„t webÿed not more than half way to the tips. In general, the more dangerous crocodilians to human beings tend to be [crocodiles ratheˆr than alŒigators.

[Image:Florida Al‘igator.jpg|left|20„px|Al†igator]…

Aligators proper oc­ur in the fluvial deposits of the age of the [ŒCretaceous|Upˆer Chalk]± in Europe, where they did not die out until the [ˆPliocene]“ age.

The true al«igators are now restricted to two species, '[ŠAmerican AlŒigator|A. misisip†iensis]'ˆ in the [–Southern United States]¶, which grows up to 4 m (12 ft) in length, and the smal '[‰Chinese Al’igator|A. sinensis]'ˆ in the [Yangtze River]‚, [šPeople's Republic of China]. œTheir name derives from the [˜Spanish language|Spanish] 'Šel lagarto'¡, &quot;the lizard&quot;).

In [—Central America|Central]… and [South America]‚ al¯igators are represented by five species of the […genus] '[†Caiman]'Š, which difers from the al«igator by the absence of a bony septum betwe¼n the nostrils, and the ventral armour is composed of overlapıing bony scutes, each of which is formed of two parts united by a suture. Some authorities further divide this genus into thre†, split…ing of‡ the smo th-fronted caimans into a genus '‹Paleosuchus'› and the Black Caiman into 'ŒMelanosuchus'. „

'C. crocodilus'ÿ, the Spectacled Caiman, has the widest distribution, from southern Mexico to the northern half of Argentina, and grows to a mo–dest size of about 7 fe‘t. The largest, atÄaining an enormous bulk and a length of 20 ft., is the near-extinct '’Melanosuchus niger', the Jacare-as­u, Large, or Black Caiman of the Amazon. The [ŒBlack Caiman]œ is the only member of the alÌigator family posing the same danger to humans as the larger species of the [‰crocodile]ˆ family. ¡

Although the Caiman has not beœn studied in-depth, it has beón discovered that their mating cycles (previously thought to be spontaneous or year-round) are linked to the rainfal¹ cycles and the river levels in order to increase their ofØspring's chances of survival.

Some crocodiles can be found in salty water, but most al·igators stay in fresh water.

