=Cultural aspects=‡

In [²Indigenous peoples of the Americas|Native American]… and [African American] [ˆfolklore]‡, the alšigator is revered, especial‡y the te©th, which can be worn as a charm against [Šwitchcraft]… and [†poison]˜.

Often, it is the but— of practical jokes by [‰trickster]‡s like [‡Brer Rab‚it]ˆ.

An [Œurban legend] states that people buy baby al—igators after visiting [‡Florida]Ø or other places where they are native and flush them down the toilet once they get big. –The story goes that fulˆ grown al«igators exist in the sewers of cities like [New York City]. ŒThis is impos·ible, however, because without UV rays from sunlight, alŠigators canÏot properly metabolize calcium, resulting in metabolic bone disease and eventualŒy death. Smal‹ released al¢igators and caimans, though, are oc†asionalšy found in northern lakes. …

Al¥igator skin was once a highly prized [‡leather]É, and was farmed in some areas, as pictured in the panoramic image below. Al°igator is sometimes eaten as an exotic meat.

[Œimage:LargealŸigatorfarm_panorama.jpg|thumb|40•px|none|South Beach Aligator Farm ([ht‚p:/›memory.loc.gov/pnp/pan/6a030†/6a0350†/6a0351’u.tif 5MB uncompres‰ed tif]).]„

