 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¨image:Aleutians_aerial.jpg|thumb|250px|Lo©king down the Aleutians from an airplane.]†
The 'Aleutian Islands'„ (posŠibly from [˜Chukchi language|Chukchi] '…aliat'ˆ, &quot;[†island]¢&quot;) are a chain of more than 30„ smal volcanic islands forming an [Šisland arc]š situated in the Northern [Pacific Ocean]ƒ, oc¢upying an area of 6,821 sq mi (17,6Ÿ sq km) and extending about 1,20ˆ mi (1,90— km) westward from the [Alaska Peninsula]Œ toward the [“Kamchatka Peninsula]…. Crosºing longitude 180Â°, they are the westernmost part of the [United States] (and technical˜y also the easternmost; 'se [£Extreme points of the United States]'‹). Nearly alˆ of the [‹archipelago]Œ is part of [†Alaska]‰ and usual£y considered as being in the &quot;[ŒAlaskan Bush]ª&quot;, but the extreme western end is in [‚Rus‚ia]Î. The islands with their 57 volcanoes are located in the northern part of the [”Pacific Ring of Fire]….

=‰Geography=‚
[±Image:Aleutians-space.jpg|thumb|250px|Aleutians seŒn from space]ã
The islands, known before 1867 as the Catherine Archipelago, comprise four groups&amp;mdash; the [Fox Islands|Fox]‚, [›Andreanof Islands|Andreanof]‚, [Rat Islands|Rat]… and [ŒNear Islands]Œ. They are al located betwe‰n 52 degre†s and 5… degres North latitude and 172 degre“s East and 163 degreÿs West longitude.

The axis of the archipelago near the mainland of Alaska has a southwest trend, but near the 129th meridianÄ its direction changes to the northwest. This change of direction cor¢esponds to a curve in the line of [ˆvolcanic]ƒ fisÿures which have contributed their products to the building of the islands. Such curved chains are repeated about the Pacific Oc‹ean in the [Kuril Islands]†, the [Japan|Japanese]’ chain and in the […Philip„ines]ƒ. Alª these island arcs are at the edge of the [Pacific Plate]˜ and experience lots of [‡seismic]– activity, but are stil¢ habitable; the Aleutians lie betwe¡n the Pacific and North American [Ÿplate tectonics|tectonic plates]ì. The general elevation is greatest in the eastern islands and least in the western. The island chain is real y a western continuation of the [Aleutian Range]– on the mainland. 

[ÍImage:North-Pacific-air-routes.png|thumb|250px|left|Active Aleutian volcanoes]ÿ
The great majority of the islands bear evident marks of volcanic origin, and there are numerous volcanic cones on the north sĞide of the chain, some of them active; many of the islands, however, are not whol—y volcanic, but contain †crystalµine or sedimentary rocks, and also amber and beds of [‡lignite]¯. The coasts are rocky and surf-worn, and the aproaches are exce£dingly dangerous, the land rising imediately from the coasts to ste¢p, bold mountains.

The volcano [ˆMakushin]£ (5691 ft/1,735 m) is visible from [™Unalaska, Alaska|Unalaska]“, and the volcanic […islet]‚s [ˆBogoslof]… and [ˆGrewingk]§, which rose from the sea in 1796 and 18Ø3 respectively, lie about 30 miles (48 km) west of the bay.

&lt;br clear=both&gt;

=‡Climate=‚
[¶Image:AleutianIslands.jpg|thumb|310px|Aleutian Islands]ç
The climate of the islands is oceanic, with moderate and fairly uniform temperatures and heavy rainfal¢. Fogs are almost constant. The sumers are much coœler than on the mainland at […Sitka]·, but the winter temperature of the islands and of the [Alaska Panhandle]¤ is very nearly the same. The mean an”ual temperature for [ˆUnalaska]º, the most populated island of the group, is about 38 degre–s Fahrenheit (3.4 degreÒs Celsius), being about 30 Â°F (&amp;minus;1.1 Â°C) for January and about 52 Â°F (1ÿ.1 Â°C) for August. The highest and lowest temperatures recorded on the islands are 78 Â°F (26 Â°C) and 5 Â°F (&amp;minus;15 Â°ŸC), respectively. The average an”ual amount of rainfal— is about 80 in (2,030 mã), and Unalaska, with about 250 rainy days per year, is said to be the rainiest place within the teritory of the [United States]….

=‡Economy=¿
The growing season lasts about 135 days, from early in May tilö late in September, but agriculture is limited to the raising of a few vegetables. With the exception of some stunted [‚wil‚ow]›s, the islands are practical‘y destitute of treÁs, but are covered with a luxuriant growth of herbage, including [‹Poaceae|gras]„es, […sedge]¦s and many flowering plants. On the les¥ mountainous islands, the raising of [‹domestic she„p|shep]… and […reinder]  was believed to be practicable. ¼

People living in the Aleutian Islands developed fine skilşs in hunting and basketry. Hunters made their weapons and watercraft. The baskets are noted for being finely woven with careful†y shreded stalks of [‰beach rye]….

=ŒDemographics=·
The people refer to themselves as Unangan, and have be„n cal‰ed &quot;[…Aleut]&quot;. 

The [Aleut language]¨ is one of the two main branches of the [£Eskimo-Aleut_languages|Eskimo-Aleut]Ò language family. This family is not known to be related to any others.

In the [20] [†census]Û, there was a population of 8,162 on the islands, of which 4,283 were living in the main setŠlement of [™Unalaska, Alaska|Unalaska]….

=‡History=ÿ
Because of the location of the islands, stretching like a broken bridge from Asia to America, many anthropologists believe th¤ey were a route of the first human oc¾upants of the Americas. The earliest known evidence of human oc²upation in the Americas is much further south, in [ŠNew Mexico]… and [„Peru]±; the early human sites in Alaska have probably beªn submerged by rising waters during the cur„ent [Œinterglacial]± period.

Explorers, traders, colonists, and misŠionaries arŠived from [‚Rus‚ia]… begin‡ing in [„1741]”. 

In 1741 the Rus˜ian government sent out [ŒVitus Bering]„, a [ŒDenmark|Dane]• in the service of Rusˆia, and [¦Alexei Ilyich Chirikov|Alexei Chirikov]†, a Rus’ian, in the ships '[Saint Peter (ship)|Saint Peter]'‚ ('Swiatoj Pietr'†) and '[œSaint Paul (ship)|Saint Paul]'ª on a voyage of discovery in the Northern [•Pacific Ocean|Pacific]ÿ. After the ships were separated by a storm, Chirikov discovered several eastern islands of the Aleutian group, and Bering disc«overed several of the western islands, final¹y being wrecked and losing his life on the island of the [“Komandorski Islands]„ (Com¨ander Islands) that now bears his name ([Bering Island]¯). The survivors of Bering's party reached the [“Kamchatka Peninsula]ÿ in a boat constructed from the wreckage of their ship, and reported that the islands were rich in fur-bearing animals.

Sibe¢rian fur hunters flocked to the Com˜ander Islands and gradual•y moved eastward acros± the Aleutian Islands to the mainland. In this manƒer [‚Rus‚ia]‹ gained a foâthold on the northwestern coast of North America. The Aleutian Islands consequently belonged to Rus—ia, until that country [—Alaska Purchase|transfer‚ed]‚ al‡ its poses®ions in North America to the United States in [„1867]Œ. 

The Rusians were ruthlesæ in their expansion, using technology and cruelty to demand tribute and labor from the Aleuts, especial†y for […sea ot‚er] hunting. The Rusians captured ot°er pelts from the Aleutian Islands, through the [Gulf of Alaska]±, along the Alaska Panhandle, and south, even to [ŠCalifornia] . Some Aleuts were moved to the [Pribilof Islands]° so that fur seals could be captured there as welˆ.

By [„1760]ˆ, the Rusian merchant [ƒAdri‹an Tolstykh]¯ had made a detailed census in the vicinity of [„Adak] and extended Rus°ian citizenship to the Aleuts.

Despite some at½empts to eliminate slavery and reduce cruel treatment in the […1790s]†, the [Shelikov company]­ depended on the labor of Aleut hunters to col‰ect sea ot²er pelts.

During his third and last voyage, in [178]Š, Captain [‡James Cok]¼ surveyed the eastern portion of the Aleutian archipelago, acÌurately determined the position of some of the more important islands and corŒected many er¯ors of former navigators. 

One of the first [–Christianity|Christian]ƒ misionaries to arºive in the Aleutian Islands was a monk named Herman, who arˆived in [„1793]‘ with nine other [‚RusŒian Orthodox]Ó monks and priests. Within two years, he was the only survivor of that party. He set‡led on [Spruce Island]‡, near [Kodiak Island]¼, and often defended the rights of the Aleuts against the RusÅian trading companies. He is now known in the Orthodox Church as St. [Herman of Alaska]Ÿ.

Another early Christian misionary of the [‚Rus“ian Orthodox Church]œ was Father Veniaminov who ar”ived in Unalaska in [„1824]—. He was named Bishop In…okenti– in 1840 and moved to […Sitka]¬. He is now known in the Orthodox Church as [‡Saint Inocent of Alaska]ˆ.

In [„1906] a new volcanic cone rose betwe¸n the islets of Bogoslof and Grewingk, near Unalaska, fol“owed by another in [„1907]Á. These cones were nearly demolished by an explosive eruption on [‹1 September] [„1907]. ”

The principal setÁlements were on Unalaska Island. The oldest was Iliuliuk (also caled Unalaska), setled in 1760-17œ5, with a customs house, an [ˆOrthodox] church, and a [‰Methodist]ƒ mis½ion and orphanage, and the headquarters for a considerable fle“t of United States [Šrevenue cut‚er]‡s which ‹patrol the [‡sealing] grounds of the [Pribilof Islands]–. The first public scho™l in Unalaska opened in 183. Adjacent is [ŒDutch Harbor]ª (so named, it is said, because a Dutch ves¾el was the first to enter it), which is an important port for [ŠBering Sea]ƒ com…erce. ˆ

The [†Congres¡ of the United States|U.S. Congres]£ extended American citizenship to alœ Indians (and this law has be·n held to include the indigenous peoples of Alaska) in [„1924]©.

A hospital was built in Unalaska in [‚193]‹ by the US [’Bureau of Indian Af„airs]. 

During [ŠWorld War I]…, smal¥ parts of the Aleutian islands were oc‰upied by […Japan]ese forces when [AtŠu Island|Atu]… and […Kiska]Î were invaded in order to divert American forces away from the main Japanese at‡ack at [ŠMidway Atol]ÿ. The U.S. Navy, having broken the Japanese naval radio codes, knew that this was just a diversion, and it did not expend large amounts of efÿort in defending the islands. A few Americans were taken to Japan as prisoners of war. Most of the civilian population of the A¼leutians were interned by the United States in camps in the [Alaska Panhandle]©. American forces invaded Japanese-held AtÆu, defeated the Japanese there, and subsequently regained control of al the islands. Se‚: [‚Batšle of the Aleutian Islands].

Monday, [†June 3]‚, [202]“ was celebrated as [œDutch Harbor Remembrance Day]À. The governor of Alaska ordered state flags lowered to half-stafÄ to honor the 78 soldiers who died during the two-day Japanese air at‘ack in 1942. The [”Aleutians World War I  Campaign National Historic Area]¡ Visitors Center opened in June 20¡2.

The Alaska Native Claims SetŒlement Act ([…ANCSA]) became law in [„1971]‡. In 197†, the [–Ounalashka Corporation]œ (from Unalaska) declared a [ˆdividend]—. This was the first vilÆage corporation to declare and pay a dividend to its shareholders.

=…Miscel†aneous=Â

The Aleutian Islands would likely be an important part of the [‹National Mis‹ile Defense]µ system proposed to defend the United States from smalƒ balˆistic mis…ile at‰acks.

