'…Alder'Š is the comon name of a […genus]„ of [flowering plant]ƒs ('…Alnus'¨) belonging to the birch family (Family [ŠBetulaceae] ). The genus comprises about 30 [‡species]„ of [šPlant sexuality|monoecious] [‚tre]†s and […shrub]Øs, few reaching large size, distributed throughout the North Temperate zone, and in the [‰New World] also along the […Andes] southwards to […Chile]†. The [‹leaf|leaves]… are [‰deciduous]‚ ([†evergren]º or nearly so in a few species), alternate, simple, and serŠated. The [†flower]†s are [†catkin]ßs with elongate male catkins on the same plant as shorter female catkins, often before leaves apœear; they are mainly wind-polœinated, but also visited by [be]Šs to a smal extent. They difŒer from the […birch]„es ('†Betula'Á, the other genus in the family) in that the female catkins are woÀdy and do not disintegrate at maturity, opening to release the se’ds in a similar man‹er to many [šConifer cone|conifer cones]¥.

The best-known species is the Com†on or [‹Black Alder]‚ ('ŒA. glutinosa'•), native to most of [†Europe]¹ and widely introduced elsewhere. The largest species is [‰Red Alder]‚ ('ˆA. rubra'˜), reaching 35 m (the tal¢est is 32 m) on the west coast of [North America]—, with Black Alder and [Italian Alder]‚ ('ŠA. cordata'¸) both reaching about 30 m. By contrast, the widespread [‚Gre‡n Alder]‚ ('ŠA. viridis'¬) is rarely more than a 5 m shrub.

The comˆon name '…alder'˜ is derived from an old [šGermanic_language|Germanic]‚ ro”t. The botanic name '…Alnus'‘ is the original […Latin]‹ name. 

=ƒClas‰ification=Ÿ
The genus is divided into thre subgenera:

'‰Subgenus '…Alnus'.'ƒ Tre…s. Sho¾t buds stalked. Male and female catkins produced in autumn (fal¤) but staying closed over winter, polÊinating in late winter or early spring. About 15-25 species, including:
*'ŒA. acuminata'ƒ - [ŒAndean Alder]¤. Andes Mountains, South America.
*'ŠA. cordata'ƒ - [Italian Alder]‹. Italy.
*'Alnus formosana'‚ -[Formosan Alder]ƒ
*'ŒA. glutinosa'ƒ - [‹Black Alder]Œ. Europe.
*'‰A. incana'ƒ - [ŠGrey Alder]–. Europe &amp; Asia.
*'A. oblongifolia'‚ ('‰A. incana'ˆ subsp. 'Œoblongifolia'„) - [˜Grey Alder|Arizona Alder]Ÿ. Southwestern North America.
*'‰A. rugosa'‚ ('‰A. incana'ˆ subsp. '†rugosa'„) - [™Grey Alder|Speckled Alder]Ÿ. Northeastern North America.
*'A. tenuifolia'‚ ('‰A. incana'ˆ subsp. 'Štenuifolia'„) - [™Grey Alder|Thinleaf Alder]² or Mountain Alder. Northwestern North America.
*'‹A. japonica'ƒ - [Japanese Alder]‹. Japan.
*'‡A. jorul…ensis'ƒ - [Mexican Alder]—. Mexico, Guatemala.
*'A. orientalis'ƒ - [Oriental Alder]®. Southern Turkey, northwest Syria, Cyprus.
*'A. rhombifolia'ƒ - [‹White Alder]¤. Interior western North America.
*'ˆA. rubra'ƒ - [‰Red Alder] . West coastal North America.
*'…A. ser…ulata' - Hazel alder, [‰Tag Alder]† or Smo th alder. Eastern North America. ƒ
*'A. subcordata'ƒ - [Caucasian Alder]•. Caucasus, Iran.

'‰Subgenus '‹Clethropsis'.'ƒ Tres or shrubs. Sho¾t buds stalked. Male and female catkins produced in autumn (fal–) and expanding and pol‘inating then. ThreŒ species:
*'‹A. maritima'ƒ - [Seaside Alder]Æ. East coastal North America, plus disjunct population in Oklahoma.
*'A. nepalensis'ƒ - [Nepalese Alder]§. Eastern Himalaya, southwest China.
*'‰A. nitida'ƒ - [Himalayan Alder]—. Western Himalaya.

[Image:Alnus ser«ulata leaves.jpg|right|thumb|Leaves of the [‰Tag Alder]‚
'‰Subgenus 'ŠAlnobetula'.'‹ Shrubs. ShoÓt buds not stalked. Male and female catkins produced in late spring (after leaves ap™ear) and expanding and pol¥inating then. One to four species:
*'ŠA. viridis'ƒ - [‚Gre‡n Alder]. Widespread:
*'ŠA. viridis'ˆ subsp. '‡viridis'Œ. Eurasia.
*'ŠA. viridis'ˆ subsp. 'Šmaximowiczi'‚ ('A. maximowiczi'‹). Japan.
*'ŠA. viridis'ˆ subsp. '†crispa'‚ ('‰A. crispa'œ). Northern North America.
*'ŠA. viridis'ˆ subsp. '‡sinuata'‚ ('ŠA. sinuata'‚, [‹Sitka Alder]Æ or Slide Alder). Western North America, far northeastern Siberia.

=„Uses=‚
[¨Image:Alder female 8519.JPG|right|thumb|'ˆAlnus ser…ulata' (Tag Alder), female catkins, [‰Johnsonvil‘e, South Carolina]“
Alders establish [“symbiosis|symbioses]Š with the [ˆnitrogen]ˆ-fixing [Actinobacteria] '‡Frankiel†a alni'À. This bacteria converts atmospheric nitrogen into soil-soluble [‡nitrate]Ös which can be utilised by the alder, and favorably enhances the soil fertility generalÿy. Alders benefit other plants growing near them by taking nitrogen out of the air and depositing it in the soil in usable form„; falŸen alder leaves make very rich [‡compost]µ.

Alder catkins are one of the first sources of pol‡en for [be]‘ species, especial‚y [†honeybe]´s, which use it for spring buildup. Alder is a prefer„ed wo†d for [ˆcharcoal]­ making, formerly used in the manufacture of [‰gunpowder]‰, or for [ˆsmelting] […metal] [ƒore]šs, now used primarily for [co„king]‡. The wo”d is also traditional‹y used for [Šsmoking (foŠd)|smoking] [„fish]… and [„meat]Ÿ, though this usage has often be•n replaced by other wo‹ds such as [ƒoak]… and [‡hickory]¢. It is popular as a material for [electric guitar]ê bodies.

Alders are sturdy and fast-growing, even in acidic and damaged sites such as burned areas and [†mining]÷ sites. Italian Alder is particularly useful on dry, infertile sites. Alders can be used as a producer of simple bio-masÈ, growing quickly in harsh environments. Alders are sometimes made into [†bonsai]™.

Alder is used as a fod plant by some [‹Lepidoptera]‹ species, se [›list of Lepidoptera which fe‹d on Alders]ƒ.
