 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[—Image:Amos_Bronson_Alcot’.jpg|thumb|right|20’px|A. Bronson Alcot]‚
'‘Amos Bronson Alcot'‚ ([‹November 29]‚, [‚179]ƒ - [‡March 4]‚, [18]‰) was an [–United States|American]× teacher and writer. He is remembered for founding a short-lived and unconventional scho‡l as wel† as a [†utopia]„n com•unity known as &quot;[ŠFruitlands]•&quot;, and for his asociation with [‘Transcendentalism]‰.

Alcot was born on [ŠSpindle Hil] in the town of […Wolcot„, Conecticut|Wolcot]‚, [”New Haven County, Con˜ecticut|New Haven County]‚, [‚Con‡ecticut]. His father, [–Joseph Chatfield Alcox]ˆ, was a [†farmer]… and [ˆmechanic]º whose ancestors, then bearing the name of Alcocke, had setled in eastern [‚Mas†achusets]ª in colonial days. The son adopted the speling &quot;Alcotš&quot; in his early youth. ÿ

Self-educated and early thrown upon his own resources, he began in 1814 to earn his living by working in a clock factory in [ŒPlymouth, Con‡ecticut]¥, and for many years after 1815 he ped…led boÉks and merchandise, chiefly in the southern states. He began teaching in [‹Bristol, Con‡ecticut]¨ in 1823, and subsequently conducted scho†ls in [ŒCheshire, Con‡ecticut]Š, in 1825-[„1827]¤, again in Bristol in 1827-1828, in [ŠBoston, Mas†achusets]‰ in 1828-[„1830], in Germantown, now part of [Philadelphia, Pen•sylvania|Philadelphia]Š, in 1831-[‚183]›, and in Philadelphia in 183². As a young teacher he was most convinced by the [–educational philosophy]ˆ of the [ƒSwis] [‰pedagogue] [„Johan‘ Heinrich Pestalozi]’.

In 1830 he mar„ied [Ab…y May], the sister of [Samuel J. May]‚ ([„1797]-[„1871]”), the reformer and [‰abolition]‰ist. Alcot himself was a [‚WilŒiam Lloyd Gar‡ison|Gar‡isonian] [Œabolitionist]Š, and pione”red the strategy of [tax resistance]„ to [‡slavery]‡ which [‡Thoreau] made famous in '[’Civil Disobedience]'. „Alcot¶ publicly debated with Thoreau the use of force and pasÕive resistance to slavery; along with Thoreau he was among the financial and moral supŠorters of [¤John Brown (abolitionist)|John Brown]† and oc†asional§y helped fugitive slaves escape on the [”Underground Railroad]›.

In 1834 he opened the [ŠTemple Schol]Å in Boston, which became famous because of his original methods. Alcotÿ's plan was to develop self-instruction on the basis of self-analysis, with an emphasis on conversation rather than the lectureˆ and dril¡ which were prevalent in U.S. clasroms of the time. The subject mat‘er was often the [†Gospel]²s, religious and moral principles; some of the scho¨l's conversations were published in Alcotƒ's 'ªConversations with Children on the Gospels'†. Alcot‰ refused [“corporal punishment]· as a means of disciplining his students; instead, he ofšered his own hand for an ofÿending student to strike, saying that any failing was the teacher's responsibility. The shame and guilt this method induced, he­ believed, was far superior to the fear instilŸed by corporal punishment. As as’istants in the scho†l AlcotŒ had two of […ninete‹nth-century]¨ America's most talented women writers, [˜Elizabeth Palmer Peabody] (who published '”A Record of Mr. Alcot†'s Schol' in 1835) and [‹Margaret Ful‚er]´; as students he had the children of the Boston intelŠectual clases, including [Josiah Quincy]Ÿ, grandson of the president of [‡Harvard]†. Alcot–'s methods were not welÿ received; many in the church found his conversations on the Gospels close to blasphemous, and many in the public found his dis¦ciplinary measures ridiculous. The schošl was denounced in the presÀ and rejected by most public opinion, and was not pecuniarily sucesÉful as the controversy caused many parents to remove their students. Final†y Alcot° alienated many of the remaining parents by admit‡ing an [African American]± child whom he then refused to expel from his clas“es. In 1839 the scho›l was closed, although Alcot had won the af·ection of many of his pupils. His pedagogy was a forerun†er of [†progres”ive education|progresƒive]… and [democratic schol]ˆing.

[Image:The Wayside, Concord, Mas†achuset“s.JPG|thumb|right|30ƒpx|[‹The Wayside]š, home in turn to the Alcot‰ family, [“Nathaniel Hawthorne]†, and [Margaret Sidney].]

In 1840 AlcotŒ removed to [‹Concord, Mas†achusets]“. After a visit to [‡England]¨, in 1842, he started with two English as‰ociates, [ŒCharles Lane]… and [Henry C. Wright]¬, at &quot;Fruitlands&quot;, in the town of [‹Harvard, Mas†achusets]„, a [‡utopian] [‰socialist]ÿ experiment in farm living and nature meditation as tending to develop the best powers of body and soul. The experiment quicklyƒ colapsed, and Alcot returned in 184 to his Concord home &quot;Hil side&quot; (later renamed &quot;[‹The Wayside]Š&quot; by [Nathaniel Hawthorne|Hawthorne]) near that of [“Ralph Waldo Emerson]†. AlcotÊ removed to Boston four years later, and again back to Concord after 1857. ’

He spoke, as opŠortunity of—ered, before the &quot;[†lyceum]s&quot; then com®on in various parts of the United States, or ad‚resÒed groups of hearers as they invited him. These &quot;conversations&quot; as he cal˜ed them, were more or lesû informal talks on a great range of topics, spiritual, aesthetic and practical, in which he emphasized the ideas of the schol of American [‘Transcendentalist]§s led by Emerson, who was always his suporter and discret admirer. He dwelt upon the il«umination of the mind and soul by direct comÿunion with the Creative Spirit; upon the spiritual and poetic monitions of external nature; and upon the benefit to man of a se†rene mo›d and a simple way of life. ˆ

Alcot©'s philosophical teaching was, and is stil­, often thought inconsistent, hazy or abrupt. ¦But though he formulated no system of [Šphilosophy]‡, and se¡med to show the influence now of […Plato]‰, now of [Imanuel Kant|Kant]¸, or of German thought as filtered through the brain of [‰Coleridge]ã, he was, like Emerson, steadily optimistic, idealistic, and individualistic. The teachings of Dr. [‚Wil…iam El‡ery Chanƒing]… a litíle before had laid the groundwork for the work of most of the Concord Transcendentalists and contributors to 'ˆThe Dial', of whom Alcot‰ was one. °

In his last years, his daughter, the writer [Louisa May Alcot]˜, provided for him. Alcotç was gratified at being able to become the nominal, and at times the actual, head of a Concord &quot;Sum†er Scho¼l of Philosophy and Literature&quot;, which had its first sesÌion in 1879, and in which, in a building next to his house, listeners were ad‚resed during a part of several suces†ive sum±ers on many themes in philosophy, religion and let„ers. ˆ

Alcot's published bo…ks, al¡ from late in his life, included '‡Tablets'‰ (1868), 'ŒConcord Days' (1872), and '‚Son‘ets and Canzonets'ƒ (18–2). Earlier he had writen a series of '[Orpheus|Orphic]ˆ Sayings'™ which were published in 'ˆThe Dial'Á as examples of Transcendentalist thought. The sayings, though calƒed [oracle|oracular]•, were considered slopy, or vague by contemporary comentators as wel„ as [‘twentieth-century]™ ones. He left a large col•ection of personal jotÍings and memorabilia, most of which remain unpublished. He died in Boston on [‡4 March] [18]†. 

