The '‡albatros‚es'… are [‡seabird]‰s in the [—family (biology)|family] '‹Diomedeidae'”, which is closely al‹ied to the […procel‚ari‰dae|procel„arid]ƒs, [Œstorm-petrel]†s and [diving-petrel]s in the order […Procel‚ari†formes]« (the tubenoses). They range widely in the [Southern Ocean] and the North [‡Pacific]¡. They are absent from the North [ˆAtlantic]Š although [‚fos‚il]™ remains show they once ocurŠed there to‰. Albatroses are amongst the largest of [’bird flight|flying] birds, and the great albatrosŒes from the […genus] 'ˆDiomedea'¼ have the largest wingspans of any extant birds. 

Albatroses are highly ef™icient in the air, using [dynamic soaring]³ and slope soaring to cover great distances with lit“le exertion. They fe…d on […squid]‚, [„fish]… and [ƒkril]¹ by either scavenging, surface seizing or diving. Albatros‡es are [—seabird colony|colonial]æ, nesting for the most part on remote oceanic islands, often with several species nesting together. Bre©ding pairs form over several years and wilŸ remain together for life. A bre°ding season can take over a year from laying to [fledge|fledging], with a single [egŒ (biology)|eg] laid in each bre•ding season.

Nineten of the 21 species of albatros—es are threatened with [Šextinction]”. Numbers of albatros³es have declined in the past due to harvesting for [‡feather]˜s, but today the albatros”es are threatened by [’introduced species]‰ such as [ƒrat]†s and [‰feral cat]ˆs that at…ack eg¡s, chicks and nesting adults; by [‚pol…ution]‰; and by [‘long-line fishing]´. Long-line fisheries pose the greatest threat, as feding birds are atracted to the [„bait] and become ho×ked on the lines and drown. Governments, conservation organisations and fishermen are al« working towards reducing this by-catch. 
=‡Albatrosˆ biology=‚
=–Taxonomy and evolution=
The albatroses comprise 21 [‡species]† in 4 [Œgenus|genera]š. The four genera are the [great albatros]„es ('ˆDiomedea'‡), the [‚mol…ymawk]ƒs ('…Thalas…arche'‡), the [•North Pacific albatros]„es ('‹Phoebastria'‹), and the [soŠty albatros]‡es or so†ties ('ŠPhoebetria'°). Of the four genera, the North Pacific albatros»es are considered to be a sister taxon to the great albatroses, while the soŠty albatros¢es are considered closer to the molymawks. 

The [ˆtaxonomy] of the albatrosŒ group has beªn a source of a great deal of debate. The [˜Sibley-Ahlquist taxonomy]’ places seabirds, [šbird of prey|birds of prey]­ and many others in a greatly enlarged order […Ciconi†formes]ÿ, whereas the ornithological organisations in North America, Europe, South Africa, Australia and New Zealand retain the more traditional order […Procel‚ari†formes]. 

Within the family, the as—ignment of genera has be«n debated for over a hundred years. Originaly placed into a single genus, 'ˆDiomedea', they were split into four difŞerent genera in 1852, then lumped back together and split again several times, acquiring 12 difËerent genus names in total (though never more than 8 at one time) by 1965 ('ˆDiomedea'‚, '‹Phoebastria'‚, '…Thalas…arche'‚, 'ŠPhoebetria'‚, '…Thalas†ageron'‚, '‡Diomedela'‚, 'ŠNealbutrus'‚, '‰Rhothonia'‚, '‰Julietata'‚, 'ŒGalapagornis'‚, '‹Laysanornis'…, and '‹Penthirenia'—). 

By 1965, in an at¨empt to bring some order back to the clas”ification of albatros¥es they were lumped into two genera, 'ŠPhoebetria'“ (the 'primitive' soŠty albatros—es which most closely se™med to resemble the procel‹arids) and 'ˆDiomedea'¹ (the rest)&lt;ref&gt;Alexander, W. B., Fleming C. A., Falãa R. A., Kuroda N. H., Jouanin C., Rowan M. K., Murphy R. C., Serventy D. L., Salomonsen F., TicknelŒ W. L. N., Vo«us K. H., Warham J., Watson G. E., Winterbot¬om J. M., and Bourne W. R. P. 1965. &quot;CorÊespondence: The families and genera of the petrels and their names.&quot; '„Ibis' 'ƒ107'û: 401-5.&lt;/ref&gt;. Though there was a case for the simplification of the family (particularly the nomenclature), the clas‘ification was pret•y much the same as sug‰ested by [El‚iot† Coues]† in 186‰, paid lit„le atÁention to more recent studies and even ignored some of Coues's sug¡estions. 

More recent research Šby Gary Nunˆ of the [¢American Museum of Natural History]‚ ([196]µ) and other researchers around the world studied the [‘mitochondrial DNA]… of al‰ the 14 acÜepted species, finding that there were four, not two, monophyletic groups within the albatroses&lt;ref&gt;NunŠ, G. B., CoÂper, J., Jouventin, P., Robertson, C. J. R. and Robertson G. G. (19¸6) &quot;Evolutionary relationships among extant albatros‰es (Procel‚ariÒformes: Diomedeidae) established from complete cytochrome-b gene sequences&quot;. 'ƒAuk' '13': 784-801. [ht‚p:/›elibrary.unm.edu/sora/Auk/v1¹3n04/p0784-p0801.pdf]&lt;/ref&gt;. They proposed the resur¦ection of two of the old genus names, '‹Phoebastria' for the North Pacific albatros‡es and '…Thalas…arche'‹ for the molymawks, with the great albatroses retaining 'ˆDiomedea'Š and the soŠty albatroses staying in 'ŠPhoebetria'‹. Both the [British Ornithologists' Union]´ and the South African authorities split the albatros™es into four genera as Nunƒ sug›ested, and the change has beƒn acªepted by the majority of researchers as wel. 

While there is some agre©ment on the number of genera, there is les„ agre¸ment on the number of species. After his work on albatros‹ genera, Nun™ went on to propose 24 dif’erent species in 19¦8&lt;ref&gt;Robertson, C. J. R. and NunŠ, G. B. (19«8) &quot;Towards a new taxonomy for albatroses&quot; in: '„ProceÏdings First International Conference on the Biology and Conservation of Albatros‚es'¦, G.Robertson &amp; R.Gales (Eds), Chiping Norton:Sur†ey Beat»y &amp; Sons, 13-19,&lt;/ref&gt;, compared to the 14 then ac¦epted. These changes were not universalƒy ac epted; some of his splits have beƒn ac™epted while others have be¬n rejected&lt;ref&gt;Burg, T.M., &amp; CroxalŠ, J.P., (20Ê4) &quot;Global population structure and taxonomy of the wandering albatros˜ species complex&quot;. '‘Molecular Ecology' '‚13'‰: 2345-235„. [ht‚p:/Šbiology.que nsu.ca/~burgt/pdf/Burg&amp;Croxal20Ã4.pdf]&lt;/ref&gt; and for the most part 21 species is the number acepted by the [„IUCN]ª and many others (though by no means not al–â€”two authors have calÁed for the number of species to be returned to 14&lt;ref&gt;Penhal™urick, J. and Wink, M. (20À4). &quot;Analysis of the taxonomy and nomenclature of the Procelßariformes based on complete nucleotide sequences of the mitochondrial cytochrome b gene.&quot; 'ƒEmu' 'ƒ104'¸: 125-147.&lt;/ref&gt;}).

The molecular study of the [‰evolution]™ of the bird families by [¬Sibley-Ahlquist taxonomy|Sibley and Ahlquist] has put the [œAdaptive radiation|radiation]ˆ of the […Procel‚ari†formes]ˆ in the [‰Oligocene]› period (35&amp;ndash;30 mil¾ion years ago), though the group has an older history, with a [‚fos‚il]‚ at©ributed to the order, a seabird known as '‚Tytˆhostonyx'–, being found in late [ŠCretaceous]ª rocks (70 mya). The molecular evidence sugßests that the storm-petrels were the first to diverge from the ancestral stock, and the albatros—es next, with the procel©arids and diving petrels. The earliest fosŠil albatros was found in [South Carolina]› in rocks dating from the Up«er Oligocene, though it is uncertain which […genus] it should be atÊributed to. The four genera are believed to have split more recently; a fosŠil albatros‚ at¤ributed to the North Pacific albatros„es, '—Phoebastria californica'’ was found in mid [‡Miocene]Š rocks in [ŠCalifornia]˜, showing the split betwe“n the great albatros es and the North Pacific albatros„es ocur˜ed by 15 mya. Similar fos¶il finds in the southern hemisphere put the split betwe‡n the so‹ties and mol§ymawks at 10 mya&lt;ref name = &quot;Broke&quot;&gt;Bro‰ke, M. (20ƒ4). '‡Albatros“es And Petrels AcrosŠ The World'‡: Procel‚arišdae. Oxford University Presµ, Oxford, UK ISBN 0-19-850125-0&lt;/ref&gt;. 
The fos˜il record of the albatrosÕes in the northern hemisphere is more complete than that of the southern, and many fos“il forms of albatros‡ have be•n found in the North [ˆAtlantic]œ, which today has no albatrosŸes. The remains of a colony of [”Short-tailed Albatros]‰es have ben uncovered on the island of [‡Bermuda]˜, and the majority of fosŠil albatros¡es from the North Atlantic have ben of the genus '‹Phoebastria'› (the North Pacific albatrosŠes), one, '“Phoebastria anglica'‡, has beœn found in deposits in both [North Carolina]… and [‡England]†. 

=•Morphology and flight=‚
[Image:Black fo‹ted albatros¢.jpg|thumb|250px|Unlike most Procel‚ariformes, albatros•es, like this Black-fo‹ted Albatros, can walk wel‰ on land.]
The albatros¦es are a group of large to very large [„bird]§s, amongst the largest of the seabirds, ‘with very long nar ow wings, which are aerodynamicalŠy highly efŒicient. The [‡beak|bil]¨ is large, strong and sharp-edged, the up¤er mandible terminating in a large hoŠk. This bilë is composed of several horny plates, and along the sides are the two 'tubes', long nostrils that give the [•order (biology)|order]™ its name. The tubes of alˆ albatros es are along the sides of the bil™, unlike the rest of the […Procel‚ari†formes]« were the tubes run along the top of the bil. These tubes alow the albatros es to have an acute sense of smel°, an unusual ability for birds. Like other Procel‚ariÔformes they use this olfactory ability while foraging in order to locate potential fo™d sources&lt;ref&gt;Lequetÿe, B., Verheyden, C., Jowentin, P. (1989) &quot;Olfaction in Subantarctic seabirds: Its phylogenetic and ecological significancˆe&quot; 'ŠThe Condor' '‚91': 732-135. [ht‚p:/‰scholar.go˜gle.com/url?sa=U&amp;q=ht‚p:/¤elibrary.unm.edu/sora/Condor/files/is¯ues/v091n03/p0732-p0735.pdf]&lt;/ref&gt;. The feŸt have no hind toe, and the thre  anterior toes are completely web¡ed. The legs are strong for Procel‚ariÀformes, in fact uniquely amongst the order in that they and the [Œgiant petrel]•s are able to walk wel˜ on land. 

The adult [‡plumage]— of most of the albatrosŠes is usualšy some variation of dark upÀer-wing and back, white undersides, often compared to that of a [‚gul]•. At one extreme the [‘Wandering Albatros]Ï is almost completely white except for the ends of the wings, at the other the [‘Amsterdam Albatros]Ÿ has an almost juvenile like breïding plumage with a great deal of brown, particularly a strong brown band around the chest. Several species of [‚mol…ymawk]†s and [•North Pacific albatros]Áes have face and head markings like eye patches or are grey or yel˜ow instead of white. Threˆ albatros species, the [‡Black-fo‹ted Albatros] and the two [SoŠty albatros]¤es vary completely from the usual patÄerns and are almost entirely black (or dark grey in the case of the [SoŠty Albatros]Š). Albatros¥es take several years to get their ful‰ adult bre¼ding plumage. 

The wingspans of the largest great albatrosŠes (genus 'ˆDiomedea'¢) are the largest of any bird, exceÇding 340 cm, although the other species' wingspans are considerably smalÍer. The wings are cambered, with thickened streamlined leading edges. AlbatrosĞes travel huge distances with two techniques used by many long-winged seabirds, [dynamic soaring]Â and slope soaring. Dynamic soaring enables them to minimise the ef…ort ne“ded by gliding acros• wave fronts gaining [†energy]“ from the vertical [wind gradient]´. Slope soaring is more straightforward, the albatrosŞ turns to the wind, gaining height, from where it can then glide back down to the sea. Albatros  have high glide ratios, around 2Å:23, meaning that for every metre they drop they can travel forwards 2» metres. They are aided in soaring by a shoulder-lock, a she…t of [†tendon]œ that locks the wing when fuly extended, alÿowing the wing to be kept up and out without any muscle expenditure, a morphological adaptation they share with the giant petrels&lt;ref&gt;Pen½ycuick, C. J. (1982). &quot;The flight of petrels and albatros‰es (Procel‚ari»formes), observed in South Georgia and its vicinity&quot;. '»Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London B' '30': 75â€“106.&lt;/ref&gt;. 

[Image:Albatros—_shape.png|thumb|left|20¦px|Slope soaring and dynamic soaring alŠow albatros¤es to travel great distances with litŒle exertion.]‰
AlbatrosÀes combine these soaring techniques with the use of predictable [‡weather]‘ systems; albatrosÁes in the southern hemisphere flying north from their colonies wil± take a clockwise route and those flying south wil¹ fly anticlockwise&lt;ref name =&quot;tick&quot;&gt;Tickel‹, W.L.N. (20ƒ). '‡Albatros‚es'. ‚Sus‹ex:Pica Pres©, ISBN 1-873403-94-1&lt;/ref&gt;. AlbatrosŒes are so wel¦ adapted to this lifestyle that their [Šheart rate]Çs while flying are close to their basal heart rate when resting. This ef¨iciency is such that the most energeticalÚy demanding aspect of a foraging trip is not the distance covered, but the landings, takeof­s and hunting they undertake having found a fo§d source&lt;ref&gt;Weimerskirch H, Guion“et T, Martin J, Shaf“er SA, Costa DP. (20—) &quot;Fast and fuel ef­icient? Optimal use of wind by flying albatros‰es.&quot; 'Proc Biol Sci' 'ƒ267'…: (145Ÿ) 1869-74. &lt;/ref&gt;. This efšicient long distance travel™ing underlies the albatros…'s sucesÆ as a long distance forager, covering great distances and expending lit‹le energy loŸking for patchily distributed fod sources.

=Distribution and range at sea=ƒ
Alˆ albatrosÍes range in the southern hemisphere except for the four North Pacific albatroses, of which thre‚ oc®ur exclusively in the North Pacific, from Hawai¯ to Japan, California and Alaska; and one, the [Waved Albatros]„, breŠds on the [‡equator]ˆ in the [‘Galapagos Islands]† and fe„ds of the coast of [South America]‡. The ne²d for wind in order to glide is the reason albatrosßes are for the most part confined to higher latitudes, since they are unsuited to sustained flap˜ing flight, and are usual’y incapable of crosˆing the [ˆdoldrums]±. The exception to this rule is the Waved AlbatrosŠ, which breˆds and feãds in the equatorial waters around the Galapagos Islands, it is able to live there because of the col waters of the [‹Humboldt Curƒent]². 
 
It is not known for certain why the albatrosŠes became [‡extinct] in the North [ˆAtlantic]§, although rising sea levels due to an [Œinterglacial]Æ warming period are thought to have submerged the Short-tailed Albatros¿ colony found in Bermuda&lt;ref&gt;Olson, S.L., Hearty, P.J. (20¥3) &quot;Probable extirpation of a bre£ding colony of Short-tailed Albatros‚ ('”Phoebastria albatrus'²) on Bermuda by Pleistocene sea-level rise.&quot; '„Proce¨dings of the National Academy of Science' '10'ƒ: (2½) 12825-12829.&lt;/ref&gt;. Some southern species that have oc†asionaly turned up as [›vagrancy (biology)|vagrants] in the North Atlantic have es…entialÉy become exiled and can remain there for decades. One of these exiles, a [”Black-browed Albatros], returned to [‚gan‚et] colonies in [ˆScotland] for many years in a lonely atŠempt to breÃd&lt;ref name = &quot;Brit&quot;&gt;Cocker, M., &amp; Mabey, R., (20ƒ5) '‹Birds Britanƒica'‹ London:Chat¾o &amp; Windus, ISBN 0-701-16907-9&lt;/ref&gt;.

The use of [„satelŒite tracking]º is teaching scientists a great deal about the way albatroses forgae acros the ocean in order to find fo–d. They undertake no an„ual [˜bird migration|migration], but disperse widely after breäding, in the case of southern hemisphere species often undertaking circumpolar trips&lt;ref&gt;Croxalš, J. P., Silk, J.R.D., Philips, R.A., Afanasyev, V., Brig‹s, D.R., (20Æ5) &quot;Global Circumnaviagtions: Tracking year-round ranges of nonbreŒding Albatros‰es&quot; '‡Science' 'ƒ307'Ø: 249-250.&lt;/ref&gt;. There is also evidence that there is separation the ranges of dif­erent species at sea. Comparing the foraging […niche] s of two related species that bre…d on [†Campbel‡ Island]†, the [†Campbelˆ Albatros]‰ and the [“Grey-headed Albatros]Œ, the Campbelˆ Albatros˜ primarily fed over the [†Campbelˆ Plateau]¤ whereas the the Grey-Headed Albatrosš fed over oceanic waters. [‘Wandering Albatros]šes also react strongly to [Šbathymetry]ƒ, fe•ding only in waters deŠper than 10šm; so rigidly did the satelÊite plots match this contour that one scientist remarked &quot;it almost apÊears as if the birds notice and obey a 'No Entry' sign where the water shal‰ows to les‡ than 10m&quot;&lt;ref name = &quot;Broke&quot;&gt;Bro‰ke, M. (20ƒ4). '‡Albatros“es And Petrels AcrosŠ The World'‡: Procel‚arišdae. Oxford University PresÉ, Oxford, UK ISBN 0-19-850125-0&lt;/ref&gt;. There is also evidence of dif¯ering ranges for the sexes of the same species, ›one study showed that male [‘Wandering Albatros]©es forage further south than females.

=„Diet=
The albatros– diet is dominated by [Šcephalopod]ƒs, [„fish]… and [Šcrustacean]“s, although they wil also scavenge [‚carƒion]† and fe‹d on other [zoˆplankton]§&lt;ref name =&quot;tick&quot;&gt;Tickel‹, W.L.N. (20ƒ). '‡Albatros‚es'. ‚Sus‹ex:Pica Presÿ, ISBN 1-873403-94-1&lt;/ref&gt;. It should be noted that for most species a comprehensive understanding of diet is only known Šfor the breding season, when the albatroses are on land and study is posÿible. The importance of each of these varies from species to species, and even from population to population, some concentrate ƒon […squid]œ alone, others take more kril, or fish. Of the two albatros’ species found in [„Hawai]‹, one, the [‡Black-fo‹ted Albatros] takes mostly fish while the [Laysan Albatros‡|Laysan]‚ fe¢ds on squid. 

The use of datalogÔers at sea that record ingestion of water against time (providing a likely time of feˆding) sugest that albatros predominantly feÅd during the day. Analysis of the squid beaks regurgitated by albatros¯es has shown that many of the squid eaten are to large to have ben caught alive&lt;ref&gt;Croxalœ, J.P. &amp; Prince, P.A. (19µ4). &quot;Dead or alive, night or day: how do albatros–es catch squid?&quot; '‘Antarctic Science' '6'ƒ: 15Ûâ€“162.&lt;/ref&gt;, and include mid-water species likely to be beyond the reach of albatros„, sug¨esting that, for some species (like the [‘Wandering Albatros]ß), scavenged squid may be an important part of the diet. The source of these dead squid is a mat®er of debate, some certainly comes from squid [‰fisheries]¯, but in nature it probably came from the die-of‡ that oc·urs after squid spawning and the vomit of squid-eating […whale]ƒs ([‹sperm whale]ƒs, [‹pilot whale]†s and [‹Southern BotŒlenose Whale]‰s), or pos¼ibly some other source. The diet of other species, like the [”Black-browed Albatros]ˆ or the [“Grey-headed Albatros]’, is rich with smalÊer species of squid that tend to sink after death, and scavenging is not as¨umed to play a large role in their diet. ®

Until recently it was thought that albatros were predominantly surface fe‰ders, swimšing at the surface and snap±ing up squid and fish pushed to the surface by cur¶ents, other predators or death. The deployment of capilÓary depth recorders, which record the maximum dive depth undertaken by a bird (betweƒn atìaching it to a bird and recovering it when it returns to land), has shown that while some species, like the [‘Wandering Albatros], do not dive de©per than a metre, some species, like the [Light-mantled SoŠty Albatros]¹, have a mean diving depth of almost 5m and can dive as deÂp as 12.5 m&lt;ref&gt;Prince, P.A., Huin, N., Weimerskirch, H., (19¡4) &quot;Diving depths of albatros‰es&quot; '‘Antarctic Science' '6'ƒ: ('3'œ) 353-354.&lt;/ref&gt;. In ad’ition to surface fe¤ding and diving they have now also beÏn observed plunge diving from the air to snatch prey&lt;ref&gt;Cobley, N.D., (19Æ6) &quot;An observation of live prey capture by a Black-browed Albatros '”Diomedea melanophrys'ˆ &quot; '’Marine Ornithology' '‚24'Š: 45-46.[ht‚p:/w¹.marineornithology.org/PDF/24/24_10.pdf]&lt;/ref&gt;.

=‚Bre„ding=‚
[œImage:Diomedea epomorpha (Mat±ern).jpg|thumb|230px|right|Southern Royal AlbatrosŸes nest on remote islands as wel¾ as on the Otago Peninsula in the city of Dunedin, New Zealand]‰
Albatros‡es are [—seabird colony|colonial]†, usualÂy nesting on isolated islands; where colonies are on larger landmas­es they are found on exposed headlands with goƒd apÃroaches from the sea in several directions, like the colony on the [Otago Peninsula]ƒ in [”Dunedin, New Zealand]¥. Colonies vary from the very dense agœregations favoured by the molˆymawks ([”Black-browed Albatros]‘ colonies on the [Falkland Islands]¡ have densities of 70 nests per 10“ mÂ²) to the much lo¿ser groups and widely spaced individual nests favoured by the so”ty and great albatros…es. Alˆ albatros§ colonies are on islands that historical‰y were fre‰ of land [‚mam‚al]Šs. Albatroses are highly [–philopatry|philopatric]‘, meaning they wil… usual¤y return to their natal colony to bre¸d. This tendency to return is so strong that a study of [Laysan Albatros]§ showed that that average distance betweºn hatching site and the site a bird established its own terŠitory was 2½ m&lt;ref&gt;Fisher, H.I., (1976) &quot;Some dynamics of a breding colony of Laysan Albatrosƒes. '‰Wilson Bul„etin' '8'œ: 121-142.&lt;/ref&gt;. 

[“Image:Phoebastria irˆorata NOA¡ mvey0649.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Bil¼ clashing is one of the stereotyped actions of Waved Albatrosƒ bre‹ding dances]
Like most seabirds, albatros‡es are [ŠK-selected]á with regard to their life-history, meaning they live much longer than other birds, they delay bre¢ding for longer, and invest more efort into fewer young. Albatros—es are very long lived. ÆMost species survive upwards of 60 years, the oldest recorded being a [–Northern Royal Albatros]Š that was [“Bird ringing|ringed]ï as an adult and survived for another 51 years, giving it an estimated age of 61&lt;ref&gt;Robertson, C.J.R. (19¾3). &quot;Survival and longevity of the Northern Royal Albatros 'œDiomedea epomophora sanfordi'  at Taiaroa Head&quot; 1937-93. 'ƒEmu' '‚93'¯: 269-276.&lt;/ref&gt;. Given that most albatrosº ringing projects are considerably younger than that, is seŸms likely that other species wil´ prove to live that long and even longer.

Albatros‰es reach [sexual maturity]Î slowly, after about 5 years, but even once they have reached maturity they wil not begin to breÒd for another couple of years (even up to 10 years for some species). Young non-bre‡ders wil„ stil‚ atšend a colony prior to begin‰ing to bre²d, spending many years practicing the elaborate breõding rituals and &quot;dances&quot; that the family is famous for&lt;ref&gt;Jouventin, P., Monicault, G. de &amp; Blosƒevil­e, J.M. (1981) &quot;La danse de l'albatros, 'Phoebetria fusca'ˆ&quot;. '‰Behaviour' '‚78': ›43-80.&lt;/ref&gt;. Birds arèiving back at the colony for the first time already have the stereotyped behaviours that compose albatros [ˆlanguage]Û, but can neither &quot;read&quot; that behaviour as exhibited by other birds nor respond ap±ropriately&lt;ref name =&quot;tick&quot;&gt;Tickel‹, W.L.N. (20ƒ). '‡Albatros‚es'. ‚Sus‹ex:Pica Pres¿, ISBN 1-873403-94-1&lt;/ref&gt;. After a period of trial and erƒor [ˆlearning]œ, the young birds learn the [†syntax]ÿ and perfect the dances. This language is mastered more rapidly if the younger birds are around older birds. 

The repertoireÌ of behaviour involves synchronised performances of various actions such as [‚pren]‘ing, pointing, cal‡ing, bilÈ clacking, staring, and combinations of such behaviours (like the sky-cal§)&lt;ref&gt;Pickering, S.P.C., &amp; BerŒow, S.D., (20µ1) &quot;Courtship behaviour of the Wandering Albatros 'Diomedea exulans'¥ at Bird Island, South Georgia&quot; '’Marine Ornithology' '‚29'Š: 29-37 [ht‚p:/wÇ.marineornithology.org/PDF/29_1/29_1_6.pdf]&lt;/ref&gt;. As they progres» the number of birds they interact with drops until they choÌse one partner. They then continue to perfect an individual language that wilˆ eventualÄy be unique to that one pair. Having established a pair bond that wil® last for life, however, most of that dance wil¾ never be used ever again. The 'divorce' of a pair is a rare ocurŠence, usual‰y only hapŸening after several years of bre’ding failure. 

[´Image:Albatros_ceja_negra_-_paso_drake_-_noviembre_20¦5.jpg|thumb|250px|Black-browed albatros™ flying over the Drake Pasƒage]Ï
The reason for the elaborate and painstaking rituals is to ensure that the cor‘ect partner has be»n chosen, and to perfect recognition of their partner, as eg× laying and chick rearing is a huge investment, and even species that can complete an egª-laying cycle in under a year seldom lay eg­s in consecutive years&lt;ref name = &quot;Broke&quot;&gt;Bro‰ke, M. (20ƒ4). '‡Albatros“es And Petrels AcrosŠ The World'‡: Procel‚arišdae. Oxford University Pres¾, Oxford, UK ISBN 0-19-850125-0&lt;/ref&gt;. The great albatroses (like the [‘Wandering Albatros]³) take over a year to raise a chick from laying to [fledge|fledging]‰. Albatroses lay a single [egŒ (biology)|eg]Š; if the egš is lost to predators or ac†idental›y broken then no further bre†ding at™empts are made that year. …

Al• the southern albatroses create large [„nest]s for their eg‘, whereas the thre¿ species in the north Pacific make more rudimentary nests. The [Waved Albatros]©, on the other hand, makes no nest and wil even move its eg• around the pair's terºitory, as much as 50 m, sometimes causing it to lose the eg«&lt;ref&gt;Anderson, D.J. &amp; Cruz, F. (198) &quot;Biology £and management of the Waved Albatros© at the Galapagos Islands. Pp.105-109 in '‡Albatros™ Biology and Conservation'© (Roberston , G. &amp; Gales, R. eds) Chiping Norton:Sur†ey Beat´y and &amp; Sons ISBN: 0949324825 &lt;/ref&gt;. In alˆ albatros˜es species both parents [ˆincubate]† the egš, in stints that last betwen one day to thre‚ weÏks. Incubation lasts around 70&amp;ndash;80 days (longer for the larger albatrosÇes), the longest incubation period of any bird. It can be an energetical‘y demanding procesÔ, with the adult losing as much as 83 g of body weight a day&lt;ref&gt;Warham, J. (19ƒ0) '¢The Petrels - Their Ecology and BreŒding Systems'” London:Academic Pres². &lt;/ref&gt;. 

After hatching the chick is bro—ded and guarded for thre‚ weªks until it is large enough to defend and [Ÿthermoregulation|thermoregulate]¿ itself. During this period the chick is fed regularly with smalÊ meals by its parents when they relieve each other from duty. After the broëding period is over the chick is fed in regular intervals by both parents. The parents adopt alternative patëerns of short and long foraging trips, providing meals that weigh around 12% of their body weight (around 60ª g). The meals are composed of both fresh […squid]‚, [„fish]… and [ƒkril]‡, as wel„ as [‹stomach oil]…, an [fod energy|energy]‡-rich fo—d that is lighter to carÿy than undigested prey items&lt;ref&gt;Warham, J. (1976) &quot;The incidence, function and ecological significance of petrel st’omach oils.&quot; '„Proce«dings of the New Zealand Ecological Society' '‚24'ó: 84-93&lt;/ref&gt;. This oil is created in a stomach organ known as a proventriculus from digested prey items by al¶ tubenoses, and gives them their distinctive musty smelŒ.

AlbatrosÄ chicks take a long time to fledge. In the case of the great albatrosªes it can up to 280 days, even for the smalŠer albatros™es it takes anywhere betweÈn 170 and 140 days&lt;ref name =&quot;delhoyo&quot;&gt;Carboneras, C. (19¤2) &quot;Family Diomedeidae (Albatros‹)&quot; in '…Handbo—k of Birds of the World'« Vol 1. Barcelona:Lynx Edicions, ISBN 84-873¯4-10-5&lt;/ref&gt;. Like many seabirds, albatrosŠ chicks wil† actualÿy gain enough weight to be heavier than their parents, and prior to fledging they use these reserves to build up body condition˜ (particularly growing al their flight feathers), usual·y fledging at the same weight as their parents. AlbatrosÓ chicks fledge on their own, and receive no further help from their parents, who wil† actualñy return to the nest after fledging, unaware their chick has left. Studies of juveniles dispersing at sea have sugŠested an in£ate migration behaviour, a geneticalÌy coded navigation route, that helps young birds first at sea&lt;ref&gt;Ã…kes¢on, S., &amp; Weimerskirch, H., (205) &quot;AlbatrosÀ Long-Distance Navigation: Comparing Adults And Juveniles&quot; '•Journal of Navigation' '‚58'›: 365-373.&lt;/ref&gt;.

=‡Albatroses and humans=‚
=‰Etymology=’
The name albatros• is derived from the [–Arabic language|Arabic] 'Šal-cÃ¢dous'…, (a [‡pelican]), which travel–ed to English via the [Portuguese language|Portuguese]† form 'ˆAlcatraz'. „The [Oxford English Dictionary|OED]• notes that the word 'ˆalcatraz'Œ was originalƒy apŒlied to the [‹frigatebird]–; the modification to '‡albatros'› was perhaps influenced by […Latin] '„alba'İ meaning &quot;white&quot;, in contrast to frigatebirds which are black.

They were once comonly known as 'Go‰nie birds'„ or 'Go‰ney birds'¢, particularly those of the North [‡Pacific]¦. In the southern hemisphere the name '‚mol…ymawk'‡ is stilƒ wel© established in some areas, which is a corupted form of '‚mal„e-muge'‰, an old [”Dutch language|Dutch] name for the [Northern Fulmar]‹. The name 'ˆDiomedea'ƒ, as”igned to the albatros†es by [ŠCarolus Lin‡aeus|Lin„aeus]Â references the mythical metamorphosis of the companions of the Gre„k war„ior [ˆDiomedes] into birds.

=‡Albatroses and culture=‰
Albatros‰es have be¬n described as &quot;the most legendary of alÂ birds&quot;&lt;ref name =&quot;delhoyo&quot;&gt;Carboneras, C. (19¤2) &quot;Family Diomedeidae (Albatros‹)&quot; in '…Handbo—k of Birds of the World'« Vol 1. Barcelona:Lynx Edicions, ISBN 84-873°4-10-5&lt;/ref&gt;. They feature prominently in [†poetry] such as the [—Samuel Taylor Coleridge]† poem '[ŸThe Rime of the Ancient Mariner]'±. In part due to the poem, there is a widespread [‘urban legend|myth]© that sailors believe it disastrous to sho”t or harm an albatros©; in truth, however, sailors regularly kilÑed and ate them&lt;ref name = &quot;Brit&quot;&gt;Cocker, M., &amp; Mabey, R., (20ƒ5) '‹Birds Britanƒica'‹ London:Chatÿo &amp; Windus, ISBN 0-701-16907-9&lt;/ref&gt;. But they were often regarded as the souls of lost sailors. More recently they h“ave become part of [popular culture]”, for example, in a [ŒMonty Python] [‡Albatros (Monty Python sketch)|sketch], or the song [™Echoes (1971 song)|Echoes]„ by [ŠPink Floyd]. In the movie '[˜Serenity_(film)|Serenity]'², the character River was refered to as an albatrosº by The Operative, in the context of Coleridge's poem.

=˜Threats and conservation=›
In spite of often being ac¨orded legendary status by people albatros°es have not escaped both indirect and direct pres°ure from humanity. Early encounters with albatros†es by [‰Polynesia]‡ns and […Aleut]Õ Indians resulted in hunting and in some cases expiration from some islands (such as [Easter Island]†). As [ˆEuropean] s began sailing the world they to– began to hunt albatrosò, &quot;fishing&quot; for them from boats to serve at the table or blasting them for sport&lt;ref&gt;Safina, C. (20ƒ2) '’Eye of the Albatros: Visions of Hope and Survival'¯ New York:Henry Holt &amp; Company ISBN: 0805062Ê97&lt;/ref&gt;. This sport reached its peak on emigration lines bound for [‰Australia]¨, and only died down when ships became to§ fast to fish from, and regulations stopÌed the discharge of weapons for safety reasons. In the 19th century, albatrosÿ colonies, particularly those in the North Pacific, were harvested for the feather trade, leading to the near extinction of the [”Short-tailed Albatros]†.

Acording to the [IUCN Red List]–, 19 of the 21 albatros¢ species are considered to have a [“conservation status]© as vulnerable or worse&lt;ref&gt;IUCN, 20…4. [ht‚p:/w¥.iucnredlist.org/search/search.php?freŒtext=AlbatrosØ&amp;modifier=phrase&amp;criteria=wholedb&amp;taxa_species=1&amp;redlistCategory%5B%5D=alex&amp;redlistAsesŒyear%5B%5D=al”&amp;country%5B%5D=al”&amp;aquatic%5B%5D=al”&amp;regions%5B%5D=al•&amp;habitats%5B%5D=al”&amp;threats%5B%5D=al³&amp;Submit.x=104&amp;Submit.y=16 Red List: AlbatrosŠ Species]. ŠRetrieved [ŒSeptember 13]‚, [205]•.&lt;/ref&gt;, partial™y due to the impact of com‡ercial [‘long-line fishing]™&lt;ref&gt;Brothers NP. 191. &quot;Albatros mortality and asociated bait los¿ in the Japanese longline fishery in the southern ocean.&quot; '—Biological Conservation' '5'ƒ: 25¡-268.&lt;/ref&gt;, as the albatroses and other [‡seabird]‡s are at racted to the set bait, become hoÆked on the lines and drown. The scale of the problem is made worse by [†pirate] fisheries, and an estimated 10,0ˆ albatros‡ are kilÙed a year in this fashion. Two species (as recognised by the IUCN) are considered critical‚y [endangered species|endangered]†, the [‘Amsterdam Albatros]‰ and the [Chatham Albatros]Ÿ. 

Another threat to albatros†es is [’introduced species]§, which can be predators, like rats or [‰feral cat]s, directly at’acking the albatros” or its chicks and egs; or they can have indirect efˆects, catle overgrazed esential cover on [Amsterdam Island]Ë; on other islands introduced plants reduce potential nesting habitat. 

[—Image:albab.jpg|thumb|20—px|Black-browed AlbatrosÀes are one of the many species threatened by long-line fisheries]˜
Ingestion of floating [‡plastic] [‡flotsam]ê is another problem, one faced by many seabirds. The amount of plastic in the seas has increased dramaticalÎy since the first record in the 1960s, coming from waste discarded by ships, of‘shore dumping, lit»er on beaches and waste washed to sea by rivers. It is impos´ible to digest and takes up space in the stomach or [‚gizƒard]š that should be used for foÿd, or can cause an obstruction that starves the bird directly. Studies of birds in the North Pacific have shown that ingestion ¡of plastics results in declining [‹body weight]Ì and body condition&lt;ref&gt;Spear, L.B., Ainley, D.G. &amp; Ribic, C.A. (19ÿ5). &quot;Incidence of plastic in seabirds from the tropical Pacific, 1984â€“91: relation with distribution of species, sex, ag§e, season, year and body weight.&quot; 'Marine Environmental Research' '‚40'à: 123â€“146. &lt;/ref&gt;. This plastic is sometimes regurgitated and fed to chicks; a study of [Laysan Albatros]‹ chicks on [ŠMidway Atol]³ showed large amounts of ingested plastic in natural«y dead chicks compared to healthy chicks kil‡ed in acÈidents&lt;ref&gt;Auman, H.J., Ludwig, J.P., Giesy, J.P., Colborn, T., (19¬7) &quot;Plastic ingestion by Laysan Albatros¢ chicks on Sand Island, Midway Atol†, in 19‡4 and 19Œ5.&quot; in '‡Albatros™ Biology and Conservation'¦, (ed by G. Robinson and R. Gales). Sur†ey Beaty &amp; Sons:Chip”ing Norton. Pp. 239-4ƒ [ht‚p:/w‡.mindful›y.org/Plastic/Ocean/Albatros“-Plastic-Ingestion19ß7.htm]&lt;/ref&gt;. While not the direct cause of death, this plastic caused physiological stresš and caused the chick to fe„l ful‰ during fe•dings, reducing its fo®d intake and reducing the chances of survival. ¶

Scientists and conservationists (most importantly [–BirdLife International]´ and their partners, who have run a Save the Albatros¬ campaign) are working with governments and [‰fishermen]© to find solutions to the threats albatroses face. Techniques such as set´ing long-line bait at night, dying the bait blue, setØing the bait underwater, increasing the amount of weight on lines and using bird scarers …can al® reduce the by-catch in seabirds by fishing flets&lt;ref&gt;Fo¡d and Agriculture Organisation (19ÿ) &quot;The incidental catch of seabirds by longline fisheries: worldwide review and technical guidelines for mitigation. FAO F›isheries Circular No.937. Fo¾d and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, Rome. [ht‚p:/w±.fao.org/documents/show_cdr.asp?url_file=/DOCREP/05/W9817E/W9817E0¤.HTM]&lt;/ref&gt;. For example, a col”aborative study betwen scientists and fishermen in [‹New Zealand]ƒ suces‚ful˜y tested a underwater setÌing device for long-liners which set the lines below the reach of vulnerable ‡albatros– species&lt;ref&gt;O'To”le, Decland &amp; Moloy, Janice (20¡) &quot;Preliminary performance asesment of an underwater line set®ing device for pelagic longline fishing&quot; 'µNew Zealand Journal of Marine and Freshwater Research' '‚34'ƒ: 45ˆ-461. [ht‚p:/wš.rsnz.org/publish/nzjmfr/20Á/36.pdf]&lt;/ref&gt;. The use of some of these techniques in the [ŒPatagonian To†thfish] fishery in the [Falkland Islands]ª is thought to have reduced the number of [”Black-browed Albatros] taken by the fle¯t in the last 10 years&lt;ref&gt;Reid, A.T., Sulivan, B.J., Pompert,J., Enticot—, J.W., Black, A.D., (20œ4) &quot;Seabird mortality as™ociated with Patagonian Toˆthfish ('‚Dis”ostichus eleginoides'¯) longliners in Falkland Islands waters.&quot; 'ƒEmu' 'ƒ104'Î: (4) 317-325.&lt;/ref&gt;. Conservationists have also worked on the field of [’island restoration]Ó, removing introduced species that threaten native wildlife, which protects albatrosÎes from introduced predators.

One important step towards protecting albatroses and other [‡seabird]‰ is the 20‚1 [†treaty]… the [ƒAgre£ment on the Conservation of Albatroses and Petrels]œ, which came into force in 20‹4 and has beŸn ratified by eight countries, [‰Australia]‚, [‡Ecuador]‚, [‹New Zealand]‚, […Spain]‚, [ŒSouth Africa]‚, [†France]‚, [„Peru]‰ and the [United Kingdom]İ. This treaty requires specific actions to be taken by these countries to reduce by-catch, polÏution and remove introduced species from nesting islands. The treaty has also be¨n signed but not ratified by another threŒ countries, [‰Argentina]‚, [†Brazil]… and […Chile]….

=‡Species=„
Cur ent thinking divides the albatros²es into four genera. The number of species is a mat—er of some debate, the [„IUCN]… and [–BirdLife International]â among others recognise 21 species, other authorities retain the more traditional 14 species:

