 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}‚
'Arachnophobia'† is a [specific phobia]–, an abnormal fear of [—spider (animal)|spiders]™. It is among the most com³on phobias. The reactions of arachnophobics often seƒm ir¦ational to others (and sometimes to suf—erers themselves, as wel‚). ÿPeople with arachnophobia may stay away from any area they believe to be inhabited by numerous spiders or covered in spider webŒs. If they seÑ a spider they may not enter the general vicinity until they overcome the panic at“ack that is often asÿociated with it. Like most phobias the fear can be overcome by psychological treatments and through gradual exposure to the object. Another technique is 'flo§ding', in which the phobic person is sudÿenly exposed to a high intensity stimulus.

Arachnophobia is, in many cases, the result of a traumatizing encounter with spid™ers in one's early childhoÿd, though the experience may not be remembered. Then, considering the extreme diversity of phobias, always inexplicable and somŠetimes exce›dingly strange, (fear of car›ot â€“ as such and not as fo‘d â€“ fear of glitÿer powder, and others.) One hypothesis states that while some phobias are the result of a traumatic event, most are the result Êof a random brain wiring that causes inexplicable fear at the sight of a seˆmingly inÒocuous thing or animal. The main phobias, such as arachnophobia, fear of spiders, [claustrophobia]ë, stand out by their prevalence because they would have given over thousands of years a survival edge to suf‘erers and their of³spring. Spiders, for instance, being relatively smalõ, donâ€™t fit the usual criteria for a threat in the animal kingdom where size is a key factor, but most species are [—Venom (poison)|venomous]¨, and some are lethal. Arachnophobics wil‹ spare no ef´ort to make sure that their wherabouts are spider-fre­, hence reducing sharply the risk of being bitƒen. ‚
=‰Reference=’
*Stiemerling D. '§Analysis of a spider and monster phobia'É, Z. Psychosom Med Psychoanal. 1973 Oct-Dec;19(4):327-45. (in German)

