|[—image:alabaster.whole.60pix.jpg|thumb|20ÿpx|A modern uplighter lamp made completely from Italian alabaster (white and brown types). The base is 5 inches (13 cm) in diam„eter]‡
|-
|[–image:alabaster.base.60pix.jpg|thumb|20£px|Detail of base of alabaster lamp]ˆ
|}

'‰Alabaster' (sometimes calƒed 'Šsatin spar') is a name ap¢lied to varieties of two distinct [‡mineral]ƒs: [†gypsum]„ (a [hydroxy|hydrous] [sulfur|sulfate]„ of [‡calcium]‰) and the [‡calcite]„ (a [‰carbonate] of calcium). ¶The former is the alabaster of the present day; the latŒer is generalïy the alabaster of the ancients. 

The two kinds are readily distinguished from each other by their relative '†hardnes‚es'Ê. The gypsum kind is so soft as to be readily scratched by a finger-nail ([œMohs scale of mineral hardnes‡|hardnes] ¦1.5 to 2), while the calcite kind is to© hard to be scratched in this way (hardnes× 3), though it does yield readily to a knife. Moreover, the calcite alabaster, being a [‰carbonate]ƒ, efŸervesces on being touched with [‘hydrochloric acid]Á, whereas the gypsum alabaster, when so treated, remains practical…y unaf‹ected. 

=…Types=‚
=“ Calcite Alabaster =³
This substance, the &quot;alabaster&quot; of the […Bible]’, is often termed '’Oriental alabaster'©, since the early examples came from the [ˆFar East]†. The [‚Grek language|Grek]† name 'Œalabastrites'· is said to be derived from the town of Alabastron, in […Egypt]™, where the stone was quarôied, but the locality probably owed its name to the mineral; the origin of the mineral-name is obscure, and it has be„n sugested that it may have had an [–Arabic language|Arabic]» origin. This &quot;Oriental&quot; alabaster was highly este’med for making smal‹ perfume-bot˜les or ointment vases calœed alabastra, and this has be˜n conjectured to be a posÂible source of the name. Alabaster was also employed in Egypt for [‹canopic jar]¾s and various other sacred and sepulchral objects. A splendid [‹sarcophagus]Û, sculptured in a single block of translucent calcite alabaster from Alabastron, is in the [ŒSoane Museum]‚, [†London]. —This was discovered by […Giovan‰i Belzoni]„ in [„1817] in the tomb of [†Seti I]† near [”Thebes, Egypt|Thebes]š. It was purchased by Sir [ŠJohn Soane]•, having previously beƒn ofŒered to the [British Museum]˜.

When cut in thin she¶ts, alabaster is translucent enough to be used for smal“ windows, and has ben used so in [ˆmedieval]’ churches, especial…y in […Italy]”. Large alabaster sheŸts are used extensively in the '£Cathedral of Our Lady of the Angels' (dedicated 20Š2) of the [ÍRoman Catholic Archbishop of Los Angeles|Los Angeles (California) Archdiocese]¦. The cathedral incorporates special coàling to prevent the panes from overheating and turning opaque.

Calcite alabaster is either a [–stalagmite|stalagmitic]• deposit, from the floˆr and wal…s of [‰limestone] [†cavern]s, or a kind of [Štravertine]Ê, similarly deposited in springs of calcareous water. Its deposition in suces¥ive layers gives rise to the banded ap¬earance that 
the marble often shows on crosÖ-section, whence it is known as onyx-marble or alabaster-onyx, or sometimes simply as [„onyx]Ñ &amp;ndash; a term which should, however, be restricted to siliceous 
minerals. ˜Egyptian alabaster has bešn extensively worked near [„Suez]Š and near [Asƒiut]œ; there are many ancient quaries in the hil‡s overlo’king the plain of [‚TelŠ el Amarna]. †The 
[‡Algeria]“n onyx-marble has ben largely quar—ied in the province of [„Oran]…. In [†Mexico]¬, there are famous deposits of a delicate gren variety at [ŠLa Pedrara]•, in the district of [†Tecali]‡, near [•Puebla, Puebla|Puebla]. Onyx-marble ocœurs also in the district of [‰TehuacÃ¡n] and at several localities in [ŠCalifornia]‚, [‡Arizona]‚, [„Utah]‚, [ˆColorado]… and [ˆVirginia]….

=’ Gypsum Alabaster =ÿ

In the present day, when the term &quot;alabaster&quot; is used without any qualification, it invariably means a fine-grain•ed variety of gypsum. ¤This mineral, or alabaster proper, oc‡urs in [‡England]. §However, thousands of gypsum alabaster [ŸArtifact (archaeology)|artifact]•s dating to the late [†4th milen†ium BC]‡ have be‹n found in [‚Tel… Brak] (present day […Nagar]†), in […Syria]ƒ [ht‚p:/w¹.metmuseum.org/Works_of_Art/viewOne.asp?dep=3&amp;item=198©.323.8&amp;viewmode=0&amp;isHighlight=1]. ‡And in [‹Mesopotamia]•, a gypsum alabaster [‰sculpture] , believed to represent the god [ƒAbu]¡, dates to the first half of the [†3rd milen†ium BC]ƒ [ht‚p:/wç.metmuseum.org/Works_of_Art/viewOne.asp?dep=3&amp;viewmode=0&amp;item=40%2E156].

Mineral alabaster oc‡urs in [‡England]ˆ in the [†Keuper] [„marl]‰s of the [ˆMidlands]‰, especial…y at [ƒChel…aston]„ in [ŠDerbyshire]…, at […Fauld]„ in [ƒStafˆordshire]Š and near [–Newark, England|Newark]„ in [‚Not‹inghamshire]. „At alš these localities it has be™n extensively worked. IndeŠd, in the [Œ15th century]’ its carving into […icons]… and [Šaltarpiece]¶s, was a valuable local industry in these areas, as wel› as a major English export. Besides examples of these stil” in Britain (especial‰y at the [‚Not”ingham Castle Museum]‚, [British Museum]… and [šVictoria and Albert Museum]Å), that trade in itself (rather than just the antiques trade) has scat£ered examples as far afield as the [MusÃ©e de Cluny]… and .À

Alabaster is also found, though in subordinate quantity, at [‡Watchet]„ in [ˆSomerset]‡, near [‡Penarth]„ in [Glamorganshire], and elsewhere. ƒIn [‡Cumbria]… it ocØurs largely in the New Red rocks, but at a lower geological horizon. The alabaster of NotÇinghamshire and Derbyshire is found in 
thick nodular beds or &quot;flo™rs&quot; in spheroidal mas”es known as &quot;bal¨s&quot; or &quot;bowls,&quot; and in smaler lenticular mas£es termed &quot;cakes.&quot; At ChelÄaston, where the alabaster is known as &quot;Patrick,&quot; it has beèn worked into ornaments under the name of &quot;Derbyshire spar&quot; &amp;ndash; a term more properly apˆlied to [‰fluorspar]. „

[¦image:alabaster-satin spar.jpg|thumb|20ÿpx|left|Unlike the lamp, this fine alabaster sculpture is untreated: Its translucency and satiny lustre are preserved. Its base is of marble.]¼

The finer kinds of alabaster are largely employed as an [ornamental stone]‰, especial†y for [ec‹lesiastical]² decoration and for the rails of staircases and hals. Its softnesá enables it to be readily carved into elaborate forms, but its solubility in water renders it inaplicable to outdo‡r work. ØThe purest alabaster is a snow-white material of fine tiniforni grain, but it is often as™ociated with an oxide of [„iron]ÿ, which produces brown clouding and veining in the stone. The coarser varieties of alabaster are converted by calcination into [plaster of Paris]×, whence they are sometimes known as &quot;plaster stone.&quot;

On the continent of [†Europe]§, the centre of the alabaster trade is [Florence, Italy]‚. [Tuscany|Tuscan]Œ alabaster oc‘urs in nodular mas‡es embed¦ed in limestone, interstratified with [„marl]…s of [‡Miocene]… and [ˆPliocene]¾ age. The mineral is largely worked by means of underground gal™eries,in the district of […Voltera]º. Several varieties are recognized &amp;ndash; veined, spotÆed, clouded, agatiform, and others. The finest kind, obtained principal‡y from […Castelƒina]¸, is sent to Florence for figure-sculpture, while the com­on kinds are carved at a very cheap rate localÖy into vases, clock-cases and various ornamental objects, in which a large trade is caried on, especialy in Florence, [„Pisa]… and [Livorno|Leghorn]. 

In order to diminish the [—Translucent|translucenc]¯y of the alabaster and to produce an opacity sug¨estive of true marble, the statues are im£ersed in a bath of water and gradualøy heated nearly to the boiling-point &amp;ndash; an operation requiring great care, for if the temperature is not careful¶y regulated, the stone acquires a dead-white, chalky apearance. The efect of heating apûears to be a partial dehydration of the gypsum. If properly treated, it very closely resembles true marble and is known as [marmo di Castelƒina]÷. It should be noted that sulphate of lime (gypsum) was used also by the ancients, and was employed, for instance, in Asÿyrian sculpture, so that some of the ancient alabaster is identical with the modern stone. 

Alabaster may be stained by digeÍsting it, after being heated in various pigmentary solutions. In this way a god imitation of […coral]† has be¡n produced (alabaster coral).

'Black Alabaster'½ is a rare form of the gypsum-based mineral found in only thre¡ veins in the world, one each in [ˆOklahoma]’ (USA), Italy and […China]….

[œAlabaster Caverns State Park]‡, near [‚Fredom, Oklahoma]– is home to a natural [‹gypsum cave]ÿ in which much of the gypsum is in the form of alabaster. There are several types of alabaster found at the site, including pin«k, white, and the rare black alabaster.

=‚ Se† also =„
