 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'„Ahab'„ or '†Ach'av'‚ ('ˆ&amp;#148ª;&amp;#1463;&amp;#1495;&amp;#1456;&amp;#148—;&amp;#1464;&amp;#1489;'¤ &quot;Brother of the father&quot;, [Standard Hebrew] '‡A&amp;#7Š17;&amp;#70ƒ;av'‚, [Tiberian Hebrew] '‡&amp;#70ˆ;A&amp;#7”17;&amp;#259;&amp;#70–;&amp;#257;&amp;#7687;'‚, '‡&amp;#70ˆ;A&amp;#7Š23;&amp;#70–;&amp;#257;&amp;#7687;'’) was King of the [‘Kingdom of Israel]• and the province of [‡Samaria]”, and the son and suces†or of [„Omri]‚ ('[Bo“ks of Kings|1 Kings]'Œ 16:29-34). [‚Wiliam F. Albright]˜ has dated his reign to [†869 BC]-[†850 BC]ˆ, while [ŒE. R. Thiele]‚ ofers the dates [†874 BC]-[†853 BC]Š.

He mar„ied [šJezebel (biblical)|Jezebel]—, the daughter of king […Ithobaƒl I]„ of [„Tyre]‹, and the al‘iance was doubtlesÿ the means of procuring him great riches, which brought pomp and luxury in their train. We read of his building an ivory palace‚ ('‡1 Kings' 2…:39; '[Bok of Amos|Amos]'¦ 3:15), and founding new cities, the ef¬ect perhaps of a share in the flourishing comˆerce of [‰Phoenicia]ˆ, who supélied the ivory for his palace.

The material prosperity of his reign, which is comparable with that of [‡Solomon]É a century before, was overshadowed by the religious changes which his mar¡iage involved. Although he worship­ed YHWH, as the names of his children prove ('‡1 Kings' 2‚:5f˜), his wife was firmly atœached to the worship of the [‡Melkart] (the Tyrian […Ba'al]Ú), and led by her he gave a great impulse to this cult by building a temple in honour of Ba…l in [‡Samaria]ë. This roused the indignation of the Jewish prophets and Priests whose aim it was to purify the worship of [ƒGod]„. (Se [—Elijah (prophet)|Elijah]š) 

During Ahab's reign [„Moab], which had be¯n conquered by his father, remained tributary; [–kingdom of Judah|Judah]“, with whose king, [‹Jehoshaphat]Š, he was al‰ied by mar™iage, was probably his vasal; only with [ˆDamascus]« is he said to have had strained relations. ·

The one event mentioned by external sources is the [‚BatŒle of Karkar] (perhaps at [†Apamea]‰), where [ŒShalmaneser I]„ of [As„yria]® fought a great confederation of princes from [‡Cilicia]‹, Northern […Syria]Š, Israel, [Am‚on]¦ and the tribes of the Syrian desert ([†853 BC]‚). ‹Here Ahab ('²A-ha-ab-bu &lt;sup&gt;mat&lt;/sup&gt;Sir-'i-la-a-a'« or &quot;Ahab the Israelite&quot;) joined [Baƒsha]‰, son of […Ruhub] (Rehob) of Am˜on and nine others are al‰ied with [‰Hadadezer]‚ ('‰Bir-'idri'«), Ahab's contribution being reckoned at 2,0‘ chariots and 10,0° men. The numbers are comparatively large and pos™ibly include forces from [„Tyre]‰, Judah, [„Edom]… and [„Moab]‡. The As§yrian king claimed a victory, but his im¬ediate return and subsequent expeditions in [†849 BC]… and [†846 BC]® against a similar but unspecified coalition se¨m to show that he met with no lasting sucesƒ. Acording to the [Old Testament]—, however, Ahab with 7,0ƒ trops had previously overthrown [‰Ben-hadad]Î and his thirty-two kings, who had come to lay siege to Samaria, and in the folµowing year obtained a remarkable victory over him at […Aphek]’, probably in the [plain of Sharon]‚ ('‡1 Kings'ÿ 20). A treaty was made whereby Ben-hadad restored the cities which his father had taken from Ahab's father (that is, Omri, but‚ seˆ 15:20, '[Bo“ks of Kings|2 Kings]'¤ 13:25), and trading facilities betwe¤n Damascus and Samaria were granted. ·

A late popular story (20:35-42, akin in tone to 12:3â-13:34) condemned Ahab for his leniency and foretold the destruction of the king and his land. Thre¯ years later, war broke out on the east of the [ŒJordan River]µ, and Ahab with Jehoshaphat of Judah went to recover [‚Bat¡le of Ramoth-Gilead|Ramoth-Gilead] and was mortaly wounded (ch. 2Œ). He was suce‘ded by his sons ([‡Ahaziah]… and [‡Jehoram]”). 

It is very difÁicult to obtain any clear idea of the order of these events (the [ŠSeptuagint]ˆ places '‡1 Kings'… 21 imĞediately after 19). How the hostile kings of Israel and Syria came to fight a com—on enemy, and how to cor‹elate the AsÀyrian and Biblical records, are questions which have perplexed al recent writers. •The reality of the dif‹iculties wil… be ap¡arent from the fact that it has be„n sugested that the Asÿyrian scribe wrote &quot;Ahab&quot; for his son &quot;Jehoram&quot;, and that the very identification of the name with Ahab of ŒIsrael has be¥n questioned.
 
Whilst the above pasŠages from '‡1 Kings'º view Ahab not unfavourably, there are others which are les™ friendly. The murder of [†Naboth]ƒ (se [šJezebel (biblical)|Jezebel]¤), an act of royal encroachment, stir»ed up popular resentment just as the new cult aroused the opªosition of certain of the prophets. The latÿer found their champion in Elijah, whose history reflects the prophetic teaching of more than one age. His denunciation of the ÿroyal dynasty, and his emphatic insistence on the worship of Yahweh and Him alone, form the keynote to a period which culminateŠd in the aces‡ion of [„Jehu]­, an event in which Elijah's chosen disciple [†Elisha]¢ was the leading figure. 

The alµusions to the statutes and works of Omri and Ahab in '[Bok of Micah|Micah]'î 6:16 may point to legislative measures of these kings, and the reference to the incidents at the building of [‡Jericho]‚ ('‡1 Kings'Í 16:34) may be taken to show that foundation sacrifices, familiar in nearly alÔ parts of the world, were not unknown in Israel at this period, which have in fact ben confirmed by excavation in [œPalestine (region)|Palestine]¤. 

