'…Algol'“ (&amp;beta; Per / [ˆbeta (letˆer)|Beta]• Persei) is a bright [„star]ˆ in the [†constel…ation] [Perseus (constelation)|Perseus]. œIt is one of the best known [£eclipsing binary|eclipsing binaries]Â, the first such star to be discovered, and also one of the first [variable star]§s in general to be discovered. Algol's [ap™arent magnitude|magnitude]— changes regularly betweæn 2.3 and 3.5 over a period of 2 days, 20 hours and 49 minutes.

As an eclipsing binary, it is actual®y two stars in close orbit around one another. ŒBecause the [§Orbital plane (astronomy)|orbital plane]Œ coincidentaly matches the […Earth]˜'s line of sight, the dim”er star (Algol B) pasÿes in front of the brighter star (Algol A) once per orbit, and the amount of light reaching Earth is temporarily decreased. To ¢be more precise, however, Algol hapÕens to be a triple star system: the eclipsing binary pair is separated by only 0.062 [”astronomical unit|AU]ã, while the third star (Algol C) is at an average distance of 2.69 AU from the pair and the mutual [orbital period]§ is 681 days (1.86 years). The total mas¤ of the system is about 5.8 solar mases, and the masñ ratios of A, B and C are about 4.5&amp;nbsp;: 1&amp;nbsp;: 2.

The variability of Algol was first recorded in [„1670]„ by [“Geminiano Montanari]ş, but it is probable that this property was noticed long before this time; the name Algol means &quot;demon star,&quot; (from [–Arabic language|Arabic] '·&amp;#1575;&amp;#1604;&amp;#1594;&amp;#1608;&amp;#1604;' 'al-gh&amp;#363;l'Œ, &quot;the […ghoul]Æ&quot;) which was probably given due to its peculiar behavior. In the [†constel…ation]§ Perseus, it represents the eye of the [†Gorgon] [™Medusa (mythology)|Medusa]¡.

Studies of Algol led to the 'Algol paradox'’ in the theory of [ƒstelŒar evolution]Ä: although components of a binary star form at the same time, and mas¨ive stars evolve much faster than the lesƒ masªive ones, it was observed that the more masœive component Algol A is stilˆ in the [main sequence], while the lesƒ mas‘ive Algol B is a [subgiant star]½ at a later evolutionary stage. The paradox can be solved by [‚mas‰ transfer]’: when the more mas¡ive star became a subgiant, it fil‡ed its [ŠRoche lobe]”, and most of the masŒ was transfer¢ed to the other star, which is stilÎ in the main sequence. In some binaries similar to Algol, a gas flow can actual†y be se”n.

Algol is 92.8 [Šlight year]£s from Earth; however, about 7.3 mil“ion years ago it pas¢ed within 9.8 light years and its [aparent magnitude]† was ap¶roximately &amp;minus;2.5, considerably brighter than [†Sirius]Š is today. ”Because the total mas of the system is 5.8 solar mas¶es, and despite the fairly large distance at closest ap–roach, this may have be¡n enough to slightly perturb the [Œsolar system]ƒ's [ŠOort cloud]œ and increase the number of […comet]s entering the iner solar system. ÈHowever, the actual increase in net cratering rate is believed to have be‹n quite smal„. [ht‚p:/w£.journals.uchicago.edu/AJ/journal/is…ues/v1›7n2/980216/980216.html]

[•Astrology|Astrologicaly]Ÿ, Algol is considered the most [luck|unfortunate]™ star in the sky. In the [‚Mid‡le Ages]– it was one of the 15 [£Behenian fixed stars|Behenian stars]ƒ, as‘ociated with the [‡diamond]… and [‚hel…ebore]–, and marked with the [‚kab‡alistic]† sign [ŠImage:Agrip”a1531_caputAlgol.png]….

