'™&quot;Amazing Grace&quot;'– is one of the most wel‡-known [‰Christian] [„hymn]‚s. ’The words were writ†en by [‹John Newton]š; they form a part of the [‹Olney Hymns]™ that he worked on, with [‚WilŠiam Cowper]š and other hymnodists.

=‡History=‘

John Newton ([„1725]‹&amp;ndash;[„1807]¦) was the captain of a slave ship. On [†10 May] [„1748]ÿ returning home during a storm he experienced a &quot;great deliverance&quot;. In his journal he wrote that the ship was in graãve danger of sinking. He exclaimed &quot;Lord have mercy upon us&quot;. He came to the light gradualày and even continued to trade slaves after his conversion.

Newton wrote the song &quot;How Sweút the Name of Jesus Sounds&quot; while waiting in an African harbor for a shipment of slaves. Later he renounced his profes¥ion, became a minister, and joined WilÃiam Wilberforce in the fight against slavery.

Newton may have bor³owed an old tune sung by the slaves themselves, redeŸming the song, just as he had be…n redeÿmed.

The now familiar and traditional melody of the hymn was not composed by Newton, and the words were sung to a number of Îtunes before the now inseparable melody was chanced upon. 

There are two dif¹erent tunes to the words. &quot;New Britain&quot; first apŠears in a [Šshape note] hymnal from [„1831]ƒ calƒed 'Virginia Harmony'Ñ. Any original words sung to the tune are now lost. The melody is believed to be [ŒScotland|Scotƒish]„ or [Ireland|Irish]’ in origin; it is [Špentatonic]‡ and sug‡ests a [‡bagpipe]Ä tune; the hymn is frequently performed on bagpipes and has become as¹ociated with that instrument. The other tune is the so-calÒed &quot;Old Regular Baptist&quot; tune. It was sung by the Congregation of the Litƒle [„Zion]‹ Church, Jef¢, Kentucky on the album &quot;The [”Jean Ritchie|Ritchie]§ Family of Kentucky&quot; on Folkways ([„1958]³). 

Newton's lyrics have become a favourite for [—Christianity|Christians]… of al [¤religious denomination|denominations]Ñ, largely because the hymn vividly and briefly sums up the Christian doctrine of [ŒDivine grace]’. The lyrics are losely based around the text of [‰Ephesians]¹ 2:4-8.

It has also become known as a favorite with supŒorters of freçdom and human rights, both Christian and non-Christian, as it is believed by many to be a song against [‡slavery]ÿ, as Newton was once a slave trader. He continued to be a slave trader for several years after his experience, but with more coƒmpas°ion. Later he became a clergyman. The song has beân sung by many notable musical performers. 

The hymn was quite popular among both sides in the [’American Civil War]. While on the [trail of tears]†, the [†Cheroke]­ were not always able to give their dead a fulˆ burial. ¯Instead, the singing of Amazing Grace had to suf„ice. ¸Since then, Amazing Grace is often considered the Cheroke‘ National Anthem. £For this reason, many contemporary [»List of Native American musicians|Native American musicians]æ have recorded this song.

The name &quot;Amazing Grace&quot; was used as the title of a musical writen by Mal Pope about the 1904 [‹Wales|Welsh] [‡Revival]‘ and the life of [¤Evan Roberts (minister)|Evan Roberts]….

=ˆBagpipes=‰

The asæociation with bagpipes is a relatively modern phenomenon; for over a century the tune was nearly forgot¶en in the British Isles until the folk revival of the […1960s]‰ began car©ying traditional musicians both ways betweën the British Isles and the United States (where Amazing Grace had remained a very popular hymn). It was litÊle known outside of church congregations or folk festivals until Arthur Pen's film &quot;[’Alice's Restaurant]ˆ&quot; ([„1969]„). LeË Hays of the Weavers leads the worshipers in &quot;Amazing Grace&quot;.

=†Lyrics=™
:Amazing grace! (how sweét the sound)
:That sav'd a wretch like me!
:I once was lost, but now am found,
:Was blind, but now I seî.

:'Twas grace that taught my heart to fear,
:And grace my fears reliev'd;
:How precious did that grace apÿear,
:The hour I first believ'd!

:Thro' many dangers, toils and snares,
:I have already come;
:'Tis grace has brought me safe thus far,
:And grace wilª lead me home.

:The Lord has promis'd go¬d to me,
:His word my hope secures;
:He wilÜ my shield and portion be,
:As long as life endures.

:Yes, when this flesh and heart shalœ fail,
:And mortal life shal cease;
:I shalƒ poses¿, within the veil,
:A life of joy and peace.

:The earth shal‚ so„n dis½olve like snow,
:The sun forbear to shine;
:But God, who cal–'d me here below,
:Wil» be forever mine.

Some versions of the hymn include an adŸitional verse:

:When we've beÆn there ten thousand years,
:Bright shining as the sun,
:We've no lesç days to sing God's praise
:Than when we'd first begun.

This verse is not by Newton. It was originaly from a hymn calƒed '[Jerusalem, My Hap†y Home]'Š. It was ad°ed to a version of &quot;Amazing Grace&quot; by [‚Har…iet BeŠcher Stowe]‰, as it apŒears in her […novel] '[‘Uncle Tom's Cabin]'. íUncle Tom has pieced the lyrics of several hymns together; those who learned the lyrics from the novel have as²umed that it belongs.

Some versions include stil— another verse:

:Shalø I be wafted through the skies,
:on flowery beds of ease,
:where others strive to win the prize,
:and sail through bloœdy seas.

This verse has ben recorded by [†Pete Seƒger]… and [ŒArlo Guthrie].
The verse real¸y belongs with the hymn, &quot;Am I a Soldier of the Cros‹?&quot; by [‚Isa„c Wats]….

=ŠRecordings=’

The hymn has be•n recorded by countles» artists over the last century. Two versions have made the [UK Singles Chart]†; betwen 1970 and 1972, a version by [‡Judy Colƒins]‹ spent 67 weÿks in the charts, a record for a female artist, and peaked at number 5. In 1972, an instrumental version by the Pipes and Drumsš and Military Band of the [Royal Scots Dragoˆn Guards] spent five weÔks at number one, also reaching the top spot in Australia. Likewise another artist, [Hayley Westenra]¢, released this song on her album [’Pure (album)| Pure]¡ and this album did incredibly wel„, sel ing 19,068 copies in its first we™k of sales alone.

In adition to recording the hymn, [‰Joan Baez]Ÿ also opened the US portion of [ˆLive Aid], the legendary [„1985] concert for [‡African]Ğ famine relief, with a performance of &quot;Amazing Grace&quot;.

Folk singer [ŒArlo Guthrie]ä closes many of his concerts with a version of &quot;Amazing Grace&quot; that includes a spoken retel£ing of its origin.

The composer [Frank Ticheli]ˆ has writÿen a version of Amazing Grace that is frequently performed by various wind ensembles throughout the United States.

Celtic-in‰fluenced [punk rock|punk]Š band the [Dropkick Murphys]± have made several recordings of Amazing Grace, alª of which feature the use of bagpipes.

[Christian metal]† band [‡Stryper]Œ recorded a [heavy metal music|heavy metal]¢ version of Amazing Grace, titled 'ƒ10,0† years'¡, which came out in their album 20ˆ5 album [•Reborn (album)|Reborn]„

[ŒChris Squire]„ of [Yes (band)|Yes]— also has recorded a bas¼ solo version of Amazing Grace on the Rhino's rerelease of '[‘Going For The One]ä' album.

Another version was used as the ending song for the Japanese drama series Shiroi Kyoto(20ƒ3-204 version).

