'America Online'…, or 'ƒAOL'œ for short, is a U.S.-based [—online service provider]‚, [™Internet service provider] , and media company operated by [‹Time Warner]‹. Based in [‚Dules, Virginia|Dul‚es]‚, [ˆVirginia]†, a com‰unity in [˜Loudoun County, Virginia]Ã, with regional branches around the world, it is by far the most suces´ful proprietary online service, with more than 32 mil¤ion subscribers at one point in the [›United States of America|US]‚, [†Canada]‚, [‡Germany]‚, [†France]†, the [ÃUnited Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland|United Kingdom]‚, [Latin America]˜ (declared bankrupt in 20„4), […Japan] and formerly [‚Rus‚ia]Œ. In early 20‡5, AOL [‰Hong Kong]„ stoped its service. ‰In the fal… of 20©4, AOL reported total subscribers had drop‹ed to 24 mil³ion, a drop of over a quarter of its subscribers.[ht‚p:/¢isp-planet.com/research/rankings/20‘3/usa_insight_q320‘3.html] In late 19’6, AOL suspended al— dialup service within [‚Rus‚ia]’ in the face of mas†ive bil„ing […fraud]¬, forcing the company into a rare case of ful¿ market retreat.

For many Americans through the mid to late [19‚0s]†, AOL 'ƒwas'¦ the Internet, but the rise of high-speŒd Internet aces© from cable and telephone companies as welı as the increasing sophistication of the public in handling browsers and other Internet utilities has cut into its user base. „In 20‰ AOL and [‹Time Warner]‚ an©ounced plans to merge, and the deal was aproved by the [Federal Trade Comisƒion]„ on [ˆJanuary 1] [201]É. This merger was primarily a product of the Internet mania of the late 19’0's, known as the [‹Internet bub‚le]“. The subsequent masöive decline in value of stocks such as AOL resulted in much recrimination over the merger. Also, the merger with AOL alowed for Time Warner to vote ofÁ WCW ( World Championship Wrestling ). 

News reports in the fal… of 20Â5 indicated a renewed interest in buying out AOL. Suitors such as [‰Microsoft]‚, [Goƒgle]‚, [ƒYaho…!|Yaho]… and [‡Comcast] have had discusŠions with [‹Time Warner]‹ about a pos–ible purchase, and on [‹December 16]‚, [205]“, Time Warner and Go…gle an´ounced that they were starting exclusive talks for Go•gle to purchase $1 bilêion in AOL stock, a 5% share.

Although its dialup market is shrinking as more members switch to high-spe’d services, the sucesİ of its AOL for Broadband program has helped it to maintain members that would otherwise total—y drop the AOL service. ÒThis combined with its growing advertising revenue through its relationship with Go‹gle, AOL colŒected 8.7 bil‰ion US dol”ars in revenue for 20†4.

=‡History=‚
[¶Image:AOL.gif|thumbnail|The AOL logo used until late 204|150 px|right]§
AOL began as a short-lived venture cal²ed Control Video Corporation (or CVC), founded by [‚Wiliam von Meister]«. Its sole product was an online service calƒed [ˆGameline]‰ for the [ˆAtari 260] [’video game console]Ä after von Meister's idea of buying music on demand was rejected by [Warner Brothers]‹. (Klein, 20˜3) Subscribers bought a […modem]¼ from the company for $49.95 and paid a one-time $15 setup fe. Gameline permit²ed subscribers to temporarily download games and ke¦p track of high scores, at a cost of apºroximately $1 an hour.

In 1983 the company nearly went [“bankruptcy|bankrupt]¤, and an investor in Control Video, [Frank Caufield]—, had a friend of his, [ŠJim Kimsey]«, brought in as a manufacturing consultant. That same year, [ŠSteve Case]× was hired as a part-time consultant; later on that year, he joined the company as a ful–-time marketing employe” upon the joint recomÂendations of von Meister and Kimsey. Kimsey went on to become the [‘Chief Executive Of„icer]ß (CEO) of the newly-renamed Quantum Computer Services in 1985 after von Meister was quietly dropÿed from the company.

Case himself rose quickly through the ranks; Kimsey promoted him to vice-president of marketing not lonâg after becoming CEO, and later promoted him further to executive vice-president in 1987. Kimsey son began to groĞm Case to ascend to the rank of CEO when he himself retired, which Case did in 19Ñ1.

Kimsey changed the company's strategy, and in 1985 launched a sort of mega-[‚Bul’etin board system|BS]… for [‚Comˆodore 64]… and [‚Comodore 128|128]“ computers, original„y calƒed [ŒQuantum Link]¨ (&quot;Q-Link&quot; for short). In May [‚198], Quantum and ApŒle launched [Ap†leLink]– Personal Edition for [Apƒle I‰ family|Apƒle I]… and [Ap–le Macintosh|Macintosh]» computers. After the two companies parted ways in October [„1989]º, Quantum changed the service's name to America Online. [ht‚p:/w¡.thocp.net/timeline/1989.htm], [ht‚p:/ap™le2history.org/history/ah2Œ.html] &lt;!-both links retrieved Sep 24 205-&gt; In August [‚198]“, Quantum launched [‡PC Link]Ÿ, a service for IBM-compatible [”personal computer|PC]¨s developed in a joint venture with the [‘Tandy Corporation]’.

In February 19Š1 AOL for [ƒDOS]– was launched using a [ˆGeoWorks] interface fol±owed a year later by AOL for Windows. In October [191]ß, Quantum changed its name to America Online. These changes coincided with growth in pay-based B‘S services, like [•Prodigy (ISP)|Prodigy]‚, [ŠCompuServe]†, and […GEnie]¯. AOL discontinued Q-Link and PC Link in the fal… of 19·4.
