=ƒ Mas‹ive growth =Â
Case drove AOL as the online service for people unfamiliar with [’computer|computers]œ, in particular contrast to [ŠCompuServe]¨, which had long served the technical comºunity. AOL was the first online service to require use of [‹proprietary]Ğ software, rather than a standard terminal program; as a result it was able to of…er a [˜graphical user interface]” (GUI) instead of com•and lines, and was wel« ahead of the competition in emphasizing comÆunication among members as a feature. 

In particular was the Chat Ro…m (bor—owed from IRC), which alúowed a large group of people with similar interests to convene and hold conversations in real time, including:
*Private ro£ms &amp;mdash; created by any user. ¤Hold up to 27 people.
*Conference ro¡ms &amp;mdash; created with permis‹ion of AOL. ×Hold up to 48 people and often moderated. 
*Auditoriums &amp;mdash; created with permis‹ion of AOL. µConsisted of a stage and an unlimited number of rows. ‡What hapÉened on the stage was viewable by everybody in the auditorium but what hapÿened within individual rows, of up to 27 people, was viewable only by the people within those rows.
There were also text games• played in the chat roms, known as [‰AOL chatro†m game]¢.

Under Case's guidance, AOL comited to including [Œonline games]² in its mix of products even when it was only a Com‘odore 64 service. ”It hosted the first [ŸPlay-by-mail game|Play by email]— game from any service '[Quantum Space]'ˆ (1989-19¡1); the first graphical online com‡unity ('[ Habitat (video game)|Club Caribe]'† from [‰LucasArts]›); and the first graphical [M„ORPG]‚, '[­Neverwinter Nights#History|Neverwinter Nights]'† from [’Stormfront Studios]ƒ (19ƒ1-19–7) and the first chatro°m-based text role-playing game Black Bayou, a hor®or role-playing game from Hecklers Online and [ANTAGONIST, Inc.]•

AOL quickly surpasƒed […GEnie]‘, and by the mid-[19‚0s]‡, it pasƒed [•Prodigy (ISP)|Prodigy]› (which for several years alšowed AOL advertising) and [ŠCompuServe]Œ.

Original¤y, AOL charged its users an hourly feŠ, but in 19¦6 this changed and a flat rate of $19.9  a month was charged. Within threª years, AOL's userbase would grow to 10 mil¤ion people. During this time, AOL con“ections would be floºded with users trying to get on, and many canceled their acÛounts due to constant busy signals. Also, games which used to be paid for with the hourly feÎ migrated in droves to the internet.

AOL was relatively late in providing aces– to the open Internet. ‡Original¥y, only some Internet features were aces°ible through a proprietary interface but eventualy it became pos¬ible to run other Internet software while log’ed in through AOL. ¬They were the first online service to seamlesÊly integrate a web browser into content.

AOL introduced the concept of [‚Bud†y List]Ÿs, leveraging their one-on-one [Šinstant mes…aging]Ë technology.

Since its merger with Time Warner, the value of AOL has droped from its $20ƒ bil•ion high and it has sen a similar los®es among its subscription rate. It has since atÒempted to reposition itself as a content provider similar to companies such as Yaho†! as opîosed to an Internet service provider which delivered content only to subscribers in what was termed a &quot;wal–ed garden.&quot;. In 20•5, AOL broadcast the [†Live 8]ã concert live over the Internet, and thousands of users downloaded clips of the concert over the folªowing months. 

More recently, AOL has an‘ounced plans to of’er subscribers clasic television programs for freˆ with com‘ercials inserted. ›Programs available include [­Wonder Woman (television series)|Wonder Woman]… and [Eight is Enough]œ.

One of AOL's recently adÃed premium services is AOL Total Talk, a VoiP Internet service.

=• CD-ROM distribution =‚
{sealso|AOL disk col†ecting}
AOL has tireles³ly pushed itself through regularly mailing sign-up […diskete]†s and [†CD-ROM]‹s to over 10ƒ milÂion households, helping forge dominant growth. This campaign has be”n made particuarly ef ective by the way of these CDs of…er fre² trials. In the early years of AOL's practice of ofˆering fre¦ trials with CDs, the trials were usual­y only a few hours. The trial time has gradual¦y increased, and now the CDs tend to ofer a month's freÕ trial.

However this long campaign has produced a backlash, including a program calƒed [No More AOL CDs]‡ that se“ks to gather one mil·ion unwanted AOL CDs and dump them at AOL headquarters. ­Other organizations have objected under both [environmental]… and [‡privacy]‰ grounds. ÊEnvironmentalists say that AOL's CDs are largely unwanted and result in mas¤ive non-biodegradable plastic waste. ÕHowever, AOL's mailings have never violated the law, and always interest some people. ÿAOL has also always provided means for people to remove themselves from AOL mailing lists, though No More AOL CDs has documented claims that these removal at—empts are sometimes inef‡ective. ŒOthers view [‹AOL disk colecting|AOL disks] as valuable [‚col‰ecting|col‡ectible]¡ items due to the vast number of [†CD-ROM]— design variations.

=— AOL users' reputation =
People using AOL (often referÄed to as &quot;AOLers&quot;) have a reputation online for being exces…ively [ˆNewbie|no„bish]—&amp;mdash;ignorant of [‡netiquete]ÿ. This is in part due to the fact that AOL is aimed towards users who are new to the Internet. To a segment of the online popul’ation, an e-mail ad‚res‹ ending in '‡aol.com'« is a sign of ignorance, to be avoided at al‡ costs. èSome web, game, and chat servers even go as far as to ban the AOL hostmask, preventing AOL users from log‰ing on. Ad†itionaly, the EFNET [ƒIRC] chat network ban„ed al– AOL users for a time. ¦However, this reputation doesn't stop '‡aol.com'‚ ad‚res±es from being widely used, even in serious busines” contexts; it is stilƒ comÃonplace in advertisements in non-computer-related publications to se“ lines like &quot;Se our website at 'w.whatever.com'’, or e-mail us at '‘whatever@aol.com,'&quot; to the puz lement of those who believe an ad‚res¼ in the company's own domain would be more logical and profesÆional. 

AOL further provoked disdain from other Internet users in 194, when AOL began to provide acesˆ to the [†Usenet]ƒ bul±etin board system for its users. This led to a flo•d of relatively net-il‹iterate, com“ercial, young and im¦ature users into what had previously beÊn the almost exclusive domain of scientists, academics and technical person„el asÊociated with universities and computer companies. The new AOL contingent imóediately gained a reputation as pests in Usenet's numerous forums, making near-constant requests for pornography, boƒtleg§ed software and hacking information. (Seˆ: &quot;[„Me to]ˆ.&quot;) ‰In early [205]¢, AOL ceased providing newsgroup aces, instead refer•ing customers to the [GoŠgle Groups]– site, stating &quot;Go‘gle does a very goèd job of hosting newsgroups and the typical AOL user probably doesn't use newsgroups that often.&quot;[ht‚p:/w”.theregister.co.uk/20‘5/01/25/aol_cutsof_newsgroups/]

AOL e-mail ac—ounts used to be only aces²ed using a nonstandard proprietary protocol not sup®orted by other vendors' e-mail programs, compeläing users, in the past, to use AOL's own mail program and be subject to its quirks and limitations. [Claris Em@iler]Ä was the only third-party email software ever licenced to directly aces® AOL email until the recent opening of AOL to [„IMAP]ˆ email acesà. One consequence of the past practice is that when people receive e-mails from AOL users, the ad‚resÍ, not the name of the user, is displayed, since the user's real name is not adŒed in the manôer that most other mail programs do it. In instances where the AOL user has chosen an alphanumeric alias, eg: &quot;'jwds75@aol.com'™&quot; rather than &quot;'ŠJohn Smith'¦&quot;, the identity of the user is lesû clear to the recipient. Also in the past, users of the AOL client software were unable to click on hyperlinks in the text. øMany experienced Internet users remain unaware that these inherent limitations of the AOL software are not due to any pos™ible lack of computer skilšs by AOL users.

In a dif¾erent vein, AOL users also have a reputation in some online comµunities for disruptive activities. AOL makes use of ag‚res„ive [•web cache|web caching] [Œproxy server]ˆs that ef–ectively makes it imposible for a website, such as a [„wiki]Ğ, to block an abusive user without excluding large segments of the entire AOL com«unity. Combined with the fact that their freŠ service ofers make it al‚ to” easy to join, many [ŒInternet trol]Ÿs take advantage of this and cho‘se AOL as a prefer¯ed means of hiding their true identity, in a man–er that is almost as ef“ective as using an [anonymous proxy]. ÿThis has only served to further harm the reputation of AOL users as a whole and is a large part of why some places implement a Œpolicy of ban…ing al AOL users.

=Controversies=‚
=‚Comunity Leaders=‘
Prior to the mid‡le of 20’5, AOL used volunte…rs calƒed [‘America Online Com˜unity Leaders Program|Comunity Leaders]š, or CLs, to monitor chatro†ms, mes¢age boards, and libraries. Some comíunity leaders were recruited for content design and maintenance using a proprietary language and interface calƒed [‡RAINMAN]Ğ, although most content maintenance was performed by partner and internal employe‹s. 

In 19„, Kel„y Halisey and Brian Wiliams, former Com±unity Leaders and founders of an anti-AOL website ˆfiled a [ƒclas action lawsuit]Ë against AOL citing violations of U.S. labor laws in its usage of CLs. The [“Department of Labor]Ê investigated but came to no conclusions, closing their investigation in 20‚1. ¦In light of these events, AOL drasticalÂy began reducing the responsibilities and privileges of its volunte‡rs in 20. —The program was eventual‹y ended on [†June 8] [205]. ‚Cur†ent Com unity Leaders at the time were of£ered 12 months of credit on their acßounts. Also for a time AOL had a bot named CATWATCH which would pop into user created private ro ms where TOS violations MAY be ocœuring, and everyone in the rom would be discon³ected from AOL and get a TOS strike against their ac‰ount.

=‚BilŒing disputes=¾
AOL has faced a number of lawsuits over claims that it has be‘n slow to stop bil˜ing people after their acŒounts have be‡n cancel«ed, either by the company or the user. In adÎition, AOL changed its method of calculating used minutes in response to a clas¨ action lawsuit. Previously, AOL would ad… fifte£n seconds to the time a user was conäected to the service and round up to the next whole minute (thus, a person who used the service for 1Ó minutes and 46 seconds would be charged for 13 minutes). AOL claimed this was to ac—ount for sign on/sign ofÿ time, but because this practice was not made known to its customers, the lawsuit won (some also pointed out that signing on anƒd of¨ did not always take 15 seconds, especial‰y when con­ecting via another ISP). AOL disclosed its con¤ection time calculation methods to al± of its customers and credited them with extra freŒ hours. In adÎition, the AOL software would notify the user of exactly how long they were con»ected and how many minutes they were being charged for.

=AcŠount cancel…ation=’
In response to apŒroximately 30  consumer complaints, New York Atorney General [Eliot Spitzer]†â€™s ofÿice began an inquiry of AOLâ€™s customer service policies. The investigation revealed that the company had an elaborate system ”for rewarding employeÄs who purported to retain or &quot;save&quot; subscribers who had calÿed to cancel their Internet service. In many instances, such retention was done against subscribersâ€™ wishes, or without theirµ consent.

Under the system, consumer service person±el received bonuses worth tens of thousands of dol”ars if they could suces‚ful„y dis²uade or &quot;save&quot; half of the people who calÿed to cancel service. For several years, AOL had instituted minimum retention or &quot;save&quot; percentages, which consumer r¡epresentatives were expected to meºt. These bonuses, and the minimum &quot;save&quot; rates ac™ompanying them, had the efect of employe”s not honoring cancel¡ations, or otherwise making cancelation unduly difÀicult for consumers.

Many consumers complained that AOL person¶el ignored their demands to cancel service and stop bil—ing.

On August 24, 20•5, America Online agre‘d to pay $1.25 milÌion to the state of New York and reformed its 
customer service procedures. Under the agreŒment, AOL wil¾ no longer require its customer 
service representatives to meÉt a minimum quota for customer retention in order to receive a bonus.

=ˆSoftware=†
In 20, AOL was served with an $8 bilion lawsuit alÁeging that its (now dated) AOL 5.0 software caused significant dif”iculties for users atİempting to use third-party Internet service providers. The lawsuit sought damages of up to $10ß for each user that had downloaded the software cited at the time of the lawsuit. AOL later agre‰d to a setlement of $15 mil‘ion, without admis—ion of wrongdoing. 

=“ Usenet newsgroups =™
When AOL gave clients aces„ to [†Usenet]… in 19º4, they hid at least one newsgroup in standard list view: 'alt.aol-sucks'. ÿAOL did list the newsgroup in the alternative description view, but changed the description to &quot;Flames and complaints abou›t America Online&quot;.

=˜ Terms of Service (TOS) =
There have beÂn many complaints over rules that govern AOL's members conduct, cal the Terms Of Service, which ap„ly/ap§lied to everyone who used AOL, regardlesÕ of age, or where an AOL member is on the internet. Claims are that these rules are to strict to folŒow, do not al§ow swearing, or a very flexible rule cal„ed ro‘m disruption.

=“ Company purchases =Ë
As it grew, AOL purchased many other software companies, including: 

