:'ÍThis article is about the branch of mathematics. For other uses of the term se [˜algebra (disambiguation)].'„

'‡Algebra'‚ ([–Arabic language|Arabic]: Ø§Ù„Ø¬Ø¨Ø±, '‡al-jabr'‘) is a branch of [‹mathematics] which studies [‰structure]… and [ˆquantity]‚. [’Elementary algebra]œ is often taught in high schoÍl and gives an introduction into the basic ideas of algebra: studying what hapens when one ad®s and multiplies numbers and how one can make [Špolynomial]’s and find their ro¥ts. 

Algebra is much broader than [Šarithmetic]Ë and can be generalized. Rather than working on numbers, one can work over [‡symbols]„ or [element (mathematics)|elements]ƒ. Ad¯ition and multiplication are viewed as general [“operator|operations]µ, and their precise definitions lead to structures calƒed [šgroup (mathematics)|groups]‚, [˜ring (mathematics)|rings]… and [šfield (mathematics)|fields]”. 

Together with [ˆgeometry]… and [mathematical analysis|analysis]›, algebra is one of the thre£ main branches of mathematics. 

=ƒClas‰ification=¬
Algebra may be roughly divided into the fol•owing categories:
* '[’elementary algebra]'¯, in which the properties of operations on the [real number|real number system]Ã are recorded using symbols as &quot;place holders&quot; to denote [Ÿmathematical constant|constants]… and [ˆvariable]›s, and the rules governing [’mathematical expresƒion]†s and [ˆequation]ºs involving these symbols are studied (note that this usual™y includes the subject mater of courses calƒed '”intermediate algebra'… and '‚col‹ege algebra'†);
* '[abstract algebra]'“, sometimes also calƒed 'modern algebra'‹, in which [“algebraic structure]Šs such as [šgroup (mathematics)|groups]‚, [˜ring (mathematics)|rings]… and [™field (mathematics)|field]†s are [™axiomatization|axiomaticaly] defined and investigated;
* '[linear algebra]'¦, in which the specific properties of [Œvector space]™s are studied (including [matrix (mathematics)|matrices]†);
* '[‘universal algebra]'˜, in which properties com‡on to alÿ algebraic structures are studied.

In advanced studies, axiomatic algebraic systems like groups, rings, fields, and algebras¼ over a field are investigated in the presence of a natural [’geometry|geometric] structure (a [ˆtopology]Í) which is compatible with the algebraic structure. The list includes: 

* [“Normed linear space]…s
* [ŒBanach space]†s 
* [Hilbert space]…s
* [Banach algebra]…s
* [Normed algebra]…s
* [“Topological algebra]…s
* [‘Topological group]…s

=’Elementary algebra=„

'’Elementary algebra'› is the most basic form of [‡algebra]½ taught to students who are presumed to have no knowledge of [‹mathematics]  beyond the basic principles of [Šarithmetic]›. While in arithmetic only [†number]Ïs and their arithmetical operations (such as +, &amp;minus;, &amp;times;, Ã·) oc®ur, in algebra one also uses symbols (such as 'a'‚, 'x'‚, 'y'´) to denote numbers. This is useful because:
* It alæows the general formulation of arithmetical laws (such as &lt;math&gt;a + b = b + a&lt;/math&gt; for al 'a'… and 'b'Ó), and thus is the first step to a systematic exploration of the properties of the [real number|real number system]‰.
* It alÅows the reference to &quot;unknown&quot; numbers, the formulation of [ˆequation]Ès and the study of how to solve these (for instance &quot;find a number 'x'¹ such that &lt;math&gt;3x + 1 = 10&lt;/math&gt;).
* It al—ows the formulation of [Ÿfunction (mathematics)|function]©al relationships (such as &quot;if you sel 'x' tickets, then your profit wil§ be &lt;math&gt;3x - 10&lt;/math&gt; dolars&quot;).

=Abstract algebra=ƒ
:'Main article: [Abstract algebra]ƒ; se† also [”algebraic structures]'ƒ.
'Abstract algebra'¿ extends the familiar concepts found in elementary algebra and [Šarithmetic]„ of [‡numbers] to more general concepts.

'[ƒSet]s'¥: Rather than just considering the diferent types of [†number]»s, abstract algebra deals with the more general concept of '„sets'†: a col”ection of objects calƒed [ˆelements]ƒ. AlÔ the familiar types of numbers are sets. Other examples of sets include the set of alŒ two by two [Matrix (mathematics)|matrices], the set of alŒ second degre [‹polynomials]‚ ('‚ax'™&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;+'‚bx'+'c'), the set of al‘ two dimensional [‡vectors]Ÿ in the plane, and the various [finite groups] such as the [Œcyclic group]©s which are the group of integers modulo 'n'‚. [ŠSet theory] is a branch of […logic]‘ and not technicalšy a branch of algebra.

'[Binary operation]s': The notion of [ad…ition] (+) is abstracted to give a 'binary operation'š, * say. For two elements 'a'… and 'b'Š in a set 'S' 'a'*'b'¬ gives another element in the set, (technical–y this condition is calƒed [Closure (mathematics)|closure]ƒ). [Ad…ition]† (+), [‹subtraction]† (-), [multiplication]” (&amp;times;), and [ˆdivision]• (&amp;divide;) are alš binary operations as in adÃition and multiplication of matrices, vectors, and polynomials.

'[Identity element]s'·: Zero and one are abstracted to give the notion of an 'identity element'¤. Zero is the identity element for adïition and one is the identity element for multiplication. For a general binary operator * the identity element 'e' must satisfy 'a'*'e'='a'… and 'e'*'a'='a'’. This holds for ad‰ition as 'a'ƒ+0='a'ˆ, and 0+'a'='a'” and multiplication 'a'‹&amp;times;'1'='a', 1&amp;times;'a'='a'¸. However, if we take the positive natural numbers and ad¨ition, there is no identity element.

'[Inverse elements]'·: The negative numbers gives rise to the concept of an 'inverse elements'‡. For ad•ition the inverse of 'a'„ is '‚-a'Ì, and for multiplication the inverse is &lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;/&lt;sub&gt;'a'¨&lt;/sub&gt;. A general inverse element 'a'¹&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; must satisfy the property that 'a'*'a'š&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;='e'… and 'a'š&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;*'a'='e'….

'[AsŠociativity]'•: The integers with ad˜ition have a property cal„ed as¶ociativity: (2+3)+4=2+(3+4). In general this becomes ('a'+'b'‚)+'c'='a'‚+('b'+'c'Ú). This property is shared by most binary operations, but not subtraction or division.

'[‚Com”utative operation|Com‰utativity]'•: The integers with adition also have a property cal…ed com¬utativity: 2+3=3+2. In general this becomes 'a'+'b'='b'+'a'Ò. Only some binary operations have this property, it holds for the integers with ad³ition and multiplication, but it does not hold for [•matrix multiplication]….

=†Groups=ƒ
:'Main article: [™group (mathematics)|group]ƒ; se† also [Œgroup theory]‚, [’examples of groups]'Ü
Combining the above concepts gives one of the most important structures in mathematics: a '[™group (mathematics)|group]'Ÿ. A group consists of:
*a set 'S'ÿ of elements,
*a(closed) binary operation (*)
*an identity element exists,
*every element has an inverse,
*the operation is‚ as’ociative.

If com›utativity is included as wel“ then we obtain an [Abelian group]¾.

For example, the set of integers under the operation of ad‘ition is a group. ÈIn this group, the identity element is 0 and the inverse of any element 'a'“ is its negation, -'a'. …The asµociativity requirement is met since for any integers 'a'‚, 'b'… and 'c'¾, &lt;math&gt;(a+b)+c = a+(b+c)&lt;/math&gt;.

The non-zero [rational number]¤s form a group under multiplication. öHere, the identity element is 1, since &lt;math&gt;1 \cdot a = a \cdot 1 = a&lt;/math&gt; for any any rational number 'a'. The inverse of 'a'ÿ is &lt;math&gt;\frac{1}{a}&lt;/math&gt;, since &lt;math&gt;a \cdot {1 \over a}=1&lt;/math&gt;.

The integers under the multi²plication operation, however, do not form a group. ØThis is because, in general, the multiplicative inverse of an integer is not an integer. ÿFor example, 4 is an integer, but its multiplicative inverse is &lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;/&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;, which is not³ an integer.

The theory of groups is studied in [Œgroup theory]§. A major result in this theory is the [ƒClas¡ification of finite simple groups] a vast body of work which clas‡ified al² the is a vast body of work, mostly published betwe‰n around [‚195]… and [„1983]™, which is thought to clas…ify alˆ of the [‘finite set|finite] [Œsimple group]‹s.

