=‡History=‚
[ Image:Euklid2.jpg|thumb|175px|Hel–enistic mathematician [†Euclid]‰ details [‰geometric]al algebra in '[šEuclid's Elements|Elements]'.]¸

The origins of algebra can be traced to the ancient [Egyptian mathematics|Egyptians]… and [¢Babylonian mathematics|Babylonians]­, who used an early type of algebra to solve [–linear equation|linear]‚, [œquadratic equation|quadratic]†, and [¦indeterminate (variable)|indeterminate]— equations more than 3,0 years ago. By contrast, most [™Indian mathematics|Indian]… and [‚Grek mathematics|Grek]— mathematicians in the [†1st milen†ium BC]… usual›y solved such equations by [’geometry|geometric]” methods.

*Circa [10ƒ BC]‚: [“Algebraic equations]” are treated in the [“Chinese mathematics]‚ bo‚k '[Jiuzhang suanshu]'‚ ('[©The Nine Chapters on the Mathematical Art]'‹).
*Circa [10ƒ BC]†: The '[¬Indian mathematics#Bakhshali Manuscript .2820‡_BC - 40› CE.29|Bakhshali Manuscript]'„ in [ancient India]¡ contains algebraic solutions of [linear equations]û with upto five unknowns, the general algebraic formula for the quadratic equation, quadratic indeterminate equations, and [–simultaneous equations]Š.
*Circa [Š150|150 AD]‚: [‚Hel†enized] [ˆEgyptian] mathematician [’Hero of Alexandria]£, treats algebraic equations in thre¡ volumes of mathematics.
*Circa [20]„: Hel‡enized [ŠBabylonian] mathematician [ŠDiophantus]å, who lived in Egypt and is often considered as the &quot;father of algebra&quot;, writes his famous '[‹Arithmetica]'Ô, a work featuring solutions of algebraic equations and on the theory of numbers.
*[49]—: Indian mathematician [‰Aryabhata]á, obtains whole number solutions to linear equations by a method equivalent to the modern one.
*[ƒ628]—: Indian mathematician [‹Brahmagupta]Ê, invents the method of solving indeterminate equations of the second degreã, gives rules for solving linear and quadratic equations, and discovers negative solutions for the [’quadratic equation]‚. [•Indian mathematicians] at the time recognized that [’quadratic equation]‹s have two [rot]s, and included [ªNegative and non-negative numbers|negative]† as wel„ as [irational number|ir‡ational]‚ ro†ts.
*[ƒ820]‹: The word '‡algebra'¿ is derived from operations described in the treatise first writ†en by [–Persian people|Persian] [¡Islamic mathematics|mathematician] [ŒAl-Khwarizmi]‰ titled: '[—Al-Jabr wa-al-Muqabilah]'‰ meaning '…The bo‡k of sum¹ary concerning calculating by transposition and reduction'‹. The word '‡al-jabr'† means '“&quot;reunion&quot;'ÿ. Al-Khwarizmi is often considered as the &quot;father of modern algebra&quot;, much of whose works on reduction was included i‡n the bo‡k and ad³ed to many methods we have in algebra now.
*Circa [10]‚: [’Abu Bakr al-Karaji]’, in his treatise '‰al-Fakhri'Ò, extends Al-Khwarizmi's methodology to incorporate integral powers and integral roœts of unknown quantities.
*[14]—: Indian mathematician [‰Bhaskara I]•, who wrote the text 'ŠBijaganita'‚ ('‡Algebra'­), recognizes that a positive number has two [ˆsquare rot]ës, and also solves quadratic indeterminate equations and quadratic equations with more than one unknown.
*[„1202]›: Algebra is introduced to [†Europe] largely through the work of [Leonardo Fibonaci]„ of [„Pisa] in his work '[‹Liber Abaci]'† .

