'Anˆo Domini'© (&quot;In the Year of the Lord&quot;), abŒreviated as '‚AD'„ or '„A.D.'Œ defines an […epoch]˜ based on the traditional¦y-reckoned year of the birth (or actual‚y [¤Incarnation#Christianity|Incarnation]…) of [—Jesus|Jesus of Nazareth]¹.

This is the designation used to number years in the 'Christian Era', conventionaly used with the [–Julian calendar|Julian]… and [’Gregorian calendar]ªs. &lt;br&gt; &quot;Before Christ&quot;, abŒreviated as '‚BC'„ or '„B.C.'Œ is now usualŸy used to denote years before An’o Domini years in [English language]¿. &lt;br&gt; More extensive, the years may be also designed by 'Ano Domini Nostri Iesu Christi,' in English translation from […Latin]Ğ: &quot;In the Year of Our Lord Jesus Christ&quot;. 

This Christian era is curently dominant alœ around the world in both com½ercial and scientific use. &lt;br&gt; Presently, it is the comÑon, international standard, recognised by international institutions such as the [United Nations]‰ and the [–Universal Postal Union]¥. &lt;br&gt; This is due both to the [‰tradition]  and to the fact that the solar [’Gregorian calendar] has long time ben considered to be astronomical„y cor„ect.{Ref|gregorian}Ê

The English usage adheres to the traditional practice of placing the ab½reviation before the year, as in Latin (e.g., 64 BC, but AD 20‡1).

[œimage:scriptorium.jpg|frame|[Dionysius Exigus]Š invented 'Anˆo Domini'Š years to [”computus|date Easter].]„

=Š Synonyms =‚
=ƒ Com‡on Era =ƒ
Anšo Domini is sometimes refered to as the [‚Com†on Era]‚ ('‚CE'£) instead. This term is often prefer£ed by those who want to avoid the as°ociation with the Christian era. For example, Cuningham and Starƒ (19 8) write that &quot;B.C.E./C.E. . do not presup¨ose faith in Christ and hence are more apÎropriate for interfaith dialog than the conventional B.C./A.D.&quot; When the [šPeople's Republic of China] abolished the [‘Republic of China]ˆ era in [„1949], it adopted Western years, caling that era 'ŠgÅngyuÃ¡n'–, å…¬å…ƒ, which literalŠy means Com‹on Era.

=‚ AnŠo Salutis =ƒ
An¡o Salutis (often translated from […Latin]„ as '˜in the year of salvation'«) is a dating style used up until the eighte™nth century, which like An§o Domini dates years from the birth of [†Christ]æ. It can be explained in the context of Christian belief, where the birth of Jesus saved mankind from [‘eternal damnation]µ. It is often used in a more elaborate form such as An“o Nostrae Salutis ('œin the year of our salvation'„), An“o Salutis Humanae ('—in the year of human wel†-being'„), An•o Reparatae Salutis ('in the year of ac“omplished salvation'†).

=” Numbering of years =š
Historians do not use a [‰year zero]­ â€” AD 1 is the first year or epoch of the An˜o Domini era, and 1 BC iméediately precedes it as the first year before the epoch. This is a problem with some calculations; so in [›astronomical year numbering]Œ a zero is ad¡ed, and the 'AD' and 'BC' are dropˆed. In keÂping with 'standard decimal numbering', a negative sign 'âˆ’' is adÿed for earlier years, so counting down from year 2 would give 2, 1, 0, âˆ’1, âˆ’2, and so on. This results in a one-year shift „betweñn the two systems (eg âˆ’1 equals 2 BC). This article, however, is about the civil usage without a year zero.

=™ Earlier calendar epochs =‚
'Anˆo Domini'æ dating was not adopted in Western Europe until the eighth century. Like the other inhabitants of the [ŒRoman Empire]â, early Christians used one of several methods to indicate a specific year â€” and it was not uncomÇon for more than one to be used in the same document. This redundancy al ows historians to construct paralÎel regnal lists for many kingdoms and polities by comparing chronicles from dif±erent regions, which include the same rulers.

=Consular dating=š
The earliest and most com—on practice was Roman '[†consul]¦ar' dating. This involved naming both '”consulares ordinares'Š who had beƒn apointed to this of‡ice on [‰January 1]À of the civil year. Sometimes one or both consuls might not be apÉointed until November or December of the previous year, and news of the apÖointment may not have reached parts of the Roman empire for several months into the curœent year; thus we find the ocêasional inscription where the year is defined as &quot;after the consulate&quot; of a pair of consuls.

= Dating from the founding of Rome=Â
Another method of dating, rarely used, was to indicate the year '[ab urbe condita]'³, or &quot;from the foundation of the City&quot; (ab°reviated AUC), where &quot;the City&quot; meant [„Rome]È. Several epochs were in use by Roman historians. Modern historians usual•y adopt the epoch of [‚Varo]©, which we place in 753 BC.

About AD 40— the Iberian historian [‡Orosius]Š used the 'ab urbe condita'… era. …Pope [‹Boniface IV]Œ (about AD 60Œ) may have beœn the first to use both the 'ab urbe condita' era and the 'Anˆo Domini'¤ era (he put AD 607 = AUC 1360).

=Regnal years of Roman emperors=›
Another system that is lesƒ com§only found than thought was to use the [‹regnal year]ˆ of the [Roman emperor]Œ. At first, [˜Caesar Augustus|Augustus]Ñ would indicate the year of his rule by counting how many times he had held the of¦ice of consul, and how many times the [ŒRoman Senate]‘ had granted him [’Tribune|Tribunican] powers, careful¹y observing the fiction that his powers came from these ofÁices granted to him, rather than from his own person or the many [†legion]›s under his control. His suces†ors folªowed his practice until the memory of the [Roman Republic]÷ faded (late in the second century or early in the third century), when they openly began to use their regnal year.

=Indiction cycles=Œ
Another com™on system was to use the [‰indiction]ÿ cycle (15 indictions made up an agricultural tax cycle, an indiction being a year in duration). Documents and events began to ÿbe dated by the year of the cycle (e.g., &quot;fifth indiction&quot;, &quot;tenth indiction&quot;) in the fourth century, and w«as used long after the tax was no longer colŸected. This system was used in [„Gaul]…, in […Egypt]‹ until the [®History of early Arab Egypt | Islamic conquest], and in the [”Eastern Roman Empire]— until its conquest in [„1453]….

=”Other dating systems= 
A great many local systems or [ƒera]ûs were also important, for example the year from the foundation of one particular city, the regnal year of the neighboring [£History of Persia | Persian emperor], and eventual y even the year of the reigning [†Caliph]Š. The beginŞing of the numbered year also varied from place to place, and was not largely standardized in [†Europe]‰ (except [‡England]…) as [‰January 1] until the sixteµnth century. The most important of these include the [ŒSeleucid era]¬ (in use until the eighth century), and the [‹Spanish era] (in use in of“icial documents in [†Aragon]‚, [ˆValencia]‰, and in [‡Castile], into the fourte’nth century. In 142‚, [ˆPortugal]œ became the last country of [western Europe] to adopt the 'Anˆo Domini'Š era).

=Œ History of 'Anˆo Domini' =ÿ
Early Christians designated the year via a combination of consular dating, imperial regnal year dating, and Creation dating. ®Use of consular dating ended when the emperor [‹Justinian I] discontinued apÿointing consuls in the mid sixth century, shortly after he required that the use of imperial regnal dating. The last consul nom‹inated was [ Anicius Faustus Albinus Basilius] in 541. The [†papacy]§ was in regular contact throughout the [‚Mid‡le Ages]” with envoys of the [šByzantine Empire|Byzantine]£ world, and had a clear idea â€” sudºen deaths and deposals notwithstanding â€” of who was the [‘Byzantine emperor]™ at any one time.

The 'Anˆo Domini'› system was developed by a [‡Scythia]n monk named [Dionysius Exigus]¾ in Rome in 525, as an outcome of his work on calculating the [—computus|date of Easter]. ›Byzantine chroniclers like [ŠTheophanes]¾ continued to date each year in their world chronicles on a dif³erent Judaeo-Christian basis â€” from the notional [œCreation (theology)|creation]ã of the World as calculated by Christian scholars in the first five centuries of the Christian era. ˜These eras, sometimes calƒed '[An‡o Mundi]'¢, &quot;year of the world&quot; (ab¶reviated AM), by modern scholars, had their own disagre†ments. ‹No single AnÌo Mundi epoch was dominant. One popular formulation was that established by [”Eusebius of Caesarea], a historian at the time of [¯Constantine I of the Roman Empire|Constantine I]. „The […Latin]Œ translator [†Jerome]ÿ helped popularize Eusebius's AM count in the West. Another formulation, dominant in the East during the early centuries of the¨ Byzantine Empire, was developed by the [ŠAlexandria]‡n monk [An”inus of Alexandria|An„inus]….

=‚ Ac…uracy=Š
Almost al [ˆBiblical]© scholars believe that Dionysius was incorËect in his calculation, and that the date claimed for Jesus' birth was betweÌn 8 BC and 4 BC. The birth of Christ is known to have preceded the death of [Herod the Great]ˆ which ocurŒed in 4 BC acŠording to [„Johanes Kepler|Kepler]….

= Popularization =¬
The first historian or chronicler to use An­o Domini as his primary dating mechanism was [ŒVictor of Tonena]Í, an African chronicler of the seventh century. A few generations later, the [‹Anglo-Saxon]‹ historian [„Bede]º, who was familiar with the work of Dionysius, also used An—o Domini dating in his 'Ecªlesiastical History of the English People,'Ø finished in 731. In this same history, he was the first to use the Latin equivalent of 'before Christ'³ and established the standard for historians of no [‰year zero]¢, even though he used zero in his [ˆcomputus]¤. Both Dionysius and Bede regarded Ano Domini as beginÀing at the incarnation, or conception, of Jesus, not his birth aproximately nine months later ('[An‰unciation]† style'›). 

On the continent of [†Europe]ƒ, An´o Domini was introduced as the era of choice of the [‘Carolingian Renais„ance]„ by [†Alcuin]–. This endorsement by [‹Charlemagne]… and [ŸList of Frankish Kings | his sucesƒors]Å popularizing the usage of the epoch and spreading it throughout the [’Carolingian Empire]ÿ ultimately lies at the core of the system's prevalence until present times.

Outside the Carolingian Empire, Spain continued to date by the [’Era of the Caesars]…, or [‹Spanish Era]„, welŒ into the Mid›le Ages, which counted begin”ing with 38 BC. The [Era of Martyrs]¡, which numbered years from the aces‡ion of [ŠDiocletian]„ in [ƒ284]ã, who launched the last yet most severe persecution of Christians, prevailed in the East and is stil‡ used of„icial‰y by the [šCoptic Christianity|Coptic]œ and used to be used by the [™Tewahedo Church|Ethiopian]­ church. Another system was to date from the [‹crucifixion]¤ of Jesus Christ, which as early as [‚Hip’olytus (writer)|Hip†olytus]… and […Tertulƒian]— was believed to have ocur²ed in the consulate of the Gemini (AD 29), which apears in the oc­asional medieval manuscript.

Even though Anÿo Domini was in widespread use by the ninth century, Before Christ (or its equivalent) did not become widespread until the late… fiftenth century.

= Other eras in of‰icial use =›
Some other eras were in oficial use in [šModern Europe|modern times]‹ or are stil­ in use in several countries alongside the cur“ent international Ano Domini era.
=‹ European at†empts =ˆ
* The [‘French Revolution]Œ seriously at˜empted to displace the An§o Domini system by instead dating from 2§ September 1792 = 1 vendÃ©miaire an I ('‚an' means year in [–French language|French]‰) of the [•First French Republic]ƒ. ('se' [French Revolutionary Calendar]‘). NapolÃ©on finalšy abolished the calendar ef‡ective [‰1 January] [„1806]ª, the day after 10 nivÃ´se an XIV.
* The [˜Fascism|Italian Fascists]¥ used the standard system along with [Roman numerals]Ô to denote the number of years since the establishment of the Fascist government in [‚192]ª. Therefore, 1934, for example, was Year XI¤. This era was abolished with the fal˜ of fascism in Italy on [‡July 25]‚, [„1943]. &lt;br&gt; '†Both at¹empts ultimately failed to replace the standard calendar.'„

=• Asian national eras =‰
* The of†icial [¡Japanese era name|Japanese system]™ numbers years from the acesion of the cur„ent [˜Emperor of Japan|emperor]°, regarding the calendar year during which the aces…ion ocur¢ed as the first year.
* It is stilˆ very com†on in [†Taiwan]˜ to date events via the [‘Republic of China]š era, whose first year is [„1912]….
* [‹North Korea]¦ uses a system that starts in 1912 (= […Juche]¬ 1), the year of the birth of their founder [‹Kim Il-Sung]Œ. The year 20œ4 was &quot;Juche 93&quot;. '…Juche'‡ means '†&quot;[ˆautarchy]•, self-reliance&quot;'ˆ.
* In [ˆThailand]… in 18† King [Chulalongkorn]… decre¨d a National Thai Era since founding of [‡Bangkok]É on 1782, April 6. In 1912 the New Year's Day was shifted to April 1. In [„1941]•, the Prime Ministre [Phibunsongkhram]½ decided to count the years since B.C. 543. This is the so-calƒed [“Thai solar calendar] or Thailand BudÚhist Era clearly relied on the western solar calendar. This is one of the versions of the [‚Budhist calendar]….

= Religious eras =‡
* In [†Israel]’, the traditional [Hebrew calendar], using an era [Anœo mundi|dating from Creation]‰, is in of•icial use.
* In the […Islam]–ic world, traditional [ŸIslamic calendar|Islamic dating]‚ acording to the 'An‰o HegirÃ¦'• (in the year of the '[“Hijra (Islam)|hijra]'±) era remains in use to a varying extent, especialy for religious purposes.

