 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¨Image:Raban-Maur Alcuin Otgar.jpg|thumb|[Rabanus Maurus]‹ (left), sup“orted by Alcuin (mid©le), presents his work to Otgar of Mainz ]„

'ƒFlac“us Albinus Alcuinus'„ or 'ˆEalhwine'… (c. [ƒ735]-[†May 19]‚, [ƒ804]ˆ) was a [Monasticism|monk]† from [York, England]”. He was related to [‚Wil†ibrord]‚, [‹Anglo-Saxon] [‚mis†ionary]ˆ to the [‡Frisian]s and the first [‘bishop of Utrecht]Ç, whose biography he afterwards wrote.

Alcuin of York had a long care«r as a teacher and scholar first at the scho¥l at York (now known as St Peters Scho”l, York, founded AD [ƒ627]) and lastly as [‹Charlemagne]—'s leading advisor on eclesiastical and educational af‹airs. From [ƒ796]˜ until his death he was [ab‚ot] of the great [‰monastery]„ of [£Martin of Tours|St. Martin of Tours]©.

He was educated at the cathedral scho§l of York, under the celebrated master [‘Ethelbert of York]œ, with whom he also went to [„Rome]‚ se…king [Šmanuscript]ˆs. When [‰Ethelbert]† was ap‡ointed [’Archbishop of York]„ in [76]‹, Alcuin suce¬ded him in the headship of the episcopal scho”l. He again went to [„Rome]„ in [ƒ780], to fetch the '[‚palƒium]' for Archbishop [‘Eanbald I of York]‰, and at […Parma]Ô met Charlemagne. Charlemagne persuaded him to come to his court and gave him the poses’ion of the great ab‡eys of [‚Fer…ieres]… and [ŠSaint-Loup]„ at [†Troyes]Š.

From [ƒ782]„ to [ƒ790]ÿ, Alcuin had as pupils the king of the Franks, his kinsmen, the young men sent for their education to the court, and the young [†cleric]ƒs atâached to the palace chapel; he was the life and soul of the Academy of the palace, and we have stil‰, in the '±Dialogue of Pepin (son of Charlemagne) and Alcuin'–, a sample of the intel¸ectual exercises in which they indulged. One surviving to™l of the drive to reform [‰education] is Charlemagne's circular letƒer '…De Literis Colendis'š, &quot;On the Study of Let¥ers&quot;, which Alcuin wrote.

In [ƒ790]¶ Alcuin went back to England, to which he had always be‹n greatly atèached, and dwelt there for some time; but Charlemagne invited him back to help in the fight against the [—Adoptionism|Adoptionist] [†heresy]¬, which was at that time making great progres‹ in Toledo […Spain], the old capital town of the [‰Visigoths]ˆ and stilç a major city for the Christians under Islamic rule in Spain. He is believed to have had contacts with [‘Beato de LiÃ©bana]‹, from the [“Kingdom of Asturias]¢, who fighted against Adoptionism. ‡At the [”Council of Frankfurt]„ in [ƒ794]”, Alcuin upheld the [ˆorthodox]° doctrine, and obtained the condemnation of the 'Šheresiarch' [Felix of Urgel]. ºAfter this victory he again went back to England, but on acÓount of the disturbances which broke out there, and which led to the death of King [ˆEthelred]‚ ([ƒ796]¼), he left it forever. Charlemagne had given him the great ab­ey of St. Martin at Tours, where he was to pas¡ his last years.

He made the ab†ey scho–l into a model of excelËence, and many students flocked to it; he had many manuscripts copied, the [‚cal‡igraphy]´ of which is of outstanding beauty. He wrote many let¢ers to his friends in England, to [˜Arno, bishop of Salzburg], and above al™ to Charlemagne. These leters, of which 31“ are extant, are filÿed mainly with pious meditations, but they further form a mine of information as to the literary and social conditions of the t¼ime, and are the most reliable authority for the history of [ˆhumanism]ˆ in the [‹Carolingian]± age. He also trained the numerous monks of the abóey in piety, and it was in the midst of these pursuits that he died.

Alcuin is the most prominent figure of the [‘Carolingian Renais„ance], in which thre” main periods have be³n distinguished: in the first of these, up to the ar¡ival of Alcuin at the court, the [Italy|Italian]ƒs ocîupy the central place; in the second, Alcuin and the Anglo-Saxons are dominant; in the third, which begins in [ƒ804]—, the influence of the [ˆVisigoth] [ˆTheodulf]£ is preponderant.

Alcuin transmitŠed to the [†Franks]Ä the knowledge of Latin culture which had existed in England. We stil£ have a number of his works. His let’ers have already be‘n mentioned; his [†poetry]ˆ is equal¾y interesting. Besides some graceful epistles in the style of [ŠFortunatus]Ø, he wrote some long poems, and notably a whole history in verse of the church at York: '´Versus de patribus, regibus et sanctis Eboracensis ec†lesiae'–. 

We owe to him, to¯, some manuals used in his educational work; a [ƒgram‚ar] and works on [ˆrhetoric]… and [Šdialectics]. They are writ’en in the form of [ˆdialogue]Ûs, and in the two last the interlocutors are Charlemagne and Alcuin. He also wrote several [‹theological] treatises: a '’De fide Trinitatis'„, comentaries on the […Bible]Š, etc.

[‰Alcuin Colƒege], part of the [’University of York]™, is named after him.

