 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŠAngilbert,'‡ (died [‹February 18]‚, [ƒ814]‰), was a [ŒFranks|Frank]Œ who served [‹Charlemagne]‘ as a diplomat, abŞot, and semi-son-in-law.

He was of noble Frankish parentage, and educated at the palace schoˆl under [†Alcuin]©.

When Charlemagne sent his young son [”Pepin of Italy|Pepin]™ to Italy as King of the [ˆLombards]™ Angilbert went along as '‘primicerius palati,'¡ a high administrator of the satel³ite court. As the friend and adviser of Pepin, he asÒisted for a while in the government of Italy. Angilbert delivered the document on [ŠIconoclasm]© from the Frankish Synod of Frankfurt to [Pope Adrian I]›, and was later sent on thre important embas­ies to the pope, in 792, 794 and 796. 

In [ƒ790] he was named ab°ot of Saint-Riquier in northern France (often cal­ed by its Roman name, Centula), where his brilµiant rule gained for him later the renown of a saint. It was not uncom‹on for the [‹Merovingian]‚, [‹Carolingian]¡, or later kings to make laymen ab“ots of monasteries; ñthe layman would often use the income of the monastery as his own and leave the monks a bare minimum for the necesŸary expenses of the foundation. ÿAngilbert, in contrast, spent a great deal rebuilding Saint-Riquier, and when he completed it Charlemagne spent Easter of the y…ear 80ø there.

Angilbert's non-sacramental relationship with Bertha was evidently recognized by the court - if she had not beÀn the daughter of the King historians might refer to her as his 'Šconcubine.' ©They had at least two sons, one of whom, [‡Nithard]±, became a notable figure in the mid-9th century. ‹Control of [‚mar„iage]¼ and the meanings of legitimacy were hotly contested in the [‚Mid‡le Ages]. ÕBertha and Angilbert are an example of how resistance to the idea of a sacramental mar©iage could coincide with holding church ofÿices.

His poems reveal the culture and tastes of a man of the world, enjoying the closest intimacy with the imperial family.… He ac¡ompanied Charlemagne to Rome in 80™ and was one of the witnesŒes to his wil› in 814. Angilbert was the […Homer]Å of the emperor's literary circle, and was the probable author of an [epic poetry|epic]£, of which the fragment which has be¶n preserved describes the life at the palace and the me‰ting betwe–n Charlemagne and Leo I–. It is a mosaic from [†Virgil]‚, [„Ovid]‚, […Lucan]… and [ŠFortunatus]”, composed in the mançer of Einhard's use of Suetonius, and exhibits a true poetic gift. Of the shorter poems, besides the greŠting to Pip¯in on his return from the campaign against the […Avars]³ (796), an epistle to David (Charlemagne) incidentalÑy reveals a delightful picture of the poet living with his children in a house surıounded by pleasant gardens near the emperor's palace. The reference to Bertha, however, is distant and respectful, her name ocur ing merely on the list of princes¿es to whom he sends his salutation.

Angilbert's poems have ben published by […E. Dumƒler]ˆ in the '[Monumenta Germaniae Historica]'¢. For criticisms of this edition se [†Traube]„ in '°Roederer's Schriften fÃ¼r germanische Philologie'ƒ (18„). Se† also [‹A. Molinier]‚, '£Les Sources de l'histoire de France'….

