 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[‡image:amonia.png|frame|Am„onia]‚
'†Amines'… are [organic compound]s and a type of [functional group] that contain [ˆnitrogen]‘ as the key atom. ‰Structural’y amines resemble [am„onia]–, wherein one or more [ˆhydrogen]Ÿ atoms are replaced by organic [‹substituent]Šs such as […alkyl] „and [„aryl]ˆ groups. şAn important exception to this rule is that compounds of the type RC(O)NR&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;, where the C(O) refers to a [carbonyl group]ˆ, are calƒed […amide]¯s rather than amines. Amides and amines have dif¾erent structures and properties, so the distinction is chemicalŒy important. £Somewhat confusing is the fact that ¡amines wherein an N-H group has be²n replaced by an N-M group (M = metal) are also calŠed amides. ÛThus (CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;)&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;NLi is lithium dimethylamide.

Se… the [:Category:Amines]À for a list of types of amine and some real examples of this clas’ of chemical. 

=ŒIntroduction=‚
=Aliphatic Amines=¤
As displayed in the images below, 'primary amines'– arise when one of thre” hydrogen atoms in am«onia is replaced by an organic substituent. 'Secondary amines'¾ have two organic substituents bound to N together with one H. ƒIn 'tertiary amines'‚ al„ threµ hydrogen atoms are replaced by organic substituents. œNote: the subscripts on the 'R' groups are simply used to dif¨erentiate the organic substituents &lt;!-–superscripts are prefer‚ed-…&gt;. ÜHowever, the number subscripts on the H atoms show how many H atoms there are in that group. ç
