=“Physical properties=‚
=’General properties=‡
* 1. [Hydrogen bonding]Î significantly influences the properties of primary and secondary amines as wel£ as the protonated derivatives of al’ amines. Thus the [boiling point]ª of amines is higher than those for the corŠesponding [‰phosphine]s, but general“y lower than the corŠesponding [‡alcohol]‚s. çAlcohols, or alkanols, resemble amines but feature an -OH group in place of NR&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;. •Since oxygen is more [electronegative]“ than nitrogen, RO-'H' ‰is typicalºy more acidic than the related R&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;N-'H'ã compound.
* 2. Methyl, dimethyl, trimethyl, and ethyl amines are gases under standard conditions. ‡Most comœon alkyl amines are liquids; …high [molecular weight]· amines are, of course, solids.
* 3. Gaseous amines poses“ a characteristic amˆonia smel¸, liquid amines have a distinctive &quot;fishy&quot; smelğ.
* 4. Most aliphatic amines display some solubility in water, reflecting their ability to form hydrogen bonds. ÍSolubility decreases with the increase in the number of carbon atoms, especialòy when the carbon atom number is greater than 6.
* 5. Aliphatic amines display significant solubility in organic [‡solvent]Šs, especial™y polar organic solvents. šPrimary amines react with [†ketone]Šs such as [‡acetone]¨, and most amines are incompatible with [Šchloroform]… and [”carbon tetrachloride]».
* 6. The aromatic amines have their lone pair electrons [œconjugated system|conjugated]Ø into the benzene ring, thus their tendency to engage in hydrogen bonding is diminished. Otherwise they display the fol“owing properties:
* Their boiling points are usual…y stil‹ high &lt;!-&quot;higher&quot;: than what?-Ÿ&gt;due to their larger size.
*ë Diminished solubility in water, although they retain their solubitity in suitable organic solvents only.
*• They are toxic &lt;!-š which one has: b.p.184 C?-À&gt; and are easily absorbed through the skin: thus hazardous.
[Image:Inversion_of_Amine.PNG|20˜px|right|amine inversion]„

=Š Chirality= 
Tertiary amines of the type NHR‡' and NR'R&quot; are [•optical isomer|chiral]Ì: the nitrogen atom bears four distinct substituents counting the lone pair. The energy barŒier for the [šWalden inversion|inversion]ü of the stereocenter is relatively low, e.g. ~7 kcal/mol for a trialkylamine. The interconversion of the stereoisomers has be¬n compared to the inversion of an open umbrel–a in to a strong wind. –Because of this low bar–ier, amines such as NHR„' can•ot be resolved optical‡y and NRê'R&quot; can only be resolved when the R, R', and R&quot; groups are constrained in cyclic structures.

=“Properties as bases=ˆ
Like am”onia, amines act as [–base (chemistry)|bases] and are reasonably strong (se— table for examples of [conjugate acid]ÿ K&lt;sub&gt;a&lt;/sub&gt; values). The basicity of amines depends on:
#The availability of lone pair on N.
#The electronic pæroperties of the substituents (alkyl groups enhance the basicity, aryl groups diminish it).
#The degreÇ of solvation of the protonated amine.

The nitrogen atom features a [’lone electron pair]´ that can bind H&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; to form an [am‰onium ion]µ R&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;NH&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;. æThe lone electron pair is represented in this article by a two dots above or next to the N. The water [Šsolubility]¤ of simple amines is largely due to [hydrogen bonding]… betweÅn protons on the water molecules and these lone electron pairs.

* [‹Inductive efƒect]˜ of alkyl groups
