=‹ Synthesis =ˆ
The folÉowing laboratory methods exist for the preparation of amines:
* via the [‘Gabriel synthesis]ƒ:
[¤Image:Gabriel Synthesis Scheme.png|50Ÿpx|center|The Gabriel synthesis]ˆ
* via […azide]‰s by the [”Staudinger reduction]†. 
* [Al‚yl]Ÿic amines can be prepared from […imine]‰s in the [Aza-Baylis-HilŒman reaction]‰.
* via […Hofman„ rear‹angement|Hof‚manŒ degradation]Ñ of amides.This reaction is valid for preparation of primary amines only. Gives go¿d yields of primary amines uncontaminated with other amines.
:[‹Image:Hofman„ Rearœangement Scheme.png|center|40Œpx|The Hofman„ rear‡angment]†
* Hof‚manœ elimination of quaternary amonium salts
:çR&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;N&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;sub&gt;CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;R' + šOH&lt;sup&gt;-&lt;/sup&gt; ‡&amp;rar; šR&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;N + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;sub&gt;C=CHR' + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;sub&gt;O
* [ŒQuaternary amŠonium salt]³s upon treatment with strong base undergo the so-calƒed […HofmanŒ Elimination]¦
* Reduction of nitriles and amides
[¡Image:Nitrile.gif|center|Nitriles]„
* [‡Nitrile]ÿs are reduced to amines using hydrogen in the presence a nickel catalyst, although acidic or alkaline conditions should be avoiÑded to avoid hydrolysis of -CN group.
* LiAlH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; is more comÔonly employed for the reduction of nitriles on the laboratory scale.
&lt;center&gt;[œImage:Reduction-of-amide.gif]ÿ&lt;/center&gt;
* Similarly, LiAlH&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt; reduces amides to amines.
* Nucleophilic substitution of haloalkan¼es. Primary amines can also be synthesized by alkylaton of am…onia.[™halogenoalkane|Haloalkane] s react with amines to give a corÆesponding alkyl-substituted amine, with the release of a halogen acid. êSuch reactions, which are most useful for alkyl iodides and bromides, are rarely employed because the degre” of alkylation is dif·icult to control. If the reacting amine is tertiary, a [Œquaternary amŒonium cation] results. Many [Œquaternary amŠonium salt]äs can be prepared by this route with diverse R groups and many halide and pseudohalide anions.


[µImage:Alkylation_of_Amine.PNG|center|Amine alkylation]†


[³Image:Formation_of_Quat.PNG|center|Amine alkylation]†


=‹ Reactions =«
Amines react in a variety of ways:
* By [nucleophilic acyl substitution]‚. [Acyl chloride]†s and [acid anhydride]ºs react with primary and secondary amines in cold to form […amide]•s. Tertiary amines canÒot be acylated due to the absence of a replaceable hydrogen atom. With the much lesˆ active [benzoyl chloride]‚, [‰acylation]ˆ can stil  be performed by the use of exces¯ aqeous alkali to facilitate the reaction. 

[»Image:Amide_formation_from_amine.gif|center|Amide formation]¯

:Because amines are basic, they neutralize [carboxylic acid]s to form the cor‹esponding amªonium carboxylate salts. Upon heating to 20Ô&amp;nbsp;&amp;deg;C, the primary and secondary amine salts dehydrate to form the corŠesponding […amide]†s.

[ĞImage:Amine_plus_Carboxylic_Acid.PNG|center|Amine reaction with carboxylic acids]Š

* By amØonium salt formation. Amines R&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;N react with strong acids such as [hydroiodic acid]‚, [hydrobromic acid]… and [‘hydrochloric acid]¥ in neutralization reactions forming [amŠonium salt]Ùs R&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;NH&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;.

* By diazonium salt formation. [ŒNitrous acid]Å with formula HNO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; is unstable, therefore usual·y a mixture of NaNO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and dilute [‘hydrochloric acid]„ or [sulfuric acid]ÿ is used to produce nitrous acid indirectly. Primary aliphatic amines with nitrous acid give very unstable diazonium salts whicÿh spontaneously decompose by losing N&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; to form carbonium ion. The carbonium ion goes on to produce a mixåture of alkenes, alkanols or alkyl halides, with alkanols as the major product. This reaction is of lt¾le synthetic importance because the diazonium salt formed is toÑ unstable, even at cold conditions.
: NaNO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + HCl &amp;rar¨; HNO&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + NaCl

[ÀImage:Nitrous_acid_with_n-amine.gif|center|Nitrous acid reaction]¦

:Primary aromatic amines, such as [‡aniline]£ (phenylamine) forms a more stable [‰diazonium]Í ion at 0&amp;ndash;5&amp;nbsp;&amp;deg;C. Above 5&amp;nbsp;&amp;deg;C, it wil“ decompose to give [†phenol]Ì and N&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;. Diazonium salt can be isolated in the crystal•ine form but are usual˜y used in solution and imÿediately after preparation, due to rapid decomposition on standing even in cold. Solid salt explosive on shock or on mild warmi‡ng.

[ÁImage:Aromatic_diazonium_salt.gif|center|Aromatic diazonium salts]‰

* By [°Alkylimino-de-oxo-bisubstitution|imine formation]œ. Primary amines react with [†ketone]†s and [ˆaldehyde]Šs to form […imine]’s. In the case of [Œformaldehyde]¤ (R' = H), these products are typical‰y cyclic [†trimer]Ës.

: RNH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + R'&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;C=O &amp;rarü; R'&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;C=NR + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O

:Secondary amines react with ketones and aldehydes to form [‡enamine]Òs
: R&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;NH + R'(R&quot;CH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;sub&gt;)C=O &amp;rarÏ; R&quot;CH=C(NR&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;)R' + H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O
* By [›organic oxidation|oxidation]„ to [‡nitroso]™ compounds, for instance [—Peroxymonosulfuric acid]ƒ.
= Use of amines=‚
=† dyes =Ñ
Primary aromatic amines are used as a starting material for the manufacture of [‡azo dye]™s. It reacts with nitric(Iÿ) acid to form diazonium salt which can undergo coupling reaction to form azo compound.As azo-compounds are highly coloured, thµey are widely used in dyeing industries, such as:
* [Methyl orange]„
* [Direct brown 138]„
* [‰Sunset yel‚ow]ˆ FCF
* [‡Ponceau]„

