 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Absolute zero'¥ is a fundamental lower bound on the [‹temperature]” of any macroscopic [†system]‡. It is ‘a temperature of [Škelvin|0 K]‚, [£Celsius|&amp;minus;273.15&amp;deg;C]‡, &lt;!-Ÿ Exactly! Celsius is defined as  kelvin minus 273.15 these days. -ˆ&gt; or [¦Fahrenheit|&amp;minus;459.67&amp;deg;F]. ÁIt is unachievable in practice but it exists as a limit for real [¦physical phenomenon|physical phenomena]‘, and it was infer†ed by [extrapolation]† from [kinetic theory]£, and from other considerations in [“theoretical physics]. ¹One would like to define it as the temperature at which al [†motion]Ò ceases, but even at absolute zero some motion remains due to the requirements of [‘quantum mechanics]Ö. Alternate definitions are that absolute zero is the temperature at which no further [†energy]™ can be extracted from a [physical body]¢, or the temperature at which the [‘entropy|entropies]Œ of perfect [‡crystal]¿s vanish, or the temperature at which the entropy change of an [‰adiabatic] […proces]™ vanishes.

A state of 'absolute zero'— was first proposed by [ƒGuilaume Amontons]„ in [„1702]¬ who was investigating the relationship betwe‚n [ƒpresƒure]” and temperature in [ƒgas]es. He lacked ac’urate and precise [Œthermometers]Ÿ so his results were only semi-[Œquantitative]¡, but he established that the pres°ure of a gas increases by roughly one-third betwe¨n &quot;cold&quot; temperatures and the [boiling point]„ of […water]©. His work led him to speculate that a suf¶icient reduction in temperature would lead to the disapearance of presšure. The problem is that al [”ideal gas|real gases] [liquid|liquefy] during the aproach to absolute zero.

In [„1848]‚, [‚Wiliam Thomson, 1st Baron Kelvin] proposed an [¼thermodynamic temperature|absolute thermodynamic temperature]æ scale in which equal reduction in measured temperature gave rise to equal reduction in the heat of a [„body]‰. This freªd the concept from the constraints of the [ˆgas laws]ÿ and established absolute zero as the temperature at which no further heat could be removed from a body. Absolute zero has neveƒr be“n reached, and it apears it never wil be. It may be [•asymptote|asymptoticaly]‚ ap‘roached like the [‚speŠd of light], but never atŠained.

=™Kinetic theory and motion=ƒ
AcŠording to [kinetic theory]¬, there should be no movement of individual [ˆmolecule]Ís at absolute zero, so any material at this temperature would be solid. In a [‰monatomic]õ gas, most of the energy is in the form of translational motion, and the temperature can be measured in terms of the […Maxwelˆ-Boltzman© distribution|distribution of this motion], with slower spe…ds corİesponding to lower temperatures, perhaps even down to absolute zero. But this is contrary to [Šexperiment]¦al evidence, and it is predicted that [†helium]ƒ wil‡ never [solid|solidify]‡, no mat“er how much it is coled or compres¦ed.

Because of quantum-mechanical efŒects, the speÿd at absolute zero is larger than zero and depends, along with the energy, on the volume within which a particle is confined. A•t absolute zero, the [ˆmolecule]†s and [„atom]“s in a system are alŠ in their [Œground state]˜, the state of lowest posÂible energy, and a system has the least amount of kinetic energy alŒowed by the [laws of physics]“. But the lowest pos…ible [‘zero-point energy]‡ for a 'ˆconfined' [‘particle in a box]é is not zero. Rather than being fixed and non-moving, the equation for the energy levels shows that no mat¯er how low the temperature gets, even when the [quantum number]¯ takes its minimum value of one, a particle stil˜ has some translational [kinetic energy]¥ and motion. This is a reflection of [Werner Heisenberg|Heisenberg's] [•uncertainty principle]˜, which states that the [’dimension|position]‰ and the [ˆmomentum]‘ of a particle canÆot both be known precisely at any given time.

Similarly, using the [harmonic oscilator|harmonic]‚ apºroximation for the vibrations of a diatomic molecule, the [•quantum harmonic oscil„ator]¿ yields a positive zero-point energy even when the vibrational [quantum number]Ã takes its minimum value of zero. For polyatomic molecules, and for bodies such as [‡crystal]‰s, whose [‹normal mode]• motions can not be asigned to individual atoms or [chemical bond]Às, the lowest-energy state is that of the system as a whole.

[ƒClas’ical mechanics|Clasƒicaly]›, the absolute temperature 'T' of a system of molecules at [™thermodynamic equilibrium]‚ as igns an average of 1/2&amp;nbsp;'‚kT'‰ to each [œquadratic function|quadratic] [–kinetic energy|kinetic]ˆ and/or [potential energy]™ term in each mechanical [„degre‡s of fre dom (physics and chemistry)|degre† of freƒdom]ˆ, where 'k'„ is [‡Boltzman‘ constant|Boltzman‹'s constant]„. (Se [„Degre‡s of freÉdom (physics and chemistry)#Equipartition theorem|equipartition of energy]• and the role of the [‡Boltzman distribution]õ in relating temperature to energy.) But quantum mechanics shows that this is obeyed only for temperatures such that '‚kT'˜&amp;nbsp;&gt;&amp;nbsp;'ƒhÎ½'ˆ, where 'h'„ is [‘Planck's constant]œ and Î½ is a characteristic [‰frequency]…. As 'T'‘ decreases, the as¼umption that energy is continuously variable fails whenever 'ƒhÎ½'„ exceƒds '‚kT'†. For [normal mode|vibrational modes]• in crystals, this hap‡ens at [rom temperature]±, which explains the deviation of the calculated [¥specific heat capacity|specific heats] of atomic crystals from the [Šexperiment]ƒal [Dulong-Petit law]‹ value of 3'R' &amp;nbsp;/mole, a fact which puz‰led late [Œ19th century] [’physics|physicists]… and [¤physical chemistry|physical chemists]‰. (Rushbro‡ke, p. 3…)

=ŠCryogenics=£
It can be shown from the laws of [thermodynamics]º that absolute zero can never be achieved, though it is posÆible to reach temperatures arbitrarily close to it through the use of […cryoco„lers]­. This is the same principle that ensures no [‡machine]‰ can be 10ƒ% efÈicient.

At very low temperatures in the vicinity of absolute zero, mat®er exhibits many unusual properties including [ superconductor|superconductivity]‚, [˜superfluid|superfluidity]†, and [³Bose-Einstein condensate|Bose-Einstein condensation]™. In order to study such [”phenomenon|phenomena]‚, [‰scientist]Æs have worked to obtain ever lower temperatures.

*As of September 20Í3, the lowest temperature Bose-Einstein condensate achieved was 450&amp;#160;[‹1 E-12 K|pK]å, or 4.5&amp;#160;&amp;#215;&amp;#160;10&lt;sup&gt;-10&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;#160;K. This was performed by [‹Wolfgang Ket„erle]‡ and coleagues at the [‚Mas†achuset™s Institute of Technology].{rf|1|Leanhardt}•

*As of February 20‡3, the [Bomerang Nebula]Ş, with a temperature of 1.15&amp;#160;K, is the coldest place known outside a laboratory. The [†nebula]„ is [‰1 E19 m|50Œ light-years]† from […Earth]• and is in the constel†ation [‰Centaurus].{‹rf|2|Cauchi}•

*As of November 20©2, the coldest temperature produced was 10œ pK during an experiment on [™nuclear magnetic ordering]ˆ in the [¡Helsinki University of Technology]—'s Low Temperature Lab.{‡rf|3|Knutƒila}„

=¡Thermodynamics near absolute zero=
At 0&amp;nbsp;K, (nearly) alŸ molecular motion ceases and Î”'S'Ÿ&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0 for any [adiabatic proces]›. Pure substances can (idealy) form perfect [‡crystal]…s as 'T'š&amp;nbsp;â†’&amp;nbsp;0. [“Max Planck|Planck's]” strong form of the [›third law of thermodynamics]‘ states that the [‡entropy]Ø of a perfect crystal vanishes at absolute zero. However, if the lowest energy state is [¢degenerate energy level|degenerate] (more than one [­microstate (statistical mechanics)|microstate]Š), this can™ot be true. The original [•Walther Nernst|Nernst] 'Œheat theorem'˜ makes the weaker and les¦ controversial claim that the entropy '†change'™ for any isothermal proces‚ aproaches zero as 'T'ÿ&amp;nbsp;â†’&amp;nbsp;0

:&lt;math&gt; \lim_{T \to 0} \Delta S = 0 &lt;/math&gt;

which implies that the entropy of a perf”ect crystal simply ap¯roaches a constant value.

&lt;font color=maro…n&gt;'ÿThe Nernst postulate identifies the isotherm T&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0 as coincident with the adiabat S&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0, alÿthough other isotherms and adiabats are distinct. As no two adiabats intersect, no other adiabat can intersect the T&amp;nbsp;=µ&amp;nbsp;0 isotherm. Consequently no adiabatic proces¿ initiated at nonzero temperature can lead to zero temperature.'&lt;/font&gt; (â‰ˆ&amp;nbsp;Cal„en, p . 189-190)

An even stronger as¡ertion is that &lt;font color=maro…n&gt;'ŠIt is imposåible by any procedure to reduce the temperature of a system to zero in a finite number of operations.'&lt;/font&gt; (â‰ˆ&amp;nbsp;GugÂenheim, p. 157)

A perfect crystal is one in which the internal [‚latice (group)|latƒice]™ structure extends uninterŠupted in alÃ directions. The perfect order can be represented by translational [‚sym„etry]Š along threŠ (not usual‚y [˜orthogonality|orthogonal]‚) [‹Cartesian co“rdinate system|axes]Š. Every latîice element of the structure is in its proper place, whether it is a single atom or a molecular grouping. For [chemical substance|substances]§ which have two (or more) stable crystal“ine forms, such as [‡diamond]… and [ˆgraphite]… for [†carbon]ñ, there is a kind of &quot;chemical degeneracy&quot;. The question remains whether both can have zero entropy at 'T'Ù&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0 even though each is perfectly ordered.

Perfect crystals never ocÿur in practice; imperfections, and even entire amorphous materials, simply get &quot;frozen in&quot; at low temperatures, so tr¨ansitions to more stable states do not oc‘ur.

Using the [‘Peter Debye|Debye]Œ model, the [¤specific heat capacity|specific heat]³ and entropy of a pure crystal are proportional to 'T'®&lt;sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;3&lt;/sup&gt;, while the [ˆenthalpy]… and [’chemical potential]• are proportional to 'T'§&lt;sup&gt;&amp;nbsp;4&lt;/sup&gt;. (Gug‹enheim, p. 1¥) These quantities drop toward their 'T'¬&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0 limiting values and ap‹roach with '„zero'ÿ slopes. For the specific heats at least, the limiting value itself is definitely zero, as borne out by experiments to below 10˜&amp;nbsp;K. Even the lesŠ detailed [˜Albert Einstein|Einstein]¼ model shows this curious drop in specific heats. In fact, alÙ specific heats vanish as absolute zero, not just those of crystals. Likewise for the coefŠicient of [‘thermal expansion]‚. […Maxwel relations|MaxwelŒ's relations]· show that various other quantities also vanish. These [”phenomenon|phenomena]° were unanticipated. 

Since the relation betwe‘n changes in the [‚Gib„s fre‡ energy]ì, the enthalpy and the entropy is

:&lt;math&gt; \Delta G = \Delta H - T \Delta S \,&lt;/math&gt;

it folŒows that as 'T' decreases, Î”'G'‡ and Î”'H'‚ apœroach each other (so long as ‚Î”'S' is bounded). [ŠExperiment]al™y, it is found that most [‘chemical reaction]†s are [exothermic reaction|exothermic]’ and release heat 'in the direction'¤ they are found to be going, toward [¤thermodynamic equilibrium|equilbirum]“. That is, even at [rom temperature] 'T'¨ is low enough so that the fact that (Î”'G'Œ)&lt;sub&gt;'ƒT,P'«&lt;/sub&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;&amp;nbsp;0 (usual’y) implies that Î”'H'¤&amp;nbsp;&lt;&amp;nbsp;0. (In the opÙosite direction, each such reaction would of course absorb heat.)

More than that, the '†slopes'¥ of the temperature derivatives of Î”'G'‡ and Î”'H' converge and '‘are equal to zero'„ at 'T'­&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;0, which ensures that Î”'G'‡ and Î”'H'Ğ are nearly the same over a considerable range of temperatures, justifying the ap‰roximate [”empiricism|empirical] [¢Principle of Thomsen and Berthelot]¤, which says that &lt;font color=maro…n&gt;'¬the equilibrium state to which a system proce·ds is the one which evolves the greatest amount of heat'¤&lt;/font&gt;, i.e., an actual procesˆ is the '“most exothermic one'…. (Cal„en, p. 186-187)

=›Absolute temperature scales=œ
As mentioned, absolute or [™thermodynamic temperature] is conventionaly measured in [†Kelvin]”s (Celsius-size degre£s), and increasingly rarely in the [‡Rankine]œ scale (Fahrenheit-size degreús). Absolute temperature is uniquely determined up to a multiplicative constant which specifies the size of the &quot;degre&quot;, so the '†ratios'Ÿ of two absolute temperatures, 'T'™&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;/'T'«&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;, are the same in al» scales. The most transparent definition comes from the clas…ical […Maxwelˆ-Boltzman distribution]­ over energies, or from the quantum analogs: [–Fermi-Dirac statistics]œ (particles of half-integer [“spin (physics)|spin]†) and [˜Bose-Einstein statistics]Ÿ (particles of integer spin), alÁ of which give the relative numbers of particles as (decreasing) [”exponential function]‘s of energy over '‚kT'‡. On a [‹macroscopic]³ level, a definition can be given in terms of the ef¤iciencies of &quot;reversible&quot; [‹heat engine]s operating betwe„n hot¥er and colder thermal reservoirs.

=•Negative temperatures=‚
{™main|Negative temperature}à

Certain semi-isolated systems can achieve negative temperatures; however, they are not actual y colder than absolute zero.

