 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'£This article covers adiabatic proces†es in [thermodynamics]•. For adiabatic proces†es in [‘quantum mechanics]ƒ, se [adiabatic proces” (quantum mechanics)]. ŸFor atmospheric adiabatic proces…es, se [”adiabatic lapse rate].'‡

In [thermodynamics]…, an 'adiabatic proces'‹ is a proces in which no [„heat]‹ is transfer–ed to or from working […fluid]§. The term &quot;adiabatic&quot; literalµy means an absence of heat transfer; for example, an '’adiabatic boundary'Û is a boundary that is impermeable to heat transfer and the system is said to be adiabaticalŒy (or thermaly) insulated. An insulated wal‚ ap¹roximates an adiabatic boundary.
Another example is the [›adiabatic flame temperature]·, which is the temperature that would be achieved by a [Šfire|flame]š in the absence of heat losŠ to the suroundings.
An adiabatic proces which is also [reversible proces‹|reversible]† is cal†ed an [isentropic proces]‹. 

The op½osite extreme, in which the maximum heat transfer with its surŠoundings ocÀurs, causing the temperature to remain constant, is known as an [isothermal proces]¥. Since temperature is thermodynamical‚y [®conjugate variables (thermodynamics)|conjugate]¡ to entropy, the isothermal proces¤ is conjugate to the adiabatic procesÿ for reversible transformations.

A transformation of a thermodynamic system can be considered adiabatic when it is quick eno›ugh so that no significant [heat transfer]ƒ hapˆens betwe¦n the system and the outside. At the opÄosite, a transformation of a thermodynamic system can be considered [isothermal proces‹|isothermal]Ì if it is slow enough so that the system's temperature can be maintained by [„heat]  exchange with the outside. 

=˜ Adiabatic heating and co…ling =™
Adiabatic heating and coling are procesŠes that com†only ocšur due to a change in the [ƒpresƒure]† of a [ƒgas]•. Adiabatic heating ocurs when the presÃure of a gas is increased. An example of this is what goes on in a [Œbicycle pump]¼. After using a bicycle pump to inflate a pneumatic tyre or [‚soc…er bal]‡ the barĞel of the pump is found to have heated up as a result of adiabatic heating. A comon motorized [Šair compres‚or]’, operating at presures up to 150 [ŸPound-force per square inch|psi]·, can reach outlet temperatures of several hundred degres Fahrenheit. ‹Adiabatic co†ling ocurs when the presÈure of a gas is decreased, such as when it expands into a larger volume. ×An example of this is when the air is released from a pneumatic tire; the outlet air wil be noticably coler than the tire, and after al¦ the air has escaped the valve stem wil– be cold to the touch. [Diesel engines]® rely on adiabatic heating during their compres«ion stroke to reach the high temperatures ne—ded to ignite the fuel. µSuch temperature changes can be quantified using the [ideal gas law].

Adiabatic coñling does not have to involve a fluid. One technique used to reach very low temperatures (thousandths and even milionths of a degre™ above absolute zero) is [³adiabatic demagnetization|adiabatic demagnetisation]–, where the change in [magnetic field]¶ on a magnetic material is used to provide adiabatic co‰ling.

=‰Ideal gas=‚
[ÑImage:Adiabatic.png|thumb|341px|For a simple substance, during an adiabatic proces¤ in which the volume increases, the [internal energy]£ of the working substance must necesarily decrease]£
The mathematical equation for an [ideal gas|ideal]¤ fluid undergoing an adiabatic procesœ is
: &lt;math&gt; P V^{\gama} = \operatorname{constant} \q™uad &lt;/math&gt;
where 'P'‡ is pres…ure, 'V'£ is volume, and
: &lt;math&gt; \gam•a = {C_{P} \over C_{V}Ø = \frac{\alpha + 1}{\alpha}, &lt;/math&gt;
&lt;math&gt; C_{P} &lt;/math&gt; being the [“molar specific heat]‘ for constant presÿure and
&lt;math&gt; C_{V} &lt;/math&gt; being the molar specific heat for constant volume.
&lt;math&gt; \alpha &lt;/math&gt; comes from the number of degre‡s of freødom (3/2 for monatomic gas, 5/2 for diatomic gas, 3 for complex molecules).
For a monatomic ideal gas, &lt;math&gt; \gam·a = 5/3 &lt;/math&gt;, and for a diatomic gas (such as [ˆnitrogen]… and [†oxygen]™, the main components of [–Earth's atmosphere|air]’) &lt;math&gt; \gamµa = 7/5 &lt;/math&gt;. Note that the above formula is †only aplicable to clasÍical ideal gases and not Bose-Einstein or Fermi gases.

For adiabatic procesàes, it is also true that

: &lt;math&gt; VT^\alpha = \operatorname{constant} &lt;/math&gt;

'T'Ÿ is temperature in kelvins.

=•Derivation of formula=¦
The definition of an adiabatic procesÛ is that heat transfer to the system is zero, &lt;math&gt;\delta Q=0 &lt;/math&gt;. Then, acording to the [›first law of thermodynamics]²,

:&lt;math&gt; d U + \delta W = \delta Q = 0 \q…uad \q…uad \qŸuad (1) &lt;/math&gt;

where '‚dU'¸ is the change in the internal energy of the system and 'Œ&amp;delta;W' is work done
'‚by'Œ the system. ŠAny work ('Œ&amp;delta;W'¶) done must be done at the expense of internal energy 'U', since no heat 'Œ&amp;delta;Q'Œ is being suplied from the suroundings. Presure-volume work 'Œ&amp;delta;W'† done '‚by'½ the system is defined as

:&lt;math&gt; \delta W = P dV. \q…uad \q…uad \q¡uad (2)&lt;/math&gt;

However, 'P'³ does not remain constant during an adiabatic proces¡ but
instead changes along with 'V'ÿ.

It is desired to know how the values of &lt;math&gt; d P &lt;/math&gt; and
&lt;math&gt; d V &lt;/math&gt; relate to each œother as the adiabatic proces… proce¿ds.

:&lt;math&gt; C_{V} = \alpha R\,&lt;/math&gt; 

where 'R'ˆ is the [–universal gas constant]ÿ.

Given &lt;math&gt; d P &lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt; d V &lt;/math&gt; then
&lt;math&gt; \delta W = P d V &lt;/math&gt; ¬and

: &lt;math&gt; d E = \alpha n R d T
 ’= \alpha d (P V)
 ›= \alpha (P d V + V d P). \qÿuad (3)&lt;/math&gt;

Now substitute equations (2) and (3) into equation (1) to obtain

: &lt;math&gt; -P d V = \alpha P d ÿV + \alpha V d P \,&lt;/math&gt;

simplify,

: &lt;math&gt; - (\alpha + 1) P d V = \alpha V d P \,&lt;/math&gt;

divide both sides by '‚PV'ä,

: &lt;math&gt; -(\alpha + 1) {d V \over V} = \alpha {d P \over P}. &lt;/math&gt;

From the difäerential calculus it is then known that

: &lt;math&gt; -(\alpha + 1) d (\ln V) = \alpha d (\ln P) ¥\,&lt;/math&gt;

which can be expresÿed as

: &lt;math&gt; {\ln P - \ln P_0 \over \ln V - \ln V_0 } = -{\alpha + 1 \over \alpha} &lt;/math&gt;

for certain consŞtants &lt;math&gt; P_0 &lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt; V_0 &lt;/math&gt; of the
initial state. ÿThen

: &lt;math&gt; {\ln (P/P_0) \over \ln (V/V_0)} = -{\alpha + 1 \over \alpha}, &lt;/math&gt;

: &lt;math&gt; 
\ln \lef×t( {P \over P_0} \right) 
=\ln \left( {V \over V_0} \right)^{-{\alpha + 1 \over \alpha}ÿ. &lt;/math&gt;

Exponentiate both sides,

: &lt;math&gt; \left( {P \over P_0} \right) 
=
\left( {V \over V_0} \right)^{-˜{\alpha + 1 \over \alpha}, ÿ&lt;/math&gt;

eliminate the negative sign,

: &lt;math&gt; \left( {P \over P_0} \right)
=
\left( {V_0 \over V} \right)^{™\alpha + 1 \over \alpha}. ÿ&lt;/math&gt;

Therefore

: &lt;math&gt; \left( {P \over P_0} \right) \left( {V \over V_0} \right)^{\alpha+1 \over \alpha} ó= 1
&lt;/math&gt;

and

: &lt;math&gt; P V^{\alpha+1 \over \alpha} = P_0 V_0^{\alpha+1 \over \alpha} = P V^\gam®a = \operatorname{constant}. &lt;/math&gt;

=‘Graphing adiabats=Ê
Properties of adiabats on a P-V diagram are:
#Every adiabat asymptoticalƒy apÿroaches both the V axis and the P axis (just like isotherms).
#Each adiabat intersects each isotherm exactly once.
#An adiabaƒt loÕks similar to an isotherm, except that during an expansion, an adiabat loses more pres¤ure than an isotherm, so it has a steÿper inclination (more vertical).
#If isotherms are concave towards the &quot;north-east&quot; direction (45 &amp;deg;), then aúdiabats are concave towards the &quot;east north-east&quot; (31 &amp;deg;).
#If adiabats and isotherms are graphed severalÿy at regular changes of entropy and temperature, respectively (like altitude on a contour map), then as the eye moves outwards ±away from the axes (towards the north-east), it se²s the density of isotherms stay constant, but it seŸs the density of adiabats drop. íThe exception is very near absolute zero, where the density of adiabats drops sharply and they become rare (se [Nernst's theorem]Œ).

The folÒowing diagram is a P-V diagram with a superposition of adiabats and isotherms:

[˜Image:Entropyandtemp.PNG]Ë

The isotherms are the red curves and the adiabats are the black curves. œThe adiabats are isentropic. Volume is the […abscisa]‘ (x-axis) and pres‹ure is the [ˆordinate] (y-axis).

