 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¬Image:Amide.png|right|Amide functional group]ƒIn [‰chemistry]…, an '…amide' is either the [™organic chemistry|organic] [functional group]” characterized by a [ˆcarbonyl]“ group linked to a [ˆnitrogen]É atom or a compound that contains this functional group, or a particular [‰inorganic] […anion]¥. In organic chemistry, an amide is es…ential…y an […amine]› where one of the nitrogen [˜substituent|substituents]¨ is a carbonyl group, represented general¬y by the formula: R&lt;sub&gt;1&lt;/sub&gt;([ˆcarbon|C][ˆoxygen|O])[Šnitrogen|N]ÿR&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;R&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; where either or both of R&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; and R&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&‹gt; may be [ˆhydrogen]‹. Specifical¶y, an amide can also be regarded as a derivative of a [carboxylic acid]¢ in which the hydroxyl group has be‘n replaced by an […amine]„ or [am„onia]š.

Compounds in which a [ˆhydrogen]— atom on nitrogen from [am„onia]‡ or an […amine]’ is replaced by a […metal] [†cation] are also known as amides or 'ˆazanides'Õ.

The inorganic amide anion is an extremely strong base, due to the extreme weaknes… of amŠonia as a [BrÃ¸nsted acid]….

=Amide synthesis=‚
[°Image:Amide react.png|right|Amide bond formation]
*Amides are com£only formed from the reaction of a [carboxylic acid]Šs with an […amine]¡.This is the reaction that forms [Œpeptide bond]†s betwe‚n [Šamino acid]£s. These amides can participate in [hydrogen bond|hydrogen bonding]“ as hydrogen bond aceptors and donors, but do not [Šion|ionize]ÿ in aqueous solution, whereas their parent acids and amines are almost completely ionized in solution at neutral pH. Amide form¬ation plays a role in the synthesis of some [”condensation polymer]‹s, such as […nylon]… and [†Kevlar]«. 
* Cyclic amides are synthesized in the [†Beckman„ rearˆangement]· from oximes.
* Other amide forming reactions are the [‚Paserini reaction]‰ and the [ŒUgi reaction]‚
=‘ Amide reactions =™
* Amide breakdown is pos‰ible via [amide hydrolysis]Œ.
* In the [‹Vilsmeier-Ha‹ck reaction]§ an amide is converted into an imine.
=’ Amide Properties =
An amide linkage is kineticalŒy stable to [Šhydrolysis]¸. However, it can be hydrolysed in boiling alkali, as welÿ as in strong acidic conditions. Unlike the hydrolysis of esters, the kinetics of amide hydrolysis is third order: first order Úwith respect to the amide and second order with respect to hydroxide. Amide linkages in a [™biochemistry| biochemical] context are calƒed [peptide link|peptide linkages]¼. Amide linkages constitute a defining molecular feature of [protein|proteins]†, the [“secondary structure]É of which is due in part to the hydrogen bonding abilities of amides.

=‹Derivatives=‚
[‹Sulfonamide]Ås are analogs of amides in which the atom double bonded to oxygen is [†sulfur]˜ rather than carbon.

=“Naming Convertions =Ì
*Example: CH&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;CONH&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; is named [‰acetamide]„ or [Šethanamide]¼
*Other examples: propan-1-amide, N,N-dimethylpropanamide, [Šacrylamide]”
