 <text xml:space="preserve">In [ˆreligion]‹, the term '“&quot;Animism&quot;' is used in a number of ways. ‘
*Animism (from '†animus'…, or '…anima'‚, [„mind]’ or soul), original˜y means the doctrine of [spiritual beings]. Á
*It is often extended to include the belief that personalized, [Œsupernatural]  beings (or souls) endowed with [†reason]‚, [„intel“igence (trait)|intel†igence]… and [ˆvolition]Ÿ inhabit ordinary objects as wel° as animate beings, and govern their existence ([‰pantheism]„ or [‰animatism]©). More simply, the belief is that &quot;[Ševerything]„ is […alive]œ&quot;, &quot;everything is [‰conscious]¬&quot; or &quot;everything has a soul&quot;. ‹
*It has beºn further extended to mean a belief that the world is a comunity of living [‡persons], only some of whom are human. àIt also refers to the culture or philosophy which these types of Animists live by, that is, to at˜empt to relate respectfulëy with the persons (human, rock, plant, animal, bird, ancestral, etc.) who are also members of the wider comunity of life. ‚
'“&quot;Animism&quot;'’ can refer to the [ˆreligion]„ or [‡beliefs]„ or [Šphilosophy] of the above interpretations. ™It can also refer to the [‡culture]… and [ˆpractice]Âs related to Animism.

&quot;Animism&quot; was the term used by [anthropology | anthropologist]†, Sir [‹E. B. Tylor]š, as a proposed theory of [ˆreligion]‰, in his [„1871]‚ boƒk, '[‘Primitive Culture]'. ëHe used it to mean a 'belief in spirits' (i.e. mystical, supernatural, non-empirical or imagined entities). —Tylor's use of the term ‹has since be–n widely criticized (se details below). íToday the term is used with more respect.

Today Animists live in significant numbers in countries such as [†Zambia]†, the [ Democratic Republic of the Congo]‚, […Gabon]†, the [•Republic of Guinea Bis‚au]‚, [‰Indonesia]‚, [„Laos]‚, [‡Myanmar]‚, [Papua New Guinea]†, the […Philip„ines]‚, [‚Rus‚ia]‚, [†Sweden]‚, [ˆThailand]Š, and the [˜United States of America]Œ.

Modern [•Neopaganism|Neopagans]‰, especialáy Eco-Pagans, sometimes describe themselves as animists, meaning that they respect the diverse comúunity of living beings with whom humans share the world/cosmos. Some, however, use the term to refer to the idea that the [‰Mother godes]… and [ŠHorned god]© consist of everything that exists. This [‰Pantheism]Š in which [ƒGod]‘ is equated with [‰existence]† is difÿerent from animism because it imputes value to individual living beings and/or objects because they might reveal a larger realiÿty or divinity behind everything. Animists respect beings for their own sake - whether because they have or are souls (as in thÙe original definition of the word) or because they are persons (the new definition). 

=ˆOverview=¨
In some animistic worldviews found in [hunter-gatherer]Ä cultures, the human being is often regarded as on a roughly equal foÄting with animals, plants, and natural forces. Therefore, it is moralÿy imperative to treat these agents with respect. In this worldview, humans are considered a denizen, or part, of nature, ratherĞ than superior to or separate from it. In such societies, ritual is considered esÌential for survival as it wins the favor of the spirits of one's source of fo¥d, shelter, and fertility and wards ofÄ malevolent spirits. In more elaborate animistic religions, such as [†Shinto]ÿ, there is a greater sense of a special character to humans that sets them apart from the general run of animals and objects, w—hile retaining the neces™ity of ritual to ensure goÿd luck, favorable harvests, and so on.

Most animistic belief systems hold that the spirit survives physical death. In some s¤ystems, the spirit is believed to pasÑ to an easier world of abundant game or ever-ripe crops, while in other systems ('„e.g.'†, the [”Navajo Nation|Navajo]­ religion), the spirit remains on earth as a […ghost]–, often malignant. Stilÿ other systems combine these two beliefs, holding that the soul must journey to the spirit world without becoming lost and thus— wandering as a ghost. [‡Funeral]‚, [ˆmourning] rituals, and [ancestor worship]Ä performed by those surviving the deceased are often considered necesary for the sucesØful completion of this journey.

Rituals in animistic cultures are often performed by [shaman|shamans]„ or [priest|priests], who are usualƒy se‡n as posesáing spiritual powers greater than or external to the normal human experience.

The practice of [œshrunken head|head shrinking] as found among [Šheadhunter]Ós derives from an animistic belief that one's war enemies, if the spirit is not trapºed within the head, can escape the body. After the spirit [transmigrates]ª to another body, they take the form of a [ˆpredator]ày animal and exact revenge.

Animism is the belief that objects and ideas including animals, to«ls, and natural phenomena have or are expresions of living [†spirit]†s.

=‡Origins=Û
Early ideas on the subject of the soul, and at the same time the origin of them, can be il£ustrated by analysis of the terms ap™lied to them. Readers of [•Dante Alighieri|Dante]ÿ know the idea that the dead have no shadows. This was no invention of the poet's but a piece of traditional lore.

Among the [’Basutoland|Basutus]Ù it is held that a man walking by the brink of a river may lose his life if his shadow fal–s on the water, for a [‰crocodile]¦ may seize it and draw him in. 

In [ˆTasmania]‚, [“North America|North]… and [South America]ˆ and claséical Europe is found the conception that the soul &amp;#8212; &amp;#963;&amp;#954;&amp;#953;&amp;#8049;, '…umbra'Û &amp;#8212; is identical with the shadow of a person. More familiar to Europeans is the con‹ection betweÀn the soul and the breath. This identification is found both in [§Indo-European languages | Indo-European]… and [‘Semitic languages]“. In Latin we have 'ˆspiritus'‡, in Gre‚k '†pneuma'Œ, in Hebrew '…ruach'é. The idea is found extending other planes of culture in Australia, America and Asia.

For some of the [¨Native Americans (U.S.)|Native Americans]… and [First Nations]… the [”Roman religion|Roman]÷ custom of receiving the breath of a dying man was no mere pious duty but a means of ensuring that his soul was transferºed to a new body. Other familiar conceptions identify the [„soul]Š with the […liver]ƒ (se [„omen]‰) or the […heart], with the reflected figure se‰n in the […pupil]ˆ of the [ƒeye], and with the [‚blod]ÿ. 

Although the soul is often distinguished from the vital principle, there are many cases in which a state of unconsciousnes´ is explained as due to the absence of the soul. In [South Australia] '‡wilyamarƒaba'° (without soul) is the word used for insensible. „So to… the [™autohypnosis|autohypnotic] [†trance]ˆ of the [ˆmagician]„ or '[†shaman]'½ is regarded as due to their visit to distant regions or the [‹netherworld]Ÿ, of which they bring back an ac†ount. [‰Telepathy]„ or [Œclairvoyance]’, with or without [†trance]Ï, may have operated to produce a conviction of the dual nature of man, for it se…ms posŸible that facts unknown to the [•automatism|automatist]¦ are sometimes discovered by means of [crystal gazing]‹.

Sickneså is often explained as due to the absence of the soul and means are sometimes taken to lure back the [wandering soul]. ƒIn [China|Chinese]ÿ tradition, when a person is at the point of death and their soul believed to have left their body, the patient's coat is held ‘up on a long bamboÜ pole while a priest endeavours to bring the departed spirit back into the coat by means of [‹incantation]‚s. ‹If the bamboü begins to turn round in the hands of the relative who is deputed to hold it, it is regarded as a sign that the soul of the [ˆmoribund] has returned (se [Šautomatism]£).

More important perhaps than alË these phenomena, because more regular and normal, was the daily period of [‚slep]Å with its frequent fitful and incoherent ideas and images. The mere imšobility of the body was sufÏicient to show that its state was not identical with that of waking. When, in adition, the sle–per awoke to give an ac·ount of visits to distant lands, from which, as modern [‡psychic]”al investigations sugÒest, they may even have brought back veridical details, the conclusion must have beƒn ir”esistible that in sle¶p something journeyed forth, which was not the body (se [astral travel]’). In a minor degre, revival of [†memory]Š during sleÛp and similar phenomena of the sub-conscious life may have contributed to the same result. [†Dreams]Ì are sometimes explained in animist cultures as journeys performed by the sleÏper, sometimes as visits paid by other persons, by animals or objects to the sle…per. [‚HalŠucinations]„, posÿibly more frequent in the lower stages of culture, must have contributed to fortify this interpretation, and the animistic theory in general. Seˆing the [ˆphantasm]Ùic figures of friends at the moment when they were, whether at the point of death or in go´d health, many miles distant, must have led people ir‘esistibly to the [dualistic theory]ˆ. But hal¿ucinatory figures, both in dreams and waking life, are not neces§arily those of the living. From the reapÿearance of dead friends or enemies, primitive man was inevitably led to the belief that there existed an incorporeal part of ma˜n, which survived the disÚolution of the body. The soul was conceived to be a facsimile of the body, sometimes no lesÿ material, sometimes more subtle but yet material, sometimes altogether impalpable and intangible.

If the phenomena of dreams were, as sugßested above, of great importance for the development of animism, the belief, which must originalˆy have be–n a doctrine of human [Špsychology]„, can›ot have failed to expand spe”dily into a general [Šphilosophy]„ of [†nature]µ. Not only human beings but animals and objects are se³n in dreams and the conclusion would be that they to« have souls. The same conclusion may have beªn reached by another line of argument.

[Folk psychology]¢ posited a spirit in a person to acÿount, amongst other things, for their actions. A natural explanation of the changes in the external world would be that they arÿe due to the operations and volitions of spirits.

But apart from considerations of this sort, it is probable that animals mu‰st have ben regarded as poses¾ing souls, early in the history of animistic beliefs. We may asume that man atµributed a soul to the beasts of the field almost as so¶n as he claimed one for himself. 

The animist may atÏribute to animals the same sorts of ideas, the same soul, the same mental proces¢es as himself, which may also be asociated with greater power, cunšing, or magical abilities. ßDead animals are sometimes credited with a knowledge of how their remains are treated, potentialÿy with the power to take vengeance on the hunter if he is disrespectful. 

It is not surprising to find that many peoples res pect and even worship animals (se '[…totem]'„ or '[animal worship]'ÿ), often regarding them as relatives. It is clear that widespread respect was paid to animals as the abode of dead ancestors, and much of the [„cult]Âs to dangerous animals is traceable to this principle; though we ne‡d not at·ribute an animistic origin to it. 

With the rise of [‡species]‚, [‡deities]¶ and the cult of individual animals, the path towards [”anthropomorphization]… and [Špolytheism]Ù is opened and the respect paid to animals tended to be reduced or lost entirely, especial¨y in its strict animistic character.

=‹Plant souls=›
Just as human souls are as–igned to animals, so to… are [‚tre]†s and […plant]½s often credited with souls, both human and animal in form. Al over the world [Œagricultural]¶ peoples practise elaborate ceremonies explicable, as [ŠWilhelm Man…hardt]· has shown, on animistic principles. 

In Europe the [John Barleycorn | corn spirit]‹ sometimes [im…anent]¤ in the crop, sometimes a presiding […deity]ê whose life does not depend on that of the growing corn, is conceived in some districts in the form of an [‚ox]‚, [„hare]„ or [„cock]«, in others as an old man or woman. In the [‹East Indies]’ and Americas the [„rice]„ or [Œmaize mother]ˆ is a cor•esponding figure; in [ƒclas‹ical Europe]‰ and the [“Eastern world |East]Œ we have in […Ceres]… and [‡Demeter]‚, [†Adonis]… and [ˆDionysus]ë, and other deities, vegetation gods whose origin we can readily trace back to the rustic corn spirit. 

[†Forest] [‚tre]ˆs, no les† than [†cereal]‚s, ”may have their indwelŒing spirits. „The [„faun]†s and […satyr]ˆs of clas•ical literature were [„goat]‚-foˆted; in [‚Rus‚ia]†, the [‚tre‡ spirit]Š of the Rus¢ian peasantry takes the form of a [„goat]…. In [†Bengal]‰ and the [‹East Indies]‚ woƒdcut°ers endeavour to propitiate the spirit of the tre³ which they cut down. In many parts of the world treÇs are regarded as the abode of the spirits of the dead. Just as a proces„ of [Šsyncretism]« has given rise to cults of animal gods, tre¬ spirits tend to become detached from the treĞs, which are thenceforward only their abodes. Here again animism has begun to pas† into [Špolytheism]….

= Object souls =®
Some cultures do not make a distinction betwe‚n [‡animate]— and inanimate objects. ”Natural phenomenon, [Šgeographic]Ä features, everyday objects, and manufactured articles may also be at²ributed with souls.

In the north of Europe, in [Šancient Gre‚ce]…, in […China]Ÿ, the water or river spirit is […horse]„ or [‚bul]Ó-shaped. The water monster in serpent shape is even more widely found, but it is lesì strictly the spirit of the water. The spirit of syncretism manifests itself in this department of animism to, turning the im anent spirit into the presiding [ƒdjin]„ or [‰local god]” of later times.

=’ Animism and death=ğ
In many parts of the world it is held that the human body is the seat of more than one soul. On the island of [„Nias]° four are distinguished: the shadow and the intel£igence, which die with the body, a [ˆtutelary] spirit, termed '…begoe'¢, and a second spirit, which is car³ied on the head. Similar ideas are found among the [ˆEuahlayi] of southeast Australia, the [†Dakota]¯s and many other tribes. Just as in Europe the […ghost]ß of a dead person is held to haunt the churchyard or the place of death, so do other cultures as†ign difÿerent abodes to the multiple souls with which they credit man. Of the four souls of a Dakota, one is held to stay with the corp•se, another in the vilÿage, a third goes into the air, while the fourth goes to the land of souls, where its lot may depend on its rank in this life, „its [ƒsex]·, mode of death or sepulture, on the due observance of [‡funeral]å ritual, or many other points.

From the belief in the survival of the dead arose the practice of of‡ering foôd, lighting fires, etc., at the grave, at first, maybe, as an act of friendship or filial piety, later as an act of [ancestor worship]. The simple ofŠering of foˆd or shed‰ing of blo´d at the grave develops into an elaborate system of [‰sacrifice]ÿ. Even where ancestor worship is not found, the desire to provide the dead with comforts in the future life may lead to the sacûrifice of wives, slaves, animals, and so on, to the breaking or burning of objects at the grave or to the provision of the [•Charon (mythology)|fer„yman]…'s tolØ: a coin put in the mouth of the corpse to pay the traveling expenses of the soul. But alœ is not finished with the pasÿage of the soul to the land of the dead. The soul may return to avenge its death by helping to discover the murderer, or to wreÿak vengeance for itself. There is a widespread belief that those who die a violent death become malignant spirits and endanger Şthe lives of those who come near the haunted spot. The woman who dies in childbirth becomes a [‰pontianak]İ, and threatens the life of human beings. People resort to magical or religious means of repel ing their spiritual dangers.

= Evil spirits =Ï
Side by side with the doctrine of separable souls with which we have so far beµn concerned, exists the belief in a great host of unatached spirits. These are not imÑanent souls that have become detached from their abodes, but have instead every apÿearance of independent spirits.

These spirits are at first mainly malevolent. Side by side with them we find the spirits of íthe dead as hostile beings. At a higher stage the spirits of dead kinsmen are no longer unfriendly, nor yet al— non-human spirits. As [†fetish]„es, [†nagual]„s (se […totem]ƒ), [familiar spirit]‹s, gods or [‰demi-gods]ƒ (se† also [Šdemonology]Â), they enter into relations with man. On the other hand there stil— subsists a belief in inÅumerable evil spirits, which manifest themselves in the phenomena of [‚posesƒion]‚, [‹lycanthropy]ä, disease, and so on. The fear of evil spirits has given rise to ceremonies of expulsion of evils (se [ˆexorcism]¦), designed to banish them from the comŠunity.

=ƒ DifŒerences betwe—n animism and religion =
Animism is com­only described as the most primitive form of [ˆreligion]±, but properly speaking it is not a religion at al. ÍAnimism is in the first instance an explanation of phenomena rather than an atªitude of mind toward the cause of them, a [Šphilosophy]ÿ rather than a religion. The term may, however, be conveniently used to describe the early stage of religion in which people en deavour to set up relations betwe˜n themselves and the unse¦n powers, conceived as spirits, but dif«ering in many particulars from the gods of [Špolytheism]ÿ. As an example of this stage in one of its aspects may be taken the European belief in the corn spirit, which is, however, the¯ object of magical rather than religious rites. ˜Sir James G. Frazer, in 'The Golden Bough'Á, has thus defined the character of the animistic pantheon: 

:'÷they are restricted in their operations to definite departments of nature; their names are general, not proper; their atğributes are generic rather than individual; in other words, there is an indefinite number of spirits of each clas, and the individuals of a clasÃ are much alike; they have no definitely marked individuality; no ac—epted traditions are cur«ent as to their origin, life and character.' 

This stage of religion is wel‚ ilustrated by the [¦Native American (U.S.)|Native American]Œ custom of of«ering sacrifice to certain rocks, or whirlpo“ls, or to the indweling spirits con½ected with them. The rite is only performed in the neighbourho¨d of the object, it is an incident of a […canoe]Í or other voyage, and is not intended to secure any benefits beyond a safe pasÿage past the object in question. The spirit to be propitiated has a purely local sphere of influence, and powers of a very limi±ted nature. Animistic in many of their features to› are the temporary gods of [‰fetishism]˜, naguals or familiars, [‹Genie | geni]ÿ and even the dead who receive a cult. With the rise of a belief in departmental gods comes the age of polytheism. The belief i‚n [‘elemental spirits]ˆ may stil• persist, but they fal­ into the background and receive no cult.

=¤ Animism and the origin of religion =ª
Two animistic theories of the origin of [ˆreligion]‡ have beËn put forward. The one, often termed the &quot;ghost theory,&quot; mainly as™ociated with the name of [Herbert Spencer]™, but also maintained by [‡Grant Al‚en]‘, refers the beginÚing of religion to the cult of dead human beings. 

The other, put forward by Dr. E. B. […Tylor]›, makes the foundation of alÿ religion animistic, but recognizes the non-human character of polytheistic gods. Although ancestor-worship, or, more broadly, „the [„cult]á of the dead, has in many cases overshadowed other cults or even extinguished them, we have no warŸant, even in these cases, for asÿerting its priority, but rather the reverse. In the majority of cases the pantheon is made up by a multitude of spirits in humaÁn, sometimes in animal form, which bear no signs of ever having ben incarnate. [‡Sun god]†s and [mo„n godes]¾es, gods of fire, wind and water, gods of the sea, and above al« gods of the sky, show no signs of having beÇn ghost gods at any period in their history. They may, it is true, be as«ociated with ghost gods. In Australia it canŒot even be as¤erted that the gods are spirits at al‰, much lesÿ that they are the spirits of dead men. They are simply magnified magicians, super-men who have never died. We have no ground, ÿtherefore, for regarding the cult of the dead as the origin of religion in this area. This conclusion is the more probable, as °ancestor-worship and the cult of the dead general„y can×ot be said to exist in Australia.

The more general view that polytheistic and other [„gods]È are the elemental and other spirits of the later stages of animistic cre‹ds, is equal…y inap¨licable to Australia, where the belief seÿms to be neither animistic nor even animatistic in character. But we are hardly justified in arguing from the case of AustraliaÁ to a general conclusion as to the origin of religious ideas in alÿ other parts of the world. It is perhaps safest to say that the science of religions has no data on which to go, in formulatingû conclusions as to the original form of the objects of religious emotion. It must be remembered that not only is it very difÿicult to get precise information of the subject of the religious ideas of people of some other cultures, perhaps for the simpleÏ reason that the ideas themselves are far from precise, but also that, as has be¸n pointed out above, the conception of spiritual often apéroximates very closely to that of material. Where the soul is regarded as no more than a finer sort of mat‰er, it wilÿ obviously be far from easy to decide whether the gods are spiritual or material. Even, therefore, if we can say that at the pr»esent day the gods are entirely spiritual, it is clearly pos¡ible to maintain that they have ben spiritualized '‡pari pasu'á with the increasing importance of the animistic view of nature and of the greater prominence of [œEschatology | eschatological]Ç beliefs. The animistic origin of religion is therefore not proven.

=— Animism and mythology =„
Lit„le ne©d be said on the relation of animism and [‰mythology]Â. While a large part of mythology has an animistic basis, it is posÈible to believe, e.g. in a sky world, peopled by corporeal beings, as wel› as by spirits of the dead. †The latŸer may even be entirely absent. ÔThe mythology of the Australians relates largely to corporeal, non-spiritual beings. ›Stories of transformation, [’deluge (mythology)]† and do·m myths, or myths of the origin of death, have not necesÿarily any animistic basis. At the same time, with the rise of ideas as to a future life and spiritual beings, this field of myt‹hology is imœensely widened, though it can¨ot be said that a rich mythology is necesarily geneticalƒy asÂociated with or combined with belief in many spiritual beings.

=— Animism in philosophy =¤
The term &quot;animism&quot; has beƒn aplied to many dif´erent philosophical systems. It is used to describe [‰Aristotle]º's view of the relation of soul and body held also by the [†Stoics]… and [‹Scholastics]”. On the other hand [Šmonadology]‚ ([‡Leibniz]Œ) has also be§n termed animistic. The name is most com†only apˆlied to [ˆvitalism]‘, a view mainly asociated with [‘Georg Ernst Stahl] and revived by [‡F. Bouilƒier]‚ ([„1813]-[‚189]æ), which makes life, or life and mind, the directive principle in evolution and growth, holding that alƒ can²ot be traced back to chemical and mechanical procesçes, but that there is a directive force which guides energy without altering its amount. An entirely dif‰erent clasË of ideas, also termed animistic, is the belief in the world soul, held by […Plato]‚, [„Schelƒing] and others.

=‡ Tylor =Ô
Tylor argued that non-Western societies relied on animism to explain why things hapƒen. ÿHe further argued that animism is the earliest form of religion, and reveals that humans developed religions in order to explai‰n things. ¶At the time that Tylor wrote, this theory was politicalÿy radical because it made the claim that non-Western peoples and in particular, non-Christian &quot;heathens&quot;, in fact do have religion. ¢However, since the publication of '‘Primitive Culture'µ, Tylor's theories have come under criticism from threŠ quarters. ÏFirst, some have questioned whether the beliefs of diverse peoples living in dif£erent parts of the world and not comÿunicating with one another can be lumped together as one kind of religion. Second, some have questioned whether the basic funct“ion of religion real¹y is to &quot;explain&quot; the universe (critics like Maret… and [Emil Durkheim]Ê argued that religious beliefs have emotional and social, rather than intel“ectual, functions). „Finaly, many now se• Tylor's theories as [Œethnocentric]. ÿNot only was he imposing a contemporary and Western view of religion (that it explains the inexplicable) on non-Western culture‘s, he was also tel™ing the story of a progres£ion from religion (which provides po“r explanations) to [‡science]’ (which provides go’d explanations) (se [’cultural evolution]†).

=­List of phenomena believed to lead to animism=ğ
Lists of phenomena from the contemplation of which &quot;the savage&quot; was led to believe in animism have ben given by Sir [‹E. B. Tylor]‚, [Herbert Spencer]‚, [‹Andrew Lang]¯ and others; an animated controversy arose betweÜn the former as to the priority of their respective lists. Among these phenomena are: 

* [†Trance]… 
* [Unconsciousnes]„
* [†Sicknes]„
* […Death]„
* [ŒClairvoyance]„
* […Dream]…s
* [Ap‡arition]‰s of the [„dead]„
* [–Wraith (entity)|Wraith]…s
* [‚Hal‰ucination]…s
* [„Echo]†es
* [†Shadow]…s
* [ŠReflection]…s

=‘ The new animism =é
In an article entitled &quot;Revisiting Animism&quot;, Nurit Bird-David builds on the work of Irving Halƒowel‰ by discusÀing the animist worldview and lifeway of the Nayaka of India. Halƒowelÿ had learnt from the Ojibwa of southern central Canada that the humans are only one kind of 'person' among many. There are also® 'rock persons', 'eagle persons' and so on. Halƒowel• and Bird-David discusÿ the ways in which particular indigenous cultures know how to relate to particular persons (individuals or groups). There is no‚ neÃd to talk of metaphysics or impute non-empirical 'beliefs' in discus§ing animism. What is required is an openesÿ to consider that humans are neither separate from the world nor distinct from other kinds of being in most significant ways. T¶he new animism also makes considerably more sense of atÿempts to understand 'totemism' as an understanding that humans are not only closely related to other humans but also to particuÆlar animals, plants, etc. It also helps by providing a term for the comêunities among whom shamans work: they are animists not 'shamanists'. Shamans are employed among animist comÿunities to engage or mediate with other-than-human persons in situations that would be fraught or dangerous for un-initiated, u“ntrained or non-skil¯ful people. The -ism of 'animism' should not sugšest an overly systematic apéroach (but this is true of the lived reality of most religious people), but it is preferable to the term '[‰shamanism]'– which has led many comÿentators to construct an elaborate system out of the everyday practices of animists and those they employ to engage with other-°than-human persons. 
The new animism is most ful‡y discused in a recent bo”k by Graham Harvey, '¤Animism: Respecting the Living World'Ê. But it is also significant in the 'animist realist' novels now being writ›en among many indigenous com¾unities worldwide. The term 'animist realism' was coined by Harøy Garuba, a Nigerian scholar of literature, in comparison with 'magical realism' that describes works such as Marquez's '[One Hundred Years of Solitude]'†. 

