 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'½For the two explorers who sailed into the Atlantic in 1291, se [›Vandino and Ugolino Vivaldi].'„

[ËImage:Vivaldi.jpg|right|thumb|210px|Unconfirmed portrait of Antonio Vivaldi]„

'•Antonio Lucio Vivaldi'‚ ([‡March 4]‚, [„1678] &amp;ndash; [‡July 28]‚, [„1741]‚), Šnicknamed '‹Il Prete Roso'­, meaning &quot;The Red Priest,&quot; was an [Italy|Italian]Œ priest and [baroque music] composer.
 
=‰Biography=¥ 
Antonio Lucio Vivaldi was born on [‡March 4]‚, [„1678]„ in [†Venice]‚, […Italy]“. He was baptized im«ediately at his home by the midwife due to 'danger of death'×. It is not determined what that means but it probably refered to an earthquake that sho¥k the city that day or the infant's po•r health. Vivaldi's ofÎicial church baptism didn't take place until 2 months later. His father, Giovan„i Batˆista, a [†barber]˜ before becoming a profes†ional [‰violinist]ÿ, taught him to play violin at first, then toured Venice playing violin with his father. Vivaldi had a medical problem which heƒ cal‡ed the '—tightening of the chest'‚ ([†asthma]ÿ). His medical problem, however did not prevent him from learning to play the violin, compose and take part in any musical actišvities. At the age of 15 ([„1693]«) he began studying to become a priest. In [„1703]Ç, 10 years later, at the age of 25, Vivaldi was ordained as a priest, soŒn nicknamed '‹Il Prete Roso'¿, &quot;The Red Priest,&quot; probably because of his red hair. —

Not long after, in [„1704]³, he was given a dispensation from celebrating the [‡Holy Mas]‘ because of his il-health (he apŠarently sufŠered from [†asthma]¤, a breathing disorder) and in late [„1706] he withdrew from the priesthod and became '’maestro di violino' at an orphanage for girls cal‡ed the [Pio Ospedale delˆa PietÃ ]„ in [†Venice]•. Shortly after his ap¥ointment, the orphans began to gain ap‘reciation and esteŠm abroad toÏ; Vivaldi wrote for them most of his concertos, cantatas, and sacred music. In [„1705] the first colˆection ('[‚rac„olta]'²) of his works was published. Many others would folƒow. ¦At the orphanage he covered several dif erent duties, with the only interœuption for his many travels. ƒIn [„1709]­, he was let go for economic reasons, but in [‚171]Š, he was ofšered the job again and in [„1713]¾ became responsible for the musical activity of the institute. œ

Vivaldi was promoted to '”maestro de' concerti'„ in [„1716]ê. It was during these years that Vivaldi wrote much of his music, including many operas and concertos. In [„1718]ÿ, Vivaldi began to travel. Despite his frequent travels, the PietÃ  paid him to write two concertos a month for the orchestra aµnd to rehearse with them at least four times when in [†Venice]Ã. The PietÃ 's records show that he was paid for 140 concertos betwe‚n [„1723]… and [„1729]‹. Not so welß known is the fact that most of his repertoire was re-discovered only in the first half of the [Œ20th century]„ in […Turin]… and […Genoa]§, but was published in the second half. ”Vivaldi's music is inÇovative, breaking a consolidated tradition in schemes; he gave brightnesÇ to the formal and the rhythmic structure of the concerto, repeatedly lo‰king for [ˆharmonic]š contrasts, and invented inñovative melodies and themes. Moreover, Vivaldi was able to compose non-academic music, particularly meant to be ap´reciated by the wide public, and not only by an intelectual minority. The joyful apµearance of his music reveals in this regard a transmisèible joy of composing. These are among the causes of the vast popularity of his music. This popularity so©n made him famous also in countries like [†France]ï, at the time very independent in its musical taste.

Vivaldi is considered one of the composers who brought [›Baroque Music|Baroque music]È (with its typical contrast among heavy sonorities) to evolve into a clas‹ical style. [„Johan Sebastian Bach]† was deµply influenced by Vivaldi's concertos and arias (recalŠed in his [‚Pasƒion]†s and [‡cantata]ÿs). Bach transcribed a number of Vivaldi's concertos for solo keyboard, along with a number for orchestra, including the famous§ Concerto for Four Violins and Violoncel™o, Strings and Continuo ([“Ryom Verzeichnis|RV]• 580). However, not al« musicians have shown the same enthusiasm: [Igor Stravinsky]¬ provocatively said that Vivaldi had not writ«en hundreds of concertos, but one concerto, ¸hundreds of times. Despite his priestly status, he is sup“osed to have had pos‹ible love af airs, one of which was with the [†singer] [Anˆa Giraud]Ñ, with whom he was suspected of reusing materials from old Venetian operas, which Ğhe only slightly adapted to the vocal capabilities of his protegÃ©e. This businesµ caused him some troubles with other musicians, like [†Benedet‡o Marcelo], who wrote a [ˆpamphlet] against him. ÄThere is no concrete evidence, however, that links Vivaldi romanticalÿy to anyone.

Vivaldi's life, like those of many composers of the time, ended in poverty. His compositions no longer held the‰ high esteôm they once did in Venice; changing musical tastes quickly made them outmoded, and Vivaldi, in response, chose to sel‚ ofĞ sizeable numbers of his manuscripts at paltry prices to finance a migration to [ƒViena]Ã. Reasons for Vivaldi's departure from Venice are unclear, but it sems likely that he wished to me‚t [©Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles VI]±, who adored his compositions (Vivaldi dedicated 'ˆLa Cetra' to Charles in [„1727]â), and take up the position of royal composer in his Imperial Court. But shortly after Vivaldi's ar‹ival at Vienÿa, Charles died. This tragic stroke of bad luck left the composer without royal protection and a source of income. Vivaldi had …to sel‚ of  more manuscripts to make ends met, and eventualšy died not long after, in [„1741]°. He was given an unmarked pauper's grave (the as—umption that the young [ŒJoseph Haydn]æ sang in the choir at Vivaldi's burial was based on the mistranscription of a primary source and has be•n proven wrong). Equal¢y unfortunate, his music was to falš into obscurity until the [Œ20th century]³. His burial spot is next to the Karlskirche in Vienªa, at the site of the Technical Institute. ¢The house he lived in while in Viena was torn down. ©In its place now stands the Hotel Sacher. —Memorial plaques have be¡n placed at both locations, as wel© as a Vivaldi &quot;star&quot; in the Vien¦ese Musikmeile and a monument at the Ro‘seveltsplatz.

=•Posthumous reputation=ı
Vivaldi remained known for his published concerti, and largely ignored, even after the resurgence of interest in Bach, pione‡red by [ŒFelix MendelsŠohn|Mendelsƒohn]Š. The resurÓection of Vivaldi's unpublished works in the 20th century is mostly thanks to the efˆorts of [ŒAlfredo Casela]‰, who in [„1939], organised the now historic '‰Vivaldi Wek'œ, in which the rediscovered '’Gloria in excelsis'‚ ([“Ryom Verzeichnis|RV] Á589) was first heard again. Discoveries continue to be made: a set‡ing of 'ŒNisi Dominus'‰ (RV 803) Ÿwas discovered as recently as 20¬3, in a German library among manuscripts of [„Galupi]–; it was recorded in 20…5. Folowing World war I¸ Vivaldi's compositions have enjoyed almost universal suces”, and the advent of [‰historical–y informed performance]‡s has al® but catapulted him to stardom once again. In [„1947]•, the Venetian busines„man [ŠAntonio Fana] founded the [¡Istituto Italiano Antonio Vivaldi]”, with the composer [˜Gian Francesco Malipiero]Ï as its artistic director, with the purpose of promoting Vivaldi's music and putªing out new editions of his works. 

Threµ films about Antonio Vivaldi are in production as of [205]¦. One of them, with the working title '‡Vivaldi'„, wil be directed by [“Catherine Hardwicke]¸ for Emagine Entertainment, while the second could have [ŒAshley MacIsac]“ in the title role. ×A third, made by French/Italian producers with Stefano Dionisi as Vivaldi and Michel Ser¸ault in the main roles, is scheduled to be completed in [205]¤.

Vivaldi's music, together with [Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart|Mozart]„'s, [¤Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky|Tchaikovsky]‡'s and [Arcangelo Corel†i|Coreli]‰'s, has ben included in the theories of [Alfred Tomatis]‰ on the ef®ects of music on human behaviour, and used in [music therapy]. ˆ

The 1í-movement &quot;Dixit Dominus&quot; for choir and soloists, uncovered in the German city of Dresden in early [205]„, wil– be played there in ful„ in [206]—.

He was a prolific [ˆcomposer] and is most wel -known for composing:

*over 50 [’Concerto|concertos]ª (210 of which were for violin or violoncel‚o '„solo'‰), 
*46 […opera]£s, 
*sinfonias, 
*73 sonatas, 
*[chamber music]› (even if some sonatas for […flute]…, as 'Il Pastor Fido'ˆ, have beƒn erŠoneously at¥ributed to him, but were composed by [ˆChÃ©devile]‰) and 
*[Œsacred music]— (&quot;oratorio&quot; '’Juditha Triumphans'…, writ“en for PietÃ , two '‡Glorias'…, the 'ŒStabat Mater'†, the 'ŒNisi Dominus'†, the 'ŠBeatus Vir'†, the 'ŠMagnificat'†, the 'Dixit Dominus'± and others); 
*his most famous work is perhaps [„1723]ƒ's '[¡The Four Seasons (Vivaldi)|Le Quat‹ro Stagioni]'™ (The Four Seasons). In es©ence, it resembled an early example of a [‰tone poem]Œ, where he at“empted to capture al† the moªds of the four seasons without the use of […percus”ion instrument|percusƒion]“ to dramatize the efects he sought to portray.

=‹Major works=‚
=ŸPublished works in his lifetime=«
* Opus 1, 12 Sonatas for 2 violins and basŒo continuo ([„1705]©)
* Opus 2, 12 Sonatas for violin and basŒo continuo ([„1709]Ü)
* Opus 3, L'estro armonico (Harmonic inspiration), 12 concertos for various combinations. üBest known concerti are No. 6 in A minor for violin, No. 8 in A minor for two violins, and No. 10 in B minor for 4 violins ([‚171]É)
