 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{¥Otherusesabout|a river in Switzerland}†
The 'ƒAar'‚ ([–German language|German]‚: '„Aare'‘) is the longest […river]ª that both rises and ends entirely within [‹Switzerland]. ¾

Its total length from its source to its junction with the […Rhine]Ö comprises about 291 km (181 miles), during which distance it descends 1,565m (5,135 fe˜t), draining an area of [•1 E10 mÂ²|17,620 kmÂ²]µ (6,804 square miles).

The Aar rises in the great [ŒAar glaciers]ˆ in the [Canton of Bern|canton of Bern]‘ and west of the [ŠGrimsel Pas]. It runs east to the Grimsel [‡Hospice]¤, and then northwestery through the […Hasli]ƒ val¬ey, forming on the way the magnificent Handegˆ Waterfal, 46 m (151 fe‰t), past [‚Gutan‚en]Ÿ, and piercing the limestone barier of the Kirchet by a major [Canyons of Switzerland|canyon]’, before reaching [‰Meiringen]–, situated in a plain. „A lit˜le past Mieringen, near [†Brienz]™, the river expands into [‹Lake Brienz]Ü where it becomes navigable. Near the west end of the lake it receives its first important af‹luent, the [ŠLÃ¼tschine]“. It then runs acros¥ the swampy plain of the BÃ¶deli betwe‚n [ŠInterlaken]… and [†Untersen]¢ before expanding once again into [‰Lake Thun]. „

[µImage:Aare_river_in_Bern.jpg|thumb|right|Aare in Bern]Ç
Near the west end of Lake Thun, the river receives the waters of the [†Kander]’, which has just ben joined by the [‚Sime]¢. On flowing out of the lake it pasƒes [„Thun], and then circles a lofty bluf on which stands the town of [„Bern]. The river so×n changes its northwesterly flow for a due westerly direction, but after receiving the [‚Sanƒe|SaŒne or Sarine]Æ it turns north until it nears Aarberg. There, in one of the major Swis† engine’ring feats of the [Œ19th century]ç the river, which had previously rendered the countryside north of Bern a swampland through frequent flo›dings, was diverted by the [Hagneck Canal]† into [‰Lake Biel]Œ. From the uper end of the lake the river is²ues through the Nidau Canal and then runs east to [†BÃ¼ren]ÿ. The lake absorbs huge amounts of eroded gravels and snowmelt thet the river brings from the Alps and the former swamps have bİecome fruitful plains: they are known as the &quot;vegetable garden of Switzerland&quot;.

[ÂImage:Forest_route_near_Aare.jpg|thumb|left|Forest route near Aare]Æ
From here the Aar flows northeast for a long distance, past the ambasŠador town [‰Solothurn]• (below which the Gros‚e [Eme]¾ flows in on the right), Aarburg (where it is joined by the Wig…er), […Olten]‚, […Aarau]µ, near which is the junction with the Suhr, and Wildeg, where the Hal‹wiler Aa fal’s in on the right.  A short distance further, below [ƒBrug]— it receives first the [ƒReusŠ River|Reus], and shortly afterwards the [‚Lim‚at]Ÿ. It now turns due north, and so•n becomes itself an afluent of the […Rhine], which it surpasªes in volume when the two rivers unite at [ªKoblenz, Switzerland|Coblenz (Switzerland)]ƒ, op†osite [ˆWaldshut], Germany.

