 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{–mergeto|Abgarus of Edesa}‚
'…Abgar'„ or '…Agbar'¹ was the favored name among a dynasty of local rulers at [ƒEdes’a, Mesopotamia|Edesa]Ê in northern Mesopotamia, the most famous of whom figured in the forged cor˜espondence with Jesus, ac”epted as genuine in [”Eusebius of Caesarea]ƒ's 'Ec“lesiastical History'Û and extensively quoted. The story of the conversion of Abgar to Christianity is traditionalŒy linked to [Abgarus of Edes‰a|Abgar V]Ğ, but Walter Bauer made a case for the Abgarus who converted being Abgar IX.

=ŠReferences=…

*[ŒWalter Bauer]‚, '­Orthodoxy and Heresy in Earliest Christianity', 1934, (in English 1971) ([ht‚p:/cŠat.sas.upenˆ.edu/~humÂ/Resources/Bauer/bauer01.htm#FN1 On-line text]
*Robert Eisenman, 'éJames the Brother of Jesus : The key to Unlocking the Secrets of Early Christianity and the Dead Sea Scrol‚s,'‚ 19›7 (Viking Penguin). Especial˜y thje section &quot;Thad¬eus, Judas Thomas and the conversion of the [ƒEdes˜a, Mesopotamia|Osrhoeans]ˆ&quot;, p„ 189f….

