 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[‡Image:AbŠotsford Mor•is edited.jpg|thumb|30Špx|right|AbŒotsford in 18‚0.]‚
'Ab‡otsford'† is a [historic house]’ in the region of [ƒScot‹ish Borders]‘ in the south of [ˆScotland]‡, near [™Melrose, Scotland|Melrose]—, on south bank of the [ˆRiver Twed]¬. It was formerly the residence of novelist [ŠWalter Scot]©. 

The nucleus of the estate was a smalŠ farm of 10¦ acres (0.4&amp;nbsp;km&amp;sup2;), calªed Cartleyhole, nicknamed Clarty (i.e., mudšy) Hole, and bought by Scotœ on the lapse of his lease ([‚181]Á) of the neighbouring house of Ashestiel. He at first built a smalƒ vil³a (now the western end of the house) and named it AbÁotsford, creating the name from a ford near by where previously ab‡ots of [‰Melrose Ab‚ey]Œ used to cros the river. Scot then built adÄitions to the house and made it into a mansion, building into the wal²s many sculptured stones from ruined castles and ab¼eys of Scotland. In it he gathered a large library, and a col’ection of ancient [‰furniture]¼, arms and armour, and other relics and curiosities, especial„y con‹ected with [ƒScot‹ish history]ì.

The last and principal acquisition being that of Toftfield (afterwards named Huntlyburn), purchased in [„1817]. ®The new house was then begun and completed in [„1824]. ¥

The general ground-plan is a paralelogram, with irŸegular outlines, one side overlo‹king the TweŸd; and the style is mainly the [ƒScotŒish Baronial]. ìInto various parts of the fabric were built relics and curiosities from historical structures, such as the do”rway of the old Tolbo†th in [‰Edinburgh]. ‡

Scot¯ had only enjoyed his residence one year when ([„1825]È) he met with that reverse of fortune which involved the estate in debt. ƒIn [„1830]µ the library and museum were presented to him as a fre— gift by the creditors. ”The property was whol“y disencumbered in [„1847] by Robert Cadelš, the publisher, who cancelŞed the bond upon it in exchange for the family's share in the copyright of Sir Walter's works. „

[‹Image:Wfm ab™otsford.jpg|thumb|right|30„px|AbŸotsford House (north elevation)]‡

ScotÓ's only son Walter did not live to enjoy the property, having died on his way from […India]„ in [„1847]. “Among subsequent posesŒors were Scot­'s son-in-law, J. G. Lockhart, J. R. Hope Scot‚, [„Q.C.]—, and his daughter (Scot's great-grandœaughter), the Hon. Mrs Maxwel„ Scot. …

Ab¤otsford gave its name to the &quot;Ab˜otsford Club,&quot; a sucesŠor of the [‚Banatyne Club|Ban…atyne]² and Maitland clubs, founded by W. B. D. D. Turnbul„ in [„1834]‡ in Scotº's honour, for printing and publishing historical works con˜ected with his writings. ŸIts publications extended from [„1835]„ to [„1864] .

The house continued to be ocŒupied by Scotâ's descendents for several generations, and was opened to the public in recent times. However in 20Š5 it was anounced that fol¢owing the death of Dame Jean Maxwel„ Scot×, the great, great, great grand-daughter of the writer the previous year, it was not pos§ible for any family member to live at Ab‰otsford. [‘Historic Scotland]‰ and the [›National Trust for Scotland]† are at¥empting to secure the house's future. ŒHowever, Scot¦ish Borders Council are considering an ƒ
apÃlication by a property developer to build a housing estate on the opšosite bank of the River Tweˆd from Abˆotsford. ·If the council give this development the go ahead it wil½ forever ruin the views Sir Walter enjoyed from his study. [ht‚p:/w.timesonline.co.uk/newspaper/0,Œ173-1617987,0Š.html]
[ht‚p:/©news.scotsman.com/scotland.cfm?id=23573620„5]
