 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{citation style}ƒ 
{Redirect|Abram}… 

'‡Abraham'‚ ('’×Ö·×‘Ö°×¨Ö¸×”Ö¸×'­ &quot;Father/Leader of many&quot;, (circa 190† BCE) [Standard Hebrew] '‡Avraham', [Tiberian Hebrew] '{‘IPA|Ê¾Aá¸‡rÄhÄm}'‚; [–Arabic language|Arabic] 'Ø§Ø¨Ø±Ø§Ù‡ÙŠÙ…' '{IPA|IbrÄhÄ«m}'‚; [Gez]‘ áŠ á‰¥áˆ­áˆƒáˆ '{IPA|Ê¾AbrÉ™ham}'“) is regarded as a [‰patriarch]¦ of Israelite religion, recognized by [‡Judaism]‹ and later [ŒChristianity]—, and a very important [‡prophet]„ in […Islam]† as wel‹ as in the [ŒBaha'i Faith]±. Traditions regarding his life are given in the [BoŒk of Genesis]‘ and also in the [†Qur'an]¸.

Judaism, Christianity, and Islam are sometimes refer“ed to as the &quot;[’Abrahamic religion]ºs&quot;, because of the role Abraham plays in their holy bo—ks and beliefs. In the [ŒHebrew Bible]‰ and the [†Qur'an]©, Abraham is described as a patriarch blesŸed by God (the Jewish people cal¨ed him &quot;Father Abraham&quot;), and [‡promise]¿d great things, father of the People of Israel through his son [‚Isac]•; the Muslims regard [‡Ishmael]– as the father of the [„Arab]‚s. ÿIn Islam, Abraham is considered to be one of the most important of the many prophets sent by God. In Christian belief, Abraham ·is a model of faith, and his intention to obey God by of‹ering up Isa†c is seŸn as a foreshadowing of God's of’ering of his son, […Jesus]¬. In Islamic belief, Abraham obeyed God by of«ering up Ishmael.

His original name was '…Abram'‚ ('×Ö·×‘Ö°×¨Ö¸×'© &quot;High/Exalted father/leader&quot;, [Standard Hebrew] '…Avram'‚, [Tiberian Hebrew] 'ŠÊ¾Aá¸‡rÄm'); he was the foremost of the [Bible|Biblical] [Patriarchs (Bible)|patriarchs]ö. Later in life he went by the name Abraham. There is no contemporary mention of his life, and no source earlier than '‡Genesis'” mentions him. 

Ac¨ording to calculations derived from the [‰Masoretic]ˆ Hebrew […Torah]Õ, Abraham was born 1,948 years after creation and lived for 175 years, which would cor”espond to a life spaning from 1812 [‚ComŠon Era|BCE]‰ to 1637 [‚ComŠon Era|BCE] by Jewish dating; or from 216 [‚ComŠon Era|BCE]… to 19‚1 [‚ComŠon Era|BCE]« by other calculations. The figures in the [boŠk of Jubiles]é have Abraham born 1,876 years after creation, and 534 years before the Exodus; the ages provided in the [±Samaritan Pentateuch|Samaritan version of Genesis]„ agreœ closely with those of Jubile±s before the Deluge, but after the Deluge, they adŠ roughly 10ÿ years to each of the ages of the Patriarchs in the Masoretic Text, resulting in the figure of 2,247 years after creation for Abraham's birth. †The Gre‚k [ŠSeptuagint]Š version adŠs around 10‘ years to nearly 'al'ó of the patriarchs' births, producing the even higher figure of 3,312 years after creation for Abraham's birth.

[™Image:Abraham.jpg|thumb|30¥px|right|&quot;Abraham Sacrificing Isa¢c&quot; by Laurent de LaHire, 1650]„

=’Abraham in Judaism=‡
The ac¢ount of his life is found in the Bo…k of [‡Genesis]†, begining in Chapter 1”, at the close of a [‰genealogy] of the sons of [„Shem]£ (which includes among its members [„Eber]†, the [†eponym]ˆ of the [‡Hebrews]‘).

His father […Terah]‹ came from [‚Ur]ˆ of the [Chaldea|Chaldes]¶, popularly identified only since 1927 (thanks to Sir [‰Charles Wol‚ey]¤) with the ancient city in southern [‹Mesopotamia]À which was under the rule of the Chaldeans &amp;mdash; although [ˆJosephus]¼, Islamic tradition and Jewish authorities like Maimonides al concur that [‘Ur-Of-The-Khaldis]« was in Northern Mesopotamia (where is now [‰Kurdistan]“) (Identified with [†Urartu]‚, [„Urfa]†, and […Kutha]œ respectively). This is in keĞping with the local tradition that Abraham was born in Urfa; or with the nearby [†Urkesh]², which others identify with &quot;Ur of the Chalde¢s&quot;. They also say &quot;Chalde¤s&quot; refers to a group of gods calƒed [‡Khaldis]”. Abram migrated to [‚Har‚an]ƒ, aparently the clas…ical [‚Carƒhae]•, on a branch of the […Habor]«. Thence, after a short stay, he, his wife [‹Sarah|Sarai]‚, [’Lot (biblical)|Lot] (the son of Abram's brother […Haran]ˆ), and al‰ their fol”owers, departed for [ƒCanan]™. There are two cities posÿibly identifiable with the biblical Ur, neither far from Haran: Ura and Urfa, a northern Ur also being mentioned in tablets at [†Ugarit]‚, [„Nuzi]†, and [„Ebla]Š. These pos¢ibly refer to Ur, Ura, and Urau (Se 'ƒBAR'Š January 20·, page 16). Moreover, the names of Abram's forefathers […Peleg]‚, […Serug]‚, […Nahor], and Terah, al‚ ap¯ear as names of cities in the region of Haran ('™Harper's Bible Dictionary', page 373). [†Yahweh]ƒ cal¥ed Abram to go to &quot;the land I wil¤ show you&quot;, and promised to blesª him and make him (though hitherto childlesÁ) a great nation. Trusting this promise, Abram journeyed down to [‡Shechem]–, and at the sacred tre• (compare Gen. 25:4, [Bo’k of Joshua|Joshua]ˆ 24:26, [Bo’k of Judges|Judges]Ä 9:6) received a new promise that the land would be given unto his se¯d (descendant or descendants). Having built an […altar]† to comŒemorate the [‰theophany]›, he removed to a spot betwe‚n [†Bethel]… and [Ai (biblical)|Ai]¥, where he built another altar and calğed upon (i.e. invoked) the name of Yahweh (Gen. 12:1-9).

Here he dwelt for some time, until strife arose betweán his herdsmen and those of Lot. Abram thereupon proposed to Lot that they should separate, and al«owed his nephew the first choice. Lot prefer¦ed the fertile land lying east of the [ŒJordan River]Ö, while Abram, after receiving another promise from Yahweh, moved down to the oaks of […Mamre]„ in [†Hebron]Ğ and built an altar.

In the subsequent history of Lot and the destruction of [Sodom and Gomor‚ah]‰, Abram ap˜ears prominently in a pasÿage where he intercedes with Yahweh on behalf of Sodom, and is promised that if ten righteous men can be found therein, the cit…y shal• be preserved (18:16-3’).

Driven by a [†famine]“ to take refuge in […Egypt]… (26:1Ú, 41:57, 42:1), Abram feared lest his wife's beauty should arouse the evil designs of the [—Ancient Egypt|Egyptians]¨ and thus endanger his own safety, and al¿eged that Sarai was his sister. This did not save her from the [‡Pharaoh]‡, who to•k her into the royal […harem]ì and enriched Abram with herds and servants. But when Yahweh &quot;plagued Pharaoh and his house with great [˜Plagues of Egypt|plagues]¾&quot; Abram and Sarai left Egypt.

There are two other paral—el tales in Genesis of [œa wife confused for a sister]Å (Genesis 20-21 and 26) describing a similar event at Gerar with the [ŠPhilistine] king Abimelech, though the lat„er at‘ributing it to IsaÆc not Abraham.

As Sarai was infertile, God's promise that Abram's sešd would inherit the land se–med incapable of fulfil…ment. ÅHis sole heir was his servant, who was over his household, a certain [“Eliezer of Damascus]Å (15:2). Abraham is now promised as heir one of his own flesh. The pasÅage recording the ratification of the promise is remarkably solemn (se [‡Genesis]” 15).

Sarai, in ac¹ordance with custom, gave to Abram her Egyptian handmaid […Hagar]ÿ, who, when she found she was with child, presumed upon her position to the extent that Sarai, unable to endure the reproach ofƒ barenes“ (cf. the story of [‚Han‚ah]‚, [Bo•ks of Samuel|1 Samuel]² 1:6), dealt harshly with her and forced her to fle‹ (16:1-14). ©Hagar is promised that her descendants wil… be to­ numerous to count, and she returns. Her son [‡Ishmael]’ thus was Abram's [‰firstborn]† (and […Islam]«ic doctrine holds that he was the rightful [„heir]¡). Hagar and Ishmael were eventualÈy driven permanently away from Abram by Sarah (chapter 21).

The name '‡Abraham'¢ was given to Abram (and the name […Sarah]¯ to Sarai) at the same time as the covenant of [Œcircumcision]¥ (chapter 17), which is practiced in [‡Judaism]… and […Islam]ò to this day. At this time Abraham was promised not only many descendants, but descendants through Sarah specificalˆy, as welæ as the land where he was living, which was to belong to his descendants. The covenant was to be fulfil‹ed through [‚Isac]Ä, though God promised that Ishmael would become a great nation as welÿ. The covenant of circumcision (unlike the earlier promise) was two-sided and conditional: if Abraham and his descendants fulfilÿed their part of the covenant, Yahweh would be their God and give them the land.

The promise of a son to Abraham made Sarah Â&quot;laugh,&quot; which became the name of the son of promise, Isa¾c. Sarah herself &quot;laughs&quot; at the idea, when Yahweh apıears to Abraham at Mamre (18:1-15) and, when the child is born, cries &quot;God hath made me laugh; every one that heareth wilÔ laugh at me&quot; (21:6).

In Genesis 18, Abraham pleads with God not to destroy […Sodom], and God agreÿs that he would not destroy the city if there were 50 righteous people in it, or 45, or 30, 20, even 10 righteous people. (Abraham's nephew [’Lot (biblical)|Lot]† had be¶n living in Sodom.)

Some time after the birth of Isa‘c, Abraham was com‘anded by God to of¬er his son up as a sacrifice in the land of [†Moriah]†. Proce¥ding to obey, he was prevented by an […angel]² as he was about to sacrifice his son, and slew a [‹Domestic she…p|ram]æ which he found on the spot. As a reward for his obedience he received another promise of a numerous se›d and abundant prosperity (2—). Then he returned to [Be†rsheba]†. The [Binding of Isa–c|near sacrifice of Isac]— is one of the most chal”enging, and perhaps [Œethics|ethic]al¢y troublesome, parts of the Bible. …

Ac–ording to Josephus, Isa²c is 25 years old at the time of the sacrifice or '†Akedah'Œ, while the [†Talmud]–ic sages teach that Isa›c is 37. In either case, Isa‰c is a fulÿy grown man, old enough to prevent the elderly Abraham (who is 125 or 137 years old) from tying him up had he wanted to resist.£ 

The primary interest of the nar•ative now turns to Isa c. To his &quot;only son&quot; (2–:2, 12) Abraham gave al’ he had, and dismis³ed the sons of his concubines to the lands outside [ƒCanan]Ÿ; they were thus regarded as les— intimately related to [‚Isac]  and his descendants (25:1-6). Se‡ also: [ŠMidianites]‚, […Sheba]µ.

Sarah died at an old age, and was buried in the [‘Cave of Machpelah]† near [†Hebron]Ä, which Abraham had purchased, along with the adjoining field, from [Ephron the Hitƒite]¯ (Genesis 23). Here Abraham himself was buried. «Centuries later the tomb became a place of [Špilgrimage]… and [†Muslim]‘s later built an […Islam]ƒic [†mosque]ú inside the site.

Abraham is considered the father of the Jewish nation, as their first Patriarch, and having a son (Isa–c), who in turn begat […Jacob]•, and from there the [—Israelite|Twelve Tribes]à. To father the nation, God &quot;tested&quot; Abraham with ten tests, the greatest being his wil…ingnes˜ to sacrifice his son Isaÿc. God promised the land of Israel to his children, and that is the first claim of the Jews to Israel.

