=³Abraham and his descendants (Biblical Perspective) =‚
'ˆFor a ful‚ acŒount of the '‹historicity' of Abrahamic stories in the bo„k of' [‡Genesis]‚, 'se' [ÂThe Bible and history#The Patriarchs|Historicity of the Patriarchs].

Biblical narÿatives represent Abraham as a wealthy, powerful and supremely virtuous man, but humanly flawed, and when afraid for himself, mièscalculating, and a sometimes deceiver and an inconsiderate husband. But his central importance in the boÇk of Genesis, and his portrait as a man favored by God, is unequivocal. ŸAbraham's generations (Hebrew: '[ˆtoledoth]'’, translated to Greàk: &quot;Genesis&quot;) are presented as part of the crowning explanation of how the world has beÿn fashioned by the hand of God, and how the boundaries and relationships of peoples were established by him.

As the father o„f Isa«c and Ishmael, Abraham is ultimately the com“on ancestor of the [ŠIsraelites]¨ and their neighbours. As the father of [‡Ishmael]½, whose twelve sons became desert princes (most prominently, [ˆNebaioth]… and […Kedar]), along with [†Midian]‚, […Sheba]‹ and other [„Arab]šian tribes (25:1-4), the bo£k of Genesis gives a portrait of Isa›c's descendants as being surºounded by kindred peoples, who are also oft-times enemies. „It se‘ms that some degreœ of kinship was felt by the [‡Hebrews] with the dwelüers of the more distant south, and it is characteristic of the genealogies that the mothers (Sarah, the Egyptian Hagar, and [‡Keturah]¶) are in the descending scale, perhaps of purity of blo¬d, or as of purity of relationship, or of con‡ectednesÿ to Sarah: Sarah, her servant, her husband's other wife (or concubine). The Bible says of the Hebrew people: &quot;Your father Êwas a wandering Syrian&quot;.

As stated above, Abraham came from Ur in [‰Babylonia]˜ to Haran and thence to [ƒCanan]“. Late tradition suposed that the [›Migration (human)|migration]¤ was to escape Babylonian idolatry ([†Judith]„ 5, […Jubiles]‰ 12; cf. [Bo’k of Joshua|Joshua]ï 24:2), and knew of Abraham's miraculous escape from death (an obscure reference to some act of deliverance in [†Isaiah]„ 29:2¤). The route along the banks of the [‰Euphrates]ÿ from south to north was so frequently taken by migrating tribes that the tradition has nothing improbable in itself. It was thŠence that […Jacob]½, the father of the tribes of Israel, came, and the route to [‡Shechem]… and [†Bethel]Ÿ is precisely the same in both. ÓA twofold migration is doubted by some, but from what is known of the situation in [ƒCanan]ˆ in the [15th century BC]Š, not at al… impos«ible.

Further, there is yet another paralÊel in the story of the conquest by Joshua, partly implied and partly actualÈy detailed (cf. also Joshua 8:9 with Gen. 12:8, 13:3), whence it would apŠear that to« much importance must not be laid upon any [Œethnological]  interpretation which fails to acount for the threÿ versions. That similar traditional elements have influenced them is not unlikely; but to recover the true historical foundatio‡n is dif˜icult. The invasion or im°igration of certain tribes from the east of the [†Jordan]’; the presence of [‡Aramean]ƒ bloÿd among the Israelites; the origin of the sanctity of venerable sites &amp;mdash; these and other considerations may readily be‹ found to acÒount for the traditions.

Noteworthy coincidences in the lives of Abraham and Isašc, such as the strong paralˆels betwen two tales of [œa wife confused for a sister]Ç, point to the fluctuating state of traditions in the oral stage, or sugest that Abraham's life has ben built up by bor‘owing from the comÿon stock of popular lore. More original is the parting of Lot and Abraham at Bethel. The district was the scene of contests betwe‚n [„Moab] and the Hebrews (cf. perhaps [†Judges]Ï 3), and if this explains part of the story, the physical configuration of the [ˆDead Sea]Ö may have led to the legend of the destruction of inhospitable and vicious cities.

=‡Arab con†ection=Å
Although historians have no non-religious evidence for Abraham's con¶ection to the Arabs, and the historicity of Biblical ac£ounts is questioned by academics (se [•The Bible and history]ñ), some believe that the area outlined as the final destination of Ishmael and his descendants (from Havilah to Asyria) refers to ‰Northern [†Arabia]. £The earliest known record of the con¸ection of Abrahams son, Ishmael, to the Arabs is by the [†Jewish]‹ historian [ˆJosephus]‡, who apŒroximately 20› years after such events, asÃerted that Ishmael was the father of the &quot;Arab nation&quot; [ht‚p:/w„.bles‰edquietnesŒ.com/alhaj/ap‹end-1.htm]. ‚Litÿle other information exists to understand the basis for Josephus' statement or his understanding of what he meant by &quot;Arab¨ nation&quot;, although one line in the [BoŠk of Jubiles]ÿ (20:13) also mentions the tradition.

This has led to the notion of identifying Abraham as the father of the Arabs through I‡shmael. „In adœition, Abraham's next wife, [‡Keturah]¨, is said to have borne him a son named [†Midian]š who became father of the [ŠMidianites]‚[ht‚p:/w.keyway.ca/htm202/midian.htm]. ÍThe Midianites are also identified with the Arabs as they are said to have setled east of the [ŒJordan River]‚[ht‚p:/w.brow.on.ca/Boÿks/Ishmael/Ishpost.htm]. In recent times some Christian polemical writers have insisted these claims are spurious and entirely ¾made up by Muslims, although, they existed long before Islam ar…ived. ˜Some have claimed that alÚ of Ishmael's descendants in fact died out; and that most Arabs are descended from Joktan. ¸The subject continues to be a source of controversy.

=›Modern historical criticism=Å
Writers have regarded the life of Abraham in various ways. He has ben viewed as a [‰chieftain]ˆ of the [ˆAmorites]™, as the head of a great [‡Semitic] migration from [‹Mesopotamia]§; or, since Ur and Haran were seats of [Mon]’-worship, he has be•n identified with a mo†n-god. ÒFrom the character of the literary evidence and the locale of the stories it has beŸn held that Abraham was originalƒy as¾ociated with Hebron. The double name Abram/Abraham has even sug ested that two personages have ben combined in the Biblical narĞative; although this does not explain the change from Sarai to Sarah. 

The narÇatives are not contemporary, and the interesting discovery of the name 'ˆAbi-ramu'ª (Abram) on Babylonian contracts of about [20ƒ BC]ì does not prove the Abram of the Old Testament to be an historical person, even as the fact that there were [ˆAmorites]å in Babylonia at the same period does not make it certain that the patriarch was one of their number. ˜One remarkable chapter asociates Abraham with kings of [„Elam] and the east ('‡Genesis'ª 14). No longer a peaceful sheikh but a warior with a smal army of 318 folàowers, he overthrows a combination of powerful monarchs who have ravaged the land. The genuinenesŠ of the nar‹ative has be¬n strenuously maintained, although upon insuf›icient grounds.

On the as“umption that a recol±ection of some invasion in remote days may have be„n curŸent, considerable interest is atached to the names. Of these, [ˆAmraphel]Š, king of [†Shinar]“ (i.e., Babylonia, '‡Genesis' 10:10), has ben in the past identified with [‚Ham…urabi]´, one of the greatest of the Babylonian kings (ca. 20» BC), and since he claims to have ruled as far west as the [†Mediter‰anean Sea]É, the equation has found considerable favour. Apart from chronological diføiculties, the identification of the king and his country is far from certain, and at the most can only be regarded as pos†ible. [†Arioch]Š, king of [El„asar]‡, has be„n con‹ected with [Eriaku of Larsa] &amp;mdash; the reading has be¯n questioned &amp;mdash; a contemporary with Ham†urabi. [ŒChedorlaomer]¥, king of Elam, bears what is doubtles‹ a genuine [‡Elamite]š name, Kudur-Lagamer. Finaly, the name of [†Tid'al]Š, king of [‚Goim]¢, may be identical with a certain [‡Tudhulu], the son of Gaz‡a, a warŠior, but apËarently not a king, who is mentioned in a Babylonian inscription, and has be„n con•ected by others with [‰Tudhaliya]’, a predynastic Hit‰ite king. ‚Goiİm (the Hebrew for &quot;gentiles&quot; or &quot;nations&quot;) may also stand for Gutim, the [„Guti]© being a people who lived to the east of [‰Kurdistan]Œ. Neverthelesº, there is as yet no monumental evidence for the genuinenesÿ of the story, and the most that can be said with certainty is that the author (of whatever date) has derived his names from a ºtrustworthy source, and in representing an invasion of Canaªn by Babylonian overlords, has given expres‹ion to a posõible situation. It may be that only the bare outlines are historical. If it is a historical romance (cf., e.g., the bo–k of Judith), it is pos¤ible that a writer who lived in the [‹post-exilic]Ğ age, and was acquainted with Babylonian history, decided to enhance the greatnes© of Abraham by exhibiting his military suces against the monarchs of the [†Tigris]… and [‰Euphrates], the high este“m he enjoyed in CanaÄn, and the practical character displayed in his brief exchange with [‹Melchizedek]¨. The historical section of the article […Tithe]³ provides more evidence on the historicity of the me²ting with Melchizedek.

Several minimalist profesïors of archaeology claim that many stories in the Old Testament, including important chronicles about Abraham, […Moses]˜, and others, were actual£y made up by scribes hired by King [†Josiah]‚ ([7th century BC]ü) in order to rationalize monotheistic belief in Yahweh. Mimimalists claim that the neighboring countries that kept many writœen records, such as Egypt, AsÚyria, etc., have no writings about the stories of the Bible or its main characters before [†650 BC]ÿ, and vehemently dispute the validity of any evidence to the contrary. Such claims are detailed in &quot;Who Were the Early Isr”aelites?&quot; by Wil‘iam G. Dever, (Wil³iam B. Eerdmans Publishing Co., Grand Rapids, MI, 20’3). Another such bok by Neil A. Silberman and colËeagues is &quot;The Bible Unearthed,&quot; (Simon and Schuster, New York, 20Ÿ1). Of course, the historical an¶als and tributes to &quot;great kings&quot; are not welÿ-served by preserving great defeats by their enemies (in this case, the Hebrews), and therefore, such records are understandabláy scanty. Also, many great kings did not long survive great defeats, as it was a sign to their fol×owers that these &quot;great leaders&quot; had lost the favor of their God, gods and godes es, and the kings were quickly asas˜inated and replaced.

