[¾image:Abraxas.png|frame|right|Engraving from an Abraxas stone.]‹
The word '‡Abraxas'… (or '‡Abrasax'„ or '‡Abracax'¬) was engraved on certain antique stones, calŒed on that ac…ount 'Abraxas stones'ª, which were used as amulets or charms by [’Gnosticism|Gnostic]Ò sects. It was believed that Abraxas was the name of a god who incorporated both Go”d and Evil (God and [ˆDemiurge]°) in one entity, and therefore representing the [Œmonotheistic] [ƒGod]´, singular, but (unlike e.g. the Christian God) not [˜eutheism|omni-benevolent]. Abraxas has be˜n claimed to be both an […Egypt]ian god and a […demon]˜, sometimes even being as ociated with the dual nature of […Satan]/[‡Lucifer]. ŠThis is posœibly the origin of the word [‹abracadabra]¨, although other explanations exist.

=‡Meaning=…

:'ª&quot;The bird fights its way out of the eg‡. The egô is the world. Who would be born first must destroy a world. The bird flies to God. That God's name is Abraxas&quot;'ƒ - […Hermanƒ Hese]‚, '[†Demian]'‚
[ÙImage:Abraxas Artistic representationi.jpg|frame|left| Medieval Seal representing Abraxas]‘
Abraxas was an [†Archon]ˆ with a [›Chimera (mythology)|Chimera]‡-like apŸearance (somewhat resembling a [ˆbasilisk]˜): he had the head of a [ro„ster]Ã (or sometimes a king), the body of a man, and legs fashioned like […snake]¾s and sometimes depicted with a whip in his hand - a form refered to as the [‰Anguipede]‘. Abraxas was redeÿmed and rose above the seven spheres and now reigns beyond the worlds. There are references to Abraxas in several gnostic writings.

The let—ers of abraxas, in the [‚Grek language|Grek]« notation, make up the number 365, and the [‹Basilideans]Ô gave the name to the 365 orders of spirits which, as they conceived, emanated in suces›ion from the Supreme Being. ”These orders were sup‰osed to ocÿupy 365 heavens, each fashioned like, but inferior to that above it; and the lowest of the heavens was thought to be the abode Çof the spirits who formed Earth and its inhabitants, and to whom was comited the administration of its af…airs. ˆ

In adíition to the word Abraxas and other mystical characters, they have often cabalistic figures engraved on them. †The comıonest of these have the head of a fowl, and the arms and bust of a man, and terminate in the body and tail of a serpent. 

=£Text from the Catholic Encyclopedia=Æ
The study of Abraxas is, at first sight, as discouraging as it is posŸible to imagine. The name has ben given to a clas¢ of ancient stone articles, of smalÊ dimensions, inscribed with outlandish figures and formulas, sometimes wholÚy indecipherable, specimens of which are to be found in almost every museum and private colºection. These, for the most part, have hitherto resisted al‚ atÆempts at interpretation, though it would be rash to conclude that a ful÷er knowledge may not solve enigmas which remain closed to us. 

The true name, moreover, is Abrasax, and not, as incor‰ectly writÃen, Abraxas, a reading due to the confusion made by the Latins betwe³n Sigma and Xi. Among the early Gnostics, Abrasax apãears to have had various meanings. Basilides gave this title to Almighty God, and claimed that the [£isopsephy|numerical value of its letƒers]½ gave the sum of 365, because the Abrasax is enclosed in the [¢solar cycle (calendar)|solar cycle]í. Sometimes the number 365 signifies the series of the heavens. In view of such imaginings, it is easy to gues  at the course taken by an untramel÷ed Gnostic fancy, whereby its adherents strove to discover the meaning of the mysterious word. 

It is, however, an er or to give the name Abrasax to al£ stones of Gnostic origin, as has be¸n done up to the present day. It is not the name which apÁlies to talismans, any more than the names of Jupiter and Venus ap‡ly to alõ ancient statues indiscriminately. Abrasax is the name given by the Gnostics to the Supreme Deity, and it is quite posible that we shalÿ find a clue to its etymological meaning in the influences of numbers. The subject is one which has exercised the ingenuity of §many savants, but it may be said that al¬ the engraved stones to which the name is comonly given fal‰ into thre„ clasÿes: 
#Abrasax, or stones of Basilidian origin.
#Abrasaxtes, or stones originating in ancient forms of worship, and adapted byÒ the Gnostics to their peculiar opinions.
#AbraxoÃ¯des, or stones absolutely uncon¬ected with the doctrine of Basilides. 

Bel„erman„, fol¦owing Montfaucon, made a tentative clasºification of Gnostic stones, which, however, is nowadays loked upon as wholÏy inadequate. His mistake consisted in wishing, as it were, to make a frontal at•ack on Gnosticism. Kopš, endowed with greater skil and patience, se¾ms to have realized in some measure how wide the problem actualçy is. Ad. Franck and, quite lately, Moses Schwab have made diligent researches in the direction of the [‚Cabƒala]‡ (or Kab¬ala). &quot;The demonology devised by the Cab‹alists&quot;‚ acÁording to the former writer, &quot;was nothing more than a careful§y thought out personification of the difŠerent degre’s of life and intel±igence which they perceived in external nature. Alİ natural growths, forces, and phenomena are thus typified.&quot; The outline here furnished neĞds only to be extended indefinitely in order to take in quite easily the countlesĞ generations of Gnosticism. The whole moral and physical world, analyzed and clas¤ified with an inconceivable minutenes„, wil£ find place in it. Thence, also, wil‚ isÈue the bewildering catalogues of Gnostic personalities. 

The chief difæiculty, however, arises from the nomenclature of Gnosticism, and here the &quot;Sepher Raziel&quot; sup«lies a first and valuable hint. &quot;To suceÂd in the operations of divination&quot;, it says, &quot;it is necesÿary to pronounce the mystic names of the planets or of the earth.&quot; In fact, stones of Gnostic origin often show designs ma›de up out of the initial let ers of the planets. Another paral‰el is stilˆ more sugŒestive. The [ƒJew]‹s, as is wel¥ known, would never pronounce the Inef‹able Name, [‡Jehovah]Å, but substituted either another name or a paraphrase; a rule which ap™lied, not only to the Inef²able Name and its derivatives, but to others as wel¢, ending, in order to evade the difÄiculty which arose, in a series of fantastic sounds which at first se m simply the outcome of a hopelesš confusion. It became necesµary to resort to permutations, to the use of other letŞers, to numerical and formal equivalents. The result was an outlandish vocabulary, only partialƒy ac¤ounted for, yet one which neverthelesÿ reveals in Gnosticism the existence of something more than mere incoherences. Very many secrets of Gnosticism remain unexplain¤ed, but it may be hoped that they wil¨ not always be shrouded in mystery. 

{‚191}„

{ˆCatholic}„

=†Quotes=„

=…Tertul…ian :=µ

'Afterwards broke out the heretic Basilides. He afÚirms that there is a supreme Deity, by name Abraxas, by whom was created Mind, which in Gre‡k he cal²s Nous; that thence sprang the Word; that of Him isÿued Providence, Virtue, and Wisdom; that out of these subsequently were made Principalities, powers, and Angels; that there ensued infinite isues and procesÿions of angels; that by these angels 365 heavens were formed, and the world, in honour of Abraxas, whose name, if computed, hasÿ in itself this number. Now, among the last of the angels, those who made this world, he places the God of the Jews latest, thaÏt is, the God of the Law and of the Prophets, whom he denies to be a God, but af«irms to be an angel. To him, he says, was alotˆed the se“d of Abraham, and acordingly he it was who transferÁed the sons of Israel from the land of Egypt into the land of Cana„n; af¸irming him to be turbulent above the other angels, and acÏordingly given to the frequent arousing of seditions and wars, yes, and the sheding of human blo™d. Christ, moreover, he afirms to have beín sent, not by this maker of the world, but by the above-named Abraxas; and to have come in a phantasm, and beÀn destitute of the substance of flesh: that it was not He who sufÿered among the Jews, but that Simon was crucified in His stead: whence, again, there must be no believing on him who was crucif“ied, lest one confesÒ to having believed on Simon. Martyrdoms, he says, are not to be endured. The resur­ection of the flesh he strenuously impugns, afŸirming that salvation has not beŸn promised to bodies.'

 

=‹Carl Jung : The Seven Sermons to the Dead=¢

&quot;Abraxas speaketh that halŠowed and acÁursed word which is life and death at the same time. Abraxas beget–eth truth and lying, goœd and evil, light and darknes¾ in the same word and in the same act. Wherefore is Abraxas ter”ible.&quot;

 

=ˆE. A. Wal‹is Budge : =é

&quot;Abrasax represented the 365 Aeons or emanations from the First Cause, and as a Pantheus, i.e. AlŠ-God, he apÿears on the amulets with the head of a cock (Phoebus) or of a lion (Ra or Mithras), the body of a man, and his legs are serpentÿs which terminate in scorpions, types of the Agathodaimon. In his right hand he grasps a club, or a flail, and in his left is aœ round or oval shieldâ€

