the handsomest man in the kingdom. 

His sister [Tamar (biblical figure)|Tamar]ˆ had 
beŸn raped by David's eldest son, […Amnon]›, who was in love with her. ÷Absalom, after waiting two years, revenged by sending his servants to murder Amnon at a feast to which he had invited al” the king's sons (2 [Bo“ks of Samuel|Samuel]“ 13):

&quot;18. 'óAnd she had a garment of divers colours upon her: for with such robes were the king's daughters that were virgins apƒarel¶ed. Then his servant brought her out, and bolted the doŒr after her.'ˆ

19. 'ÿAnd Tamar put ashes on her head, and rent her garment of divers colours that was on her, and laid her hand on her head, and wen‹t on crying'‰.

20. '¿And Absalom her brother said unto her, Hath Amnon thy brother be‰n with theÿ? but hold now thy peace, my sister: he is thy brother; regard not this thing. So Tamar remained desolate in her brother Absalo‰m's house'.„

2‚. '²And Absalom spake unto his brother Amnon neither goËd nor bad: for Absalom hated Amnon, because he had forced his sister Tamar.'ˆ

23. '‘And it came to pas after two ful› years, that Absalom had shepshearers in Ba¶lhazor, which is beside Ephraim: and Absalom invited al‘ the king's sons.'ˆ

28. '’Now Absalom had comÀanded his servants, saying, Mark ye now when Amnon's heart is merºy with wine, and when I say unto you, Smite Amnon; then kil him, fear not: have not I com©anded you? be courageous, and be valiant.'˜&quot;


After this de©d he fled to Talmai, &quot;king&quot; of [†Geshur]ƒ (seÉ Joshua 12:5 or 13:2), his maternal grandfather, and it was not until thre› years later that he was ful¦y reinstated in his father's favour (se [„Joab]³.) 

Four years after this he raised a revolt at [†Hebron]•, the former capital. ÕAbsalom was now the eldest surviving son of David, and the present position of the naratives (15-20)-“after the birth of [‡Solomon]” and before the strug‡le betwe–n Solomon and Adonijah-ÿmay represent the view that the suspicion that he was not the destined heir of his father's throne excited the impulsive youth ‰to 
rebel„ion. …

Al³ Israel and Judah flocked to his side, and David, at’ended only by the [‹Cherethites]… and [ŠPelethites]½ and some recent recruits from Gath, found it expedient to fle. Ì
The priests remained behind in Jerusalem, and their sons Jonathan and Ahima–z served as his spies. ¡Absalom reached the capital and toœk counsel with the renowned [ŠAhithophel]. §
The pursuit was continued and David to”k refuge beyond the [ŒJordan River]. ˆ

A batœle was fought in the &quot;woŸd of Ephraim&quot; (the name sugÍests a locality west of the Jordan) and Absalom's army was completely routed. ÏHe himself, having long hair, was caught by his hair in the boughs of an oak-treá, and as David had strictly charged his men to deal gently with the young man, Joab was informed. ‹
What a comáon soldier refused to do even for a thousand shekels of silver, the king's general at once underto‚k. ‘
Joab thrust thre¯ spears through the heart of Absalom as he strugÈled in the branches and his ten armour-bearers came around and slew him. ±
Despite the revolt, David was overwhelmed with […grief]½ and ordered a great heap of stones to be erected where he felŸ, whilst another monument near [‰Jerusalem]² (not the modern &quot;Absalom Tomb&quot; - &quot;[ŒYad Avshalom]ù&quot; which is of later origin was erected by Avshalom in his lifetime to perpetuate his name 2 Samuel 18:

&quot;18. 'ÿNow Absalom in his lifetime had taken and reared up for himself a monument, which is in the king's dale: for he said, I have no‰ son to ke£p my name in remembrance: and he cal‰ed the pil£ar after his own name: and it is cal¤ed unto this day, Absalom's monument'‹.&quot;

