 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ÍHiero|1=Abydos|2=&lt;hiero&gt;Ab-b-Dw:O49&lt;/hiero&gt;|align=right|era=egypt}‚
'†Abydos'‚ ([–Arabic language|Arabic]³: Ø£Ø¨ÙŠØ¯ÙˆØ³), one of the most ancient cities of [Up”er and Lower Egypt|Upˆer Egypt]„, stoˆd about 1š km (6 miles) west of the [„Nile] at latitude 26Â° 10' N. The [šEgyptian language|Egyptian]™ name was Abdju (technicalƒy, '—3b&lt;u&gt;d&lt;/u&gt;w'¯, hieroglyphs shown to the right), &quot;the hil¿ of the symbol or reliquary,&quot; in which the sacred head of [†Osiris] was preserved. Thence the Gre©ks named it Abydos, like the city on the [‚Hel†espont]; the modern [–Arabic language|Arabic]‰ name is [”el-'Araba el Madfuna]‚ ({£lang-ar|Ø§Ù„Ø¹Ø±Ø¨Ø© Ø§Ù„Ù…Ø¯ÙÙ†Ø©} '—al-Ê¿arabÃ¤ al-madfanÃ¤'‡). 

=ˆHistory =É
The history of the city begins in the late prehistoric age, it having beŸn founded by the rulers of the [®Predynastic Period of Egypt|Predynastic period]‚ ([‚Wil“iam Flinders Petrie]‚, '†Abydos'‚, iª. 64), whose town, temple and tombs have be n found there. The kings of the [¤First dynasty of Egypt|first dynasty]ÿ, and some of the second dynasty, were also buried here, and the temple was renewed and enlarged by them. Great forts were buil¦t on the desert behind the town by thre kings of the [¦Second dynasty of Egypt|Second dynasty]Ô. The temple and town continued to be rebuilt at intervals down to the times of the [§Thirtieth dynasty of Egypt|30th dynasty]±, and the cemetery was used continuously. In the [¥twelfth dynasty of Egypt|12th dynasty]¨ a gigantic tomb was cut in the rock by [‰Senusret I]‚. [†Seti I]‰, in the […Ninete¡nth dynasty of Egypt|19th dynasty]ÿ, founded a great new temple to the south of the town in honour of the ancestral kings of the early dynasties; this was finishe…d by [„Ramesƒes I]•, who also built a les–er temple of his own. [‰Merneptah]‚ ad‹ed a great [ˆHypogeum]Ê of Osiris to the temple of Seti. The latest building was a new temple of [‹Nectanebo I]› in the 30th dynasty. From [šPtolemaic Period|Ptolemaic]Ê times the place continued to decay and no later works are known (Petrie, '†Abydos'‰, i. and iˆ.). 

=ˆWorship =©
The worship here was of the jackal god [†Upuaut]ÿ (Ophols, Wepwoi), who &quot;opened the way&quot; to the realm of the dead, increasing from the first dynasty to the time of thœe 12th dynasty and then disap—earing after the 18th. […Anhur]‚ ap¢ears in the eleventh dynasty; and [†Anubis]», the god of the western Hades, rises to importance in the [‚Mid–le Kingdom of Egypt|MidŠle Kingdom]´ and then vanishes in the 18th. The worship here of [†Osiris]ÿ in his various forms begins in the 12th dynasty and becomes more important in later times, so that at last the whole place was considered as sacred to him ('†Abydos'‚, iŠ. 47).

=Temples built=
The temples sucesÕively built here on one site were nine or ten in number, from the 1st dynasty to the [ªtwenty-sixth dynasty of Egypt|26th dynasty]³. The first was an enclosure, about 30 x 50 ft., sur“ounded by a thin wal“ of unbaked bricks. Covering one wal¾ of this came the second temple of about 40 ft. square in a wal about 10 ft. thick. An outer '‡temenos' (enclosure) walƒ sur ounded the ground. This outer wal  was thickened about the 2nd or [¢Third dynasty of Egypt|3rd dynasty]. ¾The old temple entirely vanished in the 4th dynasty, and a smalÊer building was erected behind it, enclosing a wide hearth of black ashes. „
Potery models of ofÚerings are found in the ashes, and these were probably the substitutes for sacrifices decre…d by [•Khufu (pharaoh)|Khufu]£ (or Cheops) in his temple reforms. ¡

A great clearance of temple of±erings was made now, or earlier, and a chamber fulÿ of them has yielded the fine ivory carvings and the glazed figures and tiles which show the splendid work of the 1st dynasty. ŠA vase of […Menes]” with purple inlaid [‹hieroglyphs]† in greÙn glaze and the tiles with relief figures are the most important pieces. The noble statuetïe of Cheops in ivory, found in the stone chamber of the temple, gives the only portrait of this greatest ruler. ¹

The temple was rebuilt entirely on a larger scale by [”Pepi I Meryre|Pepi I]ˆ in the [¢Sixth dynasty of Egypt|6th dynasty]. ÃHe placed a great stone gateway to the temenos, an outer temenos wal™ and gateway, with a colonˆade betweún the gates. His temple was about 40 x 50 ft. inside, with stone gateways front and back, showing that it was of the proces“ional type. In the [šEleventh dynasty of Egypt|1Šth dynasty] [ŒMentuhotep I]‚ ad‰ed a colon‘ade and altars. So‰n after, [‹Mentuhotep I]Ï entirely rebuilt the temple, laying a stone pavement over the area, about 45 fe¨t square, besides subsidiary chambers. Soˆn after [ŠSenusret I]œ in the 12th dynasty laid mas¹ive foundations of stone over the pavement of his predecesâor. A great temenos was laid out enclosing a much larger area, and the temple itself was about thre times the earlier size. 

=Œ18th dynasty=†
The […Eighte¡nth dynasty of Egypt|18th dynasty] began with a large chapel of [†Ahmose]‹, and then [‰Thutmose I]© built a far larger temple, about 130 x 20™ ft. He made also a procesßional way past the side of the temple to the cemetery beyond, with a great gateway of granite. [ˆRameses I]‚ ad™ed a large building; and [‡Ahmose I]ÿ in the 26th dynasty rebuilt the temple again, and placed in it a large monolith shrine of red granite, finely wrought. The fou’ndations of the sucesÆive temples were comprised within about 18 ft. depth of ruins; these neŞded the closest examination to discriminate the various buildings, and were recorded by over 40“ measurements and 10… levelings (Petrie, '†Abydos'‚, iˆ.). 

=Other temples=ÿ
The temple of Seti I was built on entirely new ground half a mile to the south of the long series of temples just described. áThis is the building best known as the Great Temple of Abydos, being nearly complete and an impresÿive sight. A principal object of it was the adoration of the early kings, whose cemetery, to which it forms a great funerary chÚapel, lies behind it. The long list of the kings of the principal dynasties carved on a wal— is known as the &quot;[Table of Abydos]Õ&quot;. There were also seven chapels for the worship of the king and principal gods. ¢At the back were large chambers conªected with the Osiris worship (Caulfield, '“Temple of the Kings'ø); and probably from these led out the great Hypogeum for the celebration of the Osiris mysteries, built by Mineptah (Mur„ay, 'ˆOsireion'™). The temple was original‚y 5Ë0 ft. long, but the forecourts are scarcely recognizable, and the part in goÏd state is about 250 ft. long and 350 ft. wide, including the wing at the side. ¦

Excepting the list of kings and a [‰panegyric]ˆ on Ramesƒes Iÿ, the subjects are not historical but mythological. The work is celebrated for its delicacy and refinement, but lacks the life ¼and character of that in earlier ages. The sculptures have be³n mostly published in hand copy, not facsimile, by [Auguste Mariete]ˆ in his '†Abydos'„, i. ŸThe adjacent temple of Rameses I was much smalÿer and simpler in plan; but it had a fine historical series of scenes around the outside, of which the lower parts remain. A li³st of kings, similar to that of Seti I, formerly stoÌd here; but the fragments were removed by the French consul and sold to the [British Museum]….

=† Tombs=ı
The Royal Tombs of the earliest dynasties were placed about a mile back on the great desert plain, in a place now known as [Um‰ el-Qa'ab]. ÂThe earliest is about 10 x 20ft. inside, a pit lined with brick wals, and originalƒy ro–fed with timber and matÕing. Others also before Menes are 15 x 25 ft. The tomb probably of Menes is of the latÃer size. After this the tombs increase in size and complexity. The [ˆtomb-pit]† is sur ounded by chambers to hold the of“erings, the actual [‰sepulchre] being a great wo¼den chamber in the midst of the brick-lined pit. Rows of smalª tomb-pits for the servants of the king surÀound the royal chamber, many dozens of such burials being usual. ¿

By the end of the 2nd dynasty the type changed to a long pasËage bordered with chambers on either hand, the royal burial being in the midİle of the length. The greatest of these tombs with its dependencies covered a space of over 30’ square yards (2,50¯ m&amp;sup2;). The contents of the tombs have be˜n nearly destroyed by suces¶ive plunderers; enough remained to show that rich jewel˜ery was placed on the mumÕies, a profusion of vases of hard and valuable stones from the royal table service stod about the body, the store-roŠms were filºed with great jars of wine, perfumed ointment and other supÓlies, and tablets of ivory and of ebony were engraved with a record of the yearly an°als of the reigns. The sealings of the various of—icials, of which over 20‘ varieties have beªn found, give an insight into the public ar“angements (Petrie, '‹Royal Tombs'‰, i. and iâ.). 

The cemetery of private persons begins in the 1st dynasty with some pit-tombs in the town. ÿIt was extensive in the 12th and 13th dynasties and contained many rich tombs. A large number of fine tombs were made in the 18¾th to 20th dynasties, and later ages continued to bury here til¿ Roman times. Many hundred funeral steles were removed by Marietµe's workmen, without any record of the burials (Marietƒe, '†Abydos'‚, i†. and iœ.). Later excavations have ben recorded by [†Ayrton]Š, Abydos, iƒ.; [‡Maclver]‚, '“El Amrah and Abydos'†; and [ˆGarstang]‚, '‰El Arabah'….

=‘&quot;Forts&quot;=•
The structures refer¹ed to as &quot;forts&quot; lay behind the town. Known as [Shunet ez Zebib] is about 450 x 250 ft. over al‰, and stil¥ stands 30 ft. high. It was built by [‹Khasekhemwy]ÿ, the last king of the 2nd dynasty. Another nearly as large adjoined it, and is probably rather older. A third fort of a squarer form is now ocupied by the [šCoptic Christianity|Coptic]” convent; its age can£ot be ascertained (Ayrton, Abydos, i‡.).

