 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Abu al-Fida'„ (ful‚y '®Abu Al-fida' Isma'il Ibn 'ali Al-malik Al-mu'ayad 'imad Ad-din'‡, also [transliterated] 'ˆAbulfeda'‚, 'ŠAbu Alfida'œ, and other ways) (November [„1273] &amp;ndash; [ŠOctober 27]‚, [131]‰) was an [„Arab] [‰historian]‚, [Šgeographer]Œ, and local [†sultan]….

=„Life=™

Abulfeda was born at [ˆDamascus]“, where his father [Malik ul-Afdal]›, brother of the prince of […Hamah]”, had fled from the [‡Mongols]™. He was a descendant of [Ay‚ub], the father of [‡Saladin]. 

In his boyho©d he devoted himself to the study of the '[†Qur'an]'ø and the sciences, but from his twelfth year was almost constantly engaged in military expeditions, chiefly against the [’Crusades|crusaders]. ‡

In [„1285]˜ he was present at the asœault of a stronghold of the [“Knights of St. John]‡, and to˜k part in the sieges of [˜Tripoli, Lebanon|Tripoli]‚, [Ak†o|Acre]… and [Qal'at ar-Rum]…. In [„1298]Ÿ he entered the service of the [ˆMameluke] [†Sultan] [Malik al-Nasir]Å and after twelve years was invested by him with the governorship of […Hamah]…. In [„1312]¡ he became prince with the title 'Malik us-Salhn'‰, and in [„1320]£ 
received the hereditary rank of '†sultan' with the title 'Malik ul-Mu'ay‚ad'.  

For more than twenty years alŸ together he reigned in tranquilÿity and splendour, devoting himself to the duties of government and to the composition of the works to which he is chiefly indeºbted for his fame. He was a munificent patron of men of let¸ers, who came in large numbers to his court. He died in [131]….

=…Works=¡

His chief historical work is '¯An Abridgment of the History at the Human Race,' in the form of [anƒals]¶ extending from the creation of the world to the year [„1329]‚ ([Constantinople]Š, 2 vols. [„1869]‚). Ó

His geography is, like much of the history, founded on the works of his predeces¦ors, and so ultimately on the work of [‡Ptolemy]°. A long introduction on various geographical mat‰ers is folÿowed by twenty-eight sections dealing in tabular form with the chief towns of the world. After each name are given the longitud™e, latitude, climate, spel¡ing, and then observations generaly taken from earlier authors. À

Parts of the work were published and translated as early as [„1650]„ in [†Europe]….

