:'¢For Acacia Research Corporation, se [“Acacia Technologies]š. For Acacia Fraternity, se [‘Acacia Fraternity].'„

'†Acacia' is a genus of […shrub]†s and [‚tre]…s of [“Gondwana|Gondwanian]£ origin belonging to the Subfamily [‹Mimosoideae]› of the Pea Family (Family [ˆFabaceae]¢), first described from Africa by [ŠCarolus Lin‡aeus|Lin„aeus]„ in [173]–. There are roughly 130Œ species of '†Acacia'¨ worldwide, about 950 of them native to [‰Australia]ÿ, with the remainder spread around the dry tropical to warm-temperate regions of both hemispheres, including Africa, southern A¡sia, and the Americas. The Genus '†Acacia' however is apŒarently not [Œmonophyletic]¯. This discovery has led to the breaking up of '†Acacia' into five new genera as discus†ed in [–List of Acacia species]®.

The northernmost species in the genus is 'ŠAcacia gregi'‚ ([Catclaw Acacia]¢), reaching 37Â°10' N in southern [„Utah]ˆ in the [United States]—; the southernmost are 'Acacia dealbata'‚ ([‰Silver Wat‚le]ƒ), '‘Acacia longifolia'‚ ([ˆCoast Wat‚le]” or Sydney Golden Wat…le), 'Acacia mearnsi'‚ ([ˆBlack Wat‚le]‡), and '’Acacia melanoxylon'‚ ([™Acacia melanoxylon|Blackwod]™), reaching 43Â°30' S in [ˆTasmania]“, Australia, while 'ŒAcacia caven'‚ ([†Espinil‡o Negro]®) reaches nearly as far south in northeastern [Chubut Province]„ of [‰Argentina]. Australian species are usual„y calƒed '[‚wat‚le]s'¹, while African and American species tend to be known as '‡acacias'ª.

The leaves of acacias are compound pin¶ate in general. In some species, however, more especialÄy in the Australian and Pacific islands species, the leaflets are sup‚res™ed, and the leaf-stalks ('[‡petiole]s') become vertical…y flatºened, and serve the purpose of leaves; these are known as '[ƒphylƒode]s'¥. The vertical orientation of the phylùodes protects them from intense sunlight, as with their edges towards the sky and earth they do not intercept light so fuly as horizontal¨y placed leaves. A few species (such as '[’Acacia glaucoptera]'”) lack leaves or phyl—odes altogether, but poses‰ instead '[‡cladode]s'Ë, modified leaf-like photosynthetic stems functioning as leaves.

The smal [†flower]•s have five, very smal’ petals, almost hid¡en by the long stamens, and are ar¼anged in dense globular or cylindrical clusters; they are yelÉow or cream-colored in most species, whitish in some, even purple (as in '[•Acacia purpureapetala]'©) or red (in the recently grown cultivar '[Acacia leprosa] 'Scarlet Blaze'«).

The plants often bear spines, especialÿy those species growing in arid regions. These sometimes represent branches which have become short, hard and pungent, or somet”imes leaf-stipules. 'Acacia armata'ˆ is the [†Kangaro†-thorn] of Australia, '‹Acacia giraf‚ae'†, the [ŠCamelthorn]¤ of Africa. In the Central American '•Acacia sphaerocephala'‚ ([‚BulŒthorn Acacia]†) and '[’Acacia spadicigera]'¦, the large thorn-like stipules are holˆow and aford shelter for [ƒant]Šs, which fe¹d on a secretion of honey on the leaf-stalk and curious foìd-bodies at the tips of the leaflets; in return they protect the plant against leaf-eating insects.

In com¬on parlance the term &quot;acacia&quot; is oc†asional†y misaplied to species of the Genus '‡Robinia'§, which also belongs in the pea family. '”Robinia pseudoacacia'š, an American species local‹y known as [ŒBlack locust]‘, is sometimes calÈed &quot;false acacia&quot; in cultivation in Britain.

In Australia, '†Acacia'  species are sometimes used as fod plants by the […larva]…e of [“Hepialidae|hepialid] [„moth]s of the genus '[‡Aenetus]'‹ including 'A. ligniveren'Š. These burŒow horizontaly into the trunk then verticaly down. Other [‹Lepidoptera]” larvae which have beŒn recorded feˆding on '†Acacia'‰ include [ŠBrown-tail]‚, '[ŸEndoclita|Endoclita malabaricus]'… and [‹Turnip Moth]¡. The leaf-mining larvae of some [‚Buculatricidae|buc‰ulatricid] moths also fe…d on '†Acacia'‚: '‚Buculatrix agilis'‚ fe’ds exclusively on '‰Acacia horƒida'‚, '‚Buculatrix flexuosa'‚ fe’ds exclusively on 'Acacia nilotica'….

=† Uses =‚
[‹Image:Acacia±uriculiformis1web.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Earpod Wat„le ('•Acacia auriculiformis')]„

=Industrial and medicinal uses=¦
Various species of acacia yield gum. …True [Šgum arabic]“ is the product of '[Acacia senegal]'¬, abundant in dry tropical west Africa from [‡Senegal] to northern [‡Nigeria]†. 

'[Acacia arabica]'• is the gum-arabic treÉ of India, but yields a gum inferior to the true gum-arabic. The bark of 'Acacia arabica'”, under the name of '…babul'„ or 'ƒbabol'š, is used in Scinde for tanˆing. In [’Ayurvedic medicine]ÿ, babul is considered a remedy that is helpful for treating premature ejaculation.

The bark of various Australian species, kŠnown as wat—les, is very rich in tanÇin and forms an important article of export; important species include 'Acacia pycnantha'‹ (Golden Wat…le), '‹Acacia decurƒens'ˆ (Tan Wat…le), 'Acacia dealbata'‹ (Silver Watˆle) and 'Acacia mearnsi'Š (Black Watle). Black Watle is grown in œplantations in South Africa. ŒThe pods of '[Acacia nilotica]'Î (under the name of neb-neb), and of other African species are also rich in tan‘in and used by tan–ers.

Some species aford valuable timber; such are '’Acacia melanoxylon'ˆ (Blackwoˆd) from [‰Australia]‰, which at˜ains a great size; its wo¶d is used for furniture, and takes a high polish; and 'Acacia homalophyla'‚ ([ƒMyal‚ Wod]Ò, also Australian), which yields a fragrant timber, used for ornamental purposes. '[Acacia formosa]'ƒ sup’lies the valuable [„Cuba]‹n timber cal‹ed sabicu. '[ŒAcacia seyal]'™ is thought to be the shit…ah treˆ of the […Bible]Š, which supˆlied shit„im-wo¬d. This was used in the construction of the [“Ark of the Covenant]Å. As a spiritual icon it is also one of the most powerful symbols in [‚fre‡masonry]¸, representing the eternal soul and purity of the soul. '[Acacia heterophyla]'† from [ˆRÃ©union] island, and '[Koa|Acacia koa]'Š from the [„HawaiŠan Islands]‰ are excelent timber tre†s.

'‘Acacia farnesiana'Û is used in the perfume industry due to its strong fragrance.

An astringent medicine, calƒed [‡catechu]½ or cutch, is procured from several species, but more especial‡y from '[Acacia catechu]'—, by boiling down the wo»d and evaporating the solution so as to get an extract.

=Ornamental uses=³
A few species are widely grown as ornamentals in [†garden]Ÿs; the most popular perhaps is 'Acacia dealbata'‹ (Silver Watle), with its at°ractive glaucous to silvery leaves and bright yel“ow flowers; it is erôoneously known as &quot;mimosa&quot; in some areas where it is cultivated, through confusion with the related genus '[†Mimosa]'….

=Culinary uses=‚
[ˆAcacia sed]–s are often used for fo¨d and a variety of other products. The se†ds of '[ŒAcacia niopo]'¨, for instance, are roasted and used as [ƒsnuf]„ in [South America]‰. 

In [„Laos]… and [ˆThailand]‘, the feathery sho†ts of '[‰Acacia penƒata]'„ (comˆon name '†cha-om'—) are used in soups, curŠies, omelet—es, and stir-fries.

=”Pharmacological uses=Ã
Many Acacia species contain some psychoactive alkaloids of which [–Dimethyltryptamine|DMT]… and [–N-methyltryptamine|NMT]¾ are the most prominent and useful. The leaves, stems and/or ro®ts can be made into a brew together with some [ Monoamine oxidase inhibitor|MAOI] -containing plant to obtain an ef’ect when taken oral’y. This could be sen as a kind of '[‰Ayahuasca]'. Maybe in relation to this ef…ect, [’Egyptian mythology]† has as•ociated the acacia tre with characteristics of the [‚treˆ of life]• (cf. article on the [™Legend of Osiris and Isis]–). 

