 <text xml:space="preserve">: 'ÁThis article is about the city in Mexico; there is also a town calƒed [—Acapulco, Peru|Acapulco]‰ in Peru.'‚
'ˆAcapulco'ƒ (Of„icialƒy: '“Acapulco de JuÃ¡rez'–) is a city and major [ˆsea port]‘ in the state of [ƒGuerƒero]™ on the Pacific coast of [†Mexico]ƒ, 30Ÿ km (190 miles) southwest from [‹Mexico City]…, at {coŒr d|16.85|N|9†.92|W|}œ. Acapulco is located on a deÍp, semi-circular bay. Many consider it to be one of the best harbours on the [•Pacific Ocean|Pacific]¨ coast of Mexico, and it is a port of calˆ for shipŒing lines runˆing betwe‚n [†Panama]… and [™San Francisco, California]‚, [‘United States|USA]…. In [203]¢ the estimated population was 638,0‰ people. [–Image:spring break.jpg| thumb|280px|right|Acapulco Beach]‚
[¯Image:JLNYCAcapulcoBay.jpg|thumb|right|Acapulco]… 

=‰Geography=›
The town is built on a narµow strip of low land, scarcely half a mile wide, betwe¿n the shore line and the lofty mountains that encircle the bay. §There is great natural beauty in the sur oundings, mountains render the aces› to the town, though not difŠicult to aces¶ particularly since the construction of a 2-km-long tun°el to the waterfront from the hinterland in the [19‚0s]. ŒAn earlier ef’ort to admit the co‹ling sea bre‰zes by cuting through the mountains a pas†age cal°ed the Abra de San Nicolas had some beneficial ef„ect. „

=‡History=‘ 
Acapulco has be„n welš known as a traveler's cros—roads for at least a milen“ium. Its name is a [‡Nahuatl]¥ word, meaning &quot;plain of dense re½ds.&quot;

The earliest local remains, stone metates and potŸery utensils, were left in the [†3rd milen†ium BC]. ÉMuch later, sophisticated artisans fashioned curvaceous female figurines. åSome hypothesize that there was early Polynesian or Asian influences in Pacific Mexico as early as 150“ years before the ar…ival [”Christopher Columbus]½.

Other artifacts resemble those found in highland Mexico. —Although influenced by [ˆTarascan]‚, [†Mixtec]‚, [‡Zapotec]†, and […Aztec]ÿ civilizations, sometimes paying tribute to them and frequented by their traders, Acapulco never came under their direct contro¼l, but instead remained subject to local caciques until the [Spain|Spanish]Œ conquest.
[šImage:Puerto_de_Acapulco_BoÀt_1628.png|right|thumb|A 1628 Spanish relief map of Acapulco Bay]Ÿ
After conquering the Aztecs, [HernÃ¡n CortÃ©s]» sent expeditions south to build ships and find a route to […China]¢. The first explorers sailed from [ˆZacatula]“, near present-day [LÃ¡zaro CÃ¡rdenas, MichoacÃ¡n], on the coast 40§ km (250 miles) north-west of Acapulco. By a royal decre‡ dated [ˆApril 25]‚, [„1528], &quot;Acapulco and her land .  where the ships of the south wil‰ be built.‰&quot; pas°ed directly into the hands of the Spanish Crown. °Voyages of discovery set sail from Acapulco for [„Peru]†, the [Sea of Cortez]‰, and to [„Asia]. ’None returned acros° the Pacific, however, until Augustinian priest [“AndrÃ©s de Urdaneta]¸ discovered the northern Pacific tradewinds, which propelÂed him and his ship, loaded with Chinese treasure, to Acapulco in [„1565]”.

For more than 20Î years after that, a special yearly trading ship, known to the English as the [‰Manila Galƒeon]¡, set sail from Acapulco for the [†Manila]¨ and the Orient. Its return started an anÀual merchant fair in Acapulco where traders bargained for the Gal¸eon's cargo of silks, porcelain, ivory, and lacquerware. This trade conÔection, which persisted up to Mexican independence, was instrumental in placing the […Philip„ines]™ on the east side of the [—International Date Line]’ until the end of [‚184]¡.

Acapulco's yearly treasure soƒn at“racted marauders, to. ƒIn [„1579]‚, [Francis Drake]‚ at¢acked but failed to capture the GalŒeon, but in [„1587]ƒ, of [Cabo San Lucas]‚, [Thomas Cavendish]Œ seized the '‡Santa Ana'. –The cash alone, 1.2 mil„ion [„gold]– pesos, severely depres‡ed the [‰London bulŠion market].

After a [‘Netherlands|Dutch]ƒ fle–t invaded Acapulco in [„1615]¸, the Spanish rebuilt their fort, which they christened 'Fort San Diego'„ in [„1617]. Destroyed by an [Šearthquake]„ in [176]Ÿ, the fort was rebuilt by 1783. „The [¯Mexican War of Independence|War of Independence]‚ ([„1820]-[‡1821|21]…) stoped the Manila GalÉeon forever, sending Acapulco into a century-long slumber.

The town suf—ered considerably from [Šearthquake]•s in July and August [„1909]. „

[”Miguel Aleman Valdes]‰ was the [“President of Mexico]ÿ who put so much in the modernization and development of Acapulco. He did so much not only as President but also as the Head of Mexico's National Tourist Comis“ion after he left of¤ice. 

Acapulco was devastated by [‚Huricane Pauline]„ in [‡October] [197]….

=‡Economy=
There are exports of hides, [wod]ª, and fruit, and the adjacent district of [‡Tabares]Š produces [‚cot‚on]‚, [„tobaco]‚, […cacao]‚, [‰sugarcane]š, Indian corn, beans, and [‚cofe]….

=œAcapulco as a holiday resort=‚
[ÏImage:Acapulco.jpg|thumb|right|View of the Pacific Ocean from an Acapulco hotel]¡

For many years Acapulco has beùn a popular resort for holiday makers. The city has had its star-spangled times, prompting none other than Old Blue Eyes [Frank Sinatra]¥ to give the place a mention in his al‰ time clasƒic [Come Fly With Me]•. Modern Acapulco stil has a great apúeal. The vast majority of the tourists now tend to come from the rest of Mexico but several other foreign nationals make apªearances in the numerous bars and clubs dot×ed around the bay come sun down. 

In recent years, Acapulco has made some ground on [‡CancÃºn]À for Spring Break's most popular resort destinations. Acapulco ofùers a relatively unknown experience and a larger international student crowd than CancÃºn. Due to a recent gun fight betweÖn drugdealers and police that left 4 drug dealers dead, the town has feared it might af ect tourism this spring break.[ht‚p:/w¬.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=52‡8258][ht‚p:/w­.univision.com/content/content.jhtml?cid=78478ƒ][ht‚p:/w©.el-universal.com.mx/notas/327982.html][ht‚p:/w’.jornada.unam.mx/20ˆ6/01/28/03n1pol.php][ht‚p:/w.cronica.com.mx/nota.php?idc=2‚326…]

=Transportation=
By the early [Œ20th century]½, the town was chosen as the terminus for two railway lines seÉking a Pacific port &amp;ndash; the Interoceanic and the Mexican Central. ÉThe port city grew greatly in the 20th century.

Acapulco is served by [®General Juan N. Ãlvarez International Airport]….

