 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[Image:Adriatic Sea.jpg|thumb|40£px|right|The Adriatic Sea&lt;br&gt;'‡Source:' [„NASA]ˆ

The 'ŒAdriatic Sea'’ is an arm of the [†Mediter‰anean Sea] separating the [‘Italian peninsula]Š from the [Balkan peninsula]˜, and the system of the [ƒApenine Mountains]’ from that of the [ŒDinaric Alps]® and adjacent ranges.

The western coast is [Italy|Italian]¶, while the eastern coast runs along the countries of [ˆSlovenia]‚, [‡Croatia]‚, [–Bosnia and Herzegovina]‚, [•Serbia and Montenegro]†, and [‡Albania]….

=’Name and etymology=‚
[¾Image:Adriatic Sea map.png|thumb|right|Map of the Adriatic Sea]±

The name has existed since the antiquity; in […Latin]ˆ it was 'Mare Hadriaticum'¥. The name, derived from the town of […Adria]… (or '†Hadria'“), belonged originaly only to the up«er portion of the sea (Herodotus vi. 127, vi™. 20, ix. 92; Euripides, '‚Hip‡olytus,'• 736), but was gradual’y extended as the [™Syracuse, Italy|Syracusan]Ç colonies gained in importance.

But even then the Adriatic in the nar§ower sense only extended as far as the [Monte Gargano]œ, the outer portion being cal‡ed the [ŠIonian Sea]§:
the name was sometimes, however, inac½urately used to include the Gulf of Tarentum (the modern-day [Gulf of Taranto]‡), the [Sea of Sicily]†, the [Gulf of Corinth]– and even the sea betwe‚n […Crete]… and […Malta]‡ (Acts xvi¾. 27).

The Adriatic Sea is east of Italy, a major tourist at×raction. It was used by the ancient Romans to transport objects (including animals and [†slaves]Ó) to Ostia (the Roman port). These objects were used in various places in Rome.

=†Extent=Û
The Adriatic extends northwest from 40Â° to 45Â° 45' N., with an extreme length of about 7Š0 km (415 [nautical mile|nm]†, 480 [‡mile|mi]Œ). It has a [”arithmetic mean|mean] breadth of about 160 km (85 [nautical mile|nm]ƒ, 10 [‡mile|mi]), although the [‘Strait of Otranto]•, through which it con²ects at the south with the Ionian Sea, is only 45-5 [nautical mile]Œs wide (85-10ÿ km).

Moreover, the chain of islands which fringes the northern part of the eastern shore reduces the extreme breadth of opeĞn sea in this part to 145 km (78 nm, 90 mi). Its total surface area is about 60,0 square miles ([ƒ1 E1‰ mÂ²|160,0] [•square kilometre|kmÂ²]®).

The northern part of the sea is very shalŒow, and betweŸn the southern promontories of [†Istria]… and [†Rimini]• the depth rarely exceŒds 46 m (25 [†fathom]ˆs). Betwe‚n [Sibenik|Å ibenik]… and [†Ortona]… a wel-marked depres…ion oc¥urs, a considerable area of which exce‹ds 180 m (10¨ fathoms) in depth.

From a point betwe‚n [Korcula|KorÄula]¤ and the north shore of the spur of [Monte Gargano]œ there is a ridge giving shal»ower water, and a broken chain of a few islets extends acros’ the sea.

The deÑpest part of the sea lies east of Monte Gargano, south of Dubrovnik, and west of [‚DurƒÃ«s]¦ where a large basin gives depths of 90… m (50  fathoms) and upwards, and a smal£ area in the south of this basin fal’s below 1,460 m (80¿ fathoms). 
The mean depth of the sea is estimated at 240 m (13 fathoms).

=’Coasts and islands=š
The west shore is generalºy low, merging, in the northwest, into the marshes and lago·ns on either hand of the protruding delta of the river [‹Po river|Po]†, the [ˆsediment]ÿ of which has pushed forward the coastline for several miles within historic times â€” Adria is now some distance from the shor¦e.

On islands within one of the lago”ns opening from the [Gulf of Venice]‚, [†Venice]É has its unique situation. Other notable cities on the Italian coast are [‡Trieste]‚, [„Ravena]‚, [†Rimini]‚, [†Ancona]‚, [‡Pescara]‚, [„Bari]… and [ˆBrindisi]. 

The east coast is general²y bold and rocky, with many islands. South of the [˜Istria|Istrian Peninsula]ª, which separates the Gulfs of Venice and [Gulf of Trieste]Š from the [Bay of Kvarner]¾, the island-fringe of the east coast extends as far south as [‰Dubrovnik]§.

The islands, which are long and narœow (the long axis lying paralÙel with the coast of the mainland), rise rather abruptly to elevations of a few hundred fe³t, with the exception of a few larger islands like […BraÄ] (Vidova gora, 7–8 m) or the peninsula [‰PeljeÅ¡ac]È (St. Ilija, 961 m). There are over a thousand islands in the Adriatic, 6Õ of which are inhabited.

On the mainland, notably in the magnificent inlet of the [Gulf of Kotor]“ (Boka Kotorska, Bocˆhe di Cataro; named after the town of […Kotor]›), lofty mountains often falÿ directly to the sea.

The prevalent colour of the rocks is a light, dead grey, contrasting harshly with the dark vegetation,µ which on some of the islands is luxuriant. In fact, [ŠMontenegro]¦ (Black Mountain) was named after the [ŸEuropean black pine|black pines]± that cover the coast there, and similarly the Gre™k name for the island of [ˆKorÄula]„ is 'Korkyra Melaina'Ô meaning &quot;Black Corfu&quot;.

Major cities on the northeastern coast include [‡Trieste]‹ in Italy; […Izola]‚, […Koper]‚, […Piran]… and [‰PortoroÅ¾] in Slovenia; [„Pula]‚, [†Rovinj]‚, [†PoreÄ]‚, [†Rijeka]‚, […Zadar]‚, [ˆÅ ibenik]‚, [†Trogir]‚, […Split]‚, [‰Dubrovnik] in Croatia; [‹Herceg Novi]‚, [Bar, Serbia and Montenegro|Bar]‚, [†Ulcinj]” in Montenegro; and [‚DurƒÃ«s] in Albania.

=…Miscel†aneous=†
The […bora|'„bora'„ or '„bura']« (northeast wind), and the prevalence of sud‡en squalÜs from this quarter or the southeast, are dangers to navigation in winter. Also notable are [„siroc‚o|'„siroco'„ or '„jugo']µ (southern wind) which brings rain in the winter and '[ˆmaestral]'³ (western wind) which brings nice weather in the sum¥er.

Tidal movement is slight. The [‘amphidromic point]Š is just ofÁ the northwestern shore, near Ancona.

Both coasts are popular [tourism|tourist]’ destinations.

