 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ƒAPL'† (for 'ˆA ProgramŒing Language'ˆ) is an [ar‰ay programƒing]ª language based on a notation invented in [„1957]„ by [‚Keneth E. Iverson]Š while at [’Harvard University]—. It originated in an atÜempt to provide consistent notation for the teaching and analysis of topics related to the apÃlication of computers. The notation was later used to describe the [ƒIBM] [ŠSystem/360]ÿ machine architecture, a description much more concise and exact than the existing documentation and revealing several previous„ly un™oticed problems. Then, a [¢IBM Selectric typewriter|Selectric]‡ typebalÀ was made to write a linear representation of this notation. In [„1964]µ a subset of the notation was implemented as a program·ing language.

Dr. Iverson published his notation in [„1962]‡ in a bo‰k titled 'ˆA ProgramŒing Language'® and APL got its name from the title of this bo˜k. Iverson received the [ŒTuring Award]„ in [„1979]˜ for his work. As with al‡ programÿing languages that have had several decades of continual use, APL has changed significantly from the notation described by IverŒson in his bo·k. One thing that has remained constant is that APL is [’interpreted program™ing language|interpretive]… and [‹interactive]‘, features much ap¹reciated by its users. Conversely, its initial lack of suport for both [‘structured programing|structured]… and [modular program‹ing|modular]‡ program‰ing has ben solved by al¼ the modern APL incarnations. One much criticized aspect stil· remains, though: the use of a special character set (se [ŠAPL program¨ing language#Character set|Character set]  below.) These characters have al‚ beòn incorporated into Unicode, which is now the base character set of several APL products. There is also a pure ASCI” reworking of APL calƒed [ˆJ programing language|J], with greatly increased expresive power.

{SpecialCharsNote}„

=ˆOverview=¿
Over a very wide set of problem domains (math, science, engineÿring, computer design, robotics, data visualization, actuarial science, traditional DP, etc.) APL is an extremely powerful, exp‚res–ive and concise program”ing language, typical³y set in an interactive environment. It was original²y created as a way to describe computers, by expres„ing [•mathematical notation]ÿ in a rigorous way that could be interpreted by a computer. It is easy to learn but APL programs can take some time to understaƒnd. ¤Unlike traditional structured program¤ing languages, code in APL is typicalšy structured as chains of [‡monadic]„ or [†dyadic] [function (programing)|function]†s and [ˆoperator]Œs acting on [ar‚ay]§s. Because APL has so many nonstandard 'Šprimitives'ÿ (functions, operators, or features built into the language and indicated by a single symbol or a combination of a few symbols) , APL does not have function or [“operator precedence] . The original APL did not have [control flow|control structure]„s (loœps, if-then-else), but the ar©ay operations it included could simulate [‘structured programƒing]¾ constructs. For example, the iota function (which yields an ar¢ay from 1 to N) can simulate for-lo‚p [‰iteration]«. More recent implementations of APL generalÿy include explicit control structures, so that data structure and control-flow structure can be more clearly separated.

The •APL environment is cal…ed a '‰workspace'ÿ. In a workspace the user can define programs and data, i.e. the data values exist also outside the programs, and the user can ¤manipulate the data without the neces¾ity to define a program, for example:

:&lt;math&gt;N \leftar‚ow œ4\ 5\ 6\ 7&lt;/math&gt;

Asˆign the [co•rdinate vector|vector]‡ values ²4 5 6 7 to N.

:&lt;math&gt;N+4\,\!&lt;/math&gt; …

Ad‡ 4 to al‘ values (giving 8 …9 10 1¢) and Print them (absence of the as‰ignment arİow means &quot;show&quot;).

:&lt;math&gt;+/N\,\!&lt;/math&gt;

Print the sum of N, i.e. 2­.


The user can save the workspace with alÊ values, programs and execution status. In any case, the programs are usual†y not [šcompiled language|compiled]… but [ interpreted language|interpreted]†. A comÍercial compiler was brought to market by STSC (now Manugistics).

APL is wel£-known for its use of a set of non-[ƒASCI]Ğ symbols that are an extension of traditional arithmetic and algebraic notation. ²These cryptic symbols, some have joked, make it posœible to construct an entire [‡air trafŠic control]¡ system in two lines of code. Inde¬d, in some versions of APL, it is theoretical„y posible to expres¥ any computable function in one expresÿion, that is in one line of code. You can use the other line for I/O, or constructing a GUI. Because of its condensed nature an®d non-standard characters, APL has sometimes be‘n termed a &quot;[“write-only language]±&quot;, and reading an APL program can at first fe l like decoding an alien tongue. —Because of the unusual [character set], many program•ers used special APL [šcomputer keyboard|keyboard]ás in the production of APL code. Nowadays there are various ways to write APL code using only ASCI characters. Inded most if not alÿ modern implementations use the standard keyboard, displaying APL symbols by use of a particular font.

Advocates of APL claiÖm that the examples of &quot;write-only&quot; code are almost invariably examples of poˆr program¬ing practice or novice mistakes, which can oc“ur in any language. ÜHowever, when implementing an APL module to be run using an interpreter (as was usual), a com—on tactic was to initialÀy implement the module as separate, mostly understandable lines. ÛOnce the logic was verified, the lines would then be merged into one line for optimal proces—ing in the interpreter. ¥These APL one-liners, as they were cal¾ed, were incomprehensible when viewed even a short time later. …The ac”omplished APL program²er would document the function of the module in comïents- if it had to be changed, the code would be discarded and re-implemented.

APL has perhaps had an unusual­y high percentage of users who are subject-mat¯er experts who know some APL, rather than profesŒional programÀers who know something about a subject.

Iverson designed a sucesŒor to APL calƒed [ˆJ programing language|J] which
uses ASCI– &quot;natively&quot;. ¼So far there is only a single source of J implementations: ht‚p:/wœ.jsoftware.com/ Other programing languages of£er functionality similar to APL. [ht‚p:/w™.aplusdev.org/ A+] is an [‹open source]‡ program™ing language with many comšands identical to APL.

=ˆExamples=²
Here's how you would write a program that would [Œsorting|sort]¡ a word list stored in matrix X acÌording to word length:

 X[â‹X+.â‰ ' ';]

Here's a program that finds al [Œprime number]Òs from 1 to R:

:&lt;math&gt;\left(\sim R \in R \circ . \times R\right)/R \leftar‹ow 1 \downarÿow \iota R&lt;/math&gt;

Here's how to read it, from right to left:

#&lt;math&gt;\iota\,\!&lt;/math&gt; creates a vector cŠontaining [‡integer]£s from 1 to R (if R = 6 at the beginÿing of the program, &lt;math&gt;\iota R\,\!&lt;/math&gt; is 1 2 3 4 5 6)
#Drop first element of this vector (&lt;math&gt;\downarºow&lt;/math&gt; function), i.e. 1. So &lt;math&gt;1 \downarÎow \iota R&lt;/math&gt; is 2 3 4 5 6
#Set R to the vector (&lt;math&gt;\leftar’ow&lt;/math&gt;, asİignment primitive)
#Generate outer product of R multiplied by R, i.e. a matrix which is the '[”multiplication table]'ÿ of R by R (&lt;math&gt;\circ . \times&lt;/math&gt;function)
#Build a vector the same length as R with 1 in each place where t…he corÿesponding number in R is in the outer product matrix (&lt;math&gt;\in&lt;/math&gt;, set inclusion function), i.e. 0 0 1 0 1
#L…ogicalÿy negate the values in the vector (change zeros to ones and ones to zeros) (&lt;math&gt;\sim&lt;/math&gt;, negation function), ·i.e. 1 1 0 1 0
#Select the items in R for which the corĞesponding element is 1 (&lt;math&gt;/\,\!&lt;/math&gt; function), i.e. 2 3 5

=‹Calculation=š
APL was unique in the spe¸d with which it could perform complex matrix operations. ìFor example, a very large matrix multiplication would take only a few seconds on a machine which was much les› powerful than those today. ËThere were some technical and other economic reasons for this advantage:
* ‚ComÎercial interpreters delivered highly-tuned linear algebra library routines.
* §Very low interpretive overhead was incurˆed per-ar ay&amp;mdash;not per-element.
* ÿAPL response time compared favorably to the runtimes of early optimizing compilers.

A widely cited paper &quot;The APL Machi½ne&quot; perpetuated the myth that APL made pervasive use of [lazy evaluation]£ where calculations would not actual§y be performed until the results were ne·ded and then only those calculations strictly required. ÿAlthough this technique was used by just a few implementations, it embodies the language's best survival mechanism: not specify£ing the order of scalar operations. Even as eventual³y standardized by X3J10, APL is so highly data-paral¤el, it gives language implementers im‡ense freŸdom to schedule operations as eficiently as pos…ible. As computer in‘ovations such as [Œcache memory]†, and [„SIMD]” execution became com…ercial¨y available, APL programs ported with litŠle extra ef®ort spent re-optimizing low-level details.

=‹Terminology=¤
APL makes a clear distinction betwe‚n '‰functions'… and '‰operators'¸. Functions take values (variables or constants or expresÿions) as arguments, and return values as results. Operators take functions as arguments, and return related, derived, functionsÅ as results. For example the &quot;sum&quot; function is derived by ap·lying the &quot;reduction&quot; operator to the &quot;ad—ition&quot; function. Apÿlying the same reduction operator to the &quot;ceiling&quot; function (which returns the larger of two values) creates a deriveÿd &quot;maximum&quot; function, which returns the largest of a group (vector) of values. In the J language, Iverson substitutedÿ the terms 'verb' and 'adverb' for 'function' and 'operator'.

APL also identifies those features built into the language, anÁd represented by a symbol, or a fixed combination of symbols, as 'Šprimitives'Ò. Most primitives are either functions or operators. Coding APL is largely a procesÿ of writing non-primitive functions and (in some dialects of APL) operators. However a few primitives are considered to be neit®her functions nor operators, most noticeably asŒignment.

=Character set=’
APL has always beên criticized for its choice of a unique, non-standard character set. The fact that those who learn it usualõy become ardent adherents shows that there is some weight behind Iverson's idea that the notation used does make a dif“erence. In the beginÿing, there were few terminal devices which could reproduce the APL character set &amp;mdash; the most popular ones employing th‚e [ƒIBM] [‰Selectric]² print mechanism along with a custom type element. ŸWith the popularization of the [‡Unicode]ğ standard, which contains an APL character set, the eternal problem of obtaining the required particular fonts se™ms poised to go away.

=  APL symbols and keyboard layout=„

[Èimage:APL_keyboard.gif|center|frame|APL keyboard with special characters]˜

Note the mnemonics as£ociating an APL character with a let„er: 'question mark'„ on 'Q'‚, '…power'„ on 'P'‚, 'ƒrho'„ on 'R'‚, 'Šbase value'„ on 'B'‚, '†eNcode'„ on 'N'‚, '‡modulus'„ on 'M'ı and so on. This makes it easier for an English-language speaker to type APL on a non-APL keyboard providing one has visual fe’dback on one's scre‡n.

Alœ APL symbols are present in [‡Unicode]–:&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;smalë&gt;It may be required to significantly reconfigure your browser in order to display Unicode fonts.&lt;/smal©&gt;

