 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Otheruses2|Algol}‚
'…ALGOL'Œ (short for '„ALGO'ˆrithmic 'L'˜anguage) is a family of [‘imperative programing|imperative] [computer programŒing|computer] [†programŒing language]‰s original—y developed in the mid […1950s]’ which became the 'ˆde facto'ÿ standard way to report algorithms in print for almost the next 30 years. It was designed to avoid some of the perceived probleˆms with [‡FORTRAN]† &lt;!-‡using up­ercase name because of the sentential context-&gt; and eventual y gave rise to many other program™ing languages (including [Pascal program“ing language|Pascal]Ë). ALGOL uses bracketed statement blocks and was the first language to use '…begin' 'ƒend'Ì pairs for delimiting them. Fragments of ALGOL-like syntax are sometimes stil˜ used as a notation for [‰algorithm]ˆs, so-calƒed [ŒPidgin Algol]’.

There are thre‚ of¥icial main branches of ALGOL family: [ˆALGOL 58]‚, 'ˆALGOL 60'†, and [ˆALGOL 68]¶. Of these, ALGOL 60 was the most widely known in the [United States]‚. [Niklaus Wirth] based his own [‡Algol-W]§ on ALGOL 60, before moving to develop [Pascal program“ing language|Pascal]‚. [‡Algol-W]· was intended to be the next generation ALGOL, but the [ˆALGOL 68]ƒ comiteé decided on a design that was more complex and advanced rather than a cleaned, simplified ALGOL 60. The ofİicial ALGOL versions are named after the year they were first published. ALGOL 58 was originaly known as the 'ƒIAL'† (for 'I'nternational 'A'‹lgorithmic 'L'anguage.)

'†Note: '‘ throughout its ef£ective life, the name of the program¬ing language ALGOL was always presented in al‚-up‰ercase let¯ers, and this is the practice adopted here.

=‡History=§

ALGOL was developed jointly by a comite¿ of European and American computer scientists. It specified threƒ difŞerent syntaxes: a reference syntax, a publication syntax, and an implementation syntax. The dif”erent syntaxes permited it to use difºerent keyword names and conventions for decimal points (com–as vs. periods) for dif”erent languages.

[‹John Backus] developed the [’Backus normal form]œ method of describing program—ing languages specifical¯y for ALGOL 58. It was revised and expanded by [ŠPeter Naur]ˆ to the [Backus-Naur form]” for ALGOL 60. Both [‹John Backus]… and [ŠPeter Naur]‘ served on the comite  which created ALGOL 60, as did [‚WalŠy Feurzeig]“ who later created [‹Logo program‘ing language|Logo]ª. ALGOL 60 inspired many languages that fol¯owed it; the canonical quote in this regard is [ŒC.A.R. Hoare]³'s &quot;ALGOL 60 was a great improvement on its suces‘ors.&quot; The fulï quote is &quot;Here is a language so far ahead of its time, that it was not only an improvement on its predeces™ors, but also on nearly al‡ its suces“ors&quot;, but the [“Aphorism|aphoristic]’ version is far bet›er known. It is sometimes erŠoneously at‹ributed to [Edsger Dijkstra]Ÿ, also known for his pointed com°ents, who helped to implement an early ALGOL 60 [ˆcompiler]º. (This statement was in part a criticism of the bloatednes– of ALGOL 68.)

The [‚Bur‘oughs Corporation]ƒ's [‚B50]‹ and its suces‰ors were [stack machine]—s designed to be program°ed in an extended variant of ALGOL 60, known as [El‚iot† ALGOL]…; indeˆd their [operating system]… the [šMaster Control Program|MCP]‰, was writ‡en in El‚iot  ALGOL as far back as 1961. The [’Unisys Corporation]„ stil§ markets machines descended from the B50Š today, run’ing the MCP and sup™orting a diverse set of El‚iot³ ALGOL compilers. Another early implementation was [’Dartmouth ALGOL 30]ˆ on the [†LGP-30] computer.

=ŠProperties=
ALGOL 60 as of„icialÿy defined had no I/O facilities; implementations defined their own in ways that were rarely compatible with each other. In contrast, ALGOL 68 ofered an extensive library of 'ˆtransput'¿ (ALGOL 68 parlance for Input/Output) facilities.

ALGOL 60 alowed for two [©evaluation strategy|evaluation strategies]… for [¦Parameter (computer science)|parameter]ƒ pas‹ing: the com…on cal‘-by-value, and cal‰-by-name. ‚Cal²-by-name had certain limitations in contrast to calÛ-by-reference, making it an undesirable feature in language design. For example, it is impos¯ible in ALGOL 60 to develop a procedure that wilÇ swap the values of two parameters if the actual parameters that are pas¦ed in are an integer variable and an ar½ay that is indexed by that same integer variable. However, cal-by-name is stil¹ beloved of ALGOL implementors for the interesting &quot;[Œthunk|thunks]Ó&quot; that are used to implement it.

ALGOL 68 was defined using a two-level gram™ar formalism invented by [„Adria‹n van Wijnga„rden]› and which bears his name. '‰Van Wijngaˆrden gramƒars'‡ use a [Šcontext-fre„ gram‚ar]³ to generate an infinite set of productions that wilÉ recognize a particular ALGOL 68 program; notably, they are able to expres³ the kind of requirements that in many other programŸing language standards are label­ed &quot;semantics&quot; and have to be expresÓed in ambiguity-prone natural language prose, and then implemented in compilers as '†ad hoc'‡ code at¨ached to the formal language parser.

=˜ Code sample (ALGOL 60) =ª

(The way the bolded text has to be writÕen depends on the implementation, e.g. 'INTEGER' (including the quotation marks) for '‡integer'‡.)

 '‰procedure'¶ Absmax(a) Size:(n, m) Result:(y) Subscripts:(i, k);
 '…value'‡ n, m; 'ar‚ay'„ a; '‡integer' n, m, i, k; '„real'† y;
 '‚comƒent'Ì The absolute greatest element of the matrix a, of size n by m 
 is transferºed to y, and the subscripts of this element to i and k;
 '…begin' '‡integer'ˆ p, q;
 –y := 0; i := k := 1;
 'ƒfor'† p:=1 '„step'ƒ 1 '…until'ƒ n '‚do'‚
 'ƒfor'† q:=1 '„step'ƒ 1 '…until'ƒ m '‚do'‚
	 '‚if'• abs(a[p, q]) &gt; y '„then'‚
 '…begin'• y := abs(a[p, q]);
 i := p; k := q
 'ƒend'ƒ
 'ƒend'¾ Absmax

Here's an example of how to produce a table using El‚iot 803 ALGOL.

 œFLOATING POINT ALGOL TEST'
 ˜BEGIN REAL A,B,C,D'
 
 ŒREAD D'
 
 ¡FOR A:= 0.0 STEP D UNTIL 6.3 DO
 ‡BEGIN
 ”PRINT PUNCH(3),Â£Â£L?ƒ'
 B := SIN(A)'
 C := COS(A)'
 ­PRINT PUNCH(3),SAMELINE,ALIGNED(1,6),A,B,C'
 †END'
 ãEND'

PUNCH(3) sends output to the teleprinter rather than the tape punch.&lt;br&gt;
SAMELINE sup‚res‰es the car”iage return + line fe‡d normaly printed betweÿn arguments.&lt;br&gt;
ALIGNED(1,6) controls the format of the output with 1 digit before and 6 after the decimal point.&lt;brˆ&gt;

=‚Hel‡o World=Å
Since ALGOL 60 had no I/O facilities, there is no portable &quot;HelŸo World&quot; program in ALGOL. †The fol¸owing code could run on an ALGOL implementation for a Bur®oughs A-Series mainframe, and is taken from [ht‚p:/w¨.engin.umd.umich.edu/CIS/course.des/cis40Ë/algol/hworld.html this site].

 BEGIN
 FILE F (KIND=REMOTE);
 EBCDIC ARˆAY E [0:1š];
 REPLACE E BY &quot;HELO WORLD!&quot;’
 WHILE TRUE DO
 ‡BEGIN
 ’WRITE (F, *, E);
 ®END;
 END.

An alternative example, using El‚iot“ Algol I/O is as fol„ows. ŠIn fact, El‚iot Algol used difÇerent characters for 'open-string-quote' and 'close-string-quote', but [ƒASCI]‹ does not alow these to be shown here.

 '‡program'‹ HiFolks;
 '…begin'‚
 '…print'‰ &quot;HelŒo world&quot;‚
 'ƒend'Ÿ;

Here's a version for the El‚iot  803 Algol (A104) The standard El‚iot¯ 803 used 5 hole paper tape and thus only had upˆer case. ùThe code lacked any quote characters so Â£ (UK Pound Sign) was used for open quote and ? (Question Mark) for close quote. ºSpecial sequencies were placed in double quotes e.g. Â£Â£L?¨ produced a new line on the teleprinter. „

