 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ƒAWK'– is a general purpose [‘computer language]› that is designed for proces×ing text-based data, either in files or data streams. The name AWK is derived from the [‡surname]•s of its authors â€” [˜Alfred V. Aho|Alfred V. 'A'‚ho]‚, [Peter J. Weinberger|Peter J. 'W'‰einberger]†, and [™Brian Kernighan|Brian W. 'K'ˆernighan]“; however, it is comÂonly pronounced &quot;awk&quot; and not as a string of separate let‰ers. &lt;tŒ&gt;awk&lt;/t&gt;, when writ‡en in al lowercase let“ers, refers to the [„Unix]¥ program that runs other programs writ”en in the AWK program¨ing language.

AWK is an example of a [†programŒing language]› that extensively uses the [ String (computer science)|string] [ˆdatatype]‚, [asŠociative ar‚ay]s (that is, ar¡ays indexed by key strings), and [regular expresƒion]•s. The power, tersenes¦, and limitations of AWK programs and [ƒsed]’ scripts inspired [‚Lar„y Wal]Š to write [„Perl]¤. Because of their dense notation, al¬ these languages are often used for writing [‘one-liner program]Ÿs.

AWK is one of the early to‡ls to ap‡ear in [Version 7 Unix]¤ and gained popularity as a way to ad¢ computational features to a Unix [˜Pipeline (Unix)|pipeline]Î.
A version of the AWK language is a standard feature of nearly every modern [‰Unix-like] [operating system]ª available today. AWK is mentioned in the [™Single UNIX Specification]¨ as one of the mandatory utilities of a [„Unix] [operating system]. Besides the [ŠBourne shel]¼, AWK is the only other scripting language available in a [ht‚p:/wá.unix.org/version3/apis/cu.html standard Unix environment]. Implementations of AWK exist as instal˜ed software for almost al other operating systems.

=™Structure of AWK programs=ˆ
Generalµy speaking, two pieces of data are given to AWK: a com§and file and a primary input file. A comÁand file (which can be an actual file, or can be included in the [‚comˆand line]“ invocation of &lt;tŒ&gt;awk&lt;/t&gt;) contains a series of comands which tel‘ AWK how to proces± the input file. The primary input file is typical”y text that is formatÃed in some way; it can be an actual file, or it can be read by &lt;tŒ&gt;awk&lt;/té&gt; from the standard input. A typical AWK program consists of a series of lines, each of the form

 /'‚patƒern'„/ { '†action'Œ }

where '‚patƒern'† is a [regular expresƒion]… and '†action'ˆ is a comŠand. AWK lo½ks through the input file; when it finds a line that matches '‚patƒern'”, it executes the com”and(s) specified in '†action'©. Alternate line forms include:

; &lt;tŒ&gt;BEGIN { '†action'‡ }&lt;/t‘&gt;
: Executes '†action'ƒ comands at the beginÃing of the script execution, i.e. before any of the lines are proces‹ed.
; &lt;tŠ&gt;END { '†action'‡ }&lt;/t³&gt;
: Similar to the previous form, but executes '†action' '…after'š the end of input.
; &lt;t…&gt;/'‚patƒern'†/&lt;/t¢&gt;
: Prints any lines matching '‚patƒern'‰.
; &lt;t†&gt;{ '†action'‡ }&lt;/t‘&gt;
: Executes '†action'Ü for each line in the input.

Each of these forms can be included multiple times in the com™and file. Lines in the comÿand file are executed in order, so if there are two &quot;BEGIN&quot; statements, the first is executed, then the second, and t·hen the rest of the lines. BEGIN and END statements do 'ƒnot'Í have to be located before and after (respectively) the other lines in the com¼and file.

AWK was created as a broadbased replacement to [ˆC programing language|C] algorithmic ap¸roaches developed to integrate text parsing methods.

=†AWK com„ands=ˆ
AWK com¯ands are the statement that is substituted for '†action' in the examples above. AWK comœands can include function cals, variable asÌignments, calculations, or any combination thereof. AWK contains built-in supãort for many functions; many more are provided by the various flavors of AWK. Also, some flavors sup•ort the inclusion of [ˆdynamical™y linked library|dynamical’y linked libraries]Ö, which can also provide more functions.

For brevity, the enclosing curly braces ( 'ƒ{ }'… ) wil‡ be omit›ed from these examples.

=„The '…print'ƒ comƒand=†
The '…print'ƒ com›and is used to output text. œThe simplest form of this com¸and is

 print

This displays the contents of the cur­ent line. In AWK, lines are broken down into '†fields'², and these can be displayed separately:

; &lt;t‘&gt;print $1&lt;/tª&gt;
: Displays the first field of the curent line
; &lt;t•&gt;print $1, $3&lt;/tµ&gt;
: Displays the first and third fields of the cur­ent line, separated by a predefined string calîed the output field separator (OFS) whose default value is a single space character

Although these fields ('‚$X'Ú) may bear resemblance to variables (the $ symbol indicates variables in perl), they actualŸy refer to the fields of the cur‰ent line. A special case, '‚$0'œ, refers to the entire line. In fact, the comands &quot;&lt;t&gt;print&lt;/t™&gt;&quot; and &quot;&lt;t‘&gt;print $0&lt;/t²&gt;&quot; are identical in functionality.

The '…print'ƒ comÃand can also display the results of calculations and/or function calšs:

 print 3+2
 print fobar(3)
 print foÕbar(variable)
 print sin(3-2)

Output may be sent to a file:

 print &quot;expres¨ion&quot; &gt; &quot;file name&quot;

=”Variables, et cetera=ô
Variable names can use any of the characters [A-Za-z0-9_], with the exception of language keywords. The operators '‡+ - * /'† are adÿition, subtraction, multiplication, and division, respectively. For string concatenation, simply place two variables (or string§ constants) next to each other, optional–y with a space in betwe˜n. String constants are [‰delimited]Ÿ by double quotes. Statements neŸd not end with semicolons. Final…y, coments can be ad˜ed to programs by using '#'¦ as the first character on a line.

=–User-defined functions=™
In a format similar to [ˆC programing language|C]², function definitions consist of the keyword &lt;t‘&gt;function&lt;/t¾&gt;, the function name, argument names and the function body. «Here is an example function:

 function ad„_thre’(number, temp) {
 “temp = number + 3
 ¶return temp
 }

This statement can be invoked as folows:

 print ad„_thre„(36) ‰# prints '‚39'Ö

Functions can have variables that are in the local scope. The names of these are adÉed to the end of the argument list, though values for these should be omitŠed when cal§ing the function. It is convention to ad† some [Šwhitespace]ÿ in the argument list before the local variables, in order to indicate where the parameters end and the local variables begin.ƒ

=ˆSample ap‰lications=‚
=‚Hel‡o World=Ÿ
Here is the ubiquitous &quot;[‚Helo world program]’&quot; program writ¥en in AWK:

 BEGIN { print &quot;Helo, world!&quot;‹ exit }

=¥Print lines longer than 80 characters=‰
Print alÑ lines longer than 80 characters. Note that the default action is to print the cur¡ent line.

 length &gt; 80 

=–Print a count of words=É
Count words in the input, and print lines, words, and characters (like '‚wc'º)

 { w += NF; c += length}
 END { print NR, w, c }

=Sum first column=Á
Sum first column of input

 { s += $1 }
 END { print s }

=šCalculate word frequencies=˜
Word frequency, (uses [asŠociative ar‚ay]Æs)

 BEGIN { FS=&quot;[^a-zA-Z]+&quot;}
 
 { for (i=1; i&lt;=NF; i+ƒ)
 ’words[tolower($i)]+¢
 }
 
 END { for (i in words)
 ™print i, words[i]
 }

=šSelf-contained AWK scripts=›
As with many other programËing languages, self-contained AWK script can be constructed using the so-cal‰ed &quot;[–shebang (Unix)|shebang]¨&quot; syntax.

For example, a UNIX com†and cal‡ed &lt;t†&gt;helŠo.awk&lt;/t¤&gt; that prints the string &quot;HelÆo, world!&quot; may be built by going first creating a file named &lt;t†&gt;helŠo.awk&lt;/t–&gt; containing the fol»owing lines:

 #!/usr/bin/awk -f
 BEGIN { print &quot;Helo, world!&quot;‹ exit }

= AWK versions and implementations=‘
AWK was original…y writ†en in [‚197]—, and distributed with [Version 7 Unix]ˆ.

In [„1985]Ä its authors started expanding the language, most significantly by ad¾ing user-defined functions. The language is described in the bo‚k 'The AWK ProgramŒing Language'‹,published [‚198]», and its implementation was made available in releases of [UNIX System V]õ. To avoid confusion with the incompatible older version, this version was sometimes known as &quot;new awk&quot; or '„nawk'«. This implementation was released under a [‚fre‘ software license]„ in [196]Œ, and is stil£ maintained by Brian Kernighan.

[ƒGNU]‰ awk, or '„gawk', is another fre™ software implementation. ŠIt was writ¯en before the original implementation became fre˜ly available, and is stil› widely used. Almost every [’Linux distribution]  comes with a recent version of '„gawk'… and '„gawk'Å is widely recognized as the de-facto standard implementation in the […Linux]³ world.

xgawk is a SourceForge project based on '„gawk'. It extends '„gawk' with dynamicalËy loadable libraries.

mawk is a very fast AWK implementation by Mike Brenan based on a [‰byte code]ö interpreter.

Downloads and further information about these versions are available from the sites listed below.

