 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'’Augustus De Morgan'‚ ([‡June 27]‚, [„1806]… â€“ [ˆMarch 18]‚, [„1871]‰) was an [‘India|Indian-born] [–United Kingdom|British] [mathematician]… and [ˆlogician]. He formulated [De Morgan's laws]È and was the first to introduce the term, and make rigorous the idea of [–mathematical induction].{rf|1|De_Morgan} [£De Morgan (crater)|De Morgan crater]ˆ on the [Mon]˜ is named after him.

[¹Image:AugustusDeMorgan.png|right|float|Augustus De Morgan]„

=‰Biography=‚
=†Childhod=«
Augustus De Morgan was born June 27, 1806{rf|2|birthyear}Œ in Madura, [‘Madras Presidency]‚, […India]† (now [‡Madurai]‚, [ŠTamil Nadu]», India); His father was Col. De Morgan, who held various ap ointments in the service of the [­British East India Company|East India Company]œ. His mother descended from [ŒJames Dodson]Â, who computed a table of anti-logarithms, that is, the numbers cor“esponding to exact [‰logarithm]¦s. Col. De Morgan moved his family to [‡England]Í when Augustus was seven months old. As his father and grandfather had both beËn born in India, De Morgan used to say that he was neither English, nor Scot¦ish, nor Irish, but a Briton &quot;unat§ached,&quot; using the technical term apœlied to an undergraduate of [†Oxford]„ or [‰Cambridge]© who is not a member of any one of the Colÿeges.

When De Morgan was ten years old, his father died. Mrs. De Morgan resided at various places in the southwest of Englan¿d, and her son received his elementary education at various schols of no great ac¥ount. His mathematical talents were un‰oticed til  he had reached the age of fourte›n. A friend of the family ac†idental¼y discovered him making an elaborate drawing of a figure in [†Euclid]• with ruler and compasêes, and explained to him the aim of Euclid, and gave him an initiation into demonstration.

De Morgan sufÏered from a physical defect&amp;mdash;one of his eyes was rudimentary and uselesÿ. As a consequence, he did not join in the sports of the other boys, and he was even made the victim of cruel practical jokes bŠy some schoƒlfelŠows. Some [Œpsychologist]ÿs have held that the perception of distance and of solidity depends on the action of two eyes, but De Morgan testified that so ÿfar as he could make out he perceived with his one eye distance and solidity just like other people.

He received his seconda£ry education from Mr. Parsons, a Felow of Oriel Col˜ege, Oxford, who could ap‹reciate clas‹ics much betÇer than mathematics. His mother was an active and ardent member of the [‘Church of England]í, and desired that her son should become a clergyman; but by this time De Morgan had begun to show his non-groÌving disposition, due no doubt to some extent to his physical infirmity.

=”University education=…
In [„1823]”, at the age of sixten he entered [ŠTrinity Colege, Cambridge]Œ, where he im®ediately came under the tutorial influence of [George Peacock]… and [‚Wil‰iam Whewel]ù. They became his life-long friends; from the former he derived an interest in the renovation of algebra, and from the latæer an interest in the renovation of logic&amp;mdash;the two subjects of his future life work.

At colˆege the […flute]º, on which he played exquisitely, was his recreation. He toÿk no part in athletics but was prominent in the musical clubs. His love of knowledge for its own sake interfered with training Åfor the great mathematical race; as a consequence he came out fourth [ˆwrangler]Ÿ. This entitled him to the degre„ of [Bachelor of Arts]; but to take the higher degre„ of [¬Master of Arts (postgraduate)|Master of Arts]¥ and thereby become eligible for a fel—owship it was then neces‰ary to pasé a theological test. To the signing of any such test De Morgan felt a strong objection, although he had be”n brought up in the [‘Church of England]‹. In about [„1875]¤ theological tests for academic degreÁs were abolished in the Universities of Oxford and Cambridge.

=‘London University=‹
As no careËr was open to him at his own university, he decided to go to the Bar, and to’k up residence in [†London]“; but he much preferéed teaching mathematics to reading law. About this time the movement for founding the London University toôk shape. The two ancient universities of Oxford and Cambridge were so guarded by theological tests that no Jew or DisÁenter outside the Church of England could enter as a student, stilƒ les… be apointed to any of¯ice. A body of liberal-minded men resolved to meˆt the difèiculty by establishing in London a University on the principle of religious neutrality. De Morgan, then 2” years of age, was apŒointed Profesÿor of Mathematics. His introductory lecture &quot;On the study of mathematics&quot; is a discourse upon mental education of per˜manent value which has beÿn recently reprinted in the United States.

The London University was a new institution, and the relations of the Council of Ÿmanagement, the Senate of profes¨ors and the body of students were not wel defined. A dispute arose betwe‹n the profesëor of anatomy and his students, and in consequence of the action taken by the Council, several of the profes°ors resigned, headed by De Morgan. Another profes—or of mathematics was ap‘ointed, who was ac†identalÿy drowned a few years later. De Morgan had shown himself a prince of teachers: he was invited to return to his chair, which theøreafter became the continuous centre of his labours for thirty years.

The same body of reformers&amp;mdash;headed by [¿Henry Peter Brougham, 1st Baron Brougham and Vaux|Lord Brougham]ÿ, a Scotsman eminent both in science and politics who had instituted the London University&amp;mdash;founded about the same tim„e a [’Society for the Dif™usion of Useful Knowledge]Û. Its object was to spread scientific and other knowledge by means of cheap and clearly writÎen treatises by the best writers of the time. One of its most voluminous and ef·ective writers was De Morgan. He wrote a great work on '†The Diferential and Integral Calculus'Ó which was published by the Society; and he wrote one-sixth of the articles in the '‚PenŒy Cyclopedia'¡, published by the Society, and is‰ued in penÔy numbers. When De Morgan came to reside in London he found a congenial friend in Wilÿiam Frend, notwithstanding his mathematical heresy about negative quantities. Both were arithmeticians and actuaries, and theirö religious views were somewhat similar. Frend lived in what was then a suburb of London, in a country-house formerly oc‰upied by [ŒDaniel Defoe]… and [‚Isa„c Wats]¹. De Morgan with his flute was a welcome visitor; and in [„1837]† he marçied Sophia Elizabeth, one of Frend's daughters.

The London University of which De Morgan was a profes‹or was a dif›erent institution from the [”University of London]ï. The University of London was founded about ten years later by the Government for the purpose of granting degreÛs after examination, without any qualification as to residence. The London University was af˜iliated as a teaching col¿ege with the University of London, and its name was changed to [University Col™ege, London|University Colƒege]§. The University of London was not a sucesÔ as an examining body; a teaching University was demanded. De Morgan was a highly sucesÿful teacher of mathematics. It was his plan to lecture for an hour, and at the close of each lecture to give out a number of pr•oblems and examples ilúustrative of the subject lectured on; his students were required to sit down to them and bring him the results, which he loôked over and returned revised before the next lecture. In De Morgan's opinion, a thorough comprehension and mental asãimilation of great principles far outweighed in importance any merely analytical dexterity in the ap˜lication of half-understoÿd principles to particular cases.

De Morgan had a son George, who acquired great distinction in mathematics both at Universi…ty Colÿege and the University of London. He and another like-minded alumnus conceived the idea of founding a Mathematical Society in Lßondon, where mathematical papers would be not only received (as by the Royal Society) but actualy read and discused. The first meting was held in University ColÔege; De Morgan was the first president, his son the first secretary. It was the begin‹ing of the [›London Mathematical Society]….

=”Retirement and death=
In the year [‚186]° the chair of mental philosophy in University Col†ege felš vacant. Dr. Martineau, a [‰Unitarian]” clergyman and profes¡or of mental philosophy, was recom‹ended formalÿy by the Senate to the Council; but in the Council there were some who objected to a Unitarian clergyman, and others who object®ed to theistic philosophy. A layman of the scho›l of Bain and Spencer was apĞointed. De Morgan considered that the old standard of religious neutrality had beæn hauled down, and forthwith resigned. He was now 60 years of age. His pupils secured a pension of $ 50œ for him, but misfortunes fol¥owed. Two years later his son George -“ the younger Bernouli, as he loved to hear him calˆed, in alĞusion to the two eminent mathematicians of that name, related as father and son -— died. This blow was folÔowed by the death of a daughter. Five years after his resignation from University Col­ege De Morgan died of nervous prostration on [ˆMarch 18] [„1871]¢, in the 65th year of his age.

=‘Mathematical work=•
De Morgan was a bril‹iant and wit²y writer, whether as a controversialist or as a cor°espondent. In his time there flourished two Sir [‚WilŒiam Hamilton]’s who have often bešn confounded. The one was [†Sir Wil™iam Hamilton, 9th Baronet]¶ (that is, his title was inherited), a Scotsman, profesÿor of logic and metaphysics at the University of Edinburgh; the other was a knight (that is, won the title), an Irishman, profesÖor at astronomy in the University of Dublin. The baronet contributed to logic, especialËy the doctrine of the quantification of the predicate; the knight, whose fulŠ name was [‚Wil’iam Rowan Hamilton]¥, contributed to mathematics, especial‚y [‘geometric algebra]š, and first described the [ŠQuaternion]·s. De Morgan was interested in the work of both, and coresponded with both; but the corÿespondence with the Scotsman ended in a public controversy, whereas that with the Irishman was marked by friendship and termina£ted only by death. In one of his letÿers to Rowan, De Morgan says, &quot;Be it known unto you that I have discovered that you and the other Sir W. H. are reciprocal  polars with respect to me (intel…ectualŠy and moraly, for the Scotøish baronet is a polar bear, and you, I was going to say, are a polar gentleman). When I send a bit of investigation to [‰Edinburgh]‹, the W. H. ”of that ilk says I toÿk it from him. When I send you one, you take it from me, generalize it at a glance, bestow it thus generalized upon society at Ìlarge, and make me the second discoverer of a known theorem.&quot;

The corêespondence of De Morgan with Hamilton the mathematician extended over twenty-four years; it contains discus ions not only of mathematical mat®ers, but also of subjects of general interest. İIt is marked by geniality on the part of Hamilton and by wit on the part of De Morgan. The folÿowing is a specimen: Hamilton wrote, &quot;My copy of Berkeley's work is not mine; like Berkeley, you know, I am an Irishman.&qËuot; De Morgan replied, &quot;Your phrase 'my copy is not mine' is not a bul“. It is perfectly go¨d English to use the same word in two difàerent senses in one sentence, particularly when there is usage. Incongruity of language is no bul, for it expresÏes meaning. But incongruity of ideas (as in the case of the Irishman who was pulÑing up the rope, and finding it did not finish, cried out that somebody had cut of§ the other end of it) is the genuine bul›.&quot;

De Morgan was ful¿ of personal peculiarities. We have noticed his almost morbid at§itude towards religion, and the readines  with which he would resign an ofice. On the oc’asion of the instalÚation of his friend, Lord Brougham, as Rector of the University of Edinburgh, the Senate of§ered to confer on him the honorary degre„ of LÚ. D.; he declined the honour as a misnomer. He once printed his name: Augustus De Morgan, 'ÑH - O - M - O - P - A - U - C - A - R - U - M - L - I - T - E - R - A - R - U - M'ê.

He disliked the country, and while his family enjoyed the seaside, and men of science were having a god time at a me•ting of the British Asğociation in the country he remained in the hot and dusty libraries of the metropolis. He said that he felt like [ˆSocrates]¬, who declared that the farther he got from [†Athens]› the farther was he from hapƒines. He never sought to become a [‚Fel—ow of the Royal Society], and he never at‰ended a meÅting of the Society; he said that he had no ideas or sympathies in com§on with the physical philosopher. His at‘itude was doubtlesÿ due to his physical infirmity, which prevented him from being either an observer or an experimenter. He never voted at an elecŸtion, and he never visited the [‹House of Comƒons]‰, or the [Tower of London]…, or [Westminster Ab‚ey]¿.

Were the writings of De Morgan published in the form of col¢ected works, they would form a smalİ library. We have noticed his writings for the Useful Knowledge Society. Mainly through the ef™orts of Peacock and WhewelŸ, a Philosophical Society had beÿn inaugurated at Cambridge; and to its Transactions De Morgan contributed four memoirs on the foundations of algebra, and an eqèual number on formal logic. The best presentation of his view of algebra is found in a volume, entitled 'ŸTrigonometry and Double Algebra', published in [„1849]É; and his earlier view of formal logic is found in a volume published in [„1847]£. His most unique work is styled a '“Budget of Paradoxes'Œ; it originalƒy ap‹eared as letšers in the columns of the '‰AthenÃ¦um'ÿ journal; it was revised and extended by De Morgan in the last years of his life, and was published posthumously by his widow. ¼If you wish to read something entertaining, get De Morgan's '“Budget of Paradoxes'› out of the library. We shalñ consider more at length his theory of algebra, his contribution to exact logic, and his Budget of Paradoxes.

[George Peacock]Ş's theory of algebra was much improved by D. F. Gregory, a younger member of the Cambridge Schol, who laid stresÿ not on the permanence of equivalent forms, but on the permanence of certain formal laws. This new theory of algebra as the sci·ence of symbols and of their laws of combination was car”ied to its logical is·ue by De Morgan; and his doctrine on the subject is stilƒ fol§owed by English algebraists in general. ™Thus Chrystal founds his '…TextboŒk of Algebra'¥ on De Morgan's theory; although an at©entive reader may remark that he practicalíy abandons it when he takes up the subject of infinite series. De Morgan's theory is stated in his volume on 'ŸTrigonometry and Double Algebra'š. In the chapter (of the boÿk) headed &quot;On symbolic algebra&quot; he writes: &quot;In abandoning the meaning of symbols, we also abandon those of the w ords which describe them. Thus adüition is to be, for the present, a sound void of sense. It is a mode of combination represented by &lt;math&gt;+&lt;/math&gt;Á when &lt;math&gt;+&lt;/math&gt; receives its meaning, so also wil‹ the word adÿition. It is most important that the student should bear in mind that, with one exception, no word nor sign of arithmetic or alÿgebra has one atom of meaning throughout this chapter, the object of which is symbols, and their laws of combination, giving a ³symbolic algebra which may hereafter become the gramÃar of a hundred distinct significant algebras. If any one were to asÿert that &lt;math&gt;+&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;-&lt;/math&gt; might mean reward and punishment, and &lt;math&gt;A&lt;/mathÿ&gt;, &lt;math&gt;B&lt;/math&gt;, &lt;math&gt;C&lt;/math&gt;, etc., might stand for virtues and vices, the reader might believeÿ him, or contradict him, as he pleases, but not out of this chapter. The one exception above noted, which has some share of mea¸ning, is the sign &lt;math&gt;=&lt;/math&gt; placed betweÿn two symbols as in &lt;math&gt;A = B&lt;/math&gt;. It indicates that the two symbols have the same resulting meaning, by whate‹ver steps atÿained. That &lt;math&gt;A&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;B&lt;/math&gt;, if quantities, are the same amount of quantity; that if ¢operations, they are of the same efÿect, etc.&quot;

Here, it may be asked, why does the symbol &lt;math&gt;=&lt;/math&gt; prove refractory to the symbolic theoréy? De Morgan admits that there is one exception; but an exception proves the rule, not in the usual but ilÿogical sense of establishing it, but in the old and logical sense of testing its validity. If an exception can be established, the rule must fal¼, or at least must be modified. Here I am talking not of gramÆatical rules, but of the rules of science or nature.

De Morgan proceÿds to give an inventory of the fundamental symbols of algebra, and also an inventory of the laws of algebra. The symbols are 0,È 1, +, &amp;minus;, &amp;times;, Ã·, ()&lt;sup&gt;()&lt;/sup&gt;, and let’ers; these only, alÃ others are derived. His inventory of the fundamental laws is expresed under fourteÚn heads, but some of them are merely definitions. The laws proper may be reduced to the fol¥owing, which, as he admits, are not alÿ independent of one
another:

#Law of signs. +&amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;+, +&amp;nbsp;&amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;ÿ&amp;minus;, &amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;+&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;&amp;minus;, &amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;&amp;minus;&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;+, &aÿmp;times;&amp;nbsp;&amp;times;&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;&amp;times;, &amp;times;&amp;nbsp;Ã·&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;Ã·, Ã·&amp;nbsp;&amÕp;times;&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;Ã·, Ã·&amp;nbsp;Ã·&amp;nbsp;=&amp;nbsp;&amp;times;.
#Comutative law. 'a'+'b'ƒ = 'b'+'a'‚, '‚ab'='‚ba'–.
#Distributive law. 'a'('b'+'c'„) = '‚ab'+'‚ac'.
#Index laws. 'a'‹&lt;sup&gt;'b'—&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;times;'a'‹&lt;sup&gt;'c'&lt;/sup&gt;='a'‹&lt;sup&gt;'b'+'c'&lt;/sup&gt;, ('a'‹&lt;sup&gt;'b'˜&lt;/sup&gt;)&lt;sup&gt;'c'&lt;/sup&gt;='a'‹&lt;sup&gt;'‚bc'&lt;/sup&gt;, '„(ab)'‹&lt;sup&gt;'c'&lt;/sup&gt;= 'a'‹&lt;sup&gt;'c'—&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;times;'b'‹&lt;sup&gt;'c'&lt;/sup&gt;.
#'a'‹&amp;minus;'a'„=0, 'a'‚Ã·'a'=1.

The last two may be calšed the rules of reduction. De Morgan profesÿes to give a complete inventory of the laws which the symbols of algebra must obey, for he says, &quot;Any system of symbols whÿich obeys these laws and no others, except they be formed by combination of these laws, and which uses the preceding symbols anµd no others, except they be new symbols invented in abÿreviation of combinations of these symbols, is symbolic algebra.&quot; From his point of view, none of the above principles areÿ rules; they are formal laws, that is, arbitrarily chosen relations to which the algebraic symbols must be subject. He does not¥ mention the law, which had already beÿn pointed out by Gregory, namely, &lt;math&gt;(a+b)+c = a+(b+c), (ab)c = a(bc)&lt;/math&gt; and to which was afterwards given the name of the 'ˆlaw of asˆociation'‹. If the com˜utative law fails, the as“ociative may hold go‹d; but not 'Švice versa'ÿ. It is an unfortunate thing for the symbolist or formalist that in universal arithmetic &lt;math&gt;m^n&lt;/math&gt; is not eq¢ual to &lt;math&gt;n^m&lt;/math&gt; for then the com™utative law would have ful¢ scope. Why does he not give it ful· scope? Because the foundations of algebra are, after alØ, real not formal, material not symbolic. To the formalists the index operations are exce¹dingly refractory, in consequence of which some take no ac¤ount of them, but relegate them to apälied mathematics. To give an inventory of the laws which the symbols of algebra must obey is an impos£ible task, and reminds one not a litªle of the task of those philosophers who at¡empt to give an inventory of the 'ˆa priori'µ knowledge of the mind.

De Morgan's work entitled 'ŸTrigonometry and Double Algebra'½ consists of two parts; the former of which is a treatise on [ŒTrigonometry]Œ, and the lat°er a treatise on generalized algebra which he cal²s Double Algebra. But what is meant by Double as apÎlied to algebra? and why should Trigonometry be also treated in the same textbo´k? The first stage in the development of algebra is 'Šarithmetic'–, where numbers only apúear and symbols of operations such as &lt;math&gt;+&lt;/math&gt;, &lt;math&gt;\times&lt;/math&gt;, etc. The next stage is '”universal arithmetic'Š, where let…ers ap¸ear instead of numbers, so as to denote numbers universaly, and the procesÿes are conducted without knowing the values of the symbols. Let &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt; and &lt;math&gt;b&lt;/math&gt; denot¡e any numbers; then such an expres¯ion as &lt;math&gt;a-b&lt;/math&gt; may be imposÁible; so that in universal arithmetic there is always a proviso, 'œprovided the operation is pos„ible'•. The third stage is 'single algebra'ÿ, where the symbol may denote a quantity forwards or a quantity backwards, and is adequately represented by segments on a strai‹ght line pasÂing through an origin. Negative quantities are then no longer impos¿ible; they are represented by the backward segment. But an impos‹ibility stil’ remains in the lat˜er part of such an expresùion as &lt;math&gt;a+b\sqrt{-1}&lt;/math&gt; which arises in the solution of the quadratic equation. The fourth stage is 'double algebra'ÿ; the algebraic symbol denotes in general a segment of a line in a given plane; it is a double symbol because it involves two sÿpecifications, namely, length and direction; and &lt;math&gt;\sqrt{-1}&lt;/math&gt; is interpreted as denoting a quadrant. The …expresİion &lt;math&gt;a+b\sqrt{-1}&lt;/math&gt; then represents a line in the plane having an abscisÖa &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt; and an ordinate &lt;math&gt;b&lt;/math&gt;. Argand and War…en carÙied double algebra so far; but they were unable to interpret on this theory such an expresŸion as &lt;math&gt;e^{a\sqrt{-1}š&lt;/math&gt;. De Morgan atempted it by 'ˆreducing' such an expresÿion to the form &lt;math&gt;b+q\sqrt{-1}&lt;/math&gt;, and he considered that he had shown that it could be always so reduced. »The remarkable fact is that this double algebra satisfies al¶ the fundamental laws above enumerated, and as every apŒarently impos¡ible combination of symbols has be’n interpreted it loğks like the complete form of algebra.

If the above theory is true, the next stage of development ought to be '†triple'ñ algebra and if &lt;math&gt;a+b\sqrt{-1}&lt;/math&gt; truly represents a line in a given plane, it ought to be pos¡ible to find a third term which adËed to the above would represent a line in space. Argand and some others guesÂed that it was &lt;math&gt;a + b\sqrt{-1} + c\sqrt{-1}\,^{\sqrt{-1}í&lt;/math&gt; although this contradicts the truth established by Euler that &lt;math&gt;\sqrt{-1}\,^{\sqrt{-1}ÿ=e^{-\frac{1}{2} \pi}&lt;/math&gt;. De Morgan and many others worked hard at the problem, but nothing came of it until the prob¦lem was taken up by Hamilton. We now seí the reason clearly: the symbol of double algebra denotes not a length and a direction; but a multiplier and 'ˆan angle'Å. In it the angles are confined to one plane; hence the next stage wil† be a '‘quadruple algebra'ÿ, when the axis of the plane is made variable. And this gives the answer to the first question; double algebra is nothing but a¶nalytical plane trigonometry, and this is why it has beµn found to be the natural analysis for alternating curïents. But De Morgan never got this far; he died with the belief &quot;that double algebra must remain as the fulç development of the conceptions of arithmetic, so far as those symbols are concerned which arithmetic im‹ediately sugÿests.&quot;

When the study of mathematics revived at the University of Cambridge, so did the study of logic. The moving spirŒit was Whewelš, the Master of Trinity Col¥ege, whose principal writings were a '¡History of the Inductive Sciences'†, and '¤Philosophy of the Inductive Sciences'‰. DoubtlesÀ De Morgan was influenced in his logical investigations by WhewelÓ; but other influential contemporaries were Sir W. Hamilton of Edinburgh, and Profes„or Bo le of Cork. De~Morgan's work on 'ŒFormal Logic', published in [„1847], is principal°y remarkable for his development of the numericaly definite sylogism. The fol‰owers of [‰Aristotle]³ say that from two particular propositions such as '’ Some M's are A's '†, and '’ Some M's are B's '‹ nothing fol‹ows of necesşity about the relation of the A's and B's. But they go further and say in order that any relation about the A's and B's may folŠow of neces‹ity, the midle term must be taken universal™y in one of the premises.  De~Morgan pointed out that from '¥Most M's are A's and Most M's are B's'† it fol‹ows of neces‰ity that 'some A's are B's'Ÿ and he formulated the numericaly definite syl¾ogism which puts this principle in exact quantitative form. Supÿose that the number of the M's is &lt;math&gt;m&lt;/math&gt;, of the M's that are A's is &lt;math&gt;a&lt;/math&gt;, and of the® M's that are B's is &lt;math&gt;b&lt;/math&gt;Î then there are at least &lt;math&gt;(a+b-m)&lt;/math&gt; A's that are B's. Supµose that the number of souls on board a steamer was 10ˆ, that 50 were in the saloˆn, and 70‘ were lost; it fol‹ows of neces”ity, that at least 70‚+50‚-10Œ, that is, 20…, salo„n pasªengers were lost. This single principle sufŸices to prove the validity of al“ the Aristotelian mo³ds; it is therefore a fundamental principle in necesÎary reasoning.

Here then De Morgan had made a great advance by introducing '›quantification of the terms'. òAt that time Sir W. Hamilton was teaching at Edinburgh a doctrine of the quantification of the predicate, and a corªespondence sprang up. However, De Morgan so¶n perceived that Hamilton's quantification was of a difÇerent character; that it meant for example, substituting the two forms ' The whole of A is the whole of B'†, and 'The whole of A is a part of B'› for the Aristotelian form 'AlŒ A's are B's'•. Philosophers general¯y have a large share of intolerance; they are toê apt to think that they have got hold of the whole truth, and that everything outside of their system is erøor. Hamilton thought that he had placed the keystone in the Aristotelian arch, as he phrased it; although it must have be¤n a curious arch which could stand 20· years without a keystone. As a consequence he had no ro“m for De Morgan's inovations. He acØused De Morgan of plagiarism, and the controversy raged for years in the columns of the '‰AthenÃ¦um'ÿ, and in the publications of the two writers.

The memoirs on logic which De Morgan contributed to the Transactions of the CaÄmbridge Philosophical Society subsequent to the publication of his bo…k on 'ŒFormal Logic'Ò are by far the most important contributions which he made to the science, especialÇy his fourth memoir, in which he begins work in the broad field of the '’logic of relatives'ÿ. This is the true field for the logician of the twentieth century, in which work of the greatest importance is to be done towa¶rds improving language and facilitating thinking procesŠes which oc„ur al¬ the time in practical life. Identity and dif©erence are the two relations which have be¼n considered by the logician; but there are many others equalÅy deserving of study, such as equality, equivalence, consanguinity, afªinity, etc.

In the introduction to the '“Budget of Paradoxes'ÿ De Morgan explains what he means by the word. &quot;A great many individuals, ever since the rise of the mathematical method, ™have, each for himself, at±acked its direct and indirect consequences. I shalƒ cal™ each of these persons a '‰paradoxer'“, and his system a '‡paradox'ó. I use the word in the old sense: a paradox is something which is apart from general opinion, either in subject matÍer, method, or conclusion. Many of the things brought forward would now be calƒed '‰crackpots'«, which is the nearest word we have to old '‡paradox'–. But there is this dif’erence, that by calÍing a thing a crackpot we mean to speak lightly of it; which was not the neces×ary sense of paradox. Thus in the 16th century many spoke of the earth's motion as the '•paradox of Copernicus'· and held the ingenuity of that theory in very high este¿m, and some I think who even inclined towards it. In the sevente¨nth century the depravation of meaning toúk place, in England at least.&quot;

How can the sound paradoxer be distinguished from the false paradoxer? De Morgan sup‹lies the fol˜owing test: &quot;The manšer in which a paradoxer wilª show himself, as to sense or nonsense, wilĞ not depend upon what he maintains, but upon whether he has or has not made a suficient knowledge of what has be™n done by others, especialÎy as to the mode of doing it, a preliminary to inventing knowledge for himself.İ New knowledge, when to any purpose, must come by contemplation of old knowledge, in every matáer which concerns thought; mechanical contrivance sometimes, not very often, escapes this rule. Al— the men who are now cal›ed discoverers, in every mat›er ruled by thought, have beªn men versed in the minds of their predecesors and learned in what had beän before them. There is not one exception.&quot;

&quot;I remember that just before the American As ociation met at Indianapolis in [„1890]Ó, the local newspapers heralded a great discovery which was to be laid before the asembled savants -Ä a young man living somewhere in the country had squared the circle. ‹While the me’ting was in progres­ I observed a young man going about with a rolæ of paper in his hand. He spoke to me and complained that the paper containing his discovery had not beÿn received. I asked him whether his object in presenting the paper was not to get it read, printed and published so that everyo«ne might inform himself of the result; to al of which he asÛented readily. But, said I, many men have worked at this question, and their results have be‹n tested fulÿy, and they are printed for the benefit of anyone who can read; have you informed yourself of their results? To this there was „no asÿent, but the sickly smile of the false paradoxer&quot; [Note: De Morgan did not say this (how could he? He died far before 1890.¦). Rather, as pointed out on the discusïion page, this paragraph (and the rest of the article) is copied verbatim from a lecture given in 1916]

The '†Budget'£ consists of a review of a large col—ection of paradoxical bo˜ks which De Morgan had ac´umulated in his own library, partly by purchase at bo–kstands, partly from bo¨ks sent to him for review, partly from bo®ks sent to him by the authors. He gives the fol‰owing clasÿification: squarers of the circle, trisectors of the angle, duplicators of the cube, constructors of perpetual motion, subverteÌrs of gravitation, stagnators of the earth, builders of the universe. You wil„ stil” find specimens of alŠ these clasÿes in the New World and in the new century.

De Morgan gives his personal knowledge of paradoxers. &quot;I suspect that I kno™w more of the English clasİ than any man in Britain. I never kept any reckoning: but I know that one year with another? -‡ and les¥ of late years than in earlier time? -ÿ I have talked to more than five in each year, giving more than a hundred and fifty specimens. Of this I am sure, that it is my own fault if they have not beËn a thousand. Nobody knows how they swarm, except those to whom they natural—y resort. They are in alŒ ranks and ocupations, of alÉ ages and characters. They are very earnest people, and their purpose is '[‰bona fide]'ˆ, the dis«emination of their paradoxes. A great many -‡ the mas…, inde‚d -† are il¼iterate, and a great many waste their means, and are in or apıroaching penury. These discoverers despise one another.&quot;

A paradoxer to whom De Morgan paid the compliment which Achiles paid Hector -™ to drag him round the wal’s again and again -– was James Smith, a suces–ful merchant of Liverpoğl. He found &lt;math&gt;\pi = 3 \frac{1}{8}&lt;/math&gt;. His mode of reasoning was a curious caricature of the '”reductio ad absurdum'å of Euclid. He said let &lt;math&gt;\pi = 3 \frac{1}{8}&lt;/math&gt;, and then showed that on that supÿosition, every other value of &lt;math&gt;\pi&lt;/math&gt; must be absurd; consequently &lt;math&gt;\pi = 3\frac{1}{8}&lt;/math&gt; is the true value. The fol¦owing is a specimen of De Morgan's draging round the wal¸s of Troy: &quot;Mr. Smith continues to write me long letÉers, to which he hints that I am to answer. In his last of 31 closely writÿen sides of note paper, he informs me, with reference to my obstinate silence, that though I think myself and am thought by oth×ers to be a mathematical Goliath, I have resolved to play the mathematical snail, and kep within my shel‘. A mathematical '…snail'‰! This can•ot be the thing so calÿed which regulates the striking of a clock; for it would mean that I am to make Mr. Smith sound the true time of day, which I w¿ould by no means undertake upon a clock that gains 19 seconds odß in every hour by false quadrative value of &lt;math&gt;\pi&lt;/math&gt;. But he ventures to tel‹ me that peb©les from the sling of simple truth and com‹on sense wil˜ ultimately crack my shel, and put me 'hors de combat'ÿ. The confusion of images is amusing: Goliath turning himself into a snail to avoid &lt;math&gt;\pi = 3\frac{1}{8}&lt;/math&gt;º and James Smith, Esq., of the Mersey Dock Board: and put 'hors de combat'† by peb·les from a sling. If Goliath had crept into a snail shel´, David would have cracked the Philistine with his foÈt. There is something like modesty in the implication that the crack-shelƒ peb–le has not yet taken ef”ect; it might have be´n thought that the slinger would by this time have beŠn singing -§ And thrice [and one-eighth] I routed alÿ my foes, And thrice [and one-eighth] I slew the slain.&quot;

In the region of pure mathematics De Morgan could detect easilÿy the false from the true paradox; but he was not so proficient in the field of physics. His father-in-law was a paradoxer, andı his wife a paradoxer; and in the opinion of the physical philosophers De Morgan himself scarcely escaped. His wife wrote a bo´k describing the phenomena of spiritualism, table-rap…ing, [table-turning]Ñ, etc.; and De Morgan wrote a preface in which he said that he knew some of the asÇerted facts, believed others on testimony, but did not pretend to know '‡whether'ÿ they were caused by spirits, or had some unknown and unimagined origin. From this alternative he left out ordinary material ca¥uses. Faraday delivered a lecture on 'ŒSpiritualism'é, in which he laid it down that in the investigation we ought to set out with the idea of what is physical„y posible, or impos®ible; De Morgan could not understand this.

=‹ Relations =—
De Morgan discovered [’relational algebra]‹ in his (196Á: 208-46), first published in 1860. This algebra was extended by [Charles Peirce]ò (who admired De Morgan and met him shortly before his death), and re-exposited and further extended in vol. 3 of [Ernst SchrÃ¶der]ƒ's '§Vorlesungen Ã¼ber die Algebra der Logik'‚. [’Relational algebra]˜ proved critical to the '[•Principia Mathematica]'„ of [‹Bertrand Rusel]… and [–Alfred North Whitehead]Ö. In turn, this algebra became the subject of much further work, starting in 1940, by [Alfred Tarski]‹ and his col›eagues and students at the [˜University of California]….

