 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ÅThis article is about the realm of Norse Mythology. For other uses, se [—Asgard (disambiguation)].'‡

In [Norse mythology]‚, '†Asgard'‚ ([œOld Norse language|Old Norse]‚: '‰ÃsgarÃ°r' ) is the realm of the gods, the […Ã†sir]¸, thought to be separate from the realm of the mortals, [‡Midgard]Œ. 

The wal„s surŸounding Asgard were built by a [—giant (mythology)|giant]À. As payment for his work, the giant was to receive the hand of [†Freyja]† in mar‹iage, as welˆ as the [ƒsun]‰ and the [mon]. This was agreŞd, provided that the work was completed within six months. In order to avoid honouring the agre†ment, [„Loki]Ç transformed himself into a mare to lure away the giant's magic horse, [ŠSvadilfari]à. The job was therefore not completed on time, and the gods evaded the payment.

The plain of […Idavol]¥ is the centre of Asgard. The Ã†sir me‘t there for discus“ions on important is”ues: the male gods me‰t in a halƒ calƒed [‰Gladsheim], and the female gods in a halƒ calƒed [ˆVingÃ³lf]. They also met daily at the [‚Wel‡ of Urd]Š, beneath [Yg…drasil]†. 

