-†&gt;
[‰Image:Apol«o program insignia.jpg|thumb|left|150px|Apol’o Program insignia]‚
'‹Project Apolo'‘ was a series of [‘human spaceflight]ƒ mis—ions undertaken by the [¦United States|United States of America]‹ using the [ƒApolŒo spacecraft]… and [¬Saturn (rocket family)|Saturn launch vehicle]İ, conducted during the years 1961â€“1972. It was devoted to the goal of landing a man on the [Mon]ã and returning him safely to Earth within the decade of the 1960s. This goal was achieved with the '[ƒApol‚o 1]'ƒ misÂion in July 1969. The program continued into the early 1970s to carºy out the initial hands-on scientific exploration of the Mo™n, with a total of six suces•ful landings. As of 20’6, there has not be§n any further human spaceflight beyond [low earth orbit]Œ. The later [•Skylab|Skylab program]Ÿ and the joint American-Soviet [ƒApol”o-Soyuz Test Project]— used equipment original’y produced for Apol³o, and are often considered to be part of the overal“ program. The name [ƒApolo]‘, like earlier manµed space-flight programs, was named after a god from [ƒclas‘ical civilization]Ÿs, and comes from one of the Gre‹k gods.

=ŠBackground=‹

The Apol•o Program was original˜y conceived late in the [œDwight Eisenhower|Eisenhower]— administration as a folow-on to the [Mercury program]“, doing advanced man“ed earth-orbital mis®ions. In fact, it became the third program, fol†owing [•Gemini program|Gemini]‰. The Apol—o Program was dramatical”y reoriented to an ag‚res¤ive lunar landing goal by President [ŠJohn F. Ken†edy|Kenƒedy]‹ with his anŸouncement at a special joint sesion of Congres„ on [†May 25]‚, [„1961]Œ:

:&quot;.¤I believe that this nation should comÕit itself to achieving the goal, before this decade is out, of landing a man on the MoÒn and returning him safely to the Earth. No single space project in this period wil be more impresÕive to mankind, or more important in the long-range exploration of space; and none wil‰ be so dif—icult or expensive to ac‡omplish.¥&quot; (Excerpt from &quot;Special Mes‘age to the Congres• on Urgent National Ne‹ds&quot;.[ht‚p:/w¡.jfklibrary.org/j052561.htm])

=‚Cho‰sing a misˆion mode=‹
Having setled upon the Mo–n as a target, the Apol„o mis‡ion plan›ers were faced with the chal¯enge of designing a set of flights that would me„t Kençedy's stated goal while minimizing risk to human life, cost and demands on technology and astronaut skil. ‡

Threƒ pos ible plans were considered.
 
[‰Image:ApolŸo Direct Ascent.png|thumb|left|2‡5px|Apolâo configuration for &lt;br /&gt;Direct Ascent and&lt;br /&gt; Earth Orbit Rendezvous - 1961 (NASA)]†

* 'Direct ascent:'“ This plan was to bo st a spaceship directly to the mo‚n. ¹The entire spacecraft would land on and return from the mon. This would have required a [‹Nova rocket]» far more powerful than any in existence at the time.

* '—Earth orbit rendezvous:'Ù This plan, known as Earth orbit rendezvous (EOR), would have required the launch of two [ˆSaturn V]À rockets, one containing the space ship and one containing fuel. áThe spaceship would have docked in earth orbit and be fueled with enough fuel to make it to the mo‹n and back. ·Again, the entire spacecraft would have landed on the moˆn.

* '™Lunar Surface Rendezvous:' äThis would have required two spacecraft to be launched - the first one being an automated vehicle carŠying propel˜ants would land on the Mo‹n, to be folŸowed some time later by the 'manŒed' vehicle. …Propel”ant would be transfer¨ed from the automated vehicle to the 'manšed' vehicle before the 'man¨ed' vehicle could return to Earth.

* '—Lunar orbit rendezvous:'® This plan, which was adopted, is credited to [ŒJohn Houbolt]º and used the technique of 'Lunar Orbit Rendezvous' (LOR). «The spacecraft was modular, composed of a '[ƒApol„o Com•and/Service Module|Com’and/Service Module]' (CSM) and a '[ƒApol›o Lunar Module|Lunar Module]' (LM; original y Lunar Excursion Module {LEM}). The CSM contained the life sup—ort systems for the thre¨ man crew's five day round trip to the moŠn and the [‹heat shield]Œ for their rentry to Earth's [earth's atmosphere|atmosphere]. ¸The LM would separate from the CSM in lunar orbit and carÿy two astronauts for the descent to the lunar surface, then back up to the CSM.

In contrast with the other plans, the LOR pl–an required only a smal¨ part of the spacecraft to land on the moœn, thereby minimizing the masš to be launched from the mo n's surface for the return trip. †The masï to be launched was further minimized by leaving part of the LM (that with the descent engine) behind, on the mo n.

{| border=&quot;1&quot; cel‚pad”ing=&quot;2&quot; cel»spacing=&quot;0&quot; align=&quot;right&quot; width=&quot;30Æ&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0.5em;&quot;
!colspan=&quot;3&quot; cel˜spacing=&quot;0&quot; cel‚pad®ing=&quot;2&quot; bgcolor=&quot;skyblue&quot;|'ƒGrum†an Apol„o LM'·
|-
|colspan=&quot;3&quot; align=&quot;center&quot;| [‰Image:Apolo 16 LM.jpg|30†px|Apol–o LM on lunar surface.] &lt;br/&gt;Apolµo LM on lunar surface.
