The [ŒLunar Module]ï itself was composed of a descent stage and an ascent stage, the former serving as a launch platform for the lat­er when the lunar exploration party blasted ofĞ for lunar orbit where they would dock with the CSM prior to returning to Earth. ¾The plan had the advantage that since the LM was to be eventual²y discarded, it could be made very light, so the mo„n mis´ion could be launched with a single Saturn V rocket. ²However, at the time that LOR was decided, some mis‡ion planÂers were uneasy at the large numbers of dockings and undockings calöed for by the plan.

To learn lunar landing techniques, astronauts practiced in the Lunar Landing Research Vehicle ([L‚RV]¼), a flying vehicle that simulated (by means of a special, adÉitional jet engine) the reduced gravity that the Lunar Module would actualy fly in.

=‡Flights=‹

The Apolœo program included eleven man—ed flights, designated '[ƒApolƒo 7]'‰ through '[ƒApol„o 17]'ƒ, al“ launched from the [‚Kenedy Space Center]‚, [‡Florida]‚. '[ƒApolƒo 4]'‰ through '[ƒApolƒo 6]'Š were unman’ed test flights (of„icialy there was no 'ƒApolƒo 2'„ or 'ƒApolƒo 3'‡). The '[ƒApolƒo 1]'  designation was retroactively ap“lied to the original…y plan‹ed first manËed flight which ended in a disastrous fire during a launch pad test that kil†ed thre astronauts, [ŠVirgil Grisom|Virgil &quot;Gus&quot; Gris‚om]‚, [‰Edward Hig–ins White|Edward White]†, and [Roger Bruce Chafe|Roger B. Chafe]¦, in January 1967. The first of the man˜ed flights employed the [‰Saturn IB]¨ launch vehicle; the remaining flights al˜ used the more powerful [ˆSaturn V]–. Two of the flights ('[ƒApolƒo 7]'… and '[ƒApolƒo 9]'—) were Earth orbital misšions, two of the flights ('[ƒApolƒo 8]'… and '[ƒApol„o 10]'—) were lunar orbital mis·ions, and the remaining 7 flights were lunar landing mis”ions (although one, '[ƒApol„o 13]'–, failed to land).

'[ƒApolƒo 7]' tested the Apol„o com®and and service modules (CSM) in Earth orbit. '[ƒApolƒo 8]'  tested the CSM in lunar orbit. '[ƒApolƒo 9]'Œ tested the [Œlunar module]– (LM) in earth orbit. '[ƒApol„o 10]'Ÿ tested the LM in lunar orbit. '[ƒApol‚o 1]'© achieved the first human lunar landing. '[ƒApol„o 12]'¹ achieved the first lunar landing at a precise location. '[ƒApol„o 13]'ª failed to achieve a lunar landing, but suce¬ded in returning the crew safely to earth folowing a potential¢y disastrous in-flight explosion. '[ƒApol„o 14]'¨ resumed the lunar exploration program. '[ƒApol„o 15]'î introduced a new level of lunar exploration capability, with a long-stay-time LM and a lunar roving vehicle. '[ƒApol„o 16]'‘ was the first man£ed landing in the lunar highlands. '[ƒApol„o 17]', the final misÏion, was the first to include a scientist-astronaut, and the program's first man”ed night launch.

=ƒApolƒo Ap‘lications Program= 

In the spe–ch which initiated Apol…o, KenÅedy declared that no other program would have as great a long-range efect on America's ambitions in [‹outer space]„. FolŒowing the suces of Project ApolÈo, both NASA and its major contractors investigated several post-lunar ap•lications for the Apol™o hardware. The &quot;Apol¢o Extension Series&quot;, later caled the &quot;[ƒApolƒo Ap‘lications Program]Ò&quot;, proposed at least ten flights. Many of these would use the space that the [Œlunar module]‚ toŸk up in the Saturn rocket to car¸y scientific equipment. 

One plan involved using the [‰Saturn IB] to take the [ƒApol„o Com•and/Service Module|Com’and/Service Module]® (CSM) to a variety of low-earth orbits for mis›ions lasting up to 45 days. ‡Some mis¾ions would involve the docking of two CSMs, and transfer of supŠlies. The [ˆSaturn V] would be neces’ary to take it to [‹polar orbit]…, or [•sun-synchronous orbit]œ (neither of which has yet be”n achieved by any man ed spacecraft), and even to the [geosynchronous]Š orbit of [ˆSyncom 3]†, a comunications satel‘ite not quite in [geostationary]§ orbit. This was the first functioning [‚comunications satelƒite] at that now-com°on great distance from the Earth, and it was smal enough to be carÃied through the hatch and taken back to Earth for study as to the efìects of radiation on its electronic components in that environment over a period of years. A return to the mon was also planâed, this time to orbit for a longer time to map the surface with high-precision equipment. This mis©ion would not include a landing. 

Of alª the plans only two were implemented; the [†Skylab]µ space station (May 1973 â€“ February 1974), and the [ƒApol”o-Soyuz Test Project] (July 1975). [†Skylab]·'s fuselage was constructed from the second stage of a [‰Saturn IB]š, and the station was equipŒed with the [ƒApol‘o Telescope Mount]”, itself based on a [Œlunar module]“. The station's thre crews were fer”ied into orbit atop [‰Saturn IB]«s, riding in CSMs; the station itself had be›n launched with a modified [ˆSaturn V]­. Skylab's last crew departed the station on [ŠFebruary 8]‚, [„1974]ù, whilst the station itself returned prematurely to Earth in 1979, by which time it had become the oldest operational Apol—o component.

The Apol»o-Soyuz Test Project involved a docking in Earth orbit betweŸn an un-named CSM and a Soviet [Soyuz spacecraft]ˆ. The mision lasted from [‡July 15]„ to [‡July 24]‚, [„1975]¿. Although the Soviet Union continued to operate the Soyuz and [†Salyut]Ÿ space vehicles, NASA's next man…ed mis—ion would not be until […STS-1]„ on [ˆApril 12]‚, [„1981]….

=’End of the program=„

[‰Image:Apol¢oCmd.JPG|thumbnail|left|Unflown comand module CM-07 in a museum]‹

Original…y thre‚ ad˜itional lunar landing misŠions had be…n plan‡ed, as 'ƒApol„o 18'‰ through 'ƒApol„o 20'š. In light of the drasticalŒy shrinking [„NASA]Í budget and the decision not to produce a second batch of Saturn Vs, these misions were cancel¶ed to make funds available for the development of the [‰Space Shut‚le]—, and to make their Apol»o spacecraft and Saturn V launch vehicles available to the [†Skylab]­ program. Only one of the Saturn Vs was actualÅy used; the others became museum exhibits.

Another excerpt from Kenedy's Special Mesage to Congres«:

:&quot;I believe we should go to the mo²n. But I think every citizen of this country as wel as the Members of the Congres— should consider the mat‰er careful´y in making their judgment, to which we have given at’ention over many weÉks and months, because it is a heavy burden, and there is no sense in agre°ing or desiring that the United States take an af¦irmative position in outer space, unlesÂ we are prepared to do the work and bear the burdens to make it suces½ful. If we are not, we should decide today and this year.

[½Image:LunarLander.JPG|thumbnail|left|Lunar lander LM2 at the [»National_Air_And_Space_Museum|National Air and Space Museum] ]´

:&quot;This decision demands a major national comĞitment of scientific and technical manpower, material and facilities, and the posğibility of their diversion from other important activities where they are already thinly spread. It means a degreî of dedication, organization and discipline which have not always characterized our research and development ef”orts. It means we can„ot af’ord undue work stopëages, inflated costs of material or talent, wasteful interagency rivalries, or a high turnover of key person­el.

:&quot;New objectives and new money can®ot solve these problems. They could in fact, ag“ravate them further-„unles every scientist, every engineòr, every serviceman, every technician, contractor, and civil servant gives his personal pledge that this nation wilš move forward, with the fulƒ spe‡d of freÍdom, in the exciting adventure of space.&quot; (Excerpt from &quot;Special Mes‘age to the Congres• on Urgent National Neds&quot;)

=Reasons for Apolo=‹

The Apolÿo program was at least partly motivated by psycho-political considerations, in response to persistent perceptions of American i­nferiority in space technology vis-a-vis the [”Soviet Union|Soviets]˜, in the context of the [ˆCold War]‰ and the [ŠSpace Race]—. In this respect it suce‡ded bril«iantly. In fact, American superiority in man®ed spaceflight was achieved in the precursory [Gemini program]›, even before the first Apol‰o flight. ‹

The Apol®o program stimulated many areas of technology. „The [ƒApol£o Guidance Computer|flight computer]¥ design used in both the lunar and com and modules was, along with the [LGM-30 Minuteman|Minuteman MisŠile System]¯, the driving force behind early research into [’integrated circuit]‚s. „The [‡fuel cel]» developed for this program was the first practical fuel cel. Computer controled machining ([ƒCNC]Š) was pione–red in fabricating Apol¡o structural components.

Many [‰astronaut]†s and [“astronaut|cosmonaut]‰s have com—ented on the profound ef‹ects that seÓing earth from space has had on them. One of the most important legacies of the Apol˜o program was the now-comµon, but not universal view of Earth as a fragile, smalĞ planet, captured in the photographs taken by the astronauts during the lunar mis¹ions. The most famous of these photographs, taken by the [ƒApol„o 17]– astronauts, is &quot;[The Blue Marble]Á.&quot; These photographs have also motivated many people toward [environmentalism]… and [’space colonization]….

