The original pre-lunar landing program was more conservative but, as the 'al¬-up' test flights for the Saturn V proved sucesŒful, some misÒions were deleted. The revised schedule published in October 1967 had the first man†ed Apol”o CSM earth orbit mis…ion ('ƒApolƒo 7'„) folĞowed by an Earth Orbit Rendezvous of the CSM and LM launched on two Saturn 1Bs ('ƒApolƒo 8'„) fol¹owed by a Saturn V launched CSM on a Large Earth Orbit Mis…ion ('ƒApolƒo 9'„) fol¡owed by the Saturn V launched dresŸ rehearsal in Lunar Orbit with 'ƒApol„o 10'‹. By the sum¸er of 1968 it became clear to program managers that a ful¯y functional LM would not be available for the 'ƒApolƒo 8'ƒ mis³ion. Rather than perform a simple earth orbiting mis˜ion, they chose to send 'ƒApolƒo 8' around the moïn during Christmas. The original idea for this switch was the brainchild of George Low. Although it has often beÄn claimed that this change was made as a direct response to Soviet at—empts to fly a piloted [‘Zond program|Zond]˜ spacecraft around the mo«n, there is no evidence that this was actual‹y the case. †NASA ofËicials were aware of the Soviet Zond flights, but the timing of the Zond misions does not cor‰espond wel— with the extensive writen record from NASA about the 'ƒApolƒo 8'Š decision. ¢It is relatively certain that the 'ƒApolƒo 8'ò decision was primarily based upon the LM schedule, rather than fear of the Soviets beating the Americans to the mo†n.

