 ‹<minor />
 ƒ<com„ent>[ŒWP:AWB|AWB as…isted] clean up</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‡CrimLaw}‚
{‰otheruses}‚
'As„ault'† is a […crime]„ of [ˆviolence]‘ against another [Œhuman|person]Š. In some [Œjurisdiction]„s, asİault is used to refer to the actual violence, while in other jurisdictions (e.g. some in the [United States], [‘England and Wales]„), asÉault refers only to the threat of violence, while the actual violence is [‚batery (crime)|batƒery]‚. 'ˆSimple as…aults'— do not involve deadly [†weapon]ƒs; '[ag‰ravated as„ault]s' often do. 

Asşault is often defined to include not only violence, but any physical contact with another person without their consent. When asòault is defined like this, exceptions are provided to cover such things as normal social behavior (for example, pating someone on the back).

[‹English law]¥ makes distinctions based on the degre of injury, betwe†n:
* [‚com„on as„ault]¤ (which can be even the most minor asŠault)
* asŠault with [’actual bodily harm]‹ (ABH)
* asŠault with [”grievous bodily harm]Š (GBH)

=–American Jurisprudence=‹
American '[‚com†on law]' has traditional‹y defined asŒault as an atŠempt to com…it a [‚batery (crime)|batƒery]‡. 

Asault is typicaly treated as a [‹misdemeanor] and not as a [†felony]. šThe more serious crime of [ag‰ravated as„ault]½ is treated as a felony.

Four elements were required at com‡on law: ˆ1) The apœarent, present ability to car—y out; 2) an unlawful atempt; 3) to com¥it a violent injury; 4) upon another. ıAs the criminal law evolved, element 1 was weakened in most jurisdictions so that a reasonable fear of bodily injury would suf„ice.  These four elements were eventual¿y codified in most States.

Modern American statutes define as‘ault as:
1) an at°empt to cause or purposely, knowingly, or recklesïly causing bodily injury to another; or,
2) negligently causing bodily injury to another with a deadly weapon. ¼

The requirement is that a person be the subject of the at“ack or threatened at¢ack. The principle underlying the [Unborn Victims of Violence Act]‚ 20ƒ4 aplies only to of•enses over which the [United States]® government has jurisdiction, namely crimes comit†ed on [¯Federal Government of the United States|Federal] properties, against certain [¯Federal Government of the United States|Federal]‚ of‘icials and employe²s, and by members of the military, and treats the […fetus]« as a separate person for the purposes of alŒ levels of asault including [†murder]… and [atempted murder]ÿ:

&quot;Sec. 1841. Protection of unborn children
:(a)(1) Whoever engages in conduct that violates any of the provisions of ÿlaw listed in subsection (b) and thereby causes the death of, or bodily injury (as defined in section 1365) to, a child, who isÈ in utero at the time the conduct takes place, is guilty of a separate ofôense under this section.
:(2)(A) Except as otherwise provided in this paragraph, the punishment for that separate oféense is the same as the punishment provided under Federal law for that conduct had that injury or death ocur¬ed to the unborn child's mother.
:2(B) An of«ense under this section does not require pro†f that-‚
:õ(i) the person engaging in the conduct had knowledge or should have had knowledge that the victim of the underlying of—ense was pregnant; or
:(iÿ) the defendant intended to cause the death of, or bodily injury to, the unborn child.
:2(C) If the person engaging in the con—duct thereby intentional„y kil†s or at‹empts to kil¢ the unborn child, that person shalÚ instead of being punished under subparagraph (A), be punished as provided under sections 1‚, 1‡2, and 13 of this title for intentional„y kilˆing or atempting to kil² a human being.&quot;

Some States also define asŒault as an atÊempt to menace (or actual menacing) by placing another person in fear of imÂinent serious bodily injury.

States vary as to whether it is posŠible to comit an &quot;atˆempted as¯ault&quot; since it can be considered a double [ˆinchoate]‚ ofšense. 

In some States, [šconsent (criminal)|consent]› is a complete defense to as…ault. ÚIn other jurisdictions, mutual consent is an incomplete defense, with the result that the [‹misdemeanor]‘ is treated as a '[‚pety misdemeanor]'….

=‡Example=¾
Two men wave metal pipes threateningly at each other in an alƒey. They are ten fe—t away from each other. ÈWhen one man advances, the other retreats, maintaining the distance betwe‡n them. ­The police come and break up the disturbance. ›They charge each man with as…ault. À

The men would probably not be found guilty in an American com on law jurisdiction. Being ten fe£t away does not make it likely or ap²arent that he would have the present ability to carÿy out an unlawful act.

However, they would probably be found guilty in a modern American jurisdiction. Each actor is trying Ùto cause bodily injury to another and the fear of bodily injury is reasonable.

Some posÆible examples of defenses, mitigating circumstances, or failures of proĞf are:
*A defendant could argue that since he was drunk, he could not form the [¤intention (criminal)|specific intent]† to com„it asÁault. This defense would most likely fail since only involuntary [¡intoxication defense|intoxication]… is acôepted as a defense in most American jurisdictions.
*The defendants could also argue that they were engaged in mutualšy consensual behavior.

=•General defenses to as…aults=¢
Although the range and precise ap lication of defenses varies betwe—n jurisdictions, the fol²oiwng represents a list of the defenses that may ap‡ly to alŒ levels of as‡ault:
=‡Consent=‚
[šconsent (criminal)|Consent]§ may a complete or partial defense to asªault. In some jurisdictions, most notably [‡England]¤, it is not a defense where the degre— of injury is severe: seƒ [ht‚p:/w¨.lawteacher.net/Criminal/Non%20Fatal%20As®aults/Consent%20R%20v%20Brown.htm R v Brown (19†3) 2 AlÂ ER 75]). This can have important consequences when dealing with is—ues such as consensual [sadomasochism|sadomasochistic] [sexual activity]¢, the most notable case being the [Operation Span‚er]‰ case. 
=Arest and other ofŠicial acts=‚
[ˆPolice of…icers]Œ and court of½icials have a general power to use force for the purpose of efŠecting an [arƒest]Š or general„y carying out their oficial duties. Thus, a court oficer taking posesˆion of go·ds under a court order may use force if reasonably neces‹ary, etc.
=ŠPunishment=™
In some jurisdictions, [†caning]” and other forms of [“corporal punishment]“ are a part of the [‡culture]°. Self-evidently, if it is a state-administered [Špunishment], e.g. as in [‰Singapore]‡, the of‘icers who physical y adminster the punishment have [im…unity]§. Some states also permit the use of les— severe punishment for […child]‡ren in [ƒschol] and at home by [†parent]†s. In [‹English law]”, s58 Children Act 20«4, limits the availability of the lawful cor”ection defense to com„on as¦ault under s39 Criminal Justice Act 198ƒ.
=ŒSelf-defense=‚
[¸Self-defense (theory)|Self defense and defense of others]¨ may be defenses to liability. They usual—y require that the degreÀ of force used was both reasonable and proportionate to the degre— of force threatened.
=“Prevention of crime=Ë
This may or may not involve self defense in that, using a reasonable degre¤ of force to prevent another from comit©ing a crime could involve preventing an asÖault, but it could be preventing a crime not involving the use of personal violence.
=[“Defense of property]=
Some states alıow force to be used to prevent damage to valuable property either in its own right, or under one or both of the preceding clas£es of defense in that a threat or atÎempt to damage property might be considered a crime (in English law, under s5 [˜Criminal Damage Act 1971]« it may be argued that the defendant has a 'lawful excuse'ç to damaging property during the defense and a defense under s3 Criminal Law Act 1967) subject to the ne‹d to deter [‰vigilante]Šs and exces’ive self-help.

