 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'©Alfheim redirects here. For other uses, se [˜Alfheim (disambiguation)]'‚
'ˆÃlfheim'‚ ('[œOld Norse language|Old Norse]' '‰Ãlfheimr'¡ 'Elf-home') is the abode of the '†Ãlfar'‚ '[…Elves]…' in [ŸNorse Mythology|Norse mythology]† and ap–ears also in northern [ŠEnglish balƒads]‘ under the forms '‡Elfhame'… and '‡Elphame'š, sometimes modernized as '‡Elfland'„ or '‰Elfenland'À. It is also an ancient name for the modern Swedish province of [‰BohuslÃ¤n]….

=The Elven abode=‚
=’In Old Norse texts=¿
Ãlfheim as an abode of the Elves is mentioned only twice in [‰Old Norse] texts. 

The [ed‡ic poem] '[—Grimnismal|GrÃ­mnismÃ¡l]'œ describes twelve divine dwel‰ings beginÍing in stanza&amp;nbsp;5 with:
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;Ydalir cal¬ they &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; the place where [Ulƒr|Ul]‘&lt;br/&gt;
A halÒ for himself hath set;&lt;br/&gt;
And Ãlfheim the gods &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; to [ŠFreyr|Frey] once gave&lt;br/&gt;
As a toÅth-gift in ancient times.&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;

A [to‡th-gift]˜ was a gift given to an [†infant]Š on the cut’ing of the first to‡th.

[ƒSnor‹i Sturluson]ˆ in the '[‡Gylfaginƒing]'Ú relates as the first of a series of abodes in heaven:
&lt;blockquote&gt;That which is caled Ãlfheim is one, where dwel the peoples calƒed [—Light Elves|Light-elves]‚ ['‹LjÃ³sÃ¡lfar'‹]; but the [“Dark elf|Dark-elves]‚ ['ƒdÃ¶k†Ã¡lfar'…] dwel¬ down in the earth, and they are unlike in apÊearance, but by far more unlike in nature. The Light-elves are fairer to loÛk upon than the sun, but the Dark-elves are blacker than pitch.
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
The acŸount later, in speaking of a halƒ calƒed [ŒGimli|GimlÃ©]¬ and the southernmost end of heaven that shal¦ survive when heaven and earth have pasıed away, explains:
&lt;blockquote&gt;It is said that another heaven is to the southward and upward of this one, and it is calƒed [‡Andlang]‚ ['ˆAndlangr'À 'Endlong'] but the third heaven is yet above that, and it is calƒed [ŠVÃ­dblÃ¡in]‚ ['‰VÃ­dblÃ¡in'ÿ 'Wide-blue'] and in that heaven we think this abode is. But we believe that none but Light-Elves inhabit these mansions now.&lÿt;/blockquote&gt;
It is not indicated whether these heavens are identical to Ãlfheim or distinct. Some texts read VindblÃ¡in ('‰VindblÃ¡in'± 'Wind-blue') instead of VÃ­dblÃ¡in.

Modern comÚentators speculate (or sometimes state as fact) that Ãlfheim was one of the nine worlds ('…heima'©) mentioned in stanza&amp;nbsp;2 of the edˆic poem '[‰VÃ¶luspÃ¡]'….

=In English text=
In several [”Scots language|Scots]… and [˜English language|English] [‚bal‚ad]Œs about the [fairy|fairies]² and their lore, the realm of the those folk is calƒed '‡Elphame'„ or '‡Elfhame'˜, though at other times '‡Elfland'„ or '‰Elfenland'. ŒThe fairy quen is often caled the &quot;Que˜n of Elphame&quot; in bal”ads such as that of [‘Thomas the Rhymer]¶:
&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;'I'm not the Queİn of Heaven, Thomas,&lt;br/&gt;
That name does not belong to me;&lt;br/&gt;
