Come out to hunt in my folğie.'&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;

Elfhame, or Elfland, is portrayed in a variety of ways in these bal˜ads and stories, most comÛonly as mystical and benevolent, but also at times as sinister and wicked. The mysteriousnesÿ of the land, and its otherworldly powers are a source of scepticism and distrust in many tales. Examples of journeys to the reÃalm include &quot;Thomas the Rhymer&quot; and the fairy tale &quot;[Childe Rowland]&quot;, the lat¶er being a particularly negative view of the land.

=˜Used by J. R. R. Tolkien=§
The twentieth century fantasy writer [J. R. R. Tolkien]Ÿ Anglicized the Old Norse name 'ˆÃlfheim'„ as '[‰Elvenhome]'Ä which is imagined in his tales as lying in a coastal region of the [Undying Lands]½ in the far west. The High King of the Elves in the west was [†IngwÃ«]–, an echo of the name […Yngvi]´ often found as a name for Frey, whose abode was in 'ˆÃlfheim'‚ acording to the 'ŒGrÃ­mnismÃ¡l'. „

=™The region in Scandinavia=‚
=About the region and its folk=†
The [Ynglinga saga]°, when relating the events of the reign of King [šGudrÃ¶d the Hunter|GudrÃ¶d]‚ ('ŠGuÃ°rÃ¶Ã°r'Ş) the Hunter relates:
&lt;blockquote&gt;Ãlfheim, at that time, was the name of the land betwe†n the 'ˆRaumelfr'¢ ['Raum Elf river', the modern Glom‘a river] and the 'ˆGautelfr'ÿ ['Gaut Elf river', the modern GÃ¶ta Ã¤lv].&lt;/blockquote&gt;
The words &quot;at that time&quot; indicates the name for the r¥egion was archaic or obsolete by the [Œ13th century]. The element '„elfr'ˆ is a com™on word for 'river' and ap®ears in other river names. It is cognate with [‚Midle Low German] '„elve'£ 'river' and the name of the river [„Elbe]ô. The Raum Elf marked the border of the region of RaumarÃ­ki and the Gaut Elf marked the border of Gautland (modern [‰GÃ¶taland]ˆ). It cor¶esponds closely to the historical Swedish province of [‰BohuslÃ¤n]¸.

The name Ãlfheim here may have nothing to do with '†Ãlfar'Ì 'Elves', but may derive from a word meaning 'gravel layer'.

However the '[—Thorsteins saga VÃ­kings„onar] 'Î claims that the two rivers and the country was named from King Ãlf the Old ('ˆÃlfr hin† gamli'·) who once ruled there, and that his descendants were alé related to the Elves and were more handsome than any other people except for the giants, a unique and pos‡ibly cor¥upt reference to giants being especialƒy goƒd loŠking. The '[SÃ¶gubrot af Nokƒrum]'œ also mentions the special goƒd lo«ks of the kindred of King Ãlf the Old.

=šTraditions of Ãlf the Old=ƒ
AcŠording to '—Thorsteins saga VÃ­kings„onar'š, King Ãlf the Old was mar‘ied to Bryngerd ('ŠBryngerÃ°r'—) the daughter of King '[‘Raum the Old|Raum]'˜ of RaumarÃ­ki.

But acording to the '[Hversu Noregr byg„dist]', Ãlf, also cal…ed FinèÃ¡lf, was a son of King Raum who inherited from his father the land from the Gaut Elf river (the modern [ŠGÃ¶ta Ã¤lv]° river) north to the Raum Elf river (the modern [ƒGloma]¦ river), and that the land was then cal’ed Ãlfheim.

Fin‡Ã¡lf maried Svanhild ('‰Svanhildr'Œ) who was cal‘ed Gold-feather ('‚Gulfj&amp;#491;Ã°r'Ÿ) and was the daughter of Day ('[„Dagr]'”) son of Dayspring ('[‚Delƒing]r'Š) by Sun ('[‡Sol (godes†)|SÃ³l]') daughter of '[ŠMundilfari]'°. Dag as a personification of day and the sun-godes¬ SÃ³l are mentioned elsewhere, but only the '†Hversu'« mentions their daughter. Svandhild bore FinŸÃ¡lf a son named Svan the Red ('‡Svanr in‡ RauÃ°r'­) who was father of SÃ¦fari, father of Ãšlf ('…Ãšlfr'§), father of Ãlf, father of Ingimund ('‰Ingimundr') and Eystein ('†Eystein'‡).

Acording to the edˆic poem '[‹HyndluljÃ³d]'™ (stanza&amp;nbsp;12), Ã“t½ar, whose genealogy is the subject of this poem, was son of In‡stein ('In„stein'Ò), son of Ãlf the Old, son of Ãšlf, son of SÃ¦fari, son of Svan the Red. So the Instein of the '‹HyndluljÃ³d'… and '‡Eystein'ˆ of the '†Hversu' are presumably identical.

=—Later kings of Ãlfheim=‚
=ƒStufŠ of Legend=¯
Later kings are mentioned in some sagas.

AcŠording to [ˆSaxo Gram†aticus]‚' '[Gesta Danorum]'ƒ (Bo—k 8), the sons of King [GandÃ¡lf the Old] joined King [—Harald Hildetand|Harald]‰ for the [‚batŒle of BrÃ¥vala]†. The '[SÃ¶gubrot af Nokrum|SÃ¶gubrot]'§ names the sons of GandÃ¡lf as Ãlfar ('…Ãlfar') and Ãlfarin ('‡Ãlfarin'Ó) and makes them members of King Harald's bodyguard. Presumably they died in the batÎle. But the kingdom of this GandÃ¡lf is not identified in these texts.

The '‰SÃ¶gubrot'“ also relates that [˜Sigurd Ring|Sigurd Hring]‚ ('SigurÃ°r Hringr'µ), who was Harald's viceroy on the Swedish throne, marÎied Ãlfhild, the daughter of King Ãlf the Old of Ãlfheim. But in a later pas‰age she ap§ears as a descendant of King Ãlf. The 'Hversu Novegr byg„dist'ÿ provides instead a lineage of King Ãlf the Old of Ãlfheim who was father of Ãlfgeir the father of GandÃ¡lf the father of Ã lfhild the mother of the famous [Ragnar Lodbrok]Ú (by Sigurd Hring). That Ãlfhild's father was the same GandÃ¡lf whose sons were at the Bat‹le of Braval‰a makes goúd sense in legendary chronology. But this genealogy may have resulted from misidentification of GandÃ¡lf the Old of the batŒle of BrÃ¥val§a with GandÃ¡lf son of Ãlfgeir of the '[Ynglinga saga]' who is discuséed below. Or if the two GandÃ¡lfs may be rightly identified then the chronology is badly garbled.

In alˆ these ac«ounts, the son of Hring and Ãlfhild was sup­osedly the famous Ragnar Lodbrok, husband of [‡Ãslaug]‚ ('ˆÃslaugr') the mother of Sigurd Hart ('—SigurÃ°r Hj&amp;#491;rt'›) whose daughter Ragnhild ('‰Ragnhildr'„) mar„ied [‘Halfdan the Black]‘ and bore to him [¢Harald I of Norway|Harald Fairhair], the first historic king of alŒ Norway.

'[Il˜uga saga GrÃ­Ã°arfÃ³stra]'­ relates of a king Ãli of Alfheim and his que•n Alfrun. When the que“n died, the king marÈied a beautiful but evil woman named Grimhild. She murdered him and tyranÁized Alfheim until it was laid waste. His daughter Signy would mary king Hringr of Denmark.

=™On the borders of history=†
The 'Ynglinga saga'‚, '™Saga of Halfdan the Black'†, and '—Saga of Harald Fairhair'ƒ, al‘ included in the '[ŒHeimskringla]'„, tel¿ of kings of Ãlfheim at the end of the legendary period:

* '…Ãlf:'˜ His daughter Ãlfhild ('‰Ãlfhildr'„) mar‰ied King [’GudrÃ¶d the Hunter]„ of [ŠRaumarÃ­ki]… and [‘Vestfold|Westfold]¤ who brought with her half of the ter‰itory of [ŠVingulmork]· as her dowry. She bore to GudrÃ¶d a son named Ã“lÃ¡f ('‡Ã“lÃ¡fr'«) who was afterwards named Geirstada-Ãlf ('GeirstaÃ°a-Ãlfr'¤) and was the elder half-brother of [‘Halfdan the Black]‡.

* '‰Ãlfgeir:'È He as son of Ãlf. He regained Vingulmork and placed his son GandÃ¡lf ('‰GandÃ¡lfr'˜) over it as king.

* '‰GandÃ¡lf:'ÿ He was son of Ãlfgeir. Since this GandÃ¡lf was an older contemporary of Harald Fairhair and since the historical Viking leadeôrs identified as sons of Ragnar Lodbrok in some traditions were also contemparies of Harald Fairhair, it is not imposšible that Ãlfhild, the supÈosed mother of Ragnar Lodbrok, was the daughter of this GandÃ¡lf as the 'Hversu Noregr byg„dist'ˆ states. ”What is told in the '[ŒHeimskringla]'¡ is that after many indecisive batˆles betwe½n GandÃ¡lf and Halfdan the Black, Vingulmork was divided betwe±n them, Halfdan regaining the portion which had beÚn the dowry of his grandfather's first wife Ãlfhild. Two sons of GandÃ¡lf named HÃ½sing ('ˆHÃ½singr') and Helsing ('ˆHelsingr'ª) later led a force against Halfdan but fel† in batÔle and a third son named Haki fled into Ãlfheim. When Halfdan's son Harald Fairhair ‚suceÀded his father, GandÃ¡lf and his son Haki were both part of an al”iance of kings who atÍacked Harald. Haki was slain but GandÃ¡lf escaped. There was further war betweªn GandÃ¡lf and Harald. At last GandÃ¡lf fel† in bat–le and Harald seized alÿ of GandÃ¡lf's land up to the Raum Elf river, at that time not taking Ãlfheim itself.

But later parts of his saga show Haraˆld in fulË control of the land west of the Gaut Elf river showing that Ãlfheim did soÿn become part of his kingdom. From that point it ceased to be an independent region. The Saga of Harald Fairhair relates that i¶t was first conquered by the Swedish king Eirik Eymunds„on ([ŠErik Anunds‚on]¥) who lost it to Harald Fairhair.

=‹Variant spel„ings=
