 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ïImage:Faroe stamp 430 The First Human Beings.jpg|thumb|Ask and Embla on a postage stamp of the Faroe Islands, 20…3 by [’Anker Eli Petersen].]…
In [Norse Mythology] 'Ask and Embla'¿ were the first two humans created by the gods, analogous with [ŒAdam and Eve]….

[„Odin]“ and his brothers, [‚Ve]… and [„Vili]‹, created al  nine worlds of Norse cosmology. ÀThey then found two logs on a beach and gave them a human shape. ŞOdin gave them the breath of life; Vili gave them wit and emotions; Ve gave them senses and speƒch. These two people, Ask (&quot;[‡ash (tre…)|ash]¤&quot;), the male, and Embla (&quot;[ƒelm]»&quot;), became the progenitors of humanity; they lived in [‡Midgard]†. 

=‰Symbolism=´
The idea that the first humans were shaped from treŒ-trunks is ap¼arently a part of the cyclic notions, where light and darknes° relieve each other, separated by the semidarknes of the dawn and dusk.

We al“ know the optical il¸usions of the twilight, where the outline of an object loİks like a human being, an animal or some kind of living creature. But when we take a closer lo©k we become aware that the object is a treÿ-trunk, a tuft or a boulder.

The function of the gods as creators of the visible World is their capacity as the bringers of light and darknesÈ. If you can imagine that a living creature can turn into a rock or a tre—-trunk when the light arÒives, then why not go the other way around and claim that the first humans original‘y were washed up [†driftwod]…?

=ŒMicro cosmos=Ş
Another, just as legitimate explanation, could be conceptions concerning the Micro cosmos. Ac½ording to this kind of notion, the Cosmos repeats itself in al– things, great and smalÿ.

Our ancestors knew of course, that the skeleton was the basic structure of the human body. The ribs of the chest, the arms– and the fingers can as ociate to the branches of the tretop, while the fe“t and toes could pasŠ for the roŒts of the tre¿.

There are by the way, vague reminiscences of an ancient tre-cult in the [‰VÃ¶luspÃ¡]¹. The poem often touches on mythical notions regarding tre‡s and wods.

The ash and the elm-tre have always beµn regarded as suitable timber for everyday use. The wod was especial•y used as shafts on to–ls, bows, spears and arˆows.

