 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[™Image:Alabama_River.jpg|30px|thumb|The Alabama River at [Montgomery, Alabama|Montgomery]… in 204]†
The 'Alabama River'‰, in the [’United States|U.S.]Š state of [‡Alabama]“, is formed by the [‚Tal‚apo‹sa River|Tal‚apo‚sa]… and [CoŠsa River|Co‚sa]« rivers, which unite about six miles above [Montgomery, Alabama|Montgomery]. œ

It flows west as far as [”Selma, Alabama|Selma]¶, then southwest until, 
about 45 miles (72 km) from [–Mobile, Alabama|Mobile]•, it unites with the [‡Tombigbe River|Tombigbe] to form the [“Mobile River|Mobile]… and [“Tensas River|Tensas] rivers, which discharge into [ŠMobile Bay]Ä. 

The course of the Alabama is tortuous. Its width varies from 20… to 30‰ yards (20… to 30  m), and its depth from 3 to 7 fe¬t (1 to 2 m). Its length as measured by the [ŸUnited States Geological Survey]É is 312 miles (502 km), and by steamboat measurement, 420 miles (676 km). ‘

The river crosÃes the richest agricultural and timber districts of the state, and [‡railway]„s conect it with the [‡mineral]¢ regions of north central Alabama. ²

The principal tributary of the Alabama is the [ŒCahaba River]ˆ about 20‰ miles (30½ km long, which enters it about 10 miles (16 km) below Selma. «Of the rivers which form the Alabama, the Co†sa crosÎes the mineral region of Alabama, and is navigable for light-draft boats from [Rome, Georgia]¬ (where it is formed by the junction of the [›Oostanaula River|Oostanaula]… and [“Etowah River|Etowah]’ rivers) to about 1Š7 miles (18‹ km) above [—Wetumpka River|Wetumpka]º (about 102 miles below Rome and 26 miles (42 km) below Gre¶nsport), and from Wetumpka to its junction with the Tal‚apo‹sa; the chan•el of the river has be²n considerably improved by the federal government. œ

The navigation of the Tal‚apo¡sa river which has its source in [˜Paulding County, Georgia]›, and is about 250 miles (40› km) long, is prevented by […shoal]Œs and a 60 fo‹t (18 m) fal† at Talase®, a few miles north of its junction with the Coƒsa. ±The Alabama is navigable throughout the year.

