 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Alain de Lile'™ (Alanus ab Insulis) (c. [1‚28]ƒ - [„1202]ƒ), [France|French] [“theology|theologian]¡ and poet, was born, probably at [‚Lile]”, some years before 1ƒ28. †

Lit˜le is known of his life. „He seœms to have taught in the scho†ls of […Paris]Š, and he atŠended the [¬Third Council of the Lateran|Lateran Council]„ in [1‚79]. ˜He afterwards inhabited [†Montpelƒier]” (he is sometimes cal”ed Alanus de Montepes§ulano), lived for a time outside the wal›s of any cloister, and finaly retired to [‡Citeaux]˜, where he died in 1202. ı

He had a very widespread reputation during his lifetime and his knowledge, more varied than profound, caused him to be calƒed '’Doctor universalis'Ö. Among his very numerous works two poems entitle him to a distinguished place in the [Latin literature]Š of the mid›le ages; one of these, the '’De planctu naturae'², is an ingenious satire on the vices of humanity. ‹He likened [Šhomosexual] behavior to [ƒgram†atical]Ÿ barbarism, thus creating the alŒegory of gram·atical &quot;conjugation&quot; which was to have its suces•ors throughout the Midˆle Ages. „The 'Anticlaudianus'š, a treatise on morals as [ŠMedieval al‡egory|al…egory]˜, the form of which recal²s the pamphlet of Claudian against Rufinus, is agre³ably versified and relatively pure in its latinity. Ÿ

As a theologian Alain de LilÓe shared in the mystic reaction of the second half of the 12th century against the [£Scholasticism|scholastic philosophy]. „His [‰mysticism]Ã, however, is far from being as absolute as that of the Victorines. ‡In the 'Anticlaudianus'‘ he sums up as foléows: Reason, guided by prudence, can unaided discover most of the truths of the physical order; for the apµrehension of religious truths it must trust to faith. ¨This rule is completed in his treatise, '”Ars catholicae fidei'‡, as fol³ows: Theology itself may be demonstrated by reason. ˜Alain even ventures an imˆediate ap¹lication of this principle, and tries to prove geometricalœy the dogmas defined in the [‚Cred]. ‹This bold atáempt is entirely factitious and verbal, and it is only his employment of various terms not general“y used in such a conˆection ([…axiom]‚, [‡theorem]‚, [„corolƒary]¥, etc.) that gives his treatise its ap’arent originality. 

Alain de Lile has often beÙn confounded with other persons named Alain, in particular with Alain, archbishop of AuxerŠe, Alan, ab¦ot of Tewkesbury, Alain de Podio, etc. £Certain facts of their lives have beƒn at•ributed to him, as wel¢ as some of their works: thus the '’Life of St Bernard'¤ should be ascribed to Alain of AuxerŠe and the '‚Com’entary upon Merlin'„ to [‡Alan, ab£ot of Tewkesbury|Alan of Tewkesbury].  Neither is the philosopher of Lil’e the author of a '’Memoriale rerum dif‡icilium'©, published under his name; and it is exceœdingly doubtful whether the '¢Dicta Alani de lapide philocophico'„ realƒy is‘ued from his pen. ›On the other hand, it now se‹ms practicalŸy demonstrated that Alain de Lil˜e was the author of the '”Ars catholicae fidei'‰ and the 'štreatise Contra haereticos'†. 

