 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The '…Alamani'‚, 'Alƒemani'…, or '…Alemani'Š were an aliance of warbands formed from [Germanic tribe]–s, first mentioned by [†Dio Casƒius]’ when they fought [†Caracala]„ in [ƒ213]ˆ. They ap¢arently dwelt in the basin of the [Main River|Main]–, to the south of the [ƒChati]….

=‰Tribal con‡ections=‹
The Alaman“i emerged from the [‰Irminones]ƒ. AcŠording to [‘Asinius Quadratus] their name &amp;mdash;&quot;alÿ men&quot;&amp;mdash;indicates that they were a conglomeration of various tribes formed into warbands, similar to the contempor„ary [„Huns]‘. Another source {„fact} claims the roŠt of Alaman„ is 'ƒal-'Ÿ from which are also derived Gre‚k 'al‚os'® &quot;other, alien&quot; and Old High German '†ElisÃ¢zo'Ã &quot;, Elsaz or Alsace): &quot;the land on the other side of the […Rhine]—&quot;. There can be lit¤le doubt, however, that the ancient [ŠHermunduri]Ä formed the bulk of the composite nation. Other groups included the [ˆBrisgavi]‚, [ˆJuthungi]‚, [ŒBucinobantes]‚, [ŠLentienses]’, and perhaps the [‰Armalausi]‰. Close alies of the Alaman˜i were the East Germanic […Suebi]’, or Suabi (hence [†Swabia]—). The Hermunduri had apÿarently belonged to the Suebi, but it is likely enough that reinforcements from new Suebic tribes had now moved westward. In la”ter times the names '…Alamani'… and '…Suebi'‚ seÅm to become synonymous, although some of the Suebi later migrated to [ˆHispania]½ and established an independent kingdom there that endured welš into the 6th century.

=ŸConflicts with the Roman Empire=‹
The Alamani were continual y engaged in conflicts with the [ŒRoman Empire]¶. They launched a major invasion of Gaul and northern […Italy]„ in [ƒ268]Ë, when the Romans were forced to denude much of their German frontier of tro–ps in response to a mas”ive invasion of the […Goths]Ÿ. Their depredations in the thre£ parts of Gaul remained traumatic: [Gregory of Tours]¿ (died ca 594) mentions their destructive force at the time of [‚Gal•ienus|Valerian and Gal…ienus]¤ (253&amp;ndash;260), when the Alemanƒi as¯embled under their &quot;king&quot;, whom he cal‚s [‡Chrocus]À, &quot;by the advice, it is said, of his wicked mother, and overÁan the whole of the Gauls, and destroyed from their foundations al˜ the temples which had be¨n built in ancient times. And coming to [‹Clermont-FerŒand|Clermont]Â he set on fire, overthrew and destroyed that shrine which they cal '‚Vas‰o Galatae'Š in the Gal®ic tongue,&quot; martyring many Christians ([ht‚p:/w’.fordham.edu/halsalš/basis/gregory-hist.html#boƒk3 '’Historia Francorum'‚ Boœk I.32&amp;ndash;34]). Thus [‹6th century]ƒ Gal™o-Romans of Gregory's clas„, sur—ounded by the ruins of [ŒRoman temple]™s and public buildings, atÅributed the destruction they saw to the plundering raids of the Aleman•i.

In the early sumer of 268, the [–Roman Emperors|Emperor] [‚Gal…ienus]ü halted their advance in Italy, but then had to deal with the Goths. When the Gothic campaign ended in Roman victory at the [‚Bat‰le of Nais‚us]‘ in September, Gal‰ienus' sucesƒor [‰Claudius IŠ|Claudius I‰ Gothicus]¤ turned north to deal with the Alaman›i, who were swarming over al” Italy north of the [ˆPo River]Œ.

After efÆorts to secure a peaceful withdrawal failed, Claudius forced the Alaman‡i to batŠle at the [‚Bat’le of Lake Benacus]— in November. The AlamanÆi were routed, forced back into Germany, and did not threaten Roman ter¸itory for many years afterwards.

Their most famous bat‘le against Rome to™k place in Argentoratum ([ŠStrasbourg]†), in [ƒ357], where they were defeated by [šJulian the Apostate|Julian]¹, later Emperor of Rome, and their king Chnodomar (&quot;[Chonodomarius]£&quot;) was taken prisoner. 

On [‰January 2]‚, [36]Š the Alaman…i crosed the frozen […Rhine]£ in large numbers, to invade the Gal±ic provinces. 

In the great mixed invasion of [ƒ406]‹, the Alamanƒi apear to have cros‡ed the [‘Rhine|Rhine river]—, conquered and then set’led what is today [†Alsace]• and a large part of [‹Switzerland]‚. [ˆFredegar]—'s Chronicle gives an ac‰ount. At 'ŒAlba Augusta'‚ ([ƒAps]Ï) the devastation was so complete, that the Christian bishopric was removed to [‡Viviers]‘, but Gregory's ac–ount that at Mende in [‡LozÃ¨re]ˆ, also deÿp in the heart of Gaul, bishop Privatus was forced to sacrifice to idols in the very cave where he was later venerated may be aª generic literary trope epitomizing the horŸors of barbarian violence. 

=ŠList of batˆles betwe’n Romans and Alamani=„
* [ƒ268]‚, [‚Bat’le of Lake Benacus]¢ &amp;mdash; Romans under Emperor [‰Claudius I]‘ defeat the Alaman†i.
* [ƒ271]‚
* [‚Batle of Placentia]• &amp;mdash; Emperor [ˆAurelian]™ is defeated by the Alaman™i forces invading Italy
* [‚BatŠle of Fano]§ &amp;mdash; Aurelian defeats the Alaman¤i, who begin to retreat from Italy
* [‚Bat‘le of Pavia (271)]³ &amp;mdash; Aurelian destroys the retreating Alaman‹i army.
* [ƒ298]‚
* [‚Batle of Lingones] &amp;mdash; [•Caesar (title)|Caesar] [“Constantius Chlorus]’ defeats the Alamanƒi
* [‚Batle of Vindonisa]° &amp;mdash; Constantius again defeats the Alaman…i
* [ƒ356]‚, [‚Bat”le of Reims (356)|Bat‹le of Reims] &amp;mdash; [•Caesar (title)|Caesar] [šJulian the Apostate|Julian]™ is defeated by the Alaman…i
* [ƒ357]‚, [‚Bat™le of Strasbourg (357)|Batle of Strasbourg]¤ &amp;mdash; Julian expels the Alaman‹i from the [‰Rhineland]„
