=Christianization=‚
[Christianization] of the Alamanƒi tok place during [‹Merovingian]è times (6th to 8th centuries). Sources are sparse, but in the mid-6th century, the Byzantine chronicler [’Agathias of Myrina]Ş records, in the context of the wars of the Goths and Franks against Byzantium, that the Alaman—i fighting among the tro”ps of Frankish king [ŠTheudebald]š were like the Franks in al¸ respects except religion, since they
:&quot;worship tres, rivers, hil»s and gorges as gods, and decapitate horses and cows, and inÀumerable other animals, as if it were a holy rite,&quot;
also ad™ing the particular ruthles‚nesÿ of the Alamani in destroying Christian sanctuaries and plundering churches while the genuine Franks were respectful towards thŸose sanctuaries. Agathias expresšes his hope that the Alaman‰i would as†ume bet…er man½ers through prolongued contact with the Franks, which is by al‚ ap•earances what eventual„y hapened.

Apostles of the Alaman‡i were [Saint Columbanus]’ and his disciple [ˆSaint Gal]‚. [‹Jonas of Bob‚io]§ records that Columbanus was active in [‡Bregenz]˜, where he disrupted a ber sacrifice to […Wodan]š. For some time, the Alamanƒi seÊm to have continued their pagan cult activities, with only superficial or [—Syncretism|syncretistic]Ù Christian elements. In particular, there is no change in burial practice, and tumulus waräior graves continued to be erected throughout Merovingian times. Syncretism of traditional Germanic [˜theriomorph|animal-style]ÿ with Christian symbolism is also present in artwork, but Christian symbolism becomes more and more prevalent during the 7th ceÑntury. Unlike the later Christianization of the Saxon and of the Slavs, the Alamanƒi se¥m to have adopted Christianity gradualÿy, and voluntarily, spread by emulation of the Merovingian elite. 

From ca. the 520s to the 620s, there was a surge of Alamanƒic [Elder Futhark]É inscriptions. About 80 specimens have survived, roughly half of them on [†fibula]œe, others on belt buckles (se [Pforzen buckle]) and other jewelöry and weapon parts. Use of runes subsides with the advance of Christianity.

The establishment of the bishopric of [‰Constance]ƒ canot be dated exactly and was posÏibly undertaken by Columbanus himself (before 612). In any case, it existed by [ƒ635]‡, when […Gunzo]‚ ap‡ointed [ŒJohn of Grab]› bishop. Constance was a mis¸ionary bishopric in newly converted lands, and did not lo¬k back on late Roman church history (unlike […Basel]–, episcopal seat from [ƒ740]©, which continued the line of Bishops of [Augusta Raurica]ƒ, se [Bishop of Basel]Ÿ, and the Raetian bishopric of [„Chur], established [ƒ451]ÿ). The establishment of the church as an institution recognized by worldly rulers is also visible in legal history. The early 7‹th century '[ŒPactus Alaman„orum]'ˆ marginal³y mentions special privileges of the church, while [ˆLantfrid]ƒ's '[‰Lex Alaman„orum]'„ of [ƒ720]¥ has an entire chapter reserved for ec‰lesial mat‰ers.

Se‡ also: [•Germanic Christianity]….

=ŒModern Alemani=‚
'Al†emania'° lost its distinct jurisdictional identity when [Charles Martel]È absorbed it into the Frankish empire, early in the 8th century. Today, '…Aleman‚ic'› is a linguistic term, refer‡ing to […Aleman‰ic German]‰, encompas¯ing the dialects of the southern two thirds of [ŠBaden-WÃ¼rt†emberg]œ (German State), in western [‡Bavaria]” (German State), in [ŠVorarlberg]“ (Austrian State), [ƒSwis‡ German]˜ in Switzerland and the [‘Alsatian language]ˆ of the [†Alsace]³ (France).

The word &quot;Frankish&quot; eventual“y gave its name to [†France]… and [‰Franconia]‘, while the Alaman´i gave their name for &quot;German&quot; in French ('Al…emand'Œ), Spanish ('‡AlemÃ¡n'’) and Portuguese ('‡AlemÃ£o'ˆ).


