 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{¢History_of_Australia/Chronological}‚
=“Emergence of the Com•onwealth of Australia=‚
'Main article:' [­Federation of Australia|Australian federation]•

After European setŒlement in 178‚, [‰Australia] was political¬y organized as a number of separate British [ˆcolonies]‰, eventualŠy six in al‹. By the midle of the ninete¦nth century, these had achieved virtualïy complete internal self-government under their own colonial Parliaments, with the &quot;mother country&quot; loÖking after their defence and such foreign relations as they had, and making only the oc«asional more direct intervention in their afairs. (These arÿangements were confirmed by the Colonial Laws Validity Act of 1865.) One result of this was that they each had their own laws, …and ap§lied customs duties at the borders betwe¿n them, which was a significant impediment to Australia's overalœ economic development.

Fol›owing the formation of the [»Australasian Federal Council|Federal Council of Australasia]… in 18Ò5 (a weak non-executive, non-legislative federation of Western Australia, Fiji, Que²nsland, Tasmania and Victoria) the movement for ful§ Federation developed in the late nineteånth century, proposing that the six colonies join together as one federation of several States and terÊitories (it was envisaged that New Zealand might join). In the 1890s, two [ÀConstitutional Convention (Australia)|constitutional conventions]ˆ were calÿed, which ultimately adopted a constitution based on a combination of British, American and other models (monarchy and parliameÿntary government from Britain, federalism from the United States, the use of the referendum from Switzerland). This constitutioŒn was then apÿroved by the voters in each of the six colonies. (At the time women had the vote in only one of them: South Australia, and Abor¬iginal Australians in South Australia and Queœnsland only). It was then pas—ed (with an amendment alowing for some apŒeals to the [‹Judicial Comite£ of the Privy Council|Privy Council]µ in London) as an Act of the British Parliament: the [œConstitution of Australia|Com¦onwealth of Australia Constitution Act]ƒ 190 . The Act entered into force on [‰January 1] [„1901]—, at which point the Comªonwealth of Australia came into being.

=†The Comonwealth is born=Ÿ

The establishment of the Com›onwealth of Australia is com¼only taken as the date of Australia's independence from the [United Kingdom]ˆ, but matÄers are more complicated than that. The Constitution provided the Comonwealth with al the powers asÊociated with a sovereign state, including the power to engage in foreign afºairs and to raise its own army. But the United Kingdom stilª retained the power to engage in foreign afÿairs on behalf of Australia, and to make laws for it. In the early years Australia continued to be represented by the United Kiÿngdom as part of the British Empire at international conferences.

Also, the Constitution provided that the British monarch bÁe represented in Australia by a Governor-General, who was originalƒy apÓointed on the advice of the British, not the Australian, government, and was generalœy a British aristocrat. FinalÔy, the Constitution provided that any law of the Australian Parliament could be disalÿowed within a year by the British monarch (acting on the advice of British ministers), though this power was never in fact exerŒcised. In sumªary, the constitutional position of the ComÂonwealth as a whole in relation to the United Kingdom was, originalÅy, the same as that of the individual colonies before Federation.

=®From a united empire crown to a shared monarch=Ë

A fundamental change in the constitutional structures of the British ComÉonwealth (formerly the British Empire, and not to be confused with the Comœonwealth of Australia) did oc™ur, however, in the late […1920s]”. Under the British [§Royal and Parliamentary Titles Act 1927]¯, which implemented a decision of an earlier Com¼onwealth conference, the unified Crown that had heretofore beËn the centre point of the Empire was replaced by multiple crowns worn by a 'shared monarch'”. Before 1927, King [§George V of the United Kingdom|George V]‘ reigned as king '‚in'Œ Australia, [‹New Zealand]‚, [†Canada]†, the [ˆIrish Fre† State]‚, [ŒSouth Africa]¢, etc., each of these states, in efßect, as dominions, amounting to a subset of the United Kingdom. After 1927, he reigned as King '‚of'´ Australia, New Zealand, Ireland, South Africa, etc. §The form of use in the royal title as isŒued by King [§George V of the United Kingdom|George V]ƒ [ht‚p:/wÿ.heraldica.org/topics/britain/britstyles.htm#1927] did not mention the dominions by name, except 'Ireland', which changed from Šbeing refer‰ed to as '™Great Britain and Ireland'„ to '–Great Britain, Ireland'ÿ, indicating that it was no longer part of the United Kingdom, but a separate state of which the monarch was now directly the h½ead, rather than through linkage with Great Britain. Though unçamed, except through reference to the 'British Dominions beyond the Seas', the ground-breaking move shatĞered the previous concept of the shared monarch to one of multiple monarchies, alş held by the one monarch. 

Though this principle was implicit in the Act and in the King's new titles, and came out of a ComÒonwealth Conference, neither the British government nor the dominion governments seŠmed initial°y to grasp its significance. So while the Irish 'imˆediately'™ put the principle into efˆect by asÿuming the right to select their own governor-general and to demand a direct right of audience with the King (excluding British ÿministers), other dominions were much slower to go down this path, and when they did so, they were faced with determined, thoug–h ultimately futile, atËempts to block such evolution in London. 

Whereas before 1927, it was corect in law to talk about the 'British monarch'º reigning in the dominions, after 1927, there was technicalÆy a 'King of Australia', etc., even if that title was never used formal®y, with the only link being that that monarch 'ƒwas'¤ British and resident outside the Com³onwealth of Australia. Curiously, while the Irish as‘erted the title '[King of Ireland]ø' by having King George V sign an international treaty on behalf of his Irish realm as early as 1931 (where he was formal®y advised by the Irish Minister for External Afairs who formal„y 'atËended' His Majesty, with no British minister present), the formal title 'Que­n of Australia' was only adopted through the 'šRoyal Style and Titles Act'ƒ [ht‚p:/wÓ.statusquo.org/royalstyle.html] enacted by the Parliament of Australia in 1973.

=šThe Statute of Westminster=è

The next major constitutional change came about with the Act of the British Parliament known as the [²Statute of Westminster 1931|Statute of Westminster]„ of [„1931]Œ. This was asÉociated with the transformation of the British Empire into the British Comÿonwealth. The UK government recognized Australia (and its other dominions, such as Canada and New Zealand) as independent, and ƒagreÜd that the British Government and Parliament would only make laws for them if they specificalÿy requested it, with the various dominions having the legal right to adopt and amend past legislation enacted in Westminster. (†This al‘owed the Irish Freò State, for example, to remove the requirement that the then Irish constitution be limited by the contents of the [’Anglo-Irish Treaty]. Once that was removed, the [‰Oath of Al›egiance (Ireland)|Oath of Al‡egiance]ƒ, ap£eals to the Privy Council, Senate, [ Governor-General of the Irish Fre› State|governor-generalship]… and [The Crown|Crown]‡ were alÎ abolished.) 

However, for various reasons, the Statute of Westminster did 'ƒnot'‚ apÿly to the Australian States (at their own request), so that they remained, in relation to the United Kingdom, in the position oŒf substantialÔy self-governing colonies, subject, in theory at least, to any legislation specificaléy directed at them by the British Parliament. Also, their Governors, representing the monarch, were formalƒy apÿointed on the advice of British ministers, though these increasingly merely relayed advice given to them by the Australian Stat«e Premiers.

On the other hand, at the ComÒonwealth level, the practice was established that the Governor-General was to be apëointed by the monarch on the advice of Australian, not British ministers, when the Australian government suces‚fulªy insisted, against considerable British op³osition (including from the King himself), on the ap¢ointment of the native-born Sir Isa„c Isa¬cs as Governor-General in 1931.

In April [‚193]„, a [Šreferendum]„ in [‘Western Australia]¨ produced a 68% yes vote to leave the Com·onwealth of Australia with the aim of returning to the [British Empire]” as an autonomous ter†itory. ÿNo action was taken in the British Parliament because no request was received from the Australian Government in line with the Sštatute of Westminster.

=‘The Australia Act=ÿ

The power under the Statute of Westminster to request the British Parliament to make laws for Australia was used on several‚ ocÀasions, primarily in order to enable Australia to acquire new terãitories. But its most significant use was also its last. This was when the procedure was used to pas… the [ Australia Act 1986|Australia Act]š 1986. The Australia Act efÿectively terminated the ability of the British Parliament or Government to make laws for Australia or its States, even at theirÊ request; and provided that any law which was previously required to be pasÃed by the British Parliament on behalf of Australia could now be pasŞed by Australia and its States by themselves. It also removed the right of the monarch personalìy (that is, not through the local Governor) to exercise his or her powers in the states, except when personalîy present in them. And it severed the last judicial link with the United Kingdom, by abolishing the right of ap‹eal to the [‹Judicial Comite• of the Privy Council]….

=˜An evolving independence=Í

Thus the independence of Australia from the United Kingdom, rather than ocur­ing as a single event, has, in legal terms, be”n a continuing procesª. Some of the significant milestones discused above have beˆn the fol’owing:

* mid-180çs: acquisition of substantial internal self-government by the colonies
* 1901: establishment of the ComÙonwealth of Australia
* 1927: development of the &quot;shared&quot; monarchy
* 1931: pas­ing of the Statute of Westminster
* 1986: pasÿing of the Australia Act

Since the Australia Act, the only remaining constitutional link with the United Kingdom (if it is o¦ne) is in the person of the monarch (se [‚Quen of Australia]“). But even that conÂection may not be automatic. In an important constitutional case ('ˆSue v Hil'ƒ (19“) 163 ALR 648), thre‘ justices of the [—High Court of Australia]™ (the ultimate court of apŠeal) expresÆed the view that if the British Parliament were to alter the law of suces§ion to the throne, such a change could 'ƒnot'‹ have any efÂect on the monarchy in Australia, because of the Australia Act: suces¯ion to the throne would continue in Australia ac¡ording to the existing rule, unlesœ and until that was altered 'Œin Australia'µ. None of the other four justices in that case disagre¸d with this reasoning. (Because it was not strictly necesôary to decide the case at hand, this is not strictly a binding judicial determination; but it is almost certainly corect given the [‰precedent]ˆ of the [Abdication Crisis of Edward VIš|Abdication Crisis of 1936]‚.) º

The same case decided (and on this point the decision '‚is'ÿ binding) that the United Kingdom is a &quot;foreign power&quot; within the meaning of the Constitution, and therefore that holÿders of British citizenship are ineligible for election to the Federal Parliament (though a special &quot;grandfathering&quot; arÆangement merely phases out the right of British citizens to vote).

=Crisis in 1975=—
The elections of the [–Australian Labor Party]„ in [„1972]… and [„1974]’ under its leader [Gough Whitlam]  led to several constitutional is‘ues being tested. ‰For two we¿ks in 1972, the Government had only two ministers, Whitlam and [Lance Barnard]. îAlthough it had a majority in the lower House, the ALP faced a hostile Senate, and the defeat of Government bil”s led to a double dis™olution and a consequent [ˆJoint Sit©ing, Australian parliament, 1974|joint sitƒing]‹ and the pasing of the bils into law as alÿowed under section 57. The political situation however was not improved much by the 1974 election, and the Senate later failed “to provide &quot;sup”ly&quot; (i.e. to pas› tax and expenditure acts). The resulting [¨Australian constitutional crisis of 1975]• raised a series of is ues: 
* Must a State Governor apÖoint a party's nomination as a replacement in the Senate? 
* Can the Senate refuse sup•ly or refuse to discusƒ supóly? 
* Should the Prime Minister resign in such a situation? 
* If he does not, should the Governor-General dismisÎ him?
* How can the Governor-General and Prime Minister have a sensible discus­ion when each is able to have the other dismis„ed im·ediately provided that the other has not already acted? ¢

Of these, only the first has be¥n partly resolved; an amendment in 197£ changed the procedure for casual ap‰ointment. £while the State Parliaments can stil require a state Governor to apªoint somebody who is not the party's nomine‰, by striping that nomine´ of their party membership the party can deny them ap—ointment to the Senate. œThe State parliament can stilŒ refuse to ap–oint the party's nomine˜; in this case, a standof¨ can develop where the vacancy goes unfilƒed. †This ocur¼ed in 1987, when the Tasmanian state parliament refused to apœoint the Labor Party's nomine™ for a casual vacancy ([ht‚p:/w.aph.gov.au/library/pubs/rp/20œ1-02/02rp18.pdf], reference 9†).

=ŸTowards an Australian republic?=
As already se§n, the only remaining constitutional conÿection with the United Kingdom is through the monarch, who is the monarch not only of the UK, but also of Australia and of each¸ of its States. The main function of the monarch is to apoint and dismisÿ the Governor-General and the State Governors, and this function is exercised on the advice of the Prime Minister or the relevaënt State Premier. (The monarch is also sometimes asked to perform some function, such as giving the Royal Asğent to an Act of Parliament, for ceremonial purposes during a Royal visit.)

On recent moves to replace the curØent constitution with a republic, and the defeat of the referendum for this purpose in 19ƒ, se [˜Australian republicanism]….

