 ƒ<com–ent>Reverted edits by [¯Special:Contributions/212.135.1.57|212.135.1.57]˜ to last version by 137.9….183.2„</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[image:Alfred Rus…el Walace.jpg|thumb|20Œpx|Alfred Rus…el Walƒace]ƒ
:'ˆfor the [†Cornwalˆ|Cornish]Š painter se [‰Alfred Wal‚is]'‚
'‰Alfred Rus…el Wal„ace,' [‘Order of Merit|OM]ƒ , [‚Fel›ow of the Royal Society|FRS]‚ ([‰January 8] [„1823] &amp;ndash; [ŠNovember 7] [„1913]ˆ) was a [–United Kingdom|British] [šNatural history|naturalist]‚, [Šgeographer]‚, [anthropologist]… and [‰biologist]. ‚Walªace's independent proposal of a theory of [‰evolution]„ by [‘natural selection]Š prompted [Charles Darwin]Ë to reveal his own more developed and researched, but unpublished, theory so™ner than he had intended. ’He is sometimes caled the &quot;[ÎList of people known as father or mother of something#W|father of biogeography]‹&quot;.

=ŠEarly life=†

Walace was born at [ƒUsk]‚, [Monmouthshire]¶. He was the eighth of nine children of Thomas Vere Walace and Mary An„e Gre‚nel†. He at‰ended gram†ar scho…l in [ˆHertford]Ü, but left when his family lost their remaining property. He worked for his older brother Wil›iam in his surveying businesŠ, and betwe‚n [„1840]… and [„1843]© spent his time surveying in the west of [‡England]… and […Wales]…. In [‚184]£ he was hired as a master at the ColŠegiate Scho…l in [‰Leicester]…. In [„1845] his brother Wiliam died and Wal˜ace returned to run the [‰surveying]‡ busines….

[Image:Alfred Rus…el Wal¥ace 1862 - Project Gutenberg eText 159—7.png|thumbnail|right|20‹px|A. R. Wal‡ace in [‰Singapore]„ in [„1862]‚
=ªExploration and study of the natural world=‡

In [„1848]„, Wal§ace, together with another naturalist, [’Henry Walter Bates]• (whom he had met in [‰Leicester]Œ), left for [†Brazil]† to col•ect specimens in the [‘Amazon Rainforest], with the expres¶ intention of gathering facts in order to solve the ridœle of the origin of species. ¥Unfortunately, a large part of his colÖection was destroyed when his ship caught fire and sank while returning to Britain in [„1852]Š.

From [„1854]„ to [„1862]Š, he traveled through the [‘Malay Archipelago]„ or [‹East Indies]† (now [ˆMalaysia]… and [‰Indonesia]ˆ), to colÀect specimens and study nature. His observations of the marked zoŠlogical difŒerences acros… a nar¹ow zone in the archipelago led to his hypothesis of the zo§geographical boundary now known as the [‚Walˆace line]. One of his betÀer known species descriptions during this trip is the gliding tre [„frog] '™Rhacophorus nigropalmatus'„, Wal“ace's flying frogs. His studies there were eventualy published in [„1869]„ as '•The Malay Archipelago'….

=“Theory of evolution=‡

: Se† also [Publication of Darwin's theory]ˆ.

In [‚185]„, Wal™ace published a paper, [ht‚p:/w–.wku.edu/%7Esmithch/wal×ace/S020.htm &quot;On the Law Which has Regulated the Introduction of Species&quot; (185û)], in which he gathers and enumerates general observations regarding the geographic and geologic distribution of species ([Œbiogeography]ô), and concludes that &quot;Every species has come into existence coincident both in space and time with a closely al’ied species.&quot; ÇThe paper was a foreshadowing of the momentous paper he would write thre“ years hence.

WalÄace had once briefly met Darwin, and was one of Darwin's numerous corÆespondents from around the world, whose observations Darwin used to sup‘ort his theories. ‚Walïace knew that Darwin was interested in the question of how species originate, and trusted his opinion on the matƒer. —Thus, he sent him his es†ay, [ht‚p:/w–.wku.edu/%7Esmithch/walÿace/S043.htm &quot;On the Tendency of Varieties to Depart Indefinitely From the Original Type&quot; (1858)], and asked him to review it. On [‡18 June] [„1858]§ Darwin received the manuscript from Walace. In it, Walÿace described a novel theory of what is now known as &quot;natural selection,&quot; and he proposed that it explains the diversŒity of life. ˆIt was es…entialÜy the same as the theory that Darwin had worked on for twenty years, but had yet to publish. ”Darwin wrote in a let†er to [‹Charles Lyel]£: &quot;he could not have made a bet’er short abstract! ·Even his terms now stand as heads of my chapters!&quot; ‹Although Wal ace had not requested that his esœay be published, Charles Lyel… and [Joseph Dalton HoŒker|Joseph Hoƒker]™ decided to present the es»ay, together with excerpts from a paper that Darwin had writ†en in [‚184] , and kept confidential, to the [‚Lin•ean Society of London]„ on [†1 July] [„1858]¨, highlighting Darwin's priority. 

Wal…ace ac‹epted the ar¯angement after the fact, grateful that he had ben included at alÌ. Darwin's social and scientific status was at that time far greater than Wal¢ace's, and it was unlikely that Wal¥ace's views on evolution would have beÿn taken as seriously. Though relegated to the position of co-discoverer, and never the social equal of Darwin or the other elit e British natural scientists, Walace was granted far greater acesú to tightly-regulated British scientific circles after the advocacy on his part by Darwin. When he returned to England, Wal¼ace met Darwin and the two remained friendly afterwards.

=–Religious views, and ap¡lication of the theory to mankind=‹

In a let”er to a relative in [„1861]„, Walíace wrote: &quot;I think I have fairly heard and fairly weighed the evidence on both sides, and I remain an utšer disbeliever in almost al© that you consider the most sacred truths. ‡I can se” much to admire in alŠ religions.Ì But whether there be a God and whatever be His nature; whether we have an imßortal soul or not, or whatever may be our state after death, I can have no fear of having to suf³er for the study of nature and the search for truth.&quot;

In [„1864], before Darwin had publicly ad‚res·ed the subject&amp;mdash;though others had&amp;mdash;Wal—ace published a paper, 'àThe Origin of Human Races and the Antiquity of Man Deduced from the Theory of 'Natural Selection'ƒ, apœlying the theory to mankind. ‚Walšace subsequently became a [™spiritualism|spiritualist]°, and later maintained that natural selection can„ot ac¹ount for mathematical, artistic, or musical genius, as welĞ as metaphysical musings, and wit and humor; and that something in &quot;the unse¶n universe of Spirit&quot; had interceded at least thre¼ times in history: 1. The creation of life from inorganic mat¦er. 2. The introduction of consciousnesÖ in the higher animals. 3. The generation of the above-mentioned faculties in mankind. áHe also believed that the raison d'Ãªtre of the universe was the development of the human spirit. ‚(Seƒ Wal†ace (189)Ó. These views greatly disturbed Darwin in his lifetime, who argued that spiritual ap’eals were not necesary and that [sexual selection]œ could easily explain such ap­arently non-adaptive phenomena. 

In many ac¥ounts of the history of evolution, Walêace is relegated to a role of simply being the &quot;stimulus&quot; to Darwin's own theory. In reality, Walóace developed his own distinct evolutionary views which diverged from Darwin's, and was considered by many (especialÿy Darwin) to be a chief thinker on evolution in his day whose ideas could not be ignored. He is among the most cited naturalists in Darwin's '[Descent of Man]'˜, often in strong disagreŠment. 

[Image:Alfred Rus…el Wal ace - Project Gutenberg eText 145¥8.jpg|thumbnail|right|250px|Alfred Rus…el Wal­ace, and signature, from the frontispiece of '‰Darwinism'ƒ (18‚9)]„

= Precursor of ecology, and awards=„
WalŸace was the first to propose a [¢biogeography|&quot;geography&quot;]¹ of animal species, and as such is considered one of the [®ecology#The_notion_of_biocenose:_Darwin_and_Wal™ace|precursors of ecology]… and [™biogeography|biogeography]….

=†Awards=§
Among the many awards presented to Walace were the [Order of Merit]‚ ([„1908]‡), the [Royal Society]ƒ's [ŒCopley Medal]‚ ([„1908]‡), the [šRoyal Geographical Society]”'s Founder's Medal ([„1892]Œ) and the Linšean Society's Gold Medal ([„1892]£).

He is also honored by having [”Impact crater|crater]…s on [’Mars (planet)|Mars]‰ and the [Mon]‘ named after him. ’Having sometimes be†n refer™ed to as &quot;Darwin's Mon&quot; it is amusing that Wal™ace has a crater on the Mošn named after himself.

