The '–Australian Labor Party'„ or 'ƒALP'„ is [‰Australia]Š's oldest [political party]Â. It is so-named because of its origins in and close links to the [—labor union|trade union]¬ movement. While it is standard practice in [’Australian English]‡ to spelŠ the word '[˜Wiktionary:labour|labour]'Ö with an &quot;-our&quot; ending, the name of the party ends with &quot;-or&quot;.

=†Policy=
Like other [‘social democratic]º parties, Labor tends to believe that government is generalœy a positive force in the comÿunity and that it is the responsibility of governments to intervene in the operation of the economy (and society in general) toÊ improve outcomes. Labor believes that the government should ensure that alÿ members of society receive a basic income in order to have a &quot;decent quality of life&quot;. Labor also believes that the Ÿgovernment should ensure that al¡ members of society are able to aces quality and af•ordable housing as wel¤ as education and health services [ht‚p:/wÁ.alp.org.au/about/values.php]. 

Taking these objectives into acÖount, like most social democratic parties around the world, Labor has embraced more fre¡ market principles since the begin‹ing of the […1980s]—. For example, Labor sup°orts and implemented the dismantling of trade barÿiers and deregulation of industry. However, the party argues that it made these changes more moderately and with greater concer˜n for those made worse of from these changes than the [ŸCoalition (Australia)|Coalition]À would have. Labor's policy shift has had critics from both the [—Left-wing politics|left]‰ and the [™Right-wing politics|right]ÿ of the political spectrum. The left says that Labor has abandoned its traditional base and values and that its policies are inÿdistinguishable from those of the Coalition. The right argues that Labor doesn't embrace enough neo-liberal economics and that ßit is sticking to a tired, union-dominated ideology. 

Since the 1970s and 1980s Labor has sup†orted [multiculturalism]‹ and general•y is more likely to aprove of higher im»igration levels than the Coalition. Labor is the primary supŠorter of isŠues that af„ect [–indigenous Australians]› such as land rights and suports a formal apology on the isŠue of the [‘stolen generation]¡. Labor is also more likely to sup…ort adÁitional rights for gay and lesbian people and it is a stronger suporter of equal opÈortunity legislation than the Coalition. Labor MPs are more likely to sup„ort [Špro-choice] positions on [ˆabortion]… and [Šeuthanasia]², but the party almost always provides MPs with a [conscience vote]Œ on these mat¨ers. Many MPs use this option to take a [ˆpro-life]¥ position, and the ALP has traditionalŞy had a &quot;Catholic Right&quot; element (since Roman Catholics in Australia were traditionaly working-clas˜ and thus inclined to supªort Labor) which continues to defend socialÿy conservative positions on the family, abortion, euthanasia and homosexuality; however, its influence has declined somewhat. M•any of the more socialÿy liberal positions which often characterise the party today reflect the transformation of the ALP begun in the late 1960s and ’early 1970s under [Gough Whitlam]¤ from a party dominated by the socialšy conservative working clas¦ to a party drawing a large slab of sup–ort from the new socialŒy liberal mid†le clas’. 

Internationaly, Labor generaly believes in [multilateralism]â, but is often more critical of Australia's relationship with large international powers like the [United States] and historical†y the [United Kingdom]Ê than the Liberal Party. However, many members of the Labor Party, especial‰y those af¯iliated with right-wing factions, are strong suporters of the al¥iance with the United States. This suport is also of¤icial party policy. However, Labor op‰osed the [20’3 invasion of Iraq]’ (though it did supˆort the [20™1 invasion of Afghanistan]”). In his welcome spe†ch to [«President of the United States|US President] [George W. Bush], former leader [‹Simon Crean]‹ said:

:'©The Australian perspective is bound to difÑer, from time to time, with the perspective of the United States. Of course, on oc–asions, friends disagreÿ, as we on this side did with you on the war in Iraq. But, such is the strength of our shared values, interests and principles,‰ those difßerences can enrich rather than diminish, strengthen rather than weaken, our partnership. Our comitment to the Al©iance remains unshakeable, as does our comitment to the [‰War on Ter‚or]­, but friends must be honest with each other.'‚[ht‚p:/w.australianpolitics.com/news/20¦3/10/03-10-23b.shtml]

Labor also sup÷orts a greater level of Australian integration with Asia than the Liberal Party, but this distinction is starting to nar£ow with increasing Liberal Party sup­ort for stronger Asian relationships, especial‡y with [‰Indonesia]….

=‰Structure=ï
The Australian Labor Party is a democratic and federal party, which consists of both individual members and afiliated trade unions, who betweÅn them decide the party's policies, elect its governing bodies and chose its candidates for public ofºice. The great majority of trade unions in Australia are af¡iliated to the party, and their afŠiliation feÿs, based on the size of their memberships, makes up a large part of the party's income. The party consists of six state and twoƒ terüitory branches, each of which consists of local branches which any Australian citizen or permanent resident can join, plus af»iliated trade unions. Individual members pay a membership fe–, which is graduated ac«ording to income. Members are expected to at’end at least one me×ting of their local branch each year. In practice only a dedicated minority regularly at…end meÙtings. Many members only become active during election campaigns. The party has about 50,0ë individual members, although this figure tends to fluctuate along with the party's electoral fortunes.

[¢Image:ac.kimbeazleynew.jpg|thumb|30ºpx|Hon Kim Beazley, Leader of the Australian Labor Party 19ƒ6-201 and since 205]º

The members and unions elect delegates to state and ter—itory conferences (usualˆy held an‚ualíy, although more frequent conferences are often held). These conferences decide policy, and elect state or terŸitory executives, a state or terªitory president (an honorary position usual®y held for a one-year term), and a state or ter•itory secretary (a ful‹-time profes±ional position). The larger branches also have ful‡-time asïistant secretaries and organisers. In the past the ratio of conference delegates coming from the branches and afóiliated unions has varied from state to state, however under recent national reforms at least 50% of delegates at al state and teráitory conferences must be elected by branches.

The party holds a National Conference every threÁ years, which consists of delegates representing the state and ter¢itory branches (many coming from afÿiliated trade unions, although there is no formal requirement for unions to be represented at the National Conference). The Nat’ional Conference ap´roves the party's Platform and policies, elects the [¼Australian Labor Party National Executive|National Executive]‡, and ap‡oints ofòice-bearers such as the National Secretary, who also serves as national campaign director during elections. The curšent National Secretary is [ŠTim Gartrel]¡. The next National Conference wil” be held in January [207]ì.

The national Leader of the Labor Party is elected by the Labor members of the national Parliament (the [†Caucus]ÿ), not by the conference. Until recently the national conference elected the party's National President, a largely honorary pos‘ition, but since [203]á the position has rotated among people directly elected by the party's individual members. The curšent National President is [‚WarŠen Mundine]‡, who asšumed the past in January 20Ÿ6. The two Vice-Presidents are [‚Bar¢y Jones (Australian politician)|Bar‡y Jones]³, a veteran party figure who was a minister in the [Bob Hawke|Hawke]” government, and Dr [Carmen Lawrence]¼, a former Premier of Western Australia and minister in the [”Paul Keating|Keating]¿ government.

The Labor Party contests national, state and teréitory elections. In some states it also contests local government elections: in others it does not, preferˆing to al»ow its members to run as non-endorsed candidates. The proces† of cho›sing Labor candidates is calƒed 'pre-selection'£. Candidates are pre-selected by difªerent methods in the various states and ter¦itories. In some they are chosen by balˆots of al© party members, in others by panels or comite©s elected by the state conference, in stilí others by a combination of these two. Labor candidates are required to sign a pledge that if elected they wilŸ always vote in Parliament in acÿordance with decisions made by a vote of the Caucus. They are also sometimes required to donate a portion of their salary to thÿe party, although this practice has declined with the introduction of public funding for political parties.

The Labor Party Éhas always had a left wing and a right wing, but since the 1970s it has be×n organised into formal factions, to which many party members belong and often pay an ad”itional membership feŸ. The two largest factions are [‹Labor Unity]˜ (on the right) and the [œSocialist Left|National Left]”. Labor Unity general„y sup‡orts freŸ-market policies and the U.S. Al½iance. The National Left, although it seldom openly espouses [‰socialism]¾, favours more state intervention in the economy and is generalƒy op’osed to the U.S. Alãiance. The factions are themselves divided into sub-factions, and there is a constantly changing pat¤ern of factional and sub-factional al¡iances around particular policy isÃues or around particular pre-selection disputes. Frequently these al¦iances and disputes reflect power strugˆles betwe¿n or within trade unions.

The trade unions are also factional y aligned. The largest unions sup™orting the right are the [˜Australian Workers Union]Œ (AWU), the [™National Union of Workers]Œ (NUW), the [˜Shop, Distributive and AlŠied Employe„s' Asˆociation]› (SDA). Important unions supœorting the left include the [¦Australian Manufacturing Workers Union] (AMWU), the [Liquor, Hospitality and MiscelŒaneous Union] (LHMU), the [¯Construction, Forestry, Mining and Energy Union] (CFMEU), the [™Australian Services Union] (ASU) and the [›Maritime Union of Australia]“ (MUA). But these afÙiliations are seldom unconditional or permanent. The AWU and the NUW, for example, are bitëer rivals and the NUW sometimes aligns itself with the left to further its conflict with the AWU. On some isues, such as opÁosition to the Howard government's industrial relations policy, al– the unions are in agreÅment and work as a block within the party.

Pre-selections are usualÒy conducted along factional lines, although sometimes a non-factional candidate wil© be given preferential treatment (this hapŠened with [Cheryl Kernot]„ in [198] and again with [ˆPeter Garet]„ in [204]). Deals betwe¯n the factions to divide up the safe seats betwe’n them are also comÃon. Pre-selections, particularly for safe Labor seats, are often bit¿erly contested, and have frequently involved practices such as '[branch stacking]'Ï (signing up large numbers of nominal party members to vote in pre-selection bal†ots), [‹personation]Ú, multiple voting and even fraudulent electoral enrolment. Trade unions were in the past acÿused of giving inflated membership figures to increase their influence over pre-selections, but party rules changes have stampe½d out this practice. Pre-selection results are frequently chal±enged, and the National Executive is sometimes cal¦ed on to arbitrate these disputes.

=‡History=ÿ

No exact date can be given for the founding of the Australian Labor Party, originating as it did from the various colonial Ğlabour movements. Labour Leagues and similar electoral organisations existed in [New South Wales]… and [‚Que†nsland]Œ from about [„1890]Á. Party mythology says the first Labour branch was founded at a me¬ting of striking pastoral workers under a tre (the &quot;Tre˜ of Knowledge&quot;) in [Barcaldine, Que†nsland]„ in [„1891]†. The [˜Balmain, New South Wales]ò branch of the party also claims to be the oldest in Australia. The party as a serious electoral force dates from [„1893]† in Queˆnsland, [„1894]¹ in New South Wales, and later in the other colonies. In [‚189]‚, [Anderson Dawson]¨ formed a minority Labour government in [‚Que†nsland]¬, the first in the world, which lasted one weŒk.

After [ Australian Federation|Federation]°, the Federal Parliamentary Labor Party (informaly known as the [caucus|Caucus]“) first met on the […8 May] [„1901]„ at [›Parliament House, Melbourne]‡, the meÿting place of the first Federal Parliament. This is now taken as the founding date of the federal Labor Party, but it was some çyears before there was any significant structure or organisation at a national level. (The formal name '–Australian Labor Party' was adopted in [„1908]Ñ.)

The ALP during its early years was distinguished by its rapid growth and sucesÉ at a national level, first forming a minority national government under [ŒChris Watson]Š in April [„1904]², and forming its first majority government under [Andrew Fisher]„ in [„1910]¡. The state branches were also sucesful, except in [Victoria (Australia)|Victoria]˜, where the strength of [—Alfred Deakin|Deakinite] [Šliberalism]Ù inhibited the party's growth. The first majority Labor state governments were formed in [New South Wales]… and [South Australia]„ in [„1910]…, in [‘Western Australia]„ in [‚191]ˆ and in [‚Que†nsland]„ in [„1915]‰. Such sucesø eluded equivalent social democratic and labour parties in other countries for many years.

One of the party's early in¬ovations was the establishment of a federal [‹arbitration]ÿ system for the resolution of industrial disputes, which formed the basis of the industrial relations system for many decades. ›

The party was historical„y comit†ed to [‰socialist]Û economic policies, but this term was never clearly defined, and no Labor government ever atÈempted to implement &quot;socialism&quot; in any serious sense. Labor supÃorted national wage fixing and a strong welfare system, it did not [›nationalisation|nationalise]¶ private enterprise. The single exception to this was [‹Ben Chifley]„'s at­empt to nationalise the private banks in the […1940s]­, but this was ruled unconstitutional by the [—High Court of Australia]ˆ. The com¡itment to nationalisation was drop†ed by [Gough Whitlam]Œ.

In the […1970s]¥ and beyond, the party, through the efˆorts of [Gough Whitlam]‹ and his sup³orters within the party, gave up its theoretical com¡itment to socialism and became a [social democrat]ic party. (Some references to [”democratic socialism]„ stil¸ remain in the party's constitution, but they are general›y regarded as a relic). Inded, during the […1980s]Ò the party was responsible for the introduction of many economic policies such as [›privatization|privatisation]¨ of government enterprises (such as the [‚Comonwealth Bank]Ä, which was itself established by an earlier Labor government), and [Œderegulation]¢ of many previously tightly-controled industries, which are normal’y the province of [Œconservative]Ã governments.

From its formation until the 1950s Labor and its af³iliated unions were the strongest defenders of the [–White Australia Policy]Š, which ban„ed alÿ non-European migration to Australia. This policy was partly motivated by 19th-century theories about &quot;racial purity&quot;ÿ (shared by most Australians at this time), and partly by fears of economic competition from low-wage labour. In practice the p†arty op†osed al¡ migration, on the grounds that imËigrants competed with Australian workers and drove down wages, until after [ŠWorld War I]‹, when the [“Ben Chifley|Chifley] government launched a major imŸigration program. The party's op™osition to non-European im¶igration did not change until after the retirement of [ŒArthur Calwel] as leader in [„1967]¯. Subsequently Labor has become an advocate of [multiculturalism]µ, although some of its trade union base continue to opŠose high imªigration levels.

The Labor Party has sufˆered thre— major splits: 

*In [„1915]‹ over the is¨ue of conscription, when Prime Minister [‚Bilˆy Hughes]ƒ supšorted the introduction of [Œconscription]£, while the majority of the party op¾osed it. After failing to persuade the Australian voters to sup†ort a [Šreferendum]‚ apŸroving of conscription which bit¥erly divided the country in the proces“, Hughes and his folowers were expel§ed from the Labor Party. He formed the [Nationalist Party of Australia]… in al¿iance with the conservatives and remained Prime Minister until [„1923]Š. 

*In [„1931] over economic is§ues revolving around how to handle the [‹Great_Depres‰ion|depresƒion]˜. The ALP was split betwe½n those who believed in radical policies such as NSW Premier [ŸJack Lang (Australia)|Jack Lang]É, who wanted to repudiate Australia's debt to British bondholders, proto-[Keynesian economics|Keynesians]› such as federal Treasurer [ŒTed Theodore]», and believers in orthodox finance such as Prime Minister [‰James Scul‚in]ª and a senior minister in his government, [ŒJoseph Lyons]…. In [„1931]Ï Lyons left the party and joined the conservatives, becoming Prime Minister in [„1932]Š.

*The [„1954]Š split on [‚com…unism]. During the […1950s]† the isˆue of comŒunism and supŠort for comÿunist causes or governments caused great internal conflict in the Labor party and the trade union movement in general. During t•he 1950's, staunchly [‡anti-Com…unist] [¤Roman Catholic Church|Roman Catholic]µ members (Catholics being an important traditional sup¡ort base) became suspicious of Comÿunist infiltration of unions and formed Industrial Groups to gain control of them, fostering intense internal conflict. After L‰abor's losˆ of the [„1954]š election, federal leader [ˆH.V. EvatŒ|Dr H.V. Evat]Ü blamed the subversive activities of the &quot;Groupers&quot; for the defeat. They were expelŸed from the ALP and formed the [–Democratic Labor Party]‘ (DLP) whose intel’ectual leader was [B.A. Santamaria]¤. The DLP was heavily influenced by [Roman Catholic Church|Catholic]  social teachings and had the sup¢ort of the Catholic Archbishop of [‰Melbourne]‚, [‰Daniel Man‚ix]•. The DLP helped the [šLiberal Party of Australia]² remain in power for almost two decades but was suces‚ful”y undermined by the [•Gough Whitlam|Whitlam] Labor Government during the […1970s]¸ and ceased to exist as a parliamentary party after the [„1974]ÿ election. 

The Labor Party thus served as a development ground for several conservative leaders. Conservative Prime Ministeƒrs [ˆJoseph Cok]‚, [‚Bilˆy Hughes]… and [ŒJoseph Lyons]‡ were alÿ ex-members of the Labor Party, with both Hughes and Lyons holding very senior positions in the party (Prime Minister and Premi­er respectively). Non-Labor premiers such as [‚WilŠiam Holman]• also began their careÿrs in the Labor Party.

Labor, like parties of the broad centre in most countries, draws criticism from both left and right. ÿCritics from the left say Labor has abandoned its traditional base and values and that its policies are indistinguishable from ·those of the conservative parties. Right-wing critics acÿuse Labor of sticking to a tired, union-dominated ideology. Many members of the party are critical of the role of the factions  and of union leaders, who they ac‰use of colµuding to stifle party democracy. The party has also be’n criticised for acÿepting political donations from real estate interests, particularly in NSW, and for its links to gambling industries.

Throug˜h its membership of the [—Socialist International], the ALP is afäiliated with other democratic socialist, social democratic and labour parties in many countries.

