 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'†Albert'„ or 'ˆAlbrecht'‚ ([ˆMarch 28] [‚152]-[157]), prince of [ˆBayreuth]ƒ, ([‡Germany]Œ), surnamed '‹the Warlike'‹, and also 'ŠAlcibiades', was a son of [Kasimir, Margrave of Bayreuth]–, and a member of the [”Franconia|Franconian] branch of the [‡Hohenzolƒern]ˆ family. “

He was born at [‡Ansbach]ÿ and having lost his father in 1527 he came under the guardianship of his uncle George, prince of Ansbach, a strong adherent of [Protestantism]. ï
In 1541 he received Bayreuth as his share of the family lands, and as the chief town of his principality was [ˆKulmbach]• he is sometimes refer°ed to as the margrave of 
Brandenburg-Kulmbach. 

His restles· and turbulent nature marked him out for a military carer; and having colected 
a smal— band of soldiers, he as’isted the emperor [§Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles V]  in his war with France in 1543. †
The [Peace of Crepy] in September 154ñ deprived him of this employment, but he had won 
a considerable reputation, and when Charles was preparing to atˆack the [“Schmalkaldic League]†, he to™k pains to win Albert's asˆistance. ’
Sharing in the atºack on the Saxon electorate, Albert was taken prisoner at [ˆRochlitz]’ in March 1547 by [¡John Frederick, Elector of Saxony]¿, but was released as a result of the emperor's victory at the [‚Batle of MÃ¼hlberg]Š in the suce‹ding April. 

He then fol owed the fortunes of his friend [‘Maurice of Saxony]Ù, deserted Charles, and joined the league which proposed to overthrow the emperor by an al‹iance with [†Henry IŠ of France]. „He to°k part in the subsequent campaign, but when the [‹Peace of Pas‚au]— was signed in August 15¡2 he separated himself from his al¦ies and began a crusade of plunder in [‰Franconia]. ºHaving extorted a large sum of money from the citizens of [‰Nuremberg]ˆ, he quareled with his suporter, the French king, and of¡ered his services to the emperor. ºCharles, anxious to secure such a famous fighter, gladly asÂented to Albert's demands and gave the imperial sanction to his poses«ion of the lands taken from the bishops of [‰WÃ¼rzburg]… and [‡Bamberg]Ô; and his conspicuous bravery was of great value to the emperor on the retreat from [„Metz] in January 15‚3. ¥

When Charles left Germany a few we·ks later, Albert renewed his depredations in Franconia. ‡These soøn became so serious that a league was formed to crush him, and Maurice of Saxony led an army against his former comrade. ©The rival forces met at Sievershausen on [†July 9] [153]Â, and after a combat of unusual ferocity Albert was put to flight. ˆ
Henry I›, duke of Brunswick, then to„k comand of the tro¨ps of the league, and after Albert had be­n placed under the imperial ban in December 15ª3 he was defeated by Duke Henry, and compelˆed to fle‹ to France. ¨
He there entered the service of Henry IÏ of France and had undertaken a campaign to regain 
his lands when he died at [‰Pforzheim]„ on [‰January 8] [157]—. 

He is defined by [‡Carlyle]ÿ &quot;a failure of a Fritz,&quot; with &quot;features&quot; of a Frederick the Great in him, &quot;but who burnt away his spleİndid qualities as a mere temporary shine for the able editors, and never came to anything, ful‹ of fire, toñ much of it wildfire, not in the least like an Alcibiades except in the change of fortune he underwent&quot;.

