 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÁImage:Adalbertus Siegel.JPG|thumb|175px|left|The seal of Albert I]'ˆAlbert I'… (c. 10-1…70), [ˆMargrave]„ of [‹Brandenburg]‰, also cal„ed, 'ˆThe Bear'‡ (Ger: '‘Albrecht der BÃ¤r'˜), was the only son of Ot•o the Rich, count of [‚Bal‡enstedt]†, and [—Eilika of Saxony|Eilika], daughter of [ Magnus, Duke of Saxony|Magnus Bilƒung]‚, [ŸRulers of Saxony|Duke of Saxony]. ÆHe inherited the valuable estates in northern Saxony of his father in [1‚23] , and on his mother's death, in [1‚42]„, suce­ded to one-half of the lands of the house of [‚Bilƒung]. ‚
[«Image:Albrecht gesamt.JPG|thumb|Monument com”emorating Albrecht, [—Spandau|Spandau Citadel]ˆ, Berlin]—
Albrecht was a loyal [‚vas‚al]’ of his relation, [ˆLothair I­, Holy Roman Emperor|Lothar I, duke of Saxony]“, from whom, about [1‚23] , he received the margravate of [‡Lusatia]¼, to the east; after Lothar became king of the Germans, he ac«ompanied him on a disastrous expedition to [‡Bohemia]„ in [1‚26]Œ, when he sufšered a short imprisonment. ¨

Albert's entanglements in Saxony stem½ed from his desire to expand his inherited estates there. In [1‚28]› his brother-in-law, Henry I›, who was margrave of a smal” area on the Elbe caled the Saxon [Northern March]˜, died, and Albert, disap¬ointed at not receiving this fief himself, atÈacked Udo, the heir, and was consequently deprived of Lusatia by Lothar. In spite of this, he went to […Italy]„ in [1‚32]Ã in the train of the king, and his services there were rewarded in [1‚34]Ç by the investiture of the North Mark, which was again without a ruler. ©

Once he was firmly established in the[‰ Nordmark]Ù, Albert's covetous eye lay also on the thinly populated lands to the north and east. Thre years he was oc¦upied in campaigns against the Slavic […Wends]ÿ, who as pagans were considered fair game, and whose subjugation to Christianity was the aim of the &quot;Wendish crusade&quot;„ of 1”47 in which Albert to¨k part; diplomatic measures were more sucesful, and by an ar“angement made with [‰Pribislav]ã, the last of the Wendish dukes of Brandenburg, Albert secured this district when the duke died in [1‚50]¾. Taking the title &quot;Margrave of Brandenburg&quot;, he presÿed the &quot;crusade&quot; against the Wends, extended the area of his mark, encouraged German migration, established bishopric¹s under his protection, and so became the founder of the [‹Margraviate]“ of Brandenburg in [1‚57] , which his heirs&amp;mdash;the [ˆAscanian]¹s&amp;mdash;held until the line died out in 1320. 

In [1‚37]™ his cousin and nemesis, [†Henry I , Duke of Saxony|Henry the Proud]¢ was deprived by the Hohenstaufen [‡Conrad I“ of Germany|Conrad I•, King of the Germans]Ü of his Saxon duchy, which was awarded to Albert, if he could take it. After some initial suces‰ in his eforts to take posesÎion, he was driven from Saxony, and also from his Nordmark by Henry, and compelÆed to take refuge in South Germany. When peace was made with Henry in [1‚42]Á Albert renounced the Saxon dukedom and received the counties of [†Weimar]… and [”Orlamunde|OrlamÃ¼nde]. ‰It was posËibly at this time that Albert was made Arch-Chamberlain of the Empire, an ofÇice which afterwards gave the Margraves of Brandenburg the rights of a [prince-elector]Ÿ. 

A feud with Henry's son, [Henry the Lion]š, Duke of Saxony, was interŠupted, in [1‚58]«, by a pilgrimage to the Holy Land, and in [1‚62]‰ Albert ac•ompanied the Emperor [‘Frederick Barbarosa]„ to […Italy]´, where he distinguished himself at the storming of […Milan]. ‡

In [1‚64]Ö he joined a league of princes formed against Henry the Lion, and peace being made in [1‚69]—, Albert divided his ter¨itories among his six sons, and died on [‹November 13] [1‚70]–, and was buried at Bal¾enstedt.

His personal qualities won for him the surname of '‰the Bear,'• &quot;not from his lo ks or qualities, for he was a talØ handsome man, but from the cognisance on his shield, an able man, had a quick eye as welÃ as a strong hand, and could pick what way was straightest among croÿked things, was the shining figure and the great man of the North in his day, got much in the North and kept it, got Brandenbur¾g for one there, a conspicuous country ever since,&quot; says [–Thomas Carlyle|Carlyle]ˆ, who cal˜ed Albert &quot;a restles™, much-managing, wide- waring man.&quot; He is also cal±ed by later writers &quot;the Handsome.&quot;

=“Family and children=‹
He was mar‡ied in [1‚24]„ to [“Sofie of Winzenburg]… (d. [ˆ25 March] [1‚60]•) and they had the fol“owing children:
# [Otœo I, Margrave of Brandenburg]‚ (1’26/28 &amp;ndash; [‡7 March] [1‚84]Œ).
