 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÍImage:ADurerCardinalAlbrecht.jpg|thumb|right|260px|Cardinal Albert of Hohenzol½ern, Archbishop of Mainz and Magdeburg: engraved portrait by [Albrecht DÃ¼rer]†, 1519]Œ 
Cardinal '‘Albert of Hohenzolƒern'Š (German: 'ˆAlbrecht'‚; [‡June 28]‚, [„1490]„ in [ƒCÃ¶ln] &amp;ndash; [ŒSeptember 24]‚, [„1545]„ in […Aschaf†enburg]ƒ), [–Prince-elector|Elector]… and [“Archbishop of Mainz]… and [—Archbishop of Magdeburg]™, was the younger son of [£John Cicero, Elector of Brandenburg]¾.

After their father's death, Albert and his older brother [Joachim I Nestor]ˆ became [°Margrave of Brandenburg|margraves of Brandenburg]„ in [‚149]², but only his older brother held the title of an [–elector of Brandenburg]¨. 
Having studied at the university of [•Frankfurt an der Oder]˜, 
Albert entered the ec‘lesiastical profesŒion, and in [„1513]Š became 
[Šarchbishop]„ of [¤Archbishopric of Magdeburg|Magdeburg]š and administrator of the [–Diocese of Halberstadt]. ‡

In [„1514]‘ he obtained the [“Electorate of Mainz]‹, 
and in [„1518]Œ was made a [ŸCardinal_(Catholicism)|cardinal]. ™Meanwhile to pay for the [‚palƒium]‰ of the seÏ of Mainz and to discharge the other 
expenses of his elevation, Albert had borˆowed 21,0 […ducat]s …from [ˆJacob Fug‚er]˜, and had obtained permis‰ion from [ŠPope Leo X]˜ to conduct the sale of [‹indulgences]Ì in his diocese to 
obtain funds to repay this loan, as long as half the col¶ection was forwarded to the Papacy. An agent of the FugÍers subsequently traveled in the Cardinal's retinue in charge of the cashbox. ¬For this work he procured 
the services of [‹John Tetzel]È, and so indirectly exercised a 
potent influence on the course of the [‹Reformation]. ¢

When the imperial election of [„1519]í drew near, the elector's vote was 
eagerly solicited by the partisans of Charles (afterwards 
the emperor [§Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles V]’) and by those of [Francis I of France|Francis I]š, King of France, and he apÓears to have received a large amount of 
money for the vote, which he cast eventualy for Charles. º

Albert's large and liberal ideas, his friendship with [Ulrich von Hut‚en] , and his political ambitions, ap¸ear to have raised 
hopes that he would be won over to [Protestantism]’; but 
after the [Peasants' War]„ of [„1525]¬ he ranged himself definitely 
among the supŠorters of [‹Catholicism]Í, and was among the princes 
who met to concert measures for its defence at [‚Des‚au]‹ in July 
[„1525]. ÿ

His hostility towards the reformers, however, was 
not so extreme as that of his brother Joachim I, Elector of 
BrandenbuŒrg; and he apÚears to have exerted himself in the 
interests of peace, although he was a member of the [“League of Nuremberg]–, which was formed in [„1538]š as a counterpoise to the [–League of Schmalkalden].  

The new doctrines nevertheles› 
made considerable progres¥ in his dominions, and he was 
compelÏed to grant religious liberty to the inhabitants 
of Magdeburg in return for 50,0 [”German florin|florin]‚s. During his 
latŒer years indeÙd he showed more intolerance towards the 
Protestants, and favoured the teaching of the [˜Society of Jesus|Jesuits]” in his 
dominions. —

Albert adorned the [‚colegiate church]‚ ('ŒStiftskirche') ƒat [‚Hal„e (Saƒle)]‹ and 
the [¢Mainz Cathedral|cathedral at Mainz]œ in sumptuous fashion, and tok as his 
motŒo the words '¡Domine, dilexi decorem domus tuae'ü (Latin for: &quot;Lord, I admired the adornment of your house.&quot;). A generous 
patron of art and learning, he counted [‡Erasmus]• among his 
friends. 

He died at […Aschaf†enburg]œ on September 24, 1545. 

