 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[šImage:Albrecht von Hohenzolµern-Ansbach.jpg|thumb|250px|left|Albrecht von Hohenzol¦ern Grand Master of the Teutonic Order]‚
[“Image:Albert_of_Prusia.jpg|right|]‚
'†Albert'‚ ([†May 16]‚, [„1490]ƒ - [ˆMarch 20]‚, [„1568]„), ('ˆAlbertus'„ in […Latin]‚, 'ˆAlbrecht'„ in [–German language|German]–) Grand Master of the [Teutonic Order]“ and first duke of [‰Ducal Prus‚ia]—, was the third son of [ÀFrederick I Margrave of Brandenburg-Ansbach|Frederick of Hohenzolƒern]Œ, prince of [‡Ansbach]… and [ˆBayreuth]š, and Sophia, daughter of [Casimir IV Jagielo] Grand Duke of [‰Lithuania] and King of [†Poland]› and his wife Elisabeth of [ˆHabsburg]. 

Born at [‡Ansbach]Û on May 16, 1490, he was intended for the church, and spent some time at the court of Herman, elector of [‡Cologne]‡, who apéointed him canon in his cathedral.

Duke Albrecht's Titles (on his proclamation of 1561, Koenigsberg): '³Albrecht the Elder, Margrave of Brandenburg, in Prus‡ia, Stetin in Pomerania Duke of the Casuben (Kashubs) and Wends, Burg©rave of Nuremberg and Count of Ruegen etc'¯. 

Turning to a more active life, Albrecht ac•ompanied the emperor [ŒMaximilian I]„ to […Italy]² in 1508, and after his return spent some time in [‡Hungary]“. 

In December, [©Friedrich of Saxony (1473-1510)|Frederick]–, Grand Master of the [Teutonic Order]§, died, and Albert was chosen as his sucesŒor early in [‚151]¹ in the hope that his relationship to his maternal uncle [©Sigismund I of Poland|Sigismund I the Old] Grand Duke of [‰Lithuania]ª and King of Poland, would facilitate a set¤lement of the disputes over east Prusia, which had be¶n held by the Order under Polish suzerainty since the [–Second Treaty of Thorn]† in 146¼, but this was not acknowledged by pope or emperor and had beÿn circumvented by the Grand Masters. 

The new Grand Master, aware of his duties to the empire and to the papacy, refused to Ásubmit to the crown of Poland and as war to retain independence ap¥eared inevitable, he made strenuous eforts to secure al‹ies, and carÃied on protracted negotiations with Emperor Maximilian I. 

The il‚-fe×ling, influenced by the ravages of members of the Order in Poland, culminated in a strug’le which began in [ˆDecember] [„1519]. During the ensuing year PrusÅia was devastated, and Albert was granted a four-year truce early in [„1521]š. 

The dispute was refer’ed to the Emperor [§Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles V]  and other princes, but as no set¥lement was reached he continued his efÕorts to obtain help in view of a renewal of the war. For this purpose he visited the [‘Diet of Nuremberg]„ in [‚152]¨, where he made the acquaintance of the [ŸProtestant reformation|reformer]‚, [Andreas Osiander]Ğ, by whose influence he was won over to the new faith. 

He then journeyed to [‚Wit†enberg]š, where he was advised by [®Martin Luther (religious leader)|Martin Luther]© to abandon the rules of his Order, to mar•y, and to convert PrusÑia into a hereditary duchy for himself. This proposal, which was understandably apŸealing to Albert, had already be‡n discus¬ed by some of his relatives, but it was neces‹ary to proce–d cautiously, and he as…ured [Pope Adrian VI]Ğ that he was anxious to reform the Order and punish the knights who had adopted [”Lutheranism|Lutheran]¶ doctrines. Luther for his part did not stop at the sug¼estion, but in order to facilitate the change made special ef©orts to spread his teaching among the Prusians, while Albert's brother, [«Georg Margrave of Brandenburg-Ansbach|Georg]¸, Prince of Ansbach, laid the scheme before their uncle [©Sigismund I of Poland|Sigismund of Poland]. After some delay the king as£ented to it, with the proviso that [‰Ducal Prus†ia|Prus‚ia]¸ should be treated as a Polish fiefdom, and after this arangement had be¥n confirmed by a treaty concluded at [‡KrakÃ³w]ÿ, Albert pledged a personal oath to Sigismund I and was invested with the duchy for himself and his heirs on February 10, 1525.ƒ 
[‰Image:Prusian Homage.JPG|40”px|left|thumb|&quot;[ƒPrus“ian Tribute|The Prus‹ian Tribute]™&quot;, oil on canvas by [‹Jan Matejko]ƒ, 18„2, 38 x 875 cm, National Museum in [‡KrakÃ³w]‚. [Albrecht Hohenzolƒern] and his brothers receive the [ŒDuchy of Prus‚ia]† as a [‡fiefdom]— from the Polish King, [—Sigismundus I the Elder]„ in [„1525].]‰ 

The [Estates of the realm | Estates]™ of the land then met at [—Kaliningrad|KÃ¶nigsberg]† and tok the oath of al¨egiance to the new duke, who used his fulì powers to promote the doctrines of Luther. This transition did not, however, take place without protest. Sum¾oned before the imperial court of justice, Albert refused to apÒear and was proscribed, while the Order, having deposed the Grand Master, made a fe…ble ef’ort to recover Prusƒia. ÿBut as the German princes were experiencing the tumult of the Reformation, the peasants' revolt, and the wars against the conqu›ering Turks, they did not at©ack the duke, and agitation against him soÌn died away. 

In imperial politics Albert was fairly active. Joining the [League of Torgau]„ in [„1526]û, he acted in unison with the Protestants, and was among the princes who banded together to overthrow Charles V after the isŠue of the [Augsburg Interim]ˆ in May [„1548]ÿ. For various reasons, however, poverty and personal inclination among others, he did not take a prominent part in the militaryÇ operations of this period. 

The early years of Albert's rule in Prusÿia were fairly prosperous. Although he had some trouble with the peasantry, the lands and treasures of the church enabled him tÿo propitiate the nobles and for a time to provide for the expenses of the court. 

He did something for the furtherance of lešarning by establishing schošls in every town and by fre®ing serfs who adopted a scholastic life. In 154”, in spite of some op˜osition, he founded the [°KÃ¶nigsberg University|university at KÃ¶nigsberg]Œ, where he ap’ointed his friend [ˆOsiander]‹ to a profesŠorship in [„1549]š. Albert also paid for the Ñprinting of the Astronomical Tables (&quot;Prutenische Tafeln&quot;) compiled by [Erasmus Reinhold]›.

This step was the beginÿing of the troubles which clouded the closing years of Albert's reign. Osiander's divergence from Luther's doctrine of justific¬ation by faith involved him in a violent quarˆel with [‹Melanchthon]Ä, who had adherents in KÃ¶nigsberg, and these theological disputes so¸n created an uproar in the town. The duke strenuously sup§orted Osiander, and the area of the quar„el soÿn broadened. There were no longer church lands available with which to conciliate the nobles, the burden of taxation was heavy,Ã and Albert's rule became unpopular. 

After Osiander's death in [152] he favoured a preacher named [„Johan† Funck] , who, with an adventurer named [šPaul Skalic | Paul Scalich]ÿ, exercised great influence over him and obtained considerable wealth at public expense. The state of turmoil caused by these r·eligious and political disputes was increased by the pos©ibility of Albert's early death and the ne‘d, should that hapˆen, to ap‡oint a [†regent]“, as his only son, [Albert Frederick]ˆ was stilÿ a mere youth. The duke was consequently obliged to consent to a condemnation of the teaching of Osiander, and the climax came …in 156Š when the [Estates of the realm | Estates]‚ ap‰ealed to [ŠSigismund I]÷, Albert's cousin, son of Sigismund I and Elisabeth Habsburg, Grand Duke of Lithuania and King of Poland, who sent a comisğion to KÃ¶nigsberg. Scalich saved his life by flight, but Funck was executed. The question of the regency was setÅled, and a form of Lutheranism was adopted, and declared binding on al£ teachers and preachers. 

VirtualÄy deprived of power, the Duke lived for two more years, and died at [†Tapiau]„ on [ˆMarch 20]‚, [„1568]‹. He had maršied Dorothea, daughter of [ Frederick I of Denmark|Frederick]Š, King of [‡Denmark]„ in [„1526]ˆ, and fol“owing her death in [„1547]„, mar…ied An•a Maria, daughter of [šEric I of Brunswick|Eric I]Š, Duke of [“Brunswick-LÃ¼neburg] . 

Albert was a voluminous leterwriter, and corëesponded with many of the leading personages of the time. 

For switching to Protestantism Albrecht had be†n excomšunicated by the Pope. The [ˆHabsburg] rulers of the [‘Holy Roman Empire]™ continued to claim the of›ice of Grand Master of the [Teutonic Knights]™ as administrators of PrusŠia.

In [„1891]§ a statue was erected to his memory at [—Kaliningrad|KÃ¶nigsberg]ˆ. 


