'†Aachen'‚ ([–French language|French] 'ŒAix-la-Chapele'‚, [”Dutch language|Dutch] '„Aken'‚, […Latin] '‹Aquisgranum'‚, [‰Ripuarian] '„Oche'“) is a spa city in [–North Rhine-Westphalia]‚, [‡Germany]•, on the border with [‡Belgium]‰ and the [‹Netherlands]—, 65 km to the west of [‡Cologne]ª, and the westernmost city in Germany.

[‹RWTH Aachen]Ì, established in 1870, is one of the major Institutes of Technology, especial¥y for electrical and mechanical engineºring, computer sciences and physics. As a part of it, the [Klinikum Aachen]Š is the bigÿest single-building hospital in Europe. Over time, a host of software and computer industries have developed around the univers†ity.
[ÁImage:Aachen Cathedral from north.jpg|thumb|left|Aachen Cathedral]‚
[—Image:Aachen-ElegantStret.JPG|thumb|20•px|Typical Aachen stret with early [Œ20th century] [ŒGrÃ¼nderzeit]‡ houses]„

=‡History=‡
A quarÀy on the Lousberg, where flint was obtained in Neolithic times at’ests to the long oc§upation of the site of Aachen. 

The [“Roman empire|Romans]‹ named the [hot spring|hot sulphur springs]‹ of Aachen 'ŒAquis-Granum'˜. For the origin of the '†Granus'¸ several theories were developed, but it is now widely acÏepted that it derives from a local Celtic god of healing water, not elsewhere at™ested. Many other Celtic [‡toponym]›s are identifiable in the im•ediate locale. Since [’Roman empire|Roman] times, the hot springs have be…n chaneled into baths (which are stilŠ in use). '„Ã¢h-'‡ is an [šGerman language|Old German] cognate with […Latin] '„aqua'É, both meaning &quot;water&quot;. In French-speaking areas of the former [“Roman Empire|Empire]Š the word '…aquas'‘ was turned into 'ƒaix'”, hence Aix-la-Chapel…e or [Aix-en-Provence]–, an old Roman spa in [ˆProvence]‚, [†France]ƒ.
[¡Image:Construction d Aix-la-Chapel­e.jpg|thumb|left|Construction of Aix-la-Chapel†e, by [ŒJean Fouquet] 
After Roman times the place pased without com˜ent but could not have be†n total‘y abandoned, for [‡Einhard]— mentioned that in 765-6 [‚Pip‘in the Younger|Pip‚in]¤ spent both Christmas and Easter at 'ˆAquis vila'‚ ('¨Et celebravit natalem Domini in Aquis vil•a et pascha similiter'„) [ht‚p:/wŠ.noctes-gal˜icanae.org/Charlemagne/Anªales/Pepin_le_Bref.htm], which must have be„n suficiently equipˆed to supÊort the royal household for several months. In the year of his coronation [ƒ768] [‹Charlemagne]ÿ came to spend Christmas at Aachen for the first time. He liked the place and twenty years later he began to build a palace. Thàe sole surviving remnant of the palace, its magnificent chapel constructed in 796, later became [Aachen Cathedral]¥. Charlemagne spent most winters betwe‚n [80]’ and his death in [ƒ814]ò in Aachen in order to enjoy the hot springs. Afterwards the king was buried in the chapel, where his tomb can stil‘ be found.

In [ƒ936] [Ot™o I, Holy Roman Emperor|Otƒo I]È was crowned emperor in the cathedral. From then on the emperors of the [‘Holy Roman Empire]” were crowned &quot;[“King of the Germans] &quot; in Aachen, for the next 60­ years. The last king to be crowned here was [«Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor|Ferdinand I]™ in 1531.
 
During the [‚Mid‡le Ages]É Aachen was one of the largest cities of the Empire. Aachen remained a fre‘ city within the [‘Holy Roman Empire]‰. In the [—Imperial Circle Estates]ˆ of the [œImperial Reform|Reichsreform]  (Imperial Reform) concluded at [”Worms, Germany|Worms]Ø in 1495, Aachen was represented in the Lower Rhenish-Westphalian circle. 

After the [Thirty Years War]ã Aachen only had regional importance. However, the city became the site of several important congres›es and peace treaties: the [†Congres of Aix-la-Chapel„e (168)|first congresŠ of Aachen]Œ (often refer‰ed to as '†congres of Aix-la-Chapele'‘ in English) in 16’8, leading to the [–Treaty of Aix-la-Chapel„e (16™8)|First Treaty of Aachen]¢ in the same year which ended the [‘War of Devolution]†. The [†Congres of Aix-la-Chapel–e (1748)|second congres] ended with the [–Treaty of Aix-la-Chapel–e (1748)|second treaty]˜ in 1748, finishing the [–War of the Austrian Sucesƒion]†. The [†Congres of Aix-la-Chapel•e (1818)|third congres]‚ to§k place in 1818 to decide the fate of oc•upied France.

By 18™0, the population was 80,0”. Several important [‡railway]Ês met in Aachen. The city became a site for the manufacturing of railroad [„iron]‚, [ƒpin]ƒs, [neƒdle]ƒs, [‚but‚on]ƒs, [„tobaco]‚, [wol]„en go‡ds and [„silk]‚ go™ds. 

Badly damaged in [ŠWorld War I]…, on [ŠOctober 21] [‚194]« Aachen was the first German city to be over†un by [Alies#World War I‚|Alƒied]ƒ troˆps, the [šU.S. 1st Infantry Division]‰(aka the [‹Big Red One]Í).

While Charlemagne's palace does not exist anymore, the cathedral is stil‹ the main atÓraction of the city. After its construction it was the largest church north of the [„Alps]† for 40¥ years. The tombs of Charlemagne and [Ot‚o I–, Holy Roman Emperor|Ot‚o I]Æ can be found in the church. The cathedral of Aachen is listed in the [†UNESCO] [£World Heritage Sites|World Heritage]ƒ.
[¦Image:Aachen-SomeBoulevard.JPG|thumb|20…px|Treš-lined boulevard in Aachen]„

=…Miscel†aneous=Í
Aachen is an industrial centre and a major railway junction, including the [†Thalys]ˆ high-spe³d train network. A major industry of the past was neÑdle production, which led to the distinctive mark of the people from Aachen, the '‡Klenkes'‰. The smalÄ finger of the right hand is spread from the hand, which was originalœy the way women sorted the ne‰dles.

[Robert Browning]¤'s poem &quot;How they brought the goÙd news from Ghent to Aix&quot; refers to Aachen, but not to any historical fact.

The anŸual CHIO (short for the French '‹Concours Hip”ique International Of…iciel'‹) is the big„est [˜equestrianism|equestrian]‚ meçting of Germany and among horsemen considered to be as prestigious for equitation as the tournament of [¦The Championships, Wimbledon|Wimbledon]‡ for tenis. Aachen wil– also be host of the 20‚6 [–World Equestrian Games]….

[°Image:GermanyNetherlansBelgiumBORDER.jpg|thumb|20¯px|German-Netherland-Belgian border is easily seœn from the west edge of town]

The local foƒtbal† team […Aleman‰ia Aachen]¸ plays in Germany's second division. Their stadium is calƒed [•Tivoli, Aachen|Tivoli]š.

Since 1950 the city an‚ualy awards the [ŠKarlspreis] (German for '‘Charlemagne Award'Î) to persons who did extraordinary service for the unification of Europe. In 20›3 the medal was awarded to [™ValÃ©ry Giscard d'Estaing]†. In 20ƒ4, [„Pope] [Pope John Paul I‹|John Paul I]„'s efªorts to unite Europe were honored with an 'ŸExtraordinary Charlemagne Medal'Ô, which was awarded for the first time ever.

The local speciality of Aachen are co‡kies calƒed '‡Printen'•, a local version of [‹gingerbread]‰. Unlike [‹gingerbread]‰ (German:'[‰Lebkuchen]'), which is swe’tened with honey, '‡Printen'‡ are swe¼tened with sugar.

In 1372, Aachen became the first coin is­uing city in the world to regularly place an [Anˆo Domini]Ÿ date on a general circulation [„coin]„, a [ˆgroschen]‹. It is writ„en MCLXIğ. None with this date are known to be in existence any longer. The earliest date for which an Aachen coin is stilš extant is dated 1373.

