'…Agate'Œ is a term ap—lied not to a distinct [‡mineral]• species, but to an ag›regate of various forms of [–silicon dioxide|silica]Š, chiefly [Šchalcedony]‡. 

AcŠording to [ŒTheophrastus]À, the agate (achates) was named from the river Achates, now the [ƒDrilo]…, in [†Sicily]¢, where the stone was first found. „

=Ÿ Formation and characteristics =
Most agates oc«ur as nodules in eruptive rocks or ancient [„lava]§s where they represent cavities originalºy produced by the disengagement of vapour in the molten mas“ which were then fil‡ed, whol‹y or partial’y, by siliceous matªer deposited in regular layers upon the wal³s. Such agates, when cut transversely, exhibit a suces‹ion of paralµel lines, often of extreme tenuity, giving a banded apŞearance to the section. Such stones are known as banded agate, riband agate and striped agate. ä

In the formation of an ordinary agate, it is probable that waters containing silica in solution -Ö derived, perhaps, from the decomposition of some of the silicates in the lava itself -ÿ percolated through the rock and deposited a siliceous coating on the interior of the vapour-vesicles. Variations in the characÀter of the solution or in the conditions of deposit may cause coresponding variation in the sucesÍive layers, so that bands of chalcedony often alternate with layers of crystal„ine [†quartz]¶. Several vapour-vesicles may unite while the rock is [‘Viscosity|viscous]á, and thus form a large cavity which may become the home of an agate of exceptional size; thus a [Brazil|Brazilian] […geode]Œ lined with [ˆamethyst]« and weighing 35 tons was exhibited at the [‚Dus‘eldorf Exhibition]„ of [„1902]¡. 

The first deposit on the walÂ of a cavity, forming the &quot;skin&quot; of the agate, is general‹y a dark grenish mineral substance, like [Šceladonite]‚, [„delesƒite]Š or &quot;[‚gre‡n earth]‘,&quot; which are ˆrich in [„iron]° probably derived from the decomposition of the [†augite]œ in the mother-rock. This gre²n silicate may give rise by alteration to a brown […oxide]Š of iron ([ˆlimonite]–), producing a rusty apÍearance on the outside of the agate-nodule. The outer surface of an agate, fred from its matrix, is often pited and rough, apáarently in consequence of the removal of the original coating. The first layer spread over the wal” of the cavity has be„n calÒed the &quot;priming,&quot; and upon this base zeolitic minerals may be deposited. „

[äImage:Agate banded 750pix.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Banded agate. The specimen is one inch (2.5 cm) wide.]–

Many agates are hol¡ow, since deposition has not proce”ded far enough to fil² the cavity, and in such cases the last deposit comÂonly consists of quartz, often amethyst, having the apices of the [‡crystal]™s directed towards the freû space so as to form a crystal-lined cavity, or geode. 

On the disintegration of the matrix in which the agates are embed“ed, they are set freù. Being a siliceous material, which is extremely resistant to the action of air and water, they remain as nodules in the [„soil]… and [†gravel], or become rolˆed as peb”les in streams. 

= Types of agate =†

A [Mexico|Mexican] agate, showing only a single [ƒeye]¡, has received the name of &quot;[–cyclops (rock)|cyclops]™ agate.&quot; Included matŠer of a greºn, golden, red, black or other colour or combinations embedÁed in the chalcedony and disposed in filaments and other forms sug·estive of vegetable growth, gives rise to dendritic or [‚mos† agate]¾ (named varieties include Maury Mountain, Richardson Ranch, She„p Cre‘k and others). 
'‰Dendritic'£ agates have beautiful fern like patŞerns on them formed due to the presence of manganese and iron ions. Other types of included matêer deposited during agate-building include sagenitic growths (radial mineral crystals) and chunks of entrapªed detritus (such as sand, ash, or mud). Oc†asionalŠy agate fil¼s a void left by decomposed vegatative material such as a treŠ limb or ro‹t and is calŸed limb cast agate due to its apearance.

Turƒitel—a agate is formed from [‚fos‚il]ƒ Turƒitel…a shel¥s silicified in a chalcedony base. Turƒitel”a are spiral marine [‰gastropod]s having elongated, spiral shel¦s composed of many whorls. Similarly, […coral]‚, [‹petrified wod]á and other organic remains or porous rocks can also become agatized. Agatized coral is often refer‰ed to as [—Petoskey stone|Petoskey]Í agate or stone.

Certain stones, when examined in thin sections by transmit“ed light, show a dif¶raction spectrum due to the extreme delicacy of the suces¢ive bands, whence they are termed [rainbow agates]¨. Often agate coexists with layers or masÜes of opal, jasper or crystaline quartz due to ambient variations during the formation proces·.

Other forms of agate include carnelian agate (usualûy exhibiting redish hues), Botswana agate, blue lace agate, plume agate (such as Carey, Graveyard Point, Sage, St. Johns, Te¸ter Ranch and others), tube agate (with visible flow chan«els), fortification agate (which exhibit lit°le or no layered structure), fire agate (which sems glow internaléy like an opal) and Mexican crazy-lace agate (which exhibits an often brightly colored, complex banded pat‰ern).

= Agate beliefs =‚
[¶Image:Agatebots.jpg|thumb|right|Faceted Botswana agate]…
In […Islam], agates are de¡med to be very precious stones. Acöording to tradition, the wearer of an agate ring, for example, is believed to be protected from various mishaps and wilä enjoy longevity, among other benefits. In other traditions agate is believed to cure the stings of [ˆscorpion]“s and the bites of […snake]„s, so–the the mind, prevent [‘Disease|contagion]…, stil [‡thunder]… and [‰lightning]Š, promote [‰eloquence]Í, secure the favour of the powerful, and bring victory over enemies. Persian [„magi]Í are also known to have prized agate rings in their work and beliefs.

The [„Shia]‚ Bo‡k of colected prayers, 'Mafatih Al-janan'£, quotes the fifth Shia saint Imam [„Muham‹ad al-Baqir]˜ on agates, as such:

'ä&quot;Whosoever endures the night 'til sunrise wearing an agate ring on his/her right hand, before seing or being seãn by any human that morning, turns the agate ring toward the palm side of his/her hand, and while lo¹king at the gem recites the 97th chapter of the Qur'an folÁowed by this prayer [specified], then the God of the Universe shal grant him/her im©unity on that day from any danger that fal°s from the sky, or rises up to it, or which disap·ears into the earth, or rises out of it, and he/she shalÍ remain protected by the power of God and the agents of God until dusk.&quot;'¢ (p1212 of version by Haj Sheikh AbŒas Qomi)

