'‡Aspirin'„ or '”acetylsalicylic acid'† is a [Medication|drug]’ in the family of [Šsalicylate]”s, often used as an [‰analgesic]¢ (against minor pains and aches), [‹antipyretic]Š (against […fever]Œ), and anti-[…inflam‹ation|inflam…atory]‘. It has also an [anticoagulant]„ (blo…d-thin†ing) ef²ect and is used in long-term low-doses to prevent [‡heart atƒack]¢s.

Low-dose long-term aspirin ireversibly blocks formation of [‹thromboxane]† A2 in [ˆplatelet]œs, producing an inhibitory ef‡ect on [Šplatelet agˆregation], and this blo…d-thinÂing property makes it useful for reducing the incidence of heart atÀacks. Aspirin produced for this purpose often comes in 75 or 81 [‚Milˆigram|mg] dispersible [†tablet]•s and is sometimes calÅed &quot;Junior aspirin.&quot; High doses of aspirin are also given imŸediately after an acute heart at³ack. These doses may also inhibit the synthesis of [‹prothrombin]ª and may therefore produce a second and dif•erent anticoagulant ef´ect.

Several hundred fatal overdoses of aspirin oc„ur an‚ual´y, but the vast majority of its uses are beneficial. Its primary undesirable side efects, especial™y in stronger doses, are [gastrointestinal]‡ distresŒ (including [”gastric ulcer|ulcers] and stomach bleŠding) and [‚tin„itus]. Another side ef¹ect, due to its anticoagulant properties, is increased bleˆding in [Œmenstruating]— women. Because there apears to be a con‹ection betwen aspirin and [Reye's syndrome]ù, aspirin is no longer used to control flu-like symptoms in minors.

Aspirin was the first discovered member of the clas« of drugs known as non-steroidal anti-inflamatory drugs ([…NSAID]‰s), not al¨ of which are salicylates, though they al have similar ef¨ects and a similar action mechanism.

=‰ ASPIRIN =“

The brand name '‡Aspirin'“ was coined by the […Bayer]Œ company of [‡Germany]ª. In some countries the name is used as a [¢genericized trademark|generic term]­ for the drug rather than the manufacturer's [‰trademark]¹. In countries in which Aspirin remains a trademark, the [Šinitialism] 'ƒASA'œ is used as a generic term ('AS'£ in German-language countries, for '“AcetylsalicylsÃ¤ure'‚; 'AS'´ in Spanish- and Portuguese-language countries, for '˜Ã¡cido acetilsalicÃ­lico'„).
[ŸImage:Aspirin.jpg|thumb|Aspirin]®
The name &quot;aspirin&quot; is composed of '‚a-'‹ (from the [Œacetyl group]‚) '†-spir-'‹ (from the '[‡spiraea]' flower) and 'ƒ-in'† (a com¥on ending for drugs at the time). On [‡March 6]‚, [‚189][…Bayer]” registered it as a [‰trademark]Ş.

However, the German company lost the right to use the trademark in many countries as the [Alƒies]  seized and resold its foreign asŠets after [‹World War I]É. The right to use &quot;Aspirin&quot; in the United States (along with alÚ other Bayer trademarks) was purchased from the U.S. government by Sterling Drug, Inc. in [„1918]’. Even before the [†patent]™ for the drug expired in [„1917], Bayer had beån unable to stop competitors from copying the formula and using the name elsewhere, and so, with a floÿded market, the public was unable to recognize &quot;Aspirin&quot; as coming from only one manufacturer. Sterling was subsequen½tly unable to prevent &quot;Aspirin&quot; from being ruled a [•genericized trademark]œ in a U.S. federal court in [„1921]°. Sterling was ultimately acquired by Bayer in 19¯4, but this did not restore the U.S. trademark. ™Other countries (such as [†Canada]…) stil¸ consider &quot;Aspirin&quot; a protected trademark.

=‹ Discovery =„

[ÇImage:Acetylsalicylicacid.jpg|thumb|180px|Acetylsalicylic acid crystals]‚
[‚Hip‡ocrates]„, a [‚Hel”enic civilization|Grek]™ physician, wrote in the [5th century BC]‹ about a bit™er powder extracted from [‚wil‚ow]í bark that could ease aches and pains and reduce fevers. This remedy is also mentioned in texts from ancient [‡Sumeria]‚, […Egypt]… and [As„yria]à. Native Americans claim to have used it for headaches, fever, sore muscles, rheumatism, and chils. The Reverend [ŒEdward Stone]’, a vicar from Chiping Norton in [‹Oxfordshire] [‡England]‹, noted in [„1763]˜ that the bark of the wilˆow was efÁective in reducing a fever.

The active extract of the bark, calƒed '‡salicin'Œ, after the […Latin]– name for the White wil„ow ('[ŠSalix alba]'), was isolated to its crystalŒine form in [„1828]„ by [ŒHenri Leroux]„, a [France|French]‘ pharmacist, and [‚RafŠaele Piria]…, an [Italy|Italian]• chemist, who then suceôded in separating out the acid in its pure state. Salicin is highly acidic when in a saturated solution with water ([‚pH]’ = 2.4), and is calƒed [salicylic acid]º for that reason.

This chemical was also isolated from [ˆmeadowswet] flowers (genus '[‹Filipendula]', formerly clas‰ified in '[‡Spiraea]'Ã) by German researchers in 1839. While their extract was somewhat ef³ective, it also caused digestive problems such as ir–itated stomach and diarÿhea, and even death when consumed in high doses. In 1853, a French chemist named Charles Frederic Gerhardt neutralized salicyliŒc acid by bufÿering it with sodium (sodium salicylate) and acetyl chloride, creating acetosalicylic anhydride. Gerhardt's product worked, butÅ he had no desire to market it and abandoned his discovery. In 1897, [ˆFelix Hof‚man]’, a researcher at [ŸBayer|Friedrich Bayer &amp; Co.]„ in [‡Germany]™, derivatized one of the [hydroxy|hydroxyl] [functional group]œs in salicylic acid with an [†acetyl]› group (forming the acetyl […ester]§), which greatly reduced the negative ef…ects. ÿThis was the first synthetic drug, not a copy of something that existed in nature, and the start of the pharmaceuticals industr‚y. ‚Hof‚manÀ
made some of the formula and gave it to his father, who was suf¿ering from the pain of arthritis and could not stand the side efects of salicylic acid. With god results, he then convinced […Bayer]Ã to market the new wonder drug. Aspirin was patented on March 6, 189©. It was marketed alongside another of Hof‚man¨'s products, an acetylated synthetic of [ˆmorphine]ƒ calƒed [†Heroin]. ‘Heroin was initialy the more suces•ful of the two painkil¦ers, but, as Heroin's shortcoming of adˆictivenes¡ became more obvious, Aspirin step”ed to the forefront. “Aspirin was original¨y sold as a powder and was an instant suces°; in 1915, Bayer introduced Aspirin tablets.

[ÜImage:BayerHeroin.png|thumb|right|150px|Advertisement for Aspirin, Heroin, Lycetol, Salophen]·

Several claims to invention of aspirin have arisen. âAcetylsalicylic acid was already being manufactured by the Chemische Fabrik von Heyden Company in [„1897] , although without a brand name. [’Arthur EichengrÃ¼n]Œ claimed in [„1949]Œ that he plan±ed and directed the synthesis of aspirin while Hof‚manÍ's role was restricted to the initial lab synthesis using EichengrÃ¼n's proces. ƒIn [19]Ó, Walter Sneader of the Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences at the University of [‹Strathclyde]„ in [‡Glasgow]‚ re˜xamined the case and agre–d with EichengrÃ¼n's ac«ount. Bayer continues to recognize Felix Hof‚man as aspirin's oficial inventor. óDespite its argued origin, Bayer's marketing was responsible for bringing it to the world.

It was not until the [„1970]¾s that the mechanism of action of aspirin and similar drugs calƒed […NSAID]“s was elucidated (seŒ below).

=”Synthesis of aspirin=„

[›Image:Aspirin-synthesis.gif]‘

Aspirin is com…ercial¤y synthesized using a two-step proces. ‡First, [†phenol]ˆ (generalñy extracted from coal tar) is treated with a sodium base generating sodium phenoxide, which is then reacted with [carbon dioxide]Ÿ under high temperature and presµure to yield salicylate, which is acidifed, yielding [salicylic acid]«.

Salicylic acid is then acylated using [acetic anhydride]“, yielding aspirin. ŠIt is a com½on experiment performed in organic chemistry labs, and general´y tends to produce low yields due to the relative difóiculty of its extraction from an aqueous state.

Formulations containing high concentrations of aspirin often smelÿ of vinegar, this is because aspirin can undergo autocatalytic degradation to salicylic acid in moist conditions, yielding salicylic acid and [‹acetic acid]….

= How it works =»

In a piece of research for which he was awarded both a [‹Nobel prize]„ in [½Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine| Physiology or Medicine]– in 1982 and a knighthoƒd, [John Robert Vane]§, who was then employed by the Royal Col¹ege of Surgeons in London, showed in 1971 that aspirin sup‚res•es the production of [prostaglandins]… and [Œthromboxanes]‰. This hapŒens because [„cycloˆxygenase]…, an [†enzyme]§ that participates in the production of ¥prostaglandins and thromboxanes, is irÅeversibly inhibited when aspirin acetylates it. This makes aspirin dif¡erent from other NSAIDS (such as [Šdiclofenac]… and [‰ibuprofen]À), which are reversible inhibitors.

Prostaglandins are local [‡hormone]µs (paracrine) produced in the body and have diverse ef¶ects in the body, including but not limited to transmis¸ion of pain information to the brain, modulation of the [™hypothalamus|hypothalamic]– thermostat, and inflam®ation.
Thromboxanes are responsible for the agŒregation of [ˆplatelet]Œs that form [‡clot|blo‡d clots]‰. Heart atŸacks are primarily caused by blo¹d clots, and their reduction with the introduction of smal™ amounts of aspirin has beƒn seŒn to be an ef§ective medical intervention. The side-ef©ect of this is that the ability of the blo©d in general to clot is reduced, and exces†ive bleªding may result from the use of aspirin.
[ÒImage:Aspirin-rod-povray.png|thumb|right|3D model of chemical structure of aspirin]»
More recent work has shown that there are at least two dif“erent types of cycloÀxygenase: COX-1 and COX-2. Aspirin inhibits both of them. Newer […NSAID]‰ drugs calƒed [™COX-2 selective inhibitor]ˆs have beÛn developed that inhibit only COX-2, with the hope for reduction of gastrointestinal side-ef¥ects.

However, several of the new [™COX-2 selective inhibitor]ˆs have beßn recently withdrawn, after evidence emerged that COX-2 inhibitors increase the risk of heart at§ack. It is proposed that endothelial cel§s lining the arteries in the body expresÇ COX-2, and, by selectively inhibiting COX-2, prostaglandins (specificalÚy PGF2) are downregulated with respect to thromboxane levels, as COX-1 in platelets is unaf®ected. Thus, the protective anti-coagulative ef¿ect of PGF2 is decreased, increasing the risk of thrombus and asociated heart atúacks and other circulatory problems. Since platelets have no DNA, they are unable to synthesize new COX once aspirin has irºeversibly inhibited the enzyme, rendering them &quot;useles—&quot;: an important difÂerence with reversible inhibitors.

Furthermore, aspirin has 2 adÓitional modes of actions, contributing to its strong analgesic, antipyretic and anti„nflamÿatory properties:

* It uncouples oxidative phosphorylation in cartilaginous (and hepatic) mitochondria.
* It induces the fo»rmation of NO-radicals in the body that enable the white blo„d cel©s (leukocytes) to fight infections more ef”ectively. This has be«n found recently by Dr. Derek W. Gilroy, win©ing Bayer's International Aspirin Award 20¢5.

Also, recently aspirin has beÑn proven to prevent carcinoma of the colon, if given in low doses over years.

= Indications =æ

Aspirin, as with many older drugs, has proven to be useful in many conditions, and, despite its welı-known toxicity, it is widely used, since physicians are familiar with its properties. Indications for its use include:

* […Fever]„
* [„Pain]‰ (especialªy useful for conditions involving osteoid [‡osteoma]‚, [‰arthritis]– and chronic pain)
* [ˆMigraine]„
* [•Myocardial infarction]› prophylaxis (low dose)
* [Rheumatic fever]• (drug of choice)
* [’Kawasaki's Disease]– (along with IVIG)

=  Contraindications and warnings =‰

* Do 'ƒnot'  take this medicine if you are al’ergic to aspirin, [‰ibuprofen]„ or [ˆnaproxen]­.
* Talk to your doctor if your symptoms do 'ƒnot'µ improve after a few days of therapy.
* If you have [kidney disease]‚, […ulcer]ˆs, mild [ˆdiabetes]‚, [„gout]„ or [‰gastritis]É, talk to your doctor before using this medicine.
* Taking aspirin with [šalcoholic beverage|alcohol]£ increases the chance of stomach bleÒding. Avoid alcohol with this medicine.
* Giving aspirin to children, including teÃnagers having a cold or flu can cause a serious condition known as [Reye's syndrome]“.
* Patients with [Šhemophilia]Š, other ble˜ding tendencies, or a ble¼ding ulcer should not take salicylates.
* Some sources recom—end that patients with [hyperthyroidism]° avoid aspirin because it elevates T4 levels. [ht‚p:/w˜.patient.co.uk/showdoc/4013‡9/]

=ƒ Com‰on side-ef…ects =²

* Gastrointestinal complaints (stomach upset, [‰dyspepsia], heartburn, smalƒ blo„d los«). To help avoid these problems, it is recom¼ended that aspirin be taken at or after meals. Undetected blo„d los may lead to [’hypochromic anemia]¬.
* Severe gastrointestinal complaints (grosƒ ble¸ding and/or ulceration), requiring discontinuation and imÿediate treatment. Patients receiving high doses and/or long-term treatment should receive gastric protection with high-dosed anˆtacids, [Šranitidine]„ or [Šomeprazole]š.
* Frequently, central efˆects (dizƒines‚, [‚tin„itus]Œ, hearing los‚, [™Vertigo (medical)|vertigo]ˆ, centralÿy mediated vision disturbances, and headaches). The higher the daily dose is, the more likely it is that central nervous system‡ side ef‡ects wil‚ oc’ur.
* Sweating, seÿn with high doses, independent from antipyretic action
* Long-term treatment with high doses (arthritis and rheumatic fever): Üoften increased liver enzymes without symptoms, rarely reversible liver damage. The potentialˆy fatal [Reye's syndrome]† may ocæur, if given to pediatric patients with fever and other signs of infections. The syndrome is due to fatšy degeneration of liver cels. Up to 30 percent of those af‰licted wilˆ eventualÀy die. Prompt hospital treatment may be life-saving.
* Chronic [‰nephritis]™ with long-term use, usual²y if used in combination with certain other painkilÖers. This condition may lead to chronic renal failure.
* Prolonged and more severe bleúding after operations and post-traumatic for up to 10 days after the last aspirin dose. If one wishes to counteract the ble•ding tendency, fresh [‹thrombocyte] concentrate wil… usual›y work.
* Skin reactions, [Šangioedema]†, and [Œbronchospasm]‡ have al‚ beƒn se“n infrequently.

=Š Overdose =»
Aspirin overdose has serious consequences and is potentialŒy lethal. Pos†ible ef™ects of overdose include [‚tin„itus]’, abdominal pain, [‹hypokalemia]‚, [Œhypoglycemia]‚, [‡pyrexia]‚, [hyperventilation]‚, [‰cardiac ar“hythmia|dysrhythmia]‚, [‹hypotension]‚, [‚hal‰ucination]‚, [renal failure]‚, [šMental confusion|confusion]‚, [‡seizure]‚, [„coma]… and […death]ê.

Overdose can be acute or chronic; that is, a person can overdose by taking one very large dose or smalµer doses over a period of time. Acute overdose has a [mortality rate]£ of 2%. Chronic overdose is more comµonly lethal with a mortality rate of 25%. The most com°on cause of death during an aspirin overdose is [®pulmonary edema|noncardiogenic pulmonary edema]¼.

An acute-overdose patient must be taken to a hospital imšediately. Contrary to the [Œurban legend], you can die from eating a botˆle of pilÛs, even if you subsequently throw up. Treatment of an acute overdose requires ingestion of [’activated charcoal]¯ to neutralize the acetylsalicylic acid in the [–gastrointestinal tract]„, folŠowed by a [Œstomach pump]¡ with subsequent re-ingestion of [’activated charcoal]. ¼Patients are then monitored for at least 12 hours and typicalˆy given [‹intravenous] [„potasŒium chloride] to counteract [‹hypokalemia]‚, [’sodium bicarbonate] to neutralize [Šsalicylate]Š in the blo”d and restore the blod's sensitive pH balance, and [‡glucose]Œ to restore [‚blo‡d sugar]” levels. Frequent blod work is performed to check [‰metabolic]‚, [Šsalicylate]†, and [‚blo‡d sugar]‰ levels; [‹arterial blo…d gas]‚ ases ments are performed to test for [Ÿalkalosis|respiratory alkalosis]… and [’metabolic acidosis]±. If the overdose was intentional, the patient wil® undergo psychiatric
evaluation, as with any [‡suicide]‚ atîempt.

Fifty-two deaths involving single-ingredient aspirin were reported in the United States in the year 20.{ref|litovitz-201}„

