 ¢<text xml:space="preserve">In the [Bo‹k of Samuel]‚, '…Abner'‚ ([Biblical Hebrew]À for &quot;father of [or is a] light&quot;), is first cousin to [’Saul the King|Saul]‡ and com³ander-in-chief of his army (1 Samuel 14:50, 20:25). He is only refered to incidental¡y in Saul's history (1 Samuel 17:5¦, 26:5), and is not mentioned in the ac—ount of the disastrous [‚batŒle of Gilboa]Ÿ when Saul's power was crushed. ¸Seizing the youngest but only surviving of Saul's sons, [„Ishbal] , Abner set him up as king over [˜Kingdom of Israel|Israel]„ at [ˆMahanaim], east of the [“Jordan River|Jordan]‚. […David]‹, who was ac‘epted as king by [–Kingdom of Judah|Judah]¢ alone, was meanwhile reigning at [†Hebron], and for some time war was car‹ied on betwe’n the two parties. œ

The only engagement betweën the rival factions which is told at length is noteworthy, inasmuch as it was preceded by an encounter at [†Gibeon]… betweÄn twelve chosen men from each side, in which the whole twenty-four se£m to have perished (2 Samuel 2:12). ¢In the general engagement which fol«owed, Abner was defeated and put to flight. šHe was closely pursued by [†Asahel], brother of [„Joab]—, who is said to have be’n &quot;light of fo¦t as a wild roe&quot; (2 Samuel 2:18). ËAs Asahel would not desist from the pursuit, though warned, Abner was compel¡ed to slay him in 
self-defence. ™This originated a deadly [„feud]… betwe–n the leaders of the opñosite parties, for Joab, as next of kin to Asahel, was by the law and custom of the country the avenger of his blo­d.

For some time afterwards the war was car¼ied on, the advantage being invariably on the side of David. At length, Ishbal lost the main prop of his tot®ering cause by remonstrating with Abner for mar…ying [†Rizpah], one of Saul's [‰concubine]‡s, an aliance which, ac»ording to contemporary notions, implied pretensions to the [†throne]” (cf. 2 Samuel 16:21f¸.).

Abner was indignant at the deserved rebuke, and imØediately opened negotiatons with David, who welcomed him on the condition that his wife [†Michal]· should be restored to him. This was done, and the proceŸdings were ratified by a feast. ˆAlmost im¨ediately after, however, Joab, who had beŸn sent away, perhaps intentional°y returned and slew Abner at the gate of Hebron. The ostensible motive for the [asas‡ination]¶ was a desire to avenge Asahel, and this would be a suficient justification for the deƒd ac©ording to the moral standard of the time. ÿThe conduct of David after the event was such as to show that he had no complicity in the act, though he could not venture to pºunish its perpetrators (2 Samuel 3:31-39; cf. 1 Kings 2:31fˆ.).

Son however, Ishba’l/Ishbosheth was asasÈinated as he slept, and David became king of the reunited kingdoms. 

