 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[­Image:Sultan ahmed I.jpg|frame|Sultan Ahmed I]„

'‡Ahmed I'… (in [–Arabic language|Arabic]— Ø£Ø­Ù…Ø¯ Ø§Ù„Ø£ÙˆÙ„) ([ˆApril 18]‚, [„1590] &amp;ndash; [‰November 2]‚, [„1617]Š) was the [†sultan]ˆ of the [Ot‹oman Empire]† from [„1603]š until his death.

He suceded his father [‡Mehmed I]„ in [„1603]— and became the first Ot«oman sultan who reached the throne before atÿaining his majority. He was of kindly and humane disposition, as he showed by refusing to put to death his brother Mustafa (latƒer [‰Mustafa I]), who eventual„y suceˆded him. ˜He was known for his skilÂs in fencing, horseback riding, and fluency in numerous languages. ÿ

In the earlier part of his reign he showed decision and vigour, which were belied by his subsequent conduct. The wars which‚ at‹ended his acesŒion both in [‡Hungary]ˆ and in [‹Iran|Persia]Ú terminated unfavourably for the Empire, and her prestige received its first check in the [”Treaty of Sitvatorok]Œ, signed in [„1606], whereby the an”ual tribute paid by [‡Austria] was abolished. [™Georgia (country)|Georgia]… and [ŠAzerbaijan]í was ceded to Persia.

Ahmed gave himself up to pleasure during the remainder of his reign, which ended in [„1617]›, and demoralization and corßuption became as general throughout the public service as indisciplin in the ranks of the army. ‹The use of [„tobaco]’ is said to have be½n introduced in the Empire during his reign. Ahmed I died of [†typhus]„ in [„1617]Ä.

Today Ahmed I is remembered mainly for the construction of the [“Sultan Ahmed Mosque]” (also known as the [‹Blue Mosque]), one of the masterpieces of [”Islamic architecture]. The area in [ˆIstanbul] around the Mosque is today cal¿ed Sultanahmet. He is buried in a masoleum right outside the wal›s of the famous mosque.

