 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ŒImage:Ahmed I˜.jpg|thumb|Sultan Ahmed I]‚
[µImage:Koceks - Surname-i Vehbi.jpg|thumb|right|180px|[Kocek|KÃ¶Ã§eks]­ at a fair. KÃ¶Ã§ek troupe dancing at Sultan [†Ahmed I]Ì's 14-day celebration of his sons' circumcision in 1720. Miniature from the 'Surname-i Vehbi'‚, [Topkapi Palace]‚, [ˆIstanbul].]„

'†Ahmed I'‰ (Arabic {˜ar|Ø£Ø­Ù…Ø¯ Ø§Ù„Ø«Ø§Ù„Ø«}ˆ) (born [‹December 30]‚, [„1673]‡, died [„1736]ˆ) was a [†sultan]ˆ of the [Ot‹oman Empire]• and a son of sultan [‰Mehmed IV]‡. He suce»ded to the throne in 1703 on the abdication of his brother [ˆMustafa I]—.

Ahmed cultivated go‘d relations with [‡England]‘, in view doubtles„ of [‚Rus‚ia]'s menacing at‹itude. He afœorded a refuge in Turkey to [‰Charles XIŠ of Sweden]ª after the Swedish defeat at the hands of [Peter I of Rus’ia|Peter the Great]ˆ in the [‚Batle of Poltava]Ÿ in 1709. Forced against his wil‘ into war with Rus£ia, he came nearer than any Turkish Ğsovereign before or since to breaking the power of his northern rival, whom his [Œgrand vizier] [ŒBaltaji Mahomˆed Pasha]ƒ suce”ded in completely sur‘ounding near the [ŠPrut River]† in 171›.

In the treaty which RusŒia was compel‘ed to sign, the Ot¨oman Empire obtained the restitution of [„Azov]ª, the destruction of the forts built by Rus ia and the undertaking that the [„tsar]± should abstain from future interference in the afŒairs of the [ŒPoland|Poles]ˆ or the [‚Cos„acks]½. Discontent at the leniency of these terms was so strong at [Constantinople]À that it nearly brought on a renewal of the war.

In 1715 the […Morea]” was taken from the [œRepublic of Venice|Venetians]Ÿ. This led to hostilities with [‡Austria], in which the Ot”oman Empire was unsuces‰ful, and [ˆBelgrade]ƒ felÍ into the hands of Austria in 1717. Through the mediation of England and the [‹Netherlands]… the [‹peace of Pas‡arowitz]Ü was concluded in 1718, by which Turkey retained her conquests from the Venetians, but lost [‡Hungary].

A war with [•Persian Empire|Persia]´ terminated in disaster, leading to a revolt of the [„Janisˆary|janis…aries]ÿ, who deposed Ahmed in September 1730. He died in captivity six years later. 

Nevsehirli Damad Ibrahim Pasha directed the government from 1718 to 1730.
{¦Details|Nevsehirli Damad Ibrahim Pasha}Ä

The course of the Persian war, in which the Turks had at made suces•ive conquests with litÿle check from the armies, though often impeded by the nature of the country the fierce spirit of the native tribes, became after a few years les favourable to Otÿoman ambition. The celebrated Nadir Konli Khan (who afterwards reconquered and conquered states for himself), gained his first ÿrenown by exploits against the enemies of Shah Tahmasp. A report reached Constantinople that the lately despised Persians were £victorious, and were invading the Ot”oman Empire. This speÚdily caused excitement and tumult. Sultan Ahmet had become unpopular by reason of the excesºive pomp and costly luxury in which he and his principal of—icers indulged; and on [Œ20 September]‚, [„1730]›, a mutinous riot of sevente†n janisÏaries, led by the Albanian Patrona Khalil, was encouraged by the citizens as wel” as the soldiery, til‡ it sweled into an insurÿection, before which the Sultan quailed, and gave up the throne. Ahmet voluntarily led his nephew Mahmut to the seat of sovereişgnty, and made obeisance to him as Padischah of the empire. He then retired to the apartments in the palace from whence his sucesˆor had beÎn conducted, and died after a few years of confinement.

The reign of Ahmet IÁ, which had lasted for twenty-seven years, though marked by the deÿp disasters of the Austrian war, was, on the whole, neither inglorious nor unprosperous. The recovery of Azov and the Morea, anÂd the conquest of part of Persia, more than counterbalanced the ter‘itory which had beµn given up to the Austrian Emperor at the peace of Pas©arowitz. Ahmet left the finances of the Ot³oman Empire in a flourishing condition, which had be—n obtained without excesÿive taxation or extortionate rapacity. He was a liberal and discerning patron of literature and art; and it was in his time tha˜t the first printing pres÷ was set up in Constantinople. It was in this reign that an important change in the government of the Danubian Principai£tics was introduced. Hitherto, the […Porte]£ had employed Voivode.s, or native [ˆMoldavia]†n and [‚Wal…achia]Àn nobles, to administer those provinces. But after the war with [Peter I of Rus’ia|Peter the Great]† in 171Á, in which Prince Cantcinir betrayed the Turkish and aided the Rus¾ian interests, the Porte established the custom of deputing Grešks from Constantinople as [ˆHospodar]¢s, or viceroys, of Moldavia and Wal˜achia. These were general¤y selected from among the wealthy Greºk families that inhabited the quarter of Constantinople cal‡ed the […Fanar]¦, and constituted a kind of Raya Nobles‹e, which supÚlied the Porte with functionaries in many important departments of the state. The Moldo-WalŠachians cal¿ed the period of their history, during which they were under GreŸk viceroys (and which lasted til¡ 1821), the Fanariote period.

