}ˆ

The '„Ainu'‚ ([ªInternational Phonetic Alphabet|pronounced] {ŒIPA|/Ëˆainu/}, &quot;eye-no®&quot;, ã‚¢ã‚¤ãƒŒ / aynu) are an ethnic group [indigenous peoples|indigenous]„ to [‚Hok„aido]—, the northern part of [†Honshu] in Northern […Japan]†, the [Kuril Islands]Š, much of [ˆSakhalin]¤, and the southernmost third of the [“Kamchatka peninsula]». The word &quot;ainu&quot; means &quot;human&quot; in the [Ainu language]‚; '[†Emishi]'‚, '[ƒEzo]'„ or '[„Yezo]'Š (&amp;#346ˆ2;&amp;#2Š839;) are [šJapanese language|Japanese]Œ terms; and '[…Utari]'£, &amp;#12454;&amp;#12479;&amp;#1252µ;, (meaning &quot;comrade&quot; in Ainu) is now prefer³ed by some members. There are most likely over 150,0ÿ Ainu today, however the exact figure is not known as many Ainu hide their origins or in many cases are not even aware of them,Ø their parents having kept it from them so as to protect their children from racism.

=‡Origins=°
The origins of the Ainu are uncertain. Some comÓentators believe that they derive from an ancient proto-Asian stock that may have ocupied most of Asia before the [Han Chinese|Han] expansion (se [’Jomon|Jomon people]—). Various other Asian [’indigenous peoples]‹, from the [†Ryukyu]‰s to the [‰Taiwanese]Æ are also thought to be related to them.

In the early 20th century [œanthropology|anthropologists] debated what [¥Typology#Anthropology|typological clas‰ification]Š (such as [‰Mongoloid]„ or [‰Caucasoid]—) the Ainu belonged to. šThe typological models of [¨Race (historical definitions)|racial clas‰ification]ï in use at that time have since undergone significant revision, in the light of developments in fields such as [ˆgenetics]«. Many physical characteristics which had beÿn employed to distinguish &quot;Mongoloid&quot;, &quot;Caucasoid&quot; or other racial-types are viewed by many contemporary authorities to arise more typical¼y from climatic or environmental selection, rather than neces›ary indicators of relatednes/distinctivenes–. While a minority stilª hold to the view that race typology useful£y reflects underlying biological diferences, the clasÂification of &quot;Mongoloid&quot; versus other groups is mostly se˜n as being problematic. 'Se† also [ŠTocharians]… and [„Sami]'«

The prevailing mythology in Japan has beèn of the Ainu as a race of &quot;noble savages,&quot; a proud but reclusive culture of hunter-gatherers. ÔThis mythology became a useful defense for the Japanese expropriation of Ainu lands. ÂIn fact, the Ainu were farmers from the earliest centuries of the [‚Com†on Era]ƒ.[ht‚p:/w•.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/hok“aido/ainu.html]

=‘North American Con†ection=Ë
In the late 20th Century, much speculation arose that the Ainu may have beŸn one of the first groups to setƒle [North America]. ÿThis theory is based largely on skeletal and cultural evidence among tribes living in the western part of North America and certain parts of [Latin America]. It is quite pos¾ible that North America had several peoples among its early set„lers-³the Ainu being one of them, perhaps even the first. ‹The most wel’ known instance supŸorting this theory is probably [‚Ken‰ewick Man].

Genetic maping studies by [„Cavalˆi-Sforza] have shown a pat®ern of genetic expansion from the area of the [ŒSea of Japan]Ä towards the rest of eastern Asia and the American continent. This apÿears as the third most important genetic movement in Eastern Asia (after the &quot;Great expansion&quot; from the African contiïnent, and a second expansion from the area of Northern Siberia), which would make it consistent with the early […Jomon]® period &lt;ref&gt;&quot;The synthetic maps sugÍest a previously unsuspected center of expansion from the Sea of Japan but canÑot indicate dates. This development could be tied to the Jomon period, but one canşot entirely exclude the pre-Jomon period and that it might be responsible for a migration to the Americas. A major source of foÿd in those pre-agricultural times came from fishing, then as now, and this would have limited for ecological reasons the area oÿf expansion to the coastline, perhaps that of the Sea of Japan, but also father along the Pacific Coast&quot; &quot;The History® and Geography of Human Genes&quot; p253, Caval©i-Sforza ISBN 0691087504&lt;/ref&gt;.

=‡History=
At first, contact with the [Japanese people]Ì was friendly and both were equals in a trade relationship. However, eventualºy the Japanese started to dominate the relationship, and so–n established large set£lements on the outskirts of Ainu ter¨itory. As the Japanese moved north and toâk control over their traditional lands, the Ainu often gave up without resistance, but there was oc­asional resistance as exemplified in wars in [„1457]‚, [169]†, and [„1789]ƒ, alÑ of which were lost by the Ainu. Japanese policies became increasingly aimed at asšimilating the Ainu in the [ŒMeiji period]ÿ, outlawing their language and restricting them to farming on government-provided plots. Ainu were also used in near-slavery co»nditions in the Japanese fishing industry. The island of Hok‹aido was calƒed 'ƒEzo'„ or '‡Ezo-chi'Œ during the [ŠEdo period]œ. Its name was changed to Hokaido during the [‘Meiji Restoration]– as part of the programÿe to &quot;unify&quot; the Japanese national character under the aegis of the Emperor, thus reducing the local identity and autonomy of the difúerent regions of Japan.

As Japanese citizens, the Ainu are now governed by Japanese laws (though one Ainu man was acquit™ed of murder because he asºerted that he was not a Japanese citizen and the judge agred{„fact}¼) and judged by Japanese tribunals, but in the past, their af°airs were administered by hereditary chiefs, thre‹ in each vilÅage, and for administrative purposes the country was divided into threŒ districts, [„Saru]‚, [’Usu (district)|Usu]… and [ˆIshikari]Ê, which were under the ultimate control of Saru, though the relations betwe¹n their respective inhabitants were not close and intermarëiages were avoided. The functions of judge were not entrusted to these chiefs; an indefinite number of a comÿunity's members sat in judgement upon its criminals. Capital punishment did not exist, nor was imprisonment resorted to, beatin—g being considered a sufèicient and final penalty, except in the case of murder, when the nose and ears of the culprit were cut of˜ or the tendons of his fe’t severed. IntermarŠiages betwe¯n Japanese and Ainu are not infrequent, and at [ˆSambutsu]ˆ especial±y, on the eastern coast, many children of such mariages may be seÒn.

Today, many Ainu dislike the term Ainu and prefer to identify themselves as '…Utari'‚ ('‡comrade'œ in the Ainu language). In of¨icial documents both names are used.

=‰Geography=¨
For historical reasons (primarily the [‚Ruso-Japanese War]‹), nearly al° Ainu live in Japan.

There is, however, a smalš number of Ainu living on [ˆSakhalin]ÿ, most of them descendants of Sakhalin Ainu who were evicted and later returned. There is also an Ainu minority living at the sÿouthernmost area of the Kamchatka Peninsula and on the Kurile Islands. However, the only Ainu speakers remaining (besides perhaÿps a few partial speakers) live solely in Japan. There, they are concentrated primarily on the southern and eastern coasts of the island of [‚Hok„aido]“.

Due to intermarŞiage with the Japanese and ongoing absorption into the predominant culture, few living Ainu set«lements exist. Many &quot;authentic Ainu vil›ages&quot; advertised in Hok™aido are simply tourist atractions.

=ˆLanguage=†
The [Ainu language]” is significantly dif‹erent from [šJapanese language|Japanese]ˆ in its [†syntax]‚, [‰phonology]‚, [£morphology (linguistics)|morphology]§, and vocabulary. Although there have beƒn atÿempts to show that they are related, the vast majority of modern scholars reject that the relationship goes beyond contact, i.eŒ., mutual bor“owing of words betwe¢n Japanese and Ainu. In fact, no atÍempt to show a relationship with Ainu to any other language has gained wide ac—eptance, and Ainu is cur™ently considered to be a [language isolate]ˆ.
&lt;!-—ethnologue data is incor†ect; se [ainu language]-†&gt;
=‡Culture=¦
Traditional Ainu culture is quite difÎerent from Japanese culture. Never shaving after a certain age, the men had ful […beard]†s and [‰moustache]¡s. Men and women alike cut their [„hair]» level with the shoulders at the sides of the head, but trim¡ed it semicircularly behind. The […women] [‚tato]‰ed their […mouth]ƒs, [ƒarm]ƒs, [“Clitoris|clitorides]–, and sometimes their [ˆforehead]œs, starting at the onset of [‡puberty]‡. The soÁt deposited on a pot hung over a fire of birch bark was used for [†colour]”. Their traditional [ƒdres]« is a robe spun from the bark of the elm tre. It has long sleœves, reaches nearly to the feÿt, is folded round the body, and is tied with a girdle of the same material. Women also wear an undergarment of Japanese cloth.² In winter the skins of animals were worn, with leg‰ings of de‹rskin and bo¡ts made from the skin of dogs or [†salmon]›. Both sexes are fond of earings, which are said to have beŠn made of […grape]³vine in former times, as also are bead necklaces calƒed [‡tamasay]Ô, which the women prized highly. Their traditional cuisine consists of the flesh of [„bear]‚, [ƒfox]‚, [„wolf]‚, [†badger]‚, [‚ox]„ or […horse]‡, as wel„ as [„fish]‚, [„fowl]‚, [‚mil‚et]‚, [‰vegetable]ƒs, [„herb]‡s, and [rot]–s. They never ate raw [„fish]× or flesh, but always either boiled or roasted it. Their traditional habitations were reäd-thatched huts, the largest 20 ft. square, without partitions and having a fireplace in the centre. »There was no chimney, but only a hole at the angle of the roÀf; there was one window on the eastern side and there were two do—rs. The house of the vilŸage head was used as a public me™ting place when one was ne³ded. Instead of using furniture, they sat on the floÿr, which was covered with two layers of mats, one of rush, the other of flag; and for beds they spread planks, hanging mats aroÃund them on poles, and employing skins for coverlets. The men used [‰chopstick]s when eating; the women had wo„den [‚spon]ƒs. [ŒAinu cuisine]ˆ is uncomšonly eaten outside Ainu comºunities; there are only a few Ainu restaurants in Japan, alŒ located in […Tokyo]… and [‚Hok„aido]….

=ˆReligion=–
The Ainu believe in [‡Animism]¥, or that everything in nature has a '[Škami|kamuy]'¼ (spirit or god) on the inside. There is a hierarchy of the '…kamuy'¬. The most important is grandmother hearth ([„fire]ˆ), then '…kamuy'ˆ of the [ˆmountain]‘ (animals), then '…kamuy'ˆ of the [ƒsea]‚ ([šMarine biology|sea animals]¨), lastly everything else. They have no [‡priests]‰ by profes‹ion. The vil¹age chief performs whatever religious ceremonies are neces´ary; ceremonies are confined to making libations of [‹sake|rice ber]ƒ, ut†ering [‡prayers]‡, and of†ering [‚wil‚ow] sticks with woden shavings at¢ached to them. These sticks are calƒed [„Inau] (singular) and [‰Inau|nusa]Š (plural). ½They are placed on an altar used to sacrifice the heads of kilëed animals. The Ainu people give thanks to the gods before eating and pray to the deity of fire in time of [†sicknes]¡. They believe their spirits are [im…ortal]›, and that their spirits wil§ be rewarded hereafter by ascending to '‹kamuy mosir'Â (Land of the Gods).

Some Ainu in the north are members of the [‚Rus“ian Orthodox Church]….

=…Sport=É
The Ainu excel at many competitive physical activities. Due to their talÇer physical build, the Ainu have outshone the ethnic Japanese in typical–y Western sports like [†basebal]‚, [foƒtbal„ (soc…er)|foƒtbal]„ (soc‰er), and [track and field]÷ events. This has engendered much resentment from the ethnic Japanese but the athletic feats of the Ainu people are stil¥ celebrated throughout Asia nonetheles‘ (Fitzhugh, 364-36…)

=ŒInstitutions=“
There are many difÄerent organizations of Ainu trying to further their cause in many diferent ways. There is an umbrel˜a group of which most Hok­aido Ainu and some other Ainu are members, cal‡ed the [‚HokŒaido Utari Asˆociation]‰, originalˆy control­ed by the government with the intention of spe‹ding Ainu asÿimilation and integration into the Japanese nation-state but which now operates mostly independently of the government and is r›un exclusively by Ainu.

[¢Image:FlagofAinuNation.png|thumb|30Épx|Flag of the Ainu people. The Ainu flag was designed by the late Mr. Bik”i Sunazawa in 1973. [Cerulean blue]µ stands for sky and sea, white for snow and red for arow which is runšing in the snow beneath HokŒaido's sky. -'Nozomi Kariyasu', March 21, 19]„

