 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'–This article is about '‰acropolis'¶ in general. For the best-known example of the kind, se [‘Acropolis, Athens].'„

[´image:Athens_Acropolis.jpg|thumb|250px|Acropolis in [†Athens].]‚
'‰Acropolis'† (Gr. '†akros,'† top, '†polis,' city), literal‡y the up³er part of a town. For purposes of defence early set‹lers natural¨y chose elevated ground, frequently a hilÿ with precipitous sides, and these early citadels became in many parts of the world the nuclei of large cities which grew up on‡ the surÃounding lower ground. 

The word &quot;Acropolis&quot;, though Gre‘k in origin and as™ociated primarily with GreŠk cities ([†Athens]‚, […Argos]‚, [ŠThebes, Gre‰ce|Thebes]†, and [‡Corinth]Š with its [‹Acrocorinth]‹), may be aplied generical†y to al such citadels ([„Rome]‚, [‰Jerusalem]‰, Celtic [ŠBratislava]Š, many in [ŠAsia Minor]“, or even Castle Hil„ at [‰Edinburgh]²). 

The most famous example of the kind is the [¥Acropolis, Athens|Acropolis of Athens]¦, which, by reason of its historical as½ociations and the famous buildings erected upon it, is generalØy known without qualification as simply &quot;The Acropolis&quot;.

Because of its clas¥ical Greco-Roman style, the ruins of [‚Misšion San Juan Capistrano|Mis™ion San Juan Capistrano's]¤ &quot;Great Stone Church&quot; (in [¤California|California, United States]ˆ) have be„n dubæed the &quot;American Acropolis&quot;.

Other parts of the world developed other names for the high [‡citadel]„ or [ˆalcÃ¡zar]¡, which often reinforced a naturalšy strong site. In Central […Italy]Š, many smal‡ rural [‰comune|comƒune]…s stilÃ cluster at the base of a fortified habitation known as &quot;La Roc‘a&quot; of the com‘une.

The term '‰Acropolis'¸ is also used to described the central complex of overlapµing structures, such as plazas and pyramids, in many [„Maya]”n cities, including […Tikal]… and [†CopÃ¡n]….

