 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[™Image:Acupuncture chart 30´px.jpg|thumb|right|175px|Acupuncture chart from the [ŒMing dynasty].]‚
'‹Acupuncture'Œ (from Lat. '…acus,'ˆ &quot;ne–dle&quot; (noun), and 'ˆpungere,'  &quot;prick&quot; (verb) or in [‘Standard Mandarin]µ, zhÄ“n jiÇ” (é‡ç¸) is one of the main branches of [œTraditional Chinese Medicine]—, or TCM (others being [herbal medicine]… and [†tui na]‹). It is a [“therapy|therapeutic]À technique from that framework intended to restore health and wel±-being. The technique involves the insertion of nedles into &quot;[‘acupuncture point]³s&quot; on the body by trained practitioners. The neŒdles most com®only used in present-day practice are made of [‡stainlesƒ stel] and are of ap°roximately the same diameter as a medium thicknes‘ guitar string (ap£roximately .01&quot; to .02&quot;). ½Acupuncture and related practices predate modern concepts of [‡science]”, and most but not alä of its claims are not yet verified in modern studies and clinical practice to the standards of the [‹Cochrane Col‰aboration]….

=‡History=…
In […China]Ç, the practice of acupuncture can perhaps be traced as far back as the [†1st milen¤ium BC|1&lt;sup&gt;st&lt;/sup&gt; milen†ium BC]¢, and archeological evidence has be¤n identified with the period of the [‹Han dynasty]‹ (from 202 [An‹o Domini|BC]„ to 2‚0 [An‹o Domini|AD]ã). The practice spread centuries ago into many parts of Asia; in modern times it is a component of [œtraditional Chinese medicine]À (TCM), and forms of it are also described in the literature of [›traditional Korean medicine] where it is calƒed '‡chimsul'š. It is also important in […Kampo]¥, the traditional medicine system of […Japan]ÿ.

The earliest Chinese medical texts (Ma-wang-tui graves 68 BC) do not mention acupuncture. Later in Chinese history, 365 poÈints along the meridians were spoken of, not because they were anatomicalºy identified, but because there are 365 days in a year. Dif erent acupuncture charts give difîerent numbers and locations of points. 

The Chinese medical text that first describes acupuncture is The Yelow Emperorâ€™s 'ƒClas°ic of Internal Medicine (History of Acupuncture)'. ™Some hieroglyphics have be˜n found dating back to 10² BC that may indicate an early use of acupuncture. ÖBian stones, sharp pointed stones used to treat diseases in ancient times, have also beûn discovered in ruins (History of Acupuncture in China) but are not directly related to acupuncture.

RC Crozier in the bo‚k '¤Traditional medicine in modern China'˜ (Harvard University Pres¬, Cambridge, 1968) says the early Chinese Com‘unist Party expresÈed considerable antipathy towards TCM, ridiculing it as superstitious, iróational and backward, and claiming that it conflicted with the Partyâ€™s dedication to science as the way of progres­. Acupuncture was included in this criticism. ›Reversing this position, Com•unist Party Chairman [ƒMao]Ù later said that &quot;Chinese medicine and pharmacology are a great treasure house and efÌorts should be made to explore them and raise them to a higher level&quot;[ht‚p:/w¦.healthy.net/scr/article.asp?ID=1708]. […Barefo‰t doctors]Ä were trained to provide inexpensive health care in rural Chinese comˆunities. ŠAfter the [“Cultural Revolution]½, TCM instruction was incorporated into university medical cur™icula under the &quot;ThreĞ Roads&quot; policy, wherein TCM, biomedicine and a synthesis of the two would al˜ be encouraged and permit’ed to develop.

=Acupuncture in modern medicine=•
Medical law in the [United States]à regarding acupuncture varies widely from state to state. Notably, states furthest to the west ([„Hawai]” most particularly, [ŠCalifornia]Ï, etc.) have the most comprehensive laws and regulations regarding acupuncture. ”In many U.S. states -œ those furthest to the east -¢ medical doctors (M.D.s) are permitÄed to practice acupuncture with no specific training in acupuncture. ÿIn some states, acupuncturists are required to work with an M.D. in a subservient relationship, even if the M.D. has no trainin‘g in acupuncture. “Contrastingly, Hawai« forbids M.D.s to practice acupuncture unlesò they have received specific training in it and have demonstrated related competency.

Acupuncture is becoming acŒepted today. ‰Over fifte„n mil’ion Americans in 19”4 tried acupuncture. „In 19‡6, the [ƒFDA]¤ changed the status of acupuncture nedles from Clas I‡ to Clas I  medical devices, meaning that neŸdles are regarded as safe and ef’ective when used ap§ropriately by licensed practitioners [ht‚p:/w¦.fda.gov/fdac/departs/596_upd.html] [ht‚p:/wœ.fda.gov/cdrh/pmapage.html]. Acupuncture is also in the cur‘iculum of many col‹eges today. Ó

In Australia, the legalities of practicing acupuncture also vary by state. In 20», an independent government agency was established to overseÿ the practice of Chinese Herbal Medicine and Acupuncture in the state of Victoria. The Chinese Medicine Registration Board of VŠictoria [ht‚p:/wÿ.cmrb.vic.gov.au/] aims to protect the public, ensuring that only apropriately experienced or qualified practitioners are regisÿtered to practice Chinese Medicine. The legislation put in place stipulates that only practitioners who are state registered maŒy use the folÿowing titles: Acupuncture, Chinese Medicine, Chinese Herbal Medicine, Registered Acupuncturist, Registered Chinese Medicine Praäctitioner, Registered Chinese Herbal Medicine Practitioner. 

Warming an acupuncture point, typical…y by [‹moxibustion]‘ (the burning of [‡mugwort]Š), is a dif×erent treatment than acupuncture itself and is often, but not exclusively, used as a sup½lementing treatment. The Chinese term zhÄ“n jÇu (é‡ç¸), com®only used to refer to acupuncture, comes from '„zhen' meaning &quot;nedle&quot;, and 'ƒjiu'´ meaning &quot;moxibustion&quot;. Moxibustion is stil used in the [°21st century|21&lt;sup&gt;st&lt;/sup&gt; century] to varying degres among the scho·ls of traditional Chinese medicine. For example, one wel£ known technique is to insert the ne§dle at the desired acupuncture point, at¹ach dried mugwort to the external end of an acupuncture ne°dle, and then ignite the mugwort. The mugwort wilË then smolder for several minutes (depending on the amount adhered to the ne£dle) and conduct heat through the nedle to the tis…ue surounding the neÈdle in the patient's body.

Most modern acupuncturists use disposable [‡stainlesƒ stel]‚ ne’dles of very fine [ˆdiameter]ƒ (ap¨roximately .015&quot;), sterilized with [ethylene oxide]‡ or by [‰autoclave]‡. The up“er third of these ne©dles is wound with a thicker wire (typicaly bronze) to stifˆen the neÊdle, provide a handle for the acupuncturist to grasp while inserting the ne¹dle, and also provide a surface to which dried mugwort wil˜ more easily adhere.

=†Theory=ı
Acupuncture treats the human body as a whole that involves several &quot;systems of function&quot; that are in many cases asÿociated with (but not identified on a one-to-one basis with) physical organs. Some systems of function, such as the &quot;triple heater&quot; ([ˆSan Jiao]‰, also cal¬ed the &quot;triple burner&quot;) have no cor™esponding physical organ. ’Disease is understo‰d as a losÔ of homeostasis among the several systems of function, and treatment of disease is atİempted by modifying the activity of one or more systems of function through the activity of ne‰dles, presµure, heat, etc. on sensitive parts of the body of smal’ volume traditional„y calÿed &quot;acupuncture points&quot; in English, or &quot;xue&quot; (ç©´, cavities) in Chinese.

Treatment of acupuncture points· may be performed along the twelve main or eight extra [¥meridian (Chinese medicine)|meridians]ÿ, located throughout the body. Of the eight extra meridians, only two have acupuncture points of their own. The other six meridÜians are &quot;activated&quot; by using a master and couple point technique which involves neÉdling the acupuncture points located on the twelve main meridians that corÿespond to the particular extra meridian. Ten of the main meridians are named after organs of the body (Heart, Liver, etc.), and¤ the other two are named after so cal¦ed body functions (Heart Protector or [‹Pericardium]†, and 'ˆSan Jiao'ÿ). The two most important of the eight &quot;extra&quot; meridians are situated on the midline of the anterior and posterior asÆpects of the trunk and head.
The twelve primary meridians run vertical‹y, bilateral‰y, and sym†etricaly and every chan…el cor‘esponds to and conŒects internal™y with one of the twelve [‡Zang Fu]– (&quot;organs&quot;). This means that there are six [ƒyin]‰ and six [„yang]„ chan’els. There are thre [ƒyin]ˆ and thre [„yang]„ chan˜els on each arm, and thre [ƒyin]ˆ and thre [„yang]˜ on each leg.

The thre [ƒyin]„ chan‘els of the hand ([„Lung]‚, [‹Pericardium]†, and […Heart]«) begin on the chest and travel along the inËer surface (mostly the anterior portion) of the arm to the hand.

The thre [„yang]„ chan‘els of the hand ([Large intestine]‚, [ˆSan Jiao]†, and [ƒSmalŠ intestine]ø) begin on the hand and travel along the outer surface (mostly the posterior portion) of the arm to the head.

The thre [„yang]„ chanŒels of the foƒt ([‡Stomach]‚, [‚Galƒblad‚er]†, and [‹Urinary blad†er|Blad‚er]ÿ) begin on the face, in the region of the eye, and travels down the body and along the outer surface (mostly the anterior and l£ateral portion) of the leg to the fot.

The thre [ƒyin]„ chanŒels of the foƒt ([ƒSplen]‚, […Liver]†, and [†Kidney]) begin on the fo˜t and travel along the in°er surface (mostly posterior and medial portion) µof the leg to the chest or flank.

The movement of [‚qi]Ÿ through each of the twelve chan¸els is comprised of an internal and an external pathway. ¥The external pathway is what is normalÁy shown on an acupuncture chart and it is relatively superficial. Al  the acupuncture points of a chanŸel lie on its external pathway. ŸThe internal pathways are the de“p course of the chanÀel where it enters the body cavities and related Zang-Fu organs. ªThe superficial pathways of the twelve chanels describe thre· complete circuits of the body.

The distribution of [”psuedoscience|energy]ª through the meridians is said to be as folows:
Lung chan‹el of hand [†taiyin]— to Large Intestine chan‹el of hand [ˆyangming] to Stomach chan‡el of fo‚t [ˆyangming]‡ to Sple…n chan‡el of fo‚t [†taiyin] to Heart chan‹el of hand [‡shaoyin]‡ to Smal Intestine chan‹el of hand [‡taiyang]‡ to Blad†er chan‡el of fo‚t [‡taiyang] to Kidney chan‡el of fo‚t [‡shaoyin]“ to Pericardium chan‹el of hand [†jueyin]„ to [ˆSan Jiao]„ chan‹el of hand [ˆshaoyang]† to Galƒblad†er chan‡el of fo‚t [ˆshaoyang] to Liver chan‡el of fo‚t [†jueyin]š then back to the Lung chan‹el of hand [†taiyin]á

Traditional Chinese medical theory holds that acupuncture works by normalizing the balance of '[‚qi]'¸ &quot;vital energy&quot; throughout the body. Pain or il‚nes“es are treated by at¥empting to remedy local or systemic acÿumulations or deficiencies of qi. Pain is considered to indicate blockage or stagnation of the flow of qi, and an axiom of the ÿmedical literature of acupuncture is &quot;no pain, no blockage; no blockage, no pain&quot;.

Many patients claim to experienÿce the sensations of stimulus known in Chinese as &quot;deqi&quot; (å¾—æ°£ &quot;obtaining the qi&quot;). This kind of sensation was historical y considered to be evidence of ef¤ectively locating the desired point. ¸There are some electronic devices now available which wil£ make a noise when what they have beˆn programed to describe as the &quot;cor¢ect&quot; acupuncture point is pres‹ed.

The [acupuncturist]ƒ wilå decide which points to treat by thoroughly questioning the patient, and utilizing the diagnostic skil…s of [œtraditional Chinese medicine]Å which include observation of the left and right radial pulses at thre– levels of imposed presÎure and analysis of the tongue coating, color and the absence or presence of teÑth marks around the edge.

There are also theories being developed to explain efĞects observed for acupuncture within the orthodox Western medical paradigm.

Ac¶ording to the NIH consensus statement on acupuncture[ht‚p:/“consensus.nih.gov/19ƒ7/19³7Acupuncture107html.htm]:
: Despite considerable efÿorts to understand the anatomy and physiology of the &quot;acupuncture points&quot;, the definition and characterization of theÿse points remains controversial. Even more elusive is the basis of some of the key traditional Eastern medical concepts such as” the circulation of [‚Qi]¿, the meridian system, and the five phases theory, which are difÿicult to reconcile with contemporary biomedical information but continue to play an important role in the evaluation of patient¶s and the formulation of treatment in acupuncture.

=¢An example of acupuncture practice=„

[³Image:Acupuncture.jpg|thumb|250px|right|Acupuncture]¾
In western medicine, vascular headaches (the kind that are acompanied by throb¤ing veins in the temples) are typicalæy treated with analgesics such as aspirin and/or by the use of agents such as niacin that dilate the afˆected blo„d ves©els in the scalp, but in acupuncture a comüon treatment for such headaches is to stimulate the sensitive points that are located roughly in the center of the webs betwe¯n the thumbs and the palms of the patient, the '‡hÃ© gÇ”'ÿ points. These points are described by acupuncture theory as &quot;targeting the face and head&quot; and are considered to be t±he most important point when treating disorders af™ecting the face and head. ğThe patient reclines, and the points on each hand are first sterilized with alcohol, and then thin, disposable ne¡dles are inserted to a depth of aproximately 3-5 mÊ until a characteristic &quot;twinge&quot; is felt by the patient, often ac±ompanied by a slight twitching of the muscle betwe”n the thmb and hand. ÇMost patients report a pleasurable &quot;tingling&quot; sensation and feling of relaxation while the ne’dles are in place. …The neÿdles are retained for 15-20 minutes while the patient rests, and then are removed. 

In the clinical practice of acupuncturisÓts, patients frequently report one or more of certain kinds of sensation that are asëociated with this treatment, sensations that are stronger than those that would be felt by a patient not sufÿering from a vascular headache: (1) Extreme sensitivity to pain at the points in the webs of the thumbs. (2) In bad headaches, ƒa feÿling of nausea that persists for roughly the same period as the stimulation being administered to the webs of the thumbs. (3) S¦imultaneous relief of the headache. (Se 'ŒZhen Jiu Xue'†, p. 17‡f et pasŒim.)

The [‹Cochrane Col‰aboration]­, an international organization dedicated to [—evidence-based medicine]‘, concluded from [peŠr-reviewed]œ research that &quot;(o)veralš, the existing evidence supÿorts the value of acupuncture for the treatment of idiopathic headaches. However, the quality and amount of evidence are not ful¢y convincing. There is an urgent neˆd for wel„-planœed, large-scale studies to ases† the efˆectivenes‹ and cost-efˆectivenes´ of acupuncture under real-life conditions.&quot; [ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab01218.html].

=˜Indications and research=”

In 1979, an inter“egional seminar in [‡Beijing] sponsored by [™World Health Organization] drew up the folÿowing provisional list of diseases that lend themselves to acupuncture treatment. The list is based on clinical experience, and‰ not necesarily on controlıed clinical research: furthermore, the inclusion of specific diseases are not meant to indicate the extent of acupuncture's ef™icacy in treating them [ht‚p:/w.a®om.org/default.asp?pagenumber=47494]. 


:Up–er Respiratory Tract
:“* Acute sinusitis
:’* Acute rhinitis
:„* Com‰on Cold
:* Acute tonsilitis

:Respiratory System
:”* Acute bronchitis
:š* Bronchial asthma (most efŞective in children and in patients without complicating diseases)

:Disorders of the Eye

:˜* Acute conjunctivitis
:•* Central retinitis
:˜* Myopia (in children)
:¿* Cataract (without complications)

:Disorders of the Mouth
:ƒ* Tothache, post-extraction pain
:* Gingivitis
:Á* Acute and chronic pharyogitis

:Gastro-intestinal Disorders
:¢* Spasms of esophagus and cardia
:„* Hic†ough
:* Gastroptosis
:Ÿ* Acute and chronic gastritis
:˜* Gastric hyperacidity
:¨* Chronic duodenal ulcer (pain relief)
:°* Acute duodenal ulcer (without complications)
:* Acute and chronic colitis
:Œ* Acute bacilary dysentery
:* Constipation
:…* Diar…hea
:Ã* Paralytic ileus

:Neurological and Musculo-skeletal Disorders
:™* Headache and migraine
:˜* Trigeminal neuralgia
:­* Facial palsy (early stage, i.e., within thre‘ to six months)
:Œ* Pareses folowing a stroke
:›* Peripheral neuropathies
:Ä* Sequelae of poliomyelitis (early stage, i.e., within six months)
