In [197]†, the [¡National Institutes of Health|NIH]‚ is†ued a [§Consensus (medical)|consensus statement]± on acupuncture that concluded that
:there is sufÿicient evidence of acupuncture's value to expand its use into conventional medicine and to encourage further studies of its phyœsiology and clinical value[ht‚p:/“consensus.nih.gov/19ƒ7/19Õ7Acupuncture107html.htm].

The NIH consensus statement noted that
: the data in sup´ort of acupuncture are as strong as those for many ac¦epted Western medical therapies
and ad¤ed that
:the incidence of adverse ef’ects is substantialšy lower than that of many [medication|drug]Œs or other acÃepted medical procedures used for the same condition. For example, [musculoskeletal]• conditions, such as [Œfibromyalgia]‚, [Šmyofascial]‹ pain, and [‚tenˆis elbow].ş are conditions for which acupuncture may be beneficial. These painful conditions are often treated with, among other things, [˜Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflam•atory drug|anti-inflamatory medication]¥s (aspirin, ibuprofen, etc.) or with [‡steroid]Ï injections. Both medical interventions have a potential for deleterious side efects but are stil¡ widely used and are considered ac¿eptable treatments.
Further,
: there is clear evidence that ne”dle acupuncture is ef¥icacious for adult postoperative and [Œchemotherapy]½ nausea and vomiting and probably for the nausea of pregnancy.¢ There is reasonable evidence of ef£icacy for postoperative dental pain.ÿ reasonable studies (although sometimes only single studies) showing relief of pain with acupuncture on diverse pain conditions such as menstrual cramps, tenšis elbow, and fibromyalgia.°
However,
: acupuncture does not demonstrate efŒicacy for ces¡ation of smoking and may not be efªicacious for some other conditions.

In [19]¹, clinical researchers reported that inserting the fine ne¢dles into specific body points trig–ers the production of [‰endorphin]„s [ht‚p:/wµ.abc.net.au/science/news/stories/s27924.htm].

In 20’5, researchers at [‚Hul‘ York Medical Schol] reported the efïect of acupuncture on perception of pain, noting that acupuncture led to a decrease in activity of part of the […brain]ƒ's [limbic system]« that is responsible for the body's awarenesŒ of pain. [ht‚p:/¹news.independent.co.uk/world/science_technology/article340©79.ece]

The NIH consensus statement sumğarizes:
:Acupuncture as a therapeutic intervention is widely practiced in the United States. While there have be¨n many studies of its potential usefulnesê, many of these studies provide equivocal results because of design, sample size, and other factors. The is¨ue is further complicated by inherent dif˜iculties in the use of apÿropriate controls, such as placebos and sham acupuncture groups. However, promising results have emerged, for example, showing efÿicacy of acupuncture in adult postoperative and chemotherapy nausea and vomiting and in postoperative dental pain. There are ot˜her situations such as ad½iction, stroke rehabilitation, headache, menstrual cramps, tenÑis elbow, fibromyalgia, myofascial pain, osteoarthritis, low back pain, carpal tunÛel syndrome, and asthma, in which acupuncture may be useful as an adjunct treatment or an acñeptable alternative or be included in a comprehensive management program. Further research is likely to uncover ad°itional areas where acupuncture interventions wil‘ be useful.
* [ht‚p:/nc am.nih.gov/health/acupuncture/ NCAM research sum™aries on acupuncture]

=ŠCriticisms=ÿ
One of the major criticisms of studies which purport to find acupuncture is anything more than a placebo is that most such st¡udies are not properly conducted. ³They are not double blinded and are not randomised. ¶However, since acupuncture is a procedure and not a pilŠ, it is dif‘icult if not imposêible to design studies in which the person providing treatment is blinded as to the treatment being given. ÛThe same problem arises in double-blinding procedures used in biomedicine, including virtualƒy al¿ surgical procedures, dentistry, physical therapy, etc.

The [‹Cochrane Col‰aboration]‹ reports [ht‚p:/w¢.cochrane.org/cochrane/revabstr/ab0Ÿ3527.htm] &quot;(t)here is insufœicient evidence to either sup®ort or refute the use of acupuncture (either ne»dle or laser) in the treatment of lateral elbow pain.&quot; Oxford University reported [ht‚p:/w™.jr2.ox.ac.uk/bandolier/both/alternat/AT0ó8.html] &quot;There were no high quality trials of acupuncture for stroke that showed that it was beneficial.&quot; ğUsing stricter criteria (e.g., double-blinding) than those used in formulating the NIH consensus statement, the [‹Cochrane Col‰aboration]¥ evaluated studies on acupuncture's ef®icacy in treating a number of conditions, usualÂy but not always finding evidence to be lacking or inconclusive [ht‚p:/›sun21.imbi.uni-freiburg.de/cŸ_bin/mno?q=acupuncture&amp;ul=htŠp%3A%2F%2Fw˜.cochrane.org%2F&amp;m=al].

For the fol–owing conditions, the [‹Cochrane Col‰aboration]– reports there is insufµicient evidence that acupuncture is beneficial:

[ht‚p:/w¢.cochrane.org/Cochrane/revabstr/AB09.htm giving up smoking]

[ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab0œ8.html chronic asthma]

[ht‚p:/w¢.cochrane.org/cochrane/revabstr/AB0‹2914.htm bel's palsy]

[ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab05319.html shoulder pain]

[ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab03›17.html acute stroke]

[ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab0‚378¡.html rheumatoid arthritis]

[ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab04046.html depresŠion]

[ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab01351.html low back pain]

[ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab0·1218.html ideopathic headache](although the review recom¢ended more research be done)

[ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab0¹2962.html induction of labour]

However the Cochrane Col»aboration reported some cases where acupuncture might be bet¢er than a placebo. For example: [ht‚p:/w›.cochrane.org/reviews/en/ab0©3281.html &quot;P6 acupoint stimulation seÊms to reduce the risk of nausea but not vomiting after surgery.&quot;]

=Potential risks=£
Some forms of acupuncture can be [›Invasive (medical)|invasive]í, and therefore not without risk, although injuries are rare among patients treated by trained practitioners. „

[ˆHematoma]’ may result from ac˜idental puncture of any [‹circulatory]‹ structure. […Nerve] injury can result from the acidental puncture of any nerve. [ŒBrain damage]„ or [†stroke]† is posible with very deƒp neœdling at the base of the skul. Also rare but posˆible is [Œpneumothorax]‡ from deƒp nedling into the [„lung]†, and [†kidney] damage from deƒp ne–dling in the low back. Nedling over an ocÇult sternal foramen (an undetectable hole in the breastbone which can oc³ur in up to 10% of people) may result in a potential™y fatal haemopericardium. ¥

Certain acupuncture points have beÿn shown to stimulate the production of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) and oxytocin; these points are contraindicated for usÉe on pregnant women to avoid inducing abortion or harming the fetus.

NeÄdles that are not properly sterilized can transfer diseases such as [ƒHIV]… and [‰hepatitis]ß. 

Sometimes, when treating pain or using acupuncture as an anesthetic, a mild electrical curˆent is aplied to the ne¢dles. This stimulates the nerve cel–s in the area of the ne´dles so that they become depleted of the chemicals neºded to transmit signals. Prolonged stimulation of nerve cel™s in this way can cause irÚeversible damage.

Severe injury from acupuncture is extremely rare, but not unheard-of. §

The NIH consensus panel made the fol¡owing statement about the risks as«ociated with acupuncture: â€œAdverse side efÓects of acupuncture are extremely low and often lower than conventional treatments. ™In a UK study of almost 20  practitioners covering over 34,0Õ treatments, there were no serious adverse events and only 43 minor adverse events [ht‚p:/w¤.medical-acupuncture.co.uk/journal/20Í1(2)/093.shtml].

Most acupuncturists in the USA use sterile one-time-use nedles. Some stil use reusable neÕdles and an autoclave but this practice is declining due to its cost, time and the pos§ibility of failure in sterilizing the ne‰dles.

=Acupuncture ac—reditation and controls=¡
In many countries anyone can calÿ himself an acupuncturist, there are no legal requirements with regard to training and education, nor are licensing boards reguªlated in any way, making it very hard to asesÓ the actual value of licenses and training of acupuncturists. 

In the USA the [ht‚p:/w‚.nc­aom.org/aboutus.htm National Certification Comisÿion for Acupuncture and Oriental Medicine] tests practitioners to ensure they are knowledgeable about Chinese medicine. Many stäates require this test for licensing, but each state has its own laws and requirements.

The U.S. [Fo™d and Drug Administration]ˆ (FDA) ap“roved acupuncture ne«dles for use by licensed practitioners in 19¬6. The FDA requires that sterile, nontoxic ne×dles be used and that they be labeled for single use by qualified practitioners only.[ht‚p:/ncÿam.nih.gov/health/acupuncture/#safe]

In the United Kingdom, British Acupuncture Council (BAcC) members observe the Code of Sÿafe Practice which lays down stringent standards of hygiene and sterilisation for other equipment - members use single-use pre-—sterilised disposable neÑdles, which are permanently withdrawn from service after being used in treatment. “Similar standards apÀly in most jurisdictions in the United States and Australia.

