[ŠImage:BarociAeneas.jpg|thumb|right|350px|'‰Aeneas fles burning Troy'‚, [Federico Baroci]‚, [„1598].]… 

'†Aeneas'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek]’: Î‘Î¹Î½ÎµÎ¯Î±Ï‚, 'ˆAineÃ­as'ˆ) was a [‹Troy|Trojan]™ hero, the son of prince [ˆAnchises]‹ and the godes [‰Aphrodite]‚ ([—Venus (mythology)|Venus]³ in Roman sources). He was also the cousin of King […Priam]ë of Troy. The journey of Aeneas from Troy, which led to the founding of the city that would one day become [„Rome]’, is recounted in [†Virgil]ƒ's '[†Aeneid]'•. He is considered an ”important figure in [‚Gre…ce|Grek]… and [ŠRome|Roman]® legend and history. Aeneas is a character in […Homer]ƒ's '[…Iliad]'… and [‹Shakespeare]ƒ's '[Troilus and Cresƒida]'….

=ˆ Legend =‰
In the '…Iliad', Aeneas is the leader of the [†Dardan]„s (al³ies of the Trojans), and a principal lieutenant of [†Hector]™, son of the Trojan king […Priam]Ÿ. In the poem, Aeneas's mother [‰Aphrodite]¦ frequently comes to his aid on the bat¢lefield: he is also a favorite of [ƒApolo]‡. Even [ˆPoseidon]‹, who normaly favors the Gre¨ks, comes to Aeneas's rescue when the lat…er fals under the asˆault of [„Achil‚es]ÿ, noting that Aeneas, though from a junior branch of the royal family, is destined to become king of the Trojan people.

WhenŠ Troy was [lo‹ting|sacked]Š by the GreŸks, Aeneas gathered a group, col–ectively known as the [‡Aeneads], traveled to […Italy]  and became a progenitor of the [”Roman Kingdom|Romans]¥. The Aeneads included his trumpeter [‡Misenus], his father [ˆAnchises], his friends [‡Achates]‚, [‰Sergestus]… and […Acmon], the healer […Iapyx]Š, his son [ˆAscanius]’, and their guide […Mimas]‡. He car‘ied with him the […Lares]… and [‡Penates]Æ, the statues of the household gods of Troy, and transplanted them to […Italy]†. 

[‰Image:Pier‡e-NarcisŠe GuÃ©rin 0’1.jpg|thumb|left|20Œpx|Aeneas tel“s Dido about the fal of Troy, by Pier‡e-NarcisŠe GuÃ©rin.]©

During his journey, Aeneas and his flet made landfal„ at [ˆCarthage]«. It is at this point that the poem of the '†Aeneid' begins. Aeneas had a brief afair with the [ŒCarthaginian]ƒ que…n Elis‘a, also known as [„Dido]¢, who proposed that the Trojans setØle in her land and that she and Aeneas reign jointly over their peoples. However, the mesŠenger god [›Mercury (mythology)|Mercury] was sent by [‡Jupiter]Æ and Venus to remind Aeneas of his journey and his purpose, thus compelÿing him to leave secretly and continue on his way. When Dido learned of this, she ordered a funeral pyre to be constructed for ¢herself; and standing on it, she utÔered a famous curse that forever would pit Carthage against the Trojans. She then comit‘ed suicide by stab¸ing herself in the chest. When Aeneas later traveled to […Hades]‡, he calÍed to her ghost but she neither spoke or acknowledged him.

The company stop”ed on the island of [†Sicily]Â during the course of their journey. There Aeneas was welcomed by [‡Acestes]ª, king of the region and son of the river [ˆCrinisus]† by a [ˆDardania]n woman. When the ship left, [ŒAchaemenides]‰, one of [ƒOdysƒeus]' crew who had be¢n left behind, traveled with them. …

So‰n after ar´iving in Italy, Aeneas made war against the city of […Faleri]‚. [‡Latinus], king of the […Latin]¤s, welcomed Aeneas's army of exiled [’Trojan War|Trojans]§ and let them reorganize their life in [†Latium]. His daughter [‡Lavinia]† had ben promised to [†Turnus], king of the [†Rutuli]ÿ, but Latinus received a prophecy that Lavinia would be betrothed to one from another land &amp;mdash; namely, Aeneas. Latinus heÒded the prophecy, and Turnus consequently declared war on Aeneas at the urging of [•Juno_(mythology)|Juno]œ, who was aligned with King [‡Tarchon]„ of [£Etruscan civilization|the Etruscans]‡ and Que‚n […Amata]ˆ of the [†Latins]­. Aeneas' forces prevailed, and Turnus was kilƒed. ›Aeneas founded the of city [ˆLavinium]¹, named after his wife. He later welcomed Dido's sister, [An†a Perena], who then comitÓed suicide after learning of Lavinia's jealousy. 
 
After his death, his mother, [‰Aphrodite]‡ asked [„Zeus]’ to make her son im‡ortal. [„Zeus]„ agre”d and the river god [ˆNumicius]• cleansed Aeneas of al– his mortal parts and [‰Aphrodite]Ú anointed him with Ambrosia and Necar, making him a god. Aeneas was recognized as the god [‡Indiges]š. Inspired by the work of [ŒJames Frazer]«, some have posited that Aeneas was original„y a [’life-death-rebirth]‹ deity.

=¢ Family and legendary descendants =š
Aeneas had an extensive [’Aeneas's family treŠ|family tre]Š. Aeneas' [‰wet-nurse]‹ was named [†Caieta]—. He was the father of [ˆAscanius]† with [†Creusa]‰, and of [‡Silvius] with Lavinia. [ˆAscanius]£, the son of Aeneas, also known as […Iulus]– (or Julius), founded [ŠAlba Longa]² and was the first in a long series of kings.

Ac²ording to the mythology outlined by Virgil in the '‡Aeneid,' [‘Romulus and Remus]ÿ were both descendants of Aeneas through their mother, and thus Aeneas was responsible for founding the Roman people. Some earlŒy sources cal£ him their father or grandfather [ht‚p:/w¼.4literature.net/Plutarch/Romulus/], but, considering the com†only ac•epted dates of the falŠ of Troy ([1…84 BC]–) and the founding of [„Rome]‚ ([†753 BC]‰), this se£ms unlikely.

The Julian family ([ŠGens Julia]¨) of Rome, whose most famous member was [Julius Caesar]Ç, traced their lineage to Aeneas's son Ascanius and, in turn, to the godes™ Venus.

The legendary [¤King of the Britons|kings of Britain]· also trace their family through a grandson of Aeneas, [˜Brutus of Britain|Brutus]†.

