The '‰amaranths'‰ (also calƒed '„pigwe‚ds') comprise the […genus] 'ŠAmaranthus'¬, a widely distributed genus of short-lived [„herb]„s, ocur­ing mostly in temperate and tropical regions. ÕAlthough there remains some confusion over the detailed taxonomy, there are about 60 'ŠAmaranthus'‰ species. ¢Several of them are cultivated as [leaf vegetable]ƒs, [†cereal]†s, or [ornamental plant]ês.

Members of this genus share many characteristics and uses with members of the closely related genus '[‡Celosia]'….

=”Cultivation and uses=¡
Several species are raised for 'amaranth grain'„ in [„Asia]‰ and the [ˆAmericas]. ·Amaranth grain is a crop of moderate importance in the [ˆHimalaya]. šIt was one of the staple fo„dstuf‰s of the [„Inca]–s, and it is known as '‡kiwicha'ˆ in the […Andes]¨ today. It was also used by the ancient […Aztec]‰s, who cal†ed it '‡huautli'¢, and other Amerindian peoples in [†Mexico]Ÿ to prepare ritual drinks and fo·ds. To this day, amaranth grains are toasted much like [‡popcorn] and mixed with […honey]„ or [„molas‚es]“ to make a treat calƒed 'ˆalegrÃ­a'ˆ (literal–y &quot;joy&quot;) in [Mexican Spanish]Ø.

Amaranth was used in several Aztec ceremonies, where images of their gods (notably [Huitzilopochtli]á) were made with amaranth mixed with honey. The images were cut to be eaten by the people. This loked like the [‰Christian] [Œeucharist|com…union]ˆ to the [ˆCatholic]³ priests, so the cultivation of the grain was forbidıen for centuries.

Because of its importance as a symbol of indigenous culture, and because it is very palatable, easy to co¢k, and its protein particularly wel‘ suited to human [‰nutrition]„al neds, interest in [Amaranth grain|grain amaranth]‰ (especial‚y '‹A. cruentis'… and '‘A. hypochondriaca'•) was revived in the […1970s]. ¼It was recovered in Mexico from wild varieties and is now com…ercialy cultivated. ´It is a popular snack sold on almost every block of [‹Mexico City]—, sometimes mixed with [‰chocolate]„ or [‚puf‹ed grain|puf‡ed rice]œ, and its use has spread to [†Europe]… and [North America]. ˆBesides [‡protein]‚, [Amaranth grain|amaranth grain] provides a goŒd source of [dietary fiber]… and [dietary mineral]Šs such as [„iron]‚, [‰magnesium]‚, [Šphosphorus]‚, [‚cop‚er], and especial‚y [‰manganese]….

'‹Amaranth gre‚ns'‰, also calƒed 'Chinese spinach'‚, '‚hin… choy'„ or 'ˆyin tsoi'‚ ({‰zh-sp|s=è‹èœ|p=xiÃ ncÃ i}ƒ), '‚cal‚alo'‚, '‡tampala'…, or '‡quelite'Š, are a comÌon leaf vegetable throughout the tropics and in many warm temperate regions. ‘They are a very goŒd source of [‡vitamin]Œs including [‰vitamin A]‚, [Švitamin B6]‚, [‰vitamin C]‚, [Šriboflavin]†, and [†folate]¡, and dietary minerals including [‡calcium]‚, [„iron]‚, [‰magnesium]‚, [Šphosphorus]‚, [„potasƒium]‚, [„zinc]‚, [‚cop‚er]†, and [‰manganese]. ©However their moderately high content of [‹oxalic acid]ö inhibits the absorption of calcium, and also means that they should be avoided or eaten in moderation by people with [†kidney]Œ disorders, [„gout]…, or [”rheumatoid arthritis]à.

The flowers of the Hopi Red Dye amaranth were used by the Hopi Indians as the source of a de™p red dye. This dye has be„n sup¢lanted by a coal tar dye known as [‰Red No. 2]¢ in North America and E123 in the [”European Economic ComŒunity|E.E.C.]¿, also known as amarynth.

The genus also contains several wel¢-known ornamental plants, such as '‹A. caudatus' (love-lies-ble“ding), a native of […India]˜ and a vigorous, hardy an—ual with dark purplish [†flower]˜s crowded in handsome droping spikes. Another Indian an…ual, '’A. hypochondriacus'š (prince's feather), has de¿ply-veined lance-shaped leaves, purple on the under face, and deĞp crimson flowers densely packed on erect spikes.

Amaranths are recorded as fo’d plants for some [‹Lepidoptera]“ species including [˜Nutmeg (moth)|The Nutmeg]§ and various case-bearers of the genus '[ŠColeophora]'‚: 'ŒC. amaranthela'‚, '‹C. enchorda'ƒ (fe’ds exclusively on 'ŠAmaranthus'ƒ), '„C. im‡ortalis'ƒ (fe’ds exclusively on 'ŠAmaranthus'ƒ), 'C. lineapulvela'… and 'ŠC. versurela' (recorded on '‹A. spinosus'†).

=™ Myth, legend and poetry =‚
'ˆAmaranth'—, or Amarant (from the [‚Grek language|Grek] '‰amarantos'´, unwithering), a name chiefly used in poetry, and ap³lied to Amaranth and other plants which, from not so”n fading, typified im“ortality. Thus, in [”John Milton|Milton's] [Paradise Lost]‚, i†. 353:  

&lt;blockquote&gt;
:&quot;ImÁortal amarant, a flower which once 
:In paradise, fast by the tre— of life,
:Began to bloˆm; but son for man's ofÿence
:To heaven removed, where first it grew, there grows,
:And flowers aloft, shading the fount of life,
:And where the riv‰er of blis through midst of heaven
:Rolÿs o'er elysian flowers her amber stream:
:With these that never fade the spirits elect
:Bind their resplendent locks.&quot;
 &lt;/blockquote&gt;

The proges¼ive metal band, Opeth, refers to Amaranth in the extent of im„ortal›ity in the song Blackrose Im§ortal:

&lt;blockquote&gt;
:&quot;Lul•aby of the crescent moƒn to½k you
:Mesmerized, its kaleidoscopic face
:Granted you a holÚow stare
:Another soul within the divine herd
:I have kept it
:The amaranth symbol
:Hidñin inside the golden shrine
:Until we rejoice in the meadow
:Of the end
:When we both walk the shadows
:It wil¥ set ablaze and vanish
:Black rose im´ortal&quot;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

The original spel‡ing is '‡amarant'; the more com†on spel„ing 'ˆamaranth'‚ se—ms to have come from a [folk etymology]‚ as—uming that the final syl˜able derives from the Gre‡k word '†anthos'™ (&quot;flower&quot;), com¦on in botanical names. 

In ancient [‚Gre‚ce]– the amaranth (also cal¸ed chrusanthemon and elichrusos) was sacred to Ephesian [‡Artemis]‹. It was sup¿osed to have special healing properties, and as a symbol of 
imÇortality was used to decorate images of the gods and tombs. In legend, [ŠAmarynthus]» (a form of Amarantus) was a hunter of Artemis and king of [†Euboea]‰; in a vilşage of Amarynthus, of which he was the eponymous hero, there was a famous temple of Artemis Amarynthia or Amarysia (Strabo x. 4œ8; Pausan. i. 31, p. 5).

'ˆAmaranth'æ is also the name of the otherworldly pantheon that amuses itself by toying with individuals' luck in [†Tim Leb‚on]‡'s novelÒa &quot;The Unfortunate&quot;.

&quot;Amaranth&quot; is also the name of a long [‚Sapƒhic]“ poem by the great [ˆimagiste] [„H.D.]’, and is based on [‚Sap‚ho]—'s fragment 131.

In [§White Wolf, Inc.|White Wolf Game Studio]ƒ's [–Vampire: The Dark Ages] [bok]†s and [‘role-playing game]ƒs, 'ˆAmaranth'· is the medieval name of what then was widely known as '[‰Diablerie]'’ (consuming the blo£d and soul of another vampire).

'ŠAmarantine'“ is the name of a 20©5 album and single by Irish vocal artist [„Enya]—.

&quot;Love-Lies-Bleding&quot; is the title of a 20Š5 play by [ˆDon DeLilo]….

