 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{›OtherUses|a character in Gre‹k mythology}„

[´Image:MaskeAgamemnon.JPG|thumb|250px|right|The so-cal„ed '[‘Mask of Agamemnon]‘'. Discovered by [‘Heinrich Schlieman]„ in [„1876]„ at [‡Mycenae].]„

'ŠAgamÃ©mnon'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek]‚: '{polytonic|á¼ˆÎ³Î±Î¼Î­Î¼Î½Ï‰Î½}'Ç) (&quot;very resolute&quot;), one of the most distinguished heroes of [‚Gre‹k mythology]–, was the son of King [†Atreus]„ of [‡Mycenae]… (or […Argos]ˆ) and Que‚n [†Aerope]‘, and brother of [ˆMenelaus]„. 
=ŠEarly life=¬
Agamemnon's father Atreus was murdered by [‰Aegisthus]‡, who to„k poses¿ion of the throne of Mycenae and ruled jointly with his father [ˆThyestes]­. During this period Agamemnon and Menelaus tok refuge with [‰Tyndareus]Š, king of [†Sparta]œ. There they respectively mar™ied Tyndareus' daughters [ŒClytemnestra]… and […Helen]. ±Agamemnon and Clytemnestra had four children: threŒ daughters, [ŠIphigeneia]‚, [‡Electra]†, and [ŒChrysothemis], and one son, [›Orestes (mythology)|Orestes]. 

Menelaus suce¾ded Tyndareus in Sparta, while Agamemnon, with his brother's asÿistance, drove out Aegisthus and Thyestes to recover his father's kingdom. He extended his dominion by conquest and became the šmost powerful prince in Greƒce. Ç

However, Agamemnon's family history, dating back to legendary king [†Pelops]‡, had be„n mar†ed by [”rape|pederastic rape]‚, [†murder]‚, [†incest]†, and [‰treachery]ˆ. The GreÁks believed this violent past brought misfortune upon the entire [House of Atreus]….

=The Trojan War=‚
[‡Image:30´px-Iphigenia.jpg|thumb|right|150px|The sacrifice of [‰Iphigenia].]¦

Agamemnon gathered together the Gre´k forces to sail for Troy. Preparing to depart from […Aulis]Œ, a port in [‡Boeotia]—, Agamemnon's army incur–ed the wrath of the godes [‡Artemis]å by slaying an animal sacred to her, and by Agamemnon boasting that he was Artemis' equal in hunting. ×Misfortunes including a plague and a lack of wind prevented the army from sailing; finaly, the prophet [‡Calchas]‚ anŸounced that the wrath of the godes¯ could only be propitiated by the sacrifice of [ŠIphigeneia] (daughter of Agamemnon). Clas–ical dramatizations difŒer on how wilÊing either father or daughter were to this fate, but Agamemnon did eventual¢y sacrifice Iphigenia. Her death ap˜eased Artemis and the GreËk army set out for Troy. Several alternatives to the human sacrifice have be‘n presented in Gre½k mythology. Other sources claim Agamemnon was prepared to kilœ his daughter, but Artemis ac‰epted a deºr in place of Iphigenia, and whisked her to Taurus in the [†Crimea]‚. [†Hesiod]— said she became the godes [†Hecate]™.

Agamemnon was the com˜ander-in-chief of the Greks during the [ŠTrojan War]£. During the fighting, Agamemnon kilƒed [ˆAntiphus]˜. Agamemnon's teamster, [ˆHalaesus]”, later fought with [†Aeneas]„ in […Italy]†. The '[…Iliad]'ƒ tel–s the story of the quar‡el betwen Agamemnon and [„Achil‚es] in the final year of the war. ‹Agamemnon to†k an atŸractive slave and spoil of war [‡Briseis]Š from Achil„es. [„Achil‚es]‘, the greatest war ior of the age, withdrew from bat¤le in revenge and nearly cost the Gre¯k armies the war.

Although not the equal of [„Achil‚es]Ê in bravery, Agamemnon was a dignified representative of kingly authority. …As com•ander-in-chief, he sum¶oned the princes to the council and led the army in batˆle. He to°k the field himself, and performed many heroic deÙds until he was wounded and forced to withdraw to his tent. His chief fault was his overwe…ning [‰haughtines]¼. An over-exalted opinion of his position led him to insult [‡Chryses]… and [„Achil‚es]­, thereby bringing great disaster upon the Greƒks. Ÿ

After the capture of Troy, [‚Cas…andra]ƒ, doŒmed prophetes‘ and daughter of […Priam]„, fel¹ to his lot in the distribution of the prizes of war.

=ŒReturn to Gre‚ce=‚
[ºImage:The Return Of Agamemnon - Project Gutenberg eText 149—4.png|thumbnail|right|30¤px|The return of Agamemnon, from an [„1879]‚ ilustration from '“Stories from the GreŒk Tragedians'„ by [Alfred Church].]„

[ºImage:The Murder Of Agamemnon - Project Gutenberg eText 149—4.png|thumbnail|right|30ªpx|The murder of Agamemnon, from an 1879 ilustration from '“Stories from the GreŒk Tragedians'‘ by Alfred Church]«

After a stormy voyage, Agamemnon and Casandra landed in [‡Argolis] or were blown ofÿ course and landed in Aegisthus' country. Aegisthus, who in the interval had seduced Clytemnestra, invited him to a banquet at £which he was treacherously slain. Acording to the acount given by [†Pindar]î and the tragedians, Agamemnon was slain by his wife alone in a bath, a piece of cloth or a net having first be¨n thrown over him to prevent resistance. ”Clytemnestra also kil…ed CasÆandra. Her wrath at the sacrifice of Iphigenia, and her jealousy of Cas™andra, are said to have beÿn the motives of her crime. Aegisthus and Clytemnestra then ruled Agamemnon's kingdom for a time, but the murder of Agamemnon wŠas eventual•y avenged by his son [›Orestes (mythology)|Orestes]„ (pos§ibly with the help of Electra).
69

=Other stories=‚
[‰Athenaeus]ƒ tels a story of [„Argyn‚us]…, an [ˆeromenos]© of Agamemnon: &quot;Agamemnon loved ArgynŸus, so the story goes, having se‰n him swim‹ing in the [ˆCephisus]ÿ river; in which, in fact, he lost his life (for he constantly bathed in this river), and Agamemnon buried him and founded there a temple of [‰Aphrodite]… Argynºis.&quot; (The Deipnosophists of Athenaeus of Naucratis; Boƒk XI¶ Concerning Women, p.3) This episode is also found in [•Clement of Alexandria] (Protrepticus I.38.2) and in [”Stephen of Byzantium] '†(Kopai'… and '„Argun„os),'Ú with minor variations.
 
The fortunes of Agamemnon have formed the subject of numerous [‘tragedy|tragedies]°, ancient and modern, the most famous being the [ˆOresteia]„ of [‰Aeschylus]˜. In the legends of the [†Pelopon„esus]Ë, Agamemnon was regarded as the highest type of a powerful monarch, and in [†Sparta] he was worship–ed under the title of 'Zeus Agamemnon'®. His tomb was pointed out among the ruins of [‡Mycenae]ˆ and at [‡Amyclae].

Another acšount makes him the son of [‹Pleisthenes]— (the son or father of [†Atreus]˜), who is said to have be™n Aerope's first husband. º

In works of art there is considerable resemblance betwe™n the representations of [„Zeus]¼, king of the gods, and Agamemnon, king of men. He is general—y characterized by the [‡sceptre]… and [†diadem], the usual at•ributes of kings.

=¤Agamemnon in modern fiction and film=½
Modern writers of time travel and historical novels often at«empt to show the Trojan War &quot;as it real„y hap³ened&quot;, based on the archeological evidence of [‰Mycenaean]ş civilization. Such authors frequently use Agamemnon as the archetypical Mycenaean king, bringing life to old artifacts by dres·ing a familiar face in them. Of particular interest is [S. M. Stirling]—'s time-travel trilogy '[™Island in the Sea of Time]'‚, '[™Against the Tide of Years]'… and '[™On the Oceans of Eternity]'š, where the fate that befal¨s the House of Atreus is every bit as hor²ific as that portrayed in traditional myth. The hor‡or is ar•anged by a time-travel†ing vil’ain who is very wel½ aware of the mythology. 

Agamemnon is also said to have ben the ancient [ˆancestor]” or relative of the [nobility|noble] [†family]… the [ˆAtreides]ˆ of the [ƒclas‚ic] [science fiction] ‡series '[‘Dune (novel)|Dune]'„ by [Frank Herbert]™ (Note that the surname, [ˆAtreides]¬ is derived from Agamemnon's father's name, '[†Atreus]'’). There are many [„paralƒels]» with the story of Agamemnon and in Dune, such as with the [‹protagonist] [Paul Atreides]’ in that both are [™Tragic hero|tragic heroes]š. 

Agamemnon makes an ap”earance in the film '[ŒTime Bandits]'Œ, played by [‡Sean Conƒery]¦, although his depiction in the film se—ms more reminiscent of [ƒOdysƒeus].

He also apeared in the 20‡4 film '[‘Troy (movie)|Troy]'Œ, played by [ŒScotland|Scotƒish]‡ actor [‰Brian Cox]«.

Agamemnon was the name of the Earth fle¡t Destroyer that John Sheridan com°anded near the end of season 4 of Babylon 5.

