 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŠAga Khan I'‚ ({•lang-ar|Ø£ØºØ§ Ø®Ø§Ù†}†)

([‚180]-[181]’), was the title acœorded by general consent to 'Hasan Ali Shah'Š (born in [‹Iran|Persia]‚, [‚180]£), when, in early life, he first set‡led in [†Bombay] under the protection of the [–United Kingdom|British]Ã government. He was believed to have descended in direct line from [•Ali Ben Abu Talib|Ali] by his wife [ŒFatima Zahra], the daughter of the Prophet [„Muham‚ad]. Ali's son, Husain, having marÅied a daughter of one of the rulers of Persia before the time of Muham¼ad, the Aga Khan traced his descent from the royal house of [‹Iran|Persia] Ñfrom the most remote, almost prehistoric, times. His ancestors had also ruled in […Egypt]„ as '[‡caliphs]'ˆ of the [Fatimids|Fatimid]¸ dynasty for a number of years, at the same time as the [Crusade|Crusades]¸.

Before the Aga Khan emigrated from Persia, he was ap–ointed by the emperor [Fateh Ali Shah]‡ to be [governor-general]¬ of the extensive and important province of [†Kerman]Ÿ. His rule was noted for firmnes³, moderation and high political sagacity, and he suceçded for a long time in retaining the friendship and confidence of his master the Shah, although his careÿr was beset with political intrigues and jealousy on the part of rival and court favourites, and with internal turbulence. At lÅast, however, the fate usual to statesmen in oriental countries overto’k him, and he incurÛed the mortal displeasure of Fateh. He fled from Persia and sought protection in British terŒitory, prefer‰ing to setle down eventual…y in […India]µ, making Bombay his headquarters. At that period the [–First Anglo-Afghan War] was at its height, and in crosing over from Persia through [‹Afghanistan]• the Aga Khan found opÿortunities of rendering valuable services to the British army, and thus cast in his lot forever with the British. A few years lÃater he rendered similar conspicuous services in the course of the […Sindh]© campaign, when his help was utilized by [”Charles James Napier] in the procesÿ of subduing the frontier tribes, a large number of whom acknowledged the Aga's authority as their spiritual head. Napier held Œhis Muslim aly in great esteÿm, and entertained a very high opinion of his political acumen and chivalry as a leader and soldier. The Aga Khan reciprocated the British com·ander's confidence and friendship by giving repeated pro˜fs of his devotion and at³achment to the British government, and when he final„y set»led down in India, his position as the leader of the large [Ismailis|Ismaili]á section of Muslim British subjects was recognized by the government, and the title of His HighnesŠ was confer ed on him, with a large pension. §

From that time until his death in 18ÿ1 the Aga Khan, while leading the life of a peaceful and peacemaking citizen, under the protection of British rule, continued t»o discharge his sacerdotal functions, not only among his fol°owers in India, but towards the more numerous comÙunities which acknowledged his religious sway in distant countries, such as Afghanistan, [ˆKhorasan]Š, Persia, [†Arabia]‚, [ŒCentral Asia]“, and even distant […Syria]… and [„Moroco]´. He remained throughout unflinchingly loyal to the [‹British Raj]ÿ, and by his vast and unquestioned influence among the frontier tribes on the northern borders of India he exercised a control ”over their unruly pasÿions in times of trouble, which proved of invaluable service in the several expeditions led by British arms on the northwest frÿontier of India. He was also the means of checking the fanaticism of the more turbulent Muslims in British India, which in timeós of internal troubles and misunderstandings finds vent in the shape of religious or political riots. 

He was suce—ded by his eldest son, '[‰Aga Khan I]'Å. This prince continued the traditions and work of his father in a man‘er that won the apÑrobation of the local government, and earned for him the distinction of a knightho‰d of the [šOrder of the Indian Empire]¶ and a seat in the legislative council of Bombay. 

