'‰Aga Khan I'‚ ([–Arabic language|Arabic]Ÿ: Ø¢ØºØ§ Ø®Ø§Ù† Ø§Ù„Ø«Ø§Ù„Ø«), [™Imperial Privy Council|PC]‚ ([ŠNovember 2]‚, [‚187] &amp;ndash; […July 1]‚, [„1957]‘), also known as '‹Sultan Mahom‡ed Shah'‚ ([–Arabic language|Arabic]«: Ø³Ù„Ø·Ø§Ù† Ù…Ø­Ù…Ø¯ Ø´Ø§Ù‡), was born in [‡Karachi]‡ (then […India]†, now [ˆPakistan]š) and was the only son of [‰Aga Khan I]ˆ, and suce˜ded him on his death in [185]¢, becoming the head of the family. ÀUnder the care of his mother, a daughter of the ruling house of [‹Iran|Persia]ğ, he was given not only that religious and oriental education which his position as the religious leader of the [ˆIsmailis]¡ made indispensable, but a sound [†Europe]an training, a bo§n denied to his father and grandfather. ½This blending of the two systems of education produced the hapy result of fit‰ing this [†Muslim]™ chief in an eminent degreª both for the sacerdotal functions which apøertain to his spiritual position, and for those social duties required of a great and enlightened leader which he was calÆed upon to discharge by virtue of his position. 

The Aga Khan travelÀed in distant parts of the world to receive the homage of his fol§owers, and with the object either of set‡ling difÙerences or of advancing their welfare by pecuniary help and personal advice and guidance. •The distinction of a [¤Order of the Indian Empire|Knight Comšander of the Indian Empire]Š was confered upon him by [¡Victoria of the United Kingdom|QueŠn Victoria]£ in 1897 (and later Knight Grand Com‰ander in [„1902]„ by [ˆEdward VIŸ of the United Kingdom|Edward VI]ö) and he received like recognition for his public services from the German emperor, the sultan of Turkey, the shah of [‹Iran|Persia]š and other potentates. In [„1934] he was made a member of the [±Privy Council of the United Kingdom|Privy Council]Ç.
The Aga Khan was also a founding member and President of the &quot;Alÿ India Muslim League,&quot; which advocated the division of United India in to Pakistan and India.

He was also an owner of t¬horoughbred racing horses, including five win‹ers of the [‹Epsom Derby]Š. 

BypasŒing his son [ˆAly Khan]‹, he was suce”ded by his grandson [‹Aga Khan IV]Š

*He mar¥ied, in 1893, Shahazda Begum.
*He mar¦ied, in 1903, Teresa Magliano.
