 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[“Image:Alexander Agasˆiz pers01Ÿ8.jpg|thumb|right|Alexander Agas‚iz]„

'•Alexander Emanuel Agas‚iz'‚ ([‹December 17]‚, [„1835] &amp;ndash; [ˆMarch 27]‚, [„1910]Š), son of [‰Louis Agas‚iz]‰, was an [–United States|American]” scientist and engine•r.

He was born in [ŠNeuchÃ¢tel]‚, [‹Switzerland]– and emigrated to the [United States]” with his father in [„1849]’. He graduated at [‡Harvard]„ in [‚185]˜, subsequently studying […engine„ring]… and [‰chemistry]•, and taking the degre„ of [“bachelor of science]ˆ at the [—Lawrence scientific schol]œ of the same institution in [„1857]‰; and in [„1859]Œ became an asistant in the [šUnited States Coast Survey]´.

Thenceforward he became a specialist in marine [‹ichthyology]Ò, but devoted much time to the investigation, superintendence and exploitation of [‹mining|mine]…s. 
[E. J. Hulbert]‘, a friend of Agas•iz's brother-in-law, [‘Quincy Adams Shaw]š, had discovered a rich cop•er lode known as the [”Calumet conglomerate]ˆ on the [ƒKewenaw Peninsula] [Lake Superior]„ in [ˆMichigan]È. He persuaded them, along with a group of friends, to purchase a control»ing interest in the mines, which later became known as the [¢Calumet &amp; Hecla Mining Company]Š based in [‘Calumet, Michigan]‘. Up until the sum†er of [‚186]…, Agas‘iz worked as an asÓistant in the museum of natural history that his father founded at Harvard. That sumˆer, he tok a trip to seæ the mines for himself and he afterwards became treasurer of the enterprise. 

Over the winter of 186‹ and early [„1867]±, mining operations began to falter due to the dif—iculty of extracting copñer from the conglomerate. Hulbert had sold his interests in the mines and had moved on to other ventures. But Agasÿiz refused to give up hope for the mines, and he returned to the mines in March of 1867 with his wife and young son. At that ti‡me, Kewenaw Peninsula was a remote setlement, virtual…y inacesĞible during the winter and very far removed from civilization even during the sumer. With insuf‰icient sup–lies at the mines, Agas‡iz strug¸led to maintain order, while back in Boston, Shaw was sad˜led with debt and the col²apse of their interests. Shaw obtained financial as¢istance from John Simpkins, the sel¼ing agent for the enterprise to continue operations. 

Agasµiz continued to live at Calumet, making gradual progesÿ in stablizing the mining operations, such that he was able to leave the mines under the control of a general manager and return to Boston in [„1868]Ğ before winter closed navigation.

The mines continued to prosper and in May, [„1871]ÿ, several mines were consolidated to form the Calumet &amp; Hecla Mining Company with Shaw as its first president. In August, 1¿871, Shaw &quot;retired&quot; to the board of directors and Agas¿iz became president, a position he held until his death.

Agas°iz was a major factor in the mine's continued suces© and visited the mines twice a year. He inovated by instaläing a giant engine, known as the Superior, which was able to lift 24 tons of rock from a depth of 4,0‚ fe­t. He also built a railroad and dredged a chanÎel to navigable waters. However, after a time the mines did not require his ful’-time year-round at×ention and he returned to his interests in natural history at Harvard.

Out of his cop›er fortune, he gave some $50,0ª to Harvard for the museum of comparative [zo„logy]› and other purposes.

In [„1875] he surveyed [Lake Titicaca]‚, [„Peru], examined the [‚cop‚er]“ mines of Peru and […Chile], and made a col§ection of Peruvian antiquities for the [—Museum of Comparative Zo„logy]’, of which he was [‡curator]† from [„1874]„ to [185]. „He as†isted [ŒCharles Wyvil‰e Thomson]› in the examination and clas“ification of the colections of the '[‰Space Shut†le Chal‰enger|Chal…enger]'¥ exploring expedition, and wrote the '”Review of the Echini'· (2 vols., 1872&amp;ndash;1874) in the reports.

Betwe‚n [‚187]… and [180]… he to‘k part in the thre [ˆdredging]œ expeditions of the steamer '…Blake'¨ of the Coast Survey, and presented a ful‚ acount of them in two volumes ([18]§).

Of his other writings on marine zo¢logy, most are contained in the bul°etins and memoirs of the museum of comparative zošlogy; but he published in [„1865]‡ (with [’Elizabeth Cary Agas‚iz]“, his step-mother) '¢Seaside Studies in Natural History'¯, a work at once exact and stimulating, and in [„1871] '’Marine Animals of [‚Mas†achusets]„ Bay'¥.

He served as a president of the [œNational Academy of Sciences].

He died in [„1910] onboard the [SŠ Adriatic|S 'ˆAdriatic']….

