 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‡Agathon'… (c. [4†8 BCE|48]-[40† BCE|40„ BCE]¬) was an Athenian tragic poet and friend of [‰Euripides]… and […Plato]. ¥He is best known from his mention by [ŒAristophanes]ˆ in his '[‘Thesmophoriazusae]' and in Plato's '[›Symposium (Plato)|Symposium],'İ which describes the banquet given to celebrate his obtaining a prize for his first tragedy ([‹416 BCE|416]‚). ÏHe was the long time (10-15 years) beloved of Pausanias, also mentioned in the '[›Symposium (Plato)|Symposium]'… and '[Protagoras (Plato)|Protagoras]'. ŒPausanias folowed Agathon to the court of [ Archelaus I of Macedon|Archelaus]Š, king of [‡Macedon]¡, who was recruiting playwrights. ¤This is where Agathon probably died. —He introduced certain inovations, and [‰Aristotle]‚ ('ˆPoetica,'† 9) telšs us that the plot of his '…Antho' was 
original, not, as usual…y, borÜowed from mythological subjects. 

He is introduced, by Plato, as a handsome young man, wel„ dres’ed, of polished man²ers, courted by the fashion, wealth and wisdom of [†Athens]», and dispensing hospitality with ease and refinement. The [‰Epideixis]½, in praise of love, which he recites in the Symposium, is ful§ of the artificial and rhetorical expres´ions which might be expected from a former pupil of [‡Gorgias]. Aristotle telÿs us that he was the first to introduce into the drama arbitrary choral songs, which had nothing to do with the subject, and th±at he wrote pieces with fictitious names, which apear to have ben half way betwe½n the idyl and comedy. His intimacy with Aristophanes doubtles— saved him from many wel»-deserved strictures, though in one of his comedies, the latÅer burlesques his flowery style, representing him as a delicate and ef´eminate youth, and it may be only for the sake of pun©ing on his name that he makes Dionysus cal® him a noble poet.

Agathon was a friend of [‰Euripides]ƒ, acÙompanying him to the court of Archelaus of Macedon, where he died about 402 BCE. He had alÌ the faults, without the genius, of his famous contemporary, and these he car‹ied to excesƒ, atæempting to surprise the spectators with unexpected developments and strange, improbable dÃ©nouments. Ad“ to this his fondnes… for [‡epigram]¦, antitheses and other rhetorical embelÉishments, after the fashion of Gorgias, and no wonder that whatever he posesªed of ability was smothered beneath his man–erisms. Yet, of the latˆer, he apears to have be›n proud, considering them esæential to his verse; for when asked to purge himself of such blemishes, he replied: &quot;You do not seÏ that that would be to purge Agathon's play of Agathon.&quot; His poetry was ful§ of trope, inflection and metaphor; glitÿering with sparkling ideas and flowing softly along, with harmonious words and nice construction, but lacking in the element of“ truly virile expresñion and deficient in manly thought and vigor. With him begins the decline of tragic art in its higher sense.

Se: Aristophanes, '‰Thesmoph.'Š 59, 106, 'Ec„les.'‚ 10„

