 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŠAgesilaus I'…, or 'ŠAgesilaos I'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek] '“á¼ˆÎ³Î·ÏƒÎ¹Î»Î¬Î¿Ï‚'‚), ˆking of [†Sparta]‰, of the [ŠEurypontid]˜ family, was the son of [‹Archidamus I]ª and Eupolia, and younger step-brother of […Agis I]Œ, whom he suceŠded about [†401 BC]. Agis had, inde‰d, a son [‹Leotychides]›, but he was set aside as ilegitimate, curªent rumour representing him as the son of [ŠAlcibiades]. Agesilaus' suces” was largely due to [ˆLysander]Ÿ, who hoped to find in him a wil…ing toØl for the furtherance of his political designs; in this hope, however, Lysander was disap¯ointed, and the increasing power of Agesilaus so“n led to his downfalˆ.

In [†396 BC]­ Agesilaus was sent to Asia with a force of 20§ Neodamodes (enfranchized Helots) and 60‚ al’ies to secure the [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek]’ cities against a [–Persian Empire|Persian]‚ at„ack. ›On the eve of sailing from […Aulis]… he at‹empted to of“er a sacrifice, as [‰Agamemnon]• had done before the [‹Troy|Trojan]• expedition, but the [ŠThebes, GreŠce|Thebans]Î intervened to prevent it, an insult for which he never forgave them. On his arˆival at [‡Ephesus]† a thre¢ months' truce was concluded with [‚Tisˆaphernes]†, the [†satrap]„ of […Lydia]… and […Caria]¼, but negotiations conducted during that time proved fruitlesª, and on its termination Agesilaus raided [‡Phrygia]—, where he easily won im†ense bo‰ty since [‚Tisˆaphernes]˜ had concentrated his troŒps in Caria. ªAfter spending the winter in organizing a [‡cavalry]” force, he made a suces“ful incursion into […Lydia]’ in the spring of [†395 BC]‚. [‹Tithraustes]¡ was thereupon sent to replace Tis»aphernes, who paid with his life for his continued failure. ŸAn armistice was concluded betweÍn Tithraustes and Agesilaus, who left the southern satrapy and again invaded [‡Phrygia]Ÿ, which he ravaged until the folowing spring. –He then came to an agre•ment with the satrap [‹Pharnabazus]¿ and once more turned southward.

It was said that he was plan¬ing a campaign in the interior, or even an at‡ack on [‹Artaxerxes I– of Persia|Artaxerxes I]š himself, when he was recal†ed to [‚Gre‚ce]– owing to the war betwe¤n Sparta and the combined forces of [†Athens]‚, [ŠThebes, Gre‰ce|Thebes]‚, [‹Corinth, GreŠce|Corinth]‚, […Argos]š and several minor states. –A rapid march through [†Thrace]… and [‡Macedon]’ia brought him to [ƒThesƒaly]›, where he repulsed the Thesµalian cavalry who tried to impede him. Reinforced by [Phocis|Phocian]… and [–Orchomenus|Orchomenian]ƒ tro¸ps and a Spartan army, he met the confederate forces at [‰Chaeronea]„ in [‡Boeotia], and in a hotly contested bat‘le was 
technical˜y victorious, but the suces‰ was a bar¦en one and he had to retire by way of [†Delphi]ˆ to the [†Peloponƒese]˜. Shortly before this batle the Spartan [„navy]©, of which he had received the supreme comand, was totalŒy defeated of [Knidos|Cnidus]™ by a powerful Persian fleˆt under […Conon]… and [‹Pharnabazus]. 

Subsequently Agesilaus tošk a prominent part in the [Corinthian War]“, making several suces¢ful expeditions into Corinthian ter”itory and capturing [ˆLechaeum]… and [‡Piraeus]. †The los£, however, of a mora, destroyed by [ŠIphicrates]–, neutralized these suces¥es, and Agesilaus returned to Sparta. ƒIn [†389 BC]œ he conducted a campaign in [‰Acarnania]œ, but 
two years later the [“Peace of Antalcidas]‹, warmly sup®orted by Agesilaus, put an end to hostilities. ½When war broke out afresh with Thebes the king twice invaded [‡Boeotia]„ 
([†378 BC]‚, [37ƒ BC]¡), and it was on his advice that [‹Cleombrotus]¨ was ordered to march against Thebes in [†371 BC]Ë. Cleombrotus was defeated at Leuctra and the Spartan supremacy overthrown. ‡

In [†370 BC]¿ Agesilaus tried to restore Spartan prestige by an invasion of [ˆMantinea]„n terÊitory, and his prudence and heroism saved Sparta when her enemies, led by [‹Epaminondas], penetrated [‡Laconia] that same year, and again in [†362 BC]Œ when they al‡ but suce½ded in seizing the city by a rapid and unexpected march. The [‚bat—le of Mantinea (362 BC)]–, in which Agesilaus to‘k no part, was fol¬owed by a general peace: Sparta, however, sto„d alo¬f, hoping even yet to recover her supremacy. ¿In order to gain money for prosecuting the war Agesilaus had sup“orted the revolted [†satrap]Šs, and in [†361 BC]Œ he went to […Egypt]’ at the head of a [‰mercenary] force to aid [†Tachos] against Persia. „He so‰n transfer­ed his services to Tachos's cousin and rival [ŠNectanebo I]·, who, in return for his help, gave him a sum of over 20‰ talents. èOn his way home Agesilaus died at the age of 83, after a reign of some 41 years.

Agesilaus was of smal” stature and unimpres…ive apŞearance, and was somewhat lame from birth. These facts were used as an argument against his sucesˆion, an [†oracle]Ã having warned Sparta against a &quot;lame reign.&quot; He was a sucesful leader in [ƒgueril‰a warfare]Ÿ, alert and quick, yet cautious-¹a man, moreover, whose personal bravery was unquestioned. ÀAs a statesman he won himself both enthusiastic adherents and bit¸er enemies, but of his patriotism there can be no doubt. şHe lived in the most frugal style alike at home and in the field, and though his campaigns were undertaken largely to secure boÉty, he was content to enrich the state and his friends and to return as poör as he had set forth. The worst trait in his character is his implacable hatred of Thebes, which led directly to the [‚batle of Leuctra] and Sparta's fal¥ from her position of supremacy.

AcŠording to [ˆPlutarch]Ç, he was once asked whether he wanted a memorial erected in his honour. ¼He replied, â€œIf I have done any noble action, that is a suf°icient memorial; if I have done nothing noble, alœ the statues in the world wil› not preserve my memory.â€ „(In [ŠAncient Gre„k|Grek]ÿ: Î•á¼° Î³á½°Ï Ï„Î¹ ÎºÎ±Î»á½¸Î½ á¼”ÏÎ³Î¿Î½ Ï€ÎµÏ€Î¿Î¯Î·ÎºÎ±, Ï„Î¿á¿¦Ï„Î¿ Î¼Î½Î·Î¼Îµá¿–Î¿Î½ á¼ÏƒÏ„Î¯Î½; Îµá¼° Î´á½² Î¼Î·Î´á½²ÈÎ½, Î¿á½Î´' Î¿á¼± Ï€Î¬Î½Ï„ÎµÏ‚ á¼€Î½Î´ÏÎ¹Î¬Î½Ï„ÎµÏ‚.)

His daughter [‡Cynisca]¸ became the only woman in ancient history to win at the [£ancient Olympic Games|Olympic Games]Ê.

Quote
- Do not go after any man with nothing left to lose, for he wil² come back at you with the strength of 10 men.

