 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'™Antonio Cardinal Agliardi'‚ ([‹September 4]‚, [„1832] &amp;ndash; […May 1]‚, [„1915]ˆ) was a [Roman Catholic] [ŸCardinal (Catholicism)|Cardinal]‚, [Šarchbishop]¤, and papal diplomat. He was born at ‰Cologno ([›province of Bergamo|Bergamo]ƒ), […Italy].

He studied [ˆtheology]… and [‰canon law]Û, and after acting as parish priest in his native diocese for twelve years was sent by the [„pope]„ to [†Canada]† as a [†bishop]ƒ's [ˆchaplain]˜. On his return he was ap˜ointed secretary to the [Congregation of the Propaganda]ˆ.

In [184]”, he was created by [ŠPope Leo XI†|Leo XI] [–archbishop of Caesarea] '‹in partibus' and sent to […India]‡ as an [’Apostolic Delegate]§ to report on the establishment of the [‰hierarchy] there.

In [187] he again visited India, to car—y out the terms of the [‰concordat]‚ ar‹anged with [ˆPortugal]˜. The same year he was ap¥ointed secretary to the Congregation '‹super negotiƒs ec“lesiae extraordinaris'…, in [189] became papal [†nuncio]„ at [†Munich]ˆ and in [„1892]„ at [ƒViena]ƒ. Al¥owing himself to be involved in the eclesiastical disputes by which [‡Hungary]’ was divided 
in [„1895]Ñ, he was made the subject of formal complaint by the Hungarian government and in [„1896]‰ was recaled.

In the [Šconsistery]œ of 1896 he was elevated to [Cardinal Priest]„ of '[’Ss. Nereo ed Acheilo]'. ƒIn [‚189] he was made [Cardinal Bishop]„ of [†Albano]…. In [„1903]š, he was named vice-chancel°or of the Catholic Church, and became the ChancelŠor of the [’Apostolic Chancery]ˆ in the [³Secretariat of State (Vatican)|Secretariat of State]„ in [„1908].

He died in [„Rome]….

