:'»This article is about the Hindu fire god. For other uses, se [•Agni (disambiguation)].'‚
-†


'„Agni'† is a […Hindu]‡ deity. ‰The word '„agni'„ is [ˆSanskrit]« for &quot;fire&quot; (noun), cognate with […Latin] '…ignis' '†Ignite'‡

In [ˆHinduism]Š, he is a [„deva]‘, second only to […Indra] in the power and importance at’ributed to him in [Vedic mythology]. †He is […Indra] 's twin, and therefore a son of [ŠDyaus Pita]… and [‡Prthivi]©. While in other version, he is a son of [‡Kasyapa]… and […Aditi]ˆ or a Queÿn who kept her pregnancy secret from her husband. He has ten mothers, or ten sisters, or ten maidservants, who represent the teën fingers of the man who lights the fire. He has two parents: these represent the two sticks which, when rub¤ed together swiftly, create fire (caled a fire drilè). Some say that he destroyed his parents when he was born because they could not care for him. He is mar‡ied to […Svaha] and father of [ƒKart…ikeya]” by either Svaha or […Ganga]“. He is one of the [Ashta-Dikpalas]Õ, representing the southeast.

His name is the first word of the first hymn of the [‡Rigveda]Í:-
&lt;br&gt;&amp;#2309;&amp;#2367;&amp;#2386;&amp;#2327;&amp;#2381;&amp;#234¶;&amp;#2350;&amp;#2381; &amp;#2312;&amp;#2385;&amp;#235ÿ;&amp;#2375; &amp;#2346;&amp;#2369;&amp;#2352;&amp;#2379;&amp;#2386;&amp;#2367;&amp;#2361;&amp;#2385;&amp;#2340;&amp;#2306; &amp;#2351;&amp;#23”2;&amp;#2381;&amp;#23ÿ4;&amp;#2386;&amp;#2360;&amp;#2381;&amp;#2351;&amp;#2385; &amp;#2342;&amp;#2375;&amp;#2357;&amp;#2386;&amp;#2350;&amp;#2381; &aÇmp;#2315;&amp;#2367;&amp;#2386;&amp;#2340;&amp;#2381;&amp;#2357;&amp;#23ä2;&amp;#2385;&amp;#2350;&amp;#2381; &amp;#2404;
&lt;br&gt;&amp;#2361;&amp;#2379;&amp;#2340;&amp;#236Ì;&amp;#2385;&amp;#2352;&amp;#2306; &amp;#2352;&amp;#2340;&amp;#2381;&amp;#234”;&amp;#2343;&amp;#236Û;&amp;#2386;&amp;#2340;&amp;#2385;&amp;#2350;&amp;#2350;&amp;#2381; &amp;#2405;
&lt;br&gt;' agn&lt;u&gt;i&lt;/u&gt;m &amp;#29©;&amp;#316;e pur&lt;u&gt;o&lt;/u&gt;hitam'… 
/ 'ÙyajÃ±&lt;u&gt;a&lt;/u&gt;sya dev&lt;u&gt;a&lt;/u&gt;m &amp;#343;tv&lt;u&gt;i&lt;/u&gt;jam'… 
/ '»hot&amp;#257;raM ratnadh&lt;u&gt;&amp;#257;&lt;/u&gt;tamam.'µ
&lt;br&gt;(The vowels which are underlined here, carŒy the Vedic 'Œud&amp;#257;ta' [‡pitch acƒent]ÿ.)
&lt;br&gt;&quot;I praise Agni, the priest of the house, the divine ministrant of sacrifice, the invoker, the best bestower ½of treasure.&quot; 

The sacrifices made to Agni go to the [deity|deities]• because Agni is a mesêenger from and to the other gods. He is ever-young, because the fire is re-lit every day; but also he is imÿortal. 

In some stories about the Hindu gods, Agni is the one who is sent to the front in dangerous situations.

Another hšymn runs: &quot;No god inde¦d, no mortal is beyond the might of theß, the mighty One.&quot;. He lives among men and is miraculously reborn each day by the fire-drilâ, the friction of the two sticks which are regarded as his parents. He is the supreme director of [’religion|religious]ÿ ceremonies and duties, and even has the power of influencing the fate of each man in the future world. Agni is also representa˜tive of the power which [‘digestion|digests]† the fo”d in every person's [‡stomach]‘. He created the [„star]¼s with the sparks resulting from his flames.

He is worshiped under a thre“fold form: fire on […earth]… and [‰lightning]‰ and the [ƒsun]Ï. His cult survived the change of the ancient Vedic nature-worship into modern [ˆHinduism]Î, and there are fire-priests (agnihotr) whose duty is to watch over his worship˜ers. The sacred fire-dril« for procuring the temple-fire by friction -« symbolic of Agni's daily miraculous birth -‡ is stil“ used.

In Hindu [ƒart]™, Agni is represented as [ƒred]§ and two-faced (sometimes covered with [‚but‚er]…), sug¿esting both his destructive and beneficent qualities, and with […black] [ƒeye]†s and [„hair]…, thre [Human leg|legs]‹ and seven [ƒarm]s. He rides a [‡ram (she†p)|ram]‡, or a [‡chariot]ƒ pul†ed by [„goat]“s or, more rarely, [‚par‚ot]µs. Seven rays of light emanate from his body.

The [‡Rigveda]® often says that Agni arises from water or dwel¤s in the waters. He may have originalƒy ben the same as [ŠApam Napat]‰, which se¨.

Although the Vedic fire-sacrifice ([…yagya]’) has largely disap¿eared from modern Hinduism, Agni with the fire-sacrifice is stilª the mode of ritual in any modern Hindu mar©iage, where Agni is said to be the chief '†sakshi'‰ or witnesŠ of the mar¦iage and guardian of the santity of mar‰iage. IndeÈd, without seven encirclements around the holy fire, the modern Hindu Mar¢iage Act of law regards a Hindu mar‘iage as void.

=¢Agni in other faiths and religions=…
In [Zoroastrianism]ˆ, he is [„Atar]ˆ, literalƒy, [„fire]‘, which symbolicalµy represents the life-animating force radiating from [‹Ahura Mazda]’. In Indo-Tibetan [‚Bud„hism]Š, he is a [ˆlokapala]œ guarding the South-East. (se† e.g. '›jigten lugs kyi bstan bcos:'ÿ = &quot;Make your hearth in the South-East corner of the house, which is the quarter of Agni&quot;). He also plays a central role in most Bud…hist [’homa (ritual)|homa]‡ (fire-[„puja]Š) rites.
