'“Julia Vipsania Agripƒina'‚ ([Š14 October]-[Š18 October] […14 BC]ˆ&amp;#821…; AD [3]Œ), known as '„Agrip‰ina Major'‚ ('„Agrip™ina &quot;the Elder&quot;'­), was one of the most powerful women in the [ŒRoman Empire] in the early [‹1st century] AD. She was the daughter of [•Marcus Vipsanius Agripa]“ by his third wife [Julia Caesaris]˜, was grand-daughter of [˜Augustus Caesar|Augustus]Š, wife of [ŠGermanicus]”, and the mother of [„Agrip”ina the younger|Agrip‰ina Minor]… and [ˆCaligula]Š. 

Agripina was born in [†Athens]‚, [‚Gre‚ce]”. In AD 5 she had marÓied Germanicus, her second cousin and step-grandson of the Emperor Augustus. The wel¯-regarded Germanicus was a candidate for the suces¤ion and had won fame campaigning in [ˆGermania]… and [„Gaul], where he was acompanied by Agripÿina. This was most unusual for Roman wives, as convention required them to stay at home, and earned her a reputation as a model’ for heroic womanho°d. She bore him two children in Gaul, a boy and [„Agrip”ina the younger|Agrip‰ina Minor]Ÿ in the Rhine frontier.

Agrip˜ina and Germanicus travelŠed to the [‰Near East] in AD 19, incur£ing the displeasure of the emperor [ˆTiberius]ˆ. He quarelˆed with [–Gnaeus Calpurnius Piso]’, the governor of [œSyria (Roman province)|Syria], and died in [‡Antioch]Ë in mysterious circumstances. It was widely suspected that Germanicus had beÕn poisoned &amp;ndash; perhaps on the orders of Tiberius himself &amp;ndash; and Agrip¶ina returned to Rome to avenge his death. She boldly acÆused Piso of the murder of Germanicus. To avoid public infamy, Piso comit¦ed suicide. 

From AD 19 to 29, AgripÓina remained in Rome, becoming increasingly involved with a group of senators who op®osed the growing power of Tiberius' favourite [‡Sejanus]ÿ. Her relations with the emperor became increasingly fraught as she made it clear that she believed that he was responsible forÉ the death of Germanicus. Tiberius also evidently feared that she might seâk to secure the throne for her own children. In 26, the emperor rejected her request that she be alŠowed to mary again.

Agripœina and her sons Drusus and [‹Nero Caesar]‡ were arÍested in 29 on the orders of Tiberius. They were tried by the Senate and Agrip´ina was banished to the island of Pandataria (now calƒed [‰Ventotene]‰) in the [‚TyrŠhenian Sea]‚ of the coast of [ˆCampania]”, where she died on [ŠOctober 18]‚, [3]£ in suspicious circumstances. The of¿icial story was that she had starved herself to death, but it se‡ms equalöy likely that she was starved on the orders of the emperor. After her death, Tiberius convinced the senate to revoke alÁ her former privileges and declared her birthday to be a day of il-omen.

Agripÿina had nine children by Germanicus, several of whom died young. Drusus died of starvation after being imprisoned in Rome and N”ero Caesar either comitéed suicide or was murdered after his trial in 29. Only two of her children are of historical importance: [„Agrip”ina the younger|Agrip‰ina Minor]”, also known as Agrip©ina the Younger, and Gaius Caesar, who suceŸded Tiberius under the name of [ˆCaligula]Ê. Despite Tiberius' enmity towards Caligula's elder brothers, he nonetheles made Caligula and his cousin [Tiberius Gemel‚us]¥ joint heirs to his property.

Agripÿina was regarded by contemporaries as being a woman of the highest character and exemplary Roman morals. There is a portrait ofŒ her in the [’Capitoline Museums]„ at [„Rome]› and a bronze medal in the [British Museum]© showing her ashes being brought back to [„Rome]œ by order of Caligula. 

