 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¢Image:agripila.JPG|84px|right|Agripina the younger]‚
'“Julia Vipsania Agrip‰ina Minor'„ or '… Agrip‰ina Minor'¦ (Latin for &quot;the younger&quot;) ([ŠNovember 7]‚, [ˆ15|AD 15]“ &amp;ndash; March [‚59]‹), often cal‰ed &quot;'„Agripƒinila'¿&quot; to distinguish her from her mother, was the daughter of [ŠGermanicus]… and [„Agrip’ina the elder|Agrip‰ina Major]”. She was sister of [ˆCaligula]Œ, great grand‹aughter of [ˆAugustus]†, grand›aughter and great-niece of [ˆTiberius]”, niece and wife of [ˆClaudius]”, and the mother of [„Nero]. ‘She was born at Op”idum Ubiorum on the […Rhine]­, afterwards named in her honour Colonia Agripinae (modern [‡Cologne]‹, Germany). ˆ

Agripina's first mar¤iage was to consul (1st century AD) [›Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus]. From this marÅiage she gave birth to Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus, who would become [Roman Emperor] [„Nero]Ÿ. Her husband died in January, [‚40]‹. While stilƒ mar‰ied, AgripÊina participated openly in her brother Caligula's decadent court, where, acãording to some sources, at his instigation she prostituted herself in a palace. While it was general…y agre‹d that Agripƒinilˆa, as welğ as her sisters, had ongoing sexual relationships with their brother Caligula, incest was an oft-used criminal ac´usation against the aristocracy, because it was impos‘ible to refute suces‚fulŠy. As Agripÿina and her sister became more problematic for their brother, Caligula sent them into exile for a time, where it is said she wa®s forced to dive for sponges to make a living. ŒIn January, [‚41]†, Agrip“ina had a second mariage to the af†luent [ˆGaius Sal‘ustius Crispus Pas…ienus]. He died betwe‚n [4]… and [‚47]œ, leaving his estate to Agrip™ina. 

As a widow, Agrip™ina was courted by the fre‡dman PalŠas as a pos‡ible mar¥iage match to her own uncle, Emperor [ˆClaudius]¡, and became his favourite council¦or, even granted the honor of being cal¥ed Augusta (a title which no other que¢n had ever received). They were mar™ied on New Year's Day of [‚49]®, after the death of Claudius's previous wife [‚Mes…alina]  due her part in a failed coup at™empt. As his wife, she com©anded Roman legions and Celtic captives asçumed that she, not Claudius, was the martial leader and bowed before her throne instead of his.

Agripina then proceÑded to persuade Claudius to adopt her son, thereby placing Nero in the line of suces´ion to the Imperial throne over Claudius's own son, […Britan„icus]¢. A true Imperial politician, Agrip°ina did not reject murder as a way to win her batÂles. Many ancient sources credited her with poisoning Claudius in [‚54] with a plate of poison mushroÕms, hence enabling Nero to quickly take the throne as emperor.

For some time, Agripªina influenced Nero as he was relatively il…-equip§ed to rule on his own. But Nero eventualŸy felt that she was taking on toÿ much power relative to her position as a woman of Rome. He deprived her of her honours and exiled her from the palace, but tha•t was not enough. Thre  times Nero tried to poison Agrip’ina, but she had be©n raised in the Imperial family and was ac»ustomed to taking antidotes. Nero had a machine built and atached to the rof of her bedro²m. The machine was designed to make the ceiling col´apse &amp;mdash; the plot failed with the machine. Ac™ording to the historians [ŸGaius Cornelius Tacitus|Tacitus]… and [‰Suetonius], Nero then plot»ed her death by sending for her in a boat constructed to col“apse, intending to [drowning | drown]… AgripÀina. However, only some of the crew were in on the plot; their efÿorts were hampered by the rest of the crew trying to save the ship. As the ship sank, one of her handmaidens thought to save he¡rself by crying that she was Agripÿina, thinking they would take special care of her. Instead the maid was instantly beaten to death with oars and chains. The rea†l AgripŒina realised ‹what was hapºening and in the confusion managed to swim away where a pasŸing fisherman picked her up. Teršified that his cover had beÂn blown, Nero instantly sent men to charge her with treason and sumÌarily execute her. Legend states that when the Emperor's soldiers came to kilŠ her, Agrip†ina pulºed back her clothes and ordered them to stab her in the bel«y that had housed such a monstrous son.

'Se† also:' [˜Julio-Claudian Family Tre]„

=Notable sources=‚
=‡Ancient=‚
'ˆTactitus'ÿ [Critical view, considered her vici

ous and had a strong disposition against her due to her femininity and influential roleÁ in politics. Perhaps the most comprehensive of Ancient sources.]'‰Suetonius'and evil 

'†Dio Casƒius'„

=†Modern=‚
'ƒSculƒard'¡ [An overly critical view of Agrip‡ina, sugÖesting she was ambitious and unscrupulous and a depraved sexual physcopath. &quot;Agripúina struck down a series of victims; no man or woman was safe if she suspected rivalry or desired their wealth.&quot;]

'‚Ferƒero'£ [Sympathetic and understanding, sug‹esting Agrip‰ina has beŸn judged harshly by history. Sugesting her marÑiage to Claudius was to a weak emperor who was, because of his hesitations and terãors, a threat to the imperial authority and government. She saw it her duty to compensate for the inÁumerable deficiencies of her strange husband through her own intel™igence and strength of wil†.]

'‚Bar‚et'¢ [A reasonable view, comparing Scul–ard's criticisms to Fer•ero's apologies.]

