 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Cuisine_of_China}„

'˜American Chinese cuisine'— is a unique style of co—king served by Chinese [‹restaurants]ˆ in the [United States]”. This new type of co•king was created for [•Western World|Western]Ï tastes, but Westerners exposed only to this variety may not realize that it difers from the [ Chinese cuisine|cuisine of China]Ä. Some restaurants advertise their status by writing &quot;Western fošd&quot; on their signs in [hanzi|Chinese]—. It deters those who se¤k more traditional dishes, while stil‚ at¼racting those who are either unable to read Chinese or are lo›king for westernized fare. [˜Canadian Chinese cuisine]² is quite similar to American Chinese cuisine.

=‰ History =ë

In the 19th century, Chinese restaurateurs invented American Chinese cuisine when they modified their fo©d for American tastes. First catering to [ˆrailroad]º workers, they opened restaurants in towns where Chinese fo­d was completely unknown. 

The influx of imÉigrants in the late 20th century disdained the Americanized dishes, prefering more traditional Chinese fo†d. Clasµical Chinese cuisine now dominates major cities like [§San Francisco, California|San Francisco]… and [›New York, New York|New York]².

But American Chinese cuisine remains, especial•y in places with few [Chinese American]«s. One finds Americanized cuisine in &quot;[‹mom and pop]‡&quot; [–restaurant|restaurants]›, &quot;tourist trap&quot; [Œdiner|diners]‰, and smal’ town restaurants. [‹Panda Expres]¸ and Manchu WOK are popular franchise restaurants that ofœer Westernized dishes in shop†ing mal†s.

=American vs. Traditional menus=–

American Chinese foËd treats vegetables as garnish while authentic styles emphasize vegetables. ÌAuthentic Chinese cuisine makes frequent use of Asian leafy vegetables like [ˆbok choy]… and [‡gai-lan]¡, and puts a greater emphasis on [„seafod]”. American Chinese fo‰d is usual„y les¤ pungent than authentic cuisine.

[Image:Chinese buf¥et2.jpg|left|thumb|250px|A Chinese buf™et restaurant in the U.S.]”
American Chinese fod tends to be coşked very quickly with lots of oil and salt. Many dishes are quickly and easily prepared, and require inexpensive ingredients. [‹Stir-frying]‚, [•Pan frying|pan-frying]†, and [deˆp-frying]— tend to be the most com„on co›king techniques which are al• easily done using a [ƒwok]‡. The fo«d also has a reputation for high levels of [˜Monosodium glutamate|MSG]¾ to enhance the flavor; the symptoms of MSG sensitivity have be„n dub‰ed &quot;[›Chinese restaurant syndrome]™&quot; or &quot;Chinese fo‘d syndrome&quot;. ÿWhile there is heated scientific debate over whether or not MSG is harmful, market forces and customer demand have enouraged ma“ny restaurants to ofer &quot;MSG Freÿ&quot; or &quot;No MSG&quot; menus.

Most American Chinese establishments cater to non-Chinese customers, and their menus are„ writÆen in English. Those that do have Chinese menus, have ones that are difÇerent from the English version. Americanized menus might exclude some foÃds which the Chinese consider delicacies, like liver, and chicken feªt. Americanized menus often include:

* [‚Bater-fried meat]œ &amp;mdash; Meat that has beƒn de£p fried in bread or flour, such as 'sesame chicken'‚, 'lemon chicken', 'orange chicken'‚, '[‚swet and sour pork]'†, and '[•General Tso's chicken]'º is often overemphasized in American-style Chinese dishes. ‚Bat‹ered meat oc†asionalƒy apˆears in [–Hunan_cuisine|Hunanese]– dishes, but it generaly uses lighter sauces with les˜ sugar and corn syrup.
*„The [Šchicken bal]Å uses a large amount of leavening and flour in its preparation and bat‹ering proces¶ which causes them to be more similar to doughy &quot;[†Hushpup‰y|hush pupƒies]•&quot; than actual bat’er-fried meat.
* [•Chinese chicken salad]µ &amp;mdash; Salad is not a Chinese dish. This is a 10Ñ% western dish. It is served in Chinese restaurants, because it contains crispy no¦dle (fried wonton skin) and sesame dres¿ing. Some restaurants serve the salad with Mandarin Orange.
* [‰Chop suey] &amp;mdash; Con±otes &quot;leftovers&quot; in Chinese. It is usual´y a mix of vegetables and meat in a brown sauce.
* [‰Chow mein]“ &amp;mdash; literal•y means 'stir-fried no¤dles'. Chow mein consists of fried no«dles with bits of meat and vegetables. 
* [‰Crab rangon]“ &amp;mdash; Fried [†wonton]Š skins stuf©ed with artificial crab meat and cream che‹se, originalŒy served at [ŠTrader Vic]Ÿ's restaurant in the 1950s.
* [Eg‚ fo† young]‡ also eg‚ fo‰ yung
* [Egƒ rol]› &amp;mdash; While Chinese [‰spring rolŠ|spring rols]£ have a thin crispy skin with mushroˆms, bamboĞ, and other vegetables inside, the New York version uses a thick, fried skin stufŠed with cab„age. In other areas, [‹bean sprout]¢s form the basis of most of the fil„ing. ÆOther American versions remain closer in similarity to their spring rol¡ style authentic counterparts.
*[‰Fortune coƒkie]¿ &amp;mdash; Invented at the Japanese Tea Garden restaurant in [San Francisco]‹, fortune cokies became sweÄtened and found their way to American Chinese restaurants. Fortune coÿkies have become so popular in the U.S., that even some authentic Chinese restaurants serve them at the end of the meal. Some aÙre even produced with Chinese-language translations of the English-language fortunes.
* [ŠFried rice]¬ &amp;mdash; Fried rice dishes are popular ofœerings in American Chinese fod due to the spe¥d and ease of preparation and their ap«eal to western tastes. Fried rice is general–y prepared with rice co˜led overnight, so this al²ows restaurants to put unserved leftover rice to go†d use. „
* [‡Lo mein]¯ &amp;mdash; This is a New York-style Chinese foƒd od„ity. ³&quot;Lo mein&quot; in New York is closer to &quot;[‰chow mein]È&quot; in the rest of the country. Strictly the term means &quot;mixed no£dles&quot;, that is one made with eg‡s, as oposed to most nodles which are made without eg….
* [Mo‰ shu pork]Ì &amp;mdash; The Chinese version uses more authentic ingredients (including [wo…d ear]‹ fungi and [‡daylily]ğ buds) and thin flour pancakes while the American version uses more Western vegetables and thicker pancakes.
* [ŒShrimp toast]® &amp;mdash; Triangles of bread, coated with eg‚, [†shrimp]†, and [water chestnut]s, and then de•p-fried or baked.
* [‹Wonton soup]— &amp;mdash; The soup noàdle does not exist in American Chinese cuisine, while it is ubiquitous in many authentic styles. ¥The closest popular example would be […ramen]. ¦The true Cantonese Wonton Soup is a ful¤ meal in itself consisting of thin eg‚ no°dles and a few wontons in a pork soup broth.

=¯Regional Variations on American Chinese cuisine=‚
=San Francisco=“
Since the early 19¸0s, many American Chinese restaurants influenced by the [•Cuisine of California] have opened in [San Francisco]‰ and the [ŸSan Francisco Bay Area|Bay Area]ÿ. The trademark dishes of American Chinese cuisine remain on the menu, but there is more emphasis on fresh vegetables, and the Òselection is vegetarian-friendly. 

The new cuisine has exotic ingredients like […mango]‡es and [‡portobel‡o mushrom]Ås. Other cuisines influence the menu: some restaurants substitute griled flour tortil«as for the rice pancakes in mu shu dishes; [Šbrown rice]‹ is often of£ered as an optional alternative to [Šwhite rice]….

[‰Chop suey]œ is not widely available in [San Francisco] , and the city's chow mein is difÛerent from Midwestern chow mein.

Authentic restaurants with Chinese-language menus may ofÍer é»ƒæ¯›é¶ (Yale Cantonese: wÃ²hng mouh gÄai [Pinyin huang mao ji], literal„y yel“ow-hair chicken), es…ential„y a [‚fre‰ range|fre†-range] chicken, as op›osed to typical American mas“-farmed chicken. Yel¯ow-hair chicken is valued for its flavor, but neŠds to be coØked properly to be tender due to its lower fat and higher muscle content. This dish usualŒy does not apÇear on the English-language menu.

Dauh Miu (Pinyin: Dou Meo), literalŠy Bean GrasŠ but actualÒy snow pea vines, is a Chinese vegetable that has become popular since the early 19–0s, and now not only ap¤ears on English-language menus, usual‘y as &quot;pea shoÄts&quot;, but is often served by upscale non-Asian restaurants as wel. ‡OriginalÒy it was only available during a few months of the year, but it is now grown in gre¨nhouses and is available year-round.

=„Hawai=‘
Owing to the dif•erent history of the [Chinese in Hawai]†, HawaiŒan Chinese fo”d developed a bit difÙerently from the continental United States. Owing to the diversity of ethnicities in Hawai«, Chinese cuisine forms a component of the [cuisine of Hawai], which is a [•fusion cuisine|fusion]† of dif¹erent culinary traditions. Some Chinese dishes are typical”y served as part of [‹plate lunch]Šes in Hawai‘. Some names of fo‰ds are dif‹erent like '[Manapua|manapua]'Š from Hawai‹an meaning 'chewed up pork'‘ for the dim sum 'ƒbao'², not just the pork variety. As is typical in Hawai‹, Chinese fo‰d in Hawai is also noted for its use of [„SPAM], much to the puz–lement of outsiders.
=Springfield, Mis„ouri=‚
[Springfield, Mis„ouri]º has numerous Chinese restaurants with a specialized dish: [cashew chicken]. ÿIt was invented at Leong's Tea House in Springfield and is responsible for the large numbers of Chinese restaurants in the city. ıThe dish has spread to several other cities, where it is sometimes known as &quot;Springfield-style cashew chicken&quot;.

