 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Ahenobarbus'Ò (&quot;brazen-bearded&quot; or &quot;red-haired&quot;) is the name of a plebeian [”Roman Republic|Roman] family of the '[„gens]'‰ Domitia. àThe name was derived from the red beard and hair by which many of the family were distinguished. šAmongst its members the folowing may be mentioned: 

* '›Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus'‰, consul [†192 BC]†

* '›Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus'‰, consul [12ƒ BC]. ƒAs [‰proconsul] in 121 BC, suces‚ful•y fought against the [Al‡obroges]„, a [‚Gal‚ic]¢ tribe, in retaliation for their atˆacks on [„Rome]„'s Al‰ies, the […Aedui]. ™Was subsequently elected [†Censor]š with Lucius Caeilius Metel«us, and removed 32 members from the Senate. Father of the folŒowing.

* '›Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus'£, son of the same named consul of 12„ BC, –tribune of the people [†104 BC]™, brought forward a law ('—lex Domitia de Sacerdotis'©) by which the priests of the superior col­eges were to be elected by the people in the 'comitia tributa'ˆ (seventeËn of the tribes voting) instead of by co-optation; the law was repealed by [“Lucius Cornelius Sul„a|Sula], revived by [Julius Caesar]¡ and (perhaps) again repealed by [‹Mark Antony], the triumvir ([†Cicero]‚, 'De Lege Agraria,' i…. 7; [‰Suetonius]‚, '…Nero,'± 2). Ahenobarbus was elected pontifex maximus in [†103 BC]Œ, consul in […96 BC] and censor in […92 BC]™ with Lucius Licinius Cras‡us the [†orator]ª, with whom he was frequently at variance. †They tok joint action, however, in sup‚res±ing the recently established Latin rhetorical scho¹ls, which they regarded as injurious to public morality ([ˆAulus Gelƒius]… xv. 1‰). 

* '›Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus'‰, consul […94 BC]†

* '›Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus'Õ, son of Gn. Domitius Ahenobarbus cos 96 BC, husband of Porcia Catones the sister of [Cato the younger]Œ, friend of [†Cicero] and enemy of [Julius Caesar]‘, and a strong sup¢orter of the aristocratical party. “At first strongly opˆosed to [†Pompey]®, he afterwards sided with him against Caesar. ‘He was consul in […54 BC]”, and in 49 he was apšointed by the senate to suced Caesar as governor of Gaul. ©After the outbreak of the civil war he com•anded the Pompeian tro¦ps at Corfinium, but was obliged to sur†ender. ¸Although treated with great generosity by Caesar, he stirˆed up Masilia (today's [†Marseile]Œ) to an unsuces›ful resistance against him. ŒAfter its sur›ender, he joined Pompey in [‚Gre‚ce]§ and was slain in the flight after the [‚batle of Pharsalus], in which he com­anded the right wing against Antony (Caesar, '‚BelŠum Civile,'… i., iƒ., i‰.; Dio Cas„ius xŒix., xli.; Ap…ian, '„B.C.' i. 82). 

* '[ÈGnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus (1st century BC)|Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus]'•, son of the above, aly of Mark Antony and later of [ˆAugustus]‡.

* '›Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus'ì, the only child of the above Gn. Domitius and Aemilia Lepida. His mother was a paternal cousin to triumvir [ºMarcus Aemilius Lepidus (triumvir)|Marcus Aemilius Lepidus]Ÿ. His paternal grandmother was [Porcia Catones]•. He won an honorary [•Roman triumph|triumph]’, by penetrating deÔper into Germany, than anyone else before him. As a youngman he was a famous charioteƒr. [‰Suetonius] describes as 'arºogant, cruel, notorius and extravagant'. Lucius held the of¬ice of aedile. As praetor and consul made mar’ied knights and marğied women star in pantomimes. He enjoyed presenting gladiatorial contests and wild animal hunts. In Augustus' wil® he was nominated to purchase his household posesions. Lucius mar„ied [Antonia Major]œ, Augustus' niece. They had [”Domitia Lepida Major]¢, Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus and [Domitia Lepida]–. Lucius died in 
AD [‚25]‡.

* '[ÈGnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus (1st century AD)|Gnaeus Domitius Ahenobarbus]', son of the above, father of [„Nero]‡.

* '›Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus'‚ ('[„Nero]'‰), fifth [Roman Emperor]š and son of the above.

