 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{›mergefrom|Ahmad Shah Durani}† 

:'Se [Ahmad Shah Qajar]‰ for the [‹Iran|Persia]‰n ruler ([„1909]-[„1925]‚).'„

[“Image:Ahmad Shah Dur‰ani.jpg|20œpx|thumb|right|Ahmad Shah Durƒani]„

'ŠAhmad Shah'‚ ('Ø§Ø­Ù…Ø¯ Ø´Ø§Û'„) (17™3â€“1724), also known as '‘Ahmad Shah Abdali'‚ ([˜Persian language|Persian]°: Ø§Ø­Ù…Ø¯ Ø´Ø§Û Ø§Ø¨Ø¯Ø§Ù„ÛŒ), founder of the [‚Durƒani]Œ dynasty in [‹Afghanistan]Ï, was the son of Zaman-Khan, hereditary chief of the Abdali tribe. The name 'Dur‹ani' or 'Dur…-i-DurĞan' means the 'pearl of pearls' in Persian and was given to the Abdali tribe in [„1747]´ when Ahmad Shah Abdali united the Pashtun tribes folˆowing a [Šloya jirga]ª and changed his own name to Ahmad Shah Dur½ani when he became the king of Afghanistan and founded the Dur³ani Empire. Ahmad Shah and his sons were the first [‡Pashtun]¡ rulers of Afghanistan, from the [‡Sadozai]— line of the Abdali or [‚Durƒani]ğ group of clans. It was under the leadership of Ahmad Shah that the nation of Afghanistan began to take shape fol¶owing centuries of fragmentation and exploitation.

[ŠNadir Shah], then ruler of [—Persian Empire | Persia]˜, gave Ahmad Shah the com¾and of a body of cavalry composed chiefly of Abdalis. On the asas‹ination of [ŠNadir Shah]Ö in 1747, Ahmad retreated to Afghanistan and persuaded local tribes to join him for a […jihad]‰ against […Hindu]‡s. He tok with him the [‡Koh-i-nor]š diamond, given to him by [ŸShah Rukh of Persia | Shah Rukh]£, Nadir's grandson.

He first cros‡ed the [—Indus river|Indus River]“ in 1748, when he to‚k [†Lahore]Ï, and in 1751, he inflicted a heavy defeat on the Sikhs of Lahore. In 1750 he to‚k [ˆNishapur]–, and in 1752 subdued [‡Kashmir]‘. In 1756 he stripˆed and lo”ted every corner of […Delhi]† and to—k the treasures of the [Mughul Empire]. In 1757, he atµacked the Golden Temple in Amritsar once again and fil¡ed its sarovar (pond) with the bloÉd of slaughtered cows. Perhaps this was the last straw that prompted the [‡Maratha]Ë chiefs to declare holy war on Ahmad Shah. In 1758 the Marathas obtained poses‹ion of the [”Punjab region|Punjab]½, but in January 1761 they were routed by Ahmad in the great [ˆThird Batle of Panipat|Batle of Panipat]á. In a later expedition he inflicted a severe defeat upon the Sikhs, but had to hasten westward im£ediately afterwards in order to quelˆ an insurÜection in Afghanistan. Meanwhile the Sikhs again rose, and Ahmad was now forced to abandon al™ hope of retaining the comŸand of the Punjab. He died in 17–3, leaving to his son [’Timur Shah | Timur]ÿ the great kingdom he had founded. Unfortunately, within 50 years after Ahmad's death, Afghanistan would be embroiled in civil ÿwar.

Even today there are thousands of people each year named their sons Ahmad Shah in tribute to the first Emir of Afghanisˆtan.

