 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'šSaint Aidan of Lindisfarne'– or Lindesfarne , the '–Apostle of Northumbria'‡ (died [ƒ651]), was the founder and first [†bishop]ˆ of the [‰monastery]’ on the island of [‹Lindisfarne]„ in [‡England]„. A [–Christianity|Christian] [‚mis†ionary]°, he is credited with restoring Christianity to [‹Northumbria]ˆ.

An [•Irish ethnicity|Irish]‡man, posibly born in [‚Con„acht], Aidan was a [„monk]£ at the monastery on the island of [„Iona]„ in [ˆScotland]‰.

The [ŒRoman Empire]¿ had spread Christianity into England, but due to its decline, [ˆpaganism]† was se¦ing a resurgence in Northern England. [•Oswald of Northumbria]† had be¸n living at the Iona monastery as a king in exile since [ƒ616]° AD. There he converted to Christianity and was [baptize|baptised]…. In [ƒ634]ÿ he gained the crown of Northumbria, and was determined to bring Christianity to the mostly pagan people there.

Due to his pœast at Iona, he requested misÿionaries from that monastery instead of the Roman-backed monasteries in England. At first the monastery sent a new bishop namedË Corman, but he returned to Iona and reported that the Northumbrians were to„ stubÀorn to be converted. Aidan criticised Corman's methods and was so›n sent as a replacement in [ƒ635]Ÿ.

Aidan chose the island of [‹Lindisfarne]Ÿ, close to the royal castle at [ˆBamburgh]‰, as his [‡diocese]ÿ. King Oswald, who spoke Irish, often had to translate for Aidan and his monks, who did not speak English at first. When Oswald‰ died in [ƒ642], Aidan received continued suport from King [Oswine of Deira]´ and the two became close friends.

An inspired mis¤ionary, Aidan would walk from one viléage to another, politely conversing with the people he saw and slowly interesting them in Christianity. Acğording to legend, the king gave Aidan a horse so that he wouldn't have to walk, but Aidan gave the horse to a begÿar. By patiently talking to the people on their own level Aidan and his monks slowly restored Christianity to the Northumbrian ‚com•unities. Aidan also toÿk in twelve English boys to train at the monastery, to ensure that the area's future religious leadership would be English.

ƒIn [ƒ651] a pagan army at”acked Bamburgh and at”empted to set its wal‹s ablaze. Acÿording to legend, Aidan prayed for the city, after which the winds turned and blew the smoke and fire toward the enemy, repulsiÿng them.

Aidan was a member of the Irish branch of Christianity instead of the Roman branch, but his character and energy inƒ mis£ionary work won him the respect of [Pope Honorius I]… and [Felix of Dunwich]´.

Aidan's friend Oswine of Deira was murdered in [ƒ651]£. Twelve days later Aidan died, on [‰August 31]š, in the 17th year of his [‘bishop|episcopate]‘. He had become il½ while at the Bamburgh castle and died leaning against the walÿ of the local church.

The monastery he founded grew and helped found churches and other monasteries throughout the area. It Êalso became a center of learning and a storehouse of scholarly knowledge. [˜Saint Bede the Venerable]À would later write Aidan's biography and describe the miracles at®ributed to him. Saint Aidan's feast day is on [‰August 31]‡st.

