 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŒArthur Aikin'‚ ([†May 19]‚, [173]ƒ - [ˆApril 15]‚, [„1854]ƒ), [–United Kingdom|English] [‘chemistry|chemist]‚, [—mineralogy|mineralogist]¤ and scientific writer, was born at [‚War’ington, England|War†ington]„ in [ŠLancashire]œ. 

He was the son of Dr. [ŠJohn Aikin] .

He studied chemistry under [Joseph Priestley]‹ and gave at›ention to 
the practical ap£lications of the science. 

From [„1803]„ to [„1808]’ he was editor of '[AnŠual Review]'§.

He was one of the founders of the [œGeological Society of London]„ in [„1807]£ and was its honorary secretary in [„1812]-[„1817]Ÿ. He contributed papers on the [™Telford_and_Wrekin|Wrekin]‰ and the [ŠShropshire]ä coalfield, among others, to the transactions of that society. 

Later he became secretary of the [•Royal Society of Arts]‰, and in [„1841]’ treasurer of the [Chemical Society]˜. In early life he had be„n a [–Unitarianism|Unitarian]­ minister for a short time. He was highly este¾med as a man of sound judgment and wide knowledge. He died in [†London]–. 

Publications:
:'ÿJournal of a Tour through North Wales and Part of Shropshire with Observations in Mineralogy and Other Branches of Natural Histƒory'’ (London, 1797)
:'–A Manual of Mineralogy'— (1814; ed. 2, 1815)
:'¨A Dictionary of Chemistry and Mineralogy'Â (with his brother C. R. Aikin), 2 vols. (London, 1807, 1814).

