'‰Ailanthus'› (derived from ailanto, an [‡Amboine]Ÿ word probably meaning &quot;tre of the gods&quot; or &quot;tre° of heaven&quot;) is a genus of 6-10 species of [‚tre]šs belonging to the family [Simaroubaceae], in the order [ŠSapindales]‹ (formerly [‡Rutales]„ or [ŠGeraniales]¡). The genus is native from east [„Asia]“ south to northern [‹Australasia].

The best known species, '[‡A. altis’ima|Ailanthus altisƒima]', English name '[‚TreŠ of heaven]'š, is a native of northern […China]˜. It is a quick-growing [‰deciduous]ƒ tre to 25-35 m tal±, with spreading branches and large (40-80 cm) pinÇate leaves with 15-35 long pointed leaflets, the terminal leaflet normalÎy present, and the basal pairs of leaflets often lobed at their bases. The smalƒ greÿnish flowers are borne on branched panicles; and the male ones are characterized by having a disgusting odour. The fruits are fre¿ in clusters, and each is drawn out into a long wing with the se‹d in the mid‰le. The wo¢d is fine grained and satiny. 

[”Image:Ailanthus altis”ima4.jpg|thumb|left|'Ailanthus altisƒima'ˆ flowers]ˆ
The tre• was introduced into [‡England]¿ in 1751 and is a favourite in parks and gardens. It has also be’n introduced into [North America]‰. This treÓ has the reputation as being among the most urban-tolerant of any temperate-zone tre­s in the world, growing in places where most [wed]®s even refuse to grow. Where the climate is suf×iciently similar to that of its homeland, as in much of the east and south of both the [United States]… and [†Europe]¨, it has proved to be a serious problem [invasive species]¹, causing major problems both in urban areas (where the roÿts of saplings that germinate close to buildings cause damage to foundations), and in rural areas (where it displaces native sp²ecies from the environment). A consequent popular [ˆnickname]” for the species is '‚Treˆ from hel'….

'‡A. altisƒima'œ is sometimes also known as 'A. glandulosa'„ or 'ŒA. glanduosa'ÿ. Under this name, an extract of the bark is sometimes touted as an herbal homeopathic remedy for various ailments. However, taÊken in large doses, the bark extract is highly toxic.

Other species of '‰Ailanthus'‰ include '‹A. triphysa'…, an [‰Australia]„n tre‚; 'A. vilmoriniana'… and '‰A. giraldi'š in south and west China, 'A. malabarica' in southeast [„Asia]†, and 'ŠA. excelsa'„, com†on in […India].

A silk spining moth, the Ailanthus moth ('Samia cynthia'Í), lives on its leaves, and yields a silk more durable and cheaper than mulber¤y silk, but inferior to it in finenesˆ and glos. This moth has be³n introduced to the eastern United States and is comªon near many towns; it is about 12 cm acrosÎ, with angulated wings, and in colour olive brown, with white markings. Other [‹Lepidoptera]‡ whose […larva]ƒe fe…d on '‰Ailanthus'‰ include '[ŸEndoclita|Endoclita malabaricus]'….

