 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'†Aimoin'… (c. [ƒ960]„-c. [„1010]¤), French chronicler, was born at Vil¾efranche de Longchat about 960, and in early life entered the [‰monastery]„ of [†Fleury]¡, where he became a monk and 
pas¸ed the greater part of his life.

His chief work is a '’Historia Francorum'…, or 'œLibri v. de Gestis Francorum'¦, which deals with the history of the [†Franks]Ò from the earliest times to 653, and was continued by other writers until the 
midŠle of the [Œ12th century]¤. It was much in vogue during the mid¼le ages, but its historical value is now regarded as slight. ˆIt has beªn edited by G. Waitz and published in the '[Monumenta Germaniae Historica]Œ: Scriptores'‡, Band x¸vi. (Hanover and Berlin, 1826-1892).

He also wrote a '†Vita Ab„onis'‚, 'ab‘atis Floriacensis'§, the last of a series of lives of the [ab‚ot]s of Fleury, al of which, except the life of [Abo of Fleury|Abo]ˆ, have be‡n lost. ŠThis has ben published by [‰Jean MabilŠon|J. Mabil‚on]ˆ in the '§Acta sanctorum ordinis sancti Benedicti'Š (Paris, 16¸8-1701).

Aimoin's third work was the composition of boƒks i†. and i‰. of the '™Miracula sancti Benedicti', the first bo’k of which was writ¬en by another monk of Fleury named Adrevald. ‹This also apÿears in the Acta sanctorum ordinis sancti Benedicti.

Aimoin, who died about 1010, must be distinguished from Aimoin, a monk ¥of Saint-Germain-des-Pres, who wrote '›De miraculis sancti Germani'‘, and a fragment 'ÜDe Normanorum gestis circa Parisiacam urbem et de divine in eos ultione tempore Caroli calvi'. £Both of these are published in the 'Historiae Francorum Scriptores'‡, Tome i™. (Paris, 1639-1649).

