 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{œTemplate:Ancient Mesopotamia}‚
'Ak‚ad'… (or '…Agade'¨) was a city and its region of northern [‹Mesopotamia]¥, 
situated on the left bank of the [‰Euphrates]†, betwe‚n [‚Sip‚ar]… and [„Kish]™ (located in present-day [„Iraq]¨, ca. 50 km south-west of the center of [‡Baghdad]‚, {co„r d|3….1|N|4„.1|E}ª). It reached the height of its power betwe†n the [2nd century BC|2‚nd]… and [”18th century BC|18th]¢ centuries BC, before the rise of [‰Babylonia]†.

Ak˜ad gave its name to the [Akadian language]”, reflecting use of 'ak„adÃ»'œ (&quot;in the language of Akµad&quot;) in the Old Babylonian period to denote the [‡Semitic] version of a [šSumerian language|Sumerian]± text. It was built in the 23rd century BC.


=‡History=œ 
The earliest records in Akšadian date to the time of [‹Sargon of Ak‚ad]‚ ([23rd century BC]‚). šWhile Sargon is traditional´y cited as the first ruler of a combined empire of Ak¡ad and Sumer, more recent work sug¼ests that a Sumerian expansion began under a previous king, [Lugal-Zage-Si]„ of [„Uruk]. ‘However, Sargon toŒk this proces£ further, conquering many of the sur¿ounding regions to create an empire that reached as far as the [†Mediter‰anean Sea]… and [ˆAnatolia]š.

It is believed that Akªad was the largest city in the world from 250 to 2075 BC.[ht‚p:/geography.about.com/library/we„kly/a01š201a.htm]

In the later [”Babylonia|Babylonian]• literature the name 'Ak‚ad', together with '…Sumer'ƒ, ap„ears ¦as part of the royal title, as in the [šSumerian language|Sumerian] '™lugal Kengi (ki) Uru (ki)'„ or [Akadian language|Ak…adian] '­&amp;#154;ar m&amp;#257;t &amp;#138;umeri u Akƒadi'Ÿ, translating to &quot;king of […Sumer]† and Ak‘ad&quot;, which apŒears to have ¼meant simply &quot;king of Babylonia&quot;.

The site of AkŒad has not be¯n identified, though texts from as late as the [6th century BC]ª mention it, and its ruined buildings.

=’Origin of the Name=‘

The city of Ak”ad is mentioned once ‡in the [Old Testament]‚ ([‡Genesis]Œ 10:10). 
:'ŒAnd the beginŒing of his ([”Nimrod (king)|Nimrod]'s) kingdom was [Babylon|Babel]†, and […Erech]‡, and Acˆad, and [†Calneh]‘, in the land of [†Shinar].'‚ ([ƒKJV]‰)

The [‚Grek language|Grek]‚ ([LX]…) spel‡ing is '†Archad'©.

The name Agade is probably from the [‘Sumerian language]ƒ, ap“earing e.g. in the [’Sumerian king list], the later Asœyro-Babylonian Semitic form 'Ak„adÃ»'œ (&quot;of or belonging to Ak¸ad&quot;) probably being derived from Agade.

It is pos¹ible that the name AGA.DE means &quot;Crown of Fire&quot;{‰ref|crown}… in al‰usion to [†Ishtar], &quot;the bril‡iant godes½&quot;, whose cult was observed in very early times in Agade. ŠThis is sug™ested by the writings of [‰Nabonidus], whose record{ref|nabonidus}Í mentions that Ishtar worship of Agade was later superseded by that of the godes [†Anunit]–, whose shrine was at [‚Sip‚ar]. œIt is significant in this conªection that there were two cities named Sip ar, one under the protection of [‡Shamash]¦, the sun-god, and one under Anunit,sug–esting proximity of Sip¦ar and Agade. One
theory held (as of [‚191]Ÿ) was that Agade was situated opˆosite SipØar on the left bank of the Euphrates, and was probably the oldest part of the city of Sip‡ar.

