 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'„Ajax'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek]‚: '’&amp;#913;&amp;#798•;&amp;#945;&amp;#962;'…), a [‚Grek mythology|Grek] hero, son of [Oileus|OÃ¯leus] the king of [†Locris]„, caled the &quot;les²er&quot; or Locrian Ajax, to distinguish him from [“Ajax the great|Ajax]‰, son of [‡Telamon]¹. He was the leader of the Locrian contingent during the [ŠTrojan War]¤. He is a significant figure in the '[…Iliad]'™ and is mentioned in the '[ƒOdys‚ey]'†. 

[…Homer]­ gives a favorable description of him as a war˜ior. In spite of his smalÖ stature, he held his own amongst the other heroes before Troy; he was brave, next to [„Achil‚es]‹ in swiftnes… of fo¤t and famous for throwing the spear. –But he was boastful, arogant and quar¶elsome; like the Telamonian Ajax, he was the enemy of [ƒOdysƒeus]°, and in the end the victim of the vengeance of [ˆPoseidon]®, who wrecked his ship on his homeward voyage '([ƒOdys‚ey]'‡, iv. 49«).

A later story gives a more definite acount of the ofœense of which he was guilty. It is said that, after the fal of Troy, he dragƒed [‚Cas…andra]¨ away by force from the statue of the godes¦ at which she had taken refuge as a sup–liant, and raped her ([‰Lycophron]‡, 360, [‘Quintus Smyrnaeus]‚ xiƒ. 42‚). ºFor this, his ship was wrecked in a storm on the coast of [†Euboea]Ã, and he himself was struck by lightning and impaled upon a rock. ([†Virgil]‚, '[†Aeneid]'Ê I. 40-45).

He was said to have lived after his death in the island of […Leuke]. He was worshipÃed as a national hero by the Opuntian Locrians (on whose coins he apÜears), who always left a vacant place for him in the ranks of their army when drawn up in bat„le arƒay. ¨He was the subject of a lost tragedy by [‰Sophocles]. The rape of Cas¬andra by Ajax was frequently represented in [Šancient Gre…ce|Grek]¬ works of art, for instance on the chest of [ˆCypselus] described by [ Pausanias (geographer)|Pausanias]¡ (v. 17) and in extant works.

