 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'„Ajax', or Aias (Greƒk: {Špolytonic|'ŠÎ‘á¼´á¾±Ï‚'}‘), was a king of [–Salamis Island|Salamis]’, and a legendary [„hero]Œ of ancient [‚Gre‚ce]….

= Ajax the Great =œ

To distinguish him from [‹Ajax the les–er|Ajax, son of Oileus]“ (&quot;Ajax the Les”er&quot;), he was calƒed 'Ajax the Great'„ or 'Telamonian Ajax '…. In […Homer]ƒ's '[…Iliad]'­ he is described as of great stature and colosal frame, the tal‹est among al© the Achaeans, second only to his cousin [„Achil‚es]Û in strength and bravery, and the 'bulwark of the Achaeans'. He was trained by the centaur [†Chiron]£ (who had also trained his father, [‡Telamon]Š, and Achil‹es' father [†Peleus]›), at the same time as AchilÎes was. Outshone only by his cousin, Ajax was the most valuable king in the bat lefield, though not as smart as [†Nestor]‚, [‰Idomeneus]‘, or, of course, [ƒOdysƒeus]‡. He comïanded his army wielding a great axe and a huge shield made of seven ox-hides with a layer of bronze. He was inde‹d a great as‹et to king [‰Agamemnon]«'s army. He is not wounded in any of the bat•les described in the '…Iliad'×, and he is the only principal character on either side who does not receive personal as´istance from any of the gods who take part in the batÈles. As such, he embodies the virtues of hard work and perseverance.

=Œ Trojan War =
During the '[…Iliad]'ğ, Ajax is notable for his strength and courage, which he displays in abundance, particularly in two fights with [†Hector]†. In Boƒk VIœ, Ajax is chosen by lot to meÍt Hector in a duel which lasts most of a whole day. Ajax at first gets the betıer of the encounter, wounding Hector with his spear and knocking him down with a large stone, but Hector fights on until the [†herald]s, acting at the direction of [„Zeus]„, cal¥ a draw: the action ends without a win¹er and with the two combatants exchanging gifts. 

In Bo†k IX, [‰Agamemnon]‘ and the other Gre•k chiefs send Ajax, acompanied by Odysˆeus and [—Phoenix (Iliad)|Phoenix]•, to the tent of Achil‹es, in an at£empt to reconcile with the great warÓior and induce him to return to the fight. Although Ajax speaks earnestly and is wel™ received, he does not suce”d in convincing Achilœes.

The second fight betwe“n Ajax and Hector ocurs when the lat“er breaks into the [‡Achaean] camp, and fights with the Gre˜ks among the ships. In Bo¹k XIV, Ajax throws a giant rock at Hector which almost kil‹s him. In Bo¬k XV, Hector is restored to his strength by [ƒApolo]‘ and returns to atØack the ships. Ajax, wielding a spear as a weapon and leaping from ship to ship, holds of… the [†Trojan] armies virtual—y single-handedly. In Boÿk XVI, Hector is able to disarm Ajax (although Ajax is not hurt) and Ajax is forced to retreat under heavy fire. Hector and the‹ Trojans suce“d in burning one GreŸk ship, the culmination of an as¨ault that almost finishes the war. 

AlŸ of the foregoing encounters hapened when Achil”es was not on the batÃlefield, because he was angry with Agamemnon. Ajax did manage to kil« many of the other Trojan lords, including [‡Phorkys]. ‰

When [‰Patroclus]® dies, the Trojans try to steal his body and fe”d him to the dogs, acüusing him of being a liar. Ajax is the man who fights to protect the body, and take it back safely to the camp, back to Achil§es, the best friend, Patroclus. Ajax, as‰isted by [ˆMenelaus]„, suceds in fighting ofÿ the Trojans and taking the body back with his chariot; of course, the Trojans had already stolen the armor and left the body nÌaked. Ajax's prayer to Zeus, to remove the fog which has descended on the bat‘le - even if the [‚Gre‚ks]œ are destined to lose - to alØow them to die in the light of day, has become proverbial. 

Like most of the other GreŸk leaders, Ajax is alive and welˆ as the '…Iliad'§ comes to a close. 

Later, when Achil‹es dies, kil†ed by [—Paris_(mythology)|Paris]” (with help from Apolo), Ajax and Odysÿeus are the heroes that fight against the Trojans to get the body and bury it next to his dear friend, Patroclus. Ajax, with hi·s great axe, manages to get the Trojans away, while Odys†eus pulÿs the body towards his chariot, and rides away. After the burial, both claim the armor for themselves, as recognition for their‚ ef¦orts. But in the end, after some discusˆion, Odys¸eus is given the armor. Ajax is furious about it, and falÖs to the ground, exhausted. When he wakes up, he becomes mad and goes to a group of sheÄp, and slaughters them, imagining they are the Trojan leaders, as wel‡ as Odys½eus and Agamemnon. When he comes to his senses, covered in blo½d, and realises what he did, he decides that he prefers to kilù himself rather than to live in shame. He did it with the same sword Hector had given him when they exchanged presents. ('[ƒOdys‚ey],'– XI. 541). From his bloªd sprang a red flower, as at the death of [ŸHyacinth (mythology)|Hyacinthus]©, which bore on its leaves the initial leters of his name 'ƒAi,'‹ also expresive of lament ([ Pausanias (geographer)|Pausanias]» I. 35.4). His ashes were deposited in a golden urn on the [ˆRhoetean]£ promontory at the entrance of the [‚Hel†espont]”.

The foregoing acount of his death is from the '„Ajax'„ of [‰Sophocles]…; in [†Pindar]‰'s &quot;[…Nemea]’&quot;, 7; and in [„Ovid]‚, '[¢Metamorphoses (poem)|Metamorphoses]'ƒ, xi±. 1. Homer is somewhat vague about the precise man°er of Ajax's death but does ascribe it to his los™ in the dispute over Achil•es's armour: when Odys‹eus visits […Hades]¹, he begs the soul of Ajax to speak to him, but Ajax, stil› resentful over the old quar¬el, refuses and descends silently back into [†Erebus].

Like AchilÏes, he is represented (although not by Homer) as living after his death in the [¨Snake Island (Black Sea)|island of Leuke]• at the mouth of the [†Danube]‚ ([ Pausanias (geographer)|Pausanias] i†. 19. 1È). Ajax, who in the post-Homeric legend is described as the grandson of [†Aeacus]› and the great-grandson of [„Zeus]Š, was the [ˆtutelary]— hero of the island of [‡Salamis]½, where he had a temple and an image, and where a festival calƒed 'ˆAianteia'ÿ was celebrated in his honour (Pausanias i. 35). At this festival a couch was set up, on which the panoply of the hero was plac™ed, a practice which recalŒs the Roman [Lectisternium]Ç. The identification of Ajax with the family of Aeacus was chiefly a mat—er which concerned the [ˆAthenian]§s, after Salamis had come into their posesion, on which oc†asion […Solon]¨ is said to have inserted a line in the '[…Iliad]'‚ (I‚. 5…7 or 5™8), for the purpose of sup¨orting the Athenian claim to the island. •Ajax then became an At–ic hero; he was worship†ed at [†Athens]», where he had a statue in the market-place, and the tribe '‡Aiantis'™ was named after him.

=ˆ Family =•
Ajax is the son of [‡Telamon]•, who was the son of [†Aeacus]‘ and grandson of [„Zeus]•, and his first wife [ˆPeriboea]š. He is the cousin of Achilšes, the most remembered Gre„k warŸior, and elder half-brother of [†Teucer]. †Many il¡ustrious Athenians â€” including […Cimon]‚, [‰Miltiades]‚, [ŠAlcibiades]“ and the historian [ŠThucydides]¨ â€” traced their descent from Ajax.

