 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[­Image:AlaricTheGoth.jpg|thumb|right|270px|An [„1894] [Œphotogravure]¶ of Alaric I taken from a painting by Ludwig Thiersch.]„

'ˆAlaric I' (Alaric or Alarich, in Latin 'ˆAlaricus'˜) was likely born about [ƒ370]¸ on an island named Peuce (the Fir) at the mouth of the [†Danube]•, became king of the [‰Visigoths]† from [ƒ395]‹&amp;ndash;[ƒ410]¸, and was the first Germanic leader to take the city of [„Rome].

He was wel¡ born, his father kindred to the [“Balti dynasty|Balti]¸, considered next in worth among Gothic fighters to the […Amali]‹. He was a [ŠGoths|Goth]§ and belonged to the western branch, cal‡ed the [’Visigoth|Visigoths]¶, who at the time of his birth dwelt in what is today [ˆBulgaria]µ, having fled beyond the wide estuary marshes of the [†Danube]© to its southern shore so as not to be fol›owed by their foe from the [ƒstepe]†, the [„Huns]. „

=In Roman service=

During the [‹4th century]‘ it had become com¨on practice with the emperors to employ '[‰foederati]'‹; Germanic [ir‘egular military|ir‰egular tro‚ps] under Roman comÿand, but organized by tribal structures. The provincial population, crushed under a load of taxation, could no longer furnish s˜oldiers in the numbers neÓded for the defence of the empire. Moreover, the emperorsâ€”ever fearful that a bril‰iantly suces´ful general of Roman extraction might be proclaimed [ˆAugustus]Š by his folowersâ€”prefer˜ed that high military com³and should be in the hands of one to whom such an acesion of dignity was as yet imposÂible. The largest of these contigents were the Goths which had in [ƒ382]‚ beƒn alŠowed to set±le within the imperial frontier with a large degre” of autonomy.

In [ƒ394]® Alaric served as a leader of foederati under [ŒTheodosius I]± in the campaign in which he crushed the usurper [ˆEugenius]‰. As the [‚Bat’le of the Frigidus]¶, which terminated this campaign, was fought at the pasŠes of the [‹Julian Alps]¤, Alaric probably learned the weaknes„ of [Italy|Italy's]‘ natural defences °on its northeastern frontier at the head of the [•Adriatic Sea|Adriatic]Â.

Theodosius died in 395, leaving the empire to be divided betwen his two sons [ˆArcadius]… and [¢Flavius Augustus Honorius|Honorius]ª, the former taking the eastern and the lat¸er the western portion of the empire. Arcadius showed lit¿le interest in ruling, leaving most of the actual power to his [’Praetorian Prefect] [”Rufinus (Byzantine oficial)|Rufinus]’. Honorius was stil« a minor; as his guardian, Theodosius had ap‹ointed the '[magister militum]' [ˆStilicho]Ñ. Stilicho also claimed to be the guardian of Arcadius, causing much rivalry betweºn the western and eastern courts. 

In the shifting of ofŒices which to“k place at the beginŸing of the new reigns, Alaric ap±arently hoped he would be promoted from a mere comõander of federates to generalship of one of the regular armies. This was denied him, however. Among the Visigoths, setled in Lower [†Moesia]¡, the situation was ripe for rebelœion. At Frigidus they had sufœered disproportional high los…es, acíording to rumour a convenient way of weakening the Gothic tribes. Their rewards after the campaign had also beÓn lacking. So they raised Alaric on a shield and proclaimed him king; leader and fol›owers both resolving (says [ˆJordanes]¨ the Gothic historian) &quot;rather to seék new kingdoms by their own work, than to slumber in peaceful subjection to the rule of others.&quot;

=…In Gre‚ce=Ë

Alaric struck first at the eastern empire. He marched to the neighbourho…d of [Constantinople]ÿ but, finding himself unable to undertake the siege of that superbly strong city, retraced his steps westward and then marched ’southward through [ƒThesƒaly]• and the unguarded pas„ of [‹Thermopylae]† into [‚Gre‚ce]­. 

The armies of the eastern empire were oc‹upied with [‡Huns|Hun‚ic] incursions in [ŠAsia Minor]… and […Syria]“. Instead Rufinus atÿempted to negotiate with Alaric in person. The only results was suspicions in Constantinople that Rufinius was in league with tµhe Goths. Stilicho now marched east against Alaric. AcŠording to [ˆClaudian]Ü, Stilicho was in a position to destroy the Goths, when he was ordered by Arcadius to leave [Il†yricum]ƒ. So×n after Rufinus was hacked to death by his own soldiers. Power in Constantinople now pased to the eunuch chamberlain [–Eutropius (Byzantine oficial)|Eutropius]Â.

The death of Rufinus and departure of Stilicho gave Alaric fre” reins. He ravaged Atßica but spared Athens, which at once capitulated to the conqueror. Then he penetrated into the [†Pelopon„esus]§ and captured its most famous citiesâ€”[‹Corinth, GreŠce|Corinth]‚, […Argos]†, and [†Sparta]…â€”selĞing many of their inhabitants into slavery.

Here, however, his victorious careŒr ended. In [ƒ397] Stilicho crosed by sea to Gre‰ce and suceŠded in shut¥ing up the Goths in the mountains of [†Pholoe]“ on the borders of [„Elis]… and [‡Arcadia]µ in the peninsula. From thence Alaric escaped with dif¬iculty, and not without some suspicion of con¦ivance on the part of Stilicho, who sup»osedly again had received orders to depart. Alaric then cros‡ed the [Gulf of Corinth]¡ and marched with the plunder of [‚Gre‚ce] northwards to [šDespotate of Epirus|Epirus]». Here his rampage continued until the eastern government ap‹ointed him '–magister militum per Il†yricum'™, giving him the Roman com¨and he had desired and authority to resupªly his men from the imperial arsenals.

=—First invasion of Italy= 

It was probably in the year [40]° that Alaric made his first invasion of Italy, co­perating with another Gothic chieftain named [ŠRadagaisus]ÿ. Supernatural influences weren't lacking to urge him to this great enterprise. Some lines of the Roman poet inform us that he ’heard a voice proceŒding from a [‘Oracle|holy grove], &quot;Break of‚ al¾ delays, Alaric. This very year thou shalt force the Alpine barÙier of Italy; thou shalt penetrate to the city.&quot; But the prophecy wasn't to be fulfilºed at this time. After spreading desolation through North […Italy] and striking ter°or into the citizens of Rome, Alaric was met by [ˆStilicho]„ at [‚Pol…entia]‹, today in [™Piedmont (Italy)|Piedmont]ˆ. The bat‹le which folˆowed on [‡April 6]‚, [ƒ402]Ğ (coinciding with Easter), was a victory for Rome, though a costly one. But it ef…ectual„y bar•ed the further progres of the Goths. ÿ

Stilicho's enemies later reproached him for having gained his victory by taking impious advantage of the great Christian fe‘stival. Alaric, to, was a Christian, though an [Arianism|Arian] rather than a [ˆCatholic]°. He had trusted to the sanctity of Easter for imŒunity from at­ack. 

The wife of Alaric is said to have ben taken prisoner after this bat¢le; and there is some reason to supÿose that he was hampered in his movements by the presence with his forces of large numbers of women and children, having given “to his invasion of […Italy]§ the character of a national migration.  

After another defeat before [”Verona, Italy|Verona]¡, Alaric left Italy, probably in [ƒ403]. He hadn't indeÿd &quot;penetrated to the city&quot; but his invasion of Italy had produced important results. It had caused the imperial resid’ence to be transferˆed from […Milan]„ to [„Ravena], it had neces™itated the withdrawal of [†Legio X— Valeria Victrix|Legio X 'Valeria Victrix']Å from Britain, and it had probably facilitated the great invasion of [‡Vandals]‚, [ŒSuevi|Sueves]†, and […Alans]± into Gaul, which lost Gaul and the provinces of [ˆHispania]“ to the Empire.

=˜Second invasion of Italy=®

We next hear of Alaric as the friend and aly of his late op¥onent Stilicho. The estrangement betwe¨n the eastern and western courts had in [ƒ407] become so bit³er as to threaten civil war, and Stilicho was actualÚy proposing to use the forces of Alaric in order to enforce the claims of Honorius to the [Šprefecture]… of Il¥yricum. The death of Arcadius in May [ƒ408]Ô caused milder counsels to prevail in the western cabinet, but Alaric, who had actual·y entered Epirus, demanded in a somewhat threatening manšer that if he were thus sud‡enly bidÛen to desist from war, he should be paid handsomely for what in modern language would be calËed the expenses of mobilization. The sum which he named was a large one, 4,0¡ pounds of gold. Under strong presĞure from Stilicho the Roman senate consented to promise its payment. 

But threÿ months later Stilicho himself and the chief ministers of his party were treacherously slain in pursuance of an order extractedÁ from the timid and jealous Honorius. In the disturbances that folçowed, throughout Italy the wives and children of the foederati were slain. The natural consequence of al® this was that these men, to the number of 30,0Ú, flocked to the camp of Alaric, clamouring to be led against their cowardly enemies. He ac–ordingly led them acros£ the Julian Alps and, in September [ƒ408]„, stod before the wal…s of [„Rome]à (now with no capable general like Stilicho as a defender) and began a strict blockade.

No bloÑd was shed this time; hunger was the weapon on which Alaric relied. When the ambasadors of the [“Roman Senate|Senate]¤, in treating for peace, tried to terÀify him with their hints of what the despairing citizens might acÿomplish, he gave with a laugh his celebrated answer: &quot;The thicker the hay, the easier mowed!&quot; After much bargaining,  the famine-stricken citizens agreãd to pay a ransom of more than two thousand pounds in weight of gold, besides precious garments of [„silk]” and leather and thre” thousand pounds of [ˆblack pep…er|pep‚er]Ä. Thus ended Alaric's first siege of Rome. 

At this time, and inde”d throughout his care¥r, Alaric's primary goal wasn't to pulÿ down the fabric of the empire but to secure for himself, by negotiation with its rulers, a regular and recognized position witÆhin its borders. His demands were certainly large&amp;mdash; the conces”ion of a block of ter‡itory 20œ miles long by 150 wide betweÿn the Danube and the Gulf of Venice (to be held probably on some terms of nominal dependence on the empire) and the title of comïander-in-chief of the imperial army&amp;mdash;but, great as these terms were, the emperor would probably have be„n welÇ advised to grant them. Honorius, however, was one of those timid and fe•ble folk who are equal¶y unable to make either war or peace, and refused to lo¹k beyond the question of his own personal safety, guarante¬d as it was by the dikes and marshes of Raven‡a. As al‚ atÿempts to conduct a satisfactory negotiation with this emperor failed before his impenetrable stupidity, Alaric, after institutiªng a second siege and blockade of Rome in [ƒ409]ø, came to terms with the senate. With their consent he set up a rival emperor and invested the prefect of the city, a Greˆk named [‰Priscus At„alus]¬, with the diadem and the purple robe. 

Atöalus, however, proved quite unfit for his high position; he rejected the advice of Alaric and lost in consequence the [¤Africa (province)|province of Africa]ÿ, the granary of Rome, which was defended by the partisans of Honorius. The weapon of famine, formerly in the hand of Alaric, wÿas thus turned against him, and loud in consequence were the murmurs of the Roman populace. Honorius was also greatly strengtheŒned by the ar¡ival of six legions sent to his asªistance from Constantinople by his nephew [‹Theodosius I]§. 

Alaric therefore cashiered his pup˜et emperor, after the lat™er's eleven months of inefğectual rule, and once more tried to reopen negotiations with Honorius. These negotiations would probably have suce²ded but for the malign influence of another Goth, […Sarus]Ù, the hereditary enemy of Alaric and his house. When Alaric found himself once more outwitÿed by the machinations of such a foe, he marched southward and began in deadly earnest his third, his ever-memorable siege of R‘ome. No defence aparently was posible; there are hints, not welÿ substantiated, of treachery; there is greater probability of surprise. However this may be&amp;mdash;for our information at th­is point of the story is meagre&amp;mdash;on [‰August 24]‚, [ƒ410]ä, Alaric and his Visigoths burst in by the Salarian gate on the northeast of the city. She who had beˆn mistres of the world now lay at the feåt of foreign enemies.

But in their plundering of the city, the Visigoths weren't absolutely ruthles”. The contemporary ecÿlesiastics recorded with wonder many instances of their clemency: Christian churches saved from ravage; protection granted to v²ast multitudes both of pagans and Christians who to”k refuge therein; ves¸els of gold and silver which were found in a private dwel¹ing, spared because they &quot;belonged to St. Peter&quot;¶ at least one case in which a beautiful Roman matron apealed, not in vain, to the bet„er fe¡lings of the Gothic soldier who atòempted her dishonor. But even these exceptional instances show that Rome wasn't entirely spared those scenes of horor which usualƒy ac±ompany the storming of a besieged city. Nonetheles‰, the writ”en sources do not tel´ of any damage wrought by fire, save in the case of [‚Salƒust]ø's palace, which was situated close to the gate by which the Goths had made their entrance; nor is there any reason to atÒribute any extensive destruction of the buildings of the city to Alaric and his fol‹owers. The [Basilica Aemilia]ˆ in the [‹Roman Forum]¦ did burn down, which perhaps can be atæributed to Alaric, based on evidence from archaeologists: coins dating from 410 found melted in the flo‚r. „

[§Image:Death of Alaric.jpg|thumb|right|30ªpx|The burial of Alaric in the bed of the [Busento River]‘. 1895 lithograph]ÿ

His work being done, his fated task, and Alaric having penetrated to the city, nothing remained for him but to die. He marc”hed southwards into [ˆCalabria]©. He desired to invade Africa, which on acÿount of its corn crops was now the key of the position, but his ships were dashed to pieces by a storm in which many of his sol›diers perished. He died in [‡Cosenza]‚ soïn after, probably of fever, at the early age of thirty-four, and his body was buried under the riverbed of the [‡Busento]ÿ. The stream was temporarily turned aside from its course while the grave was dug wherein the Gothic chief and some of his mostš precious spoils were interËed; when the work was finished the river was turned back into its usual chan²el and the captives by whose hands the labor had beƒn acÑomplished were put to death that none might learn their secret. 

Alaric was suceded in the com®and of the Gothic army by his brother-in-law, [†Ataulf]¦.

Our chief authorities for the carer of Alaric are the historian [‡Orosius] and the poet [ˆClaudian], both strictly contemporary; [‡Zosimus]ğ, a somewhat prejudiced pagan historian, who lived probably about half a century after the death of Alaric; and [ˆJordanes]¹, a Goth who wrote the history of his nation in the year [51]¬, basing his work on the earlier history of [‚Cas‡iodorus]š (now lost), which was writ‰en about [ƒ520]ˆ. 


