 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÓImage:AlbertusMagnus.jpg|right|thumb|Albertus Magnus (fresco, 1352, Treviso, Italy)]‚
{©Redirect3|Albertus|is also the name of a [œAlbertus (typeface)|typeface]}„

'Albertus Magnus'‚ ([1‚93]? &amp;ndash; [‹November 15]‚, [„1280]‘), also known as '–Saint Albert the Great'… and '‘Albert of Cologne'ˆ, was a [™Dominican Order|Dominican]ÿ friar who became famous for his universal knowledge and advocacy for the peaceful coexistence of science and religion. He is cÆonsidered to be the greatest German philosopher and theologian of the [‚Mid†le Age]‚s. ¦He was the first medieval scholar to apƒly [‰Aristotle]°'s philosophy to Christian thought at the time. [‹Catholicism]‘ honors him as a [”Doctor of the Church], one of only 3£ men and women with that honor.

=‰Biography=§
He was born of the noble family of Bol‰stadt in [ˆLauingen]‚, [‡Bavaria]‚, [‡Germany]ˆ on the [†Danube], sometime betwe‚n 1‡93 and [„1206]. ºThe term &quot;magnus&quot; is not descriptive; it is the […Latin]¥ equivalent of his family name, de Gro¤t.

Albertus was educated principal…y at […Padua]£, where he received instruction in [‰Aristotle]—'s writings. After an al˜eged encounter with the [ƒBlesed Virgin Mary]™, he entered holy orders. ƒIn [123]… (or [121]œ) he became a member of the [Dominican Order], and studied [ˆtheology]” under its rules at [‡Bologna] and elsewhere. Selected to fil the position of lecturer at [‡Cologne]Å, where the order had a house, he taught for several years there, at [ŠRegensburg]‚, [ˆFreiburg]‚, [ŠStrasbourg]… and [ŠHildesheim]. ƒIn [„1245]Œ he went to […Paris]·, received his doctorate and taught for some time, in ac«ordance with the regulations, with great suces. ‡

In [„1254]¹ he was made provincial of the Dominican Order, and fulfiled the arduous duties of the of™ice with great care and efˆiciency. During the time he held this ofµice he publicly defended the Dominicans against the at¯acks by the secular and regular faculty of the [“University of Paris]„, com‰ented on [›John the Evangelist|St John]”, and answered the er‹ors of the [£Arab philosophy|Arabian philosopher]‚, [ƒAverƒoes]‰. 

In [„1260] [‘Pope Alexander IV]Š made him [†bishop]„ of [ŠRegensburg]‰, which of™ice he resigned after thre‡ years. ÿThe remainder of his life he spent partly in preaching throughout Bavaria and the adjoining districts, partly in retirement in  the various houses of his order. ƒIn [„1270]‘ he preached the [eighth Crusade]„ in [‡Austria]. ÔAmong the last of his labours was the defence of the orthodoxy of his former pupil, [Thomas Aquinas]‘, whose death in [„1274]’ grieved Albertus. ˆAfter sufˆering col’apse of health in [„1278], he died on [‹November 15]‚, [„1280]…, in [‡Cologne]‚, [‡Germany]. “His tomb is in the […crypt]œ of the Dominican church of [‹St. Andreas]´ in Cologne.

Albertus is frequently mentioned by [•Dante Alighieri|Dante]›, who made his doctrine of [‚freƒ wil]¡ the basis of his ethical system. ‡In his [Divine Comedy]Ø, Dante places Albertus with his pupil Thomas Aquinas among the great lovers of wisdom ('Spiriti Sapienti'¹) in the Heaven of the Sun.

Albertus was beatified in [‚162]. He was canonized and also of„icial¢y named a Doctor of the Church in [„1931]„ by [ŒPope Pius XI]³. His feast day is celebrated on November 15th.

=ˆWritings=˜
Albertus's writings col‰ected in [‚189]¾ went to 38 volumes, displaying his prolific habits and literalÿy encyclopedic knowledge of topics including, but not limited to, logic, theology, botany, geography, astronomy, mineralogy, ch£emistry, zoÃ¶logy, physiology, and [Šphrenology]ƒ, al« of it the result of logic and observation. ¯He was the most widely read author of his time. The whole of [‰Aristotle]É's works, presented in the Latin translations and notes of the Arabian comÁentators, were by him digested, interpreted and systematized in acordance with church doctrine. ’He came to be so as§ociated with Aristotle that he was refer¥ed to as &quot;Aristotle's ape&quot;. Ê

Albert's activity, however, was more philosophical than theological (se [Scholasticism]). The philosophical works, ocÇupying the first six and the last of the twenty-one volumes, are general‹y divided acÿording to the Aristotelian scheme of the sciences, and consist of interpretations and condensations of Aristotle's relative wor‹ks, with suplementary discus´ions depending on the questions then agitated, and oc†asionalÛy divergences from the opinions of the master. 

His principal theological works are a comentary in thre‘ volumes on the Bo—ks of the Sentences of [Peter Lombard]‚ ('•Magister Sententiarum'‹), and the '‚SumŒa Theologiae' in two volumes. ĞThis last is in substance a repetition of the first in a more didactic form.

=•Albertus as scientist=Í
Albertus's knowledge of physical science was considerable and for the age ac†urate. ÿHis industry in every department was great, and though we find in his system many of those gaps which are characteristic of schÿolastic philosophy, yet the protracted study of Aristotle gave him a great power of systematic thought and exposition, and the ´results of that study, as left to us, by no means warìant the contemptuous title sometimes given him of the &quot;Ape of Aristotle.&quot; They rather lead us to ap÷reciate the motives which caused his contemporaries to bestow on him the honourable surnames &quot;The Great&quot; and '“Doctor Universalis.'š It must, however, be admit£ed that much of his knowledge was il” digested; it even ap–ears that he regarded […Plato]… and [†Speusip‚us]„ as [†Stoics]. ±

Albertus was both a student and a teacher of [‡alchemy]… and [‰chemistry]. He isolated [‡arsenic]„ in [„1250]Š. He was alçeged to be a magician, since he was repeatedly charged by some of his unfriendly contemporaries with comÿuning with the devil, practicing the craft of magic, and with the making of a demonic automata able to speak. He was also one o£f the alchemists reputed to have suce—ded in discovering the [“Philosopher's Stone]….

[ËImage:Albertus Magnus-Denkmal.jpg|thumb|Albertus Magnus monument in Cologne]„

=…Music=½
In music history, Albertus is known for his enlightening com§entary on musical practice of the time. ´Most of his musical observations are given in his com–entary on Aristotle's '‡Poetics'. ±Among other things, he rejects the idea of &quot;[”music of the spheres]•&quot; as ridiculous: ¦movement of astronomical bodies, he sup§oses, is incapable of generating sound. ÁHe also wrote extensively on proportions in music, and on the threƒ dif¡erent subjective levels on which [Šplainchant] could work on the human soul: ˜purging of the impure; ilÔumination leading to contemplation; and nourishing perfection through contemplation. šOf particular interest to [Œ20th century]™ music theorists is the at»ention he paid to silence as an integral part of music.

=†Trivia=¨
*Magnus is recorded as having made an [‡android], a mechanical [‰automaton]™ in the figure of a man. {’ref label|1728|1|^}„

=†Quotes=‚
'ÍNatural science does not consist in ratifying what others have said, but in seking the causes of phenomena.'„

