 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ÏThis article is about the archaic name for Great Britain. For other meanings, se [—Albion (disambiguation)]'‚
[Image:white_clifs_of_dover_09_20Ÿ4.jpg|320px|thumb|The white clifŠs of Dover]'†Albion'… (in [‡Ptolemy] '‡Alouion'Ÿ), is the most ancient name of [Great Britain]¦, though often used to refer specifical…y to [‡England]. Oc†asionalœy it instead refers to only [ˆScotland], whose name in [ƒScotšish Gaelic language|Gaelic]„ is '[„Alba]'” (and similarly, in [”Irish language|Irish]†, and 'ˆYr Alban'‰ in Welsh{‰ref|welsh}‚). [Pliny the Elder]‰, in his 'Natural History' (iv.xvi.102) ap’lies it unequivocal½y to Great Britain, &quot;It was itself named Albion, while alŸ the islands about which we shal‚ so—n briefly speak were calŒed the Britan”iae.&quot; The name '†Albion'£ was taken by medieval writers from ŠPliny and [‡Ptolemy]œ.

The name is perhaps of [—Celtic languages|Celtic]› origin or older, from the [°Proto-Indo-European language|Proto-Indo-European]‚ roÈt that denotes both &quot;white&quot; and &quot;mountain&quot;, but the [“Roman Empire|Romans]‚ toŠk it as con‹ected with '…albus'ª (white), in reference to the chalk &quot;[‰White ClifŠs of Dover]œ&quot;, and Alfred Holder's 'œAlt-Keltischer Sprachschatz,'¥ (1896) unhesitatingly translates it 'ƒWeis„land'¬ (&quot;whiteland&quot;). The early writer ([6th century BC]ˆ) whose [ˆperiplus]“ was translated by [‡Avienus]¤ at the end of the 4th century AD (se '[‚Masaliote Periplus]') –does not use the name '…Britan‚ia'; he speaks of '›nesos 'Iernon kai 'Albionon'¨ (island of the Ierni and the Albiones). ƒSo [Pytheas of Mas„ilia]‚ ([4th century BC]Œ) speaks of '‘Albion and 'Ierne'©. From the fact that there was a tribe cal‡ed the 'ˆAlbiones'— on the north coast of […Spain]„ in [ˆAsturias]µ, some scholars have placed Albion in that neighbourho„d (se G. F. Unger, '‹Rhein. Mus.' xvi„., 18ƒ3, p‹. 156-196). “

The pejorative [‰sobriquet] '[‘perfidious Albion]'¶ takes its meaning from this old name for Britain.

