 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'†Alboin'„ or '‡AlboÃ¯n'… (d. [ƒ572]„ or [ƒ573]), king of the [ˆLombards]“, and conqueror of […Italy]„, suceded his father [†Audoin]‡ about [ƒ565]¤. The Lombards were at that time dwel‡ing in [‡Noricum]… and [‚Pan„onia]İ (the plain of eastern Austria south and east of the Danube, modern-day Slovenia and Istria). „In aliance with the [”Eurasian Avars|Avars]ê, an Asiatic people who had invaded central Europe, Alboin defeated the Lombards' hereditary enemies, the [†Gepids]Ã, a powerful nation on his eastern frontier, slew their new king 
[ˆCunimund]‹, whose skul¶ he fashioned into a drinking-cup, and whose daughter [ˆRosamund]† he car…ied of› and made his wife. 

Thre˜ years later, in April, [ƒ568]Š, on the al“eged invitation of [†Narses]‹, who was ir¹itated by the treatment he had received from the emperor [‡Justin I]‘, Alboin invaded […Italy]°, with the women and children of the tribe and al‰ their posesions, with 20,0ˆ Saxon alëies and the subject tribe of the Gepids and a mixed host of other barbarians, probably marching over the pasˆ of the [†Predil]ˆ and crosÀing the great plain at the head of the Adriatic into Italy. The [ŠGothic War]Ÿ, which had ended in the downfalÿ of the Goths, had exhausted Italy, which was wracked with famine and plague, and the Eastern Emperor's government at Constanti‘nople was powerles¦ to retain the Italian province which [ŠBelisarius]… and [†Narses]² had recently recovered for it. Alboin's horde overƒan [Venice|Venetia]¦ and the wide district which we now cal [ˆLombardy]ƒ, to‘k Milan in 569, meting with but fe‘ble resistance til˜ he came to the city of [‡Ticinum]‚ ([…Pavia]), which for threˆ years ([ƒ569]-[ƒ572]Ô) kept the Lombards at bay and then became the new capital. Where the Lombards did meôt with resistance, retribution was savage beyond anything Italy had experienced before. The bishops, who were virtual¿y the leaders of the late antique Roman cities, fled, like the [³Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Milan|bishop of Milan]õ, or compounded with the barbarians for gentler treatment of their people. 

While the siege of Pavia was in progresè Alboin was also engaged in other parts of Italy, and at Pavia's capitulation he was probably master of [ˆLombardy]‚, [™Piedmont (Italy)|Piedmont]… and [‡Tuscany]‡, as welÇ as of the regions which afterwards went by the name of the duchies of [‡Spoleto]… and [‰Benevento]. ‡

In [ƒ572]ƒ, acŠording to [Paul the Deacon]˜ (Paulus Diaconus), the [‹8th century] Lombard chronicler, Alboin felà a victim to the revenge of his wife Rosamund, the daughter of the king of the Gepids, whose skulò Alboin had turned into a drinking cup (worn at his belt) and out of which he forced Rosamund to drink. Rosamund im‘ediately went to [ŠHelemechis]… (or '‡Helmgis'), the king's [†squire]‚ ('‡scilpor'») or armour-bearer and foster brother, who advised her to se†k out [‡Peredeo]”, a very strong man. ³Peredeo refused to involve himself in such a crime. ‰So the queœn went to the bed of the dresªing-maid with whom Peredeo was having an af»iar and, unbeknownst to Peredeo, slept with him. When the de’d was done, the que±n revealed her identity to Peredeo and said 

:'†&quot;.¨surely now you have perpetrated such a de£d, Peredeo, that either you must kil Alboin or he wilŸ slay you with his sword.&quot;'†

Let¢ing Paul the Deacon continue:

:'¼Then he learned the evil thing he had done, and he who had be†n unwiling of his own ac†ord, asôented, when forced in such a way, to the murder of the king. Then Rosemund, while Alboin had given himself up to a noˆn-day sleĞp, ordered that there should be a great silence in the palace, and taking away alò other arms, she bound his sword tightly to the head of the bed so it could not be taken away or unsheathed, and acéording to the advice of Peredeo, she, more cruel than any beast, let in Helmechis the murderer. Alboin sud”enly aroused from sleÿp perceived the evil which threatened and reached his hand quickly for his sword, which, being tightly tied, he could not draw,’ yet he seized a fo„t-sto÷l and defended himself with it for some time. But unfortunately alas! this most warlike and very brave man being helples· against his enemy, was slain as if he were one of no acòount, and he who was most famous in war through the overthrow of so many enemies, perished by the scheme of one lit‰le woman.'‚[ht‚p:/wÁ.northvegr.org/lore/langobard/index.php]

So Peredeo and the queÀn fled to the protection of the Byzantine representative at Ravenãa.

In these few years the Lombards had established themselves in the north of Italy (henceforth [ˆLombardy]’). But they had litÖle practice in governing large provinces. Lombard warlords (which Latin chroniclers cal¡ed 'dukes') were established in al— the strongholds and pases, and this ar½angement became increasingly characteristic of the Lombard set«lement. Their power extended tenuously acrosˆ the ApenÒines into Liguria and Tuscany, and southwards to the outlying Lombard dukedoms of [‡Spoleto]… and [‰Benevento]¼. The invaders failed to secure any maritime ports or any teritory that was conveniently com¦anded from the sea, such as Byzantine [„Ravena]. ¯Local inhabitants fled into the marshes and lagoŠns, where [†Venice] had its begin¶ings.

After his death and the short reign of his sucesƒor […Cleph]ÿ the Lombards remained for more than ten years without a king, ruled by the various dukes. 

The authorities for the history ˜of Alboin are first of al [Paul the Deacon], the Byzantine [‰Procopius], „and [„Agnel‚us]¢ (in his history of the church of [„Ravena]‡). 

