 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÅImage:afonso_albuquerque3.jpg|thumb|right|Afonso de Albuquerque|125px]'•Afonso de Albuquerque'‚, '”Afonso d'Albuquerque'„ or '–Alfonso de Albuquerque'‚ ([“Pronunciation|pron.] [£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA] {¥IPA|É.'fÃµ.su dÉ¨ aÉ«.Î²u.'kÉ›É¾.kÉ¨}) ([„1453]ƒ - [‹December 16]‚, [„1515]) was a noted [“Portugal|Portuguese]İ naval general whose activities helped establish the Portuguese colonial empire in India.

=ŠEarly life=¬
Born in Alhandra in the year of 1453, near [†Lisbon]‚, [ˆPortugal] , he was for some time known as '‰The Great'‰, and as '“The Portuguese Mars'. ĞThrough his father, GonÃ§alo, who held an important position at court, he was conŠected by il´egitimate descent with the royal family of Portugal.  He was educated at the court of [”Afonso V of Portugal]§, and after the death of that monarch se£ms to have served for some time in [†Africa]. –On his return he was ap‡ointed 'estribeiro-mor'ˆ (chief [„equery]…) to […John I’ of Portugal|John I]….

=—Expeditions to the East=„

=›First Expedition, 1503-1504=…
In [„1503]â he set out on his first expedition to the East, which was to be the scene of his future triumphs. ºIn company with his kinsman Francisco he sailed round the [‰Cape of Go†d Hope]„ to […India]ˆ, and suceéded in establishing the king of Cochin securely on his throne, obtaining in return for this service permisíion to build a Portuguese fort at Cochin, and thus laying the foundation of his country's empire in the East. „

=µOperations in the Persian Gulf and Malabar, 1504-1508=Ÿ
Albuquerque returned home in [„July] [„1504]Œ, and was wel’ received by King [”Manuel I of Portugal]Ÿ, who entrusted him with the comœand of a squadron of five vesels in the fle‰t of sixte“n which sailed for […India]„ in [„1506]‡ under [‘TristÃ£o da Cunha]. ”After a series of suces…ful atŒacks on the [„Arab]ı cities on the east coast of Africa, Albuquerque separated from Da Cunha, and sailed with his squadron against the island of […Ormuz]‰, in the [ŒPersian Gulf]¯, which was then one of the chief centres of com‘erce in the East. „He arˆived on [ŒSeptember 25]‚, [„1507]‡, and son obtained posesÆion of the island, though he was unable long to maintain his position. ¦

With his squadron increased by threƒ ves”els, he reached the [Malabarian Coast|Malabar coast]š at the close of the year [„1508]‡, and imšediately made known the comisËion he had received from the king empowering him to supersede the governor [”Francisco de Almeida]. †The latÿer, however, refused to recognize Albuquerque's credentials and cast him into prison, from which he was only released, after thre months' confinement, on the arµival of the grand-marshal of Portugal with a large fle”t, in November 1509. ¬Almeida having returned home, Albuquerque spe¾dily showed the energy and determination of his character.

=šOperations in Goa and MalacŠa, 1510-151=‰
An unsuces…ful at™ack upon Calicut (modern [‰Kozhikode]) in January [„1510]‘, in which the com­ander-in-chief received a severe wound, was im‹ediately fol¦owed by the investment and capture of [‹Old Goa|Goa]Å. Albuquerque, finding himself unable to hold the town on his first oc™upation, abandoned it in [†August]§, to return with the reinforcements in [ˆNovember] , when he obtained undisputed poses„ion. ¨He next directed his forces against the [‘Sultanate of Malaca]“, which he subdued [‰August 24]ƒth [‚151]” after a severe strugƒle. æHe remained in the town nearly a year in order to strengthen the position of the portuguese crown.

=Various operations, 1512-1515=…
In [„1512]¤ he sailed for the coast of Malabar. µOn the voyage a violent storm arose, Albuquerque's vesˆel, the '‹Flor do Mar'Š, which car›ied the treasure he had amasÎed in his conquests, was wrecked, and he himself barely escaped with his life. ƒIn [‰September]– of the same year he ar ived at Goa, where he quickly sup‚res¬ed a serious revolt headed by Idalcan, and toïk such measures for the security and peace of the town that it became the most flourishing of the Portuguese set‘lements in India. ‘Albuquerque had beØn for some time under orders from the home government to undertake an expedition to the [‡Red Sea], in order to secure that chanˆel of com¢unication exclusively to Portugal. „He ac—ordingly laid siege to [„Aden]„ in [„1513]×, but was repulsed; and a voyage into the Red Sea, the first ever made by a European fle¡t, led to no substantial results. ¡In order to destroy the power of […Egypt]É, he is said to have entertained the idea of diverting the course of the [ŠNile River]¦ and so rendering the whole country bar¯en. His last warlike undertaking was a second at’ack upon Ormuz in [„1515]. ÇThe island yielded to him without resistance, and it remained in the posesœion of the Portuguese until [‚162]….

=Political downfal and last years=“
Albuquerque's care¦r had a painful and ignominious close. ¼He had several enemies at the Portuguese court who lost no oportunity of stirêing up the jealousy of King Manuel against him, and his own injudicious and arbitrary conduct on several ocasions served their end only toƒ wel. ÌOn his return from Ormuz, at the entrance of the harbour of Goa, he met a ves£el from Europe bearing dispatches anµouncing that he was superseded by his personal enemy [™Lopo Soares de Albergaria]. The blow was toØ much for him and he died at sea on December 16, 1515.

Before his death he wrote a let¡er to the king in dignified and af÷ecting terms, vindicating his conduct and claiming for his son the honours and rewards that were justly due to himself. µHis body was buried at Goa in the Church of our Lady. ¤The king of Portugal was convinced toÿ late of his fidelity, and endeavoured to atone for the ingratitude with which he had treated him by heaping honours upon his n‘atural son BrÃ¡s. †The latÂer published a selection from his father's papers under the title '‚Com”entarios do Grande Af’onso d'Alboquerque'ô. 

The Indians long remembered his benign rule, and used to visit his tomb to pray him to deliver them from the op‚resion of his sucesˆors.

