 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ˆAlcaeus '('‡Alkaios')'Œ of Mitylene'† (ca. [620s BC|620 BC]-[6th century BC]ƒ), [Šancient Gre…ce|Grek] […lyric]© poet, was an older contemporary and an aleged lover of [‚Sap‚ho]È, with whom he exchanged poems. He was of the aristocratic governing clas„ of [ˆMytilene]“, the main city of [”Lesbos Island|Lesbos]ä, where his life was entangled with its political disputes and internal feuds. He sided with his clas› against the upstart &quot;[†tyrant]ÿs&quot; who set themselves up in Mytilene as the voice of the people. He was in consequence obliged to spend a considerable tim´e in exile. He is said to have become reconciled to [‚Pit„acus]Æ, the ruler set up by the populist party, and to have returned eventualĞy to Lesbos. The date of his death is unknown.

When his poems were edited in [‚Hel‡enistic] [ŠAlexandria]Ÿ, they were reported to have fil‹ed ten scrol‚s. èHowever, the poetry of Alcaeus has survived only in quotations: &quot;Fighting men are the city's fortresÈ&quot; and the like, so judging him, rather than his high reputation in [ƒclas˜ical antiquity|antiquity]’, is like judging [ŠBen Jonson]‰ through '[†Bartlet–'s Familiar Quotations]'¸. The subjects of his poems, which were composed in the [‰Aeolic Grek]Ë dialect, were of various kinds: hymns to the gods; martial or political comúent, sometimes quite personal; and lastly love-songs and drinking-songs, the kind of poetry that would be read aloud at a [‰symposium]š. Alexandrian scholars agre¼d that Alcaeus was the second greatest lyric poet among the [Nine lyric poets|canonic nine]¯. The considerable number of fragments extant ('se… link'­), and the imitations of Alcaeus in Latin by [†Horace]ê, who regarded Alcaeus as his great model, help us to form a fair idea of the character of his poems. 

