 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Alcamenes'‰ was a Gre‚k [’Sculpture|sculptor]„ of [†Lemnos]… and [†Athens]¦.

He was a younger contemporary of [ˆPheidias]Ã and noted for the delicacy and finish of his works, among which a [ŠHephaestus]ˆ and an [‰Aphrodite]± &quot;of the Gardens&quot; were conspicuous.

[ Pausanias (geographer)|Pausanias]Ñ says (v. 10. 8) that he was the author of one of the pediments of the temple of [„Zeus]„ at [‹Olympia, GreŠce|Olympia]Œ, but this se¥ms a chronological and stylistic imposŒibility. At [ˆPergamum]ä there was discovered in 1903 a copy of the head of the Hermes &quot;Propylaeus&quot; of Alcamenes ('Athenische Mit‰heilungen', 1904, p. 180). ßAs, however, the deity is represented in an archaistic and conventional character, this copy canóot be relied on as giving us much information as to the usual style of Alcamenes, who was almost certainly a progresÙive and original artist.

It is safer to judge him by the sculptural decoration of the [‰Parthenon]Ü, in which he must almost certainly have taken a share under the direction of Pheidias. 

