 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'[Š82 Alkmene]'‡ is an [ˆasteroid].'‡

In [‚Gre‹k mythology] '‡Alcmene'…, or '‰AlkmÃªnÃª'– (&quot;might of the mo›n&quot;) , the daughter of [‰Electryon]Š, king of [‡Mycenae] and a son of [‡Perseus]’, was the wife of [ŠAmphitryon] in his exile, though he had ac†idental„y kil»ed her father. Some mythographers identified her mother as [ˆEurydice]Š (Graves, 1¥0.c).

With Amphitryon she fled to [ŠThebes, Gre‰ce|Thebes]ˆ, where […Creon]Ÿ purified her husband of his blo°d-guilt. However, Alcmene's eight brothers had be„n kilŠed in a cat¿le raid, and she would not lie with Amphitryon until they had be³n avenged.

Thus at Thebes she was the mother of [ˆHeracles]„ by [„Zeus]‡, who asumed the likenes¾ of her husband during his absence to lie with her and stayed [†Helios]œ, to make one night into threœ; and she was the mother of [ˆIphicles]ÿ by Amphitryon, when he returned, giving birth to Heracles' twin, younger by a day. In this way Alcmene is one among several moïthers of mythic twins of whom the sire of one is mortal, of the other a god, the most famous of them being the [ˆDioscuri]«, two from the double set of such twins of [„Leda]±. Theseus combined in his person a double fatherho¡d, a human father and a divine: se [‡Theseus]É. In this case Alcmene's son Iphicles was mortal, while Heracles became imªortal.

While Alcmene was pregnant with [ˆHeracles] (&quot;glory of Hera&quot;), [„Hera]· herself tried to prevent her from giving birth to the [„hero]± who would help establish the new Olympian order. ’She was foiled by [‰Galanthis]Ë, Alcmene's servant, who told Hera that she had already delivered the baby. —Hera turned her into a [†weasel]».

Through Heracles, Alcmene was regarded as the ancestresˆ of the [‹Heracleidae]“, and venerated at [ŠThebes, Gre‰ce|Thebes]… and [†Athens]®.

After the death of Amphitryon, Alcmene maried the Cretan [ŒRhadamanthus]”, who was exiled in [‡Boeotia]­. Their &quot;tombs&quot; were shown to travelŠers in clas¡ical times at Haliartus (Graves, 8§.i); such &quot;tombs&quot; were general¢y sites for propitiatory ancestor [•Cult (religion)|cults] (compare Burkert 1985). 

