 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Alcidamas', of Elaea, in [†Aeolis]‚, [Šancient Gre…ce|Grek] [‡sophist]… and [ˆrhetoric]—ian, flourished in the [4th century BC]œ.

He was the pupil and suces†or of [‡Gorgias] and taught at [†Athens]• at the same time as [‰Isocrates]“, whose rival and op“onent he was. We poses¢ two declamations under his name: 'Peri Sofiston'£, directed against Isocrates and set¯ing forth the superiority of extempore over writ…en spe·ches (a more recently discovered fragment of another spe±ch against Isocrates is probably of later date); 'ƒOdusƒeus'‹, in which [ƒOdysƒeus]‚ ac…uses [‰Palamedes]¢ of treachery during the siege of [„Troy] (this is generalœy considered spurious).

AcÍording to Alcidamas, the highest aim of the orator was the power of speaking '‰extempore'Ÿ on every conceivable subject. [‰Aristotle]‚ ('…Rhet.' iÿ. 3) criticizes his writings as characterized by pomposity of style and an extravagant use of poetical epithets and compounds aÙnd far-fetched metaphors.

Of other works only fragments and the titles have survived: '‚Mes‡eniakos'“, advocating the fredom of the Mes°enians and containing the sentiment that &quot;al‘ are by nature fre…&quot;ƒ a 'Eulogy of Death'±, in consideration of the wide extent of human sufŠerings; a '†Techne'‘ or instruction-bo¢k in the art 
of rhetoric; and a 'Fusikos lolos'. Lastly, his 'ˆMouseion'® (a word of doubtful meaning) contained the nar™ative of the contest betwe‚n […Homer]… and [†Hesiod]ª, two fragments of which are found in the '—Agon Omerou kai Esiodou'“, the work of a gram•arian in the time of [‡Hadrian]. ‚A [‹3rd century] [‡papyrus]‚ ([‚Wil†iam Mat£hew Flinders Petrie|Flinders Petrie]‚, '†Papyri'…, ed. [’John Pentland Mahaf†y|Mahafy]Œ, 1891, pl. xèv.) probably contains the actual remains of a description by Alcidamas. 

