 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŠAldine Pres'‰ was the [ˆprinting]‚ ofice started by [Aldus Manutius]„ in [„1494]„ in [†Venice]“, from which were is­ued the celebrated Aldine editions of the clas ics of that time. The Aldine Pres is famous in the history of [Štypography]¾, among other things, for the introduction of italics. The pres¤ was continued after Aldus death in [„1515]± by his wife and her father until his son Paolo ([„1512]-[„1574]ƒ) to¿k over. His grandson Aldo then ran the firm until his death in [„1597]….

=‰Initial Inˆovations=‰
The pres® was started by Aldus based on his love of clasÆics, and at first printed new copies of Plato, Aristotle, and other Grek and Latin clas©ics. He also printed dictionaries and gram¢ars to help people interpret the bo¡ks. Since most bibliophiles and bo„k col¡ectors come from academic and clas«ical backgrounds, his first editions are colÎectors items. His contributions are also respected in the development of a smal±er type than others in use. His contemporaries cal†ed it '‹Aldine Type'; today we cal„ it '‡italics'˜.

The goal of the pres› was to create plentiful, af‰ordable bo ks so that everyone could have acesœ to literature. When the pres expanded to curent titles, they wrote some bo´ks themselves and employed other writers, including [‡Erasmus]›. As this expansion into cur°ent languages (mainly Italian and French) and curent topics continued, the pres‚ toÿk on another role and made perhaps even more important contributions. Their logo of the anchor and dolphin is represented today½ in the symbols and names used by some modern publishers.

=—The Literacy Revolution=‚
[„Johan” Gutenberg|Gutenberg]« gets credit for inventing the printing pres with some justification, but [”Aldus Manutius|Aldus]ÿ and his sons created the revolution. Gutenberg produced some beautiful volumes. They were priced so that a man of moderate wea‘lth could buy a bok. They were stilÊ large, heavy voumes and expensive. A church that had a Bible would typical°y chain it to a reading stand. Aldus created smal„er bo†ks (calƒed '†octavo'˜) that could fit in a sad¸lebag and that the average merchant or craftsman could aford.

With boĞks readily available, it was now worthwhile to learn to read. In dealing with cur±ent topics, the second or third edition is the bet„er bo¼k. When Paolo or Aldo hired a great shipbuilder to write a bo¡k on shipbuilding, he described alÁ the best techniques he was aware of. Other builders bought the boÓk and then wrote to protest that their technique for a particular technology was betâer. Many of these improvements were then incorporated in the later editions. Before the Aldine Pres‰, a new inôovation might take a hundred years to get from Italy to the Netherlands. Afterward, information started to move in al“ directions, and comÿunication times were reduced to five or six years. This is an important step in the modernization of Europe, and can even be vi›ewed as a precursor of the [ˆInternet]….

