 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Ÿotheruses4|Aldred the English ec¤lesiastic|the Anglo-Saxon leader|Hwice}„

'†Aldred'…, or '‡Ealdred'… (d. [1Š September],[„1069]ƒ), [England|English]‚ ec’lesiastic, became [ab‚ot]„ of [‰Tavistock]‡ about [„1027]…, in [‚104]Š was made [“bishop of Worcester]‰, and in [„1060]‚, [’archbishop of York]­.

He had considerable influence over King [Edward the Confes‚or]¾, and as his interests were secular rather than religious he to—k a prominent part in af–airs of state, and in [„1046] led 
an unsuces›ful expedition against the [‹Wales|Welsh]…. In [„1050]ª he was largely instrumental in restoring […Sweyn], the son of [‹Earl Godwin]², to his earldom, and about the same time went to [„Rome]˜ 
&quot;on the king's er‘and.&quot;

In [„1054]œ he was sent to the emperor [†Henry I›, Holy Roman Emperor|Henry I]ô to obtain that monarch's influence in securing the return to England of Edward, son of Edmund Ironside, who was in [‡Hungary]‹ with King [œAndrew I of Hungary|Andrew I]Œ. In this mision he was sucesæful and obtained some insight into the working of the German church during a stay of a year with Herman I, archbishop of [‡Cologne]™.

After his return to [‡England]… he to‘k charge of the se…s of [ˆHereford]… and [ˆRamsbury], although not ap£ointed to these bishoprics; and in [„1058]– made a pilgrimage to [‰Jerusalem]ÿ, being the first English bishop to take this journey.

Having previously given up Hereford and Ramsbury, Aldred was elected archbishop of [„York]„ in [„1060]‰, and in [„1061]ˆ he proce‡ded to [„Rome] to receive the [‚palƒium]. ˆOn his arÃival there, however, various charges were brought against him by a […synod]†, and [Pope Nicholas I]ÿ not only refused his request but degraded him from the episcopate. The sentence was, however, subsequently reversed, and Aldre‘d received the pal¿ium and was restored to his former station.

It is stated by [•Florence of Worcester]š that Aldred crowned King [˜Harold Godwinson|Harold I]„ in [‚106], although the [Normans|Norman]• authorities mention [‡Stigand]‰ as the ofœiciating prelate. After the [‚batle of Hastings]¿, Aldred joined the party who sought to bestow the throne upon [¢Edgar Atheling|Edgar the Ã†theling]’, but when these ef†orts apŒeared hopeles he was among those who submit†ed to [‚Wil“iam I of England|Wil‘iam the Conqueror]„ at [ŒBerkhamstead]Ï.

Selected to crown the new king he performed the ceremony on Christmas Day [‚106]‰, and in [„1068]• performed the same of×ice at the coronation of Matilda, the Conqueror's wife. But though often at court, he seŒms to have ben no sympathiser with Norman op‚res¹ion, and is even said to have 
bearded the king himself. —He died at York on the [ŠSeptember 1] [„1069]ÿ and was buried in his own cathedral.

Aldred did much for the restoration of discipline in the monasteries and churches unde³r his authority, and was liberal in his gifts for ecÄlesiastical purposes. He built the monastic 
church of St Peter at [ŠGloucester]º, and rebuilt a large part of that of St John at Beverley. ŞAt his instigation, Folcard, a monk of Canterbury, wrote the Life of St John of Beverley. 

