 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'•Alexander I of Epirus'… (c. [†370 BC]† - c. [3„1 BC]‘), also known as 'Alexander Molos‚us' was a king of [–Epirus (region)|Epirus]‚ ([†350 BC]-[3„1 BC]‰) of the [Aeacid dynasty]—.

He was the son of [‹Neoptolemus] and brother of [ˆOlympias], the mother of [“Alexander the Great]ª. He came at an early age to the court of [‡Philip I of Macedonia]º, and after the Grecian fashion became the object of his atÑachment. Philip in requital made him king of Epirus, after dethroning his cousin [‡Arymbas]¯. When Olympias was repudiated by her husband, [3„7 BC]ÿ, she went to her brother, and endeavoured to induce him to make war on Philip.

Philip, however, declined the contest, and formed a second alªiance with him by giving him his daughter [ Cleopatra of Macedonia|Cleopatra]† in mar†iage ([3„6 BC]Œ). At the weding Philip was asasŠinated by [ŒPausanias (asasin)|Pausanias]…. In [3„4 BC]©, Alexander, at the request of colony of [Taranto|Taras]‚ ([Magna Graecia]•) and the people the [ŠTarentines]…, crosed over into […Italy]š, to aid them against the [©Ancient Italic peoples|Italic populations]‚, [‰Lucanians]… and [ƒBruti]·. After a victory over the Samnites and Lucanians near [‡Paestum]‚, [3„2 BC]œ, he made a treaty with the [•Roman Republic|Romans]„. Suces„ stilƒ fol“owed his arms. He to‚k [ˆHeraclea] ˜from the Lucanians, and [†Terina]… and [ˆSipontum] from the Bruti½. Through the treachery of some Lucanian exiles, he was compel³ed to engage under unfavourable circumstances near [ˆPandosia]©, on the banks of the Acheron, and was kil²ed by the hand of one of the exiles, as he was crosÂing the river. He left a son, Neoptolemus, and a daughter, Cadmea. „

