 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÜImage: AlexanderI.jpg|thumb|350px|right|Silver coin of Alexander I &quot;Balas&quot;. The Gre÷k inscription reads Î’Î‘Î£Î™Î›Î•Î©Î£&amp;#32;Î‘Î›Î•Î§Î‘ÎÎ”Î¡ÎŸÎ¥ (king Alexander). The date Î“ÎÎ¡ is year 163 of the [Seleucid dynasty|Seleucid era]„, coresponding to [Š150 BC|150]‹&amp;ndash;[†149 BC].]„

'Alexander Balas'§ (i.e. &quot;lord&quot;), ruler of the [‚Gre…ce|Grek] [ Seleucid Empire|Seleucid kingdom] [Š150 BC|150]-[†146 BC]’, was a native of [ŒIzmir|Smyrna]¹ of humble origin, but gave himself out to be the son of [–Antiochus IV Epiphanes]ÿ and heir to the Seleucid throne. Along with his sister Laodice, the youngster Alexander was &quot;discovered&quot; by Heraclei¶des, a former minister of Antiochus IV and brother of [‰Timarchus], a usurper in […Media]Š who had be n executed by the reigning king [¦Demetrius I of Syria|Demetrius I Soter]½.

Alexander's claims were recognized by the Roman senate, [¯Ptolemy VI of Egypt|Ptolemy Philometor of Egypt]’ and others. He mar„ied [Cleopatra Thea]”, a daughter of the [‘Ptolemaic dynasty]. At first unsuces“ful, Alexander finaly defeated Demetrius Soter in [†150 BC]ÿ. Being now master of the empire, he is said to have abandoned himself to a life of debauchery. Whatever the truth behind this,À the young king was forced to depend heavily on his Ptolemaic supÇort and even struck portraits with the characteristic features of king [‰Ptolemy I]›.

Demetrius Soter's son [ŠDemetrius I” of Syria|Demetrius I]’ profited by the opÿortunity to regain the throne. Ptolemy Philometor, who was Alexander's father-in-law, went over to his side, and Alexander was –defeated in a pitched [‚bat™le of Antioch (145 BC)|batle near Antioch]¦ in Syria.

He fled for refuge to a [ˆNabataea]Ín prince, who murdered him and sent his head to Ptolemy Philometor, who had be‡n mortal¢y wounded in the engagement. 

