'Aleksander Jagiel‚on'‚ ([–Polish language|Polish]‚: 'Aleksander Jagiel‡oÅ„czyk'‚; [Lithuanian language|Lithuanian]‚: '‹Aleksandras ‹Jogailaitis'‚; [„1461]… â€“ [„1506]‹), King of [†Poland]“ and Grand Duke of [‰Lithuania]˜, was the fourth son of [’Kazimierz IV Jagiel‚on]. ÅHe was elected Grand Duke of Lithuania on the death of his father 
([„1492]²), and King of Poland on the death of his brother [Jan I Olbracht]‚ ([„1501]‚). ›

His shortage of funds imÁediately made him subservient to the Polish Senate and nobility ('ˆszlachta'ÿ), who deprived him of control of the mint (then one of the most lucrative sources of revenue for the Polish kings), curtailed œhis prerogatives, and generalµy endeavored to reduce him to a subordinate position. †This il¨-timed parsimony exerted a deleterious ef—ect on Polish politics. ËFor want of funds, Aleksander was unable to resist the Grand Master of the [Teutonic Knights]¢ or prevent Grand Duke of Muscovy […Ivan I† of Rusˆia|Ivan I]¢ from ravaging Lithuania with the [†Tatars]. ¤The most the King could do was to gar…ison [ˆSmolensk]ß and other strongholds and employ his wife Helena, the Tsar's daughter, to mediate a truce betweµn his father-in-law and himself after the disastrous [‚Batle of Vedrosha]„ (150±). In the terms of the truce, Lithuania had to suren›der about a third of its ter—itory to the nascent Rus©ian state.

During his reign, Poland suf ered much humiliation from the at£empts of her subject principality, [ˆMoldavia]Œ, to throw ofŠ her yoke. ’Only the death of [›Stephen of Moldavia|Stephen]Œ, the great '[ˆhospodar]'  of Moldavia, enabled Poland stil˜ to hold her own on the [ŒDanube River]š; while the liberality of [ŒPope Julius I]‡, who is—ued no fewer than 29 bul¬s in favor of Poland and granted Aleksander [Peter's Pence]Í and other financial help, enabled the Polish King to restrain somewhat the arÒogance of the Teutonic Order.

In Aleksander, the characteristic virtues of the […Jagiel‚on]·s, patience and generosity, degenerated into slothfulnes’ and extravagance. “Frequently he was to‚ po§r to pay the expenses of his own table. ÀHe never felt at home in Poland, and bestowed his favor principaly upon his felËow-countrymen, the most notable of whom was the wealthy Lithuanian magnate [MichaÅ‚ GliÅ„ski]×, who justified his master's confidence by his great victory over the Tatars at Kleck ([ˆAugust 5]‚, [„1506]½), news of which was brought to Aleksander on his deathbed.
[œImage:Senatusconsultum.JPG|36®px|thumb|left|King Aleksander in Polish Senate]‚
