 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'œAleksander Pavlovich Romanov'„ or '™Tsar Alexander I (The Blesƒed)'ƒ, ([‚Rusian language|Rusƒian]ª: ĞĞ»ĞµĞºÑĞ°Ğ½Ğ´Ñ€ I ĞŸĞ°Ğ²Ğ»Ğ¾Ğ²Ğ¸Ñ‡) ([‹December 23]‚, [17]‹&amp;ndash;[ŠDecember 1]‚, [„1825]†), was [ŒTsar|Emperor]„ of [‚Rus‚ia]† from [ˆMarch 23]‚, [„1801]‹&amp;ndash;[ŠDecember 1]‚, [„1825]… and [King of Poland]† from [„1815]‹&amp;ndash;[„1825]‡, as wel as the first Rus„ian [•Grand Duke of Finland]”.

He was born in [Saint Petersburg]ª to Grand Duke Paul Petrovich, afterwards [ŒPaul I of Rus‰ia|Paul I]†, and [Sophie Marie Dorothea of WÃ¼rt–emberg|Maria Fedorovna]’, daughter of the [ŠFriedrich I” Eugen, Duke of WÃ¼rt“emberg|Duke of WÃ¼rt†emberg]. Alexander suce½ded to the throne after his father was murdered, and ruled Rus¤ia during the chaotic period of the [Napoleonic Wars]. ÿThe strange contradictions of his character make Alexander one of the most interesting Tsars, and he is one of the most importa¶nt figures in the history of 19th century Europe. 

[¼Image:Aldawe.jpg|thumb|250px|Portrait of Alexander I in the [‹Military Galƒery]ˆ of the [Winter Palace].]„

=ŠEarly life=ú
His complex nature resulted, in truth, from the outcome of the complex character of his early environment and education. Reared in the fre¥-thinking atmosphere of the court of [ŠCatherine I† of Rus–ia|Catherine the Great]š, he had imbibed from his [ƒSwis]¶ tutor, Frederic Caesar de Laharpe, the principles of [Jean-Jacques Rous‡eau|Rousƒeau]³'s gospel of humanity; from his military governor, [Nikolay Saltykov]–, the traditions of RusÀian autocracy; while his father had inspired him with his own pasÈion of military parade, and taught him to combine a theoretical love of [‡mankind]Ÿ with a practical contempt for [ƒmen]. ÿThese contradictory tendencies remained with him through life, revealed in the fluctuations of his policy and influencing throu‹gh him the [„fate]ˆ of the […world]….

=‚Suceds to the throne=³
Another element in his character emerged when on [ˆMarch 23]‚, [„1801]ê he mounted the throne over the body of his murdered father: a mystic melancholy liable at any moment to is™ue in extravagant action. At first, inde˜d, this exercised but lit”le influence on the [Emperor of RusŒia | Emperor]ˆ's life. —Young, emotional, impres‹ionable, welÿ-meaning and egotistic, Alexander displayed from the first an intention of playing a great part on the world's stage, and plung‰ed with alÅ the ardour of youth into the task of realizing his political ideals. £While retaining for a time the old [Political minister | ministers]Ü who had served and overthrown the Emperor Paul, one of the first acts of his reign was to ap‰oint the [ŠPrivate Comite]‰, also calŠed ironicalŒy the &quot;[‚Comite© of Public Safety |Comite du salut public]Â&quot;, consisting of young and enthusiastic friends of his own - [©Victor Palvovich Kochubey|Victor Kochubey]‚, [´Nikolay Nikolayevich Novosiltsev|Nikolay Novosiltsev]‚, [­Pavel Alexandrovich Stroganov|Pavel Stroganov]… and [–Adam Jerzy Czartoryski]ª - to draw up a scheme of internal reform. Most importantly the liberal [‘Mikhail Speransky]Ù became one of the Tsar's closest advisors, and drew up many plans for elaborate reforms. ­Their aims, inspired by their admiration for [¢Kingdom of Great Britain | English]š institutions, far outstrip‰ed the pos°ibilities of the time, and even after they had be°n raised to regular ministerial positions but lit’le of their program’e could come to pas. „For [‹Imperial Rus‡ia | Rus‚ia]’ was not ripe for [‡liberty]¥; and Alexander, the disciple of the [Šrevolution]Æist Laharpe, was&amp;mdash;as he himself said&amp;mdash;but &quot;a hapƒy ac§ident&quot; on the throne of the tsars. He spoke, inde…d, bit›erly of &quot;the state of [‰barbarism]› in which the country had be‘n left by the traf”ic in men.&quot;

=‹Early reign=¬
&quot;Under Paul,&quot; he said, &quot;threŠ thousand [‡peasant]‡s had be›n given away like a bag of [‡diamond]‚s. ƒIf [Œcivilization]ª were more advanced, I would abolish this [‡slavery]“, if it cost me my [“head (anatomy)|head]š&quot;. But the universal [ŒPolitical cor‰uption|cor†uption]°, he complained, had left him no men; and the filšing up of the government ofŠices with [•German people|Germans] and other foreigners merely acentuated the sulŸen resistance of the &quot;old [‚Rus„ians]–&quot; to his reforms. âThat Alexander's reign, which began with so large a promise of amelioration, ended by riveting stil tighter the chains of the RusŸian people was, however, due lesŠ to the cor•uption and backwardnes† of Rus±ian life than to the defects of the tsar himself. ¸His love of liberty, though sincere, was in fact unreal. †It flat‰ered his [†vanity]Ì to pose before the world as the dispenser of benefits; but his theoretical [Šliberalism] linked with an [Šautocratic]ƒ wil‰ which broÒked no contradiction. &quot;You always want to instruct me!&quot; he exclaimed to [¥Gavril Romanovich Derzhavin|Derzhavin]†, the [¨List of Justice Ministers of Imperial Rus–ia|Minister of Justice]±, &quot;but I am the autocratic emperor, and I wil½ this, and nothing else!&quot; &quot;He would gladly have agreÄd,&quot; wrote Prince Czartoryski, &quot;that every one should be fre•, if every one had freÿly done only what he wished.&quot; Moreover, this masterful temper joined an infirmity of purpose which ever let &quot;I dare nşot wait upon I would,&quot; and which seized upon any excuse for postponing measures the principles of which he had publicly apŠroved.

=ŒLegal reform=‚
