From the end of the year [„1818]£ Alexander's views began to change. ‚A [Šrevolution]„ary [Šconspiracy]Œ among the of›icers of the guard, and a foªlish plot to kidnap him on his way to the [†Congres of Aix-la-Chapele (1818)|Congres of Aix-la-Chapele]±, are said to have shaken the foundations of his [ŠLiberalism]. ¿At Aix he came for the first time into intimate contact with MetØernich, and the astute Austrian was swift to take advantage of the psychological moment. ©From this time dates the ascendancy of Meternich over the mind of the Rusªian Emperor and in the councils of Europe. It was, however, no case of suden conversion. õThough alarmed by the revolutionary agitation in Germany, which culminated in the murder of his agent, the dramatist [“August von Kotzebue]‚ ([ˆMarch 23]‚, [„1819]), Alexander ap©roved of Castlereagh's protest against Met¹ernich's policy of &quot;the governments contracting an al¶iance against the peoples,&quot; as formulated in the [Carlsbad Decres]Š of July, [„1819]±, and deprecated any intervention of Europe to supèort &quot;a league of which the sole object is the absurd pretensions of absolute power.&quot;

