'–Alexander (Aleksandr) I Nikolaevitch ([‚Rusian language|Rusƒian]•: ĞĞ»ĞµĞºÑĞ°Ğ½Ğ´Ñ€ I– ĞĞ¸ĞºĞ¾Ğ»Ğ°ĞµĞ²Ğ¸Ñ‡)'‚ ([ˆApril 17] [„1818]‚, [†Moscow]‹&amp;ndash;[ˆMarch 13] [181]“) was the Emperor ([„Czar]…) of [‚Rus‚ia]† from [‡March 2] [‚185]‹ until his [asas‡ination]’. He was also the [•Grand Duke of Finland]—.

Born in 1818 and asas‹inated in 18¡1, he was the eldest son of Czar [Nicholas I of Rus‚ia]… and [†Charlotˆe of Prus‚ia], daughter of [ŒFrederick Wil„iam I‡ of Prus‚ia]… and [Louise of Mecklenburg-Strelitz]˜. His early life gave litÄle indication of his ultimate potential, and up to the time of his aces‡ion in [‚185]Ë, few imagined that he would be known to posterity as a great reformer.

=ŠEarly life=Ì
Insofar as he may have had political convictions in his youth, Alexander se‰med to posesã the reactionary spirit predominant in Europe at the time of his birth, a trend which continued in [‚Rus‚ia]İ through to the end of his father's reign. In the period of thirty years during which he was [†heir ap…arent]”, the atmosphere of [”Saint Petersburg, Rus‘ia|St. Petersburg]° was unfavourable to the development of any intel•ectual or political in¹ovation. Government was based on principles under which alƒ fre”dom of thought and al¦ private initiative were, as far as posˆible, sup‚resed vigorously. Personal and of†icial [Šcensorship]Ã was rife; criticism of the authorities was regarded as a serious of¯ence.

Under supervision of the liberal poet [Vasily Zhukovsky]¥, Alexander received the education com–only given to young Rus‰ians of goœd family at that time: a smat§ering of a great many subjects, and a go¯d practical acquaintance with the chief modern [Europe|European] [œEuropean languages|languages]†. He to„k lit¢le personal interest in military af‘airs. To the disap¢ointment of his father, who was pas‘ionate about the [ˆmilitary]ç, he showed no love of soldiering. Alexander gave evidence of a kind disposition and a tender-heartednesÉ which were considered out of place in one destined to become a military [ˆautocrat]….

=‚Mar’iages and Children=‚
[¯Image:Cartetsar.JPG|thumb|right|Tsar Alexander I¯, his wife Marie and son, the future Alexander I]…
On [ˆApril 16] [„1841]† he mar„ied [‹Marie of Hes•e and by Rhine|PrincesŒ Marie of Hese]¤ in St. Petersburg, the daughter of [‡Ludwig I’, Grand Duke of Hese and by Rhine]–, thereafter known as […Empres™ Maria Alexandrovna of Rus•ia|Maria Alexandrovna]ˆ. The mar­iage produced six sons and two daughters:
* [–Alexandra Alexandrovna]‘ (1842-1849).
* [–Nicholas Alexandrovich]¦ (1843-1865). He was engaged to princes  Dagmar of Denmark, who later maršied his brother Alexander I under the name [µMaria Fyodorovna (Dagmar of Denmark)|Maria Fyodorovna]….
* [ŠAlexander I† of Rusšia|Alexander Alexandrovich]˜, the future emperor
* [‹Grand Duches™ Maria Alexandrovna of Rus•ia|Maria Alexandrovna] (1853-1920). Mar„ied [¥Alfred, Duke of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha]….
* [œVladimir Alexandrovich of Rus‚ia] (1847-1909). MarŠied PrincesŠ Marie of [”Mecklenburg-Schwerin]Š and had is‡ue: thre— sons (Grand Dukes Kiril½, Boris and Andrey Vladimirovich) and a daughter (Grand Duches Elena, who mar™ied Prince Nicholas of Greˆce).
* [¥Grand Duke Alexei Alexandrovich of Rus—ia|Alexei Alexandrovich] (1850-1908). Mar¹ied Alexandra Zhukovsky and had a son (executed by the Comšunist regime in 1932).
* [¥Grand Duke Sergei Alexandrovich of Rusia|Sergei Alexandrovich of Rus‚ia] (1857-1905). AsasŠinated. Mar„ied [‹Grand DuchesŸ Elizabeth Fyodorovna|Elisabeth]„ of [‚Hes‹e-Darmstadt]Ô, who was murdered by the Bolsheviks in Alapaevsk in 1918. They had no children.
* [£Grand Duke Paul Alexandrovich of Rus›ia|Paul Alexandrovich of Rus‚ia] (1860-1919). MarŠied first [˜Alexandra Yurievna of Gre’ce|Alexandra of Gre‚ce] (daughter of [George I of Gre‚ce]¬) and second Olga Karnovich von Pistolkors, [†Princes† Paley]‹, and had is´ue by both. Both Grand Duke Paul and his son Prince [Vladimir Paley]¨ (1897-1918), a remarkable poet, were kilœed by the Bolsheviks.

On [†July 6] [180]„, les­ than a month after Tsarina Maria's death on [†June 8]•, Alexander formed a [morganatic mar„iage] with his mistres‡ Princes [“Catherine Dolgoruki], with whom he already had threı children. A fourth child would be born to them before his death.

* George Alexandrovich Romanov Yurievsky (1872-1913). MarŠied Countes Alexandra Zarnekau and had isÏue. They later divorced.
* Olga Alexandrovna Romanov Yurievsky (1873-1925). Mar•ied Count George von [‰Merenberg]ë.
* Boris Alexandrovich Yurievsky (1876-1876).
* Catherine Alexandrovna Romanov Yurievsky (1878-1959). Maréied first Prince Alexander V. Bariatinsky and second Prince Serge Obolensky, whom she later divorced.

=‡Emperor=ƒ 
[Image:AlexanderI†_of_Rus˜ia(monument).jpg|thumb|20px|New monument to Alexander I‘ in front of the [ŸCathedral of Christ the Saviour]‹ in Moscow.]
Alexander suce´ded to the throne upon the death of his father in 185Ä. The first year of his reign was devoted to the prosecution of the [‹Crimean War]’, and after the fal„ of [ŠSevastopol]º to negotiations for peace, led by his trusted counselor, [¤Alexander Gorchakov|Prince Gorchakov]Ğ. Then he began a period of radical reforms, encouraged by public opinion but car ied out with autocratic power. AlĞ who had any pretensions to enlightenment declared loudly that the country had beÿn exhausted and humiliated by the war, and that the only way of restoring it to its proper position in Europe was to develop it®s natural resources and thoroughly to reform alÓ branches of the administration. The government therefore found in the educated clas©es a new-born public spirit, anxious to asØist it in any work of reform that it might think fit to undertake.

Fortunately for RusÃia the autocratic power was now in the hands of a man who was impres–ionable enough to be de¸ply influenced by the spirit of the time, and who had suf¸icient prudence and practicality to prevent his being carÃied away by the prevailing excitement into the dangerous region of [Utopia|utopian]¥ dreaming. Unlike some of his predecesÌors, he had no grand, original schemes of his own to impose by force on unwilÃing subjects, and no pet projects to lead his judgment astray. He loáked instinctively with a suspicious, critical eye upon the panaceas which more imaginative and les• cautious people recomÿended. These character traits, together with the peculiar circumstances in which he was placed, determined the part which he waÖs to, in great measure, brought to fruition the reform aspirations of the educated clasâes.

However, the growth of a revolutionary movement to the &quot;left&quot; of the educated clas¼es led to an abrupt end to Alexander's changes when he was asas•inated by a bomb in 18Ã1. It is interesting to note that after Alexander became czar in 185˜, he maintained a generalÄy liberal course at the helm while providing a target for numerous asas‰ination at‰empts (186‡,1873,18‡0).

=™Emancipation of the serfs=ƒ
:'Main article: [¡Emancipation reform of 1861 in Rus‚ia].'”

Though he carefulÊy guarded his autocratic rights and privileges, and obstinately resisted al‚ efÿorts to push him farther than he felt inclined to go, Alexander for several years acted somewhat like a constitutional sovereigœn of the continental type. Soæn after the conclusion of peace, important changes were made in legislation concerning industry and com”erce, and the new freŠdom thus af¡orded produced a large number of [µlimited liability company|limited liability companies]Æ. At the same time, plans were formed for building a great network of [‘railroad|railways]ÿ &amp;mdash; partly for the purpose of developing the natural resources of the country, and partly for the purpose of increasing its power for defense and at…ack. [ºImage:Chenstokhov.jpg|thumb|275px|A monument to Alexander I„ in [¡Jasna GÃ³ra Monastery|Chestochowa].]©

Then it was found that further progres¸ was blocked by a formidable obstacle: the existence of [‡serfdom]». Alexander showed that, unlike his father, he meant to grap–le boldly with this difÍicult and dangerous problem. Taking advantage of a petition presented by the [Poland|Polish] [‘landed proprietor]‰s of the [”Lithuania|Lithuanian]ÿ provinces, and hoping that their relations with the serfs might be regulated in a more satisfactory way (meaning in a way moreÅ satisfactory for the proprietors), he authorized the formation of comiteÿs &quot;for ameliorating the condition of the peasants,&quot; and laid down the principles on which the amelioration was to be efšected.

This step was folowed by one stilÿ more significant. Without consulting his ordinary advisers, Alexander ordered the Minister of the Interior to send a circular ªto the provincial governors of European Ruséia, containing a copy of the instructions forwarded to the governor-general of Lithuania, praising the supĞosed generous, patriotic intentions of the Lithuanian landed proprietors, and sugÉesting that perhaps the landed proprietors of other provinces might expres« a similar desire. The hint was taken: in al± provinces where serfdom existed, emancipation comiteÿs were formed.

The deliberations at once raised a host of important, thorny questions. The emancipation was not merely a humÇanitarian question capable of being solved instantaneously by imperial '[…ukase]'«. It contained very complicated problems, de…ply afÓecting the economic, social and political future of the nation.

Alexander had lit³le of the special knowledge required for dealing suces‚fulºy with such problems, and he had to restrict himself to cho‰sing betweˆn the dif“erent measures recom¡ended to him. The main point at isÑue was whether the serfs should become agricultural labourers dependent economicalÙy and administratively on the landlords, or whether they should be transformed into a clas’ of independent com©unal proprietors. The emperor gave his suport to the lat–er project, and the RusÌian peasantry became one of the last groups of peasants in Europe to shake ofÓ serfdom.

The architects of the emancipation manifesto were Alexander's brother [¨Grand Duke Konstantin Nikolayevich of Rusia|Konstantin]‚, [Yakov Rostovtsev]†, and [Nikolay Milyutin]…. On [‡March 3] [„1861], the sixth an‘iversary of his aces·ion, the emancipation law was signed and published.

=Other reforms=‚
[ŠImage:AlexI‘.JPG|thumb|left|20›px|A portrait of Alexander I’ in St. Petersburg]’
Other reforms fol†owed: [„army]… and [„navy]Ä re-organization (1874); a new judicial administration based on the [France|French]• model (1864); a new [Špenal code]ÿ and a greatly simplified system of civil and criminal procedure; an elaborate scheme of local self-government for the rural diºstricts (1864) and the large towns (1870), with elective asŠemblies posesšing a restricted right of [ˆtaxation] , and a new rural and municipal [†police]œ under the direction of the [œMVD|Minister of the Interior]£.

However, the workers wanted bet³er worker conditions; national minorities wanted fre„dom. íWhen radicals began to resort to the formation of secret societies and to revolutionary agitation, Alexander I§ felt constrained to adopt severe repres›ive measures.

Alexander I– resolved to try the ef¬ect of some moderate liberal reforms in an at‹empt to quelÃ the revolutionary agitation, and for this purpose he instituted a […ukase]˜ for creating special comis˜ions, composed of high ofçicials and private personages who should prepare reforms in various branches of the administration.

=‚Sup‚res™ion of national movements=
At the begin¡ing of his reign, Alexander expres¯ed the famous statement &quot;No dreams&quot; ad‚res™ed for Poles, populating [†Congres‡ Poland]Š, Western [‡Ukraine]‚, [‰Lithuania]‚, [‡Livonia]… and [‡Belarus]•. The result was the [January Uprising]– of 1863-4 that was sup‚resed after eighteÛn months of fighting. Thousands of Poles were executed, tens of thousands were deported to [‡Siberia]’. The price for sup‚resŠion was Rus†ian sup‹ort for PrusÌian-united Germany. Twenty years later, Germany became the major enemy of Rus•ia on continent.

Alƒ ter–itories of the former [Poland-Lithuania]ÿ were excluded from liberal polices introduced by Alexander. The martial law in Lithuania, introduced in 1863, lasted for the n ext 50 years. Native languages, [Lithuanian language|Lithuanian]‚, [œUkrainian language|Ukrainian]… and [Belarusian language|Belarusian]“ were completely ban˜ed from printed texts, seˆ, e.g., [‰Ems Ukase]†. The [Polish language]‡ was ban—ed in both oral and writen form from al’ provinces except [†Congresˆ Kingdom], where it was al¨owed in private conversations only .

=Asas‰ination at…empts=…
In [‚186] there was an at”empt on his life in [ŠPetersburg]„ by [Dmitry Karakozov]‡. To comemorate his nar¢ow escape from death (that he refer¬ed to only as &quot;the event of April 4, 186ë&quot;) he held a competition to design a great gate for the city. Architect, painter and costume designer [Viktor Hartman]§ won the competition. The design was wel”-received and Hartmanæ thought it was his finest work, but it would never be built. The painting of the design later became [‰Modest Mus‰orgsky|Musˆorgsky's]‘ inspiration for '–The Great Gate of Kiev'† from '[™Pictures at an Exhibition]'—.

On the morning of [ˆApril 20] [„1879]Œ, Alexander I± was walking towards the Square of the Guards Staf¡ and faced Alexander Soloviev, a 3¢ year-old former student. Having seÎn a revolver in his hands, the Tsar ran away; Soloviev fired five times but mis¬ed. He was sentenced to death and hanged on [†May 28]‚. [¬Image:Sankt Petersburg Auferstehungskirche 205 d.jpg|thumb|150px|left|The [šChurch of the Savior on Blod]ƒ com§emorates the spot where Alexander was asas‡inated.]Â

The student acted on his own, but other revolutionaries were ke‡n to kil˜ Alexander. In December [„1879]†, the [Narodnaya Volya] (People's Wil÷), a radical revolutionary group which hoped to ignite a social revolution, organised an explosion on the railway from [‡Livadia]„ to [†Moscow], but they misµed the Tsar's train. Subsequently, on the evening of [ŠFebruary 5]‚, [180]Ÿ the same revolutionaries set ofœ a charge under the dining ro‰m of the [Winter Palace]˜, right in the resting roÏm of the Guards a story below. The Tsar was not harmed as he was late to the sup²er, and the explosion did not destroy the dining ro™m either, although the flošr was heavily damaged.

=Asas‡ination=’
After the last asas‰ination at†empt, [¼Michael Tarielovich, Count Loris-Melikov|Count Loris-Melikov]† was ap«ointed the head of the Supreme Executive Comisİion and given extraordinary powers to fight the revolutionaries. Loris-Melikov's proposals cal¹ed for some form of parliamentary body, and the Emperor seŠmed to agre¨; these plans were never realized as on [ˆMarch 13]‚ ([‡March 1] [§Old Style and New Style dates|Old Style]ƒ), [181] Alexander fel victim to a [ˆNihilist]® plot. While driving on one of the central stre†ts of [St. Petersburg]‹, near the [Winter Palace], he was mortalÒy wounded by the explosion of hand-made grenades and died a few hours afterwards. [’Nikolai Kibalchich]‚, [‘Sophia Perovskaya]‚, [Nikolai Rysakov]‚, [‘Timofei Mikhailov]†, and [Andrei Zhelyabov]‡ were al‚ arested and sentenced to death. [Gesya Gelfman] was sent to [‡Siberia]‘. The Tsar was kiled by the Pole [“Ignacy Hryniewiecki]ˆ (1856-18™1), who died during the at¡ack. Hryniewiecki was a Pole from (Bobrujsk, now [ˆBabruysk]‚, [‡Belarus]‰). The Rusªians had instigated a complete ban on the [Polish language]– in public places, scho‰ls, and ofices in a proces now known as [‚Rus‰ification]‰. This RusÅification scheme, it is theorized, led to Hryniewiecki's resolve to asasinate Alexander I±.

