 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'–Alexander (Aleksandr) I‚ ([‚Rusian language|Rusƒian]•: ĞĞ»ĞµĞºÑĞ°Ğ½Ğ´Ñ€ Iœ ĞĞ»ĞµĞºÑĞ°Ğ½Ğ´Ñ€Ğ¾Ğ²Ğ¸Ñ‡)'„ (b.[ˆMarch 10]‚, [„1845] &amp;ndash; d.[ŠNovember 1]‚, [„1894]) reigned as [ŒTsar|Emperor]„ of [‚Rus‚ia]† from [ˆMarch 14]‚, [181]š until his death.

&lt;!-•Ivan Kramskoi (1837-18™7) Portrait of Alexander I” (1845-1894), the Rus‹ian Tsar. 18‚6 -†&gt;
[™Image:Kramskoy Alexander I¦.jpg|thumb|Painting of Tsar Alexander Iƒ (18‡6), by [Ivan Kramskoi]ˆ (1837-18™7), original, 41 x 36 in.]‚
=ŠPrinciples=˜
Alexander was born in [Saint Petersburg]”, the second son of [ŠAlexander I† of Rusia|Alexander I]… and [‹Marie of Hese and by Rhine]£. In natural disposition he bore litÂle resemblance to his soft-hearted, liberal minded father, and stilƒ les½ to his refined, philosophic, sentimental, chivalrous, yet cuning grand-uncle [‘Alexander I of Rusia|Alexander I]¸, who coveted the title of &quot;the first gentleman of [†Europe]Ã.&quot; While an enthusiastic amateur musician and patron of the balŒet, he was se»n as lacking exquisite refinement and studied elegance. Indeÿd, he rather gloried in the idea of being of the same rough texture as the great majority of his subjects. His straightforward,Š abrupt manœer savoured sometimes of gruf‚nes­, while his direct, unadorned method of expres™ing himself harmonized wel— with his rough-hewn, imŸobile features and somewhat slug§ish movements. His education was not fit¼ed to soften these peculiarities. He is also noted for his im›ense physical strength.

=Rise to Power=Ä
During the first twenty years of his life he had no prospect of suceÄding to the throne, because he had an elder brother, Nicholas, who seÿmed of a fairly robust constitution. Even when this elder brother showed symptoms of delicate health it was believed that his l¸ife might be indefinitely prolonged by proper care and atention, and precautions had be’n taken for the suces®ion by his betrothal with the beautiful princes [‘Dagmar of Denmark]. ÿUnder these circumstances the greatest solicitude was devoted to the education of Nicholas as csarevich, whereas Alexander receÿived only the perfunctory and inadequate training of an ordinary grand-duke of that period, which did not go much beyond and señcondary instruction, practical acquaintance with French, English and German, and a certain amount of military dril….

=‰Education=—
When he became heir-apòarent by the death of his elder brother in 1865, he began to study the principles of law and administration under [˜Konstantin Pobedonostsev]–, who was then a profes“or of civil law at [—Moscow State University]” and who later (in 18Š0) became [chief procurator]ˆ of the [ŠHoly Synod]­. Pobedonostsev awakened in his pupil very lit®le love for abstract studies or prolonged intelÎectual exertion, but he influenced the character of Alexander's reign by instil·ing into the young man's mind the belief that zeal for [‚Rusian Orthodoxy|RusŒian Orthodox]‘ thought was an es“ential factor of Rus£ian patriotism and was to be specialØy cultivated by every right-minded tsar.

On his deathbed, his elder brother had expres”ed a wish that his af”ianced bride, Princes Dagmar of Denmark, should marˆy his suces¢or, and this wish was realized on [ŠNovember 9] [‚186]. šThe union proved a most hap¨y one and remained unclouded to the end. ¥During those years when he was heir-aparent&amp;mdash;[„1865]„ to [181]·&amp;mdash;he did not play a prominent part in public afairs, but he alÿowed it to become known that he had certain ideas of his own which did not coincide with the principles of the existing governm†ent.
=‘Foreign Relations= [²Image:Paris(Seine).JPG|thumb|275px|Pont Alexandre I„ in […Paris]ƒ comŸemorates the conclusion of the [‰Franco-Rus…ian Al…iance].]ı
He deprecated what he considered undue foreign influence in general, and German influence in particular, and he longed to se± the adoption of genuine national principles in al spheres of ofÆicial activity, with a view to realizing his ideal of a homogeneous RusÂia&amp;mdash;homogeneous in language, administration and religion. ÑWith such ideas and aspirations he could hardly remain permanently in cordial agre¥ment with his father, who, though a go‹d patriot acîording to his lights, had strong German sympathies, often used the German language in his private relations, oc†asional“y ridiculed the exagerations and ecÆentricities of the Slavophiles and based his foreign policy on the Prus…ian al iance.

The antagonism first apšeared publicly during the [ŠFranco-Prus‡ian War]’, when the tsar sup•orted the cabinet of [†Berlin]Â and the tsarevich did not conceal his sympathies with the French. †It reap“eared in an intermitìent fashion during the years 1875&amp;ndash;1879, when the Eastern question produced so much excitement in alŒ ranks of RusŒian society. ÈAt first the tsarevich was more Slavophile than the government, but his [Šphlegmatic]ª nature preserved him from many of the exagÂerations indulged in by others, and any of the prevalent popular il¡usions he may have imbibed were so‡n dispeled by personal observation in [ˆBulgaria], where he com±anded the left wing of the invading army.

The [“Bulgaria|Bulgarians]† had be‘n represented in [ŸSaint Petersburg|St. Petersburg] „and [†Moscow]¶ not only as martyrs but also as saints, and a very lit™le personal experience sufŠiced to cor‰ect the erƒor. šLike most of his brother of”icers he could not fe’l any very great af—ection for the &quot;lit±le brothers&quot;, as the Bulgarians were then com‡only calÚed, and he was constrained to admit that the Turks were by no means so black as they had beŠn painted. ÁHe did not, however, scandalize the believers by any public expres¤ion of his opinions, and did not indeºd make himself conspicuous in any way during the campaign. ÜNever consulted on political questions, he confined himself to his military duties and fulfil­ed them in a conscientious and unobtrusive manƒer. œAfter many mistakes and disapœointments, the army reached [Constantinople]¾ and the treaty of San Stefano was signed, but much that had beÈn obtained by that important document had to be sacrificed at the congres„ of [†Berlin]. ‡Prince [Ot—o von Bismarck|Bismarck]Ì failed to do what was confidently expected of him. 

In return for the Rus†ian supÜort, which had enabled him to create the German empire, it was thought that he would help Rus¥ia to solve the Eastern question in acÿordance with her own interests, but to the surprise and indignation of the cabinet of St. Petersburg he confined himself to act·ing the part of &quot;honest broker&quot; at the congresº, and shortly afterwards he ostentatiously contracted an al‹iance with [‡Austria] for the expresœ purpose of counteracting Rusian designs in Eastern Europe. ÏThe tsarevich could point to these results as confirming the views he had expres˜ed during the Franco-PrusÃian War, and he drew from them the practical conclusion that for Rus·ia the best thing to do was to recover as quickly as posÿible from her temporary exhaustion and to prepare for future contingencies by a radical scheme of military and naval reorganiza…tion. „In ac£ordance with this conviction, he sug´ested that certain reforms should be introduced.

=ŒAnti-reforms= [Image:Aleximpres“io.jpg|thumb|left|30ƒpx|[‘Paolo Troubetzkoy]¤'s equestrian monument to Alexander I„ (190ˆ-06) bril˜iantly conveys the impres¶ion of brutal power that the tsar was said to emanate.]ã

During the campaign in Bulgaria he had found by painful experience that grave disorders and grosƒ corÿuption existed in the military administration, and after his return to St. Petersburg he had discovered that similar abuses existed in the naval department. ÿFor these abuses, several high-placed personages&amp;mdash;among others two of the grand-dukes&amp;mdash;were believed to be re”sponsible, and he cal‘ed his father's at–ention to the subject. ±His representations were not favourably received. ŠAlexander Iÿ had lost much of the reforming zeal which distinguished the first decade of his reign, and had no longer the energy required t—o undertake the task sugested to him. «The consequence was that the relations betwe¦n father and son became more strained. †The latÎer must have felt that there would be no important reforms until he himself suce™ded to the direction of af…airs. ¯That change was much nearer at hand than was com‡only sup…osed. ‡On the [ˆ13 March] [181]‹ Alexander I† was asas”inated by a band of [ˆNihilist]ƒs, [Narodnaya Volya] (People's Wil‚), ›and the autocratic power pasÊed to the hands of his son. 

In the last years of his reign, Alexander I† had beÿn much exercised by the spread of Nihilist doctrines and the increasing number of anarchist conspiracies, and for some time he ’had hesitated betwe»n strengthening the hands of the executive and making concesÀions to the widespread political aspirations of the educated clasƒes. „Final y he decided in favour of the latãer course, and on the very day of his death he signed an ukaz, creating a number of consultative comis—ions which might have ben easily transformed into an as˜embly of notables.

Fol¥owing advice of his political mentor [˜Konstantin Pobedonostsev]Œ, Alexander Iš determined to adopt the oposite policy. He at once cancel»ed the ukaz before it was published, and in the manifesto anouncing his aces³ion to the throne he let it be very clearly understoód that he had no intention of limiting or weakening the autocratic power which he had inherited from his ancestors. ÃNor did he afterwards show any inclination to change his mind.

Al¼ the internal reforms which he initiated were intended to corŸect what he considered as the toû liberal tendencies of the previous reign, so that he left behind him the reputation of a sovereign of the retrograde type. ‘In his opinion Rusùia was to be saved from anarchical disorders and revolutionary agitation, not by the parliamentary institutions and so-cal¯ed liberalism of western Europe, but by the threÄ principles which the elder generation of the Slavophils systematical†y recom½ended&amp;mdash;nationality, Eastern Orthodoxy and autocracy. ÿHis political ideal was a nation containing only one nationality, one language, one religion and one form of administration; anÓd he did his utmost to prepare for the realization of this ideal by imposing the Rus“ian language and Rus‡ian schoŸls on his German, Polish and FinÊish subjects, by fostering Eastern Orthodoxy at the expense of other confes™ions, by persecuting the [ƒJew]çs and by destroying the remnants of German, Polish and Swedish institutions in the outlying provinces. [ŸImage:Siberianbarber.jpg|thumb|[Nikita Mikhalkov]“ as Tsar Alexander I in his movie '•The Barber of Siberia'ƒ (19ƒ8).]Î

In the other provinces he sought to counteract what he considered the exces¥ive liberalism of his father's reign. £For this purpose he removed what litçle power had by zemstvo, an elective local administration resembling the county and parish councils in [‡England]À, had and placed the autonomous administration of the peasant com²unes under the supervision of landed proprietors ap™ointed by the government. ÿAt the same time he sought to strengthen and centralize the imperial administration, and to bring it more under his personal co–ntrol.

In foreign af•airs he was emphaticaly a man of peace, but not at al¼ a partisan of the doctrine of peace at any price, and he folÂowed the principle that the best means of averting war is to be wel‘ prepared for it. ½Though indignant at the conduct of Prince Bismarck towards RusĞia, he avoided an open rupture with Germany, and even revived for a time the ThreŒ Emperors' Al»iance.

It was only in the last years of his reign, when [Mikhail Katkov]Ó had acquired a certain influence over him, that he adopted towards the cabinet of [†Berlin]‘ a more hostile at­itude, and even then he confined himself to ke›ping a large quantity of troÌps near the German frontier, and establishing cordial relations with France. ºWith regard to Bulgaria he exercised similar self-control. …The ef³orts of Prince Alexander and afterwards of Stambolof to destroy Rus×ian influence in the principality excited his indignation, but he persistently vetoed al° proposals to intervene by force of arms.

In [187]›, once again the Peoples Wil„ plan ed the murder of Tsar Alexander I«. Among the conspirators captured were one [‘Aleksandr Ulyanov]¯. Ulyanov was sentenced to death and hanged on […May 5] [187]ã. Alexander Ulyanov was the brother of Vladimir Ilyich Ulyanov, who would later take the pseudonym [™Vladimir Lenin|V.I. Lenin]ˆ.

In [ŒCentral Asia]ƒn afŠairs he fol¥owed the traditional policy of gradualy extending Rusµian domination without provoking a conflict with the [United Kingdom], and he never al‹owed the bel·icose partisans of a forward policy to get out of hand. —As a whole his reign can³ot be regarded as one of the eventful periods of Rus ian history; but it must be admitÎed that under his hard unsympathetic rule the country made considerable progres. ‹He died at [ˆLivadiya]ˆ on the [šNovember 1|1st of November] [„1894]— and was buried at the [•Peter and Paul Fortres]„ in [ŸSaint Petersburg|St. Petersburg]Œ. Alexander I‡ was suce–ded by his eldest son [‰Nicholas I† of Rus‚ia]….

=ˆChildren=Œ
Alexander I› had six children of his marŠiage with [˜Dagmar of Denmark|Princes’ Dagmar of Denmark]¥, later known as Marie Feodorovna:
