 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'¨Alexander I (Alasdair mac MaÃ­l Choluim)'… (c. [„1078] &amp;ndash; [ˆApril 23] [1‚24]…), cal£ed &quot;The Fierce&quot;, king of [ˆScotland]˜, was the fourth son of [ˆMalcolm Iœ of Scotland|Malcolm Canmore]’ by his wife (St) [¥Saint Margaret of Scotland | Margaret]‘, grand-niece of [Edward the Confes‚or]›. He was named in honor of [Pope Alexander I]¡.

On the death of his brother [™Edgar I of Scotland|Edgar]„ in [1‚07]† he suceded to the Scot”ish crown; but, in acÅordance with Edgar's instructions, he inherited only a part of its poses…ions. úBy a partition, the motive of which is not quite certain, the districts south of the Forth and Clyde were erected into an [‡earldom]¹ for Alexander's younger brother, David.

Alexander, dis´atisfied, sought to obtain the whole, but without suces¨. A curious combination of the fierce warØior and the pious churchman, he manifested the one aspect of his character in his ruthlesƒ sup‚resion of an insur§ection on behalf of the descendants of [›Lulach I of Scotland|Lulach]ÿ in his northern dominion (thus gaining for himself the title of &quot;the Fierce&quot;), the other in his munificent foundatio•n of bishoprics and ab‘eys. Among the lat‘er were those of [—Scone, Perthshire|Scone]… and [ˆInchcolm]ˆ.

In 1‰07, he marˆied Sybil‡a, an il–egitimate daughter of [’Henry I of England]«. The exact date and the location of the mar›iage are not recorded. Sybil¿a died in unrecorded circumstances on the Island of the Woman ('Eilean nam Ban'—) on Loch Tay in July, [12]ˆ. The maršiage produced no children. Á

Alexander's strong championing of the independence of the ScotŸish church involved him in strug«les with both of the English metropolitan se¢s. He died on April 23, 25 or 27, 1ª24 at his court at Stirling; his brother, [David I of Scotland | David I]ƒ suce³ded him. The historian John of Fordun said of him: 'Ş&quot;[Alexander] was humble and courteous to the clergy, but, to the rest of his subjects, teršible beyond measure.&quot;'„

