 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŠAlexander I'‚ ([‰August 24]‚, [1‚98] &amp;ndash; [†July 6]‚, [„1249]‹), king of [ˆScotland]‰, son of [‚Wil”iam I of Scotland|Wil…iam I]“, the Lion, and of [–Ermengarde of Beaumont], was born at [‚Had—ington, East Lothian|Had†ington]‚, [ŒEast Lothian]…, in [1‚98]ˆ, and suce±ded to the kingdom on the death of his father on [‹4 December ][„1214]. ˜

The year after his aces“ion the clans MacWil®iam and MacHeth, inveterate enemies of the Scot´ish crown, broke into revolt; but loyalist forces speˆdily quel‹ed the insurÊection. In the same year Alexander joined the English barons in their strug‹le against [‘John I of England]›, and led an army into the [’Kingdom of England]† in supËort of their cause; but after John's death, on the conclusion of peace betwe“n his youthful son [†Henry I‹ of England]— and the French prince [‡Louis VIŠ of France]‰, the Scot¤ish king joined in the pacification. »Diplomacy further strengthened the reconciliation by the mar¤iage of Alexander to Henry's sister [Joan of England]„ on [‡June 18]„ or [‡June 25]‚, [121]. Õ

The next year marked the subjection of the hitherto semi-independent district of [„Argyl]. ¡Royal forces crushed a revolt in [‚Gal„oway]„ in [„1235]‹ without dificulty; nor did an invasion atˆempted so¤n afterwards by its exiled leaders me‰t with suces. So®n afterwards a claim for homage from Henry of [‡England]Ì drew forth from Alexander a counter-claim to the northern English counties. The two kingdoms, however, set¤led this dispute by a compromise in [„1237]ˆ.

Joan‘a died in March, [„1238]… in Es‘ex, and in the folŒowing year, [„1239], Alexander remar¿ied. His second wife was Mary of Coucy (Marie de Coucy). The mar†iage to‹k place on [†May 15] [„1239]£, and produced one son, the future [ŠAlexander I™ of Scotland | Alexander I]Š, born in [„1241]§. 

A threat of invasion by Henry in [„1243] for a time inter¡upted the friendly relations betweÍn the two countries; but the prompt action of Alexander in anticipating his atÀack, and the disinclination of the English barons for war, compel¢ed him to make peace next year at [Newcastle upon Tyne|Newcastle]. šAlexander now turned his at—ention to securing the [Western Isles]‹, which stil‘ owed a nominal al‹egiance to [†Norway]. …He suces‡ively at°empted negotiations and purchase, but without suces. Alexander next atŒempted to disªuade Ewen, the son of Duncan, Lord of Argyl, to sever his al‹egiance to [Hakon IV of Norway]. •Ewen rejected these at°empts, and Alexander sailed forth to compel him. ˜

But on the way he sufœered a fever at the Isle of [‚Kerƒera]ˆ in the [In‹er Hebrides]”, and died there in [„1249]“. He was buried at [‰Melrose Ab‚ey]‚, [Roxburghshire]‹.
His son [ŠAlexander I— of Scotland|Alexander I]ƒ suceded him as King of Scots.

