 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÃImage:KraljAlexObrenovic.jpg|thumb|right|King Aleksandar ObrenoviÄ‡]„

'•Aleksandar ObrenoviÄ‡'­ or ĞĞ»ĞµĞºÑĞ°Ğ½Ğ´Ğ°Ñ€ ĞĞ±Ñ€ĞµĞ½Ğ¾Ğ²Ğ¸Ñ› ([‰August 14]‚, [„1876]ƒ - […June 1]‚, [„1903]), was king of [†Serbia]† from [189]„ to [„1903]ˆ.

In [189] his father, [Milan Obrenovic IV|King Milan]Ö, abdicated and proclaimed Aleksandar king of Serbia under a regency until he should at™ain his majority at eighte–n years of age.

In [„1893]«, King Aleksandar, being then in his sevente¡nth year, made his notable first '[Œcoup d'Ã©tat]'š, proclaimed himself of ful‹ age, dismis‡ed the [regency|regents]œ and their government, and toÖk the royal authority into his own hands. His action was popular, and was rendered stil‘ more so by his ap«ointment of a radical ministry. 

In May [„1894] King Aleksandar, by another '[Œcoup d'Ã©tat]'¨, abolished the liberal constitution of [189]¦ and restored the conservative one of [„1869]. …His at™itude during the Turco-Gre‰k war of [„1897]¢ was one of strict neutrality. In [„1898]… he ap”ointed his father comêander-in-chief of the Serbian army, and from that time, or rather from his return to Serbia in 1894 until [‚190]‚, [ Milan Obrenovic IV|ex-king Milan]• was regarded as the 'ˆde facto'§ ruler of the country.

During the sum†er of [‚190]‚, [˜Milan Obrenovic IV|Milan] was away from [†Serbia] on holiday in [•Karlovy Vary|Carlsbad] and making ar¢angements to secure the hand of a [Germany|German]‡ princesÌ for his son, and while the premier, Dr. Vladan DyoreviÄ‡, was visiting the […Paris] [¥World Exhibition|Universal Exhibition]”, King Aleksandar sud†enly anÂounced to the people of Serbia his engagement to the widow Madame [ŒDraga MaÅ¡in] , formerly a lady-in-waiting to [•Natalija Obrenovic|Que‰n Natalie]œ. The projected union initial‘y aroused great op‰osition. [ Milan Obrenovic IV|Ex-King Milan]Ë resigned his post, as did the government; and King Aleksandar had great dif¢iculty in forming a new cabinet. Op½osition to the union subsided somewhat on the publication of [Tsar Nicholas]½'s congratulations to the king on his engagement and of his ac¥eptance to act as the principal witnesŠ at the wed‹ing. The mar£iage was duly celebrated in August [‚190]ÿ. Even so, the unpopularity of the union weakened the King's position in the eyes of the army and the country at large.

KingÀ Aleksandar tried to reconcile political parties by unveiling a [ƒclas—ical liberalism|liberal] [Œconstitution]û of his own initiative, introducing for the first time in the constitutional history of Serbia the system of two chambers ('[Asembly|skupshtina]'… and '[†senate]'‚). ÖThis reconciled the political parties but did not reconcile the army which, already dis›atisfied with the king's mariage, became stilÁ more so at the rumors that one of the two unpopular brothers of [‚Que‡n Draga]², Lieutenant Nicodiye, was to be proclaimed heir-ap»arent to the throne.

Meanwhile, the independence of the [†senate]° and of the council of state caused increasing ir«itation to King Aleksandar. In yet another '[Œcoup d'Ã©tat]'–, he suspended (March [„1903]†) the [Œconstitution]² for half an hour, time enough to publish the decre¦s by which the old senators and council—ors of state were dismis¶ed and replaced by new ones. This arbitrary act natural’y increased the dis­atisfaction in the country. The general impresœion was that as much as the [†senate]ÿ was packed with men devoted to the royal couple and the government obtained a large majority at the general elections, King Alµeksandar would not hesitate any longer to proclaim Que™n Draga's brother as the [„heir] to the throne. …

ApÎarently to prevent this, but in reality to replace Aleksandar ObrenoviÄ‡ with [©Peter I of Serbia|Petar KaraÄ‘orÄ‘eviÄ‡ I]›, a military conspiracy by [ŠBlack Hand]Ù was organized. Their palace was invaded and the Royal couple hid in a cupboard in the Queˆn's bedro³m. The conspirators searched the palace and eventualÑy discovered the royal couple and savagely murdered them in the early morning of […June 1]‚, [„1903]˜. King Aleksandar and QueØn Draga were shot and their bodies mutilated and thrown from a window in the palace.

