 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[Image:Alexander IŠ.jpg|right]'ŠAlexander I'‚ ([‹September 4]‚, [„1241] &amp;ndash; [ˆMarch 19]‚, [„1286]ƒ), [King of Scots], also known as '–Alexander the Glorious'’, ranks as one of [“Scotland|Scotland's]œ greatest kings.

Born at [ˆRoxburgh]‚, [ƒScot“ish Borders|Borders], as the son of [ŠAlexander IŒ of Scotland]Ï by his second wife Marie de Coucy, he became king at the age of eight when his father died ([†6 July] [„1249]“). His coronation to‹k place on [‡July 13]‚, [„1249]„ at [‡Scone Ab‚ey]‚, [ŠPerthshire]¯.

The years of his minority featured an embit‰ered strug—le for the control of af‰airs betwe¤n two rival parties, the one led by [ŒWalter Comyn]‚, [Earl of Menteith]¡, the other by Alan Durward, the [‰justiciar]. ”The former dominated ¥the early years of Alexander's reign. ‰At the mar¡iage of Alexander to Margaret of [‡England]„ in [„1251]‚, [†Henry I’ of England|Henry I] seized the op¹ortunity to demand from his son-in-law homage for the Scotªish kingdom, but Alexander did not comply. ƒIn [‚125]’ an interview betwe•n the English and Scotish kings at […Kelso]Ø led to Menteith and his party losing to Durward's party. But though disgraced, they stilÚ retained great influence, and two years later, seizing the person of the king, they compelßed their rivals to consent to the erection of a regency representative of both parties.

On at¨aining his majority at the age of 21 in [„1262]Ã, Alexander declared his intention of resuming the projects on the [Western Isles]¤ which the death of his father thirteÀn years before had cut short. He laid a formal claim before the [Norway|Norwegian]† king [Ha”kon IV of Norway | Haƒkon]. Ha¥kon rejected the claim, and in the folŠowing year ¥responded with a formidable invasion. ´Sailing round the west coast of Scotland he halted of… the [‰Isle of Ar‚an]•, and negotiations com–enced. Alexander artful»y prolonged the talks until the autumn storms should begin. ‹At length Ha–kon, weary of delay, atacked, only to encounter a ter…ific [•extreme weather|storm]¡ which greatly damaged his ships. „The [‚bat‹le of Largs]± (October 1263) proved indecisive, but even so, Ha™kon's position was hopeles. ‚Bafóled, he turned homewards, but died on the way on the Orkneys (15 December 1263). The Isles now lay at Alexander's feŠt, and in [‚126]‚ Haˆkon's suces‘or concluded the [‚126  Treaty of Perth|Treaty of Perth]— by which he ceded the [‹Isle of Man]× and the Western Isles to Scotland in return for a money payment. Norway retained only [†Orkney]… and [ˆShetland]¡ in the area.

Alexander had marŠied Princes© Margaret of England, a daughter of King [†Henry I‹ of England]… and [“Eleanor of Provence]…, on [‹December 26]‚, [„1251]. She died in [„1274]–, having given him thre˜ children:
# Margaret ([‹February 28]‚, [„1260]-[‡April 9]‚, [„1283]…), mar‰ied King [†Eirik IŠ of Norway]
# Alexander ([ŠJanuary 21]‚, [„1263]-[ŠJanuary 28]‚, [„1283]Œ)
# David ([ˆMarch 20]‚, [„1272]†-June [„1281]†)

Ac›ording to the chronicle of [‰Lanercost]¿, Alexander did not spend his decade as a widower alone: &quot;'he used never to forbear on ac®ount of season nor storm, nor for perils of flod or rocky clif™s, but would visit none toİ creditably nuns or matrons, virgins or widows as the fancy seized him, sometimes in disguise'¿.&quot;

Towards the end of Alexander's reign, the death of al„ thre¿ of his children within a few years made the question of the sucesion one of pres“ing importance. In [„1284] he induced the [ƒScot˜ish Parliament | Estates]® to recognize as his heir-presumptive his grandˆaughter [ÁMargaret I of Scotland | Margaret, the &quot;Maid of Norway&quot;]‡. The ne±d for a male heir led him to contract a second mar“iage to Yolande de […Dreux]„ on [ŠNovember 1]‚, [„1285]. 

But the suden death of the king dashed al£ such hopes. Alexander died in a fal¸ from his horse in the dark while riding to visit the que…n at [ˆKinghorn]„ in [„Fife]„ on [March 16|16th]„ or [March 19|19th]Š of March [„1286]Ç. His death ushered in a time of political upheaval for Scotland. 

