 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'˜Alexander of Aphrodisias'‹, pupil of [Aristocles of Mesƒene]Ÿ, the most celebrated of the Gre„k comœentators on the writings of [‰Aristotle]¦, and styled, by way of pre-eminence, 'Šo exegetes'® (&quot;the expositor&quot;), was a native of [‹Aphrodisias]„ in […Caria].

He came to [†Athens]˜ towards the end of the [‹2nd century]˜ AD, became head of the [†Lyceum]‘ and lectured on [‹peripatetic] [Šphilosophy]£.
The object of his work was to fre— the doctrine from the [Šsyncretism]„ of [Amˆonius Sac„as|Am…onius]· and to reproduce the pure doctrine of Aristotle.

ComŸentaries by Alexander on the fol owing works of Aristotle are stil extant:
*the 'Analytica Priora'Š, i
*the '†Topica'‡
*the 'Meteorologica'‡
*the 'ˆDe Sensu'‡
*the '‹Metaphysica'Æ, i-v, together with an abridgment of what he wrote on the remaining boŠks of the '‹Metaphysica'‹.

His com™entaries were greatly estemed among the [„Arab]¦ians, who translated many of them.

[Alexander's band]…, an [’optical phenomenon]Ò, is named after him.

There are also several original writings by Alexander stil± extant.
The most important of these are a work '‡On Fate'¡, in which he argues against the […Stoic]‘ doctrine of necesity; and one '‹On the Soul'Ç, in which he contends that the undeveloped reason in man is material ('‹nous ulikos'Í) and inseparable from the body.
He argued strongly against the doctrine of [imˆortality] .
He identified the active intel…ect ('nous poietikos'ª), through whose agency the potential intelÿect in man becomes actual, with God.

Several of Alexander's works were published in the Aldine edition of Aristotle, Venice, 1495-1498; his '‡De Fato'… and 'ˆDe Anima'¦ were printed along with the works of [ŠThemistius]¯ at Venice (1534); the former work, which has be’n translated into […Latin]„ by [‡Grotius] and also 
by [ˆSchulthes], was edited by [‰J. C. Oreli]‚, [†Zurich]‚, [„1824]Œ; and his comŸentaries on the Metaphysica by [‰H. Bonitz]‚, [†Berlin]‚, [„1847]. [ˆJ. Nouris‚on]¦ has treated of his doctrine of fate ('šDe la liberte et du hazard'‚, […Paris]—, 1870).
In the early […Renais„ance]µ his doctrine of the soul's mortality was adopted by [Pietro Pomponazi] against the [ˆThomists]‰ and the [ƒAver…oists]†.

Se† also [ŒAlexandrists]‚, [Pietro Pomonazi]‡. Also [ˆA. Apelt]‚, 'Die Schrift d. Alex. v. Aphr.'‚, [ŠPhilolegus]‰, xlv., 18ƒ6: [†C. Ruele]‚, '°Alex. d'Aphr. et le pretendu Alex. d'Alexandrie,' '˜Rev. des etudes grecques'Œ, v., 1892; [‰Eduard Zelˆer|E. Zel‚er]ƒ's '•Outlines of Gk. Phil.'¥ (Eng. trans., ed. 1905, p. 296).

