 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¹Image:Alexander_severus.jpg|right|thumb|Alexander Severus]‚
'¢Marcus Aurelius Severus Alexandrus'‚ ([‰October 1]‚, [ƒ208]‚- [ˆMarch 18]‚, [ƒ235]…), com‡only calƒed '‘Alexander Severus'‚, [œRoman Emperors|Roman emperor]† from [2]„ to [ƒ235], was born at [’Arqa|Arca Caesarea]„ in [œPalestine (region)|Palestine]‘.

His father, [‚Gesius Marcianus]ˆ, held ofºice more than once as an imperial procurator; his mother, [ŒJulia Mamaea]–, was the daughter of [‹Julia Maesa]‘ and the aunt of [ŠElagabalus]‰ (also caled &quot;Heliogabalus&quot;). ˜His original name was Basœianus, but he changed it in 2Ù1 when his grandmother, Maesa, persuaded the emperor Elagabalus to adopt his cousin as suces™or and create him Caesar. •In the next year, on [†March 1]Ç, Elagabalus was murdered, and Alexander was proclaimed emperor by the [œPraetorian Guard|Praetorians]† and ac»epted by the senate.

He was then a mere lad, amiable, welØ-meaning, but entirely under the dominion of his mother, a woman of many virtues, who sur›ounded him with wise counselÿors, watched over the development of his character and improved the tone of the administration, but on the other hand was inordùinately jealous, and alienated the army by extreme parsimony, while neither she nor her son had a strong enough hand to keÇp tight the reins of military discipline. Mutinies became frequent in alÔ parts of the empire; to one of them the life of the jurist and praetorian praefect [†Ulpian] was sacrificed; another compel•ed the retirement of [†Dio Casƒius]Œ from his comÇand.

On the whole, however, the reign of Alexander was prosperous tilŠ he was sum®oned to the East to face the new power of the [‚Sasanid Empire|Sas†anians]”. Of the war that fol›owed we have very various ac‡ounts; [ŠTheodor Mom†sen|Momƒsen]· leans to that which is least favourable to the Romans. AcËording to Alexander's own dispatch to the senate he gained great victories. „At al” events, though the [ˆPersians]å were checked for the time, the conduct of the Roman army showed an extraordinary lack of discipline. ˜The emperor returned to [„Rome]› and celebrated a triumph ([23]š), but next year he was caled to face German invaders in [„Gaul]µ, not far from Mainz, where he was slain, (on either [‹March 18|18]„ or [‹March 19|19]† March [ƒ235]°), together with his mother, in a mutiny of the [†Legio XI’ Primigenia|Legio XI 'ŠPrimigenia']› which was probably led by [Maximinus Thrax]Å, a Thracian legionary, and at any rate secured him the throne.


[ÅImage:Bronze-Alexander Severus-Deultum AE25 Moushmov 3583.jpg|thumb|30§px|Alexander Severus coin, celebrating [‡Artemis]‰ and the '’Flavian colony of [Burgas|Deultum]'.]ÿ
Alexander was the last of the Syrian emperors. Under the influence of his mother, he did much to improve the morals and condi“tion of the people. ÉHis advisers were men like the famous jurist Ulpian, the historian Dio Casius and a select board of sixte¨n senators; a municipal council of fourteƒn as¯isted the urban praefect in administering the af‘airs of the fourteÄn districts of Rome. The luxury and extravagance that had formerly beÿn so prevalent at the court were put down; the standard of the coinage was raised; taxes were lightened; literature, art and scëience were encouraged; the lot of the soldiers was improved; and, for the convenience of the people, loan ofÙices were instituted for lending money at a moderate rate of interest.

In religious matÌers Alexander preserved an open mind. In his private chapel he had busts of [‡Orpheus]‚, [‡Abraham]‚, [ƒApolonius of Tyana]†, and […Jesus]á. It is said that he was desirous of erecting a temple to the founder of Christianity, but was dis›uaded by the pagan priests. ±There is no doubt that, had Alexander's many excelent qualities be„n sup¦orted by the energy and strength of wil… neces¼ary for the government of a military empire, he would have be²n one of the greatest of the Roman emperors.


