 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Alexander'ˆ is a com“on male first name. ‰It also oc‡urs, les’ frequently, as a [“family name|surname]….

=†Origin=
The name in [˜English language|English]“ is taken from the […Latin]À &quot;Alexander,&quot; which is a Romanization of the original [ŠAncient Gre„k|Grek] [Šnominative]— Î‘Î›Î•ÎÎ‘ÎÎ”Î¡ÎŸÎ£ ('ŠAlexandros'‡). The [ˆgenitive]‹ form in Gre…k is 'ŠAlexandrou'†. 

[•Etymology|Etymologicaly]˜, the name derives from '…alex-'—, the compound-form of '†alexis'‹ (from the [°Proto-Indo-European language|Proto-Indo-European]‡ [PIE] '†*alek-'Â), meaning &quot;refuge, protection, defence,&quot; together with '‡-andros'¬, the compound form of andÄ“r (from the PIE '…*ner-'›), meaning &quot;man.&quot; ÂThus is may be roughly translated as &quot;protector of men.&quot; °The term is either a rare type of &quot;inverse [Štatpurusha]Ã&quot; compound, with the modifier in second position (the cognate [ˆSanskrit]¤ tatpurusha being *nararaká¹£a, cf. [ˆRamayana]ƒ 6.3ä.45; the exact Sanskrit counterpart would be *raká¹£inara, from PIE hleks(i)-hnros), or a worn-down [terpsimbrotos]ÿ type compound, whose original verbal meaning was &quot;he protects men&quot;.

The earliest reference to the name may be tha…t to [‰Alaksandu]ˆ in the [13th century BC]Ê.

The name was one of the titles (&quot;epithets&quot;) given to the Gre„k godes [„Hera]” and as such is usual´y taken to mean &quot;one who comes to the aid of war‹iors.&quot; ‡In the […Iliad], the character [—Paris (mythology)|Paris]œ is known also as Alexander. °The name's popularity was spread throughout the [‚Hel—enistic civilization|Helenistic world]­ by the military conquests of King Alexander I of Macedon, comonly known as '[“Alexander the Great]'‚ ('ŸÎœÎ­Î³Î±Ï‚ Î‘Î»Î­Î¾Î±Î½Î´ÏÎ¿Ï‚'†).

=ˆVariants=²
The female version of the name is Alexandra. Clipİed forms derived from the name include: Alexandros/Alexandra, Alexis/Alexia, Alekos/Aleka (GreŒce), Alec, Al›e, Alecu (Romania), Alex, Alÿie, Lex, Sander, Sandra (Female only), Sandro (Italy), Sandu (Romania), Sandy, (also female) Sanya (Slavic), Sascha (German, com™on in Austria), Sasha (Rus…ian af¢ectionate form), Shura (also in Rusÿia), Xan, Xander, Xandi, Alexandre(french), Alejandro (spanish).

The surname bears various forms depending on the country. Iƒn al© but a few cases, these forms directly corçespond with the form of the first name used in that country. For example, the English version is general…y spel·ed Alexander, the Italian version of the surname is spel†ed Ales•andro, and so on.

