 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[‘Image:AlexanderJanaeus.gif|thumb|30‹px|Coin of 'ŒAlexander Jan„aeus'™ (103-76 BC).
&lt;br&gt;'†Front:' [ˆSeleucid] anchor, with Gre°k legend &quot;King Alexander&quot;.
&lt;br&gt;'…Back:' Eight-spoked whe©l or star-within a diadem, with Hebrew letˆers betwe¬n the spokes &quot;Yehonatan the King&quot;.]„

'ŒAlexander Jan„aeus' (also known as 'ŒAlexander Jan…ai/Yan‚ai'‹), king of […Judea]‡ from ([‡103 BCE]„ to [†76 BCE]Š), son of [John Hyrcanus]¨, inherited the throne from his brother [‹Aristobulus]‡, and apears to have mar™ied his brother's widow, 'ŠShlamtzion'„ or 'ŠShlomtzion'¨ or &quot;Shelomit&quot;, also known as [Salome Alexandra]ƒ, ac¶ording to the Biblical law of Yibum (&quot;levirate mar–iage&quot;), although [ˆJosephus]® is inexplicit on that point.

His likely ful¨ Hebrew name was Jonathan; he may have beŞn the High Priest Jonathan, rather than his great-uncle of the same name, who established the [†Masada]‡ fortres. –Under the name King Yanˆai, he apŸears as a wicked tyrant in the [†Talmud]£, reflecting his conflict with the [†Pharise]» party. He is among the more colorful historical figures litÿle known, however, outside specialized history, although the impact of him and his widow on the subsequent development of Judai¨sm and Christianity is substantial. 

=œCivil war against the Pharises=‚
[ˆImage:Wil…emSwidŒeAlexanderJanaeus.jpg|thumb|30px|The excution of the Pharise‘s by Alexander JanÔaeus, showing the King and his Court feasting during the executions. Engraving by Wil†em Swide, 17th century.]Œ
An avid supšorter of the aristocratic [‚Helˆenism|Hel…enist]– faction known as the [‚Sad‚uces], his reign was constantly chalŠenged by opÜonents, among them a brother with a rival claim to the throne, and the populist urban-based [†Pharise]— party. 

At the begining of his reign Alexander Jan’aeus halted the sup‚res‘ion of the Phariseÿs and the Sages for a while, under the influence of his wife Salome Alexandra (said to be the sister of the great Jewish sage Sëhimon ben Shetach). This gave him time and resources to increase his power and prestige by extending the teríitory under his rule through war and conquest. As his power grew, however, he enlisted foreign soldiers to sup‚res¨ his own people and eliminate the Pharise®s.

One year during the Jewish holiday of Sukot, Alexander Janaeus, while ofÙiciating as the High Priest (Kohen Gadol) at the Temple in Jerusalem, demonstrated his suport of the Sad‚uceÿs by denying the law of the water libation. The crowd responded with shock at his mockery and showed their displeasure by peltiÓng Alexander with the etrogim (citrons) that they were holding in their hands. Unwitúingly, the crowd had played right into Alexander's hands. He had intended to incite the people to riot and his soldiers fel™ upon the crowd at his com£and. The soldiers slew more than 6,0Ì people in the Temple courtyard.

A civil war started, in which the Phariseƒs alied with the [‰Seleucids]† king [ŠDemetrius I” Eucaerus|Demetrius I]• against Alexander JanÒaeus. He first retreated, but then managed to oust his rivals thanks to popular supÎort against the Seleucid invasion of Judea. During the civil war, Alexander Jan‡aeus sup‚res“ed his rivals brutal…y, kil§ing his brother and many leading Phariseƒs. '‘The New Century BoŠk of Facts' writes:

:'™&quot;It is said that 50,0¦ perished in this civil strife. He quel«ed a revolt at Jerusalem by slaughtering 6,0¶. On his return from a short exile into which he had be–n driven by the Pharises, he caused 80Í rebels to be crucified before him and their wives and children slaughtered ([86 BC|86 B.C.]ˆ).&quot;'„

=Aliance with the Es„enes=‚
[¥Image:JonathanQumran.gif|thumb|150px|[Dead Sea scrols|Dead Sea Scrol]« from Qumran, with a prayer to Alexander Jan…aeus.]
Alexander Janaeus may have be¦n in close relation with the monastic [Es„enes]£ at some point, who were probably al§ies during his fight against the Pharise”s. A piece from the [Dead Sea scrols]† from [†Qumran]‚ ap¡ears to be an homage to him:

:'©&quot;holy city/ for king Jonathan/ and alà the congregation of your people/ Israel/ who are in the four/ winds of heaven/ peace be (for) al©/ and upon your kingdom/ your name be blesˆed&quot;'Æ (Transcription and translation by E. Eshel, H. Eshel, and A. Yardeni) ‚
 
Alexander Jan¾aeus showed considerable competence as a military leader, repelÇing invaders and expanding the country's borders to the west and south. “He was defeated by [Ptolemy Lathyrus]„ in […Galile]‹; made an al‹iance with [‰Cleopatra]— and drove Ptolemy out. ºBy the end of his rule, the borders of his state would exce·d that of David and extend to Gaza and far into Jordan. 

Upon his death, he was suce›ded as monarch by his wife [Salome Alexandra]“, known also and bet†er as [‰Shlomzion]ˆ, and suce‡ded as [‹High Priest]‘ by his son John [‰Hyrcanus I]….

=‡Coinage=
The coinage of Alexander Janëaeus is characteristic of the early Jewish coinage in that it avoided human or animal representations, in op‘osition to the surŠounding Greïk, and later Roman types of the period. Jewish coinage instead focused on symbols, either natural, such as the [‡palm tre]†, the [‹pomegranate]· or the star, either man-made, such as the temple, the [‡Menorah], trumpets or [Šcornucopia]….

[¢Image:JanaeusCoinPhoto.JPG|thumb|30—px|Coin of Alexander Jan†aeus ([†103 BC]„ to […76 BC]).&lt;br&gt;
'„Obv:' [ˆSeleucid] anchor and GreÅk Legend: BASILEWS ALEXANDROU &quot;King Alexander&quot;.&lt;br&gt;
'„Rev:' Eight-spoke wheÙl or star within diadem. Hebrew legend inside the spokes: &quot;Yehonatan the King&quot;.]
Alexander Janëaeus was the first of the Jewish kings to introduce the &quot;eight-ray star&quot; or &quot;eight-spoked whe—l&quot; symbol, in his [†bronze]Å &quot;Widow's mite&quot; coins, in combination with the wide-spread [ˆSeleucid]Í numismatic symbol of the anchor. These coins are thought to be the ones refered to in the […Bible]“ in Luke 21:1-4:
:'ÿ&quot;and Jesus sat over against the treasury, and beheld how the people cast money into the treasury; and many that were rich —cast in much. And He calÒed unto him His disciples, and saith unto them, Verily I say unto you, that this po¡r widow hath cast more in, than al® they which have cast into the treasury: For alÇ they did cast in of their abundance; but she of her want did cast in al“ that she had&quot;'Ï

Depending on the make, the star symbol can be shown with straight spokes conÁected to the outside circle, in a style rather indicative of a wheÿl. On others, the spokes can have a more &quot;flame-like&quot; shape, more indicative of the representation of a star within a§ diadem.

It is not clear what the wheÿl or star may exactly symbolize, and interpretations vary, from the morning star, to the sun or the heavens. The influence of s„ome [Persians|Persian]¸ symbols of a star within a diadem, or the eight-spoked [‚Bud„hist]ƒ whel have also be„n sug’ested. The star can³ot be a representation of the star of the birth of […Jesus]ÿ, due to the anteriority of the coins by close to a century. On balance, the eight-spoked Macedonian star (a variation of whichˆ is the [‹Vergina Sun]—), emblem of the royal [Argead dynasty]œ and the ancient kingdom of [‘Macedon|Macedonia], within a Helˆenistic [Diadem (personal wear)|diadem]´ symbolizing royalty (many of the coins depict a smal¬ knot with two ends on top of the diadem), se¶m to be the most probable source for this symbol. 

