 Ÿ<text xml:space="preserve">The 'ŒAlexandrists'‹ were a scho…l of […Renais„ance]Ê philosophers who, in the great controversy on the subject 
of personal im©ortality, adopted the explanation of the 'ˆDe Anima'¨ given by Alexander of Aphrodisias.

Ac—ording to the orthodox [‡Thomism]Ÿ of the Roman Catholic Church, [‰Aristotle]Ô rightly regarded reason as a facility of the individual soul. Against this, the Averoists, led by [Agostino Nito], ÿintroduced the modifying theory that universal reason in a sense individualizes itself in each soul and then absorbs the activeš reason into itself again. ÒThese two theories respectively evolved the doctrine of individual and universal imîortality, or the absorption of the individual into the eternal One.

The Alexandrists, led by Pietro Pomponaz‹i, boldly as¸ailed these beliefs and denied that either was rightly at•ributed to Aristotle. ÿThey held that Aristotle considered the soul as a material and therefore a mortal entity which operates during life only under ¢the authority of universal reason. ¤Hence the Alexandrists denied the pos˜ibility of any form of im²ortality, holding that, since the soul is organical„y con›ected with the body, the dis‘olution of the lat°er involves the extinction of the former. 


