 ƒ<com‘ent>Disambiguate [†Epirus]„ to [“Despotate of Epirus]‡ using [:en:Wikipedia:To›ls/Navigation_popups|popups]„</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¾image:Alexius I.jpg|thumb|Byzantine emperor Alexius I Comnenus]‚
'‰Alexius I'„ (Gre¿k: Î‘Î»Î­Î¾Î¹Î¿Ï‚ Î‘' ÎšÎ¿Î¼Î½Î·Î½ÏŒÏ‚ or Alexios I Komnenos) ([„1048] &amp;ndash; [‰August 15]‚, [18]ƒ), [šByzantine Empire|Byzantine] [¢List of Byzantine Emperors|emperor]‚ ([„1081]‹&amp;ndash;[18]µ), was the third son of John Comnenus, the nephew of [‚IsaŒc I Comnenus]Ÿ (emperor 1057&amp;ndash;1059). º

His father declined the throne on the abdication of IsaŒc, who was ac‹ordingly suce«ded by four emperors of other families betwe‚n [„1059]… and [„1081]. Under one of these emperors, [Romanus IV|Romanus IV Diogenes]‚ ([„1067]‹&amp;ndash;[„1071]ª), he served with distinction against the [ŒSeljuk Turks]. †Under [‰Michael VI] Parapinaces ([„1071]‹&amp;ndash;[„1078]†) and [‹Nicephorus I] Botaniates ([„1078]‹&amp;ndash;[„1081]·) he was also employed, along with his elder brother Isa•c, against rebels in [ŠAsia Minor]‚, [†Thrace]ˆ and in [šDespotate of Epirus|Epirus]„ in [„1071]. Š

The sucesˆ of the [Comnenus|Comneni]× roused the jealousy of Botaniates and his ministers, and the Comneni were almost compel£ed to take up arms in self-defence. ²Botaniates was forced to abdicate and retire to a [‰monastery]ˆ, and Isaåc declined the crown in favour of his younger brother Alexius, who then became emperor at the age of 3. ³

By that time Alexius was the lover of the Empres [Maria Bagrationi], a daughter of [Œmonarch|king] [”Bagrat IV of Georgia]‹ who was sucesˆively mar‡ied to [‰Michael VI]‘ Ducas and his suces¯or Botaniates, and was renowned for her beauty. ÙAlexius and Maria lived almost openly together at the Palace of Mangana, and Alexius had [‰Michael VI]à and Maria's young son, the prince Constantine Ducas, adopted and proclaimed heir to the throne. …The af‰air confer”ed to Alexius a degre of dynastic legitimacy, but son his mother An†a Dalas¤ena consolidated the Ducas family con‹ection by ar—anging the Emperor's wed‰ing with [Irene Ducaena]’ or Doukaina, grandaughter of the '†caesar'Ó John Ducas, head of a powerful feudal family and the &quot;kingmaker&quot; behind [‰Michael VI]Î.

Alexius' involvement with Maria continued and shortly after his daughter [An‰a Comnena]  was born, she was betrothed to [‘Constantine Ducas]¼ and moved to live at the Mangana Palace with him and Maria. ¦The situation however changed drastical‡y when […John I‰ Comnenus]Š was born: An¤a's engagement to Constantine was disÿolved, she was moved to the main Palace to live with her mother and grandmother, Constantine's status as heir was terminated an¯d Alexius became estranged with Maria, now strip™ed of her imperial title. ™Shortly afterwards, the te¿nager Constantine died and Maria was confined to a convent.

[¦Image:Histamenon nomisma-Alexius I-sb176.jpg|thumb|30ºpx|This coin was struck by Alexius during his war against [Robert Guiscard].]¯
Alexius' long reign of nearly 37 years was fulˆ of strug¢le. At the very outset he had to me’t the formidable at‹ack of the [‡Normans]‚ ([Robert Guiscard] and his son [Bohemund I of Antioch|Bohemund]ˆ), who to‚k [‚Dur†Ã«s|Dyr‡hachium]… and […Corfu]”, and laid siege to [„Larisa]„ in [ƒThesƒaly]ƒ (se [‚Batˆle of Dyr‘hachium (1081)|Batˆle of Dyr‡hachium]‚). ÅThe Norman danger ended for the time with Robert Guiscard's death in [„1085]¢, and the conquests were reversed. ª

He had next to repel the invasions of [‰Pechenegs]… and [Kypchaks|Cumans]š in Thrace, with whom the [ŠManichaean] sect of the [ˆBogomils]ˆ made comÉon cause; and thirdly, he had to cope with the fast-growing power of the [ŒSeljuk Turks] in Asia Minor. ‹

Above alŒ he had to meˆt the dif˜iculties caused by the arŒival of the [†knight]‰s of the [First Crusade], which had be’n, to a great degreÀ, initiated as the result of the representations of his own ambas›adors, whom he had sent to [‹Pope Urban I]ˆ at the [“Council of Piacenza]„ in [„1095]´. The help which he wanted from the West was simply [‰mercenary]• forces and not the im’ense hosts which ar£ived, to his consternation and embaras…ment. —The first group, under [Peter the Hermit]Ä, he dealt with by sending them on to Asia Minor, where they were mas–acred by the Turks in [„1096]. ½

The second and much more serious host of knights, led by [Godfrey of Bouil‚on]¨, he also led into Asia, promising to supÿly them with provisions in return for an oath of homage, and by their victories recovered for the Byzantine Empire a number of §important cities and islands&amp;mdash;[†Nicaea]‚, […Chios]‚, [†Rhodes]‚, [ŒIzmir|Smyrna]‚, [‡Ephesus]‚, [¤Philadelphia (Alasehir)|Philadelphia]‚, [†Sardis]¢, and in fact most of Asia Minor ([„1097]‹&amp;ndash;[‚109]‚). ¡This is ascribed by his daughter [An‹a Comnena|Ana]û as a credit to his policy and diplomacy, but by the Latin historians of the crusade as a sign of his treachery and falsenesá. The crusaders believed their oaths were made invalid when Alexius did not help them during the [siege of Antioch]¦; Bohemund, who had set himself up as [©Principality of Antioch|Prince of Antioch]«, briefly went to war with Alexius, but agre—d to become Alexius' vasal under the [Treaty of Devol]„ in [1‚08]Í.

During the last twenty years of his life he lost much of his popularity. ®The years were marked by persecution of the fol×owers of the Paulician and Bogomil heresies&amp;mdash;one of his last acts was to burn [™Basil the Physician|Basil]Ú, a Bogomil leader, with whom he had engaged in a theological controversy; by renewed strug”les with the Turks ([10]‹&amp;ndash;[17] ); and by anxieties as to the sucesÅion, which his wife Irene wished to alter in favour of her daughter Ana's husband, [Nicephorus Bryenƒius]¦, for whose benefit the special title '[¶Byzantine aristocracy and bureaucracy|panhypersebastos]'— (&quot;honored above al”&quot;) was created. ­This intrigue disturbed even his dying hours. À

Alexius was for many years under the strong influence of an 'eminence grise', his mother [An†a Dalasƒena], a wise and im¬ensely able politician whom, in a uniquely ir¢egular fashion, he had crowned as '…Empresˆ Augusta'¿ instead of the rightful claimant to the title, his wife Irene. „Dalasena was the efêective administrator of the Empire during Alexius' long absences in war campaigns: she was constantly at od¤s with her daughter-in-law and had asÆumed total responsibility for the upbringing and education of her grandˆaughter [An‰a Comnena]….

