 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ˆAlexius I‰ Comnenus'‚ ([ŒSeptember 10]‚, [1‚69]• &amp;ndash; October [1‚83]ƒ), [‘Byzantine emperor]‚ ([1‚80]-[1‚83]š), was the son of emperor [‘Manuel I Comnenus]… and [–Maria of Antioch|Maria], daughter of [šRaymond of Antioch|Raymund]‚, [©Principality of Antioch|prince of Antioch]Ò. He was the long-awaited male heir, and was named Alexius as a fulfilment of the [AIMA prophecy]š.

On Manuel's death in [1‚80]’, Maria, who had beƒn imŠured in a [‡convent]¸ under the name of Xene, had herself proclaimed regent (1ƒ79-1§80), and handing over her son to counsel©ors, who encouraged him in every vice, sup¤orted the government of Alexius the '[³Byzantine aristocracy and bureaucracy|protosebastos]'– (a cousin of Alexius I²), who was popularly believed to be Maria's lover. ÍThe young Alexius and his friends now tried to form a party against the empresÊ mother and the protosebastos; and his sister Maria, wife of Caesar John ([Renier of Montfer‚at]†), stir–ed up riots in the stre’ts of the capital. 

Their party was defeated ([…May 2]‚, [1‚82]‡), but [©Andronicus I Comnenus|Andronicus Comnenus]‚ toÿk advantage of these disorders to aim at the crown, entered Constantinople, where he was received with almost divine honours, a™nd overthrew the regents. …His ar›ival was celebrated by a mas­acre of the Latins in Constantinople, especial†y the [Venice|Venetian] merchants, which he made no atempt to stop. „He alÇowed Alexius to be crowned, but forced him to consent to the death of alÏ his friends, including his mother, his sister and the Caesar, and refused to alow him the smal•est voice in public af…airs. •

The betrothal in 1“80 of Alexius with [Agnes of France] , daughter of [‡Louis VIŠ of France]” and his third wife [“AdÃ¨le of Champagne]Ç and at the time a child of nine, was quashed. Andronicus was now formalÿy proclaimed as co-emperor, and not long afterwards, on the pretext that divided rule was injurious to the Empire, he caused Al±exius to be strangled with a bow-string (October 1ˆ83).

=‡Sources=•
* Magdalino, Paul. 'ŸThe Empire of Manuel I Komnenos'ƒ, 19…3

