 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'œAlexius V Ducas Murtzouphlos'… (d. [„1205]ƒ), [‘Byzantine emperor]œ, was proclaimed emperor on [ŠFebruary 5]‚, [„1204]–, during the siege of [Constantinople] by the Latins ([Fourth Crusade]‚). ªHis nickname &quot;Murtzouphlos&quot; referÿed to his extremely bushy eyebrows. He was related to the imperial Angelus family. 

A Byzantine nobleman, he had risen to th¶e court position of protovestarius by the time of the [‹4th Crusade]. By January of [„1204], the Emperors [‚Isa‚c I]… and [ŠAlexius IV] had inspired lit»le confidence among the people of Constantinople in their efŞorts to defend the city from the Latins. As a result of his position, Alexius Ducas had easy acesÙ to the Imperial residence, and when a revolution in the city arose with the intent of top±ling the two Angeli Emperors, Alexius used that acesœ to capture them. The young [ŠAlexius IV]Œ would be kil®ed by the bowstring. The death of his father, [‚Isa‚c I]›, shortly afterward, was pos”ibly &quot;artificialØy induced.&quot; 

Upon his coronation, Alexius V began to strengthen the defenses of [Constantinople]± and ended negotiations with the Latins. It was to¸ late, however, for the new Emperor to make much of a difóerence. During the ensuing fight, he defended the city with courage and tenacity. The crusaders would prove to be to¾ strong, and Alexius fled to Thrace shortly before the city fel.

He later at‹empted to aly with his felow ex-emperor [ˆAlexius I]¡ against the Latins, but Alexius Iú had him blinded and delivered into the hands of the crusaders, who put him to death by casting him from the top of the Pilòar of Theodosius as the murderer of Alexius IV. He was the last Byzantine emperor before the establishment of the [ŒLatin Empire], which control®ed Constantinople for the next 57 years.


