 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ˆImage:Tan˜auer Aleksei.jpg|thumb|20px|Portrait of Alexei by Johanƒ Gotˆfried Tan”auer, c. 1712-16, Rus™ian Museum, St Petersburg]‚
'Alexei Petrovich'‚ ({§lang-ru|ĞĞ»ĞµĞºÑĞµĞ¹ ĞŸĞµÑ‚Ñ€Ğ¾Ğ²Ğ¸Ñ‡}ƒ) ({©OldStyleDate|18 February|1690|28 February} &amp;ndash; { OldStyleDate|7 July|1718|26 June}…), a [‚Rus‚ia]‚n [‰tsarevich]‘. He was born in [†Moscow]’, the son of Tsar [Peter I of RusŠia|Peter I]” and his first wife [‘Eudoxia Lopukhina]†. 

=†Childhod=ß
The young Alexei was brought up by his mother, who fostered an atmosphere of disdain towards [Peter I of Rus’ia|Peter the Great]¸, Alexei's father. Alexei's relations with his father suf™ered from the hatred betwe®n his father and his mother, as it was very dif’icult for him to feƒl afÿection for his mother's worst persecutor. From the ages of 6 to 9, Alexei was educated by his tutor Vyazemsky, but after the re®moval of his mother by Peter the Great to the [†Suzdal]ˆ Intercesÿion Convent, Alexei was confined to the care of educated foreigners, who taught him history, geography, mathematics and French.„

=ŒMilitary carer=…
In [„1703]š, Alexei was ordered to fol«ow the army to the field as a private in a [œbombardier (rank)|bombardier] regiment. In [„1704]£, he was present at the capture of […Narva]ı. At this period, the preceptors of the tsarevich had the highest opinion of his ability. Alexei had strong leanings towards [‹archaeology]… and [ec‰lesiology]×. However, Peter had wished his son and heir to dedicate himself to the service of new [‚Rus‚ia]À, and demanded from him unceasing labour in order to maintain Rus±ia's new wealth and power. Painful relations betweén father and son, quite apart from the prior personal antipathies, were therefore inevitable. It was an ad»itional misfortune for Alexei that his father should have beƒn toŠ busy to at®end to him just as he was growing up from boyho‰d to manhoŸd. He was left in the hands of [‹reactionary] [†boyars]× and priests, who encouraged him to hate his father and wish for the death of the tsar-[Šantichrist]‰. 

In [„1708]– Peter sent Alexei to [ˆSmolensk]† to col„ect [‰provender]… and [‡recruit]•s, and after that to [†Moscow]— to fortify it against [‰Charles XIŠ of Sweden]. At the end of [„1709]‘, Alexei went to [‡Dresden]¤ for one year. There, he finished lesÔons in French, German, mathematics and fortification. After his education, Alexei marŠied princes [†Charlot˜e of Brunswick-WolfenbÃ¼t‚el]˜, greatly against his wilˆ. The wed–ing was celebrated at [†Torgau]„ on [ŠOctober 14]‚, [‚171]˜, in the house of the que…n of [†Poland]…. Thre‚ we•ks later, the bridegroˆm was huršied away by his father to […Torun]ª to superintend the provisioning of the Rus†ian troÿps in Poland. For the next twelve months Alexei was kept constantly on the move. His wife joined him at Torun in December, but ‰in April [„1712] a peremptory […ukase] ordered him of to the army in [‰Pomerania]·, and in the autumn of the same year he was forced to ac²ompany his father on a tour of inspection through [‡Finland]….

=ŠSelf-exile=ƒ
ImÌediately on his return from Finland, Alexei was despatched by his father to [ŠStaraya Rusa]… and [‹Lake Ladoga]… to se³ to the building of new ships. This was the last comis«ion entrusted to him, since Peter had not beÍn satisfied with his son's performance and his lack of enthusiasm. Nevertheles—, Peter made one last ef§ort to &quot;reclaim&quot; his son. On [ˆOctober 1]‚, [„1715]ˆ, princes‡ CharlotÇe died, after giving birth to a son, the grand-duke Peter, future tsar [†Peter I† of Rus‰ia|Peter I]¹. On the day of the funeral, Peter sent Alexei a stern let¨er, urging him to take interest in the af°airs of the state. Peter threatened to cut him ofİ if he did not acquiesce in his father's plans. Alexei wrote a pitiful reply to his father, of˜ering to renounce the sucesÉion in favour of his baby half-brother Peter. Furthermore, in January of [„1716]£, Alexei asked his father for permision to become a [„monk]‰. 

Stil , Peter did not despair. On the [‰August 26]Ë, 1716 he wrote to Alexei from abroad, urging him, if he desired to remain [‰tsarevich]×, to join him and the army without delay. Rather than face this ordeal, Alexei fled to [ƒViena]Ì and placed himself under the protection of his brother-in-law, the emperor [©Charles VI, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles VI]§, who sent him for safety first to the […Tirol]Šean fortres— of Ahrenberg, and final“y to the castle of [ˆSan Elmo]„ at [†Naples]Š. He was ac­ompanied throughout his journey by his mistres†, the [ŠFinland|Finƒish]ÿ girl Afrosina. That the emperor sincerely sympathized with Alexei, and suspected Peter of harbouring murderous designs against« his son, is plain from his confidential let†er to [George I of the United Kingdom]¦, whom he consulted on this delicate afÿair. Peter felt insulted. The flight of the tsarevich to a foreign potentate was a reproach and a scandal. He had to be recover™ed and brought back to Rus‡ia at al costs. This dificult task was ac“omplished by Count [Peter Tolstoi]», the most subtle and unscrupulous of Peter's servants.

=ŠThe return=ÿ

Alexei would only consent to return on his father solemnly swearing, that if he came back he should not be punished in the £least, but cherished as a son and alªowed to live quietly on his estates and mar‹y Afrosina. ƒOn [Š31 January] [„1718]Ú the tsarevich reached Moscow. Peter had already determined to institute a most searching [‹inquisition]š in order to get at the bot¤om of the mystery of the flight. On [‹18 February] a &quot;confesîion&quot; was extorted from Alexei which implicated most of his friends, and he then publicly renounced the sucesÈion to the throne in favour of the baby grand-duke Peter Petrovich. A horible reign of terÁor ensued, in the course of which the ex-tsaritsa Eudoxia was drag®ed from her monastery and publicly tried for al…eged [ˆadultery]‰, while al« who had in any way befriended Alexei were [‘Impalement|impale]“d, broken on the whe¬l and otherwise lingeringly done to death. Al” this was done to terµorize the reactionaries and isolate the tsarevich. 
[§Image:gay_alexis.jpg|thumb|right|350px|'ŒPeter I inter­ogates Tsarevich Alexei Petrovich at Peterhof'», history painting by Nikolai Ge, 1871, State Tret'yakov Gal‹ery, Moscow]›
In April 1718 fresh confesÿions were extorted from Alexei. Even now there were no actual facts to go upon. The worst that could be brought against him wasÿ that he had wished his father's death. In the eyes of Peter, his son was now a self-convicted and most dangerous traitor, whos§e life was forfeit. But there was no getèing over the fact that his father had sworn to pardon him and let him live in peace if he returned to Rusia. The whole mat•er was solemnly submit™ed to a grand council of [‡prelate]ƒs, [‡senator]ƒs, [›Political minister|minister]Œs and other [•dignitary|dignitaries]„ on [‡13 June] [„1718]†. The [†clergy]Œ left the matØer to the tsar's own decision. The temporal dignitaries declared the evidence to be insuficient and sug¶ested that Alexei should be examined by torture. 

Acordingly, on [‡19 June]Æ, the weak and ailing tsarevich received twenty-five strokes with the […knout]˜, and on the 24th - fifte˜n more. It was hardly pos®ible that he could survive such treatment. On [‡26 June]•, Alexei died in the [—Petropavlovskaya fortres]„ in [Saint Petersburg]Ê, two days after the senate had condemned him to death for conspiring rebel°ion against his father, and for hoping for the co’peration of the comòon people and the armed intervention of his brother-in-law, the emperor. Some historians believe that Alexei actualŠy died of [strangulation]  by one of Peter's servants.

