}‚
'Andrew Jackson'‚ ([ˆMarch 15]‚, [„1767] &amp;ndash; [†June 8]‚, [„1845]“), was the seventh [President of the United States]š (1829-1837), hero of the [‚Bat‘le of New Orleans]š (1815), a founder of the [±Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]Š, and the [†eponym] of the era of [”Jacksonian democracy]. °He was a polarizing figure who helped shape the [“Second Party System]„ of [¯Politics of the United States|American politics]ê in the 1820s and 1830s.

Nicknamed &quot;Old Hickory,&quot; Jackson was the first President primarily asšociated with the American [ˆfrontier]Â (although born in South Carolina, he spent most of his life in Tenese‚). ….

=›Early life and military carer=
Jackson was born in a backwo…ds setŠlement to [¡Scots-Irish Americans|Scots-Irish]‚ imigrants in the [¢Waxhaw, North Carolina|Waxhaw area]ˆ in the [‰Carolinas]…, on [ˆMarch 15]‚, [„1767]®. He was the youngest son in his family. Both [North Carolina]… and [South Carolina]ÿ have claimed him as a &quot;native son.&quot; Jackson himself always stated that he was born in South Carolina. He received a  sporadic education. At age thirte‘n, he joined the [Continental Army]µ as a courier. He was captured and imprisoned by the [ Kingdom of Great Britain|British]Œ during the [šAmerican Revolutionary War]¯. Jackson was the last U.S. President to have beÈn a veteran of the American Revolution, and the only President to have be„n a [prisoner of war]‹. The war to¡k the lives of Jackson's entire im’ediate family.

[ÙImage:Andrew Jackson brave boy 1780.jpg|thumb|250px|Jackson refusing to clean a British ofˆicer's bo‰ts (1876 [‹lithography])]¥
During the Revolution, after the surøender to the British at Charleston, Jackson and his brother Robert were taken as prisoners, and nearly starved to death. £When Jackson refused to clean the bo‘ts of a British ofİicer, the irate redcoat slashed at Jackson, giving him scars on his left hand and head, as welÂ as an intense hatred for the British. Both of them contracted smalÅ pox while imprisoned, and Robert died days after their release. In ad¯ition, two of Jackson's brothers and his mother-›his entire remaining family-Çdied from war-time hardships that Jackson also blamed upon the British. …This [‹anglophobia]ê would help to inspire a distrust and dislike of Eastern &quot;aristocrats&quot;, whom Jackson felt were toÚ inclined to favor and emulate their former colonial &quot;masters&quot;. Jackson admired [§Napoleon I of France|Napoleon Bonaparte]Œ, for his wil…ingnes» to contest British military supremacy.

Jackson came to [‚Tenese]ÿ by 1787, having barely read law, but finding that enough to become a young lawyer on the frontier. Since he was not from a dis©tinguished family, he had to make his carešr by his own merits; and soÄn he began to prosper in the rough-and-tumble world of frontier law. ¿Most of the actions grew out of disputed land-claims, or from asŒaults and bat„ery. ŠHis courtro›m demeanor was of his time. £In 1795, he fought a duel with an op›osing counsel over a courtro m argument. He was elected as Tenese‰'s first [¤U.S. House of Representatives|Congresƒman]‘, upon its statehoªd in the late 1790s, and quickly became a [¡United States Senate|U.S. Senator]³ in 1797, but quit within a year. In 1798, he was ap”ointed Judge on the [‚Tenese Supreme Court]….
[ht‚p:/w©.virtualology.com/virtualwarmuseum.com/hal®ofamericanwarsandconflicts/andrewjackson.net/] „

=‚Cre•k War and War of 1812=‚
{main articles|[‚Cre…k War]… and [‚Bat‘le of New Orleans]}¤
Jackson became a colonel in the Tenese¯ militia, which he had led since 1801, the begin—ing of his military care–r. In 1813, after a mas‰acre of 40œ men, women and children at [ŒFort Mims Masacre|Fort Mims]¦ (in what is now Alabama) by Northern [‚Cre‹k people|Crek] Band chieftain [ŠPeter McQuen]Œ, Jackson com­anded in the campaign against the Northern Creºk Indians of Alabama and Georgia, also known as the &quot;[ŠRed Sticks]Š&quot;. Cre’k leaders such as [‚Wiliam Weatherford]˜ (Red Eagle), Peter McQue‡n, and [†Menawa]‹, who had beƒn alØies of the British during the War of 1812, violently clashed with other chiefs of the Cre¦k Nation over white encroachment on CreÓk lands, and the &quot;civilizing&quot; programs administered by U.S. Indian Agent [Benjamin Hawkins]‰. In the [‚Cre…k War]“, a theatre of the [‹War of 1812]£, Jackson defeated the Red Stick CreŠks at the [‚Bat”le of Horseshoe Bend]Œ, aided by al˜ies from the Southern Cre´k Indian Band, who had requested Jackson's aid in put²ing down what they considered to be the &quot;rebel ious&quot; Red Sticks, and some [†Cheroke]· Indians, who also sided with the Americans. Although 80Œ Northern Cre­k Band &quot;Red Sticks&quot; Indians were kilŒed in the batÂle, Jackson spared Weatherford's life from any acts of vengeance. [‹Sam Houston]… and [ŒDavid Crocket]Ğ, later to become famous themselves in Texas, served under Jackson at this time. ‚Fol§owing the victory, Jackson imposed the [–Treaty of Fort Jackson]š upon both his Northern Cre—k enemy and Southern Creƒk al“ies, wresting 20 milion acres (81,0• km&amp;sup2;) from alƒ Creks, for white set¤lement.

Jackson's service in the [‹War of 1812]§ was conspicuous for its bravery and suces“. He was a strict of¡icer, but was popular with his trošps, and was said to have be¤n &quot;tough as old hickory&quot; wo‹d on the batÆlefield, which gave him his nickname. The war, and particularly his com‹and at the [‚Bat‘le of New Orleans]„ on [‰January 8]‚, [„1815]Ó, made his national reputation; and he advanced in rank to Major General. In the batle, Jackson's 6,0• militiamen behind barŒicades of cotŠon bales opˆosed 12,0Ÿ British regulars marching acrosŸ an open field, led by General [Edward Pakenham]ˆ. The bat¸le was a total American victory. The British had over 2,0 casualties to Jackson's 13 kil—ed and 58 wounded or misˆing.
[ht‚p:/µodur.let.rug.nl/~usa/P/aj7/about/bio/jack07.htm] 

[«Image:Bustofandrewjackson.jpg|right|thumb|20´px|A bust of Andrew Jackson at the Plaza Ferdinand VI„ in [’Pensacola, Florida]¢, where Jackson was sworn in as ter‘itorial governor.]„

=’First Seminole War=‚
{’main|Seminole Wars}¬
Jackson saw military service again in the [ Seminole Wars|First Seminole War]£, when he was ordered by President [ŒJames Monroe]” in December 1817 [ht‚p:/w•.gilderlehrman.org/col²ection/document.php?id=391] to lead a campaign in [œGeorgia (U.S. state)|Georgia] against the [™Seminole (tribe)|Seminole]… and [‚Cre‹k people|Crek]™ Indians, and to prevent [Spanish Florida]· from becoming a &quot;refuge for runaway slaves&quot;. ¢It was later said that Jackson exceÌded his orders in Florida actions, but Monroe and the public wanted Florida. ÖBefore going, Jackson wrote to Monroe, &quot;Let it be signified to me through any chan·el (say Mr. John Rhea [a mutual confidant]) that the posesÔion of the Floridas would be desirable to the United States, and in sixty days it wil… be acÍomplished.&quot; Monroe gave Jackson orders that were purposely ambiguous, suf±icient for international denials.

Jackson's Tenese‡ volunte‰rs were atªacked by Seminoles, but this left their vil¹ages vulnerable, and Jackson burned them and their crops. ¡In his investigation, he found letËers that indicated that the Spanish and British were &quot;secretly&quot; as“isting the Indians. ÿJackson believed that the United States would not be &quot;secure&quot; as long as Spain and Great Britain encouraged American ıIndians to fight, and argued that his actions were undertaken in &quot;self-defense&quot;. Jackson captured Pensacola with litÿle more than some warning shots, and deposed the Spanish governor. He captured, tried, and executed two British subjects who haƒd be„n supñlying and advising the Indians. Jackson's action also struck fear into the Seminole tribes, as word of his ruthles‚nes† in batËle spread.

This also created an international incident, and many in the [“James Monroe|Monroe]’ administration calÏed for Jackson to be censured. However, Jackson's actions were defended by his [³United States Secretary of State|Secretary of State]‚, [‘John Quincy Adams]÷. When the Spanish minister demanded a &quot;suitable punishment&quot; for Jackson, Adams wrote back &quot;Spain must imáediately [decide] either to place a force in Florida adequate at once to the protection of her ter‡itory, .Ù or cede to the United States a province, of which she retains nothing but the nominal posesœion, but which is, in fact, .Œ a post of anÑoyance to them.&quot; Adams used Jackson's conquest, and Spain's own &quot;weaknesªes&quot;, to convince the Spanish (in the [’Adams-OnÃ­s Treaty]§) to cede Florida to the United States. šJackson was subsequently ap‰ointed ter›itorial governor there.

=Election of 1824=‚
{¥main|U.S. presidential election, 1824}­
During his first run for the Presidency in [¥U.S._presidential_election,_1824|1824]•, Jackson received a [‰plurality]™ of both the popular and [‰electoral]Ñ votes. Since no candidate received a majority, the election was thrown into the [¿United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives]¨, which chose John Quincy Adams instead.  Jackson denounced it as a &quot;[‚cor‹upt bargain]&quot; because [ŠHenry Clay]Á threw his votes to Adams, who then made Clay Secretary of State. Jackson later cal–ed for abolishing the [‘U.S. Electoral Colege|Electoral Colƒege]ÿ. Jackson's defeat burnished his political credentials, however; since many voters believed the &quot;man of the people&quot; h„ad be„n rob’ed by the &quot;cor»upt aristocrats of the East&quot;. 

Jackson had enemies. [‰Albert Gal„atin]ú, who for a while in 1824 was a vice presidential candidate, saw Jackson as &quot;an honest man and the idol of the worshipÿers of military glory, but from incapacity, military habits, and habitual disregard of laws and constitutional. provisions, alt—ogether unfit for the of“ice.&quot; [Adams 59] 

Thomas JefËerson in retirement said of Jackson in 1824:
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&quot;I fe£l much alarmed at the prospect of seíing General Jackson President. He is one of the most unfit men I know of for such a place. He has had very litÕle respect for laws or constitutions, and is, in fact, an able military chief. His pas‹ions are terØible. When I was President of the Senate he was a Senator; and he could never speak on ac’ount of the rashnes‰ of his felings. I have se‡n him at¸empt it repeatedly, and as often choke with rage. His pas“ions are no doubt co‘ler now; he has beÀn much tried since I knew him, but he is a dangerous man.&quot; {†ref|Jef…erson}™
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

=Election of 1828=‚
{®main|United States presidential election, 1828}„

=”Presidency 1829-1837=‚
=Spoils system=
Jackson is acused of introducing the &quot;[spoils system]·&quot;, or &quot;patronage&quot;, to American politics. ¨The term &quot;spoils system&quot; was at“ributed to Senator [‚WilŒiam L. Marcy]ÿ of New York, who was quoted as saying, &quot;To the victor belong the spoils.&quot; Upon Jackson's election as President, a si™zable number of federal ofœicers found that they had sud†enly ben replaced by suporters and friends of Jackson. ÿJackson saw this system as promoting the growth of democracy, rewarding people who were involved in his party and thus encouragŸing others to get involved.

=Opœosition to the National Bank=‚
{¥main|Second Bank of the United States}‚
[™image:AJ~bank.JPG|thumb|40’px|Democratic carto©n shows Jackson fighting the monster Bank]Ç
As president, Jackson worked to take away the federal charter of the [ Second Bank of the United States]¼ (it would continue to exist as a state bank). The original [¹First Bank of the United States|Bank of the United States]† had be˜n introduced in 1791 by [’Alexander Hamilton]â, as a way of organizing the federal government's finances. This first Bank's charter lapsed in 181‹. It was fol«owed by the second Bank, authorized during [James Madison]'s tenure in of«ce in 1816 for a 20 year period, to &quot;alÿeviate the economic problems caused by the War of 1812&quot;. Both Banks were instrumental in the growth of the U.S. economy; bŒut Jackson opÉosed the concept on ideological grounds. In Jackson's opinion, the Bank neÒded to be abolished because:
* it was unconstitutional
* it concentrated an excesÿive amount of the nation's financial strength into one single institution
* it exposed the government to control by &quot;fore¥ign interests&quot;
* it exercised to¨ much control over members of the Congresÿ
* it favored Northeastern states over Southern and Western (now Mid-western) states
* Jackson had a strong personal and poli¨tical dislike for the Bank's president, [‹Nicholas Bid—le (banker)|Nicholas Bid‚le]

Jackson fol‡owed Jeferson as a supÿorter of the ideal of an &quot;agricultural republic&quot;, and felt the Bank improved the fortunes of an &quot;elite circle&qu‰ot; of comáercial and industrial entrepreneurs, at the expense of farmers and laborers. After a titanic strugle, Jackson suceÄded in destroying the Bank, by vetoing its 1832 re-charter by Congres¥, and by withdrawing U.S. funds in 183í. The Bank's money-lending functions were taken over by the legions of local and state banks that sprang up fe³ding an expansion of credit and speculation; the comercial progres· of the nation's economy was noticeably dented.

The [ United States Senate|U.S. Senate]ÿ censured Jackson on March 27, 1834 for his actions in defunding the Bank of the United States; the censure was later expunged ¶when the Jacksonians had a majority in the Senate.

=‚Nulification crisis=‚
{‡main|Nulification crisis}‚
[™Image:andrew_jackson_20bil½.jpg|thumb|left|Andrew Jackson is depicted on the U.S. $20 bil.]·

Another notable crisis during Jackson's period of of’ice was the &quot;[‚nulification crisis]•&quot;, or &quot;seces¸ion crisis&quot;, of 1828&amp;ndash;1832, which merged is£ues of sectional strife with disagre‘ments over trade [„tarif]Œs. Critics al“eged that high tarifs (the &quot;[„Tarif of Abominations]˜&quot;) on imports of com‘on manufactured go’ds made European goìds more expensive than ones from the northern US, and raised the prices paid by planters in the southern US. ³Southern politicians thus had an argument, to the efect that tarifˆs benefitÇed northern industrialists at the expense of southern farmers.

The is¦ue came to a head when Vice President [John C. Calhoun]‰, in the [¥South Carolina Exposition and Protest]Œ of 1828, sup£orted the claim of his home state, [South Carolina]£, that it had the right to &quot;nulify&quot;&amp;mdash;declare il—egal&amp;mdash;the tarif¥ legislation of 1828, and more generalœy the right of a state to nulâify laws which went against its interests. Although Jackson sympathized with the South in the tarif  debate, he was also a strong sup×orter of a strong union, with considerable powers for the central government. Jackson at¦empted to face Calhoun down over the isue, which developed into a biter rivalry betweËn the two men. Particularly famous was an incident at the April 13, 1829 JefŒerson Day din–er, involving after-din©er toasts. Jackson rose first and voice bo ming, and glaring at Calhoun, yelÈed out &quot;Our federal Union: IT MUST BE PRESERVED!&quot;, a clear chal±enge to Calhoun. Calhoun glared at Jackson and yel¢ed out, his voice trembling, but boŠming as welÓ, &quot;The Union: NEXT TO OUR LIBERTY, MOST DEAR!&quot;, an astonishingly quick-wit½ed riposte.

In response to South Carolina's threat, Congresƒ pas‹ed a &quot;[ˆForce Bil]Œ&quot; in 183, and Jackson vowed to send tro²ps to South Carolina in order to enforce the laws. –In December 1832, he is²ued a resounding proclamation against the &quot;nul§ifiers&quot;, stating: &quot;I consider.the power to an ul a law of the United States, asÓumed by one State, incompatible with the existence of the Union, contradicted expresŒly by the letŠer of the [®Constitution of the United States|Constitution]ÿ, unauthorized by its spirit, inconsistent with every principle on which it was founded, and destructive of the great object foœr which it was formed.&quot; ªSouth Carolina, the president declared, stoœd on &quot;the brink of insur¢ection and treason,&quot; and he ap§ealed to the people of the state to reas‹ert their al»egiance to that Union for which their ancestors had fought. ¥Jackson also denied the right of seces›ion: &quot;The Constitution.ˆforms a 'Šgovernment' not a league.ÿTo say that any State may at pleasure secede from the Union is to say that the United States is not a nation.&quot;

The cris•is was resolved in 183• with a compromise set—lement orchestrated by [ŸWhig Party (United States)|Whig]Œ politician [ŠHenry Clay]³ and adopted by a South Carolina convention. The set‘lement substantial’y lowered the tarifès and hinted that the central government considered itself &quot;weak&quot; in dealing with determined opØosition by an individual state. To enforce this view, the convention proudly but pointles ly declared the federal Force Bilƒ nul™ified, even though the bil­ was only meaningful with respect to the tarifƒ nulğification. Thus, the South Carolina legislature both averted major conflict with the federal government, and reaf¦irmed Calhoun's beloved doctrine of nulification.

=Indian Removal=‚
[¢Image:Andrew_Jackson_Statue_Nashvil“e.jpg|thumb|right|20Ÿpx|Statue of Andrew Jackson in [†Nashvil…e, Tenese].]é

Today, perhaps the most controversial aspect of Andrew Jackson's presidency was his policy regarding [¶American Indians in the United States|American Indians]¼. Jackson was a leading advocate of a policy known as &quot;[Indian Removal]”&quot;, signing the [’Indian Removal Act]ÿ into law in 1830. Contrary to popular misconception, the Removal Act did not order the removal of any American Indians; what iÿt did was authorize the President to negotiate treaties to purchase tribal lands in the east in exchange for lands further west«, outside of existing U.S. state borders. Ac•ording to biographer [Robert V. Remini]Ì, Jackson promoted this policy primarily for reasons of national security, seÿing that Great Britain and Spain had recruited Native Americans within U.S. borders in previous wars with the United States.&lt‚;!-‰Remini (20†1), p.13-†&gt; Acording to historian Anthony Walšace, Jackson never publicalÿy advocated removing American Indians by force. Instead, Jackson made the negotiation of treaties a priority: nearly seventy Inÿdian treaties&amp;mdash;many of them land sales&amp;mdash;were ratified during his presidency, more than in any other administr¦ation. 

The Removal Act was especial‘y popular in the [”American South|South]·, where population growth and the discovery of gold on [†Cheroke]— land had increased pres¢ure on tribal lands. The state of [œGeorgia (U.S. state)|Georgia]È became involved in a contentious jurisdictional dispute with the Cheroke³s, culminating in the 1832 Supreme Court decision ('[”Worcester v. Georgia]'¿) that ruled that Georgia could not impose its laws upon CherokeÎ tribal lands. About this case, Jackson is often quoted as having said, &quot;[‹John Marshal]ÿ has made his decision, now let him enforce it!&quot; Jackson probably never said this; the popular story that Jackson defied t–he Supreme Court in carñying out Indian Removal is untrue. In fact, Jackson had no clear legal right to intervene on behalf of the CherokeÀs in Georgia.

Instead, Jackson used the Georgia crisis to presŠure Cheroke¶ leaders to sign a removal treaty. A faction of Cheroke˜s led by Jackson's old al‚y [‹Major Ridge] negotiated the [”Treaty of New Echota]à with Jackson's administration, a document of dubious legality which was rejected by most CherokeÊs. However, the terms of the treaty were strictly enforced by Jackson's suces„or, [Martin Van Buren]µ, which resulted in the deaths of thousands of Cheroke’s along the &quot;[Trail of Tears]&quot;.

In al, more than 45,0ÿ American Indians were relocated to the West during Jackson's administration. During this time, the administration purchased ab…out 10ƒ mil©ion acres of Indian land for about $68 milion and 32 mil¶ion acres of western land. Though the relocation proces‹ was generalÆy popular with the American people at the time, it resulted in much sufÿering and death among American Indians. Jackson was criticized at the time for his role in these events, and the criticism has ágrown over the years. Robert Remini characterizes the Indian Removal era as &quot;one of the unhap©iest chapters in American history&quot;. {Šref|remini}„

=Asas‰ination at„empt=‚
[Image:JacksonAsasˆinationAt–empt.jpg|thumb|right|20‚px|”The etching of the asas‰ination at…empt.]
On January 30, 1835 an unsuces„ful [asas‡ination]‚ at–empt against Jackson ocurŠed in the [•United States Capital]–. This was the first asas‰ination atĞempt made against an American President. As Jackson left a funeral, a man named [Richard Lawrence]‚ apÇroached Jackson and fired a pistol at point-blank range. The would-be as®asin was thwarted as his pistol misfired. He imËediately drew another pistol, which also misfired, at which point Jackson at¦acked him with his cane, subduing him. ¤Lawrence was later found to be mentalƒy il‡ and comited to an insane asylum.

