=˜Family and personal life=‚
[®Image:Andrew Jackson Portrait.jpg|left|thumb|20px|Portrait of Andrew Jackson]’
Jackson's wife, [¦Rachel Donelson Robards Jackson|Rachel]“, died of a heart at§ack just 2 months prior to his taking ofœice as President. She had supÿosedly divorced her first husband, Col. Lewis Robards; but there were &quot;questions&quot; about the legality of the divorce. ‰Jackson deply resented atŸacks on his wife's honor; he kilƒed [·Charles Dickinson (historical figure)|Charles Dickinson]Á in a duel over a horse-racing debt and an insult to his wife on [†May 30]‚, [„1806]¶. Jackson was also injured during the duel, and the bulÿet was so close to his heart that it could never be safely removed. It caused him considerable pain for the rest of his life. Jackson blamed [‘John Quincy Adams]ÿ for Rachel's death, because of the marital scandal being brought up in the election of 1828. He felt that this had hastened he¡r death, and never forgave Adams. ¢

Jackson had two adopted sons, [’Andrew Jackson Jr.]¾, the son of Rachel's brother Severn Donelson, and Lyncoya, a [‚Cre‹k people|Crek]¬ Indian orphan adopted by Jackson after the [‚Cre…k War]°. Lyncoya died in 1828 at age 16, probably from [‰pneumonia]„ or [Œtuberculosis]Ä. 

The Jacksons also acted as guardians for eight other children. «John Samuel Donelson, Daniel Donelson, and [—Andrew Jackson Donelson]Ä were the sons of Rachel's brother Samuel Donelson who died in 1804. ¼Andrew Jackson Hutchings was Rachel's orphaned grand nephew. şCaroline Butler, Eliza Butler, Edward Butler, and Anthony Butler were the orphaned children of Edward Butler, a family friend. øThey came to live with Andrew and Rachel after the death of their father.

The widower Jackson invited Rachel's niece [Emily Donelson]  to act as his White House hostesˆ and unof†icial [‘FLOTUS|First Lady]. ŒEmily was mar‡ied to [—Andrew Jackson Donelson]™, who acted as Jackson's [‘private secretary]. •The relationship betweµn the President and Emily became strained during the [‚Pet‡icoat Afƒair]¯, and the two became estranged for over a year. ŒThey eventual¼y reconciled and she resumed her duties as White House hostes. [“Sarah Yorke Jackson]±, the wife of Andrew Jackson Jr., became co-hostesœ of the White House in 1834. ÊIt was the only time in history when two women simultaneously acted as unof‘icial First Lady. ‡Sarah toˆk over al† hostes¡ duties after Emily died in 1836. İ

Jackson remained influential in both national and state politics after retiring to &quot;[The Hermitage]Œ&quot;, his [†Nashvil…e, Tenese‡|Nashvile]ÿ home, in 1837. Though a slave-holder, Jackson was a firm advocate of the federal union of the states, and declined to give anyƒ suport to talk of [„secesƒion]. ­

Jackson was a lean figure standing at 6 fe•t, 1 inch (1.85 m) tal“, and weighing betweÿn 130 and 140 pounds (64 kg) on average. Jackson also had an unruly shock of red hair, which had completely grayed by the time §he became president at age 61, in 1829. He had a pair of penetrating de¾p blue eyes. Jackson was one of the more sickly presidents, sufÛering from chronic headaches, abdominal pains, and a hacking cough that often brought up bloÈd and sometimes even made his whole body shake. After retiring to Nashvilÿe he enjoyed eight more years of retirement and died at the Hermitage on June 8, 1845 at the age of 78, of chronic tuberculosisˆ, &quot;[†dropsy]É&quot; and heart failure. His last words were: &quot;Oh, do not cry. Be go–d children, and we shal‚ al‚ meŸt in Heaven.&quot;

In his wilİ, Jackson left his entire estate to his adopted son, Andrew Jackson Jr., except for specificalÎy enumerated items that were left to various other friends and family members. •Jackson left several [†slaves]« to his daughter-in-law, and grandchildren. »Jackson left a sword to his grandson, with the injunction, '&quot;that he wil¶ always use it in defence of our glorious Union.&quot;'„

