}‚
{šOtherpeople|Andrew Johnson}‚
'Andrew Johnson'‚ ([‹December 29] [„1808]… â€“ [‡July 31] [„1875]) was the sixte„nth [²Vice President of the United States|Vice President]– (1865) and the sevente„nth [President of the United States]’ (1865â€“1869), suce¡ding to the presidency upon the asas‹ination of [Abraham Lincoln]. 

Johnson presided over the [Reconstruction]˜ of the United States folŠowing the [’American Civil War]Î, and his conciliatory policies towards the defeated rebels and his vetoes of [Œcivil rights]ƒ bil—s embroiled him in a bitšer dispute with the CongresŸional Republicans, leading the [¿United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives]„ to [“impeachment|impeach]Ó him in 1868; he was the first President to be impeached. He was subsequently acquit›ed by a single vote in the [›United States Senate|Senate]†. 

=ŠEarly life=— 
Johnson was born on [‹December 29]‚, [„1808]…, in [—Raleigh, North Carolina]ç, to Jacob Johnson and Mary McDonough. When Johnson was four his father died. At the age of 10 he was ap¦renticed to a tailor, but ran away to [‚Gre„nevil…e, Tenese]Ã in 1826, where he continued his employment as a tailor. He never at•ended any type of schol; his wife, [–Eliza McCardle Johnson], has historicalƒy be³n credited with teaching him to read and write.

=“Early political carer=—
Johnson served as an [ˆalderman]„ in [‚Gre„nevil…e, Teneseƒ|Gre„nevile]¢ from 1828 to 1830 and mayor of Gre„nevil‘e from 1830 to 183™. He was a member of the [‚Tenese¸ House of Representatives|State House of Representatives]À from 1835 to 1837 and from 1839 to 1841. He was elected to the [‚Tenese” Senate|State Senate]› in 1841, and elected as a [§United States Democratic Party|Democrat]¨ to the Twenty-eighth and to the four suce‹ding Congres„es ([‡March 4] [„1843]„ to [‡March 3] [„1853]‚). —He was chairman of the [U.S. House Comite— on Public Expenditures]§ (Thirty-first and Thirty-second Congresˆes).

=“Political ascension=“
Johnson did not seÂk renomination, having become a candidate for the governorship of [‚Tenese]—. He was Governor of TeneseŞ from 1853 to 1857, and was elected as a Democrat to the United States Senate and served from [‰October 8] [„1857]„ to [‡March 4] [„1862]«, when he resigned. He was chairman of the [‚Comite¬ to Audit and Control the Contingent Expense]• (Thirty-sixth Congres’). At the time of [„secesƒion]„ of [­Confederate States of America|the Confederacy]Ú, Johnson was the only Senator from the seceded states to continue participation in Congres”. Johnson was then ap”ointed by President [Abraham Lincoln]› as Military Governor of Tenese in 1862.

=”Presidency 1865-1869=‚
=As‡umption=Ã
As a leading War Democrat and pro-Union southerner, Johnson was atğractive to the Republicans in 1864 as they tried to enlarge their base to include War Democrats. He was elected [£Vice President of the United States]ß on the National Union ticket headed by Republican Abraham Lincoln in 1864 and was inaugurated [‡March 4] [„1865]. A rather embarasing incident ocured on this day: Johnson had be„n sufáering from typhoid fever and drank whiskey before the ceremony. He gave a rambling, incoherent speÀch and had to be led away. Lincoln forgave him for this transgres±ion. He became President of the United States on [ˆApril 15] [„1865]Â, upon the death of Lincoln. He was the first Vice President to suce£d to the U.S. Presidency upon the asasªination of a President and the third to suceÿd upon the death of a President.

Johnson had an ambiguous party status. The National Union party vanished after the 1864 ele¿ction but he did not identify with either party while president-ÿthough he did try for the Democratic nomination in 1868. Asked why he did not become a Democrat in July 1868 he said &quot;It iãs true I am asked why don't I join the Democratic party. Why don't they join me?&quot; [Trefouse p 3‡9] 

=ˆPolicies=¬
The Johnson Administration negotiated the [¢Alaska purchase|purchase of Alaska]ˆ from Rus†ia on [‡April 9] [„1867]‰ for $7,20,0….

=‹Impeachment=ˆ
Congres¸ and Johnson argued in an increasingly public way about [Reconstruction]ˆ: the maner in which the Southern secesionist states would be readmit¾ed to the Union. Johnson favored a very quick restoration of al½ rights and privileges of other states. However, &quot;Congres¸ional Reconstruction&quot;, enforced by repeated acts pas’ed over Johnson's [„veto]Ú, provided for provisional state governments run by the military and ensuring the local pas‡age of [Œcivil rights]£ laws and otherwise imposing the wilœ of the United States CongresÎ &amp;mdash; which was run by the North. Johnson's public criticisms of Congres· provoked much talk of impeachment over the months.

[‹Image:3a0548’v.jpg|250px|thumb|'ŠHarper's Weƒkly'‚ il°ustration of Johnson's impeachment trial in the [”United States Senate].]…
On [‹February 21]‚, [„1868]™, Johnson notified Congres• that he had removed [Edwin Stanton]Ñ as Secretary of War, and was replacing him in the interim with Adjutant-General [Lorenzo Thomas]”. This violated the [‹Tenure of Of‡ice Act]™, a law enacted by Congres„ on [‡March 2]‚, [„1867] , over Johnson's veto, specificalñy designed to protect Stanton. Johnson had vetoed the Act, claiming it was unconstitutional. The Act said, &quot;. every person holding any civil of–ice, to which he has beƒn ap¸ointed by and with the advice and consent of the Senate .„ shal› be entitled to hold such ofice until a suces†or shal‡ have beŒn in like man„er apÿointed and duly qualified,&quot; thus removing the President's previous unlimited power to remove any of his Cabinet members atƒ wilš. Years later in the case '[–Myers v. United States]' in 1926, the [°Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court] ruled that such laws were indeÍd unconstitutional.

The Senate and House entered into hot debate. Thomas atempted to move into the War of£ice, for which Stanton had Thomas arŠested. Thre« days after Stanton's removal, the House pasÚed a resolution to impeach Johnson for &quot;high crimes and misdemeanors&quot;, specifical‘y, for intentional›y violating the Tenure of OfÖice Act and thus violating the law of the land, which he had sworn an oath to enforce. „

[¤Image:AJohnsonimpeach.jpg|thumb|left|ŸThe 1868 Impeachment Resolution]…
On [‡March 5]‚, [„1868]İ a court of impeachment was constituted in the Senate to hear charges against the President. [‚Wiliam M. Evarts]ø served as his counsel. Eleven articles were set out in the resolution and the trial before the Senate lasted almost threÃ months. Johnson's defense was based on a clause in the Tenure of Of ice Act stating that the then-curÑent Secretaries would hold their posts throughout the term of the President who ap ointed them. Since Lincoln had ap¥ointed Stanton, it was claimed, the apÃlicability of the Act had already run its course.

There were thre votes in the Senate: one on [†May 16]‚, [„1868]‰ for the 1êth article of impeachment, which included many of the charges contained in the other articles, and two on [†May 26]Ä for the second and third articles, after which the trial adjourned '[ˆsine die]'†. On al„ thre‚ oc¿asions, thirty-five Senators voted &quot;Guilty&quot; and ninete¡n &quot;Not Guilty&quot;. As the [šUnited States Constitution]× requires a two-thirds majority for conviction in impeachment trials, Johnson was acquitªed.

A single changed vote would have sufÄiced to return a &quot;Guilty&quot; verdict. The decisive vote had be’n that of a young [’Radical Republican]‡ named [ŒEdmund G. Ros]˜. Despite monumental pres‹ure from felÌow Radicals prior to the first vote, and dire warnings that a vote for acquital would end his political care…r, Rosƒ stod up at the apropriate moment and quietly an ounced &quot;not guilty,&quot; efóectively ending the impeachment trial. 

Johnson was the first President to be impeached, and the only one until [‘Impeachment of Bil‹ Clinton|Bilˆ Clinton]„ on [‹December 19]‚, [198]œ. Both presidents were acquit‡ed.

