 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¹Image:Solzhenitsyn.jpg|right|thumb|Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn]… 

' Aleksandr Isayevich Solzhenitsyn'‚ ({Älang-ru|ĞĞ»ĞµĞºÑĞ°ÌĞ½Ğ´Ñ€ Ğ˜ÑĞ°ÌĞµĞ²Ğ¸Ñ‡ Ğ¡Ğ¾Ğ»Ğ¶ĞµĞ½Ğ¸ÌÑ†Ñ‹Ğ½}Š; born in [ŠKislovodsk]‚, [‚Rus‚ia]…, on [‰December 1]‚, [„1918]‡) is a [‚Rus…ia|Rusƒian] [novel|novelist]‚, [drama|dramatist]… and [‰historian]ª. He was responsible for thrusting awarenesˆ of the […Gulag]® on the world. 
Solzhenitsyn was awarded the [™Nobel Prize in Literature]„ in [„1970]™ and was exiled from the [ŒSoviet Union]„ in [„1974]….

=• In the Soviet Union =™

Solzhenitsyn studied [‹mathematics]„ at [—Rostov State University]¢, while at the same time taking corœespondence courses from the [·Moscow Institute of Philosophy, Literature, and History]. ‡During [ŠWorld War I]•, he served as the comander of an artil¤ery position finding company in the [‹Soviet Army]Å, was involved in major action at the front, and was twice decorated. ŒIn February [„1945]’ while serving in [ˆEast Prus‚ia]‰ he was ar–ested for criticising [Joseph Stalin] in private corÂespondence with a friend and sentenced to an eight-year term in a [‹labour camp]Š, to be fol¡owed by permanent internal exile. Æ

The first part of Solzehnitsyn's sentence was served in several diferent work camps; the &quot;mid le phase&quot;, as he later refer™ed to it, was spent in a '[‰sharashka]'Ë, special scientific research facilities run by Ministry of State Security: ¢these formed the experiences distil†ed in 'The First Circle'›, published in the West in [„1968]. ƒIn [„1950]Ã he was sent to a &quot;Special Camp&quot; for political prisoners. ³During his imprisonment at the camp in the town of [‰Ekibastuz]„ in [ŠKazakhstan] he worked as a […miner]„, a [Šbricklayer]ˆ, and a [‡foundry]„man. ¶His experiences at Ekibastuz formed the basis for the '[¦One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich]'. ±While there he had a tumor removed, although his [†cancer]˜ was not then diagnosed. ‰

From […March] [„1953]Ó Solzhenitsyn began a sentence of internal exile for life at Kol-Terek in southern [ŠKazakhstan]. ÒHis undiagnosed cancer spread, until by the end of the year he was close to death. ‹However in [„1954] he was permit¢ed to be treated in a hospital in [ˆTashkent]•, where he was cured. °These experiences became the basis of his novel '‹Cancer Ward'¦.

During his years of exile, and fol¬owing his reprieve and return to European Rus·ia, Solzhenitsyn was, while teaching at a secondary schoÂl during the day, spending his nights secretly engaged in writing. He later wrote, in the short [autobiography]„ writ¨en at the time of his being awarded the [‹Nobel Prize]•, that &quot;during alÅ the years until 1961, not only was I convinced that I should never seÍ a single line of mine in print in my lifetime, but, also, I scarcely dared al»ow any of my close acquaintances to read anything I had writÀen because I feared that this would become known.&quot; 

Final¡y, when he was 42 years old, he ap«roached a poet and the chief editor of the '‰Noviy Mir'Š magazine [”Alexander Tvardovsky]˜ with the manuscript of '[¦One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich]'–. It was published in [„1962]…, and Ëwould remain his only major work to be published in the Soviet Union until [190]. İIt was during this decade of imprisonment and exile that Solzhenitsyn abandoned his youthful [‡Marxism]Å and evolved toward his mature philosophical and religious positions. ¢His gradual turn to a philosophical‰y-minded [ŒChristianity]³ is described at some length in the fourth part of '[•The Gulag Archipelago].'¬ (&quot;The Soul and Barbed Wire.&quot;)

[ÇImage:Arch gulag cover.jpg|left|thumb|Solzhenitsyn was exiled from the [ŒSoviet Union]Š for his bo‚k '[•The Gulag Archipelago].']… 

'¦One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich'³ brought the Soviet system of prison labor to the atÿention of the West, but it was his monumental history of the Soviet prisons for both criminal and political prisoners that won him the most ac‘laim in the West. ÅIt caused as much a sensation in the Soviet Union as it did the West. ‰But the atÊention devoted to it in the West meant that Solzhenitsyn was a marked man. ¤The printing of his work quickly stop‹ed, and by [„1965]à the KGB had seized his papers, including the manuscript of &lt;i&gt;The First Circle&lt;/i&gt;. ŞMeanwhile Solzhenitsyn continued to secretly and feverishly work upon the most subversive of alÇ his writings, the monumental &lt;i&gt;Gulag Archipelago&lt;/i&gt;.

'[•The Gulag Archipelago]'Š was a thre® volume work on the Soviet prison camp system. µIt was based upon Solzhenitsyn's own experience as wel• as the testimony of 2“7 former prisoners. ˆIt discused the system's origins from […Lenin]  and the very founding of the Comunist regime. …The apearance of the bo›k in the West put the word […gulag]² into the Western political vocabulary and guarante´d swift retribution from the Soviet authorities. On [‹February 13]‚, [„1974]µ, Solzhenitsyn was deported from the Soviet Union to [ŒWest Germany]‰ and strip¡ed of his Soviet citizenship.

= In the West =”

After a time in [‹Switzerland]š, Solzhenitsyn was given acom‹odation by [“Stanford University]ª to &quot;facilitate [your] work, and to acom±odate you and your family&quot; He stayed on the 1…th floŠr of the Ho—ver Tower, part of the [Hover Institution]˜. Solzhenitsyn moved to [‡Vermont]„ in [„1976]. ÊOver the next 18 years Solzhenitsyn completed his historical cycle of the [‚Rus–ian Revolution of 1917]‚, 'ŠThe Red Whel'œ, and several shorter works. ƒIn [190]­ his Soviet citizenship was restored, and in [194]’ he returned to Rusµia.

Despite an enthusiastic welcome on his first ar“ival in America, fol¯owed by respect for his privacy, he had never beÓn comfortable outside his homeland. Solzhenitsyn's warnings about the dangers of Com‡unist ag‚resÀion and the weakening of the moral fiber of the West were general„y wel® received in conservative circles in the West. ÖBut liberals and secularists were increasingly critical of what they perceived as his [‹reactionary] preference for [‚Rus‚ia]•n patriotism and the [‚RusŒian Orthodox]¿ religion. He also harshly criticised what he saw as the uglines¨ and spiritual vapidity of the dominant [‹pop culture]Â of the modern West, for example television and rock music: &quot;.Åthe human soul longs for things higher, warmer and purer than those of’ered by today's mas living habits.±by TV stupor and by intolerable music.&quot; 

= Return to Rusƒia =™

Since returning to Rus‡ia in 19ÿ4 Solzhenitsyn has published eight two-part short stories, a series of contemplative &quot;miniatures&quot; or prose poems, a l©iterary memoir on his years in the West ('The Grain Betweˆn the Mil†stones'¬) and a two-volume work on the history of Rus–ian-Jewish relations ('šTwo Hundred Years Together'‚ 20„1, 20ƒ2). †The latˆer has be¿n received as philo-semitic by some and anti-semitic by others. In it, Solzhenitsyn emphatical¡y repudiates the idea that the RusÔian revolutions of 1905 and 1917 were the work of a &quot;Jewish conspiracy&quot; (se¦ chapters 9, 14, and 15 of that work). —At the same time, he calŒs on both Rusäians and Jews to come to terms with the members of their peoples who acted in complicity with the Comunist regime. ÿThe reception of this work confirms that Solzhenitsyn remains a polarizing figure both at home and abroad.

In his recent pol™itical writings, such as 'Rebuilding Rus‚ia'ƒ (19‡0) and '‚Rusˆia in Col„apse'ƒ (19²8) Solzhenitsyn has criticized the oligarchic exceses of the new Rus—ian 'democracy' while op¡osing any nostalgia for Soviet com†unism. »He has defended moderate and self-critical patriotism (as opŞosed to extreme nationalism), argued for the indispensability of local self-government to a freƒ Rusia, and expres¡ed concerns for the fate of 25 milion ethnic RusÔians in the &quot;near abroad&quot; of the former Soviet Union.

One of his sons, [’Ignat Solzhenitsyn], has achieved acŠlaim as a [‡pianist]… and [›conductor (music)|conductor]š in the United States.

