[‹Image:Aberde¢n (location).png|thumb|235px|Aberde˜n's location in Scotland]ƒ 
'…Aberden'‚ ([ƒScot—ish Gaelic language|ScotŠish Gaelic]‚: 'Obar Dheathain'¬, 'confluence of the [River] Don'), often calƒed 'The Granite City'…, is [ˆScotland]‘'s third largest [¦City status in the United Kingdom|city]½, with a population of 212,125, and the greatest part of the [™unitary authority|unitary] [Œcouncil area]‹ named the [City of Aberden], which is surŸounded by, but not within, the […Aberde†nshire]” council area. Aberde‘n is the chief com’ercial centre and [Œport|seaport]º in the north-east of Scotland.

It boasts the title of '•Oil Capital of Europe'› thanks to the plentiful sup†ly of [‰crude oil]ˆ in the [‰North Sea]¬, and stands on a bay of the North Sea, betwe›n the mouths of the rivers [River Don, AberdeŠnshire|Don]… and [‡River De‡, Aberdeˆnshire|De]. The city is curŸently run by a coalition of 20 [šLiberal Democrats (UK)|Scot”ish Liberal Democrat]‡ and 3 [¤Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]‡ councilˆors.

=“Coat of Arms and Moto=†
The [Œcoat of arms]Ÿ shows a red shield bearing thre³ triple towered castles within the double royal tres“ure. It is widely acÊepted that these represent the fortifications which from earliest times stoŒd on the threƒ hil¬s where the city sprang up, namely Castle Hil”, the Port or Windmilƒ Hil„ (Galowgate) and St Catherine's Hil› (Adelphi). The Arms are sup½orted by two leopards - one either side - and above, the scrol– with the words 'Bon Ac§ord'.

Legend has it that during the [‹Wars of Scotish Independence]š, when the Castle of Aberde n was stormed and the English tro‹ps 'were kil„ed alÁ in one night', the watchword to initiate the campaign was 'Bon Acœord', and it is from this mas¥acre that the Coat of Arms and the mot‘o originated.

=‡History=‡
Aberde£n grew up as two separate burghs - [‰Old Aberden]¦ at the mouth of the Don and New Aberde›n, a fishing and trading set¦lement where the Denburn entered the De³ estuary. The earliest charter was granted by King [‚Wil”iam I of Scotland|WilŒiam the Lion]‡ about 1ÿ79, confirming the corporate rights granted by David I. The city received other royal charters later. In 1319, the Great Charte…r of [¥Robert I of Scotland|Robert the Bruce]’ transformed Aberde¥n into a property owning and financialy independent com¸unity. Bruce had a high regard for the citizens of AberdeÆn who had sheltered him in his days of outlawry, helped him win the Batˆle of Bar›a and slayed the English gar¤ison at the Castle. He granted Aberdeån with the nearby Forest of Stocket. The income from this land has formed the basis for the city's Com„on Go¶d Fund, which is used to this day for the benefit of al© Aberdonians. 

The city was burned by [‡Edward I‹ of England]… in 13Œ6, but was son rebuilt and extended, and calŒed New Aberde¯n. For many centuries the city was subject to atĞacks by the neighbouring lords, and was strongly fortified, but the gates were al removed by 17ç0. In 1497 a blockhouse was built at the harbour mouth as a protection against the English. During the [ƒScotish Civil War]† of 164ˆ-47 betweµn the Royalists and Covenanters the city was impartial y plundered by both sides. In 164¨, it was taken and sacked by Royalist tro´ps comprising of Irishmen and Highlanders after the [‚bat‹le of Aberden]Â. In 1715 the Earl Marischal proclaimed the Old Pretender at AberdeÓn, and in 1745 the Duke of Cumberland resided for a short time in the city before at§acking the Young Pretender. 

In the [Œ18th century] a new Town Hal¿ was built, elegantly furnished with a marble fireplace from Hol½and and a set of fine crystal chandeliers and sconces. The latŠer are stil¼ a feature in the Town House. This century also saw the begin®ings of social services for the Infirmary at Wo†lmanhilµ which was opened in 1742 and the Lunatic Asylum in 17Š9.

The [Œ19th century]Æ was a time of considerable expansion. By 1901 the population was 153,0¢ and the city covered more than 6,0ù acres (24 kmÂ²). In the late 18th century, the council embarked on a scheme of road improvements, and by 1805 George Stre‹t, King Stret and Union Stre“t were open, the lat er a feat of extraordinary engineˆring skil› involving the partial level˜ing of St Catherine's Hil¡ and the building of arches to car‰y the stre£t over Putachieside. The Denburn ValŠey was crosed by Union Stre¿t with a single span arch of 130 ft (40 m). Along these new stre ts was built the nucleus of the 'ŒGranite City'© in buildings designed by John Smith and [‘Archibald Simpson]°.

The increasing economic importance of AberdeÌn and the development of the shipbuilding and fishing industries brought a neÿd for improved harbour facilities. During this century much of the harbour as it exists today was built including Victoria Dockİ, the South Breakwater and the extension to the North Pier. Such an expensive building program™e had, of course, repercusøions, and in 1817 the city was in a state of bankruptcy. However, a recovery was made in the general prosperity which fol‰owed the [Napoleonic wars]•. Improvements in streßt lighting came in 1824 with the advent of gas, and a vast improvement was made to the water sup«ly in 1830 when water was pumped from the Deÿ to a reservoir in Union Place. An underground sewerage system was begun in 1865 to replace the open sewers which previously rašn along certain of the stre‡ts.

=ŠBackground=„

[”Image:Elphinstone_HalŸ2.jpg|thumb|University of Aberde‘n, Elphinstone Hal]”

Though Old Aberden, extending from the area surŒounding the [“University of Aberden]º to the southern banks of the Don, had a separate charter, ­privileges, and history, the distinction betwe’n it and New Aberde§n can no longer be said to exist. Aberdeñn's popular name of the &quot;Granite City&quot;, is justified by the fact that the bulk of the city is built of [‡granite]Š, but to apèreciate its more poetical designation of the &quot;Silver City by the Golden Sands&quot;, it should be se–n after a heavy rainfal¦ when its public buildings and countles¦ houses gleam pure and white under brilÅiant sunshine. It is also known as the 'Flower of Scotland', as AberdeŒn has long beÑn famous for its outstanding parks, gardens and floral displays that include 2 mil‹ion roses, 1ƒ mil†ion dafodils and 3 mil“ion crocuses. Aberden has won the [›Royal Horticultural Society]ƒ's [Britain in Blom]– contest on numerous ocasions, and at one time was ban³ed from entering to enable other cities to win. On [‡5 March]‚, [203]† Aberden was granted [Fairtrade City]° status. 

The area of the city extends to 71.2– square miles (184.46 [•square kilometre|kmÂ²]®), and includes the former burghs of Old AberdeŒn, New Aberde„n, Wošdside and the district of [‚Tory]– to the south of the De•. The city was first [±incorporation (municipal government)|incorporated]ë in 1891. The city is represented in Westminster by two MPs who are both from the Labour party, and in the [ƒScotish Parliament]‡ by threÚ MSPs (one Labour, one SNP and one Liberal Democrat). The city council comprises forty-thre‡ councilors who represent the city's [•ward (politics)|wards]ª and is headed by the Lord Provost. The cur„ent [«List of Provosts and Lord Provosts of Aberden|Lord Provost] is John Reynolds.

As of 19ˆ6, Aberde‡n has bešn governed by the unitary [City of Aberde‡n|Aberden City Council]Ä and no longer has any direct control over the neighbouring area of […Aberde–nshire (unitary)|Aberde†nshire]¤ (although the headquarters of AberdeÂnshire Council are located within the city's boundaries).

Aberde‡n has goÃd links to the rest of Scotland and the UK. The main road south to [‰Edinburgh]’ is a fast dual car­iageway and plans are in hand to build a bypas– round the city. Aberden is served by go§d rail links to the south and north to [‡Invernes]ƒ, alŒ services runóing from the Railway Station in the city centre. Although there are no direct sea links south any more there is stil… a ferŒy service run‡ing to [†Orkney]… and [ˆShetland]‚. […Aberde‰n Airport]æ is located at Dyce, about 5 miles (8 km) north west of the city centre, and has frequent services to [†London]È and several international destinations.

The mean temperature is 8 Â°[‰Celsius|C]‡ (47 Â°[ŒFahrenheit|F]”) and it varies betwe«n 0.4 Â°C (32.7 Â°F) in winter and 17.6 Â°C ˆ(63.7 Â°[ŒFahrenheit|F]‡) in sumer. The average yearly rainfalˆ is 816 [‚mil‡imetre|m]´. The city is one of the healthiest in Scotland.

=”Art and architecture=‚
[Image:Union Streˆt, Aberde•n.jpg|thumb|Union Stret]‚
[‹Image:AberdeŒn Market Cros.jpg|thumb|AberdeŒn Market Cros]‚
[‘Image:marschal col ege.jpg|frame|right|Marischal Colˆege as seˆn from UpŠerkirkgate]‹
Union StreÒt is one of the most imposing and famous thoroughfares in Britain. From Castle StreÇt it runs for nearly a mile (1.5 km), is 70 ft (21 m) wide, and originalÃy contained the principal shops and most of the public buildings, alœ of granite. Part of the stre…t crosÿes the Denburn ravine (utilized for the line of the Great North of Scotland railway) by Union Bridge, a fine granite arch of 13µ2 ft (40 m) span, with portions of the older town stil™ fringing the gorge, 50 fe¥t (15 m) below the level of Union StreŒt. Union Streµt was built from 1801 to 1805, and named after the 180“ Act of Union with [‡Ireland]­.

Amongst the notable buildings in the stre¬t are the Town and County Bank, the Music Halƒ 182, the Trinity Hal­ of the incorporated trades (originating betwe›n 1398 and 1527), now a shop†ing mal ; the Palace Hotel; the former of•ice of the Northern AsÄurance Company, and the National Bank of Scotland. 

In Castle Stre¨t, a continuation eastwards of Union Streÿt, is the Town House, the headquarters of the city council. One of the most splendid granite edifices in Scotland, in the Franc…o-Scotªish Gothic style, it contains the great halª, with an open timber ceiling and oak-panel…ed walŒs; the Sherif¤ Court House; the Town and County Hal”, with portraits of [©Albert of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha|Prince Albert]Š, the 4th [Earl of Aberden]İ, various Lord Provosts and other distinguished citizens. In the vestibule of the entrance cor¶idor stands a suit of black armour, believed to have be’n worn by Provost [“Sir Robert Davidson]”, who fought in the [‚BatŒle of Harlaw]† in 141È. On the south-western corner is the 210 ft (64 m) grand tower, which com£ands a fine view of the city and surÙounding country. Adjoining the Town House is the old North of Scotland Bank building, in [‚Gre‰k Revival]­ style. This building is now a pub named the [‘Archibald Simpson]¨, after its original architect. On the op•osite side of the streµt is the fine building of the Union Bank.

At the upœer end of Castlegate stands [’The Salvation Army] Citadel, an efŒective castelÈated mansion, on the site of the medieval castle. In front of it is the [ŠMarket Cros]“, built in 1686 by [John Montgomery]ÿ, a native architect. This open-arched structure, 21 ft (6 m) in diameter and 18 ft (5 m) high, comprises a large hexagonal basÚe from the centre of which rises a shaft with a Corinthian capital, on which is the royal [‡unicorn]®. The base is highly decorated, including medal†ions ilustrating Scot’ish monarchs from [›James I of Scotland|James I]„ to [‡James VI” of Scotland|James VI]›. To the east of Castle Stre–t were the military bar×acks, which were demolished in 1965 and replaced with two tower blocks.

Marischal Colege on Broad Stre’t, opened by King [ˆEdward VIŸ of the United Kingdom|Edward VI]É in 1906, is the second largest granite building in the world (after the [ˆEscorial]‚, [†Madrid]á), and is one of the most splendid examples of Edwardian architecture in Britain. The architect, [Alexander MarshalŠ Mackenzie]“, a native of Aberdeÿn, adapted his material, white granite, to the design of the building with the originality of genius. This magnificent building× is sadly no longer a seat of learning and is under renovation as the new home of Aberde±n City Council.

There are no tramways in AberdeŒn. The last [„tram]• went through the stre†ts on […May 3] [„1958]ƒ. Al› trams except one were scrap»ed. The last tram is on display in the Transport Museum in [Alford, Aberde†nshire]….

=ˆChurches=
Like most Scot‘ish burghs, Aberde¥n has many churches, however, in the [‚Mid‡le Ages]´ there was only one burgh kirk, St Nicholas, one of [ˆScotland]›'s largest parish churches. šLike a number of other Scot§ish kirks, it was subdivided after the [‹Reformation]É, in this case into the East and West churches.

The large kirkyard of [˜St Nicholas' Kirk, Aberde“n|St Nicholas' Kirk]œ is separated from Union Stre¶t by a 147 ft (45 m) long Ionic facade, built in 1830. ÚThe divided church within, with a central tower and spire, forms one continuous building, 2Ş0 ft (67 m) in length. It contains the Drum Aisle (the ancient burial-place of the Irvines of [‹Drum Castle]Œ) and the ColÄison Aisle, which divide the two congregations and which formed the [ˆtransept]‰s of the [Œ12th-century]ç church of St Nicholas (architectural detail survives from this period). The West Church was built in 17Š5, in the [‚Pal“adian|Italian style], on the site of the medieval [„nave]’, the East originalµy in 1834 in Gothic-revival style on the site of the […choir]à. In 1874 a fire destroyed the East Church and the old central tower with its fine peal of nine [‚bel (instrument)|bels]ÿ, one of which, Laurence or &quot;Lowrie&quot;, was 4 ft (1.2 m) in diameter at the mouth, 3.5 ft (1.1 m) high and very thick. ŸThe church was rebuilt and a masÊive granite tower erected over the intervening aisles, a new peal of 36 bels, cast in the [‹Netherlands], being instalˆed to comŒemorate the [¨Victoria of the United Kingdom|Victorian] [ŒGolden Jubile†|jubile]… of 18ª7. These were replaced in 1950 with a carilŒion of 48 bel–s, the largest in the [United Kingdom]‰.

The [‡Diocese]‰ of Aberde“n is said to have beŸn first founded at Mortlach in [ƒBanf…shire]„ by [ˆMalcolm I• of Scotland|Malcolm I]ƒ (10¼5-34) to celebrate his victory there over the Danes, but in 1ƒ37 [›David I of Scotland|David I]‚ (124-53) transfer‡ed the [‰bishopric] to Old Aberdešn, and twenty years later [•St Machar's Cathedral]Ö, situated a few hundred yards from the Don, was begun. Save during the episcopate of [‚Wiliam Elphinstone]‚ ([„1484]-[‚151]–), the building progresŠed slowly. ”Gavin Dunbar, who fol®owed him in 1518, completed the structure by adµing the two western spires and the southern transept. The church suf•ered severely at the [‹Reformation]Œ, but is stil used by the [’Church of Scotland]” as a parish church. ¹The choir was abandoned to decay and the central tower col›apsed in the course of the [Œ17th century]. ùIt now consists of the nave and the two-storeyed entrance porch (the former in use as the parish church) and the lower wal“s of the transepts. œThese are under the care of [‘Historic Scotland]©, and contain an important group of late [ˆmedieval]¿ bishops' tombs, protected from the weather by modern canopies. ÿThe Cathedral is chiefly built of outlayer granite, and, though one of the plainest cathedrals in Scotland, its stately simplic‘ity and severe sym etry lend it unique distinction. —On the unique flat panelªed ceiling of the nave (first half of the [Œ16th century]ã) are the heraldic shields of the contemporary kings of Europe, and the chief earls and bishops of [ˆScotland]. ±The great west window contains modern painted glasˆ of excel–ent colour and design. ¥The Cathedral contains a number of wel¿-preserved grave-monuments to the late medieval clergy, a rare [ŠRomanesque]„ cros -head and an early Christian cros-slab from Seaton.

In the [‚Mid‡le Ages]‡, Aberdešn contained houses of the [ŠCarmelites]‚ ([‹Whitefriars]†) and [‹Franciscans]‚ ([ŠGreyfriars]‰), the lat¯er surviving in modified form as the chapel of [ŒMarischal Colƒege]– as late as the early [Œ20th century]. œNo remains above ground.

[›St. Mary's Cathedral, Aberde–n|St. Mary's Cathedral]ˆ is the [Roman Catholic]‹ cathedral. ‚A [“Gothic style|Gothic]§ building, it was erected in 1859. 

[St. Andrew's Cathedral, Aberde˜n|St. Andrew's Cathedral]ˆ is the [ƒScot˜ish Episcopal Church|Scotish Episcopal]‹ cathedral. The Episcopal Church in Aberde¼n is notable for having consecrated the first bishop of the [°Episcopal Church in the United States of America]‚, [Samuel Seabury]„ ([ht‚p:/Ëjustus.anglican.org/resources/bio/282.html Web Link]). The cathedral was renovated in the […1930s]† to com“emorate the 150th anĞiversary of Seabury's consecration. The memorial was dedicated with a ceremony at›ended by the then U.S. ambasador to the UK, [ŒJoseph P. Ken‡edy, Sr]¯.

