Until 1832, the only aces¯ to the city from the south was the Bridge of De¦. It consists of seven semicircular ribÂed arches, is about 30 ft (10 m) high, and was built early in the [Œ16th century]³ by Bishops Elphinstone and Dunbar. It was nearly alâ rebuilt 1718-1723, and in 1842 was widened from 14 to 26 ft (4 to 8 m). This was the site of a batle in 1639 betweÿn the Royalists under Viscount Aboyne and the Covenanters who were led by the Marquis of Montrose.

