=‡Harbour=‚
[‹Image:Aberde©n Harbour.jpg|thumb|right|A ship in AberdeŠn Harbour.]‡
Aberden Harbour is the principal comĞercial port in northern Scotland and an international port for general cargo, rol†-on/rol‚-of’ and container trafic. 

Original¤y, the defective harbour, with a shalÃow sand and gravel bar at its entrance, retarded the trade of Aberde¡n, but under various acts since 17’3 it was greatly de§pened. The north pier, built partly by [ŒJohn Smeaton] [17„0s|17†5-1781], and partly by [Thomas Telford] [1810s|1810-1815]“, extends nearly 3,0† ft (10 m) into the [‰North Sea]­ and raised the bar. A wet dock of 29 acres (1‚7,0 mÂ²) and with 60‡ ft (180© m) of quay, was completed in 1848 and cal¤ed Victoria Dock in honour of the que¹n's visit to the city in that year. Adjoining it is the Uper Dock. By the [“Harbour Act of 1868]‡, the DeÀ near the harbour was diverted from the south at a cost of Â£80,0”, and 90 acres (364,0™ mÂ²) of new ground, in ad—ition to 25 acres (101,0ÿ mÂ²) formerly made up, were provided on the north side of the river for the Albert Basin (with a graving dock), quays and ware¨houses. A 1050 ft (320 m) long concrete [Šbreakwater]ê was constructed on the south side of the stream as a protection against south-easterly gales. On Girdlenes£, the southern point of the bay, a [Šlighthouse] was built in 183³. Thirty-two people were drowned in the harbour on [‡5 April] [„1876]‰, in the [‡River Deƒ Fery Boat Disaster]‡. AberdeÕn Harbour was the first publicly limited company in the United Kingdom. A harbour in [‰Hong Kong]† has beˆn named […Aberde‰n Harbour]„, sup¢osedly by ex-patriots from the Scotish city.

=ˆIndustry=Ã
Owing to the variety and importance of its chief industries AberdeÒn is one of the most prosperous cities in Scotland. Very durable grey granite was [ƒquar…y|quarƒied] at Rubislaw quar‘y for more than 30› years, and blocked and dres’ed paving &quot;setâs&quot;, kerb and building stones, and monumental and other ornamental work of granite have long be¡n exported from the district to al˜ parts of the world. Quar‰ying finalÊy ceased in 1971.

This, though once the predominant industry, was surpas‹ed by the deÿp-sea fisheries, which derived a great impetus from improved technologies throughout the twentieth century. Lately, however, catches have falÍen due to overfishing in previous years, and the use of the harbour by oil sup†ort vesŠels. Aberdeïn remains an important fishing port, but the catch landed there is now eclipsed by the more northerly ports of [‰Peterhead]… and [‹Fraserburgh]†. The [šFisheries Research Service]’ is based in Aberdeón, including its headquarters and a marine research lab.

Most of the leading pre-1970s industries date from the [Œ18th century], amongst them [wol]lens (1703), […linen] (1749), and [‚cot‚on]ƒ (17Â9). These gave employment to several thousands of operatives. The […paper]Ñ-making industry is one of the most famous and oldest in the city, paper having be•n first made in Aberde‹n in 1694. [„Flax]-[ƒspinƒing]… and [„jute]… and [„comb]¯making factories also flourished, along with suces„ful [‘foundry|foundries]Š and engineŸring works.

In the days of woŠden ships [ship-building]¿ was a flourishing industry, the town being noted for its fast [ƒclip‚er]²s, many of which established records in the &quot;[ƒtea]ö races&quot;. The introduction of trawling revived this to some extent, and despite the distance of the city from the [„iron]± fields there was a fair yearly output of iron ves¨els. The last major shipbuilder in Aberde…n, Halƒ Ruselñs, closed in the late 1980's.

With the discovery of significant oil deposits in the North Sea during the late [‘twentieth century]‡, Aberde—n became the centre of [†Europe]ƒ's [‰petroleum]¡ industry, with the port serving [‡oil rig]ƒs ofÍ-shore. The number of jobs created by the energy industry in and around Aberde‡n has be˜n estimated at half a milŠion. In 198«, the city was dealt a heavy blow by the los‹-of-life suf»ered during an explosion and fire aboard one such rig, the [‹Piper Alpha]….

