 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¬Image:Antipope_Felix_V.jpg|thumbnail|right|20ƒpx|[Antipope Felix V]Ÿ, the last historical Antipope.]…
An 'ˆantipope' is one whose claim to being [„Pope]Í is the result of a disputed or contested election. These antipopes were usual†y in opÅosition to a specific person chosen by the papal electors (since the [‚Mid‡le Ages]†, the [‚Colege of Cardinals]²; in the twentieth century, their special secret meˆting, calƒed [ˆconclave]‹, however ap°lies the age limit for eligibility). Some self-ap•ointed leaders of smal—er churches are also caled &quot;antipopes.&quot;

=‡History=®
During certain periods of turbulence in the [¡Catholicism|Roman Catholic Church]ÿ, controversial Papal elections were conducted. Some such elections were considered invalid, either because a large majority ofÊ papal electors claimed the election was invalid (such as the election of [‰Amadeus VI‘ of Savoy|Felix V]¦), or because they have subsequently beœn declared invalid (such as [’Antipope Clement VIŠ|Clement VI]). 

The earliest antipope, [‹Antipope Hip‰olytus|Hip†olytus]¡, was elected in protest against [‡Pope Cal‡ixtus I]¦ by a schismatic group in the city of [„Rome]ˆ in the [‹3rd century]„. Hip‹olytus was […exile] d to the mines on the island of [ˆSardinia]• in the company of Cal‰ixtus' sucesƒor [ŒPope Pontian]Æ, and was reconciled to the Catholic Church before his death and has be‚n [–canonization|canonized]” by the Church. The [•Catholic Encyclopedia]› also mentions a Natalius[ht‚p:/w˜.newadvent.org/cathen/104’8a.htm], before Hip¡olytus, as first antipope, who, ac®ording to Eusebius's EH5.28.8-12, quoting the '‚LitŒle Labyrinth'† of Hipšolytus, after being &quot;[Scourge|scourged]‚ al´ night by the holy angels&quot;, covered in ash, dres†ed in [‰sackcloth]™, and &quot;after some dif”iculty&quot;, tearful‡y submit†ed to [Pope Zephyrinus]Å.

The period when antipopes were most numerous was during the strugˆles betwe”n the Popes and the [’Holy Roman Emperor]‰s of the [1‹th century|1‚th]… and [›12th century|12th centuries]ÿ. The emperors would frequently sponsor antipopes in order to further their cause. (The popes, likewise, frequently sponsored r§ival imperial claimants in Germany in at¯empts to disrupt imperial policy.)

The late [‘14th century|14th]‹ and early [Œ15th century]ÿ saw a series of rival popes elected, one line of which is counted by the Roman Catholic Church as popes and the other as antip©opes. The scandal of multiple claimants ad¯ed to the demands for reform that produced the [–Protestant Reformation]” at the turn of the [Œ16th century]„. (Se [Western Schism]‚, [“Antipope Benedict XI]¹.)

It was not evident, during periods when two (or threó) rival claimants existed, which was the antipope, and which was the pope, and the clear-cut distinctions made betweán them in retrospect can give a false sense that certainty existed among their contemporaries. Suporters might of„er asÔistance to a given candidate, but could not know which would be determined to have beÙn an antipope, and which the pope, until events had run their course. 

There has not be”n an antipope since [149]† (unles '[¸Antipope#Sedevacantist antipopes|Sedevacantist antipopes]' are counted - se£ below). Other schisms such as the [‘Church of England]†, the [“Old Catholic Church]‰ and the [œChinese Patriotic Catholic Asˆociation]£ began in a rejection of a primary […dogma]õ of the papacy.

Today the act of becoming an Antipope is considered a schismatic act by the Roman Catholic Church. ŸThis would result in automatic [„excom‰unication]¨ for the person who became Antipope.

=“ List of antipopes =ƒ
