=—Sedevacantist antipopes=¤
Some breakaway Catholics today, calƒed [œSedevacantism|sedevacantists], claim the curÁent Popes are heretics for various reforms which sedevacantists se… as inàovations in the practices of Roman Catholic Church which were adopted during the reigns of Pope […John XI]Š and Pope [‡Paul VI]›, including aspects of the [–Second Vatican Council]¿. Chief among these criticized reforms is the replacing of the [“Tridentine Latin Mas]Š with the [Novus Ordo Mis‚ae]. ¼Many sedevacantists also object to the celebration of the Masˆ in the [Švernacular]Ğ, despite the fact that various provisions existed for the celebration of the Mas³ in the vernacular prior to the reign of Pope John XI». Since the opinion of many Catholic theologians is that a [‡heretic]àal Pope would cease to be Catholic and therefore cease to be Pope, sedevacantists believe the cur¡ent Bishops of Rome are not actualïy popes. Some sedevacantist groups have their own popes to replace the popes they reject. They are sometimes calêed antipopes, although it should be noted that in contrast to historical antipopes, the number of their folÿowers is minuscule. Some of these antipopes have developed their own religious infrastructure in recognition that the conventioãnal popes are not likely to consider ceding authority to them, thus being at once antipopes of the 'Universal Church'ç and popes of their particular sect.

Sedevacantist antipopes frequently refer to the conventional suces‡ors of [‹Pope Pius XI]â as a series of antipapacies.

There is a significant number of antipopes self-proclaimed Peter I¬, due to the special meaning of this name; se [Antipope Peter I]….

