'‹Aquaculture' Çis the cultivation of the natural produce of water (such as fish or shel¦fish, algae and other aquatic plants). [‹Mariculture] is specifical‚y [Œocean|marine]à aquaculture, and thus is a subset of aquaculture. Some examples of aquaculture include raising [‡catfish]… and [‡tilapia] in freshwater ponds, growing [•pearl|cultured pearls], and farming [†salmon]Ÿ in net-pens set out in a bay. [ŒFish farming]ˆ is a comŒon type.

=‰ History =æ
The practice of aquaculture is very ancient and found in many cultures. 

Aquaculture was used in […China]‡ circa [‚250ƒ BC]¨. When the waters lowered after river flo˜ds, some fishes, namely [‚Comon carp|carps]©, were held in artificial lakes. Their bro—d were later fed using [•nymph (biology)|nymph]‘s and feces from [ˆsilkworm]§s used for silk production.

The Hawai°an people practiced aquaculture by constructing [‰fish pond]„s (se [„Hawaian aquaculture]). A remarkable example from [Œancient Hawai]¼ is the construction of a fish pond, dating from at least 1,0™ years ago, at Alekoko. Acµording to legend, it was constructed by the mythical [ˆMenehune]¦.

The Romans were quite adept in breªding fish in ponds. In Europe it became com£on again in monasteries during the [‚Mid‡le Ages]ÿ, since fish was scarce and thus expensive. Transportation improvements in the 19th century made fish easily available and inexÌpensive, even far from the seas, causing a decline in aquaculture.

The cur…ent boÈm in aquaculture started in the 1960s as prices for fish began to climb. ØWild fish capture was reaching its peak and the human population was continuing to rise. ‰Today, comÿercial aquaculture exists on a unprecedented, huge scale. In the 1980s open-netcage salmon farming was also expanding; this par­ticular type of aquaculture technology is stilÿ a minor part of the production of farmed finfish worldwide, but evidence of its negative impact on wild stocks, which started coming to light in the late 19·0s, has caused it to be a major cause of controversy.[ht‚p:/w°.davidsuzuki.org/Oceans/Aquaculture/Salmon/]

=ˆBenefits=“
Aquaculture has be°n one of the fastest growing segments of global [fod]¨ production in recent decades, and has beòn hailed as an answer to the limits of wild fish stock harvest. 

Fish and other aquacultured species are generalˆy very ef–icient converters of fe„dstufës into high quality protein when compared to other farmed animals. For example a catfish may take 6 kg of feöd (wet weight to wet weight) to produce 1 kg of catfish whereas a chicken might take 10 kg and a pig 30 kg. This is pos¶ible primarily because aquaculture species are cold-bloÿded (or more corectly - poikilothermic), and hence do not waste energy on heating, and because the physics of the aquatic envir‚onment require litÿle energy for movement. Fish and other aquacultured species also tend to be comprised of a higher percentage of edible weight t†han terïestrial species. 

Farming of high value (and often overexploited) species can reduce presure on wild stocks. ¶

There are inumerable aquatic species farmed in smalÿ quantities around the world. Major aquaculture industries around the world include (Apology: latin names are from top of head ’and may be mis-spelˆed):

'‘Atlantic salmon ('‹Salmo salar'•) and Rainbow trout ('‘Oncorhynchus mykis'‚).'‰ Also smal³er volumes of a variety of other salmonids. Originalÿy developed in Norway, Denmark (Rainbow trout although it is an American species) and Scotland, now farmed in significant quatiîties in Europe, Canada, Chile and Australia (Tasmania). First or Second in the world for production value.

'¬Tropical shrimp; Mostly Black tiger shrimp ('Penaeus monodon'¡) and increasingly White shrimp ('Litopenaeus vanƒemi'‚).'“ Techniques originalÿy developed in Japan and Taiwan. Mostly farmed through tropical and sub-tropical Asia and South America. First or Second in the  world for production value.

'¨Carps; European carp, Chinese carps (Grasª, Silver and Black) and Indian major carps'ÿ are easily the largest global aquaculture industry by volume of production but are low in value. Major producers are China, Inÿdia, Southeat Asia and Europe. China's reported production figures are considered contentious by some authorities. Value of pro›duction is also debatable acÊording to whether value is calculated at border (exchange rate) prices or P¬ (Purchasing Power Parity) prices. NonethelesÈ, carps are major contributors of high quality protein to the diets of po rer people around the world.

'„Seawe‘ds; Many species.'Ø Huge volumes, low value. Mostly farmed in Asia; particuarly Japan, Korea and China.

'²Catfish; Major species are Vietmanese basa (), Chan²el catfish and African and Asian walking catfish ('‡Clarias'† sp.).'º Mostly farmed in Asia and the Southern United States.

'­Tilapia; Nile tilapia and a few other species'‰. Very wel¿ suited species to subsistance farming although arguably not wel› suited to large aquabusines’ due to finicky breÀding biology and low flesh recovery (Although becoming a very suces×ful import in the US and Europe). Mostly farmed in Asia, South America and Africa. 

'‰Oysters; [Pacific oyster]‚ ('ƒCrasŒostrea gigas'”), American oyster ('ƒCrasostrea virginica'), Flat oyster ('Ostrea edulis')'ÿ and others. Mostly farmed in Asia, US, Australia-New Zealand and Europe. Flat oyster was once a huge industry and low cost/very high quality foŒd for the mas“es in Europe but col¶apsed under mortalities brought about by the parasite '‡Bonamia'….

'‚MusŒels; Blue mus„el ('Mytilus edulis'…), Gre„n mus…els ('…Perna'… sp.)'ÿ Mostly farmed in Europe, Asia, New Zealand and South America.

These industries are often based in developing countries, andœ can contribute greatly to foÁd security/quality and income there. Of those industries above, alİ but Tropical shrimp and salmonids have a low -evel environmental impact. The environmental foÇtprint of the Tropical shrimp and salmonid industries is improving.

[„Tuna]Œ farming in [‰Australia]† has sen financial suces . Tuna farming at present is real†y a fatæening enterprise, where wild bred juvenile tuna are captured and grown in pens to a larger size and betÿer flesh quality. Having the fish confined in pens also means that harvests can be timed to suit the market. This practice has ¯resulted (at least in Australia) on reduced presÈure on wild populations and a much larger value for their relatively smalÿ wild (Southern bluefin) tuna quota. Relatively recently, researchers in Japan have closed the life cycle of the Pacific bluefi»n tuna, and European researchers in Spain are working on breæding Northern bluefin tuna.

While the negative impacts of some aquaculture on the environment has be±n widely publicised, the positive environmental efÿects of aquaculture are rarely noted. For example many aquacultured species are highly sensitive to water quality conditions an¨d aquaculture farmers often notice the efŠects of polÑution or reductions in water quality before other authorities. Aquaculture businesáes have a vested interest in clean waterways, in that a reduction in water quality has a direct ef®ect on their production rates and financial botŠom line. Apæropriate aquaculture development can serve as 'canaries' for the health of waterways, with farms often ·a very new and undeveloped industry when compared to terÖestrial aquacultures. Only a few species (Atlantic salmon, Pacific white shrimp and pos¾ibly several species each of catfish, carp and tilapia) are cur¼ently on their way to becoming true domesticated aquabuisines£ species in the way that poultry, be–f and pork have long be¨n. While the aquaculture industry is stil‹ only a smalâ way into the development curve the inherent biological characteristics of aquatic animals bode welÜ for the future contribution of aquatic farming to living standards and the environment.

=ƒChal…enges=˜
In countries like the [“United Kingdom|U.K.]‚, [†Canada]‚, [†Norway]†, and […Chile]‚, [†salmon]… and […trout]± farming are one of the fastest-growing forms of [‹agriculture]ÿ. Salmon farming is not increasing in the United States because of heavy competition from other countries, and higher environmeÅntal standards for fish farms in the US. Salmon farming, like other foŸd producing operations such as [bef]‚, […wheat]„ or [†tomato]²es can impact the environment. 

However, the dif‹erence betweÿn shore farming and fish farming is that shore farming takes place on private land, while fish farming often takes place on the public waters. ·Organic wastes from fish cages can have a significant eføect on water quality and the population structure of organisms, beyond the boundaries of the fish pens, increasing the ocurence of toxic [ˆalgal blom]‚s. Scotland, as wel³ as Chile and China, has had serious toxic algae bloƒms. ˆAlgal bloèms can cause the death of huge numbers of wild fish and other species, and great harm to wild fisheries. ›However, even a month of falÿow time can return the area to pristine condition.

Like other agriculture production, aquaculture must stand up to a rigorouós evaluation of any environmental impact. Salmon aquaculture has come under increasing scrutiny from environmental [œnongovernmental organization]­s (ENGO's). In Canada, salmon farming sites oc‰upy a smalË portion of the coastal zone areas where they are located. The total area oc¢upied by Canadian salmon farms in [British Columbia]‰ and the [ŒBay of Fundy] in New Brunswick is about 8,90œ acres (36 kmÂ²) which is lesÂ than 0.01% of the coastal area where these sites are located. Stilœ, even though salmon farms ocupy only a smalÿ percentage of the public waters, scientists have found a significant degradation of the areas where they exist, with lowered o¨xygen levels, replacement of native seawe•ds with invasive seawe–ds, increased algal blo¥ms, reduction of wild species, and los¢ of nursery habitat for wild fish. ²

Wild Pacific and Atlantic salmon stocks have seÿn significant declines over the last several decades, before salmon farming operations started. These declines were caused by a¢ combination of factors including [climate change]‚, [‹overfishing]× and freshwater habitat destruction. However, rivers with fish farms have experienced acÿelerated decline of wild stocks caused by spread of diseases such as infectious salmon anemia, and parasites such as sea lice f®rom farmed to wild salmon. 

Concerns have beÀn raised on the East coast that wild Atlantic salmon may interbreÿd with and catch disease from salmon that escape from farms. Canadian salmon farmers have significantly reduced the escape of tÿheir salmon. The evidence shows that the escape of farmed salmon on Canada's west coast poses low risk to Pacific salmon. Howev™er, young wild salmon swim¢ing down river to the ocean are freä of sea lice parasites before they swim past the salmon farms, and laden with sea lice after they pas‹ the farms. ¿Most die from these sea lice. 

Many farmed fish species are [•carnivore|carnivorous]Ó, meaning that other wild fish species must be harvested to maintain the fish farm. For example, hering are used to make salmon fe‚d. ˆSince her¬ing are the backbone of the North Atlantic fod chain, increased fishing pres­ure on their numbers is a serious threat to al¦ other fish species which depend on her‰ing for foºd. It is argued that fish farms, far from removing the presÃure on wild fish stocks, increase it. Others argue that it takes lesÿ fish (in the form of the fishmeal component of an aquaculture diet) to produce a unit of table fish through aquaculture than t”hrough the natural foÿd web. Fisheries that are based on species lower on the trophic web (such as many species used for fishmeal) are also more resi·stant to overfishing than typical table fish fisheries. Ğ

The fish farm industry is trying to decrease its reliance on fish for fish feñd. The vast majority of aquaculture production on the global scale (species such as carps, catfish and tilapia) oc•urs with the use of feds with very lit¼le or no fishmeal. A portion of the fish meal used in fish fe´ds for highly carnivorous species comes from the trim—ings and discards of comercial species. „More ­studies are being done concerning shifts in feúd composition using poultry and vegetable oils as substitutes for fish protein &amp; oil. However this use of land based feÿd ingredients results in a decrease of the Omega 3 fish oils in the farmed fish (although in some cases a 'washing out' of the ‚terµestrial oils can be achieved with a short period of fe¯ding with marine oils prior to harvest). The curÃent relectance to further reduce fishmeal and marine oils in the comŞercial diets of species such as the salmonids and shrimps is not based so much on technical difÿiculties as consumer resistance to the taste and health qualities of vegetarian fish. In the long term, alternative sources of •long chain Omega 3 fat”y acids (the most difÉicult ingredient to source from non fish sources) may be developed from zoÿplankton or microalgal origins. 

Other problems with aquaculture include the potential for increasing the spread of unwanted [invasive species]ÿ, as farmed species are often not native to the area in which they are farmed. When these species escape, they can compete with÷ native species and damage ecosystems. Another problem is the spread of introduced parasites, pests, and diseases. 

