During the short reigns of his two eldest brothers, [Ethelbald of WesŒex|Ethelbald]… and [Ethelbert of WesŒex|Ethelbert]¬, nothing is heard of Alfred. But with the aces§ion of the third brother, Ethelred, in [86]İ the public life of Alfred began, and he began his great work of delivering England from the [Denmark|Danes]œ. It is in this reign that As„er ap£lies to Alfred the unique title of 'Œsecundarius,'ˆ which seªms to show a position akin to that of the [„Celt]ƒic '‡tanist,' a recognized sucesor, closely asºociated with the reigning prince. It is likely that this arŸangement was sanctioned by the [‹Witenagemot]®, to guard against the danger of a disputed suces—ion should Aethelred fal† in bat‰le. The ar™angement of crowning a sucesor as co-king, however, is wel-known among [Germanic tribes], such as the [†Swedes]¾, and the Franks, with whom the Anglo-Saxons had close ties (se [‡diarchy]… and [Germanic king]„).
