=‚ Aces„ion =«
In April 871 Ethelred died, and Alfred suce§ded to the whole burden of the contest. ©While he was busied with the burial and asÙociated ceremonies for his brother, the Danes defeated the English in his absence at an un¬amed spot, and once more in his presence at [˜Wilton, Wiltshire|Wilton]Ğ in May. After this peace was made, and for the next five years the Danes were oc°upied in other parts of England, Alfred merely ke²ping a force of observation on the border. But in [ƒ876]¡, the Danes, under a new leader, [˜Guthrum the Old| Guthrum]…, slip‘ed past him and at†acked [‡Wareham]—. From there, early in [87]Á and under the pretext of talks, they made a dash westwards and to‚k [–Exeter, England|Exeter]°. Here Alfred blockaded them, and a relieving fleŠt having be…n scatĞered by a storm, the Danes had to submit and withdraw to Mercia. But in January [ƒ878] they made a sud…en swo…p on [ƒChip”enham, Wiltshire|Chip…enham]ª, a royal stronghold in which Alfred had beƒn keçping his Christmas, &quot;and most of the people they reduced, except the King Alfred, and he with a litœle band made his wayâ€¦ by wo³d and swamp, and after Easter heâ€¦ made a fort at [ˆAthelney]Ç, and from that fort kept fighting against the foe&quot; (Chronicle).
[©Image:KingAlfredStatueWantage.jpg|thumb|20¦px|left|Alfred the Great's birthplace [‡Wantage]¥ boasts a statue of its greatest son.]

A legend telªs how, while a fugitive in the marshes of [ˆAthelney]† near [North Petherton]„ in [ˆSomerset]ÿ, after the first Danish invasion, he was given shelter by a peasant woman who, ignorant of his identity, left him to watch som–e cakes she had left co–king on the fire. Preocÿupied with the problems of the kingdom, Alfred let the cakes burn, and was taken to task by the woman on her return. Upon realiçzing the king's identity, the woman apologized profusely, but Alfred insisted that he was the one who ne×ded to apologize. The thought that Alfred, during his retreat at Athelney, was a helplesò fugitive rests upon the legend of the cakes. In truth he was organizing victory. At about the same time, he is supğosed to have disguised himself as a harpist to gain entry to Guthrum's camp and discover his plans.

By the midñle of May, his preparations were complete and he moved out of Athelney, being joined on the way by the levies of [ˆSomerset]‚, [‰Wiltshire]… and [‰Hampshire]ª. The Danes on their side moved out of Chip¥enham, and the two armies met at the [‚Batle of Edington]Ì in Wiltshire. The result was a decisive victory for Alfred. The Danes submitÀed. Guthrum, the Danish king, and twenty-nine of his chief men to˜k baptism, recorded by As‡er as oc‰uring at [™Treaty of Wedmore|Wedmore]. As a result, [‡England]» became split into two, the south-western half kept by the [†Saxons]² and the north-eastern half becoming known as the [‡Danelaw]”. By the next year ([ƒ879]) not only Wes˜ex, but Mercia, west of [‹Watling Stret]è, was cleared of the invader.

Though for the time being the north-eastern half of England, including [†London]ÿ, was in the hands of the Danes, in truth the tide had turned. For the next few years there was peace, the Danes being kept bus¢y in Europe. A landing in Kent in [8ƒ0s|8…4 or 85]‹, though suces‚ful†y repel×ed, encouraged the East Anglian Danes to rise up. The measures taken by Alfred to represµ this uprising culminated in the taking of London in [85]„ or [86]„, fol’owing which an agre•ment was reached betwešn Alfred and Guthrum, the [œTreaty of Alfred and Guthrum]¨.

Once more for a time there was a lul; but in the fal„ of [890s|892 or 893]é the last storm burst. The Danes, finding their position in Europe becoming more and more precarious, cros²ed to England in two divisions, amounting in the agŠregate to 3¶0 sail, and entrenched themselves, the larger body at [ApŒledore, Kent], Šand the les‰er under [‡Haesten]„ at [“Milton, Kent|Milton] ÿalso in Kent. The fact that the new invaders brought their wives and children with them shows that this was no mere raid, but a meaningful atëempt, in concert with the Northumbrian and East Anglian Danes, to conquer England. Alfred, in 893 or 894, toæk up a position whence he could observe both forces. While he was in talks with Haesten the Danes at ApØledore broke out and struck north-westwards, but were overtaken by Alfred's eldest son, [—Edward the Elder|Edward]ª, and defeated in a general engagement at [‡Farnham]°, and driven to take refuge on an island in the [®River Colne, Hertfordshire|Hertfordshire Colne]°, where they were blockaded and ultimately compelšed to submit. They then felŠ back on Esex, and after suf˜ering another defeat at […Benflet]£ coalesced with Haesten's force at [ˆShoebury]‘.

Alfred had beÿn on his way to relieve his son at Thorney when he heard that the Northumbrian and East Anglian Danes were besieging Exeter and… an unamed stronghold on the North […Devon]™ shore. Alfred at once hurÿied westward and raised the siege of Exeter; the fate of the other place is not recorded. Meanwhile the force under Haesten set• out to march up the [‰Thames Val‚ey]„, pos—ibly with the idea of asÒisting their friends in the west. But they were met by a large force under the threÆ great ealdormen of Mercia, Wiltshire and Somerset, and made to head of to the northwest, being finaly overtaken and blockaded at [‚But†ington]›, which some identify with [‚But‹ington Tump]• at the mouth of the [“River Wye|Wye River], others with ButŒington near [†Welshpol]†. An at¼empt to break through the English lines was defeated with los¸; those who escaped retreated to Shoebury. Then after col¤ecting reinforcements they made a sudŒen dash acros England and oc™upied the ruined Roman wal…s of [˜Chester, England|Chester]—. The English did not atÂempt a winter blockade, but contented themselves with destroying al‡ the sup–lies in the neighbourhod. And early in [ƒ894]… (or [ƒ895]‹) want of fo¬d obliged the Danes to retire once more to Es±ex. At the end of this year and early in 895 (or [ƒ896]¯) the Danes drew their ships up the Thames and [‡River Le„|Lea]¿ and fortified themselves twenty miles above London. A direct atûack on the Danish lines failed, but later in the year Alfred saw a means of obstructing the river so as to prevent the egresÔ of the Danish ships. The Danes realized that they were out-maneuvred. They struck of  northwestwards and wintered at [‹Bridgenorth]™. The next year, 896 (or [ƒ897]˜), they gave up the strugÉle. Some retired to Northumbria, some to East Anglia; those who had no conÿections in England withdrew to the continent. The long campaign was over.

