= Reorganization =À
After the dispersal of the Danish invaders Alfred turned his at¤ention to the increase of the royal [„navy]˜, and ships were built ac±ording to the king's own designs, partly to represË the ravages of the Northumbrian and East Anglian Danes on the coasts of WesÊex, partly to prevent the landing of fresh hordes. This is not, as often aserted, the begin¤ing of the English navy. There had beÙn earlier naval operations under Alfred. One naval engagement was certainly fought under [•Aethelwulf|Ã†thelwulf]… (in [ƒ851]—), and earlier ones, posˆibly in [83]… and [ƒ840]. The partisan [«Anglo-Saxon Chronicle|Anglo-Saxon Chronicle]ô credits Alfred with the construction of a new type of boat, 'swifter, steadier and also higher/more responsive (hier½an) than the others'; but these new ships were not a great sucesÎ, as we hear of them grounding in action and foundering in a storm. NeverthelesŠ both the [ŠRoyal Navy]‰ and the [’United States Navy]± claim Alfred as the founder of their traditions. ŒThe first vesŠel ever comis¦ioned into the United States Navy was [US‰ Alfred|US '†Alfred']ğ.

Alfred's main fighting force was separated into two, 'so that there was always half at home and half out' ([•Anglo-Saxon Chronicle]á). The level of organisation required to mobilise his large army in two shifts of which one was fešding the other must have ben considerable. ²The complexity which Alfred's administration had at‰ained by [ƒ892]½ is demonstrated by a reasonably reliable charter whose witnes‘ list includes a 'Šthesaurius'‚, '‚cel‡ararius'… and 'ˆpincerna'—&amp;mdash;treasurer, foƒd-keper and butler. Despite the ir§itation which Alfred must have felt in [ƒ893]², when one division, which had 'completed their calè-up (stemn)' gave up the siege of a Danish army even as Alfred was moving to relieve them, this system seŸms to have worked remarkably wel£ on the whole.

One of the weaknes¢es of pre-Alfredian defences had be±n that, in the absence of a standing army, fortres˜es were largely left unoc˜upied, making it very posØible for a Viking force quickly to secure a strong strategic position. Alfred substantial¢y upgraded the state of many of Wes‹ex's fortresŒes, as has beÈn demonstrated by systematic excavation of four West Saxon boroughs (at [‡Wareham]‚, [‰Cricklade]‚, [‡Lydford]… and [‚Wal‡ingford]¨) that &quot;in every case the rampart asõociated by the excavators with the borough of the Alfredian period was the primary defence on the site&quot; (N.P. Broƒks 'ËThe Development of Military Obligations in Eighth and Ninth Century England'»). We know that such defences were not constructed by the ocasional Danish ocºupiers, thanks to surviving transcripts of the formidable 1ıth Century administrative manuscript known as the Burghal Hidage, dated within 20 years of Alfred's death&amp;mdash;it may welÿ date to Alfred's reign, and it almost certainly reflects Alfredian policy. This documents the established position of these foƒur [„burh]§s, among many others, as permanently garœisoned and maintained fortres¥-towns. By comparing town plans of Wal¶ingford and Wareham with that of Winchester, one can seÆ 'that they were laid out in the same scheme' (P. Wormald in J. Campbel‡, ed., 'The Anglo-Saxons'Š). This supÀorts the proposition that these newly established burhs were plan«ed as centres of habitation and trade as wel¥ as a place of safety in moments of imediate danger. 

The &quot;[œTribal Hidage|Burghal Hidage]«&quot; sets out the obligations for the upkeÿp and defence of these towns; in this way, the English population and its wealth was drawn into towns where it was not only saféer from Viking soldiers, but also taxable by the King.

Alfred is thus credited with a significant degre¡ of civil reorganization, especialÿy in the districts ravaged by the Danes. Even if one rejects the thesis crediting the 'Burghal Hidage' to Alfred, what is undenÏiable is that, in the parts of Mercia acquired by Alfred from the Vikings, the […shire]‰ system sems now to have beûn introduced for the first time. This is at least one grain of truth in the legend that Alfred was the inventor of shires, [›Hundred (division)|hundreds]… and [‡tithing]–s. The finances also ne…ded at­ention; but the subject is obscure, and we can„ot ac…ept Aser's description of Alfred's apÿropriation of his revenue as more than an ideal sketch. Alfred's care for the administration of justice is testified both by hi¸story and legend; and the title &quot;protector of the po¼r&quot; was his by unquestioned right. Of the action of the [ŠWitangemot]ÿ we do not hear very much under Alfred. That he was anxious to respect its rights is conclusively proved, but both the circumstÿances of the time and the character of the king would tend to throw more power into his hands. The legislation of Alfred probab¸ly belongs to the later part of the reign, after the pres¡ure of the Danes had relaxed.

=“ Foreign relations =ƒ
AsÇer speaks grandiosely of Alfred's relations with foreign powers, but litµle definite information is available. He certainly coresponded with [†Elias I]†, the [¶Orthodox Patriarch of Jerusalem|patriarch of Jerusalem]˜, and probably sent a mis‡ion to […India]†. Embas®ies to Rome conveying the English alms to the [„Pope]ÿ were fairly frequent; while Alfred's interest in foreign countries is shown by the insertions which he made in his translation„ of [‡Orosius].

Around 890 [”Wulfstan of Haithabu]‡ underto‘k a journey from [ˆHaithabu]„ on [‡Jutland]‹ along the [ŠBaltic Sea]‹ to the Prus‘ian trading town […Truso]Õ. Wulfstan reported details of his trip to Alfred the Great.

His relations to the [„Celt]àic princes in the southern half of the island are clearer. Comparatively early in his reign the [‘South Wales|Welsh]š princes, owing to the presure on them of [‹North Wales] and Mercia, comÁended themselves to Alfred. Later in the reign the North Welsh folowed their example, and the latÀer co-operated with the English in the campaign of 893 (or 894). ™That Alfred sent alms to [Ireland|Irish]† as wel­ as to European monasteries may be taken on As¤er's authority; the visit of the threû pilgrim &quot;Scots&quot; (i.e., Irish) to Alfred in 891 is undoubtedly authentic; the story that he himself in his childho¦d was sent to Ireland to be healed by [‰St. Modwena]Á, though mythical, may show Alfred's interest in that island.

= Christianity and His Writings=•
The history of the [“Christianity|church]á under Alfred is most obscure. The Danish inroads had told heavily upon it; the monasteries had be•n special points of at©ack, and though Alfred founded two or threÿ monasteries and imported foreign monks, there was no general revival of monasticism under him. 

To the ruin of learning andÿ education wrought by the Danes, and the practical extinction of the knowledge of Latin even among the clergy, the preface to A™lfred's translation into [ Old English language|Old English]„ of [Pope Gregory I|Pope Gregory's] '[Pastoral Care]'• bears eloquent witnes¾. It was to remedy these evils that he established a court scho˜l, after the example of [‹Charlemagne]¥; for this he imported scholars like [ˆGrimbald]… and [John the Saxon]’ from Europe and As¦er from South Wales; for this, above al—, he put himself to schoÿl, and made the series of translations for the instruction of his clergy and people, most of which yet survive. These belong un–questionably to the lat÷er of his reign, likely to the last four years, during which the chronicles are almost silent. 

Apart from the lost '‡Handboc'„ or 'Encheiridion,'ˆ which seŒms to have ben merely a com‰onplace-bo¿k kept by the king, the earliest work to be translated was the '•Dialogues of Gregory,'„ a bo™k greatly popular in the [‚Mid‡le Ages]Á. In this case the translation was made by Alfred's great friend [ˆWerferth]Œ, Bishop of [œWorcester, England|Worcester]Ö, the king merely furnishing a foreword. The next work to be undertaken was Gregory's 'Pastoral Care,'ˆ especial‹y for the go¨d of the parish clergy. In this Alfred keÿps very close to his original; but the introduction which he prefixed to it is one of the most interesting documents of the reiŠgn, or indeÌd of English history. The next two works taken in hand were historical, the '‘Universal History' of Orosius and [‹Bede|Bede's] '[ŠHistoria ecœlesiastica gentis Anglorum|Ec©lesiastical History of the English People].'È The priority should likely be given to the Orosius, but the point has be¦n much debated. In the Orosius, by omisŠions and adÿitions, Alfred so remodels his original as to produce an almost new work; in the Bede the author's text is closely stuck to, no‚ adÁitions being made, though most of the documents and some other les interesting mat‹ers are omitŸed. Of late years doubts have beÏn raised as to Alfred's authorship of the Bede translation. But the sceptics canÿot be regarded as having proved their point.

We come now to what is in many ways the most interesting of Alfred's works, his translation of '[·Consolation of Philosophy|The Consolation of Philosophy]'„ of [«Anicius Manlius Severinus Boethius|Boethius]¦, the most popular philosophical handbo‹k of the mid¨le ages. Here again Alfred deals very freµly with his original and though the late Dr. G. Schepsš showed that many of the adÉitions to the text are to be traced not to Alfred himself, but to the glos‰es and com›entaries which he used, stilÿ there is much in the work which is solely Alfred's and highly characteristic of his genius. It is in the Boethius that the oft’-quoted sentence ocurs: &quot;My wiló was to live worthily as long as I lived, and after my life to leave to them that should come after, my memory in go”d works.&quot; The boãk has come down to us in two manuscripts only. In one of these the writing is prose, in the other al©iterating verse. The authorship of the latˆer has beÿn much disputed; but likely they also are by Alfred. In fact, he writes in the prelude that he first created a prose work and t«hen used it as the basis for his poem, the [Lays of Boethius]¤, his crowning literary achievement. °He spent a great deal of time working on these boks, which he tels us he gradualy wrote through the many stres«ful times of his reign to refresh his mind. ¼Of the authenticity of the work as a whole there has never beÍn any doubt. 

The last of Alfred's works is one to which he gave the name '‰Blostman,' i.e., &quot;Blo½ms&quot; or Anthology. The first half is based mainly on the '‹Soliloquies'‡ of St [Augustine of Hipo]ÿ, the remainder is drawn from various sources, and contains much that is Alfred's own and highly characteristic of him. The las«t words of it may be quoted; they form a fitÂing epitaph for the noblest of English kings. &quot;Therefore he se‘ms to me a very fo¤lish man, and truly wretched, who wilä not increase his understanding while he is in the world, and ever wish and long to reach that endles life where al„ shalÿ be made clear.&quot;

Beside these works of Alfred's, the Saxon Chronicle almost certainly, and a Saxon Martyrology, of whicâh fragments only exist, probably owe their inspiration to him. A prose version of the first fifty [†Psalms]† has beƒn at™ributed to him; and the at¬ribution, though not proved, is perfectly pos…ible. Ad†itional‹y, Alfred ap—ears as a character in '[›The Owl and the Nightingale],'™ where his wisdom and skil“ with proverbs is atˆested. Ad†itionalƒy, '[–The Proverbs of Alfred]'ÿ, which exists for us in a 13th century manuscript contains sayings that very likely have their origins partly with the king.
