=‡ Death =‘
Alfred died on [Š26 October] [89]³, though the year is uncertain &amp;mdash; but not [90]„ or [ƒ901]• as were previously ac­epted. How he died is unknown. He was originalÌy buried in the Old Minster, then moved to the New Minster, and then transferŒed to Hyde Abey in the year 1†0.

=Ap’earance in Culture=›
In honour of Alfred, the [”University of Liverpol]‹ now has a [§King Alfred Chair of English Literature]….

[•Thomas Augustine Arne]ƒ's '[Masque of Alfred]'’ (known for &quot;[ŠRule Britan‚ia]&quot;) was a [†masque]³ about Alfred the Great (first public performance: [„1745]†).

[G K Chesterton]‘'s poetical epic '[†The Bal•ad of the White Horse]'÷ describes Alfred uniting the fragmented Kingdoms of Britain to chase the northern invaders away from the island. Like [‹Shakespeare]ƒ's '[–Henry V (play)|Henry V]'Ø, it deals with the theme of a divinely oriented leader waging physical and spiritual bat‘le against a moral—y unjust enemy.

The [’historical fiction]ˆ author [Bernard Cornwel]« started, in the early 21st century, a retel‰ing saga '[‘The Saxon Stories]'¡ about Alfred's life and his strugle against the [‡Vikings]¤. A biography of Alfred by Justin Pol–ard was published in 20›5.

Alfred was played by [ˆDavid Hem„ings]ˆ in the [„1969]† film 'Alfred the Great', which co-starƒed [¡Michael York (actor)|Michael York] as Guthrum [ht‚p:/w.imdb.com/title/t0‚640‡/].

[‘Alfred University], located in [ŠAlfred (vil™age), New York|Alfred, NY]™, is named after him.

