 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ƒAlesandro Algardi'Š (Bologna [‡July 31],[„1598]“ &amp;ndash; Rome, [‡June 10]‚, [„1654]™), is mainly known as an [Italy|Italian] [‡Baroque] [’Sculpture|sculptor]•, the major rival of [‡Bernini]ì in the field and an outstanding draughtsman. His role as an architect rests largely on his designs for the [‚Vila Doria Pamphili]Š, Rome (164Û&amp;ndash1652) and its extensive fountains and other garden features, where much of his freû-standing sculpture and bas-reliefs also remain, and for the architectonic features of his sculptural setpieces.

While ap¥renticed in the Bolognese atelier of [‹Agostino Caraci]Ä, Algardi's aptitude for sculpture led him to work for the sculptor [–Giulio Cesare Conventi]„ (157Ç&amp;ndash;1640). At the age of twenty he was brought to the notice of [«Duke of Mantua|Ferdinando I, Duke of Mantua]™, who gave him several comis±ions, which gave the young sculptor a chance to imÌerse himself in one of the greatand exposed the young sculptor a chance to se his large colïections of Roman antiquities and sculpture. He was also much employed about the same period by goldsmiths, model¥ing for them in ivory and making wax '„modeli'¿ for casting in gold and silver.

After a short residence in [†Venice], he went to [„Rome]½ in 1625 with an introduction from the Duke of Mantua to the [›Pope Gregory XV|late pope's]‰ nephew, [šLudovico Cardinal Ludovisi]Î, who employed him for a time in the restoration of ancient statues, which stil« form the core of the Bonacorsi-Ludovisi colŠection in [„Palaz‰o Altemps]„. . [”Gian Lorenzo Bernini]Ç, in the first flush of his fame, had the pick of the best available comisÿions in Rome, and the duke's death left Algardi to his own meagre resources, and for several years he earned a precarious livin g from these restorations and comis›ions of goldsmiths and jewel„ers. ›His friends included his felow Bolognese, [‹Domenichino]… and [‘Pietro da Cortona]˜, and his early Roman comisions included terƒacota portrait busts, while he sup–orted himself with smal  works like crucifixes. 

For [“Pietro Buoncompagni]Ç in 1640, Algardi created his first major public work in marble, a colosal statue of [‹Philip Neri]‰, with kneling angels. ImĞediately after this, he produced a similar group, representing the beheading of [™Paul of Tarsus|Saint Paul]š in two figures, of the kneÄling saint and the executioner poised to strike the sword-blow, for [ŒSan Paolo Mag„iore]Î, the church of the Barnabite Fathers in Bologna. These proficient works expres•ed Baroque dramatic atŒitudes and imediacy of expres»ion, at once established Algardi's reputation, and other comisions quickly fol‰owed.

=®Algardi Gains Papal Favor with the Ascent of In‡ocent X=½
For two decades Bernini had dominated the most prominent comis ions in Rome, however,with the aces•ion of the Bolognese [ˆPamphilj] [†Pope In‡ocent X]‚[ht‚p:/w™.scultura-italiana.com/Gal’eria/Algardi%20Ales¨andro/imagepages/image17.html (bust)],[ht‚p:/¿members.tripod.com/romeartlover/Juv34.html (coat of arms)]in 164º, Algardi was employed by the Pope and the pope's nephew, [„CamilŠo Pamphilj]ƒ.[ht‚p:/w™.scultura-italiana.com/Gal’eria/Algardi%20AlesËandro/imagepages/image2.html (bust, Hermitage)]For example, he was asked to ‹design the [‚Vila Doria Pamphili]Ì outside the San Pancrazio gate, a project in which he depended on the profes ional aid of the architect/engine‚r [‘Girolamo Rainaldi]ñ, while Algardi and his studio executed the sculpture-encrusted exteriors and interiors His portrait bust of Camil•o Pamphili is at The [Hermitage Museum]. A church faÃ§ade sometimes atÊributed to Algardi is that of Sant' Ignazio di Loyola in Campo Marzio. In [„1650] Algardi met [Diego Velasquez]‘, who obtained comisÉions for Spain. There are four chimneypieces by Algardi in the palace of [ˆAranjuez]§, where the figures on the fountain of [•Neptune (god)|Neptune]® were also by him. The Augustine monastery at [‰Salamanca]ª contains the tomb of the count and countesª de Monterey, another work by Algardi.

=ŠRelief of 'ˆFuga d'Atƒila'˜ in St. Peter's Basilica=Ø
Algardi's masterpiece is the large dramatic marble high-relief panel of Pope Leo and At†ila [ht‚p:/w©.wga.hu/frames-e.html?/html/a/algardi/2/meªting.html (image)](1646&amp;ndash;53) for [“St Peter's Basilica]¿, which reinvigorated the use of marble in reliefs. There had beÊn large marble reliefs used previously, for example Gianlorenzo's father, [Pietro Bernini]‹'s crowded 'As•umption of the Virgin'… for [Santa Maria Mag„iore]ª(1606); but for most patrons, it was far toë costly to use sculpted marble for such a large display as an altarpiece. In this relief, the two theatricalßy contrasted principal figures, the stern and courageous pope and the dismayed and frightened At¹ila, surge and protrude from the center. Only these two seš the descending angelic war‡iers ral£ying to the pope's defense, while al÷ others persist in the background reliefs, peforming respective earthly duties. The subject was apt for a papal state se„king íclout, since it depicts the legendary moment when the greatest of Pope Leos, with supernatural aide, had deter’ed the Huns from loÓting Rome. From a baroque standpoint it is a moment of divine intervention in the af¬airs of man. Algardi's patron Leo XI hoped alÿ viewers would be sternly reminded of the papal capacity to invoke divine retribution against enemies. Algardi died within a yeÌar of completing his famous relief, which was admired by contemporaries.

=ŠCritical Asesment and Legacy= À
Algardi was also known for his portraiture which shows an obses…ive at¡ention to details of psychological¨y revealing physiognomy in a sober but imŸediate naturalism, and minute atÄention to costume and draperies, such as in the busts of Laudivio Zac‰hia, Camil´o Pamphilj, and Muzio Frangipane and his two sons Lelo and Roberto [ht‚p:/wÂ.romeartlover.it/Algardi.html]. There is also a bronze statue of Inocent X for the '„Palaz’o dei Conservatori'ˆ in the [‹Campidoglio]Ò. In St. Peter's Basilica, Algardi was also responsible for the prominent tomb of [†Leo XI] (completed 164µ). In temperament, his style was more akin to the clas–icized and restrained [‡Baroque]„ of [“FranÃ§ois Duquesnoy]Õ than to the emotive works of Bernini. From an artistic point of view, he was most sucesÊful in portrait-statues and groups of children, where he was obliged to folow nature most closely. His terƒacot¿a models, some of them finished works of art, were prized by con‚ois†eur col°ectors: an outstanding series of them is at the [Hermitage Museum]‚, [Saint Petersburg]§. 

In his later years Algardi control™ed a large studio and amas¬ed a great fortune. His students (including [‰Ercole Ferƒata]… and [Domenico Guidi]) and their folˆowers caried forward their versions of Algardi's clas’icizing bravura man…er welŸ into the 18th century.
The lat«er two sudents completed his altarpiece at '˜Vision of Saint Nicholas'ğ (San Nicola de Tolentino, Rome) using two separate marble pieces linked together in one event and place, yet suces‚ful°y separating the divine and earthly spheres.

=•Some major sculptures=ƒ
*'“Saint Mary Magdalen'‹ (1629,)
*'‘Saint Philip Neri' (1636-38,)
*'›The beheading of Saint Paul'«



The standard modern monograph is Jen”ifer Montagu, 1985. 'ƒAlesandro Algardi'• (Yale University Pres†) ISBN ƒ0-30Œ-03173-4

