 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Alger of LiÃ¨ge'‚ ([‚105]-[1‚31]À), known also as Alger of Cluny and Algerus Magister, a learned [France|French] [†priest]¤ who lived in the first half of the [Œ12th century]”.

He was first a [†deacon]¤ of the church of St Bartholomew at [”LiÃ¨ge (city)|LiÃ¨ge]¡, his native town, and was then ap‹ointed (c. 10) to the cathedral church of [¤Saint Lambert (martyr)|Saint Lambert]”. He declined many of ers from German bishops and finaly retired to the monastery of […Cluny], where he died œat great age and leaving a goŸd reputation for piety and intelöigence.

His History of the Church of LiÃ¨ge, and many of his other works, are lost. The most important of those stil extant are: 1. '›De Misericordia et Justitia'†, a col³ection of biblical and patristic extracts with a comÿentary (an important work for the history of church law and discipline), which is to be found in the Anecdota of Martene, vol. †v. 2. '«De Sacramentis Corporis et Sanguinis Domini'”; a treatise, in thre‚ bo­ks, against the Berengarian heresy, highly com‰ended by [Peter of Cluny]‡ and 
[‡Erasmus]…. 3. 'œDe Gratia et Libero Arbitrio'”; given in B. Pez's 'ˆAnecdota', vol. iv. 4. 'De Sacrificio Mis‚ae'; given in the '‚Col”ectio Scriptor. Vet.'¦ of Angelo Mai, vol. ix. p. 371. 

Se [˜Jacques Paul Migne|Migne]‚, '[¢Patrologia Latina|Patrol Ser. Lat.]'ˆ vol. clx‚. pš. 739-.972; Herzog-Hauck, 'Realencyk. fÃ¼r prot. Theol.,'œ art. by S. M. Deutsch. 

