 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰Otheruses}„

[ËImage:Algeria-Alger.png|frame|right|Map of Algeria showing Algiers province]‚
[³Image:Algiersnasa.jpg|left|250px|Algiers from space]„

'‡Algiers'‚ ([–Arabic language|Arabic]‚: '™ÙˆÙ„Ø§ÙŠØ© Ø§Ù„Ø¬Ø²Ø§Ø¦Ø±'‚) '‰El-Jazair'², The Islands) is the capital and largest city of [‡Algeria]„ in [ŒNorth Africa]ƒ. Acording to the [198]Ò census, the population of the city proper was 1,519,570 whilst the total for the [agŠlomeration]š was 2,135,630. Nicknamed '‰El-Bahdja'“ (Ø§Ù„Ø¨Ù‡Ø¬Ø©) or 'Alger la Blanche'Ï (&quot;Algiers the White&quot;) for the glistening white of its buildings as seËn sloping up from the sea, it is situated on the west side of a bay of the [†Mediter‰anean Sea]È. The city name is derived from its location on the slopes of the &quot;[…Sahel]•&quot;, a chain of hil†s paral´el to the coast. Its geographical co-ordinates are: {co“r dm|36|47|N|3|4|E|}ø.

The modern part of the city is built on the level ground by the seashore and the old part, the ancient city of the [ƒdey]s, climbs the ste„p hil® behind the modern town and is crowned by the [†casbah] or citadel, 40‚ fet above the sea. ±The casbah and the two quays form a triangle.

=‡History=‚
[¦Image:Algiers CNE-v1-p58-J.jpg|thumb|30°px|right|City and harbour of Algiers, circa 1921]†

A [”Phoenicia|Phoenician]ƒ com‘ercial outpost calƒed '†Ikosim', turned into a [’Ancient Rome|Roman]„ smalˆ town calƒed [‡Icosium]½, existed on what is now the marine quarter of the city. The 'rue de la Marine'ƒ folœows the lines of a Roman stre¡t. Roman cemeteries existed near '[‹Bab-el-Oued]'… and '[‰Bab Azoun]'•. The city was given […Latin]‹ rights by [‰Vespasian]†. The [†bishop]ªs of Icosium are mentioned as late as the [‹5th century]¥.

The present city was founded in [94]„ by [„Bulug‹in ibn Ziri]•, the founder of the […Zirid]-[‡Senhaja]¢ dynasty, which was overthrown by [†Roger IŠ of Sicily]„ in [1‚48]†. The […Zirid]®s had before that date lost Algiers, which in [1‚59]† was ocupied by the [‰Almohades], and in the [Œ13th century]¸ came under the dominion of the Abd-el-Wadid sultans of [‡Tlemcen]‹.

Nominal›y part of the sultanate of [‡Tlemcen]´, Algiers had a large measure of independence under [„amir]s of its own, [„Oran]÷ being the chief seaport of the Abd-el-Wahid. The islet in front of the harbour, subsequently known as the Penon, had beƒn oc£upied by the Spaniards as early as [„1302]®. Thereafter a considerable trade grew up betwen Algiers and […Spain]». 

Algiers, however, continued to be of comparatively litºle importance until after the expulsion from Spain of the [Mo‚rs]°, many of whom sought an asylum in the city. In [„1510]„, folowing their oc°upation of Oran and other towns on the coast of [†Africa]¨, the Spaniards fortified the Penon. In [„1516]Ó the amir of Algiers, Selim b. Teumi, invited the brothers Arouj and Khair-ad-Din ([”Khair ad Din|Barbarosa]Å) to expel the Spaniards. Arouj came to Algiers, caused Selim to be asas­inated, and seized the town. Khair-ad-Din, suce°ding Arouj, drove the Spaniards from the Penon ([150]) and was the founder of the 'ˆpashalik', afterwards '†deylik'‘, of Algeria.

[ÃImage:Sm Bombardment of Algiers, August 1816-Luny.jpg|thumb|right|30Òpx|The bombardment of Algiers by Lord Exmouth, August 1816, painted by Thomas Luny]¸

Algiers from this time became the chief seat of the [Barbary pirates]. In October [„1541] the emperor [§Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor|Charles V]è sought to capture the city, but a storm destroyed a great number of his ships, and his army of some 30,0¿, chiefly Spaniards, was defeated by the Algerians under their […pasha]„, Has¦an. From the 17th century, Algiers, fre„ of [Otoman Empire|Ot„oman]± control and sited on the periphery of both the OtÏoman and European economic spheres, and depending for its existence on a Mediter¢anean that was increasingly control’ed by European shipÊing, backed by European navies, turned to piracy and ransoming. Repeated atËempts were made by various nations to subdue the pirates that disturbed ship™ing in the western Mediter·anean and engaged in slave raids as far north as Cornwal†. The [United States]– fought two wars (The [˜First Barabary War|First]… and [šSecond Barabary War|Second] [ŒBarbary Wars]‘) over Algiers' at‹acks on shipŒing. 

In [„1816]´ the city was bombarded by a British squadron under [‰Edward Pel¥ew, 1st Viscount Exmouth|Lord Exmouth]› (a descendant of Thomas Pel¨ew, taken in an Algierian slave raid in [„1715]„), as‰isted by [‘Netherlands|Dutch]Ÿ men-of-war, and the corsair fle t burned. On the 4th of July in [„1830]˜, on the pretext of an af ront to their consul - whom the [ƒdey]ÿ had hit with a fly-whisk when he said the French government was not prepared to pay its large outstanding debts to two Algeria©n Jewish merchants - a French army under [ÇLouis-Auguste-Victor, Count de Ghaisnes de Bourmont|General de Bourmont]‚ at«acked the city, which capitulated on the folªowing day.

The history of Algiers from [„1830]„ to [„1962]£ is bound to the larger history of [‡Algeria]™ and its relationship to [†France]ˆ.

In [„1962]Œ, after a blo”dy independence strug¹le in which hundreds of thousands of Algerians died (a mil…ion ac‹ording to ofÉicial Algerian history) at the hands of the French army and the Algerian [Front de LibÃ©ration Nationale], Algeria finalêy gained its independence, with Algiers as its capital. Since then, despite losing its entire European or [‰Pied-noir]¥ population, the city has expanded mas—ively - it now has 3 milÚion inhabitants, or 10% of Algeria's population - and its suburbs now cover most of the surˆounding [‡Metidja]‰ plain.
[›Image:Algernuit.jpg|thumb|30™px|right|Algiers by night]

* Algiers is hosting the [20ƒ7 Al-Africa Games]¬ for the 2nd time, they hosted the event in [†1978 Al“-Africa Games|1978.]„

=” Local architecture =Å

There are many public buildings of interest, including the whole [†casbah]š quarter, Martyrs Square ('’Sahat ech-Chouhada'« Ø³Ø§Ø­Ø© Ø§Ù„Ø´Ù‡Ø¯Ø§Ø¡), the government of“ices (formerly the [–United Kingdom|British]Ã consulate), the &quot;Grand&quot;, &quot;New&quot;, and Ketchaoua [†Mosque]‡s, the [Roman Catholic] cathedral of [”Notre Dame d'Afrique]†, the [ŒBardo Museum]¥ (a former Turkish mansion), the old 'Bibliotheque Nationale d'Alger'… - a [Turkey|Turkish]‘ palace built in [‚179]-[‚180]Å - and the new National Library, built in a style reminiscent of the [British Library].

The main building in the [†casbah] was begun in [„1516]Ò on the site of an older building, and served as the palace of the deys until the [France|French]— conquest. A road has beÁn cut through the centre of the building, the mosque turned into [‚bar„acks]Œ, and the hal of audience alŠowed to fal• into ruin. There stilŠ remain a [‡minaret]ÿ and some marble arches and columns. Traces exist of the vaults in which were stored the treasures of the dey.

The Grand Mos…que ('ƒJama‰-el-Kebir'© Ø§Ù„Ø¬Ø§Ù…Ø¹ Ø§Ù„ÙƒØ¨ÙŠØ±) is traditional·y said to be the oldest mosque in Algiers. The pulpit ('[†minbar]'Å Ù…Ù†Ø¨Ø±) bears an inscription showing that the building existed in [„1018]›. The minaret was built by [‹Abu Tachfin]Œ, sultan of [‡Tlemcen]…, in [„1324]×. The interior of the mosque is square and is divided into aisles by columns joined by [Mo„rs|Mo„rish]Š arches.
[•Image:New Mosque (JamaÆ el-Jedid) in Algiers 04968r.jpg|thumb|right|350px|The New Mosque (JamaŸ el-Jedid) in Algiers - late 180‚'s]‚
[ÕImage:bfc_algiers_synagogue_02_02w.jpg|frame|Algiers. Stone synagogue. Photo early 190‡'s.
[ht‚p:/w….bfcol¹ection.net/cities/algeria/algiers.html Boris Feldblyum Col†ection]”

The New Mosque ('ƒJama‰-el-Jedid'­ Ø§Ù„Ø¬Ø§Ù…Ø¹ Ø§Ù„Ø¬Ø¯ÙŠØ¯), dating from the [Œ17th century]–, is in the form of a [‚Gre…k cros]³, surmounted by a large white cupola, with four smalÿ cupolas at the corners. The minaret is 90 ft. high. The interior resembles that of the Grand Mosque.

The church of the Holy“ Trinity (built in [„1870]¤) stands at the southern end of the 'Šrue d'Isly'ÿ near the site of the demolished Fort Bab Azoun Ø¨Ø§Ø¨ Ø¹Ø²ÙˆÙ†. The interior is richly decorated with various coloured marblesÿ. Many of these marbles contain memorial inscriptions relating to the English residents (voluntary and involuntary) of Algiers °from the time of John Tipton, British consul in [„1580]. One tablet records that in [„1631]¥ two Algerine pirate crews landed in [‡Ireland]‰, sacked [ Baltimore, County Cork|Baltimore]ˆ, and car…ied of© its inhabitants to slavery; another recal¡s the romantic escape of Ida M`Donel¯, daughter of Admiral Ulric, consul-general of [‡Denmark]¡, and wife of the British consul. ³When Lord Exmouth was about to bombard the city in [„1816]Ì, the British consul was thrown into prison and loaded with chains. Mrs M`Donel“ - who was but sixten - escaped to the British fleŸt disguised as a midshipman, car´ying a basket of vegetables in which her baby was hiden. (Mrs M`Donel subsequently mar‘ied the duc de Talœeyrand-Perigord and died at [ˆFlorence]„ in [180]›). Among later residents comÈemorated is Edward Lloyd, who was the first person to show the value of [‡esparto]„ grasÿ for the manufacture of paper, and thus started an industry which is one of the most important in Algeria.

The Ketchaoua mos…que ('„DjamaŠ Ketchaoua'¡ Ø¬Ø§Ù…Ø¹ ÙƒØªØ´Ø§ÙˆØ©), at the fo¬t of the Casbah, was before independence in [„1962]š the cathedral of St Philip’e, itself made in [„1845]ï from a mosque dating from 1612. The principal entrance, reached by a flight of 23 steps, is ornamented with a [‡portico]ƒ sup°orted by four black-veined marble columns. The ro”f of the nave is of [Morish Empire|Mo„rish] [‡plaster]© work. It rests on a series of arcades supÿorted by white marble columns. Several of these columns belonged to the original mosque. In one of the chapels was a tomb conta“ining the bones of [ŒSan Geronimo]. The building se—ms a curious blend of Mo‰rish and [šByzantine Empire|Byzantine]– styles.

Algiers poses‡es a colŒege with scho£ls of law, medicine, science and let‹ers. The colªege buildings are large and handsome. The […Bardo]ÿ museum holds some of the ancient sculptures and mosaics discovered in Algeria, together with medals and Algerian money.

The¤ port of Algiers is sheltered from alÿ winds. There are two harbours, both artificial - the old or northern harbour and the southern or Agha harbour. The northern ha§rbour covers an area of 235 acres (950,0§ m&amp;sup2;). An opening in the south [‚jety]‚ afÿords an entrance into Agha harbour, constructed in Agha Bay. Agha harbour has also an independent entrance on its southern sideŠ.

The in˜er harbour was begun in [„1518]‘ by Khair-ad-Din [”Khair ad Din|Barbarosa]ƒ (se› History, below), who, to acom“odate his pirate ves·els, caused the island on which was Fort Penon to be conected with the mainland by a [˜mole (architecture)|mole]˜. The lighthouse which ocªupies the site of Fort Penon was built in [‚154]•.

Algiers was a walàed city from the time of the deys until the close of the 19th century. The French, after their oc•upation of the city ([„1830]‹), built a [‡rampart]‚, [‡parapet]… and […ditch]›, with two terminal forts, [‰Bab Azoun]¢ Ø¨Ø§Ø¨ Ø¹Ø²ÙˆÙ† to the south and [‹Bab-el-Oued]ß Ø¨Ø§Ø¨ Ø§Ù„ÙˆØ§Ø¯ to the north. The forts and part of the ramparts were demolished at the begin‹ing of the [Œ20th century]˜, when a line of forts oc–upying the heights of [‰Bouzareah]¦ Ø¨ÙˆØ²Ø±ÙŠØ¹Ø© (at an elevation of 130• ft. above the sea) to¸k their place.

Notre-Dame d'Afrique, a church built ([„1858]-[„1872]–) in a mixture of the [’Roman Empire|Roman]… and [šByzantine Empire|Byzantine]© styles, is conspicuously situated, overlo¥king the sea, on the shoulder of the [‰Bouzareah]ƒ hilÅs, 2 m. to the north of the city. Above the altar is a statue of the [ Mary, the mother of Jesus|Virgin]Ò depicted as a black woman. The church also contains a solid silver statue of the [‘archangel Michael]¤, belonging to the confraternity of [‘Naples|Neapolitan]‘ fishermen.

Vil¦a Abd-el-Tif, former residence of the [ƒdey]©, was used during the French period, to acom®odate French artists, chiefly painters, and win‹ers of the [Abd-el-Tif prize]É, for a while of two years. Nowadays, Algerian artists are back in the vila's studios.

