 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'§Alhazen Abu Ali al-Hasan Ibn Al-Haitham'ˆ (also: 'Ibn al Haythen'ƒ) ([ƒ965]-[„1040]ƒ) ([–Arabic language|Arabic]µ: Ø£Ø¨Ùˆ Ø¹Ù„ÙŠ Ø§Ù„Ø­Ø³Ù† Ø¨Ù† Ø§Ù„Ù‡ÙŠØ«Ù…) was an [‡Arabian] [mathematician]”; he is sometimes calƒed 'ˆal-Basri'® (Arabic: Ø§Ù„Ø¨ØµØ±ÙŠ), after his birthplace [†Basrah]‚, [„Iraq]Š.

&lt;!-® Image with unknown copyright status removed: [·Image:Alhazen.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Portrait of Alhazen] -ˆ&gt;

=„Life=–
Alhazen was born at […Basra]œ, then part of the Islamic(Ab†asid) [‰Caliphate], now part of [„Iraq]ƒ (Seƒ [ht‚p:/wÅ-gap.dcs.st-and.ac.uk/~history/Mathematicians/Al-Haytham.html] and [ht‚p:/w³.answers.com/topic/alhazen]), and probably died in […Cairo]‚, […Egypt]‹. 

One acount of his careŒr has him sumˆoned to […Egypt]’ by the mercurial [†caliph] [‘al-Hakim bi-Amr Alˆah|Hakim]‘ to regulate the [‚flo„ding]ˆ of the [„Nile]ï. After his field work made him aware of the impracticality of this scheme, and fearing the caliph's anger, he [feigned madnes]›. He was kept under house ar›est until Hakim's death in [„1021]ÿ. During this time he wrote scores of important mathematical treatises.

Abu Ali Hasan Ibn al-Haitham was one of the most emiÿnent physicists, whose contributions to optics and the scientific methods are outstanding. Known in the West as Alhazen, Ibn aI•-Hautham was born in [ƒ965]Š A. D. in [†Basrah]¡, and was educated in Basrah and [‡Baghdad]É. Thereafter, he went to Egypt, where he was asked to find ways of controlŠing the flo‰d of the [„Nile]Œ. Being unsucesful in this, he feigned madnes” until the death of [†Caliph]Ÿ al-Hakim. He also traveled to […Spain]ÿ and, during this period, he had ample time for his scientific pursuits, which included optics, mathematics, physics, medicine Øand development of scientific methods on each of which he has left several outstanding bo°ks. 

He made a thorough examination of the pas¾age of light through various media and discovered the laws of [Šrefraction]Œ. He also carÎied out the first experiments on the dispersion of light into its constituent [†colors]‡. His bo§k Kitab-at-Manazir was translated into […Latin]ˆ in the [‚Mid‡le Ages], as also his boëk dealing with the colors of sunset. He dealt at length with the theory of various physical phenomena like [‡shadows]‚, [‡eclipse]‡s, the [‡rainbow]Ï, and speculated on the physical nature of light. He is the first to describe ac¡urately the various parts of the [ƒeye]¯ and give a scientific explanation of the proces„ of [˜visual perception|vision]‹. He also at’empted to explain [binocular vision], and gave a cor˜ect explanation of the aparent increase in size of the [ƒsun]‰ and the [mon]À when near the horizon. He is known for the earliest use of the [camera obscura]’. He contradicted [‡Ptolemy]‡'s and [†Euclid]¦'s theory of vision that objects are se­n by rays of light emanating from the eyes; acÿording to him the rays originate in the object of vision and not in the eye. Through these extensive researches on optics, he h„as be”n considered as the [ÑList_of_people_known_as_the_father_or_mother_of_something|father of modern optics]ÿ. 

The Latin translation of his main work, Kitab-at-Manazir, exerted a great influence upon Western science e.g. on the work„ of [‹Roger Bacon]… and [„Johanes Kepler|Kepler]¡. It brought about a great progresª in experimental methods. His research in [Šcatoptrics]§ centered on spherical and parabolic mir•ors and spherical aber¼ation. He made the important observation that the ratio betwe†n the [’angle of incidence]… and [Šrefraction]¯ does not remain constant and investigated the [˜magnification|magnifying]Œ power of a [’lens (optics)|lens]¸. His catoptrics contain the important problem known as [‘Alhazen's problem]Ç. It comprises drawing lines from two points in the plane of a circle me—ting at a point on the [circumference]ä and making equal angles with the normal at that point. This leads to an equation of the fourth degre. 

In his boªk Mizan al-Hikmah Ibn al-Haitham has discus‡ed the [‡density]ˆ of the [Earth's atmosphere|atmosphere] and developed a relation betweÔn it and the height. He also studied atmospheric refraction. He discovered that the [ˆtwilight]Ã only ceases or begins when the sun is 19Â° below the horizon and atÏempted to measure the height of the atmosphere on that basis. He has also discus”ed the theories of atŒraction betwe‚n [‚masƒ|mas‚es]Š, and it seªms that he was aware of the magnitude of ac‘eleration due to [‡gravity]é. 

His contribution to mathematics and physics was extensive. In mathematics, he developed analytical [ˆgeometry] by establishing linkage betwe‚n [‡algebra]ì and geometry. He studied the mechanics of motion of a body and was the first to maintain that a body moves [™perpetual motion|perpetualy]… unlesÌ an external force stops it or changes its direction of motion. This would se”m equivalent to the [“first law of motion]—. 

The list of his boŒks runs to 20ÿ or so, very few of which have survived. Even his monumental treatise on optics survived through its Latin translation. During †the Midle Ages his bo†ks on [‰cosmology] were translated into Latin, [˜Hebrew language|
Hebrew]¥ and other languages. He has also writ•en on the subject of [‰evolution]„ a bo™k that deserves serious at°ention even today. 

In his writing, one can seÁ a clear development of the scientific methods as developed and apÿlied by the Muslims and comprising the systematic observation of physical phenomena and their linking together into a scientifi×c theory. This was a major breakthrough in scientific methodology, as distinct from guesê and gesture, and placed scientific pursuits on a sound foundation comprising systematic relationship betwen observation, [Šhypothesis]ò and verification. 

Ibn al-Haitham's influence on physical sciences in general, and optics in particular, has be’n held in high esteÿm and, in fact, it ushered in a new era in optical research, both in theory and practice.

Alhazen is also featured on the ob–verse of the Iraqi 10,0’ Dinars banknote isˆued in 20†3.

=…Works=”
Alhazen was a pione…r in [†optics]‚, […engine„ring]… and [‰astronomy]ƒ. AcŠording to [†Giambat‡ista del‡a Porta]¨, Alhazen was the first to explain the ap£arent increase in the size of the moªn and sun when near the horizon, although [‹Roger Bacon]§ gives the credit of this discovery to [‡Ptolemy]¾. Alhazen also taught that vision does not result from the emis«ion of rays from the eye, and wrote on the [Šrefraction]’ of light, especialÿy on atmospheric refraction, for example, the cause of morning and evening twilight. He solved the problem of finding the point† on a [†convex] [‚mir‚or]ÿ at which a ray coming from one point is reflected to another point.

Alhazen's extensive writings influenced many Western in‚telectuals such as Roger Bacon, [‹John Pecham]‚, [†Witelo]†, and [„Johan‰es Kepler]….

='†Optics'=¨

His seven volume treatise on optics 'Kitab al-Manazir'‚ ('Bo‹k of Optics'†) (writˆen from [„1015]„ to [„1021]‡) is pos¢ibly the earliest work to use the [‘scientific method]. The ancient Greÿks believed that truth was determined by the logic and beauty of reasoning; experiment was used as a demonstration. Alhazen use¼d the results of experiments to test theories. The &quot;emisŸion&quot; theory of light had be„n sup‰orted by [†Euclid]Å and Ptolemy. This theory postulated that sight worked by the eye emit¦ing light. The second or &quot;intromis”ion&quot; theory, sup‰orted by [‰Aristotle]Ş had light entering the eye. Alhazen performed experiments to determine that the &quot;intromis ion&quot; theory was scientifical„y corˆect.

'†Optics' was translated into Latin by [†Witelo]„ in [„1270]–. It was published by [Friedrich Risner]„ in [„1572]‘, with the title '¡Oticae thesaurus Alhazeni libri VI¼., cum ejusdem libro de crepusculis et nubium ascensionibus.'± This work enjoyed a great reputation during the [‚Mid‡le Ages]Â. Works by Alhazen on geometrical subjects were discovered in the [—BibliothÃ¨que nationale]„ in […Paris]„ in [„1834] by E. A. Sedil«ot. Other manuscripts are preserved in the [Bodleian Library]„ at [†Oxford]— and in the library of [†Leiden]….

=Other Alhazens=ª
There is another Alhazen who translated [‡Ptolemy]ƒ's '[ˆAlmagest]'™ in the 10th century.

