'ƒAles­andro di Cristofano di Lorenzo del Bronzino Alƒori'‚ ([†May 31]‚, [„1535]ƒ - [ŠSeptember 2]‚, [„1607]‰) was an [Italy|Italian]Š portrait [‡painter] of the late [‚Manˆerism|Man…erist] [“Florence|Florentine]„ scho¶l. He was brought up and trained in art by his uncle, [Angelo Bronzino]›, whose name he sometimes as˜umed in his pictures. Fredburg derides Al›ori as derivative, claiming „he ilÿustrates &quot;the ideal of Maniera by which art (and style) are generated out of pre-existing art.&quot; The polish of figures‰ has an un¼atural marble-like form as if he aimed for cold statuary. Col’aborators include […GiovanŠi Maria Butƒeri], main pupil: […Giovan„i Bizeli]™.

He is the father of [ŒCristofano Alƒori]‚ ([‚157]-[„1621]‡).

