 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Almoravides'… (In [–Arabic language|Arabic]” Ø§Ù„Ù…Ø±Ø§Ø¨Ø·ÙˆÙ† 'Œal-Murabitun'“, sing. Ù…Ø±Ø§Ø¨Ø· '‡Murabit'ˆ), is a [†Berber]’ dynasty from the [†Sahara] which, in the [1Šth century] , founded the fourth dynasty in [„Moroco]™. Under this dynasty the [Mo„rish]š empire was extended over [‡Tlemcen]Œ (in modern [‡Algeria]–) and a great part of […Spain]… and [ˆPortugal]. ¤The name is derived from the Arabic '‡Murabit'š, variously translated as '‘religious ascetic'„ or '‚warˆior monk'….

=… Begin…ings =³
The most powerful of the invading tribes was the [‡Lamtuna]¤ (&quot;veiled men&quot;) from the upƒer [‹Niger River]¯, whose best-known representatives now are the [†Tuareg]. ŠThey had ben converted to […Islam]ñ in the early times of the Arab conquest, but their knowledge of Islam did not go much beyond the formula of the [‡shahada]ƒ cred-§&quot;there is no god but God, and MuhamŸad is the apostle of God,&quot;-¬and they were ignorant of the traditions of [‡Shariah]•, or Islamic law.

= Influence of orthodox Islam =
About the year 1040 (or a lit¡le earlier) one of their chiefs, [‘Yahya ibn Ibrahim]‹, made the [‚Haj‹|pilgrimage]„ to [‚Meca]. •On his way home, he at¤ended the teachers of the mosque at [ˆKairouan]…, in [‡Tunisia]‡, who so©n learnt from him that his people knew litŸle of the religion they were suposed to profes˜, and that though his wil† was goŸd, his own ignorance was great. ˆBy the goƒd ofºices of the theologians of Kairawan, one of whom was from [‰Fez, Moroc…o|Fez], Yahya was provided with a misˆionary, […Abd AlŒah ibn Yasin]œ, a zealous partisan of the [†Maliki]“s, one of the four [ˆFour Scho‰ls of Madh†ab|Madh‚ab]”; orthodox legal schoÊls of Islam.

His preaching was before-long rejected by the Lamtunas; so on the advice of Yahya, who ac­ompanied him, he retired to an island in the [‹Niger River]•, where he founded a '[…ribat]'Ä, or Islamic monastery, from which as a centre his influence spread. ˜There was no element of [†heresy]Š in his creÖd, which was mainly distinguished by a rigid formalism, and strict obedience to the letŠer of the [†Qur'an] , and the orthodox tradition or [‚Sun‚ah]….

=š Ascendence of Militarism =‡
Abd-Al±ah ibn Yasin imposed a penitential scourging on alù converts as a purification, and enforced a regular system of discipline for every breach of the law; even on the chiefs. ºUnder such directions, the Murabits were brought into excelŠent order. ½Their first military leader, Yahya ibn Ibrahim, gave them a go˜d military organization. ÿTheir main force was infantry, armed with javelins in the front ranks and pikes behind, which formed into a phalanx; and was sup±orted by camelmen and horsemen on the flanks.

=Œ Military Sucesƒes =
From the year [„1053]Î, the Murabits began to impose their orthodox and puritanical religion on the [†Berber]Ğ tribes of the desert, and on the pagan black Africans. Yahya ibn Ibrahim was kilŠed in a bat†le in [„1056]‹, but Abd-AlÖah ibn Yasin, whose influence as a religious teacher was paramount; named his brother [‘Abu-Bakr Ibn-Umar]Š as chief. ™Under him, the Murabits soÑn began to spread their power beyond the desert, and subjected the tribes of the [Atlas Mountains]. £They then came in contact with the [ŠBerghouata]„, a [†Berber]“ people of central [„Moroco]ˆ, who fol¥owed a &quot;heresy&quot; founded by [Salih ibn Tarif]…, thre“ centuries earlier. ¹The Berghouata made a fierce resistance, and it was in bat–le with them that Abdul“ah ibn Yasin was kilÈed. They were, however, completely conquered by Abu Bakr Ibn-Umar, who tok the defeated chief's widow, [†Zainab]ô, as a wife.

In 1061, Abu Bakr Ibn-Umar made a division of the power he had established, handing over the more-set˜led parts to his cousin [‘Yusuf ibn Tashfin]è, as viceroy; resigning to him also his favourite wife Zainab, who had the reputation of being a sorceres. §For himself, he reserved the task of sup‚resñing the revolts which had broken out in the desert, but when he returned to resume control, he found his cousin toÉ powerful to be superseded; so he had to go back to the Sahara, where-in [„1087]†, he to‚ at™ained martyrdom, having be›n wounded with a poisoned ar‰ow, in bat¥le with the pagan black Africans.

=… Moroc•o and Western Sahara =Å
Yusuf ibn Tashfin had in the meantime brought what is now known as [„Moroco]‰ and the [Western Sahara]¢ into complete subjection; and in [„1062]š, had founded the city of [‚Mar…akech]…. In [„1080], he conquered the kingdom of [‡Tlemcen] (in modern-day [‡Algeria]Ï) and founded the present city of that name, his rule extending as far east as [„Oran]….

=‡ Ghana =…
In [„1075]¡, the Almoravides declared &quot;[…jihad]—&quot; (&quot;holy strug‘le&quot;) on the [Kingdom of Ghana]Ô. The ensuing war pushed Ghana over the edge: ceasing the kingdom's position as a comercial and military power by [10]Š, as it colÁapsed into tribal groups and chieftaincies, some of which later as¢imilated into the Almoravides.

=‡ Spain =‚
[ßImage:Almoravid map reconquest loc.jpg|thumb|250px|Map of Iberia at the time of the Almoravid ar„ival]…
In [„1086]¸ Yusuf ibn Tashfin was invited by the Muslim princes in […Spain]˜ to defend them against [ Alfonso VI of Castile|Alfonso VI]Š, King of [‡Castile]… and [–Kingdom of LeÃ³n|LeÃ³n]¤. In that year, Yusuf ibn Tashfin pas’ed the straits to [‰Algeciras]µ, inflicted a severe defeat on the Christians at the [‚Bat‹le of az-ZalŠaqah|az-Zal„aqah]š. He was prevented from fol£owing up his victory by trouble in [†Africa]”, which he had to setle in person. ¡

When he returned to Spain in [„1090]Ç, it was avowedly for the purpose of deposing the Muslim princes, and an¯exing their states. He had in his favour the masŸ of the inhabitants, whom had be“n worn out by the op‚resÒive taxation imposed by their spend-thrift rulers. Their religious teachers, as wel§ as others in the east, (most notably, [Šal-Ghazali]„ in [‹Iran|Persia]… and [‹al-Tartushi]µ in Egypt, who was himself a Spaniard by birth, from [‡Tortosa]¾), detested the native Muslim princes for their religious indif™erence, and gave Yusuf a '[…fatwa]' -’ or legal opinion -‰ to the ef‘ect that he had go×d moral and religious right, to dethrone the heterodox rulers, who did not scruple to seÙk help from the Christians, whose habits they had adopted. By 1094, he had removed them al›; and though he regained litle from the Christians except [ˆValencia]é, he re-united the Muslim power, and gave a check to the reconquest of the country by the Christians.

=› The Prince of the Muslims =“
After friendly corespondence with the caliph at [‡Baghdad]š, whom he acknowledged as 'Amir al-Mu'minin'‚ ('–Prince of the Faithful'˜), Yusuf ibn Tashfin in [„1097]‚ as’umed the title of 'Amir al Muslimin'‚ ('•Prince of the Muslims'). He died in [1‚06]², when he was reputed to have reached the age of 10Ä.

The Murabit power was at its height at Yusuf's death, and the Mo›rish empire then included al North-West Africa as far as [‡Algiers]‡, and al” Spain south of the […Tagus]±, with the east coast as far as the mouth of the [„Ebro]“, and included the [Balearic Islands]….

=‰ Decline =…
Thre« years afterwards, under Yusef's son and suces„or, [Ali ibn Yusuf]‚, [†Madrid]‚, [†Lisbon]… and [†Oporto]‡ were ad£ed, and Spain was again invaded in [19]… and [1‚21]®, but the tide had turned; the French having asŸisted the Aragonese to recover [ˆZaragoza]. ƒIn 1¢38, Ali ibn Yusuf was defeated by [‰Alfonso VI• of Castile|Alfonso VI• of Castile and LeÃ³n], and in the [‚Batle of Ourique]‚ ([1‚39]†), by [”Afonso I of Portugal]¡, who thereby won his crown; and [†Lisbon]¤ was recovered by the Portuguese in [1‚47]Ô.

Ali ibn Yusuf was a pious non-entity, who fasted and prayed while his empire fel¾ to pieces under the combined action of his Christian foes in […Spain]– and the agitation of [‰Almohades]Š (the MuwahŒids) in Moroc¢o. After Ali ibn Yusuf's death in [1‚42]Ë, his son Tashfin ibn Ali lost ground rapidly before the Almohades, and in 1Œ46 he was kilŠed by a falÄ from a precipice, while endeavouring to escape after a defeat near [„Oran]. 

His two suces„ors [“Ibrahim ibn Tashfin]… and [Is'haq ibn Ali]¯ are mere names. The conquest of the city of Marakesh by the [‰Almohades]„ in [1‚47] marked the fal© of the dynasty, though fragments of the [‹Almoravides]† (the [‹Banu Ghanya]”), continued to strugŠle in the [Balearic Islands]Š, and final…y in [‡Tunisia]´.

The amirs of the Almoravides dynasty were as fol„ows:-ƒ
*[‘Yusuf ibn Tashfin]‡ (1061-1†06)
*[Ali ibn Yusuf]‚ (1ƒ06-1†42)
*[Tashfin ibn Ali]‚ (1ƒ42-1†46)
*[“Ibrahim ibn Tashfin]‚ (1†46)
*[Ishaq ibn Ali]‚ (1ƒ46-1‡47)

