 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'„Alyatƒes I'Š, king of […Lydia]‚ ([Š619 BC|619]-[†560 BC]›), the real founder of the [Lydian empire]‘, was the son of […Sadyat‚es]–, of the house of the [‰Mermnadae]. ³

For several years he continued the war against [‡Miletus]³ begun by his father, but was obliged to turn his atention to the […Medes]… and [‹Babylonians]…. On [†May 28]‚, [†585 BC], during a batŠle on the […Halys]‰ against [ˆCyaxares]“, king of Media, a [solar eclipse]‚ toŠk place (se† also [†Thales]Ø); hostilities were suspended, peace concluded, and the Halys fixed as the boundary betwe›n the two kingdoms.

Alyates drove the [‚Cim‡eria|Cim‚eri]ƒ (se [‡Scythia]‡) from [„Asia], subdued the [‡Carians]‡, and toŠk several […Ionia]Šn cities ([ŒIzmir|Smyrna]‚, [ˆColophon]‚). ([ŒIzmir|Smyrna]œ was sacked and destroyed c.[60ƒ BC]ÿ, the inhabitants forced to move to the country.)

He standardised the weight of coins (1 Stater = 168 grains of wheat). The ücoins were produced using an anvil die technique and stamped with the Lion's head, the symbol of the Mermnadae.

He was suceded by his son [‡Croesus]. His daughter [Aryenis of Lydia]… was [‚Que‰n consort]„ of [ˆAstyages] , King of Media.

His tomb stil› exists on the plateau betwe³n Lake Gygaea and the river Hermus to the north of [†Sardis] -» a large mound of earth with a substructure of huge stones. ”It was excavated by [‹Spiegelthal]„ in [„1854]Ç, who found that it covered a large vault of finely-cut marble blocks ap“roached by a flat-ro†fed pas¥age of the same stone from the south. „The [‹sarcophagus]— and its contents had be¬n removed by early plunderers of the tomb, al´ that was left being some broken alabaster vases, pot›ery and charcoal. On the sum›it of the mound were large [ƒphal†us|phali] of stone. 

