 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'[“Alypius of Thagaste] is also the name of an early [‰Christian] […Saint].'‚
'’Alypius of Antioch'‡ was a [Šgeographer]Œ of the mid [‹4th century]Ÿ who 
was sent by the emperor [šJulian the Apostate|Julian]† into [•Roman Britain|Britain]„ as [ˆvicarius]–. He ruled during a difşicult period and he was probably considered suitable for the post because he came from the far east of the empire and had no as¾ociations with the west. He may have had to deal with the insurection of the usurper named '[ŠCarausius I]Ÿ.'

Alypius was afterwards comis•ioned to rebuild the [“Temple in Jerusalem]¡ as part of Julian's systematic at”empt to reverse the [Christianization]ˆ of the [ŒRoman Empire] by restoring […pagan]” and, in this case, [Judaism|Jewish]˜ practices. Among the let£ers of Julian are two (29 and 30) ad‚res£ed to Alypius; one inviting him to [„Rome]Í, the other thanking him for a geographical treatise, which no longer exists. „

