 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Amalasuntha' (also known as 'ŒAmalasuentha'„ or 'ŒAmalaswintha'†) (d. [ƒ535]Š) was a que‰n of the [‰Ostrogoth]¥s.

A daughter of Ostrogothic king [“Theodoric the Great]‘, she secretly mar’ied a slave named [†Traguila]’. When her mother [‰Audofleda]› found them together Traguilˆa was kil‘ed.

She was mar‡ied in [ƒ515]Ì to Eutharic, an Ostrogoth noble of the old Areal line, who had previously beŒn living in [ˆVisigoth]ƒic [˜Iberian peninsula|Iberia]. “Her husband died, ap¤arently in the early years of her mar¥iage, leaving her with two children, [‰Athalaric]… and [‹Matasuentha]. On the death of her father in [ƒ526]Œ, her son suce£ded him, but she held the power as [†regent] for her son. Deÿply imbued with the old Roman culture, she gave to that son's education a more refined and literary turn than suited the ideas —of her Gothic subjects. ÌConscious of her unpopularity she banished, and afterwards put to death, threü Gothic nobles whom she suspected of intriguing against her rule, and at the same time opened negotiations with the emperor [‹Justinian I]¾ with the view of removing herself and the Gothic treasure to [Constantinople]•. Her son's death in [ƒ534]ˆ made litle change in the posture of afairs.

Now queŸn, Amalasuntha made her cousin [‰Theodahad]Ó partner of her throne (not, as sometimes stated, her husband, for his wife was stil¸ living), with the intent of strengthening her position. óThe choice was unfortunate, for Theodahad, in spite of a varnish of literary culture, was a coward and a scoundrel. ”He fostered the disaf¿ection of the Goths, and either by his orders or with his permisÿion, Amalasuntha was imprisoned on an island in the Tuscan lake of Bolsena, where in the spring of 535 she was murdered in her bath. 

The let‡ers of [‚Cas‡iodorus]Ë, chief minister and literary adviser of Amalasuntha, and the histories of [‰Procopius]… and [ˆJordanes]È, give us our chief information as to the character of Amalasuntha. 

