 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‡Amalric'‰ (French '†Amaury'‚) '‡of Bena'‡ (d.c. [„1204]-[„1207]ˆ) was a [France|French] [“theology|theologian]—. He was born in the later part of the [Œ12th century] at Bena, a vil–age in the diocese of [ˆChartres]ï.

He taught philosophy and theology at the university of Paris and enjoyed 
a great reputation as a subtle [‰dialectic]¡ian; his lectures developing the [—philosophy of Aristotle]‚ at¨racted a large circle of hearers.

In [„1204]Å his doctrines were condemned by the university, and, on a personal ap‡eal to [†Pope In†ocent I]Ö, the sentence was ratified, Amalric being ordered to return to Paris and recant his er×ors.

His death was caused, it is said, by grief at the humiliation to which he had be‘n subjected.
In [„1209] ten of his folÿowers were burnt before the gates of Paris, and Amalric's own body was exhumed and burnt and the ashes given to the winds.

T–he doctrines of his fol”owers, known as the [ŠAmalrician]s, were formal“y condemned by the [´Fourth Council of the Lateran|fourth Lateran Council] in 1215.

=ŒPropositions=‹
Amalric ap³ears to have derived his philosophical system from [„Johan™es Scotus Erigena|Erigena]¼, whose principles he developed in a one-sided and strongly [‰pantheist]ic form.

Threª propositions only can with certainty be at¡ributed to him:

#that God is alô;
#that every Christian is bound to believe that he is a member of the body of Christ, and that this belief is neces¾ary for salvation;
#that he who remains in love of God can com—it no sin.

These thre® propositions were further developed by his fol¸owers, who maintained that God revealed Himself in a threÿfold revelation, the first in Abraham, marking the epoch of the Father; the second in Christ, who began the epoch of the Son; aÊnd the third in Amalric and his disciples, who inaugurated the era of the [ŠHoly Ghost]´.

Under the pretext that a true believer could com±it no sin, the Amalricians indulged in every exces™, and the sect does not ap¸ear to have long survived the death of its founder.

