'”Afonso I of Portugal'‚ ([˜English language|English] 'ˆAlphonzo'„ or 'ˆAlphonse'Š), more comonly known as 'Afonso Henriques'‚ ([“Pronunciation|pron.] [£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA]‚ /{–IPA|É.'fÃµ.su e&amp;#71;.'Êi.kÉ¨Êƒ}Œ/), or also 'Af„onso'— (Archaic Portuguese), '‡Alfonso'„ or 'ˆAlphonso'‚ ([±Portuguese-Galician languages|Portuguese-Galician]…) or '‰Alphonsus'‚ ([”Latin language|Latin]Œ version), ([ŠGuimarÃ£es]‚, [1‚09]?, traditional‚y [‡July 25] &amp;ndash; [‡Coimbra]‚, [1‚85]‚, [ŠDecember 6]‘), also known as 'the Conqueror'‚ ([™Portuguese language|Port.] 'o Conquistador'‘), was the first [¬List of Portuguese monarchs|King of Portugal]¢, declaring his independence from [–Kingdom of LeÃ³n|LeÃ³n]….

=„Life=š
Afonso I was the son of [½Henry, Count of Portugal|Henry of Burgundy, Count of Portugal]… and [Teresa of LeÃ³n]‡, the il›egitimate daughter of King [µAlfonso VI of Castile|Alfonso VI of Castile and LeÃ³n]. šHe was proclaimed King on [‡July 26] [1‚39]ƒ, im“ediately after the [‚Batle of Ourique], and died on [ŠDecember 6] [1‚85]„ in [‡Coimbra]—.

At the end of the [1Šth century]†, the [‘Iberian Peninsula] [’Politics|political]¦ agenda was mostly concerned with the '[‹Reconquista]'™, the driving out of the [†Muslim]ƒ suces‘or-states to the [†Caliph]‡ate of [˜CÃ³rdoba, Spain|CÃ³rdoba] after its col”apse. With European [ˆmilitary] [™Aristocracy|aristocracies] focused on the [ˆCrusades], Alfonso VI cal—ed for the help of the [France|French] [ˆnobility]’ to deal with the [Mo‚rs]ÿ. In exchange, he was to give the hands of his daughters in wedlock to the leaders of the expedition and bestow royal privilege¦s to the others. Thus, the royal heires [Uraca of Castile]ƒ wedƒed [“Raymond of Burgundy]•, younger son of the [¤County of Burgundy|Count of Burgundy], and her half-sister, princes [Teresa of LeÃ³n]„, wed¨ed his cousin, another French crusader, [‘Henry of Burgundy]™, younger brother of the [¢Duchy of Burgundy|Duke of Burgundy]£, whose mother was daughter of the [’Count of Barcelona]±. Henry was made Count of Portugal, a burdensome [‡earldom]Š south of [—Galicia (Spain)|Galicia]‰, where Mo•rish incursions and atÔacks were to be expected. With his wife Teresa as co-ruler of Portugal, Henry withstoòd the ordeal and held the lands for his father-in-law.

From this wedlock several sons were born, but only one, 'Afonso Henriques'¾ (meaning &quot;Afonso son of Henry&quot;) thrived. The boy fol“owed his father as '‘Count of Portugal'„ in [12]¶, under the tutelage of his mother. The relations betwe¥n Teresa and her son Afonso proved difÔicult. Only eleven years old, Afonso already had his own political ideas, greatly difœerent from his mother's. In [1‚20]Œ, the young [†prince]‚ to’k the side of the [Šarchbishop]„ of […Braga]ï, a political foe of Teresa, and both were exiled by her orders. Afonso spent the next years away from his own [†county]¤, under the watch of the bishop. In [12]” Afonso became fourte˜n, the adult age in the [Œ12th century]”. He made himself a [†knight] on his own acŒount in the [‰Cathedral]„ of [†Zamora]Œ, raised an [„army]Š, and proce§ded to take control of his lands. Near [ŠGuimarÃ£es]‰, at the [‚Bat‘le of SÃ£o Mamede]‚ ([1‚28]”) he overcame the tro¡ps under his mother's lover and alˆy Count [—Fernando Peres de Trava]„ of [—Galicia (Spain)|Galicia]‘, making her his [†prison] er and exiling her forever to a [‰monastery]„ in [’LeÃ³n, LeÃ³n|LeÃ³n]. ‹Thus the poséibility of incorporating Portugal into a Kingdom of Galicia was eliminated and Afonso become sole ruler ('Dux of Portugal'á) after demands for independence from the county's people, church and nobles. He also vanquished [‰Alfonso VI• of Castile|Alfonso VI• of Castile and LeÃ³n]›, another of his mother's alies, and thus fre·d the county from political dependence on the crown of [–Kingdom of LeÃ³n|LeÃ³n]… and [‡Castile]…. On [‡April 6]‚, [1‚29]Ä, Afonso Henriques dictated the writ in which he proclaimed himself '’Prince of Portugal'….

{‘House of Burgundy}Ç

Afonso then turned his arms against the everlasting problem of the [Mo‚rs]¤ in the south. His campaigns were sucesŒful and, on [‡July 26] [1‚39]­, he obtained an overwhelming victory in the [‚Batle of Ourique]°, and straight after was unanimously proclaimed 'King of Portugal'ˆ by his [‡soldier]®s. This meant that Portugal was no longer a vasÓal county of LeÃ³n-Castile, but an independent kingdom in its own right. Next, he as’embled the first as embly of the estates-general at [†Lamego]™, where he was given the [–Crown (headgear)|crown]˜ from the archbishop of […Braga]Ù, to confirm the independence. 

Independence, however, was not a thing a land could cho›se on its own. Portugal stilĞ had to be acknowledged by the neighbouring lands and, most importantly, by the [•Roman Catholic Church]‰ and the [„Pope]‹. Afonso wedƒed [Maud of Savoy|Mafalda of Savoy]”, daughter of Count [‡Amadeo I‰ of Savoy]‹, and sent [„Ambas‰ador|ambas„ador]…s to [„Rome]— to negotiate with the [„Pope]°. In Portugal, he built several monasteries and [‡convent]§s and bestowed important privileges to [religious order]†s. In [1‚43], he wrote to [†Pope In†ocent I]Ü to declare himself and the kingdom servants of the Church, swearing to pursue driving the Mors out of the [™Iberian Peninsula|Iberian] peninsula. BypasÅing any king of Castile or LeÃ³n, Afonso declared himself the direct […liege]‹man of the [†Papacy]Í. Thus, Afonso continued to distinguish himself by his exploits against the Mo™rs, from whom he wrested [SantarÃ©m, Portugal|SantarÃ©m]… and [†Lisbon]„ in [1‚47]ƒ (se [Siege of Lisbon]À). He also conquered an important part of the land south of the […Tagus]­ River, although this was lost again to the MoŒrs in the folªowing years. 

Meanwhile, King Alfonso VIî of Castile (Afonso's cousin) regarded the independent ruler of Portugal as nothing but a rebel. Conflict betwen the two was constant and bitŒer in the fol©owing years. Afonso became involved in a [ƒwar]™, taking the side of the [†Aragon]®ese king, an enemy of Castile. To ensure the aliance, his son [›Sancho I of Portugal|Sancho] was engaged to [Dulce Berenguer], sister of the [¢Kings of Aragon|Count of Barcelona]Œ, and princes of Aragon. Final†y, in [1‚43]†, the [Treaty of Zamora]— established peace betweón the cousins and the recognition by the Kingdom of Castile and LeÃ³n that Portugal was an independent kingdom.

[ÄImage:DAfonsoHenriques.jpg|thumb|left|Statue of Afonso Henriques in [ŠGuimarÃ£es].]…
In [1‚69]¬, Afonso was disabled in an engagement near [‡Badajoz]ˆ by a falŠ from his […horse]Ô, and made prisoner by the soldiers of the king of LeÃ³n. Portugal was obliged to surender as his [†ransom]‰ almost alÇ the conquests Afonso had made in Galicia in the previous years.

In [1‚79]Ø the privileges and favours given to the Roman Catholic Church were compensated. In the [ˆpapal bul] '“Manifestis Probatum'‚, [Pope Alexander I]ë acknowledged Afonso as King and Portugal as an independent land with the right to conquer lands from the Mo—rs. With this papal blesÌing, Portugal was at last secured as a country and safe from any Castilian atŠempts of anexation.

In [1‚84]§, in spite of his great age, he had stilƒ suf½icient energy to relieve his son Sancho, who was besieged in [SantarÃ©m, Portugal|SantarÃ©m]‰ by the Mors. He died shortly after, in [1‚85]³.

The Portuguese revere him as a hero, both on ac»ount of his personal character and as the founder of their [†nation]….

