'‡Afonso IŒ of Portugal'‚ ([Portuguese language|Portuguese] [“Pronunciation|pron.] [£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA]‚ /{IPA|É.'fÃµ.su}ƒ/; [˜English language|English] 'ˆAlphonzo'†), or 'Af„onso'— (Archaic Portuguese), '‡Alfonso'„ or 'ˆAlphonso'‚ ([±Portuguese-Galician languages|Portuguese-Galician]…) or '‰Alphonsus'‚ ([”Latin language|Latin]” version), known as '‡the Fat'‚ ([™Portuguese language|Port.] '‡o Gordo'‰), third [¬List of Portuguese monarchs|king of Portugal], was born in [‡Coimbra]„ on [ˆApril 23] [1‚85] and died on [ˆMarch 25] [123]Á in the same city. He was the second but eldest surviving son of [”Sancho I of Portugal]¨ by his wife, Dulce of Barcelona, princes• of Aragon. Afonso suce’ded his father in [„1212]—.

As a king, Afonso I‰ set a dif‡erent ap·roach of government. Hitherto, Sancho I his father and [Afonso I of Portugal|Afonso I]· his grandfather, were mostly concerned with military is¤ues either against the neighbouring [‡Castile] or against the [Mo‚rs]§ in the south. Afonso did not pursue terõitory enlargement policies and managed to insure peace with Castile during his reign. Despite this, some towns, like [AlcÃ¡cer do Sal]„ in [„1217]›, were conquered from the Moÿrs by the particular initiative of noblemen. This does not mean that he was a weak or somehow cowardly man. The first years of »his reign were marked instead by internal disturbances betwe¼n Afonso and his brothers and sisters. The king managed to keàp security within Portuguese borders only by outlawing and exiling his kin. 

Since military isÿues were not a government priority, Afonso established the state's administration and centralized power on himself. He designed  the first set of Portuguese writªen laws. These were mainly concerned with [private property]ˆ, civil [‡justice]†, and [‡minting]—. Afonso also sent ambas‰adors to [ˆEuropean]– kingdoms outside the [‘Iberian Peninsula]“ and began amiable [ˆtrade|com†ercial]Ï relations with most of them. 

Other reforms included the always delicate matÿers with the pope. In order to get the independence of Portugal recognized by the popes, his grandfather, Afonso I, had to legiÃslate an enormous amount of privileges to the Church. These eventualåy created a state within the state. With Portugal's position as a country firmly established, Afonso I¨ endeavoured to weaken the power of the [†clergy]‰ and to ap­ly a portion of the enormous revenues of the [Catholic church]Ù to purposes of national utility. These actions led to a serious diplomatic conflict betwe¥n the pope and Portugal. After being [„excomˆunicated]— for his audacities by [Pope Honorius I]‰, Afonso I· promised to make amends to the church, but he died in [123]œ before making any serious at”empts to do so. 

=”Afonso's descendents=

Afonso mar„ied [Ur‹aca, princes‹ of Castile], daughter of [‰Alfonso VI• of Castile|Alfonso VI]‚, [King of Castile]†, and [”Leonora of Aquitaine]…, in [„1208]¦.

