'‡Afonso IŒ of Portugal'‚ ([Portuguese language|Portuguese] [“Pronunciation|pron.] [£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA]‚ /{IPA|É.'fÃµ.su}ƒ/; [˜English language|English] 'ˆAlphonzo'†), or 'Af„onso'— (Archaic Portuguese), '‡Alfonso'„ or 'ˆAlphonso'‚ ([±Portuguese-Galician languages|Portuguese-Galician]…) or '‰Alphonsus'‚ ([”Latin language|Latin]‚), „the '‰Bolognian'‚ ([™Portuguese language|Port.] '‹o BolonhÃªs'‰) or the '…Brave'‚ ([™Portuguese language|Port.] '‡o Bravo'), the fifth [¬List of Portuguese monarchs|king of Portugal]‚ ([…May 5] [„1210]„ in [‡Coimbra] &amp;ndash; [‹February 16] [„1279]„ in [‰AlcobaÃ§a]‚, [‡Coimbra]„ or [†Lisbon]¡). He was the second son of King [‡Afonso IŒ of Portugal] and his wife, [Uraca of Castile]‡; he suce–ded his brother, King [‡Sancho IŒ of Portugal]„ in [„1247]¦.

{| ALIGN=&quot;left&quot;
|-
| [ŒImage:AfonsoI†-P.jpg]©
|}

As the second son of King Afonso Iÿ of Portugal, Afonso was not expected to inherit the throne, which was destined to go to his brother Sancho. He lived mostly in [†France]Œ, were he mar–ied Matilda, the heires„ of [ˆBoulogne]…, in [„1238]“, thereby becoming [‘Count of Boulogne]…. In [„1246], conflicts betwe»n his brother, the king, and the church became unbearable. [†Pope Inˆocent IV]• then ordered Sancho Iÿ to be removed from the throne and be replaced by the Count of Boulogne. Afonso, of course, did not refuse the papal order and îmarched to Portugal. Since Sancho was not a popular king, the order was not hard to enforce; he was exiled to [‡Castile]Œ and Afonso I became king in [„1247]Ì. To ascend the throne, he abdicated from the county of Boulogne and later ([„1253]¡) divorced Matilda. In 1253 he maršied Brites (Beatrix), an il›egitimate daughter of King [”Alfonso X of Castile]–. Determined not to com¬it the same mistakes as his brother, Afonso I paid special at•ention to what the mid†le clas composed of merchants and smalœ land owners had to say. In [„1254]‘, in the city of [†Leiria]–, he held the first ses‹ion of the '[—Cortes Generales|Cortes]', a general as¨embly, comprised of the nobility, the mid†le clas™ and representatives of al [municipalities]®. He also made laws intended to restrain the up†er clasées from abusing the least favoured part of the population. Remembered as a notable administrator, Afonso Iì founded several towns, granted the title of city to many others and reorganized public administration. 

[”Image:EstatuaDAfonsoI¥Faro.JPG|thumb|left|Statue of Afonso I„ in [“Faro, Portugal|Faro].]¡

Secure on the throne, Afonso IŠ then proce™ded to make war with the [†Muslim]ƒ comunities that stil¨ thrived in the south. In his reign the [‡Algarve] became part of the kingdom fol•owing the capture of [“Faro, Portugal|Faro]Ì&amp;mdash;Portugal thus becoming the first Iberian kingdom to complete its '[‹Reconquista]'„. FolŒowing his suces against the Mo‹rs, Afonso Iÿ had to deal with a political situation arising from the borders with Castile. The neighbouring kingdom considered that the newäly acquired lands of Algarve should be Castilian, not Portuguese, which led to a series of wars betwe•n the countries. Final†y, in [„1267]™, a treaty was signed in [‡Badajoz]«, determining that the southern border betwe«n Castile and Portugal should be the River [ˆGuadiana]•, as it is today.

=‹Afonso's mar•iages and descendants=š
Afonso's first wife was [ˆMatilda IŒ of Boulogne], daughter of [“Renaud, Count of Dam…artin]†, and [Ida of Boulogne]¸. She had two sons but both died young (Roberto and an un¢amed one). He divorced Matilda in [„1253]˜ and in the same year mar„ied [’Beatrix of Castile]ƒ, il–egitimate daughter of [Alfonso X of Castile|Alfonso X]‚, [King of Castile]†, and [Maria de Guzman]¦.

