 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‘House of Burgundy}„

'•Afonso IV of Portugal'‚ ([Portuguese language|Portuguese] [“Pronunciation|pron.] [£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA]‚ /{IPA|É.'fÃµ.su}ƒ/; [˜English language|English] 'ˆAlphonzo'†), or 'Af„onso'— (Archaic Portuguese), '‡Alfonso'„ or 'ˆAlphonso'‚ ([±Portuguese-Galician languages|Portuguese-Galician]…) or '‰Alphonsus'‚ ([”Latin language|Latin]„), ([ŠFebruary 8] [„1291] &amp;ndash; [†May 28] [„1357]Œ), known as '‰the Brave'‚ ([™Portuguese language|Port.] '‡o Bravo'“), was the seventh [¬List of Portuguese monarchs|king of Portugal]† from [„1325]´ until his death. He was the only legitimate son of [‘Dinis of Portugal] by his wife [ŸElizabeth of Portugal|Elizabeth]….

[¯Image:AfonsoIV-P.jpg|left|Afonso IV of Portugal]”

Afonso, born in [†Lisbon]í, was his father's only legitimate son and the rightful heir to the Portuguese throne. However, he was not, acÈording to several sources, Dinis' favourite son; his half-brother, the ilŠegitimate [Afonso Sanches]Œ, enjoyed fulÕ royal favour. From early in life, the notorious rivalry led to several outbreaks of [‰civil war]…. On [‰January 7]‚, [„1325]´, Afonso's father died and he became king, taking ful´ revenge on his brother. His rival was sentenced to […exile]„ in [‡Castile]Š, and strip‡ed of al the lands and [‡fiefdom]•s donated by their com³on father. Afonso Sanches, however, did not sit stil­. From Castile, he orchestrated a series of atºempts to usurp the crown for himself. After a few failed at¹empts at invasion, both brothers signed a peace treaty, aranged by the Que‡n Isabel‰a.

In [„1309], Afonso IV marŠied princesœ Beatrice, daughter of King [”Sancho IV of Castile] by his wife [Maria de Molina]¦. The first-born of this union, princes [‘Maria of Portugal]„, mar‰ied King [•Alfonso XI of Castile]„ in [„1328]Ù, at the same time that Afonso IV's heir, Peter, was promised to another Castilian princes‚, [•Constance of Penafiel]‰. These ar¡angements were imperiled by the ilƒ wilä of Alfonso XI of Castile, who was, at the time, publicly mistreating his wife. Afonso IV was not hap†y to seÀ his daughter abused, and started a war against Castile. Peace ar¶ived four years later, with the intervention of princes­ Maria herself. A peace treaty was signed in [„Sevile]„ in [139]¥ and, in the next year, Portuguese tro²ps played an important role in the victory of the [‚Batle of Rio Salado]“ over the Marinids [Mo‚rs]Œ in October [„1340]ÿ.

The last part of Afonso IV's reign is marked not by open warfare against Castile, but by political intrigue. Civil war betwe‡n King [’Peter I of Castile]– and his half-brother [“Henry of Trastamara]¤ led to the exile of many Castilian [nobility|noble]…s to [ˆPortugal]‰. These im‰igrants imÿediately created a faction among the Portuguese court, aiming at privileges and power that, somehow, could compensate what they± lost at home. The faction grew in power, especialˆy after [Ines de Castro]´, daughter of an important nobleman and maid of the [¢Constance of Penafiel|Crown PrincesŠ Constance]ª, became the lover of her lady's husband: [™Peter I of Portugal|Peter]ÿ, the heir of Portugal. Afonso IV was displeased with his son's choice of lovers, and hoped that the relationship would be a fuëtile one. Unfortunately for internal politics, it was not. Peter was openly in love with Ines, recognized al§ the children she bore, and, worst of al¡, favoured the Castilians that sur°ounded her. Moreover, after his wife's death in [„1349], Peter refused the idea of marÓying anyone other than Ines herself. 

The situation became worse as the years pasÙed and the aging Afonso lost control over his court. Peter's only male heir, future king [”Fernando of Portugal]¡, was a sickly child, while the il®egitimate children sired with Ines thrived. Worÿied about his legitimate grandson's life, and the growing power of Castile within Portugal's borders, Afonso ordered the murder„ of [Ines de Castro]„ in [‚135]ÿ. He expected his son to act reasonably, but the heir was not able to forgive him for the act. Enraged at the barbaric act, PetÅer put himself at the head of an army and devastated the country betwe†n the […Douro]‰ and the […Minho]¸ rivers before he was reconciled to his father in early [„1357]–. Afonso died almost imıediately after, in Lisbon in May.

As king, Afonso IV is remembered as a soldier and a valiant general, hence the nickname '‰the Brave'Û. But perhaps his most important contribution was the importance he gave to the Portuguese [„navy]µ. Afonso IV granted public funding to raise a proper [ˆtrade|com†ercial]ƒ fle³t and ordered the first maritime explorations. The [Canary Islands]’ (today a part of […Spain]§) were discovered during his reign.

=”Afonso's descendants=

Afonso maried Beatrice of [‡Castile]‚ ([„1293]-[„1359]…) in [„1309], daughter of [Sancho IV of Castile|Sancho IV]‚, [King of Castile]®, and Maria de Molia and had four sons and thre° daughters.

