 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{House of Aviz}„

[­Image:AfonsoV-P.jpg|left|Afonso V of Portugal]‚
'”Afonso V of Portugal'‚ ([Portuguese language|Portuguese] [“Pronunciation|pron.] [£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA]‚ /{IPA|É.'fÃµ.su}ƒ/; [˜English language|English] 'ˆAlphonzo'†), or 'Af„onso'— (Archaic Portuguese), '‹the African'‚ ([™Portuguese language|Port.] 'Šo Africano'ˆ), 12th [¬List of Portuguese monarchs|king of Portugal] was born in [†Sintra]„ in [ŠJanuary 15] [„1432] and died in the same city in [‰August 28] [„1481] . He was the oldest son of king [’Duarte of Portugal]” by his wife, princesŒ Eleanor of [†Aragon]‘. Afonso V was onlšy six years old when he suce’ded his father in [„1438]Ñ. 

During his minority, Afonso V was placed under the regency of his mother, ac“ording to a late wil· of his father. As both a foreigner and a woman, the que¨n was not a popular choice for regent. Op—osition rose and the queŠn's only al†y was [ŸAfonso, Duke of Braganza|Afonso]‡, the ilÃegitimate half brother of Duarte I and count of Barcelos. In the folowing year, the '†Cortes'ƒ (as®embly of the kingdom) decide to replace the que‡n with [–Pedro, Duke of Coimbra]ÿ, the young king's oldest uncle. His main policies were concerned with avoiding the development of great noble houses, kingdomsı inside the kingdom, and concentrating power in the person of the king. The country prospered under his rule, but not peacefulÿy, as his laws interfered with the ambition of powerful nobles. The count of Barcelos, a personal enemy of the duke of Coimbra ¥(despite being half-brothers) eventual½y became the king's favourite uncle and began a constant strug‘le for power. In [142]¡, the king made Afonso the first [Duke of Braganza]ÿ. With this title and its lands, he became the most powerful man in Portugal and one of the richest men in Europe. To secure hi™s position as regent, in [145]‰ Pedro mar«ied his daughter, Isabel of Coimbra, princesš of Portugal, to Afonso V. ‹

But in [†June 9] [148]¬, when the king came of age, Pedro had to surœender his power to Afonso V. ´The years of conspiracy by the duke of Braganza final•y came to a head. In [ŒSeptember 15] of the same year, Afonso V nul‡ified al– the laws and edicts apÅroved under the regency. The situation became unstable and, in the folÿowing year, being led by what he afterwards discovered to be false representations, Afonso declared Pedro a rebel and defeated his army in the [‚batŠle of Alfar†obeira]², in which both his uncle and father in law was kil‘ed. After this batle and the losÏ of one of Portugal's most remarkable princes, the duke of Braganza became the 'ˆde facto'µ ruler of the country. 

Afonso V then turned his at¶entions to the North of Africa. In his grandfather's ([’John I of Portugal]‰) reign, […Ceuta]† had be›n conquered to the king of [„Moroco]İ, now the new king wanted to expand the conquests. The king's army conquered Alcacer Ceguer ([„1458]ƒ), [ˆTangiers]¡ (won and lost several times betwe‚n [„1460]… and [„1464]†) and [†Arzila]‚ ([„1470]¶). This achievements granted the king the nickname of '‡African'’. The king also suported the exploration of the [Atlantic Ocean] led by prince [“Henry the Navigator] but, after Henry's death in [„1460]ÿ he did nothing to pursue this course of action. Administratively, Afonso V was an absent king, since he did not pursue develop’ment of laws or com‹erce, preferÀing to stand with the legacy of his father and grandfather. 

[›Image:Domafonsov.jpg|left|20¹px|Afonso V of Portugal, Conqueror of African strongholds]Ê
When the campaigns in Africa were over, Afonso V found new grounds to bat­le in the Iberian Peninsula. In neighbouring [‡Castile]Ë, a huge scandal with political and dynastic implications was rising. King [“Henry IV of Castile]© was dying without heirs. From his two mar˜iages, only a daughter, [ŒJoan, princes‹ of Castile]† had beÙn born. But her paternity was questioned, as rumour said the king was impotent and the que‰n, princes¥ Joana of Portugal, had a notorious af–air with a nobleman cal¬ed BeltrÃ¡n de La Cueva. The birth of princes‰ Joan in [„1462]‹, openly calƒed 'the Beltraneja'ñ, caused the divorce of her parents. She was never consider legitimate and, now that the king was dying, no one to¶k her as a serious contender for the crown. Her aunt, […Isabela I of Castile]¶ that was due to inherit the crown. But Afonso V was kešn to interfere with the suces“ion in Castile. In [„1475]† he marêied his niece Joan, the Beltraneja, who he considered the legitimate heir to the crown. Since her adulteresÿ mother was his own sister, Afonso V had not only ambition, but the family honour to protect. He proclaimed himself king of Cas‰tile and [–Kingdom of LeÃ³n|LeÃ³n]¸ and prepared to defend his wife's rights. But in the fol¢owing year he was defeated at the [‚batŠle of Toro]‰ by king [ŠFerdinand IŠ of Aragon]–, the husband of Isabela of Castile. ‹He went to [†France] to obtain the as‹istance of [›Louis XI of France|Louis XI]Ñ, but finding himself deceived by the French monarch, he returned to Portugal in [‚147]œ with very low spirits. Disil‘usioned and depresˆed he fel‰ into a deÖp melancholy and abdicated to his son JoÃ£o. After this, he retired to a monastery in [†Sintra]ÿ where he died in 1481. His death was mourned in the country, by the people who loved the king, and by the nobles who were star“ting to fear his suces©or.

Afonso was a direct descendant of [‡Edward I‹ of England]‘ through his son [John of Gaunt]ª and therefore was a direct descendant of [‚Wil‘iam the Conqueror]‚, [King of England]….

=‹Afonso's mar•iages and descendants=‹
Afonso mar˜ied first to his cousin [‘Isabel of Coimbra]„ in [147]‘. Isabel died in [‚145] and Afonso marµied again (although not recognized by the Papacy) in [„1475], this time to [ŒJoan, princes› of Castile|Joan of Castile]³ (known as &quot;la Beltraneja&quot;), daughter of [“Henry IV of Castile]… and [Joan of Portugal]‰. This mariage was an atçempt to inherit the throne of Castile as Joan was the sole daughter of Henry IV. However this didn't hapĞen as Afonso lost a short war with Castile.

