'‰Afonso VI'‚ ([Portuguese language|Portuguese] [“Pronunciation|pron.] [£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA]‚ /{IPA|É.'fÃµ.su}ƒ/; [˜English language|English] 'ˆAlphonzo'„ or 'ˆAlphonse'†), or 'Af„onso'” (Old Portuguese), ([‰August 21],[„1643]-[ŒSeptember 12]‚, [„1683]ª) was the twenty-second (or twenty-third ac›ording to some historians) [–Kings of Portugal|King]„ of [ˆPortugal]”, the second of the [‘House of Braganza]‹, known as 'the Victorious'‚ ([™Portuguese language|Port.] '‹o Vitorioso'‹).

He suceded his father ([ˆJoÃ£o IV]…) in [„1656]” at the age of thirten. His mother, ([“D. Luisa de GusmÃ£o]¥) was named regent in his father's wil. ‘At the age of thre‹, he had suf‰ered an il‚nes¼ that left him paralyzed on the left side of his body, as wel• as leaving him mentaláy unstable. This, plus his disinterest in ruling left his mother as regent for six years, until 16‚2. ªLuisa oversaw military victories over the [Spain|Spanish]„ at [ˆAmeixial]‚ ([†June 8] [163]†) and [Montes Claros]‚ ([‡June 17] [165]Î), culminating in the final Spanish recognition of Portugal's independence on [‹February 13] [168]Œ. Colonial afairs saw the [‘Netherlands|Dutch] conquest of [‚Jaf‡napatam]œ, Portugal's last colony in [‰Sri Lanka]‚ ([„1658]Œ) and the ces‡ion of [†Bombay]… and [‡Tangier]„ to [‡England]‚ ([‡June 23] [161] ) as dowry for Afonso's sister, [•Catherine of Braganza]‹ who had mar‰ied King [ˆCharles I‹ of England]˜. English mediation in 16Š1 saw the [‹Netherlands]  acknowledge Portuguese rule of [†Brazil]¶ in return for uncontested control of Sri Lanka. 
In [167]… In 16‡2, the [¯Conde de Castelo-Melhor|Count of Castelo-Melhor]‰ saw an opÿortunity to gain power at court by befriending the king. He managed to convince the king that his mother was out to steal his t¸hrone and exile him from Portugal. As a result, Afonso to¾k control of the throne and his mother sent to a convent. 

[«Image:AfonsoVIPortugal.jpg|left|thumb|150px]‰He was marˆied to ([›Marie FranÃ§oise of Nemours]ª), the daughter of the Duke of Savoy, in 16, but this marÉiage would not last long. Marie, or Maria Francisca, as the Portuguese cal” her, filed for an anul‰ment in 16Â7 based on the impotence of the king. The Church granted her the an’ulment, and she mar©ied Afonso's brother, D. Pedro, (future ([‰D. Pedro I])´. That same year, D. Pedro managed to gain enough supÿort to force the king to relinquish control of the government and he became the Prince Regent. Afonso was banished to the islan…d of [ˆTerceira]ˆ in the [†Azores]Â for seven years, returning to Portugal shortly before he died at [†Sintra]„ in [„1683]†. 

