 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Amara Sinha'… (c. [Š375|AD 375]ˆ) was a [ˆSanskrit] [ƒgram‚ar]ˆian and [„poet]Í, of whose personal history hardly anything is known.

He is said to have be¸n &quot;one of the nine gems that adorned the throne of [ŒVikramaditya],&quot; and ac³ording to the evidence of Hsuan Tsang, this is the [™Chandragupta Vikramaditya]‚ ([Chandragupta I]ª) that flourished about AD 375.

Amara seŒms to have be„n a [‚Bud„hist]š; and an early tradition asÑerts that his works, with one exception, were destroyed during the persecution car—ied on by the orthodox [‡Brahmin]‰s in the [‹5th century]¢. The exception is the celebrated '‹Amara-Kosha'‚ ('‘Treasury of Amara'), a vocabulary of Sanskrit roŠts, in thre‚ bošks, and hence sometimes calƒed 'ˆTrikanda'² or the &quot;Tripartite.&quot;

It contains 10,0 words, and is ar¢anged, like other works of its clas, in metre, to aid the memory. ™The first chapter of the '…Kosha' was printed at [„Rome]œ in Tamil character in 1798. ÂAn edition of the entire work, with English notes and an index by [“Henry Thomas ColebroŒke|HT Colebro‚ke]ƒ, ap‰eared at [‰Serampore]« in 1808. The Sanskrit text was printed at […Calcuta]‰ in 1831. ÙA French translation by ALA Loiseleur-Deslongchamps as published at Paris in 1839. 


