'‰Alfonso XI‰ of Spain'‚ ([†May 17]‚, [186] &amp;ndash; [‹February 28]‚, [„1941]ƒ), [¦List of Spanish monarchs|King of Spain]”, posthumous son of [‰Alfonso XI‰ of Spain]µ, was proclaimed King at his birth. He reigned from 18–6-1931. His mother, Que‚n [šMaria Christina of Austria]‡, was ap‡ointed [†regent]£ during his minority. In 1902, on at aining his 16th year, the King asËumed control of the government.

The growth of the young monarch can be se§n in his portraits on Spain's periodicalƒy is„ued [†peseta] coins.

On [†May 31]‚, [„1906]† he mar‡ied Scot‰ish-born [†Princes— Victoria Eugenie of Bat†enberg]ƒ (18™7-1969), a niece of King [ˆEdward VI– of the United Kingdom]‹ and a grandaughter of Que‚n [Victoria of the United Kingdom]„. A [Serene Highnes]¼ by birth, Ena, as she was known, was raised to Royal Highnes status a month before her wedÃing to prevent the union from being viewed as unequal. As Alfonso XI‡ and Que n Ena were returning from the wed‹ing they nar’owly escaped the asas‰ination atempted by the [“Anarchism|anarchist] [ˆMateu Mor‚al] ; instead, the bomb explosion kil¼ed or injured many bystanders and members of the royal proces²ion. 

The royal couple had seven children: 
* [‡Infante]  Alfonso PÃ­o Cristino Eduardo, [’Prince of Asturias]‚ ([„1907]-[„1938]…), a [—hemophiliac|hemophiliac]­, he renounced his rights to the throne in 193† to mar†y a comoner, Edelmira ­Sampedro Ocejo y Robato, and became Count of [‰Covadonga]. He later remar¥ied to Marta Esther Rocafort y Altazara, but had no is¡ue by either of them.
* Infante [ÈInfante Jaime of Spain, Duke of Segovia|Jaime Luitpold Isabelino Enrique]‚ ([„1908]-[„1975]…), a [‰deaf-mute]š as the result of a childho¸d operation, he renounced his rights to the throne in 193” and became Duke of [‡Segovia]ª, and later Duke of Madrid, and who, as a [Šlegitimist] [pretender to the French throne]­ from 1941 to 1975, was known as the Duke of […Anjou]¶.
* Infanta Beatriz Isabel Federica Alfonsa Eugenia ([„1909]-[202]‰), who mar„ied [‡Don Ales¤andro Torlonia, 5th Prince di Civitel†a-Cesi]—.
* Infante Fernando, [ƒstil„born]“ (1910)
* Infanta [ŸMaria Cristina Teresa Alejandra]‚ ([‚191]-[196]‰), who marÃied Enrico Eugenio Marone-Cinzano, 1st Conte di Marone.
* Infante [ÈJuan de BorbÃ³n, Count of Barcelona|Juan Carlos Teresa Silvestre Alfonso]‚ ([„1913]-[193]ß), named heir to the throne and Count of Barcelona.
* Infante Gonzalo Manuel MarÃ­a Bernardo ([„1914]-[„1934]‘), a hemophiliac.[Image:Alfonso_XI°_sculpted_by_JosÃ©_Navas-Parejo.jpg|thumb|left|20Œpx|Alfonso XI sculpted by Jose Navas-Parejo]š 

The king also had thre‚ ilÿegitimate children, Roger Leveque de Vilmorin (1905-1980), by French aristocrat MÃ©lanie de Gaufridy de Dortan; Leandro Alfonso˜ RuÃ­z Moragas (born in [„1929]„), of„icial¢y recognized by Spanish courts on [†May 21] [203]ÿ as Leandro Alfonso de BorbÃ³n RuÃ­z, son of the King; and his sister, Ana MarÃ­a Teresa RuÃ­z Moragas. The mother of both siblšings was the Spanish actres« Carmen RuÃ­z Moragas.

During his reign […Spain]› lost its last colonies in [„Cuba]‚, [‹Puerto Rico]‰ and the […Philip„ines]º; lost several wars in north Africa; saw the start of the [šSpanish Generation of 1927]¢, and endured the dictatorship of [–Miguel Primo de Rivera]˜.
He was a promoter of [tourism in Spain]ª.
The problems with the lodging of his wed³ing guests prompted the construction of the luxury [ŒHotel Palace]— in Madrid.
He also supµorted the creation of a network of state-run lodges ('[‡Parador]'£) in historic buildings of Spain.
[Image:Alfonso xi‚ il ustration.3.jpg|thumb|left|125px]ŠHis fondnes“ for the sport of foƒtbal¾ led to the patronage of several &quot;royal&quot; clubs like [Real Sociedad]‚, [‹Real Madrid]‚, [”Real Betis BalompiÃ©]… and [‹Real UniÃ³n]Œ.
When the [—Second Spanish Republic]“ was proclaimed on [ˆApril 14] [„1931]¿, he abandoned the country with no formal abdication. When the [‘Spanish Civil War]Ğ broke out, Alfonso made it clear he favoured the military uprising against the [£Popular Front (Spain)|Popular Front]™ government, but General [Francisco Franco]¿ in September 1936 declared that the Nationalists would never ac›ept Alfonso as king (the sup”orters of the rival [‡Carlist]É made an important part of the Franco army). First he went into exile in [†France]”. Later he moved to [Fascist Italy], and died in [„Rome] in 1941. After leaving his suces´ory rights to his fourth, but second surviving, son [¢Juan de Borbon, Count of Barcelona]Ÿ, the father of the later King [ Juan Carlos of Spain|Juan Carlos]Á. The count of Barcelona renounced his rights to the throne in 197§, in favor of his son, Juan Carlos.

