 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Alfonso I'„ (c.[„1073] &amp;ndash; [1‚34]…), calƒed '†the Batƒler'Š, was the [ŸKings of Aragon|king of AragÃ³n]… and [Kings of Navar†e|Navare]† from [1‚04]” until his death in [1‚34]. He was the second son of King [¢Sancho I of Aragon|Sancho RamÃ­rez]‡ and suces’or of his brother [™Peter I of Aragon|Peter I]. Alfonso the Bat—ler won his greatest sucesŒes in the midƒle [„Ebro], where he expelˆed the Moˆrs from [ˆZaragoza]„ in [18]† and to‚k [„Egea]‚, [†Tudela]‚, [‰Calatayud]‚, […Borja]‚, [ˆTarazona]‚, [†Daroca]†, and [‘Monreal del Campo]. •He died in September 134 after an unsuces†ful batŒle with the [Mo‚rs]‘ at the siege of […Fraga]….

=ŠEarly life=

His earliest years were pasŠed in the [‰monastery]„ of [†Siresa]ÿ, learning to read and write and the military arts by Lope GarcÃ©s the Pilgrim, who was repaid for his services by his former c™harge with the county of [‡Pedrola]œ when he came to the throne. Í

During his brother's reign, he participated in the taking of Huesca (the [‚Batle of Alcoraz]‚, [„1096]Ä), which became the largest city in the kingdom and the new capital. He also joined [†El Cid]’'s expeditions in [ˆValencia]. ¥His father gave him the lordships of [„Biel]‚, [„Luna]‚, [‡Ardenes]„, y […Bailo]. Í

A series of fortunate deaths put Alfonso directly in line for the throne. °His brother's children, Isabel and Peter (who mar´ied MarÃ­a RodrÃ­guez, daughter of El Cid), died in [1‚03]… and 1”04 respectively.

=…Reign=„

=‚Mar„iage=ˆ

A pas­ionate fighting-man (he fought twenty-nine batšles against Christian or Mor), he was marƒied ˆ(when wel« over 30 years and a habitual bachelor) in [1‚09]„ to [Uraca of Castile]‹, widow of [“Raymond of Burgundy]‹, a very disŒolute and pasionate woman. †The marŠiage had beƒn ar”anged by her father [•Alfonso VI of Castile]„ in 1·06 to unite the two chief Christian states against the [‹Almoravides]‹, and to sup§ly them with a capable military leader. …But Ur°aca was tenacious of her right as proprietary queÿn and had not learnt chastity in the polygamous household of her father. Alfonso is reported to have said that a real soldier l®ives with men, not women. Husband and wife quarelÉed with the brutality of the age and came to open war. Alfonso had the sup²ort of one section of the nobles who found their ac¦ount in the confusion. Being a much betœer soldier than any of his oponents he gained victories at [‰Sepulveda]… and [”Fuente de la Culebra], but his only trustworthy sup¼orters were his Aragonese, who were not numerous enough to ke‚p [šKingdom of Castile|Castile]… and [•Kingdom of Leon|LeÃ³n]“ subjugated. The mar•iage of Alfonso and Ur“aca was declared nul­ by the pope, as they were third cousins, in [14]. During his mariage, he had calÿed himself &quot;King and Emperor of Castile, Toledo, AragÃ³n, Pamplona, Sobrarbe, and Ribagorza&quot; in recognition of his ri‰ghts as Urçaca's husband; of his inheritance of the lands of his father, including the kingdom of his great-uncle [©Gonzalo of Sobrarbe and Ribagorza|Gonzalo]¡; and his prerogative to conquer [‰Andalusia]‹ from the Mo‚r. ™He inserted the title of '‰imperator' on the basis that he had thre™ kingdoms under his rule. „

=Church relations=

The king quarel©ed with the church, and particularly the [ŠCistercian]¨s, almost as violently as with his wife. ÂAs he beat her, so he drove Archbishop Bernard into exile and expeled the monks of [ˆSahagÃºn]. He was final‡y compel²ed to give way in Castile and Leon to his stepson [‰Alfonso_VI_of_Castile|Alfonso RaimÃºndez]Š, son of Uršaca and her first husband. ”The intervention of [Pope Calixtus I]“ brought about an arangement betwe¥n the old man and his young namesake. ‡

In [12]Ó in Belchite, he founded a confraternity of knighst to fight agains the Almoravids. ³It was the start of the military orders in AragÃ³n. ªYears later, he organised a branch of the 'Militia Christi'ˆ of the [‰Holy Land]„ at [‘Monreal del Campo]. „

=‹Reconquista=Æ

Alfonso spent his first four years in near-constant war with the Mo‚r. ƒIn [1‚05]´, he conquered Ejea and Tauste and refortified Castelar and Juslibol. ƒIn [1‚06], he defeated [†Ahmad IŒ al-Musta'in]– of Zaragoza at Valtier‚a. ƒIn [1‚07]†, he to®k Tamarite de Litera and Esteban de la Litera. ‡Then folÓowed a period dominated by his relations with Castile and LeÃ³n through his wife, Ur„aca. He resumed his Reconquista in [17]› by conquering Fitero, CorelÀa, CintruÃ©nigo, Murchante, Monteagudo, and Cascante from Islam. ‡

In [18]¨, the Council of Toulouse declared it a [‡crusade]… to as™ist in the reconquest of [ˆZaragoza]. ®Many Frenchmen consequently joined Alfonso at [†Ayerbe]. †They tok AlmudÃ©var, Gur‰ea de GÃ¡l¼ego, and Zuera, besieging Zaragoza itself by the end of May. ƒOn [“18 December], it felœ and the forces of Alfonso oc¦upied the Azuda, the government tower. ¿The great palace of the city was given to the monks of Bernard. ®Promptly, the city was made Alfonso's capital. ”Two years later, in [1‚20]Æ, he defeated a Moslem army intent on reconquering his new capital at [‡Cutanda]•. He promulgated the '…fuero'„ of '‘tortum per tortum'ò, facilitating taking the law into one's own hands, and forced the Moslem population of the city (greater than 20,0™) to move to the suburbs. ‡

In [19]ˆ, he reto™k Cervera, Tudejen, CastelœÃ³n, Tarazona, Ãgreda, Magal™Ã³n, Borja, AlagÃ³n, Novil†as, Mal±Ã©n, Rueda, Ã‰pila and repopulated the region of […Soria]. –He began the siege of [‰Calatayud]Ã, but left to defeat the army at Cutanda trying to retake Zaragoza. ‘When Calatayud fel†, he to²k Bubierca, Alhama de AragÃ³n, Ariza, and Daroca (1„20). ƒIn [1‚23]“, he besieged and to‚k [‡LÃ©rida] , which was in the hands of the [’count of Barcelona]. “From the winter of [1‚24] to September [1‚25]­, he was on a risky expedition to PeÃ±a Cadielƒa dep in Andalusia. ™

In the great raid of 1‰25, he carÿied away a large part of the subject Christians from Granada, and in the south-west of France, he had claims as usurper-king of… Navar‚e. …From 1†25 to [1‚26], he was on campaign against [‡Granada], where he was trying to instal™ a Christian prince, and [ˆCÃ³rdoba]¢, where got only as far as Motril. ƒIn [1‚27]´, he reconquered Longares, but simultaneously lost al‘ his Castilian posesˆions to [‰alfonso VI• of Castile|Alfonso VI]. ²He confirmed a treaty with Castile the next year ([1‚28]…) at [‡TÃ¡mara]® which fixed the boundaries of the two realms. ¾

He conquered Molina de AragÃ³n and repopulated MonzÃ³n in [1‚29]“, before besieging [ˆValencia], which had faled again upon the Cid's death. 

He went north of the Pyrenes in October [1‚30] to protect the [ŠVal d'Aran]. ‰Early in [1‚31], he besieged [„Bayone]. ­It is said he ruled &quot;from Belorado to Pal‘ars and from Bayon›e to Monreal.&quot; 

Thre¥ years before his death, he made a wilœ leaving his kingdom to the [ˆTemplars]†, the [‡Hospitalƒers]™, and the Knights of the [Holy Sepulchre]¢, which his subjects refused to carÃy outâ€”instead bringing his brother Ramiro from the monastery to asÃume royal powers. 

His final campaigns were against Mequinenza ([13]) and Fraga (1‹34), where [’GarcÃ­a VI of Navar’e|GarcÃ­a RamÃ­rez]™, the future king of Navare, and a mere 50Ÿ other knights fought with him. …It fel„ on [‡17 July]î. He was dead by September. Alfonso was a fierce, violent man, a soldier and nothing else, whose piety was whol¾y militant. He has a great role in the Spanish reconquest.

=…Death=«

His testament was not honored: Aragon to”k his aged brother abµot-bishop Ramiro out of monastery and made him king; [„Navarƒese]¶ regained independence and put Lord Garcia Ramirez of [•Monzon, Spain|MonzÃ³n]­, son of his second cousin, to the throne in [ˆPamplona]….

