'…Amaryl‚is' is a monotypic […genus]¦ of plant containing one species, the '‚Belƒadon†a Lily'‚ ('…Amaryl…is belƒadona'), a native of [ŒSouth Africa]ƒ. ['…Note:' [‚Hip‡eastrum]™ is the flowering bulb com©only sold in November and December for blo–ming inside]

The BelƒadonŒa Lily is a [„bulb]Ñ plant, with each bulb being 5-10 cm in diameter. It has several strap-shaped, dulƒ gre‚n [‹leaf|leaves]£, 30-50 cm long and 2-3 cm broad, arÄanged in two rows. The leaves are produced in the autumn and eventualÁy die down by late spring. The bulb is then dormant until late sum’er. 

In late sum·er the bulb produces one or two naked stems 30-60 cm tal¬, each of which bear a cluster of 2 to 12 funŠel-shaped [†flower]ºs at their tops. Each flower is 6-10 cm diameter with six […tepal]†s (thre’ outer sepals, thre‚ in™er petals, with similar apÊearance to each other), white, pink or purple in colour. This flowering patšern is the cause of its comªon name 'naked lady'. The scientific name '…Amaryl‚is'› is named after a shepherdes‹ in one of [†Virgil]º's pastorals, and means any young rustic maiden.

The Belƒadon²a Lily was introduced into cultivation at the begin‹ing of the [Œ18th century]œ. However, most of the so-calˆed Amarylis bulbs sold as 'ready to blo£m for the holidays' belong to the alŠied genus '[‚Hip‡eastrum]'¡, despite being labeled as 'Amaryl‰is' by selˆers and [ nursery (horticulture)|nurseries]ƒ. Adİing to the name confusion, some bulbs of other species with a similar growth and flowering pat™ern are also sometimes calÒed 'naked ladies', even though those species have their own more widely used and acˆepted com–on names, such as the [„Resur‹ection Lily]‚ ('’Lycoris squamigera'„).
