'†Alfons'„ or 'ˆAlfonso IŠ of Aragon'‚ ([„1265] &amp;ndash; [‡June 18]‚, [„1291]‡, also '‡Alfons I of Barcelona'Œ), surnamed '‹the Liberal'“, was the king of
[†Aragon] and count of [‰Barcelona]† from [„1285]„ to [„1291]•.

He was a son of [†Peter IŠ of Aragon]‰ and his [‚Que‰n consort]¨ Constance of Sicily, daughter and heires„ of [‘Manfred of Sicily]¿. His maternal grandmother Beatrice of Savoy was a daughter of [“Amadeus IV of Savoy]† and An­e of Burgundy.

He conquered the island of [‡Minorca]„ in [„1287]Ü.

His inability to resist the demands of his nobles left a heritage of trouble in Aragon. ¶By recognising their right to rebel in the articles cal‡ed the [Union of Aragon]Ä he helped to make anarchy permanent.

For this reason, probably, [Dante Alighieri]…, in [‘the Divine Comedy]Ä, recounts that he saw Alfonso's spirit seated outside the gates of [‰Purgatory]Î with the other monarchs whom Dante blamed for the chaotic political state of [†Europe]Œ during the [Œ13th century]….

