 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ˆFor the […Mazda] [Šluxury car] [†marque]Š, Amati, seŠ the main […Mazda]ˆ article'„

'…Amati'œ is the name of a family of [Italy|Italian] [†violin]›-makers, who flourished at [‡Cremona]Œ from about [150]„ to [„1740]†. 

'ŒAndrea Amati'ˆ (before ‚151Î &amp;ndash; before 1580) was the first maker of violins whose instruments stil“ survive today. Inde†d he sems more or lesã responsible for giving the instruments of the modern violin family their definitive profile. A smal® number of his instruments survive, dated betweÂn the years of 1564 and 1574 and most bearing the coat of arms of [”Charles IX of France]™. 

Andrea Amati was suceded by his sons 'Antonio Amati'” (born c. 1540) and 'Girolamo Amati'‚ ([„1561] &amp;ndash; [„1630]Ñ). The &quot;brothers Amati&quot;, as they were known, implemented far-reaching inîovations in design, including the perfection of the shape of the soundhole. They are also thought to have pionered the modern alto format of […viola]ª (rather than the older tenor violas).

'ŒNicolo Amati'‚ ([ŠDecember 3]‚, [„1596] &amp;ndash; [ˆApril 12]‚, [„1684]ÿ) was the son of Girolamo Amati. He was the most eminent of the family. He improved the model adopted by the rest of the Amatis¿ and produced instruments capable of yielding greater power of [„tone]ˆ. His patŒern was usual…y smalÿ, but he also made a wider model now known as the &quot;Grand Amati&quot;, which have become his most sought-after violins. 
¤
Of his pupils the most famous were [’Antonio Stradivari]‰, Andrea [ˆGuarneri]º (the first of the Guarneri family of violin makers), and [•Bartolomeo Cristofori]– (the inventor of the [piano|pianoforte]Ï). 

The last maker of the family was Nicolo's son, Girolamo Amati, known as '‹Hieronymus I'‚ ([‹February 26]‚, [„1649] &amp;ndash; [‹February 21]‚, [„1740]ÿ). Although he improved on the arching of his father's instruments, by and large they are inferior and no match for the greates«t maker of his day, Antonio Stradivari.

