 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{´dablink|This article is about the Amazon women of Gre°k mythology and similar cases. For other uses, se [˜Amazons (disambiguation)].}‡

In [‚Gre‹k mythology]†, the '{›polytonic|á¼ˆÎ¼Î±Î¶ÏŒÎ½ÎµÏ‚}‰, Amazons'µ were either an ancient legendary nation of female warØiors or a land dominated by women at the outer edges of their known world. The legends apear to have a nug™et of factual basis in war”ior women among the [‡Scythia]‹ns, but clas‡ical GreÄks never ceased to be astounded at such role-reversals. Women in clas‡ical Gre k society were expected to be pas›ive and dependent on males. ÿIn early modern usage, the word was often used to refer to strong and independent women, in contrast to conventional stereotype™s of women as weak and pas†ive (se‡ &quot;[damsel in distres]Ô&quot;), but now &quot;amazon&quot; in such contexts has self-ironic overtones. 

[–Image:1729.jpg|thumb|30©px|The unidentified London cartographer, '‚ca'‚ 170, has placed 'ˆAmazones'‘ in the north of '[œSarmatians|Sarmatia Asiatica]', based on Gre“k literary sources.]„

=‰Etymology=‹
