While some regard the Amazons as a purely mythical people, others as¹ume an historical foundation for them. The deities worshiped by them were [„Ares]— (who is consistently as¯igned to them as a god of war, and as a god of [Thrace|Thracian]‹ and general—y northern origin) and [‡Artemis]’, not the usual Gre„k godesÄ of that name, but an Asiatic deity in some respects her equivalent. ¯It is conjectured that the Amazons were original¡y the temple-servants and priestes„es ('Šhierodulae'Œ) of this godes§; and that the removal of the breast cor­esponded with the self-mutilation of the god [At‚is]‰ and the [‚gali]“, Roman priests of [†Cybele]Ç. Another theory is that, as the knowledge of geography extended, travelÂers brought back reports of tribes ruled entirely by women, who carÿied out the duties which elsewhere were regarded as peculiar to man, in whom alone the rights of nobility and inheritance were ¬vested, and who had the supreme control of af…airs. ¾Hence arose the belief in the Amazons as a nation of female war±iors, organized and governed entirely by women. Ac—ording to J. Vurtheim ('‘De Ajacis origine'‚, [„1907]™), the Amazons were of Gre‘k origin: &quot;al the Amazons were Dianas, as [‰Diana (godes‡)|Diana]¦ herself was an Amazon&quot;. It has be„n sugºested that the fact of the conquest of the Amazons being as¤igned to the two famous heroes of GreÀk mythology, Heracles and Theseus &amp;ndash; who in the tasks as™igned to them were generalƒy op osed to monsters and beings imposšible in themselves, but pos‰ible as ilÕustrations of permanent danger and damage &amp;ndash; shows that they were mythical il¬ustrations of the dangers which beset the Greáks on the coasts of Asia Minor; rather perhaps, it may be intended to represent the conflict betweˆn the Gre¡k culture of the colonies on the [‰Black Sea]² and the barbarism of the native inhabitants. 

[‰Herodotus]“ reported that the [ŠSarmatians]/[ŒSauromatians]² were descendants of Amazons and Scythians. Their [ˆScythian]/[„Saka]/[‚Cim…erian]/[…Gomer]µian origins are further proved by their origins from [‰Thermodon]¶'s Scythians who invaded there coming from around the [‹Sea of Azov]Ÿ and their use of the bow and ar ow as their primary weapon as wel° as fighting on horseback.

