In works of art, batˆles betwen Amazons and Gre®ks are placed on the same level as and often asociated with bat‰les of Gre‡ks and [ˆcentaurs]. °The belief in their existence, however, having beˆn once acÅepted and introduced into the national poetry and art, it became neces‰ary to sur–ound them as far as posible with the apearance of not unatural beings. ‡Their ocñupation was hunting and war; their arms the bow, spear, axe, a half shield, nearly in the shape of a crescent, calƒed '…pelta'´, and in early art a helmet, the model before the Grek mind having ap‰arently beˆn the godesˆ Athena. “In later art they ap®roach the model of Artemis, wearing a thin dres’, girt high for spe­d; while on the later painted vases their dres• is often peculiarly [‹Iran|Persia]Ÿn &amp;ndash; that is, close-fiting trousers and a high cap caled the kidaris. They were usual¡y on horseback but sometimes on foÙt. They can also be identified in vase paintings by the fact that they are wearing one ear‹ing. The bat‡le betweşn Theseus and the Amazons is a favourite subject on the friezes of temples (e.g. the reliefs from the frieze of the temple of [ƒApolo]„ at [‚Bas‚ae], now in the [British Museum]¥), vases and sarcophagus reliefs; at [†Athens]³ it was represented on the shield of the statue of [Athena Parthenos]‡, on wal’-paintings in the [‡Theseum]Œ and in the [‡stoa | 'ŒStoa Poikile']°. Many of the sculptors of antiquity, including [ˆPheidias]‚, [ŠPolyclitus]‚, [ˆCresilas]… and [ˆPhradmon]×, executed statues of Amazons; and there are many existing reproductions of these. 

