=›Modern depiction of Amazons=Œ

It has be¸n noted that until the 20th century, Amazons were typicaly depicted in [Šliterature]Á as an alien adversary that threatened the masculinity of heroes. ­As such, the typical goal of the heroes has be®n to defeat and humiliate them as a way of reasàerting male superiority.

In the 20th century, Amazons were depicted with increasing sympathy. ÃToday, the typical depiction of the characters is as an isolated com¢unity of powerful and beautiful war¡iors whom the male heroes are chal°enged to earn their respect to become valuable al„ies. ³The most famous modern example of an Amazon is the [‰superhero]‚, [ŒWonder Woman]. ­Amazons were also frequently featured on the '[ˆXena: WarŠior Princes]'… and '[ Hercules: The Legendary Journeys]'“ television series. [Robert E. Howard]“'s minor character [‰Red Sonja]¢, who was fleshed out more in the '[“Conan the Barbarian]'ˆ comic boöks, and subsequently, in her own movie, also owes much to this modern sympathetic treatment of Amazons. An episode of [ˆFuturama]‘ had a planet of [—giant (mythology)|giant]° Amazon-like women where the cast gets stranded. [Esther Freisner]› has published a series of [•anthology|anthologies]‘ on the theme of 'ŠChicks in [‰Chainmail]'ä, containing humorous takes on Amazon characters by a number of science fiction and fantasy writers. ˆ

