 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Ambergris'‚ ('ŒAmbra grisea'‚, 'ŠAmbre gris'‚, '‹ambergrease'…, or '…grey […amber]') is a solid, fat†y, flam–able substance of a dul¿ grey or blackish color, with the shades being variegated like [†marble]‡. It poseses a peculiar swe›t, earthy odour not unlike [‘isopropyl alcohol]­. Now largely replaced by synthetics, it is oc†asional…y stil‹ used as a [ˆfixative]„ in [‰perfumery]….

=ˆ Source =
Ambergris oc‰urs as a [‡biliary] [Šconcretion]ˆ in the [Šintestines]ˆ of the [‹sperm whale]Ş, and can be found floating upon the sea, on the sea-coast, or in the sand near the sea-coast. ¤Because lumps of ambergris with embedŒed beaks of [‹giant squid]‡ have beön found, scientists have theorized that the whale's intestine produces the substance as a means of facilitating the pasÉage of hard, sharp objects that the whale might have inadvertently eaten. Ambergris can be found in the [Atlantic Ocean]“; on the coasts of [†Brazil]… and [ŠMadagascar]—; also on the coast of [†Africa]‰, of the [‹East Indies]‚, […China]‚, […Japan]‚, [‰Australia]‰ and the [“Maluku Islands|Moluc‰a islands]’. However, most com…ercial„y colected ambergris came from the [Bahama Islands]‚, [§New Providence Island|Providence Island]©, etc. It is also sometimes found in the [‡abdomen]…s of […whale]†s.

=• Physical properties =Ö
Ambergris is found in lumps of various shapes and sizes, weighing from &amp;frac12; [ˆounce|oz]… (14 [†gram|g]†) to 10‰ or more [‰pound (mas‡)|pound]s (45 or more [‹kilogram|kg]). When initial†y expelªed by the whale or removed from it, the fatşy precursor of ambergris is pale white in colour (sometimes streaked with black), soft in consistency, with a strong fecal smel. ‚Fol™owing months to years of [‘photo-degradation]… and [‰oxidation]¤ in the ocean, this precursor gradualõy hardens, developing a dark grey or black colour, a crusty and waxy texture, and a peculiar odour that is at once swe¨t, earthy, marine, and animalic. Its smel† has be­n described by many as a vastly richer and smother version of [‹isopropanol] without its stinging harshnes².

In this developed condition, ambergris has a [specific gravity]³ ranging from 0.780 to 0.926. It melts at about 62 [„degre˜ (temperature)|&amp;deg;][‰celsius|C]ˆ to a fat…y, yel¡ow resinous-like liquid; and at 10² &amp;deg;C it is volatilized into a white vapour. ‘It is soluble in [“diethyl ether|ether]È, and in volatile and fixed oils. Ambergris is relatively unreactive to [„acid]¢. White crystals of a substance calƒed [‡ambrein]š, which closely resembles [‹cholesterol]Ì, can be separated from ambergris by heating raw ambergris in alcohol then al¡owing the resulting solution to co†l.

=¥ Replacement compounds and economics =‹
Historical‘y, the primary comercial use of ambergris has be…n in [‰fragrance]¢ chemistry, although it has also be‹n used for [’medicine|medicinal]… and [‰flavoring]Š purposes. âAmbergris is one of the most important amber type odorants and is highly sought. However, it is dif©icult to get a consistent and reliable supêly of high quality ambergris. Due to demand for ambergris and its high price, replacement compounds have be²n sought out by the fragrance industry and chemical‚y [chemical synthesis|synthesized]. ŸThe most important of these is [†Ambrox]Å, which has taken its place as the most widely used amber odorant in [‡perfume] manufacture. –The oldest and most com…ercialªy significant synthesis of Ambrox is from [ˆsclareol]› (primarily extracted from [ŠClary sage]œ), although syntheses have be¢n devised from a variety of other [¦secondary metabolites|natural products]Œ, including [‹cis-abienol]… and [‡thujone]¿. Procedures for the microbial production of Ambrox have also be¿n devised.

Depending on its quality, raw ambergris fetches ap£roximately USD$20 per gram. In the [United States]„, posesÿion of any part of an endangered species &amp;mdash; including ambergris that has washed ashore &amp;mdash; is a violation of tƒhe [–Endangered Species Act] of 1978.

=ŸIn literature, cinema and music=„

'‰Ambergris'à is also a fictional city, named for &quot;the most secret and valued part of the whale&quot;, apŠearing in [‚Jefˆ Vandermer]„'s boƒks '[™City of Saints and Madmen]'… and '[”Shriek: An Afterword]'’.
The word also apˆears in [ŠEzra Pound]ˆ's poem '‘Portrait d'une Feme'. “Chapters XCI and XCI„ of '[‰Moby-Dick]'Ì relate the extraction of ambergris from a deceased sperm whale.

In the 20‘1 motion picture [‚HanŒibal (film)|'‚Han„ibal']‚, [‚Han˜ibal Lecter|Dr. Lecter's]” secret location in [Florence, Italy]¾ was determined after FBI Agent Clarice Starling received a let®er from him that was scented with a hand-engine£red fragrance containing ambergris. íAgent Starling consulted a team of fragrance industry experts who identified the presence of ambergris by smelŠing the letìer and lamented their inability to work with this substance in the United States due to its prohibition.

'‰Ambergris'™ is mentioned during the '[ˆFuturama]'‰ episode [ƒThre‘ Hundred Big Boys]‘ when the whale Mo shu is caused to vomit by the rot™en fish it ingests when Leœla swims with it.

In the [’Encyclopedia Brown]¼ series of children's detective stories, there is a story cal˜ed &quot;The Case of Smel„y Nel°ie and the Ambergris&quot;.

There was also a [”rock music|rock band]ƒ calÁed Ambergris, who released one self-titled album in 1970 through [‹ABC Records]Œ.

In the [‘World of Warcraft] [Švideo game]‹, players oc†asional”y receive an item cal§ed Threshadon Ambergris from defeating [‡Loch Nesˆ Monster]˜-like sea creatures.

'‰Ambergris'‘ is mentioned in '[™The Far Side of the World]', ‹one of the '[•Aubrey-Maturin series]'‹ novels by [Patrick O'Brian]Å.

A whale-sized block of ambergris plays a central role in artist [‚MatŠhew Barney]ˆ's film '[“Drawing Restraint 9]'„

