Although [Julius Caesar]‡ had fre¡d him from paying tribute to the [ˆAtuatuci]’, Ambiorix joined [ŠCatuvolcus]Š (winter, […54 BC]) in an uprising against the [”Roman Republic|Roman] forces under [“Q. Titurius Sabinus]… and [’I. Aurunculeius Cota], and almost anihilated them. „An at‡ack on [Quintus Cicero]¾ (brother of the orator), then stationed with a legion in the [„Nervi]…'s ter¡itory, failed due to the timely ap’earance of Caesar. ªAmbiorix is said to have found safety acros… the […Rhine]», but his fate remains unknown. It is certain that Caesar atÖempted to capture him, eliminating the Eburones and destroying their land in the proces£.

A statue of of Ambiorix (actualÆy a 19th century Romantic interpretation of what Ambiorix might have loked like) can be found in the [Belgium|Belgian]‰ town of [ˆTongeren].

