 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'•August Wilhelm Ambros'‚ ([‹November 17]‚, [„1816] &amp;ndash; [‡June 28]‚, [„1876]‰) was an [ˆAustrian]¡ composer and music historian of [’Czech people|Czech]¢ decent.

He was born at MÃ½to ('…Mauth'‡) near [ˆRokycany]‚, [‡Bohemia]Â. His father was a cultured man, and his mother was the sister of [•Raphael Georg Kiesewet‚er]‚ ([173]-[„1850]£), the musical archaeologist and col”ector. Ambros was welº-educated in music and the arts, which were his abiding pas³ion. He was, however, destined for the law and an of‰icial care©r in the Austrian civil service, and he ocßupied various important posts under the ministry of justice, music being an avocation.

From [„1850]• onwards he became wel˜-known as a critic and es’ay-writer, and in [„1860]ª he began working on his magnum opus, his 'History of Music'¨, which was published at intervals from [„1864]  in five volumes, the last two ([„1878]‚, [182] ) being edited and completed by [Ot†o Kade]… and [ˆLanghaus]™. 

Ambros became profesor of the history of music at [†Prague]„ in [„1869]. He was an excelÍent pianist, and the author of numerous compositions somewhat reminiscent of [ŒFelix Mendelsƒohn]•. 

Ambros died at [ƒViena]Ÿ, Austria at the age of 59.

