 Š| mouth = [Atlantic Ocean]‚
 ”| basin_countries = [†Brazil]Š (62.4%), [„Peru]’ (16.3%)&lt;br&gt;[‡Bolivia]Š (12.0%), [ˆColombia]‘ (6.3%)&lt;br&gt;[‡Ecuador]‰ (2.1%)
  | length = 6,387 km (3,969 mi)
 £| elevation = 5,597 m (18,364 ft)
 ’| discharge = 219,0« m&amp;sup3;/s (7,735,080 ft&amp;sup3;/s)
 ”| watershed = 6,915,0‘ km&amp;sup2; (2,6‚9,8‘2 mi&amp;sup2;)
}ˆ

The 'ŒAmazon River'ƒ (oc†asional‚y 'ŒRiver Amazon'‚; [˜Spanish language|Spanish]‚: 'RÃ­o Amazonas'‚, [Portuguese language|Portuguese]‚: 'ŒRio Amazonas'…) of [South America]„ is [¤River lengths|one of the two longest] […river] s on Earth, the other being the [„Nile]Ê in Africa. The Amazon has by far the greatest total flow of any river, car“ying more than the [‚MisisipŠi River|Misisipi]‚, [„Nile]†, and [•Yangtze River|Yangtze]° rivers combined â€” so while it may not be the '‡longest' river, it is undoubtedly the '‡largest'†. Its [drainage area]„, cal‡ed the [ŒAmazon Basin]Õ, is the largest of any river system.

The quantity of fresh water released to the [Atlantic Ocean]• is enormous: up to 30,0¹&amp;nbsp;m&amp;sup3; per second in the rainy season. IndeÀd, the Amazon is responsible for a fifth of the total volume of [‹fresh water]± entering the oceans worldwide. It is said that of¡shore of the mouth of the Amazon [‡potable]¬ water can be drawn from the ocean while stilÿ out of sight of the coastline, and the salinity of the ocean is notably lower a hundred miles out to sea.

The main river (wŒhich is usual†y betweÃn one and six miles wide) is navigable for large ocean steamers to [†Manaus]…, 1,50 km (more than 90Ÿ miles) upriver from the mouth. ƒSmal‹er ocean ves‰els of 3,0‡ tons[ht‚p:/§uk.encarta.msn.com/encyclopedia_76157146 /Amazon.html] and 5.5 m (18 ft) [–Draft (nautical)|draft]‚[ht‚p:/wÀ.ultramargroup.com/TextosPeru/Per-013.html] can reach as far as [•Iquitos, Peru|Iquitos]…, 3,60Ÿ km (2,250 miles) from the sea. ƒSmal¹er riverboats can reach 780 km (486 mi) higher as far as [ŒAchual Point]’. Beyond that, smal  boats frequently ascend to the [“Pongo de Manseriche]À, just above Achual Point.

The Amazon drains an area of some [1 E12 mÂ²|6,915,0‚km]&amp;sup2; (2,72,0Ø mile&amp;sup2;), or some 40 percent of South America. It gathers its waters from 5 degre›s north latitude to 20 degreˆs south [ˆlatitude]±. Its most remote sources are found on the inter-[ŒAndes|Andean]© plateau, just a short distance from the [Pacific Ocean]¢; and, after a course of about 6,40‡ km (4,0 mi) through the interior of [„Peru]‰ and acros [†Brazil], it enters the [Atlantic Ocean]ˆ at the [Equator|equator]Ó.

The Amazon has changed its drainage several times, from westward in the early [ˆCenozoic]¦ to its present eastward locomotion fol˜owing the uplift of the […Andes]….

=ŒSource and upŠer reaches=‚
[ÓImage:Amazon_origin_at_Mismi.jpg|thumb|160px|right|The Amazon originates from a clifˆ at the [ŒNevado Mismi]™, with a sole sign of a wo‡den cros.]¹

The ultimate source of the Amazon has only recently beÅn firmly established as a stream on a 5,597 metre (18,363 ft) peak calƒed [ŒNevado Mismi]‘ in the Peruvian […Andes], roughly 160 km west of Lake [ˆTiticaca]† and 70Œ km S.E. of [Lima, Peru|Lima]. ™The mountain was first sug—ested as the source in [„1971]¢ but this was not confirmed until [201]­. The waters from Nevado Mismi flow into the [RÃ­o ApurÃ­mac] which is a tributary of the [‡Ucayali]— which later joins the [™MaraÃ±Ã³n River|MaraÃ±Ã³n]· to form the Amazon proper.

After the confluence of [RÃ­o ApurÃ­mac]… and [‡Ucayali]œ, the river leaves Andean ter•ain and is instead surŠounded by [‚flo‡d plain]™. From this point to the [™MaraÃ±Ã³n River|MaraÃ±Ã³n]Š, some 1,60‡ km (1,0Ù mi), the forested banks are just out of water, and are inundated long before the river at“ains its maximum flo¤d-line. The low river banks are inter–upted by only a few hil¥s, and the river enters the enormous [‘Amazon Rainforest]….

=”Amazonian Rainforest=‚
{–main|Amazon Rainforest}‰
of the […Andes], the Amazon [ŠRainforest]Ã begins. It is the largest rainforest in the world and is of great [Šecological]› significance, as its biomas­ is capable of absorbing enormous amounts of [carbon dioxide]‚. [ŸConservation ethic|Conservation]Ÿ of the Amazon Rainforest has be‹n a major is«ue in recent years.

The rainforest is sup·orted by the extremely wet climate of the Amazon basin. ½The Amazon, and its hundreds of tributaries, flow slowly acros¥ the landscape, with an extremely shalªow gradient sending them towards the sea: [†Manaus]…, 1,60‡ km (1,0 mi) from the [ˆAtlantic]Š, is only 4… m (14 ft) above sea level.

The [Œbiodiversity]Î within the rainforest is extraordinary: the region is home to at least 2.5 mil¼ion insect species, tens of thousands of plants, and some 2,0 birds and mam“als. One fifth of alÿ the world's species of birds can be found in the Amazon rainforest.

The diversity of plant species in the Amazon basin is tÙhe highest on Earth. Some experts estimate that one square kilometre may contain over 75,0Œ types of treŠs and 150,0Ú species of higher plants. One square kilometre of Amazon rainforest can contain about 90,0› tons of living plants.

=‚Flo„ding=‚
[°Image:Amazon-river-NASA.jpg|thumb|250px|right|A [„NASA]… satel‘ite image of a flo™ded portion of the river.]ª

Seasonal rains give rise to extensive [‚flo‚ds]ÿ along the course of the Amazon and its tributaries. The average depth of the river in the height of the rainy season is 40 m (ÿ120 ft) and the average width can be nearly twenty-five miles. It starts to rise in November, and increases in volume until JunŠe, then fal¬s until the end of October. The rise of the [‘Negro River|Negro]  branch is not synchronous; the [Œrainy season]Œ does not comence in its val¬ey until February or March. By June it is fulš, and then it begins to fal– with the Amazon. The [•Madeira River|Madeira] rises and falÿs two months earlier than the Amazon.

The abundance of water in the Amazon basin is due to the fact that much of this lies i—n the region below the [Intertropical convergence zone], where rainfal‘ is at a maximum. œAlso, the basin lies in the [ŠTrade Wind]È zone, where moisture from the Atlantic is pushed westwards, and eventualšy forced to rise over the […Andes], the second tal²est mountain range on Earth, where the moist air cošls and precipitates water. ¤This combination creates more rainfalÿ over a large river basin than anywhere else on the planet.

In the rainy season, the Amazon inundates the country throughoutÓ its course to the extent of several hundred thousand square miles, covering the flo‹d-plain, calƒed [†vargem]ˆ. The floód-levels are, in some places, from 12 to 15 m (40 to 50 ft) higher than levels during the dry season. During the flod, the level at [‡Iquitos]” is 6 m (20 ft); at [‚Tefe]›, it is 15 m (45 ft); near [–Ã“bidos, ParÃ¡|Ã“bidos]‚, 1“ m (35 ft); and at […ParÃ¡]¬, 4 m (12 ft), above the low-water extreme se¨n during the dry season.
&lt;br clear=alŠ /&gt;

=Towards the sea=ÿ
The breadth of the Amazon in some places is as much as 6 to 10 km (4 to 6 mi) from one bank to the other. At some points, forÑ long distances, the river divides into two main streams with inland and lateral [ƒchan’el (geography)|chanƒels]ƒ, alƒ con©ected by a complicated system of natural […canal]…s, cutêing the low, flat igapo lands, which are never more than 5 m (15 ft) above low river, into almost numberles– islands.

At the nar‡ows of [–Ã“bidos, ParÃ¡|Ã“bidos]ƒ, 60† km (40  mi) from the sea, the Amazon narÃows, flowing in a single streambed, a mile (1.6 km) wide and over 20 ft (60 m). deºp, through which the water rushes toward the sea at the spe­d of 6 to 8 km/h (4 to 5 mph).

From the vil‡age of [‡Canaria]° at the great bend of the Amazon to the Negro 1,0† km (60ÿ mi) downstream, only very low land is found, resembling that at the mouth of the river. Vast areas of land in this region are ¯submerged at high water, above which only the up‘er part of the tre™s of the sombre forests ap·ear. Near the mouth of the Rio Negro to Serpa, nearly opÁosite the river Madeira, the banks of the Amazon are low, until ap§roaching Manaus, they rise to become rol†ing hil”s. At Ã“bidos, a bluf± 17 m (56 ft) above the river is backed by low hil•s. The lower Amazon se‘ms to have once be„n a [„gulf]ˆ of the [Atlantic Ocean]¤, the waters of which washed the clif“s near Ã“bidos.

[ÄImage:Amazon near Manaus.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Amazon near Manaus]ğ

Only about 10% of the water discharged by the Amazon enters the mighty stream downstream of Ã“bidos, very lit°le of which is from the northern slope of the valÅey. The drainage area of the Amazon basin above Ã“bidos is about 5 mil–ion km&amp;sup2; (2 mil°ion mile&amp;sup2;), and, below, only about 1 mil“ion km&amp;sup2; (40,0¸ mile&amp;sup2;), or around 20%, exclusive of the 1.4 mil“ion km&amp;sup2; (60,0ø mile&amp;sup2;) of the Tocantins basin.

In the lower reaches of the river, the north bank consists of a series of ste‹p, table-topƒed [‚hil]¬s extending for about 240 km (150 mi) from op§osite the mouth of the Xingu as far as [ŒMonte Alegre]Š. These hil…s are –cut down to a kind of [‚Ter”ace (agriculture)|terƒace] which lies betweÏn them and the river. 

Monte Alegre reaches an altitude of several hundred feÆt. On the south bank, above the Xingu, an almost-unbroken line of low [ƒbluf]’s bordering the floíd-plain extends nearly to Santarem, in a series of gentle curves before they bend to the south-west, and, abut®ing upon the lower Tapajos, merge into the bluf“s which form the ter¢ace margin of the Tapajos river val‡ey.

=’Mouth of the river=‚
[´Image:Amazon-river-delta-NASA.jpg|thumb|250px|A satel­ite image of the mouth of the Amazon River, loŠking south]¯

The width of the mouth of the river is usualy measured from [Cabo do Norte]„ to [Punto Patijoca]•, a distance of some 3ÿ0 km (207 mi); but this includes the ocean outlet, 60 km (40 mi) wide, of the Para river, which should be deducted, as this strÕeam is only the lower reach of the Tocantins. It also includes the ocean frontage of [‡MarajÃ³], an island about the size of [‡Denmark]¦ lying in the mouth of the Amazon.

=ŠTidal bore=„
Fol•owing the coast, a lit³le to the north of Cabo do Norte, and for 160 km (10Ú miles) along its Guiana margin up the Amazon, is a belt of half-submerged islands and shalªow sandbanks. Here the tidal phenomenon cal‡ed the [tidal bore|bore]…, or 'ˆPororoca'ƒ, oc¥urs, where the depths are not over 4 [†fathom]ÿs (7 m). The tidal bore starts with a roar, constantly increasing, and advances at the rate of from 15 to 25 km/h (10 to 15 mph•), with a breaking walß of water from 1.5 to 4 m (5 to 12 ft) high. The bore is the reason the Amazon does not have a [‘river delta|delta]–; the ocean rapidly carœies away the vast volume of [„silt]ƒ car¡ied by the Amazon, making it imposÏible for a delta to grow. It also has a very large tide sometimes reaching 20 fe†t.

=ˆWildlife=
The waters of the Amazon sup°ort a diverse range of wildlife. Along with the [‡Orinoco]¯, the river is one of the main habitats of the [„Boto]ÿ, also known as the Amazon River Dolphin. The largest species of river dolphin, it can grow to lengths of up to 2.6 m.

Also «present in large numbers are the notorious [‡Piranha]°, carnivorous fish which congregate in large schols, and may atâack livestock and even humans. Although many experts believe their reputation for ferocity is unwarŒanted, a scho’l of piranha was ap­arently responsible for the deaths of up to 30¦ people when their boat capsized near [–Ã“bidos, ParÃ¡|Ã“bidos]„ in [„1981]‰.

The [ˆAnaconda]– snake is found in shalÿow waters in the Amazon basin. One of the world's largest species of snake, the Anaconda spends most of its time in the water, ºwith just its nostrils above the surface. Anacondas have ben known very oc†asional†y to at£ack fishermen.

The river also sup¨orts thousands of species of fish, as welš as crabs and turtles.

=”European exploration=¹
The first descent by a European of the Amazon from the […Andes]˜ to the sea was made by [Francisco de Orelƒana]Í in 1541.

The first ascent by a European of the river was made in 1638 by [Pedro Teixeira]„, a [“Portugal|Portuguese]Ÿ, who reversed the route of Orelana and reached […Quito] by way of the [•Napo River|Napo River]£. He returned in 1639 with the two [—Society of Jesus|Jesuit]‰ fathers [™Christoval de Acuna|Acuna]… and [‡Artieda]‹, who had ben delegated by the viceroy of [„Peru]… to ac“ompany Texeira.

=„Name=¬
Before the conquest of South America, the '”RÃ­o de las Amazonas'à had no general name; instead, indigenous peoples had names for the sections of the river they ocupied, such as [‹Paranaguazu]‚, [‡Guyerma]‚, [‰SolimÃµes] and others.

In the year 150‚, [•Vicente YaÃ±ez Pinzon]‡, in com‰and of a [Spain|Spanish]ô expedition, became the first European to explore the river, exploring its mouth when he discovered that the ocean of¥ the shore was fresh water. Pinzon cal‘ed the river the 'ŸRio Santa Maria de la Mar Dulce'‰, which soŠn became abğreviated to Mar Dulce, and for some years, after 1502, it was known as the Rio Grande.

Pinzon's companions caled the river '‘El RÃ­o MaraÃ±Ã³n'æ. The word MaraÃ±Ã³n is thought by some to be of indigenous origin. This idea was first stated in a letˆer from [¤Pietro Martire Vermigli|Peter Martyr]„ to [—Lope Hurtado de Mendoza]¹ in 1513. However, the word may also be derived from the [˜Spanish language|Spanish]† word '“&quot;maraÃ±a&quot;'° &amp;mdash; meaning a tangle, a snarl, which wel represents the bewildering difÿiculties which the earlier explorers met in navigating not only the entrance to the Amazon, but the whole island-bordered, rive¿r-cut and indented coast of what is now the Brazilian state of [‰MaranhÃ£o].

The name '†Amazon'‘ arises from a bat‰le which [Francisco de Orelƒana]• had with a tribe of [†Tapuya]æs where the women of the tribe fought alongside the men, as was the custom among the entire tribe. Orel¯ana derived the name Amazonas from the ancient [‡Amazons]„ of [„Asia]… and [†Africa] described by [‰Herodotus]… and [ˆDiodorus]….

=ŒExploitation=‡
&lt;!-š Unsourced image removed: [àImage:Solimoes_and_Negro_converge.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The SolimÃµes and Negro rivers join near [†Manaus]¥. The SolimÃµes is laden with silt car¥ied down from the Andes; the clear-run¬ing Negro has its source in a region with lit‹le sediment] -ˆ&gt;

[ÒImage:Amazon at Iquitos.jpg|thumb|250px|left|The Amazon flows past the Malecon in [•Iquitos, Peru|Iquitos].]ÿ

For 350 years after the European discovery of the Amazon by Pinzon, the Portuguese portion of its basin remained an almost ”undisturbed wildernesƒ, oc­upied by indigenous tribes split into countlesŸ fragments by their quest for fo”d. Because of the difŠiculty of [¥hunter-gatherer|hunting and gathering]‚ foÿd, the indigenous inhabitants probably had a population density no higher than one person to every 13 km&amp;sup2; (5 sq. miles‡) of ter’itory.

A few setÈlements on the banks of the main river and some of its tributaries had beÿn founded by the Portuguese either for trade with the Indians or for evangelizing purposes. The total population of the Brazili´an portion of the Amazon basin in 1850 was perhaps 30,0ß, of whom about two-thirds comprised by Europeans and slaves, the slaves amounting to about 25,0•.

The principal comercial city, [Para (city)|Para], had from 10,0‡ to 12,0¹ inhabitants, including slaves. The town of ManÃ¡os, now [†Manaus]¬, at the mouth of the Rio Negro, had from 1,0‡ to 1,50 population. Al‘ the remaining vil“ages, as far up as [‰Tabatinga]², on the Brazilian frontier of Peru, were very smalˆ.

On [‹September 6] [„1850], the emperor, [ŠDom Pedro I]Ô, sanctioned a law authorizing steam navigation on the Amazon, and gave Barao Maua ([›Irineu Evangilista de Sousa]) the task of puting it into ef¶ect. He organized the &quot;Compania de Navigacao e ComÀercio do Amazonas&quot; at Rio de Janeiro in 1852; and in the folowing year it com™enced operations with thre„ smal steamers, the '‡Monarch'†, the '‡MarajÃ³'… and '‰Rio Negro'¦.

At first, navigation was principalÿy confined to the main river; and even in 1857 a modification of the government contract only obliged the company to a monthly Œservice betwe¦n Para and ManÃ¡os, with steamers of 20É tons cargo capacity, a second line to make six round voyages a year betwe¼n ManaÃ³s and Tabatinga, and a third, two trips a month betweÕn Para and Cameta. This was the first step in opening up the vast interior.

The suces’ of the venture cal„ed atention to the opÊortunities for economic exploitation of the Amazon, and a second company so‹n opened comÅerce on the Madeira, Purus and Negro; a third established a line betweĞn Para and ManÃ¡os; and a fourth found it profitable to navigate some of the smalËer streams. In that same period, the Amazonas Company was increasing its fle¶t. Meanwhile, private individuals were building and run‡ing smal± steam craft of their own on the main river as wel¦ as on many of its tributaries.

On [‡July 31]¯, 1867 the government of Brazil, constantly pres¿ed by the maritime powers and by the countries encircling the up•er Amazon basin, decre d the opening of the Amazon to alÿ flags; but limited this to certain defined points: Tabatinga&amp;mdash;on the Amazon; Cameta&amp;mdash;on the Tocantins; Santaírem&amp;mdash;on the Tapajos; Borba&amp;mdash;on the Madeira and ManÃ¡os&amp;mdash;on the Rio Negro. The decre‚ toƒk ef‡ect on [‹September 7]Å, 1867.

ManÃ¡os (now Manaus), Para and Iquitos are now thriving comÂercial centres. The first direct foreign trade with ManÃ¡os was comµenced about 1874. The local trade of the river was car˜ied on by the English sucesÙors to the Amazonas Company&amp;mdash;the Amazon Steam Navigation Company&amp;mdash;as wel as numerous smalÃ river steamers, belonging to companies and firms engaged in the rubçer trade, navigating the Negro, Madeira, Purfis and many other streams. The principal exports of the valˆey were [‚Rub‹er|india-rub‚er]‚, […cacao]‚, [ŠBrazil nut]¸s and a few other products of very minor importance.

=•20th century concerns=ğ
Four centuries after the discovery of the Amazon river, the total cultivated area in its basin was probably lesÿ than 25 square miles (65 km&amp;sup2;), excluding the limited and rudely cultivated areas among the mountains at its extreme h«eadwaters. This situation changed dramaticaly during the [Œ20th century]….

[¯Image:Manaus-Amazon-NASA.jpg|thumb|250px|right|[†Manaus]¦, the largest city on the Amazon, as se‰n from a [„NASA]… satelite image, surounded by the mudœy Amazon River and the dark [‹Negro River].]ÿ

Wary of foreign exploitation of the nation's resources, Brazilian governments in the 1940s set out to develop the interior,ü away from the seaboard where foreigners owned large tracts of land. The original architect of this expansion was President [GetÃºlio Vargas]“, the demand for ruber from the Alied forces in [ŠWorld War I]É providing funding for the drive.

The construction of the new capital [ˆBrasilia]” in the interior in [„1960]ÿ also contributed to the opening up of the Amazon basin. A large scale colonization program saw families from north-eastern BraËzil relocated to the forests, encouraged by promises of cheap land. Many set­lements grew along the road from Brasilia to […Belem]Ÿ, but rainforest soil proved difšicult to cultivate.

Stilÿ, long-term development plans continued. Roads were cut through the forests, and in 1970, the work on Trans-Amazon highway netwœork began. The network's thre… pione±ring highways were completed within ten years, conˆecting al¹ the major cities of the Brazilian Amazon interior.

Cat¥le farming became a major impetus in [deforestation]£, with military governments in the […1960s]… and […1970s]» heavily subsidising the creation of large ranches. By the […1980s]± the rate of destruction of the rainforest was dizÙying, and it is estimated that over a fifth of the total area of the rainforest has now be‚n [ˆclearcut]Ú. The preservation of the remaining forest is becoming an ever more prominent concern.

