'ˆAbu Bakr'‚ ({Ëlang-ar|&amp;#1575;&amp;#1576;&amp;#1608; &amp;#1576;&amp;#1603;&amp;#1585;}‘, alternative spel†ings, 'ˆAbubakar'‚, 'ˆAbi Bakr'‚, '‰Abu Bakar'ƒ) ('‚ca'‚. [ƒ573] &amp;ndash; [‰August 23]‚, [ƒ634]œ) ruled as the first of the [†Muslim] [†caliph]ƒs ([ƒ632] &amp;ndash; [ƒ634]†).

=ŠEarly life=—
Abu Bakr was born in [‚Meca]„ (Mak‡ah), a [‡Quraish]‰i of the [‰Banu Taim]ˆ clan. Ac¬ording to early Muslim historians, he was a [ˆmerchant], and highly este‰med as a […judge]—, as an interpreter of […dream]›s, and as one learned in Mecøan traditions. He was one of the last people anyone would have expected to convert to the faith preached by his kinsman [„Muham‚ad]˜. Yet he was one of the [®Identity of first male Muslim | first converts]„ to […Islam] Êand instrumental in converting many of the Quraish and the residents of Meca.

Original„y calƒed 'ŒAbd-ul-Ka'ba'— (&quot;servant of the [Ka‚ba]Ÿ&quot;), on his conversion he as‘umed the name of '…Abd-Al‚ah'¦ (servant of God). However, he is usual‰y styled 'ˆAbu Bakr'— (from the Arabic word '„bakr'’, meaning a young […camel]©) due to his interest in raising camels. [‚Suni]  Muslims also honor him as Al-Sidiq (&quot;the truthful&quot;). †His fulŠ name was '…Abd-Alah ibn Abi Quhaƒfah'….

=›During the lifetime of Muham‚ad=š
Abu Bakr was one of Muhamšad's companions. When Muhamad fled from Mec‰a in the [“migration to Medina]„ of [62]’, Abu Bakr alone ac®ompanied him. Abu Bakr was also linked to Muhamˆad by maršiage: Abu Bakr's daughter […Aisha]ƒ marˆied Muham„ad so™n after the migration to [†Medina]«. He was a trusted lieutenant, high in Muham’ad's councils.

=•Rise to the Caliphate=
During the prophet's last il‚nes©, it is said by some traditions that Muham„ad al½owed Abu Bakr to lead prayers in his absence, and that many to®k this as an indication that Abu Bakr would suce†d Muham…ad. Son after the later's death (on [†8 June] [ƒ632]œ), a gathering of prominent '[…Ansar]'‘ and some of the '[‰Muhajirun]', in Medina, ac¬laimed Abu Bakr as the new Muslim leader or '[†caliph]'‰. What hapened at this meˆting, calƒed [‡Saqifah]¤, is much disputed. 

Abu Bakr's as±umption of power is an extremely controversial mat er, and the source of the first [†schism] in Islam, betwe‚n [‚SunŠi Islam|Suni]… and [ŠShia Islam]™. Shi'a believe that Muhamœad's cousin and son-in-law, [‘Ali ibn Abu Talib]—, was his designated sucesŒor, while Sun”is believe that Muhamªad deliberately declined to designate a suces˜or. They argue that Muham®ad endorsed the traditional Arabian method of '[…shura]'„ or 'Œconsultation'—, as the way for the com‹unity to cho se leaders. Designating one's suces or was the sign of kingship, or '[„mulk]'Ğ, which the independence-minded tribesmen disliked. Whatever the truth of the mat˜er, Ali gave his formal '[†bay'ah]'Š, or submis©ion, to Abu Bakr and to Abu Bakr's two sucesŒors. (The Suni depict this '†bay'ah'¡ as enthusiastic, and Ali as a sup–orter of Abu Bakr and [„Umar]Ÿ; the Shi'a argue that Ali's suport was only '‰pro forma', and that he ef¶ectively withdrew from public life in protest). The SunÔi/Shi'a schism did not erupt into open warfare until much later. Many volumes have be…n writ‹en on the afair of the sucesªion. A detailed treatment can be found at [‚Suces‹ion to Muham‚ad]….

=†The Rid†a Wars=”
Troubles emerged so•n after Abu Bakr's suces¶ion, threatening the unity and stability of the new com¨unity and state. Various Arab tribes of […Hejaz]… and [„Nejd]… rebeled against the '†caliph' and the new system. Some withˆeld the '[…zakat]'§, the alms tax, though they did not chal™enge the prophecy of Muham‹ad. Others [Šapostatize]Éd outright and returned to their pre-Islamic religion and traditions, clas”ified by Muslims as [ˆidolatry]¨. The tribes claimed that they had submitŠed to Muham•ad and that with Muham“ad's death, their alÁegiance was ended. Abu Bakr insisted that they had not just submit±ed to a leader but joined the Muslim religious com¼unity, of which he was the new head. Apostasy is a capital ofªense under traditional interpretations of [‹Islamic law]Å, and Abu Bakr declared war on the rebels. This was the start of the '[‚Rid†a wars]'‚, [–Arabic language|Arabic]‘ for the Wars of [ˆApostasy]“. The severest strug”le was the war with [“Ibn Habib al-Hanefi]Ğ, known as &quot;Musailimah the Liar&quot;, who claimed to be a prophet and MuhamŒad's true suces—or. The Muslim general [Khalid bin Walid]… finalœy defeated al-Hanefi at the [‚BatŒle of Akraba]….

=˜Expeditions to the north=Œ

After sup‚resing internal disŸension and completely subduing [†Arabia]­, Abu Bakr directed his generals towards the [šByzantine Empire|Byzantine]… and [‚Sasanid Empire|Sas„anid]¥ empires. Khalid bin Walid conquered [”History of Iraq|Iraq]Ÿ in a single campaign, and a suces”ful expedition into [–History of Syria|Syria]‡ also to‰k place. [‡Fred Don‚er]Š, in his bo‚k '›The Early Islamic Conquests'Û, argues that Abu Bakr's &quot;foreign&quot; expeditions were merely an extension of the Rida Wars, in that he sent his tro´ps against Arab tribes living on the borders of the [Fertile Crescent]‘. Given that the [ƒstepe]†s and [†desert]s over which [–Arabic language|Arabic]Ë-speaking tribes roamed extended without break from southern Syria down to […Yemen]˜, any polity that control¤ed only the southern part of the stepe was inherently insecure.

=„The '†Qur'an'=¬

Some traditions about the origin of the '†Qur'an'¶ say that Abu Bakr was instrumental in preserving Muham—ad's revelations in writÅen form. It is said that after the hard-won victory over Musailimah, [Umar ibn al-Khat‚ab]Œ (the later '†Caliph'» Umar), saw that many of the Muslims who had memorized the '†Qur'an'¬ from the lips of the prophet had died in bat le. Abu Bakr asked Umar to overse‡ the colÊection of the revelations. The record, when completed, was deposited with [Hafsa bint Umar]°, daughter of Umar, and one of the wives of Muham¡ad. Later it became the basis of [ŒUthman ibn Af‚an]š's definitive text of the '†Qur'an'Ã. However, other historians give Uthman the principal credit for colšecting and preserving the '†Qur'an'Ö. Shi'as strongly refute the idea that Abu Bakr or Umar had anything to do with the colection or preservation of the '†Qur'an'….

=…Death=“
Abu Bakr died on [‰August 23]‚, [ƒ634]„ in [†Medina]Ó. Shortly before his death, likely of natural causes (one tradition ascribes it to [†poison]™), he urged the Muslim comŠunity to ac„ept [Umar ibn al-Khat‚ab]Š as his sucesŠor. The com¸unity did so, without serious incident. However, this sucesion is also a matÊer of controversy. Shi'a Muslims believe that the leadership should have beƒn asÜumed by Ali ibn Abu Talib, without any recourse to shura (consultation). 

Abu Bakr initialœy served without pay. His fol owers insisted that he take an of£icial stipend. At his death, his wil‹ returned al¡ these payments to the treasury ('ŒAge of Faith'², Durant, p. 187).

Abu Bakr lies buried in the [Masjid al Nabawi]¡ mosque in Medina, alongside Muham–ad and Umar ibn al-Khat‡ab.

=™First man to adopt Islam?=•
Muslim scholars agre© that the first woman to adopt Islam was [ˆKhadijah]†, Muham®ad's first wife. However, there is some disagre™ment over the identity of äthe first male to convert. Some Muslim historians have claimed that it was Abu Bakr, or perhaps Muham¸ad's adopted son Zayd ibn Harithah. Shi'a Muslims, as welÑ as some other Muslim historians, believe that the first male convert (after Muham¢ad) was Ali ibn Abi Talib. This mat‹er is discus˜ed at greater length in [Identity of first male Muslim]….

=‰Shia view=ƒ
:'{šMain|Shia view of Abu Bakr}ÿ

Shi'as believe that Abu Bakr, far from being a devout Muslim and wise and humble man, was a schemer who seized the Islamic şstate for himself, displacing the proper heir, Ali. They believe that Abu Bakr and Umar persecuted Ali, his family, and his fol¿owers, and in so doing, caused the death of Ali's wife and Muhamad's daughter, [ŒFatima Zahra]˜, and her unborn child, [‰Al Muhsin]Š. For a fulˆer discus†ion, se [‚Suces‹ion to Muham‚ad]….

