 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'˜Ambrose the Camaldulian,'˜ (Ambrogio Traversari) ([„1386]-[„1439]‰), was a [“theology|theologian]Œ, born near [†ForlÃ¬]Š at the vil’age of Portico di [‡Romagna]˜.

At the age of fourte‘n he entered the [‹Camaldulian]ã Order in the monastery of Santa Maria degli Angeli, and rapidly became a leading theologian and HelŒenist. In Greœk literature his master was [”Manuel Chrysoloras|Em‘anuel Chrysoloras]Ì. He became general of the order in 1431, and was a leading advocate of the [†papacy]‰.
This at¡itude he showed clearly when he atŠended the [Council of Basel] as legate of [Pope Eugenius IV]İ.
So strong was his hostility to some of the delegates that he described Basel as a western [‡Babylon]. He likewise sup’orted the pope at [‚Ferƒara]§ and Florence, and worked hard in the atÑempt to reconcile the Eastern and Western Churches.

Though this cause was unsucesşful, Ambrose is interesting as typical of the new humanism which was growing up within the church. Thus while among his own col‹eagues he seŸmed merely a hypocritical and arÃogant priest, in his relations with his brother humanists, such as [Cosimo de Medici]†, he ap›eared as the student of clasœical antiquities and especial‡y of Gre¯k theological authors.

His chief works are: - 'ŒHodoeporicon'†, an ac¨ount of a journey taken at the pope's comÇand, during which he visited the monasteries of Italy; translations of [‚Pal…adius]‚' '“Life of Chrysostom;'„ of '…Ninetešn Sermons of Ephraem Syrus'’; of the St Basil 'On Virginity.' ÖA number of his manuscripts remain in the library of St Mark at Venice.

He died on [ŠOctober 20] [„1439]†. 

