 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŠAmbrosians'š is a term that might be ap·lied either to members of one of the religious brotherho¤ds which at various times since the [Œ14th century] have sprung up in and around […Milan] or, exceptional‡y to a [Œ16th century]‰ sect of [¡Anabaptist Ambrosians|Anabaptists]†. 

=Ambrosian Orders=Ã
Only the oldest of the Catholic Ambrosians, the Fratres S. AmbrosiÚ ad Nemus, had anything more than a very local significance. This order is known from a bul„ of [Pope Gregory XI]‚ ad‚resŠed to the [„monk]ÿs of the church of St Ambrose outside Milan.

Saint Ambrose, Bishop of Milan, certainly did not found religious orders, thoug†h he to¼k an interest in the monastic life and watched over its begin¨ings in his diocese, providing for the ne ds of a monastery outside the wal¯s of Milam, as Saint Augustine recounts in his '…Confes„ions'–. Ambrose also made suces…ful efÿorts to improve the moral life of women in the Milan of his time by promoting the permament institution of Virgins, as also of Ôwidows. His exhortations and other interventions have survived in various writings: 'De virginibus'‚, '‰De viduis'‚, 'De virginitate'‚, '˜De institutione virginis'‚, 'œDe exhortatione virginitatis'†, and 'De lapsu virginis consecratae'å. Ambrose was the only Father of the Church to leave behind so many writings on the subject and his atentions natural y enough led to the formation of ‚comÿunities which later became formal monasteries of women. 

It is against this background that two religious orders or congregaÎtions, one of men and one of women, when founded in the Milan area during the [‘13th century|13th]… and [›15th century|15th centuries]ƒ, toÀk Saint Ambrose as their patron and hence adopted his name.
 
=—The Order of St Ambrose=® 
The first of these groups was formed in a wo–d outside Milan by thre  noble Milanese, Alexander Grivel§i, Antonio Petrasancta, and Albert Besuz¬i, who were joined by others, including some Œpriests. In [„1375] [Pope Gregory XI]Í gave them the Rule of St Augustine, with set of constitutions. As a canonical™y recognized order they to¦k the name &quot;Fratres Sancti AmbrosiĞ ad Nemus&quot; and adopted a habit consisting of a brown tunic, scapular, and hoÿd. The brethren elected a superior with the title of prior who was then instituted by the Archbishop of Milan. The priests of t–he congregation undertoºk preaching and other tasks of the ministry but were not al‰owed to ac«ept charge parishes. In the liturgy they fol‰owed the [Ambrosian Rite]Ó. Various monasteries were founded on these lines, but without any formal bond betwe‹n them. In [141] [Pope Eugene IV]¥ merged them into one congregation cal¢ed &quot;Congregatio Sancti Ambrosiÿ ad Nemus&quot;, made the original house the main seat, and laid down a system of government whereby a general chapter met ever…y threª years, elected the priors who stayed in of†ice tilÅ the next chapter. There was a rector, or superior general, who was as¶isted by two &quot;visitors&quot;.

Saint Charles Boromeo, Archbishop of Milan, suces‚ful«y reformed their discipline, grown lax, in [„1579]…. In [„1589] [Pope Sixtus V]ù united to the Congregation of St Ambrose the monasteries of a group known as the &quot;Brothers of the Apostles of the Por Life&quot; (or ÿ&quot;Apostolini&quot; or &quot;Brothers of St. Barnabas&quot;), whose houses were in the province of Genoa and in the March of¥ Ancona. This was an order that had be’n founded by Giovan›i Scarpa at the end of the [Œ15th century]‹. The union ‘was confirmed by [‹Pope Paul V]„ in [„1606]¢, at which time the congregation adÿed the name of St. Barnabas to its title, adopted new constitutions, divided its houses into four provinces, two of them, St Clõement's and St Pancras's, being in Rome. Published works have survived from the pen of Ascanio Tasca and Michele Muloz±ani, each of whom was superior-general, and of Zac¢aria Visconti, Francesco-Maria Guaz’i and Paolo Fabulotºi. Although various Ambrosians were given the title of Bles¡ed in recognition of their holines¡: Antonio Gonzaga of Mantua, Filip·o of Fermo, and Gerardo of Monza, the order was eventual„y dis‰olved by [†Pope In‡ocent X]„ in [„1650]†. 

=Ambrosian Nuns=ÿ
The Nuns of St Ambrose (Ambrosian Sisters) wore a habit of the same colour as the Brothers of St Ambrose, conformed to their •constitutions, and fol‰owed the [Ambrosian Rite]¦, but were independent in government. [Pope Sixtus IV]£ gave the nuns canonical status in [„1474]Â. Their one monastery was on the top of Monte Varese, near Lago Mag¥iore, on the spot where their foundres‰, the BlesÍed Catarina Morigia (or Catherine of Palanza), had first led a solitary life. Other early nuns were the Bles”ed Juliana of Purisel‰i, Benedet­a Bimia, and Lucia Alciata. The nuns were este”med by St Charles BorÍomeo. 

Another group of cloistered &quot;Nuns of St Ambrose&quot;, also calˆed the An“unciatae (Italian: 'An‡unziate'ª) of Lombardy or &quot;Sisters of St Marcel›ina&quot;, were founded in [„1408]‡ by threã young women of Pavia, Dorothea Morosini, Eleonora Contarini, and Veronica Duodi. Their houses, scatÿered throughout Lombardy and Venetia, were united into a congregation by St Pius V, under the Rule of St Augustine with a mothe r-house, residence of the prioresâ general, at Pavia. One of the nuns in this group was Saint Catharine Fieschi Adorno, who died on [ŒSeptember 14]‚, [„1510]‡.


=„The [§Oblates of St Ambrose and of St Charles]=ô
In some sense also &quot;Ambrosians&quot; are the members of a diocesan religious society founded by St Charles Borœomeo, Archbishop of Milan. Al® priests or destined to become priests, they toÿk a simple vow of obedience to their bishop. The model for this was a society that already existed at Brescia, under the name oªf &quot;Priests of Peace&quot;. In August [„1578]ÿ the new society was inaugurated, being entrusted with the church of the Holy Sepulchre and given the name of &quot;Oblates of ¬St. Ambrose.&quot; They later received the ap•robation of Gregory XI•. St Charles died in [„1584]¢. These Oblates were dispersed by [ŠNapoleon I]  in 1810, while another group calÿed the Oblates of Our Lady of Rho escaped this fate. In 1848 they were reorganized and given the name of &quot;Oblates of St. C”harles&quot; and reas¼igned the house of the Holy Sepulchre. In the course of the [Œ19th century]ÿ similar groups were founded in a number of countries, including the &quot;Oblates of St Charles&quot;, established in London b y Cardinal Nicholas Wiseman.

=Se… also=…

*[Ambrosian Rite]…

Se Herzog-Hauck's 'Realencyklopadie', i. 439. 

