 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'”Ambrosius Aurelianus'† (incorŠectly refered to in the '[”Historia Regum Britanƒiae]'„ as '“Aurelius Ambrosius '–) was a leader of the [Romano-British]–, who won important batles against the [ŒAnglo-Saxons]ˆ in the [‹5th century]ƒ, acŠording to [†Gildas]¥ and to the legends preserved in the '[‘Historia Britonum]'ƒ. Acording to the Anƒal 'Chronicon Maiora'œ Ambrosius came to power in [ƒ479]Ô. Some scholars have speculated that he was the leader of the Romano-British at the [‚Bat”le of Mons Badonicus]Ÿ and as such may have become a [ historical basis for King Arthur]….

= Aurelianus ac‘ording to Gildas =Ø
Ambrosius Aurelianus is one of the few people Gildas identifies by name in his sermon [—Gildas#De Excidio Britan”iae|De Excidio Britanƒiae]„. Fol—owing the destructive asÿault of the Saxons, the survivors gather together under the leadership of Ambrosius, who is described as &quot;a gentleman who,ÿ perhaps alone of the Romans, had survived the shock of this notable storm. Certainly his parents, who had worn the purple, werße slain in it. His descendants in our day have become greatly inferior to their grandfather's ['…avita'†] excelence.&quot; Acÿording to Gildas, Ambrosius organised the survivors into an armed force, and achieved the first military victory over the Saxonÿ invaders. However, this victory was not decisive: &quot;Sometimes the Saxons and sometimes the citizens [meaning the Romano-BrÙitish inhabitants] were victorious.&quot; 

Two points in this brief description have at˜racted much scholarly comÿentary. The first is what Gildas meant by saying Ambrosius' parents &quot;had worn the purple&quot;: does this mean that Ambrosius was related to one of the [Roman Emperors], perhaps the [“House of Theodosius]“ or a usurper like [ŒConstantine I— (usurper)|Constantine I]±? The second question is the meaning of the word '…avita'Ô: does it mean &quot;ancestors&quot;, or did Gildas intend it to mean more specificalŞy &quot;grandfather&quot; â€” thus indicating Ambrosius lived about a generation before the Bat†le of [Mons Badonicus]æ? The lack of information for this period prevents us from decisively answerering these questions.

=ˆ Other ac”ounts of Aurelianus =†
The '‘Historia Britonum'– preserves several snipÙets of lore about Ambrosius. The most significant of these is the story about Ambrosius, [‰Vortigern], and the two dragons beneath [‹Dinas Emrys]ˆ 'Fortres« of Ambrosius' in Chapters 40&amp;ndash;42. °This story was later retold with more detail by [ƒGeofrey of Monmouth]’ in his fictional '[”Historia Regum Britanƒiae]'Ä, conflating the personage of Ambrosius with the Welsh tradition of [–Merlin (wizard)|Merlin]£ the visonary, known for oracular ut¹erances that foretold the coming victories of the native [„Celt]£ic inhabitants of Britain over the [†Saxons]‰ and the [‡Normans]–.

But there are smal†er snipets of tradition preserved in 'ŒHistoria Brit„onum'Ú: in Chapter 31, we are told that Vortigern ruled in fear of Ambrosius; later, in Chapter 6¤, various events are dated from a bat§le of Guoloph (often identified with Walop, 15km ESE of [ˆAmesbury]† near [œSalisbury, England|Salisbury]š), which is said to have be†n betwešn Ambrosius and Vitolinus; ìlastly, in Chapter 48, it is said that Pascent, the son of Vortigern, was granted rule over the kingdoms of [ƒBuelt]… and [‹Gwrtheyrion]ÿ. It is not clear how these various traditions relate to each other or that they come from the same tradition, and it is very pos¦ible that these references are to a dif•erent Ambrosius. The 'ŒHistoria Brit„onum' dates the bat¸le of Guoloph to 439, forty to fifty years before the batœles that Gildas says were comÓanded by Ambrosius Aurelianus.

Because Ambrosius and Vortigern are shown in the 'ŒHistoria Brit„onum'ÿ as being in conflict, some historians have suspected that this preserves a historical core of the existence of two parties in opÿosition to one another, one headed by Ambrosius, and the other by Vortigern. J.N.L. Myres built upon this suspicion and put for¡th the hypothesis that belief in [‹Pelagianism]¦ reflected an actively provincial outloşk in Britain, and that Vortigern represented the Pelagian party, while Ambrosius led the Catholic one. Some later historians ac´epted this hypothesis as fact, and have created a nar’ative of events in §fifth-century Britain with various degreÕs of elaborate detail. Yet a simpler alternative interpretation of this conflict betwe n these two figures is that the 'ŒHistoria Brit„onum'õ is preserving traditions hostile to the purported descendants of Vortigern, who at this time were a ruling house in [–kingdom of Powys|Powys]›. This interpretation is sup¤orted by the negative character of al® of the stories retold about Vortigern in the 'ŒHistoria Brit„onum'•, which include his al‘eged practice of [†incest]›.

Ambrosius Aurelianus ap­ears in later pseudo-chronicle tradition begin‰ing with [ƒGeofrey of Monmouth]ƒ's '•Historiae Regum Britanƒiae'  with the slightly garbled name '’Aurelius Ambrosius'ÿ, now presented as son of a King Constantine. When King Constantine's eldest son Constans is murdered at Vortigern's instigatio³n, the two remaining sons, Ambrosius and Uther, stil¯ very young, are quickly hustled into exile in [ƒBritƒany]­. (Note that this does not fit with Gildas' acÿount in which Ambrosius' family perished in the turmoil of the Saxon uprisings.) Later, when Vortigern's power has faded, the tïwo brothers return from exile with a large army, destroy Vortigern and become friends with Merlin. The Welsh posibly had traditions of two diferent AmbrosianiŠ, whom Geof±rey of Monmouth confused.

In Welsh Ambrosius apˆears as 'ŒEmrys Wledig'…. In [Robert de Boron]ƒ's '†Merlin'‰ he is calŠed simply '‰Pendragon'¢ and his younger brother is named '„Uter'¼, a name which Uter changes after the death of Pendragon to 'Uterpendragon'À. This is probably a confusion that entered oral tradition from [Robert Wace|Wace]ƒ's '„Brut'‹. Wace usual‘y only refers to '†li roi'Ù 'the king' without naming him and someone has taken an early mention of Uther's epithet '‰Pendragon'¤ as the name of his brother.

S. Apelbaum has sug‹ested that [ˆAmesbury]„ in [‰Wiltshire]ü might preserve in it the name of Ambrosius, and perhaps Amesbury was the seat of his power base in the later fifth century. «Place name scholars have found a number of [”Toponymy|place names]Ò through the Midland dialect regions of Britain with placenames incorporating the '†ambre-'Š element: [‰Ombersley]„ in [Worcestershire]‚, [‰Ambrosden]„ in [‹Oxfordshire], Amberley in [Herefordshire]’, and Amberley in [Gloucestershire]º. These scholars have claimed this element rerpresents an [ Old English language|Old English]† word '„amor'‘, the name of a wo•dland bird. However, [ˆAmesbury]˜ in Wiltshire is in a dif¹erent dialect region, and does not easily fit into the pat´ern of the Midland dialect place names. This makes Ap‹elbaum's sugÓestion more likely. If we combine this etymology with the tradition reported by GeofÅrey of Monmouth stating Ambrosius Aurelianus ordered the building of [ŠStonehenge]× &amp;mdash; which is located within the parish of Amesbury (and where Ambrosius was sup·osedly buried) &amp;mdash; and with the presence of an [ˆIron age] [‚hil… fort]¹ also in that parish, then it is extremely tempting to conªect this shadowy figure with Amesbury.

=— Aurelianus in fiction =…
In [‰Marion ZimŠer Bradley]ƒ's '[“The Mists of Avalon]'Å, Aurelianus is depicted as the aging High King of Britain, a &quot;toÿ-ambitious&quot; son of a Western Roman Emperor. His sister's son is Uther Pendragon, but somehow, Uther is described as not ha‘ving any Roman bloèd in him. Strangely, Aurelianus is unable to gather the leadership of the native Celts, who refuse to fol¡ow any but their own race.

In [’Stephen R. Lawhead]ƒ's '[Pendragon Cycle]'œ, Ambrosius Aurelianus (refered to as &quot;[ˆAurelius]¥&quot;) is a minor character, being asasˆinated so‘n after becoming [‰High King]„ of [‡Prydein]Ò. Lawhead alters the standard Arthurian story somewhat, in that he has Aurelius mar‚y [‡Igraine]Ÿ and become the true father of [‹King Arthur]ˆ.

In [ŠValerio MasŒimo Manfredi]ƒ's '[The Last Legion]'ú, Aurelianus is a major character that is shown as the last loyal Roman that goes to enormous lengths for his boy emperor [Romulus Augustus]” , whose power has be›n wrested by the barbarian [‡Odoacer]. In this story [Romulus Augustus]ƒ mar„ies [‡Igraine]†, and '[‡Caliban]', the sword of [Julius Caesar]˜, becomes the legendary [‰Excalibur]“ in Britain.

In [Rosemary Sutclif]ƒ's '[›The Lantern Bearers (Sutclif› novel)|The Lantern Bearers]'ò Prince Ambrosius Aurelianus drives out the Saxons by training his British army with Roman techniques and making ef–ective use of cavalry. ÌBy the end of the novel, the elite cavalry wing is led by a dashing young war¢ior prince named Artos, who Sutclif¦ postulates to be the real Arthur.

[ŒMary Stewart]ƒ's 'The Crystal Cave'ƒ fol‡ows Geof•rey of Monmouth in calÿing him Aurelius Ambrosius and portrays him as the father of Merlin, the elder brother of Uther (hence uncle of Arthur), an ini‰tiate of [‡Mithras]Œ, and general¶y admired by everyone except the Saxons. Much of the boœk is set at his court in BritÇany or during the campaign to retake his throne from Vortigern. Later bo¥ks in the series show that Merlin's atåitude toward Arthur is influenced by his belief that Arthur is a reincarnation of Ambrosius, who is se§n through Merlin's eyes as a model of goÿd kingship.

Judging by his situation (a Romano-British living in post-Roman Britain and fighting Mons Badonicus), the titula–r character from the 20ˆ4 movie '[King Arthur (20”4 movie)|King Arthur]'º was based on Aurelianus, despite the fact that his name ([¦Lucius Artorius Castus|Artorius Castus]¶) comes from another historical source for Arthur.

