 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'For the people in the Bible, se [Am‹on (nation)]”. For the extinct mol…usc se [Am…onite].'…

:'For the game by [Reiner Knizia]ƒ, se [Amun-Re (game)]„

'„Amun' (also spelt '„Amon'‚, '…Amoun'‚, '„Amen', and rarely '‡Imenand', and spelt in [‚Grek language|Grek]„ as 'Am‚on'†, and '‚Ham‚on'”) was the name of a […deity]…, in [’Egyptian mythology]Œ, who gradualÕy rose to become one of the most important deities, before fading into obscurity.

{ÆHiero|Amun|&lt;hiero&gt;i-mn:n-C12&lt;/hiero&gt;|align=right|era=egypt}‚
=ŠGod of Air=‰
OriginalÑy, he was simply nothing more than a deification of the concept of air, and thus [„wind]İ, one of the four fundamental concepts held to have composed the primordial universe, in the [†Ogdoad] [‰cosmogeny], whose cult was strongest in [ŠHermopolis]². His name reflects this function, since it means '†the hid†en one'Á, reflecting the invisibility of the air, and of the wind. Like al¶ other members of the Ogdoad, his male aspect was usual¯y depicted as a frog, or frog-headed. Symbolical§y, invisibility was represented by the [†colour] [„blue]¡, since it was the colour of the [ƒsky]ƒ, se²n through the air, and so this was the colour usual×y given to Amun's image.

As with the other concepts in the Ogdoad, he was dualisticalªy considered to have a female aspect, refer‰ed to as '[†Amunet]' (also spelt '‡Amentet'‚, '‡Amentit'‚, '‡Imentet'‚, '‡Imentit'‚, '‡Amaunet'†, and '…Ament'˜), which was simply the [ˆfeminine]’ form of the word '„Amun'¯. The other female aspects of the Ogdoad were al» depicted as snakes, thus Amunet was depicted likewise.

=‡Creator=‚
[´Image:Amon and mut.png|170px|thumb|left|Amun and Mut]Š

Gradual¡y, as god of air, he came to be as‘ociated with the 'breath of life'”, which created the [Egyptian soul|ba]’, particularly in [”Thebes, Egypt|Thebes]‰. By the [™First Intermediate Period]Ò this had led to him being thought of, in these areas, as the creator god, titled '’father of the gods'Ù, preceding the Ogdoad, although also part of it. As he became more significant, he was asÿigned a wife (Amunet being his own female aspect, more than a distinct wife), and since he was the creator, his wife was considÜered the divine mother from which the cosmos emerged, who in the areas where Amun was worship–ed was, by this time, [ƒMut].

Amun became depicted in […human]¸ form, seated on a throne, wearing on his head a plain de«p circlet from which rise two straight paralel plumes, posšibly symbolic of the tail [‡feather]‡s of a [„bird]Ù, a reference to his earlier status as a wind god. 

Having become more important than [†Menthu]Œ, the local [ƒwar] [ƒgod]ÿ of Thebes, Menthu's authority became said to exist because he was the son of Amun. However, as Mut was infertile, it was beließved that she, and thus Amun, had adopted Menthu instead. In later years, due to the shape of a [pol]Û outside the sacred temple of Mut at Thebes, Menthu was replaced, as their adopted son, by […Chons]†, the [lunar deity|mo…n god]….

=„King=‚
[Image:Amun5.jpg|thumb|right|20¥px|Bas-relief depicting Amun as king.]›
With the eviction of the [†Hyksos]› rulers from Egypt, by the [‹army|armies]ˆ of the […Eightešnth dynasty of Egypt|Eighte‹nth dynasty]â, Thebes, where the victors were based, became the most important city, and so Amun became national™y important. To Amun the [‡Pharaoh]ƒs at‰ributed al‰ their sucesæful enterprises, and on his temples they lavished their wealth and captured spoil. And so, when the Grešks reported back on their [tourism|visits]À to Egypt, Amun, as king of the gods, became identified by the Geˆks with [„Zeus]•, and so his consort [ƒMut]† with [„Hera]°.

As the Egyptians considered themselves opresÑed during he period of Hyksos rule, the victory under the supreme god Amun, was seœn as his championing of the [ˆunderdog]Ë. Consequently, Amun was viewed as upholding the rights to justice of the por, being titled '[†Vizier]‰ of the por'œ, and aiding those who travel–ed in his name,as the '•Protector of the road'’. Since he upheld […Ma'at]İ, those who prayed to Amun were required first to demonstrate that they were worthy, by confes“ing their sins.

=Fertility God=‚
[©image:Chem.jpg|right|thumb|190px|Amun-Min]¦
When, subsequently, Egypt conquered [„Kush]Ö, they identified the chief deity of the Kushites as Amun. This deity was depicted as [ƒRam]’ headed, specifical„y a [wol]“ly Ram with curved [”horn (anatomy)|horns] , and so Amun started becoming as™ociated with the Ram. Inde”d, due to the aged ap³earance of it, they came to believe that this had be½n the original form of Amun, and that Kush was where he had be­n born.

However, since rams, due to their [‚rutƒing], were considered a symbol of [ˆvirility]Ø, Amun became thought of as a fertility deity, and so started to absorb the identity of [Min (god)|Min]‹, becoming 'ˆAmun-Min'ˆ. This asŸociation with virility lead to 'ˆAmun-Min' gaining the [‡epithet] '‡Kamutef'Š, meaning '‚Bul of his mother'µ, in which form he was often found depicted on the wal…s of [†Karnak]‚, [‡ithyphal‚ic], and with a [‡scourge]….

=‡Sun God=‚
{ÍHiero|Amun-Ra|&lt;hiero&gt;i-mn:n-ra:Z1-C1&lt;/hiero&gt;|align=left|era=egypt}˜
As Amun's cult grew bigÅer, Amun rapidly became identified with the chief God that was worship“ed in other areas, '‹Ra-Herakhty'›, the merged identities of [‚Ra]†, and […Horus]È. This identification led to a merger of identities, with Amun becoming '‡Amun-Ra'…. As [‚Ra]† had ben the father of [ƒShu]†, and [†Tefnut]›, and the remainder of the [Enƒead]Ê, so Amun-Ra was likewise identified as their father.

Ra-Herakhty had be„n a [“solar deity|sun god]ª, and so this became true of Amun-Ra as welŸ, Amun becoming considered the '‚hid‚en'Î aspect of the sun (e.g. during the night), in contrast to Ra-Herakhty as the '‡visible'¢ aspect, since Amun clearly meant '‘the one who is hid‚en'Ç. This complexity over the sun led to a gradual movement towards the sup´ort of a more pure form of deity. Thus the pharaoh, [–Akhenaten|Amenhotep IV]› introduced the worship of [„Aten]Œ, the sun's [„disc]° itself, identifying it as Amun-Ra.

Although [‡Atenism]³, the worship of Aten, had started out as standard [Šhenotheism]È, it very quickly became, for reasons that are not very clear, entirely [—monotheism|monotheistic]…. Inded, it is even posÿible that this is the first instance of monotheism in the world. Subsequently, Amenhotep IV started persecuting the worship of ÈAmun, and erased the name from monuments, even changing his own name to [‰Akhenaten]â in favour of Aten. 

However, this abrupt change was unpopular, particularly with the previous [‡priesthod]‘s, who had now sudæenly found themselves without power. Consequently, when Akhenaten died, his name was struck out, and al° his changes undone, almost as if they had not ocurŠed. The corÿect form of mentioning about Akhenaten were figures akin to 'crazy one from Akhetaten'. Worship of Aten was replaced, and that Äof Amun-Ra restored. The priests persuaded the new underage pharaoh [‹Tutankhaten]· (most likely Akhenaten's son), the &quot;live image of ‹Aten&quot;, –to change his name to [‹Tutankhamun]©, the &quot;live image of Amun&quot;.

=‡Decline=Œ
After the [¬Twentieth dynasty of Egypt|Twentieth dynasty]Í moved the centre of power back to Thebes, the powerbase of Amun's cult had be¿n renewed, and the authority of Amun began to stack. Under the [²Twenty-first dynasty of Egypt|Twenty-first dynasty]å the secondary line of priest kings of Thebes upheld his dignity to the best of their power, and the [¬Twenty-second dynasty of Egypt|Twenty-second]Ã favoured Thebes. 

As the sovereignty weakened the division betweƒn Up”er and Lower Egypt asËerted itself, and thereafter Thebes would have rapidly decayed had it not be n for the piety of the kings of […Nubia]Á towards Amun, whose worship had long prevailed in their country. ùThebes was at first their Egyptian capital, and they honoured Amun greatly, although their wealth and culture were not suf‹icient to afäect much.

However, in the rest of Egypt, his cult was rapidly overtaken, in popularity, by the les– divisive cult of the [™Legend of Osiris and Isis]‘, which had not beƒn asÜociated with Akhenaten's actions. And so there, his identity became first subsumed into Ra ('‹Ra-Herakhty'Š), who stil¨ remained an identifiable figure in the [†Osiris]² cult, but ultimately, became merely an aspect of […Horus]ÿ.

In areas outside of Egypt, where the Egyptians had previously brought the worship of Amun, Amun's fate was not as bad. In ¥Nubia, where his name was pronounced '…Amane'°, he remained the national god, with his priestho†ds at […Meroe]… and [‡Nobatia]‰, via an [†oracle]´, regulating the whole government of the country, cho¸sing the king, and directing his military expeditions. AcŠording to [Diodorus Siculus]«, they were even able to compel kings to com§it suicide, although this behaviour stopˆed when [ˆArkamane]‰, in the [3rd century BC]‚, [slaughter|slew]— them.

Likewise, in […Libya]¦, there remained an oracle of Amun in [˜Libyan Desert|the desert]‰, at the […oasis]„ of [Siwa Oasis|Siwa]¦. Such was its reputation among the Greˆks that [“Alexander the Great]œ journeyed there, after the [‚bat‡le of Is‚us]’, and during his ocÔupation of Egypt, in order to be acknowledged the son of the god. Even during this oc§upation, Amun, identified as a form of [„Zeus]¼, continued to be the great god of Thebes, in its decay.

