=Derived Terms=­

Several words derive from Amun via the Greˆk form Am„on: [am„onia]… and [am…onite]ƒ. Am‹onia, as wel¯ as being the chemical, is a genus name in the [Œforaminifera] . Both these foraminiferans (shelƒed [ˆProtozoa]‡) and am“onites (extinct shelƒed [Šcephalopod]–s) have/had spiral shel›s resembling a ram's, and AmŒon's, horns. AmÊonia the chemical derives its name in a more round-about way &amp;ndash; se end of article [am„onia]•. The regions of the [‚hip‡ocampus]ˆ in the […brain]‡ are cal‡ed the '[‡cornu am„onis]'“ &amp;ndash; literal¯y &quot;Amun's Horns&quot;, due to the horned ap§earance of the dark and light bands of [‚cel (biology) | cel„ular]Œ layers.

