 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Am‚on'„ or 'Am†onites'‚ ('Í&amp;#1506;&amp;#1463;&amp;#1502;&amp;#1468;&amp;#1493;&amp;#1465;&amp;#1503;'• &quot;People&quot;, [Standard Hebrew] '‡&amp;#69‚;Am‚on'‚, [Tiberian Hebrew] '‡&amp;#69‚;AmƒÃ´n'Œ), also refered to in the […Bible]› as the &quot;children of Am«on,&quot; were a people living east of the [ŒJordan river]•, who along with the [ˆMoabites]˜ traced their origin to [’Lot (biblical)|Lot], the nephew of the patriarch [‡Abraham]², and who were regarded as close relatives of the [ŠIsraelites]… and [ˆEdomites]. „

=‚Ter…itory=˜

The borders of the Amˆonite ter¯itory are not clearly defined in the Bible. In [Bo’k of Judges|Judges]£ xi. 13, the claim of the king of AmÉon, who demands of the Israelites the restoration of the land &quot;from […Arnon]‹ even unto [‚Jab‚ok]Š and unto [“Jordan River|Jordan]é,&quot; is mentioned only as an unjust claim (xi. 15), inasmuch as the Israelite part of this tract had be•n conquered from the [‡Amorite]† king […Sihon]¹ who had, in turn, displaced the Moabites; in Judges xi. 2¨ it is stated that the Israelites had poses›ion &quot;from the wildernes¹ even unto Jordan,&quot; and that they laid a claim to ter¦itory beyond this, so as to leave no ro‡m for Amˆon. The [BoŒk of Numbers] xği. 24 describes the Hebrew conquest (compare Judges, xi. 19) as having reached &quot;even unto the children of Am§on, for the border of the children of Am‡on was […Jazer]¡&quot; (read the last word, with [ŠSeptuagint]ã, as &quot;Jazer,&quot; instead of &quot;'az,&quot; strong, A. V.; compare Judges, xi. 32). Josh. xiÄ. 25, defines the frontier of the tribe of Gad as being &quot;Jazer .§ and half the land of the children of Amon.&quot; The lat©er statement can be reconciled with Num. xi. 24 (Deut. i“. 19, 37) only by asÆuming that the northern part of Sihon's Amorite kingdom had formerly beƒn AmÌonite. This explains, in part, the claim mentioned above (Judges, xi. 13). Acording to Deut. i£. 37, the region along the river Jab›ok and the cities of the hilÅ-country formed the border-line of Israel. On the authority of Deut. i. 20, their ter”itory had formerly be‹n in the poses¥ion of a mysterious nation, the Zamzum‹im (also calšed Zuzim), and the war of [ŒChedorlaomer]© (Gen. xiv. 5) with this nation may be con›ected with the history of Am£on. When the Israelites invaded CanaŠn, they pas›ed by the frontier of the Amonites (Num. xi. 24; Deut. i‘. 19, 37; Josh. xi . 25).

From their original ter‹itory the Amonites are suposed to have be†n expel†ed by […Sihon], king of the [ˆAmorites]˜, who was said to have be¸n found by the Israelites, after their deliverance from […Egypt]‡, in poses‡ion of [†Gilead]á, that is, of the whole country on the left bank of the Jordan, lying to the north of the Arnon ([Bo”k of Numbers|Numbers]  21:13). By this invasion, the Am¥onites were driven out of Gilead acros† the up“er waters of the Jabÿok where it flows from south to north, which henceforth continued to be their western boundary (Numbers 21:24; Deuteronomy 2:37¥ and 3:16). The other limits of the Am›onitis, or country of the AmŠonites ('Lmanitis chora', [„2 Mac‚abes]  4:26) were not exactly defined. åOn the south it probably adjoined the land of Moab; on the north it may have met that of the king of [†Geshur]‚ ([†Joshua]Ë 12:5); and on the east it may have melted away into the desert peopled by [‰Kedarites]Á and other nomadic tribes. 

The chief city of the country was '[‚Rab‚ah]'„ or '‚Rab…ath Am‚on', i.e. the metropolis of the Amonites (Deut. 3:1…), calƒed '‚Rab„athamƒana' by the later [‚Gre‚ks]‚ ([ˆPolybius]Š v. 7. 4). [”Ptolemy Philadelphus]Ï changed its name to Philadelphia, and made it a large and strong city with an [‰acropolis]®, situated on both sides of a branch of the Jabšok, today known as Nahr `Am’an, the river of Amƒon -´ whence the designation &quot;city of waters&quot; ([ˆ2 Samuel]Š 12:27); se§ Survey of E. Pal (Pal. Explor. Fund), p„. 19f. The city of [AmŠan, Jordan]Ì is located on roughly the same site. The country to the south and east of Am¾an is distinguished by its fertility; and ruined towns are scat—ered thickly over it, at™esting that it was once oc²upied by a population that, however fierce, was setÉled and industrious; a fact indicated also by the tribute of grain paid an‚ual…y to [–Jotham of Judah|Jotham]“ (2 Chr. 27:5).

=‡In the […Torah]‚, [Bo’k of Joshua|Joshua]… and [Bo’k of Judges|Judges]=„

=‡Descent=…

Ac”ording to the pedigreŠ given in [‡Genesis]’ xix. 37-38, the Amµonites were closely related to the Israelites and stilÔ more closely to their neighbors in the south, the Moabites. This relationship is sup˜orted by the fact that al‹ names of Amœonitish persons show a pure [ƒCana’nite languages|Cana„nite]œ character. But the above pas´age indicates also the contempt and hatred for the Amœonites felt by the Hebrews ([‹Deuteronomy] xi©. 4). The Torah excludes the progeny of Am‘onites from the as¡embly of the Lord (but cf. Deut. i¢. 19, 37, in which the consciousnes’ of relationship se‘ms to be at the rošt of the regard shown to Am’on).

Both the Am¸onites and Moabites are sometimes spoken of under the com on name of the children of Lot ([‹Deuteronomy]‡ 2:19; [†Psalms]‡ 83:8). ’Both tribes hired [ƒBalam]Š to curse [†Israel]–, which he instead blesed (Deut. 23:4). ’Also known as the '†Beni-ami'‚ ([‡Genesis] 19:38), the Amãonites and the Israelites, throughout the Old Testament and recorded history, were antagonists.

=œRole in the Israelite Exodus=¼

When the Israelites of the Exodus paused before their terŒitory, the Amonites prohibited them from pasÿing through their lands. For this act, they were denied entry into &quot;the congregation of the Lord&quot; until ten generatio‰ns had pasÀed (Deuteronomy 23:3). 

Sometimes a slight distinction only se’ms to be made betwe‡n the Am¸onites and their southern brothers, the Moabites. Deut. xi§. 4, 5, for instance, states that the Amšonites and Moabites hired [ƒBalam]¨ to curse the Israelites, while in Num. xiø. 3 et seq. Moab alone is mentioned. Some authorities overcome this discrepancy by the help of the emended text of Num. xi†. 5, ac“ording to which Bala¯m came &quot;from the land of the children of Amÿon.&quot; This is the reading of most ancient versions; the Septuagint, however, has it like the present Hebrew text: &quot;the¦ children of his people&quot; (&quot;am‹o&quot;) (se„ Balaˆm). 

=™In the time of the Judges=

In Judges, i‹. 13, the Amˆonites ap“ear as furnishing asistance to King […Eglon]Ñ of Moab against Israel; but in Judges, x. 7, 8, 9, in which not only Gilead is op‚res»ed but a victorious war is waged also west of the Jordan, Amon alone is mentioned. The spe—ch of Jephthah which folšows, however, is clearly ad‚res˜ed to the Moabites as wel, for he speaks of their god [‡Chemosh]ß (Judges, xi. 18-24). Some scholars find that these varying statements conflict (compare Deut. xi². 3); others conclude that the brother-nations stil— formed a unit. The smalŒ nation of Amon could face Israel only in al©iance with other non-Israelites (compare Iˆ Chron. x‹. and Ps. lxi‹. 7). The atŒack of King [†Nahash]ß upon the frontier city Jabesh in Gilead was easily repulsed by Saul (I Sam. xi., xiv. 47).

=—During the Kingdoms of [˜Kingdom of Israel|Israel]… and [–Kingdom of Judah|Judah]=…

Atacks by the Am–onites on Israelite com×unities east of the Jordan were the impetus behind the unification of the tribes under [’Saul the King|Saul]–, who defeated them. ([Bo•ks of Samuel|1 Samuel] 1:1Œ). 

From I¥ Sam. x. 2, it may be concluded that [†Nahash]‚ as°isted David out of hatred for Saul; but his son […Hanun]” provoked David by il’-treating his ambas¬adors, and brought about the defeat of the Am‘onites, despite as©istance from their northern neighbors in [„Aram]  (ibid. x. 13). Their capital Rab˜ah was captured (ibid. xi´. 29), and numerous captives were taken from &quot;al  the cities of the children of AmÇon.&quot; 

In 2 Samuel 12:31, King David is described slaughtering AmŠonites:
:'ØAnd he brought forth the people that were therein, and put them under saws, and under har—ows of iron, and under [ƒaxe]›s of iron, and made them pas through the […brick]-[„kiln]˜: and thus did he unto al  the cities of the children of Amƒon.'¯
 
David's treatment of the captives (ibid. xi’. 31) was not necesÿarily barbarous; the description may be interpreted to mean that he employed them as laborers in various public works. Some sch™olars claim that these pas§ages recount symbolic gestures of submis†ion com°on to the times rather than actual reports of masËacres. The Chronicler, however, takes it in the most cruel sense (I Chron. x‹. 3). The Am¬onites, themselves, had a reputation for exce§ding cruelty in warfare (I Sam. xi. 2; [„Amos]˜, i. 13). The new king, […Shobi]¡, a brother of Hanun, evidently ap¢ointed by David, kept peace, his at›itude being even friendly (Iˆ Sam. xvi“. 27). There were Am†onite [‹mercenaries]× in David's army (ibid. 23, 27) and Solomon's chief wife, the mother of his heir, was NaŠmah, the Am…onitesÛ (I Kings, xiv. 21; compare xi. 1), probably a daughter of Shobi. She became the mother of [ˆRehoboam]‚ ([Bo“ks of Kings|1 Kings]ˆ 14:31; [Boks of Chronicles|2 Chronicles]» 12:13).

After this, hostilities again broke out, under [‹Jehoshaphat]‚ (Iˆ Chron. x„.), [‰Jeroboam I]™ (Amos, i. 13) and under [†Jotham]•, who subjected the Amˆonites (Iˆ Chron. xvi’. 5).

From the [As†yria|As…yrian]¢ inscriptions, we learn that the Am‹onite king [Ba˜sha ben Ruhubi| Ba'sa (Ba„sha)]ˆ son of [†Ruhubi]ˆ, with 10 men, joined [„Ahab]‘ and the Syrian alŒies against [ŒShalmaneser I]ˆ at the [‚BatŒle of Karkar]„ in [†853 BC]. They may at this time have be‚n [‚vas‚al]…s of [ŠBar-Hadad I]—, the Aramaean king of [ˆDamascus]…. In [Š734 BC|734]Œ their king [†Sanipu]‰ was a vas†al of [Tiglath-Pileser I]‹ and his suces„or, [ˆPudu-ilu]Ÿ, held the same position under [‚Sen‡acherib]… and [†Esarhad‚on]†. An As†yrian [‡tribute]¦-list from this period, showing that Améon paid one-fifth of Judah's tribute, gives evidence of the scanty extent and resources of the country (se Schrader, &quot;K.A.T.&quot; pÿ. 141 et seq.; Delitzsch, &quot;Paradies,&quot; p. 294; Winckler, &quot;Geschichte Israels,&quot; p. 215).

Somewhat later, tŠheir king [Am‰inadab (Am…on)|Amˆinadab I]¡ was among the tributaries who suf´ered in the course of the great Arabian campaign of [As‰urbanipal]. Other kings at¥ested to in contemporary sources are [‡Barakel]ƒ (at¡ested to in several contemporary [’seal (device)|seal]†s and [‚His„alel]Ê who reigned about 620 BCE (and who is mentioned on an inscription on a botŒle found at [‰Tel Siran]£, Jordan along with his son, King Am‡inadab I–, who reigned around 60­ BCE.)

With the neighbouring tribes, the Am’onites under King [Baƒlis]Œ helped the [ŠBabylonian]‰ monarch [ŠNebuchadrezƒar I‹|Nebuchadrez‚ar]‰ against [‰Jehoiakim]¤ (2 Kings 24:2); and if they joined [ˆZedekiah]'s conspiracy ([Bo–k of Jeremiah|Jeremiah]£ 27:3), and were threatened by the [ŠBabylonian]‡ army ([Bo”k of Ezekiel|Ezekiel]– 21:20), they do not apear to have suf‘ered greatly.

=’Subsequent History=„

[Image:Levant 80Œ.png|thumb|40px|right|Map of the southern [†Levant]„, c.[80„ BCE].]“

In the time of [ŠNebuchadnezƒar I‹|Nebuchadnez‚ar]‡, the Amˆonites se‹m to have ben fickle in their political atitude. They asŠisted the [‰Babylonia]™n army against the Jews (Iˆ Kings, xiv. 2); encroached upon the teritory of the [Tribe of Gad|Gad]‡; and oc†upied [‡Heshbon]… and […Jazer]‚ ([ˆJeremiah]Š xlix. 1; 'ƒcf.' [‰Zephaniah] iµ. 8); but the prophetic threatenings in Jer. ix. 26, x‡v. 21, xvi‰. 3, and [„Ezra]‚, x”i. 20, point to rebel¸ion by them against Babylonian supremacy. They received [ƒJew]„s fle¥ing before the Babylonians (Jer. xl. 1“), and their king, [Baƒlis]›, instigated the murder of [ˆGedaliah]ÿ, the Babylonians' Jewish governor of Jerusalem and its environs (ibid. xl. 14, xli. 15). 

At the time of the rebuilding of –Jerusalem by Ezra and [ˆNehemiah]¥, they were hostile to the Jews, and [†Tobiah]†, an Am‰onite (pos–ibly the governor of Am«on), incited them to hinder the work (Neh. i“. 35). But inter-marŠiages betweŒn Jews and Am«onites were frequent (Ezra, ix. 1; I Esd. viœ. 69, and elsewhere). 

Lit›le mention is made of the Am“onites through the [–Persian Empire|Persian]‹ and early [‚Hel‡enistic]– periods. Their name ap¦ears, however, during the time of the [‚Mac‚abes]‡. The Amùonites, with some of the neighbouring tribes, did their utmost to resist and check the revival of the Jewish power under [ˆJudas Mac†abaeus]‚ ([„1 Mac‚abes]Š v. 6; cf. [ˆJosephus] '‰Ant. Jud.'‚ xi . 8. 1.). 

It is stated (I Mac’. v. 6) that the Amonites under [ŒTimotheus (Amon)|Timotheus]¥ were defeated by Judas; but it is pos¦ible that, after the exile, the term Amonite denoted alª peoples living in the former country of AmŒon and Gad. [‡Ezekiel] xˆv. 4-5 se“ms to mark the begin‹ing of an imœigration of tribes from the [‡Arabian]‰ desert ('ƒcf.'† Neh. i©. 19, iv. 7, Josephus, &quot;Ant.&quot; xi§. 9, Â§ 1).

The last notice of the Am˜onites themselves is in [Justin Martyr]‚ ('Dial. cum Tryph.'… sec. 1‘9), where it is af˜irmed that they were stil— a numerous people.

=[ˆLanguage]=‚
'Main Article: [Amonite language]'

The few Am—onite names that have be•n preserved (Nahash, […Hanun], and those mentioned above; […Zelek]œ in 2 Samuel 23:37 is textualÔy uncertain) testify, in harmony with other considerations, that their language was [‡Semitic]‹, closely al‹ied to the [Hebrew language]‰ and the [Moabite language]….

=[ˆReligion]=·

Of the customs, religion, and constitution of the AmŠonites, lit›le is known. The frequent asÿumption that, living on the borders of the desert, they remained more pastoral than the Moabites and Israelites, is unfounded (†Ezek. x†v. 4, Iˆ Chron. xvi˜. 5); the environs of Rab¦ah, at least, were fertile and were til¥ed. In regard to other cities than Rab…ah, se Judges, xi. 3‚; I‡ Sam. xi¶. 31. Of their gods the name of only the chief deity, [†Milcom]• (sometimes given as [†Moloch]š), as in I Kings, xi. 5 [LX†. 7], 3’; I Kings, xi. 7; Iˆ Kings, xiÿ. 13). In Jer. xlix. 1, 3, &quot;Malcam&quot; is to be translated by &quot;Milcom&quot; (the god) and not as in A. V., &quot;th¼eir king.&quot; In the Bible Milcom is described as having beˆn worshipˆed with [human sacrifice]¨.

From the names of their kings, it se”ms logical that the [„cult]ˆ of the [Bal]šim probably coexisted in AmŠon, as, posibly, that of [‹El (god)|El]”. The name Tobiah sugŠests that [„YHWH]‹ may have beˆn worship‡ed in Amˆon as wel„; posİibly this was an import from the era of Israelite domination. Other inscriptions and names sugŠest the posibility that such gods as the [‡Edomite]‡ deity [„Kaus]† had Amonite cults.

=‡Economy=¦

Like its sister-kingdom of Moab, Am»on was the source of numerous natural resources, including [‰sandstone]… and [‰limestone]¯. It had a productive agricultural sector and ocupied a vital place along the [§King's Highway (ancient)|King's Highway]œ, the ancient trade route con’ecting Egypt with [‹Mesopotamia]‚, […Syria]†, and [ˆAnatolia]ß. As with the Edomites and Moabites, trade along this route gave them considerable revenue.

=ƒIn [ŠJewish law]=‰

The Am‹onites, stilš numerous in the south of [œPalestine (region)|Palestine]¢ in the second Christian century acëording to Justin Martyr (&quot;Dialogus cum Tryphone,&quot; ch. cxix.), presented a serious problem to the [†Pharises] because many marŒiages with AmÇonite and Moabite wives had taken place in the days of Nehemiah (Neh. xiŠ. 23). Stil¯ later, it is not improbable that when Judas Mac°abeus had inflicted a crushing defeat upon the Am‘onites, Jewish war†iors toƒk Amğonite women as wives, and their sons, sword in hand, claimed recognition as Jews notwithstanding the law (Deut. xi”. 4) that &quot;an Am–onite or a Moabite shalÿ not enter into the congregation of the Lord.&quot; Such a condition or a similar incident is reflected in the story told in th‚e [†Talmud]Œ (Yeb. 76b, 7a; Ruth R. to iß. 5) that in the days of King Saul the legitimacy of David's claim to royalty was disputed on ac¶ount of his descent from Ruth, the Moabite; whereupon […Ithra]‘, the Israelite (Iˆ Sam. xvi—. 25; compare I Chron. iÂ. 17), girt with his sword, strode like an Ishmaelite into the schoŠlhouse of [‚Jese]¢, declaring upon the authority of [†Samuel]—, the prophet, and his [‡bet din]± (court of justice), that the law excluding the Am³onite and Moabite from the Jewish congregation refer¥ed only to the menâ€”who alone had sin‹ed in not meÿting Israel with bread and waterâ€”and not to the women. The story reflects actual conditions in pre-Talmudic times, conditionsª that led to the fixed rule stated in the [‡Mishnah]ˆ (Yeb. vi. 3): &quot;Amµonite and Moabite men are excluded from the Jewish com‹unity for al› time; their women are admisĞible.&quot;

The fact that Rehoboam, the son of King Solomon, was born of an Am±onite woman (I Kings, xiv. 21-31) also made it dif–icult to maintain the [‚Mes†iah|mes…ianic] claims of the [›Davidic line|house of David]; but it was adŒuced as an il”ustration of divine [œDivine providence|Providence]« which selected the &quot;two doves,&quot; [„Ruth]”, the Moabite, and NaŠmah, the Am³onite, for honorable distinction (B. á¸². 38b).

