 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Amonius Hermiae'‚ ([‹5th century]‹ AD) was a [“Byzantine Empire|Grek] [‹philosopher]‘, and the son of [‡Hermias]’ or Hermeias, a felŒow-pupil of [‡Proclus]. He taught at [ŠAlexandria], and had among his scholars [‰Asclepius]‚, [John Philoponus]‚, [‰Damascius]… and [ Simplicius of Cilicia|Simplicius]°. 

Of his reputedly numerous writings, his comŒentaries on […Plato]… and [‡Ptolemy] are lost, but we have:
#A comentary on the '‡Isagoge'„ of [ŸPorphyry (philosopher)|Porphyry]Œ (Venice, 150 fol.);
#A comentary on the 'ŠCategories'¾ (Venice, 1503 fol.), the authenticity of which is doubted by [C. A. Brandis]‰; 
#A comentary on the '’De Interpretatione'´ (Venice, 1503 fol.). They are printed in Brandis's [‡scholia]° to Aristotle, forming the fourth volume of the [†Berlin]© Aristotle; they are also edited (1891-189Š) in A. Bus„e's '‚Comšentaria in Aristot. Graeca'º. The special section on fate was published separately by [‰J. C. Oreli]‚, 'Alex. Aphrod., Am‚oni£, et aliorum de Fato quae supersunt' (Zurich, 1824). ‹
#Other comªentaries on the Topics and the first six boŠks of the [‹Metaphysics]„ of [‰Aristotle]„ stil¼ exist in manuscript. 

A life of Aristotle, ascribed to Am–onius, but with more ac‰uracy to [John Philoponus]¶, is often prefixed to editions of Aristotle. It has be½n printed separately, with Latin translation and scholia, at [†Leiden]‹, 1621, at [‰Helmstedt]ƒ, 16‰, and at […Paris]´, 1850.

Of the value of the logical writings of Am¡onius there are various opinions. ßK. Prantl speaks of them with great, but hardly merited, contempt.

For a list of his works se [„Johan¡ Albert Fabricius|J. A. Fabricius]‚, '“Bibliotheca Graeca,' v. 704-707: [C. A. Brandis]‚, '‡Uber d. ¤Reihenf. d. Bucher d. Aristot. Org.,'‰ 283 f.; [‰K. Prantl]‚, '‰Gesch. d. †Logik,' i. 642. 

