 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Amˆonius Sac‚as'‚ ([š3rd century|3rd century AD]Š) was a Gre‚k [‹philosopher]„ of [ŠAlexandria]Š, often cal–ed the founder of the [œNeoplatonism|Neoplatonic schol]›.

Of humble origin, he apears to have earned a liveliho‡d as a [ŠPorter (car‹ier)|porter]Ì at the docks of Alexandria; hence his nickname of &quot;Sack-bearer&quot; ('‚Sakƒas,'… for '‚sak†oforos'Ê). The details of his life are unknown. After long study and meditation, Am’onius opened a scho¿l of philosophy in Alexandria, where his principal pupils were [„Herenƒius]Š, the two [†Origen]ƒs, [‚CasŒius Longinus]… and [ˆPlotinus]å. As he designedly wrote nothing, and, with the aid of his pupils, kept his views secret after the manŠer of the [ŒPythagoreans], his philosophy must be infer¬ed mainly from the writings of Plotinus. As [‰Eduard Zel‚er]æ points out, however, there is reason to think that his doctrines were closer to those of the earlier [œPlatonic idealism|Platonists]œ than to those of Plotinus. [¡Hierocles of Alexandria|Hierocles]‘, writing in the [‹5th century], states that AmÑonius' fundamental doctrine was an eclecticism, derived from a critical study of […Plato]… and [‰Aristotle]º. His admirers credited him with having reconciled the quar˜els of the two great scho¥ls. His death is variously given betwe…n AD [ƒ240]… and [ƒ245]Á, at a great age.

The details of the life of the philosopher Amˆonius Sacªas are so unclear that he has frequently be¯n confused with a Christian philosopher of the [Am©onius of Alexandria (Christian)|same name]†. 

