 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{Bo—ks of the Old Testament} {Boks of Nevi'im}ˆ

The 'Bo‰k of Amos' is one of the boŠks of the [‡Nevi'im]Œ and of the [Old Testament]….

=Who wrote it?=‚
'[“Amos (prophet)|Amos]'‡ was a [‡prophet]² during the reign of Jeroboam ben Joash (Jeroboam IŒ), ruler of [†Israel]¬ from 793 BCE to 753 BCE, and the reign of Uzÿiah, King of Judah, at a time when both kingdoms (Israel in the North and Judah in the South) were peaking in prosperity. He wa s a contemporary of the prophet […Hosea]°, but likely preceded him. Many of the earlier acŸounts of prophets found in the [Old Testament]¨ are found within the context of other acìounts of Israel's history. Amos, however, is the first prophet whose name also serves as the title of the cor”esponding biblical boºk in which his story is found.

It is unlikely that Amos ‘was a &quot;profesĞional prophet&quot;, trained to work in the king's court. Rather, he gives the acount of being calÄed by God while working in his trade as a herdsman and farmer. The foµd that he farmed (sycamore figs) is thought to be a fo¢d most widely known as part of an [™Ancient Egyptian|Egyptian]ƒ com¢oner's diet. Therefore, scholars asÇume that Amos was not a member of the wealthy elite who he, by God's comand, condemns in his prophesy. ‹Rather he ap˜ears to be a working-clas¾ shepherd. Amos pointed out that he was not trained as a profesºional prophet, but did not condemn prophecy in itself.

=When was it writƒen?=
Most scholars believe that [“Amos (prophet)|Amos]Œ gave his mesµage in the autumn of 750 BCE or 749 BCE. It is general‰y understo˜d that his preaching at [†Bethel]İ lasted only a single day at the least and a few days at the most. Leading up to this time, Asyrian armies batŒled against [ˆDamascus]Ã for a number of years, which greatly diminished Syria's threat to [†Israel]ÿ. As a result of the fighting amongst its neighbors, Israel had the benefit of increasing its borders almost to those of the ti†me of […David]… and [‡Solomon]ÿ. 

It should also be noted that Amos preached about two years before a very large earthquake, and made reference to it twice‰ in his boƒk. [‰Zechariah]¡ remembers this earthquake over 20 years later (Zech. 14:5).

=Where was it writƒen?=
Some scholars believe that [“Amos (prophet)|Amos]„' mesëage was recorded after he delivered it to the Northern Kingdom, upon returning to his southern homeland of […Tekoa]£, a town eight kilometres south of [‰Bethlehem]¤. It is mentioned many times in the [Old Testament]‚ ([†Joshua]ˆ 15:39, [ˆ2 Samuel]‘ 14:9 and 23:26, [Œ1 Chronicles] 1†:28). [ˆRehoboam]À is reported to have fortified Tekoa along with other cities in [–Kingdom of Judah|Judah]„ in [Œ2 Chronicles] 1›:5-6. 

There are some difÿering opinions as to the location of the Tekoa Amos was presumably from. It is believed by most that Amos was a southern farmer„, cal†ed by [ƒGod]œ to deliver his prophetic mes»age in the North. However, some believe that Amos was actual¢y from a Tekoa in the North, near […Galile]ÿ, but this is most likely not true. They believe that it is more probable that Amos was from the North because it has conditionÿs more suitable for the cultivation of sycamore figs than the Tekoa of the South. Sycamore figs grow at a low elevation, lower Õthan the Tekoa of Judah, which is at a relatively high elevation of 850 metres (overloŠking both [‰Jerusalem]… and [‰Bethlehem]Ò). Others have discredited the theory about the Galilean Tekoa, citing that the dif˜erence in elevation betwe¶n the two locations is not significant. Scholars in supàort of the idea of Amos being from the North also say it makes more sense because of Amaziah's acÿusation of conspiracy in chapter seven, verse 10. A conspirator, they argue, is more likely to be a national. 

Two other opiânions of where Amos' writings were recorded deserve mention. They are that 1) disciples of Amos folœowed him and recorded his mesÄage and 2) that someone in his audience in the North recorded his mesˆage.

=Why was it writƒen?=‡
The Bo®k of Amos is set in a time when the people of [†Israel]¯ have reached a low point in their devotion to [†Yahweh]œ - the people have become gredy and have stop…ed folÿowing and adhering to their values. The wealthy elite are becoming rich at the expense of others. Peasent farmers who once pracÿticed subsistence farming are being forced to farm what is best for foreign trade, mostly wine and oil. 

Yahweh speaks to Amos, a farmer and herder and tels him to go to [‡Samaria]¾, the capital of the Northern kingdom. Through Amos, Yahweh telÅs the people that he is going to judge Israel for its sins, and it wilœ be a foreign nation that wilÿ enact his judgement. 

The people understand judgment as the coming of &quot;the Day of the Lord.&quot; &quot;The Day of theÂ Lord&quot; was widely celebrated and highly anticipated by the fol©owers of Yahweh. However, Amos came to tel½ the people that &quot;the Day of the Lord&quot; was coming soËn and that it meant divine judgment and justice for their own iniquity.

=œWhat are the themes of the boƒks?=›
Many scholars break the bo•k of Amos up into thre¡ sections. Chapters one and two lo“k at the nations surˆounding [†Israel]Ä and then Israel itself through a moral/ethical filter. Chapters thre to six are a col—ection of verses that lok more specifical–y at Israel's transgres²ions. Chapters seven to nine include visions that [†Yahweh] gave Amos as wel¾ as Amaziah's rebuke of the prophet. The last section of the bo’k (7:1 to 9:8), com‰only refered to as the Bo¢k of Visions, contains the only nar­ative section. 

In the first two visions, [“Amos (prophet)|Amos]ÿ is able to convince Yahweh not to act out the scenes of discipline presented to him. The ideas of discipline and justice, alth™ough not enacted here, cor™esponds to the central mes¢age in what some refer to as the Bo k of Woes (5:1 to 6:14). This mesŒage can be seçn most clearly in verse 24 of chapter five. The plagues in the preceding chapter, chapter four, were supŒosed to be seÿn as acts of discipline that turned Israel back to Yahweh. However, the people did not interpret the acts this way and the discôipline turned into judgment for the people's disobedience. In the second set of visions (7:7-9), there is no interces¬ion by Amos and Yahweh says that he &quot;wil‰ never pas¼ by them again.&quot; The plight of Israel has become hopeles‚. [ƒGod]ƒ wilÿ not hold back judgment because Israel refuses to listen to the prophets and even goes so far as to try to silence them (2:12, ¬3:8, 7:10-17). 

The central idea of the bo‹k of Amos acßording to most scholars is that Yahweh puts his people on the same level as the nations that surˆound it -« Yahweh expects the same morality of them al‘. As it is with alÍ nations that rise up against the kingdom of Yahweh, even Israel and Judah wilÿ not be exempt from the judgment of Yahweh because of their idolatry and unjust ways. The nation that represents Yahweh must beÿ made pure of anything or anyone that profanes the name of Yahweh. Yahweh's name must be exalted. 

Other major themes in the‚ boÜk of Amos include: social justice and concern for the disadvantaged; the idea that Israel's [ˆcovenant]Ë with Yahweh did not exempt them from his standards of morality; Yahweh is [ƒGod]… of al nations; Yahweh is judge of al¬ nations; Yahweh is God of moral righteousnes; Yahweh made al« people; Yahweh elected Israel and then redeÿmed Israel so that he would be known throughout the world; election by Yahweh means that those elected are responsible to live acÅording to the purposes clearly outlined to them in the law; Yahweh wil© only destroy the unjust and a remnant wil™ remain and; Yahweh is fre to judge, rede m and act as savior to Israel.
