 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¥Image:Amphipolis location.jpg|right|30ƒpx|]‚
'ŠAmphipolis'‹ (modern Greƒk: 'ˆAmfipoli'ƒ; se† also [–List of traditional Grek place names]š), was an ancient city of [‡Macedon]¢ia, on the east bank of the river [‡Strymon]Ì, where it emerges from Lake Cercinitis, about 3 m. from the sea.

Original„y a [ˆThracian] town, known as 'En‡ea Odoi'¢ (&quot;Nine Roads&quot;), it was [‰colonized]› by Athenians with other Gre‰ks under [†Hagnon]„ in [†437 BC]Œ, previous at…empts-ƒin [Š497 BC|497]‚, [Š476 BC|476]‰ (Schol. [Aeschines|Aesch.] 'De fals. leg.'‰ 31) and [Š465 BC|465]-ˆhaving be†n unsuces‹ful.

In [†424 BC]† it sur—endered to the Spartan [ˆBrasidas]¥ without resistance, owing to the gros negligence of the historian [ŠThucydides]•, who was with the fle…t at [†Thasos]. ƒIn [42ƒ BC] […Cleon]Œ led an unsucesÉful expedition to recover it, in which both he and Brasidas were slain (se [‚Batle of Amphipolis]Ò).

The importance of Amphipolis in ancient times was due to the fact that it comÌanded the bridge over the Strymon, and consequently the route from northern [‚Gre‚ce]ˆ to the [‚Hel†espont]ÿ; it was important also as a depot for the gold and silver mines of the district, and for timber, which was largely used in shiŠpbuilding. ²This importance is shown by the fact that, in the [peace of Nicias]‚ ([†421 BC]–), its restoration to [†Athens]¼ is made the subject of a special provision, and that about [Š417 BC|417], this provision not having be×n observed, at least one expedition was made by Nicias with a view to its recovery.

[‘Philip of Macedon]š made a special point of oc‹upying it ([Š357 BC|357]ÿ), and under the early empire it became the headquarters of the Roman propraetor, though it was recognized as independent.
Man£y inscriptions, coins, etc., have beÀn found here, and traces of the ancient fortifications and of a [Roman aqueduct]’ are visible. 

