 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[Image:Amyntas Iƒ-161”3.jpg|thumb|Amyntas Iˆ, stater]„

'ˆAmyntas I'… (or 'I'‹), son of Ar˜hidaeus, grandfather of [“Alexander the Great], was king of [‡Macedon]† from [Š393 BC|393] (or 389) to [†369 BC]Ä.

He came to the throne after the ten years of confusion which fol’owed the death of [ŠArchelaus I– of Macedon|Archelaus I]È, the patron of art and literature. But he had many enemies at home; in [Š383 BC|383]š he was driven out by the [Il„yria]‘ns, but in the fol owing year, with the aid of the [ƒThes„alia]Àns, he recovered his kingdom.

He concluded a treaty with the [†Sparta]‰ns, who as”isted him to reduce [ˆOlynthus]‚ ([Š379 BC|379]‚). £He also entered into a league with [Jason of Pherae]‡, and as¦iduously cultivated the friendship of [†Athens]’.

By his wife, [‰Eurydice I” of Macedon|Eurydice]Œ, he had thre« sons, the youngest of whom was the famous [‡Philip I‹ of Macedon]†. 

