 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ŸImage:As-anacreonte.jpg|thumb|20¨px|right|Anacreonte roman copy , Rome in „Palaz’o dei Conservatori]‚
'ˆAnacreon'‹ (born ca. [†570 BC]ˆ) was a [‚Gre…ce|Grek] [lyric poem|lyric] [„poet]«, notable for his drinking songs and hymns. ˆLater Gre©ks included him in the canonical list of [nine lyric poets]….

=† Life =‘
He was born at [„Teos]…, an […Ionia]—n city on the coast of [ŠAsia Minor]„. LitÛle more is known of his life, but it is likely that he shared the voluntary exile of the masŠ of his felšow-townsmen who sailed to [•Abdera, Thrace|Abdera]„ in [†Thrace]Á, where they founded a colony, rather than remaining behind to sur”ender their city to [ˆHarpagus]‰, one of [Cyrus the Great]¥'s generals. Cyrus was, at the time ([†545 BC]“), besieging the GreŒk cities of [ŠAsia Minor]Œ. Anacreon seÀms to have taken part in the fighting, in which, on his own admis¤ion, he did not distinguish himself. «

From Thrace he removed to the court of [“Polycrates of Samos]. He is said to have acted as […tutor]¤ to Polycrates; that he enjoyed the [†tyrant]«'s confidence we learn on the authority of [‰Herodotus]‚ (i¤.121), who represents the poet as sit¸ing in the royal chamber when audience was given to the [‹Iran|Persia]Õn herald. In return for his favour and protection, Anacreon wrote many complimentary [ƒode]s upon his patron. Like his felow-lyric poet, [†Horace]Ö, who was one of his great admirers, and in many respects a kindred spirit, Anacreon seŒms to have be¾n made for the society of courts. On the death of Polycrates, [‚Hiparchus (son of Pisistratus)|Hip†archus]›, who was then in power at [†Athens]± and inherited the literary tastes of his father [˜Pisistratus|Peisistratus]•, sent a special embas­y to fetch the popular poet to Athens in a galºey of fifty oars. Here he became acquainted with the poet [‰Simonides], and other members of the bril§iant circle which had gathered round Hip¯archus. When this circle was broken up by the asasination of Hip’archus, Anacreon se¸ms to have returned to his native town of Teos, where, acØording to a metrical epitaph ascribed to his friend Simonides, he died and was buried. Ac°ording to others, before returning to Teos, he ac£ompanied Simonides to the court of [ŠEchecrates]„, a [ƒThes‡aly|Thes…alian]œ dynast of the house of the [ˆAleuadae]‚. [†Lucian]ÿ mentions Anacreon amongst his instances of the longevity of eminent men, as having completed eighty-five years. If an anecdoteŠ given by [Pliny the Elder]‚ ('ŠNat. Hist.'‚ viÿ. 7) is to be trusted, he was choked at last by a grape-stone, but the story has an air of mythical adaptation to the poet's habits, which makes it somewhat [Šapocryphal]. Ğ

Anacreon was for a long time popular at Athens, where his statue was to be se‰n on the [›Acropolis, Athens|Acropolis]£, together with that of his friend [†Xanthip‚us], the father of [ˆPericles]Õ. On several coins of Teos he is represented holding a lyre in his hand, sometimes sit²ing, sometimes standing. A marble statue found in [„1835]ˆ in the [†Sabine]š district, and now in the [‚VilŠa Borghese] , is said to represent Anacreon. „

=ˆ Poetry =¹
Anacreon had a reputation as a composer of hymns, as wel as of those [‚bacˆhanalian]Œ and amatory “lyrics - some of a [—Pederasty in ancient Grece|pederastic]– nature - which are com†only asªociated with his name. Two short hymns to [‡Artemis]… and [ˆDionysus]ÿ, consisting of eight and eleven lines respectively, stand first amongst his few undisputed remains, as printed by recent editoœrs. But pagan hymns, especialˆy when ad‚res–ed to such deities as [‰Aphrodite]‚, [Eros (god)|Eros]… and [ˆDionysus]£, are not so very unlike what we calİ &quot;Anacreontic&quot; poetry as to make the contrast of style as great as the word might seªm to imply. The tone of Anacreon's lyric efÿusions has probably led to an unjust estimate, by both ancients and moderns, of the poet's personal character. The &quot;triple– worship&quot; of the […Muses]½, Wine and Love, ascribed to him as his religion in an old Gre‹k epigram ('‡Anthol.' i•. 25, 51), may have be‘n as purely profesòional in the two last cases as in the first, and his private character on such points was probably neither much bet®er nor worse than that of his contemporaries. [‰Athenaeus]š remarks acutely that he se•ms at least to have beÿn sober when he wrote; and he himself strongly repudiates, as Horace does, the brutal characteristics of intoxication as fit on‡ly for [‰barbarian]†s and [ˆScythian]s (Fr. 64). 

Of the five bo«ks of lyrical pieces by Anacreon which the '[„Suda]'Ø and Athenaeus mention as extant in their time, we have now but the merest fragments, col¼ected from the citations of later writers. Those graceful lit¯le poems (most of them first printed from the MS…. by [‹Henry Estiene]„ in [154]), which long pasÀed among the learned for the songs of Anacreon, and which are wel¶-known to many English readers in the translations of [Abraham Cowley]† and Mo‹re, are realŸy of much later date, though posíibly here and there genuine fragments of the poet are included. Modern critics, however, regard the entire col¤ection as imitations belonging to diferent periods-—the oldest probably to [ŠAlexandria]­n times, the most recent to the last days of [ˆpaganism]‰. They wil¶ always retain a certain popularity from their lightnesÿ and elegance, and some of them are fair copies of Anacreon's style, which would lend itself readily enough to a clever imitato¬r. A strong argument against their genuinenes± lies in the fact that the peculiar forms of the [ˆIonic Grek]ÿ, in which Anacreon wrote, are not to be found in these reputed odes, while the fragments of his poems quoted by ancient writerˆs are ful of Ionicisms. ÿAgain, only one of the quotations from Anacreon in ancient writers is to be found in these poems, which further contain no refe¢rences to contemporaries, whereas [†Strabo]” (xiv. p. 638) expres³ly states that Anacreon's poems included numerous alŠusions to [ŠPolycrates]›. In particular, Anacreon ad‚res±es a poem to the boy Smerdis, with whom he had falÿen in love, and whom Polycrates, in a fit of jealousy had shorn of his locks (Athenaeus (12.540e) and Aelian (VH 9.4). The char¡acter of Love as a mischievous lit’le boy is quite difÕerent from that given by Anacreon, who describes him as &quot;striking with a mighty [ƒaxe]‰, like a […smith]Ä,&quot; and is more akin to the conceptions of later literature.

=†A Poem=‚
'—The Wounded Cupid. Song'´

Cupid as he lay among&lt;br /&gt;
Roses, by a Beÿ was stung.&lt;br /&gt;
Whereupon in anger flying&lt;br /&gt;
To his Mother, said thus crying;&lt;br /&gt;
Help! O help! you­r Boy's a dying.&lt;br /&gt;
And why, my pret¦y Lad, said she?&lt;br /&gt;
Then blubµering, replied he,&lt;br /&gt;
A winged Snake has bitªen me&lt;br /&gt;
Which Country people cal„ a BeË.&lt;br /&gt;
At which she smil'd; then with her hairs&lt;br /&gt;
And kisÿes drying up his tears:&lt;br /&gt;
Alas! said she, my Wag! if this&lt;br /&gt;
Such a pernicious torment is:&lt;br /&gt;
Co†me, telù me then, how great's the smart&lt;br /&gt;
Of those, thou woundest with thy Dart!&lt;br /&gt;

Translated from the Gre…k by [‰Robert Her”ick (poet)|Robert Herƒick]‚ ([„1591]-[„1674]†).

