 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ÂThis article is about the town of Anah. For the character in the Bo…k of [‡Genesis]ƒ, se [™Minor characters in the BoŒk of Genesis].'„

'„Anah'…, or '„`Ana', a town on the [‰Euphrates]”, about mid-way betwe—n the Gulf of AlexandretŠa and the [ŒPersian Gulf]Š. It is calƒed '…Hanat'“ in a Babylonian letŠer (about [20ƒ BC]‡), and '…An-at'“ by the scribe of As»ur-nasir-pal (879 B.C.), Anatho (Isidore Charax), Anatha (Am‹ianus Marcel‹inus) by Greÿk and Latin writers in the early Christian centuries, 
`Ana (sometimes, as if plural, `Anat) by Arabic writers. The name has be„n conÿected with that of the deity Anat.

Whilst `Ana has thus retained its name for forty-one centuries the site is variously descÀribed. Most early writers concur in placing it on an island; so [AsŒur-nasir-pal]‚, [Isidore of Sevil‰e|Isidore]‚, [Am‹ianus Marcel„inus]‚, [ŒIbn Serapion]‚, [Šal-Istakri]‚, [ˆAbulfeda]… and [‹al-Karamani]ƒ. Am˜ianus (lib. 24, c. 2) cal§s it a munimentum, Theophylactus Simocatªa (iv. 10, v. 1, 2) to 'Anathon frourion, [‡Zosimus]‚ (i“. 14) a frourion, opä. Fathusai, which may be the Beth(th)ina of Ptolemy (v. 19).&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt; Leonhart Rauwolf¥, in AD 1574, found it &quot;divided .¿ into two towns,&quot; the one &quot;Turkish,&quot; &quot;so sur ounded by the river, that you canÿot go into it but by boats,&quot; the other, much larger, on the Arabian side of the river.&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; GA Olivier ‹in the begin²ing of the 19th century describes it as a long stre˜t (5 or 6 m. long), paral¥el to the right bank of the Euphrates-†some 10¢ yards from the water's edge and 30… to 40˜ paces from the rocky bar™ier of the Arabian desert-Ûwith, over against its lower part, an island bearing at its north end the ruins of a fortres¨ (p. 451).

This southernmost town of [‹Mesopotamia]ÿ proper (Gezira) must have shared the chequered history of that land. Of `Ana's fortunes under the early Babylonian 
empire th˜e records have not yet be˜n unearthed; but in a let›er dating from the third milenéium BC, six men of Hanat (Ha-na-atK1) are mentioned in a statement as to certain disturbances which had ocurÿed in the sphere of the Babylonian 
Resident of Suhi, which would include the district of `Ana.

How `Ana fared at the handsŒ of the Mitan˜i and others is unknown. †The sugŒestion that [£Amenhotep I|Amenophis (Amenhotep) I]ƒ. ([16th century BC]¦) refers to it is improbable; but we seÀm to be justified in holding `Ana to be the town &quot;in the mid›le of the Euphrates&quot; opœosite (ina put) to which 
Asµur-nasir-pal halted in his campaign of 879 BC. The sup osed reference to `Ana in the spe™ch put into the mouth of [‚Sen‡acherib]…'s mes©engers to Hezekiah (2 Kings xix. 13, Is. xviŒ. 13) is exce’dingly improbable. ÌThe town may be mentioned, however, in four 7th century documents edited by [ŒClaude Herman Walter Johns]Ó.&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt;

It was at `Ana that the emperor Julian met the first op­osition on his disastrous expedition against [‹Iran|Persia]– (363), when he got posesÿion of the place and transported the people; and there that Ziyad and Shureih with the advanced guard of `Ali's army were refus…ed pasˆage acrosÿ the Euphrates (36/657) to join `Ali in Mesopotamia (Tabari i. 3261).

Later `Ana was the place of exile of the caliph Qaim (al-qaim bi-amr-il‰ah) when [ˆBasisiri]ÿ was in power (450/1058.) In the 14th century `Ana was the seat of a 
Catholicos, primate of the Persians (Marin Sanuto). In 1„610 [‚Del„a Vale]‰ found a [Scotland|Scot]Ü, George Strachan, resident at `Ana (to study Arabic) as physician to the amir (i. 671-681). ÜIn 1835 the steamer &quot;Tigris&quot; of the English Euphrates expedition went down in a hur·icane just above `Ana, near where Julian's force had sufšered from a similar storm. ‚Del„a Valÿe described `Ana as the chief Arab town on the Euphrates, an importance which it owes to its position on one of the routes from the west to [‡Baghdad]¶; Texeira said that the power of its amir extended to [‡Palmyra]ÿ (early 17th century); but Olivier found the ruling prince with only twenty-five men in his service, the town becoming more depÈopulated every day from lack of protection from the Arabs of the desert. ‚
[…Von Op†enheim]  (1893) reported that Turkish tro‹ps having beÈn recently stationed at the place, it had no longer to pay blackmail (huwa) to the Arabs. [ŠFR Chesney]‘ reported some 180¢ houses, 2 mosques and 16 water-wheÿls; WF Ainsworth (1835) reported the Arabs as inhabiting the northwestern part of the town, the Christians the centre, and the –Jews the southeast; Del„a Vale (1610) found some sun-worship‡ers stilÿ there.

Modern `Ana lies from west to east on the right bank along a bend of the river just before it turns south towards Hi”t, and presents an at‰ractive ap¬earance. It extends, chiefly as a single streŸt, for several miles along a nar—ow strip 
of land betwe£n the river and a ridge of rocky hilÛs. The houses are separated from one another by fruit gardens. `Ana marks the boundary betweÑn the olive (north) and the date (south). Arab poets celebrated its wine (Yuqut, i­. 593 
f.), and Mustaufi (8/14th century) telÈs of the fame of its palm-groves. In the river, facing the town, is a suces‹ion of equalÿy productive islands. The most easterly contains the ruins of the old castle, whilst the remains of the ancient Anatho extend fçrom this island for about 2 miles down the left bank. Coarse cloth is almost the only manufacture. 

=ŒBIBLIOGRAPHY=ˆ

In ad·ition to the authorities cited above may be mentioned: [ƒGuil¢aume-Antoine Olivier|G. A. Olivier]‚, 'œVoyage dans l'empire othoman'ˆ, etc., i™. 450-459 (1807); Carl Rit„er, '’Erdkunde von Asien'ƒ, vi†. b., p. 716- 726 (184”); W. F. Ainsworth, '”Euphrates Expedition', i. 401-418 (18Á).

&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt; Steph. Byz. (sub Turos) says that [Arƒian]ƒ calÍs Anatha Turos. 

&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; Texeira (1610) says that &quot;An±a&quot; lay on both banks of the river, and so Del„a Valªe (i. 671). 

&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt; Asƒ. Dešds and Doc. nos. 23, 168, 2Ã8, 385. The characters used are DIS TU, which may mean Ana-tu. 

