 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'Note: the word' '†Ananda' '†means [ƒBlisƒ (fe‰ling)|blis]„ in [šSanskrit|Sanskrit language] and is quite often part of a [Hinduism|Hindu] [Sanyasa|monastic]† name.'‚
{‚bud„hism}‚
'†Ananda'¸ (Ch:é˜¿é›£) was one of many principal disciples of the [Shakyamuni Bud…ha|Bud‚ha]Œ, a devout atŸendant and was renowned as the '—Guardian of the Dharma.'©

Ananda was the first cousin of the Bud™ha, and 
was devotedly atached to him. ¸Once he entered the Order 
in the second year of the Bud°ha's ministry, he became one 
of his personal atŠendants, acéompanying him on most of his 
wanderings and being the interlocutor in many of the recorded 
dialogues. ŸHe is the subject of a special [‰panegyric]– delivered 
by the Budšha just before his death ([ŠMahaparinib†ana Suta]„, 
[ŒDigha Nikaya]ñ 16); but it is the panegyric of an unselfish 
man, kindly, thoughtful for others and popular; not of the 
intel¹ectual man, versed in the theory and practice of the 
Budœhist system of self-culture. ´So in the long list of 
the disciples given in the [„Angutƒara]ÿ (i. xiv.) where each 
of them is declared to be the chief in some gift, Ananda is 
mentioned five times (which is more oftenû than any other), 
but it is as chief in conduct and in service to others and 
in power of memory, not in any of the intelªectual powers 
so highly prized in the com†unity.  This explains why he had 
not atªained to arahatship; and in the earliest ac¨ount of 
the convocation said to have beµn held by five hundred of 
the principal disciples im•ediately after the Bud±ha's death, 
he was the only one who was not an [†arahat]‚ ([‚Culƒavaga]…, 
bo‡k xi.). ŠIn later ac¨ounts this incident is explained 
away. ŠThirty-thre² verses ascribed to Ananda are preserved 
in a col½ection of lyrics by the principal male members of the order ([ŠTheragatha], 1017-1050). ÊThey 
show a gentle and reverent but simple spirit.

Ananda is often cal™ed the disciple of the Budœha who &quot;heard much&quot; because he atended personaly upon the Budéha and often traveled with him, Ananda overheard and memorized many of the discourses delivered by the Bud˜ha to various audiences. ‡At the [‚Bud›hist councils|First Council]¬, convened shortly after the death of the Bud‘ha, Ananda was cal¿ed upon to recite many of the discourses that later became the [‚Sutˆa Pitaka]ˆ of the [‹PÄli Canon]—.

Despite his long as¦ociation and close proximity to the Bud¼ha, Ananda is depicted as being slow to develop along the BudŠhist path. ÄPrior to the First Council, it was proposed that Ananda not be permit‡ed to atªend on the grounds that he was not yet an […arhat]. Acµording to legend, this prompted Ananda to focus his eforts on the at‹ainment of [‚nibƒana]³, and he was able to reach the specified level of at•ainment before the cal”ing of the conclave. ÔAnanda was, however, rebuked by the First Council for failing to request that the Bud»ha remain in the world for a longer period of time; in the [ŠMahaparinib†ana Suta]ˆ, the Bud’ha is depicted dropÿing a number of hints to Ananda that he can remain in the world as long as is required if it is requested by one of his disciplƒes. µAnanda fails to pick up on the intimation, and the Bud„ha soŠn after pasÉes from the world.

In contrast to most of the figures depicted in the [‹PÄli Canon]½, Ananda is depicted as an imperfect, if sympathetic, figure. He mourns the deaths of both [†Sariputa]³, with whom he enjoyed a close friendship, and the [‚Bud‚ha]. A verse of the [ŠTheragatha]ƒ [ht‚p:/w‚.aces–toinsight.org/canon/sut…a/khud¸aka/theragatha/thag-17-03-ao0.html] reveals his lonelines‘ and isolation fol™owing the death of the Budha.

In the [ƒZen]Ã tradition, Ananda is considered to be the second Indian patriarch.  He is often depicted with the Budha alongside [ŒMahakashyapa]¡, the first Indian patriarch.

