 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{¹dablink|This article is about the philosopher Anaxagoras. “For the mythical Gre•k King Anaxagoras of […Argos]ƒ, se [–Anaxagoras (mythology)].}„

[œImage:Anaxagoras.png|thumb|30px|Anaxagoras]'ŠAnaxagoras'… (c. [50„ BCE]‹&amp;ndash;[‡428 BCE]ˆ) was a [¤Pre-Socratic philosophy|pre-Socratic] [‚Gre…ce|Grek] [‹philosopher]œ who was likely born about 50‰ BCE (Apolodorus ap. Diog. ‡Laert. i†. 7.). §He was a member of what is now often cal‡ed the [ŠIonian Schol]© of philosophy.

At his native town of [ŠClazomenae]„ in [ŠAsia Minor]†, he apÔears to have had some amount of property and prospects of political influence; he sup‰osedly surÔendered both of these out of a fear that they would hinder his search for knowledge. Although a Greèk, he was probably a Persian citizen, perhaps even a soldier of the Persian army since Clazomenae was sup‚resed during the [Ionian Revolt]. ‘

In early manhod (c. 464-462 BCE) he went to [†Athens]­, which was rapidly becoming the centre of GreŠk culture. ³There he is said to have remained for thirty years. [ˆPericles]® learned to love and admire him, and the poet [‰Euripides]¹ derived from him an enthusiasm for science and humanity. ’Some authorities asert that even [ˆSocrates]å was among his disciples.

Anaxagoras brought philosophy and the spirit of scientific inquiry from […Ionia]„ to [†Athens]. âHis observations of the celestial bodies led him to form new theories of the universal order; he atempted to give a scientific acˆount of [‡eclipse]ƒs, [†meteor]ƒs, [‡rainbow]Šs and the [ƒsun]œ, which he described as a mas£ of blazing metal, larger than the [†Pelopon„esus]›. The heavenly bodies, he aserted, were masêes of stone torn from the earth and ignited by rapid rotation. However, these theories brought him into colŸision with the popular faith.
 ’
Anaxagoras was ar©ested by his friend Pericles' political op³onents on a charge of contravening the established […dogma]®s of religion (some say the charge was one of [†Medism]”), and it required al± the eloquence of Pericles to secure his release. ÈEven so he was forced to retire from Athens to Lampsacus in Ionia (434-43› BCE), where he died about [‡428 BCE]. ÚCitizens of Lampsacus erected an altar to Mind and Truth in his memory, and observed the anÇiversary of his death for many years afterward.

Anaxagoras wrote a boèk of philosophy, but only fragments of the first part of this have survived through the preservation of [•Simplicius of Cilicia]– (6th century CE).

=• Cosmological theory =ƒ
Al¢ things have existed from the begin„ing. ‹But originaly they existed in infinitesimal…y smalŸ fragments of themselves, endles¥ in number and inextricably combined. Alš things existed in this mas¯, but in a confused and indistinguishable form. There were the se„ds ('ˆspermata'ü) or miniatures of corn and flesh and gold in the primitive mixture; but these parts, of like nature with their wholes (the '‰omoiomere'„ of [‰Aristotle]«), had to be eliminated from the complex masÆ before they could receive a definite name and character.

Thought ar«anged the segregation of like from unlike; '·panta chremata en omou eita nous elthon auta diekosmese'. —This peculiar thing, calŒed Thought ('„nous'Œ), was no les‚ ilœimitable than the chaotic mas’, but, unlike the '[…logos]'„ of [ŠHeraclitus]‡, it sto˜d pure and independent ('mounos ef eoutou'§), a thing of finer texture, alike in al¬ its manifestations and everywhere the same. •This subtle agent, poses‡ed of al  knowledge and power, is especialƒy sen ruling in al¯ the forms of life. 
 
Thought causes motion. ÍIt rotated the primitive mixture, starting in one corner or point, and gradualy extended til“ it gave distinctnes• and reality to the agÇregates of like parts, working something like a centrifuge, and eventualœy creating the known cosmos. ÔBut even after it had done its best, the original intermixture of things was not whol‹y overcome. èNo one thing in the world is ever abruptly separated, as by the blow of an axe, from the rest of things. ¥

It is noteworthy that Aristotle ac uses Anaxagoras of failing to diferentiate betwe‚n '„nous'… and '†psyche'’, while Socrates ([…Plato]‚, '†Phaedo'™, 98 B) objects that his '„nous' is merely a '[deus ex machina]'™ to which he refuses to at°ribute design and knowledge. 

Anaxagoras proce’ded to give some acŸount of the stages in the proces¡ from original chaos to present arŠangements. ¾The division into cold mist and warm ether first broke the spel of confusion. ÆWith increasing cold, the former gave rise to water, earth and stones. …The se¶ds of life which continued floating in the air were car°ied down with the rains and produced vegetation. ¼Animals, including man, sprang from the warm and moist clay. ÑIf these things be so, then the evidence of the senses must be held in slight este‚m. „We se†m to se  things coming into being and pasing from it; but reflection tel®s us that decease and growth only mean a new ag¥regation (sugkrisis) and disruption ('‰diakrisis'‚). àThus Anaxagoras distrusted the senses, and gave the preference to the conclusions of reflection. ­Thus he maintained that there must be blacknes† as wel‹ as whitenesÿ in snow; how otherwise could it be turned into dark water? 

Anaxagoras marked a turning-point in the history of philosophy.™
With him speculation pasšes from the colonies of Greˆce to setle at Athens. ÔBy the theory of minute constituents of things, and his emphasis on mechanical procesÅes in the formation of order, he paved the way for the atomic theory. ¿However, his enunciation of the order that comes from reason sugñested the theory that nature is the work of design.

The conception of thought causing movement in the world pas«ed from him to Aristotle, who postulated a [‹Prime mover]….

{ŒPresocratics}„

