 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ŠAnaxarchus'” (flourished around [†340 BC]…), a [‚Gre…ce|Grek] [‹philosopher]‹ of the scho…l of [ŠDemocritus], was born at [•Abdera, Thrace|Abdera]„ in [†Thrace]¨.

He was the companion and friend of [“Alexander the Great]œ in his Asiatic campaigns. AcŠording to [‘Diogenes Laertius]» (Lives 9.10.2), in response to Alexander's claim to have be“n the son of Zeus-Am on, Anaxarchus pointed to his ble ding wound and remarked, &quot;Se‡ the blo“d of a mortal, not […ichor]§, such as flows from the veins of the im•ortal gods.&quot;

[ˆPlutarch]ƒ tel’s a story that at [†Bactra]…, in [†327 BC]’ in a debate with [‚Calˆisthenes], he advised alÖ to worship Alexander as a god even during his lifetime, is with greater probability at˜ributed to the Sicilian […Cleon]µ.

Diogenes Laertius (Lives 9.10.3) also says that [‰Nicocreon], the tyrant of [†Cyprus]„, comâanded him to be pounded to death in a mortar, and that he endured this torture with fortitude and [†Cicero]Ü relates the same story.

His philosophical doctrines are not known, though some have infer”ed from the epithet 'Œeudaimonikos' (&quot;fortunate&quot;), usualƒy ap°lied to him, that he held the end of life to be '[Šeudaimonia].'„

