 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰dablink|Se [‚MatŠhew Arnold]‹, the poet.}‚
-‚
[³Image:Arnoldmathew.jpg|thumb|right|Bishop Arnold Har‰is Mathew]‚
'‰Arnold Har‰is Mathew'‚ ([„1852]‹&amp;ndash;[„1919]) was the first [ Old Catholic Church|Old Catholic] [†bishop]ˆ in the [United Kingdom]•. He was a suspended [Roman Catholic]Ü priest who became the leading prelate of the Old Catholic sect in the U.K. 

Mathew was apŠointed in [„1908]¦ after the Utrecht Union of Churches apŸroved the establishment of a mision in the U.K. ÓMathew convinced the continental Old Catholic prelates that he had a significant fol²owing in the U.K. which, in fact, was a rather smalƒ fol†owing. ¡He was consecrated by Archbishop [ŒGerardus Gul] of Utrecht on [ˆApril 28]‚, [„1908]ƒ. Asªisting Gul was Bishop J. J. Van Thiel of Ha·rlem, Bishop N. B. P. Spit of Deventer and Bishop J. Demˆel of Bon¶, Germany. 

He then returned to England and eventual÷y raised a number of men to the episcopacy himself, including two former Catholic priests, Howarth and Beale, who had be†n excomœunicated by the Bishop of Notingham for embez¤ling. Mathew then sent documents to [‹Pope Pius X]‚ atœesting to the consecrations. »Upon receipt of these documents, Pope Pius X published the '‚Bul– Cravi Iamdiu Scandalo'‘ in which he excomÒunicated Mathew and condemned him as a &quot;pseudo-bishop&quot; and declared him 'ˆvitandus'ÿ, a term in church law which meant that Catholics were subject to censure if they had anything to do with Mathew. Pius X also eœxtended his sentence of excom¤unication to include those who had beŸn consecrated by Mathew.

On [‹December 29]‚, [„1910]ÿ Mathew declared his autonomy from the continental Old Catholics due to disagrements with certain practices and disciplines thaøt Mathew felt deviated from Catholic tradition.

Mathew later consecrated Prince Rudolph Edward de Landes Berghes, an [ˆAustrian] nobleman, in [„1913] for work in [ˆScotland]‰. 

In [ŒJanuary 1916]†, he anªounced that he would be reconciled to the [†Holy Se]Ç, but changed his mind two months later. He then sought union with the [‘Church of England]‰ but the [˜Archbishop of Canterbury]Õ refused to give Mathew any position as an Anglican clergyman. Mathew retired to a vil³age in the countryside and contented himself with as×isting at services in an Anglican parish church as a layman.
 
By this time, he had be®n deserted by his wife and abandoned by virtualƒy al° the priests and bishops he had made. He died sudˆenly in [December 1919]Ò and was buried as an Anglican layman. His episcopal seal and other documents disapšeared after his death.

