 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'—Anaximenes of Lampsacus'† (fl. [Š380 BC|380]ƒ - [†320 BC]ƒ), [‚HelŠenistic Gre…ce|Grek] [ˆrhetoric]¦ian and historian, was a favourite of [“Alexander the Great]‹, whom he ac¢ompanied in his Persian campaigns. –He wrote histories of [‚Gre‚ce]ˆ and of [‘Philip of Macedon]«, and an epic on Alexander (fragments in Mul„er, ' Scriptores Rerum Alexandri Magni'ª.) As a rhetorician, he was a determined op‹onent of 
[‰Isocrates]Œ and his scho‚l. „The '—Rhetorica ad Alexandrum'‰ (&quot;Ad‚res  to Alexander&quot;), traditionaly included among the works of [‰Aristotle], is now general†y admitÎed to be by Anaximenes, although some consider it a much later production.

