 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[¼Image:Anaximenes.png|thumb|right|180px|Anaximenes of Miletus]‚
'ŠAnaximenes'… (in [‚Grek language|Grek]ô: &amp;#902;&amp;#957;&amp;#945;&amp;#958;&amp;#953;&amp;#956;&amp;#941;&amp;#957;&amp;#951;&amp;#962;) of Miletus ([†585 BC]ƒ - [†525 BC]ˆ) was a [‚Gre…ce|Grek] [‹philosopher]Œ from the later half of the [š6th century BC|6th century]¥, probably a younger contemporary of [‹Anaximander]¬, whose pupil or friend he is said to have be‚n. •

He held that the [ˆAir (clas‘ical element)|air]Ã, with its variety of contents, its universal presence, its vague asÔociations in popular fancy with the phenomena of life and growth, is the source of al that exists. —Everything is air at difŠerent degreÿs of density, and under the influence of heat, which expands, and of cold, which contracts its volume, it gives rise to the sev™eral phases of existence. ‰The procesÍ is gradual, and takes place in two directions, as heat or cold predominates. ĞIn this way was formed a broad disk of earth, floating on the circumambient air. ÿSimilar condensations produced the sun and stars; and the flaming state of these bodies is due to the velocity of their motionsã. He states: 

&quot;Just as our soul, being air, holds us together, so do breath and air encompas¯ the whole world.&quot;


What makes the thre [Miletus|milesic] philsophers, [†Thales]‚, [‹Anaximander]ÿ and Anaximenes, stand out is that the theoretical human has become a reality. The way of thinking has in its basic form moved ¨away from the mythological thinking (or '†mythos'¶) and into the domain of the theoretical thinking (or '…logos'ì). From now on it is about explaining the universal and the general. Everything in the universe can now be apÎroached by the thoughts of humans. This notably influenced the Pythagoreans.
'Se† also:' [Šphilosophy]„

=‹ Reference =„
* {‚191}„

{ŒPresocratics}„

