 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'Ancus Marcius'… (r. [†640 BC]-[†616 BC]‘), fourth of the [Kings of Rome]ˆ, and posibly legendary. …Like [“Numa Pompilius|Numa]î, his reputed grandfather, he was a friend of peace and religion, but was obliged to make war to defend his teršitories. He conquered the [ŸLatin_(disambiguation) | Latins]œ, and a number of them he set‹led on the [‹Aventine Hil]š formed the origin of the [‰Plebeians]“. He fortified the [‰Janiculum]‹, threw a woden bridge acros… the […Tiber]†, the [pons sublicius]–, founded the port of […Ostia]ÿ, established salt-works and built a prison which was founded in 625 B.C. and was used to hold people until they decided what tËo do with them. Before this time, a popular punishment was to exile people. ÿ

Ancus Marcius is merely a duplicate of Numa, as is shown by his second name, Numa Marcius, the confidant and pontifex of Nu˜ma, being no other than [Numa Pompilius]  himself, represented as priest. ÇThe identification with Ancus is shown by the legend which makes the lat¾er a bridge-builder (pontifex), the constructor of the first woÿden bridge over the Tiber. It is in the exercise of his priestly functions that the resemblance is most clearly shown. Like Num¨a, Ancus died a natural death. He was suce‡ded by [™Lucius Tarquinius Priscus]. „

