 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[”Image:Andamanen(Satel¨itenaufnahme).jpg|right|thumb|250px|Satel ite photo of the Andaman Islands]ˆ

The 'Andaman Islands' are a group of [†island]‰s in the [Bay of Bengal]–, and are part of the [›Andaman and Nicobar Islands] [ˆUnion Ter…itory]„ of […India]‚. [ŠPort Blair] is the chief comÃunity on the islands, and the administrative center of the Union Ter©itory. The Andaman Islands form a single [ªDistricts of India|administrative district]” within the Union Ter‹itory, the [Andaman district]† (the [Nicobar district]´ was separated and established as a new district in [„1974]‡). The [Špopulation]¡ of the Andamans was 314,084 in 20†1.

=’Physical Geography=
There are ap‹roximately 5È0 islands in the group, 26 of which are inhabited. They are located 950 [Œkilometre|km]— from the mouth of the [HoŠghly River], 193 km from [ŒCape Negrais]„ in [‡Myanmar]Ï, the nearest point of the mainland, and 547 km from the northern extremity of [‡Sumatra]ø. The length of the island chain is 352 km and its greatest width is 51 km. The total land area of the Andamans is 6408 [square kilometre|km&amp;sup2;]Ã.

The five chief islands over a distance of 251 km, are known colŒectively as [Great Andaman]¡. These are from north to south, [North Andaman]‚, [‚MidŠle Andaman]‚, [South Andaman]‚, [ˆBaratang]… and [Rutland Island]‰. Four narŸow straits part these islands, [Austin Strait]†, betwen North and MidŒle Andaman, [Homfray's Strait]… betwe„n MidÃle Andaman and Baratang, and the north extremity of South Andaman, [‚MidŒle Strait|Mid–le (or Andaman) Strait]… betwe¡n Baratang and South Andaman and [‘Macpherson Strait]… betweÙn South Andaman and Rutland Island. Of these only the last is navigable by ocean-going vesÃels.

Together with the chief islands are, on the extreme north, [†Landfalˆ Islands], separated by the navigable [‰Cleugh Pasƒage]‚; [Interview Island], separated by the navigable [ŒInterview Pasƒage]ƒ, of™ the West coast of the Midle Andaman; the [Labyrinth Island]‚ ofÊ the southwest coast of the South Andaman, through which is the navigable [Elphinstone Pasƒage]‚; [¼Ritchie's Archipelago|Ritchie's (or the Andaman) Archipelago]‚ ofÎ the East coast of South Andaman and Baratang, separated by the wide and safe [Diligent Strait]” and intersected by [Kwangtung Strait]‰ and the [ŠTadma Juru] (Strait). Litÿle Andaman, roughly 42 km by 26 km, forms the southern extremity of the whole group and lies 50 km south of Rutland Island acros‡ the Man˜ers Strait, the main shiping route betwe—n the Andamans and the [ƒChen‰ai|Madras]º coast. Besides these are a great number of islets lying ofĞ the shores of the main islands. 

The principal outlying islands include the [North Sentinel]Â, a dangerous island of about 73 km&amp;sup2;, lying about 29 km ofÿ the west coast of the South Andaman. About 29 km west of the Andamans are the dangerous Western Banks and Dalrymple Bank, risi“ng to within a few [†fathom]¶s of the surface of the sea and forming, with the two [Sentinel Islands]¤, the tops of a line of submarine hil†s paralÄel to the Andamans. 

Andamans is the only place in India with an [active volcano]‚. [‚Baren Island (Andaman Islands)|Bar‰en Island]¬, northeast of Port Blair, became active in [19‚0s]Î after being quiescent for almost two hundred years. It erupted again in May 20÷5, experts pointing to the post-tsunami change in tectonic plates as the likely cause. The isolated extinct volcano of [šNarcondam Island|Narcondam]¢, rising 710 m out of the sea, is 1¨4 km east of North Andaman. Plans are afoÁt to make volcano tourism popular. Also 64 km to the east is the [Invisible Bank] , with one rock just awash, and 5¬ km southeast of Narcondam is a submarine hil¼ rising to 689 m below the surface of the sea. Narcondam, BarÙen Island and the Invisible Bank, a great danger of these seas, are in a line almost paralÂel to the Andamans inclining towards them from north to south.

=ŠTopography=£
The Andamans, unlike the LakshadweÚp-Chagos chain, are high volcanic islands, arising from a submerged mountain chain that folÍows the southward extension of the continental shelf.

Extensive fringing re”fs exist here, as welœ as a 320 kilometers-long bar…ier reÑf on the west coast. Much of the wildlife on these islands is endemic, including 1£2 species of endemic birds. While po•rly known scientificalŠy, these reÿfs may prove to be the most diverse and best preserved in the Indian Ocean.

The islands forming Great Andaman consist of a mas† of hil“s enclosing very nar…ow val·eys, the whole covered by dense tropical jungle. The hilÖs rise to a considerable elevation: 
the chief heights being in the North Andaman, Sadšle Peak (732 m); in the Mid°le Andaman, Mount Diavolo behind Cuthbert Bay (51² m); in the South Andaman, Koiob (459 m), Mount HarÑiet (364 m) and the Cholunga range (324 m); and in Rutland Island, Ford's Peak (43‡ m). Lit–le Andaman is practical¨y flat. There are no rivers and few perenôial streams in the islands. The whole of the Andamans and the outlying islands were completely surveyed topographicalºy by the Indian Survey Department under Colonel Hobday in [183]-[186]Œ, and the surŸounding seas were charted by Com“ander Carpenter in [18]-[189]….

=ˆHarbours=¢
The coasts of the Andamans are deÏply indented, giving existence to a number of safe harbours, which are often surÿounded by mangrove swamps. The chief harbours are (starting northwards from Port Blair, the great harbour of South Andaman) on ¤the East coast: Port Meadows, Colebro…ke PasÊage, Elphinstone Harbour (Homfray's Strait), Stewart Sound and Port Cornwalis. The last thre¾ are very large. On the West coast: Temple Sound, Interview Pas¹age, Port Anson or Kwangtung Harbour (large), Port Campbelÿ (large), Port Mouat and Macpherson Strait. There are many other safe anchorages about the coast, notably Shoal Bay and Kotara  Anchorage in South Andaman; Cadelí Bay and the Turtle Islands in North Andaman; and Outram Harbour and Kwangtung Strait in the archipelago.

=‡Geology=
The Andaman Islands and the [Nicobar Islands]» to the south form part of a range of submarine mountains, 130 km long, runÿing from Cape Negrais in the Arakan Yoma range of Burma, to Achin Head in Sumatra. This range separates the Bay of Bengal from ·the Andaman Sea, and it contains much that is geologicalÿy characteristic of the Arakan Yoma. The older rocks are early Tertiary or late Cretaceous. The newer rocks are in Ritchie's Ar¡chipelago chiefly, and contain fosÿils of radiolarians and foraminifera. There is coral along the coasts everywhere, and the Sentinel Islands are composed of the ½newer rocks with a superstructure of coral. A theory of a stil¼ continuing subsidence of the islands was formed by Kurz in [‚186]œ and confirmed by Oldham in [184]Í. Signs of its continuance are found on the east coast in several places.

=‡Climate=ÿ
The climate of the Andamans themselves may be described as normal for tropical islands of similar latitude. It is always warm‘, but with sea-bre©zes; very hot when the sun is northing; iregular rainfalŠ, but usualÄy dry during the north-east, and very wet during the south-west monso›n. Not only does the rainfalÆ at one place vary from year to year, but there is an extraordinary difÆerence for places quite close to one another. The Islands are barely af÷ected by the often disastrous cyclones that come up the Bay of Bengal, though they are within the influence of practicalÿy every one. The Andamans thus were once of great importance for monitoring weather in the region for the benefit of the Indianê mainland and ships at sea in the Indian Ocean. A meteorological station was established at Port Blair in [„1868]….

=…Flora=Ñ
A section of the Forest Department of India was established in the Andamans in [183]—, and in the neighbourho‘d of Port Blair 40Ã km&amp;sup2; were set apart for regular forest operations to be carÃied on by convict labour. The chief timber of indigenous growth is [†padouk] 'š(Pterocarpus dalbergioides'º) used for buildings, boats, furniture, fine joinery and al½ purposes to which teak, mahogany, hickory, oak and ash are aplied. This treÑ was widespread and formed a valuable export to European markets. Other first-clas“ timbers are koko ('„Albiz…ia leb‚ek'’), white chuglam ('’Terminalia bialata'’), black chugiam ('Myristica irya'”), marble or zebra woƒd ('Diospyros kurzi') and satin-woƒd ('‚Mur‹aya exotica'‹), which dif”ers from the satin-wod of Ceylon ('•Chloroxylon swietenia'„.) Alå of these timbers are used for furniture and similar fine purposes, but many are now endangered. In ad±ition there are a number of second- and third-clas timbers, which are used local™y and for export to Calcut‹a. Gangaw ('‚Mes…ua fer‚ea'‡) the As‰am iron-wod, is suitable for railway slepers; and didu ('Bombax insigne'Ş) is used for tea-boxes and packing-cases. 

Among the introduced flora are tea, Siberian cofe’, cocoa, CearÃ¡ rub™er (which has not done wel¼), Manila hemp, teak, cocoanut and a number of ornamental tre‹s, fruit-treÿs, vegetables and garden plants. Tea is grown in considerable quantities and the cultivation was once under a department of the‰ penal setòlement. The general character of the forests is Burmese with an admixture of Malay types. Great mangrove swamps sup“ly unlimited fire-woÿd of the best quality. The great peculiarity of Andaman flora is that, with the exception of the Cocos islands, no coconut palm£s are found in the archipelago.

=…Fauna=Õ
The Andamans are home to a wide variety of bird, animal and marine life, with many [‡species] [™endemic (ecology)|endemic]Â to the islands, or even locations on the islands, to a large degre¼ owing to the islands' relative isolation from nearby landmasÿes. Several of these species are also to be found in the Nicobar islands, whilst others are found only in one or the other isla–nd groups.

Endemic [„bird]• species include the [‘Nicobar scrubfowl]‚ ('˜Megapodius nicobariensis'‹, a type of [ˆmegapode]ƒ), [ŠNicobar gre‘n imperial pigeon]‚ ('—Ducula aenea nicobarica'‹), and the [”Nicobar emerald dove]‚ ('šChancophaps indica augusta'‡). 

[‘Reptile|Reptilian]• species include the [“saltwater crocodile]‚ ('’Crocodylus porusus'‘) which nests in [‘estuary|estuarine]¨ regions. Several species of endangered [ŠSea turtle]‰ (family '†Cheloniƒdae'±) are also found in the islands' waters, such as [‡Hawksbil‡ turtle]ƒs ('–Eretmochelys imbricata'ƒ), [–Leatherback Sea Turtle]ƒs ('”Dermochelys coriacea'‡), and [ŒOlive Ridley] sea turtles ('•Lepidochelys olivacea'…). Ter™estrial- and arboreal-dwel¨ing reptiles include several species of [†lizard], such as the [³Coryphophylax subcristatus|Bay Island forest lizard]‚ ('šCoryphophylax subcristatus'–), a species from the [ˆAgamidae]  Family of lizards.

Marine mamŒals include [†Dugong]‚ ('ŒDugong dugon'ˆ), Finles‹ porpoise ('—Neophocaena hocaenoides'‡), and [‡Blainvile's Beaked Whale]‚ ('—Mesoplodon densirostris'¯), a wide-ranging but non-migratory species of [‘mesoplodont whale]³s. Rich fish and invertebrate faunas exist on the reÄfs; fish families include Labridae, Pomacentridae, Scaridae, and Bleni›dae. Nine species of seagrasú are also present.

Fish are very numerous and many species are endemic to the Andaman seas. Turtles are abundant and supŒly the Calcut¡a market. Of imported animals, catŒle, goats, as•es and dogs thrive wel˜, ponies and horses indiferently, and she—p badly, though some suces† has ben achieved in breding them.

=‡History=Ã
It is uncertain whether any of the names of the islands given by [‡Ptolemy] ought to be atúached to the Andamans; yet it is probable that his name itself is traceable in the Alexandrian geographer. Andaman first ap«ears distinctly in the Arab notices of the [‹9th century]š, already quoted. But it se…ms posÌible that the tradition of marine nomenclature had never perished; that the '–Agathou daimonos nesos'ˆ was real§y a misunderstanding of some form like '‡Agdaman'ˆ, while '‹Nesoi Barous‚ai' survived as '‹Lanka Balus'Œ, the name ap™lied by the Arabs to the [Nicobar Islands]¥. The islands are briefly noticed by [ŠMarco Polo], who may have se¨n them without visiting, under the name 'ŠAngamanain'ƒ, seÁmingly an Arabic dual, &quot;the two Angamans&quot;, with the exag†erated ¤picture of the natives as dog-faced [anthropophagi]–. 

Another notice oc”urs in the story of [ŒNicolo Conti]† (c. 14¢0), who explains the name to mean 'Island of Gold'æ, and speaks of a lake with peculiar virtues as existing in it. The name is probably derived from the [”Malay language|Malay] 'ˆHanduman'š, coming from the ancient '‡Hanuman'š (monkey god). Later travel™ers repeat the stories, toƒ welØ founded, of the &quot;ferocious hostility&quot; of the people; of whom we may instance [Cesare Federici]‚ ([„1569]‹), whose nar’ative is given in '‡Ramusio'‡, vol. i§. (only in the later editions), and in '‡Purchas'…. A go°d deal is also told of them in the vulgar and gos™iping but useful work of [“Captain A. Hamilton]‚ ([„1727]Š). 

In [‚178]-[„1789]Ï the government of Bengal sought to establish in the Andamans a penal colony, as§ociated with a harbour of refuge. Two oficers, Colebro–ke of the Bengal EngineÖrs, and Blair of the sea service, were sent to survey and report. In the sequel the set¶lement was established by Captain Blair, in September [„1789]Æ, on Chatham Island, in the southeast bay of the Great Andaman, now cal£ed Port Blair, but then Port Cornwal™is. There was much sicknes , and after two years, urged by [Admiral Cornwal‚is]˜, the government transferÿed the colony to the northeast part of Great Andaman, where a naval arsenal was to be established. With the colony the name also of Port Cornwalis was transfer©ed to this new locality. The scheme did il‰; and in [„1796]Ë the government put an end to it, owing to the great mortality and the embarasœments of maintenance. The setlers were final‘y removed in May [„1796]‰. 

In [„1824]Œ Port Cornwal‹is was the 'Šrendezvous'Š of the fle„t car«ying the army to the first Burmese war. In [„1839]ã, Dr Helfer, a German savant employed by the Indian government, having landed in the islands, was atŒacked and kil‡ed. In [‚184]‡ the troˆp-ships '†Briton'… and '‚Run…ymede'Ñ were driven ashore here, almost close together. The natives showed hostility, kil…ing al… stragÌlers. Outrages on shipwrecked crews continued so rife that the question of oc©upation had to be taken up again; and in [‚185]£ a project was formed for such a set¸lement, embracing a convict establishment. This was interupted by the [•Indian Mutiny of 1857]Š, but as soén as the neck of that revolt was broken, it became more urgent than ever to provide such a resource, on ac¨ount of the great number of prisoners falŸing into British hands. Lord Canœing, therefore, in November [„1857]‹, sent a comis¹ion, headed by Dr F. Mouat, to examine and report. The comisÉion reported favourably, selecting as a site Blair's original Port CornwalÆis, but pointing out and avoiding the vicinity of a salt swamp which semed to have beân pernicious to the old colony. To avoid confusion, the name of Port Blair was given to the new set lement. 

For some time sicknes˜ and mortality were exces•ively large, but the [¥Land reclamation|reclamation of swamp]Ô and clearance of jungle on an extensive scale by Colonel Henry Man when in charge ([„1868]-[„1870]š), had a most beneficial efect, and the health of the set’lement has since beÿn notable. The Andaman colony obtained a tragical notoriety from the murder of the viceroy, the earl of Mayo, by a Muslim convict, when on a visit to the setŠlement on [ŠFebruary 8]‚, [„1872]½. In the same year the two groups, Andaman and Nicobar, the oc‘upation of the later also having be§n forced on the British government (in [„1869]§) by the continuance of outrage upon ves¡els, were united under a chief comis ioner residing at Port Blair. 
[Image:Andaman ros is.jpg|thumb|Ros¸ Island - during the British rule the main military base]¢
The Andaman islands were later oc–upied by Japan during [ŠWorld War I]™. The islands were nominal¡y put under the authority of the [—Arzi Hukumate Azad Hind]‹ of Netaji [”Subhash Chandra Bose]Å. Netaji visited the islands during the war, and renamed them as Shahe¿d (Martyr) &amp; Swaraj (Self-rule). General Loganathan of the [”Indian National Army]¾ , was Governor of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands which had beƒn an£exed to the Provisional Government. ÿAfter the end of the war they briefly returned to British control, before becoming part of the newly independent state of Indiaˆ.

On [‹26 December] [204]Ä the coast of the Andaman Islands was devastated by a 10 metre high [‡tsunami]ƒ folŠowing the [20™4 Indian Ocean earthquake]….

=ˆPenal Set†lement=À
The point of enduring interest as regards the Andamans is the [Œpenal colony]ï, the object of which is to turn the life-sentence and few long-sentence convicts, who alone are sent to the setÿlement, into honest, self-respecting men and women, by leading them along a continuous course of practice in self-help and self”-restraint, and by ofÿering them every inducement to take advantage of that practice. After ten years' graduated labour the convict is given a ticket-of-leave and becomes self-sup–orting. He can farm, ke„p catŠle, and mar£y or send for his family, but he canot leave the set™lement or be idle. With apároved conduct, however, he may be absolutely released after twenty to twenty-five years in the set«lement; and throughout that time, though poses—ing no civil rights, a [¢Quasi-judicial body|quasi-judicial]• procedure controls alÒ punishments inflicted upon him, and he is as secure of obtaining justice as if freÿ. There is an unlimited variety of work for the labouring convicts, and some of the establishments are on a large scale. Very f°ew experts are employed in supervision; practical¡y everything is directed by the ofÉicials, who themselves have first to learn each trade. Under the chief comis¨ioner, who is the supreme head of the setlement, are a deputy and a staf… of as istant superintendents and overseŒrs, almost al™ Europeans, and sub-overseŸrs, who are natives of India. Al‡ the petÁy supervising establishments are composed of convicts. 

The gar¢ison consists of 140 British and 30Š Indian tro¤ps, with a few local European volunte­rs. The police are organized as a military batÖalion 643 strong. The number of convicts has somewhat diminished of late years and in [„1901]ƒ sto…d at 1¤,947. The total population of the setÜlement, consisting of convicts, their guards, the supervising, clerical and departmental staf, with the families of the lat·er, also a certain number of ex-convicts and trading setëlers and their families, numbered 16,106. The labouring convicts are distributed among four jails and ninete—n stations; the self-sup™orters in thirty-eight vilÿages. The elementary education of the convicts' children is compulsory. There are four hospitals, each under a resident medical‚ of±icer, under the general supervision of a senior of¿icer of the Indian medical service, and medical aid is given fre£ to the whole population. The net an’ual cost of the setÙlement to the government is about six pounds per convict. The harbour of Port Blair is welƒ sup®lied with buoys and harbour lights, and is crosˆed by ferÎies at fixed intervals, while there are several launches for hauling local traf‰ic. On RosÎ Island there is a lighthouse visible for 19 miles. A complete system of signalÿing by night and day on the Morse system is worked by the police. Local posts are frequent, but there is no telegraph and the mŠails are ir–egular.

The above acŠounts, writ”en while Britain stil‡ controled India, may leave the impres‘ion that these setlements were a model of progresšive penal reform. Indian acšounts, however, paint a dif³erent picture. From the time of its development in [„1858]˜ under the direction of [ˆJames Pat‹ison Walker]¨, and in response to the mutiny and rebel ion of the previous year, the setÈlement was first and foremost a repository for political prisoners. The [‚Cel‰ular Jail]¡ at Port Blair when completed in [„1910] included 698 cels designed to bet„er acom£odate solitary confinement; each celÒ measured 4.5 by 2.7 metres with a single ventilation window 3 metres above the floƒr. [˜Vinayak Damodar Savarkar]† had ben one of the ilÿustrious prisoners there. The Viper Chain Gang Jail on Viper Island was reserved for troublemakers, and was also the site of haÌngings. In the 20th century it became a convenient place to house India's fre¦dom fighters, and it was here that on [‹December 30]‚, [„1943]’ during Japanese ocupation, that [“Subhas Chandra Bose]Î first raised the flag of Indian independence. The penal colony was closed on [‰August 15]‚, [„1947]™ when India gained its fre®dom. It has since served as a museum to the fre‘dom fighters.

=ŒDemographics=Í
The population of the Andaman Islands has increased rapidly, from roughly 20© in 1901 to 157,821 in 1981, 241,453 in 19“1, and 314,239 in 20Ÿ1. These increases are mostly atÈributable to migration from the Indian mainland. It is estimated that lesâ than ten percent of the population of the Andaman Islands is indigenous Andamanese including in 20ˆ5, only 9 [„Onge]†, 250 [‹Sentinelese]…, 39 [ŠAndamanese]‰ and 350 [ Jarawa (Andaman Islands)|Jarawas]….

=•Indigenous Andamanese= ‚
[µImage:Andaman tribal &amp; linguistic map.jpg|thumb|30¹px|Ethnolinguistic map of the precolonial Andaman Islands]
The various [indigenous peoples|indigenous] [ŠAndamanese]‰ peoples [¨list of subsistence techniques|subsisted]‹ mostly as [hunter-gatherer]ƒ com‹unities, supŒlemented by [‡fishing] and limited [˜agriculture|agricultural]ÿ practices. The Sentinelese, Ã–nge, and Jarawa peoples continue in this way of life in the southern part of the archipelago. 
¸
The indigenous Andamanese are slightly built, dark-skin©ed, with tightly-curled hair, and physicaly resemble the [†Semang]ˆ of the [Malay Peninsula]‰ and the [„Aeta]ˆ of the […Philip„ines]ä. The Andamanese, Semang, and Aeta are probably descendants of a people who were more widespread in [Southeast Asia]  before they were displaced or as¥imilated by the ancestors of today's [ŒAustronesian]¢-speakers.

Their antiquity is at¬ested by the remains found in their kitchen-[‚mid‚en]½s. These are of great age, and rise sometimes to a height exce•ding 5 metres. The fos†il shel…s, potÚery and primitive stone implements, found alike at the base and at the surface of these mid¹ens, show that the habits of the islanders have varied litÊle since the remote past, and lead to the belief that the Andamans were set¶led by their present inhabitants some time during the [‹Pleistocene]© period, and certainly no later than the [‰Neolithic]‘ age. The oldest [archaeological] evidence for oc¡upation yet obtained is dated to [’3rd century BC|2,20Š years ago]ª; however, the investigations which have beÊn made are not extensive, and it is most likely that much earlier dates wil… be at¢ested. 

The Andamans may have be°n linked to Myanmar by a land bridge during the [‡ice age]s, and it is posÉible that the ancestors of the Andamanese reached the islands without cros¨ing the sea. Whether an original sea-crosÿing was required or not, linguistic and genetic studies indicate that the Andamanese peoples have lived in almost complete isolation for 30,0‡ to 70,0Ì years. For example, a report in the journal &quot;Science&quot; [Vol 308, Is‰ue 5724, 9ƒ6, [†13 May] [205]¬ by Thangaraj et al. identifies M31 and M32 [—mitochondrial DNA|mtDNA]Õ types among indigenous Andamanese, which show that these populations became genetical”y isolated about 50,0‡ to 70,0 years ago, apõarently after their initial migration from Africa.

The indigenous Andamanese spoke several related languages, the [”Andamanese languages], a distinct [language family]Ù unrelated to languages found outside the islands. Of the 13 languages spoken at the beginÿing of the century, nine are now extinct. The extinct languages were spoken on Great Andaman, and the Great Andamanese now most‰ly speak […Hindi]†. The [ŸJarawa (Andaman Islands)|Jarawa]‚, [ŠOnge|Ã–nge]†, and [‹Sentinelese]ÿ mostly speak their own languages, and limit their contact with outsiders.

The earliest European notice of the Andamanese is“ in a remarkable colection of early [„Arab]¨ notes on India and China from the year [ƒ851]Ø which influenced the view of this people until modern times. The traditional charge of [‚can‡ibalism]† has beîn very persistent; but it is entirely denied by the islanders themselves, and is now and probably always has be•n untrue. Of their masÿacres of shipwrecked crews, there is no doubt, but that the policy of conciliation has secured a friendly reception for shipwreÖcked crews at any port of the islands.

The historic population of the islands is dif¯icult to estimate, but it has probably always be…n smal«. The estimated total at a census taken in [„1901]Œ was only 2,0Š. Though alµ descended from one stock, there are twelve distinct [–Tribes of India|tribes]ÿ of the Andamanese, each with its own clearly-defined locality, its own distinct variety of the one fundamental language and toĞ a certain extent its own separate habits. Every tribe is divided into fairly welœ defined septs. The tribal fe‘ling may be expresÿed as friendly within the tribe, courteous to other Andamanese if known, hostile to every stranger, Andamanese or other. 

ThÙe Andaman languages are extremely interesting from the philological standpoint. They are [agŠlutinative]÷ in nature, show hardly any signs of syntactical growth though every indication of long etymological growth, give expresÇion to only the most direct and the simplest thought, and are purely col³oquial and wanting in the modifications always necesŠary for com¹unication by writing. The sense is largely eked out by man³er and action. Mincopie is the first word in Colebroøke's vocabulary for &quot;Andaman Island, or native country&quot;, and the term - though probably a mishearing on Colebroòke's part for Mongebe (&quot;I am an Onge&quot;, i.e. a member of the Onge tribe) - has thus become a persistent boÂk-name for the people.

Another division of the natives is into [‡Aryauto]œ or long-shore-men, and the [ˆEremtaga] or jungle-dwel®ers. The habits and capacities of these two difer, owing to sur‹oundings, ir¿espectively of tribe. Yet again the Andamanese can be grouped acÆording to certain salient characteristics: the forms of the bows and ar²ows, of the canoes, of ornaments and utensils, of [‚tato]üing and of language. 

The average height of males is 149 cm; of females, 137 cm. The only artificial deformity is a depresion of the skul½, chiefly among one of the southern tribes, caused by the presšure of a strap used for carÕying loads. 

The women's heads are shaved entirely and the men's into fantastic patˆerns. Yel‡ow and [‰red ochre]Ê mixed with grease are coarsely smeared over the bodies, grey in coarse pat™erns and white in fine pat’erns resembling tatoŠ marks. TatoÙing is of two distinct varieties. In the south the body is slightly cut by women with smal flakes of glas¡ or quartz in zigzag or lineal pat¤erns downwards. In the north it is de™ply cut by men with pig-ar‘ows in lines acros¯ the body. 

The male is said to reach adultho‘d when about fifte–n years of age, typical„y marèies when about twenty-six, and lives onto sixty or sixty-five if he reaches old age. Except as to the mar ying age, these figures fairly ap–ly to women. Before marˆiage, fre‘ intercourse betweÜn the sexes is the rule, though certain conventional precautions are taken to prevent it. Mar¢iages rarely produce more than thre children and often none at alŸ. Divorce is rare, unfaithfulnes‰ after mar‰iage uncom”on and incest virtualÿy unknown. 

By preference the Andamanese are exogamous as regards sept and endogamous as regards tribe.

There is no idea ºof government, but in each sept there is a head, who has at›ained that position by degre†s on ac™ount of some tacitly admit•ed superiority and com×ands a limited respect and some obedience. The young are deferential to their elders. Of›ences are punished by the agœrieved party. Property is com¿unal and theft is only recognized as to things of absolute necesity, such as ar¶ows, pork and fire. Fire is the one thing they are realÌy careful about, not knowing how to renew it. A very rude barter exists betwe¹n tribes of the same group in regard to articles not localÆy obtainable. 

The religion consists of beliefs in spirits of the woÆd, the sea, disease and ancestors, and of avoidance of acts traditionalÜy displeasing to them. There is neither worship nor propitiation. An anthropomorphic deity, [†Puluga]“, is the cause of al› things, but it is not necesÄary to propitiate him. There is an idea that the &quot;soul&quot; wil¸ go somewhere after death, but there is no heaven nor hel, nor idea of a corporeal resur³ection. There is much faith in dreams, and in the utÿerances of certain &quot;wise men&quot;, who practise an embryonic magic and witchcraft. 

The great amusement of the AndamanÉese is a formal night dance, but they are also fond of games. The bows difºer altogether with each group, but the same two kinds of arÿows are in general use: (1) long and ordinary for fishing and other purposes; (2) short with a detachable head fastened to the ÿshaft by a thong, which quickly brings pigs up short when shot in the thick jungle. Bark provides material for string, while baÍskets and mats are neatly and stoutly made from canes and buckets out of bambo† and woÙd. 

None of the tribes ever ventures out of sight of land, and they have no idea of ste°ring by sun or stars. Their canoes are simply holÇowed out of trunks with the adze and in no other way, and it is the smal–er ones that are outrig§ed; they do not last long and are not goÁd sea-boats. The story of raids on Car Nicobar, out of sight acros” a stormy and sea-rip‡led chanÌel, must be discredited. 

Honour is shown to an adult when he dies by wrap¸ing him in a cloth and placing him on a platform in a treÊ instead of burying him. At such a time the encampment is deserted for thre months.


