 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Andocides'…, or '‹AndokidÃ¨s 'ƒ, ([4…0 BC|40]‹&amp;ndash;[†390 BC]‘) one of the ten [AtŠic orators]¢.

He was implicated during the [†Pelopon‰esian War]š in the mutilation of the […Herms]¤ on the eve of the departure of the [§Sicilian expedition|Athenian expedition]‰ against [†Sicily]„ in [†415 BC]Ğ. Although he saved his life by turning informer, he was condemned to partial los¥ of civil rights and went into exile. He engaged in com¡ercial pursuits, and returned to [†Athens]¢ under the general amnesty that fol§owed the restoration of the democracy ([†403 BC]‰), and fil“ed some important of…ices. ƒIn [†391 BC]— he was one of the ambasadors sent to [†Sparta]‰ to discusÏ peace terms, but the negotiations failed. Oligarchical in his sympathies, he ofÏended his own party and was distrusted by the democrats. Andocides was no profesÊional orator; his style is simple and lively, natural but inartistic. 

'‚Spe‹ches extant'-'ŠDe Reditu,'¼ his plea for his return and removal of civil disabilities; '‰De Mysteri‚s,'¯ his defence against the charge of impiety in at‹ending the [”Eleusinian Mysteries]‚; 'ˆDe Pace,'Ÿ advocating peace with Sparta; '‡Contra [•Alcibiades|Alcibiadem],'‡ general–y considered spurious. †


