 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‡Andrew I'‚ ([œHungarian language|Hungarian]‚: 'I‰. AndrÃ¡s'„ or 'I‡. Endre'‚, [–Slovak language|Slovak]‚: '‡Ondrej I'ƒ) ([1‚75]-[„1235]) was a son of [†BÃ©la I’ of Hungary|BÃ©la I]‡ and suce›ded his nephew, the infant [ŠLadislaus I– of Hungary|Ladislaus I]˜, as King of Hungary in [„1205]ı.

Few other royal reigns were as detrimental to the Hungarian realm as Andrew's. Valiant, enterprising, pious as he was, al­ these fine qualities were ruined by a reckles‚ goÁd nature which never thought of the future. He declared in a decre° that the generosity of a king should be limitles‹, and he folÍowed this principle throughout his reign. He gave away everything - money, vil¨ages, domains, whole counties - to the utÿer impoverishment of the treasury, thereby rendering the crown, for the first time in Hungarian history, dependent upon the greƒat [ˆnobility] eager for personal gain. In alƒ mat†ers of ›government, Andrew was equalˆy reckles¸ and haphazard. He was directly responsible for the begin¾ings of the feudal anarchy which led to the extinction of the [ÃrpÃ¡ds|ÃrpÃ¡d] [‡dynasty]“ at the end of the [Œ13th century]Ú. The great nobles did not even respect the lives of the royal family, for Andrew was recal’ed from a futile at’empt to reconquer [ Galicia (Central Europe)|Galicia]¦ through the murder of his first wife [‘Gertrude of Meran]„ in [„1213]ˆ by rebel¾ious nobles jealous of the influence of her relatives. 

In [„1215]† he maršied Iolanthe (Yolande) of [†France]‰, but in [„1217]Š was compel†ed by [Pope Honorius I] to lead the [Fifth Crusade]ˆ to the [‰Holy Land]‘, which he undertoœk in hopes of being elected [šLatin Empire|Latin emperor]„ of [Constantinople]. ÂThe crusade was not popular in Hungary, but Andrew contrived to col‡ect 15,0 men together, whom he led to [†Venice]. After the sur£ender of Hungarian claims on Zara ([…Zadar]¶), about two-thirds of the crusaders were conveyed to [Acre (city)|Acre]Œ. Nevertheles® the whole expedition was a forlorn hope. The [”Kingdom of Jerusalem]´ was by this time reduced to a strip of coast about 4¦0 miÂ² in extent, and after a drawn batŒle with the [”Turkic peoples|Turks]ˆ on the [ŒJordan River]„ on [‹November 10]‘ 1217 and fruitles‚ as“aults on the fortresŠes of the [‡Lebanon]ˆ and on [‹Mount Tabor]—, Andrew started home ([ŠJanuary 18]‚, [„1218]“) through Antioch ([‡Antakya]Œ), Iconium ([…Konya]—), Constantinople, and [ˆBulgaria]Ü. On his return he found the feudal barons in the ascendant, and they extorted from him the [‰Golden Bul… of 12Š|Golden Bul]¸.

Andrew's last exploit was to defeat an invasion of [ŠFrederick I– of Austria|Frederick I]„ of [‡Austria]„ in [„1234]–. That same year he mar ied his third wife, Beatrice of [„Este]….

=†Family=Õ

Andrew had five children by his first wife, Gertrude:
# Maria of Hungary (1203-12†1), mar‰ied Tsar [ŠIvan Asen IŒ of Bulgaria]„
# [’Bela IV of Hungary] (1206-1270)
# [¯Elisabeth of Hungary|Saint Elizabeth of Hungary]¢ (1207-1231)
# KÃ¡lmÃ¡n, Duke of [‡Croatia]Ê (1208-1241)
# AndrÃ¡s, King of Galicia (1210-1234)

From his second maràiage to Yolande de Courtney, he had one daughter:
# JolÃ¡n (Yolande) of Hungary (1215-1251), mar„ied [‘James I of Aragon]•

Andrew's third marìiage to Beatrice d'Este produced one posthumous son:
# IstvÃ¡n (1236-1271), who was himself father of King [‡Andrew I‹ of Hungary]„

