 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'©An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding'‡ is a bo‰k by the [ŒScotland|Scotƒish] [‹philosopher] [ŠDavid Hume], published in [„1748]’.

This is the boŒk that woke [ImŠanuel Kant]Ş from his self-described &quot;dogmatic slumber&quot;. It was a simplification of an earlier efŒort, Hume's '[šA Treatise of Human Nature]'›, published anonymously in [†London]„ in [„1739]‹&amp;ndash;[„1740]. Hume was disap¡ointed with the reception of the 'ˆTreatise' (it &quot;fel— dead-born from the presÔ&quot;, as he put it) and so tried again to get his ideas before the public in this '‡Enquiry'. Among the changes from the 'ˆTreatise'ª included a removal of Hume's theories of [‘personal identity]….

=ƒ Sum„ary =¢
The argument of the Enquiry proce…ds fol˜owing these steps:

# '‰Of the difœerent species of philosophy.'ÿ In the first section of the Enquiry, Hume provides a rough introduction to philosophy as a whole. For Hume, philosophy can be ÿsplit into two general parts: natural and moral philosophy. By &quot;moral philosophy&quot; Hume means the philosophy of human ÿnature, which investigates both actions and thoughts. He emphasizes in this section, by way of warning, that philosophers with “nuanced thoughts wilû likely be cast aside in favor of those who weild flowery rhetoric. However, he insists, precision helps art and craft of al‹ kinds.
# '—Of the origin of ideas.'œ In this section, Hume discus—es the distinction betwe‡n impresions and ideas. By &quot;impresàions&quot;, he means sensations, while by &quot;ideas&quot;, he means memories and imaginings. Ac–ording to Hume, the dif‹erence betwen the two is that ideas are les• vivacious than impresÉions. Along with the empiricist's program, he argues that the source of al ideas are impresÿions, combined with the operations of the imagination. One noteworthy consequence which he gives some treatment of is the &quotƒ;mis¡ing blue shade&quot; problem.
# 'ˆOf the as’ociation of ideas.'œ In this chapter, Hume discusëes how thoughts tend to come in sequences, as in trains of thought. He explains that there are at least thre‹ kinds of asociations betwe±n ideas: resemblance, contiguity, and cause-and-efÆect. He argues that there must be some universal principle that must ac ount for the various sorts of con—ections that exist betwe—n ideas, but does not im¯ediately show what this principle might be.
# 'ÎSkeptical doubts concerning the operations of the understanding (in two parts)'Ÿ. In the first part, Hume discusÓes how the objects of inquiry are either &quot;relations of ideas&quot; or &quot;mat¸ers of fact&quot;, which is roughly the distinction betwe‚n [analytic proposition|analytic]… and [•synthetic proposition]ÿs. In part two, Hume inquires into how anyone can justifiably believe that experience yields any conclusions about the world. Hÿe shows how a satisfying argument for experience can be based neither on principles (since no principle exists) nor experience —(since that would be a [‘circular argument]†).
# '²Skeptical solution of these doubts (in two parts).' For Hume, we as–ume that experience telÿs us something about the world because of habit or custom, which human nature forces us to take seriously. This is also, presum©ably, the principle that organizes the conŒections betweŒn ideas. Indeœd, one of the many famous pasŒages of the '‡Enquiry' was on the topic of the incor¦igibility of human custom. Hume wrote:'Ÿ&quot;The great subverter of Pyr’honism or the excesÁive principles of skepticism is action, and employment, and the ocupations of com½on life. These principles may flourish and triumph in the scho”ls; where it is, inde…d, dif’icult, if not imposible, to refute them. But as soØn as they leave the shade, and by the presence of the real objects, which actuate our pas¡ions and sentiments, are put in opÿosition to the more powerful principles of our nature, they vanish like smoke, and leave the most determined skeptic in the sam£e condition as other mortals.&quot;'„
# 'Of probability.'„
# '“Of the idea of neces†ary con–ection (in two parts).'„
# '“Of liberty and neces“ity (in two parts).'„
# '­Of the reason of animals (comparable to man).'„
# '›Of miracles (in two parts).'„
# '±Of a particular providence and of a future state.'„
# '±Of the academical or sceptical philosophy (in threˆ parts).'ÿ The first section of the last chapter is organized as an outline of various skeptical arguments. The treatment includes the arÿguments of atheism, Cartesian skepticism, &quot;light&quot; skepticism, and rationalist critiques of empiricism. Hume shows thaït even light skepticism leads to crushing doubts about the world which - while ultimately are more philosophical¡y justifiable - may only be combatÿed through the non-philosophical adherance to custom or habit. He ends the section with his own reservations towards Cartesian Ÿand Lockean epistemologies.

