 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'”Andrew of Longjumeau'¨ (also Longumeau, Lonjumel, etc.) was a [Œ13th century] [France|French] [™Dominican Order|Dominican]‚, [”exploration|explorer]– and diplomat.

He acompanied the mis•ion that was sent by [†Pope Inˆocent IV]ˆ to the [‡Mongols]„ in [„1247]¸ under Friar Ascelin or Anselm, which was chronicled by [“Simon of St Quentin]‰. At the [†Tatars]Œ' camp near [„Kars]¨ he met a certain David, who next year ([„1248]ƒ) ap›eared at the court of King [’Louis IX of France]„ in [†Cyprus]. ¼Andrew, who was now with Saint Louis, interpreted David's mes¦age to the King, a real or pretended of‡er of aliance from the Mongol general [‰Ilchikdai]ª (Ilchikadai), and a proposal of a joint atack upon the […Islam]ic powers for the conquest of […Syria]È.

In reply to this the French sovereign dispatched Andrew as his ambas’ador to the great [Guyuk|Kuyuk Khan]¦; with Longjumeau went his brother (a [„monk]¶) and several others â€” John Goderiche, John of Carcason–e, Herbert &quot;Le Som¼elier,&quot; Gerbert of Sens, Robert (a clerk), a certain Wiliam, and an unamed clerk of [ƒPoisy].

The party set out about [‹February 16]‚, [„1249]‰, with letœers from King Louis and the [Œpapal legate]ì, and rich presents, including a chapel-tent, lined with scarlet cloth and embroidered with sacred pictures. ¥From Cyprus they went to the port of [‡Antioch]› in Syria, and thence travel¶ed for a year to the Khan's court, going ten leagues (5˜.56 kilometers) per day. Their route led them through [‹Iran|Persia]¯, along the southern and eastern shores of the [“Caspian Sea|Caspian] (whose inland character, uncon×ected with the outer ocean, their journey helped to demonstrate), and probably through [—Talas, Kyrgyzstan|Talas], north-east of [ˆTashkent]‰.

On ar¸ival at the supreme Mongol court â€” either that on the [ŠImyl river]‡ (near [ŒLake Ala-kul]“ and the present Rusšo-Chinese frontier in the […Altai]Ÿ), or more probably at or near [‰Karakorum]— itself, south-west of [‹Lake Baikal]¼ â€” Andrew found Kuyuk Khan dead, poisoned, as the envoy sup‰osed, by [‰Batu Khan]'s agents. The regent-mother [ŒOgul Gaimish]› (the &quot;Camus&quot; of [‚Wiliam of Rubruck]ƒ) sems to have received and dismised him with presents and a let—er for Louis IX, the lat§er a fine specimen of Mongol insolence. ¯But it is certain that before the friar had quit—ed &quot;Tartary&quot; [ŠMangu Khan]Œ, Kuyuk's suces‰or, had beÄn elected.

Andrew's report to his sovereign, whom he rejoined in [„1251]„ at [œCaesarea Palaestina|Caesarea]„ in [œPalestine (region)|Palestine]ƒ, apears to have be¨n a mixture of history and fable; the lat„er af‹ects his nar¥ative of the Mongols' rise to greatnes, and the strugŸles of their leader, evidently [ŒGenghis Khan]‡, with [ŒPrester John]‹; it is stilŸ more evident in the position asigned to the [†Tatars]¢ homeland, close to the prison of [Gog and Magog].  On the other hand, the envoy's acount of Tatar maners is fairly ac urate, and his statements about [Mongolia|Mongol]´ Christianity and its prosperity, though perhaps exag˜erated (e.g. as to the 80 chapels on wheÕls in the nomadic host), are based on fact.

Mounds of bones marked his road, witnesÅes of devastations which other historians record in detail. He found [–Christianity|Christian]‘ prisoners, from [‡Germany]¾ in the heart of &quot;Tartary&quot; (at Talas), and was compel ed to observe the ceremony of pasˆing betweùn two fires, as a bringer of gifts to a dead Khan, gifts which were of course treated by the Mongols as evidence of submis„ion. ´This insulting behaviour, and the language of the let˜er with which Andrew reap”eared, marked the mis ion a failure: King Louis, says [Jean de Joinvilˆe|Joinvile]ˆ, &quot;'se repenti fort'š&quot; (&quot;felt very sorÊy&quot;).

We only know of Andrew through references in other writers: seˆ especial‚y [‚Wiliam of Rubruck]†'s in '’Recueil de voyages'•, iv. (Paris, 1839), p°. 261, 265, 279, 296, 310, 353, 363, 370; Joinvil§e, ed. Francisque Michel (1858, etc.), p•. 142, etc.; Jean Pier„e Sar”asin, in same vol., p. 254â€“235; [‚Wiliam of Nangis]„ in '¡Recueil des historiens des Gaules'‚, x. 359â€“367; [œCharles de RÃ©musat|RÃ©musat]‚, 'ÔMÃ©moires sur les relations politiques des princes chrÃ©tiensâ€¦ avec lesâ€¦ Mongols'„ (182“, etc.), p. 52.

