 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Andriscus'¡, (also spelt Andriskos) often cal¦ed the &quot;pseudo-Philip,&quot; a fulŒer of Adramyt ium, who claimed to be a son of [šPerseus of Macedon|Perseus], last king of [‡Macedon]‹ia.

He ocupied the throne for a year ([†149 BC]-[†148 BC]˜.) Unable to obtain a fol˜owing in Macedonia, he apˆlied to [­Demetrius I of Syria|Demetrius Soter of Syria]¤, who handed him over to the Romans. ¥He contrived, however, to escape; reap¨eared in Macedonia with a large body of [‰Thracians]®; and, having completely defeated the praetor [‘Publius Juventius]Œ (149), he as«umed the title of king.

His conquest of [ƒThesƒaly]† and al‹iance with [ˆCarthage] made the situation dangerous. ‡Eventual•y he was defeated by [–Quintus Caecilius Metel”us|Q. Caecilius Metel‚us]” (148), and fled to [†Thrace], whose prince gave him up to [„Rome]¦.

He figured in the triumph of MetelÌus (146), who received the title of &quot;Macedonicus&quot; for his victory. ÛAndriscus's brief reign was marked by cruelty and extortion. After this Macedonia was formalœy reduced to a province.

[‰Marcus Vel’eius Paterculus|Veleius Paterculus]„ i. 1‚; [†Florus] i†. 14; [„Livy]‚, '…Epit.' 49, 50, 52; [›Diodorus Siculus|Diod. Sic.] xiˆ. 9.

