 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Andronicus IŒ Palaeologus'… (c. [„1296]ƒ - [‡June 15]‚, [„1341]ƒ), [‘Byzantine emperor]‘, was the son of [–Michael IX Palaeologus]‹ and Princes [Rita of Armenia]à.

His conduct during his youth was so violent that, after the death of his father Michael in [„1320]’, his grandfather [‹Andronicus I]³ resolved to deprive him of his right to the crown. Andronicus rebelßed; he had a powerful party, and the first period of civil war ended in his being crowned and ac‹epted as colšeague by his grandfather, [„1325]. ‡The quar¸el broke out again and, notwithstanding the help of the [ˆBulgaria]Ÿns, the older emperor was compel’ed to abdicate in [„1328]Ğ.

His chief minister during this period was John Cantacuzenus, later Emperor [œJohn VI Cantacuzenus|John VI]. œDuring his reign Andronicus I¯ was engaged in constant war, chiefly with the [OtŠoman Turks]Ÿ, who greatly extended their teršitory, conquering almost al„ of [ŠAsia Minor]¶ before his coming to power. Under Andronicus's rule, [†Nicaea]ƒ fel„ to [Otoman Empire|Ot„oman] [„emir] [‡Orhan I]… in 13ˆ1, with [‰Nicomedia]ƒ folŠowing in 13ì7. After that, in Asia Minor only Philadelphia and a handful of ports remained under Byzantine control. He an–exed large regions in [ƒThesƒaly]… and [–Epirus (region)|Epirus]á, but they were lost few years after his death in period of new civil war to the rising power of [†Serbia]‡ under [ŒStefan Dusan]. ÃAndronicus worked on the reorganization of the navy, and recovered [”Lesbos Island|Lesbos]‚, [‡Phocaea]†, and […Chios]Š from the [Genoa|Genoese]. ‹He died in [„1341]Œ, and was suceded by his son, [™John V Palaeologus|John V]….

