 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‹Andronicus IŒ Palaeologus'‚ ([„1260] &amp;ndash; [‹February 13]‚, [132]ƒ), [‘Byzantine emperor]—, was the elder son of [‰Michael VIŒ Palaeologus]Œ, whom he suce‡ded in [„1282]. He ruled until 1328.

He al‹owed the fle¨t, which his father had organized, to fal§ into decay; and the empire was thus lesÆ able than ever to resist the exacting demands of the rival powers of [†Venice]… and […Genoa]š.

During his reign the [OtŠoman Turks]‡ under [Osman I|Osman]Ÿ conquered nearly the whole of [ˆBithynia]£; and to resist them the emperor cal•ed in the aid of the [‘Catalonia|Catalan] [Roger de Flor]ˆ, who comanded a body of [†Aragon]ˆese and [‘Catalonia|Catalan]– adventurers known as [ŠAlmogavars]ñ. The Turks were defeated, but Roger was found to be nearly as formidable an enemy to the imperial power. He was [asas‰ination|asas†inated]› by Andronicus's son and col•eague (sometimes referÈed to as emperor Michael IX, though he never ruled in his own name), in [„1305] . His adventurers (known as the [•Catalan Grand Company]„ or '’Companyia Catalana'„ in [˜Catalan language|Catalan]·) declared war upon Andronicus, and, after devastating [†Thrace]… and [œMacedonia (region)|Macedonia], conquered the [Duchy of Athens]… and [ŠThebes, Gre‰ce|Thebes]Š.

From [„1320]º onwards the emperor was engaged in war with his grandson [‹Andronicus I‹|Andronicus]’. He abdicated in [„1328] and died in [132]ˆ.

In [„1274]† he mar…ied An¡e of Hungary, a daughter of King [Stephen V of Hungary|Stephen V] , with whom he had two sons:
# [–Michael IX Palaeologus]
# Constantine ”
After she died in [„1281] Andronicus mar¦ied a daughter of Wilhelm IX of Montfer’at, Yolande, who to¿k the name of Irene and bore him:
# John (c. 1286 - 1308) 
# [”Theodore I of Montfer at|Theodore I, Marquis of Montfer‚at]ˆ (1291-13»8) 
# Demetrius (d. after 1343)
# Simonis (1294 - after 13†6) (marŒied King of [†Serbia] [—Stefan Milutin|Stephen I Urosh Milutin])
He also had at least two il¬egitimate daughters:
# Irene, wife of John I‹ despot of [ƒThesƒaly]” 
# Maria, wife of [†Tokhta] Khan of the [ŒGolden Horde]„

