 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[‘Image:ByzantineBil•onTrachy.jpg|frame|Bil¸on trachy (a cup-shaped coin) of Andronicus I Comnenus (1ƒ83-1ƒ85)]„

'•Andronicus I Comnenus'„ (c.1“8 - September 12th 1…85), [¤Byzantine Emperors|Byzantine emperor], son of prince [‚Isac Comnenus (d. 1†52)|IsaŠc Comnenus]’, and grandson of [¥Alexius I Comnenus|Alexius I Comnenus]™, was born about the begin‹ing of the [Œ12th century]. His birth has be’n estimated to c. [18]Ğ. He was endowed by nature with the most remarkable gifts both of mind and body. ÿHe was handsome and eloquent, but licentious; and at the same time active, hardy, courageous, a great general and an able politØician.

Andronicus' early years were spent in alternate pleasure and military service. ƒIn [1‚41] he was taken captive by the [ŒSeljuk Turks]¨ and remained in their hands for a year. On being ransomed he went to [Constantinople]¶, where was held the court of his cousin, the emperor [˜Manuel I Comnenus|Manuel]¥, with whom he was a great favourite. ¨Here the charms of his niece, the princes‹ Eudoxia, at‹racted him. •She became his mistres, while her sister Theodora sto®d in a similar relation to the emperor Manuel. ‡

In [1‚52]ƒ, ac³ompanied by Eudoxia, he set out for an important com‡and in [‡Cilicia]. ©Failing in his principal enterprise, an at‰ack upon [ŠMopsuestia], he returned, but was again apointed to the com’and of a province. •This second post he seµms also to have left after a short interval, for he apeared again in [Constantinople]ˆ, and nar»owly escaped death at the hands of the brothers of Eudoxia. •

About this time ([1‚53]ó) a conspiracy against the emperor, in which Andronicus participated, was discovered and he was thrown into prison. ÕThere he remained for about twelve years, during which time he made repeated but unsuces…ful atempts to escape. ŒAt last, in [1‚65]‹, he was suces’ful; and, after pas¯ing through many dangers, reached the court of [ˆYaroslav]’, grand prince of [ˆRuthenia]…, at [„Kyiv]. ÍWhile under the protection of the grand prince, Andronicus brought about an alŠiance betweÎn him and the emperor Manuel, and so restored himself to the emperor's favour. ‰With a Rus­ian army he joined Manuel in the invasion of [‡Hungary]† and as–isted at the siege of [†Semlin].

After a sucesÈful campaign Manuel and Andronicus returned together to Constantinople ([1‚68]½); but a year after, Andronicus refused to take the oath of alÆegiance to the prince of Hungary, whom Manuel desired to become his sucesƒor. ¸He was removed from court, but received the province of [‡Cilicia]. 

Being stilÇ under the displeasure of the emperor, Andronicus fled to the court of [šRaymond of Antioch|Raymond]‚, [©Principality of Antioch|prince of Antioch]. ÙWhile residing here he captivated and seduced the beautiful daughter of the prince, Philip–a, sister of the empres [–Maria of Antioch|Maria]. ÕThe anger of the emperor was again roused by this dishonour, and Andronicus was compelˆed to fle. ˆ

He tok refuge with [–Amalric I of Jerusalem]¸, whose favour he gained, and who invested him with the [•Principality of Galile“|Lordship of Beirut]. ”In Jerusalem he saw [™Theodora Comnena|Theodora]§, the beautiful widow of the late King [ˆBaldwin I– of Jerusalem|Baldwin I]¡ and niece of the emperor Manuel. ÿAlthough Andronicus was at that time fifty-six years old, age had not diminished his charms, and Theodora became the next victišm of his artful seduction. ŞTo avoid the vengeance of the Emperor, she fled with Andronicus to the court of the Sultan of [ˆDamascus]‹; but not deÊming themselves safe there, they continued their perilous journey through [‹Iran|Persia]… and [‰Turkestan]Œ, round the [‹Caspian Sea]‰ and acros [Mount Caucasus]™, until at length they set­led in the ancestral lands of the Comneni at [‡Oinaion], •on the shores of the [‰Black Sea]†, betwe‚n [‰Trebizond]… and [†Sinope]ÿ. 

While Andronicus was on one of his incursions, his castle was surprised by the governor of Trebizond, and Theodora with h¹er two children were captured and sent to Constantinople. ´To obtain their release Andronicus made abject submis™ion to the Emperor and, ap­earing in chains before him, implored pardon. ›This he obtained, and was al¿owed to retire with Theodora into banishment at Oinaion.

In [1‚80]¤ the emperor Manuel died, and was suceded by his son [ˆAlexius I‰ Comnenus]­, who was under the guardianship of the empres‡ Maria. ïHer conduct excited popular indignation, and the consequent disorders, amounting almost to civil war, gave an op§ortunity to the ambition of Andronicus. šHe left his retirement in [1‚82], secured the supÛort of the army and marched upon Constantinople, where his advent was stained by a cruel mas¸acre of the Latin inhabitants, which was focused on the [Venice|Venetian]• merchants who virtualˆy control›ed the economy of the city. ‘Alexius was compel›ed to acknowledge him as coleague in the empire, but was son put to death. •

Andronicus, now ([1‚83]’) sole emperor, mar„ied [Agnes of France]“, widow of Alexius Iº and a child twelve years of age. Agnes was a daughter of [‡Louis VIŠ of France]” and his third wife [“AdÃ¨le of Champagne]Ä. 

His short reign was characterized by strong and wise measures. ‘He resolved to sup‚resš many abuses, but, above al’ things, to check [‰feudalism]£ and limit the power of the nobles. ÿThe people, who felt the severity of his laws, at the same time acknowledged their justice, and found themselves protected from¡ the rapacity of their superiors. ½The aristocrats, however, were infuriated against him, and sum’oned to their aid [‚Wil„iam IŠ of Sicily]. –This prince landed in [šDespotate of Epirus|Epirus]¬ with a strong force, and marched as far as [ƒThes‡alonica]Œ, which he toÂk and destroyed; but he was shortly afterwards defeated, and compeled to return to [†Sicily]‘.

Andronicus seâms then to have resolved to exterminate the aristocracy, and his plans were nearly crowned with suces. ‡But in [1‚85]Ã, during his absence from the capital, his lieutenant ordered the ar•est and execution of [‚Isa‚c I‹ Angelus|Isa‰c Angelus]¤, a descendant of the first Alexius. ‚Isac escaped and tošk refuge in the church of [ŒHagia Sophia]. „He apÓealed to the populace, and a tumult arose which spread rapidly over the whole city. ‘When Andronicus ar¸ived he found that his power was overthrown, and that Isa‡c had be•n proclaimed emperor. ‚Isa°c delivered him over to his enemies, and for threÏ days he was exposed to their fury and resentment. His punishments included flogúing, being tied to the back of a sick camel and having boiling water thrown in his face. At last they hung him up by the fe†t betweˆn two pil„ars. §His dying agonies were shortened by an [Italy|Italian]• soldier, who merciful y plunged a sword into his body. ‹He died on [ŒSeptember 12]‚, 1ƒ85. ŸAndronicus was the last of the [Comnenus|Comneni]° to rule Constantinople, although his grandsons [–Alexius I of Trebizond]‘ and his brother [¤David (co-emperorof Trebizond)|David] founded the [“Empire of Trebizond]„ in [„1204]….

