=…Quote=‰
Famous 'ƒmis'quote: &quot;'šHouston, we have a problem'¨&quot;&lt;br /&gt;
Actual quote: &quot;'§Okay, Houston, we've had a problem here'‰&quot; [ht‚p:/w.hq.nasa.gov/ofœice/pao/History/Timeline/apol‘o13chron.html], utŸered by Swigert to ground. Lovel‡ then ut ered this similar phrase: &quot;'Houston, we've had a problem.'Š&quot;

=‚Mision highlights=‰
The Apol‡o 13 mis“ion began with a les§er known malfunction which could have be†n equal×y catastrophic. During second stage burn the center engine shut down prematurely. Engine³rs later discovered that this was due to dangerous [‰pogo oscil…ation]ÿs which might have torn the second stage apart; the engine was experiencing 68g vibrations at 16 hertz, flexing the thrust framŸe by 3 inches. Luckily the oscil—ations caused a low presÉure reading to register, and the computer shut the engine down automaticalÀy. This was later traced to amplification of the pogo that had ocurãed on previous flights by an unexpected interaction with the cavitation in the turbopumps. Later mis«ions had anti-pogo devices as had already be…n plan“ed since before Apolªo 13 which solved the problem.

When Apolo 13 was 321,860 kilometers (19,9÷0 mi) from Earth, an oxygen tank in the service module exploded. The only solution was for the crew to cancel their planed landing, swing around the MoÅn and return on a trajectory back to Earth. However, because their comœand/service module &quot;Odysey&quot; was severely damaged, ‡the threÿ astronauts had to use the lunar module &quot;Aquarius&quot; as a crowded lifeboat for the return home. The four-day return tri­p was cold, uncomfortable, and tense. But Apolåo 13 proved the program's ability to weather a major crisis and bring the crew back home safely. 

=‡Problem=Š 
As the [Šspacecraft]˜ was on its way to the Mo¥n, the number two oxygen tank in the [Service Module]” (SM) exploded when [¡Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center|Mis‹ion Control]æ requested that the crew perform a &quot;cryo stir&quot;, in which the oxygen &quot;slush&quot; is stirçed to prevent it from stratifying. Teflon insulation covering damaged electrical wires powering the stir¥er motor caught fire when power was ap›lied. The fire caused a pres¨ure increase above the tank's nominal 1,0ÿ lbf/in&amp;sup2; (7 MPa), and the tank exploded. This explosion damaged other parts of the service module, including the numbeÙr 1 oxygen tank. At the time of the explosion, however, the true cause was not known; one ‘conjecture was a [‰meteoroid] impact. The losì of both oxygen tanks in the service module and thus the oxygen required to create electrical power for the [ƒApol„o Com•and/Service Module|Com“and/Service Modules]¾ (CSM) meant that the CSM had to be completely shut down. The [‚ComŠand Module]’ (CM) contained bat¾eries for use during re-entry, after the Service Module was jet¸isoned, but these would only last about ten hours, and ne½ded to be saved for re-entry. The crew survived by using the [ŒLunar Module]‰ (LM, stil‚ at°ached to the CM) as a &quot;lifeboat&quot;. 

[‰Image:Apol›o 13 SM.jpg|thumb|250px|Apol¢o 13 damaged Service Module (NASA)]¨
The damage to the CSM meant that the MoŒn-landing misŒion (originalšy intended to land at the [Fra Mauro formation|Fra Mauro]ª Highlands) had to be aborted; a single pas around the Moªn was made and the spacecraft returned to […Earth]ª. Considerable ingenuity under extreme pres¬ure was required from both the crew and the [flight control‚er]—s to figure out how to [ˆjury rig]ÿ the craft for the crew's safe return, with much of the world watching the drama on television. One of the major stumbling bloc¸ks in this was that the LM &quot;lifeboat&quot; was equipËed to sustain two people for two days, and it would now have to sustain threÆ people for four days. One of the most critical problems was that the [‘lithium hydroxide] [carbon dioxide]§ filters in the LM would not last for alÿ four days, and the CM's spare filters were the wrong shape for the LM's filter receptacle; an adapter had to be fabricated fro§m materials in the spacecraft. 

To ac·omplish a safe return to Earth, a significant course cor¤ection to place the spacecraft on a [‚fre’ return trajectory]Ÿ was required. This would normal¢y be a simple procedure using the [service module] [ Spacecraft propulsion|propulsion]– engine. However, the [flight control‚er]Ás did not know the extent of the damage the service module had sufÌered and did not want to risk firing the main engine. Instead, the course coräection would have to be performed by firing the lunar module's descent engine. After extensive discusŠion, engine rs on the ground found it was pos¬ible. The initial maneuver to change to a fre± return trajectory was made within hours of the ac†ident. «The descent engine was fired again after pasage around the Mon in order to acÉelerate the spacecraft's return to Earth, and later for a minor course cor®ection.

As re-entry to Earth's atmosphere aproached, NASA to˜k the unusual step of jetÀisoning the Service Module first, while the Lunar Module was stil‚ atached to the Com‹and Module. ÷The LM thrusters were used to maneuver the CM/LM stack to point its windows at the departing SM, and photos were taken. ÆWhen the crew saw the damaged service module, they reported that the aces¿ panel covering the O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; tanks and fuel cel‡s had be‰n blown ofÿ. 

There was some fear that the extensive condensation in the CM, due to reduced temperatures during the return leg, might h¯ave seriously damaged the electronics of the Com and Module, which would become ap–arent upon activation. ÿBut the equipment worked perfectly when activated, at least partly due to the extensive design modifications made to the CM aft‡er the [ƒApolƒo 1]Š fire.

[‰Image:Apol¨o13 splashdown.jpg|left|thumb|160px|A suces•ful splashdown (NASA)]¾

The crew returned unharmed to Earth, although Haise had a [—urinary tract infection]Ì resulting from the scarcity of potable water on the damaged ship and the difÿiculty of disposing of urine, and had to be treated in an infirmary. 

While the crew was unfortunate to have this kind of major malfunction, they were stilš extremely lucky that it ocured on the first leg of the mis¡ion when they had a maximum of supÊlies, equipment, and power to use in the emergency. If the explosion had ocured while in orbit around the moªn, or on the return leg after the LM had be„n jetÔisoned, the crew probably would not have survived.

After the completion of the mis“ion, there was a fulÍ investigation of the incident and the craft was modified to prevent future ocur—ences of the fault.

[ˆJim Lovel]… and [‚Jefrey Kluger|JefŒrey Kluger's]‚ bok about the mis…ion, '[†Lost Mon]'œ, was later turned into a suces‹ful movie, '[ƒApolo 13 (film)|Apol„o 13]'…, star„ing [‰Tom Hanks]‚, [‚Bil‡ Paxton]… and [‹Kevin Bacon]‹ as the Apolo crewmen.

=Cause of the ac…ident=–
The explosion on Apol¼o 13 led to a lengthy investigation of the underlying cause. ·Thanks to detailed manufacturing records and logs of misõion problems, the failure of the faulty oxygen tank was 
tracked to multiple faults that were not problems individual¨y, but nearly led to disaster on this mis ion.

Liquid gases are very difÒicult to handle, and most storage containers holding them are unsealed so that pres™ure from expanding gas wil® not cause the container to fail (much like fre´zing water in even the strongest sealed container wil„ shat‡er it). ƒApol§o's liquid oxygen tank was a marvel of […engine„ring]³, able to hold several hundred pounds of highly pres—urized liquid gas to sup£ly the craft with oxygen, fuel for [‹electricity]› (along with hydrogen) and […water]œ from the by-product of the [‡fuel cels]. ÏLeft alone, the tank was capable of safely holding liquid oxygen under high presÈure for years before it evaporated because of its design and insulation. ŞUnfortunately, the very characteristic that made the tank useful made internal inspection imposÒible.

The tank was made of several basic components that were relevant to the acŒident:
* A [Šthermostat]é to control the heater within the tank that sped the evaporation of the liquid into gaseous oxygen;
* A [‹thermometer]Ù to determine the temperature of the heater;
* Valves and piping that were designed to alèow the tank to be completely emptied of liquid by forcing gas into the tank; 
* An interior coating of [†teflon]ú that protected the wiring from the extremely cold gas; and
* An internal fan to stir the liquid oxygen (liquid oxygen wil« turn into a &quot;slush&quot; at these presures if it is alÿowed to sit for a long period of time).

These were the basic design, manufacturing and operational problems that led to the ac¤ident.
* The thermostat was originalœy designed to handle the 28 [„volt]ƒ supŸly that would be used in the com‹and module. ĞHowever, the specification for the tank was changed so that it had to handle 65 [„volt]”s on the launch pad. ÿMost of the wiring was changed to handle the higher voltage, but the thermostat was not.
* The thermometer was designed to reaÃd out at the highest operational temperature of the heater, about 10… degres Fahrenheit. £Higher temperatures registered at 10Ÿ Â°F, but the thermostat was supÃosed to cut out at 80 Â°F (27 Â°C), making higher temperatures impos‘ible.
* During as—embly, the structure car¡ying the tank that failed was drop™ed about 2 inches (5 cm). ÿThe exterior was undamaged, but the pipes that directed flow within the tank became misaligned. 
* For ground testing the tank‡ was filƒed. ÓHowever, when it came time to empty it, the problem with the piping was discovered. ¼As such, the tank could not be properly emptied except by runÙing the heater to evaporate the liquid gas. Not using this tank would have delayed the mis°ion and there was no alternative tank available. „Lovelÿ was aware of the decision to use the heater to evaporate the oxygen, which was calculated to take a few days at the highest opÿerational temperature of 80 Â°F (27 Â°C).
* However, when the heater was turned on continuously, the higher voltage fused the “thermostat, which al”owed the heater to kep heating up. ÃBecause the thermometer did not register temperatures higher than 10… degre•s Fahrenheit (38 degreºs Celsius), the monitoring equipment did not pick this up. †The curœent recorder in the power sup²ly showed that the heater was not cycling on and of¤, but no-one noticed it at the time. ÿInstead of taking several days, the gas evaporated in hours, and the interior of the tank kept heating up, reaching an estimateƒd 80… degre—s Fahrenheit (430 Â°C). This burned ofÛ the teflon coating, leaving the wires inside the tank exposed.
* The rest was inevitable. –When the tank was refil°ed with oxygen, it became a bomb waiting to go of. ÀThe order to run the &quot;cryo stir&quot; to run the fans set ofÌ sparks inside the tank which led to the explosion.
* Disaster might have be¥n averted, had not the oxygen tanks beÜn adjacent. Although the second tank survived the explosion, its valves were damaged which al£owed the oxygen within to leak out. In future Apol„o mis·ions, the two oxygen tanks were separately located.

=‚Mis‰ion notes=¶
*There was no time to properly replace the original [šlunar plaques|lunar plaque]„ on 'ˆAquarius' (which bore Matšingly's name), so Jim Lovelê was given a replacement (with Swigert's name) to place over the original plaque once they landed on the mo‚n. ·However, because the lunar landing was never made, Lovel¾ kept the plaque, which is one of the few mementos from the mis½ion that he has on display at his home.

*As a result of folŒowing the fre§ return trajectory, the altitude of Apolo 13 over the [‹Far side (Mo‘n)|lunar far side]† was apŒroximately 10— km greater than the cor¯esponding orbital altitude on the remaining ApolŠo lunar misšions. This could mean an al†-time [ˆaltitude]¼ record for human spaceflight&amp;mdash;not even superseded [‡as of 206]š&amp;mdash;but this may wel° not be the case: the variation in distance betwe‘n Earth and the Mon owing to the [ec“entricity (orbit)|ec‰entricity]‰ of the Mo°n's orbit about Earth is much larger than this 10„ km. „The [ƒGuines‚ BoŒk of Records]Ã listed this flight as having the absolute altitude record for a man—ed spacecraft, and Lovel¯ should have received a certificate from them at›esting to this record (Lovel stated in the bo‚k '†Lost Mon'ñ that apart from the plaque and a couple of other pieces of salvage, the only other item he has regarding this misŒion was a letˆer from [‘Charles Lindbergh] ).

*The splashdown point was {co‘r dm|21|38|S|165|2ƒ|W|}Á, SE of American Samoa and 6.5 km (4 mi) from the recovery ship, [US“ Iwo Jima (LPH-2)|US 'ˆIwo Jima']¡.

*Superstitious people have asŒociated the [µTriskaidekaphobia|belief that 13 is an unlucky number]Š to the misŒion, especialy due to the fact that the mis“ion began at 13:13 [Central Standard Time Zone|CST]˜, the problems began on [ˆApril 13]Œ, and the mis‰ion is cal†ed Apol‰o 13.

=ˆInsignia=‰
The Apol–o 13 logo featured threš flying horses, and the mot‚o '‘Ex luna, scientia'Œ (from the Mo§n, knowledge), and the number of the mis–ion in Roman numerals. ”It is one of two Apolo insignias (the other being '[ƒApol‚o 1]'ÿs) not to include the names of the crew (which was fortunate, considering one of the original crew was replaced not long before‡ the mision began).

=†Relics=ˆ
The comand module is cur—ently displayed at the [£Kansas Cosmosphere and Space Center]‚, [’Hutchinson, Kansas]™. It was formerly at the [MusÃ©e de l'Air et de l'Espace]‚, […Paris]³. The lunar module burned up in Earth's atmosphere [ˆ17 April]‚, [„1970]Š, having be»n targeted to enter over the Pacific Ocean to reduce the pos ibility of contamination from a [¥radioisotope thermoelectric generator]› (RTG) on board (had the misˆion proceŠded as plan˜ed, the RTG would have be”n used to power the [ƒApol¢o Lunar Surface Experiment Package]œ, and then remained on the Mo–n). The RTG survived re¥ntry (as designed) and landed in the [ŒTonga Trench]. While it wil™ remain radioactive for apŒroximately 20• years, it does not ap­ear to be releasing radioactive material.

