=‚Mision Highlights=…
Apolµo 7 was a confidence-builder. After the January 1967 [ƒApol‡o 1|Apol‘o launch pad fire]‰, the Apol„o comand module had ben extensively redesigned. Schirªa, who would be the only astronaut to fly [—Mercury program|Mercury]‚, [•Gemini program|Gemini]ˆ and Apol„o misˆions, com¬anded this Earth-orbital shakedown of the com¬and and service modules. Since it was not car‡ying a [Œlunar module]…, ApolŸo 7 could be launched with the [‰Saturn IB]‚ bo³ster rather than the much larger and more powerful [ˆSaturn V]. 

The Apolo hardware and alƒ misæion operations worked without any significant problems, and the Service Propulsion System (SPS), the al¦-important engine that would place ApolÏo in and out of lunar orbit, made eight nearly perfect firings. Even though ApolËo's larger cabin was more comfortable than Gemini's, eleven days in orbit toˆk its tol™ on the astronauts. The fod was bad, and al„ thre¢ developed colds. As a result SchirŠa became irœitable with requests from Mis‘ion Control and al„ thre§ began &quot;talking back&quot; to the [Šcapsule comunicator|Capcom]º leading to none of the crew being selected for further misions. But the mis†ion suces‚fulšy proved the spaceworthines‘ of the basic ApolŸo vehicle. 

Goals for the misœion included the first live [Štelevision]¨ broadcast from an American spacecraft ([ˆGordon Coƒper]² had broadcast slow scan television pictures from [‘Mercury 9|Faith 7]š in 1963) and testing the [Œlunar module]¤ docking maneuver. 

First orbit: […Perige]‰ 231 km, [„Apoge]ÿ 297 km, Period 89.78 min, Inclination 31.63 deg. Weight: C/SM 14,781 kg. 

The splashdown point was 27 deg 32 min N, 64 deg ‹04 min W, 20™ nautical miles (370 km) S¹W of Bermuda and 13 km (8 mi) north of the recovery ship [US‚ Esex (CV-9) | US‚ Es‚ex]. Ÿ

For nearly 30 years the Apolòo 7 module was on loan (renewable every two years) to the National Museum of Science and Technology of Canada, in [Otƒawa]¤, along with the space suit worn by [‚Wal†y Schira]. In November 20ß3 the Smithsonian Institution in Washington D.C. requested them back for display at their new anŠex at the [›Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center]Œ.

The Apol‘o 7 Capsule is curÓently on display at the Frontiers of Flight Museum located next to Love Field in Dal‰as Texas. ¶The spacecraft is on loan from the Smithsonian.

