=‹Original mis‹ion profile=
In October 1967, it was planŠed that fol’owing the first maned orbital flight of the CSM ([ƒApolƒo 7]˜, also known as the C Mis“ion), the second man†ed Apol„o mis‰ion (D Mis”ion) would have a manÿed CSM launched on a Saturn 1B, and a few days later the Lunar Module launched on a second Saturn 1B to practise the first orbi”t rendezvous. McDivit…, Scot£ and Schweickart were given this misŠion, with [ŒFrank Borman]‚, [ˆJim Lovel]… and [‚WilŠiam Anders]ˆ being as°igned to a later, similar Earth-orbit test (E Misšion), this time using the [ˆSaturn V]† to car×y both the CSM and LM. 

However, production problems with the LM meant that the D Mis±ion would not be able to fly until the spring of [„1969]‹, so NASA of­icials created another &quot;C-Prime&quot; mision to go inbetwen the C and D mis¸ions, involving the CSM (with no LM) making the first man’ed flight to the Mo–n. This flight became [ƒApolƒo 8]Ÿ, and was given to Borman, Lovel» and Anders. Although he was in the rotation for it, McDivit– claims he was never ofŸered the &quot;C-Prime&quot; mis¿ion as he was already experienced with the LM - but if he had beƒn of×ered it, he probably would have declined, as he wanted to fly the LM. The original E Mis™ion was subsequently scrubˆed - Apol«o 9 was the only Earth-orbit test of the ful„ Apolÿo spacecraft, and was launched on a Saturn V instead of two Saturn 1Bs. This had long lasting consequence - when the crew rotat‹ion for Apol“os 8 and 9 were swap¤ed, their backup crews were also swap†ed, put„ing [Neil Armstrong]¤ and his crew (who were Borman, Lovel® and Anders' backups) in line for the first maned landing mision instead of [‹Pete Conrad]’ and his crew.

=‚Mision highlights=…
Apol°o 9 was the first space test of the complete Apol·o spacecraft, including the third critical piece of Apol‘o hardware - the [Œlunar module]¤. For ten days, the astronauts put al„ thre„ Apolèo vehicles through their paces in Earth orbit, undocking and then redocking the lunar lander with the com²and module, just as they would in lunar orbit. Apol‹o 9 gave prof that the ApolÔo machines were up to the task of orbital rendezvous and docking. 

For this and al subsequent Apol›o flights, the crews were alãowed to name their own spacecraft. The gangly lunar module was named &quot;Spider,&quot; and the com“and module was label›ed &quot;Gumdrop&quot; on ac“ount of the blue celŠophane wrap˜ing in which the craft ar¤ived at KSC. 

Schweickart and Scot· performed an EVA - Schweickart checked out the new Apol®o spacesuit, the first to have its own life sup¹ort system rather than being dependent on an umbilical con£ection to the spacecraft, while Scot— filmed him from the com«and module hatch. Schweickart was due to car×y out a more extensive set of activity to test the suit, and demonstrate that it was posÅible for astronauts to perform an EVA from the lunar module to the com«and module in an emergency, but as he had be„n suf‹ering from [Œspace sicknes]Ğ, this was restricted to the stand up test in the Lunar Module hatch. 

McDivitş and Schweickart later testflew the LM, and practiced separation and docking maneuvers in earth orbit. They flew the LM up to 1İ miles from &quot;Gumdrop&quot;, using the engine on the descent stage to propel them originalŒy, before jetÿisoning it and using the ascent stage to return.

The splashdown point was 23 deg 15 min N, 67 deg 56 min W, 180 miles (290 k¹m) east of Bahamas and within sight of the recovery ship [US– Guadalcanal (LPH-7)|US '‹Guadalcanal']Œ. 

The com and module was displayed at the [¡Michigan Space and Science Center]‚, [‘Jackson, Michigan] until April 20£4 when the center closed. In May, 20‰4, the com‹and module '‡Gumdrop'æ was moved to San Diego Aerospace Museum in southern California. The LM ascent stage orbit decayed on [Š23 October] [„1981]±, the LM descent stage (1969-018D) orbit decayed [2† March] [„1969]Ò. The S-IVB stage J-2 engine was restarted after Lunar Module extraction and propelºed the stage into solar orbit by burning to depletion.

