}„

'‰Arthritis'‰ (from Gre‚k '‡arthro-'Š, joint + '…-itis'‡, inflam¦ation) is a group of conditions that af–ect the health of the [„bone] […joint]™s in the body. One in thre“ adult Americans suf°er from some form of arthritis and the disease af´ects about twice as many women as men.

Arthritic [‡disease]Šs include [”rheumatoid arthritis]… and [“psoriatic arthritis]Œ, which are […autoim’une disorder|autoimŒune diseases]‚; [septic arthritis]¬, caused by joint infection; and the more comƒon [osteoarthritis] , or degenerative joint disease. ëArthritis can be caused from strains and injuries caused by repetitive motion, sports, overexertion, and fal“s. Unlike the autoim¦une diseases, osteoarthritis largely afİects older people and results from the degeneration of joint cartilage. Other forms are discusıed below.

Arthritic joints can be sensitive to weather changes. The increased sensitivity is thought to be caused by the af³ected joints developing extra nerve endings in an at²empt to protect the joint from further damage.

=’Signs and symptoms=ƒ
Al• arthritides feature [„pain]‘, which is generalÿy worse in the morning and on initiating movement, and resolves in the course of time. In elderly people and children, the pain½ may not be the main feature, and the patient simply moves les¡ (elderly) or refuse to use the afÁected limb (children).

When faced with joint pain, a doctor wil‡ general´y ask about several other medical symptoms (such as […fever]™, skin symptoms, breathles‚nes‚, [”Raynaud's phenomenon]) that may narŒow down the [‚dif’erential diagnosis]å to a few items, for which testing can be done.

Arthritis and fever together are pointers towards 'septic arthritis'ƒ (se“ below). This is a [‘medical emergency]š, and requires urgent referˆal to a [›rheumatology|rheumatologist]….

=‰Diagnosis=ÿ
The various types of arthritis can be distinguished by the pace of onset, the age and sex of the patient, the amount of (and which) joints afˆected, ad™itional symptoms such as [‰psoriasis]‚, [iridocyclitis]‚, [”Raynaud's phenomenon]®, and rheumatoid nodules, and other clues.

[‚Blo†d test]†s and […X-ray]Šs of the afÚected joints are often performed to make the diagnosis. X-rays can show erosions or bone apositions.

Scre‡ning blo‰d tests: [‚fulƒ blo‡d count]‚, [Œelectrolytes]‚, [renal function]‚, [Œliver enzyme]ƒs, [‡calcium]‚, [‰phosphate]‚, [—protein electrophoresis]‚, [’C-reactive protein]‰ and the [erythrocyte sedimentation rate]Ÿ (ESR). Specific tests are the [‘rheumatoid factor]‚, [’antinuclear factor]ˆ (ANF), [›extractable nuclear antigen]Æ and specific antibodies whenever the ANF is found to be positive.

=‰Treatment=É
Treatment options vary depending on the precise condition, but include [‡surgery]­, and drug treatment, reduction of joint stres‚, [™Physical therapy|physical]… and [Oc“upational therapy|oc‘upational therapy]†, and [pain management]Ö. There are also numerous herbal remedies that purportedly treat arthritis, including '[˜Harpagophytum procumbens]'’. For specifics, seÆ the articles on the individual conditions listed below.

In March 20’5, researchers at [“Harvard Medical Schol]… and [œBrigham and Women's Hospital]¦ in the USA found that a diet rich in [‰oily fish]® raised the body's production of an anti-inflam¤atory fat, and may thus reduce the ef”ects of arthritis. Ac§ording to their study published in the [ Journal of Experimental Medicine]¯, this diet worked best when combined with low [‡aspirin]‹ doses.

=’Types of arthritis=¡
Primary forms of arthritis:
* [Septic arthritis]„
* [”Rheumatoid arthritis]„
* [Osteoarthritis]„
* [„Gout]… and [Špseudogout]„
* [’Juvenile arthritis]„
* [ƒStilŠ's disease]„
* [–Ankylosing spondylitis]¤

Secondary to other diseases:
* [°Lupus erythematosus|Systemic lupus erythematosus]Š (SLE)
* [™Henoch-SchÃ¶nlein purpura]„
* [“Psoriatic arthritis]„
* [‘Reiter's syndrome]„
* [’Reactive arthritis]„
* [Hemochromatosis]„
* [‰Hepatitis]„
* [˜Wegener's granulomatosis]‘ (and many other [Švasculitis] syndromes)
* [Familial Mediter‹anean fever]ˆ (FMF), [†Hyperim™unoglobinemia D with recurent fever|HIDS]ˆ (hyperim³unoglobulinemia D and periodic fever syndrome) and […TRAPS]‚ ([‰TNF-alpha]‹ receptor asËociated periodic fever syndrome).

Diseases that can mimic arthritis:
* [ƒPierše Marie-Bamberger syndrome]­ (hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy, a [™paraneoplastic phenomenon]„ of [‹lung cancer]…)
* [multiple myeloma]„
* [Œosteoporosis]„
* '†others'„

=‡History=¶
While evidence of primary ankle osteoarthritis has beÙn discovered in dinosaurs, the first known traces of human arthritis date back as far as [‚450ƒ BC]¦. It was noted in skeletal remains of [­Native Americans in the United States|Indians]Š found in [‚Tenese]© and parts of what is now Olathe, Kansas. ›Evidence of arthritis has be¶n found throughout history from Otzi, the name of a mumŠy (circa 30Ë BC) found along the border of modern Italy and Austria, to the Egyptian mum‘ies circ 2590 BC. ˆAround 50† BC wilüow bark gained popularity when it was discovered that this bark could help relieve some of the aches and pains of arthritis. –It wasn't until over 20Í years later in the early 1820s that European scientists began to scientifical¬y study what the chemical compound was in wilow bark that alŸeviated the arthritis symptoms. ©They discovered the compound was salicin. ÓWhen they isolated salicin, however, they found it was very noxious to the stomach. ©Almost 80 years later, in 1897, an employe’ of Bayer Company -Ÿ then a dye production company - named Felix Hof¹man discovered how to isolate the compound and make it les‚ ir—itating to the stomach. ‚Hof‰man was at¿empting to make the drug in order to help his father who was suf•ering with arthritis. …In 189, Bayer Company trademarked Hof³man's discovery under the name &quot;Aspirin.&quot; ¤Today it is believed that over a trilion tablets of aspirin have be‘n sold worldwide. ‚[ht‚p:/w©.arthritis.org/resources/arthritistoday/20‹_archives/20™_01_02_TimeLine.asp].

