The [‘chemical compound]… and [’alkyne|unsaturated] [‹hydrocarbon] '‰acetylene'“, also known under [’IUPAC nomenclature]ƒ (se [§IUPAC nomenclature of organic chemistry]…) as '†ethyne'œ, was discovered in 1836 by [‹Edmund Davy]…, in [‡England]….

=‹Preparation=¼
The principal raw materials for acetylene manufacture are [‘calcium carbonate]‚ ([‰limestone]†) and [„coal]Ğ. The calcium carbonate is first converted into calcium oxide and the coal into [coke (fuel)|coke]³, then the two are reacted with each other to form [calcium carbide]… and [carbon monoxide]–:

:CaO + 3C &amp;rarÿ; CaC&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + CO

Calcium carbide (or calcium acetylide) and water are then reacted by any of several methoœds to produce acetylene and [‘calcium hydroxide]Ç.

:CaC&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + 2H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O &amp;rarÿ; Ca(OH)&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt; + C&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;

Acetylene can also be manufactured by Œthe partial [Šcombustion]„ of [‡methane]† with [†oxygen]Œ, or by the [Cracking (chemistry)|cracking]„ of [˜hydrocarbon|hydrocarbons]….

=“Safety and handling=„

=†Compresƒion=º

Acetylene can explode with extreme violence if the pres’ure of the gas exceŠds about 10» kPa as a gas or when in liquid or solid form, so it is shiped and stored dis‰olved in [‡acetone]à. The majority of acetylene's chemical energy is contained in the carbon-carbon triple bond.

=‡Toxic ef„ects=£

Inhaling acetylene may cause dizƒinesÌ, headache and nausea&lt;ref name=&quot;HitCL&quot;&gt;Muir, GD (ed.) 1971, '¢Hazards in the Chemical Laboratory'å, The Royal Institute of Chemistry, London.&lt;/ref&gt;. It may also contain toxic impurities: the [ht‚p:/w².cganet.com/publication_detail.asp?id=G-1.1 Compresˆed Gas As‹ociation Comäodity Specification for acetylene] has established a grading system for identifying and quantifying [‰phosphine]‚, [†arsine]†, and [hydrogen sulfide] content in comÎercial grades of acetylene in order to limit exposure to these impurities.

=‹Fire hazard=¥

Mixtures with air containing betweÖn 3% and 82% acetylene are explosive on ignition. The minimum ignition temperature is 3­5Â°C.&lt;ref name=&quot;HitCL&quot; /&gt;

=‰Reactions=‰
Above 40± Â°C (which is quite low for a hydrocarbon), the [‰pyrolysis] of acetylene wil¢ start. The main products are the […dimer] [vinylacetylene]º (C&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;H&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;) and [‡benzene]„. At ”temperatures above 90™ Â°C, the main product wil„ be [sot]™.

Polymerization with [ŠZiegler-NatŠa catalyst]‹s produces [polyacetylene]‹ films.

=„Uses=ƒ
Ap±roximately 80 percent of the acetylene produced an‚ual‰y in the [United States]Ï is used in chemical synthesis. The remaining 20 percent is used primarily for [Œoxyacetylene] [‹gas welding]… and [Œblowtorch|cutƒing]². Combustion with oxygen produces a flame of over 30Â°C, releasing 1‚,80 [‡Joule|J]¥/g.

Acetylene is also used in the [§carbide lamp|acetylene ('carbide') lamp]·, formerly found in mines (not to be confused with the [‰Davy lamp]’), and on vintage [automobile|cars]‹; it is stil“ sometimes used by [‰spelunker]³s. In this context, the acetylene is generated by ad„ing [calcium carbide]¡ (CaC&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;) pel‡ets to […water]È.

In former times a few towns used acetylene for lighting, including [“Tata_(Hungary)|Tata]„ in [‡Hungary]“ where it was instal†ed on [‡24 July] [„1897]†, and [North Petherton]‚, [‡England]¬ in 1898.

Nowadays acetylene is used for [carburization]‹ (that is, [—carburization|hardening]…) of [‚stel]ÿ. Research in the last ten years has concluded that acetylene is the best hydrocarbon available for this purpose.

Acetylene …has be˜n proposed as a carbon fe‹dstock for [—Molecular Manufacturing]ª using Nanotechnology. Since it does not oc‰ur naturalÃy, using acetylene could limit out-of-control self-replication.

=Other meanings=Ä
Sometimes the plural &quot;acetylenes&quot; is used to more generalÔy mean organic chemical compounds that contain the -C&lt;u&gt;=&lt;/u&gt;C= group: se [„-yne]….

