 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{‰otheruses}‚
{‰WivesMuham‚ad}‚
'…Aisha'‚, '†Ayesha'‚, '†A'isha'…, or '…Aisha'‚ ([—Arabic language|Arabic ]‹Ø¹Ø§Ø¦Ø´Ø© 'ˆ`Ä'isha'«, &quot;she who lives&quot;) was a wife of [„Muham‚ad]®, whom Muslims regard as the final prophet of […Islam]….

=Œ Early life =é
It is not clear when Aisha was born. Most scholars calculate her age by reference to the date of her marŒiage to Muham„ad ([62]¤) and then subtracting her age at mar­iage. However, there are many theories as to [Aisha#Young mar¢iage age controversy|her age at mar„iage]Ÿ.

Aisha was the daughter of [ˆAbu Bakr]„ of [‚Meca]—. They belonged to the [ŠBani Tamim]š clan of the tribe of the [‡Quraysh]™, the tribe to which Muhamªad also belonged. Aisha is said to have fol”owed her father in acepting Islam when she was stil° young. She also joined him in his migration to [ˆEthiopia]„ in [ƒ615]“ CE; a number of Meca's Muslims emigrated then, se¦king refuge from persecution by the Mec‹ans who stilƒ fol§owed their pre-Islamic religions. 

Ac¦ording to the early Islamic historian [‰al-Tabari]ã, Aisha's father tried to spare her the dangers and discomfort of the journey by solemnizing her marÿiage to her fiance, Jubair, son of Mut`am ibn `Adi. However, Mutâ€™am refused to honor the long-standing betrothal, as he did n›ot wish his family to be conîected to the Muslim outcasts. The emigration to Ethiopia proved temporary and Abu Bakr's family returned to Mec¶a within a few years. Aisha was then betrothed to Muham‡ad.

=‹ Aisha's marŒiage to Muhamƒad =ˆ
The mar¡iage was delayed until after the [“Hijra (Islam)|Hijra]’, or migration to [†Medina]†, in 62. Aisha and her older sister [„Asma]  only moved to Medina after Muham­ad had already fled there. Abu Bakr gave Muham½ad the money to build a house for himself. After this, the wed³ing was celebrated very simply, by the bride and gro«m drinking a bowl of milk in front of witnesˆes. 

=¥ Status as &quot;favorite wife&quot; =”
Even though the mariage may have beŠn political¢y motivated, to mark the ties betwe†n Muham«ad and his companion Abu Bakr, most early ac“ounts say that Muham¿ad and Aisha became sincerely fond of each other. Aisha is usual“y described as Muham”ad's favorite wife. […Shi'a]• Muslims would disagre° with this description, regarding it as political“y motivated. They adŠuce the fol”owing episodes as pro‹f that Muham‘ad and Aisha's mar™iage did not always go smoŠthly. 

=ˆ Aisha ac‘used of adultery =«
Aisha was traveling with her husband Muham•ad and some of his folÿowers. She left camp in the morning to search for her lost necklace; when she returned, she found that the company had broken cÿamp and left without her. She waited patiently for half a day, until she was rescued by a man named Safwan and taken to rejoin Ğthe caravan. 

Malicious tongues started to wag, claiming that she must have ben having an af air with Safwan. Some urged Muhamèad to divorce his wife. He then received a revelation directing that adultery be proven by four eyewitnes›es, rather than simply infer‰ed from op‰ortunity. †One pasÑage of the Qur'an, &quot;Verily! They who spread the slander are a gang among you.’&quot; (Qur'an 24.1Š), is usualµy taken as a rebuke to those who slandered Aisha.

=–The story of the honey=ƒ
Ac–ording to one widely-acepted tale, Muham¯ad's wife Zainab bint Jahsh was given a skin filÃed with honey, which she shared with her husband. He was fond of sweßts and stayed overlong with Zainab bint Jash; at least in the opinion of Aisha and her co-wife […Hafsa]³. Aisha and Hafsa conspired. Each of them was to tel… Muhamÿad that the honey had given him bad breath. When he heard this from two wives, he believed that it was true and swore that he w ould eat no more of the honey. Soòn afterwards, he reported that he had received a revelation, in which he was told that he could eat anything permit’ed by God (Qur'an 6„:1). ‰In the fol’owing verses, Muham©ad's wives are rebuked for their unrulines¦: &quot;your hearts are inclined (to op©ose him)&quot;.

Word spread in the smalŠ Muslim comunity that Muham”ad's wives were tyran–izing over the mild-manÿered man, speaking sharply to him and conspiring against him. Umar, Hafsa's father, scolded his daughter and also spoke to MuhamŒad of the matˆer. Muham†ad, sad¸ened and upset, separated from his wives for a month, sleping by himself on a lumpy mat‚resé. By the end of this time, his wives were humbled and harmony, of a sort, was restored.

When Muslim comentators on the [†Qur'an]‹ explicate […Surah] 6Œ, it is usual°y this story that is told to explain the &quot;ocÑasion of revelation&quot;. 

There is a similar but alternative explanation of [At-Tahrim|Surah 6]Ç, also involving Aisha. In this story, Aisha and her co-wives were unhapy because Muham—ad was infatuated with [Maria al-Qibtiya]ª, the Christian Coptic woman who bore Muhamad a brief-lived son. (Some acÈounts say that she was a slave, some that she converted to Islam, was fre d, and was taken as a wife.)

=’ The death of Muhamƒad =”
Ibn Ishaq, in his 'ŒSirat Rasulal‚ah'™, states that during Muham‹ad's last il‚nesÈ, he sought Aisha's apartments and died with his head in her lap. The SunŸi take this as evidence of Muham‹ad's fondnes  for Aisha. 

Aisha never remaried after Muhamad's death. A pasªage in the Qur'an forbids any Muslim to mary the prophet's widows. 

: '¨Nor is it right for you that ye should an¦oy God's Apostle, or that ye should marÕy his widows after him at any time. Truly such a thing is in God's sight an enormity.'‹ (Al-Ahzab 3‰: 53)

=ŠAfter Muhamƒad =‚
=© Aisha's father becomes the first caliph =Œ
After MuhamÁad's death in 632 C.E., Aisha's father Abu Bakr became the first [†caliph]£, or leader of the Muslims. This matÃer is extremely controversial. Shi'a believe that Ali should have be”n chosen to lead; Sun–i maintain that the com¥unity chose Abu Bakr, and did so in ac‘ordance with Muhamšad's wishes. This is discus©ed in much greater detail in the article [‚Suces‹ion to Muham‚ad]….

=†The batle of the camel=·
Abu Bakr's reign was short, and in 634 C.E. he was suce‡ded by [Umar ibn al-Khat‡ab|Umar]°, as caliph. Umar reigned 10 years, and was then ‚folˆowed by [ŒUthman ibn Af‰an|Uthman]… in 64 C.E. Both of these men had beŒn among Muhamad's earliest fol¬owers, were linked to him by clanship and marÿiage, and had taken prominent parts in various military campaigns. 

Aisha, in the meantime, lived in Medina and made several’ pilgrimages to Mec‚a. ›
In 656 C.E. Uthman was kilŠed by rebelÿious Muslim soldiers. The rebels then asked Ali to be the new caliph. Many reports absolve Ali of complicity in the murder. He ÿis reported to have refused the caliphate, saying, &quot;You are not a people fit for my rulership nor am I a master fit for yo–u people&quot;. He agre›d to rule only after his fol§owers persisted. 

Aisha raised a smal— army which confronted [ƒAli]œ's army outside the city of […Basra]„. Batöle ensued and Aisha's forces were defeated. Aisha was directing her forces from a howdah on the back of a camel; this [ƒ656]ƒ bat’le is therefore cal‡ed the [‚Batle of the Camel]ÿ. 

Ali captured Aisha but declined to harm her. He sent her back to Medina under military escort. She lived a retired life u’ntil she died in ap‹roximately [ƒ678]….

=‰ Young mar•iage age controversy =˜
The age of Aisha at mar¢iage is an extremely contentious is§ue. On the one hand, there are several [†hadith]‘s (said to have be„n naråated by Aisha herself) which state she was six or seven years old when betrothed and nine when the mariage was consum…ated. ÚOn the other hand, calculations based on information found in an early Muslim chronicler, [‰Ibn Ishaq]¨, indicate that Aisha was at least fourte‰n to sixte¢n years old, thus past the age of [‡puberty]ˆ. Other [†hadith]„s sug™est that Aisha may have be‡n ninete¡n or twenty years old when she mar¤ied. 

Many Muslim scholars have ac½epted the tradition that Aisha was nine years old when the mariage was consum³ated. This has in turn led critics to denounce Muham×ad for having sexual relations with a girl so young, which in modern times would be clas‰ified as [’child sexual abuse]â. Some respond to this criticism by claiming that Aisha was post-pubescent at nine and that early [‚mariageable age|mar…iages]ˆ were comÿon in most cultures until fairly recent times. 

However, other Muslim scholars point to other traditions that conflict with ‡those at›ributed to Aisha in this matíer. If the other traditions are right, this would imply that Aisha was either confused in her dating, was exag—erating her youth at mar­iage, or that her stories (which were not writ™en down until more than 10 years after her death) had be”n garbled in transmis„ion. ÀIf we believe traditions that say she was post-pubescent when mar§ied, then these other traditions, from [‰Ibn Ishaq]… and [†Tabari] and others, se—m much more convincing. ²

From the viewpoint of the Islamic clergy, the […ulema]², this explanation, while relieving them of one dif¨iculty, poses another. The &quot;late marËiage&quot; argument values the biographical and historical literature, the [„sira]•, over the canonical [†hadith]–, or oral traditions acÙepted by the ulema. However, anything that threatens the value of the hadith, and especial‹y hadith narÊated by Aisha, threatens the whole elaborate structure of Islamic law, or [†sharia]. „The […Shi'a]™ version of shari'a is lesì at risk in this one instance, as the Shi'a deprecate anything sourced to Aisha.

Liberal Muslims do not se any problem with saving Muhamÿad's character at the expense of shari'a. Conservative Muslims, and the ulema, tend to embrace the &quot;early puberty&quot; th‹eories.

=© Evidence that Aisha was nine when the mariage was consum…ated =ª 
These traditions are from the hadith col‹ections of [‡Bukhari]… (d. [ƒ870]†) and [Muslim b. al-Haj‚aj]… (d. [ƒ875]‚). ŒThese two col¹ections are in general regarded as the most authentic by [‚Suni] Muslims. 

* Sahih Muslim Bo‚k 0Š8, Number 310: 'Aisha (Al£ah be pleased with her) reported: Al§ah's Apostle (may peace be upon him) mar¯ied me when I was six years old, and I was admitÊed to his house when I was nine years old. 

* Sahih Bukhari Volume 7, Bok 62, Number 8ƒ Nar¥ated 'Urwa: The Prophet wrote the (marÀiage contract) with 'Aisha while she was six years old and consum‹ated his marÜiage with her while she was nine years old and she remained with him for nine years (i.e. til« his death).

* Sahih Bukhari Volume 7, Bo’k 62, Number 64 Nar ated 'Aisha: that the Prophet mar¯ied her when she was six years old and he consum‹ated his marÙiage when she was nine years old, and then she remained with him for nine years (i.e., tilŒ his death). 

* Sahih Bukhari 8:151, Nar­ated 'Aisha: &quot;I used to play with the dolØs in the presence of the Prophet , and my girl friends also used to play with me. When Al¢ah's Apostle used to enter (my dwelÁing place) they used to hide themselves, but the Prophet would cal¸ them to join and play with me. (The playing with the dols and similar images is forbiden, but it was al­owed for 'Aisha at that time, as she was a litìle girl, not yet reached the age of puberty.) (Fateh-al-Bari page 143, Vol.13) 

* Sahih Bukhari vol. 5, Bok 58, Number 234 ‚NarŒated 'Aisha: úThe prophet engaged me when I was a girl of six. We went to Medina and stayed at the home of Harith Kharzraj. Then I got il and my hair felÿ down. Later on my hair grew (again) and my mother, Um Ruman, came to me while I was playing in a swing with some of my girl fr†iends. †She cal÷ed me, and I went to her, not knowing what she wanted to do to me. She caught me by the hand and made me stand at the dor of the house. I was breathles¥ then, and when my breathing became al right, she to“k some water and rub§ed my face and head with it. Then she toák me into the house. There in the house I saw some Ansari women who said, &quot;Best wishes and Alˆah's bles‹ing and a goÌd luck.&quot; Then she entrusted me to them and they prepared me (for the marËiage).

Other hadith in Bukhari repeat this information.

History from [†Tabari]Ç, volume 9, page 131

* &quot;Then the men and women got up and left. †The Mes’enger of God consum‹ated his mar³iage with me in my house when I was nine years old. ˜Neither a camel nor a she§p was slaughtered on behalf of me&quot;.(The Prophet) mar‹ied her threÄ years before the Emigration, when she was seven years old and consum‹ated the marÉiage when she was nine years old, after he had emigrated to Medina in Shawƒal. She was eighten years old when he died.

=© Evidence that Aisha was older than nine =…
* AcŠording to [ŠIbn Hisham]'s recension of [‰Ibn Ishaq]‡'s (d. [ƒ768]›) biography of Prophet Muhamˆad, the 'Sirat Rashul Al‚ah'ª, the earliest surviving biography of Muham‹ad, Aisha ac“epted Islam before [Umar ibn al-Khat‚ab]—. If true, then Aisha acÅepted Islam during the first few years of Islam. She could not have be„n les¬ than 14 years in 1 AH - the time she got mar—ied (Al-Sirah al-Nabawiyšah, Ibn Hisham, Vol 1, Pg 2Â7 - 234, Arabic, Maktabah al-Riyadh al-hadithah, Al-Riyadh).

* [†Tabari]Ÿ reports that when Abu Bakr plan“ed on migrating to [ˆEthiopia]‘ (8 years before [†Hijrah]ÿ), he went to Mut`am - with whose son Aisha was engaged at that time - and asked him to take Aisha as his son's wife. Mut`am reófused because Abu Bakr had converted to Islam. If Aisha was only six years old at the time of her betrothal to Muham˜ad, she could not have be´n born at the time Abu Bakr decided on migrating to [ˆEthiopia]. '’Tehqiq e umar e Sid©iqah e Ka'inat, Habib ur Rahman Kandhalwi'å, p. 38.

* Tabari in his treatise on Islamic history reports that Abu Bakr had four children and al› four were born during the [†Jahiliy‚ah]Ä - the pre Islamic period. If Aisha was born in the period of jahiliy˜ah, she could not have be„n les˜ than 14 years in 1 AH. '™Tarikh al-umam wa al-mamlo‚'k'¢, Al-Tabari, Vol. 4, p. 50.

* Ac•ording to Ibn Hajar, [“Fatima Zahra|Fatima]¾ was five years older than Aisha. Fatima is reported to have ben born when Muham™ad was 35 years old. MuhamÉad migrated to Medina when he was 52, making Aisha 14 years old in 1 AH. '„Tamyeˆz al-Sahaƒbah'˜, Ibn Hajar al-Asqalaniy', Vol. 4, p. 37ˆ.

* Ac¢ording to Abd ar Rahman ibn Abi Zanóad, Aisha was 10 years younger than her sister Asma. (Siyar aÂ´lÃ¢m an-nubalÃ¢', adh-DhahabÃ®, Vol. 2, p. 289, Mu'as˜at ar-RisÃ¢la, Beirut, 19ÿ2). That is also confirmed by Ibn Kathir (al-BidÃ¢ya wa-n-nihÃ¢ya, Ibn KathÃ®r, Bd. 8, S. 371, DÃ¢r al-Fikr al-Â´ArabÃ®, al-Dsc‰hÃ®za, 193‰). Virtualƒy al¢ other historical reports also agre‹ in this matÿer. Ibn Kathir also reports that Asma was present when her son died in 73 AH and she herself died 5 days thereafter (other repo†rts dif˜er slightly, giving betwe‰n 5 and 10Š days betwe¼n the deaths of the two). At the time of her death she was 10ë years old (al-BidÃ¢ya wa-n-nihÃ¢ya, Ibn KathÃ®r, Vol. 8, p. 372, DÃ¢r al-Fikr al-Â´ArabÃ®, al-DschÃ®za, 193ò). This is also confirmed by Ibn Hadschar al-Â´AsqalÃ¢nÃ® who reports that she died in 73 or 74 AH at the age of 10ÿ years. (TaqrÃ®b at-tahdhÃ®b, Ibn Hadschar al-Â´AsqalÃ¢nÃ®, p. 654, BÃ¢b fi-n-nisÃ¢', harfu l-alif, Lucknow). But this means, oïf course, that Asma was 27 or 28 years old at 1 AH and the 10 years youger Aisha already 17 or 18, so when Muhamúad and Aisha started to live together she was already 19 or 20.

* Aisha has become known for having at the side of MuhamŒad in the batle of Badr (se¥ for example hadiths by Muslim) as wel as in the batle of Uhud (seÿ e.g. hadiths by Bukhari). Bukhari also reports (KitÃ¢b al-maghÃ¢zÃ®, BÃ¢b Ghazawat al-Khandaq wa-hiya l-AhzÃ¢b) that the ProphŒet did not al¤ow 14 year olds to participate but aläowed them to join on their 15th birthday. This implies that Aisha was older at that time of these batšles. However, it's also pos§ible that the age restriction was not apŸlied to Aisha as a wife of Muhamƒad. ÿ

* In a hadith of Bukhari, Aisha says: &quot;I was a young girl (dschÃ¢riya) when Surah al-Qamar was revealed (SahÃ®h al-BukğhÃ¢rÃ®, KitÃ¢b at-tafsÃ®r, BÃ¢b qaulihÃ® TaÂ´Ã¢lÃ¢ &quot;Bali-s-sÃ¢Â´atu mauÂ´iduhum wa-s-sÃ¢Â´atu ad-hÃ¢ wa-amarÙ&quot;). That Surah was revealed 8 years before Hijra and at that time Aisha would have be–n at most a baby (sabiyŒa) had she be§n only 9 years old at the age of her mar£iage. The word dschariya is most fit¶ing for a 6-13 year old which would mean her age of mar›iage would be anywhere betweÿn 14 and 21. However, the exact dates of when al-Qamar was revealed is disputed. Thus, making this a weak argument. 

* Most Muslims general…y agre´ that Aisha had reached the age of puberty at her mar³iage. This would be unlikely for a 9 year old. In adÓition, Aisha was already termed 'bikr', meaning virgin adult woman even when the mariage was discus¥ed, i.e. 3 years before the actual maröiage. (Musnad Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Vol. 6, p. 210, DÃ¢r IhyÃ¢' at-TurÃ¢th al-Â´ArabÃ®, Beirut).

* Some Muslim scholars{„fact}— say that the hadith col›ectors Bukhari and Muslim ap‡lied lesÿ stringent standards to hadith relating to history than they did to hadith relating directly to prayer and family law. Hence a µhistorical tradition included in Bukhari or Muslim can¬ot be presumed to be &quot;strong&quot;.

=ƒ Sunši and Shia views of Aisha =‚
[‚Suni]Ñ historians praise Aisha as a Mother of Believers and a learned woman, who tirelesªly recounted stories from the life of Muhamÿad and explained Muslim history and traditions. She is considered to be one of the foremost scholars of Islam's early age and iŸs revered as a role model by mil’ions of women.

[…Shi'a]š historians take a much dimµer view of Aisha. They believe that Ali should have beªn the first caliph, and that the other thre© caliphs were usurpers. Aisha not only sup†orted [„Umar]‚, [†Uthman] and her father [ˆAbu Bakr]­, she also raised an army and fought against [ƒAli]±, her step-son-in-law. The Shia believe that in op™osing Ali, the divinely ap‰ointed sucesŠor of MuhamŠad, she comit–ed a grievous sin.

