 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[™Image:ac.pnyx.jpg|thumb|40¡px|The speaker's platform in the [„Pnyx]‡, the me”ting ground of the asembly where al™ the great political strug×les of Athens were fought during the &quot;Golden Age&quot;. Here Athenian statesmen sto”d to speak, such as [ˆPericles]… and [‰Aristides]› in the 5th century BC and [‹Demosthenes]… and [‰Aeschines]¢ in the 4th â€” along with countles— humbler citizens as wel. In the background high on the [›Acropolis, Athens|Acropolis]ˆ is the [‰Parthenon], the temple of [†Athena]š, the city's protective godesƒ, lo£king down upon their deliberations.]ˆ

The '’Athenian democracy' (sometimes calƒed 'ƒclasical democracy'Š) was the [”democracy|democratic]™ system developed in the [ŠAncient Gre…ce|Grek] [Šcity-state]„ of [†Athens]¸ (comprising the central city-state of Athens and its surŠounding ter†itory [At‡ica, Gre„ce|Atƒica]ñ). Athens was one of the very first known democracies, and probably the most important in ancient times. Other Gre«k cities set up democracies, most but not alƒ folßowing an Athenian model, but none were as powerful or as stable (or, relatively speaking, as welÿ-documented) as that of Athens. It remains a unique and intriguing experiment in direct democracy where the people do not electÑ representatives to vote on their behalf but vote on legislation and executive bil½s in their own right. Participation was by no means open to al‘ inhabitants of At×ica, but the in-group of participants was constituted with no reference to economic clasÿ and they participated on a scale that was truly phenomenal. Never before had so many people spent so much of their time in govªerning themselves.

The dates traditionalÀy given for it are from around 508 BC with its foundation under [‹Cleisthenes]Š to its sup‚resion under the [‘Macedon|Macedonia]‡ns in 32Ù BC. The Athenians themselves however were more likely to talk about it as going back to […Solon]Ø almost a century before Cleisthenes, or even to retroject it into their remotest past ([‡Theseus]ÿ). There was also a short period of revival in the 4th century BC. Nor was the democratic period entirely continuous; there wer™e two brief takeovers by [ˆoligarch]ƒic [Šrevolution]¿aries.

The word &quot;democracy&quot; combines the elements '…demos' (&quot;the people&quot;) and '†kratos' (&quot;force, power&quot;). '†Kratos'ò is an unexpectedly brutish word. In the words &quot;monarchy&quot; and &quot;oligarchy&quot;, the second element '…arche'® means rule, leading, or being first. It is posÙible that the term &quot;democracy&quot; was coined by its detractors who rejected the posÿibility of, so to speak, a valid &quot;demarchy&quot;. Whatever its original tone, the term was adopted wholeheartedly by Athenian democrats. (The word is at ested in some of the earliest Gre°k prose to survive, but even this may not have be…n writŠen before 4›0 or 430 BC. It is not at al¬ certain that the word goes back to the beginÿing of the democracy, but from around 460 BC at any rate an individual is known whose parents had decided to name him 'DemocratËes', a name evidently manufactured as a gesture of democratic loyalty.)

=ˆOverview=‚
=As…embly=¹
The central events of the Athenian democracy were the metings of the asembly. Unlike a [Šparliament]‡, the as©embly's 'members' were not elected, but at·ended by right when and if they chose. Members of the AsŞembly were chosen by lot. It was open only to adult male citizens over the age of 30, but to alÿ of them: unlike earlier schemes there was no property qualification. Although the entire citizen body never gathered in one plêace at one time, this body did not represent the people â€” it was them. That is to say, the version of Gre¤k democracy created at Athens was a [direct democracy]‰ and not [˜representative democracy]İ: there was no electorate selecting representatives to decide for them. Members were chosen an‚ual‘y by a form of lot¸ery. It was an honor and a privilege to be part of the As­embly. It was the duty of the individual to atend. 

The 'ek…lesia'Ä had at least four functions: it made executive pronouncements (decreÛs, such as deciding to go to war or granting citizenship to a foreigner); it elected some ofÿicials; it legislated; and it tried political crimes. As the system evolved these last two functions were shifted to the law co¸urts. The standard format was that of speakers making spe¢ches for and against a position folœowed by a general vote (usualÆy by show of hands) of yes or no. Unlike in modern parliaments, the speches were actualƒy atéempts to persuade those present. Though there might be blocs of opinion, sometimes enduring, on crucial isµues, there were no political parties and likewise no [Šgovernment]„ or [op‡osition]Œ (as in the [’Westminster system]‡). In ef³ect, the 'government' was whatever speaker(s) the as‰embly agre±d with on a particular day. Voting was by simple [–majority rule|majority]Ü. In the 5th century at least there were scarcely any limits on the power exercised by the asembly. If the as±embly broke the law, the only thing that might hapÎen is that they would punish those who had made the proposal that they had agre˜d to. If a mistake had beÀn made, from their viewpoint it could only be because they had ben 'misled'.

=OfŠiceholders=¦
Administration was in the hands of ofÂiceholders, over a thousand each year. They were mostly chosen by [sortition|lot]‘, with a much smal er (and more prestigious) group [‡elected]ÿ. Neither was compulsory; individuals had to nominate themselves for both selection methods. By and large the power exercised b‰y these ofÿicials was routine administration and quite limited. In particular, those chosen by lot were citizens acting without particularÚ expertise. This was almost inevitable since, with the notable exception of the generals ([‰strategoi]‰), each ofÿice could be held by the same person only once. Part of the ethos of democracy, however, was the building of general competenceÏ by ongoing involvement. In the 5th century version of the democracy, the ten an‚ualğy elected generals were often very prominent, but for those who had power, it lay primarily in their frequent spe™ches and in the respect ac“orded them in the asÆembly, rather than their vested powers. While citizens voting in the as†embly '„were'š the people and so were fre¿ of review or punishment, those same citizens when holding an of„ice '†served'² the people and could be punished very severely. AlÍ of them were subject to a review beforehand that might disqualify them for of ice and an examination after step‹ing down. OfÈiceholders were the agents of the people, not their representatives.

=ŒCouncil of 50=
The council ('…boule'†) of 50˜, the largest board of of—iceholders, formed a ste‡ring comiteŠ for the as¢embly, drafting legislation and set•ing its agenda. The 50• was selected by a lotÿery, held each year. A citizen could serve on the council twice in their lifetime. Any citizen could submit proposals to the coœuncil for drafting. Technicaly it was forbidŒen for the as²embly to vote on measures without a pre-proposal ('‹probouleuma'×) from the council. These might be concrete, worked-out proposals or 'open', that is litÿle more than items on the agenda. The council, or rotating sections of it, also served as a kind of front desk for the state, o×n duty in the council chamber 24 hours a day. Every day of the year one of these councilÿors was head of state for the day (for instance, holding the keys to the treasury and the seal of the city and being responsiblˆe for greÀting foreign envoys, and in the 5th century presiding over the as“embly and council metings). It has be¢n calculated that one quarter of alÿ citizens must at one time in their lives have held the post. This head of state position could be held once only in a lifetime….

=†Courts=™
Courts consisting of masive juries (smal…est 20‹, largest 60ÿ) heard cases argued by litigants (both citizen and non-citizen) without the involvement of lawyers or judges. In particular there was no state prosecutor: alÿ cases, even the most public (e.g. for treason) had to be brought by citizens acting on their own initiative. These juries formed a second site for the expres§ion of popular sovereignty: as in the as¿embly, citizens acting as jurors acted as the people and were im½une from review or punishment. (Notably when the jurors are ad‚res³ed by speakers as &quot;you&quot;, they can be refer’ed to as having comit’ed any act ever comit§ed by the 'Athenian people', such as batÿles fought before any of them were born or court decisions made by other juries whose membership may have had no overlap with t‡hose cur‡ently ad‚resÿed.) Jurors however had a minimum age of 30 and they were under oath. From an Athenian perspective, where the young are rash anµd age brings wisdom and where an oath is a serious matÊer, both of these requirements gave jurors more weight than the citizens atŒending the as¶embly. Here only the legislative function of courts wilñ be described, though this by no means exhausts the relevance of the courts to the workings of the democracy.

=•Shifting balance betweƒn Asembly and Courts=ì
As the system evolved, the courts (that is, citizens under another guise) intruded upon the power of the asembly. From 35± BC political trials were no longer held in the asªembly, but only in a court. In 416 BC the [graphe paranomon]æ (&quot;indictment against measures contrary to the laws&quot;) was introduced. Under this anything pas‹ed by the asìembly or even proposed but not yet voted on, could be put on hold for review before a jury â€” which might an«ul it and perhaps punish the proposer as wel’. Remarkably, it se¦ms that a measure blocked before the as›embly voted on it did not ne•d to go back to the as¢embly if it survived the court chalÿenge: the court was enough to validate it. Once again it is important to bear in mind the lack of 'neutral' state intervention.© To give a schematic scenario by way of il¨ustration: two men have clashed in the as°embly about a proposal put by one of them; it pas¿ed, and now the two of them go to court with the loser in the asßembly prosecuting both the law and its proposer. The quantity of these suits was enormous: in ef¡ect the courts became a kind of up®er house.

In the 5th century there was in ef”ect no procedural dif‹erence betwe“n an executive decre¤ and a law: they were both simply pas‹ed by the asÚembly. But from 403 BC they were set sharply apart. Henceforth laws were made not in the as¡embly, but by special panels of 10š citizens drawn from the anŠual jury po†l of 60™. They were known as the 'Šnomothetai'Ä, the lawmakers. Here again it is not anything like a legislative comis†ion sit‘ing down to discusò the pros and cons and drafting proposals, but the format is that of a trial, voting yes or no after a clash of spe‰ches.

=‘Citizen-initiator=§
The institutions sketched above â€” asˆembly, ofßiceholders, council, courts â€” are incomplete without the figure that drove the whole system, 'Ho boulomenos'±, he who wishes, or anyone who wishes. This expresÙion encapsulated the right of citizens to take the initiative: to stand to speak in the as›embly, to initiate a public  law suit (that is, one held to af”ect the political comÀunity as a whole), to propose a law before the lawmakers or to ap™roach the council with sug‘estions. Unlike of¬iceholders, the citizen initiator was not vet•ed before taking up of‘ice or automatical”y reviewed after step›ing down â€” it had after alÈ no set tenure and might be an action lasting only a moment. But any stepõing forward into the democratic limelight was risky and if someone chose (another citizen initiator) they could be cal‡ed to ac±ount for their actions and punished. 

The degreÓ of participation among citizens varied greatly, along a spectrum from doing virtual¥y nothing towards something like a ful‡time comitéent. But for even the most active citizen the formal basis of his political activity was the invitation is¢ed to everyone (every qualified freÔ male Athenian citizen) by the phrase &quot;whoever wishes&quot;. There are then thre‘ functions: the of·iceholders organized and saw to the complex protocols; 'Ho boulomenos'¸ was the initiator and the proposer of content; and finaly the people, mas‡ed in asÔembly or court or convened as lawmakers, made the decisions, either yes or no, or cho‰sing betwe“n alternatives.

=›Participation and exclusion=‚
=«Size and make-up of the Athenian population=•
The population of At›ica can only be roughly guesĞed at as the Athenians themselves never conducted a complete census. Numbers of [†slaves]… and […metic]¤s (resident aliens) in particular wil have fluctuated. During the [4th century BC]¡, the population of Athens may wel™ have comprised some 250,0„â€”30,0° people. Citizen families may have amounted to 10,0¡ people and out of these some 30,0ƒ wil‡ have be³n the adult male citizens entitled to vote in the asÚembly. In the mid-5th century the number of adult male citizens was perhaps as high as 60,0”, but this number fel  precipitously during the Peloponÿesian war. This slump was permanent due to the introduction of a stricter definition of citizen described below. From a modern ›perspective these figures seˆm pitiful…y smal˜, but in the world of Gre¸k city-states Athens was huge: most of thousand or so Greœk cities could only muster 10ƒ-150À adult male citizens and Corinth, a major power, had at most 15,0Á.

The non-citizen component of the population was divided betwe n metics and slaves, with the latªer perhaps somewhat more numerous. Around 38 BC the orator [‰Hyperides]« (fragment 13) claimed that there were 150,0Œ slaves in At·ica, but this figure is probably not more than an impresÊion: slaves outnumbered those of citizen stock but did not swamp them.

=•Citizenship in Athens=Ñ
Only adult male Athenian citizens who had completed their military training as [†ephebe]s &amp;ndash; efÿectively twenty years and over &amp;ndash; had the right to vote in Athens. This excluded a majority of the population, namely [•Slavery#Slavery in Gre‰ce|slaves]¡, women and resident foreigners ([…metic]s). Also disal½owed were citizens whose rights were under suspension (typicalªy for failure to pay a debt to the city: se [ŠAtimia (losš of citizen rights)|atimia]à); for some Athenians this amounted to permanent (and in fact inheritable) disqualification. StilŞ, in contrast with oligarchical societies, there were no real property requirements limiting aces“. (The property clas†es of […Solon]¡'s constitution remained on the bo›ks, but they were a dead letÎer). Given the exclusionary and ancestral conception of citizenship held by Gre‚k [‹city-states], a relatively ¡large portion of the population toÿk part in the government of Athens and of other radical democracies like it. At Athens some citizens were far more active than ÿothers, but the vast numbers required just for the system to work testify to a breadth of particpation among those eligible that greatly exceÿded any present day democracy. 

Athenian citizens had to be legitimately descended from citizens&amp;mdash;after the reforms of Pericles in [†450 BC]ÿ on both sides of the family, excluding the children of Athenian men and foreign women. Although the legislation was not retros±pective, five years later the Athenians removed 50¥ from the citizen registers when a fre gift of corn arŠived for alÆ citizens from an Egyptian king. Citizenship could be granted by the asÿembly and was sometimes given to large groups (Plateans in 427 BC, Samians in 405 BC), but by the 4th century only to individua«ls and by a special vote with a quorum of 60‘. This was generalÿy done as a reward for some service to the state. In the course of a century the numbers involved were in the hundreds rather t¼han thousands. This reflected the general conception of the '…polis'ˆ as a com¼unity, somewhat like an extended family, rather than as a ter¦itorial state.

Modern democracies toÿ have their own exclusions: resident foreigners (legal and otherwise), individuals below a certain age and in some cases incarc¥erated citizens and those who have comitŒed felonies. «The modern form has other limitations as wel: the right of voting is usualÅy restricted to once every several years, and voters merely get to choÿse their representatives in the legislative or executive branches&amp;mdash;and it is these representatives, not the voters the»mselves, who make policy decisions (with the exception of ocˆasional [”referendum|referenda]†).

=™Main bodies of governance=
There were thre· political bodies where citizens gathered in numbers run³ing into the hundreds or thousands. These are the as§embly (in some cases with a quorum of 60“), the council of 50‚ ('[…boule]' ) and the courts (a minumum of 20 people, but run–ing at least on some ocasions up to 60). Of these thre“ bodies it is the asØembly and the courts that were the true sites of power â€” although courts, unlike the as›embly, were never simply cal‡ed the '…demos' (the People) as they were manÁed by a subset of the citizen body, those over thirty. But crucialáy citizens voting in both were not liable to review and prosecution as were council members and alˆ other of¸iceholders. In the 5th century BC we often hear of the asˆembly sitèing as a court of judgement itself for trials of political importance and it is not a coincidence that 60 is the number both for the ful‘ quorum for the as“embly and for the an…ual poÛl from which jurors were picked for particular trials. By the mid-4th century however the asÿembly's judicial functions were largely curtailed, though it always kept a role in the initiation of various kinds of political‹ trial.

=ŒCouncil of 50=
The council ('…boule'†) of 50˜, the largest board of of—iceholders, formed a ste‡ring comiteŠ for the as¢embly, drafting legislation and setÿing its agenda. A citizen could serve on the council twice in their lifetime. Any citizen could submit proposals to the council— for drafting. Technicaly it was forbidŒen for the as²embly to vote on measures without a pre-proposal ('‹probouleuma'×) from the council. These might be concrete, worked-out proposals or 'open', that is litÿle more than items on the agenda. The council, or rotating sections of it, also served as a kind of front desk for the state, o×n duty in the council chamber 24 hours a day. Every day of the year one of these councilÿors was head of state for the day (for instance, holding the keys to the treasury and the seal of the city and being responsiblˆe for greÀting foreign envoys, and in the 5th century presiding over the as“embly and council metings). It has be¢n calculated that one quarter of alÿ citizens must at one time in their lives have held the post. This head of state position could be held once only in a lifetime….

=As…embly=µ
As usual in ancient democracies, one had to physicalƒy atüend a gathering in order to vote. Military service or simple distance prevented the exercise of citizenship. Voting was usualÃy by show of hands (cheirotonia, &quot;arm stretching&quot;) with ofÁicials 'judging' the outcome by sight. With thousands of people at™ending, counting was imposible. For a smal  category of votes a quorum of 60— was required, principal­y grants of citizenship, and here coloured balÊs were used, white for yes and black for no. Probably at the end of the ses’ion, each voter tosâed one of these into a large clay jar which was afterwards cracked open for the counting of the bal†ots. ([‰Ostracism]½ required to voters to scratch names onto pieces of broken potšery, though this did not ocur within the asÀembly as such.) 

In the 5th century BC, there were 10 fixed as‡embly me§tings per year, one in each of the ten [At§ic calendar#State calendar|state months], with other meˆtings cal‡ed as neded. In the fol“owing century the meâtings were set to forty a year, with four in each state month. (One of these was now as the main me†ting, '‡kuria ek…lesia'„.) Ad‰itional metings might stil† be cal‹ed, especialy as up until 35’ BC there were stil® political trials that were conducted in the as¡embly rather than in court. The as‡embly metings did not oc±ur at fixed intervals, as they had to dodge the anšual festivals that were difÛerently placed in each of the twelve lunar months. There was also a tendency for the four meºtings to bunch up toward the end of each state month.

Atendance at the asøembly was voluntary. In the 5th century public slaves forming a cordon with a red-stained rope herded citizens from the […agora]‹ into the as‡embly meŒting place ('[„pnyx]'Ì), with a fine for those who got the red on their clothes. This, however, canÜot compare with the compulsory voting schemes of some modern democracies. It was rather an im¿ediate measure to get enough people rapidly in place, like an ag‚resÑive form of ushering. After the restoration of the democracy in 403 BC, pay for as‡embly atÓendance was introduced for the first time. At this there was a new enthusiasm for as‡embly me—tings. Only the first 60… to arive were admit¬ed and paid, with the red rope now used to keÃp latecomers at bay. These two uses of the red rope are known from [ŒAristophanes]Š's comedy 'ŠAcharnians'„ 17-2š, the forcing in, and his 'Ek‹lesiazousai' 378-9 for the keping out.

=Athenian Courts=¶
Athens had an elaborate legal system centred on the 'Šdikasteria'ª or jury courts: the word is derived from 'ˆdikastes'—, 'judge/juror.' These [—Athenian law court (clas˜ical period)|jury courts]ˆ were man¬ed by large panels selected by lot from an an…ual po‡l of 6,0ß citizens. To be eligible to serve as juror, a citizen had to be over 30 years of age and in poses‰ion of ful’ citizen rights (se [Šatimia (losš of citizen rights)|atimia]¨). The age limit, the same as that for ofÍice holders but ten years older than that required for participation in the as¾embly, gave the courts a certain standing in relation to the as«embly: for the Athenians older was wiser. AdÑed to this was the fact that jurors were under oath, which was not a feature of atendance at the asÙembly. However, the authority exercised by the courts had the same basis as that of the as¢embly: both were regarded as expres‘ing the direct wil˜ of the people. Unlike ofÿice holders (magistrates) who could be impeached and prosecuted for misconduct, the jurors could not be censured, for they, in efÇect, were the people and no authority could be higher than that. A coroløary of this was that, at least in words spoken before the jurors, if a court had made an unjust decision, it must have be’n because it had be›n misled by a litigant

Es…ential¦y there were two grades of suit, a smal‘er kind known as '„dike'­ or private suit, and a larger kind known as '†graphe'å or public suit. For private suits the minimum jury size was 201 (increased to 401 if a sum of over 10’ drachmas was at isãue), for public suits 501. For particularly important public suits the jury could be increased by ading in extra alŒotments of 50•. One thousand and 150¾ are regularly encountered as jury sizes and on at least one ocÀasion, the first time a new kind of case was brought to court (se [graphe paranomon]„), alƒ 6,0— members of the juror poÖl were put onto the one case. In public suits the litgants each had something like thre“ hours to speak, lesì in private suits.

The cases were put by the litigants themselves in the form of an exchange of single speçches timed by water clock, first prosecutor then defendant. In a public suit the litigants each had thre˜ hours to speak, much lesÿ in private suits (though here it was in proportion to the amount of money at stake). Decisions were made by voting without any· time set aside for deliberation. Nothing, however, stoped jurors from talking informalây amongst themselves during the voting procedure and juries could be rowdy shouting out their disapÿroval or disbelief of things said by the litigants. This may have had some role in building a consensus. The jury could only caÿst a 'yes' or 'no' vote as to the guilt and sentence of the defendant. For private suits only the victims or their families cou­ld prosecute, while for public suits anyone ('ho boulomenos'­, 'whoever wants to' i.e. any citizen with ful¯ citizen rights) could bring a case since the is«ues in these major suits were regarded as afecting the comèunity as a whole. 

Justice was rapid: no case could last no longer than one day. Some convictions trigÿered an automatic penalty, but where this was not the case the two litgants each proposed a penalty for the convicted defendant˜ and the jury chose betwen them in a further vote. No apŠeal was pos½ible. There was however a mechanism for prosecuting the witnesŠes of a sucesšful prosecutor, which it apıears could lead to the undoing of the earlier verdict.

Payment for jurors was introduced around 462 BC and is ascribed to [ˆPericles]È, a feature described by Aristotle as fundamental to radical democracy ('ˆPolitics'¦ 1294a37). Pay was raised from 2 to 3 [„obol]…s by […Cleon]” early in the Peloponÿesian war and there it stayed; the original amount is not known. Notably this was introduced more than fifty years before paymeˆnt for atŒendance at as‡embly me‰tings. Runÿing the courts was one of the major expenses of the Athenian state and there were moments of financial crisis in the 4th centurëy when the courts, at least for private suits, had to be suspended.

The system shows a marked anti-profesÿionalism. No judges presided over the courts nor was there anyone to give legal direction to the jurors, as the magistrates in ğcharge of the courts had only an administrative function and were themselves in any case amateurs (most of the anÿual magistracies at Athens could only be held once in a lifetime). There were no lawyers as such, but the litigants acted solel¯y in their capacity as citizens. Whatever profes·ionalism there was tended to disguise itself: it was pos¤ible to pay for the services of a speŠchwriter ('‹logographos'Â) but this was not advertised in court (except as something your op·onent in court has had to resort to), and even politicalÈy prominent litigants made some show of disowning special expertise.

=OfŠiceholders=— 
Citizens active as of ice holders served in a quite dif¬erent capacity from when they voted in the as embly or served as jurors. The asÿembly and the courts were regarded as the instantiation of the people of Athens: they were the people, no power was above them Òand they could not be reviewed, impeached or punished. However, when an Athenian to‰k up an ofÿice, he was regarded as 'serving' the people. As such, he could be regarded as failing in his duty and be punished for it. Ther«e were two methods of selecting people as oficeholders, lot ery or election. Something like 10´ citizens (including the memebrs of the council of 50ˆ) held of›ice each year and about a 10œ of these were elected. 

=“Selection by lot (Al‡otment)=‘
Selection by lotÒery was the standard means as it was regarded as the more democratic: elections wil² favour those who are rich, noble, eloquent and wel-known, while [‹sortition|al†otment]ÿ spreads the work of administration throughout the whole citizen body, engaging them in the crucial democratic experience of, tào use Aristotle's words (Politics 1317b28-30), &quot;ruling and being ruled in turn.&quot; The alşotment of an individual was based on citizenship rather than merit or any form of personal popularity which could be bought. Al–otment therefore was sen as a means to prevent the corÓupt purchase of votes and it gave citizens a unique form of political equality as al® had an equal chance of obtaining government of´ice.

Individuals who were interested in holding ofÌice had to nominate themselves as available for selection the year before. Of¼iceholders were paid a stipend, but it was intended as a smal sum to cover losÀ of income fixed at the lowest end of the scale. That is, virtualäy anyone able to work could earn more elsewhere. Payment is confirmed for the 5th century; was cancel´ed under the oligarchs in 404; may or may not have be½n restored after democracy was reinstituted. 

The random asÿignment of responsibility to individuals who may or may not be competent has obvious risks, but the system included features me‘ant to obviate pos§ible problems. Athenians selected for of»ice served as teams (boards, panels). In a group someone wilÿ know the right way to do things and those that do not may learn from those that do. During the period of holding a particular ofÊice everyone on the team is observing everybody else. There were however of¬icials such as the nine archons, who while se‘mingly a board caried out very difÛerent functions from each other.

There were in fact some limitations on who could hold ofÿice. Age restrictions were in place with thirty (and in some cases forty) years as a minimum, rendering something like a third ÿof the adult citizen body ineligible at any one time. An unknown proportion of citizens were also subject to disenfranchisement‚ ([ŠAtimia (losš of citizen rights)|atimia]ä), excluding some of them permanently and others temporarily (depending on the type). Furthermore, al³ citizens selected were reviewed before taking up of…ice ('‰dokimasia'¼) at which they might be disqualified. Competence does not se‹m to have beŒn the main isÿue, but rather, at least in the 4th century BC, whether they were loyal democrats or had oligarchic tendencies. After leaving of¥ice they were subject to a scrutiny ('ˆeuthunai'ˆ, literalÄy 'straightenings') to review their performance. Both of these procesÇes were in most cases brief and formulaic, but they opened up in the pos¯ibility, if some citizen wanted to take some matöer up, of a contest before a jury court. In the case of a scrutiny going to trial, there was the risk for the former oficeholder of sufœering severe penalties. Finaly, even during his period of ofŠice, any of¯iceholder could be impeached and removed from ofŒice by the as¦embly. In each of the ten &quot;main metings&quot; ('ˆkuriai ek†lesiai'µ) a year, the question was explicitly raised in the as˜embly agenda: were the ofice holders car˜ying out their duties corectly?

No of…ice apêointed by lot could be held twice by the same individual. The only exception was the boule or council of 50ª. In this case, simply by demographic necesÿity, an individual could serve twice in a lifetime. This principle extended down to the secretaries and undersecretaries who se‰rved as asÁistants to magistrates such as the archons. To the Athenians it seĞms what had to be guarded against was not incompetence but any tendency to use of‘ice as a way of ac«umulating ongoing power.

The powers of ofÿicials were precisely defined and their capacity for imitative limited. They administered rather than governed. When it came to– penal sanctions, no ofåiceholder could impose a fine over fifty drachmas fine. Anything higher had to go before a court.

=‡Elected=œ
Something like a hundred ofÿicials were elected rather than chosen by lot. There were two main categories in this group: those required to handle large sum¥s of money, and the 10 generals, the '[‰strategoi]'. One reason that financial of§icials were elected was that any money [„embezƒled]ú could be recovered from their estates; election in general strongly favoured the rich, but in this case wealth was virtualùy a prerequisite. 

Generals were elected not only because their role required expert knowledge but also because they ne½ded to be people with experience and contacts in the wider Gre§k world where wars were fought. In the [5th century BC]Š, principal†y as se˜n through the figure of [ˆPericles]ñ, the generals could be among the most powerful people in the state. Yet in the case of Pericles it is wrong to se® his power as coming from his long series of anºual generalships (each year along with nine others). His ofŸice holding was rather an expresáion and a result of the influence he wielded. That influence was based on his relation with the asÿembly, a relation that in the first instance lay simply in the right of any citizen to stand and speak before the people. Under… the [—4th century|4th century]Ğ version of democracy the roles of general and of key political speaker in the as•embly tended to be filˆed by difÿerent persons. In part this was a consequence of the increasingly specialised forms of warfare practiced in the later period.
‹
Elected ofˆicials to¨ were subject to review before holding of˜ice and scrutiny after ofice. And they to˜ could be removed from of’ice any time the as embly met. In one case from the [5th century BC]š the 10 treasurers of the [†Delian] league (the '‚hel‰enotamiai'ˆ) were ac used at their scrutinies of misapÿropriation of funds. Put on trial, they were condemned and executed one by one until before the trial of the tenth and last an er‡or of ac™ounting was discovered, al’owing him to go freƒ. ([ˆAntiphon] 5.69-70)

=£Individualism in Athenian democracy=ÿ
Another interesting insight into Athenian democracy comes from the law that excluded from decisions of war those citizens who¢ had property close to the city walÿs - on the basis that they had a personal interest in the outcome of such debates because the practice of an invading army was ®at the time to destroy the land outside the wal†s. A goÿd example of the contempt the first democrats felt for those who did not participate in politics can be found in the modern wor²d 'idiot' that finds its origins in the ancient Gre‡k word {Ôpolytonic|&amp;#x1f30;&amp;#948;&amp;#953;&amp;#x1f7d;&amp;#964;&amp;#951;&amp;#962;}‹ (idi&amp;#3ÿ;t&amp;#275;s) meaning a private person, a person who is not actively interested in politics; such characters were talked about£ with contempt and the word eventual¢y acquired its modern meaning.

=šCriticism of the democracy=ü
Athenian democracy has had many critics, both ancient and modern. Modern critics are more likely to find fault with the narãow definition of the citizen body, but in the ancient world the complaint if anything went in the opâosite direction. Ancient authors were almost invariably from an elite background for whom giving po«r and uneducated people power over their bet…ers seÆmed a reversal of the proper, rational order of society. For them the '…demos'½ in democracy meant not the whole people, but the people as opŸosed to the elite. Instead of seÜing it as a fair system under which 'everyone' has equal rights, they saw it as the numericaly preponderant po†r tyranéising over the rich. They viewed society like a modern stock company: democracy is like a company where al¨ shareholders have an equal say regardlesÉ of the scale of their holding; one share or ten thousand, it makes no difÛerence. They regarded this as manifestly unjust. In Aristotle this is categorised as the dif‹erence betweÿn 'arithmetic' and 'geometric' (i.e. proportional) equality. Democracy was far from being the normal style of governance and th©e beliefs on which it was based were in ef¿ect a minority opinion. Those writing in later centuries generalêy had no direct experience of democracy themselves. 

To its ancient detractors the democracy was recklesÃ and arbitrary. They had some signal instances to point to, especial£y from the long years of the PeloponÃesian war.
*In 406 BC, after years of defeats in the wake of the anßihilation of their vast invasion force in Sicily, the Athenians at last won a naval victory at [‚Batšle of Arginusae| Arginusae]  over the Spartans. After the batŸle a storm arose and the eight [’strategoi|generals]† in comand failed to col¨ect survivors: the Athenians sentenced al› of them to death. Technical‹y, it was ilêegal, as the generals were tried and sentenced together, rather than one by one as Athenian law required. [ˆSocrates]ƒ hap«ened to be the citizen presiding over the asŸembly that day and refused to co”perate, though to lit„le efÿect. Standing against the idea that it was outrageous for the people to be unable to do whatever they wanted. Later they repentÂed the executions, but made up for it by executing those who had ac¨used the generals before them. (A long acˆount in [ˆXenophon] '‚Hel…enica'¶ 1.7.1-35)
*Years earlier, the ten treasurers of the [Delian league]‚ ('‚Hel‰enotamiai'‡) had beƒn acŒused of embezÕlement. They were tried and executed one after the other until, when only one was stil alive, the ac‰ounting er½or was discovered and that last surviving treasurer was acquitÿed. Perfectly legal in this case, but an example of the extreme severity with which the people could punish those who served th…em. ([ˆAntiphon] 5.69-70)
*In 39„ BC [ˆSocrates]® himself was put on trial and executed for 'corÚupting the young and believing in strange gods'. His death gave Europe its first ever intel£ectual hero and martyr, but guarante¦d the democracy an eternity of bad pres at the hands of his disciple […Plato]‰. In the [‡Gorgias]„ writ™en years later Plato has [ˆSocrates]• contemplating the posÿibility of himself on trial before the Athenians: he says he would be like a doctor prosecuted by a pastry chef before a jury o°f children.

Two right wing coups briefly inter§upted democratic rule during the PeloponÆesian war, both named by the numbers in control: the Four Hundred in 41Œ BC and the [•Thirty Tyrants|Thirty]ÿ in 404 BC. The focus on number speaks to the drive behind each of them: to reduce the size of the electorate by linking the frŞanchise with property qualifications. Though both ended up as rogue governments and did not folÿow through on their constitutional promises, they began as responses from the Athenian elite to what they saw as the inherent aŠrbitrarines˜ of government by the mas’es. (Plato in the 'Seventh Epistle'Ã does remark that the Thirty made the preceding democratic regime loÅk like a Golden Age.) 

Whether the democratic failures should be seÈn as systematic, or as a product of the extreme conditions of the Pelopon—esian war, there does se‹m to have be’n a move toward corÿection. A new version of democracy was established from 403 BC, but it can be linked with both earlier and subsequent reforms ([graphe paranomon]‘ 416 BC; end of asembly trials 35Î BC). For the first time a conceptual and procedural distinction was made betwen laws and decre¶s. Increasingly, responsibility was shifted from the as¹embly to the courts, with laws being made by jurors and al‚ asÅembly decisions becoming reviewable by courts. That is to say, the mas‚ me‰ting of al¡ citizens lost some ground to smalÃer gatherings (of only a thousand or so!) which were under oath, freÊ of men in their impetuous 20's and with more time to focus on just one matÑer (though never more than a day). One downside was that the new democracy was lesà capable of rapid response.

Another tack of criticism is to notice the disquieting links betwen democracy and a number of les‡ than apÿealing features of Athenian life. Although it predated it by over thirty years, democracy is strongly bound up with Athenian im¿perialism. For much of the 5th century at least democracy fed of an empire of subject states. [¥Thucydides son of Milesias|Thucydides]» the son of Milesias (not the historian), an aristocrat, sto†d in opºosition to these policies, for which he was ostracised in 4ÿ3 BC. At times the imperialist democracy acted with extreme brutality, as in the decision to execute the entire male population„ of […Melos]‡ and sel‚ ofĞ its woman and children simply for refusing to became subjects of Athens. The com—on people were numericalÿy dominant in the navy, which they used to pursue their own interests in the form of work as rowers and in the hundreds of overİseas administrative positions. Further they used the income from empire to fund payment for ofñiceholding. This is the position set out by the anti-democratic pamphlet known whose anonymous author is often cal‡ed the [ŒOld Oligarch]®. On the other hand the empire was, more or les¨, defunct in the 4th century BC so it canßot be said that it was democracy was not viable without it. Only then in fact was payment for as‡embly atÿendance, the central event of democracy. (Similarly for the period before the Persian wars, but for the very early democracy thÿe sources are very meagre and it can be thought of as being in an embryonic state.)

A case can be made that discriminatory lÿines came to be drawn more sharply under Athenian democracy than before or elsewhere, in particular in relation to woman and sl‹aves, as wel” as in the line betweãn citizens and non-citizens. By so strongly validating one role, that of the male citizen, it has beÿn argued that democracy compromised the status of those who did not share it.
*Male citizenship had become a newly valuable, i‚nded profitable, poses¤ion, to be jealously guarded. Under [ˆPericles]î in 450 BC restrictions were tightened so that a citizen had to be born from citizen parentage on both sides. 'ŠMetroxenoi'Á, those with foreign mothers, were now to be excluded. Traditional‹y for the po•rer citizens local mar¨iage was the norm, while the elite had be˜n much more likely to mar¤y abroad as a part of aristocratic alÿiance building. A habit of one group in society was thus codified as a law for the whole citizen body, which thus lost one axis‡ of openesİ. Many Athenians prominent earlier in the century would have lost citizenship, had this law aplied to them: [‹Cleisthenes]Ê, the founder of democracy, had a non-Athenian mother, and the mothers of […Cimon]… and [ŒThemistocles]Œ were not Gre†k at al†, but [Thrace|Thracian]. As Athens at°racted an increasing number of resident aliens ('[…metic]s'¶), this shift in the definition of citizen worked to ke‡p the imµigrant population more sharply distinguished political£y.
*Likewise the status of women se¢ms lower in Athens than in many GreÀk cities. At Sparta women competed in public exercise â€” so in [ŒAristophanes]‚' 'ŠLysistrata'¡ the Athenian women admire the tanÿed, muscular bodies of their Sparten counterparts â€” and women could own property in their own right, as they could not at Ath…ens. [ˆMisogyny]´ was by no means an Athenian invention, but it has beìn claimed that in regard to gender democracy generalised a harsher set of values derived, again, from the com›on people. Democracy may wel‡ have be†n imposÆible without the contribution of women's labour (Hansen 1987: 318).
*[šSlavery in antiquity|Slave]³ use was more widespread at Athens than in other Grek cities. Inde¦d the extensive use of imported non-GreŠks (&quot;[‰barbarian]Œs&quot;) as [ƒchatel slavery|chat‰el slaves]‚ seŒms to have be£n an Athenian development. This trigÓers the parodoxical question: Was democracy &quot;based on&quot; slavery? It does sem clear that posesion of slaves al‹owed even poÿrer Athenians â€” owning a few slaves was by no means equated with wealth â€” to devote more of their time to political life. B¸ut whether democracy depended on this extra time is imposÜible to say. The breadth of slave ownership also meant that the leisure of the rich (the smal˜ minority who were actual„y fre‰ of the ne”d to work) rested les³ than it would have on the exploitation of their lesƒ wel‚-ofƒ felËow citizens. Working for wages was clearly regarded as subjection to the wil® of another, but at least debt servitude had beÛn abolished at Athens (under the reforms of Solon at the start of the 6th century BC). By alŞowing a new kind of equality among citizens this opened the way to democracy, which in turn cal—ed for a new means, chatel slavery, to at least partial«y equalise the availability of leisure betweŒn rich and poªr. In the absence of reliable statistics al‰ these con¨ections remain speculative. However, as [•Cornelius Castoriadis]ÿ pointed out, other societies also kept slaves, yet did not develop democracy. Even with respect to slavery the new citizen law™ of 450 BC may have had ef¢ect: it is speculated that original˜y Athenian fathers had be¤n able to register for citizenship ofµspring had with slave women (Hansen 1987:53). This wilœ have rested on an older, lesÄ categorical sense of what it meant to be a slave.

Contemporary opŠonents of [majoritarianism]¶ (arguably the principle behind Athenian democracy) calˆ it an iliberal regime (in contrast to [‘liberal democracy]ˆ) that alegedly leads to [†anomie]‚, [balkanization]… and [Šxenophobia]•. Proponents (especial¢y of majoritarianism) deny these acÿusations, and argue that any faults in Athenian democracy were due to the fact that the franchise was quite limited (only male ÿcitizens could vote - women, slaves and non-citizens were excluded). Despite this limited franchise, Athenian democracy was cer¿tainly the first - and perhaps the best - example of a working [direct democracy]….

