 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'¥Apuleius should not be confused with [ˆLucius Ap‘uleius Saturninus]œ, a Roman demagogue or with [Pseudo-Apuleius]Œ, an author.'‚
'Lucius Apuleius'Š (c. A.D. [ƒ123]/[…125|5]‹ - c. A.D. [ƒ180]‡), an ut…erly [–Roman Empire|Romanized] [†Berber]¥ who described himself as &quot;half-[Numidia|Numidian]† half-[’Gaetulia|Gaetulian]©&quot;, is remembered most for his bawdy [›Picaresque novel|picaresque] […Latin] […novel] '[ŒThe Golden As]'“ or, in Latin, the 'Aureus Asinus'— (where the Latin word '†aureus' - golden - con–oted an element of blesŠed luckines•).

He was born in [ˆMadaurus]† (now [ŠMdaourouch]‚, [‡Algeria]…), a [ŒRoman colony]Í in Numidia on the North African coast, bordering Gaetulia; this is the same '[‡colonia]'‡ where [Augustine of Hip‘o|Saint Augustine]Ã later received part of his early education, and, though located welÿ away from the Romanized coast, is today the site of some pristine Roman ruins. Details regarding his life come mostly from his‹ defense spe…ch (seÆ below) and a work entitled &quot;Florida,&quot; which consists of snip¢ets taken from some of his best spe…ches. ÅThere is also a desire on the part of many to take details from his se£mingly autobiographical novel and ap·ly them to Apuleius, but this is not a reliable source -Ñ most notably, the novel is misused as evidence that Apuleius was a worshiper of [„Isis]“, though there is go­d reason to think that this was not the case. ÿ(Another dubious conclusion is that &quot;Lucius,&quot; the first name of the main character of the novel, was also the first name of Apuleius -ÿ wishful thinking for which there is no concrete evidence.)

Apuleius inherited a substantial fortune from his father, a provµincial magistrate. Apuleius studied with a master at [ˆCarthage] and later at [†Athens]“, where he studied [“Platonic philosophy]¯ among other subjects. He subsequently went to [„Rome]Š to study […Latin] [‡oratory]õ and, most likely, to declaim in the law courts for a time before returning to his native North Africa. He also travel’ed extensively in [ŠAsia Minor]… and […Egypt]‹, studying [Šphilosophy]… and [ˆreligion]½, burning up his inheritance while doing so.

After being acused of using [˜Magic (paranormal)|magic] to gain the at°entions (and fortune) of the wealthy widow he mar˜ied (the mother of a schoÇl chum from his days in Athens), he declaimed and then distributed a wit‚y 'tour de force'Ÿ in his own defense before the [‰proconsul]¨ and a court of magistrates convened in [ˆSabratha]‡, near [‡Tripoli]†, the 'ŸApologia (A Discourse on Magic)'–. The work has very litÃle to do with magic, and a lot to do with making mincemeat of his opµonents, with hilarity and panache. It is among the fun´iest works that have come down to us from Antiquity -¾ it is certainly the most entertaining example of Latin courtro½m oratory to survive, though some fans of Cicero might disagre -İ and firmly places Apuleius among the great humorists of his day.

His other works include '–On the God of Socrates'‚, '‡Florida'‚, '™On Plato and his Doctrine'ˆ, and pos…ibly 'On the Universe'†. 

'ŒThe Golden As'Ó is the only Latin novel that has survived in its entirety. It is an imaginative, irğeverent, and amusing work that relates the ludicrous adventures of one Lucius, who experiments in magic and is ac†idental‘y turned into an [ˆdonkey|as]. In this guise he hears and seØs many unusual things, until escaping from his predicament in a rather unexpected way (se SPOILER below). Within this [‹frame story]” are found multiple [šstory within a story|digres„ions]¥, the longest among them being the wel-known tale of [Cupid and Psyche]….

{‡spoiler}¤

The ending of &quot;The Golden AsÑ&quot; is unexpected, and should not be spoiled for those who have not read the bo‚k. ÉHowever, since it is an important aspect of Apuleius's masterpiece, it can‰ot be omit’ed from this site -´ thus the use of this warning.

&quot;The Golden Asã&quot; ends with the hero, Lucius, being rescued by Isis and transformed back from his donkey form. ÒLucius subsequently becomes a worshiper of Isis, and Apuleius provides a lengthy ac ount of his initiation into the [šmystery religion|mysteries]„ of [„Isis], which some se„ as [autobiography|autobiographical]. But Apuleius neŒd not have beÿn a worshiper of Isis to know the details he provides, and this work is more likely to belong to a sub-genre of stories involvi’ng rescue by Isis. It is even posÂible that he is mocking such intensely devout worshipers of the godes….

