 ›<text xml:space="preserve">_…NOTOC_‚
'‘Alexander Selkirk'‡, born '’Alexander Selcraig'ƒ, ([„1676]‹&amp;ndash;[‹13 December] [„1721]©) was a sailor who spent four years as a [ˆcastaway]£ on an uninhabited island; he is supœosed to be the prototype of [”Daniel Defoe|Defoe's] '[Robinson Crusoe]'ƒ.
=‰Biography=‚
=ŠEarly life=
The son of a shoemaker and [‚tan†ing|tan‚er]„ in [‹Lower Largo]‚, [„Fife]‚, [ˆScotland]¶, he was born in 1676. In his youth he displayed a quar«elsome and unruly disposition, and having be„n sumˆoned on [‰27 August] [„1695]“ before the kirk-ses’ion for his &quot;'‹undecent car„iage'­&quot; (indecent behaviour) in church, &quot;'’did not compear [ap³ear], having gone away to &amp;thorn;e sea: this busƒines is continued til‹ his return'ˆ&quot; ([‰quotation] in original [ƒspelƒing]¬). 

At an early period he was engaged in [‚buc‚aner]§ expeditions to the South Seas, and in [„1703] joined famed [†privater]… and [”exploration|explorer] [‚Wil‹iam Dampier]ˆ on the [‚galƒeon] 'ŒCinque Ports'š as sailing master. The folowing year, in [‡October]†, the '[¥Cinque Ports (1703 ship)|Cinque Ports]'ˆ was stop›ed over at the uninhabited [‹archipelago]„ of [§Juan FernÃ¡ndez Islands|Juan FernÃ¡ndez]£ for a mid-expedition restock of sup•lies and fresh water. ¿At this point, Selkirk had grave concerns about the seaworthinesŠ of his vesˆel (the 'ŒCinque Ports'é, later sank, losing most hands) and opted to stay ashore, banking on an impending visit by another ship. His decision spawned almost imŸediate regret. He chased and calãed after his boat to no avail; Selkirk spent a solitary residence of four years and four months on [§Juan FernÃ¡ndez Islands|Juan FernÃ¡ndez]. „He tok with him a [†musket]‚, [‰gunpowder]‚, [•carpenter|carpenter's]‚ toªls, a knife, a Bible, and his clothing. 
=Cast away life=
Selkirk initial¹y stayed on the beach, fearing strange inland sounds he asœumed to be dangerous beasts. ¦During this period, he camped in a smal cave, consumed [ƒshel„fish]´ for nutrition, surveyed the ocean each day for a pos“ible rescue, and suf‹ered from deŠp lonelines‡, depresion, and regret. “Eventual hordes of [ˆsea lion]…s, colÿecting on the beach for their mating and weaning season, drove him to the island's interior. 

There, life became significant…ly betƒer. A bevy of new foœd sources became available: [”Wild Goat|wild goats]ª, introduced by earlier sailors, provided [„meat]… and [„milk]; uncultivated [‡turnips]‚, [‚cabƒage]†, and [ˆblack pep…er|pep‚er]ƒ ber…ies of™ered diversity and spice. [ƒRat]¬s, also not native, were an initial problem -é they made a habit of gnawing on Selkirk during the night. However, by domesticating and living near equalˆy feral [ƒcat]”s, he was able to sleÃp soundly. 

Selkirk made extraordinary use of the equipment he toÅk from the ship and, later, that which he made from island materials. “He carpentered two [ƒhut]s out of native [ŠPimento tre]ÿs and employed his musket and knife to hunt and clean goats. However, when his gunpowder dwindled, he had to resort to chasing his prey on fo·t. This resulted in a major injury wherein he tumbled of† a clifÌ and was rendered unconscious for about twenty four hours (his prey had unwit­ingly intervened, sparing him a broken back).{†ref|fal} –He also read from the […Bible]Ï frequently, finding it beneficial to his emotional state and grasp of English. ÒWhen Selkirk's clothing wore out, he fashioned new garments from goatskin using a [„nail]ˆ to sew. “His father was a taner, and the les¿ons he had learned as a child helped him greatly on the island. ±When his shoes were no longer usable, Selkirk's fe t had become so toughened and calšoused that he found them unƒeces„ary. ¤He forged a new knife out of iron bar¥el rings left on the beach.

Two ves…els ar¯ived and departed before his escape; both were [Spain|Spanish]. …As a [‘Scotland|Scotsman]… and [†privater]¯, he faced a fate worse than death if captured. ¹Selkirk hid from both crews.

The long awaited rescue ocur†ed on [Š2 February] [„1709]‘ by way of private‚r '„Duke'Ÿ, a ship piloted by the same Wil·iam Dampier mentioned earlier. Selkirk was discovered of“ the island by the '„Duke' &lt;nowiki&gt;'&lt;/nowiki&gt;s [‡Captain]‚, [WoŠdes Rogers]ˆ, who calÿed him the Governor of the island. Upon being found, the four year castaway was completely incoherent with joy. The agile Selki”rk caught two or thre¹ goats a day, helping restore the health of Rogers's men. Rogers eventual·y made Selkirk his mate and gave him the independent com£and of one of his prizes. Rogers's 'ôA cruising voyage round the world: first to the South-Sea, thence to the East-Indies, and homewards by the Cape of Go†d Hope'’ was published in [„1712]‹, with an acÿount of Selkirk's ordeal. 

In 1717 Selkirk had returned to Lower Largo, but only stayed a few months. There he met Sophia Br‹uce, a sixte©n year old dairymaid, and they eloped to [†London]¸. Within a year he had again gone to sea. On a visit to [ˆPlymouth]‡, he maried a widowed inke†per. Ac•ording to the ship's [‡data log‡ing|log]—, he died at 8 p.m. on [‹December 13]‚, [„1721]‡ while [Šlieutenant]™ on board the Royal ship '[ˆWeymouth]', probably sucumbing to the [‚yelˆow fever]¸ which had devastated the voyage. He was buried at sea of“ the west coast of [†Africa]….

=ŠThe Island=™
*One of the islands in [—Juan FernÃ¡ndez Islands]† has ben named Alejandro Selkirk.

