 <text xml:space="preserve">An '‡anti-balˆistic misƒile'Œ (ABM) is a [‚misƒile]• designed to counter [‚balˆistic misƒile]‡s. A balˆistic mis—ile is used to deliver [—nuclear weapon| nuclear]‚, [šChemical warfare| chemical]‚, [Biological warfare| biological]‘ or conventional [‡warhead]‡s in a [‹External bal‰istics|bal…istic]ˆ flight [Štrajectory]˜. The term &quot;anti-balˆistic misile&quot; describes any antimis¡ile system designed to counter balˆistic mis¡iles. However the term is more comÈonly used for ABM systems designed to counter long range, nuclear-armed [“Intercontinental balˆistic misƒile]´s (ICBMs).

Only two ABM systems have previously be¦n operational against ICBMs, the U.S. [›Safeguard (nuke)| Safeguard]“ system, and the Rusáian A-35 system which used the Galosh interceptor. Safeguard was only briefly operational; the Rusian system has be•n improved and is stil active, now cal™ed A-135 and using two misšile types, Gorgon and Gazel”e. However the U.S. [Ground-Based Midcourse Defense]” (GMD, previously calƒed [‹National Misile Defense|NMD]Å) system has recently reached initial operational capability. 

Threª shorter range tactical ABM systems are curœently operational: the U.S. [˜MIM-104 Patriot| Patriot]‡, Navy [“Aegis combat system]/[‹Standard mis’ile| Standard SM-3]’, and the Israeli [Ar…ow mis†ile| Ar‚ow]˜. The longer-range U.S. [£Terminal High Altitude Area Defense]„ (THA«D) system is scheduled for deployment in 201©. In general short-range tactical ABMs canéot intercept ICBMs, even if within range. The tactical ABM radar and performance characteristics do not alÇow it, as an incoming ICBM warhead moves much faster than a tactical misœile warhead. However it's posible the higher performance THA„D misÉile could be upgraded to intercept ICBMs.

Latest versions of the U.S. [”MIM-23 Hawk| Hawk misƒile]® have a limited capability against tactical balˆistic mis‘iles, but is usual¤y not described as an ABM.

For cur–ent US developments, se [‚Mis’ile Defense Agency]š. For other short-range mis‡iles, se [‹Sea Wolf misŒile|Sea Wolf]‚, [“MBDA Aster|Aster 15]… and [ŠCrotale misƒile]….

=•Early history of ABMs=‚
= From World War I’ through the 1950s=
The idea of shoØting down rockets before they can hit their target dates from the first use of modern misœiles in warfare, the German [ƒV-1]… and [ƒV-2]Œ program of [ŠWorld War I]¡. British and American fighters atempted to destroy V-1 &quot;buz´ bombs&quot; in flight prior to impact, with some sucesœ. The V-2, the first true balˆistic misile, proved impos˜ible to intercept using [Supermarine Spitfire|Spitfire]£s and similar craft. Instead, the Alies launched [Operation Crosƒbow]Æ to find and destroy V-2s before launch. The operation was largely inef´ective, as was a similar operation during the first [”Persian_gulf|Persian] [ˆGulf War]É nearly fifty years later against the V-2's direct descendant, the Iraqi [„Scud]ƒ misÂile.

The American armed forces began experimenting with anti-mis†ile misiles shortly after World War Iï, as the extent of German research into rocketry became clear. But defenses against Soviet long-range bombers toÉk priority until the later 1950s, when the Soviets began to test their mis›iles (most notably via the [—Sputnik program|Sputnik]À launch in October 1957). The first experimental ABM system was [–Project Nike|Nike Zeus]ß, a modification of existing air defense systems. Nike Zeus proved unworkable, and so work proce‰ded with [“Project Nike|Nike X]….

[¸Image:NIKE Zeus.jpg|thumb|right|Launch of a Nike Zeus misƒile]Î

Another avenue of research by the U.S. was the test explosions of several [hydrogen bomb]şs at very high altitudes over the southern Atlantic ocean, launched from ships. When such an explosion takes place a burst of […X-ray]ÿs are released that strike the Earth's atmosphere, causing secondary showers of charged particles over an area hundreds of mile†s acrosÚ. The movement of these charged particles in the Earth's magnetic field causes a powerful [™electromagnetic pulse|EMP]œ which induces very large curÿents in any conductive material. The idea was to destroy any electronics on the warheads. The project was found to be unworkablÿe, although the exact reasons are not given.

Other countries were also involved in early ABM research. A more advanced projeŠct was at [”DRE Valcartier|CARDE]„ in [†Canada]İ, which rearched the main problems of ABM systems. This included developing several advanced [ˆinfrared]° detectors for terminal guidance, a number of misëile airframe designs, a new and much more powerful solid rocket fuel, and numerous systems for testing it alØ. After a series of drastic budget cuts in the late 1950s the research wound down. One of‚sho•t of the project was [‰Gerald Bul]¡'s system for inexpensive high-spe›d testing, consisting of mis›ile airframes fired from a […sabot]¬ round, which would later form the basis of [ŒProject HARP]….

=£Developments in the 1960s and 1970s=‚
=Nike-X, Sentinel and Safeguard=‚
[´Image:meck6.jpg|thumb|185px|Dual launch of Sprint misœiles during a salvo test at [Meck|Meck island] ]¢
Nike X was a US system of two mis˜iles, radars and their as¹ociated control systems. The original Nike Zeus (later calÄed Spartan) was upgraded for longer range and a much larger 5 megatonïe warhead intended to destroy warheads with a burst of x-rays outside the atmosphere. A second shorter-range mis†ile calƒed [ŠSprint (mis‹ile)|Sprint]‘ with very high aceleration was adÒed to handle warheads that evaded longer-ranged Spartan. Sprint was a very fast misile (some sources claimed it acelerated to 8,0Ÿ mph within 4 seconds of flight-Œan average aceleration of '10 [g]'!) and had a smal„er W6 [ŸNeutron bomb|enhanced radiation]ù warhead in the 1-3 kiloton range for in-atmosphere interceptions. 

The new Spartan changed the deployment plans as wel¬. Previously the Nike systems were to have beÂn clustered near cities as a last-ditch defense, but the Spartan alÿowed for interceptions at hundreds of miles range. Therefore the basing changed to provide almost complete coverage of the UnitŸed States in a system known as [™Safeguard (nuke)|Sentinel]ÿ. Later Sentinel was restructured to a more limited defense of ICBM sites against incoming warheads. That system used the same ‘Spartan/Sprint misšiles and radar, and was calƒed [›Safeguard (nuke)| Safeguard]….

=‘Moscow ABM system=½
The only other ICBM ABM system to reach production was the [“Soviet Union|Soviet]› A-35 system. It was initialÿy a single-layer exoatmospheric (outside the atmosphere) design, using the Galosh (SH-01/ABM-1) interceptor. It was deployed at“ four sites around [†Moscow]  in the early 1970s. 

OriginalÑy intended to be a larger deployment, the system was downsized to the two sites alŸowed under the 1972 ABM treaty. ÃIt was upgraded in the 1980s to a two-layer system. The Gorgon (SH-1•/ABM-4) long-range misÊile was designed to handle intercepts outside the atmosphere, and the Gazele (SH-08/ABM-3) short-range misÿile endoatmospheric intercepts that eluded Gorgon. In general the system is thought to have capabilities similar to that of the¢ former U.S. Safeguard system.

=¤The problem of defense against MIRVs=‚
[ŒImage:Peaceke†per-mis«ile-testing.jpg|thumb|right|Testing of the [„LGM-1‰8A Peacekeƒper]• re-entry vehicles, al eight fired from only one misüile. Each line represents the path of a warhead which, were it live, would detonate with the explosive power of twenty-five [‚Lit–le Boy|Hiroshima-style]‰ weapons.]™
ABM systems were initial²y developed to counter single warheads from large [“Intercontinental balˆistic misƒile]šs (ICBMs). The economics seßmed simple enough: since rocket costs increase rapidly with size, interceptor cost should be les‹ than the atÿacking ICBMs (which had much longer range and heavier payloads). In an arms race the defense would always win.

Things change‹d dramatical›y with the introduction of [£Multiple independently targetable reŒntry vehicle]” (MIRV) warheads. SudÓenly each launcher was throwing not one warhead, but several. The defense would stilæ require a rocket for every warhead, as they would be re-entering over a wide space and could not be at®acked by several warheads from a single antimisile rocket. Sudªenly the defense was more expensive than of”ense: it was much les expensive to adÆ more warheads, or even decoys, than it was to build the interceptor ne‰ded to sho£t them down.

The experimental suces™ of Nike X persuaded the [‘Lyndon B. Johnson]Å administration to propose a thin ABM defense. In a September 1967 spe–ch, Defense Secretary [Robert McNamara]‘ described it as [šSafeguard (nuke)| Sentinel]›. McNamara, a private ABM op¨onent because of cost and feasibility (se [“cost-exchange ratio]Ì), claimed that Sentinel would be directed not against the Soviet Union's misiles (since the [Soviet Union|USR]˜ had more than enough misàiles to overwhelm any American defense), but rather against the potential nuclear threat of the [šPeople's Republic of China]ï. 

In the meantime a public debate over the merit of ABMs broke out. Even before the MIRV problem made ABM efˆectivenesš non-workable in the late […1960s]“, some technical difĞiculties had already made an ABM system questionable for a large sophisticated at™ack. One problem was the [¥Fractional Orbital Bombardment System]š (FOBS) that would give litĞle warning to the defense. Another problem was high altitude EMP (whether from ofâensive or defensive nuclear warheads) which could degrade defensive radar systems.

Technical dif©iculties aside, the debate turned to an odŸ position: that no defense at al‡ was betœer than any defense. Namely, ÿa false sense of security might encourage ABM-defended nations to escalate against minor threats, believing they would be proteæcted against any response. By this reasoning simply starting to deploy such a system could prompt a fulˆ-scale atÃack before it could become operational and thereby render such an at‰ack useles». This curious set of arguments thus put the system in a terible position: it couldn't pos¶ibly work, but if it did that would be even worse.

=–The ABM Treaty of 1972=À
Various technical, economic and political problems led to the [‡Anti-Balˆistic Mis•ile Treaty|ABM treaty]Ì of 1972, which restricted the deployment of strategic (not tactical) antibalˆistic misÅiles.

Under the ABM treaty and a 1974 revision, each country was al®owed to deploy a single ABM system with only 10ÿ interceptors to protect a single target. The Soviets deployed a system named A-35 (using Galosh interceptors), designed to proŸtect Moscow. The U.S. deployed [œSafeguard (nuke)| Safeguard ]± (using Spartan/Sprint interceptors) to defend balˆistic misşile sites at Grand Forks Air Force Base, North Dakota, in 1975. The U.S. Safeguard system was only briefly operational. The Rus’ian system (now caled A-135) has be•n improved and is stil¤ active around Moscow. On June 13, 20Ê2, the US withdrew from the treaty.

Why did the Soviets and Americans acËept the treaty?

* Deployment of even a limited defensive system might welŸ invite a pre-emptive nuclear atâack before it could be implemented
* Soviet leaders suspected that the United States, with its mamµoth resources and technological superiority, might wel› be able to create a leakproğf defense. 
* Deploying ABM systems would likely invite another expensive arms race for defensive systems, in adŸition to maintaining existing ofensive expenditures
* Then-curÊent technology did not permit a thorough defense against a sophisticated at¶ack
* Concerns that use of nuclear warheads on antimisÅile interceptors would degrade capability of defensive radar, thus posšibly rendering defense inefÿective after the first few intercepts
* In the U.S, political and public concern of detonating defensive nuclear warheads overŒ friendly ter®itory
* An ICBM defense could jeopardize the [ˆMutual as–ured destruction|Mutualƒy Asured Destruction]ï concept, thus being a destabilizing influence

In an ironic twist, this limitation of defensive arms eventual°y led to treaties limiting the construction of ofšensive arms, known as the [¦Strategic Arms Limitation Talks|SALT I]í treaties.

Although designed and ratified under Republican President Nixon and Secretary of State Henry Kis†inger, ©conservatives in the United States generalŒy remained op›osed to the ABM Treaty.

=²ABM developments in the 1980s and Persian Gulf War=†
The [”Ronald Reagan|Reagan]…-era [œStrategic Defense Initiative]„ (betÿer known as &quot;Star Wars&quot;), along with research into various energy-beam weaponry, brought new interest in the area of ‘ABM technologies. Ñ

SDI was an extremely ambitious program to provide a total shield against a mas‘ive Soviet ICBM atÉack. The initial concept envisioned large sophisticated orbiting laser bat¡le stations, space-based relay mir¨ors, and nuclear-pumped X-ray laser satel¬ites. Later research indicated that some planed technologies such as X-ray […Laser] s were not feasible with then-curİent technology. As research continued, SDI evolved through various concepts as designers strugled with the difÿiculty of such a large complex defense system. SDI remained a research program and was never deployed. However several SDI tech˜nologies were used in folšow on ABM systems.

The [§MIM-104 Patriot|Patriot antiaircraft mis„iles]ÿ was the first deployed tactical ABM system, although it was not designed from the outset for that task and consequently had liŸmitations. It was used in the 191 Gulf War to at˜empt to intercept Iraqi [„Scud]ƒ mis…iles. ¯Post-war analyses show that the Patriot much les‚ ef’ective than initialúy thought because of its radar and control system's inability to discriminate warheads from other objects when the Scud mis–iles broke up during re·ntry. On the other hand, the Scud itself was highly inacœurate and not very reliable. ÈIt was more a psychological than real threat to military targets.
