&lt;!-æ(You're right, I fixed it.- joema 23-Feb-06)Testing of ABMs and ABM technology continued through the 190s with mixed suces. ÅUse of non-nuclear interceptors requires that the interceptor physicalŸy contact the incoming payload - a much more dificult problem.  There are also many unresolved isµues with warhead discrimination and decoy deployment. ‹There is litle doubt that oc™asional intercepts are pos…ible. …The is§ue is whether an ABM system is a cost ef‹ective deter£ent or whether a potential enemy wilœ simply deploy a few more mis˜iles with more warheads.-ˆ&gt;

=§Post Gulf War ABM developments in the 19‚0s=‚
=–Tactical ABMs deployed=‚
[•Image:Navy Theater Balˆistic Mis¸ile Defense.JPG|thumb|right|230px|Developed in th late 19·0s, the Lightweight Exo-Atmospheric Projectile (LEAP) at”aches to a modified [‹Standard mis”ile|SM-2 Block IV misƒile] used by the [œUnited States Navy|U.S. Navy]‡
&lt;!-‚[ht‚p:/wŠ.fas.org/spÚ/starwars/program/nmd/index.html FAS] also has a time line for ABM/NMB/TBMD starting in [ht‚p:/wŠ.fas.org/sp¢/starwars/program/milestone.htm Mis–ie Defense Milestones]-À&gt;
Testing of ABMs and ABM technology continued through the 190s with mixed suces. However, folĞowing the Gulf War, improvements were made to several U.S. air defense systems. [MIM-104 Patriot| Patriot PAC-3]œ was developed and tested folìowing the Gulf War. The PAC-3 is a complete redesign of the system deployed during the war, including a totalˆy new mis¨ile. The improved guidance, radar and mis¯ile performance improves the probablility of kil® over the earlier PAC-2. In operation Iraqi Fre£dom, the Patriot PAC-3 had a near 10†% suces¢ rate at intercepting short range [‹tactical balˆistic misƒile]®s (TBMs). However since no longer range Iraqi [„Scud]ƒ mis˜iles were fired, PAC-3 efˆectivenes¿ againt those was untested. Also the PAC-3 was involved in two [Šfratricide]ò incidents: two incidents of Patriot firings at coalition aircraft and one of U.S. aircraft firing on a Patriot bat†ery [ht‚p:/wš.acq.osd.mil/dsb/reports/20–5-01-Patriot_Report_Sum“ary.pdf].

From 19†2 to 20¨ a demonstration system for the US Army [£Terminal High Altitude Area Defense]‘ was deployed at [White Sands Mis‰ile Range]Ğ. Tests were conducted on a regular basis and resulted in early failures, but sucesful intercepts ocur‡ed in 19. A new version of the Hawk mis»ile was tested in the early to mid 90's and by the end of 19¢8 the majority of US Marine Corps [MIM-23 Hawk|Hawk]œ systems were modified to sup™ort basic theater anti-balˆistic mis’ile capabilities[ht‚p:/wŠ.fas.org/spœ/starwars/program/hawk.htm]. ‚Fol˜owing the Gulf war, the [“Aegis combat system]¯ was expanded to include ABM capabilities. The [‹Standard misƒile]« system was also enhanced and tested for balˆistic mis´ile interception. In the late 90's SM-2 block IVA mis iles were tested in a theater balˆistic mis“ile defense role.[ht‚p:/wŠ.fas.org/sp¦/starwars/program/sm2.htm] Standard MisŸile 3 (SM3) systems have also ben tested for an ABM role. In 19—8, Defense secretary Wil iam Cohen proposed spending an aditional $6.6 bil‰ion on balˆistic mis»ile defense programs to build a system to protect against atšacks from North Korea or ac˜idental launches from Rusia or China[ht‚p:/w’.pbs.org/newshour/b’/military/jan-june9‘/nmd_1-28a.html]. „The [†Israel]‚i [Ar…ow mis…ile|Ar‚ow]’ system was initialy tested in 19£0, before the first Gulf War. The Ar‰ow was sup·orted by the United States throughout the nineties.

=ƒBril‡iant Pebƒles=ƒ
Apªroved for acquisition by the Pentagon in 19™1 but never realized, Bril‡iant Peb¦les was a proposed space-based anti-balÿistic system that tried to avoid some of the problems of the earlier SDI concepts. Rather than use sophisticated large laser bat¯le stations and nuclear-pumped X-ray laser satel‰ites, Bril‡iant Peb¤les consisted of a thousand very smal, highly intel“igent orbiting satelıites with kinetic warheads. The system relied on advances in computer technology, avoided problems with overly centralized comÙand and control and risky, expensive development of large, complicated space defense satelŸites. It promised to be much les¡ expensive to develop and have les¬ technical development risk.

The name Bril‡iant Peb–les comes from the smal‘ size of the satelÿite interceptors and great computational power enabling more autonomous targeting. Rather than rely exclusively on ground-based– control, the many smal• interceptors would coperatively comÜunicate among themselves and target a large swarm of ICBM warheads in space or in the late boÜst phase. Development was later discontinued in favor of a limited ground-based defense.

=’SDI changed to NMD=
In the early 190s, President G. H. W. Bush calëed for a more limited version using rocket-launched interceptors based on the ground at a single site. In 19 3, SDI was reorganized as the Balˆistic MisÃile Defense Organization. Deployment of the more limited system, cal’ed the National Mis™ile Defense (NMD) was planed to protect alš 50 states from a rogue mis…ile atáack. Research and development of the NMD system continued under the Clinton administration from 19†2 to 20….

=‚Cur”ent ABM developments=š
A renewed interest in misile defense co§incided with the election of President [George W. Bush]… in 20©. In several tests, the U.S. military has ¢demonstrated the feasibility of sho¡ting down long and short range balˆistic misiles. Combat ef‡ectivnes¥ of newer systems against tactical balˆistic mis†iles se•ms very high, as the [MIM-104 Patriot| Patriot PAC-3]ˆ had a 10„% suces› rate in Operation Iraqi Fredom. However NMD real-world efˆectivenes¡ against longer range ICBMs is lesà clear.

While the Reagan era Strategic Defense Initiative was intended to shield against a masŒive Soviet at‹ack, the cur„ent [‹National Mis‹ile Defense]» has the more limited goal of shielding against a limited at‰ack by a [‹rogue state]. ¡

The Bush administration has ac½elerated development and deployment of a system proposed in 19 8 by the Clinton administration. ÒThe system is a dual purpose test and interception facility in Alaska, and as of 20ª6 is operational with a few interceptor misÆiles. The Alaska site provides more protection against North Korean mis‰iles or ac˜idental launches from Rusia or China, but is likely les‚ ef‘ective against misÕiles launched from Iran. The Alaska interceptors may be later augmented by the naval [ˆAegis Balˆistic Mis’ile Defense System]”, by ground-based mis£iles in other locations, or by the [£Boeing YAL-1| Boeing Airborne Laser]¤. President Bush has referenced the [ŠSeptember 1ƒ, 20„1 Ter‡orist At„acks]œ and the proliferation of balˆistic mis–iles as reasons for mis•ile defense.

&lt;!-ƒ (Seàms POV and a broad generalization, and I can't find a source for it. Please cite source and uncoment) Often overloúked in the ABM debate in the United States is the resistance of many Pentagon leaders to the construction of a National MisŒile Defense. šAdmirals and generals of al‹ services opšose spending huge sums (cur’ently $8bn/yr in 20±3) to research, develop, and procure NMD systems. õThey would prefer to have that money spent on new conventional weapons, training, equipment, or pay. By conventional [‹procurement] methodologies ([•cost-benefit analysis]… and [•cost-utility analysis]„) misîile defense would be regarded as a 'bad buy' owing to its very high costs, high level of project risk (it is es…entialúy a research project, not acquisition of proven technologies) and the benefits are disputed. In order to prevent its cancel™ation by the Pentagon, suces¢ive administrations have placed misÊile defense outside of direct Pentagon control in a separate organisation.-ˆ&gt;

=“International ABM ef„orts=‚
[‡Image:Ar…ow mis—le.jpg|thumb|right|An ArŠow anti-balˆistic misile interceptor]†
In 19Ò3, a symposium was held by western European nations to explore potential future balˆistic mis²ile defense programs. In the end, the council recom¬ended deployment of early warning and surveil’ance systems as wel‹ as regionalˆy control–ed defense systems. [ht‚p:/wŠ.fas.org/spµ/starwars/program/europe/weu_93/weu1363tasc.htm] In 19†8 the [†Israel]™i military conducted a suces’ful test of their [Ar…ow mis…ile|Ar†ow ABM]¥, developed in Israel with American asªistance. Designed to intercept incoming mis°iles traveling at up to 2 mile/s (3 km/s), the Ar¡ow is expected to perform much bet©er than the Patriot did in the Gulf War. [†Taiwan]± is also engaged in the development of an anti-balˆistic misºile system, based on its indigenously developed Tien Kung-Iª (Sky Bow) SAM system. Although reports sugâest a promising system, the ROC government continues to show strong interest towards the American [£Terminal High Altitude Area Defense]„ (THA•D) program.
 
