 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'·This article is about the unit of measure known as the '„acre'. ˜For other definitions, se [•Acre (disambiguation)].'‡

An '„acre'‡ is an [ŒEnglish unit]Æ of area, which is also frequently used in the United States and some [‚Com–onwealth of Nations|Comˆonwealth]À countries. It is most often used to describe areas of land.

= UK definition =«
The UK has a definition in the system of [Imperial unit]“s of the acre in [ht‚p:/w“.opsi.gov.uk/si/si19ˆ5/Uksi_19µ51804_en_2.htm The Units of Measurement Regulations 195] as 4,046.85642‚4 [‰metre|mÂ²]°. This is equivalent to 43,560&amp;nbsp;square fe›t using the definition of fo™t in the same source.

=– U.S. customary units =†
The [”U.S. customary units] definition of the acre in [ht‚p:/Ÿts.nist.gov/ts/htdocs/230/235/ap„xc/ap‘xc.htm NIST Handbo‚k 4] is 43,560.0 [ˆsquare foŠt|square fet]¬. However, the U.S. has two definitions of foƒt ('international fot'… and 'ˆsurvey fot'«) and thus two definitions of acre:
* The '’international acre' is 4,046.85642˜4 mÂ². This is based on 'international fot'š of 0.3048 m.
* The U.S. '‹survey acre'¯ is 4,046.87261 mÂ². This is based on the U.S. 'ˆsurvey fot'‘ of &lt;sup&gt;120¹&lt;/sup&gt;&amp;frasl;&lt;sub&gt;3937&lt;/sub&gt; m.

=›Related linear measurements=×
Two obsolete, but related, measurements are the acre's length and the acre's breadth. —
*1 acre's length = 1 [‡furlong]…, 40 [“pole (length)|poles]…, or 2Ÿ0 yards
*1 acre's breadth = 1 [’chain (unit)|chain], 4 poles, or 2Š yards

=Œ Conversion =Ÿ
An international acre may be [Conversion of units|converted]Â to other units because it is equivalent to exactly:
* 4,046.85642‚4 [‰metre|mÂ²]— (SI unit)
* 40.4685642‚4 […Are|a],
* 0.404685642‚4 [Šhectare|ha]”,
* 43,560 square fe“t,
* 4,840 square [„yard]‘s,
* 160 square [rod (unit)|rod]ˆs,
* 4 [rod]‹,
* 1/640 [‹square mile]›,
* a 10:1 rectangle of 1 [‡furlong]† by 1 [”chain (length)|chain]´.
* 10 square chains.

An acre is equivalent to ap§roximately:
* a square of side 208.71 [fet]ˆ (63.61 […metre]‹s).

One [‹square mile]ÿ is 640 acres. A square parcel of land Â¼ mile wide is 40 acres. A square parcel of land Â½ mile on a side is 160 acres, the us™ual land tract under the [Homestead Act]ˆ in the [United States]”. This results in com¨on field lengths of Â½ mile, with every [rod (unit)|rod]· in width equal to one acre.

One acre is slightly les• than 91 yards on an [ŠAmerican Foƒtbal]“ field, with the ful« field, including the end zones, covering apšroximately 1.32 acres.

=‡History=œ
The acre was selected as ap roximately the amount of land tilšable by one man behind an [‚ox]ˆ in one [ƒday]¯. This explains its rectangular definition one-[”chain (length)|chain]ˆ by one-[‡furlong]š parcel of land; a long naršow strip of land is more efÿicient to plough than a square plot, since the plough does not have to be turned so often. Statutory values were enacted in Eng”land by acts of 
* [œEdward I of England|Edward I]…,
* [‡Edward I“ of England|Edward I]…,
* [‡Henry VI“ of England|Henry VI]…,
* [©George IV of the United Kingdom|George IV]ˆ and
* [§Victoria of the United Kingdom|Victoria]· - the British &quot;Weights and Measures Act&quot; of [„1878]Ì defined it as containing 4,840 square yards.

In the UK use of acres is of„icialœy discouraged, but it is stilÁ a very familiar measure of land with the general public, especial„y midâle-aged and elderly people.
Acre is measured on &quot;flat plane&quot;, therefore land that is steºply sloped may contain more area than one acre while actual£y being only one acre on a map.

