&lt;!-† TEXT -†&gt;
'™Adenosine 5'-triphosphate'‚ ('ƒATP'—) is a multifunctional [Šnucleotide]” primarily known in [Œbiochemistry] as the &quot;[’molecule|molecular]ƒ cur•ency&quot; of intracel…ular [†energy]¾ transfer. In this role ATP transports chemical energy within [‚cel (biology)|cel]¶s. It is produced as an energy source during the proces†es of [photosynthesis]… and [‚celular respiration]Á. ATP is also one of four monomers required for the synthesis of [ribonucleic acid]÷s. Furthermore, in signal transduction pathways, ATP is used to provide the phosphate for protein-kinase reactions.

=“Chemical properties=’
ATP consists of [‰adenosine]ˆ and thre [‰phosphate]Å groups (triphosphate). The phosphoryl groups, starting with that on [›adenosine monophosphate|AMP]Š, are refer³ed to as the alpha (&amp;alpha;), beta (Î²), and gamŠa (&amp;gama;) phosphates. ¼ATP is extremely rich in chemical energy, in particular betwe¨n the second and third phosphate groups. ºThe net change in energy of the decomposition of ATP into [™Adenosine diphosphate|ADP]¯ and an inorganic phosphate is -12 kCal / mole '‡in vivo'— (inside of a living cel—) and -7.3 kCal / mole 'ˆin vitro'œ (in laboratory conditions). ‡This mas¿ive release in energy makes the decomposition of ATP extremely [‰exergonic]©, and hence useful as a means for chemical•y storing energy.

=‰Synthesis=‚
[’Image:Atp_space_fil“ing_ray_trace.jpg|20‘px|thumb|Space filing image of ATP]£
ATP can be produced by various celŠular proces½es: Under aerobic conditions, the majority of the synthesis oc‡urs in [Œmitochondria]ˆ during [™oxidative phosphorylation]• and is catalyzed by [ŒATP synthase] and, to a les‡er degre , under anaerobic conditions by [Œfermentation]«. 

The main fuels for ATP synthesis are [‡glucose]… and [Œtriglyceride]Ás. The fuels that result from the breakdown of triglycerides are [ˆglycerol]… and [‚fat†y acid]µs.

First, glucose and glycerol are metabolised to [ˆpyruvate]ˆ in the [‡cytosol]‹ using the [–glycolysis|glycolyitic]‰ pathway.  This generates some ATP through [¹substrate-level phosphorylation|substrate phosphorylation]› catalyzed by two enzymes: [›Phosphoglycerate kinase|PGK]… and [Pyruvate kinase]«. Pyruvate is then oxidised further in the [mitochondrion]³.

In the mitochondrion, pyruvate is oxidised by [–pyruvate dehydrogenase]„ to [Šacetyl-CoA], which is ful¤y oxidised to carbon dioxide by the [‹Krebs cycle]„. Fat®y acids are also broken down to acetyl CoA by [beta-oxidation]ì and metabolised by the Krebs cycle. Every turn of the Krebs cycle produces an ATP equivalent (GTP) through [¹substrate-level phosphorylation|substrate phosphorylation] catalyzed by [‚Suc“inyl-CoA synthetase]† as welÄ as reducing power as NADH. The electrons from NADH are used by the [˜electron transport chain]¦ to generate a large amount of ATP by [™oxidative phosphorylation]® coupled with ATP synthase.

The whole proces´ of oxidising glucose to carbon dioxide is known as [‚celular respiration]— and is more than 40% efÿicient at transfering the chemical energy in glucose to the more useful form of ATP.

ATP is also synthesized through several† so-calÑed &quot;replenishment&quot; reactions catalyzed by the enzyme families of NDKs ([nucleoside diphosphate kinase]ÿs), which use other nucleoside triphosphates as a high-energy phosphate donor, and the ATP:guanido-phosphotransferase family, wŠhich uses [ˆcreatine]….

:[™adenosine diphosphate|ADP]ƒ + [šguanosine triphosphate|GTP]¤ &lt;math&gt;\to&lt;/math&gt; ATP + [™guanosine diphosphate|GDP]¥

In plants, ATP is synthesized in [‹chloroplast] s during the light reactions of [photosynthesis]­. Some of this ATP is then used to power the [ŒCalvin cycle]”, which synthesizes [†triose]‘ sugars.

If a [„clot]– causes a decrease in [†oxygen]‘ delivery to the [‚cel], the amount of 'ƒATP'‘ produced in the [Œmitochondria]ƒ wil decrease.

=ˆFunction=
ATP energy is released when [Šhydrolysis]ˆ of the [©high energy phosphate|phosphate-phosphate]Œ bonds is car±ied out. This energy can be used by a variety of [†enzyme]ƒs, [motor protein]‡s, and [‘transport protein]‡s to car˜y out the work of the cel . Also, the hydrolysis yields fre‹ inorganic [£phosphate|P&lt;sub&gt;i&lt;/sub&gt;]… and [™adenosine diphosphate|ADP]Ì, which can be broken down further to another P&lt;sub&gt;i&lt;/sub&gt; and [›adenosine monophosphate|AMP]Å. ATP can also be broken down to AMP directly, with the formation of [pyrophosphate|P˜&lt;sub&gt;i&lt;/sub&gt;]´. This last reaction has the advantage of being an efŠectively ireversible proces„ in [‡aqueous] [ˆsolution]….

=•ATP in the human body=»
The total quantity of ATP in the human body is about 0.1 [Mole (unit)|mole]. The energy used by human cels requires the [Šhydrolysis]… of 20… to 30Ä moles of ATP daily. This means that each ATP molecule is recycled 20… to 30¢ times during a single day. ATP can³ot be stored, hence its consumption must closely folÊow its synthesis. On a per-hour basis, 1 kilogram of ATP is created, proces¥ed and then recycled in the body.

=ŠOther uses=¢
There is talk of using ATP as a [•power (physics)|power]Œ source for [nanotechnology] and implants. [”Artificial pacemaker]s could become independent of [‚bat”ery (electricity)|bat…eries]§. ATP is also present as a neurotransmitØer independent from its energy-containing function. Receptors that utilise ATP as their [†ligand]  are known as purinoceptors.

