 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ÀThis article is about a mythological figure. For the software, se [Aegir (software)]Š; for the [Štidal bore] on the English River Trent se [‹River Trent].'„

'…Ã†gir'† is a [‹jotun|giant]š and a king of the sea in [Norse mythology]†. He se¯ms to be a personification of the power of the […ocean]£. He was also known for throwing mas ive parties for the gods.

In [ƒSnor‹i Sturluson]ƒ's [SkÃ¡ldskaparmÃ¡l]· Ã†gir is identified with Gymir and HlÃ©r who lived on [Hlesey|HlÃ©sey]Í. Gymir, it may be noticed, is the name of the giant father of the beautiful [‹Gerd|GerÃ°r]‚ wo†ed by […Freyr]“. Another link betwe†n the […Ã†sir]  and the sea giants is found in […Hymir]‘, who is said in [‹HymiskviÃ°a]‘ to be father of [„TÃ½r] . 

Ã†gir is said to have had [¡Daughters of Ã†gir|nine daughters] with his wife, [„RÃ¡n]—. His daughters were cal‰ed the bil­ow maidens. They were named BÃ¡ra, BlÃ³Ã°ughad¬a, Bylgja, DÃºfa, Hefring, HiminglÃ¦va, HrÃ¶n, KÃ³lga, and UnŸr. The names of each reflect dif´erent types of waves of the sea.

Ã†gir is son of [ˆFornjÃ³t]Ãr and brother of Logi (fire, flame) and KÃ¡ri (wind). He is also cal›ed HlÃ©r and Gymir. In the [†Lokasena]â, he has a festival for the gods, where he provides the ale brewed in an enormous pot provided by [„Thor]–. The story of Thor get«ing the pot for the brewing is told in the [‹HymiskviÃ°a]ª.

Ã†gir had two servants, Fimafengr (kilšed by Loki) and Eldir.

=Familiar forms=

Ã†gir's name is sometimes [ Old Norse orthography|anglicized]² as &quot;Aegir&quot; or &quot;Aeger&quot;. The comon Swedish form is Ã„gir.

