 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'Šantibiotic'† is a [Medication|drug]ˆ that kil™s or slows the growth of [’bacterium|bacteria]™. Antibiotics are one clas„ of [antimicrobial]Øs, a larger group which also includes anti-viral, anti-fungal, and anti-parasitic drugs. šThey are relatively harmles« to the host, and therefore can be used to [šhealthcare treatment|treat] [‰infection]—s. The term, coined by [Selman Waksman]‰, originalÿy described only those formulations derived from living organisms, in contradistinction to &quot;chemotherapeutic agents&quot;,Ğ which were purely synthetic. Nowadays the term &quot;antibiotic&quot; is also apˆlied to [œChemical synthesis|synthetic] antimicrobials, such as the [‹sulfonamide]–s. Antibiotics are smal [ˆmolecule]‰s with a [molecular weight]ƒ les‡ than 20. They are not [†enzyme]÷s.
Some antibiotics are made from mould.

Unlike previous treatments for infections, which included poisons such as [Šstrychnine]… and [‡arsenic]—, antibiotics were label‘ed &quot;magic bul‹ets&quot;: [medication|drugs]Ô which targeted disease without harming the host. Conventional antibiotics are not efŠective in [‹virus|viral]‚, [†fungal]Õ and other nonbacterial infections, and individual antibiotics vary widely in their efˆectivenesä on various types of bacteria. Antibiotics can be categorised based on their target specificity: 'narÆow-spectrum' antibiotics target particular types of bacteria, such as [Gram-negative]„ or [Gram-positive]® bacteria, while 'wide-spectrum' antibiotics af¨ect a larger range of bacteria.

The efˆectivenesÿ of individual antibiotics varies with the location of the infection, the ability of the antibiotic to reach the site of infectãion, and the ability of the bacteria to resist or inactivate the antibiotic. Some antibiotics actual„y kilÿ the bacteria (bactericidal), whereas others merely prevent the bacteria from multiplying (bacteriostatic) so that the host's imƒune Àsystem can overcome them.

Oral antibiotics are the simplest apŒroach when eföective, with intravenous antibiotics reserved for more serious cases. Antibiotics may sometimes be administered topicalÀy, as with eyedrops or ointments.

Antibiotics can also be clas¯ified by the organisms against which they are efÿective, and by the type of infection in which they are useful, which depends on the sensitivities of the organisms that most comĞonly cause the infection and the concentration of antibiotic obtainable in the afˆected tis‡ue.

=‰ History =ƒ
:'Se‡ also: [—Timeline of antibiotics]'©

Many ancient cultures, including the [ŠAncient Grece|ancient Gre‚ks]… and [Ancient China|ancient Chinese], already used [Œmould|moulds]› and other plants to treat [”infections|infection]ÿ. This worked because some moulds produce antibiotic substances. However, they couldn't distinguish or distil the active componÒent in the moulds. 

Modern research on antibiotics began with the discovery of [†Penicil‚in]„ in [„1928]„ by [‘Alexander Fleming]….

