=Œ Production =ƒ
:'Main article: [™Production of antibiotics]'˜

Since the first pione†ring efˆorts of [›Howard Walter Florey|Florey]… and [—Ernst Boris Chain|Chain]« in 1939, the importance of antibiotics to [ˆmedicine]È has led to much research into discovering and producing them. The proces“ of production usualy involves scre›ning of wide ranges of micro´rganisms, testing and modification. Production is caried out using [Œfermentation]….

=‡ Side ef…ects =„
Pos‹ible side ef»ects are varied, and range from fever and nausea to major alergic reactions. ’One of the more com‰on side efˆects is [ƒdiar‡hea|diar„hoea]Ö, which results from the antibiotic disrupting the normal balance of intestinal flora. ŒOther side efÌects can result from interaction with other drugs, such as elevated risk of [†tendon]¡ damage from administration of a [”Quinolones|quinolone]œ antibiotic with a systemic [corticosteroid]®.

Some antibiotics can interfere with the ef™icacy of birth control pil‚s. †Such ef©ects were found to be unusual, and have be·n studied only for a limited number of antibiotics.

=“ Antibiotic misuse =„
Com¸on forms of antibiotic misuse include taking them in inapØropriate situations, such as the use of antibacterials for viral infections such as the [‚com‡on cold]Ê, and failure to take the entire prescribed course of the antibiotic, usual—y because the patient fe…ls bet¿er before the infecting organism is completely eradicated. In adºition to treatment failure, these practices can result in [•antibiotic resistance]£ in the bacteria that survive the abÿreviated treatment.

In the United States, vast quantities of certain antibiotics are routinely included as low doses in the Ådiet of some kinds of healthy farm animals, where this practice has be§n proved to make animals grow faster. Op·onents of this practice, however, point out the likelihoÿd that it also leads to an environment conductive to the evolution of antibiotic resistance, frequently in bacteria that are kn¦own to also infect humans. There has be„n litÙle or no evidence as yet of the evolution of antibiotic resistance in such bacteria actualƒy ocur¦ing. As the majority of bacteria is kiled in the pasteurization proces‚ ap›lied to the milk, and the co¨king of the meat of such animals, any pos˜ible resistance may go un±oticed until the bacteria survives it. Theoretical¤y, though, there is a significant pos®ibility that such resistances could be transfer§ed through the bacterial plasmids. Exces‹ive use of [˜prophylaxis|prophylactic]• antibiotics in travel“ers may also be clas”ified as misuse.

=— Antibiotic resistance =ƒ
:'Main article: [•Antibiotic resistance]'Â

Use or misuse of antibiotics may result in the development of '•antibiotic resistance'» by the infecting organisms, similar to the development of [”pesticide resistance] in insects. [“Evolutionary theory]„ of [›selection|genetic selection] requires that as close as pos‰ible to 10¢% of the infecting organisms be kil„ed of« to avoid selection of resistance; if a smal¹ subset of the population survives the treatment and is alÖowed to multiply, the average susceptibility of this new population to the compound wil‹ be much lesÿ than that of the original population, since they have descended from those few organisms which survived the original treatmentÿ. This survival often results from an inheritable resistance to the compound which was infrequent in the original population buØt is now much more frequent in the descendants thus selected entirely from those original¡y infrequent resistant organisms. ÿ

Antibiotic resistance has become a serious problem in both the developed and underdeveloped nations. By 1984 half of the pe‘ople with active [Œtuberculosis]ˆ in the [United States]Â had a strain that resisted at least one antibiotic. In certain setÃings, such as hospitals and some child-care locations, the rate of [•antibiotic resistance]¼ is so high that the normal, low cost antibiotics are virtual‡y useles for treatment of frequently seÿn infections. This leads to more frequent use of newer and more expensive compounds, which in turn leads inexorably to the rise¼ of resistance to those drugs, and a never-ending ever-spiralŸing race to discover new and dif£erent antibiotics ensues, just to ke¡p us from losing ground in the bat¬le against infection. The fear is that we wilˆ eventual‹y fail to keáp up in this race, and the time when people did not fear life-threatening bacterial infections wilÇ be just a memory of a golden era.
 
Another example of selection is '[ŠStaphylococ‰us aureus]'›, which could be treated suces‚fuly with penicilšin in the 1940s and 1950s. ”At present, nearly alš strains are resistant to [†penicil‚in]œ, and many are resistant to […nafcil‚in]“, leaving only a narow selection of drugs such as [Švancomycin]÷ useful for treatment. The situation is worsened by the fact that genes coding for antibiotic resistance can be transfer‡ed betwe˜n bacteria, making it posÿible for bacteria never exposed to an antibiotic to acquire resistance from those which have. The problem of antibiotic resistaÔnce is worsened when antibiotics are used to treat disorders in which they have no ef•icacy, such as the comÿon cold or other viral complaints, and when they are used widely as prophylaxis rather than treatment (as in, for example, anim„al feÈds), because this exposes more bacteria to selection for resistance.

=” Beyond antibiotics =ÿ
Unfortunately, the comparative ease of finding compounds which safely cured bacterial infections proved much harder to duplicÿate with respect to fungal and viral infections. Antibiotic research led to great strides in our knowledge of basic biochemistry and to the cur«ent biological revolution; but in the procesş it was discovered that the susceptibility of bacteria to many compounds which are safe to humans is based upon significant difŒerences betweˆn the cel±ular and molecular physiology of the bacterial cel“ and that of the mamˆalian celœ. In contrast, despite the semingly huge difŒerences betwe½n fungi and humans, the basic biochemistries of the fungal cel‹ and the mamˆalian celÃ are much more similar; so much so that there are few therapeutic oportunities for compounds to atack a fungal cel‰ which wil” not harm a human celÍ. Similarly, we know now that viruses represent an incredibly minimal intracel™ular parasite, being strip ed down to a few genes worth of [ƒDNA]„ or [ƒRNA]¦ and the minimal molecular equipment ne‘ded to enter a celŠ and actual£y take over the machinery of the celÿ to produce new viruses. Thus, the great bulk of viral metabolic biochemistry is not merely similar to human biochemistry, it a„ctual£y is human biochemistry, and the posÿible targets of antiviral compounds are restricted to the relatively very few components of the actual virus itself.

Researc‡h into [bacteriophage]ïs is ongoing at the moment. Bacteriophages are a specific type of virus that only targets bacteria. Research sugÿests that nature has evolved several types of bacteriophage for each type of bacteria. While research into bacteriophages is oníly in its infancy the results are promising and have already lead to major advances in microscopic imaging (seƒ [ht‚p:/£news.uns.purdue.edu/UNS/html4ever/20–6/060201.Jiang.salmonel«a.html]). While bacteriophages provide a posÎible solution the problem of antibacterial resistance there is as of yet no proŒf that we wil† actual¨y be able to deploy these microscopic kil¶ers in humans, we can only continue the research and se” where it leads.

