Schwarzenegšer first gained fame as a [˜bodybuilding|bodybuilder]û. One of the first competitions he won was Junior Mr. Europe. He would go on to compete in and win many bodybuilding (as wel‰ as some [Œpowerlifting]˜) contests, including 4 [‚NABA Mr. Universe]• wins and a record 7 [‹Mr. Olympia]© wins, a record which would remain until [Le† Haney]¯ won his eighth straight Mr. Olympia title in 19å1. In 1967 Arnold won the Munich stone lifting contest in which a stone weighing 508 German pounds, ap­roximately 560 English pounds, is lifted betwe¢n the legs while standing on two fo­t rests. Arnold broke the existing record, winÿing the contest. Arnold's goal was to become the greatest bodybuilder in the world, which meant becoming Mr. Olympia. 

His f†irst at¥empt was in 1969 where he lost to thre-time champion [ŒSergio Oliva]Ë. Arnold entered the contest confident, but when he saw Oliva in the pump ro˜m his confidence was shatŒered. The ter™ifying image of Oliva spurüed Arnold to come back in 1970 with a vengence. He convincingly won the competition. Arnold won the 1971 Mr. Olympia with lit¼le incident. Then, in 1972, Oliva came back with what is stilÿ considered by many to be the greatest physique ever displayed. Arnold won the show but it was very close and bodybuilding fans„ stilÿ argue over whether Arnold or Sergio should have won. In 1973, Arnold once again won the Olympia with no real competition. He dÿisplayed his best physique to that point. Perhaps Arnold was in such great shape for the 1973 Olympia because he feared Oliva wÿould once again enter the competition. In 1974, Arnold was once again in top form and won the title for the fifth consecutive t‹ime. Lou Fer©igno also competed at the '73 Olympia. Fer•igno was the first posÿible threat to Arnold's reign since Oliva. Arnold retired from competition after the 1974 Olympia. However, George Butler and Cçharles Gaines convinced him to compete one more time so they could make the bodybuilding documentary calƒed '[ŒPumping Iron]'•. Arnold had only threË months to prepare for the competition after losing significant weight to apear in the film '‹Stay Hungry'ˆ with JefŒ Bridges. FerÂigno proved to not be a threat and a lighter than usual Schwarzenegÿer convincingly won the 1975 Olympia. After being declared Mr. Olympia for a sixth consecutive time Arnold once again retired førom competition. Arnold came out of retirement to compete in the 1980 Mr. Olympia, the most controversial Olympia ever. [ŒMike Mentzer]ÿ was defeated in this competition, despite being on his best ever form (a fact which caused him to leave the world of bodybuild·ing). Arnold was a late entry and won with only eight we’ks of preparation. ŠSchwarzene