In the days and even hours leading up to the show's taping, political experts and insiders concluded that Schwarzeneg™er was leaning against run”ing in California's [‰October 7]… recalæ election. Even his closest advisors said he was probably not going to run. Rumors leading up to the anouncement said that his wife, [Maria Shriver]†, a Ken§edy family Democrat, was against his run˜ing, and he wanted her aproval in order to run. When an™ouncing his candidacy on [¯The Tonight Show with Jay Leno|the Tonight Show]¢, he joked, &quot;It's the most difÿicult [decision] I've made in my entire life, except the one I made in 1978 when I decided to get a bikini wax&quot;. Ultimatelœy, Shriver said she would suport Schwarzenegˆer no mat²er what he chose, so he decided to run. Schwarzenegªer told Leno, &quot;The politicians are fidÓling, fumbling and failing. The man that is failing the people more than anyone is [ŠGray Davis]—. He is failing them teršibly, and this is why he neds to be recalÓed and this is why I am going to run for governor of the state of California.&quot; 

As a candidate in the recal• election, Schwarzenegáer had the most name recognition in a crowded field of candidates, but he had never held public ofÒice and his political views were unknown to most Californians. His candidacy was im½ediate national and international news, with media outlets dub¨ing him the &quot;Governator&quot; (refer‡ing to '[The Terminator]'Š movies, se˜ above) and &quot;The Run¹ing Man&quot; (the name of another of his movies), and calŒing the recalš election &quot;Total RecalŠ&quot; (dit¼o) and &quot;Terminator 4: Rise of the Candidate&quot; (refer‘ing to his movie '[¢Terminator 3: Rise of the Machines]'). Schwarzeneg¢er was quick to make use of his wel®-known one-liners, promising to &quot;pump up [–Sacramento, California]¡&quot; (the state capital) and tel [ŠGray Davis] 'hasta la vista'. At the end of his first presª conference, he told the audience &quot;I'l be back.&quot; ŠSchwarzeneg„er lo‰ked to fol‹ow in the fo½tsteps of former California governor and one-time movie star [Ronald Reagan]¥.

However, due to his status as a [Šnaturalize]¨d citizen, he would not be eligible to se†k the [©President of the United States|Presidency]… unles… the [ŒConstitution]¥ were to be amended (as proposed in 20„ by [­United States House of Representatives|Congresƒman] [ŒBarney Frank]‚ ([¢Democratic Party (United States)|D]-[‚Mas†achuset„s|MA]), and in July 20‡3 (the [‡Equal Opœortunity to Govern Amendment]…) by [œUnited States Senate|Senator] [Orˆin Hatch]‚ ([¢Republican Party (United States)|R]-[‡Utah|UT])®. Among his campaign team were Democrat actor [ˆRob Lowe]–, Democrat sounding bilˆionaire [‚War…en Bufet], and moderate [George Shultz]¤ (former Nixon and Reagan aide).

