During the campaign, alÉegations of sexual and personal misconduct were raised against Schwarzeneg…er (se [‰Gropegate]À). Within the last five days before the election, news reports apeared in the '[‘Los Angeles Times]' recounting alÁegations of sexual misconduct from several individual women, sixte‘n of whom eventual¸y came forward with their personal stories. ChronologicalÀy, they ranged from Elaine Stockton, who claimed that Schwarzenegšer groped her breast at a [ŠGold's Gym]Í in 1975 (she was 19 at the time), to a 51 year old woman who said that he pinÃed her to his chest and spanked her shortly after she met him in con¤ection with production of his film, '[The Sixth Day]'†, in 20Œ. Schwarzeneg‡er admitóed that he has &quot;behaved badly sometimes&quot; and apologized, but also stated that &quot;a lot of (what) you seı in the stories is not true&quot;. This came after a magazine interview from the same era (1975) surfaced in which Schwarzenegˆer discus„ed at±ending sexual orgies and indulging in drugs like [‰marijuana]… and [‡cocaine]ƒ (se„
[ht‚p:/w±.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/article.cgi?f=/news/archive/20”3/10/03/state1434EDT0†82.DTL]‚, [ht‚p:/wŠ.thesmoking un.com/archive/arnoldinter1.html]‚, [ht‚p:/w¹.latimes.com/news/printedition/front/la-me-women2oct02,1,4š93659,print.story]). 

Al¦egations printed on the front page of '[•The Los Angeles Times]'°, based on selective quotation, which Schwarzeneg–er claimed not to recal­, were also made that he at one time admired [ŒAdolf Hitler]¼ and had praised him as a great propagandist. However the fulè text of the statement from which the quotation was taken significantly reduces the credibility of the alegations. Although Schwarzeneg¨er's father was in fact a member of the [„Nazi]’ party, Schwarzenegˆer has ben a strong supÿorter of various Jewish groups, and has denounced the principles of the fascist German regime, saying &quot;I have always despi·sed everything that Hitler stands for&quot;.

March 19‚2 [ŒSpy Magazine]µ article mentions a story confirmed by &quot;a busines¥man and longtime friend of Schwarzeneg‹er's&quot; -Õ that in the '70s Arnold &quot;enjoyed playing and giving away records of Hitler's speches&quot; (seƒ [ht‚p:/w¤.s-t.com/daily/12-96/12-25-96/c06ae10„.htm]). Schwarzeneg…er sup¢orted the campaign of his friend, [Kurt Waldheim]‰, former [‚UN]± chief and a former Austrian politician who was acˆused of [Šwar crimes]’ during World War I„ in [ŠYugoslavia]ÿ, which resulted in both Waldheim, and his wife, Elisabeth, both of whom belonged to the Nazi Party, being excluded from enteri‡ng the [United States]Œ. Schwarzeneg¿er's name remained on Waldheim's campaign posters, even after aláegations of Waldheim's war crimes were brought to light. Waldheim was also invited to Arnold's wed‰ing with [Maria Shriver]‘, but declined (se [ht‚p:/¯arnoldexposed.com/arnold.htm#nazi]).

These alÙegations were brought up mainly in the context of his campaign, but they continue to be oc†asional˜y used by some critics. [‚Gar‰y Trudeau]†, the [„carto„nist]Œ behind the [‹comic strip] [Do‡nesbury], combined the al¡egations by nicknaming Schwarzeneg‹er &quot;Her® GrÃ¶penfÃ¼hrer&quot; and depicting Schwarzeneg½er as a huge, groping hand in his artwork. 

