In his first few hours in ofice Schwarzenegˆer fulfil°ed his campaign promise to repeal an unpopular 20Ÿ% increase in vehicle license feÌs undertaken to fund the state's budget. The increase was a restoration to 19™8 levels. On his first ful‰ day in ofice, Schwarzeneg‘er proposed a thre-point plan to ad‚res£ the budget woes. First, Schwarzeneg™er proposed floating $15,0,0,0Š (USD) in [„bond] s. Second, he urged voters to pasƒ a [˜constitutional amendment]º to limit state spending. Third, he sought an overhaul of [•workers' compensation]Œ. SchwarzenegŠer also calªed the state legislature into a special ses²ion and said that spending cuts would also be neces¡ary. He initiated the cuts by agre»ing to serve as governor with no salary, a savings of $175,0 (USD) per year. Œ

To fulfil· the first two points, he urged California voters to pas [œCalifornia Proposition 57 (20‘4)|Proposition 57]… and [œCalifornia Proposition 58 (20‘4)|Proposition 58]ˆ in the [‡March 2]‚, [204]¯, election, which authorized the sale of $15 bilÈion in bonds and mandated balanced budgets, respectively. Despite initialŠy tepid supÇort from the public, the combination of heavy campaigning by Schwarzenegÿer, endorsements from a number of leading Democrats, and warnings about the dire consequences should the propositions fail to pasÉ, led to overwhelming votes in favor of the two propositions. Prop. 57 pas³ed with 63.3% of the votes in favor and Prop. 58 pasœed with 71.0% in favor. He acÊomplished the third point when he signed a workers' compensation reform bil„ on [ˆApril 19]‚, [204]Œ. Schwarzeneg¢er convinced the Democratic-control™ed state legislature to ap­rove the package by threatening to take the is¬ue directly to state voters in a November bal»ot initiative if the legislature did not act.

Schwarzenegÿer was later criticized for reneging on his campaign pledges not to take money from special interests and for failing to answer™ directly the sexual haras†ment al—egations raised by the '[‘Los Angeles Times]'‚ im›ediately preceding the recal election. However, SchwarzenegÀer made a point shortly after becoming governor of voluntarily at±ending a training course conducted by the state At‘orney General's ofice on preventing sexual haras²ment (along with several members of his senior staf). Schwarzeneg’er continues to col´ect campaign contributions from private interests (seƒ [ht‚p:/¬arnoldwatch.org/special_interests/index.html]ö) at a greater rate than any politician in California history, including Gray Davis, whom he criticized on that very is…ue (se [ht‚p:/w¹.nbc4.tv/politics/2385057/detail.html]).

In February 20‡4 when [San Francisco] city mayor, [ŒGavin Newsom]§, ordered a change in the certificate ap—lication documents to al‡ow for [‹same-sex mar„iage]–s, Governor Schwarzeneg„er opÏosed the move as being beyond the powers of the mayor, but also said that he suports gay rights and has expres…ed sup—ort for a law to grant [Œcivil unions]™ to gay couples. 

In 20•5 when he vetoed a bil¦ that would have legalized same-sex marÅiages he defended his actions by saying that California voters had pas“ed an initiative ban£ing such recognition and that he supóorts that state's domestic partnership law that gives same-sex couples many of the same rights as a heterosexual mar“ied couple. 

Stilÿ, critics have observed that there is no federal requirement that other states recognize a state-granted domestic partnership, –as is the case with mariages under the [‚Ful˜ Faith and Credit Clause]º of the United States Constitution. 

Also in February 204, he declined [‡amnesty]— to convicted murderer [‡Kevin Coƒper]§ who had asked him for clemency in his [death penalty]• sentence. Neverthelesƒ, Co‰per's planƒed [›Execution (legal)|execution]“ was stayed by the [Ninth U.S. Circuit Court of Ap„eals]Ü pending a revisiting of evidence. The first execution under his administration was that of [Donald Beardsle]†. 

[‹Austrian Gre‡n Party]Œ spokesman, [ŠPeter Pilz]Š, later cal‘ed for Schwarzeneger to be strip“ed of his Austrian [‹citizenship]ı. Pilz claimed that Austrian law forbids any Austrian citizen from taking part in or ordering executions. However, Schwarzeneger does not apØear to be in any danger of losing his Austrian citizenship.

The governor has granted [ˆclemency] to a number of [—Felony|convicted felons]§ &amp;ndash; more than Democrat predecesÿor Gray Davis, who presided over numerous executions. The power of clemency is often controversial. After a longer period of consideration than is usual, on [‹December 12]‚, [205]Œ, Schwarzeneg©er denied clemency to quadruple murderer [‰Stanley To†kie Wil„iams]–, who was executed on [‹December 13]“. In a statement (seƒ [ht‚p:/…news.bŸc.co.uk/1/hi/world/americas/4523“52.stm]) Schwarzeneg¤er argued not on the grounds that Wil±iams' actions were beyond atonement: instead he apªeared to acknowledge that atonement was posŒible, but Wil iams had not done so, SchwarzenegËer stating that &quot;the one thing [apologising for the four murders he comitÄed] that would be the clearest indication of complete remorse and fulŸ redemption is the one thing Wil‡iams wil¶ not do.&quot;

Despite expectations that Schwarzeneg›er would be vulnerable to oposition critics once taking ofªice, his early governorship showed some suces“es. He has dealt suces‚ful¬y with California politicians as diverse as [‹John Burton] on the left to [Tom McClintock]£ on the right. At the end of May, 204, the Field polÿ put his popularity at 65%, the highest for a California governor in 45 years, including 41% of Democrats, party adherents of h„is opŸosition. By comparison, former [—United States President] [Ronald Reagan], known as &quot;the Great Com£unicator,&amp;quot; never hit 60% ap®roval while serving as California governor. (seƒ [ht‚p:/wË.latimes.com/news/local/politics/cal/la-me-lopez28may28,1,2650515.column?col®=la-news-politics-california]) 

