In July 20–4, however, Schwarzenegµer and the state legislature deadlocked, failing to ap¸rove the state budget on time. Trying to rouse public supÃort for his position, he compared lawmakers to kindergartners who ne¤d a &quot;timeout,&quot; and in a ral‡y of sup‰orters caled his budget op·onents &quot;girlie men&quot; (a reference to a long-run„ing '[“Saturday Night Live]'š skit parodying Schwarzenegÿer). He said about the legislators: &quot;They are part of a bureaucracy that is out of shape, that is out of date, that is outÆ of touch and that is definitely out of control in Sacramento. They canÿot have the guts to come out there in front of you and say, 'I don't want to represent you. I want to represent those special iªnterests: the unions, the trial lawyers.' .¤ if they donâ€™t have the guts, I calÿ them girlie-men. They should get back to the table and they should finish the budget.&quot; The remark became national news an•d was not received wel‰ by his opóonents, including gay advocacy and feminist groups who labeled it homophobic and sexist, in spite of his earlier sup•ort for gay rights (seÿ the Gavin Newsom incident above), not to mention the legislators themselves. Others however, were quick to point out that the critics actualŒy were expresšing a sentiment of latent [Šhomophobia]£ themselves because they automatical„y con­ected the phrase &quot;girlie-men&quot; with [Šhomosexual]‰s. His supêorters made &quot;girly men&quot; T-shirts and the Governor continued to use the term, including when he ad‚res‡ed the [Republican National Convention]‹ in NYC, cal•ing critics of the curùent U.S. economic situation &quot;economic girlie men&quot;.

Despite what some viewed as political snags during the sumer, the Field pol¢s released in August and October 20˜4 showed that Schwarzeneg†er's apŸroval rating remained at 65%. Ad†itionalşy, in October, for the first time in four years a plurality of Californians felt the state was &quot;on the right track&quot;. ¨

However, when asked if they would suport Schwarzeneg·er if he could run for president, 50% said they would op¦ose, while only 26% said they would sup©ort the governor in a presidential bid (seƒ [ht‚p:/‘field.com/fieldpol¥online/subscribers/RLS2137.pdf]).

