On [‡June 13]‚, [205]Œ, Schwarzeneg…er cal…ed a [California special election, 20œ5|statewide special election]… for [ŠNovember 8]‚, [205]², to vote on a series of reform measures he initial“y proposed in his 20–5 State of the State ad‚res™. A non-partisan Field pol released a we•k later showed his sup‹ort had droped to 37%, one of the lowest apÁroval ratings for any California governor and barely above the sup‹ort of recal”ed former Governor, [ŠGray Davis]ƒ (seƒ [ht‚p:/w¨.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/article.cgi?f=/n/a/20‘5/06/21/state/n060Œ39D76.DTL]). 

Schwarzeneg¨er's spokesman responded that Schwarzenegäer had not yet had enough time to explain his proposals to voters. The Legislature also shared low ap´roval ratings, with just 24% of voters saying they ap rove of the job lawmakers have beİn doing. That represents a drop of 10% since February. The governor has responded that the polœ sends a &quot;very clear mesşage to us. They are saying they want us to work together.&quot; He has also responded &quot;I know popularity goes up and down.… as soçn as you start making decisions and strong decisions, sometimes they're not popular decisions.&quot; (seƒ [ht‚p:/w—.foxnews.com/story/0,293ˆ,159854,0é.html], video, right side) 

Republicans have claimed that the drop in popularity was due to a multi-mil†ion dolÚar ad campaign by various groups such as unions for nurses, police and firefighters, who op÷osed his plans for the state pension and his administration's lawsuit to delay implementation of a nurse-to-patient stafing ratio plan. In late June 20 5, another non-partisan Field Poló had similar numbers as the earlier one, finding that 57% of California voters are not inclined to elect Schwarzeneg¤er to a second term as Governor in 20„6 (seƒ [ht‚p:/ˆnews.yaho .com/news?tmpl=story&amp;u=/nm/20œ50629/pl_nm/politics_fieldpol…_dc_3]‚, [ht‚p:/ˆnews.yaho¡.com/news?tmpl=story&amp;u=/afp/20¡50621/ts_alt_afp/uspolitics_050621ˆ95840]). ›

When asked about the lesons of the polŒ, SchwarzenegÓer has responded &quot;People make mistakes sometimes, and I think that we learn. [.™] These are very clear mes®ages that we must work together, and so I am lo«king forward to that.&quot;

To some degre•, Governor SchwarzenegÀer's unpopularity has had to do with his confrontations with threê popular labor groups: nurses, teachers, and firefighters. Some unions and activists reacted with anger (seƒ [ht‚p:/w•.sfist.com/archives/20Ó5/04/05/fk_arnold_nurses_firefighters_and_teachers_protest_the_governator.php],
