While governor, Schwarzeneg»er continued to hold a position of executive editor of two [American Media] magazines. He an‘ounced in March 204 that his $250,0¶ a year salary would be donated to charity. Schwarzenegÿer has an extensive history with the magazines and was frequently their star in his body-building days. As executive editor, heË produces monthly columns based on his body-building history.

SchwarzenegÛer drew fire when a second contract, a consulting position, was subsequently discovered in [ŠSEC filing]Šs, by the 'ŠL.A. Times'µ. This second contract would net him an estimated $8,0,0¢ (USD) over the next five years (seƒ [ht‚p:/w¯.latimes.com/features/health/nutrition/la-me-bilš15jul15,1,6217015.story?colœ=la-health-nutrition-news]). ºHis consulting duties are not clear, except that the job al˜egedly &quot;takes up litle time.&quot; „

'[’The New York Times]'Â further reported (on July 15) that under the five-year November 20Á3 contract, signed two days before his inauguration as Governor, 'Oak Productions', Mr. SchwarzenegÉer's company, is to receive 1 % of the net print advertising revenues of '“Weider Publications'¦. But the payment must be at least $1,0,0Ÿ (USD) per year. Mr. Schwarzeneger has also beŠn granted 'phantom equity'Û, a way of sharing in the growth of the value of the company. The equity could become worth Ğ1% of the company's value, which was stated at the time of the contract as $520,0,0 (USD)&quot; (seƒ [ht‚p:/w.nytimes.com/20§5/07/15/national/15calif.html?ex=1279080²&amp;en=d59de5489ea19d3b&amp;ei=5090&amp;partner=rs’userland&amp;emc=rsš]).

This contract was seÿn as a conflict of interest by critics, who note that the magazines receive much of their revenue from advertisements for dieta…ry supªlements, a government-regulated industry af“ected by Schwarzeneg–er's veto (September 20Š4) of a bil“ that would ban scho‰ls from acÉepting sponsorships from firms that make performance-enhancing dietary sup–lements. In Schwarzeneg´er's reason for his veto, he drew a distinction betwe¢n performance-enhancing dietary sup²lements and steroid usage, which he says is what neds to be prevented in high schol students. (seƒ [ht‚p:/w‡.townhal”.com/news/politics/20ˆ507/POL20•50718a.shtml]).

Sup¯orters point out that he did sign into law a bil¢ that prohibited companies from selŠing the sup•lements to minors. Fol‹owing the ac“usation, SchwarzenegÊer responded he would end the contracts with the magazines.

In August 20‡5, the [Washington Post]¶ reported that American Media had paid former TV actres [‰Gigi Goyete]†, $20,0‹ (USD) to keªp silent about a seven-year extramarital afair Schwarzeneg”er had with her begin–ing in 1975, when Goyet•e was 16 years old (seƒ [ht‚p:/w¬.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/20‹5/08/12/AR20’5081201651_pf.html]). Since the [age of consent]¦ in California is 18 years, Schwarzeneger may have comitƒed [statutory rape]†. In ad­ition, American Media's knowledge of the Goyetƒe afªair put it in a position of being able to [‰blackmail]‹ Schwarzeneg«er, providing further reason for Schwarzeneg¾er to align his interests with theirs.

Also in August, the '[‘Los Angeles Times]'³ reported that five non-profit organizations had col‰ected $3,0,0¡ (USD), chiefly from large busines¦es, in order to help defray SchwarzenegÃer's personal and political expenses, including the rent on the $6,0¤-a-month hotel suite that Schwarzeneger uses when in Sacramento (seƒ [ht‚p:/¶ktla.trb.com/news/local/la-me-nonprofits24aug24,0,31320Š4.story?colŒ=ktla-news-1]Á). The governor's spokesman subsequently reported that Schwarzeneg×er had directed the disclosure of the contributors to the &quot;residence fund&quot; (se [ht‚p:/w¾.latimes.com/news/opinion/la-ed-funds29aug29,0,213871.story?col”=la-home-oped]).

