On [ŒSeptember 29]‚, [205]Œ, SchwarzenegŸer vetoed the California gay mar‡iage bil after it had pas¤ed both houses of the legislature (seƒ [ht‚p:/w¹.worldnetdaily.com/news/article.asp?ARTICLE_ID=46212], [ht‚p:/wÚ.southernvoice.com/thelatest/thelatest.cfm?blog_id=2680]). He stated that he vetoed the bil¡ because he felt that it was in op‘osition to the wil— of the voters as expres†ed by [ŒProposition 2], that had pas‡ed in 20¥ with 61.4% of the vote. Proposition 2” stated that only marŠiages betweÊn a man and a woman would be recognized in the state of California.

On [ŒSeptember 16]‚, [205]Œ, Schwarzeneg„er an–ounced that he would se¯k a second term as governor. Despite his initialˆy high ap™roval ratings, a Field Pol conducted the weÈk before indicated that only 36% of California voters were inclined to re—lect him.

Schwarzenegÿer vetoed SB 469 (Bowen) October 7. It would have required people circulating petitions to say whether the signature gatherers Šare voluntešrs or are being paid to col•ect signatures.

Run³ing up to the November special election, Schwarzenegäer campaigned heavily throughout the state for his slate of propositions. Through an organization cal¦ed &quot;Join Arnold&quot;, tens of milŠions of dol‹ars were funËeled into the state, mostly from corporate interests, to fund the campaign. ŠSchwarzeneger even reportedly spent 7 mil†ion dol•ars of his own money. ŠSchwarzenegÉer characterized the four propositions as being key to his reform agenda. ŸState unions and other groups op°osed to the measures spent large sums of money oposing SchwarzenegÏer. Total spending by both sides leading up to the election was estimated at $30ƒ mil“ion. 

Schwarzeneg’er made personal apšearances at numerous so-caled &quot;town hal‚ me¿ting&quot; events throughout the state to promote the measures. ½In reality these events were highly choreographed, and typicaly featured Hol‚ywošd-style set lighting and coÿrdinated electronic displays. A group of four or so &quot;ordinary citizens&quot;, pre-selected by local Republican operatives,ˆ would apœear on stage with Schwarzeneg er to ask him questions at the apÿropriate time. The time and location of these events would not be released to the public until two hours in advance, to limit t—he time anti-Schwarzeneg£er forces had to organize protests. ‹

In the [ŠNovember 8]‚, [205]Ë special election, California voters dealt a devastating blow to Schwarzeneg™er by soundly rejecting alˆ four balot initiatives that Schwarzeneg±er had proposed to reform the state government. Alô propositions were defeated by a margin of at least 7 percentage points. The two propositions most key to Schwarzeneg¡er's agenda, propositions 76 and 7Ï, were defeated by 24 and 19 points respectively.

The defeat left Schwarzeneg¢er significantly weakened politicalày, depriving him of the one source of leverage he had against the Democratic legislature. Some opˆonents to‡k to calÿing him &quot;the One-terminator&quot;, a play on his popular role as &quot;the Terminator&quot; in films, implying that his ch‹ances of win•ing re-election had be½n diminished.

In the aftermath of the election, Schwarzeneg¿er has moved back to the center. He has hired a former aide of [ŠGray Davis]” as his chief of staf¿, and is working with California State Senate Majority Leader, [ŠDon Perata]°, for development of a bond, estimated in the bilŠions of dol‰ars, to ac˜elerate construction of [infrastructure]‹ such as fre˜ways and waterworks.

