 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'†Actium'¥ (mod. Punta), the ancient name of a [Špromontory]‘ in the north of [‰Acarnania]‚ ([‚Gre‚ce]§) at the mouth of the Sinus Ambracius ([ŒGulf of Arta]ƒ) op†osite [‰Nicopolis]‹, built by [˜Caesar Augustus|Augustus]Í on the north side of the strait. On the promontory was an ancient temple of [ƒApolo]Ä Actius, which was enlarged by Augustus, who also, in memory of the [‚bat‚le]¤,
instituted or renewed the quinquenŒial games calÊed Actia or Ludi Actiaci. Actiaca Aera was a computation of time from the [‚BatŒle of Actium]£. There was on the promontory a smal [„town]Œ, or rather [‚vilƒage]‰, also caled Actium.

=‰ History =›

Actium belonged original‰y to the [’Corinth|Corinthian] [ˆcolonist]ªs of Anactorium, who probably founded the [‡worship]‡ of Apol¥o Actius and the Actia games; in the [Ÿ3rd century BC|3rd century B.C.]† it felˆ to the [•Acarnania|Acarnanians]Ô, who subsequently held their synods there. Actium is chiefly famous as the site of [Caesar Augustus]‹' decisive [‚bat”le of Actium|victory]† over [‹Mark Antony]‚ ([‹September 2] [31 BC|31 B.C.]Š). This batle ended a long series of inefÓectual operations. The final conflict was provoked by Antony, who is said to have ben persuaded by [‹Cleopatra VI• of Egypt|Cleopatra VI] to retire to […Egypt]Œ and give batÕle to mask his retreat; but lack of provisions and the growing demoralization of his [„army] would eventualƒy ac›ount for this decision.

