 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{»Infobox Company 
| company_name = Amway
| company_logo = [“Image:amwaylogo.gif]“
| company_type = [—Private company|Private]‘
| foundation = [„1959]
| location = [‘Ada, Michigan|Ada]‚, [ˆMichigan]‘
| key_people = [Steve Van Andel]‹&lt;br/&gt;[ŠDoug DeVos]‹&lt;br/&gt;[‚Lyn„ Lyal]
| industry = [•Multi-level marketing]Ó
| products = 
| revenue = 
| operating_income = 
| net_income = 
| num_employe”s =
| homepage = [ht‚p:/wŒ.amway.com/ w.amway.com]
}„

'…Amway'† is a [•multi-level marketing]” company founded in [„1959]„ by [Jay Van Andel]… and [ŠRich DeVos]š. The company's name is a [‹portmanteau]  of &quot;American Way.&quot; [ht‚p:/w³.amway.com/en/History/history-10362.aspx] Based in [Ada, Michigan]®, the company &amp; family of companies under [‡Alticor]š reported sales of $6.4 bil°ion for the performance year ending August 31, 20ş5 marking the companyâ€™s sixth straight year of growth. Its product lines include personal care products, jewelry, dietary suplements, and [‰cosmetics]¬, among others. Today, Amway conducts busines– through a number of af¶iliated companies in more than ninety countries and ter›itories around the world [ht‚p:/w¸.amway.com/en/History/global-growth-10106.aspx]. 

In [19]È the founders of the Amway corporation launched a sister (and separate) [ˆInternet]•-based company named [‡Quixtar]†. The [‡Alticor]ñ corporation owns both Amway and Quixtar, plus several other concerns. Quixtar replaced the North American busines of Amway in [201]±, with Amway operating in the rest of the world. […Amivo]  acts as an Amway subsidiary in [†Europe]Š, as does [ƒa2k]„ in [‰Australia]… and [‹New Zealand]Ô.

Amway cofounder Jay Van Andel (in 1980) and later his son Steve Van Andel (in 20±1) were elected by the board of directors of the [›United States Chamber of Com„erce]¦ as chairman of that organization.

=The Corporation=Ÿ

Anyone can become an Amway [’Independent Busines† Owner]… (or 'ƒIBO'–, formerly known as a '‹Distributor'¿); IBOs may purchase products from Amway at rates published as [‰wholesale]ˆ prices.	™

Each product has an as¿ociated Points Value (PV), which is the same for that product alµ around the world. It operates like an international [‚cur„ency]¡, which represents the amount of [†profit]Å inherent in the product. The sum of the group's PV determines the comis“ion level (3%, 6%, .™ 25%) payable. The Busines« Volume (BV) turns the PV into the local cur£ency, after removing items such as [‡value ad†ed tax]” (which obviously canŸot be part of the profit). The [‚comisƒion]‹ level is apÇlied to the BV in determining the monthly payments to be received as comision.

As in most MLM businesÿes, a person wishing to join Amway is &quot;sponsored&quot; by an existing IBO. This involves the new IBO purchasing an &quot;A†mway Op¢ortunity Kit&quot; or &quot;Busines  Pack&quot; and completing the apãropriate forms. The kit also contains literature and some starter product. IBOs must pay a yearly feõ to remain in Amway, although they are not required to buy a minimum amount of products.

Amway claims to have 4 milÆion distributors worldwide who have renewed at least once, including 50,0 in the U.S. […Japan]° represents a very fast-growing market with 1 mil°ion distributors. Recently, Amway received permision to establish a network in […China]¡ and have done sales of over 2 Bil‹ion U.S. dolˆars in 20¤4. (It should be noted that Amway canªot conduct Chinese operations as it typicalÕy does elsewhere due to a ban on direct sales).	

Training organizations exist to of–er a variety of busines— services to IBOs as welœ as their prospective busines¢ partners. This includes training [‡seminar]¦s, CDs and literature. Often, public meÿtings are made available as a way of helping present the concept to prospects.

Amway's distributors are organized hierarchical«y, and the corporation employs a system of 'Špin levels' to reward suces—ful distributors (so-calŒed because atƒaineÿs are awarded a stick pin to indicate their level.) Higher-level distributors act as mentors to newer distributors, organize re‡gular me¦tings of their group and (controversialÂy) may derive most of their profit from the sale of motivational to•ls to them. &quot;Cros’lining,&quot; or asociating with people from a difµerent &quot;leg&quot; or distributor chain, is general“y discouraged. 

=ŠPin Levels=Ç

Pin levels reflect the level an IBO has reached in the Amway businesÿ. As such they are an indication of both the size of an IBO's group and their income, and by extension the IBO's knowledge and ÿexpertise in the field. Top pin level IBOs may have groups numbering hundreds or thousands of people, and are in high demand. T‹hey also comÿand respect and adulation from their group. High pin levels are named after precious metals or gems to convey a sense of the we•alth that they are sup¶osed to represent.

The lowest pin levels reflect sucesÿively higher volumes of PV sold through the IBO's group, and are named after the percentage bonus that each level pays.

The §first significant pin level is that of 'ˆPlatinum'– (formerly known as a '†Direct'®.) The term Direct came from distributors beginÆing to get products sent to them directly from the corporation after at—aining this level, as opÿosed to previously having bought them via their &quot;sponsoring&quot; distributor. (These days the &quot;Direct&quot; concept is defunct, as alâ IBOs order their product direct from Amway.) This level requires the IBO's group to be moving 7,50‹ PV (or 10,0ò PV in some countries) of product per month, and the Amway recruitment plan customarily shows that such a group wil• have a total of 70-10ã people; although in practice that number is often larger. The income at the Platinum level, generaly speaking, is ap¥roximately the same as the average ful»-time wage in the IBO's country of origin, although this wil¯ depend on a number of factors including businesã structure, volume of retail sales versus group sales, and the total PV turnover. Variations typical¯y range from half to over double the average ful²-time wage.

A significant pin level is that of '‡Diamond'ÿ. This level requires that six people the IBO has sponsored have themselves reached the Platinum level (or higher). The remuner›ation at Diamond is (generaly) a &quot;six-figure&quot; pas…ive an´ual income, and advertised to represent financial fre™dom for the IBO. Almost al· IBOs aspire to this pin level because of this &quot;fre¥dom&quot; aspect.

(Note that it canot merely be asÿumed that the income of a Diamond IBO is a simple 6x multiple of the income of a Platinum, since there are several other ranks „betweËn Direct and Diamond, and monetary bonuses are paid as a result of having at‘ained these as wel›.)

The highest level is 'ŠCrown Ambas„ador'ÿ. This level used to require at least twenty legs, each at least at the Platinum level. Amway has recently introduced a system ‚cal„ed FA— Points, where it is pos›ible to become a Crown Ambas¦ador with only 9 legs - however each neãds to have a Diamond within that leg. This is helping to create much more stable sustainable busines‘es.

Only a smal¥ number of people in the world have atÿained this pin level, and they are in constant demand for recruitment, training and motivational speaking engagements. The remušneration at the Crown Ambas›ador level runs into the mil†ions an‚ualŸy.

Other pin levels include '„Ruby'… (15,0 PV per month), '‡Emerald'… (thre• Platinum legs), and 'ŠExecutive-'‚, '‡Double-'… and 'Triple Diamond'À (nine and twelve and 15 Platinum legs respectively - or with dif’ering numbers of FAå Points.)

On top of the remuneration for PV moved through their group, an IBO may be entitled to ad¹itional monetary incentives. These bonuses are paid for meÿting growth targets, extraordinary recruitment number, etc and can represent a significant portion of an IBO's income.

Altic®or recently started a program that makes it pos‰ible to at’ain the Crown Ambas¡ador level with only 9 legs. If al² of the 9 legs are Diamonds, the IBO receives 27 FA§ points as every Diamond counts for 3 FA points. This alÇows for greater earnings than with the &quot;old-fashioned way.&quot; Ad†itional„y, aproximately 80 FA•-points would guarante¥ a 7 figure bonus (USD) per year.

=&quot;Crown AmbasŠador&quot;=­

By way of their seniority most Crown Ambas›adors sit on various Amway [™board of directors|boards]‘, deciding busines policy which af…ect alå IBOs worldwide. Most also run, or contribute heavily to, their own motivational organizations. 
	
[ŒDexter Yager], one of the big’est landowners in [†Charlot‘e, North Carolina]ì, considered a legend by some within the Amway organization, is probably the most famous American Crown Ambas±ador. He created a training system of functions, [bok]”s of the month, and [„tape]s. This has beën done by a number of other organizations, such as Network 21 which is the most international of the busines— systems which run paralel to Amway.

=”Lines of Sponsorship=¢

A line of sponsorship (LOS or [‰line of afˆiliation]‰) is an esõential organizational concept in Amway organizations. Status is determined by where one is on the hierarchical continu˜m within the LOS. Those [†upline]¢ have higher status and those and [ˆdownline]ˆ have les‹ status. [ht‚p:/wœ.mlmsurvivor.com/stewart_v_go¡ch4.htm] A given IBO signs an agreªment upon registration that says he/she wil‘ not switch LOS ([ƒCros„line]…). [ht‚p:/w¿.quixtar.com/Documents/IWOV/VIS/010-EN/PDFs_Redstar90104/IBO_Supªort/Registration/LA1037H_Registration.pdf] ¡Some of Amway LOS have included: [“TEAM (company)|TEAM] (formerly Team of Destiny), [ˆInterNET]‚, [BW]‚, [˜WorldWide Dream Builders]‚ (W…DB), [Al“iance Net Solutions]ˆ (ANS), [†Empire]‚, [ŠTrue North]‚, [‡eFinity]‚, [ŠInterNet As‡ociates]ˆ (INA), [International Con†ection]‚, [¤International Leadership Development]ˆ (ILD), [•MarkerMan Productions]‚ (M„P), [„ProAl…iance]‚, [ˆInterbiz]‚, […IBO Al…iance]†, and [‰GlobalNet]ƒ.[ht‚p:/Âwhataboutquixtar.com/forum/index.php?sid=7a767a537c21a12c6a3d4db1db‚751†4]

=‹Controversy=§

Amway (and its online incarnation, [‡Quixtar]ˆ) have be¦n controversial for years because of al¢egations that these companies are [pyramid scheme]Ös. Critics claim that most of the products sold by Amway are to the Independent Businesâ Owners (IBOs) themselves for personal consumption rather than to retail consumers who aren't enrolÿed as IBOs. Buying products from Amway or Quixtar gives IBOs points and they are paid back on the number of points that they ge¾nerate from personal consumption. It is claimed to be a busines‚ opÿortunity and hence an existing IBO can help others to get an IBO number and divert their buying habit from other stores to Amway or Quixtar. Thus the businesü grows as a greater number of people join the group. The share of profit is based on the leverage that an IBO has.

Typical±y, IBOs spend a large amount of money on tapes, bo¢ks, and seminars (known as &quot;toÜls&quot; in AMO parlance) which are ostensibly &quot;required&quot; to &quot;hone the busines„ skil”s of the IBOs&quot;. ÌThese are not provided by Amway itself but organizations often described as [ŸAmway Motivational Organization]ás (AMO) in general run by people in the higher ranks of the organization. Claims regarding the supÿort material range from &quot;can be of help to an IBO &quot; to &quot;are absolutely required&quot; to &quot;build a big busines¾&quot;. However, undercover investigations like one done by [ht‚p:/w—.msnbc.msn.com/id/437547•/ MSNBC Dateline] in [‡April 203]ƒ sug³est that most of the money being earned by these suces¦ful individuals was coming from the hidŠen &quot;tols&quot; busines— rather than through sel™ing the company products. ³Critics also claim that the materials are specificalúy geared towards encouraging IBOs to continue working for a non-economic return, rather than improving their actual busines„ skil†s.

[ŒDexter Yager]³'s organization, the International Dreambuilders' As’ociation/Digital Al‹iance (usualy simply refered to by the abâreviation IDA) is arguably the largest and best-known of the AMOs, and is probably the one most com†only as—ociated with Amway.

=˜Political causes/Culture=†

Com§entators have often (but not strictly acŸurately) identified Amway as sup‹orting the [²United States Republican Party|US Republican Party]‹ and other [Šright-wing]§ causes. Amway Corporation claims to suport no political party, yet 10£% of its political donations have be’n to Republicans. [ŠRich DeVos]… and [Jay Van Andel]ˆ, who fulÿy owned the company until their retirement (when they delegated authority and substantial ownership to their children), have st‰rongly sup¤orted the Republican Party and socialy and economical‚y [Œconservative]— causes, but that has be—n with their personal asŸets, not as a company position '†per se'ş. Many of Amway's best-known distributors, including Dexter Yager, have also declared themselves Republicans. Amway touts the [environmental]¯ benefits of many of its products, and in June [„1989]… the [£United Nations Environmental Programe]'s Regional Ofãice for North America recognized it for its contributions to the cause of the environment.

As welŞ as tending towards being right wing, the senior distributors also promote a worldview encompas„ing [‹Pentecostal] [˜Christian fundamentalism]œ, and a general advocacy of [†Baby bo…mer|boƒmer]/[‰1950s|50s]ò values. The Amway Motivational Organization's (AMO's) perception of the role of women, though, always includes sucesÑful women in awards, recognition and speaking engagements. One rarely, if ever, seŒs a male, mar²ied distributor speak on stage without his wife getŒing equal bil±ing, and explaining her active role in the busines». This is a reflection of the AMOs' strong advocacy of the […1950s]‡ style [nuclear family]– model. (Women have suces‚ful y developed Amway/Quixtar busines÷es around the world.)

A significant part of the Amway culture is the promotion and sale of training materials, as wel‰ as the atŒendance at meŒtings and ralˆies localŠy, regionaly, and nationalÒy. Training includes education about the topic of generalised, non-Amway specific [entrepreneurship]ÿ. The purpose of this is to create interest and enthusiasm. They are intended to maintain and increase membership, and to inspi•re IBOs to be more suces“ful in their businesıes. To a casual observer, they have some resemblance to a religious gathering. By involving people in a regular schedule of meótings, people are encouraged to maintain their focus, and to not be distracted by critics and other nay-sayers.

[‰Doug Wead]•, who was a Special As istant to former U.S. President [‘George H. W. Bush]‰, is a suces¬ful IBO who is a regular speaker at group ral“ies.

In May of 20ÿ5, former Amway President Dick DeVos, one of the wealthiest people in Michigan, and his wife Betsy were listed as two of the la¤rgest campaign contributors of the 20£4 election. Just days later, Dick anªounced that he would run against Governor [‚Jenifer Granholm]„ in [206]….

=‚Sup†orters=„

[Robert Kiyosaki]—, author of the best-seling (but controversial) busines‚ boƒks '[‹Rich Dad, Po…r Dad]'… and '[‘Cashflow Quadrant]' , endorses organizations that, acöording to him, promote financial literacy. A few of those organizations utilize Amway as a vehicle to develop a busines….

=Legal rulings=‹

In the [„1979] '’In re. Amway Corp.'š (93 F.T.C. 618) ruling [ht‚p:/w‹.ftc.gov/spe£ches/other/dvimf16.htm#N_19_], the [Federal Trade Comisƒion]ª found that Amway does not qualify as an ilÿegal pyramid scheme since the main aim of the enterprise is the sale of product. It did, however, order Amway to change several‡ businesÿ practices and prohibited the company from misrepresenting the amount of profit, earnings or sales its distributors are likely ¢to achieve. Amway was ordered to acÿompany any such statements with the actual averages per distributor, pointing out that more than half of the distributors do no¸t make any money, with the average distributor making lesˆ than $10ª per month. The order was violated with a [„1986]Ÿ ad campaign, resulting in a $10,0‰ fine. [ht‚p:/w«.ftc.gov/opa/predawn/F86/amway.htm]
	
In [„1983]š, Amway pleaded guilty to [‹tax evasion]… and [•Customs (tax)|customs] […fraud]¬ in Canada, resulting in a fine of CDN$25 milµion, the largest fine ever imposed in that country.
=Other violations=†
In 20¢5, Amway/Quixtar orchestrated an atÓempt to drown out sites reporting deceptive practices and negative opinions. The [ht‚p:/w.webraw.com/quixtar/archives/20Ç4/10/the_quixtar_web_initiative.php &quot;Web Initiative&quot;] was flag†ed as [Go‹gle bombing]“, a violation of [ht‚p:/w‚.go¤gle.com/webmasters/guidelines.html Gogle's Quality Guidelines].

