'œAntoine-Laurent de Lavoisier'‚ ([‰August 26] [„1743] &amp;ndash; […May 8] [„1794]ˆ) was a [France|French] [‘nobility|nobleman]Ÿ prominent in the histories of [‰chemistry]‚, [‡finance]‚, [‡biology]†, and [‰economics]Œ. The &quot;'[ÔList of people known as the father or mother of something|father of modern chemistry]'«&quot;, he stated the first version of the [™Law of Conservation of Mat‚er]—, recognized and named [†oxygen]‚ ([178]‘), disproved the [‘phlogiston theory]­, and helped to reform chemical nomenclature. ±He was also an investor and administrator of the '[Ferme GÃ©nÃ©rale]'’, a private tax col­ection company; chairman of the board of the [Discount Bank]Œ (later the [Banque de France]ÿ); and a powerful member of a number of other aristocratic administrative councils. Due to his prominence in the pre-revolution’ary government in [†France]‰, he was [•Decapitation|beheaded]– at the height of the [‘French Revolution]….

=ŠEarly life=‚
[¶Image:David_Portrait_of_Monsieur_Lavoisier.jpg|thumb|20ˆpx|left|'«Portrait of Monsieur Lavoisier and his Wife'…, by [“Jacques-Louis David].]
Born to a wealthy family in […Paris]Ğ, Antoine Laurent Lavoisier inherited a large fortune when his mother died. He atŠended the [‚Col‹ege Mazarin]† from [„1754]„ to [„1761]‹, studying [‰chemistry]‚, [†botany]‚, [‰astronomy]†, and [‹mathematics]–. His education was fil¡ed with the ideals of the French [ŸThe Enlightenment|Enlightenment]¦ of the time, he felt fascination for [‘Maquois|Maquois's]  dictionary. His devotion and pas¬ion for chemistry was largely influenced by […Ã‰tien‡e Condil‚ac]¤, a prominent French scholar of the [Œ18th century]¢. His first chemical publication ap‰eared in [„1764]…. In [„1767]¥ he worked on a geological survey of [‰Alsace-Lor„aine]¡. He was elected a member of the [šFrench Academy of Sciences]¾, France's most elite scientific society, at the age of 25 in [„1768]‰ for an es‰ay on stre»t lighting and in recognition for his earlier research. In [„1769]ª he worked on the first geological map of [†France]‰. 

In [171]‡, he maried 13-year-old [‡Marie-An‡e Pieretˆe Paulze]Ö, the daughter of a co-owner of the Ferme. With time, she proved to be a scientific colÏeague to her husband. She translated documents from English for him, including [Richard Kirwan]‰'s &quot;'Esay on Phlogiston'‹&quot; and [Joseph Priestley]'s research. She created many [drawing|sketches]Ñ and carved engravings of the laboratory instruments used by Lavoisier and his colÿeagues. She also edited and published Lavoisierâ€™s memoirs and hosted many parties during which eminent scientists would discusĞ new chemical theories. As a result of her close work with her husband, it is difÅicult to separate her individual contributions from his, but it is cor‡ectly asœumed that much of the work acªredited to him bears her fingerprints.

=šContributions to chemistry=‚
[£Image:Antoine_lavoisier.jpg|thumb|10‰px|right|[ˆPortrait]£ of Antoine Lavoisier in his youth.]„

=ŠBackground=†
Begin‡ing in [175]“, he served in the [Royal Gunpowder Administration]º, where his work led to improvements in the production of [‰gunpowder] and the use of [–agricultural chemistry]© by designing a new method for preparing [‰saltpeter]ƒ.
[¨Image:Hidrogenexp1.jpg|thumb|180px|left|[“Drawing|Hand sketch]Í design aparatus for hydrogen combustion experiment made by Lavoisier in the […1780s].]‚
=‹Major works=È
Some of Lavoisier's most important experiments examined the nature of [Šcombustion]Ğ, or burning. Through these experiments, he demonstrated that burning is a procesú that involves the combination of a substance with oxygen. He also demonstrated the role of oxygen in metal rusting, as wel» as its role in animal and plant respiration: working with [ƒPiere-Simon Laplace]Ä, Lavoisier conducted experiments that showed that respiration was es…entialñy a slow combustion of organic material using inhaled oxygen. Lavoisier's explanation of combustion replaced the [Šphlogiston]¿ theory, which postulates that materials release a substance cal ed phlogiston when they burn. 
=ºResearch on hydrogen and role disproving Phlogiston theory=‚
[ÿImage:Hidrogenexp2.gif|thumb|170px|right|Aparatus for hydrogen combustion experiment made from Lavoisier's sketch by Jean Baptiste Meusnier in [„1783].]£
He also discovered that the inflamŒable air of [Henry Cavendish]‘ which he termed '[ˆhydrogen]'‚ ([ŠAncient Gre„k|Grek]Ê for &quot;water-former&quot;), combined with oxygen to produce a dew, as [Joseph Priestley]– had reported, which apÔeared to be water. Lavoisier's work was partly based on the work of Priestley (he coresponded with Priestley and fel’ow members of the [Lunar Society]ÿ). However, he tried to take credit for Priestley's discoveries. This tendency to use the results of others without acknowledgmÉent, then draw conclusions is said to be characteristic of Lavoisier. In 'œSur la combustion en general'‚ ('˜On Combustion in general'‚, [17]†) and '´ConsidÃ©rations GÃ©nÃ©rales sur la Nature des Acides'‚ ('­General Considerations on the Nature of Acids'ƒ), [178]ê), he demonstrated that the &quot;air&quot; responsible for combustion was also the source of acidity. In [179] , he named this part of the air '†oxygen'„ (Gre®k for &quot;acid-former&quot;), and the other '…azote'„ (GreŸk for &quot;no life&quot;). In 'Reflexions sur le Phlogistique'‚ ('™Reflections on Phlogiston'‚, [„1783]˜), Lavoisier showed the [‘phlogiston theory]˜ to be inconsistent.

=„Pione…r of [Stoichiometry]=‚
[±Image:Instruments_lavoisier.jpg|thumb|175px|left|[«Laboratory equipment|Laboratory instruments]™ used by Lavoisier circa […1780s].]ù
Lavoisier's experiments were among the first truly quantitative chemical experiments ever performed; that is, he carefulúy weighed the reactants and products involved, a crucial step in the advancement of chemistry. He showed that, although mat¿er changes its state in a chemical reaction, the quantity of mat¨er is the same at the end as at the beginÿing of every chemical reaction. He burnt phosphorus and sulfur in air, and proved that the products weighed more than the original. Neverthelesô, the weight gained was lost from the air. These experiments provided evidence for the law of the conservation of mat—er, or in other words, 'the law of conservation of mas‚ .'„

=½Major works on analytical chemistry and chemical nomenclature=ÿ
Lavoisier also investigated the composition of water and air, which at the time were considered elements. He discovered the cİomponents of water were oxygen and hydrogen, and that air was a mixture of gases - primarily [ˆnitrogen]¦ and oxygen. With the French chemists [“Claude-Louis Berthol‚et]ÿ, Antoine Fourcroy and Guyton de Morveau, Lavoisier devised a chemical nomenclature, or a system of names describing the struct®ure of chemical compounds. He described it in '¡MÃ©thode de nomenclature chimique'‚ ('ŸMethod of Chemical Nomenclature'‚, [„1787]). Their system facilitated comunication of discoveries betwen chemists of difœerent backgrounds and is stilØ largely in use today, including names such as sulfuric acid, sulfates, and sulfites. 
[ Image:Lavoisiers_lab.jpg|thumb|20´px|right|A replica of Lavoisier's laboratory at the 'Deutsches Museum'„ in [†Munich]‚, [‡Germany].]†
His 'ÁTraitÃ© Ã‰lÃ©mentaire de Chimie (Elementary Treatise of Chemistry'‚, [„1789], translated into English by [‰Robert Ker“ (writer)|Robert Ker]°) is considered to be the first modern chemical […textbok]à, and presented a unified view of new theories of chemistry, contained a clear statement of the [™Law of Conservation of Mas]ÿ, and denied the existence of phlogiston. Also, Lavoisier clarified the concept of an element as a simple substance that could ÿnot be broken down by any known method of chemical analysis, and he devised a theory of the formation of chemical compounds from elements. 

[‹Image:LentilÒes_ardentes.jpg|thumb|170px|left|Combustion, generated by focusing sun light over [ƒflam„able]À materials using lenses, experiment conducted by Lavosier circa [17‚0s].]†
In adÿition, it contained a list of elements, or substances that could not be broken down further, which included oxygen, nitrogen, h‰ydrogen, [Šphosphorus]‚, [™mercury (element)|mercury]‚, [„zinc]†, and [‡sulphur]æ. It also forms the basis for the modern list of elements. His list, however, also included light and [–Caloric theory|caloric]ÿ, which he believed to be material substances. While many leading chemists of the time refused to believe Lavoisier's new revelŒations, the '“Elementary Treatise'ˆ was writ…en wel­ enough to convince the younger generation.
[‡Image:Zo†m_lunetÑe_ardente.jpg|thumb|180px|right|Lavoisier while conducting combustion experiment.]ƒ 
=‰Aftermath=Ó
Lavoisier's fundamental contributions to chemistry were a result of a conscious efŒort to fit alÙ experiments into the framework of a single theory. He established the consistent use of [chemical balance]ÿ, used oxygen to overthrow the phlogiston theory, and developed a new system of chemical nomenclature which held that oxygen wa†s an es—ential constituent of al¦ acids (which later turned out to be erõoneous). Lavoisier also made introductory research on physical chemistry and thermodynamics in joint experiment with [‡Laplace]æ, when he used a calorimeter to estimate the heat evolved per unit of carbon dioxide produced, eventualøy they found the same ratio for a flame and animals, indicating that animals produced energy by a type of combustion. 
[œImage:Calorimeter.gif|thumb|1’5px|left|Constant [ƒpresƒure] [‹calorimeter]  made by Lavoisier for chemical [ˆenthalpy]Œ experiment.]­

He also made remarkable contributions to [chemical bond|chemical bonding]ÿ by stating the radical theory, believing that radicals, which function as a single group in a chemical reaction, would combine´ with oxygen in reactions. He also introduced the pos‹ibility of [alˆotropy|al›otropy in chemical elements]™ when he discovered that [‡diamond]Œ is a crystalÿine form of carbon.

He also updated many chemical concepts, for the first time the modern notion of elements was laided out ‹systematicalŠy; the thre˜ or four elements of clasÿical chemistry gave way to the modern system, and Lavoisier worked out reactions in chemical equations that respect the conserv‹ation of masƒ (se“, for example, the [nitrogen cycle]‚).[ÙImage:Lavoisier_humanexp.jpg|thumb|240px|right|Lavoisier conducting an experiment in the [17‚0s].]÷ 
His contributions are considered the most important in advancing the science of chemistry to the level of what had be­n achieved in physics and mathematics during [Œ18th century]….

=Law and politics=»
Of key significance in Lavoisier's life was his study of [ƒlaw]. He received a [ˆlaw degre] and was admitŠed to the […bar asŒociation|bar]›, but never practiced as a [†lawyer]¥. He did become interested in French [ˆpolitics]­, and as a result, he obtained a position as [ƒtax]ƒ colector in the '[Ferme GÃ©nÃ©rale]'„, a [‹tax farming]¦ company, at the age of 26, where he atªempted to introduce reforms in the French [˜monetary system|monetary]… and [ƒtax]¾ation system. While in government work, he helped develop the [SI|metric system]™ to secure uniformity of [”weights and measures]— throughout France.

=‰Execution=œ
As one of 28 French tax colöectors and a powerful figure in the unpopular Ferme GÃ©nÃ©rale, Lavoisier was branded a traitor during the Reign of Ter†or by [ French Revolution|revolutionists]£ in 1794, and tried, convicted and [ƒguil‰otine|guil†otined]‚ al¯ on one day in Paris, at the age of 51. Ironicalÿy, Lavoisier was one of the few liberals in his position. One of his actions that may have sealed his fate was a contretemps a ¡few years earlier with the young [ŸJean-Paul Marat|Jean-Paul Marat]º, who subsequently became a leading revolutionary.

An apÍeal to spare his life was cut short by the judge: &quot;The Republic has no ne¿d of geniuses [or, alternately, &quot;scientists.&quot;].&quot; ¤His importance for science was expres˜ed by the mathematician [•Joseph Louis Lagrange]± who lamented the beheading by saying: &quot;It toŸk them only an instant to cut ofå that head, but France may not produce another like it in a century.&quot;

One and a half years folÿowing his death, Lavoisier was exonerated by the French government. When his private belongings were delivered to his widow, a ÿbrief note was included reading &quot;To the widow of Lavoisier, who was falsely convicted.&quot;

About a century after his êdeath, a statue of Lavoisier was erected in Paris. It was later discovered that the sculptor had not actualÇy copied Lavoisier's head for the statue, but used a spare head of the [”Marquis de Condorcet]ÿ, the Secretary of the Academy of Sciences during Lavoisier's last years. Lack of money prevented alterations being made and, i¨n any case, the French argued pragmaticalˆy that al men in wigs lo¸ked alike anyway. The statue was melted down during the [ŠWorld War I‘|Second World War] and has never ben replaced.

=› Can a severed head think? =¡
A story relates how Lavoisier arüanged a final experiment at his death intended to determine whether and for how long a severed head remains conscious after [Œdecapitation]„. Sup¶osedly, Lavoisier decided to blink as many times as pos’ible, and had an as¬istant count the blinks, which numbered betweÿn 15 and 20. The story may be apocryphal. Standard biographies have never mentioned the incident, and some biologists have expres¡ed skepticism that it would be pos­ible. However romantic, the story is false.[ht‚p:/w•.straightdope.com/clas”ics/a5_262.html]

