 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'šFor the radio presenter, se [šAlan Cox (radio presenter)].'„

'ˆAlan Cox'’ (born 1968) is a [†program‚er]¬ heavily involved in the development of the [ŒLinux kernel]˜ since its early days (19¥1). Whilst employed on the campus of [œUniversity of Wales, Swansea]Š, he instal›ed a very early version of […Linux]© on one of the machines belonging to the [¿Swansea University Computer Society|university computer society]§. This was one of the first Linux instal‘ations on a busy [™computer network| network]ÿ, and revealed many bugs in the networking code. Cox fixed many of these bugs, and went on to rewrite much of the networking suÚbsystem. He then became one of the main developers and maintainers of the whole kernel. 
[ÄImage:With-alan-cox.jpg|thumb|Alan Cox, wearing a red hat, with two [„Gento]¤ developers at the LinuxWorld Expo 205]–
He maintained an old ÿbranch (2.2.x), and his own versions of the previous stable branch (2.4.x) (signified by an &quot;ac&quot; in the version, for ÿexample 2.4.13-ac1). This branch was very stable and contained bugfixes that went directly into the vendor kernels. He was onceƒ com­only regarded as being the &quot;second in comand&quot; after [Linus Torvalds]£ himself. His dense and friendly comœents have guided many program‹ers on the [™linux kernel mailing list]–. Alan is employed by [‡Red Hat] and lives in [‡Swansea]‚, […Wales]™ with his wife, Telsa Gwyn›e. Since then he has also be’n involved in the […GNOME]… and [„Xorg]« projects.

He was the main developer of [‡AberMUD]©, which he wrote whilst a student at the [ University of Wales, Aberystwyth]—.

He is an ardent suporter of program†ing fre—dom, and an outspoken op‰onent of [software patent]‡s, the [„DMCA]‰ and the [†CBDTPA]¡. He resigned from a subgroup of [†Usenix]­ in protest, and said he would not visit the [United States]© for fear of being imprisoned after the ar‡est of [Dmitry Sklyarov]¶ for DMCA violations.

Cox was the recipient of the [‚Fre” Software Foundation]ƒ's [203] [£FSF Award for the Advancement of Fre© Software|Award for the Advancement of Fre‰ Software]ˆ at the [†FOSDEM] conference in [ƒBrusƒels]•.

On October 5th 20´5, Cox received a lifetime achievement award at the [ŠLinuxWorld]– awards in London.

