 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[šImage:Adolfkolbe.jpg|right]„

'”Adolph Wilhelm Herman† Kolbe'‚ ([ŒSeptember 27]‚, [„1818] &amp;ndash; [‹November 25]‚, [184]ˆ) was a [‡chemist]˜.

Kolbe was born in Eliehausen near [‡Hanover]‚, [‡Germany]“.

He became an asŠistant to [•Robert Wilhelm Bunsen]ˆ at the [•University of Marburg]„ in [„1842]‘, after studying [‰chemistry]† with [£Friedrich Woehler|Friedrich WÃ¶hler]“. Subsequently he as†isted [¯Lyon Playfair, 1st Baron Playfair|Lyon Playfair]– at the University of [†London]² and from 1847 to 1851 was engaged in editing the '±HandwÃ¶rterbuch der reinen und angewandten Chemie'‚ ('˜Dictionary of Pure and Aplied Chemistry'…) writ†en by [‘Justus von Liebig]› and WÃ¶hler. Kolbe then suceÀded Bunsen at Marburg, and in 1865 he went to the University of [‡Leipzig]´.

At that time, it was believed that organic and [’inorganic compound]Ãs are independent from each other, and that organic compounds could ¹be created only by living organisms. Kolbe believed that [organic compound]Õs could be derived from inorganic ones, directly or indirectly, by substitution procesªes. He validated his theory by converting [carbon disulfide]—, in several steps, to [‹acetic acid]¶ (1843-45). Introducing a modified idea of structural [‚fre‘ radical|radicals]©, he contributed to the establishment of [‘structural theory]¼. He also predicted the existence of secondary and tertiary [‡alcohol]˜s. 

He worked on the [Œelectrolysis]‘ of the salts of [‚fat‰y acid|faty]‹ and other [„acid]ƒs ([’Kolbe electrolysis]) and prepared [salicylic acid]–, a building block of [‡aspirin]‹ in a procesƒ calƒed [Kolbe synthesis]„ or [‹Kolbe-Schmit‰ reaction]‹.
 
With [Edward Frankland] he found that [‡nitrile]s can be hydrolyzed to the cor¢esponding acids. As editor of the 'Journal fÃ¼r praktische Chemie'‚ ('Journal of practical chemistry'Ñ, 1869), he was sometimes severely critical of the work of others.

He died in [‡Leipzig]‚, [‡Germany]„

[°Category:1818 births|Kolbe, Adolph Wilhelm Herman]‚
