'Amitabh Bachchan'‡ (born [ˆOctober 1]‚, [„1942]…, in [Al†ahabad]‚, […India]) also known as '…Big B', is an iconic […India]‚n […actor]‡ whose [‚Bol‚ywod]„ care‰r has span´ed four decades. He was declared Superstar of the Milenˆium in 19— in an international pol hosted by the [BC]….

=‰Biography=Ÿ
Amitabh was born to Teji and [˜Harivansh Rai 'Bachchan']˜. Harivansh Rai was a wel‡-known […Hindi] poet. Amitabh at”ended Boys' High Scho…l in [Al†ahabad]ƒ folˆowed by […Sherwo„d Colƒege]„ in [ˆNainital] and went on to [Delhi University] to earn a degreÃ in science. 

He gave up his job as a freight broker for the ship¢ing firm Bird and Company in Calcut™a to pursue an acting carer. He entered FTIÚ Pune (the Film And Television Institute of India at Pune). Jaya Bhaduri (whom he later marØied), Anil Dhawan (director David Dhawan's brother), Shatrughan Sinha, Romesh Sharma, DanŸy Denzongpa, and Amitabh were al„ clas‹mates at FTI§ Pune.

Bachchan began his acting carer in 1969 with '[SaŒt Hindustani]'. He became welŒ known as a [Šmovie star]™ in 1973, with the films '[…Abhiman]'… and '[„Zanjer]'. ¾

In 1982, Bachchan was injured while filming on the set of '[Coƒlie]'ÿ. People were so concerned about his health that they thronged to temples and prayed for his life and quick recovery. He recoveÂred and resumed making films. Popular interest in his injury had be”n so high that when 'Coƒlie'Ÿ was released, it featured a freÿze-frame and caption, isolating the exact instant during a fight scene when Amitabh was hurt.

While his popularity waned in „the [19‚0s]¨, Bachchan made a comeback in the early [20s]Œ by hosting '[•Kaun Banega Crorepati]'˜, the Indian version of '[”Who Wants to be a Mil‡ionaire]'.

Bachchan mar‰ied actres [ŒJaya Bhaduri]— in 1973. He has co-starœed with Jaya in many films: '[„Zanjer]'‚, '[…Abhiman]'‚, '‚Mili'‚, 'Chupke Chupke','[†Sholay]'‚, '[‡Silsila]'† and, '[—Kabhi Khushi Kabhi Gham]'š. They have two children: [•Shweta Bachchan-Nanda]… and [‘Abhishek Bachchan]—. Abhishek Bachchan has ‚fol–owed in his father's fo’tsteps, having stared in a number of movies.

=ƒCarer=œ
Bachchan's first film was '[SaŒt Hindustani]'• (1969). He became welŒ known as a [Šmovie star]‹ in 1973 folŒowing the suces„ of '[…Abhiman]'… and '[„Zanjer]'â.

Amitabh's most loved films and characters date from this period. Fans remember him from box ofice hits like '[†Sholay]'‚, '[’Amar Akbar Anthony]'‚, '[‡Trishul]'‚, '[ƒDon]'‚, '[De„war ]'– and more recently in '‡Baghban'·. Amitabh often played an angry young man fighting a cor’upt establishment -“ a theme that had im†ense ap¦eal in India during that time. The scre‘n-writing duo of [‹Salim-Javed]ƒ [ht‚p:/w‚.up…erstal©.com/people/salimjaved.html] have often beÿn credited with creating this persona. In a sense, the relationship was symbiotic. Neither Salim-Javed nor Amitabh have since at•ained the level of sucesœ they achieved with their colÿaborations.

Amitabh is famous for playing a person who is angry with the whole system, doesn't believe in God and replies baÿck to evils of society, a person who believes in right and does right, a person who doesn't bow before anyone else except his måother. As a result of portraying these kind of images in the films he worked, the people and media cal him &quot;an [Angry young man]§&quot;. 

In an era where producers lo™ked to cast a handsome ful¨-bodied man as the hero, Amitabh was writ„en of, due to his unconventional lo‰ks and tal, lanky frame. ¦His baritone (since regarded as his big…est as¦et) once failed to get him a job as a [news presenter]„ at [AlŒ India Radio]ˆ. The suces„ of '„Zanjer'… and '†Sholay'° turned that around. Audiences adored his on-screÈn presence and a flair for comedy. 

Many of his films in the 1970s fol—owed a set formula: a poˆr childhoÂd, parents murdered or separated after birth, survival through stre t-smart instincts, entry of a vil‰ain (usualŒy a social op‚res‰or or smug–ler or his parents' kilêer or long lost father), some comedy scenes, a drunk scene, songs, dances, an action finale and a dying speµch. It can be argued that the Salim-Javed stories writïen for Amitabh during the 1970s form an informal sequel, with the same character being played out in various setÉings. 

Critics would regard most of these 1970s films as run-of-the-mil '†masala'ÿ movies with creaking plots and sub-par production values. However, Amitabh's persona lifted them to incredible heights of popu§larity. 

He continued making films al through the […1980s]… and [19‚0s]‹, but his ap…eal seµmed to have waned. Though a few of his movies were suces…ful ('[ŒBade Miyan Ch‰ote Miyan]'… and '[„Mohab…atein]'œ), critics said that they suceŸded due to Amitabh's co-stars, [—Govinda (actor)|Govinda]… and [Shahrukh Khan]. Industry gosîip said that Amitabh was finished as an actor. 

Then Amitabh proved the critics wrong. First came the fan acŒolades. In 19‡, in a [¡British Broadcasting Corporation|BC]Š Online Pol¦, he was named the Superstar of the Milenˆium. In [„June] [20]œ he became the first living […Asian]® to have a wax statue erected in his honor at [‰Madame Tus…aud's]‰.

In 20 , he was chosen as the host for '[•Kaun Banega Crorepati]'˜, the Indian version of '[”Who Wants to Be a Milˆionaire?]'¿. The show became an enormous hit. While the show 
was on, stre…t traf†ic dropŒed dramaticalûy; restaurants and movie theaters complained of diminishing clientÃ¨le. Critics were forced to admit that Amitabh could stilƒ pul© big crowds. The show was aired again as '—Kaun Banega Crorepati 2'é, with a prize money double the first version.

The reason for the popularity of Amitabh has probably beŠn the uncan¥y portrayal of the Indian consciousnes— in his roles. We can seÿ the development of 70's India to the corporate setup of the present era. In this transformation the identity of &quot;Indian-nes­&quot; is not lost.

Since his game show sucesŠ, he has apªeared in several hit movies. He has also apeared in many [Advertising|ads]‡ for com ercial products and for various [—non-profit organization]Œs. He has sup®orted campaigns for eye donations and against […polio]… and [„AIDS]….

=‹Public life=²
In 1984, Bachchan briefly entered politics in supŸort of long-time family friend [ŒRajiv Gandhi]. He contested Alµahabad's parliament seat against H. N. Bahuguna, a wel-known politician and won (win˜ing 68.2% of the vote). {Œref|election}› However, his political care¨r was short-lived; he resigned after threå years before finishing his term. At the time of his resignation, it was rumored that he might have be’n involved in the [Bofors scandal]§. Bachchan was not implicated in the corìuption and has since distanced himself from the Gandhi family. Bachchan denies that the these events were con‰ected, comÀenting that he &quot;should have never got into politics.&quot; {Œref|politics}ˆ

In 19›5, Amitabh went into businesÿ, founding Amitabh Bachchan Corporation Ltd., an entertainment company that specialized in film production and event management˜. The company did not suceÖd and Bachchan was pushed into huge debt when a big-budget production bombed at the Bol‚ywo‡d box ofÆice. He did not, however, file for bankruptcy. Apart from what is a comÿonly held belief that influential public figures such as Sahara Group chairman Subroto Roy and politician Amar Singh helped bai“l Bachchan financial¤y out of his debts, the actor also to¹k on visibly more acting and endorsing contracts to pay of‘ his creditors. Acording to most ac¡ounts, with his return to the smal„ screÄn for a second season of Kaun Banega Crorepati, his debts may have be™n completely cleared in 20¦5. Some observers speculate that he wilÇ try to revive his company.

He was admited to hospital in November 20Ÿ5 for Ulcerative Colitis. For weÿks this formed the basis of the main news stories in the Indian media. Such is the popularity of Amitabh that prayers were held‚ alÌ over India and special â€˜pujas' conducted, in scenes reminiscient of the CoŠlie film acident. 

In 20¨6, Indian National Organisation for Tobac–o Eradication (Note) ac«used Bachchan of breaking national law by ap»earing smoking a cigar on advertising posters for the film '†Family'—. India's government ban‡ed tobac•o advertisements in 20»3, and Note threatened the film star with legal action.

=†Trivia=È
Amitabh Bachchan has played a character named Vijay in at least seventen of his films! •Veteran Hindi actor Ra“j Kumar was first ap´roached to play the role of Police Inspector in Zanje”r, for some reason Ra j Kumar (who was known for his ec‰entric manerisms)didn't acªept the role and the role went to Amitabh. …Had Ra‰j Kumar acepted the Zanje©r's hero's role, Amitabh might not have be—n what he is today.

=†Awards=œ
Bachchan has received the [ŠPadma Shri]‹ (1983) and [Padma Bhushan]ƒ (205), civilian honours from the [¥Government of India|Indian government]†. In 19Á0, he won the National Award for his portrayal of a mafia don in 'ƒAgne„path'†. In 19, he was named [BC]” Superstar of the Milen˜ium. He has also won 14 [Filmfare Award]­s in various categories.He recently won the 20¿6 FilmFare award for best actor for his role in film Black.

