In [‹linguistics]„ an 'al†omorph'˜ is a variant form of a [ˆmorpheme]Ñ. The meaning remains the same, while the sound can vary.

For example, in the [English language]  the past tense morpheme is -ed. „It ocurs in several al´omorphs depending on its phonological environment, as¹imilating voicing of the previous segment or inserting a […schwa]ˆ when fol owing an alveolar stop:

* as {‰IPA|/É™d/}  in 'hunted' or 'banded',
* as {‡IPA|/d/}‡ in 'buzŒed', 
* as {‡IPA|/t/}‘ in 'fished'

Al¶omorphy can also exist in case distinctions, as in Clasƒic [ˆSanskrit]‹:

