 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ªThis article is about the sense of &quot;al¡ophone&quot; used in linguistics. “For other senses, se [al—ophone (disambiguation)].'‡

In [‰phonetics]…, an 'al†ophone'› is one of several similar […phone]šs that belong to the same [‡phoneme]ÿ. A phone is a sound that has a definite shape as a sound wave, while a phoneme is a basic group of sounds that can distinguishÿ words (i.e. changing one phoneme in a word can produce another word); speakers of a particular language perceive a phoneme as ¬a single distinctive sound in that language. ‰Thus an al¸ophone is a phone considered as a member of one phoneme. Š

Each alÓophone is used in a specific phonetic context and many times there is some sort of [–phonology|phonological]† proces. …Not alŸ phonemes have significantly dif‡erent al¦ophones, but there are always minor dif¬erences in articulation from one piece of spech to the next. ‘

For example, {IPA|[p&amp;#68‚;]}‡ as in 'ƒpin'… and {‡IPA|[p]}‡ as in 'ƒcap'† are al£ophones for the phoneme /p/ in the [English language] because they oc†ur in [šcomplementary distribution]™. English speakers general°y treat these as the same sound, but they are dif†erent. †The lat†er is [‹unaspirated]‰ (plain): ‰if plain {‡IPA|[p]}« is also found in words such as the second 'p'„ in '…paper' {IPA|[p&amp;#68‰;eÉª.pÉš]}„ or '„spin' {‹IPA|[spÉªn]}¡. Outside of contexts that plain 'p'‚ ap©ears in English, speakers may hear it as 'b' since English 'b'Š is typical“y unaspirated. 

[˜Chinese language|Chinese]› treats these two phones dif’erently and the later is always writ†en as 'b'„ in [†pinyin]–; thus, they are not alÔophones in Chinese.

Similarly, English-speaking people may become aware of the dif‹erence betwe‡n two al—ophones of the phoneme 't' ¯when they consider the pronunciations of the fol«owing phrases:

:night rate
:nitrate

