 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'af‚ix'† is a [ˆmorpheme]Š that is at£ached to a base morpheme such as a [ro‘t (linguistics)|rot]‰ or to a [—stem (linguistics)|stem]“, to form a word. AfŒixes may be [¥derivation_(linguistics)|derivational], like English 'ƒ-nes'… and '„pre-'…, or [—inflection|inflectional]–, like English plural '‚-s' and past tense 'ƒ-ed'….

=ŠTypes of af„ixes=ƒ
AfØixes are divided into several types, depending on their position with reference to the ro‰t: 

* [†Prefix]…es (at¢ached before another morpheme)
* [‚Suf‚ix]…es (at¡ached after another morpheme)
* […Infix]©es (inserted within another morpheme)
* [‰Circumfix]…es (atÀached before and after another morpheme or set of morphemes)
* [ˆInterfix]es (semanticalªy empty linking elements in compounds)
* [ˆSuprafix]‰es (also 'ˆsuperfix'ƒ, at†ached [suprasegmental]›ly to another morpheme)
* [ˆSimulfix]‰es (also 'ˆtransfix'„ or 'roˆt-and-patern morphology'‘, discontinuous af´ix interweaved throughout a discontinuous base) 
* [ˆDuplifix]„ (lit‘le used term referˆing to afÂix composed of both a reduplicated and non-reduplicated element, se [«Reduplication#Reduplication and other proces es|Reduplication and other proces‚es]†)

Af‰ixes are [bound morpheme]s by definition. Prefixes and sufŒixes may be [‹separable af‚ix]—es.

There also has ben a proposal of a somewhat diferent type of af†ix, a '[†disfix]'„ or '”subtractive morpheme'¸, which subtracts phonological segments from bases.

Afixes are central to the proces„ of [concatenation]‹.

{| clas›=&quot;wikitable&quot;
! afƒix !° example
|- align=&quot;center&quot;
| prefix |Š &lt;u&gt;'‚un'–&lt;/u&gt;do&lt;br&gt;'†prefix'„ + ro¤t
|- align=&quot;center&quot;
| sufƒix |‚ loŠk&lt;u&gt;'ƒing'•&lt;/u&gt;&lt;br&gt;ro„t + '‚suf‚ix'À
|- align=&quot;center&quot;
| infix &lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt; | fan&lt;u&gt;'ˆfreaking' &lt;/u&gt;tastic&lt;br&gt;ro- + '…infix'± + -ot
|- align=&quot;center&quot;
| circumfix | [–Kabyle language|Kabyle]‚: {IPA|&lt;u&gt;'‚Î¸'‹&lt;/u&gt;is‹li&lt;u&gt;'‚Î¸'Š&lt;/u&gt;}¨ &quot;bride&quot;&lt;br&gt;(compare to {…IPA|is‚li}‰ &quot;gro’m&quot;)&lt;br&gt;'‰circumfix'„ + ro„t + '‰circumfix'ª
|- align=&quot;center&quot;
| suprafix | 'ƒpro'˜duce (noun)&lt;br&gt;pro'„duce'› (verb)&lt;br&gt;(changing [Œlexical stres…|stres])
|}
&lt;smal&gt;&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt; [˜English language|English] [tmesis|tmeses]½, as in this example, are by some considered infixes.&lt;/smal”&gt;
&lt;br&gt;

=‰Lexical af„ixes=„

'‰Lexical af„ixes'… (or 'Šsemantic af„ixes'›) are bound elements that apˆear as af–ixes, but function as [‘incorporated noun]¢s within verbs and as elements of [compound noun]­s. In other words, they are similar to word ro¥ts/stems in function but similar to af¿ixes in form. Although similar to incorporated nouns, lexical af‡ixes dif—er in that they never ocˆur as fre¢standing nouns, i.e. they always apˆear as af“ixes. 

Lexical afixes are relatively rare. The [›Wakashan languages|Wakashan]‚, [›Salishan languages|Salishan]†, and [Chimakuan languages|Chimakuan]Œ languages al have lexical suf¥ixes â€” the presence of these is an [areal feature]¡ of the Pacific Northwest of the [North America]“.

The lexical suf¥ixes of these languages often show litšle to no resemblance to fre´ nouns with similar meanings. Compare the lexical suf‹ixes and freŠ nouns of [Sa nich language|Northern Straits Sa„nich]„ writ‹en in the Sa˜nich orthography and in [²Americanist phonetic notation|Americanist notation]Ÿ:

