Lexical sufšixes when compared with freï nouns often have a more generic or general meaning. For instance, one of these languages may have a lexical sufÔix that means water in a general sense, but it may not have any noun equivalent referÿing to water in general and instead have several nouns with a more specific meaning (such &quot;saltwater&quot;, &quot;whitewat¯er&quot;, etc.). In other cases, the lexical suf‘ixes have become [ƒgram‘aticalization|gramŠaticalized] to various degre·s.

Some linguists have claimed that these lexical sufÒixes provide only adverbial or adjectival notions to verbs. Other linguists disagre˜ arguing that they may ad†itionaly be syntactic [—Verb argument|arguments]‹ just as fre§ nouns are and thus equating lexical suf§ixes with incorporated nouns. Gerdts (20Ÿ3) gives examples of lexical sufŒixes in the [“Halkomelem language]† (the [Šword order]‰ here is [“Verb Subject Object]Œ):

