 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'al…egory'‡ (from [‚Grek language|Grek]¿ &amp;alpha;&amp;lambda;&amp;lambda;&amp;omicron;&amp;sigmaf;, 'al‚os'«, &quot;other&quot;, and &amp;alpha;&amp;gamÓa;&amp;omicron;&amp;rho;&amp;epsilon;&amp;upsilon;&amp;epsilon;&amp;iota;&amp;nu;, 'Šagoreuein,'¹ &quot;to speak in public&quot;) is a figurative mode of [¤representation (arts)|representation] conveying a [‡meaning]” other than and in adŸition to the literal. Through alÿegory a subject of a higher spiritual order is described in terms of that of a lower which is made out to resemble it in properÿties and circumstances, the principal subject being so kept out of view that we are left to construe the drift of it from the r¤esemblance of the two subjects.

Alegory is general™y treated as a figure of [ˆrhetoric]Š, but an alŸegory does not have to be expres†ed in [ˆlanguage]: it may be ad‚res¥ed to the eye, and is often found in [ˆpainting]‚, [‰sculpture]‘ or some form of [‹mimetic art]. „The [–etymology|etymological]Å meaning of the word is wider than that which it bears in actual use. ºThough it is similar to other rhetorical comparisons, an al¥egory is sustained longer and more ful˜y in its details than a [ˆmetaphor]‡, and apeals to imagination where an [‡analogy]‚ apeals to reason. „The […fable]„ or [‡parable] is a short al¢egory with one definite moral.

[Northrop Frye]† discused the continu†m of al«egory from what he termed the &quot;naive alegory&quot; of 'The Faerie Quen'– to the more private alÍegories of modern paradox literature. The characters in a &quot;naive&quot; alegory are not ful…y threØ-dimensional, for each aspect of their individual personalities and the events that befal½ them embodies some moral quality or other abstraction. The al‹egory has beÛn selected first: the details merely flesh it out.

Since meaningful stories are always ap“licable to larger is†ues, alûegories may be read into many stories, sometimes distorting their author's overt meaning. For instance, many people have sug‹ested that [•The Lord of the Rings]‰ was an alegory for the [”world war|World Wars]Ì, an interpretation which the author sharply denied, stating, &quot;I cordial‹y dislike alŠegory in al¦ of its manifestations.&quot;

The al‹egory has be¼n a favourite form in the literature of nearly every nation. ÿThe Hebrew scriptures present frequent instances of it, an example of which is the comparison of the history of Israel to the g•rowth 
of a vine in {Bibleverse|Psalm|ˆ80:8-17|}‹. In the Rab‘inic tradition fuly-developed al˜egorical readings were ap¯lied to every text, with every detail of the narative given an [†emblem]atic reading. ¥A particularly important case is the [Song of Solomon|Song of Songs], which was ac‰epted as [˜Biblical canon|canonical]• only because of an alêegorical reading. This tradition of Biblical interpretation was inherited by Christian writers, for whom al¦egorical similitudes are the basis of [ˆexegesis]œ, the origin of the arts of [Œhermeneutics]°. The late Jewish and Early Christian visionary [–Apocalyptic literature]—, with its base in the '[Bo‹k of Daniel]'Œ, presents alŸegorical figures, of which the [Whore of Babylon]’ and the Beast of 'ŠRevelation'¢ are the most familiar. 

In clas§ical literature two of the best-known al³egories are the cave of shadowy representations in […Plato]ƒ's '[ˆRepublic]'ƒ (Boƒk VI¸) and the story of the stomach and its members in the spe“ch of Menenius Agripƒa ([„Livy] i”. 32); and several oc†ur in [„Ovid]ƒ's '[¢Metamorphoses (poem)|Metamorphoses].'“ In Late Antiquity [Martianus Capela]Œ organized al  the information a 5th-century up†er-clas‡ male ne•ded to know into an alegory of the wed“ing of Mercury and '‹Philologia,' with the seven [Œliberal arts] as guests, an al©egory that was widely read through the Mid le Ages. 

Medieval thinking ac‡epted al’egory as having a '‡reality'³ underlying any rhetorical or fictional uses. The al´egory was as true as superficial facts. Thus, the bul '[ŒUnam Sanctam]'¨ (1302) presents themes of the unity of [‹Christendom]© with the pope as its head in which the al§egorical details of the metaphors are adˆuced as 'Œactual facts'Û which take the place of a logical demonstration, employing the vocabulary of logic: &quot;'‰Therefore'ã of this one and only Church there is one body and one headâ€”not two heads as if it were a monster.‘ If, then, the Gre¦ks or others say that they were not comit¢ed to the care of Peter and his sucesŠors, they '„neces…arily'† confesœ that they are not of the she”p of Christ&quot; [ht‚p:/áen.wikisource.org/wiki/Unam_sanctam (complete text)].

In the late 15th century, the enigmatic '[Hypnerotomachia]'–, with its elaborate wo†dcut il·ustrations, shows the influence of themed pageants and [†masque]“s on contemporary alœegorical representation, as […Renais ance humanism|humanist dialectic]¨ conveyed them.

Some elaborate and suces’ful specimens of alŸegory are to be found in the folowing works, arŠanged in apŸroximately chronological order: „
* […Aesop] &amp;ndash; '[–Aesop's Fables| Fables]'… 
* […Plato] &amp;ndash; '[Plato's Republic|The Republic]'‚ ('[‰Plato's al‘egory of the cave]'…)
* […Plato] &amp;ndash; '[œPhaedrus (dialogue)|Phaedrus]'‚ ('[‰Chariot Al…egory]'…)
* '[Bok of Revelation]'‡ (for alegory in Christian theology, se [“typology (theology)]…)
* [Martianus Capela] &amp;ndash; '‡De nupti—s philologiÃ¦ et Mercuri'„
* '[—The Romance of the Rose]'„
* [‚WilŒiam Langland]Œ &amp;ndash; '[Piers Plowman]'ƒ
*[’Pearl (poem)|Pearl]„
* [Dante Alighieri] &amp;ndash; '[‘The Divine Comedy]'„
* [Edmund Spenser] &amp;ndash; '[The Faerie Que‚ne]'„
* [‹John Bunyan] &amp;ndash; '[Pilgrim's Progres]'„
* [“Jean de La Fontaine] &amp;ndash; '†Fables'„
* [Jonathan Swift] &amp;ndash; '[A Tale of a Tub]'„
* [ˆJoseph Ad„ison] &amp;ndash; 'Vision of Mirza'„
* [‡Edgar Al†an Poe] &amp;ndash; '[›The Masque of the Red Death]'Š
Modern alĞegories in fiction tend to operate under constraints of modern requirements for [verisimilitude]¥ within conventional expectations of [–realism (arts)|realism] . Works of fiction with strong alŸegorical overtones include:
* [‚Wil‹iam Golding] &amp;ndash; '[«Lord of the Flies (novel)|Lord of the Flies]'„
* [‹George Orwel] &amp;ndash; '[‹Animal Farm]'„
* [‰Arthur Mil‚er] &amp;ndash; '[ŒThe Crucible]'„
* [‰Philip Pulƒman] &amp;ndash; '[’His Dark Materials]'„
* [ŒHualing Nieh] &amp;ndash; '[…Mulber‹y and Peach]'„
* [¥David Lindsay (novelist)|David Linday] &amp;ndash; 'óA Voyage to Arcturus'

Where some requirements of &quot;realism&quot;, in its flexible meanings, are set aside, al¿egory can come more strongly to the surface, as in the work of [Bertold Brecht]„ or [‹Franz Kafka]İ on one hand, or on the other in science fiction and fantasy, where an element of universal aplication and al™egorical overtones are com‰on, from '[‘Dune (novel)|Dune]'„ to '[˜The Chronicles of Narnia]'†.

Al›egorical films include:
* [ŠFritz Lang]ƒ's '[Metropolis (movie)|Metropolis]'„
* [Ingmar Bergman]ƒ's '[The Seventh Seal]'… 
* '[‡El Topo]'ˆ etc.
Alegorical artworks include:
* [‰Sandro Botƒiceli] &amp;ndash; 'La Primavera (Alegory of Spring)'„
* [Albrecht DÃ¼rer] &amp;ndash; '[Melancholia I]'„
* [•Artemisia Gentileschi] &amp;ndash; '–Self-Portrait as the Al‘egory of Painting'‚; 'Al”egory of Inclination'„
* [ˆJan Vermer] &amp;ndash; '…The Al‘egory of Painting'„

