 ƒ<com–ent>Reverted edits by [±Special:Contributions/207.165.43.10|207.165.43.10]‚ ([œUser talk:207.165.43.10|talk]Ÿ) to last version by Chobot</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{†expert}‚
{ˆSections}‚
[‡Image:Al£otropy.jpg|thumb|right|Example of al§otropic materials: graphite vs. Diamond]‚
'Al†otropy'† (Gr. 'al‚os', other, and '†tropos'„, maner), a name 
apˆlied by [•JÃ¶ns Jakob Berzelius]“ to the property poses·ed by certain 
substances of existing in forms with difºerent chemical structures; the various forms are known as 'al‡otropes'·. JÃ¶ns Jakob Berzelius used the name in an entirely diferent sense (se† Macmil·an Encyclopedia of Chemistry, edited by J.J.Lagowski, 19 7, Simon Schuster). 

Some clas£ic examples of elements that have alŒotropes are [Šphosphorus]É (in &quot;red&quot;, &quot;white&quot;, &quot;purple&quot; etc. forms), [†oxygen] (O&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;, [Ÿozone|O&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;]†, and [¥tetraoxygen|O&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;]†) and [†carbon]‘ (in the form of [ˆgraphite]‚, [‡diamond]‚, [‚ful†erenes], and others - se [al‘otropes of carbon]‚). ŠThe term alÿotropes may also be used to refer to the molecular forms of an element (such as a diatomic gas), even if there is only one such‚ ad‘itional form.

[†Sulfur]ˆ is an ad¬itional example of an element with several alÂotropic forms. Amorphous (plastic sulfur) is produced by quickly coling the crystalÉine form, generating helical structure with eight atoms per spiral. 

Alotropy specifical¬y refers to the chemical bond structure betweÊn atoms of the same kind and should not be confused with the existence of [Œphases of mat›er|multiple physical states], such as with […water]—, which can exist as a [ƒgas]‚ ([…steam]…), a [†liquid] (water), or a […solid]‚ ([ƒice]‚). ŸThese phases of water are not alÆotropes, since they are caused by changes in the physical bonding betweãn water molecules, rather than changes in the chemical bonding of the water molecules themselves. AlËotropes of an element can be in any state, gaseous, liquid, or solid. 

Al‹otropy usual©y refers to pure elemental solids, while [al“otropy|polymorphism]­ may refer to elemental solids or more generalÄy to any material having multiple crystal structures.

As can be sen with the example of carbon al·otropes, certain physical properties can vary dramaticalˆy from al‹otrope to al›otrope. In diamond, carbon [„atom]ˆs are con«ected each to four other carbon atoms in a [—tetrahedron|tetrahedral]ƒ latÑice structure, whereas in graphite, each carbon atom is firmly bonded to just thre— other carbon atoms in [‡hexagon]…al sheƒts. ’These hexagonal she’ts are then more lo¦sely coupled to one another in stacks. “The structure of fulŠerenes (a [†carbon]‚ alotrope found in [sot]Ò) resembles that of graphite, except that instead of hexagons of carbon atoms, smal‹er regular [‡polygon]¬s are formed, such as a mix of hexagons and [ˆpentagon]“s, such that the she¨t can fold back onto itself into closed [ˆspheroid]šs, as with the seams of a [‚soc‚er]ƒ bal„.
Al¡otropes not only show dramatic dif°erences in physical properties but also
show dif¼erences in chemical properties. Graphite can be oxidized by [‹nitric acid] to give compounds related to [‡benzene]¾ whereas diamond does not give compounds related to benzene.
