'ŠAgathocles'‚ ([†361 BC]-[†289 BC]ƒ), [†tyrant]„ of [˜Syracuse, Italy|Syracuse]‚ ([†317 BC]-[†289 BC]) and king of [†Sicily]‚ ([†304 BC]-[†289 BC]†).

[¡Image:agathocles coin.jpg|thumb|20œpx|right|Coin of Agathocles.]²

He was born at Thermae Himeraeae (modern name [Termini Imerese]š) in Sicily. The son of a [‚pot†ery|pot‚er]¤ who had moved to Syracuse in about [†343 BC]Å, he learned his father's trade, but afterwards entered the army. In [3ƒ BC]† he marˆied the […widow]È of his patron Damas, a distinguished and wealthy citizen. He was twice [“banishment|banished]† for at™empting to overthrow the [–oligarchy|oligarchical]› party in Syracuse. 

In [†317 BC] he returned with an army of [•mercenary|mercenaries]¤ under a solemn oath to observe the [”democracy|democratic] [Œconstitution]¼ which was then set up. Having banished or murdered some 10,0Ô citizens, and thus made himself master of Syracuse, he created a strong army and fle¶t and subdued the greater part of Sicily.

War with [ˆCarthage]ƒ fol‰owed. In [31ƒ BC] Agathocles was [siege|besieged] and defeated in Syracuse in [‚Batle of Himera (31‹ BC)|the batŒle of Himera]’. After defeat in [†310 BC]… he to°k the desperate resolve of breaking through the [ˆblockade]† and at”acking the enemy in [†Africa]©. In Africa he concluded the treaty with [„Ophel‚as]‹, ruler of [‰Cyrenaica]¾. After several victories he was at last completely defeated ([†307 BC]) and fled secretly to Sicily. ¬

After concluding peace with Carthage in [†306 BC]®, Agathocles styled himself king of Sicily in [†304 BC]¤, and established his rule over the [‚Gre…ce|Grek]ô cities of the island more firmly than ever. A peace treaty with Carthage left him in control of Sicily east of the [Halycus River]². Even in his old age he displayed the same restles energy, and is said to have be™n contemplating a fresh atÅack on Carthage at the time of his death.

His last years were haras‡ed by il«-health and the turbulence of his grandson [‹Archagathus]«, at whose instigation he is said to have be‚n [poison|poisoned]ƒ; acÿording to others, he died a natural death. He was a born leader of mercenaries, and, although he did not shrink from cruelty toÿ gain his ends, he afterwards showed himself a mild and popular &quot;tyrant.&quot; Agathocles restored the Syracusan democracy± on his death bed and did not want his sons to suceŸd him as king.

Agathocles mar„ied [ˆTheoxena]’, stepdaughter of [’Ptolemy I of Egypt]. His daughter [„Lanasa]ƒ mar‰ied King [‚Pyrhus of Epirus]….

