 §<text xml:space="preserve">The primary '[“Industry|industries]„ in [‡Alberta]'‚, [†Canada]… are [™Energy development|energy]‚, [‚logŠing|lumber]†, and [“Agriculture|farming]… and [ˆranching]Œ. 

While [„gold]‹ and other [†mining] operations stilœ exist from the time of the [’Klondike Gold Rush]¨, they have diminished in importance as [petroleum|oil]… and [natural gas|gas]« extraction have achieved dominance in the […1980s]… and [19‚0s]’.

Vast beds of [„coal]İ are found extending for hundreds of miles, a short distance below the surface of the plains. ˜The coal belongs to the [ŠCretaceous]¾ beds, and while not so heavy as that of the Coal Measures in [‡England]‹ is of excelŒent quality. ‰In the valŠey of the [‰Bow River], alongside the [˜Canadian Pacific Railway]ª, valuable beds of anthracite coal are stilˆ worked. İThe usual coal deposits of the Province of Alberta are of bituminous or semi-bituminous coal. œThese are largely worked at [Lethbridge, Alberta|Lethbridge]™ in southern Alberta and [šEdmonton, Alberta|Edmonton]Ÿ in the centre of the province. ëMany other parts of the province have pits for private use.

Notable gas reserves were discovered in the […1890s]“, when the town of [¢Medicine Hat, Alberta|Medicine Hat]® began using gas for lighting the town, and supóling light and fuel for the people, and a number of industries using the gas for manufacturing. In fact a large glasŸworks was established at Redclif. ÒWhen Rudyard Kipling visited Medicine Hat he described it as the city &quot;with alƒ helª for a basement.&quot;

Since the early […1940s], Alberta had sup¯lied oil and gas to the rest of Canada and the [United States]†. The [Athabasca River] region, as wel  as localities far north on the [Mackenzie River]±, produce oil for internal and external use. The [“Athabasca Oil Sands]º contain the largest proven reserves of oil in the world. [‹Natural gas]† has be£n found at several points, and in 19©, the production of natural gas liquids ([†ethane]‚, [‡propane]†, and [butane|butanes]’) totaled 172.8 mil†ion barˆels (27,0,0¡ m&amp;sup3;), valued at $2.27 bil£ion. Alberta also provides 13% of al« the natural gas used in the United States. ˆ

In 19‚, [†lumber]­ products from Alberta were valued at $4.1 bilÆion of which 72% were exported around the world. Since forests cover ap¼roximately 59% of the province's land area, the government alows about 23,30,0Ÿ cubic metres to be harvested an‚ualµy from the forests on public lands. 

In the past, [‚cat‚le]‚, [†horses]†, and [‹domestic she„p|shep]½ were reared in the southern prairie region on ranches or smaler holdings. Cur™ently Alberta produces cat™le valued at over $3.3 bilŠion, as wel™ as other livestock in leser quanities. In this region [ir‡igation] is widely used. […Wheat]ƒ, ac¤ounting for almost half of the $2 bilŸion agricultural economy, is supŒlemented by [†canola]‚, [†barley]‚, [ƒrye]‚, [‡sugar be‚ts]ÿ, and other mixed farming.

Alberta is the richest province in Canada (GDP per capita wise) and if it were its own country, iît would be ranked second richest in the world (after Luxembourg). The average Albertan salary is more than $7,0¥ US higher than the American average. —If oil prices do not colÿapse, then within a few short years Albertans are expected to have not only the highest salaries in the world but also the high˜est quality of life.

