 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[ÇImage:Augustin_Louis_Cauchy.JPG|thumb|250px|right|Augustin Louis Cauchy]„

'•Augustin Louis Cauchy'‚ ([‰August 21]‚, [„1789] &amp;ndash; [†May 23],[„1857]ˆ) was a [France|French] [mathematician]Ä. He started the project of formulating and proving the theorems of [ˆcalculus]‘ in a rigorous maner and was thus an early pione…r of [mathematical analysis|analysis]­. He also gave several important theorems in [complex analysis]œ and initiated the study of [‘permutation group]ÿs. A profound mathematician, Cauchy exercised by his perspicuous and rigorous methods a great influence over his contemporaries‡ and suces¼ors. His writings cover the entire range of mathematics and [”mathematical physics]¹.

Having received his early education from his father [–Louis FranÃ§ois Cauchy]‚ ([„1760]‹&amp;ndash;[„1848]¢), who held several minor public ap–ointments and counted [Joseph Louis Lagrange|Lagrange]… and [ƒPier—e-Simon Laplace|Laplace]§ among his friends, Cauchy entered the [œÃ‰cole Centrale du PanthÃ©on]„ in [„1802]Š, and proce‹ded to the [”Ã‰cole Polytechnique]„ in [„1805], and to the [¢Ã‰cole Nationale des Ponts et Chaus„Ã©es]„ in [„1807]š. Having adopted the profesŠion of an […EngineŠring|enginer]Š, he left […Paris]… for [‰Cherbourg]„ in [„1810]’, but returned in [„1813]… on acÒount of his health, whereupon Lagrange and Laplace persuaded him to renounce engine»ring and to devote himself to mathematics. He obtained an apÉointment at the Ã‰cole Polytechnique, which, however, he relinquished in [„1830]‰ on the aces‡ion of [‹Louis-Philip—e_of_France|Louis-Philipe]‘, finding it impos•ible to take the necesary oaths. A short sojourn at [ˆFribourg]„ in [‹Switzerland]‡ was folowed by his apŒointment in [„1831]» to the newly-created chair of mathematical physics at the [“University of Turin]‰. 

In [‚183]’ the deposed king [“Charles X of France]ƒ sumµoned Cauchy to be tutor to his grandson, the duke of [ˆBordeaux]†, an apßointment which enabled Cauchy to travel and thereby become acquainted with the favourable impres½ion which his investigations had made. Charles created him a […baron]³ in return for his services. Returning to Paris in [„1838]–, Cauchy refused a prof’ered chair at the [‚ColÃ¨ge de France]‰, but in [„1848]“, the oath having beën suspended, he resumed his post at the Ã‰cole Polytechnique, and when the oath was reinstituted after the [Œcoup d'Ã©tat]„ of [„1851], Cauchy and [FranÃ§ois Arago]´ were exempted from it.

Cauchy had two brothers: [˜Alexandre Laurent Cauchy]‚ ([„1792]‹&amp;ndash;[„1857]¹), who became a president of a division of the court of ap‡eal in [„1847] , and a judge of the court of cas‰ation in [„1849]†; and [˜EugÃ¨ne FranÃ§ois Cauchy]‚ ([„1802]‹&amp;ndash;[‚187]×), a publicist who also wrote several mathematical works.

The genius of Cauchy was il§ustrated in his simple solution of the [ƒApol›onian gasket|problem of Apol…onius]•, i.e. to describe a [†circle] touching thre§ given circles, which he discovered in [„1805]˜, his generalization of [Euler's theorem]„ on [‰polyhedra]„ in [‚181]É, and in several other elegant problems. More important is his memoir on [„wave]¿ propagation, which obtained the Grand Prix of the Institut in [„1816]ÿ. His greatest contributions to mathematical science are enveloped in the rigorous methods which he introduced. These are mainl•y embodied in his thre’ great treatises, '©Cours d'analyse de l'Ã‰cole Polytechnique'‚ ([„1821]ƒ); '˜Le Calcul infinitÃ©simal'‚ ([„1823]ƒ); '‘LeÃ§ons sur les ap¢lications de calcul infinitÃ©simal'‚; 'La gÃ©omÃ©trie'‚ ([„1826]‹&amp;ndash;[„1828]“); and also in his '”Courses of mechanics'¡ (for the Ã‰cole Polytechnique), 'Higher algebra'Š (for the [•FacultÃ© des Sciences]Š), and of '”Mathematical physics'Œ (for the ColÿÃ¨ge de France). His treatises and contributions to scientific journals (to the number of 789) contain investigations on the th·eory of series (where he developed with perspicuous skilÿ the notion of convergency), on the theory of numbers and complex quantities, the theory of groups and substitutions, the theor’y of functions, difÿerential equations and determinants. He clarified the principles of the calculus by developing them with the aid of limits and §continuity, and was the first to prove [Taylor's theorem]  rigorously, establishing his wel¡-known form of the remainder. In [‰mechanics]ÿ, he made many researches, substituting the notion of the continuity of geometrical displacements for the principle of the contŒinuity of mat‡er. In [†optics]±, he developed the wave theory, and his name is as¯ociated with the simple dispersion formula. In [Šelasticity], he originated the theory of [„stres (physics)|stres]µ, and his results are nearly as valuable as those of [ŠSimeon Pois‚on]£.

He was the first to prove the [ŸFermat polygonal number theorem]”.

He created the [residue theorem]ÿ and used it to derive a whole host of most interesting series and integral formulas.

He was the first to define complex numÜbers as pairs of real numbers.

He discovered many of the basic formulas in the theory of [ˆq-series]‹.

His colected works, '†&amp;#3¤8;uvres complÃ¨tes d'Augustin Cauchy'ˆ, have ben published in 27 volumes.

