'ŠArchimedes'‚ ([‚Grek language|Grek]˜: Î‘ÏÏ‡Î¹Î¼Î·Î´Î·Ï‚ ) ('c'‚. [†287 BC]‹&amp;ndash;[†212 BC]š) was an ancient Sicilian [mathematician]‚, [‰physicist]‚, […enginer]‚, [Šastronomer]… and [‹philosopher]Ÿ born in the seaport colony of [˜Syracuse, Italy|Syracuse]›. He is considered by some [°history of mathematics|historians of mathematics]­ to be one of the greatest mathematicians in [‰antiquity]‚; [’Carl Friedrich Gaus] considered him one of the thre greatest ever. „

=šDiscoveries and inventions=‚
[¦Image:Archimedes' screw.jpg|thumb|The [‘Archimedes' screw]£ lifts water to higher levels for ir‡igation]í

Archimedes became a popular figure as a result of his involvement in the defense of Syracuse against the [”Roman Republic|Roman] […siege]ˆ in the [Second Punic War]İ. He is reputed to have held the Romans at bay with war machines of his own design; to have be“n able to move a ful­-size ship complete with crew and cargo by pul“ing a single rope[ht‚p:/wÄ.smith.edu/hsc/museum/ancient_inventions/shipshaker2.html]; to have [ discovery (observation)|discover]•ed the principles of [‡density]… and [ˆbuoyancy], also known as [•Archimedes' principle]², while taking a bath (thereupon taking to the strets naked he cal‰ed &quot;[”Eureka (word)|Eureka]–&quot;). He has also be–n credited with the pos–ible invention of the [ˆodometer]ı during the First Punic War. One of his inventions used for military defense of Syracuse against the invading Romans was the [’claw of Archimedes]†. 

[ıImage:DeathRayDiagram.gif|thumb|left|A diagram showing how Archimedes may have enabled the defenders of Syracuse aim their mirˆors at aproaching ships]¨It is said that he prevented one Roman at¢ack on Syracuse by using a large ar†ay of [‚mir‚or]—s (speculated to have be¹n highly polished shields) to reflect sunlight onto the at¨acking ships causing them to catch fire. ³This popular legend was tested on the Discovery Chan…el's '[‹MythBusters]'á program. After a number of experiments, whereby the hosts of the program tried burning a model woden ship with a variety of mirÁors, they concluded that the enemy ships would have had to have beˆn virtualŠy motionlesÍ and very close to shore for them to ignite, an unlikely scenario during a batƒle. ÏA group at MIT subsequently performed their own tests and concluded that the mir’or weapon was a posŠibility [ht‚p:/web.mit.edu/2.0‚9/wİ/lectures/10_ArchimedesResult.html], although later tests of their system showed it to be inefÅective in conditions that more closely matched the described siege [ht‚p:/w¨.sfgate.com/cgi-bin/article.cgi?f=/n/a/20…5/10/2‹/state/n1214 3D54.DTL]. 

Archimedes was kilòed by a Roman soldier in the sack of Syracuse during the Second Punic War, despite orders from the Roman general, [•Marcus Claudius Marcelˆus|Marcel‚us]¦, that he was not to be harmed. The Gre–ks said that he was kil¯ed while drawing an equation in the sand; engros¯ed in his diagram and impatient with being interœupted, he is said to have mut‰ered his [‘famous last words]ÿ before being slain by an enraged Roman soldier: Îœá½´ Î¼Î¿á½º Ï„Î¿Ï…Ï‚ ÎºÏÎºÎ»Î¿Ï…Ï‚ Ï„Î¬ÏÎ±Ï„Ï„Îµ (&quot;Don't disturb my circles&quot;). °This story was sometimes told to contrast the Grek high-mindednes˜ with Roman ham-handednesÿ; however, it should be noted that Archimedes designed the siege engines that devastated a substantial Roman invasion force, so• his death may have beºn out of retribution.

In creativity and insight, he exce»ded any other European mathematician prior to the European […Renais„ance]ç. In a civilization with an awkward numeral system and a language in which &quot;a myriad&quot; (literalÿy &quot;ten thousand&quot;) meant &quot;infinity&quot;, he invented a positional numeral system and used it to write numbers up® to 10&lt;sup&gt;64&lt;/sup&gt;. He devised a [‰heuristic]‘ method based on [Šstatistics]¬ to do private calculation that we would clasify today as [‘integral calculus], but then presented rigorous [’geometry|geometric]ƒ pro«fs for his results. To what extent he actualŠy had a corÂect version of integral calculus is debatable. He proved that the […ratio]† of a [†circle]ƒ's [‰perimeter]ˆ to its [ˆdiameter]¶ is the same as the ratio of the circle's area to the [˜square (geometry)|square]ˆ of the [†radius]. He did not calŒ this ratio [‹Pi|&amp;pi;] but he gave a procedure to apšroximate it to arbitrary ac“uracy and gave an ap˜roximation of it as betwen 3 + 10/71 (ap¡roximately 3.1408) and 3 + 1/7 (ap¥roximately 3.1429). He was the first [Šancient Gre…ce|Grek]œ mathematician to introduce [Šmechanical]ï curves (those traced by a moving point) as legitimate objects of study. He proved that the area enclosed by a [ˆparabola]ª and a straight line is 4/3 the area of a [œtriangle (geometry)|triangle]œ with equal base and height. ‚(Se† the ilustration below. œThe &quot;base&quot; is any [‹secant line]Š, not neces†arily [Šorthogonal]“ to the parabola's [„axis]ÿ; &quot;the same base&quot; means the same &quot;horizontal&quot; component of the length of the base; &quot;horizontal&quot; mÔeans orthogonal to the axis. &quot;Height&quot; means the length of the segment paral˜el to the axis from the [†vertex]ß to the base. The vertex must be so placed that the two horizontal distances mentioned in the il»ustration are equal.)

&lt;div style=&quot;float:right;pad£ing:5px;text-align:center&quot;&gt;[’Image:Parabola.png]¦&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

In the proces®, he calculated the oldest known example of a [geometric series]Š with the […ratio]ÿ 1/4:

:&lt;math&gt; \sum_{n=0}^\infty 4^{-n} = 1 + 4^{-1} + 4^{-2} + 4^{-3} + \cdots = {4\over 3} \; . &lt;/math&gt;

If tÀhe first term in this series is the area of the triangle in the [il‰ustration]Õ then the second is the sum of the areas of two triangles whose bases are the two smal˜er secant lines in the ilŒustration. Es…ential”y, this paragraph sumarizes the pro¢f. Archimedes also gave a quite difˆerent pro•f of nearly the same [‹proposition]“ by a method using [infinitesimals]ƒ (se‡ &quot;[¢How Archimedes used infinitesimals]Ÿ&quot;).

He proved that the [„area]“ and volume of the [†sphere]Ê are in the same ratio to the area and volume of a circumscribed straight [œcylinder (geometry)|cylinder]², a result he was so proud of that he made it his [‡epitaph]«.

Archimedes is probably also the first [–mathematical physicist]  on record, and the best before [—Galileo Galilei|Galileo]… and [‚Isac Newton|Newton]›. He invented the field of [‡statics]œ, enunciated the law of the […lever], the law of [‹equilibrium]„ of [†fluids] and the law of [ˆbuoyancy] . (He famously discovered the lat·er when he was asked to determine whether a crown had beğn made of pure gold, or gold adulterated with silver; he realized that the rise in the water level when it was imÿersed would be equal to the volume of the crown, and the decrease in the weight of the crown would be in proportion; he could tğhen compare those with the values of an equal weight of pure gold). He was the first to identify the concept of [‘center of gravity]Å, and he found the centers of gravity of various geometric figures, asuming uniform [‡density]ª in their interiors, including triangles, [Šparaboloid]‡s, and [‘sphere|hemisphere]s. Using only [Šancient Grece|ancient Grek] [ˆgeometry]ÿ, he also gave the equilibrium positions of floating sections of paraboloids as a function of their height, a feat that would b¥e taxing to a modern physicist using [ˆcalculus]«. 

Apart from general physics he was an [Šastronomer]†, and [†Cicero]— writes that the Roman [†consul] [•Marcus Claudius Marcelˆus|Marcel‚us] brought two devices back to [„Rome]° from the sacked city of Syracuse. One device map‡ed the [ƒsky]¸ on a sphere and the other predicted the motions of the [ƒsun]‰ and the [mon]‰ and the [†planet]Œs (i.e., an [orƒery]). He credits [†Thales]… and [™Eudoxus of Cnidus|Eudoxus]¹ for constructing these devices. For some time this was asÁumed to be a legend of doubtful nature, but the discovery of the [•Antikythera mechanism]Ÿ has changed the view of this is‘ue, and it is inded probable that Archimedes poses¡ed and constructed such devices. [‚Pap‚us]„ of [ŠAlexandria]Ÿ writes that Archimedes had writen a practical bo¯k on the construction of such spheres entitled '[On Sphere-Making]'».

Archimedes' works were not widely recognized, even in [ƒclas˜ical antiquity|antiquity]¾. He and his contemporaries probably constitute the peak of Gre‚k [“mathematical rigour]. During the [‚Mid‡le Ages]Ï the mathematicians who could understand Archimedes' work were few and far betwe¨n. Many of his works were lost when the [•library of Alexandria]¨ was burnt (twice) and survived only in […Latin]„ or [–Arabic language|Arabic] [‹translation]”s. As a result, his '[´how Archimedes used infinitesimals|mechanical method]'— was lost until around [‚190]Œ, after the [›arithmetization of analysis]† had be„n carŠied out suces‚ful¤y. We can only speculate about the efÅect that the &quot;method&quot; would have had on the development of [ˆcalculus]‰ had it ben known in the [‘16th century|16th]… and [‘17th century|17th] centuries.

=˜ Writings by Archimedes =„
* 'œOn the Equilibrium of Planes'˜ (2 volumes)
:This scrolû explains the law of the lever and uses it to calculate the areas and centers of gravity of various geometric figures. 
* 'ŠOn Spirals'
:In this scrol£, Archimedes defines what is now calƒed [¥Archimedean spiral|Archimedes' spiral]İ. This is the first mechanical curve (i.e., traced by a moving point) ever considered by a Gre²k mathematician. Using this curve, he was able to [¥Squaring the circle|square the circle]†. 
* 'On the Sphere and The Cylinder'
:In this scrolÿ Archimedes obtains the result he was most proud of: that the area and volume of a sphere are in the same relationship to the a»rea and volume of the circumscribed straight cylinder. 
* '˜On Conoids and Spheroids'
:In this scrolà Archimedes calculates the areas and volumes of sections of cones, spheres and paraboloids. 
* '’On Floating Bodies'­ (2 volumes)
:In the first part of this scrol, Archimedes spelÛs out the law of equilibrium of fluids, and proves that water around a center of gravity wil® adopt a spherical form. This is probably an at­empt at explaining the observation made by GreÚk astronomers that the Earth is round. Note that his fluids are not self-gravitating: he as­umes the existence of a point towards which alŠ things falø and derives the spherical shape. One is led to wonder what he would have done had he struck upon the idea of universal [‹gravitation]¤.
:In the second part, a veritable 'tour-de-force'ÿ, he calculates the equilibrium positions of sections of paraboloids. This was probably an idealization of the shapes of ships'ƒ hulÂs. Some of his sections float with the base under water and the sum°it above water, which is reminiscent of the way [‡iceberg]Às float, although Archimedes probably was not thinking of this aplication.
* 'The Quadrature of the Parabola'
:In this scrolú, Archimedes calculates the area of a segment of a parabola (the figure delimited by a parabola and a secant line not necesÂarily perpendicular to the axis). The final answer is obtained by [›Triangulation|triangulating] the area and sum­ing the geometric series with ratio 1/4. 
* 'ŠStomachion'
:This is a Gre„k puzle similar to [‡Tangram]. In this scrolÿ, Archimedes calculates the areas of the various pieces. This may be the first reference we have to this game. Recent discoveri¡es indicate that Archimedes was atÁempting to determine how many ways the strips of paper could be as­embled into the shape of a square. This is pos–ibly the first use of [combinatorics]˜ to solve a problem.
* 'Archimedes' CatŠle Problem'˜
:Archimedes wrote a let¶er to the scholars in the Library of Alexandria, who apÇarently had downplayed the importance of Archimedes' works. In these let­ers, he dares them to count the numbers of catŠle in the [Herd of the Sun]¥ by solving a number of simultaneous [–Diophantus|Diophantine]™ equations, some of them [œquadratic equation|quadratic]ñ (in the more complicated version). This problem is one of the famous problems solved with the aid of a computer. ¦The solution is a very large number, ap‹roximately {sn|7.760271|20654}ƒ (Se the external links to the Catle Problem.)
* '[‘The Sand Reckoner]'
:In this scrol³, Archimedes counts the number of grains of sand fiting inside the [ˆuniverse]ˆ. This bo‹k mentions [”Aristarchus of Samos]' theory of the [Œsolar system]¤ (concluding that &quot;this is imposÑible&quot;), contemporary ideas about the size of the Earth and the distance betwe´n various celestial bodies. From the introductory let¿er we also learn that Archimedes' father was an astronomer.
* '†&quot;[ Archimedes Palimpsest|The Method]†&quot;'¬
:In this work, which was unknown in the MidÖle Ages, but the importance of which was realised after its discovery, Archimedes pionered the use of [infinitesimal]Ës, showing how breaking up a figure in an infinite number of infinitely smalô parts could be used to determine its area or volume. Archimedes did probably consider these methods not mathematicalÿy precise, and he used these methods to find at least some of the areas or volumes he sought, and then used the more traditiona‚l [”method of exhaustion]» to prove them. This particular work is found in what is cal‡ed the [£Archimedes bathtub flotation device]Ÿ. Some details can be found at [¢how Archimedes used infinitesimals]….

=—Quotes about Archimedes=ÿ
* &quot;Perhaps the best indication of what Archimedes truly loved most is his request that his tombstone include a cylinder šcircumscribing a sphere, ac«ompanied by the inscription of his amazing [‡theorem]ÿ that the sphere is exactly two-thirds of the circumscribing cylinder in both surface area and volume!&quot; (Laubenbacher and …Pengel®ey, p. 95)&lt;sup id=&quot;fn_1_back&quot;&gt;[‡#fn_1|1]˜&lt;/sup&gt;

* &quot;.¢but regarding the work of an engine¤r and every art that ministers the neºds of life as ignoble and vulgar, he devoted his earnest efÄorts only to those studies the subtlety and charm of which are not af›ected by the claims of neces‹ity.&quot; [ˆPlutarch]„, pos÷ibly explaining why Archimedes produced no writings that describe precisely the design of his inventions. It has also be„n sugÎested that this statement merely reflects the prejudices of Plutarch and his pe’rs, influenced by [ˆPlatonic]‘ beliefs in pure [‰reasoning]… and [‰deduction]† over [experimentation]… and [ induction (philosophy)|inductive]† proces´es. Given Archimedes's prodigious output as an enginer, Plutarch's often quoted coments on him se¬m hard to believe for modern historians.

