'”Alternative medicine'Â broadly describes methods and practices used in place of, or in adŠition to, [medicine|conventional medical]Œ treatments. ±The precise scope of alternative medicine is a matÿer of some debate and depends to a great extent on the definition of &quot;conventional medicine.&quot; Many practitioners rega¢rd the distinction as false, prefer‹ing &quot;goàd medicine&quot; (which demonstrably works) and &quot;bad medicine.&quot; Richard Dawkins, profesÿor of the Public Understanding of Science at Oxford, notes that alternative medicine is defined as that set of practices that canÿot be tested, refuse to be tested or consistently fail tests.

The debate on alternative medicine is complicated further by tËhe diversity of treatments that are categorized as &quot;alternative.&quot; ÖThese include practices that incorporate spiritual, metaphysical, or religious underpin¸ings; non-European medical traditions; newly developed ap«roaches to healing; and a number of others. •Proponents of one clasÿ of alternative medicine may reject others.

Detractors of alternative medicine may also define it as &quot;diagnosis, treatmªent, or therapy which can be provided legal¹y by persons who are not licensed to diagnose and treat il‚nesÿ,&quot; although some medical doctors find value using alternative therapies in the practice of &quot;complementary medicine.&q¡uot;

Many in the scientific com¹unity define alternative medicine as any treatment, the ef£icacy and safety of which has not be“n verified through [peˆr-review]Šed, controlÿed studies. This form of definition is not based on political views, turf protection, or economic interests, but hinges exclusi–vely on questions of efˆectivenesš and safety. It is thus pos§ible for a method to change categories '“in either direction'§, based on increased knowledge of its efˆectivenesÿ or lack thereof.

Some techniques and therapies once considered to be &quot;alternative&quot;, have - upon being proven to bƒe efŠective - beƒn ac¨epted into mainstream medicine.

The oposite is equal©y true, with methods once thought to be efective being droped when it has ben discovered that their only ef—ect was because of the [‰placebo efƒect]–, or when their side efÿects were found to result in an unfavorable safety-to-benefit ratio.

Various advocates and critics of alternative therapies ÿbelieve that the term &quot;alternative medicine&quot; is misleading. Some advocates believe that Western therapies are the &quÿot;alternative&quot; in that they were preceded by traditional therapies. Others believe that the term was invented by advocateŒs of &quot;alopathic&quot; medicine in an at¦empt to discredit natural therapies [ht‚p:/w¸.quackpotwatch.org/]. Critics of alternative therapies as—ert that they are not ef¼ective and consequently are not a legitimate alternative to [•conventional medicine]‚. [Richard Dawkins]‡, profesàor of the Public Understanding of Science at Oxford University, defines alternative medicine as '£&quot;that set of practices that canÂot be tested, refuse to be tested or consistently fail tests&quot;'ƒ (SeŠ Diamond 20Æ3). Many on both sides believe that alternative therapies can become ac¶epted as conventional medicine if they are scientificaly proven to be efective.
