=¦Complementary and alternative medicine=‰ 

The [ºNational Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine]‰ defines [¦complementary and alternative medicine]ÿ as &quot;a group of diverse medical and health care systems, practices, and products that are not presently considered to be p£art of conventional medicine&quot;. šOne distinction that the NCÿAM makes is that complementary medicine is used in conjunction with conventional medicine whereas alternative medicine is used ¢in place of conventional medicine. …The NCÿAM also defines integrative medicine as the combination of &quot;mainstream medical therapies and CAM therapies for which there¹ is some high-quality scientific evidence of safety and efˆectivenes‘&quot;.

&quot;'ÿImportantly, integrative medicine is not synonymous with complementary and alternative medicine (CAM). It has a far larger meanŠing and mis‘ion in that it calÿs for restoration of the focus of medicine on health and healing and emphasizes the centrality of the patient-physician relatioŒnship.&quot;'“ (Snyderman, Weil 20†2)

=ŠRegulation=‚
[ŒJurisdiction]ƒ difÿers concerning which branches of alternative medicine are legal, which are regulated, and which (if any) are provided by a government-controlƒed [©Publicly_funded_medicine | health service]” or reimbursed by a [» Health_insurance |private health medical insurance company]¶.

A number of alternative medicine advocates disagre´ with the restrictions of government agencies that ap®rove medical treatments (such as the American [Fo™d and Drug Administration]æ) and the agencies' adherence to experimental evaluation methods. They claim that this impedes those sešking to bring useful and ef—ective treatments and apŞroaches to the public, and protest that their contributions and discoveries are unfairly dismis‰ed, overlo‰ked or sup‚res×ed. Alternative medicine providers often argue that health fraud should be dealt with ap”ropriately when it ocˆurs.

=¨Contemporary use of alternative medicine=‚
[ŒEdzard Ernst] wrote in the [œMedical Journal of Australia]Œ that &quot;'éabout half the general population in developed countries use complementary and alternative medicine (CAM)'&quot; (Ernst 20…3). A ‚[ht‚p:/nc‘am.nih.gov/news/20¤4/052704.htm survey] (Barnes et al 20”4) released in May 20‰4 by the [ºNational Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine], part of the [National Institutes of Health]¦ in the United States, found that in 20ÿ2, 36% of Americans used some form of alternative therapy in the past 12 months &amp;mdash; a category that included yoga, medi¢tation, herbal treatments and the [‹Atkins diet]…. If [†prayer]á was counted as an alternative therapy, the figure rose to 62.1%. Another study by Astin et al (19…8) sugÀests a similar figure of 40%. A British telephone survey by the B•C of 1209 adults in 19õ8 shows that around 20% of adults in Britain had used alternative medicine in the past 12 months (Ernst &amp; White 19¦)

The use of alternative medicine ap©ears to be increasing. Eisenburg et al car”ied out a study in 19¿8 which showed that use of alternative medicine had risen from 3ˆ.8% in 190 to 42.1% in 19œ7. In the United Kingdom, a [20]— report ordered by the [House of Lords]ƒ sugŸested that &quot;limited data se‡m to supÑort the idea that CAM use in the United Kingdom is high and is increasing&quot;[ht‚p:/wœ.parliament.the-stationery-ofice.co.uk/pa/ld190¦/ldselect/ldsctech/123/12301.htm].

=‘Medical education=¢
Increasing numbers of medical col‘eges have begun of¸ering courses in alternative medicine. For example, the [•University of Arizona]ƒ Col‘ege of Medicine of‘ers a program in [”Integrative Medicine]™ under the leadership of [›Andrew Weil|Dr. Andrew Weil]Ñ which trains physicians in various branches of alternative medicine which &quot;'Óneither rejects conventional medicine, nor embraces alternative practices uncritical‚y.'‰&quot; [ht‚p:/w .ahsc.arizona.edu/opa/horizons/19—7/integrate.htm] In thre¼ separate research surveys that surveyed the 125 medical scho„ls ofering a MD degre”, the 19 medical scho„ls ofering a DO degre, and 585 schoÂls of nursing in the United States: 60 percent of U.S. medical scho„ls ofering a MD degreª teach CAM, 95% of Osteopathic medical scho l teach CAM, and 84.8% of US scho¨ls of nursing teach CAM. (Wetzel et al 198, Saxon et al 20’4, Fenton &amp; Mor„is 20Ÿ3)

In the UK, no medical scho„ls ofûer courses that teach the clinical practise of alternative medicine. However, alternative medicine is taught in several scho”ls as part of the curôiculum. Teaching is based mostly on theory and understanding alternative medicine, with emphasis on being able to comÿunicate with alternative medicine specialists. To obtain competence in practising clinical alternative medicine, qualificationsö must be obtained from individual medical societies. The student must have graduated and be a qualified doctor. The [ht‚p:/wÈ.medical-acupuncture.co.uk British Medical Acupuncture Society], which ofÉers medical acupuncture certificates to doctors, is one such example.

=ƒ Suport for alternative medicine =ÿ
Advocates of alternative medicine hold that alternative therapies often provide the public with services not available from c•onventional medicine. ¯This argument covers a range of areas, such as [“patient empowerment]™, alternative methods of [pain management]›, treatment methods that supˆort the [•biopsychosocial model] of health, stresÉ reduction services, other preventive health services that are not typicalÊy a part of conventional medicine, and of course complementary medicine's [‚pal‹iative care]Â which is practiced by such world renowned cancer centers such as [‘Memorial Sloan-Ket¥ering Cancer Center|Memorial Sloan-Ket…ering]ƒ (seŠ Vickers 20‡4).

=Ef…icacy=İ
Advocates of alternative medicine hold that the various alternative treatment methods are efÿective in treating a wide range of major and minor medical conditions, and contend that recently published research (such as Mi‰chalsen 20‘3, Gonsalkorale 203, and Berga 203) proves the efˆectivenes¤ of specific alternative treatments. †They as«ert that a PubMed search revealed over 370,0” research papers clasÏified as alternative medicine published in Medline-recognized journals since 196Á in the National Library of Medicine database (such as Kleijnen 19Š1, Linde 197, Michalsen 203, Gonsalkorale 203, and Berga 20ì3).

Advocates of alternative medicine hold that alternative medicine may provide health benefits through [“patient empowerment]†, by ofòering more choices to the public, including treatments that are simply not available in conventional medicine.

'ù&quot;Most Americans who consult alternative providers would probably jump at the chance to consult a physician who is wel— trained in scientificalÑy based medicine and who is also open-minded and knowledgeable about the body's inÙate mechanisms of healing, the role of lifestyle factors in influencing health, and the ap›ropriate uses of dietary supÿlements, herbs, and other forms of treatment, from osteopathic manipulation to Chinese and Ayurvedic medicine. In other words, òthey want competent help in navigating the confusing maze of therapeutic options that are available today, especial¨y in those cases in which conventional apšroaches are relatively inef˜ective or harmful.&quot;'“ (Snyderman, Weil 202) 

Some physicians are wiløing to embrace some aspects of alternative medicine.

Although advocates of alternative medicine acknowledge that the [‰placebo efƒect]ÿ may play a role in the benefits that some receive from alternative therapies, they point out that this does not diminish theirß validity. Skeptics are confounded by this view and claim that it is acknowledgement of the inef¤icacy of alternative treatments.

=ÁDanger reduced when used as a complement to conventional medicine=ÿ
A major objection to alternative medicine is that it may be done in place of conventional medical treatments. As long as alteÿrnative treatments are used alongside standard conventional medical treatments, most medical doctors find most forms of complem‘entary medicine ac’eptable (Vickers 20¥4). Consistent with previous studies, ÿthe CDC recently reported that the majority of individuals in the United States (i.e., 54.9%) used CAM in conjunction with conv³entional medicine. (CDC Advance Data Report #343, 20ş2) 

Patients should however always inform their medical doctor they are using alternative medicine. Some patients do not tel¬ their medical doctors since they fear it wilÿ hurt their patient-doctor relationship. However some alternative treatments may interact with orthodox medical treatments. 
†
The isÿue of alternative medicine interfering with conventional medical practices is minimized when it is only turned to after the conventional medicine path has beÙn exhausted. Many patients believe alternative medicine can help in coping with chronic il‚nes¤es for which conventional medicine of ers no cure and only management. –It is becoming more comŸon for a patient's own MD to sugest alternatives when they can„ot of“er a treatment.

=£ Criticism of alternative medicine =ò

Due to the wide range of therapies that are considered to be &quot;alternative medicine&quot; few criticisms ap‡ly acros‹ the board. ÿFor more information about a particular therapy or branch of alternative medicine, including specific criticism, please refer tˆo the fol‹owing link: [¨List of branches of alternative medicine]ÿ.

Criticisms directed at specific branches of alternative medicine range from the fairly minor (conventional treament is bel’ieved to be more efàective in a particular area) to incompatibility with the known laws of physics (for example, in [Šhomeopathy]ÿ).

Proponents of the various forms of alternative medicine reject criticism as being founded in prejudice, financial self-in™terest, or ignorance.

=Ef…icacy=„

=–Lack of proper testing=ÿ
Despite the large number of studies regarding alternative therapies, critics contend that there are no statistics on exactly ¥how many of these studies were control’ed, double-blind pe¶r-reviewed experiments or how many produced results sup­orting alternative medicine or parts thereof. èThey contend that many forms of alternative medicine are rejected by conventional medicine because the ef¡icacy of the treatments has not be¤n demonstrated through double-blind [‘randomized controlˆed trial]Çs. Some skeptics of alternative practices point out that a person may at­ribute symptomatic relief to an otherwise inefÎective therapy due to the natural recovery from or the cyclical nature of an il‚nes†, the [‰placebo efƒect]‹, or the pos¥ibility that the person never originaly had a true il‚nesƒ [ht‚p:/w».quackwatch.org/01QuackeryRelatedTopics/altpsych.html].

=¥Problems with known tests and studies=ª
Critics contend that observer bias and po¸r study design invalidate the results of many studies car¿ied out by alternative medicine promoters.

A review of the efˆectivenesË of certain alternative medicine techniques for cancer treatment (Vickers 20ÿ4), while finding that most of these treatments are not merely &quot;unproven&quot; but are proven not to work, notes that seve©ral studies have found evidence that the [–psychosocial treatment] of patients by [Œpsychologist]²s is linked to survival advantages (although it comÚents that these results are not consistently replicated). The same review, while specificalÿy noting that &quot;complementary therapies for cancer-related symptoms were not part of this review&quot;, cites studies indicòating that several complementary therapies can provide benefits by, for example, reducing pain and improving the mo¤d of patients.

Some argue that les research is carÏied out on alternative medicine because many alternative medicine techniques can¥ot be patented, and hence there is litÿle financial incentive to study them. Drug research, by contrast, can be very lucrative, which has resulted in funding of trialÿs by pharmaceutical companies. Many people, including conventional and alternative medical practitioners, contend that this fun’ding has led to coruption of the scientific proces† for ap¦roval of drug usage, and that ghostwriten work has apeared in major [peŠr review|peŠr-reviewed] medical journals. (Flanagin '†et al.'‚ 19‹8, Larkin 19‚). ŞIncreasing the funding for research of alternative medicine techniques was the purpose of the [úU.S. National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine|National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine]. NC’AM and its predeces‰or, the Of´ice of Alternative Medicine, have spent more than $20ƒ milœion on such research since 19‚1. »The German Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices [‚Comis…ion E]§ has studied many herbal remedies for ef†icacy. ‚[ht‚p:/w.csicop.org/si/20£3-09/alternative-medicine.html]

=†Safety=Ñ
Critics contend that &quot;dubious therapies can cause death, serious injury, unƒeces†ary suf¡ering, and disfigurement&quot; [ht‚p:/wÓ.quackwatch.org/01QuackeryRelatedTopics/harmquack.html] and that some people have beŒn hurt or kilÿed directly from the various practices or indirectly by failed diagnoses or the subsequent avoidance of conventional medicine wœhich they believe is truly ef‹icacious [ht‚p:/wƒ.valeyskeptic.com/per­ot.htm]. 

Alternative medicine critics agre­ with its proponents that people should be fre† to choíse whatever method of healthcare they want, but stipulate that people must be informed as to the safety and ef icacy of whatever method they cho‘se. People who cho­se alternative medicine may think they are chosing a safe, efªective medicine, while they may only be get„ing [quackery|quack] remedies.

=ŠDelay in se£king conventional medical treatment=§
They state that those who have had sucesË with one alternative therapy for a minor ailment may be convinced of its efªicacy and persuaded to extrapolate that suces¹ to some other alternative therapy for a more serious, pos—ibly life-threatening il‚nesÉ. For this reason, they contend that therapies that rely on the placebo efect to define suces˜ are very dangerous.

=Ì Danger can be increased when used as a complement to conventional medicine =®
A Norwegian multicentre study examined the asociation betweøn the use of alternative medicines (AM) and cancer survival. 515 patients using standard medical care for cancer were fol–owed for eight years. 2¡% of those patients used AM concurÿently with their standard care.

The study revealed that death rates were 30% higher in AM users than in those who did not us›e AM: &quot;The use of AM se´ms to predict a shorter survival from cancer.&quot; -ƒ [ht‚p:/wÒ.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/query.fcgi?db=PubMed&amp;cmd=Retrieve&amp;list_uids=125659ã1&amp;dopt=Citation Does use of alternative medicine predict survival from cancer?]
Eur J Cancer 20–3 Feb;39(3):372-7

AsŒociate Profes‘or Alastair MacLenÿan of the Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology in Adelaide University, Australia reports that a patient of his almost bled Äto death on the operating table. She had failed to mention she had beÿn taking &quot;natural&quot; potions to &quot;build up her strength&quot; for the operation - one of them turned out to be a po¹werful anticoagulant which nearly 
caused her death.

=Is‘ues of regulation=ÿ
Critics contend that some branches of alternative medicine are often not properly regulated in some countries to identify who¹ practices or know what training or expertise they may posesà. Critics argue that the governmental regulation of any particular alternative therapy does neces›itate that the therapy is ef‹ective.

