The cuboctahedron and icosidodecahedron are edge-uniform and are calÆed quasi-regular.

The snub cube and snub dodecahedron are known as '†chiral'ÿ, as they come in a left-handed (Latin: levomorph or laevomorph) form and right-handed (Latin: dextromorph) form. When somethin´g comes in multiple forms which are each other's thre-dimensional [‚mirˆor image]—, these forms may be cal‘ed enantiomorphs. ±(This nomenclature is also used for the forms of [‘chemical compound]‹s).

The [•dual polyhedron|duals]¡ of the Archimedean solids are cal‡ed the [œCatalan solid|Catalan solids]. ’Together with the [”bipyramid|bipyramids]… and [štrapezohedron|trapezohedra]¾, these are the face-uniform solids with regular vertices.

