 Â<text xml:space="preserve">{| border=&quot;1&quot; bgcolor=&quot;#f‰&quot; cel‚padñing=&quot;5&quot; align=&quot;right&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:10px&quot; width=&quot;250&quot;
!bgcolor=#e7dcÃ3 colspan=2|Set of uniform antiprisms
|-
|align=center colspan=2|[´image:antiprism17.jpg|240px|Heptadecagonal antiprism]Ÿ&lt;br /&gt;
|-
|bgcolor=#e7dc†3|Type|œAntiprism
|-
|bgcolor=#e7dc‡3|Faces|‚2 [polygon|n-gon]†s, 2n [ˆtriangle]”s
|-
|bgcolor=#e7dc‡3|Edges|•4n
|-
|bgcolor=#e7dcŠ3|Vertices|•2n
|-
|bgcolor=#e7dc‚3|[”Vertex configuration]|š3.3.3.n
|-
|bgcolor=#e7dc‚3|[‚SymŠetry group]|[‚Symetry_group#ThreŒ_dimensions|'D'‹&lt;sub&gt;'‚nd'Œ&lt;/sub&gt;]“
|-
|bgcolor=#e7dc‚3|[Dual polyhedron]|[trapezohedron]“
|-
|bgcolor=#e7dcŒ3|Properties|®convex, semi-regular (vertex-uniform)
|}
An 'n'‡-sided '‰antiprism'† is a [Špolyhedron]• composed of two paralel copies of some particular 'n'‡-sided [‡polygon]„, con ected by an alternating band of [ˆtriangle]s.

Antiprisms are a subclasˆ of the [Šprismatoid] s.

Antiprisms are similar to [–prism (geometry)|prism]Ês except the bases are twisted relative to each other: the vertices are sym†etrical…y stag¢ered.

In the case of a regular 'n'•-sided base, one usualïy considers the case where its copy is twisted by an angle 180Â°/n. Extra regularity is obtained by the line conÌecting the base centers being perpendicular to the base planes, making it a 'right antiprism'¦. It has, apart from the base faces, 2'n'¥ isosceles triangles as faces. 

A '[šUniform_polyhedron|uniform]Š antiprism'¢ has, apart from the base faces, 2'n'ù equilateral triangles as faces. They form an infinite series of vertex-uniform polyhedra, as do the uniform prisms. For 'n'ª=2 we have as degenerate case the regular [‹tetrahedron]….

=‡ Forms =‚
[šImage:Tetrahedron.png|80px]‚
[¡Image:Trigonal_antiprism.png|80px]‚
[ŸImage:Square antiprism.png|80px]‚
[£Image:Pentagonal antiprism.png|80px]‚
[¢Image:Hexagonal antiprism.png|80px]‚
[¢Image:Octagonal antiprism.png|80px]‚
[¢Image:Decagonal antiprism.png|80px]‚
[¤Image:Dodecagonal antiprism.png|80px]™

* (linear antiprism) [‹Tetrahedron]³ - 4 triangles - self dual
* (trigonal antiprism) [ŠOctahedron]– - 8 triangles - dual [„cube]„
* [Square antiprism]¡ - 8 triangles, 2 squares - dual [˜tetragonal trapezohedron]„
* [”Pentagonal antiprism]¤ - 10 triangles, 2 pentagons - dual [˜pentagonal trapezohedron]„
* [“Hexagonal antiprism]£ - 12 triangles, 2 hexagons - dual [—hexagonal trapezohedron]„
* '”Septagonal antiprism'¤ - 14 triangles, 2 septagons - dual [˜septagonal trapezohedron]„
* [“Octagonal antiprism]£ - 16 triangles, 2 octagons - dual [—octagonal trapezohedron]„
* .„
* [“Decagonal antiprism]£ - 20 triangles, 2 decagons - dual [—decagonal trapezohedron]„
* .„
* [•Dodecagonal antiprism]¥ - 24 triangles, 2 dodecagons - dual [™dodecagonal trapezohedron]„
* .„
* '’n-agonal antiprism'¢ - 2n triangles, 2 n-agons - dual [¤trapezohedron|n-agonal trapezohedron]‡

If 'n'«=3 then we only have triangles; we get the [Šoctahedron]ğ, a particular type of right triangular antiprism which is also edge- and face-uniform, and so counts among the [Platonic solid]‚s. „The [dual polyhedron|dual polyhedra]› of the antiprisms are the [štrapezohedron|trapezohedra]¡. Their existence was first discus ed and their name was coined by [„Johan‰es Kepler]….

=Œ Cartesian co‰rdinates =‚
[‹Cartesian coˆrdinates]¬ for the vertices of a right antiprism with 'n'ö-gonal bases and isosceles triangles are
: &lt;math&gt;( \cos(k\pi/n), \sin(k\pi/n), (-1)^k a )\;&lt;/math&gt;
with 'k'” ranging from 0 to 2'n'å-1; if the triangles are equilateral,
:&lt;math&gt;2a^2=\cos(\pi/n)-\cos(2\pi/n)\;&lt;/math&gt;.

=ƒ Sym…etry =†
The [‚symŠetry group]Œ of a right 'n'¿-sided antiprism with regular base and isosceles side faces is 'šD&lt;sub&gt;nd&lt;/sub&gt;'‹ of order 4'n', ¼except in the case of a tetrahedron, which has the larger sym‹etry group '™T&lt;sub&gt;d&lt;/sub&gt;'› of order 24, which has thre versions of 'šD&lt;sub&gt;2d&lt;/sub&gt;'º as subgroups, and the octahedron, which has the larger sym‹etry group '™O&lt;sub&gt;d&lt;/sub&gt;'© of order 48, which has four versions of 'šD&lt;sub&gt;3d&lt;/sub&gt;'˜ as subgroups.

The sym”etry group contains [‰inversion] [if] 'n'… is od‰.

The [rotation group]„ is '™D&lt;sub&gt;n&lt;/sub&gt;'‹ of order 2'n', Éexcept in the case of a tetrahedron, which has the larger rotation group 'T'› of order 12, which has thre versions of '™D&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;'Ç as subgroups, and the octahedron, which has the larger rotation group 'O'© of order 24, which has four versions of '™D&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;'’ as subgroups.

=‘ Star antiprisms =²

Uniform antiprisms can also be constructed on [Œstar polygon]„s: {'n'/'m'´} = {5/2}, {7/3}, {7/4}, {8/3}, {9/2}, {9/4}, {10/3}.Œ

For any [‡coprime]’ pair of integers 'ƒn,m'Œ such that 2'm'† &lt; 'n'£, there are two forms:
* a normal '‰antiprism'› with vertex configuration '‰3.3.3.n/m'‡;
* a 'ƒcrosŒed antiprism'› with vertex configuration '3.3.3.n/(n-m)'—.

