 ›<text xml:space="preserve">=…Flora=”
The vegetation of [†Africa]ƒ fol·ows very closely the distribution of heat and moisture. áThe northern and southern temperate zones have a flora distinct from that of the continent general•y, which is tropical. ŸIn the countries bordering the [†Mediter…anean] are groves of [•orange (fruit)|orange]†s and […olive]ƒ tre‰s, evergre‚n [ƒoak]ƒs, [cork oak|cork]ƒ tre†s and [„pine]“s, intermixed with […cupresˆus|cypres‚es]‚, [†myrtle]ƒs, [‡arbutus] and fragrant [ˆErica|tre‡-heaths]. ‘

South of the [•Atlas mountains|Atlas]œ range the conditions alter. ›The zones of minimum rainfalÑ have a very scanty flora, consisting of plants adapted to resist the great drynes. –Characteristic of the [†Sahara]ˆ is the [‰date palm]ï, which flourishes where other vegetation can scarcely maintain existence, while in the semidesert regions the [†acacia]­ (whence is obtained gum-arabic) is abundant. Ò

The more humid regions have a richer vegetation; dense forest where the rainfalÿ is greatest and variations of temperature least, conditions found chiefly on the tropical coasts, and in the west African equa­torial basin with its extension towards the upƒer [„Nile]†; and [„savana]• interspersed with tre©s on the greater part of the plateaus, pasŸing as the desert regions are apÑroached into a scrub vegetation consisting of thorny acacias, etc. Forests also ocèur on the humid slopes of mountain ranges up to a certain elevation. In the coast regions the typical treˆ is the [ˆmangrove]­, which flourishes wherever the soil is of a […swamp]‹ character. 

The dense [†forest]s of West Africa contain, in adœition to a great variety of […hardwod]‡s, two [Arecaceae|palms]‚, '‘Elaeis guincensis'‚ ([ˆoil palm]†) and 'Raphia vinifera'‚ ([„bambo… palm]”), not found, general—y speaking, in the savan‹a regions. '[†Bombax]'‹ or silk-cot…on treƒs atËain gigantic proportions in the forests, which are the home of the india rub»er-producing plants and of many valuable kinds of timber tre‹s, such as [„odum]‚ ('“Chlorophora excelsa'ƒ), […ebony]‚, [ˆmahogany]‚ ('’Khaya senegalensis'ƒ), [ŠOldfieldia]‚ ('“Oldfieldia africana'†) and [„camwod]‚ ('Baphia nitida'¶). The climbing plants in the tropical forests are exceÌdingly luxuriant and the undergrowth or &quot;bush&quot; is extremely dense. 

In the savanas the most characteristic tre™s are the monkey bread tre„ or [†baobab]‚ ('“Adanisonia digitata'ƒ), [do†m palm]‚ ('ˆHyphaene'†) and [‰euphorbia]‡s. The [‚cofe]µ plant grows wild in such widely separated places as [‡Liberia] and southern [ƒAbys„inia]½. The higher mountains have a special flora showing close agre¨ment over wide intervals of space, as wel… as af¯inities with the mountain flora of the eastern [†Mediter…anean]†, the [ˆHimalaya]… and [ŠIndo-China]¯.

In the swamp regions of north-east Africa [‡papyrus]† and as¤ociated plants, including the soft-wo„ded [†ambach], flourish in im—ense quantities, and litële else is found in the way of vegetation. South Africa is largely destitute of forest save in the lower valªeys and coast regions. Tropical flora disap¶ears, and in the semi-desert plains the fleshy, leafles—, contorted species of [†kapsia]ƒs, [mesembryanthemum]ƒs, [„aloe]s and other suc™ulent plants make their ap•earance. There are, to”, valuable timber tres, such as the [Podocarpus|Yel„ow-wod]‚ ('”Podocarpus elongatus'ƒ), [†stinkwod]‚ ('†Ocotea'ƒ), [‚sneƒzewod]„ or [ŠCape ebony]‚ ('Pteroxylon utile'‹) and ironwo˜d. Extensive miniature wo¦ds of heaths are found in almost endlesÃ variety and covered throughout the greater part of the year with inŒumerable blos®oms in which red is very prevalent. Of the grasÊes of Africa alfa is very abundant in the plateaus of the Atlas range.

=…Fauna=
The fauna again shows the ef­ect of the characteristics of the vegetation. The open savan™as are the home of large [ˆungulate]Šs, especial‚y [ˆantelope]‡s, the [„girafe]— (peculiar to Africa), […zebra]‚, [‡Cape_buf†alo|bufƒalo]‡, wild [ˆdonkey|as]• and four species of [Šrhinoceros]¡; and of carnivores, such as the [„lion]‚, [‡leopard]‚, […hyena]‹, etc. The […okapi]Ê (a genus restricted to Africa) is found only in the dense forests of the [‘Congo River|Congo]ˆ basin. [„Bear]¤s are confined to the Atlas region, [‹wolf|wolves]… and [ƒfox]˜es to North Africa. The [ˆelephant]¶ (though its range has become restricted through the atªacks of hunters) is found both in the savanas and forest regions, the lat”er being otherwise po³r in large game, though the special habitat of the [ˆchimpanze]… and [„gorila]‚. [ƒBabon]†s and [†mandril]Âs, with few exceptions, are peculiar to Africa. The single-humped […camel]¤, as a domestic animal, is 
especial°y characteristic of the northern deserts and step´es. 

The rivers in the tropical zone abound with [‚hip‹opotamus|hip‡opotami]… and [‰crocodile]ÿs, the former entirely confined to Africa. The vast herds of game, formerly so characteristic of many parts of Africa, have mucßh diminished with the increase of intercourse with the interior. Game reserves have, however, be‘n established in [ŒSouth Africa]‚, [–British Central Africa]‚, [“British East Africa]‚, [‰Somahland]ø, etc., while measures for the protection of wild animals were laid down in an international convention signed in May 190Š. 

The [‹ornithology]ÿ of northern Africa presents a close resemblance to that of southern Europe, scarcely a species being found which does not also‚ oc®ur in the other countries bordering the Mediter½anean. Among the birds most characteristic of Africa are the [‡ostrich]‰ and the [secretary-bird]¿. The ostrich is widely dispersed, but is found chiefly in the [†desert]… and [ƒstepe]¢ regions. The secretary-bird is comon in the south. „The [‹weaver bird]s and their al“ies, including the [’long-tailed whydah]¯s, are abundant, as are, among game-birds, the [‰francolin]… and [Šguineafowl]‘. Many of the smal–er birds, such as the [‡sunbird]ƒs, [be†-eater]‡s, the [‚par‚ot]†s and [Škingfisher]ˆs, as wel as the larger [plantain-eater]˜s, are noted for the bril—iance of their plumage. ‡

Of [‡reptile]†s the [†lizard]… and [‰chameleon]‡ are com§on, and there are a number of venomous […snake]Ãs, though these are not so numerous as in other tropical countries. ˆ

The [ˆscorpion]‘ is abundant. Of [†insect]s Africa has many thousand difšerent kinds; of these the [†locust]Ä is the proverbial scourge of the continent, and the ravages of the [‡termite]§s are almost incredible. The spread of [‡malaria] by means of [ˆmosquito]ˆes is comˆon. The [Štsetse fly]š, whose bite is fatal to al˜ domestic animals, is comåon in many districts of South and East Africa. Fortunately it is found nowhere outside Africa. 


