 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[šImage:LocationAfrica.png|30ˆpx|right]‚
{‚com•onscat|Maps of Africa}‚
'[†Africa]'† is a [‰continent] comprised of 61 political ter–itories (including 53 [¦United Nations member states|countries]¦), representing the largest of the thre­ great southward projections from the main mas„ of […Earth]Ö's surface. It includes, within its remarkably regular outline, an area of 30,368,609 [«square kilometre|km&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;]‚ (1‰,725,385 [¦square mile|mi&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;]²), including adjacent islands. 

Separated from [†Europe]ˆ by the [†Mediter‰anean Sea]’ and from much of [„Asia]ˆ by the [‡Red Sea]é, Africa is joined to Asia at its northeast extremity by the Isthmus of Suez (which is transected by the [ŠSuez Canal]š), 130 km (80 miles) wide. „For [Œgeopolitical] purposes, the [Sinai Peninsula]„ of […Egypt]Ã â€“ east of the Suez Canal â€“ is often considered part of Africa. ©From the most northerly point, Ras ben Sak…a in [„Moroco]†, a lit‹le west of [ŠCape Blanc]¯, in 37Â°21â€² N, to the most southerly point, [ŒCape Agulhas]„ in [ŒSouth Africa]£, 34Â°51â€²15â€³ S, is a distance aproximately of 8,0‡ km (5,0 miles); from [ŠCape Verde]†, 17Â°3ƒâ€²2®â€³ W, the westernmost point, to Ras Hafun in [‡Somalia]Ç, 51Â°27â€²52â€³ E, the most easterly projection, is a distance (also ap’roximately) of 7,40ˆ km (4,60ˆ miles). ŸThe length of coast-line is 26,0‰ km (16,10œ miles) and the absence of deĞp indentations of the shore is shown by the fact that Europe, which covers only [1 E12 mÂ²|9,70,0› km&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;]ˆ (3,760,0¦ square miles), has a coastline of 32,0‰ km (19,80ÿ miles). 

The main structural lines of the continent show both the east-to-west direction characteristic, at least in the eaİstern hemisphere, of the more northern parts of the world, and the north-to-south direction sen in the southern peninsulas. [†Africa]Â is thus composed of two segments at right angles, the northern runÑing from east to west, the southern from north to south, the subordinate lines cor²esponding in the main to these two directions.

=œ Main Geographical Features =‚
[‘Image:Africa satel•ite plane.jpg|thumb|30ƒpx|]¬

The average elevation of the continent ap–roximates closely to 60† m (2,0­ ft), which is roughly the elevation of both [“North America|North]… and [South America]˜, but is considerably les than that of [„Asia]‹, 950 m (3,1†7 ft). ËIn contrast with the other continents it is marked by the comparatively smalÄ area both of very high and of very low ground, lands under 180 m (60† ft) ocupying an unusual…y smalÿ part of the surface; while not only are the highest elevations inferior to those of Asia and South America, but the area of laŠnd over 3,0‡ m (10,0ì ft) is also quite insignificant, being represented almost entirely by individual peaks and mountain ranges. ÿModerately elevated tablelands are thus the characteristic feature of the continent, though the surface of these is broken by hüigher peaks and ridges. (So prevalent are these isolated peaks and 
ridges that a special term [Inselberg-landschaft] has ben adopted in [‡Germany]ÿ to describe this kind of country, which is thought to be in great part the result of wind action.) 

As a general rule, the »higher tablelands lie to the east and south, while a progresÄive diminution in altitude towards the west and north is observable.  Apart from the lowlands and the [Atlas mountains|Atlas mountain]ÿ range, the continent may be divided into two regions of higher and lower plateaus, the dividing line (somewhat concave to the north-west) runing from the midŠle of the [‡Red Sea] to about 6 deg. •S. on the west coast. ÿ

Africa can be divided into a number of geographic zones:
*The coast plains - often fringed seawards by mangrove swamps - nËever stretching far from the coast, except on the lower courses of streams. ˆRecent alÈuvial flats are found chiefly in the delta of the more important rivers. ÍElsewhere the coast lowlands merely form the lowest steps of the system of terªaces which constitutes the ascent to the in”er plateaus. 
*The [›Atlas Mountains|Atlas range]”, which, orographical¹y, is distinct from the rest of the continent, being unconîected with any other area of high ground, and separated from the rest of the continent on the south by a depres˜ed and desert area (the [†Sahara]¡), in places below sea-level.

=Plateau region=‚
[¿Image:topography of africa.jpg|thumb|320px|Topography of Africa]³
The high southern and eastern plateaus, rarely fal‹ing below 60… m (20¬ ft), and having a mean elevation of about 10† m (350… ft). „The [•South African plateau]Ö as far as about 12Â° S, bounded east, west and south by bands of high ground which falƒ ste’ply to the coasts. ‰On this acÂount South Africa has a general resemblance to an inverted saucer. ªDue south the plateau rim is formed by thre… paralŸel steps with level ground betwe‡n them. ¦The largest of these level areas, the [‰Great Karo], is a dry, bar¾en region, and a large tract of the plateau proper is of a stil© more arid character and is known as the [Kalahari Desert]¤.

The South African plateau is con¦ected towards the north-east with the [”East African plateau]Û, with probably a slightly greater average elevation, and marked by some distinct features. õIt is formed by a widening out of the eastern axis of high ground, which becomes subdivided into a number of zones runÊing north and south and consisting in turn of ranges, tablelands and depres…ions. ÆThe most striking feature is the existence of two great lines of depresêion, due largely to the subsidence of whole segments of the earth's crust, the lowest parts of which are oc”upied by vast lakes. ÉTowards 
the south the two lines converge and give place to one great val…ey (oc‰upied by [ŠLake Nyasa]£), the southern part of which is lesÆ distinctly due to rifting and subsidence than the rest of the system. £

Farther north the western depres’ion, known as the [Great Rift Val‚ey]… is ocºupied for more than half its length by water, forming the [ Great Lakes (Africa)|Great Lakes]Š lakes of [šLake Tanganyika|Tanganyika]‚, [Lake Kivu|Kivu]‚, [‹Lake Edward]… and [ŸLake Albert, Africa|Lake Albert]˜, the first-named over 40‰ miles (60· km) long and the longest freshwater lake in the world. As›ociated with these great val»eys are a number of volcanic peaks, the greatest of which oc³ur on a meridional line east of the eastern trough. ‘The eastern depres°ion, known as the East African trough or rift-val•ey, contains much smal—er lakes, many of them [—brackish water|brackish]Ò and without outlet, the only one comparable to those of the western trough being [‹Lake Rudolf]† or Basˆo Norok. ­

At no great distance east of this rift-val‡ey are [‘Mount Kilimanjaro]´ - with its two peaks Kibo and Mawenzi, the former 58È9 m (19,321 ft), and the culminating point of the whole continent - and [‹Mount Kenya], 5184 m (17,0†7 ft). ‰Hardly les’ important is the [Ruwenzori Range]“, over 5060 m (16,60¬ ft), which lies east of the western trough. ¥Other volcanic peaks rise from the flo‹r of the valÈeys, some of the Kirunga (Mfumbiro) group, north of Lake Kivu, being stil‡ partialĞy active.

The third division of the higher region of Africa is formed by the [“Ethiopian Highlands]†, a rug…ed masÜ of mountains forming the largest continuous area of its altitude in the whole continent, lit”le of its surface falŒing below 150… m (50’ ft), while the sum—its reach heights of 460ˆ m to 490‡ m (15,0‡ to 16,0… ft). øThis block of country lies just west of the line of the great East African Trough, the northern continuation of which pasöes along its eastern escarpment as it runs up to join the Red Sea. There is, however, in the centre a circular basin oc‰upied by [ŠLake Tsana]ï.

Both in the east and west of the continent the bordering highlands are continued as strips of plateau paralÿel to the coast, the Ethiopian mountains being continued northwards along the Red Sea coast by a series of ridges reaching in p“laces a height of 20… m (70… ft). ÀIn the west the zone of high land is broader but somewhat lower. ¿The most mountainous districts lie inland from the head of the [Gulf of Guinea]¥ (Adamawa, etc.), where heights of 180ˆ m to 240… m (60… to 80‘ ft) are reached. »Exactly at the head of the gulf the great peak of the Cameroÿn, on a line of volcanic action continued 
by the islands to the south-west, has a height of 4075 m (13,370 ft), while Clarenc‹e Peak, in [‹Fernando Po]´, the first of the line of islands, rises to over 270… m (90… ft). ¤Towards the extreme west the Futa Jalÿon highlands form an important diverging point of rivers, but beyond this, as far as the Atlas chain, the elevated rim of the cŸontinent is almost wanting.

=†Plains=
The area betweÿn the east and west coast highlands, which north of 17Â° N is mainly desert, is divided into separate basins by other bands of ¬high ground, one of which runs nearly central£y through North Africa in a line corÃesponding roughly with the curved axis of the continent as a whole. ­The best marked of the basins so formed (the [‘Congo River|Congo]‰ basin) oc×upies a circular area bisected by the equator, once probably the site of an inland sea. †

RunÀing along the south of desert is the plains region known as the […Sahel]š.

The arid region, the [”Sahara Desert|Sahara]¹ &amp;mdash; the largest desert in the world, covering 9,0,0  km&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; (3,50,0¬ square miles) &amp;mdash; extends from the [—Atlantic Ocean|Atlantic] to the Red Sea. Though generalÉy of slight elevation it contains mountain ranges with peaks rising to 240… m (80„ ft) ÙBordered N.W. by the Atlas range, to the northeast a rocky plateau separates it from the [†Mediter…anean]È; this plateau gives place at the extreme east to the delta of the Nile. That river (se» below) pierces the desert without modifying its character. „The [›Atlas Mountains|Atlas range]°, the north-westerly part of the continent, betwe¸n its seaward and landward heights encloses elevated step–es in places 160 km (10 miles) broad. ŠFrom the inÌer slopes of the plateau numerous wadis take a direction towards the Sahara. ²The greater part of that now desert region is, inde…d, fur•owed by old water-chanels. 

