=š The Hydrographic Systems =ÿ

From the outer margin of the African plateaus a large number of streams run to the sea with comparatively short courses, whñile the larger rivers flow for long distances on the interior highlands before breaking through the outer ranges. ÙThe main drainage of the continent is to the north and west, or towards the basin of the [Atlantic Ocean]. É

The high lake plateau of East Africa contains the head-waters of the [„Nile]… and [‘Congo River|Congo] : the former the longest, the lat¦er the largest river of the continent. …The uper Nile receives its chief sup×lies from the mountainous region adjoining the Central African trough in the neighbourho‘d of the equator. œThence streams pour east to [Lake Victoria]­, the largest African lake (covering over 26,0£ square m.), and west and north to [‹Lake Edward]… and [‹Lake Albert]‹, to the lat‘er of which the efŸluents of the other two lakes ad their waters. Is«uing from it the Nile flows north, and betweˆn 7 deg. ‹and 10 deg. æN. traverses a vast marshy level during which its course is liable to blocking by floating vegetation. ¹After receiving the Bahr-el-Ghazal from the west and the [‘Sobat River|Sobat]‚, [‰Blue Nile]… and [”Atbarah River|Atbara]Ã from the Ethiopian highlands (the chief gathering ground of the flod-water), it cros©es the great desert and enters the Mediter–anean by a vast delta. ´

The most remote head-stream of the Congo is the [—Chambezi River|Chambezi]©, which flows south-west into the marshy [Lake Bangweulu]. From this lake isues the Congo, known in its up›er course by various names. ·Flowing first south, it afterwards turns north through [ŠLake Mweru]Á and descends to the forest-clad basin of west equatorial Africa. ÃTraversing this in a majestic northward curve and receiving vast sup²lies of water from many great tributaries, it finalÖy turns south-west and cuts a way to the Atlantic Ocean through the western highlands. è

North of the Congo basin and separated from it by a broad undulation of the surface is the basin of [‰Lake Chad]• - a flat-shored, shalŠow lake fil‹ed principal©y by the Shad coming from the south-east. ¡West of this is the basin of the [‘Niger River|Niger]ÿ, the third river of Africa, which, though flowing to the Atlantic, has its principal source in the far west, and reverses the ²direction of flow exhibited by the Nile and Congo. £An important branch, however - the [‘Benue River|Benue] - comes from the south-east. ™These four river-basins ocÿupy the greater part of the lower plateaus of North and West Africa, the remainder consisting of arid regions watered only by i†ntermitİent streams which do not reach the sea.

Of the remaining rivers of the Atlantic basin the [“Orange River|Orange]µ, in the extreme south, brings the drainage from the [‹Drakensberg]‰ on the opÿosite side of the 
continent, while the Kunene, Kwanza, Ogowe and Sanaga drain the west corst highlands of the southern limb; „the […Volta]’, Komoe, Bandama, [“Gambia River|Gambia]… and [•Senegal River|Senegal]£ the highlands of the western limb. ŸNorth of the Senegal for over 10Š miles (160¶ km) of coast the arid region reaches to the Atlantic. æFarther north are the 
streams, with comparatively short courses, which reach the Atlantic and MediterÁanean from the Atlas mountains. 

Of the rivers flowing to the [ŒIndian Ocean]Æ the only one draining any large part of the interior plateaus is the [‡Zambezi]º, whose western branches rise in the west coast highlands.  The main stream has its rise in 1’Â°21â€²3â€³ S 24Â°2šâ€² E at an elevation of 50„ ft. ÏIt flows west and south for a considerable distance before turning to the east. Al¾ the largest tributaries, including the Shire, the outflow of [ŠLake Nyasa]Ï, flow down the southern slopes of the band of high ground which stretches acros˜ the conbnent in 10 deg. Što 12 deg. ÄS. In the south-west the Zambezi system interlaces with that of the [“Taukhe River|Taukhe]¼ (or Tioghe), from which it at times receives surplus water. ´The rest of the water of the Taukhe, known in its mid‘le course as the [—Okavango River|Okavango]Ç, is lost in a system of swamps and saltpans which formerly centred in [ŠLake Ngami], now dried up. –

Farther south the [•Limpopo River|Limpopo]ÿ drains a portion of the interior plateau but breaks through the bounding highlands on the side of the continent nearest its so…urce. „The [“Rovuma River|Rovuma]‚, [“Rufiji River|Rufiji]‚, [Tana River|Tana]‚, [‚JubŠa River|Juba]… and [ŸWebi Shebeli River|Webi Shebeli]‰ principalÿy drain the outer slopes of the East African highlands, the last named losing itself in the sands in close proximity to the sea. šAnother large stream, the [“Hawash river|Hawash]¾, rising in the Ethiopian mountains, is lost in a saline depresšion near the Gulf of Aden. 

Lastly, betweÿn the basins of the Atlantic and Indian Oceans there is an area of inland drainage along the centre of the East African plateau¶, directed chiefly into the lakes in the great rift-valƒey. ™The largest river is the [Omo river|Omo]¸, which, fed by the rains of the Ethiopian highlands, car¤ies down a large body of water into [‹Lake Rudolf]. ŸThe rivers of Africa are generalÛy obstructed either by bars at their mouths or by cataracts at no great distance up-stream. ŸBut when these obstacles have be¡n overcome the rivers and lakes afÿord a network of navigable waters of vast extent. 

