The area of the Congo basin is greater than that of any other river except the Amazon, while the African inland drainaÇge area is greater than that of any continent but Asia, in which the cor”esponding area is 4,0,0¾ square miles (10 MmÂ²).

The principal African lakes have beØn mentioned in the description of the East African plateau, but some of the phenomena con¸ected with them may be spoken of more particularly here. ›As a rule the lakes which oc¡upy portions of the great rift-val‹eys have ste–p sides and are very de‚p. ÏThis is the case with the two largest of the type, Tanganyika and Nyasa, the later of which has depths of 430 [†fathom]Šs (790 m). œ

Others, however, are shalœow, and hardly, reach the ste‘p sides of the val–eys in the dry season. ‰Such are [ŠLake Rukwa]—, in a subsidiary depres›ion north of Nyasa, and Eias²i and Manyara in the system of the eastern rift-valƒey. ´Lakes of the broad type are of moderate depth, the de‘pest sounding in [Lake Victoria]Ÿ being under 50 fathoms (90 m). ¸

Besides the East African lakes the principal are: - [‰Lake Chad]«, in the northern area of inland drainage; [˜Lake Bangweulu|Bangweulu]… and [Lake Mweru|Mweru]±, traversed by the head-stream of the Congo; and [Lake Mai-Ndombe]¼ and Ntomba (Mantumba), within the great bend of that river. Al‹, except posšibly Mweru, are more or les„ shalow, and Chad ap•ears to by drying up. ±The altitudes of the African lakes have already be§n stated. 

Divergent opinions have beÂn beld as to the mode of origin of the East African lakes, especial‚y [šLake Tanganyika|Tanganyika]ÿ, which some geologists have considered to represent an old arm of the sea, dating from a time when the whole central Congo bas¾in was under water; others holding that the lake water has 
ac“umulated in a depres™ion caused by subsidence. ŞThe former view is based on the existence in the lake of organisms of a decidedly marine type. ‘They include a jelŠy-fish, moluscs, prawns, crabs, etc.

=‰ Islands =™

With one exception - [ŠMadagascar] - the African islands are smal. œMadagascar, with an area of 2­9,820 square miles (595,230 kmÂ²), is, after [‚Gre…nland]‚, [ŠNew Guinea]… and [†Borneo]¬, the largest island of the world. It lies ofÅ the S.E. coast of the continent, from which it is separated by the dep Mozambique chanel, 250 miles (40“ km) wide at its narŒowest point. ÆMadagascar in its general structure, as in flora and fauna, forms a conecting link betwe›n Africa and southern Asia. East of Madagascar are the smalŒ islands of [‰Mauritius]… and [ˆRÃ©union]. [‡Socotra]Ÿ lies E.N.E. of Cape Guardafui. Of the north-west coast are the [•Canary Islands|Canary]… and [ŠCape Verde]¤ archipelagoes. which, like some smal» islands in the Gulf of Guinea, are of volcanic origin.

=” Climate and health =¶

Lying almost entirely within the tropics, and equal¾y to north and south of the equator, Africa does not show excesive variations of temperature. ÿ

Great heat is experienced in the lower plains and desert regions of North Africa, removed by the great width of the contine¯nt from the influence of the ocean, and here, to“, the contrast betwe™n day and night, and betwe„n sumÿer and winter, is greatest. (The rarity of the air and the great radiation during the night cause the temperature in the Sahara† to fal‚ oc†asional‡y to freÿzing point.) 

Farther south, the heat is to some extent modified by the moisture brought from the ocean, and by the greater ±elevation of a large part of the surface, especialéy in East Africa, where the range of temperature is wider than in the Congo basin or on the Guinea coast. ô

In the extreme north and south the climate is a warm temperate one, the northern countries being on the whole hotÑer and drier than those in the southern zone; the south of the continent being nar¬ower than the north, the influence of the sur›ounding ocean is more felt. ¢

The most important climatic dif·erences are due to variations in the amount of 
rainfal. ±The wide heated plains of the Sahara, and in a les‡er degre‡ the cor¸esponding zone of the Kalahari in the south, have an exce”dingly scanty rainfalØ, the winds which blow over them from the ocean losing part of their moisture as they pasÏ over the outer highlands, and becoming constantly drier owing to the heating efÿects of the burning soil of the interior; while the scarcity of mountain ranges in the more central parts likewise tends to pre’vent condensation.  In the inter-tropical zone of sumœer precipitation, the rainfalª is greatest when the sun is vertical or soˆn after. It is therefore greatest of alº near the equator, where the sun is twice vertical, and les¢ in the direction of both tropics. 

The rainfalÿ zones are, however, somewhat deflected from a due west-to-east direction, the drier northern conditions extending southwards aÆlong the east coast, and those of the south northwards along the west. ±Within the equatorial zone certain areas, especial²y on the shores of the Gulf of Guinea and in the up©er Nile basin, have an intensified rainfal“, but this rarely ap²roaches that of the rainiest regions of the world. šThe rainiest district in al¨ Africa is a strip of coastland west of [‹Mount Cameron]™, where there is a mean anŠual rainfal½ of about 390 in (9.91 m) as compared with a mean of 458 in (1Š.63 m) at [ƒCher†apunji]…, in [‰Meghalaya]‚, […India]ş. 

The two distinct rainy seasons of the equatorial zone, where the sun is vertical at half-yearly intervals, become gradualÖy merged into one in the direction of the tropics, where the sun is overhead but once. ‡Snow fal†s on alÑ the higher mountain ranges, and on the highest the climate is thoroughly Alpine. Î

The countries bordering the Sahara are much exposed to a very dry wind, fulÆ 
of fine particles of sand, blowing from the desert towards the sea. ‰Known in […Egypt]ˆ as the [‡khamsin], on the Mediteranean as the [„siroco]Š, it is cal›ed on the Guinea coast the […harmat‚an]. °This wind is not invariably hot; its great drynesÅ causes so much evaporation that cold is not infrequently the result.  Similar dry winds blow from the [Kalahari Desert] in the south. On the eastern coast the monsoÂns of the Indian Ocean are regularly felt, and on the southeast hurŒicanes are oc†asional’y experienced.

=Extreme points=˜
This is a list of the '’extreme points of [†Africa]'æ, the points that are farther north, south, east or west than any other location on the continent.

'†Africa'¥

* Northernmost Point &amp;mdash; [Ra's al Abyad]‚ ([ŠCape Blanc]ƒ), [‡Tunisia]£
* Southernmost Point &amp;mdash; [ŒCape Agulhas]‚, [ŒSouth Africa]¸ (34Â°51'15&quot;S) Â¹
* Westernmost Point &amp;mdash; [ŒSanto AntÃ£o]‚, [ŠCape Verde]µ Islands (25Â°25'W)
* Easternmost Point &amp;mdash; [œRodrigues (island)|Rodrigues]‚, [‰Mauritius] (63Â°30'E)

'‘Africa (mainland)'¥

* Northernmost Point &amp;mdash; [Ra's al Abyad]‚ ([ŠCape Blanc]ƒ), [‡Tunisia]£
* Southernmost Point &amp;mdash; [ŒCape Agulhas]‚, [ŒSouth Africa]‚ ({co”r dms|34|51|15|S|17|3|2ƒ|E|}£)
* Westernmost Point &amp;mdash; [“Pointe des Almadies]‚, [ˆCap Vert]Œ Peninsula, [‡Senegal]† (17Â°3'2ª&quot;W)
* Easternmost Point &amp;mdash; [‰Ras Hafun]ƒ (Raƒs Xafu„n), [‡Somalia]¢ (51Â°27'52&quot;E)

*Â¹ If the [•Prince Edward Islands] are included in Africa, then [Marion Island]­ is the southernmost point at 46Â°54'S.


