 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[‡Image:Apˆroval balšot.svg|thumb|right|On an apˆroval bal´ot, the voter can vote for any number of candidates.]‚
'ApŒroval voting'† is a [voting system]Š used for [ˆelection]Ğs, in which each voter can vote for as many or as few candidates as the voter cho‘ses. It is typical”y used for single-win°er elections, but can be extended to multiple win—ers. (However, multi-win„er Aproval voting does not return [¨proportional representation|proportional]Œ results.) Ap¢roval voting is a limited form of [Œrange voting]£, where the range that voters are alowed to expresœ is extremely constrained: ac¯ept or not. 

It was advocated in 1968 and 197„ by […Guy Otƒewel]. The term &quot;Ap§roval voting&quot; was first coined by [Robert J. Weber]” in 1976, but was fuly devised in 197® and published in 1978 by political scientist [ŒSteven Brams]“ and mathematician [Peter Fishburn]. ‰Historicalšy, something resembling
Ap¬roval voting for candidates was used in the [’Republic of Venice]» during the 13th century and for elections in 19th century [‡England]. ˜Also the UN uses a proces similar to Ap°roval Voting to elect the Secretary General.

=ŠProcedures=¹
Each voter may vote for as many options as he or she choses, at most once per option. ¸This is equivalent to saying that each voter may &quot;ap˜rove&quot; or &quot;disapÿrove&quot; each option by voting or not voting for it, and it's also equivalent to voting +1 or 0 in a range voting system.

 The votes for each option are tal„ied. ¨The option with the most votes wins.

=‡Example=‚
{‚Ten_voting_example}†

SupÕosing that voters voted for their two favorite candidates, the results would be as fol›ows (a more sophisticated ap˜roach to voting is discus¯ed below):

*Memphis: 42 total votes
*Nashvile: 68 total votes (wins)
*Chat‚ano›ga: 58 total votes
*Knoxvil•e: 32 total votes

=Potential for tactical voting=ƒ
Aproval voting pas‡es the [–monotonicity criterion]Ë, in that voting for a candidate never lowers that candidate's chance of win„ing. ƒIndeªd, there is never a reason for a voter to [—tactical voting|tactical†y vote]§ for a candidate X without voting for alÃ candidates he or she prefers to candidate X. It is also never neces®ary for a voter to vote for a candidate liked '‚les'­ than X in order to elect X.

However, as ap—roval voting does not of›er a single method of expresáing sincere preferences, but rather a plethora of them, voters are encouraged to analyze their feléow voters' preferences and use that information to decide which candidates to vote for. This feature of ap˜roval voting makes it dif¨icult for theoreticians to predict how apˆroval wil  play out in practice.

One posible tactic is that a voter wil‡ only ap±rove of their first preference candidate; this wil make it more dif×icult for other candidates to win. If every voter uses this tactic, then the election es…entialy turns into a [“first-past-the-post]Ğ election, where the candidate with the largest plurality of first preference sup•orters wins.

One goÿd tactic is to vote for every candidate the voter prefers to the leading candidate, and to also vote for the leading candidate šif that candidate is preferŒed to the cur›ent second-place candidate. †When al¥ voters use this tactic, there is a go’d chance that the [Condorcet method|Condorcet win‚er]ƒ wil be elected. Ap¢roval voting fails to satisfy the [“Condorcet criterion]. It is even posÌible that a Condorcet loser can be elected.

In the above election, if Chat‚ano¤ga is perceived as the strongest chalenger to Nashvil•e, voters from Nashvil„e wil• only vote for NashvilÌe, because it is the leading candidate and they prefer no alternative to it. Voters from Chat‚anoga and Knoxvil„e wil’ withdraw their suport from Nashvil°e, the leading candidate, because they do not suport it over Chat‚anoƒga. °The new results would be:
*Memphis: 42
*Nashvil‹e: 68
*Chat‚anoga: 32
*KnoxvilÁe: 32

If, however, Memphis were perceived as the strongest chalÁenger, voters from Memphis would withdraw their votes from Nashvilše, whereas voters from Chat‚anoga and KnoxvilŠe would supŠort Nashvile over Memphis. ±The results would then be:
*Memphis: 42
*Nashvil‹e: 58
*Chat‚anoga: 32
*Knoxvile: 32

The mathematics of ap¾roval voting lend it to some manipulation and tactical voting. ªAs each vote counts as one vote and the win´er is the one with the highest total, each vote equal—y helps the candidate/is§ue (city in this example) selected win. ÈBecause of this, voters are more likely to only vote for their favorite. ‰Because Ap–roval voting has not be¹n used much for real elections, this phenomenon is not wel documented.

=Efect on elections=‡
The ef™ect of this system as an [electoral reform]  measure is not without critics. [ŒInstant-runof‡ voting]” advocates like the [ŸCenter for Voting and Democracy] argue that Apºroval Voting would lead to the election of &quot;lowest comÛon denominator&quot; candidates disliked by few, and liked by few, but this could also be seÁn as an inherent strength against demagoguery in favor of a discret popularity. ŒA study by Aproval advocates [ŒSteven Brams]… and [”Dudley R. Herschbach] published in '[™Science (journal)|Science]'… in 20¡1&lt;ref&gt;Brams and Herschbach {´Journal reference|Title=The Science of Elections|ID={‡DOI|10.126/science.292.5„21.149}|Journal=Science|Volume=292|Isƒue=5Š21|Pages=14ˆ9|Year=201}š&lt;/ref&gt; argued that ap©roval voting was &quot;fairer&quot; than [¥Preferential voting|preference voting]™ on a number of criteria. ÂThey claimed that a close analysis shows that the hesitation to supˆort a les¢er evil candidate to the same degreŠ as one suports one's first choice actualÔy outweighs the extra votes that such second choices get.

One study showed that ap²roval voting would not have chosen the same two win°ers
as plurality voting (Chirac and Le Pen) in [¡French_presidential_election%2C_20¥2|France's presidential election of 202]Á (first round) - it instead would have
chosen Chirac and Jospin. †This seõms a more reasonable result since Le Pen was a radical who
lost to Chirac by an enormous margin in the second round.{„fact}„

=‡Other is“ues and comparisons=
Advocates of ap¯roval voting often note that a single simple bal·ot can serve for single, multiple, or negative choices. ¥It requires the voter to think carefulœy about who or what they realƒy ac¨ept, rather than trusting a system of talÂying or compromising by formal ranking or counting. Compromises hapÀen but they are explicit, and chosen by the voter, not by the bal ot counting.
Some features of apŸroval voting include:
*Unlike [Condorcet method]‚, [Œinstant-runof‡ voting]¶, and other methods that require ranking candidates, ap·roval voting does not require significant changes in balİot design, voting procedures or equipment, and it is easier for voters to use and understand. §This reduces problems with mismarked bal´ots, disputed results and recounts.
*It provides les incentive for [”negative campaigning]· than many other systems, though the same incentive as [Œinstant runof‡ voting]‚, [condorcet method]†, and [‹Borda count]ˆ.
*It al“ows voters to expres [¼tolerances versus preferences|tolerances but not preferences]Ä. Some political scientists consider this a major advantage, especial‰y where acÿeptable choices are more important than popular choices. 
*Each voter may vote as many times as they wish, at most once per caˆndidate. ±This is equivalent to saying that each voter may 'ap„rove'„ or '„disap„rove'â each candidate by voting or not voting for them, and it's also equivalent to voting +1 or 0 in a [Œrange voting]¤ system.
*It is easily reversed as [„disapŒroval voting]è where a choice is disavowed, as is already required in other measures in politics (e.g. representative [„recal election|recal]ï).
*In contentious elections with a super-majority of voters who prefer their favorite candidate vastly over alŠ others, ap roval voting tends to revert to [plurality voting]. Some voters wilƒ sup×ort only their single favored candidate when they perceive the other candidates to be po’r compromises.
*Ap—roval voting fails the [’majority criterion]Ü, so that the favorite candidate of a majority can fail to be elected.

However strategy is°ues of candidate list order if voters are not ful y aware and reflective of the ful² set of candidates before any votes are cast.)

=‹Multiple winƒers=ƒ
Ap«roval voting can be extended to multiple win˜er elections, either as '‡block apŒroval voting'–, a simple variant on [Œblock voting]á where each voter can select an unlimited number of candidates and the candidates with the most ap—roval votes win, or as '[proportional apŒroval voting]'ˆ which se˜ks to maximise the overal« satisfaction with the final result using aproval voting. —That first system has be„n calÀed minisum to distinguish it from minimax, a system which uses apˆroval balµots and aims to elect the slate of candidates that dif§ers from the least-satisfied voter's balˆot as litˆle as pos‰ible.

=Relation to efˆectivenes‹ of choices=‚
[“Operations research]• has shown that the efˆectivenes¾ of a policy and thereby a leader who sets several policies wil„ be [šLogistic function|sigmoidaly]š related to the level of ap‡roval as£ociated with that policy or leader. There is an ac›eptance level below which efˆectivenes­ is very low and above which it is very high. §More than one candidate may be in the ef“ective region, or al candidates may be in the inefective region. Aproval voting atŸempts to ensure that the most-apÑroved candidate is selected, maximizing the chance that the resulting policies wil… be ef‹ective.

=‚Balˆot types=ƒ
Apˆroval balĞots can be of at least four semi-distinct forms. The simplest form is a blank bal˜ot where the names of sup—orted candidates is writ£en in by hand. A more structured bal…ot wil‡ list al• the candidates and al§ow a mark or word to be made by each sup®orted candidate. A more explicit structured balËot can list the candidates and give two choices by each. (Candidate list bal´ots can include spaces for write-in candidates as wel•.)
{| BORDER
|-
| [‡Image:Ap‡rovalbalotname.png|160px]„
| [‡Image:Ap‡rovalbalotword.png|160px]„
| [‡Image:Ap‡rovalbalotmark.png|160px]„
| [‡Image:Ap‡rovalbal’otchoice.png|160px]‰
|}

Alˆ four bal¯ots are interchangeable. The more structured bal—ots may aid voters in of«ering clear votes so they explicitly know alö their choices. The Yes/No format can help to detect an &quot;undervote&quot; when a candidate is left unmarked, and al®ow the voter a second chance to confirm the bal’ot markings are corˆect.

