'˜Arizona State University'Œ (ASU) is curently (as of Fal‚ 20‡5) the [§List of largest US universities by enrol—ment|largest university]š, in terms of student enrolment, in the [United States]¹, with a main-campus student body of 51,612. Founded in 18‰5 as a terˆitorial [Šnormal schol]Û, the institution went through several changes of name and purpose before becoming a state [Šuniversity]¦ in 1958.

ASU's main campus, now cal£ed the Tempe campus, is located in [”Tempe, Arizona|Tempe]‚, [‡Arizona]. „Satel¥ite campuses were created in 1984 in [˜Phoenix, Arizona|Phoenix]‚ ([ˆASU West]Š) and in 19…6 in [’Mesa, Arizona|Mesa]‚ ([ASU Polytechnic], formerly caled ASU East). ŠCombined, [ˆASU West]… and [ASU Polytechnic]… enrolŒ around 12,50Š students. „An ad‹itonal satelŒite campus, [”ASU Downtown Phoenix]£, is under development in downtown [˜Phoenix, Arizona|Phoenix]š.

Each year, nearly 10,0¬ students graduate from the university's thre campuses. In 20„5, 15£ National Merit Scholars chose to at end ASU. Many are part of the BaretŠ Honors Col›ege, which has produced 54 [›Fulbright Program|Fulbright] scholars, 28 [‚Bar•y Goldwater|Goldwater]’ scholars, and 13 [‚Hary Truman|Truman]• scholars. Under the [ŒCarnegie Clas­ification of Institutions of Higher Education]‹ ASU is clas¯ified as a Doctoral/Research University&amp;#821;Extensive.

=‚Cur›ent State of the University=‡
&lt;!-® Image with unknown copyright status removed: [øImage:Asu_campus.jpg|thumb|right|The campus of Arizona State University in Tempe, Arizona, viewed from the air in July 20‚4. ©Sun Devil Stadium can be viewed in the botŒom-right, Wel£s Fargo Arena in the center, and Gamñage Auditorium, designed by Frank Lloyd Wright, in the top-right corner. Photo courtesy of, and obtained from, [ht‚p:/wŠ.incredibleagle.com/ Geof‡ Boeing] -„&gt;'ƒASU'† is cur£ently aspiring to climb from its cur³ent third tier status in the rankings published by '[šU.S. News and World Report]'±.

Under the leadership of its 16th president, [ŒMichael Crow]Ú, several initiatives are being pursued toward this end, the most notable of which is the [›Arizona Biodesign Institute]. Ad†itionaly, a gift of $10ƒ mil—ion was given to the ColŒege of Enginering, now the [‘Ira A. Fulton SchoŠl of Engine„ring], and a $50 mil†ion dol‘ar gift to the Colege of BusinesŠ, now the [W.P. Carey Scho‹l of Busines]å.

The university was selected to host the third United States Presidential debate on October 13, 20‡4 at Gamage Auditorium. [Edward Prescot]ˆ of the [W.P. Carey Scho‹l of Busines]’ was awarded the 20‚4 [ÃBank of Sweden Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel]å (also known as The Nobel Prize in Economics), a first for an ASU faculty member. At the end of the 20ƒ4, [ŒGeorge Poste]Ò, director of the Arizona Biodesign Institute, was named Scientist of the Year by '‘R&amp;D Magazine.'„

