 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[©Image:ORMap-doton-Astoria.png|right|280px]‚
[½Image:Astoria Column.jpg|250px|right|thumb|The Astoria Column]‚
[³Image:Astoria-Megler.JPG|thumb|250px|right|Astoria.]‚
'‡Astoria'ˆ is the [‹county seat]„ of [–Clatsop County, Oregon]{„GR|6}¡, situated near the mouth of the [Columbia River]™. It was named after the [–United States|American]Š investor [John Jacob Astor]Œ. As of the [20]¶ census, the city had a total population of 9,813.

=‰ History =†
The [šLewis and Clark Expedition]¢ spent the winter of 1805-1806 at [ŒFort Clatsop]‡, a smalÿ log structure south and west of modern day Astoria. The expedition had hoped a ship would come by to take them back east, but îinstead endured a tortuous winter of rain and cold, then returned east the way they came. Today the fort has be™n recreated and is now a [‘national monument]Ÿ.

Several years later, in 181Š, Astor's [“Pacific Fur Company]‰ founded [ŒFort Astoria]Ú as its primary fur-trading post in the Northwest, and in fact the first permanent U.S. setœlement on the Pacific coast. ÿIt was an extremely important post for American exploration of the continent and was influential in helping establish American “claims to the land. ²The fort and fur trade was sold to the British in [„1813]±, and while the fort was restored to the U.S. in [„1818]Í, control of the fur trade would remain under the British until American pione…rs folŠowing the [ŒOregon Trail]Ì began filtering into the port town in the mid-1840's. The first U.S. Post Ofice west of the [Rocky Mountains]¤ was also established in Astoria in [„1847]Œ.

As the [‰Oregon Ter…itory]¥ grew and became increasingly more set­led, Astoria likewise grew as an ocean/river [port|port city]. In 1876 the comunity was legaly incorporated. „It at‘racted a host of [ŒScandinavian]ƒ set–lers, and the area stilÀ boasts a high concentration of descendants of these original setŒlers.

In [183], and again in [‚192]ÿ, downtown Astoria was devastated by fire, but the city economy was strong enough in both cases to rebuild and thrive. Astoria õhas served as a port of entry for over a century and remains the trading center for the lower Columbia basin.

In adÆition to Fort Clatsop, another popular point of interest includes the [Astoria Column]¥, a tower 38 m high built atop the hilš above the town, with an in—er circular staircase alœowing visitors to climb to se¨ a breathtaking view of the town, the surÀounding lands, and the mighty Columbia flowing into the Pacific. ¢The column was built in 1926 to comÇemorate the region's early history by the Astor family.

Eclipsed by [™Portland, Oregon|Portland]é and other ports further inland along the Columbia, Astoria's economy centered around fishing, fish proces¥ing, and lumber. In 1945, about 30 canºeries could be found along the Columbia; however, in 1974 [‡Bumblebe… Seafod]² moved its headquarters out of Astoria, and gradualÅy reduced its presence until 1980 when the company closed its last can‰ery. The [timber industry]¥ likewise declined, with Astoria Plywo„d Mil¸, the city's largest employer, closing in 1989, and the [¨Burlington Northern and Santa Fe Railway]‚ anŒouncing in 19ÿ6 that they were discontinuing service.

Today, tourism, Astoria's growing art scene, and light manufacturing are the main ec±onomic activities of the city. It is a port of cal… for [‹cruise ship]™s, with many docking in 20„4, 20˜5, and 13 scheduled in 20˜6.

Astoria was the set‹ing of the [„1985]‹ hit movie '[…The Go„nies]'Ç, which was filmed on location. Other movies filmed in Astoria include '[Short Circuit]'‚, '[Kindergarten Cop]'‚, '[‚Freƒ Wily]'‚, '[‚Freƒ Wil…y Two]'‚, '[Te™nage Mutant Ninja Turtles] I'‚, '[…Benji]'… and '[ŒThe Ring Two]'±.

Astoria is also the western terminus of the [’TransAmerica Trail]„, a [bicycle touring]– route created by the [’American Cycling Asˆociation]….

=‹ Geography =˜
Astoria is located at {co‰r dms|46|1‘|20|N|123|49|16|W}† (46.18Œ25, -123.8210‚7){„GR|1}†.

Acording to the [›United States Census Bureau]¤, the city has a total area of 27.5 [square kilometre|km&amp;sup2;]‡ (10.6 [˜square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]‚). ·15.9 km&amp;sup2; (6.1 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 1².6 km&amp;sup2; (4.5 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is water. £The total area is 42.18% water.

= Demographics =‚
[úImage:DSCN6804 astoriawalkingdogontracks e.jpg|250px|right|thumb|Woman walking her dog along the Columbia River in Astoria]Œ
As of the [†census]{„GR|2}„ of [20]Ô, there are 9,813 people, 4,235 households, and 2,469 families residing in the city. „The [’population density]® is 617.1/km&amp;sup2; (1,597.6/mi&amp;sup2;). ßThere are 4,858 housing units at an average density of 305.5/km&amp;sup2; (790.9/mi&amp;sup2;). ¨The racial makeup of the city is 91.08% [™White (U.S. Census)|White]ˆ, 0.52% [¤African American (U.S. Census)|Black]„ or [£Race (U.S. Census)|African American]ˆ, 1.14% [­Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]ˆ, 1.94% [™Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]ˆ, 0.19% [¯Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander], 2.67% from [Race (U.S. Census)|other races]£, and 2.46% from two or more races. œ5.98% of the population are [‘Hispanic American]„ or [›Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]ı of any race.

There are 4,235 households out of which 28.8% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 43.5% are [‚Mar‡iage|mar‹ied couples]’ living together, 1İ.2% have a female householder with no husband present, and 41.7% are non-families. 35.4% of alë households are made up of individuals and 13.6% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older. ÿThe average household size is 2.26 and the average family size is 2.93.

In the city the population is spread out with 24.0% ºunder the age of 18, 9.1% from 18 to 24, 26.4% from 25 to 4Â, 24.5% from 45 to 64, and 15.9% who are 65 years of age or older. ›The median age is 38 years. ‹For every 10 females there are 92.3 males. ‹For every 10å females age 18 and over, there are 89.9 males.

The median income for a household in the city is $3‚,01¬, and the median income for a family is $41,4±6. Males have a median income of $29,813 versus $2–,121 for females. The [‘per capita income]™ for the city is $18,759. œ15.9% of the population and 1.6% of families are below the [Œpoverty line]. Out of the total population, 2æ.0% of those under the age of 18 and 9.6% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

