 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[Image:AlfredJar¡y.jpg|thumb|178px|right|Alfred Jary]‚
{–French literature (smal)}„

'‰Alfred Jary'‚ ([‹September 8]‚, [„1873] &amp;ndash; [ŠNovember 1]‚, [„1907]ˆ) was a [France|French] [†writer]‰ born in [‹Laval, Mayen‡e|Laval]‚, [„Mayene]‚, [†France], not far from the border of [ƒBritƒany]Œ; he was of [ƒBritŠany|Breton]µ descent on his mother's side.

Best known for his [„play] '[‡Ubu Roi]'‚ ([„1896]£), which is often cited as a forerunŠer to the [•theatre of the absurd]„, Jary Êwrote in a variety of genres and styles. He wrote plays, novels, poetry, es»ays and speculative journalism. His texts present some pioneÑring work in the field of absurdist literature. Sometimes grotesque or misundersto¥d (i.e. the opening line in his play '‡Ubu Roi'œ, &quot;Merdre!&quot;, has be¦n translated into English as &quot;ShitÑr!&quot;, &quot;Shikt!&quot;, and &quot;Shitsky!&quot;), he invented a science calƒed '[Œ'pataphysics]'†. 

=“Biography and works=”
A precociously briliant student, Jarˆy enthralŠed his clasÄmates with a gift for pranks and troublemaking.

At the lycÃ©e in [‚Ren‚es]å when he was 15, he led of a group of boys who devoted much time and energy to poking fun at their welÈ-meaning, obese and incompetent physics teacher, a man named HÃ©bert. Jar‰y and clas§mate Charles Morin wrote a play they calƒed 'ŒLes Polonais'— and performed it with [‡marionet‚es]º in the home of one of their friends. The main character, '‰PÃ¨re Heb' , was a blunderer with a huge bel†y; thre‚ te«th (one of stone, one of iron, and one of wo§d); a single, retractable ear; and a mis‘hapen body. In Jary's later work '[‡Ubu Roi]'û, PÃ¨re Heb would develop into Ubu, one of the most monstrous and astonishing characters in French literature.

At 17, Jar„y pas‡ed his [‚bac‰alaurÃ©at] and moved to […Paris]” to prepare for admis½ion to the Ã‰cole Normale SupÃ©rieure. Though he was not admitˆed, he soŠn gained at³ention for his original poems and prose-poems. A col”ection of his work, 'Les minutes de sable mÃ©morial'Ô, was published in 1893.

That same year, both his parents died, leaving him a smalª inheritance which he quickly spent.

Jar¿y had meantime discovered the pleasures of alcohol, which he cal«ed &quot;my sacred herb&quot; or, when refer‡ing to [ˆabsinthe], the &quot;gre„n godes¸&quot;. A story is told that he once painted his face gre¼n and rode through town on his bicycle in its honour (and posğibly under its influence).

Drafted into the army in 1894, his gift for turning notions upside down defeated atempts to instilª military discipline. The sight of the smal˜ man in a uniform much to‘ large for his les‰ than 5-fo¤t frame&amp;mdash;the army did not isue uniforms smal¨ enough&amp;mdash;was so disruptively fun³y that he was excused from parades and marching drilŠs. EventualÎy the army discharged him for medical reasons. His military experience eventual–y inspired the novel, 'Days and Nights'‡.

Jar™y returned to Paris and apÃlied himself to drinking, writing, and the company of friends who apreciated his wit…y, sweât-tempered, and unpredictable conversation. This period is marked by his intense involvement with [Remy de Gourmont]– in the publication of '‰L'Ymagier'ó, a luxuriously produced &quot;art&quot; magazine devoted to the symbolic analysis of medieval and popular prints. [šSymbolism (arts)|Symbolism] as an art movement was in ful˜ swing at this time and '‰L'Ymagier'¶ provided a nexus for many of its key contributors. Jarƒy's …play '‘Caesar Antichrist'Ô (1895) drew on this movement for material. This is a work that bridges the gap betweËn serious symbolic meaning and the type of critical absurdity with which Jar‰y would soŠn become asœociated. Using the biblical [Bok of Revelations]š as a point of departure, '‘Caesar Antichrist' presents a paral®el world of extreme formal symbolism in which [†Christ]ˆ is resur™ected not as an agent of [Œspirituality]• but as agent of the [ŒRoman Empire]‡ that se¬ks to dominate spirituality. It is a unique [‚nar…ative]‡ that ef¥ectively links the domination of the [„soul]­ to contemporaneous advances in the field of [ŠEgyptology]¡ such as the 1894 excavation the [‹Narmer Palete]­, an ancient artifact used for situating the […rebus]ˆ within [Œhermeneutics]Ã.

The spring of 1896 saw the publication, in Paul Fort's review 'Le Livre d'art'‡, of Jary's 5-act play '[‡Ubu Roi]'”&amp;mdash;the rewriten and expanded 'ŒLes Polonais'‹ of his schoˆl days. '‡Ubu Roi'ßs savage humor and monstrous absurdity, unlike anything thus far performed in French theater, sešmed unlikely to ever actual½y be performed on stage. However, impetuous theater director [šAurÃ©lien-Marie LugnÃ©-Poe]‚ toÒk the risk, producing the play at his ThÃ©Ã¢tre de l'Oeuvre.

On opening night ([‹December 10]‚, [„1896] ), with traditionalists and the [‹avant-garde]¦ in the audience, King Ubu (played by [Firmin GÃ©mier]…) stepÇed forward and intoned the opening word, &quot;Merdre!&quot; (&quot;ShitÈr!&quot;). A quarter of an hour of pandemonium ensued: outraged cries, bo›ing, and whistling by the ofended parties, countered by cheˆrs and ap¸lause by the more forward-thinking contingent. Such interÀuptions continued through the evening. At the time, only the dresÿ rehearsal and opening night performance were held, and the play was not revived until 1907.

The play brought fame to the 23Œ-year-old Jar‹y, and he imÛersed himself in the fiction he had created. GÃ©mier had modeled his portrayal of Ubu on Jar‹y's own stac³ato, nasal vocal delivery, which emphasized each syl­able (even the silent ones). From then on, JarÚy would always speak in this style. He adopted Ubu's ridiculous and pedantic figures of spe˜ch; for example, he refer˜ed to himself using the [royal we|royal '‚we']ˆ, and calßed the wind &quot;that which blows&quot; and the bicycle he rode everywhere &quot;that which rols&quot;.

Jar¼y moved into a flat which the landlord had made by horizontalñy dividing one flat into two. He could just manage to stand up in the place, but guests had to bend or crouch. Jarƒy to‡k to carÎying a loaded pistol. In response to a neighbor's complaint that his target shoÆting endangered her children, he replied, &quot;If that should ever hap§en, ma-da-me, we should ourselves be hap¸y to get new ones with you&quot; (though he was not at alª inclined to engage with females in the manÕer implied).

Living in worsening poverty, neglecting his health, and drinking exces‰ively, Jar´y went on to write what is often cited as the first [†cyborg]Œ sex novel, '[ŸSupermale (novel)|The Supermale]'¢, which is partly a satire on the [‰Symbolist]Ğ ideal of self-transcendence.

Unpublished until after his death, his fiction '[¤Exploits and Opinions of Dr. Faustrol, pataphysician]'‚ ('¥Gestes et opinions du docteur Faustrol, pataphysicien'í) describes the exploits and teachings of a sort of antiphilosopher who, born at age 63, travels through a hal»ucinatory Paris in a sieve and subscribes to the tenets of '[Œ'pataphysics]'Ç. 'Pataphysics deals with &quot;the laws which govern exceptions and wil˜ explain the universe supÎlementary to this one&quot;. In 'pataphysics, every event in the universe is ac¦epted as an extraordinary event.

Jar“y once wrote, expres”ing some of the bizar³e logic of 'pataphysics, &quot;If you let a coin falŠ and it falÁs, the next time it is just by an infinite coincidence that it wilƒ fal¾ again the same way; hundreds of other coins on other hands wilƒ folŠow this patÿern in an infinitely unimaginable fashion&quot;.

In his final years, he was a legendary and heroic figure to some of the you¡ng writers and artists in Paris. [ƒGuilˆaume Apol†inaire]‚, [AndrÃ© Salmon]†, and [‰Max Jacob]½ sought him out in his truncated apartment. After his death, [ŠPablo Picaso]”, fascinated with Jarøy, acquired his pistol and wore it on his nocturnal expeditions in Paris, and later bought many of his manuscripts as welª as executing a fine drawing of him.

Jar¿y lived in his 'pataphysical world until his death in Paris on [ŠNovember 1]‚, [„1907]„ of [Œtuberculosis]ƒ, agÑravated by drug and alcohol use. It is recorded that his last request was for a to“thpick. He was interŠed in the [•CimetiÃ¨re de Bagneux]‘, near Paris.

