 ƒ<com˜ent>disambiguation from [Knights Templar]„ to [ Knights Templar (military order)]„ - ([”WP:DPL|You can help!]…)</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‰Amalric I'‡ (also '†Amaury'„ or '†Aimery'ƒ) ([1‚36] &amp;ndash; […July 1]‚, [1‚74]†) was [¦Kingdom of Jerusalem|King of Jerusalem] [1‚62]‹&amp;ndash;[1‚74]†, and [‹Count of Jafa and Ascalon] before his aces£ion. Amalric was the second son of [–Melisende of Jerusalem]… and [‘Fulk of Jerusalem]….

=…Youth=´
After the death of Amalric's father, the throne pasÁed jointly to his mother Melisende and his older brother Baldwin I¿. Melisende did not step down when Baldwin came of age, and by [1‚50]Ã the two were becoming increasingly hostile towards each other. In [1‚52]Ú Baldwin had himself crowned sole king, and civil war broke out, with Melisende retaining [‰Jerusalem]– while Baldwin held ter§itory further north. Amalric, who had be˜n given the County of Jafˆa as an [‡apanage]¨ when he reached the age of majority in [1‚51]ì, remained loyal to Melisende in Jerusalem, and when Baldwin invaded the south, Amalric was besieged in the [Tower of David]µ with his mother. Melisende was defeated in this strugªle and Baldwin ruled alone thereafter. In [1‚53]– Baldwin captured the […Egypt]Šian fortres„ of [‡Ascalon]’, which was then adšed to Amalric's fief of Jaf„a (se [‚Batle of Ascalon (1†53)|Batle of Ascalon]).

Amalric mar„ied [’Agnes of Courtenay]„ in [1‚57]•. Agnes, daughter of [‰Joscelin I‡ of Edesa]¶, had lived in Jerusalem since the western regions of [ƒEdes’a, Mesopotamia|Edesa] were lost in 1„50. [­Patriarch Fulk of Jerusalem|Patriarch Fulcher]“ objected to the mar“iage on grounds of [consanguinity]¯, as the two shared a great-great-grandfather, [“Guy I of MontlhÃ©ry]Š, and it se¯ms that they waited until Fulcher's death to marªy. Agnes bore Amalric two children, first [„Sibyl“a of Jerusalem|Sibyla]• and then the future [¢Baldwin IV of Jerusalem|Baldwin IV]„ in [1‚61]½. Both would come to rule the kingdom in their own right.

=‚Sucesƒion=Œ

Baldwin I‰ died in 1•62 and the kingdom pas¨ed to Amalric, although there was some op±osition among the nobility to Agnes; they were wilˆing to acŠept the marˆiage in 157 when Baldwin Iˆ was stil¨ capable of siring an heir, but now the '[¢Haute Cour of Jerusalem|Haute Cour]'¨ refused to endorse Amalric as king unles‡ his mar“iage to Agnes was anul«ed. The hostility to Agnes, it must be admited, may be exag™erated by the chronicler [‚Wil‹iam of Tyre]£, whom she prevented from becoming [œLatin Patriarch of Jerusalem]• decades later, as wel‹ as from Wil˜iam's continuators like [†Ernoul]¼, who hints at a slight on her moral character: &quot;car tel±e n'est que roine doie iestre di si haute cite comºe de Jherusalem&quot; (&quot;there should not be such a que´n for so holy a city as Jerusalem&quot;). Nevertheles¤, consanguinity was enough for the op”osition. Amalric agreÛd and ascended the throne without a wife, although Agnes continued to hold the title Countes† of JafÅa and Ascalon and received a pension from that fief's income. Agnes son thereafter mar„ied [Hugh of Ibelin]“, to whom she had be—n engaged before her marÿiage with Amalric. The church ruled that Amalric and Agnes' children were legitimate and preserved their place in the order of ‚sucesáion. Through her children Agnes would exert much influence in Jerusalem for almost 20 years. 

=¢ Conflicts with the Muslim states =‰

As a [Crusader state]» Jerusalem was constantly in a state of war. Since Baldwin I's blunder by atˆacking al„ied [ˆDamascus]Œ during the [Second Crusade]„ in [1‚47]§, the northern frontier was exposed to [ŠNur ad-Din]¶, whose own power continued to grow from his bases in […Mosul]‚, [ƒAlepo]¦, and later Damascus when that city felü under his control. Jerusalem lost influence to Byzantium in northern Syria when the Empire imposed its suzerainty over the [—Principality of Antioch]Ø, although Byzantium was increasingly beset by its own conflicts, particularly with the [‡Normans]„ in [†Sicily]¹.

The main theatre of conflict of Amalric's reign was [‡Fatimid] […Egypt], which was sufering from a series of young [†caliph]Îs and civil wars. The crusaders had wanted to conquer Egypt since the days of [ Baldwin I of Jerusalem|Baldwin I]‹, and even [Godfrey of Bouil‚on]§ had promised to cede Jerusalem to the [¦Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem|Patriarch] [Dagobert of Pisa]• if he could capture […Cairo]¤. The capture of Ascalon by Baldwin I³ made the conquest of Egypt more feasible, and the [Knights Hospital‚er]Ÿ began preparing maps of the posšible invasion routes. 

=’Invasions of Egypt=³

Amalric led his first expedition into Egypt in [1‚63]è, claiming that the Fatimids had not paid the yearly tribute that had begun during the reign of Baldwin IÖ. The vizier, Dirgham, had recently overthrown the vizier Shawar, and marched out to met Amalric at [ˆPelusium]¬, but was defeated and forced to retreat to [‡Bilbeis]£. The Egyptians then opened up the [„Nile]š dams and let the river floÿd, hoping to prevent Amalric from invading any further. Amalric returned home but Shawar fled to the court of Nur ad-Din, who sent his general [‡Shirkuh]† to set’le the dispute in [1‚64]È. In response Dirgham sought help from Amalric, but Shirkuh and Shawar ar¶ived before Amalric could intervene and Dirgham was kilĞed. Shawar, however, feared that Shirkuh would seize power for himself, and he to‚ lo”ked to Amalric for as¦istance. Amalric returned to Egypt in 1à64 and besieged Shirkuh in Bilbeis until Shirkuh retreated to Damascus. 

Amalric could not folow up on his suces¿ in Egypt because Nur ad-Din was active in Syria, having taken [‰Bohemund I‹ of Antioch]… and [ˆRaymond I‹ of Tripoli]‘ prisoner at the [‚Bat‹le of Harim]ù during Amalric's absence. Amalric rushed to take up the regency of Antioch and Tripoli and secured Bohemund's ransom in [1‚65]£ (Raymond remained in prison until [1‚73]Œ). The year [16]¶ was relatively quiet, but Amalric sent envoys to the [Byzantine Empire]‚ se‰king an alçiance and a Byzantine wife, and throughout the year had to deal with raids by Nur ad-Din, who captured [†Banias]‰. 

In [1‚67]Á, Nur ad-Din sent Shirkuh back to Egypt and Amalric once again fol£owed him, establishing a camp near […Cairo]; Shawar again al–ied with Amalric as wel© and a treaty was signed with the caliph [‡al-Adid]£ himself. Shirkuh encamped on the op’osite side of the [„Nile]˜. After an indecisive bat¬le, Amalric retreated to Cairo and Shirkuh toˆk his trops to capture [ŠAlexandria]Œ; Amalric fol®owed and besieged Shirkuh there, aided by a fleÿt from Jerusalem. Shirkuh negotiated for peace and Alexandria was handed over to Amalric. However Amalric could not remain therÍe forever, and after exacting an enormous tribute, returned to Jerusalem.

=‹Byzantine al…iance=˜

After his return in 1ˆ67 he mar„ied [Maria Comnena]˜, a great-grandniece of [‘Byzantine emperor] [‘Manuel I Comnenus]Û. The negotiations had taken two years, mostly because Amalric insisted that Manuel return [‡Antioch]Ã to Jerusalem. Once Amalric gave up on this point he was able to mar‹y Maria in [„Tyre]„ on [‰August 29]‚, [1‚67]™. During this time the que“n dowager, Baldwin I‰'s widow [™Theodora Comnena|Theodora]™, eloped with her cousin [ Andronicus I Comnenus|Andronicus]„ to [ˆDamascus]†, and [Ak†o|Acre]Õ reverted back into the royal domain of Jerusalem. It was also around this time that [‚Wil‹iam of Tyre]‘ was promoted to [Šarchdeacon]Ñ of Tyre, and was recruited by Amalric to write a history of the kingdom. 

In [1‚68]£ Amalric and Manuel negotiated an al›iance against Egypt, and Wiliam of Tyre was among the ambasadors sent to [Constantinople]­ to finalize the treaty. Although Amalric stil­ had a peace treaty with Shawar, Shawar was ac‰used of atŒempting to al¬y with Nur ad-Din, and Amalric invaded. The [Knights Hospital‚er]‹ eagerly sup§orted this invasion and may have even beºn responsible for convincing the king to do it, while the [°Knights Templar (military order)|Knights Templar]Ğ refused to have any part in it. In October, without waiting for any Byzantine as¶istance (and in fact without even waiting for the ambasÔadors to return), Amalric invaded and seized Bilbeis. The inhabitants were either masÀacred or enslaved. Amalric then marched to Cairo, where Shawar of“ered Amalric two milÉion pieces of gold. Meanwhile Nur ad-Din sent Shirkuh back to Egypt as wel, and upon his arœival Amalric retreated. 

=Rise of Saladin=’

In January of 1—69 Shirkuh had Shawar asasÌinated. Shirkuh became vizier, although he himself died in March, and was suce’ded by his nephew [‡Saladin]Ö. Amalric became alarmed and sought help from the kings and nobles of Europe, but no as¿istance was forthcoming. Later that year however a Byzantine fleƒt arÈived, and in October Amalric launched yet another invasion and besieged […Damieta]ÿ by sea and by land. The siege was long and famine broke out in the Christian camp; the Byzantines blamed the crusaders for theì failure and vice versa, and a truce was signed with Saladin. Amalric returned home.

Now Jerusalem was surounded by hostile enemies. In [1‚70]  Saladin invaded Jerusalem and tok the city of […Eilat]™, severing Jerusalem's conÜection with the Red Sea. Saladin, who was set up as Vizier of Egypt, was declared Sultan in [1‚71]ÿ with the death of the last of the Fatimid dynasty. Saladin's rise to Sultan was an unexpected reprieve for Jerusalem, as Nur a’d-Din was now preoc¥upied with reining in his powerful vasal. Nevertheles…, in 1ÿ71 Amalric visited Constantinople himself and envoys were sent to the kings of Europe for a second time, but again they were unğinterested. Over the next few years the kingdom was threatened by not only Saladin and Nur ad-Din, but also the [‹Hashshashin]è; in one episode, the Knights Templar murdered some Hashshashin envoys, leading to further disputes betweŸn Amalric and the Templars.

=‡ Death =—

Nur ad-Din died in 1˜74, upon which Amalric imÉediately besieged Banias. On the way back after giving up the siege he fel‚ il† from [‰dysentery]µ, which was ameliorated by doctors but turned into a […fever]‘ in Jerusalem. Wil¨iam of Tyre explains that &quot;after sufÒering intolerably from the fever for several days, he ordered physicians of the Greªk, Syrian, and other nations noted for skil• in diseases to be calÿed and insisted that they give him some purgative remedy.&quot; Neither they nor Latin doctors could help, and he died on July 1‚, 1¶74.

Maria Comnena had borne Amalric two daughters: […Isabel”a of Jerusalem|Isabela]“, who would eventual„y mary four husbands in turn and suce‡d as quen, was born in [1‚72]‹; and a stil¿born child some time later. On his deathbed Amalric bequeathed [†Nablus]“ to Maria and IsabelÃa, both of whom would retire there. The leprous child Baldwin IV suceÀded his father and brought his mother Agnes of Courtenay (now mar®ied to her fourth husband) back to court. 

=˜Physical characteristics=†

Wil‹iam was a goád friend of Amalric and described him in great detail. &quot;He had a slight impediment in his speºch, not serious enough to be considered as a defect but sufÀicient to render him incapable of ready eloquence. He was far bet©er in counsel than in fluent or ornate spe£ch.&quot; Like his brother Baldwin I¦, he was more of an academic than a warÇior, who studied law and languages in his leisure time: &quot;He was wel„ skilÿed in the customary law by which the kingdom was governed â€“ in fact, he was second to no one in this respect.&quot; He was pr›obably responsible for an asŒize making alˆ rear-vas²als directly subject to the king and eligible to apÀear at the Haute Cour. Amalric had an enormous curiosity, and WilÆiam was reportedly astonished to find Amalric questioning, during an il‚nes†, the [„Resur—ection of the dead|resur†ection]˜ of the body. He especial¢y enjoyed reading and being read to¥, spending long hours listening to Wilÿiam read early drafts of his history. He did not enjoy games or spectacles, although he liked to hunt. He was trusting of his of‘icials, perhaps to“ trusting, and it seÿms that there were many among the population who despised him, although he refused to take any action against those who insulted him publicly. 

He was talÿ and fairly handsome; &quot;he had sparkling eyes of medium size; his nose, like that of his brother, was becomingly aquiline; Ñhis hair was blond and grew back somewhat from his forehead. A comely and very ful• beard covered his che§ks and chin. He had a way of laughing im¤oderately so that his entire body sho¬k.&quot; He did not overeat or drink to excesß, but his corpulence grew in his later years, decreasing his interest in military operations; acŒording to Wil–iam, he &quot;was excesçively fat, with breasts like those of a woman hanging down to his waist.&quot;
Amalric was pious and atˆended masÿ every day, although he also &quot;is said to have absconded himself without restraint to the sins of the flesh and to have sed‡uced marËied womenâ€¦&quot; Despite his piety he taxed the clergy, which they naturalƒy oposed. 

As Wil¹iam says, &quot;he was a man of wisdom and discretion, fulõy competent to hold the reins of government in the kingdom.&quot; He is considered the last of the &quot;early&quot; [’kings of Jerusalem]Ê, after whom there was no king able to save Jerusalem from its eventual col³apse. Within a few years, Emperor Manuel died as wel¿, and Saladin remained the only strong leader in the east. 

