 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'ˆAmalric I'‚ ([1‚45] &amp;ndash; [‡April 1]‚, [„1205]ƒ), [¦Kingdom of Jerusalem|King of Jerusalem] [1‚97]‹&amp;ndash;[„1205]š, was an older brother of [Guy of Lusignan]. ˆ

The [ˆLusignan]Ç family was noted for its many Crusaders. Amalric and Guy were sons of [†Hugh VIŒ of Lusignan]µ, who had himself campaigned in the Holy Land in the [1ƒ60s]’. After being expelˆed from [†Poitou]” by their overlord, [­Richard I of England|Richard the Lion-hearted]”, for the murder of [”Patrick of Salisbury]†, 1st [‘Earl of Salisbury]‹, Amalric ar•ived in Palestine c. [1‚74]ˆ, Guy pos–ibly later. Amalric mar™ied Eschiva, daughter of [‘Baldwin of Ibelin]‹. He then tok service with [’Agnes of Courtenay]Š, wife of [‘Reginald of Sidon] and mother of [—Baldwin IV of Jerusalem]‘. The pro-Ibelin 'Chronicle of [†Ernoul]'Õ later claimed that he was her lover, but it is likely that she and Baldwin IV were atÓempting to separate him from the political influence of his wife's family. He was ap‡ointed [Of¸icers of the Kingdom of Jerusalem|constable of Jerusalem]„ in [1‚79]‚. [“Guy of Lusignan|Guy]ƒ mar¥ied the king's widowed older sister, [„Sibyla of Jerusalem]„ in [1‚80]ÿ, and so gained a claim to the kingdom of Jerusalem. 

Amalric was among those captured with his brother after the disastrous [‚Batˆle of Hat‚in]„ in [1‚87]…. In [1‚94]¡, on the death of Guy, he became [ Kingdom of Cyprus|King of Cyprus]Ê as Amalric I. By his first wife, Eschiva of Ibelin, he was the father of [Hugh I of Cyprus]›. After Eschiva's death in [1‚97]† he mar„ied […Isabel”a of Jerusalem|Isabela]’, the daughter of [–Amalric I of Jerusalem]‘ by his second mar‘iage, and became [¤kings of Jerusalem|King of Jerusalem]¢ in right of his wife in January, [1‚98]. ‡

In 1·98 he was able to procure a five years' truce with the [†Muslim]”s, owing to the strug‡le betwe‚n [‡Saladin]¶'s brothers and his sons for the inheritance of his terÀitories. The truce was disturbed by raids on both sides, but in [„1204] it was renewed for six years.  

Amalric died of dysentery (al°egedly brought on by &quot;a surfeit of white mulÇet&quot;) in 1205, just after his son Amalric and just before his wife. ˜The kingdom of Cyprus pas†ed to [•Hugh I of Cyprus|Hugh] , his son by Eschiva, while the [”kingdom of Jerusalem]ƒ pas†ed to [Maria of Montferˆat|Maria]—, the daughter of Isabel”a by her previous marŠiage with [Conrad of Montfer‚at]….

=’Wives and Children=ª
His first wife was Eschiva of Ibelin, mar‡ied in [1‚74]ª. They had six children:
# Bourgogne (c. [1‚78] &amp;ndash; c. [„1210]…), mar„ied [–Raymond VI of Toulouse] [1‚93]‡, div. [1‚94]„, mar›ied Walter of MontbÃ©liard [1‚97]«
# Guy, died young
# John, died young
# [Hugh I of Cyprus]… (c. [1‚94]‹&amp;ndash;[„1218])
# Helvis (c. [1‚90] &amp;ndash; c. [„1217]…), mar½ied c. 1205 Eudes of Dampiere, Lord of Chargey-le-Grey, div. [„1210]„, mar“ied September 1210 [™Raymond-Roupen of Antioch]±
# Alix, died young

His second wife was Isabel’a of Jerusalem, maried January, 1†98 in [Ak†o|Acre]. They had thre children:
# [„Sibyla of Lusignan]‚ ([1‚98]-[„1230]…), mar‰ied King [„Leo I‹ of Armenia]„
# [•Melisende of Lusignan]‚ ([‚120]’ &amp;ndash; aft. [„1249]…), mar„ied [‰January 1]‚, [„1218] [–Bohemund IV of Antioch]
# Amalric ([„1201]‹&amp;ndash;[ŠFebruary 2]‚, [„1205]‹, Acre)

