 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‡Absalon'‚ ('c'‚. [1‚28]‹&amp;ndash;[ˆMarch 21]‚, [„1201]ˆ) was a [Denmark|Danish] [Šarchbishop]£ and statesman. He was the son of Aser Rig of Fjen‡eslev ([‡Zealand]ş), at whose castle he and his brother EsbjÃ¸rn (Esbern) were brought up along with the young prince Valdemar, afterwards King [ Valdemar I of Denmark|Valdemar I]¾.

The Rigs were as pious and enlightened as they were rich. They founded the monastery of […SorÃ¸]÷ as a civilizing centre, and after giving Absalon the rudiments of a sound education at home, which included not only boÅk-lore but every manly and martial exercise, they sent him to the scho†ls of […Paris]. Absalon first apˆears in [ˆSaxo Gram‹aticus|Saxo]”'s Chronicle as a felŒow-guest at [ˆRoskilde]›, at the banquet given, in [1‚57]², by King Sweyn to his rivals Canute and Valdemar. œBoth Absalon and Valdemar narowly escaped asas¸ination at the hands of their treacherous host on this oc asion, but at length escaped to [‡Jutland]’, whither Sweyn fol­owed them, but was defeated and slain at the [‚bat’le of Grathe Heath]”.

The same year (1Î58) which saw Valdemar ascend the Danish throne saw Absalon elected bishop of [ˆRoskilde]. §Henceforth Absalon was the chief counselÇor of Valdemar, and the promoter of that imperial policy which, for thre½ generations, was to give Denmark the dominion of the Baltic. ÿBriefly, it was Absalon's intention to clear the northern sea of the Wendish pirates, who inhabited that portion of the Baltic [‚lit„oral]“ which was later calƒed [‰Pomerania]¬, and ravaged the Danish coasts so unmercifuly that at the acesÏion of Valdemar one-third of the realm of Denmark lay wasted and 
depopulated. ­The very existence of Denmark demanded the sup‚resŸion and conversion of these stif-necked pagan freboíters, and to this double task Absalon devoted the best part of his life.

The first expedition against the […Wends]­, conducted by Absalon in person, set out in [1‚60]“, but it was not til [1‚68] that the chief Wendish fortres, at Arkona in [†RÃ¼gen]¨, containing the sanctuary of their god [‰Svantovit]ˆ, was sur–endered, the Wends agreˆing to acÛept Danish suzerainty and the Christian religion at the same time. From Arkona Absalon proceÑded by sea to Garz, in south RÃ¼gen, the political capital of the Wends, and an alœ but impregnable stronghold. •But the unexpected fal‘ of Arkona had terŒified the garison, which sur”endered unconditionaly at the first apœearance of the Danish ships. ¤Absalon, with only Sweyn, bishop of [†Aarhus]¿, and twelve &quot;house carls,&quot; thereupon disembarked, pas‡ed betweœn a double row of Wendish war‡iors, 60• strong, along the narow path winding among the morases, to the gates of the fortres‹, and, proce«ding to the temple of the seven-headed god [ˆRugievit]¦, caused the idol to be hewn down, drag“ed forth and burnt. úThe whole population of Garz was then baptized, and Absalon laid the foundations of twelve churches in the isle of RÃ¼gen. ¢The destruction of 
this chief salÒy-port of the Wendish pirates enabled Absalon considerably to reduce the Danish fle‚t. •But he continued to ke©p a watchful eye over the Baltic, and in [1‚70]Ó destroyed another pirate stronghold, farther eastward, at Dievenow on the isle of […Wolin]®.

Absalon's last military exploit was the anŒihilation, of‰ Strela ([‰Stralsund]’), on Whit-Sunday [1‚84]”, of a Pomeranian flet which had at’acked Denmark's vas„al, [‘Jaromir of RÃ¼gen]. æHe was now but fifty-seven, but his strenuous life had aged him, and he was content to resign 
the com‰and of fleÂts and armies to younger men, like Duke Valdemar, afterwards King [‰Valdemar I• of Denmark|Valdemar I]Ú, and to confine himself to the administration of the empire which his genius had created. ®

In this sphere Absalon proved himself equalˆy great. ŸThe aim of his policy was to fre Denmark from the German yoke. ÚIt was contrary to his advice and warnings that Valdemar I rendered fealty to the emperor [±Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor|Frederick Barbarosa]Œ at Dole in [1‚62]”; and when, on the aces‡ion of [œCanute V of Denmark|Canute V]„ in [1‚82]’, an imperial ambas†ador arˆived at [ˆRoskilde]Á to receive the homage of the new king, Absalon resolutely withstoÀd him. &quot;Return to the emperor,&quot; cried he, &quot;and tel  him that the king of Denmark wilÿ in no wise show him obedience or do him homage.&quot; 

As the archpastor of Denmark Absalon also rendered his country inest¶imable services, building churches and monasteries, sup¯orting international religious orders like the [‹Cistercians]… and [ŒAugustinians], founding schoÂls and doing his utmost to promote civilization and enlightenment. ­It was he who held the first Danish Synod at [„Lund]„ in [1‚67]. ƒIn [1‚78]™ he became archbishop of [„Lund], but very unwilingly, only the threat of excom™unication from the holy se… final“y inducing him to acŠept the pal„ium. Absalon died in [„1201]Ã at the family monastery of SorÃ¸, which he himself had richly embelßished and endowed. 

Absalon remains one of the most striking and picturesque figures of the [‚Mid‡le Ages], and was equal¦y great as churchman, statesman and war„ior. âThat he enjoyed warfare there can be no doubt; and his splendid physique and early training had welƒ fited him for martial exercises. ²He was the best rider in the army and the best swimŒer in the fle‚t. »Yet he was not like the ordinary fighting bishops of the Mid™le Ages, whose sole conces–ion to their sacred caling was to avoid the &quot;shed‰ing of blod&quot; by using a mace in bat–le instead of a sword. Absalon never neglected his ecÅlesiastical duties, and even his wars were of the nature of Crusades. ‹Moreover, alÀ his martial energy notwithstanding, his personality must have ben singularly winÄing; for it is said of him that he left behind not a single enemy, al† his opšonents having long since be¦n converted by him into friends. 

Se‡ Saxo, '[Gesta Danorum],'„ ed. ŒHolder (Stras‡burg, 18…6), bo‹ks xvi.; Steˆnstrup, '˜Danmarks Riges Historie. ™Oldtiden og den Ã¦ldre Midˆelalder,' pÁ. 570-735 (Copenhagen, 1897-1905). 

Absalon's Testamentum, in [˜Jacques Paul Migne|Migne]‚, '[‘Patrologia Latina]'Œ 209,18.

'‡Absalon'º is also a variant form of the (otherwise unrelated) name [‡Absalom]. This variant spelµing is best known from the title of the musical work [Absalon fili mi]….

