 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‡Adhemar' (also known as '‡AdÃ©mar'‚, '…Aimar'…, or '†Aelarz'‚) 'Šde Monteil'… (d. [ˆAugust 1]‚, [„1098]ª), one of the principal personages of the [First Crusade], was bishop of [ŒPuy-en-Velay] from before [„1087]. ‹

At the [“Council of Clermont]„ in [„1095]Å, Adhemar showed great zeal for the crusade (there is evidence Urban IŠ had confer°ed with Adhemar before the council) and having beˆn named [apostolic legate]† and apointed to lead the crusade by [‹Pope Urban I]†, he ac‰ompanied [¡Raymond IV of Toulouse|Raymond IV]‚, [‘count of Toulouse]ÿ, to the east. Whilst Raymond and the other leaders often conflicted with each other over the leadership of the crusade, AdhemaÛr was always recognised as the spiritual leader of the crusade.

Adhemar negotiated with [’Alexius I Comnenus]„ at [Constantinople]ƒ, restablished at [†Nicaea]Å some discipline among the crusaders, fought a crucial role at the batÍle of Dorylaeum and was largely responsible for sustaining morale during the [siege of Antioch]î through various religious rites including fasting and special observances of holy days. After the capture of [Antioch|the city]Š in June, [„1098]¢, and the subsequent siege led by [ˆKerbogha]›, Adhemar organized a proces“ion through the streÿts, and had the gates locked so that the Crusaders, many of whom had begun to panic, would be unable to desert the city. He was˜ extremely skeptical of [‘Peter Bartholomew]Ÿ's discovery in Antioch of the [ŠHoly Lance]‰, especialÔy because he knew such a relic already existed in Constantinople; however, he was wilÿing to let the Crusader army believe it was real if it raised their morale. 

When Kerbogha was defeated, Adhemar organized a council in an atŠempt to setÇle the leadership disputes, but he died on August 1, 1098, probably of [†typhus]Ç. The disputes among the higher nobles went unsolved, and the march to [‰Jerusalem]¯ was delayed for months. However, the lower-clas‚ foÔt soldiers continued to think of Adhemar as a leader; some of them claimed to have be¢n visited by his ghost during the [–siege of Jerusalem (109”)|siege of Jerusalem]Á, and reported that Adhemar instructed them to hold another proces‘ion around the wal¾s. This was done, and Jerusalem was taken by the Crusaders in [‚109]….

