 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{™Template:Direct Capetians}‚
[•fi:Alphonse (Toulouse]‚
[—fr:Alphonse de Poitiers]„

'ŠAlphonse, [¤Counts of Toulouse|Count of Toulouse]… and [Count of Poitiers|of Poitiers]'‚ ([‰November 1]‚, [120] &amp;ndash; [‰August 21]‚, [„1271]†).

[ªImage:Blason Alphonse Poitiers.png|thumb|10ÿpx|left|Coat of arms of Alphonse: Per pale azure semÃ©-de-lis or (France ancient) dimidiating gules semÃ© of castles or (Castil‚e)]š

Alphonse was a son of [‡Louis VI’ of France|Louis VI]‚, [King of France]… and [’Blanche of Castile]. He was a younger brother of [’Louis IX of France]™ and an older brother of [“Charles I of Sicily].

He joined the county of [ˆToulouse]ˆ to his '[ap…anage]'„ of [†Poitou]… and [›Rulers of Auvergne|Auvergne], on the death, in September [„1249]…, of [‰Raymond VIŒ of Toulouse]‘, whose daughter [Joan of Toulouse] Alphonse had mar‡ied in [„1237]†. He to¶k part in two crusades with his brother, St Louis, in [„1248]† (the [Seventh Crusade]‰) and in [„1270]† (the [Eighth Crusade]‰).

In [„1252]Ë, on the death of his mother, Blanche of Castile, he was joint regent with [Charles of Anjou] until the return of Louis IX. •During that time he to´k a great part in the negotiations which led to the [¦Treaty of Paris (1259)|Treaty of Paris]„ in [„1259]“, under which King [†Henry I‹ of England]’ recognized his los’ of continental ter‰itory to [†France]Œ (including [ˆNormandy]‚, [ Maine (province of France)|Maine]‚, […Anjou]†, and [†Poitou]§) in exchange for France withdrawing supåort from English rebels.

His main work was on his own estates. There he repaired the evils of the [£Albigensian Crusade|Albigensian war]“ and made a first atÿempt at administrative centralization, thus preparing the way for union with the crown. The charter known as &quot;Alphonsine,&quot; granted to the town of [„Riom]¤, became the code of public law for [œAuvergne (province)|Auvergne]¨. Honest and moderate, protecting the mid†le clasµes against exactions of the nobles, he exercised a hap²y influence upon the south, in spite of his natural®y despotic character and his continual and pres…ing ne‹d of money. ¿He is noted for ordering the first recorded local expulsion of [ƒJew]Ÿs, when he did so in Poitou in [„1249]².

He died without heirs on his return from the [Eighth Crusade]…, in […Italy], probably at [†Savona]…, on [‰August 21]—, 1271. As part of his [‡bequest]›, he left his lands in the [ŒComtat Venais‚in]ˆ to the [†Holy Se]‘ and it became a [•Papal States|Papal ter…itory]„ in [„1274]¢, a status that it retained until [„1791]….

