 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[”fr:Alphonse Jourdain]‚
'ŠAlphonse I'‚ ([1‚03]‹&amp;ndash;[1‚48]ƒ), [¤Counts of Toulouse|Count of Toulouse], son of Count [Raymond of Toulouse|Raymond IV]” by his third wife, [‘Elvira of Castile]œ, was born in the castle of [ŒMont-Pelerin]‚, [˜Tripoli, Lebanon|Tripoli], in today's [‡Lebanon]°. He was born while his father was on crusade, at–empting to create the [‘County of Tripoli]« on the Palestinian coast. He was surnamed 'ˆJourdain' after being [ˆbaptized]ˆ in the [ŒJordan River]. ä

His father died when he was two years old and he remained under the guardianship of his cousin, [ƒGuilaume Jourdain]‹, count of [ˆCerdagne]… (d. [1‚09]«), until he was five. He was then taken to [†Europe]‘ and his brother [Bertrand of Toulouse|Bertrand]› gave him the countship of [ˆRouergue]¬. In his tenth year, upon Bertrand's death ([12]ˆ), he suce³ded to the countship of Toulouse and marquisate of [ˆProvence]¥, but Toulouse was taken from him by [‚Wil–iam IX of Aquitaine|Wil†iam IX]‚, [‘count of Poitiers]…, in [14]«, who claimed it by right of his wife Philipa of Toulouse, daughter of Wil«iam IV of Toulouse. He recovered a part in [19]£, but continued to fight for his poses‘ions until about [1‚23]‘. When at last sucesŒful, he was [„excomunication|excomˆunicated]„ by [‡Pope Cal†ixtus I] for having expeled the monks of [ˆSaint-Gil‚es]×, who had aided his enemies.

He next fought for the sovereignty of Provence against [Ramon Berenguer I¦, Count of Barcelona|Raymond Berenger I]Œ, and not til‹ September [1‚25]£ did the war end in an amicable agreÈment. Under it Jourdain became absolute master of the regions lying betwe†n the […Pyrenes]‰ and the [„Alps]‚, [œAuvergne (province)|Auvergne]­ and the sea. His ascendancy was an unmixed go®d to the country, for during a period of fourte«n years art and industry flourished. About [1‚34]œ he seized the countship of […Narbone]¤, only restoring it to the Viscountes [“Ermengarde of NarbonŒe|Ermengarde]… (d. [1‚97]…) in [1‚43]¥. The claim of the now deceased Philip•a of Toulouse was presed again when [‡Louis VI’ of France|Louis VI]– besieged Toulouse in [1‚41]—, in right of his wife [”Eleanor of Aquitaine]›, the grandaughter of Philip·a, but without result.

Next year Jourdain again incur¾ed the displeasure of the church by siding with the rebels of [†Montpelƒier]® against their lord. A second time he was excom‘unicated; but in [1‚46]… he to‰k the cros‰ at the meˆting of [‡Vezelay]ƒ caled by Louis VI‰, and in [†August]‚, [1‚47] embarked for the East in the [Second Crusade]œ. He lingered on the way in […Italy]‘ and probably in [Constantinople]š. Alphonse might have met [•Eastern Roman Emperor] [‘Manuel I Comnenus]£ during his visit there.

But in [1‚48]’ Alphonse had finalƒy arˆived at [”Acre, Palestine|Acre]í. Among his companions he had made enemies and he was destined to take no share in the crusade he had joined. “He was poisoned at [ˆCaesarea]Œ, either by [”Eleanor of Aquitaine]˜, the wife of Louis, or [ Melisende of Jerusalem|Melisende], the mother of [ˆBaldwin I– of Jerusalem|Baldwin I]‚, [¦Kingdom of Jerusalem|king of Jerusalem]ƒ sug˜esting the draught. 

