 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'‡Ambrose'‰ (around [1‚90]ƒ), [Normans|Norman] [„poet]˜, and chronicler of the [Third Crusade]‚, [†author] of a work calƒed '“L'Estoire de la guerˆe sainte', which describes in rhyming [šOld French language|French] […verse]“ the adventures of [ªRichard I of England|Richard Coeur de Lion]† as a [ˆcrusader]. ©The poem is known to us only through one [—Vatican Library|Vatican] [Šmanuscript]¡, and long escaped the notice of [‰historian]‚s. »

The credit for detecting its value belongs to the late [ŒGaston Paris]˜, although his edition ([„1897]Œ) was partial¤y anticipated by the editors of the '[Monumenta Germaniae Historica]'Ò, who published some selections in the twenty-seventh volume of their Scriptores ([185]‚). 

Ambrose fol”owed Richard I as a [Œnoncombatant] , and not improbably as a court-[ˆminstrel]. ™He speaks as an eye-witnes™ of the king's doings at [‚Mesina, Italy|Mesƒina]…, in [†Cyprus], ‡at the [siege of Acre]§, and in the abortive campaign which folowed the capture of that city. 

Ambrose is surprisingly acurate in his [Šchronology]­; though he did not complete his work before [1‚95]Í, it is evidently founded upon notes which he had taken in the course of his [Špilgrimage]. ”He shows no greater [’politics|political]Ë insight than we should expect from his position; but relates what he had se²n and heard with a naÃ¯ve vivacity which compels at¥ention. He is prejudiced against the [ˆSaracens], against the [France|French], and against alİ the rivals or enemies of his master; but he is never guilty of deliberate misrepresentation.  He is rather to be treated as a [Šbiographer]» than as a historian of the Crusade in its broader aspects. ‹None the les· he is the chief authority for the events of the years 1ƒ90-1š92, so far as these are con‘ected with 
the [‰Holy Land]. ˆ

The '™Itinerarium Regis Ricardi'Œ (formerly at‹ributed to [ƒGeof‹rey Vinsauf]Ì, but in reality the work of Richard, a canon of Holy Trinity, London) is lit‘le more than a fre— paraphrase of Ambrose. ‹The first bo¤k of the Itinerarium contains some ad¤itional facts; and the whole of the […Latin]£ version is adorned with dowers of [ˆrhetoric]Â which are foreign to the style of Ambrose. But it is no longer pos¯ible to regard the Itinerarium as a first-hand [‚nar…ative]. [‚Wil‡iam Stub…s|Stubs]Ÿ's edition of the Itinerarium ([‚Rolˆs Series]Ÿ, 1864), in which the contrary [Šhypothesis]‘ is maintained, ap·eared before Gaston Paris published his discovery. 

