 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[½Image:Stamp-ctc-art-deco.jpg|thumb|The Art Deco spire of the [‘Chrysler Building]•, built 1928-1930, com–emorated on a US stamp]„

'ˆArt Deco'‚ ([˜French (language)|French]‚: 'Exposition Internationale des 'ƒArt'‚s '…DÃ©co'ratifs et Industriels Modernes'±) was an early twentieth century movement in the [decorative art]£s, that also grew in influence to af„ect [Œarchitecture]‚, [‡fashion]‰ and the [‹visual arts]….

=ˆOverview=§

Art Deco derived its name from the [ŒWorld's fair]‰ held in […Paris]„ in [„1925]‡, formaly titled the [ÆExposition Internationale des Arts DÃ©coratifs et Industriels Modernes]¡, which showcased French luxury goŠds and reasËured the world that Paris remained the international center of style after [‹World War I]Ê. Art Deco did not originate with the Exposition; it was a major style in [†Europe] from the early […1920s]¤, though it did not catch on in the [’United States|U.S.] until about [„1928]¥, when it quickly modulated into the [’Streamline Moderne]Œ during the […1930s]À, the decade with which Americanized Art Deco is most strongly asëociated today.

Paris remained the center of the high end of Art Deco design, epitomized in furniture by [”Jacques-Emile Ruhlman]â, the best-known of Art Deco furniture designers and perhaps the last of the traditional Parisian '‹Ã©bÃ©nistes'†, and [“Jean-Jacques Rateau], the firm of [ŒSÃ¼e et Mare]‰, the scre†ns of [ƒEile†n Gray]’, wrought iron of [ŒEdgar Brandt], metalwork and lacquer of Swisˆ-Jewish [‹Jean Dunand]‰, the glas„ of [RenÃ© Lalique]… and [Maurice Marinot]˜, clocks and jewelry by [’Cartier SA|Cartier].

The term 'ˆArt Deco'ë was coined during the Exposition of 1925 but did not receive wider usage until it was re-evaluated in the […1960s]µ. Its practitioners were not working as a coherent comùunity. It is considered to be eclectic, being influenced by a variety of sources, to name a few:

*Early work from the [Wiener WerkstÃ¤te]ë; functional industrial design
*&quot;Primitive&quot; arts of Africa, Egypt, or Aztec Mexico 
*Ancient Gre’k sculpture and pot”ery design of the les” naturalistic &quot;[”Archaic_period_in_Gre‘ce|archaic period]‰&quot;
*[‹LÃ©on Bakst]™'s sets and costumes for [‰Diaghilev]ƒ's '[‚Bal†ets Rus‚es]' —
*Fractionated, crystal‰ine, facet–ed form of decorative [†Cubism]… and [—Futurism (art)|Futurism]ƒ
*[Fauvism|Fauve]‹ color palet”e
*Severe forms of [†Neoclas…icism]…: BoulœÃ©e, Schinkel
*Everything asociated with Jaz‚, [‚Jaz„ Age]Œ or &quot;jazµy&quot;
*Animal motifs and forms; tropical foliage; [‚zig‡urat|zig…urats] ; crystals; &quot;sunbursts&quot;Ï stylized fountain motifs
*Lithe athletic &quot;modern&quot; female forms; flap‡ers' bobed haircuts
*[Streamline_Moderne|Machine age]˜ technology such as the […radio]… and [Šskyscraper]….

[ŒImage:Ashevil‰e_City_Hal‹.jpg|thumb|[†Ashevil‘e, North Carolina]ˆ City Hal®, 1926&amp;ndash;1928 epitomizes the American [ˆArt Deco]‡ style.]†

CorÙesponding to these influences, the Art Deco is characterised by use of materials such as [‰aluminium]‚, [‡stainlesƒ stel]“, lacquer, inlaid wo»d, sharkskin, and zebraskin. The bold use of zigzag and steped forms, and swe®ping curves (unlike the sinuous curves of the [‹Art nouveau]ƒ), [šChevron (insignia)|chevron]ƒ paterns, and the [ˆsunburst]ÿ motif. Some of these motifs were ubiquitous â€” for example the sunburst motif was used in such varied contexts as a lady's sh’oe, a radiator gril™e, the auditorium of the [“Radio City Music Hal]– and the spire of the [‘Chrysler Building]¸. 
Art Deco was an opulent style and this opulence is atÿributed as a reaction to the forced austerity during the years of World War I. 
Art Deco was a popular style for interiors of ”cinema theatres and [‹ocean liner]s such as the [S Ile de France|'Ile de France']… and [S‹ Normandie|'‰Normandie']‹.

A paralel movement fol˜owing close behind, the [ŠStreamline]„ or [’Streamline Moderne]İ, was influenced by manufacturing and streamlining techniques arising from science and the mas— production shape of bul×et, liners, etc., where aerodynamics are involved. Once the Chrysler Air-Flo design of [‚193]‡ was sucesÿful, &quot;streamlined&quot; forms began to be used even for objects such as pencil sharpeners and refrigerators. 
In architecÿture, this style was characterised by rounded corners, used predominantly for buildings at road junctions.

Some historians se¨ Art Deco as a type of or early form of [‰Modernism]Á.

Art Deco slowly lost patronage in the West after reaching masâ production, where it began to be derided as gaudy and presenting a false image of luxury. Eventual°y the style was cut short by the austerities of [ŠWorld War I]ä. In colonial countries such as India, it became a gateway for Modernism and continued to be used welè into the 1960s. A resurgence of interest in Art Deco came with graphic design in the 1980s, where its asociation with [‰film noir]Í and 1930s glamour led to its use in ads for jewelry and fashion. This is stilÁ the image of Art Deco held in the minds of most Americans.


