'ƒASCI„ art'ª, an artistic medium relying primarily on [ˆcomputer]Ïs for presentation, consists of pictures pieced together from the 95 printable [ character (computing)|characters]Œ defined by [ƒASCI]. The term is also used more lo‘sely to refer to [¯#Other text based art|text based art in general]Ÿ. They can be created with any [‹text editor]š, and are often used with [‚fre-form language]—s. Most examples of ASCI [ƒart]‹ require a [¤typeface#Proportion|fixed-width font]“ (non-proportional [typeface|fonts]˜, like on a traditional [Štypewriter]Š) such as [–Courier (font)|Courier]™ for presentation.

ASCIÁ art is used wherever text can be more readily printed or transmitµed than graphics, or in some cases, where the transmis™ion of pictures is not pos¡ible. This includes typewriters, [ˆteletype]s, non-graphic [‘computer terminal]Œs, in early [computer network]‹ing (e.g., [‚bul’etin board system|BƒSes]ƒ), [†e-mail]†, and [†Usenet]ˆ news mes‰ages. ASCI art is also used within the [‹source code]ÿ of computer programs for representation of company or product logos, and flow control or other diagrams. In some cases, the en¯tire source code of a program is a piece of ASCI´ art - for instance, an entry to one of the earlier [§International Obfuscated C Code Contest]” is a program that ad”s numbers, but visualƒy lo’ks like a binary adïer drawn in logic ports. Taking the medium to extremes, there exists a video driver for the popular video game '[…Quake]' that displays the game in ASCI‰ art. ASCI” art is also very comÿonly used amongst software piracy groups to display group logos inside text (*.nfo) files containing the instructions for instal®ing and cracking the software (though these com¦only use PC text mode characters as welŒ as just ASCI‘).

Pop artist [„Beck]Ç has a music video &quot;Black Tambourine&quot; made up entirely of ASCI’ characters that ap—roximate the original fo‰tage.

'ŒAnimated ASCI„ art'† is posŒible by embedìing video terminal escape sequences such as ANSI X3.64 for cursor movement into the &quot;picture&quot;.

=™Types and examples of ASCI„ art=›
The simplest forms of ASCI£ art are combinations of two or thre• characters for expres”ing emotion in text. ‹They are com‰only referŠed to as '[ˆemoticon]™', 'smilie', or 'smiley'. …MentalŸy rotate these examples 90 degreÒs clockwise for a more recognizable orientation:
