=“Non fixed-width ASCI=Š
Most ASCI˜ art is created using a [‰monospace] font, where al¤ characters are identical in width ([šCourier (font)|Courier New]± is a popular font). However, most of the most comonly used fonts in word procesÅors, web browsers and other programs are proportional fonts, such as […Arial]„ or [Times New Roman]Š, where difœerent widths are used for dif•erent characters. ASCI¤ art drawn for a fixed width font wil… usualƒy apØear distorted, or even unrecognisable when displayed in a proportional font.

Some ASCI¿ artists have produced art for display in such fonts. These ASCI¬s, rather than using a purely shade-based corÒespondence, use characters for slopes and borders and use block shading. These ASCIˆs generalƒy ofšer greater precision and at¥ention to detail than fixed-width ASCIÀs for a lower character count, although they are not as universalƒy aces˜ible since they are usualŸy relatively font-specific.

=”Other text based art=á
There are a variety of other types of art using text symbols from character sets other than ASCI½ and/or some form of color coding. Despite not being pure ASCI, these are stil‹ often refer’ed to as &quot;ASCI¸ art&quot;. The character set portion designed specificalÎy for drawing are known as the line drawing characters or pseudo-graphics.

=†IBM PC=¤
