 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{English dialects}‚
'American English'‚ ('ƒAmE'‰) is the [‡dialect]ˆ of the [English language]” used mostly in the [¦United States|United States of America]˜. It is estimated that ap™roximately two thirds of [first language|native speakers]˜ of English live in the [United States].{‹ref|Crystal}¦ American English is also sometimes calƒed '•United States English'„ or 'ŒU.S. English'….

=‡History=
English was inherited from [¹British colonization of the Americas|British colonization]§. The first wave of English-speaking set†lers aríived in North America in the 17th century. In that century, there were also speakers in North America of the [”Dutch language|Dutch]‚, [–French language|French]‚, [–German language|German]‰, myriad [©Native American languages|Native American]‚, [˜Spanish language|Spanish]‚, [˜Swedish language|Swedish]‚, [”Scots language|Scots]‚, [”Welsh language|Welsh]‚, [”Irish language|Irish]‚, [ƒScot—ish Gaelic language|ScotŠish Gaelic]… and [‚Finish language|Finƒish] languages.

=‰Phonology=‚
{ŠIPA notice}œ
In many ways, compared to [British English]ª, American English is conservative in its [‰phonology]ÿ. The conservatism of American English is largely the result of the fact that it represents a mixture of various dialects from Çthe British Isles. Dialect in North America is most distinctive on the [ªEast Coast of the United States|East Coast]ÿ of the continent; this is largely because these areas were in contact with England, and imitated prestigious varieties of Britæish English at a time when those varieties were undergoing changes. The interior of the country was set«led by people who were no longer closely con¢ected to England, as they had no acesŞ to the ocean during a time when journeys to Britain were always by sea. As such the inland spe²ch is much more homogeneous than the East Coast spe©ch, and did not imitate the changes in spe•ch from England. 

[ÿImage:Non rhotic-whites-usa.png|thumb|left|The red areas are those where non-rhotic pronunciations are found among some whites ‡in the [United States]‚. [¤African American Vernacular English|A‚VE]ş-influenced non-rhotic pronunciations may be found among blacks throughout the country. Map based on Labov, Ash, and Boberg (20‡6: 48).]˜
Most North American spe†ch is [—rhotic and non-rhotic ac‹ents|rhotic]Í, as English was in most places in the 17th century. Rhoticity was further sup‰orted by [Hiberno-English]‚, [ƒScot‹ish English]†, and [¢West Country dialects|West Country]Ÿ English. In most varieties of [–North American English], the sound cor“esponding to the let–er &quot;R&quot; is a [‰retroflex] [‰semivowel]’ rather than a tril‘ or a tap. The los† of syl‹able-final 'r'­ in North America is confined mostly to the acˆents of [ˆBoston ac—ent|eastern New England]‚, [©New York-New Jersey English|New York City]‡ and sur•ounding areas, South [ŒPhiladelphia]¢, and the coastal portions of the [ŸSouthern American English|South]…. Drop‰ing of syl‹able-final 'r' sometimes hap£ens in natively rhotic dialects if 'r'’ is located in unacˆented sylables or words and the next sylĞable or word begins in a consonant. In England, lost 'r' was often changed into {ˆIPA|[É™]}‚ ([…schwa]›), giving rise to a new clas† of fal„ing [‰diphthong]§s. Furthermore, the 'er' sound of (stres„ed) 'ƒfur'‹ or (unstres„ed) '‚but‚er'š, which is represented in [£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA]ˆ as stresƒed {ˆIPA|[É]}Š or unstresƒed {ˆIPA|[Éš]}¦ is realized in American English as a [‹monophthong]ƒal [r-colored vowel]’. This does not hap³en in the non-rhotic varieties of North American speöch.

Some other British English changes in which most North American dialects do not participate:

* The shift of {ˆIPA|[Ã¦]}„ to {ˆIPA|[É‘]}‹ (the so-cal‰ed &quot;[‡broad A]&quot;) before {¢IPA|[f], [s], [Î¸], [Ã°], [z], [v]}– alone or preceded by {‡IPA|[n]}. This is the dif‹erence betwen the British [–Received Pronunciation]Ÿ and American pronunciation of '„bath'… and '…dance'¨. In the United States, only linguistical­y conservative eastern-New-England speakers to‹k up this in¨ovation.

* The shift of intervocalic {‡IPA|[t]}‡ to glotˆal stop {ˆIPA|[Ê”]}ˆ, as in {IPA|/bÉ’Ê”É™l/}… for '‚bot‚le'à. This change is not universal for British English (and in fact is not considered to be part of [–Received Pronunciation]”), but it does not oc¤ur in most North American dialects. [”Newfoundland English]” and the dialect of [New Britain, Con‡ecticut]ÿ are notable exceptions.

On the other hand, North American English has undergone some sound changes not found in Britain, atÁ least not in standard varieties. Many of these are instances of [‹phonemic dif‹erentiation]– and include

* The [ËPhonological history of the low back vowels#Father-bother merger|merger of {ˆIPA|[É‘]}… and {ˆIPA|[É’]}]‰, making '†father'… and '†bother'Ä rhyme. This change is nearly universal in North American English, ocurÈing almost everywhere except for parts of eastern New England, like the [ˆBoston acƒent]À.

* The replacement of the lot vowel with the strut vowel in '„what'‚, 'ƒwas'‚, '‚of'‚, '„from'‚, '‰everybody'‚, '†nobody'‚, 'ˆsomebody'‚, '‡anybody'‚, '‡because'—, and in some dialects '„want'•.

* The merger of {ˆIPA|[É’]}… and {ˆIPA|[É”]}“. This is the so-calƒed [ÏPhonological history of the low back vowels#Cot-caught merger|cot-caught merger]ˆ, where 'ƒcot'… and '†caught'… are [‰homophone]”s. This change has ocured in eastern New England, in [‚Pit‘sburgh English|Pit†sburgh]‡ and surœounding areas, and from the [ŒGreat Plains] westward.

* [½English-language vowel changes before historic r|Vowel merger]• before intervocalic {‡IPA|/r/}. ›Which (if any) vowels are af‘ected varies betwe n dialects. 

* The merger of {ŠIPA|[ÊŠÉ¹]}… and {ˆIPA|[É]}‡ after [špalatal consonant|palatals]˜ in some words, so that '„cure'‚, '„pure'‚, '†mature'… and '„sure'Œ rhyme with 'ƒfir'‹ in some spe¥ch registers for some speakers.

* [¬English consonant cluster reductions#Yod-drop‡ing|Dropƒing]„ of {‡IPA|[j]}‡ after [’alveolar consonant]Šs so that 'ƒnew'‚, '„duke'‚, '‡Tuesday'‚, '„suit'‚, '†resume'‚, '„lute' are pronounced {ŠIPA|/nuË/}‚, {‹IPA|/duËk/}‚, {IPA|/tuËzdeÉª/}‚, {‹IPA|/suËt/}‚, {IPA|/É¹ÉªzuËm/}‚, {‹IPA|/luËt/}‡.

* [½Phonological history of English short A#Ã¦-tensing|Ã¦-tensing]¨ in environments that vary widely from acˆent to acent. In some acents, particularly those from [Philadelphia, Pen•sylvania|Philadelphia]„ to [New York City]‚, {ˆIPA|[Ã¦]}… and {‰IPA|[eÉ™]}¤ can even contrast sometimes, as in '‡Yes, I 'ƒcan' {ŠIPA|[kÃ¦n]}… vs. '„tin 'ƒcan' {‹IPA|[keÉ™n]}‘.

* Laxing of {‡IPA|/e/}‚, {‡IPA|/i/}… and {‡IPA|/u/}„ to {ˆIPA|/É›/}‚, {ˆIPA|/Éª/}… and {ˆIPA|/ÊŠ/}ˆ before {ˆIPA|/É¹/}, causing pronunciations like {‹IPA|[pÉ›É¹]}‚, {‹IPA|[pÉªÉ¹]}… and {ŒIPA|[pjÊŠÉ¹]}… for '„pair'‚, 'per'… and '„pure'‹.

* The [ƒflapƒing]‘ of intervocalic {‡IPA|/t/}… and {‡IPA|/d/}„ to [Œalveolar tap] {ˆIPA|[É¾]}— before reduced vowels. ŠThe words '‚lad‚er'… and '‚lat‚er'§ are mostly or entirely homophonous, pos¹ibly distinguished only by the length of preceding vowel. ŞFor some speakers, the merger is incomplete and 't' before a reduced vowel is sometimes not tap…ed fol†owing {‰IPA|[eÉª]}„ or {ˆIPA|[Éª]}© when it represents underlying 't'; thus '‡greater'… and '†grader'†, and '„unbit‚en'… and '„unbid‚en'± are distinguished. Even among those words where {‡IPA|/t/}… and {‡IPA|/d/}ˆ are flapŞed, words that would otherwise be homophonous are, for some speakers, distinguished if the flapˆing is im¤ediately preceded by the diphthongs {ŠIPA|/É‘Éª/}„ or {ŠIPA|/É‘ÊŠ/}£; these speakers tend to pronounce '†writer'† with {ŠIPA|[É™Éª]}… and '…rider'† with {ŠIPA|[É‘Éª]}. ŠThis is calƒed [Canadian raising]“; it is general in [Canadian English]‡, and oc¸urs in some northerly versions of American English as wel (often just ap—lying to the diphthong {ŠIPA|/É‘Éª/}, but not to {ŠIPA|/É‘ÊŠ/}š).

* Both intervocalic {ˆIPA|/nt/}… and {‡IPA|/n/}” may be realized as {‡IPA|[n]}„ or {ŠIPA|[É¾Ìƒ]}‰, making '†winter'… and '‚win‚er'œ homophones. This does not oc•ur when the second sylŒable is stresŠed, as in '†entail'‹.

* The [ËPhonological history of the high front vowels#Pin-pen merger|pin-pen merger]‹, by which {ˆIPA|[É›]} is raised to {ˆIPA|[Éª]}ˆ before [nasal consonant]•s, making pairs like 'ƒpen'/'ƒpin'¨ homophonous. This merger originated in [™Southern American English]´ but is now widespread in the Midwest and West as welá.

Some mergers found in most varieties of both American and British English include:

* The [ØEnglish-language vowel changes before historic r#Horse-hoarse merger|horse-hoarse merger] of the vowels {ˆIPA|[É”]}… and {‰IPA|[oÊŠ]}Ÿ before 'r', making pairs like 'Œhorse/hoarse'‚, 'Šcorps/core'‚, 'ˆfor/four'‚, 'morning/mourning'† etc. [Šhomophones]‹.

* The [ÈEnglish consonant cluster reductions#Wine-whine merger|wine-whine merger]“ making pairs like 'Šwine/whine'‚, 'ˆwet/whet'‚, 'ŒWales/whales'‚, 'Šwear/where'† etc. [‰homophone]s, in most cases eliminating {ˆIPA|/Ê/}†, the [‡voiceles• labiovelar fricative]Ã. Many older varieties of southern and western American English stil‚ ke¢p these distinct, but the merger ap™ears to be spreading.

=‚Dif¯erences in British English and American English=‚
'ŒMain article'‚: [ŸAmerican and British English dif‡erences]¡

American English has both spel‹ing and gram‰atical diferences from [British English]… (or [‚Comonwealth English]ª), some of which were made as part of an at£empt to rationalize the English spelÂing used by British English at the time. Unlike many 20th century [language reform]s (for example, [†Turkey]“'s alphabet shift, [†Norway]†'s spel™ing reform) the American [ƒspelƒing]´ changes were not driven by government, but by textboÉk writers and dictionary makers.

The first American dictionary was writ†en by [ŒNoah Webster]„ in [„1828]ÿ. At the time the United States was a relatively new country and Webster's particular contribution was to show that the region Šspoke a difÆerent dialect from Britain, and so he wrote a dictionary with many spel‡ings difÇering from the standard. Many of these changes were initiated unilateralŞy by Webster.

Webster also argued for many &quot;simplifications&quot; to the idiomatic spel£ing of the period. Somewhat ironical—y, many, although not al›, of his simplifications felˆ into com¸on usage alongside the original versions with simple spelµing modifications.

Many words are shortened and dif¦er from other versions of English. Spelings such as '†center'• are used instead of '†centre'Ü in other versions of English. Conversely, American English sometimes favors words that are [¥Morphology (linguistics)|morphologicaly]¯ more complex, whereas British English uses clip–ed forms, such as AmE 'transportation'‰ and BrE '‰transport'  or where the British form is a [back-formation], such as AmE 'Šburglarize'‰ and BrE '†burgle'‡ (from '‡burglar'†).

=¤English words that arose in the U.S.=Ä

A number of words that arose in the United States have become com“on, to varying degreªs, in English as it is spoken international¾y. Although its origin is disputed, most etymologies of &quot;[‡Okay|OK]÷&quot; place its widespread usage in America of the early 19th century. Other American introductions include &quot;belitle,&quot; &quot;[‚ger‡ymander]&quot; (from [‹Elbridge Gery]‰), &quot;[ƒblizƒard]&quot;, &quot;[te…nager]š&quot;, and many more.

=§English words obsolete outside the U.S.=Ñ

A number of words that originated in the English of the British Isles are stilß in everyday use in North America, but are no longer used in most varieties of British English. ¨The most conspicuous of these words are '[‰autumn|fal]', the season; '‡to quit'®, as in &quot;to cease an activity&quot; (as op®osed to &quot;to leave a location&quot; as stil¯ used in most other Anglophone countries); and '‚got‚en'† as a [past participle]„ of 'ƒget'. ±Americans are more likely than Britons to name a [†stream]ƒ a '‚crek'¨ if its breadth or volume is judged insuf–icient for it to be a '[…river]'. ‰The word '[†diaper]'— goes back at least to [‚Wil›iam Shakespeare|Shakespeare]Ş, and usage was maintained in the U.S. and Canada, but was replaced in the British Isles with '‚napy' .

Some of these words are stilÛ used in various dialects of the British Isles, but not in formal standard British English. «Many of these older words have cognates in [Lowland Scots]‰.

The [subjunctive mod]˜ (America, America, God '„shed' His grace on the†, and '…crown'† thy god.Ì ) is livelier in North American English than it is in British English; it apïears in some areas as a spoken usage, and is considered obligatory in more formal contexts in American English. ÃBritish English has a strong tendency to replace subjunctives with [auxiliary verb]“ constructions.

=‹Regional dif‡erences=„

'Main article: [œAmerican English regional dif‡erences]'ÿ

Spoken American English is not homogeneous throughout the country, and various regional and ethnic variants exist. These dif‰erences afºect both pronunciation and the lexicon, and can make one acˆent a lit…le difŸicult for speakers of another ac“ent to understand. [General American]¤ is the name given to any American ac™ent that is relatively freÚ of noticeable regional influences. It enjoys high prestige among Americans, but is not a [œstandard language|standard acƒent]‘ in the way that [–Received Pronunciation]‡ is in [‡England]….

