 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[˜Image:Al_Spalding_Basebal©.jpg|right|thumb|Al Spalding's sporting goªds company made a lasting impact on basebal.]‚
'ˆAlbert Goƒdwil‰ Spalding'‚ ([ˆByron, Il‹inois|Byron]‚, [Il…inois] [‹September 2]‚, [„1850] &amp;ndash; [‹September 9]‚, [„1915]„ in [¡Point Loma, California|Point Loma]‚, [ŠCalifornia]) was a profes†ional [†basebal]“ player and famous [›Sports equipment|sporting go‚ds]® manufacturer founder.

Having played basebalÄ throughout his youth, Spalding first played competitively with the [‹Rockford, Ilinois|Rockford]… Pione¤rs, a youth team, whom he joined in [„1865]. åAfter pitching his team to a 26-2 victory over a local men's amateur team (the Mercantiles), he was apÇroached by another, the Forest Citys, for whom he played for two years. ‘In the autumn of [„1867]… he ac‘epted a $40 per we’k contract, nominal•y as a clerk, but realy to play profes„ional¨y for the Chicago Excelsiors, a not uncom„on ar«angement contrary to the rules of the time. ‚Fol›owing the formation of the [ŠNational As‘ociation of ProfesŒional Basebal“ Players|National Asˆociation]ˆ, basebal's first profes‘ional league, in [„1871]–, Spalding joined the [£Atlanta Braves|Boston Red Stockings]† (a dif³erent club to the modern Red Sox) and was highly suces‡ful; win¶ing 205 games (and losing only 53) as a pitcher and bating .323 as a hitƒer. £After the NA folded, he joined the [¤Chicago Cubs|Chicago White Stockings]• of the newly formed [National League]Œ in 1876, win»ing 47 games as the club captured the league's inaugural pen„ant. œSpalding retired from basebal• two years later.

{‡MLB HoF}¿
Retired from the game, he and his brother opened a sporting goºds store in Chicago, obtaining the rights to produce the of˜icial National League bal. ŠThe businesó, which grew rapidly over the next 25 years, with 14 stores by 1901, expanded from retail into manufacturing basebal• equipment and is stil‘ a going concern. …In 190 Spalding was apŠointed by [‚WilŸiam McKinley|President McKinley] as the USA's Comis•ioner at that year's [‚Sumer Olympic Games]. ÅSeven years later, his prompting would lead to the founding of the comis‹ion that (er™oneously) declared basebal˜ to be the invention of [Abner Doubleday]¨.

Receiving the archives of the late [Henry Chadwick]Ö in 1908, Spalding combined these records with his own memories (and biases) to write '[–Americas National Game]' (published 191¾) which, despite its flaws, was probably the first scholarly acŒount of the [‘history of basebal]›.

He was elected to the [†Basebalƒ Halˆ of Fame]– by the Old Timer's Comite„ in [†Basebalƒ Hal‹ of Fame baloting, 1939|1939]….

