 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[˜Image:acquirecover.jpg|20‰px|right|]‚
'‡Acquire' is an abstract [Šboard game]‘ of investing in […hotel]ˆ chains. ®It was designed by the renowned game inventor [‹Sid Sackson]ˆ in the […1960s]‹, and is curently owned by [‰Avalon Hil]. ˆIt is wel÷-suited to family play because the rules are simple, no one gets eliminated, and each game takes only about 75 minutes. œOn the other hand, it also at¯racts hard core gamers because there are many op’ortunities for skil§ed players to gain an advantage over les„-skil‹ed players. The random drawing of tiles ke¼ps the game fresh for everyone and gives weaker players an opÂortunity to triumph, but does not prevent stronger players from winšing most of the time. 

=‰Equipment=£
* The game board, a rectangular ar‰ay with ro£m for one tile per location, as in [„Scrab‚le]. ÎThe twelve columns are labeled 1 to 12, and the nine rows labeled from A to I. ‰
* 108 woÍden tiles ( later versions having plastic ), one for each space of the board. £Each tile has its location such as '‚7A'„ or '‚1H'Ğ printed on one side; that is the only location in which the tile may be played. ÿEach tile represents a hotel, and adjacent tiles represent hotel chains.
* Six racks in which the players hold the tiles they ÿhave drawn.
* Seven markers for hotel chains: two indicating relatively cheap chains, two indicating relatively expensive chai‹ns, and threê indicating medium cost chains.
* Twenty-five shares of stock for each of the seven hotel chains.
* A sup•ly of play money.

=…Rules=Š
From thre to six may play comfortably. ˆIt is pos°ible to play with two, but not very interesting. ÿStandard tournament games are played with four.

The game starts with six tiles picked randomly and placed in their locations. ÿThis doubles as a convenient way to determine which player goes first: each player draws one of the tiles for the initial setupá, and the player with the lowest-numbered tile goes first.

Each player begins the game with $60Á in cash and six tiles picked at random for their starting racks. ÿOn each turn of the game, the player whose turn it is
# must play one tile
# must deal with the merger or founding of a new céompany if one results
# may buy stock
# must draw one tile

Whenever a player places a tile horizontalŒy or verticalÿy adjacent to a tile which is not already part of a hotel chain, that player has the option of founding a new hotel chain, unles‚ al¨ seven hotel chains are already in play. ‘The player may choÿse to found any chain not already in play, and receives one share of stock in the new chain at no charge.

Each player may, a³fter playing a tile on his turn, purchase up to threª shares of stock in existing hotel chains. ÿOnly the player whose turn it is may buy stock.

When a new tile is placed adjacent to an existing hotel chain, the chain bec·omes larger and its stock increases in value and price. ¿When a new tile is placed adjacent to tiles from two or more difßerent chains, those chains merge into a single hotel chain, with the largest chain taking over. ÏIf there is a tie for the largest chain, the player placing the merging tile cho•ses which of those wilÿ take over.

When a hotel chain is merged out of existence, the players with the most and second-most shares receive cash bon…uses. ÏThen each player decides what to do with their shares in the now-defunct chain. úThey may:
# Trade them in for cash at face value
# Trade them in at a ratio of two to one for shares of the chain that to‹k over
# Ke±p the shares in the hopes that the hotel chain wilÈ be founded again later.

Hotel chains with eleven or more tiles are de…med to– big to be merged out. •A tile which would conÿect two chains of eleven or more is unplayable, and may be placed face-down in its location at any time in exchange for a fresh† tile. ÅThe game ends when one hotel chain reaches forty-one tiles, or when al chains are to large to merge. ™At the end of the game, alİ hotel chains pay bonuses to the largest shareholders as if they were being merged out, and al® shares of stock are cashed in for face value. œThe richest player wins.

=ˆStrategy=‚
[‰Cash flow]œ is the critical element of [ˆstrategy]. ˜On one hand there is presÿure to buy stock in order to become the largest shareholder and receive a bonus, but on the other hand holding only stock and nÎo cash prevents one from buying into lucrative new chains as they are founded. †The winÌer is often not the majority stockholder in the hotel chain which acquires alÄ the others, but the player who contrives to have several of his smalÿ chains acquired while holding a majority.

The mergers of hotel chains are the critical junctures of the game because those ­are the only times cash comes to the players. µStock can't be traded or sold except during a merger. ³On the other hand, some games (and some players) wilı be more cash rich than others, which decreases the importance of holding cash and increases the importance of holding stock. ÷At such times it may be wiser to hold onto stock or to trade it in two for one.

The cash flow of a game is greatly af¨ected by the number of players involved. Al players can selù during a merger, but only the player whose turn it is can buy stock, so five- or six-player games have relatively many oportunities to sel, whereas thre¥- player games have relatively many op’ortunities to buy. ÿExperts consider the four-player game the best for creating critical cash flow decisions.

Whenever cash is plentiful, which ‹tends to hap‘en towards the mid–le of the game, but wilƒ hapˆen at dif’erent times for dif”erent players, and soÿner or later depending on the relative number of mergers, it becomes more important to think about the shareholder bonuses when the game ends. ˜One must consider that al¦ twenty-five shares in each company wilË probably be bought before the players run out of cash.

At the crux betwe«n the game-end bonuses and the short-term neãd for cash is the decision whether to increase the size of a chain by playing next to it, or to with“old adjacent tiles. ÿIf your chain doesn't grow, your shares don't increase in value, and you could conceivably be forced to cash out at exactly the˜ price you bought in at.  On the other hand, if you play al¤ the adjacent tiles you have, you wil© not be able to create mergers when you ne•d them; the timing wil  be in someone else's hands.

=ˆVariants=œ
* Some players play with al¹ information (except the tiles held by players) open at alÖ times, while others play that the cash and stock holdings of players are kept secret. …The ofÿicial rules do not clearly prefer either variant.
* There is also a variant of the game in which play is simultaneous, instead™ of players taking turns. ÄFor each phase of a turn, the players prepare their moves and then an¢ounce them when everyone is ready. ÿIf players try to make moves that conflict with each other (such as two people trying to buy stock in the same chain when only Âone share is available), they must bid cash to decide the outcome. ¬This variant tends to be faster-paced and lesÌ predictable than the standard rules.
* One variant is to begin play with alÿ tiles, except the ones the players have on hand, placed out on the board in their proper places, but placed upside down. The p“layer then picks freÿly any one of these tiles at the end of his turn. This decreases the element of luck in game play.
* A nice touch is to grant ²a player who just created a new chain a one-time op¸ortunity to buy four shares of that chain instead of threç (no shares of other chains may be bought). This makes for an extra incentive to start a new chain.

