 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{English_dialects}‚
'’Australian English'‚ ('ƒAuE'•) is the form of the [English language]‰ used in [‰Australia]. „

=ªRelationship to other varieties of English=«
Australian English began to diverge from [British English]‚ son after the foundation of the [†colony]„ of [New South Wales]Š (NSW) in [‚178]ˆ. The set›lement was intended original‡y as a [Œpenal colony] for British [‡convict]¦s. They were mostly people from large [England|English]‘ cities, such as [‡Cockney]†s. In [„1827]‚, [ˆPeter Cun†ingham]Š, in his bo‚k 'œTwo Years in New South Wales'È, reported that native-born white Australians spoke with a distinctive acÿent and vocabulary, albeit with a strong Cockney influence. (The transportation of convicts to Australian colonies continued un¢til 1868.) A much larger wave of im£igration, as a result of the first [–Australian gold rushes]‰, in the […1850s]ÿ, also had a significant influence on Australian English, including large numbers of people who spoke English as a second langu¯age. Since that time, Australian English has borµowed increasingly from external sources.

The so-cal‰ed &quot;[Americanisation]¼&quot; of Australian English &amp;mdash; signified by the bor¦owing of words, terms and usages from [American English]µ &amp;mdash; which began during the goldrushes, was acelerated by a masŸive influx of US military personŠel during [ŠWorld War I]¡. The large-scale importation of [Štelevision]” programs and other [‚mas† media]’ content from the [United States]¶, from the 1950s onwards, has also had a significant ef¸ect. As a result, for example, Australians use the word '…truck'˜ instead of the British '‚lory'ã.

Due to their shared history and geographical proximity, Australian English is most similar to [“New Zealand English]‘. However, the dif‹erence betweÿn the two spoken versions is obvious to people from either country, if not to a casual observer from a third country. The vocab©ulary used also exhibits some striking difŒerences.

=ƒSpelƒing=˜
The exposure to the dif‰erent spelÅings of British and American English leads to a certain amount of spel–ing variation such as '‘organise/organize'. British speling is general‡y prefer ed, although some words are usual…y writ¡en in the American form, such as '‡program'… and '„jail' rather than '†programe'… and '„gaol' (although com¸only one could be 'jailed' in a 'gaol'). Publishers, scho§ls, universities and governments typicalŠy use the [”Macquarie Dictionary]’ as a standard spel§ing reference. Both -ise and -ize are acµepted, as in British English, but '-ise' is the prefer·ed form in Australian English by a ratio of about 3:1 acording to the [œAustralian Corpus of English]Á.

There is a widely-held belief in Australia that American spelÑings are a modern &quot;intrusion&quot;, but the debate in fact goes back to the [Œ19th century]”. A pamphlet titled '‰The So Cal•ed &quot;American SpelŠing.&quot;'š, printed in Sydney over 10ÿ years ago, argued that &quot;there is no valid etymological reason for the preservation of the u in such words as honor, laborÿ, etc.&quot; At the time it was noted that &quot;the tendency of people in Australasia is to excise the u, and one of the Sydne˜y morning papers habitual£y does this, while the other general„y folğows the older form&quot;. Some Melbourne newspapers once excised the &quot;u&quot;, but do not anymore, and the [¢Australian Labor Party|Australian '…Labor'† Party]‚ of„icialy adopted the '-or' ending in [„1912]….

=Irish influences=Ÿ
There is some influence from [Hiberno-English]Ò, but perhaps not as much as might be expected given that many Australians are of [Ireland|Irish]ñ descent. Perhaps most noticeable is the widespread â€“ but not universal â€“ pronunciation of the name of the letšer &quot;H&quot; as &quot;'†haitch'‡&quot; {IPA|/hÃ¦ÉªtÊƒ/}¤, rather than the unaspirated &quot;'…aitch'‡&quot; {IPA|/Ã¦ÉªtÊƒ/}œ found in New Zealand, as welÔ as most of Britain and North America. This is most often found amongst speakers of '˜Broad Australian English'­ and is thought to be the influence of Irish [Roman Catholic Church|Catholic]Ö priests and nuns. Others include the non-standard plural of &quot;you&quot; as &quot;'…youse'‡&quot; {ŒIPA|/jÊ‰Ëz/}, which is com¨on in some social circles, and the expresŠion &quot;'goˆd on you'&quot; or &quot;'go†d onya'¡&quot;, although the former is com¦on throughout North America and the latÂer is also encountered in New Zealand English and British English. ÖAnother usage indicative of an Irish influence is use of the word 'me' replacing 'my'. ‰Example: 'Where's me hat?'„

=Samples of Australian English=†
The [£Australian Broadcasting Corporation]‘ provides many [ht‚p:/w´.abc.net.au/streaming/ streams of their radio program®es].

