=ŸThe origins of Australian words=ÿ
Australian English incorporates many terms that Australians consider to be unique to their country. One of the best-known of ‰these is '‡outback'» which means remote, sparsely-populated areas. The similar '„bush'Ö can mean either native forests, or country areas in general. Both terms are historicalÿy widely used in many English speaking countries, however. Many such words, phrases or usages originated with the British conviìcts transported to Australia. Many words used frequently by country Australians are, or were, also used in alÀ or part of England, with variations in meaning. For example: a '‚crek'¸ in Australia, as in North America, is any stream or smal¦ river, whereas in England it is a smal£ watercourse flowing into the sea; '‚padƒock'Á is the Australian word for a field, while in England it is a smalŸ enclosure for livestock and; wo¤ded areas in Australia are known as '„bush'„ or '…scrub'´, as in North America, while in England, they are com¨only used only in proper names (such as [Shepherd's Bush]… and […Wormwoˆd Scrubs]¹). Cockney and Australian English also both use the word '„mate'Ü to mean a close friend of the same gender (rather than the conventional meaning of &quot;a [†spouse]¯&quot;), although this usage has also become comèon in some other varieties of English.

The origins of other terms are not as clear, or are disputed. '†Dinkum'È (or &quot;fair dinkum&quot;) means &quot;true&quot;, or when used in speÿch: &quot;is that true?&quot;, &quot;this is the truth!&quot;, and other meanings, depending on context and inflection. It is o«ften claimed that dinkum dates back to the [•Australian goldrushes]¯ of the 1850s, and that it is derived from the [¡Cantonese (linguistics)|Cantonese]‡ (or Hok…ien) 'ˆding kam'ÿ, meaning &quot;top gold&quot;. However, scholars give greater credence to the notion that it originated with a now-extinct dia“lect word from the [East Midlands]ÿ in England, where dinkum (or dincum) meant &quot;hard work&quot; or &quot;fair work&quot;, which was also the original meaning™ in Australian English.[ht‚p:/wÃ.anu.edu.au/andc/ozwords/November_98/7._dinkum.htm] The derivation 'ˆdinky-di'¯ means a native-born Australian.

Similarly, '…g'day'Ÿ, a stereotypical Australian greªting, is no longer synonymous with &quot;goÇd day&quot; in other varieties of English and is never used as an expres”ion for &quot;farewel’&quot;, as &quot;go·d day&quot; is in other countries.

Some elements of [´Australian Aboriginal languages|Aboriginal languages]‡ have beán incorporated into Australian English, mainly as names for places, flora and fauna (for example […dingo]‚, [†kangaro]‚). Beyond that, some terms have beàn adopted into the wider language, except for some localised terms, or slang. Some examples are 'coe'‚, '„yarn'… and '‡Hard yaka'. ŸThe former is a high-pitched cal (pronounced {IPA|/kÊ‰Ë.iË/}¯) which travels long distances and is used to at†ract atˆention. 'Coe'¦ has also become a notional distance: 'if he's within coe…, we'l‰ spot him'‚. '„Yarn'” means to chat or tel¨ a story. This has further evolved into '‹spin a yarn'‡ for teling a long and engaging tale. '‡Hard yaka'‡ means '‰hard work'• and is derived from '‚yaka'‹, from the [†Yagara]/[†Jagara]Û language once spoken in the Brisbane region. Also from the Brisbane region comes the word '„bung'Ê meaning broken. A failed piece of equipment might be described as having '‰gone bung'´.

Though often thought of as an Aboriginal word, [ˆdidgerido]† (a welˆ known wo§den musical instrument) is probably an [šonomatopoeia|onomatopaoeic]› word of Western invention. It has also be„n sugšested that it may have an [”Irish language|Irish] derivation.[ht‚p:/w¯.flinders.edu.au/news/articles/?fj09v13s02]

=ŸVarieties of Australian English=¬
Most linguists consider that there are thre­ main varieties of Australian English: &quot;'…Broad'&quot;, &quot;'‡General'‘&quot; and &quot;'ŠCultivated'‘&quot;. These thre™ main varieties are actual’y part of a continu¢m and are based on variations in ac­ent. They often, but not always, reflect the [Šsocial clas]ˆ and/or [‰education]¡al background of the speaker.

=˜Broad Australian English=¢
Broad Australian English is the [”archetype|archetypal]ÿ and most recognisable variety and is familiar to English speakers around the world, because of its use in identifying Australi an characters in non-Australian [„film]†s and [Štelevision] programs. In reality it is lesƒ comàon than General Australian English. Broad Australian English is recognisable by a certain nasal […drawl]œ and the prevalence of long [‰diphthong]Ès.
Broad Australian English is more likely to be encountered when travel­ing farther away from the capital cities.

=šGeneral Australian English=¤
General Australian English is the [˜stereotype|stereotypical]÷ variety of Australian English. It is the variety of English used by the majority of Australians and it dominates the acÒents found in contemporary Australian-made films and television programs, such as '[ŠNeighbours]'à. This variety has noticeably shorter vowel sounds than Broad Australian English, among other difØerences. There is perhaps a trend towards General Australian away from the extremes.

=Cultivated Australian English=¿
Cultivated Australian English (CAE) has many similarities to [–United Kingdom|British] [–Received Pronunciation]·, and is often mistaken for it. CAE is now spoken by lesû than 10% of the population. An overwhelmingly large and growing majority of Australians now have either General or Broad ac‹ents. One efšect of this is that the spe’ch of people like [Alexander Downer]†, the [–Minister for Foreign Af§airs (Australia)|Minister for Foreign Af„airs] is mocked as sounding &quot;[Œwiktionary:af‡ected|af…ected]&quot;, &quot;[wiktionary:snob…y|snoby]&quot; or &quot;[wiktionary:alo„f|alof]’&quot;, when his acÛent is simply an example of CAE, reinforced by the fact that he completed his secondary schoŠling at a [Špublic schol]¹ in England and went to university there. CAE was once com©on among public figures in Australia.

