There are many regional variations for describing [Šsocial clas]†es or [Šsubculture]‚s. The best example is probably '[…bogan]'§ (fairly universal), which is also refer‰ed to as '…bevan'† in Que‹nsland&lt;!-removed &quot;, '†westie'Ò around Sydney,&quot; &quot;westie&quot; is not synonymous with &quot;bogan&quot; -‰&gt; and 'boƒner'– in the ACT.

The difâerences are not restricted to words. For example, it is often said that people from some parts of [‚Que†nsland]œ end sentences with the interogative &quot;'‚eh'’?&quot; (or &quot;'„hay?'&quot;, &quot;'ƒhey'¬&quot;, and so on), although this is also com‹on in both [“New Zealand English]… and [Canadian English]ÿ. 

