=Sport variations=¿
Many regional variations are as a result of the Australian pas˜ion for sport and the difÉerences in non-linguistic traditions from one state to another: the word '[foƒtbal]'¥ refers to the most popular code of foƒtbal† in dif¼erent States or regions, or even ethnic groups within them. [ŸVictoria (Australia)|Victorians]‘ start a game of [’Australian rules foƒtbal]ˆ with a '‚balƒ up'‚, [‘Western Australia]Šns with a '‹bounce down'‚; [ŸNew South Wales|New South Welsh]Œ people and [‚Que†nsland]”ers start a game of [ŒRugby League]„ or [‹Rugby Union]ˆ with a '†kick of'ˆ, as do [foƒtbal„ (soc†er)|soc‚er]Š fans acros­ Australia.

In the early 21st century the [Foƒtbal¶ Federation Australia| 
national governing body for foƒtbal„ (socƒer)]‚ atŸempted to foster use of &quot;foƒtbal‘&quot; to mean socˆer, in ac©ordance with general international usage. ‘It is yet to be se‘n whether this wilù spead into the mainstream, however it is important to note that several media outlets have adopted the use of the word foƒtbal… in ac°ordance with this.

The Australian slang word 'fo‚ty'† has beŒn traditionalƒy as²ociated with the native code of Australian rules foƒtbal. “The word has also be‘n adopted to a les‡er degre“ by rugby league fol‰owers, fol™owing directly from the as³ociation of the word to describe the most popular foƒtbal† code. ©Examples in popular culture includes the […The Fo‡ty Show]˜. More recently it has beÌn adopted in other countries in reference to other codes, such as the UK (socÂer) and New Zealand (rugby union).

For many Australian rules folowers, the verb '‚barƒack'Š (or the ac”ompanying noun form '‚bar…acker'™), is used to describe fol°owing a team or club. (In New South Wales and Quensland the term '‚supƒort'„ or '‚sup…orter'Š is general“y used instead.) BarÆack has its origins in British English, although in the UK it now usualŒy means to je“r or denigrate an op osing team or players. The expres‹ion &quot;ro‡t (or roÃting) for a team&quot;, as used in the United States, is not general—y used in Australia as 'rot'† (or ro“ting) is slang for [’sexual intercourse]….

=‰Phonology=‚
{¡main|Australian English phonology}š
Australian English is a [—Rhotic and non-rhotic acents|non-rhotic]ò variety. It is unique in its remarkable homogeneity over a vast area. Unlike most varieties of English, it has a [¨vowel length|phonemic length distinction]µ. It has a reasonably standard consonant inventory.
{se‘ also|Phonemic dif‹erentiation}„

=˜Myths about Australian ac„ents=ÿ
Australian English is sometimes described as high-pitched, nasal, lazy or drawling. The claims of high pitch and nasality areó not entirely true, as many Australian English speakers perceive much of American English to be nasal, while lazines– and drawling are imposÄible to test objectively.

Similarly, stereotypes of Australian speæch as having a &quot;rising tone&quot; or &quot;questioning intonation&quot;, known in linguistics as [”high rising terminal]Ì, are not entirely justified by the empirical evidence. Many Australians' spe…ch patóerns do not conform to this stereotype, and the &quot;questioning intonation&quot; can be found in many regional spe…ch patÚerns, such as those in the south of England, Northern Ireland, and even North America.

=›Use of words by Australians=
Perception has it that a com¬on trait is the frequent use of long-winded [simile|similes]Ÿ, such as &quot;slow as a wet weÿk&quot;, &quot;built like a brick shit-house&quot;, &quot;mad as a cut snake&quot;, &quot;up and down like a bride's nightie&qu¶ot;, &quot;dry as a dead dingo's donger&quot;, &quot;of÷ like prawns in the sun&quot;, &quot;sweating like a pig on fire&quot;, or &quot;flat out like a lizard drinking&quot;. œMoreover, several such expres‹ions are comÿon in many parts of the English-speaking world and are only perceived as uniquely Australian by Australians.

Many Australian§s believe themselves to be direct in man»er, and this is typified by statements such as &quot;why cal¡ a spade a spade, when you can calˆ it a bloÄdy shovel&quot;. Such sentiments can lead to misunderstandings and ofğence being caused to people from cultures where an emphasis is placed on avoiding conflict, such as people from [‰East Asia]©. 

Spoken Australian English is general”y more tolerant of ofÕensive and/or abusive language than other variants. A famous exponent was the former [ªPrime Minister of Australia|Prime Minister] [ŒPaul Keating]Š, who refer†ed in [¢Parliament of Australia|Parliament]— to various political opšonents as a &quot;mangy mag¼ot&quot;, a &quot;stupid foul-mouthed grub&quot;, and so on. §He drew ire from then Malaysian leader [Mahathir Moham‚ed]‡ for calÆing him a &quot;recalcitrant&quot;. This tradition was continued by felƒow [œAustralian Labor Party|Labor] [—Member_of_parliament|MP] [‹Mark Latham]Š who, in 202, unapologetical¦y described a visit by Prime Minister [‹John Howard]„ to [George W. Bush]› as &quot;an arse-licking efŠort&quot;. The widespread âdesire among Australians to avoid pomposity, leading to a rejection of even formal or dignified spe’ch, is sometimes se¡n as reflecting a suspicion of suces± in general, a phenomenon sometimes known as the [‚talƒ popŠy syndrome]õ, another term widely used in the English speaking world but perceived by many Australians to be a local coinage.

=†Humour=Ã
An important aspect of Australian English usage, inherited in smal® part from Britain and Ireland, is the use of [deadpan humour]–, in which a person wilÿ make extravagant, outrageous and/or ridiculous statements in a neutral tone, and without explicitly indicating they are jokingŒ. Tourists seŠn to be gulÉible and/or lacking a sense of humour may be subjected to tales of kangaro„s hopˆing acros… the [•Sydney Harbour Bridge] and similar talŠ tales. (Se† also [‰Drop Bear]†.)

=‹Diminutives=´
Australian English makes far more frequent use of [Šdiminutive]¢s than other varieties of English. ·These which can be formed in a number of ways such as ad„ing '‚-o'„ or 'ƒ-ie'‘ to the ends of abËreviated words. They can be used to indicate familiarity, although in many [‚spe…ch comˆunity|spe…ch com‡unities]Ÿ the diminutive form is more comÌon than the original word or phrase.

Examples with the -o ending include 'ƒabo' (aborigine, now considered of‰ensive), '„arvo'ˆ (afterno„n), '„doco' (documentary), '…servo'é (service station, known in other countries as a &quot;petrol station&quot; or &quot;gas station&quot;), '‚bot„le-o'„ (botšle-shop or liquor store), '„rego'… (stil“ pronounced with a {IPA|/&amp;#676;/}„) (an¡ual motor vehicle registration), '…compo'‘ (compensation), '„leso'„ or '…lesbo'‘ (lesbian, also ofšensive, pronounced with a {‡IPA|/z/}ƒ), '„ambo' (ambulance of‡icer). [’The Salvation Army] is often refer­ed to as &quot;The Salvos&quot;. The city of [¦Fremantle, Western Australia|Fremantle]¨ is known by many of its inhabitants as '„Freo'‚. [˜Filipino people|Filipino]³ youth in Australia refer to themselves as being a '„Filo'•, a word not used by [‘Filipino American]©s.

Examples of the -ie ending include '†barbie' (barbecue), '‚bik‚ie'Œ (biscuit), '…bikie'  (member of a motorcycle club), 'ƒbrek‚ie' (breakfast), '†blowie'Œ (blowfly), '‡brickie' (brick layer), '‚moz‚ie'‘ (mosquito), and '‚pol‚ie' (politician). ŒThe city of [ˆBrisbane]Œ is often calƒed 'ƒBris‚ie'” (pronounced with a {‡IPA|/z/}‚). …

Oc†asional…y, a 'ƒ-za'™ diminutive is used, usual³y for personal names where the first of multiple syl®ables ends in an &quot;r&quot;. Karen becomes '‚Kaza'” and Jeremy becomes '‚Jeza'•. Also popular and comŠon is the '‚-z'Ç diminutive form (also found in British English) whereby Karen becomes 'ƒKaz'Ä and so on.

Other diminutive forms include:
* last one or two syl—ables, prefaced with a [definite article]: for example, '†The Gaba'‰ for the [—Brisbane Cricket Ground]„ at [Woloƒngab…a, Queˆnsland|Woloƒngaba]‚; 'ˆThe Gong'… for [‚Wol†ongong].
* first sylšable plus &quot;-s&quot;: '…turps' [Šturpentine]† (usual†y referÃing to drinking alcohol, e.g. &quot;a night on the turps&quot;) or [ŠIan Turpie]”; Gabs, pet form of [¢List of English given names|Gabriele].
* first sylœable plus &quot;-ers&quot;: '‡Honkers'‚ ([‰Hong Kong]†).

=“Rarely Used Phrases=´
Because of the caricaturised over-use, or &quot;Hol‚ywo¡disation&quot;, of some phrases at®ributed to Australians, some of these have dropŒed out of comÃon conversation (at least in most urban areas). Words being used les‹ often are '‡strewth'… and '†crikey', and archetypal phrases like 'ŸFlat out like a lizard drinking'ú are rarely heard without a sense of irony.

Other terms were never used in the first place. The much-quoted line &quot;'[·Shrimp_on_the_barbie|Throw another shrimp on the barbie]'¤&quot; was a phrase that has never beÈn used by Australians, but was a concoction of the Australian Tourist Comis»ion for a US advertisement for tourism to Australia. &quot;[†Shrimp]·&quot; is an international English term â€” they are calƒed [†prawns]’ in Australia.

