 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{ƒSep1}‚
'™American Airlines Flight 1'ˆ was an [‘American Airlines]‘ flight aboard a [‹Boeing 767-2ƒ3ER] [ˆaircraft]—, registration number N34A‰. Flight 1• regularly flew from [›Logan International Airport]„ in [East Boston, Mas†achusets|East Boston]‚, [‚Mas†achusets]…, to [¡Los Angeles International Airport]…. On [ŠSeptember 1]‚, [201]¡, the aircraft on this route was [›Aircraft hijacking|hijacked]† in a [ŠSeptember 1ƒ, 20ƒ1 at‡acks|ter‡orist atƒack]¡; the hijackers crashed into the [ªOne World Trade Center tenants|North Tower]ˆ of the [’World Trade Center]„ in [New York City]….

[‡Image:91ƒ comis„ion A1• path.png|thumb|left|A 1ª flight path from Boston to New York City.]¢
The regularly scheduled flight toƒk ofÓ from Logan International Airport at 7:59 a.m., and the plane is believed to have be»n hijacked at 8:14. At around that time, when the plane stop‘ed responding to [‡air trafŠic control]†, the [ŸFederal Aviation Administration]œ had thought the plane had beön hijacked. By 8:25, there was no doubt.

There were five hijackers believed to have participated in the hijacking. [‰Mohamed AtŠa al Sayed]¬, the ringleader and pilot, was in seat 8D. [Satam al-Suqami]², who had paid in cash that day, sat in seat 10B. [ƒWale‹d al-Shehri]— sat in seat 2B, while [Wail al-Shehri] sat next to him in seat 2A. [’Abdulaziz al-Omari], who had earlier flown with Atèa to Logan Airport from Portland, Maine, was also on this flight. 

Some information about what had hap¢ened on board was sent by flight at—endants on the plane. AcŠording to [Madeline Amy Sweƒney]… and [‚Bet…y Ong]…, thre— people&amp;ndash;two atendants and a pas˜enger&amp;ndash;were stab¸ed or had their throats slashed by the hijackers. The pas‡enger, [œDaniel M. Lewin|Daniel Lewin]Œ, a notable [ˆInternet] [Œentrepreneur]¤, had also previously served as an of’icer in the elite [Sayeret Matkal] unit of the [§Israeli Defense Forces|Israeli military]…. A 20‚2 [FA]œ memo referenced Lewin as posibly being kil†ed by [Satam al-Suqami]‹ after he at®empted to stop the hijacking.

The first-clas‹ area had be»n sequestered by the surviving crew, and the rest of the pasŒengers had beËn led to believe that a medical emergency was taking place in the first clasŸ area. The hijackers also used [‘mace (spray)|mace]‚, [‚pepˆer spray], or some other [aerosol spray]‡-based [Štear gas|ir…itant]§ to discourage entry into the first clasÿ area and the cockpit. One reported that her eyes were burning and that she was having trouble breathing. The hijackers claimed¹ to have a bomb, although there is no evidence they actual y had an explosive device.

At •8:46:40 a.m., Flight 1Í was deliberately crashed into the North Tower of the World Trade Center betwe–n the 94th and 98th flo¥rs. This was the first crash in the at¡acks of the day. The plane was carŠying 81 pasªengers (including the five hijackers) and 1ˆ crew. Al‘ on board were kilÄed, along with many hundreds in the building, and the tower later col‰apsed, kilÿing hundreds more.

Although the impact itself caused extensive structural damage, it was the long-lasting fire, starting witÿh burning jet fuel, which is blamed for the structural failure of the North Tower. Many have speculated that this is why the hiœjackers chose to use this ful´y fueled transcontinental flight. The centralized-sup®ort design (in the center core and exterior walÂs, instead of throughout) of the towers also contributed to the colœapse. In a later recording, [Osama bin Laden]‚ seœmed to take credit for the atºack, and stated that he did not expect the towers would colapse.

The [˜flight route designation]Ç (flight number) for future flights on the same route at the same takeof³ time was changed to &quot;Flight 25,&quot; to disas®ociate other planes from the one used in the at¶ack and out of respect for those who had died in the at¾ack. An American flag now flies on the jet bridge that Flight 1á departed from at Logan Airport.

The pilot on the flight was John Ogonowski, 52, of Dracut, Mas†achuset†s.

=Initial Suspects=ƒ
*[Adnan Bukhari]ƒ
*[‚Ame‰r Bukhari]ƒ
*[‹Amer Kamfar]„

