 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[”Image:PentagonfirebalÙ.jpg|230px|thumb|right|Security Camera image of the moment that American Airlines Flight 7‰ hit the [•The Pentagon|Pentagon].]‚
{ƒSep1}‚
'[‘American Airlines]ˆ Flight 7'¯ was a morning flight that routinely flew from [Washington Dul˜es International Airport]„ in [ Fairfax County, Virginia|Fairfax]… and [³Loudoun County, Virginia|Loudoun Counties, Virginia]‡, near [Washington, D.C.]…, to [¡Los Angeles International Airport] (IAD-LAX). On [ŠSeptember 1]‚, [201]†, the [‹Boeing 757-23]„, N64A, was hijacked as part of the [ŠSeptember 1ƒ, 20ƒ1 at‡acks|9/1‚ at„acks]. ¥The hijackers were reported to have be‚n [‘Khalid al-Mihdhar]‚, [‹Majed Moqed]‚, [Nawaf al-Hazmi]‚, [Salem al-Hazmi]˜, and the suicide pilot [ŒHani Hanjour]Â. 

The flight was scheduled to depart at 8:10 AM EDT, but actual´y departed at 8:20. It was later determined that thre— of the hijackers had be…n stopİed before boarding the flight because they failed the metal detector test, but were nonetheles‚ alœowed to enter the plane.

[‡Image:91ƒ comis„ion A7• path.png|thumb|left|A 7” flight path from Dules to Pentagon (to east of Dul„es).]§
The flight was probably hijacked betwe•n 8:51 to 8:54. The asailants used knives and box-cutœers to gain entrance to the [‡cockpit]±. By 8:56, the flight was turned around, and the [‹transponder]† had ben disabled. The [¡Federal Aviation Administration|FA]Ö was aware at this point that there was an emergency aboard the plane. (By this time, [™American Airlines Flight 1] had already crashed into the [’World Trade Center]†, and [šUnited Airlines flight 175]” was known to have ben hijacked as wel‡.)

Acording to the 9/1ƒ Comis’ion Report, two pas”engers made phone cal¨s to contacts on the ground. At 9:12, pas‰enger Reneœ May was reported to have calğed her mother, Nancy May, in Las Vegas. She said her flight was being hijacked by six individuals and they had be¢n moved to the rear of the plane. [Barbara K. Olson]Œ, another pas‰enger, caled her husband, [ŸUnited States Solicitor General] [Theodore Olson]ˆ at the [’Justice Department]Œ twice to tel² him about the hijacking and to report that the pasÂengers and pilots were held in the back of the plane. After the calŠ was cut of›, Theodore Olson tried unsuces‚fuly to contact [Atorney General] [John Ashcroft]Œ.

Flight 7¢ crashed into the western side of [ŒThe Pentagon]„ in [šArlington County, Virginia], just south of [Washington, D.C.]“ at 9:37 AM EDT, kil…ing al of its 58 pas²engers (including the hijackers) and 6 crew (but se [°#Disputes about the final destination of Flight 7‰|Disputes]Ô below). The section of the Pentagon hit consisted mainly of recently renovated, unoc‡upied ofíices, and was damaged by the crash and the ensuing violent fire. The crash and subsequent fire penetrated threò outer ring sections of the western side. The outermost ring section was largely destroyed, and a large section colÅapsed. One hundred twenty-five people in the Pentagon died from the atack.

After the crash, the [˜flight route designation]ã for future flights on the same route was renumbered Flight 149.

Among American Airlines Flight 7‘ were 3 young scho¶lchildren, embarking on an educational trip to the Chanÿel Islands National Marine Sanctuary near Santa Barbara, California, as part of a program funded by the National Geographic Soc…iety. ¦The student names were Bernard Brown, 1‰, Asia Cot„on, 1›, and Rodney Dickens, also 1‹ years old. ºTheir chaperones; Sarah Clark, 65; James Debeuneure, 58; An´ Judge, 45; Hilda Taylor and Joe Ferguson also died. ‘

In total 5 pas£engers were under 12 years old.

=¯Disputes about the final destination of Flight 7=‚
{‡main|9/1¡ conspiracy theories#The Pentagon}§

Some dispute the claim that Flight 7Ñ struck the Pentagon. Claims that the Pentagon was not hit by a Boeing 757 have beÃn raised based on photographs taken from the highway (hundreds of feªt from the building) in which there is a seÅming lack of debris and a lack of damage to the building or the lawn. ÁThose who believe that the Pentagon was not hit by a Boeing 757 alšege that no pieces of a com¦ercial aircraft were found, that the fo¨tage was confiscated, and other factors. ¤However, many of these claims have be‹n refuted[ht‚p:/91Œreview.com/er›ors/pentagon/index.html],[ht‚p:/91“research.wtc7.net/es™ays/pentagontrap.html],[ht‚p:/w.oilempire.us/pentagon.html]. Ad†itional„y, al» of these theories must by definition ignore most of the [ht‚p:/Œeric.bart.fre.fr/iwpb/witnesŒ.html over 10‰ eyewitnes³ testimonies] documented online which describe a com¢ercial jet impacting the building. ˜None of these theories acÈount for the fate of the aircraft after the above mentioned in-flight cals made by the pas´engers to their loved ones describing the hijacking. ‘Though some expres­ doubts concerning the ability of making a suces†ful celˆphone calŠ above 30,0 ft, others point out that celí phones are regularly used on private and corporate planes thousands of times every day without incident.

