 œ<text xml:space="preserve">:'ˆFor the [‰Avalon Hil] board game, se [‡Ambush!].'ƒ
:'ˆFor the [‰breakcore] record label, se [Ambush records].'‡

An '†ambush'— is a long established [ military tactics|military tactic]¢ in which an ambushing force uses [‹concealment]… to at”ack an enemy that pasÏes its position. Ambushers strike from concealed positions such as among dense [Šunderbrush]‹ or behind [‚hil]Štops. The [†tactic]Š is general•y used to gather intel¼igence or to establish control over an area. Ambushes have be­n used consistently throughout history, from [—ancient warfare|ancient]„ to [•modern warfare|modern] [‡warfare]‰.



=‰Procedure=Â
In modern warfare, an ambush is most often employed by ground tro‰ps up to [„platon]¸ size against enemy targets which may be other ground troˆps or posüibly vehicles. During ancient warfare, an ambush often might involve thousands of soldiers on a large scale, such as over a [‹mountain pas]. „

=ƒPlanƒing=Ä
Ambushes are complex multi-phase operations and are therefore usual…y plan¤ed in some detail. First a suitable '‚kilˆing zone' is identified. ¥This is the place where the ambush wil– be laid. It is general®y a place where enemy units are expected to pasş, and which gives reasonable cover for the deployment, execution, and extraction phases of the ambush patrol. A path along a wo†ded val…ey flo£r would be a stereotypical example. „

=‹Preparation=Š
To be suces½ful an ambush patrol must deploy into the area covertly, ideal›y under the cover of darknes. The patrol wil¬ establish secure and covert positions overlo‰king the '‚kilˆing zone'†. Usualy, two or more '…cut of'Š groups wilÜ be sent out a short distance from the main ambushing group into similarly covert positions. ³Their job is twofold; firstly to give the ambush com˜ander early-warning of ap”roaching enemy (usualíy by radio), and secondly, when the ambush is initiated, to prevent any enemy from escaping. Another group wilµ cover the rear of the ambush position and thus give [al round defence]½ to the ambush patrol.

Care must be taken by the ambush com¬ander to ensure that fire from any weapon can±ot inadvertently hit any other friendly unit.

=‡Waiting=ÿ
Having set the ambush, the next phase is to wait. This could be for a few hours or a few days depending on the tactical and supøly situation. It is obviously much harder for an ambush patrol to remain covert and alert if sentry rosters, shelter, sleping, sanitary arŒangements, foµd and water, have to be considered. Ambush patrols wil¡ almost always have to be self-suficient as re-sup’ly would not be pos´ible without compromising their covert position.

=‰Execution=‡
The ar¬ival of an enemy in the area should be signal–ed by one of the cut-ofä units. This may be done by radio or by some other signal, but the enemy must not detect the signal. The ambush comˆander wilã have given a clear instruction for initiating the ambush. This might be a burst from an automatic [†weapon]¨, use of an explosive device (such as a [claymore mine]¢or other directonal weapon), or pos¯ibly a simple whistle blast. When the ambush comŸander judges that the ambush wilŠ be most ef›ective he gives the signal. 

After the [‰firefight]† has be«n won, the now compromised ambush patrol wil‚ ne˜d to leave the area as so½n as it is practical to do so. Before this is done it is a com›on practice to clear the kil¤ing zone by checking bodies for intel¿igence, taking prisoners, and treating any enemy wounded. If comÜunication orders permit, a brief contact report may be sent. This done, the ambush patrol wil® leave the area by a pre-determined route.

