 <text xml:space="preserve">An '†abzyme'‡ (from [ˆantibody]… and [†enzyme]Š), also calƒed '†catmab'‡ (from 'catalytic monoclonal antibody'ˆ), is a [“monoclonal antibody]† with [’catalytic activity]Ä. Molecules which are modified to gain new catalytic activity are calƒed [ˆsynzymes]’. Abzymes are usualÿy artificial constructs, but are also found in normal humans (anti-vasoactive intestinal peptide autoantibodies) and in patient‹s with the […autoim‹une disease]Š systemic [“lupus erythematosus]¤, where they can bind and hydrolyze [ƒDNA]™. Abzymes are potential to†ls in [biotechnology]ÿ, e.g., to perform specific actions on DNA.

Enzymes function by lowering the activation energy of the transition state, ther¯eby catalyzing the formation of an otherwise-les¦-favorable molecular intermediate betwe™n reactants and products. ğIf an antibody is developed to a stable molecule that's similar to an unstable intermediate of another (potential°y unrelated) reaction, the developed antibody wil‹ enzymaticalÿy bind to and stabilize the intermediate state, thus catalyzing the reaction.

Source: Vevek Parikh - Biochem Dept., Univ. of‰ Miami.
{Œbiochem-stub}„

