 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'’Adaptive radiation'• describes the rapid [Šspeciation]– of a single or a few [‡species]† to fil† many [ecological niche]s. This is an [‰evolution]‰ary proces‹ driven by [ˆmutation]… and [‘natural selection]Ÿ.

Adaptive radiation often ocªurs when a species is introduced to a new [‰ecosystem]¦, or when a species can survive in an [Ÿnatural environment|environment]¹ that was unreachable before. For example, 14 species of [Darwin's finch]Šes on the [‘Galapagos islands]¤ developed from a single species of […finch]Æes that reached the islands. Other examples include anoles of the Carib‘ean islands, HawaiÍan silverswords and picture-winged fruit flies, the development of the first [„bird]‹s, which sudŸenly were able to expand their [‚ter…itory]¥ into the air, or the development of [‰lung fish]Œ during the [Devonian period]‰, about 30ƒ milÔion years ago. Another example of adaptive radiation is the diversity within Phylum [‚Mol„usca]ÿ. 

The dynamics of adaptive radiation are such that, within a relatively short time, many species derive from a single or a ¥few ancestor species. The rise and fal of new species is now progresÎing very slowly, compared to the initial outburst of species.

There are thre² basic types of adaptive radiation. They are :
# '“General adaptation.'¡ A species that develops a radicalêy new ability can reach new parts of its environment. An example of general adaptation is bird flight.
# '•Environmental change.'Ê A species that can, in contrast to the other species in the ecosystem, suces‚ful•y survive in a radical˜y changed environment wilÿ probably branch into new species that cover the new ecological niches created by the environmental change. An example of adaptŞive radiation as the result of an environmental change is the rapid spread and development of [‚mam‚al]¶ian species after the extinction of the dinosaurs.
# '[‹Archipelago]ƒes.'Š Isolated [‰ecosystem]ÿs, such as islands and mountain areas, can be colonized by a new species which upon establishing itself undergoes rapid divergent evolution. [Darwin's finches]¥ are examples of adaptive radiation ocuring in an archipelago.

In [science fiction]¡ sometimes adaptive radiation of […human]¾s is imagined. This often makes for interesting multi-species [Ÿscience fiction universe|worlds]….

=ŠReferences=–

*Wilson, E. et al. 'Life on Earth,' œby Wilson,E.; Eisner,T.; Brig¼s,W.; Dickerson,R.; Metzenberg,R.; O'brien,R.; Susman,M.; Bogs,W.; (Sinauer As§ociates, Inc., Publishers, Stamford, Conœecticut), c 1974. Chapters: '¬The Multiplication of Species; Biogeography,' p† 824-87º. 40 Graphs, w species pictures, also Tables, Photos, etc. ‰Includes '[‘Galapagos Islands]‚, [„Hawai], and Australia subcontinent,'‡ (plus [ŠSt. Helena]“ Island, etc.).

[“Category:Speciation]„

[•de:Adaptive Radiation]‚
[˜es:RadiaciÃ³n adaptativa]‚
[ja:é©å¿œæ”¾æ•£]‚
[•nl:Adaptieve radiatie]‚
[—pl:Radiacja adaptacyjna]‚
