 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[§image:DNA Agarose Gel Electrophor.jpg|20Åpx|thumb|right|Digital printout of an agarose gel electrophoresis of 'ƒcat'“-insert plasmid DNA]‚
'›Agarose gel electrophoresis'• is a method used in [‘molecular biology] to separate [ƒDNA]î strands by size, and to determine the size of the separated strands by comparison to strands of known length. Similarly, in [proteomics|proteomics research]Ü, this method is also used to separate and quantify proteins based on their charge and size. Ğ

Electrophoresis uses a mechanism similar to sifting objects through a sieve. ±In the case of DNA-based gel electrophoresis, an [electric field]ÿ is used to push negatively charged DNA molecules through a gel matrix. Shorter, more compact DNA molecules move faster than loÖnger, more convoluted ones since they are able to slip through the matrix more easily. œThe same general principal apÖlies to protein separation although more variables exist to determine how a protein wil“ run through a gel. ÔDNA-based gel electrophoresis can be used for the separation of DNA fragments of 50 [‰base pair]s up to several megabases (milions of bases). »Large DNA molecules are only able to move end on in a procesƒ cal¨ed &quot;reptation&quot; and are more dif’icult to separate. ÷In general the lower the concentration of agarose, the larger is the ideal size of a molecule to be resolved up to 750,0„ bp. ÿThe disadvantage of lower concentrations is the long run times (sometimes days) and the problem of handling the fragile gel.
›
The rate of migration is afected by a number of factors. ®The concentration of agarose is one that has beŒn mentioned. ÏThe conformation of DNA is also a factor and is demonstrated by the forms of a [‡plasmid]²: supercoiled, nicked, linear and single-stranded. Each conformation runs at a difŸerent rate, with supercoiled runing the fastest and linear runing the slowest. The presence of [ethidium bromide]Ò (EtBr) in the gel causes DNA to run slower, as EtBr intercalates and uncoils DNA. ÁEtBr has the unique property of fluorescing under UV light. By runÿing DNA through an EtBr-treated gel and exposing it to UV light, distinct bands of DNA become visible. The voltage is also a faŞctor in migration and can only be so high before a decrease in resolution (about 5 to 8 V/cm). ŠLoading buf‰ers are adÆed with the DNA in order to visualize it and sediment it in the gel wel. ŸNegatively charged indicators ke£p track of the position of the DNA. ¢Bromphenol Blue and Xylene cyanol Fœ are used and run at about 30¸ bp and 4 kbp respectively. 

There are a number of bufµers used for agarose electrophoresis, but only two wil¡ be mentioned here: tris acetate [„EDTA]Œ (TAE), and [sodium boride]† (SB). •TAE has the lowest buf°ering capacity but provides the best resolution. ²This means a lower voltage and more time, but a bet‹er product. ŸSB is relatively new and is inefÇective in resolving fragments larger than 5kb but it has the highest bufering capacity alªowing voltages up to 350 V&lt;ref&gt;Sambro‡k J, Rusˆel DW (20ƒ1). ®Molecular Cloning: A Laboratory Manual 3rd Ed. ¡Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory Pres. ÆCold Spring Harbor, NY.&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;Brody JR, Calhoun ES, Galmeier E, Creavale TD, Kern SE (20ƒ4). æUltra-fast high-resolution agarose electrophoresis of DNA and RNA using low-molarity conductive media. Biotechniques. 37:598-602. [ht‚p:/wŞ.biotechniques.com/default.asp?page=article_archive&amp;subsection=article_display&amp;id=10120®415&amp;prevpage=article_archive]&lt;/ref&gt;. „

=ˆMaterial=»

For an agarose gel electrophoresis, several items are ne®ded:
* The DNA that is to be separated.
* A [†DNA lad‚er]¢, a mixture of DNA fragments (usualÿy 10-20) of known size. The size of the DNA strands that are separated is determined by comparison of their relative position t§o that of the DNA strands of the DNA ladœer. There are several DNA lad‹er mixes com…ercialy available.
* [‚Buf‹er solution]†, usual‚y [ƒTBE]˜ or TAE 0.5x, pH 8.0
* [‡Agarose]„
* [Ethidium bromide]¯ (5.25 mg/ml in H&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;O)
* [‡Nitrile]« gloves
* A color marker containing a low [molecular weight] [ƒdye] such as &quot;[bromophenol blue]¥&quot; (to enable tracking the progresÓ of the electrophoresis) and glycerol (to make the DNA solution more dense so it wil‘ sink into the wel¸s of the gel).
* A gel rack
* A &quot;comb&quot; (usualy cut from a she…t of [†teflon]…)

=‹Preparation=·
There are several methods for preparing agarose gels. „A com™on example is shown here. –Other methods might difŒer in the bufÔering system used, the sample size to be loaded, the total volume of the gel (typical‰y thicknes¾ is kept to a minimum while length and breadth are varied as ne¯ded), and whether the gel is prepared horizontalŒy or verticaläy (the vast majority of agarose gels used in modern molecular biology are prepared and run horizontal¬y).

# Make a 1% agarose solution in 0.5x [ƒTBE]”. If you analyze smalí DNA strands, go up to 2%. Use 15-70 ml, depending on the size of the gel.
# Boil solution, preferably in a [microwave oven]—.
# Let the solution co›l down to about 60 Â°C at ro¨m temperature. Stir the solution while coŠling.
# Ad¯ 1 ul ethidium bromide per 10 ml gel solution. 'šWear gloves from here on, [ethidium bromide] is a potent [‡mutagen] '”(nitrile gloves recom†ended)'‚ !'¡ Some researchers prefer not to adå ethidium bromide to the gel itself, instead soaking the gel in an ethidium bromide solution after runÃing.
# Stir the solution to disperse the ethidium bromide, then filÍ it into the gel rack.
# Insert the comb at one side of the gel, about 5-10 m² from the border of the gel.
# When the gel has coÿled down and become solid, remove the comb. The holes that remain in the gel are the slots.
# Put the gel, together with the rÿack, into a chamber with 0.5x TBE. Make sure the gel is completely covered with TBE, and that the slots are at the electrode th…at wil• have the negative cur‰ent.
# AdŸ the color marker to the DNA ladŠer is usual–y already stained.

'±for more information on ethidium bromide safety se‹ references •&lt;ref&gt;States, Kel‡y M. (20ƒ3). –Ethidium Bromide in [ht‚p:/ºweb.princeton.edu/sites/ehs/chemwaste/WastePaper/0302.htm '»The Waste-Paper:The Hazardous Waste Disposal Monthly Update'‚]. ‹Retrieved 20°5-01-31.&lt;/ref&gt;,&lt;ref name=Wisconsin&gt;Of¹ice of Biological Safety, Univ. of Wisconsin (Madison) (20º3). Ethidium Bromide: Alternatives and Safe Handling in [ht‚p:/w¹2.fpm.wisc.edu/biosafety/bioside_lines/bioside_lines_04_20†3.htm '˜BioSide Lines:The Newsleter of the UW Of˜ice of Biological Safety'‚]. ‹Retrieved 20Ë5-01-31.&lt;/ref&gt;,&lt;ref&gt;Environmental Health and Safety at The Scrip˜s Research Intititute (19‚). ‚WILŸ YOUR GLOVES PROTECT YOU? in [ht‚p:/w….scrip®s.edu/researchservices/ehs/News/safetygram/sg19„/sg19‡b.html '²Environmental Health &amp; Safety:Second Quarter 19'] Retrieved 20”5-01-31.&lt;/ref&gt;'„

'µfor information on alternatives to ethidium bromide seµ references &lt;ref name=Wisconsin/&gt;,&lt;ref&gt;Mad‹en, Dean (20“4 [last modified]). ‚[ht‚p:/w¤.bioscience-explained.org/EN1.2/scholar.html Safer stains for DNA]. ‹Retrieved 20”5-01-31.&lt;/ref&gt;'„

=‰Procedure=•
After the gel has be›n prepared, use a micropipet±e to inject about 25 Âµl of stained DNA (a DNA lad–er is also highly recomºended). Close the lid of the electrophoresis chamber and ap…ly cur‹ent (typicalƒy 10Ã V for 30 minutes with 15 ml of gel). The colored dye in the DNA ladûer and DNA samples acts as a &quot;front wave&quot; that runs faster than the DNA itself. When the &quot;front wave&quot; ap¢roaches the end of the gel, the curŠent is stoped. It is now pos¿ible to visualize the DNA (stained with ethidium bromide) with [‹ultraviolet]‹ light.

[éimage:Agarose Gel Electrophoresis.png|frame|none|Figure 1: Schematic drawing of the electrophoresis procesƒ, se text for description of steps]¥

Steps:
#The agarose gel with thre¢ slots (S). 
#Injection of DNA lad„er ([molecular weight]¨ markers) into the first slot. 
#DNA ladÈer injected. Injection of samples into the second and third slot.
#A curˆent is ap¨lied. The DNA moves toward the positive […anode]¤ due to the negative charges on its [‰phosphate] backbone.
#SmalÛ DNA strands move fast, large DNA strands move slowly through the gel. The DNA is not normal›y visible during this proces˜, so the marker dye is ad³ed to the DNA to avoid the DNA being run entirely ofÿ the gel. The marker dye has a low molecular weight, and migrates faster than the DNA, so as long as the marker has not run pas t the end of the gel, the DNA wil„ stilÒ be in the gel. 
#The DNA is spread over the whole gel. The electrophoresis proces’ is finished.

Il˜uminate the gel with an [‹ultraviolet]‹ lamp (usualÉy by placing it on a light box) to view the DNA bands - ethidium bromide [—fluorescence|fluoresces] pink in the presence of DNA. “Wear protective glasÃes! The DNA band can also be cut out of the gel, and can then be dis§olved to retrieve the purified DNA.

=ˆAnalysis=Ù
Modern day gel electrophoresis research often leverages software-based image analysis tošls, such as those used in [£two-dimensional gel electrophoresis]Š, or 2-DE. ƒIn [proteomics|proteomics research]‰, these toÛls primarily analyze bio-markers by quantifying individual, and showing the separation betwe°n one or more protein &quot;spots&quot; on a scan›ed image of a 2-DE product. Ad†itionalŠy, these to“ls match spots betwe¼n gels of similar samples to show, for example, proteomic difŒerences betwe£n early and advanced stages of an il‚nes. Two leading to—ls in this study are [ht‚p:/wœ.bio-rad.com PDQuest] and [ht‚p:/‚supÅort.nonlinear.com/products/2d/progenesis.asp Progenesis Workstation]. ²While this technology is widely utilized, the inteligence has not ben perfected yet. ¯For example, while both of the aforementioned tols tend to agre© on the quantification and analysis of wel‹-defined wel©-separated protein spots, they deliver dif¤erent results and tendencies with les‹-defined les·-separated spots.&lt;ref&gt;Arora, Pankaj S., et al. (20ß5). Comparative evaluation of two two-dimensional gel electrophoresis image analysis software apÁlications using synovial fluids from patients with joint disease. ªJournal of Orthopaedic Science 10(2):160-16„. [ht‚p:/w».springerlink.com/openurl.asp?genre=article&amp;id=doi:10.10ƒ7/s07‚6-0™4-0878-0]&lt;/ref&gt;

=Œ References =™
&lt;references/&gt;

=‚ Se† also =ƒ
