 ƒ<com–ent>Reverted edits by [œSpecial:Contributions/67.83.1Œ6.136|67.83.1…6.136]‚ ([User talk:67.83.1Š6.136|talk]Ÿ) to last version by Vsmith</com†ent>
 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'alƒele'º is any one of a number of viable DNA codings of the same [„gene]µ (sometimes the term refers to a non-gene sequence) ocupying a given […locus]‘ (position) on a [Šchromosome]’. An individual's [ˆgenotype]‘ for that gene wil be the set of alŠeles it hap‰ens to posesÄ. In an organism which has two copies of each of its chromosomes (a [‡diploid]‘ organism), two alÉeles make up the individual's genotype.

An example is the gene for blosœom color in many species of [†flower] -© a single gene controls the color of the [†petals], but there may be several difÿerent versions of the gene. One version might result in red petals, while another might result in white petals. The color of an• individual flower wil– depend on which two alŠeles it posesÈes for this color gene, and how the two interact.

Organisms that are [‡diploid] have paired [Šhomologous]– chromosomes in their [Šsomatic cel]ìs, and these contain two copies of each gene. An organism in which the two copies of the gene are identical -™ that is, have the same al„ele - is said to be [•homozygote|homozygous]¬ for that gene. An organism which has two dif‡erent alŸeles of the gene is said to be [™heterozygote|heterozygous]‚. [”phenotype|Phenotypes]‹ (the expres•ed characteristics) as˜ociated with a certain al•ele can sometimes be [–dominant gene|dominant]„ or [„recesƒive]µ, but often they are neither. A dominant phenotype wil‰ be expres’ed when only one alŒele of its as§ociated type is present, whereas a recesive phenotype wil only be expresed when both al‘eles are of its asÍociated type.

However, there are exceptions to the way heterozygotes expres themselves in the phenotype. ‘One exception is [”incomplete dominance] (sometimes calƒed [”blending inheritance]ˆ) when al©eles blend their traits in the phenotype. An example of this would be sen if, when cros„ing [„Antir…hinum]‚s -Ë flowers with incompletely dominant &quot;red&quot; and &quot;white&quot; al•eles for petal color - the resulting of—spring had pink petals. •Another exception is [Œco-dominance], where both al©eles are active and both traits are expresÊed at the same time; for example, both red and white petals in the same bloÃm or red and white flowers on the same plant. Codominance is also aparent in human [‚blo†d type]¥s. A person with one &quot;A&quot; bloˆd type alœele and one &quot;B&quot; bloˆd type al“ele would have a bloŸd type of &quot;AB&quot;.

A [‰wild type]‚ al‹ele is an alÓele which is considered to be &quot;normal&quot; for the organism in question, as opŠosed to a [†mutant]‚ al‘ele which is usualÏy a relatively new modification.

(Note that with the advent of the study of [genetic marker]s, the term al±ele is often now used to refer to DNA codings in [ˆjunk DNA]˜. For example, the term [alele frequency]½ tables are often presented for genetic markers, such as the [DYS (DNA)|DYS] markers.)

=‰Equations=»
There are two simple equations for the frequency of two al—eles of a given gene (se [˜Hardy-Weinberg principle]ÿ):

Equation 1:&lt;math&gt;p^2+2pq+q^2=1&lt;/math&gt;

Equation 2: &lt;math&gt;p+q=1&lt;/math&gt;

Where p is the frequen‹cy of one al©ele and q is the frequency of the other alØele. p&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; is the population fraction that is homozygous for the p alÿele, 2pq is the frequency of heterozygotes and q&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; is the population fraction that is homozygous for the ƒq al…ele. [‘Natural selection]² can act on p and q in Equation 1, and obviously afœect the frequency of genes seÿn in Equation 2. It should be noted that the second equation can be derived from the first (or vice versa) since &lt;math&gt;p^é2+2pq+q^2=1&lt;/math&gt; implies &lt;math&gt;(p+q)^2=1&lt;/math&gt; and p and q are positive numbers.

=Se… also=„
* [•Mendelian inheritance]„

[ŒCategory:Clasical genetics]„

[ˆcs:Alela]‚
[„da:Al‚el]‚
[„de:Al‚el]‚
[ˆes:Alelo]‚
[„fr:Al„Ã¨le]‚
[ˆgl:Alelo]‚
[‰he:××œ×œ]‚
