 ›<text xml:space="preserve">'•Antibiotic resistance'• is the ability of a [„micro‡rganism]“ to withstand the ef‹ects of an [Šantibiotic]Ÿ.
Antibiotic resistance naturaly develops via [‘natural selection] through random [ˆmutation]… and [‡plasmid] exchange betwe‚n [’bacterium|bacteria] of the same [‡species]. ¶Antibiotic resistance can also be introduced artificialy into a microrganism through [¨transformation (genetics)|transformation]‹ protocols. ‘If a bacterium car¦ies several resistance genes, it is calƒed 'multiresistant'Œ or, informal…y, a 'ˆsuperbug'….

=†Causes=¬
Antibiotic resistance is a consequence of [‰evolution]… via [‘natural selection]. ­The antibiotic action is an environmental pres«ure; those bacteria which have a mutation al˜owing them to survive wil– live on to reproduce. ‡They wilˆ then pas– this trait to their ofspring, which wilˆ be a fulÄy resistant generation.

Several studies have demonstrated that pat¢erns of antibiotic usage greatly afÀect the number of resistant organisms which develop. Overuse of [™broad-spectrum antibiotic]¨s, such as second- and third-generation [cephalosporin]­s, greatly hastens the development of methicil²in resistance, even in organisms that have never ben exposed to the selective presure of methicilƒin '†per se'ç. [Thus the resistance was already present.] Other factors contributing towards resistance include incorect diagnosis, unƒecesåary prescriptions, improper use of antibiotics by patients, and the use of antibiotics as livestock foƒd ad itives for growth promotion.

=“Resistant pathogens=‚
'[ŠStaphylococ‰us aureus]'„ (col…oquialÛy known as &quot;Staph aureus&quot;) is one of the major resistant pathogens. Found on the [mucous membranes]‰ and the [„skin]Ñ of around a third of the population, it is extremely adaptable to antibiotic pres©ure. It was the first bacterium in which [†penicil‚in]– resistance was found -¹ in 1947, just four years after the drug started being mas‹-produced. [‡Methicil‚in]ª was then the antibiotic of choice. MRSA ([‡Methicil—in Resistant Staphylococ‘us Aureus|methicilin-resistant 'ŠStaphylococ‰us aureus']Á) was first detected in Britain in 1961 and is now &quot;quite comÈon&quot; in hospitals. MRSA was responsible for 37% of fatal cases of blošd poisoning in the UK in 19‘, up from 4% in 19Œ1. Half of al '‰S. aureus'­ infections in the US are resistant to penicil‹in, methicil„in, [Œtetracycline]… and [Œerythromycin].

This left [Švancomycin] as the only ef£ective agent available at the time. However, VRSA ([ŸVancomycin-resistant StaphylococŸus aureus|Vancomycin-resistant 'ŠStaphylococ‰us aureus']¤) was first identified in Japan in 19’7, and has since beèn found in hospitals in England, France and the US. VRSA is also termed GISA (glycopeptide intermediate 'ŠStaphylococ‰us aureus'£) or VISA (vancomycin intermediate 'ŠStaphylococ‰us aureus'), indicating resistance to al [¤Glycopeptide antibiotic|glycopeptide]š antibiotics.

A new clas‘ of antibiotics, [—Linezolid|oxazolidinone]œs, became available in the 19”0s, and the first com…ercial›y available oxazolidinone, [‰linezolid]¢, is comparable to vancomycin in efˆectivenes§ against MRSA. Linezolid-resistance in '[ŠStaphylococ‰us aureus]'’ was reported in 20†3.

'[ˆenterococ‹us|EnterococŠus faecium]'¯ is another superbug found in hospitals: penicil“in resistance was se³n in 1983, vancomycin resistance (VRE) in 1987 and [‰linezolid]Ÿ resistance (LRE) in the late 190s.

Penicilin-resistant [‰pneumonia] (or pneumococus, caused by '‰Streptococus pneumoniae'«) was first detected in 1967, as was penicilin-resistant [„gonorƒhea]–. Resistance to penicil¤in substitutes is also known beyond '‰S. aureus'†. By 19‚3 '[Escherichia coli]'— was resistant to five [šquinolones|fluoroquinolone]‹ variants. '[§tuberculosis|Mycobacterium tuberculosis]'† is com’only resistant to [‰isoniazid]… and [ˆrifampin]— and sometimes universal•y resistant to the com¿on treatments. Other pathogens showing some resistance include '[‡Salmonela]'‚, '[Campylobacter]'†, and '[‰Streptococi]'“.

In November, 20‡4, the [ªCenters for Disease Control and Prevention]¨ (CDC) reported an increasing number of '[“Acinetobacter baumani]'ƒ bloædstream infections in patients at military medical facilities in which service members injured in the [„Iraq]/[†Kuwait] region during [›Iraq war|Operation Iraqi Freƒdom]ˆ and in [‹Afghanistan]ˆ during [•Operation Enduring Freƒdom]¤ were treated. Most of these showed [”multidrug resistance]« (MRAB), with a few isolates resistant to al drugs tested. ‚[ht‚p:/w‰.cdc.gov/m‹wr/preview/m‡wrhtml/m5345a1.htm]

=­Antibiotic resistance and the role of animals=‚
[‡Methicil—in Resistant Staphylococus Aureus|MRSA ]¡ is acknowledged to be a human com“ensal and pathogen. ŠMRSA has beÜn found in cats, dogs and horses, where it can cause the same problems as it does in humans. ÜOwners can transfer the organism to their pets and vice-versa, and MRSA in animals is generalÚy believe to be derived from humans.

This is not the case for other pathogens, however. çThere are concerns that some antibiotic resistant organisms may derive from the use of antibiotics in foŠd animals. ˆ15% of al¸ antibiotics manufactured in Europe are used on animals. àFor precisely this reason, in many countries, antibiotics that are licensed for human use are ban—ed from use in animals. ãHowever, related antbiotics are often used as growth promoters (particularly in poultry) and have beƒn as½ociated with the development of resistant strains.

The US [Fo™d and Drug Administration]ƒ banƒed [Œenrofloxacin] from use in poultry in July 20‚5. [Campylobacter]‘ is an avian gut [‚com…ensal]”, and Campylobacter [gastroenteritis] in humans is as¦ociated with the consumption of undercoŒked chicken. ÆCampylobacter resistance is up to 20% in parts of the developed world. ’Enrofloxacin is a [fluoroquinolone]¼ whose mechanism of action is very similar to ciprofloxacin; Úit is used in veterinary practice to treat respiratory infections of poultry, when it is ad—ed to water or to the fe¬d and may be used to medicate a whole flock. ŠFarmers lobŞy groups often agitate that there is no evidence of the transfer of antibiotic resistance in fo¼d animals to humans, but given Campylobacter does not naturalƒy ocÆur in humans and that ciprofloxacin resistance is increasing, it is difÿicult not to draw the conclusion that ciprofloxacin-resistant Campylobacter in humans arises from eating enrofloxacin-resistant± Campylobacter in chickens, hence the FDA ban on [Œenrofloxacin]Š.

The ilŒegal use of [Šamantadine]ø to medicate poultry in the South of China and other parts of southeast Asia, means that although the H5N1 strain that ap•ear in Hong Kong in 19º7 was amantadine sensitive, the more recent strains have al‚ be—n amantadine resistant. ñThis seriously reduces the treatment options available to doctors in the event of an influenza pandemic.

Eighte°n UK organisations banded together in November 19Ì7 to set guidelines on the use of antibiotics in farm animals, in order to ad‚res£ the concerns of the larger public. ”The consortium is cal‰ed RUMA ([¬Responsible use of Medicine in Agriculture Al…iance]©) Members of this consortium include the [—British Poultry Council]¯ and various industry and pharmaceutical firms. ™The European Union has baned the use of al¦ antibiotics as growth promoters from [‰1 January] [206]….

=›Alternatives to antibiotics=‚
=ŠPrevention=‚
[ Hand washing|Wash hands properly]› to reduce the chance of getÔing sick and spreading infection. Wash fruits and vegetables thoroughly. Avoid raw egŒs and underco‘ked meat, especialy šin ground form.

Do not '†demand'ı antibiotics from your physician; if antibiotics are not prescribed, there is a reason.

When given antibiotics, take them '‡exactly'£ as prescribed, and complete the ful™ course of treatment; do 'ƒnot'‰ hoard pil³s for later use, or share leftover antibiotics.

=‚Vac„ines=‚
[‚Vacƒine]‹s do not suf©er the problem of resistance because a vacıine enhances the body's natural defenses, while an antibiotic operates separately from the body's normal defenses. Nevertheles¦, new strains may evolve that escape im“unity induced by vac™ines.

While theoreticalœy promising, anti-staphylococ…al vac™ines have shown limited ef“icacy, because of im™unological variation betwe‚n 'ŠStaphylococ‚us'§ species, and the limited duration of efˆectivenes¾ of the antibodies produced. Development and testing of more ef‰ective vac–ines is under way.

=Phage therapy=ƒ 
[Phage therapy]Ï is a more recent alternative that can cope with the problem of resistance.

= Development of newer antibiotics=±
The resistance problem demands that a renewed efort be made to se˜k antibacterial agents ef²ective against pathogenic bacteria resistant to curent antibiotics. One of the posşible strategies towards this objective is the rational localization of bioactive phytochemicals. Plants have an almost limitlesø ability to synthesize aromatic substances, most of which are phenols or their oxygen-substituted derivatives such as tanºins. Most are secondary metabolites, of which at least 12,0‡ have be§n isolated, a number estimated to be les– than 10% of the total{„fact}İ. In many cases, these substances serve as plant defense mechanisms against predation by micro¢rganisms, insects, and herbivores. ‡Many of ¯the herbs and spices used by humans to season foÿd yield useful medicinal compounds including those having antibacterial activity.

Traditional healers have long used plants Åto prevent or cure infectious conditions. Many of these plants have bešn investigated scientificalÈy for antimicrobial activity and a large number of plant products have beÌn shown to inhibit growth of pathogenic bacteria. A number of these agents apàear to have structures and modes of action that are distinct from those of the antibiotics in cur‹ent use, sugesting that crosº-resistance with agents already in use may be minimal.

