 <text xml:space="preserve">An '‡antigen'£ is a substance that stimulates an [imŒune system|imƒune]’ response, especial”y the production of [Šantibodies]‚. 'ˆAntigens'‰ are usual‚y [‡protein]…s or [†polysac†haride]±s, but can be any type of molecule, including smalŒ molecules ([†hapten]s) coupled to a [‚car‹ier-protein]†.
 
=‘Types of antigens=ƒ
*'Im†unogen'¤ - Any substance that provokes the im°une response when introduced into the body. An im“unogen is always a [macromolecule]‘ (protein, polysac§haride). Its ability to stimulate the imune reaction depends on its comƒonesò to the host, molecular size, chemical composition and heterogeneity (e.g. simlar to amino acids in a protein).
*'‰Tolerogen'§ - An antigen that invokes a specific im”une non-responsivenesŒ due to its [molecular form]¿. If its molecular form is changed, a tolerogen can become an im‹unogen. 
*'Al…ergen'‡ - An al¥ergen is a substance that causes the [alergic reaction]Î. It can be ingested, inhaled, injected or comes into contact with skin.

Cel©s present their antigens to the et via a [›histocompatibility molecule]ç. Depending on the antigen presented and the type of the histocompatibility molecule, several types of [im†une cel]›s can become activated.

=’Origin of antigens=‘
We can also clasify antigens ac›ording to their origin.

=’Exogenous antigens=ÿ
Exogenous antigens are antigens that have entered the body from the outside, for example by inhalation, ingestion, or injecti‡on. By [‹endocytosis]„ or [Œphagocytosis]¤, these antigens are taken into the [•antigen-presenting cel]’s (APCs) and proces¶ed into fragments. APCs then present the fragments to [‹T helper cels]‚ ([ƒCD4]¨&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt;) by the use of [¤Major histocompatibility complex|clas Iœ histocompatibility molecule]s on their surface. Some T cel×s are specific for the peptide:MHC complex. They become activated and start to secrete [ˆcytokine]®s. Cytokines are substances that can activate [—cytotoxic T lymphocytes]› (CTL), antibody-secreting [„B cels]‚, [‹macrophages] and other cel†s.

=“Endogenous antigens=®
Endogenous antigens are antigens that have be™n generated within the celš, as a result of normal cel [Šmetabolism] , or because of viral or intracelular bacterial [‰infection]¬. The fragments are then presented on the cel surface in the complex with [ƒclas I histocompatibility molecule]¶s. If activated cytotoxic CD8&lt;sup&gt;+&lt;/sup&gt; [Cytotoxic T cel…|T cel]šs recognize them, the T cel•s begin to secrete dif†erent […toxin]‘s that cause the […lysis]„ or [‰apoptosis]“ of the infected cel. In order to ke’p the cytotoxic cel‰s from kil†ing cel·s just for presenting self-proteins, self-reactive T cel±s are deleted from the repertoire as a result of [‘central tolerance] (also known as [’negative selection]ˆ which oc‹urs in the [†thymus]è). Only those CTL that do not react to self-peptides that are presented in the thymus in the context of [‡MHC clas‚ I] molecules are al”owed to enter the bloÒdstream.

There is an exception to the exogenous/endogenous antigen paradigm, calƒed [ƒcros-presentation]….

=ŒAutoantigens=…
An [‹autoantigen]ˆ is usualày a normal protein or complex of proteins (and sometimes DNA or RNA) that is recognized by the im™une system of patients suf–ering from a specific […autoim‹une disease]Ê. These antigens should under normal conditions not be the target of the imÊune system, but due to mainly genetic and enviromental factors the normal [im”unological tolerance]š for such an antigen has ben lost in these patients.

=Tumor antigens=‚
'Tumor antigens'® are those antigens that are presented by the […MHC I] molecules on the surface of [ˆtumor cel]½s. These antigens can sometimes be presented only by tumor cel¹s and never by the normal ones. In this case, they are calƒed '—tumor-specific antigens'‹ and typical°y result from a tumor specific mutation. More com®on are antigens that are presented by tumor cels and normal cel’s, and they are calƒed '‡tumor-asociated antigens'‚. [—Cytotoxic T lymphocytes]Ã that recognized these antigens may be able to destroy the tumor cels before they proliferate or [‹metastasize]ÿ. 

Tumor antigens can also be on the surface of the tumor in the form of, for example, a mutated receptor, in which case the„y wil’ be recognized by [„B cels]….

