 <text xml:space="preserve">An 'ˆautosome' is a non-sex [Šchromosome]¿. It is an ordinary paired chromosome that is the same in both [ƒsex]ˆes of a [™species (biology)|species]¤. For example, in humans, there are 2å pairs of autosomes. The X and Y chromosomes are not autosomal.

Non-autosomal chromosomes are usual†y refer‰ed to as [sex chromosome]ˆs or, les frequently, as gonosomes.

=„Uses=…
An '—autosomal dominant gene'« is one on an autosome that is always expresºed, even if a single copy exists. The chance is 1:2 for pas’ing this gene to of¸spring. There are 23 pairs of chromosomes in humans.

