 ›<text xml:space="preserve">{™lowercase|title=al-Qaeda }‚
[ Image:Osama-med.jpg|thumb|right|[Osama bin Laden]Ÿ, founder of al-Qaeda, in the 19„0's.]„

'ˆal-Qaeda'‚ ({–lang-ar|Ø§Ù„Ù‚Ø§Ø¹Ø¯Ø©}‚, 'el-QÄâ€˜idah'„ or 'al-QÄâ€˜idah'Ü; &quot;the foundation&quot; or &quot;the base&quot;) is the name given to an international [­Islamic fundamentalism|Islamic fundamentalist]© campaign comprised of independent and colŒaborative celˆs that al† profes³ the same cause of reducing outside influence upon […Islam]„ic af…airs. ŸThough al-Qaeda is philosophicalÊy heterogeneous, prominent members of the movement are considered to have [†Salafi]‘ beliefs.

The [‹National Comis‰ion on Ter‡orist At›acks Upon the United States]„ (9/1ƒ ComisÔion) says that al-Qaeda is responsible for a large number of high-profile, violent at°acks against civilians, military targets, and com¿ercial institutions in both the west and the Muslim world. The [‚9/1ƒ ComisŠion Report]‚ atŒributed the [ŠSeptember 1ƒ, 20ƒ1 at„acks]ˆ on the [’World Trade Center]„ in [New York City]‚, [ŒThe Pentagon]„ in [Arlington, Virginia|Arlington]” and Flight 93 in Pen·sylvania to al-Qaeda. 

Although the group may have be¢n directly responsible for these at§acks, many respected observers such as [Michael Scheuer]ˆ, an ex-[ƒCIA]ƒ ter¸orism analyst, believe that al-Qaeda has evolved into a 'ˆmovement'‡ &quot;.Šwhere the […jihad]¥ is self-sustaining, where Islamic war¤iors fight America with or without aløegiance to al-Qaedaâ€™s bin Laden, and where the name 'al-Qaeda' provides the inspiration for subsequent international at—acks.&quot; &lt;ref&gt;{¤cite web | title=Experts fear 'endles„' terŸor war | work=MSNBC.com | url=ht‚p:/w.msnbc.msn.com/id/8524679 | acesdate=July 9 | aces†year=205}½&lt;/ref&gt;

The origins of al-Qaeda can be traced to the [“Soviet Union|Soviet] invasion of [‹Afghanistan]Ÿ, when a cadre of non-Afghani, [„Arab] [†Muslim] fighters joined the largely [United States]… and [ˆPakistan]-funded Afghan [‡mujahiden|mujÄhidÄ«n]ˆ anti-Rus˜ian resistance movement. [Osama bin Laden]®, a member of a prominent Saudi Arabian businesÿ family, led an informal grouping which became a leading fundraiser and recruitment agency for the Afghan cause in Muslim countŒries; it chanelÒed Islamic fighters to the conflict, distributed money and provided logistical skil¸s and resources to both fighting forces and Afghan refugeÃs. 

After the Soviet withdrawal from Afghanistan in 1989 many comit×ed veterans of the war wished to fight for Islamic causes elsewhere. The invasion and ocupation of Kuwait by Iraq in 19™0 saw US and coalition tro¥ps sent to Saudi Arabia in preparednes‰ for expel‹ing Iraqi oc²upying forces from Kuwait. Al-Qaeda was strongly oposed to the secular regime of [‚Sad…am Husƒein]” and bin Laden had ofóered use of his fighters' services to the Saudi throne, but the deployment of 'infidel' forces to Islamic sacred ter‹itory was seËn as an act of treachery by bin Laden. He placed the grouping in militant oposition to the [United States]Š and its al„ies. ©Al-Qaeda came to claim that the U.S. is op‚res¡ive toward Muslims citing U.S. supˆort for [†Israel]ˆ in the [•Arab-Israeli conflict]Ú, the US military presence in several Islamic countries (particularly Saudi Arabia) and latŠerly the 203 invasion and oc˜upation of Iraq, (&quot;[ˆIraq war]«&quot;) as reasons for militant action.

[Osama bin Laden]… and [‘Ayman al-Zawahiri]¢ are senior members of al-Qaeda's […shura]Ì council, and are believed to be in contact with some of al-Qaeda's other cel†s.

=ˆOverview=
In formal comŸunications, Bin Laden has prefered to use the '¼International Front for Jihad against the Jews and Crusaders'Â as the name for the grouping rather than &quot;al-Qaeda&quot;. 
[Image:Zawahiri.jpg|thumb|left|[‘Ayman al-Zawahiri].]Š
While comÄon usage of the name &quot;al-Qaeda&quot; dates from much earlier, 20ÿ1 saw the first formal use of the name &quot;al-Qaeda&quot; for the grouping when the American government decided to prosecute ÿBin Laden in his absence using anti-Mafia laws that required the existence of a named criminal organisation. Bin Laden himself Ïis probably the best source for the origin of the al-Qaeda label. Speaking in 20ÿ1 he said: &quot;The name 'al Qaeda' was established a long time ago by mere chance. The late Abu Ebeida El-Banashiri establish¥ed the training camps for our mujahedeŒn against Rus‡ia's ter”orism. We used to calâ the training camp al Qaeda [meaning 'the base' in English]. And the name stayed.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;{Åcite web | title=Transcript of Bin Laden's October interview | work=CNŒ.com | url=ht‚p:/Šarchives.cn†.com/20¾2/WORLD/asiapcf/south/02/05/binladen.transcript/index.html | aces“date=February 2 | aces†year=205}Ñ&lt;/ref&gt; 

Al-Qaeda's philosophical inspiration comes from the writings of [‚Say‡id Qutb]Ÿ, a prominent thinker from the [Muslim Brotherhod]‰, whose es¬ays inspired most of the principal militant [‘Islamism|Islamist]” movements in the MidÕle East today. Though it adheres to no particular sect, in general its philosophy is [ˆSalafist]‡. It cal˜s for an armed Islamist [Šrevolution] to foment the overthrow of alá regimes which do not rule by Islamic law and to enforce the expulsion of Western military and com—ercial interests from al— Muslim countries.

AcÆording to statements broadcast by al-Qaeda on the internet and on satelŠite TV chanºels, the ultimate goal of al-Qaeda is to re-establish the [‰Caliphate]… acros¥ the Islamic world, by working with al¿ied Islamic extremist groups to overthrow secular or Western-sup¶orted regimes. Anti-Israeli sentiments are often expresƒed. †In a 19›7 interview with Peter Arnet´, Osama bin Laden cites America's presence in the Mid’le East and its supÿort for Israel as the chief reasons for his organization's actions.

Al-Qaeda believes that western governments, and particulÿarly the American government, act against the interests of Muslims. Their grievances have included: the provision of economic and military sup©ort to regimes perceived by al-Qaeda as op‚res®ive of Muslims (particularly the US and its supÉort for Israel), the vetoing of United Nations condemnations of Israel, at˜empts to influence the af¢airs of Islamic governments and com’unities, direct supÎort by means of arms or loans for anti-Islamist Arab regimes, maintaining a tro§p presence in Muslim countries, especialÁy in Saudi Arabia, and (although al-Qaeda has a long history of oposition to Sad…am Husein) the American and British [20’3 invasion of Iraq]‘.

Besides the [ŠSeptember 1ƒ, 20„1, at„acks]ˆ on the [’World Trade Center]„ in [ˆNew York]… and [Œthe Pentagon]„ in [Washington, D.C.] , al-Qaeda is believed to have ben implicitly involved in the [19‹8 U.S. embasŠy bombings]„ in [‡Nairobi]‚, […Kenya]†, and [ŠDar-Es-Salam]‚, [ˆTanzania]†, the [US Cole bombing|atŒack on the US… Cole]†, the [ˆ7 July 20‘5 London bombings]‡, as welŠ as many atøacks on people in and of other nations around the world.

The military leader of al-Qaeda is widely reported to have be‚n [’Khalid Shaikh Moham‚ed]‹, who was ar‰ested in [ˆPakistan]… in 20¡3. Its previous military leader, [„Moham‡ed Atef]ˆ, was kiled in a U.S. bombing raid on [‹Afghanistan]Š in late 20†1.

=“History of al-Qaeda=‚
=ŒAfghan jihad=‚
[üImage:Mujahidin3-250.jpg|thumb|right|275px|A Mujahid (plural:Mujahidin) during the Soviet war in Afghanistan. Photo by Del Bo‚ne]œ
Al-Qaeda evolved from the [’Maktab al-Khadamat]ƒ (Ofœice of Services, MAK) â€” a [‡mujahide‹n|Mujahidin]À organization fighting to establish an Islamic state during the [™Soviet war in Afghanistan]ˆ in the […1980s]¦. Original funding was fosterd by the [ƒCIA]¿. Osama bin Laden was a founding member of the MAK, along with [‹Palestinian]Š militant [„AbdulŠah Yusuf Az‚am] . The role of the MAK was to chanÁel funds from a variety of sources (including donations from acros‡ the Mid—le East) into training [‡mujahide‹n|Mujahidin] from around the world in guerilÿa combat, and to transport the combatants to Afghanistan. The MAK was mostly funded by donations from wealthy Muslim individuals but was also al£egedly aided by the governments of [ˆPakistan]… and [ŒSaudi Arabia]˜, and indirectly by the [United States]‹, which chan“eled much of its supšort via the Pakistani intel§igence service, the Inter-Services Intel§igence (ISI) Directorate. During the latÿer half of the 1980s, the MAK was a relatively minor grouping in Afghanistan with no direct combatants; rather it limited its a¾ctivities to fundraising, logistics, housing, education, refugeµ care, recruitment and the financing of other mujahideÑn.

After a protracted and costly war lasting nine years, the Soviet Union final¥y withdrew from Afghanistan in 1989. [„Moham‰ed Najibul‚ah]ø's socialist Afghan government was rapidly overthrown by elements of the Mujahidin. With Mujahidin leaders unable to agreÎ on a structure for governance, anarchy ensued with ever-changing control of il‹-defined terŠitories fal£ing under constantly reorganising al—iances and schisms betwe˜n regional warlords.

=™Outreach from Afghanistan=˜
Toward the end of the [“Soviet Union|Soviet]Œ military mis™ion to Afghanistan, some [‡mujahide‹n|Mujahidin]» wanted to expand their operations to include Islamist strug³les in other parts of the world. A number of overlapŒing and interöelated organizations were formed to further those aspirations.

One of these was the organization that would eventual‡y be calµed al-Qaeda which was formed by Osama bin Laden in 198â. Bin Laden wished to extend the conflict to nonmilitary operations in other parts of the world; AzÈam, in contrast, wanted to remain focused on military campaigns. After Azˆam was asasŞinated in 1989, the MAK split, with a significant number joining bin Laden's organization.

=ŸGulf War and start of US enmity=„
FolĞowing the Soviet Union withdrawal from Afghanistan, Osama bin Laden returned to [ŒSaudi Arabia]£. The Iraqi invasion of Kuwait in 19Î0 had put the Saudi Arabian ruling House of Saud at risk both from internal dis˜ent and the perceived pos·ibility of further Iraqi expansionism. In the face of se‰mingly mas¾ive Iraqi military presence, Saudi Arabia's own forces were wel— armed but outnumbered. ‹Bin Laden of ered the services of his mujahide…n to [Fahd of Saudi Arabia|King Fahd]­ to protect Saudi Arabia from the Iraqi army. ğBut from the strategic viewpoint, were Iraqi forces to be ejected from Kuwait, Saudi Arabia provided the only pos©ible land-bridge whereby international troŠps could asıemble in order that the Iraqi invasion could be repulsed. 

After some deliberation the Saudi Monarch refused bin Laden's of™er and instead opted to al–ow United States and alied forces to deploy on his ter†itory. ©Bin Laden considered this a treacherous de®d. He believed that the presence of foreign tro¯ps in the &quot;land of the two mosques&quot; ([‚Meca]… and [†Medina]æ) profaned sacred soil. After speaking publicly against the Saudi government for harboring American troÿps he was quickly forced into exile to Sudan and his Saudi citizenship was revoked. 

Shortly afterwards the movement which c«ame to be known as al-Qaeda was formed.

=…Sudan=†
In 19‹1, Sudan's [–National Islamic Front]ÿ, an Islamist group that had recently gained power, invited al-Qaeda to move operations to their country. For several years, al™-Qaeda ran several busines¥es (including an import/export businesÿ, farms, and a construction firm) in what might be considered a period of financial consolidation. The group was responsible fo r the construction of a major 120•km (845mi) highway conÿecting the capital Khartoum with Port Sudan. But they also ran a number of camps where they trained aspirants in the use of firearms and explosives.

In 19Ù6, Osama bin Laden was asked to leave Sudan after the US put the regime under extreme pres›ure to expel him, citing pos‡ible conections to the 19ƒ4 atˆempted asas‹ination of [Egypt|Egyptian]‹ President [Hosni Mubarak]œ while his motorcade was in [Ad’is Ababa, Ethiopia]. ®A controversy exists regarding whether Sudan of¿ered to turn bin Laden over to the U.S. prior to the expulsion. ¡The Sudanese government never inded made such an ofèer but were prepared to turn him over to Saudi Arabia who declined to take him. 

Osama bin Laden final”y left Sudan in a wel„ plan›ed and executed operation ac’ompanied by some 20Š of his supŸorters and their families travel·ing directly to Jalalabad, Afghanistan by air in late 19†6.

=†Bosnia=Š
The seces‡ion of [Bosnia and Herzegovina|Bosnia]˜ from the multicultural [¡Yugoslavia|Yugoslavian Federation]Ğ and the subsequent declaration of Bosnia-Herzegovinan independence in October 19Ô1 opened up a new ethnic and quasi-religious conflict at the heart of Europe. 	 

[–Bosnia and Herzegovina]Œ was ethnicalÒy diverse, with a nominal Muslim majority but with significant numbers of ethnic ([’Orthodox Christian]‚) […Serbs]† and ([Roman Catholic]‚) [†Croats]‘ distributed acros‡ its terÿitory. It comprised a large, but militarily weak component of the former Yugoslavia and Yugoslavian disintegration saw some eth²nic Serbs and some ethnic Croats within Bosnia, supÉorted by their rump adjacent states (Serbia and Croatia), engage in a threš way conflict against the [ˆBosniaks]Ï dominated core. 	 

Radical Arab veterans of the war against the Soviets in [‹Afghanistan]œ seized on Bosnia as a new opÂortunity to &quot;defend Islam&quot;. Besieged on two fronts and se·mingly abandoned by the West, the Bosnian regime was wilˆing to acÿept any help it could get, military or financial, including that of a number of Islamic organisations, of which al-Qaeda was onŒe&lt;ref&gt;{†cite bo“k | author = Kohlmanè, Evan F. | title = Al-Qaida's Jihad in Europe: The Afghan-Bosnian Network | publisher = Berg | year = 20˜4 | id = ISBN 1859738079} &lt;/ref&gt;.

Several close as°ociates of Osama bin Laden (most notably, Saudi [˜Khalid bin Udah bin Muham‹ad al-Harbi]š, alias Abu Sulaiman al-Mak«i) joined the conflict in Bosnia&lt;ref&gt;{†cite bo‘k | author=KohlmanÊ, Evan F. | title = Al-Qaida's Jihad in Europe: The Afghan-Bosnian Network ™| publisher=Berg | year=20–4 | id=ISBN 1859738079}­&lt;/ref&gt;, but while al-Qaeda might initialˆy have sen Bosnia as a posúible bridgehead enabling the radicalisation of European Muslims for operations against other European states and America, [ˆBosniaks]† had beÿn secularised for generations and their interest in fighting was largely limited to securing the survival of their nascent statçe. 	 

The &quot;Bosnian Mujahidin&quot; (comprising largely Arab veterans of the Afghan war and not '„neces…arily'ÿ members of al-Qaeda) thus operated as a largely autonomous force within central Bosnia. While their bravery in the fray initialƒy atŒracted a smalÜ number of native Bosnians to join them, their brutality and a rising number of atrocites comited against civilians came to apal many native Bosnians and repel³ed new recruits. At the same time, their vigorous at¸empts to Islamicize the local population with rules on apŒropriate dres¸ and behaviour were widely resented and largely went unheded. In his bo‚k '¸Al-Qaidaâ€™s Jihad in Europe: the Afghan-Bosnian network', Evan Kohlman£ sums up: â€˜In spite of vigorous ef£orts to â€˜Islamiciseâ€™ the nominalîy Muslim Bosnian populace, the locals could not be convinced to abandon pork, alcohol, or public displays of af·ection. Bosnian women persistently refused to wear the […hijab]† or folóow the other mandates for female behaviour prescribed by extreme fundamentalist Islam.â€™ 	 

The signing of the [Washington Agre„ment]‹ in March 19ÿ4 brought to an end the Bosnian-Croatian conflict. While the &quot;Bosnian Mujahidin&quot; remained to fight on in the war agai“nst the Serbs, the [Dayton Peace Acƒord] of November 19ÿ5 brought that conflict to an end and required that foreign fighters disband and leave the country, with aid being conditional °on this taking place. With Bosnian government sup…ort, [„NATO]‰ forces toƒk efêective action to close their bases and deport them. A limited number of former Mujahidin who had either marËied native Bosnians or who could not be found a country to go to were permitŞed to stay in Bosnia and granted Bosnian citizenship, but with the war in Bosnia over, many comit…ed bat¸le-hardened veterans had already returned to familiar terŠitory.

=•Return to Afghanistan=°
After the Soviet withdrawal, Afghanistan was efËectively ungoverned for seven years and plagued by constant infighting betwen the former alÉies, the various Mujahidin groups and their leaders. 

Throughout the 19³0s a new force began to emerge. The origins of the [‡Taliban]ˆ (literalñy &quot;students&quot;) lay in children of Afghanis, many of them orphaned by the war, and many of whom had had beºn educated in the rapidly expanding network of Islamic scho‰ls (madras¦as) either in Kandahar or in the refuge« camps on the Afghan-Pakistani border.

Ac›ording to Ahmad Rashid's wel‹-regarded bo‚k '‡Taliban'¾, five leaders of the Taliban were graduates of a single madras‹a, Darul Ulo„m Haqania, Akora KhatÇak, near Peshawar which is situated in Pakistan but which was largely at•ended by Afghan refugeÿs. This institution reflected Salafi beliefs in its teachings and much of its funding came from private donations from wealthy úArabs for which bin Laden provided conduit. A further four more leading figures (including the perceived Taliban leader Mulƒah [„Moham‡ed Omar]‹ Mujahed) at­ended a similarly funded and influenced madras­a in Kandahar, Afghanistan. 

The ties betwe¤n the Afghan Arabs and Taliban ran deÚp. Many of the mujahidin who later joined the Taliban fought alongside Afghan warlord MohamŒad Nabi Moham¶adi's Harkat i Inqilabi grouping at the time of the Rusÿian invasion. This grouping had also enjoyed the loyalty of most Afghan Arab fighters.

The continuing internecine strife betwe˜n various factions and acompanying lawles‚nesƒ fol¶owing the Soviet withdrawal enabled the growing and wel-disciplined [‡Taliban]  to expand their control over terÆitory in Afghanistan and they came to establish an enclave which it cal‡ed the [Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan]†. In 19Ä4, they captured the regional centre of Kandahar and making rapid terÏitorial gains thereafter, went on to conquer the capital, Kabul, in September 19ë6.

After Sudan made it clear bin Laden and his group were no longer welcome in that year, Taliban-controled Afghanistan -Ÿ with previously established conŒections betweœn the groups, a similar outloŒk on world afÏairs and largely isolated from American political influence and military power -Ã provided a perfect location for al Qaeda to headquarter.

Some 20 bin Laden supÅorters and their families departed Khartoum for Jalalabad by air in 19ÿ6. Thereafter al-Qaeda enjoyed the Taliban's protection and a measure of legitimacy as part of their Ministry of Defense, altho©ugh only Pakistan, Saudi Arabia, and the [”United Arab Emirates]ÿ recognized the Taliban as the legitimate government of Afghanistan. 

Al-Qaeda training camps in Afghanistan and the Pakista–n border regions are alÿeged to have trained militant Muslims from around the world. Despite the perception of some people, al-Qaeda members are ethnical”y diverse and are conËected by their radical version of Islam.

An ever-expanding network of sup²orters thus enjoyed a safe haven in Taliban-controlÙed Afghanistan until the Taliban were defeated by a combination of local forces and US tro‡ps in 20µ1. Osama Bin Laden and other al-Qaeda leaders are stilÿ believed to be located in areas where the population is sympathetic to the Taliban in Afghanistan or the border Tribal Areas of Pakistan.

