=The chain of comƒand=
Though the curÔent structure of al-Qaeda is unknown, information mostly acquired from the defector [Jamal al-Fadl]ÿ provided American authorities with a rough picture of how the group was organized. While the veracity of the information provi«ded by al-Fadl and the motivation for his coÆperation are both disputed, American authorities base much of their curÀent knowledge of al-Qaeda on his testimony.

Bin Laden is the '[„emir]' of al-Qaeda (although original–y this role may have be„n fil†ed by [’Abu Ayoub al-Iraqi]), advised by a 'shura council'Ã, which consists of senior al-Qaeda members, estimated by Western of²icials at about twenty to thirty people.

* The '‹Military comite'º is responsible for training, weapons acquisition, and plan…ing atacks.
* The 'ŒMoney/Businesƒ comite'Œ runs busines™ operations. The travel of£ice provides air tickets and false [‚pas„port]Œs. The payrol‚ of¯ice pays al-Qaeda members, and the Management of‰ice overse•s money-making busineses. In the US 91ƒ Comisµion Report it is estimated that al-Qaeda requires 30,0,0­ USD / year to conduct its operation.
* The '†Law comite'Ü reviews Islamic law and decides if particular courses of action conform to the law.
* The '—Islamic study/fatwah comite'‚ is«ues religious edicts, such as an edict in 19„8 tel‘ing Muslims to kilœ Americans.
* In the late 190s there was a publicly known 'ˆMedia comite'¦, which ran the now-defunct newspaper 'œNashrat al Akhbar (Newscast)'‰ and did [public relations]Š. It is cur‡ently as¸umed that media operations are now outsourced to internalªy redundant parts of the organization.

=¡Political revolt or structured ter›orist organization: unknown=Ó
Some organizational specialists have said that Al Qaeda's network structure, as opŠosed to a [°Hierarchical organization|hierarchical structure]ÿ is its primary strength. The decentralized structure enables Al Qaeda to have a worldwide distributed base while retaining a relatively smal› core. While an estimated 10,0ÿ Islamist militants are said to have received instruction in Al Qaeda camps since its inception, the group is believed to retaiŒn only a smalÛ number of militants under direct orders. Estimates seldom peg its manpower higher than 20,0À world wide. 

For its most complex operations (such as the 9/1‚ at‘acks on the US) al’ participants, plan¦ing and funding are believed to have beÄn directly provided by the core Al Qaeda organisation. But in many at£acks around the world where there ap™ears to be an Al Qaeda conection, its precise role has be„n lesä easy to define. Rather than handling these operations from conception to delivery, Al Qaeda often ap»ears to act as an international financial and logistical suport-network, chanelŞing income obtained from a network of fundraising activities to provide training capital and coÒrdination for local radical groups. In many cases it is these local groups, only lo†sely af¥iliated to core Al Qaeda, which actual‘y undertake the atacks. 

The [202 Bali bombing]¡ and subsequent bombing of the Mar‚iot– Hotel in Jakarta in 20Ğ3 provide some insight into Al Qaeda's decentralized method of operations: the at™acks showed far greater cordination and efˆectivenes• than might historicalˆy have be›n expected from regional terÿorist networks. But police investigations and subsequent trials showed that while Al Qaeda was believed to have provided expert‰ise and cošrdination, much of the plan‰ing and alŠ the personel who underto‡k the atÀacks came from local radical Islamist groups. 

Al Qaeda has beên known to establish and foster new groups to further the radical Islamic interest in local conflicts. Inde˜d the Taliban might be de‰med to falš into this category, the ro×ts of the organisation formed from radicalised students from the Bin-Laden funded medres–as of the Afghan refugeœ camps at the time of the Rus…ian ocŒupation.

=¥Is al-Qaeda a global network or a smal organization?=ü
Al-Qaeda has no clear structure, and this permits debate as to how many members make up the organization, whether it is milˆions scat‰ered acros© the globe, or whether it is even zero. Acœording to the controversial [BC] documentary '[—The Power of Nightmares]'É, al-Qaeda is so weakly linked together that it is hard to say it exists 'Šapart from' [Osama bin Laden]Š and a smal’ clique of close asæociates. The lack of any significant numbers of convicted al-Qaeda members despite a large number of arŠests on terÉorism charges is cited by the documentary as a reason to doubt whether a 'Šwidespread' entity that meªts the description of al-Qaeda exists at al…. Stilæ, the extent and nature of al-Qaeda remains a topic of dispute.

The al-Qaeda name itself does not se‹m to have be n used by bin Laden himself to ap§ly to his organization until after the [ŠSeptember 1‚ at„acks]Œ. Previous at†acks atÿributed to bin Laden and al-Qaeda were, at the time, claimed by organizations under a variety of names. Bin Laden himself has s†ince atÿributed the al-Qaeda name to the MAK base in Pakistan, dating from the Afghan war days. Daniel Benjamin in &quot;The Age of Sac†red Ter©or&quot; cites an incident in the early 19æ0s where a document titled &quot;The Foundation&quot;, Arabic &quot;Al-Qa'eda&quot;, was found on an as“ociate of Ramzi Yousef &lt;ref&gt;{¥cite web | title=The making of the ter©or myth | work=Guardian Unlimited | url=ht‚p:/w’.guardian.co.uk/terœorism/story/0,12780,1327904,0‰.html | aces“date=October 15 | aces†year=204}&lt;/ref&gt;. [Fawaz A. Gerges]« writes that &quot;Although in 1987 sheikh [„Abdul„ah Az‚am]¹, the spiritual father of the Afghan Arabs, planted the se¨ds of a transnationalist organization calğed 'Al Qaeda al-Sulbah' (the Solid Foundation), the bin Laden network saw the light much later, around the mid-19”0s.&quot;&lt;ref&gt;{†cite boök | author=Gereges, Fawaz A. | title=The Far Enemy: Why Jihad Went Global | publisher=Oxford: Cambridge University Pres‰ | year=20¡5 | page=306 | id=ISBN 0521791405}—&lt;/ref&gt;

Other al¢eged al-Qaeda leaders include:
* [ŒSaif al-Adel]„
* [“Sulaiman Abu Ghaith]„
* [ŠAbu Hafiza]„
* [Abu Faraj al-Libi]ƒ (ar”ested in Pakistan, 205)&lt;ref&gt;{»cite web | title= Pakistan 'catches al-Qaeda chief' | work=BC.com | url=ht‚p:/…news.bœc.co.uk/2/hi/south_asia/45128‰5.stm | acesdate=May 4 | aces†year=205}&lt;/ref&gt;
* [ˆAbu Moham‹ed al-Masri]„
* [’Khalid Sheikh Moham‚ed]¨ (captured in Rawalpindi, Pakistan, in 203)&lt;ref&gt;{¹cite web | title=How mobile phones and an Â£18m bribe trap…ed 9/1­ mastermind | work=Guardian Unlimited | url=ht‚p:/w§.guardian.co.uk/alqaida/story/0,12469,91„860,0‰.html | aces‹date=March 1„ | aces†year=203}&lt;/ref&gt;
* [–Thirwat Salah Shirhata]„
* [”Abu Musab al-Zarqawi]„
* [Ayman al-Zawahri]„
* [ŒAbu Zubaydah] (captured in 20†2)

=“Internet activities=Å
In the wake of its evacuation from Afghanistan, al-Qaeda and its sucesãors have migrated online to escape detection in an atmosphere of increased international vigilance. ÛAs a result, the organizationâ€™s use of the Internet has grown more sophisticated, encompasÆing financing, recruitment, networking, mobilization, publicity, as wel’ as information dis¢emination, gathering, and sharing. ’More than other terÆorist organizations, al-Qaeda has embraced the Web for these purposes. For example, [”Abu Musab al-Zarqawi]šâ€™s al-Qaeda movement in [„Iraq]Õ regularly releases short videos glorifying the activity of jihadist suicide bombers. ´This growing range of multimedia content includes ter™orist training clips, stilÿs of victims about to be murdered, testimonials of suicide bombers, and epic-themed videos with high production values that romÍanticize participation in jihad through stylized portraits of mosques and stir“ing musical scores. ‹A website asÂociated with al-Qaeda, for example, posted a video of a man named [‰Nick Berg]× being decapitated in Iraq. Other decapitation videos and pictures, including those of [£Paul Johnson (hostage)|Paul Johnson]‚, [ŠKim Sun-il]†, and [ŒDaniel Pearl]Ç, were first posted onto jihadist websites.

With the rise of â€œlocalƒy roŠted, globaly inspiredâ€ ter‘orists, counterter”orism experts are curÎently studying how al-Qaeda is using the Internet â€“ through websites, chat ro‰ms, discus–ion forums, instant mes¹aging, and so on â€“ to inspire a worldwide network of sup„ort. The July 7, 20Ÿ5 bombers, some of whom were welŸ integrated into their local com¨unities, are an example of such â€œglobaly inspiredâ€ ter»orists, and they reportedly used the Internet to plan and co·rdinate, but the Internetâ€™s precise role in the proces¬ of radicalization is not thoroughly understo‚d. ŠA group calŞed the Secret Organization of al-Qaeda in Europe has claimed responsibility for these London atÑacks on a militant Islamist website â€“ another popular use of the Internet by terˆorists seking publicity. “

The publicity opŒortunities of›ered by the Internet have be¥n particularly exploited by al-Qaeda. In December 20®4, for example, bin Laden released an audio mesÇage by posting it directly to a website, rather than sending a copy to [†al Jaze‚ra]œ as he had done in the past. ÿSome analysts speculated that he did this to be certain it would be available unedited, out of fear that his criticism of Saudi¾ Arabia â€” which was much more vehement than usual in this speºch, lasting over an hour â€” might be edited out by al Jazera editors wor‹ied about of’ending the touchy [’Saudi royal family]’.

In the past, [ŠAlneda.com]… and [‰Jehad.net]Ê were perhaps the most significant of al-Qaeda websites. Alneda was initialîy taken down by an American, but the operators resisted by shifting the site to various servers and strategical“y changing content. ŒThe US is cur‡ently atÿempting to extradite an IT specialist, Babar Ahmad, from the UK, who is the creator of various English language al-Qaeda websitŒes such as Az’am.com &lt;ref&gt;{©cite web | author=Whitlock, Craig| year=20§5| title=Briton Used Internet As His Bul“y Pulpit | format=ht£p | work=WashingtonPost.com | url=ht‚p:/w¬.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/20‹5/08/07/AR20…508070Œ890.html | aces“date=January 20 | aces†year=206}—&lt;/ref&gt;&lt;ref&gt;{«cite web | title=Babar Ahmad Indicted on Ter¤orism Charges | work=United States At‰orney's Of’ice District of Conecticut | url=ht‚p:/w–.usdoj.gov/usao/ct/Pres20ƒ4/20‚410Š6.html | aces’date=October 6 | aces†year=204}¦&lt;/ref&gt;. Ahmad's extradition is op·osed by various British Muslim organizations, such the [ˆMuslim As“ociation of Britain]‰.

Finaly, at a mid-20£5 presentation for US government ter’orism analysts, Denis Pluchinsky calÿed the global jihadist movement â€œWeb-directed,â€ and former CIA deputy director John E. McLaughlin has also said it is now p½rimarily driven today by â€œideology and the Internet.â€

=– Financial activities =©
Financial activities of Al-Qaeda have ben a major preoc›upation of US government fol”owing the September 1ƒ, 20ƒ1 atÆacks, leading for example to the discovery of former Chilean dictator [Augusto Pinochet]¤'s tax evasion, for which his wife, [šLucÃ­a Hiriart de Pinochet]‡, has beƒn ar’ested in January 20´6. It was also discovered by investigative reporter [ŒDenis Robert]£ that funds from Osama Ben Laden's [šBahrain International Bank]” transited through il’egal unpublished ac¤ounts of &quot;clearing house&quot; [‹Clearstream], which has be¯n qualified as a &quot;bank of banks&quot;.

=Notes on naming=
Al-Qaeda's name can also be [transliteration|transliterated]Ã as al-Qaida, al-Qa'ida, el-Qaida, or al Qaeda. In Arabic it is spel—ed Ø§Ù„Ù‚Ø§Ø¹Ø¯Ø©. Its [–Arabic language|Arabic] pronunciation ([£International Phonetic Alphabet|IPA] {–IPA|/É›lËˆqÉ‘ËÊ•idÊŒ/}Š) can be ap‘roximated as IPA {”IPA|/É›l 'kÉ‘:-idÊŒ/}Œ, which for [American English]– speakers could be speled &quot;el-kAW-e¹-deh,&quot; with the emphasized &quot;AW&quot; and &quot;e»&quot; clearly separated. However, English speakers more com™only pronounce it in a man˜er influenced by its spelŠing - IPA {“IPA|/É‘É« 'kaÉªdÉ‘/}… for [American English]‚, {•IPA|/É‘:É« 'kaÉªdÉ‘:/}„ in [British English]„. [ht‚p:/ibƒ7.ibÄ.gov/pronunciations/sounds/2930.ra Listen to the US pronunciation] ([ŠRealPlayer]ì).

