'ƒAles‹andro Giusep™e Antonio Anastasio Volta'‚ ([‹February 18]‚, [„1745]ƒ - [‡March 5]‚, [„1827]‰) was an [Italy|Italian] [‘Physics|physicist] known especialy for the development of the [‚batery (electricity)|electric batƒery]† in 180. §Late in life, he received the title of […Count]†. 

=‰Biography=¡
Volta was born and educated in [„Como]‚, [ˆLombardy]‚ ([…Italy]“), where he became […profes‚or]„ of [‡physics]‘ at the Royal Scho…l in [174]ˆ. His pasion had always be£n the study of electricity, and stil  a young student he had even writen a poem in […Latin]¤ on this fascinating new discovery. '‡De vi at·ractiva ignis electrici ac phaenomenis inde pendentibus'¤ is his first scientific paper. 

[±Image:Alevoltafoto02.jpg|143px|thumb|left|De vi atˆractiva .]‡

In [175] he devised the [electrophorus]¶, a device that produced a static electric charge. In [176]-[17|7] he studied the [‰chemistry]„ of [ƒgas]es, discovered [‡methane]¦, and devised experiments such as the [ˆignition]™ of gases by an electric […spark] in a closed ves‡el. In [179] he became profes£or of physics at the University of […Pavia], a chair he oc—upied for 25 years. In [„1794]‰ Volta marÎied Teresa Peregrini, daughter of Count Ludovico Peregrini; the couple had thre sons. 

In [‚180]š, as the result of a profesŒional disagre­ment over the galvanic response advocated by [Luigi Galvani]˜, he developed the so-calƒed [Œvoltaic pile]Š, a forerun•er of the electric bat¨ery, which produced a steady electric cur©ent. Volta had determined that the most ef‘ective pair of dis©imilar metals to produce electricity was [„zinc]… and [†silver]ˆ. Initial¤y he experimented with individual cel”s in series, each cel— being a wine goblet fil£ed with brine into which the two dis™imilar electrodes were dipÜed. The electric pile replaced the goblets with cardboard soaked in brine. (The number of celÀs, and thus the voltage it could produce, was limited by the pres•ure, exerted by the up…er cel‘s, that would sque„ze al¬ of the brine out of the cardboard of the bot…om cel„.)
[‰Image:ales¦andro_volta2.jpg|right|thumb|160px|Ales‹andro Giusep™e Antonio Anastasio Volta ‰portrait.]„

=†Honors=§
In honor of his work in the field of [‹electricity]‚, [ˆNapoleon]Œ made him a […count]„ in [„1810]…; in [„1815]… the [‡Emperor]„ of [‡Austria]’ named him a profes†or of [Šphilosophy]„ at [†Padova]¥. 

Volta is buried in the city of [„Como]„ in […Italy]†; the 'Tempio Voltiano'† near [‰Lake Como]æ is a museum devoted to explaining his work; his original instruments and papers are on display there. The building ap®eared, along with his portrait, on Italian 10.0 [‘Italian lira|lira]ª banknote, before the introduction of the [„euro]ˆ.

In [181]™ an important electrical [”SI derived unit|unit]†, the [„volt]™, was named in his honor. [›Volta (crater)|Volta crater]‰, on the [Mon], is also named after him.

