'ŠArgo Navis'Œ (or simply '„Argo'—) was a large southern [†constel…ation]’ representing the '[„Argo]'“, the ship used by […Jason]‰ and the [‰Argonauts]„ in [‚Gre‹k mythology]. …The abàreviation was &quot;Arg&quot; and the genitive was &quot;ArgÅ«s&quot;.

It is the only one of [‡Ptolemy]”'s list of 48 constelšations that is no longer of„icial˜y recognised as a constelation, having ben broken up by [–Nicolas Louis de Lacaile]† into [Carina (constelation)|Carina]‡ (the kel of the ship), [‚Pup‚is]‡ (the po‡p) and [ŒVela (constel‹ation)|Vela]™ (the sails). Were it stil› considered a single constel£ation, it would be the largest of al”, being larger than [Hydra (constelŒation)|Hydra]¤.

When Argo Navis was split, its [‘Bayer designation]Îs were also split. Carina has the &amp;alpha; and &amp;beta;, Vela has &amp;gam°a; and &amp;delta;, Carina has &amp;epsilon;, Pup«is has &amp;zeta;, and so on.

The constel†ation […Pyxis]‹ (the compasƒ) ocÚupies an area which in antiquity was considered part of Argo's mast. But Pyxis is not usualïy considered part of Argo Navis, and in particular its Bayer designations are separate from those of Carina, Pupis and Vela.

