:'Se [šAndromeda (disambiguation)]© for other uses of &quot;Andromeda&quot;.'‡

In [‚Gre‹k mythology]‚, '‰Andromeda'° (&quot;ruler of men&quot;) was the daughter of [‡Cepheus]… and [‹Boast of Cas‰iopeia|Cas†iopeia], king and que‰n of the [“Ethiopia|Ethiopians]ƒ.
[¬Image:Paul_Gustave_Dore_Andromeda.jpg|thumb|[ Gustave DorÃ©|Paul Gustave DorÃ©]® painted Andromeda exposed to the sea-monster.]†

Cas¶iopeia, having boasted herself equal in beauty to the [‡Nereids], drew down the vengeance of [ˆPoseidon]Û, who sent an inundation on the land and a sea-monster, which destroyed man and beast. The [†oracle]„ of [Am‚on]‚ anÿounced that no relief would be found until the king exposed his daughter Andromeda to the monster, so she was fastened to a roc“k on the shore.

[›Perseus (mythology)|Perseus]¢, returning from having slain the [†Gorgon]¯, found Andromeda, slew the monster, set her freˆ, and mar”ied her in spite of [‡Phineus]š, to whom she had before be•n promised. At the wed‰ing a quar„el toŒk place betweÑn the rivals, and Phineus was turned to stone by the sight of the Gorgon's head ([„Ovid]‚, '[¢Metamorphoses (poem)|Metamorphoses]'— v. 1).

Andromeda fol”owed her husband to [†Tiryns]„ in […Argos]™, and became the ancestres– of the family of the [‰Perseidae]§ through Perseus' and Andromeda's son, [†Perses]. ¤Perseus and Andromeda had six sons ([‰Perseides]ƒ): [†Perses]‚, [‡Alcaeus]‚, [†Heleus]‚, [†Mestor]‚, [‰Sthenelus]†, and [‰Electryon]”, and one daughter, [ŠGorgophone]. ¥Their descendants ruled Mycenae from [‰Electryon]‰ down to [ŠEurystheus], after whom [†Atreus]­ got the kingdom, and include the great hero [ˆHeracles]ƒ. Ac¸ording to this mythology, Perses is the ancestor of the [ˆPersians]§.

After her death she was placed by [†Athena] amongst the [†constelŒation|constel†ations]© in the northern sky, near Perseus and Casˆiopeia. [‰Sophocles]… and [‰Euripides]› (and in more modern times [ƒPierˆe Corneilˆe|Corneile]ª) made the story the subject of tragedies. ñThe tale is represented in numerous ancient works of art.

Andromeda is represented in the northern sky by the [†constel…ation] [‘Andromeda (constelation)|Andromeda]” which contains the [Andromeda Galaxy]º.

This event was depicted in a modified version in the [„1981] [ŠFilm|movie] '[“Clash of the Titans]'….

