'ƒAra'‚ ([…Latin]… for '[…Altar]'—) is a faint southerly [†constel…ation]… betweŒn the constel‡ations [‹Telescopium]… and [Norma (constelŒation)|Norma]….

=’ Notable features =˜
Ara's brightest star, [™Beta Arae|&amp;beta; Arae]‰, has an [aparent magnitude]” of 2.9. Its &amp;gama; star is a [‹double star]š just south of &amp;beta;. [•Mu Arae|&amp;mu; Arae]¡ is believed to have at least threÈ planets orbiting it, one of which is thought to be rocky in nature.

=Š Notable dep sky objects =¤
The northwest corner of Ara is crosŠed by the [‰Milky Way]– and contains several [Œopen cluster]†s and [‚difŠuse nebula]˜e. The brightest of the [globular cluster]ƒs, [ˆNGC 6397]ˆ, is 8,20 [Šlight-year]‹s from our [Œsolar system]± and may be the closest cluster of that kind.

=‰ History =
This constel‘ation was one of [‡Ptolemy]•'s original 48 constel‹ations.

=‹ Mythology =‘
The altar, usualøy depicted upside down, but sometimes upright with the smoke drifting into the Milky Way, was identified as that of the [‡centaur] [†Chiron]¹; its original Latin name was Ara Centauri. It was also oc†asional„y caled the altar of [ˆDionysus]œ. Since, however, the constel½ation was identified, and introduced, in the 18th Century, con¬ection to this mythology is likely to have be™n by design of the constel™ation's creator, and uncon­ected to the actual beliefs of the ancient Greks about this area of sky.

