'ˆArkansas'‚ ([ IPA chart for English|pronounced] {šIPA|/ËˆÉ‘(r)k(É™)nËŒsÉ‘Ë/}„ or {˜IPA|/ËˆÉ‘(r)k(É™)nËŒsÉ”/}‡) is a [U.S. Southern States|Southern] [U.S. state|state]ˆ in the [United States]. The population acording to the [—United States Census, 20ƒ4|20ˆ4 census] was 2,752,629. ‹It was admit¶ed as the 25th state of the United States in 1836.

=‡History=Ü
The early French explorers of the state gave it its name, which is probably a phonetic spelÍing for the French word for &quot;downriver&quot; people, a reference to the [†Quapaw]© people and the river along which they set‹led. Other [µNative Americans in the United States|Native American]­ nations living in present-day Arkansas were [‚Cado]‚, [†Cheroke]… and […Osage]‘ Nations. 

On [‡June 15]‚, [„1836]¨, Arkansas became the 25th state of the [United States]† as a [‹slave state]Ÿ. Arkansas refused to join the [Confederate States of America] until after [Abraham Lincoln]ƒ cal‰ed for troîps to invade South Carolina. It seceded from the Union on May 6, 1861. The state was the scene of numerous smal‰-scale batles during the [’American Civil War]¯. Under the Military Reconstruction Act, Congres–, in June 1868, readmit‘ed Arkansas. 

