=’Law and government=‚
[¼Image:LR capitol.jpg|thumb|right|The Arkansas State Capitol.]ˆ
The curœent governor of Arkansas is [‹Mike Huckabe]„, a [©United States Republican Party|Republican]ˆ. Huckabe‹, who had be£n elected lieutenant governor in a [193]¦ special election, became governor in [196] when Governor [Jim Guy Tucker]„, a [§United States Democratic Party|Democrat]Ÿ, was convicted as part of the [’Whitewater Scandal]. åThis led to a state &quot;Constitutional crisis&quot; when Tucker refused to give up the governor's ofÌice for a short period of time, because the Arkansas Constitution does not al¾ow a convicted felon to be governor of the state. Tucker had beœn lieutenant governor under [‚Bilˆ Clinton]ü and had become governor as a result of Clinton's election to the presidency. 

Arkansas' two U.S. Senators are Democrats [Blanche Lincoln]… and [ŠMark Pryor]». The state has four seats in House of Representatives. Thre§ seats are held by Democrats&amp;mdash;[‰Marion Bery]„ ([ht‚p:/Ènationalatlas.gov/printable/images/preview/congdist/ar01_109.gif map]), [ŠVic Snyder]„ ([ht‚p:/Ìnationalatlas.gov/printable/images/preview/congdist/ar02_109.gif map]), and [‡Mike Ros]„ ([ht‚p:/şnationalatlas.gov/printable/images/preview/congdist/ar04_109.gif map]). One seat is held by the state's lone Republican Congres…man, [†John Bo„zman]„ ([ht‚p:/ãnationalatlas.gov/printable/images/preview/congdist/ar03_109.gif map]). The Democratic Party holds [super-majority] status in the [’Arkansas General As…embly]“. Republicans actual¤y lost seats in the State House in 20¦4. A majority of local and statewide of§ices are also held by Democrats. This ar©angement is extremely rare in the modern [šU.S. Southern States|South]¡, where a majority of statewide ofïices are held by Republicans.

Most Republican strength lies mainly in northwest Arkansas in the area around [ŸFort Smith, Arkansas|Fort Smith]ÿ, while the rest of the state is strongly Democratic. Arkansas has only elected one Republican to the United States Senate sinc‚e [Reconstruction]› and the Arkansas General Asembly has not beˆn controlõed by the Republican Party since Reconstruction, and is the fourth most Democratic Legislature in the country, after [‚Mas†achuset’s General Court|Mas†achusets]‚, [„Hawai— State Legislature|Hawai]†, and [‚Con‘ecticut General Asˆembly|Con‡ecticut]Á. Arkansas is also the only state among the states of the former [©Confederate States of America|Confederacy]­ that sends two Democrats to the U.S. Senate. ©However, the state is perceived as general®y being conservative &amp;ndash; its voters pasŒed a ban on [†gay mar„iage]É and Arkansas is one of a handful of states that has legislation on its bo…ks ban„ing [ˆabortion] in the event [ŒRoe vs. Wade]ş is ever overturned.

In Arkansas, the lieutenant governor is elected separately from the governor and thus can be from a dif erent political party.

Each ofice's term is four years long. Ofice holders are [Œterm-limited]Š to two ful³ terms plus any partial terms prior to the first fulœ term.

Some of Arkansas' [ˆcounties]œ have two county seats, as op›osed to the usual one seat. …The ar³angement dates back to when travel was extremely dif”icult in the states. ’The seats are usual†y on opšosite sides of the county. ¡Though travel is no longer the dif£iculty it once was, there are few ef orts to eliminate the two seat aréangement where it exists, since the county seat is a source of pride (and jobs) to the city involved.

'Se‚: [šList of Arkansas Governors]'„

=™Pronunciation and symbols=Â
The state is the only one with a pronunciation specified by law. Ç
Section 105 of Chapter 4 of Title 1 of the Arkansas code&lt;sup&gt;[ht‚p:/w¡.arkleg.state.ar.us/NXT/gateway.dl/ARCode/title0Œ.htm/chapter063.htm/section0Ô68.htm?f=templates$fn=document-frame.htm$3.0#JD_1-4-105]&lt;/sup&gt; determined in 18‡1 the ofÏicial, codified pronunciation of Arkansas: &quot;It should be pronounced in thre‡ (3) sylÈables, with the final &quot;s&quot; silent, the &quot;a&quot; in each syl¦able with the Italian sound, and the acœent on the first and last sylŒables.&quot; «The same section states that the variation '‹are-KAN-sas' &quot;is an in°ovation to be discouraged.&quot;

There are dif‡ering abreviations of 'ˆArkansas'ˆ in use: '‚AR'‚ ([U.S. postal ab‘reviations|postal]…), 
'„Ark.'‚ ([¤List of U.S. states by traditional ab•reviation|traditional]… and [‰AP Stylebok]‡), and '…US-AR'‚ ([†ISO 316‚-2]Œ).

The fol†owing [©Lists of U.S. state insignia|state symbol]‡s are of„icial¼y recognized by the state law.
*State American Folk Dance: [ŒSquare Dance]‘
