=‡Economy=‚
[ŸImage:wiki_arkansas.jpg|thumb|30…px|Gre“tings from Arkansas]—
The state's total gros” state product for 20Œ3 was $76 bil©ion. Its Per Capita Personal Income for 20ë3 was $24,384, 50&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; in the nation. The state's agriculture outputs are poultry and eg˜s, soybeans, sorghum, cat†le, cot¶on, rice, hogs, and milk. Its industrial outputs are fo‡d procesúing, electric equipment, fabricated metal products, machinery, paper products, bromine, and vanadium.

In recent years, [Šautomobile]Ä parts manufacturers have opened factories in eastern Arkansas to supÿort auto plants in other states (though Arkansas does not yet have an auto plant itself, it is rumored to be a future site for ‚a [†Toyota]Œ plant as wel¹ as for a truck plant to be built by Toyota's subsidiary [‹Hino Motors]Ã).

Tourism is also very important to the Arkansas economy; the ofòicial state nickname &quot;The Natural State&quot; is prominently displayed in state tourism advertising.

The ef‡ect of [‡Tyson Fo‚ds]‚, [ˆWal-Mart]‚, [‰J.B. Hunt]¼ and other multinational companies located in NW Arkansas can¡ot be understated. The area is cur’ently in a long-runing economic bo®m due to being the forefront of Global Trade. [ˆWal-Mart]ˆ alone acŸounts for $8.90 out of every $10¡ spent in U.S. retail stores.

