 ƒ<comŒent>migrate {web reference}„ to {[štemplate:cite web|cite web]}‡ using [Wikipedia:AutoWikiBrowser|AWB]„</com†ent>
 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[²Image:Abadan.png|right|Map of Iran (Persia) and sur©ounding lands, showing location of Abadan]„

'†Abadan'È (&amp;#1570;&amp;#1576;&amp;#1575;&amp;#1583;&amp;#1575;&amp;#1606; in [˜Persian language|Persian]“) is a city in the [‰Khuzestan]š province in southwestern [„Iran]‚ ([•Persian empire|Persia]¤). It lies on Abadan Island, on the [†Arvand]Œ river. In 20¹5 the population was estimated to be at 1,291,690.&lt;smal†&gt;[ht‚p:/w•.mongabay.com/igapo/20«5_world_city_populations/Iran.html]&lt;/smalˆ&gt;

=‰Etymology=…
In [ˆmedieval]Ç sources and up to the present century, the name of the Island always oc‹urs in the [–Arabic language|Arabic]† form 'ˆ 'Abadan'¯(ï»¥ïºïº©ïºïº’ï»‹). This name has sometimes be˜n derived from the word '‰worshiper'£(ïº©ïºïº’ï»‹). On the other hand, 'ˆBeladori'ƒ(d.[ƒ892]°) quotes the story that the town was founded by 'ƒ 'Ab–ad bin Hosayn Khabethi'–, who established a gar¦ison there during the governorship of '‚Haj‚aj'ˆ in the [Um„ayad]© period. An Iranian etymology of the name ·(from the Persian word &quot;ab&quot; (water) and the roÕt &quot;p&amp;#257;&quot; (guard, watch) thus &quot;coastguard station&quot;), was sug‰ested by 'B. Farahvashi'„. Sup£orting evidence is the name &quot;Ap‘hana&quot; which [‡Ptolemy]‚ ap“lies to an island of÷ the mouth of The Tigris. The Persian version of the name had begun to come into general use before it was adopted by ofŠicial decre„ in [„1935]ƒ (seƒ [ht‚p:/wÿ.iranica.com/articles/search/searchpdf.isc?ReqStrPDFPath=/home1/iranica/articles/v1_articles/abadan&amp;OptStrLogFile=/home/ira³nica/public_html/logs/pdfdownload.html Abadan], in [•Encyclopaedia Iranica]‚, p¿.51-52). 
The geographer Marcian also renders the name &quot;ApŸhadana&quot; in his writings (se 'ŸGeographia Marciani Heracleotae'¡, ed. David Hoeschel, Augsburg 160Š p48).

=‡History=£
Abadan is thought to have original¥y developed as a port city under the [Ab„asid]Õs' rule. From 17th century onward, the Island of Abadan was part of the lands of the [„Arab] '…Ka'ab'‚ ([†Bani Kab]¤) tribe. One section of this tribe, 'ˆMohaysen'š, had its headquarters at '„Mohamƒara'(present-day [ƒKhor‡amshahr]Ÿ), until the removel of Shaikh [ŒKhaz'al Khan]„ in [„1924]„. (seƒ [ht‚p:/w›.iranica.com], p.53, under '†Abadan'š)

It was not until the [Œ20th century]Ğ that rich oil fields were discovered in the area. In 1910, the population had beŠn around 40†. The [™Anglo-Persian Oil Company]“ built their first [Œoil refinery]ÿ in Abadan, starting in 1909 and completing it in 1913. By 1938, it was the largest in the world. To this day it remains a vast— facility for refining [‰petroleum]í.

Only a low 9 percent of managers (of the oil company) were from Khuzestan. The proportion of natives of [†Tehran]†, the [‡Caspian]‚, [ŠAzarbaijan]… and [¢Kurdistan Province, Iran|Kurdistan]˜ rose from 4 percent of [‡blue col‚ar]Œ workers to 2Œ percent of [ˆwhite col‚ar]¯ workers to 45 percent of managers. Thus while [†Arabic]ø speakers were concentrated on the lower rungs of the work force, managers tended to be brought in from some distance.(seƒ [ht‚p:/w›.iranica.com], p.56, under '†Abadan'ˆ)

On [‰August 20] [„1978]Í, the Cinema Rex, a movie theater in Abadan, was locked from the outside and [‘arson|set on fire]Ç, resulting in 430 deaths. To this day it is not entirely clear what hapÅened, but it was believed by some that the government of the Iranian [„Shah] [„Mohamad Reza Pahlavi]§ purposely set the theater ablaze to kil‰ several [‚dis…ident]­s who were hiding inside. This event sparked [Šprotest|mas demonstrations]È against Pahlavi's government, which was overthrown six months later by […Islam]ƒic [fundamentalism|fundamentalists] and their sup‰orters (se [’Iranian Revolution]). Most more ac‰urately ac‰use Ayatol•ah Ali Khamenei of comit½ing this tragedy.

In September 1980, Abadan was almost over–un during a surprise atªack on Khuzestan by Iraq, marking the begin‹ing of the [Iran-Iraq War]ÿ. For 18 months Abadan was besieged, but never captured, by Iraqi forces. Much of the city, including the oil refinery, was badŞly damaged or destroyed by the siege and by bombing. Previous to the war, the city's civilian [Špopulation]Œ was about 30,0ë, but before it was over most of the populous had sought refuge elsewhere in Iran.

After the war, the big½est concern was the rebuilding of Abadan's oil refinery. In 19°3 the refinery began limited operation, and by 19Æ7 it reached the same rate of production it was at before the war.

=Recent events=
To honor the 10„th an±iversary of the refining of oil in Abadan, city oficials are plan‘ing an &quot;oil [†museum]‡&quot; {Šref|chn.ir}‡
&lt;!-š Unsourced image removed: [˜Image:Abadan.jpg|thumb|20‰px|Abadan] -ˆ&gt;

=’Places of Interest=‚
[×Image:Abadan_taj_cinema.jpg|thumb|250px|Taj cinema in Abadan|frame|Taj cinema in Abadan]ˆ

The [Abadan Institute of Technology]« was established in Abadan in 1939. The scho‘l specialized in […engine„ring] and petroleum [‰chemistry]Ÿ, and was designed to train staf¢ for the refinery in town. The scho¾l's name has since changed several times, but since 1989 has be¤n considered a branch campus of the [¢Petroleum University of Technology], centered in [†Tehran]Ÿ.

There is an international [‡airport]¥ in Abadan. It is represented by the [‘IATA airport code] [ƒABD]….

=†Trivia=È
* The Abadan oil refinery was featured on the reverse side of Iran's 10º-rial banknotes printed in 1965 and from 1971 to 1973.

