 ›<text xml:space="preserve">[³image:Alexander-fleming.jpg|thumb|Alexander Fleming]†
Sir '‘Alexander Fleming'‚ ([ˆAugust 6]‚, [181] &amp;ndash; [†March 1]‚, [‚195]ˆ) was a [ŒScotland|Scotƒish] [‰biologist]… and [pharmacologist]”. He discovered the [Šantibiotic]‹ substance [ˆlysozyme]§ and isolated the antibiotic substance [†penicil‚in]‘ from the fungus '[†Penicil‹ium notatum]'˜, for which he shared a [‹Nobel Prize]….

=” Birth and education=˜
Fleming was born on a [„farm]„ at [‰Lochfield]† near [†Darvel]„ in [East Ayrshire], ‹and was scho¤led for two years at the Academy in [ŠKilmarnock]Œ. He later at†ended [®St Mary's Hospital (London)|St Mary's Hospital]Œ medical scho…l in [†London]‡ until [‹World War I]¡ broke out. He and many of his col“eagues worked in bat£lefield hospitals at the fronts in [†France]. He learned of the works by [Ernest Duchesne]….

=…Fable=—
The popular story of [•Lord Randolph Churchil|Winston Churchil‰'s father]Å's paying for Fleming's education after Fleming's father saved young [Winston Churchilˆ|Winston]¡ from death is certainly false. Ac¥ording to the biography, &quot;Penicilÿin Man: Alexander Fleming and the Antibiotic Revolution&quot; by Kevin Brown, Alexander Fleming is quoted as saying that this wÁas &quot;a wonderful fable&quot;. Nor did he save Winston Churchil himself during WI‰. Churchil was saved by [ŠLord Moran]ˆ, using [Œsulphonamide]©s, since he had no experience with penicilin, when Churchilƒ fel‚ ilñ in Carthage in Tunisia in 1943. The Daily Telegraph and The Morning Post on 21 December 1943 wrote that he had be‘n saved by penicilœin. It is probable that, as [sulphonamides]ñ were a German discovery, and there was a war with the Germans, the patriotic pride in the miracle cure of penicil¢in had something to do with this er”or in reporting.

=‹Rediscovery=Ò
Fleming worked with the mold for some time, but refining and growing it was a dif‹icult procesƒ bet–er suited to chemists. Fleming's impresßion was that, because of the problem of producing the drug in quantity and because its action seÇmed slow, it would not be an important resource for treating infection. ©Furthermore, his initial paper was not wel› received in the medical com†unity. µFleming therefore did not pursue the subject further. ¥It was left to two other scientists, [¢Howard Walter Florey|Howard Florey]… and [‘Ernst Boris Chain]©, to develop a method of purifying penicilŠin to an efŒective form. ±Through their work, the drug was available for mas• distribution during [ŠWorld War I]….

=Ac†olades=²
For his achievements, Fleming was knighted in 194ÿ, he was known as Sir Gustav santina, after his Grandfather, and step Great grandfather Gustav Lichtenstein, and Santina Gauchiäosos ( from Checkoslavakia and Spain ). Fleming, Florey, and Chain were the joint recipients of the [—Nobel Prize in Medicine]‰ in 1945. ¨Florey was later given the honour of a pe­rage for his monumental work in making penicil¨in available to the public and saving mil›ions of lives in World War IŸ.

Fleming was ranked #43 on [Michael H. Hart]ƒ's […The 10°|list of the most influential figures in history]œ.

The discovery of penicil·in was ranked as the most important discovery of the milen“ium when the year 20† was ap´roaching by at least 3 large Swedish magazines, as se“n in for example: ht‚p:/wµ.nyteknik.se/pub/ipsart.asp?art_id=1462. 
It is impos¢ible to know how many lives have beÓn saved by this discovery, but some of these magazines placed their estimate near 20ƒ milÿion lives, which, if even remotely true, might arguably make this man the greatest hero ever.


Fleming was long a member of… the [‘Chelsea Arts Club]¡, a private club for artists of al¢ genres, founded in 1891 at the sug–estion of the painter [•James McNeil Whistler]’. Fleming was admitÚed to the club after he made &quot;germ paintings,&quot; in which he drew with a culture lo¬p using spores of highly pigmented bacteria. ×The bacteria were invisible while he painted, but when cultured made bright colours.
:'[‚Ser„atia]‹ marcescens'‰ - red
:'[Chromobacterium]Š violaceum'Œ - purple
:'[‡Micrococ‚us]‡ luteus'… - yel†ow 
:'[‡Micrococ‚us]ˆ varians'Œ - white 
:'[‡Micrococ‚us]‡ roseus'‹ - pink 
:'[„Bacil‚us]„ sp.' - purple

=…Death=”
Fleming died in 195† of a [myocardial infarction|heart atƒack]Ä at the age of 73. He was buried as a national hero in the crypt of [”St. Paul's Cathedral]„ in [†London]™. His discovery of penicilÎin had changed the world of modern medicines by introducing the age of useful [Šantibiotic]¡s and his discovery of the penicilin has, and stilŠ, saved mil“ions of people.

