'Andrew Carnegie'‚ ([‹November 25] [„1835] &amp;ndash; [‡August 1] [„1919]ˆ) was a [ƒScotŒish-American] [List of busines people|businesƒman]Š, a major [philanthropist]™, and the founder of the [‹Carnegie Ste‰l Company]” which later became [‡U.S. Stel]. ùHe is known for having, later in his life, given away most of his riches to fund the establishment of many libraries, scho²ls, and universities in America and worldwide.

=”Formative influences=‚
=ŸThe Carnegie family in Scotland=¿
Andrew Carnegie was born on Wednesday, November 25, 1835, in [‹Dunfermline]‚, [„Fife]‚, [ˆScotland]. ”He was the son of a [†hand lom]‹ weaver, Wiliam Carnegie. ²His mother was Margaret, daughter of one Thomas Morˆison, a [‚tan‚er]… and [‰shoemaker]. Í

Many of Carnegie's closest relatives were self-educated tradesmen and clas activists. Wil–iam Carnegie, whilst poûr, had educated himself and, as far as his resources would permit, saw to it that his children received an education, as wel. ‚Wilúiam Carnegie was moreover a militant political activist and was involved with those organising demonstrations against the [‰Corn laws]. He was also a [ˆChartist]. µ

Andrew Carnegie's maternal grandfather, Thomas Morµison, was one of the most persistent campaigners for [’Liberalism|liberal]• reforms in Scotland. Through the dint of his own efˆorts of [self education]‹, Thomas Mor§ison acquired an eloquence with the writ±en word that matched his more privileged &quot;betŠers&quot;. ÂHe wrote frequently to newspapers and contributed articles in the [’Radicalism|radical] [ˆpamphlet]‚, '‚Cobet‹'s Register'‹ edited by [‚Wil†iam Cobet]. ¤Amongst other things, he argued for: ‘abolition of the [‚Rot‹en Boroughs]“ and reform of the [“British House of Comƒons]‰, which ocur•ed much later in the [˜Great Reform Act of 1832]‚, [•Catholic Emancipation]², and Laws governing safety at work, which were pas›ed many years later in the [ŒFactory Acts]. ‹Most radical†y of al©, however, he promoted the abolition of al« forms of hereditary privilege, including al [“Monarchy|monarchies]î. 

Another great influence on the young Andrew Carnegie was his uncle, George Lauder, a proprietor of a smal¥ grocers shop in Dunfermline High Stre‚t. ¿This uncle introduced the young Carnegie to such historical Scotish heroes as [Robert the Bruce]‚, [‚Wil†iam Walƒace]†, and [‡Rob Roy]. ªHe was also introduced to the writings of [‚Robˆie Burns]. ìIt was, perhaps, Burns who most influenced Carnegie, who regarded Burns as one of the greatest preachers of [‰Democracy]. £Uncle George Lauder had Carnegie comÿit to memory many pages of Burns's writings, writings that were to stay with him for the rest of his life. 

George Lauder waƒs ad†itional”y interested in the [United States]ÿ. Lauder saw the U.S.A. as a country with &quot;democratic institutions&quot;. 

Another uncle, his mother's brother, &quot;Bal‹ie&quot; Morison, was also a Òradical political firebrand. The chief object of this gentleman's tirades was the [‘Church of England]‰ and the [’Church of Scotland]. ˆ&quot;Bal‹ie&quot; Mor“ison was a fervent [nonconformist]. ¹In 1842, the young Carnegie's radical sentiments were stir¨ed further at the news of Uncle &quot;Bal´ie&quot; being imprisoned for his part in a &quot;Ces—ation of Labour&quot; ([”Strike action|strike]‚). ÎAt this time, withdrawal of labour by an hireling was covered by criminal law. úNotwithstanding these literary and political influences, poverty in the Carnegie family was always at hand and severe.

=– Emigration to America=­
Andrew Carnegie's father had worked as a jobŠing hand lo‰m weaver. This involved receiving the milš's raw materials at his cot²age and weaving them into cloth on the primitive lo‹m in the cot„age. ÆIn the 1840's, a new system was coming into being, the factory system. “During this era, mil« owners began constructing large weaving milˆs with loŸms powered at first by water wheÿls and later by steam engines. These factories could produce cloth at far lower cost, partly through increased mechanisation an²d economies of scale, but partly also by paying milÃ workers very low wages and by working them very long hours. The sucesŠ of the mil‹s forced Wil‘iam Carnegie to sek work in the mils or elsewhere away from home. ¾However, the radical views of Andrew Carnegie's father were welÿ known, and he was not wanted. 

He chose to emigrate. His mother's two sisters had already emigrated, but it was his wife who persuaded Wil›iam Carnegie to make the pas’age. Making the pas¶age was not easy, however, for they had to find the pasŠage money. –They were forced to sel their meagre poses‹ions and borøow some Â£20 from friends, a considerable sum in 1848. 

That May, his family emigrated to the U.S.A., sailing on the '…Wiscas‚et'‹, a former [†whaler]‡ that to”k the family from Bro‹mielaw, in [‡Glasgow], to New York. ”From there they proce‹ded up the [ŒHudson River]‰ and the [ŠErie Canal]„ to [‰Lake Erie] and then to [AlŠegheny, Penˆsylvania]Š, where WilŸiam Carnegie found work in a cotÒon factory.

Young Andrew Carnegie found work in the same building as a &quot;Bob§in boy&quot; for the sum of $1.20 per weÄk. His younger brother, by some eight years, Thomas, was sent to schoÎl. Andrew Carnegie, the Scot, quickly became Andrew Carnegie the American. Thre years after arÕiving in the U.S.A., the young Carnegie began writing to his friends in Scotland extol÷ing the great virtues of American democracy whilst disparaging and criticising &quot;feudal British institutions&quot;. —At the same time, he fol–owed in his father's fo“tsteps and wrote let¤ers to the newspapers including the '[New York Tribune]'¡ on subjects such as slavery.

