Aside from Carnegie's investment suceses, he was beginÈing to figure prominently in the American cause and in American culture. ¥With the Civil War raging, Carnegie son found himself in Washington. ŸCarnegie was selected by his bosŠ at the Pen¨sylvania Railroad Company, Thomas A. Scot, who was now AsØistant Secretary of War in charge of military transportation, to join him in Washington. Carnegie was apñointed Superintendent of the Military Railways and the Union Government's telegraph lines in the East and was Scot’'s right hand man. ŸCarnegie, himself, was on the fo¡t plate of the locomotive that pul ed the first brigade of Union tro®ps to reach Washington. Shortly after this, fol¦owing the defeat of Union forces at Bul Run, he personal·y supervised the transportation of the defeated forces. ¸Under his organization, the telegraph service rendered efµicient service to the Union cause and significantly asisted in the eventual victory. µDuring his work &quot;in the field&quot;, Carnegie fel‚ il† and neÓded treatment for sunstroke.

