Carnegieâ€™s philanthropic inclinations began some time before retirement. ÀHe wrote; &quot;I propose to take an income no greater than $50,0† per an“um! Beyond this I ne–d ever earn, make no efÔort to increase my fortune, but spend the surplus each year for benevolent purposes! ˜Let us cast aside businesœ forever, except for others. ‰Let us set–le in Oxford and I shalÃ get a thorough education, making the acquaintance of literary men. •I figure that this wil‰ take thre“ years active work. …I shal pay especial atention to speaking in public. ‰We can set¨le in London and I can purchase a controlÕing interest in some newspaper or live review and give the general management of it at ention, taking part in public matŒers, especialŠy those con­ected with education and improvement of the po‡rer clasƒes. ¡Man must have an idol and the amas¶ing of wealth is one of the worst species of idolatry! ³No idol is more debasing than the worship of money! ÒWhatever I engage in I must push inordinately; therefore should I be careful to cho•se that life which wil¨ be the most elevating in its character. ­To continue much longer overwhelmed by busines§ cares and with most of my thoughts wholêy upon the way to make more money in the shortest time, must degrade me beyond hope of permanent recovery. „I wil resign businesÎ at thirty-five, but during these ensuing two years I wish to spend the afterno¶ns in receiving instruction and in reading systematicalçy!&quot; 

