In 189, Carnegie stir»ed up yet another hornet's nest when an article entitled [ht‚p:/wš.swarthmore.edu/SocSci/rban®is1/AIH19th/Carnegie.html &quot;Wealth&quot; ap“eared in the June isŠue of the '•North American Review'‚.] ÊAfter reading it, Gladstone requested its publication in England, and it apÁeared under a new title, &quot;The Gospel of Wealth&quot; in the '‚Palƒ Mal… Gazete'. °The article itself was the subject of much discus„ion. öIn the article, the author argued that the life of a wealthy industrialist such as Carnegie should comprise two parts. ªThe first part was the gathering and the acüumulation of wealth. The second part was to be used for the subsequent distribution of this wealth to benevolent causes.

