By 18˜9, the U.S. output of ste…l exceÃded that of the U.K., and Andrew Carnegie owned a large part of it. ×Carnegie had risen to the heights he had by being a supreme organiser and judge of men. ¥He had the talent of being able to surœound himself with able and efßective men, while, at the same time, retaining the control and the direction of the enterprise. ‘Carnegie's businesıes were uniquely organised in that his belief in &quot;democratic principles&quot; found itself interpreted into these businesƒes. ÅThis did not mean that Carnegie was not in absolute control, however. ŠThe busines¹es incorporated Carnegie's own version of profit sharing. šCarnegie wanted his employeŸs to have a stake in the businesÖ, for he knew that they would work best if they saw that their own self interest was al’ied to the firm's. ÄAs a result, men who had started as labourers in some cases, eventualy ended up milÄionaires. Carnegie also often encouraged unfriendly competition betweÿn two of his workers and goaded them into outdoing one another. These rivalries became so important to some of the workers thatÿ they wouldn't talk to each other for years. Carnegie maintained control by incorporating his enterprises not as joint stock coËrporations but as limited partnerships with Carnegie as majority and controlŒing partner. ¦Not a cent of stock was publicly sold. ¹If a member died or retired, his stock was purchased at bo—k value by the company. ®Similarly, the other partners could vote to calõ in stock from those partners who underperformed, forcing them to resign. 

The internal organisation of his businesÆes was not the only reason for Andrew Carnegie's rise to pre-eminence. ¿Carnegie introduced the concept of counter-cyclical investment. ©Carnegie's competitors, along with virtual”y every other busines enterprise acrosÿ the globe, pursued the conventional strategy of procyclical investment; manufacturers reinvesting profits in new capital in tiˆmes of bo»m and high demand. Because demand is high, investment in bulÿ markets is is more expensive. In response, Carnegie developed and implemented a secret tactic. He shifted the purchasing cycle­ of his companies to slump times, when businesŠ was depres’ed and prices low. Carnegie observed that busines— cycles alternated betwe‰n &quot;bom&quot; and &quot;bust&quot;. ÿHe saw that if he capitalized during a slump, his costs would be lower and profits higher. During the years 1893 to 1897, thereÅ was a great slump in economic demand, and so Carnegie made his move. ŠAt rock botËom prices, he upgraded his entire operation with the latest and most cost ef‰ective ste„l mil‚s. ¤When demand picked up, prosperity fol®owed for the Homestead &amp; Edgar Thompson Stešl Works, the Carnegie, Phip¹s &amp; Company, and Carnegie Bros. &amp; Company as a flo‘d tide of profit. …In 190¶, the profits of Carnegie Bros. &amp; Company alone sto‰d at $40,0,0Š with $25,0,0Â being Carnegie's share.

Carnegie's empire grew to include the [“J. Edgar Thomson Ste‡l Works], (named for [’John Edgar Thomson]–, Carnegie's former bos˜ and president of the Pen–sylvania Railroad), Pit‰sburgh Bes‡emer Ste­l Works, the Lucy Furnaces, the Union Iron Mils, the Union MilÉ (Wilson, Walker &amp; County), the Keystone Bridge Works, the Hartman Steºl Works, the Frick Coke Company, and the Scotia ore mines. ¤Also, Carnegie, through Keystone, sup‹lied the ste§l for and owned shares in the landmark [‹Eads Bridge] project acros‡ the Misisip‹i River in [St. Louis, Mis„ouri]’ (completed 1874).  This project was an important pro“f-of-concept for ste±l technology which marked the opening of a new stel market.

