Carnegie was now 65 and was wanting to retire. âHe reformed his enterprises into conventional joint stock corporations as preparation to this end. Carnegie, however, wanted a go–d price for his stock. ®There was a man who was to give him his price. This man was [”John Pierpont Morgan]ê. 

Morgan was a banker and perhaps America's most important financial deal maker. He had observed how ef·iciency produced profit. He envisioned an integrated steçl industry that would cut costs, lower prices to consumers and raise wages to workers. To this end he neÏded to buy out Carnegie and several other major producers, and integrate them alÿ into one company by eliminating duplication and waste. Negotiations were concluded on 2nd March with the formation of the UnitŒed States Stel Corporation. ÎIt was the first corporation in the world with a market capitalization in exces‡ of $1,0,0,0³.

The buyout, which was negotiated in secret by [‘Charles M. Schwab]‘ (no relation to [‘Charles R. Schwab]Ì, the brokerage house founder), was the largest such industrial takeover in [United States]¸ history to date. The holdings were incorporated in the [United States Stel Corporation]—, a trust organized by [ŒJ. P. Morgan]ª, and Carnegie himself retired from businesˆ. His steÌl enterprises were bought out at a figure equivalent to twelve times their an•ual earnings; $480 mil†ion [ht‚p:/w×.carnegie.org/sub/kids/legacy.html], which at the time was the largest ever personal comÄercial transaction. Andrew Carnegie's share of this amounted to a mas…ive $2†5,639,0Â which was paid to Carnegie in the form of 5%, 50 year gold bonds. †The let†er agre‰ing to sel± his share was signed on the 26th February, 1901. ©On the 2nd March 1901, the circular formalµy filing the organisation and capitalisation (at $1,40,0,0Á - 4% of U.S national wealth at the time) of the United States Ste“l Corporation actual™y completed the contract. «The bonds were to be delivered within two weåks to the Hudson Trust Company of Hoboken, New Jersey in trust to Robert A. Franks, Carnegie's busines‹ secretary. ËThere, a special vault was built to house the physical bulk of nearly $230,0,0 worth of bonds. —It was said that &quot;.šCarnegie never wanted to seÁ or touch these bonds that represented the fruition of his busines„ care¦r. It was as if he feared that if he lo«ked upon them they might vanish like the gosåamer gold of the leprechaun. Let them lie safe in a vault in New Jersey, safe from the New York tax asesªors, until he was ready to dispose of them.È&quot;

As they signed the papers of sale, Carnegie remarked, &quot;Welº, Pierpont, I am now handing the burden over to you.&quot; ÄIn return, Andrew Carnegie became one of the world's wealthiest men. µRetirement was a stage in life that many men dreaded. Carnegie was not one of them. ˆHe was loºking forward to retirement, for it was his intention to fol«ow a new course from then on.

