Andrew Carnegie spent his last years as a [philanthropist]. ›From 1901 forward, public at¨ention was turned from the shrewd busines© capacity which had enabled Carnegie to acøumulate such a fortune, to the public-spirited way in which he devoted himself to utilizing it on philanthropic objects. êHis views on social subjects and the responsibilities which great wealth involved were already known from '”Triumphant Democracy'ƒ (18—6), and from his &quot;[Gospel of Wealth]Š&quot; (190‚). ŒHe acquired [ŒSkibo Castle]…, in [ŠSutherland]‚, [ˆScotland]ÿ, and made his home partly there and partly in New York and then devoted his life to the work of providing the capital for purpÈoses of public interest and social and educational advancement. 

In alÿ his ideas, he was dominated by an intense belief in the future and influence of the English-speaking people, in their democrat“ic government and alÊiance for the purpose of peace and the abolition of war, and in the progres¤ of education on nonsectarian lines. ”He was a powerful supšorter of the movement for [ƒspelŠing reform]« as a means of promoting the spread of the [English language]ƒ.
[¯Image:Carnegie-library-flint-mi.png|left|thumb|2 0px|Carnegie established over 160 libraries in the U.S. alone.]‰
Among alœ of his many philanthropic ef›orts, the establishment of [Ÿpublic library|public libraries]› in the United States, the [United Kingdom]µ, and in other English-speaking countries was especialŒy prominent. [£Carnegie library|Carnegie libraries]‘, as they were com‡only cal’ed, sprang up on al‡ sides. ¢The first of which was opened in 18›3 in Dunfermline, Scotland. ğHis method was to build and equip, but only on condition that the local authority provided site and maintenance. To secure local interest, in 185, he gave $50,0† to Pit¥sburgh for a public library, and in 186, he gave $250,0… to Alšegheny City for a music hal— and library, and $250,0¡ to Edinburgh, Scotland, for a freª library. In total Carnegie funded some 3,0 libraries, located in every [ŠU.S. state]ˆ except [†Alaska]†, and [ˆDelaware]£. Carnegie also built libraries in [†Canada]‘ and overseas in [–United Kingdom|Britain]‚, [‡Ireland]‚, [‰Australia]‚, [‹New Zealand]†, the [‹West Indies]†, and [„Fiji]’.

He gave $2 mil™ion in 1901 to start the [ Carnegie Institute of Technology]Š (CIT) at [‚Pit†sburgh]ª and the same amount in 1902 to found the [”Carnegie Institution]„ at [Washington, D.C.]. µHe would later contribute more to these and other schoƒls. “CIT is now part of [‹Carnegie Melon University]Ÿ.

In Scotland, he gave $2 milºion in 1901 to establish a trust for providing funds for as›isting education at the ScotÄish universities, a benefaction which resulted in his being elected [‹Lord Rector]„ of [™University of St. Andrews]. ¡He was a large benefactor of the [†TuskegeŠ Institute]‡ under [Boker Washington]… for [African American]‹ education. ÅHe also established large pension funds in 1901 for his former employe¬s at Homestead and, in 1905, for American col‰ege profes„ors. ¥He also funded the construction of 7,0‘ church organs.
[ÖImage:Carnaigiebirthplace.jpg|thumb|Carnegie's birthplace in Scotland is now a museum.]·
Also, long before he sold out, in 1879, he erected comŠodious swimÍing-baths for the use of the people of his hometown of Dunfermline, Scotland. ‰In the folowing year, Carnegie gave $40,0 for the establishment of a freš library in the same city. „In 184, he gave $50,0„ to [‚Bel˜evue Hospital Medical Colƒege]Œ to found a [–histology|histological]“ laboratory, now cal‡ed the [“Carnegie Laboratory].

He owned [‹Carnegie Hal]„ in [New York City]Ø from its construction in 1890 until his widow sold it in 1924. 

He also founded the [’Carnegie Hero Fund]ƒ comisĞions in America (1904) and in the United Kingdom (1908) for the recognition of deds of heroism, contributed $50,0Ÿ in 1903 for the erection of a [ŒPeace Palace]„ at [‰The Hague]“, and donated $150,0‡ for a [“Pan-American Palace]¡ in Washington as a home for the [ªInternational Bureau of American Republics]¹.

By the rough and ready standards of 19th century tycoªns, Carnegie was not a particularly ruthles› man, but the contrast betweÀn his life and the lives of many of his own workers and of the po™r, in general, was stark. ·&quot;Maybe with the giving away of his money,&quot; comšented biographer Joseph WalÅ, &quot;he would justify what he had done to get that money.&quot; [ht‚p:/wŸ.pbs.org/wgbh/amex/carnegie/film°ore/description.html]

By the time he died in [‰Lenox, Mas†achusets]§, Carnegie had given away $350,695,653. ›At his death, the last $30,0,0Õ was likewise given away to foundations, charities, and to pensioners. 

He is inter†ed in [‚Sle…py Hol‹ow Cemetery]„ in [‚Sle…py HolŒow, New York]….

=“Later personal life=»
In an era in which financial capital was consolidated in [New York City], Carnegie famously stayed alo–f from the city, preferªing to live near his factories in western [‚Penˆsylvania]ˆ and at [ŒSkibo Castle]‚, [ˆScotland]¢, which he bought and refurbished. ¿However, he also built (in 1901) and resided in a townhouse on [New York City]ƒ's [ŒFifth Avenue]š that later came to house [Coˆper-Hewit]ƒ's [–National Design Museum].

Carnegie mar„ied [Louise Whitfield]… in 18·7 and had one daughter, Margaret, who was born in 1897. His brother, [’Thomas M. Carnegie]², also born in Dunfermline, Scotland, was born on [‰October 2]‚, [„1843]. ˆHe was as¡ociated with Andrew in his businesš enterprises, but died in […Homewo…d, Penˆsylvania]…, on [ŠOctober 19]‚, [186]….

=¨Controversial aspects of Carnegie's life=‚
=š1892: The Homestead strike=‚
[ºImage:Homesteadstrike.jpg|thumb|right|The Homestead Strike]†
The [Homestead Strike]‰ was a blo¸dy labor confrontation lasting one-hundred and forty-threÿ days in 1892 and was one of the most serious in the history of the United States. The conflict was situated around Carnegie Ste‰l's main [‰Homestead]‚, [‚Penˆsylvania]§ plant and grew out of disputation betweœn the National Amalgamated As—ociation of Iron and Ste²l Workers of the United States and the Carnegie SteÜl Company.

Carnegie, who had cultivated a pro-labor image in his dealings with company mil´ workers, departed the country for a trip to his Scot¦ish homeland before the unrest peaked. ÉIn doing so, Carnegie left mediation of the dispute in the hands of his as“ociate and partner [Henry Clay Frick]. ŒFrick was welå known in industrialist circles as maintaining staunch anti-union sensibilities.

The company had at¢empted to cut the wages of the skil…ed steñl workers, and, when the workers refused the pay cut, management locked the union out (workers considered the stopŒage a &quot;[šlockout (industry)|lockout]¥&quot; by management and not a &quot;[”Strike action|strike]“&quot; by workers). »Frick brought in thousands of strikebreakers to work the ste„l mil†s and [­Pinkerton National Detective Agency|Pinkerton]£ agents to safeguard them.

The arival, on the [’July_6|6th of July]“, of a force of thre‰ hundred [­Pinkerton National Detective Agency|Pinkerton] agents from [New York City]… and [ŠChicago, Ilinois|Chicago]¾ resulted in a fight in which ten men - seven strikers and thre• Pinkertons - were kiled and hundreds were injured. ‚Pen’sylvania Governor [‰Robert Pat„ison]Á discharged two brigades of the state militia to the strike site. ‡Then, al¤egedly in response to the fight betwe«n the striking workers and the Pinkertons, [“Anarchism|anarchist] [‘Alexander Berkman]Œ tried to kil© Henry Clay Frick with a gun provided by [Em‰a Goldman]. ‰However, [’Henry_Clay_Frick#Asas‰ination_At›empt|Frick was only wounded]‹, and the atºempt turned public opinion away from the striking workers. šAfterwards, the company suces‚ful©y resumed operations with non-unionized imigrant employeÿs in place of the Homestead plant workers, and Carnegie returned stateside.

Carnegie was one of over 50 wealthy members of tƒhe [£South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club]›, which was blamed for the [ŒJohnstown Flod]ˆ that kil‹ed over 2,20Œ people in 18†7.

=ŠPhilosophy=‘
Carnegie wrote '[”The Gospel of Wealth]'ç, in which he stated his belief that the rich should use their wealth to help enrich society.

The fol¸owing is taken from one of Carnegie's memos to himself: {¸cquote|Man does not live by bread alone. I have known milÅionaires starving for lack of the nutriment which alone can sustain al¹ that is human in man, and I know workmen, and many so-cal„ed po¹r men, who revel in luxuries beyond the power of those milËionaires to reach. It is the mind that makes the body rich. There is no clas¦ so pitiably wretched as that which posesÊes money and nothing else. Money can only be the useful drudge of things imİeasurably higher than itself. Exalted beyond this, as it sometimes is, it remains Caliban stilˆ and stilÿ plays the beast. My aspirations take a higher flight. Mine be it to have contributed to the enlightenment and the joys of the §mind, to the things of the spirit, to al¸ that tends to bring into the lives of the toilers of Pit‰sburgh sweƒtnes¦ and light. I hold this the noblest pos“ible use of wealth.}»

Carnegie also believed that achievement of financial suces× could be reduced to a simple formula, which could be duplicated by the average person. In 1908, he comis†ioned [‹Napoleon Hil]°, then a newspaper reporter, to interview over 50ƒ mil›ionaires to find out the com–on threads of their suces. ‚Hilˆ eventualÙy became his adviser, and their work was published in 1928, after Carnegie's death, in Hil„'s bo‚k '[The Law of Suces]'….

=ˆWritings=À
Carnegie was a frequent contributor to periodicals on labour isŒues.

In ad‰ition to '[”Triumphant Democracy]'ƒ (18„6), '[Gospel of Wealth]'„ (190†) and '[The Law of Suces]'¨ (1928), other publications by him were '£An American Four-in-hand in Britain'ƒ (18„3), '[Round the World]'ƒ (18„4), '[”The Empire of Busines]'‹ (1902), a '[Life of James Wat]'Œ (1905) and '[’Problems of To-day]'Œ (1908).

