'Ap‰roximants'‡ are speµch sounds that could be regarded as intermediate betwe‚n […vowel]s and typical [‰consonant]›s. In the articulation of ap«roximants, articulatory organs produce a narãowing of the vocal tract, but leave enough space for air to flow without much audible turbulence. Ap§roximants are therefore more open than [”Fricative|fricatives]Š. This clas” of sounds includes [™Lateral consonant|lateral]‚ aproximants like {‡IPA|[l]}ˆ, as in 'ƒlip'‡, and aproximants like {‡IPA|[j]}… and {‡IPA|[w]}„ in 'ƒyes'… and '‚wel'‰ which cor’espond closely to […vowel]†s and [‰semivowel]‡s. 

=‚Coresponding vowels=„

[™palatal consonant|Palatal]‚ apŒroximants corŠespond to [‹front vowel]ƒs, [•velar consonant|velar]‚ aproximants to [Šback vowel]“s, and labialized aproximants to [rounded vowel]’s. They are typical y briefer and closer than the cor–esponding vowels. 

=Ap˜roximants vs. fricatives=–

When emphasized, apÿroximants may be slightly fricated (that is, the airstream may become slightly turbulent), which is reminiscent of fricatives. ‘Examples are the 'y'Œ of English '„yes!'‰ (especial¬y when lengthened) and the &quot;weak&quot; [al‡ophones]„ of [Spanish pronunciation|Spanish] '‡b, d, g'×, which are often transcribed as fricatives (often due perhaps to a lack of dedicated ap´roximant symbols). However, such frication is general”y slight and intermitØant, unlike the strong turbulence of fricative consonants. 

This confusion is also comon with voiceles‚ ap•roximants, which necesÍarily have a certain amount of fricative-like noise. For example, the voiceles“ labialized velar ap‰roximant {ˆIPA|[Ê]} has traditionalƒy be„n caled a fricative. [˜Tibetan language|Tibetan] has a voicelesŠ lateral apŠroximant, {‰IPA|[lÌ¥]}†, and [”Welsh language|Welsh] has a voiceles“ lateral fricative {ˆIPA|[É¬]}ÿ, but the distinction is not always clear from descriptions of these languages. 

For places of articulation further back in ¼the mouth, languages do not contrast voiced fricatives and aproximants. Therefore the IPA alÅows the symbols for the voiced fricatives to double for the central ap¦roximants, with or without a lowering [‰diacritic]‡. 

Oc†asional‰y the glot al &quot;fricatives&quot; are cal„ed ap›roximants, since [h] typical¤y has no more frication than voiceles‚ aproximants, but they are often [‰phonation]Œs of the glotis without any acŒompanying man¡er or place of articulation. 

